window.CARD_DATA = [
{"id":"wh-001","num":"1","category":"World · History","question":"Known only from what people left behind — tools, bones, hearths and cave art — <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covers the human past before written records.","answer":"Prehistory","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – 3200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">until writing arrives locally</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Prehistory</b> is the stretch of the human past that comes before people in a given place began keeping written records of themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because there are no texts to read, archaeologists have to reconstruct it from the things people left behind — tools, bones, hearths and cave art — and from the ground those things were buried in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What makes a period prehistoric is simply the absence of writing, not how sophisticated its people were or how well they understood their world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a different way of dividing the past from the Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages, which are defined by the main material people used for tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two schemes can come apart: a society can be in the Iron Age and still count as prehistoric if it left no writing behind. <br><br> Prehistory is usually taken to begin with the first stone tools, made about 2.6 million years ago, although some researchers argue for an earlier starting point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has no single ending, because writing did not spread everywhere at once but reached different parts of the world at very different times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It appears in Mesopotamia and Egypt around 3200 BCE, considerably later in China, and in some regions only within the last few centuries, so the same calendar year can be prehistoric in one place and historical in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word itself can wrongly imply that people without writing had no history at all, when in fact they preserved and passed on their past through stories, songs and other spoken tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That implication was put to colonial use against the peoples it was applied to, and scholars who work on those histories now argue for retiring the word rather than applying it to anyone's ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Prehistory","questions":["Beginning with the first stone tools some 2.6 million years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no single ending, since writing reached Mesopotamia around 3200 BCE but other regions only recently.","Defined by the absence of writing rather than by any material, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can include an Iron Age society, and many who study those histories now argue the word should be retired altogether."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["Gesa Mackenthun and Christen Mucher, “Introduction,” in <i>Decolonizing “Prehistory”: Deep Time and Indigenous Knowledges in North America</i>, ed. Gesa Mackenthun and Christen Mucher (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2021), 6–8, https://open.uapress.arizona.edu/projects/decolonizing-prehistory. [Open access]","Christopher Woods, “Visible Language: The Earliest Writing Systems,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 15–17, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","Podjanok Kanjanajuntorn, “The Three-Age System: A Struggle for Southeast Asian Prehistoric Periodisation,” <i>SPAFA Journal</i> 4 (2020): 2–3, https://doi.org/10.26721/spafajournal.v4i0.623. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,” <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/PSM_V44_D647_Delineations_on_pieces_of_antler.jpg","title":"Prehistory","desc":"Delineations on pieces of antler. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PSM_V44_D647_Delineations_on_pieces_of_antler.jpg","alt":"PSM V44 D647 Delineations on pieces of antler"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"wh-002","num":"2","category":"World · History","question":"Sorting the deep past by the material used for tools, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arranges prehistory into three successive periods — Stone Age, Bronze Age, Iron Age.","answer":"Three-age system","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1810s–1820s, by C. J. Thomsen</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Three-age system</b> is a framework that sorts the human past into three successive periods: the Stone Age, then the Bronze Age, then the Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each age takes its name from the material that dominated the making of cutting tools and weapons at the time, which makes technology rather than politics or dynasty the organising principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system is the whole ordering method, so the Stone Age is only its first period and not the system itself. What it delivers is a relative order — which finds came earlier and which came later — rather than exact calendar dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That limitation matters less than it sounds, because a sequence lets undated objects be slotted into place by what they are made of, so a store of unlabelled finds becomes a chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, a Danish curator, worked the scheme out in the early 1800s — one of three Scandinavian scholars whose relative orderings remade the subject — drawing his answer from the objects rather than from any written record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sorting a museum's collection, he saw that the oldest finds held stone tools but no metal, set the arrangement out in print in 1836, and his colleague Jens Jacob Worsaae then carried it across Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient writers had imagined ages named for metals long before him, but Hesiod's races of gold, silver, bronze, heroes and iron are a tale of moral decline rather than a claim tested against evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the scheme was built from European and Near Eastern material, it does not fit everywhere, and John Lubbock, who extended it in English, wrote that he applied his own classification to Europe alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across much of Africa south of the Sahara iron appears early with little sign of bronze before it, and the term is largely missing from the archaeology of the Americas and Oceania too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Three-age system","questions":["Sorting a museum's collection, the Danish curator Christian Jürgensen Thomsen built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the objects themselves and set it out in print in 1836.","Because it gives only a relative order, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lets undated finds be placed by what they are made of, though it fits Europe far better than Africa or the Americas."],"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","science"],"sources":["Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, “Of the Different Periods to Which the Heathen Antiquities May Be Referred,” in <i>Guide to Northern Archaeology</i>, ed. the Earl of Ellesmere (London: James Bain, 1848), 64–67, https://archive.org/details/guidetonorthern00ellegoog. [Open access]","Peter Rowley-Conwy, “The Three Age System in English: New Translations of the Founding Documents,” <i>Bulletin of the History of Archaeology</i> 14, no. 1 (2004): 4–15, esp. 4–5, https://doi.org/10.5334/bha.14102. [Open access]","Podjanok Kanjanajuntorn, “The Three-Age System: A Struggle for Southeast Asian Prehistoric Periodisation,” <i>SPAFA Journal</i> 4 (2020): 2, 5, https://doi.org/10.26721/spafajournal.v4i0.623. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Works and Days</i>, lines 109–201, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, in <i>Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica</i>, Project Gutenberg ebook 348, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/348/pg348.txt. [Open access]","John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Julien Cooper and Yegor Grebnev, “Introduction: Bronze Age Civilizations,” <i>Old World: Journal of Ancient Africa and Eurasia</i> 3, no. 1 (2023): 3–4, https://doi.org/10.1163/26670755-20230009. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Thomsen.jpg","title":"Three-age system","desc":"Christian Jürgensen Thomsen (1777–1863) showing visitors around in the Danish National Museum. Contemporary drawing. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomsen.jpg","alt":"A contemporary drawing of Christian Jürgensen Thomsen holding up an object for a group of museum visitors, in a gallery lined with glass cases of arranged antiquities"}},
{"id":"wh-003","num":"3","category":"World · History","question":"First and longest of the three ages that archaeologists count as stone, bronze and iron, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the vast stretch of prehistory in which cutting tools were made chiefly from stone.","answer":"Stone Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – 3300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in the Near East, later elsewhere</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Stone Age</b> is the earliest and longest period of human prehistory, the first of the three technological ages into which early material culture is traditionally sorted: stone, then bronze, then iron. It is a technological label rather than a fixed span of dates, defined by stone as the main material for tools with an edge, a point, or a striking surface. The scheme was devised in the 1820s by the Danish curator Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, who noticed while ordering the National Museum of Denmark that stone, bronze, and iron objects fell into a regular succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It therefore has no single worldwide beginning or end: it opened with the first shaped stone tools around 2.6 million years ago and closed only when a given people took up metalworking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That closing came at very different times from place to place: about 3300 BCE in the Near East, later across Europe and Asia, and, by the usual scheme, never in some regions. <br><br> Archaeologists divide it into three parts: the Paleolithic, the age of chipped stone, filling roughly 99 percent of it; the brief post-glacial Mesolithic; and the Neolithic, when farming, villages, pottery, and polished stone tools appeared. The names came later than the framework: John Lubbock coined 'Paleolithic' and 'Neolithic' in 1865, and 'Mesolithic' was added soon after to bridge the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its tools were the work of many kinds of human across almost 3 million years — early Homo, the far-ranging Homo erectus, the Neanderthals, and at last our own species. The great turning point near its end was the Neolithic Revolution, the shift to farming that began about 10,000 BCE in the Fertile Crescent and independently elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because it was built from European and Near Eastern finds, the scheme fits much of the world poorly — parts of Africa passed straight from stone to iron — and 'Stone Age' must never be used of any living people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Stone Age","questions":["Devised in the 1820s by the Danish curator Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the first of the three technological ages, followed by bronze and then iron.","Archaeologists split the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the chipped-stone Paleolithic, the brief post-glacial Mesolithic, and the Neolithic, when farming, villages and polished stone tools first appeared."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Peter Rowley-Conwy, \"The Three Age System in English: New Translations of the Founding Documents,\" <i>Bulletin of the History of Archaeology</i> 14, no. 1 (2004): 4–15, esp. 4–5, https://doi.org/10.5334/bha.14102. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., \"3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,\" <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., \"Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under \"The Origins of Domestication,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Podjanok Kanjanajuntorn, \"The Three-Age System: A Struggle for Southeast Asian Prehistoric Periodisation,\" <i>SPAFA Journal</i> 4 (2020): 2–3, https://doi.org/10.26721/spafajournal.v4i0.623. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Paulnabrone.jpg","title":"Stone Age","desc":"Poulnabrone Dolmen, County Clare, Ireland, downloaded from pdphoto.org, en:sr:Слика:Paulnabrone.jpg. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paulnabrone.jpg","alt":"Paulnabrone"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"wh-009","num":"9","category":"World · History","question":"Walking upright on two legs while still carrying a small, ape-sized brain, the African hominin <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> — the genus of 'Lucy' — lived from about 4.2 to 2 million years ago.","answer":"Australopithecus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4.2 – 2 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1925, by Raymond Dart</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Australopithecus</b>, whose name means 'southern ape', was a genus of early hominins that lived across eastern and southern Africa from roughly 4.2 to 2 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Despite the name they were not apes in the ordinary sense, but members of our own evolutionary line, standing near its base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their defining feature was a striking blend of traits: they stood on two legs and regularly walked upright, yet kept small braincases of less than 500 cubic centimetres, about a third the size of ours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their arms stayed long and strong and their fingers curved, still adapted for climbing trees, so a single body carried a human-like lower half and an ape-like upper half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> This is why the genus matters so much: it shows that upright walking evolved long before large brains, overturning the old assumption that the brain came first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The anatomist Raymond Dart described and named the first of them in 1925 from the Taung child, a small skull found in South Africa the year before, whose braincase held only about 400 cubic centimetres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its most famous member is Lucy, a partial skeleton of Australopithecus afarensis some 3.2 million years old, unearthed at Hadar in Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Older still are the Laetoli footprints in Tanzania, about 3.66 million years old, a trail pressed into volcanic ash that catches these hominins walking plainly upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Small, with males averaging about 151 centimetres (4 ft 11 in) and females 105 (3 ft 5 in), and living mainly on leaves, fruit, seeds, roots, nuts and insects, they may be ancestral to Paranthropus, to later australopiths and to our own genus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether any of them made stone tools is unresolved: flakes and cores from Lomekwi in Kenya are dated to 3.3 million years, older than any known Homo, but whether hominins shaped them at all has been contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Australopithecus","questions":["Named by Raymond Dart in 1925 for the Taung Child, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the genus whose long arms and curved fingers still suited climbing even as it walked upright.","Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> walked upright yet kept a brain a third the size of ours, it shows two-legged walking evolved long before large brains or stone tools."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Australopithecus anamensis</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-anamensis. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Australopithecus africanus</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-africanus. [Open access]","Dean Falk et al., “Metopic Suture of Taung (<i>Australopithecus africanus</i>) and Its Implications for Hominin Brain Evolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 22 (2012): 8467–70, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3365196/. [Open access]","Fidelis T. Masao et al., “New Footprints from Laetoli (Tanzania) Provide Evidence for Marked Body Size Variation in Early Hominins,” <i>eLife</i> 5 (2016): e19568, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5156529/. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,” <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá, “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools and Butchery Traces? More Evidence Needed,” <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2016 (2016): 46–53, esp. 49 and 51, https://paleoanthro.org/media/journal/content/PA20160046.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Australopithecus_afarensisIMG_2930.JPG/1920px-Australopithecus_afarensisIMG_2930.JPG","title":"Australopithecus","desc":"A cast of the Australopithecus afarensis skull. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australopithecus_afarensisIMG_2930.JPG","alt":"Australopithecus afarensis skull"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-014","num":"14","category":"World · History","question":"In the badlands west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, the site of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded stone flakes struck about 3.3 million years ago, the oldest tools yet found.","answer":"Lomekwi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tools</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3.3 Mya</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lomekwi</b> is an archaeological site in the eroded badlands west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, where excavators uncovered the oldest stone tools yet known, struck about 3.3 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The spot catalogued as Lomekwi 3 yielded roughly 20 artefacts still sealed in the ground and about 130 more lying on the surface: cores, the sharp flakes struck from them, and heavy anvils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These are not the neat, hand-sized pebble tools of later ages — the largest weighs some 15 kilograms (33 pounds), too heavy to swing, and was probably rested on the ground to hammer against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deposit was dated by its position between two layers of volcanic ash and by reversals of the Earth's magnetic field recorded in the sediment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That age pushed the origins of stone technology back roughly 700,000 years beyond the previous record from Gona in Ethiopia, and the excavators named the new industry the Lomekwian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The discovery was an accident: in July 2011 a team led by Sonia Harmand and Jason Lewis took a wrong turn while hunting for a fossil site and stopped on an unexplored hillside strewn with worked stone. One flake could be fitted back onto the core it was struck from, showing that a hominin had knapped stone on that very spot rather than the pieces washing in or breaking by chance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The workmanship looks experimental, battering a block against an anvil resting on the ground rather than using the controlled free-hand blow that defines the later Oldowan toolkit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since the oldest fossils of our own genus, Homo, are about half a million years younger, the makers were probably Australopithecus afarensis or Kenyanthropus platyops, whose flat-faced skull came from these same beds in 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The claim is not settled: several archaeologists argue that the layers are dated less securely than reported and that natural forces can fracture stone in similar ways, so a second site of such age would settle it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lomekwi","questions":["The knapping at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks experimental, a block battered against an anvil resting on the ground rather than struck by the controlled free-hand blow of the later Oldowan.","Too old to be the work of our own genus, the giant cores of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were probably struck by Australopithecus afarensis or the flat-faced Kenyanthropus platyops."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","kenya"],"sources":["Sonia Harmand et al., \"3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,\" <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá, \"3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools and Butchery Traces? More Evidence Needed,\" <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2016 (2016): 46–53, esp. 49 and 51, https://paleoanthro.org/media/journal/content/PA20160046.pdf. [Open access]","Thomas W. Plummer, Sonia Harmand, Emma M. Finestone, and Evan P. Wilson, \"The First Million Years of Technology: The Lomekwian and the Early Oldowan,\" <i>Annual Review of Anthropology</i> 54 (2025): 359–75, under \"Context and Characteristics of the Lomekwian Industry,\" https://par.nsf.gov/biblio/10631785. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Kenyanthropus platyops</i>,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/kenyanthropus-platyops. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Kenyanthropus-Lomekwi.jpg","title":"Lomekwi","desc":"Lomekwi (Kenya), place of discovery of Kenyanthropus platyops. Chartep, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kenyanthropus-Lomekwi.jpg","alt":"Kenyanthropus-Lomekwi"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Lomekwi","at":[35.7508,3.8742]}},
{"id":"wh-015","num":"15","category":"World · History","question":"Shaping stone by striking thin slices off it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> works only on glassy rocks such as flint and obsidian, which break in smooth curved surfaces so the crack follows the blow.","answer":"Knapping","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – present</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Knapping</b> is the craft of shaping stone by striking it so that thin slices, called flakes, break away from the core. The process is purely subtractive: material can only be removed, never put back, so one misjudged blow can ruin the whole piece. It works only on rocks such as flint, chert and obsidian, which are glassy or built from crystals far too small to see. Struck properly, these materials break in smooth, curved, shell-like surfaces, a pattern known as conchoidal fracture, in which the stone splits along the line of force rather than along any internal grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That predictability is what makes the craft possible: because the crack follows the blow, a knapper can aim, and can judge roughly where the next flake will come away, whereas coarse rock such as granite simply crumbles. <br><br> The hammer decides the result: a hard hammerstone drives off large, thick flakes and roughs out the shape, while a softer club of antler or bone delivers the thin, flat flakes that finish the edge of a handaxe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the most delicate work the hammer is set aside altogether: the knapper presses a pointed bone compressor against the edge until small flakes pop off, which is how points were given their saw-like teeth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A struck flake often carries a smooth swelling where the blow landed, and even the waste chips, known as debitage, usually betray a human hand rather than natural breakage. Grinding a stone edge smooth against another stone is a separate craft, far older than farming — edge-ground hatchets were being made in northern Australia 65,000 years ago — and it never replaced knapping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Modern experiments show that the skill takes years to learn, and it has never quite died out: gunflint makers were still striking flints for muskets in the 1800s, and archaeologists knap today to read the workshops of the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Knapping","questions":["In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a hard hammerstone drives off thick flakes to rough out the shape, while a softer antler billet delivers the thin flakes that finish an edge.","A struck flake bears a smooth swelling where the blow landed, and even the waste chips of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, called debitage, betray a human hand."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Li Li et al., “A Synthesis of the Dibble et al. Controlled Experiments into the Mechanics of Lithic Production,” <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 30, no. 4 (2023): 1284–1325, esp. 1290 and 1301–2, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-022-09586-2. [Open access]","Antoine Muller, Ceri Shipton, and Chris Clarkson, “Stone Toolmaking Difficulty and the Evolution of Hominin Technological Skills,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 5883, under “Introduction,” https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-09914-2. [Open access]","Sydney B. J. Skertchly, <i>On the Manufacture of Gun-Flints, the Methods of Excavating for Flint, the Age of Palæolithic Man, and the Connexion between Neolithic Art and the Gun-Flint Trade</i>, Memoirs of the Geological Survey of England and Wales (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1879), 5, https://archive.org/details/onmanufactureofg00skerrich. [Open access]","Veerle Rots et al., \"Pressure Flaking to Serrate Bifacial Points for the Hunt during the MIS5 at Sibudu Cave (South Africa),\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 12, no. 4 (2017): e0175151, under \"Evidence for Pressure Flaking,\" https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0175151. [Open access]","Elspeth H. Hayes et al., \"65,000-Years of Continuous Grinding Stone Use at Madjedbebe, Northern Australia,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 11747, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-15174-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Soft_Hammer.jpg/1920px-Soft_Hammer.jpg","title":"Knapping","desc":"An example of soft hammer percussion used in flintknapping. en:ZenTrowel. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soft_Hammer.jpg","alt":"Soft Hammer"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-016","num":"16","category":"World · History","question":"Named after a gorge in northern Tanzania, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest widespread stone tool industry, a kit of battered cores and razor-sharp flakes.","answer":"Oldowan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 – 1.7 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936, after Olduvai Gorge</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Oldowan</b> is the earliest widespread stone toolmaking tradition, a plain but effective technology that spread across Africa, later reached Eurasia, and lasted for roughly a million years. Its method was direct: a knapper held a rounded cobble in one hand and struck it with a hammerstone at a steep angle, driving off flakes with naturally razor-sharp edges. The resulting kit is modest — battered cores often called choppers, the flakes struck from them, the hammerstones themselves, and unworked stones carried in from elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Archaeologists still argue about which of these was the point: many now think the cutting flakes were the goal all along and the shaped cores merely the leftovers. In the shorthand scheme that Grahame Clark devised for the world's stone technologies, this whole tradition counts as Mode 1, the simplest of his five. <br><br> The industry takes its name from Olduvai Gorge in northern Tanzania, where Louis Leakey coined the term in 1936 and Mary Leakey later worked out the classification of tool types. The oldest securely dated examples come from Gona and Ledi-Geraru in Ethiopia at about 2.6 million years ago, though finds at Nyayanga in Kenya may push the tradition back towards 2.9 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Butchery is the clearest use: bones from these sites carry slicing cut marks and the crushing scars of a hammerstone swung to crack a shaft open for its marrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who did the striking is unsettled — Homo habilis was long assumed to be the maker, but teeth of the heavy-jawed Paranthropus lay among the tools at Nyayanga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By about 1.7 million years ago the teardrop hand axes of the Acheulean began to appear, and the simpler kit gradually gave way, having already travelled out of Africa to Dmanisi in Georgia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Oldowan","questions":["Counting as Mode 1 in Grahame Clark's five-part scheme, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is best shown at work by butchery — cut marks on bones and shafts cracked open for their marrow.","Tools from Gona in Ethiopia, dated to about 2.6 million years ago, are the oldest securely dated of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which may have been knapped by Homo habilis or heavy-jawed Paranthropus."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Thomas W. Plummer, Sonia Harmand, Emma M. Finestone, and Evan P. Wilson, “The First Million Years of Technology: The Lomekwian and the Early Oldowan,” <i>Annual Review of Anthropology</i> 54 (2025): 359–75, under “Context and Characteristics of the Early Oldowan Industry” and “Potential Manufacturers of Stone Tools 3.3–2.3 Ma,” https://par.nsf.gov/biblio/10631785. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, under “The Hard Evidence for the Earliest Acheulean: The Current View,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Early Stone Age Tools,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/behavior/stone-tools/early-stone-age-tools. [Open access]","Fernando Diez-Martín et al., \"The Origin of The Acheulean: The 1.7 Million-Year-Old Site of FLK West, Olduvai Gorge (Tanzania),\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 5 (2015): 17839, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep17839. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Canto_tallado_2-Guelmim-Es_Semara.jpg","title":"Oldowan","desc":"Canto tallado de tradición Olduvayense procedente de la región del Sáhara atlántico Guelmim-Es Semara (Museo Arqueológico Nacional de Madrid). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canto_tallado_2-Guelmim-Es_Semara.jpg","alt":"Canto tallado 2-Guelmim-Es Semara"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-017","num":"17","category":"World · History","question":"Cutting across the Serengeti Plain of Tanzania, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a ravine whose layered walls lay open 2 million years of deposits.","answer":"Olduvai Gorge","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.03 Mya – 15,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1931, by the Leakeys</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Olduvai Gorge</b> is a steep-sided ravine cutting through the Eastern Serengeti Plains of northern Tanzania, inside the Ngorongoro Conservation Area, and now officially written Oldupai after the Maasai word behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The main gorge runs about 37 kilometres (23 miles) east across the plain before a side gorge joins it from the south, then another nine to the Balbal depression, and it is roughly 100 metres (330 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name comes from Duvai, the Maasai word for the sisal plant that grows there, which a European traveller first wrote down in 1892.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Early Pleistocene the basin had no outlet and held a shallow salt lake, fed by streams off a line of volcanoes to the west whose ash falls are now dated one by one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those dated ashes are what make the place so valuable, since the fossils and tools between them can be fixed in time; the oldest tools reach 2.03 million years, and most finds come from the numbered Beds I to IV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Wilhelm Kattwinkel and his wife Martha came upon the gorge in 1911 and carried home to Munich the bones of a proboscidean, a three-toed horse, a hippopotamus and a sivathere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later generations remembered him as a butterfly collector who had stumbled on the place by accident, though his journey was a scientific expedition prompted by his medical interest in sleeping sickness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hans Reck dug there from October to December 1913 and came back with Louis Leakey's Third East African Archaeological Expedition of 1931 to 1932, after which the Leakeys worked the gorge for more than thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 17 July 1959 Mary Leakey uncovered the massive-jawed cranium the couple announced as Zinjanthropus, nicknamed Nutcracker Man and now called Paranthropus boisei, about 1.8 million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between 1960 and 1963 the same team found the remains named Homo habilis in 1964, its type specimen the jaw and hand bones called OH 7, and the gorge has yielded Homo erectus as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olduvai Gorge","questions":["Beds I to IV stack up the walls of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, interleaved with volcanic ash whose falls can be dated one by one, which is why its fossils sit firmly in time.","When Mary Leakey uncovered the massive-jawed skull nicknamed Nutcracker Man on 17 July 1959, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded a fossil of about 1.8 million years and made the Leakeys famous."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","tanzania"],"sources":["A. W. Gentry, Anthea Gentry and H. Mayr, “Rediscovery of Fossil Antelope Holotypes (Mammalia, Bovidae) Collected from Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania, in 1913,” <i>Mitteilungen der Bayerischen Staatssammlung für Paläontologie und historische Geologie</i> 35 (1995): 125–35, at 125–26, https://www.zobodat.at/pdf/Mitt-Bayer-Staatsslg-Pal-hist-Geol_35_0125-0135.pdf. [Open access]","Ngorongoro Conservation Area Authority, “Olduvai Gorge,” accessed 31 July 2026, https://www.ncaa.go.tz/olduvai-gorge/. [Open access]","Julio Mercader et al., “Earliest Olduvai Hominins Exploited Unstable Environments ~2 Million Years Ago,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 12 (2021): 3, under “Stratigraphy and chronology” and “Early Oldowan ecology at ~2 Ma,” https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-20176-2. [Open access]","Julien Favreau et al., “Petrographic Characterization of Raw Material Sources at Oldupai Gorge, Tanzania,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 8 (2020): 158, abstract and “Geological Setting,” https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2020.00158. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “OH 5,” accessed 31 July 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/oh-5. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo habilis</i>,” accessed 31 July 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-habilis. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Olduvai_ast.jpg/1920px-Olduvai_ast.jpg","title":"Olduvai Gorge","desc":"Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania from space image description here Image Courtesy: NASA/GSFC/METI/ERSDAC/JAROS, and U.S./Japan ASTER Science Team. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olduvai_ast.jpg","alt":"Olduvai ast"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Olduvai Gorge","at":[35.3511,-2.9936]}},
{"id":"wh-018","num":"18","category":"World · History","question":"Small-bodied, long-armed and with a brain about half the size of ours, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest widely accepted member of our own genus.","answer":"Homo habilis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.4 – 1.4 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1964, by Leakey, Tobias and Napier</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo habilis</b> is an extinct early human of eastern and southern Africa, living between about 2.4 and 1.4 million years ago, and is usually counted as the earliest widely accepted member of our own genus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was small: the known individuals stand between roughly 100 and 135 centimetres (3 ft 3 in and 4 ft 5 in) and weighed around 32 kilograms (71 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its braincase was markedly larger than an australopith's, the average for early Homo running just short of 40 per cent above theirs, and its type specimen's volume is now put at 729 to 824 millilitres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The face was flatter and less projecting than in earlier hominins and the teeth were smaller, but below the neck the body stayed archaic, which is what makes the species so hard to place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Louis Leakey, Phillip Tobias and John Napier announced it in 1964, choosing a Latin word for 'handy' or 'skilful' because Oldowan stone tools lay in the same deposits at Olduvai Gorge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The type specimen, catalogued as OH 7 and nicknamed Jonny's Child, was found by Jonathan Leakey between 1960 and 1963: a lower jaw, pieces of the braincase and, crucially, hand bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Admitting it to Homo meant moving the goalposts, because Arthur Keith's cerebral Rubicon of 750 cubic centimetres was dropped to 600 to let the new species through, with manual dexterity and upright walking weighing more than brain size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That decision has been argued over ever since, and Bernard Wood and Mark Collard have held the species too unlike Homo in its whole way of life to stay in the genus at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Others hold that the fossils lumped under the name are really more than one species, and the record between 2 and 1.7 million years ago does show clear diversity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor did it neatly give rise to Homo erectus, which appears by 1.89 million years ago and lies alongside it in the upper beds at Olduvai, while the oldest Oldowan tools, at more than 2.58 million years, are older than any fossil of the genus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo habilis","questions":["Announced in 1964 and given a Latin name for 'handy', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> won its place in our genus only after the brain-size threshold was lowered from 750 to 600 cubic centimetres.","A juvenile jaw and hand bones nicknamed Jonny's Child are the type specimen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, cast as the likely maker of the Oldowan flakes in the same Olduvai deposits."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo habilis</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-habilis. [Open access]","William H. Kimbel and Brian Villmoare, “From <i>Australopithecus</i> to <i>Homo</i>: The Transition That Wasn't,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150248, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920303/. [Open access]","Camilo J. Cela-Conde and Francisco J. Ayala, “Genera of the Human Lineage,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 100, no. 13 (2003): 7684–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC164648/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo erectus</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Homo_habilis_IMG_2911.JPG/1920px-Homo_habilis_IMG_2911.JPG","title":"Homo habilis","desc":"On display at Lausanne Natural History Museum in Rumin palace Homo habilis. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_habilis_IMG_2911.JPG","alt":"On display at Lausanne Natural History Museum in Rumin palace Homo habilis"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-019","num":"19","category":"World · History","question":"Tall, long-legged and built much like us below the neck, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first human species to walk out of Africa, lasting nearly 2 million years.","answer":"Homo erectus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2 Mya – 110,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1894, by Eugène Dubois</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo erectus</b> is an extinct human species that appeared in Africa around 2 million years ago and survived until roughly 110,000 years ago, a span no other member of our genus has come close to matching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was the first of our relatives with long legs and short arms, built for walking and running across open country, though its trunk stayed deeper and wider than ours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Brain size climbed over that long career, from something like 600 cubic centimetres in the earliest African skulls to about 1,000 in later Asian ones, closing much of the gap to our own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The face stayed heavy, with a thick brow ridge above the eyes, a low forehead and no real chin. It was also the first human species to leave Africa, reaching the Caucasus by about 1.8 million years ago and eventually spreading as far as Java and northern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It was found long before anyone thought to look in Africa, when the Dutch army surgeon Eugène Dubois sailed east determined to dig up the missing link and struck a skullcap at Trinil in Java in 1891.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He named it Pithecanthropus erectus, the upright ape-man, in 1894, and only in the 1950s was the species folded into our own genus as Homo erectus. The most complete skeleton is the Turkana Boy from Nariokotome in Kenya, a boy of about 11 or 12 who died some 1.5 million years ago, tall and long-limbed but broad through the chest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its handiwork includes the teardrop hand axes of the Acheulean, and a handful of sites such as Wonderwerk Cave and Gesher Benot Ya'aqov hint at the occasional use of fire. The famous Peking Man fossils from Zhoukoudian near Beijing belong to the same species, but the originals vanished in 1941 while being evacuated ahead of the Japanese advance, leaving only plaster casts behind.","citation":"","answerText":"Homo erectus","questions":["First dug up by Eugène Dubois at Trinil in Java in 1891, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was called Pithecanthropus, the upright ape-man, before being folded into our own genus in the 1950s.","Growing a brain from about 600 to 1,000 cubic centimetres across nearly two million years, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried Acheulean hand axes and perhaps fire as far as China."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Susan C. Antón et al., “Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, §§1–2 and table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Yan Rizal et al., “Last Appearance of <i>Homo erectus</i> at Ngandong, Java, 117,000–108,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 577, no. 7790 (2020): 381–85, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1863-2. [Open access]","Markus Bastir et al., “Rib Cage Anatomy in <i>Homo erectus</i> Suggests a Recent Evolutionary Origin of Modern Human Body Shape,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 4, no. 9 (2020): 1178–87, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-020-1240-4. [Open access]","Sabrina C. Curran et al., “Hominin Presence in Eurasia by at Least 1.95 Million Years Ago,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 836, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-56154-9. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Homo_erectus_skull_cross-section.png","title":"Homo erectus","desc":"Horizontal cross-section through the ophryon–inion plane of Sinanthropus Skulls III (A) and XII (B), and Pithecanthropus Skull II (C). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_erectus_skull_cross-section.png","alt":"Homo erectus skull cross-section"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-020","num":"20","category":"World · History","question":"Proposed in 1975 for a lower jaw from Koobi Fora, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name under which some researchers keep Africa's earliest Homo erectus fossils apart from the Asian ones.","answer":"Homo ergaster","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fossils</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.9 – 1.5 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975, by Groves and Mazák</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo ergaster</b> is the name given to a group of early human fossils from eastern and southern Africa, dating from roughly 1.9 to 1.5 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Colin Groves and Vratislav Mazák proposed it in 1975, and the specimen the name was built on is a lower jaw catalogued as KNM-ER 992, from Koobi Fora on the eastern shore of Lake Turkana in Kenya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best skull attributed to the group, KNM-ER 3733, came out of the same deposits in the same year, found by Bernard Ngeneo and about 1.8 million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is a mature female and one of the best preserved skulls of its time, and it carries the body plan that Homo erectus shows wherever it is found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the name describes a real species is the whole question, because the fossils are otherwise much like the Homo erectus long known from Java and China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Some scientists therefore keep the African fossils apart as Homo ergaster and the Asian ones as Homo erectus in the strict sense, while others lump them together as a single widespread species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two raise no difficulty about sitting in the same genus, and what is argued over is how finely a species should be cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brain size will not settle it, since the African and Georgian skulls run from 546 to 1,067 cubic centimetres, the Chinese from 855 to 1,225 and the Indonesian from 813 to 1,251, which is one broad range rather than two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor will age settle it, because later work on the rock sequences narrowed and in some cases wiped out the difference in time between the African and the Asian fossils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Turkana Boy, the near-complete skeleton of a youth from Nariokotome, is the best-known casualty of the disagreement, catalogued as Homo erectus by some museums and as Homo ergaster elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo ergaster","questions":["Neither brain size nor age can settle it, since the African and Asian skulls fall in one broad range, which is why sceptics call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> merely African Homo erectus.","The near-complete Turkana Boy is the best-known casualty of the dispute over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, labelled with this name in some museums and Homo erectus in others."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Camilo J. Cela-Conde and Francisco J. Ayala, “Genera of the Human Lineage,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 100, no. 13 (2003): 7684–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC164648/. [Open access]","Susan C. Antón et al., “Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, §2 and table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo erectus</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “KNM-ER 3733,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/knm-er-3733. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “KNM-WT 15000,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/knm-wt-15000. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/70/Homo_ergaster.jpg","title":"Homo ergaster","desc":"Skull of Homo ergaster (I took this photo in october 2005) Skull KNM-ER 3733 discovered by Bernard Ngeneo in 1975 (Kenya). No machine-readable author provided. Luna04~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright claims).,. CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_ergaster.jpg","alt":"Skull of Homo ergaster (I took this photo in october 2005) Skull KNM-ER 3733 discovered by Bernard Ngeneo in 1975 (Kenya)"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-021","num":"21","category":"World · History","question":"Found beside Lake Turkana in northern Kenya in 1984, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the most complete early human skeleton known, a boy who died about 1.6 million years ago.","answer":"Turkana Boy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.6 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1984, by Kamoya Kimeu</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Turkana Boy</b> is the skeleton of a boy who died beside a lake in northern Kenya about 1.6 million years ago, and it is the most complete early human ever recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kamoya Kimeu, a fossil hunter of long experience working with Richard Leakey's team, spotted a scrap of skull at Nariokotome, west of Lake Turkana, in 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Patient sieving of the slope above it produced most of the rest over several seasons, until about 40 per cent of the skeleton had been recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He stood 1.6 metres (5 ft 3 in) and weighed 48 kilograms (106 pounds) when he died, and his braincase held 880 cubic centimetres, which would have reached about 909 had he grown up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The skeleton is catalogued as KNM-WT 15000, and museums label it either Homo erectus or Homo ergaster depending on where they stand in that dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He was about eight or nine years old, and he had been growing at a rate closer to a living great ape's than to ours, so his kind reached adulthood faster than we do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His adult height has been revised in the same spirit: an early estimate of 185 centimetres (6 ft 1 in), which made him a strapping figure, is now put nearer 163.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For years he was also thought to be diseased, since an unusually narrow spinal canal was read as a rare inherited disorder of the skeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A reassessment grouped his vertebrae with those of ordinary modern people and found the anomalies confined to a few bones of the chest and lower back, consistent with an injury rather than a developmental disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His rib cage turns out not to be modern either: reconstructed in three dimensions it is short, wide and deep, unlike the much shallower chest we carry, so fully modern body shape came later than his skeleton was long taken to show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Turkana Boy","questions":["Catalogued as KNM-WT 15000 and standing about 1.6 metres (5 ft 3 in) tall, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was spotted by the fossil hunter Kamoya Kimeu at Nariokotome while working with Richard Leakey's team.","A boy of about eight or nine at death who had been growing at a great ape's pace, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would have reached adulthood faster than we do."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","kenya"],"sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “KNM-WT 15000,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/knm-wt-15000. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo erectus</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]","Susan C. Antón et al., “Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, §2, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Markus Bastir et al., “Rib Cage Anatomy in <i>Homo erectus</i> Suggests a Recent Evolutionary Origin of Modern Human Body Shape,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 4, no. 9 (2020): 1178–87, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-020-1240-4. [Open access]","Ronda R. Graves et al., “Just How Strapping Was KNM-WT 15000?,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 59, no. 5 (2010): 542–54, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2010.06.007. [Paywalled]","Regula Schiess et al., “Revisiting Scoliosis in the KNM-WT 15000 <i>Homo erectus</i> Skeleton,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 67 (2014): 48–59, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2013.12.009. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Chlapec_od_jezera_Turkana_03.jpg","title":"Turkana Boy","desc":"Lebka a spodní čelist turkanského chlapce. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chlapec_od_jezera_Turkana_03.jpg","alt":"Chlapec od jezera Turkana 03"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-023","num":"23","category":"World · History","question":"Beneath a medieval town in southern Georgia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has produced five skulls 1.8 million years old, the oldest human remains known outside Africa.","answer":"Dmanisi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hominins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.8 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">tools 1983, first jaw 1991</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Dmanisi</b> is an archaeological site in southern Georgia where the ruins of a medieval town sit directly over the oldest human fossils known outside Africa, though an earlier presence in Eurasia is now claimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Excavators digging beneath the medieval layers found stone tools in 1983 and the first hominin jawbone in 1991, and the deposits are dated to about 1.8 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The site has now yielded five skulls along with jaws and limb bones, an unusually rich haul for hominins of that age, since most are known from scraps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were small-bodied people, and the smallest of their braincases holds only about 546 cubic centimetres, closer to Homo habilis than to the Homo erectus they are usually assigned to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stone tools found with them are simple flakes and cores of Oldowan type, with no sign of the hand axe. <br><br> One of the skulls belonged to a man who had lost all but one of his teeth, the empty sockets long since closed over, meaning he lived for years without being able to chew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some read this as the earliest hint that the sick were looked after; others answer that soft foods and bone marrow would have kept him alive without anyone's help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The most complete of the five, published in 2013, set off a larger argument, because it pairs a jutting face with a braincase of only 546 cubic centimetres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its describers held that the variation among the five Dmanisi skulls is no wider than within a single population, and concluded that Homo habilis, Homo rudolfensis and Homo erectus may all be one evolving lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That has never been generally accepted, and a study of the tooth crowns published in 2025 came to the opposite view, arguing that two kinds of human lived at Dmanisi side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dmanisi","questions":["Scattered with the bones are thousands of simple Oldowan flakes and cores but no hand axe, and one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> skull belonged to a man who had lost all but one tooth.","A skull published in 2013 pairs a jutting face with a braincase of just 546 cubic centimetres, sparking the claim that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows several named humans as one species."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","georgia"],"sources":["Victor Nery, Walter Neves, Leticia Valota, and Mark Hubbe, “Testing the Taxonomy of Dmanisi Hominin Fossils through Dental Crown Area,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 11 (2025): e0336484, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12674552/. [Open access]","David Lordkipanidze, Marcia S. Ponce de León, Ann Margvelashvili, et al., “A Complete Skull from Dmanisi, Georgia, and the Evolutionary Biology of Early <i>Homo</i>,” <i>Science</i> 342, no. 6156 (2013): 326–31, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1238484. [Paywalled]","David Lordkipanidze, Abesalom Vekua, Reid Ferring, et al., “The Earliest Toothless Hominin Skull,” <i>Nature</i> 434, no. 7034 (2005): 717–18, https://doi.org/10.1038/434717b. [Paywalled]","Christoph P. E. Zollikofer, Vincent Beyrand, David Lordkipanidze, Paul Tafforeau, and Marcia S. Ponce de León, “Dental Evidence for Extended Growth in Early <i>Homo</i> from Dmanisi,” <i>Nature</i> 635 (2024): 906–11, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11602720/. [Open access]","Sabrina C. Curran, Virgil Drăgușin, Briana Pobiner, et al., “Hominin Presence in Eurasia by at Least 1.95 Million Years Ago,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 836, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11747263/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Homo_Georgicus_IMG_2921.JPG/1920px-Homo_Georgicus_IMG_2921.JPG","title":"Dmanisi","desc":"Homo Georgicus. Rama. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_Georgicus_IMG_2921.JPG","alt":"Homo Georgicus"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Dmanisi","at":[44.2036,41.3317]}},
{"id":"wh-024","num":"24","category":"World · History","question":"Prised from a riverbank in eastern Java in 1891 and 1892, the skullcap, molar and thigh bone known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the first fossils of Homo erectus ever found.","answer":"Java Man","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">830,000 – 773,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">and 560,000 – 380,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1891–1892, by Eugène Dubois</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1894</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Java Man</b> is the name given to a skullcap, a molar and a thigh bone dug out of the bank of the Solo River at Trinil, in eastern Java, in 1891 and 1892.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were the first remains of Homo erectus ever found, and the Dutch physician who dug them up, Eugène Dubois, had crossed the world looking for exactly that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Convinced by Darwin that the fossil link between apes and humans lay somewhere in Asia, he enlisted as a medical officer in the colonial army of the Dutch East Indies, landing on Sumatra in December 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A molar came out of the riverbank in September 1891, the skullcap a month later from the same level barely a metre (3 feet) away, and the left thigh bone in August 1892, some 15 metres (49 feet) upstream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern measurement puts the braincase at about 940 cubic centimetres, far beyond any ape's and still well short of ours, though Dubois himself reckoned it at more than a thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He named the creature Pithecanthropus erectus in 1894, 'the upright ape-man', taking the genus from a word Ernst Haeckel had coined in 1868 for a creature nobody had yet seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reception was cold, and the trouble was the thigh bone, essentially modern where the skullcap is low and heavily browed, so scholars argued whether the braincase belonged to the human family at all, or to an out-sized gibbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fieldwork published in 2023 reconstructed the old excavation and found younger river channels cutting through the spot where the thigh bone had lain, which would account for its modern build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The several genera then in use were folded into Homo erectus by Ernst Mayr, formalising what most researchers had already come to accept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Dutch state commission found in 2025 that Dubois had dug with up to 50 forced labourers at a place he called Java's hell, and that December the three bones were handed over in Jakarta, first of more than 28,000 specimens returning to Indonesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Java Man","questions":["Hunted down by a Dutch army doctor who had joined the colonial forces purely to reach the Indies, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded a braincase of about 940 cubic centimetres, well beyond any ape's.","Because a thigh bone of almost modern build lay beside a low, heavy-browed skullcap, scholars asked whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was one creature at all, or an out-sized gibbon."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","indonesia"],"sources":["Eugène Dubois, <i>Pithecanthropus erectus: eine menschenaehnliche Uebergangsform aus Java</i> (1894; repr. New York: G. E. Stechert, 1915), 1–2, 11, https://archive.org/details/Pithecanthropus00Dubo. [Open access]","Frank Huffman et al., “Geology and Discovery Record of the Trinil <i>Pithecanthropus erectus</i> Site, Java,” <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2022, no. 2, abstract and n. 4, https://doi.org/10.48738/2022.iss2.83. [Open access]","Eduard Pop et al., “Reconstructing the Provenance of the Hominin Fossils from Trinil (Java, Indonesia) through an Integrated Analysis of the Historical and Recent Excavations,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 176 (2023): 103312, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103312. [Open access]","Xiujie Wu, Lynne A. Schepartz and Wu Liu, “A New <i>Homo erectus</i> (Zhoukoudian V) Brain Endocast from China,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 277, no. 1679 (2010): 337–44, table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2842660/. [Open access]","Susan C. Antón et al., “Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, §2, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Commissie Koloniale Collecties, <i>Advies</i> ID-2025-1 [advice to the Dutch State Secretary for Education, Culture and Science on Indonesia's request for the return of the Dubois collection], 5 September 2025, 2, 5–6, 11, 20–21, https://commissie.kolonialecollecties.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2025/09/26/indonesie/id-2025-1-advies-collectie-dubois.pdf. [Open access]","Ministerie van Onderwijs, Cultuur en Wetenschap, “Topstukken Dubois-collectie overhandigd in Indonesië” [Key objects of the Dubois collection handed over in Indonesia], Rijksoverheid, 17 December 2025, https://www.rijksoverheid.nl/actueel/nieuws/2025/12/17/topstukken-dubois-collectie-overhandigd-in-indonesie. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/The_most_ancient_skeletal_remains_of_man_Plate_1.png/1920px-The_most_ancient_skeletal_remains_of_man_Plate_1.png","title":"Java Man","desc":"The Locality of the Pithecanthropus find, on the Bengavian River, Near Trinal, Java (After MME. SELENKA AND M. BLANKENHORN). The two white squares show where the femur (on left) and the skullcap (on right and more behind) were discovered. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_most_ancient_skeletal_remains_of_man_Plate_1.png","alt":"The most ancient skeletal remains of man Plate 1"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-025","num":"25","category":"World · History","question":"Named in 1927 from a single tooth, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the early human of a cave near Beijing whose entire original collection vanished in the winter of 1941.","answer":"Peking Man","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750,000 – 230,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927, by Davidson Black</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1941</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Peking Man</b> is the name for the Homo erectus remains found at Zhoukoudian, a limestone hill about 50 kilometres (31 miles) southwest of Beijing, in deposits whose ages run from more than 750,000 to roughly 230,000 years ago by different dating methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1927 the Canadian anatomist Davidson Black named a whole new genus and species, Sinanthropus pekinensis, on the strength of a single lower molar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a reckless thing to do, and it was borne out two years later when Pei Wenzhong lifted the first skullcap out of the deposit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the late 1930s the hill had given up parts of more than 40 individuals, along with thousands of stone tools and layers of burnt material, making it the richest early human site anyone had dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The braincases run from about 915 to 1,225 cubic centimetres, far above any ape's and reaching into the lower part of our own range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Then it all disappeared, because as war closed in the most important of the fossils were packed into two large wooden footlockers late in 1941 and handed to the US military for shipment to New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Witnesses put the crates on a Marine vehicle, the cargo ship meant to carry them ran aground and never reached the port, and the gates of the Peking Union Medical College remain the last reliable sighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Every theory since, from sinking at sea to burial near the college, is a guess, and no verifiable trace of the crates has ever been recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What saved the science was Franz Weidenreich, who had the skulls photographed, drawn, measured and cast before the war and the casts shipped to America, so the species is still studied largely from plaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not all of it is plaster, since digs from 1949 to 1959 and again in 1966 recovered six teeth and a jaw of original bone, while the burnt layers, doubted from the 1980s, have been partly restored by recent work on the cave floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Peking Man","questions":["A braincase of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may hold 1,225 cubic centimetres, which reaches into the lower part of the modern range and stands far above any ape's.","Because the skulls had all been cast, drawn and measured before the war, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is still studied largely from plaster, the bones themselves having gone missing in 1941."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","china"],"sources":["Franz Weidenreich, <i>The Skull of</i> Sinanthropus pekinensis<i>: A Comparative Study on a Primitive Hominid Skull</i>, Palaeontologia Sinica, n.s. D, no. 10 (Pehpei, Chungking: Geological Survey of China, 1943), iii, 2–3, 190, https://archive.org/details/TheSkullOfSinanthropusPekinensisAComparativeStudyOnAPrimitive. [Open access]","Song Xing, María Martinón-Torres and José María Bermúdez de Castro, “The Fossil Teeth of the Peking Man,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 2066, introduction, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-20432-y. [Open access]","Xiujie Wu, Lynne A. Schepartz and Wu Liu, “A New <i>Homo erectus</i> (Zhoukoudian V) Brain Endocast from China,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 277, no. 1679 (2010): 337–44, table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2842660/. [Open access]","Chao Huang, Jingshu Li and Xing Gao, “Evidence of Fire Use by <i>Homo erectus pekinensis</i>: An XRD Study of Archaeological Bones from Zhoukoudian Locality 1, China,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 9 (2022): 811319, introduction, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2021.811319. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger, Wu Liu and Xiujie Wu, “Investigation of a Credible Report by a US Marine on the Location of the Missing Peking Man Fossils,” <i>South African Journal of Science</i> 108, nos. 3–4 (2012), commentary, https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0038-23532012000200003. [Open access]","Xiaoya Ma, Guangxu Wang and Min Wang, “Impact of Chinese Palaeontology on Evolutionary Research,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 377, no. 1847 (2022): 20210029, background, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8819365/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Peking_Man_Weidenreich.png/1920px-Peking_Man_Weidenreich.png","title":"Peking Man","desc":"Franz Weidenreich's reconstruction of the Peking Man skull. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peking_Man_Weidenreich.png","alt":"Peking Man Weidenreich"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-026","num":"26","category":"World · History","question":"A limestone hill in the suburbs of Beijing, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds a record of occupation running from Homo erectus to people sewing skins with eyed bone needles.","answer":"Zhoukoudian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Layers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750,000 – 400,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1987</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zhoukoudian</b> is a limestone hill in the southwestern suburbs of Beijing, about 50 kilometres (31 miles) from the centre, known in Chinese as Dragon Bone Hill and riddled with separate fossil localities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The most famous of them, known simply as Locality 1, is a karst deposit rather than an open cavern, and the debris filling it is divided into numbered layers running from the youngest at the top to the oldest at the bottom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That filling took several hundred thousand years to gather, the topmost layers dated to about 400,000 years ago and the deepest reaching back beyond 750,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Out of it came the Peking Man fossils, parts of more than 40 individuals, along with thousands of stone tools and a rich Ice Age fauna of straight-tusked elephants, woolly rhinoceros, wolves and sabre-toothed cats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Weidenreich already knew that hyenas denned there and cracked the bones while they were fresh, and from the 1980s such arguments were pressed hard enough to cast doubt on the burnt layers as the hearths of a home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Higher up the same hill lies the Upper Cave, dug in the 1930s and sitting directly above the southwestern wall of Locality 1, which belongs to a wholly different world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people were modern humans, at least eight of them, and its cultural layers are dated to roughly 38,300 to 33,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They left ornaments of pierced fox, badger, deer, marten and tiger teeth, of carp and bird bone, of sea shell and of limestone, and three badger canines show they were sewn onto clothing rather than strung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cave's one formal bone tool is a needle with an eye at one end, and Pei Wenzhong found three complete skulls there, every one of them coated in ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Upper Cave material was packed up with the Peking Man collection in 1941 and vanished along with it, so its ornaments too are studied from casts and descriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zhoukoudian","questions":["Locality 1 fills a limestone karst with layers reaching back beyond 750,000 years, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also holds an Upper Cave whose modern humans left an eyed bone needle.","Because hyenas denned in the same deposit and cracked the bones fresh, arguments from the 1980s recast <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a carnivore lair as much as a human home."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","china"],"sources":["Francesco d'Errico et al., “Zhoukoudian Upper Cave Personal Ornaments and Ochre: Rediscovery and Reevaluation,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 161 (2021): 103088, §§1–2 and fig. 1, https://shs.hal.science/hal-03445635/document. [Open access]","Song Xing, María Martinón-Torres and José María Bermúdez de Castro, “The Fossil Teeth of the Peking Man,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 2066, introduction, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-20432-y. [Open access]","Chao Huang, Jingshu Li and Xing Gao, “Evidence of Fire Use by <i>Homo erectus pekinensis</i>: An XRD Study of Archaeological Bones from Zhoukoudian Locality 1, China,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 9 (2022): 811319, introduction, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2021.811319. [Open access]","Franz Weidenreich, <i>The Skull of</i> Sinanthropus pekinensis<i>: A Comparative Study on a Primitive Hominid Skull</i>, Palaeontologia Sinica, n.s. D, no. 10 (Pehpei, Chungking: Geological Survey of China, 1943), 190, https://archive.org/details/TheSkullOfSinanthropusPekinensisAComparativeStudyOnAPrimitive. [Open access]","Peter Dodson, “China Shares Fossil Treasures with the World,” <i>The Anatomical Record</i> 308, no. 10 (2025): 2806–12, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12435128/. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger, Wu Liu and Xiujie Wu, “Investigation of a Credible Report by a US Marine on the Location of the Missing Peking Man Fossils,” <i>South African Journal of Science</i> 108, nos. 3–4 (2012), commentary, https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0038-23532012000200003. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Sinanthropus_Skull_V_%282%29.png","title":"Zhoukoudian","desc":"Sinanthropus Skull V. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sinanthropus_Skull_V_(2).png","alt":"Sinanthropus Skull V (2)"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Zhoukoudian","at":[115.9461,39.6797]}},
{"id":"wh-027","num":"27","category":"World · History","question":"A teardrop-shaped stone worked on both faces to a continuous edge defines the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the longest-lived toolmaking tradition known, lasting over a million and a half years.","answer":"Acheulean","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.76 Mya – 125,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1872, by Gabriel de Mortillet</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Acheulean</b> is the stone tool industry built around the hand axe, a slab of stone flaked on both faces into a teardrop or oval with a cutting edge running right round it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cleavers with a broad chisel edge and heavy picks belong to the same kit, along with the flakes struck off in shaping them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Making one demands forethought of a kind the older Oldowan never required, since the knapper works towards a shape held in mind rather than simply striking off a useful edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest examples come from Kokiselei west of Lake Turkana in Kenya and from Konso in Ethiopia, both about 1.76 to 1.74 million years old, which pushed the tradition back some 300,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It then ran on until about 125,000 years ago, ending far earlier in some regions than in others, which makes it by far the longest-lived way of working stone that humans have ever kept up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Gabriel de Mortillet named it in 1872 after Saint-Acheul, a locality in the Somme valley near Amiens where such tools came out of the river gravels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hand axes are found across Africa, the Levant, Europe and the Indian subcontinent, and Homo erectus and later Homo heidelbergensis are credited with most of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hallam Movius noticed that they thin out towards eastern Asia, where simpler chopping tools prevail, and the boundary he drew still carries his name, though hand axes have since turned up in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What nobody agrees on is why they are so symmetrical, because butchery experiments suggest a neat outline cuts no better than a rough one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Suggestions run from the demands of the knapping itself to a proposal that a finely made axe advertised its maker's skill to prospective mates, an idea critics dismiss as untestable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Acheulean","questions":["Named in 1872 by Gabriel de Mortillet after Saint-Acheul in the Somme valley, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is credited mostly to Homo erectus and later Homo heidelbergensis.","Because a neat outline cuts no better than a rough one, why the hand axes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are so symmetrical is unsettled, with mate-display one contested idea."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, under “A Historical Context for the Acheulean” and “The Hard Evidence for the Earliest Acheulean: The Current View,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","Alastair J. M. Key and Stephen J. Lycett, “Form and Function in the Lower Palaeolithic: History, Progress, and Continued Relevance,” <i>Journal of Anthropological Sciences</i> 95 (2017): 67–108, esp. 73–76 and 81–82, https://doi.org/10.4436/jass.95017. [Open access]","Fernando Diez-Martín et al., \"The Origin of The Acheulean: The 1.7 Million-Year-Old Site of FLK West, Olduvai Gorge (Tanzania),\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 5 (2015): 17839, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep17839. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Early Stone Age Tools,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/behavior/stone-tools/early-stone-age-tools. [Open access]","Andy I. R. Herries, \"A Chronological Perspective on the Acheulian and Its Transition to the Middle Stone Age in Southern Africa: The Question of the Fauresmith,\" <i>International Journal of Evolutionary Biology</i> 2011 (2011): 961401, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3139141/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Acheuleanhandaxes.jpg","title":"Acheulean","desc":"Acheuleanhandaxes. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acheuleanhandaxes.jpg","alt":"Acheuleanhandaxes"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-028","num":"28","category":"World · History","question":"Long taken for a thunderbolt fallen from the sky, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a stone worked to an edge all round and gripped without any handle.","answer":"Hand axe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.76 Mya – under 200,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1800, by John Frere</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accepted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1859</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>hand axe</b> is a stone tool flaked on both faces into a teardrop, oval or pear shape, with a cutting edge running round most of its rim. It has no handle and no socket: the wide butt sits in the palm while the working edge is turned against the material, which is where the name comes from. Most are between 12 and 20 centimetres (4.7 and 7.9 inches) long, though British river gravels have yielded giants of more than 30, the largest a point from Furze Platt measuring 32.3 centimetres (12.7 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The maker roughs out the shape with a hammerstone and then finishes the edge with a softer billet of antler, bone or hardwood, which lifts thin flakes without shattering the piece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wear along the edges shows them used to butcher and skin carcasses and to work wood, and digging has been proposed as well, which is why they are often called the pocket knife of the Stone Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For centuries in Europe they were taken for thunderstones, dropped where lightning struck, and were kept in houses as charms against storms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1800 the Society of Antiquaries of London printed John Frere's two-page account of pointed stone tools dug deep from a brick clay pit at Hoxne in Suffolk, beside the bones of giant animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He concluded that they came from a very remote period indeed, even beyond that of the present world, and was then ignored for sixty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jacques Boucher de Perthes gathered similar stones from the gravels at Abbeville and argued the same case, to much the same reception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1859 the geologist Joseph Prestwich, the antiquary John Evans and the geologist Charles Lyell went to see the Somme gravels at Amiens and Abbeville for themselves, all came away convinced, and the antiquity of humankind was settled at last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Hand axe","questions":["Roughed out with a hammerstone and finished with a soft antler billet, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> served to butcher carcasses, dig for roots and work wood — the pocket knife of the Stone Age.","Ignored when John Frere reported it from Hoxne in 1800, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> finally proved humankind's antiquity in 1859, when British visitors came to see the Somme gravels for themselves."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Alastair J. M. Key and Stephen J. Lycett, “Form and Function in the Lower Palaeolithic: History, Progress, and Continued Relevance,” <i>Journal of Anthropological Sciences</i> 95 (2017): 67–108, esp. 69–71 and 77–78, https://doi.org/10.4436/jass.95017. [Open access]","Kristiina Johanson, “The Changing Meaning of ‘Thunderbolts’,” <i>Folklore: Electronic Journal of Folklore</i> 42 (2009): 129–74, esp. 129–31, https://doi.org/10.7592/FEJF2009.42.johanson. [Open access]","James Sackett, \"Boucher de Perthes and the Discovery of Human Antiquity,\" <i>Bulletin of the History of Archaeology</i> 24 (2014): art. 2, https://doi.org/10.5334/bha.242. [Open access]","Li Li et al., \"A Synthesis of the Dibble et al. Controlled Experiments into the Mechanics of Lithic Production,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 30, no. 4 (2023): 1284–1325, esp. 1301–2, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-022-09586-2. [Open access]","Letty Ingrey, Martin Bates, Sarah Duffy and Matt Pope, \"A New Palaeolithic Giant Handaxe from Britain: Initial Results from Excavations at Maritime Academy, Medway, Kent,\" <i>Internet Archaeology</i> 61 (2023), under \"Description of the Maritime Academy 'Giant' Handaxe\" and table 2, https://doi.org/10.11141/ia.61.6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Vuistbijl_in_silex%2C_250_000_tot_38_000_BP%2C_vindplaats-_Meeuwen%2C_In_den_Damp%2C_21.10.1985.jpg/1920px-Vuistbijl_in_silex%2C_250_000_tot_38_000_BP%2C_vindplaats-_Meeuwen%2C_In_den_Damp%2C_21.10.1985.jpg","title":"Hand axe","desc":"Vuistbijl in silex, 250 000 tot 38 000 BP, vindplaats: Meeuwen, In den Damp, 21.10.1985, 5°31'25\" NB - 51°03'37\" OL, collectie Gallo-Romeins Museum Tongeren, IDD 4. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vuistbijl_in_silex,_250_000_tot_38_000_BP,_vindplaats-_Meeuwen,_In_den_Damp,_21.10.1985.jpg","alt":"Vuistbijl in silex, 250 000 tot 38 000 BP, vindplaats- Meeuwen, In den Damp, 21.10.1985"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-029","num":"29","category":"World · History","question":"Warmth in the cold, light after sunset, safety from predators and food made softer and safer all followed from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose hardest step was kindling one at will.","answer":"control of fire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Contested traces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1.5 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wonderwerk Cave</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Habitual use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 400,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>control of fire</b> covers three quite different achievements, and archaeologists are careful to keep them apart: using a blaze that nature lit, keeping one alive and carrying it about, and kindling a new one whenever it is wanted. Each step is harder than the one before, and only the last frees a group from waiting on lightning and bush fires. The rewards were enormous: warmth in cold latitudes, light after sunset, a defence against large predators, and food made softer, safer and quicker to digest. Richard Wrangham argues that cooking did more than feed people, and that the energy it released paid for the smaller gut and larger brain of early Homo from about 1.7 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The trouble is that scorched ground proves nothing by itself, because wildfires, lightning strikes and even smouldering dung leave much the same residue. <br><br> A convincing case needs burning that happened where it now lies, deep inside a cave or gathered into a single patch rather than smeared across a landscape. The oldest widely accepted are the ashed plants some 30 metres (100 feet) inside Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa, about a million years old, and the burnt seed, wood and flint at Gesher Benot Ya'aqov in Israel, about 790,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa argued in 2011 that a handful of early sites is not the same as habit, and that fire becomes routine equipment only around 400,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even those sites showed people using fire rather than making it, and until recently the oldest proof of deliberate ignition was Neanderthal hand axes from France struck with pyrite some 50,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then in December 2025 a team at Barnham in Suffolk reported heated ground, fire-cracked hand axes and two lumps of iron pyrite, a mineral rare in that landscape and so carried in, all about 400,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"control of fire","questions":["Archaeologists split the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into three feats — using a blaze that nature lit, keeping one alive to carry about, and kindling a fresh flame whenever it is wanted.","Richard Wrangham holds that cooking made possible by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> released the energy for the smaller gut and larger brain of early Homo, from about 1.7 million years ago."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Francesco Berna et al., “Microstratigraphic Evidence of in situ Fire in the Acheulean Strata of Wonderwerk Cave, Northern Cape Province, South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 20 (2012): E1215–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3356665/. [Open access]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]","A. C. Sorensen, E. Claud, and M. Soressi, “Neandertal Fire-Making Technology Inferred from Microwear Analysis,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 10065, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-28342-9. [Open access]","Rob Davis et al., “Earliest Evidence of Making Fire,” <i>Nature</i> 649, no. 8097 (2025): 631–37, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09855-6. [Paywalled]","John A. J. Gowlett, \"The Discovery of Fire by Humans: A Long and Convoluted Process,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1696 (2016): 20150164, under \"Fire and the Cooking Hypothesis,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4874402/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-030","num":"30","category":"World · History","question":"Reaching 140 metres (460 feet) into a hillside at the dry edge of the Kalahari, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds some 2 million years of deposits, one of the longest records of human life anywhere.","answer":"Wonderwerk Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1.8 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fire traces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 Mya</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Wonderwerk Cave</b> is a long, level tunnel running about 140 metres (460 feet) into a hillside of the Kuruman Hills in the Northern Cape of South Africa, at the dry edge of the Kalahari. Its Afrikaans name means 'miracle', and the deposits on its floor, dug so far to about 4 metres (13 feet), cover roughly 2 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That makes it one of the longest records of human life known anywhere, and unusually it is a single sheltered sequence rather than a scatter of open-air camps. Oldowan tools lie near the bottom, left about 1.8 million years ago, which is the oldest evidence anywhere of people living inside a cave rather than merely passing one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above them come Acheulean hand axes, then the tools of later ages, and near the top the engraved stones and painted walls left within the last 10,000 years, the paintings clustered in the first 40 metres (130 feet) where daylight still reaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> For all that depth of time, the cave is best known for one thing: fire. In 2012 a team examining undisturbed sediment under the microscope found ashed plant material and burnt bone about 30 metres (100 feet) inside, in Acheulean layers around a million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the burning had happened where it lay, and far enough in that a bush fire could not have reached it, this became the earliest solid evidence of fire used by human hands. Work published in 2026 pushed the traces back further still, to somewhere between 1.07 and 1.79 million years ago, using a new way of reading heat damage in fossil bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> If that holds, the hominins carrying flame into this hillside were not our own kind but Homo erectus, tending a fire they had brought in from outside rather than kindled themselves.","citation":"","answerText":"Wonderwerk Cave","questions":["Oldowan tools left about 1.8 million years ago make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the oldest known place where people lived inside a cave rather than merely passing through one.","Ashed plants and burnt bone found 30 metres (100 feet) inside in 2012 made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the site of the earliest solid evidence of fire used by human hands."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","south africa"],"sources":["Liora Kolska Horwitz and Michael Chazan, “Past and Present at Wonderwerk Cave (Northern Cape Province, South Africa),” <i>African Archaeological Review</i> 32, no. 4 (2015): 595–612, esp. 596, 603–4 and 608, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10437-015-9208-5. [Open access]","Michael Chazan, “Technological Trends in the Acheulean of Wonderwerk Cave, South Africa,” <i>African Archaeological Review</i> 32, no. 4 (2015): 701–28, under “Context” and “Technological Development in the Wonderwerk Sequence,” https://hdl.handle.net/1807/90627. [Open access]","Francesco Berna et al., “Microstratigraphic Evidence of in situ Fire in the Acheulean Strata of Wonderwerk Cave, Northern Cape Province, South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 20 (2012): E1215–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3356665/. [Open access]","M. Dolores Marin-Monfort et al., “New Evidence for Early Pleistocene Use of Fire at Wonderwerk Cave (South Africa),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 6 (2026): e0347480, under “Wonderwerk Cave” and “Conclusions,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0347480. [Open access]","Andy I. R. Herries, \"A Chronological Perspective on the Acheulian and Its Transition to the Middle Stone Age in Southern Africa: The Question of the Fauresmith,\" <i>International Journal of Evolutionary Biology</i> 2011 (2011): 961401, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3139141/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Wonderwerk Cave","at":[23.5539,-27.845]}},
{"id":"wh-032","num":"32","category":"World · History","question":"Known almost entirely from one layer of a filled-in sinkhole in northern Spain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived some 800,000 years ago and startled everyone with a strikingly modern face.","answer":"Homo antecessor","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fossils</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 860,000 – 780,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997, by Bermúdez de Castro</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo antecessor</b> is an extinct human species known almost entirely from one layer of one site: level TD6 of the Gran Dolina, a filled-in sinkhole laid open by a railway trench cut through the Sierra de Atapuerca in northern Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bones lie somewhere between 780,000 and 860,000 years old, which makes them among the oldest human remains anywhere in western Europe, from a time when the continent was only thinly and intermittently peopled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A team led by José María Bermúdez de Castro named the species in 1997, choosing the Latin word for 'a scout or forerunner', the one who goes ahead of the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What made the find famous was a partial face, catalogued ATD6-69, belonging to a boy of about 10, whose cheeks are hollowed and whose midface is flat in a way that looks startlingly like ours. No other human of that antiquity has a face like it, and later work on how the skull grew has confirmed that the adults kept those modern-looking features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The same layer holds something else: the oldest known evidence of cannibalism anywhere in the human record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The human bones there carry the same cut marks, the same smashing for marrow and the same casual discard as the deer and horse bones lying beside them, and most of the victims were children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The species was announced as the last common ancestor of ourselves and the Neanderthals, a claim that made it famous and that specialists doubted almost at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The question was settled in 2020 by a technique that reads ancient proteins locked in tooth enamel, which survive far longer than DNA and can be recovered where no genome could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those proteins place it as a close cousin of the line leading to us, the Neanderthals and the Denisovans, standing just outside that line rather than at its root.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo antecessor","questions":["Named in 1997 for the Latin word meaning 'a scout or forerunner', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became famous for ATD6-69, the flat, modern-looking face of a boy of about 10.","The TD6 layer that yielded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also holds the oldest known evidence of cannibalism, its human bones cut and smashed for marrow like the deer beside them."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Frido Welker et al., “The Dental Proteome of <i>Homo antecessor</i>,” <i>Nature</i> 580, no. 7802 (2020): 235–38, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2153-8. [Open access]","I. Campaña et al., “New Interpretation of the Gran Dolina-TD6 Bearing <i>Homo antecessor</i> Deposits through Sedimentological Analysis,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6 (2016): 34799, under “The Gran Dolina Site” and “Results,” https://doi.org/10.1038/srep34799. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, \"Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","James Cole, \"Assessing the Calorific Significance of Episodes of Human Cannibalism in the Palaeolithic,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7 (2017): 44707, table 1, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep44707. [Open access]","Marina Mosquera et al., \"Shedding Light on the Early Pleistocene of TD6 (Gran Dolina, Atapuerca, Spain): The Technological Sequence and Occupational Inferences,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 13, no. 1 (2018): e0190889, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0190889. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Homo_antecessor_reconstruccion.jpg","title":"Homo antecessor","desc":"Reconstruction of Homo antecessor skull at Museu d'Arqueologia de Catalunya (Barcelona, Spain) Reconstrucción del cráneo de Homo antecessor, Museo de Arqueología de Cataluña (Barcelona, España). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_antecessor_reconstruccion.jpg","alt":"Homo antecessor reconstruccion"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-033","num":"33","category":"World · History","question":"A low limestone range in the province of Burgos, sliced open by a railway cutting, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hold caves in which five different hominin species have been identified.","answer":"Atapuerca Mountains","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Record</span><span class=\"dt-v\">back to c. 1.4 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Atapuerca Mountains</b> are a low limestone range about 15 kilometres (9 miles) east of Burgos, in northern Spain, riddled with caves that filled with sediment over the past million and a half years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their sites have been known since the late 19th century, and what opened them was a railway cutting through the hill, the Trinchera del Ferrocarril, whose fossils were reported to the authorities in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Systematic excavation began in 1978 with the founding of the Atapuerca Project, and the range became a World Heritage Site in 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five hominin species are represented in its caves, among them Homo antecessor from the Gran Dolina, identified in 1994 and then the oldest species known in western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The strangest site is the Sima de los Huesos, the Pit of Bones, where the skulls of several people some 430,000 years old came to light in 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> After three decades of digging, the number of individuals in that one deposit is put at 29, and the collection has nearly doubled since 2004, when about 4,000 specimens were known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are mostly teenagers and young adults, with ages at death of the kind a catastrophe leaves rather than a burial ground, and many of the fossils were destroyed by amateur cavers before proper excavation began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anatomy and ancient DNA read them not as a separate species but as a basal population of the Neanderthal line, and a paper of 2003 argued the pit holds the earliest possible evidence of funerary behaviour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest find came out of the Sima del Elefante in 2022 and was published in 2025: most of the midface of a hominin, nicknamed Pink, from a level dated between 1.4 and 1.1 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the earliest human face known in western Europe, primitive enough to be set apart from the Gran Dolina's Homo antecessor and assigned instead to Homo affinis erectus, which its describers read as a population turnover at the end of the Early Pleistocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Atapuerca Mountains","questions":["Deep in the Pit of Bones at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the bones of 29 people, mostly teenagers and young adults, came out of a single deposit some 430,000 years old.","Published in 2025, the fossil nicknamed Pink came from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and is most of a hominin midface, 1.1 to 1.4 million years old and the oldest face in western Europe."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","spain"],"sources":["Fundación Atapuerca, “Los yacimientos de la sierra de Atapuerca,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://www.atapuerca.org/es/atapuerca/Yacimientos-de-Atapuerca. [Open access]","Fundación Atapuerca, “Proyecto Atapuerca,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://www.atapuerca.org/es/atapuerca/Proyecto-Atapuerca. [Open access]","José María Bermúdez de Castro et al., “The Sima de los Huesos Middle Pleistocene Hominin Site (Burgos, Spain): Estimation of the Number of Individuals,” <i>The Anatomical Record</i> 304, no. 7 (2021): 1463–77, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.24551. [Paywalled]","Rosa Huguet et al., “The Earliest Human Face of Western Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 640 (2025): 707–13, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08681-0. [Paywalled]","Alessandro Urciuoli et al., “Semicircular Canals Shed Light on Bottleneck Events in the Evolution of the Neanderthal Clade,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 972, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-56155-8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Sierra_de_Atapuerca.jpg/1920px-Sierra_de_Atapuerca.jpg","title":"Atapuerca Mountains","desc":"Overview of the Sierra de Atapuerca. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sierra_de_Atapuerca.jpg","alt":"Sierra de Atapuerca"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Atapuerca Mountains","at":[-3.5222,42.3667]}},
{"id":"wh-034","num":"34","category":"World · History","question":"Named in 1908 from a single lower jaw dug out of a German sand pit, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was long cast as the common ancestor of the Neanderthals and ourselves.","answer":"Homo heidelbergensis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700,000 – 300,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1908, by Otto Schoetensack</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo heidelbergensis</b> is a proposed human species of the Middle Pleistocene, named in 1908 by Otto Schoetensack from a single lower jaw that quarrymen had dug out of a sand pit at Mauer, near Heidelberg, the year before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The jaw is oddly mixed: the bone is thick and heavy and the chin is missing altogether, yet the teeth are small and almost modern. Careful dating in 2010 put it at about 609,000 years old, give or take 40,000, which makes it one of the oldest human fossils anywhere in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Skulls later gathered under the name carry brains averaging around 1,200 cubic centimetres, not far short of ours, in bodies that were tall and powerfully built. For most of the 20th century the species was given the grandest job in the family tree: the common ancestor from which the Neanderthals grew in Europe and we grew in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The trouble is that the name has been made to carry almost anything that does not fit elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Middle Pleistocene fossils from three continents that are plainly not Homo erectus, not Neanderthal and not us have been swept into it, a habit specialists call the muddle in the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient DNA then removed its best European exhibit, showing that the people in the Pit of Bones at Atapuerca sit on the Neanderthal line rather than at the fork above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 2021 a group of researchers proposed abandoning the name outright, along with its African twin Homo rhodesiensis, moving the African fossils to a new species called Homo bodoensis and the European ones, Mauer included, to the Neanderthals, a suggestion that has not been widely taken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most workers now use the term narrowly, for the Mauer jaw and a handful of European fossils like it, and treat the wider category as a filing cabinet rather than a species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo heidelbergensis","questions":["The Mauer jaw, thick and chinless yet with small, almost modern teeth and dated to about 609,000 years, is the type specimen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and one of Europe's oldest.","Because Middle Pleistocene fossils from three continents were all swept under it, the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became a filing cabinet — the muddle in the middle — rather than one clear species."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Mirjana Roksandic et al., “Resolving the ‘Muddle in the Middle’: The Case for <i>Homo bodoensis</i> sp. nov.,” <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 31, no. 1 (2022): 20–29, https://doi.org/10.1002/evan.21929. [Open access]","Otto Schoetensack, <i>Der Unterkiefer des Homo heidelbergensis aus den Sanden von Mauer bei Heidelberg</i> (Leipzig: Wilhelm Engelmann, 1908), under “I. Geologisch-paläontologischer Teil,” https://archive.org/details/o-schoetensack-der-unterkiefer-des-homo-heidelbergensis. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, \"Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","Aurélien Mounier and Marta Mirazón Lahr, \"Deciphering African Late Middle Pleistocene Hominin Diversity and the Origin of Our Species,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 10 (2019): 3406, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-019-11213-w. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., \"Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?\" <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Homo_heidelbergensis_%28Erstbeschreibung%29_04.jpg/1920px-Homo_heidelbergensis_%28Erstbeschreibung%29_04.jpg","title":"Homo heidelbergensis","desc":"Type specimen of Homo heidelbergenis from Mauer (near Heidelberg, Germany): radiography from the first description. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_heidelbergensis_(Erstbeschreibung)_04.jpg","alt":"Homo heidelbergensis (Erstbeschreibung) 04"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-035","num":"35","category":"World · History","question":"Blasted out of a German cave by quarrymen in 1856 and named as the first fossil human species ever recognised, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was drawn for a century as a stooping brute.","answer":"Neanderthal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Type fossil</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1856, Neander valley</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1864, by William King</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neanderthal</b> was a human species of Europe and western and central Asia, present from at least 400,000 years ago until about 40,000, and the closest relative our own species has ever had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name comes from the Neander valley near Düsseldorf, where quarrymen blasting limestone out of a cave in 1856 threw a skullcap and some limb bones onto the spoil heap. That valley had itself been named after Joachim Neander, a 17th-century hymn writer whose family had dressed their surname Neumann up in Greek, so the fossils are called, in effect, new man. William King proposed the species name Homo neanderthalensis in 1864, the first fossil human ever given one, though older finds from Engis and Gibraltar had already been dug up without anyone recognising what they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bones show a stocky, heavily muscled body built for cold, with a jutting midface, thick brow ridges, almost no chin, and a brain averaging slightly larger than ours. <br><br> They made Mousterian tools, glued stone points to shafts with birch tar, kept fire, hunted large animals at close range, and sometimes buried their dead. Deep in Bruniquel Cave in southern France, 336 metres (1,100 feet) from daylight, they broke stalagmites into two low rings about 176,500 years ago, which meant carrying fire far underground long before modern humans reached Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their brutish reputation traces to a single skeleton, the old man of La Chapelle-aux-Saints, whose spine was deformed by arthritis: Marcellin Boule reconstructed him in 1911 as a stooping, bent-kneed creature and took that for the whole species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 2010 a team led by Svante Pääbo published a draft Neanderthal genome and found what no bone could show, that Neanderthals and our own ancestors had interbred; everyone descended from those who left Africa carries a per cent or two of their DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They vanish around 40,000 years ago, and why is still argued over: competition, swinging climate, thin scattered populations, or simply being absorbed into far more numerous newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Neanderthal","questions":["One arthritic skeleton, the old man of La Chapelle-aux-Saints, was reconstructed in 1911 as a stooping <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and taken for the whole species, which is where the brutish image began.","Deep in Bruniquel Cave, 336 metres (1,100 feet) from daylight, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke stalagmites into rings about 176,500 years ago, long before modern humans reached Europe."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["William King, \"The Reputed Fossil Man of the Neanderthal,\" <i>Quarterly Journal of Science</i> 1 (1864): 88–97, https://archive.org/details/biostor-195152. [Open access]","Richard E. Green et al., \"A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,\" <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","Jacques Jaubert et al., \"Early Neanderthal Constructions Deep in Bruniquel Cave in Southwestern France,\" <i>Nature</i> 534, no. 7605 (2016): 111–14, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature18291. [Paywalled]","Marcellin Boule, \"L'homme fossile de La Chapelle-aux-Saints,\" <i>Annales de Paléontologie</i> 6–8 (1911–13), https://archive.org/details/b22463355. [Open access]","Tom Higham et al., \"The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,\" <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Krist Vaesen et al., \"Inbreeding, Allee Effects and Stochasticity Might Be Sufficient to Account for Neanderthal Extinction,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 11 (2019): e0225117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0225117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Neanderthal_at_AMNH.jpg/1920px-Neanderthal_at_AMNH.jpg","title":"Neanderthal","desc":"Neanderthal composite skeleton at the American Museum of Natural History. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neanderthal_at_AMNH.jpg","alt":"Neanderthal at AMNH"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"wh-036","num":"36","category":"World · History","question":"Recognised from flints dug from gravel quarries near Paris in the 19th century, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shapes a core in advance so a flake of intended size and outline comes away in one blow.","answer":"Levallois technique","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 40,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Levallois technique</b> is a way of working stone in which the core, the block being struck, is shaped in advance so that a flake of intended size and outline comes away in one blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It takes its name from Levallois-Perret, a suburb of Paris whose gravel quarries yielded such pieces during the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stoneworker trims around the edge of the block, works one face into a low dome, prepares a small flat surface to strike, and only then delivers the blow that frees the target flake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What is left is domed on one side, flat on the other and covered in the scars of that preparation, which is why such pieces are called tortoise cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The point of all this is predetermination: the flake exists in the maker's intention several steps before it exists in the stone, which is why it is read as evidence of planning ahead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Some cores were set up to give one large flake, others a series of flakes, points or blades from the same prepared face, and a fresh round of trimming could make a core ready again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The method appears across Africa and Eurasia by about 300,000 years ago, and at Nor Geghi in Armenia it lies beside handaxes in layers dated to roughly 335,000 to 325,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because it turns up in widely separated regions that all had a long handaxe tradition behind them, many specialists argue it was worked out more than once rather than invented once and carried outwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both Neanderthals and early Homo sapiens used it, and the flakes alone rarely reveal which; one find at Umm el Tlel in Syria is unusually direct, a broken Levallois point lodged in the neck bone of a speared wild ass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Europe it holds on until roughly 40,000 years ago, when long narrow blades struck from tall cores take over, though in parts of Africa and Asia prepared cores were still worked long after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Levallois technique","questions":["Cores domed on one face and scarred by trimming are called tortoise cores, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reads as planning because the flake exists in the maker's mind first.","Appearing across Africa and Eurasia by about 300,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was used by both Neanderthals and early Homo sapiens, so its flakes rarely reveal the maker."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Éric Boëda et al., “A Levallois Point Embedded in the Vertebra of a Wild Ass (<i>Equus africanus</i>): Hafting, Projectiles and Mousterian Hunting Weapons,” <i>Antiquity</i> 73, no. 280 (1999): 394–402, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00088335. [Paywalled]","Metin I. Eren and Stephen J. Lycett, “Why Levallois? A Morphometric Comparison of Experimental ‘Preferential’ Levallois Flakes versus Debitage Flakes,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 1 (2012): e29273, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3264556/. [Open access]","Daniel S. Adler et al., “Early Levallois Technology and the Lower to Middle Paleolithic Transition in the Southern Caucasus,” <i>Science</i> 345, no. 6204 (2014): 1609–13, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1256484. [Paywalled]","Sylvain Soriano and Paola Villa, “Early Levallois and the Beginning of the Middle Paleolithic in Central Italy,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 10 (2017): e0186082, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5650164/. [Open access]","James Blinkhorn, Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Michael D. Petraglia, and Simon Blockley, “Directional Changes in Levallois Core Technologies between Eastern Africa, Arabia, and the Levant during MIS 5,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 11465, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8169925/. [Open access]","Li Li et al., “A Synthesis of the Dibble et al. Controlled Experiments into the Mechanics of Lithic Production,” <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 30, no. 4 (2023): 1284–1325, esp. 1290 and 1301–2, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-022-09586-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Levallois_core_%28FindID_967752%29.jpg/1920px-Levallois_core_%28FindID_967752%29.jpg","title":"Levallois technique","desc":"A flint levallois core of Middle Paleolithic date, c.250,000 BCE - 40,000. Description: The core is rounded on three sides and flat on the bottom where the final removal occurred. It has covering scaled semi-abrupt retouch. Kent County Council, Jo Ahmet, 2019-08-21 11:19:16, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Levallois_core_(FindID_967752).jpg","alt":"Levallois core"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-037","num":"37","category":"World · History","question":"Named after a pair of rock shelters in the Dordogne, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the flake-tool industry the Neanderthals carried across Europe for more than a hundred thousand years.","answer":"Mousterian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1860s–1870s, Lartet and Christy</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mousterian</b> is the stone tool industry of the Middle Paleolithic in Europe, western Asia and North Africa, current from roughly 300,000 to 40,000 years ago and, in Europe, the work of the Neanderthals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> It is named after Le Moustier, a pair of rock shelters above the Vézère in the Dordogne whose flint tools Lartet and Christy published in the 1860s and 1870s, and it is still the industry's type site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its assemblages are made of flakes rather than the great core tools of the earlier Acheulean: side scrapers above all, along with points and pieces notched or toothed along one edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The flakes come off prepared cores, worked by the Levallois method or by turning a disc-shaped core, and many were then mounted on wooden shafts with birch tar or natural bitumen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> What has kept the Mousterian argued over for a century is that its assemblages differ sharply from cave to cave, in ways no one has agreed how to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> François Bordes sorted the tools into 63 types and the assemblages into five recurring kinds, among them the scraper-heavy Quina and a variant that still made handaxes, and read each as the mark of a separate people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Lewis and Sally Binford answered in 1966 that one people doing different jobs at different places would leave exactly that pattern, and the Bordes–Binford debate became one of the defining arguments of 20th-century archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A later line of work suggested something plainer still: a scraper resharpened until it is worn down changes shape as it goes, so one type may simply be another further through its life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The makers were not the same everywhere: in Europe it is Neanderthal work, but in the Levant, at caves such as Skhul, Qafzeh and Tabun, Neanderthals and early modern humans left assemblages that cannot be told apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It ends around 40,000 years ago, as blade-based Upper Paleolithic industries spread and the Neanderthals disappear, though whether one caused the other is still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mousterian","questions":["Side scrapers, points and notched flakes struck from prepared cores and fixed to shafts with birch tar mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across Europe for over a hundred thousand years.","The 63 tool types were read as separate peoples until 1966, when the answer came that one people doing different jobs would leave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looking just as varied."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Édouard Lartet and Henry Christy, <i>Reliquiae Aquitanicae: Being Contributions to the Archaeology and Palaeontology of Périgord and the Adjoining Provinces of Southern France</i>, ed. Thomas Rupert Jones (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865–75), plates III and V, https://archive.org/details/reliquiaeaquitan00lartuoft. [Open access]","François Bordes, “Mousterian Cultures in France,” <i>Science</i> 134, no. 3482 (1961): 803–10, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.134.3482.803. [Paywalled]","Lewis R. Binford and Sally R. Binford, “A Preliminary Analysis of Functional Variability in the Mousterian of Levallois Facies,” <i>American Anthropologist</i> 68, no. 2 (1966): 238–95, https://doi.org/10.1525/aa.1966.68.2.02a001030. [Paywalled]","Tom Higham et al., “The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Chris Stringer, and Michael D. Petraglia, “Skhul Lithic Technology and the Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i> into Southwest Asia,” <i>Quaternary International</i> 515 (2019): 30–52, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:26de242d-8c79-44a8-9dea-5631a7400664. [Open access]","Marcel J. L. Th. Niekus et al., “Middle Paleolithic Complex Technology and a Neandertal Tar-Backed Tool from the Dutch North Sea,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 44 (2019): 22081–87, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6825292/. [Open access]","Michael J. Shott, “Dibble’s Reduction Thesis: Implications for Global Lithic Analysis,” <i>Journal of Paleolithic Archaeology</i> 7, no. 1 (2024): 12, https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41982-024-00178-y. [Open access]","Patrick Schmidt, Radu Iovita, Armelle Charrié-Duhaut, Gunther Möller, Abay Namen, and Ewa Dutkiewicz, “Ochre-Based Compound Adhesives at the Mousterian Type-Site Document Complex Cognition and High Investment,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 8 (2024): eadl0822, https://hal.science/hal-04484835v1/document. [Open access]","Jacopo Gennai, “The Mousterian in North-Western Tuscany: Publishing Fieldwork Documentation Leads to a New Stratigraphical Interpretation of the Piano di Mommio Sites,” <i>Open Research Europe</i> 4 (2024): 37, under “The Mousterian industries,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11109565/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Mousterian_Culture_and_Late_Stone_Age_Stone_Tools._Notch_for_sharpening_wood%2C_and_denticulate_for_sawing_wood_and_bone._Rosh_En_Mor_and_En_Aqev._250%2C000-22%2C000_BP._Israel.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Mousterian","desc":"Mousterian Culture and Late Stone Age Stone Tools. Notch for sharpening wood, and denticulate for sawing wood and bone. Rosh En Mor and En Aqev. 250,000-22,000 BP. Israel Israel Museum, Jerusalem, Israel. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mousterian_Culture_and_Late_Stone_Age_Stone_Tools._Notch_for_sharpening_wood,_and_denticulate_for_sawing_wood_and_bone._Rosh_En_Mor_and_En_Aqev._250,000-22,000_BP._Israel.jpg","alt":"Mousterian Culture and Late Stone Age Stone Tools. Notch for sharpening wood, and denticulate for sawing wood and bone. Rosh En Mor and En Aqev. 250,000-22,000 BP. Israel"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-038","num":"38","category":"World · History","question":"Known first from one girl's finger bone and the genome read out of it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were an Asian people whose DNA still runs in New Guinea and Australia.","answer":"Denisovans","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lineage split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 400,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Identified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2010, from DNA</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Denisovans</b> are an extinct human population of Asia, and the first kind of human ever recognised from DNA rather than from the shape of its bones. They came to light in 2008 in Denisova Cave in the Altai mountains of southern Siberia, as a fragment of one girl's finger bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2010 a team led by Svante Pääbo sequenced it and found a lineage that was neither Neanderthal nor modern human, then named it after the cave, itself named after a hermit said to have sheltered there in the 18th century. They still have no agreed scientific name, because naming a species requires a described fossil and for years there was almost nothing to describe: a finger bone, a few teeth and some splinters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The genome put their split from the Neanderthal line at roughly 400,000 years ago and showed them spread across Asia, from Siberia to the Tibetan Plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One splinter from the same cave, from a girl who died about 90,000 years ago, turned out to have had a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father, the only first-generation cross between two kinds of human ever found. Their descendants are alive today: people in New Guinea and Aboriginal Australians carry roughly 4 to 6 per cent Denisovan DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tibetans carry a Denisovan version of a gene that helps the body cope with thin air, so the inheritance is still doing useful work high on the plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A jawbone from Baishiya Karst Cave in Tibet, about 160,000 years old, was identified as Denisovan in 2019 from proteins surviving in its teeth, the first such fossil found outside Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then in 2025 the almost complete Harbin skull, once named Homo longi, was tied to them both by its proteins and by mitochondrial DNA taken from the hardened plaque on its teeth, giving a people known from a fingertip a face at last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Denisovans","questions":["Being the first humans known from DNA rather than bones, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still have no agreed scientific name, because naming a species needs a described fossil.","One splinter from Denisova Cave showed that a girl who died about 90,000 years ago had a Neanderthal mother and a father among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the only known first-generation cross."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","genetics"],"sources":["David Reich et al., “Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, abstract and “A Denisovan Individual,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Genome of the Offspring of a Neanderthal Mother and a Denisovan Father,” <i>Nature</i> 561, no. 7721 (2018): 113–16, abstract and opening paragraphs, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6130845/. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, “Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, §4.2 “Who Were the Denisovans?,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","Emilia Huerta-Sánchez et al., “Altitude Adaptation in Tibetans Caused by Introgression of Denisovan-Like DNA,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7513 (2014): 194–97, abstract, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4134395/. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu et al., “The Proteome of the Late Middle Pleistocene Harbin Individual,” <i>Science</i> 389, no. 6762 (2025): 704–7, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adu9677. [Paywalled]","Qiaomei Fu et al., “Denisovan Mitochondrial DNA from Dental Calculus of the >146,000-Year-Old Harbin Cranium,” <i>Cell</i> 188, no. 15 (2025): 3919–26, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2025.05.040. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Yuan_%282021%29_Fig._3-_Archaic_introgression_in_modern_humans_across_the_world_%28Denisovan_only%29.png","title":"Denisovans","desc":"Yuan (2021) Fig. 3- Archaic introgression in modern humans across the world (Denisovan only). 2021-10-29. Kai Yuan et al.,. CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuan_(2021)_Fig._3-_Archaic_introgression_in_modern_humans_across_the_world_(Denisovan_only).png","alt":"Yuan (2021) Fig. 3- Archaic introgression in modern humans across the world (Denisovan only)"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-039","num":"39","category":"World · History","question":"Cool enough inside for ancient DNA to survive, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Altai mountains is the only place known to have housed Neanderthals, Denisovans and modern humans.","answer":"Denisova Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">back to c. 300,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1977, dug from 1982</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Finger bone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2008</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Denisova Cave</b> is a limestone cave above the Anuy river in the Altai mountains of southern Siberia, and the only place on earth known to have been lived in by three different kinds of human.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its three chambers hold deposits reaching back some 300,000 years, sifted since 1977 and dug systematically from 1982 by archaeologists from Novosibirsk, and it takes its name from a hermit said to have sheltered in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cave is cool and its temperature steady, so ancient DNA survives here far better than at the warm sites where most human fossils turn up. That is why a scrap of a girl's finger bone found in 2008 could be sequenced at all, revealing the Denisovans, a human population nobody had suspected existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neanderthals lived here too, modern humans arrived by about 45,000 years ago, and the one known child of a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father came out of these same deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Working out who was present when took years of painstaking dating: optical readings on sand grains and radiocarbon on bone, published in 2019, traced occupation of the cave back about 300,000 years and put its oldest Denisovan fossil as early as 195,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The greater trick was pulling human DNA straight out of the dirt with no bone at all, shown in 2017 to work across four Eurasian caves including this one, and turned here in 2021 on 728 sediment samples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That sediment DNA sketched the order of tenants: Denisovans first, Neanderthals arriving later, a stretch when Denisovan traces vanish altogether, then both again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Among the objects left behind are a sewing needle of bird bone about 50,000 years old and a polished green stone bracelet with a neatly drilled hole, whose age and whose maker are both still argued over. The cave's lasting contribution is the method it proved: that a few scraps and a bucketful of sediment can hold the history of three kinds of human passing through one doorway.","citation":"","answerText":"Denisova Cave","questions":["From a girl's finger bone found here in 2008 came the first evidence of the Denisovans, because the cool, steady air of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let ancient DNA survive.","By pulling human DNA straight from the dirt of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, researchers traced its tenants — Denisovans first, then Neanderthals — across more than 700 sediment samples."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","russia"],"sources":["David Reich et al., “Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, under “Introduction,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “Neandertal and Denisovan DNA from Pleistocene Sediments,” <i>Science</i> 356, no. 6338 (2017): 605–8, abstract, author manuscript at https://eprints.bournemouth.ac.uk/29206/3/Revised_manuscript.pdf. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Genome of the Offspring of a Neanderthal Mother and a Denisovan Father,” <i>Nature</i> 561, no. 7721 (2018): 113–16, abstract, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6130845/. [Open access]","Elena I. Zavala et al., “Pleistocene Sediment DNA Reveals Hominin and Faunal Turnovers at Denisova Cave,” <i>Nature</i> 595, no. 7867 (2021): 399–403, abstract, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8277575/. [Open access]","Zenobia Jacobs et al., “Timing of Archaic Hominin Occupation of Denisova Cave in Southern Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 565, no. 7741 (2019): 594–99, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-018-0843-2. [Paywalled]","Katerina Douka et al., “Age Estimates for Hominin Fossils and the Onset of the Upper Palaeolithic at Denisova Cave,” <i>Nature</i> 565, no. 7741 (2019): 640–44, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-018-0870-z. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/%D0%98%D0%B7%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%8C_%D0%9C%D0%B8%D1%80_%D0%94%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%89%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0._01.jpg/1920px-%D0%98%D0%B7%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%8C_%D0%9C%D0%B8%D1%80_%D0%94%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%89%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0._01.jpg","title":"Denisova Cave","desc":"Денисова пещера: Солонешенский район, Алтайский край. Демин Алексей Барнаул, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%98%D0%B7%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%8C_%D0%9C%D0%B8%D1%80_%D0%94%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%89%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0._01.jpg","alt":"Известная на весь Мир Денисова пещера. 01"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Denisova Cave","at":[84.6761,51.3975]}},
{"id":"wh-040","num":"40","category":"World · History","question":"Standing about a metre (3 ft 3 in) tall with a brain the size of a chimpanzee's, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was dug out of an Indonesian cave in 2003 and nicknamed the hobbit.","answer":"Homo floresiensis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Skeletons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100,000 – 60,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2003, at Liang Bua</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2004</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo floresiensis</b> is a very small human species from the island of Flores in Indonesia, known to almost everyone as the hobbit. A joint Indonesian and Australian team led by Mike Morwood found the first bones in 2003 in Liang Bua, a wide cave in the island's limestone hills, and named the species the following year. The type specimen is a partial female skeleton standing about a metre (3 ft 3 in) tall, and bones of several other individuals have come out of the same cave since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her braincase held about 426 cubic centimetres, close to a chimpanzee's and under a third of ours, yet stone tools lie in the very same deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first published dates put her at about 18,000 years ago, but a restudy of the cave's layers in 2016 moved the skeletons back to between 100,000 and 60,000 years, and the tools to around 50,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Many specialists refused to accept a new species and argued this was a modern human with a disease, proposing in turn microcephaly, a thyroid disorder, Laron syndrome and Down syndrome. Each was tested against the bones, and in the features that separate healthy people from sick ones the skeleton kept falling in with fossil humans rather than with either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where it came from is still open: a Homo erectus that shrank after reaching the island, or a smaller and older kind of human that left Africa, since the wrists and feet look strikingly primitive. Digging in the So'a basin has pushed the story back: jaws and teeth from about 700,000 years ago are as small or smaller, and an arm bone published in 2024 came from an adult smaller still, the shortest such bone ever reported for a fossil human.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Flores lies beyond the deep strait Wallace drew as a line, so reaching it meant crossing open sea to an island that also held dwarf elephants and giant rats, and the hobbits vanish about 50,000 years ago, roughly when our own species arrives.","citation":"","answerText":"Homo floresiensis","questions":["First dated to about 18,000 years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was pushed back to between 100,000 and 60,000 years when a 2016 restudy corrected the cave's layers.","Many refused a new species and read <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a diseased modern human, but tests for microcephaly, thyroid disorder and Down syndrome each fell in with fossils instead."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","indonesia"],"sources":["Peter Brown et al., “A New Small-Bodied Hominin from the Late Pleistocene of Flores, Indonesia,” <i>Nature</i> 431, no. 7012 (2004): 1055–61, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02999. [Paywalled]","Daisuke Kubo, Reiko T. Kono, and Yousuke Kaifu, “Brain Size of <i>Homo floresiensis</i> and Its Evolutionary Implications,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 280, no. 1760 (2013): 20130338, under “Endocranial Volume of LB1,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3652458/. [Open access]","Thomas Sutikna et al., “Revised Stratigraphy and Chronology for <i>Homo floresiensis</i> at Liang Bua in Indonesia,” <i>Nature</i> 532, no. 7599 (2016): 366–69, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature17179. [Paywalled]","Karen L. Baab et al., “A Critical Evaluation of the Down Syndrome Diagnosis for LB1, Type Specimen of <i>Homo floresiensis</i>,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 6 (2016): e0155731, abstract and “Conclusions,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0155731. [Open access]","Yousuke Kaifu et al., “Early Evolution of Small Body Size in <i>Homo floresiensis</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 6381, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-50649-7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Homo_floresiensis_skull_-_Naturmuseum_Senckenberg_-_DSC02091.JPG/1920px-Homo_floresiensis_skull_-_Naturmuseum_Senckenberg_-_DSC02091.JPG","title":"Homo floresiensis","desc":"Exhibit in Naturmuseum Senckenberg, Frankfurt am Main, Germany. Photography was permitted in the museum without restriction. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_floresiensis_skull_-_Naturmuseum_Senckenberg_-_DSC02091.JPG","alt":"Homo floresiensis skull - Naturmuseum Senckenberg"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-041","num":"41","category":"World · History","question":"Hauled out of a South African cave chamber reachable only through a chimney 18 centimetres (7 inches) wide, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a brain under half the size of ours yet lived alongside the earliest Homo sapiens.","answer":"Homo naledi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">335,000 – 236,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2015, by Lee Berger</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo naledi</b> is a small-brained human species from the Rising Star cave system in South Africa, named in 2015 after the caves themselves, since <i>naledi</i> means 'star' in Sesotho. Two recreational cavers squeezed down a narrow chimney in September 2013 and came out into a chamber whose floor was littered with bones. That chimney drops about 12 metres (39 feet) and squeezes down to some 20 centimetres (8 inches), so the digging was done by six scientists slim enough to pass, recruited by Lee Berger through a notice on Facebook and all, as it happened, women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They brought up 1,550 pieces from at least 15 individuals, the richest haul of a single hominin species anywhere in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The anatomy is a mosaic: a brain of only 465 to 610 cubic centimetres, well under half of ours, a body about 1.44 metres (4 ft 9 in) tall, curved fingers and shoulders made for climbing, but wrists, legs and feet built for walking far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Everything about that skeleton suggested great antiquity, so the dates published in 2017 came as a shock: between 335,000 and 236,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That places this small-brained cousin in Africa at the same time as the earliest Homo sapiens, which means archaic bodies were not a phase the family left behind but a branch running alongside us. The harder question is how the bodies got so deep underground, since the bones carry no carnivore damage, arrived with almost no other animals, and show no sign of having been washed in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Berger argued they were carried in on purpose, and in 2023 his team went further, claiming deliberate burial and engravings cut into the cave walls, released as preprints alongside a Netflix documentary while review was still running. Both papers were published in 2025 with the journal's own verdict attached: one reviewer found the burial case convincing and another found it incomplete, while the engravings were judged an important but preliminary report, so the bones are certain and the story around them is not yet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo naledi","questions":["More than 1,550 bones of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from at least 15 individuals, were brought up by six slim scientists recruited through Facebook to pass a 12-metre chimney.","A 2023 claim that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had buried its dead and cut engravings into the cave walls was released alongside a Netflix film while peer review was still running."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","south africa"],"sources":["Lee R. Berger et al., “<i>Homo naledi</i>, a New Species of the Genus <i>Homo</i> from the Dinaledi Chamber, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 4 (2015): e09560, under “Introduction,” “Description” and “Body Size and Proportions,” https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.09560. [Open access]","Paul H. G. M. Dirks et al., “Geological and Taphonomic Context for the New Hominin Species <i>Homo naledi</i> from the Dinaledi Chamber, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 4 (2015): e09561, under “Cave Setting” and “Surface Modifications and Vertebrate–Bone Interactions,” https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.09561. [Open access]","Paul H. G. M. Dirks et al., “The Age of <i>Homo naledi</i> and Associated Sediments in the Rising Star Cave, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 6 (2017): e24231, abstract, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.24231. [Open access]","John Hawks et al., “New Fossil Remains of <i>Homo naledi</i> from the Lesedi Chamber, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 6 (2017): e24232, abstract, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.24232. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger et al., “Evidence for Deliberate Burial of the Dead by <i>Homo naledi</i>,” <i>eLife</i> 12 (2025): RP89106, eLife assessment, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.89106.3. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger et al., “An Initial Report of circa 241,000- to 335,000-Year-Old Rock Engravings and Their Relation to <i>Homo naledi</i> in the Rising Star Cave System, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 12 (2025): RP89102, eLife assessment, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.89102.3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Homo_naledi_skeletal_specimens.jpg/1920px-Homo_naledi_skeletal_specimens.jpg","title":"Homo naledi","desc":"Dinaledi skeletal specimens. The figure includes approximately all of the material incorporated in this diagnosis, including the holotype specimen, paratypes and referred material. These make up 737 partial or complete anatomical elements, many of which consist of several refitted specimens. Lee Roger Berger research team, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_naledi_skeletal_specimens.jpg","alt":"Homo naledi skeletal specimens"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-042","num":"42","category":"World · History","question":"In the 1758 classification every other species got a list of distinguishing features, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> got the instruction to know itself and no bodily characters whatever.","answer":"Homo sapiens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest fossils</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 315,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1758, by Carl Linnaeus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo sapiens</b> is the species every living person belongs to, and the only surviving member of a genus that for most of its history held several kinds of human at once. Carl Linnaeus named it in 1758, and where his other species each received a list of distinguishing features, ours received the instruction to know itself and no defining characters at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two centuries later a botanist tidied up the gap by designating Linnaeus himself as the type specimen, on the grounds that he was the one human the author had studied closely. The joke conceals a real difficulty, since a definition that cleanly separates our fossils from everybody else's has never been easy to write.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the bones do show is a high rounded braincase, a small face tucked beneath it, light brow ridges, and a chin, which is the one piece of bony anatomy no other human has ever had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In Africa those features arrive piecemeal rather than together: Jebel Irhoud in Morocco carries some of them by about 315,000 years ago, Omo Kibish in Ethiopia by about 233,000, and no single site shows the whole package appearing at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why the current picture is of a population spread across the continent and exchanging ancestry, rather than one cradle from which everything follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ventures beyond Africa began early and mostly failed, with a jaw from Misliya cave in Israel dated to roughly 180,000 years ago and later people at Skhul and Qafzeh, none of whom left descendants alive today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The expansion that held began around 60,000 years ago, reaching Australia by about 50,000 years ago, or 65,000 if the earliest Australian site is taken at face value, and Europe by about 45,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Along the way our ancestors met Neanderthals and Denisovans and had children with both, so being the only human left is a condition roughly 40,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo sapiens","questions":["A chin is the one piece of bony anatomy no other human ever carried, marking <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> apart along with a high rounded braincase and a small tucked-in face.","The expansion that finally held began around 60,000 years ago and carried <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Australia by about 50,000 and Europe by 45,000, meeting Neanderthals along the way."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Carl Linnaeus, <i>Systema Naturae per Regna Tria Naturae</i>, 10th ed., vol. 1 (Stockholm: Laurentius Salvius, 1758), 20, https://archive.org/details/mobot31753000798873. [Open access]","Chris Stringer, \"The Origin and Evolution of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150237, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920294/. [Open access]","Simon Neubauer, Jean-Jacques Hublin, and Philipp Gunz, \"The Evolution of Modern Human Brain Shape,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 1 (2018): eaao5961, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5783678/. [Open access]","Céline M. Vidal et al., \"Age of the Oldest Known <i>Homo sapiens</i> from Eastern Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 601, no. 7894 (2022): 579–83, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8791829/. [Open access]","Andra Meneganzin, Telmo Pievani, and Giorgio Manzi, \"Pan-Africanism vs. Single-Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>: Putting the Debate in the Light of Evolutionary Biology,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 31, no. 4 (2022): 199–212, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9540121/. [Open access]","Israel Hershkovitz et al., \"The Earliest Modern Humans outside Africa,\" <i>Science</i> 359, no. 6374 (2018): 456–59, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aap8369. [Paywalled]","Chris Clarkson et al., \"Human Occupation of Northern Australia by 65,000 Years Ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 547, no. 7663 (2017): 306–10, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22968. [Paywalled]","Richard E. Green et al., \"A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,\" <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","David Reich et al., \"Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,\" <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Tom Higham et al., \"The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,\" <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Homo_sapiens_-_Paleolithic_-_reconstruction_-_MUSE.jpg","title":"Homo sapiens","desc":"Ricostruzione di Homo sapiens del Paleolitico. Matteo De Stefano/MUSE This file was uploaded by MUSE - Science Museum of Trento in cooperation with Wikimedia Italia.,. CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_sapiens_-_Paleolithic_-_reconstruction_-_MUSE.jpg","alt":"Homo sapiens - Paleolithic - reconstruction - MUSE"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"wh-043","num":"43","category":"World · History","question":"A worked-out mine in Morocco whose fossils carry a modern face above a long low braincase nobody alive has, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pushed the age of our species back by about 100,000 years.","answer":"Jebel Irhoud","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 315,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Jebel Irhoud</b> is a worked-out barite mine about 100 kilometres (62 miles) west of Marrakesh in Morocco, and it holds the oldest fossils yet assigned to our own species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miners blasting the hillside in the early 1960s opened the deposits and turned up a human skull, which was read for a long time as an African Neanderthal and thought to be only a few tens of thousands of years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavations from 2004, led by Jean-Jacques Hublin and Abdelouahed Ben-Ncer, recovered 22 more fossils from at least five people, along with stone tools and the bones of the gazelles they ate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The deposits are full of flint that had been heated in ancient fires, and measuring the light stored in those burnt flakes put the layer at about 315,000 years ago, some 100,000 years older than anything previously called Homo sapiens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The faces are the first surprise, because they are small and tucked in, with jaws and teeth that would not draw a second glance today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The braincase behind those faces is the second surprise, since it is long and low rather than the globe that modern people carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Taken together the two say that our features were assembled piece by piece, with the modern face early and the rounded skull only later, and that brain shape went on changing well inside our own lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The location was a third surprise, because north-west Africa is about as far from the East African rift valleys as the continent allows, and the rift had long been treated as the cradle. That is part of why specialists moved from picturing one birthplace to picturing a population spread across the whole of Africa, with our features surfacing here and there and spreading between groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stone tools are among the earliest directly dated Middle Stone Age assemblages anywhere, which ties that toolkit to our species from close to its beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Jebel Irhoud","questions":["Heated flint from ancient fires dated the layer at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to about 315,000 years ago, which is roughly 100,000 years before any other fossil assigned to our species.","Sitting in the north-west corner of Africa, far from the East African rift long treated as our cradle, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> helped replace one birthplace with a whole continent."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","morocco"],"sources":["Jean-Jacques Hublin et al., \"New Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco and the Pan-African Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,\" <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 289–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22336. [Paywalled]","Andra Meneganzin, Telmo Pievani, and Giorgio Manzi, \"Pan-Africanism vs. Single-Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>: Putting the Debate in the Light of Evolutionary Biology,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 31, no. 4 (2022): 199–212, under \"Challenges and Implications of Jebel Irhoud,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9540121/. [Open access]","Daniel Richter et al., \"The Age of the Hominin Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco, and the Origins of the Middle Stone Age,\" <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 293–96, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22335. [Paywalled]","Simon Neubauer, Jean-Jacques Hublin, and Philipp Gunz, \"The Evolution of Modern Human Brain Shape,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 1 (2018): eaao5961, table 1 and \"Gradual Globularization within <i>H. sapiens</i>,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5783678/. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., \"Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?\" <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, \"Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Jebel_Irhoud-1_NMNH.jpg","title":"Jebel Irhoud","desc":"Homo sapiens skull cast (Jebel Irhoud-1) on display at the Smithsonian's National Museum of Natural History. Jonathan Chen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jebel_Irhoud-1_NMNH.jpg","alt":"Jebel Irhoud-1 NMNH"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Jebel Irhoud","at":[-8.8725,31.855]}},
{"id":"wh-044","num":"44","category":"World · History","question":"Dug out of an Ethiopian riverbank in 1967, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were for four decades the oldest known members of our own species.","answer":"Omo remains","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 233,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967, by a team under Richard Leakey</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Omo remains</b> are two early human individuals, Omo I and Omo II, from the Kibish Formation on the lower Omo river in southwestern Ethiopia, and for forty years they were the oldest known members of our own species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A team from the Kenya National Museums under Richard Leakey found them in 1967, as part of a large international expedition working the valley. The two spots are named for the men who found them, Omo I coming from Kamoya's Hominid Site, after Kamoya Kimeu, the fossil hunter who would later find the Turkana Boy. Omo I is much of a skull vault with parts of the face and jaw and a good deal of the skeleton, and it is unmistakably modern, with a rounded braincase and a chin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Omo II is a nearly complete braincase, longer and lower and thicker in the wall, and it looks distinctly archaic, though it comes from beds of much the same age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That contrast is what makes the pair interesting, since two people from one formation refuse to look like a single tidy species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their age has moved three times: shells first suggested about 130,000 years, argon dating of crystals in volcanic ash gave 195,000 in 2005, and in 2022 the ash of a huge eruption lying above Omo I pushed it beyond 233,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each shift mattered because these bones were the yardstick against which the age of our species was measured. Jebel Irhoud in Morocco has since taken the record at about 315,000 years, but Omo I is still the earliest fossil that looks fully modern rather than partly so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nothing about the bones changed across those revisions; what changed was the reading of the rock around them, which is how a fossil can age by 100,000 years without anyone digging up anything new.","citation":"","answerText":"Omo remains","questions":["Made up of a modern-looking Omo I and an archaic Omo II from beds of one age, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stubbornly refuse to look like a single tidy species.","Aged by shells, then by argon in 2005, then beyond 233,000 years by a 2022 eruption above them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew older each time the rock was reread."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","ethiopia"],"sources":["Ian McDougall, Francis H. Brown, and John G. Fleagle, \"Stratigraphic Placement and Age of Modern Humans from Kibish, Ethiopia,\" <i>Nature</i> 433, no. 7027 (2005): 733–36, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature03258. [Paywalled]","Céline M. Vidal et al., \"Age of the Oldest Known <i>Homo sapiens</i> from Eastern Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 601, no. 7894 (2022): 579–83, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8791829/. [Open access]","Simon Neubauer, Jean-Jacques Hublin, and Philipp Gunz, \"The Evolution of Modern Human Brain Shape,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 1 (2018): eaao5961, table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5783678/. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, \"Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., \"Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?\" <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Omo_Kibish_-_MCN_4152_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Omo_Kibish_-_MCN_4152_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Omo remains","desc":"Reproduction du crâne de l'Homme de Kibish. Musée des Civilisations Noires de Dakar (Sénégal). GuillaumeG, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omo_Kibish_-_MCN_4152_(cropped).jpg","alt":"Omo Kibish - MCN 4152"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-045","num":"45","category":"World · History","question":"Not the first woman, not the only woman of her day, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name headline writers gave to the woman every living person descends from through an unbroken chain of mothers.","answer":"Mitochondrial Eve","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200,000 – 150,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1987, by Cann, Stoneking and Wilson</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mitochondrial Eve</b> is the name given to the woman from whom every person alive today descends through an unbroken chain of mothers, daughter after daughter, down to the present. The chain can be traced because mitochondria, the small structures that release energy in a cell, carry a scrap of DNA of their own, and a fertilised egg keeps only the mother's copy. That DNA is never shuffled with a father's either, so it passes down almost unchanged apart from the odd copying error, and those errors accumulate at a rough but measurable rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Comparing the mitochondrial DNA of living people therefore builds a single family tree of mothers, and any such tree, however many branches, must meet at one woman somewhere in the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She is that meeting point, and current estimates put her in Africa roughly 150,000 to 200,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Rebecca Cann, Mark Stoneking and Allan Wilson published the finding in 1987, after reading the mitochondrial DNA of 147 people and seeing that the deepest divisions in the tree all lay within Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their tree was built with a method they applied badly, as critics soon showed, but the two conclusions that mattered survived every better analysis since: the root is African, and the branches are short, which means recent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The nickname was never in the paper, arriving instead with the commentary and the headlines, and a <i>Newsweek</i> cover the following January put a genetic Adam and Eve on newsstands worldwide. It has misled readers ever since, because she was not the first woman, not the only woman of her time, and not everyone's mother in any simple sense, since thousands of her contemporaries also have living descendants, just not through daughters all the way down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor is the title permanently hers, because every time the last woman in some maternal line dies childless the meeting point slides forward, which makes Mitochondrial Eve a moving position rather than a person history could ever name.","citation":"","answerText":"Mitochondrial Eve","questions":["Because a fertilised egg keeps only its mother's mitochondrial DNA, comparing that DNA across 147 people in 1987 let researchers trace <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Africa some 150,000 years ago.","Every time the last woman in some maternal line dies childless, the meeting point slides forward, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a moving position rather than any person history could name."],"tags":["concept","genetics","evolution","science"],"sources":["Rebecca L. Cann, Mark Stoneking, and Allan C. Wilson, \"Mitochondrial DNA and Human Evolution,\" <i>Nature</i> 325, no. 6099 (1987): 31–36, https://doi.org/10.1038/325031a0. [Paywalled]","Candela L. Hernández, \"Mitochondrial DNA in Human Diversity and Health: From the Golden Age to the Omics Era,\" <i>Genes</i> 14, no. 8 (2023): 1534, https://doi.org/10.3390/genes14081534. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu et al., \"A Revised Timescale for Human Evolution Based on Ancient Mitochondrial Genomes,\" <i>Current Biology</i> 23, no. 7 (2013): 553–59, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5036973/. [Open access]","Teresa Rito et al., \"The First Modern Human Dispersals across Africa,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 8, no. 11 (2013): e80031, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0080031. [Open access]","Aaron P. Ragsdale et al., \"A Weakly Structured Stem for Human Origins in Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 617, no. 7962 (2023): 755–63, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06055-y. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/MacaulayRichardsmtDNAtree.jpg","title":"Mitochondrial Eve","desc":"A detailed phylogeny of the human mitochondrial DNA haplogroups. Vincent Macaulay and Martin Richards, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MacaulayRichardsmtDNAtree.jpg","alt":"A phylogenetic tree of human mitochondrial DNA haplogroups"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-046","num":"46","category":"World · History","question":"For roughly 99 percent of the human story, people lived in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the age of chipped-stone tools, hunting and gathering, fire and the first cave art.","answer":"Paleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – 9700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Paleolithic</b>, or <b>Old Stone Age</b>, is the earliest and longest stretch of human prehistory, the first great division of the Stone Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name comes from the Greek for 'old stone', and John Lubbock coined it in 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The age is defined above all by tools of chipped stone, shaped by knapping — the controlled striking of one stone against another to detach sharp flakes — which first appear about 2.6 million years ago, or 3.3 million if disputed finds are counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It ended with the last Ice Age, about 11,700 years ago — spanning, by common reckoning, some 99 percent of the human story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Through all of it people lived by hunting wild game, fishing, and gathering plants, in small nomadic bands sheltering in caves and camps, without farms, herds, or settled villages. <br><br> The age was the work not of one people but of many, its toolmakers ranging from early Homo and the far-roaming Homo erectus to the Neanderthals and, from about 300,000 years ago, our own Homo sapiens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The stoneworking grew steadily finer across that span, from crude Oldowan choppers to the shapely Acheulean hand-axe, the flake tools of the Neanderthals, and at last the slender blades of the late Paleolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its makers mastered fire for warmth, cooking, and safety, and over successive ages spread out of Africa to people almost the whole earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In its later phase came a flowering of the mind — vivid cave paintings of animals, carved figurines, beads, bone flutes, and the deliberate burial of the dead — signs that point to art, belief, and language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> All of this is known to us only through archaeology and fossils, for the Paleolithic lies wholly before writing; it closed when, in several regions, people began to domesticate plants and animals, the turn to farming that opened the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Paleolithic","questions":["Coined in 1865 from the Greek for 'old stone', the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marks the earliest and longest division of the Stone Age, also called the Old Stone Age.","From early Homo and the far-roaming Homo erectus to Neanderthals and our own species, the toolmakers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mastered fire and spread out of Africa."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Li Li et al., “A Synthesis of the Dibble et al. Controlled Experiments into the Mechanics of Lithic Production,” <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 30, no. 4 (2023): 1284–1325, esp. 1290 and 1301–2, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-022-09586-2. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,” <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., “Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,” <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–23, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]","Jean-Jacques Hublin et al., “New Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco and the Pan-African Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,” <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 289–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22336. [Paywalled]","Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]","Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, et al., “Oldest Cave Art Found in Sulawesi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abd4648. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under “The Origins of Domestication,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/api/collection/v1/iiif/754048/1814306/main-image","title":"Nine Acheulean bifaces (700,000–200,000 BCE)","desc":"Suitable for a variety of tasks, including digging, chopping, and butchering, Paleolithic hand axes are the earliest and longest-used kind of tool made by diverse species of hominids, with documented usage over a period of 1.7 million years. These nine examples, which were all discovered within a small geographic area in Aisne, France, are remarkable for their variety and the high quality of their workmanship. They are a potent manifestation of an early and continuous human interest in perfection of form, and aesthetics in general, as demonstrated by their makers' efforts to endow them with symmetrical faces and edges, well beyond practical requirements. The largest bifaces in this group are so substantial that they would have been of little to no use as tools. Although their specific functions remain a matter of speculation, it is clear that they must have been prized for their appearance rather than utility.","credit":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/754048","alt":"Nine flaked stone hand axes of varying size arranged in rows against a plain ground"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-047","num":"47","category":"World · History","question":"The earliest and longest phase of the Old Stone Age, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from the first chipped-stone tools to about 300,000 years ago, defined by the Oldowan chopper and the Acheulean hand-axe.","answer":"Lower Paleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – 300,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lower Paleolithic</b> is the earliest and by far the longest division of the Old Stone Age, the opening chapter in the human record of stone tools, and it alone accounts for nearly nine-tenths of that whole period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It begins with the first chipped-stone tools, conventionally dated to about 2.6 million years ago, and it closes roughly 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Disputed finds from Lomekwi in Kenya may push toolmaking back toward 3.3 million years ago, so the opening date is a convention rather than a fixed boundary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word Paleolithic comes from the Greek for 'old stone' and was coined by John Lubbock in 1865; prehistorians later divided its immense span into a Lower, a Middle and an Upper phase, this being the lowest and oldest of the three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Across the whole of it people lived as small, mobile bands of hunter-gatherers, without farms, villages, pottery, metal or writing of any kind. <br><br> The older of its two great toolkits is the Oldowan, named for Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania, where it was first recognised, and usually credited to Homo habilis, an early human who struck sharp flakes from simple pebble cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The later Acheulean, named in the same way for Saint-Acheul in France, produced the symmetrical teardrop hand-axe, one of the longest-lived tool designs ever made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Homo erectus above all carried that design out of Africa, and its fossils at Dmanisi in Georgia are the oldest yet found beyond the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Whether these early humans could control fire is still genuinely uncertain, and widely accepted traces of hearths appear only in the later part of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The age ends not with a sudden break but with a new skill: shaping a core in advance so that a flake of planned size and shape breaks away, the prepared-core method that opens the Middle Paleolithic and the world of the Neanderthals.","citation":"","answerText":"Lower Paleolithic","questions":["It was Homo erectus who carried the teardrop hand-axe of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of Africa, leaving at Dmanisi in Georgia the oldest human fossils yet found beyond the continent.","The older Oldowan toolkit, credited to Homo habilis, gave way within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Acheulean hand-axe, one of the longest-lived tool designs ever made."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,” <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá, “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools and Butchery Traces? More Evidence Needed,” <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2016 (2016): 46–53, esp. 49 and 51, https://paleoanthro.org/media/journal/content/PA20160046.pdf. [Open access]","John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","David Lordkipanidze, Marcia S. Ponce de León, Ann Margvelashvili, et al., “A Complete Skull from Dmanisi, Georgia, and the Evolutionary Biology of Early <i>Homo</i>,” <i>Science</i> 342, no. 6156 (2013): 326–31, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1238484. [Paywalled]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Museum_of_Anatolian_Civilizations_Lower_Paleolithic_finds_in_2014_03.jpg/1920px-Museum_of_Anatolian_Civilizations_Lower_Paleolithic_finds_in_2014_03.jpg","title":"Lower Paleolithic","desc":"Tools in several varieties. From Lower Paleolithic, 1.000.000-200.000 BCE Excavated in Dursunlu (Konya province), which is the oldest dated archeological site in Turkey (up to approximately 900.000 BCE). Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museum_of_Anatolian_Civilizations_Lower_Paleolithic_finds_in_2014_03.jpg","alt":"Museum of Anatolian Civilizations Lower Paleolithic finds in 2014 03"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-048","num":"48","category":"World · History","question":"Between the age of the hand-axe and the age of the cave painters, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the middle stretch of the Old Stone Age, the time of the Neanderthals.","answer":"Middle Paleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 50,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Middle Paleolithic</b> is the central of the three divisions of the Old Stone Age, the long chapter between the earlier hand-axe cultures and the later blade-and-art ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the usual convention it ran from about 300,000 to 50,000 years ago, and the Neanderthals were gone from Europe by about 40,000, though both edges are gradual and shift from region to region rather than marking any single moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its opening is defined by a technique rather than a date: the prepared-core or Levallois method, in which a knapper, a stoneworker striking flakes from stone, shapes a core in advance so that a flake of planned size and shape comes off in one blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> This lighter, standardised kit replaced the older Acheulean hand-axe, and its flakes could be fitted onto shafts to make composite tools such as stone-tipped spears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Europe and western Asia this is above all the world of the Neanderthals, an early kind of human whose toolkit, rich in scrapers and points, is called Mousterian after the rock-shelter of Le Moustier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"11\"></sup> <br><br> Yet the Neanderthals were not alone: Denisovans ranged across Asia, and early members of our own species, Homo sapiens, met Neanderthals in the Near East at Skhul and Qafzeh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Because both used the same Levallois and Mousterian methods, the tools alone rarely reveal which kind of human made them, and Africa's parallel record, which belongs to Homo sapiens, carries its own name, the Middle Stone Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> That African record holds the earliest strong hints of symbolic life, among them ochre engraved with patterns and pierced shell beads from Blombos Cave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Firm evidence shows these populations mastered fire and cooking, hunted large game, and glued stone points to shafts with tar, though deliberate burial, art and ritual remain debated and far less certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Upper Paleolithic follows from about 50,000 years ago, overlapping Europe's last Neanderthals by several thousand years; the famous figurative cave paintings belong to that later age, not to this one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Middle Paleolithic","questions":["Opening with the Levallois method of shaping a core in advance, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from about 300,000 to 50,000 years ago and produced the Mousterian toolkit of scrapers and points.","In Africa the parallel record of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, known there as the Middle Stone Age, holds the earliest hints of symbolic life, such as the engraved ochre of Blombos Cave."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Christopher S. Henshilwood et al., “Emergence of Modern Human Behavior: Middle Stone Age Engravings from South Africa,” <i>Science</i> 295, no. 5558 (2002): 1278–80, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1067575. [Paywalled]","Lyn Wadley, Tamaryn Hodgskiss, and Michael Grant, “Implications for Complex Cognition from the Hafting of Tools with Compound Adhesives in the Middle Stone Age, South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 24 (2009): 9590–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2700998/. [Open access]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]","Francesco d’Errico and Chris B. Stringer, “Evolution, Revolution or Saltation Scenario for the Emergence of Modern Cultures?,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 366, no. 1567 (2011): 1060–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3049097/. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren and Stephen J. Lycett, “Why Levallois? A Morphometric Comparison of Experimental ‘Preferential’ Levallois Flakes versus Debitage Flakes,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 1 (2012): e29273, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3264556/. [Open access]","Tom Higham et al., “The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Jean-Jacques Hublin et al., “New Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco and the Pan-African Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,” <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 289–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22336. [Paywalled]","Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Chris Stringer, and Michael D. Petraglia, “Skhul Lithic Technology and the Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i> into Southwest Asia,” <i>Quaternary International</i> 515 (2019): 30–52, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:26de242d-8c79-44a8-9dea-5631a7400664. [Open access]","Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, et al., “Oldest Cave Art Found in Sulawesi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abd4648. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Xiujie Wu, “Making Sense of Eastern Asian Late Quaternary Hominin Variability,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9479, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11531466/. [Open access]","Patrick Schmidt, Radu Iovita, Armelle Charrié-Duhaut, Gunther Möller, Abay Namen, and Ewa Dutkiewicz, “Ochre-Based Compound Adhesives at the Mousterian Type-Site Document Complex Cognition and High Investment,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 8 (2024): eadl0822, https://hal.science/hal-04484835v1/document. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Stone_tools%2C_Neanderthal%2C_Bad_Urach%2C_Wittlingen%2C_c._50%2C000_to_70%2C000_years_old_-_Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_-_Stuttgart%2C_Germany_-_DSC02685.jpg/1920px-Stone_tools%2C_Neanderthal%2C_Bad_Urach%2C_Wittlingen%2C_c._50%2C000_to_70%2C000_years_old_-_Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_-_Stuttgart%2C_Germany_-_DSC02685.jpg","title":"Middle Paleolithic","desc":"Stone tools, Neanderthal, Bad Urach, Wittlingen, c. 50,000 to 70,000 years old - Landesmuseum Württemberg - Stuttgart, Germany. Daderot. 2015-12-06 05:46:54. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stone_tools,_Neanderthal,_Bad_Urach,_Wittlingen,_c._50,000_to_70,000_years_old_-_Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_-_Stuttgart,_Germany_-_DSC02685.jpg","alt":"Stone tools, Neanderthal, Bad Urach, Wittlingen, c. 50,000 to 70,000 years old - Landesmuseum Württemberg - Stuttgart, Germany"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-049","num":"49","category":"World · History","question":"The last of the three divisions of the Old Stone Age, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the age when modern humans spread across Eurasia, struck slender blade tools and left a burst of animal art.","answer":"Upper Paleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 50,000 – 11,700 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Upper Paleolithic</b> is the third and final division of the Old Stone Age, following the Middle Paleolithic. By the usual convention it ran from about 50,000 to 11,700 years ago, ending as the last Ice Age did, though both edges are gradual and shift from region to region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is above all the age of modern humans, Homo sapiens, who spread across Eurasia and on into Australia and, later, the Americas; the Neanderthals, an older kind of human with whom they overlapped and interbred, faded from the record around 40,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its technological signature is the blade, a long, straight-edged flake struck in series from a carefully prepared core, which yielded far more cutting edge from each lump of stone than earlier methods. Alongside such blades came a rich craft in bone, antler, and ivory — eyed needles for sewn, fitted clothing, barbed harpoons, and the spear-thrower that launched darts with new force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The period is famous for its wealth of art, ornament, music, and elaborate burial, a flowering once cast as a sudden \"creative explosion\" unique to Europe. Most researchers now doubt that reading, arguing that such art and symbols built up slowly, and far earlier, in Africa. The oldest known cave paintings of real animals come not from Europe but from the Indonesian island of Sulawesi, at least 45,500 years old, with a narrative scene nearby dated to 51,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Europe's own record is nonetheless spectacular, from the ivory figurines and bone flutes of the Swabian caves to the painted animals of Chauvet, Lascaux, and Altamira and the ivory-bead burials of Sungir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The cultures used to order it — Aurignacian, Gravettian, Solutrean, Magdalenian — form a Western European scheme rather than a worldwide one; when the glaciers at last drew back, the Upper Paleolithic gave way to the Mesolithic and, in time, to farming.","citation":"","answerText":"Upper Paleolithic","questions":["The eyed needle, the barbed harpoon and the spear-thrower all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when Homo sapiens worked bone, antler and ivory alongside slender stone blades.","The painted caves of Chauvet, Lascaux and Altamira date from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the oldest known animal paintings come earlier still, from the Indonesian island of Sulawesi."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Mike Walker et al., “Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,” <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–23, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]","Tom Higham et al., “The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Ian Gilligan, Francesco d’Errico, Luc Doyon, Wei Wang, and Yaroslav V. Kuzmin, “Paleolithic Eyed Needles and the Evolution of Dress,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 26 (2024): eadp2887, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11212769/. [Open access]","Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, et al., “Oldest Cave Art Found in Sulawesi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abd4648. [Open access]","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim et al., “Narrative Cave Art in Indonesia by 51,200 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 631 (2024): 814–18, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07541-7. [Open access]","Harald Floss, “The Oldest Portable Art: The Aurignacian Ivory Figurines from the Swabian Jura (Southwest Germany),” <i>Palethnologie</i> 7 (2015), paras. 7 and 13, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.888. [Open access]","Anita Quiles, Hélène Valladas, Hervé Bocherens et al., “A High-Precision Chronological Model for the Decorated Upper Paleolithic Cave of Chauvet-Pont d’Arc, Ardèche, France,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 17 (2016): 4670–75, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1523158113. [Open access]","Erik Trinkaus and Alexandra P. Buzhilova, “Diversity and Differential Disposal of the Dead at Sunghir,” <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 361 (2018): 7–21, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.223. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/Venus_de_Brassempouy.jpg","title":"Upper Paleolithic","desc":"Venus of Brassempouy, photographed at the Musée d'Archéologie Nationale. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_de_Brassempouy.jpg","alt":"Venus de Brassempouy"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-050","num":"50","category":"World · History","question":"The geological epoch of the great ice ages, running from about 2.58 million to 11,700 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> saw ice sheets advance and retreat dozens of times.","answer":"Pleistocene","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Epoch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2.58 Mya – 11,700 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Pleistocene</b> is a geological epoch, a formally defined slice of deep time within the Quaternary Period of the Cenozoic Era. It ran from roughly 2.58 million years ago to about 11,700 years ago, when the warmer Holocene, our current epoch, began. Its start was pushed back from about 1.8 million years ago to 2.58 million only in 2009, so that the epoch now opens near the onset of major Northern Hemisphere glaciation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crucially, it is defined by the planet's climate and rock record rather than by anything people did, and its boundaries are fixed by 'golden spikes' in Sicilian strata and a Greenland ice core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> This makes it a different kind of period from the archaeological Paleolithic, or Old Stone Age, which is defined by human stone tools; though the two overlap across almost the same vast span of time, their boundaries do not line up. <br><br> It is popularly called the Ice Age, but that name flattens the picture: the epoch held dozens of swings between cold glacial phases, when vast ice sheets spread, and warm interglacials like today. These swings were paced by the Milankovitch cycles, slow and regular changes in Earth's orbit and the tilt of its axis that alter how sunlight falls across the globe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the ice was at its greatest, so much water was locked up that the sea fell by as much as 130 metres (430 feet), opening land bridges such as Beringia between Siberia and Alaska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of human evolution unfolded within it, for Homo erectus, the Neanderthals, the Denisovans and our own species all lived through the epoch, and humans first crossed such bridges into Australia and the Americas, though the exact dates are debated. It was also the age of the megafauna, from woolly mammoths to sabre-toothed cats, most of which died out near its close for reasons — climate, human hunting, or both — that scholars still dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pleistocene","questions":["Defined by the rock and climate record rather than by human tools, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is fixed by golden spikes in Sicilian strata and a Greenland ice core.","When ice locked up the seas during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the water fell as much as 120 metres (390 feet) and opened land bridges such as Beringia between Siberia and Alaska."],"tags":["era","geology","science","nature"],"sources":["Philip L. Gibbard and Martin J. Head, \"The Newly-Ratified Definition of the Quaternary System/Period and Redefinition of the Pleistocene Series/Epoch, and Comparison of Proposals Advanced Prior to Formal Ratification,\" <i>Episodes</i> 33, no. 3 (2010): 152–158, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2010/v33i3/002. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Definition and Dating of the GSSP (Global Stratotype Section and Point) for the Base of the Holocene Using the Greenland NGRIP Ice Core, and Selected Auxiliary Records,\" <i>Journal of Quaternary Science</i> 24, no. 1 (2009): 3–17, https://timescalefoundation.org/references/HoloceneGSSP_JQuatSci090.pdf. [Open access]","J. D. Hays, John Imbrie, and N. J. Shackleton, \"Variations in the Earth’s Orbit: Pacemaker of the Ice Ages,\" <i>Science</i> 194, no. 4270 (1976): 1121–32, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.194.4270.1121. [Paywalled]","Rachel M. Spratt and Lorraine E. Lisiecki, \"A Late Pleistocene Sea Level Stack,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 12, no. 4 (2016): 1079–92, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-12-1079-2016. [Open access]","Martin Jakobsson et al., \"Post-Glacial Flooding of the Bering Land Bridge Dated to 11 cal ka BP Based on New Geophysical and Sediment Records,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 13, no. 8 (2017): 991–1005, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-13-991-2017. [Open access]","Jens-Christian Svenning et al., \"The Late-Quaternary Megafauna Extinctions: Patterns, Causes, Ecological Consequences and Implications for Ecosystem Management in the Anthropocene,\" <i>Cambridge Prisms: Extinction</i> 2 (2024): e5, https://doi.org/10.1017/ext.2024.4. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/EarlyPleistoceneAnimals.png","title":"Pleistocene","desc":"Animals of the early Pleistocene, from an illustration of 1921. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EarlyPleistoceneAnimals.png","alt":"EarlyPleistoceneAnimals"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-051","num":"51","category":"World · History","question":"Rather than a slice of time, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a condition in which Earth keeps permanent ice sheets on land — and we are in one right now.","answer":"Ice age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Quaternary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2.6 Mya</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>ice age</b> is not a slice of time but a climate condition — a long interval, usually lasting millions of years, during which Earth keeps permanent ice sheets on its land. The term carries two meanings that are easily muddled: to scientists it is that whole ice-sheet interval, while in everyday speech it means a single cold spell, the mammoth world of the most recent glaciation. Within one, the climate swings between colder glacial phases, when the ice advances, and warmer interglacial ones, when it retreats, as in our own time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The counterintuitive result is that we are still in an ice age today, the Quaternary, because permanent ice still caps Antarctica and Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Our comfortable Holocene is simply its current interglacial, a warm pause rather than the finish line, which is what sets an ice age apart from a named geological epoch like the Pleistocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Earth has passed through several such intervals since at least 2.4 billion years ago, the most dramatic being the Cryogenian, roughly 720 to 635 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During it the ice may have reached almost to the equator, an idea known as 'Snowball Earth', though how completely the planet froze is still debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What switches an ice age on works over millions of years: the drifting of continents into polar positions, shifting ocean currents, and a fall in the carbon dioxide that traps the Sun's heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Milankovitch cycles, slow changes in Earth's orbit and tilt, only set the beat of the glacials within an ice age, being far too weak to start one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The famous Ice Age of popular imagination was merely the latest glacial, peaking about 20,000 years ago, when great ice sheets buried the northern lands and the sea fell by roughly 130 metres (430 feet), opening land bridges such as Beringia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ice age","questions":["Earth has passed through at least five, the fiercest being the Cryogenian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 720 to 635 million years ago, when ice may have reached almost to the equator.","Drifting continents, shifting currents and falling carbon dioxide switch an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on over millions of years, while the Milankovitch cycles only pace the cold spells within it."],"tags":["era","geology","science","nature","climate"],"sources":["Past Interglacials Working Group of PAGES, \"Interglacials of the Last 800,000 Years,\" <i>Reviews of Geophysics</i> 54, no. 1 (2016): 162–219, under \"Defining Interglacials,\" https://hal.science/hal-03245836. [Open access]","Paul F. Hoffman et al., \"Snowball Earth Climate Dynamics and Cryogenian Geology-Geobiology,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 11 (2017): e1600983, abstract and table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5677351/. [Open access]","Rachel M. Spratt and Lorraine E. Lisiecki, \"A Late Pleistocene Sea Level Stack,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 12, no. 4 (2016): 1079–92, esp. 1080, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-12-1079-2016. [Open access]","James Hansen et al., \"Climate Sensitivity, Sea Level and Atmospheric Carbon Dioxide,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 371, no. 2001 (2013): 20120294, under \"Cenozoic Climate Change,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3785813/. [Open access]","Philip L. Gibbard and Martin J. Head, \"The Newly-Ratified Definition of the Quaternary System/Period and Redefinition of the Pleistocene Series/Epoch, and Comparison of Proposals Advanced Prior to Formal Ratification,\" <i>Episodes</i> 33, no. 3 (2010): 152–58, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2010/v33i3/002. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Ice_Age_Human_Footprints_%28a45c5031-d519-43f0-8d64-7b81d653c3ff%29.JPG/1920px-Ice_Age_Human_Footprints_%28a45c5031-d519-43f0-8d64-7b81d653c3ff%29.JPG","title":"Ice age","desc":"Seven human footprints preserved in Ice Age gypsum cement. Ice Age footprints from adolescemt female estimated to 23,000-21,000 years ago. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ice_Age_Human_Footprints_(a45c5031-d519-43f0-8d64-7b81d653c3ff).JPG","alt":"Ice Age Human Footprints (a45c5031-d519-43f0-8d64-7b81d653c3ff)"},"difficulty":1,"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-052","num":"52","category":"World · History","question":"The most recent great cold of the current ice age and the world of mammoths, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what popular culture simply calls 'the Ice Age'.","answer":"Last Glacial Period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">115,000 – 11,700 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Last Glacial Period</b> was the most recent cold stage of the current ice age — the specific span, roughly 115,000 to 11,700 years ago, that popular culture loosely calls 'the Ice Age'. Sitting within the Late Pleistocene, it followed the last warm interglacial and reached its frozen peak, the Last Glacial Maximum, around 20,000 years ago. Geologists give this one period a different name in each region — the Würm in the Alps, the Weichselian in northern Europe, the Wisconsin in North America, the Devensian in Britain — yet all describe the same glacial. At its coldest, great ice sheets buried roughly a third of the land: the vast Laurentide over Canada, the Fennoscandian over northern Europe, and smaller ones over the mountains and islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So much water was locked into ice that the sea fell by some 130 metres (430 feet), laying bare land bridges such as Beringia between Siberia and Alaska, and Doggerland between Britain and mainland Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Across the cold, dry, dusty north spread the grassy 'mammoth steppe', grazed by woolly mammoths, woolly rhinoceroses, cave bears and giant deer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For people this was the Upper Palaeolithic: modern humans spread across the planet, and it was in these millennia that they painted the caves at Chauvet, some 36,000 years ago, and Lascaux, about 17,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Crossing the newly exposed land, they reached Australia and, later, the Americas, though exactly when they first arrived in each is still debated. From about 20,000 years ago the ice began to melt and the seas to rise, but the thaw was jolted by the Younger Dryas, a sharp return to near-glacial cold roughly 12,900 to 11,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The period finally closed about 11,700 years ago as the world warmed into our own Holocene, a transition that also saw many of the great cold-loving animals die out, for reasons scholars still dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Last Glacial Period","questions":["Known as the Würm in the Alps, the Weichselian in northern Europe and the Wisconsin in North America, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached its frozen peak about 20,000 years ago.","As modern humans painted Chauvet and Lascaux and crossed into Australia and the Americas, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> laid bare land bridges like Beringia and Doggerland."],"tags":["era","geology","science","climate"],"sources":["Christine L. Batchelor et al., \"The Configuration of Northern Hemisphere Ice Sheets through the Quaternary,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 10 (2019): 3713, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-019-11601-2. [Open access]","Rachel M. Spratt and Lorraine E. Lisiecki, \"A Late Pleistocene Sea Level Stack,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 12, no. 4 (2016): 1079–92, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-12-1079-2016. [Open access]","Anita Quiles et al., \"A High-Precision Chronological Model for the Decorated Upper Paleolithic Cave of Chauvet-Pont d’Arc, Ardèche, France,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 17 (2016): 4670–75, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1523158113. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Definition and Dating of the GSSP (Global Stratotype Section and Point) for the Base of the Holocene Using the Greenland NGRIP Ice Core, and Selected Auxiliary Records,\" <i>Journal of Quaternary Science</i> 24, no. 1 (2009): 3–17, https://timescalefoundation.org/references/HoloceneGSSP_JQuatSci090.pdf. [Open access]","Yucheng Wang et al., \"Late Quaternary Dynamics of Arctic Biota from Ancient Environmental Genomics,\" <i>Nature</i> 600, no. 7887 (2021): 86–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04016-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Global_sea_levels_and_vegetation_during_the_last_Ice_Age_%28South_Asia%29.jpg","title":"Last Glacial Period","desc":"Global sea levels and vegetation during the last Ice Age (South Asia). Sea levels: National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration Vegetation: Locoluis. 2020-03-22. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Global_sea_levels_and_vegetation_during_the_last_Ice_Age_(South_Asia).jpg","alt":"Global sea levels and vegetation during the last Ice Age (South Asia)"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-053","num":"53","category":"World · History","question":"With ice nearly four kilometres (2.5 miles) thick over Canada and the sea some 125 metres (410 feet) lower, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of roughly 21,000 years ago left the planet colder, drier and dustier than now.","answer":"Last Glacial Maximum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 26,000 – 19,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ice greatest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 21,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Last Glacial Maximum</b> is the coldest phase of the last Ice Age, running from roughly 26,000 to 19,000 years ago, with the world's ice at its greatest volume about 21,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ice sheets buried northern North America, Scandinavia and the northern half of Britain, and the Laurentide sheet over what is now Canada stood almost 4 kilometres (2.5 miles) thick at its dome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Locking that much water into ice pulled the sea about 125 metres (410 feet) below its present level, so the continental shelves emerged as dry land and Beringia joined Siberia to Alaska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Global temperatures averaged about 6 degrees Celsius colder than today, and atmospheric carbon dioxide fell to roughly 190 parts per million, the lowest of the entire glacial cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A cold world is also a dry one, since colder air holds less moisture, so deserts spread, forests shrank into pockets, and the dust off the bare ground still shows in ice cores as a dirty band. <br><br> For people the effect was a squeeze, and northern Europe emptied as its inhabitants contracted into southern refugia in Iberia, Italy and the Balkans. The Cantabrian coast of northern Spain was one of the richest of these, and the Solutrean toolmakers belong to exactly this window, which is why fitted clothing and the eyed needle matter as much as their stone points. Ancient DNA from a man buried in southern Iberia about 23,000 years ago links the people of western Europe before the cold peak to those who came after it, which is the sort of evidence a refugium predicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The retreat began around 19,000 years ago as the Earth's orbit turned the sunlight back up, and meltwater raised the sea over the following ten thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Doggerland was drowned by about 8,000 years ago, and the coastlines we take for granted are the recent product of that thaw rather than anything permanent.","citation":"","answerText":"Last Glacial Maximum","questions":["Northern Europe emptied of people during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose survivors crowded south into refuges in Iberia, Italy and the Balkans until the ice began pulling back.","By dropping atmospheric carbon dioxide to about 180 parts per million and baring the continental shelves, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned Beringia into dry land linking Siberia to Alaska."],"tags":["era","geology","science","nature","climate"],"sources":["Peter U. Clark et al., \"The Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Science</i> 325, no. 5941 (2009): 710–14, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1172873. [Paywalled]","Daniel Moreno-Parada et al., \"Simulating the Laurentide Ice Sheet of the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>The Cryosphere</i> 17, no. 6 (2023): 2139–56, https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-17-2139-2023. [Open access]","Jessica E. Tierney et al., \"Glacial Cooling and Climate Sensitivity Revisited\" (preprint), <i>EarthArXiv</i> (2019), https://doi.org/10.31223/osf.io/me5uj. [Open access]","Bernhard Bereiter et al., \"Revision of the EPICA Dome C CO2 Record from 800 to 600 kyr before Present,\" <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 42, no. 2 (2015): 542–49, Antarctic composite CO2 data at https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/pub/data/paleo/icecore/antarctica/antarctica2015co2composite.txt. [Open access]","Vanessa Villalba-Mouco et al., \"A 23,000-Year-Old Southern Iberian Individual Links Human Groups That Lived in Western Europe before and after the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 7 (2023): 597–609, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10089921/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Aridity_ice_age_vs_early_holocene_vs_modern.jpg","title":"Last Glacial Maximum","desc":"During the Last Glacial Maximum 14000 years ago, thermal-driven evaporation from the oceans onto continental landmasses was low, causing large areas of extreme desert, including polar deserts (cold but with low rates of precipitation). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aridity_ice_age_vs_early_holocene_vs_modern.jpg","alt":"Aridity ice age vs early holocene vs modern"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-054","num":"54","category":"World · History","question":"Living on wild food rather than crops or herds, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> way of life was the only one humans knew until farming began about 12,000 years ago.","answer":"Hunter-gatherer","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Only way of life</span><span class=\"dt-v\">until c. 12,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Hunter-gatherers</b>, also called foragers, live on wild food — hunting, fishing and gathering plants — rather than growing crops or keeping herds. The term names a subsistence strategy rather than a period of the past: some peoples were still living this way in the 20th century, and a few do today. Until farming began about 12,000 years ago, every human on earth ate this way, which covers almost the whole of the human story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There was never a single forager diet, since what a group ate followed what its own landscape offered: Arctic peoples lived almost entirely on animals, while in warm places gathered plants supplied most of the food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The familiar picture is a small nomadic band of a few dozen people, moving camp with the seasons, sharing what they bring in rather than storing it up, and owning little beyond what can be carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That is only one form the life took: on the Pacific Northwest Coast, the Tlingit and Haida built large permanent villages of cedar plank houses. They grew no crops, living instead on salmon they caught, dried and stored in bulk, and their societies were steeply ranked, with noble titles inherited within families and slaves held as property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So permanent villages, stored food and sharp differences of rank did not need farming to arise; on several continents they grew up among peoples who lived entirely on wild food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Peoples who still forage today, such as the Hadza and the San, are modern people living modern lives, and they are not a window into the deep past. Most live on land nobody else wanted, and they trade, work for wages and go to school like anyone else.","citation":"","answerText":"Hunter-gatherer","questions":["On the Pacific Northwest Coast the Tlingit and Haida built permanent plank-house villages on stored salmon, showing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> life could raise ranked societies without any farming.","Peoples who still forage today, such as the Hadza and San, follow a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> economy yet are modern people, not a living window into the deep past."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["Greger Larson et al., \"Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under \"The Origins of Domestication,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Dan Zhu, Eric D. Galbraith, Victoria Reyes-García, and Philippe Ciais, \"Global Hunter-Gatherer Population Densities Constrained by Influence of Seasonality on Diet Composition,\" <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 5, no. 11 (2021): 1536–45, under \"Seasonality Constrains Diet Composition,\" https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC7611941. [Open access]","Eric Alden Smith and Brian F. Codding, \"Ecological Variation and Institutionalized Inequality in Hunter-Gatherer Societies,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 13 (2021): e2016134118, under \"Introduction\" and \"Discussion,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8020663/. [Open access]","Hannah M. Lewis, Lucio Vinicius, Janis Strods, Ruth Mace, and Andrea Bamberg Migliano, \"High Mobility Explains Demand Sharing and Enforced Cooperation in Egalitarian Hunter-Gatherers,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 5 (2014): 5789, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms6789. [Open access]","Andrea B. Migliano et al., \"Hunter-Gatherer Multilevel Sociality Accelerates Cumulative Cultural Evolution,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 9 (2020): eaax5913, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aax5913. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Kalina_hunter_gatherer.jpg","title":"Hunter-gatherer","desc":"A Kali'na hunter with a woman gatherer. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalina_hunter_gatherer.jpg","alt":"Kalina hunter gatherer"},"difficulty":1,"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-055","num":"55","category":"World · History","question":"Named in the 1920s because Europe's labels fitted the African record badly, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the long middle chapter of the African Stone Age, belonging to our own species.","answer":"Middle Stone Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1928, by John Goodwin</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Middle Stone Age</b> is the middle division of the African Stone Age, running from roughly 300,000 until about 40,000 years ago, though both edges are gradual and shift from region to region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name goes back to a 1928 paper by the archaeologist John Goodwin, elaborated the next year in <i>The Stone Age Cultures of South Africa</i>, his joint work with the engineer Clarence van Riet Lowe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That book laid out an Earlier, a Middle and a Late Stone Age for the continent, in place of early schemes that leaned on European frameworks and imported French terminology wholesale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meeting at Livingstone in 1955, the Third Pan-African Congress on Prehistory spent over three hours framing a resolution that pressed the scheme on the whole continent, and carried it against two votes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Despite the name it is not the Mesolithic, a word Goodwin's term was chosen to stand apart from; in time it corresponds roughly to the Middle Paleolithic of Europe and Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The record is ours: the oldest known fossils of Homo sapiens, from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, lay among one of the earliest directly dated Middle Stone Age toolkits, about 315,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its toolmakers worked by the prepared-core method, shaping a block so that a flake of planned size and edge came away in one blow, then trimming those flakes into points and scrapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two southern African phases show how quickly things could move: the Still Bay, dated to a window between about 71,900 and 71,000 years ago, and the Howiesons Poort that followed between 64,800 and 59,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Still Bay layers at Blombos Cave hold the earliest accepted signs of symbolic life, among them pierced shell beads and two pieces of ochre engraved with crosshatched patterns, dated to about 75,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From about 40,000 years ago the Late Stone Age takes over, its earliest phase still mixing prepared cores with newer bipolar and microlithic work, the fully microlithic industries arriving thousands of years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Middle Stone Age","questions":["Set out in 1929 by John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded the oldest known Homo sapiens fossils, from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, about 315,000 years old.","The Blombos layers, roughly 75,000 years old, hold the earliest widely accepted signs of symbolic life, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries pierced shell beads and crosshatched ochre."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","africa"],"sources":["Emily Hallinan, “The Southern African Stone Age Site Index (SASSI): A Spatial, Chronological and Contextual Resource for Archaeological Research,” <i>Journal of Open Archaeology Data</i> 14 (2026): 2, under “Overview” and table 1, https://doi.org/10.5334/joad.186. [Open access]","B. D. Malan, “The Term ‘Middle Stone Age’,” in <i>Third Pan-African Congress on Prehistory, Livingstone 1955</i>, ed. J. Desmond Clark and Sonia Cole (London: Chatto and Windus, 1957), 223–27, https://archive.org/details/in.gov.ignca.6340. [Open access]","“Resolutions of the Congress,” in <i>Third Pan-African Congress on Prehistory, Livingstone 1955</i>, ed. J. Desmond Clark and Sonia Cole (London: Chatto and Windus, 1957), resolution 6 and the closing session, https://archive.org/details/in.gov.ignca.6340. [Open access]","Daniel Richter et al., “The Age of the Hominin Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco, and the Origins of the Middle Stone Age,” <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 293–96, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22335. [Paywalled]","Zenobia Jacobs et al., “Ages for the Middle Stone Age of Southern Africa: Implications for Human Behavior and Dispersal,” <i>Science</i> 322, no. 5902 (2008): 733–35, esp. 733, https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/bitstreams/3e6b212a-1edc-45c8-a0b6-067293fb544c/download. [Open access]","Abdeljalil Bouzouggar, Nick Barton, Marian Vanhaeren, et al., “82,000-Year-Old Shell Beads from North Africa and Implications for the Origins of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 24 (2007): 9964–69, esp. 9964, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1891266/. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren and Stephen J. Lycett, “Why Levallois? A Morphometric Comparison of Experimental ‘Preferential’ Levallois Flakes versus Debitage Flakes,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 1 (2012): e29273, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3264556/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Blombos_point.JPG","title":"Middle Stone Age","desc":"Bifacial silcrete point from M1 phase (71,000 BCE) layer of Blombos Cave, South Africa; scale bar = 5 cm. Vincent Mourre / Inrap, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blombos_point.JPG","alt":"Blombos point"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-057","num":"57","category":"World · History","question":"Scraped from iron-rich earth and in use from at least 300,000 years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the red and yellow pigment that colours painted cave walls and Ice Age burials.","answer":"Ochre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from at least c. 300,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ochre</b> is a natural earth coloured by iron oxide, running from pale yellow through orange to deep blood red, and the oldest pigment people are known to have used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its red comes from the mineral hematite and its yellow from goethite, mixed in the ground with clay and sand, so a piece of ochre is a rock rather than a made paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It forms wherever iron-rich rock weathers, so it lies almost everywhere, and it can be rubbed on like a crayon or crushed to powder on a grindstone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Heating yellow ochre drives the water out of the goethite and turns it red, and burnt crumbs at some Stone Age sites are read as people doing that on purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ground fine and worked into fat, marrow, plant gum or water, it yields a paint that grips skin, hide, bone and stone and holds its colour for tens of thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Lumps carried into caves and scraped for powder appear by about 300,000 years ago at Twin Rivers in Zambia and in the Kapthurin beds of Kenya, and claims for older use are argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Blombos Cave in South Africa a paste of ochre, bone, charcoal and fat was ground and stored in two abalone shells about 100,000 years ago, and lumps engraved with cross-hatched patterns lie in its 77,000-year-old layers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the ochre in a Stone Age cave, though, was probably working material: powdered into resin it makes a stronger glue for setting a stone point into a shaft, it helps in tanning hides, and it may have guarded skin against sun and insects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Archaeologists therefore disagree about how much of it stands for art or identity and how much is workshop debris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What is harder to explain away is the red powder heaped over the dead, in Ice Age Europe and in many later societies, and the fact that people carried good ochre tens of kilometres when duller earth lay closer to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ochre","questions":["Ground with bone, charcoal and fat, a paste of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was mixed and stored in two abalone shells at Blombos Cave in South Africa about 100,000 years ago.","Powdered into tree resin, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made a stronger glue for setting a stone point into its shaft, so much of it in a cave was workshop debris rather than art."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","geology"],"sources":["Christopher S. Henshilwood et al., “Emergence of Modern Human Behavior: Middle Stone Age Engravings from South Africa,” <i>Science</i> 295, no. 5558 (2002): 1278–80, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1067575. [Paywalled]","Lyn Wadley, Tamaryn Hodgskiss, and Michael Grant, “Implications for Complex Cognition from the Hafting of Tools with Compound Adhesives in the Middle Stone Age, South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 24 (2009): 9590–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2700998/. [Open access]","Christopher S. Henshilwood et al., “A 100,000-Year-Old Ochre-Processing Workshop at Blombos Cave, South Africa,” <i>Science</i> 334, no. 6053 (2011): 219–22, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1211535. [Paywalled]","Paola Villa et al., “A Milk and Ochre Paint Mixture Used 49,000 Years Ago at Sibudu, South Africa,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 6 (2015): e0131273, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4488428/. [Open access]","Daniela Eugenia Rosso, Francesco d’Errico, and Alain Queffelec, “Patterns of Change and Continuity in Ochre Use during the Late Middle Stone Age of the Horn of Africa: The Porc-Epic Cave Record,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0177298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5443497/. [Open access]","Rimtautas Dapschauskas, Matthias B. Göden, Christian Sommer, and Andrew W. Kandel, “The Emergence of Habitual Ochre Use in Africa and Its Significance for the Development of Ritual Behavior during the Middle Stone Age,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 35, nos. 3–4 (2022): 233–319, https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10963-022-09170-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Links_gebrannte_Siena%2C_rechts_nat%C3%BCrliche.JPG/1920px-Links_gebrannte_Siena%2C_rechts_nat%C3%BCrliche.JPG","title":"Ochre","desc":"Burnt sienna (darker) and raw sienna (paler). Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Links_gebrannte_Siena,_rechts_nat%C3%BCrliche.JPG","alt":"Links gebrannte Siena, rechts natürliche"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-058","num":"58","category":"World · History","question":"Dug into an old sea cliff above the Indian Ocean, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has given up engraved ochre and shell beads, pushing symbolic behaviour back beyond 70,000 years ago.","answer":"Blombos Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 101,000 – 71,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1991, by Christopher Henshilwood</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Blombos Cave</b> is a small cave cut into an ancient wave-cut cliff on the southern Cape coast of South Africa, standing 34.5 metres (113 feet) above the sea and barely 100 metres (330 feet) from the Indian Ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reaches only six or seven metres (20 to 23 feet) from the drip line to the back wall, yet the excavated Middle Stone Age sequence beneath its floor runs more than four metres (13 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its deepest phase dates by luminescence and uranium series to between about 101,000 and 94,000 years ago, and a sterile band of sand seals it off from the Late Stone Age layers above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Christopher Henshilwood began excavating in 1991 and has directed the work with Karen van Niekerk ever since, recovering among much else nine human teeth and dental fragments from at least five people, perhaps seven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The upper levels belong to the Still Bay, a short-lived southern African tradition of slender leaf-shaped points flaked on both faces, dated elsewhere to between about 71,900 and 71,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> What made the cave famous is what those upper layers held, finds that carried the origins of symbolic behaviour deep into the African Middle Stone Age. In 2002 Henshilwood's team published two pieces of red ochre engraved with cross-hatched designs, from a phase dated to about 75,000 years ago and then the oldest deliberate patterns known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same sediments held 41 tick shells of the sea snail Nassarius kraussianus, pierced on purpose and bearing traces of use, alongside worked bone tools and 400 bifacial Still Bay points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A deeper layer preserved a pigment workshop from around 100,000 years ago, where an ochre-rich compound was mixed with bone, charcoal and crushed stone and stored in two abalone shells, one still holding its quartzite grinder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 2018 the excavators announced a cross-hatched pattern drawn with an ochre crayon on a ground silcrete flake about 73,000 years ago, at least 30,000 years older than any drawing then known, though what it meant is beyond recovery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Blombos Cave","questions":["About 73,000 years old, a cross-hatched pattern drawn on a silcrete flake with an ochre crayon made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the source of the oldest drawing then known.","A layer around 100,000 years old preserved a whole pigment workshop at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where ochre paste was ground and stored in two abalone shells."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","south africa"],"sources":["Katja Douze, Sarah Wurz, and Christopher S. Henshilwood, “Techno-Cultural Characterization of the MIS 5 (c. 105–90 Ka) Lithic Industries at Blombos Cave, Southern Cape, South Africa,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 10, no. 11 (2015): e0142151, under “Research background on Blombos Cave,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0142151. [Open access]","Abdeljalil Bouzouggar, Nick Barton, Marian Vanhaeren, et al., “82,000-Year-Old Shell Beads from North Africa and Implications for the Origins of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 24 (2007): 9964–69, esp. 9964, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1891266/. [Open access]","Daniela Eugenia Rosso, Africa Pitarch Martí and Francesco d’Errico, “Middle Stone Age Ochre Processing and Behavioural Complexity in the Horn of Africa: Evidence from Porc-Epic Cave, Dire Dawa, Ethiopia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 11 (2016): e0164793, under “Introduction,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0164793. [Open access]","Christopher S. Henshilwood, Francesco d'Errico, Karen L. van Niekerk, et al., “An Abstract Drawing from the 73,000-Year-Old Levels at Blombos Cave, South Africa,” <i>Nature</i> 562, no. 7725 (2018): 115–18, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-018-0514-3. [Paywalled]","Frederick E. Grine and Christopher S. Henshilwood, “Additional Human Remains from Blombos Cave, South Africa: (1999–2000 Excavations),” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 42, no. 3 (2002): 293–302, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1006/jhev.2001.0525. [Paywalled]","Zenobia Jacobs et al., “Ages for the Middle Stone Age of Southern Africa: Implications for Human Behavior and Dispersal,” <i>Science</i> 322, no. 5902 (2008): 733–35, esp. 733, https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/bitstreams/3e6b212a-1edc-45c8-a0b6-067293fb544c/download. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/BBC-strat-date-.jpg","title":"Blombos Cave","desc":"Stratigraphy and dates of the west section of Blombos Cave. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BBC-strat-date-.jpg","alt":"BBC-strat-date"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Blombos Cave","at":[21.2225,-34.4144]}},
{"id":"wh-059","num":"59","category":"World · History","question":"Small crescents of stone, blunted along one edge so glue and binding could hold them in a wooden shaft, are the signature of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a southern African tradition.","answer":"Howiesons Poort","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 64,800 – 59,500 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Howiesons Poort</b> is a distinctive toolmaking tradition of the southern African Middle Stone Age, recognised at rock shelters and caves across South Africa, Lesotho and Namibia. Its knappers struck long, narrow blades from carefully prepared cores of fine-grained stone such as silcrete and chert, then snapped them into short lengths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One edge of each piece was blunted by steep retouch, a treatment archaeologists call backing, which left the opposite edge razor-sharp while giving glue and binding a blunt curve to grip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The result was a small crescent or trapeze, often no longer than a fingernail, made to sit in a wooden shaft as a replaceable part rather than be held in the hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Composite weapons of that kind are ordinary in far later prehistory, so the industry has long been read as a sign of thoroughly modern technical thinking. <br><br> The name comes from a small shelter above the pass on the road into Grahamstown, now Makhanda, in the Eastern Cape, dug in 1927 and 1928 by John Hewitt of the Albany Museum and Father Stapleton, a teacher at St Aidan's College; they named it after the pass, which carried the name of a Mr Howison, misspelled that name in print, and the error has been repeated ever since. Single-grain luminescence dating published by Zenobia Jacobs, Richard Roberts and colleagues in 2008 placed the industry between roughly 64,800 and 59,500 years ago at sites hundreds of kilometres apart, a shared style lasting some 5,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later thermoluminescence work at Diepkloof Rock Shelter has returned much older ages, so the true span is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same layers have yielded 270 fragments of ostrich eggshell engraved with repeated hatched bands about 60,000 years old, and a bone point from Sibudu Cave that its excavators read as an arrow tip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most puzzling is the ending, because backed segments largely drop out of the levels above, and whether cooling climate, thinning populations or changed hunting ranges explains their loss is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Howiesons Poort","questions":["Single-grain luminescence dating puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 64,800 and 59,500 years ago, a single style shared across sites hundreds of kilometres apart.","Named after a pass on the road into Grahamstown that carried a Mr Howison's misspelled name, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was dug at a small shelter in 1927 and 1928."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","south africa"],"sources":["Zenobia Jacobs et al., \"Ages for the Middle Stone Age of Southern Africa: Implications for Human Behavior and Dispersal,\" <i>Science</i> 322, no. 5902 (2008): 733–35, esp. 733, https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/bitstreams/3e6b212a-1edc-45c8-a0b6-067293fb544c/download. [Open access]","Pierre-Jean Texier et al., \"A Howiesons Poort Tradition of Engraving Ostrich Eggshell Containers Dated to 60,000 Years Ago at Diepkloof Rock Shelter, South Africa,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 14 (2010): 6180–85, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2851956/. [Open access]","Lucinda Backwell, Francesco d’Errico, and Lyn Wadley, \"Middle Stone Age Bone Tools from the Howiesons Poort Layers, Sibudu Cave, South Africa,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 35, no. 6 (2008): 1566–80, esp. 1573–76, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jas.2007.11.006. [Paywalled]","Sylvain Soriano et al., \"The Still Bay and Howiesons Poort at Sibudu and Blombos: Understanding Middle Stone Age Technologies,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 10, no. 7 (2015): e0131127, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0131127. [Open access]","Martin Ziegler et al., \"Development of Middle Stone Age Innovation Linked to Rapid Climate Change,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 4 (2013): 1905, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms2897. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Annals_of_the_South_African_Museum_-_Annale_van_die_Suid-Afrikaanse_Museum_%281929%29_%2818394935036%29.jpg/1920px-Annals_of_the_South_African_Museum_-_Annale_van_die_Suid-Afrikaanse_Museum_%281929%29_%2818394935036%29.jpg","title":"Howiesons Poort","desc":"Title : Annals of the South African Museum = Annale van die Suid-Afrikaanse Museum Identifier : annalsofsouthafr27192sout (find matches) Year : 1898 (1890s) Authors : South African Museum Subjects : Natural history Publisher : Cape Town : The Museum Contributing Library : Smithsonian Libraries… Public domain (No restrictions), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annals_of_the_South_African_Museum_-_Annale_van_die_Suid-Afrikaanse_Museum_(1929)_(18394935036).jpg","alt":"Annals of the South African Museum - Annale van die Suid-Afrikaanse Museum (1929)"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Howiesons Poort","at":[26.5267,-33.31],"kind":"region","area":[[16.3,-28.6],[16.9,-26.6],[19.9,-26.4],[22.5,-26],[25.5,-25.8],[28.4,-25.7],[31.3,-26.5],[32.4,-27.8],[30.5,-30.6],[28.3,-32.6],[25.6,-34.2],[22.3,-34.6],[19,-34.5],[17.3,-32.4]]}},
{"id":"wh-060","num":"60","category":"World · History","question":"Named for a site in eastern Algeria and known by the narrow stem worked into the base of its stone points, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread across North Africa and a Sahara that was then green.","answer":"Aterian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 145,000 – 30,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">both ends debated</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Aterian</b> is a Middle Stone Age toolkit found across North Africa and the Sahara, named after Bir el Ater in eastern Algeria, where the collection that defined it was gathered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What sets it apart is the tang, a narrow stem worked into the base of a point or scraper so that the tool could be seated in a split shaft and bound tight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A tang is the clearest early sign of a stone tool designed from the outset to be mounted rather than held, which is why these points are often called the oldest arrowheads, though that goes further than the evidence allows. Alongside them the assemblages hold leaf-shaped bifaces, scrapers and flakes struck by the Levallois method, which shapes a core in advance so that a flake of the wanted form comes off in a single blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Luminescence dating puts the earliest Aterian at about 145,000 years ago, and the tradition ran on until roughly 30,000, though both dates are debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its makers were Homo sapiens, settled by skulls and jaws from Dar es-Soltane, Contrebandiers and El Harhoura on the Moroccan coast that lay in Aterian layers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Taforalt in eastern Morocco people pierced sea snail shells, rubbed them with red ochre and wore them, and at roughly 82,000 years old these are among the oldest ornaments known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bone was worked into shaped tools at Contrebandiers between about 120,000 and 90,000 years ago, probably for hides and fur, and the caves hold hearths and traces of built structures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The spread of the toolkit followed the Sahara's mood, since the desert filled with grassland and lakes during humid spells, opening corridors that closed again when it dried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Specialists now hesitate over the word itself, because the tanged points vary greatly from place to place and the assemblages shade into the rest of the North African Middle Stone Age, so the Aterian may be a habit shared across a wide region rather than one people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aterian","questions":["Made by Homo sapiens and dated at Ifri n'Ammar in Morocco to about 145,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tanged its points to seat them in split shafts.","At Taforalt in eastern Morocco its people pierced sea-snail shells and rubbed them with ochre, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left ornaments among the oldest known, roughly 82,000 years old."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","africa"],"sources":["Inga Bergmann, Jean-Jacques Hublin, Abdelouahed Ben-Ncer, Fatima Zohra Sbihi-Alaoui, Philipp Gunz, and Sarah E. Freidline, “The Relevance of Late MSA Mandibles on the Emergence of Modern Morphology in Northern Africa,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 8841, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9133045/. [Open access]","Emilie Campmas, Matthieu Lebon, et al., “Aterian Shell Beads from the Coastal Site of El Mnasra Cave (Rabat-Témara, Morocco): Specificities of the North African MSA Personal Ornaments,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21 (2026): e0338785, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13016355/. [Open access]","Abdeljalil Bouzouggar, Nick Barton, Marian Vanhaeren, et al., “82,000-Year-Old Shell Beads from North Africa and Implications for the Origins of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 24 (2007): 9964–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1891266/. [Open access]","Emily Y. Hallett, Curtis W. Marean, Teresa E. Steele, et al., “A Worked Bone Assemblage from 120,000–90,000 Year Old Deposits at Contrebandiers Cave, Atlantic Coast, Morocco,” <i>iScience</i> 24, no. 9 (2021): 102988, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8478944/. [Open access]","Yassine Ait Brahim, Lijuan Sha, Jasper A. Wassenburg, et al., “The Spatiotemporal Extent of the Green Sahara during the Last Glacial Period,” <i>iScience</i> 26, no. 7 (2023): 107018, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10320408/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/At%C3%A9rien_%28Djelfa%29.JPG","title":"Aterian","desc":"Atérien, région de Djelfa (Zaccar), Algérie. Michel-georges bernard, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:At%C3%A9rien_(Djelfa).JPG","alt":"Atérien (Djelfa)"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"wh-061","num":"61","category":"World · History","question":"The traits archaeologists once treated as the mark of a fully human mind — art, ornament, burial, language — go under the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and where they arose is still argued.","answer":"Behavioural modernity","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolution model</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 50,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gradual model</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 300,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Behavioural modernity</b> is the name archaeologists give to the bundle of habits and abilities taken to mark a mind like our own, not merely a body like our own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual list runs to art and ornament, deliberate burial, music, fishing and specialised hunting, tools of bone and antler, blades struck from prepared cores, goods carried far from home, and above all symbols and language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None leaves a fossil of thought, so each is read as a proxy: a pierced shell bead is taken as proof that its wearer meant something by it and expected others to understand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea took shape around how abruptly cave paintings, figurines and beads appear in the European Upper Paleolithic, after about 45,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On one reading that suddenness is real, some neurological change around 50,000 years ago having made the modern mind possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Against it stands the African record: pigment working, blades, bone points, shell beads and engraved ochre are known there from between 300,000 and 70,000 years ago, scattered and intermittent rather than sudden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That makes the European burst a late local flourish rather than the birth of the mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Population size may explain the flickering, since dense and connected groups keep hard-won skills alive where thin ones lose them, so the record may track numbers rather than intelligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neanderthals used pigment and pierced shells at Cueva de los Aviones in Spain about 115,000 years ago, which makes the trait list a poor boundary between them and us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> So the checklist is now widely set aside in favour of what people actually did, the traits turning up patchily across a subdivided African population, though the term stays in wide use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Behavioural modernity","questions":["A paper of 2000 titled 'The revolution that wasn't' scattered the trait list of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across African sites tens of thousands of years before Europe's cave art.","One account ties <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a neurological change around 50,000 years ago, reading the sudden burst of Upper Paleolithic art and beads as a real event."],"tags":["concept","archaeology","prehistory","evolution"],"sources":["Sally McBrearty and Alison S. Brooks, “The Revolution That Wasn’t: A New Interpretation of the Origin of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 39, no. 5 (2000): 453–563, https://doi.org/10.1006/jhev.2000.0435. [Paywalled]","Richard G. Klein, “Out of Africa and the Evolution of Human Behavior,” <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 17, no. 6 (2008): 267–81, https://doi.org/10.1002/evan.20181. [Paywalled]","Adam Powell, Stephen Shennan, and Mark G. Thomas, “Late Pleistocene Demography and the Appearance of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Science</i> 324, no. 5932 (2009): 1298–1301, https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/168654/. [Open access]","João Zilhão et al., “Symbolic Use of Marine Shells and Mineral Pigments by Iberian Neandertals,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 3 (2010): 1023–28, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2824307/. [Open access]","Francesco d’Errico and Chris B. Stringer, “Evolution, Revolution or Saltation Scenario for the Emergence of Modern Cultures?,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 366, no. 1567 (2011): 1060–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3049097/. [Open access]","Dirk L. Hoffmann, Diego E. Angelucci, Valentín Villaverde, Josefina Zapata, and João Zilhão, “Symbolic Use of Marine Shells and Mineral Pigments by Iberian Neandertals 115,000 Years Ago,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 2 (2018): eaar5255, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5833998/. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., “Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?,” <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/VenusHohlefels2.jpg","title":"Behavioural modernity","desc":"Paleolithic figure Venus of Hohlefels (of mammoth ivory). Ramessos, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VenusHohlefels2.jpg","alt":"The Venus of Hohle Fels"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-064","num":"64","category":"World · History","question":"Built on a real Sumatran supereruption, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held that our species was nearly wiped out 74,000 years ago, until diggers found people carrying on through the ash.","answer":"Toba catastrophe theory","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Eruption</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 74,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Proposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1998, by Stanley Ambrose</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Toba catastrophe theory</b> is the proposal that a volcanic eruption in Sumatra about 74,000 years ago brought our species close to extinction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eruption itself is not in doubt: Toba expelled something like 5,300 cubic kilometres of magma, the largest eruption known in the last two million years, and laid a bed of ash from the Arabian Sea to the South China Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1998 Stanley Ambrose argued that a volcanic winter had ushered in a thousand years of the coldest temperatures of the later Pleistocene, around 71,000 to 70,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He tied that to a puzzle in our own biology, since human genetic diversity is oddly low for so widespread a species, which had been read as the mark of a crash to a few thousand breeding individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Put together, the two made an irresistible story: a mountain in Indonesia nearly ended us, and everyone alive descends from the handful who scraped through. <br><br> The evidence has not been kind to it. In India, stone tools lie both below and above thick beds of Toba ash — at Jwalapuram in the south and at Dhaba in the Son valley — and look much the same in each, so whoever was making them carried straight on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cores drilled from Lake Malawi contain glass shards from the eruption, and the mud around them records a cooling of only about 1.5 degrees, nothing like a volcanic winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Pinnacle Point in South Africa the same ash falls in the middle of an occupation that not only continues but grows busier afterwards, with new kinds of tool appearing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Newer climate models still give a sharp global chill but leave Africa and India among the mildest places on the map, so the eruption stands as one of the largest in the history of the planet while the near-extinction it was supposed to have caused has quietly gone away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Toba catastrophe theory","questions":["Stanley Ambrose proposed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1998, arguing a volcanic winter opened a thousand years of extreme cold after a blast that laid ash from the Arabian Sea to the South China Sea.","At Jwalapuram in India, tools lie little changed below and above the Toba ash, one of the finds that quietly undid the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its near-extinction."],"tags":["theory","geology","science","prehistory"],"sources":["Stanley H. Ambrose, “Late Pleistocene Human Population Bottlenecks, Volcanic Winter, and Differentiation of Modern Humans,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 34, no. 6 (1998): 623–51, https://doi.org/10.1006/jhev.1998.0219. [Paywalled]","Michael Petraglia et al., “Middle Paleolithic Assemblages from the Indian Subcontinent Before and After the Toba Super-Eruption,” <i>Science</i> 317, no. 5834 (2007): 114–16, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1141564. [Paywalled]","Chris Clarkson et al., “Human Occupation of Northern India Spans the Toba Super-Eruption ~74,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 961, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7042215/. [Open access]","Christine S. Lane, Ben T. Chorn, and Thomas C. Johnson, “Ash from the Toba Supereruption in Lake Malawi Shows No Volcanic Winter in East Africa at 75 ka,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 20 (2013): 8025–29, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3657767/. [Open access]","Eugene I. Smith et al., “Humans Thrived in South Africa through the Toba Eruption about 74,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 555, no. 7697 (2018): 511–15, https://oasis.library.unlv.edu/geo_fac_articles/145. [Open access]","Benjamin A. Black et al., “Global Climate Disruption and Regional Climate Shelters after the Toba Supereruption,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 29 (2021): e2013046118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8307270/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Map_of_Lake_Toba.jpg/1920px-Map_of_Lake_Toba.jpg","title":"Toba catastrophe theory","desc":"Lake Toba - Danau Toba. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Lake_Toba.jpg","alt":"Map of Lake Toba"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-065","num":"65","category":"World · History","question":"Long blamed on our own arrival, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now dated to between 41,000 and 39,000 years ago, and may owe more to tiny inbred populations.","answer":"Neanderthal extinction","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 41,000 – 39,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Redating</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2014</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neanderthal extinction</b> is the disappearance of the Neanderthals from Europe and western Asia around 40,000 years ago, after a run of several hundred thousand years. A dating programme published in 2014 rebuilt the chronology of 40 sites from Spain to Russia with cleaner radiocarbon methods and placed the end of their toolkit between about 41,000 and 39,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a single moment: they went from different regions at different times, so the map empties patchily rather than all at once. Where both peoples are found, the same work put their overlap with arriving modern humans at roughly 2,600 to 5,400 years, long enough to meet and to interbreed, far too long for a single conquest. An earlier study had already undercut the claim that late bands held out in southern Iberia long after the north, since redating with better purification pushed those young ages back beyond the reach of the method.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Three explanations are argued over, and none excludes the others. Competition is the oldest: modern humans arrived in larger groups with wider networks for exchanging materials, and may simply have worked the same country harder. Climate is the second, since the period is full of violent cold swings, and a vast Italian eruption falls near the same window, though its worst effects were brief and largely missed western Europe. The third is demography, and the genomes have made it the strongest: a Siberian cave held a father and his daughter in a band of perhaps a couple of dozen, and a late individual from France came from a line isolated for 50,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A modelling study in 2019 found that inbreeding, the difficulty of finding mates in tiny groups and plain bad luck could have finished them within 10,000 years with nobody else involved, so most specialists now expect several pressures at once, and no clean ending, since their DNA is still in us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Neanderthal extinction","questions":["A 2014 dating programme across 40 sites placed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 41,000 and 39,000 years ago and showed it happened patchily, region by region, not all at once.","A 2019 model found that inbreeding, the trouble of finding mates in tiny bands and plain bad luck could have driven the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with no one else involved."],"tags":["event","palaeontology","prehistory","evolution"],"sources":["Tom Higham et al., “The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Rachel E. Wood et al., “Radiocarbon Dating Casts Doubt on the Late Chronology of the Middle to Upper Palaeolithic Transition in Southern Iberia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 8 (2013): 2781–86, abstract and “Results and Discussion,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3581959/. [Open access]","Laurits Skov et al., “Genetic Insights into the Social Organization of Neanderthals,” <i>Nature</i> 610, no. 7932 (2022): 519–25, abstract and “Genomic Diversity,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/. [Open access]","Ludovic Slimak et al., “Long Genetic and Social Isolation in Neanderthals before Their Extinction,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 4, no. 9 (2024): 100593, summary, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11480857/. [Open access]","Krist Vaesen et al., “Inbreeding, Allee Effects and Stochasticity Might Be Sufficient to Account for Neanderthal Extinction,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 11 (2019): e0225117, abstract and “Materials and Methods,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0225117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Neanderthal_distribution.jpg","title":"Neanderthal extinction","desc":"Neanderthal distribution. 2019-05-05. Berria, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neanderthal_distribution.jpg","alt":"Neanderthal distribution"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-066","num":"66","category":"World · History","question":"If Neanderthals really made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of France and northern Spain, with its curved backed blades, bone awls and pierced teeth, then ornament was never ours alone.","answer":"Châtelperronian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 44,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">dates disputed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1938, by Dorothy Garrod</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Châtelperronian</b> is a stone tool industry of central and south-western France and northern Spain, made at the moment the Middle Paleolithic was giving way to the Upper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Henri Breuil defined it in 1909 from the Grotte des Fées at Châtelperron, a cave in the Allier, filing it under the Aurignacian as that industry's earliest phase, and the name we use is Dorothy Garrod's, from 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Its signature tool is the Châtelperron knife, a blade blunted down one curved edge so that it could be gripped or hafted without cutting the hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> With it come bone awls and pierced animal teeth, the sort of worked bone and ornament that archaeologists had long treated as the mark of modern humans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sits between the Mousterian below and the Protoaurignacian above, roughly 44,000 to 40,000 years ago, though the dates are argued over as fiercely as everything else about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The long quarrel is about who made it, and the answer decides whether ornament and blade-making were ever ours alone. A Neanderthal skeleton dug out at Saint-Césaire in 1979 lay in a Châtelperronian level, and teeth from the Grotte du Renne at Arcy-sur-Cure pointed the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 2016 the proteins in bone scraps from that cave were read as archaic and the ancient DNA in them as Neanderthal, which their authors took as settling that the tools and the bones belonged together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The doubts have not gone: dates from the Grotte du Renne scatter as though the layers had been churned, the Saint-Césaire association was challenged in 2018, and in 2023 the hip bone of a newborn modern human was identified in the same collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even granting that Neanderthals made it, whether they worked it out themselves or copied the modern humans arriving in Europe is unsettled, and the whole question rests on a handful of disturbed cave floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Châtelperronian","questions":["Henri Breuil named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1906 after the Grotte des Fées at Châtelperron, having at first filed the very same material under the Aurignacian.","A Neanderthal skeleton found at Saint-Césaire in 1979 lay in a level of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but churned dates and a 2023 newborn modern human keep who made it disputed."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","france"],"sources":["Igor Djakovic, Morgan Roussel, and Marie Soressi, \"Stone tools in shifting sands: past, present, and future perspectives on the Châtelperronian stone tool industry,\" <i>Journal of Paleolithic Archaeology</i> 7 (2024): 29, https://hdl.handle.net/1887/4108948. [Open access]","Frido Welker, Mateja Hajdinjak, Sahra Talamo, et al., \"Palaeoproteomic evidence identifies archaic hominins associated with the Châtelperronian at the Grotte du Renne,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 40 (2016): 11162–11167, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5056053/. [Open access]","Arthur Gicqueau, Alexandra Schuh, Juliette Henrion, et al., \"Anatomically modern human in the Châtelperronian hominin collection from the Grotte du Renne (Arcy-sur-Cure, northeast France),\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 12682, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403518/. [Open access]","Thomas Higham, Roger Jacobi, Michèle Julien, et al., \"Chronology of the Grotte du Renne (France) and implications for the context of ornaments and human remains within the Châtelperronian,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 47 (2010): 20234–20239, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2996711/. [Open access]","François Caron, Francesco d'Errico, Pierre Del Moral, et al., \"The reality of Neandertal symbolic behavior at the Grotte du Renne, Arcy-sur-Cure, France,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 6 (2011): e21545, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3126825/. [Open access]","Brad Gravina, François Bachellerie, Solène Caux, et al., \"No reliable evidence for a Neanderthal–Châtelperronian association at La Roche-à-Pierrot, Saint-Césaire,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 15134, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6181958/. [Open access]","Igor Djakovic, Alastair Key, and Marie Soressi, \"Optimal linear estimation models predict 1400–2900 years of overlap between <i>Homo sapiens</i> and Neandertals prior to their disappearance from France and northern Spain,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 15000, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9561710/. [Open access]","Davide Delpiano, Brad Gravina, and Marco Peresani, \"Back(s) to basics: the concept of backing in stone tool technologies for tracing hominins' technical innovations,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 33, no. 6 (2024): e22045, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11624295/. [Open access]","Karl J. Narr, \"On the stratigraphy of Upper Palaeolithic types and type groups,\" <i>DEUQUA Special Publications</i> 3 (2021): 27–34, https://doi.org/10.5194/deuquasp-3-27-2021. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Pointes_de_chatelperron.jpg","title":"Châtelperronian","desc":"Pointes de chatelperron. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pointes_de_chatelperron.jpg","alt":"Pointes de chatelperron"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"wh-067","num":"67","category":"World · History","question":"Named after a rock shelter in the Pyrenean foothills, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought Europe its first figurines and flutes as the last Neanderthals disappeared.","answer":"Aurignacian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 43,000 – 33,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1906, by Breuil and Cartailhac</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Aurignacian</b> is the first Upper Paleolithic culture to spread across Europe, and it is generally attributed to modern humans rather than to the Neanderthals it replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its name comes from a rock shelter at Aurignac in the Pyrenean foothills of south-western France, where in 1852 a workman broke through a slab and found seventeen skeletons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mayor had them reburied in the parish cemetery, where they were promptly lost, and when Édouard Lartet dug the shelter in 1860 he found something far older beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Worked flint and bone lay among the remains of mammoth, cave bear and reindeer, and Émile Cartailhac proposed the site's name for the industry in 1906, with Henri Breuil defining it the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is recognised by steep-fronted carinated scrapers, long blades with a distinctive nibbled retouch, and slim antler points split at the base to take a shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What sets it apart is less the toolkit than what came with it, because the Aurignacian is where the European record stops being a matter of stone alone and fills with art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> From the caves of the Swabian Jura in southern Germany come ivory figurines, among them a lion-headed man and a small full-bodied woman, the latter carved at least 35,000 years ago and perhaps nearer 40,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> From the same caves come flutes cut from bird bone and mammoth ivory, lying in layers dated to between about 42,500 and 37,000 years ago, among the oldest musical instruments known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The painted lions and rhinoceroses of Chauvet in the Ardèche are often placed here too, though that attribution is disputed, and what is striking is how accomplished they already are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"11\"></sup> All of this appears in the few thousand years around 40,000 years ago, the same window in which the Neanderthals vanish, and whether that timing is cause or coincidence is argued over still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"12\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aurignacian","questions":["At Aurignac in 1852 a workman broke through a slab onto seventeen skeletons that were reburied and lost, before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the site's name in 1906.","From the Swabian caves the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left ivory figurines and bird-bone flutes, one about 42,000 years old and the oldest musical instruments known anywhere."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Charles Lyell, <i>The Geological Evidences of the Antiquity of Man</i>, 2nd American ed. (Philadelphia: George W. Childs, 1863), 182–186, https://archive.org/details/geologicaleviden00lyeluoft. [Open access]","Édouard Lartet and Henry Christy, <i>Reliquiae Aquitanicae: Being Contributions to the Archaeology and Palaeontology of Périgord and the Adjoining Provinces of Southern France</i>, ed. Thomas Rupert Jones (London: Williams & Norgate, 1875), https://archive.org/details/reliquiaeaquitan00lartuoft. [Open access]","Keiko Kitagawa and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Split-based points from the Swabian Jura highlight Aurignacian regional signatures,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0239865, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0239865. [Open access]","Benjamin Schürch, Svenja Schray, and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Reconstructing flexible pathways of Aurignacian blade and bladelet production at Vogelherd,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0331921, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0331921. [Open access]","Ana B. Marín-Arroyo, Joseba Rios-Garaizar, Lawrence G. Straus et al., \"Chronological reassessment of the Middle to Upper Paleolithic transition and Early Upper Paleolithic cultures in Cantabrian Spain,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 4 (2018): e0194708, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0194708. [Open access]","Jonathan A. Haws, Michael M. Benedetti, Sahra Talamo, et al., \"The early Aurignacian dispersal of modern humans into westernmost Eurasia,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 117, no. 41 (2020): 25414–25422, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2016062117. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Conard and Veerle Rots, \"Rope making in the Aurignacian of central Europe more than 35,000 years ago,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 5 (2024): eadh5217, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adh5217. [Open access]","Sara E. Rhodes, Britt M. Starkovich and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Did climate determine Late Pleistocene settlement dynamics in the Ach Valley, SW Germany?,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 5 (2019): e0215172, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0215172. [Open access]","Jean Bouyssonie, \"L'Aurignacien,\" <i>Bulletin de la Société préhistorique française</i> 51, no. 8 (1954): 49–53, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bspf_0249-7638_1954_hos_51_8_12409. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Conard, \"A female figurine from the basal Aurignacian of Hohle Fels Cave in southwestern Germany,\" <i>Nature</i> 459, no. 7244 (2009): 248–252, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature07995. [Paywalled]","Paul Pettitt and Paul Bahn, \"An alternative chronology for the art of Chauvet Cave,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 89, no. 345 (2015): 542–553, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2015.21. [Paywalled]","Tom Higham, Katerina Douka, Rachel Wood, et al., \"The timing and spatiotemporal patterning of Neanderthal disappearance,\" <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–309, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Pendants%2C_Aurignacian_culture%2C_31000-24000_BC%2C_various_materials_-_Cinquantenaire_Museum_-_Brussels%2C_Belgium_-_DSC08704.jpg/1920px-Pendants%2C_Aurignacian_culture%2C_31000-24000_BC%2C_various_materials_-_Cinquantenaire_Museum_-_Brussels%2C_Belgium_-_DSC08704.jpg","title":"Aurignacian","desc":"Exhibit in the Cinquantenaire Museum - Brussels, Belgium. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pendants,_Aurignacian_culture,_31000-24000_BC,_various_materials_-_Cinquantenaire_Museum_-_Brussels,_Belgium_-_DSC08704.jpg","alt":"Pendants, Aurignacian culture, 31000-24000 BCE, various materials - Cinquantenaire Museum - Brussels, Belgium"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-068","num":"68","category":"World · History","question":"Railway workers in the Dordogne uncovered five skeletons in 1868 at the shelter that gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its name, long the word for every early modern human in Europe.","answer":"Cro-Magnon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 32,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1868</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cro-Magnon</b> is the name long given to the early modern humans of Ice Age Europe, and for a century it meant, in ordinary speech, the first people who looked like us. It comes from a rock shelter in the Vézère valley of south-western France, where in March 1868 workmen digging a road struck flints, animal bones and human remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The geologist Louis Lartet took charge and recovered four adults and an infant, laid in the shelter with pierced sea shells and worked reindeer antler, though later study found several more newborns among the bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is local and plain: in the region's Occitan a <i>cros</i> is 'a hollow' or 'a cave', while <i>Magnon</i> is either a personal name or an old word for 'large'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The find mattered because it placed people whose skeletons look like living Europeans in the same deposits as Ice Age animals, which at the time was still contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  Cro-Magnon 1, the skull of a middle-aged man, is among the most familiar fossils of its kind, and its owner was ill: a lesion had eaten into his forehead, read by some as a tumour, by others as a fungal infection. These were people of the Upper Paleolithic, striking long slender blades, carving bone and antler, and stringing shells and teeth into ornaments, and more than 300 perforated sea shells were laid in the graves with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A pierced shell from the burials has since been radiocarbon dated to about 32,000 years ago, later than anyone had assumed, which places them in the early Gravettian rather than the Aurignacian their tools had suggested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Like everyone whose ancestors left Africa they carried a little Neanderthal DNA, but Europeans today draw only part of their ancestry from such hunters, the rest arriving later with farmers and herders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word has quietly left the scientific literature, which prefers European early modern humans and keeps Cro-Magnon for the shelter and its fossils; it survives in museums, textbooks and the popular imagination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cro-Magnon","questions":["From the Occitan, where a cros is a hollow or cave, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took a plain local name for people whose skeletons already looked like living Europeans.","Because a pierced shell from the burials dates to about 32,000 years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now sits in the early Gravettian rather than the Aurignacian its tools suggested."],"tags":["people","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","france"],"sources":["Dominique Henry-Gambier, \"Les fossiles de Cro-Magnon (Les Eyzies-de-Tayac, Dordogne): nouvelles données sur leur position chronologique et leur attribution culturelle,\" <i>Bulletins et Mémoires de la Société d'Anthropologie de Paris</i> 14, no. 1–2 (2002): 89–112, https://journals.openedition.org/bmsap/459. [Open access]","Dominique Henry-Gambier, Roland Nespoulet, and Laurent Chiotti, \"An early Gravettian cultural attribution for the human fossils from the Cro-Magnon rock shelter (Les Eyzies-de-Tayac, Dordogne),\" <i>PALEO</i> 24 (2013): 121–138, https://doi.org/10.4000/paleo.2859. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu, Cosimo Posth, Mateja Hajdinjak, et al., \"The genetic history of Ice Age Europe,\" <i>Nature</i> 534, no. 7606 (2016): 200–205, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4943878/. [Open access]","Wolfgang Haak, Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, et al., \"Massive migration from the steppe was a source for Indo-European languages in Europe,\" <i>Nature</i> 522, no. 7555 (2015): 207–211, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5048219/. [Open access]","Erik Trinkaus, \"European early modern humans and the fate of the Neandertals,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 18 (2007): 7367–7372, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1863481/. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Abri de Cro-Magnon,\" https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/en/a-propos/abri-cro-magnon. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Cro-Magnon-male-skull.png","title":"Cro-Magnon","desc":"Cro-Magnon-male-skull. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cro-Magnon-male-skull.png","alt":"Cro-Magnon-male-skull"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-069","num":"69","category":"World · History","question":"Carved from a mammoth tusk about 40,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Swabian Jura is the oldest sculpture anyone has made of a creature that never existed.","answer":"Lion-man","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 August 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reassembled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1969</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lion-man</b> is a standing figure with a lion's head, cut from a single mammoth tusk about 40,000 years ago and now the most famous object of the European Ice Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Otto Völzing found it on 25 August 1939 in the Stadel cave of the Hohlenstein cliff, in the Lone valley of southern Germany, on the last day of the dig.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany invaded Poland a week later, the dig was abandoned, and the boxed splinters lay unstudied for thirty years, reaching the Museum Ulm only in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joachim Hahn opened it in 1969, recognised what he had, and fitted some 200 pieces together into a figure with a human body and the head of a cave lion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later digging in the cave returned hundreds more fragments, and the sculpture was taken apart and rebuilt once more in 2012 and 2013, reaching its present height of 31.1 centimetres (12.2 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Nothing about it was useful, and that is what has kept it interesting. An experiment carving a replica with flint tools of the kind its maker had ran to hundreds of hours, an enormous outlay for a small band living hand to mouth. Whoever spent them was not carving a lion or a person but something that has never walked the earth, and no older sculpture of an imagined creature is known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether it is a god, a spirit, a person in a lion skin or a figure from a story nobody can now hear, there is no way to tell. Its sex was argued over for decades, and the rebuilt figure is now read as male on the evidence of a triangular sliver at the groin, while a second, much smaller lion-headed figure from the nearby cave of Hohle Fels shows the idea was not one carver's alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lion-man","questions":["Otto Völzing found it on the dig's last day in 1939, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sat in a box for thirty years until Joachim Hahn fitted its 200 ivory pieces together.","Carving a replica of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with flint tools took hundreds of hours, a huge outlay for a small band to spend on a creature that never walked the earth."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","prehistory","germany"],"sources":["Museum Ulm, “Löwenmensch,” Archäologie, inv. no. P 1939/2013, museum-digital:baden-württemberg, accessed 31 July 2026, https://bawue.museum-digital.de/object/2497. [Open access]","Urgeschichtliches Museum Blaubeuren, “Hohlenstein,” Welterbe-Fundstellen, accessed 31 July 2026, https://www.urmu.de/welterbe/fundstellen/hohlenstein. [Open access]","Harald Floss, “The Oldest Portable Art: The Aurignacian Ivory Figurines from the Swabian Jura (Southwest Germany),” <i>Palethnologie</i> 7 (2015), paras. 4 and 7, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.888. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Conard, “Palaeolithic Ivory Sculptures from Southwestern Germany and the Origins of Figurative Art,” <i>Nature</i> 426, no. 6968 (2003): 830–32, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02186. [Paywalled]","Welterbe Höhlen und Eiszeitkunst der Schwäbischen Alb, “UNESCO-Welterbe,” accessed 31 July 2026, https://www.iceageart.de/unesco-welterbe. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Loewenmensch1.jpg/1920px-Loewenmensch1.jpg","title":"Lion-man","desc":"The lion man from the Stadel Cave in Hohlenstein, Lonetal. Dagmar Hollmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loewenmensch1.jpg","alt":"Loewenmensch1"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-070","num":"70","category":"World · History","question":"Named from a Dordogne rock shelter, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the Ice Age its Venus figurines, its first fired clay and its first woven cloth.","answer":"Gravettian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 33,000 – 24,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1938, by Dorothy Garrod</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Gravettian</b> is the Upper Paleolithic culture that followed the Aurignacian, and from about 33,000 years ago it reached from the Atlantic coast to the Russian plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It takes its name from La Gravette, a rock shelter at Bayac in the Dordogne, and the label is credited to Dorothy Garrod, who in 1939 became the first woman to hold a professorial chair at Oxford or Cambridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Its marker is the Gravette point, a narrow blade blunted down one straight edge so that it could be set into a shaft as a spear tip or a knife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The people who made it hunted across open cold steppe, and on the Russian plain they raised circular structures framed from mammoth bone, among the oldest of them at Kostenki as the ice reached its greatest extent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its last few thousand years run into the coldest stretch of the Ice Age, when much of northern Europe emptied and people drew back towards the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br>  The carved figures a reader pictures as Ice Age art belong to this culture, though the great painted caves do not, Chauvet being older and Lascaux and Altamira younger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Venus figurines are Gravettian, among them the one from Willendorf in Austria and the one from Dolní Věstonice in Moravia, and the second of those is not stone or ivory but fired clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At roughly 31,000 to 29,000 years old the Moravian figures are among the oldest fired ceramics known, made thousands of years before anyone thought to fire a pot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"11\"></sup> Clay also caught what does not normally survive, since impressions on fired lumps at nearby Pavlov preserve cord, netting and woven fibre, among the earliest evidence of textile technology anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup> Ancient DNA has since sprung a surprise: the Gravettians of France and Spain and those of central, eastern and southern Europe carried distinct ancestries while sharing one toolkit, and only the western line is traceable after the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Gravettian","questions":["Dorothy Garrod named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1938 after La Gravette, the year before she became the first woman to hold a professorial chair at Oxford or Cambridge.","Ancient DNA has revealed that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was shared by two distinct populations, the French-Spanish and the central European, of whom only the western group survived the coldest ice."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Cosimo Posth, He Yu, Ayshin Ghalichi, et al., \"Palaeogenomics of Upper Palaeolithic to Neolithic European hunter-gatherers,\" <i>Nature</i> 615, no. 7950 (2023): 117–126, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9977688/. [Open access]","Rebecca Farbstein and April Nowell, \"Children at play: the role of novices in the production of Europe's earliest Upper Paleolithic ceramics,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 10 (2024): e0309107, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0309107. [Open access]","Rebecca Farbstein, Dinko Radić, Dejana Brajković, and Preston T. Miracle, \"First Epigravettian ceramic figurines from Europe (Vela Spila, Croatia),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 7 (2012): e41437, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3404104/. [Open access]","Gerhard W. Weber, Alexander Lukeneder, Mathias Harzhauser, et al., \"The microstructure and the origin of the Venus from Willendorf,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 2926, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8885675/. [Open access]","Colin D. Wren and Ariane Burke, \"Habitat suitability and the genetic structure of human populations during the Last Glacial Maximum (LGM) in western Europe,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0217996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0217996. [Open access]","Andreas Maier, Robin John, Florian Linsel et al., \"Analyzing trends in material culture evolution—a case study of Gravettian points from Lower Austria and Moravia,\" <i>Journal of Paleolithic Archaeology</i> 6 (2023): 15, https://doi.org/10.1007/s41982-023-00145-z. [Open access]","Wei Chu, Pavlo Shydlovskyi, and Andreas Maier, \"A revised radiocarbon chronology for the mammoth bone structures and associated features at Mezhyrich, Ukraine,\" <i>Open Research Europe</i> 5 (2025): 198, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12639289/. [Open access]","Alexander J. E. Pryor, David G. Beresford-Jones, Alexander E. Dudin, et al., \"The chronology and function of a new circular mammoth-bone structure at Kostenki 11,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 94, no. 374 (2020): 323–341, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2020.7. [Paywalled]","Pamela Jane Smith, \"Dorothy Garrod, first woman Professor at Cambridge,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 74, no. 283 (2000): 131–136, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00066230. [Paywalled]","James M. Adovasio, Olga Soffer and Bohuslav Klíma, \"Upper Palaeolithic fibre technology: interlaced woven finds from Pavlov I, Czech Republic, c. 26,000 years ago,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 70, no. 269 (1996): 526–34, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X0008368X. [Paywalled]","Pamela B. Vandiver, Olga Soffer, Bohuslav Klíma, and Jiří Svoboda, \"The origins of ceramic technology at Dolni Věstonice, Czechoslovakia,\" <i>Science</i> 246, no. 4933 (1989): 1002–1008, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.246.4933.1002. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/V%C3%A9nus_de_Lespugue_Gravettien_Mus%C3%A9e_de_l%27Homme_04022018_3.jpg/1920px-V%C3%A9nus_de_Lespugue_Gravettien_Mus%C3%A9e_de_l%27Homme_04022018_3.jpg","title":"Gravettian","desc":"Venus of Lespugue (back view), mammoth ivory, found in the Grotte des Rideaux (Lespugue, Haute-Garonne, France). Dated from the Gravettian, (Upper Paleolithic, 23 000 years). Currently in the Musée de l'Homme, in Paris. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V%C3%A9nus_de_Lespugue_Gravettien_Mus%C3%A9e_de_l%27Homme_04022018_3.jpg","alt":"Vénus de Lespugue Gravettien Musée de l'Homme 3"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-071","num":"71","category":"World · History","question":"Found from the Pyrenees to Siberia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nearly all share the same swollen hips, thin arms and small blank head.","answer":"Venus figurines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mostly c. 30,000 – 23,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Venus figurines</b> are small carvings of women made across Ice Age Europe and Siberia, and they are among the most familiar objects the period has left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They come from a band of country running from the French Pyrenees to Lake Baikal, some 6,000 kilometres (3,700 miles), and most were made between roughly 30,000 and 23,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were cut from whatever lay to hand: mammoth ivory, limestone, calcite, steatite and antler, and at one Moravian site from clay that was then fired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What is strange is not that people carved women, but that they carved them the same way across that whole distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The breasts, belly, hips and thighs are swollen, the arms are thin or missing, the hands and feet trail away to nothing, and the head is usually small, bare and without a face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The exceptions are the most interesting objects in the set. The oldest of them all is a little ivory woman from Hohle Fels in Germany, carved at least 35,000 years ago and older by several thousand years than the Gravettian series that follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The figurines from Mal'ta in Siberia are slender rather than heavy, and several seem to be wearing fitted fur clothing, which is what a Siberian winter would demand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 2022 a scan of the Willendorf figure matched its stone to an outcrop near Lake Garda, on the far side of the Alps, so either the carving or its owners had travelled a very long way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the figures were for is unknown, but conventions held in common over such distances mean the people who made them were in touch, and were handing on more than a way of working flint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Venus figurines","questions":["The oldest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a little ivory woman from Hohle Fels in Germany, carved at least 35,000 years ago, thousands of years before the rest.","Carved from ivory, limestone, steatite and even fired clay, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> include the Willendorf figure, whose stone a 2022 scan matched to an outcrop across the Alps."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","prehistory"],"sources":["Gillian Morriss-Kay, “A New Hypothesis on the Creation of the Hohle Fels “Venus” Figurine,” <i>Palethnologie</i> 5 (2013), paras. 1–3, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.5135. [Open access]","Gerhard W. Weber, Alexander Lukeneder, Mathias Harzhauser, et al., “The Microstructure and the Origin of the Venus from Willendorf,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 2926, under “Introduction” and “Discussion,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8885675/. [Open access]","Harald Floss, “The Oldest Portable Art: The Aurignacian Ivory Figurines from the Swabian Jura (Southwest Germany),” <i>Palethnologie</i> 7 (2015), paras. 4 and 7, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.888. [Open access]","Liudmila Lbova, “The Siberian Paleolithic Site of Mal’ta: A Unique Source for the Study of Childhood Archaeology,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 3 (2021): e9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427291/. [Open access]","Rebecca Farbstein and April Nowell, “Children at Play: The Role of Novices in the Production of Europe’s Earliest Upper Paleolithic Ceramics,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 10 (2024): e0309107, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11508051/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Venus_figurine_of_Grimaldi_MAN_35308.jpg/1920px-Venus_figurine_of_Grimaldi_MAN_35308.jpg","title":"Venus figurines","desc":"Venus figurine of Grimaldi MAN 35308. Marie-Lan Taÿ Pamart. 2026-05-13. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_figurine_of_Grimaldi_MAN_35308.jpg","alt":"Venus figurine of Grimaldi MAN 35308"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-072","num":"72","category":"World · History","question":"Cut from limestone that outcrops 730 kilometres (450 miles) away across the Alps, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has bands wound round her head and no face beneath them.","answer":"Venus of Willendorf","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 30,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1908</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Venus of Willendorf</b> is a woman 11.1 centimetres (4.4 inches) tall, cut from oolitic limestone and still tinted here and there with red ochre, which probably once covered her entirely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Josef Szombathy's excavation above the Danube at Willendorf in Lower Austria turned her up on 7 August 1908, lifted by a workman named Johann Veran, and she has been in Vienna ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She was carved about 30,000 years ago, in the Gravettian, and she has become one of the best-known objects in the whole of prehistory, reproduced past counting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her arms are thin and laid across heavy breasts, her belly and hips are enormous, and her legs taper to nothing where the feet should be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The carver had to work around hard iron-oxide lumps scattered through the stone, and the cavity at her navel may be where one of them broke away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The head is the strangest part of her, wrapped in bands that run right round it and leave no face at all, not an eye and not a mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some read those bands as braided or coiled hair, others as a woven cap or a netted snood, which would make her a picture of a textile that rotted away long ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reading is not idle, because carvers far to the east at Mal'ta in Siberia marked their figures with what look like fitted fur coveralls and hoods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stone she is cut from does not occur anywhere near Willendorf, and in 2022 a scan of her interior found its closest match at an outcrop by Lake Garda, some 730 kilometres (450 miles) off on the far side of the Alps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether she was carried north over the mountains or her owners walked that road themselves, she is evidence that the Ice Age world was wider and better connected than a palm-sized carving suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Venus of Willendorf","questions":["A workman named Johann Veran picked the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of Josef Szombathy's excavation above the Danube in Lower Austria on 7 August 1908.","Traces of red ochre once covered the whole of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an 11-centimetre limestone woman whose stone comes from an outcrop far south of the Alps."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","prehistory","austria"],"sources":["Gerhard W. Weber, Alexander Lukeneder, Mathias Harzhauser, et al., “The Microstructure and the Origin of the Venus from Willendorf,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 2926, under “Introduction,” “Results” and “Discussion,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8885675/. [Open access]","Naturhistorisches Museum Wien, “Venus-Forschung,” Prähistorische Abteilung, accessed 31 July 2026, https://www.nhm.at/en/research/prehistory/research/venus-research. [Open access]","Gillian Morriss-Kay, “A New Hypothesis on the Creation of the Hohle Fels “Venus” Figurine,” <i>Palethnologie</i> 5 (2013), para. 1, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.5135. [Open access]","Harald Floss, “The Oldest Portable Art: The Aurignacian Ivory Figurines from the Swabian Jura (Southwest Germany),” <i>Palethnologie</i> 7 (2015), para. 4, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.888. [Open access]","Liudmila Lbova, “The Siberian Paleolithic Site of Mal’ta: A Unique Source for the Study of Childhood Archaeology,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 3 (2021): e9, under “Clothing,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427291/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Venus_of_Willendorf_-_All_sides.jpg/1920px-Venus_of_Willendorf_-_All_sides.jpg","title":"Venus of Willendorf","desc":"Venus of Willendorf as shown at the Naturhistorisches Museum in Vienna, Austria, in January 2020. Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_of_Willendorf_-_All_sides.jpg","alt":"Venus of Willendorf - All sides"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-073","num":"73","category":"World · History","question":"Laurel-leaf blades 35 centimetres (14 inches) long and barely a centimetre (0.4 inches) thick make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the high point of stoneworking in Ice Age Europe.","answer":"Solutrean","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 25,000 – 19,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1869, by Gabriel de Mortillet</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Solutrean</b> is an Upper Paleolithic industry of France, Spain and Portugal, made between roughly 25,000 and 19,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gabriel de Mortillet gave it the name in 1869, after Solutré in eastern Burgundy, which lies well away from the south-western country where most of its sites are found. It falls across the coldest stretch of the last Ice Age, when northern Europe emptied and people were pressed back into the south-western corner of the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its knappers thinned the blanks by percussion and finished them by pressure flaking, pressing the last flakes off with a point of antler, and some pieces had been heated beforehand to make the flint work more sweetly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The result is the laurel leaf, a blade worked on both faces and thinned to a few millimetres, and the finest of them are among the most beautiful things the Stone Age produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br>  A hoard found at Volgu holds laurel leaves over 30 centimetres (12 inches) long and barely a centimetre (0.4 inches) thick, worked far beyond anything hunting requires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Solutrean also brings the eyed needle to western Europe, where finer sewing answered the cold of the glacial maximum, though fitted clothing had been made with awls and burins long before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two things commonly said about it are worth knowing precisely because both of them are wrong. The horse bones heaped at Solutré were long explained as herds driven off the rock above, a story that held until the 1950s, but they are now read as the leavings of hunters ambushing horses along their migration route at the foot of the rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The other is the claim that Solutrean people crossed the Atlantic ice to become the Clovis hunters, which almost every archaeologist and geneticist rejects: the one Clovis genome ever read is closer to living Native Americans than to anybody in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Solutrean","questions":["Gabriel de Mortillet named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1869 after Solutré in Burgundy, and it spans the coldest stretch of the Ice Age between 22,000 and 17,000 years ago.","Working by pressure flaking with an antler point, and heating the flint first to soften it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knappers thinned blades on both faces to a few millimetres."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Morten Rasmussen, Sarah L. Anzick, Michael R. Waters, et al., \"The genome of a Late Pleistocene human from a Clovis burial site in western Montana,\" <i>Nature</i> 506, no. 7487 (2014): 225–229, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4878442/. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren, Robert J. Patten, Michael J. O'Brien, and David J. Meltzer, \"Refuting the technological cornerstone of the Ice-Age Atlantic crossing hypothesis,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 40, no. 7 (2013): 2934–2941, https://cladistics.coas.missouri.edu/assets/pdf_articles/Eren_et_al_2013.pdf. [Open access]","João Cascalheira and Nuno Bicho, \"On the chronological structure of the Solutrean in southern Iberia,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 9 (2015): e0137308, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0137308. [Open access]","João Cascalheira, \"Territoriality and the organization of technology during the Last Glacial Maximum in southwestern Europe,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0225828, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0225828. [Open access]","Ian Gilligan, Francesco d'Errico, Luc Doyon, Wei Wang, and Yaroslav V. Kuzmin, \"Paleolithic eyed needles and the evolution of dress,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 26 (2024): eadp2887, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11212769/. [Open access]","Julie Bachellerie, \"Chapitre 10. Outillage « solutréen » et schémas de production associés,\" <i>PALEO</i>, hors-série (2025): 150–193, https://doi.org/10.4000/14tzv. [Open access]","Elaine Turner, \"Horse, reindeer and bison hunters at Solutré: an archaeozoological analysis of a Magdalenian bone assemblage,\" <i>Bonner zoologische Beiträge</i> 50 (1999): 143–182, https://archive.org/details/biostor-136046. [Open access]","Thierry Aubry, Bruce Bradley, Miguel Almeida, et al., \"Solutrean laurel leaf production at Maîtreaux: an experimental approach guided by techno-economic analysis,\" <i>World Archaeology</i> 40, no. 1 (2008): 48–66, https://doi.org/10.1080/00438240701843538. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Biface_feuille_de_laurier.JPG","title":"Solutrean","desc":"Biface en forme de feuille de laurier. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biface_feuille_de_laurier.JPG","alt":"Biface feuille de laurier"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-074","num":"74","category":"World · History","question":"Toolmakers who worked antler as readily as flint and depended so completely on reindeer gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, named after a Dordogne rock shelter, its old label as the age of the reindeer.","answer":"Magdalenian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 21,000 – 14,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Magdalenian</b> is the last Upper Paleolithic industry of western Europe, made from roughly 21,000 to 14,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It takes its name from the rock shelter of La Madeleine in the Dordogne, and it was Gabriel de Mortillet, who also named the Solutrean, who attached the label to the whole epoch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its people leaned heavily on reindeer, so much so that scholars long called this the age of the reindeer, though bison and horse were at first more abundant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Where the Solutreans had put their skill into stone, the Magdalenians put it into antler and bone, turning out barbed harpoon heads, eyed needles, fine awls and spear-throwers that added range and force to a thrown shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The barbs and shapes of those harpoon heads change steadily through the sequence, which is why archaeologists have long used them to slot a site into its part of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> This is also the great age of cave painting, and the polychrome bison crowded across the ceiling at Altamira in northern Spain are Magdalenian work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So is much of the finest portable art, including spear-throwers carved into animals and the pair of swimming reindeer carved from ivory found at Montastruc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Burial could be elaborate, and a woman laid in El Mirón cave in Cantabria was covered in red ochre, with the engraved block beside her stained the same colour. As the ice pulled back, Magdalenian groups pushed north and east into Belgium, Germany and Poland, reaching Britain by about 14,800 years ago, where Gough's Cave at Cheddar records a short seasonal stay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient genomes tie these scattered groups together and trace their ancestry back through the Gravettian populations of western Europe to the much earlier Aurignacian, setting them apart from the Gravettians of central and southern Europe, and the whole way of life gave way around 14,000 years ago to people spreading from the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Magdalenian","questions":["The polychrome bison crowding the ceiling at Altamira are the work of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose people also carved spear-throwers and engraved a pair of swimming reindeer on mammoth ivory.","As the ice pulled back the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread north and east into Belgium, Germany and Poland, reaching Britain by about 14,700 years ago for short seasonal stays."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Qiaomei Fu, Cosimo Posth, Mateja Hajdinjak, et al., \"The genetic history of Ice Age Europe,\" <i>Nature</i> 534, no. 7606 (2016): 200–205, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4943878/. [Open access]","Cosimo Posth, He Yu, Ayshin Ghalichi, et al., \"Palaeogenomics of Upper Palaeolithic to Neolithic European hunter-gatherers,\" <i>Nature</i> 615, no. 7950 (2023): 117–126, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9977688/. [Open access]","M. García-Diez, D. L. Hoffmann, J. Zilhão, et al., \"Uranium series dating reveals a long sequence of rock art at Altamira Cave (Santillana del Mar, Cantabria),\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 40, no. 11 (2013): 4098–4106, https://cir.cenieh.es/handle/20.500.12136/420. [Open access]","Silvia M. Bello, Lucile Crété, Julia Galway-Witham, and Simon A. Parfitt, \"Knapping tools in Magdalenian contexts: new evidence from Gough's Cave (Somerset, UK),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 12 (2021): e0261031, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0261031. [Open access]","Andy Needham, Izzy Wisher, Andrew Langley, Matthew Amy, and Aimée Little, \"Art by firelight? Using experimental and digital techniques to explore Magdalenian engraved plaquette use at Montastruc (France),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0266146, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0266146. [Open access]","Pere Gelabert, Victoria Oberreiter, Lawrence Guy Straus, et al., \"A sedimentary ancient DNA perspective on human and carnivore persistence through the Late Pleistocene in El Mirón Cave, Spain,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 107, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11696082/. [Open access]","Henri Breuil, \"Le Magdalénien,\" <i>Bulletin de la Société préhistorique française</i> 51, no. 8 (1954): 59–66, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bspf_0249-7638_1954_hos_51_8_12411. [Open access]","Gabriel de Mortillet, <i>Le Préhistorique: Antiquité de l'homme</i>, 2nd ed. (Paris: C. Reinwald, 1885), chap. 4, https://archive.org/details/laprhistoirean00mort. [Open access]","Andreas Maier, Carmen Liebermann, and Sebastian J. Pfeifer, \"Beyond the Alps and Tatra Mountains—the 20–14 ka repopulation of the northern mid-latitudes as inferred from palimpsests deciphered with keys from western and central Europe,\" <i>Journal of Paleolithic Archaeology</i> 3 (2020): 398–452, https://doi.org/10.1007/s41982-019-00045-1. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Atlatls%2C_17-12_kya%2C_upper_from_La_Madeleine_rockshelter%2C_lower_from_Le_Mas_d%27Azil%2C_France_-_Houston_Museum_of_Natural_Science_-_DSC02033.JPG/1920px-Atlatls%2C_17-12_kya%2C_upper_from_La_Madeleine_rockshelter%2C_lower_from_Le_Mas_d%27Azil%2C_France_-_Houston_Museum_of_Natural_Science_-_DSC02033.JPG","title":"Magdalenian","desc":"Exhibit in the Houston Museum of Natural Science, Houston, Texas, USA. Photography was permitted in the museum without restriction. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlatls,_17-12_kya,_upper_from_La_Madeleine_rockshelter,_lower_from_Le_Mas_d%27Azil,_France_-_Houston_Museum_of_Natural_Science_-_DSC02033.JPG","alt":"Atlatls, 17-12 kya, upper from La Madeleine rockshelter, lower from Le Mas d'Azil, France - Houston Museum of Natural Science"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-075","num":"75","category":"World · History","question":"Made with ochre and charcoal on walls deep underground, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> such as the bison of Altamira or the horses of Chauvet usually shows large game animals rather than people.","answer":"cave painting","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 45,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Leang Tedongnge, Sulawesi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 40,800 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>cave painting</b> is an image applied to the wall or ceiling of a cave or rock shelter, and archaeologists file it, along with engravings and reliefs, under the heading of parietal art. The palette is narrow and earthy, with reds and yellows coming from ochre, an iron-rich earth, and blacks from charcoal or ground manganese oxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Painters spread the colour with a finger, a pad of moss or fur, or a brush of animal hair, and they also blew a fine mist of it through a hollow bone or reed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Blowing pigment around a hand held flat against the rock leaves a stencil, and those pale hand shapes turn up in caves from France to Indonesia and Argentina. Large game animals dominate the subjects — horses, bison, aurochs, deer, mammoths and lions — while people are rare and sketchy, often outnumbered by dots, grids and other abstract signs. <br><br> The oldest securely dated painting is a Sulawesi warty pig at Leang Tedongnge in Indonesia, made at least 45,500 years ago, and a scene of pigs and human-like figures in a nearby cave has been dated to 51,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Europe's earliest date so far is a red disk at El Castillo in northern Spain, at least 40,800 years old, though the lions and rhinoceroses of Chauvet, drawn around 36,000 years ago, are better known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lascaux, painted between about 21,500 and 21,000 years ago and stumbled into by four teenagers in 1940, holds some 600 animal images, horses far outnumbering the stags, aurochs, ibexes and bison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the polychrome bison of Altamira came to light in 1879, the leading specialists refused to believe that anything so accomplished could be so old and accused their finder of forgery. One of them, Émile Cartailhac, published a famous apology in 1902, but what the paintings actually meant is still open, with hunting magic, a symbolic order and the visions of trance all proposed and none of them fitting every cave.","citation":"","answerText":"cave painting","questions":["Blowing a mist of pigment around a hand pressed to the rock leaves the pale stencils that mark a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in sites from France to Indonesia and Argentina.","The oldest securely dated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a warty pig at Leang Tedongnge in Indonesia, made at least 45,500 years ago, older than anything so far known from Europe."],"tags":["practice","art","archaeology","prehistory"],"sources":["Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, et al., \"Oldest cave art found in Sulawesi,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abd4648. [Open access]","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim et al., \"Narrative cave art in Indonesia by 51,200 years ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 631 (2024): 814–818, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07541-7. [Open access]","A. W. G. Pike, D. L. Hoffmann, M. García-Díez, et al., \"U-Series Dating of Paleolithic Art in 11 Caves in Spain,\" <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6087 (2012): 1409–1413, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1219957. [Paywalled]","Anita Quiles, Hélène Valladas, Hervé Bocherens et al., \"A high-precision chronological model for the decorated Upper Paleolithic cave of Chauvet-Pont d'Arc, Ardèche, France,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 17 (2016): 4670–4675, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1523158113. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Themes\" and \"Dating the Figures at Lascaux,\" <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"The Raw Materials,\" <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 3 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/raw-materials. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"The Techniques,\" <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 3 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/techniques. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Lubang_Jeriji_Sal%C3%A9h_cave_painting_of_Bull.jpg","title":"cave painting","desc":"Representation of a wild bovid, the Banteng, made in ochre, discovered in the Lubang Jeriji Saléh cave, East Kalimantan, Borneo, Indonesia, dated to 40 ka. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lubang_Jeriji_Sal%C3%A9h_cave_painting_of_Bull.jpg","alt":"Lubang Jeriji Saléh cave painting of Bull"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"wh-076","num":"76","category":"World · History","question":"Sealed by a rockfall for some 20,000 years, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Ardèche region of southern France preserved charcoal lions and rhinoceroses that three cavers found in December 1994.","answer":"Chauvet Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 37,000 – 33,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">and c. 31,000 – 28,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 2014</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chauvet Cave</b> is a limestone cavern in the Ardèche of southern France, near the natural arch of Pont-d'Arc, and it holds some of the oldest and best preserved figurative art known. Three cavers, Jean-Marie Chauvet, Éliette Brunel Deschamps and Christian Hillaire, felt cool air seeping from a narrow gap in the cliff on 18 December 1994 and dug their way through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A rockfall had closed the original entrance some 20,000 years earlier, sealing the interior so completely that hearths, torch smears and the footprints of animals and of a child survived on the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon dates on charcoal from the black drawings point to two periods of use, one about 37,000 to 33,500 years ago and another roughly 31,000 to 28,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those dates upset the accepted sequence of Paleolithic styles, and a minority of specialists still argue from style and stratigraphy that the art is thousands of years younger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Roughly a thousand images have been catalogued, more than 400 of them animals of at least 14 species, among them cave lions, mammoths, woolly rhinoceroses, cave bears, aurochs, ibex and an owl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The artists scraped the rock pale for a background, cut outlines with a flint edge, smudged charcoal with a finger to shade, and blew pigment around a flattened hand to leave a stencil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Overlapping heads suggest movement and depth, above all on the Panel of the Horses, where four charcoal muzzles lean from the wall beside two rhinoceroses locking horns. Bears occupied the cave far longer than people did, scooping about 300 nests into the clay floor and leaving several thousand bones, among them about 190 skulls, one set on a block of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cave has never been open to visitors, only researchers, so most people meet it through Werner Herzog's 2010 film <i>Cave of Forgotten Dreams</i> or the full-size replica opened nearby in 2015.","citation":"","answerText":"Chauvet Cave","questions":["Radiocarbon dates on its charcoal drawings, some as old as 37,000 years, made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> far older than experts expected and upset the accepted sequence of Paleolithic styles.","Closed to all but researchers, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the public mainly through Werner Herzog's 2010 film <i>Cave of Forgotten Dreams</i> and the full-size replica opened nearby in 2015."],"tags":["place","art","archaeology","prehistory","france"],"sources":["Anita Quiles, Hélène Valladas, Hervé Bocherens et al., \"A high-precision chronological model for the decorated Upper Paleolithic cave of Chauvet-Pont d'Arc, Ardèche, France,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 17 (2016): 4670–4675, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1523158113. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Discovery,\" <i>La grotte Chauvet-Pont d'Arc</i>, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/chauvet/en/discovery-3. [Open access]","Benjamin Sadier, Jean-Jacques Delannoy, Lucilla Benedetti, et al., \"Further constraints on the Chauvet cave artwork elaboration,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 21 (2012): 8002–8006, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1118593109. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Bear hollows,\" <i>La grotte Chauvet-Pont d'Arc</i>, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/chauvet/en/bear-hollows. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Aurignacian Cave Art,\" <i>La grotte Chauvet-Pont d'Arc</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/chauvet/en/aurignacian-cave-art. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Paintings_from_the_Chauvet_cave_%28museum_replica%29.jpg","title":"Chauvet Cave","desc":"Replica of the painting from the Chauvet cave, in the Anthropos museum, Brno. The original art is approximately 31,000 years old, probably Aurignacien. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paintings_from_the_Chauvet_cave_(museum_replica).jpg","alt":"Paintings from the Chauvet cave (museum replica)"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Chauvet Cave","at":[4.4142,44.3875]}},
{"id":"wh-077","num":"77","category":"World · History","question":"Painted in the Dordogne some 21,000 years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closed to visitors in 1963 after green algal films spread across walls that had been safe for twenty millennia.","answer":"Lascaux","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 21,500 – 21,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1963</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1979</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lascaux</b> is a cave near Montignac in the Dordogne, in south-western France, whose density and confidence of drawing earned it the nickname Sistine Chapel of prehistory. Four boys aged 15 to 17 found it on 12 September 1940 — Marcel Ravidat, Georges Agniel, Simon Coencas and Jacques Marsal — and two of them later worked there as guides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cave is traditionally divided into seven sectors, among them the Hall of the Bulls, the Axial Gallery, the Nave and the Shaft, and its floor drops 19 metres (62 feet) from the entrance to the foot of the Shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 600 animals are painted or drawn on the walls, dominated by horses, with stags and aurochs equal behind them and then ibex and bison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is one human being in the whole cave, in the Shaft Scene, where a bird-headed figure lies beside a wounded bison in one of the very few narrative images Palaeolithic art attempts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Radiocarbon dates measured on reindeer bone from the main sectors put the occupations between about 21,500 and 21,000 years ago, at the turn from the Badegoulian to the Magdalenian and older than the 17,000 years long quoted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those reindeer are part of the puzzle, since the animal the dating rests on is one the painters left off the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Working in the dark meant carrying fire, and over a hundred lamps have been found, among them a polished red sandstone bowl with a handle and an engraved decoration, from the foot of the Shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Figures set where wall meets ceiling raise the question of how they were reached, and André Glory reported encrusted sockets for interlocked beams along the Axial Gallery, though the cave's record warns against generalising from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tourism then ruined the air: green algal films spread in the 1960s, the cave closed in 1963, and a fungus raised white stains in 2001 and black ones later that year, so visitors go to the facsimiles Lascaux II and Lascaux IV, of 1983 and 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lascaux","questions":["Discovered on 12 September 1940 by four boys aged 15 to 17, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds some 600 painted and drawn animals, among which the horse far outnumbers every other.","The one human being in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a bird-headed figure in the Shaft lying beside a wounded bison, in one of Paleolithic art's rare scenes."],"tags":["place","art","archaeology","prehistory","france"],"sources":["Ministère de la Culture, “The Four Discoverers,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/four-discoverers. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “The Lascaux Cave,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/lascaux-cave. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Themes,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/themes-0. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Dating the Figures at Lascaux,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/dating-figures-lascaux. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Lighting,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/lighting. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Access to the Walls,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/access-walls. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Lascaux II and III,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/lascaux-ii-and-iii. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Lascaux IV,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/lascaux-iv. [Open access]","Zélia Bontemps et al., “Microbial Diversity and Secondary Metabolism Potential in Relation to Dark Alterations in Paleolithic Lascaux Cave,” <i>npj Biofilms and Microbiomes</i> 10 (2024): 121, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41522-024-00589-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Lascaux-IV_01.jpg/1920px-Lascaux-IV_01.jpg","title":"Lascaux","desc":"Lascaux 4, Montignac, Dordogne, France. Pictures takes in the part called atelier. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lascaux-IV_01.jpg","alt":"Lascaux-IV 01"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Lascaux","at":[1.17,45.0536]}},
{"id":"wh-078","num":"78","category":"World · History","question":"Dismissed as a forgery for over twenty years after 1879, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Cantabria holds a crowded ceiling of life-sized bison whose shoulders and flanks are the rock's own bulges.","answer":"Cave of Altamira","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 36,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Entered again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1879</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1985</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cave of Altamira</b> lies on a hillside above Santillana del Mar in Cantabria, in northern Spain, and its painted ceiling was the first Paleolithic art the world had to argue about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A local man, Modesto Cubillas, came upon the blocked entrance around 1868 while out hunting and told the landowner Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola, who first looked inside in 1875. Sautuola went back in 1879 with his young daughter María, and it was she, small enough to look up in comfort, who noticed the animals crowding the low ceiling. He published the find in 1880 in a short pamphlet arguing that it was Paleolithic, at which the leading prehistorians called it a fraud and pointed to a painter who had been staying with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sautuola died discredited in 1888, and only after similar caves turned up in France did Émile Cartailhac recant, in an 1902 article he titled <i>Mea culpa d'un sceptique</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Polychrome Hall carries about 25 large figures in red, black and violet, mostly bison between 125 and 170 centimetres (4 ft 1 in and 5 ft 7 in) long, with a hind of two metres (6 ft 7 in) among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Volume came from the rock itself: painters chose swellings in the limestone for a bison's shoulder and flank, then engraved and outlined over ochre, charcoal and iron oxide. A rockfall later sealed the cave mouth, and uranium-series dates on the calcite crusts run from a red sign at least 36,000 years old to Magdalenian work near the end, spanning more than 20,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Breath and lamps did more damage than the intervening millennia, so Spain closed the cave in 1977, let in 8,500 people a year from 1982, then shut it again in 2002 over green mould.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A full-size replica opened at the museum next door in 2001, and from 2014 a conservation study drew five visitors a week by lot to see the original ceiling in protective suits.","citation":"","answerText":"Cave of Altamira","questions":["Found blocked around 1868 by a hunter and reopened by Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried a ceiling his young daughter María first noticed while looking up.","Damaged more by breath and lamps than by the millennia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was closed in 1977 and again in 2002, and a full-size replica opened next door in 2001."],"tags":["place","art","archaeology","prehistory","spain"],"sources":["M. García-Díez, D. L. Hoffmann, J. Zilhão, et al., \"Uranium series dating reveals a long sequence of rock art at Altamira Cave (Santillana del Mar, Cantabria),\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 40, no. 11 (2013): 4098–4106, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jas.2013.05.011. [Open access]","Émile Cartailhac, \"Les cavernes ornées de dessins. La grotte d'Altamira, Espagne. Mea culpa d'un sceptique,\" <i>L'Anthropologie</i> 13 (1902): 348–354, https://archive.org/details/Cartailhac1902. [Open access]","Oscar Moro Abadía, \"The reception of Palaeolithic art at the turn of the twentieth century: between archaeology and art history,\" <i>Journal of Art Historiography</i> 12 (June 2015), https://arthistoriography.wordpress.com/12-jun-2015/. [Open access]","Vicente Bayarri, Alfredo Prada, and Francisco García, \"A Multimodal Research Approach to Assessing the Karst Structural Conditions of the Ceiling of a Cave with Palaeolithic Cave Art Paintings: Polychrome Hall at Altamira Cave (Spain),\" <i>Sensors</i> 23, no. 22 (2023): 9153, https://doi.org/10.3390/s23229153. [Open access]","Alfredo Prada and Vicente Bayarri, \"Methodology for the Monitoring and Control of the Alterations Related to Biodeterioration and Physical-Chemical Processes Produced on the Paintings on the Ceiling of the Polychrome Hall at Altamira,\" <i>Conservation</i> 4, no. 4 (2024): 703–30, https://doi.org/10.3390/conservation4040042. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/Altamira%2C_bison.jpg","title":"Cave of Altamira","desc":"A modern interpretation of the bison from the Altamira cave ceiling, one of the most famous paintings from there. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altamira,_bison.jpg","alt":"Altamira, bison"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Cave of Altamira","at":[-4.1203,43.3825]}},
{"id":"wh-079","num":"79","category":"World · History","question":"Pecked, incised or abraded into stone rather than painted onto it, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives in the open air, as at Murujuga in Australia or the Côa Valley in Portugal.","answer":"petroglyph","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Côa Valley</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 30,000 – 12,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>petroglyph</b> is an image cut into rock, made by taking stone away from the surface rather than adding pigment to it, and that difference gives the form its extraordinary durability. The commonest technique in the older phases was pecking, striking the surface again and again with a hard hammerstone to leave a broad line of fresh, pale stone. Engravers also incised fine grooves with a sharp point and abraded whole areas smooth, and many panels carry all three treatments on one surface. In arid country the effect depends on desert varnish, a thin dark skin of clay and manganese that builds slowly on exposed rock, so a new cut reads as a bright line against a near-black ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That contrast fades as varnish reforms inside the cut, which is why the most weathered images on a panel are usually its oldest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Dating them absolutely is genuinely hard, because varnish does not thicken at a steady rate and no laboratory method works across every rock type, so a credible age rests on several converging arguments rather than one measurement. The open-air Palaeolithic engravings of the Côa Valley in northern Portugal came to light during survey work for a dam that would have drowned them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeologists and journalists campaigned under the slogan that petroglyphs cannot swim, a change of government cancelled the dam in 1995, and UNESCO listed the valley three years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Murujuga, on the Burrup Peninsula in Western Australia, holds the densest concentration known anywhere, and its Traditional Owners won World Heritage listing for it in 2025 after decades of campaigning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Industrial plants stand inside that rock-art landscape, and whether their emissions are damaging the engravings is the sharpest question hanging over the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"petroglyph","questions":["In arid country a fresh <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows as a bright line against the dark skin of desert varnish, and as that varnish reforms the oldest images slowly fade back.","Campaigners saved the Côa Valley engravings from a dam under the slogan that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot swim, and Portugal cancelled the project in 1995 before UNESCO listed the valley."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","prehistory"],"sources":["Usha F. Lingappa, Chris M. Yeager, Ajay Sharma, et al., \"An ecophysiological explanation for manganese enrichment in rock varnish,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 25 (2021): e2025188118, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2025188118. [Open access]","Thierry Aubry and Jorge Davide Sampaio, \"Fariseu: New Chronological Evidence for Open-air Palaeolithic Art in the Côa Valley (Portugal),\" <i>Antiquity</i> 81, no. 312 (2007), https://antiquity.ac.uk/ProjGall/aubry316/. [Open access]","K. A. Evans et al., \"Impacts of Industrial Emissions on Rock Art at Murujuga, Western Australia,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 13805, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-44180-6. [Open access]","Fundação Côa Parque, \"The Côa Battle: World Heritage,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://arte-coa.pt/en/the-coa-battle/. [Open access]","Jolam T. Neumann et al., \"Artificial Weathering of Rock Types Bearing Petroglyphs from Murujuga, Western Australia,\" <i>Heritage Science</i> 10 (2022): 77, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-022-00706-5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Negev_camel_petroglyph.jpg/1920px-Negev_camel_petroglyph.jpg","title":"petroglyph","desc":"Petroglyph of a camel; Negev, southern Israel. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Negev_camel_petroglyph.jpg","alt":"Negev camel petroglyph"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-081","num":"81","category":"World · History","question":"By adding a forearm's length of leverage to the throw, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> launched a light spear far faster and further than any bare arm could manage.","answer":"spear-thrower","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest hooks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 20,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>spear-thrower</b> is a rod or board with a hook or spur at one end, which engages the butt of a light spear and works as an extension of the thrower's arm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hunter grips it near the far end, lays the spear along it, and whips the whole assembly forward, so the hook travels through a far longer arc than the hand alone could describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That extra leverage turns into speed at the tip, and controlled tests give darts launched this way roughly two-thirds more velocity than javelins thrown by hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gain in force is enough to drive a point through the hide of large game from a distance that keeps the hunter clear of hooves and horns. One study also found that it evens out the gap between men's and women's throwing speeds, which hand-throwing does not, so the weapon widens who can hunt effectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The device is far older than the names we know it by, which come from peoples who were still using it when Europeans arrived: atlatl from Nahuatl in Mexico, woomera from Australia. In Europe the oldest definite hooks are Magdalenian, from about 20,000 years ago, and the reindeer-antler piece from Combe Saunière in south-west France, often called the earliest, comes from a layer whose dates are disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Magdalenian carvers turned these hooks into sculpture, and the best known, from the cave of Mas d'Azil in the Pyrenees, shows a young animal turning to look at two birds perched on something emerging from its body, traditionally read as a dropping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bow displaced it across most of the world, being easier to aim and needing a far lighter projectile, but it held on in Australia and among Arctic hunters, who throw from a kayak where a bow would be unwieldy. It was still a weapon of war in Mexico when the Spanish arrived, which is why the Nahuatl word is the one that stuck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"spear-thrower","questions":["Known as the atlatl to the Nahuatl of Mexico and the woomera in Australia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was still in use by both peoples when Europeans first arrived.","The oldest securely dated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a reindeer-antler hook from Combe Saunière in south-west France, made about 17,500 years ago in the last stretch of the Ice Age."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Michelle R. Bebber, Briggs Buchanan, Metin I. Eren, Robert S. Walker, and Dexter Zirkle, \"Atlatl use equalizes female and male projectile weapon velocity,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 13349, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10432391/. [Open access]","Pierre Cattelain and Jean-Marc Pétillon, \"« Type 2a », the earliest Palaeolithic spear-thrower: a new Middle Magdalenian object from Isturitz (Pyrénées-Atlantiques, France) and its implications,\" <i>PALEO</i> 26 (2015): 17–32, https://doi.org/10.4000/paleo.3025. [Open access]","Pierre Cattelain, \"Un crochet de propulseur solutréen de la grotte de Combe-Saunière 1 (Dordogne),\" <i>Bulletin de la Société préhistorique française</i> 86, no. 7 (1989): 213–216, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bspf_0249-7638_1989_num_86_7_9386. [Open access]","Justin Coppe, Noora Taipale, and Veerle Rots, \"Terminal ballistic analysis of impact fractures reveals the use of spearthrower 31 ky ago at Maisières-Canal, Belgium,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 18305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600151/. [Open access]","Paule Steiner, \"Un petit poucet magdalénien ?,\" <i>Cahiers de littérature orale</i> 67–68 (2010): 85–94, https://doi.org/10.4000/clo.904. [Open access]","Stephanie Wood, ed., <i>Online Nahuatl Dictionary</i>, s.v. \"atlatl\" (Eugene, OR: Wired Humanities Projects, University of Oregon, 2000–), https://nahuatl.wired-humanities.org/content/atlatl. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Silver_Atlatl_Spear_Thrower_MET_VS1987_394_244.jpeg/1920px-Silver_Atlatl_Spear_Thrower_MET_VS1987_394_244.jpeg","title":"spear-thrower","desc":"Chimú; Spear thrower; Metal-Implements. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_Atlatl_Spear_Thrower_MET_VS1987_394_244.jpeg","alt":"Silver Atlatl Spear Thrower 394 244"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-082","num":"82","category":"World · History","question":"Storing the archer's slow effort in a bent stave and releasing it in an instant, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let one hunter shoot from cover, kneeling, and alone.","answer":"bow and arrow","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest evidence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 64,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Sibudu Cave, South Africa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest bows</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>bow and arrow</b> is a system for storing muscular effort and letting it go in an instant, since the archer bends a springy stave, the wood holds the energy, and releasing the string drives much of it into a light shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is a different trick from throwing, where the speed of the projectile can never exceed what the arm itself can generate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because the work is done slowly and stored, a bow can be drawn kneeling, from behind cover, or held at full draw while the target walks into range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Arrows are lighter than the darts a spear-thrower needs, so a hunter carries a dozen or more and can shoot several in the time it takes to retrieve one dart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest claimed evidence comes from Sibudu Cave in South Africa, where stone segments the size of arrowheads are about 64,000 years old and a bone point close to 62,000, though the excavators call the reading tentative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br>  Outside Africa the earliest clear case was long Fa-Hien Lena in Sri Lanka, where bone arrow points made between about 48,000 and 34,000 years ago show people hunting in dense rainforest, until impact damage at Grotte Mandrin in France pushed it back to 54,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The weapon itself almost never survives: wood and sinew rot away unless they lie waterlogged, frozen or desert-dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oldest known arrow shafts were pine, found with thousands of reindeer bones at Stellmoor near Hamburg and roughly 11,500 years old; they were destroyed in an air raid in 1944, though a few fragments turned up among the excavator's effects in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest complete bows are the elm staves from Holmegaard in Denmark, roughly 10,000 to 8,500 years old and cut, like every prehistoric European bow, from a single stave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Everything since refines the same principle, from Ötzi's unfinished yew stave to composite bows of horn and sinew, and the weapon remade hunting and war by letting one person kill at a distance, from cover and alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"bow and arrow","questions":["The oldest evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from Sibudu Cave in South Africa, where stone segments the size of arrowheads, some scarred by impact, are about 64,000 years old.","Because arrows weigh far less than the darts a spear-thrower needs, a hunter with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could carry a dozen and loose several while retrieving a single dart."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Michelle C. Langley, Noel Amano, Oshan Wedage, et al., \"Bows and arrows and complex symbolic displays 48,000 years ago in the South Asian tropics,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 24 (2020): eaba3831, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7292635/. [Open access]","John Meadows, Carl Heron, Matthias Hüls, Bente Philippsen, and Mara-Julia Weber, \"Dating the lost arrow shafts from Stellmoor (Schleswig-Holstein, Germany),\" <i>Quartär</i> 65 (2018): 105–114, https://quartaer.obermaier-gesellschaft.de/pdfs/2018/2018_05_meadows.pdf. [Open access]","Jürgen Junkmanns, Johanna Klügl, Werner Schoch, Giovanna Di Pietro, and Albert Hafner, \"Neolithic and Bronze Age archery equipment from Alpine ice-patches: a review on components, construction techniques and functionality,\" <i>Journal of Neolithic Archaeology</i> 21 (2019): 283–314, https://doi.org/10.12766/jna.2019.10. [Open access]","B. W. Kooi and C. A. Bergman, \"An approach to the study of ancient archery using mathematical modelling,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 71, no. 271 (1997): 124–134, https://www.bio.vu.nl/thb/users/kooi/kobe97.pdf. [Open access]","John J. Shea and Matthew L. Sisk, \"Complex projectile technology and <i>Homo sapiens</i> dispersal into western Eurasia,\" <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2010 (2010): 100–122, https://paleoanthro.net/media/journal/content/PA20100100.pdf. [Open access]","Matthew L. Sisk and John J. Shea, \"The African origin of complex projectile technology: an analysis using tip cross-sectional area and perimeter,\" <i>International Journal of Evolutionary Biology</i> 2011 (2011): 968012, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3132613/. [Open access]","Laure Metz, Jason E. Lewis, and Ludovic Slimak, \"Bow-and-arrow, technology of the first modern humans in Europe 54,000 years ago at Mandrin, France,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 8 (2023): eadd4675, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946345/. [Open access]","Lucinda Backwell, Francesco d'Errico, and Lyn Wadley, \"Middle Stone Age bone tools from the Howiesons Poort layers, Sibudu Cave, South Africa,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 35, no. 6 (2008): 1566–1580, https://in-africa.org/wp-content/uploads/2012/12/Backwell-et-al-2008-JAS-MSA-bone-tools-from-HP-layers-Sibudu.pdf. [Open access]","Lucinda Backwell, Justin Bradfield, Kristian J. Carlson, et al., \"The antiquity of bow-and-arrow technology: evidence from Middle Stone Age layers at Sibudu Cave,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 362 (2018): 289–303, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2018.11. [Paywalled]","Marlize Lombard and Laurel Phillipson, \"Indications of bow and stone-tipped arrow use 64 000 years ago in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 84, no. 325 (2010): 635–648, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00100134. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Indian_with_bow_%26_arrow_001.jpg","title":"bow and arrow","desc":"An archer drawing a bow, from the Massengill Postcard Collection. Public domain (No restrictions), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_with_bow_%26_arrow_001.jpg","alt":"Indian with bow & arrow 001"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"wh-083","num":"83","category":"World · History","question":"Rarely more than 3 centimetres (1.2 inches) long, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not meant to be used alone but glued in rows into a wooden shaft to arm an arrow or a knife.","answer":"microlith","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest bladelets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 71,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Backed pieces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 65,000 – 59,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>microlith</b> is a small retouched stone tool struck from a slender bladelet rather than shaped down from a core, and there is no agreed ceiling on its size, with published limits running from about 25 to 50 millimetres (1 to 2 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word is built from the Greek roots for 'small' and 'stone', and the smallness is the whole point, because a tool this size is useless held in the hand. It was made to sit in a groove cut along a shaft of wood, bone or antler, fixed with birch-bark tar or resin, so that several of them worked together as a single edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Knappers sort them into backed forms, blunted along one edge so the other stays keen, and geometric forms trimmed into triangles, crescents and trapezes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Many were produced by a set procedure in which a notch is cut into the side of a blade and the blade snapped across it, yielding a fragment of predictable shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The advantage of building a weapon this way is repair, since a shaft armed with a row of small pieces can be made good in the field, whereas a single large point that snaps is finished. It also stretches scarce good stone a long way, and one standard blank can serve as a barb, a tip or a cutting edge depending on how it is mounted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A bog find at Rönneholm in southern Sweden preserves the arrangement directly, with four flint microliths still held by birch-bark tar in a groove cut down a hazel arrow tip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest are far older, since backed pieces dominate the Howiesons Poort of southern Africa between about 65,000 and 59,000 years ago, and bladelet making at Pinnacle Point reaches back some 71,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> After the Ice Age they became the standard kit across most of Europe, Africa and South Asia, which is why the little blade is the signature of the Mesolithic and of the African Late Stone Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"microlith","questions":["Backed examples reach back roughly 70,000 years in the Howiesons Poort of southern Africa, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is far older than the Mesolithic it came to define.","Because a shaft armed with a row of them can be mended in the field, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let hunters repair a weapon that a single snapped point would have ruined."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Lars Larsson, Arne Sjöström, and Carl Heron, \"The Rönneholm arrow: a find of a wooden arrow-tip with microliths in the bog Rönneholms Mosse, central Scania, southern Sweden,\" <i>Lund Archaeological Review</i> 22 (2016): 7–20, https://journals.lub.lu.se/lar/article/view/21616. [Open access]","Lars Larsson and Arne Sjöström, \"Early Mesolithic flint-tipped arrows from Sweden,\" <i>Antiquity</i> Project Gallery 85, no. 330 (2011), https://antiquity.ac.uk/projgall/larsson330. [Open access]","Amy M. Way, Paloma de la Peña, Eduardo de la Peña, and Lyn Wadley, \"Howiesons Poort backed artifacts provide evidence for social connectivity across southern Africa during the Final Pleistocene,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 9227, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9184481/. [Open access]","Manuel Will and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Regional patterns of diachronic technological change in the Howiesons Poort of southern Africa,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 9 (2020): e0239195, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0239195. [Open access]","Oshan Wedage, Andrea Picin, James Blinkhorn et al., \"Microliths in the South Asian rainforest ~45–4 ka: new insights from Fa-Hien Lena Cave, Sri Lanka,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 10 (2019): e0222606, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0222606. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski, Hong Chen, Cheng Liu, et al., \"Versatile use of microliths as a technological advantage in the miniaturization of Late Pleistocene toolkits: the case study of Neve David, Israel,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 6 (2020): e0233340, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7269238/. [Open access]","Nicolò Fasser and Federica Fontana, \"Exploring the 'microburin blow': an insight into the variability of the microburin blow method for the production of Sauveterrian geometrics in the site of Mondeval de Sora (N-E, Italy),\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 33, no. 2 (2026): art. 21, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-025-09755-z. [Open access]","Kyle S. Brown, Curtis W. Marean, Zenobia Jacobs, et al., \"An early and enduring advanced technology originating 71,000 years ago in South Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 491, no. 7425 (2012): 590–593, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature11660. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Kebaran_culture_microliths_22000-18000_BP.jpg","title":"microlith","desc":"Kebaran culture microliths 22000-18000 BP. Gary Todd. 2019-05-05. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kebaran_culture_microliths_22000-18000_BP.jpg","alt":"Kebaran culture microliths 22000-18000 BP"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-084","num":"84","category":"World · History","question":"Insulated by small ears, a thick layer of fat and outer hairs over 70 centimetres (28 inches) long, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grazed northern grasslands until its last island herds died out about 4,000 years ago.","answer":"woolly mammoth","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Split off</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800,000 – 600,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mainland end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wrangel Island</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>woolly mammoth</b> was a cold-adapted elephant of the northern hemisphere, and it split from the steppe mammoth in northeastern Siberia somewhere between 800,000 and 600,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A big bull stood 2.7 to 3.4 metres (8 ft 10 in to 11 ft 2 in) at the shoulder and weighed between 5.5 and 8 tonnes (6 and 8.8 tons), which is roughly the build of an African elephant today, though the legs were shorter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything about the body fought the cold: small ears and a short tail lost less heat, and up to 8 centimetres (3 inches) of fat lay beneath the skin. Over a woolly undercoat about 2.5 centimetres (1 inch) thick grew guard hairs longer than 70 centimetres (28 inches), and a fatty hump rose over the shoulders. The trunk ended in two finger-like tips, and curving tusks reached 4.2 metres (13 ft 9 in) in the largest bulls, used for display, for fighting and for shovelling snow off the grass below. <br><br> Its range ran from Iberia across Eurasia and over the Bering land bridge deep into North America, on a dry, open grassland that has no real equivalent anywhere today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> People lived alongside it for tens of thousands of years, painting it on cave walls, carving its ivory into figurines and flutes, and building huts out of its bones. Siberian permafrost has given back whole carcasses with hair, skin and stomach contents intact, and mammoth teeth held the oldest DNA yet sequenced from any animal, one about 1.2 million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mainland herds were gone by roughly 10,500 years ago as their grassland shrank and hunters pressed them, and genetics shows the last islanders were inbred without being doomed by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rising seas left those remnants behind, on St Paul until about 5,600 years ago and on Wrangel until about 4,000, by which time the pyramids at Giza had stood for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"woolly mammoth","questions":["Split from the steppe mammoth in Siberia perhaps 800,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried curving tusks up to 4.2 metres (13 ft 9 in), used for fighting and shovelling snow.","Preserved whole in Siberian permafrost with hair and stomach intact, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded the oldest DNA yet sequenced, from an animal about 1.2 million years old."],"tags":["animal","palaeontology","prehistory","nature"],"sources":["Tom van der Valk et al., \"Million-Year-Old DNA Sheds Light on the Genomic History of Mammoths,\" <i>Nature</i> 591, no. 7849 (2021): 265–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7116897/. [Open access]","Asier Larramendi, \"Shoulder Height, Body Mass, and Shape of Proboscideans,\" <i>Acta Palaeontologica Polonica</i> 61, no. 3 (2016): 537–74, https://doi.org/10.4202/app.00136.2014. [Open access]","Glen M. MacDonald et al., \"Pattern of Extinction of the Woolly Mammoth in Beringia,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 3 (2012): 893, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms1881. [Open access]","Marianne Dehasque et al., \"Temporal Dynamics of Woolly Mammoth Genome Erosion Prior to Extinction,\" <i>Cell</i> 187, no. 14 (2024): 3531–40, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2024.05.033. [Open access]","Russell W. Graham et al., \"Timing and Causes of Mid-Holocene Mammoth Extinction on St. Paul Island, Alaska,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 33 (2016): 9310–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4995940/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Woolly_mammoth_with_downward_spiralling_tusk.jpg/1920px-Woolly_mammoth_with_downward_spiralling_tusk.jpg","title":"woolly mammoth","desc":"Woolly mammoth from Bzianka, Poland, with one downward spiralling tusk. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woolly_mammoth_with_downward_spiralling_tusk.jpg","alt":"Woolly mammoth with downward spiralling tusk"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"wh-085","num":"85","category":"World · History","question":"Because it took mammoths, ground sloths and Diprotodon while sparing small animals, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is unusual among extinction events in having selected almost entirely by body size.","answer":"Quaternary extinction event","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Concentrated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 50,000 – 11,700 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Australia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 45,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Americas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,000 – 11,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Quaternary extinction event</b> is the loss of most of the world's very large land animals, concentrated in the last 50,000 years and continuing in places into recorded history. What marks it out is that it selected by size: almost everything over about 44 kilograms (97 pounds) was at risk, while small animals came through largely untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gone are the mammoths and mastodons, the woolly rhinoceros, the giant ground sloths and glyptodonts, the cave lion, the giant short-faced bear and Diprotodon, the largest marsupial known. The damage was very unevenly spread: Africa lost about a fifth of its large genera, Eurasia around a third, North America close to three-quarters, South America more than four-fifths and Australia nearly nine in ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That gradient tracks how long each continent's animals had lived alongside hominins, which is the most striking pattern in the whole event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Two explanations have been argued over for decades: hunting and other pressure from arriving modern humans, and the violent climate swings at the end of the last glacial period. Dung-fungus spores, which fall away as large herbivores disappear, drop before the vegetation changes in records from the eastern United States and northern Australia, and that order fits animals going first with habitat following.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against it, some analyses of North American records find the declines match falling temperatures better than they match human numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A 2025 review of 360 papers found the field still divided, and divided along disciplinary lines, so anyone who tells you the question is settled is ahead of the evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What is not in doubt is the aftermath: fruits left with nothing large enough to carry their seeds, nutrients no longer walked inland from the coasts, and the mammoth steppe giving way to tundra and forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Quaternary extinction event","questions":["Sparing Africa but taking nearly nine in ten of Australia's large genera, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hit each continent in step with how long its animals had known hominins.","Argued for decades between human hunting and violent climate swings, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remains unsettled, a 2025 review of 360 papers finding the field still divided."],"tags":["event","palaeontology","prehistory","nature"],"sources":["Paul L. Koch and Anthony D. Barnosky, \"Late Quaternary Extinctions: State of the Debate,\" <i>Annual Review of Ecology, Evolution, and Systematics</i> 37 (2006): 215–50, https://ib.berkeley.edu/labs/barnosky/Koch%20%20and%20Barnosky%202006.pdf. [Open access]","Christopher Sandom et al., \"Global Late Quaternary Megafauna Extinctions Linked to Humans, Not Climate Change,\" <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 281, no. 1787 (2014): 20133254, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2013.3254. [Open access]","Jacquelyn L. Gill et al., \"Pleistocene Megafaunal Collapse, Novel Plant Communities, and Enhanced Fire Regimes in North America,\" <i>Science</i> 326, no. 5956 (2009): 1100–1103, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1179504. [Paywalled]","Susan Rule et al., \"The Aftermath of Megafaunal Extinction: Ecosystem Transformation in Pleistocene Australia,\" <i>Science</i> 335, no. 6075 (2012): 1483–86, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1214261. [Paywalled]","Jack M. Broughton and Elic M. Weitzel, \"Population Reconstructions for Humans and Megafauna Suggest Mixed Causes for North American Pleistocene Extinctions,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 9 (2018): 5441, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-07897-1. [Open access]","Mathew Stewart et al., \"The State of the Late Quaternary Megafauna Extinction Debate: A Systematic Review and Analysis,\" <i>Frontiers in Mammal Science</i> 4 (2025): 1678231, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmamm.2025.1678231. [Open access]","Paulo R. Guimarães Jr., Mauro Galetti, and Pedro Jordano, \"Seed Dispersal Anachronisms: Rethinking the Fruits Extinct Megafauna Ate,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 3, no. 2 (2008): e1745, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0001745. [Open access]","Christopher E. Doughty et al., \"Global Nutrient Transport in a World of Giants,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 4 (2016): 868–73, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1502549112. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Ice_age_fauna_of_northern_Spain_-_Mauricio_Ant%C3%B3n.jpg/1920px-Ice_age_fauna_of_northern_Spain_-_Mauricio_Ant%C3%B3n.jpg","title":"Quaternary extinction event","desc":"Woolly mammoths were driven to extinction by climate change and human impacts. The image depicts a late Pleistocene landscape in northern Spain with woolly mammoths (Mammuthus. primigenius ), equids , a woolly rhinoceros ( Coelodonta antiquitatis ), and European cave lions ( Panthera leo spelaea )… Mauricio Antón, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ice_age_fauna_of_northern_Spain_-_Mauricio_Ant%C3%B3n.jpg","alt":"Ice age fauna of northern Spain - Mauricio Antón"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-087","num":"87","category":"World · History","question":"Buried in two caves in what is now Israel, one child with deer antlers on the chest, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> record an early departure from Africa that left almost no trace in anyone alive today.","answer":"Skhul and Qafzeh hominins","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Burials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 120,000 – 90,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Skhul dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1931–1932, by Theodore McCown</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Skhul and Qafzeh hominins</b> are the human remains from two caves in what is now Israel, one on Mount Carmel and one above Nazareth, and they are among the earliest of our species known outside Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Skhul was dug in 1931 and 1932 by Theodore McCown, part of Dorothy Garrod's work on Carmel, Qafzeh by René Neuville from 1933 and Bernard Vandermeersch from 1965, the second cave alone yielding 27 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their skulls are a mixture: the braincase is globular with a high forehead like ours, while Skhul 5 has an unbroken bar of bone above the eyes and the most jutting face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both were dated by heating flints from the deposits and measuring the light released, Qafzeh in 1988 and Skhul in 1993, putting them at 120,000 to 90,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That was far older than assumed, since the caves had been read as a late stage in a tidy local sequence. <br><br> The dates turned that sequence around, because the Neanderthals buried at Kebara and Amud, in the same corridor, proved far younger, at 70,000 to 50,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two populations were moving in and out of the same corridor rather than one replacing the other, and the Levant looks less like a staircase than a doorway used repeatedly from both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Several of the dead were buried on purpose, among the earliest deliberate burials known for our species: a child of 12 or 13 at Qafzeh lay on its back in a pit, arms bent, deer antlers on the chest, and a man at Skhul held a boar's jaw in his arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sea shells carried inland, holed by nature but worn from stringing and rubbed with colour, turn up at both caves with red ochre, among the oldest evidence anywhere for ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Genetics has since given them a melancholy role, because people outside Africa today descend overwhelmingly from a later dispersal around 50,000 years ago, so these families look like an earlier attempt that ended without issue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Skhul and Qafzeh hominins","questions":["Dated by heating buried flints to roughly 120,000 to 90,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proved far older than a tidy local sequence had assumed.","Carrying a high rounded braincase like ours yet an unbroken bar of bone above the eyes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> look to early students like a puzzling mixture of two kinds of human."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","israel"],"sources":["Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Chris Stringer, and Michael D. Petraglia, “Skhul Lithic Technology and the Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i> into Southwest Asia,” <i>Quaternary International</i> 515 (2019): 30–52, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:26de242d-8c79-44a8-9dea-5631a7400664. [Open access]","Hélène Valladas et al., “Thermoluminescence Dating of Mousterian ‘Proto-Cro-Magnon’ Remains from Israel and the Origin of Modern Man,” <i>Nature</i> 331, no. 6157 (1988): 614–16, https://doi.org/10.1038/331614a0. [Paywalled]","Rachel Sarig et al., “Malocclusion in Early Anatomically Modern Human: A Reflection on the Etiology of Modern Dental Misalignment,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 11 (2013): e80771, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3835570/. [Open access]","Hélène Coqueugniot et al., “Earliest Cranio-Encephalic Trauma from the Levantine Middle Palaeolithic: 3D Reappraisal of the Qafzeh 11 Skull,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 7 (2014): e102822, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0102822. [Open access]","Daniella E. Bar-Yosef Mayer et al., “On Holes and Strings: Earliest Displays of Human Adornment in the Middle Palaeolithic,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 7 (2020): e0234924, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0234924. [Open access]","Yossi Zaidner et al., “Evidence from Tinshemet Cave in Israel Suggests Behavioural Uniformity across <i>Homo</i> Groups in the Levantine Mid-Middle Palaeolithic circa 130,000–80,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 9, no. 5 (2025): 886–901, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12106087/. [Open access]","Rasmus Nielsen et al., “Tracing the Peopling of the World through Genomics,” <i>Nature</i> 541, no. 7637 (2017): 302–10, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5772775/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Qafzeh_11_child_skull_cast.jpg/1920px-Qafzeh_11_child_skull_cast.jpg","title":"Skhul and Qafzeh hominins","desc":"A skull cast of the Qafzeh 11 child. Natural History Museum, London. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qafzeh_11_child_skull_cast.jpg","alt":"Qafzeh 11 child skull cast"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"wh-091","num":"91","category":"World · History","question":"Neither the first man nor the only one of his day, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the nickname for the man from whom every living man descends in an unbroken line of fathers.","answer":"Y-chromosomal Adam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 200,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">2015 estimate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">254,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Y-chromosomal Adam</b> is the name given to the man from whom every living man descends through an unbroken line of fathers, son after son, down to the present. The Y chromosome makes that line traceable: it passes only from father to son, and almost all of it escapes the shuffling that mixes a person's other chromosomes each generation. It therefore travels down the male line nearly intact, picking up copying errors that serve as markers, and those markers sort all living men into one branching tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Any such tree has to converge somewhere, and he is where it converges, a statement about one stretch of DNA rather than about ancestry in general. Current estimates place him in Africa somewhere between about 200,000 and 300,000 years ago, though that figure has moved further and more often than almost any number in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The largest jump came in 2013, when a sample sent to a consumer ancestry service by the family of an African-American man named Albert Perry matched no known branch of the tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Researchers at the University of Arizona traced the same rare lineage to a few Mbo men in western Cameroon, and adding this new deepest branch pushed the common ancestor back by roughly two hundred thousand years in a single paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Critics argued the new date was too old and later analyses trimmed it, and the episode is a fair reminder that a date built from living samples can be overturned by one unusual donor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The nickname misleads in the same way its female counterpart does, because he was not the first man, not the only man of his time, and not everyone's father in any ordinary sense, since countless of his contemporaries have living descendants through lines that pass through daughters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He never met the woman his name is paired with either, since the two are worked out from different pieces of DNA with separate histories, and nothing requires them to be contemporaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Y-chromosomal Adam","questions":["In 2013 a sample from an African-American man named Albert Perry matched no known branch, and tracing it to Mbo men in Cameroon pushed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back roughly a hundred thousand years.","Worked out from a different stretch of DNA with its own history, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> need never have met the woman his name is paired with, nor lived in her time."],"tags":["concept","genetics","evolution","science"],"sources":["Fernando L. Mendez et al., \"An African American Paternal Lineage Adds an Extremely Ancient Root to the Human Y Chromosome Phylogenetic Tree,\" <i>American Journal of Human Genetics</i> 92, no. 3 (2013): 454–59, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3591855/. [Open access]","Monika Karmin et al., \"A Recent Bottleneck of Y Chromosome Diversity Coincides with a Global Change in Culture,\" <i>Genome Research</i> 25, no. 4 (2015): 459–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4381518/. [Open access]","Pille Hallast et al., \"The Y-Chromosome Tree Bursts into Leaf: 13,000 High-Confidence SNPs Covering the Human Y-Chromosome Phylogeny,\" <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 32, no. 3 (2015): 661–73, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msu327. [Open access]","Aaron P. Ragsdale et al., \"A Weakly Structured Stem for Human Origins in Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 617, no. 7962 (2023): 755–63, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06055-y. [Open access]","Teresa Rito et al., \"The First Modern Human Dispersals across Africa,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 8, no. 11 (2013): e80031, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0080031. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/World_Map_of_Y-DNA_Haplogroups.png/1920px-World_Map_of_Y-DNA_Haplogroups.png","title":"Y-chromosomal Adam","desc":"World Map of Y-Chromosome Haplogroups - Dominant Haplogroups in Pre-Colonial Populations with Possible Migrations Routes Behance page Notes: The Y-DNA haplogroup. (s) with the highest % in that area (or is notable) Population/language/region name in which the haplogroup is the majority or the… Chakazul, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:World_Map_of_Y-DNA_Haplogroups.png","alt":"World Map of Y-DNA Haplogroups"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-093","num":"93","category":"World · History","question":"Dug at the foot of the Arnhem Land escarpment, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up more than 10,000 stone artefacts dated to about 65,000 years ago, which would make it Australia's oldest human site.","answer":"Madjedbebe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 65,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">still disputed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Madjedbebe</b> is a sandstone rock shelter at the foot of the Arnhem Land escarpment in the far north of Australia, on the country of the Mirarr people and ringed by Kakadu National Park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Older publications call it Malakunanja II, and it stands inside the Jabiluka mineral lease, a uranium deposit the Mirarr have long fought to keep unmined. The apron of sand under the overhang has been dug four times, in 1973, in 1989, and again in 2012 and 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The recent work was led by Chris Clarkson under an agreement with the Gundjeihmi Aboriginal Corporation that gave the Mirarr control of where the trenches went and of what came out of them. Those two seasons produced more than 10,000 stone artefacts from the lowest dense band alone, and sand grains from that band were dated by optically stimulated luminescence, which measures how long a grain has lain buried in the dark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  The result, published in 2017, put the first occupation at about 65,000 years ago, 5,000 to 15,000 years earlier than the oldest dates from any other Australian site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same layer holds the earliest known hatchet heads with ground edges, the earliest seed-grinding stones in Australia, and quantities of ground ochre along with glittering mica, apparently used as pigment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charred plant remains show what was eaten there between 65,000 and 53,000 years ago, pandanus nuts and yams among them, so the shelter records a way of living rather than a single visit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The date is not settled, because the deposit is loose sand worked over by termites, and critics led by Jim Allen and James O'Connell argue that rocks and artefacts could have sunk into the older sand beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Molecular clocks have been read as pointing to settlement nearer 50,000 years ago, though the split from Eurasians is put at about 58,000 in a range wide enough to hold 65,000, which on the excavators' reckoning leaves people among the giant marsupials for over 20,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Madjedbebe","questions":["Australia's oldest ground-edge hatchet heads and its earliest seed-grinding stones came from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a rock shelter on Mirarr country whose lowest band also held ground ochre and glittering mica.","Because its loose sand is riddled by termites and later pits, critics argue heavy tools sank into older layers, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its 65,000-year date stay disputed."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","australia"],"sources":["Chris Clarkson, Zenobia Jacobs, Ben Marwick, et al., \"Human occupation of northern Australia by 65,000 years ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 547, no. 7663 (2017): 306–310, http://faculty.washington.edu/bmarwick/PDFs/Clarkson_Jacobs_Marwick_2017.pdf. [Open access]","Chris Clarkson, Richard G. Roberts, Zenobia Jacobs, et al., \"Reply to comments on Clarkson et al. (2017) 'Human occupation of northern Australia by 65,000 years ago,'\" <i>Australian Archaeology</i> 84, no. 1 (2018): 84–89, https://anthropology.washington.edu/sites/anthropology/files/documents/research/clarkson-roberts-jacobs-marwick-et-al-2018-aa-reply.pdf. [Open access]","James F. O'Connell, Jim Allen, Martin A. J. Williams, et al., \"When did <i>Homo sapiens</i> first reach Southeast Asia and Sahul?,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 34 (2018): 8482–8490, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6112744/. [Open access]","Chris Clarkson, Mike Smith, Ben Marwick, et al., \"The archaeology, chronology and stratigraphy of Madjedbebe (Malakunanja II): a site in northern Australia with early occupation,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 83 (2015): 46–64, https://faculty.washington.edu/bmarwick/PDFs/Clarkson_et_al._2015_MJB_1989.pdf. [Open access]","S. Anna Florin, Andrew S. Fairbairn, May Nango, et al., \"The first Australian plant foods at Madjedbebe, 65,000–53,000 years ago,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 924, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7026095/. [Open access]","Anna-Sapfo Malaspinas, Michael C. Westaway, Craig Muller, et al., \"A genomic history of Aboriginal Australia,\" <i>Nature</i> 538, no. 7624 (2016): 207–214, https://www.eva.mpg.de/documents/Nature/Malaspinas_Genomic_Nature_2016_2351078.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Madjedbebe","at":[132.8833,-12.5]}},
{"id":"wh-094","num":"94","category":"World · History","question":"Burnt and crushed in one case, laid under red ochre in the other, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are two graves about 40,000 years old from a dry lake bed in New South Wales.","answer":"Lake Mungo remains","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Burials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mungo Lady 1968, Mungo Man 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1981</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lake Mungo remains</b> are two burials that weathered out of a sand dune beside a lake bed in south-western New South Wales, dry for more than 10,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The geologist Jim Bowler found the first in 1968: a woman whose body had been burnt, her bones then crushed and buried in a shallow pit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Six years later, a few hundred metres away, he came upon a man laid out full length on his back with his hands folded and red ochre scattered over him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> They are known as Mungo Lady and Mungo Man, and they lie in the country of the Mutthi Mutthi, Barkindji and Ngiyampaa peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Willandra Lakes around them, a chain of basins that held water through the last ice age and dried as it ended, became a World Heritage site in 1981. <br><br> Their age has been revised more than once, first put near 30,000 years, then claimed at 62,000 in 1999, and settled in 2003 at about 40,000 by a team that included Bowler himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That still leaves the ochre burial the oldest ritual burial known anywhere and the burnt woman the earliest burnt human remains on record, though the burning is incomplete and no pyre survives, so specialists now hedge the word 'cremation'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 2001 a study of mitochondrial DNA taken from Mungo Man reported a lineage carried by no living person, which its authors read as evidence that one modern human lineage in Australia had been replaced by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Re-sequencing in 2016 showed those readings to be contamination, five modern European sequences in the bone and a sixth in the original extracts matching the first author of the 2001 study, and the claim collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both were eventually returned to their Traditional Owners, Mungo Man in 2017, and after years of disagreement over whether to keep them accessible for study, the reburial of the Willandra ancestors was approved in 2022 and has since gone ahead.","citation":"","answerText":"Lake Mungo remains","questions":["Found weathering out of a dune in 1968, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> include the earliest burnt human remains on record, a woman whose bones were crushed and buried in a shallow pit.","A 2001 study of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> claimed a DNA lineage no living person carries, until re-sequencing in 2016 exposed the readings as modern contamination and the claim collapsed."],"tags":["fossil","archaeology","prehistory","australia"],"sources":["James M. Bowler, Harvey Johnston, Jon M. Olley, et al., \"New ages for human occupation and climatic change at Lake Mungo, Australia,\" <i>Nature</i> 421, no. 6925 (2003): 837–840, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01383. [Paywalled]","Alan Thorne, Rainer Grün, Graham Mortimer, et al., \"Australia's oldest human remains: age of the Lake Mungo 3 skeleton,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 36, no. 6 (1999): 591–612, https://doi.org/10.1006/jhev.1999.0305. [Paywalled]","Gregory J. Adcock, Elizabeth S. Dennis, Simon Easteal, et al., \"Mitochondrial DNA sequences in ancient Australians: implications for modern human origins,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 98, no. 2 (2001): 537–542, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC14622/. [Open access]","Tim H. Heupink, Sankar Subramanian, Joanne L. Wright, et al., \"Ancient mtDNA sequences from the First Australians revisited,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 25 (2016): 6892–6897, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4922152/. [Open access]","Jessica I. Cerezo-Román, Elizabeth Sawchuk, Flora Schilt, et al., \"Earliest evidence for intentional cremation of human remains in Africa,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 1 (2026): eadz9554, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12757068/. [Open access]","Joanne L. Wright, Sally Wasef, Tim H. Heupink, et al., \"Ancient nuclear genomes enable repatriation of Indigenous human remains,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaau5064, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6300400/. [Open access]","Kathryn E. Fitzsimmons, Nicola Stern, Colin V. Murray-Wallace, et al., \"The Mungo mega-lake event, semi-arid Australia: non-linear descent into the last ice age, implications for human behaviour,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 6 (2015): e0127008, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0127008. [Open access]","Richard Gillespie, \"Dating the First Australians,\" <i>Radiocarbon</i> 44, no. 2 (2002): 455–472, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0033822200031830. [Open access]","Adam Brumm and Mark W. Moore, \"Symbolic revolutions and the Australian archaeological record,\" <i>Cambridge Archaeological Journal</i> 15, no. 2 (2005): 157–175, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0959774305000089. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Uomo_di_Mungo.jpg","title":"Lake Mungo remains","desc":"Uomo di Mungo. Benutzer:Schomynv de:Benutzer:Schomynv. 2011-05-19. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uomo_di_Mungo.jpg","alt":"Uomo di Mungo"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-096","num":"96","category":"World · History","question":"A four-year-old boy buried by the Belaya made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> matter far beyond Siberia, because his genome ties these mammoth hunters to the first Americans.","answer":"Mal'ta-Buret' culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mal'ta burial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 24,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1928, by Mikhail Gerasimov</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mal'ta-Buret' culture</b> is named for two Upper Paleolithic camps on the rivers west of Lake Baikal, in south-central Siberia, far beyond the eastern edge of Gravettian Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mikhail Gerasimov began digging at Mal'ta in 1928 and came back to it, on and off, for thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The camp's floors held the remains of dwellings, and it is inside them that most of the carved ivory was found, some of it in what look like ritual corners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They carved about thirty small female figures from mammoth ivory, and these are unlike the Venus figurines further west: slender rather than heavy, and marked in a way that looks like fitted fur clothing with a hood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They also carved birds with their wings out as though in flight, and flat bone plaques cut with zigzag patterns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What has made the culture famous, though, is neither a house nor a carving but a child buried at Mal'ta. Gerasimov found a grave holding two children, one known only from a few teeth and the other a boy of about four, laid in the ground with a necklace of 120 beads, a bird pendant and an ivory plaque and dusted with ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His genome was published in 2013, and at about 24,000 years old it was then the oldest genome of a modern human that anyone had read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It held two surprises: he was related to the ancestors of western Eurasians, and not at all closely to East Asians, although he had lived in Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also shares a great deal with living Native Americans, who draw a large part of their ancestry from people like him, and that solved an old puzzle about why their genes had never looked purely East Asian, as the geography alone would lead you to expect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mal'ta-Buret' culture","questions":["Unlike the heavy Venus figures further west, the ivory women of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are slender and marked as if wearing fitted fur clothing with a hood.","Mikhail Gerasimov, who first dug the camps of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1928, found most of its carved ivory lying inside the dwellings themselves."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","russia"],"sources":["Maanasa Raghavan, Pontus Skoglund, Kelly E. Graf, et al., “Upper Palaeolithic Siberian Genome Reveals Dual Ancestry of Native Americans,” <i>Nature</i> 505, no. 7481 (2014): 87–91, https://escholarship.org/uc/item/79h679zc. [Open access]","Liudmila Lbova, “The Siberian Paleolithic Site of Mal’ta: A Unique Source for the Study of Childhood Archaeology,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 3 (2021): e9, under “The Mal’ta burial,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427291/. [Open access]","Gerhard W. Weber, Alexander Lukeneder, Mathias Harzhauser, et al., “The Microstructure and the Origin of the Venus from Willendorf,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 2926, under “Introduction,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8885675/. [Open access]","Junzo Uchiyama, J. Christopher Gillam, Alexander Savelyev, and Chao Ning, “Populations Dynamics in Northern Eurasian Forests: A Long-Term Perspective from Northeast Asia,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e16, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427466/. [Open access]","J. Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Ben A. Potter, Lasse Vinner, et al., “Terminal Pleistocene Alaskan Genome Reveals First Founding Population of Native Americans,” <i>Nature</i> 553, no. 7687 (2018): 203–7, https://researchonline.ljmu.ac.uk/id/eprint/7887/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Malta_artefacts_%28reworked%29.jpg","title":"Mal'ta-Buret' culture","desc":"Malta artefacts (reworked). Лапоть. 2023-02-25. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malta_artefacts_(reworked).jpg","alt":"Malta artefacts (reworked)"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Mal'ta-Buret' culture","at":[103.5174,52.85],"kind":"region","area":[[102.75,52.6],[102.95,53.1],[103.6,53.3],[104.25,53.1],[104.35,52.7],[103.85,52.35],[103.1,52.4]]}},
{"id":"wh-097","num":"97","category":"World · History","question":"Whenever the sea fell far enough to bare the floor of the Bering Strait, Siberia and Alaska became one land called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, grazed by mammoth.","answer":"Beringia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Last land bridge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 35,700 – 11,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1937, by Eric Hultén</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Beringia</b> is the name for the land that joined Siberia to Alaska whenever the sea fell far enough to lay bare the shallow floor of the Bering Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Swedish botanist Eric Hultén coined the word in 1937, after Vitus Bering, the navigator in Russian service who sailed through the strait in 1728.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was no narrow causeway but a plain hundreds of kilometres across, so that anyone crossing had no sense of leaving one continent for another. Too little snow fell there for glaciers to form, so while ice sheets buried Canada and Scandinavia this corner of the Arctic stayed open grassland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That cold steppe carried mammoth, horse, bison and saiga antelope, and it is known today as the mammoth steppe, after its largest grazer, a dry grassland running west into Siberia and east into the Yukon, though the low bridge itself may have been wetter tundra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Seafloor sediments analysed in 2022 suggest the bridge appeared surprisingly late, around 35,700 years ago, while separate work on the flooding puts its final drowning nearer 11,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people who became Native Americans passed through that window, and their genetics carry a set of mutations that sets them apart from their nearest living relatives in Asia and turns up in indigenous populations throughout the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The standstill hypothesis explains those mutations by a long pause: a population cut off in Beringia for thousands of years, unable to go further east while ice sheets blocked the way, and spreading south only after about 16,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Not everyone accepts the word, since the founding population seems not to have sheltered in a refuge but to have spread across an immense arctic plain, which is a poor fit for a standstill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How they travelled south is likewise unsettled, since the corridor between the two Canadian ice sheets seems to have opened too late to have been the first route, which has turned attention to boats and the Pacific coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Beringia","questions":["The Swedish botanist Eric Hultén coined <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1937, naming it for Vitus Bering, the navigator in Russian service who sailed through the strait in 1728.","The standstill hypothesis holds that the ancestors of Native Americans were penned for thousands of years in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, spreading south only after about 16,000 years ago once the ice retreated."],"tags":["place","geography","prehistory","migration"],"sources":["Jesse R. Farmer, Tamara Pico, Ona M. Underwood et al., \"The Bering Strait was flooded 10,000 years before the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 1 (2023): e2206742119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2206742119. [Open access]","Martin Jakobsson, Christof Pearce, Thomas M. Cronin, et al., \"Post-glacial flooding of the Bering Land Bridge dated to 11 cal ka BP based on new geophysical and sediment records,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 13 (2017): 991–1005, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-13-991-2017. [Open access]","John F. Hoffecker, Scott A. Elias, G. Richard Scott, and Dennis H. O'Rourke, \"Beringia and the peopling of the Western Hemisphere,\" <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 290, no. 1990 (2023): 20222246, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9832545/. [Open access]","Erika Tamm, Toomas Kivisild, Maere Reidla, et al., \"Beringian standstill and spread of Native American founders,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 2, no. 9 (2007): e829, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0000829. [Open access]","Bastien Llamas, Lars Fehren-Schmitz, Guido Valverde, et al., \"Ancient mitochondrial DNA provides high-resolution time scale of the peopling of the Americas,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 2, no. 4 (2016): e1501385, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4820370/. [Open access]","Kari B. Schroeder, Theodore G. Schurr, Jeffrey C. Long, et al., \"A private allele ubiquitous in the Americas,\" <i>Biology Letters</i> 3, no. 2 (2007): 218–223, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2375964/. [Open access]","C. R. Harington and Jacques Cinq-Mars, \"Radiocarbon dates on saiga antelope (<i>Saiga tatarica</i>) fossils from Yukon and the Northwest Territories,\" <i>Arctic</i> 48, no. 1 (1995): 1–7, https://doi.org/10.14430/arctic1218. [Open access]","Mikkel W. Pedersen, Anthony Ruter, Charles Schweger, et al., \"Postglacial viability and colonization in North America's ice-free corridor,\" <i>Nature</i> 537, no. 7618 (2016): 45–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature19085. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Global_sea_levels_during_the_last_Ice_Age.jpg/1920px-Global_sea_levels_during_the_last_Ice_Age.jpg","title":"Beringia","desc":"Global sea levels during the last Ice Age. National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration. 2008-03-28. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Global_sea_levels_during_the_last_Ice_Age.jpg","alt":"Global sea levels during the last Ice Age"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-098","num":"98","category":"World · History","question":"Because ice sealed the inland corridor, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> most likely ran down the Pacific shore, small groups out of Siberia spreading into two empty continents.","answer":"Settlement of the Americas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founding split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 36,000 – 25,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">South of the ice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 16,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>settlement of the Americas</b> was the last great continental expansion of our species, carrying people out of north-eastern Asia into two continents on which no human being had ever set foot. Their ancestors began separating from their East Asian relatives somewhere between roughly 36,000 and 25,000 years ago, a division still written into the DNA of indigenous American populations today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The road ran through Beringia, the cold grassland plain that joined Siberia to Alaska whenever glacial seas fell far enough to lay bare the floor of the Bering Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genetics suggests the travellers were held up for thousands of years in the north, unable to press on while the ice sheets of the Last Glacial Maximum sealed the way south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That long pause is called the standstill hypothesis, and though not everyone accepts it, by about 16,000 years ago people were certainly living far beyond the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> For most of the 20th century archaeologists held that the first arrivals were the Clovis hunters, named for their fluted stone points and dated tightly to between about 13,050 and 12,750 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That model collapsed as older sites accumulated: a camp at Cooper's Ferry in Idaho at roughly 16,000 years, preserved human faeces in the Paisley Caves of Oregon at a little over 12,000 radiocarbon years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Fossil footprints at White Sands push the story much further back, to between 23,000 and 21,000 years ago, a range upheld by a second dating programme in 2023 and still being tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The corridor between the two Canadian ice sheets cannot have been the first road south, since the plants and animals a traveller needed returned to it only around 12,600 years ago, which points instead to boats and the Pacific coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Individual dates at the earliest end remain fiercely contested, but the Asian origin is not: in 2014 the genome of a Clovis-era boy buried in Montana showed his people ancestral to many indigenous Americans living today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Settlement of the Americas","questions":["Long credited to the Clovis hunters, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been pushed back by fossil footprints at White Sands dated to between 23,000 and 21,000 years ago.","A Clovis-era boy buried in Montana yielded a 2014 genome tying the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Asia, his people ancestral to indigenous Americans living today."],"tags":["event","archaeology","prehistory","migration","north america"],"sources":["J. Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Ben A. Potter, Lasse Vinner, et al., \"Terminal Pleistocene Alaskan genome reveals first founding population of Native Americans,\" <i>Nature</i> 553, no. 7687 (2018): 203–207, https://researchonline.ljmu.ac.uk/id/eprint/7887/. [Open access]","John F. Hoffecker, Scott A. Elias, G. Richard Scott, and Dennis H. O'Rourke, \"Beringia and the peopling of the Western Hemisphere,\" <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 290, no. 1990 (2023): 20222246, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9832545/. [Open access]","Erika Tamm, Toomas Kivisild, Maere Reidla, et al., \"Beringian standstill and spread of Native American founders,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 2, no. 9 (2007): e829, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1952074/. [Open access]","Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr., and David L. Carlson, \"The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","Loren G. Davis, David B. Madsen, David A. Sisson, et al., \"Dating of a large tool assemblage at the Cooper's Ferry site (Idaho, USA) to ~15,785 cal yr B.P. extends the age of stemmed points in the Americas,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 51 (2022): eade1248, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9788777/. [Open access]","Lisa-Marie Shillito, Helen L. Whelton, John C. Blong, et al., \"Pre-Clovis occupation of the Americas identified by human fecal biomarkers in coprolites from Paisley Caves, Oregon,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 29 (2020): eaba6404, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7363456/. [Open access]","Matthew R. Bennett, David Bustos, Jeffrey S. Pigati, et al., \"Evidence of humans in North America during the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Science</i> 373, no. 6562 (2021): 1528–1531, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abg7586. [Paywalled]","Vance T. Holliday, Jason D. Windingstad, Jordon Bright, et al., \"Paleolake geochronology supports Last Glacial Maximum (LGM) age for human tracks at White Sands, New Mexico,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 25 (2025): eadv4951, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12175891/. [Open access]","Alia J. Lesnek, Jason P. Briner, Charlotte Lindqvist, et al., \"Deglaciation of the Pacific coastal corridor directly preceded the human colonization of the Americas,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 5 (2018): eaar5040, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5976267/. [Open access]","Morten Rasmussen, Sarah L. Anzick, Michael R. Waters, et al., \"The genome of a Late Pleistocene human from a Clovis burial site in western Montana,\" <i>Nature</i> 506, no. 7487 (2014): 225–229, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4878442/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Footprints_of_Human_Adolescent_and_Teen_%28b47488a1-0f52-4d94-9da3-b7b8fea91d0e%29.JPG/1920px-Footprints_of_Human_Adolescent_and_Teen_%28b47488a1-0f52-4d94-9da3-b7b8fea91d0e%29.JPG","title":"Settlement of the Americas","desc":"12 human footprints belonging to one adolecent and possibly female teenager. The size of the set of footprints indicates that it was left by a human child. The second set of tracks were left by a possibly female teenager. Keywords: Ice Age; Footprint; Size; Adolescent. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Footprints_of_Human_Adolescent_and_Teen_(b47488a1-0f52-4d94-9da3-b7b8fea91d0e).JPG","alt":"Footprints of Human Adolescent and Teen (b47488a1-0f52-4d94-9da3-b7b8fea91d0e)"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-099","num":"99","category":"World · History","question":"A waterlogged bog in southern Chile preserved wood, cordage and seaweed, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the site that broke the belief that Clovis hunters were the first Americans.","answer":"Monte Verde","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Main layer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975, dug from 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Endorsed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Monte Verde</b> is an archaeological site on the bank of Chinchihuapi Creek in southern Chile, a little inland from Puerto Montt and more than 10,000 kilometres (6,200 miles) from the Bering Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Local people came upon bones there in the mid-1970s, and from 1977 Tom Dillehay and Mario Pino uncovered a settlement buried and sealed under waterlogged peat. That peat is why the site matters: it shut out the oxygen, so wood, cordage, knotted rushes and plant remains survived instead of rotting away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavators found the wooden frame of a long hide-covered shelter, hearths, digging sticks, chewed medicinal leaves, wild potato and seaweed carried up from the distant coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> A human footprint was pressed into the clay beside it, and there were gomphothere bones, but the stone tools are mostly expedient pebble flakes and bola stones, with no fluted points at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The dating put the main layer at about 14,500 years ago, roughly 1,500 years before Clovis and at the far southern end of two continents people were supposed to have entered from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because that could not be squared with Clovis hunters arriving first, the site was resisted for twenty years and its stratigraphy repeatedly questioned. In 1997 a panel of well-known archaeologists visited the excavation and accepted in print that the main layer was about 12,500 radiocarbon years old, and the Clovis First model did not recover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In March 2026 a paper in <i>Science</i> argued the opposite, that a layer of volcanic ash beneath the finds is only 11,000 years old and the deposit is thousands of years younger than claimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dillehay and others answered in May with three critiques, objecting that the new samples came from cutbank exposures away from the site rather than from the excavation itself, so the matter is now open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Monte Verde","questions":["Buried under peat on Chinchihuapi Creek, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> preserved a hide-covered shelter frame, chewed medicinal leaves and a child's footprint pressed into the clay beside it.","A 2026 paper in <i>Science</i> argued that ash beneath the finds is only 11,000 years old, making <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> thousands of years younger than its excavators had long claimed."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","chile"],"sources":["Mario Pino and Tom D. Dillehay, \"Monte Verde II: an assessment of new radiocarbon dates and their sedimentological context,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 393 (2023): 524–540, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.32. [Open access]","Tom D. Dillehay, Carlos Ocampo, José Saavedra, et al., \"New archaeological evidence for an early human presence at Monte Verde, Chile,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 11 (2015): e0141923, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0141923. [Open access]","Todd A. Surovell, César Méndez, Juan-Luis García, et al., \"A mid-Holocene age for Monte Verde challenges the timeline of human colonization of South America,\" <i>Science</i> 391, no. 6791 (2026): 1283–1288, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adw9217. [Paywalled]","Tom D. Dillehay, Mario Pino, Luis Lara, et al., \"Geomorphological and archaeological evidence at Monte Verde II, Chile supports the claim of human occupation 14,500 years ago,\" eLetter, <i>Science</i>, May 4, 2026, https://liberalarts.tamu.edu/csfa/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2026/05/Three-Critiques.pdf. [Open access]","David J. Meltzer, J. Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Thomaz Pinotti, et al., \"Genetic evidence and the peopling of the Americas: reply to Surovell et al. 2026,\" eLetter, <i>Science</i>, May 4, 2026, https://liberalarts.tamu.edu/csfa/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2026/05/Three-Critiques.pdf. [Open access]","Michael R. Waters, Jessi J. Halligan, Rolfe D. Mandel, et al., \"Geoarchaeological assessment of the suggested middle Holocene age for Monte Verde II, Chile,\" eLetter, <i>Science</i>, May 5, 2026, https://fundacionmonteverde.cl/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Waters-et-al.-Rebuttal-to-Surovell-et-al.-2026.pdf. [Open access]","David J. Meltzer, Donald K. Grayson, Gerardo Ardila, et al., \"On the Pleistocene antiquity of Monte Verde, southern Chile,\" <i>American Antiquity</i> 62, no. 4 (1997): 659–663, https://doi.org/10.2307/281884. [Paywalled]","Tom D. Dillehay, C. Ramírez, M. Pino, et al., \"Monte Verde: seaweed, food, medicine, and the peopling of South America,\" <i>Science</i> 320, no. 5877 (2008): 784–786, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1156533. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Monte_Verde_2012.jpg/1920px-Monte_Verde_2012.jpg","title":"Monte Verde","desc":"Vista del sitio de Monte Verde. Geología Valdivia, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Verde_2012.jpg","alt":"Monte Verde 2012"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Monte Verde","at":[-73.2044,-41.5047]}},
{"id":"wh-100","num":"100","category":"World · History","question":"Chasing mammoth and giant bison across a thawing continent, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left fluted stone points from Alaska to Patagonia before the Archaic way of life began.","answer":"Paleo-Indians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">until 8000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Clovis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,050 – 12,750 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Paleo-Indians</b> were the first peoples of the Americas, the hunters and foragers who spread through two empty continents at the close of the last Ice Age. The archaeologist Frank H. H. Roberts Jr. used the name in a 1940 survey without ever defining it, and it has since come to cover everyone in the Americas before about 8000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After that date the climate settles, the great animals are gone, and archaeologists speak instead of the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Archaic_period_(North_America)\">Archaic period</span>. Their world was colder, drier and stranger than ours, grazed by mammoth, mastodon, horse, camel and a bison half again the size of the living kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They moved in small bands and carried little, yet worked fine stone quarried hundreds of kilometres away, which is among the clearest signs of how far and how often they travelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their signature is the fluted point, a spear tip with a channel flake struck from the base up each face so that it could be seated in a socket of bone or ivory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The best known is the Clovis point, named after the town of Clovis in eastern New Mexico and dated to about 13,050 to 12,750 years ago; the Folsom point that follows it on the Plains is smaller and made for bison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Folsom point found in 1927 in place among the ribs of an extinct bison settled a long argument, proving beyond doubt that people had been in the Americas while Ice Age animals still lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How much of their food those animals supplied is still disputed: some sites yield plants, small game and fish, while isotopes published in 2024 from a Clovis child buried in Montana suggest mammoth made up much of his mother's diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within a few thousand years the mammoth and the giant bison were gone, whether from hunting or from the violent climate swings at the end of the Pleistocene, and their descendants became the Archaic peoples of the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Paleo-Indians","questions":["The name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was used in a 1940 survey without ever being defined, and it now covers everyone in the Americas before about 8000 BCE.","A fluted Folsom point found in 1927 lodged in the ribs of an extinct bison proved the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had hunted while Ice Age animals still lived."],"tags":["people","archaeology","prehistory","north america"],"sources":["Frank H. H. Roberts Jr., \"Developments in the problem of the North American Paleo-Indian,\" in <i>Essays in Historical Anthropology of North America</i>, Smithsonian Miscellaneous Collections 100 (Washington, DC: Smithsonian Institution, 1940), 51–116, https://archive.org/details/smithsonianmisce1001940smit. [Open access]","James C. Chatters, Ben A. Potter, Stuart J. Fiedel, et al., \"Mammoth featured heavily in western Clovis diet,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 49 (2024): eadr3814, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adr3814. [Open access]","Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr., and David L. Carlson, \"The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","Paul L. Koch and Anthony D. Barnosky, \"Late Quaternary extinctions: state of the debate,\" <i>Annual Review of Ecology, Evolution, and Systematics</i> 37 (2006): 215–250, https://ib.berkeley.edu/labs/barnosky/Koch%20%20and%20Barnosky%202006.pdf. [Open access]","Linda S. Cordell, \"The Folsom site in retrospect,\" in <i>Vermejo Park</i>, New Mexico Geological Society 27th Annual Fall Field Conference Guidebook (Socorro: NMGS, 1976), 83–86, https://doi.org/10.56577/FFC-27.83. [Open access]","Jeff M. Martin, Jim I. Mead and Perry S. Barboza, \"Bison body size and climate change,\" <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 8, no. 9 (2018): 4564–4574, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.4019. [Open access]","Alan M. Slade, \"To haft and to hold: evidence for the hafting of Clovis fluted points,\" <i>Journal of Lithic Studies</i> 8, no. 3 (2021): 133–151, https://doi.org/10.2218/jls.3033. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Eden_points_and_Dalton_projectile_points_-_Paleo-Indian_collection_-_Robbins_Museum_-_Middleborough%2C_Massachusetts_-_DSC03772.jpg/1920px-Eden_points_and_Dalton_projectile_points_-_Paleo-Indian_collection_-_Robbins_Museum_-_Middleborough%2C_Massachusetts_-_DSC03772.jpg","title":"Paleo-Indians","desc":"Archaeological exhibit in the Robbins Museum, 17 Jackson Street, Middleborough, Massachusetts, USA. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eden_points_and_Dalton_projectile_points_-_Paleo-Indian_collection_-_Robbins_Museum_-_Middleborough,_Massachusetts_-_DSC03772.jpg","alt":"Eden points and Dalton projectile points - Paleo-Indian collection - Robbins Museum - Middleborough, Massachusetts"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-101","num":"101","category":"World · History","question":"Every one of its points carries a channel flake struck from the base, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread that single design across a continent in barely 300 years.","answer":"Clovis culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,050 – 12,750 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Type site found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1930s</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Clovis</b> is a North American toolmaking tradition of the very end of the Ice Age, named after Blackwater Draw, a dry lake bed near the town of Clovis in New Mexico. Its signature is a lance-shaped stone point with a flute, a channel flake driven off the base up each face so that the point could be seated in a bone or ivory socket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That last removal was the risky one, since a flute struck badly wrecks a point already close to finished, and broken examples litter the workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The knappers also struck overshot flakes that ran right across the piece to carry off part of the far edge, though whether they meant to, and whether it sets Clovis apart at all, is now disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their points turn up right across North America, from coast to coast and south into Mexico and Central America. <br><br> The startling thing is the timing: careful redating puts the whole tradition between 13,050 and 12,750 years ago, and it is not the work of the first Americans, who were already there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Clovis people also buried caches of tools, at East Wenatchee, Fenn, Simon and elsewhere, some of the pieces far larger and finer than working gear needs to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Anzick site in Montana holds the only Clovis burial known, an infant boy covered in red ochre with more than 100 stone and bone objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At about a dozen sites their points lie among mammoth or mastodon bones, though whether every one is a kill rather than a scavenged carcass is now questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tradition ends as the Younger Dryas cold sets in, giving way on the Plains to the smaller Folsom point and elsewhere to a scatter of regional styles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Clovis culture","questions":["Named after Blackwater Draw in New Mexico, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left more than 10,000 fluted points at some 1,500 places from coast to coast.","The only burial known from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the Anzick infant in Montana, covered in red ochre with more than 100 stone and bone objects."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","north america"],"sources":["Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr., and David L. Carlson, \"The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","Morten Rasmussen, Sarah L. Anzick, Michael R. Waters, et al., \"The genome of a Late Pleistocene human from a Clovis burial site in western Montana,\" <i>Nature</i> 506, no. 7487 (2014): 225–229, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4878442/. [Open access]","Donald K. Grayson, David J. Meltzer, and Ryan P. Breslawski, \"Overkill and the North American archaeological record—not guilty by association? A comment on Wolfe and Broughton (2020),\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 128 (2021): 105312, https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/people.smu.edu/dist/5/554/files/2021/08/Grayson-Meltzer-and-Breslawski-2021-JAS-Overkill-and-the-North-American-archaeological-record%E2%80%94not-guilty-by-association.pdf. [Open access]","Brett A. Story, Metin I. Eren, Kaitlyn Thomas, Briggs Buchanan, and David J. Meltzer, \"Why are Clovis fluted points more resilient than non-fluted lanceolate points? A quantitative assessment of breakage patterns between experimental models,\" <i>Archaeometry</i> 61, no. 1 (2019): 1–13, https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/people.smu.edu/dist/5/554/files/2019/01/Story-et-al.-2019-ARCHAEOMETRY-Why-are-Clovis-fluted-points-more-resilient-than-non-fluted-lanceolate-points-2205ct4.pdf. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren, David J. Meltzer, and Brian N. Andrews, \"Is Clovis technology unique to Clovis?,\" <i>PaleoAmerica</i> 4, no. 3 (2018): 202–218, https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/people.smu.edu/dist/5/554/files/2018/12/Eren-Meltzer-Andrews-2018-PALEOAMERICA-Is-Clovis-Technology-Unique-to-Clovis-1zq67w7.pdf. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren, Robert J. Patten, Michael J. O'Brien, and David J. Meltzer, \"Refuting the technological cornerstone of the Ice-Age Atlantic crossing hypothesis,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 40, no. 7 (2013): 2934–2941, https://cladistics.coas.missouri.edu/assets/pdf_articles/Eren_et_al_2013.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Clovis_spearpoints_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg/1920px-Clovis_spearpoints_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg","title":"Clovis culture","desc":"Clovis spearpoints on display at the Cleveland Museum of Natural History in Cleveland, Ohio. Named for Clovis, New Mexico, where such uniquely designed spearpoints were first found by archeologists, these spearpoints date from 13,500 to 13,000 years ago. Tim Evanson, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clovis_spearpoints_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg","alt":"Clovis spearpoints - Cleveland Museum of Natural History"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-102","num":"102","category":"World · History","question":"Knapped from stone carried hundreds of kilometres, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was resharpened and rebased until it wore out, and how it was actually used is still argued.","answer":"Clovis point","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,050 – 12,750 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Clovis point</b> is a leaf-shaped stone spear tip, worked on both faces and thinned to a shallow lens, with its edges ground blunt near the base where it met the shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They range from a few centimetres to more than twenty, and the largest known, from the East Wenatchee cache in Washington state, is an agate point 24.5 centimetres (9.6 inches) long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The stone matters: knappers chose fine cherts, jaspers and obsidians that flake predictably, and carried them extraordinary distances, in some cases more than 900 kilometres (560 miles) from the quarry. The blunted base was set into a foreshaft, probably a socket of bone or ivory though the method is still argued, and the flutes thinned it so the point could be bound in without a bulky joint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A worn point was not thrown away but resharpened at the tip and sometimes rebased and refluted, so an old one can be much shorter than it began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> How it was used is the open question, and three answers have long been on the table: a dart thrown with a spear-thrower, a hand-thrown javelin, and a spear driven in close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There is still no consensus on which of them the design suits best, and the low frequency of impact fractures on Clovis points has been read as evidence both for and against hunting big game.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A 2024 study added a fourth possibility, a braced pike planted in the ground so that a charging animal drove itself onto the point and its own weight did the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Impact damage and the way the points break give some support to each idea, which is part of why an argument about a simple stone tip has run for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because collectors pay heavily for them, a flourishing market in fakes and replicas surrounds them, and a point with no recorded findspot is worth very little as evidence.","citation":"","answerText":"Clovis point","questions":["Worked on both faces to a shallow lens, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> usually ran 4 to 20 centimetres (1.6 to 7.9 inches), though a white agate giant from the East Wenatchee cache reached 23.","The base was ground blunt to sit in a split foreshaft, and the flutes thinned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so it could be bound in without a bulky joint."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","north america"],"sources":["R. Scott Byram, Kent G. Lightfoot, and Jun Ueno Sunseri, \"Clovis points and foreshafts under braced weapon compression: modeling Pleistocene megafauna encounters with a lithic pike,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 8 (2024): e0307996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0307996. [Open access]","David K. Thulman, Michael J. Shott, Alan M. Slade, and Justin P. Williams, \"Clovis point allometry, modularity, and integration: exploring shape variation due to tool use with landmark-based geometric morphometrics,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 8 (2023): e0289489, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0289489. [Open access]","Alan M. Slade, \"To haft and to hold: evidence for the hafting of Clovis fluted points,\" <i>Journal of Lithic Studies</i> 8, no. 3 (2021): 133–151, https://doi.org/10.2218/jls.3033. [Open access]","Richard B. Waitt, \"Megafloods and Clovis cache at Wenatchee, Washington,\" <i>Quaternary Research</i> 85 (2016): 430–444, https://fop.cascadiageo.org/pacific_northwest_cell/2018/Background_Literature/Waitt2016WenatcheeMegafloods.pdf. [Open access]","Briggs Buchanan et al., \"A Morphometric Assessment of the Intended Function of Cached Clovis Points,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 7, no. 2 (2012): e30530, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0030530. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Clovis_point%2C_11500-9000_BC%2C_Sevier_County%2C_Utah%2C_chert_-_Natural_History_Museum_of_Utah_-_DSC07376.JPG/1920px-Clovis_point%2C_11500-9000_BC%2C_Sevier_County%2C_Utah%2C_chert_-_Natural_History_Museum_of_Utah_-_DSC07376.JPG","title":"Clovis point","desc":"Exhibit in the Natural History Museum of Utah, Salt Lake City, Utah, USA. Photography was permitted in the museum without restriction. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clovis_point,_11500-9000_BC,_Sevier_County,_Utah,_chert_-_Natural_History_Museum_of_Utah_-_DSC07376.JPG","alt":"Clovis point, 11500-9000 BCE, Sevier County, Utah, chert - Natural History Museum of Utah"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-103","num":"103","category":"World · History","question":"Fluting almost the whole face of each point and wrecking up to three in four in the attempt, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent extraordinary skill on tips for hunting bison on the Plains.","answer":"Folsom tradition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,900 – 12,300 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Type find</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927, Wild Horse Arroyo</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Folsom tradition</b> is a hunting culture of the Great Plains and the American Southwest, named after the village of Folsom in New Mexico, where the bones came out of Wild Horse Arroyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> George McJunkin, a Black cowboy born into slavery, noticed bones washing out of the arroyo wall in 1908 and spent years trying to get anyone to come and look. He died in 1922, four years before the Colorado Museum of Natural History finally dug the place under Jesse Figgins, so he never learned what he had found. On 29 August 1927 a point turned up wedged between the ribs of an extinct bison, and Figgins stopped the work and had scientists travel out to see it lying there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That was the moment the long argument ended, since a worked spear tip inside an animal dead for thousands of years cannot be explained away as a coincidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The point itself is small, seldom more than 8 centimetres (3 inches), and fluted almost from base to tip on both faces, which is far harder than the shallower Clovis flute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Estimates of how many were ruined in the fluting run from about 10 to 60 percent, so the design cost a great deal of good stone and skill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Why they took that risk is still argued, one reading being that the thinned faces let the wooden haft grip the point like a splint against snapping. These were specialists in <i>Bison antiquus</i>, and at Wild Horse Arroyo the remains of some 32 of them lay with 19 points, an animal trap rather than single kills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the Cooper site in Oklahoma a bison skull painted with a red zigzag in hematite sat in the middle of one such kill, the first Paleoindian art known from the Americas outside a burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Folsom tradition","questions":["George McJunkin, a Black cowboy born into slavery, saw the bones eroding from Wild Horse Arroyo in 1908, years before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took its name from the nearby village.","At the Cooper site the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a bison skull painted with a red zigzag in hematite, the oldest painted object known anywhere in North America."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","north america"],"sources":["Briggs Buchanan, J. David Kilby, Marcus J. Hamilton, et al., \"Bayesian revision of the Folsom age range using IntCal20,\" <i>PaleoAmerica</i> 7, no. 2 (2021): 133–144, http://marcusjhamilton.weebly.com/uploads/2/5/5/3/25533140/buchanan_et_al_2021.pdf. [Open access]","Lawrence J. Jackson, \"Carl Schwachheim and the Folsom site,\" <i>Current Research in the Pleistocene</i> 14 (1997): 42–44, https://liberalarts.tamu.edu/csfa/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2023/07/CRP-14-1997.pdf. [Open access]","Leland C. Bement, Marian Hyman, Michael E. Zolensky, and Brian J. Carter, \"A painted skull from the Cooper site: a Folsom bison kill in NW Oklahoma,\" <i>Current Research in the Pleistocene</i> 14 (1997): 6–8, https://liberalarts.tamu.edu/csfa/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2023/07/CRP-14-1997.pdf. [Open access]","J. D. Figgins, \"The antiquity of man in America,\" <i>Natural History</i> 27, no. 3 (1927): 229–239, https://archive.org/details/sim_natural-history_may-june-1927_27_3. [Open access]","Linda S. Cordell, \"The Folsom site in retrospect,\" in <i>Vermejo Park</i>, New Mexico Geological Society 27th Annual Fall Field Conference Guidebook (Socorro: NMGS, 1976), 83–86, https://doi.org/10.56577/FFC-27.83. [Open access]","Kaitlyn A. Thomas, Brett A. Story, Metin I. Eren, et al., \"Explaining the origin of fluting in North American Pleistocene weaponry,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 81 (2017): 23–30, https://cladistics.coas.missouri.edu/assets/pdf_articles/2017-clovis-fluting.pdf. [Open access]","David T. Kirkpatrick, \"The prehistory of northeastern New Mexico,\" in <i>Vermejo Park</i>, New Mexico Geological Society 27th Annual Fall Field Conference Guidebook (Socorro: NMGS, 1976), 77–82, https://doi.org/10.56577/FFC-27.77. [Open access]","David J. Meltzer, Lawrence C. Todd, and Vance T. Holliday, \"The Folsom (Paleoindian) type site: past investigations, current studies,\" <i>American Antiquity</i> 67, no. 1 (2002): 5–36, https://doi.org/10.2307/2694875. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Soapstone-LindenmeierSite.JPG","title":"Folsom tradition","desc":"The arroyo surrounding the Lindenmeier archaeological site (Folsom culture) on Soapstone Prairie Natural Area near Fort Collins, Colorado. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soapstone-LindenmeierSite.JPG","alt":"Soapstone-LindenmeierSite"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-104","num":"104","category":"World · History","question":"Named after an arctic flower whose pollen fills the layer, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> threw the northern world back into near-glacial cold for about 1,200 years, ending as abruptly as it began.","answer":"Younger Dryas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,900 – 11,700 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Younger Dryas</b> was a sharp return to near-glacial cold across the northern hemisphere, running from roughly 12,900 to 11,700 years ago and lasting about 1,200 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It takes its name from <i>Dryas octopetala</i>, an arctic flower whose pollen crowds the Scandinavian sediments laid down while it lasted. The world had been warming for nearly two thousand years when it struck, and the interruption undid a great deal of that gain inside a single human lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Central Greenland was perhaps 10 to 14 degrees colder than today, European forests gave way again to tundra and steppe, and dust in the ice cores rose several times over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The exit was faster still, with the cores recording the closing warm-up in 40 to 50 years, and that boundary is the formal start of the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The standard explanation is a flood of meltwater from the shrinking North American ice sheet, which freshened the North Atlantic and slowed the current that carries warmth north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where the water went out is still argued: the old answer was the St Lawrence, but a good deal of evidence now favours the Mackenzie and the Arctic Ocean instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also unclear how one flood could hold that circulation down for more than a thousand years, so the mechanism is not fully settled. A comet or asteroid airburst has been proposed as the trigger, but the markers claimed for it have largely failed independent replication and most specialists do not accept it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For people the timing mattered: the cold overlaps the end of Clovis toolmaking in North America and the last stretch before farming in the Levant, though whether it pushed anyone into agriculture is disputed.","citation":"","answerText":"Younger Dryas","questions":["Blamed on a flood of meltwater that freshened the North Atlantic and slowed the current carrying warmth north, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began about 12,900 years ago.","A warm-up of forty to fifty years in the ice cores closes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and marks the start of the Holocene, which overlaps the end of Clovis toolmaking."],"tags":["era","geology","science","climate"],"sources":["Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Definition and Dating of the GSSP (Global Stratotype Section and Point) for the Base of the Holocene Using the Greenland NGRIP Ice Core, and Selected Auxiliary Records,\" <i>Journal of Quaternary Science</i> 24, no. 1 (2009): 3–17, https://timescalefoundation.org/references/HoloceneGSSP_JQuatSci090.pdf. [Open access]","Christo Buizert et al., \"Greenland Temperature Response to Climate Forcing during the Last Deglaciation,\" <i>Science</i> 345, no. 6201 (2014): 1177–80, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1254961. [Paywalled]","Jerry F. McManus et al., \"Collapse and Rapid Resumption of Atlantic Meridional Circulation Linked to Deglacial Climate Changes,\" <i>Nature</i> 428, no. 6985 (2004): 834–37, https://www.whoi.edu/cms/files/mcmanus04nat_100524.pdf. [Open access]","Julian B. Murton et al., \"Identification of Younger Dryas Outburst Flood Path from Lake Agassiz to the Arctic Ocean,\" <i>Nature</i> 464, no. 7289 (2010): 740–43, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature08954. [Paywalled]","David J. Meltzer et al., \"Chronological Evidence Fails to Support Claim of an Isochronous Widespread Layer of Cosmic Impact Indicators Dated to 12,800 Years Ago,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 21 (2014): E2162–71, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4040610/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Younger_Dryas_and_Holocene_temperature_changes.png","title":"Younger Dryas","desc":"Air temperature changes, determined as proxy temperatures, taken from the central region of Greenland's ice sheet during the Late Pleistocene and the Holocene. Chart in natural, left to right, time axis, with modern temperature indicated for clarity. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Younger_Dryas_and_Holocene_temperature_changes.png","alt":"Younger Dryas and Holocene temperature changes"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-105","num":"105","category":"World · History","question":"Every farm, city, state and written word belongs to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the warm epoch that began when the Younger Dryas ended and that we are still living in.","answer":"Holocene","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Epoch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11,700 BP – present</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Holocene</b> is the epoch we are living in, the second and far shorter half of the Quaternary Period, and it began 11,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its name joins the Greek holos and kainos to mean 'entirely recent', and the French naturalist Paul Gervais introduced it in the 1860s, before the geological congresses took it up. The boundary is not a guess but a marked point: nearly 1,493 metres (4,898 feet) down in the NGRIP ice core from Greenland, at the sharpest signal of the warming that ended the Younger Dryas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because ice preserves annual layers, that base can be counted rather than estimated, and it is quoted as 11,700 years before 2000 with an error of only 99 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strictly it is an interglacial like others before it, a warm pause inside the ice age that still holds Greenland and Antarctica, and not the end of the cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What makes it exceptional is steadiness, since the Pleistocene lurched between glacial and warm phases while Holocene climate has stayed mild enough for people to build on it. Geologists split it in 2018 into three ages, each beginning at a climate shock: the Greenlandian, then the Northgrippian at the cold snap 8,200 years ago, then the Meghalayan at the drought of 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That last boundary is defined in a stalagmite from Krem Mawmluh cave in Meghalaya, north-eastern India, whose layers record an Asian monsoon that faltered at a time when several early civilisations came apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost everything usually called history sits inside this epoch: farming taken up independently in several regions, the first villages and cities, writing, states, and a population grown from a few million to eight billion. That weight of human activity is why the Anthropocene was proposed as a new epoch beginning in 1952, but stratigraphers voted the proposal down in March 2024, so the Holocene remains, formally, our own time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Holocene","questions":["Introduced by Paul Gervais in the 1860s, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has its base fixed nearly 1,493 metres (4,898 feet) down in a Greenland ice core at the sharpest warming signal.","Split in 2018 into the Greenlandian, Northgrippian and Meghalayan ages, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survived a 2024 vote that would have ended it at the proposed Anthropocene."],"tags":["era","geology","science","nature"],"sources":["Subcommission on Quaternary Stratigraphy, International Commission on Stratigraphy, \"Major Divisions,\" https://quaternary.stratigraphy.org/major-divisions. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,\" <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–223, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]","Subcommission on Quaternary Stratigraphy, International Commission on Stratigraphy, \"Working Group on the ‘Anthropocene’,\" https://quaternary.stratigraphy.org/working-groups/anthropocene. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Definition and Dating of the GSSP (Global Stratotype Section and Point) for the Base of the Holocene Using the Greenland NGRIP Ice Core, and Selected Auxiliary Records,\" <i>Journal of Quaternary Science</i> 24, no. 1 (2009): 3–17, https://timescalefoundation.org/references/HoloceneGSSP_JQuatSci090.pdf. [Open access]","Past Interglacials Working Group of PAGES, \"Interglacials of the Last 800,000 Years,\" <i>Reviews of Geophysics</i> 54, no. 1 (2016): 162–219, under \"Defining Interglacials,\" https://hal.science/hal-03245836. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Global_temperature_relative_to_peak_Holocene_temperature%2C_based_on_ocean_cores_%28NASA%29.png/1920px-Global_temperature_relative_to_peak_Holocene_temperature%2C_based_on_ocean_cores_%28NASA%29.png","title":"Holocene","desc":"These two graphs show global temperatures for two different time periods (Hansen and Sato, 2011). The top graph shows global surface temperature for the past 5.3 million years as inferred from cores of ocean sediments taken all around the global ocean. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Global_temperature_relative_to_peak_Holocene_temperature,_based_on_ocean_cores_(NASA).png","alt":"Global temperature relative to peak Holocene temperature, based on ocean cores (NASA)"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-106","num":"106","category":"World · History","question":"Squeezed between the Old and New Stone Ages, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covers the hunter-gatherers of the warming world after the Ice Age, armed with tiny blades set in wooden shafts.","answer":"Mesolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9700 – 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in Europe</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Coined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1866, by Hodder Westropp</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mesolithic</b>, or Middle Stone Age, is the phase of the Stone Age belonging to the hunter-gatherers of the early Holocene, who lived after the last Ice Age but before farming reached them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its name comes from the Greek for 'middle' and 'stone', and Hodder Westropp proposed it in 1866, a year after John Lubbock named the Paleolithic and the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Westropp's own three terms that year ran Palaeolithic, Mesolithic and Kainolithic; by 1872 he had swapped the last for Lubbock's Neolithic, and Grahame Clark's 1932 division of the age is still the backbone of the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Europe the age opens around 9700 BCE and closes wherever farming arrived, early in the southeast and only about 4000 BCE in Britain and southern Scandinavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is defined by a way of life rather than by dates, which is why the Levant's equivalent is called the Epipalaeolithic, and why researchers in Southeast Asia found early on that the label did not fit their evidence at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The warming remade the land: tundra gave way to dense forest, rising seas swallowed coastal plains, and the great herds of open country thinned or moved north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Hunters answered with the microlith, a blade rarely longer than 3 centimetres (1.2 inches), glued in rows into a shaft of wood or antler to arm arrows, harpoons and knives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Diets broadened to fish, shellfish, waterfowl, small game and hazelnuts, a shift Kent Flannery named the broad spectrum revolution in 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup> Star Carr in North Yorkshire, in use between about 9300 and 8500 BCE, gave up barbed antler points, a lake-edge platform of split timbers, and 33 red deer skullcaps worked into headdresses, of which only nine were pierced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"11\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"12\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"13\"></sup> Water dominated the richest sites: the Pesse canoe of the Netherlands, a hollowed log just under 3 metres (10 feet) long, is reckoned the oldest boat known, and Denmark's shell middens hold the oysters of coastal foragers who lived off the sea long before any crop was sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"14\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"15\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mesolithic","questions":["Coined by Hodder Westropp in 1866 alongside a third term he later dropped, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took its modern shape from Grahame Clark’s division of the age in 1932.","At Star Carr in North Yorkshire the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left barbed antler points and 33 red deer skullcaps worked into headdresses, though only nine of them were pierced."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["Hodder M. Westropp, “On the Analogous Forms of Implements among Early and Primitive Races,” <i>Memoirs Read before the Anthropological Society of London</i> 2 (1866): 288–93, at 291, https://archive.org/details/memoirsreadbefor02anth. [Open access]","Hodder M. Westropp, <i>Pre-historic Phases; or, Introductory Essays on Pre-historic Archæology</i> (London: Bell and Daldy, 1872), 65, https://archive.org/details/cu31924029915240. [Open access]","John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Ben Elliott and Seren Griffiths, “Living Mesolithic Time: Narratives, Chronologies and Organic Material Culture,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 31, no. 3 (2018): 347–65, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-018-9119-x. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., “Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,” <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–23, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under “The Origins of Domestication,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Podjanok Kanjanajuntorn, “The Three-Age System: A Struggle for Southeast Asian Prehistoric Periodisation,” <i>SPAFA Journal</i> 4 (2020): 5, https://doi.org/10.26721/spafajournal.v4i0.623. [Open access]","Heikki Seppä et al., “Last Nine-Thousand Years of Temperature Variability in Northern Europe,” <i>Climate of the Past</i> 5 (2009): 523–35, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-5-523-2009. [Open access]","Lars Larsson, Arne Sjöström, and Carl Heron, “The Rönneholm Arrow: A Find of a Wooden Arrow-Tip with Microliths in the Bog Rönneholms Mosse, Central Scania, Southern Sweden,” <i>Lund Archaeological Review</i> 22 (2016): 7–20, https://journals.lub.lu.se/lar/article/view/21616. [Open access]","Mary C. Stiner, “Thirty Years on the ‘Broad Spectrum Revolution’ and Paleolithic Demography,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 98, no. 13 (2001): 6993–96, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC34611/. [Open access]","Jessica Bates et al., “Spatial Organisation within the Earliest Evidence of Post-Built Structures in Britain,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 7 (2024): e0306908, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11249238/. [Open access]","Ben Elliott, Becky Knight, and Aimée Little, “Antler Frontlets,” in <i>Star Carr Volume 2: Studies in Technology, Subsistence and Environment</i>, ed. Nicky Milner, Chantal Conneller, and Barry Taylor (York: White Rose University Press, 2018), 297–333, at 297 and 321, https://doi.org/10.22599/book2.l. [Open access]","Aimée Little et al., “Technological Analysis of the World’s Earliest Shamanic Costume: A Multi-Scalar, Experimental Study of a Red Deer Headdress from the Early Holocene Site of Star Carr, UK,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0152136, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4830440/. [Open access]","Cultural Heritage Agency of the Netherlands, “Pesse, kano van (-7510),” Maritime Stepping Stones database, accessed 31 July 2026, https://mass.cultureelerfgoed.nl/pesse. [Open access]","Harry K. Robson et al., “The Effects of Mid-Holocene Foragers on the European Oyster in Denmark,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 48 (2024): e2411692121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11573498/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Stone_Age_Stone_Mortar_%26_Pestle%2C_Kebaran_culture%2C_22000-18000_BP.jpg/1920px-Stone_Age_Stone_Mortar_%26_Pestle%2C_Kebaran_culture%2C_22000-18000_BP.jpg","title":"Mesolithic","desc":"En Gev Epipaleolithic period Basalt Israel Antiquities Authority H: 29 cm; Diam: 25-28 cm; Weight: 11 kg Prehistoric Periods The Israel Museum, Jerusalem Archaeology. Stone Age Stone Mortar & Pestle, Kebaran culture, 22000-18000 BP Israel Museum, Jerusalem, Israel. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stone_Age_Stone_Mortar_%26_Pestle,_Kebaran_culture,_22000-18000_BP.jpg","alt":"Stone Age Stone Mortar & Pestle, Kebaran culture, 22000-18000 BP"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-107","num":"107","category":"World · History","question":"Where the end of the Ice Age changed little and farming followed soon after, archaeologists prefer the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Mesolithic, a name stressing continuity with what came before.","answer":"Epipaleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 23,000 – 11,600 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Epipaleolithic</b> is the last stretch of the Paleolithic in the Levant and the wider Near East, the hunter-gatherers who lived through the end of the Ice Age and the millennia after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name is built on the Greek <i>epi</i>, meaning 'upon': this is the age laid on top of the Old Stone Age rather than a break from it, and that emphasis is why the word exists. It covers the same ground as the Mesolithic, between the Upper Paleolithic and the Neolithic, but the two names lean opposite ways, one stressing what carried on and the other what was coming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists reach for it where the retreat of the ice altered the landscape comparatively little, and where farming followed too closely for any long middle age. It runs from roughly 23,000 to 11,600 years ago in the southern Levant, and the label reaches North Africa too, where the Iberomaurusian of Taforalt belongs to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> The Levantine sequence runs from the Kebaran through the Geometric Kebaran, whose microliths are trimmed into neat trapezes and triangles, to the Natufian of about 15,000 to 11,650 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Natufians are why the period matters: at Ain Mallaha they cut curved houses into the slope and walled them with undressed limestone, over some 2,000 square metres that may have held 50 to 100 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> They cut wild cereals with flint-bladed sickles in a harvest that ran from mid-spring to late, ground them on stone, stored food, and kept the dog, their only domesticated animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Shubayqa 1 in north-eastern Jordan a team recovered charred flatbread of wild einkorn and club-rush tubers, baked about 14,400 years ago, the oldest bread known and some 4,000 years older than farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> None of this was agriculture, yet the huts, the graves and the heavy ground stone show hunter-gatherers already settling down, and the first farming villages of the Pre-Pottery Neolithic grew out of exactly these camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Epipaleolithic","questions":["Natufians cut curved houses into the slope at Ain Mallaha, where 50 to 100 people may have lived, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows foragers settling down before any crop was sown.","At Shubayqa 1 in Jordan the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left charred flatbread of wild einkorn baked about 14,400 years ago, the oldest bread known and older than farming."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["Lisa A. Maher et al., “A Unique Human-Fox Burial from a Pre-Natufian Cemetery in the Levant (Jordan),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 1 (2011): e15815, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3027631/. [Open access]","Laurent Davin et al., “Modeling Identities among the First-Sedentary Communities: Emergence of Clay Personal Ornaments in Epipaleolithic Southwest Asia,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12 (2026): eaea2158, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12998513/. [Open access]","Ceri Shipton et al., “The Natufian Epipalaeolithic and Pre-Pottery Neolithic in the Desert of Northern Arabia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 16842, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13226711/. [Open access]","Gil Haklay and Avi Gopher, “A New Look at Shelter 131/51 in the Natufian Site of Eynan (Ain-Mallaha), Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 7 (2015): e0130121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4496039/. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]","Simon J. M. Davis and François R. Valla, “Evidence for Domestication of the Dog 12,000 Years Ago in the Natufian of Israel,” <i>Nature</i> 276 (1978): 608–10, https://doi.org/10.1038/276608a0. [Paywalled]","Amaia Arranz-Otaegui et al., “Archaeobotanical Evidence Reveals the Origins of Bread 14,400 Years Ago in Northeastern Jordan,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 31 (2018): 7925–30, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6077754/. [Open access]","Jonathan Santana et al., “Multi-Isotope Evidence of Population Aggregation in the Natufian and Scant Migration during the Early Neolithic of the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 11857, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8178372/. [Open access]","Zineb Moubtahij et al., “Isotopic Evidence of High Reliance on Plant Food among Later Stone Age Hunter-Gatherers at Taforalt, Morocco,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 8 (2024): 1035–45, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11090808/. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under “The Origins of Domestication,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Lovers_9000BC_british_museum.jpg","title":"Epipaleolithic","desc":"Photo of an 11 thousand year old sculpture believed to depict a couple having sex. Now in the British Museum. Originates from Ain Sakhri, Judea. Geni, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lovers_9000BC_british_museum.jpg","alt":"Lovers 9000 BCE british museum"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"wh-108","num":"108","category":"World · History","question":"Named after a sandbank and mapped from oil-industry seismic data, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the drowned country that joined Britain to the continent until the sea closed over it.","answer":"Doggerland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dry land</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 16,000 – 8,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Storegga tsunami</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,150 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1998, by Bryony Coles</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Doggerland</b> is the name for the country that lay where the southern North Sea is now, joining Britain to the Netherlands, Germany and Denmark by dry land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With so much water locked up in ice, the sea stood far lower, and from roughly 16,000 years ago this was a plain of rivers, lakes, marsh and open woodland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Rhine, the Meuse, the Thames and the Scheldt ran together across it, and its wetlands would have been some of the richest hunting and fishing country in Europe. Bryony Coles gave it the name in 1998, after the Dogger Bank, which is itself named for the doggers, the old Dutch fishing boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it is under water, almost everything known about it has been dredged up, washed ashore or read off seismic surveys made by the oil industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The find that started it came up in a North Sea trawler's net in 1931, a lump of peat that broke open to reveal a barbed antler point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Since then bones of mammoth, lion, deer and aurochs have come up with the fish, along with worked flint, human remains and Mesolithic tools of bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Seismic data gathered to look for gas has been reworked into maps of the buried rivers and coastlines, covering tens of thousands of square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sea took it gradually and then, about 8,150 years ago, a huge landslip off Norway sent a tsunami across whatever was left of the low ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A study in 2020 argued that even so it was not finished at a stroke, and that the slow rise of the sea rather than the wave is what took the last of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Doggerland","questions":["The story began in 1931 when a Norfolk trawler struck a lump of peat and pulled up a barbed antler point, the first sign that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had ever been dry land.","Where the Rhine, Meuse, Thames and Scheldt once ran together, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a plain of marsh and woodland that made some of Europe's richest hunting and fishing country."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","prehistory"],"sources":["James Walker et al., \"A Great Wave: The Storegga Tsunami and the End of Doggerland?\" <i>Antiquity</i> 94, no. 378 (2020): 1409–25, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2020.49. [Open access]","Vincent Gaffney, Kenneth Thomson, and Simon Fitch, eds., <i>Mapping Doggerland: The Mesolithic Landscapes of the Southern North Sea</i> (Oxford: Archaeopress, 2007), https://www.archaeopress.com/Archaeopress/DMS/F9B49C6FC7E04728B2346A365BC405F1/9781905739141-sample.pdf. [Open access]","Bernhard Weninger et al., \"The Catastrophic Final Flooding of Doggerland by the Storegga Slide Tsunami,\" <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 35 (2008): 1–24, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.35.1. [Open access]","Vincent Gaffney et al., \"Multi-Proxy Characterisation of the Storegga Tsunami and Its Impact on the Early Holocene Landscapes of the Southern North Sea,\" <i>Geosciences</i> 10, no. 7 (2020): 270, https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences10070270. [Open access]","J. H. M. Peeters and G. Momber, \"The Southern North Sea and the Human Occupation of Northwest Europe after the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Netherlands Journal of Geosciences</i> 93, no. 1–2 (2014): 55–70, https://doi.org/10.1017/njg.2014.3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Doggerbank.jpg","title":"Doggerland","desc":"Doggerbank. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doggerbank.jpg","alt":"Doggerbank"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Doggerland","at":[3,52],"kind":"region","area":[[0.9,53],[1.1,55.3],[2.6,56.5],[5.2,57],[7.6,55.9],[8.3,54.4],[6.2,53],[4.2,52.2],[2.6,51.9],[1,52.3]]}},
{"id":"wh-113","num":"113","category":"World · History","question":"Warmest around 6,500 years ago and driven by a wobble in Earth's orbit, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lifted northern summers above today's, while the tropics stayed cooler.","answer":"Holocene climatic optimum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Interval</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 – 5,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Warmest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6,500 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Holocene climatic optimum</b> is the warm interval of the early and middle Holocene, running roughly from 9,000 to 5,000 years ago and warmest near 6,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carries a crowd of names: the Hypsithermal, the Altithermal, the Holocene Thermal Maximum and the Mid-Holocene Warm Period, a fair sign of how loosely its edges are drawn. The warmth came from the slow wobble of Earth's orbit, since precession brought the northern summer nearer the Sun and raised the sunlight falling on high northern latitudes. That forcing peaked before the warmth did, because the Laurentide ice sheet still lay over North America, and its cold and its whiteness held temperatures down until it had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the optimum is no single moment shared by the planet: early and strong at high northern latitudes, later and fainter in the tropics, and dated at some sites anywhere between 11,000 and 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its evidence lies in living things and ice, for the northern treeline stood further north than now, Arctic glaciers shrank, and warm-water molluscs lived on Canadian coasts too cold for them today. The most striking change was in Africa, where a stronger summer monsoon watered much of the Sahara, filling it with grassland, rivers and lakes that drew herders and their cattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That African humid period ended about 5,500 years ago, though the drying came neither everywhere at once nor everywhere abruptly, running earlier in the north than the south. The warmth was seasonal and regional rather than global, gathered into northern summers, while the tropics and parts of the Southern Hemisphere sat cooler than they do now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That makes it a weak analogue for present warming: one reconstruction puts its warmest centuries 0.7 degrees above the 19th century but 0.3 degrees below the average of 2011 to 2019, and the IPCC holds with medium confidence that the recent decade is the warmer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Holocene climatic optimum","questions":["When roughly 9 million square kilometres (3.5 million square miles) of the Sahara held grassland, rivers and lakes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood at its height, and that green ended about 5,500 years ago.","Known also as the Hypsithermal and the Altithermal, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> warmed northern summers through orbital precession, yet it makes a poor analogue for the even warming of today."],"tags":["era","geology","science","climate"],"sources":["Darrell S. Kaufman et al., \"Holocene Global Mean Surface Temperature, a Multi-method Reconstruction Approach,\" <i>Scientific Data</i> 7 (2020): 201, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0530-7. [Open access]","Olivier Cartapanis et al., \"Complex Spatio-temporal Structure of the Holocene Thermal Maximum,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 13 (2022): 5662, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-33362-1. [Open access]","Jessica E. Tierney, Francesco S. R. Pausata, and Peter B. deMenocal, \"Rainfall Regimes of the Green Sahara,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 1 (2017): e1601503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601503. [Open access]","IPCC, \"Summary for Policymakers,\" in <i>Climate Change 2021: The Physical Science Basis</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), para. A.2.2, https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg1/chapter/summary-for-policymakers/. [Open access]","Olivier Marchal et al., \"Apparent Long-Term Cooling of the Sea Surface in the Northeast Atlantic and Mediterranean during the Holocene,\" <i>Quaternary Science Reviews</i> 21, no. 4–6 (2002): 455–83, https://www.whoi.edu/cms/files/marchal02qsr_42274.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/202006_KaufmanEtAl_Holocene_Temp_NH_Bond.png","title":"Holocene climatic optimum","desc":"Holocene temperature anomalies in latitudes 30°-60° and 60°-90° N and Bond-events. DeWikiMan, based on work by Kaufman, D., McKay, N., Routson, C. et al.,. CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:202006_KaufmanEtAl_Holocene_Temp_NH_Bond.png","alt":"KaufmanEtAl Holocene Temp NH Bond"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-114","num":"114","category":"World · History","question":"About 8,200 years ago the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chilled the northern hemisphere for some 160 years, the sharpest cold snap of the Holocene, before the warmth of the epoch returned.","answer":"8.2-kiloyear event","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Event</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,200 BP, or 6,200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">some 160 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>8.2-kiloyear event</b> was an abrupt turn to cold, dry conditions about 8,200 years ago, and it stands as the sharpest climate reversal of the Holocene, the warm epoch we still live in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greenland ice cores register it as a plunge in oxygen isotopes that arrived within a couple of decades and lasted roughly 160 years, with temperatures over the ice falling by some 3 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the northern hemisphere cooled by 1 to 3 degrees, a drop legible in lake muds, pollen, cave formations and ocean sediments across four continents. The usual explanation begins with a flood, for Lakes Agassiz and Ojibway, vast sheets of meltwater ponded against the shrinking Laurentide ice, burst through Hudson Bay into the Labrador Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A vast pulse of fresh water reached the ocean in a few years at most, and that lid of light water slowed the Atlantic overturning circulation that carries warmth north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Not everyone accepts so simple a chain, and some argue that the collapse of the ice saddle over Hudson Bay, rather than the lakes alone, delivered the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whatever the trigger, the consequences were global, since the tropical rain belts shifted south, the Asian and African monsoons weakened and the Middle East turned arid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The outburst also raised the sea by a metre or two (3 to 7 feet), leaving Doggerland a shrinking archipelago, and the Storegga landslide off Norway sent a tsunami across it during the coldest decades of the event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Near East the tightly linked world of the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B came apart within these centuries, its great sites emptying across the Levant, Syria and Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How much of that the cold caused is still argued, but the event is firm enough in the record that geologists made it a boundary, its Greenland horizon of 8,236 years before 2000 opening the Northgrippian, the middle age of the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"8.2-kiloyear event","questions":["When Lakes Agassiz and Ojibway burst through Hudson Bay into the Labrador Sea, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed, because that flood of fresh water slowed the Atlantic's overturning circulation.","Geologists cut the Holocene in two at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Greenland ice-core boundary at 8,236 years before 2000 opens the Northgrippian, the middle age of the epoch."],"tags":["event","geology","science","climate"],"sources":["Richard B. Alley et al., \"Holocene Climatic Instability: A Prominent, Widespread Event 8200 yr Ago,\" <i>Geology</i> 25, no. 6 (1997): 483–86, https://doi.org/10.1130/0091-7613%281997%29025%3C0483:HCIAPW%3E2.3.CO;2. [Open access]","Ilkka S. O. Matero et al., \"The 8.2 ka Cooling Event Caused by Laurentide Ice Saddle Collapse,\" <i>Earth and Planetary Science Letters</i> 473 (2017): 205–14, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.epsl.2017.06.011. [Open access]","E. R. Thomas et al., \"The 8.2 ka Event from Greenland Ice Cores,\" <i>Quaternary Science Reviews</i> 26, no. 1–2 (2007): 70–81, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.quascirev.2006.07.017. [Paywalled]","James Walker et al., \"A Great Wave: The Storegga Tsunami and the End of Doggerland?\" <i>Antiquity</i> 94, no. 378 (2020): 1409–25, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2020.49. [Open access]","Bernhard Weninger et al., \"Climate Forcing Due to the 8200 cal yr BP Event Observed at Early Neolithic Sites in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>Quaternary Research</i> 66, no. 3 (2006): 401–20, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.yqres.2006.06.009. [Paywalled]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,\" <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–223, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Climate_Relapse_around_6250_BC.png","title":"8.2-kiloyear event","desc":"This is the most prominent temperature fallback (regression) of the Holocene immediately preceding the Atlantic temperature peak. Hans J.J.G. Holm, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Climate_Relapse_around_6250_BC.png","alt":"Climate Relapse around 6250 BCE"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-001","num":"1","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Running from about 3100 to 1050 BCE across Crete, the Cyclades and the Greek mainland, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is divided into Minoan, Cycladic and Helladic sequences.","answer":"Aegean Bronze Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Aegean Bronze Age</b> runs from about 3100 to 1050 BCE, and three regional traditions divide it, Minoan on Crete, Cycladic in the islands and Helladic on the Greek mainland, each cut into Early, Middle and Late phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islanders built first, and by about 2650 BCE the Cyclades held towns of two-storeyed houses, while the long ships they drew on clay pans needed crews of up to fifty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Crete followed with the palaces, which rose at Knossos and Phaistos around 1900 BCE and called for trained masons and workforces far larger than anything the island had assembled before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Metal explains much of the traffic between them, since Crete is badly supplied with copper, tin, silver and gold, and that shortage pushed Cretans to sea to find what their smiths needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Crete produced the first literate high civilisation in Europe, and its palaces reached out to Egypt, the Levant and Cyprus, their finest pottery passing to foreign courts as gifts between ruling families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From about 1750 BCE Minoan ways spread through the islands, and Cretan pottery, Linear A tablets and frescoes turn up at Phylakopi, Ayia Irini and Akrotiri, whose towns fortified themselves while Crete did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The eruption that buried the Theran town of Akrotiri is the age's sharpest quarrel, and Sturt Manning dates it to between 1609 and 1560 BCE, shifting the landmarks of the whole period with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The mainland rose later and differently, its lords at Mycenae buried in shaft graves heavy with gold, much of it worked to their own taste by Cretan smiths living among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After about 1470 BCE the balance turned towards the mainland, and Knossos alone went on as a palace, keeping its records in Linear B, the script shown in 1952 to record Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That made Greek the oldest language in Europe still spoken; the mainland palaces burned around 1200 BCE, a drying climate ensuring none rose again, but the age itself runs on to about 1050.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aegean Bronze Age","questions":["Crete's shortage of copper, tin, silver and gold sent its people to sea, and that search for metal shaped trade across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from its earliest centuries.","The mainland palaces burned around 1200 BCE and no central authority returned, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran on to about 1050 before giving way to the Greek Early Iron Age."],"tags":["era","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 4: The Early Cycladic Period,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-4/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, <i>Minoan Crete: An Introduction</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), 2, 5–8, https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/40493/excerpt/9781108440493_excerpt.pdf. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, \"Second Intermediate Period Date for the Thera (Santorini) Eruption and Historical Implications,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 9 (2022): e0274835, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0274835. [Open access]","Martin Finné et al., \"Late Bronze Age Climate Change and the Destruction of the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor at Pylos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 12 (2017): e0189447, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0189447. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/Aegean_Civilization%2C_H._G._Wells%27_Outline_of_History%2C_page_95.jpg","title":"Aegean Bronze Age","desc":"Illustration from page 95 of The outline of history; being a plain history of life and mankind, the definitive edition revised and rearranged by the author, by H.G. Wells, illustrated by J. F. Horrabin, showing \"Aegean Civilization.\". Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aegean_Civilization,_H._G._Wells%27_Outline_of_History,_page_95.jpg","alt":"Aegean Civilization, H. G. Wells' Outline of History, page 95"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-002","num":"2","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Farming hamlets, cemeteries of 20 graves or fewer, and towns whose houses had upper floors all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the central Aegean islands.","answer":"Cycladic civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grotta-Pelos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Keros-Syros</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2650 – 2400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phylakopi I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1850 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cycladic civilisation</b> is the Early Bronze Age culture of the Cyclades, the islands of the central Aegean, and one of the three that make up the Greek Bronze Age beside Minoan Crete and the Helladic mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people began as farmers in scattered hamlets, each holding one kin group for three or four generations and each with its own small cemetery outside the houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those burial grounds usually held 20 graves or fewer, the dead laid on their sides with the knees drawn up, and very often nothing was buried with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By about 2650 BCE some islanders were living in real towns, and at Skarkos on Ios the neat masonry still stands almost three metres (10 feet) high, most houses having had an upper floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the islands had to offer the wider Aegean was metal: lead and silver from Siphnos, copper from Kythnos, and all three from the Laurion ores across the water in Attica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> None of it moved without boats, and the long ships incised on Cycladic clay pans needed crews of up to fifty, more than most single settlements could raise, so neighbours had to combine to put one to sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They sailed a long way, and while the obsidian used on Keros came mostly from Melos, pieces came in from the Dodecanese and central Anatolia, along the routes that brought Anatolian tin and gold into the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Keros became the meeting point of those routes, a barren island where islanders gathered to lay marble figures and vessels in the ground, every one broken somewhere else and carried in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Late in the 3rd millennium BCE small walled forts went up on Syros, Naxos and Delos and were abandoned within a few generations, after which the hamlets drew together into single large towns and marble carving died away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through all of it the islanders were not newcomers, since Early Bronze Age Aegean genomes are closely alike and descend mostly from the Neolithic farmers already living there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cycladic civilisation","questions":["Lead and silver from Siphnos, copper from Kythnos and Melian obsidian all moved by sea, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> depended on boats crewed by more men than one village held.","The most prosperous phase of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closes late in the 3rd millennium BCE, when the small walled forts on Syros, Naxos and Delos were abandoned within a few generations."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 4: The Early Cycladic Period,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-4/. [Open access]","Florian Clemente et al., \"The Genomic History of the Aegean Palatial Civilizations,\" <i>Cell</i> 184, no. 10 (2021): 2565–86, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2021.03.039. [Open access]","Colin Renfrew et al., \"The Sanctuary at Keros in the Aegean Early Bronze Age: From Centre of Congregation to Centre of Power,\" <i>Journal of Greek Archaeology</i> 7 (2022): 1–36, https://doi.org/10.32028/jga.v7i.1709. [Open access]","Tristan Carter et al., \"Characterizing Centrality: Obsidian Consumption, Supra-Regional Connectivity, and Social Reproduction at the Early Bronze Age Sanctuary of Keros (Cyclades, Greece),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0325218, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0325218. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Marble_seated_harp_player_MET_gr47.100.1.R.jpg","title":"Cycladic civilisation","desc":"Cycladic; Statuette of a seated harp player; Stone. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_seated_harp_player_MET_gr47.100.1.R.jpg","alt":"Marble seated harp player"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"The Cyclades","at":[25.1667,37],"kind":"region","area":[[24.1,37.3],[24.55,37.95],[25.3,38.05],[25.95,37.2],[25.9,36.45],[25.2,36.2],[24.3,36.5],[24.05,36.95]]}},
{"id":"gr-003","num":"3","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Carved from island marble and found mostly in tombs, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were made to lie on their backs, since the feet and knees are rendered for a reclining figure.","answer":"Cycladic figurines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cycladic figurines</b> are the marble human figures carved in the islands of the central Aegean during the Early Bronze Age, and most of them came out of graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are not one thing: the earliest have prominent ears and kneecaps with hands meeting across the stomach, others hold stubby arms out at shoulder level, and the plainest are barely more than ovoid pebbles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The famous type, arms folded across the chest, belongs to the centuries around 2600 BCE, and was never meant to stand, since the feet and knees are carved for a figure lying on its back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor are they all women, for among the earliest the sexes are plainly marked and the males are almost as numerous as the females.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beside them the islanders carved people doing things, harpists seated with their instruments, pipers standing to play, warriors wearing the strap that held a dagger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Carving one cost a practised hand 20 to 30 hours with nothing but stone tools, and the workshop at Skarkos on Ios held emery, obsidian and pumice beside half-finished figures and pigment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pigment is the point, because the white marble we admire is an accident of survival: these figures were painted, black and blue for eyes, eyebrows and hair, red for necklaces, bracelets and diadems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the paint has gone it often leaves a paint ghost, a smoother patch standing in faint relief because the stone beneath weathered differently from the bare stone around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What those patterns meant is genuinely open — body paint for particular occasions, marks of status, attributes of the figure, badges of a shared identity — and the graves hold the colour kits too, stained bowls with pestles and bone tubes for pigment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not all stayed whole, for at the sanctuary on Keros they arrive already broken, one piece carried over the sea and the rest left behind, and because so many others were dug for the market rather than recorded, most have no findspot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cycladic figurines","questions":["Black and blue once marked their eyes and hair and red their necklaces, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> we admire as white marble are stripped of the colour they once carried.","Harpists, pipers and warriors with a dagger-strap were carved alongside the folded-arm figures, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show far more than one repeated female form."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 4: The Early Cycladic Period,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-4/. [Open access]","\"The Use of Colour in Cycladic Art,\" Museum of Cycladic Art, Athens, https://cycladic.gr/en/essay/i-chrisi-tou-chromatos-stin-kykladiki-te/. [Open access]","\"Manufacture of Cycladic Figurines,\" Museum of Cycladic Art, Athens, https://cycladic.gr/en/essay/technikes-kataskevis-ton-kykladikon-e/. [Open access]","Colin Renfrew et al., \"The Sanctuary at Keros in the Aegean Early Bronze Age: From Centre of Congregation to Centre of Power,\" <i>Journal of Greek Archaeology</i> 7 (2022): 1–36, https://doi.org/10.32028/jga.v7i.1709. [Open access]","David W. J. Gill and Christopher Chippindale, \"Material and Intellectual Consequences of Esteem for Cycladic Figures,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 97, no. 4 (1993): 601–59, https://doi.org/10.2307/506716. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Cycladic_female_figurine_2.jpg","title":"Cycladic figurines","desc":"Cycladic female figurine, from Museum of Cycladic Art at Athens - Canonical type, early work of the Spedos variety, 17.3 cm, #104a in the 1968 catalogue. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cycladic_female_figurine_2.jpg","alt":"Cycladic female figurine 2"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-004","num":"4","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cycladic islanders sailed to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for centuries carrying single pieces of figurines and bowls broken at home, and left them in deposits whose fragments never join up.","answer":"Keros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2750 – 2250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Keros</b> is a small, rugged and now uninhabited island at the centre of the Cyclades, and for five hundred years of the Early Bronze Age it was among the best-connected places in the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That is the strange part, because the island has poor soil, little farmland and almost nothing worth quarrying, so nearly everything on it had to be carried there across open water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Facing its western tip across a narrow strait stands the islet of Dhaskalio, which a natural causeway joined to Keros while the place was in use, giving visiting ships good shelter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its builders cut the steep slopes into terraces to make level ground and raised their walls from marble ferried 10 kilometres (6 miles) from Naxos, more than 7,000 tonnes (7,700 tons) of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stairways, massive entrances and a drainage system running across the whole islet mark it out as a planned settlement, on a scale its excavator compares with Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Yet almost nobody lived there: kitchens and rubbish heaps are missing, the food came from neighbouring islands, and the buildings look made to receive crowds rather than to house them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What those crowds came to do is why Keros is famous, for at Kavos on that western tip they laid marble figures and stone vessels in the ground already broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pieces do not fit together, most objects being present as a single fragment whose remainder is always missing, so the breaking was done on the islands the visitors had sailed from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dhaskalio worked metal beyond anything else known in the Early Bronze Age Cyclades, casting axes, chisels, spearheads and daggers, and its obsidian came from Melos, the Dodecanese and central Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Radiocarbon places its three phases between roughly 2750 and 2250 BCE, after which the island was left and, unusually for the Aegean, never built on again, so it stands as the oldest maritime sanctuary yet known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Keros","questions":["More than 7,000 tonnes (7,700 tons) of marble were shipped 10 kilometres (6 miles) from Naxos to build Dhaskalio, the terraced settlement joined by a causeway to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its two special deposits.","With poor soil and almost nothing worth quarrying, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> imported even its building stone, yet its metalworkers cast more bronze than anywhere else in the Early Bronze Age Cyclades."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Colin Renfrew et al., \"The Sanctuary at Keros in the Aegean Early Bronze Age: From Centre of Congregation to Centre of Power,\" <i>Journal of Greek Archaeology</i> 7 (2022): 1–36, https://doi.org/10.32028/jga.v7i.1709. [Open access]","Colin Renfrew, \"The Sanctuary at Keros: Questions of Materiality and Monumentality,\" <i>Journal of the British Academy</i> 1 (2013): 187–212, https://doi.org/10.5871/jba/001.187. [Open access]","Colin Renfrew, Michael Boyd, and Christopher Bronk Ramsey, \"The Oldest Maritime Sanctuary? Dating the Sanctuary at Keros and the Cycladic Early Bronze Age,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 86, no. 331 (2012): 144–60, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00062517. [Paywalled]","\"Evidence for Advanced Architectural Planning at the Early Prehistoric Site of Dhaskalio in the Aegean,\" British School at Athens, 6 May 2019, https://www.bsa.ac.uk/2019/05/06/evidence-for-advanced-architectural-planning-at-the-early-prehistoric-site-of-dhaskalio-in-the-aegean/. [Open access]","\"Keros Project,\" Department of Archaeology, University of Cambridge, https://www.arch.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/current-projects/keros-project. [Open access]","Tristan Carter et al., \"Characterizing Centrality: Obsidian Consumption, Supra-Regional Connectivity, and Social Reproduction at the Early Bronze Age Sanctuary of Keros (Cyclades, Greece),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0325218, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0325218. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Figurilla_de_mujer%2C_Cultura_Keros-Silos_%28British_Museum%29.jpg/1920px-Figurilla_de_mujer%2C_Cultura_Keros-Silos_%28British_Museum%29.jpg","title":"Keros","desc":"Estatuillas cicládicas. Figurilla esculpida en mármol de la Cultura Keros-Siros, tipo Spedos (2500-2300 a.C.). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Figurilla_de_mujer,_Cultura_Keros-Silos_(British_Museum).jpg","alt":"Figurilla de mujer, Cultura Keros-Silos (British Museum)"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Keros","at":[25.65,36.89]}},
{"id":"gr-005","num":"5","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"More than 70 round tholos tombs cluster around the Mesara plain in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each taking hundreds of bodies over centuries with no regard paid to the burials already inside.","answer":"Early Minoan Crete","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">EM I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EM II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2650 – 2150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EM III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2150 – 2000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Early Minoan Crete</b> is the island in the Early Bronze Age, running from about 3100 to 2000 BCE, the long stretch of Cretan life before any palace was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> People lived in small settlements of a few families, and the fullest recovered, Myrtos Fournou Korifi on the south coast, packs some 65 rooms and yards into a sixth of a hectare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That warren was first read as one complex run by a central authority, until Todd Whitelaw took it apart into five or six family houses of much the same size, holding perhaps fifty people between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sameness is what matters: kitchens, storerooms and rooms of looms and washtubs repeat house by house instead of being shared, and nothing in the buildings or the finds puts anyone above anyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were not poor for it, and Mochlos in the north-east grew into a large village of houses and streets, with workshops turning out gold jewellery, stone vases and obsidian blades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Their dead went into communal tombs, in the south the round stone tholos, four to thirteen metres (13 to 43 feet) across and almost always entered from the east, more than 70 of them known from over 45 sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hundreds of bodies went into most of them with no regard for the burials already there, each laid down with personal belongings and with food and drink for whatever came after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where a settlement had two such tombs they are much of a size and hold much the same goods, the best evidence that Mesara society was one of rough equals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The north-east buried differently, in rectangular house tombs, and at Mochlos two unusually large and isolated ones held gold diadems and a silver vessel, marks of rank that may belong to chiefly families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Myrtos and Vasiliki both burned near the end of the period, yet no incomers explain it, since ancient genomes give Minoans at least three-quarters of their ancestry from the first farmers of western Anatolia and the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Early Minoan Crete","questions":["Once the warren of 65 rooms at Myrtos was taken apart into five or six ordinary family houses rather than one ruler's complex, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looked strikingly egalitarian.","Houses of much the same size, each with its own kitchen, storerooms and looms, are why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks like a society with nobody standing above anyone else."],"tags":["era","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 5: The Early Minoan Period: The Settlements,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-5-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"Mochlos,\" INSTAP Study Center for East Crete, https://instapstudycenter.net/archaeological-projects/ascsa-projects/mochlos/. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis et al., \"Genetic Origins of the Minoans and Mycenaeans,\" <i>Nature</i> 548, no. 7666 (2017): 214–18, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature23310. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Minoan_-_Sealstone_of_a_Bull_-_Walters_42769.jpg","title":"Early Minoan Crete","desc":"Carved sealstones were popular ornaments among Bronze Age Minoans and Mycenaeans and served as insignia of rank and social status. They were influenced by the elaborate traditions of stamp and cylinder seals developed in the Near East and Egypt. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_-_Sealstone_of_a_Bull_-_Walters_42769.jpg","alt":"Minoan - Sealstone of a Bull"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-006","num":"6","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Small rival states in separate valleys, palaces built above all for storage, and an undeciphered script are what make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Bronze Age culture of Crete.","answer":"Minoan civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Prepalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1720 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1720 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Postpalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1470 – 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan civilisation</b> is the Bronze Age culture of Crete, running from about 3100 to 1050 BCE and named after Minos, the king in whom the Greeks summed up the age before their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crete was never one kingdom, for its mountains cut the island into separate valleys and plains, each with its own town, so Minoans lived among small rival states more like Renaissance Italy than Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That made close communities where everyone knew everyone, the opposite of the Near Eastern kingdoms with their distant ruler and vast peasantry, and Cretan neighbours repeatedly destroyed each other's settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around 1900 BCE the palaces appeared and changed how the island worked, each a mass of rooms around a paved central court with long storage magazines in its west wing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Storage is the heart of them: Malia perhaps gives more of its ground floor to magazines than any other palace, and at three of the four the outer wall of those magazines is the building's most carefully built face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They fed and entertained too, with banquet halls upstairs at the north end, served by one stair from the kitchens and another from the courts, and shallow stepped areas where a crowd could stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Along the west side of the court lay cult rooms whose pillars are cut with double axes and tridents, read by some as places where a god blamed for earthquakes was appeased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their script was Linear A, written from about 1800 to 1450 BCE and chiefly on Crete, undeciphered still across fewer than two thousand surviving documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Minoan goods and habits travelled with their ships, and pottery, Linear A tablets, frescoes and terracotta women in Cretan dress turn up at Phylakopi, Ayia Irini and Akrotiri, while Kastri on Kythera looks settled outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around 1470 BCE destructions ended that order, and the richly armed graves appearing near Knossos soon after were long read as an incoming Mycenaean military caste, though the newest study argues for Minoans of three ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan civilisation","questions":["Linear A, the undeciphered script of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was written from about 1800 to 1450 BCE and survives on fewer than two thousand documents, most of them from Crete.","Around 1470 BCE a wave of destructions ended the palace order of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and richly armed graves appeared near Knossos soon afterwards, long read as an incoming Mycenaean caste."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, <i>Minoan Crete: An Introduction</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), 2, 5–7, https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/40493/excerpt/9781108440493_excerpt.pdf. [Open access]","Ester Salgarella and Simon Castellan, \"SigLA: The Signs of Linear A. A Palæographical Database,\" in <i>Grapholinguistics and Its Applications: Proceedings of Grapholinguistics in the 21st Century, Part II</i> (Brest: Fluxus Editions, 2020), https://doi.org/10.36824/2020-graf-salg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Knossos_women_fresco.jpg/1920px-Knossos_women_fresco.jpg","title":"Minoan civilisation","desc":"Knossos women fresco. Gleb Simonov. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_women_fresco.jpg","alt":"Knossos women fresco"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Minoan civilisation","at":[25.1631,35.2981],"kind":"region","area":[[23.35,35.75],[24.5,35.95],[25.3,36.6],[25.6,36.6],[25.75,36.15],[26.55,35.45],[26.45,34.8],[25.5,34.75],[24.3,34.75],[23.35,35.05]]}},
{"id":"gr-007","num":"7","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Chasing a script he had spotted on Cretan seal stones, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travelled the island from 1894 and reported a pre-Phoenician writing in two phases, one pictorial and one linear.","answer":"Arthur Evans","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 July 1851</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 July 1941</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ashmolean</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Keeper, 1884 – 1908</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Knossos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Excavated 1900 – 1931</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sir Arthur Evans</b>, son of the prehistorian Sir John Evans, gave Europe a Bronze Age civilisation nobody had known was there, and gave it the name it still carries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to Crete after a script rather than a palace, having met in Greece in 1893 small perforated stones cut with symbols that convinced him the Aegean had written long before the Greek alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Travelling the centre and east of the island from 1894, he was soon able to report a pre-Phoenician writing in two phases, one pictorial and one linear, which grew into the three scripts he named Cretan Hieroglyphic, Linear A and Linear B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The culture those scripts belonged to he called Minoan, after the legendary king Minos, and the word has framed every account of Bronze Age Crete since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From 1900 to 1931 he dug at Knossos, uncovering the largest palace site then known in the Aegean and opening a new chapter in the archaeology and art of the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He did not work alone, for Duncan Mackenzie ran the field archaeology, Theodore Fyfe and Christian Doll were his architects, and Piet de Jong drew the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His account of it, <i>The Palace of Minos</i>, ran to six volumes and an index, 3,108 pages and 2,433 images, a publisher's feat and by modern standards not an archaeological publication at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He worked in a Crete that had just become an autonomous state, and employed Christian and Muslim Cretans together at a time when the island was divided between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He paid for much of the digging himself, and in 1924 gave the site to the British School at Athens by deed of gift, with £6,800 endowed to keep a curator on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What most visitors see at Knossos now is his restoration work, which the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial, and which is argued over still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Arthur Evans","questions":["It was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who named the culture Minoan after the legendary king Minos, a word that has framed every account of Bronze Age Crete since.","The restorations that most visitors to Knossos take for the palace itself are the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the Oxford museum he once kept calls them extensive and controversial."],"tags":["person","archaeology","history","greece"],"sources":["\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","\"The Sir Arthur Evans and Knossos Archives: Overview and Structure,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/archive/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Cretan Pictographs and Prae-Phoenician Script</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch, 1895), 5–6, https://archive.org/details/cretanpictograph00evan. [Open access]","Arthur Evans, <i>The Palace of Minos: A Comparative Account of the Successive Stages of the Early Cretan Civilization as Illustrated by the Discoveries at Knossos</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Neolithic and Early and Middle Minoan Ages</i> (London: Macmillan, 1921), v–vi, https://archive.org/details/palaceofminoscom01evanuoft. [Open access]","\"Notable Donations and Legacies to the British School at Athens,\" British School at Athens, https://www.bsa.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Legacy-and-Donation-Case-Studies.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Margaret_and_Arthur_Evans_22_June_1888.jpg","title":"Arthur Evans","desc":"Arthur Evans (1851 - 1941) with his wife Margaret (nee Freeman, 1848 - 1893) in 1888. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margaret_and_Arthur_Evans_22_June_1888.jpg","alt":"Margaret and Arthur Evans 22 June 1888"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-008","num":"8","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Standing on the low hill of Kephala six kilometres (four miles) inland from the north coast of Crete, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the largest Minoan palace and governed the whole of north-central Crete.","answer":"Knossos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6900 – 6600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Knossos</b> is the largest of the Minoan palaces, standing on the low hill of Kephala about six kilometres (four miles) inland from the north coast of Crete, above the stream Evans knew as the Kairatos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a mound as much as a palace, since an extensive Neolithic settlement lies beneath it, and modelled radiocarbon puts the first people on the hill between 6900 and 6600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the Bronze Age it governed north-central Crete, one of a set of separate states each holding its own valley or plain, with Mount Jouktas rising to the south as the peak sanctuary that served it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The palace covers about 13,000 square metres measured at one level, much of that carried up through two or more storeys, and everything in it is arranged around a paved central court 54 by 27 metres (177 by 89 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Along the west side of that court lay the cult rooms, and behind the west front the long storage magazines, so the two things the building most plainly did were worship and keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Above the magazines, on the floor later writers call the piano nobile, lay halls of state reached by broad stairs leading up from the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A banquet hall stood at the north end of the building, served by one staircase from the kitchens below and another from the courts, so dining was kept well away from audience and residence alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the southeast quarter were the residential quarters, suites of hall, forehall and lightwell with a more private room beyond, a sunken lustral basin and often a toilet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Outside the west front lay a paved court crossed by raised limestone causeways, with three great stone-lined pits sunk into it, and at the northwest corner the stepped theatral area where a crowd could stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The site draws over a million visitors a year, and the walls they walk between are largely Evans's restoration, which the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Knossos","questions":["Storage magazines behind the west front and cult rooms along the court are the two things <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> most plainly did, under halls of state reached by broad stairs.","Beneath the palace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies a Neolithic settlement whose first occupation modelled radiocarbon dates to between 6900 and 6600 BCE, one of the earliest in the Aegean and in Europe."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 3–70, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Katerina Douka et al., \"Dating Knossos and the Arrival of the Earliest Neolithic in the Southern Aegean,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 91, no. 356 (2017): 304–21, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.29. [Paywalled]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, <i>Minoan Crete: An Introduction</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), 5–7, https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/40493/excerpt/9781108440493_excerpt.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Knossos_%E2%80%94_Throne_room_grass_fresco.jpg/1920px-Knossos_%E2%80%94_Throne_room_grass_fresco.jpg","title":"Knossos","desc":"Knossos — Throne room grass fresco. Gsimonov. 2024-01-30 15:30:56. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_%E2%80%94_Throne_room_grass_fresco.jpg","alt":"Knossos — Throne room grass fresco"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Knossos","at":[25.1631,35.2981]}},
{"id":"gr-009","num":"9","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Storage magazines filling the ground floor, halls of state above them and an empty paved court in the middle are the shape of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from about 1900 BCE.","answer":"Minoan palace","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Protopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1720 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1720 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kommos palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 50 years in use</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Minoan palace</b> is a building set around a rectangular central court, the type that defines Crete from about 1900 BCE, the classic four being Knossos, Phaistos, Malia and Zakros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They stand roughly one to a region, Knossos over north-central Crete, Phaistos over the Mesara plain, Chania in the west, the eastern valleys each with their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What a palace mostly did was store, since all four give much of their ground floor to magazines, and at three of them that magazine wall is the building's most carefully built face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above them lay halls of state reached by broad stairs from the court, and at the north end a banquet hall with separate stairs from the kitchens and from the public courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Suites of hall, forehall and lightwell, each with a private room and a sunken lustral basin beyond, recur in the same form at Knossos, Phaistos and Malia, and in villas and townhouses outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The court is usually laid north to south, and why is disputed: to light the colonnades along it, to aim its long axis at a sacred mountain, or to face the west-side cult rooms towards the sunrise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all daily activity happened in the rooms around that court rather than out on it, so the plan works outwards from an empty middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the middle was for is the live question, and the plastered court at Sissi, 450 square metres and holding ritual fittings, is read as ground where crowds ate and drank together at festivals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There are also more of them than the handbooks suggest, on far more scales and not all founded or abandoned together, the courts at Kommos, Galatas, Gournia, Petras and Palaikastro running from 1,102 square metres down to 78, and the Kommos palace lasting fifty years at most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word palace is an inheritance rather than a description, because a Minoan court-centred building was not the monumental residence of a powerful ruler, and its excavators increasingly say so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan palace","questions":["Why the central court of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was usually laid north to south is disputed, whether to light its colonnades, aim at a sacred mountain, or face the cult rooms towards sunrise.","Excavators now often write court-centred building instead of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the ritual eating and drinking found at Sissi's court is the newest evidence for what such courts were for."],"tags":["building","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jan Driessen, \"Revisiting the Minoan Palaces: Ritual Commensality at Sissi,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 95, no. 381 (2021): 686–704, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2021.30. [Paywalled]","Jan Driessen, \"Site History,\" Sissi Archaeological Project, Université catholique de Louvain, https://sarpedon.be/site/site-history/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, <i>Minoan Crete: An Introduction</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), 2, 5–7, https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/40493/excerpt/9781108440493_excerpt.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Phaistos_Palace_Layout_Scan.jpg","title":"Minoan palace","desc":"This is a scanned picture of the layout of the Phaistos palace from the book, Crete: The Forerunner of Greece by Charles Henry Hawes (1867-1943) and Harriet Boyd Hawes (1871-1945). The book (fourth edition) was published in 1922 by Harper & Brothers. The first edition was published in 1909. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phaistos_Palace_Layout_Scan.jpg","alt":"Phaistos Palace Layout Scan"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-010","num":"10","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A gypsum seat 1.06 metres (3 ft 6 in) tall, hollowed to fit a body and raised 21 centimetres (8 inches) above the benches around it, is what gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its name.","answer":"Throne Room at Knossos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Late phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Throne Room at Knossos</b> is a small chamber on the west side of the palace's central court, at its north end, among the rooms given over to cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against one wall stands a gypsum seat with a high back of undulating leaf-shaped outline, 1.06 metres (3 ft 6 in) tall in all, its seat hollowed to fit a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gypsum benches run round the other walls with their tops 21 centimetres (8 inches) below that seat, so whoever sat in the chair sat just above everyone else in the room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Facing it across the floor is a sunken tank of closely fitted gypsum slabs, nearly three metres (10 feet) long, reached by six steps and with no outlet at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Painted walls closed the room in, a landscape of running water, rushes, hills and palms, and on either side of the inner doorway two couchant griffins facing outward in the position of guardians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The room is later than the palace around it, belonging to the last palace epoch, which opens as the Neopalatial age closes about 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That is the age in which Knossos alone went on working as a palace, those at Phaistos, Malia and Zakros having been destroyed and never rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who held it then is not settled, the candidates being a Mycenaean conqueror, a viceroy installed by one, and a Minoan lord keeping his place with Mycenaean mercenaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Richly furnished graves appear around Knossos at the same time, their men buried with weapons and hoards of bronze vessels in a manner new to Crete and long familiar on the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The griffin fresco belongs to that period, and its heraldic stiffness is among the art read as evidence of Mainland Greeks at Knossos, though much of what a visitor sees in the room now is Evans's restoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Throne Room at Knossos","questions":["Two couchant griffins faced outward beside the inner doorway of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the position of guardians, over walls painted with water, rushes and palms.","The whole arrangement of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to the latest palace epoch at Knossos, and its griffin fresco is read as evidence of Mainland Greeks ruling on Crete."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 35–41, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Arthur Evans, <i>The Palace of Minos: A Comparative Account of the Successive Stages of the Early Cretan Civilization as Illustrated by the Discoveries at Knossos</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Neolithic and Early and Middle Minoan Ages</i> (London: Macmillan, 1921), 204, https://archive.org/details/palaceofminoscom01evanuoft. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Knossos_07.JPG/1920px-Knossos_07.JPG","title":"Throne Room at Knossos","desc":"Am 13. April 1900 bei Ausgrabungen entdeckter „Thronsaal“ von Knossos südlich von Iraklio, Kreta, Griechenland. Olaf Tausch, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_07.JPG","alt":"Knossos 07"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Knossos","at":[25.1631,35.2981]}},
{"id":"gr-011","num":"11","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The builders of the New Palace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set its west façade far east of the old one, which is why an Old Palace façade, west court and theatral area still lie open.","answer":"Phaistos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-2nd century BCE, by Gortyn</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Phaistos</b> is a Minoan palace on a hill above the great valley of the Mesara in southern Crete, with the sea and the ancient harbour lying away to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its central court is the longest of any Minoan palace at 63 metres (207 feet) from north to south, though at 22.5 metres (74 feet) across it holds slightly less ground than the court at Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its excavators stress that the building never had the plan usually credited to Cretan palaces, a court closed on four sides: it was a western block, a southern retaining wall and separate buildings round the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> West of the main façade lies a large paved court crossed by causeways of limestone slabs raised a little above the cobbles, with a theatral area of steps at its north end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A grand flight of well-joined limestone slabs about 13.5 metres (44 feet) wide climbs into the building,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the main storerooms run north to south either side of a broad corridor just inside the monumental west entrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first palace was built in Middle Minoan IB, about 1900 BCE, and the Protopalatial period closed at the end of Middle Minoan IIB in a destruction horizon probably caused by an earthquake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What sets the site apart is how much of that first palace is still visible, because the builders of the New Palace put their western façade well east of the old western margin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The old west façade, the west court before it, the theatral area and much of the west wing were left in the open rather than buried under the later building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By far the richest assortment of polychrome Kamares ware comes from the palace here, and the Mesara is arguably where that pottery reached its technical and aesthetic height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second palace was destroyed at the close of the Neopalatial age and never rebuilt, though the hill was lived on almost without a break into the Hellenistic age, until Gortyn destroyed the city in the mid-2nd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phaistos","questions":["At 63 metres (207 feet) north to south, the central court of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the longest of any Minoan palace, though its area falls just short of the court at Knossos.","By far the richest assortment of polychrome Kamares ware comes from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the Mesara plain of southern Crete is arguably where that style reached its technical height."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 45–46, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]","\"Festòs,\" Centro di Archeologia Cretese, Università degli Studi di Catania, https://www.cac.unict.it/festos. [Open access]","Keith Branigan, \"Phaistos,\" in <i>The Princeton Encyclopedia of Classical Sites</i>, ed. Richard Stillwell (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1976), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0006:entry=phaistos. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Archaeological_site_Phaistos_detail.jpg/1920px-Archaeological_site_Phaistos_detail.jpg","title":"Phaistos","desc":"Detail of the archaeological site of Phaistos, Crete, Greece. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaeological_site_Phaistos_detail.jpg","alt":"Archaeological site Phaistos detail"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Phaistos","at":[24.8136,35.0514]}},
{"id":"gr-012","num":"12","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"About 300 metres (980 feet) west of the palace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood Quartier Mu, the largest complex known on Crete from the age of the first palaces, sealed by a violent fire.","answer":"Malia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Quartier Mu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 – 1700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Malia</b> is a Minoan palace and the town about it, on the north coast of Crete, where people were living from the Early Minoan II period long before any palace stood there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its central court measures 48 by 23 metres (157 by 75 feet) and the palace covers some 7,600 square metres (81,800 square feet), the largest of the Cretan palaces after Knossos, though very few frescoes have been found in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More of its ground floor goes to storage than in any other palace, with magazines inside the west façade, in the east wing and in the north-east quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eight shallow kouloures in two rows of four sit in a walled enclosure recessed between the west court and the southern end of the west façade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The central court was laid out for ceremony, with a pit dug at its middle, perhaps for sacrifices, a limestone ball bearing one cup-mark planted to the north-west, and a polished stone with cup-marks set into the southern paving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The first palace was raised at the opening of the Protopalatial period, about 1900 BCE, and was destroyed at the end of Middle Minoan IIB by a cause that is still unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A genuine town grew up around it, and 300 metres (980 feet) to the west stood Quartier Mu, the largest complex known on Crete from the age of the first palaces, more than 3,000 square metres (32,000 square feet) of it uncovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two large buildings stood there among five small craftsmen's houses — a seal-cutter's, a potter's, a smelter's — and a violent fire about 1700 BCE sealed the ruins with their contents inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those two are among the few buildings outside the palaces to yield archives in Cretan hieroglyphic, and some 150 soapstone seals from the workshop define a manner of engraving called the Malia style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second palace was built over the first, destroyed in Late Minoan I and never raised again, while the monumental enclosure at Chrysolakkos, perhaps the tomb of the palace's occupants, produced the famous gold bee pendant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Malia","questions":["More of the ground floor is given over to storage at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than at any other Cretan palace, and eight shallow kouloures sit in a walled enclosure beside its west court.","The central court at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries a pit dug at its middle, a limestone ball planted to the north-west and a polished stone with cup-marks set into the paving at its south."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"5a. Malia – The Palace,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-palais-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]","\"5b. Malia – The Mu Quarter,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-quartier-mu-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Malia_Palace%2C_Crete%2C_Sept_2019_a.jpg/1920px-Malia_Palace%2C_Crete%2C_Sept_2019_a.jpg","title":"Malia","desc":"Minoan Palace at Malia, Crete, Greece. Cayambe, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malia_Palace,_Crete,_Sept_2019_a.jpg","alt":"Malia Palace, Crete, Sept 2019 a"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Malia","at":[25.4647,35.2833]}},
{"id":"gr-013","num":"13","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Nearly 500 clay nodules stamped with seal impressions, most bearing two or three faces, came out of one room of a house on the shore at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the easternmost Minoan palace.","answer":"Zakros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1470 BCE, in Late Minoan IB</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zakros</b> is a Minoan palace and harbour town at the eastern end of Crete, on a delta where the Zakro river escapes from a gorge about 8 kilometres (5 miles) long, beside a natural port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That port was reckoned the last on the directest sea route from the Aegean to the shore of Cyrenaica in North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The palace is much the smallest of the four, covering about 2,800 square metres (30,000 square feet) where Knossos covers some 13,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its central court measures 29 by 12 metres (95 by 39 feet) and is aligned north-east to south-west, where the courts of the others run north to south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Major magazines are not a feature of it, only a group of lesser storerooms in the north-west corner, and the halls usually put on an upper floor sit here at ground level in the east half of the west wing, fronting straight onto the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Rooms west of the central court seem to have held the cult equipment, and the settlement belongs to the small class of Minoan towns with a palace at the middle ringed by large houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the spur above the shore stood a building its excavator called perhaps the most significant domestic structure then uncovered on any Aegean site, and took for the residence of a local chief or governor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of its rooms held nearly 500 well-preserved clay nodules stamped with seal impressions, most of them bearing two or three faces and plainly once attached to documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With them lay a small tablet of fine red clay inscribed in the linear script of Knossos, and two countersigns pressed into its rim with a signet showing wild goats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper came ashore as oxhide ingots, Zakros being one of the Cretan findspots for them in a trade reaching Cyprus and the Syrian coast, and the palace was destroyed about 1470 BCE in the Late Minoan IB horizon that took virtually every Minoan site of consequence, and never rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zakros","questions":["The smallest of the Minoan palaces, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a central court of 29 by 12 metres (95 by 39 feet) aligned north-east to south-west where the others run north to south.","Copper came ashore as oxhide ingots at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose port was reckoned the last on the directest sea route from the Aegean to the North African shore."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["D. G. Hogarth, \"Excavations at Zakro, Crete,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 121–49, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Palace_of_Zakros_ruins.jpg/1920px-Palace_of_Zakros_ruins.jpg","title":"Zakros","desc":"Palace of Zakros ruins, Eastern Crete. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palace_of_Zakros_ruins.jpg","alt":"The excavated palace at Zakros seen from above, its central court and the rooms around it"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Zakros","at":[26.2614,35.0981]}},
{"id":"gr-014","num":"14","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Some fifty well-preserved houses along cobbled streets, with a central court and a small palace among them, make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most extensively cleared Neopalatial town on Crete.","answer":"Gournia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Town</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Late Minoan IB, c. 1490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Gournia</b> is a Minoan town on the north coast of the Isthmus of Ierapetra in eastern Crete, and the most extensively cleared town of the Neopalatial period on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some fifty well-preserved houses stand along a system of cobbled streets, with a central court, a small palace and a cemetery among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A large open court lies on the south side of the hill's crest, resembling the central courts of the great palaces, and north of it stands a much-eroded building with palatial features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its principal façade faces west onto a small paved court and is built of cut blocks of poros limestone, with storage magazines within and setbacks along its front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Near the summit of the hill is a small rectangular shrine of a single room, approached from the west by a cobbled street.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The place belongs to the class of Minoan settlements built around one central main building, sometimes in the form of a palace, with a town of small buildings about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What came out of its houses is the furniture of ordinary life: household goods, tools and weapons, vessels, jewellery and objects of cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Harriet Boyd found the site on 19 May 1901 and cleared it in three seasons, employing about a hundred local men and ten women, and what she uncovered is of Middle Minoan III to Late Minoan I date, roughly 1750 to 1490 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the shore below, later work has identified a monumental shipshed, two sets of fortification walls with towers, a cobbled street running up to the town, a gate by the river, two dams at its mouth and stretches of Minoan agricultural terracing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town went down in the Late Minoan IB destructions, the horizon that took virtually every Minoan residential site of consequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Gournia","questions":["North of the open court at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood a building faced west in cut poros limestone, and near the summit a shrine of one room reached by a cobbled street.","A monumental shipshed, walls with towers and two dams at a river mouth belong to the shore below <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Harriet Boyd found in May 1901."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Excavation History,\" Gournia Excavation Project, University at Buffalo, https://www.gournia.org/excavation-history.php. [Open access]","\"Gournia Excavations,\" Gournia Excavation Project, University at Buffalo, https://www.gournia.org/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, \"The Harbor Complex of the Minoan Town at Gournia,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 116, no. 3 (2012): 521–41, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.116.3.0521. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Gournia_024.jpg/1920px-Gournia_024.jpg","title":"Gournia","desc":"Ausgrabungsstätte von Gournia (Γουρνιά) bei Pachia Ammos (Παχειά Άμμος), Gemeinde Ierapetra, Kreta, Griechenland. Olaf Tausch, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gournia_024.jpg","alt":"Gournia 024"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Gournia","at":[25.7889,35.1056]}},
{"id":"gr-015","num":"15","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The rise of the first Cretan palaces in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been read as redistribution, as élites herding people off their land, as farmers insuring each other, and as the wealth of trade.","answer":"Protopalatial period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Protopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1925 – 1720 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Protopalatial period</b> is the age of the first Cretan palaces, running from Middle Minoan IB to the end of Middle Minoan IIB, roughly 1900 to 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its absolute dates are soft at both ends, given as 1925 or 1900 for the opening and 1750 or 1720 for the close, and individual excavations work with figures of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first certain palaces are built at Knossos and Phaistos, and at Malia a genuine town grows up around the palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Potters take up the fast wheel and thin their vessel walls until the best Kamares ware earns the name eggshell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing appears with it: a pictographic or hieroglyphic script from Middle Minoan IA, and Linear A from about Middle Minoan IB to IIA, read from left to right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hilltops and caves show unambiguous evidence of cult for the first time, and close contacts with the eastern Peloponnese now extend on a similar scale to the central Aegean islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Away from the palaces the record is best at Quartier Mu in Malia, where two large buildings stood among five craftsmen's houses holding seal-cutters, potters and a smelter, and where hieroglyphic archives were kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Why palaces appeared at all is argued rather than settled: Renfrew read them as redistributive centres for a countryside specialising in oil and wine, and Gamble as the work of élites driving people into large settlements away from their fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Halstead saw instead an outgrowth of farmers trading surplus grain to insure one another against bad years, needing no central authority at all, while van Andel and Runnels put the weight on trade, shipping and metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period ends at Knossos and Phaistos in a major destruction horizon, probably an earthquake, and that horizon is what defines the close of the Old Palace age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Protopalatial period","questions":["The fast wheel, eggshell-thin Kamares ware and the first Cretan writing all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which runs from Middle Minoan IB to the end of Middle Minoan IIB.","A major destruction horizon at Knossos and Phaistos, probably an earthquake, is what defines the close of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the age of the first Cretan palaces."],"tags":["era","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 11: The First Palaces in the Aegean,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-11-narrative/. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]","\"5b. Malia – The Mu Quarter,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-quartier-mu-2/?lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/MM_II_relief-_and_applique-decorated_cup_from_Malia_-_Herakleion_AM_19814_-_01.jpg/1920px-MM_II_relief-_and_applique-decorated_cup_from_Malia_-_Herakleion_AM_19814_-_01.jpg","title":"Protopalatial period","desc":"A Minoan handleless clay cup decorated in relief with a cat and shells. in landscape with trees and other plants - period: middle minoan II (1900/1800-1700 BCE?) - findspot: Malia, quartier mu -… ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MM_II_relief-_and_applique-decorated_cup_from_Malia_-_Herakleion_AM_19814_-_01.jpg","alt":"MM II relief- and applique-decorated cup from Malia - Herakleion AM 19814 - 01"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-016","num":"16","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The villa, a large and sumptuously appointed building that is neither a palace nor an ordinary house, is new in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and has no clear Protopalatial counterpart.","answer":"Neopalatial period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1720 – 1490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neopalatial period</b> is the age of the second Cretan palaces, running from Middle Minoan IIIA to the end of Late Minoan IB, roughly 1750 to 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its dates are given variously, from 1750 or 1720 for the opening to 1490 or 1470 for the close, and a single excavation may work with figures of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> New palaces are raised over the ruins of the old at Knossos, Phaistos and Malia, each still built around a central court with a paved west court outside its main façade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A class of building appears that the age before it had not: the villa, large and sumptuously appointed, neither a palace nor an ordinary house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Villas stand alone in the countryside at Vathypetro and Sklavokampos, at the centre of villages, in clumps of their own, and among the mansions ringing the great palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pier-and-door partitions, lustral basins and the paired halls of a residential quarter recur through this architecture from one end of the island to the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same forms travel: pottery above all, whether imported or copied, but also Linear A, frescoes, bronze statuettes and those very partitions and basins turn up across the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thera, Melos and Kea are the most thoroughly Minoanised of the islands, Phylakopi on Melos having yielded both frescoes and Linear A inscriptions, while Kythera and Trianda on Rhodes look like colonies settled by Minoans and their descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether that reach was colonisation, a sea empire or simply trade and imitation is argued, and it need not imply any Minoan army at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The age ends in the Late Minoan IB destructions, which took virtually every Minoan residential site of consequence, palaces and villa complexes and towns and isolated villas alike, and left Knossos alone still working as a palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Neopalatial period","questions":["Pottery, Linear A, frescoes, bronze statuettes, pier-and-door partitions and lustral basins carry the forms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the Aegean, to Thera, Melos, Kea, Kythera and Rhodes.","The Late Minoan IB destructions close the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taking palaces, villa complexes, towns and isolated villas alike, and leaving Knossos alone still working as a palace."],"tags":["era","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/The_Parisian%2C_fresco%2C_Knossos%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Neopalatial period","desc":"The \"Parisian\", fresco, Knossos, Greece. Neopalatial period 1350 BCE. Herakleion Archaeological Museum. Height: 25 cm. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Parisian,_fresco,_Knossos,_Greece.jpg","alt":"The Parisian, fresco, Knossos, Greece"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-017","num":"17","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Rectangular cists lined with lead sheeting lie sunk in the floors among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Knossos, one range under another in the eighth magazine, and every one was found empty.","answer":"Palace storerooms and pithoi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"Minoan <b>palace storerooms and pithoi</b> go together: through both palace ages, from about 1900 to 1470 BCE, every Cretan palace gives much of its ground floor to magazines filled with rows of huge jars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Knossos they open west off a long north-south corridor in the west wing, kept almost entirely to the strip inside the west façade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That corridor, Evans's Long Gallery, runs 53 metres (174 feet) and is about 3.4 metres (11 feet) wide, with limestone door-jambs down its western side and thirteen magazines opening off it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The jars stood thickly: over twenty in the fifth magazine, twelve of them more or less whole, nine in a row along the south wall of the fourth, and twelve from the third with burnt grain still in some.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Phaistos runs its magazines either side of a broad corridor, Malia gives more ground-floor space to storage than any other palace and keeps rooms in the east wing and north-east quarter besides, and Zakros has no major magazines at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Sunk into the paved floors at Knossos are rectangular cists lined with lead sheeting, six in a row down the fourth magazine, six more in the fifth and five in the seventh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the eighth the arrangement is doubled, one range of cists beneath another, the lower reachable only by lifting the whole pavement above, the two together 1.3 metres (4 feet 3 inches) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans found them empty, and concluded that receptacles hidden so carefully, under a pavement solid enough to carry the jars standing on it, were meant for the safe-keeping of things of value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Outside the magazines stand kouloures, large half-sunken cylinders of rubble, several at Malia keeping a central base to carry a raised floor and so keep dry the grain they probably held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What all that capacity was for is argued rather than settled: Renfrew's reading of the palaces as redistributive centres for a countryside specialising in oil and wine makes the storerooms its machinery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Palace storerooms and pithoi","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Knossos open west off a Long Gallery 53 metres (174 feet) long, and over twenty jars were counted in the fifth magazine alone.","Every Cretan palace devotes a large part of its ground floor to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but Malia gives over more of it than any other and Zakros keeps almost no major magazines at all."],"tags":["object","building","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 19–25, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","\"5a. Malia – The Palace,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-palais-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 11: The First Palaces in the Aegean,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-11-narrative/. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg","title":"Palace storerooms and pithoi","desc":"Orientalizing pithos. Found in Arcades (?), Crete, ca. 675 BCE. Terracotta, stamped and incised decoration. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg","alt":"Pithos Louvre CA4523"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-018","num":"18","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Copper reached Crete under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as oxhide ingots weighing about 20 kilograms (45 pounds) apiece, cast from ore that was mined on Cyprus.","answer":"Minoan palace economy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In operation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan palace economy</b> is what the Cretan palaces did with goods: they gathered staples in bulk, kept a record of them, put craftsmen to work and brought in what the island could not supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every palace gives much of its ground floor to magazines, and beside them stand kouloures, large half-sunken cylinders of rubble that probably held grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What could not be grown or dug at home was bought: gold, silver and electrum, ivory from both elephant and hippopotamus, ostrich eggs, tin, and the spices that went into perfumed oils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Copper arrived as oxhide ingots averaging 0.60 by 0.45 metres (24 by 18 inches) and about 4 centimetres (1.6 inches) thick, each weighing some 20 kilograms (45 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They turn up at Palaikastro, Zakros, Mochlos, Knossos, Kommos and Ayia Triada, and the metal in those tested was mined on Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Craft was organised as well as supplied, and at Quartier Mu in Malia two large buildings stood among five workshop houses, one kept by a seal-cutter, one by a potter, one by a smelter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some 150 soapstone seals from that workshop are enough to define a manner of engraving of their own, and the buildings beside it held hieroglyphic archives, so writing and accounting were not the palaces' alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The accounting itself survives in clay: nearly 500 nodules stamped with seal impressions lay in one room of a house at Zakros, most bearing two or three faces and plainly once fixed to documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Knossos a file of inscribed tablets lay on the floor of the eighth magazine, the remains, it seems, of a gypsum coffer, and the figures added to them appear to refer to bronze single-edged axes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the whole adds up to is argued rather than settled, one reading making the palaces redistributive centres for a countryside specialising in oil and vine, another putting the weight on trade and on wool, cheese and hides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan palace economy","questions":["Nearly 500 clay nodules at Zakros and a file of inscribed tablets at Knossos are the surviving paperwork of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which counted what it stored.","Five workshop houses at Quartier Mu, one each for a seal-cutter, a potter and a smelter, show how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> organised craft as well as supplying it."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 24–25, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","D. G. Hogarth, \"Excavations at Zakro, Crete,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 132–34, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","\"5b. Malia – The Mu Quarter,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-quartier-mu-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 11: The First Palaces in the Aegean,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-11-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Palace_of_Malia_-_Pillar_Crypt.jpg/1920px-Palace_of_Malia_-_Pillar_Crypt.jpg","title":"Minoan palace economy","desc":"The Pillar Crypt in the Palace of Malia, Crete, Greece. Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palace_of_Malia_-_Pillar_Crypt.jpg","alt":"Palace of Malia - Pillar Crypt"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-019","num":"19","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The signary of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs to 135 signs, 45 of them known only as cut seal types, 43 only in scratched forms on clay, and 45 in both.","answer":"Cretan hieroglyphic script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Appears</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IA, c. 2050 – 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IIIB, c. 1700 – 1675 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cretan hieroglyphic script</b> is the earlier of the two writing systems of Bronze Age Crete, a pictorial one that appears in Middle Minoan IA, about 2050 to 2000 BCE, and runs on into Middle Minoan IIIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its life therefore covers the whole age of the first palaces and the opening of the second, ending about 1700 to 1675 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The signs are pictures and the script has an overall glyptic character, which is to say it was made to be cut into stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest examples are on Middle Minoan I seals with three or four sides, and later texts appear on clay bars, medallions, cones, noduli and sealings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surviving texts are few and very short, and they come from Knossos, Phaistos and Malia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> There is no uniformity in the direction of writing, and beyond the numerals, which run on a decimal system, the script is undeciphered and likely to remain so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arthur Evans came to it in 1889, when a four-sided cornelian bead-seal cut with figures on every face reached the Ashmolean Museum, wrongly said to be from Sparta, and he read it at once as a conventionalised system of picture-writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1909 he could set out a signary of 135 signs, of which 45 were known only as cut seal types, 43 only in the scratched forms on clay, and 45 in both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The elongated bead-seals, cut as three- or four-sided prisms, could take whole lines of inscription, and both they and the small signets fell out of use as the script ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was no palace monopoly either: the buildings of Quartier Mu at Malia, a quarter of workshops that included a seal-cutter's, are among the few outside a palace to have yielded hieroglyphic archives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cretan hieroglyphic script","questions":["Appearing in Middle Minoan IA and running on into Middle Minoan IIIB, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut above all on seal-stones of three or four sides.","About a third of Linear A's signs closely resemble those of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and beyond its decimal numerals that older system is undeciphered and likely to remain so."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 5, 235, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","\"5b. Malia – The Mu Quarter,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-quartier-mu-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/The_palace_of_Minos_-_a_comparative_account_of_the_successive_stages_of_the_early_Cretan_civilization_as_illustrated_by_the_discoveries_at_Knossos_%281921%29_%2814771199524%29.jpg","title":"Cretan hieroglyphic script","desc":"Identifier : cu31924081667796 (find matches) Title : The palace of Minos : a comparative account of the successive stages of the early Cretan civilization as illustrated by the discoveries at Knossos Year : 1921 (1920s) Authors : Evans, Arthur, Sir, 1851-1941 Evans, Joan, 1893-1977 Subjects :… Public domain (No restrictions), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_palace_of_Minos_-_a_comparative_account_of_the_successive_stages_of_the_early_Cretan_civilization_as_illustrated_by_the_discoveries_at_Knossos_(1921)_(14771199524).jpg","alt":"The palace of Minos - a comparative account of the successive stages of the early Cretan civilization as illustrated by the discoveries at Knossos (1921)"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-020","num":"20","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Of Linear B's syllabic signs, 45 have close equivalents in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and ten more have doubtful parallels, so many can be given a sound.","answer":"Linear A","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Appears</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IIA, perhaps MM IB, c. 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Late Minoan I, to c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Linear A</b> is the writing of the Cretan palaces, and the early texts called Proto-Linear from the first palace at Phaistos push its appearance back to Middle Minoan IIA or perhaps to Middle Minoan IB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It never appears on seals, has a general graphic character, reads uniformly from left to right, and makes extensive use of ligatures, which are signs combined into one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About a third of its signs closely resemble the older pictographic forms, though the two scripts now look virtually contemporary rather than one growing out of the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Texts occur most often on clay tablets, but also on six stone libation tables, spoon-shaped mortars, a doorjamb, a gold ring, a miniature gold axe, silver and gold pins, a bronze tablet and a fair number of bronze ingots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two cups from Knossos carry inscriptions written round the inside in cuttlefish ink with a reed pen, before the clay was fired, and a house at Zakros held a small tablet of fine red clay inscribed in the same script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It turns up at some twenty sites, and only three places outside Crete have produced true texts: Ayia Irini on Kea, Phylakopi on Melos and Akrotiri on Thera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The latest inscriptions are no later than Late Minoan I, which closes about 1470 BCE, so the script goes down with the palaces it served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The name is Arthur Evans's: in 1909 he set out the linear writing of Crete as two classes, A and B, and the labels have stuck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of Linear B's syllabic signs, 45 have close equivalents in the older script and ten more have doubtful parallels, so a great many of its signs can be given a sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is as far as it goes, because Linear B was derived from Linear A in order to write a different, non-Minoan language, which is why deciphering the younger script in 1952 did not unlock the elder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Linear A","questions":["Six stone libation tables, a doorjamb, a gold ring, silver and gold pins and cuttlefish ink inside a cup all carry <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is found mostly on clay tablets.","Crete's linear writing falls into two classes, and it is the older of them, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, that appears at some twenty sites across the island and is still unread."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 28–29, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","D. G. Hogarth, \"Excavations at Zakro, Crete,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 133, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Linear_A_cup.png","title":"Linear A","desc":"Linear A. Ink-witten inscriptions round the inner surface of cup (Third Middle Minoan). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_A_cup.png","alt":"Linear A cup"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-021","num":"21","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Every sign was pressed into the wet clay of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a punch, the same stamp each time, so just 45 stamps produced all 241 characters on it.","answer":"Phaistos Disc","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IIIB, c. 1700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Phaistos Disc</b> is a fired clay disc stamped on both faces with pictographic signs, from the palace at Phaistos in southern Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not perfectly round, its diameter running from 158 to 165 millimetres (6.2 to 6.5 inches) and its thickness from 16 to 21 millimetres (0.6 to 0.8 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every character was stamped in relief with a punch while the clay was still wet, each variety of sign always by the same stamp, so that 45 stamps were used in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That makes it in effect a printed inscription, the punches taken for hard wood or ivory by one of its first students and for metal cast in matrices of engraved steatite by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Face A carries 31 sign-groups and 123 signs, face B 30 groups and 118, making 241 characters in 61 groups, the inscription spiralling between rim and centre on each face and closed on each side by a line of five punctuations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The signs stand in groups of two to seven divided by incised lines, and the spiral is read from the outside inward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It lay in a small room at the north-eastern edge of the site, in dark earth mixed with ash, charcoal and potsherds, having fallen from a floor above with a fragment of a clay tablet in the Minoan linear script beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The context is Middle Minoan IIIB, so the disc belongs to the years around 1700 BCE, and one of the excavators judged its clay not to be Cretan at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phaistos itself was a palace of two lives, the first raised in Middle Minoan IB and thrown down by an earthquake at the end of MM IIB, the second rebuilt in MM IIIA, with a settlement of some 50 hectares (124 acres) around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its 45 signs suggest a syllabary rather than an alphabet, it has no close parallel on Crete and is probably an import, and it is undeciphered, an approximation to printing that one scholar called 'immense in potentiality but null in effect, a freak'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phaistos Disc","questions":["The inscription on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spirals between rim and centre on each face, 61 sign-groups in all, each face closed off by a line of five punctuations.","With 45 different signs and no close parallel on Crete, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is probably a syllabary, probably an import, and still undeciphered."],"tags":["object","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Luigi Pernier, \"Il disco di Phaestos con caratteri pittografici,\" <i>Ausonia</i> 3 (1908): 255ff, https://archive.org/details/ausonia03sociuoft. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 22–24, 273–74, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"Festòs,\" Centro di Archeologia Cretese, Università degli Studi di Catania, https://www.cac.unict.it/festos. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Phaistos_Disc_%E2%80%94_Side_A.jpg/1920px-Phaistos_Disc_%E2%80%94_Side_A.jpg","title":"Phaistos Disc","desc":"Phaistos Disc — Side A. Gleb Simonov. 2024-01-30. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phaistos_Disc_%E2%80%94_Side_A.jpg","alt":"Phaistos Disc — Side A"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-022","num":"22","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Men are painted red and women white in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a convention perhaps taken from Egypt, and every known figured example is of Neopalatial date.","answer":"Minoan frescoes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Neopalatial, c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan frescoes</b> are the wall paintings of Bronze Age Crete, and every known figured example belongs to the Neopalatial age, roughly 1750 to 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of the surviving corpus comes from Knossos and Ayia Triada, while Malia and Phaistos have produced very few — though painted plaster was not kept for grand rooms alone, and the storeroom walls at Knossos carried a dado of red and blue or grey bands rising to about the height of the jars standing against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The subjects run to bull-leaping, boxing and wrestling, processions of figures carrying vessels, heraldic griffins and lions, and scenes of the natural world with cats stalking birds, monkeys and flowers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Men are painted red and women white, a convention that may have been taken over from Egyptian painting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where a painter wanted relief he made it physically, modelling the figure out in the plaster itself, though only on a large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A miniature style exists beside the large one, small-figured and crowded, in the Grandstand and Sacred Grove paintings from Knossos and the Boxers from Tylissos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans found the Cup-bearer in 1900 lying face upwards on a passage floor, two large pieces making up most of a life-sized youth in a close-fitting embroidered loin-cloth and an ornamental belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His head and face survived, with a silver necklace and earrings, and on his left wrist an agate lentoid seal whose banding the painter had taken care to show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He carries a pointed cup of silver with gold mountings, of the same funnel-shaped kind held by the Keftiu chieftains in Egyptian tomb paintings of the time of Thutmose III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Frescoes travelled with the rest of Minoan practice, turning up at Phylakopi on Melos and Akrotiri on Thera, and much of what a visitor sees at Knossos today is Evans's restoration, which the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan frescoes","questions":["The Cup-bearer, a life-sized youth wearing an agate seal on his wrist and carrying a silver cup, is one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which come mostly from Knossos and Ayia Triada.","Where a painter of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wanted relief he modelled it out in the plaster itself, and most of the surviving corpus comes from Knossos and Ayia Triada."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 15–16, 20, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Akrotiri_Antelopes_fresco.jpg/1920px-Akrotiri_Antelopes_fresco.jpg","title":"Minoan frescoes","desc":"Akrotiri Antelopes fresco. Gleb Simonov. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akrotiri_Antelopes_fresco.jpg","alt":"Akrotiri Antelopes fresco"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-023","num":"23","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A performer in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grips a charging bull by the horns and lets them pass beneath her armpits, while another turns a somersault over its back.","answer":"Bull-leaping fresco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Neopalatial, c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>bull-leaping fresco</b> is the Minoan wall painting from Knossos showing acrobats vaulting a charging bull, the most familiar image of a sport its art returns to again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its fragments lay in a basement chamber built against the eastern terrace wall, having fallen from an upper room, and were found about 1.5 metres (5 feet) above the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parts of two galloping bulls were put back together from the broken stucco, each about a quarter of natural size and drawn to a much larger scale than the people beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over the back of one bull a figure turns a somersault; another stands between the horns of a charging bull, the horns passing under her armpits as she grasps them higher up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every performer wears the same loin-cloth, narrow metallic girdle, striped socks and slippers, and what separates them is skin colour, the hues of the costume, blue and red diadems and rather curlier hair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The convention across Minoan painting is red skin for men and white for women, perhaps taken over from Egypt, so the white-skinned leapers here are taken for women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Maidens as well as youths were therefore trained for the arena, and the later Greek story of Athenian youths and maidens sent as tribute to the Minotaur at Knossos may keep a memory of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The subject was no one-off: at the south-west portico three successive layers of plaster each carried a large painting whose central figure was a life-sized bull, spotted and in violent action, probably grappled with by a man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bull-leaping and bull-catching were painted flat and modelled in stucco relief at several sizes, and the boxer rhyton from Ayia Triada carries a miniature version in which the jumper is gored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every figured Minoan fresco known is Neopalatial, of the years between about 1750 and 1470 BCE, and much of what stands at Knossos today is restoration the Ashmolean Museum calls extensive and controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Bull-leaping fresco","questions":["Both sexes performed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the Athenian tribute of youths and maidens to the Minotaur at Knossos may remember the Cretan sport.","Every performer in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wears the same loin-cloth, girdle, striped socks and slippers, and it is skin colour, hair and diadems that tell the women from the men."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 94–96, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 13, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Knossos_bull_leaping_fresco.jpg/1920px-Knossos_bull_leaping_fresco.jpg","title":"Bull-leaping fresco","desc":"Knossos bull leaping fresco. Gleb Simonov. 2024-01-30. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_bull_leaping_fresco.jpg","alt":"Knossos bull leaping fresco"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-024","num":"24","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"One of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands only 20 centimetres (8 inches) to the neck, having lost her head altogether, her left arm wanting and her right holding out a small snake, tail upwards.","answer":"Snake Goddess figurines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM III, c. 1700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Snake Goddess figurines</b> are faience figures from the Temple Repositories at Knossos, stone cists sunk under a floor of the sanctuary west of the central court, filled with Middle Minoan III material of around 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two shallow empty cists lay above two larger and deeper ones, whose upper fill held Middle Minoan III pottery and whose lowest part held broken cult equipment, greasy earth with charred plant remains, and stag horns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three largely preserved figures came out of them together with fragments of others, the tallest usually counted a goddess and the smaller ones votaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The goddess as reconstituted stands 34.2 centimetres (13.5 inches) high in a purplish-brown tiara, an embroidered jacket with a laced bodice and a skirt with a double apron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her eyes and raised eyebrows are black and her ears abnormally large, and the ground colour is a milky white with details picked out in purple and black.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three snakes with greenish bodies spotted purple-brown coil about her: one held out in her right hand, two interlaced round her hips, one running up the jacket and over an ear to the tiara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The votary is smaller, 20 centimetres (8 inches) to the neck, and had lost her head altogether; her left arm is wanting and her right holds out a small snake, tail upwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both figures are part modern: the goddess's left forearm and the head of the snake rising from her tiara are restored, as are her apron and most of her skirt, made good from a better-preserved one of the same pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The repositories also held miniature votive robes in faience, plaques of a cow and a wild goat nursing their young, shells, crystal and ivory inlays, stone tables of offering, a marble cross, gold foil and a clay tablet in Linear A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the figures represent is unsettled: the snake goddess is usually taken for a household divinity and does not appear on seals, and Minoan figures plausibly divine rather than mortal are overwhelmingly female.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Snake Goddess figurines","questions":["Stone cists sunk under a floor at Knossos, filled with Middle Minoan III material of about 1700 BCE, held the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> along with miniature votive robes in faience.","Three snakes with greenish bodies spotted purple-brown coil about the larger of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one held out in her right hand and two interlaced round her hips."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report for the Year 1903,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 74–78, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 29, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Minoan_-_Snake_Goddess_-_Walters_23196.jpg","title":"Snake Goddess figurines","desc":"This statue of a snake goddess has been carved with a drill and flat chisel. The upper part is dowelled into the lower. The goddess wears a tall headdress with a snake wound around it. Her hands are pierced to hold snakes. The headdress is broken off and the upper part has been repaired. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_-_Snake_Goddess_-_Walters_23196.jpg","alt":"Minoan - Snake Goddess"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-025","num":"25","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Named after a cave above a village in the chain of Mount Ida, visible from the palace at Phaistos, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the fine polychrome pottery of the first Cretan palaces.","answer":"Kamares ware","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IB – IIB, c. 2000 – 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Kamares ware</b> is the fine polychrome pottery of the first Cretan palaces, and it takes its name from the cave above the village of Kamares where it was first found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That cave lies in the chain of Mount Ida, and its entrance can be made out with the naked eye from the palace at Phaistos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ware belongs to Middle Minoan IB through IIB, the age of the Old Palaces, from about 2000 to 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It arrives with the fast potter's wheel and with vessel walls thinned until the finest pieces earn the name eggshell ware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its decoration is light on dark, abundant white with reds, oranges and yellows on a black ground, in complex abstract patterns and now and then stylised plants, animals or human beings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Mosso thought the potters worked with bronze vessels in front of them, reproducing in clay the bosses and folds of the metal and even the way the handles were attached, and giving the surface a metallic sheen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some pieces carry their ornament in relief, one kind with clay applied as bunches of grapes, leaves or ears of corn, and a jug from Ayia Triada that looks like an imitation of repoussé work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sometimes the black ground gives way to the natural brick red of the clay, with the design laid on in white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By far the richest assortment comes from the palace at Phaistos, and the Mesara of southern Crete is arguably where the art reached its technical and aesthetic height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ranked with seals and ceremonial weaponry among the luxury goods the palaces had made, and at Knossos one tall Kamares jar survived because a later floor cut across its top, a smaller spouted vase, a pedestalled cup and obsidian still inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Kamares ware","questions":["The fast wheel and walls thinned to what is called eggshell ware belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose decoration is white, red, orange and yellow on a black ground.","The makers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seem to have had bronze vessels before them, copying in clay the bosses and folds of metal and even the way the handles were attached."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 44, 54, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 21, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 11: The First Palaces in the Aegean,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-11-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Decorated_cups_Phaistos_archmus_Heraklion.jpg/1920px-Decorated_cups_Phaistos_archmus_Heraklion.jpg","title":"Kamares ware","desc":"Various cups decorated with white and polychrome Kamares Ware motifs. Phaistos, 1800 - 1700 BCE. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Decorated_cups_Phaistos_archmus_Heraklion.jpg","alt":"Decorated cups Phaistos archmus Heraklion"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-026","num":"26","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Rhyta, bridge-spouted jugs and stirrup jars carry <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and because so many of them are fillers it has been suggested the vases were made for religious use.","answer":"Marine Style pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM IB, c. 1600 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM IB to c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Marine Style pottery</b> is the Cretan ware of Late Minoan IB, decorated with densely packed sea creatures drawn as though from life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The motifs are the murex or whorl-shell, the nautilus or argonaut, the octopus, the dolphin, seaweed, the sea anemone and the star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shapes are specialised ones: rhyta of conical and piriform kinds, bridge-spouted jugs and stirrup jars, with very few cups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since rhyta are fillers, and so common in this style, it has been suggested that the vases were made for religious use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The style may derive from a branch of fresco painting particularly at home in the Aegean islands, especially Thera, and may even have been developed on Crete by Cycladic artists who fled the eruption of the Santorini volcano.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Mosso watched Pernier dig one such vessel at Phaistos in 1906 and set down what it looked like: corals interlacing their branches in fantastic shapes, floating seaweeds, and the argonaut stretching out arms covered with suckers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The painter had sketched it freehand in sepia, repeating the same motive with slight variations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The vase is double, a cone descending from the neck nearly to the bottom and a small opening below, so that wine poured in stayed put until the vessel was turned over at the altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Evans met the same taste at Knossos, where fragments of good vases carrying naturalistic designs of plants and marine objects had fallen from an upper chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Late Minoan IB is dated on one reckoning to about 1600 to 1500 BCE and on another to a close about 1470, and it is the horizon in which nearly every Minoan site of consequence was destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Marine Style pottery","questions":["The murex, the nautilus, the octopus, the dolphin, seaweed, sea anemone and star are the creatures packed across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Cretan ware of Late Minoan IB.","One reading traces <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to island fresco painting and to Cycladic artists who came to Crete after the Santorini eruption, though the case is not settled."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 261–63, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 94, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/AMI_-_Oktopusvase.jpg","title":"Marine Style pottery","desc":"Archaeological Museum in Herakleion. Minoan clay bottle showing an Octopus (1500 BCE). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMI_-_Oktopusvase.jpg","alt":"AMI - Oktopusvase"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-027","num":"27","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Laid over a container or a door, a clay lump stamped by one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not be got past without destroying both the lump and the impression on it.","answer":"Minoan sealstones","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Prism seals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lentoids, rings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan sealstones</b> are small portable objects, mostly of stone but also of bone, ivory, metal or paste, engraved with a design and made to be worn on the body, as ornament, amulet or badge of rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first true Aegean seals belong to the second phase of the Early Bronze Age, and more than 10,000 seals and impressions survive, the most important source for Aegean imagery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A seal secured, first of all: laid over a container or a door, the clay lump it was pressed into could not be got past without destroying the mark on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It marked, the lump hanging from an object by a string or the stamp going straight onto a pot, to say what was inside or who owned it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> And it authorised, stamped on a free-standing lump perhaps used as a token or a receipt; one lump from Chania carries the marks of twelve different seals and, on its back, the impression of a folded leather sheet tied with string.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest Cretan seals are almost exclusively of soft stone or ivory, though the pear-shaped signet was now and then cut from rock crystal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the age of the first palaces, from about 1900 BCE, the three-sided prism was a characteristic shape, and a workshop in Quartier Mu at Malia was given over to seal-cutters who specialised in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their flat faces suited whole lines of Cretan hieroglyphic writing, and that use was given up once the linear script came in, since Linear A never appears on seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Engravers turned instead to the bossed oval field of the lentoid bead-seal and the bezel of the signet ring, where bold pictures replaced calligraphy, a change Evans set at about 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A basement room at Knossos held a mass of broken sealings stamped mostly with animals, the clay nodules prepared to make more, and a matrix taken from a real seal that could stamp counterfeits — used, Evans thought, to forge a royal signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan sealstones","questions":["Cut as three-sided prisms in the age of the first palaces and later as lentoids and the bezels of signet rings, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the most important source there is for Aegean imagery.","A clay lump from Chania carries the marks of twelve different <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and, on its back, the impression of a folded leather sheet tied with string."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Seals &amp; sealings — About,\" Corpus der minoischen und mykenischen Siegel, Institute of Classical Archaeology, Heidelberg University, https://www.uni-heidelberg.de/fakultaeten/philosophie/zaw/cms/seals/sealsAbout.html. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 37–38, 120–21, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 17–19, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Agate_lentoid_seal_MET_DP141656_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Agate_lentoid_seal_MET_DP141656_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Minoan sealstones","desc":"Minoan; Seal, lentoid; Gems. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agate_lentoid_seal_MET_DP141656_(cropped).jpg","alt":"Agate lentoid seal"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-028","num":"28","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Deep layers of ash carrying no animal bones, and great quantities of clay figurines, are what mark out <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Cretan cult places on prominent local hills.","answer":"Peak sanctuaries","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from MM I, c. 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buildings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from MM III, c. 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Decline</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after LM I, c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Peak sanctuaries</b> are Cretan cult places at or just below the tops of prominent local hills, not necessarily true mountain peaks, marked by deep layers of ash and great quantities of clay figurines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ash holds no animal bones, so it is read as bonfire debris rather than blood sacrifice; metal objects are exceptional and pottery, apart from miniature vases, is rare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of some fifty hilltops claimed as peak sanctuaries at least 25 are generally accepted, and at none does the evidence for cult go back before Middle Minoan I, about the time the first palaces were built around 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They and the cult caves are virtually the only places besides the palaces to yield the finest Kamares pottery, tables of offerings and Linear A inscriptions off clay tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of the human figurines are single arms, legs and heads, modelled separately and pierced for suspension, probably the parts of whole dolls once held together by string.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Petsofa above Palaikastro is one of the two excavated and published in full, and its key layer is black earth 17 to 20 centimetres (7 to 8 inches) thick, crowded with figurines, many whole and still holding their paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Myres read it as a bonfire into which figurines were thrown, raked over from time to time to clear the larger ones, then levelled for fresh fuel and fresh offerings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its male figures wear a loin-cloth, a white wrapper and a knotted girdle, with close-fitting white boots, and several carry a broad triangular dagger worn nearly horizontally outside the belt, as long as the wearer's forearm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The votive arms run 8 to 12 centimetres (3 to 5 inches) from shoulder to fingertip, painted white, probably for women, or brown-red and black for men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On Juktas, at 811 metres (2,660 feet) and taken to have served Knossos and Archanes, Evans found the summit ringed by a wall of great rough blocks and no buildings inside it; in Middle Minoan III three terraces went up, with a stepped altar over cracks in the bedrock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Peak sanctuaries","questions":["Single arms, legs and heads, modelled separately and pierced for suspension, turn up in numbers at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and were probably parts of whole dolls held together by string.","Only Petsofa above Palaikastro and Juktas above Knossos, of the 25 or so generally accepted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, have been excavated and published in full."],"tags":["place","practice","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","John L. Myres, \"Excavations at Palaikastro. II. §13. The Sanctuary-Site of Petsofà,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 356–58, 374–75, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 23–24, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Petsophas_Gipfelheiligtum_17.jpg/1920px-Petsophas_Gipfelheiligtum_17.jpg","title":"Peak sanctuaries","desc":"Reste des minoischen Gipfelheiligtums Petsophas auf dem gleichnamigen Berg Petsofas bei Palekastro, Gemeinde Sitia, Kreta, Griechenland. Olaf Tausch, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petsophas_Gipfelheiligtum_17.jpg","alt":"Petsophas Gipfelheiligtum 17"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-029","num":"29","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"First lived in during the Neolithic and later used as cemeteries, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only became places of worship early in the Middle Minoan period, at about the time the first palaces went up.","answer":"Cretan cult caves","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM I, c. 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM III, to c. 1075 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cretan cult caves</b> are caves on Crete used for worship in the Bronze Age, having served first as dwellings in the Neolithic and then, from the end of that period, widely as cemeteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cult use begins early in the Middle Minoan period, around 1900 BCE, when the first palaces were being built, and the two may well be connected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The evidence is pottery, animal figurines and now and then bronze, and several caves hold quantities of animal bones, mostly deer, ox and goat, from some form of sacrifice, where the hilltop sanctuaries' ash holds none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are fewer than the hilltops — Amnisos, the Idaean cave, Juktas, Kamares, Psychro, Skoteino and Stavromyti — and at none of them does cult go back before Middle Minoan I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The richest votive deposits come from Kamares high on Mount Ida, the Dictaean or Psychro cave above the Lasithi plain, the Idaean cave, and Arkalochori, which gave up an astonishing array of bronze swords, daggers and double axes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Psychro cave is double, water from the east having cut an abyss that falls sheer and then slopes some 60 metres (200 feet) to an icy pool out of which rises a forest of stalactites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The innermost recess of the upper grot was walled off and roughly paved with squared blocks, an enclosure Hogarth took for a temenos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beneath it lay ashes 0.7 metres (2 feet 3 inches) thick, then black mould yielding bronze weapons, then more ashes, then black earth full of painted pottery, to a total depth of 2.1 metres (7 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the lower grot bronze blades, fibulae and votive double axes stood edgeways in the crevices of the stalactite pillars, some chinks holding ten apiece and many so closed over by lime they could not be got out without breaking the stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nearly 500 bronze pieces came out of the cave, along with 550 unbroken little wheel-made cups, all plainly new when set down, the same pale cups found in votive deposits in the caves of Dikte and Ida.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cretan cult caves","questions":["Bronze blades, fibulae and votive double axes stood edgeways in the stalactite pillars of Psychro, one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some chinks holding as many as ten apiece.","Deer, ox and goat bones in quantity, left from some form of sacrifice, are found in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and not on the hilltop sanctuaries, whose ash holds no bones at all."],"tags":["place","practice","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","David G. Hogarth, \"The Dictaean Cave,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 96, 99–101, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 24, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Cave_Dikti12%28js%29.jpg/1920px-Cave_Dikti12%28js%29.jpg","title":"Cretan cult caves","desc":"Cave of Dikti (Psychro Cave), Crete. Jerzy Strzelecki, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cave_Dikti12(js).jpg","alt":"Cave Dikti12(js)"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-030","num":"30","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A base or impost ending at each side in two horn-like projections, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> served as stands for double axes and libation jugs, or as crowning members on altars and roofs.","answer":"Horns of consecration","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">On frescoes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Neopalatial, c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Knossos pair</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Horns of consecration</b> are a piece of Minoan ritual furniture: a base or impost ending at each side in a horn-like projection, at times like real ox horns, more often a conventional imitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They survive as objects of stone or terracotta, often stuccoed, and are painted or carved on murals, altars, vases, seals and larnakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They serve either as stands for a narrow range of cult implements, double axes, libation jugs and branches, or as crowning members on altars and roofs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is Evans's, and what they originally meant is uncertain: stylised bulls' horns, the crescent moon, the rising sun, or an odd form of pot support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Evans met them everywhere in Minoan imagery: at the foot of sacred trees and pillars, at the foot of the double axe, above an archway, on the roof of a shrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On a signet impression from Knossos they stand before the columns of a shrine and again on its entablature, which told Evans that those columns were a made pillar form of the cult object, against the natural peak the goddess stands on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Four pairs are visible above the wall of another sanctuary on a fresco fragment from the palace, and every known figured Minoan fresco is Neopalatial, between about 1750 and 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Actual examples survive, among them a painted terracotta pair from the votive cave at Patso, with a conical stem and two curved objects rising between the horns, broken away above and of lost meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A limestone pair from a house at Knossos, 20 centimetres (8 inches) wide and 19 (7.5 inches) high, stood near the east wall of a small room, on a platform of potsherds rounded as though in running water, during the building's last partial occupation, after about 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That pair had no socket between the horns, where those on the raised base of the Shrine of the Double Axes did: their sockets held the handles of the axes that stood in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Horns of consecration","questions":["The sockets between the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the raised base of the Shrine of the Double Axes at Knossos held the handles of the axes that stood in them.","Stylised bulls' horns, the crescent moon and the rising sun have all been proposed as the origin of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and so has an odd form of pot support."],"tags":["object","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 37–39, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report for the Year 1903,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 12, 91, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 30, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Horns_of_concesration%2C_Clay_model%2C_1500-1450_BC%2C_AMH%2C_145010_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Horns_of_concesration%2C_Clay_model%2C_1500-1450_BC%2C_AMH%2C_145010_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Horns of consecration","desc":"Clay model of the horns of concesration,1500-1450 BCE. Archaeological Museum of Heraklion. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horns_of_concesration,_Clay_model,_1500-1450_BC,_AMH,_145010_(cropped).jpg","alt":"Horns of concesration, Clay model, 1500-1450 BCE, AMH"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-031","num":"31","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch reports that the Carian word for the double axe was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a word probably connected with labyrinthos, the name given to Minos's palace at Knossos.","answer":"Labrys","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid Early Minoan, before 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM III, to c. 1075 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>labrys</b> is the double axe, the commonest of all Minoan religious symbols, made from the middle of the Early Minoan period onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some large bronze examples were working tools, but miniatures in gold, silver, lead, steatite and terracotta, and bronze ones too fragile to swing, can only be symbolic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In pictures it is usually set in a stone base or between horns of consecration, and what it stood for is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Near East such axes are wielded by male divinities and read as thunderbolts, but in Crete no male divinity holds one, which favours the view that it owes its frequency to being the instrument of sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is not Minoan: Plutarch, in the 2nd century CE, reports that the Carian word for a double axe was labrys, probably connected with labyrinthos, the palace's later name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Knossos the sign is cut into the gypsum of two pillar rooms, three sides of the eastern pillar and all four of the western, and on the principal jambs besides, which led Evans to call the palace the House of the Double Axe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He tied the name to the Carian Zeus Labraundos, and argued that the axe was itself an object of worship, a material shape the divinity's essence could enter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bronze votive axes were laid up in the cave sanctuaries of Ida and Dikte, 18 in the lower grot at Psychro, almost every one still standing in a stalactite niche.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All were too small or thin to be of use; two kept their shafts, and the largest, 280 millimetres (11 inches) long, carries fine oblique lines across the blades like those Evans had seen on axes engraved on gems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The clearest arrangement to survive is the Shrine of the Double Axes at Knossos, abandoned with its furniture in place, where two stuccoed clay horns of consecration stand on a bench, a round socket between each pair to take an axe and a small steatite one resting against the left-hand pair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Labrys","questions":["Eighteen bronze examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came out of the lower grot at Psychro, nearly all still standing in the stalactite niches, and every one too thin to have been used.","Incised on three sides of one pillar at Knossos and on all four of another, the sign of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave that building the name House of the Double Axe."],"tags":["object","symbol","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 32–34, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","David G. Hogarth, \"The Dictaean Cave,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 108–9, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 9, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Minoan_Labrys1.jpg","title":"Labrys","desc":"Votive double ax Early Aegean, MinoanBronze Age, Late Minoan IA Period, about 1550–1500 BCE. Gold double axe composed of thin sheet gold mounted on a hollow gold shaft and outlined by bands of five incised lines. On left blade, inscription of four signs in undeciphered Linear A;. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_Labrys1.jpg","alt":"Minoan Labrys1"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-032","num":"32","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Because Linear A is undeciphered there is effectively no contemporary text about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is known instead from the places of cult, the pictures of it and its furniture.","answer":"Minoan religion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Minoan Crete</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 – 1075 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult places</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from MM I, c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan religion</b> is known almost entirely from things rather than words, Linear A being undeciphered and no contemporary text about it surviving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usable evidence falls into three classes: the places where cult happened, pictures of cult on seals, rings, murals, larnakes and pottery, and the furniture of cult itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The places are cult caves, hilltop sanctuaries and shrines in towns, palaces and houses, the last sorted into bench sanctuaries, lustral basins and pillar crypts, of which only the bench goes back to the Prepalatial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Working from the pictures, Evans read the sacred trees and pillars that recur in them as aniconic forms of the divinity, worshipped as a material shape the god could enter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The figures plausibly divine rather than mortal are overwhelmingly female, and several distinct goddesses can be told apart by the attributes they carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A Snake Goddess, known from the faience figures of the Temple Repositories at Knossos, is usually taken for a household divinity and never appears on seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Mistress of Animals stands on her rocky peak between two lions on a signet impression from Knossos, a shrine on one side and a saluting man on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Male divinities are rare and often drawn smaller than the women beside them: one descends through the air with a spear, another stands beardless between horns of consecration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Animal sacrifice is not in doubt, and two finds suggest it reached people: a sanctuary at Anemospilia burned at the end of Middle Minoan II, about 1750 BCE, held an 18-year-old trussed on a platform with a bronze dagger 0.4 metres (16 inches) long among his bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His bones were white on the upper side and black on the lower, which suggested to the anthropologist who saw them that he had bled to death; and in the North House at Knossos 327 children's bones carried fine knife marks like the marks butchery leaves on animal bone, though later analysis put them at four children at most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan religion","questions":["Overwhelmingly female divinities, several of them distinguishable by their attributes, are what the art of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows, with male gods rare and often drawn smaller than the women beside them.","A trussed 18-year-old with a bronze dagger among his bones, at a sanctuary that burned at Anemospilia, is the sharpest evidence that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may have included human sacrifice."],"tags":["practice","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 29–30, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report for the Year 1903,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 74–78, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 9–10, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Epiphany_cycle_%E2%80%94_Sacred_Conversation.jpg","title":"Minoan religion","desc":"Epiphany cycle — Sacred Conversation. Gsimonov. 2024-01-30 13:23:05. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epiphany_cycle_%E2%80%94_Sacred_Conversation.jpg","alt":"Epiphany cycle — Sacred Conversation"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-033","num":"33","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"On the front of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> women pour liquid into a krater between two double axes with birds perched on them, to the music of a lyre.","answer":"Ayia Triada sarcophagus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM IIIA, c. 1400 – 1350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ayia Triada sarcophagus</b> is a limestone burial chest painted on lime plaster, found in a looted tomb of the early 14th century BCE in southern Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Burial in Late Minoan III Crete usually meant a chamber tomb with the body on the floor or in a terracotta larnax or pithos, so a stone chest is rare and its painted scenes are unique in Aegean funerary art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the front, women pour liquid into a krater standing between two double axes with birds perched on them, to the music of a lyre, the mixing read as wine and water for a goddess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans had already argued that a double axe could be the divinity's own material shape, which lets the birds settling on them read as the deity arriving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beside it a motionless figure with concealed arms stands before a building, taken for the spirit of the dead man watching his own obsequies, with a boat and cattle brought to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the back a bull lies strapped to a table with a conical rhyton fixed in the ground beneath it to take the blood, a flute player behind and a procession of women approaching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The women hold their palms downward and the blood drains into the earth, which marks the sacrifice as one to a power of the underworld.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each end carries a chariot, one drawn by two agrimia, the Cretan wild goats, the other by winged griffins, the pairs of women riding in them read as divine escorts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the whole is one theme is disputed: some separate the front as a cult of the dead from the back as the cult of a god, others deny the funerary reading and make the standing figure a deity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nilsson resolved it by supposing the dead man deified, a Mycenaean overlord rather than a Minoan, and Burke reads the chest as a Mycenaean elite claiming power by dressing itself in the Minoan past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ayia Triada sarcophagus","questions":["A bull strapped to a table with a rhyton below to take its blood fills the back of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, marking the sacrifice as one to a power of the earth.","Chariots drawn by agrimia at one end and by winged griffins at the other carry pairs of women across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, read as divine escorts for the dead man's journey."],"tags":["object","art","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 9, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]","Brendan Burke, \"Materialization of Mycenaean Ideology and the Ayia Triada Sarcophagus,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 109, no. 3 (2005): 403–22, https://ajaonline.org/article/107/. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Sarcophagus_archmus_Heraklion.jpg/1920px-Sarcophagus_archmus_Heraklion.jpg","title":"Ayia Triada sarcophagus","desc":"One of the lateral faces of the \"Agia Triada\" sarcophagus, Crete, Greece. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarcophagus_archmus_Heraklion.jpg","alt":"Sarcophagus archmus Heraklion"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-034","num":"34","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Set on four feet with drain-holes in the bottom, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> repeats in painted clay the framing and sunk panels of an ordinary wooden chest.","answer":"Larnax","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM III, c. 1470 – 1075 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>larnax</b> is a terracotta chest used to hold the dead in Bronze Age Crete, the commonest container of a Late Minoan burial, and most of the tombs holding them are of the Post-Palatial period after about 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Burial then usually meant a chamber tomb, with the body laid on the floor or set inside a larnax or a pithos, and the two practices were at times followed side by side in one tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Evans held that these chests were not models of houses but translations into painted clay of the wooden chests of household furniture, and that some were made for burial from the start, since they have drain-holes in the bottom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their narrow capacity suggested to some that they were ossuaries filled once the flesh had gone, but a larnax from Zapher Papoura and another from Artsa held skeletons lying on their backs with the knees drawn up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The painted stone chest from Ayia Triada is a rare version in stone of this otherwise ordinary object.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The painted larnax Bosanquet published from Palaikastro is of well-baked dark red clay under a creamy slip, 1.22 metres (4 feet) long and about a metre (3 feet) high with its gabled lid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands on four feet, carries four vertical handles a side and one at each end, and has two holes bored near the edge of the lid so that lid and body could be laced together for carrying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its body faithfully repeats the framing and six sunk panels of a wooden chest rather than the gabled roof of a house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One panel carries a slender column with a moulded capital supporting a pair of sacred horns and a double axe, springing from between two flowers; the others a winged griffin, a fish drawn upside down, and a bird.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Larnakes are so common around Palaikastro that Bosanquet found them serving as a wash-tub beside a well, a huge flower-pot, and the receiver of a wine-press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Larnax","questions":["A column with a moulded capital carrying sacred horns and a double axe fills one panel of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Palaikastro, with a griffin, a fish and a bird on the rest.","The narrow capacity of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suggested it held bones alone, until examples were found with the skeleton laid on its back and the knees drawn up."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Robert C. Bosanquet, \"Excavations at Palaikastro. I. §3. The Painted Larnax, and Some Other Larnax-Burials,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 297–300, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Prehistoric Tombs of Knossos</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch, 1906), 6–10, 30, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictombs00evan. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Minoan_larnax%2C_brids%2C_carriage%2C_seascape%2C_Crete%2C_AMH%2C_145315.jpg/1920px-Minoan_larnax%2C_brids%2C_carriage%2C_seascape%2C_Crete%2C_AMH%2C_145315.jpg","title":"Larnax","desc":"Minoan larnax with painting of brids, carriage and seascape. Crete, probably Postpalatial period. Archaeological Museum of Heraklion. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_larnax,_brids,_carriage,_seascape,_Crete,_AMH,_145315.jpg","alt":"Minoan larnax, brids, carriage, seascape, Crete, AMH"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-035","num":"35","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Circular chambers of unworked fieldstone bonded with clay, built above ground and entered from the east through a doorway barely a metre square, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> housed hundreds of burials each.","answer":"Mesara tholos tombs","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">EM I, c. 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">into LM IIIA, after c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mesara tholos tombs</b> are circular stone burial chambers of southern Crete, between 4 and 13 metres (13 and 43 feet) across, first built in Early Minoan I from about 3000 BCE and some of them still in use more than a thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their thick walls are of unworked fieldstones bonded with clay, founded on bedrock and standing above ground rather than sunk into it, and the doorway, rarely over a metre (3 feet) square and closed by a slab, is almost always on the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most corbel slightly inward, and whether the larger ones ever carried a complete stone vault was for decades the central question of the subject; a full vault is now allowed to the smaller tombs at least.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans, who saw the ruins himself, called them true beehive tombs raised on the principle of the horizontal arch, and took the 23 cubic metres of fallen stone inside one Platanos ossuary as proof of solid building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The larger Platanos tholos measures 13.10 metres (43 feet) inside, only a little less than the Tomb of Clytemnestra at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hundreds of inhumations went into most of them with no regard for what was already there, so the interiors are a confused mass of bones and grave goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Traces of great fires on the rock floor, and layers of clean white sand between one burial stratum and the next, are read as periodic fumigation and tidying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tholos at Hagia Triada gave up miniature figures of terracotta and steatite and more than a hundred seals of ivory, bone and steatite, every one pierced to hang from a cord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the form came from is unsettled: derivation from Syria, from Nubia and Egypt and from Cycladic prototypes have all been argued and all meet objections, while Branigan takes them for free-standing imitations of caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are not the ancestors of the Mycenaean tholos in any simple way, since the mainland tombs are sunk into hillsides under an earthen mound, fully vaulted, and entered down a long dromos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mesara tholos tombs","questions":["Whether the biggest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ever carried a complete stone vault was for decades the central question of the subject, and only the smaller ones are now allowed one.","The mainland tombs are sunk into hillsides under an earthen mound and entered down a long dromos, where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stand above ground with a small door facing east."],"tags":["building","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Stephanos Xanthoudides, <i>The Vaulted Tombs of Mesará</i> (London: Hodder and Stoughton, 1924), xi–xii, https://archive.org/details/vaultedtombsofme00xant. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 249, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/The_arch%C3%A6eology_of_prehistoric_Greece.jpg","title":"Mesara tholos tombs","desc":"The archæeology of prehistoric Greece. 2014-07-30. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_arch%C3%A6eology_of_prehistoric_Greece.jpg","alt":"The archæeology of prehistoric Greece"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-036","num":"36","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"At Knossos a portico of twelve pillars flanked one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for some 20 metres, and a guard-house faced a bastion where it met the paved road running west.","answer":"Minoan roads","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan roads</b> are known from the causeways, roadways and cobbled streets that survive at and around the palaces and towns of Bronze Age Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Knossos a roadway ran north from the palace's northern entrance carrying its own main drain, and for some 20 metres (65 feet) it was flanked by a portico of twelve pillars set in a double row.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it met a second paved road running west stood a tower or guard-house facing a massive bastion, so that the chief avenue of public access to the palace was jealously watched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across the West Court ran a narrow causeway more carefully paved than the rest and raised a little above it, striking diagonally north-west from the West Portico to avoid the angle of the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phaistos has a precisely similar diagonal causeway across its own west court, leading to a stepway that climbs the tiers of seats above, and that parallel is what settled the Knossos strip as a path rather than the base of a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> North-west of the palace the paved ways branch, one causeway running west to east towards the northern entrance and a narrower one turning north-east towards the pillar hall that faces it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where they fork lies the stepped Theatral Area, a paved space bisected by a causeway and overlooked on two sides by tiers of stone steps, eighteen of them on the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The causeway coming down from the west cuts the southern flight diagonally, which is why that flight falls from six tiers to three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside a town the roads are streets: at Palaikastro and Pseira cobbled streets define the blocks of living units in the Neopalatial period, from about 1750 BCE, as they do in contemporary settlements of the Cyclades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The paved ground had been walked a long time: a test pit through the West Court found Mycenaean and then Kamares sherds beneath it, and nearer the west wall Kamares sherds ran two metres (6 feet) down to the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan roads","questions":["A narrow strip across the West Court at Knossos was read as a wall until Phaistos produced the same thing, which settled it as one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, raised and carefully paved.","At Palaikastro and Pseira cobbled streets define the blocks of living units, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> look like inside a town rather than between them."],"tags":["building","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1902,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 5–6, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 5–6, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report for the Year 1903,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 99–100, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Malia_Minoan-site_processional-way_20230608_101244.jpg/1920px-Malia_Minoan-site_processional-way_20230608_101244.jpg","title":"Minoan roads","desc":"North side of the Minoan palace of Malia; processional way from the north entrance to the east. Rigorius, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malia_Minoan-site_processional-way_20230608_101244.jpg","alt":"Malia Minoan-site processional-way"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-037","num":"37","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A stone conduit under flat slabs, a metre high and half that wide, runs beneath the Domestic Quarter at Knossos, the backbone of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and wide enough to walk along.","answer":"Minoan water management","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Later Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan water management</b> is best seen at Knossos, where an elaborate drainage system runs through the later palace's Domestic Quarter, from about 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stone shafts descend from the upper floors to a stone conduit under flat covering slabs, about a metre (3 feet) high and half that wide for most of its course, coated inside with cement and easy for a man to walk along.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first purpose was to carry off surface water: one shaft took the rain off the Central Court and another the drainage from the neighbouring halls' roofs, both descending gradually with an occasional step down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cretan rains are torrential, so the conduits were flushed hard from time to time, and the architect turned that force to account by tying the sanitary arrangements into the same system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lightwell, one of the features that make Minoan architecture its own, is where the water is caught: at the Hall of the Colonnades the open floor is cemented rather than paved and slopes to a drain hole in the corner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> From there the drain runs along that hall's southern wall and across the angle of the Hall of the Double Axes, gaining capacity with every drainage area until a man can crawl along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath the floor of the Corridor of the Draught Board, 1.45 metres (5 feet) down, ran terracotta pipes 76 centimetres (30 inches) long, socketed one into the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each mouthpiece carries a stop-ridge solidly backed behind, which met the butt of the next tube and resisted the pressure that would otherwise have wedged the two together to splitting point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The latrine off the Room of the Plaster Couch had a wooden seat at the back with a stone foot-rest before it, about 57 centimetres (22 inches) from the ground, within a centimetre of the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mosso, a physiologist by trade, said he admired that alabaster-lined room more than the throne of Minos, and after a downpour at Hagia Triada watched water run from sewers a man could walk upright in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan water management","questions":["Under a corridor at Knossos <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> included terracotta pipes 76 centimetres long, socketed one into the next, each mouthpiece carrying a stop-ridge to stop them wedging together.","The rains of Crete are torrential, so the conduits of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were flushed hard from time to time, and the sanitary arrangements were tied into that same system."],"tags":["building","technology","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1902,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 13–14, 81–86, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 109–10, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 80, 123, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Tuyaux_de_poterie_et_jonctions_sur_le_r%C3%A9seau_d%27eau_sous_pression_%C3%A0_Knossos.jpg/1920px-Tuyaux_de_poterie_et_jonctions_sur_le_r%C3%A9seau_d%27eau_sous_pression_%C3%A0_Knossos.jpg","title":"Minoan water management","desc":"Terracotta pipes and junctions on the pressurized water network at Knossos. Le plombier du désert, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuyaux_de_poterie_et_jonctions_sur_le_r%C3%A9seau_d%27eau_sous_pression_%C3%A0_Knossos.jpg","alt":"Tuyaux de poterie et jonctions sur le réseau d'eau sous pression à Knossos"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-038","num":"38","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"About 180 metres off the north coast of eastern Crete, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rises in cliffs on one side and slopes to the sea on the other, where the Minoan town lay.","answer":"Mochlos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rich graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Early Minoan, before c. 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Town</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Neopalatial, c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mochlos</b> is a circular mass of limestone about 180 metres (200 yards) off the north coast of eastern Crete, rising in cliffs some 60 metres (200 feet) on its north side and sloping to sea level on its south, where the town lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The water between island and mainland is very shallow at one point, and Seager reasoned that if the subsidence seen elsewhere in Crete happened here, the island was once joined to it and had the best harbour on that coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Minoan house walls survive on the shore opposite, so the town lay on both sides of that neck whether or not it stood dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cemetery is a hillside of small tombs plus six large chamber tombs along a narrow ledge, a precipice overhead and a 30-metre (100-foot) drop to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Five kinds of tomb were used: ossuaries closed by upright slabs, cist graves of Cycladic type, tombs walled in small stones like houses, burials in holes in the rock, and, from Middle Minoan III, bodies laid in pithoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The house tomb is better represented here than anywhere, its slab walls sometimes standing 2.5 metres (8 feet), sometimes taking the cliff for a back wall, and roofed flat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly 150 gold ornaments came out of the graves, besides eleven necklaces of rock crystal, gold, shell, carnelian, amethyst, steatite, breccia and faience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The gold is worked two ways, beaten over a mould or pricked through from behind into raised dots, and most was worn in life rather than made for the grave: the diadems carry pin holes where their owners jabbed them tight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That upset the picture of a poor Early Minoan Crete before about 1900 BCE: Seager asked what the capitals must have held, if so small a settlement had precious metal in such abundance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether the tombs also show a ranked society is argued, Soles reading size, position and grave goods that way and Watrous explaining them otherwise; in the Neopalatial period the houses stand large and separate along the waterfront.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mochlos","questions":["Nearly 150 gold ornaments and eleven necklaces came out of the graves at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is what proved that Early Minoan Crete was not a poor place.","Six large ossuaries stand on a narrow ledge at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a precipice overhead and a drop of 30 metres to the sea below."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Richard B. Seager, \"Excavations on the Island of Mochlos, Crete, in 1908,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 13, no. 3 (1909): 273–74, https://archive.org/details/jstor-497023. [Open access]","Richard B. Seager, <i>Explorations in the Island of Mochlos</i> (Boston: American School of Classical Studies at Athens, 1912), 13–15, 104–6, https://archive.org/details/explorationsinis00seaguoft. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/%CE%9C%CF%8C%CF%87%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_05.jpg/1920px-%CE%9C%CF%8C%CF%87%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_05.jpg","title":"Mochlos","desc":"Μόχλος 05. 2015-08-17 10:33:42. Vgargan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9C%CF%8C%CF%87%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_05.jpg","alt":"Μόχλος 05"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Mochlos","at":[25.9075,35.1875]}},
{"id":"gr-039","num":"39","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cliffs rise sheer 240 metres from the sea on one side of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the other slopes to three sandy coves, on the middle one of which the Minoan town stood.","answer":"Pseira","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Town</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM I – LM I, c. 1900 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pseira</b> is a barren mass of rock about 3 kilometres (2 miles) off eastern Crete, roughly that long itself and nowhere more than 1.6 kilometres (1 mile) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its west coast is cliffs rising sheer 240 metres (800 feet) out of the sea, while the east slopes to three sandy coves divided by tongues of land, the middle one running out some 180 metres (200 yards).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that point stood a Minoan town, already important in Middle Minoan I from about 1900 BCE, which Seager thought owed its existence to the sheltered cove on its south side, open only to the east, whence gales rarely come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island offered little else: terrace walls show the slopes were cultivated, but the ground was too small and thin to feed a farming population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seager set that beside Gournia, which had no harbour and never reached the same wealth or such close dealings with Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The town is a huddle of streets and houses over the top and sides of the point, with long flights of steps dropping to the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two roads climb from the head of the cove and a stepway divides near the summit into four or five ways across the hilltop; the level roads are unpaved, floored with natural rock, while every side alley down to the sea has a paved stepway still in good repair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The walls are heavy, sometimes roughly squared blocks in the Late Minoan I manner over lighter Middle Minoan I work, with no ashlar and no house to match the palace at Gournia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brick seems not to have been used at all, unlike Gournia and Vasiliki: it would have come from the mainland, while the island's stratified limestone breaks off ready to build with and a soft slaty stone splits into slabs for upper floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No well or cistern has been found in the town, and its early graves held clay and stone vases with little metal, the mark of a poorer community than the one buried at Mochlos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pseira","questions":["The level roads of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are unpaved and floored with natural rock, while every side alley running down to the sea has a paved stepway still in good repair.","Brick seems not to have been used at all at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since it would have had to come from the mainland and the island's limestone breaks off ready to build with."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Richard B. Seager, <i>Excavations on the Island of Pseira, Crete</i> (Philadelphia: University Museum, 1910), 5–9, https://archive.org/details/excavationsonisl00seag. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Richard B. Seager, <i>Explorations in the Island of Mochlos</i> (Boston: American School of Classical Studies at Athens, 1912), 13, https://archive.org/details/explorationsinis00seaguoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Psira_island.jpg","title":"Pseira","desc":"Island of Psira north of Crete in the Gulf of Mirabella. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Psira_island.jpg","alt":"Psira island"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Pseira","at":[25.8597,35.1903]}},
{"id":"gr-040","num":"40","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Both fists held against the chest and the left foot slightly advanced, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands about 50 centimetres high, its body of hippopotamus ivory on a wooden frame.","answer":"Palaikastro Kouros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM IB, c. 1500 – 1450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1450 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Palaikastro Kouros</b> is a Minoan statuette of a young man, made of several materials at once and recovered in some hundreds of fragments from an urban sanctuary in eastern Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stands about 50 centimetres (20 inches) high and 18 (7 inches) wide, both fists held against the chest in the attitude of the Minoan worshipper and the left foot slightly advanced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The body is hippopotamus ivory built on a wooden armature, the eyes are rock crystal, and thin sheets of gold render the loincloth and the sandals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Faure adds plaques of Egyptian blue, and reads both the method of assembly and the proportions as Egyptian, of a kind met in other Minoan divine figures at Knossos, Malia and Archanes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the head is cut from is not agreed: the excavators call it steatite and their reviewer serpentine, from the country north of Ida.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It is the most complete Minoan male figure to survive apart from the ivory acrobat from Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pieces came up over several seasons, the torso and an arm first in 1987 and the legs last in 1990, and only the middle of the body, probably of wood, and a little of the face are still missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The town at Palaikastro reached its greatest extent in the Neopalatial period, with substantial houses and wide streets in blocks, and many of its buildings burned about 1450 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The statuette comes from that Late Minoan IB destruction, and its final publication argues that the figure is the young god of nearby Mount Petsofas, an ancestor of the Diktaian Zeus named in Greek inscriptions a thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Faure refuses the link on three grounds: the Linear A dedications from Petsofas name three apparently female powers, the name Dikte was given to sacred mountains all over Crete and beyond it, and the equation with the Linear B address to the Diktaian god is not established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Palaikastro Kouros","questions":["Only the middle of the body, probably of wood, and a little of the face are missing from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was recovered in several hundred fragments.","The final publication makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the young god of nearby Mount Petsofas and an ancestor of the Diktaian Zeus, which one reviewer flatly refuses."],"tags":["object","art","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jean-Claude Poursat, review of <i>The Palaikastro Kouros: A Minoan Chryselephantine Statuette and Its Aegean Bronze Age Context</i>, <i>Topoi. Orient-Occident</i> 11, no. 2 (2001): 707–10, https://www.persee.fr/doc/topoi_1161-9473_2001_num_11_2_1961. [Open access]","Paul Faure, review of <i>The Palaikastro Kouros: A Minoan Chryselephantine Statuette and Its Aegean Bronze Age Context</i>, <i>Revue des Études Grecques</i> 114, no. 1 (2001): 284–85, https://www.persee.fr/doc/reg_0035-2039_2001_num_114_1_4448_t1_0284_0000_2. [Open access]","Gilles Touchais, \"Palaikastro,\" <i>Bulletin de Correspondance Hellénique</i> 120, no. 3 (1996): 1320, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bch_0007-4217_1996_num_120_3_14084. [Open access]","\"Chiona Beach Project,\" British School at Athens, https://www.bsa.ac.uk/research-2/chiona-beach-project-2/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Palaikastro_Kouros_12.jpg/1920px-Palaikastro_Kouros_12.jpg","title":"Palaikastro Kouros","desc":"Kouros von Palaikastro (1480–1425 v. Chr.), minoische Götterstatue aus Haus 5 der Ausgrabungsstätte von Roussolakkos, Archäologisches Museum von Sitia, Kreta, Griechenland. Olaf Tausch, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palaikastro_Kouros_12.jpg","alt":"Palaikastro Kouros 12"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-041","num":"41","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The central court of the first palace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> measures 13 by 6 metres, where the court at Knossos runs to 54 by 27.","answer":"Petras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Late Neolithic on, before 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemetery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">EM II – MM IIB, to c. 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Petras</b> is a Minoan settlement on a hill east of modern Siteia, looking out over Siteia Bay, with a palatial building at its centre and a town around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its palace is among the smallest of the court-centred Cretan buildings: the central court of the first phase measures 13 by 6 metres (43 by 20 feet), where the court at Knossos runs to 54 by 27.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Occupation goes back much further, a Late Neolithic settlement on the eastern slope and an extensive Early Minoan I one on the north, and long afterwards a Late Minoan III wall was thrown round the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Sector I, north-east of the palace, House I.1 went up in Middle Minoan IIIB, was last lived in during Late Minoan IA, and was occupied again in Late Minoan II to III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the neighbouring hill stands a cemetery of thirteen house tombs, used from Early Minoan II to Middle Minoan IIB, with an undisturbed rock shelter beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Only six have been dug out completely; corridors run between them and two ceremonial areas were laid out among them, across four phases of the cemetery's life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are built much as the house tombs at Mochlos are, in several phases with several rooms apiece, and fragments of marble figurines point to the Cyclades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The shelter on the western side held a secondary deposit a metre deep, probably emptied out of one or more of the tombs, and seems to have gone out of use when the palace was founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It gave up at least 95 skulls and some 7,000 human bones, with 100 clay vases and five of stone, 10 silver and seven bronze objects including daggers, four gold bands and five seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bones were carried there once the flesh was gone, traces of burning suggest fumigation where they first lay, and one woman shows what may be artificial deformation of the skull and trephination shortly before she died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Petras","questions":["A rock shelter beside the house tombs at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held at least 95 skulls and some 7,000 human bones, moved there after the flesh had gone.","Thirteen house tombs of Early Minoan II to Middle Minoan IIB stand on the hill beside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with corridors between them and two ceremonial areas laid out among them."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["R. Angus K. Smith, review of <i>Petras, Siteia II: A Minoan Palatial Settlement in Eastern Crete. Late Bronze Age Pottery from Houses I.1 and I.2</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2023.08.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.08.04/. [Open access]","Ilaria Caloi, review of <i>Petras, Siteia: The Pre- and Proto-Palatial Cemetery in Context</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2017.10.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.10.45. [Open access]","\"KEPHALA, Petras — 2009,\" <i>Chronique des fouilles en ligne</i>, no. 1793, École française d'Athènes, https://chronique.efa.gr/?kroute=report&id=1793. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Petras_Ausgrabungsst%C3%A4tte_50.jpg/1920px-Petras_Ausgrabungsst%C3%A4tte_50.jpg","title":"Petras","desc":"Minoische Ausgrabungsstätte von Petras (Πετράς), Gemeinde Sitia, Kreta, Griechenland. Olaf Tausch, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petras_Ausgrabungsst%C3%A4tte_50.jpg","alt":"Petras Ausgrabungsstätte 50"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Petras","at":[26.1,35.2]}},
{"id":"gr-042","num":"42","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"No bodies and hardly any metal were found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its people had warning, and a first thin fall of pumice was probably the warning they took.","answer":"Akrotiri","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late LM IA, c. 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1607 BCE, by radiocarbon</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Akrotiri</b> is a Bronze Age town on Thera, buried late in Late Minoan IA, around 1500 BCE, by the eruption of the island's volcano and preserved under metres of debris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The caldera it left measures as much as 83 square kilometres (32 square miles) and reaches 480 metres (1,570 feet) below sea level inside cliffs standing 300 metres (1,000 feet) above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An earthquake had wrecked much of the town beforehand, and its people set about pulling down what was unsafe, levelling the rubble and rebuilding, work unfinished when the volcano went off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Large grooved ellipsoidal stones on that levelled rubble served much as a wrecking ball does, and the pottery and the scale of the repairs argue years, probably decades, between earthquake and eruption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An upstairs bedroom in the West House was still being plastered and painted when work stopped, two vessels of dried plaster and one of paint left standing in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> No bodies were found and hardly any metal or portable valuables, so the people had warning: the first thin fall of pumice, 3 centimetres (1 inch) deep and weathered as though open for months, was probably it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A study of 8,000 animal bones shows goats and sheep kept far more than pigs and cattle used mostly for draught, and two of the richest buildings were each raised from one quarry's stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fava beans in the West House jars were infested with the bean weevil, and a radiocarbon date on the beetles gives a midpoint of 1607 BCE, range 1744 to 1538, well before the pottery's date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same beetles date the season: they lay eggs in green pods in the field and emerge once the crop is stored, and adults had emerged here, so the eruption came in summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The frescoes survive in larger pieces than anywhere else and can be set back on their own walls, so whole rooms can be reconstructed: the fleet crossing between two towns, the blue monkeys, the boxing boys, and the crocus-gatherers bringing flowers to a seated goddess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Akrotiri","questions":["Bean weevils in the storage jars of the West House give <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a radiocarbon midpoint of 1607 BCE, and their life cycle puts the eruption in summer.","An earthquake wrecked much of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> years before the eruption, and the demolition, levelling and rebuilding that followed were still unfinished when the volcano went off."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","\"THERA — 2011,\" <i>Chronique des fouilles en ligne</i>, no. 2761, École française d'Athènes, https://chronique.efa.gr/?id=2761&kroute=report. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"THERA. — Akrotiri. — 1976,\" <i>Chronique des fouilles en ligne</i>, no. 21919, École française d'Athènes, https://chronique.efa.gr/?id=21919&r=report. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Fresco_of_a_fisherman%2C_Akrotiri%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Akrotiri","desc":"Fresco of a fisherman holding dolphinfish, Akrotiri, Santorini, Greece. Height: 1.10 m. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_of_a_fisherman,_Akrotiri,_Greece.jpg","alt":"Fresco of a fisherman, Akrotiri, Greece"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Akrotiri","at":[25.4036,36.3514]}},
{"id":"gr-043","num":"43","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Tephra from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies in Late Minoan IA levels on Rhodes, Melos and Crete, well below the burnt horizon that ends the Cretan palaces.","answer":"Thera eruption","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Erupted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1627 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1540 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caldera filled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">within 2 days</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Thera eruption</b>, which vulcanologists call the Minoan eruption, is among the largest of the Holocene and emptied a Cycladic island of its people late in Late Minoan IA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The island was already a ring of land round a flooded bay, left by the Cape Riva eruption 22,000 years ago, with a lava island growing at its centre ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four phases are read off the deposits: a plinian pumice fall, surges thrown up where seawater reached the vent, a coarse ignimbrite 55 metres (180 feet) thick carrying blocks 10 metres (33 feet) across, and a finer, hotter one from several vents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third phase blew the central island apart and the fourth ran with the collapse of the caldera, a hollow 10 by 7 kilometres (6 by 4 miles) across and the youngest of at least four there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The erupted mass may reach 117 to 129 cubic kilometres (28 to 31 cubic miles), a ceiling rather than a settled figure, since the offshore deposits are poorly measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ash lies over 5 metres (16 feet) deep at Akrotiri and up to 50 metres (160 feet) elsewhere, and vulcanologists agree the sequence was rapid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prevailing winds carried the pumice south-east, sparing the mainland and western Crete and leaving eastern Crete under 10 centimetres (4 inches) at most, thin enough, some argue, for wind and rain to clear within a year or two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new caldera was sealed off from the sea while it collapsed, and only afterwards did water break in through a channel 3 kilometres (2 miles) long and fill it in under two days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Collapse therefore cannot have raised the waves that reached Crete; pyroclastic flows running into the sea did, and debris at Palaikastro records a run-up of at least 9 metres (30 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon has long put it near 1600 BCE and a Therasia shrub now favours the mid-16th century, but on either date its tephra lies in Late Minoan IA levels on Rhodes and Melos as well as Crete, before the Late Minoan IB fires that ended the Cretan palaces, so no direct link can be shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Thera eruption","questions":["Four phases carried the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a plinian pumice fall through wet surges to an ignimbrite 55 metres thick, and the caldera collapsed at the end of it.","The caldera left by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was sealed from the sea while it formed, so the waves that reached Crete came from pyroclastic flows running into the water instead."],"tags":["event","geology","nature","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","Dávid Karátson et al., \"Towards Reconstruction of the Lost Late Bronze Age Intra-Caldera Island of Santorini, Greece,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 7026, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5935677/. [Open access]","Paraskevi Nomikou et al., \"Post-Eruptive Flooding of Santorini Caldera and Implications for Tsunami Generation,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 7 (2016): 13332, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5105177/. [Open access]","Global Volcanism Program, \"Santorini,\" <i>Volcanoes of the World</i>, Smithsonian Institution, https://volcano.si.edu/volcano.cfm?vn=212040. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Charlotte Pearson, Kostas Sbonias, Iris Tzachili and Timothy J. Heaton, \"Olive Shrub Buried on Therasia Supports a Mid-16th Century BCE Date for the Thera Eruption,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 6994, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10147620/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Santorini_ASTER.jpg","title":"Thera eruption","desc":"Santorini island, Greece - EOS photo NASA, public domain. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santorini_ASTER.jpg","alt":"Santorini ASTER"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-044","num":"44","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A fleet moving between two towns fills the south wall of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and warriors come ashore under tower shields and boars'-tusk helmets on the north.","answer":"Flotilla fresco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1627 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1540 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Flotilla fresco</b> is the miniature frieze that ran round the top of Room 5, on the upper storey of the West House at Akrotiri on Thera, above the niches in its walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is arguably the most complex single work of Aegean Bronze Age art yet recovered in anything like its entirety, and it answers to three names: 'the flotilla', 'the naval battle' and 'the river landscape'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The south wall, 40 centimetres (16 inches) high, carries a fleet of ships moving between two towns, and the north wall the same height, with a ceremony on a hilltop and warriors coming ashore under tower shields and boars'-tusk helmets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The east wall, half as tall, shows a river running through a landscape; the frieze went along the west wall too, of which little survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below it two life-sized nude fishermen stood in narrow panels, and under each window a painted band imitated the veining of coloured marble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Marinatos dug the fragments out in 1971 and 1972, and the frieze has drawn an enormous literature, some of it treating the fleet as the record of a particular voyage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who the ships belong to, who the warriors are and where the two towns stood are all disputed, and it is doubtful whether the evidence now available can settle any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Caution is owed on general grounds: Minoan painting offers no unmistakably particularised scene, historical or mythological, and one study of Aegean architectural painting argues that a painted town is built from an idea rather than from observation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the ships and towns do have is company: the Town Mosaic from Knossos before them, the silver Siege Rhyton from Grave Circle A at Mycenae beside them, and boats incised on Cycladic frying pans a thousand years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Men carry more of this frieze than of most Minoan painting, the fishermen, the warriors and the ships' captains all being male, and the whole of it went under the ash late in Late Minoan IA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Flotilla fresco","questions":["Two life-sized nude fishermen stood in narrow panels below the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and under each window a painted band imitated the veining of coloured marble.","The ships of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> share features with boats incised on Cycladic frying pans a thousand years before, and its towns with the Town Mosaic from Knossos."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Alexandre Farnoux, \"Image et paysage: l'exemple des fresques de la Maison Ouest de Théra,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 15 (1990): 133–42, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ktema_0221-5896_1990_num_15_1_2013. [Open access]","Catherine Lyon Crawford, review of <i>Aegean Wall Painting: A Tribute to Mark Cameron</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.12.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.12.18/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/Museum_of_Prehistoric_Thera_city_detail.jpg","title":"Flotilla fresco","desc":"City detail from the flotilla fresco. Museum of Prehistoric Thera. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museum_of_Prehistoric_Thera_city_detail.jpg","alt":"Museum of Prehistoric Thera city detail"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-045","num":"45","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Thera lay empty for as much as a century or two under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the same ash has since been traced in lake beds in western Anatolia.","answer":"Theran tephra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Laid down</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-16th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ice-core spikes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1611, 1562 – 1555 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Theran tephra</b> is the ash and pumice thrown out by the Late Bronze Age eruption on Thera, and the layer by which that eruption is traced and dated across the eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It buried its own island so deeply that Thera was uninhabitable for as much as a century or two, and Akrotiri was never lived in again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deep-sea cores from the southern Aegean and cuttings on Crete map where it fell, and a layer of it turned up in lake sediments in western Anatolia, further north and east than anyone had supposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it landed thinly, on eastern Crete, its effect is argued over: one side holds that even a few centimetres would have wrecked the harvests, the other that so thin a layer enriches the soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument matters because one long-standing view made the eruption the cause of the fires that ended the Cretan palaces, through failed harvests or a fleet lost to the waves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The opposing view holds that it had nothing to do with them and puts the destructions down to local earthquakes, or to Mycenaeans arriving from the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tephra decides between them: it sits in Late Minoan IA levels at Trianda on Rhodes and Phylakopi on Melos as well as on Crete, so the eruption comes well before the Late Minoan IB destructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For its size the eruption put comparatively little sulfate into the air, so its power to cool the climate was smaller than its bulk suggests, though its columns rose more than 30 kilometres (19 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sulfate spikes in the polar ice at 1611 BCE and at 1562 to 1555 BCE are the leading candidates, and which is right turns on the eruption's own date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That date is still argued: radiocarbon on buried material has long pointed near 1600 BCE, a shrub buried on Therasia raises the odds on the middle of the 16th century, and dating of Egyptian objects puts the eruption before the reign of Ahmose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Theran tephra","questions":["One camp blamed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for ruining the farmland of eastern Crete, another read the same thin layer as harmless, or even as good for the soil.","The eruption that laid down <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put comparatively little sulfate into the air, so its effect on climate was smaller than its size would suggest."],"tags":["object","geology","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Charlotte Pearson, Kostas Sbonias, Iris Tzachili and Timothy J. Heaton, \"Olive Shrub Buried on Therasia Supports a Mid-16th Century BCE Date for the Thera Eruption,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 6994, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10147620/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Hendrik J. Bruins and Johannes van der Plicht, \"The Minoan Thera Eruption Predates Pharaoh Ahmose: Radiocarbon Dating of Egyptian 17th to Early 18th Dynasty Museum Objects,\" <i>PLOS One</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0330702, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12422448/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Akrotiri-Santorini_volcanic-pumice_Aegean-Sea_Greece.jpg","title":"Theran tephra","desc":"Two layers of pumice, long time eroded, mined in quarries, and in one layer small scale rock intrusions of the great minoan, Late-Bronze-Age (3,5 ka) volcanic eruption of the caldera island Thera/Santorini (Minoan Eruption). Ulrichstill, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akrotiri-Santorini_volcanic-pumice_Aegean-Sea_Greece.jpg","alt":"Akrotiri-Santorini volcanic-pumice Aegean-Sea Greece"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-046","num":"46","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cretan pottery lying at Kahun and Harageh in levels of the early 19th century BCE is the firmest early evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the palaces were new when it arrived.","answer":"Minoan trade with Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest finds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 19th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palatial era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1930 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan trade with Egypt</b> ran through most of the Bronze Age and is known almost entirely from objects found out of place: Cretan pottery in the Nile valley, Egyptian goods in the Cretan palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest contacts were indirect, with Levantine trading centres standing between the two, and only later did Crete and Egypt deal with one another face to face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The firmest early evidence is Cretan pottery of Middle Minoan IB to IIA date from Kahun and Harageh, in levels of the early 19th century BCE, when the first palaces at Knossos and Phaistos were new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What went out was Kamares ware, the eggshell-thin polychrome tableware of the palaces, whose use was confined to the palaces that made it, to the peak and cave sanctuaries, and to a few centres abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That last group is the point: the foreign holdings look less like a market than like gifts passing between one ruling class and another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Coming the other way, a cache of 24 Neopalatial stone vessels fell from an upper room at Knossos, twelve of them rhyta and several of them Egyptian imports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Figurines, amulets, stone bowls and scarabs from the Mesara tombs were once read as direct Egyptian imports and used to derive the tholos tomb from Egypt or Nubia, an argument the dating has since undercut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ostrich eggs were mounted as Minoan rhyta from shells that can only have reached Crete from Egypt or Syria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Egyptian religion left a mark as well: the Minoan genius, the animal-headed figure that carries a libation jug in Cretan art, is a reworking of Ta-weret, the hippopotamus goddess who in Egypt is a kindly spirit rather than a great divinity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Long after the palaces fell, the harbour town of Kommos on the south coast was taking in vases from Egypt, the Levant, Cyprus and Italy at once, the exchange by then plain commerce rather than a traffic between courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan trade with Egypt","questions":["Because Kamares ware went only to the palaces, the mountain sanctuaries and a few courts abroad, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks in its early phase like gift-giving between rulers rather than a market.","Ostrich-egg rhyta, Egyptian stone vases fallen from an upper room at Knossos and the jug-carrying Minoan genius are all marks left by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the island."],"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","greece","egypt","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Maria Cannata, review of <i>Beyond the Nile: Egypt and the Classical World</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2019.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.04.37/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Cretans_Bringing_Gifts%2C_Tomb_of_Rekhmire_MET_DT10883.jpg/1920px-Cretans_Bringing_Gifts%2C_Tomb_of_Rekhmire_MET_DT10883.jpg","title":"Minoan trade with Egypt","desc":"Facsimile, Rekhmire (TT 100), foreigners, Keftiu. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cretans_Bringing_Gifts,_Tomb_of_Rekhmire_MET_DT10883.jpg","alt":"Cretans Bringing Gifts, Tomb of Rekhmire"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-047","num":"47","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Standing for the west beside a man of Punt, a Nubian and a Syrian, the man of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> personifies one quarter of the world in the Theban tombs.","answer":"Keftiu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb paintings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1479 – 1425 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Keftiu</b> is the name Egyptian texts and tomb paintings give to a land and a people beyond the sea, taken since the early 20th century to be Crete and the Aegean islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Theban tombs of the 18th Dynasty the four quarters of the world are personified in turn, and the man of Keftiu stands for the west, beside a man of Punt for the east, a Nubian for the south and a Syrian for the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is drawn as a people apart, unrelated to the Syrian who represents the north, and wears a waistcloth rather than Semitic robes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egyptian usage put Keftiu among the isles, at the far end of the ring of coasts a ship followed to reach it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The identification was long resisted because Ptolemaic scribes used Keftiu to render the Greek name for Phoenicia, which made the earlier placing in the west look like a blunder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What tells against that is the freight: the tombs of Puamra, Senenmut, Rekhmire and Menkheperraseneb show the Keftiu bringing metal vessels, and one long one-handled vase is drawn just like the jug held by the Cup-Bearer at Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Rekhmire's tomb a man with a drawn sword turns back to speak to the next in the file while carrying a great white vase whose curved handles match those of an alabaster vase from Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Metropolitan Museum, which holds Nina de Garis Davies's full-size copies of the scene, files it both as gifts from the Keftiu and as Cretans bringing gifts, and puts the tomb at about 1479 to 1425 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name stuck to a pot as well: the plain straight-sided cup of Minoan Crete, the commonest drinking vessel of the Middle Minoan III period, is still called a Keftiu cup, or a Vapheio cup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Behind the ceremony lies ordinary traffic, Egyptian goods in the Cretan palaces and Cretan pottery in the Nile valley, which is what a procession of gift-bearers turns into ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Keftiu","questions":["Ptolemaic scribes used <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to render the Greek name for Phoenicia, which is why the earlier Egyptian placing of it in the west was long read as a blunder.","A long one-handled vase carried by the men of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Theban tombs is drawn just like the jug held by the Cup-Bearer at Knossos."],"tags":["people","history","archaeology","greece","egypt","bronze age"],"sources":["H. R. Hall, \"Keftiu and the Peoples of the Sea,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 157–89, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Maria Cannata, review of <i>Beyond the Nile: Egypt and the Classical World</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2019.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.04.37/. [Open access]","\"Gifts from the Keftiu, Tomb of Rekhmire,\" facsimile by Nina de Garis Davies, accession 30.4.85, collection record, Metropolitan Museum of Art, https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/544611. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Minoan_-_Rhyton_in_the_Form_of_a_Bull%27s_Head_-_Walters_23194_-_Side.jpg","title":"Keftiu","desc":"This rhyton is carved in the round with details incised. It is interior drilled with a large hole in the top of the head, ending in a small hole through the mouth. The eyes are to be inlaid. There is a double axe on the forehead. One horn is broken and has been repaired. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_-_Rhyton_in_the_Form_of_a_Bull%27s_Head_-_Walters_23194_-_Side.jpg","alt":"Minoan - Rhyton in the Form of a Bull's Head - Side"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-048","num":"48","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The whole basis of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one sentence of Thucydides, saying that Minos was the first man tradition records as having built a navy.","answer":"Minoan thalassocracy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or later</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1375 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan thalassocracy</b> is the sea-empire that later Greeks credited to Minos of Crete, and the question is how much Bronze Age reality lies behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says Minos is the first man known by tradition to have built a navy, that he made himself master of what the Greeks called their own sea and ruled the Cyclades, and that he put piracy down there to secure his revenues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that Minos sent the first colonies into most of those islands, driving out the Carians and setting his own sons over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elsewhere he says that once the navy existed the sea became easier to cross, the coasts turned to getting rich, and some towns grew strong enough to build walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether any of this describes the 2nd millennium BCE is unsettled, and the argument has run for decades against a backdrop of real Minoan expansion across the southern Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Such a power would have to belong either to the Neopalatial period, roughly 1750 to 1500 BCE, or to the century after it when Knossos alone still had a working palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kastri on Kythera, and probably Trianda on Rhodes, do look to have held Minoans and nobody else in the earliest Late Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Ayia Irini, Phylakopi and Akrotiri, though, where Minoan influence runs deepest and Linear A was written, the evidence will not show Minoans in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Warships certainly existed, since one Theran frieze shows a fleet under way and a cluster of them lying offshore, but those ships need not be Cretan, nor kept up to police the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked whether Minoan influence across the Aegean, Cyprus, the Levant and Egypt can be explained by trade alone, together with Cretan craftsmen working abroad and no Minoan navy at all, Rutter answers yes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan thalassocracy","questions":["The trouble with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that Kastri on Kythera looks to have held Minoans and nobody else, while at Ayia Irini and Phylakopi no evidence puts them in charge.","The weak joint in any argument for a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that warships plainly existed in the Bronze Age Aegean but need not have been Cretan."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.4, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.8, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Minoan_copper_ingot_from_Zakros%2C_Crete.jpg","title":"Minoan thalassocracy","desc":"Minoan copper ingot from Zakros, Crete. Chris 73, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_copper_ingot_from_Zakros,_Crete.jpg","alt":"Minoan copper ingot from Zakros, Crete"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-049","num":"49","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"In the earliest Late Bronze Age <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks to have held Cretans and nobody else, which cannot be said of any Cycladic town.","answer":"Minoan Kythera","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Minoan phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan Kythera</b> is the Cretan settlement on the island lying between Crete and the southern Peloponnese, and the clearest case anywhere of Minoans living outside Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre was Kastri, on the east coast, probably first settled permanently by colonists from Crete in Middle Minoan IA, around 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the earliest phases of the Late Bronze Age the place looks to have held Minoans and nobody else, which cannot be said of the Cycladic towns where Minoan influence is strongest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its dead were laid in chamber tombs, and both the tombs and the goods put in them are thoroughly Minoan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is where an old theory of Evans's comes apart: he made the lords of the Shaft Graves at Mycenae Cretan conquerors, and Kythera shows what Minoans abroad actually did with their dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Above the settlement, on the hill called Vouno, stood a peak sanctuary of the Cretan kind, one of a small group whose worshippers left stone libation tables and ladles cut with Linear A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Peak sanctuaries were a Cretan institution and few of them lasted: only nine of the 39 known survive into Late Minoan I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other signs incised on objects from the island have been claimed as Linear A as well, and are not confirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The island sat on the way to Laconia, where Minoan stone-workers were after the green-flecked porphyry called lapis Lacedaemonius by early Late Minoan IA, and where a Minoan potter may have been living, out of Crete or out of Kythera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kastri stayed in step with Crete to the end of the palaces, the two Late Minoan IB painting styles turning up together in its deposits as they do at Ayia Irini on Keos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan Kythera","questions":["A peak sanctuary of the Cretan kind stood on the hill above the settlement of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its worshippers left stone libation tables cut with Linear A.","Evans made the lords of the Shaft Graves Cretan conquerors, but the chamber tombs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show what Minoans abroad really did with their dead."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Thomas Palaima and Aren Wilson-Wright, review of Brent Davis, <i>Minoan Stone Vessels with Linear A Inscriptions</i>, Aegaeum 36, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.11.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.11.36/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Kythera","at":[22.9975,36.2575]}},
{"id":"gr-050","num":"50","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Mallia, Phaistos and Zakros never held a court again after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and only Knossos went on, under someone nobody can name.","answer":"destruction of the Minoan palaces","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1490 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>destruction of the Minoan palaces</b> is the wave of burning that ended the Neopalatial period on Crete, at some point around 1490 to 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It reached virtually every Minoan settlement of any consequence: the palaces at Mallia, Phaistos and Zakros, the villa complexes at Ayia Triada and Tylissos, the town of Gournia, the village at Myrtos Pyrgos and the lone villa at Sklavokampos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the destructions involved fire, and things were left where they lay: the Sanctuary Rhyton, which shows a peak sanctuary in considerable detail, came out of the burnt level at Zakros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mallia, Phaistos and Zakros were never rebuilt as palaces, and only Knossos went on, though it is far from clear who was in charge of it afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An earlier horizon had done something similar two and a half centuries before, when Knossos and Phaistos were wrecked at the end of Middle Minoan IIB, probably by an earthquake, and Quartier Mu at Mallia burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What caused the later wave is not agreed, and the argument now runs between human agency and natural, with a few sites read as having suffered both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor has anyone settled which natural disaster is meant: earthquake is the usual answer and still wants proving, since buildings and layers cannot always show when a tremor turned into a social collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Thera eruption was long a candidate, but its ash lies in Late Minoan IA levels well before this horizon, and a post-eruption Late Minoan IA phase is now clear at Mochlos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the burning was one event is unsettled too, ceramic work having read it as a generation or more of unquiet years while the current feeling among specialists is that the destructions fell relatively close together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is certain is that they cannot be lined up across the island, since the last fires at Chania in the west and Zakros in the east will not correlate easily with what happened at Knossos in the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"destruction of the Minoan palaces","questions":["Earthquake is the usual answer to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and still wants proving, since buildings and layers cannot show when a tremor turned into a social collapse.","The last fires at Chania in the west and at Zakros in the east will not line up with Knossos in the middle, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may be several events."],"tags":["event","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Luca Girella, review of Thomas M. Brogan and Erik Hallager, eds., <i>LM IB Pottery: Relative Chronology and Regional Differences</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2013.05.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.05.08/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Knossos_-_North_Portico_02.jpg/1920px-Knossos_-_North_Portico_02.jpg","title":"destruction of the Minoan palaces","desc":"The North Portico in Knossos, Crete, Greece. Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_-_North_Portico_02.jpg","alt":"Knossos - North Portico 02"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-051","num":"51","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Shaft-niche graves found nowhere else on the island, and chamber tombs with dromoi leaning inward in the mainland manner, cluster round <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the palaces burn.","answer":"Mycenaean Knossos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Final Palatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1490 – 1375 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palace burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1375 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Knossos</b> is the century or so after the Cretan palaces burned, when Knossos alone still worked as a palace and the question is who was running it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period, Late Minoan II to IIIA2 and roughly 1490 to 1375 BCE, is now called Final Palatial Crete, and it is when a Knossian hegemony rises over the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around the palace a set of richly furnished tombs appears, the so-called Warrior Graves, in cemeteries at Zapher Papoura, Ayios Ioannis and Sellopoulo, most of them newly opened in Late Minoan II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their forms are not Minoan: shaft-niche graves, a small group found nowhere else on Crete, and chamber tombs with long dromoi whose walls lean inward in the mainland manner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their contents are not either, with weapons buried beside the dead, hoards of bronze vessels, quantities of jewellery, mainland shapes such as the two-handled kylix, and the first amber to reach the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three readings have long been on offer: a Mycenaean king who conquered Crete around 1500 BCE, a viceroy installed by one, or a Minoan lord keeping Mycenaean mercenaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The graves are the evidence everyone reaches for, and the field is split down the middle over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alberti takes them for the burials of Mycenaean conquerors, while allowing that the mainland parallels are stronger in the pottery than in the jewellery, the bronzes or the weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Preston writes about the same tombs without using the words conqueror or warrior grave at all, reading them as elite burials, and an earlier study of the grave goods sorted the dead into three ranks and argued they were Minoans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pottery cuts both ways: the mainland shapes are made at Knossos rather than imported, and at Knossos this pottery comes only out of the tombs, while elsewhere on Crete the same wares turn up only in settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean Knossos","questions":["One scholar reads the Warrior Graves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the tombs of conquerors while another describes the same burials without using the word conqueror once.","Weapons beside the dead, hoards of bronze vessels and the first amber to reach Crete mark out the rich tombs around <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from anything Minoan before them."],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","J. D. Muhly, review of Gerald Cadogan, Eleni Hatzaki and Antonis Vasilakis, eds., <i>Knossos: Palace, City, State</i>, BSA Studies 12, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.09.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.09.17/. [Open access]","Sean Hemingway, review of Nicoletta Momigliano, ed., <i>Knossos Pottery Handbook: Neolithic and Bronze Age (Minoan)</i>, BSA Studies 14, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.05.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.05.21/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Antiquities_from_the_cemetery_of_Zafer_Papoura.png/1920px-Antiquities_from_the_cemetery_of_Zafer_Papoura.png","title":"Mycenaean Knossos","desc":"Tripod Plaster Hearth and group of Bronze Vessels found in south-west corner of Chamber-Tomb (No. 14). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antiquities_from_the_cemetery_of_Zafer_Papoura.png","alt":"Antiquities from the cemetery of Zafer Papoura"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Knossos","at":[25.1631,35.2981]}},
{"id":"gr-052","num":"52","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Where Pylos kept its records in one archive room, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came out of deposits scattered through the palace, and not all of them need be of one date.","answer":"Knossos Linear B archive","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chariot Tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1390 – 1370 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Knossos Linear B archive</b> is the body of clay tablets found in the palace, written in an early dialect of Greek and preserved because fire baked them hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Unlike Pylos, Knossos has no central archive room: its tablets came out of deposits scattered through the building, and those deposits need not all be of one date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are more broken than the Pylos texts and carry fewer signs apiece, which makes reading across them hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Something like 66 to 75 scribal hands have been counted in the corpus, most of them working inside one sector of the palace economy, on one commodity or place or group of people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A statistical study drew 1,331 personal names out of 49 of those hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hand 117 is the sheep scribe, and his tablets usually set a second man above the shepherd, a 'collector' read by some as the flock's owner and by others as an official answerable to the palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The shepherds may themselves have been supervisors, contracting others to do the day's pasturing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hands 103 and 105 keep the textile records, while the Room of the Chariot Tablets stands apart from the rest of the corpus, sharing fewer names with it than chance would give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When any of it was written is the oldest quarrel about it: the tablets were long tied to the palace's fall early in Late Minoan IIIA2, and Palmer, Hallager and others move most of them to the middle of the 13th century BCE, while Driessen has redated even the Chariot Tablets by twenty years and the specialists in a single volume can be found using two rival chronologies at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The standard edition of the Linear B texts, Ventris and Chadwick's <i>Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i> of 1956 and 1973, was replaced only in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Knossos Linear B archive","questions":["Sheep are counted by one scribal hand and cloth by two others in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of its scribes keeping to a single commodity, place or group of people.","Above each shepherd in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands a second man, read by some as the flock's owner and by others as an official answerable to the palace."],"tags":["text","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Stavroula Nikoloudis, review of Hedvig Landenius Enegren, <i>The People of Knossos: Prosopographical Studies in the Knossos Linear B Archives</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2010.05.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.05.10/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of John Killen, ed., <i>The New Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.12.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.20/. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, eds., <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Linear_B_tablet_KN_Ra_1548_%28photo_and_tablet_drawing%29.png","title":"Knossos Linear B archive","desc":"Linear B tablet KN Ra 1548. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_B_tablet_KN_Ra_1548_(photo_and_tablet_drawing).png","alt":"Linear B tablet KN Ra 1548 (photo and tablet drawing)"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-053","num":"53","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"No single centre ruled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> again after the fire at Knossos, and the story from there is fragmentation and a material culture growing more regional.","answer":"Postpalatial Crete","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Knossos burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1385 – 1375 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Linear B in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to c. 1250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Postpalatial Crete</b> is the island after the fire that gutted Knossos early in Late Minoan IIIA2, about 1385 to 1375 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Everyone takes that fire for human work, since the signs of a Mycenaean military presence around Knossos vanish with it, and the lead in the southern Aegean passes to the mainland kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independent polities appear elsewhere on the island for the first time since the Late Minoan IB destructions, and the story from there is fragmentation and a material culture growing more regional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Mesara a huge megaron-like building went up at Ayia Triada over the two rich villas that had run the region before, with an outsize two-storeyed stoa 75 metres (82 yards) north-east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two thick-walled chamber tombs stand above ground nearby, of the form that becomes the island's commonest in these centuries, and the better preserved held the painted stone sarcophagus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> At Kommos, the harbour 16 kilometres (10 miles) south, ashlar from the old Neopalatial building was re-used to raise Building P, a six-gallery shed that is the largest structure of its period on Crete and seems to have housed ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ruins gave thousands of fragments of a plain transport amphora made for the job, and vases from Egypt, the Levant, Cyprus and Italy, none of which reached Ayia Triada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second kingdom shows in Linear B tablets from 13th-century levels at Chania in the west, ancient Kydonia, where the Bronze Age town lies beneath five later cities and the small finds outshine the buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> East Crete probably had polities of its own, its pottery being unlike that of the centre and west, while the wares of these centuries lose their lustrous dark-on-light decoration and turn largely plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Worship shrank to fit: the shrine of the double axes at Knossos is a metre and a half (5 feet) square and was left with its furniture standing, and the town shrine at Gournia belonged to no palace but stood alone near the top of the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Postpalatial Crete","questions":["A six-gallery shed at Kommos, the largest building raised anywhere on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was assembled from the cut ashlar of the palace it stood over and seems to have housed ships.","Linear B tablets from 13th-century levels at Chania mark out a second kingdom in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Bronze Age town lies beneath five later cities."],"tags":["era","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Sean Hemingway, review of Nicoletta Momigliano, ed., <i>Knossos Pottery Handbook: Neolithic and Bronze Age (Minoan)</i>, BSA Studies 14, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.05.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.05.21/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Clay_male_figurine%2C_hands_on_chest%2C_Crete%2C_Postpalatial%2C_AMH%2C_145258.jpg/1920px-Clay_male_figurine%2C_hands_on_chest%2C_Crete%2C_Postpalatial%2C_AMH%2C_145258.jpg","title":"Postpalatial Crete","desc":"Clay male figurine with hands on chest wearing an apron. Crete, Palaikastro, around 1300-1200 BCE, Postpalatial period. Archaeological Museum of Heraklion. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_male_figurine,_hands_on_chest,_Crete,_Postpalatial,_AMH,_145258.jpg","alt":"Clay male figurine, hands on chest, Crete, Postpalatial, AMH"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-054","num":"54","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Praisos, the town of the people who spoke <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is archaeologically ordinary: a handful of inscriptions apart, nothing there distinguishes it from any other Cretan city.","answer":"Eteocretan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscriptions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Eteocretan</b> is the name given to a pre-Greek language of eastern Crete, known from a handful of inscriptions cut in the Greek alphabet between the 6th and the 3rd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word is Homer's: in a line Strabo quotes, Crete holds a mixture of tongues, and among its peoples are Achaeans, Eteocretans proud of heart, Cydonians, Dorians and Pelasgians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Staphylus gave the Dorians the east of the island, the Cydonians the west and the Eteocretans the south, and put the other peoples in the plains because they were the stronger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo thought it reasonable that the Eteocretans and the Cydonians were the island's own and the rest incomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their town was Praisos, which held the temple of Dictaean Zeus, lay 60 stadia above the sea between Samonium and the Cherronesus, and was razed to the ground by the men of Hierapytna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Halbherr found the first of the inscriptions at the foot of the Altar Hill at Praisos in 1884, and another came out of the same hill in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are written in Greek letters and are not Greek, so every attempt on them has had to guess at the language behind the script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Conway, publishing the 1901 stone, worked on the hypothesis that the language was Indo-European, on the ground that its clusters of vowels and consonants and its word endings were all ordinary in such languages around 500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hardest modern finding about the Eteocretans is negative: apart from those few texts, nothing in the material remains of Praisos marks it out from any other Cretan city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A survey of its region has traced occupation at the town from about 1200 to 500 BCE and a refuge settlement in the hills nearby, so a people Homer names are visible in what they wrote and almost nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Eteocretan","questions":["The people whose language we call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the south of Crete in Staphylus' division, which gave the east to the Dorians and the west to the Cydonians.","One hypothesis about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, tried on a stone from the Altar Hill at Praisos, makes it an Indo-European language, judging by its endings and its letter clusters."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.6, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D6. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.12, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D12. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Post-Minoan Crete: Proceedings of the First Colloquium on Post-Minoan Crete</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2000.05.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.05.17/. [Open access]","R. S. Conway, \"The Pre-Hellenic Inscriptions of Praesos,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 125–56, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","R. C. Bosanquet, \"Excavations at Praesos. I,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 231–70, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Eteocretan_inscritpion_in_alphabet%2C_Praisos%2C_4_th_c_BC%2C_AM_Herakleon%2C_258462.jpg/1920px-Eteocretan_inscritpion_in_alphabet%2C_Praisos%2C_4_th_c_BC%2C_AM_Herakleon%2C_258462.jpg","title":"Eteocretan","desc":"Eteocretan inscritpion. Minoan language in the alphabet. Praisos, 4 th century BCE. Archaeological museum Iraklio (Herakleon). Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eteocretan_inscritpion_in_alphabet,_Praisos,_4_th_c_BC,_AM_Herakleon,_258462.jpg","alt":"Eteocretan inscritpion in alphabet, Praisos, 4 th c BCE, AM Herakleon"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-055","num":"55","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Minos went up to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> every ninth year, Strabo reports, and came back with written commandments he said were the god's own.","answer":"Idaean Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Idaean Cave</b> is a sanctuary on Mount Ida, the loftiest mountain in Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo puts Ida in the widest part of the island, roughly circular, with a circuit of six hundred stadia and the best of the Cretan cities around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It should not be confused with Dicte, though the ancients did: Strabo corrects Aratus for setting Dicte close to Ida when it lies a thousand stadia east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The cave was the cave of Zeus, and Strabo reports that Minos would go up to it every ninth year and come back with commandments in writing that he said were the god's own ordinances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the same mountain Strabo has the Idaean Dactyls, said to be the first settlers of its lower slopes, whom every version of the story credits with the first working of iron, though he records that others put them in Phrygia instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Worship there was old: Kamares ware, the eggshell pottery of the first Cretan palaces, has come from the mountain, which puts people on it by about 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Votive bronze double axes, the special emblem of the Cretan Zeus, have been found in cave sanctuaries of the god on Ida and on Dicte alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Caves were used for worship across the Greek world, and because a cave's rock contains everything that happens inside it, such places keep unusually direct traces of how people once moved and where they set objects down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A recent study of this cave, written by a member of its own excavation team, reads what was found there together with what the place itself does to anyone standing in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What the mountain kept in the end was its names: the smiths who were said to have struck the first iron on its slopes, and the god whose laws a king was said to have fetched down out of its cave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Idaean Cave","questions":["Votive bronze double axes, the special emblem of the Cretan Zeus, turn up in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and in the cave on Dicte alike, and Kamares ware puts people on the mountain by 1900 BCE.","Aratus set Dicte close to Ida and Strabo corrected him, which is one reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is so often confused with the other cave of the Cretan Zeus."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.4, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D4. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.8, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D8. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.12, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D12. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.3.22, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D22. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900), https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Carolyn Laferrière, review of Stella Katsarou and Alexander Nagel, eds., <i>Cave and Worship in Ancient Greece: New Approaches to Landscape and Ritual</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2022.10.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.03/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Idaean_Cave_panorama.jpg/1920px-Idaean_Cave_panorama.jpg","title":"Idaean Cave","desc":"Idaean Cave (Ideon Antron) or Cave of Zeus, Mount Ida (Psiloritis), Crete, Greece. Tomisti, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idaean_Cave_panorama.jpg","alt":"Idaean Cave panorama"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Idaean Cave","at":[24.7725,35.2267]}},
{"id":"gr-056","num":"56","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The palaces of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept their accounts in Linear B, an early form of Greek, and stood behind boulder walls of a kind the palaces of Crete never had.","answer":"Mycenaean civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shaft graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean civilisation</b> is the Late Bronze Age culture of the Greek mainland, named after Mycenae in the Argolid; specialists use the label interchangeably with Late Helladic, the mainland's own pottery sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens in a burst of wealth, the shaft graves at Mycenae holding gold and silver vessels, weapons, amber and stone vases richer than anything else found in the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of that metalwork is Minoan in shape and probably Cretan in making, so the first mainland princes seem to have risen through a close relationship with the palaces of Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their heirs were laid in tholos tombs, round chambers roofed by corbelled vaults and entered down a long passage, the earliest in Messenia and then in the Argolid, Laconia and Attica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Palaces followed from about 1400 BCE at Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos, Thebes, Gla, Orchomenos and Athens, each built around a megaron of porch, vestibule and throne room with a hearth ringed by four columns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Unlike Crete's, these palaces stood behind fortifications finished about 1250 BCE, of boulders so large that Strabo reports Tiryns as walled by the Cyclopes; the same rulers drained a whole Boeotian lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Their clerks wrote Greek in Linear B, and archives survive at Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos and Thebes and at Knossos and Chania on Crete, 3,369 tablets at Knossos alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> They record a state under a <i>wanax</i>, or king, with a <i>lawagetas</i> whose title means 'leader of the people', landholding <i>telestai</i>, and the <i>damos</i>, a village body that held land in common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its reach ran far beyond Greece, Baltic amber coming south from about 1700 BCE and the ship wrecked off Cape Gelidonya carrying copper and tin ingots, bronze scrap, pottery and weights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Then it ended in fire around 1200 BCE, Mycenae, Tiryns, Thebes and Pylos all burning, and specialists blame some combination of raiding, drought, revolt, lost trade and changed warfare rather than one cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean civilisation","questions":["Beginning in the shaft graves of the Argolid, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread across the mainland and reached Crete, where the Knossos archive was kept in Greek rather than in the island's older script.","Every mainland palace of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burned around 1200 BCE, and no single cause among raiding, drought, revolt, lost trade and changing warfare has been made to account for all of it."],"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.6.11, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D6%3Asection%3D11. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Facsimile_of_a_Mycenaean_fresco_%28Tiryns%29%2C_Woman_Carrying_an_Ivory_Pyxis%2C_by_Emile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_p%C3%A8re%2C_1912.jpg","title":"Mycenaean civilisation","desc":"Woman Carrying an Ivory Pyxis, Facsimile of reconstructed fresco from Tiryns, Greece. Source of identification. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facsimile_of_a_Mycenaean_fresco_(Tiryns),_Woman_Carrying_an_Ivory_Pyxis,_by_Emile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_p%C3%A8re,_1912.jpg","alt":"Facsimile of a Mycenaean fresco (Tiryns), Woman Carrying an Ivory Pyxis, by Emile Gilliéron père, 1912"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Mycenaean civilisation","at":[22.7575,37.7303],"kind":"region","label":[22.25,37.85],"area":[[20.45,38.85],[21.2,39.45],[22.3,39.75],[23.4,39.65],[23.55,39.05],[24.65,38.65],[24.35,37.65],[23.45,37.4],[23.35,36.4],[24.5,35.85],[26.55,35.65],[26.35,34.75],[23.35,34.85],[22.45,36.25],[21.6,36.65],[21,37.55],[20.75,38.3]]}},
{"id":"gr-057","num":"57","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A merchant who had made his fortune in indigo and tea, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut a great trench through Hisarlik in the 1870s and told the world he had found Priam's Troy.","answer":"Heinrich Schliemann","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 January 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 December 1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Excavated 1870 – 1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mycenae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Excavated from 1876</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Heinrich Schliemann</b>, born on 6 January 1822 at Neubukow in Mecklenburg-Schwerin, the son of a Protestant clergyman, was the merchant who put Troy and Mycenae into the historical record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He learned languages as a clerk in Amsterdam after a shipwreck stranded him in 1841, traded indigo and tea from St Petersburg, and was out of business altogether by 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In his autobiography he said that as a boy he had settled on digging up Troy, a story his modern biographers read as a piece of staging rather than a memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He turned the first sod at Hisarlik in April 1870 and drove a huge trench down through the mound, demolishing the upper strata, by his own account, to reach the city he expected below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1873 he cut a hoard of gold out of the wall with a knife while his workmen rested and carried it off in his wife's shawl; the Ottoman government was owed half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He called it 'Priam's Treasure', the largest building 'Priam's Palace' and the entrance 'the Scaean Gate', and those three names were enough for most scholars to dismiss the whole book.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Mycenae from July 1876 he opened graves inside the citadel and lifted out gold masks, diadems and breastplates, taking the dead for Agamemnon and the companions murdered with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both identifications were out by centuries: those graves hold burials of about 1650 to 1500 BCE, and the second city he took for Priam's long predates the Troy VIIa or VIh that specialists now prefer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He dug on at Tiryns, sinking shafts in 1876 and clearing the palace from 1884, and at Orchomenos, latterly with Wilhelm Dörpfeld, and he died suddenly at Naples on 26 December 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His reputation has never settled: work by Calder and Traill made him a pathological liar for many, while others answer that his mistakes were a pioneer's and that before him the Bronze Age Aegean was unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Heinrich Schliemann","questions":["At Mycenae in 1876 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lifted gold masks, diadems and breastplates out of graves inside the citadel and took the dead for Agamemnon and the companions murdered with him.","Cutting a hoard out of the wall with a knife while his workmen rested, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried it off in his wife's shawl, keeping a find the Ottoman government was owed half of."],"tags":["person","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Henry Schliemann, <i>Ilios: The City and Country of the Trojans</i> (London: John Murray, 1880), 1–4, 40–41, 59, https://archive.org/details/ilioscitycountry1880schl. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 1–14, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Henry Schliemann, <i>Mycenae: A Narrative of Researches and Discoveries at Mycenae and Tiryns</i> (London: John Murray, 1878), preface by W. E. Gladstone, https://archive.org/details/mycenaenarrative00schl. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 27: Troy VII and the Historicity of the Trojan War,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-27-narrative/. [Open access]","Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of Stefanie Samida, <i>Heinrich Schliemann</i>, UTB Profile, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2012.12.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.12.25/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Daheim_Kalender_1892_-_Johann_Ludwig_Heinrich_Julius_Schliemann.png","title":"Heinrich Schliemann","desc":"An illustration of Heinrich Schliemann, from a German almanac of 1892. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daheim_Kalender_1892_-_Johann_Ludwig_Heinrich_Julius_Schliemann.png","alt":"Daheim Kalender 1892 - Johann Ludwig Heinrich Julius Schliemann"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"gr-058","num":"58","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Homer calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rich in gold and makes it Agamemnon's seat, and its walls of boulders were credited by later Greeks to the Cyclopes.","answer":"Mycenae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tholos tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1525 – 1275 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walls finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Razed by Argos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">468 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenae</b> is the citadel above the Argive plain that gave its name to the civilisation of Late Bronze Age Greece, and which Homer makes Agamemnon's seat and calls rich in gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greeks made Perseus its founder, and Pausanias gives two accounts of the name, the cap that fell from his scabbard there, and the mushroom he drank from, both called <i>myces</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its walls are of boulders so large that the Greeks credited them to the Cyclopes, first raised late in the 14th century BCE and finished in their final form around 1250.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Inside them a palace stood on the summit, dominating the highest point of the citadel and sprawling over artificial terraces, above the shaft graves of much earlier kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nine great tholos tombs lie around the citadel, built between about 1525 and 1275 BCE, the largest 14.5 metres (48 feet) across and faced with sculpture in relief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> A destruction level inside the walls marks the end of its palatial age around 1200 BCE, when Tiryns, Thebes and Pylos burned as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> People stayed on, and when Xerxes invaded, Mycenae alone in the Argolid sent men to fight beside the Spartans at Thermopylae, eighty of them by Pausanias's count, while Argos held back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Argos had refused to march unless given a share of the command, and it feared that a Mycenae trading on its ancient prestige would come to dispute the leadership of the Argolid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 468 BCE the Argives besieged and stormed the town, sold its people into slavery, dedicated a tenth of them to the god and razed it, though the walls were beyond removing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias still saw them six centuries later, and the gate with the lions; Thucydides had already warned that Mycenae was small, and that a city's power is not read off its external show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenae","questions":["Alone in the Argolid, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent eighty men to fight beside the Spartans at Thermopylae, and Argos, which had held back, besieged and razed it in 468 BCE.","Pausanias gives two accounts of how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> got its name, one from the cap that fell from Perseus's scabbard there and one from the mushroom he drank from, both called <i>myces</i>."],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 7.180, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D7%3Acard%3D181. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.3–6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.65, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0084%3Abook%3D11%3Achapter%3D65. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.10, trans. Thomas Hobbes, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0247%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Eberzahnhelm_auf_Fresko_aus_Mykene_140.jpg/1920px-Eberzahnhelm_auf_Fresko_aus_Mykene_140.jpg","title":"Mycenae","desc":"Boar tusk helmets on fresco from Mycenae. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eberzahnhelm_auf_Fresko_aus_Mykene_140.jpg","alt":"Eberzahnhelm auf Fresko aus Mykene 140"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Mycenae","at":[22.7575,37.7303]}},
{"id":"gr-059","num":"59","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Two beasts with their forepaws on altars flank a column in the triangle above the door of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the main entrance to the citadel of Mycenae.","answer":"Lion Gate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still standing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lion Gate</b> is the main entrance to the citadel of Mycenae, named for the two opposing animals carved in relief and set in the triangle above its door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek ministry that keeps the site calls it the symbol of the Mycenaean rulers' power, the acropolis is entered through it, and the ramp up to the palace begins there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relieving triangle it fills is a typical feature of Mycenaean architecture, an opening left above the lintel so that the wall above does not bear on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two beasts stand with their forepaws on altars either side of a column, which carries a capital and the beam-ends of a roof above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their heads are gone, and the closest study of the tool marks reconstructs them as having turned backward, out of the face of the relief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That study, by Nicholas Blackwell, reads tubular drills and saws in the surface, a large pendulum saw and a smaller convex blade, and finds the methods paralleled in central Anatolia, which raises the question of Hittite contact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The gate belongs with the final fortifications, finished about 1250 BCE, and where Tiryns and Gla built ramps up to their gates Mycenae used a fold of its own ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its approach is a narrow passage between wall and tower, so an attacker at the door could be shot at from both sides; the inner gate at Tiryns is virtually identical in plan and elevation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it means has long been argued: the lions have been read as the military might of those holding the walls, and Evans took them for the sacred animals of a deity whose image the column was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was never lost, unlike almost all else Mycenaean, for Pausanias saw the gate with the lions still standing in the ruined wall, and put it down, like the wall, to the Cyclopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lion Gate","questions":["Pausanias saw the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still standing in the ruined wall in the 2nd century CE and put it down, like the wall itself, to the Cyclopes.","The heads on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are missing, and a study of its tool marks reconstructs them as having turned backward out of the face of the relief."],"tags":["building","art","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 58–60, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]","Nicholas G. Blackwell, \"Making the Lion Gate Relief at Mycenae: Tool Marks and Foreign Influence,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 118, no. 3 (2014): 451–88, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.118.3.0451. [Paywalled]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 140–41, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Underwood_%26_Underwood_-_The_Lion_Gate_at_Mycenae_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Underwood_%26_Underwood_-_The_Lion_Gate_at_Mycenae_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Lion Gate","desc":"Underwood & Underwood - The Lion Gate at Mycenae. Underwood & Underwood Details on Google Art Project. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Underwood_%26_Underwood_-_The_Lion_Gate_at_Mycenae_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"Underwood & Underwood - The Lion Gate at Mycenae"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-060","num":"60","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The slabs ringing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut a corner off two of its six shaft graves, which proves the ring was raised after the graves were already there.","answer":"Grave Circle A","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Grave Circle A</b> is the ring of upright slabs standing inside the citadel of Mycenae, a little south of the Lion Gate, around six shaft graves cut into the rock beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The graves are the older part of it, dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE, and they held gold and silver vessels, weapons, amber, stone vases and seals, with gold masks laid over the faces of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ring is a double row of slabs about a metre (3 feet) apart, capped with horizontal slabs and packed between with stone and earth, so what looks like a fence is the facing of a solid wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An entrance was left at the point nearest the Lion Gate, and in the fill above the graves stood carved stelae and a round altar, set precisely over the centre of the fourth grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ring is later than the graves it rings, and the proof is that it cuts a corner off two of them, which it could not have done had it stood there first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The citadel's final walls were finished about 1250 BCE, and the circle stands inside them, where Grave Circle B, the other and slightly older cemetery, lies outside to the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the Mycenaeans of the palace age kept an old burial ground inside their fortress and raised a monument over it, centuries after the last body had gone in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias wrote that Agamemnon and those murdered with him lay within the wall, while Clytemnestra and Aegisthus were buried a little outside it, thought unworthy of a place inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was that passage, on Schuchhardt's account, that convinced Schliemann the graves of the house of Atreus lay inside the citadel rather than outside it, and sent him digging there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was right about the place and out by three centuries or more about the people; what the circle shows is mainland lords buried in gold long before Mycenae had a palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Grave Circle A","questions":["Pausanias put Agamemnon's grave inside the wall of Mycenae and Clytemnestra's outside it, and that sentence sent Schliemann digging where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to light.","Dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE, the six graves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held gold and silver vessels, weapons, amber and stone vases, with gold masks laid over the faces of the dead."],"tags":["building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 5, 138, 152–57, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.6–7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/The_Grave_Circle_A_%2816th_cent._B.C.%29_in_the_citadel_of_Mycenae_on_26_March_2019.jpg/1920px-The_Grave_Circle_A_%2816th_cent._B.C.%29_in_the_citadel_of_Mycenae_on_26_March_2019.jpg","title":"Grave Circle A","desc":"Grave Circle A in the citadel of Mycenae, the double ring of upright slabs enclosing the shaft graves. George E. Koronaios, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Grave_Circle_A_(16th_cent._B.C.)_in_the_citadel_of_Mycenae_on_26_March_2019.jpg","alt":"Grave Circle A at Mycenae seen from above: a double ring of upright stone slabs enclosing a sunken area of shaft graves, with the citadel wall behind and the hillside beyond"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-061","num":"61","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Fourteen shaft graves lie inside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the older of Mycenae's two circles, which was left outside the fortification walls west of the Lion Gate.","answer":"Grave Circle B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Grave Circle B</b> is the older of the two shaft-grave cemeteries at Mycenae, lying outside the fortification walls west of the Lion Gate and enclosing fourteen shaft graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was in use from about 1650 to 1550 BCE, a generation or two ahead of Grave Circle A, though one chronology compresses both into the same span of 1600 to 1500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both were the resting place of the Mycenaean elite and yielded impressive burial goods, so the two are the same elite's burying ground a few generations apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One man, buried in Grave Gamma, wore a face-mask of electrum, the natural alloy of gold and silver, earlier than any of the gold masks from the circle inside the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From Tomb N there survives a very degraded dark fabric that still shows patterns woven in with an extra weft, in the tapestry technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The features that mark the latest graves here, the carved stelae, the masks, the signs of chariots, are the features of Circle A, so the one cemetery runs on into the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It sits in the lower town among four of Mycenae's nine tholos tombs, and about fifty metres (160 feet) from the houses whose Linear B tablets record workers, oil and perfumes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is the contrast with Circle A: the citadel wall keeps that one inside, while this older cemetery was left outside among the tombs and the workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its skeletons have twice been used to ask whether those buried together were relatives: seven skulls were rebuilt as faces in 1995, which found the man in the electrum mask to resemble the woman beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then in 2008 ancient DNA was typed from the same cemetery, and that study too reported kinship between the burials, a question the gold and the masks could never settle on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Grave Circle B","questions":["A man buried in Grave Gamma at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wore a face-mask of electrum, earlier than any of the gold masks from the circle inside the citadel wall.","Seven skulls from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were rebuilt as faces in 1995 and ancient DNA was typed from it in 2008, both asking whether those buried together were relatives."],"tags":["building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Stella Spantidaki, \"Elite Fabrics from the Grave Circles of Mycenae,\" <i>CHS Research Bulletin</i> (Washington, DC: Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 2022), https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2022/08/29/elite-fabrics-from-the-grave-circles-of-mycenae/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","J. H. Musgrave, R. A. H. Neave and A. J. N. W. Prag, \"Seven Faces from Grave Circle B at Mycenae,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 90 (1995): 107–36, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400016105. [Paywalled]","Abigail S. Bouwman, Keri A. Brown, A. John N. W. Prag and Terence A. Brown, \"Kinship between Burials from Grave Circle B at Mycenae Revealed by Ancient DNA Typing,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 35, no. 9 (2008): 2580–84, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jas.2008.04.010. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Findings_from_Grave_%CE%91_of_Grave_Circle_B_1.JPG/1920px-Findings_from_Grave_%CE%91_of_Grave_Circle_B_1.JPG","title":"Grave Circle B","desc":"Archaeological Museum of Mycenae: Type A bronze sword from shaft grave Alpha of grave circle B in Mycenae. About 1625-1600 BCE (MH III B). Schuppi, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Findings_from_Grave_%CE%91_of_Grave_Circle_B_1.JPG","alt":"Findings from Grave Α of Grave Circle B 1"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-062","num":"62","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Found over the face of a man in Shaft Grave V at Mycenae, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.","answer":"Mask of Agamemnon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mask of Agamemnon</b> is the gold funerary mask found over the face of a man in Shaft Grave V of Grave Circle A at Mycenae, and now in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He lay at the south end of the grave, and the face on the mask is well formed, with the eyes set close together, a long delicate nose and thin lips.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eyes are ringed by a double rim, and the line running across them, marking where the lids meet, shows that they are shut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eyebrows and the full beard are raised in relief and then worked over with sunk lines to make them more lifelike, though the eyelashes were left unmarked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Schuchhardt took the closed eyes as proof that these masks were portraits of the men they covered, and found the gold plate too thick to have been simply moulded over one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The other mask from the same grave is beardless and almost spherical, and the cutting of its brows and mouth he put down to a want of skill or care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that he concluded the two were not made by the same hand, and that the bodies can scarcely have been buried at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The graves of Circle A were dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE, and the man in the mask went into the ground with a gold breastplate, amber beads and a gold band still round his arm bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a 19th-century attribution, from Schliemann taking the Circle A dead for Agamemnon and his murdered companions, and it is out by some three centuries against any date for a Trojan War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Schuchhardt, writing in 1891, still called it simply 'the bearded mask'; the other name stuck, and the face has become the image of the Aegean Bronze Age, the <i>Mona Lisa</i> of prehistory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mask of Agamemnon","questions":["The closed eyes and the full beard worked over with sunk lines set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> apart from the beardless mask found at the other end of the same grave.","Schliemann's name for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is out by some three centuries, since the graves of Circle A were dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE."],"tags":["object","art","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 5, 225–26, 256–59, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 27: Troy VII and the Historicity of the Trojan War,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-27-narrative/. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","James P. Holoka, review of Cathy Gere, <i>The Tomb of Agamemnon</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.12.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.12.22/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Athens_%E2%80%94_Mask_of_Agamemnon.jpg/1920px-Athens_%E2%80%94_Mask_of_Agamemnon.jpg","title":"Mask of Agamemnon","desc":"Athens — Mask of Agamemnon. Gleb Simonov. 2024-01-23. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_%E2%80%94_Mask_of_Agamemnon.jpg","alt":"Athens — Mask of Agamemnon"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-063","num":"63","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a deep rectangular pit cut straight down into rock, the dead laid at the bottom under a roof of slabs and the shaft filled in above them.","answer":"shaft grave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>shaft grave</b> is a deep rectangular pit cut straight down into rock, with the dead laid at the bottom and the shaft filled in over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The twenty at Mycenae, six in Grave Circle A and fourteen in Circle B, are the ones the type is known by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their sides stand 3 to 5 metres (10 to 16 feet) high, and the plan runs from about 2.7 by 3 metres (9 by 10 feet) in the smallest to 5 by 6.7 in the largest, which held one body and five respectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The walls were lined with small quarry-stones and clay, and the bodies bedded in river pebbles among their ornaments, tools and weapons, then covered over with fine clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lid of slate slabs rested on one or two heavy beams laid across the shaft, whose ends were shod in sheet copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When the beams rotted the lid fell in, and that is what disturbed the contents; it had been read as the disorder of a hurried burial until someone asked where the fallen slabs had been lying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above ground each grave was marked by a limestone stele about 1.5 metres (5 feet) high, some carved in low relief and others left as plain polished slabs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Schliemann read the smoke-blackened walls, the ashes and the unburnt wood as cremation carried out inside the tomb, but parts of the bodies came through, so they were not burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The graves belong to about 1650 to 1500 BCE and the type then gives way to the tholos tomb, whose earliest examples are in Messenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The shafts filled up and were forgotten, so that the graves Pausanias was shown as Agamemnon's were not these but the tholos tombs still standing above ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"shaft grave","questions":["The lid of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was slate slabs on one or two heavy beams, and when those rotted it fell in, which is what disturbed the contents rather than a hurried burial.","Each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Mycenae was marked above ground by a limestone stele about 1.5 metres (5 feet) high, some carved in low relief and others left as plain polished slabs."],"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 157–61, 167–70, 176, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.6–7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Grave-Circle-A-Mycenae.jpg/1920px-Grave-Circle-A-Mycenae.jpg","title":"shaft grave","desc":"The Grave Circle A, and the main entrance of the citadel (left), at Mycenae. Andreas Trepte, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave-Circle-A-Mycenae.jpg","alt":"Grave-Circle-A-Mycenae"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-064","num":"64","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a round burial chamber roofed by a corbelled vault, built into a hillside and entered along an open passage called a dromos.","answer":"tholos tomb","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1525 – 1275 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>tholos tomb</b> is a round burial chamber roofed by a corbelled vault, built of stone into a hillside and entered along an open passage called a dromos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Corbelling means laying each course of stone a little further in than the one below until the courses meet overhead, and Mycenaean builders carried it further than anyone: spans of eight metres (26 feet) are ordinary and the largest tombs pass fourteen (46 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The doorway is monumental, framed by mouldings cut with a saw rather than a chisel, with half-columns standing on low plinths either side of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine of them stand at Mycenae, built between about 1525 and 1275 BCE, and Alan Wace, who dug the site, sorted them into three groups by their masonry and their ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four lie near Grave Circle B, the tombs of the Lions, of Aegisthus and of Clytemnestra, with the Treasury of Atreus further south, which the ministry that keeps the site calls the most perfect example of the type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest are in Messenia, and the form spreads from there to the Argolid, Laconia and Attica, staying rare in Boeotia and reaching the Cyclades once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were built for the uppermost rank of Mycenaean society, and as fewer sites go on raising them they mark where power has gathered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They take over from the shaft grave, and standing above ground rather than under it they stayed open, which is why the Treasury of Atreus was rifled long before anyone thought to record what was in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias calls them the underground chambers of Atreus and his children, in which their treasures were stored, and the name treasury stayed on them for as long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tomb at Menidi in Attica settled it in 1879, when a building of exactly this kind was opened north of Athens and found to hold six bodies lying undisturbed with their ornaments about them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"tholos tomb","questions":["Pausanias called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Mycenae underground chambers where the treasures of Atreus were stored, and the name treasury stuck until Menidi in 1879 proved them tombs.","Pausanias called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Mycenae underground chambers where the treasures of Atreus were stored, and the name treasury stuck until one opened at Menidi in Attica in 1879 held six undisturbed bodies."],"tags":["building","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), xxiv, 143–48, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","W. G. Cavanagh and R. R. Laxton, \"The Structural Mechanics of the Mycenaean Tholos Tomb,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 76 (1981): 109–40, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400019493. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Schatzhaus_des_Atreus%2C_Querschnitt.jpg","title":"tholos tomb","desc":"Schatzhaus des Atreus, Querschnitt. Treasure of Atreus. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schatzhaus_des_Atreus,_Querschnitt.jpg","alt":"Schatzhaus des Atreus, Querschnitt"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-065","num":"65","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The largest tholos tomb at Mycenae, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is entered along a 35-metre (115-foot) passage and roofed by 33 courses of stone closed at the top by a single slab.","answer":"Treasury of Atreus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1275 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Treasury of Atreus</b> is the biggest and best preserved of the nine tholos tombs at Mycenae, half-way down the eastern slope of the hill, and it is popularly called the Tomb of Agamemnon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dromos 35 metres (115 feet) long and 6 wide runs level into the hillside between walls of ashlar masonry and ends at a façade about 14 metres (46 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The doorway is 5.4 metres (17 feet 9 inches) high, framed by three bands cut with a saw, with half-columns of dark grey alabaster either side, their shafts thickening upward and carved with spirals, triangles and lozenges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans traced the fluted foliation of their capitals and the inlay that went with it to contemporary Egypt, comparing capitals from Akhenaten's palace at Amarna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Over the door a relieving triangle with sides 3 metres (10 feet) long was closed by courses of red porphyry cut with spirals, and dowel holes show the whole façade was faced in something further, marbles or bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The inner lintel is a single block of about 120 tonnes (120 tons), which had to be quarried, moved to the spot, worked accurately and laid to a close fit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside, the chamber is about 15 metres (50 feet) across and as high, built in 33 horizontal courses each stepped in from the one below and closed at the top by a single slab.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stones are cut to the curve and polished, so the line runs unbroken from floor to apex, and bronze nails still sit in holes across the vault, which carried scattered ornaments rather than the metal sheathing once imagined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belongs to the last of the three groups Wace sorted the Mycenae tholoi into, which run to about 1275 BCE, and it was empty when opened, rifled long before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its façade is now split between London and Athens, and Pausanias knew the building as a chamber where the treasure of Atreus was kept, which is the name it still carries although the ministry that keeps the site calls it a tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Treasury of Atreus","questions":["A single block of about 120 tonnes (120 tons) spans the doorway of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and dowel holes show its façade once carried marble or bronze on top of the carving.","Pausanias knew the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a chamber where the treasure of Atreus was kept, and the name has stayed on it although the building is a tomb."],"tags":["building","art","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 143–48, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 32–34, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/The_tholos_tomb_Treasury_of_Atreus_or_Tomb_of_Agamemnon_in_Mycenae_on_March_26%2C_2019.jpg/1920px-The_tholos_tomb_Treasury_of_Atreus_or_Tomb_of_Agamemnon_in_Mycenae_on_March_26%2C_2019.jpg","title":"Treasury of Atreus","desc":"The tholos tomb of Agamemnon in Mycenae. Argolis, Greece. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_tholos_tomb_Treasury_of_Atreus_or_Tomb_of_Agamemnon_in_Mycenae_on_March_26,_2019.jpg","alt":"The walled approach, doorway and relieving triangle of the Treasury of Atreus, with its mound behind"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-066","num":"66","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Greeks credited <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Cyclopes: Strabo says Proetus walled Tiryns with the help of seven of them, called 'Bellyhands' and brought in from Lycia.","answer":"Cyclopean masonry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Walls finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cyclopean masonry</b> is walling built of huge unworked limestone boulders roughly fitted together, with the gaps between them packed out by smaller stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the ordinary fortification masonry of Mycenaean Greece, at Mycenae, Tiryns, Argos, Krisa and the Athenian acropolis, with somewhat smaller stones at Midea and large slabs at Gla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The walls it makes are enormously thick: at Mycenae 3 to 7 metres (10 to 23 feet) where both faces can still be traced, and as much as 14 (46 feet) along two stretches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Tiryns, where the circuit stands best, the broadest stretches hold galleries inside them like casemates, and traces of the same have since been found in the north wall at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cut stone is kept for the gateways, and a third masonry of polygonal blocks with very close joints appears at particular points of the circuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest of these circuits go up late in the 14th century BCE, and the final systems were finished around 1250.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is not a modern coinage but a Greek explanation, since the stones were held to be past the strength of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strabo says Proetus used Tiryns as a base and walled it with the help of the Cyclopes, seven of them, called 'Bellyhands' because they got their food by their craft, and fetched in from Lycia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias says the same at Mycenae of the surviving wall and of the gate with the lions on it, pointing back to the Cyclopes who had built for Proetus at Tiryns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The technique reaches well beyond the Argolid: at Troy a stretch of wall is of great unwrought blocks with the interstices filled by smaller stones, exactly as at Tiryns and Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cyclopean masonry","questions":["<span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is walling of huge unworked limestone boulders roughly fitted together, the gaps packed with smaller stones, and it is the ordinary fortification masonry of Mycenaean Greece.","At Tiryns the broadest stretches of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hold galleries inside them like casemates, and at Mycenae the walls run 3 to 7 metres (10 to 23 feet) thick and as much as 14 in places."],"tags":["practice","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 54, 136–38, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.6.11, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D6%3Asection%3D11. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/The_Lion_Gate_on_26_March_2019.jpg/1920px-The_Lion_Gate_on_26_March_2019.jpg","title":"Cyclopean masonry","desc":"The Lion Gate. Mycenae. Argolis, Greece. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Lion_Gate_on_26_March_2019.jpg","alt":"The Lion Gate on 26 March 2019"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-067","num":"67","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Homer's Catalogue of Ships calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> famed for its walls, and Pausanias found that the wall was all of it that still stood.","answer":"Tiryns","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Walls finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tiryns</b> is a fortified citadel on a low limestone rock in the Argive plain, which Homer's Catalogue of Ships names alongside Argos and calls famed for its walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rock is about 300 metres (328 yards) long and 100 (109 yards) wide, standing 18 metres (59 feet) above the plain and isolated on every side, and it falls from south to north in three terraces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The palace stood on the upper terrace at the south, filling the whole space inside the walls, while the middle citadel carried small dwellings and the lower one was left undug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Found in excellent preservation, its plan is the Homeric house: a forecourt with colonnades and an altar, a megaron with hearth and columns and an ante-room, and a bathroom close by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There is also a second, smaller set of the same rooms, reached from the first only with difficulty, which its excavators took for the women's quarters, an arrangement Homer knows nothing of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The walls are the thing here, standing better than anywhere else, with galleries running inside the broadest stretches; the palace within kept its wall-paintings and an alabaster frieze along the vestibule of the men's hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its first circuit belongs to the later 14th century and the last was complete by about 1250 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Strabo makes the place the base of operations of Proetus, the king for whom the Cyclopes were said to have raised the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias, who came to the ruins, reports the wall as the only part still remaining, a work of the Cyclopes of unwrought stones so large that a pair of mules could not move the smallest of them; and he notices that small stones were set in to bind the large blocks together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tirynthians themselves were removed by the Argives, who wanted Argos larger, so the town went the way of Mycenae and only the walls that had made it famous stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tiryns","questions":["A Mycenaean palace stands on the upper terrace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a forecourt, a megaron and a bathroom laid out as Homer describes a great house.","Pausanias said the stones of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were so big that a pair of mules could not shift the smallest, and noticed the small stones set in to bind the big blocks."],"tags":["place","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 2.559, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D2%3Acard%3D546. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.25.8, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.6.11, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D6%3Asection%3D11. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), xxi–xxii, 14, 96–97, 107, 117, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/Walls_of_Tiryns_-_Dodwell_Edward_-_1834.jpg","title":"Tiryns","desc":"Edward Dodwell. Views and Descriptions of Cyclopian, or, Pelasgic Remains, in Greece and Italy; with Constructions of a later Period, London, Adolphus Richter, MDCCCXXXIV (1834). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walls_of_Tiryns_-_Dodwell_Edward_-_1834.jpg","alt":"Walls of Tiryns - Dodwell Edward - 1834"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Tiryns","at":[22.8,37.5994]}},
{"id":"gr-068","num":"68","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Homer puts Nestor at Pylos, the well-built citadel of Neleus, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Ano Englianos in Messenia is the Mycenaean palace that carries his name.","answer":"Palace of Nestor","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Palace of Nestor</b> is the Mycenaean palace at Ano Englianos in Messenia, which the Greek ministry responsible for it calls the best preserved of them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Homer puts Nestor at Pylos, the well-built citadel of Neleus, where Telemachus comes ashore to ask the old king for news of his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is a complex rather than one building, four main structures — a south-west block, a central building, a north-east block and a wine store — with 105 ground-floor rooms between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its heart is the throne room, a great rectangle whose central hearth is ringed by four columns, with the ruler's throne standing against one wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Near it are a room holding a clay bath tub and storerooms full of vessels, and the walls throughout carried fine paintings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its archive holds 1,107 clay tablets written in Linear B by 32 different scribes, the most substantial collection anywhere on the mainland, and it is what tells us what the place did and who worked for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site was located by Konstantinos Kourouniotis in 1939 and dug by Carl Blegen of the University of Cincinnati, the work broken off almost at once by the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The palace reached the height of its prosperity in the 13th century BCE and was destroyed by fire in the early years of the 12th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Exactly when is not settled: the burning is put either late in Late Helladic IIIB or somewhere early in IIIC, and the pottery will not decide between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What followed is not in doubt, for the emptying of Messenia in the century after is more striking than in any other part of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Palace of Nestor","questions":["The archive of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds 1,107 tablets in Linear B written by 32 different scribes, and its throne room has a circular hearth ringed by four columns.","A complex of 105 ground-floor rooms in four main buildings, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burned early in the 12th century BCE, after which Messenia emptied more strikingly than anywhere else."],"tags":["building","place","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Palace of Nestor: Description,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","\"Palace of Nestor: History,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh351.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Odyssey</i> 3.4–20, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0136%3Abook%3D3%3Acard%3D1. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%AC%CF%84%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B1_-_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%AD%CE%B3%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%80%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%83%CF%84%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg/1920px-%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%AC%CF%84%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B1_-_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%AD%CE%B3%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%80%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%83%CF%84%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg","title":"Palace of Nestor","desc":"Παλάτι του Νέστορα - Νέο στέγαστρο προστασίας. 2016-08-18. Dimitris19933, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%AC%CF%84%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B1_-_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%AD%CE%B3%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%80%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%83%CF%84%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg","alt":"Παλάτι του Νέστορα - Νέο στέγαστρο προστασίας"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Palace of Nestor","at":[21.695,37.0272]}},
{"id":"gr-069","num":"69","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"About 250 clay tablets came out of the Armoury at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1993, which makes it one of only four mainland sites to have produced a Linear B archive.","answer":"Mycenaean Thebes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Thebes</b> is the palace centre on the Kadmeia, the low citadel of Thebes in Boeotia, and one of the most important administrative centres of the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is also the least recoverable of them, since the town has been lived in without a break and a modern one stands over the ancient remains, which the ministry says makes a full investigation of the site impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is known has come instead from rescue digs scattered through the streets, and the palace is known in parts rather than as a plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Called the palace of Kadmos, it stood near the middle of the citadel, its painted annexes and departments covering a great part of the hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among the parts recovered are a Room of the Treasure and an Armoury, and the site has given up inscribed stirrup jars as well as tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> About 250 clay tablets came out of the Armoury between 1993 and 1995, and Thebes is one of only four places on the mainland to have produced a Linear B archive, beside Mycenae, Tiryns and Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The palace belongs to the 14th and 13th centuries BCE, and the bulk of it was probably destroyed by fire late in Late Helladic IIIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Of the seven gates the tradition gives Thebes, only the entrance between the two round towers of the Electran Gates still stands, though stretches of the Mycenaean wall survive where two others were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The epic may remember something about the place: Homer's Catalogue of Ships runs through the Boeotian towns and Thebes is not among them, only lower Thebe, the well-built citadel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the hills at Kastellia stands a monumental chamber tomb known as the tomb of the sons of King Oedipus, a Mycenaean grave carrying the name of the city's own myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean Thebes","questions":["Homer's Catalogue of Ships runs through the Boeotian towns without naming Thebes, listing only lower Thebe, while the palace of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burned late in the 13th century BCE.","A modern town sits over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its palace is known in parts — painted annexes, a Room of the Treasure and an Armoury — rather than as a plan."],"tags":["place","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Thebes: History,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2459. [Open access]","\"Thebes: Monuments,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh351.jsp?obj_id=2459. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 2.505, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D2%3Acard%3D494. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Vue_Nord_Ville_-_Th%C3%A8bes_%28GR03%29_-_2022-03-21_-_3.jpg/1920px-Vue_Nord_Ville_-_Th%C3%A8bes_%28GR03%29_-_2022-03-21_-_3.jpg","title":"Mycenaean Thebes","desc":"Vue du nord de la ville, Thèbes. Chabe01, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_Nord_Ville_-_Th%C3%A8bes_(GR03)_-_2022-03-21_-_3.jpg","alt":"Vue Nord Ville - Thèbes (GR03) - 2022-03-21 - 3"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Thebes","at":[23.3178,38.3208]}},
{"id":"gr-070","num":"70","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The third fortified citadel of the Argolid, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a Cyclopean wall like those of Mycenae and Tiryns but built of somewhat smaller stones.","answer":"Midea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Midea</b> is the third of the fortified Mycenaean citadels of the Argolid, standing behind Mycenae and Tiryns in fame and holding a Cyclopean wall like theirs, though built of somewhat smaller stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its circuit has two gates, one east and one west, and the excavation divides the acropolis between those two areas and the lower terraces below them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A megaron on those terraces shows two successive phases within Late Helladic IIIB, the second of them with a large hearth set slightly off centre and four columns standing round it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A shrine area lies hard against the Cyclopean wall, as the religious centre does at Mycenae, and it gave up a miniature tripod offering table and a naturalistic female figure in terracotta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery imported from western Anatolia turned up on the site as well, along with four sword pommels of early Mycenaean date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Late Helladic IIIB at Midea was ended by a destruction caused by an earthquake and the fires that came with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mycenae, Tiryns and Thebes burned at about the same point, around 1200 BCE, and the wider pattern is what the argument is now about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Explanations by one coordinated cause have largely been given up in favour of a confluence of disasters, read site by site rather than across the whole map at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pausanias makes Midea part of the share of Proetus, who took the Heraeum, Midea, Tiryns and the Argive coast when the sons of Abas divided the kingdom between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He passed the place himself on the road from Argos and found nothing of it left but the foundations, adding that Electryon, the father of Alcmena, had once been its king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Midea","questions":["Late Helladic IIIB at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was ended by an earthquake and the fires that came with it, and Pausanias later found nothing of the place but its foundations.","A shrine against the wall at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up a miniature tripod offering table and a terracotta figure of a woman, and its megaron had a hearth ringed by four columns."],"tags":["place","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of Gisela Walberg, <i>Midea: The Megaron Complex and Shrine Area. Excavations on the Lower Terraces 1994–1997</i>, Prehistory Monographs 20, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2008.03.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.03.43/. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.25.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Trevor Van Damme, review of Reinhard Jung and Eleftheria Kardamaki, eds., <i>Synchronizing the Destructions of the Mycenaean Palaces</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.04.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.04.12/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Midea.JPG/1920px-Midea.JPG","title":"Midea","desc":"Midea archeological site. Stellea, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Midea.JPG","alt":"Midea archeological site"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Midea","at":[22.8414,37.6497]}},
{"id":"gr-071","num":"71","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Standing on a limestone island in the drained Kopais basin, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is unusual among Mycenaean fortresses in having four major gates at roughly the cardinal points.","answer":"Gla","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Gla</b> is a Mycenaean fortress on a low limestone island rising from the floor of the Kopais basin in Boeotia, near its north-east corner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a fortified administrative centre and military strongpoint set there to protect the drainage network that had turned a lake into farmland, and a building of the palace kind stood inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its walls are of large limestone slabs rather than the boulders used in the Argolid, and they went up all at once early in Late Helladic IIIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is unusual in having four major gates at roughly the cardinal points, where an ordinary Mycenaean fortress has one main entrance and a postern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each of them is reached by a long, fairly steep, artificially built ramp, as at Tiryns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The scale of the undertaking, this citadel and the draining of the basin together, dwarfs any other Mycenaean building project yet known, the northern dyke alone running up to 66 metres (217 feet) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The basin was remembered in the epic geography, since Homer's Catalogue of Ships lists Copae among the Boeotian towns, the name the lake itself carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who did the work is argued over: the standard account attaches Gla and the Kopais to Orchomenos, a major rival of Thebes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kramer-Hajos has argued instead for a joint venture between the two cities, pointing to a system of fortifications along the northern Kopais with nothing at all facing Thebes to the south; her reviewer objects that this leaves the destructions that follow soon after at Thebes and at Gla unexplained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gla was destroyed, and presumably the drainage with it, well before the end of the 13th century BCE, earlier than the burning of the Argive citadels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Gla","questions":["Whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belonged to Orchomenos alone or to Orchomenos and Thebes together is argued, since its fortifications run along the northern Kopais with nothing facing Thebes.","The walls of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are of large limestone slabs rather than boulders and went up all at once, part of a project that dwarfs any other Mycenaean building work known."],"tags":["place","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Alex R. Knodell, review of Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, <i>Mycenaean Greece and the Aegean World: Palace and Province in the Late Bronze Age</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2017.04.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.04.13. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 2.502, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D2%3Acard%3D494. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cd/Plan_of_the_Palace_of_Gla.jpg","title":"Gla","desc":"A plan of the palace at Gla. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plan_of_the_Palace_of_Gla.jpg","alt":"Plan of the Palace of Gla"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Gla","at":[23.1822,38.4833]}},
{"id":"gr-072","num":"72","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Porch, vestibule and throne room in a line, with a great round hearth and four columns around it, are the three parts of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a Mycenaean palace.","answer":"megaron","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palatial form</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>megaron</b> is the great hall at the heart of a Mycenaean palace, divided into three along one axis: a columned porch, a vestibule the Greeks called the <i>prodomos</i>, and beyond it the <i>domos</i>, the main chamber that held the ruler's throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A large circular hearth stands at the middle of that chamber with four columns set square around it, and the throne against the middle of the right-hand wall at Pylos, at Tiryns and probably at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The floors of all three parts were plastered and painted with patterns, and frescoes ran over the walls throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The form is not an import: the throne room with its central hearth is a monumental version of the ordinary private house of the Early and Middle Helladic periods, grown grand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the Mycenaeans took from Crete they took selectively, and the plan they built is the opposite of a Minoan one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A Minoan palace works outward from its central court, while a Mycenaean palace is centripetal, funnelling anyone who enters towards a hall left standing isolated and dominant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A palace usually holds more than one: Tiryns, Pylos and Mycenae each have a smaller second unit of the same shape, sometimes called the queen's megaron, and at Tiryns the smaller one is hard to reach from the greater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gla has several smaller and plainer ones, none of them in a central position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The form reaches past the great palaces as well, for the megaron on the lower terraces at Midea has the same large hearth, set a little off centre, with its four columns round it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Homer's great houses have the same parts exactly reproduced, the forecourt with its colonnades and altar, the hall with pillars and hearth and ante-room, and the bathroom beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"megaron","questions":["A Minoan palace works outward from its court, but a Mycenaean one funnels whoever enters towards the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a hall left standing isolated and dominant at the centre.","The throne room of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a monumental version of the ordinary house of the Early and Middle Helladic periods, so the form is inherited rather than borrowed from Crete."],"tags":["building","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 107–17, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Walter Leaf, introduction to Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i> (London: Macmillan, 1891), xxi–xxii, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of Gisela Walberg, <i>Midea: The Megaron Complex and Shrine Area. Excavations on the Lower Terraces 1994–1997</i>, Prehistory Monographs 20, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2008.03.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.03.43/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/MiceneMegaron.jpg/1920px-MiceneMegaron.jpg","title":"megaron","desc":"Mycenae, Megaron. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MiceneMegaron.jpg","alt":"MiceneMegaron"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-073","num":"73","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The same motifs turn up in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1250 BCE and in Cretan painting five centuries older, a tenacity of tradition compared with that of oral formulaic poetry.","answer":"Mycenaean fresco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pylos set</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Mycenaean fresco</b> is a wall painting from one of the mainland palaces, and substantial sets survive from Mycenae, Orchomenos, Thebes, Tiryns and Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest belong to Late Helladic IIA, the late 16th or early 15th century BCE, and the fullest set, from Pylos, to about 1250 to 1200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Painting ran right through the great hall, over the walls of porch, vestibule and throne room alike, above floors plastered and painted with patterns of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The subjects are processions on a large scale and, on a small one, hunting, preparation for battle, sacrifice and feasting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Animals appear in human settings or as emblems of royal power, lions and wingless griffins among them, while sea creatures were put on floors rather than walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Tiryns the masterpiece is a bull galloping at full speed on a blue ground, its body yellow with many red spots, a man balanced on its back with one leg thrown high and a hand on its horn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The strangest thing about this painting is how little it changes: the same motifs recur from 1250 BCE back to the Crete of 1750 to 1375, a tenacity of tradition that has been compared with that of oral formulaic poetry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One consequence is practical, that a Mycenaean mural cannot be dated by its subject matter alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other is a doubt about what the pictures show, since Cretan scenes depict things really done where the mainland versions may be formulas repeated after the sense of them was lost, and that bull-leaper may be exactly the case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Pylos the lyre player, the feasting figures and the bull of the throne room have been read as one composition, a bull led into the palace to be sacrificed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean fresco","questions":["Because the subjects hardly change, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot be dated by what it shows, and the mainland versions may be formulas repeated after their sense was lost.","The masterpiece among the Tiryns examples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a bull galloping on a blue ground, yellow with red spots, a man balanced on its back holding a horn."],"tags":["art","object","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 24: Mycenaean Pictorial Art and Pottery,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-24-narrative/. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 118–20, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","\"Palace of Nestor: Description,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","\"Thebes: Monuments,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh351.jsp?obj_id=2459. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Gillieron_Shield_Fresco_Tiryns.png","title":"Mycenaean fresco","desc":"Reproduction of the \"Shield Frieze\" from the Mycenaean palace at Tiryns, painted by Émile Gilliéron and acquired by the Metropolitan Museum in New York before 1912. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gillieron_Shield_Fresco_Tiryns.png","alt":"Gillieron Shield Fresco Tiryns"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-074","num":"74","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A principally syllabic script of some 89 signs with over a hundred ideograms beside them, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut into clay at six palaces and turned out to record Greek.","answer":"Linear B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Most tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Linear B</b> is the writing of the Mycenaean palaces, a principally syllabic script of some 89 signs standing for vowels and for consonant-vowel combinations, with over a hundred ideograms alongside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was pressed into unbaked clay, and what survives survives because the fires that ended the palaces hardened the tablets that recorded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Archives are known from six places, Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos and Thebes on the mainland and Knossos and Chania on Crete, and most of the tablets belong to about 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arthur Evans set Crete's linear writing out as two classes, A and B, in 1909, and 45 of Linear B's syllabic signs have close equivalents in the older of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The older script has never been read, so the sound of many signs is known while the language behind them is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The language behind Linear B is Greek, and the Greek ministry calls these texts the proof that it is the earliest known Greek script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reading it pushed the earliest examples of written Greek back to more than 3,000 years ago and made Greek the oldest known European language still spoken today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it was used for is narrow: the tablets record land tenure, the assignment of people to tasks, military organisation and economic transactions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything surviving in it is administration, so a script that turns out to preserve the oldest Greek preserves not a line of literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That the palaces of Greece kept their accounts in Greek is what the reading of it settled, and that mattered as much to Indo-European linguistics as to Aegean archaeology, altering the understanding of the early civilisations of Greece and Crete completely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Linear B","questions":["Deciphering <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1952 pushed the earliest written Greek back more than 3,000 years and made Greek the oldest European language still spoken today.","Everything surviving in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is administrative, from land tenure to military organisation, so a script that records Greek preserves no literature at all."],"tags":["concept","object","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 28–29, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","\"Palace of Nestor: Description,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Linear_B_tablet_An_661.png","title":"Linear B","desc":"Linear B tablet An 661. -1203-04-10. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_B_tablet_An_661.png","alt":"Linear B tablet An 661"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-075","num":"75","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came in 1952, when a young British architect showed the script recorded Greek, and a Cambridge philologist became the first scholar to accept it.","answer":"decipherment of Linear B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deciphered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1952</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Confirmed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1953 and 1956</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>decipherment of Linear B</b> was achieved in 1952 by Michael Ventris, a young British architect, working on the clay tablets Arthur Evans had sorted into two classes of Cretan linear writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What he showed was that the script of the Mycenaean palaces, some 89 syllabic signs and a hundred ideograms, recorded Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within weeks he was in touch with John Chadwick, a philologist just appointed to a lectureship at Cambridge, who had been given the work by Sir John Myres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chadwick was the first scholar to accept the decipherment as correct, and his first letter, of 13 July 1952, offered whatever 'a mere philologist' could do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In it he supplied the sound-value <i>pu</i>, which let the name Pylos itself be read; Ventris answered the same day that he had been feeling the need of a mere philologist to keep him on the right lines, and asked him to collaborate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their first scholarly publication came out in the <i>Journal of Hellenic Studies</i> in 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dramatic confirmation came on 7 April 1955, when Chadwick, joining fragments in the Herakleion Museum, put together the horse tablet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It carried <i>i-qo</i>, the Mycenaean <i>hikkwos</i>, which is the later Greek <i>hippos</i>, a horse, and their letters call it a dramatic confirmation of the correctness of the decipherment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two worked closely together for four years, until Ventris died in 1956 within weeks of the publication of their joint <i>Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The original letters are kept in the Mycenaean Epigraphy Room at Cambridge and have been digitised, so the whole of it can be followed letter by letter, from July 1952 to shortly before Ventris's death in September 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"decipherment of Linear B","questions":["The dramatic confirmation of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a tablet joined in 1955 carrying <i>i-qo</i>, the Mycenaean <i>hikkwos</i>, which is the later Greek <i>hippos</i>, a horse.","Chadwick's first letter about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered whatever 'a mere philologist' could do and supplied the sound-value <i>pu</i>, which let the name Pylos be read."],"tags":["event","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Ventris–Chadwick Correspondence,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/archive/correspondence. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","\"Palace of Nestor: Description,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 28–29, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Linear_B_tablet_An_657.png","title":"decipherment of Linear B","desc":"Linear B tablet An 657. -1203-04-10. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_B_tablet_An_657.png","alt":"Linear B tablet An 657"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-076","num":"76","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Told apart by their handwriting, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> number 32 at Pylos and 100 at Knossos, and nothing they wrote describes their own work or their training.","answer":"Linear B scribes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Script in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tablets fired</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1180 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Linear B scribes</b> are the writers of the palace records of Bronze Age Crete and Greece, in a script used there between about 1400 and 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word scribe is a convention that claims nothing about their standing: they may have been palace administrators, writers assisting them, or workers whose own job included recording what they produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are told apart by their handwriting, chiefly by palaeographic analysis of the writing itself, though subject matter, formatting, orthography and language all play a part; much of it now read from mid-20th-century photographs that preserve details no longer visible on the tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Counted that way, 1,107 tablets at Pylos are the work of 32 hands and 3,369 at Knossos the work of 100, while a recent study of the Pylos writers puts them at 30 to 40.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing they wrote describes their own work or their training, and no certain school text or practice text has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What stands in for that is the writing itself: the same conventions of spelling and record-keeping hold across every site, so all of them were trained in one shared tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within it variation was in certain cases permissible and entirely normal, even within the work of a single writer, so the training set conventions rather than fixing every form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Teaching may have been informal, since learning of this kind can happen through interaction with others as much as through formal instruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clay was worked by rolling out a cylinder and flattening it with the palms, or by folding up a sheet, into long narrow palm-leaf tablets of a line or two, or rectangular page-shaped ones carrying more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether a writer made the tablet they wrote on is unknown, palmprints identifying only four makers across more than one tablet, and none of it was meant to keep: the clay was air-dried, and fired only by the fire that ended the palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Linear B scribes","questions":["The clay the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote on was rolled into a cylinder and flattened with the palms, or folded up from a sheet, and only ever air-dried.","Spelling varied even within one writer's work, so the training that produced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set shared conventions rather than fixing every form, and may have been informal."],"tags":["people","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Anna P. Judson, \"Learning to Spell in Linear B: Orthography and Scribal Training in Mycenaean Pylos,\" <i>The Cambridge Classical Journal</i> 68 (2022): 133–63, https://doi.org/10.1017/S1750270522000057. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, \"The Tablet-Makers of Pylos: An Experimental Investigation into the Production of Linear B Tablets,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 118 (2023): 147–70, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245423000059. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"Research and Archival Collections,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/archive. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Clay_Tablet_inscribed_with_Linear_B_script.jpg/1920px-Clay_Tablet_inscribed_with_Linear_B_script.jpg","title":"Linear B scribes","desc":"The clay tablet KN Fp 13, dated to 1450-1375 BCE, is Minoan and was found at Knossos by Arthur Evans. It records, in Greek written in Linear B, quantities of oil apparently offered to various deities. vintagedept, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_Tablet_inscribed_with_Linear_B_script.jpg","alt":"Clay Tablet inscribed with Linear B script"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-077","num":"77","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"About 80 per cent of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay in two small rooms immediately inside the palace's main entrance, the rest scattered through storerooms and two outlying buildings.","answer":"Pylos tablets","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Pylos tablets</b> are the Linear B records kept at the Palace of Nestor in Messenia, the largest such archive from the Greek mainland, in which the place calls itself PU-RO, capital of a kingdom covering Messenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 80 per cent of them lay in the Archives Complex, rooms 7 and 8, two small rooms immediately inside the palace's main entrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest were scattered through the site, in a pottery storeroom, three rooms holding oil, and two buildings outside the palace proper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> All of it is firmly dated by the burnt destruction contexts in which it was found, to the end of the 13th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Excavation ran from 1939 into the 1960s, and every text has since been published twice over, as a transcription and as a corpus giving each tablet a drawing, a colour photograph, its size, its fragments and its find-spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their subject is administration, and there is enough of it for major portions of the administrative system of a Mycenaean kingdom to be reconstructed from this one archive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Land takes up the largest and most argued-over part: a complex system of tenure evidently existed in the Pylian kingdom, the wanax, the lawagetas and the telestai stand out as major landholders, and the palace is interested in all land in the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Beside it run narrower sets: the Jn tablets on bronze, twenty of them reconstructed from 34 pieces and over a hundred unnumbered fragments, and the o-ka series, listing bodies of troops called watchers of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The military tablets account for some 800 coast watchers and 600 rowers, and others record textile work, land taxation and religious dedications, among them banquet materials for Poseidon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the archive gives, then, is not a history but a kingdom caught working: its officials, its landholders, its bronze, its cloth, its gods and the men it had posted along its shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pylos tablets","questions":["Among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the military sets account for some 800 coast watchers and 600 rowers, while the o-ka series lists bodies of troops called watchers of the sea.","So much of a Mycenaean kingdom's administration survives in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that major portions of its system can be reconstructed, with the wanax and the lawagetas among its landholders."],"tags":["object","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Silvia Ferrara, review of <i>Les archives du roi Nestor: Corpus des inscriptions en linéaire B de Pylos, I–II</i>, by Louis Godart and Anna Sacconi, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2020.12.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.12.28/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, \"The Tablet-Makers of Pylos: An Experimental Investigation into the Production of Linear B Tablets,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 118 (2023): 147–70, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245423000059. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Eleanor Dickey, review of <i>The Pylos Tablets Transcribed</i>, 2nd ed., by Jean-Pierre Olivier and Maurizio Del Freo, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2022.12.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.12.05/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Linear_B_tablet_PY_Ta_716_%28photo%29.png","title":"Pylos tablets","desc":"Photograph of Linear B tablet PY Ta 716. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_B_tablet_PY_Ta_716_(photo).png","alt":"Linear B tablet PY Ta 716"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-078","num":"78","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Written WA-NA-KA in the tablets, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held land three times the size of the lawagetas', and the word survives in the <i>Iliad</i> as an archaic term for a king.","answer":"wanax","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>wanax</b> is the ruler at the head of a Mycenaean kingdom, written WA-NA-KA in the Linear B tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word outlived the palaces that used it, surviving in the <i>Iliad</i> as an archaic form beside the ordinary <i>basileus</i> for a king, and Agamemnon is <i>anax andron</i>, lord of men, in the poem's seventh line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which man held the office is nearly recoverable at Pylos, where it has been suggested, on evidence its proposers call inconclusive, that his name was Enkhelyawon or something like it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was the kingdom's greatest landholder, the lawagetas below him controlling land one third the size of his, though three telestai together hold as much as he does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Land was held from him as well as by him, the telestai holding theirs in return for their service, <i>telos</i>, to the wanax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The adjective <i>wanakteros</i>, royal, marks what belonged to his household, and a potter, a fuller and possibly an armourer are named by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Painted stirrup jars from Thebes, Eleusis, Tiryns and Chania carry the same word in paint, and presumably held wine or oil from royal vineyards or olive groves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The title reaches into religion as well, since in some tablets wanax appears to be used as a divine title, though that does not by itself make him a priest-king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His pairing with the goddess Potnia in one Pylos series has been read as making the two human substitutes for some kind of divine pair, in what one contributor calls a theocratic system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Feasting belonged to the office too, in festivals organised by the ruler and high officials for political purposes, and the wanax is placed by modern accounts across religion and ritual, the economy, the military sphere and the social hierarchy at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"wanax","questions":["The adjective <i>wanakteros</i> marks what belonged to the household of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and painted stirrup jars from four sites carry the same word in paint.","Paired with the goddess Potnia in one Pylos series, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been read as a human substitute for a divine pair, though the tablets do not make him a priest-king."],"tags":["title","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 2, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 116, no. 3 (2012), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/1142/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 1.7, ed. D. B. Monro and T. W. Allen, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0133%3Abook%3D1%3Acard%3D1. [Open access]","Silke Knippschild, review of <i>Ancient Greece: From the Mycenaean Palaces to the Age of Homer</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Irene S. Lemos, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.08.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.39/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Ceramic_fragment_with_WANAKTI_inscription.jpg","title":"wanax","desc":"Ancient Greek ceramic fragment depicting a horse with rider. Votive pinax from the sanctuary of Poseidon at Penteskouphia, Corinth (Berlin, Antikensammlung F.552;. BishkekRocks , (Retouched by AM ), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceramic_fragment_with_WANAKTI_inscription.jpg","alt":"Ceramic fragment with WANAKTI inscription"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-079","num":"79","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Written ra-wa-ke-ta in the tablets, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes second after the wanax among the Pylos landholders, with an estate a third the size of the king's.","answer":"lawagetas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>lawagetas</b> is the second man of a Mycenaean kingdom, written ra-wa-ke-ta in Linear B, attested at both Pylos and Knossos and apparently one man at either place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The etymology is transparent, <i>laos</i> and <i>agein</i>, leader of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the <i>laos</i> was is less so, and one reading takes it to mean not an army but any weak group of followers in need of protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Pylos list of major landholders he stands immediately after the wanax, with an estate a third the size of the king's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> People are attached to him as they are to the king: landholders can be named by their membership in his household, an adjective built from his title, <i>lawagesios</i>, qualifies certain personnel, and two Pylos land tablets, Ea 421 and Ea 809, record men holding land from his wheelwright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What he actually did is the open question of the office, since the name suggests a war leader and the tablets do not confirm one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The comparison usually drawn is with the Germanic societies Tacitus describes, where a king directs the leader who commands his fighting men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that stands the plain fact that no solid evidence from Mycenaean archaeology shows him filling such a role, and he may have been a crown prince, or something else altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Military associations do cling to the title even so, and one suggestion gives him a part in bringing new peoples into the community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word for the people he led outlasted the palaces that employed him: <i>laos</i> is Homer's word for the host, and it is the <i>laoi</i> who begin to perish when Apollo sends his pestilence through the camp in the tenth line of the <i>Iliad</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"lawagetas","questions":["The name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means leader of the people, but no solid evidence makes him a war leader, and he may have been a crown prince instead.","Two Pylos tablets record men holding land from the wheelwright of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and an adjective formed from his title qualifies certain personnel."],"tags":["title","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Gregory Nagy, \"Thinking Comparatively about Greek Mythology XIII, with a Focus on the Role of Hēraklēs as Kingmaker,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 18 October 2019, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/thinking-comparatively-about-greek-mythology-xiii-with-a-focus-on-the-role-of-herakles-as-kingmaker/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 1.10, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D1%3Acard%3D8. [Open access]","Rachele Pierini and Thomas G. Palaima, eds., \"MASt@CHS — Fall Seminar 2020 (Friday, November 6): Summaries of Presentations and Discussion,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 23 December 2020, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/mast-chs-friday-november-6-2020-summaries-of-presentations-and-discussion/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-080","num":"80","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"In the tablets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no grander than the chief of a small group, in one case a group of bronzesmiths, and heads work groups at Pylos, Knossos and Thebes.","answer":"qa-si-re-u","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>qa-si-re-u</b> is a minor official of the Mycenaean palaces, known at Pylos, Knossos and Thebes, and the ancestor of the ordinary Greek word for a king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the tablets he is nothing grander than some kind of chief or leader of a small group, in one case a group of bronzesmiths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is the head of work groups, and one Pylos tablet gives a whole section of seven entries the heading qa-si-re-wi-jo-te, men performing as <i>qa-si-re-u</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another lists him beside the ko-re-te and the po-ro-ko-re-te, officials connected with the kingdom's second-order centres, where the koreter had charge of one of the sixteen major administrative units of the Pylian kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In some contexts he may have run small outlying districts, though on an extreme view the word never occurs outside contexts involving craftsmen and means no more than the man in charge of a manufacturing unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The connection with Homer's <i>basileus</i>, meaning king, is undeniable, and the metamorphosis from a Mycenaean village headman to a Homeric chieftain is explicable in view of the chaotic conditions that followed the collapse of the palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came through that collapse because his power had never been the palace's to give: officials of this kind derived it from local communities, which accounts both for their survival and for their rise in status once the centre was gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>geronsia</i>, whose duties are not fully understood, came through the same way and ended as the classical <i>gerousia</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The line has been traced beyond Greek proper as well, as far as the <i>pa-si-le-wo-se</i> of the Cypriot syllabary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the ninth line of the <i>Iliad</i> Apollo is angry at the <i>basileus</i>, two lines after Agamemnon is <i>anax andron</i>, so that a king's word and a foreman's have come to mean the same man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"qa-si-re-u","questions":["Because his power came from local communities rather than the palace, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outlived the collapse and rose in status, ending as the ordinary Greek word for a king.","Beside the ko-re-te and the po-ro-ko-re-te, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appears on a Pylos tablet among officials connected with the kingdom's second-order centres, far below the wanax."],"tags":["title","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Rachele Pierini and Thomas G. Palaima, eds., \"MASt@CHS — Fall Seminar 2020 (Friday, November 6): Summaries of Presentations and Discussion,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 23 December 2020, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/mast-chs-friday-november-6-2020-summaries-of-presentations-and-discussion/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 1.9, ed. D. B. Monro and T. W. Allen, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0133%3Abook%3D1%3Acard%3D8. [Open access]","Silke Knippschild, review of <i>Ancient Greece: From the Mycenaean Palaces to the Age of Homer</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Irene S. Lemos, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.08.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.39/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-081","num":"81","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Best translated as village, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can mean either the people of a community or the land they hold, and it is an entity that allocates landholdings.","answer":"damos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>damos</b> is the local community of a Mycenaean kingdom, written da-mo in the tablets, and an entity that can allocate landholdings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is perhaps best translated as village, an English word that can refer either to the people of a community or to the land that community holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Linear B evidence strongly suggests it is nothing more than a group of individual landholders, that is, a collective landholding body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These are the individual communities found within palatial territory, and each of them maintained a significant degree of independence from the palace, against an older view that saw them as subject to its political and economic control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Records of the kind that name it were used to calculate taxes, which is one reason so much of the archive turns on who held what.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The land is described by two words, ki-ti-me-na and ke-ke-me-na, which Ventris and Chadwick read as private plots against communal ones belonging to the damos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Work through the 1980s and 1990s overturned that reading, showing that both terms describe land associated with the damos, and rendering one private and the other common is now called idiosyncratic and not generally accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plots called ki-ti-me-na at Pylos are never said to be held from a woman, and women there have severely restricted access to land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The collective could also be argued with, and one Pylos tablet records the priestess e-ri-ta in dispute with it over a holding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it did not have was politics: nothing suggests the word carried in the Bronze Age the political sense it took on in the Archaic and Classical periods, so the term that would later mean the people is here the name of a body that holds fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"damos","questions":["Nothing suggests the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried political weight in the Bronze Age, though the word took on exactly that sense in the Archaic and Classical periods.","One Pylos tablet records the priestess e-ri-ta in dispute with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over a holding, which shows the collective could be argued with."],"tags":["institution","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Women in Mycenaean Greece: The Linear B Tablets from Pylos and Knossos</i>, by Barbara A. Olsen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.03.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.03.45/. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33. [Open access]","Susan Lupack, \"A View from Outside the Palace: The Sanctuary and the <i>Damos</i> in Mycenaean Economy and Society,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 207–17, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0207. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-082","num":"82","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Managers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> collected dispersed raw materials and gave them to specialists, who added value by skilled labour and made goods the palace then handed to a chosen few.","answer":"Mycenaean palace economy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palatial era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean palace economy</b> is what the mainland palaces did with goods, and it worked by allocation: managers collected dispersed raw materials and gave them out to specialists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those specialists added value to them through skilled labour and produced composite artefacts, which palatial authorities then redistributed to an exclusive group of recipients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The industries run this way were chariots, perfumed oils and textiles, so what the system turned out was not daily bread but prestige.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The allocation has a name in the tablets, <i>ta-ra-si-ja</i>, and a Pylos record of bronzesmiths can list them instead as <i>a-ta-ra-si-jo</i>, men without one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Landholding records were used to calculate taxes, so the archive turns on who held what quite as much as on who made what.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What the palace did not do matters as much: the concepts brought to bear are staple and wealth finance, and the argument is that palatial control worked largely as a wealth finance system meant to raise the prestige of the rulers and secure the allegiance of local elites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That control was partial rather than absolute, and the live question has become the mechanisms and strategies by which a palace exerted it over some areas but not others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Redistribution is not one thing either, since several forms of it may occur at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The offerings the tablets record could not have fully supported the sanctuaries, which removes the main argument for treating those sanctuaries as subject to palatial authority, and they cannot be interpreted as part of a real system of redistribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At least three spheres of economic influence can be posited in a Mycenaean state, the palace, the sanctuaries and the damos, which is a very different picture from one centre directing everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean palace economy","questions":["Control under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was partial rather than absolute, and the live question is how a palace exerted it over some areas and not others.","Beside the palace, sanctuaries and the damos were spheres of influence in their own right, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was never one centre directing everything."],"tags":["concept","institution","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Susan Lupack, \"A View from Outside the Palace: The Sanctuary and the <i>Damos</i> in Mycenaean Economy and Society,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 207–17, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0207. [Open access]","Robert Schon, \"By Appointment to His Majesty the Wanax: Value-Added Goods and Redistribution in Mycenaean Palatial Economies,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 219–27, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0219. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Rethinking Mycenaean Palaces II</i>, rev. and expanded 2nd ed., ed. Michael L. Galaty and William A. Parkinson, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2008.07.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.57. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33. [Open access]","Rachele Pierini and Thomas G. Palaima, eds., \"MASt@CHS — Fall Seminar 2020 (Friday, November 6): Summaries of Presentations and Discussion,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 23 December 2020, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/mast-chs-friday-november-6-2020-summaries-of-presentations-and-discussion/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg/1920px-Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg","title":"Mycenaean palace economy","desc":"Pilo di Messenia - Palazzo di Nestore - Sala del trono. YukioSanjo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg","alt":"Hearth of the megaron of Pylos"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-083","num":"83","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Every female producer at Knossos whose duties can be identified worked in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, largely as corvée labour rather than as slaves, and one tablet records their monthly rations.","answer":"Mycenaean textile industry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean textile industry</b> is the largest single operation the Linear B archives record, and it ran on the <i>ta-ra-si-ja</i>, the distribution or allocation of raw material by the palace to specialist workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Behind the cloth stood the flocks, and the archives carry flock records at Pylos and shearing and lambing records at Knossos, where the sheep of the D-tablets have been counted as population statistics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The people who worked the wool were women: at Knossos every female producer whose duties can be identified is occupied with textiles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were largely corvée labourers rather than slaves, and one Knossos tablet, E 777, records monthly rations issued to a group of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across Knossos and Pylos together the evidence has been gathered for some 2,000 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the palace wanted from them is set out in the Lc series of tablets, which records textile production targets for central and western Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cloths named are <i>te-pa</i>, <i>pa-we-a</i> and <i>tu-na-no</i>, and the targets differ according to the groups assigned to fulfil them, groups designated by ethnicity and occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Comparing the total target for non-collector workers in central Crete with the individual targets suggests that the annual planned production was not their sum but a predefined amount of cloth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That in turn answers why only some places in Crete were put under the obligation at all, and it suggests the target was only part of what a Cretan work group owed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cloth was one of the industries whose products left the palace again as value added by skilled labour, redistributed to a restricted group of recipients, and the craft marked the language as well, in the conceptual links drawn between weaving, sewing, devising plots and musical performance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean textile industry","questions":["The Lc tablets set targets for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in central and western Crete, for cloths called <i>te-pa</i>, <i>pa-we-a</i> and <i>tu-na-no</i>, differing by the group assigned to meet them.","Raw material went out to specialist workers under the <i>ta-ra-si-ja</i>, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was organised, and the flocks behind it fill the shearing and lambing records."],"tags":["practice","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Marie-Louise B. Nosch, \"The Mycenaean Administration of Textile Production in the Palace of Knossos: Observations on the Lc(1) Textile Targets,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 4 (2011): 495–505, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.4.0495. [Paywalled]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Women in Mycenaean Greece: The Linear B Tablets from Pylos and Knossos</i>, by Barbara A. Olsen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.03.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.03.45/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>KE-RA-ME-JA: Studies Presented to Cynthia W. Shelmerdine</i>, ed. Dimitri Nakassis, Joann Gulizio and Sarah A. James, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.12.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.12.03/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of <i>The New Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>, by John Killen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.12.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.20/. [Open access]","Robert Schon, \"By Appointment to His Majesty the Wanax: Value-Added Goods and Redistribution in Mycenaean Palatial Economies,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 219–27, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0219. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Mycenaean_stone_tools%2C_1clay_loom_weights%2C_AM_Ag_Andreas%2C_Sifnos%2C_53530.jpg/1920px-Mycenaean_stone_tools%2C_1clay_loom_weights%2C_AM_Ag_Andreas%2C_Sifnos%2C_53530.jpg","title":"Mycenaean textile industry","desc":"Mycenaean stone tools (pounders, polishers, grinders) - and clay loom weights. Archaeological Museum of Agios Andreas on Sifnos. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_stone_tools,_1clay_loom_weights,_AM_Ag_Andreas,_Sifnos,_53530.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean stone tools, 1clay loom weights, AM Ag Andreas, Sifnos, 53530"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-084","num":"84","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The E- series at Pylos records assessed lands and amounts on one reading to a cadastre, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most complex thing the tablets preserve.","answer":"Mycenaean land tenure","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean land tenure</b> is the most complex thing the Linear B texts record, and the system that evidently existed in the Pylian kingdom is known almost entirely from one run of tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These are the E- series at Pylos, which set down assessed lands and on one reading amount to a cadastre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Holdings come in named kinds: the wanax, the lawagetas and the telestai hold <i>temenoi</i>, while <i>ka-ma</i> land differs from those in some as yet undetermined way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One recent argument makes <i>ka-ma</i> parts of the community's own land set aside for intensive cultivation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The telestai hold theirs in return for their service, their <i>telos</i>, and three of them together hold as much as the king and on average as much as the second man of the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether any of this amounts to feudalism is old ground: some have argued the system at least partly feudal, with lands held from the king in return for services rendered to him, and others deny that there is evidence for feudalism at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One comparison drawn is with the economy typical of medieval monasteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The purpose of the records is clearer than their vocabulary, since landholding texts were used to calculate taxes in kind precisely, and the round figures in them may reflect lands measured in exact units rather than rough reckoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are local as well as fiscal: the E- records are taken to concern places close to Pylos, perhaps within one administrative district, where the flax assessments and the miscellaneous tribute of other series reach further out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even the best-known episode in the run is contested, the tablet on which a priestess appears to argue with the community over what she holds having had its reading as a dispute questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean land tenure","questions":["Some read <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as at least partly feudal, lands held from the king for services rendered, while others deny any evidence for feudalism at all.","Records of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were used to calculate taxes in kind, and their round figures may reflect lands measured in exact units rather than rough reckoning."],"tags":["concept","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Women in Mycenaean Greece: The Linear B Tablets from Pylos and Knossos</i>, by Barbara A. Olsen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.03.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.03.45/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>KE-RA-ME-JA: Studies Presented to Cynthia W. Shelmerdine</i>, ed. Dimitri Nakassis, Joann Gulizio and Sarah A. James, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.12.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.12.03/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Pylos_5.jpg/1920px-Pylos_5.jpg","title":"Mycenaean land tenure","desc":"Pylos (ruins of Mycenaean city, containing so-called Palace of Nestor). Julià Minguillón, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pylos_5.jpg","alt":"Pylos 5"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-085","num":"85","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Among <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Poseidon is the most important at Pylos, while two names, Enyalios and Paiawon, survive only as later epithets of Ares and Apollo.","answer":"Mycenaean gods in Linear B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean gods in Linear B</b> are the divine names the palace tablets record, and most of them are Olympians, some centuries before Homer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poseidon stands out: he is named at Knossos and at Pylos, where he is evidently the most important deity to judge from the frequency with which he is mentioned, and one tablet assigns him banquet materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Zeus appears at Knossos as <i>di-wo</i>, with a female counterpart Diwia; Hera stands beside him on one Pylos tablet, and Artemis appears as <i>a-ti-mi-te</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two names arrive as gods and survive only as epithets, Enyalios, later attached to Ares, and Paiawon, later Paian and attached to Apollo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysos is present as <i>di-wo-nu-so-jo</i>, though the name may belong to a person rather than a god, and Potnia, the mistress, is named at both palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The hierarchy is not the later one: in the Knossos oil allocations Zeus does not get the most, having not yet the pre-eminent place he occupied later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor are the old Aegean powers present, since none of the goddesses generally taken for them is mentioned in these texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the gods received was modest and bloodless, a pot of honey, spices such as fennel and coriander, jugs of oil, with honey going to all the gods on one Knossos tablet and a Priestess of the Winds having honey of her own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oddities are what a reader remembers: perfumed oil goes to an Aswian Potnia and to a Divine Mother, and a Theban wine record has been read as an offering to the Snakes, beside the terracotta snakes from the cult centre at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How much of this the palace controlled is open, since the offerings recorded could not have fully supported the sanctuaries, and the standard edition now spreads texts once filed together as tribute and ritual offerings across land tenure, taxation and religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean gods in Linear B","questions":["Offerings to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were bloodless and modest, a pot of honey, spices such as fennel and coriander, and jugs of oil, with Zeus getting far from the most.","None of the goddesses usually taken for old Aegean powers appears among <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a Theban wine record has been read as an offering to the Snakes."],"tags":["concept","deity","religion","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 26: Mycenaean and Late Cycladic Religion and Religious Architecture,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-26-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>KE-RA-ME-JA: Studies Presented to Cynthia W. Shelmerdine</i>, ed. Dimitri Nakassis, Joann Gulizio and Sarah A. James, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.12.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.12.03/. [Open access]","Susan Lupack, \"A View from Outside the Palace: The Sanctuary and the <i>Damos</i> in Mycenaean Economy and Society,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 207–17, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0207. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of <i>The New Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>, by John Killen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.12.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.20/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Mycenaean_figurines%2C_Bird_Goddesses%2C_Psi_type%2C_202185.jpg/1920px-Mycenaean_figurines%2C_Bird_Goddesses%2C_Psi_type%2C_202185.jpg","title":"Mycenaean gods in Linear B","desc":"Late Helladic figurines, so called Bird Goddesses, 1400-1300 BCE. Archaeological Museum of Nafplio: (22) Female figurine, Psi type. Nafplion, Evangelistria, 1350-1300 BCE. (23, 24) Female figurine, Psi type. Nafplion, Evangelistria, chamber tomb ΛΓ, 1400-1350 BCE. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_figurines,_Bird_Goddesses,_Psi_type,_202185.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean figurines, Bird Goddesses, Psi type"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-086","num":"86","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A major deity at both Knossos and Pylos, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a name that means simply mistress, and at Knossos she is the mistress of the Labyrinth.","answer":"Potnia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Potnia</b> is a major deity of the Linear B tablets, worshipped at both Knossos and Pylos, and her name is simply the word for mistress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She rarely stands alone: at Knossos she is <i>da-pu-ri-to-jo po-ti-ni-ja</i>, Potnia of the Labyrinth, and <i>a-ta-na po-ti-ni-ja</i>, which may be Athena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Pylos the additions are mostly places, <i>pa-ki-ja-ni-ja</i> and <i>a-si-wi-ja</i> among them, with <i>i-qe-ja</i>, Hippeia, perhaps meaning of horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether that leaves one goddess with many local faces or many goddesses sharing a title is the question those names raise, and the evidence has been weighed for a monotheistic and a polytheistic reading alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One line drawn through it is grammatical: alone, Potnia is a goddess, but when the name is modified or associated with a place she may be a queen instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What she received was of a piece with the rest of the cult, and honey on one Knossos tablet goes to all the gods and to Potnia of <i>da-pu-ri-to</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sheep are connected with her, though not as offerings, and Amnisos is often named as the site of an offering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Perfumed oil reaches an Aswian Potnia, one of a set of recipients at Pylos whose associations lie outside the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The women who served such cults held a standing they held nowhere else, since most women appearing in prominent roles at Pylos are religious officials and religion has been called the one institution that could trump gender, though at Knossos it made little difference to the hierarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word outlived the palaces that wrote it down, for Homer still calls Artemis <i>potnia theron</i>, mistress of wild beasts, in book 21 of the <i>Iliad</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Potnia","questions":["Alone <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a goddess, but when the name is modified or attached to a place she may be a queen, which is why the epithets matter.","The word for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outlived the palaces, since Homer still calls Artemis <i>potnia theron</i>, mistress of wild beasts, in book 21 of the <i>Iliad</i>."],"tags":["deity","religion","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 26: Mycenaean and Late Cycladic Religion and Religious Architecture,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-26-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>KE-RA-ME-JA: Studies Presented to Cynthia W. Shelmerdine</i>, ed. Dimitri Nakassis, Joann Gulizio and Sarah A. James, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.12.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.12.03/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 21.470, ed. D. B. Monro and T. W. Allen, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0133%3Abook%3D21%3Acard%3D468. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Women in Mycenaean Greece: The Linear B Tablets from Pylos and Knossos</i>, by Barbara A. Olsen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.03.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.03.45/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/NAMA_Art%C3%A9mis_Orthia.jpg","title":"Potnia","desc":"Relief plate, of a bronze fibula. Ivory, 8 cm. Sparta, sanctuary of Artemis Orthia. Initial Archaic, 660 AEC. National Archaeological Museum, Athens (A15502). User:Marsyas, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_Art%C3%A9mis_Orthia.jpg","alt":"NAMA Artémis Orthia"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-087","num":"87","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Scenes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are common on the carved stelae over the Shaft Graves, though whether they show warfare, hunting or races at funeral games is open.","answer":"Mycenaean chariot","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">On stelae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On pottery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean chariot</b> is the light horse-drawn vehicle of Late Bronze Age Greece, and the earliest sign of it stands over the Shaft Graves at Mycenae, carved on their stelae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chariot scenes are quite common on those stelae, though what they show is open, whether illustrations of warfare, of hunting, or of races at funeral games.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing of the kind existed in northern or central Europe at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The animal that pulled it appears in the tablets as <i>i-qo</i>, from the Indo-European <i>ekwos</i>, and one Knossos tablet sets horses and asses down together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In palatial art the horses are shown hitched to chariots at both Tiryns and Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The clearest evidence for the vehicle is the ground it ran on: the Argolid carried a road network linking the major Mycenaean sites, reaching north into the Corinthia and east across the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was built with bridges across ravines and with drainage culverts of limestone boulders, corbel-vaulted beneath the roadbed, and the only rationale for such constructions is that they were designed to accommodate vehicular traffic in the form of chariots and wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Traces of the same thing turn up in Messenia, in Attica and, it is claimed, in Phocis, and carefully constructed roads are not a feature of later Greek civilisation until the 5th century BCE at the earliest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The vehicles themselves were the palace's work, the North-Eastern Building at Pylos having been argued to be a secondary workshop for their final assembly, and they belong with armour, leather goods and furniture among the state-controlled industries that supplied the elite with wealth and prestige.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their uses, which include warfare and status display, are held ultimately to have served the ideological and material interests of the palatial elite, while who drove them is less settled, one suggestion taking the chariot tablets to record a Mycenaean version of a Near Eastern warrior-charioteer caste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean chariot","questions":["At Pylos the North-Eastern Building has been argued to be a secondary workshop for the final assembly of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of the palace's controlled industries.","Who drove the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would be settled by one suggestion, which takes the chariot tablets to record a Mycenaean version of a Near Eastern warrior-charioteer caste."],"tags":["object","technology","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 24: Mycenaean Pictorial Art and Pottery,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-24-narrative/. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Rethinking Mycenaean Palaces II</i>, rev. and expanded 2nd ed., ed. Michael L. Galaty and William A. Parkinson, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2008.07.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.57. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>The People of Knossos: Prosopographical Studies in the Knossos Linear B Archives</i>, by Hedvig Landenius Enegren, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 114, no. 2 (2010), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/682/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Mycenaean_potery_krater%2C_horse-drawn_chariot%2C_1350-1300_BC%2C_Enkomi%2C_BM_Cat_Vases_C340%2C_142835.jpg/1920px-Mycenaean_potery_krater%2C_horse-drawn_chariot%2C_1350-1300_BC%2C_Enkomi%2C_BM_Cat_Vases_C340%2C_142835.jpg","title":"Mycenaean chariot","desc":"Mycenaean potery krater decorated with a horse-drawn chariot and riders, 1350-1300 BCE (LH IIIa2). Found in Tomb 70, Enkomi, Cyprus. British Museum, GR 1897.4-1.1113. BM Cat Vases C340. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_potery_krater,_horse-drawn_chariot,_1350-1300_BC,_Enkomi,_BM_Cat_Vases_C340,_142835.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean potery krater, horse-drawn chariot, 1350-1300 BCE, Enkomi, BM Cat Vases C340"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-088","num":"88","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Buried in the Cuirass Tomb in the Argolid, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one of the oldest complete suits of armour from the European Bronze Age, its surviving bronze weighing about 18 kilograms (40 pounds).","answer":"Dendra panoply","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dendra panoply</b> is a suit of bronze plate armour from a tomb near the village of Dendra in the Argolid, one of the oldest complete suits of armour from the European Bronze Age and the example reached for when the look of Mycenaean metal armour has to be shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It came out of the Cuirass Tomb, tomb 12, a chamber tomb of the ordinary Mycenaean kind, an underground room reached by a long entrance corridor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tombs of that sort were cut into rock rather than built and normally hold several burials, so they are generally considered family tombs, which places this warrior among his relatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The suit is about 3,500 years old, its plates edged with leather strips and joined with leather thongs, and its head was covered by plaques imitating a boar's tusk helmet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is estimated to weigh about 18 kilograms (40 pounds), one handguard and parts of the greaves being lost and the bronze oxidised over three and a half millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether a man could fight in it has been doubted almost since it came to light, museum captions calling it too cumbersome to be worn except on ceremonial occasions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others restricted it to a man riding rather than walking, and Drews put that plainly, holding the corslet armour for a chariot rider and inappropriate for an infantryman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A study published in 2024 tested the question instead of arguing it, putting thirteen volunteers from a Hellenic Army marine brigade into a replica weighing 23.32 kilograms (51 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All of them completed an eleven-hour simulation of Late Bronze Age combat, which its authors take as evidence that the armour was entirely compatible with use in extended combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The written record is less help than it might be, since the armour tablets at Pylos describe nothing similar to this suit, in spite of a logogram among them that looks exactly like it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dendra panoply","questions":["Museum captions once called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> too cumbersome to wear except on ceremonial occasions, and its corslet has been held inappropriate for an infantryman to fight in.","Thirteen volunteers from a marine brigade fought an eleven-hour simulation in a 23.32-kilogram (51-pound) replica of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and every one of them finished it."],"tags":["object","technology","warfare","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Andreas D. Flouris et al., \"Analysis of Greek Prehistoric Combat in Full Body Armour Based on Physiological Principles: A Series of Studies Using Thematic Analysis, Human Experiments, and Numerical Simulations,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301494, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301494. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","David Gill, review of <i>The End of the Bronze Age: Changes in Warfare and the Catastrophe ca. 1200 B.C.</i>, by Robert Drews, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 1994.01.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.01.09/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>Brill's Companion to Warfare in the Bronze Age Aegean</i>, ed. Lynne A. Kvapil and Kim Shelton, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2025.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Mycenaean_armour_from_chamber_tomb_12_of_Dendra_1.JPG/1920px-Mycenaean_armour_from_chamber_tomb_12_of_Dendra_1.JPG","title":"Dendra panoply","desc":"Mycenaean armour from chamber tomb 12 of Dendra 1. Schuppi, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_armour_from_chamber_tomb_12_of_Dendra_1.JPG","alt":"Mycenaean armour from chamber tomb 12 of Dendra 1"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-089","num":"89","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Homer describes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as wrought of hide, stiffened within by tight-stretched thongs, faced without with the white teeth of a boar and lined with felt.","answer":"boar's tusk helmet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In art from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>boar's tusk helmet</b> is the characteristic headgear of Mycenaean warriors, a cap of hide faced with plates cut from the tusks of wild boar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fullest description of one is in the tenth book of the <i>Iliad</i>, where Meriones gives Odysseus a helmet wrought of hide and made stiff within by many a tight-stretched thong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without, the white teeth of a boar of gleaming tusks were set thick on this side and that, well and cunningly, and within was fixed a lining of felt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Homer gives it a pedigree as well: Autolycus stole it from Eleon after breaking into the house of Amyntor, and gave it to Amphidamas, who passed it to Molus as a guest-gift, and Molus gave it to his son to wear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four owners stand between the theft and the man who puts it on for a night raid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The helmet runs through Mycenaean art, and warriors wearing them crowd the scene of combat worked in relief on the silver Battle Krater from the Shaft Graves at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are shown away from the mainland too, on the north wall of the miniature fresco in the West House at Akrotiri, where the warriors carry tower shields as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who those warriors are is unresolved, and the evidence presently available will not decide it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They may even appear in Egypt, since a painted papyrus from el-Amarna in the British Museum shows soldiers in helmets and short-cropped oxhide tunics that can be paralleled in Aegean representations, which has been read as evidence for direct rather than indirect contact between the two cultures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The shape was still worth copying in metal, and when a full reconstruction of the Dendra armour was built its helmet plaques imitated a boar's tusk helmet over an inner cap of leather, as Homer describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"boar's tusk helmet","questions":["Warriors wearing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crowd the relief on the silver Battle Krater from the Shaft Graves, and appear again on the north wall of a fresco at Akrotiri.","Soldiers on a papyrus from el-Amarna wear helmets and oxhide tunics paralleled in Aegean art, which may put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in an Egyptian painting."],"tags":["object","technology","warfare","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 10.260–71, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D10%3Acard%3D254. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","Andreas D. Flouris et al., \"Analysis of Greek Prehistoric Combat in Full Body Armour Based on Physiological Principles: A Series of Studies Using Thematic Analysis, Human Experiments, and Numerical Simulations,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301494, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301494. [Open access]","Louise Schofield and R. B. Parkinson, \"Of Helmets and Heretics: A Possible Egyptian Representation of Mycenaean Warriors on a Papyrus from el-Amarna,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 89 (1994): 157–70, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400015343. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Boars%27s_tusk_helmet_NAMA6568_Athens_Greece1.jpg/1920px-Boars%27s_tusk_helmet_NAMA6568_Athens_Greece1.jpg","title":"boar's tusk helmet","desc":"Boar’s tusk helmet with cheek-guards and a double bone hook on top. Mycenae, chamber Tomb 515, 14th – 13th cents. BCE. N°6568. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boars%27s_tusk_helmet_NAMA6568_Athens_Greece1.jpg","alt":"Boars's tusk helmet NAMA6568 Athens Greece1"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-090","num":"90","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Named from the outline of its two lobes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was painted as a whole frieze on the second floor of the Residential Quarters at Knossos.","answer":"figure-of-eight shield","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In art</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>figure-of-eight shield</b> is the waisted body shield of Bronze Age Aegean art, named from the outline its two lobes make.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shields on that scale are what Homer gives his greatest fighters, and making one was a craftsman's work in hide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ajax carries a shield like a city wall, of seven hides of sturdy bulls with an eighth layer of bronze wrought over them, made by Tychius, far best of workers in hide, whose home was in Hyle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another such shield in the poem reaches to the feet and is called a defence against javelins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It could be a liability as easily as a protection: Periphetes, turning to flee, tripped upon the rim of the shield he himself bore, fell backward, and Hector killed him where he lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In art the shape is everywhere, and at Knossos a whole Frieze of Figure-of-Eight Shields was painted on the second floor of the Residential Quarters, the centrepieces worked with parallel chevrons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Painters used it on pottery as well, one of the motifs of the Alternating Style, in a set that runs double axe, sacral knot, sea anemone and trefoil rockwork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fighting is a subject the Shaft Grave workshops returned to, a silver krater from them carrying a scene of crowded combat worked in relief and a silver rhyton the attack on a fortified town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The other body shield of Aegean art is the tower shield, rectangular rather than waisted, and it is one of those that the disputed warriors of the Akrotiri flotilla fresco carry ashore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both shapes are shields a man stands behind rather than holds up, which is what the poem means by one reaching to the feet and by one likened to a city wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"figure-of-eight shield","questions":["Beside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the other Aegean body shield is the tower shield, rectangular rather than waisted, carried by the warriors on the West House fresco at Akrotiri.","Painters set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among their Alternating Style motifs, in a set running double axe, sacral knot, sea anemone and trefoil rockwork."],"tags":["object","technology","warfare","art","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 7.219–23, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D7%3Acard%3D206. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 15.645–47, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D15%3Acard%3D638. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Wall_Painting_of_Figure-of-Eight_Shield_%283400853679%29.jpg/1920px-Wall_Painting_of_Figure-of-Eight_Shield_%283400853679%29.jpg","title":"figure-of-eight shield","desc":"Represents an original made of cow's hide. Defensive weapon and symbol of a war goddess. Mycenaean, 13th century BCE. Sharon Mollerus, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wall_Painting_of_Figure-of-Eight_Shield_(3400853679).jpg","alt":"Wall Painting of Figure-of-Eight Shield"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-091","num":"91","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Most examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were made in Crete, and analysis of their clay separates three main production areas chemically and 24 fabrics under the microscope.","answer":"stirrup jar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>stirrup jar</b> is the characteristic closed pot of the Late Bronze Age Aegean, a shape whose origins can be traced to Middle Minoan III and Late Minoan IA in Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the mainland it first appears in appreciable quantity in Late Helladic IIIA1, and in the phase after that the stirrup jar, the piriform jar and the alabastron are the shapes most frequently found in tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its form was conspicuous enough to be copied elsewhere, at Akrotiri among other sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The version that travelled is the coarse-ware transport jar, and preliminary results recognise perfumed oil, and probably wine, as the commodities it most often carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most were produced in Crete, and analysis of their clay separates three main production areas chemically and 24 fabrics petrographically, though only 15 of those fabrics are shared by more than one vessel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some carry Linear B painted on before firing, up to about 200 such inscriptions being known, and a recent supplement added 48 more to the published corpus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they say is usually a personal name, a place name, or both, and not one of the inscribed transport jars was made in mainland Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the writing was for is disputed, the jars being taken by some for gifts marked as sent from one palace to another and by others for documents of a Cretan administration, their appearance on the mainland then being secondary re-use in trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A few are painted <i>wanaktero</i>, royal, and those turn up at Thebes, Eleusis, Tiryns and Chania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where a jar came from can now be told from its fabric, and those at Tiryns are mostly from Chania and the western Messara, arriving after the palace at Knossos was destroyed, which points to a new exchange network between the mainland and Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"stirrup jar","questions":["Up to about 200 Linear B inscriptions were painted on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before firing, usually a personal name, a place name, or both.","The examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found at Tiryns come mostly from Chania and the western Messara, arriving after the palace at Knossos was destroyed."],"tags":["object","technology","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Andrea Vianello, review of <i>Transport Stirrup Jars of the Bronze Age Aegean and East Mediterranean</i>, by Halford W. Haskell, Richard E. Jones, Peter M. Day and John T. Killen, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 117, no. 3 (2013), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/1617/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of <i>Supplemento al Corpus delle iscrizioni vascolari in lineare B</i>, by Louis Godart and Anna Sacconi, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2018.10.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.41/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 24: Mycenaean Pictorial Art and Pottery,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-24-narrative/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Maritime Transport Containers in the Bronze–Iron Age Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean</i>, ed. Stella Demesticha and A. Bernard Knapp, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 122, no. 3 (2018), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/3691/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Mycenaean_-_Stirrup_Jar_-_Walters_482084_-_Three_Quarter_Top.jpg","title":"stirrup jar","desc":"This jar is named for its handle, which resembles a stirrup. Originally created in Crete in the 16th century BCE, the stirrup jar was extremely popular throughout the Aegean region for both storage and transport and became the most characteristic Mycenaean vessel type. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_-_Stirrup_Jar_-_Walters_482084_-_Three_Quarter_Top.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean - Stirrup Jar - Three Quarter Top"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-092","num":"92","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Ellen Davis argued that of the two gold <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one was the work of a Minoan craftsman and the other came out of a Mycenaean workshop.","answer":"Vapheio cups","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Vapheio cups</b> are a pair of gold cups from the tholos tomb at Vapheio in Laconia, and the question they raise is who made them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ellen Davis, whose study of them is the standard one, proposed that one was the work of a Minoan craftsman and the other the product of a Mycenaean workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shape is Minoan and the name has stuck to it: a straight-sided Vapheio cup, inspired by Cretan prototypes, is common in the Cycladic repertoire too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the quiet cup a bull stands hobbled, and it is often cited to show that Minoan pictorial art chooses the moment immediately following violent action rather than the violence itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same eye picks the moment before, as in the cat stalking the bird from Ayia Triadha, though the instant of maximum violence is depicted occasionally as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Attributing precious metalwork on the mainland is an old difficulty, and the Cretan origin of such objects is still called uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Shaft Graves show why: of their 28 vessels of solid gold most are rather clumsily made, with technical features atypical of Minoan craftsmanship, and are almost without exception taken for the work of local craftsmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The 42 silver vessels from the same graves are far more carefully made, matching silver plate from Crete, and are read either as imports or as Minoan work done to order for mainland patrons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most of that plate is of Minoan shapes, Vapheio cups and ewers and rhyta among them, while mainland taste shows in the goblets and kantharoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What a pair of cups was for has an answer of its own: they have been read as instruments of diplomacy, tools in the cultivation of host-guest relations in the tradition of <i>xenia</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Vapheio cups","questions":["A bull stands hobbled on the quiet one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, cited to show Minoan art choosing the moment immediately after violent action rather than the violence itself.","Paired vessels like the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have been read as instruments of diplomacy, tools in the cultivation of host-guest relations in the tradition of <i>xenia</i>."],"tags":["object","art","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jessica Doyle, review of <i>Studies in Aegean Art and Culture: A New York Aegean Bronze Age Colloquium in Memory of Ellen N. Davis</i>, ed. Robert B. Koehl, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2017.06.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.06.28. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Philip P. Betancourt, review of <i>The Manufacture of Minoan Metal Vessels: Theory and Practice</i>, by Christina F. Clarke, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 121, no. 1 (2017), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/3368/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Goldbecher_von_Vafio.JPG","title":"Vapheio cups","desc":"Mycenaen golden cup from Vafeio, Laconia. Approximately first half of 15th century BCE. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goldbecher_von_Vafio.JPG","alt":"Goldbecher von Vafio"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-093","num":"93","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"What moved in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> falls into three kinds: raw materials like ivory and ostrich eggs, manufactured goods like seals and textiles, and produce like wine and wool.","answer":"Mycenaean trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First amber</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1725 – 1675 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gelidonya wreck</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean trade</b> is the movement of goods between the Greek mainland and the rest of the Bronze Age Mediterranean, and what moved falls into three kinds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The raw materials are precious metals, gold and silver and electrum, ivory from both elephants and hippopotami, ostrich eggs, stones for architecture and jewellery and tools, and the spices used in perfumed oils, coriander and frankincense and myrrh among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The manufactured goods are pottery, seals, carved ivories, textiles, furniture, stone and metal vessels, and weaponry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The agricultural produce is wine, olive oil, flax, hides and wool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Baltic amber came south as well, almost exclusively as beads and multiply perforated rectangular plaques, the first of it reaching Greece about 1725 to 1675 BCE, possibly in only three consignments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One ship is known in detail: the wreck off Cape Gelidonya went down around 1200 BCE or a little later, nine or ten metres (30 or 33 feet) long, and probably served both as a carrier of raw materials and as a sort of itinerant smithy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whose it was is not settled, Bass having argued the ship Phoenician or Canaanite and Muhly and others Mycenaean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far the goods travelled is measurable in pottery, since Aegean-type ware from 103 sites on the Italian mainland makes up about a fifth of the Mycenaean ceramics circulating in the central Mediterranean, and Italian potters were making it themselves as well as taking it in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Transport stirrup jars carried oil and wine eastward, Cyprus being a major market for them, and who ran that traffic is argued, since private traders not controlled by palatial authorities seem not only possible but probable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the top it was diplomacy, and the Mycenaean pottery reaching Egypt has been read as a highly organised system engaging the ruling elite at Mycenae and the pharaonic court, with olives and olive oil a crucial part of the connection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean trade","questions":["Aegean-type pottery from 103 sites in Italy shows how far <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached, and local potters there were making it as well as importing it.","Private traders outside palace control seem probable in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while at the top it was diplomacy, olives and oil going to Egypt as royal greeting gifts."],"tags":["concept","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Andrea Vianello, review of <i>Mycenaean Greece, Mediterranean Commerce, and the Formation of Identity</i>, by Bryan E. Burns, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/891/. [Open access]","Andrea Vianello, review of <i>Transport Stirrup Jars of the Bronze Age Aegean and East Mediterranean</i>, by Halford W. Haskell, Richard E. Jones, Peter M. Day and John T. Killen, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 117, no. 3 (2013), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/1617/. [Open access]","Christine Johnston, review of <i>Italo-Mycenaean Pottery: The Archaeological and Archaeometric Dimensions</i>, by Richard Jones, Sara T. Levi, Marco Bettelli and Lucia Vagnetti, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2018.02.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.42. [Open access]","Jorrit M. Kelder, \"Royal Gift Exchange between Mycenae and Egypt: Olives as 'Greeting Gifts' in the Late Bronze Age Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 3 (2009): 339–52, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.113.3.339. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mycenaean_stirrup_vase_Louvre_AO19201.jpg/1920px-Mycenaean_stirrup_vase_Louvre_AO19201.jpg","title":"Mycenaean trade","desc":"Mycenaean stirrup vase, 14th-13th centuries BCE, imported to Ugarit. Found in the acropolis of Ras Shamra (ancient Ugarit), tomb 37. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_stirrup_vase_Louvre_AO19201.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean stirrup vase Louvre AO19201"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-094","num":"94","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The bulk cargo of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was copper and tin in the ten-to-one ratio that makes bronze, with 354 of the copper ingots of the oxhide type.","answer":"Uluburun shipwreck","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1320 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Uluburun shipwreck</b> is a Late Bronze Age vessel that went down off Uluburun near Kaş on the Turkish coast, found in 1982 by a sponge diver who described metal biscuits with ears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was excavated over 11 seasons between 1984 and 1994, in more than 22,000 dives, at depths beyond 46 metres (150 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hull was Lebanese cedar, pegged mortise-and-tenon, with a proto-keel rather than a true one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bulk cargo was copper and tin in the ten-to-one ratio that makes bronze, 354 of the copper ingots being of the oxhide shape, and its tin came as oxhide ingots of metallic tin, in far greater quantities than any earlier find.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around that lay carved ivory containers, gold jewellery set with carnelian and agate, glass ingots, elephant tusks, ostrich eggshells and faience beads, with at least 149 Canaanite jars of terebinth resin and a gold scarab naming Nefertiti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The wreck is dated to about 1320 BCE, give or take fifteen years, older than the ship at Cape Gelidonya, until then the earliest ever excavated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its tin has since been traced: 105 ingots, 91 per cent of that cargo and about a tonne of metal, were put through lead isotope, trace element and tin isotope analysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two-thirds proved to come from the Taurus mountains of Turkey and a third, 35 ingots, from deposits in Tajikistan and Uzbekistan more than 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) away, which the authors read as a culturally diverse, multiregional and multivector system rather than one under central control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not everyone is confident of such sourcing, since these ingots carry more lead and antimony and less indium than others, and their lead isotopes probably suffered contamination in corroding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For Greece the question is who was aboard: the excavator proposes two men of Aegean origin and a ship bound for the Aegean, and the cargo mirrors in many respects the palatial gift exchange of the Amarna Letters, raising a diplomatic voyage tied to Mycenaean palatial enterprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Uluburun shipwreck","questions":["Analysis of the tin from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put two-thirds of it in the Taurus mountains and a third in Tajikistan and Uzbekistan, over 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) away.","The excavator of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposes that two men of Aegean origin were aboard and that the ship was sailing to the Aegean."],"tags":["event","object","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Uluburun Late Bronze Age Shipwreck Excavation,\" Institute of Nautical Archaeology, https://nauticalarch.org/projects/uluburun-late-bronze-age-shipwreck-excavation/. [Open access]","Wayne Powell et al., \"Tin from Uluburun Shipwreck Shows Small-Scale Commodity Exchange Fueled Continental Tin Supply across Late Bronze Age Eurasia,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 48 (2022): eabq3766, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abq3766. [Open access]","Christoph Bachhuber, \"Aegean Interest on the Uluburun Ship,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 110, no. 3 (2006): 345–63, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.110.3.345. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"Isotope Systematics and Chemical Composition of Tin Ingots from Mochlos (Crete) and Other Late Bronze Age Sites in the Eastern Mediterranean Sea: An Ultimate Key to Tin Provenance?,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0218326, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218326. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Uluburun1.jpg","title":"Uluburun shipwreck","desc":"Information Description: Wooden model from the Shipwreck from Uluburun. Source: own work Date: June 2006 Author: Martin Bahmann. Martin Bahmann, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uluburun1.jpg","alt":"Uluburun1"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-095","num":"95","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Written ah-hi-ya-wa-a in the Hittite syllabary, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now almost unanimously identified with an Aegean Late Bronze Age and apparently palatial power.","answer":"Ahhiyawa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hittite texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ahhiyawa</b> is a land named in the Hittite records, and whether the name means the Greek world has been argued since the 1920s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is written <i>ah-hi-ya-wa-a</i> in the Hittite syllabary, with an older form <i>a-ah-hi-ya</i> behind it, and one reconstruction derives both the Hittite name and the Greek <i>Akhaioi</i> from a proto-form <i>akhai-wi-a</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between about 1400 and 1200 BCE the Hittite state had several encounters with Ahhiyawans on Anatolian soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The documents that record them are the famous ones: the Tawagalawa letter, the Indictment of Madduwatta, the Sausgamuwa Treaty, the Annals of Mursili, the Alaksandu Treaty and the Milawata letter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One runs the other way, for the fragmentary letter KUB 26.91, found at Hattusa, was sent by a king of Ahhiyawa, and messages were conveyed in writing to the frontier outposts of each kingdom in its own language and script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Where the land lay has been put on the Greek mainland or among the Aegean islands, and also in Pamphylia, in Cyprus and on Rhodes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The boldest reading places it at Mycenae itself, on the ground that no other site on the Helladic mainland fits the references the texts make.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The present consensus is wide but shallower, since Ahhiyawa is now almost unanimously identified with an Aegean and apparently palatial power, with friction between Hatti and Ahhiyawa around Millawanda, later Miletus, and Hiyawa-men read as Mycenaean-Anatolian mercenaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against that stands the view that the equation is hypothetical and largely a matter of faith, the earliest Greek attestation of Achaeans being Homer, with the disputes running on to linguistic proof, geography, the chronology of fragmentary texts and whether contact was direct or ran through Arzawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is not in doubt is that the texts record conflicts in western Anatolia, and one reading takes those conflicts for the historical basis of Homer's account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ahhiyawa","questions":["Sceptics hold that equating <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the Greek world is hypothetical and largely a matter of faith, since the earliest attestation of Achaeans in Greek is Homer.","The fragmentary letter KUB 26.91, found at Hattusa, was sent by a king of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which makes the correspondence run both ways."],"tags":["place","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jorrit M. Kelder, review of <i>Die Ahhijawa-Frage: Mit einer kommentierten Bibliographie</i>, by Robert Fischer, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2010.10.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.10.32. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>Brill's Companion to Warfare in the Bronze Age Aegean</i>, ed. Lynne A. Kvapil and Kim Shelton, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2025.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.08/. [Open access]","H. Craig Melchert, \"Mycenaean and Hittite Diplomatic Correspondence: Fact and Fiction,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 6 May 2020, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/mycenaean-and-hittite-diplomatic-correspondence-fact-and-fiction/. [Open access]","Gregory Nagy, \"East of the Achaeans: Making Up for a Missed Opportunity while Reading Hittite Texts,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 22 July 2015, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/east-of-the-achaeans-making-up-for-a-missed-opportunity-while-reading-hittite-texts/. [Open access]","Steven Karacic, review of <i>1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization Collapsed</i>, by Eric H. Cline, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.08.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.08.37/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/La_Dame_de_Myc%C3%A8nes%2C_fresco.jpg/1920px-La_Dame_de_Myc%C3%A8nes%2C_fresco.jpg","title":"Ahhiyawa","desc":"La Dame de Mycènes, fresque, hauteur : 53 cm. L'expression sérieuse et pensive de la déesse révèle la solennité du moment, alors qu'elle accepte une offrande — un collier qu'elle tient serré dans sa main droite. Elle porte un corsage à manches courtes sur un chemisier qui souligne sa poitrine. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Dame_de_Myc%C3%A8nes,_fresco.jpg","alt":"La Dame de Mycènes, fresco"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-096","num":"96","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one sign covers a family of consonants, k standing for g and k and kh, r for both l and r, and q for the labiovelars.","answer":"Mycenaean Greek","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Greek</b> is the language of the Linear B tablets, an early dialect of Greek and the oldest form of the language on record, and the new standard edition gives it a chapter of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reading it is not simply reading Greek, because the writing that carries it was never designed for the sounds it had to represent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A single sign stands for a whole family of consonants: k covers g and k and kh, p covers b and p and ph, t covers t and th, r covers both l and r, and q covers the labiovelars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Final stops were probably lost by Mycenaean times and are therefore not noted, which is why honey is written <i>me-ri</i> rather than with the consonant it ends in later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aspiration is not systematically noted either, though a special sign is used for it when the vowel is <i>a</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What follows is ambiguity a reader has to resolve from context, since <i>pa-te</i> can be either father or all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It leaves modern transcriptions disagreeing among themselves as well, one word being rendered <i>doelos</i> by some scholars and <i>dohelos</i> by others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some questions cannot be settled from spelling at all, and the conventions of Mycenaean word formation are firm enough that handling them loosely is a fault reviewers name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the dialect sits among the others is a live question, and one innovation, a preposition governing the dative rather than the genitive, is shared with Arcadian, though whether that is real or an illusion has been doubted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Individual words show the language on the move: <i>i-qo</i>, horse, comes from the Indo-European <i>ekwos</i> and turns up aspirated in later Greek as <i>hippos</i>, perhaps through hypercorrection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean Greek","questions":["Because final stops are not written in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, honey appears as <i>me-ri</i>, and <i>pa-te</i> has to be read from context as either father or all.","The word for horse in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is <i>i-qo</i>, from Indo-European <i>ekwos</i>, which turns up aspirated in later Greek as <i>hippos</i>, perhaps by hypercorrection."],"tags":["concept","language","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 3, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 119, no. 3 (2015), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/2170/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","José Miguel Jiménez Delgado, review of <i>Introducción al griego micénico: Gramática, selección de textos y glosario</i>, 2nd ed., by Alberto Bernabé and Eugenio R. Luján, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2021.07.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.36/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of <i>The New Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>, by John Killen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.12.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.20/. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/NIE_1905_Arch%C3%A6ology_-_Mycen%C3%A6an_and_early_Greek_%282%29.png","title":"Mycenaean Greek","desc":"A drawing of Mycenæan and early Greek archeological artifacts: 1. Island idol; 2. Head of bull from Cnossus; 3. Siege scene from silver vase; 4. Gold mask; 5. Silver pin and gold ornament; 6. Gem. From the New International Encyclopaedia 1st edition 1905, volume 1, after page 726. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NIE_1905_Arch%C3%A6ology_-_Mycen%C3%A6an_and_early_Greek_(2).png","alt":"NIE 1905 Archæology - Mycenæan and early Greek (2)"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-097","num":"97","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"After Knossos fell about 1375 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows in settlements at Trianda, Ayia Irini and Phylakopi that had been Minoan in character until then.","answer":"Mycenaean expansion in the Aegean","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominance from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1375 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean expansion in the Aegean</b> is the spread of mainland material culture and influence through the islands and along the Anatolian coast once Crete's dominance had ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the destruction at Knossos about 1375 BCE, mercantile and probably political dominance within the southern Aegean passes from Crete to the kingdoms of the southern Greek mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The change shows in the settlements themselves, since places Minoan in character until the end of Late Minoan IB are by this time Mycenaean, at Trianda on Rhodes, Ayia Irini on Keos, Phylakopi on Melos, and Miletus and Iasos in Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It shows abroad as well, the almost complete absence of Minoan pottery at Amarna being one indication of Mycenaean mercantile dominance within the Aegean by then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Late Helladic IIIA, sites in the Dodecanese, coastal Anatolia, Cyprus, the Levant and the Cyclades were Mycenaeanised to varying degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What that amounts to is carefully limited: Mycenaeans may have lived at some of these places, but none seems to have been a colony or under mainland control, except perhaps Phylakopi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vectors were trade, diplomacy and raiding or warfare, with pottery the most visible export and textiles and perfumed oil beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the seafaring behind it was local rather than imperial, working through maritime small worlds of neighbouring coastscapes held together by regular contact, with Kalamianos serving as Mycenae's own major harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Minoan intermediaries had acted in the earlier exchange networks, and private traders working outside palatial control seem not only possible but probable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The last movement is the sharpest, exported pottery falling away in Late Helladic IIIB and rising steeply in IIIC at Emporio, Enkomi, Kition, Ashdod and Tel Miqne-Ekron, while Cyprus took at least two incursions of Mycenaean refugees, the first reaching Enkomi, Kition, Palaeokastro Maa and Sinda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean expansion in the Aegean","questions":["Trade, diplomacy and raiding were the vectors of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though none of the Mycenaeanised sites seems to have been a colony or under mainland control.","Cyprus took at least two incursions of refugees at the end of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first reaching Enkomi, Kition, Palaeokastro Maa and Sinda."],"tags":["concept","event","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jennifer Moody, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to the Aegean Bronze Age</i>, ed. Cynthia W. Shelmerdine, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 114, no. 4 (2010), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/717/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Jason W. Earle, review of <i>Maritime Networks in the Mycenaean World</i>, by Thomas F. Tartaron, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.08.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.08.34/. [Open access]","Andrea Vianello, review of <i>Mycenaean Greece, Mediterranean Commerce, and the Formation of Identity</i>, by Bryan E. Burns, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/891/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Mycenaean_World_en.png","title":"Mycenaean expansion in the Aegean","desc":"Map of Mycenaean Greece 1400-1200 BCE: Palaces, main cities and other settlements. User:Alexikoua , User:Panthera tigris tigris , TL User:Reedside, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_World_en.png","alt":"Mycenaean World en"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-098","num":"98","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Linear A appears at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Late Minoan IA vessels, at least five of which were made on the spot, in a town that was Minoan before it was Greek.","answer":"Mycenaean Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Minoan from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Miletus</b> is the Bronze Age settlement on the Anatolian coast that later Greeks knew as Miletos, and it was Minoan long before it was Mycenaean, the site going back to about 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the New Palace period it looks like a settlement colony, with abundant Minoan domestic pottery, evidence of Minoan cult, and frescoes and walls executed in Minoan technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Linear A appears there on Late Minoan IA vessels, at least five of which were produced at Miletus itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first of those phases ended in a destruction associated with the eruption of the Theran volcano, tephra in the level showing as much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town was rebuilt and destroyed again at the end of Late Minoan IB or during Late Minoan II, probably along with the other destructions that mark the end of Minoan power in the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From Late Helladic IIIA the Mycenaeans dominate, and the place is one of those whose Minoan character has by then given way to a mainland one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The evidence for it is an overwhelming proportion of Mycenaean pottery and ritual figurines, with a Corridor House and Mycenaean burial rites in the phase that follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavator identifies the town in this period with Millawanda, known from Hittite sources, and a careful weighing of those sources concludes that the evidence is sufficient to support the link.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That matters because Millawanda is where the friction between Hatti and Ahhiyawa is placed, and one of the Ahhiyawa documents is the Milawata letter itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What became of the people is a separate question, and one reading has the Mycenaean population staying where it was with only the aristocracy changing, since Mycenaean remains continue in sufficient quantity from the Mycenaean into the Hittite period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean Miletus","questions":["The excavator identifies <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Millawanda of the Hittite records, and a careful review of the sources finds the evidence sufficient to support the link.","One reading of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has the population staying put with only the aristocracy changing, since Mycenaean remains continue in quantity into the Hittite period."],"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Barbara A. Barletta, review of <i>The Greeks in the East</i>, ed. Alexandra Villing, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.07.65, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.07.65/. [Open access]","P. J. Smith, review of <i>Miletos, the Ornament of Ionia: A History of the City to 400 B.C.E.</i>, by Vanessa B. Gorman, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2002.06.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.06.17/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jorrit M. Kelder, review of <i>Die Ahhijawa-Frage: Mit einer kommentierten Bibliographie</i>, by Robert Fischer, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2010.10.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.10.32. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>Brill's Companion to Warfare in the Bronze Age Aegean</i>, ed. Lynne A. Kvapil and Kim Shelton, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2025.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.08/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Miletus","at":[27.2783,37.5303]}},
{"id":"gr-099","num":"99","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Mycenaean engineers led two rivers into one course running east to the natural sinkholes, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned a shallow Boeotian lake into farmland until the water came back.","answer":"drainage of Lake Copais","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reflooded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>drainage of Lake Copais</b> was the Mycenaean scheme that emptied a shallow seasonal lake in Boeotia by leading the two rivers that fed it to the sinkholes at the basin's north-eastern corner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The basin has no outlet to the sea, so the Boeotian Kephisos pooled there, and Strabo could still write that the lake's outlets were nowhere to be seen except the fissure that admitted the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That fissure was one of the katavothres, crevices worn in the limestone through which the water sank and came up again on the Euboean Gulf, and the height of them set the level the lake stood at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The engineers turned the greater part of the Kephisos out of its own bed into that of the Melas a few kilometres from Orchomenos, so that the two ran east together towards those sinkholes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A levee some 22 kilometres (14 miles) long then held the combined flow off the reclaimed ground, faced along its outer sides with retaining walls of Cyclopean masonry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Those walls were set in a foundation trench packed with clay and small stones, built up in rough courses of boulders, and backed by a core of yellowish lake clay chosen for its plasticity and its waterproofing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They stand today to 2.3 metres (7 feet 6 inches), and about 6 kilometres (4 miles) east of Orchomenos the levee and its two walls together measure 30 metres (98 feet) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the sinkholes silted up and needed clearing, a tunnel was begun beside them at Kephalari, planned to run over 2.2 kilometres (1.4 miles) with 16 shafts sunk to keep it on line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only about a fifth of it was ever cut, the bore stops dead against solid rock, and some hold the work Hellenistic rather than Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery from the levee places its building in Late Helladic IIIB, the water came back near the end of the Mycenaean period once the sinkholes were plugged, and Pausanias still found a lake over most of the Orchomenian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"drainage of Lake Copais","questions":["At Kephalari a tunnel meant to carry water 2.2 kilometres (1.4 miles) to the sea was part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but only a fifth was cut before it stopped against solid rock.","Strabo has Crates of Chalcis clearing the choked channels to renew the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when party strife among the Boeotians stopped him, though many places, he wrote, had already been drained."],"tags":["building","event","technology","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","E. Kountouri et al., \"The Mycenaean Drainage Works of North Kopais, Greece: A New Project Incorporating Surface Surveys, Geophysical Research and Excavation,\" <i>Water Science and Technology: Water Supply</i> 13, no. 3 (2013): 710–18, preprint, https://www.itia.ntua.gr/en/getfile/1360/2/documents/2013WST_WS_MycenaeanDrainage_pp1.pdf. [Open access]","N. Mamassis, S. Moustakas, and N. Zarkadoulas, \"Representing the Operation of Ancient Reclamation Works at Lake Copais in Greece,\" <i>Water History</i> (2015), preprint, https://www.itia.ntua.gr/en/getfile/1539/1/documents/Copais_WH10_9_14.pdf. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.2.18–19, trans. H. L. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D2%3Asection%3D18. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 9.38.6–7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-100","num":"100","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Bridges of Cyclopean boulders on the line from Tiryns to Epidauros are the best-preserved pieces of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and two of them still carry farm traffic today.","answer":"Mycenaean road network","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1190 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean road network</b> is the system of built roads that joined the citadels of the Argive plain and ran on beyond it, carried over broken ground on terraces and crossing ravines on bridges of Cyclopean boulders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its best-known stretch runs from Tiryns towards Epidauros, where four such structures survive near Arkadiko, two of them still crossed today for local farm work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are built of unwrought limestone blocks laid without mortar, each opening roofed by courses stepped inward until they meet, a corbel rather than a true arch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The floor of an opening is a laid slab that steadied its walls and kept the stream from washing them out, which argues for real hydraulic experience in the builders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An abutment near Mycenae stands 4.7 metres (15 feet) above a dry bed some 40 metres (130 feet) wide, so that crossing probably took several spans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The same abutment measures 5 metres (16 feet) across, leaving a usable roadway of about 3.8 metres (12 feet) once side parapets are allowed for, enough for two vehicles to pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smaller streams were taken under the road instead of over it, through stone channels roofed in the same stepped fashion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Boeotia may show the engineering in another form, since the retaining walls of the Copais levee created an artificial plateau along which a road could have run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pausanias, going from Argos past the ruins of Tiryns towards Epidauros, still called that route the highway, and he described the Tirynthian walls as the work of the Cyclopes, stones so large a pair of mules could not stir the smallest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nothing so deliberately built appears in Greece again for centuries, though a later network of cart roads across the Peloponnese, known from wheel ruts cut in bedrock to a gauge of about 1.4 metres (4 feet 7 inches), has been placed by some in the archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean road network","questions":["An abutment near Mycenae stands 4.7 metres (15 feet) above a dry bed 40 metres (130 feet) wide, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> probably included a bridge of several spans.","Pausanias still called the way from Argos past Tiryns to Epidauros the highway, roughly the line taken by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> more than a thousand years before him."],"tags":["building","object","technology","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Sławomir Karaś and Nien-Tsu Tuan, \"The World's Oldest Bridges: Mycenaean Bridges,\" <i>American Journal of Civil Engineering and Architecture</i> 5, no. 6 (2017): 237–44, https://doi.org/10.12691/ajcea-5-6-3. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.25.7–9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","D. Graham J. Shipley, review of <i>Verkehrswege der Argolis: Rekonstruktion und historische Bedeutung</i>, by Klaus Tausend, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.03.40. [Open access]","E. Kountouri et al., \"The Mycenaean Drainage Works of North Kopais, Greece: A New Project Incorporating Surface Surveys, Geophysical Research and Excavation,\" <i>Water Science and Technology: Water Supply</i> 13, no. 3 (2013): 710–18, preprint, https://www.itia.ntua.gr/en/getfile/1360/2/documents/2013WST_WS_MycenaeanDrainage_pp1.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Arkadiko_bridge%2C_mycenaean%2C_Late_Heladic_age%2C_202418.jpg/1920px-Arkadiko_bridge%2C_mycenaean%2C_Late_Heladic_age%2C_202418.jpg","title":"Mycenaean road network","desc":"Arkadiko (or Kazarma) bridge, Argolis, Peloponnesos, Greece. Mycenaean (Achaean) construction, Late Bronze age, Late Helladic III (probably III B), 1340/1300 to 1190/1100 BCE. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arkadiko_bridge,_mycenaean,_Late_Heladic_age,_202418.jpg","alt":"Arkadiko bridge, mycenaean, Late Heladic age"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-101","num":"101","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Four small towers stand eight or nine metres (26 to 31 feet) apart on the northern face of the best-preserved stretch of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the side from which its builders expected attack.","answer":"Isthmian wall","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Isthmian wall</b> is a fortification of Cyclopean masonry laid across the Isthmus of Corinth, the only land approach to the Peloponnese, late in the Mycenaean palace period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its line can be followed about two kilometres (1.2 miles) inland from the Saronic Gulf to a deep gully; beyond that nothing certain survives, and it has never been traced to the Corinthian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is two faces of large unworked stones packed out with smaller ones around a rubble core, 3.6 to 4 metres (12 to 13 feet) thick and standing in one place nearly 2.5 metres (8 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The longest unbroken run measures 45.5 metres (149 feet) and carries four small towers projecting about 0.7 metres (2 feet 4 inches) from the face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every tower is on the north side, and that face is the higher and better built, so its builders expected attack from the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its date rests on the masonry, which is that of the Mycenaean citadels, and on some seventy Mycenaean sherds, a stirrup jar and a pitcher among them dating to the turn from Late Helladic IIIB to IIIC, about 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the generation in which Mycenae, Tiryns and Athens finished their own circuits and drove passages to water outside them, the first clear sign of a fear of siege in the Bronze Age Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No settlement in the Corinthia was large enough to have paid for such a barrier, so Oscar Broneer, who cleared it, took it for a joint work of the Argolid kingdoms, a first line of defence before their citadels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence is thinner than that argument needs: only one stretch yielded Mycenaean pottery wedged among its own stones, and a study of Cyclopean building holds the stretches differ too much to be one structure, dating the finest after the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Broneer himself allowed good reason to doubt it was ever finished, on a line others tried again: a wall against the Persians in 480 BCE, and at last the Hexamilion, barring the way south for a thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Isthmian wall","questions":["The only land route into the Peloponnese was fortified late in the Bronze Age by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a barrier of unworked boulders traced for about two kilometres (1.2 miles).","Because no Corinthian settlement was large enough to have raised it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been read as evidence that the kingdoms of the Argolid could still act together around 1200 BCE."],"tags":["building","warfare","technology","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Oscar Broneer, \"The Cyclopean Wall on the Isthmus of Corinth and Its Bearing on Late Bronze Age Chronology,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 35, no. 4 (1966): 346–62, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147564.pdf. [Open access]","Oscar Broneer, \"The Cyclopean Wall on the Isthmus of Corinth, Addendum,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 37, no. 1 (1968): 25–35, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147536.pdf. [Open access]","Nancy Claire Loader, \"The Definition of Cyclopean: An Investigation into the Origins of the LH III Fortifications on Mainland Greece\" (PhD diss., Durham University, 1995), 166–67, https://etheses.durham.ac.uk/id/eprint/5374/. [Open access]","William R. Caraher and Timothy E. Gregory, \"Fortifications of Mount Oneion, Corinthia,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 75, no. 3 (2006): 327–56, https://doi.org/10.2972/hesp.75.3.327. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.71, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Korinth_Isthmus_de.png","title":"Isthmian wall","desc":"Karte des Isthmus von Korinth. Fremantleboy 15:25, 18 June 2007 (UTC), CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korinth_Isthmus_de.png","alt":"Korinth Isthmus de"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-102","num":"102","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Nine cities stand one on top of another on the mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from a settlement of about 3000 BCE up to the Roman town on the summit.","answer":"Troy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 BCE – 500 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy VI from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Troy</b> is the city on the mound of Hisarlık in the north-west corner of Anatolia, called Ilios in Homer and Ilion by the Greeks and Romans after them, and lived on for some 4,500 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was destroyed and rebuilt so often that the site is a stack of cities rather than one, each levelling the wreckage of the last; excavators count nine, Troy I at the bottom and Roman Troy IX on the summit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first was a village of about 3000 BCE, the second a small walled citadel that burned, the Burnt City that Schliemann took for Homer's Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three poorer settlements followed, and the hill seems to have stood empty for about 110 years after the third, the fourth city beginning around 2060 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From about 1750 BCE the sixth city rose over them, its sloping walls of great stones the most conspicuous thing on the mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What that city amounted to is still argued: one side reads the evidence as the capital of a significant regional power in western Anatolia, the other holds it cannot even be proven to have been a city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mound had probably been identified as the Troy of legend by the beginning of the Archaic period, and that identity proved worth more than the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the 7th century BCE the Lokrians sent two aristocratic daughters a year to serve in the sanctuary of Athena Ilias, in atonement for Ajax's rape of Kassandra, and the townspeople might kill them if they were caught outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Athens planted its first colony in the eastern Aegean at Sigeion nearby, beside the mounds it took for the graves of Achilles, Patroklos and Ajax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Xerxes climbed the citadel of Priam on his march into Greece and sacrificed a thousand cattle to Athena of Ilion, and it was the Greek and Roman town that finished the Bronze Age city off, the terrace cut for Athena's temple destroying the middle of the old citadel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Troy","questions":["The Lokrians sent two aristocratic daughters a year to serve Athena at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in atonement for Ajax's rape of Kassandra at the fall of the city.","Xerxes turned aside on his march into Greece to climb the citadel of Priam at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and sacrifice a thousand cattle to Athena of Ilion."],"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","greece","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Wilhelm Dörpfeld, <i>Troja und Ilion: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen in den vorhistorischen und historischen Schichten von Ilion, 1870–1894</i> (Athens: Beck &amp; Barth, 1902), 26–35, https://archive.org/details/trojaundilionerg00drpf. [Open access]","Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Donald Easton and Bernhard Weninger, \"Troy III–V: New Radiocarbon Dates Confirm a Gap in Blegen's Sequence,\" <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 50 (2023): 396–419, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.50.14. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Troy VI: A Trading Center and Commercial City?\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 577–613, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.577. [Open access]","C. Brian Rose, \"Separating Fact from Fiction in the Aiolian Migration,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 77, no. 3 (2008): 399–430, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/40205756.pdf. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 7.43, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Legendary_walls_of_Troy_%288708672267%29.jpg/1920px-Legendary_walls_of_Troy_%288708672267%29.jpg","title":"Troy","desc":"The legendary walls of Troy. Jorge Láscar, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Legendary_walls_of_Troy_(8708672267).jpg","alt":"The stone city walls at the site of Troy, with visitors on the path beside them"},"difficulty":1,"locator":{"name":"Troy","at":[26.2389,39.9575]}},
{"id":"gr-103","num":"103","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The houses crowded inside the patched walls of the old citadel, each with storage jars sunk below its floor, belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which then burned.","answer":"Troy VIIa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy VI</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy VIIa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1180 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Troy VIIa</b> is the settlement raised in the ruins of Troy VI at Hisarlık in north-west Anatolia, and the layer most often put forward as the city Homer's poems besiege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Troy VI, standing from about 1750 BCE, was the grandest thing built on the mound: a horseshoe circuit of dressed limestone over 4 metres (13 feet) thick and 9 metres (30 feet) high in places, carried higher in mudbrick, with five gates and projecting towers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its foundations were bedded on a cushion of earth rather than rock, perhaps against earthquakes, and if so it did not work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the end of phase VIh the walls came down in what most authorities call an earthquake, collapsed stonework everywhere and no convincing sign of fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reading moved Homer's city one layer up, and some take the wooden horse of the story for a memory of the earthquake, the horse being the animal of Poseidon, who shakes the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The survivors rebuilt inside the patched circuit far more tightly: sturdy houses of one to three rooms sharing party walls, set in places against the inner face of the wall, which Troy VI kept clear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their floors are honeycombed with pits holding storage jars capped by stone slabs, often read as stocking up for a siege, though these houses stood one storey high and had nowhere else to put the stores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Troy VIIa then burned in a general fire that took the buildings inside the citadel and out, leaving human bones unburied in the wreckage, so its end is credited to violence rather than the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The date is argued: Blegen first said about 1240 BCE and later raised it to 1270, others bring it down to 1200 or 1190 on the latest Mycenaean pottery, and the present excavators end it about 1175.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor does everyone accept the promotion: to its critics Troy VIIa is a poor village in the ruins of a great city, and the Greeks put the war anywhere between 1334 and 1184 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Troy VIIa","questions":["Blegen argued that an earthquake, not an army, brought Troy VI down, and that the city Homer sang of was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the crowded settlement raised in its ruins.","Human bones lay unburied in the fire-scarred ruins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why its end, unlike the fall of the city beneath it, is credited to violence."],"tags":["place","city","era","warfare","history","archaeology","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 23: Troy VI,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-23-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 27: Troy VII and the Historicity of the Trojan War,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-27-narrative/. [Open access]","George E. Mylonas, \"Priam's Troy and the Date of Its Fall,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 33, no. 4 (1964): 352–80, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147283.pdf. [Open access]","Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Donald Easton and Bernhard Weninger, \"Troy III–V: New Radiocarbon Dates Confirm a Gap in Blegen's Sequence,\" <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 50 (2023): 396–419, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.50.14. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Mycenaean_Troy_p034-Plan_II.jpg","title":"Troy VIIa","desc":"Mycenaean Troy, Plan II - Citadel of II Stratum. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_Troy_p034-Plan_II.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean Troy p034-Plan II"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Troy","at":[26.2389,39.9575]}},
{"id":"gr-104","num":"104","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A rock-cut ditch traced for 600 metres (about 660 yards) south of the citadel is the best evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its size is still fought over.","answer":"lower city of Troy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inner ditch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silted up by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1175 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>lower city of Troy</b> is the settlement outside the citadel walls at Hisarlık, and the argument over its size is the argument over whether Bronze Age Troy was a city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The citadel is small, 2 to 3 hectares (5 to 7 acres), climbing in concentric terraces whose buildings are set along radii of the circle rather than parallel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Large free-standing houses stand on the lowest and best preserved terrace, but no palace survives: the crown of the mound was shaved away in Hellenistic and Roman times below the Bronze Age levels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blegen took a town outside the walls for granted, writing that an extramural lower town of undetermined size certainly existed, and Troy VIIa's fire took buildings beyond the citadel as well as within.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nobody tested it until magnetic survey and trial trenches crossed the plateau to the south from 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> About 400 metres (440 yards) out they found a ditch cut into the rock, 4 metres (13 feet) wide and 1 to 2 metres (3 to 7 feet) deep, traced for over 600 metres (660 yards) without reaching an end, with a second ditch further out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line breaks in places, and at one break the rock is cut for the posts of a wooden palisade behind a causeway, which is a gate, hard to explain otherwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pottery and radiocarbon put the inner ditch in the 15th century BCE, silting up towards the end of Troy VI and through Troy VIIa, and a cemetery lies between the two, so the town had spread over its own graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the excavators' reading that gives a defended lower city of 25 to 35 hectares (62 to 86 acres) and 5,000 to 6,000 people, ten times the citadel and the size of Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their chief critic reads the same ground as some 11 hectares (27 acres) and at most 2,000 people, takes the ditches for quarries or water channels, and notes that Mycenae's acropolis is nearly twice the size of Troy's — to which the answer is that water runs neither uphill nor over the gaps in a ditch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"lower city of Troy","questions":["Magnetic survey and trial trenches from 1992 found the two ditches and the palisade gate that are now the case for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Blegen had assumed but never tested.","Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covered 35 hectares (86 acres) and held 6,000 people, or 11 hectares (27 acres) and held 2,000, decides whether the place deserves to be called a city."],"tags":["place","city","building","history","archaeology","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Troy VI: A Trading Center and Commercial City?\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 577–613, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.577. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 23: Troy VI,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-23-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 27: Troy VII and the Historicity of the Trojan War,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-27-narrative/. [Open access]","Wilhelm Dörpfeld, <i>Troja und Ilion: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen in den vorhistorischen und historischen Schichten von Ilion, 1870–1894</i> (Athens: Beck &amp; Barth, 1902), 33, 107–82, https://archive.org/details/trojaundilionerg00drpf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Troy","at":[26.2389,39.9575]}},
{"id":"gr-105","num":"105","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Because the current runs out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the wind blows down it from the north-east, a sailing ship bound for the Black Sea had to wait at the mouth.","answer":"Dardanelles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Beşik Bay graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1360 – 1320 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dardanelles</b> is the narrow strait between the Aegean and the Sea of Marmara, the only sea route from the Mediterranean to the Black Sea, and Troy stands at its mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Troy's site is a crossroads: a shallow bay beside it, its hinterland's largest river reaching the sea there, and beyond that the water dividing Europe from Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs the wrong way for anyone going north, its outflow of Black Sea water exceeding every river of the northern Aegean put together, and the winds prevail from the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A sailing ship therefore cannot simply go in; it waits at the mouth for a day that allows it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beşik Bay, 8 kilometres (5 miles) south-west of Troy, is the first natural harbour below the entrance and the obvious place to wait, with a cemetery of the late 14th century BCE beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Manfred Korfmann built a large claim on that delay: crews held up for weeks or months had to be fed, and Troy grew rich on them as a hub of trade between the Aegean, Anatolia, Thrace and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His colleagues put it more carefully, calling Troy a gateway settlement at the edge of the Bronze Age urban world, where two economies met under a local power's protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what they cite lies at the far end of the route: 150 stone anchors of Aegean and Near Eastern type off the Bulgarian coast, oxhide ingots from Bulgaria and the Marmara shores, and a ceremonial axe of Pontic origin aboard the Uluburun ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their chief critic answers that nothing on the mound points to the Black Sea, that no Bronze Age stone anchor has turned up at Beşik Bay or at Troy, and that no harbour settlement has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He would as soon send goods overland across the Thracian peninsula, which beats waiting months for a wind, and a year later still wrote that Mycenaean pottery is absent from the Black Sea and Black Sea imports from Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dardanelles","questions":["Beşik Bay is the first natural harbour below the entrance to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why Troy's excavators made it the anchorage where ships waited for a favourable wind.","Troy was said to have grown rich on crews delayed at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though its chief critic replies that nothing dug up on the mound points to the Black Sea at all."],"tags":["place","geography","history","archaeology","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Troy VI: A Trading Center and Commercial City?\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 577–613, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.577. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 23: Troy VI,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-23-narrative/. [Open access]","Vassiliki H. Kourafalou and Kostas Tsiaras, \"A Nested Circulation Model for the North Aegean Sea,\" <i>Ocean Science</i> 3, no. 1 (2007): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.5194/os-3-1-2007. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to P. Jablonka and C. B. Rose,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 109, no. 1 (2005), https://ajaonline.org/wp-content/uploads/2011/08/1091_Kolb.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Graphic_map_of_the_Dardanelles.JPG/1920px-Graphic_map_of_the_Dardanelles.JPG","title":"Dardanelles","desc":"Map of the Dardanelles drawn by G.F. Morrell, 1915. The map shows the Gallipoli peninsula and west coast of Turkey, and the location of front line troops and landings. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graphic_map_of_the_Dardanelles.JPG","alt":"Graphic map of the Dardanelles"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"locator":{"name":"Dardanelles","at":[26.4,40.2]}},
{"id":"gr-106","num":"106","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A Hittite treaty of the 13th century BCE binds a ruler named Alaksandu, whose land <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> occupied the Troad, to the great king at Hattusa.","answer":"Wilusa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Hittite texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alaksandu treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Wilusa</b> is a land named in the Hittite archives at Hattusa, one of the four vassal states into which the kingdom of Arzawa in western Anatolia was divided, and the one that held the Troad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its neighbours were Mira, which covered Ionia, the Seha River Land running from Adramyttion to Smyrna and taking in Lesbos, and Hapalla inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hittites counted the people of Arzawa as allies but not as equals, and the whole coast was fought over between Hatti and the kingdom their scribes called Ahhiyawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wilusa sits at the centre of that quarrel: a letter from the Hittite king to the king of Ahhiyawa implies that the two had at last settled a dispute over who held it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fullest document is a treaty of the 13th century BCE between Muwattalli II and Alaksandu, ruler of Wilusa, in which the Hittite king takes for granted that Alaksandu's income comes from vineyards, threshing floors, fields and stock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What makes Wilusa matter to Greek history is a pair of names: Wilusa answers to Wilios, the older form of Ilios, and Alaksandu to Alexandros, the other name of the Trojan prince Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most scholars now take Wilusa for the state whose capital stood at Hisarlık, which would make Bronze Age Troy a real kingdom with a named king corresponding with an empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Hittite evidence puts that land firmly in north-western Anatolia, which is the standing answer to attempts to move Homer's Troy to Cilicia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Against it stands the fact that Wilusa is never named outside an Anatolian context, and that nothing written has been dug up at Troy: since Hittite kings exchanged letters with Wilusa's rulers an archive must have existed there, and finding it is the one thing that would settle the question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The names are meanwhile a pattern rather than a proof, since history is turned into myth as readily as myth is turned into history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Wilusa","questions":["The name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answers to Wilios, the older form of Ilios, and its ruler Alaksandu to Alexandros, which is the other name Homer gives the Trojan prince Paris.","Hatti and Ahhiyawa quarrelled over who held <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a letter between their kings implies that the dispute was settled in the end."],"tags":["place","state","history","archaeology","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["C. Brian Rose, \"Separating Fact from Fiction in the Aiolian Migration,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 77, no. 3 (2008): 399–430, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/40205756.pdf. [Open access]","Gregory Nagy, \"East of the Achaeans: Making Up for a Missed Opportunity while Reading Hittite Texts,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 22 July 2015, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/east-of-the-achaeans-making-up-for-a-missed-opportunity-while-reading-hittite-texts/. [Open access]","Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Troy VI: A Trading Center and Commercial City?\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 577–613, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.577. [Open access]","Catalin Pavel, review of <i>Lag Troia in Kilikien? Der aktuelle Streit um Homers Ilias</i>, ed. Christoph Ulf and Robert Rollinger, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.10.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.10.03/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/Mycenaean_Troy_p040-Plan_III.jpg","title":"Wilusa","desc":"Mycenaean Troy, Plan III - Citadel of Mycenaean Troy. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_Troy_p040-Plan_III.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean Troy p040-Plan III"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-107","num":"107","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Destructions spread over 150 years and 6 million square kilometres (2.3 million square miles) are what historians gather under the name the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though no single cause explains them.","answer":"Late Bronze Age collapse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Destructions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek palaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1150 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Late Bronze Age collapse</b> is the run of destructions, abandonments and political failures that ended the palace states of the eastern Mediterranean between the middle of the 13th century BCE and the end of the 12th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not one event: the changes fall at different times over some 6 million square kilometres (2.3 million square miles), from Greece in the west to the borders of Mesopotamia, and from Anatolia down to Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Greece the palaces at Mycenae, Tiryns, Midea, Thebes and Pylos burned late in Late Helladic IIIB or early in IIIC, roughly between 1200 and 1150 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the east Ugarit ended altogether, its last cuneiform letters reporting enemy ships off the coast and begging neighbours for help, and Hittite Anatolia was among the regions worst hit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whatever else happened, international trade fell away deeply, and it would be folly to pretend that did not matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The explanations offered are legion: migration, attack from outside, political struggle within the palaces or among them, the gap between centres and peripheries, climate change, earthquakes and plague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strongest scientific case is the climatic one, since pollen and isotopes from Cyprus and Syria record an abrupt dry episode, and one study reads famine, then seaborne invasion, then political and economic collapse in sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against that, a proposed earthquake storm of 1225 to 1175 BCE is unsupported by the study of ancient faults, and the dates themselves are unsettled, radiocarbon from northern Greece running 70 to 100 years earlier than the conventional chronology of the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Societies survive droughts, so the reading now favoured makes climate a catalyst rather than a trigger, with collapse coming out of several stresses cascading together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word itself is doing too much work: Cyprus, several Levantine towns and parts of Crete survived or flourished, so what happened is better called a decline with clear cultural continuity in many places, and the popular framing of a single year is a place to start an argument, not a date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Late Bronze Age collapse","questions":["Because societies survive droughts, the dry episode recorded in Cyprus and Syria is now read as a catalyst of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than its trigger.","Cyprus, several Levantine towns and parts of Crete came through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and even flourished, which is why the word itself is thought to claim too much."],"tags":["event","era","history","archaeology","climate","greece","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Steven Karacic, review of <i>1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization Collapsed</i>, by Eric H. Cline, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.08.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.08.37/. [Open access]","Kenneth Wardle, Thomas Higham, and Bernd Kromer, \"Dating the End of the Greek Bronze Age: A Robust Radiocarbon-Based Chronology from Assiros Toumba,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 9 (2014): e106672, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0106672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III._Tempel_Nordostwand_Abzeichnung_01.jpg/1920px-Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III._Tempel_Nordostwand_Abzeichnung_01.jpg","title":"Late Bronze Age collapse","desc":"Resignature of the \"war of sea peoples\" on the northeast wall of the temple of the dead Ramses III in Medinet Habu, Egypt. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III._Tempel_Nordostwand_Abzeichnung_01.jpg","alt":"Medinet Habu Ramses III. Tempel Nordostwand Abzeichnung 01"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-108","num":"108","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Ramesses III's temple at Medinet Habu names five of the groups gathered under the modern label the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and shows Egypt beating them on land and at sea.","answer":"Sea Peoples","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the records</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ramesses III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">year 8 of his reign</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Sea Peoples</b> is the modern label for a set of groups named in Egyptian, Hittite and Ugaritic records between the 14th and the 11th centuries BCE, long treated as the destroyers of the Bronze Age world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fullest account is Ramesses III's inscription at Medinet Habu, which names the Peleset, Tjeker, Shekelesh, Denyen and Weshesh and says they laid waste Hatti, Arzawa, Qode, Alashiya and Carchemish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its text has northerners in their isles disturbed and swept away at once, nobody standing before them, and a camp set up in Amurru; the temple walls then show Egypt beating them on land and at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Other names turn up elsewhere and earlier: the Sherden, Ekwesh, Shekelesh, Teresh and Lukka in Merneptah's fifth year, the Lukka and Sherden already in the Amarna letters, and the Shikila who live on ships in a letter from Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The names are spread over four centuries and several archives, not confined to one invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Egyptian texts are propaganda before they are chronicles, and Egyptologists have questioned the date and the historicity of the Medinet Habu account alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One study of how foreigners are shown in those reliefs concludes that their purpose was to record the king's own actions rather than an invasion, and doubts that 150 years of scholarship built on them can establish one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor does anyone know how far the movements reached or whether these peoples ever acted as one force, their proposed origins running from Sardinia to the Balkans, Anatolia and the steppe beyond Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The confident equations of the older books, making the Peleset Cretans and forerunners of the Philistines, the Tjeker Sicilians and the Denyen the Danaans of Greek epic, were read off the shapes of the names, and such groups are still seldom identified rigorously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is a real pattern of seaborne violence and displaced people, now read as one strand of a wider crisis rather than its cause, and tied by some to famine after an abrupt drought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Sea Peoples","questions":["The Sherden and Lukka appear in the Amarna letters and the Peleset in Ramesses III's eighth year, so the names collected as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are spread over four centuries.","Because their proposed origins run from Sardinia to the steppe beyond Mesopotamia, nobody can say whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ever acted together as a single force."],"tags":["people","event","warfare","history","archaeology","migration","bronze age"],"sources":["A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Records of Egypt</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Twentieth to the Twenty-sixth Dynasties</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1906), §§59–64, https://archive.org/details/ancient-records-of-egypt-vol-4_202012. [Open access]","The Epigraphic Survey, <i>Medinet Habu</i>, vol. 1, <i>Earlier Historical Records of Ramses III</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 8 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1930), https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip8.pdf. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","Steven Karacic, review of <i>1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization Collapsed</i>, by Eric H. Cline, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.08.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.08.37/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Champollion%27s_description_of_the_peoples_named_on_the_Second_Pylon_at_Medinet_Habu.png","title":"Sea Peoples","desc":"Champollion's description of the peoples named on the Second Pylon at Medinet Habu. w:Jean-François Champollion. 1832. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Champollion%27s_description_of_the_peoples_named_on_the_Second_Pylon_at_Medinet_Habu.png","alt":"Champollion's description of the peoples named on the Second Pylon at Medinet Habu"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-109","num":"109","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Burnt cattle bones from a feast held shortly before it lay in the Archives Complex when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended the Palace of Nestor for good.","answer":"destruction of Pylos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palace burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1180 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruins reused</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to the 10th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>destruction of Pylos</b> is the fire that ended the Palace of Nestor in Messenia, in the south-west of the Peloponnese, and the palace was never rebuilt after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pottery from the destruction levels dates it closely to the turn from Late Helladic IIIB to IIIC, and a stalagmite from a cave nearby puts it at roughly 3,150 to 3,130 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The building burned hot enough to leave hard red mudbrick and a black char across its floors, and its upper storey came down on top of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a corner of room 7 of the Archives Complex lay the burnt bones of at least ten cattle, the remains of one sacrifice and feast held not long before the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The kingdom's own records show its bronze-workers short of copper, and lists of men posted as watchers by the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Those watchers have been read as a kingdom bracing for attack from the water, and the palace as having fallen to a surprise raid by the people it was watching for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is offered as an interpretation rather than a finding, since the tablets record an administration going about its year and nothing in them announces an emergency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Climate supplies no trigger either: the cave record shows no clear match in time between the destruction and drier conditions, though the aridity that followed probably cut yields and made any recovery harder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What came after was emptier here than anywhere else in southern Greece, Messenia losing more of its people in the following century than any other region, and the Aegean as a whole was among the worst hit parts of the Bronze Age world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ridge was not quite abandoned, though: rooms of the main building still standing were used again between the fire and the early 10th century BCE, and people settled on and around the ruins afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"destruction of Pylos","questions":["Lists of men posted as watchers by the sea are why some read the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a surprise raid from the water, though nothing in the archive announces an emergency.","A stalagmite from a cave near the palace shows no match in time between drier conditions and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which removes climate as its trigger."],"tags":["event","place","warfare","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Shannon LaFayette Hogue, \"New Evidence of Post-Destruction Reuse in the Main Building of the Palace of Nestor at Pylos,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 151–57, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0151. [Open access]","Martin Finné et al., \"Late Bronze Age Climate Change and the Destruction of the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor at Pylos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 12 (2017): e0189447, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0189447. [Open access]","Sharon R. Stocker and Jack L. Davis, \"Animal Sacrifice, Archives, and Feasting at the Palace of Nestor,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 73, no. 2 (2004): 179–95, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/4134892.pdf. [Open access]","A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/Griffin%2C_Golden_Seal_from_Pylos.jpg","title":"destruction of Pylos","desc":"Griffin from a golden signet bead, unearthed in a tomb in Pylos, Messinia, by american archaeologist Carl Blegen in 1953 (NAMA 7986 - National Archaeological Museum of Athens, Hellenic Ministry of Culture). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Griffin,_Golden_Seal_from_Pylos.jpg","alt":"Griffin, Golden Seal from Pylos"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-110","num":"110","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Houses were rebuilt inside the walls of Mycenae and the lower town at Tiryns went up again during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but no palace was ever raised in either place.","answer":"Postpalatial Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1050 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mycenae again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 – 1125 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Postpalatial Greece</b> is the 12th century BCE on the Greek mainland, between the burning of the palaces and the Dark Age, and is called Late Helladic IIIC.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The palaces were never rebuilt, but the citadels were not abandoned: at Mycenae houses went up again inside the walls, on old foundations and new, and the Granary beside the Lion Gate stayed in use well into the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Tiryns the lower town was rebuilt after the flood that wrecked its predecessor, and a large hall with a central hearth and three columns in a row went up outside the walls late in the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A life-sized painted woman from Mycenae shows fresco was still a major art somewhere, and substantial building of the period is known at Lefkandi, Asine, Korakou and Teichos Dymaion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What did not come back was the palace itself, with its writing and its accounting; Pylos shows the pattern in miniature, rooms still standing lived in a while, then people settled on the ruins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The century was violent: Mycenae burned again about 1150 to 1125 BCE, the great hall outside Tiryns went at much the same time, and Lefkandi's levels record destruction after destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Messenia and Laconia are nearly empty of it, and by the end the citadels of Athens and Mycenae were burial grounds, which is what abandonment looks like on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two funeral customs arrive in its second half: cremation, at Perati in Attica and Ialysos on Rhodes, and burial singly in cist graves, now taken for a revival of older mainland practice rather than an import.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The end of Mycenaean civilisation was therefore not the fall of the palaces but a slide of a century or more after it, and its date is unsettled, radiocarbon from northern Greece running 70 to 100 years earlier than the southern chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lasting change is regional: the uniform Mycenaean culture of the 14th and 13th centuries broke into local styles, and Greece stayed local for centuries after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Postpalatial Greece","questions":["Cremation at Perati and burial singly in cist graves both spread through the second half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the century after the palaces burned.","Because the uniform Mycenaean culture broke into local styles during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Greece stayed a patchwork of regions for the next seven hundred years."],"tags":["era","event","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Shannon LaFayette Hogue, \"New Evidence of Post-Destruction Reuse in the Main Building of the Palace of Nestor at Pylos,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 151–57, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0151. [Open access]","Kenneth Wardle, Thomas Higham, and Bernd Kromer, \"Dating the End of the Greek Bronze Age: A Robust Radiocarbon-Based Chronology from Assiros Toumba,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 9 (2014): e106672, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0106672. [Open access]","A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/AGM_Ancient_Greek_Pair_of_Terracotta_Boots.jpg/1920px-AGM_Ancient_Greek_Pair_of_Terracotta_Boots.jpg","title":"Postpalatial Greece","desc":"Ancient Greek pair of terracotta boots. Early geometric period cremation burial of a woman, 900 BCE. Ancient Agora Museum in Athens. Sharon Mollerus, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AGM_Ancient_Greek_Pair_of_Terracotta_Boots.jpg","alt":"AGM Ancient Greek Pair of Terracotta Boots"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-111","num":"111","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Greece kept no written records at all through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so what is known of those centuries comes almost entirely from graves and pottery.","answer":"Greek Dark Ages","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protogeometric</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Greek Dark Ages</b> is the name long given to the centuries between the end of Mycenaean civilisation and the Archaic period, running roughly from 1050 to 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The darkness is partly the period's own and partly ours: writing, palace buildings and the economies that ran on them had ceased around 1200 BCE, and Greece kept no records again for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is left is mostly graves and pots, and the data remain so limited that almost anything said beyond a single site rests on theory rather than evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The label is contested as well, borrowed as it was from medieval Europe: specialists argue over Dark Age, Early Iron Age and naming the period after its pottery instead, and none of the three has carried the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even the dates shift, radiocarbon from northern Greece running 70 to 100 years earlier than the conventional chronology of the south, and the Protogeometric phase has been argued to open about 1020 rather than 1050 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It is not three and a half blank centuries, though.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The material sorts into phases, Submycenaean first, then Protogeometric from about 1050 to 900 and Geometric after it, which means the pottery kept changing steadily enough to be followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trade across the sea continued at a lower level: amphoras made in the Thessalian and Euboean area between about 800 and 675 BCE reached Troy, Antissa on Lesbos, and coastal sites in the Chalkidike, Thessaly and Euboea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cult places were kept up too, one of them at Troy built against the ruined Bronze Age wall, where 28 stone-paved circles were laid out about 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The period was therefore a poorer, more local and far less visible Greece rather than an empty one, and how dark it looks still depends a good deal on which regions have been dug and published.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek Dark Ages","questions":["Trade across the sea did not stop in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: amphoras made in Thessaly and Euboea between about 800 and 675 BCE reached Troy and Lesbos.","Specialists dispute the very name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, borrowed from medieval Europe, and offer Early Iron Age or a pottery phase in its place."],"tags":["era","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]","Paul D. Scotton, review of <i>The Aegean from Bronze Age to Iron Age: Continuity and Change Between the Twelfth and Eighth Centuries BC</i>, by O. T. P. K. Dickinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.62, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.62/. [Open access]","Kenneth Wardle, Thomas Higham, and Bernd Kromer, \"Dating the End of the Greek Bronze Age: A Robust Radiocarbon-Based Chronology from Assiros Toumba,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 9 (2014): e106672, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0106672. [Open access]","C. Brian Rose, \"Separating Fact from Fiction in the Aiolian Migration,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 77, no. 3 (2008): 399–430, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/40205756.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Early_Geometric_Jewelry_from_the_Areopagus_of_Athens._Ancient_Agora_Museum_of_Athens.jpg/1920px-Early_Geometric_Jewelry_from_the_Areopagus_of_Athens._Ancient_Agora_Museum_of_Athens.jpg","title":"Greek Dark Ages","desc":"14-16: Broad gold finger rings. 17-19: Gold finger rings 20: Pair of gold earrings with trapezoid endings Finds are part of an early Geometric Cremation Burial of a Pregnant Wealthy Woman (from the N.W. of the Areopagus). About 850 BCE. Ancient Agora Museum (Athens). Dorieo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Geometric_Jewelry_from_the_Areopagus_of_Athens._Ancient_Agora_Museum_of_Athens.jpg","alt":"Early Geometric Jewelry from the Areopagus of Athens. Ancient Agora Museum of Athens"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-112","num":"112","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Athenians of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up the family chamber tomb for single graves cut in earth or lined with stone slabs, and began burying iron beside bronze.","answer":"Submycenaean period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1070 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protogeometric</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1020 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Submycenaean period</b> is the phase of Greek pottery and burial between the last Mycenaean styles and the Protogeometric, known above all from the cemeteries of Athens and of Salamis across the bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre is the Kerameikos, where graves dug near the Pompeion and the Dipylon Gate since the 1960s were published as tombs 115 to 148.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burial had changed shape: where Mycenaean families lay together in rock-cut chamber tombs, the dead now went singly into pits, into boxes lined with stone slabs, or into wooden coffins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A few were burnt instead and their ashes gathered into an amphora, and what went in with them is modest rather than poor: weapons and tools, pins and fibulae, beads of glass, faience and amber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Early Iron Age graves of the Athenian Agora hold a different gathering of people from the Kerameikos, most of the adults being women, with infants and children of every age unusually well represented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Iron had at first served only for rings and other small ornaments, and it is in the decades closing the phase that iron swords, daggers and a knife appear in Athenian graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Taken as the moment the metal displaced bronze for tools, that is where the Greek Iron Age begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Athens took to burning its dead about the same time, a change the Argolid never followed, so cremation is an Attic habit of these years rather than a Greek one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several pot shapes come from outside Attica, amphorae with handles from shoulder to lip and vases in the form of birds being Cypriot ideas, while handmade wares point north towards Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth opens the phase around 1070 and closes it near 1000 BCE, and because almost every pot comes from a grave rather than a house some doubt it is a period at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Submycenaean period","questions":["The Attic pottery of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sorts into four stages, the last of them transitional to the Protogeometric style that followed it.","Radiocarbon from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth ends the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 1020 BCE, leaving the style far less time than the whole century once allowed it."],"tags":["era","object","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Kerameikos: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen, Bd. XVIII. Die submykenische Nekropole: Neufunde und Neubewertung</i>, by Florian Ruppenstein, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.16. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"The Last Mycenaeans at Corinth,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48, no. 4 (1979): 348–92, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147841.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Lekythos_in_protogeometric_style%2C_Submycenaean_Greece%2C_c._1050_BC%2C_H_5057_-_Martin_von_Wagner_Museum_-_W%C3%BCrzburg%2C_Germany_-_DSC05623.jpg/1920px-Lekythos_in_protogeometric_style%2C_Submycenaean_Greece%2C_c._1050_BC%2C_H_5057_-_Martin_von_Wagner_Museum_-_W%C3%BCrzburg%2C_Germany_-_DSC05623.jpg","title":"Submycenaean period","desc":"Exhibit in the Martin von Wagner Museum - Würzburg, Germany. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lekythos_in_protogeometric_style,_Submycenaean_Greece,_c._1050_BC,_H_5057_-_Martin_von_Wagner_Museum_-_W%C3%BCrzburg,_Germany_-_DSC05623.jpg","alt":"Lekythos in protogeometric style, Submycenaean Greece, c. 1050 BCE, H 5057 - Martin von Wagner Museum - Würzburg, Germany"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-113","num":"113","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The concentric circles that give the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its name were drawn with a multiple brush turning on a pivot rather than by a steady hand.","answer":"Protogeometric period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiocarbon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1020 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Protogeometric period</b> takes its name from its pottery, the style that follows the last Mycenaean wares in central and southern Greece and runs to about 900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its signature is the ring of concentric circles and semicircles, laid down not by a steady hand but with a multiple brush turning on a pivot, a technique worked out by experiment and long missing from the standard accounts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New shapes entered the Athenian repertoire across the transition into it, among them ring vases, bird-shaped askoi and amphorae with handles from shoulder to lip, all on Cypriot models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stirrup jar, the workhorse container of Mycenaean Greece, appears for the last time in the graves of that same transition and then goes out of use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Iron displaced bronze as the metal for tools over the same decades, which on the usual definition is where the Iron Age in Greece begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A cremation from the Athenian Agora shows the period's end on the ground: the burned bones of a warrior in a neck amphora half a metre high, its mouth shut with a field stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His iron sword, spearheads, knives and a horse's bit had gone on the pyre with him and were gathered up afterwards in cloth whose weave still shows on the rusted metal, and the pyre sweepings held carbonised figs and grapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When any of this happened is unsettled: radiocarbon from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth supports the conventional scheme, which opens the style around 1020 to 1000 rather than 1050 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 21 dates from the mound at Sindos near Thessaloniki push it far higher, placing the late phase in the first half of the 11th century, and its authors note that almost the whole Protogeometric is dated by scholarly intuition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name is contested as well, since parts of the Aegean have no recognisable Protogeometric pottery at all, so calling the age after one central Greek workshop tradition makes those regions a periphery by definition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Protogeometric period","questions":["A warrior cremated in the Athenian Agora at the close of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was buried with iron weapons still wrapped in the cloth that carried them from the pyre.","21 radiocarbon dates from Sindos in northern Greece would push the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> higher than the conventional scheme allows, which rests largely on a handful of eastern sherds."],"tags":["era","object","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Carl W. Blegen, \"Two Athenian Grave Groups of About 900 B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 21, no. 4 (1952): 279–94, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/146860.pdf. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Amphora_protogeometric_BM_A1123.jpg","title":"Protogeometric period","desc":"Late protogeometric belly-handled amphora with chequer-board design on the shoulder and parallel wavy lines in a panel at the handle zone. Made in Athens, from Athens? Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphora_protogeometric_BM_A1123.jpg","alt":"Amphora protogeometric BM A1123"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-114","num":"114","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The six stages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are dated by a few sherds from Tel Abu Hawam and a handful more from Megiddo and Samaria.","answer":"Geometric period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Geometric</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 760 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Geometric period</b> is the last phase of the Greek Early Iron Age, about 900 to 700 BCE, and is named after its pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is cut into six stages, Early, Middle and Late Geometric each divided in two, of a generation or so apiece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those dates rest on very little: the move into Early Geometric hangs on a couple of sherds from one layer at Tel Abu Hawam, and the later divisions on a handful from Megiddo and Samaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Radiocarbon from the mound at Sindos in northern Greece would push the whole scheme higher, so the conventional figures are a working frame rather than a settled one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Real wealth appears early: a woman cremated on the Areopagus slope at Athens about 850 BCE was buried with granulated and filigreed gold jewellery, ivory stamp seals, and beads of faience and glass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Her ashes lay in an urn stopped with a cup, and beside it stood a terracotta chest with five model granaries on its lid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The century after her is the one that remakes Greece, and its tempo of social and material change has never been satisfactorily explained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The usual answer is that the population grew, and the Athenian Agora seems to show it: of some thirty wells of the period, fourteen were dug between 900 and 760 BCE and sixteen in the fifty years that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The graves that carry the same argument are less safe than they look, because many burials may mean that more people were dying rather than living, and the Agora has 28 of them from the second half of the 8th century against only four from the first half of the 7th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every well in use around 700 BCE was filled in at much the same moment, which has been read as a long drought, and either way the Attic record goes quiet for a generation just as the Greek world was sending out its first colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Geometric period","questions":["Sixteen of the thirty Geometric wells in the Athenian Agora were dug in the last fifty years of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is usually read as a growing population.","A woman cremated on the Areopagus slope early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was buried with gold jewellery, ivory stamp seals and beads of faience and glass."],"tags":["era","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Evelyn Lord Smithson, \"The Tomb of a Rich Athenian Lady, ca. 850 B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 37, no. 1 (1968): 77–116, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147539.pdf. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Lynn Roller, review of <i>Greece in the Making, 1200–479 BC</i>, by Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.02.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.09/. [Open access]","John McK. Camp II, \"A Drought in the Late Eighth Century B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48 (1979): 397–411, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147843.pdf. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Clay_amphora%2C_Middle_Geometric_II_Period%2C_800-760_BC_%2827931792873%29.jpg/1920px-Clay_amphora%2C_Middle_Geometric_II_Period%2C_800-760_BC_%2827931792873%29.jpg","title":"Geometric period","desc":"Ancient Greek Art Gallery, Museum of Cycladic Art, Athens, Greece. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_amphora,_Middle_Geometric_II_Period,_800-760_BC_(27931792873).jpg","alt":"Clay amphora, Middle Geometric II Period, 800-760 BCE"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-115","num":"115","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Pollen from a Messenian lagoon records the least human impact in 4,000 years, the clearest sign of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed the Mycenaean palaces.","answer":"depopulation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palaces burn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pollen recovers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Depopulation</b> after the palaces is the steep fall in occupied sites and burials across southern Greece in the century following the destructions of about 1200 BCE, when the centres at Mycenae, Pylos, Thebes, Tiryns and elsewhere burned and sites across the Argolid, the Corinthia, Laconia, Messenia, Attica and Boeotia were given up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The destruction and the emptying run together along a rough north-south axis through those same regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Messenia shows it more starkly than anywhere else in southern Greece, its palace never rebuilt and its countryside largely deserted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pollen cored from the Osmanaga Lagoon beside the Bay of Navarino agrees from a wholly different direction: oak spread, olive fell away, and through the Early Iron Age the Messenian landscape carried the least intensive human impact of the last 4,000 years, a trend reversed only around 800 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That independent line matters, because the rest of the evidence is thin enough that almost any claim reaching beyond a single site rests on theory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The emptying was also uneven, and in places it runs the other way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Achaea and the Ionian islands, Kephallenia above all, have more tombs in these years rather than fewer, which is the reverse of what Messenia, Laconia and even the Argolid record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cyprus received two separate waves of Aegean arrivals, the first early in the 12th century at Enkomi, Kition, Maa and Sinda, the second perhaps two generations later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On Crete people moved rather than vanished, up to Karphi on its peak above the Lasithi plain and to ridges and ledges such as Kavousi-Kastro and Katalimata.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the safe statement is that far fewer people can be seen, which is not quite the same as far fewer people: some left, some died, and some lived in ways that leave less behind, while the largest migration accounts are judged implausible on the sheer numbers and shipping they would need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"depopulation","questions":["Achaea and the Ionian islands show more tombs rather than fewer after 1200 BCE, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that emptied Messenia was far from universal.","Cyprus took two waves of Aegean arrivals in the 12th century BCE, often read as one destination of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that emptied parts of southern Greece."],"tags":["concept","event","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]","Susan E. Alcock, Andrea M. Berlin, Ann B. Harrison, Sebastian Heath, Nigel Spencer, and David L. Stone, \"Pylos Regional Archaeological Project, Part VII: Historical Messenia, Geometric through Late Roman,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 74, no. 2 (2005): 147–209, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/25067953.pdf. [Open access]","Paul D. Scotton, review of <i>The Aegean from Bronze Age to Iron Age: Continuity and Change Between the Twelfth and Eighth Centuries BC</i>, by O. T. P. K. Dickinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.62, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.62/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2},
{"id":"gr-116","num":"116","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The cemeteries at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run from about 1050 to 825 BCE, the very stretch of centuries that used to be called the Greek Dark Age.","answer":"Lefkandi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xeropolis phases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1050 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemeteries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 825 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lefkandi</b> is a coastal site on the western side of Euboea, on the narrow strait that separates the island from the Greek mainland, and people lived there from about 2100 BCE down into the Geometric period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The settlement mound is called Xeropolis, walled and continuously occupied from the Middle Helladic period onwards, with the Mycenaean palace age barely represented and the 12th century spreading over most of the hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three building stages of that century have been separated there, the first of them with two-storey houses whose upper floors were used for dining while clay storage bins and the cooking stood below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the first settlement burned down the second was laid out on a tidy grid of squares about five metres across, and the dead were now buried under the floors of rooms still lived in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crucible fragments and moulds show metal being worked on site, and an iron knife from that phase is among the earliest in the Aegean, probably brought in from Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its excavators read the community as decentralised but comfortable, letting the old fortification wall go, spreading valuables and skilled crafts through the settlement rather than concentrating them, and burying its dead privately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cemeteries are what made the site famous, running from about 1050 to 825 BCE, which is exactly the stretch of centuries once written off as the Greek Dark Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Graves in its Skoubris cemetery correspond to the Attic phase transitional between Submycenaean and Protogeometric, and radiocarbon taken here helped place that change at about 1020 to 1000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The site has since anchored the argument that Euboea set the pace for the Aegean in these centuries, its pottery style reaching Boeotia, Thessaly, the Cyclades and further still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That case rests almost wholly on pottery, and it has been objected that a dominant style is not a dominant state, so the importance of the site is agreed while its supremacy is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lefkandi","questions":["An iron knife from the settlement mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is among the earliest in the Aegean and was probably brought there from Cyprus.","The claim that Euboea led the Aegean rests largely on pottery from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a dominant style is not the same as a dominant state."],"tags":["place","site","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of <i>Lefkandi IV: The Bronze Age. The Late Helladic IIIC Settlement at Xeropolis</i>, ed. R. D. G. Evely, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.06.15. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]","Lefkandi Excavation Project, \"Excavations at Lefkandi,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Oxford, https://lefkandi.classics.ox.ac.uk/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Centaur_Chiron_of_Lefkandi%2C_Archaeological_museum_of_Eretria%2C_Greece.jpg/1280px-Centaur_Chiron_of_Lefkandi%2C_Archaeological_museum_of_Eretria%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Lefkandi","desc":"Centaur of Lefkandi, 1050–900 BCE, clay. Identified as Chiron, found in two pieces in two different tombs at the site of Toumba. Archaeological Museum of Eretria. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centaur_Chiron_of_Lefkandi,_Archaeological_museum_of_Eretria,_Greece.jpg","alt":"A painted terracotta centaur, a bearded man’s upper body on a horse’s hindquarters, standing on a museum plinth"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Lefkandi","at":[23.6753,38.4125]}},
{"id":"gr-117","num":"117","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A linen cloth folded inside a Cypriot bronze urn survived three thousand years in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, protected by the metal around it.","answer":"Toumba building at Lefkandi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 950 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze urn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 BCE or earlier</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Toumba building at Lefkandi</b> is a monumental hall of about 50 by 13.8 metres (164 by 45 feet), raised in the middle of the 10th century BCE on the eastern side of the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is divided into an east room, a central room and an apse, with a row of wooden columns down its axis, a curved western end, and a wooden veranda that is the earliest known in Greek architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two shafts were cut into the floor of the central room: one held four horses, the other a man whose body had been burned and a woman who had not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His ashes were placed in a bronze amphora made in Cyprus, already an heirloom at least a century old when it went into the ground, and an iron sword, a spearhead and a whetstone were laid beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She wore gold, and among it a necklace made in Syria around 1700 BCE, some seven centuries before her own funeral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Folded carefully inside the urn was a large rectangular linen cloth, woven in plain tabby with fringes and bands of geometric pattern, which the bronze around it preserved for three thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the most nearly complete early Greek garment known, restored at the National Archaeological Museum in Athens and put on show there for the first time in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Radiocarbon on the textile returned about 1126 to 1001 BCE, while a horse bone from the same complex had lost its collagen and could not be dated at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hall was destroyed not long after the funeral and then deliberately covered by a mound, and the ground in front of its eastern door became a cemetery for people who were probably of the same household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The find was disruptive as well as spectacular, since the emergency excavation drew staff and time away from the settlement dig, whose final publication then waited decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Toumba building at Lefkandi","questions":["Four horses lay in one shaft and a cremated man and an unburnt woman in the other beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was pulled down soon afterwards.","A necklace made in Syria about 1700 BCE was worn by the woman buried in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, seven centuries after the thing was made."],"tags":["building","place","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Lefkandi Excavation Project, \"Early Excavations at Lefkandi: The Protogeometric Building and the Cemetery of Toumba,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Oxford, https://lefkandi.classics.ox.ac.uk/Toumba.html. [Open access]","British School at Athens, \"Lefkandi Textile on Display in the National Archaeological Museum,\" 24 January 2019, https://www.bsa.ac.uk/2019/01/24/lefkandi-textile-on-display-in-the-national-archaeological-museum/. [Open access]","ARTEX, Hellenic Centre for Research and Conservation of Archaeological Textiles, \"Project Lefkandi,\" https://artextiles.org/en/content/project-lefkandi. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of <i>Lefkandi IV: The Bronze Age. The Late Helladic IIIC Settlement at Xeropolis</i>, ed. R. D. G. Evely, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.06.15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Lefkandi_Heroon_from_E.jpg/1920px-Lefkandi_Heroon_from_E.jpg","title":"Toumba building at Lefkandi","desc":"Lefkandi, Toumba. Protogeometric \"Heroon\" in 1991, from east. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lefkandi_Heroon_from_E.jpg","alt":"Lefkandi Heroon from E"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Lefkandi","at":[23.6753,38.4125]}},
{"id":"gr-118","num":"118","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A hall of 13.6 by 8 metres with a pit hearth and an apse at one end stood at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it is among the largest buildings known from Dark Age Greece.","answer":"Nichoria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dark Age II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dark Age III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 850 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nichoria</b> is a flat-topped ridge about 500 metres (1,600 feet) long in Messenia, some 2 kilometres (1.2 miles) from the northwestern coast of the Messenian Gulf, and it carries one of the few Greek settlements that can be followed right through the Dark Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Mycenaean village there was destroyed late in the 13th century BCE, along with the palaces and much else in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What followed is set out in phases, the second falling in the 10th century and perhaps running into the middle of the 9th, the third from about 850 to 750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its most striking building is Unit IV-1, an apsidal hall of 13.60 by 8 metres (45 by 26 feet) not counting its courtyard, which its excavators call one of the largest Dark Age apsidal structures in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wooden posts set 1.80 metres (6 feet) apart carried its roof, a line of column bases ran down the long axis, and a pit hearth about a metre across was sunk near the middle of the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The hall was rebuilt at least once, its south wall moved outward and a new floor laid, and a small walled courtyard added in front of the eastern door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was dropped on those floors is domestic rather than grand: spindle whorls, finger rings, a whetstone, an iron spike and axe blade, coarse cooking ware, charred seeds, and the bones of sheep, goat, pig and cattle bearing knife marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that evidence the excavators read it as a dwelling, perhaps a chieftain's house, rather than a temple, while noting that a Mycenaean hall had combined sacred and domestic uses too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another apsidal building went up directly over the tip of its apse, so little time passed between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ridge was given up before the middle of the 8th century, though Late Geometric graves show the land was not abandoned, and later surveyors put its quiet down to lying in a buffer zone facing Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Nichoria","questions":["Spindle whorls, a whetstone, cooking pots and butchered animal bones on the floors at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pointed its excavators to a dwelling rather than a temple.","The ridge at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was given up before the middle of the 8th century BCE, though Late Geometric graves show the land around it was still used."],"tags":["place","site","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["William A. McDonald et al., \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1972–1973,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 44, no. 1 (1975): 69–141, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147426.pdf. [Open access]","William A. McDonald, \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1969–71,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 41, no. 2 (1972): 218–73, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147682.pdf. [Open access]","Susan E. Alcock, Andrea M. Berlin, Ann B. Harrison, Sebastian Heath, Nigel Spencer, and David L. Stone, \"Pylos Regional Archaeological Project, Part VII: Historical Messenia, Geometric through Late Roman,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 74, no. 2 (2005): 147–209, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/25067953.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Entrance_Tholos_Tomb_at_Nichoria.jpg/1920px-Entrance_Tholos_Tomb_at_Nichoria.jpg","title":"Nichoria","desc":"Entrance of the Tholos Tomb at the Mycenaean archaeological site of Nichoria, located in Messinia, Greece. Leporello78, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entrance_Tholos_Tomb_at_Nichoria.jpg","alt":"Entrance Tholos Tomb at Nichoria"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Nichoria","at":[21.9128,37.0033]}},
{"id":"gr-119","num":"119","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"No spring or well was found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its flat roofs were built to catch the rain its people had to live on.","answer":"Zagora on Andros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiocarbon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1015 – 925 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zagora on Andros</b> is an Early Iron Age town on a headland on the south-western coast of that Cycladic island, spread over about 6.7 hectares (17 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was lived in through the 9th and 8th centuries BCE, with signs that people were already there late in the 10th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fortification wall with an elaborate gate closed the landward side, and it is among the more important pieces of Geometric military architecture to survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Australian excavations of the late 1960s and early 1970s opened about a tenth of the town and found 55 stone-built rooms making up at least 25 houses, grouped in clusters much like the island villages of today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No spring or well has been found on the headland, so the flat roofs were built to catch rainwater and store it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the place so valuable is that almost nothing was built over it afterwards, so an Early Iron Age town survives as a plan rather than as fragments under later walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people left at the end of the 8th century, in the very decades when the Greek world was changing fastest, and why they went is still unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> All that was raised there later was a small temple of the 6th century, built mostly of schist, with the altar standing in a square room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Work resumed in 2010 as a joint project of the University of Sydney and the Archaeological Society at Athens, and a trench dug then produced a stratified radiocarbon sequence: its earliest levels fall in 1015 to 925 BCE and its Late Geometric ones in 935 to 850 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those dates would begin the Late Geometric at least a century before the conventional 760 BCE, the same direction in which dates from northern Greece push, and they are among the very few absolute sequences the period has.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zagora on Andros","questions":["Because almost nothing was built over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after it was abandoned, an Early Iron Age town survives there as a plan rather than as fragments.","Radiocarbon from a stratified trench at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would start the Late Geometric a century before the conventional date of 760 BCE."],"tags":["place","site","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Zagora Archaeological Project, \"About Zagora,\" University of Sydney and the Australian Archaeological Institute at Athens, https://zagoraarchaeologicalproject.org/the-site/about-zagora/. [Open access]","Rudolph Alagich, Lorena Becerra-Valdivia, Margaret C. Miller, Katerina Trantalidou, and Colin Smith, \"Mediterranean Early Iron Age Chronology: Assessing Radiocarbon Dates from a Stratified Geometric Period Deposit at Zagora (Andros), Greece,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 98, no. 398 (2024): 454–69, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.16. [Open access]","Australian Archaeological Institute at Athens, <i>AAIA Bulletin</i> 4 (2006/2007), https://aaia.gr/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Bulletin-4-web.pdf. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Lynn Roller, review of <i>Greece in the Making, 1200–479 BC</i>, by Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.02.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.09/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Zagora%2C_Andros%2C_224941x.jpg/1920px-Zagora%2C_Andros%2C_224941x.jpg","title":"Zagora on Andros","desc":"Near perpendicular rocks had been in the 9th to 7th century capital of Andros, Zagora. A little further north (left in image) was a late Neolithic settlement Strofilias. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zagora,_Andros,_224941x.jpg","alt":"Zagora, Andros, 224941x"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Zagora","at":[24.9,37.8333]}},
{"id":"gr-120","num":"120","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A shaft furnace, charcoal and air at about 1,200 °C gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a spongy bloom rather than liquid metal, and the rest was done with a hammer.","answer":"iron metallurgy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Iron over bronze</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest smithy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Iron metallurgy</b> takes over in Greece across the transition from the Submycenaean to the Protogeometric, when iron displaced bronze as the ordinary metal for tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The method was the bloomery: ore mixed with charcoal in a shaft furnace one to two metres (3 to 7 feet) high and 40 to 80 centimetres (16 to 31 inches) across inside, with air driven in at the bottom by bellows or natural draught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the working temperature of around 1,200 °C (2,200 °F) the slag ran liquid but the iron did not, so what came out was a solid sponge, or bloom, shot through with slag and charcoal that had to be hammered out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek smiths could not melt and cast iron at all, so every object was shaped by long hammering, and the carbon that turns iron into steel was taken up in the smithing hearth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Steel could be hardened by quenching and the resulting brittleness eased by tempering, and blades were often made by welding a hard steel edge into a softer iron body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The technology reached the Aegean from the east, from Anatolia, the Levant and Cyprus, and one of the earliest iron objects in Greece is a knife from Lefkandi that was probably brought in from Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was in ordinary hands soon enough, and the floors of the Dark Age hall at Nichoria gave up an iron spike, an axe blade and pieces of another blade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Evidence for making the metal is far thinner than evidence for using it, and the earliest excavated smithy on the Aegean coast, at Phokaia, belongs to the 11th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A little smelting waste from the cemeteries at Kastri on Thasos points to local production in small quantities, which fits the trinket-sized iron finds of those years, mostly knives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The craft kept improving long afterwards: blades from Zone in Thrace were made by welding low-carbon to high-carbon steel and twisting the two together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"iron metallurgy","questions":["The earliest excavated blacksmith's workshop on the Aegean coast belongs to the 11th century BCE, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is known far better from objects than from workshops.","Welding a hard steel edge into a softer iron body was routine, which shows how well <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> understood the materials it was working."],"tags":["practice","technology","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Thilo Rehren, \"Metallurgy, Greece and Rome,\" in <i>The Encyclopedia of Ancient History</i>, ed. Andrew Erskine, David B. Hollander, and Arietta Papaconstantinou (Wiley, 2021), https://repository.cyi.ac.cy/bitstream/CyI/1462/1/Rehren%202021%20Metallurgy%20%20Greece%20and%20Rome%20EncAncHist.pdf. [Open access]","Nikos Nerantzis, Nathan Arrington, Domna Terzopoulou, and Maria Tasaklaki, \"Iron Working in Aegean Thrace during Antiquity: New Evidence for Smelting Activity of the 4th Century BC at the City on the Molyvoti Peninsula,\" <i>Scientific Culture</i> 8, no. 3 (2022): 105–23, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6640260. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of <i>Lefkandi IV: The Bronze Age. The Late Helladic IIIC Settlement at Xeropolis</i>, ed. R. D. G. Evely, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.06.15. [Open access]","William A. McDonald et al., \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1972–1973,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 44, no. 1 (1975): 69–141, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147426.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Bas_fourneau.png","title":"iron metallurgy","desc":"Bas fourneau. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas_fourneau.png","alt":"Bas fourneau"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-121","num":"121","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A fully burned adult leaves about 2,400 grams of bone, yet most Greek graves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hold a small fraction of that.","answer":"cremation burial","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First appears</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Usual at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cremation burial</b> arrives in Greece in the second half of the 12th century BCE and is generally thought to have come from the east, where the cremation cemetery of Troy VI and Hittite funeral custom offer precedents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It spread unevenly and never became universal: at Athens it displaced burial of the body over the following centuries, while the Argolid kept to inhumation throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Kerameikos it becomes common only in the phase transitional to the Protogeometric, where the burned bones were gathered into an amphora and set in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The pyres there stood close to the graves, in structures built for the occasion and not meant to last, and the ash, the earth beneath it, the burned sherds and what was left of the jewellery and weapons were swept together into the grave pit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At some sites the ash holds seashell and smooth beach pebbles, so at least some bodies were burned on the shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Burned bone is unusually informative, because fire records the conditions it was burned under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bone calcined white has passed above 700 °C (1,300 °F), and where one fragment shows several colours at once the body was burned with the flesh still on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The quantities are the puzzle: a fully cremated adult leaves about 2,400 grams (5 pounds) of bone, and urns at Lefkandi average about 1,660 grams while those at the Kerameikos hold 595 and at Torone 301.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whole cemeteries fall lower still, the unurned deposits at Voulokalyva in Thessaly averaging 73 grams (2.6 ounces) apiece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 71 pyres dug at Lefkandi, 33 held no burned bone at all, which raises the question whether the remains were routinely collected or whether burning and burying were separate acts with separate meanings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"cremation burial","questions":["Bone calcined white in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has passed 700 °C, and several colours in one fragment mean the body burned with its flesh still on.","Athens took up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in place of burying the body, while the Argolid kept to inhumation, so the custom never became a Greek rule."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","history","greece","iron age","bronze age"],"sources":["Anna Lagia, Anastasia Papathanasiou, Zoi Malakasioti, and Foteini Tsiouka, \"Cremations of the Early Iron Age from Mound 36 at Voulokalyva (Ancient Halos) in Thessaly: A Bioarchaeological Appraisal,\" in <i>Brandbestattungen von der mittleren Donau bis zur Ägäis zwischen 1300 und 750 v. Chr.</i>, ed. Michaela Lochner and Florian Ruppenstein (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2013), 197–219, https://austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572%200x002debf5.pdf. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Kerameikos: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen, Bd. XVIII. Die submykenische Nekropole: Neufunde und Neubewertung</i>, by Florian Ruppenstein, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.16. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Vatya_culture_cremation_urn_burial.jpg","title":"cremation burial","desc":"Vatya culture cremation urn burial. 2022-12-28 17:23:43. Cavazzuti, C., Arena, A., Cardarelli, A. et al.,. CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vatya_culture_cremation_urn_burial.jpg","alt":"Vatya culture cremation urn burial"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"gr-122","num":"122","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The sides of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are usually upright stone slabs, though boulders, cut bedrock or even small rubble walling were all used instead.","answer":"cist grave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revival</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Common at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>cist grave</b> is a rectangular box sunk into the ground to hold a single body, and in Greece its sides are most often upright stone slabs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other builds are known: walls of large boulders capped by a rough course or two of smaller stones, sides cut straight into earth or bedrock, and, rarely, proper rubble walling carried up from the floor, the kind called a Mauerkiste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of that rare sort was found by the sanctuary of Demeter and Kore at Corinth, its south wall standing for 1.10 metres (3 feet 7 inches) and the box small enough that whoever lay in it was probably a child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The form is very old in Greece and never went away entirely, since burial of one person in a cist continued quietly through the centuries of the Mycenaean palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It returns to prominence in the later 12th century BCE, alongside cremation, as the family chamber tomb falls out of use on the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Desborough read that return as an intrusion from the north, brought south from Epirus, but most scholars now take it for a revival of Middle Helladic practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One explanation offered for the change is practical: cutting a chamber tomb for a whole family is heavy work to begin when nobody knows how long they will be staying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cists turn up in the ruins of the old centres, on the Acropolis at Athens and inside the citadel at Mycenae, where they mark walled towns given over to the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Submycenaean cemetery of the Kerameikos they are much the commonest grave, 17 of the 34 tombs published from the Pompeion area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small slab-built cists held children at Nichoria, and at Lefkandi cremation urns were set inside cists, so the box outlived the rite it had been built for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"cist grave","questions":["Desborough thought a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came south with newcomers from Epirus, but most scholars now read its return as a revival of much older Greek practice.","17 of the 34 Submycenaean tombs published from the Kerameikos take the form of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which makes it the commonest grave of that cemetery."],"tags":["practice","object","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"The Last Mycenaeans at Corinth,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48, no. 4 (1979): 348–92, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147841.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Kerameikos: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen, Bd. XVIII. Die submykenische Nekropole: Neufunde und Neubewertung</i>, by Florian Ruppenstein, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.16. [Open access]","William A. McDonald et al., \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1972–1973,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 44, no. 1 (1975): 69–141, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147426.pdf. [Open access]","Anna Lagia, Anastasia Papathanasiou, Zoi Malakasioti, and Foteini Tsiouka, \"Cremations of the Early Iron Age from Mound 36 at Voulokalyva (Ancient Halos) in Thessaly: A Bioarchaeological Appraisal,\" in <i>Brandbestattungen von der mittleren Donau bis zur Ägäis zwischen 1300 und 750 v. Chr.</i>, ed. Michaela Lochner and Florian Ruppenstein (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2013), 197–219, https://austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572%200x002debf5.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cd/PSM_V35_D530_Stone_cist_near_skottened.jpg","title":"cist grave","desc":"Gallery grave (or stone cist) near Skottened. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PSM_V35_D530_Stone_cist_near_skottened.jpg","alt":"PSM V35 D530 Stone cist near skottened"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-123","num":"123","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A fourth-century boundary stone found at the Agora's north-west corner shows the civic centre stood inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the potters' quarter.","answer":"Kerameikos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Iron Age graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Boundary stone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Kerameikos</b> is the district in the north-west of ancient Athens named after its potters, and it was very large, taking in the city centre and reaching out beyond the walls as far as the Academy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The convenient labels Inner and Outer Kerameikos are inventions of modern topographers: ancient writers say simply the Kerameikos, and none of them uses the word to mean the cemetery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A boundary stone of the 4th century BCE was found where it had stood at the north-west corner of the Classical Agora, facing the road in from the Dipylon Gate, which places the civic centre inside the potters' quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Potters worked there for a practical reason: the ground is low and prone to flooding, watered by the Eridanos, by many wells and by natural lines of drainage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their workshops of the 6th and 5th centuries cluster just inside and around the Dipylon Gate and run on to the north-west, and there is almost no trace of potteries anywhere else within the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What made the name famous is the burial ground, whose first excavation volume of 1939 defined the transitional phase between Submycenaean and Protogeometric that specialists still use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its oldest graves are plain ones, and of the thirty-odd Submycenaean tombs in the newest volume of the excavation most carried few offerings at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not everyone lay poor, and one woman of that phase was sent off richly enough to stand apart from her neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the Protogeometric the pyres were burning close to the graves in structures put up for the day, and the urns hold on average 595 grams (21 ounces) of burnt bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its dead are not a cross-section of Athens either: the graves under the later Agora hold many infants and children and mostly adult women, unlike the Kerameikos, so where a body went was a decision somebody made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Kerameikos","questions":["Potters settled in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because the ground there was low and well watered, by the Eridanos, by many wells and by natural drainage.","The graves under the later Agora hold many children and mostly adult women, unlike those of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so burial there was a matter of choice."],"tags":["place","site","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John K. Papadopoulos, \"The Original Kerameikos of Athens and the Siting of the Classical Agora,\" <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 37, no. 2 (1996): 107–28, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/download/2841/5837/15737. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Kerameikos: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen, Bd. XVIII. Die submykenische Nekropole: Neufunde und Neubewertung</i>, by Florian Ruppenstein, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.16. [Open access]","Anna Lagia, Anastasia Papathanasiou, Zoi Malakasioti, and Foteini Tsiouka, \"Cremations of the Early Iron Age from Mound 36 at Voulokalyva (Ancient Halos) in Thessaly: A Bioarchaeological Appraisal,\" in <i>Brandbestattungen von der mittleren Donau bis zur Ägäis zwischen 1300 und 750 v. Chr.</i>, ed. Michaela Lochner and Florian Ruppenstein (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2013), 197–219, https://austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572%200x002debf5.pdf. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Kerameikos%2C_excavations_1915.jpg/1920px-Kerameikos%2C_excavations_1915.jpg","title":"Kerameikos","desc":"Ausgrabungen auf dem Bereich der Kerameikosstraße durch das Deutsche Archäologische Institut 1914/1915. Blick vom Dipylon-Tor in Richtung der Piräus-Straße. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerameikos,_excavations_1915.jpg","alt":"Kerameikos, excavations 1915"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Kerameikos","at":[23.7189,37.9783]}},
{"id":"gr-124","num":"124","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The band between the handles of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a prothesis, the dead laid out on a couch among mourners with their arms raised.","answer":"Dipylon Amphora","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 760 – 750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dipylon Amphora</b> is a monumental Athenian vase of about 760 to 750 BCE, the name piece of the painter known as the Dipylon Master, and it was made to stand over a grave as its marker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belongs to a group of 13 figure-decorated kraters and three monumental amphorae named after the Dipylon Gate, near which their burial plot lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The band between its handles carries a prothesis, the ceremonial laying out of the corpse among mourners, which is much the commonest scene on these vases, while three of them show the ekphora instead, the body carried to the cemetery on a chariot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pictures of this kind broke a silence of centuries, since Greek art had shown almost no human figures since the fall of Mycenaean culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The markers are usually taken to be gendered, kraters standing over men and monumental belly-handled amphorae over women, an assumption that has never been tested against the bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Which grave it stood on is not known, since the plot was dug in 1871 and 1872 by an art dealer and the vase could afterwards be tied only to one of two burials, both adult inhumations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One marker alone was found still in place there, a krater 0.95 metres (3 feet 1 inch) high above a trench holding a bronze urn with the ashes of a youth or a girl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That plot gave up more than 231 graves in three or four layers, running from the Middle Geometric down to the 4th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burials multiply across these decades, 28 in the Agora and 50 in the Kerameikos in the second half of the century alone, so the marked graves are a small minority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor was the choice uniform: the Agora graves hold many children and mostly adult women where the Kerameikos does not, and by the end of the century figured vases were going into graves as offerings rather than standing above them in public view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dipylon Amphora","questions":["Kraters are thought to have marked men's graves and vases like the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> those of women, though nobody has tested that against the bones beneath them.","Nobody knows which of two graves the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood over, because the plot was dug by an art dealer in 1871 and 1872."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Alexandra Alexandridou, \"The 'Dipylon' Vases and Their Graves: The End of Exclusivity in Early Iron Age Athens,\" <i>Revista M. Estudos sobre a morte, os mortos e o morrer</i> 7, no. 14 (2022): 342–61, https://doi.org/10.9789/2525-3050.2022.v7i14.342-361. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, \"The Original Kerameikos of Athens and the Siting of the Classical Agora,\" <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 37, no. 2 (1996): 107–28, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/download/2841/5837/15737. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","John McK. Camp II, \"A Drought in the Late Eighth Century B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48 (1979): 397–411, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147843.pdf. [Open access]","Anna Lagia, Anastasia Papathanasiou, Zoi Malakasioti, and Foteini Tsiouka, \"Cremations of the Early Iron Age from Mound 36 at Voulokalyva (Ancient Halos) in Thessaly: A Bioarchaeological Appraisal,\" in <i>Brandbestattungen von der mittleren Donau bis zur Ägäis zwischen 1300 und 750 v. Chr.</i>, ed. Michaela Lochner and Florian Ruppenstein (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2013), 197–219, https://austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572%200x002debf5.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Grave_amphora._8th_cent._B.C.jpg/1920px-Grave_amphora._8th_cent._B.C.jpg","title":"Dipylon Amphora","desc":"Monumental Attic grave amphora. From the Cerameicus cemetery. The main scene shows the ‘prothesis’ and mourning of the dead. Over the bier is the shroud. Men, women, and a child lament with the hands on their heads, in the usual mourning gesture. Work by the “Dipylon Painter”. George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave_amphora._8th_cent._B.C.jpg","alt":"Grave amphora. 8th cent. B.C"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-125","num":"125","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The measured zigzags and evenly hatched maeanders of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave way late on to quicker work, painted with a flick of a practised wrist.","answer":"Geometric pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Style</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attic lead ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Geometric pottery</b> is the painted ware of Greece between about 900 and 700 BCE, and it is what the whole period is named after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ornament is drawn in dark glaze on the pale clay: banded bodies, measured zigzags, hatched maeanders and panels of pattern set between the handles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Middle Geometric painters worked with excruciating care, where the later ones drew quickly and with a practised wrist, being potters with many orders to fill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Late Geometric brings panels rather than continuous friezes, ornament by the unit, and birds and other animals in quantity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its grandest pieces are the figured vases that stood over graves, from the workshop of the Dipylon Master and then that of the Hirschfeld Painter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the ware is not grand at all, and the wells of the Athenian Agora are full of the ordinary sort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Banded utility amphorae are among the commonest things in them, the cheap pots used to haul water, lost whenever a rope broke or the jar struck the side of the shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kitchen ware ran on its own track: the micaceous cooking jugs are coarse and heavy in the earlier phases, and in the 8th century some change of technique, still not understood, made them light-walled and shapely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What people buried was not what they used, since flat pyxides are everywhere in the graves and almost absent from the wells, having been made for the grave alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Attic work led Greece until the middle of the 8th century, after which Athenian potters stopped setting the fashion and their pots are rarely found outside Attica, though the style as a whole still dates the Iron Age across the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Geometric pottery","questions":["Flat pyxides are everywhere in the graves and almost absent from the wells, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not all made for the same purposes.","Attic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led the whole of Greece until about 750 BCE, after which Athenian pots are rarely found outside Attica at all."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Eva Brann, \"Late Geometric Well Groups from the Athenian Agora,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 30, no. 2 (1961): 93–146, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147205.pdf. [Open access]","Alexandra Alexandridou, \"The 'Dipylon' Vases and Their Graves: The End of Exclusivity in Early Iron Age Athens,\" <i>Revista M. Estudos sobre a morte, os mortos e o morrer</i> 7, no. 14 (2022): 342–61, https://doi.org/10.9789/2525-3050.2022.v7i14.342-361. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","John McK. Camp II, \"A Drought in the Late Eighth Century B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48 (1979): 397–411, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147843.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Clay_skyphos%2C_Middle_Geometric_II_-_Late_Geometric_IA_Period%2C_775-750_BC_%2828469156951%29.jpg/1920px-Clay_skyphos%2C_Middle_Geometric_II_-_Late_Geometric_IA_Period%2C_775-750_BC_%2828469156951%29.jpg","title":"Geometric pottery","desc":"Ancient Greek Art Gallery, Museum of Cycladic Art, Athens, Greece. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_skyphos,_Middle_Geometric_II_-_Late_Geometric_IA_Period,_775-750_BC_(28469156951).jpg","alt":"Clay skyphos, Middle Geometric II - Late Geometric IA Period, 775-750 BCE"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-126","num":"126","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Geometric pots left in Mycenaean chamber tombs are the evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though whether the visitors meant worship is disputed.","answer":"hero cult at Bronze Age tombs","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Offerings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Menidi until</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Hero cult at Bronze Age tombs</b> is the practice, visible from the 8th century BCE, of leaving offerings at Mycenaean chamber and tholos tombs that were centuries old and long closed by then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chamber tombs had gone out of use as a mainland burial form before the Bronze Age ended, so the people who came back to them were visiting somebody else's monuments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Carl Blegen was the first to notice and publish the pattern, at Prosymna below the Argive Heraion, where many of the 53 chamber tombs he dug had been frequented again in the Geometric and Archaic periods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He read the pottery and bronze bowls as continuous family veneration running through the Dark Age, but that continuity cannot be supported: the gap at Prosymna is real.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual explanation instead is political, that 8th-century Argos wanted conspicuous Mycenaean ancestors to validate its claims and found them in these tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The evidence is more mixed than either story allows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Post-Bronze Age burials were also made in such tombs across the Argolid, at Argos, Berbati, Asine, Mycenae and Dendra, at dates running from the Submycenaean period to the Roman, which speaks less of worshipping heroes than of ordinary funerals held among the ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One specialist accordingly prefers to call it tomb cult, and to treat the visits as part of Geometric funerary ritual rather than as a cult of epic figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Attica shows the same thing: at the Menidi tholos, the best preserved in the region and built in the 14th or 13th century BCE, finds in its corridor suggest honour paid to the ancestors as late as the 5th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Messenia it went on intermittently through the centuries of Spartan rule, and the best-explored case there, a tholos at Nichoria whose Bronze Age use ended late in the 13th century, has been published as a hero cult and read by others as a tomb reused for shelter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"hero cult at Bronze Age tombs","questions":["Burials of Submycenaean to Roman date inside the same tombs complicate <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since a funeral among the ancestors is not the same as worship.","Argos is thought to have wanted Mycenaean forebears to back its claims, which is the political reading of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 8th century."],"tags":["practice","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Carla M. Antonaccio, \"Terraces, Tombs, and the Early Argive Heraion,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 61, no. 1 (1992): 85–105, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/148184.pdf. [Open access]","Susan E. Alcock, Andrea M. Berlin, Ann B. Harrison, Sebastian Heath, Nigel Spencer, and David L. Stone, \"Pylos Regional Archaeological Project, Part VII: Historical Messenia, Geometric through Late Roman,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 74, no. 2 (2005): 147–209, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/25067953.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","Ephorate of Antiquities of West Attica, \"Tholos Tomb of Acharnai, Municipality of Acharnes,\" Hellenic Ministry of Culture and Sports, https://www.efada.gr/en-us/Archaeological-Sites-Monuments/-Catalogue-of-Sites-Monuments/tholos-tomb-of-acharnai-municipality-of-acharnes. [Open access]","William A. McDonald et al., \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1972–1973,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 44, no. 1 (1975): 69–141, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147426.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Hero_cult_Louvre_CA308.jpg/1920px-Hero_cult_Louvre_CA308.jpg","title":"hero cult at Bronze Age tombs","desc":"Hero cult (?); epitaph dedicated to Oedipus on the stele. Side A of a lucanian red-figure pseudo-panathenaic amphora, ca. 380–370 BCE. From Apulia. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hero_cult_Louvre_CA308.jpg","alt":"Hero cult Louvre CA308"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-127","num":"127","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Phemius and Demodocus are the <i>Odyssey</i>'s two portraits of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the singer who made his tale as he performed it rather than reciting one learnt by heart.","answer":"aoidos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Odyssey</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>aoidos</b> was the singer of early Greece, who performed narrative song to a stringed instrument and composed as he sang rather than reciting a text learnt by heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four appear in the <i>Odyssey</i>: Phemius in Ithaca, the blind Demodocus among the Phaeacians, and two unnamed, one attached to Agamemnon at Mycenae and one to Menelaus at Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Agamemnon left his singer watching over Clytemnestra when he sailed for Troy, and Aegisthus had him killed, a use of a singer found nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None of the four lives in a house of his own: the singer belongs to somebody else's hall, and above all to a palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is as unthinkable without his instrument as a warrior without arms, and the <i>Odyssey</i> gives its singers the lyre and the kithara, while in the <i>Iliad</i> the phorminx is in the hands of Achilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The instrument had a long past behind it, since Mycenaean Greece already had a large concert lyre of seven strings, known from ivory fragments and painted on a wall at Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The songs were short and chosen for the moment: Demodocus sings a quarrel of Achilles and Odysseus, then the loves of Ares and Aphrodite, then the wooden horse, each on request and none near the length of the poem they sit inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is an improviser rather than a reciter, able to lengthen or shorten a tale to suit the room, and Telemachus says outright that people most applaud the newest song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The singers explained the gift by the gods: the Muse loved Demodocus, took his eyes and gave him sweet song, while Phemius, pleading for his life, called himself self-taught and said a god had planted songs of every kind in his mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those two claims are the halves of an oral poetics, a tradition received and an occasion answered by the singer alone, though the portraits are a poet's own and the later rhapsodes, reciting fixed poems, were self-taught in no such sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"aoidos","questions":["Begging for his life, Phemius called himself self-taught and said a god had planted songs in his mind, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> explained his gift.","Demodocus sings a quarrel, then the loves of Ares and Aphrodite, then the wooden horse, each on request, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked at a feast."],"tags":["person","practice","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]","Carol Dougherty, \"Phemius' Last Stand: The Impact of Occasion on Tradition in the Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 6, no. 1 (1991): 93–103, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/6i/9_dougherty.pdf. [Open access]","Mauricio Vélez Upegui, \"Cantores palaciegos: un estudio sobre la figura del aedo en la Odisea,\" <i>Folios</i> 50 (2019): 83–96, https://doi.org/10.17227/folios.50-7457. [Open access]","John G. Younger, \"The Aegean Bard: Evidence for Sound and Song,\" in <i>EPOS: Reconsidering Greek Epic and Aegean Bronze Age Archaeology</i>, ed. Sarah P. Morris and Robert Laffineur, Aegaeum 28 (Liège and Austin: Université de Liège, 2007), 71–78, https://hdl.handle.net/1808/5716. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/OdysseyDemodokos.png/1920px-OdysseyDemodokos.png","title":"aoidos","desc":"Illustrations of Odyssey. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OdysseyDemodokos.png","alt":"OdysseyDemodokos"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-128","num":"128","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Nothing about the man called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is established, and the poems' Ionic-heavy mixture of dialects is better evidence of where the epic began than any ancient life of him.","answer":"Homer","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Poems composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">not much before 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pre-eminent by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">end of 6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homer</b> is the name Greek tradition attached to the <i>Iliad</i> and the <i>Odyssey</i>, and nothing about the man is established: no dated life, no certain birthplace, no word from anyone who could have known him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The poems themselves point to Ionia: their language is a mélange of Ionic, Aeolic, Mycenaean and Attic with Ionic predominant, which is where ancient testimony put his birth and his work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also give a rough date, since the objects and customs they describe mean they cannot have been composed much before 700 BCE, and they address an eighth-century audience with tales of a more confident past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The verse shows what kind of poetry this is, its constant formulae and repeated scenes coming out of a long process of oral composition and recomposition in performance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The diction was shaped by the metre, obsolete and dialect forms surviving wherever they gave a poet a useful alternative, so the Greek of the poems belongs to no one place or time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> About the man the Greeks had stories rather than records, and the surviving Lives are built from the motifs that shape other early poets' lives: exile, a strange death, a cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The longest passes itself off as the work of Herodotus, and one branch of the tradition, going back to Alcidamas around 400 BCE, has Homer beaten in a contest by Hesiod, who wrote about the works of peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Such biographies may have been made by schools of rhapsodes like the Homeridae on Chios, and what they record is less the poet than how widely he was read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name also covered more poetry than it does now, since the epic cycle was sometimes given to Homer, though most sources assigned those poems to younger men counted as his pupils or continuers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That was settled late: by the end of the 6th century BCE the two epics had a standing the rest did not, and everything else sank to a secondary place, surviving only in fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homer","questions":["The surviving Lives of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are built from motifs shared with other early poets, exile and a strange death and a cult, rather than from anything remembered.","The epic cycle was sometimes credited to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in antiquity, though most sources gave those poems to younger men counted as his pupils or continuers."],"tags":["person","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]","Mary R. Lefkowitz, review of <i>Early Greek Poets' Lives: The Shaping of the Tradition</i>, by Maarit Kivilo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.38/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]","William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]","Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part I,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 1, no. 2 (1986): 171–230, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/1ii/2_edwards.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Townley_Homer.jpg","title":"Homer","desc":"Folio 119 of the codex. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Townley_Homer.jpg","alt":"Townley Homer"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"gr-129","num":"129","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Wolf's Prolegomena of 1795 opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Parry's fieldwork among Yugoslav singers a century and a half later changed the terms it was argued in.","answer":"Homeric Question","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Prolegomena</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1795</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fieldwork</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1933 – 1935</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Homeric Question</b> is the long argument over how the <i>Iliad</i> and the <i>Odyssey</i> came to be: whether one poet made them, how much of them was inherited, and how they became fixed texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its prehistory is at Alexandria, where Zenodotus, Aristophanes of Byzantium and above all Aristarchus worked over the poems in the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE, marking lines they judged spurious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some ancient scholars separated the two, giving the <i>Odyssey</i> an author other than the <i>Iliad</i>'s, and Aristarchus already treated its last book and part of its underworld scene as later additions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Antiquity told of a gathering of the poems at Athens: the spurious Platonic <i>Hipparchus</i> credits a son of Peisistratus with putting them into the Panathenaia and having them written down, and Diogenes Laertius credits Peisistratus or Solon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those say more about the founding of that recitation than about how the text came down, and the modern argument opens with Friedrich August Wolf, whose <i>Prolegomena ad Homerum</i> of 1795 put the author in question and saw that the epic began with singers, not writers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Analysts of the 19th century took that method further, looking for the seams between the shorter poems they believed had been stitched together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The terms changed with Milman Parry, who joined a study of Homeric diction to fieldwork on the oral epic of Yugoslavia in 1933–5, and with Albert Lord, whose <i>Singer of Tales</i> set out the theory and carried composition in performance well beyond classics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The singers showed that each performance is an act of making rather than of reproduction: the same song, never the same words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That settled most of the question, the poems being song made and remade in performance, and few doubt their conventions were formed where nobody wrote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is unsettled is how they became texts, proposals running from a singer dictating to a slow hardening over generations, one seating that singer in 6th-century Athens on evidence reviewers found too confident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homeric Question","questions":["Aristarchus at Alexandria already marked the last book of the <i>Odyssey</i> as a later addition, which is an ancient anticipation of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its analysts.","Nobody now doubts that the poems come out of an oral tradition, so what is left of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is how they ever became fixed written texts."],"tags":["concept","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","Minna Skafte Jensen, review of <i>The Singer of Tales</i>, 3rd ed., by Albert Bates Lord, ed. David F. Elmer, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.10.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.09/. [Open access]","Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part II,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 3, no. 1–2 (1988): 11–60, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/3i-ii/3_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Jonathan Burgess, review of <i>Tales of Heroes: The Origins of the Homeric Texts</i>, by Ione Mylonas Shear, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.10.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.10.18/. [Open access]","Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Rembrandt_Harmensz._van_Rijn_061.jpg/1920px-Rembrandt_Harmensz._van_Rijn_061.jpg","title":"Homeric Question","desc":"One of two surviving painting of a series of seven by Rembrandt, Guercino, Mattia Preti, Salvator Rosa, and Giacinto Brandi. Parts were removed from all four sides as a result of fire damage, following an earthquake that struck Antonio Ruffo's palace in Messina in 1783. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_Harmensz._van_Rijn_061.jpg","alt":"Rembrandt Harmensz. van Rijn 061"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-130","num":"130","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The first word names the anger of Achilles, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follows that anger through a quarrel, a friend's death and a duel, stopping short of Troy's fall.","answer":"Iliad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">not much before 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Book division</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 2nd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Iliad</b> is the older of the two Homeric epics, and its subject is not the Trojan war but the anger of one man, <i>menis</i> being its first word and the theme that word names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its spine is the quarrel of the chiefs in the first book, the defeat of the Greeks in the eleventh, the killing of Patroclus in the sixteenth and the death of Hector in the twenty-second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Achilles withdraws from the fighting after that quarrel, and it is his grief at the death of Patroclus, his closest friend, that brings him back to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The end of the war lies outside the poem altogether, with no wooden horse and no fall of Troy, that matter belonging to the other epics of the Trojan cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its narrator keeps an even, objective tone throughout, where the <i>Odyssey</i> hands whole books to its hero to tell his own adventures like a bard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The division into 24 books is not the poet's but Alexandrian, one book to each letter of the Greek alphabet, and papyri older than Aristarchus carry no sign of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Readers have long felt other joints in it, and Oliver Taplin reads the poem as three long movements, each about the length of one evening's performance, treating book 10 as a later intrusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Analysts before him suspected that same book, with the embassy to Achilles and one of the battles, of having been added to something shorter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What nobody disputes is the length, which is hard to account for by the festival occasions a singer would ordinarily have had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was learnt by heart as well as heard, since Xenophon has a rich Athenian say his father made him memorise both poems to turn him into a man of worth, and Simonides could quote a line of it and expect it recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Iliad","questions":["The division of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into 24 books is Alexandrian rather than the poet's, and papyri older than Aristarchus carry no sign of it.","Xenophon has a rich Athenian say his father made him learn the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its companion by heart so that he would become a man of worth."],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","S. Douglas Olson, review of <i>Homeric Soundings: The Shaping of the Iliad</i>, by Oliver Taplin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1993.01.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.01.21/. [Open access]","Michael Lynn-George, review of <i>The Anger of Achilles: Menis in Greek Epic</i>, by Leonard Charles Muellner, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.02.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.02.10/. [Open access]","Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Achilles_weapons_MNA_Naples.jpg/1920px-Achilles_weapons_MNA_Naples.jpg","title":"Iliad","desc":"Thetis at Hephaestos' forge waiting to receive Achilles' new weapons. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achilles_weapons_MNA_Naples.jpg","alt":"Achilles weapons MNA Naples"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"gr-131","num":"131","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Four books follow Telemachus before his father appears, and in the middle of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the hero takes over the telling himself for four books more.","answer":"Odyssey","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">not much before 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Book division</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 2nd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Odyssey</b> is the second of the two Homeric epics and tells the return of one man from Troy, a subject it shares with a whole family of poems, since the cycle's <i>Nostoi</i> gave the returns of the other Greeks and left Odysseus out as meriting an epic to himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into halves, books 1 to 12 carrying the hero out to a world of wonders and 13 to 24 bringing him home to Ithaca, each half in three movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Four books follow Telemachus before his father appears at all, and the last four are revenge, reunion and reconciliation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the middle the hero takes the telling over, books 9 to 12 being his own account of the wanderings given to the Phaeacians, so that the narrator's voice mixes with his and he recounts his exploits like a bard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The poem draws a distinction there, since its own singers sing traditional matter where Odysseus draws on what he says happened to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Once home he tells further tales that are deliberately false, the longest to the swineherd Eumaeus, a story assembled out of 48 narrative elements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The poem also revises itself as it goes, laying Agamemnon's murder at first on Aegisthus alone, and only the two scenes among the dead, in books 11 and 24, bring Clytemnestra into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ending was doubted early, Aristarchus treating the last book and part of the first of those scenes as later additions, and the Analysts doubting the opening four books as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They took it, on the whole, for the work of a single poet other than the <i>Iliad</i>'s, more unified in structure than that poem and yet fuller of internal inconsistency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The story did not stop with it, for the cycle's <i>Telegony</i> opened on the burial of the suitors, sent Odysseus off again to make the sacrifices Teiresias had ordered, and married him in Thesprotia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Odyssey","questions":["Odysseus tells the swineherd Eumaeus a long and deliberate lie, one of several false tales that make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a poem about storytelling as well as homecoming.","The cycle's <i>Nostoi</i> told the returns of every Greek but Odysseus, who was held to merit the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a monumental epic of his own."],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]","William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]","Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]","Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","Silvio Bär, review of <i>Reading Homer's Odyssey</i>, by Kostas Myrsiades, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.05/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Odyssey-crop.jpg/1920px-Odyssey-crop.jpg","title":"Odyssey","desc":"Homer, Odyssey manuscript. Date: 3rd quarter of the 15th century. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odyssey-crop.jpg","alt":"Odyssey-crop"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"gr-132","num":"132","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Six feet to the line, each a dactyl or a spondee, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the verse Greek epic was made in and elegy and drama borrowed afterwards.","answer":"dactylic hexameter","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Epic verse from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cola described</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1926</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Dactylic hexameter</b> is the verse of Greek epic, six feet to the line, each a dactyl, one heavy syllable and two light, or a spondee, two heavy ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is quantitative rather than accentual, built on the length of syllables and not on stress, and so hard to hear for speakers whose own verse runs on beats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Since Hermann Fränkel's study of 1926 the line has been read not as six feet but as a run of cola, the word-groups that are its real compositional units, separated by sense-breaks or caesurae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He found four cola and three caesurae in every Homeric verse, the first break having four possible positions and the other two having two each, so the parts come out in different shapes from line to line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That variety is the point, as two lines of Longfellow's <i>Hiawatha</i> run to about the same length with four identical cola, three fixed breaks and almost no flexibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A word's metrical shape therefore decides where in the line it can go: 'Achaeans' sits mostly in the second half and takes an epithet to help it fit, where 'Trojans', 'Argives' and 'Danaans' are more accommodating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The traditional phrases are cut to the same measure, each shaped to fill a particular stretch of the line, which is what let a singer build his verses at speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What a listener hears comes as much from the placing of words as from the feet: Dionysius of Halicarnassus praised the line where Sisyphus's rock rolls back for holding nothing but dactyls, though about one Homeric line in five does, and missed the four word-breaks splitting its pairs of short syllables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the metre came from is still argued, derivations having been proposed from a lyric pattern expanded by dactyls, from an Indo-European ancestor shared with Vedic and Iranian verse, and from the dactylo-epitrite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It long outlived the epic, since Hesiod, the elegists, Attic drama, Apollonius, Theocritus and the Greek Christian poets all wrote in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"dactylic hexameter","questions":["Fränkel read <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as four word-groups divided by three sense-breaks rather than as six feet, which changed how Homeric verse is analysed.","A word's metrical shape decides where it can sit in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why 'Achaeans' keeps to the second half of the line."],"tags":["concept","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part I,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 1, no. 2 (1986): 171–230, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/1ii/2_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Margalit Finkelberg, review of <i>Struttura e storia dell'esametro greco</i>, ed. Marco Fantuzzi and Roberto Pretagostini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.07/. [Open access]","Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part II,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 3, no. 1–2 (1988): 11–60, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/3i-ii/3_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","John Gibert, review of <i>Hesperos: Studies in Ancient Greek Poetry Presented to M. L. West on his Seventieth Birthday</i>, ed. Patrick Finglass, Christopher Collard and Nicholas Richardson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.23/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/IliadScansion.png","title":"dactylic hexameter","desc":"\"The Fight for the Body of Patroclus\" by John Flaxman, with superimposed scansion marks representing the last half of a line of dactylic hexameter, and incidentally reflecting the 7 characters in the image. Phil wink, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IliadScansion.png","alt":"IliadScansion"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-133","num":"133","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A singer could build a line fast because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave him a phrase of the right length for every hero and every action.","answer":"formulaic composition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Homeric epic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1928</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Formulaic composition</b> is the technique Milman Parry found in Homer, a formula being a group of words used regularly under the same metrical conditions to express one essential idea, and the formulas together making a system a singer could compose in without writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its clearest case is a name with its epithet: Parry counted four ways of saying Odysseus in the nominative, <i>dios Odysseus</i>, 'godlike Odysseus', 60 times, <i>polymetis Odysseus</i>, 'of many wiles', 81, and two longer phrases 38 and 4 times, each of a different length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Swap in another figure whose phrase is the same length, <i>phaidimos Hektor</i> or <i>Pallas Athene</i>, and the line still scans, so one pattern serves a whole cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The system is at once extensive and thrifty, covering a great range of ideas with little duplication between entries of the same sense and length, which one recent account restates as a nucleus with a peripheral element attached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why the epithets are called ornamental: they are chosen for the metre rather than for the moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Parry set it out in his monograph of 1928, with tables of the name-and-epithet phrases falling after the mid-verse break and after the break in the fourth foot, for eleven gods and heroes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second unit sits above the formula, the type-scene, which Walter Arend identified without sharing Parry's view of oral composition at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The strength of the case was its thoroughness: the whole system of formulas and the use of epithets can already be made out in the master's thesis he wrote in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later work loosened the definition, Hainsworth dropping the metrical conditions and Hoekstra showing that linguistic innovation flexed the diction, the first proof that a late-looking form need not be a later insertion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That cost the formula some of its force as evidence, since a definition vague enough to cover everything stops indicating oral origin, and metricians have called Parry's own inexact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"formulaic composition","questions":["Parry counted four nominative phrases for Odysseus, each a different length, which is the clearest evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Homeric poems.","The repetitions that irritated the Alexandrians and the analysts turn out to be the technical basis of the verse, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means."],"tags":["concept","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part I,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 1, no. 2 (1986): 171–230, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/1ii/2_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part II,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 3, no. 1–2 (1988): 11–60, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/3i-ii/3_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","James Bradley Wells, review of <i>Pointing at the Past: From Formula to Performance in Homeric Poetics</i>, by Egbert J. Bakker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.06.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.06.28/. [Open access]","Margalit Finkelberg, review of <i>Struttura e storia dell'esametro greco</i>, ed. Marco Fantuzzi and Roberto Pretagostini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.07/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-134","num":"134","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Pindar calls the Homeridai singers of stitched verses, which is a clue both to what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was and to what the name may mean.","answer":"rhapsode","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Syracuse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 504 – 501 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>rhapsode</b> was the professional reciter of Homer in archaic and classical Greece, a performer of poems already settled rather than a singer making his tale as he went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name may come from the verb for stitching, and Pindar calls the Homeridai singers of 'stitched verses' who begin most often from Zeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those Homeridai of Chios were in effect a guild of such professionals, said at first to be Homer's own descendants and later merely performers with no claim of blood, though everything in their repertoire was still put down to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scholiast on that passage of Pindar names Cynaethus of Chios among them, credited with composing many verses and slipping them into Homer's poetry, and with the first recitation of Homer at Syracuse around the 69th Olympiad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Martin West went further and suggested that such a company could have invented the ancestral Homer, the blindness a plausible touch and the name perhaps formed from their own rather than the other way about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They performed in succession, one man taking up the poetry where the last had left it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also travelled, so whatever local version a school of them carried did not stay fixed in one place for long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their delivery seems to have been closer to recitative than to song, on one account of how the performance of epic differed from that of lyric.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What they had lost was the older singer's freedom, since once writing had settled the texts, memorising and reciting took the place of improvising, and no rhapsode called himself self-taught as Phemius does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are nonetheless part of the answer to how the poems reached us at all, repeated recomposition by rhapsodes being one of the explanations offered for their fixing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"rhapsode","questions":["Cynaethus of Chios is said to have been the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to recite Homer at Syracuse, around the 69th Olympiad, and to have inserted verses of his own.","Once writing had settled the poems, memorising and reciting them replaced making them up, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was no longer self-taught as the old singers claimed to be."],"tags":["title","practice","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","Carol Dougherty, \"Phemius' Last Stand: The Impact of Occasion on Tradition in the Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 6, no. 1 (1991): 93–103, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/6i/9_dougherty.pdf. [Open access]","Mary R. Lefkowitz, review of <i>Early Greek Poets' Lives: The Shaping of the Tradition</i>, by Maarit Kivilo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.38/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]","Ralph Rosen, review of <i>Pindar's Homer: The Lyric Possession of an Epic Past</i>, by Gregory Nagy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1991.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1991/1991.01.13/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/%28Venice%29_Rhapsode_by_Giandomenico_Tiepolo.jpg/1920px-%28Venice%29_Rhapsode_by_Giandomenico_Tiepolo.jpg","title":"rhapsode","desc":"(Venice) Rhapsode by Giandomenico Tiepolo. Didier Descouens. Taken on 8 May 2024. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Rhapsode_by_Giandomenico_Tiepolo.jpg","alt":"(Venice) Rhapsode by Giandomenico Tiepolo"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-135","num":"135","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Households, gifts, assemblies and chiefs make up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and scholars have dated that world anywhere from Mycenae to the poet's own century.","answer":"Homeric society","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Poems' date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World depicted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Bronze Age to 8th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homeric society</b> is the world the poems describe, its exchange of gifts and its ranks of chiefs and followers, and the standing question about it is whose world it actually is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The poems hold memories of the Bronze Age, yet the objects and customs they take for granted cannot be much older than 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Scholars have accordingly placed that society in the Mycenaean age, in the Dark Age, in the 8th century and in the 7th, and the editors of one conference volume on the subject admitted outright that their contributors took sharply different views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of those papers argued for the 7th century, one of them from the peoples and places the poems name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third observed that the <i>Iliad</i> gives the Mycenaean period no historical date at all, and treats the age of the heroes as a splendour cut off from the present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The strongest case for the Bronze Age has been built from the furniture of the poems: houses compared with Mycenaean architecture, weapons and clothing, the Catalogue of Ships, and the Phaeacians read as a hierarchy of the Linear B kind rather than as an early polis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has not carried the field, its reviewer finding the arguments well short of their confident rhetoric, though he granted that opinion had swung a long way against Bronze Age origins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Part of the trouble is that an oral tradition carries the old and the new along together, so a late-looking feature need not be a late addition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The poet is also speaking to his own listeners about a past he presents as better ordered and more confident than their present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lefkandi is the whole difficulty in miniature, its great building assigned by turns to the early Mycenaean period, to the earlier Iron Age and to the late 8th century, and each of the three claimed as decisive for the poems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homeric society","questions":["The <i>Iliad</i> gives the Mycenaean age no historical date at all, which is one reason <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is so hard to place in time.","An oral tradition carries old and new together, so a late-looking detail in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> need not be a late addition to the poems."],"tags":["concept","history","literature","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Homer's World: Fiction, Tradition, Reality</i>, ed. Øivind Andersen and Matthew Dickie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.05.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.05.02/. [Open access]","Jonathan Burgess, review of <i>Tales of Heroes: The Origins of the Homeric Texts</i>, by Ione Mylonas Shear, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.10.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.10.18/. [Open access]","Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]","Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-136","num":"136","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The whole of the <i>Odyssey</i> turns on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the household held together in its master's absence and taken back from the suitors by force.","answer":"oikos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>oikos</b> was the household, the basic unit of Greek social and economic life, meaning at once the house, the land that fed it, the property it worked and the people in it, free and enslaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What mattered in practice were the relationships inside it, husband and wife, parent and child, master and slave, rather than the long descent lines that older scholarship put first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A holding of about 12 hectares (30 acres) was reckoned enough to support a slave in Aristotle's ideal city, though most Greek states were made up of small farmers who had none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A household that hired its labour instead counted as incomplete, on the reasoning that a slave belonged to the master where a hired man did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Homer the oikos is the thing at stake, since the <i>Odyssey</i> is the story of one household kept together through its master's absence and taken back by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The suitors eat their way through it for years, and their killing and burial are the last thing the poem describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its second half moves between the hut of the swineherd and the hall of the king, two households of the same kind at very different scales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wealth also moved between households as gifts, a traffic long thought to be what held early Greek society together, though the assumption has been re-examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The old story that the rise of the city swept the household aside has been refuted, since the family stayed fundamental throughout and the polis could not have emerged without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two were held together rather than opposed, and households were expected to reproduce in miniature the constitution of the city they belonged to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"oikos","questions":["What mattered inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the ties of husband and wife, parent and child, master and slave, rather than any long line of descent.","The rise of the polis did not sweep the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> away, and the city could not have emerged without the household's continued strength."],"tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Tim Parkin, review of <i>The Family in Greek History</i>, by Cynthia Patterson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.06.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.06.07/. [Open access]","Cathal Woods, review of <i>The Household as the Foundation of Aristotle's Polis</i>, by D. Brendan Nagle, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.11.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.11.14/. [Open access]","Silvio Bär, review of <i>Reading Homer's Odyssey</i>, by Kostas Myrsiades, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.05/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Homer's World: Fiction, Tradition, Reality</i>, ed. Øivind Andersen and Matthew Dickie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.05.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.05.02/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Ancient_Olynthos_Chalkidiki_-_Greece_-_048.jpg","title":"oikos","desc":"Reconstructed remains of ancient city of Olynthos, Chalkidiki, Greece (northern hill of ancient town). Hippodamic town layout (rectangular streets and housing blocks) visible. User:Christaras A, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Olynthos_Chalkidiki_-_Greece_-_048.jpg","alt":"Ancient Olynthos Chalkidiki - Greece - 048"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-137","num":"137","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A stranger at the door was owed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the <i>Odyssey</i> uses that obligation to judge the moral worth of nearly everyone in it.","answer":"xenia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Xenia</b> was the guest-friendship binding Greek households to one another, a code of hospitality owed to the stranger who arrived at the door and answered in gifts that passed between the two houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the <i>Odyssey</i> it is one of the three themes that hold the poem together, beside homecoming and identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also works as a touchstone, a standard by which almost every figure in the epic can be judged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Telemachus's journey is accordingly a tour of houses, the good order of Pylos set against the chaos he left at home, and Menelaus and Helen receiving their guest and recognising him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The suitors are placed by the same measure, since they prepare to welcome Telemachus with an ambush, and the killing of them is where the poem ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Phaeacians are harder to place, unusual in the prominence they give to Arete and ambiguous in their attitude to strangers even as they carry Odysseus home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The worst breach of all is the Cyclops, whose meeting with Odysseus has been read as a contest between civilisation and something older and cruder, a division the poem sharpens with similes drawn from shipbuilding and the smith's craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That scene had a long afterlife, and Euripides made a satyr play of it in which hospitality is among the words to watch, the Cyclops turning from monster into gourmand and philosopher while Odysseus comes off as a cynical agnostic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gifts moving between houses were long treated as the mechanism that held such a society together, an assumption more recent work has questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The swineherd's hut shows that the code needed no wealth, since four books of the poem pass there and the loyalty shown a beggar is the point of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"xenia","questions":["By the standard of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the poem settles what it thinks of the suitors, who prepare to welcome Telemachus home with an ambush.","Euripides turned the Cyclops into a satyr play in which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a word to watch and the monster becomes a gourmand and a philosopher."],"tags":["practice","concept","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["David Pollio, review of <i>A Companion to Homer's Odyssey</i>, by James V. Morrison, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.07.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.07.04/. [Open access]","Ian C. Storey, review of <i>Euripides: Cyclops. A Satyr Play</i>, by Carl A. Shaw, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.24/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Homer's World: Fiction, Tradition, Reality</i>, ed. Øivind Andersen and Matthew Dickie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.05.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.05.02/. [Open access]","Silvio Bär, review of <i>Reading Homer's Odyssey</i>, by Kostas Myrsiades, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.05/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-138","num":"138","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A hero risked his life in battle for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the fame that outlives him, and it exists only for as long as somebody goes on singing it.","answer":"kleos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Kleos</b> is the fame a Greek hero won and left behind him, the return for which a man would put his life at risk in battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word means something heard, and it is fixed in the epic formula <i>kleos aphthiton</i>, 'imperishable fame'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gregory Nagy matched that phrase with a Vedic expression, <i>śravas akṣitam</i>, and traced both to an Indo-European ancestor, which would make the idea older than the Greek language, though the correspondence has not convinced everyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heroism itself is measured in the poems by lineage, by era and by behaviour, and risking one's life in a fight is its defining act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Odysseus is a hero of another kind, tricky rather than simply brave, which is part of why the <i>Odyssey</i> reads so differently from the poem of Achilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The catch is that kleos exists only in the singing of it, and the Muse stirs Demodocus to sing the glories of men, a tale whose fame goes up into the broad sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phemius makes the point plainly when he pleads for his life, since alive he can go on singing the praises of a godlike Odysseus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The hero's immortality is therefore in the singer's keeping, which is a good argument for not killing the singer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later poetry took the idea over, and epic and Pindar's victory odes are both fundamentally concerned with kleos, the odes justifying their praise of a living athlete by reference to the praise of dead heroes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That praise poetry sets out to close the gap between the heroic past and the historic present, and even Herodotus has been read as carrying on the same business of conveying fame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"kleos","questions":["The formula for imperishable fame has been matched with a Vedic phrase, which would make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> older than the Greek language itself.","Epinician and epic are both about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since Pindar's victory odes praise a living athlete by way of the dead heroes of the past."],"tags":["concept","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part I,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 1, no. 2 (1986): 171–230, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/1ii/2_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]","Carol Dougherty, \"Phemius' Last Stand: The Impact of Occasion on Tradition in the Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 6, no. 1 (1991): 93–103, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/6i/9_dougherty.pdf. [Open access]","David Pollio, review of <i>A Companion to Homer's Odyssey</i>, by James V. Morrison, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.07.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.07.04/. [Open access]","Ralph Rosen, review of <i>Pindar's Homer: The Lyric Possession of an Epic Past</i>, by Gregory Nagy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1991.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1991/1991.01.13/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Achilles_mourning_the_death_of_Patroclus%2C_Corinthian_Chytra.jpg","title":"kleos","desc":"Korinthische Chytra. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achilles_mourning_the_death_of_Patroclus,_Corinthian_Chytra.jpg","alt":"Achilles mourning the death of Patroclus, Corinthian Chytra"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-139","num":"139","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"In the Linear B tablets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> names men well down the hierarchy, and only after the palaces fell did the word come to mean the leading man.","answer":"basileus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Iron Age</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Basileus</b> is the Greek word that later means king, but in the Bronze Age tablets it belongs to people a long way down the hierarchy, the word for the king himself being wanax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Oswyn Murray set that single Mycenaean wanax against the many basileis found at the end of the Dark Age, heads of noble families rather than monarchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The contrast is not as clean as it looks, since the tablets themselves may imply almost as many wanaktes in Mycenaean times as there were basileis afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word's descent has been followed from the Mycenaean qa-si-re-u through Archaic Cyprus and the Assyrian records, which is one of the few continuous threads across the collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the title meant once the palaces were gone was settled locally, as leaders reinvented themselves in the break between the Submycenaean and the Protogeometric.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Such a man's standing rested on more than fighting, and keeping trade open, with it the supply of metal and fine objects, and giving banquets have been argued to count for as much as prowess in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Access to the sea and the means of travelling it were central to how these elites formed, in the Euboean Gulf above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Religion belonged to the same structure of power, with hero cult in the Early Iron Age tied to it and the mound at Toumba standing at the centre of the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Homer's world is full of such men, and the poems have even been read as commissioned by 8th-century kings to support the idea of kingship itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether the society they head is an early polis or a hierarchy of the older Linear B kind is precisely what remains in dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"basileus","questions":["Keeping trade open and giving banquets are argued to have mattered as much as fighting to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Early Iron Age.","The single Mycenaean wanax set against the many later heads of noble families is the usual way of introducing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Early Iron Age."],"tags":["title","institution","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Silke Knippschild, review of <i>Ancient Greece: From the Mycenaean Palaces to the Age of Homer</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.39/. [Open access]","Edmund Bloedow, review of <i>Early Greece</i>, by Oswyn Murray, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.01/. [Open access]","David Pollio, review of <i>A Companion to Homer's Odyssey</i>, by James V. Morrison, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.07.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.07.04/. [Open access]","Jonathan Burgess, review of <i>Tales of Heroes: The Origins of the Homeric Texts</i>, by Ione Mylonas Shear, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.10.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.10.18/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Homer's World: Fiction, Tradition, Reality</i>, ed. Øivind Andersen and Matthew Dickie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.05.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.05.02/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1d/Manuel_II_Helena_sons.JPG","title":"basileus","desc":"А miniature from the Louvre MS. Ivoires 100 manuscript, depicting the Byzantine emperor Manuel II Palaiologos, empress Helena and three of their sons - the co-emperor John VIII and the Despots Theodore and Andronikos. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_II_Helena_sons.JPG","alt":"Manuel II Helena sons"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-140","num":"140","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Almost everything antiquity knew about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was read back out of his own verse, including the Muses teaching him song on Helicon.","answer":"Hesiod","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Hesiod</b> is the first Greek poet to say much about himself, and almost everything later writers knew of his life was read back out of his own verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He tells how the Muses taught him fine song beneath Helicon while he was tending his flocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also claims a victory in a poetic contest, lines that later readers took as a boast of having beaten Homer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That grew into the <i>Certamen</i>, a text of the 2nd century CE whose sources go back at least to Alcidamas around 400 BCE, and in which Hesiod wins because he had written about the works of peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The opposition of war to farming is the one stable element across the many versions of that contest, though they differ over whether the judge is a king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His death was told as a Locrian legend, murder by two brothers who believed he had seduced their sister, and another tradition made him twice young and twice buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later writers built him elaborate genealogies linking him to Orpheus or to Atlas, filling in an ancestry his poems had left blank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What antiquity actually took from him was authority, since he is quoted on justice and the city, on moderation and on dealings between people, and his standing rests on knowledge rather than on inspiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There is a paradox in that reception, because Greek tradition made him the poet of farming and the seasons while quoting his farming almanac and his calendar of days comparatively little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Studies of the archaic hexameter treat the <i>Iliad</i> and his two poems as one body of verse, and some scholars doubt that an individual Hesiod ever existed at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Hesiod","questions":["War against farming is the one stable element across the versions of the contest in which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> defeats Homer before a judge.","Greek tradition made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the poet of farming and the seasons, and then quoted his farming almanac and his calendar of days remarkably little."],"tags":["person","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mary R. Lefkowitz, review of <i>Early Greek Poets' Lives: The Shaping of the Tradition</i>, by Maarit Kivilo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.38/. [Open access]","Ruth Scodel, review of <i>Hesiod: The Other Poet. Ancient Reception of a Cultural Icon</i>, by Hugo H. Koning, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.10.05/. [Open access]","Carol Dougherty, \"Phemius' Last Stand: The Impact of Occasion on Tradition in the Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 6, no. 1 (1991): 93–103, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/6i/9_dougherty.pdf. [Open access]","Margalit Finkelberg, review of <i>Struttura e storia dell'esametro greco</i>, ed. Marco Fantuzzi and Roberto Pretagostini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.07/. [Open access]","Hugo Koning, review of <i>Hesiodic Voices: Studies in the Ancient Reception of Hesiod's Works and Days</i>, by Richard Hunter, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.10.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.10.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Hesiod_and_the_Muse.jpg","title":"Hesiod","desc":"Hesiod and the Muse. Gustave Moreau. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hesiod_and_the_Muse.jpg","alt":"Hesiod and the Muse"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-141","num":"141","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Chaos, Gaia and Eros stand at the head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose line of divine rule then passes by castration and swallowing until Zeus settles it.","answer":"Theogony","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Theogony</b> is the hexameter poem that gives the Greek gods a single family tree, running from the earliest powers down to the settled kingship of Zeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its narrator does not merely call on the Muses but is given something by them, a god-like voice and a staff, together with a riddling claim about truth that has troubled readers ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the head of it stand Chaos, Gaia and Eros, and what Chaos means is still argued over, whether a gap between heaven and earth, a being that itself gives rise to further gods, or both at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tartaros belongs among those first beings too, and later fathers Typhon on Gaia, so that the poem carries an agent of disorder inside its own order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rule then changes hands by violence, Kronos castrating Ouranos and swallowing his own children until Rhea contrives to save Zeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Jenny Strauss Clay argued that theology trumps chronology in how the material is arranged, and that the moments where a genealogy is anticipated or held back are where the poet's choices show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Gaia's revenge the poem doubles back to catalogue the children of Night and of Strife, underlining its cycles of generation, violence and deception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zeus's manoeuvring reads as an example set for human kings, and the whole has been taken as an intensely political poem about the city rather than a compendium of inherited theology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Babylonian <i>Enuma Elish</i> tells a story of the same shape, ending in a storm god's victory that ties order to kingship, and castration, absent from the Ugaritic texts but present in the Hurro-Hittite Kumarbi cycle, has been used to argue an Anatolian route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How far any of that was really transmitted is unproven, and the text is unsettled in smaller ways as well, several verses being judged spurious, the ending deleted by some editors and defended by others, and the date of composition pulled later than it once was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Theogony","questions":["Because the Babylonian <i>Enuma Elish</i> tells a story of much the same shape, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has long been read against the theogonies of the Near East.","Theology is argued to trump chronology in the arrangement of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose held-back genealogies are where the poet's own choices show most clearly."],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Cashman Kerr Prince, review of <i>Hesiod's Cosmos</i>, by Jenny Strauss Clay, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.23/. [Open access]","Wm. Blake Tyrrell, review of <i>The Narrative Voice in the Theogony of Hesiod</i>, by Kathryn Stoddard, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005-01-13.html. [Open access]","Carolina López Ruiz, review of <i>Chaos, Cosmos and Creation in Early Greek Theogonies: An Ontological Exploration</i>, by Olaf Almqvist, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.02.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.02.16/. [Open access]","Carolina López-Ruiz, review of <i>Cosmologies et cosmogonies dans la littérature antique</i>, ed. Pascale Derron, Entretiens sur l'Antiquité classique 61, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.05.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.05.39/. [Open access]","Mehmet-Ali Ataç, review of <i>When the Gods Were Born: Greek Cosmogonies and the Near East</i>, by Carolina López-Ruiz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.12.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.12.11. [Open access]","Richard Janko, review of <i>Hesiod: Theogony, Works and Days, Testimonia</i>, ed. and trans. Glenn W. Most, Loeb Classical Library 57, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.03.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.31/. [Open access]","R. B. Rutherford, review of <i>Hellenica: Selected Papers on Greek Literature and Thought, Volume I: Epic</i>, by M. L. West, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.11.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.11.33. [Open access]","Robert Lamberton, review of <i>Hesiod's 'Theogony', From Near Eastern Creation Myths to 'Paradise Lost'</i>, by Stephen Scully, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.05.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.05.25/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Hesiod%2C_Theogony%2C_Venice%2C_Gr._464.jpg","title":"Theogony","desc":"Hesiod, Theogony (lines 566-586, detailing the creation of Pandora as punishment for Prometheus stealing fire for mankind), with scholia, in ms. Venice, Biblioteca Marciana, Gr. 464, fol. 158v. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hesiod,_Theogony,_Venice,_Gr._464.jpg","alt":"Hesiod, Theogony, Venice, Gr. 464"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-142","num":"142","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Three parts make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: a stretch of myth, then a long section on justice and farming, and finally a list of days.","answer":"Works and Days","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Works and Days</b> is Hesiod's poem of instruction, and it falls into three parts: myth as far as line 212, justice and farming to line 694, and then a run of days hedged about with taboos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is addressed to his brother Perses, who had taken the larger share of their father's estate with the gift-eating chiefs on his side, and the word Hesiod sets against them is dike, the right of a case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Out of the quarrel the poem builds a contrast, the virtuous and self-sufficient poet against the idle dependent brother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At line 40 he tells them that fools do not know how much more the half is than the whole, one of several sayings still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Prometheus and Pandora's jar belong to the same opening section, and why Hope alone stayed inside it has plagued interpreters, while the succession of races named after metals is told just as obliquely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The ideal held up in the working half is running one's own household without needing anybody else's, which one study calls the Iron Age ideal of the poem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A small set of ideas keeps returning, strife and emulation, hope and shame, and the teaching is thought to work by making the hearer reason for himself rather than by handing down a rule, though the abstract words for self-sufficiency never occur in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The farm work is pinned to the risings and settings of stars rather than to any civil calendar, which makes it a rudimentary solar calendar and the best evidence for how Greeks reckoned time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Whether it would serve a farmer is doubted, one reading holding that it contains no genuine agricultural instruction and is art meant to move rather than to teach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On that reading the year's round teaches the will of Zeus and justice is a recognition that hardship is necessary, while the chiefs the <i>Theogony</i> praises are condemned here, a tension running through Hesiod's work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Works and Days","questions":["Because its farming advice is pinned to the risings and settings of stars, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the best evidence there is for how the Greeks reckoned their year.","Hesiod addressed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a brother who had taken the larger share of their inheritance and had the gift-eating chiefs of the place on his side."],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["James Holoka, review of <i>Hesiod's Works and Days</i>, ed. and trans. David W. Tandy and Walter C. Neale, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.05.11/. [Open access]","John Lewis, review of <i>Farming and Poetry in Hesiod's Works and Days</i>, by Maria S. Marsilio, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.10.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.10.16. [Open access]","Brendon Reay, review of <i>God and the Land: The Metaphysics of Farming in Hesiod and Vergil</i>, by Stephanie A. Nelson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.08.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.08.16/. [Open access]","J. J. Mulhern, review of <i>Hesiod's Works and Days: How to Teach Self-Sufficiency</i>, by Lilah Grace Canevaro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.17. [Open access]","Chad Matthew Schroeder, review of <i>Theogony; and, Works and Days</i>, trans. Catherine Schlegel and Henry Weinfield, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.09.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.09.38/. [Open access]","Anne-Marie Lewis, review of <i>Greek and Roman Calendars: Constructions of Time in the Classical World</i>, by Robert Hannah, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.10.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.10.04/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Werke_und_Tage.jpg","title":"Works and Days","desc":"Initial page of Hesiod’s poem Works and Days, with the Greek original text on the left side, and a Latin translation on the right side. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Werke_und_Tage.jpg","alt":"Werke und Tage"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-143","num":"143","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Field survey put only slight Protogeometric and Geometric finds at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so Hesiod's village was a small place at the dawn of the Archaic age.","answer":"Ascra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hesiod's village</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ascra</b> is the Boeotian village where Hesiod farmed, set in the Valley of the Muses under Mount Helicon, and his father had come there from Aeolic Cumae across the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A survey run from Bradford and Cambridge identified the site, and its Protogeometric and Geometric finds are slight, so the place of about 700 BCE was a very small community at the dawn of the Archaic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did not stay small, reaching some 11 hectares (27 acres) and perhaps 1,300 people by Classical times, which made it larger than several Boeotian towns that counted as cities in their own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its neighbour Thespiae grew far more, to about 100 hectares (250 acres) and maybe 13,000 people, and traces of a defence wall of Archaic or Early Classical style at Ascra may be a mark of trouble between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What kind of place it was in Hesiod's own day is the harder question, and the same evidence is read both ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Anthony Edwards argued that it governed itself, the chiefs of Thespiae judging Ascran quarrels only when a quarrel was brought to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He rested that on what Hesiod does not say, since the poet complains of lawsuits, of false swearing and of his brother's gifts to those chiefs, but never of rents, taxes, land shortage or debts owed outside the village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that reading it was thinly settled and roughly equal, its borrowing a matter of food and neighbours rather than of markets, and a bad year could turn anybody into a debtor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The survey reads the poem the other way, as showing a village already dependent on Thespiae by the close of the Geometric period, and a recent study has it subordinated to that city while lording it over the households inside itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Edwards's own reviewer was not persuaded, and behind the whole argument sits the difficulty that the village is being reconstructed out of the poems written in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ascra","questions":["Hesiod complains of lawsuits and false swearing but never of rents or taxes, which is the argument that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still governed itself in his day.","By Classical times <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covered some 11 hectares and held perhaps 1,300 people, while its neighbour Thespiae had grown to roughly a hundred."],"tags":["place","geography","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Timothy Howe, review of <i>Hesiod's Ascra</i>, by Anthony T. Edwards, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.09.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.09.13/. [Open access]","John Bintliff, \"The Archaeological Survey of the Valley of the Muses and Its Significance for Boeotian History,\" in <i>La montagne des muses</i>, Recherches et Rencontres 7 (Geneva: Droz, 1996), 193–217, https://revues.droz.org/RR/article/view/RR_7_193-217. [Open access]","Olivier Mariaud, review of <i>Finding People in Early Greece</i>, by Carol G. Thomas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.07.63, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.07.63. [Open access]","Ruth Scodel, review of <i>City, Countryside, and the Spatial Organization of Value in Classical Antiquity</i>, ed. Ralph M. Rosen and Ineke Sluiter, Mnemosyne Supplements 279, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.07.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.07.23/. [Open access]","Samuel Ortencio Flores, review of <i>Voiceless, Invisible, and Countless in Ancient Greece: The Experience of Subordinates, 700-300 BCE</i>, by Samuel D. Gartland and David W. Tandy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.11/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Map_of_Boeotia.jpg/1920px-Map_of_Boeotia.jpg","title":"Ascra","desc":"Map of Ancient Boeotia. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Boeotia.jpg","alt":"Map of Boeotia"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Ascra","at":[23.0742,38.327]}},
{"id":"gr-144","num":"144","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The innovation that makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> different from its Phoenician model is that four Semitic signs became vowels, with a fifth split off the consonant wau.","answer":"Greek alphabet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE, still argued</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Greek alphabet</b> is the Phoenician letters turned to writing Greek, and what makes it more than a borrowing is that the vowels get signs of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The adapter took four Semitic letters whose names opened on sounds Greek had no consonant for — alf, he, yod and ain — and drew a fifth vowel off the consonant wau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is agreement that this was done once and not repeatedly, and none about the circumstances, imagined both as a chance meeting of traders in some harbour and as a formal occasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The letters phi, chi and psi have no Phoenician counterpart and are argued over separately, as the adapter's own inventions or as a later second borrowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One case puts the work in Cyprus, among scribes already writing Greek in the Cypriot syllabary, and rests on the single letters for ks and zd, exactly the two clusters that syllabary wrote with one sign; against it stands the absence of any early alphabetic writing on Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The date keeps moving earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rhys Carpenter argued from the want of anything before 700 BCE that it could be no older, and later finds took the earliest evidence back some seventy years, while radiocarbon dates from Carthage and two Italian sites have been used to argue for the 9th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There was no single Greek alphabet at first but a crowd of local ones, and the regional groupings drawn up by Adolf Kirchhoff and Anne Jeffery, tested by network modelling, came out less tidy than the map suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its uses ran from marks cut into pots before firing and scratched in afterwards to verse and to short functional notes, which is why one study prefers to speak of local literacies rather than of Greek literacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Barry Powell made the boldest claim, that it was devised to take down hexameter and Homer in particular; his reviewer thought that rested on willingness to believe rather than on demonstrable probability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek alphabet","questions":["Rhys Carpenter argued from the want of anything earlier that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could be no older than 700 BCE, and later finds took that back about seventy years.","Single letters for the clusters ks and zd are the argument for putting the making of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Cyprus, among scribes who already wrote Greek in a syllabary."],"tags":["concept","technology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mabel Lang, review of <i>Homer and the Origin of the Greek Alphabet</i>, by Barry B. Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1991.05.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1991/1991.05.15/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]","Natalia Elvira Astoreca, review of <i>Panhellenes at Methone: Graphê in Late Geometric and Protoarchaic Methone</i>, ed. Jenny Strauss Clay, Irad Malkin and Yannis Z. Tzifopoulos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 44, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.32. [Open access]","Megan Daniels, review of <i>Connecting Communities in Archaic Greece: Exploring Economic and Political Networks through Data Modelling</i>, by Michael Loy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.01.28/. [Open access]","Eleanor Dickey, review of <i>Greek Writing from Knossos to Homer: A Linguistic Interpretation of the Origin of the Greek Alphabet and the Continuity of Ancient Greek Literacy</i>, by Roger D. Woodard, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.04.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.04.04/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Dipylon_Inscription.JPG/1920px-Dipylon_Inscription.JPG","title":"Greek alphabet","desc":"One of the four oldest known samples of the use of the Greek alphabet. It has been dated to ca. 740 BCE…(h)ος νῦν ὀρχεστôν πάντον ἀταλό(τατα)… Durutomo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dipylon_Inscription.JPG","alt":"Dipylon Inscription"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"gr-145","num":"145","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A set of 22 letters, every one of them a consonant, makes up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which leaves a reader to supply the vowels from the sense.","answer":"Phoenician alphabet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Phoenician coast</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Phoenician alphabet</b> is the writing of the Levantine coast, 22 letters and every one of them a consonant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ancestry runs back to the Proto-Sinaitic inscriptions of the early 2nd millennium BCE, where Egyptian signs were turned to the writing of a West Semitic language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The simplification is argued to have come from Egypt rather than Mesopotamia, out of the Egyptian one-consonant signs, and one account puts the first adaptation down to the practical needs of mercenary soldiering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Writing of this kind is known at Ugarit by about 1400 BCE and on the Phoenician coast by about 1000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people who used it there did not call themselves by that name, which is a modern label for a coast that took its shape after the collapse of about 1200 to 1150 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Whether the thing is an alphabet at all is disputed, and Barry Powell would rather call it a consonantal syllabary, on the argument that speech falls into syllables and not into separate consonants and vowels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He doubts the acrophonic principle too, the idea that each letter is named for an object whose word begins with that sound, since most of the letters carry no such association.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where one word ends and the next begins was marked, though not in one manner: some texts divide what a speaker would treat as a single word and others the whole phrase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The surviving material is not thin, about ten thousand Phoenician and Punic inscriptions having been counted by 2018, with more turning up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aramaic came to be written in a consonantal alphabet of the same 22 signs, thought to descend from this one, and the Greeks made their own use of it, which is why a recent treatment would have the alphabet read as a technology shaped by its circumstances rather than as a miracle that explains itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phoenician alphabet","questions":["It has been proposed that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is better called a consonantal syllabary, since speech falls into syllables rather than into separate consonants and vowels.","The line behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaches to Sinai in the early 2nd millennium BCE, where Egyptian signs were first put to work on a Semitic language."],"tags":["concept","technology","history","bronze age","iron age"],"sources":["L. R. Siddall, review of <i>Writing: Theory and History of the Technology of Civilization</i>, by Barry B. Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.04.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.04.24/. [Open access]","Russell Clark, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of the Phoenician and Punic Mediterranean</i>, ed. Carolina López-Ruiz and Brian R. Doak, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.08.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.08.13/. [Open access]","D. M. Goldstein and Dieter C. Gunkel, review of <i>A Companion to the Ancient Greek Language</i>, ed. Egbert J. Bakker, Blackwell Companions to the Ancient World, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.07/. [Open access]","Michela Piccin, review of <i>Avventure della scrittura: documenti dal Mediterraneo orientale antico</i>, ed. Raffaella Pierobon Benoit, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.09.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.09.41/. [Open access]","Joseph Lam, review of <i>The Semantics of Word Division in Northwest Semitic Writing Systems: Ugaritic, Phoenician, Hebrew, Moabite and Greek</i>, by Robert S. D. Crellin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.22/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Barth%C3%A9l%C3%A9my_Alphabet_ph%C3%A9nicien.jpg","title":"Phoenician alphabet","desc":"Barthélémy Alphabet phénicien. Jean-Jacques Barthélémy, 1758. 1754-01-01. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barth%C3%A9l%C3%A9my_Alphabet_ph%C3%A9nicien.jpg","alt":"Barthélémy Alphabet phénicien"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-146","num":"146","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A complete line of hexameter scratched on a wine jug makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the earliest pieces of writing in the Greek alphabet.","answer":"Dipylon inscription","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wine jug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 740 BCE, conventional</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dipylon inscription</b> is a complete line of hexameter scratched on a Late Geometric wine jug from Athens, put at about 740 BCE and among the earliest writing in the Greek alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The jug is number 192 in the National Archaeological Museum, which bought it from Ioannis Palaiologos, an Athenian dealer who had dug it up himself in the Outer Kerameikos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Palaiologos had learned to dig on the Archaeological Society's own excavations at the Kerameikos in the early 1860s and then worked for the trade until about 1900, arrested and fined more than once without stopping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So one of the oldest Greek inscriptions there is has no recorded grave behind it, which is a fact about the 19th century rather than about the 8th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ypsilon and chi are already on the pot, and those are among the letters Greek added rather than took over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Names begin to be scratched on Athenian pottery about the middle of the 8th century, and only in the 7th are inscriptions painted on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That this line scans at all puts verse on a pot within a generation or so of the alphabet's arrival, which one study places about 775 BCE, roughly half a century earlier than used to be supposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same study reads that arrival together with the sudden appearance of figured scenes in vase painting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The date of 740 is less solid than it looks, since the conventional chronology of the Geometric periods hangs on a handful of sherds from Megiddo and Samaria dated by destruction layers there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Radiocarbon dates from Sindos put the start of Late Geometric I at 870 rather than 760 or 750, an offset of fifty to eighty years running across centuries, which its authors say bears directly on when the alphabet was taken up; other radiocarbon work has been used to push the earliest inscriptions into the 9th century as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dipylon inscription","questions":["The museum bought the jug carrying the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from an Athenian dealer who had dug it up himself, so it has no recorded grave behind it.","Radiocarbon dates from Sindos unsettle the conventional 740 BCE given to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, putting the start of Late Geometric I a century or so earlier."],"tags":["text","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Yannis Galanakis, \"Of Grave Hunters and Earth Contractors: A Look at the 'Private Archaeology' of Greece,\" <i>Center for Hellenic Studies Research Bulletin</i>, 15 November 2012, https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2012/11/15/of-grave-hunters-earth-contractors-a-look-at-the-private-archaeology-of-greece/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]","Barry B. Powell, review of <i>Image and Myth: A History of Pictorial Narration in Greek Art</i>, by Luca Giuliani, trans. Joseph O'Donnell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.04.35, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.35. [Open access]","Catalin Pavel, \"Art and the Alphabet in the Times of the Dipylon Master,\" <i>GAIA: Revue interdisciplinaire sur la Grèce ancienne</i> 19 (2016): 25–56, https://doi.org/10.3406/gaia.2016.1697. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Natalia Elvira Astoreca, review of <i>Panhellenes at Methone: Graphê in Late Geometric and Protoarchaic Methone</i>, ed. Jenny Strauss Clay, Irad Malkin and Yannis Z. Tzifopoulos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 44, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.32. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/NAMA_Dipylon_Oinochoe.JPG","title":"Dipylon inscription","desc":"Enócoe del estilo llamado cerámica de Dipylon. Finales del periodo geométrico. Museo Arqueológico Nacional de Atenas. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_Dipylon_Oinochoe.JPG","alt":"NAMA Dipylon Oinochoe"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-147","num":"147","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Three lines scratched on a small clay cup from Ischia make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the earliest pieces of Greek writing and, to many, the first epigram.","answer":"Nestor's Cup","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cup inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 730 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nestor's Cup</b> is a drinking cup out of the cemeteries at the foot of Monte Vico on Ischia, cut with three lines of Greek about 730 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The verses run, in one rendering, \"I am the goblet of Nestor, fine to drink from, but whoever drinks from this goblet will instantly be seized by desire for the well-crowned Aphrodite\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The joke measures that against the <i>Iliad</i>, where Nestor's cup is so heavy that only he can lift it when full, while the thing itself is a modest piece of clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is among the earliest Greek writing there is and is commonly counted the earliest epigram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the cup was made is not agreed, one account calling it Rhodian and another placing it in northern Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Irad Malkin took it as showing that Greeks in the west already had Homer in their heads by the third quarter of the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Erwin Cook took that apart, observing that the cup has nothing in common with the shape of the vessel Homer describes, and that nothing guarantees its owners drank from it at a symposium, could read Greek, or caught the allusion at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He added that Nestor was linked with remarkable cups in other traditions too, so the <i>Iliad</i> need not be the thing referred to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A friendlier reading takes the metre as a sign that the cup went round a company of drinkers, and so that a developed drinking culture was already in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The grave was re-examined in 2021, and what had been read for decades as one child of ten to fourteen proved to be at least three people, with sheep and bird burnt alongside them, which unsettles the picture of a boy buried with a wine cup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Nestor's Cup","questions":["The joke in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns on the <i>Iliad</i>, where the old king's cup is so heavy that only he can lift it when full.","A 2021 study of the burial that held <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found at least three people and burnt animal bone where one child had long been assumed."],"tags":["object","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Melania Gigante, Alessia Nava, Robert R. Paine, et al., \"Who Was Buried with Nestor's Cup? Macroscopic and Microscopic Analyses of the Cremated Remains from Tomb 168 (Second Half of the 8th Century BCE, Pithekoussai, Ischia Island, Italy),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257368, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257368. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","Adam Rabinowitz, review of <i>The Rise of the Greek Aristocratic Banquet</i>, by Marek Węcowski, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.10.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.10.42/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Coupe_de_Nestor_-_mus%C3%A9e_Arch%C3%A9ologique_de_Pithecusae.jpg","title":"Nestor's Cup","desc":"Coupe de Nestor - musée Archéologique de Pithecusae. Shonagon. 2022-04-30. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coupe_de_Nestor_-_mus%C3%A9e_Arch%C3%A9ologique_de_Pithecusae.jpg","alt":"Coupe de Nestor - musée Archéologique de Pithecusae"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-148","num":"148","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The earliest lasting Greek settlement in the western Mediterranean was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, made by Eretrians and Chalcidians on Ischia off the Bay of Naples.","answer":"Pithekoussai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek goods by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 780 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Large settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pithekoussai</b> stood on Ischia, off the Bay of Naples, and is the earliest lasting Greek settlement in the western Mediterranean, made by Eretrians and Chalcidians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its earliest Greek pottery belongs to the first half of the 8th century BCE, but it comes from a dump and may mean nothing more than trade running ahead of settlement, somebody selling Greek goods to Campanians by about 780.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By about 750 the built-up ground ran at least a kilometre (half a mile) along its axis, three times the size of Smyrna at that date, with four to five hundred households.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 493 graves have been dug, reckoned at no more than a twentieth of a cemetery that filled between 750 and 700, and the population behind them has been put as low as about 900 and as high as nearly ten thousand; the dead were burnt, at times with sheep or birds burnt beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were not all Greeks, since Levantine amphorae, north Syrian face jugs and Phoenician lamps came up with them, and one amphora over a baby's grave carried a label in Aramaic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The settlers worked metal, bringing iron ore across from Elba, and sent out Late Geometric pottery of their own making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three Corinthian potters lived among them and shipped their small perfume flasks to Campania, Caere and Bologna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Analysis of the clays now supports that pattern more widely, since potters and their methods travelled the Euboean routes rather than finished pots being carried out from Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What to call the place is unsettled: David Ridgway had it for an emporion, a planned commercial venture in which hundreds of families lived by selling their skills, while a later contributor puts it past an emporion and short of a proper colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people went on to found Cumae on the mainland, which was a city of the ordinary kind, and its cemeteries at the foot of Monte Vico are where the inscribed cup called Nestor's was buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pithekoussai","questions":["Levantine amphorae, north Syrian face jugs and Phoenician lamps came out of the graves at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its people were plainly not all Greeks.","Whether to call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an emporion or something nearer a colony is unsettled, since it was a planned commercial venture with hundreds of households."],"tags":["place","archaeology","history","greece","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Jean Turfa, review of <i>The First Western Greeks</i>, by David Ridgway, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.19. [Open access]","Claudio Vacanti, review of <i>The Emporion in the Ancient Western Mediterranean: Trade and Colonial Encounters from the Archaic to the Hellenistic Period</i>, ed. Éric Gailledrat, Michael Dietler and Rosa Plana-Mallart, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.09.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.09.45/. [Open access]","Daniel Frank, review of <i>Greek Iron Age Pottery in the Mediterranean World: Tracing Provenance and Socioeconomic Ties</i>, ed. Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.06.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.33/. [Open access]","Melania Gigante, Alessia Nava, Robert R. Paine, et al., \"Who Was Buried with Nestor's Cup? Macroscopic and Microscopic Analyses of the Cremated Remains from Tomb 168 (Second Half of the 8th Century BCE, Pithekoussai, Ischia Island, Italy),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257368, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257368. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B12_085-ISCHIA_Isola_%2C_olim_AENARIA.jpeg/1920px-Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B12_085-ISCHIA_Isola_%2C_olim_AENARIA.jpeg","title":"Pithekoussai","desc":"The map of the Italian island Ischia, near the coast of Napels, was published in 1640 by Joan Blaeu (1598-1673) and Cornelius Blaeu (1610-1642) in the third volume of the Atlas Novus. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B12_085-ISCHIA_Isola_,_olim_AENARIA.jpeg","alt":"Atlas Van der Hagen-KW1049B12 085-ISCHIA Isola, olim AENARIA"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Pithekoussai","at":[13.8957,40.7312]}},
{"id":"gr-149","num":"149","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"At the mouth of the Orontes in northern Syria stood <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose buildings held more Greek pottery than any other site in the region.","answer":"Al Mina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Euboean pottery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Al Mina</b> is a site at the mouth of the Orontes in northern Syria, where Leonard Woolley uncovered warehouse-like buildings holding large amounts of Greek pottery, Euboean in the 8th century and Attic later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those finds nourished the idea of Greek ascendancy in the Early Iron Age Levant, and John Boardman made the place a Greek settlement holding some sort of charter from the Syrian kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His case rests on there being a greater proportion of Greek pottery here than anywhere else in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against it stands a tiny sounding at Tyre, which produced more Greek pottery than all of Al Mina in the same years, and nobody questions that Tyre was Phoenician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The alternative offered is a Syrian town with a few Euboean merchants working in it, or a Syrian commercial enclave whose traders dealt in Greek wares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pottery cannot be counted as people in any case, and much of the undecorated ware was thrown away as the site was dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A study of the Syrian and Phoenician pottery that was kept found a significant Phoenician presence and came out inconclusive about the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground under all of it is softer than it looks, since the strata here and at other Levantine sites are now reckoned less well defined than they once were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chemical testing of the later pottery, from the years between 539 and 301 BCE, showed that much of what had been taken for local work came in from western Asia Minor, Miletos above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A further reading makes the settlement a mercenary one rather than a commercial one, and its author and Boardman published in the same volume without either mentioning the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Al Mina","questions":["A tiny sounding at Tyre produced more Greek pottery than all of contemporary <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and nobody questions that Tyre was Phoenician.","Because much undecorated ware was thrown away as it was dug, the pottery from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot be counted as a census of who lived there."],"tags":["place","archaeology","history","syria","iron age"],"sources":["Jean Turfa, review of <i>The First Western Greeks</i>, by David Ridgway, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.19. [Open access]","Barbara A. Barletta, review of <i>The Greeks in the East</i>, ed. Alexandra Villing, British Museum Research Publication 157, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.07.65, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.07.65/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Ancient Greeks West and East</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Mnemosyne Supplements 196, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.20/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume One</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.38/. [Open access]","David Gill, review of <i>Archaic Cyprus: A Study of the Textual and Archaeological Evidence</i>, by A. T. Reyes, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.03.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.03.34. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Al Mina","at":[35.9871,36.0804]}},
{"id":"gr-150","num":"150","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Chalcis and Eretria, the two cities behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, colonised jointly for much of the 8th century before falling out over the Lelantine plain.","answer":"Euboean trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Trade and colonies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lelantine War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 710 – 650 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Euboean trade</b> is the reach of two cities on one island, Chalcis and Eretria, across the Mediterranean in the 9th and 8th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chalcis was settled in the 11th century in a favourable spot and had a large part in the metal trade, its ruling families named in a well known passage of Hesiod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eretria was founded in the 9th where there was an excellent harbour, took its name from the word for a rower, and held Andros, Tenos and Keos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xeropolis at Lefkandi commanded the eastern half of the plain of the Lelas and Chalcis the western, and one reading makes Eretria the harbour of Lefkandi rather than a rival to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two colonised together for a good part of the 8th century, in Chalcidice well before 700, at Pithekoussai in its second quarter and then at Cumae, with Rhegion the last of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both were in the Levant by the second half of the century, which Al Mina is cited to show, and the exchange they joined had been running through the Early Iron Age before they entered it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Northward, the port of Methone speaks a dialect and writes an alphabet tied to Euboea and to Eretria in particular, with imported pots from a great many Greek cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the west somebody was selling Greek goods to Campanians by about 780, ahead of any settlement there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What moved along these routes was not only cargo, since analysis of the clays indicates that potters and their methods travelled rather than finished pots being shipped out from Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The war between the two cities over the plain between them, put by one study at roughly 710 to 650, closed the golden age of both, a war without winners after which neither kept the impulse it had shown before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Euboean trade","questions":["Analysis of clays suggests that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried potters and their methods westward rather than finished pots being shipped out from Greece.","The Lelantine War, put at roughly 710 to 650 BCE, closed the golden age of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and left neither city with the impulse it had shown before."],"tags":["practice","history","greece","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Adolfo Domínguez, review of <i>Untersuchungen zum Lelantischen Krieg und verwandten Problemen der frühgriechischen Geschichte</i>, by Victor Parker, Historia Einzelschriften 109, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.06.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.06.12/. [Open access]","Jean Turfa, review of <i>The First Western Greeks</i>, by David Ridgway, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.19. [Open access]","Daniel Frank, review of <i>Greek Iron Age Pottery in the Mediterranean World: Tracing Provenance and Socioeconomic Ties</i>, ed. Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.06.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.33/. [Open access]","Natalia Elvira Astoreca, review of <i>Panhellenes at Methone: Graphê in Late Geometric and Protoarchaic Methone</i>, ed. Jenny Strauss Clay, Irad Malkin and Yannis Z. Tzifopoulos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 44, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.32. [Open access]","Joanna Smith, review of <i>Italy and Cyprus in Antiquity, 1500-450 BC</i>, ed. Larissa Bonfante and Vassos Karageorghis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.03.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.03.41/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-151","num":"151","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A tripillar shrine of Phoenician type built inside a Cretan temple is the firmest evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than merely for their goods.","answer":"Phoenicians in the Aegean","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kommos imports from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Levantine visits end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Phoenicians in the Aegean</b> are known far better from what they left than from where they lived: metal bowls, ivories, glass beads, faience, scarabs and the techniques of jewellery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carolina López-Ruiz carries the debt further, to the mortise-and-tenon method of building ships, to the antecedents of the Ionic and Aeolic capitals, and to Greek monumental sculpture, which she has arriving by Phoenician adaptation rather than from Egypt direct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The winged sphinx, everywhere in Phoenician art of the Iron Age, is the badge of that traffic, and Herodotus has a Tyrian Cadmus bringing the Greeks their letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The firmest sign of people rather than objects is at Kommos on the south coast of Crete, where a shrine of the Phoenician or Punic three-pillar kind was built inside the temple, with Phoenician pottery from the late 10th or early 9th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its excavators read those contexts as showing not merely goods passed along a chain but the actual presence of Phoenician traders, if not residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A recent study of the same site is colder about it: there was no Phoenician colonisation of Crete, it is not even known whether a settlement stood there, and Levantine visits had stopped before the end of the 7th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Elsewhere the traces are of workshops rather than of homes, with Phoenician art-wares made on Rhodes and at Carthage, the factories put wherever the materials or the customers were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Outside the Aegean the case is easier, since at Pithekoussai in the west somebody painted a Semitic funerary symbol on an amphora over a baby's grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the northern Aegean, rich in timber and metals, early Greek and Phoenician dealings run alongside Trojans, Phrygians and the people already there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether 'the Phoenicians' are a people at all is now itself argued, López-Ruiz rejecting what she calls Phoenicoskepticism and holding them a real group with shared ritual and cultural traits, while a contributor to the same volume declines to call the famous metal bowls Phoenician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phoenicians in the Aegean","questions":["Metal bowls, ivories, glass beads and faience mark <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though objects travel a great deal more easily than the people who made them.","Workshops making Phoenician art-wares on Rhodes are part of the case for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a workshop is not the same thing as a settlement."],"tags":["people","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Hélène Sader, review of <i>Phoenicians and the Making of the Mediterranean</i>, by Carolina López-Ruiz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.06.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.06.11/. [Open access]","Jérémy Lamaze, review of <i>Thirsty Seafarers at Temple B of Kommos: Commercial Districts and the Role of Crete in Phoenician Trading Networks in the Aegean</i>, by Judith Muñoz Sogas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.10.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.10.25/. [Open access]","Jean Turfa, review of <i>Phoenicians</i>, by Glenn Markoe, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.05.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.05.09/. [Open access]","Grace Erny, review of <i>The Connected Iron Age: Interregional Networks in the Eastern Mediterranean, 900-600 BCE</i>, ed. Jonathan M. Hall and James F. Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.08/. [Open access]","Donald Haggis, review of <i>Kommos IV: The Greek Sanctuary</i>, ed. Joseph W. Shaw and Maria C. Shaw, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.06.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.06.18/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-152","num":"152","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Twelve cities on the western coast of Asia Minor traced themselves to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with Athens as the mother city in most of the stories.","answer":"Ionian migration","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ionian migration</b> is the tradition that Greeks crossed from the mainland to settle the middle stretch of the western coast of Asia Minor, in the twelve cities of the dodekapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Athens is the mother city in those stories, and the founders they hang on are Neileus at Miletos and Androklos at Ephesos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo and Pausanias preserve a whole variety of such tales, which between them supplied a base for a shared Ionianness rather than one agreed account of what happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Something can be said for the Athenian claim: Vanessa Gorman found the evidence of social institutions and of the calendar consistent with Athens as the mother city of Miletos, and put the settlement at about 1050 BCE from the likeness of Milesian and Athenian pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miletos was old ground by then, Minoan from about 1700 BCE and Mycenaean afterwards, so whoever arrived was arriving somewhere rather than nowhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The trouble lies in the middle, since the argument welds pre-Archaic archaeology from Ionia to post-Archaic literature from Athens with nothing said about the centuries in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Local Ionian identities may have grown up on their own and only later been fastened to the migration story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oliver Dickinson has set out the case against the historical reliability of this tradition along with those of the Trojan war and the Dorian invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor does the material read as an Athenian transplant: at Klazomenai local potters adopted or adapted Greek shapes while working several different clays, which points to an overlapping manufacture rather than to one Ionian style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> And the stories counted for most late, rising in significance from the reign of Hadrian, when the Panhellenion made Doric, Aeolic and Ionic into categories worth claiming and the cities of Asia were competing with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ionian migration","questions":["Milesian and Athenian pottery are alike enough to put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 1050 BCE, on a site already Minoan and then Mycenaean.","Arguments for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> weld pre-Archaic archaeology from Ionia to post-Archaic literature from Athens with almost nothing said about the centuries in between."],"tags":["event","history","migration","greece","iron age"],"sources":["P. J. Smith, review of <i>Miletos, the Ornament of Ionia: A History of the City to 400 B.C.E.</i>, by Vanessa B. Gorman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.06.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.06.17/. [Open access]","Dies van der Linde, review of <i>Roman Ionia: Constructions of Cultural Identity in Western Asia Minor</i>, by Martin Hallmannsecker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.09.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.09.15/. [Open access]","Lee E. Patterson, review of <i>The Land of Ionia: Society and Economy in the Archaic Period</i>, by Alan M. Greaves, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.04.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.04.17/. [Open access]","Manolis Mikrakis, review of <i>Collapse and Transformation: The Late Bronze Age to Early Iron Age in the Aegean</i>, ed. Guy D. Middleton, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.04.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.04.25/. [Open access]","Daniel Frank, review of <i>Greek Iron Age Pottery in the Mediterranean World: Tracing Provenance and Socioeconomic Ties</i>, ed. Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.06.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.33/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-153","num":"153","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Lesbian, Thessalian and Boeotian all descend from a common Aeolic, which is the linguistic fact any account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to explain.","answer":"Aeolian migration","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Aeolian migration</b> is the tradition of a movement out of mainland Greece into the north-west of Asia Minor and the islands off it, Lesbos above all, in the early Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is certainly an Aeolic dialect group behind the name, Lesbian and Thessalian and Boeotian, and all three descend from a common Aeolic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For Greek settlement in Asia Minor at large, the literary record has been found generally to accord with the span of years covered by the Greek pottery along the coast of western Turkey, though the process was neither momentary nor short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On Samothrace the Aeolian elements outweigh the Ionian, and the first settlers are thought to have come from Aeolis, from the nearby Troad or from Lesbos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How Lesbian itself took shape is a separate question, and the part played in it by the Ionic of Asia Minor is held not to be exaggerated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The difficulty is the archaeology, since it has been argued that there is no archaeological evidence at all for Aeolian Greeks moving from Balkan Hellas to Anatolia in the early Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roger Woodard takes that argument as a working model and follows where it goes, which is backwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If no such crossing happened, then common Aeolic was already being spoken in Anatolia in the Bronze Age, as the language of the Ahhiyawa, the Mycenaean community of the western Anatolian coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lesbian would then be the dialect of that community's descendants, sitting where it had always sat, and Thessalian and Boeotian would belong to Aeolians who went the other way, out of Anatolia into Balkan Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The archaeology does not rule that out, and a wider reading of the Protogeometric Aegean sees peaceful development by communities settled in one place rather than people on the move as nomads or invaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aeolian migration","questions":["One account turns the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around, proposing that Aeolic was already spoken in Anatolia and that its speakers moved the other way, into Greece.","Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> happened at all is doubted, since it has been argued that no archaeology supports Greeks crossing from Balkan Hellas to Anatolia."],"tags":["event","history","migration","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Roger Woodard, \"Aeolian Origins,\" <i>Center for Hellenic Studies Research Bulletin</i> 7 (2019), https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2019/03/15/aeolian-origins/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume One</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.38/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume Two</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Mnemosyne Supplements 193, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]","Marina Veksina, review of <i>Studies in Ancient Greek Dialects: From Central Greece to the Black Sea</i>, ed. Georgios K. Giannakis, Emilio Crespo and Panagiotis Filos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 49, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.02.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.02.12. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-154","num":"154","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Handmade burnished ware was the best candidate for newcomers behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, until it turned out to appear well before the palaces were destroyed.","answer":"Dorian invasion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palaces fall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dorian invasion</b> is the idea that newcomers came down into the Peloponnese and the south of Greece once the Mycenaean palaces had fallen, and it has been given up, put back where it came from among the myths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The palaces went in the closing years of the 13th century, and life for the Mycenaeans by then must have been very insecure and unstable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is offered in place of invaders is internal strife, natural disaster and the collapse of a system, and historians now prefer several causes at once to any single one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jonathan Hall doubts the migration stories on their own ground, since they show the marks of different traditions run together into one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where regions turn out to share a language, an institution or a custom, contact and adoption will account for it without anybody needing to invade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The best material candidate for an incoming people was handmade burnished ware, pottery built by hand and polished, quite unlike the wheel-made Mycenaean pottery it lies beside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is a great deal of it, rising at Tiryns to 30.6% of the whole assemblage early in the last Mycenaean phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It arrives well before the palace there was destroyed, however, which takes it out of the event it was called on to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nor does it point north: up to 82% of the locally made ware has parallels in southern Italy, and Klaus Kilian read the makers as craftsmen from there, a small minority working outside the palace and its elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oliver Dickinson has set the invasion beside the Trojan war and the Ionian migration among legends whose reliability does not survive examination, and the Protogeometric Aegean now reads as communities developing peacefully in fixed places rather than as populations in motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dorian invasion","questions":["Doubt is cast on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because the migration stories show different traditions run together, and contact would explain the shared features anyway.","Internal strife, natural disasters and systems collapse have replaced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the explanation for why the Mycenaean palaces fell in the first place."],"tags":["event","history","migration","greece","bronze age","iron age"],"sources":["Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of <i>The Mycenaeans</i>, by Louise Schofield, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.07.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.07.56. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200-479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of <i>Die handgemachte geglättete Keramik mykenischer Zeitstellung</i>, by Klaus Kilian and Tobias Mühlenbruch, Tiryns 15, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.09.59, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.09.59. [Open access]","Manolis Mikrakis, review of <i>Collapse and Transformation: The Late Bronze Age to Early Iron Age in the Aegean</i>, ed. Guy D. Middleton, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.04.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.04.25/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-155","num":"155","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Sparta's kings drew their standing from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which made them founders of the same kind as the men who led out colonies.","answer":"Return of the Heracleidae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Laconian genealogy by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Return of the Heracleidae</b> is the story that the descendants of Heracles came back to the Peloponnese to take what was already theirs, a right recovered rather than a country conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were worked into the Laconian genealogy perhaps as early as the beginning of the 7th century, and what the story presses is exactly that right of return to Laconian ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sparta's kings took their legitimacy from descent among them, which put them in a class with the founders who led out colonies elsewhere in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The story did harder work as well, since the Spartan case for taking Messenia is implicit in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a separate tradition from the Dorian invasion, and the Spartans themselves had to find some way of holding the two together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Neither the archaeology nor the language will support one coherent account of where the Spartans came from, and it is not until the 7th century that a Sparta appears which is not legendary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lakonia in the 8th century was thinly settled, almost wholly at Sparta and Amyclae in the valley of the Eurotas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside Sparta the story was fought over too, and giving Lykourgos the credit for the constitution was a win for the factions that wanted the kings stripped of their standing as founders of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kinship myths of this kind were made and remade for use between cities, and Greeks took to obviously new stories far more readily than might be expected, so that invention turned into tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Athens eventually turned the family against Sparta, Euripides putting the children of Heracles on stage as suppliants under Athenian protection, an answer to Spartan claims to be liberating cities early in the Peloponnesian war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Return of the Heracleidae","questions":["Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a different story from the Dorian invasion, the Spartans themselves had to find some way of reconciling the two accounts.","Euripides answered Spartan claims by putting the children of Heracles under Athenian protection, turning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the city it had served."],"tags":["event","mythology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Naoíse Mac Sweeney, review of <i>Kinship Myth in Ancient Greece</i>, by Lee E. Patterson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.08.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.08.26. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Kõiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]","Timothy Doran, review of <i>Myth, Text, and History at Sparta</i>, ed. Thomas Figueira, Gorgias Studies in Classical and Late Antiquity 18, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.16/. [Open access]","Sophie Mills, review of <i>City of Suppliants: Tragedy and the Athenian Empire</i>, by Angeliki Tzanetou, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.01.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.01.27/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-156","num":"156","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The usual sorting of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is fourfold: Ionic, Doric or West Greek, Aeolic, and the Achaean pair of Arcadian and Cypriot.","answer":"Greek dialects","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Greek dialects</b> are the local forms of the language before a common Greek came in, and they are known above all from what was cut in stone rather than from what was written in books.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual sorting is fourfold: Ionic, Doric or West Greek, Aeolic, and the Achaean pair of Arcadian and Cypriot, with Pamphylian attached to the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aeolic covers Lesbian, Thessalian and Boeotian, all three of them descended from a common Aeolic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Behind the lot lies Mycenaean, and the Linear B tablets already preserve two forms of it, conventionally called Normal and Special Mycenaean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the language first came apart, and how the groups should be divided below the top level, is still open, and a good deal more has been written about the Greek that came before them than about their own internal history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The tidy map misleads, since a dialect is really a matter of one city at a time and it travels with the people who speak it, so that certain forms at Byzantium point to a community of Asiatic Aeolic speakers in the colony there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether a group of dialects marks off a people is doubted: Jonathan Hall would rather explain shared language, institutions and customs by contact and adoption than by anybody's arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In literature a dialect goes with a genre rather than with a place, and the ancient grammarians heard temperaments in them, Ionic poetic and soft against Doric austere and masculine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Andreas Willi argues that the odd forms of tragic language are not borrowings from Doric or Ionic at all, but everyday Attic deliberately made weightier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How any one dialect came to be is its own problem, and the part played by the Ionic of Asia Minor in the shaping of Lesbian is held not to be exaggerated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek dialects","questions":["Ancient grammarians heard temperaments in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, calling Ionic poetic and soft while Doric was austere and masculine, as though a dialect carried a character.","The features shared across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may be explained by contact and adoption rather than by any people arriving from somewhere else."],"tags":["language","history","literature","greece","iron age"],"sources":["M. J. C. Scarborough, review of <i>Ἑλληνικὲς διάλεκτοι στὸν ἀρχαῖο κόσμο</i>, ed. Anna Panayotou and Giovanbattista Galdi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.28/. [Open access]","Lucien van Beek, review of <i>Formes et fonctions des langues littéraires en Grèce ancienne</i>, ed. Andreas Willi, Entretiens sur l'Antiquité classique 65, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.07.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.07.24. [Open access]","Marina Veksina, review of <i>Studies in Ancient Greek Dialects: From Central Greece to the Black Sea</i>, ed. Georgios K. Giannakis, Emilio Crespo and Panagiotis Filos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 49, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.02.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.02.12. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200-479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]","Daniel Kölligan, review of <i>The Linguistic Roots of Ancient Greek</i>, by Don Ringe, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.04.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.04.18/. [Open access]","Roger Woodard, \"Aeolian Origins,\" <i>Center for Hellenic Studies Research Bulletin</i> 7 (2019), https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2019/03/15/aeolian-origins/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Decree_Triphylians_Louvre_Br4069.jpg/1920px-Decree_Triphylians_Louvre_Br4069.jpg","title":"Greek dialects","desc":"Decree of the Triphylians granting citizenship and immunity from all obligations to three named persons (Pyladas, Gnathon and Pyrus) and their issue. Bronze disc bearing a clockwise spiral inscription beginning at the circumference in Elean dialect. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Decree_Triphylians_Louvre_Br4069.jpg","alt":"Decree Triphylians Louvre Br4069"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-157","num":"157","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Arcadian in the middle of the Peloponnese and Cypriot on Cyprus are the two halves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which specialists call the Achaean dialects.","answer":"Arcadocypriot","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Arcadocypriot</b> is the group that puts together Arcadian, spoken in the middle of the Peloponnese, and Cypriot, spoken on Cyprus, which specialists call the Achaean dialects and to which Pamphylian is usually joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rupert Thompson has asked whether the prepositional usage that Arcado-Cypriot shares with Mycenaean is an isogloss of Bronze Age date, a feature held in common since before the palaces went down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Linear B itself already carries two forms of Mycenaean, conventionally called the Normal and the Special.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cyprus is where the case for something surviving is strongest, since Greek culture there stayed extremely conservative and kept a great deal that was Mycenaean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Greeks of Cyprus are also held to have escaped the destructions of about 1200 BCE that put an end to the Mycenaean kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The writing points the same way, since Cyprus in the 1st millennium used a different verb for the act of writing from the rest of the Greek world, which implies a practice carried on in a culture still using a syllabic script of the older kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That script served Cypriot Greek throughout the 1st millennium BCE, and it served Eteocypriot as well, a language that is not Greek at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two lived side by side: four bilingual inscriptions survive, Greek names are written out in Eteocypriot, and the kings of Amathous bore Greek names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island was mixed in another direction too, with substantial Phoenician settlements there from the middle of the 9th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What any of it proves about descent is a separate question, since features shared between dialects can come of contact and adoption as readily as of common ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Arcadocypriot","questions":["Rupert Thompson asked whether the prepositions shared by Mycenaean and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are an isogloss going back to before the palaces of the Bronze Age fell.","Cypriot, the eastern half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was written in a syllabic script that also served Eteocypriot, a language that is not Greek."],"tags":["language","history","greece","cyprus","iron age"],"sources":["M. J. C. Scarborough, review of <i>Ἑλληνικὲς διάλεκτοι στὸν ἀρχαῖο κόσμο</i>, ed. Anna Panayotou and Giovanbattista Galdi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.28/. [Open access]","John Bennet, review of <i>Donum Mycenologicum: Mycenaean Studies in Honour of Francisco Aura Jorro</i>, ed. Alberto Bernabé and Eugenio R. Luján, BCILL 131, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.04.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.04.25/. [Open access]","Eleanor Dickey, review of <i>Greek Writing from Knossos to Homer: A Linguistic Interpretation of the Origin of the Greek Alphabet and the Continuity of Ancient Greek Literacy</i>, by Roger D. Woodard, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.04.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.04.04/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>A Linguistic History of Ancient Cyprus: The Non-Greek Languages and their Relations with Greek, c. 1600-300 BC</i>, by Philippa M. Steele, Cambridge Classical Studies, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.01.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.01.24/. [Open access]","Roger Woodard, \"Aeolian Origins,\" <i>Center for Hellenic Studies Research Bulletin</i> 7 (2019), https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2019/03/15/aeolian-origins/. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200-479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Panoses_stele.jpg/1920px-Panoses_stele.jpg","title":"Arcadocypriot","desc":"Cypriot syllabary script stele, with the name Panoses (𐠞𐠜𐠩𐠩 - pa-no-se-se), found in Ormideia (in Larnaca district), Cypro-classical period, now in Larnaca District Archaeological Museum. פעמי-עליון, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoses_stele.jpg","alt":"Panoses stele"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-158","num":"158","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Paphos, Amathous, Salamis, Idalion, Marion and Kition were among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the separate states into which the island was divided in the 1st millennium BCE.","answer":"Cypriot city-kingdoms","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cypriot city-kingdoms</b> were the separate states into which Cyprus was divided in the 1st millennium BCE, among them Paphos, Amathous, Salamis, Idalion, Marion and Kition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When they formed is unknown, and it is one of the sharper arguments in the archaeology of the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One reading finds no general break at the turn of the Bronze Age, between the phases called Late Cypriot IIC and IIIA in the 13th and 12th centuries BCE, some areas breaking down economically while others flourished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other holds that even at Paphos, Kition and Enkomi-Salamis, the cases usually cited, the evidence for a political system carrying through to the 8th century BCE is meagre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that reading Amathous may be the earliest of them all, founded not in the 11th century but in the 9th or 8th under Phoenician influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Kition had been a substantial town since the 13th century BCE, with its temples and a massive wall along the sea, and in the 9th century it passed to the Phoenicians, who rebuilt the Bronze Age temple complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Copper is what made an inland kingdom worth taking, and is read as the reason Idalion became a desirable target and was in the end annexed by Kition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Assyria dealt with the island as a set of kings, and a cuneiform inscription records the gifts of seven of them to Sargon II in about 707 BCE, although the text has no secure findspot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How far a kingdom faced outward could differ inside its own borders: at Amathous the town tombs are full of Greek and Levantine pottery while the Vasilikos and Maroni valleys behind yielded almost none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That looks like a deliberate want of interest in imported pottery rather than isolation, the inland dead being given iron weapons instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cypriot city-kingdoms","questions":["Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew out of the Bronze Age or followed a break turns on the 12th century, where one reading finds continuity across it and another finds none.","Copper made an inland member of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worth taking, and is read as the reason Idalion was in the end annexed by Kition."],"tags":["state","history","archaeology","greece","cyprus","iron age"],"sources":["Christian Körner, review of <i>Les royaumes de Chypre à l'épreuve de l'histoire: transitions et ruptures de la fin de l'âge du Bronze au début de l'époque hellénistique</i>, ed. Anna Cannavò and Ludovic Thély, Bulletin de correspondance hellénique Supplément 60, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.39/. [Open access]","Christian Körner, review of <i>La naissance des cités-royaumes cypriotes</i>, by Thierry Petit, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.06.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.06.02/. [Open access]","Gerald Cadogan, review of <i>Kition de Chypre</i>, by Marguerite Yon, Guides archéologiques de l'Institut français d'archéologie du Proche-Orient 4, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.64, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.64/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>The Topography of Ancient Idalion and its Territory</i>, by Stephan G. Schmid and Will M. Kennedy, Studia Cyprologica Berolinensia 2, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.03.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.03.36/. [Open access]","David Gill, review of <i>Archaic Cyprus: A Study of the Textual and Archaeological Evidence</i>, by A. T. Reyes, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.03.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.03.34. [Open access]","Grace Erny, review of <i>The Connected Iron Age: Interregional Networks in the Eastern Mediterranean, 900-600 BCE</i>, ed. Jonathan M. Hall and James F. Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B13_007-CYPRVS_INSVLA.jpeg/1920px-Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B13_007-CYPRVS_INSVLA.jpeg","title":"Cypriot city-kingdoms","desc":"The island Cyprus was conquered in 1570 by the Turkse army from the Republic of Venice. For three hundred years it became part of the Turkish Empire. This map of Cyprus was published in 1635 by Willem Jansz. Blaeu (1571-1638) and Joan Blaeu (1598-1673). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B13_007-CYPRVS_INSVLA.jpeg","alt":"Atlas Van der Hagen-KW1049B13 007-CYPRVS INSVLA"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-159","num":"159","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Greek was written on Cyprus in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the 8th century BCE into Hellenistic times, an Aegean script akin to Linear B and read since 1872.","answer":"Cypriot syllabary","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cypriot syllabary</b> is the script in which Greek was written on Cyprus from the 8th century BCE well into Hellenistic times, an Aegean syllabic script akin to Linear B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was read from 1872 onwards, far earlier than the Aegean scripts it resembles, so that Greek in a syllabary was legible while the Bronze Age tablets were still shut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind it stands Cypro-Minoan, the Late Bronze Age writing of the island, whose signary runs to 112 identified signs with their variants and which no attempt has yet succeeded in reading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That earlier script was in use between about 1650 and 1050 BCE, largely for trade, cut into vessel handles, weights and oxhide ingots, and it turns up at Enkomi and across the water at Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The link forward is argued from signs that share identical or close form and phonetic values with Linear B, which points to some genetic connection between the Aegean systems and the Cypriot ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Linear B survives only as administration, where the syllabary was never so confined: it is attested for all the usual epigraphic genres, and well over a thousand texts are now known against the 456 in Masson's corpus of 1983.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It served languages besides Greek, Eteocypriot among them, and at Golgoi an unread local tongue called Golgian appears in syllabic inscriptions as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The alphabet reached the island in the 6th century BCE and the two ran together for centuries, so that a grave marker from Golgoi names the dead man twice, as <i>ka-ru-xe</i> in syllabic Greek and as <i>ΚΑΡΥΞΕΜΙ</i> in alphabetic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Kafizin more than 300 dedications to the Nymphs, dated between 225 and 218 BCE, carry both scripts on the same pot, while at Idalion nothing but the syllabary has turned up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nicocles, the last king of Paphos, reformed his own signary into a deliberately archaic form, and the script and the dialect it carried were still in use at the end of the 3rd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cypriot syllabary","questions":["A grave marker from Golgoi names the dead man twice, once in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and once in the Greek alphabet, the two scripts running side by side for centuries.","Behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands Cypro-Minoan, the Bronze Age writing of the island, which has 112 identified signs and which nobody has yet succeeded in reading."],"tags":["concept","technology","history","greece","cyprus","iron age"],"sources":["Andreas Willi, review of <i>Le dialecte grec ancien de Chypre. Tome I: Grammaire; Tome II: Répertoire des inscriptions en syllabaire chypro-grec</i>, by Markus Egetmeyer, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/le-dialecte-grec-ancien-de-chypre.tome-i-grammaire-tome-ii-repertoire-des-inscriptions-en-syllabaire-chypro-grec/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>Cypro-Minoan and its Writers: At Home and Overseas</i>, by Cassandra M. Donnelly, Cambridge Elements: Writing in the Ancient World, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.11.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.11.24/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>Syllabic Writing on Cyprus and its Context</i>, ed. Philippa M. Steele, Cambridge Classical Studies, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.04.53, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.53/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>Inscriptiones Graecae XV: Inscriptiones Cypri, Pars II Inscriptiones Cypri alphabeticae, Fasc. 1</i>, ed. Maria Kantiréa and Daniela Summa, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.12.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.12.26/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>The Topography of Ancient Idalion and its Territory</i>, by Stephan G. Schmid and Will M. Kennedy, Studia Cyprologica Berolinensia 2, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.03.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.03.36/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>Classical Cyprus: Proceedings of the Conference, University of Graz, 21-23 September 2017</i>, ed. Maria Christidis, Antoine Hermary, Gabriele Koiner and Anja Ulbrich, Kypriaka 5, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.02.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.02.43/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Idalion_bilingual_and_the_decipherment_of_the_the_Cypriot_syllabary.jpg/1920px-Idalion_bilingual_and_the_decipherment_of_the_the_Cypriot_syllabary.jpg","title":"Cypriot syllabary","desc":"Idalion bilingual and the decipherment of the the Cypriot syllabary. George Smith. 1871. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idalion_bilingual_and_the_decipherment_of_the_the_Cypriot_syllabary.jpg","alt":"Idalion bilingual and the decipherment of the the Cypriot syllabary"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-160","num":"160","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Athenian festival of the Synoikia gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its name, and the Greek word means, quite literally, 'the coming together of houses'.","answer":"synoecism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Synoecism</b> is several communities becoming one state, and the word is more literal than it looks: the Athenian festival of the Synoikia means 'the coming together of houses'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it does not say is whether anybody moved, since it covers a political union among communities that stay where they are as readily as a physical gathering of them into one town, and Marcel Piérart insists the two be kept apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Argos he takes a synoecism of the 8th century BCE, which gave that city the mastery of the Argolid, to be beyond doubt, while the place itself took an urban shape only centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind the distinction sits the question the whole subject turns on, whether anywhere can be counted a full polis when it has no town at its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Athenians told the story as Theseus', and Robert Parker lays out four possibilities for the date and the nature of that synoecism without settling on any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Noel Robertson goes further and holds that the Athenians misread their own festival, a celebration of a synoecism being properly a Synoikisteria, and that the Attic state was assembled out of phratry lodges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His reviewer is unpersuaded, unwilling to write off the 6th-century events Herodotus records as fictions projected backwards out of ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cases that can be checked are the late ones, and they do not always do what the sources say: Diodorus and Strabo have Elis made a city about 471 BCE, where the digging and the inscriptions show a nucleated settlement already working as a polis, so Roy reads a reinforcement rather than a creation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rhodes is the clearest instance of the other kind, Ialysos, Cameiros and Lindos becoming one state in 408/7 BCE, after which the priest of Helios gave the island's year its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It could also be done to people rather than by them, Mausolos forcing half a dozen small native settlements together into a new and much enlarged Halikarnassos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"synoecism","questions":["Marcel Piérart would keep two things apart under the heading of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: a political union of communities that stay put, and their physical gathering into one town.","In 408/7 BCE Ialysos, Cameiros and Lindos made one state by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the priest of Helios became the magistrate the Rhodian year was named after."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Bob Develin, review of <i>Festivals and Legends: The Formation of Greek Cities in the Light of Public Ritual</i>, by Noel Robertson, Phoenix Supplementary Volume 31, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1993.03.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.03.11/. [Open access]","Marek Wecowski, review of <i>La naissance de la ville dans l'antiquité</i>, ed. Michel Reddé et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.18/. [Open access]","Jon Mikalson, review of <i>Athenian Religion: A History</i>, by Robert Parker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.05.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.05.01/. [Open access]","Matthew Dillon, review of <i>Even More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, Papers from the Copenhagen Polis Centre 6, Historia Einzelschriften 162, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.14. [Open access]","Gaia De Luca, review of <i>Religious Life in Late Classical and Hellenistic Rhodes</i>, by Juliane Zachhuber, Oxford Classical Monographs, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.10.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.10.04/. [Open access]","Gary Reger, review of <i>The Hellenistic Settlements in Europe, the Islands, and Asia Minor</i>, by Getzel M. Cohen, Hellenistic Culture and Society 17, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.11.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.11.19/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-161","num":"161","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A town and the body of citizens whose town it was are the two senses the Greeks ran together in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which one inventory lists 1,035.","answer":"polis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>polis</b> is the form of community most of the Greek world organised itself into, and the word carries two senses at once, an urban centre and the body of citizens whose centre it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Copenhagen Polis Centre set out to establish what the Greeks themselves meant by it, by listing every settlement contemporary sources explicitly call a polis, and its inventory discusses 1,035 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A place earns its entry on what it was called and what it left behind, tested against toponymy and ethnics, territory, constitution, dependent status, civic subdivisions, walls, coins, religious organisation and urban features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city-ethnic, the name borne by a person of the place rather than by the place itself, is the most dependable sign of the status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independence turns out not to be required at all, since a dependent polis was still a polis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Autonomia is self-government of a limited sort rather than anything like the modern notion of autonomy, and a settlement Greek writers declined to call a polis was a kome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the thing arose is the argument: one case puts a structural revolution in 8th-century Greece, tying the rise of the polis to the enormous changes in material culture of that same century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Against it the shift is called far less drastic than usually posited and the 6th century the one that matters, buildings with specialised political functions appearing only then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The word's own history is turned round with it, the linguistic weight falling on the polis as a place, with the community of citizens looking like a secondary meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On that account the gathering of people into towns began after the Bronze Age collapse and was still unfinished in the classical period, and the places that became poleis first were those with a Mycenaean past behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"polis","questions":["A structural revolution in the 8th century BCE is put behind the rise of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, against the answer that the 6th century matters more.","Independence was never required of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: a dependent one was still counted as such, and the surest sign of the status is the name borne by its people."],"tags":["concept","politics","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of 'Poleis'</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Historisk-Filosofiske Meddelelser 74, and <i>More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Kurt Raaflaub, Historia Einzelschriften 108, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Frederick Naerebout, review of <i>An Inventory of Archaic and Classical Poleis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.02.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.02.29/. [Open access]","Simon Goldhill, review of <i>Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Kurt A. Raaflaub, Papers from the Copenhagen Polis Centre 2, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.10.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.10.11/. [Open access]","Sarah C. Murray, review of <i>An Age of Experiment: Classical Archaeology Transformed (1976-2014)</i>, ed. Lisa Nevett and James Whitley, McDonald Institute Monographs, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.40/. [Open access]","Sara Owen, review of <i>Archaic Greece: New Approaches and New Evidence</i>, ed. N. R. E. Fisher, Hans van Wees and Deborah Dickmann Boedeker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.11.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.11.27/. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200-479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Attica_06-13_Athens_50_View_from_Philopappos_-_Acropolis_Hill.jpg/1920px-Attica_06-13_Athens_50_View_from_Philopappos_-_Acropolis_Hill.jpg","title":"polis","desc":"View from Philopappos Hill in Athens (Attica, Greece) — Acropolis of Athens. A.Savin,. CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attica_06-13_Athens_50_View_from_Philopappos_-_Acropolis_Hill.jpg","alt":"Attica 06-13 Athens 50 View from Philopappos - Acropolis Hill"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-162","num":"162","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A Greek people spread over a region called itself by its own ethnic when it acted as a state, so an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appears in the record as 'the Aetolians' or 'the Boeotians'.","answer":"ethnos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>ethnos</b> is a Greek people settled across a region rather than gathered into one town: the Thessalians, Boeotians, Aetolians, Acarnanians, Phocians, Arcadians and Achaeans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Acting as a state such a people called itself by its own ethnic, 'the Aetolians' or 'the Boeotians', where scholars say koinon, an ancient word used for a community, for the body that passed decrees and for a federal assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Religion did much of the work of making one, shared sanctuaries and festivals building a sense of community beyond the single polis in Boeotia, while in Aetolia it came before the polis grew at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The leagues worked as interest groups competing for resources, with federal coinage, taxation and arbitration between members, the Aetolian League arising in the 4th century BCE against territorial loss and outside aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How tight the union was is another matter, since a third of the Aetolian and Peloponnesian coins tested fall short of 90% silver, and a member city kept its own standards so long as the coin carried the same message.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Whether an ethnos was ever one thing is doubted, the Aeolians having been called a phantom one, since togetherness need not translate into political expression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that reading ethnic claims were pressed by cities only when it served their own purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thessaly refuses the choice between a full federation and a loose ethnic affiliation, internal competition keeping the region going until Philip II ended its independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The upland peoples of Phocis, Aetolia, Acarnania and Arcadia mounted sophisticated defence through the 5th and 4th centuries BCE with no central government, giving up ground and striking without warning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Phocians are said to have whitened themselves with chalk about 490 BCE and fallen on the enemy camp to kill four thousand Thessalians, and in 426 the Aetolians abandoned their land, turned, and killed 300 Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"ethnos","questions":["The Aeolians have been called a phantom <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since a sense of ethnic togetherness need not translate into any political expression at all.","The upland peoples who made up an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Phocis, Aetolia, Acarnania and Arcadia defended themselves ably with no central federal government at all."],"tags":["concept","politics","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Elizabeth A. Meyer, review of <i>Greek Federal Terminology</i>, by Jacek Rzepka, Akanthina 12, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.05.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.05.27/. [Open access]","Roy van Wijk, review of <i>Ethnos and Koinon: Studies in Ancient Greek Ethnicity and Federalism</i>, ed. Hans Beck, Kostas Buraselis and Alex McAuley, Heidelberger Althistorische Beiträge und Epigraphische Studien 61, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.11.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.11.11/. [Open access]","Jacek Rzepka, review of <i>Creating a Common Polity: Religion, Economy, and Politics in the Making of the Greek Koinon</i>, by Emily Mackil, Hellenistic Culture and Society 55, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.09.58, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.09.58/. [Open access]","Nicholas Cross, review of <i>Greek Warfare beyond the Polis: Defense, Strategy, and the Making of Ancient Federal States</i>, by David A. Blome, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.03.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.03.08/. [Open access]","Roy van Wijk, review of <i>The \"Koina\" of Southern Greece: Historical and Numismatic Studies in Ancient Greek Federalism</i>, ed. Catherine Grandjean, Numismatica antiqua 12, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.06.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.06.05/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-163","num":"163","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The earliest wells at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belong to the early 7th century BCE, substantially later than 776, the year tradition gives its games.","answer":"Olympia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Games from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">776 BCE, by tradition</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Olympia</b> is the great sanctuary of the western Peloponnese where the Olympic games were held, and the ground under it was in use long before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Beneath the later Pelopion lies a mound ringed and covered with stones, of Early Helladic II date rather than Mycenaean, no tomb but a focus of rituals, its top demonstrably still visible in the Early Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The earliest objects from the site are Late Neolithic, while the oldest cult activity that can be reliably ascribed, ash from the altar with terracotta and bronze votives, runs from 900 to 600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The famous black layer holds kylikes of the late 11th century BCE and protogeometric jugs at its lowest and material of the advanced 7th at its top, and it was not laid down gradually but in a few brief grading operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That bears on the traditional date of the games, since the earliest wells belong to the early 7th century BCE, substantially later than 776.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Catherine Morgan reads the offerings in three stages, of which the first, before 800 BCE, has Olympia as a meeting place for the petty chiefs of the west, with some Arcadian jewellery and small bronzes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Through the 8th century the figurines stay small and simple while the tripods grow larger and more elaborate and are mostly brought from workshops elsewhere, the elites of emerging poleis competing beyond their own borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the 7th century pottery appears with a settlement at Elis beside the sanctuary, and tripods fall away as dedications here while becoming civic symbols in other places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> András Patay-Horváth would derive the games from elite hunting of wild cattle in the region, the tripods and animal figurines being its traces and a ritual chase its enactment, which his reviewer calls innovative but speculative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A rural sanctuary served as a rallying point where neighbouring communities exchanged hospitality, which is what this was long before Elis held it, as it did from at least 550 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olympia","questions":["Catherine Morgan reads the offerings at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a meeting place for the petty chiefs of the west before 800 BCE, with Arcadian jewellery and small bronzes.","A mound of the 3rd millennium BCE lies beneath the Pelopion at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, no tomb but a focus of rituals, its top still visible in the Early Iron Age."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Richard Hamilton, review of <i>Greek Sanctuaries: New Approaches</i>, ed. Nanno Marinatos and Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.03.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.03.17. [Open access]","Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of <i>Olympia: Archäologie eines Heiligtums</i>, by Helmut Kyrieleis, Zaberns Bildbände zur Archäologie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.06.15. [Open access]","William Childs, review of <i>Olympia 1875-2000: 125 Jahre deutsche Ausgrabungen</i>, ed. Helmut Kyrieleis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.02.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.02.26. [Open access]","Dina Guth, review of <i>The Origins of the Olympic Games</i>, by András Patay-Horváth, Archaeolingua Series Minor 36, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.47, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.47/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Placing the Gods: Sanctuaries and Sacred Space in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.06.01/. [Open access]","Nicola Giaccone, review of <i>Olympia: A Cultural History</i>, by Judith M. Barringer, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.21/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Restored_ruins_of_the_Temple_of_Hera%2C_ancient_Doric_Greek_temple_at_Olympia%2C_Greece_%2816187889508%29.jpg/1920px-Restored_ruins_of_the_Temple_of_Hera%2C_ancient_Doric_Greek_temple_at_Olympia%2C_Greece_%2816187889508%29.jpg","title":"Olympia","desc":"Restored ruins of the Temple of Hera, ancient Doric Greek temple at Olympia, Greece. 2014-04-23 11:06. Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Restored_ruins_of_the_Temple_of_Hera,_ancient_Doric_Greek_temple_at_Olympia,_Greece_(16187889508).jpg","alt":"Restored ruins of the Temple of Hera, ancient Doric Greek temple at Olympia, Greece"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Olympia","at":[21.63,37.6383]}},
{"id":"gr-164","num":"164","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Settlement comes first and cult eventually displaces it at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the earliest evidence is of the 8th century rather than the 10th.","answer":"Delphi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Delphi</b> is the sanctuary of Apollo on the flank of Parnassus that housed the most famous oracle of the Greek world, and there is evidence of settlement in the area from the Mycenaean period to the end of antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cult is late by comparison, since Catherine Morgan finds the earliest evidence of the 8th century rather than the 10th, and here the settlement comes first and is eventually displaced by the cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In that it differs from Olympia, which it otherwise resembles in being geographically remote and in holding large numbers of early votives, competitive dedications by the elite among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oracle, on Morgan's reading, is panhellenic from the start, consulted on community questions probably beyond the collective wisdom of the local elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hélène Aurigny and Michael Scott would rather take it for a discussion-inciting force, a catalyst for negotiation instead of a unilateral authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The bronzes of the late 8th and 7th centuries BCE are where the sanctuary's reach shows, tripods and cauldrons and vessels with griffin, siren and bull protomai fixed to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their provenance tables count 24 pieces from north Syria, 15 griffins from Samos, 14 from Crete, 13 from Cyprus and 12 more Levantine but unplaced, numbers that reflect the importance of the East and the processes of Orientalization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The building came later than the giving, the earliest historic temple of Apollo being put at about 600 BCE, a date inferred from reused blocks of Sikyonian poros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before Apollo the shrine is said to have belonged to the Python, and the springs of Castalia and Kassotis rise on the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cult did carry through the end of the Bronze Age elsewhere in Phocis, at Kalapodi, where intensive activity is securely dated from the second half of the 9th century BCE, although it is still unknown whether that sanctuary is Artemis of Hyampolis or the oracle of Apollo at Abai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Delphi","questions":["The early bronzes at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came from north Syria, Samos, Crete and Cyprus, numbers that reflect the importance of the East and the processes of Orientalization.","Morgan holds the oracle at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> panhellenic from the start, consulted on questions probably beyond the collective wisdom of the local elite."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Richard Hamilton, review of <i>Greek Sanctuaries: New Approaches</i>, ed. Nanno Marinatos and Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.03.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.03.17. [Open access]","Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of <i>Das antike Delphi</i>, by Michael Maass, Beck'sche Reihe 2431, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.24/. [Open access]","Christine Davidson, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World, Vol. IV: Cyrene to Metapontion</i>, ed. Paul Cartledge and Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.03.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.03.24/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Bronzes du haut-archaïsme à Delphes: Trépieds, chaudrons et vaisselle de bronze (fin VIIIe-VIIe siècle)</i>, by Hélène Aurigny, Fouilles de Delphes 5, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.08/. [Open access]","Nassos Papalexandrou, review of <i>Kalapodi: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen im Heiligtum der Artemis und des Apollon von Hyampolis in der antiken Phokis</i>, ed. Rainer C. S. Felsch et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.09.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.09.23. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/%27Delphi%27_by_Edward_Lear%2C_watercolor%2C_12_by_19_cm..jpg","title":"Delphi","desc":"'Delphi' by Edward Lear, watercolor, 12 by 19 cm. Edward Lear. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%27Delphi%27_by_Edward_Lear,_watercolor,_12_by_19_cm..jpg","alt":"'Delphi' by Edward Lear, watercolor, 12 by 19 cm."},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Delphi","at":[22.5013,38.4823]}},
{"id":"gr-165","num":"165","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A Bronze Age settlement of some 35,000 m² (8.6 acres) inside a circuit wall, one of the largest in the Aegean, lies beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Hera was later worshipped.","answer":"Heraion of Samos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 BCE, by its altars</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th century BCE, perhaps</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Altar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Heraion of Samos</b> is the sanctuary of Hera on that island, and it stands on ground that was crowded long before any god was worshipped there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beneath it lies a settlement with a nearly continuous sequence running from the Middle Chalcolithic, the second half of the 5th millennium BCE, into the Middle Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the late Early Bronze Age it was one of the largest settlements in the Aegean and western Anatolia, some 35,000 m² (8.6 acres) enclosed in a circuit wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sanctuary's own rise is traced from the first Greek settlers at the end of the Bronze Age, shown by its early altars, with a monumental temple standing there perhaps already in the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two much larger replacements followed in the 6th, together with the Great Altar and sculptural dedications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Telling a temple from a temple-like building is the difficulty of the place, ten of them standing in the archaic period, and Helmut Kyrieleis argues that many were oriented towards the great altar of Hera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others sit along the main routes through the sanctuary beside votive sculpture, which marks them out as treasuries, and the thrice life-size Isches kouros is the grandest thing given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was brought there is what makes the site remarkable, and Kyrieleis puts it plainly: no other excavation in Greece has produced as many imports, among them Egyptian and Near Eastern reliefs and figurines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A study of 1,105 dedications reads Hera through them, finding her tied to agriculture and vegetation and receiving military offerings, with 35 building models taken for her care of home and family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jan Bremmer sets the local against all that traffic, since the myths, dedications and festivals of Samian Hera, the Toneia and the Heraia among them, carry a flavour of their own and a maritime cast, the cult shifting from global to local over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Heraion of Samos","questions":["Helmut Kyrieleis says no other excavation in Greece has produced as many imports as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them Egyptian and Near Eastern reliefs and figurines.","Ten temple-like buildings stood at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the archaic period, many oriented towards the great altar and others marked out as treasuries by the sculpture beside them."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Richard Hamilton, review of <i>Greek Sanctuaries: New Approaches</i>, ed. Nanno Marinatos and Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.03.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.03.17. [Open access]","Barbara A. Barletta, review of <i>Sanctuaries and the Sacred in the Ancient Greek World</i>, by John Griffiths Pedley, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.10.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.10.20/. [Open access]","Nassos Papalexandrou, review of <i>The Significance of Votive Offerings in Selected Hera Sanctuaries in the Peloponnese, Ionia and Western Greece</i>, by Jens David Baumbach, BAR International Series 1249, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.05.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.05.55/. [Open access]","Daniel J. Pullen, review of <i>Ausgrabungen in der frühbronzezeitlichen Siedlung im Heraion von Samos 1966</i>, by Hans Peter Isler, Samos 30, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.01.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.01.34/. [Open access]","Ranjani Atur, review of <i>The Local Horizon of Ancient Greek Religion</i>, ed. Hans Beck and Julia Kindt, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.05.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.05.16/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Kore_Heraion_Samos_Louvre_Ma686.jpg","title":"Heraion of Samos","desc":"Kore (archaic statue of a young maiden) from the Heraion of Samos. Bears an inscription in Ionian alphabet: \"Cheramyes dedicated me to Hera, as a gift\". Marble, made in Samos, ca. 570–560 BCE. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kore_Heraion_Samos_Louvre_Ma686.jpg","alt":"Kore Heraion Samos Louvre"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Heraion of Samos","at":[26.8856,37.6719]}},
{"id":"gr-166","num":"166","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The sanctuary of Hera Akraia stood at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than in Corinth itself, and the children of Medea are argued to have been buried there.","answer":"Perachora","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Expansion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Perachora</b> is a sanctuary of Hera on the far side of the gulf from Corinth, one of the places through which that city ordered its sacred landscape, along with Isthmia and Temple Hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Catherine Morgan finds real changes in the material expression of religious belief across the Corinthia in the 8th century BCE, and a period of great expansion at Perachora with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her study of where wealth was put down, in graves against sanctuaries, has the two changing places about 750 BCE, when it moves out of the grave and into cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> De Polignac had brought the region into his account as early as 1984, mentioning Perachora and listing Isthmia as a border sanctuary in the 8th century and the site of a major temple in the 7th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Which Hera stood here has been argued over, and B. Menadier puts the sanctuary of Hera Akraia at Perachora rather than at Corinth itself, on the archaeology and on the texts together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On the same reading the children of Medea were buried here rather than in the middle of Corinth, and this was the only seat of the rites owed them, the cenotaph Pausanias saw in the city being a Roman monument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The place is rich in small valuable things and in imports, and a study of Hera's sanctuaries counts 3,417 votive offerings from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among them are four terracotta building models, bone pipes of the 6th century BCE whose purpose is still argued over, and Egyptian or Egyptianizing figures of Bes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across the Geometric and Archaic periods the Hera worshipped here stays the same kind of goddess, concerned with growing up, with home and family, and with agriculture and vegetation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of its buildings has been read as a hestiatorion, a hall for ritual dining, although the identification has never been settled, and much of what is said about the site still rests on a publication of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Perachora","questions":["A study of Hera's sanctuaries counts 3,417 votive offerings from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them four terracotta building models and Egyptian figures of Bes.","Corinth ordered its sacred landscape through Isthmia, Temple Hill and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last of which expanded greatly in the 8th century BCE."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Placing the Gods: Sanctuaries and Sacred Space in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.06.01/. [Open access]","Gina Salapata, review of <i>Peloponnesian Sanctuaries and Cults: Proceedings of the Ninth International Symposium at the Swedish Institute at Athens</i>, Acta Instituti Atheniensis Regni Sueciae Series 4, 48, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.04.30. [Open access]","Nassos Papalexandrou, review of <i>The Significance of Votive Offerings in Selected Hera Sanctuaries in the Peloponnese, Ionia and Western Greece</i>, by Jens David Baumbach, BAR International Series 1249, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.05.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.05.55/. [Open access]","Sara Owen, review of <i>Ancient Greek Cult Practice from the Archaeological Evidence</i>, ed. Robin Hägg, Acta Instituti Atheniensis Regni Sueciae 8°, 15, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.08.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.08.16/. [Open access]","Paul Scotton, review of <i>The Archaeology of Ancient Greece</i>, by James Whitley, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.09.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.09.41/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/%CE%A0%CE%B5%CF%81%CE%B1%CF%87%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%B1_%CE%9A%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%BD%CE%B8%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg/1920px-%CE%A0%CE%B5%CF%81%CE%B1%CF%87%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%B1_%CE%9A%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%BD%CE%B8%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg","title":"Perachora","desc":"Περαχώρα Κορινθίας. 2025-03-23 14:56:00. Apaleutos25, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A0%CE%B5%CF%81%CE%B1%CF%87%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%B1_%CE%9A%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%BD%CE%B8%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg","alt":"Περαχώρα Κορινθίας"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Perachora","at":[22.95,38.0333]}},
{"id":"gr-167","num":"167","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"An object given to a god and left in a sanctuary is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its inscription, where it has one, names the giver and the god.","answer":"votive dedication","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>votive dedication</b> is an object given to a god and left in a sanctuary, and where it carries an inscription that inscription says who gave it and to whom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 154 metrical inscriptions survive on Attic offerings of the 7th to 5th centuries BCE, most of them to Athena but others to Hermes, Poseidon, Aphrodite, Demeter and Kore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their dedicators run from the public offering to the individual benefactor, and take in women sustaining households and foreign craftsmen such as Euphron of Paros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The object is made to speak, and one of them answers a passer-by outright: 'To all men alike who ask I answer'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such a text is a concise presentation of the act and ritual of dedication, built from a handful of basic elements and reactivated whenever the object is seen and the words are read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The gift on that account is a charis, an exchange between human and divine that renews itself at each reading, the epigram written for reading aloud and meant to be repeated over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The inscribing is itself a material act, one that sets the offering inside a network of people rather than simply labelling it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Olympia and on the Athenian Acropolis, where arms and armour were given in quantity, the inscribed and the uninscribed sit together in no tidy system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not everything given was bought, since wreaths of greenery, cakes shaped into figures and votives woven or cut from wood were made by the giver, though how that making felt is hard to recover from evidence so patchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor was every dedication devout, those set up in the gymnasia answering to conventions political and institutional rather than to religious feeling, and 25 documents survive in which a Greek community regulates what may be placed in its sanctuary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"votive dedication","questions":["The epigram on a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a concise presentation of the ritual, reactivated whenever the object is seen and the words are read aloud.","Inscribed and uninscribed examples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sit together at Olympia and on the Athenian Acropolis, where arms and armour were given in quantity, in no tidy system."],"tags":["practice","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Alcorac Alonso Déniz, review of <i>Archaic and Classical Attic Dedicatory Epigrams: An Epigraphic, Literary and Linguistic Commentary</i>, by Sara Kaczko, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 33, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.06.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.06.13/. [Open access]","Eleonora Santin, review of <i>Archaic Greek Epigram and Dedication: Representation and Reperformance</i>, by Joseph W. Day, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.01.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.01.22. [Open access]","Signe Barfoed, review of <i>The Stuff of the Gods: The Material Aspects of Religion in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Matthew Haysom, Maria Mili and Jenny Wallensten, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.08.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.08.09/. [Open access]","Vinciane Pirenne-Delforge, review of <i>Personal Experience and Material Devotion in Ancient Greek Religion</i>, by K. A. Rask, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.12.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.31/. [Open access]","Paraskevi Martzavou, review of <i>Dediche sacre nel mondo greco-romano: diffusione, funzioni, tipologie</i>, ed. John Bodel and Mika Kajava, Acta Instituti Romani Finlandiae 35, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.38/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/-11032901_Etruscan_Male_Votive_Head_III_cen._BC.jpg/1920px--11032901_Etruscan_Male_Votive_Head_III_cen._BC.jpg","title":"votive dedication","desc":"An Etruscan terracotta votive head, 3rd century BCE, 28 centimetres high. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:-11032901_Etruscan_Male_Votive_Head_III_cen._BC.jpg","alt":"Etruscan male votive head, 3rd century BCE"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-168","num":"168","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The three-legged bronze vessels called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as cooking and heating gear before becoming the most prestigious dedications in Greek sanctuaries.","answer":"bronze tripod cauldrons","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Prized from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In decline</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>bronze tripod cauldron</b> is a three-legged bronze vessel, and it began as a thing for cooking and heating before it became anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the Bronze Age some classes of tripod had passed beyond that use into status symbols, princely gifts and prizes, and through the Geometric and Early Archaic periods they were the most prestigious dedications made in Greek sanctuaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are the votives that embody prestige and tradition above all others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tripod has been read as the symbol above all of authoritative discourse, and so of political power and of holding ground, tied as well to truthful revelation and to the consulting of Apollo at Delphi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reading is worth having, though it has been warned against for theoretical overreach and for speculation set down as established fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They could be enormous, the monumental cauldrons of the 8th century BCE from the Athenian Acropolis reaching more than 2 m (6 ft 7 in) in height, with one gorgon-decorated ring handle reconstructed at 77 cm (30 inches) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those handles were too fragile to lift anything and served mainly as decoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Figures were fastened to them from the 9th century BCE, at first bulls' heads and birds on a minority of examples, then horses through the 8th and human forms from its middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Towards the end of that century the spear-brandishing warrior gives way to handle-holders, pairs of figurines naked but for their helmets grasping opposite sides of the ring, the community's act of dedication acted out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the 7th century orientalising cauldrons arrive with griffin and siren attachments, most of them made on Samos and the same protomai fixed to the bronzes at Delphi, while at Olympia tripods fall away as offerings and become civic symbols elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"bronze tripod cauldrons","questions":["One ring handle from a monumental example of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is reconstructed at 77 cm (30 inches) across, and was too fragile to lift anything.","Pairs of helmeted figurines grasping the ring handles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replace the spear-brandishing warrior towards the end of the 8th century BCE, standing for the community's dedication."],"tags":["object","technology","religion","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["M. Jones, review of <i>The Visual Poetics of Power: Warriors, Youths, and Tripods in Early Greece</i>, by Athanasios Christou Papalexandrou, Greek Studies: Interdisciplinary Approaches, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.08.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.08.23/. [Open access]","Helle Hochscheid, review of <i>Hephaistus on the Athenian Acropolis: Current Approaches to the Study of Artifacts Made of Bronze and Other Metals</i>, ed. Nassos Papalexandrou and Amy Sowder Koch, Selected Papers on Ancient Art and Architecture 7, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.01.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.01.08/. [Open access]","Richard Hamilton, review of <i>Greek Sanctuaries: New Approaches</i>, ed. Nanno Marinatos and Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.03.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.03.17. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Bronzes du haut-archaïsme à Delphes: Trépieds, chaudrons et vaisselle de bronze (fin VIIIe-VIIe siècle)</i>, by Hélène Aurigny, Fouilles de Delphes 5, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.08/. [Open access]","Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of <i>Olympia: Archäologie eines Heiligtums</i>, by Helmut Kyrieleis, Zaberns Bildbände zur Archäologie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.06.15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Bronze_tripod_cauldron_%282%29%2C_Late_Haelladic_III_C%2C_AM_of_Mycenae%2C_201680.jpg/1920px-Bronze_tripod_cauldron_%282%29%2C_Late_Haelladic_III_C%2C_AM_of_Mycenae%2C_201680.jpg","title":"bronze tripod cauldrons","desc":"Bronze tripod cauldron (2), Late Haelladic III C, 1180 - 1050 BCE. Archaeological Museum of Mycenae. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_tripod_cauldron_(2),_Late_Haelladic_III_C,_AM_of_Mycenae,_201680.jpg","alt":"Bronze tripod cauldron (2), Late Haelladic III C, AM of Mycenae"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-169","num":"169","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A grave is called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because weapons were put in it, and at Fortetsa on Crete that is the whole of the reasoning.","answer":"warrior burial","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>warrior burial</b> is a grave taken for a fighting man's because weapons were put into it, and the identification is usually made on that ground alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At chamber tomb P at Fortetsa near Knossos, among the most important tombs on Crete in the Early Iron Age, a bronze girdle and a quiver accompanied two burials in pithoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> D'Acunto takes both to be warriors mainly because of the weapons standing beside the cinerary urns, which is the reasoning the whole category rests on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the sample is large the pattern does hold, and a study of 1,146 burials from Macedonia between 550 and 300 BCE finds weapons the purview of men, with elite men often presented in death as warriors, though not as Homeric ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same study finds little sign of women's part in war, since weapons are not usually found in their graves, while elite women reached for other marks of standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Reading a person off their goods is riskier than it looks, and the Athenian Agora cemeteries make the point: 83 tombs were catalogued and 53 bodies examined, and the burial long called the 'Young Lady' turns out to be probably male.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Weapons were also not the only way to be conspicuous in death, since 11 Athenian graves of the Middle and Late Geometric use bronze cauldrons as cremation urns, which Simona Dalsoglio reads as an exceptionally elite display bound up with feasting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Several contributors to the same volume argue for kinship groupings within the cemeteries, so a grave may say as much about a family as about the person laid in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the hinterland of Amathous on Cyprus the choice ran the other way, iron weapons serving as the display where the town's own tombs took imported pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A warrior burial therefore names a way of being buried rather than a proven occupation, and what the weapons announce may be rank, family or the manner of a funeral as much as a life spent fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"warrior burial","questions":["Behind every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies the finding of a study of 1,146 Macedonian graves, that weapons were the purview of men and elite men were presented in death as warriors.","The burial long called the 'Young Lady' in the Athenian Agora is probably male, a reminder that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read from its goods rather than its bones."],"tags":["practice","warfare","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos, The Athenian Agora 36, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","Vyron Antoniadis, review of <i>Kreta in der geometrischen und archaischen Zeit</i>, ed. W.-D. Niemeier, O. Pilz and I. Kaiser, Athenaia 2, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.09.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.09.29. [Open access]","Sabine Müller, review of <i>Age, Gender and Status in Macedonian Society, 550-300 BCE: Intersectional Approaches to Mortuary Archaeology</i>, by Elina M. Salminen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.08.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.08.30/. [Open access]","Nathan Arrington, review of <i>Rethinking Athens before the Persian Wars</i>, ed. Constanze Graml, Annarita Doronzio and Vincenzo Capozzoli, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.11.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.11.12/. [Open access]","Grace Erny, review of <i>The Connected Iron Age: Interregional Networks in the Eastern Mediterranean, 900-600 BCE</i>, ed. Jonathan M. Hall and James F. Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Birka%2C_Sweden_Viking_grave_Bj_581_by_Hjalmar_Stolpe_in_1889.jpg","title":"warrior burial","desc":"Sketch of archaeological grave found and labelled \"Bj581\" by Hjalmar Stolpe in Birka, Sweden. Published 1889. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birka,_Sweden_Viking_grave_Bj_581_by_Hjalmar_Stolpe_in_1889.jpg","alt":"Birka, Sweden Viking grave Bj 581 by Hjalmar Stolpe in 1889"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-170","num":"170","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Snodgrass's graph of burials rising steeply in Athens, Attica and the Argolid is the best-known evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Morris took it apart.","answer":"eighth-century revival","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>eighth-century revival</b> is the name for the burst of change that closes the Greek Dark Age, and its best-known evidence is a graph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Anthony Snodgrass published in 1980 a count of burials from Athens, Attica and the Argolid climbing steeply in the late 8th century BCE, and the climb was read as a rise in population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ian Morris took the count apart in 1987, showing that adult and child burials had been run together and the 7th century left out, after which population rise ceased to be a plausible, or even a possible, explanation of the figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Walter Scheidel puts it lower still: the yearly increase in Attica through the 8th century cannot pass 0.3 to 0.4 per cent, and cannot reach the 3 to 4 per cent hitherto assumed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On his view the sample is extremely small and not necessarily representative, and few conclusions drawn from it statistically deserve confidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That the material changes is not in doubt, and Morris set the massive change in material culture of the 8th century BCE beside the rise of the polis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Robin Osborne reads the densification of settlement in Attica as a mark of political unification, asking how such growth in the archaeological visibility of settlements could occur without raising political questions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Visibility is the word doing the work there, and his reviewer answers that the growth rates do not bear the reading, and that bronze tripod dedications on the acropolis do not begin only in the late 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even the century may be wrong, since Coldstream's chronology for the Iron Age Aegean faces a stiff challenge from radiocarbon at Sindos and Sidon, which would move everything between about 1150 and 735 upward, in the earlier phases by over a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On that revision the invention of the alphabet would have to be raised well into the 9th century, although Lane Fox's dating of Homer to about 780 to 750 BCE is thought likely to remain cogent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"eighth-century revival","questions":["Walter Scheidel holds that Attica's yearly population increase during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot pass 0.4 per cent, far below the 3 to 4 per cent once assumed.","Radiocarbon from Sindos and Sidon would move the whole chronology upward, so even the century named in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may turn out to be the wrong one."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Robin Osborne, review of <i>A History of Greece: 1300 to 30 BC</i>, by Victor Parker, Blackwell History of the Ancient World, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.09.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.09.11/. [Open access]","Helen Hatzimavroudi, review of <i>Griechische Archaik: interne Entwicklungen - externe Impulse</i>, ed. Robert Rollinger and Christoph Ulf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.08.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.08.12. [Open access]","Maximilian Rönnberg, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World, Vol. II: Athens and Attica</i>, by Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.09.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.09.41/. [Open access]","Sara Owen, review of <i>Archaic Greece: New Approaches and New Evidence</i>, ed. N. R. E. Fisher, Hans van Wees and Deborah Dickmann Boedeker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.11.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.11.27/. [Open access]","Richard Janko, review of <i>Homer and his Iliad</i>, by Robin Lane Fox, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.03.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.03.03/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-171","num":"171","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Greek cities built their earliest laws into sanctuary walls, and the block from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was set on the east wall of the temple of Apollo Delphinios.","answer":"Dreros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanctuary from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 8th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Dreros</b> is a Cretan city whose temple wall carried the oldest law surviving from anywhere in the Greek world, and the first text in which the word 'polis' appears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The block stood on the east wall of the temple of Apollo Delphinios, and it rules that a man who has held the office of kosmos may not hold it again for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kosmos was the most important office the city had, and the law sets out punishments for anyone found breaking the rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Three bodies swore the oath that confirmed it, the kosmos, the damioi and the ikati of the polis, and the last two are offices nobody can now identify.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is dated between 650 and 600 BCE, with the earliest legal inscriptions from Gortyn, and belongs to a turn by Greek cities towards inscribing law and displaying it in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Michael Gagarin reads the publication of such a law as a stage in the growth of a communal self-awareness, one that pushed people to identify with the polis of Dreros itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Written law appears about 650 BCE and quickly becomes a fixture of civic life in many Greek cities though not all, most of it built into a sanctuary wall, the letters big, painted and up to 25 centimetres (10 inches) tall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Karl-Joachim Hölkeskamp counted lawgiving attested for 53 Greek communities from Argos to Zancle, Dreros among those he treated at length, and the famous law from Chios is later, at about 575 to 550 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Neither Dreros nor Gortyn was a democracy and both published their laws regardless, while Zinon Papakonstantinou reads the three swearing bodies as authority that was multiple, overlapping and sometimes in conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Digging on the west acropolis has found three phases in the sanctuary, the offerings of the 7th-century phase richer than the deposit beneath them and perhaps the mark of a smaller and more powerful elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dreros","questions":["The temple wall at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried the oldest Greek law that survives, forbidding a man to hold the office of kosmos again for ten years.","The word polis is first found in writing on a block from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the kosmos, the damioi and the ikati swore to keep the law."],"tags":["place","politics","history","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Alex Gottesman, review of <i>Lawmaking and Adjudication in Archaic Greece</i>, by Zinon Papakonstantinou, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.08.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.08.28. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>Writing Greek Law</i>, by Michael Gagarin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.06.49, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.06.49/. [Open access]","Vyron Antoniadis, review of <i>Cretan Cities: Formation and Transformation</i>, ed. Florence Gaignerot-Driessen and Jan Driessen, Aegis 7, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.10.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.10.17. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>Cité, démocratie et écriture: histoire de l'alphabétisation d'Athènes à l'époque classique</i>, by Christophe Pébarthe, Culture et cité 3, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.05.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.05.17. [Open access]","Eric Robinson, review of <i>Schiedsrichter, Gesetzgeber und Gesetzgebung im archaischen Griechenland</i>, by Karl-Joachim Hölkeskamp, Historia-Einzelschriften 131, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.04.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.04.16. [Open access]","Maria Ilaria De Rossi, “Dreros Law,” <i>Axon</i> 1, no. 2 (2017), https://doi.org/10.14277/2532-6848/Axon-1-2-17-01. [Open access]","Federica Carugati, Gillian K. Hadfield and Barry R. Weingast, “Building Legal Order in Ancient Athens,” <i>Journal of Legal Analysis</i> 7, no. 2 (2015): 291–324, https://doi.org/10.1093/jla/lav003. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Dreros_12.jpg/1920px-Dreros_12.jpg","title":"Dreros","desc":"Archaeological site of Dreros (Δρῆρος), municipality of Agios Nikolaos, Crete, Greece. Olaf Tausch, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dreros_12.jpg","alt":"Dreros 12"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Dreros","at":[25.6283,35.2567]}},
{"id":"gr-172","num":"172","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A Greek city's farms, tombs and country shrines lay in its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the land outside the town, and the inventory of poleis gives the size of each.","answer":"chora","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>chora</b> is the land a Greek city held outside its town, and the great inventory of archaic and classical poleis states the size of one for each of the 1,035 places it discusses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town itself is the astu, and the same introduction sets that word beside polis and polisma, each with its own meaning and reference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word 'polis' will not stay in the town: Mogens Hansen finds it used for the political community, for the urban centre and for the hinterland or territory alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Susan Alcock presses the point that chora and polis cannot be separated, and that field survey is what shows where the people of a city actually lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her picture of the countryside has rural dwellings in it, and tombs and shrines with them, the Southern Argolid standing as the worked example.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The archaic city walled its town and not its land, and the forts and towers standing out in the territory are few and far between before 480 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> De Polignac made the rural sanctuary a rallying point that articulated a city's territory, some of them standing on the frontier between the inhabited world and the empty one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Carter's work at Metaponto denies it, holding that the rural shrines there mark no boundary, while Cole finds sanctuaries of Demeter in the town, beside it and out at the borders alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A chora could be laid out on a grid, the territory of Chersonesos organised on orthogonal lines and the European part of the Bosporan kingdom cut into rectangular plots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sparta drew the sharpest line of the lot, Cartledge finding town and chora divided by status and by obligation, and its greatest festival held out at Amyklai rather than in the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"chora","questions":["Paul Cartledge found Sparta drawing an exceptionally sharp line between the town and its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with lower status and different obligations for those who lived out there.","On the Black Sea the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Chersonesos was organised on orthogonal lines, and the European part of the Bosporan kingdom was likewise cut into rectangular plots."],"tags":["concept","geography","history","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Frederick Naerebout, review of <i>An Inventory of Archaic and Classical Poleis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.02.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.02.29/. [Open access]","Jacek Rzepka, review of <i>The Return of the Polis: The Use and Meanings of the Word Polis in Archaic and Classical Sources</i>, by Mogens Herman Hansen, Papers from the Copenhagen Polis Centre 8, Historia Einzelschriften 198, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.02.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.02.46/. [Open access]","Lise Hannestad and Sidsel Westh-Hansen, review of <i>Classical Archaeology</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Blackwell Studies in Global Archaeology 10, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.05.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.05.42/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Greek City Walls of the Archaic Period, 900–480 BC</i>, by Rune Frederiksen, Oxford Monographs on Classical Archaeology, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.05.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.05.08/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Placing the Gods: Sanctuaries and Sacred Space in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.06.01/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Surveying the Greek Chora: The Black Sea Region in a Comparative Perspective</i>, ed. Pia Guldager Bilde and Vladimir F. Stolba, Black Sea Studies 4, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.05.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.05.06/. [Open access]","Graham Shipley, review of <i>Sparta in Laconia: The Archaeology of a City and its Countryside</i>, ed. William G. Cavanagh, BSA Studies Series 4, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.03.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.03.02. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-173","num":"173","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Every Arkadian city but Mantineia built its walls around a patch of defended high ground, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> giving its name to a whole type of fortification.","answer":"acropolis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>acropolis</b> is the high ground at the heart of a Greek town, and the word 'polis' is held to have widened from it to the settlement around it and then to a whole territorial state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It names a kind of fortification as well as a place, the acropolis type leaning on natural defences where a horizontal type and an uneven one do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every Arkadian polis but Mantineia worked a naturally protected height into its circuit, 19 of them catalogued in detail with 14 more besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among archaic walls, Emporio on Chios encloses 2.4 hectares (5.9 acres) with nobody living inside it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Athens the walls Kylon's men were besieged behind in about 632 BCE were presumably Mycenaean, the Pelargikon among them, and no archaic circuit there has ever been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Athenian hill is the one everybody knows, in use from Neolithic times to the closing of the ancient sanctuary in the late 5th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A height carried cult as well as walls: the West Acropolis at Dreros holds a sanctuary of three phases, opening on offerings and animal bones of the late 8th or early 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Assos a temple of Athena went up on the acropolis about 540 BCE, built of andesite quarried from that same rock and mixing Doric, Ionic and Aeolic in one building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Which god held a height is not always recoverable, the sanctuary at Stymphalos having been given to Athena Polias on an inscription, to Artemis on its sculpture and to Artemis Eileithyia on the animal bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its temple is a plain house-like building with an altar and a court of five aniconic stone pillars beside it, and the offerings run to an archaic kore and over 325 pieces of jewellery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"acropolis","questions":["Athena Polias, Artemis and Artemis Eileithyia have all been argued for as the goddess of the sanctuary on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Stymphalos, and its excavators disagree.","Kylon's men were besieged at Athens in about 632 BCE behind walls on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that were presumably Mycenaean, no archaic circuit having ever been found there."],"tags":["place","warfare","religion","archaeology","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]","Matthias R. Nöth, review of <i>The Fortifications of Arkadian City States in the Classical and Hellenistic Periods</i>, by Matthew P. Maher, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.52, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.52/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Greek City Walls of the Archaic Period, 900–480 BC</i>, by Rune Frederiksen, Oxford Monographs on Classical Archaeology, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.05.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.05.08/. [Open access]","Susan I. Rotroff, review of <i>The Athenian Acropolis: History, Mythology, and Archaeology from the Neolithic Era to the Present</i>, by Jeffrey M. Hurwit, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.07.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.07.17. [Open access]","Vyron Antoniadis, review of <i>Cretan Cities: Formation and Transformation</i>, ed. Florence Gaignerot-Driessen and Jan Driessen, Aegis 7, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.10.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.10.17. [Open access]","Tuna Şare, review of <i>The Temple of Athena at Assos</i>, by Bonna Daix Wescoat, Oxford Monographs on Classical Archaeology, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.05.40/. [Open access]","Jeannette Marchand, review of <i>Stymphalos: The Acropolis Sanctuary, Volume 1</i>, ed. Gerald P. Schaus, Phoenix Supplementary Volumes 54, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.08.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.08.02. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg/1920px-Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg","title":"acropolis","desc":"The village and castle (acropolis) of Lindos, island of Rhodes, Greece. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg","alt":"Lindos Rhodes 3"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"gr-174","num":"174","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Before it was a square it was a meeting, and the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belonged to the assembly of all a community's adult men, who sat under a council of elders.","answer":"agora","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>agora</b> is a gathering before it is a place: the word names the assembly of all a community’s adult men, sitting under a council of elders drawn from the noble families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Homer the council, the assembly and the law court together are the government, and across the archaic period the council gained ground as the assembly lost it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name then passed from the meeting to the ground it met on, so an agora is a political institution and an architectural type at once; some 70 are known across the mainland and the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stoas, council houses, temples, shrines, gateways, concert halls, market halls and libraries stood around one, and legal business and public proclamation went on there beside the trading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What an agora looked like therefore depended on what a city had put around its edges rather than on any one plan; what they shared was the open ground and the uses it was put to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Two routes led to that ground: a new foundation could reserve it from the start, and a city that grew had to clear it of what stood there already.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Megara Hyblaia in Sicily is the first kind, keeping ground clear from the 8th century with its earliest foundations already respecting the line between domestic and public, then filling it with religious and public building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens is the second and among the latest: its ground was a potters’ field and a burial ground, pottery was still being made there in the mid-7th century BCE, and it was not laid out as an agora until the end of the archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The most famous agora is therefore not the typical one, but a late example of a form already old when Athens took it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Greek west the type runs from archaic Megara Hyblaia to Halaesa in the 2nd century BCE, the agora at Soluntum holding a stoa, porticoes, cisterns and an odeon that doubled as a council house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"agora","questions":["Megara Hyblaia in Sicily kept ground clear for its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the 8th century, and religious and public building filled that reserved space over the next two hundred years.","Pottery was still being made on the ground of the Athenian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the mid-7th century BCE, and it was a potters' field and a cemetery long before it was anything civic."],"tags":["place","politics","archaeology","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Edmund F. Bloedow, review of <i>Early Greece</i>, by Oswyn Murray, 2nd ed., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.01/. [Open access]","Mischa Meier, review of <i>Ancient Greece: A Political, Social, and Cultural History</i>, by Sarah B. Pomeroy et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.07.08/. [Open access]","Samantha L. Martin-McAuliffe, review of <i>On the Agora: The Evolution of a Public Space in Hellenistic and Roman Greece (c. 323 BC – 267 AD)</i>, by Christopher P. Dickenson, Mnemosyne Supplements 398, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.02.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.06/. [Open access]","David Smith, review of <i>Megara Hyblaia and Selinous: The Development of Two Greek City-States in Archaic Sicily</i>, by Franco De Angelis, Oxford University School of Archaeology Monograph 57, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.28. [Open access]","Patrick Thomas, review of <i>Ceramicus Redivivus: The Early Iron Age Potters' Field in the Area of the Classical Athenian Agora</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos, Hesperia Supplement 31, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.03.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.03.21/. [Open access]","Laura Pfuntner, review of <i>Die Agora von Solunt: öffentliche Gebäude und öffentliche Räume des Hellenismus im griechischen Westen</i>, by Markus Wolf, Sonderschriften des Deutschen Archäologischen Instituts Rom 16, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.11.35, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.11.35/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Stoa_in_Athens.jpg","title":"agora","desc":"Stoa in Athens. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stoa_in_Athens.jpg","alt":"Stoa in Athens"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"gr-175","num":"175","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Archaic Greeks called a man of Athens an Athēnaios and had no abstract noun for what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possessed, which is why defining early citizenship is so contested.","answer":"polites","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>polites</b> is a member of a polis, and the two are inseparable by definition: a town that is also a community of citizens, and a community of citizens that is also a town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clearest mark of the standing is the city-ethnic, the name a person of the place bore, which denotes membership of a polis and is only primarily political.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the archaic period there is little evidence that the Greeks had a clear concept of citizenship, or even a word for it: they had ethnics like Athēnaios and no abstract noun behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the standing rested on is disputed, whether descent from the founders, a performance rather than a grant, or a distinguished behaviour before ever a legal position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Legal regulation was stricter than had once been thought, with formal military obligations on the elite, while the thētes at the bottom sit in an ambiguous place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Against the performance case stands the plain fact that women, metics and slaves all did essential civic work and none of them was institutionalised as a citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle marks off citizenship in its fullest sense, the male prerogative, from the more fragmented forms held by women and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A range of middling positions lies between the two, women holding functions of their own distributed by criteria that probably included gender, and metics keeping their ties home while travelling freely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of the citizen body was poor: the Athenian working class made up 90 per cent of it in the early 6th century BCE and about half by the classical period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> For women and minors the standing could be precarious, since even a distant male relative might threaten it, and with it their protection from being enslaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"polites","questions":["Being a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is argued to have been something performed rather than granted, and answered with the case that it was a legal status and not a civic function.","The working class made up 90 per cent of Athens' citizen body in the early 6th century, so the ordinary <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> laboured for a living."],"tags":["concept","politics","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Jacek Rzepka, review of <i>The Return of the Polis: The Use and Meanings of the Word Polis in Archaic and Classical Sources</i>, by Mogens Herman Hansen, Papers from the Copenhagen Polis Centre 8, Historia Einzelschriften 198, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.02.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.02.46/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of 'Poleis'</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Historisk-Filosofiske Meddelelser 74, and <i>More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Kurt Raaflaub, Historia Einzelschriften 108, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","J. J. Mulhern, review of <i>Defining Citizenship in Archaic Greece</i>, ed. Alain Duplouy and Roger W. Brock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.03.14/. [Open access]","Christopher J. Joyce, review of <i>Citizenship Practised, Citizenship Imagined: Multiple Ways of Experiencing Citizenship in the Greek World</i>, ed. Lucia Cecchet and Chiara Lasagni, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.02.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.02.23/. [Open access]","Anna Accettola, review of <i>Citizenship in Antiquity: Civic Communities in the Ancient Mediterranean</i>, ed. Jakub Filonik, Christine Plastow and Rachel Zelnick-Abramovitz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.05.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.05.20/. [Open access]","Samuel Ortencio Flores, review of <i>Voiceless, Invisible, and Countless in Ancient Greece: The Experience of Subordinates, 700–300 BCE</i>, by Samuel D. Gartland and David W. Tandy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.11/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-176","num":"176","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Kyrene had three, Korinth eight and the Aeolic cities none at all, so the number of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a Greek city divided its citizens into was nothing like standard.","answer":"phyle","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>phyle</b> is the largest of the divisions a Greek city cut its citizens into, and it did the work of taking newcomers in, building solidarity and keeping tyranny off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The number varied from city to city: three at Kyrene, four at Sikyon, eight at Korinth and three at Argos until a fourth was added about 460 BCE, while the Aeolic cities had none at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Chios the council sat fifty men to the phyle, at Miletus six of them coordinated the picking of magistrates, and Spartan phylai fought together in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleisthenes of Sikyon gave his city's phylai the names Archelaoi, Hyatai, Oneatai and Choireatai, marking Sikyon off from its rival Argos and stiffening its army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kyrene reformed its own after a defeat that followed 565 BCE, the work of Demonax, and at Athens Cleisthenes built ten phylai on top of trittyes and demes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Civic subdivisions are standard enough to be one of the headings under which all 1,035 poleis of the archaic and classical periods are described, across 45 regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where a phyle came from is disputed: one account derives them from bands of settlers who later attached to places, named by who arrived first, against an older view that they were artificial creations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below the phyle a citizen belonged to a phratry, organised by kinship, and to the gene, oikos, orgeones and thiasos below that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was a unit of the army as well, the tribal taxis being raised from one, though whether that or the men of a single deme formed the hoplite's primary group is argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Military service of this kind bought citizens a share in the political decisions of the polis, in Sparta and Boiotia as much as at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"phyle","questions":["One account has each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beginning as a band of settlers who later attached to a place, against the older view that they were artificial creations.","Spartan units of this kind fought together, and the tribal taxis, the unit raised from one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is argued to have been the Athenian hoplite's primary group."],"tags":["concept","politics","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["James Kierstead, review of <i>Die griechischen Phylen: Funktion – Entstehung – Leistungen</i>, by Oliver Grote, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.09.47, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.09.47/. [Open access]","Frederick Naerebout, review of <i>An Inventory of Archaic and Classical Poleis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.02.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.02.29/. [Open access]","Susan Lape, review of <i>The Associations of Classical Athens: The Response to Democracy</i>, by Nicholas F. Jones, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.04.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.04.18. [Open access]","Christopher Matthew, review of <i>The Psychology of the Athenian Hoplite: The Culture of Combat in Classical Athens</i>, by Jason Crowley, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.25/. [Open access]","Adam Schwartz, review of <i>The Spear, the Scroll, and the Pebble: How the Greek City-State Developed as a Male Warrior-Citizen Collective</i>, by Richard A. Billows, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.15/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/L%27Attique_apr%C3%A8s_Clisthene.jpg/1920px-L%27Attique_apr%C3%A8s_Clisthene.jpg","title":"phyle","desc":"L'Attique après les réformes de Clisthène, avec les dix \"tribus\", les trois \"pays\", les trente \"trittyes\" et les dèmes. Claude Zygiel, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%27Attique_apr%C3%A8s_Clisthene.jpg","alt":"L'Attique après Clisthene"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-177","num":"177","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Draco's homicide law is argued to imply that every Athenian belonged to a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a case that has never been universally accepted.","answer":"phratry","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>phratry</b> is one of the associations a Greek citizen belonged to below the phyle, organised by kinship and on principles that were not democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cult practice was uniform enough to build a common Athenian identity, and generic phratry monuments stood in the agora for city dwellers cut off from their ancestral centres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below it came the gene, the oikos, the orgeones and the thiasos, sorted by kinship and by wealth rather than by any democratic rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Twelve are counted at Athens, three to each of the revised Ionic phylai, so that a group of three made up the unit called a trittys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Solonian legislation opened citizenship to men outside the elite through the phratries, and through them the ideas of Athenian descent and citizenship were in the end mapped onto territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Their vitality fell away through periods of warfare and political upheaval, some deme groups absorbing phratry cults outright, and the fit between the two was never a tidy one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The decree of the Demotionidai is the main document for the Attic phratry, and its reading is contested down to whether the Dekeleis named in it are a subgroup of the Demotionidai or something else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost all of what is known is Attic, and whether every Athenian belonged to one in the archaic period is unsettled, one argument for it resting on what Draco's homicide law implies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Attic polis has been explained as a combination of phratry lodges scattered across Attica, an account that asks the Athenians to have misunderstood their own institutional language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What counted in daily life was the household and the ties inside it rather than any extended bloodline, whatever the wider groups a man was enrolled in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"phratry","questions":["Organised by kinship rather than by democratic principle, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built a common Athenian identity out of cult practice that was the same from one to the next.","The decree of the Demotionidai is the centrepiece of the standard collection of inscriptions for the Attic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its reading is contested."],"tags":["concept","politics","religion","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Susan Lape, review of <i>The Associations of Classical Athens: The Response to Democracy</i>, by Nicholas F. Jones, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.04.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.04.18. [Open access]","Antony Keen, review of <i>The Phratries of Attica</i>, by S. D. Lambert, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.04.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.04.13/. [Open access]","Bob Develin, review of <i>Festivals and Legends: The Formation of Greek Cities in the Light of Public Ritual</i>, by Noel Robertson, Phoenix Supplementary Volume 31, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1993.03.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.03.11/. [Open access]","Christopher Smith, review of <i>Kinship in Ancient Athens: An Anthropological Analysis</i>, by Sarah C. Humphreys, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.12.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.12.24/. [Open access]","Tim Parkin, review of <i>The Family in Greek History</i>, by Cynthia B. Patterson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.06.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.06.07/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-178","num":"178","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not found in the sources with the sense of 'clan' until the 4th century BCE, though modern accounts apply it to much earlier families.","answer":"genos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>genos</b> is one of the groups an Athenian phratry divided into, sorted with the oikos, the orgeones and the thiasos by kinship and by wealth rather than by any democratic rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What such a group chiefly did was transmit a priestly office, passing that right down among its members, and there is little evidence it ever did anything else at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Athenian gene look geographical in origin rather than fundamentally aristocratic, taking their character from where their members lived rather than from descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Belonging to one placed a man inside a phratry and so inside the phyle above it, which is how a Greek city reached down as far as the household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What counted in practice was that household and the ties inside it, between husband and wife, parent and child, master and slave, rather than any extended bloodline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The word is not found in the sources with the sense of 'clan' until the 4th century BCE, though modern accounts routinely apply it to families centuries older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The standing of a genos moved rather than holding still, and at Athens one that had been privileged at first was later limited in what it could claim and then repudiated altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two studies of 1976 concluded independently that the evidence will not carry more than the priestly function, and the older picture has not recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether institutions sharing one name across different periods hold any fundamental identity is doubted, and the same doubt has been pressed on the Roman gens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The groups themselves were real enough, since a phratry did subdivide and did so by kinship and standing; it is the ancient clan behind them that has gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"genos","questions":["Two studies of 1976 concluded independently that there is little evidence the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was ever anything but a group that passed on priestly office.","The Athenian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks geographical in origin rather than fundamentally aristocratic, taking its character from where its members lived rather than from descent."],"tags":["concept","politics","religion","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Susan Lape, review of <i>The Associations of Classical Athens: The Response to Democracy</i>, by Nicholas F. Jones, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.04.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.04.18. [Open access]","Mischa Meier, review of <i>Ancient Greece: A Political, Social, and Cultural History</i>, by Sarah B. Pomeroy et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.07.08/. [Open access]","Ayelet Haimson Lushkov, review of <i>The Roman Clan: The Gens from Ancient Ideology to Modern Anthropology</i>, by Christopher John Smith, W. B. Stanford Memorial Lectures, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.11.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.11.06/. [Open access]","Antony Keen, review of <i>The Phratries of Attica</i>, by S. D. Lambert, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.04.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.04.13/. [Open access]","Tim Parkin, review of <i>The Family in Greek History</i>, by Cynthia B. Patterson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.06.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.06.07/. [Open access]","Christopher Smith, review of <i>Kinship in Ancient Athens: An Anthropological Analysis</i>, by Sarah C. Humphreys, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.12.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.12.24/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-179","num":"179","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Medieval and modern images of nobility have distorted what is written about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the term itself is now held to be only rarely useful.","answer":"archaic aristocracy","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>archaic aristocracy</b> is the elite of Greece in the 7th and 6th centuries BCE, and standing in it was not fixed at birth but had to be earned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Access to the higher ranks was open to enterprising men, and the standing was tied not to lineage but to wealth and its display.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had to be won and won again, so that a man who stopped competing for prestige lost it whatever his birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Six ways of being recognised run through the record, claimed descent, marriage, funerals, luxury goods, where a statue was dedicated and how original it was, across the 10th to 5th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Honour was at first tied to holding property, and only later, once civic offices were held unpaid, could it be kept apart from material wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The drinking party was the arena where a fluid and contested standing was settled, open on equal terms to anyone with the right training whatever his background.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its practice is traced by pottery from about 800 BCE, reclining came in later rather than being the defining mark, and the form looks Greek rather than borrowed from the Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Constant pressure to pile up wealth and perform the part drove men into ruinous behaviour, which is what the mobility of the period looked like from inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Archaic tyrants were less a new thing than a newly unacceptable one, elite power-seeking having gone on all along and been condemned as law rose between about 750 and 650 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether a unified nobility existed in archaic Greece at all is doubted, a structured one being placed only in 5th-century Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"archaic aristocracy","questions":["Standing in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was something won and re-won rather than inherited, so a man could lose it whatever his birth.","The drinking party was open to anyone with the right training, which made it a way into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a fence around it."],"tags":["concept","politics","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Massimiliano Di Fazio, review of <i>'Aristocracy' in Antiquity: Redefining Greek and Roman Elites</i>, ed. Nick Fisher and Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.11.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.11.31/. [Open access]","Timothy Power, review of <i>Le Prestige des élites: Recherches sur les modes de reconnaissance sociale en Grèce entre les Xe et Ve siècles avant J.-C.</i>, by Alain Duplouy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.08.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.08.22/. [Open access]","Adam Rabinowitz, review of <i>The Rise of the Greek Aristocratic Banquet</i>, by Marek Wecowski, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.10.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.10.42/. [Open access]","Evan Vance, review of <i>From Homer to Solon: Continuity and Change in Archaic Greece</i>, ed. Johannes C. Bernhardt and Mirko Canevaro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.03.14/. [Open access]","Christian Mann, review of <i>“Adel” und gesellschaftliche Differenzierung im archaischen und frühklassischen Griechenland</i>, by Jan Bernhard Meister, Historia Einzelschriften 263, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.03.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.03.43/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Statue_of_Nicandre_NAMA1_Athens_Greece.jpg","title":"archaic aristocracy","desc":"Female statue. Island marble. Found in Delos, in a sanctuary of Artemis. One of the earliest monumental statues in stone, it probably represents the goddess Artemis. To the left thigh of the statue is an inscription, dedication to Apollo by the Naxian Nikandre. Daedalic style. Ca.650 BCE. N°1. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Nicandre_NAMA1_Athens_Greece.jpg","alt":"Statue of Nicandre NAMA1 Athens Greece"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-180","num":"180","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The middle-class army of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now called a myth resting on one isolated ancient generalisation, these being ill-trained amateurs rather than a settled middle rank.","answer":"hoplite","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>hoplite</b> is the heavily armed infantryman of the Greek city, and the middle-class hoplite army is now called a myth resting on one isolated and ill-founded ancient generalisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These were amateurs: archaic and classical land armies were made up of ill-trained non-specialists, with no place to train but the gymnasia, which the wealthy kept to themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Battle descriptions hardened the divisions between ranks rather than levelling them, and the citizen militias behind them were haphazard about command, mobilisation, supply and discipline alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bronze bell cuirass of the Argos panoply weighs 3.360 kilograms (7.4 pounds), a figure hard to place as either impossibly heavy or unexpectedly light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not every cuirass was bronze, compound versions in fabric being lighter and presumably cheaper, and the shapes flattered: a bell cuirass narrowed the waist and broadened the chest, while a muscle cuirass exaggerated the musculature of chest and stomach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Greek armies were never hoplites alone, cavalry, slingers and archers serving beside them and never getting the credit in antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Light infantry beat them in the field more than once, Chalcidaeans at Megara in 458 BCE and at Spartolus in 429, and Athenian missile troops on Sphacteria in 425.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What held a man in the line is argued over, the men of his own deme on one account and the tribal regiment raised from his phyle on another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Below the hoplites stood the thetes, whose invisibility in the record is put down to stigma and to land-based census classes falling out of use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Skirmishers, slaves and mercenaries stood at the centre of Greek fighting, and their absence from the record is blamed on an elite taste for the heavily armed and the face-to-face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"hoplite","questions":["Chalcidaean light infantry beat them at Spartolus in 429 BCE, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not own the battlefield the way antiquity's writers suggest.","The bronze bell cuirass of the Argos panoply worn by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> weighs 3.360 kilograms, a figure hard to place as either heavy or light."],"tags":["concept","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Kevin Daly, review of <i>Greek Warfare: Myths and Realities</i>, by Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.66, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.66/. [Open access]","Stewart Flory, review of <i>Reinstating the Hoplite: Arms, Armour and Phalanx Fighting in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Adam Schwartz, Historia Einzelschriften 207, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.23/. [Open access]","Nadya Williams, review of <i>Combined Arms Warfare in Ancient Greece: From Homer to Alexander the Great and His Successors</i>, by Graham Wrightson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.04.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.04.55/. [Open access]","Christopher Matthew, review of <i>The Psychology of the Athenian Hoplite: The Culture of Combat in Classical Athens</i>, by Jason Crowley, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.25/. [Open access]","Samuel Ortencio Flores, review of <i>Voiceless, Invisible, and Countless in Ancient Greece: The Experience of Subordinates, 700–300 BCE</i>, by Samuel D. Gartland and David W. Tandy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.11/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Amphora_phalanx_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1429.jpg/1920px-Amphora_phalanx_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1429.jpg","title":"hoplite","desc":"Phalanx. Side A of an Attic black-figure Tyrrhenic amphora, ca. 560 BCE. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphora_phalanx_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1429.jpg","alt":"Amphora phalanx Staatliche Antikensammlungen 1429"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-181","num":"","category":"","question":"Each man's shield covered the flank of the neighbour on his left, which is why a whole battle line of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drifted rightward as it advanced.","questions":["A general could not pass an order down a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once it was moving, so where the contingents stood before the fighting began was the whole of Greek tactical thought.","The bloodiest part of a Greek battle came after a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke, when men threw away shield and armour to run and cavalry rode them down."],"answer":"phalanx","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze panoply</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 725 – c. 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>phalanx</b> is the massed close-order formation in which Greek heavy infantry fought, drawn up in files and ranks, and the word itself carries an older sense of 'beam' or 'roller'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>aspis</i> shaped it: about 90 centimetres (three feet) across and some 6.8 kilograms (15 pounds), it locked to the elbow, leaving its bearer's right side bare while reaching well past his left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That lopsidedness pulled whole classical battle lines rightward, each man edging in behind the shield of the neighbour on that side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the contingents stood before the fighting began was the substance of Greek tactical thought, since a general could not pass an order down a moving line and few of his men had ever drilled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The right end is usually called the post of honour, yet in more than half the documented battles another part held it, and the recorded depths ignore the neat multiples of eight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Nor was battle the ritual meeting on an open plain it is often taken for: two thirds of the classical engagements recorded turned on deception, surprise or a bid for better ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the ranks then physically shoved, the <i>othismos</i> or mass push that Greek historians name without explaining, has been argued over for close to a century, as has the date when a citizen formation replaced looser fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Reconstruction and computer modelling of the archaic bronze panoply of bell cuirass, Corinthian helmet and <i>aspis</i> suggest it was built to withstand crushing pressure, putting massed pushing at 700 BCE at the latest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A line broke by degrees rather than at a stroke, and the rout that followed was the bloodiest part of the day, fleeing men shedding shield and armour as cavalry rode them down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cavalry fought in the line as well as after it and stayed decisive into the Hellenistic age; of the fields themselves only a handful still carry a mound or trophy where a phalanx stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"phalanx","tags":["concept","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["H. W. Lanphier, \"The Bronze Hoplite Panoply and the <i>Othismos</i> in the Early Phalanx,\" <i>Greece &amp; Rome</i> 72, no. 2 (2025): 292–310, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0017383525100375. [Open access]","Kyle Fingerson, review of <i>Classical Greek Tactics: A Cultural History</i>, by Roel Konijnendijk, Mnemosyne Supplements 409, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.11.48, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.11.48/. [Open access]","Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Clemens Koehn, review of <i>Understanding Greek Warfare</i>, by Matthew A. Sears, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.02.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.02.24/. [Open access]","Emil Nankov, review of <i>Battles and Battlefields of Ancient Greece: A Guide to Their History, Topography and Archaeology</i>, by C. Jacob Butera and Matthew A. Sears, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.02.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.02.25/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Stele_of_the_vultures_%28phalanx%29.jpg/1920px-Stele_of_the_vultures_%28phalanx%29.jpg","title":"phalanx","desc":"Stele of the vultures (phalanx). Ernest de Sarzec - Choquin de Sarzec, Ernest (1832-1901). 1886-01-01. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_the_vultures_(phalanx).jpg","alt":"Stele of the vultures (phalanx)"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"gr-182","num":"","category":"","question":"Locked to the forearm by a bronze band at its centre, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not be shifted across to cover its bearer's right side, nor slung on his back in flight.","questions":["Held straight on, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stuck out well past its bearer's left while leaving his right side bare, wasting protection and weight in the same movement.","The deep bowl of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let its rim sit on the cuirass rather than the bare shoulder, so pressure went into the armour and not the collarbone."],"answer":"aspis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Agora shield</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE fill</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>aspis</b> is the round shield of the Greek heavy infantryman, about 90 centimetres (three feet) across and around 6.8 kilograms (15 pounds) for its robust construction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It hung on two grips rather than one: a band at the centre, the porpax, through which the left forearm passed to the elbow, and a handle at the rim, the antilabe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That arrangement locked the shield to the arm and cost it its reach, so it could not be shifted across to cover the right side as a single-handled shield could, nor slung on the back in flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Held straight on it left its bearer's right side bare and stuck out well past his left, wasting protection and weight at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bowl was deep enough for the rim to sit on the cuirass where front and back plates overlap rather than on the bare shoulder, so pressure passed into the armour instead of the collarbone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That bowl also kept the press of a crowd off the chest, and the rim would strike its bearer's own nose and mouth unless he tucked it hard against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How heavy it really was is argued over, one side defending the old estimates of a considerable burden and the other asking whether they had ever been checked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it did is not in question: it was a defensive and an offensive weapon at once, and the plain tactics of the massed line followed from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also outlasted the rest of the bronze panoply, staying in common use long after the bell cuirass and the Corinthian helmet had gone out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One survives largely complete from a cistern filling in the Athenian Agora, bent and chipped, 0.97 metres across at its widest, its narrow rim worked in a fine guilloche and its plain centre carrying an inscription punched in letters eight centimetres high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"aspis","tags":["object","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["H. W. Lanphier, \"The Bronze Hoplite Panoply and the <i>Othismos</i> in the Early Phalanx,\" <i>Greece &amp; Rome</i> 72, no. 2 (2025): 292–310, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0017383525100375. [Open access]","Debra Hamel, review of <i>Warfare in Ancient Greece: A Sourcebook</i>, by Michael M. Sage, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.06.01/. [Open access]","Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Stewart Flory, review of <i>Reinstating the Hoplite: Arms, Armour and Phalanx Fighting in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Adam Schwartz, Historia Einzelschriften 207, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.23/. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Agora Object B 262,\" <i>Athenian Agora Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.agathe.gr/id/agora/object/b%20262. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Hoplitodromos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1471.jpg","title":"aspis","desc":"Hoplitodromos (armoured race); on the right some tripods as winning prizes. Side A of an Attic black-figure neck-amphora, ca. 550 BCE. From Vulci. Group E, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoplitodromos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1471.jpg","alt":"Hoplitodromos Staatliche Antikensammlungen 1471"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-183","num":"","category":"","question":"The thickest bronze on a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> guards the nose and cheeks, which a weapon rarely reached, and the thinnest sits on the crown, where a blow was likeliest to land.","questions":["Forward-angled cheekpieces left the throat open to a careless man, but let the wearer of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pull his neck in and brace his head as the lines met.","Covering the whole head and most of the face, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave more protection than anything else a Greek wore and took his sight, hearing and movement in exchange."],"answer":"Corinthian helmet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – c. 475 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Corinthian helmet</b> is the bronze helmet of the Greek heavy infantryman, covering the whole head and most of the face, and it was made far beyond the city it is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Surviving examples stand about 23 centimetres (nine inches) high, and much of the hoplite equipment that survives at all was dedicated at sanctuaries such as Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One was probably cast first and then hammered to its final shape, and the crest it often carried was of horsehair or the like, which survives on none of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gave more protection than anything else a Greek wore, and took vision, hearing and movement in exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Replica testing bears the first of those out: a helmet of this type genuinely restricts what its wearer can see.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its bronze is deliberately uneven: one taken from the plain of Marathon weighs 1,193 grams and runs to 10 millimetres at the nasal and 2 or 3 across the face, while its crown is under a millimetre thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is the reverse of what the damage suggests: the crown is where a blow was likeliest to land and is barely thick enough for one, while the nose and face were unlikely to be struck at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The explanation offered is the shield, since as two lines collided the rim of a man's own <i>aspis</i> came at his nose and mouth, so the thickest metal on his head was armour against his own equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cheekpieces angle forward, which leaves the front of the throat open to a careless man but lets him pull his neck in and brace his head at the moment of impact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Smiths learned to work-harden the bronze in the late 6th century and made the helmets thinner as they hardened, and within a generation the type had all but left the vases, worn by more than four hoplites in five painted between 525 and 500 BCE and by fewer than one in five painted after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Corinthian helmet","tags":["object","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Cleveland Museum of Art, \"Corinthian Helmet,\" accession 2024.63, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.clevelandart.org/art/2024.63. [Open access]","Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Stewart Flory, review of <i>Reinstating the Hoplite: Arms, Armour and Phalanx Fighting in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Adam Schwartz, Historia Einzelschriften 207, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.23/. [Open access]","Robert Mason, \"Weapon Wednesday: The Nugent Marathon Corinthian Helmet,\" Royal Ontario Museum, 19 February 2014, https://www.rom.on.ca/blogs/weapon-wednesday-nugent-marathon-corinthian-helmet. [Open access]","H. W. Lanphier, \"The Bronze Hoplite Panoply and the <i>Othismos</i> in the Early Phalanx,\" <i>Greece &amp; Rome</i> 72, no. 2 (2025): 292–310, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0017383525100375. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Corinthian_helmet_BM_2838.jpg","title":"Corinthian helmet","desc":"Corinthian helmet BM 2838. Marie-Lan Nguyen. 2006. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corinthian_helmet_BM_2838.jpg","alt":"Corinthian helmet BM 2838"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-184","num":"","category":"","question":"Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ever happened turns on the double-grip shield, which is supposed to have brought a sudden revolution in tactics early in the archaic period and then in politics.","questions":["The modern case for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rests on a remark of Aristotle's, that more people gained a share in government once the phalanx displaced the aristocrats and their horses.","Against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it is argued that the equipment was lighter than supposed and that the middling farmer at the heart of the case did not exist in numbers."],"answer":"hoplite reform","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Argued over</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since the 19th century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Orthodox from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1947</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>hoplite reform</b> is the proposed change by which the double-grip shield brought a sudden revolution in Greek tactics early in the archaic period, and with it a great shift in society and politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea descends from Aristotle, who wrote that aristocrats and their horses had ruled the battlefield and the state alike until the phalanx and its greater numbers gave more people a share in government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In its fullest modern form the farmers who filled that phalanx are credited with remaking the polis, laying the ground for political equality and for citizenship itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the standard account in English after an influential article of 1947, and it has been held in one form or another for more than a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind it stood a 19th-century model of Greek battle as a ritual: armies meeting by arrangement on an open plain, always eight ranks deep, the general on the right, and no part for cavalry or light troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From the 1960s the study of the arms dedicated at sanctuaries such as Olympia began turning that sudden revolution into a gradual one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later work held that the equipment was lighter than supposed, so men could fight outside the densest formations, and that the fighting in the <i>Iliad</i> was not clearly different from the archaic phalanx.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The middling farmer at the heart of the case came under attack as well, the middle-class hoplite army being called a myth resting on a single ill-founded ancient generalisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On one full alternative the early polis gave equal rights to about 15% of its people and the broader citizen phalanx appeared only in the very late 6th century, while field survey tells against the spread of farming onto poor land the older case needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing has settled it: reconstruction of the bronze panoply has since been used to argue that a mass phalanx must be as old as the equipment, and the question is still described as a vexed one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"hoplite reform","tags":["theory","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Kyle Fingerson, review of <i>Classical Greek Tactics: A Cultural History</i>, by Roel Konijnendijk, Mnemosyne Supplements 409, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.11.48, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.11.48/. [Open access]","Kevin Daly, review of <i>Greek Warfare: Myths and Realities</i>, by Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.66, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.66/. [Open access]","H. W. Lanphier, \"The Bronze Hoplite Panoply and the <i>Othismos</i> in the Early Phalanx,\" <i>Greece &amp; Rome</i> 72, no. 2 (2025): 292–310, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0017383525100375. [Open access]","Clemens Koehn, review of <i>Understanding Greek Warfare</i>, by Matthew A. Sears, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.02.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.02.24/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Vix_crater_hoplite_circa_500_BCE.jpg","title":"hoplite reform","desc":"Vix crater hoplite circa 500 BCE. Oblomov2. 2018-10-18. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vix_crater_hoplite_circa_500_BCE.jpg","alt":"Vix crater hoplite circa 500 BCE"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-185","num":"","category":"","question":"The top frieze carries the earliest surviving picture of massed hoplites, which puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the centre of the argument over how that formation arose.","questions":["Under the handle of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the oldest known picture of the Judgement of Paris, damaged, with inscriptions naming two of the goddesses and Alexander.","Every warrior on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries two spears, gripped along with the shield handle, and the pair being taken up behind them are painted with throwing loops."],"answer":"Chigi vase","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 640 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Chigi vase</b> is a Protocorinthian olpe of about 640 BCE, 26.2 centimetres high, painted with three friezes of human figures and among the most ambitious pots ever made at Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is probably the earliest surviving example of the olpe, a shape that became one of the commonest Corinth produced, and it is a jug for pouring wine at a feast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It ended up in Etruria, in the main chamber of a princely tomb under a mound 60 metres across outside Veii, among some 230 objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its friezes run from a hare hunt at the foot to a lion hunt and a procession of a chariot and four horsemen in the middle band, and hoplites closing at the top; read upward they have been taken for the career of a Corinthian nobleman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the handle is the Judgement of Paris, the oldest known picture of it, damaged but carrying inscriptions that name two of the goddesses and Alexander, with Hermes gone except for the tip of his herald's staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The top frieze holds the earliest surviving picture of massed hoplites, and a piper with them: piping is named twice in Homer and never in war, and the cheek-strap that holds the pipes cannot be traced before the 7th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That frieze is treated as the canonical image of the phalanx in archaic art, and it is central material evidence in the case against the older account of how the formation arose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What unsettles it is that the men carry two spears each, gripped along with the shield handle, and that the pair being taken up in the arming scene behind them are painted with throwing loops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A reading published in 1947 explained the second shafts away as an unpainted rank standing behind, which fails because the hands close on shaft and handle together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of the painted evidence is ambiguous in this way, which is why flatly opposed accounts of archaic warfare have both survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chigi vase","tags":["object","art","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Tom Rasmussen, \"Paris on the Chigi Vase,\" <i>Cedrus</i> 1 (2013): 55–64, https://doi.org/10.13113/cedrus/20131675. [Open access]","Thierry Petit, \"Les sphinx sur le Vase François et l'Olpè Chigi : l'héroïsation des élites,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome — Antiquité</i> 131, no. 2 (2019), https://doi.org/10.4000/mefra.9092. [Open access]","Wawrzyniec Miścicki, \"Notes on Representations of Hoplites with Double Spears in Archaic Greek Art and the Usage of Javelins and Spears by the Archaic Phalanx,\" <i>Studies in Ancient Art and Civilisation</i> 17 (2013): 113–20, https://doi.org/10.12797/SAAC.17.2013.17.09. [Open access]","Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Kevin Daly, review of <i>Greek Warfare: Myths and Realities</i>, by Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.66, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.66/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Detail_from_the_Chigi-vase.jpg/1920px-Detail_from_the_Chigi-vase.jpg","title":"Chigi vase","desc":"Hoplites go to battle at the sounds of music. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_from_the_Chigi-vase.jpg","alt":"Detail from the Chigi-vase"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-186","num":"","category":"","question":"Both held unlimited power for a time, which is why a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is hard to tell from a lawgiver, and each usually rose in answer to a crisis in his own city.","questions":["Justifying himself as the corrector of injustice, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could point to oracles like those gathered around Cypselus, which present his rule as the answer to unjust leaders.","On one reading a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was no innovator at all but the culmination of aristocratic rule, and the men he oppressed were other aristocrats rather than the people at large."],"answer":"tyrannos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>tyrannos</b> is a ruler who took sole power in a Greek city outside its ordinary order, and the tyrants of the archaic age are a varied group that resists easy definition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyranny grew out of the economic and political crisis many communities went through, and a tyrant commonly rose in answer to a local one, often holding an office of the city while he did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line between him and a lawgiver is thin enough to look artificial, since both held unlimited power for a time, and his rule was usually limited too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was not necessarily an innovator: on one reading archaic tyranny is the culmination of aristocratic rule rather than a break with it, and the men it oppressed were other aristocrats rather than the people at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tyrants tended to be benefactors of their cities, strengthening their position by patronage and by networks among the elite, and they commissioned works of art that advertised them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> They justified themselves as correctors of injustice, and the oracles gathered around Cypselus of Corinth present his rule as the answer to unjust leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What legitimised a ruler was excellence shown in public, in distinguished or divine descent, in war, in the games, or in founding a city; wealth counted because it displayed that rather than by itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As support fell away they dressed themselves as founders or liberators to prolong their power, while the justice they dispensed came to look arbitrary and in need of correction of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The neat sequence in which hereditary kings gave way to tyrants grown rich is doubted, and so is the sharp line ancient writers drew between king and tyrant: tyrants founded dynasties, and three generations was enough to count as well established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether a man was called a tyrant at all could turn on the politics of whoever wrote about him, and rule of this kind faded as it grew more repressive and fell out of step with the cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"tyrannos","tags":["title","concept","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]","Lynette Mitchell, review of <i>A Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. Kurt A. Raaflaub and Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.45/. [Open access]","Philip Davies, review of <i>The Heroic Rulers of Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Lynette Mitchell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.30/. [Open access]","B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]","Marcaline J. Boyd, review of <i>Tyranny: New Contexts</i>, ed. Sian Lewis, Dialogues d'histoire ancienne supplement 21, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.10.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.10.37/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Tiranicidas_04.JPG/1920px-Tiranicidas_04.JPG","title":"tyrannos","desc":"Naples. National Archaeological Museum. Harmodius and Aristogeiton. Miguel Hermoso Cuesta, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiranicidas_04.JPG","alt":"Tiranicidas 04"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-187","num":"","category":"","question":"A prophecy of usurpation sent the Bacchiadae to kill the infant <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who was hidden from them in a ceramic beehive, or so Herodotus tells it.","questions":["Having ended the rule of the Bacchiadae, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have needed neither bodyguard nor mercenaries, and to have lived as a highly esteemed aristocrat.","The oracles gathered around <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> present his rule as the correction of an injustice done by the men before him, which is the standard justification a tyrant offered."],"answer":"Cypselus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 580 BCE, or as late as 540</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cypselus</b> ended the rule of the Bacchiadae at Corinth and made himself sole ruler of the city, founding a dynasty that is one of the standard examples of archaic tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His father Eetion came from outside the Bacchiad clan and his mother was Labda, and what is told of the family is fictive beyond reasonable doubt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest telling is Herodotus's: a prophecy of usurpation sends the Bacchiadae to kill the child, who is hidden from them in a ceramic beehive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is his mother whom Herodotus makes lame, not him, though the story is often retold the other way about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oracles gathered around him too, presenting his rule as the correction of an injustice done by the men before him, which is the standard justification a tyrant offered for taking power at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Once the Bacchiadae were gone he seems to have been accepted by most Corinthians, the aristocracy among them, since they were the men the Bacchiadae had shut out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is said to have needed neither bodyguard nor mercenaries, to have had no rival left to fear, and to have lived as a highly esteemed aristocrat rather than as anything new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against that stands Herodotus, who has him drive many Corinthians into exile and kill many more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His son Periander met a different generation of nobles who wanted power for themselves, and held his by keeping a bodyguard and dealing ruthlessly with his enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the dynasty ended is not settled: the traditional date falls in the 580s BCE, and a case has been made for bringing it down as late as 550 or 540, the ancient figures behind both coming from Herodotus, Thucydides and the later chronographers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cypselus","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Christopher J. Joyce, review of <i>The Tyrants of Corinth: Legends of Cypselus and Periander</i>, by Daniel Ogden, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.50/. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]","Alden Mosshammer, review of <i>Il POxy. 664 di Eraclide Pontico e la cronologia dei Cipselidi</i>, by Walter Lapini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.11/. [Open access]","B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]","Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-188","num":"","category":"","question":"Cut down whatever stands tallest: in Aristotle's telling that is the advice <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Thrasybulus, a lesson in reducing one's political opponents.","questions":["Facing nobles who wanted power for themselves, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept his by a bodyguard and by ruthless measures, turning his father's tyranny towards coercion.","The ghost of his dead wife Melissa would not tell <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> where the treasure lay until her burial rites were made good, or so the story runs."],"answer":"Periander","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cypselid rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ends c. 580 BCE, or as late as 540</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Periander</b> is the second of the Cypselid tyrants of Corinth, the son of Cypselus, and in the tradition that grew up around him one of the Seven Sages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He met a different generation of nobles from his father's, men who wanted power for themselves and disputed his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He held on to it by keeping a bodyguard and dealing ruthlessly with his enemies, turning the tyranny towards coercion while remaining bound to the aristocratic world he had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The parable of the ears of corn belongs to him: in Aristotle's telling it is Periander who advises Thrasybulus, and the advice to cut down whatever stands tallest is a lesson in reducing one's political opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyrants of this kind were benefactors of their cities as well, strengthening their position by patronage and by networks among the elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What is told of his household is grim and mostly invention: he is said to have consulted the ghost of his dead wife Melissa, and for selfish reasons, to learn where treasure was hidden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She would not answer him until her burial rites were made good, which he saw to by defrauding the women of Corinth of their clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His son Lycophron stands in that tradition as the counter to Cypselus: where the grandfather moves from the margin to the centre, the grandson is condemned to wander without ever reaching home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An oracle that the Cypselid line would fail in a later generation ties the two stories together, and they are one strand of the long Corinthian narrative through which tyranny is presented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All of it is enveloped in fiction, and even the dynasty's dates are unsettled, the ancient reckonings resting on Herodotus, Thucydides and the later chronographers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Periander","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Christopher J. Joyce, review of <i>The Tyrants of Corinth: Legends of Cypselus and Periander</i>, by Daniel Ogden, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.50/. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]","Leone Porciani, review of <i>Reading Herodotus: A Guided Tour through the Wild Boars, Dancing Suitors, and Crazy Tyrants of The History</i>, by Debra Hamel, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.01.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.01.23/. [Open access]","Alden Mosshammer, review of <i>Il POxy. 664 di Eraclide Pontico e la cronologia dei Cipselidi</i>, by Walter Lapini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.11/. [Open access]","Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Periander_Pio-Clementino_Inv276.jpg","title":"Periander","desc":"Bust of Periander bearing the inscription “Periander, son of Cypselus, Corinthian”. Marble, Roman copy after a Greek original from the 4th century. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Periander_Pio-Clementino_Inv276.jpg","alt":"Periander Pio-Clementino"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-189","num":"","category":"","question":"The seven monolithic columns still standing above <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belong to a temple built in the middle of the 6th century BCE to replace a destroyed predecessor.","questions":["For three centuries the Potters' Quarter at the western edge of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned out pots, figurines, painted plaques and votive shields from the white marl clay of the place.","The rock of Acrocorinth stands 575 metres high above <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and commanded the road across the Isthmus, the pass south towards Argos and the coastal road west to Sicyon."],"answer":"Corinth","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Apollo temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Potters' Quarter</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Corinth</b> lies below Acrocorinth, a rock 575 metres (1,886 feet) high that controlled the road across the Isthmus, the pass south towards Cleonae and Argos, and the coastal road west to Sicyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Water made the place as much as the rock did: at the spring of Peirene there is activity from the Neolithic onwards, the first works to manage the water date from the Geometric period, and improvement goes on from the archaic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city's great archaic building is the temple of Apollo, put up in the middle of the 6th century BCE to replace a 7th-century predecessor that had been destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carried six columns at each end and 15 along each side, all of them monolithic, and its great length against its width is a mark of its date; seven of those columns are standing still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That it belonged to Apollo is a deduction rather than a record, resting on the description of the city Pausanias left and on a small dedicatory plaque found in the area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At the western edge of the walled city lay the Potters' Quarter, a complex of workshops and living quarters that worked for three centuries, from the 7th to the 4th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its potters used the white marl clay of the place, and the moulds they left show they turned out figurines, painted plaques and votive shields as well as pots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wasters, pieces melted by accident in the firing, came out of the ground there, and two shrines with standing stones were found in it by later excavators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rock above the city went on being fortified long after all this: the earliest walls surviving on Acrocorinth are of the later 4th century, and what stands over them is the work of Byzantine, Ottoman, Venetian and modern hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archaic city underneath was held by the Bacchiadae until Cypselus broke their grip, after which he was accepted by most Corinthians, the aristocracy among them, whom the Bacchiadae had shut out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Corinth","tags":["city","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Temple of Apollo,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/temple%20of%20apollo. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Potters' Quarter,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/potters%27%20quarter. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Acrocorinth,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/acrocorinth. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Peirene,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/peirene. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-58-09.jpg/1920px-Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-58-09.jpg","title":"Corinth","desc":"Greece, Ancient Corinth, Temple of Apollo. Berthold Werner, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-58-09.jpg","alt":"Korinth BW 2017-10-10 10-58-09"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Corinth","at":[22.8802,37.9053]}},
{"id":"gr-190","num":"","category":"","question":"Ships were moored and emptied of their cargo before being moved on a wheeled vehicle along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which spared them the voyage round the Peloponnese.","questions":["About 8 kilometres long and 3.5 to 6 metres wide, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is associated with Periander and was in use from at least the 5th century BCE to the 1st.","One argument holds that no constant flow of ships and cargoes crossed on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that the commercial life of the isthmus lay in its emporion instead."],"answer":"Diolkos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 100 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Diolkos</b> is the paved trackway that carried boats overland across the Isthmus of Corinth, sparing them the long and dangerous voyage round the Peloponnese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That crossing is the route the rock of Acrocorinth commanded, together with the pass south towards Argos and the coastal road west to Sicyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The road ran about 8 kilometres (5 miles) and was 3.5 to 6 metres (11 to 20 feet) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was built in the 6th century BCE and is associated with Periander, tyrant of Corinth from 625 to 585.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A ship was moored and emptied of its cargo before being moved with the help of a wheeled vehicle, and the road was particularly useful for warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was in use from at least the 5th century to the 1st, and had fallen out of use after Corinth was destroyed in 146 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Part of the paved road was dug out in 1956 and 1960, and the beachrocks at its western end record a subsidence of some 35 centimetres (14 inches) after that destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the middle of the 19th century the road was taken as the key to Corinth's fortunes and to the commercial usefulness of its isthmus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A re-examination of the archaeological, logistical and textual evidence has argued instead that it carried no constant flow of ships and cargoes, and that it served the communication, transport, travel and strategic ends of Corinth and her allies rather than the goods of other states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that account the commercial life of the isthmus lay in its emporion for exchange rather than in the road, hauling ships across was rare and attested only in military contexts, and the trackway served the supply of Isthmia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Diolkos","tags":["building","object","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Giannis Saitis, Anna Karkani, Niki Evelpidou, and Hampik Maroukian, \"Palaeogeographical Reconstruction of Ancient Diolkos Slipway by Using Beachrocks as Proxies, West Corinth Isthmus, Greece,\" <i>Quaternary</i> 5, no. 1 (2022): art. 7, https://doi.org/10.3390/quat5010007. [Open access]","David K. Pettegrew, \"The Diolkos of Corinth,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 4 (2011): 549–74, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.4.0549. [Paywalled]","Michael Kleu, review of <i>The Isthmus of Corinth: Crossroads of the Mediterranean World</i>, by David K. Pettegrew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.07.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.07.36/. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Diolkos,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/diolkos. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Acrocorinth,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/acrocorinth. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Istmo_de_Corinto_ESC_large_ISS011_ISS011-E-13188.JPG/1920px-Istmo_de_Corinto_ESC_large_ISS011_ISS011-E-13188.JPG","title":"Diolkos","desc":"KORINTHOS, KHILIOMODHION Camera Camera Tilt: 24 Camera Focal Length: 400mm Camera: E4: Kodak DCS760C Electronic Still Camera Film: 3060E : 3060 x 2036 pixel CCD, RGBG array. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istmo_de_Corinto_ESC_large_ISS011_ISS011-E-13188.JPG","alt":"Istmo de Corinto ESC large ISS011 ISS011-E-13188"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-191","num":"","category":"","question":"Winning the four-horse chariot race at the Pythian games in 582/81 was one way <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> displayed the excellence by which a ruler of his kind justified holding power.","questions":["The Dorian tribes of Sicyon were renamed Hyatai, Oneatai and Choireatai by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Herodotus says the names stayed in use for 60 years after his death.","Agariste, the daughter of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, married Megacles of the Alcmaeonid house at Athens, and the Spartans later deposed those descended from her father."],"answer":"Cleisthenes of Sicyon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – c. 569 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Orthagorids</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 620 – c. 510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cleisthenes of Sicyon</b> is an Orthagorid, of the dynasty that held Sicyon for about a century, its rule reckoned to run from around 620 or 610 to around 520 or 510 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His own reign is put at about 600 to about 569, though the ancient figures are approximate and the canonical hundred years of the tyranny cannot be taken exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He took part in the First Sacred War, traditionally dated about 590, and won the four-horse chariot race at the Pythian games in 582/81.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A victory of that kind was one of the ways a ruler showed the excellence that justified his rule, along with distinguished or divine descent, success in war, and the founding of a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His daughter Agariste married Megacles, of the Alcmaeonid house at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He gave the Dorian tribes of Sicyon new names, Hyatai, Oneatai and Choireatai, and Herodotus says those names stayed in use for 60 years after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Orthagorids were fiercely hostile to Argos, which is the setting for the renaming and for the rest of what Herodotus reports of the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rule of this kind was aristocratic in character rather than a break with aristocracy, on one account its culmination, and the men it pressed on were other aristocrats rather than the people at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tyrants were benefactors of their cities as well, strengthening their position by patronage and by networks among the elite, and a tyrant's rule was usually a limited one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dynasty ended when Sparta deposed those descended from Cleisthenes of Sicyon, and where to put that turns on Herodotus's 60 years and on the later chronographers, giving anything from about 520 down to about 505.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cleisthenes of Sicyon","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Victor Parker, \"The Dates of the Orthagorids of Sicyon,\" <i>Tyche</i> 7 (1992): 165–75, https://doi.org/10.15661/tyche.1992.007.20. [Open access]","Philip Davies, review of <i>The Heroic Rulers of Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Lynette Mitchell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.30/. [Open access]","Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]","Lynette Mitchell, review of <i>A Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. Kurt A. Raaflaub and Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.45/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-192","num":"","category":"","question":"Told to throw away his most valued possession to appease the gods, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cast a ring into the sea, and a fisherman brought it back to him inside a fish.","questions":["Penteconters, triremes and samainae made up the fleet of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and tradition credits him with inventing the last of those three, as Greek writers often credit famous men with famous inventions.","Taunted for never having added Samos to the king's dominions, the Persian governor at Sardis baited <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with an offer of wealth and had him killed at Magnesia."],"answer":"Polycrates of Samos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">522 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Magnesia</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Polycrates of Samos</b> is the tyrant whose navy was said to be one of the greatest in archaic Greece, and whose fleet Herodotus's <i>Histories</i> describe more fully than any other surviving source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those accounts conflict over how the fleet was used, how it was made up, and what part it played in his wider strategy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ships were penteconters, triremes and samainae, the last a type whose invention tradition ascribes to him, though Greek historiography habitually credits famous inventions to famous men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How he paid to keep so large a navy is a further question, and one answer canvassed is that he turned to pillaging and privateering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lavish dipteral temple of Hera on the island was commissioned under his rule, and Herodotus reckoned him more magnificent than Pisistratus on the ground that the greatest of Greek temples stood at Samos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Amasis, pharaoh of Egypt from 570 to 526 BCE, made an agreement with him that suited both, since the pharaoh sought connections to reinforce his own sovereignty and the tyrant needed manpower and support to hold his island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus tells how Amasis grew uneasy at his run of luck and urged him to throw away his most valued possession, a ring, which a fisherman then brought back inside a fish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He broke the agreement and sent the triremes, most likely built at Samos with Egyptian money, to Cambyses instead, and they arrived late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Without them the pharaoh's successor Psammetichus III could not check the Persians beyond his frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The end came through Oroetes, the Persian governor at Sardis, who had been taunted for never adding Samos to the king's dominions and who baited the tyrant with an offer of the wealth his projects needed; he sailed to Magnesia at the start of 522 despite his daughter's dream, and was killed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Polycrates of Samos","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Jakub Kuciak, \"The Fleet as the Basis for Polycrates of Samos' Thalassocracy,\" <i>Electrum</i> 27 (2020): 45–66, https://doi.org/10.4467/20800909el.20.003.12793. [Open access]","Olga Constanza Ojeda Sánchez, \"Polícrates onírico: el sueño en la obra de Heródoto,\" <i>Espacio, Tiempo y Forma. Serie II, Historia Antigua</i> 35 (2022): 41–54, https://doi.org/10.5944/etfii.35.2022.30839. [Open access]","Hannah L. Ringheim, \"Hera and the Sea. Decoding Dedications at the Samian Heraion,\" <i>Studia Hercynia</i> 23, no. 1 (2019): 11–25, https://studiahercynia.ff.cuni.cz/wp-content/uploads/sites/79/2020/02/Hannah_L_Ringheim_11-25.pdf. [Open access]","Anthony J. Papalas, review of <i>Trouble in the West: Egypt and the Persian Empire, 525–332 BCE</i>, by Stephen Ruzicka, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.03.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.03.21/. [Open access]","Joel Alden Schlosser, review of <i>Sophist Kings: Persians as Other in Herodotus</i>, by Vernon L. Provencal, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.50/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/The_crucifixion_of_Polycrates_the_tyrant_after_his_capture_by_the_Persians_MET_DP837539.jpg/1920px-The_crucifixion_of_Polycrates_the_tyrant_after_his_capture_by_the_Persians_MET_DP837539.jpg","title":"Polycrates of Samos","desc":"The crucifixion of Polycrates the tyrant after his capture by the Persians. Salvator Rosa. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_crucifixion_of_Polycrates_the_tyrant_after_his_capture_by_the_Persians_MET_DP837539.jpg","alt":"The crucifixion of Polycrates the tyrant after his capture by the Persians"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-193","num":"","category":"","question":"Herodotus records that the digging of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began at two portals at once, and names the engineer as Eupalinos, son of Naustrophus, of Megara.","questions":["About 270 metres into the mountain the straight line of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was abandoned, its digger swinging west in search of sounder rock before turning back towards the original course.","Water from a spring at Agiades ran through more than 4,000 pipes laid in a deep trench along the floor of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, delivering some 400 cubic metres a day."],"answer":"Tunnel of Eupalinos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 540 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,040 m</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bore</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.8 by 1.8 m</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Tunnel of Eupalinos</b> is the aqueduct bore cut through the Kastro mountain on Samos in the middle of the 6th century BCE, and Herodotus is the first to describe it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He names the engineer as Eupalinos, son of Naustrophus, of Megara, and records what makes the work remarkable, that the digging began at two portals at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It runs about 1,040 metres (3,400 feet), 612 from the north and 424 from the south, the two headings meeting beneath a summit rising 225 metres (738 feet) above the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cross-section is roughly 1.8 metres (5 ft 11 in) square, and the floor is almost perfectly level, the fall being carried instead by a deep trench along it holding the pipes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water came from a spring at Agiades north of the city, ran some 3 kilometres (1.9 miles) through more than 4,000 pipes, and delivered an estimated 400 cubic metres a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The work probably took more than a decade, and went wrong early, the rock in the north proving so weak that walls and roof collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For 165 metres (541 feet) the bore had to be lined with dry masonry walls and vaults, and about 270 metres (890 feet) in, Eupalinos abandoned the straight line and swung west in search of sounder rock before turning back towards it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One reading of the zigzag that follows holds that the straight line was the only figure he planned to use, that an equal-legged triangle was improvised for the detour, and that a later remeasurement corrected the errors it introduced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The route as a whole has been read as evidence that he understood the theory of errors and could grade rock by its mechanical strength, a rough forerunner of modern classification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Masons left graffiti along the way, and the letters HP on slabs used in it suggest the sanctuary of Hera controlled the quarries at Agiades that supplied them; Megara itself had a water system of tunnels and fountains, though tying its own aqueduct to Eupalinos on the masonry alone has been called rash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tunnel of Eupalinos","tags":["building","object","technology","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Åke Olson, \"How Eupalinos navigated his Way through the Mountain (An empirical approach to the geometry of Eupalinos' tunnel on Samos),\" <i>Anatolia Antiqua</i> 20 (2012): 25–34, https://doi.org/10.3406/anata.2012.1323. [Open access]","Stathis Stiros and Villy Kontogianni, \"Selection of the Path of the Eupalinos Aqueduct at Ancient Samos on the Basis of Geodetic and Geological / Geotechnical Criteria,\" <i>Bulletin of the Geological Society of Greece</i> 43, no. 2 (2010): 866–75, https://doi.org/10.12681/bgsg.11252. [Open access]","Georgios Angistalis, Georgios Dounias, Grigorios Tsokas, and Costas Zambas, \"The Walls of Eupalinos Aqueduct, Samos Island, Greece. Description, Pathology and Proposed Restoration Measures,\" <i>Bulletin of the Geological Society of Greece</i> 53, no. 1 (2018): 193–228, https://doi.org/10.12681/bgsg.18710. [Open access]","Margarit Damyanov, review of <i>Mégarika: nouvelles recherches sur Mégare, les cités de la Propontide et du Pont-Euxin</i>, ed. Adrian Robu and Iulian Bîrzescu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.32/. [Open access]","Eleni Tziligkaki and Michael Stamatakis, \"Underground quarries in the area of Agiades, Samos Island, Greece: Notes on historical topography and chronology,\" <i>Bulletin of the Geological Society of Greece</i> 53, no. 1 (2018): 161–92, https://doi.org/10.12681/bgsg.18835. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Eupalinian_aqueduct.JPG","title":"Tunnel of Eupalinos","desc":"Inside the Tunnel of Eupalinos (Eupalinian aqueduct) in Samos, Greece. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eupalinian_aqueduct.JPG","alt":"Eupalinian aqueduct"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-194","num":"","category":"","question":"Aristotle describes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a hereditary king who turned himself into a tyrant, which makes him the earliest example of archaic tyranny anyone can name.","questions":["Weights, measures, coinage and the iron spits called obeliskoi are all credited by ancient writers to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the traditions about him are late and hard to reconcile.","A late tale of the Corinthian Actaeon's death ties <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Corinth and to the founding of Syracuse, doubling as part of a foundation myth for the Corinthian state."],"answer":"Pheidon of Argos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the tradition disagrees</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pheidon of Argos</b> is the ruler whom Aristotle describes as a hereditary king who made himself a tyrant, which makes his the earliest example of archaic tyranny that can be named.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the reckoning of many scholars that would put him near in time to the poets Archilochus and Semonides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of the surviving detail about him goes back to the fourth-century historian Ephorus rather than to anything contemporary, which is the root of the difficulty he presents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He and Pittacus of Mytilene are war-leaders rather than demagogues, and the rise of Cypselus at Corinth has been connected with the pressure Argos was putting on that city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A late tale in which the Corinthian Actaeon dies ties him to Corinth again, and through it to the founding of Syracuse, so that the story doubles as part of a foundation myth for the Corinthian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What he is chiefly remembered for is a set of standards: weights, measures, coinage and the iron spits called obeliskoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pheidonian measures were in use abroad early, but the tradition tying him to the silver coins of Aegina has been challenged, one argument being that those coins were struck to a weight standard already bearing his name and that the link to the coinage itself came later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is also given a hand in the running of Olympia, where Elis and its neighbour Pisa contested control of the sanctuary, and an Olympic celebration of his is bound up with what is called the Pisan anolympiad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His date is the hardest question about him, since Herodotus puts him relatively late while other accounts synchronise him with Lycurgus of Sparta, or with a break at Olympia and the end of the Corinthian monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the phalanx wants a first inventor, he has been proposed for that too, on the argument that hoplite arms were made for aristocratic duelling and only afterwards suited a formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pheidon of Argos","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Kõiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08/. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]","Ben Brown, review of <i>Elis: Internal Politics and External Policy in Ancient Greece</i>, by Graeme Bourke, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.02.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.02.36/. [Open access]","Adam Schwartz, review of <i>The Spear, the Scroll, and the Pebble: How the Greek City-State Developed as a Male Warrior-Citizen Collective</i>, by Richard A. Billows, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.15/. [Open access]","B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-195","num":"","category":"","question":"The genealogies of Phoroneus, Danaos and Perseus all lean towards <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the 6th century BCE, and the handling of its Heraclid founders goes back further still.","questions":["Asine was destroyed and its people moved to Korone in Messenia, perhaps helped by Sparta, an enemy of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a likely ally of the Asineans.","Three phylai are reckoned for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a fourth mentioned only from about 460 BCE, and its votive-rich sanctuary on the Aspis hill belongs mostly to the 7th century."],"answer":"Archaic Argos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rises</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phylai</span><span class=\"dt-v\">three, a fourth from c. 460 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Archaic Argos</b> is the city of the Argive plain in the centuries after the Bronze Age, and the rise of its power in the north-east Argolid is dated to the second half of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cult of Apollo Pythaieus spread through the Peloponnese around then, and a league of Argos, Sparta and Epidaurus may have been founded on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Argive Heraion, the great sanctuary of the plain, is taken with that evidence to show a city already holding some hegemony in its own region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genealogies of Phoroneus, Argos, Melampous, Proitos, Danaos and Perseus all show marked pro-Argive leanings by the 6th century, and some by the 7th or late 8th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tradition of the Heraclid founders of the city was handled the same way, and that handling can be traced back to the second half of the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its neighbours felt the change: Asine was destroyed and its people moved to Korone in Messenia, perhaps helped by Sparta, an Argive enemy and a likely ally of the Asineans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Argos and Sparta then fought border wars for centuries, in the Thyreatis or in the Argolid, and most of those conflicts fall in the archaic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The stories told about them were not fixed: the traditions of Hysiae, the Battle of the Champions and Sepeia were still being shaped in the 5th century by whatever course Argive foreign policy was taking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three phylai are reckoned for the city in this period, with a fourth mentioned only from about 460.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the Aspis hill the votives run from the Late Geometric period to the Early Hellenistic and most belong to the 7th and early 6th centuries, confirming a sanctuary there, while a 6th-century building inscription from the city has been read as evidence that such work was made deliberately anonymous, so that no family could claim the credit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Archaic Argos","tags":["city","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Kõiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08/. [Open access]","Anna Philippa-Touchais and Alexandra Alexandridou, \"A New Heraion (?) on the Aspis hilltop at Argos,\" in <i>Argos Through the Argive Lens</i>, ed. Clémence Weber-Pallez and Evan Vance (Athens: École française d'Athènes, 2025), 47–64, https://doi.org/10.4000/16637. [Open access]","Evan Vance, \"Archaic Construction and the Public Sphere: The Contribution of the Epigraphic Sources from Aegina and Argos,\" in <i>Argos Through the Argive Lens</i>, ed. Clémence Weber-Pallez and Evan Vance (Athens: École française d'Athènes, 2025), 65–88, https://doi.org/10.4000/1663c. [Open access]","Elena Franchi, \"Memory of the Past in Classical Argos: The Archaic Border Wars with Sparta and Argos' 5th-c. Foreign Policy,\" in <i>Argos Through the Argive Lens</i>, ed. Clémence Weber-Pallez and Evan Vance (Athens: École française d'Athènes, 2025), 163–90, https://doi.org/10.4000/16638. [Open access]","James Kierstead, review of <i>Die griechischen Phylen: Funktion – Entstehung – Leistungen</i>, by Oliver Grote, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.09.47, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.09.47/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/Argos_-_Coronelli_Vincenzo_-_1688.jpg","title":"Archaic Argos","desc":"Vincenzo Coronelli - Repubblica di Venezia p. IV. Citta, Fortezze, ed altri Luoghi principali dell' Albania, Epiro e Livadia, e particolarmente i posseduti da Veneti descritti e delineati dal p. Coronelli, Venice, 1688. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Argos_-_Coronelli_Vincenzo_-_1688.jpg","alt":"Argos - Coronelli Vincenzo - 1688"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Argos","at":[22.7167,37.6167]}},
{"id":"gr-196","num":"","category":"","question":"One reading of the archaic history of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has Argive domination, then Epidaurian rule under the tyrant Procles, then an oligarchy of its own from about 618 BCE.","questions":["The weights, measures and coins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named a standard used far beyond it, and the story that Solon replaced that system at Athens is anachronistic on the archaeology.","The quarrel with Athens was almost wholly naval, and Corinth is said to have sold the Athenians twenty ships to use against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the years before Salamis."],"answer":"Aegina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 618 – 613 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple of Aphaia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late archaic, 13 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Aegina</b> is the island polis of the Saronic Gulf, whose harbour mound at Kolonna was occupied from the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Aegina Treasure, a hoard of gold jewellery, probably came from one or more Middle Helladic tombs there, and its pieces were made in different parts of the eastern Mediterranean at somewhat different dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Excavation in the eastern suburb of Bronze Age Kolonna has uncovered the destruction deposit of two early Mycenaean buildings of the 16th century BCE, the older of them read as a purple-dye works from its pigment-bearing sherds and its heaps of mollusc shells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island's archaic political history has been set out as a sequence of dominations: Argive, then Epidaurian under the tyrant Procles, and then an oligarchy of its own from about 618 or 613.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dating independence that late puts the first war with Athens, the origin of the ancient enmity Herodotus reports between them, correspondingly late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That quarrel was almost wholly naval, and Corinth is said to have sold the Athenians twenty ships to use against the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its weights, measures and coins gave their name to a standard used far beyond it, and the tradition that Solon replaced that system at Athens is anachronistic on the archaeological evidence, though it makes sense as a symbol of Athens setting itself apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sanctuary of Aphaia is the great building of the island, and the cost of its late archaic temple has been reckoned at 13 talents, or perhaps 6.3 without the sculpture in its pediments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An inscription commemorating that temple names a priest where a city would name its eponymous magistrate, and presents the finished monument without giving anyone the credit, which has been read as deliberate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the Persians burnt the older temple on the site, and whether the sculptures of its successor honour Aeginetan prowess at Salamis in 480, have been argued over for years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aegina","tags":["place","city","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["William Loomis, review of <i>Excursions in Epichoric History: Aiginetan Essays</i>, by Thomas J. Figueira, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.01.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.01.01/. [Open access]","Evan Vance, \"Archaic Construction and the Public Sphere: The Contribution of the Epigraphic Sources from Aegina and Argos,\" in <i>Argos Through the Argive Lens</i>, ed. Clémence Weber-Pallez and Evan Vance (Athens: École française d'Athènes, 2025), 65–88, https://doi.org/10.4000/1663c. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>The Aigina Treasure: Aegean Bronze Age Jewellery and a Mystery Revisited</i>, ed. J. Lesley Fitton, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.02.64, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.02.64/. [Open access]","Lydia Berger, Gerhard Forstenpointner, Peter Frühauf, and Fabian Kanz, \"More than just a color: Archaeological, analytical, and procedural aspects of Late Bronze Age purple-dye production at Cape Kolonna, Aegina,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 6 (2024): e0304340, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0304340. [Open access]","Hansgeorg Bankel and Andrew Stewart, \"New observations on the pediments of the early Classical temple of Aphaia on Aegina,\" <i>Journal of Greek Archaeology</i> 7 (2022): 173–212, https://doi.org/10.32028/jga.v7i.1714. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Temple_of_Apollo_Aegina_Greece.jpg/1920px-Temple_of_Apollo_Aegina_Greece.jpg","title":"Aegina","desc":"Temple of Apollo in Ægina, Greece. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Apollo_Aegina_Greece.jpg","alt":"Temple of Apollo Aegina Greece"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Aegina","at":[23.49,37.73]}},
{"id":"gr-197","num":"","category":"","question":"The names of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are read as the city of the bronzeworkers and the city of the rowers, and the two of them colonised together rather than separately.","questions":["Both Pithekoussai and Cumae are accepted as joint foundations of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while Corcyra's Eretrian origin seems sure and Rhegion was the last of the western settlements.","A treaty between the two cities of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is reported to have regulated which weapons might be used, and one of them was governed by aristocrats called Hippobotai."],"answer":"Chalcis and Eretria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chalcis founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Joint colonies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Strait bridged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 425 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chalcis and Eretria</b> are the two cities of the Lelantine plain on Euboea, and their names are read as the city of the bronzeworkers and the city of the rowers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chalcis was established in the 11th century BCE in a very favourable place, on the strait that has been bridged since the late 5th century and makes Euboea one of only two Greek islands a traveller can reach on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is nevertheless not a well known city, and Eretria is much the better known of the two, though its excavations have run for decades without being fully published.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eretria is one of the main archaeological sites of the Greek Early Iron Age, and its heroon at the West Gate, its House of the Mosaics and its sanctuary of Apollo Daphnephoros are standard illustrations in the handbooks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earlier phases of that sanctuary were a long-awaited publication of their own, and the ground beneath the city goes back to the Late Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The two colonised together rather than separately, in Chalcidice, in the Levant around Al Mina, and in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both are accepted as founders of Pithekoussai and of Cumae, and the Eretrian origin of Corcyra seems sure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Naxos, Zankle and Rhegion complete the western list, and Rhegion, the last of them, belongs to the closing years of the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chalcis was governed by aristocrats called Hippobotai, horse-feeders, whose horses carried noble warriors to the field rather than serving as a weapon, and a treaty between the two cities is reported to have regulated which weapons might be used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tie between Eretria and the sea is the constant in it: a votive inscription found near its theatre records a body called the Aeinautai, the ever-sailors, and carries the earliest trace of the cult of Hermes in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chalcis and Eretria","tags":["city","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Adolfo Domínguez, review of <i>Untersuchungen zum Lelantischen Krieg und verwandten Problemen der frühgriechischen Geschichte</i>, by Victor Parker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.06.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.06.12/. [Open access]","Olivier Mariaud, review of <i>Eretria: A Guide to the Ancient City</i>, by Pierre Ducrey, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.05.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.05.24/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Le sanctuaire d'Apollon Daphnéphoros à l'époque géométrique</i>, by Samuel Verdan, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.11.59, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.11.59/. [Open access]","David Michael Smith, review of <i>An Island between Two Worlds: The Archaeology of Euboea from Prehistoric to Byzantine Times</i>, ed. Žarko Tankosić, Fanis Mavridis and Maria Kosma, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.42/. [Open access]","Barbara Leone, \"Dedication by the <i>Aeinautae</i> in Eretria,\" <i>Axon</i> 1, no. 1 (2017), https://doi.org/10.14277/2532-6848/Axon-1-1-17-9. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-198","num":"","category":"","question":"Three brief ancient notices of the fighting survive, and not one of them names the plain that gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the modern title scholars know it by.","questions":["Samos, Corinth and Thessaly are named on the Chalcidian side of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with Miletus and Megara on the Eretrian, which is what makes it look panhellenic.","Thucydides took the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the most notable quarrel among the Greeks before his own, and said the rest of Greece took sides in it."],"answer":"Lelantine War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lefkandi burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chalcis taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">506 BCE, by Athens</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lelantine War</b> is the conflict between Chalcis and Eretria over the plain that lies between them on Euboea, and its title is a modern one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three brief notices of hostilities survive, in Herodotus, Thucydides and Aristotle, and not one of them names that plain as the thing being fought over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fighting on Euboea is mentioned four times in poetry, by Hesiod, Archilochus and Theognis and in an anonymous elegiac fragment, while the fuller accounts come from Strabo and Plutarch drawing on older writers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides took the quarrel for the most notable among the Greeks before his own war, and his remark that the rest of Greece took sides in it makes it a panhellenic tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the later writers preserve is strange enough: that the legendary Curetes fought endlessly for the plain, and that the Chalcidians took up the custom of pederasty after watching a lover die gloriously against the Eretrians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The archaeological support usually offered for a real war is the burning of Lefkandi-Xeropolis at about 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Allies are named on both sides, Samos, Corinth and Thessaly with Chalcis and Miletus and Megara with Eretria, and the Thessalian part is put down to ties between the Hippobotai, the horse-feeding aristocrats of Chalcis, and the Thessalian nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hoplite tactics were already known to Archilochus, whose best-dated poems fall between 655 and 645, which places the fighting he mentions near the middle of the 7th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Euboean settlers led the colonising of the north until the war ended their power there, after which Chalcis worked with the Andrians, hostile to Eretria, to plant colonies where its own were threatened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the war actually was remains open: one account makes both cities outgrown offshoots of Lefkandi that came to blows over a fertile plain, another turns the fighting into ritual combat between young aristocrats until Athens took Chalcis in 506, and it has become the standard case of an archaic subject whose sources will not carry a narrative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lelantine War","tags":["event","history","greece","archaic","warfare"],"sources":["Adolfo Domínguez, review of <i>Untersuchungen zum Lelantischen Krieg und verwandten Problemen der frühgriechischen Geschichte</i>, by Victor Parker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.06.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.06.12/. [Open access]","Natasha Bershadsky, \"Impossible Memories of the Lelantine War,\" in <i>Dossier: Place aux objets!</i> (Paris: Éditions de l'École des hautes études en sciences sociales, 2018), 191–213, https://doi.org/10.4000/books.editionsehess.5679. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200–479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>Αρχαϊκή Ελλάδα και πόλεμος</i>, by Katerina S. Meidani, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.10.48, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.10.48/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Lelantine_War.png/1920px-Lelantine_War.png","title":"Lelantine War","desc":"Jonathan M. Hall, A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200–479 BCE, 2nd Edition, Chichester, Wiley Blackwell, 2014, pp. 2, 3. ISBN: 9781118301272. T8612, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lelantine_War.png","alt":"Lelantine War"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-199","num":"","category":"","question":"Workshops making the pottery of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have been identified in the Hellespont, around the Black Sea and in the Nile Delta, which are the very regions it was settling.","questions":["The oracle of Apollo at Didyma, joined by a Sacred Way, was the great extra-urban sanctuary of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its temple ranked with those of Ephesus and Samos.","The name sits close to Milatos on Crete, and the Hittite records that call the Greeks Ahhiyawa may name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as Millawanda."],"answer":"Archaic Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pottery abroad</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionian Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 – 494 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Archaic Miletus</b> is the Greek city on the Anatolian coast, standing on the cusp between the Greek world and that of the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its foundation stories are many, its name sits close to Milatos on Crete, and the Hittite records that call the Greeks Ahhiyawa may name the place Millawanda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its heyday came before the Classical age, which is part of why so little survives about it, the Greeks not yet writing on any large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sanctuary that mattered most to the city stood outside it, the oracle of Apollo at Didyma, joined to it by a Sacred Way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That temple ranked with the temples of Artemis at Ephesus and Hera on Samos among the largest and most spectacular cult buildings of the archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The city's own pottery has been placed by decades of excavation, and Miletus holds a central position in the development of its painting style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the middle of the 7th century BCE that pottery was wanted from the Black Sea to Egypt, and workshops making it have been identified in the Hellespont, around the Black Sea and in the Nile Delta, which are the very regions the city was settling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One argument sets it in a colonising alliance with Megara, the two sharing the oligarchic office of the aisymnetai and an elite kinship claimed to reach back to the Lelantine War, in which both had sided with Eretria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Apollo Didymeos had his part in those ventures, while within the walls the sanctuary of Aphrodite on the Zeytintepe has supplied much of the material by which archaic Milesian pottery is dated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its archaic career closes with the Ionian Revolt, where Herodotus has Aristagoras call his rule over the city a kingship, and where the help Athens sent the rebels became the beginning of troubles for Greeks and barbarians alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Archaic Miletus","tags":["city","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["P. J. Smith, review of <i>Miletos, the Ornament of Ionia: A History of the City to 400 B.C.E.</i>, by Vanessa B. Gorman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.06.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.06.17/. [Open access]","Thomas G. Schattner, review of <i>La céramique milésienne et apparentée à l'époque archaïque</i>, ed. Anne Coulié, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.04.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.04.21/. [Open access]","Udo Schlotzhauer, review of <i>Der archaische Heiligtumsbefund vom Taxiarchis-Hügel in Didyma</i>, by Helga Bumke, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.04/. [Open access]","Margarit Damyanov, review of <i>Mégarika: nouvelles recherches sur Mégare, les cités de la Propontide et du Pont-Euxin</i>, ed. Adrian Robu and Iulian Bîrzescu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.32/. [Open access]","David J. DeVore, review of <i>Reading Herodotus: A Study of the Logoi in Book 5 of Herodotus' Histories</i>, by Elizabeth Irwin and Emily Greenwood, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.08.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.08.05/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Miletus%2C_Illustration_for_La_Terre-Sainte_et_les_lieux_illustr%C3%A9s_par_les_ap%C3%B4tres%2C_by_Adrien_Egron%2C_1837_%2839%29.jpg","title":"Archaic Miletus","desc":"Miletus, Illustration for La Terre-Sainte et les lieux illustrés par les apôtres, by Adrien Egron, 1837 (39). published on 1837. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miletus,_Illustration_for_La_Terre-Sainte_et_les_lieux_illustr%C3%A9s_par_les_ap%C3%B4tres,_by_Adrien_Egron,_1837_(39).jpg","alt":"Miletus, Illustration for La Terre-Sainte et les lieux illustrés par les apôtres, by Adrien Egron, 1837 (39)"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Miletus","at":[27.2783,37.5303]}},
{"id":"gr-200","num":"","category":"","question":"Running between the Hermos and the Maiandros rivers and taking in Samos and Chios, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had Lydians and Karians for neighbours as well as Dorians and Aeolians.","questions":["Herodotus counted twelve cities in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the dodekapolis, though later writers use the name loosely enough to mean the Greeks of Asia Minor at large.","Serving as mercenaries in Egypt and the Near East and trading into the Black Sea left the identity of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> soft and open to its neighbours."],"answer":"Ionia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12, the dodekapolis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian Wars end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ionia</b> is the stretch of the western seaboard of Asia Minor held by the Ionian Greeks, running between the Hermos and the Maiandros rivers and taking in the islands of Samos and Chios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus counted twelve cities there, the dodekapolis, and the name stayed slippery afterwards, meaning in some later writers the cities of the Ionian League and in others the Greeks of Asia Minor at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Miletus, the greatest of them, stood between the Greek world and that of the Near East, and its heyday fell before the Classical age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The region's pottery was made chiefly in its south, where decades of excavation have placed the workshops, while Chios and the northern cities went their own ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its neighbours were not only Dorians and Aeolians but Lydians and Karians, and its people served as mercenaries in Egypt and the Near East while trading into the Mediterranean and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ionian identity was accordingly soft and permeable, open to the cultures around it rather than fenced off from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persian invasion is the point past which that could not return, and where a Greek identity built on a sense of separateness begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The region's foundation stories and traditions were themselves inventions of some date, and fixing that date means reading the literary sources against what the ground holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ionia has usually been valued for what it wrote, philosophy and poetry and history, much of it later than the archaic period, though those writers were a tiny minority of the people who lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beginning instead with the landscape, then the archaeology, and only then the texts, gives a region shaped by Anatolian influences as much as by Greek ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ionia","tags":["place","region","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Lee E. Patterson, review of <i>The Land of Ionia: Society and Economy in the Archaic Period</i>, by Alan M. Greaves, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.04.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.04.17/. [Open access]","Omar Coloru, review of <i>Ethnic Constructs in Antiquity: The Role of Power and Tradition</i>, ed. Ton Derks and Nico Roymans, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.07.71, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.07.71/. [Open access]","Naoíse Mac Sweeney, review of <i>Accustomed to Obedience? Classical Ionia and the Aegean World, 480–294 BCE</i>, by Joshua P. Nudell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.46/. [Open access]","Thomas G. Schattner, review of <i>La céramique milésienne et apparentée à l'époque archaïque</i>, ed. Anne Coulié, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.04.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.04.21/. [Open access]","P. J. Smith, review of <i>Miletos, the Ornament of Ionia: A History of the City to 400 B.C.E.</i>, by Vanessa B. Gorman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.06.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.06.17/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg/1920px-Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg","title":"Ionia","desc":"Mount Mycale as seen from the island of Samos, across the Mycale Strait,. Athens2004, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg","alt":"Mount Mycale and Mycale Strait"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Ionia","at":[27.5,38.2],"kind":"region","area":[[26.65,38.75],[27.45,38.72],[27.85,38.45],[27.9,38.05],[27.75,37.7],[27.35,37.45],[26.85,37.55],[26.3,37.95],[25.9,38.55],[26.15,38.8]]}},
{"id":"gr-201","num":"","category":"","question":"Poseidon Helikonios is the god of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and cities across Ionia and its Black Sea colonies kept shrines of their own to him.","questions":["A site on Otomatik Tepe was long taken for the archaic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a newer identification places it in the Mycale beside the Carian town of Melia.","The festival called the Panionia was held either at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> itself or in one of the member states, and repeated attendance is what kept their bonds alive."],"answer":"Panionion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionian League</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Member states</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12, later 13</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Panionion</b> is the common sanctuary of the Ionian Greeks, the place of the festival called the Panionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its god is Poseidon Helikonios, one of three divinities counted as distinctively Ionian alongside Dionysos Phleus and Apollo Delphinios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Individual Ionian cities kept shrines of their own to that god, and so did the Ionian colonies on the shores of the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The festival was held either at the sanctuary itself or in one of the member states, and taking part in it again and again is what kept the bonds between them alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It belongs to the class of supra-regional sanctuary, the kind that binds states together without being the property of any one of them, and the coastal life of the region may itself lie behind the choice of a sea god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Where the archaic sanctuary stood has been argued over, since a site on Otomatik Tepe was long taken for it and that identification proved hard to maintain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A newer one places the archaic Panionion, together with the Carian town of Melia, in the Mycale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If it holds, the dates of the finds there would put the founding of the Ionian League at about 600 BCE, and the League itself may have been formed in answer to conflict between Ionian Greeks and Carians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How many cities belonged is not fixed either, Herodotus counting twelve while the later association has thirteen member states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Poseidon serves elsewhere as the god of leagues asserting a past that is not Dorian, and what this sanctuary held together was an identity grounded less in the geography of Ionia than in things done in common, and open to its neighbours until the Persian invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Panionion","tags":["building","place","practice","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Denise Demetriou, review of <i>Landscape, Ethnicity and Identity in the Archaic Mediterranean Area</i>, ed. Gabriele Cifani and Simon Stoddart, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.11.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.11.34/. [Open access]","Floris van den Eijnde, review of <i>Kult-Politik-Ethnos: Überregionale Heiligtümer im Spannungsfeld von Kult und Politik</i>, ed. Klaus Freitag, Peter Funke and Matthias Haake, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.10.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.10.23/. [Open access]","Dies van der Linde, review of <i>Roman Ionia: Constructions of Cultural Identity in Western Asia Minor</i>, by Martin Hallmannsecker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.09.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.09.15/. [Open access]","Lee E. Patterson, review of <i>The Land of Ionia: Society and Economy in the Archaic Period</i>, by Alan M. Greaves, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.04.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.04.17/. [Open access]","Omar Coloru, review of <i>Ethnic Constructs in Antiquity: The Role of Power and Tradition</i>, ed. Ton Derks and Nico Roymans, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.07.71, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.07.71/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Panionium_theatre.jpg/1920px-Panionium_theatre.jpg","title":"Panionion","desc":"Panionium theatre near Güzelçamlı, on the north slope of Mt. Mycale, on the top of a low hill called Otomatik Tepe. Isabeau, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panionium_theatre.jpg","alt":"Panionium theatre"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Panionion","at":[27.235,37.7127]}},
{"id":"gr-202","num":"","category":"","question":"Counted among the seven wonders in Hellenistic times, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands over an archaic predecessor whose remains were uncovered beneath the 4th-century building.","questions":["The mysteries for which the goddess became famous are unattested at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the 4th century BCE, appearing first amid the rivalry of Alexander's successors.","Ranking with the temple of Hera on Samos and the temple of Apollo at Didyma, the archaic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was one of the great cult buildings of its age."],"answer":"Artemision at Ephesus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest pottery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 775 – 750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rediscovered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1870 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Artemision at Ephesus</b> is the sanctuary of Artemis outside that city, counted in Hellenistic times among the seven wonders of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath its 4th-century temple lies an archaic predecessor, and the early growth of the sanctuary is bound up with a natural site that changed shape over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That archaic building ranked with the temple of Hera on Samos and the temple of Apollo at Didyma among the largest and most spectacular cult buildings of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city it served lay on the Kaystros, and its founder hero was Androklos, as Miletus had Neileus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mysteries for which the goddess later became famous are unattested before the 4th century BCE, appearing first amid the competition of Alexander's successors for the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The temple was lost for centuries and found again only in 1870, after a seven-year search, and what survived of the 4th-century building and of its archaic predecessor was uncovered then and in a second campaign a century later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The community that raised it is far less well understood than the sanctuary itself, and the reason is the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Ephesus, as along that whole coast, the earlier levels lie under substantial Hellenistic and Roman ruins and can be reached only in keyholes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so, 74 pieces of Geometric and Archaic pottery from one such excavation, set beside notes made in the 1920s, are enough to show that the northern slope of Panayır Dağ was a settlement quarter before the Hellenistic period, while the earliest pottery from another deposit on the site belongs to the second quarter of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is archaeological rather than merely literary evidence for where the people who built the Artemision lived, though the Greek and Greek-style pottery there cannot be weighed properly against the non-Greek styles of the same date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Artemision at Ephesus","tags":["building","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Frederick G. Naerebout, review of <i>Die Archäologie der ephesischen Artemis: Gestalt und Ritual eines Heiligtums</i>, ed. Ulrike Muss, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.05.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.05.31/. [Open access]","Alan M. Greaves, review of <i>Archäologische Forschungen zur Siedlungsgeschichte von Ephesos in geometrischer, archaischer und klassischer Zeit</i>, by Martin Steskal, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.17/. [Open access]","Udo Schlotzhauer, review of <i>Der archaische Heiligtumsbefund vom Taxiarchis-Hügel in Didyma</i>, by Helga Bumke, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.04/. [Open access]","Dies van der Linde, review of <i>Roman Ionia: Constructions of Cultural Identity in Western Asia Minor</i>, by Martin Hallmannsecker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.09.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.09.15/. [Open access]","Heidi Wendt, review of <i>The Mysteries of Artemis of Ephesos: Cult, Polis, and Change in the Graeco-Roman World</i>, by Guy MacLean Rogers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.05/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Philips_Galle%2C_Dianatemplet_i_Efesos%2C_1572%2C_KKSgb10001-5%2C_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Philips_Galle%2C_Dianatemplet_i_Efesos%2C_1572%2C_KKSgb10001-5%2C_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Artemision at Ephesus","desc":"Verdens otte vidundere. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philips_Galle,_Dianatemplet_i_Efesos,_1572,_KKSgb10001-5,_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst_(cropped).jpg","alt":"Philips Galle, Dianatemplet i Efesos, 1572, KKSgb10001-5, Statens Museum for Kunst"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Artemision at Ephesus","at":[27.3639,37.9497]}},
{"id":"gr-203","num":"","category":"","question":"Reaching from southern Italy and Sicily to the Propontis and the Black Sea, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> planted settlements overseas from the 8th century BCE onward.","questions":["The western and eastern halves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have usually been studied by different specialists, so comparing the two is a recent undertaking.","The regions Miletus was settling during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are where its pottery workshops turn up: the Hellespont, the shores of the Black Sea and the Nile Delta."],"answer":"Greek colonisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlements</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 6th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Corcyra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">733 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Greek colonisation</b> is the name given to the settlements Greeks planted overseas from the 8th century BCE onward, reaching from southern Italy and Sicily to the Propontis and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the north Aegean the Euboeans led, planting the Chalcidice, until the Lelantine War put an end to their power there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chalcis afterwards worked with the Andrians, who were hostile to Eretria, to plant colonies where its own were threatened, and those Andrian foundations belong to the middle of the 7th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Megarians took the Propontis and the shores of the Black Sea, in what one argument calls a colonising alliance with Miletus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Milesian pottery workshops turn up in the Hellespont, around the Black Sea and in the Nile Delta, which are the very regions that city was settling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Poteidaea was founded by Corinthians at a time when the Euboeans were no longer a great power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient sources give Corcyra an early Eretrian settlement, which many reject for want of archaeological evidence to match it, and the next stage there is the Corinthian foundation of 733 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The western and eastern halves of the phenomenon have usually been worked on by different specialists, so that comparing them is recent, and it raises the question whether the movements of the 8th century were a break with what went before or one more stretch of a migration that never stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pull-factors have been sought in Magna Graecia and Sicily, mobility and the settling-down that follows are treated as two poles of one analysis, and a line is drawn between colonies founded from the homeland and those founded from other colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The traditional picture has been questioned over the last two decades, particularly what organisation these ventures had, if any, and how the settlers stood towards the people already there, and a survey of the whole field is now reckoned beyond any one person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek colonisation","tags":["event","practice","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume 1</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.38/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Conceptualising Early Colonisation</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.43/. [Open access]","Dominik Delp, review of <i>Comparing Greek Colonies: Mobility and Settlement Consolidation from Southern Italy to the Black Sea (8th–6th Century BC)</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.06.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.06.38/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume 2</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]","Margarit Damyanov, review of <i>Mégarika: nouvelles recherches sur Mégare, les cités de la Propontide et du Pont-Euxin</i>, ed. Adrian Robu and Iulian Bîrzescu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.32/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Ancient_colonies.PNG/1920px-Ancient_colonies.PNG","title":"Greek colonisation","desc":"Map of the colonies of Ancient Greece and Phoenicia — circa 550 BCE. Also other regional 'dynasties' of the Mediterranean region. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_colonies.PNG","alt":"Ancient colonies"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-204","num":204,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Where the line falls between an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and an emporion is much argued over, since Herodotus applies the second word to Naukratis, Oinoussai and places on the Black Sea.","questions":["Olbia was laid out from the first with cult buildings among its houses and grew from an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a state that had absorbed neighbouring Berezan by the end of the Archaic period.","Settlers in an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed citizens of their mother city by origin while building identities of their own, holding on to only part of what they had brought."],"answer":"apoikia","answerText":"apoikia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"An <b>apoikia</b> is a settlement the Greeks planted away from the community that sent it out, read as an organised emigration whose emphasis falls on departure from the oikos, the household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first settlers were apoikoi, and those despatched later to strengthen a foundation already standing were epoikoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those who went remained citizens of their metropolis by origin, and in the new place built identities and memories of their own, holding on to only part of what they had brought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As the Greek population grew such a settlement could become a full polis, and Olbia on the Black Sea, laid out from the first with cult buildings among its houses, turned into a state that had absorbed neighbouring Berezan by the end of the Archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A foundation need not answer to a single mother city: Locri Epizephyrii in southern Italy may have been a joint venture of East and West Locris, or of that people as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The word stands beside emporion, which Herodotus applies to Oinoussai, Naukratis and several places around the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the line between the two falls is much argued over, and one study makes the emporion a proto-settlement rather than the trading station usually understood, leaving the apoikia a regular Greek city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The vocabulary shifted in the 4th century BCE, when nearly all Athenian foundations became cleruchies and their settlers cleruchs, so that Plutarch later used the newer words of far older ventures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether any of it should be called colonisation is disputed, and one position rejects the word for these early ventures, traces the modern habit to notions of cultural superiority, and prefers the neutral migration, since colonisation implies missions organised by a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other defends organised colonisation as a continuum running from private enterprises to politically organised communities already in the 8th century BCE, while a recent study takes apoikismos, expatriation, through migration theory and sets aside explanations by overpopulation, shortage and trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Conceptualising Early Colonisation</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.43/. [Open access]","D. Erciyas, review of <i>Hellenic Colonization in Euxeinos Pontos: Penetration, Early Establishment and the Problem of the “Emporion” Revisited</i>, by Elias K. Petropoulos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.05.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.05.24/. [Open access]","Emma Buckingham, review of <i>Greek Colonization in Local Contexts: Case Studies in Colonial Interactions</i>, ed. Jason Lucas, Carrie Ann Murray and Sara Owen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.10.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.10.57/. [Open access]","Gregory Crane, review of <i>Athens and Aigina in the Age of Imperial Colonization</i>, by Thomas J. Figueira, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1993.01.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.01.09/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Zwischen Ansässigkeit und Mobilität: die sogenannte Grosse Kolonisation der Griechen aus migrationstheoretischer Perspektive</i>, by Dominick Delp, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.08.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.08.18/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-205","num":205,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Herodotus was told by the Sybarites that Dorieus, the Spartan <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, came to grief because he had failed to follow the instructions Delphi gave him.","questions":["The privileges Cyrene's colonists granted their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed to his descendants, and the tomb of Battus stood in the agora, where no ordinary Greek was buried.","Homer's Skherie is laid out by an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who divides the farmland and builds a monumental agora with fixed stone seating, a temple beside it, city walls and a water supply."],"answer":"oikist","answerText":"oikist","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"An <b>oikist</b> is the leader of a Greek expedition sent out to found a settlement overseas, a part that fell to members of the elite whose motives lay in the antagonism of political life at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among his duties as founder was the introduction of religious and political institutions brought from the homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Homer's Skherie is laid out by such a man, who divides the farmland and raises a monumental agora with fixed stone seating, a temple beside it, city walls and a water supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was invested with sacral authority by Delphi, and it was probably he who marked out the temenoi, the precincts of the gods, in the new place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sybarites told Herodotus that the Spartan Dorieus came to grief because he had not followed the instructions Delphi gave him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Miltiades was designated oikist of the Thracian Chersonese by the Pythia rather than by himself, and the Dolonkoi and the colonists who went with him recognised him before he ever sailed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was then chosen tyrannos by his fellow citizens, and his prerogatives passed intact to Stesagoras and to the younger Miltiades after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A founder was honoured after death with a cult of his own and buried where no ordinary Greek was buried, in the middle of the city, so that the tomb of Battus stood in the agora at Cyrene while the royal privileges his colonists had conceded him went on to his descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A new settlement had no old monuments to hang a memory on, and a founder cult was one of the ways it made itself a past, and with that past a new social reality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Such a cult could keep alive what the man had actually been, since the honours paid to Miltiades recalled his autocratic rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Zwischen Ansässigkeit und Mobilität: die sogenannte Grosse Kolonisation der Griechen aus migrationstheoretischer Perspektive</i>, by Dominick Delp, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.08.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.08.18/. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","Corinne Pache, review of <i>Ancient Greek Hero Cult</i>, ed. Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.12.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.12.17/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-206","num":206,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The medicinal and culinary plant silphium became emblematic of the wealth and identity of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the largest and most prosperous Greek city in Libya.","questions":["Power at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay first with the Battiad monarchy, was restructured in the 6th century BCE by Demonax of Mantinea, and returned briefly to the dynasty with Persian support.","The beginnings of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew poets from Pindar to Callimachus, and Pindar's fourth Pythian, for king Arcesilas IV, ties them to the Argonauts' voyage to Lake Tritonis."],"answer":"Cyrene","answerText":"Cyrene","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Cyrene</b> is the Greek city planted inland in Libya in the 7th century BCE, and on both the written and archaeological evidence it was the largest and most prosperous Greek city there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The part played by the island of Thera in the original foundation is attested by many writers over a long period and may be taken as historically true, but Thera was only a starting point, and other parts of the Greek world shared in the foundation or in the expansion that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aegean connections show in the ground from the start, with an unusual quantity of Cretan material beside the commoner East Greek and Rhodian wares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stood at the head of a group of Greek towns in Cyrenaica, among them Tauchira, Barca, Euesperides and the coastal Apollonia, about which the sources say almost nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its economy was tied to the exploitation of silphium, the medicinal and culinary plant emblematic of the city's wealth and identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Power lay at first with the Battiad monarchy, was restructured in the 6th century BCE by Demonax of Mantinea, and returned briefly to the dynasty with Persian support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No other Greek foundation is related in such detail by the ancient writers, chiefly Herodotus, though their accounts begin only in the 5th century BCE and see the whole region from this one city's side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He gives two versions of the departure, one told on Thera and one by the Cyreneans, independent of each other, running the same events in another order, and the Delphic oracle runs through his telling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He seems to have gone to Libya himself, but his informants came from elite families for whom the fall of the Battiads was a recent memory, and what they told him leans against the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The foundation drew poets from Pindar to Callimachus and Apollonius, beside them stands the inscription known as the Oath of the Founders, and recent excavation has worked on structures in and around the sanctuary of Apollo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Christine Davidson, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World. Vol. IV: Cyrene to Metapontion</i>, ed. Paul Cartledge and Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.03.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.03.24/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume 2</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]","Evangelia Anagnostou-Laoutides, review of <i>Myth and History in Ancient Greece: The Symbolic Creation of a Colony</i>, by Claude Calame, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.11.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.11.34/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominguez, review of <i>Mythe et histoire dans l'Antiquité grecque: la création symbolique d'une colonie</i>, by Claude Calame, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.02.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.02.25/. [Open access]","Arthur Thourson Jones, review of <i>For the Preservation of the Cultural Heritage in Libya</i>, ed. Savino di Lernia and Davide Zampetti, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.09.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.09.27/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Archaeological_Site_of_Cyrene-109025.jpg","title":"Cyrene","desc":"Archaeological Site of Cyrene (Libya). Giovanni Boccardi, CC BY-SA 3.0 igo, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaeological_Site_of_Cyrene-109025.jpg","alt":"Archaeological Site of Cyrene"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-207","num":207,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Cyreneans acknowledged <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as founder and ruler, buried him in the agora, and let the privileges they had conceded him pass to his descendants.","questions":["Pindar's fourth Pythian gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an Argonaut ancestor named Euphemus and ties the founding of Cyrene to the voyage of the Argonauts to the Libyan lake Tritonis.","Callimachus, himself a Cyrenean, has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raise a temple of Apollo Carneius at Aziris, where Greeks first danced the Carneia with Libyan women."],"answer":"Battus","answerText":"Battus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded Cyrene</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Battus</b> is the Theran who led the settlers to Cyrene and was acknowledged by the Cyreneans as founder and ruler, holding both roles at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No other individual founder receives as much attention in the ancient literary sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus's account of the business reaches far back for its causes, and its focus moves from Sparta to Thera and then to Cyrene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The unflattering picture of him as a stuttering bastard comes from Cyrenean informants of Herodotus whose families remembered the overthrow of the dynasty, and whose telling leans against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His tomb stood in the agora, and the royal privileges the first colonists conceded him passed to his descendants, one of whom later used them to claim as much for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pindar's fourth Pythian, written for king Arcesilas IV, gives him an Argonaut ancestor named Euphemus and ties the founding to the voyage of the Argonauts to the Libyan lake Tritonis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A clod of earth handed over as a token of friendship is lost at sea and washed up on Thera, and comes home generations later when the Therans under his leadership settle in Libya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fifth Pythian sets the Theban Aegids and the Trojan Antenorids together at the site, so that the Theban and Trojan wars coincide and the distance between the fall of Troy and his own act closes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The hero cult he was given at Cyrene is presented as matching the cults established for the great heroes of the Mycenaean past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Making him a founding hero was also a way of making Arcesilas one, and Callimachus, himself a Cyrenean, has him raise a temple of Apollo Carneius at Aziris, where Greeks first danced the Carneia with Libyan women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]","Christine Davidson, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World. Vol. IV: Cyrene to Metapontion</i>, ed. Paul Cartledge and Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.03.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.03.24/. [Open access]","Evangelia Anagnostou-Laoutides, review of <i>Myth and History in Ancient Greece: The Symbolic Creation of a Colony</i>, by Claude Calame, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.11.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.11.34/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominguez, review of <i>Mythe et histoire dans l'Antiquité grecque: la création symbolique d'une colonie</i>, by Claude Calame, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.02.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.02.25/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume 2</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-208","num":208,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The expedition that planted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Sicily around 733 BCE was led out from Corinth by Archias, a Bacchiad, according to Thucydides.","questions":["The nymph Arethusa of the island of Ortygia is prominent on the coins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE, beside votive deposits going back to the 7th.","Because the Peloponnesian river Alpheos was held to feed the spring on Ortygia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could claim a tie to Olympia and the wider Greek world."],"answer":"Syracuse","answerText":"Syracuse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 733 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Syracuse</b> is the Greek city Corinth planted in Sicily around 733 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides names Archias, a Bacchiad, as the man who led the expedition out from Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch's account of him makes violence at home the cause of the departure, which sits beside Thucydides' picture of early settlement as violent towards the people already on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sicily lies at the geographical centre of the Mediterranean and carried a dense network of Greek, Punic and native Sikel, Sikan and Elymian settlements, so that the island was synonymous with conflict and competition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Megara Hyblaea was planted by Megarians shortly after Syracuse itself, and in turn founded Selinus, which brought the Greeks to the edge of Carthage's sphere of influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The island of Ortygia carries the spring Arethusa, whose nymph is prominent on the city's coins of the late 6th and early 5th centuries, and votive deposits there go back to the 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Artemis is associated with the island, and her cult is at once uniquely Syracusan and a link back to Corinth, from which it was imported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Peloponnesian river Alpheos, which runs past Olympia, was held to be the source of that spring, tying the city to the wider Greek world; the association is carried by the foundation oracle Pausanias cites and hinted at earlier in Ibycus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Gelon refounded the city in the 5th century BCE he mingled new peoples and groups into it, and the mixing of Alpheos with Arethusa served ancient writers as the figure for what he had done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides' account of the peopling and settlement of Sicily is written to look forward to the Athenian expedition, and his Syracuse comes to resemble Athens itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Parrish Elizabeth Wright, review of <i>Myth, Locality, and Identity in Pindar's Sicilian Odes</i>, by Virginia M. Lewis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.27/. [Open access]","Vivienne Gray, review of <i>A Commentary on Thucydides. Volume III: Books 5.25-8.109</i>, by Simon Hornblower, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.01.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.01.24/. [Open access]","Mark Edwards, review of <i>The Poetics of Colonization: From City to Text in Archaic Greece</i>, by Carol Dougherty, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.05.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.05.04/. [Open access]","Tim Sorg, review of <i>The Fight for Greek Sicily: Society, Politics, and Landscape</i>, ed. Melanie Jonasch, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.03.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.03.51/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/The_Greek_theatre_of_Syracuse%2C_Sicily.jpg/1920px-The_Greek_theatre_of_Syracuse%2C_Sicily.jpg","title":"Syracuse","desc":"The Greek theatre of Syracuse lies on the south slopes of the Temenite hill, overlooking the modern city of Syracuse in southeastern Sicily. Nicolas Chadeville, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Greek_theatre_of_Syracuse,_Sicily.jpg","alt":"The Greek theatre of Syracuse, Sicily"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"gr-209","num":209,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The story of friendly help given the settlers by the native king Hyblon belongs to the founding of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, planted in eastern Sicily shortly after Syracuse.","questions":["Gelon destroyed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 483 or 482 BCE and absorbed its people, ending a quarrel with Syracuse that had begun as an alliance.","Eighth-century foundations keeping domestic and public ground apart show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a town plan from its earliest years, as does the early separation of its cemeteries."],"answer":"Megara Hyblaea","answerText":"Megara Hyblaea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 733 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">483 or 482 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Megara Hyblaea</b> is the Greek city planted in eastern Sicily by Megarians shortly after Syracuse, and it in turn sent out Selinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It arrived among the well-developed and widespread native Pantalica and Thapsos culture, and the story of friendly help given the settlers by the native king Hyblon belongs in that setting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had a town plan from its earliest years, shown by 8th-century foundations that keep domestic and public ground apart and by the early separation of the cemeteries from the settlement, the 61 hectares later enclosed by the 6th-century wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavation of the archaic town shows a planned and apparently egalitarian settlement in place around the last quarter of the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the 7th to the 6th century religious and public building filled in the space set aside earlier for public use, which may have been more than half the site if the agora area is anything to go by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> House sizes and styles begin to differ in the 7th century, which points to an early start for social stratification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was governed by an oligarchy broad enough to stop any of its members setting up as tyrant, allied with neighbouring Syracuse at first and later its enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That quarrel ended the place: Gelon destroyed it in 483 or 482 BCE and absorbed its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the very few early colonial settlements that is well excavated and published, most of the others being known chiefly from their cemeteries, and if its urban development began at the end of the 8th century and was in part complete in the 7th, Greek urbanisation may have begun at what looks like the periphery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It stands with Syracuse, Naxos, Gela and others as evidence that the western Greeks worked out their town planning for themselves, dividing land into lots and attending to orientation, precincts and civic centres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["David Smith, review of <i>Megara Hyblaia and Selinous: The Development of Two Greek City-States in Archaic Sicily</i>, by Franco De Angelis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.28/. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Conceptualising Early Colonisation</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.43/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis; More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Irad Malkin, review of <i>Difference</i>, ed. Sian Lewis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.11.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.11.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/Monnaie_-_Argent%2C_Sicile%2C_Megara_Hyblaea_-_btv1b103174640_%282_of_2%29.jpg","title":"Megara Hyblaea","desc":"Appartient à l’ensemble documentaire : MonnGre Langue : grec ancien. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Argent,_Sicile,_Megara_Hyblaea_-_btv1b103174640_(2_of_2).jpg","alt":"Monnaie - Argent, Sicile, Megara Hyblaea"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-210","num":210,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Crete and Rhodes planted the first of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 688 BCE, and the second of the pair has no named founders at all in the tradition.","questions":["The crab on the coins of the younger of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands for the river of the same name, which the odes written before Theron came to power already dwell on.","A Thesmophorion at Bitalemi served the elder of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the middle of the 7th century BCE until the Carthaginians destroyed that city in 405."],"answer":"Gela and Acragas","answerText":"Gela and Acragas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gela founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">688 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gela destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">405 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Gela and Acragas</b> are two Greek cities of Sicily, and what is known of how each began is very unequal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gela was planted by settlers from Crete and Rhodes in 688 BCE, some 45 years after Corinth founded Syracuse, and by the 680s the Sicilian colonies were sending out daughters of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the Sicilian sites carrying evidence for early town planning, Gela is the one studied in most detail, and it proves a preconceived urban structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The small sanctuary at Bitalemi just outside the city, probably a Thesmophorion, was founded in the middle of the 7th century BCE, some time after the city itself, and was rearranged in the mid-6th century in a way that closed off the earlier stratigraphy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sanctuary was mostly, though not completely, abandoned after the Carthaginians destroyed Gela in 405 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Acragas, by contrast, has no named founders at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That left Pindar free to put the ancestors of its tyrant Theron into those legendary roles, and to build the family a Panhellenic heroic genealogy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The crab on Akragantine coins stands for the river Akragas, which the odes written before Theron came to power already dwell on, so that the later emphasis tied the tyrant to a symbol the city already had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Heraclea Minoa was established, perhaps between 580 and 570 BCE, as an attempt to check the expansion of Acragas under Phalaris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its temples are famous enough to distort the study of it, the splendour of the Valley of the Temples drawing attention from the ground between and around them, while less grand remains have been assigned, as so many in Sicily are, to Demeter and Persephone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis; More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Vladimir Stissi, review of <i>Gela: il Thesmophorion di Bitalemi. La fase arcaica, scavi P. Orlandini 1963-1967</i>, ed. Marina Albertocchi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.05/. [Open access]","Dominic Dalglish, review of <i>The Akragas Dialogue: New Investigations on Sanctuaries in Sicily</i>, ed. Natascha Sojc and Monica de Cesare, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.09.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.09.32/. [Open access]","Parrish Elizabeth Wright, review of <i>Myth, Locality, and Identity in Pindar's Sicilian Odes</i>, by Virginia M. Lewis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.27/. [Open access]","David Smith, review of <i>Megara Hyblaia and Selinous: The Development of Two Greek City-States in Archaic Sicily</i>, by Franco De Angelis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.28/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-211","num":211,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Planted in western Sicily by Megara Hyblaea in the second half of the 7th century, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought the Greeks up to the edge of Carthage's sphere.","questions":["Between the late 7th century and 460 BCE <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built no fewer than seven temples of more than 112,000 tons of stone, one of the greatest building programmes of the ancient world.","Hemmed in by Phoenicians to the west, Greeks to the east and Elymians to the north, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a position that governed its foreign policy and its internal development alike."],"answer":"Selinus","answerText":"Selinus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temples built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 7th century – 460 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Selinus</b> is the Greek city planted in western Sicily by Megara Hyblaea in the second half of the 7th century BCE, and it brought the Greeks up to the edge of Carthage's sphere of influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It found itself among the complex and dynamic native Sant'Angelo Muxaro and Polizzello, Punic and Elymian cultures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within decades of settlement its centre covered all the ground it would still occupy in the 5th century, some 100 hectares bounded by cemeteries and by the lie of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its town plan had certainly taken shape by 580 to 570 BCE and may go back in part to the 7th century, when the sanctuary of Demeter Malophoros was first laid out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is among the Sicilian sites that give conclusive evidence for early town planning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Between the late 7th century and 460 BCE it carried through one of the greatest building programmes of the ancient world, no fewer than seven temples of more than 112,000 tons of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The metopes of Temples Y and C are read not as sculpture standing on its own but by where they sat within the building, the sanctuary and the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Beside the Malophoros sanctuary lay an independent precinct of Zeus Meilichios, given over largely to unofficial, family cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city was hemmed in by Phoenicians to the west, Greeks to the east and Elymians and other native peoples to the north, and that position governed its foreign policy and its internal development alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its wealth and its position together brought the tyrant Theron to power in the mid-6th century, after which nothing more is heard of fighting with its non-Greek neighbours until the 5th, which suggests that its later pro-Punic stance had roots this early.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["David Smith, review of <i>Megara Hyblaia and Selinous: The Development of Two Greek City-States in Archaic Sicily</i>, by Franco De Angelis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.28/. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis; More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Lela M. Urquhart, review of <i>Zeus Meilichios a Selinunte</i>, by Cristoforo Grotta, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.12.49, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.12.49/. [Open access]","Barbara A. Barletta, review of <i>Temple Decoration and Cultural Identity in the Archaic Greek World: The Metopes of Selinus</i>, by Clemente Marconi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.11.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.11.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Selinunte_-_Templi_Orientali_%28Temple_E%29_18.JPG/1920px-Selinunte_-_Templi_Orientali_%28Temple_E%29_18.JPG","title":"Selinus","desc":"Temple E - Selinunte, Italy. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selinunte_-_Templi_Orientali_(Temple_E)_18.JPG","alt":"Selinunte - Templi Orientali (Temple E) 18"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-212","num":212,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"An expedition from Achaia planted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in southern Italy towards the end of the 8th century BCE, and it later founded Metapontion and Poseidonia in its turn.","questions":["Croton took Herakles for an ancestor and then took over the cult of Philoktetes, in an expansion that finished with the destruction of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and left an enormous ruined site.","Houses of the final phase at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are rectangular, one large room and two small ones, and were found stripped bare, as though emptied by people being forced out."],"answer":"Sybaris","answerText":"Sybaris","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 725 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Refounded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">444 – 443 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Sybaris</b> is the Greek city planted in southern Italy by an expedition from Achaia, south of the gulf of Corinth, towards the end of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country around it, the Sybaritide, was already settled, and its people were part of a widening network by the late 8th century as Greek settlements went up across southern Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It sent out daughters of its own, founding Metapontion about 630 BCE with help from Corinth and Poseidonia a generation later without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Siris lost its independence about 570 BCE and was drawn into its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It shared with Croton, Metapontion and Poseidonia a set of conspicuous extra-urban sanctuaries, each with a temple of Hera standing in the chora, which may have been read as marks of a common Achaean identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its end came from Croton, whose expansion, carried out under the cult of a hero taken over from the peoples in between, finished with a victory in 510 BCE and the destruction of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Enough of the city has survived that destruction to show it covered an enormous area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Houses of the final phase are rectangular, one large room and two small ones, and were found stripped of everything, as though those living in them had emptied them on being forced out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site was settled again as Thurii, a Panhellenic foundation of 444 to 443 BCE that is as good a marker as any for the end of the colonising movement in Magna Graecia and Sicily, on a territory smaller than the one Sybaris had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among the gold Orphic tablets, three from Thurii stand apart in showing the dead moving easily through the underworld before being granted a solution to their problem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Conceptualising Early Colonisation</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.43/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","William M. Balco, review of <i>Contexts of Early Colonization</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.42/. [Open access]","Kirsten Bedigan, review of <i>Mystic Cults in Magna Graecia</i>, ed. Giovanni Casadio and Patricia A. Johnston, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.04.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.04.13/. [Open access]","Simon Trépanier, review of <i>Plato and Pythagoreanism</i>, by Phillip Sidney Horky, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.05.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.05.18/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Sibari-scavi-teatro.jpg/1920px-Sibari-scavi-teatro.jpg","title":"Sybaris","desc":"Sibari: Theater. Mboesch, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sibari-scavi-teatro.jpg","alt":"Sibari-scavi-teatro"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-213","num":213,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"An expedition from Achaia planted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in southern Italy towards the end of the 8th century BCE, and its great sanctuary stood on Cape Lacinium to the south.","questions":["Pythagoras of Samos emigrated to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and founded there a puritanical way of life built on the doctrine of reincarnation, being a charismatic guru rather than a philosopher.","Taking Herakles for an ancestor and then the cult of Philoktetes, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> expanded until it destroyed Sybaris, over which it won a victory in 510 BCE."],"answer":"Croton","answerText":"Croton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 725 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sybaris defeated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Croton</b> is one of the two cities that settlers from Achaia founded in southern Italy at the close of the 8th century BCE, and it was to destroy the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its great sanctuary, the Heraion on Cape Lacinium to the south of the city, has been set beside the Heraion on Samos, and in a colonial city such a cult stands outside the walls, turning the field of competition away from the citizens and towards the indigenous peoples around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It claimed an antiquity of its own by taking Herakles for an ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It then took over the cult of Philoktetes, a hero who had belonged to the non-Greek communities lying between it and Sybaris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That was an expansionary move, and it ended in the destruction of Sybaris, over which the city won its victory in 510 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pythagoras of Samos, who lived from about 570 to 490 BCE, emigrated to the city and founded there a puritanical way of life built on the doctrine of reincarnation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He appears to have been a charismatic guru rather than a philosopher, and still less a mathematician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the victory over Sybaris the city seems to have held a dominant position in the region under a community led by him, and Pythagorean membership seems to have been extended to the leaders of neighbouring states as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Philolaus of Croton, who lived from about 470 to 400 BCE, is one of only three mathematical Pythagoreans who can be made out at all through the mists of the period before Plato, the others being Archytas of Tarentum and Hippasus of Metapontum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city also produced the athlete Milo, who for all his fame never received worship and so never attained the standing of a hero, where Theagenes of Thasos did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","Simon Trépanier, review of <i>Plato and Pythagoreanism</i>, by Phillip Sidney Horky, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.05.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.05.18/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Placing the Gods: Sanctuaries and Sacred Space in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.06.01/. [Open access]","William Morison, review of <i>Ancient Greek Athletics</i>, by Stephen G. Miller, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.12.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.12.10/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Capo_Colonna_-_Colonna.jpg/1920px-Capo_Colonna_-_Colonna.jpg","title":"Croton","desc":"Capo Colonna (Crotone, Calabria, Italy) - The column remaining from the temple of Hera Lacinia. Benjamin Smith, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capo_Colonna_-_Colonna.jpg","alt":"Capo Colonna - Colonna"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-214","num":214,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The traditions about the founding of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are tied to the Messenian war and turn on the Partheniai, surviving in variant forms both there and in Sparta.","questions":["Archytas presided over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the mid-380s to the mid-350s BCE, elected general seven times, and under him the city led the Italian League.","Hostilities with Rome broke out in 282 and 281 BCE and ended with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seeking military help from Pyrrhus of Epirus against them."],"answer":"Taras","answerText":"Taras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Archytas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 385 – 355 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War with Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">282 – 281 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Taras</b> is the Greek city of southern Italy founded from Sparta, and the traditions of its foundation are tied to the Messenian war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They turn on the Partheniai, and they survive in variant forms at both ends, in the colony and in Sparta itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those traditions are initiatory in nature, and yet they also corroborate the 8th-century archaeological evidence for the foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legends of the Partheniai have been read as showing that anything resembling colonisation beyond Lakonia, the honours given to Brasidas among such things, could look like a threat to the patrilineal rights of the Spartan royal houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its own expansion was felt by its neighbours, and Metapontion is said to have taken Nestor for a founder in the face of the Spartan city pushing towards Sybaris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Archytas presided over the city from the mid-380s to the mid-350s BCE in what seems to have been a form of democracy, being said to have been elected general seven times, though not necessarily one year after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was a most successful general, and under him the city led the Italian League, though for all that precious little is really known about him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He gave considerable thought to political harmony between rich and poor, balancing the privileges of wealth against the duties of the well-off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hostilities with Rome broke out in 282 and 281 BCE, and they ended with the city seeking military help from Pyrrhus of Epirus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ancient accounts of that outbreak turn on odd episodes and flamboyant individuals: a demagogue who talked the citizens into attacking a Roman fleet passing by, a drunk who fouled a Roman envoy's toga before the assembled citizenry, and a man who warned them in vain against inviting Pyrrhus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Massimo Nafissi and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08/. [Open access]","Timothy Doran, review of <i>Myth, Text, and History at Sparta</i>, ed. Thomas J. Figueira, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.16/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","John Dillon, review of <i>Archytas of Tarentum: Pythagorean, Philosopher and Mathematician King</i>, by Carl A. Huffman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.03.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.34/. [Open access]","Michael Fronda, review of <i>Images and Insults: Ancient Historiography and the Outbreak of the Tarentine War</i>, by Christopher L. H. Barnes, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.04.37/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Gioielli_dall%27antica_Taras_%28Taranto%29.jpg/1920px-Gioielli_dall%27antica_Taras_%28Taranto%29.jpg","title":"Taras","desc":"Tre orecchini d'oro, una collana ed un anello provenienti dall'antica Taras (Taranto). IV-III secolo a.C. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gioielli_dall%27antica_Taras_(Taranto).jpg","alt":"Gioielli dall'antica Taras (Taranto)"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-215","num":215,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Archaeology puts <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first Greek colony on the Italian mainland, no later than 730 to 720 BCE, with an earlier date of about 750 not disproved.","questions":["A long gallery cut into the rock at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has for many years been shown as the Sibyl’s grotto, though as it now looks it began as a covered way for troops.","Statuettes from the votive deposit on the acropolis at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were every one found without its head, which probably means the heads were taken off as part of the rite."],"answer":"Cumae","answerText":"Cumae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 720 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">421 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Cumae</b> is the first Greek colony on the Italian mainland, and the archaeological evidence puts it no later than 730 to 720 BCE, with an earlier date of about 750 not disproved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was founded a little after Pithekoussai, the island settlement off the Bay of Naples whose people went on to make it, and after Cumae came a flood of colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavation here has turned up Greek burial ritual and Greek sherds in a possible proto-urban setting as early as the earliest material from the island, which blurs the order of the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archival records of the old digs at both places are being gone through again, since they bear on the Early Iron Age chronology of the whole Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city is where the Cumaean Sibyl gave her prophecies, and a long gallery cut into the rock has for many years been shown as her grotto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The gallery as it now looks is dated to the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, when it began as a covered way for troops climbing to the upper defences of the acropolis, its floor being lowered only later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A votive deposit from the lower terrace of that acropolis held terracotta feet, hands, heads, masks, statuettes, body parts and small altars, with a relief plaque and a few bronzes among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Statuettes make up about half of what survives and body parts the rest, among them forty feet, four hands, 58 pieces of finger and four male genitalia, most of them made in a mould.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every one of the statuettes was found without its head, which probably means the heads were taken off as part of the rite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The native Campanians took the city in 421 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Jean Turfa, review of <i>The First Western Greeks</i>, by David Ridgway, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.19/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Ancient Greeks West and East</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.20/. [Open access]","Ingrid Edlund-Berry, review of <i>Il deposito dall'Acropoli di Cuma</i>, by Marina Catucci and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.09.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.09.29/. [Open access]","William M. Balco, review of <i>Contexts of Early Colonization</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.42/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Charles-Louis_Cl%C3%A9risseau%2C_Ancient_Gate_at_Cumae_near_Naples%2C_1769%2C_private_collection.jpg","title":"Cumae","desc":"Charles-Louis Clérisseau, Ancient Gate at Cumae near Naples, 1769, private collection. Chalres-Louis Clérisseau. 1769. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles-Louis_Cl%C3%A9risseau,_Ancient_Gate_at_Cumae_near_Naples,_1769,_private_collection.jpg","alt":"Charles-Louis Clérisseau, Ancient Gate at Cumae near Naples, 1769, private collection"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-004","num":4,"category":"Human origins","question":"Traced through fossil bones, stone tools and lately ancient DNA, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the long process by which people descended from apelike ancestors, beginning in Africa millions of years ago.","questions":["Upright walking came first and big brains much later in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why the earliest members of our lineage stood on two legs with brains no bigger than an ape's.","Often drawn as a single file marching towards a modern man, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is better pictured as a bush, with 15 to 20 early human species known and several overlapping in time."],"answer":"human evolution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ape split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8 – 6 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Our species</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 300,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"human evolution","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Human evolution</b> is the long process of change by which people descended from apelike ancestors, and the science that reconstructs that process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does not mean that people descended from any living ape: humans and the great apes of Africa share a common ancestor that lived between about 8 and 6 million years ago, and the two lines have gone their own ways ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of what followed happened in Africa, which is where the earliest members of the human line are found and where our own species later appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The evidence is of three kinds: fossil bones and teeth, the stone tools their makers left in ground that can be dated, and, for the most recent stretch, DNA recovered from ancient bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its central lesson is that the traits we think of as human did not arrive together as a package but were assembled piecemeal, over millions of years and in no obvious order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Walking upright came first, and by about 4 million years ago Australopithecus was doing it habitually.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its brain nevertheless stayed small, usually under 500 cubic centimetres, roughly a third the size of a modern one, so an upright body cannot have been a consequence of a clever mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Flaked stone tools follow much later, the systematic making of sharp edges being known from about 2.6 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Brains then enlarged, a rise that ran within species rather than in jumps between them and that quickened towards the present, reaching an average near 1,300 cubic centimetres in Homo sapiens, which appears in Africa around 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nothing about this was a march in single file: some 15 to 20 early human species are recognised, several were alive at the same time, and the last of them interbred with ours before dying out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Introduction to Human Evolution,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/education/introduction-human-evolution. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Walking Upright,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/human-characteristics/walking. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]","Thomas A. Püschel et al., \"Hominin Brain Size Increase Has Emerged from within-Species Encephalization,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 49 (2024): e2409542121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11626186/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo sapiens</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-sapiens. [Open access]","Richard E. Green et al., \"A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,\" <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., \"Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","evolution","palaeontology","prehistory","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Fossil_hominids.jpg/1920px-Fossil_hominids.jpg","title":"human evolution","desc":"Fossil Hominid Skull Display at The Museum of Osteology. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fossil_hominids.jpg","alt":"Fossil hominids"},"difficulty":1,"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-005","num":5,"category":"Human origins","question":"Marked out above all by habitual upright walking, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is any member of the human branch of the family tree, a group taking in Homo, Australopithecus, Paranthropus and Ardipithecus.","questions":["Older books call the same creatures hominids, but once the great apes were gathered into one family that word had to widen, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took over its old meaning.","Brain size is no guide to whether a fossil is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: the oldest candidates carry braincases no larger than a chimpanzee's, and the edge of the group is argued over."],"answer":"hominin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7 Mya – present</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest claimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sahelanthropus, c. 7 – 6 Mya</span></div>","answerText":"hominin","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>hominin</b> is any member of Hominini, the branch of the family tree holding modern humans, the extinct human species and our immediate ancestors — Homo, Australopithecus, Paranthropus and Ardipithecus among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hominini is a tribe, a rank between subfamily and genus, which is why the name ends in -ini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word is recent in this sense, and it exists because molecular work rearranged the apes: humans, chimpanzees and gorillas turned out to be more closely related to one another than any of them is to the orangutan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That gathered the great apes into a single family, Hominidae, so 'hominid' — which had meant roughly what 'hominin' means now — widened to take in chimpanzees, gorillas and orangutans as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A narrower word was then needed for the human branch alone, which is the work 'hominin' does; older books using 'hominid' of the same creatures are out of date rather than wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What marks a hominin out is not the brain but the way it moves: habitual upright walking as the chief means of getting about, with teeth of essentially human form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest candidates bear this out, carrying braincases no larger than a chimpanzee's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oldest of them is Sahelanthropus tchadensis from Chad, dated between 7 and 6 million years ago, whose foramen magnum, the opening where the spinal cord leaves the skull, sits further forward than in any primate but humans — the mark a head balanced on an upright spine leaves behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where the edge of the group falls is a question of classification rather than of fact, and it moves: the accepted genera went from three to seven in the years to 2003, and one review left Sahelanthropus <i>incertae sedis</i>, of uncertain position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 15 to 20 early human species are recognised in all,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and several were alive at once: around 1.9 million years ago Homo erectus shared East Africa with Homo habilis, Homo rudolfensis and Paranthropus boisei, sometimes at the same sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Australian Museum, \"Hominid and Hominin — What’s the Difference?,\" last updated 2 December 2020, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/hominid-and-hominin-whats-the-difference/. [Open access]","Camilo J. Cela-Conde and Francisco J. Ayala, \"Genera of the Human Lineage,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 100, no. 13 (2003): 7684–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC164648/. [Open access]","Morris Goodman and Kirstin N. Sterner, \"Phylogenomic Evidence of Adaptive Evolution in the Ancestry of Humans,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, suppl. 2 (2010): 8918–23, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3024020/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/sahelanthropus-tchadensis. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Introduction to Human Evolution,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/education/introduction-human-evolution. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Evidence_as_to_Man%27s_Place_in_Naturep2.jpg","title":"hominin","desc":"SKELETONS OF THE Gibbon. Orang. Chimpanzee. Gorilla. Man. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evidence_as_to_Man%27s_Place_in_Naturep2.jpg","alt":"Evidence as to Man's Place in Naturep2"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-006","num":6,"category":"Human origins","question":"Known from a crushed cranium nicknamed Toumaï, recovered in the Djurab Desert of northern Chad, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the oldest creature yet proposed as a member of the human branch.","questions":["At roughly 7 million years old, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sits close to the parting of the human and chimpanzee lines, which is why the argument over whether it walked upright matters so much.","A forward-set foramen magnum and small canines put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the human side for some, while its chimp-sized brain and chimp-like limb bones leave others unconvinced."],"answer":"Sahelanthropus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7.2 – 6.8 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Toros-Menalla, Chad</span></div>","answerText":"Sahelanthropus","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sahelanthropus</b> tchadensis is the oldest creature yet proposed as a member of the human branch, known from fossils recovered in the Djurab Desert of northern Chad and named in 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best of them is a nearly complete but badly crushed cranium nicknamed Toumaï, 'hope of life' in the local language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cosmogenic dating of the beds that held it brackets the animal between 6.8 and 7.2 million years old, with a weighted mean near 7.04 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That sits close to the time the human and chimpanzee lines are thought to have parted, which is what makes the fossil so valuable and so fiercely argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its brain was small, roughly 320 to 380 cubic centimetres, much like a chimpanzee's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What first suggested it belonged on the human side was the skull: the foramen magnum, where the spinal cord leaves the braincase, sits further forward than in apes or any other primate but humans, which is where a head balanced on an upright spine would put it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The teeth pointed the same way, the canines and incisors being relatively small, though a heavy brow ridge sets the face apart from anything among the australopithecines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A thigh bone and two forearm bones from the same locality were later brought into the argument, and they cut both ways: in size and shape both limb bones sit closest to chimpanzees, while features of the femur have been read as the hip and knee of a habitual walker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The literature is accordingly polarised, one group finding enough here for the earliest hominin and another dismissing that evidence and doubting the animal belongs on our branch at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Formal classifications have hedged to match, one review leaving the genus <i>incertae sedis</i>, of uncertain position, so whether this is an ancestor, a cousin or an ape of some other line is still open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/sahelanthropus-tchadensis. [Open access]","Australian Museum, \"<i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/sahelanthropus-tchadensis/. [Open access]","Anne-Elisabeth Lebatard et al., \"Cosmogenic Nuclide Dating of <i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i> and <i>Australopithecus bahrelghazali</i>: Mio-Pliocene Hominids from Chad,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 9 (2008): 3226–31, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2265126/. [Open access]","Scott A. Williams et al., \"Earliest Evidence of Hominin Bipedalism in <i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 1 (2026): eadv0130, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12758524/. [Open access]","Camilo J. Cela-Conde and Francisco J. Ayala, \"Genera of the Human Lineage,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 100, no. 13 (2003): 7684–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC164648/. [Open access]"],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Sahelanthropus_tchadensis_-_TM_266_location.jpg","title":"Sahelanthropus","desc":"A map of Chad marking TM 266, where the first Sahelanthropus tchadensis was found, with the inferred maximum extent of Holocene Lake Mega Chad outlined. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sahelanthropus_tchadensis_-_TM_266_location.jpg","alt":"Sahelanthropus tchadensis - TM 266 location"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-007","num":7,"category":"Human origins","question":"Older than stone tools, large brains or any other trait of our branch, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is habitual movement on two legs, and the change that rebuilt the skeleton from skull to foot.","questions":["Three individuals crossed wet volcanic ash at Laetoli 3.6 million years ago, leaving in about 70 prints the earliest direct evidence of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> anywhere in the record.","Walking costs a human roughly 75 per cent less energy than moving about costs a chimpanzee, which is the leading explanation for why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was worth evolving at all."],"answer":"bipedalism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Evident from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Footprints</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3.6 Mya, Laetoli</span></div>","answerText":"bipedalism","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Bipedalism</b> is habitual movement on two legs, and in its human form it means not merely standing upright but walking as the only way of getting about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the oldest of the traits that mark the human branch, evolving over 4 million years ago, long before large brains, stone tools or anything else distinctively human.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reaching it rebuilt the skeleton from head to foot. The opening where the spinal cord leaves the skull moved forward, so that the head balances on the spine rather than hanging from it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the lower back gained the forward curve that absorbs the shock of each step, a curve found in no other animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hip bones broadened, the thigh bones lengthened, and the knee grew strong enough to carry the whole body on one leg at a time — the arrangement Homo erectus had by 1.9 million years ago, climbing having been given up for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest direct evidence is not a bone but a set of footprints: at Laetoli in Tanzania three individuals crossed wet volcanic ash 3.6 million years ago, leaving about 70 prints along nearly 27 metres (89 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their big toes lie in line with the rest of the foot, and the tracks record a heel strike followed by a push off the toes — how a modern human walks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Why it began is less settled than that it did, and the leading answer is economy: human walking costs about 75 per cent less than either walking or knuckle-walking costs a chimpanzee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That saving is not automatic, since in living chimpanzees the two gaits cost much the same; what matters is that individuals with longer hind limbs and a straighter hip were cheaper on two legs, which is where selection could take hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Other advantages have been proposed and none excludes the rest: an upright animal has its hands free for food, tools or infants, reaches fruit on low branches, and looks larger to whatever is watching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Walking Upright,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/human-characteristics/walking. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Laetoli Footprint Trails,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/behavior/laetoli-footprint-trails. [Open access]","Michael D. Sockol, David A. Raichlen, and Herman Pontzer, \"Chimpanzee Locomotor Energetics and the Origin of Human Bipedalism,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 30 (2007): 12265–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1941460/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Introduction to Human Evolution,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/education/introduction-human-evolution. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/sahelanthropus-tchadensis. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","evolution","palaeontology","prehistory","anatomy"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-008","num":8,"category":"Human origins","question":"Named from the Afar word for 'ground' joined to the Greek for 'ape', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a genus of early Ethiopian hominins whose best-known member is the skeleton called Ardi.","questions":["The pelvis of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> combines climbing with upright walking while the big toe still splays out to grasp, which is why it is called a mosaic rather than a halfway house.","Small unhoned canines and a foot that both grasps and pushes off make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the fossil behind the argument that our line never descended from a knuckle-walking ape."],"answer":"Ardipithecus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5.8 – 4.4 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Middle Awash, Ethiopia</span></div>","answerText":"Ardipithecus","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ardipithecus</b> is a genus of early hominins from Ethiopia, its name joining the Afar word for 'ground' or 'floor' to the Greek for 'ape'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The older of its two species, Ardipithecus kadabba, comes from the Middle Awash and lived between about 5.8 and 5.2 million years ago, known from a modest set of teeth, jaws and a few bones of the hand, foot and arm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The younger and far better known is Ardipithecus ramidus, at about 4.4 million years ago, first reported in 1994 and now represented by over 100 specimens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among them is the partial female skeleton nicknamed Ardi, announced in 2009; the remains of well over 100 further individuals have since confirmed that her anatomy is typical of the species rather than peculiar to her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her brain was small, roughly 300 to 350 cubic centimetres, about the size of a female chimpanzee's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the genus matter is that it is a mosaic rather than a halfway house: the pelvis carries features for climbing alongside features for walking upright, while the big toe still splays out to grasp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The teeth say the same thing from the other end, the canines being less projecting and smaller than in any other known ape, with no sign of the honing that sharpens an ape's upper canine against a lower premolar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earlier species carries the signal in miniature, a single toe bone from 5.2 million years ago having been read as showing the push-off of an upright stride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From all this its discoverers argue that the ancestor we share with chimpanzees was no chimpanzee but a generalised ape, so that our line never descended from a knuckle-walker — the claim the genus is famous for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is disputed: a re-examination of the foot reads it instead as evidence that what preceded hominin walking was ordinary African-ape quadrupedalism and climbing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/ardipithecus-ramidus. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Ardipithecus kadabba</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/ardipithecus-kadabba. [Open access]","Tim D. White et al., \"Neither Chimpanzee nor Human, <i>Ardipithecus</i> Reveals the Surprising Ancestry of Both,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 112, no. 16 (2015): 4877–84, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4413341/. [Open access]","Thomas Cody Prang, \"The African Ape-like Foot of <i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i> and Its Implications for the Origin of Bipedalism,\" <i>eLife</i> 8 (2019): e44433, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC6491036/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Australian Museum, \"<i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/ardipithecus-ramidus/. [Open access]"],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ardipithecus_%28finger_bones%29.jpg/1920px-Ardipithecus_%28finger_bones%29.jpg","title":"Ardipithecus","desc":"Finger bones of \"Ardi\" (casts). Gerbil, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ardipithecus_(finger_bones).jpg","alt":"Ardipithecus (finger bones)"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-010","num":10,"category":"Human origins","question":"Catalogued as AL 288-1 and found at Hadar in Ethiopia in 1974, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the partial skeleton that showed upright walking had come long before any growth of the brain.","questions":["Several hundred fragments make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the most complete early hominin skeletons known, enough of the pelvis, leg and spine surviving to say how she moved.","Also called Dinkinesh, Amharic for 'you are marvellous', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood about 1.05 metres tall and carried a walker's hips beneath a braincase the size of a chimpanzee's."],"answer":"Lucy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3.2 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Hadar, Ethiopia</span></div>","answerText":"Lucy","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lucy</b> is a partial skeleton of Australopithecus afarensis, catalogued as AL 288-1 and found in 1974 at Hadar in the Afar region of Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several hundred fragments survive, making her one of the most complete early hominin skeletons known and leaving enough of the pelvis, leg and spine to say how she moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She stood about 1.05 metres (3 ft 5 in) tall and lived some 3.2 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her species ranged across eastern Africa between about 3.85 and 2.95 million years ago, lived mainly on plants, and may be ancestral to later Australopithecus, Paranthropus and Homo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She was nicknamed after a Beatles song playing in camp the night she was found, and is also known as Dinkinesh, Amharic for 'you are marvellous'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What made her decisive is the combination she preserves: a short broad pelvis and thigh bones angled inward to bring the knees under the body, which is the arrangement of a habitual walker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her braincase was nevertheless small, her species averaging about 446 cubic centimetres, close to a chimpanzee's and roughly a third of a modern human's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That pairing is the whole significance of her, since walking upright evolved over 4 million years ago while large and complex brains came much later, and here both facts sit in one skeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> She had not left the trees, either: her species kept long strong arms and curved fingers throughout, so the animal to picture is at home on the ground and above it alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Her own kind left the direct proof a few hundred thousand years before her, in the footprint trails at Laetoli in Tanzania, which are attributed to A. afarensis on the strength of bones from the same beds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"AL 288-1 ('Lucy'),\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/al-288-1. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]","Ashleigh L. A. Wiseman, \"Three-Dimensional Volumetric Muscle Reconstruction of the <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i> Pelvis and Limb, with Estimations of Limb Leverage,\" <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 10, no. 6 (2023): 230356, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10265029/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Ian Tattersall, \"Endocranial Volumes and Human Evolution,\" <i>F1000Research</i> 12 (2023): 565, table 1, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10517302/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Introduction to Human Evolution,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/education/introduction-human-evolution. [Open access]","Australian Museum, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/australopithecus-afarensis/. [Open access]"],"tags":["fossil","hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Lucy_cast_front_view_CMNH.jpg","title":"Lucy","desc":"Lucy (Australopithecus) skeletal cast in its new display at the Cleveland Museum of Natural History. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucy_cast_front_view_CMNH.jpg","alt":"Lucy cast front view CMNH"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-011","num":11,"category":"Human origins","question":"Set in wet volcanic ash and sealed by the next eruption, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> record a gait no bone can give directly: heel down first, then a push off the toes.","questions":["The roughly 70 impressions called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were left when three individuals crossed the same Tanzanian ash surface 3.66 million years ago, the smallest treading in the largest one's tracks.","Because the big toe lies in line with the others rather than splaying to grasp, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the earliest direct evidence that upright walking had become habitual."],"answer":"Laetoli footprints","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3.66 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Laetoli, Tanzania</span></div>","answerText":"Laetoli footprints","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Laetoli footprints</b> are trails of hominin prints preserved in hardened volcanic ash at Laetoli in northern Tanzania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They survive by an accident of timing: a nearby volcano laid down a fine ash, rain wetted it, animals and hominins walked across it, and a later eruption buried the surface before it could be destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best-known trail runs almost 27 metres (88 feet) and holds about 70 impressions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three individuals made it, moving in the same direction, the smallest treading in the tracks of the largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surface has been dated to about 3.66 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their value is that they record a gait, which no bone can give directly: the big toe lies in line with the rest of the foot rather than splaying out to grasp, and each step lands on the heel and pushes off from the toes, the way a modern human walks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That makes them the earliest direct evidence that upright walking had become habitual rather than occasional, and the close spacing of the prints suggests short legs and correspondingly short strides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are attributed to Australopithecus afarensis, whose fossils lie nearby in the same sediment layer and whose bones independently show a body that walked upright,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> though the case rests on that association rather than on any skeleton found in the tracks themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A second set of trackways on the same surface carries the prints of a much larger walker, put at about 1.65 metres (5 ft 5 in), which has been read as a sign of marked size difference between the sexes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prints at another spot, first noticed in 1976, were left by a small hominin with an odd cross-stepping walk, and have been argued to record a second kind of hominin crossing the same landscape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Laetoli Footprint Trails,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/behavior/laetoli-footprint-trails. [Open access]","Fidelis T. Masao et al., \"New Footprints from Laetoli (Tanzania) Provide Evidence for Marked Body Size Variation in Early Hominins,\" <i>eLife</i> 5 (2016): e19568, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5156529/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Ellison J. McNutt et al., \"Footprint Evidence of Early Hominin Locomotor Diversity at Laetoli, Tanzania,\" <i>Nature</i> 600, no. 7889 (2021): 468–71, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8674131/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]","Australian Museum, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/australopithecus-afarensis/. [Open access]"],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa","tanzania"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Homo_footprints.jpg","title":"Laetoli footprints","desc":"Interpretation of a diagramatics o Human footprint in Laetoli (3.7 My) thought to be from A. afarensis; Ileret (1.5 My), from H. erectus, and H. sapiens. The green lines represent the points under the same pressure/weight. As in Science, vol 323, pg 1197. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_footprints.jpg","alt":"Homo footprints"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-012","num":12,"category":"Human origins","question":"Blasted out of lime workings in South Africa in 1924, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a young australopith's skull whose braincase filled with limestone and set into a natural cast of the brain.","questions":["Milk teeth and erupting first molars put the age at death at three or four, and a forward-set foramen magnum made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first fossil evidence that hominins walked upright.","Punctures at the bottom of the eye sockets, matching the damage crowned eagles leave on monkeys, suggest the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed and eaten by a large bird of prey."],"answer":"Taung Child","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.8 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Taung, South Africa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died aged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 or 4 years</span></div>","answerText":"Taung Child","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Taung Child</b> is the fossilised skull of a young Australopithecus africanus, blasted out of lime workings at Taung in South Africa in 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It preserves the face, much of the braincase and the jaws, with the milk teeth and the first molars still in place, which puts the child's age at death at three or four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Limestone had filled the empty braincase and hardened, so the fossil carries a natural cast of the brain's outer surface, made by the ground rather than by anyone's hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That cast gives a cranial capacity now put at between 382 and 407 cubic centimetres, which falls within the range of the great apes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The specimen is about 2.8 million years old, and its species ranged across southern Africa between roughly 3.3 and 2.1 million years ago, walking upright while keeping shoulders and hands still suited to climbing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What made the skull decisive is where the spinal cord left it: the foramen magnum sits beneath the braincase rather than towards the back, which is the placement of a head carried on an upright body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That was the first fossil evidence of hominin bipedality, and the description published in 1925 made the skull the type specimen of a new species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was also the first fossil support for Darwin's prediction of 1871 that the human lineage had begun in Africa rather than in Asia or Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How the child died has a likely answer of its own: punctures at the bottom of the eye sockets, with gouges and scratches around the orbits and across the front of the skull, match the damage crowned eagles leave on the monkeys they kill and eat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The deposit around the skull holds eggshells and the bones of many small animals, which is what gathers below a large raptor's nest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Taung Child,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/taung-child. [Open access]","Dean Falk et al., \"Metopic Suture of Taung (<i>Australopithecus africanus</i>) and Its Implications for Hominin Brain Evolution,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 22 (2012): 8467–70, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3365196/. [Open access]","John Rowan and Bernard Wood, \"Dart and the Taung Juvenile: Making Sense of a Century-Old Record of Hominin Evolution in Africa,\" <i>Biology Letters</i> 20, no. 7 (2024): 20240185, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11267397/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus africanus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-africanus. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger and W. Scott McGraw, \"Further Evidence for Eagle Predation of, and Feeding Damage on, the Taung Child,\" <i>South African Journal of Science</i> 103, nos. 11–12 (2007): 496–98, https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0038-23532007000600013. [Open access]"],"tags":["fossil","hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Tobias_and_Taung.jpg/1920px-Tobias_and_Taung.jpg","title":"Taung Child","desc":"Dr. Philip V. Tobias and the Taung Child. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tobias_and_Taung.jpg","alt":"Tobias and Taung"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-013","num":13,"category":"Human origins","question":"Flaring cheekbones, a crest along the top of the skull and cheek teeth four times the size of a modern human's make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the robust australopithecines.","questions":["Carbon isotopes show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived largely on grasses and sedges rather than nuts, which overturned the reading its massive jaws had suggested for decades.","The name means 'beside man', which is roughly where most scholars still put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: a side branch of the human tree that led nowhere."],"answer":"Paranthropus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.7 – 1.2 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">eastern and southern Africa</span></div>","answerText":"Paranthropus","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Paranthropus</b> is an extinct genus of African hominins, the robust australopithecines, whose skulls are built around the machinery of chewing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three species are recognised: P. aethiopicus in the Turkana basin from about 2.7 to 2.3 million years ago,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> P. boisei in eastern Africa from about 2.3 to 1.2 million,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and P. robustus in southern Africa from about 1.8 to 1.2 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The genus name means 'beside man', which is where it has stood since 1938: a side branch rather than an ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Robust describes the face and teeth rather than the body, a male P. boisei standing about 1.37 metres (4 ft 6 in) and weighing some 49 kilograms (108 lb).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chewing anatomy is what defines the group: a crest along the midline of the skull to anchor the jaw muscles, wide cheekbones to carry them, and cheek teeth four times the size of a modern human's beneath the thickest enamel of any early human.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The best-known skull, OH 5 from Olduvai Gorge, was nicknamed Nutcracker Man for exactly that apparatus,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and for decades the reading was that a head built like this existed to crush hard objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The teeth themselves say otherwise. Microwear on the molars of P. boisei resembles a fruit-eater's rather than a hard-object specialist's, so tough foods like roots and nuts were probably fallbacks, eaten when better ones ran short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carbon isotopes go further, showing a diet dominated by C4 plants such as grasses and sedges, sustained over more than half a million years and across the Turkana, Baringo, Natron and Olduvai regions, more heavily than in any other hominin yet measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The southern species fed more widely, on nuts, seeds, roots and tubers alongside soft fruit, insects and meat,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> but the eastern specialist may have been undone by its own narrowness: C4 grasslands contracted as woodland spread around a million years ago, sharpening competition among the animals that lived on them, and P. boisei disappears just before that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Paranthropus boisei</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/paranthropus-boisei. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Paranthropus aethiopicus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/paranthropus-aethiopicus. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Paranthropus robustus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/paranthropus-robustus. [Open access]","Thure E. Cerling et al., \"Diet of <i>Paranthropus boisei</i> in the Early Pleistocene of East Africa,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 23 (2011): 9337–41, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3111323/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Rhonda L. Quinn and Christopher J. Lepre, \"Contracting Eastern African C4 Grasslands during the Extinction of <i>Paranthropus boisei</i>,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 7164, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8009881/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"OH 5,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/oh-5. [Open access]"],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Paranthropus-boisei-Nairobi.JPG/1920px-Paranthropus-boisei-Nairobi.JPG","title":"Paranthropus","desc":"The skull of Paranthropus boisei, known as KNM ER 406, photographed at the Nairobi National Museum in August 2012. Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paranthropus-boisei-Nairobi.JPG","alt":"Paranthropus-boisei-Nairobi"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-022","num":22,"category":"Human origins","question":"Long before our own species existed, the dispersal called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried hominins from Africa into western Asia, and eventually as far as China and Indonesia.","questions":["The spread known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put hominins at Dmanisi in Georgia by 1.8 million years ago and in Java before 1.5 million.","The travellers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were not big-brained pioneers: the smallest braincase among them holds 546 cubic centimetres, barely above an australopith's."],"answer":"Out of Africa I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest claim</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.95 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dmanisi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.8 Mya</span></div>","answerText":"Out of Africa I","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Out of Africa I</b> is the name given to the first spread of hominins beyond Africa, an event more than a million and a half years older than our own species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The species associated with it is Homo erectus, which appears about 1.89 million years ago and whose range runs from northern, eastern and southern Africa through western Asia to Georgia, China and Indonesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best-dated early site outside the continent is Dmanisi in Georgia, where hominins were present by 1.8 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cut-marked animal bones at Grăunceanu in Romania have been dated to more than 1.95 million years ago, which would push the crossing earlier still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the other direction the record reaches Java by more than 1.5 million years ago, at the far end of a route across most of the breadth of Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What is striking about the travellers is how unremarkable they were, judged by the traits usually taken to mark the human career. Skulls of this period from Africa and Georgia hold brains ranging from about 546 to 1,067 cubic centimetres, so the smallest sits barely above an australopith's, and the spread within one region is nearly twofold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor did they carry the newest toolkit out with them: the Acheulean hand axe appears in Africa only around 1.76 million years ago, by which time hominins were already at Dmanisi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Homo erectus went on to become possibly the longest-lived of all early human species, persisting in Indonesia until roughly 110,000 years ago, long after it had gone from Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The numeral in the name matters, because Homo sapiens did not appear in Africa until about 300,000 years ago: the spread that eventually carried modern people across the world is a separate event, and a far later one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Sabrina C. Curran et al., \"Hominin Presence in Eurasia by at Least 1.95 Million Years Ago,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 836, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11747263/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]","Yan Rizal et al., \"Last Appearance of <i>Homo erectus</i> at Ngandong, Java, 117,000–108,000 Years Ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 577, no. 7790 (2020): 381–85, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1863-2. [Open access]","Susan C. Antón et al., \"Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Ignacio de la Torre, \"The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo sapiens</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-sapiens. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","migration","hominin","palaeontology","prehistory","evolution"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Out_of_Africa_I_and_II_hypothesis.jpg","title":"Out of Africa I","desc":"A map of the Out of Africa I and II hypotheses. Gianreali, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Out_of_Africa_I_and_II_hypothesis.jpg","alt":"Out of Africa I and II hypothesis"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-031","num":31,"category":"Human origins","question":"Heat breaks food down before it is swallowed, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposes that this is what paid for the small gut and the large brain of early Homo.","questions":["The trouble with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is chronology: it needs fire around 1.7 million years ago, and burnt material genuinely in place goes back only about a million.","Evidence from jaws and teeth alone cannot settle the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because cutting and pounding with stone tools soften food as well as heat does."],"answer":"cooking hypothesis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Change dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.7 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fire in place</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1 Mya</span></div>","answerText":"cooking hypothesis","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>cooking hypothesis</b> holds that the control of fire for cooking was a driving force behind the adaptations that define early Homo, above all a reduced digestive system and an increased brain volume.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its logic is energetic rather than culinary. Heat breaks food down before it is swallowed, so more of a meal's energy is recovered and less gut is needed to recover it, which is what would make a costly brain affordable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Homo erectus, appearing about 1.89 million years ago, is the species in which those changes show most clearly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The hypothesis accordingly dates the shift to somewhere around 1.7 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One line of support comes from the jaws: the ability to generate molar bite force falls away at the very origin of the genus, Homo habilis producing less of it at the molars than the australopiths before it, which implies that changes in diet or in food processing mattered from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That evidence does not require fire, though, since cutting and pounding with stone tools soften food as well, and the jaws cannot tell the two apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The harder problem is the dating. Burnt material genuinely in place goes back about a million years at Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa, and fire becomes routine equipment only around 400,000 years ago, which is a long way short of 1.7 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A bioenergetic analysis presses further still, finding that cooking meat improves its digestibility by only about 8 per cent, some 1,200 calories an hour, too little to repay the cost of keeping a fire alight, and proposing that preserving meat and fat and keeping predators off were the likelier first reasons for Homo erectus to burn anything at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["John A. J. Gowlett, \"The Discovery of Fire by Humans: A Long and Convoluted Process,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1696 (2016): 20150164, under \"Fire and the Cooking Hypothesis,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4874402/. [Open access]","Miki Ben-Dor and Ran Barkai, \"A Bioenergetic Approach Favors the Preservation and Protection of Prey, Not Cooking, as the Drivers of Early Fire,\" <i>Frontiers in Nutrition</i> 12 (2025): 1585182, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12122349/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Justin A. Ledogar et al., \"Bite Force Production and the Origin of <i>Homo</i>,\" <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 12, no. 4 (2025): 241879, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12014231/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, \"On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]","Francesco Berna et al., \"Microstratigraphic Evidence of in situ Fire in the Acheulean Strata of Wonderwerk Cave, Northern Cape Province, South Africa,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 20 (2012): E1215–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3356665/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]"],"tags":["theory","evolution","prehistory","science","archaeology"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-056","num":56,"category":"The Palaeolithic","question":"Last of the three divisions of the African Stone Age, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from roughly 40,000 years ago into historical times, far outlasting the last Ice Age.","questions":["The names were chosen in 1929 so that the African sequence would not be read as the European one, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not the continent's Upper Palaeolithic.","Small tools are its signature, and in eastern Senegal the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was in use at the same time as Middle Stone Age technology, by separate populations."],"answer":"Late Stone Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 40,000 BP – historical times</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Africa</span></div>","answerText":"Late Stone Age","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Late Stone Age</b> is the last of the three divisions into which the African Stone Age is sorted, running from about 40,000 years ago into historical times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scheme was laid out by John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe in 1929, and its names were chosen so that the African sequence would not be read as the European one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That caution still matters, because this is not the African Upper Palaeolithic: it begins later, lasts very much longer, and ends not with the Ice Age but whenever a given people took up metal or entered the written record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its beginning is usually placed at a strong technological change at Border Cave in KwaZulu-Natal, around 44,000 to 42,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The early phase is informal, mixing prepared cores with bipolar work and small tools, and fully microlithic industries such as the Wilton appear only much later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What its toolkits have in common is smallness: cores worked down to yield flakes, bladelets and blades, with segments dominating and backed bladelets and end-scrapers beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The label is applied right across the continent rather than in the south alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two sites in the Falémé valley of eastern Senegal, dated between about 17,000 and 12,000 years ago, are the oldest such assemblages known in that country and add to a very small number for West Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They also show that the divisions are not clean slices of time, since through the Final Pleistocene there Middle and Late Stone Age technologies were in contemporaneous use by separate populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What people ate can now be read directly from their bones: at Taforalt in Morocco, hunter-gatherers of 15,000 to 13,000 years ago relied heavily on plant foods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Emily Hallinan, “The Southern African Stone Age Site Index (SASSI): A Spatial, Chronological and Contextual Resource for Archaeological Research,” <i>Journal of Open Archaeology Data</i> 14 (2026): 2, under “Overview” and table 1, https://doi.org/10.5334/joad.186. [Open access]","B. D. Malan, “The Term ‘Middle Stone Age’,” in <i>Third Pan-African Congress on Prehistory, Livingstone 1955</i>, ed. J. Desmond Clark and Sonia Cole (London: Chatto and Windus, 1957), 223–27, https://archive.org/details/in.gov.ignca.6340. [Open access]","Paola Villa et al., “Border Cave and the Beginning of the Later Stone Age in South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 33 (2012): 13208–13, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1202629109. [Paywalled]","Zineb Moubtahij et al., “Isotopic Evidence of High Reliance on Plant Food among Later Stone Age Hunter-Gatherers at Taforalt, Morocco,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 8, no. 5 (2024): 1035–45, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11090808/. [Open access]","Matar Ndiaye et al., “Two New Later Stone Age Sites from the Final Pleistocene in the Falémé Valley, Eastern Senegal,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0294346, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10977785/. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","africa","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Pieza_foli%C3%A1cea_africana.jpg","title":"Late Stone Age","desc":"African biface artifact dated in Late Stone Age period. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pieza_foli%C3%A1cea_africana.jpg","alt":"Pieza foliácea africana"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-062","num":62,"category":"The Palaeolithic","question":"Saying nothing about food or shelter and everything about display, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the oldest personal ornaments whose purpose is not in doubt.","questions":["A deliberate hole, and the polish where one shell rubbed against the next on a string, are what separate worn <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from shells that were merely gathered.","At Taforalt in Morocco, pierced and ochre-stained <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about 82,000 years old carry wear showing they had been suspended and worn."],"answer":"shell beads","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Collected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 240,000 – 160,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pierced and worn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 120,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"shell beads","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shell beads</b> are the oldest personal ornaments whose purpose is not in doubt, and their interest is that they say nothing about food or shelter and everything about display.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An ornament only works if it is seen: it must carry a meaning understood between groups, and be worn where others can look at it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What shows that a shell was worn rather than merely gathered is a deliberate perforation together with the wear that stringing leaves behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Misliya Cave in Israel, shells from between about 240,000 and 160,000 years ago carry no trace of human handling at all, so they were collected and not displayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Qafzeh, around 120,000 years ago, the shells are pierced and show fine striations beside the hole and polish where one valve rubbed against the next on a string.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both of those assemblages are of Glycymeris, a bivalve broad enough to be noticed on a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In North Africa the beads are sea snails instead: pierced Nassarius shells from Taforalt in Morocco, dated to roughly 82,000 years ago, worn enough to show they had been suspended and coated in red ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Similar Tritia shells run through the Aterian sites of the Moroccan coast, so the practice is regional rather than one site's peculiarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oldest ornament known to have been buried with a person is a perforated Conus shell laid with an infant of four to six months at Border Cave in South Africa, in layers dated to 74,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later assemblages reach far past shell: the Upper Cave at Zhoukoudian yielded ornaments of badger, fox, deer, marten and tiger teeth, of fish and bird bone, of shell, limestone and a perforated pebble, and three badger canines among them had been sewn onto clothing rather than hung round a neck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Daniella E. Bar-Yosef Mayer et al., “On Holes and Strings: Earliest Displays of Human Adornment in the Middle Palaeolithic,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 7 (2020): e0234924, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0234924. [Open access]","Abdeljalil Bouzouggar, Nick Barton, Marian Vanhaeren, et al., “82,000-Year-Old Shell Beads from North Africa and Implications for the Origins of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 24 (2007): 9964–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1891266/. [Open access]","Francesco d'Errico and Lucinda Backwell, “Earliest Evidence of Personal Ornaments Associated with Burial: The Conus Shells from Border Cave,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 93 (2016): 91–108, esp. 91, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2016.01.002. [Paywalled]","Emilie Campmas, Matthieu Lebon, et al., “Aterian Shell Beads from the Coastal Site of El Mnasra Cave (Rabat-Témara, Morocco): Specificities of the North African MSA Personal Ornaments,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21 (2026): e0338785, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13016355/. [Open access]","Francesco d'Errico et al., “Zhoukoudian Upper Cave Personal Ornaments and Ochre: Rediscovery and Reevaluation,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 161 (2021): 103088, §§1–2, https://shs.hal.science/hal-03445635/document. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","art","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Shell_bead_necklace.webp","title":"shell beads","desc":"This is a reconstruction of a shell bead necklace, including 10 shell beads from Cro-Magnon, France, (bottom part of necklace, in color) and 10 facsimile beads (top part of necklace, in white). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shell_bead_necklace.webp","alt":"Shell bead necklace"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-063","num":63,"category":"The Palaeolithic","question":"An infant of four to six months laid in a cut pit at Border Cave with a pierced shell is the oldest African <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> known to include an ornament.","questions":["At Sungir near Vladimir, some 13,000 mammoth ivory beads went into one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is more than 2,500 hours of work laid in the ground.","Two children at Mal'ta lay in a slab-lined structure floored with red ochre, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> furnished with an ivory tiara and a necklace of 120 bone beads."],"answer":"Paleolithic burial","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Border Cave</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 74,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sungir</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 34,000 cal BP</span></div>","answerText":"Paleolithic burial","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Paleolithic burial</b> is the deliberate interment of a body during the Old Stone Age, and the oldest in Africa known to include an ornament holds an infant of four to six months at Border Cave in South Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pit was cut into Howiesons Poort layers dated to 74,000 years, give or take four thousand, and a perforated Conus shell lay with the child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Far to the north, at Mal'ta beside the Belaya in Siberia, two children were laid in a slab-lined structure whose floor was covered with red ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With them went a tiara of mammoth ivory, a necklace of 120 bone beads, a large oval pendant, a bone plaque cut with a zigzag, and a carving of a bird in flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Goods of that kind could hardly have been the property of a child of three or four, which is the excavator's reason for reading them as given rather than owned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The most elaborate Upper Paleolithic burial yet known is at Sungir near Vladimir in Russia, dated to about 34,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One adult lay beneath some 3,000 mammoth ivory beads, with twelve pierced fox canines across the forehead and 25 ivory arm bands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two others, buried head to head, were adolescents of roughly ten and twelve, and 13,000 ivory beads in all were associated with the interments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is a cost rather than a decoration: the beads alone stand for more than 2,500 hours of work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient DNA has since changed how the group is read, since all of them were genetically male and none was a close relative of any other, so the labour was not spent on a single family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Francesco d'Errico and Lucinda Backwell, “Earliest Evidence of Personal Ornaments Associated with Burial: The Conus Shells from Border Cave,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 93 (2016): 91–108, esp. 91, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2016.01.002. [Paywalled]","Erik Trinkaus and Alexandra P. Buzhilova, “Diversity and Differential Disposal of the Dead at Sunghir,” <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 361 (2018): 7–21, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.223. [Open access]","April Nowell, “Adolescence and Innovation in the European Upper Palaeolithic,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e36, under “Box 1,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427464/. [Open access]","Martin Sikora, Andaine Seguin-Orlando, Vitor C. Sousa, et al., “Ancient Genomes Show Social and Reproductive Behavior of Early Upper Paleolithic Foragers,” <i>Science</i> 358, no. 6363 (2017): 659–62, https://escholarship.org/uc/item/7vx791kv. [Open access]","Liudmila Lbova, “The Siberian Paleolithic Site of Mal'ta: A Unique Source for the Study of Childhood Archaeology,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 3 (2021): e9, under “The Mal'ta burial,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427291/. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","religion","art"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dolni_Vestonice_burial_DV13-DV14-DV15.jpg/1920px-Dolni_Vestonice_burial_DV13-DV14-DV15.jpg","title":"Paleolithic burial","desc":"The triple burial of Dolní Věstonice, Moravia, dated to around 31,000 years before present. From left to right: DV 13, DV 15, DV 14. Alissa Mittnik, Chuan-Chao Wang, Jiří Svoboda, Johannes Krause, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolni_Vestonice_burial_DV13-DV14-DV15.jpg","alt":"Dolni Vestonice burial DV13-DV14-DV15"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-080","num":80,"category":"The Palaeolithic","question":"The securest evidence there is of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a vulture wing bone 21.7 centimetres long with four surviving finger holes, lifted from Hohle Fels.","questions":["The Swabian caves were made a World Heritage property in 2017 for holding the oldest portable art and instruments of humankind, which is where the case for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chiefly rests.","A pierced cave bear thigh bone from Slovenia would push <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back to the Neanderthals, if the holes in it were cut rather than bitten."],"answer":"Paleolithic music","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest secure</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 40,000 – 35,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Swabian Jura, Germany</span></div>","answerText":"Paleolithic music","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"Sound leaves nothing behind, so <b>Paleolithic music</b> is known almost entirely from the instruments that survive, and the oldest accepted without dispute are the flutes of the Swabian Jura in southern Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The best preserved is 21.7 centimetres (8.5 inches) long, cut from the wing bone of a griffon vulture, and was lifted from the cave of Hohle Fels in 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four finger holes survive along it, and the lower end appears to have broken away at a fifth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mouthpiece is a bevelled edge cut into the shaft to leave a notch, which is where the breath is directed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is between 35,000 and 40,000 years old, and came from the same cave and the same years as a small ivory figure of a woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Six caves across two valleys were inscribed together as a World Heritage property in 2017, on the grounds that they hold the oldest portable art and musical instruments of humankind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A much older claim comes from Divje Babe in western Slovenia: the thigh bone of a cave bear cub, about 11 centimetres (4.3 inches) long, pierced by two clean round holes in a line, from a layer of 60,000 to 50,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> If those holes were cut on purpose the instrument is Neanderthal, because in Europe at that date there was nobody else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The objection is that a carnivore's canine punches a neat round hole through thin bone, and one study reads the piece as a bear cub gnawed by spotted hyenas in a cave bear den.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its finders answer that no bite aligns holes down a shaft, the museum in Ljubljana displays it as the oldest musical instrument in the world, and the question is not settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Urgeschichtliches Museum Blaubeuren, “Gänsegeierflöte,” Digitale Sammlung, inv. no. 1977-0119-0089-9000, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.urmu.de/digitale-sammlung/objekt/gaensegeierfloete-1977-0119-0089-9000. [Open access]","Urgeschichtliches Museum Blaubeuren, “Hohle Fels,” Welterbe-Fundstellen, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.urmu.de/welterbe/fundstellen/hohle-fels. [Open access]","Welterbe Höhlen und Eiszeitkunst der Schwäbischen Alb, “UNESCO-Welterbe,” accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.iceageart.de/unesco-welterbe. [Open access]","Matija Turk, Ivan Turk, and Marcel Otte, “The Neanderthal Musical Instrument from Divje Babe I Cave (Slovenia): A Critical Review of the Discussion,” <i>Applied Sciences</i> 10, no. 4 (2020): 1226, https://doi.org/10.3390/app10041226. [Open access]","Cajus G. Diedrich, “‘Neanderthal Bone Flutes’: Simply Products of Ice Age Spotted Hyena Scavenging Activities on Cave Bear Cubs in European Cave Bear Dens,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 2, no. 4 (2015): 140022, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4448875/. [Open access]","National Museum of Slovenia, “Neanderthal Flute,” Collection Highlights, inv. no. D. b. 652, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.nms.si/en/collections/highlights/343-Neanderthal-flute. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","art","archaeology","prehistory"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Divje_Babe_flute_%28Late_Pleistocene_flute%29.jpg/1920px-Divje_Babe_flute_%28Late_Pleistocene_flute%29.jpg","title":"Paleolithic music","desc":"Divje Babe flute (alleged Late Pleistocene flute) dating to 43100 ± 700 BP. National Museum of Slovenia. Measurements: 133.6 mm (5.26 in) long. Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Divje_Babe_flute_(Late_Pleistocene_flute).jpg","alt":"Divje Babe flute (Late Pleistocene flute)"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-086","num":86,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"The case for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made by mitochondrial DNA from 147 people, all of it stemming from one woman who had lived in Africa.","questions":["Where multiregional evolution had Homo erectus becoming modern in place right across the Old World, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has our species arising on one continent and spreading from it.","Neanderthal DNA in everyone descended from those who left the continent is why the strict replacement form of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> no longer stands, though its core does."],"answer":"recent African origin of modern humans","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest fossils</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Jebel Irhoud, Morocco</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Genetic case</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1987</span></div>","answerText":"recent African origin of modern humans","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>recent African origin of modern humans</b> is the model holding that our species arose in Africa and spread out of it, so that every person alive descends from that African population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chief rival was multiregional evolution, in which Homo erectus turned by degrees into archaic and then modern humans in place across the Old World, the regions kept in step by gene flow between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In China that was taken to mean that people living there today trace their ancestry directly back to the Homo erectus of Zhoukoudian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The finding that turned the argument came in 1987: mitochondrial DNA from 147 people, all of it stemming from one woman who is reckoned to have lived about 200,000 years ago, probably in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fossils point the same way, the earliest members of our lineage yet recognised being those from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, dated to about 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What did not survive is the strict form of the model, and the thing that broke it was the draft Neanderthal genome of 2010.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It showed Neanderthals sharing more genetic variants with living Eurasians than with people in sub-Saharan Africa, which points to gene flow from Neanderthals into the ancestors of those who left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Replacement was therefore not total, and the reading now taken as the most economical combines the two models: dispersal out of Africa in several waves, with regular mixing among the smaller populations already in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Africa itself has been rethought as well, because the early fossils show a striking range of forms over a wide spread of country rather than a straight march towards ourselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What has replaced the idea of a single birthplace is one of strongly subdivided populations living across the continent and joined by occasional gene flow, a picture some call African multiregionalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Christopher J. Bae and Xiujie Wu, “Making Sense of Eastern Asian Late Quaternary Hominin Variability,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9479, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11531466/. [Open access]","Rebecca L. Cann, Mark Stoneking, and Allan C. Wilson, “Mitochondrial DNA and Human Evolution,” <i>Nature</i> 325, no. 6099 (1987): 31–36, https://doi.org/10.1038/325031a0. [Paywalled]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, “Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., “Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?,” <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]","Richard E. Green et al., “A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,” <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]"],"tags":["theory","evolution","genetics","palaeontology","prehistory","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Migration_routes_of_modern_humans_%282023%29.png/1920px-Migration_routes_of_modern_humans_%282023%29.png","title":"recent African origin of modern humans","desc":"This map shows the migration routes of modern humans within and Out of Africa, based on archaeogenetic data and environmental factors. The map was designed and created by me, Jayasinghe Sevan, in 2023. Jayasinghe23, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Migration_routes_of_modern_humans_(2023).png","alt":"Migration routes of modern humans (2023)"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-088","num":88,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"Crossing at the Strait of Bab-el-Mandeb and following the shores of Arabia and India eastward is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposes, against a road through Egypt and Sinai.","questions":["Rainfall modelling gives a window near 65,000 years ago, when the northern road closed for some 40,000 years and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reopened for a long spell.","Backed microliths were once offered as its signature and did not hold up, so lithic miniaturization is now proposed instead to track the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> eastward from Africa."],"answer":"southern dispersal route","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Climate window</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 65,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Genetic estimate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">75,000 – 55,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"southern dispersal route","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>southern dispersal route</b> is the proposed path of modern humans out of Africa across the Strait of Bab-el-Mandeb at the foot of the Red Sea, rather than overland by the Nile and the Sinai land bridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two are set against each other as the southern and the northern route, and which of them carried the people who went on to fill Eurasia is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genetics gives the departure a date, putting the migration out of Africa between about 75,000 and 55,000 years ago and the arrival across Eurasia and Australia between 55,000 and 45,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rainfall decides whether either road can be walked at all, and modelling it opens a window near 65,000 years ago, the point after which the northern route shut for some 40,000 years and the southern one reopened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The southern road carries an obstacle the northern one does not, since Bab-el-Mandeb has to be crossed by water, and the archaeological support for anyone doing so is thin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> People had reached the Levant long before, the caves of Skhul and Qafzeh holding our species at roughly 130,000 to 90,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those families left no descendants among us, so the question is not whether anybody went early but which road the later dispersal took, in a picture of several waves that mixed with the populations already in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Stone tools were expected to settle it, and small backed pieces were once put forward as the route's signature, being found in southern Africa and in the Indian subcontinent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That case has not held, because the type was invented repeatedly rather than carried, the ground between the two regions is blank, and east of India it is missing at the dates required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The proposal now is miniaturization itself, a shift to very small tools that can be followed west to east from Panga ya Saidi on the Kenyan coast to Sri Lanka and on to Timor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Robert M. Beyer, Mario Krapp, Anders Eriksson, and Andrea Manica, “Climatic Windows for Human Migration out of Africa in the Past 300,000 Years,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 12 (2021): 4889, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8384873/. [Open access]","Ceri Shipton, “Lithic Miniaturization Provides a Signature of an MIS4-3 Southern Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,” <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 1 (2026): e70027, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12965195/. [Open access]","James Blinkhorn, Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Michael D. Petraglia, and Simon Blockley, “Directional Changes in Levallois Core Technologies between Eastern Africa, Arabia, and the Levant during MIS 5,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 11465, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8169925/. [Open access]","Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Chris Stringer, and Michael D. Petraglia, “Skhul Lithic Technology and the Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i> into Southwest Asia,” <i>Quaternary International</i> 515 (2019): 30–52, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:26de242d-8c79-44a8-9dea-5631a7400664. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Xiujie Wu, “Making Sense of Eastern Asian Late Quaternary Hominin Variability,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9479, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11531466/. [Open access]"],"tags":["theory","migration","archaeology","prehistory","genetics","geography"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"wh-089","num":89,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"The clearest sign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that Neanderthals share more genetic variants with living Eurasians than with people in sub-Saharan Africa, which points to gene flow.","questions":["A girl from Denisova Cave with a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father shows that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran between archaic populations too, not only into our own.","Tibetans carry a version of a hypoxia gene explicable only by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, inherited from Denisovans and helping the body cope with thin mountain air."],"answer":"archaic human admixture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Denisova 11</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 90,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">a first-generation hybrid</span></div>","answerText":"archaic human admixture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Archaic human admixture</b> is the interbreeding between our own species and the older human populations it met on leaving Africa, and it is read not from bones but from the DNA of people alive now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The draft Neanderthal genome of 2010 is where it was first shown, in a pattern nobody had expected: Neanderthals share more genetic variants with living Eurasians than with people in sub-Saharan Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That asymmetry is hard to explain except by gene flow from Neanderthals into the ancestors of those who left the continent, and everyone descended from them carries a per cent or two of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same year a finger bone from Denisova Cave in southern Siberia gave up a genome belonging to a group that shares a common origin with Neanderthals and had never been suspected from any fossil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those Denisovans contributed 4 to 6 per cent of the genetic material of present-day Melanesians, which suggests they were once widespread across Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The two legacies are not spread alike, Neanderthal DNA turning up in everyone outside Africa while the Denisovan share is concentrated far to the east and south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor did the mixing run only into us: a girl from the same Siberian cave had a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father, which is as close to the act as the evidence gets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her parents' populations had separated more than 390,000 years before, so what the cave records is two long-parted lineages meeting again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of what was inherited still works: Tibetans carry a version of a gene governing the response to low oxygen that is found in Denisovans and almost nowhere else, and no explanation but introgression fits it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Denisovans remain the harder group to place, since most of what is assigned to them is isolated teeth or undiagnostic fragments and they have no agreed species name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Richard E. Green et al., “A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,” <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","David Reich et al., “Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Genome of the Offspring of a Neanderthal Mother and a Denisovan Father,” <i>Nature</i> 561, no. 7721 (2018): 113–16, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6130845/. [Open access]","Emilia Huerta-Sánchez et al., “Altitude Adaptation in Tibetans Caused by Introgression of Denisovan-Like DNA,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7513 (2014): 194–97, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4134395/. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, “Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","genetics","evolution","prehistory","palaeontology","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Le_Moustier_skull_in_Berlin_reconstitution.jpg/1920px-Le_Moustier_skull_in_Berlin_reconstitution.jpg","title":"archaic human admixture","desc":"Le Moustier skull in Berlin reconstitution. Gary Todd. 2019-05-12 06:53:46. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Moustier_skull_in_Berlin_reconstitution.jpg","alt":"Le Moustier skull in Berlin reconstitution"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-090","num":90,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"Fragments 30 to 100 base pairs long, chemically damaged and few in number, are what a laboratory has to work with when it recovers <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from bone or sediment.","questions":["The petrous bone is sampled before any other because it gives up to a hundred times more <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than a long bone does, being the densest in the body.","Cytosine deamination, showing as changes at the ends of fragments, is how genuine <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is told apart from the modern kind that contaminates a laboratory."],"answer":"ancient DNA","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First genomes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2010</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Neanderthal and Denisovan</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fragments</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 – 100 base pairs</span></div>","answerText":"ancient DNA","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ancient DNA</b> is genetic material recovered from the remains of the past, and it is the technique behind most of what has lately been learned about prehistoric populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is never a clean sample: it is fragmented, chemically altered and present in very small quantities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bonds of the molecule break by reaction with water, oxygen attacks the bases, and cytosine decays into uracil, so the pieces a laboratory gets are typically 30 to 100 base pairs long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That damage is also the proof, because the decay of cytosine shows up as a distinctive pattern of changes at the ends of each fragment, which is what separates genuinely old material from modern contamination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contamination is the standing danger of the field, met with cleanrooms, bleach and ultraviolet light, dedicated reagents and blank controls; early work on Egyptian mummies was later admitted to have been spoiled by the researchers' own DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Where the sample is taken from matters as much as how it is handled, and the petrous bone behind the ear, the densest in the body, can yield up to a hundred times more human DNA than a long bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method changed with the machines: copying single stretches gave way to sequencing on a massive scale, which can assemble a whole genome out of badly broken material, and newer library preparation recovers the very shortest fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The results reshaped prehistory rather than decorating it, the draft Neanderthal genome of 2010 showing that Neanderthals and our own ancestors had interbred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same year a finger bone from a Siberian cave yielded a genome belonging to a population nobody had suspected, the Denisovans, who are known first from their DNA and only afterwards from their bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its reach is still widening at one end, since DNA can now be read from cave sediments with no skeleton present at all, and hard-stopped at the other, where nothing genetic survives and only ancient proteins still speak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jay Silverstein and Amber Chapman, “Recovery and Analysis of Ancient DNA: Challenges, Methods, and Applications in Forensic and Archaeological Science,” <i>Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology</i> 24, no. 2 (2026): 100702, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13156627/. [Open access]","Richard E. Green et al., “A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,” <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","David Reich et al., “Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Pere Gelabert et al., “A Sedimentary Ancient DNA Perspective on Human and Carnivore Persistence through the Late Pleistocene in El Mirón Cave, Spain,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 107, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11696082/. [Open access]","Frido Welker et al., “The Dental Proteome of <i>Homo antecessor</i>,” <i>Nature</i> 580, no. 7802 (2020): 235–38, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2153-8. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","genetics","science","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Neanderthal_DNA_extraction.jpg/1920px-Neanderthal_DNA_extraction.jpg","title":"ancient DNA","desc":"Working in a clean room, researchers at the Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology in Leipzig, Germany, took extensive precautions to avoid contaminating Neanderthal DNA samples - extracted from bones like this one - with DNA from any other source, including modern humans. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neanderthal_DNA_extraction.jpg","alt":"Neanderthal DNA extraction"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-092","num":92,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"Australia, New Guinea and Tasmania stood as one landmass called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while the sea was low, and it lasted until about nine thousand years ago.","questions":["Drift modelling gives under a five per cent chance of anyone reaching <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by accident, which is why the first voyages there are read as deliberate.","A long chronology putting arrival at 60,000 to 65,000 years ago and a short one at 47,000 to 51,000 are the two positions on when <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reached."],"answer":"Sahul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Arrival debated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">65,000 – 47,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Landmass ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"Sahul","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sahul</b> is the name for the single landmass that New Guinea, Australia and Tasmania formed while the sea stood low, a continent that lasted until roughly 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To its west lay Sunda, the exposed shelf of Southeast Asia, and between the two ran Wallacea, a belt of islands that was never joined to either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sahul could therefore only ever be reached across open water, by a series of crossings through those islands of which at least one approached 100 kilometres (60 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modelling how currents carry a drifting party, and working out which islands can actually be seen from which, puts the chance of arriving there by accident below five per cent unless forty or more adults were swept off at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conclusion drawn is that the voyages were meant, since establishing a population that could survive required repeated and coordinated crossings by hundreds of people within a few centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When this happened is the most contested question in Australian prehistory, and the two positions have names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The long chronology puts the first substantial arrivals at 60,000 to 65,000 years ago, resting on the rock shelter of Madjedbebe in Arnhem Land, where occupation begins about 65,000 years ago with grinding stones, ground ochres and hatchet heads with ground edges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The short chronology puts them at 47,000 to 51,000 instead, on the argument that any age beyond 50,000 for that shelter is unlikely to stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An analysis of 2,456 mitochondrial genomes published in 2025 came down on the longer side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genomes also show the continent was not uniform before it drowned: the ancestors of Papuans and of Aboriginal Australians had diverged from one another 25,000 to 40,000 years ago, while both had parted from Eurasians 51,000 to 72,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Michael I. Bird et al., “Early Human Settlement of Sahul Was Not an Accident,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9 (2019): 8220, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6579762/. [Open access]","Francesca Gandini et al., “Genomic Evidence Supports the ‘Long Chronology’ for the Peopling of Sahul,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11 (2025): eady9493, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12662211/. [Open access]","James F. O'Connell et al., “When Did <i>Homo sapiens</i> First Reach Southeast Asia and Sahul?,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 34 (2018): 8482–90, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1808385115. [Paywalled]","Chris Clarkson et al., “Human Occupation of Northern Australia by 65,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 547, no. 7663 (2017): 306–10, http://faculty.washington.edu/bmarwick/PDFs/Clarkson_Jacobs_Marwick_2017.pdf. [Open access]","Anna-Sapfo Malaspinas et al., “A Genomic History of Aboriginal Australia,” <i>Nature</i> 538, no. 7624 (2016): 207–14, https://www.eva.mpg.de/documents/Nature/Malaspinas_Genomic_Nature_2016_2351078.pdf. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","geography","migration","prehistory","archaeology","genetics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Map_of_Sunda_and_Sahul_2.png","title":"Sahul","desc":"Modified map of Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) Source=Base map derived from File:Karte von Sunda und Sahul.png. Work of Map of Sunda and Sahul and the Wallace Line, the Lydekker Line and the Weber Line. Altaileopard, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Sunda_and_Sahul_2.png","alt":"Map of Sunda and Sahul 2"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-095","num":95,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"The earliest evidence of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is three people from Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, dated between 45,930 and 42,580 years ago and found with Initial Upper Palaeolithic tools.","questions":["Western hunter-gatherers, ancient north Eurasians and early farmers of largely Near Eastern origin are the three populations the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left behind in living genomes.","Corded Ware people in Germany drawing three-quarters of their ancestry from Yamnaya herders is the last great wave of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, about 4,500 years ago."],"answer":"peopling of Europe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First arrivals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 45,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Steppe migration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,500 BP</span></div>","answerText":"peopling of Europe","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>peopling of Europe</b> happened in waves rather than once, and the ancestry of Europeans alive today was laid down over some 45,000 years by populations that arrived long apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Modern humans were present by at least 45,000 years ago, and the Neanderthals they found there had gone by about 40,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest yet recovered are three individuals from Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, dated between 45,930 and 42,580 years ago and lying with an assemblage of Initial Upper Palaeolithic tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not every early arrival has descendants: people of much the same age from Romania and from Siberia contributed nothing detectable to later populations, so those lines simply stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neanderthal ancestry was heavier at the start and has thinned, falling from 3 to 6 per cent in the first Europeans to around 2 per cent now, which is read as selection working against those variants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From roughly 37,000 to 14,000 years ago the sampled dead all descend from a single founder population, and that one does form part of the ancestry of Europeans today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Ice Age pressed those people into refuges, and a man buried in southern Iberia 23,000 years ago links the western European groups living before the glacial maximum with those after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The second layer came with farming, closely related groups of early farmers appearing in Germany, Hungary and Spain between about 8,000 and 7,000 years ago, quite distinct from the hunter-gatherers already in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The third came off the grasslands: around 4,500 years ago the Corded Ware people of Germany drew some three-quarters of their ancestry from the Yamnaya herders of the east, and that steppe ancestry is now everywhere in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The upshot is that most Europeans descend from at least three sharply different populations — western hunter-gatherers, ancient north Eurasians, and early farmers of largely Near Eastern origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mateja Hajdinjak et al., “Initial Upper Palaeolithic Humans in Europe Had Recent Neanderthal Ancestry,” <i>Nature</i> 592, no. 7853 (2021): 253–57, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8026394/. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu et al., “The Genetic History of Ice Age Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 534, no. 7606 (2016): 200–205, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature17993. [Paywalled]","Vanessa Villalba-Mouco et al., “A 23,000-Year-Old Southern Iberian Individual Links Human Groups That Lived in Western Europe before and after the Last Glacial Maximum,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 7 (2023): 597–609, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10089921/. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis et al., “Ancient Human Genomes Suggest Three Ancestral Populations for Present-Day Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 513, no. 7518 (2014): 409–13, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4170574/. [Open access]","Wolfgang Haak et al., “Massive Migration from the Steppe Was a Source for Indo-European Languages in Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 522, no. 7555 (2015): 207–11, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14317. [Paywalled]"],"tags":["concept","migration","genetics","prehistory","archaeology","geography"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/Expansion_of_early_modern_humans_from_Africa.jpg","title":"peopling of Europe","desc":"Maps showing the distribution of Neanderthals and after the dispersal of anatomically modern humans out of Africa, and the locations of major archeological sites for each group. Berria, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expansion_of_early_modern_humans_from_Africa.jpg","alt":"Expansion of early modern humans from Africa"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-109","num":109,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"The earliest post-built structure known in Britain is a platform of large split timbers laid along the lake edge at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in use between about 9300 and 8500 BCE.","questions":["About 90 per cent of all the red deer headdresses known from early prehistoric Europe come from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where 33 have been found and only nine are pierced.","The only engraved pendant known from Mesolithic Britain came out of the lake-edge deposits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 2015, mistaken at first for an ordinary piece of stone."],"answer":"Star Carr","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9300 – 8500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Headdresses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">33, nine of them pierced</span></div>","answerText":"Star Carr","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Star Carr</b> is an Early Mesolithic site on the shore of a vanished lake in North Yorkshire, in use between about 9300 and 8500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a stopping place but one returned to for something like 800 years, which is what makes it worth as much as it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Along the water's edge its people laid a platform of large split timbers, the earliest post-built structures known anywhere in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wood of that age almost never survives, so what is ordinary carpentry elsewhere is here a rare glimpse of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its most striking finds are red deer skulls worked into headdresses, each made from the upper part of a stag's cranium with the antlers left attached, the lower jaw and the surrounding bone cut away and the plate behind pierced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Star Carr holds more than 90 per cent of every such object known from prehistoric Europe, so a whole class of artefact is effectively one site's evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The original excavations yielded 21 and later work recovered twelve more, which makes 33 in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only nine of the 33 are pierced, and the antlers were cut down by grooving and splintering and the remaining beams hollowed out, so these were worked for wearing rather than merely kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 2015 a piece of shale came out of the lake-edge mud that had been taken for a natural stone, its drilled hole packed with sediment and its engraved lines invisible until the mud fell away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sub-triangular, about 31 by 35 millimetres (1.2 by 1.4 inches) and 3 thick, it is the only engraved pendant known from Mesolithic Britain, and no other in Europe is made of shale rather than amber, antler or bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jessica Bates et al., “Spatial Organisation within the Earliest Evidence of Post-Built Structures in Britain,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 7 (2024): e0306908, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11249238/. [Open access]","Aimée Little et al., “Technological Analysis of the World's Earliest Shamanic Costume: A Multi-Scalar, Experimental Study of a Red Deer Headdress from the Early Holocene Site of Star Carr, UK,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0152136, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4830440/. [Open access]","Ben Elliott, Becky Knight, and Aimée Little, “Antler Frontlets,” in <i>Star Carr Volume 2: Studies in Technology, Subsistence and Environment</i>, ed. Nicky Milner, Chantal Conneller, and Barry Taylor (York: White Rose University Press, 2018), 297–333, https://doi.org/10.22599/book2.l. [Open access]","Nicky Milner et al., “A Unique Engraved Shale Pendant from the Site of Star Carr: The Oldest Mesolithic Art in Britain,” <i>Internet Archaeology</i> 40 (2016), https://doi.org/10.11141/ia.40.8. [Open access]","Nicky Milner, Chantal Conneller, and Barry Taylor, eds., <i>Star Carr Volume 1: A Persistent Place in a Changing World</i> (York: White Rose University Press, 2018), https://doi.org/10.22599/book1. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","practice","art","europe"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Star_Carr_headdress_cropped.JPG/1920px-Star_Carr_headdress_cropped.JPG","title":"Star Carr","desc":"Star Carr collection at Yorkshire museum - mesolithic headdress made from deer skull. From the earliest known post glacial settlement in England. Jonathan Cardy, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Star_Carr_headdress_cropped.JPG","alt":"Star Carr headdress cropped"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Star Carr","at":[-0.4233,54.2142]}},
{"id":"wh-111","num":111,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Cultivated crops and domesticated animals rather than wild food alone are what define the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last phase of the Stone Age before metal.","questions":["Because farming was taken up independently in at least eleven separate centres, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no single span and begins thousands of years apart around the world.","Villages of a few hundred growing into settlements of thousands, with land tenure and hardening rank, are how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> changed the way people lived together."],"answer":"Neolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 – 3300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in the Near East; later elsewhere</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neolithic</b>, or New Stone Age, is the last phase of the Stone Age, the one in which people came to live on cultivated crops and domesticated animals rather than on wild food alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Lubbock coined the name in 1865, in a scheme of four ages with no Mesolithic in it, and said plainly at the time that he was applying it to Europe only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Domestication for food began around the world between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago, as the last ice age released its grip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began independently in at least eleven separate centres, each working on the plants and animals it had to hand rather than on any shared stock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The changes did not start with the period itself, since the Epipalaeolithic of 23,000 to 12,000 years ago already shows them gathering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The largest early farming villages held populations in the hundreds, and as the Neolithic wore on settlements in the thousands arose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With that size came land ownership and rights of tenure attached to emerging elites, so rank hardened into something inherited rather than earned within a lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its dead were often laid in graves built of massive stones, whether roofed chambers, passage graves or long galleries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the last third of the 5th millennium BCE those tombs could be opened again, and bodies were added to them across centuries, which makes them monuments to a line rather than to a person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The whole change was called a revolution in 1936, on an analogy with the industrial one, though it is now read as a transition slow enough that nobody living through it need have noticed a thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Dorian Q. Fuller and Chris J. Stevens, “Between Domestication and Civilization: The Role of Agriculture and Arboriculture in the Emergence of the First Urban Societies,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 28, no. 3 (2019): 263–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6499764/. [Open access]","Trevor Watkins, “From Pleistocene to Holocene: The Prehistory of Southwest Asia in Evolutionary Context,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 39, no. 3 (2017): 22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5556129/. [Open access]","Bettina Schulz Paulsson, “Radiocarbon Dates and Bayesian Modeling Support Maritime Diffusion Model for Megaliths in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 9 (2019): 3460–65, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6397522/. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","agriculture","history","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Neolithic_Stone_Spades_%26_Axes.jpg/1920px-Neolithic_Stone_Spades_%26_Axes.jpg","title":"Neolithic","desc":"Xinzheng City Museum, Henan Province, China. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neolithic_Stone_Spades_%26_Axes.jpg","alt":"Neolithic Stone Spades & Axes"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-112","num":112,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Sedentism, cemeteries and architecture appear in the Levant before farming does, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is better described as a transition than an event.","questions":["Happening independently in at least eleven separate centres, each with its own plants and animals, is what makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a repeated event rather than a single one.","A livestock disease splitting from its cattle-infecting relative about 9,800 years ago is among the prices of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, paid in penned animals and crowded settlements."],"answer":"Neolithic Revolution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Transition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 10,000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Centres</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 11, independent</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic Revolution","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neolithic Revolution</b> is the shift from hunting and gathering to farming and settled village life, a social, economic and technological transformation rather than merely a change of diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Settling down came first, though: the Natufian of the southern Levant, between about 14,500 and 11,600 years ago, already had sedentism, cemeteries and architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had begun on animals too, since the Natufian already shows food production including animal domestication, and at 'Ain Mallaha an adult woman was buried with a juvenile domestic dog, with a second such burial from Hayonim Terrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The traits we file under the word Neolithic emerged and developed across the Epipalaeolithic, between 23,000 and 12,000 years ago, instead of arriving with the crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cultivation for food followed between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago, as the last ice age let go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It then happened again and again, independently, in at least eleven separate centres, each working the plants and animals of its own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it took hold the settlements grew, the largest early farming villages holding populations in the hundreds across the whole Old World, and later ones running into the thousands as the period wore on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Land ownership and rights of tenure came with them, attached to emerging elites, so that rank began to be inherited rather than won inside a single life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There was a price, and part of it was disease: the bacterium behind brucellosis in sheep and goats split from its cattle-infecting relative about 9,800 years ago, exactly as livestock economies were consolidating and spreading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The settlement itself supplied the mechanism, since penning and confinement, thin hygiene among new herders, denser herds and the mixing of species that had never met all favour a pathogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Lisa A. Maher et al., “A Unique Human-Fox Burial from a Pre-Natufian Cemetery in the Levant (Jordan),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 1 (2011): e15815, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3027631/. [Open access]","Dorian Q. Fuller and Chris J. Stevens, “Between Domestication and Civilization: The Role of Agriculture and Arboriculture in the Emergence of the First Urban Societies,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 28, no. 3 (2019): 263–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6499764/. [Open access]","Trevor Watkins, “From Pleistocene to Holocene: The Prehistory of Southwest Asia in Evolutionary Context,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 39, no. 3 (2017): 22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5556129/. [Open access]","Louis L'Hôte et al., “An 8000 Years Old Genome Reveals the Neolithic Origin of the Zoonosis <i>Brucella melitensis</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 6132, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11271283/. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","prehistory","agriculture","archaeology","history","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Egyptian_Domesticated_Animals.jpg/1920px-Egyptian_Domesticated_Animals.jpg","title":"Neolithic Revolution","desc":"Old Egyptian hieroglyphic painting showing an early instance of a domesticated animal (cow being milked). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_Domesticated_Animals.jpg","alt":"Egyptian Domesticated Animals"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"wh-115","num":115,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Curving from the Mediterranean round to the Persian Gulf, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a cultivable fringe with mountains on one side and desert on the other.","questions":["Ancient genomes show the first farmers of the southern Levant and of the Zagros each descended from local hunter-gatherers, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took up farming from within.","The local Epipalaeolithic runs from about 23,000 years ago, so people had been living in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for ten millennia before anyone there planted a crop."],"answer":"Fertile Crescent","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Farming begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 – 11,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">and more than once within it</span></div>","answerText":"Fertile Crescent","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Fertile Crescent</b> is the cultivable fringe of the western Asian desert, a borderland between desert and mountains with the mountains on one side of it and the dry country on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs as an approximate semicircle, its western end at the southeast corner of the Mediterranean, its centre directly north of Arabia and its eastern end at the head of the Persian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a modern invention made because there was no other: no geographical or political term covered the whole semicircle, so one was coined for it, lower-case in 1914 and capitalised as a proper name two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> People had been living there a very long time before they farmed it, the Epipalaeolithic of the southern Levant running from roughly 23,000 to 11,600 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its final phase, the Natufian of about 14,500 to 11,600 years ago, already had sedentism, cemeteries and architecture, so the settling came before the sowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Domestication for food then began between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago, as the last ice age released its hold on the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> This was one of at least eleven separate centres where that happened independently, so the crescent is a first among several rather than the single place farming was invented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient genomes have since been read from 44 people of the region spanning roughly 12,000 to 1,400 BCE, running from Natufian hunter-gatherers through to farmers of the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They show that the first farmers of the southern Levant and the first farmers of the Zagros mountains in Iran each descended from the hunter-gatherers of their own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Farming here was therefore taken up by the people already living in the land rather than carried in by newcomers, and taken up more than once inside the crescent itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["James Henry Breasted and James Harvey Robinson, <i>Outlines of European History, Part I</i> (Boston: Ginn, 1914), 56–57, https://archive.org/details/outlinesofeurope01robi. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Times: A History of the Early World</i> (Boston: Ginn, 1916), 100–101, https://archive.org/details/ancienttimeshist00brea_2. [Open access]","Lisa A. Maher et al., “A Unique Human-Fox Burial from a Pre-Natufian Cemetery in the Levant (Jordan),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 1 (2011): e15815, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3027631/. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1323964111. [Paywalled]","Iosif Lazaridis et al., “Genomic Insights into the Origin of Farming in the Ancient Near East,” <i>Nature</i> 536, no. 7617 (2016): 419–24, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature19310. [Paywalled]"],"tags":["place","geography","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/NLI_Quarta_Asie_tabula.jpg/1920px-NLI_Quarta_Asie_tabula.jpg","title":"Fertile Crescent","desc":"Map of the levant by Ptolemy, 1486 reproduction by Johann Reger. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NLI_Quarta_Asie_tabula.jpg","alt":"NLI Quarta Asie tabula"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Fertile Crescent","at":[40,36],"kind":"region","area":[[34.2,31.3],[35,33.5],[36,36],[38.5,37.4],[41.5,37.6],[44.5,36.5],[46.5,33.5],[48.3,30.4],[47.3,30.3],[45.3,33.3],[43.3,35.5],[40.5,36.3],[38,36.2],[36.6,34.5],[36,32],[35.4,31.2]]}},
{"id":"wh-116","num":116,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"People of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built semi-subterranean round houses of undressed limestone into the slope at 'Ain Mallaha, where 50 to 100 of them lived for 3,000 years.","questions":["Use-wear on flint sickle blades shows the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> harvesting cereals through a long spring, some 4,500 years before any of those cereals was domesticated.","Settling down came before farming, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the phase in which it happened: cemeteries, architecture and stone houses, with the crops still wild."],"answer":"Natufian culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Culture</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,500 – 11,600 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ain Mallaha</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 15,000 – 12,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"Natufian culture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Natufian culture</b> is the last phase of the Epipalaeolithic in the Levant, running from about 14,500 to 11,600 years ago, and it is where people settled down before they took up farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is read as a transitional entity, marking the beginning of the change away from the mobile foraging bands of the Palaeolithic, and one study brackets it slightly wider, at 15,000 to 11,650 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The clearest of its villages is 'Ain Mallaha in the north of what is now Israel, occupied for some 3,000 years between roughly 15,000 and 12,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It covered about 2,000 square metres (half an acre) and is estimated to have held between 50 and 100 people at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its houses were semi-subterranean and curved rather than square, made of undressed limestone, cut back into the slope with retaining walls to hold the hillside off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> They were reaping cereals long before anyone had changed them, since wear on the flint blades of their sickles, from el-Wad Terrace and from Salibiya I, shows the cutting of grain both semi-ripe and ripe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pattern is of a protracted harvest beginning in mid-spring and running to the very end of it, which is a season worked through rather than a windfall gathered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is cultivation roughly 4,500 years before those same cereals were domesticated, so the crops were being tended long before they were altered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Staying put brought the rest with it: cemeteries among the houses, architecture worth maintaining, and a beginning made on the domestication of animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor did it end when the period did, because at 'Ain Mallaha the occupation runs on unbroken, with settlement deepening and finds carrying over from the late Natufian into the Neolithic that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lisa A. Maher et al., “A Unique Human-Fox Burial from a Pre-Natufian Cemetery in the Levant (Jordan),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 1 (2011): e15815, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3027631/. [Open access]","Laurent Davin et al., “Modeling Identities among the First-Sedentary Communities: Emergence of Clay Personal Ornaments in Epipaleolithic Southwest Asia,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12 (2026): eaea2158, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12998513/. [Open access]","Gil Haklay and Avi Gopher, “A New Look at Shelter 131/51 in the Natufian Site of Eynan (Ain-Mallaha), Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 7 (2015): e0130121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4496039/. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]","Jonathan Santana et al., “Multi-Isotope Evidence of Population Aggregation in the Natufian and Scant Migration during the Early Neolithic of the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 11857, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8178372/. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","agriculture","history","practice"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Basalt_%26_Limestone_Mortar_%26_Pestle%2C_Natufian_Culture.jpg/1920px-Basalt_%26_Limestone_Mortar_%26_Pestle%2C_Natufian_Culture.jpg","title":"Natufian culture","desc":"Basalt & Limestone Mortar & Pestle, Natufian Culture Israel Museum, Jerusalem, Israel. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basalt_%26_Limestone_Mortar_%26_Pestle,_Natufian_Culture.jpg","alt":"Basalt & Limestone Mortar & Pestle, Natufian Culture"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-117","num":117,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Tameness, coat colour changes, floppy ears, smaller teeth and smaller brains are the suite of traits <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leaves on a mammal, whatever the species.","questions":["Cereals were reaped for some 4,500 years before they changed, which is the clearest sign that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a protracted process rather than a decision anyone took.","Loss of seed shattering, larger seeds, less branching and reduced dormancy make up the plant side of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and crops from unrelated centres converge on it."],"answer":"domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">For food, from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 – 11,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plant species</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000 – 2,500, in 120 – 160 families</span></div>","answerText":"domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Domestication</b> is the process by which a wild species is reshaped under human keeping, and the first thing to understand about it is that it is typically protracted, shaped more by unconscious selection than by any intentional breeding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nobody set out to do it, in other words, and the gap between using a species and changing it can be enormous: Natufian sickles were reaping wild cereals some 4,500 years before those grains were domesticated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Domestication for food began between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago, as the last ice age ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began independently in at least eleven separate centres, each working on whatever grew or grazed nearby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 1,000 and 2,500 plant species have been at least partly domesticated since, drawn from something like 120 to 160 plant families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What makes it a single subject is that it leaves the same marks on unrelated creatures, a pattern noticed by Darwin and still called the domestication syndrome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In mammals that means tameness and docility, changes of coat colour, smaller teeth, altered skull shape, floppy ears and tails, breeding out of season, juvenile behaviour carried into adulthood, and a reduction in brain size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parts of the same suite turn up in domesticated birds and fish, which is why it is read as a property of the process rather than of any one lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plants have their own version of it: loss of seed shattering, larger seeds, less branching, reduced dormancy and shifted flowering time, all of which make a crop easier to harvest and useless at sowing itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Crops from centres with no contact between them converge on that suite, often through the very same genes, and what began in these fields ended in the first cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Adam S. Wilkins, Richard W. Wrangham, and W. Tecumseh Fitch, “The ‘Domestication Syndrome’ in Mammals: A Unified Explanation Based on Neural Crest Cell Behavior and Genetics,” <i>Genetics</i> 197, no. 3 (2014): 795–808, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4096361/. [Open access]","Yu Cao, Jianbing Yan, Jeffrey Ross-Ibarra, and Ning Yang, “Plant Domestication Revisited: Genomic Insights into Origins, Mechanisms, and Convergent Evolution,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 1 (2026): 114062, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12799793/. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1323964111. [Paywalled]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]","Dorian Q. Fuller and Chris J. Stevens, “Between Domestication and Civilization: The Role of Agriculture and Arboriculture in the Emergence of the First Urban Societies,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 28, no. 3 (2019): 263–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6499764/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","agriculture","prehistory","evolution","science","nature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Wheat_domestication_illustrated_-_Vavilov_Institute_of_Plant_Industry.jpg","title":"domestication","desc":"Wheat domestication illustrated. Luigi Guarino from Rome, Italy, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wheat_domestication_illustrated_-_Vavilov_Institute_of_Plant_Industry.jpg","alt":"Wheat domestication illustrated - Vavilov Institute of Plant Industry"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"wh-118","num":118,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Cereals, winter pulses and one oil plant travelled together out of the Fertile Crescent as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, rather than spreading one species at a time.","questions":["Einkorn and emmer wheat most likely come from the Karaca Dağ range in southeast Turkey, which is one corner of where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were first brought under cultivation.","Flax is the odd member of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, being grown for oil and fibre rather than for food, and taken first for the oil."],"answer":"Neolithic founder crops","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in and around the Fertile Crescent</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic founder crops","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neolithic founder crops</b> are the small group of plants first brought under cultivation in and around the Fertile Crescent, and what makes them a group rather than a list is that they travelled as a package, turning up together wherever farming spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cereals are its core: by the early Neolithic, about 10,000 years ago, three wheats had been domesticated there, the diploid einkorn, the tetraploid emmer and hexaploid bread wheat, with barley beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Einkorn and emmer most likely came from the Karaca Dağ range in what is now southeast Turkey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Barley may have more than one origin, with centres both in the Fertile Crescent and on the Iranian plateau, and emmer may have been domesticated a second time in the southern Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The package is therefore not one event in one valley but a set of overlapping ones across a wide arc of country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Pulses went with the grain, lentils and peas making up the winter half of the package as it moved east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pea has two wild relatives, one spread across the Mediterranean and one confined to the Levant, so even a single crop can carry a complicated parentage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Flax is the odd member, grown not for food but for oil and fibre, and domesticated about 10,000 years ago from pale flax, its wild progenitor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The genetic work suggests it was taken first for the oil rather than for the thread, which is the reverse of what the plant is now known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None of it appeared from nothing, since the wild cereals of the same country had been reaped with flint sickles for roughly 4,500 years before any of them was altered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Yu Cao, Jianbing Yan, Jeffrey Ross-Ibarra, and Ning Yang, “Plant Domestication Revisited: Genomic Insights into Origins, Mechanisms, and Convergent Evolution,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 1 (2026): 114062, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12799793/. [Open access]","Jonas Gregorio de Souza, Javier Ruiz-Pérez, Carla Lancelotti, and Marco Madella, “Environmental Effects on the Spread of the Neolithic Crop Package to South Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 7 (2022): e0268482, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9273075/. [Open access]","Yong-Bi Fu, “Flax Domestication Processes as Inferred from Genome-Wide SNP Data,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 8731, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11906640/. [Open access]","Oldřich Trněný et al., “Molecular Evidence for Two Domestication Events in the Pea Crop,” <i>Genes</i> 9, no. 11 (2018): 535, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6265838/. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","nature","history"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Triticum_monococcum_illustration_%2801f%29.png/1920px-Triticum_monococcum_illustration_%2801f%29.png","title":"Neolithic founder crops","desc":"Triticum monococcum (Einkorn wheat) from Icones et descriptiones Graminum austriacorum Author: Host, Nikolaus Thomas Publisher: Vindobonae : A. Schmidt, 1801-1809. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Triticum_monococcum_illustration_(01f).png","alt":"Triticum monococcum illustration (01f)"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-119","num":119,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Wild wheat and barley break up along the spike and scatter their grain, so the first thing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to undo was the plant's own way of sowing itself.","questions":["Two genes called Brittle rachis 1 and 2 were the target of the mutations that made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possible, and they are found only in the wheat and barley tribe.","One region does not fit the slow picture of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since in southeast Anatolia the wheat appears to have changed faster than that model predicts."],"answer":"cereal domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Key genes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Brittle rachis 1 and 2</span></div>","answerText":"cereal domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cereal domestication</b> turned plants that scatter their own seed into plants that hold it, and the loss of that natural mode of grain dispersal is perhaps the single most critical event in the whole process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A wild wheat or barley breaks up progressively along the spike as it ripens and dries, dropping its grain on the ground where the next generation can grow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two genes govern that shattering in the wild progenitors, Brittle rachis 1 and Brittle rachis 2, and both were the target of the mutations that domesticated the crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are found only in the tribe that contains wheat and barley, and are thought to have arisen by a gene duplication whose copy then took on a new job.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tough spike sits alongside larger seeds and reduced dormancy in the plant side of the domestication syndrome, and it leaves the crop unable to sow itself at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Harvesting is what did the selecting, and it had been going on a long time, flint sickles reaping wild cereals for some 4,500 years before any of them changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The usual picture is accordingly a slow one, shaped more by unconscious selection than by any deliberate breeding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where it happened is plural too, einkorn and emmer pointing to the Karaca Dağ range in southeast Turkey while barley may have a second origin on the Iranian plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One region does not fit the slow picture: grain measurements at an early Neolithic site in southeast Anatolia, set beside the plant remains and the dates, suggest wheat there changed faster, with no long stage of cultivating it wild beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The explanation offered is a social one, that cereals were mattering more at feasts in settlements whose architecture grew steadily more elaborate across the 11th millennium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mohammad Pourkheirandish and Takao Komatsuda, “Grain Disarticulation in Wild Wheat and Barley,” <i>Plant and Cell Physiology</i> 63, no. 11 (2022): 1584–91, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9680857/. [Open access]","Yu Cao, Jianbing Yan, Jeffrey Ross-Ibarra, and Ning Yang, “Plant Domestication Revisited: Genomic Insights into Origins, Mechanisms, and Convergent Evolution,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 1 (2026): 114062, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12799793/. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]","Ceren Kabukcu, Eleni Asouti, Nadja Pöllath, Joris Peters, and Necmi Karul, “Pathways to Plant Domestication in Southeast Anatolia Based on New Data from Aceramic Neolithic Gusir Höyük,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11, no. 1 (2021): 2112, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7822836/. [Open access]","Jonas Gregorio de Souza, Javier Ruiz-Pérez, Carla Lancelotti, and Marco Madella, “Environmental Effects on the Spread of the Neolithic Crop Package to South Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 7 (2022): e0268482, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9273075/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","science","nature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Various_grains.jpg/1920px-Various_grains.jpg","title":"cereal domestication","desc":"Oats, barley, and some products made from them. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Various_grains.jpg","alt":"Various grains"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"wh-120","num":120,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Because a herder shows up in the age and sex of the animals killed rather than in the shape of their bones, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is hard to date from a skeleton alone.","questions":["The oldest of the three independent cradles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Southwest Asia, where sheep, goats, pigs and cattle all followed the cereals and pulses into settled farming villages.","Pigs on the Lower Yangtze were eating cooked food and human waste, a commensal route into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in which the animal moves towards the settlement rather than being driven in."],"answer":"animal domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Livestock</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,500 – 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Llama, alpaca</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 – 8,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Horse, camel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,000 – 2,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"animal domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Animal domestication</b> is a coevolutionary relationship rather than a single act, and the globally important livestock were taken up inside settled farming communities, in three independent cradles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Southwest Asia is the oldest: sheep, goats, pigs and cattle all followed the cereals and pulses there, between about 10,500 and 10,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cattle descend from the aurochs, a wild ox now extinct, in two lineages: the taurine cattle of Southwest Asia and the humped zebu traced to a South Asian population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> China is the second cradle, where pigs followed millet and rice, and the Andes the third, where llamas and alpacas were taken between 9,000 and 8,000 years ago, with the turkey added in North America later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A further wave was about muscle rather than meat, the donkey, horse and camel arriving between 5,000 and 2,000 years ago and transforming transport, trade and the scale of warfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Dating the start is hard, because a herder shows up first in the age and sex of the animals killed rather than in the shape of their bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Ganj Dareh in the Zagros only about a fifth of the goats lived past four years, and 60 to 70 per cent of males were killed before two and a half while as many females survived beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genomes from those animals are already distinct from wild goats by around 8200 BCE, the oldest livestock genomes yet recovered, and visible change in the skeleton follows roughly a thousand years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aşıklı Höyük in central Anatolia gives the same lesson from the other end: a millennium of managing sheep and goats, from catching wild lambs and kids to grow on through to large captive herds, produced no reduction in body size at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor was there one route in, since the pigs at two early sites on the Lower Yangtze were eating cooked food and human waste, a commensal path along which the animal moves towards the settlement rather than being driven in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Thomas Cucchi and Benjamin Arbuckle, “Animal Domestication: From Distant Past to Current Development and Issues,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 6–9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214435/. [Open access]","Mary C. Stiner, Natalie D. Munro, Hijlke Buitenhuis, Güneş Duru, and Mihriban Özbaşaran, “An Endemic Pathway to Sheep and Goat Domestication at Aşıklı Höyük (Central Anatolia, Turkey),” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 4 (2022): e2110930119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8795544/. [Open access]","Kevin G. Daly et al., “Herded and Hunted Goat Genomes from the Dawn of Domestication in the Zagros Mountains,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 25 (2021): e2100901118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8237664/. [Open access]","Benjamin S. Arbuckle and Theo M. Kassebaum, “Management and Domestication of Cattle (<i>Bos taurus</i>) in Neolithic Southwest Asia,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 10–19, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214434/. [Open access]","Jiajing Wang et al., “Early Evidence for Pig Domestication (8,000 cal. BP) in the Lower Yangtze, South China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 24 (2025): e2507123122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12184640/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","nature","history"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Haustiere_III_in_Brockhaus_1937.jpg","title":"animal domestication","desc":"Haustiere III in Brockhaus 1937. scan: Julo. 1937, scan: 2010. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haustiere_III_in_Brockhaus_1937.jpg","alt":"Haustiere III in Brockhaus 1937"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"gr-216","num":216,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Settlers from Phocaea founded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about 600 BCE, and the cults gathered there point back to several places in Ionia rather than to their own mother city alone.","questions":["Two boats built about 550 BCE were left on the shore at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one hull sewn together and the other joined by mortise and tenon.","Pytheas, who sailed north as far as Britain and wrote a lost book <i>On the Ocean</i>, was a citizen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Greek window on the Gallic world."],"answer":"Massalia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First houses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 – 560 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fell to Caesar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">49 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Massalia</b> is the Greek city on the site of Marseille, founded about 600 BCE by settlers from Phocaea in Ionia, and the most important Greek city of the far west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its character was Ionian before it was Phocaean: the cults gathered there point back to particular places in Ionia — Athena from Phocaea, Apollo Delphinios from Miletus, Artemis of Ephesus — and Phocaean traits of its own surface only in the second half of the 6th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not one of its temples has been found, so the sanctuaries are placed only from what Strabo and Justin wrote long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town lay on a strip of land between a sheltered inlet and three hills, the highest 42 metres (140 feet) above the sea and probably the acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its foundation story has been told so often that scholars now say it has blinded the historiography of the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It lived by trade, the Greek window on the Gallic world and a port for tin, slaves and wine, one of a chain of Phocaean stations reaching Gallic and Tartessian resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Wine and the vessels that came with it changed the peoples inland, who had a hand in the change themselves, taking imported and hybrid wares into pottery of their own in shapes that suited existing habits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor was it the territory-less trading post it was long taken for: vineyards were laid out east of the city on a planned system, one at Saint-Jean-du-Désert about 5 kilometres (3 miles) from the old harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The harbour had a quay by the 6th century BCE, and two boats built about the middle of it were left on the shore, one hull sewn and the other joined by mortise and tenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle thought the place worth a <i>Constitution of the Massaliots</i>, now lost, the city kept a treasury at Delphi, and its citizen Pytheas sailed north to Britain and beyond, writing a lost book <i>On the Ocean</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Massalia","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","france","geography"],"sources":["Christine Davidson, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World. Vol. IV: Cyrene to Metapontion</i>, ed. Paul Cartledge and Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.03.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.03.24/. [Open access]","Ulrike Krotscheck, review of <i>Greek Marseille and Mediterranean Celtic Region</i>, ed. Sophie Bouffier and Dominique Garcia, H-France Review 18, no. 204 (2018), https://h-france.net/vol18reviews/vol18no204krotscheck.pdf. [Open access]","Institut national de recherches archéologiques préventives, “L’Antiquité grecque à Marseille,” Atlas archéologique de Marseille, https://multimedia.inrap.fr/atlas/marseille/synthese-periodes/antiquite-grecque. [Open access]","Timothy Teeter, review of <i>Ancient Greek France</i>, by A. Trevor Hodge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.11.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.11.17/. [Open access]","Emma Buckingham, review of <i>Greek Colonization in Local Contexts: Case Studies in Colonial Interactions</i>, ed. Jason Lucas, Carrie Ann Murray and Sara Owen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.10.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.10.57/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas</i>, vol. 1, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.38/. [Open access]","Jerker Blomqvist, review of <i>Pythéas: Explorateur du Grand Nord</i>, by François Herbaux, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.01.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.01.38/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Marseille_-_Le_jardin_des_vestiges.JPG/1920px-Marseille_-_Le_jardin_des_vestiges.JPG","title":"Massalia","desc":"Le jardin des vestiges de Marseille. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marseille_-_Le_jardin_des_vestiges.JPG","alt":"Marseille - Le jardin des vestiges"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Massalia","at":[5.37,43.2964]}},
{"id":"gr-217","num":217,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Strabo says the Greeks and the Iberians at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to share one walled town divided by an inner wall, under a legal code made of barbarian and Greek laws.","questions":["A lead sheet from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, written in Ionian Greek about 500 BCE, sets out a shipping deal a Phocaean merchant was handing to his agent there.","The Greeks at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began on an island the residents later called the old city, and moved across to the mainland after the middle of the 6th century BCE."],"answer":"Emporion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 575 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Neapolis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman landing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Emporion</b> is the Greek trading town on the Bay of Roses in Catalonia, the most important Greek settlement on the Mediterranean coast of Iberia and the best known of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phocaean merchants working west out of Massalia settled there for good in the second quarter of the 6th century BCE, on a promontory local people had held since the late Bronze Age, where Phoenician goods had been arriving for a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo calls that first site the Palaia Polis, +m[1]+, and after mid-century a second nucleus grew on the mainland across the port hollow, now called the Neapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town stayed small — barely three or four hectares (7 to 10 acres) — and commercial, though it struck its own silver from the 5th century BCE, in fractions stamped +m[1]+ or +m[1]+.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even a rival Greek port lived off Etruscan goods: its 6th- and 5th-century houses gave up more Etruscan amphorae and bucchero than anyone expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo also describes an Iberian settlement beside the Greek one, later taken into a single town divided by an inner wall, under laws made of barbarian and Greek together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lead sheet from the Neapolis, in Ionian Greek of about 500 BCE, sets out a shipping deal a Phocaean merchant handed to his agent, part of it to be done near Sagunt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Iberian names appear among the parties, and Iberian sgraffiti on pots and lead run through the town, so its inscriptions show Greeks and Iberians cooperating far more than the Roman-period writers do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It fed Attic pottery to the Iberian interior, though the Iberians may have decided who received it, and it was one of a string of small coastal ports rather than a bridgehead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its cemeteries, the one Phocaean case where burials survive in quantity, keep Greek and Iberian apart where the houses do not, and in 218 BCE its harbour was where a Roman army first landed in Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Emporion","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","spain","geography"],"sources":["Maite Miró and Marta Santos, “The Greek Presence on the East Coast of the Iberian Peninsula: Colonial Establishments and Rhythms of Trade with Iberian Societies,” <i>Catalan Historical Review</i> 7 (2014): 9–28, https://doi.org/10.2436/20.1000.01.95. [Open access]","Generalitat de Catalunya, “Empúries,” Cultural Heritage, https://patrimoni.gencat.cat/en/collection/empuries. [Open access]","Claudio Vacanti, review of <i>The Emporion in the Ancient Western Mediterranean: Trade and Colonial Encounters from the Archaic to the Hellenistic Period</i>, ed. Éric Gailledrat, Michael Dietler and Rosa Plana-Mallart, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.09.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.09.45/. [Open access]","Andy Fear, review of <i>Colonial Encounters in Ancient Iberia: Phoenician, Greek, and Indigenous Relations</i>, ed. Michael Dietler and Carolina López-Ruiz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.05.43/. [Open access]","Jean MacIntosh Turfa, review of <i>Gli Etruschi da Genova ad Ampurias</i>, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.05.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.05.09/. [Open access]","Michael Eisman, review of <i>Greek Identity in the Western Mediterranean</i>, ed. Kathryn Lomas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.36/. [Open access]","Emma Buckingham, review of <i>Greek Colonization in Local Contexts: Case Studies in Colonial Interactions</i>, ed. Jason Lucas, Carrie Ann Murray and Sara Owen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.10.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.10.57/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Greek_mosiac_at_Emp%C3%BAries.jpg/1920px-Greek_mosiac_at_Emp%C3%BAries.jpg","title":"Emporion","desc":"Ηδύκοιτος--\"The pleasure of lying down\"--can be seen at the top of the photograph. AugusteBlanqui, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek_mosiac_at_Emp%C3%BAries.jpg","alt":"Greek mosiac at Empúries"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Emporion","at":[3.1206,42.1347]}},
{"id":"gr-218","num":218,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The literary and the archaeological dates for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disagree by decades, Eusebius putting the first settlement on Berezan in 647 BCE where the earliest strata are of the last quarter of that century.","questions":["Miletus planted most of the cities of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the exception being Mesambria, the one Dorian polis on those shores, settled from Megara, Byzantium and Chalcedon.","The earliest material for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is very thin, about ten sherds for the second quarter of the 7th century BCE and ten more for the third."],"answer":"Black Sea colonisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Colonised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th – 6th centuries BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Berezan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">647 BCE in Eusebius, c. 625 by finds</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bosporan kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 5th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Black Sea colonisation</b> is the planting of Greek settlements round the Pontos, mostly in the 7th and 6th centuries BCE, from Apollonia and Histria in the west to Phasis and Dioscurias in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miletus founded most of them, the exception being Mesambria, the one Dorian polis on those shores, settled from Megara, Byzantium and Chalcedon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The written sources are thin and mostly much later — Strabo, Arrian, Pseudo-Scylax, Stephanus of Byzantium — and they tell us as much about the world that wrote them as about the foundations they report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pseudo-Scymnus has Sinope founded by Thessalian heroes in the 8th century BCE and taken over by Milesians afterwards, which is the shape a good many of these traditions have.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Berezan, ancient Borysthenes, between the mouths of the Dnieper and the Bug, was probably the first, and Eusebius dates it to 647 BCE where its earliest houses and graves are of the last quarter of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The material from before that is very thin, about ten sherds for the second quarter of the 7th century and ten more for the third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the last quarter pottery is plentiful at Berezan, at least three permanent settlements stood on the northern shore, and their people lived at first in dugouts and half-sunken huts before building above ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land was not empty: hand-made pottery shows people at Berezan before the Greeks came, Greek pots travelled inland to the hill fort of Nemirov, and on the Taman peninsula Maeotian groups from the Kuban camped beside a Greek settlement at harvest and worked its fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the settlers were after is argued over, the older answer being trade and the newer one land and population pressure at home, though neither alone covers the range of motives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the 5th century BCE the Bosporan kingdom grew up around Panticapaeum and its grain became vital to Athens, while Berezan itself declined as Olbia took the lead in its own region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Black Sea colonisation","tags":["event","practice","history","greece","archaic","migration"],"sources":["Balbina Baebler, review of <i>Ancient Greek Colonies in the Black Sea</i>, ed. Dimitrios V. Grammenos and Elias K. Petropoulos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.09.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.09.01/. [Open access]","Balbina Baebler, review of <i>Ancient Berezan: The Architecture, History and Culture of the First Greek Colony in the Northern Black Sea</i>, by S. L. Solovyov, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.06.01/. [Open access]","D. Erciyas, review of <i>Hellenic Colonization in Euxeinos Pontos: Penetration, Early Establishment and the Problem of the “Emporion” Revisited</i>, by E. K. Petropoulos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.05.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.05.24/. [Open access]","Dominik Delp, review of <i>Comparing Greek Colonies: Mobility and Settlement Consolidation from Southern Italy to the Black Sea (8th–6th Century BC)</i>, ed. Camilla Colombi and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.06.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.06.38/. [Open access]","Sara Owen, review of <i>The Greek Colonisation of the Black Sea Area: Historical Interpretation of Archaeology</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.02.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.02.06/. [Open access]","Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, review of <i>Ancient Sacral Monuments in the Black Sea</i>, ed. Elias K. Petropoulos and Alexander A. Maslennikov, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/PontosGreekColonies.jpg","title":"Black Sea colonisation","desc":"Colonisation grecque antique en Mer Noire. Spiridon Ion Cepleanu, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PontosGreekColonies.jpg","alt":"PontosGreekColonies"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-219","num":219,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The earliest coins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show a cow stepping over a dolphin, read as its claim to be where Io crossed the strait, a claim Chalcedon opposite made for itself.","questions":["A tenth on shipping was levied at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the 430s BCE, broke off when the city revolted against Athens in 411, and was reimposed by Alcibiades across the water in 409.","Currents and winds let ships through the Bosporus only at certain times, so they gathered in convoys near <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose wealth came of standing where they had to wait."],"answer":"Byzantium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE, from Megara</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First coins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 5th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rhodian war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">220 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Refounded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 May 330 CE, by Constantine</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Byzantium</b> is the Greek city on the European side of the Bosporus, founded from Megara in the 7th century BCE and refounded by Constantine as his capital nearly a thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Megara and Miletus appear to have colonised the Propontis and the Pontos together, since given where their foundations lie neither set of cities could have worked on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Byzantium became a mother city in its turn, joining Megara and Chalcedon in settling Mesambria, the one Dorian polis on the Black Sea coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What made the place was the strait: currents and winds let ships through only at certain times, so they gathered in convoys to wait, especially at Hieron on the Asian shore under the sanctuary of Zeus Ourios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its earliest coins show a cow stepping over a dolphin, read as a claim to be where Io crossed, which Chalcedon opposite made for itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The city sits very high on the Athenian tribute lists, and that is now taken less as a measure of its wealth than as Athens making it the hinge of its control over the strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tenth on shipping was levied there from the 430s BCE, broke off when the city revolted in 411, and was put back by Alcibiades at Chrysopolis across the water in 409.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Athens it guaranteed passage itself, and when the Galatians of Tylis demanded sums four times its highest tribute assessment it reimposed the tenth, which brought Rhodes to war in 220 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its other wealth swam past it, the tunny and mackerel migrating between the Black Sea and the Aegean, netted and cured in shore works the city leased out under a monopoly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of what is known of the strait's places and stories comes from the <i>Anaplus Bospori</i> of Dionysius of Byzantium, whose Greek survives only in part, the rest read through a 16th-century Latin translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Byzantium","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","anatolia","politics"],"sources":["Paul Kimball, review of <i>Byzantium and the Bosporus: A Historical Study, from the Seventh Century BC until the Foundation of Constantinople</i>, by Thomas Russell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.05.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.05.51/. [Open access]","Margarit Damyanov, review of <i>Mégarika: nouvelles recherches sur Mégare, les cités de la Propontide et du Pont-Euxin</i>, ed. Adrian Robu and Iulian Bîrzescu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.32/. [Open access]","Balbina Baebler, review of <i>Ancient Greek Colonies in the Black Sea</i>, ed. Dimitrios V. Grammenos and Elias K. Petropoulos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.09.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.09.01/. [Open access]","Tommaso Braccini, review of <i>Dionysios von Byzanz, Anaplus Bospori: Die Fahrt auf dem Bosporos</i>, by Margarethe Billerbeck, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.07.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.07.05/. [Open access]","Kevin Feeney, review of <i>From Byzantium to Constantinople: An Urban History</i>, by John Matthews, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.01.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.01.19/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/1784_Bocage_Map_of_The_Bosphorus_and_the_City_of_Byzantium_-_Istanbul_-_Constantinople_-_Geographicus_-_Bosphorus-white-1793.jpg/1920px-1784_Bocage_Map_of_The_Bosphorus_and_the_City_of_Byzantium_-_Istanbul_-_Constantinople_-_Geographicus_-_Bosphorus-white-1793.jpg","title":"Byzantium","desc":"This lovely map, depicting the Bosphorus and the city of Byzantium, was prepared by Jean-Denis Barbié du Bocage in 1784 for the “Travels of Anarcharsis”. The map shows the narrow naval passage from the Propontis (Sea of Marmara) to the Pontus Euxinus (Black Sea). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1784_Bocage_Map_of_The_Bosphorus_and_the_City_of_Byzantium_-_Istanbul_-_Constantinople_-_Geographicus_-_Bosphorus-white-1793.jpg","alt":"1784 Bocage Map of The Bosphorus and the City of Byzantium - Istanbul - Constantinople - Geographicus - Bosphorus-white-1793"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Byzantium","at":[28.9847,41.0153]}},
{"id":"gr-220","num":220,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The bronze dolphins and arrowheads found in quantity at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are now read as small offerings to Apollo before they were ever used as money.","questions":["The island settlement of Berezan grew, spread its farmland along the Bug, and gave rise to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which in time became a state and absorbed its own parent.","One reading of Greek trade with the Scythians inland from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Milesian city on the Bug estuary, puts slaves rather than grain at its centre."],"answer":"Olbia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 500 BCE, from Miletus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanctuary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in use c. 600 BCE – c. 100 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Olbia</b> is the Milesian city on the estuary of the Bug in what is now southern Ukraine, and it became the leading Greek settlement of the northern Black Sea as Berezan out to seaward declined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew out of Berezan itself: as that island settlement worked its crafts and pushed its farmland up the river, Olbia was laid out on the mainland with cult buildings and houses from the start, and in time became a state that absorbed its own parent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stands on the Bug rather than the larger Dnieper, and the people already living there and the networks they used are what shaped where the Greeks could settle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its earliest houses were largely dugouts, a form that may be local rather than Greek, and indigenous pottery, crafts and building run right through the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its western religious precinct was in use from the 6th century BCE until the 1st century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What drew the Greeks is not settled: a fishing station, a farming settlement and a trading post have all been proposed, and so has the idea that the place was planted to supply Miletus with metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What went the other way is disputed as well, one account putting slaves rather than grain at the centre of Greek dealings with the Scythians in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The small bronze dolphins and arrowheads found in quantity here and along the western coast have long been taken for an early form of money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They fit no known pattern of how coinage develops, and are better read as small offerings to Apollo, the dolphin to Delphinios and the arrowhead to Ietros, which took on a second life as tokens of exchange once real coin arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Out in the fields the settlers were Greek: the countryside gives them as the dominant part of the population in the chora as in the city, with at most a few of the surrounding peoples among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olbia","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","ukraine","geography"],"sources":["Stanley Burstein, review of <i>The Cauldron of Ariantas: Studies Presented to A. N. Sceglov on the Occasion of His 70th Birthday</i>, ed. Pia Guldager Bilde, Jakob Munk Højte and Vladimir F. Stolba, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.11.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.11.06/. [Open access]","Emma Buckingham, review of <i>Greek Colonization in Local Contexts: Case Studies in Colonial Interactions</i>, ed. Jason Lucas, Carrie Ann Murray and Sara Owen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.10.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.10.57/. [Open access]","John Brendan Knight, review of <i>Advances in Ancient Black Sea Studies: Historiography, Archaeology and Religion</i>, ed. Victor Cojocaru and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.03.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.03.02/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Surveying the Greek Chora: The Black Sea Region in a Comparative Perspective</i>, ed. Pia Guldager Bilde and Vladimir F. Stolba, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.05.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.05.06/. [Open access]","Balbina Baebler, review of <i>Ancient Berezan: The Architecture, History and Culture of the First Greek Colony in the Northern Black Sea</i>, by S. L. Solovyov, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.06.01/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Olbia","at":[31.9036,46.6925]}},
{"id":"gr-221","num":221,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The earliest pottery showing firm occupation at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is dated about 615 BCE, forty years before the pharaoh Herodotus credits with founding it.","questions":["Greeks from nine cities kept a shared sanctuary at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and 22 dedications from it are addressed to the gods of the Hellenes rather than to any one city's god.","Amasis gave the Greeks who settled in Egypt a place of their own at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with its own internal organisation, though they remained subject to the king."],"answer":"Naucratis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 615 – 610 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amasis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">570 – 526 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Naucratis</b> is the Greek trading settlement in the Nile Delta, the first Greek town in Egypt and where Greeks from many cities lived and traded under an Egyptian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus says Amasis gave it to them: somewhere to live for those who settled in Egypt, and land for shrines and altars for the merchants who did not want to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He calls it a polis, but that word can mean no more than a town, and what he describes is a separate Greek community with its own organisation, still subject to the king, as the Phoenicians at Memphis and the Jews at Elephantine were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The date is the standing problem: the earliest pottery showing firm occupation is put at about 615 to 610 BCE, forty years before Amasis, and one answer is that archaic Greek pottery is dated too early rather than that Herodotus is wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dedication from Priene by a mercenary who served under Psammetichus I keeps open the possibility that the place began under him instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its greatest sanctuary was the Hellenion, founded by nine cities together in Herodotus's list, which counts Rhodes as one where its three cities were still separate, so the founders may have been eleven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The site carries 22 dedications addressed simply to the gods of the Hellenes, far too many for a phrase used loosely, and a mark of one shared cult place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what makes it remarkable: it is the one archaic settlement where the idea of Hellenism is explicit, and the suggestion is that Egyptians saw Greeks from many cities as one kind of stranger, and the Greeks took that up as their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same pull worked a level down, the three cities of Rhodes counting as Rhodian here and carrying that back home with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Greeks kept their own gods in public, but the houses tell another story from the temples, Egyptian household religion having got well inside private life while the institutions held the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Naucratis","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","egypt","religion"],"sources":["Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis</i> and <i>More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Edward Harris, review of <i>La cité marchande</i>, by Alain Bresson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.09.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.09.40/. [Open access]","Marek Wecowski, review of <i>La naissance de la ville dans l’antiquité</i>, ed. Michel Reddé and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.18/. [Open access]","Meritxell Ferrer-Martín, review of <i>Negotiating Identity in the Ancient Mediterranean: The Archaic and Classical Greek Multiethnic Emporia</i>, by Denise Demetriou, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.08.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.08.23/. [Open access]","Maria Cannata, review of <i>Beyond the Nile: Egypt and the Classical World</i>, ed. Jeffrey Spier, Timothy F. Potts and Sara E. Cole, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.04.37/. [Open access]","Danielle L. Kellogg, review of <i>A Small Greek World: Networks in the Ancient Mediterranean</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.12.60, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.12.60/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Plate_sphinx_BM_GR1965.9-30.705.jpg/1920px-Plate_sphinx_BM_GR1965.9-30.705.jpg","title":"Naucratis","desc":"Seated sphinx. Plate, Eastern Greek Orientalizing, 6th century BCE. From Naukratis in Egypt. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_sphinx_BM_GR1965.9-30.705.jpg","alt":"Plate sphinx BM GR1965.9-30.705"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Naucratis","at":[30.5925,30.9011]}},
{"id":"gr-222","num":222,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The best-known record of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is five lines cut into the leg of a colossus at Abu Simbel by two men of Greek and Carian descent on campaign in Nubia.","questions":["Evidence for the Carians in Egypt is in fact fuller than the evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the ancient literary sources leave the opposite impression.","Egypt gained new battlefield methods along with the men, since <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought the hoplite way of fighting with them from the reign of Psammetichus I onwards."],"answer":"Greek mercenaries in Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Psammetichus I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">664 – 610 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abu Simbel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">593 – 592 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Greek mercenaries in Egypt</b> are attested from the reign of Psammetichus I, and many of the Greek settlers who first appear in the country served in the armies of the pharaohs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The pharaohs of the 26th dynasty were among the three great employers of hired Greek soldiers in the archaic age, along with the tyrants of mainland Greece and those of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Greeks, Carians and Cypriots turn up in Egypt as traders, settlers and soldiers alike, and Memphis became a city of Syrians, Phoenicians, Jews, Carians and Ionians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Carians who served beside them are in fact better documented than the Greeks, which is the reverse of the impression the written sources give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus is the chief of those sources and is often stretched further than he goes: he does not mention Assyrian rule over Egypt at all, and does not say Greeks fought for Persia against the last Saite king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A dedication at Priene by a man who had served under Psammetichus I shows how early the traffic ran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its best-known trace is at Abu Simbel, where Archon son of Amoibichos and Peleqos son of Eudamos cut five lines into the leg of one of the colossi of Ramesses II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They record the expedition Psammetichus II sent against the Nubians in 593 and 592 BCE, and the letter-forms and dialects of the graffiti around theirs show men of several different origins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What Egypt was buying was not only manpower, since the hoplite way of fighting was new and the pharaohs had reason to want it, whatever the economic terms of the arrangement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek itself had no word for a mercenary, and the words used shifted as coined money spread, from <i>epikouros</i>, +m[1]+, to <i>misthophoros</i>, +m[1]+, and <i>xenos misthophoros</i>, +m[1]+.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek mercenaries in Egypt","tags":["practice","history","greece","archaic","egypt","warfare"],"sources":["Stefano Struffolino, “Inscription of Greek Mercenaries to Abu Simbel,” <i>Axon</i> 2, no. 1 (2018): 9–30, https://doi.org/10.30687/Axon/2532-6848/2018/01/001. [Open access]","Ian Worthington, review of <i>Greek Mercenaries: From the Late Archaic Period to Alexander</i>, by Matthew Trundle, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.04/. [Open access]","Nino Luraghi, review of <i>Mercenaries in the Classical World: To the Death of Alexander</i>, by Stephen English, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.12.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.12.50/. [Open access]","Maria Cannata, review of <i>Beyond the Nile: Egypt and the Classical World</i>, ed. Jeffrey Spier, Timothy F. Potts and Sara E. Cole, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.04.37/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis</i> and <i>More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Peter Nadig, review of <i>‘Because I am Greek’: Polyonymy as an Expression of Ethnicity in Ptolemaic Egypt</i>, by Sandra Coussement, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.04.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.04.21/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-001","num":"1","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Strabo saw <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a triangle with the Alps for its base and the strait of Sicily for its vertex, cut down its length by the Apennines.","answer":"Ancient Italy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 1 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Population</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 – 16 million, disputed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ancient Italy</b> names the peninsula and its peoples before Rome made one country of them, and it was a geographical expression long before it was a political one. Strabo describes a triangle running south, the Alps for its base and the strait of Sicily for its vertex, six thousand stadia from the Adriatic to the heel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Apennines run its length, so the workable country comes in pockets between the ridges and the two coasts rather than in one broad expanse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The one great lowland lies to the north between those mountains and the Alps: the Po plain, today among the most densely populated and productive country in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For most of this period it was not reckoned part of Italy at all, and the name had begun far to the south, where Antiochus of Syracuse used <i>Italia</i> of the toe alone before it spread north and took in the Cisalpine Gauls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How many people the peninsula held is disputed, and the argument turns on two readings of the same census totals and the same scatter of settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A low count gives about 6 million around 1 CE after two centuries of slow decline, where a high count gives about 16 million after rapid growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost nothing survives in which these peoples describe themselves at length — Umbrian is known chiefly from one document of about 4,500 words — so they reach us through Greek and Roman authors writing after Rome absorbed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology has carried the weight since the 19th century and ancient DNA since the 2010s: 82 people buried in Etruria and the south show a gene pool holding steady through the 1st millennium BCE, then shifting abruptly under the empire to about half eastern Mediterranean ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The frame has moved with the evidence: 'Romanization' is contested enough that some would drop the word, the spread of Roman ways being explained through a common elite culture rather than as something done to the conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ancient Italy","questions":["Read the Roman census totals one way and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held about 6 million people; read the same figures the other way and it held nearer 16 million.","Because its peoples left almost nothing about themselves, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaches us through Greek and Roman authors, through archaeology, and lately through DNA from its graves."],"tags":["place","history","geography","archaeology","genetics","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.1.1–3, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5A*.html. [Open access]","Michele Livani et al., \"Subsurface Geological and Geophysical Data from the Po Plain and the Northern Adriatic Sea,\" <i>Earth System Science Data</i> 15 (2023): 4261–93, https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-15-4261-2023. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 6.1.4, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/6A*.html. [Open access]","Saskia Hin and Emilio Zagheni, \"Population Dynamics in Historical Roman Italy: The Impact of Warfare\" (conference paper, International Union for the Scientific Study of Population), https://iussp.org/sites/default/files/event_call_for_papers/Hin%26Zagheni_PopulationModeling.pdf. [Open access]","Cosimo Posth et al., \"The Origin and Legacy of the Etruscans through a 2000-Year Archeogenomic Time Transect,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7 (2021): eabi7673, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abi7673. [Open access]","Kathryn Lomas, review of <i>Italy and the West: Comparative Issues in Romanization</i>, ed. Simon Keay and Nicola Terrenato, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2002.07.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.07.23/. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Umbrian: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=58&lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Samnite_soldiers_from_a_tomb_frieze_in_Nola_4th_century_BCE.jpg/1920px-Samnite_soldiers_from_a_tomb_frieze_in_Nola_4th_century_BCE.jpg","title":"Ancient Italy","desc":"Samnite soldiers from a tomb fresco from Nola, 4th century BCE. Museo archeologico nazionale di Napoli (inv. nr. 9363). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samnite_soldiers_from_a_tomb_frieze_in_Nola_4th_century_BCE.jpg","alt":"Samnite soldiers from a tomb frieze in Nola 4th century BCE"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"rm-002","num":"2","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Running about 1,200 kilometres (750 miles) from north-west to south-east, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divide the Italian peninsula lengthwise and leave its flat ground in strips along two facing coasts.","answer":"Apennines","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Apennines</b> are the mountain spine of Italy, a set of parallel ranges running about 1,200 kilometres (750 miles) from north-west to south-east down the length of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are conventionally cut into three sectors, northern, central and southern, and the highest ground stands in the centre, where the Gran Sasso reaches 2,912 metres (9,554 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chain divides the country without closing it, and the flat land lies to either side, sloping away west towards the Tyrrhenian Sea and east towards the Adriatic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain falls unevenly across that divide, the relief gathering the wet on one flank and leaving the Adriatic side of the range drier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rock changes along the chain, and at its southern front it is clay-rich flysch whose slopes fail readily: one sector in northern Apulia, 1,460 square kilometres (564 square miles) in extent, has been mapped for its landslides alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The mountains are still rising, by as much as 1 millimetre (0.04 inches) a year in the central Apennines, and the fastest uplift follows the highest ground and the drainage divide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Extensional faults take up that movement, and earthquakes along them reach magnitudes between 5.8 and 6.9, so this is unquiet ground rather than a settled backdrop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Along the western flank the peninsula turns volcanic, and the Smithsonian's Global Volcanism Program files Vesuvius and Campi Flegrei together among the Italian Peninsula provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Vesuvius is a stratovolcano of 1,281 metres (4,203 feet) that last erupted in 1944; Campi Flegrei is a caldera reaching 458 metres (1,503 feet), last in eruption in 1538.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the range leaves is a country whose workable ground runs in strips along two coasts with a barrier between them,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and that is the shape the rest of Roman history is written across.","citation":"","answerText":"Apennines","questions":["Still rising by up to a millimetre a year and shaken by earthquakes of magnitude 5.8 to 6.9, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the unquiet backbone of the Italian peninsula.","The Gran Sasso reaches 2,912 metres (9,554 feet) in the central sector of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the chain conventionally divided into northern, central and southern parts."],"tags":["place","geography","geology","history","italy"],"sources":["Vincenzo Capozzi et al., \"Historical Snow Measurements in the Central and Southern Apennine Mountains: Climatology, Variability, and Trend,\" <i>The Cryosphere</i> 19 (2025): 565–595, https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-19-565-2025. [Open access]","Patience A. Cowie et al., \"Orogen-Scale Uplift in the Central Italian Apennines Drives Episodic Behaviour of Earthquake Faults,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7 (2017): 44858, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep44858. [Open access]","Francesca Ardizzone et al., \"Geomorphological Landslide Inventory Map of the Daunia Apennines, Southern Italy,\" <i>Earth System Science Data</i> 15 (2023): 753–767, https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-15-753-2023. [Open access]","\"Vesuvius,\" Global Volcanism Program, Smithsonian Institution, https://volcano.si.edu/volcano.cfm?vn=211020. [Open access]","\"Campi Flegrei,\" Global Volcanism Program, Smithsonian Institution, https://volcano.si.edu/volcano.cfm?vn=211010. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Monte_Pollino_%28P.N.P.%29.jpg/1920px-Monte_Pollino_%28P.N.P.%29.jpg","title":"Apennines","desc":"Il versante nord di Monte Pollino. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Pollino_(P.N.P.).jpg","alt":"Monte Pollino (P.N.P.)"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"locator":{"name":"The Apennines","at":[13.5656,42.4692],"kind":"range","spine":[[8.45,44.35],[9.55,44.45],[10.3,44.3],[11.2,44.1],[11.85,43.85],[12.6,43.45],[13.1,42.8],[13.55,42.47],[14.05,41.95],[15,41.3],[15.55,40.8],[16.05,40.15],[16.2,39.55],[16.35,39],[16.05,38.4],[15.9,38.15]]}},
{"id":"rm-003","num":"3","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Rising in the Apennines and reaching the Tyrrhenian Sea beside Rome, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Italy's third-longest river and was the city's road to the sea before it was anything else.","answer":"Tiber","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Tiber</b> is the third-longest river in Italy, running about 405 kilometres (250 miles) from a source in the Apennines to the Tyrrhenian Sea beside Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It drains a catchment of roughly 17,400 square kilometres (6,700 square miles), which takes about 95 centimetres (37 inches) of rain a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its flow is anything but steady: mean discharge runs at around 225 cubic metres a second, the highest annual figures pass 1,500 and the lowest fall to 60, a range wide enough to change what the river could carry from one year to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The valley it occupies was cut tens of metres below present sea level at the Last Glacial Maximum, and it has been filling ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Along the middle valley that filling has left a staircase of four aggradational terraces standing above the modern alluvial plain, beneath an older tabular plateau that reaches 250 to 270 metres (820 to 890 feet) above sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Three phases of aggradation brought the plain at Rome to a relatively stable 6 to 8 metres (20 to 26 feet) above sea level by the late 3rd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of them was abrupt, laying 4 to 6 metres (13 to 20 feet) of alluvium in roughly a century around 2,600 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Tiber Island, which splits the channel in the middle of the city, owes its existence to tectonic movement rather than to silt alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The river was Rome's route to the sea before the city had any other, and its earliest harbour lay upstream at the Forum Boarium, on the bank within the city itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mouth was the difficulty: a sandbank grounded the ship carrying the Magna Mater in 205–204 BCE, Strabo called the harbour there unsheltered, and laden vessels had to tranship into lighters and river boats to reach Rome at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tiber","questions":["Laden ships could not pass the sandbank at the mouth of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and had to tranship into lighters, which is how cargo from the sea reached Rome at all.","Three phases of silting raised the floodplain of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a stable 6 to 8 metres (20 to 26 feet) above sea level by the late 3rd century BCE."],"tags":["place","geography","geology","history","italy"],"sources":["Silvia Fornasaro et al., \"Total Mercury Mass Load from the Paglia–Tiber River System: The Contribution to Mediterranean Sea Hg Budget,\" <i>Toxics</i> 10 (2022): 395, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics10070395. [Open access]","Jaime Pitarch et al., \"Linking Flow-Stream Variability to Grain Size Distribution of Suspended Sediment from a Satellite-Based Analysis of the Tiber River Plume,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9 (2019): 19729, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-56409-8. [Open access]","Fabrizio Marra et al., \"Rome in Its Setting: Post-Glacial Aggradation History of the Tiber River Alluvial Deposits and Tectonic Origin of the Tiber Island,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13 (2018): e0194838, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0194838. [Open access]","Ferréol Salomon et al., \"The Development and Characteristics of Ancient Harbours: Applying the PADM Chart to the Case Studies of Ostia and Portus,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11 (2016): e0162587, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0162587. [Open access]","Fabrizio Marra, Fabio Florindo, and Carmelo Petronio, \"Quaternary Fluvial Terraces of the Tiber Valley: Geochronologic and Geometric Constraints on the Back-Arc Magmatism-Related Uplift in Central Italy,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7 (2017): 2517, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-02437-1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Hendrik_Frans_van_Lint_-_Rome_-_A_View_of_the_Tiber_-_WGA13056.jpg","title":"Tiber","desc":"Hendrik Frans van Lint - Rome - A View of the Tiber. Hendrik Frans van Lint. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hendrik_Frans_van_Lint_-_Rome_-_A_View_of_the_Tiber_-_WGA13056.jpg","alt":"Hendrik Frans van Lint - Rome - A View of the Tiber"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"locator":{"name":"Tiber","at":[12.2334,41.7405],"kind":"river","zoom":22}},
{"id":"rm-004","num":"4","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"South of the Tiber on Italy's Tyrrhenian side, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is built on a thick plateau of volcanic rock laid down by two districts of dormant volcanoes that flank Rome.","answer":"Latium","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Latium</b> is the coastal country of west-central Italy lying south of the Tiber, and the ground under it is volcanic. Two districts built that ground: Monti Sabatini north of Rome and the Alban Hills to the south, erupting from about 600,000 years ago in a sequence punctuated by pulses of tectonic uplift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their eruptions laid a thick pyroclastic plateau, and the tuffaceous hills Rome was founded on are cut from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither district is extinct, only dormant: the last eruptions came about 70,000 years ago at Monti Sabatini and 36,000 at the Alban Hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across the wider province that activity has been waning from north-west to south-east, from northern Latium towards the bay of Naples, and it crossed the site of Rome on the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> People were on this ground long before any of it settled, and Acheulian and early Middle Palaeolithic assemblages at Torre in Pietra put them here hundreds of millennia back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The coast is a line of shallow lagoons behind dunes, with a wide plain running south of the Anzio promontory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Inland the limestone of the Monti Lepini and Monti Ausoni holds caves that were used for burial through the Copper and Bronze Ages, among them La Sassa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On an island in the Caprolace lagoon a Middle Bronze Age workshop made salt by briquetage, boiling brine down until a cake of it was left, a technique in use across Europe from the Neolithic to Roman times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The evidence there points to an elite-driven workshop rather than a household one, so this coast carried a specialised industry well before Rome amounted to anything, and the marshy ground behind the dunes was not drained until the 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Latium","questions":["In the Middle Bronze Age a workshop on a lagoon island in coastal <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> boiled brine into cakes of salt, a specialised industry running there long before Rome existed.","Acheulian assemblages at Torre in Pietra show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was occupied for hundreds of millennia before any of the peoples Rome would later contend with arrived."],"tags":["place","geography","geology","history","italy"],"sources":["Fabrizio Marra et al., \"Monti Sabatini and Colli Albani: The Dormant Twin Volcanoes at the Gates of Rome,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 8666, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-65394-2. [Open access]","Paola Villa et al., \"The Acheulian and Early Middle Paleolithic in Latium (Italy): Stability and Innovation,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11 (2016): e0160516, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0160516. [Open access]","Luca Alessandri et al., \"Salt or Fish (or Salted Fish)? The Bronze Age Specialised Sites along the Tyrrhenian Coast of Central Italy: New Insights from Caprolace Settlement,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14 (2019): e0224435, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0224435. [Open access]","Marco Romboni et al., \"La Sassa Cave: Isotopic Evidence for Copper Age and Bronze Age Population Dynamics in Central Italy,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18 (2023): e0288637, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0288637. [Open access]","Fabrizio Marra et al., \"The Archaeological Ensemble from Campoverde (Agro Pontino, Central Italy): New Constraints on the Last Interglacial Sea Level Markers,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 17837, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-36111-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/I_Colli_Albani_visti_dal_parco_degli_acquedotti_Roma.JPG/1920px-I_Colli_Albani_visti_dal_parco_degli_acquedotti_Roma.JPG","title":"Latium","desc":"The Alban Hills seen from the Park of the Aqueducts, Rome. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I_Colli_Albani_visti_dal_parco_degli_acquedotti_Roma.JPG","alt":"The Alban Hills rising beyond the flat plain south-east of Rome"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Latium","at":[12.7167,41.9],"kind":"region","area":[[12.24,41.83],[12.75,42],[13.1,41.85],[13.35,41.55],[13.1,41.25],[12.85,41.25],[12.45,41.55],[12.23,41.75]]}},
{"id":"rm-005","num":"5","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"About 20 kilometres (12 miles) south-east of Rome, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are a volcanic caldera whose eruptions helped lay the plateau the city's own hills are cut from.","answer":"Alban Hills","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Alban Hills</b>, the Colli Albani of Italian geology, are a volcanic caldera about 20 kilometres (12 miles) south-east of Rome, rising to 949 metres (3,114 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With Monti Sabatini to the north of the city they laid the thick pyroclastic plateau that Rome's own hills are cut from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their eruptions began about 600,000 years ago and ran on in a long series of ignimbrites and ash-falls, with people living in the region while they continued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Geologists divide the volcano's life into three periods, named Vulcano Laziale, Tuscolano-Artemisio-Faete and Via dei Laghi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its youngest vents are maars, craters blasted open where rising magma met groundwater, and the largest of them holds Lake Albano.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> When it last erupted is genuinely unsettled: one study dates the last magmatic eruption to 36,000 years ago, another to about 23,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second reports phreatic activity, steam blasts driven without fresh magma, going on through the Holocene and into historical times, while the Smithsonian's catalogue records the last known eruption simply as uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is agreed is that the volcano is quiescent rather than extinct, and that across the last 600,000 years its average dormancy has run to some 38,500 years, about as long as the interval since it last erupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twenty years of satellite radar show the ground above the youngest vents inflating by more than 2 millimetres (0.08 inches) a year, which is read as magma recharging at depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gas still reaches the surface: at Cava dei Selci, a neighbourhood of 4,000 people on the flank of the volcano, carbon dioxide carrying about 1 per cent hydrogen sulphide has killed animals, caused blowouts and road collapses, and forced houses to be evacuated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Alban Hills","questions":["Satellite radar shows the ground above the youngest vents of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> inflating by more than 2 millimetres (0.08 inches) a year, read as magma recharging at depth.","At Cava dei Selci a neighbourhood of 4,000 people sits on gas rising from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, carbon dioxide with hydrogen sulphide in it that has killed animals and forced evacuations."],"tags":["place","geography","geology","nature","italy"],"sources":["Fabrizio Marra et al., \"Monti Sabatini and Colli Albani: The Dormant Twin Volcanoes at the Gates of Rome,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 8666, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-65394-2. [Open access]","Mónica Ágreda-López et al., \"The Crystal Cargo Provides a Chronicle of Pre-Caldera Dynamics in Mafic Volcanic Systems: Insights from Colli Albani,\" <i>Bulletin of Volcanology</i> 87 (2025): 78, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00445-025-01865-6. [Open access]","\"Colli Albani,\" Global Volcanism Program, Smithsonian Institution, https://volcano.si.edu/volcano.cfm?vn=211004. [Open access]","Maria Luisa Carapezza et al., \"Health Impact of Natural Gas Emission at Cava dei Selci Residential Zone,\" <i>Environmental Geochemistry and Health</i> 45 (2023): 707–729, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10653-022-01244-6. [Open access]","Paola Villa et al., \"The Acheulian and Early Middle Paleolithic in Latium (Italy): Stability and Innovation,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11 (2016): e0160516, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0160516. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Louis_Gurlitt_-_Albaner_Berge.jpg/1920px-Louis_Gurlitt_-_Albaner_Berge.jpg","title":"Alban Hills","desc":"Louis Gurlitt - Albaner Berge. Louis Gurlitt. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Gurlitt_-_Albaner_Berge.jpg","alt":"Louis Gurlitt - Albaner Berge"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Alban Hills","at":[12.7,41.73],"kind":"region","zoom":120,"area":[[12.56,41.83],[12.68,41.87],[12.8,41.85],[12.88,41.77],[12.86,41.66],[12.74,41.61],[12.6,41.64],[12.55,41.73]]}},
{"id":"rm-006","num":"6","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Po plain filled with large defended centres, Sardinia raised its drystone towers, and copper from the southern Alps travelled as far as the Balkans.","answer":"Bronze Age Italy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nuraghi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1100 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Bronze Age Italy</b> was never one society, and the regions of the peninsula and its islands went their own ways at their own speeds. In the north the communities of the Po plain settled progressively through the 2nd millennium BCE, ending in the large defended centres that stood at the turn of the Middle and Late Bronze Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people in them were drawing on networks that reached into continental Europe and the eastern Mediterranean, and isotopes taken from their dead show movement into those settlements as well as goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the metal behind all this came out of the southern Alps, and analysis of 57 objects found that copper matching those ores was almost omnipresent in the western and central Balkans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Italy was therefore exporting raw material into Europe rather than only receiving it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In Sardinia the age built something no other part of Italy did, the nuraghi, drystone towers put up between about 1700 and 1100 BCE and still the island's most prominent Bronze Age monument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Far to the south, mining near the Sila Plateau made protohistoric Calabria one of the significant metallurgical territories of Italy, tying it into exchange along the Tyrrhenian coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Middle Bronze Age burial in the karst cave of Grotta della Monaca, 600 metres (2,000 feet) up near the river Esaro, has yielded the first Bronze Age mitogenomes recovered from that region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the centre, the Final Bronze Age settlement of Monte Croce Guardia in the Marche ground its grain on tools cut from volcanic rock, chosen because lava wears well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tracing that rock back to the outcrops it came from puts people and heavy goods on the move across central Italy some three thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Bronze Age Italy","questions":["The first mitogenomes from a Middle Bronze Age cave burial in Calabria were recovered to study <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a period whose southern communities lived beside some of the peninsula's richest ore.","Sardinia raised thousands of drystone towers between about 1700 and 1100 BCE, the most prominent monument left anywhere by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and unmatched on the mainland."],"tags":["era","history","italy","archaeology","technology"],"sources":["Claudio Cavazzuti et al., \"Flows of People in Villages and Large Centres in Bronze Age Italy through Strontium and Oxygen Isotopes,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14 (2019): e0209693, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0209693. [Open access]","Mario Gavranović et al., \"Emergence of Monopoly: Copper Exchange Networks during the Late Bronze Age in the Western and Central Balkans,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17 (2022): e0263823, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0263823. [Open access]","Davide Schirru and Alessandro Vanzetti, \"Climbing the Giara: A Quantitative Reassessment of Movement and Visibility in the Nuragic Landscape of the Gesturi Plateau,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18 (2023): e0289023, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0289023. [Open access]","Francesco Fontani et al., \"First Bronze Age Human Mitogenomes from Calabria (Grotta della Monaca, Southern Italy),\" <i>Genes</i> 12 (2021): 636, https://doi.org/10.3390/genes12050636. [Open access]","Patrizia Santi et al., \"Tracing the Human Movements of Three Thousand Years Ago by Volcanic Grinding Tools in the Final Bronze Age Settlement of Monte Croce Guardia,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 7022, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-34033-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Nuraghe_village.JPG","title":"Bronze Age Italy","desc":"Nuraghe et son environnement. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuraghe_village.JPG","alt":"Nuraghe village"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"rm-007","num":"7","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Sub-quadrangular, banked and ditched, the villages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood on the ridges of the Po plain and carried the densest population Bronze Age Italy reached.","answer":"Terramare culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Terramare culture</b> is the Middle and Recent Bronze Age culture of the Po plain, its banked villages the nearest thing Bronze Age Italy had to a landscape of towns. The name is modern: it comes from <i>terra marna</i>, a local word for the organically rich earth of the mounds the villages left behind, which was quarried as fertiliser in the 19th century and so brought them to notice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They were sub-quadrangular, raised on alluvial ridges and old river terraces, and laid out to a plan rather than grown at random: the huts stood either on the ground or on built platforms, aligned to the perimeter works that enclosed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their sizes ran from small to substantial: Sant'Eurosia grew from 1.5 to 2.5 hectares (3.7 to 6.2 acres) over its life, while large ones such as Fondo Paviani reached 15 to 20 hectares (37 to 49 acres), and the economy under all of it was farming and herding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Oppeano near Verona the floors of two houses of 1650 to 1550 BCE hold laminated dung from small livestock stalled indoors, so people and their sheep or goats were sharing a roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What they ate was changing across the same centuries, as broomcorn and foxtail millet, rye, spelt and new legumes spread through the peninsula alongside the older cereals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They were not, however, a mobile people: strontium and oxygen isotopes from more than a hundred individuals at three sites put most movement inside a radius of about 50 kilometres (30 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They could build well beyond the scale of a household, and the clearest case is Noceto, where a great tank of oak was sunk into a terrace at the plain's southern edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tree rings and radiocarbon date its lower tank to 1444 BCE and the upper one twelve years later, to 1432, each to within about four years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The whole system then collapsed around 1150 BCE, and the explanation offered is a combination of land worked past what it could bear and a shift in the climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Terramare culture","questions":["At Noceto a tank of oak sunk into a terrace of the Po plain belongs to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its timbers dated by tree rings to 1444 BCE and a second tank to 1432.","Around 1150 BCE the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> collapsed, an end laid to land worked past what it could bear together with a shift in the climate."],"tags":["culture","history","italy","archaeology","bronze age"],"sources":["Claudio Cavazzuti et al., \"Flows of People in Villages and Large Centres in Bronze Age Italy through Strontium and Oxygen Isotopes,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14 (2019): e0209693, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0209693. [Open access]","Giacomo Capuzzo et al., \"Highly Diverse Bronze Age Population Dynamics in Central-Southern Europe and Their Response to Regional Climatic Patterns,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13 (2018): e0200709, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0200709. [Open access]","Cristiano Nicosia et al., \"High-Resolution Sediment Analysis Reveals Middle Bronze Age Byre-Houses at the Site of Oppeano,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17 (2022): e0272561, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272561. [Open access]","Mauro Cremaschi et al., \"Dating the Noceto Vasca Votiva, a Unique Wooden Structure of the 15th Century BCE, and the Timing of a Major Societal Change in the Bronze Age of Northern Italy,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16 (2021): e0251341, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0251341. [Open access]","Alessandra Varalli, Jacopo Moggi-Cecchi, and Gwenaëlle Goude, \"A Multi-Proxy Bioarchaeological Approach Reveals New Trends in Bronze Age Diet in Italy,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 12203, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-15581-0. [Open access]","Mauro Cremaschi, Paolo Storchi, and Alessandro Perego, \"Geoarchaeology in Urban Context: The Town of Reggio Emilia and River Dynamics during the Last Two Millennia in Northern Italy,\" <i>Geoarchaeology</i> 33 (2018): 52–66, author copy at https://air.unimi.it/bitstream/2434/530920/5/CREMASCHI%20GEOARCH.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Scavi_alla_Terramara_di_Castione_Marchesi%2C_1877.jpg/1920px-Scavi_alla_Terramara_di_Castione_Marchesi%2C_1877.jpg","title":"Terramare","desc":"Fotografia tratta da L. Pigorini, Terramara dell’età del Bronzo situata in Castione dei Marchesi (territorio parmigiano), in “Atti Reale Accademia dei Lincei”, s. III, VIII, 1882-83. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scavi_alla_Terramara_di_Castione_Marchesi,_1877.jpg","alt":"Scavi alla Terramara di Castione Marchesi, 1877"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Terramare culture","at":[10.93,44.62],"kind":"region","area":[[9.45,45.15],[10.4,45.45],[11.2,45.42],[11.95,45.2],[12.05,44.8],[11.6,44.45],[10.6,44.5],[9.7,44.72],[9.4,44.95]]}},
{"id":"rm-008","num":"8","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Burning its dead and sealing them in necked urns under in-turned bowls, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gathered central Italy onto plateaus that would become the Etruscan cities.","answer":"Villanovan culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Villanovan culture</b> is the culture of Italy's Early Iron Age, running from the end of the 10th century to the 8th BCE across the Tyrrhenian side of central Italy and the southern Po plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known above all from how it buried its dead: cremated bone in a vase with a distinctive neck, covered by an in-turned rim bowl, both worked in a geometric style, the urn set in a pit and packed round with the ashes of the pyre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bronze helmets and disc fibulae recur in the graves often enough to be diagnostic of the whole group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burning was so much the rule that the exceptions stand out: at Tarquinia twenty people were laid unburnt in the sacred heart of the settlement while their contemporaries went to cemeteries on its edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The living changed as much as the dead, the scattered villages of the preceding period giving way to large settlements spread over plateaus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The scale is what startles: principal sites covered 120 to 200 hectares (300 to 490 acres) and secondary ones 25 to 90 hectares (62 to 220 acres), each ringed by its own cemeteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The process behind it was nucleation, and one reading is that bands of descent groups agreed to coalesce on a defensible site rather than being driven there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What came out has been interpreted as a form of early state formation, which is to say that these are the cities of Etruria in their first shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The countryside kept pace, since livestock in Italy grew larger through the Iron Age, earlier than the same change reached the western provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The culture also travelled: Pontecagnano in Campania was founded early in the 9th century BCE by Villanovan groups out of southern Etruria and grew in time into an Etruscan city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Villanovan culture","questions":["Principal sites of 120 to 200 hectares (300 to 490 acres), each ringed by cemeteries, mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose gathering onto plateaus has been read as early state formation.","Pontecagnano in Campania was founded early in the 9th century BCE by groups of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> coming out of southern Etruria, and became an Etruscan city."],"tags":["culture","history","italy","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Carmen Esposito et al., \"Intense Community Dynamics in the Pre-Roman Frontier Site of Fermo (Ninth–Fifth Century BCE, Marche, Central Italy) Inferred from Isotopic Data,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 3632, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-29466-3. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Angela Trentacoste et al., \"Pre-Roman Improvements to Agricultural Production: Evidence from Livestock Husbandry in Late Prehistoric Italy,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13 (2018): e0208109, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0208109. [Open access]","Roberto Germano et al., \"Health and Lifestyle in the Iron Age Italian Community of Pontecagnano,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21 (2026): e0338448, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0338448. [Open access]","Giovanna Bagnasco et al., \"Bioarchaeology Aids the Cultural Understanding of Six Characters in Search of Their Agency (Tarquinia, Ninth–Seventh Century BC, Central Italy),\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 11895, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-61052-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Villanovan_Horse_Bit_LACMA_AC1992.152.17.jpg/1920px-Villanovan_Horse_Bit_LACMA_AC1992.152.17.jpg","title":"Villanovan culture","desc":"Tools and Equipment; horse trappings Bronze Overall length: 9 7/16 in. (24 cm); Height of bit: 2 7/16 in. (6.2 cm) Gift of Varya and Hans Cohn (AC1992.152.17) Greek, Roman and Etruscan Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Villanovan_Horse_Bit_LACMA_AC1992.152.17.jpg","alt":"Villanovan Horse Bit LACMA AC1992.152.17"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Villanovan culture","at":[11.46,44.5],"kind":"region","area":[[10.15,43.95],[10.45,44.35],[11.3,44.68],[11.85,44.58],[12.05,44.2],[11.85,43.75],[12.25,43.2],[12.45,42.8],[12.55,42.35],[12.5,42.05],[12.35,41.9],[11.95,41.8],[11.3,42.25],[10.5,42.85],[10.15,43.5]]}},
{"id":"rm-009","num":"9","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Speakers of one branch of Indo-European spread across the peninsula, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were never a nation and never included every community living in Italy.","answer":"Italic peoples","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Italic peoples</b> are the communities of ancient Italy who spoke a language of the Italic branch of Indo-European, which makes them a linguistic category rather than a nation and leaves a good deal of the peninsula outside it. The branch falls in two: Latin together with Faliscan on one side, and on the other the Sabellic or Osco-Umbrian languages. Oscan ran through the middle and the south, and the peoples associated with it include the Peligni, Vestini, Marrucini, Marsi and Volsci, with the Aequi a possible sixth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> South Picene, spoken in what are now the Marche and Abruzzo, is plainly Italic and shows considerable affinity with Umbrian, though whether it belongs inside the Osco-Umbrian group or branched away earlier is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It survives in 23 inscriptions of the 6th to 3rd centuries BCE, together with two inscribed helmets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Faliscan sat on the right bank of the Tiber in an enclave among Etruscan neighbours, beside Veii and Capena, and of all these languages it is the closest to Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How close is contested: a peripheral dialect of Latin to some scholars, a separate language within the same taxon to others, a fully independent Italic language to a third view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of these identities was as ancient as the peoples holding them liked to claim, since distinct Italic ethnicities emerged only in the Late Bronze Age and hardened into well-defined cultural and linguistic identities during the Iron Age of the 10th to 3rd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Umbri, whom ancient writers counted among the most ancient Italic populations of all, are a case in point, their origins still debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Almost every mitochondrial lineage recovered from the pre-Roman cemetery at Plestia can still be found in Umbria today, which argues for continuity in the population whatever happened to the label.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Italic peoples","questions":["Latin and Faliscan on one side, the Sabellic tongues on the other: the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are grouped by the branch of Indo-European they spoke rather than by any shared state.","Distinct ethnicities among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> emerged only in the Late Bronze Age and hardened during an Iron Age running from the 10th to the 3rd century BCE."],"tags":["people","history","italy","language","iron age"],"sources":["Anna Marinetti, \"South Picene: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=69&lang=en. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Faliscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=66&lang=en. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Oscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=56&lang=en. [Open access]","Francesco Ravasini et al., \"The Genomic Portrait of the Picene Culture Provides New Insights into the Italic Iron Age and the Legacy of the Roman Empire in Central Italy,\" <i>Genome Biology</i> 25 (2024): 292, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-024-03430-4. [Open access]","Alessandra Modi et al., \"The Mitogenome Portrait of Umbria in Central Italy as Depicted by Contemporary Inhabitants and Pre-Roman Remains,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 10700, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-67445-0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Ancient_Samnium.png","title":"Italic peoples","desc":"Reference map for Samnium. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Samnium.png","alt":"Ancient Samnium"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"rm-010","num":"10","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Sharing a material culture, a burial rite and a language but never a single state, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled the country between the Tiber and the Liri.","answer":"Latins","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 950 – 509 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Latins</b> were the people of Latium vetus, the country lying between the rivers Tiber and Liri, and they spoke the language that carries their name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were not a state but a set of communities holding a material culture in common, the Latial culture I to IV, along with similar burial customs and a similar political organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The kinship of their language with Umbrian, Sabellian and Oscan is plain but unexplained, scholars still divided over whether it descends from a shared ancestor or grew out of long contact between neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its oldest surviving evidence is a handful of inscriptions of the 7th and 6th centuries BCE, among them the Duenos inscription and the Lapis Niger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people were no more primordial than the language, having emerged as a distinct Italic ethnicity in the Late Bronze Age and hardened into a defined cultural identity across the Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Their settlements were many and closely tied together, numbering between 78 and 107 across the stretch from about 950 to 509 BCE and linked by an unusually dense web of roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Power among them was never evenly held, and by the earlier 9th century BCE one of those settlements, Rome, had become by far the largest in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That imbalance is what most distinguishes Latium from Etruria over the river, where the proto-urban centres stood on a more equal footing with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> These were not isolated communities either, since goods from far outside the peninsula reach Italic sites of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> And though Latins and Etruscans spoke unrelated languages, DNA from Latins at Rome and its environs shows a local genetic profile shared with their Etruscan neighbours, so what separated the two was culture rather than descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Latins","questions":["By the earlier 9th century BCE Rome had become the largest of the settlements of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose power was never evenly held among them.","DNA from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Rome shows a genetic profile shared with their Etruscan neighbours, so what divided the two peoples was culture rather than descent."],"tags":["people","history","italy","language","iron age"],"sources":["Francesca Fulminante et al., \"Coordinated Decisions and Unbalanced Power: How Latin Cities Shaped Their Terrestrial Transportation Network,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 4 (2017): 4, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2017.00004. [Open access]","Filippo Battistoni, revised by Mirko Donninelli, \"Latin: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=36&lang=en. [Open access]","Francesco Ravasini et al., \"The Genomic Portrait of the Picene Culture Provides New Insights into the Italic Iron Age and the Legacy of the Roman Empire in Central Italy,\" <i>Genome Biology</i> 25 (2024): 292, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-024-03430-4. [Open access]","Cosimo Posth et al., \"The Origin and Legacy of the Etruscans through a 2000-Year Archeogenomic Time Transect,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7 (2021): eabi7673, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abi7673. [Open access]","Francesca Fulminante, \"Editorial: Where Do Cities Come From and Where Are They Going To? Modelling Past and Present Agglomerations to Understand Urban Ways of Life,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 7 (2021): 633838, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2020.633838. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3},
{"id":"rm-011","num":"11","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Modern historians speak of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the singular, where the ancient writers describe at least three separate assemblies of Latin communities, each meeting at a different sanctuary.","answer":"Latin League","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 6th century – 338 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Latin League</b> is a modern name for the alliances binding the communities of Latium, and the singular flatters the evidence: the ancient writers describe assemblies at three different sanctuaries, which have almost always been run together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One gathered at the spring and grove of Ferentina, where Livy and Dionysius of Halicarnassus have the peoples of Latium meet, and where terms between Rome and those cities were settled more than once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its site is now placed near the via Appia, some 25 kilometres (16 miles) from Rome, at a small lake once called the Laghetto di Turno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ground beside it has produced a terracotta warrior’s head of the very beginning of the 5th century BCE, a fragment of roof sculpture and so the trace of a sanctuary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second met at the grove of Diana in the wood of Aricia, whose founding inscription Cato copied out: it was dedicated by Egerius Baebius of Tusculum, dictator of the Latins, for eight named peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The third and longest-lived met on the Alban Mount for the feriae Latinae, the festival of Jupiter Latiaris, which Dionysius says 47 cities shared, bringing lambs, cheeses or milk to a bull sacrificed for all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a movable feast, and alone among such festivals its date was set by the consuls rather than the priests, which made it a moment of consular authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The words the Roman writers use for the meat give the arrangement away: Rome distributed it and the cities asked for their share, a ceremony under Roman direction rather than a rite among equals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Power in Latium had long been unequal, Rome having grown much the largest settlement in the region,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> so several of these alliances look less like instruments of Roman policy than like attempts to resist it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Rome broke the Latins in 338 BCE it dissolved the league, its federal army and its courts, and kept the festival, fixing its date thereafter while leaving the member states to preside in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Latin League","questions":["At the festival on the Alban Mount, one gathering of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some of the 47 cities brought lambs, some cheeses, and some a measure of milk.","Rome distributed the sacrificial meat and the Latin cities asked for their share, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was never the gathering of equals its name suggests."],"tags":["institution","history","italy","religion","iron age"],"sources":["Rianne A. M. Hermans, \"Latin Cults through Roman Eyes: Myth, Memory and Cult Practice in the Alban Hills\" (PhD diss., University of Amsterdam, 2017), chap. 4, https://hdl.handle.net/11245.1/cd8d636c-def7-44a4-9043-231c0d428d28. [Open access]","Alexandre Grandazzi, \"Identification d'une déesse: Ferentina et la ligue latine archaïque,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 140, no. 1 (1996): 273–94, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1996.15581. [Open access]","Filippo Coarelli, \"Le sanctuaire de Diana Nemorensis: nouvelles découvertes,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 156, no. 1 (2012): 555–69, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2012.93450. [Open access]","Bernadette Liou-Gille, \"Naissance de la ligue latine: mythe et culte de fondation,\" <i>Revue belge de philologie et d'histoire</i> 74, no. 1 (1996): 73–97, https://doi.org/10.3406/rbph.1996.4096. [Open access]","Francesca Fulminante et al., \"Coordinated Decisions and Unbalanced Power: How Latin Cities Shaped Their Terrestrial Transportation Network,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 4 (2017): 4, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2017.00004. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Ligue-latine-carte.png","title":"Latin League","desc":"Map of Latium with the cities of the Latin League. Cassius Ahenobarbus, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ligue-latine-carte.png","alt":"Ligue-latine-carte"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Latin League","at":[12.7,41.71],"kind":"region","label":[12.72,41.78],"area":[[12.3,41.95],[12.6,42.03],[12.85,41.99],[13.02,41.86],[13.06,41.66],[13.15,41.4],[13.05,41.2],[12.8,41.28],[12.45,41.52],[12.25,41.72]]}},
{"id":"rm-012","num":"12","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Latin never formed a place name on their name, as it did Samnium and Latium, which is one sign of how loosely the country of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was defined.","answer":"Sabines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Iron Age – 290 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Sabines</b> were the people of the ager Sabinus, the hill country north-east of Rome, never a political unit, its borders vague enough that ancient authors disagree over which towns lay in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cures, Reate and Amiternum are always counted Sabine, and the looseness shows in a fact of language: Latin has no place name formed on the ethnic, where the Samnites have Samnium and the Latins Latium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their settlements stood on heights commanding the routes of the Tiber valley, a pattern set in the Final Bronze Age and carried into the Iron Age at Magliano Sabina, Foglia and Poggio Sommavilla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the 7th century BCE the scattered nuclei drew together into single centres on the Etruscan and Latial model, their cemeteries on the heights immediately around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eretum spread over some 20 hectares (49 acres) on urban lines by the later 7th century, passing from a family grouping under one leader to something more complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its cemetery at Colle del Forno was in use from the end of the 7th century until the end of the 4th, chamber tombs with loculi cut into the walls, holding whole generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they spoke is far less certain than where they lived: the corpora disagree over which inscriptions are Sabine at all, and most writing found there is on pottery — a flask 5.7 centimetres (2.2 inches) long need not have been made where it was buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That leaves 39 words the ancient grammarians label Sabine, and the sound-changes behind them do not agree, some being characteristic of Latin, some of Faliscan and some of one or another Sabellic language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome took the country in 290 BCE, and in 271 a channel some 2,200 metres (7,200 feet) long drained the Lacus Velinus, a lake of over 90 square kilometres (35 square miles) whose plain around Reate became farmland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cures, from which Roman tradition drew the city’s second king, contracted and was gradually abandoned, a decline read against the earthquake of 174 BCE that Livy reports from Sabine country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Sabines","questions":["The ancient grammarians label 39 surviving words as belonging to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the sound-changes behind them point to several different languages rather than to one.","Rome took the country of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 290 BCE, and in 271 a channel some two kilometres long drained the lake at its heart, turning the plain around Reate into farmland."],"tags":["people","history","italy","language","iron age"],"sources":["Annie Cecilia Burman, \"De Lingua Sabina: A Reappraisal of the Sabine Glosses\" (PhD diss., University of Cambridge, 2017), https://doi.org/10.17863/CAM.18502. [Open access]","Adriana Emiliozzi, Paola Moscati and Paola Santoro, \"The Princely Cart from Eretum,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i>, Supplemento 1 (2007): 143–62, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/Suppl_1/10_Emiliozzi.pdf. [Open access]","Francesca Colosi et al., \"Sabina Tiberina: modellizzazione della distribuzione delle presenze archeologiche,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 10 (1999): 275–87, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/PDF10/10_19_Colosi-G_Espa-S_Espa-Gabrielli-Ricci-Verga.pdf. [Open access]","Carlo Carrara et al., <i>The Rieti Intermountain Basin and S. Francesco d’Assisi</i>, Field Trip Guide Book D07, 32nd International Geological Congress, Florence, 20–28 August 2004 (Rome: APAT, 2004), https://www.isprambiente.gov.it/contentfiles/00006500/6589-d07.pdf. [Open access]","Antonio Colapietro, Mauro Lucarini and Gennaro Maria Monti, \"Cures Sabini: indagine geo-archeologica preliminare,\" <i>Bollettino della Società Geografica Italiana</i>, ser. 14, 1, no. 1 (2018): 201–13, https://doi.org/10.13128/bsgi.v1i1.100. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/The_Intervention_of_the_Sabine_Women_%28Vincent%29.png","title":"Sabines","desc":"1781 painting by François-André Vincent. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Intervention_of_the_Sabine_Women_(Vincent).png","alt":"The Intervention of the Sabine Women (Vincent)"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Sabina","at":[12.8667,42.4],"kind":"region","area":[[12.45,42.3],[12.66,42.12],[12.95,42.1],[13.2,42.2],[13.45,42.42],[13.3,42.72],[13,42.8],[12.7,42.62],[12.48,42.48]]}},
{"id":"rm-013","num":"13","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"A country full of hillforts, sanctuaries and scattered farms but with few if any cities is what set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> apart from the Latins and the Etruscans.","answer":"Samnites","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hillforts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th – 3rd century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Samnite Wars</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 343 – 290 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Samnites</b> were the people of the central and southern Apennines, and their country looked nothing like the Italy of the plains: full of hillforts, sanctuaries and dispersed farms and small clusters, with few if any cities of the kind the Latins and Etruscans built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek and Roman writers turned that into a moral fact rather than a settlement one, calling them rough highlanders and village dwellers and sometimes likening them to wild beasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their own tradition explained their spread by the ver sacrum or 'sacred spring', in which a community facing overpopulation sent part of itself away to seek land and become another people, often following an animal that carried a god's will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A lidar survey of 15,300 square kilometres (5,900 square miles) across five modern regions found 305 suspected hillforts, 299 of them recorded in no published material at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several command the tratturi, the drove roads of transhumance, the largest of them set on a spur above the route from Lucera to Castel di Sangro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What those walls enclosed is now being reopened: at Monte Santa Croce-Cognolo, an 18-hectare (44-acre) site long assumed to be inhabited throughout, magnetometry found nothing suggesting permanent housing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it did find were alignments of postholes and trenches more consistent with timber enclosures for livestock, so a monumental circuit need not mean a town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Public life ran instead through the sanctuaries, and Pietrabbondante, the great sanctuary of the Pentri, is taken for their political centre, the focus of the people under arms, where captured weapons including Roman armour were dedicated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Oscan inscriptions there name a meddix tuticus, chief magistrate of a touto, though the tidy pyramid of pagus, touto and Samnite League that handbooks build from such words rests on thinner evidence than it appears to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether Samnium had a hereditary aristocracy or any state-level administration before the wars is not clear from what survives, since that kind of evidence begins to appear only in the 2nd century BCE, after Rome had taken the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Samnites","questions":["A lidar survey of the central Apennines found 305 suspected hillforts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and 299 of them appeared in no published record at all.","A tradition in which part of a community breaks away to seek land elsewhere and becomes another people is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> explained their spread across the mountains."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","archaeology"],"sources":["Giacomo Fontana, \"Italy's Hidden Hillforts: A Large-Scale Lidar-Based Mapping of Samnium,\" <i>Journal of Field Archaeology</i> 47, no. 4 (2022): 245–61, https://doi.org/10.1080/00934690.2022.2031465. [Open access]","Giacomo Fontana and Wieke de Neef, \"Italy's Empty Hillforts: Reassessing Urban-Centric Biases through Combined Non-Invasive Prospection Methods on a Samnite Site (Fourth–Third Centuries BC),\" <i>Antiquity</i> 98, no. 402 (2024): 1558–75, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.157. [Open access]","Rafael Scopacasa, \"The Negotiation of Social Hierarchies in a Mountain Community of Pre-Roman Samnium, Central Italy,\" in <i>Dependency and Social Inequality in Pre-Roman Italy</i>, ed. Martin Bentz and Patrick Zeidler (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2024), 255–72, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/94264. [Open access]","Massimiliano Di Fazio, \"Herdsmen into Warriors: An Overview on Pastoralism and War in Ancient Central Italy,\" in <i>Dependency and Social Inequality in Pre-Roman Italy</i>, ed. Martin Bentz and Patrick Zeidler (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2024), 297–314, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/94264. [Open access]","Tesse D. Stek, <i>Cult Places and Cultural Change in Republican Italy</i> (Amsterdam: Amsterdam University Press, 2009), https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/34581. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Samnite_Bronze_Cuirass_Body_Armor%2C_South_Italy%2C_400-300_BC_%2810458801043%29.jpg/1920px-Samnite_Bronze_Cuirass_Body_Armor%2C_South_Italy%2C_400-300_BC_%2810458801043%29.jpg","title":"Samnites","desc":"Getty Villa Museum, Los Angeles, California: Roman, Greek, and Etruscan Antiquities. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samnite_Bronze_Cuirass_Body_Armor,_South_Italy,_400-300_BC_(10458801043).jpg","alt":"Samnite Bronze Cuirass Body Armor, South Italy, 400-300 BCE"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Samnium","at":[14.2397,41.6028],"kind":"region","area":[[13.95,41.88],[14.45,41.95],[15,41.55],[15.55,41.05],[15.3,40.72],[14.85,40.85],[14.45,41.05],[14.2,41.35]]}},
{"id":"rm-014","num":"14","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Where Etruscan culture predominated it was written in an Etruscan-derived alphabet and where Greek culture did in a Greek-derived one, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one language written several ways.","answer":"Oscan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE – 79 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Oscan</b> was the language of much of southern Italy, found across Campania, Samnium, Hirpinia, Lucania and Bruttium and as far as Messina, in some 800 inscriptions running from the 6th century BCE to the 1st century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the Sabellic languages, with Umbrian and several smaller traditions, and Sabellic sits inside Italic beside Latin and Faliscan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it was ever one tongue or a koiné drawn together from local traditions is argued, and the writing is the strongest evidence either way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where Etruscan culture predominated, in Campania and around it, Oscan was written in an alphabet developed from the Etruscan one; where Greek culture did, in Lucania and Bruttium, in an alphabet developed from the Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later documents use the Latin alphabet instead, so a single language is written three ways according to whose script was to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the texts are short: votive dedications, marks of ownership, epitaphs and boundary stones, with a handful of long legal and religious documents such as the Tabula Bantina and the law from Roccagloriosa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost nothing of an Oscan literature survives, though the Ludi Osci and the Atellan farces carried from Campania to Rome imply there was one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its most public moment was the Social War, where the rebels' first coins carried ITALIA in Latin, the one language they all shared, and Oscan became the commoner legend only after the Umbrians and Etruscans left the revolt in 90 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the war it was never used in an official text again, and how much longer it was written at all turns on one Pompeian inscription, long read as an imperial copy of a 2nd-century BCE original and argued to be that original itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The corpus can now be questioned as a whole, its inscriptions annotated alongside Umbrian, Messapic, Venetic and early Latin in one database of more than 36,000 words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Oscan","questions":["Some 800 inscriptions of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survive from Campania, Samnium, Lucania and Bruttium, running from the 6th century BCE to graffiti at Pompeii in 79 CE.","After the Social War no official text was written in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> again, though graffiti at Pompeii show it was still being written when the town was buried."],"tags":["language","history","italy","iron age"],"sources":["\"The Oscan Language,\" <i>Lingue e culture dell'Italia antica: linguistica storica e modelli digitali</i> (PRIN project, University of Florence), https://www.prin-italia-antica.unifi.it/vp-133-the-oscan-language.html. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Oscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=56&lang=en. [Open access]","Katherine McDonald, \"The Testament of Vibius Adiranus,\" <i>Journal of Roman Studies</i> 102 (2012): 40–55, https://katherinemcdonald.net/wp-content/uploads/2015/08/mcdonald-testament-of-vibius-adiranus.pdf. [Open access]","Lucia Carbone, \"The First ITALIA on Coinage,\" <i>Pocket Change</i> (American Numismatic Society), 13 October 2020, https://numismatics.org/pocketchange/italia/. [Open access]","Reuben J. Pitts, \"Corpus of the Epigraphy of the Italian Peninsula in the 1st Millennium BCE (CEIPoM),\" <i>Journal of Open Humanities Data</i> 8 (2022): 1, https://doi.org/10.5334/johd.65. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg/1920px-OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg","title":"Oscan","desc":"Stele bearing an inscription in Oscan (British Museum, London). Around 300 - 100 BCE.C. Found in the inner arch of the Nola Gate in Pompeii. Reads from right to left: \"Vibius Popidius, son of Vibius, chief magistrate, was responsible for this work and approved it\". Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg","alt":"OsqueBritishMuseum"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-015","num":"15","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Pliny called them the oldest people of Italy, and by the 9th century BCE the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had one of the first well-defined identities in central Italy.","answer":"Umbrians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Identity formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th – 8th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 260 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Umbrians</b> were the people of ancient Umbria, larger than the modern region: it ran east and west of the Tiber to the edge of the Sabina and over the Apennines into the modern Marche and Emilia-Romagna, leaving out Etruscan places such as Perusia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pliny called them the oldest people of Italy and passed on a Greek explanation of the name, that they had survived the floods; their origins are still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the Early Iron Age of the 9th and 8th centuries BCE they were among the first communities of central Italy with a well-defined identity, beside the Etruscans, the Picentes and the Samnites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They began on the left bank of the Tiber and spread west, until around the 6th century BCE the Etruscans took that ground and the river became the border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their settlements gathered in the Apennine basins at Todi, Terni, Gubbio and Ameria and on the Colfiorito plateau, where villages of huts stood a regular 500 metres (1,600 feet) apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their sacred places were mostly open air, sometimes no more than a pit in bedrock, and of nine fully excavated Umbrian sanctuaries only three had a structure marking the holy ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Above Gubbio the summits of Monte Ingino and Monte Ansciano carried such places, used seasonally — sheep and pig teeth point to spring — and one took offerings again after a gap of three centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Umbrian is a Sabellic language written two ways, in an alphabet derived from the Etruscan and later in the Latin one, its epigraphy running from the 4th century BCE to the 1st.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The corpus is badly lopsided: one long ritual text from Gubbio and some thirty short inscriptions, the weightiest of those the bronze Mars of Todi and a helmet from Bologna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rome reached Umbria in the 4th century BCE and held it by the middle of the 3rd, though whether the Umbrians fought at Sentinum in 295 is doubtful: Livy and Polybius report them absent and the triumphal record omits them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Umbrians","questions":["One long ritual text from Gubbio and about thirty short inscriptions are nearly all that survives of what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote in their own language.","Starting on the left bank of the Tiber, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread west until the Etruscans took that ground and the river became the border between the two."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","religion"],"sources":["Alessandra Modi et al., \"The Mitogenome Portrait of Umbria in Central Italy as Depicted by Contemporary Inhabitants and Pre-Roman Remains,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 10700, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-67445-0. [Open access]","Arianna Zapelloni Pavia, <i>Continuity and Change in Ancient Umbrian Cult Places</i>, Mnemosyne Supplements 485 (Leiden: Brill, 2024), https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/98984. [Open access]","\"The Umbrian Language,\" <i>Lingue e culture dell'Italia antica: linguistica storica e modelli digitali</i> (PRIN project, University of Florence), https://www.prin-italia-antica.unifi.it/vp-151-the-umbrian-language.html. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Umbrian: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=58&lang=en. [Open access]","Marianna Negro et al., \"Revisiting Gubbio: Settlement Patterns and Ritual from the Middle Palaeolithic to the Roman Era,\" <i>Land</i> 13, no. 9 (2024): 1369, https://doi.org/10.3390/land13091369. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Typological_distribution_of_votive_figurines_across_Umbrian_sanctuaries.png","title":"Umbrians","desc":"Typological distribution of votive figurines across Umbrian sanctuaries. Arianna Pavia. 2024. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Typological_distribution_of_votive_figurines_across_Umbrian_sanctuaries.png","alt":"Typological distribution of votive figurines across Umbrian sanctuaries"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Umbria","at":[12.5793,43.353],"kind":"region","area":[[12.3,43.5],[12.5,44],[12.75,44.08],[13.35,43.65],[13.15,43.28],[12.95,42.85],[12.72,42.55],[12.45,42.6],[12.25,43.05]]}},
{"id":"rm-016","num":"16","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Seven bronze tablets from Gubbio, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry the longest ritual text to survive from ancient Italy, with no Latin or Greek liturgy approaching their detail.","answer":"Iguvine Tables","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 1st century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Iguvine Tables</b> are seven bronze tablets from Iguvium, the modern Gubbio, and they carry the longest ritual text surviving from ancient Italy: no liturgy in Latin or Greek approaches them for detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They came to light near the Roman theatre, traditionally in 1444, though the earliest secure document is the deed of 1456 by which the seven passed to the commune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were inscribed at different times between the 3rd and 1st centuries BCE, first in the alphabet the Umbrians had taken from the Etruscans and later in the Latin one, though the rites themselves are older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are also nearly the whole of Umbrian: about 4,000 words here against some thirty short inscriptions everywhere else, which makes them the longest text in any non-Latin language of ancient Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What they record are the proceedings and prayers of the Atiedian Brothers, a priestly association who in early times were probably also the political leaders of the community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rites are purifications and sacrifices, some to be performed when the omens came out wrong, others fixed to feasts or to points in the farming year, and in places the words of the prayer itself are written down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before a lustration of the people the officiant is told to go out and watch the birds, and only on returning to put on his vestment and drive the victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One fire ritual has the fire kindled on the way out to the field and set on the altar in the grove, carried there in a vessel the text names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same ceremony is set out twice over, once in the native script and once in Latin letters, which is what allows the older alphabet to be read against the newer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The text also instructs the community to shun its neighbours, so the liturgy is as much a statement of who Iguvium was not as a record of what it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Iguvine Tables","questions":["Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set out the same ceremony in the native Umbrian script and again in Latin letters, the older alphabet can be read against the newer.","The prayers and proceedings of a priestly brotherhood at Iguvium are set down in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which tell the officiant to go out and watch the birds before purifying the people."],"tags":["text","history","italy","religion","language"],"sources":["\"Room with the Iguvine Tables,\" Museo Civico di Palazzo dei Consoli, Gubbio, https://www.palazzodeiconsoli.it/en/room-with-the-iguvine-tables/. [Open access]","\"The Iguvine Tables,\" Museo Civico Palazzo dei Consoli, Gubbio, <i>MeTU: Musei e Territorio dell'Umbria</i>, https://www.umbriametu.it/museums/museo-civico-palazzo-dei-consoli-gubbio/the-iguvine-tables/?lang=en. [Open access]","Gregory Nagy, \"The Fire Ritual of the Iguvine Tables: Facing a Central Problem in the Study of Ritual Language,\" <i>Classical World</i> 100, no. 2 (2007): 151–57; 2nd ed. online 2015, https://chs.harvard.edu/curated-article/gregory-nagy-the-fire-ritual-of-the-iguvine-tables-facing-a-central-problem-in-the-study-of-ritual-language/. [Open access]","Arianna Zapelloni Pavia, <i>Continuity and Change in Ancient Umbrian Cult Places</i>, Mnemosyne Supplements 485 (Leiden: Brill, 2024), https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/98984. [Open access]","\"The Umbrian Language,\" <i>Lingue e culture dell'Italia antica: linguistica storica e modelli digitali</i> (PRIN project, University of Florence), https://www.prin-italia-antica.unifi.it/vp-151-the-umbrian-language.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Tavole_iguvine%2C_in_lingua_umbra_III-I_secolo_ac._ca.%2C_00.JPG/1920px-Tavole_iguvine%2C_in_lingua_umbra_III-I_secolo_ac._ca.%2C_00.JPG","title":"Iguvine Tables","desc":"Iguvine Tablets. Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tavole_iguvine,_in_lingua_umbra_III-I_secolo_ac._ca.,_00.JPG","alt":"Tavole iguvine, in lingua umbra III-I secolo ac. ca., 00"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"rm-017","num":"17","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Roman writers set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside the Aequi among the enemies who pressed hardest on the young city, coming down from the Apennines towards the Pontine plain.","answer":"Volsci","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Took Satricum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">488 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Their graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th – early 4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Volsci</b> were a people of the hills and plains south-east of Rome, and Roman writers set them beside the Aequi among the enemies who pressed hardest on the young city. The historical narrative makes them aggressive: a community that came down from the Apennine country towards the Pontine plain during the 5th century BCE, until it occupied Satricum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their speech was Sabellic, and Volscian is counted among the minor Italic traditions that with Paelignian, Vestinian, Marrucinian, Marsian and perhaps Aequian make up a Middle-Italic linguistic area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Satricum lay about 60 kilometres (37 miles) south of Rome on the river Astura, grown from a hamlet of huts in the 9th century BCE to nearly 40 hectares by the 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1980 a burial ground of at least 200 graves came to light in its south-west corner, its pottery of the 5th and early 4th centuries BCE, and those graves have been given to the Volsci.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Among them were about twenty children's graves, some of the richest in the cemetery, and one held a fine miniature lead axe inscribed in a non-Latin language identified as Volscian, in an alphabet unknown in Latium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The burials sit awkwardly with the warlike picture: 16 weapons in almost 200 graves, mostly with older people, which reads as a mark of rank rather than a working armoury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Isotope work on 43 third molars from three of the presumed Volscian cemeteries found every individual local, and the excavators' own account calls these Volsci an indigenous community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Aequi held the mountain country further east, and Rome's answer to them stands at Alba Fucens, on a hill of 950 to 1,000 metres (3,100 to 3,300 feet) above the Fucine plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That Latin colony was planted in 303 BCE in Aequian territory, on the line of the Via Tiburtina Valeria, and is now the largest archaeological area in the Apennines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Volsci","questions":["A miniature lead axe from a child's grave at Satricum carries an inscription in the language of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, written in an alphabet unknown in Latium.","Only 16 weapons turned up in almost 200 graves at Satricum, which fits badly with the warlike picture Roman writers drew of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who took the town."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","language"],"sources":["Massimiliano Di Fazio, \"Herdsmen into Warriors. An Overview on Pastoralism and War in Ancient Central Italy,\" in <i>Dependency and Social Inequality in Pre-Roman Italy</i>, ed. Martin Bentz and Patrick Zeidler (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2024), 297–314, https://doi.org/10.1515/9783111558417-012. [Open access]","\"Satricum,\" \"Excavations Southwest Necropolis 1980–1986\" and \"Epigraphic Finds,\" Satricum Project, Department of Archaeology, University of Amsterdam, https://www.uva.nl/en/discipline/archaeology/research/satricum/southwest-necropolis/southwest-necropolis.html. [Open access]","Maria Alicandro et al., \"Alba Fucens Archaeological Site: Multiscale and Multidisciplinary Approach for Risk Assessment and Conservation,\" <i>International Archives of the Photogrammetry, Remote Sensing and Spatial Information Sciences</i> XLII-2/W11 (2019): 47–53, https://doi.org/10.5194/isprs-archives-XLII-2-W11-47-2019. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Oscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=56&lang=en. [Open access]","Amanda Sengeløv et al., \"Understanding the Post-Archaic Population of Satricum, Italy: A Bioarchaeological Approach,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science: Reports</i> 31 (2020): 102285, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jasrep.2020.102285. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/John_Harris_Valda_-_A_Roman_battle_with_the_Volscians.jpg","title":"Volsci","desc":"John Harris Valda - A Roman battle with the Volscians. John Harris Valda. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Harris_Valda_-_A_Roman_battle_with_the_Volscians.jpg","alt":"John Harris Valda - A Roman battle with the Volscians"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Satricum","at":[12.755083,41.513128],"zoom":9}},
{"id":"rm-018","num":"18","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Living in hilltop forts behind dry-stone walls, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the country from modern Piedmont and Liguria across into southern France before Rome came.","answer":"Ligurians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deported</span><span class=\"dt-v\">180 – 179 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ligurians</b> were the Iron Age people of the north-west, settled across what are now Liguria, Piedmont, western Lombardy and Emilia and on into southern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They took shape between the Middle Bronze Age, about 1600 BCE, and the opening of the Iron Age around 900, and are counted a people of the 9th to the 2nd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They lived in castellari, hilltop forts ringed with dry-stone walls and set where the ground already defended them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their graves are those of fighters: the chief buried at Pietra Ligure went down with weapons including an antenna sword of central European type, and at Savignone a sword was folded back on itself before burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek and Roman writers set them down as tribes of barbarians — illiterate, treacherous and lying, but daring and brave in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The charge of lying can be traced to one man: Cato wrote in his <i>Origines</i> that they were unlettered and untruthful, and he wrote it to explain why he could not establish where they had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had defended them in the senate in 190 BCE, and what turned him was the risings of 187 to 175 and the welcome they had given Hannibal's brother Mago in 205; the slur stayed rare, picked up by Virgil and Cicero and almost nobody else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome's own answer to those risings was deportation: about 47,000 Apuan Ligurians were moved from the Magra and Vara valleys to Samnium in two operations, in 180 and 179 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Planted on land confiscated from the Samnites, they became the Ligures Baebiani, named for the consul who organised the transfer, and magnetic survey at Macchia di Circello has traced their main street and a market building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A bronze plate of 117 BCE, found in 1506 in the Polcevera valley, shows the settlement of it: two Roman senators fixing what Genoa and the neighbouring Ligurian tribes might each take from the public land they had grazed for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ligurians","questions":["About 47,000 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were moved from the Magra and Vara valleys to Samnium in two operations of 180 and 179 BCE, and settled on confiscated land.","Cato wrote that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were unlettered and untruthful, which was less an observation than a way of explaining why he could not establish where they came from."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","warfare"],"sources":["\"The Metal Ages,\" Museo Diffuso del Finale, Istituto Internazionale di Studi Liguri – Sezione Finalese, https://mudifinale.com/en/percorso-tematico/the-metal-ages/. [Open access]","\"The Ligurians and the Birth of Genoa\" and \"The Ligurians and Rome,\" Museo di Archeologia Ligure, Musei di Genova, https://www.museidigenova.it/en/ligurians-and-birth-genoa. [Open access]","Maciej Piegdoń, \"Sententia Minuciorum/Tabula Polcevera from 117 BCE – Arbitration as an Instrument for Resolving Disputes among Allies during the Republican Period and a Source to Analyse Rome's Policy Regarding Subordinate Lands,\" <i>In Gremium: Studies in History, Culture and Politics</i>, no. 18 (2025): 77–91, https://doi.org/10.61826/ig.vi18.449. [Open access]","Sina Clara Lehnig and Nikola Babucic, \"Forced Resettlement as an Imperial Strategy: Evidence from the Ligures Baebiani in Roman Samnium,\" <i>Antiquity</i>, First View (2026): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2026.10391. [Open access]","Michel Dubuisson, \"Caton et les Ligures: l'origine d'un stéréotype,\" <i>Revue belge de Philologie et d'Histoire</i> 68, no. 1 (1990): 73–83, https://www.persee.fr/doc/rbph_0035-0818_1990_num_68_1_3700. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Celtas_ligures_iberos.jpg","title":"Ligurians","desc":"Map of the distribution of prerroman tribes in the Iberian Peninsula. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celtas_ligures_iberos.jpg","alt":"Celtas ligures iberos"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Liguria","at":[8.9339,44.4072],"kind":"region","area":[[6.9,43.62],[7.1,44.3],[7.3,44.95],[8.2,45.35],[9.3,45.15],[10.35,44.72],[10.25,44.1],[9.85,44.05],[8.95,44.28],[7.6,43.79]]}},
{"id":"rm-019","num":"19","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"At the sanctuary of Reitia at Este the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left bronze writing tablets and styluses as votives, which is how we know how they taught the alphabet.","answer":"Veneti","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE – 50 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Veneti</b> lived in north-eastern Italy, in what is now the Veneto, and spoke an Indo-European language slightly similar to Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It survives on several hundred brief inscriptions scattered across the region and dating between about 550 BCE and 50 CE, some of them still obscure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hierarchy and proto-urban settlement are visible from the 8th century BCE, and Padua and Este may have had a full urban layout by the 6th, when many of the great sanctuaries were flourishing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They wrote in an alphabet taken from the Etruscans in two phases: a northern Etruscan model through the 6th century, then a southern one that split into distinct local varieties at Este, Padua and Vicenza.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Situla art, the figured bronze vessels of the region, ran from about 650 to 275 BCE, and it and writing alike spread first in elite hands through deep contact with Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The sanctuary of the goddess Reitia at Este holds the best evidence in ancient Italy for how writing was taught, because the tablets and styluses used to teach it were offered there as votives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bronze tablets carry a grid of five rows of sixteen boxes holding a list of fifteen consonants and the five vowels repeated, and the styluses, 13 to 26 centimetres (5 to 10 inches) long, are large enough to have been working tools rather than models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their inscribed grave markers are arranged in strikingly different ways, some running round a cylinder, some beside figured panels, some with the second line upside down against the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cremation was the usual rite and inhumation may have been reserved for low-ranking people; isotopes from nineteen inhumed individuals at Padua's CUS-Piovego cemetery show a narrow diet leaning heavily on millet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They kept their independence through the Celtic invasion of the Po plain in the 4th century BCE and the first Roman advance north; Roman policy hardened in the 2nd, and the region was annexed in the 1st.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Veneti","questions":["Holding out through the Celtic invasion of the Po plain and the first Roman advance north, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were not annexed until the 1st century BCE.","Several hundred short inscriptions in an Indo-European language close to Latin, running from about 550 BCE to 50 CE, are what survive of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and their speech."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","language"],"sources":["Laura Montagnaro, \"Venetic,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Scrittura&id=31&lang=en. [Open access]","Elisa Perego, \"Magic and Ritual in Iron Age Veneto, Italy,\" <i>Papers from the Institute of Archaeology</i> 20 (2010): 67–96, https://doi.org/10.5334/pia.342. [Open access]","Elisa Perego, \"The Other Writing: Iconic Literacy and Situla Art in Pre-Roman Veneto (Italy),\" in <i>Writing as Material Practice: Substance, Surface and Medium</i> (London: Ubiquity Press, 2013), 253–70, https://doi.org/10.5334/bai.m. [Open access]","Giusy Capasso et al., \"Dietary Perspectives on Social Asymmetry in a Full Iron Age Community of Northern Italy: Stable Isotope Evidence from the Patavine CUS-Piovego Necropolis,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 15208, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-51643-3. [Open access]","Ruth D. Whitehouse, \"'Tombstones' in the North Italian Iron Age: Careless Writers or Athletic Readers?,\" in <i>Writing as Material Practice: Substance, Surface and Medium</i> (London: Ubiquity Press, 2013), 271–88, https://doi.org/10.5334/bai.n. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Panel_of_Este%2C_Venetic_Inscription.jpg","title":"Veneti","desc":"The Panel of Este (VI sec. b.c.). An ancient instrument for teaching the Venetic language. The inscription represents the possible articulations of the same word. Giorgio Roncolato, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panel_of_Este,_Venetic_Inscription.jpg","alt":"Panel of Este, Venetic Inscription"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Venetia","at":[11.8733,45.4078],"kind":"region","area":[[11.05,45.4],[11.35,45.85],[12.05,46.05],[12.75,45.95],[13.05,45.75],[12.75,45.4],[12.35,45.05],[11.75,44.95],[11.1,45.05]]}},
{"id":"rm-020","num":"20","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"One of the three Iapygian cultures of Iron Age Apulia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the heel of Italy and wrote in an alphabet modelled on the Greek of nearby Taranto.","answer":"Messapians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Writing ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hannibalic war, c. 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Messapians</b> held the heel of Italy, and were one of the three cultures — with the Peucetians and the Daunians — that made up the Iapygian civilisation of Iron Age Apulia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> All three names reach us from Greek documents rather than from anything the peoples themselves wrote about who they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their own writing survives as Messapic, a corpus running from the middle of the 6th century to the end of the 2nd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standard collection, published in 2002, registered 545 epigraphic items with 48 more counted doubtful and about 30 coins, and almost a hundred further texts have appeared since at Vaste, Castro and Ostuni.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nearest model for their alphabet is the Laconian one of Taranto, the Greek city on their border, and some locally made pieces carry Greek letters outright, probably the work of resident Greek craftsmen living among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the corpus is funerary; a smaller group is religious, and a scarce few public inscriptions on stone carry the longest texts of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The trozzella is theirs and nobody else's: a vase with a funerary function chiefly linked to high-ranking women, its modern name taken from the little wheels set on its handles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The burial grounds are substantial — the necropolis of Monte d'Elia at Alezio belongs to one of the most important ancient settlements in the Salento peninsula, and geophysics is now used to trace how far it runs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their landscape held towns behind major walls and lines of intervisible towers, and at Muro Leccese the largest Archaic house yet found in Messapia has been dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That house ended in arrowheads, catapult balls and fire, and the writing stops with the wars against Hannibal, after which the region blends into the Italiote world Rome was assembling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Messapians","questions":["The trozzella, a wheel-handled vase buried mainly with high-ranking women, is a form made by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and by nobody else in ancient Italy.","Some 545 inscriptions running from the mid-6th to the late 2nd century BCE are what remains of the language of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them from graves."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","language"],"sources":["Simona Marchesini, \"Messapic,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore, updated December 2022), https://doi.org/10.25429/sns.it/lettere/mnamon044. [Open access]","Serena Aneli et al., \"The Genetic Origin of Daunians and the Pan-Mediterranean Southern Italian Iron Age Context,\" <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 39, no. 2 (2022): msac014, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8826970/. [Open access]","Angela Falezza and Francesco Iacono, \"Archaeological Discoveries and New Data from South Italy,\" <i>Archaeological Reports</i> 70 (2024): 139–72, https://doi.org/10.1017/S057060842400005X. [Open access]","\"The Apulian Ceramics,\" Museum of Archaeological Sciences and Art, University of Padua, https://archeologia.musei.unipd.it/en/discover-the-museum/the-apulian-ceramics/. [Open access]","Lara De Giorgi et al., \"Electromagnetic Survey at the Messapian Necropolis in Alezio (Lecce, Italy),\" in <i>IMEKO TC4 International Conference on Metrology for Archaeology and Cultural Heritage</i> (Rome, 2023), https://doi.org/10.21014/tc4-ARC-2023.189. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Map_of_Ancient_Italy%2C_Southern_Part.jpg","title":"Messapians","desc":"Reference Map of Ancient Italy, Southern Part. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Ancient_Italy,_Southern_Part.jpg","alt":"Map of Ancient Italy, Southern Part"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Messapia","at":[18.1667,40.35],"kind":"region","area":[[17.25,40.47],[17.6,40.68],[17.95,40.72],[18.35,40.55],[18.52,40.15],[18.36,39.79],[18,39.98],[17.75,40.25]]}},
{"id":"rm-021","num":"21","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Settling the Po valley from about 400 BCE with La Tène gear, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> absorbed the older Lepontic-speaking communities of the lake country.","answer":"Cisalpine Gauls","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Arrived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Italy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th – 1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cisalpine Gauls</b> were the transalpine Celtic communities established on the Italian peninsula between the 4th and the 1st centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lake country between Lugano, Maggiore and Como had belonged before them to the Lepontii, whose speech is reckoned the oldest attested Continental Celtic and is known from about 150 short inscriptions running from the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those inscriptions fall in two phases: an earlier one belonging to the last part of the Golasecca culture, and a second opening around 400 BCE with the invasion of the Po valley by Gauls carrying the material of La Tène.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gaulish itself was written here in an Etruscan alphabet, the oldest of the three ways the language was ever set down, with half a dozen inscriptions concentrated around Lugano.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two of that corpus's three stone inscriptions, at Todi and Vercelli, are bilingual in Gaulish and Latin, and belong at least to the second part of the 3rd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the Apennines behind Bologna, Monte Bibele is a village with a necropolis and a votive deposit of the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, an Etruscan foundation of some 7,000 square metres (1.7 acres) sealed by a sudden fire late in the 3rd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It belongs to a wider reordering of those hills, small settlements set on the passes and held by Italic peoples and transalpine Celts in alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Strontium and dental evidence from 38 burials at Monterenzio Vecchio, across the valley, found locals and incomers together, the incomers sharing dental affinities with Celts from Yorkshire, and no measurable difference in grave goods between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Cenomani cemetery at Verona gives a different answer: of 49 individuals of the 3rd to 1st centuries BCE, one is clearly not local and five more may not be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The migration was real and the communities it made were mixed and settled quickly, and Lepontic was absorbed first into Gaulish and then, once Rome held the north, into Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cisalpine Gauls","questions":["At Monterenzio Vecchio the graves of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held both local people and incomers, and the grave goods do not tell the two apart.","Bilingual stones at Todi and Vercelli, cut in Gaulish and Latin, are among the few stone inscriptions the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left in Italy."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","migration"],"sources":["Francesca Ciurli, \"Celtic, Lepontic,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Scrittura&id=59&lang=en. [Open access]","Coline Ruiz Darasse, \"Celtic, Gaulish,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore, updated April 2022), https://doi.org/10.25429/sns.it/lettere/mnamon027. [Open access]","Rita Sorrentino et al., \"Unravelling Biocultural Population Structure in 4th/3rd Century BC Monterenzio Vecchio (Bologna, Italy) through a Comparative Analysis of Strontium Isotopes, Non-metric Dental Evidence, and Funerary Practices,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 3 (2018): e0193796, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0193796. [Open access]","Zita Laffranchi et al., \"'Celts' up and down the Alps: Insights on Mobility Patterns in the Pre-Roman/Celtic Population from Verona (NE Italy, 3rd–1st c. BCE),\" <i>American Journal of Biological Anthropology</i> 178, no. 3 (2022): 513–29, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9544713/. [Open access]","Annachiara Penzo, Federica Proni and Antonio Gottarelli, \"The Archaeological Settlement of Monte Bibele (Bologna),\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 28, no. 2 (2017): 571–80, https://doi.org/10.19282/AC.28.2.2017.47. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Gallia_cisalpina_-_Shepherd_png.png","title":"Cisalpine Gauls","desc":"Territori della Gallia cisalpina attorno agli inizi del I secolo a.C. Historical Atlas by William R. Shepherd, 1911. Courtesy of the University of Texas Libraries, The University of Texas at Austin. From The Historical Atlas by William R. Shepherd, 1911 edition. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gallia_cisalpina_-_Shepherd_png.png","alt":"Gallia cisalpina - Shepherd png"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-022","num":"22","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Rome's great neighbour to the north, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled the country between the Arno, the Tiber and the sea with rival city-states and never became an empire.","answer":"Etruscan civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Nucleation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Etruscan civilisation</b> was that of the rich communities of Tyrrhenian central Italy in the 1st millennium BCE, between the Arno, the Tiber and the sea, and was absorbed at last into the Roman empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its political landscape was set by a gathering of population in the 10th century BCE running alongside the making of an identity; it went furthest in the south and on the coast, less far north and inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scattered sites of 2 or 3 hectares (5 to 7 acres) were left for centres on plateaus of 50 to 185 hectares (120 to 460 acres), and by the Orientalising and Archaic periods these were full city-states, spaced on average 15 to 25 kilometres (9 to 16 miles) apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two kinds of town stood side by side: robust ones that held together for four or five centuries with practices for handing on authority, and delicate ones that lasted a few generations and then dispersed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No Etruscan empire ever came of it, because rivalry between descent group and community, and between one centre and the next, kept the whole in equilibrium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Those two loyalties were built in stone, the community in encircling city walls and the descent groups in cemeteries outside them so large that they were settlements in their own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing was distributed as unevenly as power: in the south literacy was held tightly by the great centres, while in the north it reached far down into the small ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Etruscan inscriptions run from the 8th century BCE to the 1st, and the language was spoken well beyond Etruria — south around Capua and Pontecagnano, north between Bologna and Verucchio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their reach was commercial too: amphorae shipped to Lattara in southern France about 525 to 475 BCE held wine resinated with pine and flavoured with rosemary, basil and thyme, and the trade ended in vines planted there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Prosperity had a price, and at Pontecagnano five of 29 children buried between 730 and 580 BCE show scurvy, read as the cost of an agriculture leaning ever harder on cereals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan civilisation","questions":["Robust towns that lasted five centuries stood beside delicate ones that dispersed after a few generations, and both belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Tyrrhenian central Italy.","Amphorae of resinated wine reached a port in southern France about 500 BCE from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and vines were planted there soon afterwards."],"tags":["culture","history","italy","iron age","archaeology"],"sources":["Simon Stoddart et al., \"Patterns of Etruscan Urbanism,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 7 (2020): 1, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2020.00001. [Open access]","Simon Stoddart, \"Power and Place in Etruria,\" in <i>Eurasia at the Dawn of History: Urbanization and Social Change</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2017), 304–18, https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/handle/1810/260822. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=35&lang=en. [Open access]","Rachele Simonit et al., \"Infantile Scurvy as a Consequence of Agricultural Intensification in the 1st Millennium BCE Etruria Campana,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 21396, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-48455-0. [Open access]","Patrick E. McGovern et al., \"Beginning of Viniculture in France,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 25 (2013): 10147–52, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3690885/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Bronze_chariot_inlaid_with_ivory_MET_DP137936.jpg/1920px-Bronze_chariot_inlaid_with_ivory_MET_DP137936.jpg","title":"Etruscan civilisation","desc":"Etruscan; Chariot; Bronzes. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_chariot_inlaid_with_ivory_MET_DP137936.jpg","alt":"Bronze chariot inlaid with ivory"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Etruria","at":[11.7561,42.2492],"kind":"region","area":[[10.15,43.95],[10.95,44.05],[11.55,43.85],[11.95,43.5],[12.25,43.2],[12.45,42.8],[12.55,42.35],[12.5,42.05],[12.35,41.9],[11.95,41.8],[11.3,42.25],[10.5,42.85],[10.15,43.5]]}},
{"id":"rm-023","num":"23","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The argument over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is as old as Herodotus, who derived them from Anatolia, and Dionysius of Halicarnassus, who held they had always been where they were.","answer":"Etruscan origins","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Local roots</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Genomes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">800 BCE – 1000 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan origins</b> have been disputed since antiquity, and the argument has always had two sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek writers Herodotus and Hellanicus of Lesbos derived the Etruscans from Anatolia or the Aegean, a reading the Greek elements arriving in Etruria during the Orientalising period of the 8th to 6th centuries BCE did nothing to discourage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius of Halicarnassus, writing in the 1st century BCE, held the opposite, that they had grown up where they stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists now favour Dionysius, tracing the civilisation to the late Bronze Age Proto-Villanovan communities of about 900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That consensus is a modern achievement as much as an ancient one, argued after Fascism from continuities in material culture across the turn of the 2nd and 1st millennia BCE and from unbroken occupation of the same places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What keeps the question alive is the language, a non-Indo-European isolate surrounded by Italic speakers and still not fully understood, its inscriptions running from the 8th century BCE to the 1st and consisting mostly of names and repeating formulas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is not quite alone: Etruscan, Lemnian and Raetic were grouped in 1998 into a family called Tyrsenian, and the stele from Kaminia on Lemnos shares Etruscan's case endings and words — <i>nefts</i> for a descendant, <i>avils</i> for year, <i>sealchvis</i> for forty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> But the split between the three is reckoned prehistoric and probably older than the Bronze Age, so the family points far behind any voyage of the 6th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genomes from 82 people buried in Etruria and southern Italy between 800 BCE and 1000 CE found Indo-European steppe ancestry among the Etruscans and no recent Anatolian admixture at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The local gene pool held across the 1st millennium BCE even while taking in individuals of central European, north African and Near Eastern descent, and shifted abruptly only under the Roman empire, when about half the ancestry became eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan origins","questions":["Genomes from 82 people buried between 800 BCE and 1000 CE bear on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, finding steppe ancestry and no recent Anatolian admixture in the Iron Age.","Because Etruscan, Lemnian and Raetic split prehistorically, the family bears on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by pointing much further back than any voyage from the Aegean."],"tags":["concept","history","italy","genetics","language"],"sources":["Cosimo Posth et al., \"The Origin and Legacy of the Etruscans through a 2000-Year Archeogenomic Time Transect,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 39 (2021): eabi7673, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abi7673. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Lemnian: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=26&lang=en. [Open access]","Simona Marchesini, \"Rhaetic: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=41&lang=en. [Open access]","Simon Stoddart, \"Power and Place in Etruria,\" in <i>Eurasia at the Dawn of History: Urbanization and Social Change</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2017), 304–18, https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/handle/1810/260822. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=35&lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Etruscan_funerary_stone_inscription_%28CIE_5071%29.png/1920px-Etruscan_funerary_stone_inscription_%28CIE_5071%29.png","title":"Etruscan origins","desc":"Drawing of Etruscan funerary stone inscription from Orvieto (CIE 5071). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_funerary_stone_inscription_(CIE_5071).png","alt":"Etruscan funerary stone inscription (CIE 5071)"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"rm-024","num":"24","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Each of the four great <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the south — Veii, Tarquinia, Caere and Vulci — covered between 125 and 175 hectares (310 and 430 acres) of volcanic plateau.","answer":"Etruscan cities","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plateau sites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Perugia's gates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 2nd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan cities</b> stood on volcanic plateaus, and the four great ones of the south — Veii, Tarquinia, Caere and Vulci — each covered between 125 and 175 hectares (310 and 430 acres), a scale nothing before them approached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were probably not built up all over: the evidence points to nuclei of people occupying separate parts of each plateau, with perhaps some common identity across the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The countryside around them filled and then thinned, 549 dwelling sites being known from the Iron Age and Orientalising, 1,248 from the Archaic and 914 from the Post-Archaic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fortification mattered, though how much is argued: there was large-scale effort to wall the new sites, while the early defences at Veii's Piazza d'Armi are now thought to be medieval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What certainly survives is later and unmistakable, the gates of Perugia, built between the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE and now all but swallowed by the houses grown around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Not every city grew by accretion: Marzabotto, in the Apennines above Bologna, was laid out at the end of the 6th century BCE on a regular plan of insulae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its layout is thought to have been set by astronomical and solar events, so that the streets on the ground repeated a pattern read in the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside one of those regular blocks stood a peripteral temple to Tinia, the highest god of the Etruscan pantheon, and a Tuscanic temple of about 500 BCE was found beside it in 2014 and 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city was abandoned during the 4th century BCE, when Celtic migration reached the Po plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smaller places keep the same shape of plateau and cemetery, and at San Giuliano an Etruscan town and its rock-cut necropolis are being dug to follow how settlement there shifted from about 800 BCE onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan cities","questions":["Unlike most <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Marzabotto was laid out at once on a regular plan of insulae at the end of the 6th century BCE, and abandoned when the Celts came.","The gates of Perugia, built between the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE, are among the best-preserved remains of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, now swallowed by the houses around them."],"tags":["place","history","italy","iron age","archaeology"],"sources":["Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","\"The Etruscan City of Kainua-Marzabotto,\" Department of History and Cultures, University of Bologna, https://disci.unibo.it/en/research/archaeological-missions/archaeological-missions-italy/etruscan-city-kainua-marzabotto. [Open access]","Fabio Radicioni et al., \"The Etruscan City Gates of Perugia: Geomatic Techniques for the Documentation and Study of an Urban History Heritage,\" in <i>Proceedings of ARQUEOLÓGICA 2.0</i> (Valencia: Universitat Politècnica de València, 2021), https://doi.org/10.4995/arqueologica9.2021.12058. [Open access]","Simon Stoddart et al., \"Patterns of Etruscan Urbanism,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 7 (2020): 1, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2020.00001. [Open access]","Davide Zori et al., \"From Etruscan Urban Centre to Medieval Fortified Village: San Giuliano Archaeological Research Project,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 363 (2018): e6, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2018.99. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Etruscan_civilization_map.png","title":"Etruscan cities","desc":"A map showing the extent of Etruria and the Etruscan civilization. The map includes the 12 cities of the Etruscan League and notable cities founded by the Etruscans. The dates on the map are an approximation. NormanEinstein, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_civilization_map.png","alt":"Etruscan civilization map"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"rm-025","num":"25","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Ancient authors put twelve peoples at one shared sanctuary, which is what modern scholars mean by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though no list of members has ever been agreed.","answer":"Etruscan league","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanctuary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE – 4th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statue base</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Etruscan league</b> is what modern scholars call the association of twelve peoples that ancient writers describe meeting at a shared sanctuary — and the word is a modern one, where Livy writes <i>concilium</i> or <i>foedus</i>, terms of far more transitory meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He does attest a league of twelve cities, but no list of members has ever been agreed, the membership may have shifted over time, and candidate cities cannot always be matched against physical remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The harder difficulty is that no evidence from the Etruscans themselves says they were organised in any such way, a point most accounts pass over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their own titles do not settle it either: <i>zilath mechl rasnal</i> was long read as head of a national confederation, but if <i>rasnal</i> stands for something like a commonwealth rather than 'the Etruscans', the phrase is a city magistracy and no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What can be said is that the southern cities had a federal structure for some purposes, while whether the northern ones did is unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nor were the cities alike: each had its own political environment and history, and territory sizes, settlement ranks and frontier formation all varied across Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The meeting place itself, however, has been found, at Campo della Fiera on the plain below Orvieto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Excavation there since 2000 has uncovered more than five hectares (12 acres) holding several temples, a <i>Via Sacra</i> and a broad expanse given over to offerings, which is taken beyond serious doubt for the Fanum Voltumnae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Religious use of the ground begins in the 6th century BCE and runs unbroken to the end of the 4th century CE, the cult place having been made grand — tradition credits Porsenna — with altars set along the sacred way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among the finds is a moulded trachyte statue base about 84 centimetres (33 inches) high, inscribed in the late 6th century BCE to record a gift by a woman, perhaps a freedwoman named Kanuta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan league","questions":["At Campo della Fiera below Orvieto, five hectares of temples and a sacred way are taken for the meeting place of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Fanum Voltumnae.","No inscription of their own says the Etruscans were organised as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the title once read as its head may only be a city magistracy."],"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","religion"],"sources":["Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Miriam Gillett, \"The 'Etruscan League' Reconsidered,\" <i>Australasian Society for Classical Studies 31st Annual Conference Proceedings</i> (2010), http://ascs.org.au/news/ascs31/Gillett.pdf. [Open access]","Simonetta Stopponi, \"Un grand site étrusque: les fouilles de Campo della Fiera à Orvieto,\" Auditorium Michel Laclotte, Musée du Louvre, 13 February 2025, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/2025-02/20250123_MDA_ORVIETO_bat%20web.pdf. [Open access]","\"The Celestial Place: Etruscans and Their Gods — The Federal Sanctuary of Orvieto,\" Musée national d'histoire et d'art, Luxembourg, 2018, https://www.nationalmusee.lu/en/exhibitions/the-celestial-place-etruscans-and-their-gods. [Open access]","Simon Stoddart, \"Power and Place in Etruria,\" in <i>Eurasia at the Dawn of History: Urbanization and Social Change</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2017), 304–18, https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/handle/1810/260822. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-026","num":"26","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Written from the 8th century BCE to the 1st, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pruned the Greek alphabet it borrowed of the letters it had no use for, among them beta, delta and the vowel o.","answer":"Etruscan language","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 1st century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liber Linteus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 390 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Etruscan language</b> was written from the 8th century BCE to the 1st, in what is the oldest writing system of the pre-Roman peoples of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The alphabet came with Greek colonists, spreading from Pithekoussai and then Cumae through direct contact between Etruscan aristocrats and the colonial elite, alongside the symposium and the Orientalising taste for the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was adopted entire at first, letters the language had no use for included, and then pruned: <i>beta</i> and <i>delta</i> went with the voiced plosives Etruscan lacked, and so did the vowel /o/.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Signs had to be found for what Greek did not write, a second sibilant taken from Phoenician and an /f/ set down first as a digraph and then with a new letter shaped like an 8.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There was no single Etruscan alphabet: north and south of Vulci and Orvieto the two sibilant letters were used the opposite way round, and Caere wrote a sigma of four strokes all its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The language inflects nouns for four cases with a locative besides, does not inflect the verb for person or number, and builds family names with the endings <i>-na</i> and <i>-ra</i> from a father's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the 5th century BCE unstressed vowels weaken until they stop being written, so that <i>Menerva</i> becomes <i>Menrva</i> and <i>turuce</i> becomes <i>turce</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its isolation is not total: it shares with Raetic and Lemnian the missing /o/, the absence of voiced plosives, and the habit of building words by agglutination where Indo-European languages inflect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what survives is names and repeated formulas, and the longest continuous text is the Liber Linteus, a linen book of about 390 BCE that reached us cut into strips as the wrappings of an Egyptian mummy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the end of the 1st millennium BCE the language was largely extinguished and the literature written in it lost, leaving stone, bronze and pottery to carry what remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan language","questions":["The longest continuous text in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a linen book of about 390 BCE that survived cut into strips as the wrappings of an Egyptian mummy.","Sharing a lack of the vowel o and of voiced plosives with Raetic and Lemnian, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> builds its words by agglutination where Indo-European languages inflect."],"tags":["language","history","italy","iron age","archaeology"],"sources":["Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=35&lang=en. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore, updated September 2022), https://doi.org/10.25429/sns.it/lettere/mnamon048. [Open access]","Igor Uranić, \"Liber Linteus and the Zagreb Mummy,\" in <i>Recikliraj, ideje iz prošlosti / Recycle, Ideas from the Past</i> (Zagreb: University of Zagreb, Faculty of Humanities and Social Sciences, Department of Archaeology, 2019), https://doi.org/10.17234/9789531757232-07. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Simona Marchesini, \"Rhaetic: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=41&lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Aulus-metellus-142BB03F76F7CD37B2E.jpg","title":"Etruscan language","desc":"Bronze sculpture of The Orator. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aulus-metellus-142BB03F76F7CD37B2E.jpg","alt":"Aulus-metellus-142BB03F76F7CD37B2E"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"rm-027","num":27,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are three gold sheets from the harbour sanctuary of Caere, two in Etruscan and one in Phoenician, recording a dedication Thefarie Velianas made to Uni-Astarte around 500 BCE.","questions":["Rolled up and buried beside the temples they had been nailed to, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survive intact, and their Phoenician sheet measures how close Caere stood to Carthage.","Students of Etruscan hoped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would serve as a Rosetta Stone for their language, but the Phoenician and Etruscan versions of the dedication do not match word for word."],"answer":"Pyrgi Tablets","answerText":"Pyrgi Tablets","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple B</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 510 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Pyrgi Tablets</b> are three inscribed sheets of gold, two in Etruscan and one in Phoenician, found in July 1964 in the sanctuary at Pyrgi, the harbour town of the Etruscan city of Caere on the Tyrrhenian coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pyrgi stood at the seaward end of a broad road from Caere, and its monumental sanctuary of Uni-Astarte has been dug by the Sapienza University of Rome since 1957.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sheets had been rolled up and hidden inside a container, and gold nails beside them, with holes punched along their edges, show they had once been fixed to a door frame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That careful burial is why they survive unmarked after 2,500 years, while the sea has been eating steadily into the temples they came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Phoenician was a Semitic language of the Levantine coast, known almost entirely from inscriptions, whose western form, spoken at Carthage and in the colonies it controlled, is the one scholars call Punic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> All three sheets record a dedication by Thefarie Velianas, whom the Phoenician text calls king over Cisra, an old name for Caere, and whom the two Etruscan texts leave untitled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He gives a sacred place, and by the Phoenician account a statue with it, to Astarte, whom the Etruscan sheets call Uni-Astre, Uni being the goddess the Romans would later know as Juno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lettering, language and archaeological context together date the dedication to about 500 BCE, at the latest to the years 500 to 490, and Thefarie had monumentalised the sanctuary around 510.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Students of Etruscan first hoped the Phoenician sheet would unlock their language, but the versions do not match word for word, the Punic one dating by the Phoenician calendar, so the tablets are no Rosetta Stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their weight is historical instead: among surviving inscriptions they are the oldest direct source for pre-Roman Italy, and a Punic dedication in Caere's own port measures how close the city stood to Carthage a generation after Etruscan and Carthaginian fleets met the Phocaeans off Corsica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["object","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","language","religion","etruscan"],"sources":["Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, \"Pyrgi Tablets,\" <i>Museum's Masterpieces</i>, https://www.museoetru.it/masterpieces/lamine-doro-da-pyrgi. [Open access]","Jacques Heurgon, \"Les inscriptions de Pyrgi et l'alliance étrusco-punique autour de 500 av. J.-C.,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 109, no. 1 (1965): 89–104, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1965.11813. [Open access]","Maria Paola Baglione, Barbara Belelli Marchesini, Claudia Carlucci and Laura Maria Michetti, \"Pyrgi, Harbour and Sanctuary of Caere: Landscape, Urbanistic Planning and Architectural Features,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 28, no. 2 (2017): 201–10, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/PDF28.2/14_Baglione_et_al.pdf. [Open access]","Paolo Merlo, \"Phoenician: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=15&lang=en. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Pyrgi_tablets_A.jpg","title":"Pyrgi Tablets","desc":"The \"Pyrgi tablets\". Laminated sheets of gold with a treatise both in Etruscan and Phoenician languages. From Etruscan Museum in Rome (Museo di Villa Giulia). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyrgi_tablets_A.jpg","alt":"Pyrgi tablets A"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"rm-028","num":28,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Because its religious literature is lost, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is known mainly from what practice left behind: sanctuaries, painted terracotta temples, inscribed votive offerings and the debris of sacrifice.","questions":["In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a votive column at Poggio Colla could be taken down and buried upside down beside two statue bases, with a piglet killed and a bronze bowl laid in after it.","Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a temple facade was dressed in painted terracotta rather than carved marble, one sanctuary could take dedications to several gods, and thunder and entrails were read for signs."],"answer":"Etruscan religion","answerText":"Etruscan religion","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Votive words</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Portonaccio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 530 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"Very little of what the Etruscans believed was written down in a form that has reached us, so <b>Etruscan religion</b> is known mostly from what its practice left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest offerings were made in caves, in water and in the ground, and only later are they joined by cult buildings, altars, sacrificial debris, dedicated objects and pictures of gods and rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Words start appearing on votive objects from about the second quarter of the 1st millennium BCE, and although Etruscan often resists translation, terms for giving, dedication, the gods and sanctity can be picked out of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Portonaccio, the sanctuary outside Veii, a foundation deposit of roughly 600 to 530 BCE preserves the inscribed names of the people who dedicated there, several of them from known aristocratic families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman habits must not be read back into any of this: the bargain of <i>do ut des</i> cannot be assumed, and one sanctuary could take dedications to several different gods at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A great sanctuary was a district rather than a building, and at Pyrgi two monumental temples stood beside a second cult place, with votive deposits, a fire altar and ceremonial buildings along the road serving them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Temple facades were dressed not in carved marble but in painted terracotta, worked up into figures as large as life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of them, a windswept head of Thesan, the Etruscan goddess of the dawn whom Greek writers called Leucothea, shows how readily an Etruscan god could be matched with a Greek one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Excavation can recover a whole sequence of ritual: at Poggio Colla a votive column was taken down and set upside down in a pit beside two statue bases, a piglet was killed, and a bronze bowl and gold wire were laid in after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Deposits of that kind are sorted into acts of propitiation, foundation, celebration and obliteration, and beside them stood the reading of divine will from thunder, from lots and from the entrails of sacrificed animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["practice","religion","history","italy","iron age","archaeology","etruscan"],"sources":["Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Tesse D. Stek, review of <i>The Archaeology of Sanctuaries and Ritual in Etruria</i>, ed. Nancy T. de Grummond and Ingrid Edlund-Berry, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2012.04.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.04.42/. [Open access]","Liza Cleland, review of <i>Votives, Places and Rituals in Etruscan Religion</i>, ed. Margarita Gleba and Hilary Becker, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.06.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.06.55/. [Open access]","Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, \"Head of Leucotea,\" <i>Museum's Masterpieces</i>, https://www.museoetru.it/masterpieces/head-of-leucotea. [Open access]","Maria Paola Baglione, Barbara Belelli Marchesini, Claudia Carlucci and Laura Maria Michetti, \"Pyrgi, Harbour and Sanctuary of Caere: Landscape, Urbanistic Planning and Architectural Features,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 28, no. 2 (2017): 201–10, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/PDF28.2/14_Baglione_et_al.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Etruscan_votive_heads_IV-II_century_BC.jpg/1920px-Etruscan_votive_heads_IV-II_century_BC.jpg","title":"Etruscan religion","desc":"Etruscan votive heads IV-II cen BC. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_votive_heads_IV-II_century_BC.jpg","alt":"Etruscan votive heads IV-II century BCE"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"rm-029","num":29,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"The Etruscan art of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> read the will of the gods from the entrails of a sacrificed animal, above all from the head of the liver, and Rome took the practice over.","questions":["A bronze liver found near Piacenza, its face cut into cells named for gods, is the best surviving guide to how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was actually carried out in Etruria.","Rome ended up with an order of sixty public practitioners of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the Senate still summoned grandees out of Etruria whenever it wanted a prodigy explained."],"answer":"haruspicy","answerText":"haruspicy","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman responsa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Piacenza liver</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Haruspicy</b> is divination by inspecting a sacrificed animal, and it is the branch of Etruscan religious learning the Romans took over most completely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The organs read were the <i>exta</i>, and the liver came to matter more than any other, ahead of the gall bladder, heart and lungs, with stomach and kidneys counting for little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the haruspex watched above all was the <i>caput iecinoris</i>, the head of the liver: whether it was there at all, how large it was, and how it was split.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The books governing it sat inside the wider learning Romans called the <i>Etrusca disciplina</i>, beside its books on lightning and ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How old the practice is cannot be settled easily, and it may owe something to the older extispicy of Assyria and Babylonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Clay models of a sacrificial sheep's liver, lungs or spiral gut were made across the ancient Near East, more than a hundred of them published and a whole series recovered at Mari on the Euphrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They took hold in Anatolia and along the Levantine coast more firmly than in Mesopotamia itself, and were probably carried onward to Italy from there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What survives from Etruria is the bronze liver of Piacenza, made around 100 BCE, found in the territory of Gossolengo and still kept in the city's Palazzo Farnese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its flat face is cut into cells carrying 41 names, 27 of them gods, with 16 round the rim fixing the orientation and the sun and moon on the rounded side, and a <i>pars familiaris</i> is marked off from a <i>pars hostilis</i>, so where a mark fell decided whether the sign was good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Rome the reading became business of state: from the early 3rd century BCE the answers ran steadily in favour of senatorial expansion, and imperial inscriptions name an order of sixty public haruspices, thought by some to be lesser men than the grandees the Senate summoned out of Etruria to explain a prodigy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["practice","religion","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","rome"],"sources":["Charles Guittard, \"Signes du destin et pouvoir dans les pratiques étrusco-italiques,\" <i>Collection de l'Institut des Sciences et Techniques de l'Antiquité</i> 999 (2006): 71–82, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ista_0000-0000_2006_act_999_1_3429. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Jean Nougayrol, \"La langue des haruspices babyloniens à propos d'un foie d'argile inédit,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 110, no. 1 (1966): 193–203, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1966.11961. [Open access]","Musei di Palazzo Farnese, \"Museo Archeologico,\" Comune di Piacenza, https://palazzofarnese.piacenza.it/it/page/museo-archeologico-1. [Open access]","Yann Berthelet, \"Les haruspices publics au service de Rome : des aristocrates convoqués d'Étrurie ? Quels aristocrates ?,\" <i>Collection de l'Institut des Sciences et Techniques de l'Antiquité</i> 1505 (2020): 133–53, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ista_0000-0000_2020_act_1505_1_3820. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Diagram_of_the_bronze_liver_of_Piacenza.jpg","title":"haruspicy","desc":"Diagram of the inscriptions of the bronze liver of Piacenza. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diagram_of_the_bronze_liver_of_Piacenza.jpg","alt":"Diagram of the bronze liver of Piacenza"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-030","num":30,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Romans gave the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Etruscan learning that recognised and read the signs the gods sent, set down in books on entrails, on lightning and on ritual.","questions":["A child called Tages, come up out of a ploughed furrow, and a nymph called Vegoia were held to have revealed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which great Etruscan families then wrote down and kept.","Sixteen regions of the sky, each the seat of a god, belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as does the doctrine that allotted the Etruscan people ten ages and no more."],"answer":"Etrusca disciplina","answerText":"Etrusca disciplina","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Latin version</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 56 – 44 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cited at Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st century BCE – 5th century CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Etrusca disciplina</b> is the Roman name for the body of Etruscan religious learning, the rules by which signs sent by the gods were recognised and read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls the Etruscans a people given to religious observance beyond all others, and skilled beyond all others in the art of cultivating the gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What stands behind that reputation is a habit of mind rather than a set of rites, since anything out of the ordinary was a message: a comet, an earthquake, an odd plant, a deformed birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seneca put the difference sharply, writing that where a Roman thought lightning was thrown because clouds had collided, an Etruscan thought clouds collided in order that lightning be thrown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The learning was written down in books the Romans sorted into three classes: the <i>libri haruspicini</i> on entrails, the <i>fulgurales</i> on lightning, and the <i>rituales</i> on ritual, which laid down among much else the rite for founding a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its origin was told as a revelation made to the Etruscans by Tages, a child who came up out of a ploughed furrow near Tarquinia, and by the nymph Vegoia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Great families kept the books and handed them down, and Etruscan sarcophagi show their owners holding them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome read them in translation: Tarquitius Priscus made a Latin <i>Ostentarium Tuscum</i> and an <i>Ostentarium arborarium</i>, a list of unlucky trees, both still being quoted by Macrobius centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One piece survives inside the corpus of the Roman land surveyors, Vegoia warning that men will shift boundary stones near the end of the eighth <i>saeculum</i> and be damned for it, which fits the doctrine that the Etruscan people were allotted ten such ages and no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sky was divided into sixteen regions, each the seat of a god, a scheme listed in full only by Martianus Capella in the 5th century CE and marked out on the bronze liver of Piacenza, and modern reconstructions of it still disagree about where north lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","religion","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","text"],"sources":["Dominique Briquel, \"« Gens ante alias magis dedita religionibus » : les Étrusques et le sacré,\" <i>Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé</i> 1, no. 3 (1989): 247–62, https://doi.org/10.3406/bude.1989.1404. [Open access]","Charles Guittard, \"Macrobe et l'Etrusca disciplina : ostentarium Tuscum et ostentarium arborarium,\" <i>Collection de l'Institut des Sciences et Techniques de l'Antiquité</i> 1356 (2016): 13–26, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ista_0000-0000_2016_act_1356_1_3083. [Open access]","Jean Peyras, \"Religion étrusque et droit romain d'après le bureau central des géomètres de la dynastie valentino-théodosienne,\" <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 38, no. 1 (2012): 163–76, https://doi.org/10.3406/dha.2012.3458. [Open access]","Gérard Capdeville, \"Les dieux de Martianus Capella,\" <i>Revue de l'histoire des religions</i> 213, no. 3 (1996): 251–99, https://doi.org/10.3406/rhr.1996.1203. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Etruscan_bronze_mirror_with_Menrva_and_Lasa_Vecu.jpg/1920px-Etruscan_bronze_mirror_with_Menrva_and_Lasa_Vecu.jpg","title":"Etrusca disciplina","desc":"Etruscan bronze mirror with Menrva and Lasa Vecu (Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, Rome). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_bronze_mirror_with_Menrva_and_Lasa_Vecu.jpg","alt":"Etruscan bronze mirror with Menrva and Lasa Vecu"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"rm-031","num":31,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the patterns on ceilings and upper walls copy woven cloth, standing not for a domestic interior but for the tents raised over a funeral.","questions":["Small blue-green trees with black berries punctuate the walls in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a ring of them is thought to have made the chamber a kind of sacred precinct.","Charun, hook-nosed and green as a corpse and carrying a heavy mallet, belongs to the later <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the underworld crowds out the banquet."],"answer":"Etruscan tomb painting","answerText":"Etruscan tomb painting","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shrub motif</span><span class=\"dt-v\">until the 4th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">François Tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 350 – 325 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Etruscan tomb painting</b> covers the walls of rock-cut chamber tombs, and the painted tombs of Tarquinia, with the tumuli of Caere, are the part of Etruscan archaeology best known outside the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The painted room is not a gallery of pictures but a space built out of them, and the small trees punctuating the walls, almost constant until the 4th century BCE, are what mark it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The trees are stereotyped, with lanceolate blue-green leaves and black berries, usually read as laurels, and birds perch in their branches or fly towards them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A ring of shrubs is thought to have made the chamber a kind of <i>templum</i>, the inaugurated space Roman practice also marked with trees, and the painted columns in the corners of the Tomb of the Lionesses do similar work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The patterns on ceilings and upper walls copy woven cloth, and their point is not a domestic interior but the tents and canopies raised for a funeral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The subjects are the funeral itself: games, dances and above all the banquet, whose arrangement and place in the tomb settle from the late 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Banqueters hold out fillets, wreaths and eggs, and a false door, first painted in the Tomb of the Augurs, becomes a common way of closing a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they show of eating and drinking is partly borrowed, the wine, the furniture and the sports that went with a feast, though the presence of freeborn Etruscan women at the banquet struck Greek writers as scandalous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The later tombs turn towards the underworld, and Charun appears in them: hook-nosed, with pointed animal ears and wild hair, blue or green as a corpse should be, and always carrying a heavy mallet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the François Tomb at Vulci a Greek composition of Achilles killing Trojan prisoners has a winged demon and a grimacing Charun added to it, drawn more flatly than the borrowed figures and there to say the gods were present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["art","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","religion"],"sources":["Anne-Marie Adam, \"« Horror vacui » et détails inutiles (?) dans la peinture funéraire étrusque,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 37, no. 1 (2012): 239–54, https://doi.org/10.3406/ktema.2012.1663. [Open access]","Anne-Marie Adam, \"Végétation et paysage dans la peinture funéraire étrusque,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 15, no. 1 (1990): 143–50, https://doi.org/10.3406/ktema.1990.2014. [Open access]","Jean-René Jannot, \"Charon et Charun : à propos d'un démon funéraire étrusque,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 135, no. 2 (1991): 443–64, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1991.14997. [Open access]","Dominique Briquel, \"« Gens ante alias magis dedita religionibus » : les Étrusques et le sacré,\" <i>Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé</i> 1, no. 3 (1989): 247–62, https://doi.org/10.3406/bude.1989.1404. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Tomb_of_the_augurs_detail.jpg","title":"Etruscan tomb painting","desc":"Tomba degli auguri, Tarquinia, Italia. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_the_augurs_detail.jpg","alt":"Tomb of the augurs detail"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-032","num":32,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"The town on the Civita plateau and the painted cemeteries on the ridge opposite are the two halves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Etruscan city that gave Rome the Tarquins.","questions":["Tarchon founded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Tages came up out of a furrow in its fields, and Demaratus of Corinth settled there before his descendants took the throne at Rome.","The port of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Gravisca, whose sanctuary drew Greek traders and yielded more than 200,000 catalogued finds in eleven seasons of digging."],"answer":"Tarquinia","answerText":"Tarquinia","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gravisca votives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 – 470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gravisca colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">181 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Tarquinia</b> was one of the great cities of southern Etruria, its town standing on the Civita plateau and its cemeteries on the Monterozzi ridge across the valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers gave it a founder to match, Tarchon, eponym of the city and in some accounts the brother or lieutenant of the Lydian Tyrrhenus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tarchon was also the man said to have written down the revelation of Tages, the child who came up out of a furrow near Tarquinia and gave the Etruscans their religious learning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Roman tradition tied the city to Rome's own kings, since Demaratus of Corinth was said to have settled there and his descendants to have ruled Rome as the Tarquins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It claimed Servius Tullius as well, the king the Etruscan tradition identified with Mastarna and whom Cicero reports to have been born of a Tarquinian slave woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its great temple was the Ara della Regina on the plateau, and when a new terrace was laid in front of it in the 4th century BCE care was taken to mark the position of an archaic chest and precinct wall below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The excavators suggest that chest may have been taken for the tomb of Tarchon himself, which would make the terrace an act of remembering rather than of building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Old geophysical surveys, read again, are producing a plan of the town to match those already available for Veii and Vulci.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its port was Gravisca, whose sanctuary drew Greek traders: eleven seasons of excavation from 1969 produced more than 200,000 catalogued finds, the votive offerings heaviest between 510 and 470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across the valley the Monterozzi cemetery holds the painted chambers the city is best known for, and Rome absorbed the place in stages, planting a colony at Gravisca in 181 BCE and making Tarquinia a municipium of the tribe Stellatina, run by a board of four, after the Social War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["city","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","religion"],"sources":["Maria Mainardi and Giovanna Bagnasco Gianni, \"Tarquinia,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 20 (2011): 237–316, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2011_num_20_1_4644. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Francesca Boitani and Mario Torelli, \"Un nuovo santuario dell'emporion di Gravisca,\" <i>Publications de l'École française de Rome</i> 251 (1999): 93–102, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_1999_act_251_1_5391. [Open access]","Dominique Briquel, \"« Gens ante alias magis dedita religionibus » : les Étrusques et le sacré,\" <i>Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé</i> 1, no. 3 (1989): 247–62, https://doi.org/10.3406/bude.1989.1404. [Open access]","Anne-Marie Adam, \"« Horror vacui » et détails inutiles (?) dans la peinture funéraire étrusque,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 37, no. 1 (2012): 239–54, https://doi.org/10.3406/ktema.2012.1663. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Monterozzi_necropolis_%E2%80%94_Tomb_of_the_Leopards.jpg/1920px-Monterozzi_necropolis_%E2%80%94_Tomb_of_the_Leopards.jpg","title":"Tarquinia","desc":"Monterozzi necropolis — Tomb of the Leopards. Gleb Simonov. 2024-01-03. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monterozzi_necropolis_%E2%80%94_Tomb_of_the_Leopards.jpg","alt":"Monterozzi necropolis — Tomb of the Leopards"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Tarquinia","at":[11.7561,42.2492]}},
{"id":"rm-033","num":33,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Greeks called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Agylla, an Ionian workshop there made the painted hydriae named after it, and Rome sent its sacred objects there when the Gauls came.","questions":["After its ships helped beat the Phocaeans, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stoned the prisoners to death and was told by the Delphic oracle to found a sanctuary in expiation.","Romans held that the people of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the first made citizens without the vote, though Rome later took half their land as public property."],"answer":"Caere","answerText":"Caere","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sardinian Sea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman truce</span><span class=\"dt-v\">353 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Caere</b> stood on a plateau where Cerveteri now stands, and the Greeks who traded there knew it as Agylla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo passes on a tradition that Pelasgians out of Thessaly founded it, one of several Greek stories that fastened themselves to the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the most active centre of hellenisation in Etruria: a small colony of Athenians was settled there by about 600 BCE, and between 540 and 510 an Ionian workshop in the city turned out the painted water jars still called Caeretan hydriae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its cemetery at the Banditaccia was full of the finest Attic black-figure and early red-figure pottery, which makes Caere one of the chief markets for Athenian vases in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of that made its dealings with Greeks peaceful, and about 540 BCE Caeretan ships joined Carthaginian ones against the Phocaeans in the Sardinian Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Caeretans stoned their prisoners to death, and after what they took for divine punishment the Delphic oracle set them a sanctuary in expiation; Strabo also records a Caeretan treasury standing at Delphi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo indeed says the city was honoured among Greeks for its justice, since for all its power it kept clear of piracy, which the facts contradict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its port at Pyrgi carried a monumental sanctuary of Uni-Astarte, and Dionysius of Syracuse sacked the place in 384 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Rome the city was long on unusual terms: Tarquinius Superbus took refuge there in 509 BCE, and when the Gauls held Rome in 390 the Roman sacred objects were sent to Caere for safekeeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Romans held that the Caerites were the first people made citizens without the vote, a tradition disputed in antiquity and argued over still, and the friendship did not save them: Rome granted a truce in 353 BCE, took half the territory as public land, and Strabo found the city in decline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["city","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Mauro Cristofani, \"Cerveteri,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 5 (1987): 251–66, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_1987_num_5_1_3380. [Open access]","Jacques Heurgon, \"Les inscriptions de Pyrgi et l'alliance étrusco-punique autour de 500 av. J.-C.,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 109, no. 1 (1965): 89–104, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1965.11813. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Michel Humbert, \"L'incorporation de Caere dans la civitas Romana,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome</i> 84, no. 1 (1972): 231–68, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.1972.923. [Open access]","Maria Paola Baglione, Barbara Belelli Marchesini, Claudia Carlucci and Laura Maria Michetti, \"Pyrgi, Harbour and Sanctuary of Caere: Landscape, Urbanistic Planning and Architectural Features,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 28, no. 2 (2017): 201–10, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/PDF28.2/14_Baglione_et_al.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Herakles_Kerberos_Eurystheus_Louvre_E701.jpg/1920px-Herakles_Kerberos_Eurystheus_Louvre_E701.jpg","title":"Caere","desc":"Side A: Herakles, Cerberus and Eurystheus. Side B: two eagles flying and a hare. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herakles_Kerberos_Eurystheus_Louvre_E701.jpg","alt":"Herakles Kerberos Eurystheus Louvre E701"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Caere","at":[12.105,42.0075]}},
{"id":"rm-034","num":34,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Rock-cut chambers under earthen mounds, laid out along streets like a town, make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Caere, dug into a plateau across the valley from the city.","questions":["At the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a tomb chamber imitates the rooms of a house, and an altar outside is often joined to the mound by a small bridge.","Cippi, stelae and stone lions marked graves at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while most of the writing that honoured the dead sat inside the chambers where almost nobody could read it."],"answer":"Banditaccia necropolis","answerText":"Banditaccia necropolis","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb types</span><span class=\"dt-v\">develop to c. 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sunken roads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 4th century BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Banditaccia necropolis</b> is the great cemetery of Caere, spread over a plateau across a valley from the town, and its tumuli are among the most recognisable things Etruscan archaeology has produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Burial moved onto the plateau from an area to the north in the late Villanovan period and spread across it from there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tombs are rock-cut chambers under earthen mounds, some of them satellites of a larger one, and outside there is often an altar joined to the mound by a small bridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside they imitate the rooms of a house, and their plans go on developing until about the middle of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After that the cemetery is regulated: tombs are set out along laid roads and given a uniform outside, cut as a cube, as a small palace, or behind a single façade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The same regulation holds from the 4th century BCE, when the façades stop and tombs are cut along roads sunk into the tuff, the great family hypogea excepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tomb was meant to hold the memory of the dead before the family and sometimes before the whole community, and cippi, stelae and stone lions did that work above ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing came early to Etruscan burial, but the great majority of the inscriptions are inside the chambers, where almost nobody could read them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What those chambers held made Caere one of the chief markets for Attic pottery in the west, and it also made the cemetery a quarry: between 1845 and 1856 the marchese Giampietro Campana worked it hard and took the finds into a private museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When that collection was broken up, some 12,000 works were divided between the Louvre and nearly ninety French provincial towns, others going to the Hermitage and to Florence, and many lost the record of where they had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["place","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","religion"],"sources":["Mauro Cristofani, \"Cerveteri,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 5 (1987): 251–66, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_1987_num_5_1_3380. [Open access]","Catherine Cousin, \"Une commémoration originale du mort en Étrurie,\" <i>Actes des congrès nationaux des sociétés historiques et scientifiques</i> 134, no. 1 (2010): 17–33, https://www.persee.fr/doc/acths_1764-7355_2010_act_134_1_1813. [Open access]","Jacques Heurgon, \"Les inscriptions de Pyrgi et l'alliance étrusco-punique autour de 500 av. J.-C.,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 109, no. 1 (1965): 89–104, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1965.11813. [Open access]","Laurent Haumesser, \"Les statues en terre cuite de la collection Campana. La multiplication des Atlantes,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 162, no. 1 (2018): 365–86, https://www.persee.fr/doc/crai_0065-0536_2018_num_162_1_96474. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Deceased_with_funeral_procession%2C_Cerveteri%2C_Banditaccia_necropolis%2C_Tomb_of_the_Sarcophagi%2C_2nd_quarter_of_the_4th_century_BC%2C_Circeo_marble%2C_inv._14949_-_Museo_Gregoriano_Etrusco_-_Vatican_Museums_-_DSC01156.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Banditaccia necropolis","desc":"Exhibit in the Museo Gregoriano Etrusco - Vatican Museums. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deceased_with_funeral_procession,_Cerveteri,_Banditaccia_necropolis,_Tomb_of_the_Sarcophagi,_2nd_quarter_of_the_4th_century_BC,_Circeo_marble,_inv._14949_-_Museo_Gregoriano_Etrusco_-_Vatican_Museums_-_DSC01156.jpg","alt":"Deceased with funeral procession, Cerveteri, Banditaccia necropolis, Tomb of the Sarcophagi, 2nd quarter of the 4th century BCE, Circeo marble, - Museo Gregoriano Etrusco - Vatican Museums"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Banditaccia necropolis","at":[12.105,42.0075],"within":"Caere"}},
{"id":"rm-035","num":35,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Dionysius called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most powerful city in Etruria and equal in extent to Athens, and Rome stormed it in 396 BCE after a long siege.","questions":["Walls running some 9 kilometres (5.6 miles) around 190 hectares (470 acres) enclosed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose small acropolis on the Piazza d'Armi had older defences of its own.","After Rome took <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the tribunes proposed moving half the city's people into it, and a golden krater went to Delphi as a tithe of the plunder."],"answer":"Veii","answerText":"Veii","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Piazza d'Armi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – early 5th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken by Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">396 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Veii</b> was Rome's nearest Etruscan neighbour, and Dionysius of Halicarnassus calls it the most powerful city in Etruria in the days of Romulus and equal in extent to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its city walls enclosed 190 hectares (470 acres) and ran about 9 kilometres (5.6 miles), while the small acropolis plateau of the Piazza d'Armi covered barely 6.9 hectares (17 acres) behind roughly a kilometre of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those acropolis walls are among the oldest Etruscan military architecture known, and settlement there was heaviest between the 8th century BCE and the middle of the 6th before dying away in the early 5th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city walls are later, put at the end of the 5th century BCE by their excavators and probably not much before its middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beyond them a ring of sanctuaries marked out the city's territory, and the greatest of them, Portonaccio, drew gifts from across Etruria: a foundation deposit of about 600 to 530 BCE holds fragments inscribed with the names of the men who gave them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Veii's history in Roman sources is one long quarrel with Rome, beginning under Romulus with the loss of the <i>septem pagi</i> and a truce of a hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A twenty-year war is placed at the start of Servius Tullius's reign, and in 479 BCE the Fabii fought a private war of their own against Veii and were destroyed at the Cremera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The end came in 396 BCE when Camillus stormed the city after a long siege; the Romans sent a golden krater to Delphi as a tithe of the plunder, and the tribunes of the plebs proposed moving half of Rome into the captured town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site did not simply die with the city, since excavation shows it going on at lower intensity, picking up again between 350 and 250 BCE and then tailing away into villas and perhaps a healing sanctuary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roman poets nonetheless made Veii the type of the vanished city, and in 46 BCE its land went to Caesar's veterans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["city","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","warfare"],"sources":["Francesca Boitani and Iefke van Kampen, \"Veio,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 21 (2012): 553–88, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2012_num_21_1_4754. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Paul Fontaine, \"Véies. Les remparts et la porte de la Piazza d'Armi,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome</i> 105, no. 1 (1993): 221–39, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.1993.1799. [Open access]","Françoise Gaultier, \"Sanctuaires et territoires en pays étrusque : le cas de Véies,\" <i>Publications de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 21 (2010): 115–39, https://www.persee.fr/doc/keryl_1275-6229_2010_act_21_1_1418. [Open access]","Liza Cleland, review of <i>Votives, Places and Rituals in Etruscan Religion</i>, ed. Margarita Gleba and Hilary Becker, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.06.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.06.55/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Map_of_Veii.jpg","title":"Veii","desc":"Map of Veii. George Dennis. 1848. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Veii.jpg","alt":"Map of Veii"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Veii","at":[12.3897,42.0233]}},
{"id":"rm-036","num":36,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Claudius told the senate that Mastarna, companion of the Vibenna brothers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, went on to rule Rome under the name Servius Tullius.","questions":["Rome stripped <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of its coastal strip in 280 BCE, which made room for the Latin colony at Cosa and for the prefectures of Saturnia and Forum Aurelii.","Uni, Vei, Fufluns and Thufltha were worshipped at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose personification stands beside those of Tarquinia and Vetulonia on a relief from Caere."],"answer":"Vulci","answerText":"Vulci","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">280 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">François Tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 350 – 325 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Vulci</b> stood above the river Fiora in southern Etruria, its people the <i>Velc</i> of their own inscriptions, the Olkietai of Polybius and the <i>Volcentani</i> that Pliny knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ranked with the greatest cities, its personification carved beside those of Tarquinia and Vetulonia on a relief from Caere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gods worshipped there were Uni, Vei, Fufluns, Suri and Thufltha, and the names of its leading families survive on a mass of inscriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city's largest claim on Roman history is a pair of brothers, Aulus and Caelius Vibenna, whom the emperor Claudius named in the speech preserved on a bronze tablet at Lyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Claudius said their companion Mastarna went on to rule Rome as Servius Tullius, which puts an Etruscan account beside the Roman one of a Roman king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The François Tomb, painted in the third quarter of the 4th century BCE, belonged to one of those families, and a bronze statuette may name the man buried in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 280 BCE Vulci lost its coastal strip and part of its hinterland to Rome, which made room for the Latin colony at Cosa and for the prefectures of Saturnia and Forum Aurelii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the Social War the city was a Roman municipium of the tribe Sabatina, run by a board of four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its modern history begins in October 1828, when Lucien Bonaparte, prince of Canino, opened the cemeteries with his wife Alexandrine de Bleschamps driving the work, dividing the ground into 'family districts' from the inscriptions cut on funerary cippi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His collection was shown in five rooms of the castle at Musignano and then sold and scattered, and two Attic amphorae from those digs surfaced at a Paris auction in 2005, while geophysics has lately been recovering the plan of the town they came out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["city","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Anna Maria Moretti Sgubini, \"Vulci,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 21 (2012): 1082–1154, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2012_num_21_1_4783. [Open access]","Clara Berrendonner, \"Les Tetnie à Vulci,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome</i> 118, no. 1 (2006): 21–34, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.2006.10966. [Open access]","Dominique Briquel, \"Réapparition de deux vases provenant des fouilles de Lucien Bonaparte à Vulci,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 151, no. 3 (2007): 1415–33, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2007.91372. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Dominique Briquel, \"« Gens ante alias magis dedita religionibus » : les Étrusques et le sacré,\" <i>Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé</i> 1, no. 3 (1989): 247–62, https://doi.org/10.3406/bude.1989.1404. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Gold_fibula_from_Vulci_-_British_Museum_%282%29.jpg","title":"Vulci","desc":"Gold fibula with a procession of gold lions in gold granulation, while more lions, sphinxes and heads of lions and horses decorate the bow, as well as the tip and butt of the catchplate. From Vulci, Italy British Museum (675-650 BCE). Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_fibula_from_Vulci_-_British_Museum_(2).jpg","alt":"Gold fibula from Vulci - British Museum (2)"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Vulci","at":[11.6317,42.4189]}},
{"id":"rm-037","num":37,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"The duck-billed jugs called Schnabelkannen carried <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into Switzerland, the Rhine valley and Gaul, about a hundred of them turning up outside Italy.","questions":["Engraved mirrors are the most closely studied products of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the ancient sources put mirrors among the instruments by which the will of the gods was read.","No tin slag has ever been found in Tuscany, so the tin that went into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must have been brought in from somewhere else."],"answer":"Etruscan bronzework","answerText":"Etruscan bronzework","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Schnabelkannen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">525 – 400 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Etruscan bronzework</b> travelled further than anything else the Etruscans made, and bronze vessels are the bulk of what reached Iron Age Gaul from Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The commonest of them by a wide margin was the <i>Schnabelkanne</i>, a jug with a duck-billed spout, most of them made between 525 and 400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Corded cistae, situlae and stamnoi went the same way, and the trade reached Switzerland, the Rhine valley and Gaul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their Etruscan origin is well established and their dating fairly tight, which makes them the clearest measure there is of trade between Etruria and the peoples beyond the Alps: about 150 of these jugs are known from Italy and more than a hundred from outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Etruscan bronzes also turn up as gifts and offerings in other people's sanctuaries across the western Mediterranean and western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> At home the most closely studied bronzes are the engraved mirrors, gathered into a corpus published collection by collection, with fresh pieces still emerging from private hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A mirror could be more than a mirror, since the ancient sources put mirrors among the instruments by which the will of the gods was read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin, and where Etruria found its tin is a harder question than where it found its copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1876 a mining engineer reported cassiterite in an ancient working near Campiglia Marittima, and the mine has been called Etruscan ever since, though its galleries took only the upper part of the deposit, where tin ore lay mixed with iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There is no real evidence for that attribution: the earliest published find from those mines is Roman, no tin slag has ever been found in Tuscany, only one of 28 Tuscan ore samples reaches the tin content that Bronze Age Britain and Portugal were smelting, and modern quarrying has destroyed enough of the workings that their date may never be settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["object","technology","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Bernard Bouloumié, \"Les œnochoés en bronze du type Schnabelkanne en France et en Belgique,\" <i>Gallia</i> 31, no. 1 (1973): 1–35, https://doi.org/10.3406/galia.1973.2623. [Open access]","Marco Tizzoni, \"Etruscan Tin: A Legend?,\" <i>Pallas</i> 50, no. 1 (1999): 89–100, https://doi.org/10.3406/palla.1999.1538. [Open access]","Roger Lambrechts, \"Deux miroirs étrusques inédits,\" <i>Revue belge de philologie et d'histoire</i> 76, no. 1 (1998): 159–76, https://doi.org/10.3406/rbph.1998.4259. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Liza Cleland, review of <i>Votives, Places and Rituals in Etruscan Religion</i>, ed. Margarita Gleba and Hilary Becker, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.06.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.06.55/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Sleep_and_Death_Carrying_off_the_Slain_Sarpedon_%28cista_handle%29%2C_400-380_BC%2C_Etruscan%2C_bronze_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC08259.JPG/1920px-Sleep_and_Death_Carrying_off_the_Slain_Sarpedon_%28cista_handle%29%2C_400-380_BC%2C_Etruscan%2C_bronze_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC08259.JPG","title":"Etruscan bronzework","desc":"Etruscan bronze statuette — in the Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, Ohio, USA. Photography was permitted in the museum without restriction. This artwork is old enough so that it is in the public domain. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sleep_and_Death_Carrying_off_the_Slain_Sarpedon_(cista_handle),_400-380_BC,_Etruscan,_bronze_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC08259.JPG","alt":"Sleep and Death Carrying off the Slain Sarpedon (cista handle), 400-380 BCE, Etruscan, bronze - Cleveland Museum of Art"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-038","num":"38","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The lustrous black surface of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from burnishing alone before firing, with no slip or glaze, which is what separates it from Greek and Roman black-glazed pottery.","questions":["Fired in a reducing atmosphere at 600 to 700 °C (1,100 to 1,300 °F), <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is black right through rather than coated, and 19th-century scholars called it the national pottery of the Etruscans.","Production of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began around Caere in the early 7th century BCE and intensified in the relief-figured workshops of inland Chiusi and Orvieto in the 6th."],"answer":"bucchero","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Later ware</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th – 4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Bucchero</b> is the black pottery of Etruria, black right through rather than coated, and by the 19th century scholars were calling it the national pottery of the Etruscans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What strikes a viewer first is the uniformly black lustre, and then the range of shapes and ornament, which mixes Greek borrowings with the Etruscans' own strongly orientalising inventions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Technically it belongs among the primitive ceramics: the firing and the process after it are exclusively reducing, at a low 600 to 700 °C (1,100 to 1,300 °F), probably in clamp or pit kilns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shine comes from burnishing before firing, and there is no sintered clay coating and no slip, which is the essential technical break from the black-glazed wares of Greece and Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That places it in a firing tradition reaching back into prehistory, from which it differs more in artistic than technical qualities, at the fine end of an industry that also made coarse ware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Finely turned, polished and decorated pieces appear in the first decades of the 7th century BCE in southern Etruria, chiefly around Caere, whose early <i>bucchero sottile</i> has high-quality fabrics and fine, lustrous surfaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Southern Etruria is notable for the variety and richness of both its own and its imported pottery, and the innovations of its workshops intensified between the late 8th and the late 6th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> During the 6th century production grew in central and inland Etruria, above all in the relief-figured vases of Chiusi and Orvieto, before fading rapidly at that century's end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shapes run from open forms such as the kotyle, kylix, chalice, kantharos and kyathos to closed amphorae and jugs, and ornament on impasto and bucchero is among the largest bodies of Etruscan imagery, though on these monochrome wares complex scenes are rare and mythological compositions exceptional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inscribed bucchero perfume flasks and outsize kyathoi passed between aristocratic families as gifts, and a sherd from Poggio Colla carries a stamp that may be the earliest scene of childbirth in European art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"bucchero","tags":["object","technology","archaeology","art","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Jean Gran-Aymerich, Christian Lahanier, Jeanne Gauthier, Alain Duval, Jean-Michel Malfoy, and Catherine Boyer, \"Sur deux groupes de bucchero examinés au Louvre,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome. Antiquité</i> 97, no. 2 (1985): 611–38, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.1985.1472. [Open access]","Jean Gran-Aymerich, \"Images et mythes sur les vases noirs d'Étrurie (VIIIe–VIe siècle av. J.-C.),\" <i>Publications de l'École française de Rome</i> 253 (1999): 383–404, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_1999_act_253_1_5433. [Open access]","Jean Gran-Aymerich, \"Production et circulation des céramiques d'Étrurie méridionale : l'exemple du site littoral de La Castellina, entre Cerveteri et Tarquinia,\" <i>Publications de l'École française de Rome</i> 389 (2007): 101–8, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_2007_act_389_1_9067. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Robinson Peter Krämer, \"Plebeian Rituals and Peasant Cults? Religious Transformations in Etruria during the Sixth and Early Fifth Centuries BCE and their Socio-Political Context,\" in <i>Dependency and Social Inequality in Pre-Roman Italy</i>, ed. Martin Bentz and Patrick Zeidler (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2024), 121–56, https://doi.org/10.1515/9783111558417-006. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Bucchero_kantharos_Terme.jpg/1920px-Bucchero_kantharos_Terme.jpg","title":"bucchero","desc":"Bucchero kantharos, 3rd or 4th Latial Period. From tomb no. 62 at the cemetery of Osteria dell'Osa, near Rome. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucchero_kantharos_Terme.jpg","alt":"Bucchero kantharos Terme"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"rm-039","num":"39","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still carries its paint, brown on the bare flesh and white on the black-hemmed clothing, so it shows that ancient sculpture was coloured rather than white.","questions":["Herakles has just seized the golden-horned hind in the roof group the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to, which is why the god strides towards him with one arm thrust out.","Taken down with its temple around 300 BCE and ritually buried, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came out of the ground almost intact, in pieces someone had laid in a careful row."],"answer":"Apollo of Veii","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dismantled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Apollo of Veii</b> is a terracotta statue made for the roof of a temple in the Portonaccio sanctuary at Veii at the end of the 6th century BCE, and it still carries its paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The colouring shows as a contrast between the brown of the bare flesh and the white of the clothing hemmed in black — ancient sculpture was brightly coloured, not white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The god strides barefoot to the left with the tight archaic smile, his left arm thrust forward in threat and the other lowered, perhaps once holding a bow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pose makes sense only against the statue facing him: Herakles, who has just seized the golden-horned hind sacred to Artemis as one of his labours, with Apollo coming for his sister's animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Artemis herself and Mercury as peacemaker belonged to the same scene, and Latona carrying the infant Apollo stood among the figures ranged along the roof ridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Some Etruscan bronzes show the hero in eastern dress just after that capture, an episode Pindar placed among the Hyperboreans, neighbours of the Scythians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The temple under it was the first of Tuscan type found in Etruria, three cellae on a high square podium, and its decoration points to Delphi, so it was probably dedicated to Herakles and Apollo — though the sanctuary's own dedications name Menerva above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its upper parts carried terracottas so intricate that a system of syllabic marks was used to get each piece into its place, and about twenty acroteria stood on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Veii's workshops were at their height then, and Rome's kings sent to them for modellers in clay: Vulca, the only Etruscan artist the ancient sources name, was called in for the cult statue of the Capitoline temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Around 300 BCE the temple was taken down and its sculpture ritually buried, as buildings elsewhere in Etruria were dismantled and their parts given to the gods like an offering; the Apollo came out of that ground almost intact in 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Apollo of Veii","tags":["object","art","archaeology","religion","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["\"Apollo di Veio,\" Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, https://www.museoetru.it/capolavori/apollo-di-veio. [Open access]","Françoise Gaultier, \"Sanctuaires et territoires en pays étrusque : le cas de Véies,\" <i>Publications de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 21 (2010): 115–39, https://www.persee.fr/doc/keryl_1275-6229_2010_act_21_1_1418. [Open access]","Francesca Boitani and Iefke van Kampen, \"Veio,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 21 (2012): 553–88, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2012_num_21_1_4754. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Françoise Gaultier, \"Héraclès à Cerveteri : sur quelques terres cuites étrusques du musée du Louvre,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 152, no. 4 (2008): 1465–93, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2008.92238. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Apollo_di_Veio_-_Museo_di_Villa_Giulia_2.jpg","title":"Apollo of Veii","desc":"Apollo di Veio - Santuario di Portonaccio - Museo di Villa Giulia. Vulca (?), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_di_Veio_-_Museo_di_Villa_Giulia_2.jpg","alt":"Apollo di Veio - Museo di Villa Giulia 2"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-040","num":"40","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The two clusters of Etruscan speakers in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay around Capua and Pontecagnano, the southern counterpart of the Etruscan language area in the Po valley.","questions":["Pliny wrote that the ager Picentinus had once belonged to the Etruscans, one of the ancient claims for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and graves of Villanovan character later dug there bore him out.","In 438 BCE the Campanians swore themselves into a people of equals, which cut against the clientship <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rested on, and Capua fell to the Samnites soon after."],"answer":"Etruscan Campania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Writing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Campanian oath</span><span class=\"dt-v\">438 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan Campania</b> is the Etruscan settlement, language and rule that reached south of Latium into the plain behind the Bay of Naples, from the early Iron Age to the middle of the 5th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The earliest movement was a Villanovan one: groups from the Capenate and Veientine districts into the plain around Capua, and from coastal Etruria into the <i>ager Picentinus</i> south of the Sarno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It came in small waves rather than one conquest, and its dealings with the Ausones already there were more complicated than war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Etruscan-speaking communities are attested in two clusters, one around Capua and one around Pontecagnano, the southern prolongation of the Etruscan language area, matching the northern one in the Po valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Capua was the chief of them, called Volturnum before the Romans renamed it, and Roman writers made its foundation Etruscan and remembered an Etruscan lordship over Campania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For the southern cluster the ancient testimony is Pliny, who says the <i>ager Picentinus</i> had once belonged to the Etruscans, and Stephanus of Byzantium, who calls Picentia a city of Tyrrhenia; graves of Villanovan character later dug there bore them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Etruscan writing spread in Campania later than in southern Etruria, becoming common only in the 6th century, and in tombs and sanctuaries it works above all as a marker of the family name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inland cities grew with it — Nola and Nocera, with Pompeii serving as their port at the mouth of the Sarno — and Capuan bronze vessels were still being recommended to Roman farmers centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What kind of political control to imagine behind all this is much disputed, and no convincing narrative yet fits the archaeological material to the literary claim of Etruscan rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It ended in the later 5th century: Greek settlement checked the expansion, and in 438 BCE the Campanians constituted themselves as a people, an oath among equals that cut against the clientship the Etruscan cities were built on, with Capua falling to the Samnites soon after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan Campania","tags":["place","history","archaeology","italy","iron age","etruscan","migration","language"],"sources":["Maria Bonghi-Jovino, \"Capua,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 4 (1985): 455–76, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_1985_num_4_1_4613. [Open access]","Françoise-Hélène Massa-Pairault, \"La Campanie avant Rome. Recherches actuelles et problèmes,\" <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 22, no. 1 (1996): 33–61, https://doi.org/10.3406/dha.1996.2262. [Open access]","Bruno d'Agostino, \"Pontecagnano,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 14 (1996): 187–98, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_1996_num_14_1_4037. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=35&lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Etruscan-Corinthian_Kotyle_%28cup%29_with_Gorgon%2C_from_Pontecagnano%2C_beginning_6th_century_BC%2C_Monsters._Fantastic_Creatures_of_Fear_and_Myth_Exhibition%2C_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme%2C_Rome_%2812818177694%29.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Etruscan Campania","desc":"Etruscan-Corinthian kotyle by the Painter of the Bad Wolf; figures emerge from the background among which that of the Gorgon; from Pontecagnano, tomb 856; beginning 6th century BCE, Pontecagnano, Museo Nazionale dell' Agro Picentino (inv. 35272). Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan-Corinthian_Kotyle_(cup)_with_Gorgon,_from_Pontecagnano,_beginning_6th_century_BC,_Monsters._Fantastic_Creatures_of_Fear_and_Myth_Exhibition,_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme,_Rome_(12818177694).jpg","alt":"Etruscan-Corinthian Kotyle (cup) with Gorgon, from Pontecagnano, beginning 6th century BCE, Monsters. Fantastic Creatures of Fear and Myth Exhibition, Palazzo Massimo alle Terme, Rome"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Campania","at":[14.95,40.75]}},
{"id":"gr-223","num":223,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Latin name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> renders a Greek phrase whose meaning shrank over time, until under Rome it stood for the Greek-settled coasts of Italy from Cumae to Tarentum.","questions":["The cities of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shared an Italiote League, yet there is little reliable evidence that their inhabitants felt any communal regional identity, which may have been a Roman perception.","Greek settlement in the region later called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began at Pithekoussai on Ischia about the middle of the 8th century BCE and closed with Thurii in 444 BCE."],"answer":"Magna Graecia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 443 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Magna Graecia</b>, or <i>Megale Hellas</i>, is the name for the Greek-settled coasts of southern Italy, and it progressively shrank in meaning until in the Roman period it indicated the Greek inhabited areas from Cumae to Tarentum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Settlement began in the mid-8th century BCE at Pithekoussai on Ischia and ran through Cumae, Poseidonia, Sybaris, Kroton, Metapontion and Taras, closing with the Panhellenic foundation of Thurii in 444 to 443 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sicily is often counted in with them, so that Naxos, Syracuse, Katane, Gela, Akragas and Selinus are surveyed in the same volumes as the mainland foundations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The older model of a single founding act has been questioned for Greek settlements from southern Italy to the Black Sea, since it sits poorly with archaeology that points instead to continuous immigration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They shared a common Italiote League, yet there is little reliable evidence of what sense of communal regional identity the inhabitants had, and the region may be more a Roman perception than a local claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the archaic silver of Sybaris a bull stands out against the background on both faces, in relief on the obverse and carved inward into the flan on the reverse, before double-relief striking took over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Greek cities lived among Oscan speakers, and a Lucanian pot of about 410 to 390 BCE carries <i>Posdan</i>, a variant of the theonym Poseidon whose syncope explains the Latin name Paestum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Italic newcomers took Poseidonia the <i>chora</i> was divided among them, yet Greek artistic forms, methods and materials continued there, and the sanctuary of Santa Venera stayed in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE Greek and Italic communities behaved within one framework of local rivalries and aristocratic competition, and Hannibal's arrival was an opportunity in a struggle that was essentially local.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Rome absorbed the region from about 350 BCE, with the Hannibalic War the decisive turn, after which Greek magistracies survived chiefly as vehicles for elite benefaction and public display.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Magna Graecia","tags":["place","region","history","greece","italy","archaic"],"sources":["William Owens, review of <i>Rome and the Western Greeks, 350 BC–AD 200: Conquest and Acculturation in Southern Italy</i>, by Kathryn Lomas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.03.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.03.12/. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Dominik Delp, review of <i>Comparing Greek Colonies: Mobility and Settlement Consolidation from Southern Italy to the Black Sea (8th–6th Century BC)</i>, ed. Camilla Colombi et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.06.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.06.38/. [Open access]","Jessica Brocchieri, Rosa Vitale and Carlo Sabbarese, “Double-Relief Silver Coins Minted in the Greek Colonies (444–390/340–280/270 BC) of Southern Italy Analysed by XRF,” <i>Quantum Beam Science</i> 8, no. 1 (2024): 2, https://doi.org/10.3390/qubs8010002. [Open access]","María José Estarán Tolosa, review of <i>Alloglossoi: Multilingualism and Minority Languages in Ancient Europe</i>, ed. Albio Cesare Cassio and Sara Kaczko, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.09.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.09.02/. [Open access]","John Pedley, review of <i>Ritorno a Santa Venera: storia del santuario di Afrodite Urania-Venere Iovia di Paestum</i>, by Mario Torelli, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.04.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.04.22/. [Open access]","Mark Thatcher, review of <i>Between Rome and Carthage: Southern Italy during the Second Punic War</i>, by Michael P. Fronda, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.09.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.09.22/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG/1920px-Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG","title":"Magna Graecia","desc":"Temple of Concordia - Agrigento, Italy. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG","alt":"Agrigento-Tempio della Concordia01"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-224","num":224,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The stamp that turned a weighed lump of metal into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> added an issuing authority's guarantee, so that a payment could be counted out instead of weighed and tested.","questions":["Seventh-century Anatolia is where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins, and it begins in electrum, a pale alloy of gold and silver rather than in either metal on its own.","Most explanations of the invention of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now settle on the state, which needed to make acceptable payments and then wanted to standardise and protect what it was paying in."],"answer":"coinage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Invented</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 525 – 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Coinage</b> is metal issued in standardised pieces under a stamp, and what the stamp added to weighed bullion was an authority's guarantee, so that a payment could be counted instead of weighed and tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began in seventh-century Anatolia, and it began in electrum, a pale alloy of gold and silver rather than in either metal on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Lydia the series probably opens in the reign of Gyges, with Gyges and his son Alyattes the likeliest issuers of the earliest Lydian electrum, though the evidence falls short of proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The alloy was regulated rather than natural: the old view that these coins were struck from electrum panned out of the Pactolus has been discarded, and lead content points to gold from several sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Separate gold and silver coinages followed almost certainly under Croesus, and silver coinage was spreading through the Greek world around the middle of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What Greece used before is unsettled, and one case is that weighed silver was already the preferred medium of exchange there, so that coin only formalised a practice the Greeks had long had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that, the absence of Greek <i>Hacksilber</i>, silver chopped up and weighed, has been read as showing that spits, tripods and cauldrons were the recognised medium instead, and that the move to coin was direct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state is where most explanations now settle: coin came out of the need to make acceptable payments, and the motive shifted from the fiscal towards a wish to standardise and protect the currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early electrum staters look like issues struck to make high-value payments, and the invention has been read as driven by state military priorities rather than by everyday trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wide variation in the weights of the smallest denominations implies local monetisation rather than production for foreign trade, and Athens took up coinage about 525 to 500 BCE, amid a wider fiscal restructuring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"coinage","tags":["practice","history","greece","archaic","anatolia","technology"],"sources":["Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Greek and Roman Coinage</i>, ed. William E. Metcalf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013-05-11.html. [Open access]","Elizabeth Kosmetatou, review of <i>Hacksilber to Coinage: New Insights into the Monetary History of the Near East and Greece</i>, ed. Miriam S. Balmuth, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.16. [Open access]","John H. Kroll, review of <i>Money and its Uses in the Ancient Greek World</i>, ed. Andrew Meadows and Kirsty Shipton, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.07.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.07.24/. [Open access]","Ben Akrigg, review of <i>Agoranomia: Studies in Money and Exchange Presented to John H. Kroll</i>, ed. Peter G. van Alfen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.12.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.12.04/. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, “Tout ce qui brille… Electrum and the Origins of Western Coinage,” <i>Revue numismatique</i> 178 (2021): 443–70, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7859603. [Open access]","Andrew Monson, review of <i>Ships and Silver, Taxes and Tribute: A Fiscal History of Archaic Athens</i>, by Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.08.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.08.42/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Izmir_Archaeology_museum_Greek_coins_5810.jpg/1920px-Izmir_Archaeology_museum_Greek_coins_5810.jpg","title":"coinage","desc":"From a hoard found in Clazomenae, a number of \"staters\" (a standard measure) from the 6th century BCE. The central \"lion and bull\" one is thought to come from Lydia. This puts them with the first coins ever struck, the invention is thought to be Lydian. Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Izmir_Archaeology_museum_Greek_coins_5810.jpg","alt":"Izmir Archaeology museum Greek coins 5810"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"gr-225","num":225,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The lion's head in profile is the commonest type on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and six of those coins carry the Lydian inscription WALWET, taken to name the king the Greeks called Alyattes.","questions":["Analysis has shown that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was artificially debased to about 54 per cent gold, so its composition was regulated rather than panned out of the Pactolus as it came.","The ladder of fractions in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs right down to a ninety-sixth of a stater, a tiny coin stamped with the type of a lion's paw rather than the usual head."],"answer":"Lydian electrum coinage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Struck</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alyattes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 610 – 560 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Croesus reform</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lydian electrum coinage</b> is the earliest coinage of the Lydian kingdom, and the commonest coins from the Ephesus excavations are those stamped with a lion's head in profile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was struck in graduated denominations, the largest a third of a Lydian weight stater at 4.7 grams (0.17 ounces), running down through a ladder of fractions to a forty-eighth at 0.29 grams (0.010 ounces).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smaller still is the ninety-sixth of a stater, which carries the type of a lion's paw, weighs 0.15 grams (0.0053 ounces) and measures only 4 millimetres (0.16 inches) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A twelfth-stater from the Central Basis of the Artemision sets its inscription between confronting lion heads with a single punch on the reverse, and about 155 coins with that legend are known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Six of the lion-head coins bear the Lydian inscription WALWET, which many take to record the king the Greeks knew as Alyattes, and a few others carry KUKALIM, 'of Gyges'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The dating rests on die links: the Kukalim coins share dies with the Walwet issues, and a coin from the foundations of the second shrine at the Artemision, no later than about 640 to 620 BCE, was struck from a punch used for Walwet coins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The metal is not what it looks like, for analysis shows the lion-head coins were artificially debased to about 54 per cent gold, 44 per cent silver and one or two per cent copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Electrum does occur naturally in the streams of the region, but the old view that these coins were panned straight out of the Pactolus has been discarded, the composition being regulated rather than natural.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Simple refining installations stood outside the city walls near the Pactolus in the first half of that century, parting gold from silver with salt at about 800 °C (1,470 °F) and recovering the silver by cupellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Around the middle of the 6th century BCE Croesus called in the electrum coins of the realm and exchanged them for a bimetallic coinage of pure gold and pure silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lydian electrum coinage","tags":["object","history","greece","archaic","anatolia","technology"],"sources":["John H. Kroll, “The Coins of Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-kroll-coins-of-sardis. [Open access]","Crawford H. Greenewalt, Jr., with an addition by Nicholas Cahill, “Gold and Silver Refining at Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-greenewalt-gold-silver-refining. [Open access]","“Electrum Twelfth-Stater with Lydian Inscription ‘walwet’,” cat. 20 in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Istanbul Archaeological Museum IAM 5974, Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/artifacts/latw-20. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, “Tout ce qui brille… Electrum and the Origins of Western Coinage,” <i>Revue numismatique</i> 178 (2021): 443–70, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7859603. [Open access]","Ben Akrigg, review of <i>Agoranomia: Studies in Money and Exchange Presented to John H. Kroll</i>, ed. Peter G. van Alfen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.12.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.12.04/. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Greek and Roman Coinage</i>, ed. William E. Metcalf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013-05-11.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Stat%C3%A8re_en_%C3%A9lectrum_de_Zeugitane_repr%C3%A9sentant_un_cheval_debout.jpg/1920px-Stat%C3%A8re_en_%C3%A9lectrum_de_Zeugitane_repr%C3%A9sentant_un_cheval_debout.jpg","title":"Lydian electrum coinage","desc":"Nom de l'atelier : Zeugitane, Carthage Métal : électrum Diamètre : 18,5mm Axe des coins : 12h. Poids : 7,45g. Titulature avers : Anépigraphe. Description avers : Tête de Tanit à gauche, couronnée d’épis, parée d’un collier et d’un triple pendentif d’oreilles. Titulature revers : Anépigraphe. cgb.fr,. CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stat%C3%A8re_en_%C3%A9lectrum_de_Zeugitane_repr%C3%A9sentant_un_cheval_debout.jpg","alt":"Statère en électrum de Zeugitane représentant un cheval debout"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-226","num":226,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cyrus sent camels from his baggage train against the cavalry of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the best in the world at that time, and the horses took fright and threw the line into disarray.","questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Lydian empire reached its greatest extent, running east as far as the river Halys, with all the Greeks in Asia paying him tribute.","The reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended when Sardis fell after a fourteen-day siege, a Mardian having climbed an escarpment of the acropolis the Lydians thought too sheer to need a guard."],"answer":"Croesus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 560 – 547 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sardis sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">547 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Croesus</b> is the last king of Lydia, fifth of the Mermnad line after Gyges, Ardys, Sadyattes and Alyattes, who ruled in patrilinear succession from about 680 BCE, and he held the throne from about 560 into the 540s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The western Anatolian empire those kings had built reached its greatest extent under him, running east as far as the river Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He attacked Ephesus and then made war on the other Aeolian and Ionian cities, and by the end of it all the Greeks in Asia were paying him tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lydian wealth in gold and silver became proverbial, and it came from secondary gold carried down by the mountain streams on the north side of Mount Tmolus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was he who called in the electrum coinage of the realm and exchanged it for a bimetallic coinage of pure gold and pure silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When Cyrus revolted from Astyages, king of the Medes, Croesus prepared for war, and the two armies met in Cappadocia at the city of Pteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His cavalry was the best in the world at that time and the strongest arm of his army, and it was thrown into disarray when Cyrus sent camels from his baggage train into the fray, terrifying the horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Lydians were driven within their walls and besieged, and after fourteen days a Mardian named Hyroiades climbed an escarpment of the acropolis so sheer that they had thought it needed no guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sack is dated to 547 BCE on the evidence of the Nabonidus Chronicle, the fortification wall was burned along with houses at its foot and elsewhere in the city, and Croesus himself was taken alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His dedications at Delphi, his dealings with Greek oracles and his alliances with Greek states were still being argued over in Herodotus's day, and what is handed down of the dynasty's rise and his legendary wealth is largely tales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Croesus","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic","anatolia"],"sources":["Crawford H. Greenewalt, Jr., “Introduction,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-greenewalt-introduction. [Open access]","Nicholas Cahill, “The Persian Sack of Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-cahill-persian-sack-sardis. [Open access]","John H. Kroll, “The Coins of Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-kroll-coins-of-sardis. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, “Tout ce qui brille… Electrum and the Origins of Western Coinage,” <i>Revue numismatique</i> 178 (2021): 443–70, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7859603. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>The Mother of the Gods, Athens, and the Tyranny of Asia: A Study of Sovereignty in Ancient Religion</i>, by Mark H. Munn, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.10.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.10.27. [Open access]","Catherine M. Draycott, review of <i>The Lydians and Sardis</i>, by Hasan Dedeoglu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.56/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Kroisos_stake_Louvre_G197.jpg/1920px-Kroisos_stake_Louvre_G197.jpg","title":"Croesus","desc":"Croesus at the stake. Side A from an Attic red-figure amphora, ca. 500–490 BCE. From en:Vulci. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kroisos_stake_Louvre_G197.jpg","alt":"Kroisos stake Louvre G197"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-227","num":227,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A city that adopted its neighbour's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bought compatibility with that neighbour's coinage, which is usually explained by the wish for ready exchangeability in trade.","questions":["The sources do not state a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so it is inferred from the coins, by finding the heaviest surviving examples of a denomination and taking those to approximate it.","Much of the early electrum clings to the Lydo-Milesian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the Attic one came in time to dominate the coinage of the whole Greek world."],"answer":"weight standard","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>weight standard</b> is the weight a coinage's denominations are struck to, and it is what decides whether one city's silver can be paid out against another's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is not stated in the sources but inferred from the coins, by finding the heaviest examples of a denomination and taking those to approximate the standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second method reads the 80th percentile of a large sample, and on the early electrum the whole coinage shows a remarkable conformity to a single standard, matching a twelfth of a Cyzicene stater at the higher denomination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the early electrum clings closely to what is called the Lydo-Milesian standard, whose stater weighs about 14.1 grams (0.50 ounces), and Lydia's own lion-head coins were cut as fractions of a weight stater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Aeginetan standard puts its stater at about 12.4 grams (0.44 ounces), and the Attic standard came in time to dominate the coinage of the Greek world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The first coins of Anatolia were struck on highly localised weight systems, and the use of electrum made exchange complicated on top of that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A city that took up a neighbour's standard bought compatibility with that neighbour's coinage, and the wish for ready exchangeability within trade networks is the usual explanation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tension between local coinages and the attempt to lock them into larger networks of common standards is one of the marks of ancient Mediterranean connectivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reading a standard off a table of weights is inference, and the same figures bear more than one reading: Bosporan drachms taken for Aeginetan imply a stater of 11.7 grams (0.41 ounces), nearer a Persian standard than an Aeginetan one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A full range of denominations of silver was present in northern Greece from the start, so the standard governed not just the stater but every fraction struck beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"weight standard","tags":["concept","history","greece","archaic","anatolia","technology"],"sources":["Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Beginning of Coinage in the Cimmerian Bosporus (A Hoard from Phanagoria)</i>, by Vladimir D. Kuznetsov and Mikhail G. Abramzon, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.02.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.02.46/. [Open access]","William E. Metcalf, review of <i>Money in Classical Antiquity</i>, by Sitta von Reden, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.10.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.10.06/. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, “Tout ce qui brille… Electrum and the Origins of Western Coinage,” <i>Revue numismatique</i> 178 (2021): 443–70, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7859603. [Open access]","John H. Kroll, “The Coins of Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-kroll-coins-of-sardis. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Greek and Roman Coinage</i>, ed. William E. Metcalf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013-05-11.html. [Open access]","Ben Akrigg, review of <i>Agoranomia: Studies in Money and Exchange Presented to John H. Kroll</i>, ed. Peter G. van Alfen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.12.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.12.04/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Authentic_standard_weight_of_Vienna_from_1756.jpg","title":"weight standard","desc":"Authentic weight measure of Vienna from 1756 in the National Museum of Slovenia. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Authentic_standard_weight_of_Vienna_from_1756.jpg","alt":"Authentic standard weight of Vienna from 1756"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-228","num":228,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew Greeks from many cities rather than one, and the four best known held the crown games at Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia and Nemea.","questions":["The idea that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood for Greek unity sits badly with the evidence, these being places wars were fought over and places that fuelled disunity through confrontational dedication.","At Dodona the sacred oak of Zeus, the percussive cauldrons and the flight of doves gave the oracle its voice, one shape a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could take beyond the games."],"answer":"Panhellenic sanctuary","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Panhellenic sanctuary</b> is a sanctuary used by Greeks from many cities rather than by one, and the four best known are Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia and Nemea, which held the crown games.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The label reaches wider than those four, and Dodona, Delos and the sanctuary of Zeus on Mount Lykaion in Arcadia are counted among them too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The games and the site grew together, the evolution of the games being directly related to the history of the sanctuary that housed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Olympia's history has been traced through nine chronological stages, running from the 3rd millennium BCE down to the abandonment of the site in the 7th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Delphi is the odd one out, a settlement as well as a ritual site, with little physical evidence of athletic building there before the 4th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The standard idea of Panhellenism does not tally well with the facts, and these were places wars were fought over, places that fuelled disunity through conspicuous and regularly confrontational dedication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were governed differently: Olympia is better thought of as an extra-urban sanctuary under the control of Elis, while at Delphi dedicators had a greater say in where their votives stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A monument was read against its neighbours rather than in a vacuum, so putting one up could be an act of one-upmanship with the structure beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The four festivals were not equal in standing, since catalogues of victories typically list Nemea last, and victory poetry has the laudatory compounds Olympionikos, Pythionikos and Isthmionikos but never Nemeonikos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The setting was part of the experience, wind, water, animals and people sounding in conditions imposed by natural features of oracular significance, as at Dodona with its sacred oak, its percussive cauldrons and its doves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Panhellenic sanctuary","tags":["place","concept","history","greece","archaic","religion"],"sources":["Chris Eckerman, review of <i>Delphi and Olympia: The Spatial Politics of Panhellenism in the Archaic and Classical Periods</i>, by Michael Scott, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.55. [Open access]","Paul Christesen, review of <i>Games and Sanctuaries in Ancient Greece: Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia, Nemea, Athens</i>, by Panos Valavanis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.20. [Open access]","Manolis Mikrakis, review of <i>Soundscape and Landscape at Panhellenic Greek Sanctuaries</i>, ed. Erica Angliker and Angela Bellia, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.11.35, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.11.35/. [Open access]","Roman Ivanov, review of <i>Pindar's Poetry, Patrons, and Festivals: From Archaic Greece to the Roman Empire</i>, ed. Simon Hornblower and Catherine Morgan, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.10.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.10.45/. [Open access]","David Lunt, review of <i>Two Studies in the History of Ancient Greek Athletics</i>, by Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.06.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.06.51. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_Temple_of_Apollo_%28Delphi%2C_Sanctuary_of_Apollo%29_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg/1920px-The_Temple_of_Apollo_%28Delphi%2C_Sanctuary_of_Apollo%29_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg","title":"Panhellenic sanctuary","desc":"The Temple of Apollo (Delphi, Sanctuary of Apollo) on October 4, 2020. 2020-10-04 11:56:31. George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Temple_of_Apollo_(Delphi,_Sanctuary_of_Apollo)_on_October_4,_2020.jpg","alt":"The Temple of Apollo (Delphi, Sanctuary of Apollo) on October 4, 2020"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-229","num":229,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The traditional date of 776 BCE for the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was settled on because that year synchronised Iphitus with Lycurgus, not from any written record of the festival.","questions":["Coming second or third at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counted for nothing, and the amateur-and-professional framing once used to describe the competitors is now thought misleading.","Numbering successive Olympiads from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became a standard way of assigning dates to everything of significance in Greek history, long after the festival itself had begun."],"answer":"Olympic Games","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">776 BCE traditionally</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Victor list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 BCE, by Hippias</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">385 CE at least</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Olympic Games</b> are the athletic festival held at the sanctuary of Zeus at Olympia, which is better thought of as an extra-urban sanctuary under the control of Elis than as neutral ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are the senior of the four crown games: catalogues of victories typically list Nemea last, and of nearly 200 Olympic monuments recorded by Pausanias not one commemorates a victory from outside that circuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The events fall into running, combat, pentathlon and equestrian classes, though the programme can be reconstructed only hypothetically, on the basis of fragmentary literary sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only winning counted, coming second or third counting for nothing, and the amateur-and-professional framing that used to organise accounts of the games is now thought misleading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The cases usually cited for female participation, the Hera festival at Olympia, the Arcteia at Brauron and the Spartan model of education, relate not to women but to girls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The traditional date of 776 BCE rests on Hippias of Elis, who compiled the first complete list of Olympic victors around 400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had no such list to work from and was forced to compile it himself, out of family reports and inscriptions that were not easily datable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He settled on 776 because that year synchronised Iphitus with Lycurgus, and not because of any written record of the games or of their victors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So the names he gives for the early victors can be trusted far more than the dates he assigned to their victories, or even than the order he put them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Numbering successive Olympiads became a standard way of assigning dates to everything of significance in Greek history, and the games were held until at least 385 CE, though when the last was held is unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olympic Games","tags":["event","practice","history","greece","archaic","religion"],"sources":["Stephen Brunet, review of <i>Olympic Victor Lists and Ancient Greek History</i>, by Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.67, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.67/. [Open access]","David Lunt, review of <i>Two Studies in the History of Ancient Greek Athletics</i>, by Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.06.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.06.51. [Open access]","David Gilman Romano, review of <i>Ancient Greek Athletics: Primary Sources in Translation</i>, by Charles H. Stocking and Susan A. Stephens, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.02.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.02.13/. [Open access]","Paul Christesen, review of <i>Games and Sanctuaries in Ancient Greece: Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia, Nemea, Athens</i>, by Panos Valavanis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.20. [Open access]","Chris Eckerman, review of <i>Delphi and Olympia: The Spatial Politics of Panhellenism in the Archaic and Classical Periods</i>, by Michael Scott, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.55. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>The Ancient Olympic Games</i>, 2nd ed., by Judith Swaddling, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.06.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.06.24. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Ancient_Olympic_Starting_Line.JPG/1920px-Ancient_Olympic_Starting_Line.JPG","title":"Olympic Games","desc":"Part of the starting line at the stadium at Olympia. Jamesscottbrown, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Olympic_Starting_Line.JPG","alt":"Ancient Olympic Starting Line"},"difficulty":1},
{"id":"gr-230","num":230,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"During the games arms were not laid down anywhere in the Greek world, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> guaranteed a safe trip for athletes and spectators and nothing wider.","questions":["Modern commentators keen on peace and brotherhood have made much of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which in no way sought to promote peace and cannot be linked to the brotherhood of humanity.","The secession of Mytilene was planned during the games of 428 BCE, and mercenaries used the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to go looking for employment."],"answer":"Olympic truce","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Olympic truce</b>, the <i>ekecheiria</i>, is the arrangement that surrounded the four-yearly games at the sanctuary of Zeus at Olympia, and it did a great deal less than its modern reputation suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The finding that has displaced the older picture is blunt: during the Olympic Games arms were not laid down in the Greek world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the truce actually guaranteed was a temporary safe trip for athletes, coaches and spectators, and it reached no further than the people travelling to the festival and home again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contemporary commentators wanting to stress the ideals of peace, unity and brotherhood claimed by the modern Olympics have made much of this so-called sacred truce, which in no way sought to promote peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word covers a suspension of hostilities against those on the road, not the suspension of a war, and it cannot be linked to any notion of the brotherhood of humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It served as a political instrument like any other: the secession of Mytilene was planned during the Olympic Games of 428 BCE, and mercenaries made use of it when they were looking for employment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 420 BCE the Eleans flogged the Spartan Lichas, which intensified the tensions of the moment and brought Argos into the anti-Spartan alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sanctuary of Olympia and the agonistic festivals have a bellicose character of their own, and these were places wars were fought over rather than places that suspended them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The circuit the truce served was a competitive hierarchy rather than a fellowship, catalogues of victories typically listing Nemea last of the four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athletics excluded non-Greeks and women and may even have reinforced divisions within Greek society, and the games ran on regardless, numbered by Olympiads, until at least 385 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olympic truce","tags":["practice","history","greece","archaic","religion","warfare"],"sources":["Christian Mann, review of <i>War-Peace and Panhellenic Games</i>, ed. Nikos Birgalias, Kostas Buraselis, Paul Cartledge et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.09.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.09.04. [Open access]","Michael Carter, review of <i>Onward to the Olympics: Historical Perspectives on the Olympic Games</i>, ed. Gerald P. Schaus and Stephen R. Wenn, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.56. [Open access]","Stephen Brunet, review of <i>Olympic Victor Lists and Ancient Greek History</i>, by Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.67, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.67/. [Open access]","David Lunt, review of <i>Two Studies in the History of Ancient Greek Athletics</i>, by Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.06.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.06.51. [Open access]","Chris Eckerman, review of <i>Delphi and Olympia: The Spatial Politics of Panhellenism in the Archaic and Classical Periods</i>, by Michael Scott, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.55. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>The Ancient Olympic Games</i>, 2nd ed., by Judith Swaddling, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.06.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.06.24. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-231","num":231,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Several villages together with outlying Amyklai made up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had no city wall at all and held a territory of roughly 8,000 square kilometres (3,100 square miles).","questions":["Every citizen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a hoplite, where in other cities the hoplites were only the wealthiest 30 to 50 per cent, and all of them dined in their messes every night.","The archaeology of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a central settlement of no great monumental character in the archaic and classical periods, with streets not laid on a grid until the imperial period."],"answer":"Sparta","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sparta</b> is the Greek city of Laconia, made up of several villages together with outlying Amyklai rather than a single walled town, and it held a territory of roughly 8,000 square kilometres (3,100 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It had no city wall, and the explanations offered for that run to Spartan conservatism, the presence of the <i>perioikoi</i>, and the sheer difficulty of enclosing the four central villages and Amyklai together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was like an armed camp whose citizens were required to dine in their messes every night, and all Spartiates were hoplites, where in other cities the hoplites were the wealthiest 30 to 50 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archaeology does not match that picture: the central settlement had no significantly monumental character in the archaic and classical periods, and its streets were not laid on a rectangular grid before the imperial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Material culture was no simple reflection of the development of a military ethos, and at the sanctuary of Artemis Orthia the votive lead figurines begin to increase well before the supposed 6th-century date of austerity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The city rested on the helots, enslaved as a nation to the Spartan state and given brutal treatment by their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Laconian and Messenian helots were not one group: the Messenians laid claim to an Achaean heritage older than the Dorian, and Laconian helotage outlasted Messenian liberation by centuries, ending only under Roman domination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their numbers are argued over, with 7 to 1 proposed as an upper limit on the ratio of helots to Spartans, below the higher figures once current.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The institutions attributed to the lawgiver Lycurgus include the <i>gerousia</i>, the <i>agoge</i>, the <i>krypteia</i> and the <i>syssitia</i>, and the kingship was dual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Unlocking any of it is hard, because there are very few direct Spartan sources and what survives is mostly non-Spartan and severely influenced by the Spartans' own manipulation of their image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Sparta","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","warfare","politics"],"sources":["Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Graham Shipley, review of <i>Sparta in Laconia: The Archaeology of a City and its Countryside</i>, ed. William G. Cavanagh and S. E. C. Walker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.03.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.03.02. [Open access]","Review of <i>Spartan Reflections</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.02.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.02.13/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Antique_Map_of_Classical_City_of_Sparta.jpg/1920px-Antique_Map_of_Classical_City_of_Sparta.jpg","title":"Sparta","desc":"Old Map of Classical City of Sparta Topographic map by Jean-Denis Barbié du Bocage / Jean-Baptiste-Henri Delahaye, created 1783. Map number 32 in \"Travels of Anacharsis\" published 1817. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antique_Map_of_Classical_City_of_Sparta.jpg","alt":"Antique Map of Classical City of Sparta"},"difficulty":1,"locator":{"name":"Sparta","at":[22.4236,37.0819]}},
{"id":"gr-232","num":232,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The region Sparta ruled was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its heartland the Eurotas valley, framed by Taygetos on the west and Parnon on the east and running south to Malea and Tainaron.","questions":["Survey east of Sparta found very little long-term continuity in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each broad period laying down anew its own pattern and hierarchy of settlement.","A sudden proliferation of 87 sites appears in the mid-6th century BCE in the surveyed part of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, followed by widespread abandonment in the classical period."],"answer":"Laconia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Laconia</b> is the region of the southern Peloponnese that Sparta ruled, its heartland the Eurotas valley, the Spartan plain, with the neighbouring rural landscapes running out from it on either side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains frame it, Taygetos along the west and Parnon along the east, and it runs south to the peninsulas of Malea and Tainaron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its road network divides into four zones, north, east, south and west, and a route ran into and probably across Taygetos, separate from the roads to Gytheion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The perioikic towns had territories of their own, and in the territory of Geronthrai, the modern Geraki, no fewer than 13 routes have been identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> People were on the land early, the surveyed country having been first colonised in late and final neolithic times, though dense occupation appears only from Early Helladic II onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Sparta's territory ran to roughly 8,000 square kilometres (3,100 square miles), and a survey of 70 square kilometres (27 square miles) east of the city set out to examine how the heartland stood to the country around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it found was very little long-term continuity, each broad period of time laying down anew a unique pattern and hierarchy of settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the mid-6th century BCE there is a sudden proliferation of 87 sites in the surveyed area, and in the classical period a widespread abandonment of many of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who was doing the farming is a question the survey raised and could not settle: Spartiates, perioikoi, helots, or some inferior Spartan group working the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Laconian helots were not the same people as the Messenians, and Laconian helotage outlasted Messenian liberation by centuries, coming to an end only under Roman domination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Laconia","tags":["place","region","history","greece","archaic","geography"],"sources":["David Pettegrew, review of <i>Continuity and Change in a Greek Rural Landscape: The Laconia Survey</i>, ed. William G. Cavanagh et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.09.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.09.42/. [Open access]","Graham Shipley, review of <i>Sparta in Laconia: The Archaeology of a City and its Countryside</i>, ed. William G. Cavanagh and S. E. C. Walker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.03.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.03.02. [Open access]","Review of <i>Spartan Reflections</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.02.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.02.13/. [Open access]","D. Graham J. Shipley, review of <i>The Road-Network of Lakonike</i>, by Giannes A. Pikoulas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.16. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Eurotas.JPG/1920px-Eurotas.JPG","title":"Laconia","desc":"Rivière Eurotas, quelques kilomètres en aval de Mistra. Aeleftherios, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurotas.JPG","alt":"Eurotas"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Laconia","at":[22.5,37],"kind":"region","area":[[22.05,37.3],[22.45,37.45],[22.85,37.35],[23.1,37.05],[23.2,36.75],[23.05,36.45],[22.75,36.7],[22.55,36.4],[22.3,36.65],[22.2,37]]}},
{"id":"gr-233","num":233,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The towns of Laconia and Messenia together with Sparta itself constituted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the state, where Sparta was only the city at the head of it.","questions":["Aristotle wrote that there are six so-called <i>morai</i> and that all the citizens of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are divided into them, which is where the shape of the state shows.","Of the 292 men taken alive from the garrison on Sphakteria, picked by lot from all the divisions of the army of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, roughly 120 were <i>Spartiatai</i>."],"answer":"Lacedaemon","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lacedaemon</b> is the state where Sparta is the city: the free inhabitants of the many towns of Laconia and Messenia, along with Sparta itself, constituted the state known as Lakedaimon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two names run in parallel, so <i>Spartiatai</i> and <i>Lakedaimonioi</i> can each be used of the <i>homoioi</i>, or more broadly of the citizens of the Spartan <i>polis</i> and of the Lakedaimonian state respectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The towns are the perioikic communities, and they seem to have been <i>poleis</i> in their own right, albeit ones politically dependent on Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is disputed: one case classifies them as villages whose people were citizens of the Spartan <i>polis</i> in no sense at all, another defends the traditional view that they were citizens without a Spartiate's rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The national festival, the Hyakinthia, reinforced the hierarchical divide between city and territory, where the Athenian equivalent united <i>polis</i> and <i>chora</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The army is where the arrangement shows, and Aristotle wrote that there are six so-called <i>morai</i> and that all the Lakedaimonians are divided into them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The garrison caught on Sphakteria had been picked by lot from all the divisions of the Lakedaimonian army, and of the 292 taken alive roughly 120 were <i>Spartiatai</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One reading is that as the <i>homoioi</i> declined in number the <i>morai</i> were filled mostly from the <i>hypomeiones</i>, Spartans short of full citizenship, while most <i>perioikoi</i> went on fighting in contingents of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that reading the <i>morai</i> were the civic contingent of Sparta itself within the larger Lakedaimonian army, an arrangement that strained: some perioikic soldiers refused call-up orders and others deserted once called.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below both orders stood the helots, enslaved as a nation to the Spartan state, and none of it is easy to read, since very few sources are Spartan and what survives is shaped by the Spartans' management of their own image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lacedaemon","tags":["state","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Sarah Pomeroy, review of <i>Sparta: Beyond the Mirage</i>, ed. Anton Powell and Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.08.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.08.06. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Review of <i>Spartan Reflections</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.02.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.02.13/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/The_Ancient_Theatre_of_Sparta_on_May_15%2C_2019.jpg/1920px-The_Ancient_Theatre_of_Sparta_on_May_15%2C_2019.jpg","title":"Lacedaemon","desc":"The ancient theatre of Sparta on the southern slope of the acropolis hill, a site ancient authors connect with the celebration of the Gymnopaidiae. George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ancient_Theatre_of_Sparta_on_May_15,_2019.jpg","alt":"The curved stone seating of the ancient theatre of Sparta"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Lacedaemon","at":[22.4236,37.0819],"kind":"region","area":[[21.5,37.2],[21.9,37.48],[22.2,37.52],[22.45,37.45],[22.85,37.35],[23.1,37.05],[23.2,36.75],[23.05,36.45],[22.75,36.7],[22.55,36.4],[22.3,36.65],[21.9,36.7],[21.6,36.75],[21.5,37]]}},
{"id":"gr-234","num":234,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Sparta's kings claimed descent from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the line of Herakles, and that claim is the charter on which Dorian Sparta rested its hold on the Peloponnese.","questions":["Tyrtaeus gives the earliest mention of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, writing of ancestors who came under the guidance of kings who were descendants of Herakles.","The genealogy of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was invented in Argos and developed further in Sparta, where it made conquest look like rightful inheritance rather than seizure."],"answer":"Herakleidai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the genealogy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Herakleidai</b> are the descendants of Herakles from whom the kings of Sparta claimed to come, and that claim is the charter of Dorian Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Spartan kingship was dual, and both of its royal houses traced themselves back along the same line to the same ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyrtaeus gives the earliest mention, writing of ancestors who came under the guidance of kings who were descendants of Herakles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pindar, Herodotus, Isocrates and Ephorus carry it forward, Herodotus making Aristodemus the great-grandson of Hyllus and Ephorus working the story into its dominant version.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The royal descent is a secondary reading of the older story of the Return of the Herakleidai, and the genealogy was invented in Argos before being developed further in Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is genealogical fiction serving a political purpose rather than a record of anything, and it belongs with the rest of what modern work has tried to see past under the name of the Spartan mirage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it did was legitimise Dorian settlement, since territorial right came down from Herakles, and conquest could then be presented as rightful inheritance rather than as seizure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Heraklids were brought into the local genealogy perhaps as early as the beginning of the 7th century BCE, while the fundamental formation of Spartan statehood is put in the 8th, on literary and archaeological evidence together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state the claim underwrote was Lakedaimon, the free inhabitants of the towns of Laconia and Messenia along with Sparta itself, and not the city on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> None of it is easy to check, since very few sources are Spartan and what survives is mostly non-Spartan and severely influenced by the Spartans' management of their own image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Herakleidai","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Alexey L. Darvin, “Origin of Spartan Kings from Heracles,” <i>RUDN Journal of World History</i> 10, no. 3 (2018): 237–49, https://doi.org/10.22363/2312-8127-2018-10-3-237-249. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]","Sarah Pomeroy, review of <i>Sparta: Beyond the Mirage</i>, ed. Anton Powell and Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.08.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.08.06. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Hercules_Nessus_MAN_Napoli_Inv9001.jpg/1920px-Hercules_Nessus_MAN_Napoli_Inv9001.jpg","title":"Herakleidai","desc":"Hercules carrying his son Hyllus looks at the centaur Nessus, who is about to carry Deianira across the river on his back. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hercules_Nessus_MAN_Napoli_Inv9001.jpg","alt":"Hercules Nessus MAN Napoli"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-235","num":235,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"By the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Sparta took Messenia, and the people of the conquered country were reduced to the status of helots in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.","questions":["For fifth-century historians such as Antiochus of Syracuse and Thucydides there was only one archaic conflict, and the pair of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> everyone now counts was established only in the 4th century.","When Sparta expanded into Messenia, at the time of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, there was no Messenian ethnic identity, and the region became a coherent one only under Spartan control."],"answer":"Messenian Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 460 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liberation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">370 – 369 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Messenian Wars</b> are the wars by which Sparta took Messenia, whose people were reduced to the status of helots after the conquest of their country in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The towns of Messenia thereafter counted, with those of Laconia and with Sparta itself, in the state known as Lakedaimon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their historicity does not let itself be easily reconstructed, and the number of them turns out to belong to the tradition rather than to be a fact standing behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word Messene named a smaller country then than the classical region does, and for 5th-century historians such as Antiochus of Syracuse and Thucydides there was only one archaic Messenian war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An account with two archaic wars was established in the 4th century and developed afterwards, elaborated by Myron of Priene and Rhianus, whose work Pausanias adapted freely into a highly idiosyncratic and colourful version.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When Sparta expanded into Messenia there was no Messenian ethnic identity, and Messenia was not perceived as a coherent geographical region, becoming one only under Spartan control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaic Messenia had no obviously predominant central settlement, and it was not developing as other Greek regions were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Messenian identity emerged in the 5th century, and the rebellion against the Spartans in the 460s was critical to it, the rebels choosing the name of Messenians for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those exiled at the end of that war and settled by the Athenians at Naupaktos proclaimed the identity in very visible dedications at Delphi and Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The liberation of 370 to 369 BCE created a free Messenian state, and none of the story is easy to check, since very few sources are Spartan and what survives is shaped by Sparta's management of its own image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Messenian Wars","tags":["event","history","greece","archaic","warfare","politics"],"sources":["James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","Yves Lafond, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.30/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/Aristomenes_fighting_his_way_out_of_Ira.jpg","title":"Messenian Wars","desc":"Aristomenes fighting his way out of Ira. Edmund Ollier Publication date 1882. 2018-12-08 14:34:28. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristomenes_fighting_his_way_out_of_Ira.jpg","alt":"Aristomenes fighting his way out of Ira"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-236","num":236,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Tyrtaeus wrote that the fighting of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran nineteen years, and that in the twentieth the enemy left their rich lands and fled from the great uplands of Ithome.","questions":["Pausanias dated the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to 743 to 724 BCE, but those figures are Hellenistic, resting on Spartan kinglists where one generation was reckoned at forty years.","Because Messene then meant a limited area rather than the classical region, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to the plain of the upper Pamisus and the lands about Mount Ithome."],"answer":"First Messenian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional</span><span class=\"dt-v\">743 – 724 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lower dating</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 690 – 670 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>First Messenian War</b> is the earlier of the two archaic wars in which Sparta made Messenia its own and its people helots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyrtaeus is the nearest thing to a contemporary witness, naming King Theopompus, writing of the spearmen fathers of his hearers' fathers, and giving the fighting a length of twenty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his lines the war ran nineteen years, and in the twentieth the foeman left his rich lands and fled from the great uplands of Ithome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For Tyrtaeus, as for Homer, Messene meant a limited area rather than the classical region, and the fighting belongs to the plain of the upper Pamisus, the lands about that mountain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The long narrative everyone knows is Pausanias's, a text of the 2nd century CE and the product of a very creative engagement with previous works, and it is hardly any history at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He dates the war to 743 to 724 BCE, but those figures are Hellenistic and rest on genealogies taken from the Spartan kinglists, where one generation was reckoned at forty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Olympic victor list, which carries seven Messenian winners between 768 and 736 BCE and none after, has been read as support for those dates and proves nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lower chronology rests instead on Epaminondas, who at his trial said he had freed Messenia after 230 years of subjection, putting the second war's end near 600 BCE and this one about 690 to 670.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even the number of wars belongs to the tradition rather than standing behind it, since 5th-century historians such as Antiochus of Syracuse and Thucydides knew only one archaic Messenian war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tyrtaeus himself wrote in the second half of the 7th century BCE, and his <i>Eunomia</i>, taken with the Great Rhetra, suggests that unequal distribution of land may be the most cogent explanation for the annexation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"First Messenian War","tags":["event","warfare","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","J. M. Edmonds, ed. and trans., <i>Elegy and Iambus</i>, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), Tyrtaeus fr. 3, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0479:volume=1:text=2:section=2. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","Yves Lafond, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.30/. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Ithome_east_summit.jpg/1920px-Ithome_east_summit.jpg","title":"First Messenian War","desc":"Mount Ithome (Messene): eastern summit. Mark Landon (photographed in 1990;. digitized in 2021), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ithome_east_summit.jpg","alt":"Ithome east summit"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-237","num":237,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Tyrtaeus rallied the Spartans in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and his own date puts that fighting firmly in the second half of the 7th century BCE rather than in Pausanias's 685 to 668.","questions":["Aristomenes is the Messenian hero of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, winning several victories before he was betrayed and forced to withdraw to Mount Eira, though whether he was a real person cannot be known.","Pausanias took his account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Rhianus of Crete, who had King Laotychidas commanding, though two Spartan kings bore that name and only the later was known to him."],"answer":"Second Messenian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional</span><span class=\"dt-v\">685 – 668 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lower dating</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 635 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Second Messenian War</b> is the later of the two archaic wars, and Pausanias makes it a revolt of Messenians already under Spartan rule rather than a fresh conquest, breaking out in the reign of Anaxander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tyrtaeus is the contemporary evidence, since it was he who rallied the Spartans to defeat Messenia in this second war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His vocabulary and the hortatory character of his poetry put him later than Archilochus and Callinus, and there is no reason to date any poem of his earlier than 640 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That lodges the war firmly in the second half of the 7th century, unless higher dates are wanted for Archilochus, Callinus, Gyges and the Assyrian inscriptions of Assurbanipal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias dates it to 685 to 668 BCE, figures rejected out of hand because they cannot be squared with his own dates for the earlier war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A lower chronology puts its end near 600 BCE, on Epaminondas's word at his trial that he had freed Messenia after 230 years of subjection, and its beginning about 635 to 625.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias took his account from Rhianus of Crete, who had King Laotychidas commanding; two Spartan kings bore that name and Pausanias knew only the later, whose reign began in 491 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristomenes is the war's Messenian hero, winning several victories before he was betrayed and forced to withdraw to Mount Eira, and it is impossible to know whether he was a real person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tyrtaeus gives a fair summary of the Spartan constitution as it stood in this war and knows nothing of the ephors, whom a story has appointed to run the state while the kings were away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For all its fame, Sparta's hold on Messenia was no anomaly, but the most spectacular and best attested instance of something Greek states did to one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Second Messenian War","tags":["event","warfare","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","Barbara McCauley, review of <i>Aristomenes of Messene: Legends of Sparta's Nemesis</i>, by Daniel Ogden, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.46. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Franc_Kav%C4%8Di%C4%8D_-_Dekle_re%C5%A1i_Aristomena_iz_ujetni%C5%A1tva.jpg","title":"Second Messenian War","desc":"Franc Kavčič - Dekle reši Aristomena iz ujetništva. Franz Caucig. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franc_Kav%C4%8Di%C4%8D_-_Dekle_re%C5%A1i_Aristomena_iz_ujetni%C5%A1tva.jpg","alt":"Franc Kavčič - Dekle reši Aristomena iz ujetništva"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-238","num":238,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Each man on a Spartan campaign was to give one of the songs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once the paean had been sung after supper, which is the custom Athenaeus records.","questions":["The ancient life makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an Athenian, a lame schoolmaster of little sense, sent when the Delphic oracle bade the Spartans take a counsellor from Athens.","Aristotle cites the <i>Eunomia</i> of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as evidence that civil strife breaks out when some are in want while others are rich, men pressed by the war demanding a redistribution of land."],"answer":"Tyrtaeus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 – 633 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 books in antiquity</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tyrtaeus</b> is the Spartan elegiac poet of the Second Messenian War, and his verse is the nearest thing to a contemporary account of Sparta's conquest of Messenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexandrian scholars had five books of him, holding a poem on citizenship, exhortations in elegiac verse and war songs, among them the <i>Embateria</i>, or songs of the battle charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Suidas puts his flourishing in the 35th Olympiad and Jerome in the 36th, and his vocabulary and hortatory manner place him after Archilochus and Callinus, so no poem of his need be earlier than 640 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The ancient life makes him an Athenian, a lame schoolmaster thought to have little sense, sent when the Delphic oracle bade the Spartans take a counsellor from Athens, though Suidas calls him a Laconian or a Milesian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athenaeus records that after the Spartans had beaten the Messenians it became their custom on campaign that, once the paean had been sung after supper, each man should give one of his songs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His lines on the earlier war name King Theopompus and the fathers of his hearers' fathers, run to twenty years, and end with the enemy fleeing the great uplands of Ithome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his usage Messene named a smaller country than the classical region, as it does in Homer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Eunomia</i> was a hortatory poem carrying an oracle brought from Delphi and the return of the Heracleidae, on which the Spartan kings rested their right to Laconia, and in it the people are urged to obey the kings and the elders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle cites the poem as evidence that civil strife breaks out when some are in want while others are rich, since men pressed by the war demanded a redistribution of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias has him talking the citizens out of that quarrel, and he has been read as a Spartan Solon, though whether the <i>Eunomia</i> has anything to do with the Great Rhetra is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tyrtaeus","tags":["person","literature","history","greece","archaic","warfare"],"sources":["J. M. Edmonds, ed. and trans., <i>Elegy and Iambus</i>, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), Tyrtaeus, life and fragments, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0479:volume=1:text=2. [Open access]","Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Franz_Kollar%C5%BE_-_Tyrtaeus_inspires_the_Spartans_with_his_war_songs.jpg","title":"Tyrtaeus","desc":"Franz Kollarž - Tyrtaeus inspires the Spartans with his war songs. Franz Kollarž. c. 1872. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Kollar%C5%BE_-_Tyrtaeus_inspires_the_Spartans_with_his_war_songs.jpg","alt":"Franz Kollarž - Tyrtaeus inspires the Spartans with his war songs"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-239","num":239,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The enslaved population of Laconia and Messenia were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who worked Spartiate estates and rendered their masters a proportional share of the crop, put at half of it.","questions":["The status of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was double, since they were the private property of individual Spartiates and at the same time subjects of a significant communal domination.","An average Spartiate estate held about five families of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a ratio of seven of them to each Spartiate has been proposed as an upper limit for their numbers."],"answer":"helots","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Enslaved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Messenia freed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">370 – 369 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Helots</b> are the enslaved population of Laconia and Messenia who worked the land for the Spartiates, reduced to that status after the conquest of their country in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They rendered their masters a proportional share of what they grew on Spartiate estates, put at half the crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their status was double: the private property of individual Spartiates, and at the same time subjects of a significant communal domination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the second half of that meant in practice is that the state freed helots and killed them without reference to the Spartiates who owned them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They normally stayed attached to the holdings they cultivated rather than to particular Spartiates, an arrangement that safeguarded their subsistence and made landowner and cultivator dependent on one another over the long term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What to call the arrangement is argued: one account has them enslaved as a nation to the Spartan state, and another holds that plain slavery best describes their condition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A third has them individually owned, with restrictions on how they could be disposed of, and insists that they were not chattel slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Numbers are argued over as well: an average estate held about five families of them, an ordinary citizen's holding ran to some 18 hectares (45 acres), and a ratio of seven helots to each Spartiate has been proposed as an upper limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Laconian and the Messenian helots were not one people, the Messenians appearing to have claimed an Achaean heritage older than the Dorian, and their revolt in the 460s BCE was critical to the emergence of a Messenian identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Laconian helotage outlasted the liberation of Messenia by centuries, and the familiar modern picture of the whole institution rests less on the ancient sources than on an idiosyncratic selection from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"helots","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Ellen Millender, review of <i>Property and Wealth in Classical Sparta</i>, by Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.07.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.07.26/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Yves Lafond, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.30/. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Fernand_Sabatt%C3%A9_-_Un_Spartiate_montre_%C3%A0_ses_fils_un_ilote_ivre_%281900%29.jpg","title":"helots","desc":"Fernand Sabatté - Un Spartiate montre à ses fils un ilote ivre (1900). Fernand Sabatté. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fernand_Sabatt%C3%A9_-_Un_Spartiate_montre_%C3%A0_ses_fils_un_ilote_ivre_(1900).jpg","alt":"Fernand Sabatté - Un Spartiate montre à ses fils un ilote ivre (1900)"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-240","num":240,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The free people of the towns spread across Laconia and Messenia were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, members of the state of Lakedaimon alongside Sparta itself.","questions":["Herodotus has Demaratos tell Xerxes that Sparta holds about 8,000 men, all equals of those who fell, and that the other Lakedaimonians, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, are not their equals but are good men.","The communities of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were small, of 400 to 600 people, and looked much like domestic colonies scattered through both Laconia and Messenia."],"answer":"perioikoi","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Perioikoi</b> are the free people of the towns spread across Laconia and Messenia that made up the state of Lakedaimon alongside Sparta itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They enjoyed a degree of local autonomy and their communities went on being thought of as poleis in their own right, yet they were politically dependent on the Spartans and could neither hold office in Sparta nor sit in its assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether those settlements were really city-states or only villages has been argued both ways, and with it whether their people belonged to the Spartan <i>polis</i> at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their communities were small, of 400 to 600 people, and looked much like domestic colonies scattered through both regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They farmed on their own behalf and on the kings', with whom they had special ties, and their towns had territories of their own: in that of Geronthrai, the modern Geraki, 13 routes have been identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Archaeologically they are very hard to tell apart from the helots, though the larger buildings and the more valuable offerings may be assumed to be theirs, and perioikoi in Messenia took part in the rebellion of the 460s BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus gives the Spartan account of them through the exiled king Demaratos: there is in Lakedaimon a city, Sparta, of about 8,000 men, and the other Lakedaimonians are not their equals, but they are good men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made the Spartans better, on that account, was their <i>nomos</i>, which let them fight together in a phalanx exclusively their own, and the claim legitimated their monopoly of privilege within the Lakedaimonian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How they actually fought is argued: one case has most of them in contingents of their own, another has them homogeneous with the Spartans and standing beside them in the phalanx.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The arrangement strained as full Spartan citizens grew fewer, and in the crisis of 370 to 369 BCE some refused call-up orders while whole communities went over to Sparta's enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"perioikoi","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"sources":["Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","D. Graham J. Shipley, review of <i>The Road-Network of Lakonike</i>, by Giannes A. Pikoulas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.16. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","Sarah Pomeroy, review of <i>Sparta: Beyond the Mirage</i>, ed. Anton Powell and Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.08.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.08.06. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Spartan_Territory_Before_371_BC.png","title":"perioikoi","desc":"Map of Sparta during the Classical era, before the Battle of Leuctra (371 BCE). The blue dots show the location of perioikoi cities. Sources: - D. Graham J. Shipley, \"Sparta and its perioikic neighbours: a century of reassessment\", Hermathena, 181, 2006, pp 51–82. T8612, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spartan_Territory_Before_371_BC.png","alt":"Spartan Territory Before 371 BCE"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-241","num":241,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Full standing among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rested on monthly contributions of produce to the common messes, and a man who could not make them dropped into the <i>hypomeiones</i>.","questions":["Aristotle has the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fall from 10,000 in the 5th century to fewer than 1,000 in the 4th, though the territory could ostensibly have supported 30,000 infantry.","Land in Sparta could be alienated and passed with daughters inheriting alongside sons, so the equality of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was ideology rather than fact in a plutocratic society."],"answer":"Spartiates","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartiates</b> are the citizens of Sparta itself, and the word is used narrowly of those who enjoyed full political rights, the <i>homoioi</i> or 'equals'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Used broadly it takes in anyone who belonged to the Spartan <i>polis</i> by birth, whatever rights he held: Xenophon calls the mercenary Drakontios a Spartiate although, exiled as a boy and never through the Spartan upbringing, he can hardly have been one of the equals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Full standing rested on the monthly contributions of produce to the common messes, and a man who could not make them dropped into the <i>hypomeiones</i>, the 'lesser men'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cowardice in battle and failure to pass through the upbringing were possibly other ways down, though the word itself is attested exactly once, in Xenophon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the rights amounted to was a voice: those in the assembly could speak and vote on what the elders put before them, and the final decision lay with the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Aristotle has them fall from 10,000 in the 5th century to fewer than 1,000 in the 4th, though the territory could ostensibly have supported 30,000 infantry and 1,500 cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He blamed whatever fragmented the landed estates, singling out widespread property ownership by women and policies that encouraged large families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The equality was ideology rather than fact, since land could be alienated and passed by partible inheritance with daughters inheriting alongside sons, and classical Sparta was a plutocratic society whose inequality of wealth undermined the very ideal it lived by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The shortage told on the battlefield, and the Spartans drew back from a suicidal ideal once they saw they could not be profligate with the lives of soldiers they could not replace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even the kings were not strictly <i>homoioi</i>, calling themselves Achaeans rather than Dorians and standing outside the upbringing, and the myth of Sparta is slowly crumbling into a picture more fragmentary and complex than the old one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartiates","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","politics"],"sources":["Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Property and Wealth in Classical Sparta</i>, by Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.07.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.07.26/. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Sarah Pomeroy, review of <i>Sparta: Beyond the Mirage</i>, ed. Anton Powell and Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.08.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.08.06. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians%2C_excavation.jpg/1920px-Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians%2C_excavation.jpg","title":"Spartiates","desc":"Front des Lakedaimoniergrabes bei der Ausgrabung durch das Deutsche Archäologische Institut 1914/1915, Grabungsfoto. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians,_excavation.jpg","alt":"Tomb of the Lacedaemonians, excavation"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-242","num":242,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plato's <i>Laws</i> gives the first mention of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, listing it among the things that instilled endurance, the men going barefoot in winter and roving the countryside by night and by day.","questions":["Aristotle, preserved by Plutarch, has the magistrates send the most discreet young men out with daggers in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, to hide by day and by night kill every helot they caught.","Modern readings divide the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> three ways, as an initiation rite, as a policing service and as a military unit, and no consensus has been reached on what its name conceals."],"answer":"krypteia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>krypteia</b> is a Spartan institution whose name means 'hidden thing', and it is among those the ancient sources attribute to the lawgiver Lycurgus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word appears in no literature before the 4th century BCE, its first mention coming in Plato's <i>Laws</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There a Spartan speaker lists it among the things a lawgiver set up for war and for instilling <i>karteresis</i>, or 'endurance': the men go barefoot in winter, sleep without coverlets, keep no attendants and rove the whole countryside by night and by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of killing he says nothing at all, and he does not mention the helots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle, preserved by Plutarch, has the magistrates send out the most discreet of the young men with daggers and such supplies as were necessary, to hide by day and by night come down into the highways and kill every helot they caught, and sometimes to cross the fields and slay the sturdiest and best of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch adds that the ephors declared war on the helots each year so that killing them carried no impiety, and then refuses to believe Lycurgus capable of so abominable a measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern readings divide it three ways, as an initiation rite, as a policing service and as a military unit, and calling it a secret police imports an image of modern agencies the evidence will not carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even what was hidden is unsettled: the men themselves, or what they were doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its victims are the helots, whose status, treatment and revolt are the three points current scholarship turns on, and Sparta had unrest inside its own citizen body too: the one attested mention of its 'lesser men' comes in Xenophon's account of a conspiracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind all of it lies the evidence: Plato was an Athenian writing a legislative project rather than a description of Sparta, and what was credited to Lycurgus went on shifting for centuries after he was supposed to have lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"krypteia","tags":["practice","institution","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"sources":["Julie Rayanne Newman, “Krypteia and Xenelasia: Reassessing the Notion of Spartan Secrecy” (MA thesis, University of Calgary, 2021), https://prism.ucalgary.ca/bitstreams/f878c7ac-5275-42d4-85e9-d11e85a26046/download. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-243","num":243,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch presents the Great Rhetra as a Delphic oracle received by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Diodorus has the Pythia deliver it to him directly.","questions":["Where other lawgivers got a hero cult at home, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was honoured as a hero or rather as a god, and like a colony's founder he received oracles from Delphi.","Aristotle said outright that King Theopompus added the ephorate to the constitution he had inherited, so the ancients did not credit <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with quite everything."],"answer":"Lycurgus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tradition from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lycurgus</b> is the lawgiver to whom the Spartans credited their constitution and their whole way of living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where other lawgivers received a hero cult in their own cities, he was honoured as a hero or rather as a god, and like the founder of a colony he received oracles from Delphi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch presents the Great Rhetra as one of those oracles, received by Lycurgus, and Diodorus has the Pythia deliver it to him directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything Spartan was put down to him, down to the number of the elders: Plutarch cites Aristotle that thirty of the leading men set out with Lycurgus and two of them lost their nerve and withdrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even the krypteia is his on Aristotle's authority, which Plutarch reports and then refuses to believe, judging Lycurgus by the mildness and justice he shows everywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> His date was pushed back into the age of the Heracleidae, a placing that makes sense as a founding story rather than as a chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ancients did not credit him with quite everything: Plato made the ephorate a third stage under a third saver, and Aristotle said outright that King Theopompus added it to the constitution he had inherited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern work sets the biography aside as unrecoverable and asks instead what the institutions attributed to him can be dated to, reading the constitution as a development over time rather than one man's single reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tradition is not a late invention, though: an independent and contemporaneous 7th-century tradition already ascribed the Rhetra's institutions to a Lycurgus, and those institutions preserve details of real change by the archaic period at the latest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What moved over the centuries was the man himself, whose character, laws and customs were redefined by additions and subtractions, so that reading him means reading the problems of the ancient material as much as the material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lycurgus","tags":["person","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]","Julie Rayanne Newman, “Krypteia and Xenelasia: Reassessing the Notion of Spartan Secrecy” (MA thesis, University of Calgary, 2021), https://prism.ucalgary.ca/bitstreams/f878c7ac-5275-42d4-85e9-d11e85a26046/download. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Statue_of_Lycurgus_of_Sparta%2C_at_the_Law_Courts_of_Brussels%2C_December_30%2C_2013.jpg/1920px-Statue_of_Lycurgus_of_Sparta%2C_at_the_Law_Courts_of_Brussels%2C_December_30%2C_2013.jpg","title":"Lycurgus","desc":"Statue of Lycurgus, Lawgiver of Sparta, at the Law Courts of Brussels, Belgium on December 30, 2013. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Lycurgus_of_Sparta,_at_the_Law_Courts_of_Brussels,_December_30,_2013.jpg","alt":"Statue of Lycurgus of Sparta, at the Law Courts of Brussels, December 30, 2013"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-244","num":244,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A shrine to Zeus Syllanius and a council of thirty including the kings are ordered by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which leaves the main decision and power to the <i>damos</i>.","questions":["Plutarch takes the rider to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a later addition by the kings Polydorus and Theopompus, though it is probably part of the document from the start.","Dates proposed for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run across most of a century, from 699 to 675 BCE at one end to 650 to 600 at the other."],"answer":"Great Rhetra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proposed dates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">699 – 675 or 650 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Great Rhetra</b> is one of the earliest constitutional documents of archaic Greece, a charter of the Spartan state sanctioning its cults, the divisions of its citizens, its governing bodies and how it made decisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A <i>rhetra</i> is the Spartan word for an enactment or decree, and Sparta largely did without written legislation, so even this one may not have been written down before the late archaic period or the classical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It survives through Plutarch's <i>Life of Lycurgus</i> and through Diodorus, who quotes the verses Tyrtaeus made of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It orders a shrine to Zeus Syllanius and Athena Syllania, the division of the people into <i>phylai</i> and <i>obai</i>, and a council of thirty including the <i>archagetai</i>, by which the kings are meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The assembly is then to be called from time to time between Babyca and Cnacion, proposals put there and withdrawn, and the main decision and power given to the <i>damos</i>, the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two provisions pull against that supremacy, the first being the council's power to introduce proposals and withdraw them, which sets the agenda before the people ever meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second is the rider: if the <i>damos</i> chose crookedly the elders and the kings could annul it, a clause Plutarch takes for a later addition by the kings Polydorus and Theopompus but which is probably original.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle rules out the assembly ever having held a right to amend what was put to it, that being a mark of an advanced democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its date is argued across most of a century, from 699 to 675 BCE at one end to 650 to 600 at the other, and the setting with it: after the first war with Messenia, during the second, or after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On one reading it handed the hoplites the political power they were pressing for and so kept Sparta from tyranny; on another it belongs to a constitution that grew rather than one that was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Great Rhetra","tags":["text","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/SpartaGreatRhetra.png","title":"Great Rhetra","desc":"Spartan Great Rhetra. Publius97, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SpartaGreatRhetra.png","alt":"SpartaGreatRhetra"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-245","num":245,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Two hereditary kings ruled at once under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one from each royal house and constitutionally equal in authority, so that each was a check upon the other's power.","questions":["The kings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were not strictly among the <i>homoioi</i>, identifying themselves as Achaeans rather than Dorians and not passing through the Spartan upbringing.","Under the Great Rhetra the two holders of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sat in the council of thirty as ordinary members, and Aristotle limited their importance to leading the army on campaign."],"answer":"Spartan dual kingship","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Spartan dual kingship</b> gave the state two hereditary kings at once, one from each of two royal families, constitutionally equal in authority so that each was a check upon the other's power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One house was traditionally the senior on Herodotus's telling, though that seniority carried no constitutional weight and neither king outranked the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Great Rhetra the kings sat in the council of thirty as two of its members, included with no special privileges, so that the charter of the state diminished their constitutional power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle limited their importance to the leadership of the army on campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they were not was ordinary Spartans: strictly speaking they were not among the <i>homoioi</i>, they identified themselves as Achaeans rather than Dorians, and they did not pass through the Spartan upbringing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They had special ties with the perioikoi, the free people of the outlying towns, who farmed on the kings' behalf as well as on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plato made the double kingship the work of a god, setting it apart from the council, which he gave to a human being blended with divine power, and from the ephorate later still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A recent account calls the institution a divine dyarchy and traces it through Spartan history, its religious side bound up with the hierarchical structure of the society it stood at the head of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two kings meant two claims and two followings: Herodotus has the exiled king Demaratos advising Xerxes, and Cleomenes secured the accession of the second Laotychidas in 491 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two lines also carry the chronology of early Sparta, since dating it means counting reigns in the two kinglists, which leads at best to vague results and is the bane of dating by generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan dual kingship","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-246","num":246,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Tradition has the twin sons of Aristodemus, Eurysthenes and Procles, becoming Sparta's first co-rulers, though the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> take their names from Agis and Eurypon instead.","questions":["Both of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> claimed descent from Heracles through Hyllus, who had been adopted by Aegimius, the eponym of the Dorians.","Dating early Sparta means counting reigns in the lists of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Pausanias's genealogy knew only one Laotychidas where the line in fact held two."],"answer":"Agiads and Eurypontids","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Agiads and Eurypontids</b> are the two royal houses of Sparta, each supplying one of the two kings who ruled together, so that the succession ran down two lines at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tradition has Aristodemus, the grandson of Heracles' son Hyllus, conquering Laconia and dying, after which his twin sons Eurysthenes and Procles became Sparta's first co-rulers and the double kingship began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The houses are not named for those twins, though, but for Agis and Eurypon, mythical kings standing further down each line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of the two the Agiads were traditionally the senior on Herodotus's telling, a precedence that carried no constitutional weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both lines claimed the same ancestry, and Hyllus himself had been adopted by Aegimius, the eponym of the Dorians, which tied the Heraclid claim to the Dorian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The two lists are how early Sparta gets dated at all: Theopompus stands four reigns above the first Laotychidas in the Eurypontid line, and the Agiad line yields similar results.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Antiquity got the lists wrong as well, since Pausanias's genealogy of the Spartan kings knew only one Laotychidas where the Eurypontid line held two, four generations apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the lists preserve is argued: the institutions credited to the tradition seem to keep details of real change by the archaic period, even where the biographies attached to them cannot be recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The claim underneath was a construction rather than a record, and it bought three things: hereditary right to the kingship, the priestly functions that went with it, and a title to dominion beyond Sparta's borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both houses also set themselves a little apart from the people they ruled, calling themselves Achaeans rather than Dorians and standing outside the upbringing every other Spartan went through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Agiads and Eurypontids","tags":["people","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Alexey L. Darvin, “Origin of Spartan Kings from Heracles,” <i>RUDN Journal of World History</i> 10, no. 3 (2018): 237–49, https://doi.org/10.22363/2312-8127-2018-10-3-237-249. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/L%C3%A9onidas_aux_Thermopyles_%28Jacques-Louis_David%29.PNG","title":"Agiads and Eurypontids","desc":"Léonidas aux Thermopyles (Jacques-Louis David). Jacques-Louis David. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9onidas_aux_Thermopyles_(Jacques-Louis_David).PNG","alt":"Léonidas aux Thermopyles (Jacques-Louis David)"},"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-247","num":247,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Sparta's council of elders, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was thirty strong, 28 <i>gerontes</i> with the two kings making up the number, and a man had to be past sixty to be chosen.","questions":["Candidates for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed through the assembly one by one in silence while judges shut in a nearby room marked each shout by its loudness without knowing whose it was.","Aristotle called the way men were elected to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> childish, and objected as well that a candidate had to put himself forward, a rule fitter for the ambitious than the virtuous."],"answer":"gerousia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>gerousia</b> is Sparta's council of elders, thirty strong, 28 <i>gerontes</i> with the two kings making up the number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A <i>geron</i> had to be past sixty when he was chosen, and he held the office for the rest of his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the Great Rhetra a council of powerful aristocrats had advised the kings by custom rather than by institution, and the Rhetra put it on a new basis, integrated with the kings and answering the aspirations of the leading families, which is real archaic change whatever is made of the lawgiver credited with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its power was to prepare: it introduced proposals and withdrew them, so that the assembly could approve what the council put to it but could promote no law of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was a court as well, the only Spartan one that could pronounce death or exile, and it heard the serious cases, homicide among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Election was by shouting: the people were called together, judges shut in a room where they could neither see nor be seen but could hear, and the candidates passed through one by one in an order drawn by lot, in silence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The judges marked each shout by its loudness without knowing whose it was, only that it came first, second, third, and the man with the most and loudest acclamations was declared elected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle called the procedure childish, and objected as well that a man had to put himself forward, a rule fitter for the ambitious than for the virtuous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has since been read as designed for impartiality rather than as primitive, the lot securing spontaneity and the sealed judges anonymity, and as an early form of approval voting, since a voter could acclaim as many candidates as he liked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who could stand was another matter, since it was probably only aristocrats, and rich Spartiates used their wealth to build patronal relationships that reached into the nominally equal dealings of the <i>homoioi</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"gerousia","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Charles Girard, “Acclamation Voting in Sparta: An Early Use of Approval Voting,” in <i>Handbook on Approval Voting</i>, ed. Jean-François Laslier and M. Remzi Sanver (Heidelberg: Springer, 2010), 15–18, https://hal.science/hal-03005701v1/document. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Property and Wealth in Classical Sparta</i>, by Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.07.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.07.26/. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-248","num":248,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xenophon says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could fine whom they chose and enact immediate payment, deprive magistrates of office, imprison them and prefer a capital charge against them.","questions":["Aristotle said outright that King Theopompus added the office of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the constitution he had inherited, and from the middle of the 4th century that view prevailed.","It was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who dealt with Kinadon's conspiracy, choosing not to arrest him inside Sparta and hand-picking six or seven of the <i>hippeis</i> to take him."],"answer":"ephors","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>ephors</b> are the magistrates who oversaw the Spartan state, and the sources put the beginning of the office at very different points in the city's history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plato made it a third stage, after the double kingship given by a god and the council given by a human being blended with divine power, and ascribed it to a third saver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle said outright that King Theopompus added the ephorate to the constitution he had inherited, and from the middle of the 4th century that view prevailed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One story has them appointed to run the state while the kings were away at war, and Tyrtaeus, who gives a fair summary of the constitution of his own day, knows nothing of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A modern account puts the office later still, in the second half of the 6th century BCE and out of the needs of Sparta's league, with the social origin of the ephors in well-placed aristocratic families rather than in the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon has the powerful citizens setting the office up themselves, having concluded that obedience is a very great blessing whether in a state, an army or a household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they got was power: the ephors could fine whom they chose and enact immediate payment, deprive magistrates of office, imprison them and prefer a capital charge against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They did not leave men elected to office to rule as they liked through the year, but punished a lawbreaker the moment they saw one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They could set business before the assembly as well, bringing a motion without the council having to agree among itself, so that the preparing of business was divided between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The executive work was theirs too: it was the ephors who dealt with Kinadon's conspiracy, choosing not to arrest him inside Sparta and hand-picking six or seven of the <i>hippeis</i> to take him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"ephors","tags":["institution","people","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>Constitution of the Lacedaemonians</i> 8.3–4, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, trans. E. C. Marchant and G. W. Bowersock (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0210:text=Const.%20Lac.:chapter=8. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Alberto Esu, “Divided Power and Εὐνομία: Deliberative Procedures in Ancient Sparta,” <i>The Classical Quarterly</i> 67, no. 2 (2017): 353–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0009838817000544. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Sparta_Ephoren_%28WMH_11-1861-62_S_48_LLoeffler%29.jpg","title":"ephors","desc":"Die fünf Ephoren in Sparta. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sparta_Ephoren_(WMH_11-1861-62_S_48_LLoeffler).jpg","alt":"Sparta Ephoren (WMH 11-1861-62 S 48 LLoeffler)"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-249","num":249,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch says the verb <i>apellazein</i> means to assemble the people, with a reference to Apollo, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met between Babyca and Cnacion with neither halls nor any other building.","questions":["When the multitude of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was assembled no one of them was permitted to make a motion, and what the elders and kings laid before them could only be accepted or rejected.","Even the name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is doubtful, since no ancient writer but Plutarch uses it, and the word goes back to Apollo, Apellon in the Doric dialect."],"answer":"apella","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>apella</b> is the assembly of the Spartan citizens, and even the name is doubtful, since no ancient writer but Plutarch uses it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took it in the plural, and the word goes back to Apollo, Apellon in the Doric dialect, whom Plutarch calls the source and author of the polity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Great Rhetra's verb <i>apellazein</i> means to assemble the people, and they assembled between Babyca and Cnacion, a bridge and a river on Aristotle's reading of the names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They met in the open, with neither halls nor any other kind of building for the purpose, because Lycurgus thought good counsel was not promoted by such things but discouraged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The serious purposes of an assembly were rendered foolish and futile, on that account, by men gazing at statues and paintings, scenic embellishments and the extravagantly decorated roofs of council halls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How often it met is not recoverable: the Rhetra says only from time to time, a phrase read as from season to season and as once a year, a period or a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its principal innovation was to be institutionalised at all, since its meetings no longer depended on the will of the kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the multitude was assembled no one of them was permitted to make a motion, and what the elders and the kings laid before them could be accepted or rejected and nothing more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One reading has it enacting or rejecting what the council and the ephors put to it without deliberating on anything left open, so that it could promote no law of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It decided by shouting, as it decided most affairs of moment, which is how a body that could put nothing forward still had the last word on what was put to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"apella","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 6, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0047:chapter=6. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","Charles Girard, “Acclamation Voting in Sparta: An Early Use of Approval Voting,” in <i>Handbook on Approval Voting</i>, ed. Jean-François Laslier and M. Remzi Sanver (Heidelberg: Springer, 2010), 15–18, https://hal.science/hal-03005701v1/document. [Open access]","Alberto Esu, “Divided Power and Εὐνομία: Deliberative Procedures in Ancient Sparta,” <i>The Classical Quarterly</i> 67, no. 2 (2017): 353–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0009838817000544. [Paywalled]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-250","num":250,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Instead of leaving each father to appoint a slave as tutor, Lycurgus gave the boys of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a Warden with a staff of youths carrying whips.","questions":["Plutarch has boys taken by the state at seven and enrolled in the companies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the one best in judgement and bravest in fighting was made captain.","Over each company of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood an eiren, who after supper asked who was the best man in the city and punished a faulty answer with a bite on the thumb."],"answer":"agoge","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>agoge</b> is the public upbringing that took a Spartan boy out of his father's hands and put him in the state's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon says that instead of leaving each father to appoint a slave as tutor, Lycurgus gave charge of the boys to a member of the class from which the highest offices are filled, and gave him a staff of youths carrying whips.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instead of softening their feet with sandals the boys went unshod, and instead of changes of clothing they wore one garment through the year, to be readier for heat and cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their food was kept moderate enough that they would never suffer repletion and would know what it was to go with their hunger unsatisfied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were allowed to relieve that hunger by stealing, and a boy who was caught was beaten with many stripes, since men punish a learner for not carrying out properly what he is taught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch has them taken by the state at seven and enrolled in companies, where the one who excelled in judgement and was most courageous in fighting was made captain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of reading and writing they learned only enough to serve their turn, and all the rest was calculated to make them obey commands well, endure hardships and conquer in battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over each company stood an eiren, two years out of the class of boys, who after supper asked who was the best man in the city and punished a faulty answer with a bite on the thumb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says he had seen youths expiring under the lash at the altar of Artemis Orthia, where a priestess stood holding a wooden image that grew heavy if the scourgers spared a lad for his beauty or rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word itself belongs to the Hellenistic and Roman periods and is not used before the middle of the 3rd century BCE, so how far what he describes continued the classical system is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"agoge","tags":["practice","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>Constitution of the Lacedaemonians</i> 2, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, trans. E. C. Marchant and G. W. Bowersock (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0210:text=Const.%20Lac.:chapter=2. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 16–18, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0047:chapter=16. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.16.10–11, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=3:chapter=16. [Open access]","Edmond Lévy, “Remarques préliminaires sur l'éducation spartiate,” <i>Ktèma</i> 22 (1997): 151–60, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ktema_0221-5896_1997_num_22_1_2189. [Open access]","Antony Keen, review of <i>The Gymnasium of Virtue: Education and Culture in Ancient Sparta</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 96.9.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.09.28. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"wh-110","num":110,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"More than 700 natural shelters weathered out of the Vindhyan hills of central India make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, over 400 of them painted and their floors full of stone tools.","questions":["A single shelter among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can carry five layers of painting one over another, and 16 layers have been told apart across the whole site.","The last painting done in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a conch-shell script of the 6th and 7th centuries, which always overlies the older pictures and is never overlain by them."],"answer":"Bhimbetka rock shelters","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100,000 years ago onwards</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mesolithic to c. 7th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shelters</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 700, over 400 painted</span></div>","answerText":"Bhimbetka rock shelters","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Bhimbetka rock shelters</b> are hollows and caves weathered out of the Vindhyan hills of Madhya Pradesh, in central India, about 45 kilometres (28 miles) south-east of Bhopal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They run along a stretch some 10 kilometres (6 miles) long and 3 wide, and number more than 700, of which over 400 carry paintings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rock is sandstone, weathering easily under heat, rain and wind, and several hundred million years of that opened shelters from small niches to large caves, in forest fed by perennial springs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their floors hold stone tools from the Lower Palaeolithic on: handaxes and cleavers, then smaller Middle Palaeolithic scrapers, then Upper Palaeolithic blades and burins, and finally a Mesolithic industry that drops quartzite for chalcedony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One shelter's deposit runs nearly four metres (13 feet) deep through all four of those stages, and the site's Acheulean bifaces are unusually refined, its cleavers strikingly symmetrical in section.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> At one end of the Auditorium shelter cup-shaped depressions have been hammered into the rock, some beneath Acheulean levels and dated to nearly 100,000 years, which would make them among the earliest anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of the paintings are red or white, the colour taken from haematite nodules that turn up in the habitation deposits, many with facets ground smooth where pigment was worked off them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Green, red and white colouring stones came out of the Upper Palaeolithic and Mesolithic layers and green out of none later, which is why green figures are read as the oldest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later painters worked over earlier work, so one shelter may show five layers and 16 have been told apart across the site, the topmost a conch-shell script of the 6th and 7th centuries that nothing overlies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wild animals dominate the older pictures, the ceiling of Zoo Rock crowded with them, alongside hunts, dances with drummers and masked men, while the latest phases turn instead to horse and elephant riders and battle scenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Archaeological Survey of India, “Prehistoric Rock Shelters of Bhimbetka (2003), Madhya Pradesh,” accessed August 10, 2026, https://asi.nic.in/pages/WorldHeritageBhimbetka. [Open access]","V. N. Misra, “The Prehistoric Rock Art of Bhimbetka, Central India,” <i>NCPA Quarterly Journal</i> 10, no. 1 (1981), https://www.sahapedia.org/sites/default/files/The%20Prehistoric%20Rock%20Art%20of%20Bhimbetka,%20Central%20India.pdf. [Open access]","Meenakshi Dubey-Pathak, “The Rock Art of the Bhimbetka Area in India,” <i>Adoranten</i> (2014): 5–22, https://www.rockartscandinavia.com/images/articles/a14pathak.pdf. [Open access]","Meenakshi Dubey Pathak, “Green Images in Indian Rock Art,” <i>Arts</i> 14, no. 2 (2025): 36, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts14020036. [Open access]","Ceri Shipton, “Hierarchical Organization in the Acheulean to Middle Palaeolithic Transition at Bhimbetka, India,” <i>Cambridge Archaeological Journal</i> 26, no. 4 (2016): 601–18, https://doi.org/10.1017/S095977431600041X. [Paywalled]"],"tags":["place","art","archaeology","prehistory","india"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Rock_Shelter_8%2C_Bhimbetka_02.jpg/1920px-Rock_Shelter_8%2C_Bhimbetka_02.jpg","title":"Bhimbetka rock shelters","desc":"Paintings in Rock Shelter 8, Bhimbetka, India. Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_Shelter_8,_Bhimbetka_02.jpg","alt":"Rock Shelter 8, Bhimbetka"},"locator":{"name":"Bhimbetka rock shelters","at":[77.6131,22.9384]}},
{"id":"wh-121","num":121,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Grey wolves became the first domestic animal well before any crop was sown, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the only one to predate farming altogether.","questions":["Canids from Goyet, Předmostí and Razboinichya Cave were all published as evidence of early <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until nuclear genomes showed each of them to be a now-extinct wolf.","By 14,300 years ago one genetically uniform dog population had spread across Europe and Anatolia, so whatever <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as, the animals were soon passing between unrelated peoples."],"answer":"dog domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest dogs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 17,000 – 14,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest dog DNA</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,200 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lineages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">five by c. 11,000 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"dog domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Dog domestication</b> made grey wolves into the first domestic animal, and the only one taken up before farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it happened, when, and which people were involved are all still unknown, and published estimates of the date have ranged from 135,000 to 15,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest bones that look like a dog's are European, from 17,000 to 14,000 years ago, but younger candidates turn up in East Asia, the Levant and the Americas, so the archaeology points to no one region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bone shape alone has misled repeatedly: canids from Goyet, Předmostí, Razboinichya Cave and Eliseevichi were each published as an early dog, and nuclear genomes have since shown every one of them to be a now-extinct wolf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The clearest early case is a jaw of about 14,300 years ago from Bonn-Oberkassel in Germany, buried with two people and carrying damage the animal could not have survived without long human care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest dog DNA yet recovered is from a 14,200-year-old animal at Kesslerloch in Switzerland, and it already shares ancestry with dogs worldwide, so Europe's Palaeolithic dogs were not a separate line that failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One genetically uniform dog population was spread across Europe and Anatolia by then, among Magdalenian, Epigravettian and Anatolian hunter-gatherers alike, so the animals were passing between human groups quite unlike one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 11,000 years ago at least five major lineages had diverged, and all dogs share an ancestry distinct from every living wolf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient genomes from 72 wolves spanning 100,000 years put the main source in eastern Eurasia, while dogs in the Near East and Africa draw up to half their ancestry from a second, southwest Eurasian source that no sampled wolf matches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gene flow afterwards ran mostly the other way, from dogs into wolves, though almost two-thirds of breed dogs carry a low but detectable wolf ancestry acquired roughly a thousand generations ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Anders Bergström et al., “Genomic History of Early Dogs in Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 651, no. 8107 (2026): 986–94, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10112-7. [Open access]","William A. Marsh et al., “Dogs Were Widely Distributed across Western Eurasia during the Palaeolithic,” <i>Nature</i> 651, no. 8107 (2026): 995–1003, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10170-x. [Open access]","Anders Bergström et al., “Grey Wolf Genomic History Reveals a Dual Ancestry of Dogs,” <i>Nature</i> 607, no. 7918 (2022): 313–20, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-04824-9. [Open access]","Anders Bergström et al., “Origins and Genetic Legacy of Prehistoric Dogs,” <i>Science</i> 370, no. 6516 (2020): 557–64, https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/168236/1/aba9572_revision2_1_.pdf. [Open access]","Audrey T. Lin et al., “A Legacy of Genetic Entanglement with Wolves Shapes Modern Dogs,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 48 (2025): e2421768122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2421768122. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","evolution","genetics","archaeology","nature"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Hund_Doppelgrab_Oberkassel.jpg/1920px-Hund_Doppelgrab_Oberkassel.jpg","title":"Dog domestication","desc":"Jaw, maxilla fragment and teeth of the dog from the Bonn-Oberkassel double grave. Thilo Parg, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hund_Doppelgrab_Oberkassel.jpg","alt":"Jaw, maxilla fragment and teeth of the dog from the Bonn-Oberkassel double grave"}},
{"id":"wh-122","num":122,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Farming and settled villages were both well established in Southwest Asia before anyone made a pot, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named for.","questions":["Round houses give way to square ones part way through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a change so consistent that squared building is treated as the trademark of its second phase.","Skulls detached from the body and modelled over in plaster are a mortuary practice of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, known from Jericho, 'Ain Ghazal and other sites across the Levant."],"answer":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">PPNA</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 11,700 – 10,500 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">PPNB</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,500 – 8,250 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Pre-Pottery Neolithic</b> is the first part of the Neolithic in Southwest Asia, the stretch in which people took up farming and settled villages before anyone had made pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It follows the Natufian and falls into two, the PPNA of roughly 11,700 to 10,500 years ago and the PPNB of roughly 10,500 to 8,250.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two were first distinguished at Jericho, and what separates them is a change in stone tools, in house building and in how people fed themselves, the PPNB being marked by its naviform blade cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no clear evidence of domestic animals in the PPNA, while sheep and goats are plainly domesticated in the levels above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its people stayed put: of 44 individuals sampled at Jericho, only two had grown up anywhere else, which is a settled community rather than one built by migration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The most visible change is architectural, the round buildings of the Natufian and the PPNA giving way to square ones, which is the trademark of the PPNB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was not abrupt, since at Jerf el Ahmar round and square houses stand in the same levels and some are square with rounded corners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later PPNB settlements swelled into mega-sites, mobility fell as people leaned harder on domesticated food, and obsidian and other exotic materials travelled long distances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A mortuary practice runs through the PPNB in which skulls were detached from the body, modelled over in plaster and kept, known from Jericho, Tell Ramad, Beisamoun, Kfar HaHoresh, Tell Aswad, 'Ain Ghazal and Nahal Hemar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its reach ran well past the Fertile Crescent, since tools of the kinds that define the Natufian and the Pre-Pottery Neolithic turn up at Sahout in the northern Arabian desert between 13,500 and 8,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Ceri Shipton et al., “The Natufian Epipalaeolithic and Pre-Pottery Neolithic in the Desert of Northern Arabia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 16842, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-40541-3. [Open access]","Andrea L. Balbo et al., “Squaring the Circle: Social and Environmental Implications of Pre-Pottery Neolithic Building Technology at Tell Qarassa (South Syria),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 7 (2012): e42109, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0042109. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Plastered Skulls from the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B Site of Yiftahel (Israel): A Computed Tomography-Based Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 2 (2014): e89242, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0089242. [Open access]","Xiaoran Wang et al., “Isotopic and Proteomic Evidence for Communal Stability at Pre-Pottery Neolithic Jericho in the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 16360, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-43549-1. [Open access]","Xiaoran Wang et al., “Isotopic and DNA Analyses Reveal Multiscale PPNB Mobility and Migration across Southeastern Anatolia and the Southern Levant,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 4 (2023): e2210611120, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2210611120. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","agriculture","practice","history"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Plastered_skull._Such_skulls_were_buried_in_houses_and_probably_represent_ancestral_cults._Pre-Pottery_Neolithic_B%2C_8800-6900_BCE._From_Jericho%2C_in_modern-day_Palestinian_Territories._The_Jordan_Museum%2C_Amman.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic","desc":"Plastered skull. Such skulls were buried in houses and probably represent ancestral cults. Pre-Pottery Neolithic B, 8800-6900 BCE. From Jericho, in modern-day Palestinian Territories. The Jordan Museum, Amman, Jordan. Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plastered_skull._Such_skulls_were_buried_in_houses_and_probably_represent_ancestral_cults._Pre-Pottery_Neolithic_B,_8800-6900_BCE._From_Jericho,_in_modern-day_Palestinian_Territories._The_Jordan_Museum,_Amman.jpg","alt":"Plastered skull from Jericho, Jordan Museum"}},
{"id":"wh-123","num":123,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Rings of T-shaped limestone pillars carved with foxes, boars and cranes were raised at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 10th millennium BCE, when nearly everyone alive still hunted and gathered.","questions":["More than 7,000 grinding slabs, handstones and mortars have now been analysed at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is hard to square with the old view that nobody lived there.","The two central pillars of Enclosure D at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stand 5.5 metres (18 feet) tall and wear carved belts with fox-pelt loincloths, so the T is a human figure."],"answer":"Göbekli Tepe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9600 – 8800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Later phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th millennium BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Göbekli Tepe","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Göbekli Tepe</b> is a Pre-Pottery Neolithic mound on a bare limestone ridge in south-eastern Turkey, one of a cluster of sites around Şanlıurfa where T-shaped stone pillars have been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sits about 15 kilometres (9 miles) north-east of that city, its deposits up to 15 metres (49 feet) deep over some nine hectares (22 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest Neolithic horizon dates to roughly 9600 to 8800 BCE, and to it belong all the major circular buildings, from 10 to 30 metres (33 to 98 feet) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each is ringed by ten or twelve T-shaped limestone pillars set into walls of quarry stone, with a taller pair at the centre, and the two in Enclosure D reach 5.5 metres (18 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The T is read as a human head, since several pillars carry arms and hands in relief and the central pair in Enclosure D wear belts with buckles and a loincloth of fox pelts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Reliefs across the pillars show foxes, boars, bulls, cranes, ducks, gazelles, snakes, spiders and scorpions, all of them wild and, where they can be sexed, male.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those images were not fixed once carved, since pillars in Building F were erased and re-worked repeatedly, several losing more than 10 centimetres (4 inches) of surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The excavator found no residential buildings and read the place as a group of sanctuaries where scattered communities gathered for rites, a reading that has since been qualified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavation has turned up smaller rectangular buildings raised around the monumental ones, partly at the same time and partly afterwards, and more than 7,000 grinding slabs, handstones, mortars and pestles, a concentration unusual for the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seasonal peaks in that food processing are read as large work feasts, and how many people could have been permanently present is limited by water, since no spring is known nearby and the cisterns found so far hold 153 cubic metres (5,400 cubic feet) between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Laura Dietrich et al., “Cereal Processing at Early Neolithic Göbekli Tepe, Southeastern Turkey,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 5 (2019): e0215214, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0215214. [Open access]","Klaus Schmidt, “Göbekli Tepe – the Stone Age Sanctuaries: New Results of Ongoing Excavations with a Special Focus on Sculptures and High Reliefs,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 37 (2010): 239–56, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.37.21. [Open access]","Christopher Claudio Caletti, “Göbekli Tepe and the Sites around the Urfa Plain (SE Turkey): Recent Discoveries and New Interpretations,” <i>Asia Anteriore Antica</i> 2 (2020): 95–123, https://doi.org/10.13128/asiana-679. [Open access]","Oliver Dietrich and Julia Wagner, “Early Neolithic Imagery in Flux: A Case Study on the Reliefs of Building F at Göbekli Tepe,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 50 (2023): 222–36, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.50.11. [Open access]","Bahattin Çelik, “New Neolithic Cult Centres and Domestic Settlements in the Light of Urfa Region Surveys,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 42 (2015): 353–64, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.42.25. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","religion","turkey","history"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe%2C_Urfa.jpg/1920px-G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe%2C_Urfa.jpg","title":"Göbekli Tepe","desc":"Excavated circular enclosures ringed with T-shaped stone pillars on the mound at Göbekli Tepe. Teomancimit, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe,_Urfa.jpg","alt":"Excavated circular enclosures ringed with T-shaped stone pillars on the mound at Göbekli Tepe"},"locator":{"name":"Göbekli Tepe","at":[38.9217,37.2236]}},
{"id":"wh-124","num":124,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"A stone tower over 8 metres (26 feet) high with a staircase built inside it was raised at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 8300 BCE, beside a wall about half its height.","questions":["Strontium in the teeth of 44 Neolithic dead from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found only two who had grown up elsewhere, which is a community that stayed put rather than one built by migration.","Ten skulls with faces modelled over them in plaster and shells set in for eyes have come out of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where occupation runs from the Natufian to the Ottomans."],"answer":"Jericho","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 10,500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tower</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze Age fall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1520 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Jericho","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Jericho</b> is the settlement at Tell es-Sultan in the rift valley north of the Dead Sea, and it records more than 10,000 years of occupation, from the Late Natufian to the Ottoman period, with no clear break in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Pre-Pottery Neolithic levels are among the best known anywhere, and the terms PPNA and PPNB were themselves first defined from its stratigraphy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What sets it apart from other sites of its day in the southern Levant is a stone tower over 8 metres (26 feet) high and a wall beside it, both raised around 8300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tower is conical, built of undressed stone, plastered inside and out, and holds a staircase within it, the wall standing about half its height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been read as a fortification, as flood defence, as a ritual centre and as a claim on territory, and none of those readings has settled the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Strontium in the teeth of 44 of its Neolithic dead identifies only two who grew up elsewhere, so the place was held by a community that stayed put rather than one built by incomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the earlier phase its people buried skulls apart from their bodies, grouped together and unadorned, and in the later one they began modelling faces over them in plaster, with shells set in for eyes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ten plastered skulls are known from the site in all, three of them lifted from a single square on the western side of the mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The town went on being rebuilt for thousands of years afterwards, and the walled Bronze Age city was burnt at the end of the Middle Bronze Age, radiocarbon on charred grain from that fire giving a date between about 1600 and 1520 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those measurements also rule out a destruction as late as about 1400 BCE, a date that had been proposed for the end of the fortified city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Xiaoran Wang et al., “Isotopic and Proteomic Evidence for Communal Stability at Pre-Pottery Neolithic Jericho in the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 16360, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-43549-1. [Open access]","Roy Liran and Ran Barkai, “Casting a Shadow on Neolithic Jericho,” <i>Antiquity Project Gallery</i> 85, no. 327 (2011): 1–5, https://doi.org/10.15184/S2978855200902282. [Open access]","Eugen Strouhal, “Five Plastered Skulls from Pre-Pottery Neolithic B Jericho: Anthropological Study,” <i>Paléorient</i> 1, no. 2 (1973): 231–47, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.1973.4169. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Plastered Skulls from the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B Site of Yiftahel (Israel): A Computed Tomography-Based Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 2 (2014): e89242, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0089242. [Open access]","Hendrik J. Bruins and Johannes van der Plicht, “Tell es-Sultan (Jericho): Radiocarbon Results of Short-Lived Cereal and Multiyear Charcoal Samples from the End of the Middle Bronze Age,” <i>Radiocarbon</i> 37, no. 2 (1995): 213–20, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0033822200030666. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","city","prehistory","archaeology","history","palestine"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Tower_of_Jericho.jpg/1920px-Tower_of_Jericho.jpg","title":"Jericho","desc":"The Neolithic stone tower of Jericho, a drum of undressed stone standing in an excavation trench at Tell es-Sultan. Reinhard Dietrich, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tower_of_Jericho.jpg","alt":"The Neolithic stone tower of Jericho, a drum of undressed stone standing in an excavation trench at Tell es-Sultan"},"locator":{"name":"Jericho","at":[35.46,31.8561]}},
{"id":"wh-125","num":125,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Houses at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were packed together with no streets and no doors, so people walked over their neighbours' roofs and climbed down a ladder to get in.","questions":["At least 741 people were buried beneath the floors at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and ancient genomes show that those under one house were rarely close relatives.","A wall painting from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been read as a town plan below an erupting volcano, which would make it the oldest map known."],"answer":"Çatalhöyük","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7100 – 6000 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Çatalhöyük","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Çatalhöyük</b> is a Neolithic town on the Konya Plain in central Anatolia, occupied from about 7100 to 6000 BCE and known for its size, its well-preserved mudbrick architecture and its wall paintings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its rectangular houses stand packed against one another with no streets between them and no doorways, so people crossed their neighbours' roofs and climbed down a ladder to get indoors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Estimates of how many lived there run from several hundred to perhaps a few thousand at the height of the settlement, and the figure rose and fell over the centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every building excavated shows signs of being lived in, no communal architecture or generally accessible space has been found below the topmost levels, and a study weighing many kinds of evidence at once found no sign of lasting or institutionalised inequality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Walls were plastered and painted inside, most often with red panels, geometric designs and handprints, and a minority of houses displayed complete sets of cattle horns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The dead were buried beneath the floors, and at least 741 people have been recovered from the excavated buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient genomes show that close relatives are rare among those laid under one house, so a household was not simply one biological family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pigment went into the graves as well as onto the walls, red ochre most often, with cinnabar found mainly on men and blue and green with women, and the more people a building held the more times its walls had been repainted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plant remains are dominated by wheat and barley, then lentils, peas and chickpeas, with acorns, pistachios and a wide range of wild bulbs and seasonal plants alongside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One painting has been read as a plan of the town below an erupting volcano, and a summit eruption of Hasan Dağı has been dated to about 6960 BCE, though a leopard skin over a geometric pattern remains the rival reading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Katheryn C. Twiss et al., “‘But Some Were More Equal than Others:’ Exploring Inequality at Neolithic Çatalhöyük,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 9 (2024): e0307067, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0307067. [Open access]","Reyhan Yaka et al., “Variable Kinship Patterns in Neolithic Anatolia Revealed by Ancient Genomes,” <i>Current Biology</i> 31, no. 11 (2021): 2455–68, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2021.03.050. [Open access]","Eline M. J. Schotsmans et al., “New Insights on Commemoration of the Dead through Mortuary and Architectural Use of Pigments at Neolithic Çatalhöyük, Turkey,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12, no. 1 (2022): 4055, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-07284-3. [Open access]","Axel K. Schmitt et al., “Identifying the Volcanic Eruption Depicted in a Neolithic Painting at Çatalhöyük, Central Anatolia, Turkey,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 1 (2014): e84711, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0084711. [Open access]","Carlos G. Santiago-Marrero et al., “A Microbotanical and Microwear Perspective to Plant Processing Activities and Foodways at Neolithic Çatalhöyük,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 6 (2021): e0252312, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0252312. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","city","prehistory","archaeology","history","turkey"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/%C3%87atalh%C3%B6y%C3%BCk_after_the_first_excavations_by_James_Mellaart_and_his_team..jpg","title":"Çatalhöyük","desc":"Excavated mudbrick rooms at Çatalhöyük standing wall against wall with no streets between them. Omar hoftun, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%87atalh%C3%B6y%C3%BCk_after_the_first_excavations_by_James_Mellaart_and_his_team..jpg","alt":"Excavated mudbrick rooms at Çatalhöyük standing wall against wall with no streets between them"},"locator":{"name":"Çatalhöyük","at":[32.8281,37.6667]}},
{"id":"wh-126","num":126,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Two caches of lime-plaster statues, each figure built around a bundle of reeds bound with twine, went into the ground at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beneath the floors of houses already abandoned.","questions":["By the end of the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B nearly every settlement in the central and southern Levant had been abandoned, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was still growing past 12 hectares (30 acres).","The final aceramic phase of the southern Levant, the PPNC, was defined from the deposits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which went on to be occupied again by Yarmoukian potters."],"answer":"'Ain Ghazal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At its height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8000 – 6600 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"'Ain Ghazal","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>'Ain Ghazal</b> is a Neolithic settlement beside the Zarqa river on the northern edge of Amman, in Jordan, and one of the largest known anywhere in the Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began as a modest village and grew through the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B into a mega-site of the kind that reached up to 20 hectares (49 acres) and held hundreds or even a few thousand people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its growth outlasted its neighbours: by the end of that phase nearly every known Pre-Pottery Neolithic B settlement in the central and southern Levant had been abandoned, while it went on spreading past 12 hectares (30 acres) on both banks of the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two further phases follow, a final aceramic one defined from the site itself and named the PPNC, and then a Yarmoukian occupation using pottery whose remains cover well over 13 hectares (32 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavator argued that the ground itself gave out, the clearing of woodland and the grazing of herds stripping thin soils off the steep slopes of the southern Levant in a way the flatter north escaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the site is best known for is two caches of plaster statuary, buried beneath the floors of buildings that had already stood empty for a long time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first held about ten figures 80 to 90 centimetres (31 to 35 inches) tall along with eight or more busts, each built around a bundle of reeds and twigs bound with twine and moulded over in plaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their eyes are outlined in green pigment with black irises, probably bitumen, and one face carries three vertical red stripes on the brow and three diagonal ones on each cheek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Skulls got the same attention, buried in the corners of rooms, and two crania have been shown to be modelled and painted red, one with plaster still adhering and one with a black modelling substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strontium in the teeth of 22 of its dead places all but one within the local range, so whatever drew people together into these mega-sites, its people were almost all born nearby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Gary O. Rollefson, “The Aceramic Neolithic of the Southern Levant: The View from ’Ain Ghazal,” <i>Paléorient</i> 15, no. 1 (1989): 135–40, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.1989.4491. [Open access]","Gary O. Rollefson, “Ritual and Ceremony at Neolithic Ain Ghazal (Jordan),” <i>Paléorient</i> 9, no. 2 (1983): 29–38, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.1983.4339. [Open access]","Gary O. Rollefson, “’Ain Ghazal (Jordan): Ritual and Ceremony III,” <i>Paléorient</i> 24, no. 1 (1998): 43–58, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.1998.4668. [Open access]","Michelle Bonogofsky, “Cranial Modeling and Neolithic Bone Modification at ’Ain Ghazal: New Interpretations,” <i>Paléorient</i> 27, no. 2 (2001): 141–46, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.2001.4734. [Open access]","Jonathan Santana et al., “Multi-Isotope Evidence of Population Aggregation in the Natufian and Scant Migration during the Early Neolithic of the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11, no. 1 (2021): 11857, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-90795-2. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","jordan","art"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Ain_Ghazal_Statues_Jordan_Archaeological_Museum_Amman_Jordan0822.jpg/1280px-Ain_Ghazal_Statues_Jordan_Archaeological_Museum_Amman_Jordan0822.jpg","title":"'Ain Ghazal","desc":"A two-headed plaster figure from 'Ain Ghazal, the eyes of both heads outlined in black, in the Jordan Archaeological Museum. Michael Gunther, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ain_Ghazal_Statues_Jordan_Archaeological_Museum_Amman_Jordan0822.jpg","alt":"A two-headed plaster figure from 'Ain Ghazal, the eyes of both heads outlined in black, in the Jordan Archaeological Museum"}},
{"id":"wh-127","num":127,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Two huts of about 20,000 years ago at Kharaneh IV in Jordan push <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back thousands of years before the Natufian stone houses usually credited with it.","questions":["The house mouse took up living with people in large hunter-gatherer settlements in the Levant about 14,500 years ago, which makes it a marker of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than of farming.","Villages in north-east China appear about 7,900 years ago just as the climate turned wetter, which suggests <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began there under plenty rather than under pressure."],"answer":"sedentism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Levant huts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 20,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">House mice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">North-east China</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,900 BP</span></div>","answerText":"sedentism","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sedentism</b> is a way of living with limited or no residential movement, and it is not a rung on a ladder of social evolution but one option among several.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is easier to define than to detect, since words like prolonged or regular occupation are hard to turn into measurements, and the argument over where the line falls has not closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it is read from is permanent structures, grinding stones too heavy to carry, occupation deposits deep enough to imply long repeated stays, and the animals that move in to live alongside people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Southwest Asia it came well before farming: two huts of about 20,000 years ago at Kharaneh IV in eastern Jordan, with caches of shell and red ochre, stand thousands of years before the Natufian stone houses usually credited with the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> House mice make the same point from the other end, taking up life with people in large late hunter-gatherer settlements in the Levant around 14,500 years ago, before any crop was sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In north-east China villages and cultivation appear together about 7,900 years ago, at the moment the climate turned wetter, which argues that people settled under plenty rather than under pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There the domestication of plants followed only half a millennium after cultivation began, and even then domesticated plants and animals were rare, so settling down did not wait on farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On Tanegashima in southern Japan the degree of settlement tracks what the forest offered, and stable sedentism arrives only with the Incipient Jomon, when the island's population rose as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Caribbean, strontium and oxygen in the teeth of 44 people from Canímar Abajo in Cuba describe a coastal community living on local resources, not the highly mobile foragers the island's early groups were long taken to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Staying put also made a niche for other species, and the house mouse spread out of it with farming across the Near East and much later with Europe's towns and trade routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lisa A. Maher et al., “Twenty Thousand-Year-Old Huts at a Hunter-Gatherer Settlement in Eastern Jordan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 2 (2012): e31447, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0031447. [Open access]","Thomas Cucchi et al., “Tracking the Near Eastern Origins and European Dispersal of the Western House Mouse,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10, no. 1 (2020): 8276, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-64939-9. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach-Lavi et al., “Sedentism and Plant Cultivation in Northeast China Emerged during Affluent Conditions,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 7 (2019): e0218751, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218751. [Open access]","Yadira Chinique de Armas et al., “Earliest Evidence of Sedentism in the Antilles: Multiple Isotope Data from Canímar Abajo, Cuba,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 1 (2025): e2413963121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2413963121. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Limestone_%26_basalt_mortars_from_Eynan%2C_early_Natufian_circa_12500_BC.jpg/1920px-Limestone_%26_basalt_mortars_from_Eynan%2C_early_Natufian_circa_12500_BC.jpg","title":"Sedentism","desc":"Two large limestone and basalt mortars from Natufian Eynan, each too heavy to be carried about, in the Israel Museum. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limestone_%26_basalt_mortars_from_Eynan,_early_Natufian_circa_12500_BC.jpg","alt":"Two large limestone and basalt mortars from Natufian Eynan, each too heavy to be carried about, in the Israel Museum"}},
{"id":"wh-128","num":128,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"The oldest dates for fired clay vessels anywhere come from caves in southern China, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an invention of hunter-gatherers rather than of farmers.","questions":["Fats soaked into 528 vessels from around the Baltic show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was put to regionally different uses, fish in the east and animal fat further south and west.","Reaching the Baltic from the Caspian in three or four centuries, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread across Europe several times faster than farming did, and among foragers rather than farmers."],"answer":"pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest pots</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 20,000 – 17,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Africa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 11,000 – 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 5900 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"pottery","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pottery</b> is fired clay shaped into vessels, and its history cuts across the usual account of the Neolithic, because it was often invented by hunter-gatherers rather than by farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest dates for pots anywhere are from caves in southern China, about 20,000 to 17,000 years ago, though other evidence from the same deposits has been read as putting them nearer the end of the Ice Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foragers on Tanegashima in southern Japan were making it by roughly 14,000 to 12,800 years ago, mostly on the island itself, though a few vessels were carried there across open water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Africa's early pottery belongs to hunting, gathering and fishing peoples too, appearing across the Sahara and the Nile valley between about 11,000 and 10,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modelling the radiocarbon dates there favours two or three separate inventions rather than one, with the likely centres in the Central Sahara, the Nile valley and West Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In Europe it reached hunter-gatherers before farming did, appearing near the northern shore of the Caspian shortly before 5900 BCE and then running west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It covered more than 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) to the Baltic in three or four centuries, six to ten kilometres a year, several times the speed at which farming spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is too fast to be people carrying it as they moved, so what travelled was the knowledge, passed along networks of kin and neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the pots were for can be read from the fats soaked into their walls, and 528 vessels from around the Baltic show regional habits rather than regional environments, with fish in the east, non-ruminant animal fat in the south-east and a mixture including dairy in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Near East the order is reversed: at the Mazandaran caves a Pre-Pottery Neolithic lies beneath the Pottery Neolithic, and the oldest pottery of north-eastern Iran begins only at the start of the 7th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Fumie Iizuka et al., “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]","Rocco Rotunno and Enrico R. Crema, “Bayesian Analyses of Radiocarbon Dates Suggest Multiple Origins of Ceramic Technology in Early Holocene Africa,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 8819, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63887-0. [Open access]","Ekaterina Dolbunova et al., “The Transmission of Pottery Technology among Prehistoric European Hunter-Gatherers,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 7, no. 2 (2023): 171–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-022-01491-8. [Open access]","Blandine Courel et al., “Organic Residue Analysis Shows Sub-Regional Patterns in the Use of Pottery by Northern European Hunter-Gatherers,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 7, no. 4 (2020): 192016, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.192016. [Open access]","Rahmat Abbasnejad Seresti et al., “The Oldest Pottery Neolithic (PN) Culture of Northeastern Iran: First Absolute Dating from Eastern Mazandaran Plains,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0322478, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0322478. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","technology","prehistory","archaeology","history"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Deep_Bowls.Incipient_Jomon.Tokyo_National_Museum.jpg/1920px-Deep_Bowls.Incipient_Jomon.Tokyo_National_Museum.jpg","title":"Pottery","desc":"Two deep Incipient Jomon cooking pots with pointed bases and banded decoration, in the Tokyo National Museum. Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deep_Bowls.Incipient_Jomon.Tokyo_National_Museum.jpg","alt":"Two deep Incipient Jomon cooking pots with pointed bases and banded decoration, in the Tokyo National Museum"}},
{"id":"wh-129","num":129,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Non-shattering types are only about a tenth of the rice impressed in 10,000-year-old pottery clay at Shangshan, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is called a protracted process.","questions":["Use-wear and rice phytoliths on 52 flaked stone tools from the Lower Yangtze show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was driven by harvesting, first with finger knives and later with sickles.","Most functional mutations in japonica landraces turn out to predate the Holocene, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked largely on variation already present in the wild plant rather than on new mutations."],"answer":"rice domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 4,400 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reached Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 2,000 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"rice domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Rice domestication</b> turned a wetland grass into the crop that feeds more than half the world, and its hallmark is the loss of seed shattering, which leaves a plant unable to sow itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asian rice, <i>Oryza sativa</i>, was first domesticated in China as the japonica type, beginning about 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its wild ancestor <i>Oryza rufipogon</i> grows in swampy wetland, ripens unevenly and drops its seed into the water, which makes it a hard plant to gather and a slow one to change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The change was protracted, starting in the Shangshan culture of the Lower Yangtze about 10,000 years ago and running for several millennia to the Liangzhu culture of 5,300 to 4,400 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice spikelets impressed in Shangshan pottery clay of the 10th millennium BP are 12 per cent non-shattering, and at Huxi, 9,000 to 8,400 years ago, the figure is 8.7 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Harvesting is what did the selecting, and 52 flaked stone tools from Shangshan and Hehuashan carry the traces, 30 of them worn by cutting plants and 28 of those holding rice phytoliths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two methods can be told apart, reaping the heads with a finger knife and cutting the stalks near the base with a sickle, and sickles took over as water management made the crop ripen together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice was drunk as well as eaten: vessels from the earliest phase at Shangshan held a fermented drink made with a starter of <i>Monascus</i> mould and yeast, the oldest brewing method known in East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of 76 known functional mutations in japonica landraces arose in the Pleistocene, more than 11,700 years ago, so farmers were largely selecting variation the wild plant already carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> West Africa domesticated a rice of its own from the wild <i>Oryza barthii</i>, and Asian rice reached West Asia and Europe by the close of the 1st millennium BCE, by the Silk Road and by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jiajing Wang et al., “New Evidence for Rice Harvesting in the Early Neolithic Lower Yangtze River, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 12 (2022): e0278200, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0278200. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “Identification of 10,000-Year-Old Rice Beer at Shangshan in the Lower Yangzi River Valley of China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 51 (2024): e2412274121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2412274121. [Open access]","Ornob Alam et al., “Evolutionary Histories of Functional Mutations during the Domestication and Spread of <i>japonica</i> Rice in Asia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 43 (2025): e2514614122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2514614122. [Open access]","Babatunde O. Kehinde et al., “African Cultivated, Wild and Weedy Rice (<i>Oryza</i> spp.): Anticipating Further Genomic Studies,” <i>Biology</i> 13, no. 9 (2024): 697, https://doi.org/10.3390/biology13090697. [Open access]","Robert N. Spengler et al., “A Journey to the West: The Ancient Dispersal of Rice Out of East Asia,” <i>Rice</i> 14, no. 1 (2021): 83, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12284-021-00518-4. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Zhejiang_Sheng_Bowuguan_2014.09.28_15-14-33.jpg/1920px-Zhejiang_Sheng_Bowuguan_2014.09.28_15-14-33.jpg","title":"Rice domestication","desc":"Waterlogged rice grains and rice leaves from the Hemudu site, dark with age, laid out in a dish in the Zhejiang Provincial Museum. Zhangzhugang, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhejiang_Sheng_Bowuguan_2014.09.28_15-14-33.jpg","alt":"Waterlogged rice grains and rice leaves from the Hemudu site, dark with age, laid out in a dish in the Zhejiang Provincial Museum"}},
{"id":"wh-130","num":130,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Foxtail and broomcorn were the crops of the Yellow River basin, and their non-shattering and grain-size changes put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 8300 and 4300 BCE.","questions":["Directly dating 29 charred grains at Dadiwan put the oldest secure evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 7,900 BP, against the ages assumed for the layers they came out of.","Yielding far less per hectare than wet rice, the crops produced by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread northern China's Neolithic cultures over much more ground than their Yangtze neighbours."],"answer":"millet domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8300 – 4300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dadiwan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,900 BP, directly dated</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reached Iberia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"millet domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Millet domestication</b> gave northern China its staple crops, foxtail millet and broomcorn millet, and made the Yellow River basin one of the two centres of farming in early China alongside the rice country of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The changes that mark it — the loss of shattering, larger grains and denser heads — are placed between 8300 and 4300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wild relatives, green foxtail and the ruderal broomcorn group, now grow mostly as weeds of cultivated ground, so where they are found says little about where they were first taken in hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early finds cluster in a semi-arid belt of the loess country, where monsoon rainfall runs between 400 and 700 millimetres (16 and 28 inches) and woodland gives way to steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 4500 to 3500 BCE millet-centred farming was established across that basin and the Loess Plateau above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Millets yield much less per hectare than wet rice, which is why the northern Neolithic cultures covered far more ground than their Yangtze counterparts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dating 29 charred grains from Dadiwan directly gives about 7,900 BP as the oldest secure date for the crop, and most of those dates disagree with the ages assumed for the layers the grains came out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the corrected chronology four waves carried dry-land farming west along the eastern Silk Road, at roughly 7800, 6500, 5500 and 4500 BP, and millet was being eaten on the Atlantic coast of Iberia by about 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was never a one-crop system: adzuki beans directly dated to 8985 to 8645 years ago at Xiaogao in Shandong sat in a field system with millet, rice and soybean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genomes from 74 Middle Neolithic individuals show the Yellow and Yangtze farmers as distinct populations exchanging in both directions, which is what farming spread by people rather than by ideas looks like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Chris J. Stevens et al., “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Haiming Li et al., “Multiple Waves of Westward Dry-Land Agriculture Expansions along the East Silk Road during the Neolithic Age,” <i>Fundamental Research</i> 6, no. 3 (2026): 1321–31, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fmre.2025.12.013. [Open access]","Xuexiang Chen et al., “The Discovery of Adzuki Bean (<i>Vigna angularis</i>) in Eastern China during the 9th Millennium BP and Its Domestication in East Asia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 39 (2025): e2510835122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2510835122. [Open access]","Jianxue Xiong et al., “The Genomic History of East Asian Middle Neolithic Millet- and Rice-Agricultural Populations,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 5, no. 10 (2025): 100976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12791001/. [Open access]","Borja González-Rabanal et al., “The Arrival of Millets to the Atlantic Coast of Northern Iberia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12, no. 1 (2022): 18589, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-23227-4. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Setaria_italica_kz01.jpg/1920px-Setaria_italica_kz01.jpg","title":"Millet domestication","desc":"A stand of ripening foxtail millet, the bristly heads bent over under the weight of the grain, in the botanical garden in Warsaw. Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setaria_italica_kz01.jpg","alt":"A stand of ripening foxtail millet, the bristly heads bent over under the weight of the grain, in the botanical garden in Warsaw"}},
{"id":"wh-131","num":131,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Teosinte, a wiry Mexican grass carrying a handful of hard-cased seeds, is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began from in the Balsas region about 9,000 years ago.","questions":["Cobs from Paredones in Peru are already domesticated at 6,700 years old while older Mexican ones are still intermediate, which is why the course of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is argued over.","At El Gigante in Honduras tree fruits and squash appear 6,000 years before maize does, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a long way from making it anybody's staple."],"answer":"maize domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 years ago, in Mexico</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In South America</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 7,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">A staple after</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,000 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"maize domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Maize domestication</b> turned teosinte, a wiry Mexican grass bearing a handful of hard-cased seeds, into the most productive staple crop in the world, and it began in south-western Mexico about 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wild ancestor is <i>Zea mays</i> ssp. <i>parviglumis</i>, and starch grains and phytoliths from the Balsas region put the crop in use there by about 8,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Comparing a maize landrace with a population of its ancestor across 18 domestication traits finds that the intensity of selection was weak for every one of them, weaker than natural selection often is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of those traits the total weight of grain per plant is the one selection does not appear to have improved at all, so this was not, at the start, a crop being bred for yield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It travelled quickly for all that, reaching South America by at least 7,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether it arrived finished is the live argument: on one reading it went south as a partial domesticate, and South America became a second centre of improvement where the domestication syndrome was fixed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against that, three cobs from Paredones on the Peruvian coast, 6,700 to 5,000 years old, come from the same domestication event as Mexican maize and are already domesticated, where the older Mexican specimens from Guilá Naquitz and San Marcos are intermediate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Primitive-looking cobs did survive a long way out, in the Peruaçu valley of Brazil between 1,010 and 570 years ago, the furthest from the centre of origin and the longest such characters are known to have lasted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes from El Gigante in Honduras, 2,300 to 1,900 years old, are closest to South American maize, so a later wave carried the crop back north again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was slow to matter: at that same shelter tree fruits and squash appear about 11,000 years ago and maize only about 4,400, with the turn to field crops coming after 4,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Logan Kistler et al., “Archaeological Central American Maize Genomes Suggest Ancient Gene Flow from South America,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 117, no. 52 (2020): 33124–29, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2015560117. [Open access]","Chin Jian Yang et al., “The Genetic Architecture of Teosinte Catalyzed and Constrained Maize Domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 12 (2019): 5643–52, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1820997116. [Open access]","Miguel Vallebueno-Estrada et al., “Domestication and Lowland Adaptation of Coastal Preceramic Maize from Paredones, Peru,” <i>eLife</i> 12 (2023): e83149, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.83149. [Open access]","Flaviane Malaquias Costa et al., “Archaeological Findings Show the Extent of Primitive Characteristics of Maize in South America,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 36 (2024): eadn1466, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adn1466. [Open access]","Douglas J. Kennett et al., “Trans-Holocene Bayesian Chronology for Tree and Field Crop Use from El Gigante Rockshelter, Honduras,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 6 (2023): e0287195, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0287195. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","mexico"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Maize-teosinte.jpg","title":"Maize domestication","desc":"Three ears laid side by side: a slender teosinte ear carrying about a dozen hard-cased seeds, a maize-teosinte hybrid, and a full maize cob many times the size of either. John Doebley, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maize-teosinte.jpg","alt":"Three ears laid side by side: a slender teosinte ear carrying about a dozen hard-cased seeds, a maize-teosinte hybrid, and a full maize cob many times the size of either"}},
{"id":"wh-132","num":132,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Carved stone and manufactured pestles at Waim are evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, putting a Neolithic in the New Guinea highlands a thousand years before any migrants arrived.","questions":["Where a crop varies most is where it is oldest, or so ran the reasoning behind the phrase centre of origin, which gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its vocabulary.","Indigenous peoples of eastern North America domesticated annual crops of their own, one of the least famous cases of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and several of those crops later fell out of use."],"answer":"independent origins of agriculture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Centres</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 11, independent</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 – 11,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Guinea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a Neolithic by c. 5,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"independent origins of agriculture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>independent origins of agriculture</b> are the finding that farming was not invented once and handed on but arose separately in at least eleven centres, each working whatever grew or grazed nearby, as the last ice age let go 12,000 to 11,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nikolai Vavilov gave the idea its vocabulary, coining the phrase centre of origin for the place where a crop's diversity is greatest, on the reasoning that a crop is oldest where it varies most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The method was fallible: his proposal that emmer, barley, oats and lentils were independently domesticated in Abyssinia has been disproven, though coffee, enset, t'ef and finger millet do appear to come from the Ethiopian highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scholars have never agreed what domestication is, how it happens, or even which organisms it applies to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What has given way is the idea of a single core area and a single event, in favour of protracted and largely unconscious processes running along several parallel lineages at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The New Guinea highlands are the clearest case outside the famous centres, with wetland farming from the middle of the Holocene and, at Waim, the earliest figurative stone carving and manufactured pestles in Oceania, of 5,050 to 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those finds put a Neolithic there about a thousand years before Lapita migrants reached the region from South-East Asia, which is what makes it an invention rather than an import.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eastern North America is another, where Indigenous peoples domesticated a set of annual crops of their own, several of which fell out of cultivation around the time of European colonization and survive only in the archaeological record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Calling those crops lost is the older habit; with Indigenous food sovereignty movements taking the practice up again they are better described as sleeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The record there is unusually full because flotation, which lifts charred seeds out of excavated soil in water, became standard practice in the 1960s and has since been applied to thousands of sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1323964111. [Open access]","Rosalind Emma Gillis et al., “Unravelling Domestication: Introduction to the Theme Issue,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240187, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2024.0187. [Open access]","Robert N. Spengler and Li Tang, “Seeking Consensus on the Domestication Concept,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240188, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2024.0188. [Open access]","Ben Shaw et al., “Emergence of a Neolithic in Highland New Guinea by 5000 to 4000 Years Ago,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 13 (2020): eaay4573, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aay4573. [Open access]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The Sleeping Crops of Eastern North America: A New Synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2024.0192. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","evolution"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Centres_of_origin_and_spread_of_agriculture_labelled.svg/1280px-Centres_of_origin_and_spread_of_agriculture_labelled.svg.png","title":"Independent origins of agriculture","desc":"A world map marking centres where farming began — the Fertile Crescent, the Yangtze and Yellow River basins, New Guinea, Central Mexico, north-western South America, eastern North America and the Sahel — with arrows for its spread and question marks over the least certain. Ian Alexander, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centres_of_origin_and_spread_of_agriculture_labelled.svg","alt":"A world map marking centres where farming began — the Fertile Crescent, the Yangtze and Yellow River basins, New Guinea, Central Mexico, north-western South America, eastern North America and the Sahel — with arrows for its spread and question marks over the least certain"}},
{"id":"wh-133","num":133,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Cemeteries hold a higher share of children after farming arrives, and that rising share is the signal by which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first identified.","questions":["A 420-year rise followed by some 840 years of decline is the shape the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes in the radiocarbon record, and what caused the falling half is still disputed.","Because it puts the growth after farming rather than before it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> undid the older claim that a crowded Mesolithic drove people to take up crops."],"answer":"Neolithic demographic transition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Follows farming</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 9,000 BP in Europe</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Boom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">420 years, at 0.17 per cent a year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bust</span><span class=\"dt-v\">some 840 years after that</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic demographic transition","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neolithic demographic transition</b> is the surge in human numbers that followed the arrival of farming, and its engine was fertility rather than survival: people had more children, while a new regime of higher mortality ran alongside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is read from cemeteries, where the share of skeletons that are children rises after farming reaches a region, a measure known as the juvenility index.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because farming arrived in Europe over four thousand years, the clock is set to zero at the local arrival date, which is what lets regions a millennium apart be compared at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That evidence overturned an older claim that a crowded Mesolithic had pushed people into farming: the growth comes after the crops, not before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reading is only as good as the bones, and in small samples the arithmetic behind it carries a large error, which is why the standard equation is now fitted per sample rather than taken off the shelf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A second and quite separate proxy counts radiocarbon dates from archaeological sites, on the reasoning that more people leave more datable rubbish, and the two curves correlate closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Together they give the transition its shape: a 420-year rise, then some 840 years of decline, a whole cycle of about 1,260 years, with the boom running at 0.17 per cent a year and the bust at a quarter of that in reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What caused the falling half is unsettled, and three answers are in play.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The European cemetery study found no correlation with climate, dated radiocarbon series from Britain and Ireland put the downturns alongside abrupt North Atlantic climate changes, and modelling of the periods and amplitudes finds climate cannot produce them where density-driven conflict can.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Northern China shows the same rise between 5000 and 2000 BCE, and there it is credited less to domestication itself than to the improvement of crops and animals afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Sean S. Downey et al., “The Neolithic Demographic Transition in Europe: Correlation with Juvenility Index Supports Interpretation of the Summed Calibrated Radiocarbon Date Probability Distribution (SCDPD) as a Valid Demographic Proxy,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 8 (2014): e105730, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0105730. [Open access]","Patrik Galeta and Anna Pankowská, “A New Method for Estimating Growth and Fertility Rates Using Age-at-Death Ratios in Small Skeletal Samples,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 6 (2023): e0286580, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0286580. [Open access]","Dániel Kondor et al., “Explaining Population Booms and Busts in Mid-Holocene Europe,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 9310, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-35920-z. [Open access]","Andrew Bevan et al., “Holocene Fluctuations in Human Population Demonstrate Repeated Links to Food Production and Climate,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 49 (2017): E10524–31, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1709190114. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens et al., “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","science"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/GBM_-_Linearbandkeramik_1_Grab.jpg/1920px-GBM_-_Linearbandkeramik_1_Grab.jpg","title":"Neolithic demographic transition","desc":"A crouched Linear Pottery culture burial from the cemetery at Aiterhofen-Odmuhle, laid out with shell beads and stone adzes, on display in the Gaubodenmuseum at Straubing. Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GBM_-_Linearbandkeramik_1_Grab.jpg","alt":"A crouched Linear Pottery culture burial from the cemetery at Aiterhofen-Odmuhle, laid out with shell beads and stone adzes, on display in the Gaubodenmuseum at Straubing"}},
{"id":"wh-134","num":134,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Screening 1,313 ancient Eurasians turned up the microbes behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only from about 6,500 years ago, peaking as livestock keeping spread five millennia back.","questions":["At four cemeteries by Lake Baikal 39 per cent of the dead carry plague from 5,500 years ago, and they are hunter-gatherers, which complicates the idea that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arrived with farming.","Whether the earliest strains of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> caused flea-borne plague or food-borne enteritis is unsettled, and with it how much the Neolithic decline is owed to infection."],"answer":"zoonotic disease","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zoonoses appear</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6,500 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bubonic plague</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3,800 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"zoonotic disease","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zoonotic disease</b> is illness that passes between animals and people, and taking animals into the household changed the ecology of both, opening ways for pathogens to invade and persist in each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Screening 1,313 ancient Eurasians across 37,000 years of remains turned up 5,486 hits from 492 microbial species, of which 3,384 are known human pathogens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most kinds of microbe run through that whole span, and the zoonotic ones do not: they appear from about 6,500 years ago and peak around 5,000, which is when livestock keeping spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is direct evidence for something long argued and thinly supported, a first epidemiological transition in which settling down beside animals raised the burden of infectious disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their spread grew over the millennia after, alongside the pastoralist migrations out of the Eurasian steppe, and the weight of what that meant is still visible as late as 1850, when about half of all children died before the age of fifteen and more than half of those deaths were from infection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plague is the test case, and it complicates the story rather than confirming it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <i>Yersinia pestis</i> lacked the machinery for the bubonic form until about 3,800 years ago, so what the earlier strains did to a person cannot be read off the genome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At four cemeteries by Lake Baikal it is in 39 per cent of the dead from about 5,500 years ago, within small family groups and killing children of 8 to 11 — and those people were hunter-gatherers, far outside Neolithic Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Others read the early strains as food-borne enteritis rather than flea-borne plague, and doubt that epidemics emptied Neolithic Scandinavia at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Bury in the Paris Basin, 132 genomes either side of a break in burial show two largely unrelated populations, plague and relapsing fever in the record between them, and forest growing back over the fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Martin Sikora et al., “The Spatiotemporal Distribution of Human Pathogens in Ancient Eurasia,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8073 (2025): 1011–19, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09192-8. [Open access]","Ruairidh Macleod et al., “Lethal Plague Outbreaks in Lake Baikal Hunter-Gatherers 5,500 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 654, no. 8119 (2026): 697–705, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10540-5. [Open access]","Hamadou Oumarou Hama et al., “Commentary on Seersholm et al.: <i>Yersinia pestis</i> Infection Is Not Synonymous with Deadly Plague in Neolithic Scandinavia,” <i>American Journal of Biological Anthropology</i> 189, no. 1 (2026): e70200, https://doi.org/10.1002/ajpa.70200. [Open access]","Frederik V. Seersholm et al., “Population Discontinuity in the Paris Basin Linked to Evidence of the Neolithic Decline,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 10, no. 4 (2026): 677–88, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-026-03027-z. [Open access]","Robin Bendrey et al., “Conceptualizing Emergent Animal Farming and Infectious Diseases: A One Health Framework,” <i>Evolution, Medicine, and Public Health</i> 13, no. 1 (2025): 344–54, https://doi.org/10.1093/emph/eoaf029. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","science"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Yersinia_pestis_scanned_with_electron_micrograph.jpg/1920px-Yersinia_pestis_scanned_with_electron_micrograph.jpg","title":"Zoonotic disease","desc":"Rod-shaped Yersinia pestis bacteria massed in the foregut of a flea, in a scanning electron micrograph with the instrument's own scale bar burnt in along the foot. NIAID, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yersinia_pestis_scanned_with_electron_micrograph.jpg","alt":"Rod-shaped Yersinia pestis bacteria massed in the foregut of a flea, in a scanning electron micrograph with the instrument's own scale bar burnt in along the foot"},"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-135","num":135,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Milk residues sit on European pots from the Neolithic onward, yet <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed rare for another three thousand years, which is the puzzle every explanation of it must answer.","questions":["The variants behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sit not in the lactase gene but in an enhancer inside a neighbouring one, and five of them have been shown to work.","Central Asian herders live on dairy products and only about 12 per cent of them show <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because fermenting the milk answers the same problem culturally."],"answer":"lactase persistence","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Selection began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,500 BP, between the Balkans and central Europe</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First carrier</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,960 BP, Eneolithic Ukraine</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Common in Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 3,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"lactase persistence","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lactase persistence</b> is the continued production of lactase into adulthood, which lets a person digest the sugar in milk; losing the enzyme after weaning is the ancestral condition for humans and for every other mammal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 35 per cent of people worldwide keep it, and the variants responsible sit not in the lactase gene itself but in an enhancer inside the neighbouring gene upstream of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Five have been shown to work, the European one by binding a transcription factor more tightly than the ancestral sequence does and so keeping the gene switched on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Selection on it has been put at between 3 and 19 per cent in northern Europe, which makes it one of the strongest signals of recent natural selection anywhere in the human genome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also a case of convergent evolution, separate mutations in the same short stretch of DNA doing the same job in eastern and western Africa, in Arabia and in South Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Milk itself came first, and by a long way: fat residues on some 7,000 potsherds from more than 550 sites show milk in use across Europe from the beginning of the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The allele stayed rare for millennia after that, turning up reliably 5,960 years ago, standing at about 1 per cent between 5,000 and 4,000 years ago, and reaching 31 per cent only after 3,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modelling allele frequencies against farming dates puts the start of selection around 7,500 years ago between the central Balkans and central Europe, and finds no support for the old northern-sunlight explanation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Why it rose at all is unsettled, since how much milk a region used explains its trajectory no better than steady selection does, and living carriers show no consistent advantage in health or fertility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Famine and infection are the current proposal, milk being dangerous without the enzyme to anyone already weak — while Central Asia, heavily pastoral and barely persistent because its milk is fermented, resists any simpler account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Richard P. Evershed et al., “Dairying, Diseases and the Evolution of Lactase Persistence in Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 608, no. 7922 (2022): 336–45, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/7615474. [Open access]","Laure Ségurel et al., “Why and When Was Lactase Persistence Selected For? Insights from Central Asian Herders and Ancient DNA,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 18, no. 6 (2020): e3000742, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000742. [Open access]","Jay T. Stock and Jonathan C. K. Wells, “Dairying and the Evolution and Consequences of Lactase Persistence in Humans,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 13, no. 3 (2023): 7–13, https://doi.org/10.1093/af/vfad022. [Open access]","Céleste E. Cohen, Dallas M. Swallow, and Catherine Walker, “The Molecular Basis of Lactase Persistence: Linking Genetics and Epigenetics,” <i>Annals of Human Genetics</i> 89, no. 5 (2025): 321–32, https://doi.org/10.1111/ahg.12575. [Open access]","Yuval Itan et al., “The Origins of Lactase Persistence in Europe,” <i>PLOS Computational Biology</i> 5, no. 8 (2009): e1000491, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1000491. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","evolution","genetics","science","agriculture"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Lactase_persistence_in_the_Old_World.svg","title":"Lactase persistence","desc":"An interpolated map of Africa, Europe and Asia shading the percentage of adults carrying a lactase-persistence genotype, darkest across north-western Europe and in patches of East Africa and Arabia, with small circles marking the sampled populations. Joe Roe, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lactase_persistence_in_the_Old_World.svg","alt":"An interpolated map of Africa, Europe and Asia shading the percentage of adults carrying a lactase-persistence genotype, darkest across north-western Europe and in patches of East Africa and Arabia, with small circles marking the sampled populations"}},
{"id":"wh-136","num":136,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Cattle were pulling loads in Southwest Asia by about 8200 BCE and milk sat in European pots from the first farmers, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no longer read as one horizon.","questions":["Milk, wool, traction and transport are the four uses gathered together by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, every one of them taken from a living animal rather than from a carcass.","A yoke from a village on Lake Constance and plough furrows sealed under a Wiltshire barrow are the 4th-millennium evidence the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was built on, and both still stand."],"answer":"secondary products revolution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1981, for the 4th millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest yoke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3384–3370 BCE, Lake Constance</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wheel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 3900 BCE, Carpathians</span></div>","answerText":"secondary products revolution","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>secondary products revolution</b> is a proposal that livestock were first kept for what can only be had by killing them, and that the uses of a living animal followed thousands of years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those uses are milk, wool, traction and transport, and as set out in 1981 they arrived together as a package in the 4th millennium BCE, spreading into Europe from Southwest Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The claim mattered because each use changes what an animal is worth: one that ploughs opens heavy ground, one that pulls a cart moves bulk, and a fleece or a milking comes again next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wool is the hardest to date, fleece almost never surviving, so it is tracked through the sheep instead, whose spread across Europe was reshaped by a Bronze Age movement off the Pontic steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its 4th-millennium evidence is solid — a cattle yoke from a village on Lake Constance, dated 3384–3370 BCE, ard furrows sealed under a Wiltshire barrow — and the wheel may belong there too, one argument placing its invention among Carpathian copper miners by 3900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What has gone is the single horizon, because each of the four turns out to be far older somewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fat residues on pottery show milk in use across Europe from the beginning of the Neolithic, so the first farmers were already dairying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cattle were worked as early as about 8200 BCE in Southwest Asia, by 6000 BCE in the Balkans and by 5300 BCE in Catalonia, which leaves no room for a single transfer of know-how westward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Timings, reasons and process differ region by region, and what travelled was a set of separable practices taken up where local conditions rewarded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the 4th millennium does hold it holds sharply: north of the Caucasus the early steppe herders milked sheep and nothing else, keeping cattle back for work, while at Kilshane in Ireland a herd was bred to raise large oxen for the yoke as megalith building grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Fabienne Pigière and Jessica Smyth, “First Evidence for Cattle Traction in Middle Neolithic Ireland: A Pivotal Element for Resource Exploitation,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): e0279556, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279556. [Open access]","Richard P. Evershed et al., “Dairying, Diseases and the Evolution of Lactase Persistence in Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 608, no. 7922 (2022): 336–45, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/7615474. [Open access]","Ashley Scott et al., “Emergence and Intensification of Dairying in the Caucasus and Eurasian Steppes,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 6, no. 6 (2022): 813–22, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-022-01701-6. [Open access]","Lee R. Alacoque, Richard W. Bulliet, and Kai A. James, “Reconstructing the Invention of the Wheel Using Computational Structural Analysis and Design,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 11, no. 10 (2024): 240373, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.240373. [Open access]","Damla Kaptan et al., “The Population History of Domestic Sheep Revealed by Paleogenomes,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 41, no. 10 (2024): msae158, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msae158. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","technology"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/0854_Ein_Krug_aus_Bronocice%2C_3.550_v._Chr..JPG/1920px-0854_Ein_Krug_aus_Bronocice%2C_3.550_v._Chr..JPG","title":"The Bronocice pot","desc":"A burnished brown clay vessel with a sharply angled lower body and a flaring rim, standing on a small metal support in a museum case; incised round its upper zone are schematic four-wheeled wagons, each a rectangle with a circle at every corner and a peaked outline above, beside a fir-tree motif and a zigzag band. Silar, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0854_Ein_Krug_aus_Bronocice,_3.550_v._Chr..JPG","alt":"A burnished brown clay vessel with a sharply angled lower body and a flaring rim, standing on a small metal support in a museum case; incised round its upper zone are schematic four-wheeled wagons, each a rectangle with a circle at every corner and a peaked outline above, beside a fir-tree motif and a zigzag band"}},
{"id":"wh-137","num":137,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Post-framed timber longhouses on loess soil and pots banded with incised lines are the marks of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first farming society of central Europe.","questions":["Genomes from 250 people show that the households of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were patrilocal, women more often being the ones who had come from somewhere else.","Talheim, Vrable and Asparn-Schletz are massacre sites of the last century of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose radiocarbon counts fall away around 5000 BCE."],"answer":"Linear Pottery culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5500–5000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second wave</span><span class=\"dt-v\">several centuries after 5500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000–4900 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Linear Pottery culture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Linear Pottery culture</b> is the first farming society of central Europe, running from about 5500 to 5000 BCE and named for the incised bands that wrap its fine pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of the Starčevo culture of western Hungary and the Körös of the Hungarian plain, and it spread in two waves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first, from about 5500 BCE, reached Slovakia, Austria, Moravia, Bohemia and Germany; the second, several centuries later, ran west to the Paris basin and Normandy and east into Poland, Ukraine, Moldova and Romania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its settlements cluster on the loess soils of river plains, its houses are post-framed timber longhouses, and across that whole range the material culture is strikingly uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cattle dominate the animal bones, and the ages at which they were killed across 19 sites show a husbandry of milk and meat together, with mature beasts kept on, while milk fats in perforated Polish vessels point to cheese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The people descend chiefly from Anatolian farmers and carry, on average, about 5 per cent of the ancestry of Europe's own hunter-gatherers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That mixing began at once: at Brunn in Austria, among the oldest of these sites, two of three burials are of mixed descent and one is roughly half hunter-gatherer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Data from the genomes of 250 individuals show more structure down the male line than the female and more male relatives within a site, pointing to patrilocal households, as strontium had already suggested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eastern communities carried more hunter-gatherer ancestry than the western ones, so behind the shared pots lie two different histories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ends around 5000 BCE in what the radiocarbon counts read as a collapse, and its last century leaves massacres: at least 26 people killed at Schöneck-Kilianstädten with clubs and arrows, their lower legs deliberately broken, and at Asparn-Schletz a group of dead too unrelated to have come from one village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Pere Gelabert et al., “Social and Genetic Diversity in First Farmers of Central Europe,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 9, no. 1 (2025): 53–64, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-024-02034-z. [Open access]","János Jakucs et al., “Between the Vinča and Linearbandkeramik Worlds: The Diversity of Practices and Identities in the 54th–53rd Centuries cal BC in Southwest Hungary and Beyond,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 29, no. 3 (2016): 267–336, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-016-9096-x. [Open access]","Rosalind E. Gillis et al., “The Evolution of Dual Meat and Milk Cattle Husbandry in Linearbandkeramik Societies,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 284, no. 1860 (2017): 20170905, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2017.0905. [Open access]","Alexey G. Nikitin et al., “Interactions between Earliest Linearbandkeramik Farmers and Central European Hunter Gatherers at the Dawn of European Neolithization,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9 (2019): 19544, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-56029-2. [Open access]","Christian Meyer et al., “The Massacre Mass Grave of Schöneck-Kilianstädten Reveals New Insights into Collective Violence in Early Neolithic Central Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 112, no. 36 (2015): 11217–22, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/4568710. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","migration"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Linear_Pottery_001.jpg/1920px-Linear_Pottery_001.jpg","title":"A Linear Pottery vessel","desc":"A pale grey, almost spherical Neolithic bowl reassembled from its sherds with the joins still showing, incised with arcs that hang from the rim, rows of short jabbed dashes running beside them, and ladder-like bars of parallel lines across the belly. Willow, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_Pottery_001.jpg","alt":"A pale grey, almost spherical Neolithic bowl reassembled from its sherds with the joins still showing, incised with arcs that hang from the rim, rows of short jabbed dashes running beside them, and ladder-like bars of parallel lines across the belly"}},
{"id":"wh-138","num":138,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"A dolmen, a passage grave, a standing stone and a stone circle are all built from the same thing, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose name joins the Greek words for big and stone.","questions":["Modelling 2,410 radiocarbon dates puts the first European tombs built from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in northwest France in the second half of the 5th millennium BCE.","The Shamir field on the Golan Heights holds over 400 tombs each roofed by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is one reminder that the tradition is not only European."],"answer":"megalith","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4800–4000 BCE, northwest France</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Passage graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 4000 BCE, along the Atlantic</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Scandinavia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">second half of the 4th millennium BCE</span></div>","answerText":"megalith","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>megalith</b> is a large stone raised or set into a structure, from the Greek for big and for stone, and some 35,000 still stand in Europe, mostly near a coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They recur in a few forms across the whole range: dolmens, passage and gallery graves, single stones alone or set in circles and rows, and megalithic buildings, with the graves facing east or southeast toward the rising sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where they begin was settled by modelling 2,410 radiocarbon dates, which put the first megalithic graves in the second half of the 5th millennium BCE, and only northwest France has monumental building before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Passy graves in the Paris basin run to 280 metres (920 feet) with no stone chamber at all, and the tumulus of Saint-Michel at Carnac was begun between 4782 and 4594 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From there the idea travelled by sea: thousands of passage graves went up along the Atlantic coasts in the first half of the 4th millennium, and the tradition reached Scandinavia in the second half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That reversed a long argument: a single origin spreading out of the Near East gave way to a picture of several independent centres, which has now given way again to one origin and a spread by sea, running the opposite way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genomes from 24 people buried in five northwestern tombs show close kin among them and more men than women, which points to patrilineal use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rule is not general: at Panoría in southern Spain 44 individuals were sexed by bone, DNA and protein together, and there are twice as many women as men however the count is cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nor was the rite fixed: a chamber at Los Milanes held 28,740 burnt bone fragments from at least 21 people, cremated elsewhere in the 28th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor is any of it only European: such structures run from Ireland to Korea, and over 400 dolmens stand in the Shamir field on the Golan, one roofed by a basalt capstone of about 50 tonnes (55 tons).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["B. Schulz Paulsson, “Radiocarbon Dates and Bayesian Modeling Support Maritime Diffusion Model for Megaliths in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 9 (2019): 3460–65, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1813268116. [Open access]","Federico Sánchez-Quinto et al., “Megalithic Tombs in Western and Northern Neolithic Europe Were Linked to a Kindred Society,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 19 (2019): 9469–74, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1818037116. [Open access]","Marta Díaz-Zorita Bonilla et al., “Female Sex Bias in Iberian Megalithic Societies through Bioarchaeology, aDNA and Proteomics,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 21818, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-72148-x. [Open access]","Gonen Sharon et al., “Monumental Megalithic Burial and Rock Art Tell a New Story about the Levant Intermediate Bronze ‘Dark Ages’,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 3 (2017): e0172969, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0172969. [Open access]","Paula Becerra Fuello et al., “Funerary Practices of Cremation at the Megalithic Societies of South-Eastern Iberia: The Cemetery of Los Milanes,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0330771, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0330771. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","prehistory","archaeology","history","religion","building"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Straight_lines_of_stones_%2843201526792%29.jpg/1920px-Straight_lines_of_stones_%2843201526792%29.jpg","title":"Megalith","desc":"The Carnac stones are an exceptionally dense collection of megalithic sites around the village of Carnac in Brittany, consisting of alignments, dolmens, tumuli and single menhirs. Carol, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Straight_lines_of_stones_(43201526792).jpg","alt":"Rows of standing stones receding across rough grassland at Carnac in Brittany"}},
{"id":"wh-139","num":139,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Fifty of the 52 surviving sarsens at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> share a chemistry matching West Woods, about 25 kilometres (15 miles) north on the Marlborough Downs.","questions":["The Altar Stone at the centre of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Old Red Sandstone from northeast Scotland, at least 750 kilometres (470 miles) away and most likely carried by sea.","Durrington Walls, a settlement lived in for perhaps a decade around 2500 BCE, stands 2.8 kilometres (1.7 miles) from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and was probably where its builders camped."],"answer":"Stonehenge","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest burials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3180–2965 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sarsens raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-3rd millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Builders' camp</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2535–2475 BCE, Durrington Walls</span></div>","answerText":"Stonehenge","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Stonehenge</b> is a stone circle on Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire, built and rebuilt over some six centuries, and it is made of two quite different kinds of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The great sarsens are blocks of silcrete: a typical upright runs 6 to 7 metres (20 to 23 feet) including the buried part and weighs about 20 tonnes (22 tons), the tallest standing 9.1 metres (30 feet); of an original 80 or so, 52 remain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifty of those 52 share one chemistry, which matches the sarsens of West Woods in the Marlborough Downs, about 25 kilometres (15 miles) north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The smaller bluestones were raised earlier and come from the Preseli Hills of west Wales, over 200 kilometres (125 miles) away, some of them traced to particular outcrops there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Altar Stone lying at the centre is odder still: a shaped block of 6 tonnes (6.6 tons) whose grains match the Old Red Sandstone of northeast Scotland, at least 750 kilometres (470 miles) off and almost certainly brought by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It was also a graveyard, and from early on: the cremated remains of up to 58 people were dug out of it, which makes it one of the largest Late Neolithic burial sites known in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those burials date between 3180–2965 and 2565–2380 BCE, and of the 25 individuals whose bone could be measured at least 10 had not lived on the chalk, west Wales being the likeliest home — the bluestone country again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The people who raised the sarsens seem to have camped at Durrington Walls, 2.8 kilometres (1.7 miles) away, a settlement of 2535 to 2475 BCE that may have stood for no more than a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its animal bones and the residues in its pots make it a place of feasting, and especially in winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor was the ground empty before any of this, since hunter-gatherers had long gathered at Blick Mead on the edge of it, and whether the monument went up in closed forest or in a clearing that already mattered is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["David J. Nash et al., “Origins of the Sarsen Megaliths at Stonehenge,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 31 (2020): eabc0133, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abc0133. [Open access]","Anthony J. I. Clarke et al., “A Scottish Provenance for the Altar Stone of Stonehenge,” <i>Nature</i> 632, no. 8025 (2024): 570–75, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07652-1. [Open access]","Christophe Snoeck et al., “Strontium Isotope Analysis on Cremated Human Remains from Stonehenge Support Links with West Wales,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 10790, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-28969-8. [Open access]","Piers D. Mitchell et al., “Intestinal Parasites in the Neolithic Population Who Built Stonehenge (Durrington Walls, 2500 BCE),” <i>Parasitology</i> 149, no. 8 (2022): 1027–33, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0031182022000476. [Open access]","Samuel M. Hudson et al., “Life before Stonehenge: The Hunter-Gatherer Occupation and Environment of Blick Mead Revealed by sedaDNA, Pollen and Spores,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0266789, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0266789. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","religion","building"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Stonehenge_Closeup_July_2018.jpg/1920px-Stonehenge_Closeup_July_2018.jpg","title":"Stonehenge","desc":"Standing sarsen uprights with several lintels still resting across them and other stones fallen among drought-browned grass, under a hazy summer sky, a bird perched on one upright and visitors small in the distance. Mball93, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stonehenge_Closeup_July_2018.jpg","alt":"Standing sarsen uprights with several lintels still resting across them and other stones fallen among drought-browned grass, under a hazy summer sky, a bird perched on one upright and visitors small in the distance"},"locator":{"name":"Stonehenge","at":[-1.8261,51.1789]}},
{"id":"wh-140","num":140,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"An arrowhead between his rib cage and left shoulder blade is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died, high in the Ötztal Alps some 5,300 years ago.","questions":["The copper blade of the axe found with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came from ore in southern Tuscany, though Alpine copper was known and worked at the time.","Sequenced at high coverage, the genome of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows no Steppe ancestry at all and the most Anatolian farmer ancestry of any European of his day."],"answer":"Ötzi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3350–3120 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the early Copper Age, 4th millennium BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Ötzi","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ötzi</b> is a man who died high in the Ötztal Alps, on what is now the Italian–Austrian border, between 3350 and 3120 BCE, and whose body froze into a glacier — one of the oldest such mummies known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His gear went into the ice with him, including a hand axe whose wooden shaft can be radiocarbon dated, which makes it one of the few copper objects firmly fixed to the early Copper Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The lead isotopes in that blade point to ore from southern Tuscany, though Alpine copper was known and worked then, so metal was moving a long way and the mountains divided one metal circuit from another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His genome, read at 15-fold coverage, carries no detectable Steppe ancestry and the highest Anatolian-farmer ancestry of any European of his time, which points to a rather isolated Alpine population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also says he had darker skin than Europeans today and carried the variants for male-pattern baldness, which the mummy itself bears out in its pigmentation and bare head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He was killed, and the manner is still being worked out: an arrowhead sits between his rib cage and left shoulder blade, under a deep wound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first reading was that he bled out from a torn artery under the collarbone; a fresh study of the scans finds a pooled clot of about 110 millilitres (4 fluid ounces), so the bleeding need not have killed him at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same work reconstructs the moment: a straight arrow path fits both the notch in the shoulder blade and the tear in the artery, fixing how he stood when it struck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ice that kept him is not much older than he is: a dome on a summit near where he lay has been frozen about 5,900 years, so the high Alps were coming out of nearly ice-free conditions just before his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even his gut survives, holding anaerobic bacteria whose nearest matches today are the gut communities of people living outside the industrialised world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Ke Wang et al., “High-Coverage Genome of the Tyrolean Iceman Reveals Unusually High Anatolian Farmer Ancestry,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 3, no. 9 (2023): 100377, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xgen.2023.100377. [Open access]","Gilberto Artioli et al., “Long-Distance Connections in the Copper Age: New Evidence from the Alpine Iceman's Copper Axe,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 7 (2017): e0179263, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0179263. [Open access]","Chiara Villa et al., “Ötzi the Iceman: Forensic 3D Reconstructions of a 5300-Year-Ago Murder Case,” <i>International Journal of Legal Medicine</i> 139, no. 5 (2025): 2263–71, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00414-025-03510-5. [Open access]","Pascal Bohleber et al., “New Glacier Evidence for Ice-Free Summits during the Life of the Tyrolean Iceman,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 20513, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-77518-9. [Open access]","Mohamed S. Sarhan et al., “The Iceman's Microbiome: Unveiling Millennia of Microbial Diversity and Continuity,” <i>Microbiome</i> 14 (2026): 135, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-026-02417-6. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","prehistory","archaeology","history","genetics","technology"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/%C3%96tztal_Alps.jpg/1920px-%C3%96tztal_Alps.jpg","title":"The Ötztal Alps","desc":"A wide view across the Ötztal Alps from a high ridge: broad glaciers filling the valleys on the left and right, dark rock spurs rising between them, snow-streaked peaks running to the horizon and heaped cumulus overhead. Tiia Monto, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%96tztal_Alps.jpg","alt":"A wide view across the Ötztal Alps from a high ridge: broad glaciers filling the valleys on the left and right, dark rock spurs rising between them, snow-streaked peaks running to the horizon and heaped cumulus overhead"}},
{"id":"wh-141","num":141,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Two routes carried farming across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: one inland through the Balkans and up the Danube, the other along the northern Mediterranean coast by sea.","questions":["More than 90 per cent of the ancestry of the first farmers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> traces to a single Anatolian source, so the change was a movement of people.","Around 3000 BCE the farming societies of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> faltered: megalith building stopped, numbers fell, and at Bury near Paris one population gave way to another."],"answer":"Neolithic Europe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Spread</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6600 – 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Decline</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic Europe","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Neolithic Europe</b> begins with the movement of farmers out of western Anatolia and the Aegean about 8,600 years ago, into a continent where everyone still lived by hunting and gathering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Farming travelled by two routes: an inland one through the Balkans and up the Danube and the Rhine, and a sea route along the northern Mediterranean coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two moved at different speeds, pioneering households shifting about 50 kilometres (31 miles) a generation inland against 70 (43 miles) along the coast, and the whole spread ran until roughly 4000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a movement of people rather than of ideas: over 90 per cent of the ancestry of Europe's early farmers traces to one Anatolian source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor was the advance steady, since it stalled for centuries at several frontiers, Britain, Sweden and the middle Danube among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Farmers and foragers then lived alongside each other for a long time — more than 3,000 years between the Balkans and Denmark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mixing was rarer than that overlap suggests: on both routes alike about 3.6 per cent of farmers took a hunter-gatherer partner or brought one into the farming life, so geography changed how far people moved but not how they behaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did grow with time, the local share of hunter-gatherer ancestry rising at each stage of the inland advance, and the incomers outnumbered the foragers about five to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 10 and 15 per cent of the ancestry of Europeans today is hunter-gatherer, the residue of that slow absorption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Numbers also rose and crashed repeatedly, on a rhythm climate change cannot account for and crowding-driven conflict can;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> around 3000 BCE megalith building stopped, and at Bury near Paris one population gave way to another amid forest regrowth and plague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Alexandros Tsoupas et al., “Local Increases in Admixture with Hunter-Gatherers Followed the Initial Expansion of Neolithic Farmers across Continental Europe,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 34 (2025): eadq9976, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adq9976. [Open access]","Joaquim Fort and Joaquim Pérez-Losada, “Interbreeding between Farmers and Hunter-Gatherers along the Inland and Mediterranean Routes of Neolithic Spread in Europe,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 7032, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-51335-4. [Open access]","Troy M. LaPolice, Matthew P. Williams, and Christian D. Huber, “Modeling the European Neolithic Expansion Suggests Predominant Within-Group Mating and Limited Cultural Transmission,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 7905, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63172-0. [Open access]","Dániel Kondor et al., “Explaining Population Booms and Busts in Mid-Holocene Europe,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 9310, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-35920-z. [Open access]","Frederik Valeur Seersholm et al., “Population Discontinuity in the Paris Basin Linked to Evidence of the Neolithic Decline,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 10 (2026): 677–88, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-026-03027-z. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","genetics","migration"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Expansion_of_farming_in_western_Eurasia%2C_9600%E2%80%934000_BCE.png/1920px-Expansion_of_farming_in_western_Eurasia%2C_9600%E2%80%934000_BCE.png","title":"The expansion of farming in western Eurasia, 9600–4000 BCE","desc":"A relief map from the Levant to Ireland, coloured in bands that spread outwards from a dark red core in the Fertile Crescent through orange in the Balkans to green across central Europe, blue along the Mediterranean and yellow in Britain, Ireland and Denmark, with dashed arrows tracing the routes and a calendar year against each band. Gronenborn, Horejs, Börner and Ober, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expansion_of_farming_in_western_Eurasia,_9600%E2%80%934000_BCE.png","alt":"A relief map from the Levant to Ireland, coloured in bands that spread outwards from a dark red core in the Fertile Crescent through orange in the Balkans to green across central Europe, blue along the Mediterranean and yellow in Britain, Ireland and Denmark, with dashed arrows tracing the routes and a calendar year against each band"}},
{"id":"wh-142","num":142,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The stone dresser, the slabbed beds and the hearth still standing inside the houses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survive because the fittings there were built of stone rather than of wood.","questions":["Burnt vole and mouse bones in the household waste at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the earliest evidence in Europe of Neolithic people eating rodents or keeping them down as pests.","Founded around 2900 BCE and settled again in the 28th century, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands on the Bay of Skaill in Orkney with its houses banked about by their own midden."],"answer":"Skara Brae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2900 – 2400 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Skara Brae","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Skara Brae</b> is a village of stone houses on the Bay of Skaill in Orkney, lived in through the late 4th and early 3rd millennia BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The houses are joined to one another and banked about with thick midden — the settlement's own household waste — which held the walls and made the whole village one low mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ways between them were roofed with lintels and buried under that same midden, so people passed from house to house under cover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fittings inside were built of stone rather than wood and have therefore survived: a dresser of shelves, beds framed in slabs, and a hearth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Drains run beneath the floors, laid to keep rainwater and soakage out of the houses rather than to carry sewage away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The bones in those middens are almost all food: cattle above all, sheep in quantity, pig rarely and red deer hardly at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people also took the small mammals living among them, and burnt vole and mouse bones lie in the household waste in numbers that only deliberate collection explains — the earliest evidence in Europe of Neolithic people eating rodents or keeping them down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both animals had been carried to the islands by people, and the vole, cut off there for more than 5,000 years, grew to an exceptional size on a genome that lost most of its diversity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The village belongs to an Orkney that from about 3200 BCE was the most crowded and inventive corner of Britain, sending new monuments and pottery styles south through Britain and Ireland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Founded in the centuries around 2900 BCE, it seems to have been left after a short time and settled again in the 28th century BCE; around 2800 BCE the heart of Neolithic Orkney fell quiet, and its villages were finally abandoned in the 24th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Alex Bayliss et al., “Islands of History: The Late Neolithic Timescape of Orkney,” <i>Antiquity</i> 91, no. 359 (2017): 1171–88, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.140. [Open access]","V. Gordon Childe, D. M. S. Watson, and A. Robinson, “Final Report on the Operations at Skara Brae,” <i>Proceedings of the Society of Antiquaries of Scotland</i> 65 (1931): 27–77, https://doi.org/10.9750/PSAS.065.27.77. [Open access]","Andrzej A. Romaniuk et al., “Rodents: Food or Pests in Neolithic Orkney,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 3, no. 10 (2016): 160514, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.160514. [Open access]","Katharina Dulias et al., “Ancient DNA at the Edge of the World: Continental Immigration and the Persistence of Neolithic Male Lineages in Bronze Age Orkney,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 8 (2022): e2108001119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2108001119. [Open access]","Xuejing Wang and Gerald Heckel, “Genome-Wide Relaxation of Selection and the Evolution of the Island Syndrome in Orkney Voles,” <i>Genome Research</i> 34, no. 6 (2024): 851–62, https://doi.org/10.1101/gr.278487.123. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","building","nature"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Skara_Brae_house_1.jpg/1920px-Skara_Brae_house_1.jpg","title":"House 1 at Skara Brae","desc":"An excavated drystone house seen from above: curving walls of thin flat slabs standing chest-high, two low square openings into side cells, upright slabs framing bed spaces on the sand floor, grass along the rim and visitors' feet at the top edge. Wknight94, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skara_Brae_house_1.jpg","alt":"An excavated drystone house seen from above: curving walls of thin flat slabs standing chest-high, two low square openings into side cells, upright slabs framing bed spaces on the sand floor, grass along the rim and visitors' feet at the top edge"},"locator":{"name":"Skara Brae","at":[-3.3417,59.0487]}},
{"id":"wh-143","num":143,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The passage at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is aligned on the solstice sunrise, and the chamber it lights is a cruciform one of three recesses inside a mound of 200,000 tonnes.","questions":["A skull fragment from the chamber of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from a man whose parents were full siblings or a parent and child, was read in 2020 as evidence of a dynastic elite.","Built above the River Boyne about 3200 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one of the developed Irish passage tombs raised well after the first farmers' timber houses."],"answer":"Newgrange","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3200 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chamber bone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3340 – 3020 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Newgrange","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Newgrange</b> is a passage tomb above the River Boyne in eastern Ireland, built and used in the later Neolithic, about 3200 to 3000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A passage tomb is a stone-lined passage running in to a chamber at its end under a round mound, usually ringed by a kerb; this one holds more than 200,000 tonnes of earth and stone, and its chamber is cruciform, with three recesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The passage is aligned on the solstice sunrise, and the chamber it lights would have held only a few people at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Farming had reached the island by sea centuries earlier, in an influx that left the first Irish farmers with genomes of chiefly Near Eastern origin, and its opening burst — a horizon of rectangular timber houses — falls between 3750 and 3600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Newgrange belongs to the developed passage tombs raised after 3400 BCE, so the great monuments come well after that boom rather than with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Genomes from megaliths along the Atlantic coast show close kin buried together, more men than women in the British Isles, and the same male lines recurring, which points to patrilineal kindreds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Ireland that kinship reaches between monuments as well as within them, so people buried in one tomb were related to those in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A fragment of skull from the Newgrange chamber, dated 3340 to 3020 BCE, is that of an adult man whose parents were either full siblings or a parent and child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Such unions are recorded almost only among ruling families, so his genome was read as evidence of a dynastic elite; that reading is now disputed, one case being too little to show whether the practice was routine or even known of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chamber was dug over for treasure after its rediscovery in 1699 and the layer the bone came from held at least five people, and the critics read the shared ancestry as a reordering of social relations after 3600 BCE rather than a line of rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lara M. Cassidy et al., “A Dynastic Elite in Monumental Neolithic Society,” <i>Nature</i> 582, no. 7812 (2020): 384–88, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2378-6. [Open access]","Jessica Smyth et al., “The ‘King’ of Newgrange? A Critical Analysis of a Neolithic Petrous Fragment from the Passage Tomb Chamber,” <i>Antiquity</i> 99, no. 405 (2025): 672–88, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2025.63. [Open access]","T. Rowan McLaughlin et al., “The Changing Face of Neolithic and Bronze Age Ireland: A Big Data Approach to the Settlement and Burial Records,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 29 (2016): 117–53, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-016-9093-0. [Open access]","Federico Sánchez-Quinto et al., “Megalithic Tombs in Western and Northern Neolithic Europe Were Linked to a Kindred Society,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 19 (2019): 9469–74, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1818037116. [Open access]","Lara M. Cassidy et al., “Neolithic and Bronze Age Migration to Ireland and Establishment of the Insular Atlantic Genome,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 2 (2016): 368–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4720318/. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","prehistory","archaeology","history","genetics","religion"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Newgrange_entrance_stone.jpg/1920px-Newgrange_entrance_stone.jpg","title":"The entrance stone at Newgrange","desc":"A long kerbstone lying on its side, its whole face pecked with linked spirals and lozenges, a vertical groove dividing it, with the dark opening of the passage and its lintel in the wall behind. Jal74, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newgrange_entrance_stone.jpg","alt":"A long kerbstone lying on its side, its whole face pecked with linked spirals and lozenges, a vertical groove dividing it, with the dark opening of the passage and its lintel in the wall behind"}},
{"id":"wh-144","num":144,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"A third of all the gold from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from one grave, number 43, and another third from five rich burials that held no body at all.","questions":["Computer tomography has shown that the celebrated “mask graves” of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held no masks but clay figures about half life size, crushed flat by the earth.","In use for some 250 years between 4590 and 4340 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is usually read as the first clear evidence of ranked society in Europe."],"answer":"Varna necropolis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4590 – 4340 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Complex ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4250 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Varna necropolis","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Varna necropolis</b> is a Copper Age cemetery on the Black Sea coast of Bulgaria, in use for about 250 years between 4590 and 4340 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 270 burials are known, and what was put in them is richer than anything else of that date in the Old World.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its gold is very unevenly spread: a third of all the gold objects come from a single grave, number 43, and a further third from five rich symbolic graves and deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A great many of the graves held no body at all, and there are so many of these that the cemetery probably served several settlements rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three of them contained a human head modelled in clay, and computer tomography has shown that the celebrated “mask graves” are nothing of the kind: what was buried was a figure about half life size, crushed flat by the weight of earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The people buried there belonged to the Kodžadermen-Gumelniţa-Karanovo VI complex, which at its widest around 4500 BCE reached from southern Ukraine to northern Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their genomes are those of the local Copper Age population, so the wealth was not carried in by newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Metal was being pushed hard across the same region at the same time, and crucibles from the Lower Danube show lead ore smelted around 4400 to 4300 BCE, the earliest such experiment in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Varna is usually read as the first clear evidence of ranked society in Europe;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> that reading is argued with, since across five millennia in the Carpathian Basin the potential for inequality was rarely realised for long, and putting wealth in a grave is itself a way of taking it out of circulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The complex thinned and then vanished around 4250 BCE, its settlements abandoned, and the cemetery had gone out of use a century or so before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Raiko Krauß et al., “Chronology and Development of the Chalcolithic Necropolis of Varna I,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 44 (2017): 282–305, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.44.17. [Open access]","Sandra Penske et al., “Early Contact between Late Farming and Pastoralist Societies in Southeastern Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 620, no. 7973 (2023): 358–65, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06334-8. [Open access]","Svend Hansen et al., “The Earliest Lead Ore Processing in Europe: 5th Millennium BC Finds from Pietrele on the Lower Danube,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 4 (2019): e0214218, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0214218. [Open access]","Aurélien Tafani et al., “Tracing Chalcolithic Population Mobility Using Strontium Isotopes and Proteomics at Gumelniţa Site, Romania,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 23002, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-05671-0. [Open access]","Paul R. Duffy et al., “Five Thousand Years of Inequality in the Carpathian Basin,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 32 (2025): eadu0323, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adu0323. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","technology","art"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Varna_Chalcolithic_necropolis.jpg/1920px-Varna_Chalcolithic_necropolis.jpg","title":"Grave 1 of the Varna necropolis, a symbolic burial","desc":"A museum case holding the contents of one grave: gold rings, beads, crescents and a beaten gold sheet pinned out on a white board in the position they were worn, two heavy gold bowls and a gold-hafted axe below, strings of shell beads, flint blades, and two trays of excavated earth at the left. Ivano Giambattista, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varna_Chalcolithic_necropolis.jpg","alt":"A museum case holding the contents of one grave: gold rings, beads, crescents and a beaten gold sheet pinned out on a white board in the position they were worn, two heavy gold bowls and a gold-hafted axe below, strings of shell beads, flint blades, and two trays of excavated earth at the left"},"locator":{"name":"Varna necropolis","at":[27.8644,43.2131]}},
{"id":"wh-145","num":145,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The oldest known copper smelting, slag from Belovode in eastern Serbia about 5000 BCE, falls early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the phase between the Neolithic and the Bronze Age.","questions":["The tin bronze of the Balkan <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was nobody's invention: it came out of smelting ores that already carried copper and tin together.","Because the label follows local sequences, the centuries called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Balkans are still called Neolithic in southern Scandinavia, which the copper reached from Serbian mines."],"answer":"Chalcolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Balkans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6200 – 3700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest smelting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Chalcolithic","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Chalcolithic</b>, or Copper Age, is the phase between the Neolithic and the Bronze Age in which copper was worked; in the Balkans, where it is best studied, it runs from the first use of copper minerals about 6200 BCE to about 3700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a stage every society passed through, since metallurgy followed no single path, so the name is used where copper mattered and its dates differ by thousands of years from region to region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest known copper smelting is slag from Belovode in eastern Serbia, dated to about 5000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tin bronze of those centuries was nobody's invention: it came out of smelting ores that already carried copper and tin together, rather than from deliberately mixing two metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the southern Levant the earliest metal is a copper awl of the late 6th millennium BCE, buried at Tel Tsaf and made from ore that probably came from the Caucasus, so the technique arrived by exchange long before anyone there made metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The rich hoards that make that region a centre of early metallurgy, Nahal Mishmar above all, belong to the second half of the 5th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lost-wax casting is older still and further east: the earliest object made that way is a copper amulet some 6,000 years old from Mehrgarh in Baluchistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Metal travelled a long way from its sources, and copper from Serbian mines reached the north European plain and southern Scandinavia between about 4100 and 3300 BCE, where those same objects are called Neolithic rather than Chalcolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether metalworking was invented once and carried outwards or arrived several times over was argued for a century, and radiocarbon dating undid the older answer by showing early European sites to be older than the Near Eastern ones they were derived from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The idea that metal made the elite has weakened with it, since in the Balkans metal production seems to follow how communities were organised rather than to have built a hierarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Miljana Radivojević and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Early Balkan Metallurgy: Origins, Evolution and Society, 6200–3700 BC,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 34 (2021): 195–278, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-021-09155-7. [Open access]","Christopher P. Thornton and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Introduction: The Beginnings of Metallurgy in Global Perspective,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 22 (2009): 181–84, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-009-9026-2. [Open access]","Yosef Garfinkel et al., “The Beginning of Metallurgy in the Southern Levant: A Late 6th Millennium CalBC Copper Awl from Tel Tsaf, Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 3 (2014): e92591, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0092591. [Open access]","Mathieu Thoury et al., “High Spatial Dynamics-Photoluminescence Imaging Reveals the Metallurgy of the Earliest Lost-Wax Cast Object,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 7 (2016): 13356, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms13356. [Open access]","Jan Piet Brozio et al., “The Origin of Neolithic Copper on the Central Northern European Plain and in Southern Scandinavia: Connectivities on a European Scale,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 5 (2023): e0283007, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0283007. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","technology","science"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Copper_from_Hoard_of_Ritual_Objects%2C_Judean_Desert%2C_4500-3500_BC_%2828346798417%29.jpg/1920px-Copper_from_Hoard_of_Ritual_Objects%2C_Judean_Desert%2C_4500-3500_BC_%2828346798417%29.jpg","title":"Copper objects from the Judean Desert hoard, 4500–3500 BCE","desc":"Two large limestone and basalt mortars from Natufian Eynan, each too heavy to be carried about, in the Israel Museum. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copper_from_Hoard_of_Ritual_Objects,_Judean_Desert,_4500-3500_BC_(28346798417).jpg","alt":"Eight small dark copper vessels and mace-heads standing on a museum shelf against pale marble, each cast in a different shape — spiralled, ribbed, plain-necked, one with a flat disc collar — their surfaces smooth and glinting"}},
{"id":"wh-146","num":146,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The first step in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needs no fire at all: native copper and gold can be picked up as nuggets and hammered cold into shape.","questions":["Smelting is the threshold in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since it makes metal that was never metal in nature and wants about 1100 °C (2000 °F), which five or six blowpipes can reach.","Cold-hammered gold in a burial at Jiskairumoko shows that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began among low-level food producers, without hereditary elites or a farming surplus behind it."],"answer":"early metallurgy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Copper beads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 6200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Andean gold</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2155 – 1936 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"early metallurgy","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Early metallurgy</b> begins before metal does: the first use of copper in the Balkans, from about 6200 BCE, is malachite and azurite ground into beads, minerals picked for their green and blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Working metal itself comes next and needs no fire either, since copper and gold occur native and can be hammered cold into shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest worked gold in the Americas is exactly that: cold-hammered native gold from a burial at Jiskairumoko in the Lake Titicaca basin, dated between 2155 and 1936 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its owners were low-level food producers, and the finding that matters there is a negative one — neither hereditary elites nor a farming surplus is needed before metalworking begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smelting is the real threshold, because it makes metal that was never metal in nature, and it wants around 1100 to 1200 °C (2000 to 2200 °F), which five or six blowpipes will reach without any furnace of the later kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The two uses of copper stayed apart for a long time, and in the Balkans the minerals chosen for beads are distinct from those chosen for smelting, a separation not yet identified in Anatolia or the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What early metal is mostly found doing is marking somebody out: the earliest metal object in the southern Levant, a small copper awl, was buried with a woman whose grave set her apart from her neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lead came early too, and an object of the late 5th millennium BCE from a cave in the Negev is almost pure lead, smelted from ore in the Taurus mountains and probably used as a spindle whorl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of these steps follows from the one before, and metallurgy took no single developmental path from one society to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The whole chain is now studied together — mining, fuel, smelting, casting, use and recycling — and the old question of whether metalworking was invented once has given way to asking how many times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mark Aldenderfer et al., “Four-Thousand-Year-Old Gold Artifacts from the Lake Titicaca Basin, Southern Peru,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 13 (2008): 5002–5, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0710937105. [Open access]","Miljana Radivojević and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Early Balkan Metallurgy: Origins, Evolution and Society, 6200–3700 BC,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 34 (2021): 195–278, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-021-09155-7. [Open access]","Yosef Garfinkel et al., “The Beginning of Metallurgy in the Southern Levant: A Late 6th Millennium CalBC Copper Awl from Tel Tsaf, Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 3 (2014): e92591, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0092591. [Open access]","Naama Yahalom-Mack et al., “The Earliest Lead Object in the Levant,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 12 (2015): e0142948, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0142948. [Open access]","Christopher P. Thornton and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Introduction: The Beginnings of Metallurgy in Global Perspective,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 22 (2009): 181–84, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-009-9026-2. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","prehistory","archaeology","history","technology","science"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/1280px-NatCopper.jpg","title":"Native copper","desc":"A knobbly mass of bright metallic copper photographed against white, grown into branching lumps and rounded buds with holes through it, its surface pitted and reflecting warm orange highlights. Jonathan Zander, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NatCopper.jpg","alt":"A knobbly mass of bright metallic copper photographed against white, grown into branching lumps and rounded buds with holes through it, its surface pitted and reflecting warm orange highlights"}},
{"id":"wh-147","num":147,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Henan alone holds more than 3,000 sites of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose villages, painted pottery and millet fields spread from the middle Yellow River into Gansu, Hubei, Sichuan and beyond.","questions":["Genomes from twelve burials at Zhanmatun match those of the core region, which is why the spread of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read as a movement of people rather than of ideas alone.","Raised nitrogen in millet grains shows that the fields of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were manured, most likely with pig dung, on loess soils that carry very little nitrogen of their own."],"answer":"Yangshao culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Yangshao</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3500 – 3000 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Yangshao culture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Yangshao culture</b> is the great Neolithic culture of the middle Yellow River, flourishing roughly between 5000 and 3000 BCE across what are now the provinces of Henan, Shaanxi and Shanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its scale is the first thing to grasp: Henan alone holds more than 3,000 Yangshao sites, and its reach crossed the middle Yellow River into Hebei, Gansu, Qinghai, Hubei, Ningxia and Sichuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What marks it out is a settled way of living — villages that stayed put, painted pottery, domestic pigs and dogs, and dryland farming of broomcorn and foxtail millet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The farming was not everything at first, and carbon isotopes from early sites such as Banpo point to a mixed diet in which wild plants still counted for a good deal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pigs were fed on millet, and on more of it as the centuries passed, until at Xipo it dominated their diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Loess carries very little nitrogen of its own, so the raised nitrogen in millet grains of 5500 to 3500 years ago is evidence that the fields were manured, most likely with dung from those same pigs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Villages grew with the yields: the largest covered 10 to 12 hectares (25 to 30 acres) before 5000 BCE and as much as 70 (170 acres) by about 4000, and by the late Yangshao of 3500 to 3000 BCE regional centres stood over lesser settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Drink belonged to it too: vessels carry the residues of a fermentation starter made with Monascus mould, and people dancing in a ring are painted on a house floor at Dadiwan and on pottery basins further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes from twelve burials at Zhanmatun, a site of one of its regional variants, are indistinguishable from Yangshao people of the core, which makes the spread a movement of people and not only of ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What came of it outlasts the pottery: Yangshao ancestry is one of the roots of East Asians today, and the first splitting of the Sino-Tibetan languages falls with the culture's push west and southwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lei Sun et al., “The Demic Expansion of Yangshao Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 186, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12218936/. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens, Yijie Zhuang and Dorian Q. Fuller, “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Xin Wang et al., “Millet Manuring as a Driving Force for the Late Neolithic Agricultural Expansion of North China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 5552, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-23315-4. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “Archaeological Evidence for Initial Migration of Neolithic Proto Sino-Tibetan Speakers from Yellow River Valley to Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 51 (2022): e2212006119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9907151/. [Open access]","Quan Zhang et al., “Stable Isotopes Reveal Intensive Pig Husbandry Practices in the Middle Yellow River Region by the Yangshao Period (7000–5000 BP),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257524, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257524. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","china"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/%E4%BA%BA%E9%9D%A2%E9%B1%BC%E7%BA%B9%E5%BD%A2%E5%BD%A9%E9%99%B6%E7%9B%8608737.jpg/1920px-%E4%BA%BA%E9%9D%A2%E9%B1%BC%E7%BA%B9%E5%BD%A2%E5%BD%A9%E9%99%B6%E7%9B%8608737.jpg","title":"Painted basin with human faces and fish, Banpo, Yangshao culture","desc":"Looking down into a wide orange-red earthenware basin, mended from sherds with pale fills, painted inside in black with two round human faces in pointed caps, a small fish at either side of each mouth, and two whole fish drawn between them. Augusthaiho, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BA%BA%E9%9D%A2%E9%B1%BC%E7%BA%B9%E5%BD%A2%E5%BD%A9%E9%99%B6%E7%9B%8608737.jpg","alt":"Looking down into a wide orange-red earthenware basin, mended from sherds with pale fills, painted inside in black with two round human faces in pointed caps, a small fish at either side of each mouth, and two whole fish drawn between them"},"locator":{"name":"The Yangshao culture","at":[109.1,36.3],"kind":"region","area":[[105,35.6],[107,37.85],[110.5,38.5],[113.5,38],[115.5,36],[115,33.8],[112,32.8],[108.5,33.4],[105.5,34.2]]}},
{"id":"wh-148","num":148,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Cattle reached the Central Plains during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and their shoulder blades were soon being heated until they cracked so that the cracks could be read.","questions":["Thin black pots turned on the wheel and walled towns such as Pingliangtai mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ran across the Yellow River plains from about 5,000 to 4,000 years ago.","Diets across northern China turned from millet alone to a mixture during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and tooth decay rose sharply with it, at about the time of an abrupt global climate event."],"answer":"Longshan culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 – 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cattle arrive</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2250 – 1950 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Longshan culture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Longshan culture</b> is the name given to the late Neolithic of the Yellow River plains, the period running from about 5,000 to 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is known first by its pots: thin black wares, polished and standardised and carrying the marks of the wheel, made alongside the grey and brown vessels of daily use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its settlements were walled, and Pingliangtai in Henan is one of them, standing among seasonally flooded wetlands at the close of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were also numerous: in the Songshan hills, a core area of early Chinese civilisation, 660 sites belong to these thousand years against 563 for the whole of the much longer Yangshao period before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cattle reached the Central Plains only now, between about 4,200 and 3,900 years ago, and brought with them a new use for a shoulder blade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The bone was heated until it cracked and the cracks were read as an answer, and scapulae worked that way have been found at Taosi and Zhoujiazhuang, long before the inscribed oracle bones of the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whole cattle were buried at Shantaisi and at Pingliangtai, so the animal carried a weight beyond meat across the plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What people ate was changing too: across northern and northwestern China the diet turned from millet alone to a mixture that included wheat, barley or rice at around 4,500 to 4,000 years ago, and tooth decay rose sharply thereafter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That turn falls on the abrupt global climate event of 4,200 years ago, which is why it is read as a response to a changing world rather than a change of taste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The largest settlement of these centuries stands outside the Longshan heartland: Shimao, on the Loess Plateau, a stone-walled town of some 4 square kilometres (1.5 square miles) whose tombs give pedigrees four generations deep down the male line, and whose east gate covered more than 80 human skulls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Linyu Xia et al., “Compositional Analysis of Longshan Period Pottery and Ceramic Raw Materials in the Yongcheng Region, Henan Province,” <i>Materials</i> 18, no. 12 (2025): 2681, https://doi.org/10.3390/ma18122681. [Open access]","Christina Cheung et al., “Stable Isotope and Dental Caries Data Reveal Abrupt Changes in Subsistence Economy in Ancient China in Response to Global Climate Change,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 7 (2019): e0218943, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218943. [Open access]","Xiaochen Pei et al., “The Dynamics of Bovini Exploitation Strategies on the Central Plains of China from the Middle Neolithic to the Bronze Age,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 29616, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-81065-y. [Open access]","Zehui Chen et al., “Ancient DNA from Shimao City Records Kinship Practices in Neolithic China,” <i>Nature</i> 648, no. 8094 (2025): 659–67, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09799-x. [Open access]","Lijie Yan et al., “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Clustering Patterns of Settlements from the Neolithic to the Bronze Age (9000–3000 BP) in the Songshan Mountain Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 7 (2026): e0351644, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0351644. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","technology"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Cup_with_high_handle%2C_China%2C_Shandong_province%2C_Longshan_culture%2C_c._2500-2000_BC%2C_earthenware%2C_openwork_%28eggshell_type%29_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco%2C_CA_-_DSC07691.jpg/1920px-Cup_with_high_handle%2C_China%2C_Shandong_province%2C_Longshan_culture%2C_c._2500-2000_BC%2C_earthenware%2C_openwork_%28eggshell_type%29_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco%2C_CA_-_DSC07691.jpg","title":"Eggshell-ware stemmed cup, Shandong, Longshan culture, c. 2500–2000 BCE","desc":"A tall dark goblet standing against a grey wall: a wide flaring bowl on a slender neck above a swelling barrel-shaped stem cut through with rows of narrow vertical slits, on a spreading foot, its near-black surface patched with pale encrustation. Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cup_with_high_handle,_China,_Shandong_province,_Longshan_culture,_c._2500-2000_BC,_earthenware,_openwork_(eggshell_type)_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco,_CA_-_DSC07691.jpg","alt":"A tall dark goblet standing against a grey wall: a wide flaring bowl on a slender neck above a swelling barrel-shaped stem cut through with rows of narrow vertical slits, on a spreading foot, its near-black surface patched with pale encrustation"}},
{"id":"wh-149","num":149,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted more than ten thousand years without farming, and ended only when rice and millet growers crossed from Korea about 3,000 years ago.","questions":["Shell mounds ring the coasts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and at Kawaji the share of sea food in the diet climbs through its later phases before falling away under the newcomers.","The lineage of a man buried at Ikawazu during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> parts from mainland East Asians earlier than East Asians and Native Americans part from one another."],"answer":"Jōmon period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 16,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Farming arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,000 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"Jōmon period","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Jōmon period</b> is the long hunter-fisher-gatherer age of the Japanese archipelago, beginning more than 16,000 years ago and running for over ten thousand years without farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its people are read as the direct descendants of those who carried Upper Palaeolithic stone tools into the islands some 38,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genome of a man buried at Ikawazu bears that out: his lineage parts from mainland East Asians earlier than East Asians and Native Americans part from each other, which is put at about 26,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sea was the mainstay, and shell mounds ring the coasts; in the burials at Kawaji on the Atsumi peninsula the share of marine food climbs through the later Jomon and falls away again in the centuries after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those same people knocked teeth out deliberately as a rite, and which teeth went changed from one phase to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Plants counted too: proteins from a soybean and from a nut of the walnut family survive in the charred crust inside a pot from Shimoyakebe, dated to about 2,900 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By then the population was large and settled, with hunting, foraging and cultivation all long established, so the farmers who crossed from Korea from about 3,000 years ago met a full country rather than an empty one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fats soaked into 260 pots say the meeting changed cooking only slowly: a marker for broomcorn millet turns up in the Korean vessels and in none of the Japanese ones, and food from the water went on being cooked in the new continental shapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jomon bones are hard to sequence, because a warm wet climate and acid volcanic soils destroy DNA everywhere but the far north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people did not vanish with the period: the ancestry of Japanese today is modelled as three strands, one of them Jomon, and how much of it a person carries varies across the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Takashi Gakuhari et al., “Ancient Jomon Genome Sequence Analysis Sheds Light on Migration Patterns of Early East Asian Populations,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 3 (2020): 437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-020-01162-2. [Open access]","Oliver E. Craig et al., “Lipid Residue Analysis Reveals Divergent Culinary Practices in Japan and Korea at the Dawn of Intensive Agriculture,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 30 (2025): e2504414122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2504414122. [Open access]","Soichiro Kusaka and Yasuhiro Yamada, “Temporal Changes in Diet and Mortuary Practices among the Jomon People Based on Human Skeletal Remains Excavated from the Kawaji Shell Mound in Aichi Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 133, no. 2 (2025): 79–88, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.250522. [Open access]","Takumi Nishiuchi et al., “Paleoproteomics of Pottery Food Residues Reveals Plant Cupin Superfamily Proteins Preserved for Thousands of Years,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 5 (2026): 115637, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.115637. [Open access]","Kenichi Yamamoto et al., “Genetic Legacy of Ancient Hunter-Gatherer Jomon in Japanese Populations,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9780, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-54052-0. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","japan","genetics"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Spouted_vessel_with_cord_marks%2C_Japan%2C_Hokkaido%2C_final_Jomon_period%2C_c._1000_BC%2C_earthenware_-_Peabody_Essex_Museum_-_DSC07535.jpg/1920px-Spouted_vessel_with_cord_marks%2C_Japan%2C_Hokkaido%2C_final_Jomon_period%2C_c._1000_BC%2C_earthenware_-_Peabody_Essex_Museum_-_DSC07535.jpg","title":"Spouted vessel with cord marks, Hokkaido, final Jōmon period, c. 1000 BCE","desc":"A tall dark goblet standing against a grey wall: a wide flaring bowl on a slender neck above a swelling barrel-shaped stem cut through with rows of narrow vertical slits, on a spreading foot, its near-black surface patched with pale encrustation. Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spouted_vessel_with_cord_marks,_Japan,_Hokkaido,_final_Jomon_period,_c._1000_BC,_earthenware_-_Peabody_Essex_Museum_-_DSC07535.jpg","alt":"A squat brown earthenware pot on a pale museum surface, a narrow upright neck rising from a broad shouldered body covered in raised curling bands, with a short tubular spout at one side and a row of knobbed studs around the lower edge"}},
{"id":"wh-150","num":150,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Radiocarbon on tooth enamel from 23 burials puts the aceramic cemetery at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 5200 and 4900 BCE, three thousand years later than the chronology it replaces.","questions":["Barley is more than nine tenths of the grain found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where it was taken in hand locally while the wheat beside it came from the west.","Zebu cattle were domesticated from the Indian aurochs quite separately from the cattle of the Fertile Crescent, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Balochistan is the reference point for that second event."],"answer":"Mehrgarh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemetery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5200 – 4900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pottery from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-5th millennium BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Mehrgarh","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mehrgarh</b> is a Neolithic complex on the Bolan River in Balochistan, Pakistan, and the only site in the north-west Indian subcontinent whose Neolithic deposits have been dug on any scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is usually named as the earliest evidence of farming in the subcontinent, and it stands on an alluvial fan in the hilly flanks rather than out on a river plain, water being what decided where people could live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its farming was half brought in and half its own: sheep, goats and wheat came from the west, while barley and zebu cattle were taken in hand locally, and barley is more than nine tenths of the grain recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That second half matters far beyond one site, because cattle were domesticated twice from two different wild oxen — taurine cattle in the Fertile Crescent and zebu from the Indian aurochs — and Mehrgarh is the reference point for the zebu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The imported crops came a long way, and a model of the eastward spread of wheat and barley slows most at the Zagros mountains, where the ground and the climate made the crossing hardest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The settlement itself is nine or ten metres (30 to 33 feet) of stacked mudbrick, houses built and abandoned, their ruins used for rubbish and for burial, then built over again, nine times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its dates have lately been overturned: radiocarbon on tooth enamel from 23 burials puts the aceramic cemetery between 5200 and 4900 BCE, lasting two to five centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronology it replaces began about 8000 BCE and ran for three thousand years, so the place has lost most of its depth in time while keeping every metre of its depth in earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the new dates farming reached the Indus valley late and by farmers moving in rather than beginning there, and pottery in what is now Pakistan starts no earlier than the middle of the 5th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing found so far ties the Neolithic of Mehrgarh to the Neolithic of China or of northern India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Benjamin Mutin et al., “New Radiocarbon Dates of Human Tooth Enamel Reveal a Late Appearance of Farming Life in the Indus Valley,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 11345, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-92621-5. [Open access]","Kavita Gangal, Graeme R. Sarson and Anvar Shukurov, “The Near-Eastern Roots of the Neolithic in South Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 5 (2014): e95714, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0095714. [Open access]","J. Bates et al., “Early to Mid-Holocene Land Use Transitions in South Asia: A New Archaeological Synthesis of Potential Human Impacts,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 2 (2025): e0313409, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0313409. [Open access]","Jonas Gregorio de Souza et al., “Environmental Effects on the Spread of the Neolithic Crop Package to South Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 7 (2022): e0268482, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0268482. [Open access]","Yiduan Liu et al., “The Genomic Landscape of Cattle: Domestication, Dispersal, and Adaptive Evolution,” <i>Animals</i> 16, no. 5 (2026): 776, https://doi.org/10.3390/ani16050776. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","pakistan"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Mehrgarh.jpg/1920px-Mehrgarh.jpg","title":"The mound at Mehrgarh, Balochistan","desc":"A pale eroded cliff of mudbrick and silt at an archaeological mound, cut into blocks and deep recesses with horizontal bedding lines running through it, cobbles washed out at its foot and sparse scrub along the top under a bleached sky. mhtoori, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mehrgarh.jpg","alt":"A pale eroded cliff of mudbrick and silt at an archaeological mound, cut into blocks and deep recesses with horizontal bedding lines running through it, cobbles washed out at its foot and sparse scrub along the top under a bleached sky"},"locator":{"name":"Mehrgarh","at":[67.6167,29.3833]}},
{"id":"gr-251","num":251,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Aristotle thought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> should have been paid for at public cost as in Crete, since a Spartan too poor to contribute lost his rights of citizenship.","questions":["Plutarch has candidates for one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> balloted on with pellets of soft bread dropped into a bowl, a pellet squeezed flat counting as a vote against.","Xenophon says the Spartans had once eaten privately at home, and that Lycurgus dragged them into the open by inventing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the ages were deliberately blended."],"answer":"syssitia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>syssitia</b> were the compulsory common meals at which the adult male citizens of Sparta ate together, and Herodotus lists them among the institutions of war that Lycurgus was said to have founded, beside the <i>enomotiai</i> and <i>triekades</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon says the Spartans had once messed privately at home, and that Lycurgus, tracing more than half their misdemeanours to that custom, dragged them out of holes and corners into the broad daylight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His ordinance allowed food enough to guard against want without inducing repletion, and stopped unnecessary drinking while leaving men free to quench thirst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ages were blended so that the young learned from their elders, and the talk turned by custom on honourable acts done for the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the meal broke up they walked home in the dark without a torch, having to move as freely by night as by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch says the Cretans called them <i>andreia</i> and the Spartans <i>phiditia</i>, and that Lycurgus brought them in against luxury, so that a rich man could not display his means when he ate beside a poor one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They met in companies of fifteen, a few more or less, each man contributing monthly a set quota of barley meal, wine, cheese and figs, with a little money for relishes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A candidate was balloted on with pellets of soft bread dropped into a bowl, one squeezed flat counting against him, and a single such pellet kept him out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle thought the arrangement badly made, since the meals should have been funded publicly as in Crete, whereas at Sparta the very poor could scarcely take part, and by ancient custom a man who could not contribute lost his rights of citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Phylarchus, quoted by Athenaeus, says that by the 3rd century BCE the Spartans had given up assembling for the <i>phiditia</i>, meeting instead over costly couches, foreign wines and foreign perfumes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"syssitia","tags":["practice","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.65–66, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707.txt. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 5, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 10, 12, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9–2.10, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists</i> 4.141–142, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://www.attalus.org/old/athenaeus4.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-252","num":252,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Aristotle reports a law that shamed selling one's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but allowed a man to give or bequeath it, so the land passed into the hands of a few.","questions":["Plutarch says each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded seventy measures of barley a year for its holder and twelve for his wife, the helots tilling it and handing over the produce.","Agis IV proposed cutting Laconia into 4,500 shares, one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for each Spartan, with 15,000 more outside for the provincials, three centuries after Lycurgus was said to have done it first."],"answer":"kleros","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>kleros</b> was the allotment of land that kept a Spartan citizen, worked for him by helots whose produce paid the dues that kept him in his mess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Lycurgus redistributed the land because wealth had gathered in a few hands, cutting the country of the <i>perioikoi</i> into 30,000 lots and that of Sparta itself into 9,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each was reckoned large enough to yield seventy measures of barley a year for its holder and twelve for his wife, with wine and oil in proportion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the helots owed is described two ways, as that fixed render and, in Pausanias, as half of all the produce of their fields, which he supports with Tyrtaeus on men worn like asses under great burdens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The holder did not work it himself, being forbidden every mechanical art, and Plutarch adds that the leisure this bought him was counted among the blessings Lycurgus had provided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius took the equal division of landed property, with the common diet, for one of the two things that kept Sparta free of civil strife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle, writing nearly two centuries before him, describes the opposite: some citizens holding very little and others a great deal, so that the land had passed into the hands of a few.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He blames a law that shamed the sale of an inheritance while letting anyone give or bequeath it, and reports that nearly two-fifths of the country was held by women through heiresses and large dowries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The consequence he draws is that a land able to maintain 1,500 cavalry and 30,000 hoplites had fewer than 1,000 citizens, so the city sank under a single defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Agis IV proposed dividing the land again in the 3rd century BCE, into 4,500 lots for Spartans and 15,000 outside them, Plutarch says most of Sparta's wealth was by then in the hands of its women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"kleros","tags":["concept","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 8, 12, 24, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 4.14, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.48, vol. 3, trans. W. R. Paton (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Agis</i> 7–8, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Agis*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-253","num":253,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xenophon says Lycurgus set slaves to make the clothes and ordered running and trials of strength for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, holding that a free woman's highest work was bearing children.","questions":["Aristotle complains that many things at Sparta were managed by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and asks what difference it makes whether women rule or the rulers are ruled by them.","A foreign woman told Gorgo that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone rule their men, and Plutarch says she answered that they alone give birth to men."],"answer":"Spartan women","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartan women</b> of the citizen class were reared, married and endowed under rules unlike those of any other Greek city, and it is those rules rather than any individual life that the sources describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon says that elsewhere a girl was kept on the plainest food and set to work wool sitting still, and asks how women reared so could bear splendid children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lycurgus, he says, left the making of clothes to slaves and ordered bodily training for women as for men, with contests in running and strength, on the view that strong parents made stronger offspring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch has the maidens exercise in running, wrestling, casting the discus and hurling the javelin, and go in processions and dance in tunics only while the young men watched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Marriage came at full growth rather than in childhood, and both writers describe an elderly husband bringing a younger man to his wife, or a man raising children by another's wife with that husband's consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Aristophanes put a Spartan woman on the Athenian stage in 411 BCE, greeted as handsome and strong enough to strangle a bull, which she says comes of doing gymnastics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plato has an Athenian speaker call the Spartan lawgiver half a man for taking the utmost care of the males while letting the female sex live softly with no order of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aristotle is harsher, saying that many things at Sparta were managed by the women, and asking what difference it makes whether women rule or the rulers are ruled by them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He adds that they were useless when Thebes invaded and caused more confusion than the enemy, and that nearly two-fifths of the country was held by women through heiresses and dowries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch denies the charge outright, and gives the exchange in which a foreign woman tells Gorgo that Spartan women alone rule their men and Gorgo answers that they alone give birth to men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan women","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 1, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 14–15, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Lysistrata</i>, in <i>Aristophanes: The Eleven Comedies</i>, vol. 1 (London: printed for the Athenian Society, 1912), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Aristophanes:_The_Eleven_Comedies/Lysistrata. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i>, book 7, trans. Benjamin Jowett, Project Gutenberg ebook 1750, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1750/pg1750.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Spartan_running_girl_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Spartan_running_girl_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Spartan women","desc":"Bronze figure of a running girl, 520–500 BCE, Spartan, found at Prizren. The short chiton baring one breast matches the outfit Pausanias says was worn by athletes competing in the Heraean Games. British Museum 1876,0510.1. Caeciliusinhorto, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spartan_running_girl_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A bronze figure of a girl in mid-stride, wearing a short chiton that bares one breast, mounted on a museum stand"}},
{"id":"gr-254","num":254,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Thucydides says the rich at Sparta dressed like the poor, an early sign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a city built as villages rather than adorned with temples and public buildings.","questions":["A rhetra of Lycurgus enforced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by ordaining that every house have its roof fashioned by the axe and its doors by the saw, and by no other tool.","Herodotus has Pausanias set a Persian banquet beside a Laconian meal to show his fellow commanders what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looked like against the fare Mardonius had enjoyed."],"answer":"Spartan austerity","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartan austerity</b> is the plainness of living that other Greeks took for the mark of Sparta, and Thucydides put it as a paradox: were Lacedaemon deserted, its remains would make posterity doubt its power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city, he says, was neither built compactly nor adorned with magnificent temples and public buildings, but composed of villages after the old fashion of Hellas, though the Spartans held two-fifths of the Peloponnese and led the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that a modest style of dress was first adopted at Sparta, the rich doing their best to assimilate their way of life to that of the common people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says a rhetra of Lycurgus ordained that every house have its roof fashioned by the axe and its doors by the saw and by no other tool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nobody, he adds, carries silver-footed couches and gold cups into a house like that, so a man must proportion his couch to his house and the rest to the couch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon says money-making was barred outright, the coinage being such that ten minas needed a waggon to carry them, while gold and silver were liable to search and their holder to a penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At table the plainness was the black broth, which the elders prized so much that they left the meat to the young men, and which a king of Pontus is said to have bought a Spartan cook to make and then disliked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells how <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Pausanias_the_regent\">Pausanias</span>, after Plataea, had Mardonius' cooks lay out the Persian dinner and set his own servants to prepare a Laconian one beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pointing at the two, he asked the Greek commanders to observe the senselessness of a leader who had such fare and came to take away theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athenaeus preserves Phylarchus on how the thing ended, with the Spartans no longer assembling for the common meals and taking instead to costly couches, foreign wines and foreign perfumes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan austerity","tags":["concept","practice","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.6, 1.10, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 12–13, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 7, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 9.82, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists</i> 4.141–142, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://www.attalus.org/old/athenaeus4.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"quote":{"book":"thucydides-peloponnesian-war","n":"1","cite":"Book 1.10","text":"<p>For I suppose if Lacedaemon were to become desolate, and the temples and the foundations of the public buildings were left, that as time went on there would be a strong disposition with posterity to refuse to accept her fame as a true exponent of her power. And yet they occupy two-fifths of Peloponnese and lead the whole, not to speak of their numerous allies without.</p>"}},
{"id":"gr-255","num":255,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Thucydides says that when a king was in the field every command passed down through the ranks, so that almost the whole <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> consisted of officers under officers.","questions":["At Mantinea the Argives came on with haste and fury while the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> advanced slowly to the music of many flute-players, a standing institution meant to keep the line even.","Xenophon credits Lycurgus with giving the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a crimson uniform and a heavy bronze shield, as the least womanish equipment and the quickest to burnish."],"answer":"Spartan army","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Spartan army</b> was the hoplite force of a city that trained for nothing else, and Herodotus has the exiled king Demaratus tell Xerxes that the Lacedaemonians are the best of all men when fighting in a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Though free, he says, they are not free in all things: Law is set over them as a master they fear more than the Persians fear their king, and it bids them never flee whatever the numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says every command proceeded from the king in the field and passed down the ranks, so that almost the whole army consisted of officers under officers, the care of what must be done falling upon many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that their numbers were unknown, the government being secret, so he reckons the force at Mantinea from the depth and frontage of its ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There the Argives came on with haste and fury while the Lacedaemonians advanced slowly to the music of many flute-players, a standing institution with nothing to do with religion, meant to keep the line even.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon credits Lycurgus with the crimson uniform and the heavy bronze shield, as an equipment with nothing womanish about it, quickest burnished and least readily soiled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that men past early manhood were allowed to wear their hair long, since it would make them look taller, freer and more terrible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says the young men's discipline was relaxed on campaign, so that they were the only men in the world for whom war brought a respite from training for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before battle the king sacrificed a she-goat and had the men set garlands on their heads while the pipers played the hymn to Castor and he led off a marching paean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle's verdict is that the whole constitution regarded one part of virtue only, the soldier's, so their power held while they were at war and fell when they gained empire, knowing nothing of the arts of peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan army","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.66, 5.68, 5.70, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 11, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 7.104, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 22, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2},
{"id":"gr-256","num":256,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xenophon says Lycurgus divided his citizen soldiers into six of the unit called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each under one polemarch, with four lochagoi and sixteen enomotarchs below him.","questions":["Plutarch says the size of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was disputed, at five hundred men according to Ephorus, seven hundred according to Callisthenes, and nine hundred according to Polybius.","When peltasts under Iphicrates destroyed one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outside Lechaeum, Xenophon put the death-roll at about 250 men, counting the second fight and the final flight."],"answer":"mora","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>mora</b> was the largest division of the classical Spartan army, and the two fullest ancient accounts of the units below it do not agree with each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus credits Lycurgus with establishing what appertained to war, naming enomoties and triecads and the common meals, so the smallest unit is the one the earliest source names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides gives the ladder as it worked at Mantinea, the king giving the word to the polemarchs, they to the lochages, these to the pentecostyes, these to the enomotarchs, and these to the enomoties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He reckons the army's size from that ladder because its numbers were kept secret, counting seven lochoi in the field besides the Sciritae, four pentecostyes to a lochos and four enomoties to a pentecosty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The front rank of an enomoty was four men and the depth generally eight, though each captain chose for himself, which gave a front line of 448 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon instead has Lycurgus divide the citizen soldiers into six morai of cavalry and heavy infantry, each with one polemarch, four lochagoi, eight penteconters and sixteen enomotarchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His lochos is therefore a quarter of a mora, where Thucydides names no mora at all and puts the lochos at the head of everything below it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon adds that the arrangement was far simpler than outsiders supposed, since the front-rank men are all leaders and the enomotarch passes the word that makes the line deeper or shallower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How many men a mora held was already disputed in antiquity, Plutarch reporting five hundred according to Ephorus, seven hundred according to Callisthenes and nine hundred according to Polybius among others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Xenophon shows one in the field outside Lechaeum, caught by peltasts, its polemarch sending out first the ten-years-service men and then the fifteen, and closes with about 250 dead and the destruction of the Lacedaemonian mora.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"mora","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.66, 5.68, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 11, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.65–66, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707.txt. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pelopidas</i> 17, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pelopidas*.html. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 4.5, trans. H. G. Dakyns, Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1174/pg1174.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-257","num":257,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xenophon says the ephors chose three hippagretai, each of whom picked a hundred men for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and had to explain whom he preferred in honour and whom he passed over.","questions":["Thucydides puts the three hundred <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as they were called, fighting round King Agis in the centre of the line at Mantinea while the cavalry held the wings.","Three hundred of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> escorted Themistocles as far as the Tegean border, and Herodotus says he was the only man to whom the Spartans ever gave such an escort."],"answer":"hippeis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>hippeis</b> were the three hundred picked Spartans who formed the king's guard in the field, and the word is the ordinary Greek for horsemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon says the ephors select three men in the prime of life called hippagretai, and that each chooses a hundred others, being bound to explain whom he prefers in honour and whom he disapproves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who fail are then at open war with the men who rejected them and with those chosen instead, each side watching for a slip against the code of honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides shows them at Mantinea in the centre of the line, where the three hundred knights, as he calls them, fought round King Agis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His account of the same battle posts the cavalry on the two wings, so the three hundred stood among the hoplites whatever their name meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus has three hundred picked Spartans, the same who are called the horsemen, escort Themistocles out of Lacedaemon as far as the boundaries of Tegea, after they had honoured him with an olive wreath and their best chariot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He adds that Themistocles is the only man he knows of to whom the Spartans ever gave an escort on his way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The body turned over each year, since the five eldest passing out of it became the Well-doers, bound for that year to go wherever the state sent them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of them, Lichas, is the man Herodotus credits with finding the bones of Orestes at Tegea, having come to a forge there under truce and watched iron being worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The guard served at home as well as in the field, and when the ephors moved against the conspirator Cinadon they told their agent to get six or seven men from the eldest of the hippagretai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"hippeis","tags":["institution","people","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 4, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.67, 5.72, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 8.124, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.67–68, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707.txt. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 3.3, trans. H. G. Dakyns, Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1174/pg1174.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-258","num":258,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch says Lycurgus withdrew gold and silver and gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so trifling a value for its weight that ten minas' worth needed a store-room and a yoke of cattle to shift.","questions":["Polybius says that once Sparta campaigned outside the Peloponnese neither <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nor the barter of crops would serve, so the Spartans had to beg from the Persians.","Plutarch supposed that all the earliest money was like <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some peoples using spits for coins, which is why six obols, or 'spits', still make a drachma."],"answer":"iron money","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Iron money</b> was the currency of Sparta while the rest of Greece coined silver, and Plutarch credits Lycurgus with withdrawing gold and silver from use and ordaining it alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To a great weight of it he gave a trifling value, so that ten minas' worth wanted a large store-room in the house and a yoke of cattle to move.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The iron was quenched red-hot in vinegar, which robbed it of its temper and left it brittle and worthless for any other purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon gives the same sum from the other side, saying ten minas could not come into a house without the master or one of his household noticing, since it would need a waggon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that gold and silver were themselves liable to search with a penalty on whoever was caught, and that freeborn citizens were forbidden to have anything to do with money-making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch draws the consequences: the money could not be carried into the rest of Greece nor valued there, so no merchant brought freight into their harbours and the luxury trades left with the old coinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He supposed elsewhere that all the earliest money was of this kind, some peoples using iron spits, which is why six obols, or 'spits', still make a drachma, a 'handful'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius says the arrangement served while Sparta ruled its neighbours and failed the moment it campaigned abroad, which called for a currency in universal circulation and supplies drawn from outside Laconia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lysander then filled the city with wealth and the love of wealth, and the ephors, debating whether to purge it of gold and silver, resolved that the state might use coin while any private person holding it should die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle's verdict on the whole is that there was no money in the treasury though great wars had to be fought, and that the lawgiver had made his city poor and his citizens greedy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"iron money","tags":["object","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 9, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 7, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lysander</i> 2, 16–17, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1916), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lysander*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.49, vol. 3, trans. W. R. Paton (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3},
{"id":"gr-259","num":259,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Athenaeus quotes Polycrates that the Lacedaemonians kept the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for three days, wearing no garlands and eating no bread at the feasts because they were mourning Hyacinthus.","questions":["The alliance Athens swore with Sparta was to be renewed each year by Athenians coming to Lacedaemon for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with their pillar set beside the statue of Apollo at Amyclae.","Herodotus says the Spartans were keeping the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when the envoys arrived, and were accused of disporting themselves while their allies were left to destruction."],"answer":"Hyacinthia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Hyacinthia</b> was the festival of Apollo at Amyclae, the Laconian town whose sanctuary the Spartans kept as their own and named in their treaties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias calls the image there old and uncouth, a bronze pillar except for its face, feet and hands, wearing a helmet and holding a spear and a bow, and guesses its height at thirty cubits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its pedestal was fashioned in the shape of an altar, and they said Hyacinthus was buried in it, offerings going to him as to a hero through a bronze door on the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The throne that carried the image was made by Bathycles of Magnesia, and Pausanias, who saw it, says that describing its reliefs one by one would only have bored his readers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the altar Hyacinthus is shown bearded and being carried up to heaven with his sister Polyboea, though a painter had given him instead the prime of youthful beauty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Athenaeus quotes Polycrates for the shape of the festival: three days, and for the mourning of Hyacinthus no garlands and no bread at the feasts, cheesecakes given out instead, and no paean sung to the god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The middle day was a spectacle instead, with boys playing the harp girt up in their tunics, riders crossing the theatre, choruses singing native poems, and girls carried in curved wooden chariots called <i>canathra</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Victims were sacrificed all that day, every citizen feasted his friends and his own slaves, and the city emptied itself to the show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus has the Spartans keeping the festival on the eve of Plataea, holding it of the greatest consequence to see to the god, while Athenian envoys accused them of disporting themselves and leaving their allies to destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon says the men of Amyclae came home for the sacred paean whatever campaign they were on, a custom no service abroad was allowed to interrupt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Hyacinthia","tags":["festival","practice","history","greece","religion","archaic"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.18–19, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists, or Banquet of the Learned</i> 4.139, vol. 1, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://archive.org/details/deipnosophistsor01athe. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 9.7, 9.11, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.23, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 4.5, trans. H. G. Dakyns, Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1174/pg1174.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-260","num":260,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Isocrates says the Spartans were still sacrificing at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his own day to Menelaus and Helen together, and doing it not as to heroes but as to gods.","questions":["Polybius says the hills the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands on are rocky and hard to climb, and command the strip of level ground between the city and the Eurotas.","A nurse carried an ugly child daily to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where a woman stroked her head and said she would be the fairest woman in Sparta, and so she became."],"answer":"Menelaion","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Menelaion</b> was the sanctuary of Menelaus and Helen at Therapne in Laconia, standing on high ground across the Eurotas from Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias records a temple of Menelaus there, says the place takes its name from a daughter of Lelex, and reports that Menelaus and Helen were buried in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gives the Rhodian version as well, in which Helen was driven out after Menelaus died and hanged on a tree by handmaidens dressed as Furies, which is why the Rhodians have a sanctuary of Helen of the Tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Isocrates says the Spartans were still offering holy and traditional sacrifices to the pair at Therapne in his own day, and offering them not as to heroes but as to gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Homer had already given Menelaus a destiny apart, Proteus telling him that he would not die in Argos but be taken to the Elysian plain because he had married Helen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus puts a temple of Helen at Therapne above the temple of Phoebus, and tells of a nurse who carried an ugly child there day after day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She set her before the image and prayed the goddess to rid her of her unshapeliness, until a woman appeared, stroked the child's head and said she would be the fairest woman in Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From that day her aspect changed; she married Agetos, was afterwards taken from him by King Ariston through a trick of gifts, and became the mother of Demaratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius, who calls it the Menelaïum, says the hills it stands on are rocky, hard to climb and of considerable height, and command the level strip between the city and the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Philip V came down on Sparta, Lycurgus took post round it with not less than two thousand men and dammed the river to flood that strip, leaving one narrow passage under the hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Menelaion","tags":["place","building","history","greece","religion","archaic"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.19, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 6.61–63, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 5.21–22, vol. 3, trans. W. R. Paton (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/5*.html. [Open access]","Isocrates, <i>Helen</i> 63, in <i>Isocrates</i>, vol. 3, trans. La Rue Van Hook (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1945), https://archive.org/details/isocrateswitheng03isocuoft. [Open access]","Homer, <i>The Odyssey</i> 4, trans. Samuel Butler, Project Gutenberg ebook 1727, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1727/pg1727.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Therapne%2C_Menelaion_from_NE_%281988%29_01.jpg/1920px-Therapne%2C_Menelaion_from_NE_%281988%29_01.jpg","title":"Menelaion","desc":"Therapne (Sparta). View of the Menelaion from the northeast, with Taygetos in the background. Mark Landon, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Therapne,_Menelaion_from_NE_(1988)_01.jpg","alt":"Therapne, Menelaion from the north-east"},"locator":{"name":"The Menelaion","at":[22.4541,37.0661],"within":"Sparta"}},
{"id":"gr-261","num":261,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Pausanias says the priestess stood by holding the image of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which grew too heavy to carry whenever the scourgers spared the lash for a lad's beauty or high rank.","questions":["Xenophon says stealing as many cheeses as possible off the shrine of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was encouraged, while others were told at the same moment to scourge whoever was caught.","Plutarch says he has seen youths expiring under the lash at the altar of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Cicero heard while at Sparta that boys were sometimes whipped to death there."],"answer":"Artemis Orthia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Artemis Orthia</b> had her sanctuary at the place the Spartans called Limnaeum, 'the Marshy', and Pausanias renders the epithet Orthia as 'Upright'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says the image was called Lygodesma, 'willow-bound', as well, because it was found in a thicket of willows whose encircling withies made it stand upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias holds it to be the image Orestes and Iphigenia stole out of the Tauric land, and offers as evidence that Astrabacus and Alopecus went out of their minds the moment they found it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a second proof he gives the day the Limnatians, Cynosurians and the people of Mesoa and Pitane fell to quarrelling as they sacrificed, so that many were killed at the altar and the rest died of disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An oracle then told them to stain the altar with human blood, and a man taken by lot was sacrificed until Lycurgus changed the custom to a scourging of the lads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon, writing centuries earlier, describes something looser: stealing as many cheeses as possible off the shrine of Orthia while others were told to scourge the thief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Pausanias the priestess stands by holding the wooden image, which is small and light but grows too heavy to carry if the scourgers spare the lash for a lad's beauty or high rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says he has himself seen youths expiring under the lash at the altar, and tells it as what makes credible the boy who let a stolen fox tear out his bowels rather than be caught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His Spartan sayings call the thing the Flagellation, held each year, the boys lashed all day and vying over who could bear the most blows, the winner held in especial repute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cicero, who had been to Sparta, says blood followed the lash in abundance and that he used to hear there they were sometimes whipped to death, and yet not one was ever heard to cry out or groan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Artemis Orthia","tags":["place","practice","history","greece","religion","archaic"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.16, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 2, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 18, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Instituta Laconica</i> 40, in <i>Moralia</i>, vol. 3, trans. Frank Cole Babbitt (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Instituta_Laconica*.html. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Tusculan Disputations</i> 2.14, trans. C. D. Yonge, Project Gutenberg ebook 14988, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14988/pg14988.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/The_remains_of_the_Temple_of_Artemis_Orthia_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg/1920px-The_remains_of_the_Temple_of_Artemis_Orthia_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","title":"Artemis Orthia","desc":"The remains of the Temple of Artemis Orthia in Sparta. Laconia, Greece. George E. Koronaios, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_remains_of_the_Temple_of_Artemis_Orthia_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","alt":"Remains of the temple of Artemis Orthia in Sparta"},"locator":{"name":"Artemis Orthia","at":[22.435,37.083],"within":"Sparta"}},
{"id":"gr-262","num":262,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Critias praised the kothon, a shape in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as the cup most convenient on an expedition, because a soldier cannot easily see what he is drinking in it.","questions":["The finest cups of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were found chiefly in Etruria and later at Naukratis and on Samos, and were long grouped instead under the name Cyrenaic.","Polemo says the Lacedaemonians used vessels of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, drinking from them at festivals and victory feasts and at the marriage-feasts of their maidens."],"answer":"Laconian pottery","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Laconian pottery</b> is the earthenware of Sparta and its region, whose finest phase is a black-figure ware of the 6th century BCE made up mostly of cups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The shapes are slender kylikes owing much to metal originals, which shows in the palmettes set at the ends of their handles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Designs were painted in black on a slip running from deep buff to a pale cream, with firmly drawn incised lines and a plentiful use of purple for details.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A cup of about 550 BCE in New York, worked in the manner of the Arkesilas Painter, carries birds, bulls and Herakles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That painter is named from the Arkesilas cup in Paris, which shows the king of Kyrene aboard ship superintending the weighing of a cargo, with the figures labelled and a monkey, a lizard and a panther above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The plainer ware is what the written sources notice: Critias praised the Lacedaemonian kothon as the cup most convenient on an expedition and the easiest carried in a knapsack, its whole point being that a soldier must often drink water that is not clean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One cannot easily see what one is drinking in it, and its brim curves inward so as to hold back what is foul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch repeats him and adds that common and necessary things such as bedsteads, chairs and tables were excellently made at Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polemo says the Lacedaemonians used earthenware vessels, drinking from earthenware cups at festivals, at feasts in honour of victory and at the marriage-feasts of their maidens, and using small casks at other banquets and at the phiditia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two-handled lekaina, a shape a New York example dates to the 6th century BCE, took its name from the clay it was made of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Laconian pottery","tags":["object","culture","history","greece","art","archaic"],"sources":["Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists, or Banquet of the Learned</i> 11.66, vol. 2, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://archive.org/details/deipnosophistsor02athe. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 9, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 341–43, 460, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta kylix, Greek, Laconian, ca. 550 BCE, accession 59.15, https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/255020. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta lekaina, Greek, Laconian, 6th century BCE, accession 1986.11.7, https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/255861. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Laconian_black_figure_kylix_-_cocks_and_sphinxes_-_Roma_MCPCPN_-_01.jpg/1920px-Laconian_black_figure_kylix_-_cocks_and_sphinxes_-_Roma_MCPCPN_-_01.jpg","title":"Laconian pottery","alt":"Laconian black-figure kylix with cocks and sphinxes, Rome","desc":"Laconian black-figure kylix, ca. 550–530 BCE, with an animal frieze of two confronted cocks between sphinxes; Musei Capitolini, Rome. ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laconian_black_figure_kylix_-_cocks_and_sphinxes_-_Roma_MCPCPN_-_01.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-263","num":263,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Two continents dispute in Antipater's epigram whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a Lydian or a Spartan, since the tomb is simple to look on but holds the bones of a great man.","questions":["The Suda calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a Laconian from Messoa, though Crates made him a Lydian out of Sardis, and says his parents were slaves and he wrote six books.","Sosibius says the naked choruses at the Gymnopaidiae sang songs of Thaletas and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose maiden-song praises Agido and Hagesichora as they carry a robe to Orthia."],"answer":"Alcman","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Alcman</b> was an archaic lyric poet who composed at Sparta, and the Suda credits him with six books, lyric poetry and a poem called <i>Diving Women</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same entry calls him a Laconian from Messoa while recording that Crates made him a Lydian out of Sardis, names his father as Damas or Titaros, and says his parents were slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It puts him in the 27th Olympiad, when Ardys was king of Lydia, and makes him the inventor of love poetry and the first to sing in metres other than the hexameter, though that chronology is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Velleius Paterculus denies the Spartan claim flatly, and two epigrams in the Greek Anthology turn the quarrel into a compliment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Antipater writes that the stone is plain to look on but holds the bones of a great man, and that two continents dispute him, since minstrels have many mothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Alexander of Aetolia answers in the poet's own voice: reared in ancient Sardis he would have been a eunuch beating pretty tambourines, where instead he is a citizen of Sparta of the many tripods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What survives at length is a maiden-song on a first-century papyrus, sung by a chorus of girls who praise their leader Hagesichora and a second girl, Agido, as they carry a robe to Orthia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They call the two racehorses grazing among the cattle of dreams, set Hagesichora's hair beside pure gold, say she shines like Sirius, and name eight more singers who cannot help them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other pieces show a poet of the countryside and the table: peak and ravine asleep with the creeping things, the beasts, the bees and the monsters of the brine, and Alcman himself waiting on a pot of common fare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sosibius records that the naked choruses of the Gymnopaidiae still sang his songs, and Pausanias was shown his tomb at Sparta, its charm unspoilt by the Laconian dialect, the least musical of them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Alcman","tags":["person","history","greece","literature","art","archaic"],"sources":["Suda On Line, s.v. \"Alkman, Alcman,\" adler number alpha 1289, trans. and vetted by the Suda On Line project, https://www.cs.uky.edu/~raphael/sol/sol-entries/alpha/1289. [Open access]","J. M. Edmonds, ed. and trans., <i>Lyra Graeca</i>, vol. 1 (London: William Heinemann, 1922), 45–51, 79–89, https://archive.org/details/lyragraecabeing00edmogoog. [Open access]","<i>The Greek Anthology</i> 7.18, 7.709, vol. 2, trans. W. R. Paton (London: William Heinemann, 1917), https://archive.org/details/greekanthologyii0002pato. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists, or Banquet of the Learned</i> 15.22–23, vol. 3, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://archive.org/details/deipnosophistsor03atheuoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.15, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Mosaic_Portrait_of_the_Poet_Alcman.jpg/1920px-Mosaic_Portrait_of_the_Poet_Alcman.jpg","title":"Alcman","alt":"Roman mosaic portrait head of the poet Alcman, Jerash","desc":"Imaginary portrait of Alcman in a floor mosaic of the late 2nd or 3rd century CE from Jerash, Jordan, made some eight centuries after the poet and set in a border with other Greek authors, the Seasons and the Muses. ellenm1, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_Portrait_of_the_Poet_Alcman.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-264","num":264,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Historians call it <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: Thucydides warned that were Lacedaemon deserted, posterity would refuse to believe her fame matched her power, the city being a set of villages.","questions":["One ancient admission behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Xenophon's: he would not maintain that the laws of Lycurgus were still obeyed, since Spartans now boasted of gold they had once dreaded.","Aristotle sharpens <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by reckoning that a country able to maintain 30,000 hoplites had under a thousand citizens, so the city sank under a single defeat."],"answer":"Spartan mirage","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartan mirage</b> is the name modern scholarship gives to the distance between Sparta's reputation and the Sparta the evidence will bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides saw the trap coming: were Lacedaemon deserted and only its temples and foundations left, posterity would be strongly disposed to refuse her fame as a true measure of her power, the city being neither compact nor adorned but a collection of villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He could not even count the Spartan army at Mantinea, because the secrecy of their government kept the numbers unknown and nobody trusted their opponents' guess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch, writing some seven centuries after the event, opens his life of Lycurgus by admitting that nothing can be said of the lawgiver which is not disputed, his birth, his travels, his death and above all his date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon, who lived at Sparta and admired it, would not venture to maintain that the laws of Lycurgus were obeyed in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Spartans had once dreaded to be caught with gold, he says, and now made a boast of it, and men who had preferred moderate means at home now wanted nothing so much as to end their days as governors abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle is harsher: the licence of the Spartan women defeats the intention of the constitution, so that half the city may be reckoned to have no laws at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He adds that property had fallen into few hands, that two-fifths of the land was held by women, and that a country able to maintain 1,500 cavalry and 30,000 hoplites had fewer than a thousand citizens, so that it sank under a single defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Spartans had also never found the secret of managing their subject population, whose risings he treats as a standing weakness rather than an accident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Modern work therefore assigns much of the traditional picture to an imagined Sparta, with customs credited to Lycurgus introduced later for the needs of the moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan mirage","tags":["concept","history","greece","classical","archaic"],"sources":["Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 19, 308–9, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.10, 5.68, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 1, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 14, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Ancient_Sparta_ruins_%281%29.jpg/1920px-Ancient_Sparta_ruins_%281%29.jpg","title":"Spartan mirage","alt":"Low stone footings among grass and trees on the site of ancient Sparta","desc":"Ruins on the site of ancient Sparta, the low remains Thucydides had in mind when he wrote that a deserted Lacedaemon would leave posterity refusing to believe her fame matched her power. Peulle, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Sparta_ruins_(1).jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-265","num":265,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The ancient name for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was simply the Lacedaemonians and their allies, and Thucydides says Sparta took no tribute from them but established oligarchies instead.","questions":["Corinth was told she broke her oaths in refusing the peace of 421, since a majority of the allies bound the rest of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unless gods or heroes stood in the way.","Xenophon shows the congress of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> resolving on ten thousand men against Olynthus, and letting a state pay three Aeginetan obols a day rather than send them."],"answer":"Peloponnesian League","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Peloponnesian League</b> is the modern name for the system of alliances by which Sparta led the Peloponnese from the 6th century BCE, and whose name in antiquity was simply the Lacedaemonians and their allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus dates the ascendancy behind it: once the Spartans had the better of the Tegeans they had subdued the greater part of the Peloponnese besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of that century the alliance took in Corinth, Sicyon, probably Megara and Aegina, and the cities of the Argolis, with Sparta's own territory two-fifths of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its members were bound to Sparta rather than to one another, each by a separate treaty with the leader, who was to come to an attacked ally's aid and to be aided in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says the policy of Lacedaemon was to exact no tribute but to secure the subservience of the allies to her interests by establishing oligarchies among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Sparta settled its own mind first and then summoned the rest: in 432 BCE the Lacedaemonians heard every argument, took the vote of all the allied states present in order, great and small alike, and the majority was for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That majority bound the others, and Corinth was told she had added the breaking of her oaths to her refusal of the peace, since it had been expressly agreed that the decision of the majority should stand unless the gods or heroes stood in the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon shows the congress at work again over Olynthus, the Lacedaemonians asking the allies for their joint advice and the states resolving to raise ten thousand men between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same meeting let any state that wished send money instead, at three Aeginetan obols a day for each man, with a horseman reckoned at four foot soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus closes the account: after holding the supremacy of Greece for almost five hundred years, the Lacedaemonians were beaten in one great battle and lost it irretrievably.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Peloponnesian League","tags":["institution","history","greece","warfare","classical","archaic"],"sources":["Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 16–17, 175–76, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.68, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.19, 1.125, 5.30, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 5.2, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1174/1174-h/1174-h.htm. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 15.50, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1952), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/15C*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Bronze_warrior_Laconian_from_Sparta_6th_century_BCE_British_Museum.jpg/1920px-Bronze_warrior_Laconian_from_Sparta_6th_century_BCE_British_Museum.jpg","title":"Peloponnesian League","desc":"Bronze warrior, Laconian, from Sparta, 6th century BCE. British Museum. Mary Harrsch, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_warrior_Laconian_from_Sparta_6th_century_BCE_British_Museum.jpg","alt":"A small bronze warrior in a crested helmet, carrying a round shield with his spear arm raised"}},
{"id":"gr-266","num":266,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Herodotus has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Olympia when the cauldrons of Hippocrates boiled without fire, advising him not to take a wife, or to disown any son he had.","questions":["Diodorus says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> engraved three maxims on a column at Delphi: know thyself, nothing overmuch, and a pledge, and ruin is nigh.","The tradition has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> die at Pisa of excess of joy, having just congratulated his son on winning the Olympic crown for boxing."],"answer":"Chilon of Sparta","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chilon of Sparta</b> was a Lacedaemonian of the 6th century BCE counted among the Seven Sages, whom Diogenes Laertius makes the son of Damagetas and the author of an elegiac poem some 200 lines long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was made ephor in the 55th Olympiad, or the 56th by another reckoning, and was said to have first proposed that ephors be joined to the kings as their auxiliaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That last claim is doubted: the writer Diogenes leans on seems to have recorded Chilon's first term of office rather than the first ephorate, and other accounts give the office to Lycurgus, to Theopompus or to the Messenian-war kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus puts him at Olympia when the cauldrons of Hippocrates boiled without fire beneath them, advising him not to bring a wife into his house, and to divorce her and disown any son he had already.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hippocrates would not be persuaded, and the son afterwards born to him was Peisistratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diodorus says his life agreed with his teaching, a thing one rarely finds, and has him engrave three maxims on a column at Delphi: know thyself, nothing overmuch, and a pledge, and ruin is nigh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tradition does not agree about them, since Diogenes gives only the pledge as Chilon's own apophthegm and reports 'know thyself' claimed for Thales and for the prophetess Phemonoë.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The precepts kept under his name are plain and mostly about restraint: control the tongue, especially at a banquet; use threats to nobody, for that is womanish; visit friends in adversity sooner than in prosperity; prefer a loss to a dishonest gain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He spoke so briefly that Aristagoras of Miletus called the manner Chilonean, and Herodotus has Demaratus tell Xerxes that Chilon thought Cythera a greater gain to Sparta sunk under the sea than left above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is said to have died at Pisa of excess of joy after congratulating his son on an Olympic victory in boxing, and Pausanias was shown a hero-shrine of Chilon at Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chilon of Sparta","tags":["person","history","greece","concept","archaic"],"sources":["Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of the Eminent Philosophers</i> 1.40, 1.68–73, trans. R. D. Hicks, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Lives_of_the_Eminent_Philosophers/Book_I. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.59, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 7.235, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 9.9–10, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/9*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 94–95, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.16, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Chilon_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg","title":"Chilon of Sparta","alt":"Engraved full-length figure of a bearded man in robe and turban, captioned CHILO","desc":"Imaginary portrait of Chilon, engraved for Thomas Stanley's History of Philosophy of 1655 and dressing him in the fancy costume of that century rather than anything Spartan. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chilon_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-267","num":267,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Aristagoras of Miletus showed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a bronze tablet engraved with a map of the whole earth, and was sent out of Sparta before sunset on admitting the king lay three months inland.","questions":["Told the burning grove was sacred to Argos, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> groaned that Apollo had greatly deceived him in promising he would take the city of that name.","Maeandrius of Samos offered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> his gold and silver cups, and Herodotus says the king refused them and had the ephors expel him from the Peloponnese."],"answer":"Cleomenes I","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cleomenes I</b> was Agiad king of Sparta from about 521 to about 490 BCE, the son Anaxandrides had by a second wife taken when the ephors pressed him over the first’s barrenness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus says he was not quite in his right senses but on the verge of madness, and that the Spartans followed custom anyway and gave the throne to the eldest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His half-brother Dorieus, whom Pausanias calls sounder in judgement and the better soldier, would not stay to be his subject and sailed off to found a colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At home Herodotus calls him the most upright of men: Maeandrius of Samos pressed gold and silver cups on him, and he refused and had the ephors expel the man lest he corrupt some other Spartan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He heard Aristagoras of Miletus out over a bronze tablet engraved with a map of the whole earth, then told him to be gone before sunset on learning the Persian king lay three months inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Aristagoras followed him home and raised his offer to fifty talents, at which the king’s daughter Gorgo, then eight or nine, said the stranger would do him hurt if he did not leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the field he is another man: after beating the Argives he called about fifty of those sheltering in the sacred grove out by name, pretending their ransom was paid, and killed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then had the helots fire the grove, and on learning it was sacred to the hero Argos he groaned that Apollo had deceived him in promising he would take Argos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the Athenian acropolis the priestess told him no Dorian might enter, and he answered that he was no Dorian but an Achaean; Thucydides has him drive the accursed out of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Brought back to Sparta he struck every Spartan he met in the face with his staff until his kinsmen bound him in the stocks, where he got a knife from his helot warder and cut himself to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cleomenes I","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 3.148, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 5.42, 5.49–51, 5.72, 6.75–80, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.3–3.4, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 178–82, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Bunbury_Vol_1_Map_02_Hecataeus_p_148.jpg/1920px-Bunbury_Vol_1_Map_02_Hecataeus_p_148.jpg","title":"Cleomenes I","alt":"Engraved circular map of the world ringed by Oceanus, labelled Europa, Asia and Libya","desc":"A Victorian reconstruction of the world as Hecataeus of Miletus conceived it, from Bunbury's History of Ancient Geography. It is not the bronze tablet Aristagoras carried to Sparta, but it shows the kind of map he brought and how short the road to Susa could be made to look. Edward Bunbury, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bunbury_Vol_1_Map_02_Hecataeus_p_148.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-268","num":268,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xerxes was told by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the Lacedaemonians are free but not free in all things, since Law is set over them as a master they fear more than his men fear him.","questions":["Ariston reckoned the months on his fingers before the ephors and denied on oath that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could be his son, which Cleomenes used years later to depose him.","From Susa <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent word of the invasion on a folding tablet scraped bare and waxed over again, and Gorgo worked out how to read it."],"answer":"Demaratus","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Demaratus</b> was Eurypontid king of Sparta from about 515 to 491 BCE, and stood in the shadow of his Agiad colleague Cleomenes for nearly the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus explains his name and then his ruin in the same breath: the Spartans had prayed in a body for a son to Ariston, but the boy came in less than ten months, and Ariston, reckoning on his fingers as he sat with the ephors, denied on oath that the child could be his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He repented and took the words back, and the ephors thought nothing of them then, but they were remembered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Demaratus abandoned Cleomenes at Eleusis a law was laid down that the two kings should never again take the field together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleomenes then set up Leotychides to claim the throne on that old oath, and had Cobon of Delphi persuade the prophetess Perialla to declare Demaratus no son of Ariston; when it came out, both were disgraced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias gives the flat verdict that he was deposed not rightfully but because Cleomenes hated him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Holding a lesser office afterwards, he was asked by Leotychides at the Gymnopaidiae what it was like to be a magistrate after being king, and he veiled his head, went home, sacrificed an ox and asked his mother who his father was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fled to Persia and marched with Xerxes, who asked whether Greeks would really stand, and was told that the Lacedaemonians are free and yet not free in all things, since Law is set over them as a master they fear far more than his own men fear him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From Susa he had already sent warning of the invasion on a folding tablet scraped bare and waxed over again, which Gorgo worked out how to read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus leaves open whether he did that in friendship or in malice, and his descendants still governed Pergamum and two cities beside it in Xenophon's day, on land the Persian king had given him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Demaratus","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 5.75, 6.61–70, 7.104, 7.239, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.4, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 3.1, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1174/1174-h/1174-h.htm. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.6, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/11A*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 178–82, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"gr-269","num":269,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Lacedaemonians came to Sardis to buy gold for the image of Apollo on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Croesus gave it to them as a gift instead.","questions":["Pausanias says the Spartans spent the gold Croesus sent for the Apollo of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on adorning the Amyclaean image instead, that cult being more distinguished.","Pausanias reaches <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the ruins of Sellasia and says the city lies further on, and he knew a second hill of the name in the Argolid."],"answer":"Thornax","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Thornax</b> was a place in Laconia on the road north of Sparta, carrying an image of Apollo Pythaeus made after the style of the one at Amyclae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias reaches it going along the highway past the ruins of Sellasia, and says the city lies further on from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus explains why it mattered: the Lacedaemonians had sent to Sardis and were buying gold there to use for that image, and Croesus, when they wished to buy, gave it them as a gift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That service is why they were pleased when his messengers came asking an alliance, saying the god had bidden him join with himself the Hellene as a friend and that he had heard they were the chiefs of Hellas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They exchanged oaths of friendship and alliance, a pact commonly dated to about 555 BCE and read as a clear affront to Argos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> They also had a bronze mixing-bowl made, covered outside with figures round the rim and large enough to hold three hundred amphorae, and it never reached Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Lacedaemonians said the Samians sailed out and took it; the Samians said the carriers, hearing Sardis had fallen, sold it in Samos, where it was dedicated in the temple of Hera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the herald came asking help against Cyrus the Spartans had a quarrel with Argos over Thyrea in hand, and their ships were ready before word arrived that Croesus was a prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pausanias closes the account against Herodotus: the gold went on adorning the Amyclaean image after all, that cult being in Spartan eyes the more distinguished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its exact hill has not been securely identified, Strabo's text being damaged where the name may have stood and the conjecture that restores it doubtful, and Pausanias knew a second Thornax in the Argolid, renamed Coccygium after Zeus turned cuckoo there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Thornax","tags":["place","mountain","history","geography","greece","religion","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.69–70, 1.82–83, 1.152–153, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.10–3.11, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.36, vol. 1, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/pausaniasgreece01pausuoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.5.3, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), note 254, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/8E*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 174–76, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-270","num":270,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Told that Sparta meant to restore Hippias, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that heaven would sooner lie below the earth and fishes dwell where men had lived before.","questions":["The envoy of Corinth, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, told the congress to set up a despot in Sparta itself first, and then go establishing them for others.","Herodotus says the other allies had kept silence until <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spoke freely, after which every one of them adjured Sparta to do no violence to a Greek city."],"answer":"Socles","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Socles</b> was the envoy of Corinth who spoke against Sparta at the congress of the allies about 504 BCE, the first meeting of that alliance on record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What he was answering was a reputation Thucydides states flatly: the tyrants of Athens and the far older tyrannies of the rest of Hellas were, Sicily excepted, once and for all put down by Lacedaemon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides grounds it in Sparta's own case, an unbroken freedom from tyrants and one form of government kept for more than four hundred years, which put her in a position to arrange the affairs of other states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle repeats it in a sentence, that the Lacedaemonians put down most of the tyrannies, and Herodotus supplies instances, chief among them a forty-day siege of Polycrates at Samos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus also undercuts it, since the Lacedaemonians said themselves that they sailed against Samos less to help the exiles than to avenge a mixing-bowl the Samians had robbed them of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> At Athens the lever was Delphi, the exiled Alcmaeonids having paid the Pythian prophetess to tell every Spartan who consulted her, privately or publicly, to set Athens free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta obeyed and drove out the sons of Peisistratus, though bound to them by guest-friendship, holding that the concerns of the god should be preferred to those of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Years afterwards Cleomenes brought home oracles foretelling injury from Athens, so Sparta summoned Hippias from Sigeum, told the allies plainly that it had erred, and proposed to put him back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of them disapproved and kept silence, and Socles alone spoke, telling the Corinthian story of Cypselus and Periander at length and challenging Sparta to set up a despot at home first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest then spoke out and adopted his opinion, and modern accounts read the whole policy narrowly, as hostility to particular tyrants whom an oligarchy friendly to Sparta could replace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Socles","tags":["person","history","greece","concept","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 5.63–65, 5.90–93, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.18, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 5.12, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 3.46–48, 3.54–56, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 179–81, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5},
{"id":"gr-271","num":271,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes its name from the irons the Spartans carried to Tegea, in which the captured among them were then set to till the Tegean plain.","questions":["Delphi refused Sparta the whole of Arcadia before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, offering instead a plain to measure with line and divide, which the losers measured as prisoners.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Lichas read an oracle in a Tegean smithy, taking the bellows for winds and the hammer and anvil for stroke and counterstroke."],"answer":"Battle of the Fetters","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Battle of the Fetters</b> is the modern name for a defeat the Spartans suffered at Tegea around the middle of the 6th century BCE, which the ancient accounts describe without naming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus places it after Lycurgus, when Sparta had grown prosperous, was no longer content to keep still, and consulted Delphi about conquering the whole of Arcadia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Pythian prophetess refused Arcadia, telling them that many acorn-eating men were there to prevent them, and offered instead Tegea to dance in and a fair plain to measure with line and divide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They marched on Tegea with fetters in their hands, trusting a deceitful oracle and expecting to make slaves of the Tegeans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Worsted in the encounter, those taken alive worked the Tegean plain wearing the fetters they had brought, and Herodotus says the irons still hung about the temple of Athena Alea in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Sparta went on losing until Delphi told them to bring home the bones of Orestes, a second oracle setting the grave where two winds blow by compulsion, stroke answers stroke, and trouble lies upon trouble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lichas, one of the five Agathoergoi sent about on state business each year, read the riddle in a Tegean smithy: the bellows the winds, the hammer and anvil the strokes, the iron the trouble laid upon trouble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The smith had dug up a coffin seven cubits long while sinking a well, Sparta drove Lichas out on a pretended charge, and he hired the yard, took the bones home, and from then on the Lacedaemonians had much the better of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias keeps the whole story and adds a reader's caution, that in the age of heroes the god would have called bronze the woe to man, setting beside it Cimon's finding of the bones of Theseus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modern accounts read the bones as policy rather than piety, marking the point at which Sparta stopped annexing land, claimed the Achaean inheritance, and turned to a hegemony held by treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of the Fetters","tags":["event","battle","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.65–68, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.3, 3.7, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 9.36, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/9*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 165–66, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Natasha Bershadsky, “The Border of War and Peace: Myth and Ritual in Argive-Spartan Dispute over Thyreatis,” in <i>Maintaining Peace and Interstate Stability in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, ed. Julia Wilker (Mainz: Verlag Antike, 2012), 56–57, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/43876. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Tegea_Temple_of_Athena_Alea_from_SW.jpg/1920px-Tegea_Temple_of_Athena_Alea_from_SW.jpg","title":"Temple of Athena Alea, Tegea","alt":"Tegea, temple of Athena Alea from the south-west","desc":"Temple of Athena Alea at Tegea, from southwest. Mark Landon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tegea_Temple_of_Athena_Alea_from_SW.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-272","num":272,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Three hundred a side fought for Thyrea in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and by nightfall only Alcenor and Chromios of Argos and the Spartan Othryades were still standing.","questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Argives bound themselves by a curse to wear their hair short until Thyrea was won back, and Sparta took the opposite law.","In 420 BCE Argos won a clause letting either state challenge the other over Cynuria and settle it as in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Sparta thought mere folly."],"answer":"Battle of the Champions","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Battle of the Champions</b> is the modern name for a fight of three hundred picked men a side over the border district of Thyrea, dated from Herodotus to about 546 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sparta was the aggressor, having cut Thyrea off from the Argive possessions, and when the Argives came to save their territory the two sides agreed to settle the ground by champions alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both armies were to withdraw home, for fear lest one side seeing its countrymen suffer defeat should come up to their support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chosen men proved equally matched, and by nightfall three were left of the six hundred: Alcenor and Chromios for Argos, Othryades for Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two Argives ran home reckoning themselves the victors, while Othryades stripped the Argive dead, carried their arms to his own camp and stayed where he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Next day each side claimed the win, the one on the greater number surviving and the other because those men had fled, and the dispute became a general battle that Sparta took.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Argives cut their hair short and made a law with a curse attached that no man should grow it long nor any woman wear gold until Thyrea was won back, and the Lacedaemonians laid down for themselves the opposite law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus ends with Othryades killing himself there, ashamed to return alone; Argos told it otherwise, and a monument in its theatre showed Perilaus son of Alcenor killing him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The quarrel outlasted them all, and in 420 BCE the Argives won a clause allowing either state to challenge the other and decide Cynuria by battle as before, which Thucydides says Sparta thought mere folly before agreeing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One modern reading takes the story as the trace of ritual combats fought for the district through the archaic period, each settling possession until the next, until Sparta broke the practice by annexing it outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of the Champions","tags":["event","battle","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.82, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.41, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.20, 2.38, vol. 1, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/pausaniasgreece01pausuoft. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 176–77, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Natasha Bershadsky, “The Border of War and Peace: Myth and Ritual in Argive-Spartan Dispute over Thyreatis,” in <i>Maintaining Peace and Interstate Stability in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, ed. Julia Wilker (Mainz: Verlag Antike, 2012), 49–77, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/43876. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Battle_between_Agravers_and_Spartians%2C_546_BC.jpg/1920px-Battle_between_Agravers_and_Spartians%2C_546_BC.jpg","title":"Battle of the Champions, engraved 1832","alt":"Line engraving of hoplites fighting hand to hand among the fallen, a temple beyond","desc":"Battle between Argives and Spartans (Battle of the 300 Champions), 546 BCE. Line engraving from Vorzeit und Gegenwart, vol. 2 (Augsburg: Schlosser, 1832). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_between_Agravers_and_Spartians,_546_BC.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-273","num":273,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cleomenes had the Spartan herald call breakfast at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and attacked while the Argives, who were copying every proclamation, sat down to eat.","questions":["Pausanias says the poetess Telesilla armed the women of Argos after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and set them on the wall, and that the Lacedaemonians gave way rather than kill them.","So many Argives fell at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Herodotus says slaves ruled the state until the sons of the dead were grown men."],"answer":"Battle of Sepeia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Battle of Sepeia</b> is the name given to the destruction of the Argive army by Cleomenes of Sparta near Tiryns, usually dated to 494 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Delphi had told him he would take Argos, and when the sacrifices at the river Erasinos refused him a crossing he said he admired it for not betraying its countrymen, then shipped the army from Thyrea to Tiryns and Nauplia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Argives came down to meet him and camped close by, afraid not of open fighting but of being beaten by craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So they took to doing whatever the Spartan herald proclaimed, and Cleomenes, seeing it, had the herald call breakfast and attacked while they were eating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many fell and many more fled into the grove sacred to Argos, where they were called out by name for ransom and killed until the rest saw it, and the grove was fired with them inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The dead are counted differently by everyone: Herodotus reports six thousand on the Argives' own account, Pausanias puts five thousand in the grove, and Plutarch names 7,777 only to dismiss it as a fabulous story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias and Plutarch add what Herodotus does not, that the city was saved by its women under the poetess Telesilla, who armed those of fighting age with what was left in the houses and the sanctuaries and set them on the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Lacedaemonians gave way, reckoning that to destroy the women would be an invidious success and to lose to them a shameful disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus quotes an oracle about the female conquering the male, which Pausanias applies to that fight while allowing that Herodotus may not have understood it so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Argos was left so bare of men that its slaves held the state until the sons of the fallen grew up, while Cleomenes sacrificed at the Heraion and went home to be tried for taking bribes not to storm the city, and was acquitted on an omen he had seen there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of Sepeia","tags":["event","battle","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 6.76–83, 7.148, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.20, vol. 1, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/pausaniasgreece01pausuoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.4, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Plutarch, “Bravery of Women” 245c–f, in <i>Moralia</i>, vol. 3, trans. Frank Cole Babbitt, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), https://archive.org/details/plutarchs-moralia-vol.-3-loeb-245. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 181–82, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Argos_Heraion_Plain.jpg/1920px-Argos_Heraion_Plain.jpg","title":"The Argive plain from the Heraion","alt":"Terraces and fallen column drums in the foreground, a wide cultivated plain running to mountains beyond","desc":"View from the Heraion of Argos into the Inachos plain, Argolis, Greece. Sarah Murray, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Argos_Heraion_Plain.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-274","num":274,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the dead were laid away in a scarlet robe and olive leaves with nothing else, and mourning ended on the twelfth day with a sacrifice to Demeter.","questions":["Plutarch credits Lycurgus with allowing burial inside the city as part of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that the young grew used to the sight and feared no pollution from a corpse.","A name was cut on a tomb under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only for a man fallen in war, or for a woman whose case the manuscripts and their editors describe differently."],"answer":"Spartan burial custom","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartan burial custom</b> is known chiefly from Plutarch, who credits Lycurgus with doing away with the fear of the dead by letting Spartans bury within the city and keep memorials near the sacred places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The point, he says, was to make the young familiar with such sights, so that they had no horror of death and did not count a corpse or a graveyard as polluting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing went into the grave with the body, which was covered with a scarlet robe and olive leaves and laid away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No name might be cut on a tomb except that of a man who had fallen in war or of a woman who had died in sacred office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mourning was fixed at eleven days, and on the twelfth they sacrificed to Demeter and had done with grief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A king's death was another matter: Herodotus says horsemen carried the news through Laconia while women went about the city striking a copper kettle, and two free persons of every household had to go into mourning under heavy penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fixed numbers of perioikoi were compelled to attend along with helots and Spartans, thousands beating their foreheads and crying that the king last dead was the best of all, and for ten days there was no assembly and no election.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A king killed in war was carried out in effigy, and Xenophon says the laws plainly mean that the kings of Lacedaemon are honoured not as men but as heroes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias saw the tombs of Leonidas and Pausanias opposite the theatre and a slab naming those who endured Thermopylae with their fathers' names; the rule is visible still in the Kerameikos at Athens, over the Spartans who fell there in 403 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One clause is disputed: the manuscripts of Plutarch say a woman in sacred office, while a much-adopted emendation makes it a woman dead in childbirth, a symmetry with death in battle that recent work calls an erroneous conjecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan burial custom","tags":["practice","history","greece","religion","archaic","classical"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 27, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, vol. 1, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 6.58–59, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 15, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), Project Gutenberg ebook 1178, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.14, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 129, 236, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians_7.JPG/1920px-Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians_7.JPG","title":"Tomb of the Lacedaemonians, Kerameikos","alt":"A long low wall of dressed limestone blocks in an excavated cemetery, an earth bank behind it","desc":"Kerameikos, Athens, Greece: View from north-east on the Tomb of the Lacedaemonians. Schuppi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians_7.JPG"}},
{"id":"gr-275","num":275,"category":"Sparta","question":"Sacrifice to Zeus the Leader at home, another at the border and a she-goat in the battle line all belonged to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which could turn a whole army back unfought.","questions":["Each Spartan king kept two envoys to Delphi at the public table, and every army carried a seer, because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was state business rather than a matter of private piety.","Thucydides records two full mobilisations abandoned at the border in one summer because the crossing victims came out wrong, which shows how much weight <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried in war."],"answer":"Spartan divination","answerText":"Spartan divination","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Spartan divination</b> was the reading of signs from sacrificial animals and from oracles, and Sparta built it into the machinery of kingship and war rather than leaving it to private piety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus lists among a king's privileges the right to choose two Pythioi, envoys sent to consult Apollo at Delphi who ate with him at public charge; the kings kept the oracles that came back, and the Pythioi knew them too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every army carried a seer, and the most celebrated was Tisamenus of Elis, of the Iamid family of prophets, whom the Spartans wanted so badly that he raised his price until they made him a citizen. He then demanded the same for his brother Hegias and got it, and Herodotus says the two were the only men ever made fellow citizens of Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon describes a campaign opening with sacrifice at home to Zeus Agetor, the Leader, after which a fire-bearer took fire from that altar and carried it ahead of the army, never letting it go out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the boundary the king sacrificed again, to Zeus and Athena, and crossed only when both accepted; the Greeks called this rite the diabateria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That was no formality: in a single summer Thucydides records two full Spartan mobilisations abandoned at the frontier, one under Agis at Leuctra and another at Caryae, because the crossing sacrifices came out wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sacrifice went on into the fighting itself, with an offering to the Muses before combat and, once the men were drawn up, a goat killed to Artemis Agrotera while they put on garlands and the pipers played the hymn to Castor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Plataea the offerings would not come favourable while Persian arrows fell among the Spartans, until <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Pausanias_the_regent\">Pausanias</span> fixed his gaze on the temple of Hera and prayed, the Tegeans went forward, and the victims turned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A king on campaign was therefore priest as much as general, and the sign he read could commit an army or send it home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 6.57, 9.33–35, 9.61–62, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Constitution of the Lacedaemonians</i> 13, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, trans. E. C. Marchant and G. W. Bowersock (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0210:text=Const.%20Lac.:chapter=13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.54–55, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 21–22, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 82, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-276","num":276,"category":"Athens","question":"Thucydides argued that the thin soil of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was why it escaped the faction that ruined richer parts of Greece, and so never changed its inhabitants.","questions":["Pentelic marble, Hymettus honey and the silver at Laurion all came out of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose thin soil Thucydides thought too poor to be worth invading.","Strabo knew <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by its older name Acte, because most of it lies flat along the sea, and said its countryside held demes too numerous to list."],"answer":"Attica","answerText":"Attica","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Attica</b> is the peninsula at the south-eastern corner of central Greece, sea on two sides and Athens set in its largest plain; Strabo records that the older name for it was Acte, because most of it lies below the mountains and stretches flat and narrow along the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo lists its mountains as Hymettus, Brilessus, Lycabettus, Parnes and Corydallus, and traces its coast from the Isthmus round to the cape at Sounion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias sums up what each range was for: quarries on Pentelicus, hunting for wild boar and bear on Parnes, and on Hymettus the most suitable pasture for bees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The soil between them is thin, and Thucydides turns that poverty into the explanation of Athens' whole early history: because Attica was not worth taking it escaped the faction that ruined richer lands, and never changed its inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strongest men driven out of the rest of Greece took refuge there and were naturalised, until the region could not hold them and Athens began sending colonies to Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What Attica had instead of good farmland was stone and metal, and Strabo calls the Hymettian and Pentelic quarries the finest near the city and Hymettus honey the best there is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his own day the silver at Laurion in the south-east was worked out, and the miners were re-smelting the old slag heaps for what earlier and clumsier furnaces had left in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before the Persian invasion it had been rich enough that the Athenians proposed to share a windfall out at ten drachmas a head, until Themistocles talked them into building 200 ships with it instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That fleet was meant for a war with Aegina, and Herodotus says it proved the salvation of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo thought the demes of the Attic interior too many to recount, and when Sparta invaded year after year in the Peloponnesian War that whole country population moved inside the walls of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.2, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 7.144, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.1.1–23, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D1%3Asection%3D23. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.32.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Geoff Bakewell, review of Nicholas F. Jones, <i>Rural Athens under the Democracy</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2005.04.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.03/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/As_seen_from_Cape_Sounion_Greece.jpg/1920px-As_seen_from_Cape_Sounion_Greece.jpg","title":"Attica","alt":"As seen from Cape Sounion, Greece","desc":"Coastline toward north from Cape Sounion, Greece. Jebulon, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:As_seen_from_Cape_Sounion_Greece.jpg"},"locator":{"name":"Attica","at":[23.7,38],"kind":"region","area":[[23.4,38.12],[23.58,38.28],[23.82,38.32],[24.02,38.24],[24.08,38.06],[24.06,37.86],[24.03,37.64],[23.88,37.72],[23.74,37.84],[23.56,37.94],[23.42,37.99]]}},
{"id":"gr-277","num":277,"category":"Athens","question":"Thucydides says that before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Attica was a set of independent townships that consulted the king at Athens only in danger and sometimes made war on him.","questions":["Athenians kept their land and their country homes after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but their council-chambers and magistracies were abolished and merged into the single town hall of Athens.","The festival of the Synoecia, paid for by the state, kept the memory of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Strabo says joined the twelve towns of Cecrops under Theseus."],"answer":"Synoecism of Attica","answerText":"Synoecism of Attica","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>synoecism of Attica</b> is the tradition that the region's separate communities were merged into one state centred on Athens, and the fullest ancient account of it is Thucydides'. He writes that from Cecrops down to Theseus, Attica had always consisted of independent townships, each with its own council-chamber and magistrates, which consulted the king at Athens only in danger and sometimes made war on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Theseus, being a king of equal intelligence and power, abolished those councils and magistracies and merged them in the single council-chamber and town hall of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Private property was untouched — people kept their land and went on living where they had — but they now had one political centre, and every inhabitant of Attica counted as an Athenian citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides adds that the union was still commemorated in his own day by the Synoecia, a feast paid for by the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo, following Philochorus, says Cecrops first settled the people in twelve cities and that Theseus later united the twelve into one; he names eleven — Cecropia, Tetrapolis, Epacria, Deceleia, Eleusis, Aphidna, Thoricus, Brauron, Cytherus, Sphettus and Cephisia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch has Theseus canvassing them township by township and clan by clan, offering the powerful a state without a king in which he would be only commander in war and guardian of the laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Aristotelian Constitution of the Athenians counted his settlement as the second of the eleven constitutions Athens had had, and the first that involved a constitutional point at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much of this is history is another matter: the Athenians dated it to the heroic age, and excavation in the Agora finds no radical change in where Athenians lived between the Bronze Age and the Early Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What Thucydides describes is in any case a political union rather than a move, which is why he can say a few lines later that most Athenians still found it hard to leave their country homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.14–16, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.1.20, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D1%3Asection%3D20. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Theseus</i> 24, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Theseus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41.2, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-278","num":278,"category":"Athens","question":"The claim of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was that its people had sprung from the soil and never come from anywhere else, and Pericles opened his funeral speech with it.","questions":["Erichthonius, whom Pausanias says had no human father but was born of Hephaestus and Earth, stands in the line of early kings that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was built on.","The Periclean law of 451 BCE that confined citizenship to those with two Athenian parents gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> political force, since Athenians came to justify their status by birth and descent."],"answer":"Athenian autochthony","answerText":"Athenian autochthony","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Athenian autochthony</b> was the claim that Athenians had sprung from the soil of Attica itself and had lived there ever since, never having arrived from anywhere else. Herodotus has Athenian envoys make the boast to Gelon of Syracuse: theirs is the most ancient race of all, and alone among the Greeks they have never moved from one land to another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides gives the same fact a cause, saying that Attica's thin soil made it not worth taking, so it escaped the faction that emptied richer lands and kept its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pericles opens his funeral speech with it — the ancestors dwelt in the country without break in the succession from generation to generation and handed it down free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The traditions behind the claim run through a line of early kings, and Pausanias gives them: Actaeus first, then his son-in-law Cecrops, then Cranaus, whose daughter Atthis gave the land the name Attica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Cranaus was deposed by Amphictyon and Amphictyon by Erichthonius, of whom Pausanias says that he had no human father but was born of Hephaestus and Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus knew a temple on the Acropolis of Erechtheus, said to have been born of the Earth, and in it the olive and the salt spring that Athena and Poseidon set as witnesses when they contended for the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Persians burned the temple the olive put out a shoot a cubit long the next day, and those sent up to sacrifice reported it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus also records the sequence of names the Athenians gave themselves — Cranaoi as Pelasgians, Cecropidae under Cecrops, Athenians under Erechtheus, Ionians after Ion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The claim did political work: Pericles' law of 451 BCE confined citizenship to those with two Athenian parents, and it has been argued that this pushed Athenians to define themselves by birth and descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 7.161, 8.44, 8.55, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.2, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.36, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.2.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Bruce LaForse, review of Susan Lape, <i>Race and Citizen Identity in the Classical Athenian Democracy</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2011.03.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.03.46/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Birth_Erikhthonios_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2413.jpg/1280px-Birth_Erikhthonios_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2413.jpg","title":"Athenian autochthony","alt":"Birth of Erichthonios on an Attic red-figure stamnos, Staatliche Antikensammlungen 2413","desc":"Birth of Erichthonius: Athena receives the baby Erichthonius from the hands of the earthmother Gaia. Hephaestus is watching the scene. Side A of an Attic red-figure stamnos, 470–460 BCE. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birth_Erikhthonios_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2413.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-279","num":279,"category":"Athens","question":"When Cylon seized the Acropolis, Thucydides says, most political business lay with the nine men holding the office of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which in the classical city lasted one year.","questions":["A candidate for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was asked before the Council and a jury who his grandfather was, where his family tombs lay, and whether he had done his military service.","Herodotus dates the Persian arrival in Attica by naming the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of that year, Calliades, which is how Athenians fixed a date."],"answer":"Archon","answerText":"Archon","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"An <b>archon</b> was one of the chief magistrates of Athens, and in the classical city there were nine of them, holding office for a single year. The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the greatest and oldest offices were three: the King, who was ancestral, the War-lord, added because some kings proved cowardly in war, and the Archon, instituted last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were filled by birth and wealth, held first for life and then for a ten-year term, and the six Lawgivers were added many years later, once elections had become yearly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That text also says the archonship only became great late, since the Archon administered none of the ancestral rites but only duties added afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Solon's laws were set up in the Royal Colonnade, and there the nine swore at the Stone that if they broke one they would dedicate a golden statue of a man, an oath still taken in the text's own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Their power in the archaic city was real: Thucydides says that when Cylon seized the Acropolis, most political functions were discharged by the nine archons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the archonship of Telesinus they were chosen by lot, tribe by tribe, from a list of five hundred put up by the demesmen, where before they had all been elected by vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the classical system a candidate faced a scrutiny before the Council and again before a jury, and was asked his father's name and deme, his grandfather's, his mother's, whether he had family tombs and a Family Apollo, whether he treated his parents well and had done his military service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the nine gave his name to the year, so Herodotus can say that the Persians reached Attica when Calliades was archon of the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Modern work is not bound to follow the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> on any of this, and Ste. Croix argued that selection by lot from a preselected list was not Solon's at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 3, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 55, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 8.51, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","David Whitehead, review of G. E. M. de Ste. Croix, <i>Athenian Democratic Origins and Other Essays</i>, ed. David Harvey and Robert Parker, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2004.08.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.08.16/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Stoa_Basileios.jpg/1280px-Stoa_Basileios.jpg","title":"Archon","alt":"Foundations of the Stoa Basileios in the Athenian Agora","desc":"Stoa Basileios. Vanbasten 23, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stoa_Basileios.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-280","num":280,"category":"Athens","question":"Trials for deliberate murder, for wounding, for causing death by poison and for arson were held on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while unintentional killing went to other courts.","questions":["Members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were appointed from men who had been archons, which is why it was the one Athenian office still held for life.","In the archonship of Conon, Ephialtes stripped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the powers that made it the safeguard of the constitution and handed them to the Five Hundred, the People and the jury-courts."],"answer":"Areopagus","answerText":"Areopagus","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Areopagus</b> was a council at Athens named after the hill of Ares on which it met, and its members were men who had held the archonship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says its official function was to guard the laws, but that it administered the greatest number and the most important of the affairs of state, and fined and punished offenders against public order without appeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the archons were chosen by birth and wealth and the Areopagites were appointed from them, this alone of the offices was still held for life when that text was written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the time before Solon the Council summoned and chose on its own authority the man suitable for each office and commissioned him to hold it for a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Solon set it to guard the laws, left it watching over the greatest affairs of state, let it render its accounts to the Acropolis without saying why the money had gone, and gave it the trial of men conspiring to put down the democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The constitution stayed under the leadership of the Areopagites for about seventeen years after the Persian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then Ephialtes prosecuted many of them over their conduct in office and, in the archonship of Conon, stripped the Council of the added powers that made it the safeguard of the constitution, handing them to the Five Hundred, the People and the jury-courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was murdered not long afterwards by Aristodicus of Tanagra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it kept was the gravest homicide: trials for deliberate murder and wounding, for causing death by poison and for arson were held on the Areopagus, while unintentional killing and the killing of a slave or a foreigner went elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hill carried its own stories: Pausanias says Ares was tried there first, that Orestes was later tried for killing his mother and dedicated an altar on being acquitted, and that the unhewn stones where accused and accuser stood were called Outrage and Ruthlessness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 3, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 25, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 57, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.28.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Areopagus._Mars_Hill._Temple_of_Theseus_-_D._Constantin._LCCN94513428.jpg/1280px-Areopagus._Mars_Hill._Temple_of_Theseus_-_D._Constantin._LCCN94513428.jpg","title":"Areopagus","alt":"The rock of the Areopagus at Athens in a 19th-century photograph, with the Temple of Theseus beyond","desc":"Title: Areopagus. Mars Hill. Temple of Theseus / D. Constantin. Abstract/medium: 1 photographic print : albumen. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Areopagus._Mars_Hill._Temple_of_Theseus_-_D._Constantin._LCCN94513428.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-281","num":281,"category":"Athens","question":"After Damasias was driven from office the Athenians elected ten archons instead of nine, five of them from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, three from the rustics and two from the artisans.","questions":["Plutarch says Theseus gave the noblemen of Athens, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the care of religious rites, the supply of magistrates and the interpretation of the will of Heaven.","Before Solon the supreme offices at Athens went by birth and wealth, which kept them in the hands of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and filled the Areopagus from the same families."],"answer":"Eupatridae","answerText":"Eupatridae","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Eupatridae</b> were the nobility of Athens before Solon, when appointment to the supreme offices went by birth and wealth and those offices were held first for life and afterwards for a term of ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says Theseus was the first to separate the people into noblemen, husbandmen and handicraftsmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> To the noblemen he gave the care of religious rites, the supply of magistrates, the teaching of the laws and the interpretation of the will of Heaven, the three orders being thought to excel respectively in dignity, in usefulness and in numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The arrangement fed itself, because the Council of the Areopagus was appointed from the men who had held the archonship, so the body that guarded the laws was filled from the families that supplied the magistrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beneath them the constitution was oligarchical in every respect: the poor and their wives and children were in slavery to the rich, were called Clients and Sixth-part-tenants, and could be seized with their children if the rent went unpaid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When the people rose against the notables the two parties jointly chose Solon as arbitrator and archon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was of the first rank by birth and reputation but belonged by wealth and position to the middle class, which is a reminder that the two qualifications could come apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The archonship went on being fought over after him: in the fifth year after his own archonship no archon was appointed because of party strife, and none again in the fifth year after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Damasias was then elected and held the office two years and two months until he was driven out of it by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The quarrel was settled by electing ten archons instead of nine — five from the Eupatridae, three from the Agroikoi or rustics and two from the Demiourgoi or artisans — a board dated to 580 BCE, with the office shared among the three orders rather than kept by one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Constitution of Athens</i> 13, trans. E. Poste (London: Macmillan, 1891), https://archive.org/details/aristotleonconst00arisiala. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 3, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 13, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Theseus</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Theseus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 2, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-282","num":282,"category":"Athens","question":"Told at Delphi to seize the Acropolis at the grand festival of Zeus, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> struck during the Olympic festival in the Peloponnese and made himself master of the citadel.","questions":["Pausanias could not say why the Athenians set up a bronze statue of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> despite his plotting a tyranny, and guessed it was for his beauty and his Olympic victory.","The men who killed the suppliants left behind by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called accursed, they and their descendants, and the Athenians drove them out of the city."],"answer":"Cylon","answerText":"Cylon","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Cylon</b> was an Athenian of good birth and powerful position who had won at the Olympic games and married a daughter of Theagenes, tyrant of Megara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inquiring at Delphi, he was told by the god to seize the Acropolis of Athens on the grand festival of Zeus, so he took a force from Theagenes, persuaded his friends to join him and struck when the Olympic festival came round in the Peloponnese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says he never thought to ask whether the festival meant was in Attica or elsewhere, the Athenians having a grand festival of Zeus of their own, the Diasia, celebrated outside the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Athenians flocked in from the country and laid siege to the citadel, and when most of them wearied of the blockade and left, the watch was handed to the nine archons with full powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus tells it otherwise, saying the suppliants were taken from the altar by the presidents of the naucraries, who at that time administered affairs at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Cylon and his brother escaped by Thucydides' account, while Plutarch has Megacles the archon coax the besieged down to stand trial holding a braided thread tied to the goddess's image, which broke of its own accord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those caught outside the sacred precincts were stoned, those at the altars were killed where they sat, and only the men who supplicated the archons' wives were spared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From that deed the killers were called accursed and guilty against the goddess, they and their descendants, and the Athenians drove them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were later tried before three hundred jurors chosen for noble birth, Myron prosecuting; the guilty were sentenced to everlasting banishment and their bodies cast out of their tombs, after which Epimenides of Crete purified the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias saw a bronze statue of Cylon on the Acropolis and could not say why a man who had plotted a tyranny was honoured, guessing it was for his beauty and his Olympic win in the double foot-race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 5.71, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 1, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.28.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-283","num":283,"category":"Athens","question":"Solon repealed the laws of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, all except those concerning homicide, because they were too severe and their penalties too heavy.","questions":["Asked why death was the penalty for most offences, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have answered that the lesser ones deserved it and for the greater none heavier could be found.","Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> legislated for a constitution that already existed, and that nothing in his laws is worth mentioning except their severity in imposing heavy punishment."],"answer":"Draco","answerText":"Draco","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Draco</b> was the lawgiver under whom, as the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts it, a code of laws was first published at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That text dates his ordinances to the archonship of Aristaechmus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sets out a whole system under him: citizenship for those who provided themselves with arms, the nine archons and the treasurers drawn from men holding an unencumbered estate worth at least ten minae, and the generals and masters of the horse from men worth a hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A council of 401 was to be chosen by lot from the citizens over thirty, nobody holding office twice until the whole number had been gone through, with fines for missing a meeting graded from three drachmae down to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> puts it differently, saying that Draco legislated for a constitution that already existed and that nothing in his laws is worth mentioning except their severity in imposing heavy punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch says one penalty was assigned to almost all transgressions, namely death, so that men convicted of idleness were put to death and those who stole salad or fruit were punished like those who committed sacrilege or murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked why he had made death the penalty for most offences, Draco is said to have answered that in his opinion the lesser ones deserved it, and that for the greater no heavier penalty could be found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The orator Demades said in later times that Draco's laws were written not with ink but with blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Solon established his own constitution the Athenians ceased to observe Draco's ordinances, all except those relating to homicide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch gives the reason for the repeal plainly — the laws were too severe and their penalties too heavy — so what Athens kept of Draco was the law on killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 4, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 1274b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-284","num":284,"category":"Athens","question":"In 409/8 BCE the Athenians had <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut on a stone stele and set up in front of the Royal Stoa, which is why its wording survives.","questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a man who killed without forethought was exiled, and the 51 ephetai decided whether the killing had been unwilling.","Reconciliation under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to be granted by the dead man's father, brother and sons together, since a single objector among them prevailed over the rest."],"answer":"Draco's homicide law","answerText":"Draco's homicide law","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Draco's homicide law</b> was the one part of his code the Athenians kept when Solon repealed the rest, and its wording is known because they had it cut in stone again two centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 409/8 BCE, when Diokles was archon, the Council and the People decided that the inscribers of the laws should take the law over from the King and set it on a stele in front of the Royal Stoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives opens on the first axon: even if a man kills another not from forethought, he shall be exiled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The kings were to pronounce a man responsible for homicide, whether he had killed or had planned it, and the ephetai, the appeal judges, were to decide the case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reconciliation could be granted by the dead man's father, brother or sons, but only by all of them together, since one objector prevailed; failing them the right passed outward as far as cousins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Where a killer had no such kin, 51 ephetai decided whether the killing was unwilling, and ten men of a phratry, chosen by the 51 for their excellence, could let him back in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The law bound men who had killed before it, required a proclamation against the killer in the agora, and named who joined the prosecution: cousins, cousins' sons, brothers-in-law, fathers-in-law and phratry members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also protected the exile, since anyone who killed him while he kept away from frontier markets, Amphiktyonic contests and rites was liable as for killing an Athenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Demosthenes quotes a law giving the Council of the Areopagus cognizance of homicide, intentional wounding, arson, and poisoning where a man kills by giving poison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those charges were still tried on the Areopagus in the classical city, while unintentional killing went to the court at the Palladium, which is the division the first axon already draws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Decree to Republish Draco's Law on Homicide</i> (OR 183A; IG I<sup>3</sup> 104), trans. Stephen Lambert and P. J. Rhodes, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/104. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 57, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Aristocrates</i> 22, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0074%3Aspeech%3D23%3Asection%3D22. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Protos_Axon_%28Draco%29.png","title":"Draco's homicide law","alt":"Drawing of the words ΠΡΟΤΟΣ ΑΧΣΟΝ, 'first axon', from the inscription of Draco's homicide law","desc":"Drawing of the Greek phrase \"ΠΡΟΤΟΣ ΑΧΣΟΝ\" (\"first axon\"), taken from an inscription containing Draco's law on homicide. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protos_Axon_%28Draco%29.png"}},
{"id":"gr-285","num":285,"category":"Athens","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked the land of the rich in Attica and handed over a sixth of what it produced, which is where their name comes from.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who failed to pay could be arrested along with their children, since down to Solon's time all borrowing at Athens was on the security of the debtor's person.","Solon says he removed the boundary-posts implanted in the Black Earth, freeing the land the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had worked while paying a sixth of its produce to others."],"answer":"Hektemoroi","answerText":"Hektemoroi","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>hektemoroi</b> were the dependent farmers of Attica before Solon, so called because they tilled the land of the rich and paid over a sixth of what it produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls them Sixth-part-tenants and Clients, says the whole of the country was in few hands, and adds that the poor, their wives and their children were in slavery to the rich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If they ever failed to pay their rents they and their children were liable to arrest, and down to the time of Solon all borrowing was on the security of the debtor's person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says a man who had pledged himself could be seized by his creditor, some becoming slaves at home and others being sold into foreign countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many were driven to sell their own children, there being no law against it, or to leave the country altogether because of the cruelty of the money-lenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The most grievous and bitter thing in public affairs for the masses was this slavery, though the same text says they were discontented about everything else too, finding themselves virtually without a share in anything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The many being enslaved to the few, the people rose against the notables, and after a long and violent struggle the two parties jointly chose Solon as arbitrator and archon, who freed them by prohibiting loans secured on the person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Solon's own poetry, quoted in the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i>, is where his account of the arrangement survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He calls the Black Earth to witness that it was he who removed the many boundary-posts implanted in her, so that she who had been a slave was free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He says he brought home men sold abroad, some unjustly and some justly, and others who had fled from dire need until they no longer spoke their Attic tongue, and freed those suffering base slavery at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 2, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 12, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 6, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-286","num":286,"category":"Athens","question":"Both sides of a long and violent party struggle jointly chose <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as arbitrator and archon, and put the government of Athens into his hands.","questions":["The laws of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were written up on the Boards and set in the Royal Colonnade, where everyone swore to observe them and the nine archons swore at the Stone.","Because the settlement disappointed both factions, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> might have made himself tyrant by taking either side, and chose to incur the enmity of both instead."],"answer":"Solon","answerText":"Solon","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Solon</b> was the Athenian whom both parties to a long and violent struggle jointly chose as arbitrator and archon, entrusting the government to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was of the first rank by birth and reputation but belonged by wealth and position to the middle class, which the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the other authorities admit and his own poems confirm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He laid the blame for the civil strife wholly on the rich, exhorting them to restrain their stubborn moods and to moderate their pride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the wisest of the Athenians turned to him because he was least implicated in the errors of the time, neither joined with the rich in their injustice nor caught in the necessities of the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Solon himself said that he entered public life reluctantly, fearing one party's greed and the other party's arrogance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> He established a constitution and made other laws, and from then on the Athenians ceased to observe Draco's ordinances except those relating to homicide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The laws were written up on the Boards and set in the Royal Colonnade, where all swore to observe them and the nine archons swore at the Stone to dedicate a golden statue if they broke one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He fixed them to stay unaltered for a hundred years, and when people kept coming to criticise and question them he refused to stay and explain, holding that everybody should carry out their provisions for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his own verse he says that nothing pleased his mind about compassing power by tyrannic force, nor that good and bad men should have equal portion in the fatherland's fertile soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The settlement disappointed both factions, so that he might have been tyrant had he taken either side, and he chose instead to incur the enmity of both and go abroad for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 11, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 12, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-287","num":287,"category":"Athens","question":"Solon's cancellation of debts was called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a word meaning a shaking-off of burdens, since the people shook off the load they had been carrying.","questions":["Androtion held that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> relieved the poor not by cancelling debts but by reducing the interest on them, and by an increase in measures and in the value of money.","Three of Solon's intimates, told in confidence what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would do, borrowed heavily and bought estates before it was published, then kept the land and refused their creditors."],"answer":"Seisachtheia","answerText":"Seisachtheia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>seisachtheia</b> was Solon's relief of debt at Athens, a name meaning the shaking-off of burdens, since the people shook off their load.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he made the people free both at the time and for the future by prohibiting loans secured on the person, and enacted cancellations of debts both private and public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Solon was the first to soften an ugly thing with a kindly name, the Athenians afterwards calling harlots companions, taxes contributions and the prison a chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His first public measure, by that account, was that existing debts should be remitted and that in future nobody should lend money on the person of a borrower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his own verse Solon says he took away the record-stones that were planted everywhere in the mortgaged land, so that the Earth, before in bondage, was free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the measure actually did was disputed in antiquity: Androtion and others held that the poor were relieved not by a cancelling of debts but by a reduction of the interest on them, together with an increase in measures and in the purchasing power of money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that account the mina was made to hold a hundred drachmas where it had held 73, so a debtor discharged the same sum in money of lesser value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch reports that most writers took it for a removal of all debt, and that Solon's own poems accord better with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three of his intimates, told in confidence that he meant to cancel debts but not touch the land, borrowed heavily and bought estates, then kept them and refused their creditors, which brought him into great odium until it emerged that he had remitted five talents of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He pleased neither party, the rich vexed at losing their securities and the poor more so because he did not redistribute the land, and both turned against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 6, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 15, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 12, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 11, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-288","num":288,"category":"Athens","question":"The four ranks into which citizens were sorted by the yearly yield of their land are called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and they fixed which offices each man could hold.","questions":["Plutarch says the lowest rank in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held no office at all but sat in the assembly and the law-courts, a privilege that afterwards proved of the highest importance.","It was already disputed in antiquity whether the cavalry rank of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was measured by yearly produce or simply by a man's ability to keep a horse."],"answer":"Solon's property classes","answerText":"Solon's property classes","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Solon's property classes</b> were four ranks into which the Athenians were divided by assessment, as they had been divided before, and which fixed what part each man took in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> names them Five-hundred-measure man, Horseman, Teamster and Labourer, the first three requiring 500, 300 and 200 measures of dry and liquid produce together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch gives the Greek names Pentakosiomedimnoi, Hippada Telountes, Zeugitai and Thetes, and says the second were those able to keep a horse or reaching 300 measures, who paid a knight's tax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The offices were held from the top three classes alone: the nine archons, the treasurers, the vendors of contracts, the Eleven and the paymasters, each assigned in proportion to a class's assessment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those rated in the labourer class were admitted to membership of the assembly and the law-courts alone, a share Plutarch says seemed at first of no moment and afterwards proved of the very highest importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> As proof that offices went by assessment the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> points to the law on the treasurers, which orders them to be chosen by lot from the Five-hundred-measure men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> says Solon appointed all the offices from the notable and the wealthy, leaving the people only the power of electing the magistrates and calling them to account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How the second class was measured was already disputed when the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> was written, some saying it went by produce, others by the ability to keep a horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those on the second side adduced a statue on the Acropolis inscribed for Anthemion son of Diphilus, who had exchanged the labourer rating for the cavalry, with a horse standing beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch's account of Solon's valuations reckons a sheep and a bushel of grain at a drachma apiece, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> adds that long afterwards no man drawing lots for an office would admit to a labourer's rating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274a. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 23, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-289","num":289,"category":"Athens","question":"The richest of Solon's four classes were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose land yielded 500 measures of dry and liquid produce together, and from whom the treasurers of Athena were drawn.","questions":["Plutarch says those whose land gave a yearly increase of 500 measures wet and dry were placed in the first class and called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, above the horsemen and the teamsters.","Although the treasurers of Athena had to be drawn from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> adds that the man on whom the lot fell held office even if quite poor."],"answer":"Pentakosiomedimnoi","answerText":"Pentakosiomedimnoi","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Pentakosiomedimnoi</b> were the first and richest of the four classes into which Solon divided the Athenians by assessment, the name meaning five-hundred-measure men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says a man had to be rated in the class if the produce of his estate came to 500 dry and liquid measures jointly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below them came the horsemen at 300 measures and the teamsters at 200, while the rest were rated in the labourer class and admitted to no office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch gives the same figure, placing in the first class those who enjoyed a yearly increase of 500 measures wet and dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The offices were shared out among the top three classes in proportion to the amount of their assessment, so the highest rating carried the highest posts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One office was reserved to this class alone, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> cites it as proof that office went by assessment: the law ordering the treasurers to be chosen by lot from the Five-hundred-measure men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those treasurers were the ten Treasurers of Athena, one drawn by lot from each tribe, who took over the custody of the statue of Athena, the Victories, the other monuments and the funds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Describing the Athens of its own day, the same work says that Solon's law about them was still in force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It adds in the same breath that the man on whom the lot fell held office even though he was quite a poor man, so the qualification stood in the law without being applied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch's account of Solon's valuations reckons a bushel of grain at a drachma, which gives some measure of what 500 a year meant, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says that nobody drawing lots for an office would admit to a labourer's rating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 47, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 23, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-290","num":290,"category":"Athens","question":"The third of Solon's classes were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose land yielded 200 measures wet and dry together, and who held the ordinary offices but not at first the archonship.","questions":["Five years after the death of Ephialtes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became eligible for the roll from which the nine archons were drawn by lot, and Mnesitheides was the first to hold it.","Plutarch calls the class whose yearly increase amounted to 200 measures the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ranking them below the horsemen and above the labourers who held no office at all."],"answer":"Zeugitai","answerText":"Zeugitai","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Zeugitai</b> were the third of the four classes into which Solon divided the Athenians by assessment, ranking below the five-hundred-measure men and the horsemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says men had to be rated in the class who made 200 measures, wet and dry together, from their land, the lowest of the three thresholds Solon set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch gives the same figure and the Greek name, placing in the class those whose yearly increase amounted to 200 measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both accounts define the class by produce alone, and neither attaches any military duty or equipment to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Below them were the labourers, who were admitted to no office and took part only as members of the assembly and the law-courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Offices were distributed among the top three classes in proportion to the amount of their assessment: the nine archons, the treasurers, the vendors of contracts, the Eleven and the paymasters, so a rating decided not merely whether a man could serve but where.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> counts them among the notable and the wealthy from whom Solon appointed all the offices, the fourth class alone being admitted to none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One post was closed to them outright, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> citing the law that ordered the treasurers to be chosen by lot from the five-hundred-measure men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archonship was closed to them as well: all the archons hitherto had come from the horsemen and the five-hundred-measure men, while the teamsters held the ordinary offices, unless some provision of the laws was ignored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Five years after the death of Ephialtes eligibility for the roll from which the nine archons were drawn by lot was extended to the class, and the first of them to hold the office was Mnesitheides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 26, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D26. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-291","num":291,"category":"Athens","question":"Everyone whose land fell short of 200 measures was rated among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who held no office at all but sat in the assembly and the law-courts.","questions":["Plutarch says the share in the courts given to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seemed at first of no moment, but afterwards proved of the very highest importance as most disputes came before those jurors.","When Athens broke into factions while Solon was abroad, the multitude of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood with the Hill-men under Peisistratus, being the bitter enemies of the rich."],"answer":"Thetes","answerText":"Thetes","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Thetes</b> were the fourth and lowest of the classes into which Solon divided the Athenians, taking in everyone whose land fell short of the 200 measures that made a teamster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says they were admitted to no office at all, having a share in the membership of the assembly and the law-courts alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the same, and adds that this last privilege seemed at first of no moment but afterwards proved of the very highest importance, since most disputes finally came into the hands of these jurors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He explains why: even in cases that Solon assigned to the magistrates for decision, he allowed an appeal to a popular court whenever anyone wanted one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> goes further, saying Solon founded the democracy by constituting the jury-courts from all the citizens, while bestowing on the people only the power of electing the magistrates and calling them to account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The word was older than the rating, and Plutarch uses it earlier in the same life for the men who tilled the land of the rich for a sixth of the increase, called Hectemorioi and Thetes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As a census class it was a residue rather than a threshold, since a man fell into it by failing to reach the one above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rating was not enforced for long, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says that when the presiding official asked a man about to draw lots for an office what rate he paid, nobody whatever would say he was rated a labourer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The class could also act together, and Plutarch says that when Athens broke into factions while Solon was abroad, the multitude of Thetes stood with the Hill-men under Peisistratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He calls them the bitter enemies of the rich, and says that although the city still observed the new laws, everyone was by then expecting a revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274a. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 29, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-292","num":292,"category":"Athens","question":"Solon set up a second council beside the Areopagus, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a hundred men drawn from each of the four old tribes of Athens.","questions":["Plutarch says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was to deliberate on public matters before the people did, and to let nothing come before the assembly without such deliberation first.","Plutarch calls the Areopagus the upper council and says that with it and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the city rode as it were at double anchor, less tossed by the surges."],"answer":"Council of Four Hundred","answerText":"Council of Four Hundred","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Council of Four Hundred</b> was the second council of Solon's settlement at Athens, made up of a hundred men from each of the four tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he made it and gave the Council of the Areopagus the duty of guarding the laws, as it had overseen the constitution before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says he set it up after noticing that the common people had grown uneasy and bold in consequence of their release from debt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its business was to deliberate on public matters before the people did, and to let no matter come before the popular assembly without such deliberation first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With two councils, Plutarch says, the city rode as it were at double anchor, and would be less tossed by the surges and keep its populace in greater quiet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The four tribes were the old ones, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> noting that there were four tribes as before and four tribal kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A council was not itself new, since the same work's account of Draco's ordinances describes a Council of 401 chosen by lot from the citizens over thirty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That earlier body fined a councillor who missed a sitting according to his rating: 3 drachmas from a five-hundred-measure man, 2 from a knight and 1 from a teamster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The graver business stayed with the Areopagus, which corrected offenders with sovereign power to fine and punish and tried men who conspired to put down the democracy, under a law of impeachment Solon laid down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch adds that the council took a joint oath to ratify the laws, while the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> counts Solon's settlement third among eleven changes of constitution and the Four Hundred of a later oligarchy eighth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 4, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-293","num":293,"category":"Athens","question":"The popular court at Athens was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose jurors were ordinary citizens and whose right to hear appeals the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls the chief basis of the people's power.","questions":["Plutarch says Solon's laws were reported to be obscurely and ambiguously worded on purpose, so as to enhance the power of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its jurors.","In Aristophanes' comedy the old man Philocleon is of all men the fondest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and cannot sleep for thinking of the water-clock."],"answer":"Heliaia","answerText":"Heliaia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Heliaia</b> was the popular court of Athens, a tribunal of jurors drawn from the citizens, which in Aristophanes' comedy the old man Philocleon loves above all things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> counts the right of appeal to the jury-court as one of the three most democratic features of Solon's constitution, and the one said to be the chief basis of the power of the multitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its explanation is that the people, having the power of the vote, becomes sovereign in the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the same of the arrangement, that even in cases Solon assigned to the magistrates for decision he allowed an appeal to a popular court whenever anyone wanted one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The lowest class gained most, since the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the labourers were admitted to membership of the assembly and the law-courts alone and to no office at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The two accounts then disagree about the intention behind it, Plutarch reporting that Solon's laws were said to be obscurely and ambiguously worded on purpose, to enhance the power of the popular courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> knows that opinion and rejects it as unlikely, putting the obscurity down to his not being able to define the ideal in general terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grants the effect all the same, saying that because the laws are not drafted simply or clearly many disputes arise and the jury-court is the umpire in all business both public and private.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> makes the courts the whole of Solon's democratic legacy, saying he founded the democracy by constituting the jury-courts from all the citizens, for which some blamed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By Aristophanes' day, where the name is spelled Heliaea, the court had a pay and a temper of its own, and Philocleon swears by his Heliast's pay and wakes with his mind on the water-clock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 9, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D9. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>The Wasps</i>, in <i>The Eleven Comedies</i>, vol. 2, trans. the Athenian Society, Project Gutenberg ebook 8689, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/8689. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274a. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/AGMA_Klepsydra_-_Water_Clock_1.JPG/1280px-AGMA_Klepsydra_-_Water_Clock_1.JPG","title":"Heliaia","alt":"Klepsydra, a water clock, in the Ancient Agora Museum, Athens","desc":"Klepsydra - Water Clock: reconstruction of a clay original of the late 5th century BCE. Ancient Agora Museum in Athens. Dorieo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AGMA_Klepsydra_-_Water_Clock_1.JPG"}},
{"id":"gr-294","num":294,"category":"Athens","question":"Both ancient accounts of the lawgiver quote <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at length, since the verses are where he justified what he had done and rebuked those who wanted more.","questions":["Plutarch says the Salamis elegy, one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was recited from the herald's stone by a man feigning madness to evade a law forbidding the subject.","Answering those who had wanted the land divided, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> say he stood at guard on every side, a wolf at bay among a pack of hounds."],"answer":"Solon's poems","answerText":"Solon's poems","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Solon's poems</b> are the verses in which the Athenian lawgiver argued his own case, and they survive because later writers quoted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says he composed poetry at first with no serious end in view, as amusement and diversion in his hours of leisure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later he put philosophic maxims into verse and interwove political teachings in his poems, not simply to record them but because they contained justifications of his acts, with exhortations, admonitions and rebukes for the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some said he even attempted to reduce his laws to heroic verse before publishing them, opening with a prayer that Zeus give those laws success and fame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writing of wealth, he says two men are alike wealthy, one with much silver and gold and wide domains, the other with only enough for food and clothes and the enjoyment of child and wife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the parties chose him as arbitrator only after he had composed the elegy in which he marks with sorrow that Ionia's oldest land is being done to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same poem he tells the wealthy to refrain their stubborn moods and moderate their pride, and he attaches the blame for the civil strife wholly to the rich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A whole chapter of that work is given over to quoting him, including the lines where he says he gave the people grace enough and stood protecting both sides with a strong shield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Answering those who had wanted the land divided, he says nothing pleased his mind about compassing power by tyrannic force, and that he stood at guard on every side, a wolf at bay among a pack of hounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch tells how he feigned madness to evade a law forbidding any proposal about Salamis, put a cap on his head, climbed the herald's stone and recited an elegy of a hundred verses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 3, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 12, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e5/Papyrus131%28f.6v%29-The-Aristotelian-Constitution-of-Athens.jpg","title":"Solon's poems","alt":"Papyrus 131, verso, a leaf of Aristotle's Constitution of the Athenians in the British Library","desc":"Papyrus 131 (f.6v) - Aristotle's Constitution of the Athenians, British Library. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papyrus131%28f.6v%29-The-Aristotelian-Constitution-of-Athens.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-295","num":295,"category":"Athens","question":"After making his laws, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took him to Egypt and Cyprus for ten years, since he would not stay to explain the laws or to see them altered.","questions":["The reason Herodotus gives for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that he could then not be compelled to repeal a law, the Athenians having sworn to keep them ten years.","One stop on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Cyprus, where he persuaded a king to move his city down to the plain and was honoured when it was renamed Soli."],"answer":"Solon's travels","answerText":"Solon's travels","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Solon's travels</b> were the ten years he spent abroad after making his laws, and the ancient accounts explain them in three slightly different ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says people kept coming to worry him about the laws, so rather than alter them or stay and incur enmity he went abroad to Egypt, both to trade and to see the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He said he would not come back for ten years, thinking it unfair that he should stay and explain his laws instead of everybody carrying out their provisions for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says he made his ownership of a vessel an excuse for foreign travel and obtained leave of absence for ten years, hoping the Athenians would grow accustomed to the laws in that time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He quotes Solon's own remark that in great affairs it is difficult to please all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus gives a firmer reason, that he sailed forth to see the world so as not to be compelled to repeal any of his laws, the Athenians being bound by solemn oaths to abide by them for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Egypt, Plutarch has him living where the Nile pours forth his floods near the Canobic shore, and studying with the learned priests Psenophis of Heliopolis and Sonchis of Sais.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From them he is said to have heard the story of the lost Atlantis, and to have tried to introduce it in a poetical form to the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Cyprus he persuaded the king Philocyprus to move his city down to the fair plain below it, stayed to help lay the new one out, and was honoured when it was renamed Soli after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His interview with Croesus at Sardis some thought fictitious on chronological grounds, but Plutarch refused to reject a story so famous and well attested, and so worthy of Solon's magnanimity and wisdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 11, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 26, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 27, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.29, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-296","num":296,"category":"Athens","question":"In the course of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> no archon was appointed at all in two separate years, and Damasias, once elected, had to be driven out of the office by force.","questions":["Three parties formed during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, named from where their farms lay: the Men of the Coast, the Men of the Plain and the Hillmen.","Where the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> finds three parties in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Herodotus finds two and has Peisistratus raise a third because he coveted the sovereign power."],"answer":"Athenian party strife after Solon","answerText":"Athenian party strife after Solon","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Athenian party strife after Solon</b> broke out once he had gone abroad, and it fills the space the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> leaves between his settlement, third in its list of eleven changes of constitution, and the tyranny of Peisistratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That work measures the trouble by the archonship: for four years the city kept the peace, but in the fifth year after Solon's archonship the strife was such that no archon was appointed, and again five years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Damasias was then elected and held the office for two years and two months, until he was driven out of it by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Athenians next chose ten archons at once, five from the nobles, three from the farmers and two from the artisans, which that work takes as proof of the office's power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It says elsewhere that the trouble began because the settlement had disappointed both sides, the people having expected a general redistribution of property and the notables a return to the old system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Three parties formed, named from the districts where their farms lay: the Men of the Coast under Megacles son of Alcmaeon, the Men of the Plain under Lycurgus, and the Hillmen under Peisistratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their aims differed as much as their ground: the coast was thought to want a middle form of constitution, the plain an oligarchy, and the hill had chosen an extreme advocate of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the Hillmen stood those who had lost the debts owed to them and, out of fear, those not of pure descent, which the same work proves from the revision of the citizen roll after the tyrants fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch adds that the multitude of labourers was among them as bitter enemies of the rich, and that although the city still observed the new laws everyone was by then expecting a revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells it differently, making the feud a quarrel between coast and plain into which Peisistratus raised up a third faction because he coveted the sovereign power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 13, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 29, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.59, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 11, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-297","num":297,"category":"Athens","question":"When <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was born his father had been warned by Chilon at Olympia to disown any son he had, after vessels of meat and water boiled over without fire.","questions":["Summoned to the Areopagus on a charge of murder, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appeared in person to make his defence, and the man who had issued the summons took fright and left.","The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nineteen years of rule out of 33, where the <i>Politics</i> gives seventeen out of the same total."],"answer":"Peisistratus","answerText":"Peisistratus","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Peisistratus</b> held Athens as tyrant for the last stretch of a long life, and Herodotus introduces him as the son of Hippocrates, ruling an Attica divided into factions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That father, still a private man, was at Olympia for the games when vessels standing full of meat and water boiled over without fire, and Chilon the Lacedaemonian, who saw the marvel, advised him not to take a wife who could bear children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hippocrates refused the advice, and the son afterwards born to him raised up a third faction while the coast and the plain were at feud, coveting the sovereign power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had a reputation to raise it on: the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he had won great fame in the war against Megara, and Herodotus that he had taken Nisaea and performed other great exploits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Solon is said to have opposed his request for a bodyguard, calling himself wiser than those who did not know he was aiming at tyranny and braver than those who knew and held their tongues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus says that once in power he disturbed neither the order of offices nor the laws, but governed the city according to its established constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> agrees, calling him kindly and mild in everything, merciful to offenders, and willing to administer everything according to the laws without giving himself an advantage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both it and the <i>Politics</i> record that he was once summoned to the Areopagus on a charge of murder and appeared to make his defence, whereupon the man who had issued the summons took fright and left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He grew old in office and died of disease in the archonship of Philoneos, leaving two sons by his wedded wife, Hippias and Hipparchus, and two by Timonassa of Argos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two works disagree about how much of that time he ruled: both count 33 years from his first establishment, but one gives nineteen years in office and the other seventeen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 16, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 14, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.59, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 17, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 5.1315b, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1944), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D5%3Asection%3D1315b. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-298","num":298,"category":"Athens","question":"The first of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began with a self-inflicted wound blamed on his enemies, which persuaded the people to vote him a bodyguard of club-bearers.","questions":["The second of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made with a tall woman dressed in full armour and driven into the city in a chariot, heralds proclaiming that Athena was bringing him back.","The last of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was won by force at the temple of Pallenian Athena, after money from Pangaeus, hired soldiers, and help from Thebes and Lygdamis of Naxos."],"answer":"Peisistratus' three bids for power","answerText":"Peisistratus' three bids for power","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Peisistratus' three bids for power</b> were the attempts by which he seized Athens, was driven out twice, and came back at last by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the first he wounded himself, gave out that the opposite factions had done it, and so won a vote for a bodyguard, with whose help he seized the Acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus tells it with a wagon, saying he wounded his mules too and drove into the marketplace claiming to have escaped enemies, and that his guard carried wooden clubs instead of spears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the sixth year the partisans of Megacles and Lycurgus made common cause and expelled him before his government had taken root.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second was Megacles' doing: harried by faction, he offered his daughter in marriage and brought him back by dressing a tall woman as the goddess and driving her into the city beside him, heralds proclaiming that Athena was bringing Peisistratus back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus thought the device exceptionally foolish in men setting out to deceive the subtlest of the Greeks, but reports that the townsfolk took the woman for the goddess and welcomed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That hold lasted about seven years and broke on the marriage: the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he was unwilling to live with the daughter as his wife, Herodotus that he would not have children by a house under a curse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For the third he left the country, gathered money near Pangaeus and hired soldiers, and in the eleventh year went to Eretria, backed by the Thebans, by Lygdamis of Naxos and by the knights there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Landing at Marathon he was joined by countrymen who loved the rule of one more than freedom, and met the Athenians at the temple of Pallenian Athena, a diviner prophesying that the net was spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Winning that battle he held the tyranny firmly, and disarmed the people at a muster at the Temple of Theseus, speaking so quietly that they came up the hill to hear while his men gathered the arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 14, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 15, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.59, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.60, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.61, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D61. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.62, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D62. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Return_of_Peisistratus_to_Athens_with_the_false_Minerva.jpg","title":"Peisistratus' three bids for power","alt":"Engraving of a woman in armour standing beside a bearded man in a chariot driving into a Greek city, a crowd looking on","desc":"Return of Peisistratus to Athens with the false Minerva. M. A. Barth, Vorzeit und Gegenwart, Augsburg, 1832. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Return_of_Peisistratus_to_Athens_with_the_false_Minerva.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-299","num":299,"category":"Athens","question":"The tithe on produce that funded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> paid for loans to poor farmers and for local justices who went on circuit so that men need not leave their farms.","questions":["Thucydides says the tyrants of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exacted no more than a twentieth of income, adorned the city splendidly and left its laws in force, keeping only the offices in the family.","Men were often heard saying that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the elder tyrant was the Golden Age of Cronos, and that the government grew much harsher once his sons had succeeded him."],"answer":"Peisistratid Athens","answerText":"Peisistratid Athens","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Peisistratid Athens</b> was the city under Peisistratus and then under his sons, who the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says carried on affairs in the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus says he disturbed neither the order of offices nor the laws, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls his administration moderate and more constitutional than tyrannic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He advanced loans to the poor so that they might support themselves by farming, with two objects: to keep them scattered about the country, and to leave them neither the desire nor the time to attend to public business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thoroughly cultivated land increased his revenues, for he levied a tithe from the produce, and Herodotus adds money collected from the district of the river Strymon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He organised the Local Justices and often went on circuit himself, inspecting and settling disputes, so that men might not neglect their farms by coming into the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On one such journey he found a man digging mere rocks on Hymettus, who said the farm grew all the aches and pains there are, of which Peisistratus took the tithe; pleased by the free speech, he made the farm tax-free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says their government was not grievous to the multitude nor odious in practice, that they cultivated wisdom and virtue, exacted no more than a twentieth of income, adorned the city splendidly and provided sacrifices for the temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That twentieth stands against the tithe, and he adds that the city kept its existing laws, except that care was taken to have the offices in the hands of one of the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A grandson named after the tyrant dedicated the altar of the twelve gods in the market-place during his own archonship, and the altar in the Pythian precinct, whose faded inscription Thucydides had read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus has him hold Athenian sons hostage in Naxos and purify Delos of the dead buried within sight of the temple, while men were often heard calling his rule the Golden Age of Cronos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 16, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D64. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 17, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.59, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Peisistratos_aqueduct_Syntagma_Athens.jpg/1920px-Peisistratos_aqueduct_Syntagma_Athens.jpg","title":"Peisistratid Athens","alt":"A line of terracotta water pipes with inspection holes, laid in a trench and exposed in an open archaeological area","desc":"Part of the aqueduct constructed by Peisistratos and exposed during construction of the Athens metro, now on display at Syntagma square. Badseed, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peisistratos_aqueduct_Syntagma_Athens.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-300","num":300,"category":"Athens","question":"Guiding the Persians to Marathon in old age, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost a tooth in the sand and said the land was not theirs, his own share being whatever held it.","questions":["Thucydides argues that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the eldest son, since the pillar on the Acropolis names five of his children and none of either brother's.","After his brother's murder <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had the marchers set their arms aside, then picked out everyone found carrying a dagger rather than the shield and spear of a procession."],"answer":"Hippias","answerText":"Hippias","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Hippias</b> was the son of Peisistratus who held Athens after him, the elder of the two brothers in whose hands affairs lay and, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says, statesmanlike and wise by nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides insists that he and not Hipparchus was the eldest and the reigning tyrant, a thing he says he had more exact accounts of than others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His evidence is the pillar set up on the Acropolis to record the crime of the tyrants, which names five children of Hippias and none of his brothers, and puts his name first after his father's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that the tyranny could not have passed so easily to a younger brother unused to authority, where Hippias had long been accustomed to overawe the citizens and be obeyed by the mercenaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Hipparchus was killed at the Panathenaic festival he was outside the city arranging the procession, and composed his features so as not to betray himself, sending the armed marchers to a spot without their weapons and picking out every man found with a dagger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> After that the tyranny pressed harder: he put many citizens to death, and his executions and sentences of exile in revenge for his brother left him suspicious of everybody and embittered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About four years later things stood so badly that he began fortifying Munychia, meaning to move his establishment there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also turned his eyes abroad, marrying his daughter Archedice to a man of Lampsacus whose family had great influence with Darius, and her tomb there carries verses naming her daughter, wife and sister to the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deposed in the fourth year by the Lacedaemonians and the banished Alcmaeonidae, he went under safe conduct to Sigeum, then to Lampsacus, then to the Persian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Twenty years afterwards he guided the invaders to Marathon, where he dreamt of his mother, lost a loose tooth in the sand, and said the land was not theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 18, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.55, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.58, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 19, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.59, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.107, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D107. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-301","num":301,"category":"Athens","question":"Where Thucydides makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the man who solicited Harmodius and then insulted his sister, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives both the love and the insult to a younger brother.","questions":["It was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who brought the poets Anacreon and Simonides to Athens, being fond of amusement and love-making and having literary tastes, while his elder brother controlled the government.","Thucydides says it was the sad fate of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that got him the credit with posterity of having been tyrant, when in truth his elder brother reigned."],"answer":"Hipparchus","answerText":"Hipparchus","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Hipparchus</b> was a son of Peisistratus and the brother of Hippias, with whom affairs lay by station and age, though the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the government was controlled by the elder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That work calls him fond of amusement and love-making, with literary tastes, and credits him with bringing poets such as Anacreon and Simonides to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides is blunter about his standing: it was the sad fate which made him famous that got him the credit with posterity of having been tyrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his account Hipparchus solicited Harmodius, was refused, and resolved to insult him covertly, first inviting his sister to bear a basket in a procession and then turning her away as unworthy of the honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives that love and that insult to the youngest brother, Thettalus, bold and insolent in his mode of life, whose conduct it calls the source of all their misfortunes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In that version Thettalus stopped Harmodius's sister from carrying a basket in the Panathenaic procession by saying something insulting about Harmodius as effeminate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both accounts end alike: at the festival the conspirators saw one of their number talking with Hippias, feared discovery, and killed Hipparchus by the Leocoreum as the procession was being formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus says the night before he dreamt that a tall and handsome man stood over him and told him in riddling verses to endure the unendurable, since no man does wrong without paying the penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He put the vision from his mind and led the procession in which he met his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thucydides tells the whole story at length to show that the Athenians are no more accurate than anybody else about their own tyrants and their own history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 18, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.55, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.56, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.57, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.56, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-302","num":302,"category":"Athens","question":"Thucydides says the Panathenaic festival was chosen for the deed of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because it was the one day citizens in the procession could meet together in arms without suspicion.","questions":["Seeing one of their accomplices talking familiarly with Hippias, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took fright and rushed within the gates to kill Hipparchus by the Leocoreum before they could be arrested.","One of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed on the spot and the other tortured, until he taunted Hippias with giving his right hand to his brother's murderer and was killed in a rage."],"answer":"Harmodius and Aristogeiton","answerText":"Harmodius and Aristogeiton","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Harmodius and Aristogeiton</b> were the two Athenians who killed Hipparchus, son of Peisistratus, at the Panathenaic festival, and whom Herodotus describes as men of Gephyraean descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides makes their action the consequence of a love affair, Harmodius being in the flower of youthful beauty and Aristogeiton a citizen of the middle rank who was his lover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the sister of Harmodius was invited to bear a basket in a procession and then turned away as unworthy, Aristogeiton grew more exasperated than ever and the two arranged everything with those who would join them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They waited for the great feast of the Panathenaea, which Thucydides calls the sole day on which the citizens forming part of the procession could meet together in arms without suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conspirators were few, for better security, in the hope that those not in the plot would be carried away by the example and use the arms in their hands to recover their liberty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On the day they saw one of their accomplices talking familiarly with Hippias, took fright, and rushed within the gates to be revenged first on the man who had wronged them, killing Hipparchus by the Leocoreum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Harmodius was killed on the spot and Aristogeiton escaped through the crowd running up, but was afterwards taken and dispatched in no merciful way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he was tortured a long time and named men of distinguished families who were friends of the tyrants, either to make his captors destroy their own innocent friends or because they truly were his accomplices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Unable to die whatever he did, he offered more names, induced Hippias to give his right hand as a pledge, then taunted him with giving it to his brother's murderer, and was killed on the spot in a rage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That work also denies the story that Hippias disarmed the marchers and searched them, saying men did not walk armed in processions then, which is exactly what Thucydides reports him doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.56, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.57, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 18, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.55, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.58, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-303","num":303,"category":"Athens","question":"The statues of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were carried off from Athens by Xerxes among the spoils, and afterwards restored to the city by Antiochus.","questions":["Athenian drinking songs about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised to wreathe a sword in a myrtle bough, and told Harmodius he was not dead but dwelling in the isles of the blessed.","Thucydides complains that the Athenian public credited the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with killing a tyrant, when Hipparchus was not the tyrant and the city stayed unfree for four years more."],"answer":"Tyrannicides","answerText":"Tyrannicides","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Tyrannicides</b> is the name Athens gave Harmodius and Aristogeiton after they killed Hipparchus, and the honours the city paid them were those of men who had set it free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Statues of the pair stood in the city, and Pausanias says some of the figures were made by Critius, the old ones being the work of Antenor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Xerxes took Athens after the citizens had abandoned it he carried those statues off among the spoils, and they were afterwards restored to the Athenians by Antiochus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two were buried in the public cemetery, in the company Pausanias lists there of Conon and Timotheus, Zeno and Chrysippus, Ephialtes and Lycurgus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts memorial offerings to them among the standing duties of the War-lord, beside the funeral games for those who had fallen in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Athenaeus preserves the drinking songs sung about them, one promising to wreathe a sword in a myrtle bough, the sword that laid the tyrant low, when patriots burning to be free gave Athens equality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Another tells Harmodius that though reft of breath he shall never feel the stroke of death, the happy heroes' isles being the bright abode allotted him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides set himself against the whole of it, writing that the general Athenian public fancy Hipparchus was tyrant when he fell, not knowing that Hippias, the eldest son, was really supreme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He puts the error down to the way most men deal with traditions, even those of their own country, receiving them all alike as they are delivered without applying any critical test whatever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor did the killing free the city: the tyranny pressed harder afterwards, and Hippias was deposed in the fourth year by the Lacedaemonians and the banished Alcmaeonidae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.8.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8%3Asection%3D5. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.29.15, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D29%3Asection%3D15. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 58, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists</i> 15.50, trans. C. D. Yonge, Bohn's Classical Library, vol. 3 (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://archive.org/details/deipnosophistsor03athe. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.20, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.59, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Naples_-_Tyrannicide_group_-_Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton.jpg/1920px-National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Naples_-_Tyrannicide_group_-_Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton.jpg","title":"Tyrannicides","alt":"Two marble statues in a museum gallery, a bearded man with a cloak over his arm beside a younger man with his arm raised to strike","desc":"Statue of Harmodius and Aristogeiton, Roman copy of Greek bronze, Naples National Archaeological Museum. Elliott Brown, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Naples_-_Tyrannicide_group_-_Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-304","num":304,"category":"Athens","question":"Given as much gold as he could carry at one time on his person, one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled his boots, his tunic and his mouth, and strewed gold-dust in his hair.","questions":["Herodotus refuses to believe that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held up a shield as a signal to the Persians, calling them tyrant-haters as much as Callias or even more so.","The marriage of Agariste of Sicyon into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced Cleisthenes, who gave the Athenians their tribes and their democracy, and in two more generations the mother of Pericles."],"answer":"Alcmaeonids","answerText":"Alcmaeonids","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Alcmaeonids</b> were an Athenian family whom Herodotus calls men of renown even in the old days, their standing rising from the time of Alcmaeon and then Megacles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Croesus sent Lydians to consult the Delphic oracle, Alcmaeon helped them so zealously that the king summoned him to Sardis and gave him as much gold as he could carry away at one time on his person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came prepared, in a wide tunic and the most spacious boots he could find, packed both with gold-dust, strewed more in his hair and took as much again into his mouth, coming out swollen and hardly able to drag the weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Croesus burst out laughing and gave him all that and as much again, on which the family grew very rich, kept four-horse chariots and won with them at Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The next generation raised the house higher, when Cleisthenes of Sicyon proclaimed that any Greek who thought himself worthy to be his son-in-law should come and be tried for the hand of his daughter Agariste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Megacles won her, and from that marriage came the Cleisthenes who gave the Athenians their tribes and their democracy, and two generations later the Agariste who dreamt she had given birth to a lion and bore Pericles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against all that stood a curse, Herodotus noting that the family was said to be under one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides gives its origin: Cylon's companions were raised from the altar on a promise of safety and killed, so those who did it and their descendants were called accursed, driven out, and driven out again by Cleomenes, the bones of their dead taken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Banished under the tyranny, they contracted to build the temple at Delphi and made it finer than the model showed, fronting in Parian marble what they had agreed to build in tufa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herodotus refuses to believe the story that they signalled to the Persians with a shield, calling them tyrant-haters as much as Callias or more so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.125, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D125. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.126, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.131, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D131. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.61, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D61. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.62, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D62. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.121, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D121. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-305","num":305,"category":"Athens","question":"The Spartans might never have taken the citadel in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the sons of the family were caught being smuggled out of the country and their parents came to terms.","questions":["The first Spartan force sent to bring about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> landed at Phalerum and was destroyed by a thousand Thessalian horsemen, its commander Anchimolius killed and buried in Attica.","Herodotus reckons the rule that ended in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 36 years, where the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> makes it 49 in all, seventeen of them after Peisistratus died."],"answer":"Fall of the Peisistratids","answerText":"Fall of the Peisistratids","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Fall of the Peisistratids</b> came four years after Hipparchus was killed, and it was brought about by a Spartan army rather than by any Athenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exiles headed by the Alcmaeonids could not manage it themselves: they fortified Leipsydrion on the slopes of Parnes, were besieged and dislodged by the tyrants, and were remembered afterwards in a drinking catch that mourned the good men the place threw away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Failing in everything else, they took the contract to build the temple at Delphi, which gave them money to spend on the Spartans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus reports what the Athenians say happened next, that they bribed the Pythian priestess to tell any Spartan who consulted her, on private business or public, to set Athens free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says only that she kept commanding it until she brought the Spartiates to the point, although the Peisistratids were their friends and strangers to her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A first force under Anchimolius went by sea and put in at Phalerum, where the Thessalian king Cineas met it with a thousand horse, killed the commander and drove the survivors to their ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cleomenes then came by land with a larger army, routed the Thessalian horsemen with more than forty slain, and shut the family up within the Pelasgic wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus thought the siege would have failed, since the Spartans meant to stay only a few days and the defenders were well supplied with food and drink.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What settled it was that the sons of the house were caught while being secretly carried out of the country, on which their people agreed to quit Attica within five days on Athenian terms and went to Sigeum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two reckonings of what had ended differ: Herodotus makes it 36 years of rule, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> about seventeen after Peisistratus died, or 49 years in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 19, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.63, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D63. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D64. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.65, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D65. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.59, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"wh-151","num":151,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Lakes, rivers and grassland covered what is now the Sahara during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when a stronger summer monsoon carried rain far enough north to water a desert.","questions":["Saharan sites stood empty for roughly a millennium after 8,200 years ago, a dry interruption inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the people who returned afterwards kept livestock.","The Sahara has greened and dried many times over as Earth's orbit wobbled, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is only the most recent of them, ending as northern summers dimmed."],"answer":"African humid period","answerText":"African humid period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Interval</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 15,000 – 5,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 11,000 – 5,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arid pause</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,200 – 7,200 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>African humid period</b> is the stretch of the late glacial and early Holocene when a strengthened summer monsoon watered the Sahara, turning the world's largest hot desert into grassland, lakes and rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It began about 15,000 years ago and had closed by roughly 5,500, driven by precession, the slow wobble that brings northern summers nearer the Sun, and that dragged the monsoon rain belt north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is not a one-off: more than 230 such green phases have been counted in the last eight million years, paced by the changing shape of Earth's orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plant waxes in sea-floor mud off West Africa put rainfall at a median of 640 millimetres (25 inches) a year, roughly ten times what that coast gets now, with the wet zone reaching 31 degrees north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The water left its shape on the map: river channels linked the desert basins, and Lake Megachad covered some 361,000 square kilometres (139,000 square miles), carrying fish and hippopotamus across ground that is now sand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> People followed the water, and hunter-fisher-gatherers with barbed bone harpoons settled beside the lakes; the dune field at Gobero in Niger holds about 200 burials from two quite different populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between them lies a gap of roughly a thousand years from about 8,200 years ago, an arid interruption that empties sites across the central Sahara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who came back kept livestock, and by 7,000 years ago herders were burying their dead at Takarkori in the Libyan mountains, having taken up herding from neighbours rather than been replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> How it ended is contested — abruptly within a century or two, or gradually and at different times in different places — and one Ethiopian record reads it as gradual but broken by decades-long droughts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> What the herders did to it is argued over too: grazing and burning have been proposed as an accelerant of the drying and, on one climate model, as what delayed the collapse by centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jessica E. Tierney, Francesco S. R. Pausata and Peter B. deMenocal, “Rainfall Regimes of the Green Sahara,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 1 (2017): e1601503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601503. [Open access]","Juan Cruz Larrasoaña, “A Review of West African Monsoon Penetration during Green Sahara Periods; Implications for Human Evolution and Dispersals over the Last Three Million Years,” <i>Oxford Open Climate Change</i> 1, no. 1 (2021): kgab011, https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfclm/kgab011. [Open access]","Juan C. Larrasoaña, Andrew P. Roberts and Eelco J. Rohling, “Dynamics of Green Sahara Periods and Their Role in Hominin Evolution,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 10 (2013): e76514, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0076514. [Open access]","Nick A. Drake et al., “Ancient Watercourses and Biogeography of the Sahara Explain the Peopling of the Desert,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 2 (2011): 458–62, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1012231108. [Open access]","Paul C. Sereno et al., “Lakeside Cemeteries in the Sahara: 5000 Years of Holocene Population and Environmental Change,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 3, no. 8 (2008): e2995, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0002995. [Open access]","Martin H. Trauth et al., “Early Warning Signals of the Termination of the African Humid Period(s),” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 3697, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-47921-1. [Open access]","David K. Wright, “Humans as Agents in the Termination of the African Humid Period,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 5 (2017): 4, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2017.00004. [Open access]","Nada Salem et al., “Ancient DNA from the Green Sahara Reveals Ancestral North African Lineage,” <i>Nature</i> 641, no. 8061 (2025): 144–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08793-7. [Open access]","Chris Brierley, Katie Manning and Mark Maslin, “Pastoralism May Have Delayed the End of the Green Sahara,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 9 (2018): 4018, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-06321-y. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Mega-Chad.jpg/1920px-Mega-Chad.jpg","title":"African humid period","desc":"Present-day Lake Chad and Lake Mega-Chad. Joshua Stevens, NASA Earth Observatory. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mega-Chad.jpg","alt":"Present-day Lake Chad and Lake Mega-Chad"}},
{"id":"wh-152","num":152,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Herds of cattle with their herders fill the middle phase of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, painted and engraved on massifs that are now among the driest places on Earth.","questions":["Radiocarbon on the pigments has failed again and again, so the chronology of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rests on style, on which figure is drawn over which, and on the deposits below.","Round-headed human figures, then cattle, then chariots, then camels: the phases of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follow the desert's own history from grassland down to sand."],"answer":"Saharan rock art","answerText":"Saharan rock art","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Round Heads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,900 – 7,400 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pastoral</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,500 – 4,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Horse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,000 – 2,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Camel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2,300 BP</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Saharan rock art</b> is the tens of thousands of paintings and engravings on the cliffs, boulders and rockshelters of the desert's mountain massifs, and the most visible thing its prehistory left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The great concentrations lie in the central massifs, the Tadrart Acacus in Libya and the Tassili n'Ajjer in Algeria above all; the Acacus alone holds 707 recorded sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figures are sorted into styles taken to follow one another in order: Wild Fauna, then Round Heads, then Pastoral, then Horse, then Camel, with Tifinagh, the Tuareg alphabet, last of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Round Heads are painted human figures whose heads are round and blank, set among Barbary sheep and antelope in hunts, and in dances nobody can now read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pastoral is the largest body of it, cattle drawn in outline with their herders; people really were keeping livestock in these mountains 7,000 years ago, and the engravings show their tethering stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Dating it is the hard part: radiocarbon on the pigments has failed repeatedly in both the Acacus and the Tassili, the samples too degraded and too polluted to trust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So the chronology is built sideways, from the style itself, from which figure is drawn over which, from the varnish on the rock, from luminescence dates on sediment burying a panel, and from the layers below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An engraved stone built into something datable gives a firm answer, and cattle in the Pastoral style stand on ceremonial platforms at Messak raised about 5,430 to 5,150 years ago, some over buried cattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the art records is a desert that was not one: its earliest animals are elephant, rhinoceros, giraffe and hippopotamus, and the excavated bones agree, catfish and crocodile at Takarkori while the water lasted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then come the cattle, then the horse and the chariot, then the camel, each phase a record of the living the ground could still support, until it could support almost none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Marina Gallinaro, “Saharan Rock Art: Local Dynamics and Wider Perspectives,” <i>Arts</i> 2, no. 4 (2013): 350–82, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts2040350. [Open access]","Savino di Lernia and Marina Gallinaro, “The Date and Context of Neolithic Rock Art in the Sahara: Engravings and Ceremonial Monuments from Messak Settafet (South-West Libya),” <i>Antiquity</i> 84, no. 326 (2010): 954–75, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00067016. [Paywalled]","Savino di Lernia et al., “Inside the ‘African Cattle Complex’: Animal Burials in the Holocene Central Sahara,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 2 (2013): e56879, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0056879. [Open access]","Marina Gallinaro and Savino di Lernia, “Trapping or Tethering Stones (TS): A Multifunctional Device in the Pastoral Neolithic of the Sahara,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 1 (2018): e0191765, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0191765. [Open access]","Wim Van Neer et al., “Aquatic Fauna from the Takarkori Rock Shelter Reveals the Holocene Central Saharan Climate and Palaeohydrography,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 2 (2020): e0228588, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0228588. [Open access]","Jessica E. Tierney, Francesco S. R. Pausata and Peter B. deMenocal, “Rainfall Regimes of the Green Sahara,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 1 (2017): e1601503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601503. [Open access]","Nada Salem et al., “Ancient DNA from the Green Sahara Reveals Ancestral North African Lineage,” <i>Nature</i> 641, no. 8061 (2025): 144–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08793-7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Daily_scene_with_cattle.jpg/1920px-Daily_scene_with_cattle.jpg","title":"Saharan rock art","desc":"Daily scene with cattle in Tassili n'Ajjer park in Djanet, Algeria. The description and the title are just interpretation. IssamBarhoumi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daily_scene_with_cattle.jpg","alt":"Daily scene with cattle in Tassili n'Ajjer, Algeria"}},
{"id":"wh-153","num":153,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Herds were kept in Africa long before any crop was sown there, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the older half of food production on the continent and the reverse of the Near Eastern order.","questions":["The first domestic animals in sub-Saharan Africa turn up near Lake Turkana about 5,000 years ago, brought down the White Nile as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread out of a drying Sahara.","Eastern Africans carry their own mutations for digesting milk as adults, quite separate from the European one, which is what thousands of years of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did to their genomes."],"answer":"African cattle pastoralism","answerText":"African cattle pastoralism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Sahara</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 7,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">East Africa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 5,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Southern Africa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 2,000 BP</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>African cattle pastoralism</b> is the keeping of cattle as a way of life, and it is the older half of food production in Africa, herds having been kept here long before any crop was sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the cattle came from is argued over: archaeologists have long held that Saharan foragers tamed the African aurochs, while the genetics reads them as Fertile Crescent cattle later crossed with wild African aurochs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 7,000 years ago people were keeping livestock in the Libyan mountains, and herding seems to have spread among the Saharans already there rather than arriving with newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rock art is full of them, cattle drawn with their herders, and they counted for more than meat: whole cattle lie buried under stone platforms at Messak, about 6,080 to 5,120 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the Sahara dried the herds moved, and the first domestic animals in sub-Saharan Africa appear near Lake Turkana about 5,000 years ago, most likely brought down the White Nile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Ancient DNA shows people moving with them rather than animals travelling alone, in two rounds of mixing: one in north-eastern Africa around 6,000 to 5,000 years ago, another near 4,000 as herders met local foragers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herding reached southern Africa only about 2,000 years ago, carried from eastern Africa by a migration the genomes show was heavily male-biased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farming trailed far behind: crops and iron entered eastern Africa around 2,500 years ago, so for millennia herders and foragers lived alongside one another instead of one replacing the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Milk is much of why the cattle mattered, and eastern Africans carry their own mutations for digesting it as adults, quite separate from the European one and under strong recent selection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Cattle became wealth and sacrifice as much as food, an attachment already plain in those Saharan graves, where the burials were deliberate and the animals whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mary E. Prendergast et al., “Ancient DNA Reveals a Multistep Spread of the First Herders into Sub-Saharan Africa,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6448 (2019): eaaw6275, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6827346/. [Open access]","Daniel Pitt et al., “Domestication of Cattle: Two or Three Events?,” <i>Evolutionary Applications</i> 12, no. 1 (2019): 123–36, https://doi.org/10.1111/eva.12674. [Open access]","Mário Vicente et al., “Male-Biased Migration from East Africa Introduced Pastoralism into Southern Africa,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 19 (2021): 259, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-021-01193-z. [Open access]","Savino di Lernia et al., “Inside the ‘African Cattle Complex’: Animal Burials in the Holocene Central Sahara,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 2 (2013): e56879, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0056879. [Open access]","Nada Salem et al., “Ancient DNA from the Green Sahara Reveals Ancestral North African Lineage,” <i>Nature</i> 641, no. 8061 (2025): 144–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08793-7. [Open access]","Marina Gallinaro, “Saharan Rock Art: Local Dynamics and Wider Perspectives,” <i>Arts</i> 2, no. 4 (2013): 350–82, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts2040350. [Open access]","Alessia Ranciaro et al., “Genetic Origins of Lactase Persistence and the Spread of Pastoralism in Africa,” <i>American Journal of Human Genetics</i> 94, no. 4 (2014): 496–510, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajhg.2014.02.009. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Maasai_boy_herding_cattle.jpg/1920px-Maasai_boy_herding_cattle.jpg","title":"African cattle pastoralism","desc":"Maasai boy herding cattle, photographed in 2017. Helen Amirian, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maasai_boy_herding_cattle.jpg","alt":"Maasai boy herding cattle"}},
{"id":"wh-154","num":154,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"A complete young cow lies in a clay-lined chamber under a stone mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of seven cattle tumuli dug at a basin that is now waterless desert.","questions":["The earliest claimed domestic cattle in Africa come from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Bir Kiseiba, though both the dates and the animals' domestic status are still disputed.","Nine sandstone blocks stand in a line 100 metres (110 yards) apart at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a small circle of slabs beyond them that may have marked the sky."],"answer":"Nabta Playa","answerText":"Nabta Playa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,800 – 6,200 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wells and huts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,100 – 8,900 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Megaliths</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,500 – 6,200 BP</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nabta Playa</b> is a large internally drained basin near the south-eastern edge of Egypt's Western Desert, 100 kilometres (60 miles) west of Abu Simbel and 30 kilometres (19 miles) north of the Sudanese border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is bone-dry now, but for several thousand years the summer monsoon filled it season by season, and people camped on its floor from about 10,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest sites are scatters of stone tools with no houses, wells or storage, left by groups who came when the water stood and went when it failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 9,100 years ago that had changed: settlements held large oval huts and smaller round ones, bell-shaped storage pits and deep walk-in wells, and the wells are the point, since they made it possible to stay in the desert all year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sheep or goats arrived from south-west Asia around 8,400 to 8,000 years ago, probably up the Nile, and sheep in time displaced gazelle as the main meat, while cattle bones stayed rare throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What made the place famous is the claim that its cattle were domestic as early as 11,000 years ago, which would make them the oldest in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That claim is contested on both counts: the dates and the domestic status are each disputed, the animal bones were never fully published, and no neighbouring area has produced anything so old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeozoologists now take undoubted domestic cattle there only from about 8,000 years ago, and the genetics reads African herds as Fertile Crescent stock later crossed with local aurochs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After a dry century around 7,600 years ago the basin was settled again, and this time its people built: nine sandstone blocks in a line 100 metres (110 yards) apart, a small circle of slabs beyond them that may have marked the sky, and stone mounds over buried cattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seven mounds have been dug, one holding a whole young cow in a clay-lined chamber, and the excavators read the ground as a ceremonial centre showing rank centuries before anything comparable along the Nile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Fred Wendorf and Romuald Schild, “Nabta Playa and Its Role in Northeastern African Prehistory,” <i>Journal of Anthropological Archaeology</i> 17, no. 2 (1998): 97–123, https://doi.org/10.1006/jaar.1998.0319. [Paywalled]","Veerle Linseele et al., “New Archaeozoological Data from the Fayum ‘Neolithic’ with a Critical Assessment of the Evidence for Early Stock Keeping in Egypt,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 10 (2014): e108517, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0108517. [Open access]","Daniel Pitt et al., “Domestication of Cattle: Two or Three Events?,” <i>Evolutionary Applications</i> 12, no. 1 (2019): 123–36, https://doi.org/10.1111/eva.12674. [Open access]","Jessica E. Tierney, Francesco S. R. Pausata and Peter B. deMenocal, “Rainfall Regimes of the Green Sahara,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 1 (2017): e1601503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601503. [Open access]","Martin H. Trauth et al., “Early Warning Signals of the Termination of the African Humid Period(s),” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 3697, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-47921-1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Calendar_aswan.JPG/1920px-Calendar_aswan.JPG","title":"Nabta Playa","desc":"Nabta Playa Calendar Circle, reconstructed at Aswan Nubia museum. Raymbetz, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calendar_aswan.JPG","alt":"Nabta Playa calendar circle, reconstructed at the Nubia Museum in Aswan"}},
{"id":"wh-155","num":155,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Banana phytoliths and taro starch put independent farming in the New Guinea highlands at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where mounds were being raised almost seven thousand years ago.","questions":["Six phases of drainage over ten thousand years are stacked in the peat at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from mounded plots to ditch networks cut across a highland wetland.","Agriculture began without cereals and without herds in New Guinea, and the wetland at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is where that separate beginning was first shown to be real."],"answer":"Kuk Swamp","answerText":"Kuk Swamp","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cultivated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,220 – 9,910 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mounded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6,950 – 6,440 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ditched</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,350 – 3,980 BP</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Kuk Swamp</b> is a wetland in the upper Wahgi valley of the Papua New Guinea highlands, about 1,550 metres (5,090 feet) up and 12 kilometres (7 miles) north-east of Mount Hagen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It matters because the highlands were long taken to have received farming late and from outside, and the peat here holds a sequence of cultivation ten thousand years deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest phase, about 10,220 to 9,910 years ago, is plant use and some cultivation along the wetland margin, with the forest above already being cleared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 6,950 to 6,440 years ago people were raising mounds on the swamp edge, dry ground built up out of wet, and growing bananas on them in quantity, with the rainforest above turned to grassland at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 4,350 to 3,980 years ago they were cutting ditches instead, draining the wetland in regular networks, and the digging goes on through three later phases until about 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The crops are the point: taro, bananas, yams, sugarcane and pitpit, all of them roots, stems and fruit rather than grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> New Guinea made agriculture without cereals and without livestock, which is why it is counted a separate invention rather than something borrowed from Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence for it is microscopic, since crops like these rot and leave no seed: banana phytoliths, taro starch on stone tools, and pollen recording forest turned to grass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bananas are the clearest case of all, because the wild plant behind most cultivated bananas grows in New Guinea, so the crop was taken in hand here before travelling as far as Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Taro went the other way, east into the Pacific, where it was being grown on Efate in Vanuatu by about 2,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jack Golson et al., eds., <i>Ten Thousand Years of Cultivation at Kuk Swamp in the Highlands of Papua New Guinea</i>, Terra Australis 46 (Canberra: ANU Press, 2017), chap. 1, https://press-files.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/n2540/html/ch01.xhtml. [Open access]","Tim Denham, Jack Golson and Philip Hughes, “Phase 2: Mounded Cultivation during the Mid Holocene,” in <i>Ten Thousand Years of Cultivation at Kuk Swamp in the Highlands of Papua New Guinea</i>, Terra Australis 46 (Canberra: ANU Press, 2017), https://press-files.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/n2540/html/ch12.xhtml. [Open access]","Tim P. Denham et al., “Origins of Agriculture at Kuk Swamp in the Highlands of New Guinea,” <i>Science</i> 301, no. 5630 (2003): 189–93, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1085255. [Paywalled]","Xavier Perrier et al., “Multidisciplinary Perspectives on Banana (<i>Musa</i> spp.) Domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 28 (2011): 11311–18, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1102001108. [Open access]","Giorgia Camperio et al., “Sedimentary Biomarkers of Human Presence and Taro Cultivation Reveal Early Horticulture in Remote Oceania,” <i>Communications Earth & Environment</i> 5 (2024): 667, https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-024-01831-8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Kuk_New_Guinea_2002.jpg/1920px-Kuk_New_Guinea_2002.jpg","title":"Kuk Swamp","desc":"The Kuk Early Agricultural Site consists of 116 ha of swamps in the western highlands of New Guinea 1,500 meters above sea-level. Satellite image acquired 7 May 2002. NASA/JPL, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuk_New_Guinea_2002.jpg","alt":"Satellite view of the Kuk Early Agricultural Site in the western highlands of New Guinea"}},
{"id":"wh-156","num":156,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Nine of the ten primary branches of a single language family are spoken on one island, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is reckoned to have started from Taiwan.","questions":["Madagascar was reached from southern Borneo and Rapa Nui from the opposite edge of the Pacific, the two far ends of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its 1,250 languages.","Rice and millet farmers crossed to Taiwan about five thousand years ago, and roughly a thousand years later the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began, taking their descendants south into the Philippines."],"answer":"Austronesian expansion","answerText":"Austronesian expansion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Into Taiwan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,500 – 4,800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Out of Taiwan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,200 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Remote Oceania</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,000 – 2,800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">East Polynesia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1025 – 1290 CE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Austronesian expansion</b> is the spread of Austronesian-speaking people out of Taiwan across half the world, carrying a family of about 1,250 languages that some 300 million people speak today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before European ships crossed the same water five centuries ago it was the most widely spread family on earth, running from Madagascar in the west to Rapa Nui in the east and from Taiwan south to Aotearoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The case for Taiwan is linguistic: nine of the ten primary branches are spoken on the island, and every Austronesian language beyond it belongs to the tenth, Malayo-Polynesian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology supplies the dates, since rice and millet farmers crossed from south-eastern China to Taiwan about 5,000 years ago and their descendants began moving south to the Philippines around 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlement then ran through Island Southeast Asia between 4,000 and 3,500 years ago, reached the islands east of New Guinea by about 3,250, and crossed into Remote Oceania, which nobody had ever reached, by 2,800.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The advance went in bursts with long stops between them, and the last stretch took people to the last habitable places on earth, the archipelagos of eastern Polynesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> High-precision dating puts the Society Islands at about 1025 to 1120 CE and then one rapid pulse to every remaining island, Aotearoa among them, by about 1290.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Westward the reach was as long: Malagasy descends from the South East Barito languages of southern Borneo, and its speakers crossed the Indian Ocean about 1,400 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The usual explanation is farming spreading through country held thinly by hunter-gatherers, and the exception argues for it, since New Guinea developed agriculture of its own and kept its own languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genes tell a rougher story than the languages do: every Austronesian-speaking group sampled carries ancestry closest to the Indigenous peoples of Taiwan, but western Indonesians also carry mainland Austroasiatic ancestry, and two-thirds of Malagasy ancestry is Bantu-related.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Matthew Spriggs, Paul Geraghty and Yue-Chen Liu, “Austronesian Archaeolinguistics,” in <i>The Oxford Handbook of Archaeology and Language</i>, ed. Martine Robbeets and Mark Hudson (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2025), 466–92, https://reich.hms.harvard.edu/sites/reich.hms.harvard.edu/files/inline-files/Chapter25_OHAL_AustronesianArchaeolinguistics.pdf. [Open access]","Dang Liu, Albert Min-Shan Ko and Mark Stoneking, “The Genomic Diversity of Taiwanese Austronesian Groups: Implications for the ‘Into- and Out-of-Taiwan’ Models,” <i>PNAS Nexus</i> 2, no. 5 (2023): pgad122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10187666/. [Open access]","Janet M. Wilmshurst et al., “High-Precision Radiocarbon Dating Shows Recent and Rapid Initial Human Colonization of East Polynesia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 5 (2011): 1815–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3033267/. [Open access]","Denis Pierron et al., “Genomic Landscape of Human Diversity across Madagascar,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 32 (2017): E6498–E6506, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5559028/. [Open access]","Mark Lipson et al., “Reconstructing Austronesian Population History in Island Southeast Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 5 (2014): 4689, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4143916/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Chronological_dispersal_of_Austronesian_people_across_the_Pacific_%28per_Benton_et_al%2C_2012%2C_adapted_from_Bellwood%2C_2011%29.svg/1920px-Chronological_dispersal_of_Austronesian_people_across_the_Pacific_%28per_Benton_et_al%2C_2012%2C_adapted_from_Bellwood%2C_2011%29.svg.png","title":"Austronesian expansion","desc":"Map outlining migratory paths of Austronesian speaking populations, including estimated dates. Adapted from Benton et al., 2012. Kikilipse, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chronological_dispersal_of_Austronesian_people_across_the_Pacific_(per_Benton_et_al,_2012,_adapted_from_Bellwood,_2011).svg","alt":"Map of Austronesian migration routes from Taiwan across the Pacific and to Madagascar, with dates"}},
{"id":"wh-157","num":157,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Fine toothed stamps pressed into wet clay in elaborate repeating designs are the mark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose potters carried them from the Bismarck Archipelago to Tonga.","questions":["The first people to reach Vanuatu, Fiji and Tonga belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and their ancient DNA carries almost none of the Papuan ancestry their descendants have today.","Obsidian quarried in New Britain turns up in Fiji and even in Borneo, which shows that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept its sea routes open long after landfall."],"answer":"Lapita culture","answerText":"Lapita culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Bismarcks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,450 – 3,350 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dispersal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3,250 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Remote Oceania</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,000 – 2,800 BP</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lapita culture</b> is the archaeological culture that carried the first people into the empty Pacific, beginning about 3,450 years ago, and it is known above all for its pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its potters pressed fine toothed stamps into wet clay in elaborate repeating designs, and the style appears fully formed in the earliest sites of the Bismarck Archipelago rather than growing up there by degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest of the kit is as distinctive: polished stone adzes, shell ornaments cut from many different species, and hamlets of rectangular houses, some of them raised on stilts over the reef.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pottery is there by 3,450 to 3,350 years ago, across the whole island group within a century of that, and the move south and east begins around 3,250.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 3,000 and 2,800 years ago its makers crossed into Remote Oceania, reaching Vanuatu, New Caledonia, Fiji and at last Tonga and Samoa, none of which any human had seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Obsidian quarried in the Bismarck Archipelago turns up as far off as New Caledonia and Fiji and as far west as Borneo, so the voyaging did not stop once a landfall was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Tonga and Samoa the advance halted for centuries, and the speech there drifted away from Fijian into Proto-Polynesian, which makes this culture the ancestor of Polynesian ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient DNA has rewritten who the voyagers were: the first Remote Oceanians descend almost undiluted from the population that spread Austronesian languages out of Taiwan, having passed the long-settled Papuans of New Guinea and the Solomons with hardly any mixing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Papuan-related people then pushed east from about 2,500 years ago into islands where Lapita had already ended, and most ni-Vanuatu today carry 80 to 88 per cent Papuan ancestry where the earliest arrivals carried at most 3.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The languages did not follow the genes, since Austronesian survived that near-total replacement, a pairing its investigators call extremely rare and perhaps unprecedented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jim Specht, “Small Islands in the Big Picture: The Formative Period of Lapita in the Bismarck Archipelago,” in <i>Oceanic Explorations: Lapita and Western Pacific Settlement</i>, ed. Stuart Bedford, Christophe Sand and Sean P. Connaughton, Terra Australis 26 (Canberra: ANU Press, 2007), chap. 3, https://press-files.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/p61051/pdf/ch0327.pdf. [Open access]","Matthew Spriggs, Paul Geraghty and Yue-Chen Liu, “Austronesian Archaeolinguistics,” in <i>The Oxford Handbook of Archaeology and Language</i>, ed. Martine Robbeets and Mark Hudson (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2025), 466–92, https://reich.hms.harvard.edu/sites/reich.hms.harvard.edu/files/inline-files/Chapter25_OHAL_AustronesianArchaeolinguistics.pdf. [Open access]","Pontus Skoglund et al., “Genomic Insights into the Peopling of the Southwest Pacific,” <i>Nature</i> 538, no. 7626 (2016): 510–13, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5515717/. [Open access]","Mark Lipson et al., “Population Turnover in Remote Oceania Shortly after Initial Settlement,” <i>Current Biology</i> 28, no. 7 (2018): 1157–65, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5882562/. [Open access]","Cosimo Posth et al., “Language Continuity despite Population Replacement in Remote Oceania,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 2, no. 4 (2018): 731–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5868730/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Lapita_potsherd_from_Fiji_with_face_design.jpg","title":"Lapita culture","desc":"Anthropomorphic designs on Lapita potsherds are rarely found in Fiji. Patrick Nunn, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lapita_potsherd_from_Fiji_with_face_design.jpg","alt":"Potsherd stamped with a face design, Lapita culture, Fiji"}},
{"id":"wh-158","num":158,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Squash, sunflower, marshelder and goosefoot were all brought under cultivation beside the rivers of what is now the eastern United States, which is what makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an independent invention.","questions":["Maize arrived late and was folded into the fields of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than sweeping it away, and several of its crops survived until European colonisation.","Seeds that grew larger and coats that grew thinner are the marks of domestication in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose oldest dated crop is a squash of about five thousand years ago."],"answer":"Eastern Agricultural Complex","answerText":"Eastern Agricultural Complex","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Squash</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,025 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sunflower</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,840 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">A crop complex</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 3,800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Maize integrated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1,100 – 500 BP</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Eastern Agricultural Complex</b> is the name for the crops that Indigenous people domesticated in eastern North America, one of about ten places on earth where farming was worked out rather than borrowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four species carry the case — pepo squash, sunflower, marshelder and goosefoot — each showing the marks of cultivation in seeds that grew larger or in coats that grew thinner as people sowed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Direct dating of the seeds themselves puts squash first at about 5,025 years ago, sunflower at 4,840, marshelder at 4,400 and goosefoot at 3,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 3,800 years ago these were no longer scattered single crops but a set: at Riverton in Illinois at least five domesticated seed plants were being grown together in one short occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The starchy seeds joined later, erect knotweed and maygrass and little barley among them, and through the Middle and Late Woodland centuries the group grows steadily more visible and more consistent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Why people began is argued over, but the population of the region rose sharply in the thousand years before the first domesticates, which suggests pressure on wild foods rather than leisurely tinkering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That the work happened here at all was contested, since squash, sunflower and goosefoot were each proposed as early arrivals from Mexico, and the question has been settled in eastern North America's favour for all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sequencing has since gone straight to the evidence: sunflower seeds 3,100 years old from a rockshelter in Arkansas carry organelle DNA matching modern cultivated sunflowers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Maize came late and did not sweep the rest away, being folded into the existing fields after about 1,100 years ago and only gradually becoming the more important crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Several of these crops fell out of cultivation only around European colonisation, and the domesticated forms of marshelder, goosefoot and erect knotweed are extinct, which is why they are increasingly called sleeping rather than lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Bruce D. Smith, “Eastern North America as an Independent Center of Plant Domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 103, no. 33 (2006): 12223–28, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1567861/. [Open access]","Bruce D. Smith and Richard A. Yarnell, “Initial Formation of an Indigenous Crop Complex in Eastern North America at 3800 B.P.,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 16 (2009): 6561–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2666091/. [Open access]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The Sleeping Crops of Eastern North America: A New Synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12079124/. [Open access]","Elic M. Weitzel and Brian F. Codding, “Population Growth as a Driver of Initial Domestication in Eastern North America,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 3, no. 8 (2016): 160319, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5108960/. [Open access]","Nathan Wales et al., “Ancient DNA Reveals the Timing and Persistence of Organellar Genetic Bottlenecks over 3,000 Years of Sunflower Domestication and Improvement,” <i>Evolutionary Applications</i> 12, no. 1 (2019): 38–53, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6304678/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Chenopodium_berlandieri_NPS-1.jpg/1920px-Chenopodium_berlandieri_NPS-1.jpg","title":"Eastern Agricultural Complex","desc":"Chenopodium berlandieri at Wind Cave National Park, South Dakota, USA. Jim Pisarowicz, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chenopodium_berlandieri_NPS-1.jpg","alt":"A goosefoot plant, Chenopodium berlandieri, growing wild in South Dakota"}},
{"id":"wh-159","num":159,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Six earthen ridges wrap a plaza in northeastern Louisiana at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where foragers moved a million cubic metres (1.3 million cubic yards) of earth without farming, draft animals or wheels.","questions":["Mound A rose in no more than 90 days, the second-largest earthen mound in North America and the centrepiece of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose builders never farmed.","Soapstone from the Appalachians and stone carried 1,100 kilometres (700 miles) turn up at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose exchange network reached across half a continent."],"answer":"Poverty Point","answerText":"Poverty Point","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,700 – 3,100 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earthworks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,300 – 3,200 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mound A</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 90 days or less</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2014</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Poverty Point</b> is a vast earthwork complex on a bluff above the Mississippi floodplain in northeastern Louisiana, raised by people who did not farm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Six concentric, semi-elliptical ridges wrap a flat plaza across roughly 200 hectares (500 acres), the outermost of them about 1.14 kilometres (0.7 miles) from end to end, and people lived on the ridges for generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Five mounds belong to the main period and a sixth was added about 700 CE; the largest, Mound A, is the second-biggest earthen mound in North America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mound A alone holds some 238,500 cubic metres (312,000 cubic yards) of earth and the whole landscape about a million, reckoned at roughly five million hours of work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It went up in no more than 90 days, which is a burst of concentrated work rather than the slow accumulation such a mound is usually taken to record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The builders hunted, fished and gathered, and had neither draft animals nor wheels to move any of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is what makes the site awkward, since building on this scale was long held to require farming and a ranked society to command the labour, and no sign of such ranking has been found here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stone came from a very long way off: soapstone from the southern Appalachians, novaculite from central Arkansas about 280 kilometres (175 miles) away, and material from sources up to 1,100 kilometres (700 miles) distant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Survey has since found 36 circles of timber posts, some 62 metres (203 feet) across, buried beneath the plaza, and the baked clay objects the site is named for turn up across the region as its signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronology is being rewritten as it is redated: the earthworks now look like short bursts around 3,300 to 3,200 years ago at the end of an occupation of a few centuries, and the place reads less as the source of a culture than as where several traditions converged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Seth B. Grooms, Grace M. V. Ward and Tristram R. Kidder, “Convergence at Poverty Point: A Revised Chronology of the Late Archaic Lower Mississippi Valley,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 396 (2023): 1453–69, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.155. [Open access]","Tristram R. Kidder and Seth B. Grooms, “Chronological Hygiene and Bayesian Modeling of Poverty Point Sites in the Lower Mississippi Valley, circa 4200 to 3200 cal BP,” <i>American Antiquity</i> 89, no. 1 (2024): 98–118, https://doi.org/10.1017/aaq.2023.85. [Open access]","Diana M. Greenlee, “Poverty Point,” <i>64 Parishes</i>, Louisiana Endowment for the Humanities, last updated 26 February 2024, https://64parishes.org/entry/poverty-point. [Open access]","Diana M. Greenlee et al., “Dendrogeomorphological Analysis of Earthwork Stability at Poverty Point SHS, Louisiana,” National Center for Preservation Technology and Training, National Park Service, 30 September 2013, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/dendrogeomorphological-analysis-of-earthwork-stability-at-poverty-point-shs-louisiana.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Poverty Point National Monument,” accessed 12 August 2026, https://www.nps.gov/popo/index.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Mound_A_at_the_Monumental_Earthworks_Poverty_Point_World_Heritage_Site.jpg/1920px-Mound_A_at_the_Monumental_Earthworks_Poverty_Point_World_Heritage_Site.jpg","title":"Poverty Point","desc":"Mound A at the Monumental Earthworks of Poverty Point World Heritage Site. Jennifer R. Trotter, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mound_A_at_the_Monumental_Earthworks_Poverty_Point_World_Heritage_Site.jpg","alt":"Mound A at Poverty Point seen from the air through low cloud"}},
{"id":"wh-160","num":160,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Potatoes, quinoa, llamas and guinea pigs were all taken from wild ancestors on the same slopes, which is what makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an invention rather than a borrowing.","questions":["Starch ground into stone tools at a village 3,890 metres (12,760 feet) up beside Lake Titicaca is some of the best evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and for where the potato began.","The animal half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the only large herd animals in the Americas from the guanaco and the vicuña on the high puna."],"answer":"Andean domestication","answerText":"Andean domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Camelid herding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 – 8,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Size change</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,100 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Guinea pig</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 4,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Potato in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3400 – 1600 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Andean domestication</b> is the independent working out of farming and herding along the western spine of South America, one of a handful of places on earth where both were invented rather than borrowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The crops came from wild relatives growing on the same slopes: potato, oca and ullucu among the tubers, quinoa, cañihua and amaranth grown for their seed, and beans, peppers, squashes, cotton and coca beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seed crops and root crops both mattered, and it is the tubers, grown for the starch-rich organs they store underground, that set the region apart from the cereal cradles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The potato was taken in somewhere in the high south-central Andes, and starch grains off grinding stones at Jiskairumoko, a village 3,890 metres (12,760 feet) up in the Titicaca basin, put it in use there between about 3400 and 1600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fifty of the 141 grains taken from fourteen manos and metates were consistent with cultivated potato, which is direct evidence of early use inside the plant's own homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The animals are the other half and the rarer achievement, this being the only cradle in the Americas to produce large herd animals: the llama from the guanaco, and the alpaca entangled with the vicuña.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When is argued over, since a broad reckoning puts camelid herding between 9,000 and 8,000 years ago while the first size changes visible in the bones come around 7,100, with larger animals spreading widely between 5,800 and 4,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The gap is not a contradiction, because a herding relationship runs well ahead of anything a skeleton is able to record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The guinea pig was being kept by roughly 4,000 years ago, and ancient DNA has since pointed to a second and separate centre for it in the eastern Colombian highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the package could carry shows in the Titicaca basin, where quinoa and tubers fed growing communities for two thousand years and the llama supplied transport, wool and dung as well as meat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Barbara Pickersgill, “Domestication of Plants in the Americas: Insights from Mendelian and Molecular Genetics,” <i>Annals of Botany</i> 100, no. 5 (2007): 925–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2759216/. [Open access]","Claudia U. Rumold and Mark S. Aldenderfer, “Late Archaic–Early Formative Period Microbotanical Evidence for Potato at Jiskairumoko in the Titicaca Basin of Southern Peru,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 48 (2016): 13672–77, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5137686/. [Open access]","Hugo D. Yacobaccio, “The Domestication of South American Camelids: A Review,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 43–51, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214430/. [Open access]","Thomas Cucchi and Benjamin Arbuckle, “Animal Domestication: From Distant Past to Current Development and Issues,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 6–9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214435/. [Open access]","Edana Lord et al., “Ancient DNA of Guinea Pigs (<i>Cavia</i> spp.) Indicates a Probable New Center of Domestication and Pathways of Global Distribution,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 8901, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7264122/. [Open access]","Melanie J. Miller et al., “Quinoa, Potatoes, and Llamas Fueled Emergent Social Complexity in the Lake Titicaca Basin of the Andes,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 49 (2021): e2113395118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8670472/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Types_of_Potatoes_in_Peru.jpg/1280px-Types_of_Potatoes_in_Peru.jpg","title":"Andean domestication","desc":"Types of potatoes in Peru. Cathetovar, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Types_of_Potatoes_in_Peru.jpg","alt":"Many varieties of native Andean potato in baskets and bowls on a table, with guinea pigs on straw beneath it"}},
{"id":"wh-161","num":161,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Bodies taken apart, put back together and finished in clay and pigment make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the oldest deliberate mummification anywhere in the Americas.","questions":["In a desert where nothing rots, the dead were already preserving themselves, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> improved on what the dryness was doing on its own.","Infants and children were the first to be given the treatment that produced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and adults joined them only later in a practice that ran for millennia."],"answer":"Chinchorro mummies","answerText":"Chinchorro mummies","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chinchorro</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 – 4,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mummification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,000 – 4,400 BP</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chinchorro mummies</b> are the artificially preserved dead of a people who fished and gathered along the coast of the Atacama Desert, in what is now northern Chile and southern Peru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Making one meant taking a body apart and putting it back together again: complete disassembly and reassembly, the anatomy rebuilt, and the outside finished with clay, pigments and other materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The practice appears about 7,000 to 8,000 years ago and had all but gone by 4,400, which makes it the oldest deliberate mummification anywhere in the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was long called the oldest anywhere at all, and a study of 54 pre-Neolithic burials published in 2025 argues instead that bodies were being smoke-dried in Southeast Asia and southern China thousands of years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the early period only infants and children were treated this way, which suggests they were counted full members of the community, and adults were mummified afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The setting explains a good deal, because in a hyperarid desert bodies do not rot: every corpse mummifies on its own, and the dead pile up in the landscape rather than disappearing from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rising freshwater and a productive sea let the population grow, and a demographic model puts a typical person in sight of several thousand naturally preserved dead, so the custom improved on something the desert was doing already.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second suggestion is arsenic, the water at Camarones carrying over 200 micrograms per litre against a safe level of 10, with the miscarriages that causes proposed as the spur — though the study modelling the practice says its contribution cannot be weighed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mummies were not buried and left alone but taken up and handled again and again, which blurs the line between being dead and being gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing else these people made comes near the mummies for complexity, and none of it rested on farming; the practice faded as the highlands dried and warm-water years thinned the fisheries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Pablo A. Marquet et al., “Emergence of Social Complexity among Coastal Hunter-Gatherers in the Atacama Desert of Northern Chile,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 37 (2012): 14754–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3443180/. [Open access]","Tom D. Dillehay, “Selective Complexity and Adaptive Mortuary Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 37 (2012): 14722–23, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3443118/. [Open access]","Gonzalo M. Rojas-Costa et al., “Intracranial Volume Variation in Chinchorro Mummies: A Comparative Study with Pre-Hispanic Farmers and Contemporary Populations,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 41301, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12638747/. [Open access]","Hsiao-chun Hung et al., “Earliest Evidence of Smoke-Dried Mummification: More Than 10,000 Years Ago in Southern China and Southeast Asia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 38 (2025): e2515103122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12478042/. [Open access]","Mario Apata and Susanne P. Pfeifer, “Recent Population Genomic Insights into the Genetic Basis of Arsenic Tolerance in Humans,” <i>Heredity</i> 124, no. 2 (2020): 253–62, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6972707/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Momia_Chinchorro_Negra.jpg/1280px-Momia_Chinchorro_Negra.jpg","title":"Chinchorro mummies","desc":"Chinchorro mummy of an infant, black type, Museo Arqueológico San Miguel de Azapa. WeHaKa, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Momia_Chinchorro_Negra.jpg","alt":"An infant mummy of the black Chinchorro type lying on sand in a museum case, the sticks of its internal frame showing at the shoulders and legs"}},
{"id":"wh-162","num":162,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Fractured skulls, points lodged in bone and pits full of the dead are most of the evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since nobody who fought one left a written account.","questions":["A cemetery on the Nile where two thirds of the dead carry wounds, many of them healed, is the oldest good evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as something that came back year after year.","Roughly a tenth of 2,363 Neolithic skulls from northwestern Europe carry trauma, which is the strongest measure anyone has of how common <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was."],"answer":"prehistoric warfare","answerText":"prehistoric warfare","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lethal violence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 430,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jebel Sahaba</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 13,400 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Potočani</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4200 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Prehistoric warfare</b> is organised violence between groups before anybody was writing about it, so it has to be read off bodies and settlements: broken skulls, points lodged in bone, mass graves, weapons meant for people, and defences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Killing is far older than any of that: a skull from the Sima de los Huesos in northern Spain, about 430,000 years old, carries two fractures above the brow made by the same object, and two blows imply an intention to kill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is a homicide rather than a war, and organised conflict between groups shows itself only where a number of the dead are found together. The oldest place that does is Jebel Sahaba, a cemetery beside the Nile in Sudan at least 13,400 years old, where 41 of the 61 people examined carry an injury, healed or unhealed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Re-examination found that many carry both kinds at once, so the site records raids and ambushes returning over years rather than the single battle it was long read as.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Among Europe's early farmers violence was ordinary: cranial trauma across 2,363 skeletons runs at about 10.9 per cent, of which 3.2 per cent are unhealed wounds nobody survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a genuine rise on the foragers who came before, and at sites of mass killing the figure is nearer half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Potočani in Croatia, about 4200 BCE, 41 people of both sexes and every age from two years old were killed and thrown into a pit, 13 of them with cracked or pierced skulls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sequencing nearly all of them found that about 70 per cent had no close relative among the dead, so this was an unrelated slice of a large farming population and not a feud between families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Farming is not the whole story, since on the Atacama coast 288 adults from fishing communities show violence holding steady across ten thousand years, between local groups, with combat scenes in the rock art and no defences in the settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Linda Fibiger, Torbjörn Ahlström, Christian Meyer and Martin Smith, “Conflict, Violence, and Warfare among Early Farmers in Northwestern Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 4 (2023): e2209481119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9942812/. [Open access]","Nohemi Sala et al., “Lethal Interpersonal Violence in the Middle Pleistocene,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 5 (2015): e0126589, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4446311/. [Open access]","Isabelle Crevecoeur et al., “New Insights on Interpersonal Violence in the Late Pleistocene Based on the Nile Valley Cemetery of Jebel Sahaba,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 9991, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8159958/. [Open access]","Mario Novak et al., “Genome-Wide Analysis of Nearly All the Victims of a 6200 Year Old Massacre,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 3 (2021): e0247332, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7946188/. [Open access]","Vivien G. Standen et al., “Violence in Fishing, Hunting, and Gathering Societies of the Atacama Desert Coast: A Long-Term Perspective,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 9 (2023): e0290690, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10511140/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Morella_%28combate-de-arquero.png","title":"prehistoric warfare","desc":"Rock painting of a battle between archers, Cueva del Roure, Morella la Vella, Castellón, Spain, from Eduardo Hernández Pacheco's survey of 1924. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morella_(combate-de-arquero.png","alt":"A traced copy of a rock painting in which several archers with drawn bows run and shoot at one another"},"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-163","num":163,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The main measure archaeologists have for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the size of the houses people lived in, compared within a single settlement.","questions":["The awkward fact at the centre of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that a hundred generations passed after the first farming before house sizes show any marked difference.","Land that limits production, growing populations and ranked settlements track the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> far better than the mere existence of a surplus does."],"answer":"origins of social inequality","answerText":"origins of social inequality","answerDate":"","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Origins of social inequality</b> is the question of how societies came to be divided into people with more and people with less, and archaeologically it is measured by comparing the sizes of the houses people lived in within a single settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That proxy is a conservative one: residence size tracks income closely and sets a floor under wealth inequality rather than a ceiling, so what it reports is the least that was there, and a figure taken at one settlement is not the same figure taken across a region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The largest attempt at it scores Gini coefficients across 1,173 sites and 47,019 residential structures spanning more than 10,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central finding is that persistent wealth inequality typically lags the onset of plant cultivation by more than a millennium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the first 2,000 years of the Neolithic, a hundred generations, the global sample shows no marked difference in house sizes, medians sitting between 0.2 and 0.3, and neither farming nor herding nor animal traction changed that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A surplus by itself, then, did not produce lasting inequality, which is the reverse of the usual account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What does track it is population growth, land-limited production, where land rather than labour is the binding constraint, and hierarchies of settlement size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reading offered is that polities growing one to two millennia after farming became common interfered with traditional levelling mechanisms, the enforcement of egalitarian norms among them, while ranked settlements let households in the top places pull further ahead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inheritance is the other half of it: across 21 societies material wealth passes to children strongly under farming and herding and hardly at all among foragers, and wealth Ginis run about 0.48 and 0.42 against 0.25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of it is a ratchet, since governance practices mediate how far inequality goes, iron smelting is associated with lower rather than higher house-size disparity, and neither the size nor the productivity of a settlement predicts it well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Timothy A. Kohler et al., “Economic Inequality Is Fueled by Population Scale, Land-Limited Production, and Settlement Hierarchies across the Archaeological Record,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400691122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12037049/. [Open access]","Tim Kerig et al., “100 Generations of Wealth Equality after the Neolithic Transitions,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400697122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12036991/. [Open access]","Scott G. Ortman et al., “Changes in Agglomeration and Productivity Are Poor Predictors of Inequality across the Archaeological Record,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400693122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12037055/. [Open access]","Monique Borgerhoff Mulder et al., “Intergenerational Wealth Transmission and the Dynamics of Inequality in Small-Scale Societies,” <i>Science</i> 326, no. 5953 (2009): 682–88, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2792081/. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Toward Multiscalar Measures of Inequality in Archaeology,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400700121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12037059/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg/1280px-Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg","title":"origins of social inequality","desc":"Maidanetske mega-settlement, Cucuteni-Trypillia culture, 3D model. René Ohlrau, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg","alt":"A 3D model of a prehistoric mega-settlement, hundreds of houses laid out in concentric rings with a few larger buildings among them"},"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-164","num":164,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Because fibre rots, most of what survives of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a scrap of cord stuck to a stone tool and microscopic threads in tool residues.","questions":["A three-ply cord of conifer inner bark, found stuck to a stone flake in France, is the oldest direct trace of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> anybody has found.","The wheel-and-axle principle sits inside the story of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because over a hundred perforated pebbles from a village of 12,000 years ago are read as spindle whorls."],"answer":"fibre technology","answerText":"fibre technology","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neanderthal cord</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 52,000 – 41,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dyed bast fibres</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 28,000 – 18,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spindle whorls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Woven, Iberia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3500 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Fibre technology</b> is the making of cord, net, thread and cloth out of plant and animal fibres, among the most fundamental and complex things people do and the least often found, because fibre rots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives is mostly indirect: a scrap stuck to a stone tool, microscopic threads left in tool residues, impressions in clay, and the weights and whorls used to spin and weave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oldest direct trace is a cord of conifer inner bark lying on a stone flake at the Abri du Maras in France, between 41,000 and 52,000 years old and therefore Neanderthal, a scrap 6.2 millimetres (a quarter of an inch) long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is three bundles twisted one way and plied together the other, which takes knowing which trees to strip and when, how to ret them, and a grasp of sets and pairs that amounts to numeracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Twisted fibre underlies clothing, rope, bags, nets, mats and boats, so once it exists it does not go away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Shizitan on the loess plateau of north China, tools used between 28,000 and 18,000 years ago carry 546 bast microfibres, mostly hemp, with the whole sequence of cutting, retting, pounding and scraping behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of those fibres 46 are dyed, in blue, pink, black, grey, green and red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spinning leaves harder evidence: more than a hundred perforated pebbles from the 12,000-year-old village of Nahal Ein-Gev II in Israel are read as spindle whorls, which puts the wheel-and-axle principle thousands of years before any cart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Weaving on a loom is later again, and in Iberia the first loom-woven cloth appears in the second half of the 4th millennium BCE, at the same moment loom weights do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The labour was enormous: on the Peruvian coast cultivated cotton replaced gathered wild bast and made larger fishing nets possible, and fibre work may have taken more time than any other single technology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Bruce L. Hardy et al., “Direct Evidence of Neanderthal Fibre Technology and Its Cognitive and Behavioral Implications,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 4889, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7145842/. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “Unveiling Bast Fiber Production in Upper Paleolithic North China: Microfibers and Usewear Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 4 (2026): e0346767, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13075717/. [Open access]","Talia Yashuv and Leore Grosman, “12,000-Year-Old Spindle Whorls and the Innovation of Wheeled Rotational Technologies,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 11 (2024): e0312007, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11559986/. [Open access]","David Beresford-Jones et al., “Refining the <i>Maritime Foundations of Andean Civilization</i>: How Plant Fiber Technology Drove Social Complexity during the Preceramic Period,” <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 25, no. 2 (2018): 393–425, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5953975/. [Open access]","Margarita Gleba et al., “Multidisciplinary Investigation Reveals the Earliest Textiles and Cinnabar-Coloured Cloth in Iberia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11, no. 1 (2021): 21918, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8578544/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Sandalias_procedentes_de_la_Cueva_de_los_Murci%C3%A9lagos%2C_Albu%C3%B1ol._Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a.jpg/1280px-Sandalias_procedentes_de_la_Cueva_de_los_Murci%C3%A9lagos%2C_Albu%C3%B1ol._Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a.jpg","title":"fibre technology","desc":"Esparto sandals from the Cueva de los Murciélagos, Albuñol, in the Museo Arqueológico Nacional de España. Dorieo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sandalias_procedentes_de_la_Cueva_de_los_Murci%C3%A9lagos,_Albu%C3%B1ol._Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a.jpg","alt":"Two sandals plaited from esparto grass, their soles built up from coiled and braided cord"}},
{"id":"wh-165","num":165,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The wooden body has almost never survived, so what is usually found of an early <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a criss-cross of long thin scratches cut into a buried ground surface.","questions":["Because an ox pulls a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a person swings a hoe, one household could suddenly break far more ground than it could ever prepare by hand.","Soil creeping downhill behind a field edge piles up into a lynchet, which is one of the few landforms a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> builds rather than merely scratches."],"answer":"plough","answerText":"plough","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ard marks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Belderg lynchet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3100 – 2900 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>plough</b> is dragged through soil to open a furrow for seed, and the oldest kind, the ard, cuts a shallow groove without turning the earth over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it does not invert the sod, a field was worked twice at right angles, so an ard leaves a criss-cross of long thin scratches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tool was wood and has almost never survived, so those scratches are the evidence, cut into an old ground surface and sealed under the mid-4th-millennium BCE long barrow at South Street in Wiltshire, or under an Early Neolithic house site at Ballygalley in Ireland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also builds landforms: soil creeping downhill behind a field edge piles into a bank called a lynchet, and one at Belderg Beg in County Mayo lay under peat of 3100 to 2900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two Irish traces say opposite things: a scratch beside a house is a garden plot, the lynchet part of a field system across a hillside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What separates a plough from a hoe is the ox rather than the blade: animal traction multiplies labour, letting one household break far more ground than it could prepare by hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is a change of kind, because the first farming in central Europe was the opposite: small plots dug by hand and heavily manured, which shows in the weeds among the crop and the nitrogen in charred grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Extensive farming is land-hungry, and only wealthier households could keep draught animals and hire their labour out, so ploughing is argued to have widened the gap between them and a growing landless class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is offered as the reason Old World wealth gaps outran American ones, no animal there being any use in a yoke, but a survey of house sizes finds no break in inequality where ploughing appears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its mark on bodies is plainer: across 5,400 years of farming in central Europe the arm bones of women changed more than those of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Alison A. Macintosh, Ron Pinhasi, and Jay T. Stock, “Divergence in Male and Female Manipulative Behaviors with the Intensification of Metallurgy in Central Europe,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 11 (2014): e112116, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4229139/. [Open access]","Fabienne Pigière and Jessica Smyth, “First Evidence for Cattle Traction in Middle Neolithic Ireland: A Pivotal Element for Resource Exploitation,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): e0279556, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9879418/. [Open access]","Amy Bogaard et al., “Combining Functional Weed Ecology and Crop Stable Isotope Ratios to Identify Cultivation Intensity: A Comparison of Cereal Production Regimes in Haute Provence, France and Asturias, Spain,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 25, no. 1 (2016): 57–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4709954/. [Open access]","Timothy A. Kohler et al., “Greater Post-Neolithic Wealth Disparities in Eurasia than in North America and Mesoamerica,” <i>Nature</i> 551, no. 7682 (2017): 619–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5714260/. [Open access]","Tim Kerig et al., “100 Generations of Wealth Equality after the Neolithic Transitions,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400697122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12036991/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Aratro_Lavagnone.jpg/1280px-Aratro_Lavagnone.jpg","title":"plough","desc":"The Lavagnone ard, an oak plough in the Museo civico archeologico Giovanni Rambotti. 65cla, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aratro_Lavagnone.jpg","alt":"A prehistoric wooden ard in a museum case, its dark oak sole and curved beam rising towards a reconstructed yoke, before a rock carving of a man ploughing with oxen"}},
{"id":"wh-166","num":166,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The hard part was never the disc but the joint at its centre, which is why the earliest surviving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a square hole and turned with its axle.","questions":["Logs laid under a load are useless once it has passed over them, and in a mine tunnel they cannot be carried to the front, which may be why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began underground.","More than 150 clay drinking mugs shaped as four-wheeled wagons, made no later than 3600 BCE, are the earliest known pictures of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in use."],"answer":"wheel","answerText":"wheel","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wagon models</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 3600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest wheel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3350 – 3100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spoked chariots</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2100 – 1700 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>wheel</b> is easy to picture and hard to build, because the difficulty is not the disc but the joint at its centre, which must bear a load and turn freely at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One proposed route runs from free rollers, logs laid across a path for a load to ride over, to grooved rollers held in sockets under the cargo, and then to two discs on a slender axle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rollers are useless once the load has passed over them, and in a narrow mine trench they cannot be carried back to the front, which is what makes fixing them under the cart pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the argument for copper miners in the Carpathians inventing it by 3900 BCE, whose culture left over 150 clay drinking mugs shaped as four-wheeled wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest surviving wheel comes from the Ljubljana Marshes in Slovenia, and the square hole at its centre shows it was fixed to its axle and turned with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Wheels and axles turn up in the Middle East and in Europe at much the same moment in the second half of the 4th millennium BCE, and differ enough to look separate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wooden wheels and axles have come out of waterlogged ground in Switzerland, Germany and the Netherlands, wheel tracks run beside a tomb at Flintbek, and a pot from Bronocice carries wagons with drawbars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A wheel is no use without something to pull it: the first vehicles were heavy, ran on four solid discs, and were drawn by oxen and then by the ass-onager hybrids that hauled Mesopotamia’s war wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No reconstruction of a steppe wagon and no clay model from the Carpathians or Anatolia has a driver’s seat, so the driver walked beside the team in a roadless world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The spoked wheel was a second invention: the light chariots of Sintashta took steppe travel from 3 to 16 kilometres (2 to 10 miles) an hour, and a wheeled dog figurine of the 8th century CE shows the principle reached Mesoamerica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lee R. Alacoque, Richard W. Bulliet, and Kai A. James, “Reconstructing the Invention of the Wheel Using Computational Structural Analysis and Design,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 11, no. 10 (2024): 240373, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11529624/. [Open access]","Weronika Klecel and Elżbieta Martyniuk, “From the Eurasian Steppes to the Roman Circuses: A Review of Early Development of Horse Breeding and Management,” <i>Animals</i> 11, no. 7 (2021): 1859, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8300240/. [Open access]","E. Andrew Bennett et al., “The Genetic Identity of the Earliest Human-Made Hybrid Animals, the Kungas of Syro-Mesopotamia,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 2 (2022): eabm0218, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8759742/. [Open access]","David Anthony, Martin Trautmann, and Volker Heyd, “Horse Genetics, Archaeology, and the Beginning of Riding,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 20 (2026): eady7336, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13170673/. [Open access]","Fabienne Pigière and Jessica Smyth, “First Evidence for Cattle Traction in Middle Neolithic Ireland: A Pivotal Element for Resource Exploitation,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): e0279556, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9879418/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Ljubljana_Marshes_Wheel_with_axle_%28oldest_wooden_wheel_yet_discovered%29.jpg/1280px-Ljubljana_Marshes_Wheel_with_axle_%28oldest_wooden_wheel_yet_discovered%29.jpg","title":"wheel","desc":"Ljubljana Marshes Wheel with axle, the oldest wooden wheel yet discovered. Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ljubljana_Marshes_Wheel_with_axle_(oldest_wooden_wheel_yet_discovered).jpg","alt":"A prehistoric wooden wheel and its axle in a museum case, the wheel made of two planks joined by battens with a square hole at its centre"}},
{"id":"wh-167","num":167,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"A canal silts up and a wet field dries, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is often inferred from the sedges and reeds that grew among the crop rather than from the works themselves.","questions":["Lidar over the Teotihuacan Valley found 16.9 kilometres of river course deliberately rerouted, and a city built over the earlier <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had watered its fields.","Intensive farming and states go together everywhere, but the stronger link runs from having a state to practising <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, rather than the other way about."],"answer":"irrigation","answerText":"irrigation","answerDate":"","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Irrigation</b> is bringing water to a crop rather than waiting for rain, and it frees farming from where and when the rain falls. It is also hard to see in the ground, so it is often inferred from the plants: the water-loving sedges and reeds of a wet field leave phytoliths, tiny silica bodies, among the crop’s own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Read that way, rice grown in South India between about 1000 BCE and 500 CE looks rainfed rather than irrigated, which upsets the assumption that rice implies paddy fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the works are big enough they survive as landscape: lidar over the Teotihuacan Valley in Mexico shows 16.9 kilometres (10 miles) of river course deliberately rerouted, with the city built over earlier irrigation systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Bolivian Amazon the Casarabe threw causeways and canals across savanna between 400 and 1400 CE, a network that drained and watered the ground for intensive maize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Not all irrigation is a canal: Byzantine farmers in the Negev steered runoff off bare hillsides into terraced plots and grew wine grapes in a desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Modelling one of their watersheds shows more runoff raising yields only to a point, and a long drought cutting production so far that recovery runs past a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Irrigation is one of the practices meant by intensive agriculture, alongside manuring and crop rotation, and states almost everywhere rest on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mapped across 868 cultures on a language tree the two are indeed tightly coupled, but the stronger relationship runs the other way: having a state makes intensive farming much more likely, rather than the reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> States were also far less likely to give the practice up once they had it, so their part looks like sustaining irrigation rather than being made by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Christopher Opie and Quentin D. Atkinson, “State Formation across Cultures and the Role of Grain, Intensive Agriculture, Taxation and Writing,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 10, no. 1 (2026): 156–63, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12846917/. [Open access]","Eleanor Kingwell-Banham, “Dry, Rainfed or Irrigated? Reevaluating the Role and Development of Rice Agriculture in Iron Age-Early Historic South India Using Archaeobotanical Approaches,” <i>Archaeological and Anthropological Sciences</i> 11, no. 12 (2019): 6485–6500, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6890616/. [Open access]","Nawa Sugiyama et al., “Humans as Geomorphic Agents: Lidar Detection of the Past, Present and Future of the Teotihuacan Valley, Mexico,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 9 (2021): e0257550, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8452071/. [Open access]","Barak Garty et al., “Wine Economy in Byzantine Shivta (Negev, Israel): Exploring the Role of Runoff Agriculture and Droughts through Agent-Based Modeling,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 7 (2025): e0325204, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12244680/. [Open access]","Joseph Hirst et al., “Constraining the Population Size Estimates of the Pre-Columbian Casarabe Culture of Amazonian Bolivia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 5 (2025): e0325104, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12124758/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Ipuy_shaduf.jpg/1280px-Ipuy_shaduf.jpg","title":"irrigation","desc":"Scene of a gardener using a shaduf, Tomb of Ipuy at Deir el-Medina, west bank of Thebes, TT217. Norman de Garis Davies, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ipuy_shaduf.jpg","alt":"A painted scene of a gardener working a counterweighted lever to lift a bucket of water from a lily pool towards trees and flowers"}},
{"id":"wh-168","num":168,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Because there are no records, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read backwards from objects found far from the outcrop or the shore their material came from.","questions":["The longest journey a stone tool has yet been shown to make through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took chert from south-west France 600 kilometres into central Iberia during the ice age.","Amounts fall away with distance from the source in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because goods can pass from band to band without anyone ever travelling far."],"answer":"prehistoric trade","answerText":"prehistoric trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Iberian network</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 25,300 – 23,900 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Keros obsidian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Únětice collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Prehistoric trade</b> is the movement of goods between people before anyone wrote anything down, so with no records it is read backwards from objects found far from where their material came from. The method is provenance: many raw materials carry a chemical signature of the seam they were quarried at, so a single blade or bead can be matched to one source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the Cycladic sanctuary of Keros, 207 pieces of obsidian measured by portable X-ray fluorescence came mostly from Melos, with a few from Giali in the Dodecanese and from central Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It runs far deeper than farming: chert tools left at Peña Capón in central Iberia were quarried in south-west France, 600 to 700 kilometres (370 to 430 miles) off, the longest such journey yet proved for a stone tool in Palaeolithic Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is exceptional, most stone having moved locally, and the network behind it held from about 25,300 to 23,900 years ago, through the coldest part of the ice age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A far-travelled object does not prove anybody made the journey, since goods pass down the line from band to band in short hops, which shows in the amounts: most near the source and least far from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same caution holds later, and Baltic amber reaching Iberia in the last quarter of the 2nd millennium BCE looks like the far end of Mediterranean networks rather than direct dealing with the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Exchange also works where nothing is for sale: at Baño Nuevo 1 in Patagonia, 100 kilometres (60 miles) from the Pacific and 320 (200 miles) from the Atlantic, hunter-gatherers wore beads cut from sea shells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Routes shift, and 550 metal analyses show southern Scandinavia drawing copper first from the Austrian Alps and Slovakia through hubs in central Germany, with high-tin British metal alongside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When those hubs collapsed around 1600 BCE the supply failed and smiths melted down what they had, until a new eastern route opened to the Carpathians that also carried amber to the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Marta Sánchez de la Torre et al., “Far-Reaching Hunter-Gatherer Networks during the Last Glacial Maximum in Western Europe,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 4 (2026): eadz7697, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12822654/. [Open access]","Mercedes Murillo-Barroso et al., “Amber in Prehistoric Iberia: New Data and a Review,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 8 (2018): e0202235, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6114727/. [Open access]","Heide W. Nørgaard, Ernst Pernicka, and Helle Vandkilde, “Shifting Networks and Mixing Metals: Changing Metal Trade Routes to Scandinavia Correlate with Neolithic and Bronze Age Transformations,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 6 (2021): e0252376, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8208583/. [Open access]","Tristan Carter et al., “Characterizing Centrality: Obsidian Consumption, Supra-Regional Connectivity, and Social Reproduction at the Early Bronze Age Sanctuary of Keros (Cyclades, Greece),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0325218, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12425306/. [Open access]","Heidi Hammond et al., “Middle through Late Holocene Long-Distance Transport of Exotic Shell Personal Adornments in Central West Patagonia (Southern South America): The Archaeomalacological Assemblage of Baño Nuevo 1,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0304454, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11125563/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Obsidian_from_Milos_in_Crete%2C_3000-2300_BC%2C_AMH%2C_144704.jpg/1280px-Obsidian_from_Milos_in_Crete%2C_3000-2300_BC%2C_AMH%2C_144704.jpg","title":"prehistoric trade","desc":"Blades, cores, flakes and waste chips of obsidian imported from Melos, found at Poros, Heraklion, 3000–2300 BCE. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Obsidian_from_Milos_in_Crete,_3000-2300_BC,_AMH,_144704.jpg","alt":"A museum case of black obsidian blades, cores and flakes laid out beside three bowls heaped with working waste"},"undatable":true},
{"id":"wh-169","num":169,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Because the rivers whose silt made the plain habitable went on burying it, the settlements of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are reached only in deep soundings or where sedimentation was slight.","questions":["Four sherds imported from north Syria or Mesopotamia, lying beside a shell from the Nile at a site in Israel, show how far the pottery of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travelled.","Settlements stayed small, scattered and unranked through the two thousand years of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so the public temples and social ranking that appear at its close stand out."],"answer":"Ubaid period","answerText":"Ubaid period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6000 – 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spread beyond the plain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 4500 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ubaid period</b>, roughly 6000 to 4000 BCE, is the earliest attested phase of permanent habitation on the plain of southern Mesopotamia, and it lasted some two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also the hardest of that plain's phases to reach, because the rivers whose silt made settlement possible went on burying it, so its levels are known from deep soundings or from the few sectors where sedimentation happened to be slight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What comes up is a scattered, non-hierarchical spread of relatively small settlements whose people farmed, herded, fished and hunted, with craft specialisation only beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Irrigation was used at a local scale, and is inferred indirectly from the plant remains rather than read off any surviving canal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The period is tracked across a wide area by its painted pottery, dated closely enough that its absence can date a site: a settlement on Ghagha Island in the Gulf is placed before 5550 BCE partly because no Mesopotamian ware had yet reached it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From about 4500 BCE its cultural elements turn up well outside the plain, along the coasts of the Persian Gulf, in northern Mesopotamia and in western Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Northern sites could be substantial: Tell Zeidan, on the Syrian floodplain, covered 12 hectares (30 acres) between 5300 and 3850 BCE, with public buildings, private houses and specialised craft quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four sherds imported from north Syria or Mesopotamia reached Tel Tsaf in Israel, where they lay among Anatolian obsidian beads, a clay sealing and a shell from the Nile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The copper awl found at that site, the earliest metal object in the southern Levant, matches awls from 5th-millennium BCE sites at Tepe Gawra and Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only late in the period do social stratification and public temples appear, with some sites passing 10 hectares (25 acres); the largest, set in a depression sheltered by higher ground, was the temple-town of Eridu, which the Sumerian King List remembers as one of five cities before the flood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","Kevin Lidour et al., “Exploring the Early Neolithic in the Arabian Gulf: A Newly Discovered 8,400-Year-Old Stone-Built Architecture on Ghagha Island, United Arab Emirates,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0326259, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0326259. [Open access]","Amy K. Styring et al., “Urban Form and Scale Shaped the Agroecology of Early ‘Cities’ in Northern Mesopotamia, the Aegean and Central Europe,” <i>Journal of Agrarian Change</i> 22, no. 4 (2022): 831–54, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9580239/. [Open access]","Yosef Garfinkel et al., “The Beginning of Metallurgy in the Southern Levant: A Late 6th Millennium CalBC Copper Awl from Tel Tsaf, Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 3 (2014): e92591, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0092591. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “The Earliest Cotton Fibers and Pan-Regional Contacts in the Near East,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 1045554, https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpls.2022.1045554/full. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Ubaid_III_pottery_5300-4700_BC_Louvre_Museum.jpg/1280px-Ubaid_III_pottery_5300-4700_BC_Louvre_Museum.jpg","title":"Ubaid period","desc":"Ubaid III pottery, 5300–4700 BCE, Louvre Museum AO 29598. ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ubaid_III_pottery_5300-4700_BC_Louvre_Museum.jpg","alt":"A broad buff pottery jar with a tall flaring neck, its shoulder painted in dark brown with fine bands and a row of zigzags"}},
{"id":"wh-170","num":170,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The marks originally set down for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were craft specialists released from growing their own food, metallurgy, long-distance trade and monumental public buildings.","questions":["A settlement can be large and dense without the ranking and public works the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> implies, which is why Çatalhöyük's doorless houses, entered across the roofs, complicate it.","Settlements of many thousands appeared in Ukraine and Moldova hundreds of years before Uruk, which is one reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no longer told as a single event in one place."],"answer":"urban revolution","answerText":"urban revolution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First proto-urban</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3950 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936, by V. Gordon Childe</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>urban revolution</b> is the name given in 1936 by V. Gordon Childe to the second of two thresholds between hunter-gatherers and the first civilisations, the first being the shift to farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its marks were craft specialisation and metallurgy, the trade of craft goods over long distances, and the accumulation of surplus and wealth in cities where ruling classes lived and monumental public buildings were raised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The engine proposed was the agricultural surplus, which freed resident specialists from producing their own food to work metal, weave at scale, and write down and administer what the new arrangements required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence since has pulled at that in two directions, the first being that a settlement can be large and dense without being ranked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Çatalhöyük in Anatolia held several hundred to a few thousand people in mudbrick houses packed so tightly that many crossed their neighbours' roofs to descend by ladder into doorless homes, and shows no unified trajectory of inequality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The second is that urbanism arose in more places, and more shapes, than a single revolution allows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The world's first proto-urban settlements were the Trypillia mega-sites of Ukraine and Moldova, reaching 320 hectares (790 acres) and some 15,000 inhabitants from about 3950 BCE, hundreds of years before Uruk, with houses set along a ring corridor around an empty centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Amazonia the Casarabe culture spread across roughly 4,500 square kilometres (1,700 square miles) of seasonally flooded Bolivian savannah between 500 and 1400 CE, in a four-tier hierarchy of monumental mounds joined by canals and causeways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mesoamerica produced one of the world's original urbanisation episodes with no beasts of burden, no wheeled transport and only limited and late use of metal tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What holds up best is that density as much as size decided how a city fed itself: high-density urbanism in northern Mesopotamia and the Aegean entailed radical agricultural extensification, a low-input strategy limited by land rather than labour, while lower-density settlement in south-west Germany allowed more intensive management of arable land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Dorian Q. Fuller and Chris J. Stevens, “Between Domestication and Civilization: The Role of Agriculture and Arboriculture in the Emergence of the First Urban Societies,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 28, no. 3 (2019): 263–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6499764/. [Open access]","Frank Schlütz et al., “Isotopes Prove Advanced, Integral Crop Production, and Stockbreeding Strategies Nourished Trypillia Mega-Populations,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 52 (2023): e2312962120, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756257/. [Open access]","Katheryn C. Twiss et al., “‘But Some Were More Equal than Others:’ Exploring Inequality at Neolithic Çatalhöyük,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 9 (2024): e0307067, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0307067. [Open access]","Umberto Lombardo et al., “Maize Monoculture Supported Pre-Columbian Urbanism in Southwestern Amazonia,” <i>Nature</i> 639, no. 8053 (2025): 119–23, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11882439/. [Open access]","Diane Z. Chase et al., “Mesoamerican Urbanism Revisited: Environmental Change, Adaptation, Resilience, Persistence, and Collapse,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 31 (2023): e2211558120, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10400939/. [Open access]","Amy K. Styring et al., “Urban Form and Scale Shaped the Agroecology of Early ‘Cities’ in Northern Mesopotamia, the Aegean and Central Europe,” <i>Journal of Agrarian Change</i> 22, no. 4 (2022): 831–54, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9580239/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg/1280px-Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg","title":"urban revolution","desc":"Maidanetske mega-settlement, Cucuteni-Trypillia culture, 3D model. René Ohlrau, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg","alt":"A 3D model of a prehistoric settlement in which hundreds of small houses stand in concentric rings around a large empty centre, laid over a terrain model of the site today"}},
{"id":"wh-171","num":171,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"The farming year and the river year are badly out of step in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so the flood-recession farming of Egypt and the Indus was not available and the water had to be engineered.","questions":["Rain falling on the Taurus and Zagros keeps the two rivers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> flowing all year, which is what made a plain between two deserts habitable at all.","Enamel from a third-millennium BCE town in the far south of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a diet of cereals, land animals and hardly any marine fish, despite the sea being close."],"answer":"Mesopotamia","answerText":"Mesopotamia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plain settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 6000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Large canals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2500 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mesopotamia</b> is the land of the Tigris and the Euphrates, and the vast low-gradient plain of its south was built out of their own sediment, filling a foreland basin as Arabia converged on Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain on the Taurus and Zagros headwaters keeps rivers that size flowing all year, leaving the region far less dependent on its own weather than a harsh arid zone between two deserts would otherwise be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The catch is timing: a long lag between the farming year and the river year hindered the flood-recession farming Egypt and the Indus Valley could rely on, a mismatch that had to be engineered around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That meant irrigating while the rivers ran low and defending against them while they ran high, and cuneiform texts and radiocarbon-dated canals attest to large-scale hydraulic works from about 2500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rivers also move: below Ramadi and Samarra they sweep across the plain as they lay down sediment, and the avulsion node at Kut has carried the Tigris along several historically attested courses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the plain grew was set by that water: its winter cereals were wheat and barley, and a summer crop became possible only once summer grains had been carried in from Africa and East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Broomcorn millet has now been identified from phytoliths at second-millennium BCE Khani Masi on the Upper Diyala, the earliest evidence yet for cereal multi-cropping in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Abu Tbeirah in the far south, zinc isotopes in third-millennium BCE tooth enamel give an omnivorous diet of cereals, land animals probably including pigs, and a little freshwater fish, but little or no marine fish despite the nearby sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Far to the north, villages baked dough in domed ovens on ceramic trays between 6400 and 5900 BCE, and residues on a few point to breads enriched with lard or oil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The plain itself is now going: the central floodplain has been transformed within the last five decades, many of its ancient sites destroyed outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","Elise Jakoby Laugier, Jesse Casana, and Dan Cabanes, “Phytolith Evidence for the Pastoral Origins of Multi-Cropping in Mesopotamia (Ancient Iraq),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8748697/. [Open access]","Matteo Giaccari et al., “When Collagen Fails: Zinc Isotopes Unlock Sumerian Lifeways in Southern Mesopotamia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 11 (2026): e2526276123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12993939/. [Open access]","Alessandro Pistola et al., “AI-ming Backwards: Vanishing Archaeological Landscapes in Mesopotamia and Automatic Detection of Sites on CORONA Imagery,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0330419, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0330419. [Open access]","Sergio Taranto et al., “Unveiling the Culinary Tradition of ‘Focaccia’ in Late Neolithic Mesopotamia by Way of the Integration of Use-Wear, Phytolith and Organic-Residue Analyses,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 26805, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11538533/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Mesopotamia_9_October_2020.jpg/1920px-Mesopotamia_9_October_2020.jpg","title":"Mesopotamia","desc":"Mesopotamia 9 October 2020. NASA. 2020-10-09. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mesopotamia_9_October_2020.jpg","alt":"A satellite view in which the Tigris and Euphrates run southeast across a tan desert plain as narrow green threads, converging near the head of the Persian Gulf, with mountains along the north and east"}},
{"id":"wh-172","num":172,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Ur, Uruk, Kish, Lagash and Umma competed for water and irrigable land across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, made alliances with one another, and in the end went to war over them.","questions":["Under the Third Dynasty of Ur, merchants in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked about half their time for the state and pursued their own interests with the other half.","Arid ground, salt and bitumen destroy the collagen that isotope studies rely on, so the bodies of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are far harder to read than its hundreds of thousands of texts."],"answer":"Sumer","answerText":"Sumer","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">City-states</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Third Dynasty of Ur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2112 – 2004 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sumer</b> is the collective name for the earliest network of city-states, which emerged about 5,000 years ago in southern Mesopotamia, knit together by shared cultural traditions and economic interests rather than by any single crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was agrarian, sustained by large-scale irrigation, and control over labour was institutionalised and structured around the hydraulic works that irrigation demanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ur, Uruk, Kish, Lagash and Umma competed for control of water and irrigable land, developed alliances with one another, and ultimately entered conflicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After an Akkadian imperial interlude the city-state dynasties revived, but the fall of the Third Dynasty of Ur ended the primacy of Sumer as a dominant political network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under that dynasty, which ruled from 2112 to 2004 BCE, merchants worked about half their time for the state and pursued their own interests with the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The written record it left is enormous: hundreds of thousands of cuneiform texts have been recovered over the past two centuries, most of them in Sumerian and Akkadian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its kings put their names into the building material, stamping or incising bricks in those languages before firing, so that a wall can be dated to a single reign; one assemblage carries twelve kings, Shulgi of Ur among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Weighing was invented around 3000 BCE between Mesopotamia and Egypt, and the Mesopotamian shekel it settled into ran about 8.2 to 8.8 grams (0.29 to 0.31 ounces).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bodies are the hard part, because arid ground, high soil salinity and widespread bitumen destroy the collagen that isotope studies of diet depend on: at Abu Tbeirah only three of 48 samples in earlier work yielded any.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Enough survives for the ordinary to show through, from children weaned onto animal milk and cereals to cattle at Ur and Abu Tbeirah foddered under at least two distinct regimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","Gai Gutherz et al., “Translating Akkadian to English with Neural Machine Translation,” <i>PNAS Nexus</i> 2, no. 5 (2023): pgad096, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10153418/. [Open access]","Matthew D. Howland et al., “Exploring Geomagnetic Variations in Ancient Mesopotamia: Archaeomagnetic Study of Inscribed Bricks from the 3rd–1st Millennia BCE,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 52 (2023): e2313361120, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756309/. [Open access]","Matteo Giaccari et al., “When Collagen Fails: Zinc Isotopes Unlock Sumerian Lifeways in Southern Mesopotamia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 11 (2026): e2526276123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12993939/. [Open access]","Nicola Ialongo, Raphael Hermann, and Lorenz Rahmstorf, “Bronze Age Weight Systems as a Measure of Market Integration in Western Eurasia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 27 (2021): e2105873118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8271817/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Standard_of_Ur_-_War.jpg/1280px-Standard_of_Ur_-_War.jpg","title":"Sumer","desc":"Standard of Ur, 26th century BCE, the 'War' panel. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standard_of_Ur_-_War.jpg","alt":"A mosaic panel of shell and lapis lazuli in three registers: four-wheeled battle wagons drawn by equids, a file of cloaked infantry carrying spears, and prisoners led before a taller figure"}},
{"id":"wh-173","num":173,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"For a thousand years the southern plain carried no more than 15 widely dispersed villages, and then <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew to over 250 hectares and at least 25,000 people.","questions":["At Susa, tokens lay beside bevelled-rim bowls and clay sealings in step with developments at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across what is now the Iraqi border.","Pastoralism, trade and resource diversity all played parts in the rise of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but where the agricultural surplus behind its prosperity came from is still uncertain."],"answer":"Uruk","answerText":"Uruk","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Uruk period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 – 3250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Expansion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3550 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Uruk</b>, the modern Tell al-Warka in southern Iraq, underwent full-fledged urbanisation in the 4th millennium BCE, reaching over 250 hectares (620 acres) at its peak and accommodating at least 25,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What came before makes that stranger: for a thousand years the southern plain carried no more than 15 autonomous villages, widely and fairly evenly dispersed, at an extremely low density.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From about the end of the 5th millennium BCE the population of the alluvium grew roughly eightfold over a thousand years, and the region developed two, then three, then four levels of settlement hierarchy, with marked social stratification and institutions of bureaucratic governance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Through the period named after the city, settlement grew denser and more clustered around urban institutions, and some sites reached several tens of hectares apiece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New techniques were introduced or improved in agriculture, animal husbandry, fishing, textiles, ceramics and metal, architecture and art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By about 3550 BCE, Uruk-related settlements and cultural influences had spread north into upper Mesopotamia and beyond, and east onto the Susiana plain in what is now Khuzestan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Susa the clay tokens turn up among workshops and stores alongside bevelled-rim bowls and cylinder-seal impressions, in step with what was happening at Uruk across the modern border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery changed with it across Mesopotamia as painted decoration disappeared, and at Arslantepe in eastern Anatolia roughly 15 per cent of the assemblage is mass-produced plain bowls, production turning from the symbolic towards efficiency and serialisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Precedence is not settled, and the north has a claim of its own: Tell Brak was a dispersed settlement of 55 hectares (140 acres) with monumental buildings between 4200 and 3900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What none of it explains is the basis: pastoralism, trade and resource diversity all mattered, but the origin of the agricultural surplus behind Uruk's prosperity is uncertain, and irrigation in this period is only indirectly inferred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","Roger Matthews et al., “Information Revolutions and Information Transitions: Counting, Sealing, Writing in Iran 10,000–300 BC,” <i>Journal of Ancient Near Eastern History</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 215–305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12645803/. [Open access]","Amy K. Styring et al., “Urban Form and Scale Shaped the Agroecology of Early ‘Cities’ in Northern Mesopotamia, the Aegean and Central Europe,” <i>Journal of Agrarian Change</i> 22, no. 4 (2022): 831–54, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9580239/. [Open access]","Pamela Fragnoli, “Re-assessing the Notion(s) of Craft Standardization through Diversity Statistics: A Pilot Study on Late Chalcolithic Pottery from Arslantepe in Eastern Anatolia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 1 (2021): e0245660, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0245660. [Open access]","David S. Sandeford, “Organizational Complexity and Demographic Scale in Primary States,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 5, no. 5 (2018): 171137, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5990841/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pergamon_Museum_Cone_mosaics_from_temple_district_facade_of_Eanna_in_Uruk_8330.jpg/1920px-Pergamon_Museum_Cone_mosaics_from_temple_district_facade_of_Eanna_in_Uruk_8330.jpg","title":"Uruk","desc":"Cone mosaics from the temple-district facade of Eanna in Uruk, Pergamon Museum. Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pergamon_Museum_Cone_mosaics_from_temple_district_facade_of_Eanna_in_Uruk_8330.jpg","alt":"A mud-brick wall and a row of engaged half-columns whose surfaces are covered in tens of thousands of small clay cones, their flat ends set in zigzag and lozenge patterns in buff and dark red"}},
{"id":"wh-174","num":174,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A scribe pressed the signs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into moist clay with a stylus and then left the tablet to dry in the open air, firing it only occasionally.","questions":["Because a sign is either impressed or it is not, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no bold and no underline, and scribes reached instead for red, white and yellow pigment.","Salts drawn to the surface of a buried tablet cover the inscription, so text in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that no eye can read is now being recovered with X-ray fluorescence."],"answer":"cuneiform","answerText":"cuneiform","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 BCE – c. 100 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Akkadian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2700 BCE – 75 CE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cuneiform</b> is the wedge-shaped writing of the ancient Near East, and it was a common medium there from the late 4th millennium BCE to the 1st century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scribe impressed the signs into moist clay with a stylus and let the tablet dry in the open air, and only occasionally was the clay fired to make it more durable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ancestor is the proto-cuneiform of Uruk, and by about 3000 BCE the impression or incision of styli on soft clay had become a system complex enough to be reasonably called writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is a script rather than a language: Akkadian, a Semitic tongue attested from about 2700 BCE to 75 CE, is one of the languages it carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Akkadian is rarely written entirely syllabically, and almost all of it uses Sumerian logograms, so profusely in divination literature that many sentences are made of nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Salt is what threatens the tablets now, drawn to the surface over millennia of burial by swings of temperature and humidity until it covers the inscription and the text cannot be read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Firing them or treating them with a consolidant makes the writing legible again and alters the object irreversibly, where X-ray fluorescence at a synchrotron can recover the text without altering it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The script's own logic is binary, a sign being either impressed or not impressed, which leaves no way to emphasise a word by weight or shade as a printer would.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scribes found another way: analysis of tablets from Kish of the mid-3rd millennium BCE has recovered red, white and yellow pigment in the impressed signs, some of it mixed with quartz crystals, and on one literary tablet the brilliant yellow sits in a single sign, <i>dingir</i>, the logograph marking a deity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is still untranslated, for want of readers rather than of texts: the Neo-Assyrian corpus alone runs to just over a million words, comparable to a standard corpus covering a thousand years of English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mirko Surdi et al., “Synchrotron XRF Spectroscopy for Reading Salt-Encrusted Cuneiform Tablets,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 3283, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11770115/. [Open access]","Daniele Chiriu et al., “Raman Identification of Cuneiform Tablet Pigments: Emphasis and Colour Technology in Ancient Mesopotamian Mid-Third Millennium,” <i>Heliyon</i> 3, no. 3 (2017): e00272, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5367865/. [Open access]","Gai Gutherz et al., “Translating Akkadian to English with Neural Machine Translation,” <i>PNAS Nexus</i> 2, no. 5 (2023): pgad096, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10153418/. [Open access]","Juha M. Lahnakoski et al., “Embodied Emotions in Ancient Neo-Assyrian Texts Revealed by Bodily Mapping of Emotional Semantics,” <i>iScience</i> 27, no. 12 (2024): 111365, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11700638/. [Open access]","Roger Matthews et al., “Information Revolutions and Information Transitions: Counting, Sealing, Writing in Iran 10,000–300 BC,” <i>Journal of Ancient Near Eastern History</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 215–305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12645803/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/List_of_workers_and_rations_Kish_ED_Ashmolean.jpg/1920px-List_of_workers_and_rations_Kish_ED_Ashmolean.jpg","title":"Cuneiform","desc":"Cuneiform tablet, list of workers and rations. From Kish, Early Dynastic period. Ashmolean Museum. Zunkir, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:List_of_workers_and_rations_Kish_ED_Ashmolean.jpg","alt":"A square terracotta clay tablet, chipped along its top edge, its face ruled into a grid of boxes and filled with rows of wedge-shaped impressed signs"}},
{"id":"wh-175","num":175,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica are the four places where a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was invented from nothing, and the scripts of Crete and Anatolia are counted as secondary.","questions":["The earliest signs of any <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are pictures, but a picture is rarely used for the thing it shows, one entity standing instead for another.","Rongorongo survives on fewer than 30 wooden objects, and dating one of them to the 15th century suggests the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Rapa Nui was its islanders' own invention."],"answer":"writing system","answerText":"writing system","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First invented</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oracle bones</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rongorongo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the 15th century CE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>writing system</b> is a graphic code, a set of standardized pairings between symbols and meanings in which the symbols take the form of images that can endure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One was invented from nothing only a handful of times, in Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica, and a few more, among them the scripts of Crete and Anatolia, count as secondary inventions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All of them lean on iconicity, since the earliest signs are pictures and their shapes are chosen for what they look like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A picture is rarely used for the thing it shows, though, and what turns one into a sign is metaphor, with its subcategories of pars pro toto and metonymy, whereby one entity stands for another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strategies are local and culturally marked: proto-cuneiform uses human heads and genitalia as pars pro toto, where the Egyptian script uses human body parts as measurement indices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Writing in Mesopotamia and western Iran came out of accounting: first tablets bearing only number signs, then tablets adding one or two signs for the things counted, such as herded animals or dairy products.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oracle bone inscriptions of the late Shang run to some 4,000 signs built from about 170 basic shapes, and at least half the corpus is still undeciphered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the count of primary inventions is four is open, and Rapa Nui is the test: a script called Rongorongo survives there on fewer than 30 wooden objects, and nobody can read it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything turns on whether it was devised before European ships reached the island in the 1720s: four tablets were radiocarbon-dated directly, one gave a secure mid-15th-century date and the rest the 19th, which places its use before any outside influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beyond the famous cases the world's scripts are now being counted and compared, from over 47,000 characters measured across more than 133 of them to the Dongba pictographic writing of the Naxi people of China, catalogued a manuscript at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Ludovica Ottaviano et al., “Metaphors and the Invention of Writing,” <i>Topics in Cognitive Science</i> 18, no. 1 (2026): 245–63, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12831613/. [Open access]","Silvia Ferrara et al., “The Invention of Writing on Rapa Nui (Easter Island): New Radiocarbon Dates on the Rongorongo Script,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 2794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10837134/. [Open access]","Roger Matthews et al., “Information Revolutions and Information Transitions: Counting, Sealing, Writing in Iran 10,000–300 BC,” <i>Journal of Ancient Near Eastern History</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 215–305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12645803/. [Open access]","Helena Miton and Olivier Morin, “Graphic Complexity in Writing Systems,” <i>Cognition</i> 214 (2021): 104771, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8346946/. [Open access]","Yuqi Ma et al., “Dataset for Single Character Detection in Dongba Manuscripts,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 12, no. 1 (2025): 1075, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12217708/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Rongorongo_B-r_Aruku-Kurenga.jpg/1920px-Rongorongo_B-r_Aruku-Kurenga.jpg","title":"Writing system","desc":"Recto of rongorongo Tablet B, or Aruku Kurenga, 43 by 16 centimetres (17 by 6 inches), carrying 1,135 signs across its two faces. Photograph from the Trocadéro Museum, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rongorongo_B-r_Aruku-Kurenga.jpg","alt":"A long wooden tablet with rounded ends, its dark surface covered in close ruled rows of small incised glyphs, many of them stylised human and bird figures"}},
{"id":"wh-176","num":176,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Carved in reverse so that rolling it across damp clay left a raised frieze, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closed doors, jars and sacks, and was rolled across tablets to vouch for what they said.","questions":["No example harder than quartz has been found, because a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut with sand abrasive and hand, palm, pump or bow drills rather than with metal alone.","Susa has yielded scenes of potters at work, files of figures and men fighting on clay sealed with a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an interest in daily life the Near East never matched again."],"answer":"cylinder seal","answerText":"cylinder seal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stamp seals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 6000 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>cylinder seal</b> is a small pierced stone, engraved in reverse so that rolling it across damp clay leaves a raised frieze, and it is attested in Iran and Mesopotamia from about 3500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did not arrive on empty ground, since stamp seals had been pressed into clay in Iran from at least 6000 BCE, the earliest excavated example coming from the Neolithic site of Khaleseh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roughly 2,000 survive from the six centuries between 3300 and 2300 BCE, and they have been called Sumerian art in miniature, the most characteristic object the Sumerians made and the most numerous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clay it marked was more often a fastening than a document: a door closure, a pot mouth, the tied neck of a sack, the knotted wrapping of a reed basket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the apparatus was really for is argued about: on one reading it let institutions dress extractive debt up as balanced reciprocity, a moral cover under which elites could begin charging interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They were cut without metal for most of that history, with abrasive slurries of sand or emery worked by hand, palm, pump and bow drills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No seal or bead harder than quartz has been found, which is the limit those tools imposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavations at four sites in the Diyala region east of Baghdad published them in strict order of depth and position, from the Jamdat Nasr period down to the Old Babylonian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The images repay the trouble: Susa has yielded potters at work, files of figures and men in conflict, an interest in daily activities called unmatched in any later period, while one seal shows the little-known goddess Ninishkun interceding with Ishtar for its owner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bore may yet betray a forgery, since electron microscopy of a silicone impression found concentric abrasion marks in all four genuine bores examined and, bar one exception, none in the fakes, though the authors want many more tested first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Leonard Gorelick and A. John Gwinnett, “The Origin and Development of the Ancient Near Eastern Cylinder Seal,” <i>Expedition</i> 23, no. 4 (1981), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-origin-and-development-of-the-ancient-near-eastern-cylinder-seal/. [Open access]","Roger Matthews et al., “Information Revolutions and Information Transitions: Counting, Sealing, Writing in Iran 10,000–300 BC,” <i>Journal of Ancient Near Eastern History</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 215–305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12645803/. [Open access]","Henri Frankfort, <i>Stratified Cylinder Seals from the Diyala Region</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 72 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/oip-72-stratified-cylinder-seals-diyala-region. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures, “Highlights from the Collection: Mesopotamia,” University of Chicago, https://isac.uchicago.edu/collections/highlights/highlights-collection-mesopotamia. [Open access]","Leonard Gorelick and A. John Gwinnett, “Ancient Seals and Modern Science,” <i>Expedition</i> 20, no. 2 (1978), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/ancient-seals-and-modern-science/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Cylinder_seal_and_modern_impression._Worshiper_pouring_a_libation_before_a_seated_god%2Cca._2112%E2%80%932004_B.C._Neo-Sumarian_Mesopotamia_Nippur.jpg/1920px-Cylinder_seal_and_modern_impression._Worshiper_pouring_a_libation_before_a_seated_god%2Cca._2112%E2%80%932004_B.C._Neo-Sumarian_Mesopotamia_Nippur.jpg","title":"Cylinder seal","desc":"Cylinder seal and modern impression: a worshipper pouring a libation before a seated god, c. 2112–2004 BCE, Neo-Sumerian, from Nippur. Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cylinder_seal_and_modern_impression._Worshiper_pouring_a_libation_before_a_seated_god,ca._2112%E2%80%932004_B.C._Neo-Sumarian_Mesopotamia_Nippur.jpg","alt":"A small green stone cylinder carved with two standing figures, and beside it the wider clay impression it makes when rolled out, showing figures approaching a seated god beneath a crescent"}},
{"id":"wh-177","num":177,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"The core of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is unbaked brick and only its thick skin is fired, which is why weeper-holes were left through the facing to drain the winter rain.","questions":["The longer sides were built slightly convex rather than straight, so that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would seem to gain strength toward the centre, where the building stood highest.","Nabonidus, last king of Babylon, set three more stages on the old base of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Ur, but respected the ancient building and did not alter the design of its stairways."],"answer":"ziggurat","answerText":"ziggurat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 400 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>ziggurat</b> is a stepped platform of brick raised over a temple, and the best preserved of them stands at Ur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its lowest stage measures about 40 by 59 metres (130 by 195 feet) and the tower rose some 28 metres (92 feet) above the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside it is crude unbaked brick; outside, a thick facing of baked brick laid in pitch for mortar, with reed mats dipped in pitch between courses at intervals to bond the mass together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The long sides were built slightly convex rather than straight, which makes them read as stronger, and weeper-holes were left through the facing so that winter rain could drain instead of swelling the core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nabonidus, last king of Babylon, recorded on clay cylinders that he had repaired and completed a tower begun and left unfinished by two earlier kings of Ur, setting three more stages on the old base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Eridu had one too: survey there reconstructs the temple complex of the Third Dynasty of Ur as a three-stepped artificial hill about 12 metres (39 feet) high, on a site occupied from the 6th to the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A king began such a work in person, at least in ceremony, since a foundation figurine buried in a box beneath a tower of Enlil's temple shows Ur-Nammu carrying on his head the basket of clay for its first brick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Firing a brick locks the direction and strength of the Earth's magnetic field into it, so a wall whose bricks name the reign that laid them records the field of that moment as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The fabric survives remarkably well, and much of the wall face at Ur was described on excavation as looking as clean and new as when it was first built, though the tower had been wrecked in the 5th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What a visitor sees there now is partly modern, because the ziggurat was re-faced with new brick in the 1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["C. Leonard Woolley, “The Ziggurat of Ur,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> 15, no. 2 (1924): 107–14, https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/1235/. [Open access]","William B. Hafford, “City of the Moon,” <i>Expedition</i> 59, no. 1 (2017), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/city-of-the-moon/. [Open access]","Philippe Quenet et al., “Archaeological Mission at Eridu: The 2022 Campaign of the University of Strasbourg at Abu Shahrayn. Preliminary Report Part 1,” <i>Iraq</i> 87 (2025): 263–312, https://doi.org/10.1017/irq.2025.10035. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures, “Highlights from the Collection: Mesopotamia,” University of Chicago, https://isac.uchicago.edu/collections/highlights/highlights-collection-mesopotamia. [Open access]","Matthew D. Howland et al., “Exploring Geomagnetic Variations in Ancient Mesopotamia: Archaeomagnetic Study of Inscribed Bricks from the 3rd–1st Millennia BCE,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 52 (2023): e2313361120, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756309/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Ziggurat_of_Ur_Site_in_Nasiriyah_16.jpg/1280px-Ziggurat_of_Ur_Site_in_Nasiriyah_16.jpg","title":"Ziggurat","desc":"The ziggurat of Ur, near Nasiriyah, Iraq, whose lowest stage was re-faced with modern brick in the 1960s. Alli Khalil, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziggurat_of_Ur_Site_in_Nasiriyah_16.jpg","alt":"A vast brick platform rising in stages from flat desert, its sloping facade broken by a long central stairway climbing to the upper terrace, with visitors standing small at its foot"}},
{"id":"wh-178","num":178,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A deep sounding through the temple mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found 18 building levels stacked one above another, the lowest of them numbered XVIII.","questions":["After the kingship descended from heaven it was in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the list gives Alulim a reign of 28,800 years and his successor one of 36,000.","A Sumerian hymn calls the shrine at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an artfully built mountain floating on the water, its silver and lapis lazuli shining like daylight."],"answer":"Eridu","answerText":"Eridu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6000 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple levels</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18, lowest XVIII</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Eridu</b>, the modern Abu Shahrayn, lies southwest of Nasiriyah in southern Iraq, in a depression cut off from the Euphrates plain by a ridge, and it was occupied from the 6th to the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sumerian tradition put the beginning there: after the kingship descended from heaven, the king list says, the kingship was in Eridug, where Alulim reigned 28,800 years and Alaljar 36,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Then Eridug fell and the kingship was taken to Bad-tibira, and after five cities and eight kings the flood swept over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reigns after it are ordinary lengths, so the list divides a primordial age from a historical one at that point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city belonged to Enki, god of fresh water and wisdom, and a hymn calls his shrine an artfully built mountain floating on the water, built of silver and decorated with lapis lazuli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What made the site famous is a deep sounding cut through the temple, which found 18 architectural levels one above another, the lowest numbered XVIII.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sherds through that sequence are what the chronology of the Ubaid period was built on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest shrine is a small building of medium rectangular mud bricks, a long central sanctuary with smaller rooms either side, an altar and an offering podium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its facades already alternate buttresses with recesses and its inner walls were finished in white painted mud plaster, features that match the temples of two thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The whole was eventually the E-abzu, reconstructed for the Third Dynasty of Ur as a three-stepped artificial hill some 12 metres (39 feet) high, standing over a basin that the plain's own drainage still carries the name of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Philippe Quenet et al., “Archaeological Mission at Eridu: The 2022 Campaign of the University of Strasbourg at Abu Shahrayn. Preliminary Report Part 1,” <i>Iraq</i> 87 (2025): 263–312, https://doi.org/10.1017/irq.2025.10035. [Open access]","“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Samuel Noah Kramer, “Iraqi Excavations During the War Years,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> 13, no. 2 (1948): 2–29, https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/3023/. [Open access]","Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","“Enki's Journey to Nibru,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.1.1.4 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr114.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Ziggurat_at_Eridu_%2830809118442%29.jpg","title":"Eridu","desc":"Baked bricks from the ziggurat at Eridu, southwest of Nasiriyah, Iraq, some of them stamped with the name of King Ur-Nammu. David Stanley, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziggurat_at_Eridu_(30809118442).jpg","alt":"A few surviving courses of mudbrick emerging from a low rubble-strewn mound, with empty grey desert running flat to the horizon behind it"}},
{"id":"wh-179","num":179,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Drone survey at Lagash has found monumental walls cutting a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into separate quarters, each with its own workshops rather than one centre serving the whole.","questions":["At the usual estimate of 100 to 200 people per hectare, the 500 hectares of Lagash make a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of somewhere between 60,000 and 120,000 inhabitants.","The Sumerian king list is a list of cities as much as of men, since it hands the kingship from one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the next as each in turn falls."],"answer":"Sumerian city-state","answerText":"Sumerian city-state","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Dynastic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2900 – 2334 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lagash</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 hectares</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Sumerian city-state</b> was a city with its own ruler, its own god and its own irrigated hinterland, and the king list treats the arrangement as the natural order, handing the kingship from one city to the next as each in turn falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where the water came from changed what they were: as the head of the Persian Gulf silted up and the tides that had reached far inland withdrew, the cities came to depend on river irrigation instead, and the canal networks of the Early Dynastic period followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Freshwater marshes had been established at Ur and Uruk by about 7000 years ago, so the first settlers on the plain were living beside water rather than digging for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cuneiform texts and radiocarbon-dated canals attest to large-scale hydraulic works from about 4500 years ago; before that, irrigation is only indirectly inferred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The engineering is what the competition was about, since a city upstream of a rival held its water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Lagash shows what one of them looked like on the ground: roughly 500 hectares (1,240 acres), dense across hundreds of them, and cut by monumental walls into distinct walled quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the usual estimate of 100 to 200 people per hectare, that is a population of 60,000 to 120,000 in the later 3rd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Production was scattered rather than central: pottery kilns concentrated in one area of 75 by 150 metres (246 by 492 feet), with separate zones for metal, stone and shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Housing survives too, and the largest continuous stretch of it ever uncovered anywhere is at Ur, from the Old Babylonian period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Eridu shows the other end of the same story, occupied from the 6th to the 2nd millennium BCE and still being rebuilt on its own mound long after the cities that shared the plain with it had risen and gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Augusta McMahon et al., “Dense Urbanism and Economic Multi-centrism at Third-Millennium BC Lagash,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 393 (2023): 596–615, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.36. [Open access]","Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Philippe Quenet et al., “Archaeological Mission at Eridu: The 2022 Campaign of the University of Strasbourg at Abu Shahrayn. Preliminary Report Part 1,” <i>Iraq</i> 87 (2025): 263–312, https://doi.org/10.1017/irq.2025.10035. [Open access]","William B. Hafford, “City of the Moon,” <i>Expedition</i> 59, no. 1 (2017), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/city-of-the-moon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Limestone_Victory_Stele_of_King_Eannatum_of_Lagash_%28Stele_of_the_Vultures%29%2C_c._2450_BC_%2828008189230%29.jpg/1280px-Limestone_Victory_Stele_of_King_Eannatum_of_Lagash_%28Stele_of_the_Vultures%29%2C_c._2450_BC_%2828008189230%29.jpg","title":"Sumerian city-state","desc":"Limestone victory stele of Eannatum of Lagash, known as the Stele of the Vultures, c. 2450 BCE, in the Ancient Near East gallery of the Louvre. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limestone_Victory_Stele_of_King_Eannatum_of_Lagash_(Stele_of_the_Vultures),_c._2450_BC_(28008189230).jpg","alt":"Three joined limestone fragments forming a broad gable, carved in low relief with helmeted soldiers, fallen bodies and a standing figure beside a tree, over a band of cuneiform signs"}},
{"id":"wh-180","num":180,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Twelve seasons of digging at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 1922 and 1934 employed as many as 300 workers at a time and ran four or five months a year.","questions":["The moon god Nanna held <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the freshwater marshes beside it had been there since about 7,000 years ago, long before anyone cut a canal.","Sixteen spectacular tombs and thousands of plainer graves make up the royal cemetery at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose wealth caused a great stir among archaeologists and the public alike."],"answer":"Ur","answerText":"Ur","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1922 – 1934</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ur</b> was the city of Nanna, the Sumerian moon god, and its mound holds some 5,000 years of occupation, the earliest habitation reaching back to about 5500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sat where the land met the water: freshwater marshes had been established there by about 7,000 years ago, before any of the plain's great canals were cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its best-known monument is the ziggurat, whose lowest stage measures about 40 by 59 metres (130 by 195 feet) and which still rises above the flat country around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The royal cemetery is what made it famous, with 16 spectacular tombs and thousands more ordinary graves, and the wealth of Queen Puabi's largely intact tomb caused a great stir among archaeologists and the public alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Penn Museum and the British Museum dug there jointly from 1922 to 1934, twelve seasons of four or five months, with as many as 300 workers at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Third Dynasty of Ur the city was the head of a state that ran most of the plain, and a tablet found beside a tomb under one of its house floors is dated to the reign of Shulgi, the dynasty's second king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reading the people of the southern plain has been harder than reading their documents, because arid ground, salt and bitumen destroy the collagen that isotope studies of diet rely on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Zinc in tooth enamel survives where collagen does not, and work at nearby Abu Tbeirah found milk still in the diet before weaning was complete, with cattle there and at Ur kept under at least two feeding regimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Area AH holds the largest continuous expanse of Old Babylonian housing ever uncovered, so the ordinary streets are as well known here as the tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Work has begun again since 2015, and the ziggurat a visitor climbs today was re-faced with modern brick in the 1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["William B. Hafford, “City of the Moon,” <i>Expedition</i> 59, no. 1 (2017), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/city-of-the-moon/. [Open access]","C. Leonard Woolley, “The Ziggurat of Ur,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> 15, no. 2 (1924): 107–14, https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/1235/. [Open access]","Matteo Giaccari et al., “When Collagen Fails: Zinc Isotopes Unlock Sumerian Lifeways in Southern Mesopotamia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 11 (2026): e2526276123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12993939/. [Open access]","Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","William B. Hafford and Richard L. Zettler, “The Thrill of Discovery,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-thrill-of-discovery/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Ram_Caught_in_a_Thicket%2C_from_ancient_Mesopotamia_-_British_Museum_%2842636807315%29.jpg/1280px-Ram_Caught_in_a_Thicket%2C_from_ancient_Mesopotamia_-_British_Museum_%2842636807315%29.jpg","title":"Ur","desc":"The Ram in a Thicket, from the royal cemetery at Ur, on display in the British Museum. Nate Loper, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ram_Caught_in_a_Thicket,_from_ancient_Mesopotamia_-_British_Museum_(42636807315).jpg","alt":"A goat figure of gold leaf, lapis lazuli and shell seen through museum glass, its horned head raised and its forelegs resting among the golden branches of a stylised plant"}},
{"id":"wh-181","num":181,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Of more than 2,000 graves in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, only 16 had the structure, the wealth and the sacrificed attendants that convinced the excavators they were royal.","questions":["Skulls from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went through a hospital CT scanner in 2007, the first step in overturning the story that its victims drank poison from clay cups.","Three gold pins at the shoulder of a woman buried in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> each carried a lapis cylinder seal, and one of them read Puabi, Queen."],"answer":"Royal Cemetery at Ur","answerText":"Royal Cemetery at Ur","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Royal tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 2500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemetery dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1926 – 27</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Royal Cemetery at Ur</b> is a burial ground of more than 2,000 graves, dug into waste land below the city walls where rubbish had been tipped for generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only 16 had the structure, the wealth and the evidence of human sacrifice that convinced the excavators they were royal, and those 16 gave the whole ground its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name does not really fit: the ground was in use for about a thousand years, many graves are older than the royal tombs, and their occupants belong to no recorded dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seal impressions and tablets in the rubbish above and below put the royal tombs in the century between about 2600 and 2500 BCE, the period archaeologists call Early Dynastic IIIA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They came in two kinds: graves with a built tomb chamber, and graves where only the approach pit survived, called death-pits after the bodies laid out in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The largest was the Great Death Pit, which held 74 bodies, most of them young women in elaborate dress, while the King's Grave held a man in a copper helmet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The one clearly royal body lay on a bier in a chamber of its own, its whole upper part covered in beads of gold, silver, lapis lazuli, carnelian and agate, with three gold pins at the shoulder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each pin carried a lapis cylinder seal, and one read Puabi, Queen; her name is in no king list, and because the seal names no husband she may have ruled in her own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Standard of Ur came out of this ground, and the equids drawing its war wagons are probably kungas, which ancient DNA from a Syrian burial identifies as donkey and wild-ass hybrids, the first animals people bred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Woolley had the attendants walk down the ramp and drink poison from clay cups; CT scans of two crushed skulls point instead to victims felled with a sharp instrument, heated, embalmed with mercury and laid in rows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["William B. Hafford, “A Spectacular Discovery: Burials Simple and Splendid,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-spectacular-discovery/. [Open access]","P. R. S. Moorey, “What Do We Know About the People Buried in the Royal Cemetery?,” <i>Expedition</i> 20, no. 1 (1977), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/what-do-we-know-about-the-people-buried-in-the-royal-cemetery/. [Open access]","Aubrey Baadsgaard, “Imaging Ur's Sacrificial Dead: An Archaeological CAT Scan,” <i>Expedition</i> 49, no. 2 (2007), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/imaging-urs-sacrificial-dead-an-archaeological-cat-scan/. [Open access]","Aubrey Baadsgaard et al., “Human Sacrifice and Intentional Corpse Preservation in the Royal Cemetery of Ur,” <i>Antiquity</i> 85, no. 327 (2011): 27–42, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00067417. [Paywalled]","E. Andrew Bennett et al., “The Genetic Identity of the Earliest Human-Made Hybrid Animals, the Kungas of Syro-Mesopotamia,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 2 (2022): eabm0218, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8759742/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Standard_of_Ur_-_Peace_Panel_-_Sumer.jpg/1280px-Standard_of_Ur_-_Peace_Panel_-_Sumer.jpg","title":"Royal Cemetery at Ur","desc":"Detail of the 'peace' panel of the Standard of Ur, found in the Royal Cemetery at Ur and inlaid in shell, lapis lazuli and red limestone. Juan Carlos Fonseca Mata, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standard_of_Ur_-_Peace_Panel_-_Sumer.jpg","alt":"Three horizontal registers of small figures inlaid in pale shell against deep blue lapis lazuli, with seated men raising cups at a banquet above and files of attendants leading rams, cattle and a laden ass below"}},
{"id":"wh-182","num":182,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"The Sumerian king list makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the fifth king of Uruk's first dynasty, gives his father as a phantom, and credits him with a reign of 126 years.","questions":["Asked whether to submit to Kish, the elders of Uruk said yes and the young men said fight, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the advice of the young men.","Enlil grants <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kingship but not eternal life, and in the poem on his death he is made governor of the netherworld and judge among its ghosts."],"answer":"Gilgamesh","answerText":"Gilgamesh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign in the list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">126 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">List compiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Gilgamesh</b> is the king of Uruk in Sumerian tradition, and the Sumerian king list makes him the city's fifth ruler, lord of Kullab, with a father it calls a phantom and a reign of 126 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That list is not history as we understand it but a mixture of myth, legend and historical information, opening on fantastically long reigns that shorten as it reaches rulers who can be checked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its fullest copy is a clay prism written around 1800 BCE, and it seems to have been composed to argue that only one city at a time could hold the kingship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The kings of Isin, in power when it was written, stand at the end of it, which makes the whole document an argument for their own legitimacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A drier text, the history of the Tummal, is the better evidence: it has him build the Numunbura in Enlil's temple at Nippur, and his son Ur-lugal bring the goddess Ninlil into the Tummal after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It puts him third in a line of builders, after Enmebaragesi of Kish and his son Aga, and after Mesannepada of Ur and his, which is a plainer claim than a reign of 126 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Sumerian poems about him are separate from the Akkadian epic, and in one of them envoys of Aga arrive from Kish and he puts the question of submission to two assemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The elders tell him to submit and the able-bodied men tell him to fight; he trusts the young men, Aga besieges Uruk, and Gilgamesh takes him captive and then sets him free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Enkidu is his servant there rather than his companion, and his radiance on the rampart overwhelms his own city's young and old before the besiegers see him at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the poem on his death Enlil grants him kingship but not eternal life, his household is laid down with him inside the palace at Uruk, and he is made governor of the netherworld, passing judgment among its ghosts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Ashmolean Museum, “Sumerian King List,” University of Oxford, https://www.ashmolean.org/sumerian-king-list. [Open access]","“The History of the Tummal,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.3 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr213.htm. [Open access]","“Gilgameš and Aga,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.1.8.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr1811.htm. [Open access]","“The Death of Gilgameš,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.1.8.1.3 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr1813.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Sumerian_King_List%2C_1800_BC%2C_Larsa%2C_Iraq.jpg/1280px-Sumerian_King_List%2C_1800_BC%2C_Larsa%2C_Iraq.jpg","title":"Gilgamesh","desc":"The Weld-Blundell Prism, the fullest surviving copy of the Sumerian king list, written on clay at about 1800 BCE and now in the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford. Gts-tg, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sumerian_King_List,_1800_BC,_Larsa,_Iraq.jpg","alt":"A tall four-sided clay prism standing on a clear mount in a museum gallery, its faces ruled into columns and covered from top to bottom in small cuneiform signs"}},
{"id":"wh-183","num":183,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Copied on twelve tablets from the 8th to the 2nd centuries BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the Babylonian poem that tradition credits to a scholar called Sin-leqi-unninni.","questions":["Enkidu, Humbaba in the Cedar Forest and the Bull of Heaven all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose hero fails the test set him and goes home to Uruk empty-handed.","A ship resting on a mountain and a dove sent out that found no resting place stand on the eleventh tablet of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, read in the British Museum in 1872."],"answer":"Epic of Gilgamesh","answerText":"Epic of Gilgamesh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest copies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Standard version</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 100 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Epic of Gilgamesh</b> is the long Babylonian poem about the king of Uruk, and the Babylonians referred to it by its own opening line, <i>ša nagba imuru</i>, 'He that has seen all things'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mature form, known as the Standard Babylonian version, sets some 3,000 lines out on twelve tablets, and was still being copied between the 8th and the 2nd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tradition credits that edition to a scholar named Sin-leqi-unninni, supposedly working late in the 2nd millennium BCE, but the poem was old long before he touched it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ten oldest surviving manuscripts were written in southern Iraq between about 1800 and 1500 BCE, and behind them lie separate Sumerian poems about the same hero, which are not the epic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fullest copies come from the library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh, and even those are late copies of a much older original.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Gilgamesh oppresses Uruk until the gods send the wild man Enkidu to distract him, and the two become friends, travel to the Cedar Forest and kill its guardian Humbaba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Gilgamesh spurns the goddess Ishtar she sends the Bull of Heaven against his city, the pair kill that too, and she answers with a long, lingering death for Enkidu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The last third of the poem has him searching alone for everlasting life, finding the flood survivor Uta-napishtim at the ends of the earth, failing the test set him and going home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The eleventh tablet carries Uta-napishtim's own account of that flood, and it was that tablet, pieced together from fragments in the British Museum and announced in December 1872, which made the poem known again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even now it is incomplete: the standard edition works from 184 fragments making up some 73 manuscripts, no full set survives in any one hand, and between a third and a fifth of the text has never been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Stephen Langdon, “The Epic Of Gilgamish,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> VIII, no. 1 (1917): 29–38, https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/550/. [Open access]","Eleanor Robson, review of <i>The Babylonian Gilgamesh Epic: Introduction, Critical Edition and Cuneiform Texts</i>, by A. R. George, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2004.04.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.04.21/. [Open access]","“Gilgameš and Ḫuwawa (Version A),” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.1.8.1.5 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr1815.htm. [Open access]","Morris Jastrow Jr. and Albert T. Clay, <i>An Old Babylonian Version of the Gilgamesh Epic</i>, Yale Oriental Series, Researches IV.3 (New Haven: Yale University Press, 1920), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/11000/11000-h/11000-h.htm. [Open access]","George Smith, <i>The Chaldean Account of Genesis</i>, ed. A. H. Sayce, new ed. (London: Sampson Low, 1880), 4–5, https://archive.org/stream/chaldeanaccounto00smituoft/chaldeanaccounto00smituoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/British_Museum_Flood_Tablet_1.jpg/1280px-British_Museum_Flood_Tablet_1.jpg","title":"Epic of Gilgamesh","desc":"The Flood Tablet, the eleventh tablet of the Epic of Gilgamesh, from the library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh and now in the British Museum. Mike Peel, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum_Flood_Tablet_1.jpg","alt":"A broken rectangular clay tablet held on a clear mount, its face ruled into two columns and packed from edge to edge with rows of small wedge-shaped cuneiform signs, the lower right corner lost"}},
{"id":"wh-184","num":184,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"The Sumerian king list calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the son of a gardener and the cupbearer of Ur-Zababa, says he built Agade, and gives him a reign of 56 years.","questions":["Sent to Uruk with a tablet asking for his own murder in one legend, and set adrift on the Euphrates in another, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded the dynasty of Agade.","Lugal-zage-si of Uruk was led a prisoner through the gate of Enlil by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose daughter Enheduanna became priestess of the moon god at Ur."],"answer":"Sargon of Akkad","answerText":"Sargon of Akkad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2334 – 2279 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the king list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">56 years</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sargon of Akkad</b> founded the dynasty of Agade, and the Sumerian king list introduces him as the man 'whose father was a gardener, the cupbearer of Ur-Zababa', who built the city and ruled for 56 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Louvre dates his reign to about 2334–2279 BCE on the conventional chronology, and holds a fragment of one of his stelae, made at Nippur and found at Susa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The king list has the kingship pass to Agade from Lugal-zage-si of Uruk; copies of his own monuments say what that meant, for he beat Lugal-zage-si and led him a prisoner through the gate of Enlil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ur, Lagash, Umma and Adab surrendered after further fighting and had their walls thrown down, and the empire that resulted reached from the lower sea to the upper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His daughter Enheduanna was made priestess of the moon god at Ur, where an alabaster disk showing her at the ritual survives from the goddess Ningal's courtyard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Those monuments mostly reach us at second hand: a scribe at Nippur copied their inscriptions off the stelae and statues in the temple courts, and his tablet survives where the stones do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the rest comes through omen texts, where episodes of his reign hang off the markings on a sacrificial liver, and one was long read as saying he crossed the Mediterranean when the chronicle behind it says the Persian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two legends grew around him, centuries apart and in different languages: in the Sumerian one Ur-Zababa, frightened by a dream of Inana drowning him in a river of blood, sends his cupbearer to Lugal-zage-si with a tablet asking for the bearer's own murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That text pauses to explain that in those days writing words on tablets existed but putting tablets into envelopes did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the Akkadian one his mother sets him adrift on the Euphrates, Akki the irrigator draws him out and raises him as a gardener, and Ishtar loves him and gives him the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Stèle de Sargon d'Akkad,” inv. SB 1, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010123451. [Open access]","Leon Legrain, “The Inscriptions of the Kings of Agade,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> XIV, no. 3 (1923), https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/9818/. [Open access]","Leon Legrain, “Sargon of Agade,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> X, no. 3–4 (1944), https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/2639/. [Open access]","L. W. King, <i>Chronicles Concerning Early Babylonian Kings</i>, vol. 1 (London: Luzac, 1907), 11, 28–29, 63, https://archive.org/stream/chroniclesconcer01king/chroniclesconcer01king_djvu.txt. [Open access]","“The Sargon legend,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.4 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr214.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Sargon_victory_stele_three-quarter_view.jpg/1280px-Sargon_victory_stele_three-quarter_view.jpg","title":"Sargon of Akkad","desc":"The carved upper register of the fragmentary stele of Sargon of Akkad, made at Nippur, found at Susa and now in the Louvre. ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_victory_stele_three-quarter_view.jpg","alt":"The worn top of a dark stone block carved in low relief, showing a file of figures in long fringed garments standing shoulder to shoulder beneath a band of upright spear-like shapes"}},
{"id":"wh-185","num":185,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Ruled from Agade by Sargon and his descendants, and given 11 kings and 181 years by the Sumerian king list, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached from the lower sea to the upper.","questions":["Naram-Sin's two-metre stele shows him in a horned tiara climbing a wooded mountain over his enemies, and it is the great surviving monument of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, later carried off to Susa.","A Sumerian poem blames the fall of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Naram-Sin taking axes to Enlil's temple, for which the god sent the Gutians down from the mountains."],"answer":"Akkadian Empire","answerText":"Akkadian Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sargon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2334 – 2279 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naram-Sin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2254 – 2218 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the king list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 kings, 181 years</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Akkadian Empire</b> was the state ruled from Agade by Sargon and his descendants, and the Sumerian king list gives its dynasty 11 kings and 181 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copies of those kings' own monuments put its limits at Elam and the Persian Gulf in the south and the Lebanon, the Taurus and the Hittite land in the north-west, from the lower sea to the upper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same inscriptions have the boats of Magan, Meluhha and Dilmun lining the quays in front of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Sumerian poem remembers Agade at its height, with Elam and Subir loading themselves with goods for it like packasses, and monkeys, elephants and water buffalo jostling in its public squares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sargon's grandson Naram-Sin, whom the king list also gives 56 years, left a two-metre limestone stele of his victory over the Lullubi on which he climbs a wooded mountain wearing the horned tiara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That stele stood at Sippar until an Elamite king carried it off to Susa as booty in the 12th century BCE and cut his own inscription into its carved mountain, which is where it was found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The poem blames Naram-Sin for everything that followed: unable to get a favourable omen, he took axes to Enlil's temple at Nippur and shipped its gold, silver and copper away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Enlil answered by bringing the Gutians down from the mountains, 'an unbridled people, with human intelligence but canine instincts', after which grain sold at half a litre for a shekel and the dead lay unburied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is an explanation rather than a record: nearly all surviving Sumerian literature reaches us in Old Babylonian copies of about 1900 to 1600 BCE, written out by schoolboys centuries after Agade fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The king list is barer and stranger, breaking off after Sargon's line to ask 'Then who was king?' twice over, and answering with four men who ruled three years between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Leon Legrain, “The Inscriptions of the Kings of Agade,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> XIV, no. 3 (1923), https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/9818/. [Open access]","“The cursing of Agade,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.5 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr215.htm. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Stèle de Naram-Sin,” inv. SB 4, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010123450. [Open access]","Martin Worthington, review of <i>The Literature of Ancient Sumer</i>, by Jeremy A. Black et al., <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.01.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.01.27/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Stele_Naram_Sim_Louvre_Sb4.jpg/1280px-Stele_Naram_Sim_Louvre_Sb4.jpg","title":"Akkadian Empire","desc":"Detail of the victory stele of Naram-Sin, king of Akkad, celebrating his victory over the Lullubi of the Zagros; limestone, c. 2250 BCE, brought from Sippar to Susa as spoils of war in the 12th century BCE and now in the Louvre. Jastrow. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_Naram_Sim_Louvre_Sb4.jpg","alt":"Pink limestone carved in relief: a large bearded figure in a horned helmet strides forward with a bow and axe over the bodies of smaller fallen men, while one enemy kneels and another stands with hands raised beside a panel of cuneiform"}},
{"id":"wh-186","num":186,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Founded by Ur-Nammu after the Gutians were driven out, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled Sumer for over a century and built the ziggurat that still dominates the ruins of Ur.","questions":["Shulgi's copper foundation pegs at Susa and the smashed stela of his father belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended when the Elamites carried Ibbi-Sin away.","Nabonidus dug into the old brickwork and left cylinders saying he had finished the tower begun by the kings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, fifteen centuries before his own reign."],"answer":"Third Dynasty of Ur","answerText":"Third Dynasty of Ur","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shulgi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2094 – 2047 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Third Dynasty of Ur</b> took the kingship after Utu-hegal of Uruk drove the Gutians out, and for over a century Ur-Nammu and his successors ruled from Ur as kings of Sumer and Akkad, claiming the four corners of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ur-Nammu began and his son Shulgi finished a building programme: the temple of Nanna and Ningal with its brick tower on an artificial platform, a hall of justice, a treasury, a palace and a house for the high priestess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That tower's lower stage measures about 40 by 59 metres (130 by 195 feet) and it stands 28 metres (92 feet) high, with three flights of a hundred steps meeting at a gateway in the parapet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shulgi, whose reign the Louvre dates to about 2094–2047 BCE, buried copper pegs shaped as a man carrying a builder's basket at Susa and a bronze one at Girsu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ur was a school town as well as a capital: a branch of the Edubba or Tablet House stood there, over 800 pieces of Sumerian literary text were found, and its kings are credited with a Sumerian renaissance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Shulgi raised a stela to his father in the courtyard below the hall of justice, 3 metres (10 feet) of limestone in five registers front and back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It shows Ur-Nammu shouldering the mason's tools to lay the foundation brick while workers climb ladders with baskets of mud and figures pour rain from heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the top are the crescent moon below the radiating sun, one band lists the canals opened by royal command, and on the back a bull is sacrificed while two men beat huge drums.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Ibbi-Sin the Elamites and their allies from Mari plundered city and temple and carried the king and the statue of Nanna off to Susa, leaving the stela smashed across the pavement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifteen centuries later Nabonidus, the last king of Babylon, drove a hole into the brickwork hunting the old foundation records, and left cylinders saying he had completed the tower they began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Leon Legrain, “The Third Dynasty of Ur. The Stela of Ur-Nammu,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> X, no. 3–4 (1944): 56–57, https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/2657/. [Open access]","C. Leonard Woolley, “The Ziggurat of Ur,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> XV, no. 2 (1924): 107–14, https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/1235/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Clou de fondation,” inv. SB 2879, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010176957. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Figurine de fondation,” inv. AO 27907, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010152187. [Open access]","Samuel Noah Kramer, “The Ur Excavations and Sumerian Literature,” <i>Expedition</i> 20, no. 1 (1977), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-ur-excavations-and-sumerian-literature/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Ur-Nassiriyah.jpg/1280px-Ur-Nassiriyah.jpg","title":"Third Dynasty of Ur","desc":"The ziggurat at Ur, begun by Ur-Nammu, rising behind the excavated mud-brick walls of the city near Nasiriyah in southern Iraq. M. Lubinski, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ur-Nassiriyah.jpg","alt":"A broad stepped tower of mud brick with a straight staircase climbing its front, standing above a flat desert plain beyond a field of low excavated brick walls laid out in rooms and corridors"}},
{"id":"wh-187","num":187,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A broken Nippur tablet in Istanbul carries the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose prologue promises that the orphan shall not fall a prey to the wealthy nor the widow to the powerful.","questions":["Where a later code would take an eye for an eye, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sets a fine of ten silver shekels on a man who has cut off another's foot.","Promulgated by the king who founded the Third Dynasty of Ur, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the title of oldest law collection known, three centuries older than Hammurabi's."],"answer":"Code of Ur-Nammu","answerText":"Code of Ur-Nammu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ur-Nammu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reigned from 2112 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Copies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Code of Ur-Nammu</b> is the oldest collection of laws yet recovered, written in Sumerian in the name of the king who founded an independent state centred on Ur in 2112 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His line, the third dynasty to rule that city, held Sumer until the Elamites sacked Ur in 2004 BCE, and its second king Shulgi reigned from 2094 to 2047 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The code survives on a broken tablet in the Istanbul Museum of the Ancient Orient, number 3191 of its Nippur collection, joined from two fragments by a curator there and read in 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is sun-baked and light brown, 20 by 10 centimetres (8 by 4 inches), ruled into eight columns with about 45 lines each, and more than half the writing is destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a copy rather than the original, of the kind students at Nippur and Ur wrote out to learn Sumerian, made some centuries after Ur-Nammu's own reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The obverse carries a long prologue in which An and Enlil make the moon-god Nanna king of Ur, and Nanna in turn chooses Ur-Nammu as his earthly representative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The king defeats the rival city of Lagash and kills its ruler Namhani, drives out the 'grabbers' of the citizens' oxen, sheep and donkeys, and fixes honest and unchangeable weights and measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He claims that under him the orphan 'did not fall a prey to the wealthy', the widow not to the powerful, and 'the man of one shekel' not to 'the man of one mina'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The laws began on the reverse and are so damaged that only five can be restored, of which three set silver fines where later codes take an eye for an eye: ten shekels for a severed foot, a whole mina for bones broken with a weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The title of oldest code had already passed twice in the five years before, from Hammurabi's stele in the Louvre to a Sumerian code of Lipit-Ishtar and then to a Babylonian one dug up at Tell Harmal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Samuel Noah Kramer, “Law and Love, A Hymn, A Prayer, and a Word to the Wise,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> XVII, no. 2 (1952): 23–42, https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/3637/. [Open access]","Richard Zettler, “The Empire of Ur-Nammu under His Descendants,” <i>Expedition</i> 29, no. 1 (1987), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-empire-of-ur-nammu-under-his-descendants/. [Open access]","Richard L. Zettler, “From Beneath the Temple: Inscribed Objects from Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 28, no. 3 (1986), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/from-beneath-the-temple/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Code de Hammurabi,” inv. SB 8, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010174436. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “Texts, Tablets, and Teaching: Scribal Education in Nippur and Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 40, no. 2 (1998), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/texts-tablets-and-teaching/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Ur_Nammu_code_Istanbul_cropped.jpg","title":"Code of Ur-Nammu","desc":"The broken clay tablet from Nippur carrying the Code of Ur-Nammu, in the Istanbul Archaeology Museums. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ur_Nammu_code_Istanbul_cropped.jpg","alt":"A dark, badly broken rectangular clay tablet, its surface ruled into narrow vertical columns of small wedge-shaped cuneiform signs, with much of the upper right corner and the lower edge missing"}},
{"id":"wh-188","num":188,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A Sumerian schoolroom poem copied in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a boy caned for speaking Akkadian, which says plainly enough which of the two languages he grew up with.","questions":["More than fifty houses on narrow winding lanes stood at Ur in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the dead of those households were buried beneath their own floors.","Amorite dynasties out of Syria ruled the rival cities of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them Mari in the north and the Babylon where Hammurabi came to the throne in 1792 BCE."],"answer":"Old Babylonian period","answerText":"Old Babylonian period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ur sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2004 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hammurabi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792 – 1750 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Old Babylonian period</b> runs from about 2000 to 1500 BCE, the centuries after the Elamites sacked Ur in 2004 BCE and left southern Mesopotamia with no single ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Power passed to dynasties founded by Amorites, a West Semitic people who had been moving east out of Syria and whose earliest rulers the Assyrian king list remembers as 'kings who lived in tents'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They took the old cities for their own and made rivals of them, Shamshi-Adad building an Assyrian realm in the north while the diplomatic correspondence kept at Mari records how these kings dealt with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The one who came out on top was Hammurabi, himself of Amorite descent, who ruled Babylon from 1792 to 1750 BCE and whose stele of laws now stands in the Louvre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Akkadian was by now the language people spoke, and Sumerian survived as the language of scholarship, taught in school to men who had grown up speaking something else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nearly all the Sumerian literature that can be read today was copied out in the scribal schools, most of it at Nippur, by students working a curriculum that began with sign lists and ended with poetry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Schooling took place in ordinary houses rather than in institutions, and one house at Ur held so many exercise tablets that it can only have been a schoolroom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Sumerian poem about school days, itself one of the set texts, has a boy beaten by the keeper of the gate, by the keeper of the beer jug, and by the Sumerian teacher who demands to know why he spoke Akkadian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Ur more than fifty houses stood along narrow winding lanes, and they remain the largest continuous stretch of Old Babylonian housing known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The dead were buried beneath the floors of those houses, in or near the room the family kept as its chapel, and 172 graves of the Isin-Larsa and Old Babylonian levels were recorded in that quarter alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Steve Tinney, “Texts, Tablets, and Teaching: Scribal Education in Nippur and Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 40, no. 2 (1998), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/texts-tablets-and-teaching/. [Open access]","Richard Zettler, “The Empire of Ur-Nammu under His Descendants,” <i>Expedition</i> 29, no. 1 (1987), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-empire-of-ur-nammu-under-his-descendants/. [Open access]","Theresa Howard Carter, “Early Assyrians in the Sinjar,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/early-assyrians-in-the-sinjar/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Code de Hammurabi,” inv. SB 8, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010174436. [Open access]","William B. Hafford, “City of the Moon: New Excavations at Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 59, no. 1 (2017), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/city-of-the-moon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/CBS7847_Ninmeshara_Penn_Museum.jpg","title":"Old Babylonian period","desc":"An Old Babylonian copy of the Sumerian hymn known as the Exaltation of Inanna, found at Nippur and now in the Penn Museum. Masha Stoyanova, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CBS7847_Ninmeshara_Penn_Museum.jpg","alt":"A pale, chipped rectangular clay tablet standing upright, its face covered in close-set horizontal rows of small cuneiform signs divided into ruled columns"}},
{"id":"wh-189","num":189,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Though his people spoke Akkadian, the scribes made praise poems in Sumerian for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, calling him the good shepherd who restored the rites at the moon god's shrine.","questions":["An Amorite king who rose to challenge the Assyrian realm to his north, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled Babylon for 42 years, and a stele carrying his name was later carried off to Susa as booty.","A dark stone vase carved on the Iranian Plateau was inscribed again more than 800 years later in the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which shows what such eastern goods were still worth."],"answer":"Hammurabi","answerText":"Hammurabi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Babylon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st dynasty</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Hammurabi</b> ruled Babylon from 1792 to 1750 BCE as a king of its first dynasty, and he came of Amorite stock, the West Semitic people who had been moving east into Mesopotamia out of Syria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his earlier years the greater power lay north of him, where Shamshi-Adad had built an Assyrian realm reaching from the Khabur to the Euphrates, and he emerged as a serious rival about ten years before that king died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How the kings of the age dealt with one another is known above all from the diplomatic correspondence kept at Mari.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His court had poetry made for him in Sumerian although Akkadian was what people spoke, and one praise poem calls him the good shepherd, reverent, eloquent and skilled in precious plans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same poem has him daily before Nanna and Ningal, restoring the purification rites and the divine powers at their shrine E-kish-nu-gal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A prayer written for him asks Enki to prolong the years of his life and to grant him lordship over every one of the foreign lands, which is as plain a statement of ambition as the period leaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A high official of Larsa named Lu-Nanna had a small kneeling figure of copper and gold made and dedicated it, in Sumerian, to the Amorites' own god Amurru, for the life of Hammurabi, king of Babylon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His name travelled as far as his trade did: a dark stone vase carved on the Iranian Plateau about 2400 BCE was inscribed for him more than 800 years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He is remembered above all for a stele of laws whose relief shows him standing before the sun god Shamash, a meeting that legitimises his sovereignty and the judgements cut into the stone below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stele did not stay in Babylonia: where Ur-Nammu's was smashed by invaders, this one was carried off whole as booty, and it came out of the ground at Susa in Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Musée du Louvre, “Code de Hammurabi,” inv. SB 8, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010174436. [Open access]","Theresa Howard Carter, “Early Assyrians in the Sinjar,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/early-assyrians-in-the-sinjar/. [Open access]","“A praise poem of Ḫammu-rābi (Ḫammu-rābi C),” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.8.2.3 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.2.8.2.3. [Open access]","“A prayer to Enki for Ḫammu-rābi (Ḫammu-rābi B),” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.8.2.2 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.2.8.2.2. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “L'Adorant de Larsa,” inv. AO 15704, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010140480. [Open access]","Holly Pittman, “Highlands and Lowlands: The Interaction of Communities,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/highlands-and-lowlands/. [Open access]","Jeanny Vorys Canby, “A Monumental Puzzle,” <i>Expedition</i> 29, no. 1 (1987), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-monumental-puzzle/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Worshiper_Larsa_Mp3h9123.jpg","title":"Hammurabi","desc":"The Worshipper of Larsa, a copper and gold figure dedicated to the god Amurru for the life of Hammurabi, king of Babylon. Louvre AO 15704. Rama, CC BY-SA 3.0 FR, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Worshiper_Larsa_Mp3h9123.jpg","alt":"A small metal figure of a bearded man in a high-brimmed cap, half kneeling with one hand raised before his mouth in prayer, his face and hand covered in gold, set on a base beside a reclining ram"}},
{"id":"wh-190","num":190,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A surgeon who saves an eye earns ten shekels under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and one whose knife kills the patient has his hands cut off for it.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes an eye for an eye between men of equal standing, but settles the very same injury in silver when it is done to a freed man.","Cut into a basalt stele over two metres tall and carried off to Susa in antiquity, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is numbered to 282, though 34 of its paragraphs no longer survive."],"answer":"Code of Hammurabi","answerText":"Code of Hammurabi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hammurabi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Laws</span><span class=\"dt-v\">282, of which 34 lost</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Code of Hammurabi</b> is a collection of laws set out in Old Babylonian Akkadian on a basalt stele 2.25 metres (7 feet 5 inches) tall, made for the king who ruled Babylon from 1792 to 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stone was found not in Babylonia but at Susa in Iran, dug out of the ground there over the winter of 1901–02, and it stands in the Louvre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A relief across its top shows the king receiving the symbols of kingship from Shamash, the sun god, in a scene that underscores his divinely given right to establish the law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The laws are framed by a prologue in which Anu and Bel call Hammurabi by name to bring about the rule of righteousness in the land, and by an epilogue thick with curses on anyone who would alter them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are numbered to 282 and run the gamut of a man's possible deeds and misdeeds, though 34 paragraphs between the sixty-fifth and the hundredth no longer survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The best-known provision is talion: a man who puts out another's eye loses his own, and one who breaks another's bone has his bone broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That holds only between men of equal standing, since the same injuries done to a freed man are settled in silver, so what a crime costs depends on the rank of the person it was done to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A surgeon who opens a tumour and saves the eye earns ten shekels, while one whose knife kills the patient has his hands cut off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A builder whose house collapses and kills the owner is put to death, and if it kills the owner's son then the builder's son is put to death in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A clay tablet in the Louvre carries the prologue in 120 lines, and its shorter list of the cities Hammurabi had annexed suggests it was written before the stele, so the text grew as his conquests did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Musée du Louvre, “Code de Hammurabi,” inv. SB 8, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010174436. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “The Uses of Writing: Inscribed Objects and Texts in the Middle East Galleries,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-uses-of-writing/. [Open access]","<i>The Code of Hammurabi</i>, trans. L. W. King, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/ancient/hamcode.asp. [Open access]","Samuel Noah Kramer, “Law and Love, A Hymn, A Prayer, and a Word to the Wise,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> XVII, no. 2 (1952): 23–42, https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/3637/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Tablette: prologue du code de Hammurabi,” inv. AO 10237, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010134970. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Code_of_Hammurabi_10.jpg","title":"Code of Hammurabi","desc":"The top of the stele in the Louvre, with Hammurabi standing before the enthroned sun god Shamash, beside the gallery's own panel on the code. Chad and Steph, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Code_of_Hammurabi_10.jpg","alt":"The rounded top of a tall dark stone pillar carved in relief with a seated god holding out a ring and rod to a standing bearded king, the stone below them covered in columns of cuneiform, with a lit museum wall panel to the left"}},
{"id":"wh-191","num":191,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Hammurabi’s laws close by asking that his name be repeated for ever in Esagila, the temple of Marduk at the heart of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that any wronged man read them there.","questions":["The gods gave Marduk dominion over earthly man and then called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by his name, so that city and god rose together and could not afterwards be told apart.","Eleven centuries after Hammurabi, Nebuchadnezzar II was still stamping his foundation bricks at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the title provider of Esagila, the temple at its heart."],"answer":"Babylon","answerText":"Babylon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hammurabi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nebuchadnezzar II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">604 – 562 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken by Cyrus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">539 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Babylon</b> was the city on the Euphrates, in the south of Mesopotamia, that an Amorite dynasty raised into a capital, and its rise is announced in the prologue to Hammurabi’s laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There the gods give Marduk dominion over earthly man, and then call Babylon by his name, make it great on earth and found an everlasting kingdom in it whose foundations are laid as solidly as those of heaven and earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> City and god rose together, so that the king could style himself the sun of Babylon whose rays shed light over the land of Sumer and Akkad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same prologue takes care to present him as the patron of far older places, re-establishing Eridu, enriching Ur, laying again the foundations of Sippar and giving back to the city of Ashur its protecting god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marduk’s own temple at the centre of Babylon was Esagila, and the laws close by asking that the king’s name be repeated for ever there, and that any wronged man come, stand before the royal image and read what was just.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Eleven centuries later Nebuchadnezzar II was still stamping his foundation bricks with the title provider of Esagila, first-born son of Nabopolassar, king of Babylon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His is the Babylon that can be seen, rebuilt on a scale nothing before it had matched, with a processional way walled in glazed and moulded brick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lions in coloured glaze paced along those walls about 600 BCE, and it was such bright fragments lying loose on the ground that first drew excavators to the mound at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By then Babylon had inherited an empire, the Babylonians of southern Mesopotamia having conquered Assyria in 612 BCE and taken over the territories it had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its own turn came in 539 BCE, when Cyrus the Great of Persia took the city, and Babylon passed into somebody else’s empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["<i>The Code of Hammurabi</i>, trans. L. W. King, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/ancient/hamcode.asp. [Open access]","Theresa Howard Carter, “Early Assyrians in the Sinjar,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/early-assyrians-in-the-sinjar/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Brique de fondation de Nabuchodonosor II,” inv. AO 32450, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010156663. [Open access]","Robert H. Dyson, Jr., “A Babylonian Lion in Toronto,” <i>Expedition</i> 5, no. 2 (1963), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-babylonian-lion-in-toronto/. [Open access]","Virginia R. Herrmann, “From Trash to Historical Treasure: Stamped Handles from Gibeon in Judah,” <i>Expedition</i> 64, no. 3 (2023), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/from-trash-to-historical-treasure/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Processional_Way%2C_Babylon_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Processional_Way%2C_Babylon_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Processional Way, Babylon","desc":"A striding lion in glazed and moulded brick from the Processional Way, dated to the reign of Nebuchadnezzar II and re-erected in the Pergamon Museum in Berlin, where the surviving glazed pieces stand among plain brick. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Processional_Way,_Babylon_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"A lion walking to the left, modelled in relief in brick and glazed in white, ochre and orange against a field of blue brick, with banded borders above and below, much of the surrounding brickwork plain and unglazed"}},
{"id":"wh-192","num":192,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because its worshippers left hymns, dedications and inscriptions buried under temple walls, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be read from the inside as well as dug up, city god by city god.","questions":["Since the temple was the god's own house, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made building one the king's work, and he carried in the first basket of bricks himself.","Because the great temples were not open to everyone at all times, much of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> happened at neighbourhood shrines and in the private chapels many houses kept."],"answer":"Mesopotamian religion","answerText":"Mesopotamian religion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Votive statue</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Offering stand</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2120 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple at Ur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shulgi, 2094 – 2047 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mesopotamian religion</b> was the worship of the gods of the cities between the Tigris and Euphrates, and it can be read from the inside, in hymns, dedications and building inscriptions worshippers wrote down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each city had a patron god of its own: at Ur it was Nanna, the moon god, and at Nippur, Enlil, chief of all Mesopotamian deities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A hymn has all the gods of the earth bow down to Enlil on his holy dais in Nippur, the lofty bond between heaven and earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The temple was that god's house, and the same hymn gives it a daily festival, everlasting hand-washing rites, and graded priests: the <i>lagar</i> expert in blessing, the <i>gudug</i> in lustration rites, the <i>nueš</i> in the holy prayers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Priests and priestesses ran the cult of a deity, and the most powerful at Ur was the <i>entu</i> priestess, who kept the rituals of Nanna and his consort Ningal, a post usually held by the king's daughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Building for a god was the king's work: he brought in the first basket of bricks at the groundbreaking and had his name stamped into them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath the walls of Nimintabba's temple at Ur, boxes of baked brick set in bitumen each held a steatite tablet and a statuette of a man with a filled basket on his head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their eight lines of Sumerian say whose house it was: for the goddess Nimintabba, his lady, Shulgi, mighty man, king of Ur, king of Sumer and Akkad, her house, built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Others reached the gods by giving: a scribe dedicated a diorite statue of a worshipper with joined hands to the god Erra for the life of Naram-Sin, and Ninalla gave Bawu an offering stand for the life of her husband Gudea of Lagash and her own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most worship happened closer to home, at neighbourhood shrines and in the private chapels, altar and incense chimney and all, that many houses kept, since the great temples were not always open to everyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["William B. Hafford, “Mesopotamian City Life: Four Thousand Years Ago,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/mesopotamian-city-life/. [Open access]","“Enlil in the E-kur (Enlil A),” <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i> t.4.05.1, Faculty of Oriental Studies, University of Oxford, https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.4.05.1. [Open access]","Richard L. Zettler, “From Beneath the Temple: Inscribed Objects from Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 28, no. 3 (1986), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/from-beneath-the-temple/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Fragment d’une statue votive de Naram-Sin,” inv. SB 53, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010121488. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Support d'offrandes ou brûle parfum voué à Gudea,” inv. AO 29931, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010154298. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Foundation_figurine_of_Ur-Nammu%2C_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._21st_century_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg/1280px-Foundation_figurine_of_Ur-Nammu%2C_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._21st_century_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg","title":"Foundation figurine of Ur-Nammu","desc":"A bronze figure buried with an inscribed stone tablet beneath the E-kur of Enlil at Nippur, showing the king carrying a basket of clay on his head as the builder of the temple. Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foundation_figurine_of_Ur-Nammu,_from_Nippur,_Iraq._21st_century_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg","alt":"A small bronze figure of a bearded man in a long skirt, both arms raised to steady a wide basket balanced on his head, the lower part of the skirt covered in cuneiform and tapering towards a peg"}},
{"id":"wh-193","num":193,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Man is made from the blood of Kingu, and heaven from half of Tiamat, in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the seven-tablet poem the Babylonians called after its own first two words.","questions":["Europe knew the story only from a Greek summary until the tablets of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were read, and found the sea cut in two much as the summary had said.","The seventh and last tablet is given over to proclaiming the fifty names of Marduk, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ends on the god it has spent six tablets raising above the rest."],"answer":"Enuma Elish","answerText":"Enuma Elish","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Nineveh copies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reign of Ashurbanipal, c. 670 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Enuma Elish</b> is the Babylonian poem of the creation, called after its own first two words, “When in the height”, and written out on seven tablets running to some 994 lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens before anything has a name: heaven not yet named, the earth beneath bearing no name, Apsu who begat them and Tiamat the mother of them both, their waters mingled and no field yet formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gods come forth from that chaos, Ea overthrows Apsu, and Tiamat sets about avenging him, promoting the god Kingu to lead her host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only after Ea and Anu have each failed to appease her does Anshar appeal to Marduk to become the champion of the gods, who confer their power on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marduk spreads out his net and catches her, looses the evil wind in her face, then splits her up like a flat fish into two halves and makes one a covering for heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Man was made last, out of a god’s blood, though whose stayed uncertain until tablets from Ashur closed the gap in the sixth: not Marduk’s own, as had been conjectured, but Kingu’s, condemned as the one who created warfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The seventh tablet ends by proclaiming Marduk’s fifty names, of which the recovered text gives half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before a line of it could be read again, Europe knew the story only from a priest of Babylon writing in Greek, who told how Belus cut a woman asunder to make the earth of one half of her and the heavens of the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Every copy then known had been inscribed for the library at Nineveh in the reign of Ashurbanipal, and none was an original, the Assyrians themselves copying something older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its order of events runs as Genesis does, primeval chaos, light, sky, dry land and the astral bodies, with man last of all, and the likeness reaches even the syntax of the opening verses, “When … at which time … then …”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["L. W. King, <i>The Seven Tablets of Creation</i>, vol. 1 (London: Luzac, 1902), https://archive.org/details/the-seven-tablets-of-creation.-vol.-1. [Open access]","D. D. Luckenbill, “The Ashur Version of the Seven Tablets of Creation,” <i>The American Journal of Semitic Languages and Literatures</i> 38, no. 1 (1921): 12–35, https://archive.org/details/ashurversionofse00luck. [Open access]","E. A. Speiser, “Mesopotamian Motifs in the Early Chapters of Genesis,” <i>Expedition</i> 5, no. 1 (1962), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/mesopotamian-motifs-in-the-early-chapters-of-genesis/. [Open access]","George Smith, <i>The Chaldean Account of Genesis</i> (New York: Scribner, Armstrong, 1876), https://archive.org/details/chaldeanaccount00smitrich. [Open access]","Isaac Preston Cory, <i>Ancient Fragments</i>, 2nd ed. (London: William Pickering, 1832), https://archive.org/details/CoryIPAncientFragmentsContainingWhatRemainsOfTheWritingsOfSanchoniathoBerossusAb. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Enuma_Elish_K.3473.jpg/1280px-Enuma_Elish_K.3473.jpg","title":"A tablet of Enuma Elish","desc":"Tablet K.3473, a fragment of the poem from the library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh, broken and pinned back together in the British Museum. Zunkir, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enuma_Elish_K.3473.jpg","alt":"A tall clay tablet broken into several pieces and mounted with small clips against a green cloth panel, its face covered in closely ruled lines of cuneiform"}},
{"id":"wh-194","num":194,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because a square with its diagonals drawn in was a school exercise, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knew what would be called Pythagoras’ theorem more than a thousand years before Pythagoras.","questions":["Counting in sixties and writing with only two symbols, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled thousands of tablets with tables, problems and calculations that scribes copied out at school.","A table of whole numbers at Columbia shows that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a method for making right-angled triangles out of whole numbers, a millennium and a half before Euclid."],"answer":"Babylonian mathematics","answerText":"Babylonian mathematics","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Babylonian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">School at Ur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first half of the 18th century BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Babylonian mathematics</b> is known from thousands of clay tablets of the Old Babylonian period, roughly 1900 to 1700 BCE, which carry tables, problems and calculations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its numbers are sexagesimal, counted in sixties, and written in a notation built entirely out of two symbols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of the tablets come from the scribal schools, and the commonest of them are the arithmetical tables young scribes copied out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One from Nippur is small and narrow, its script practised and elegant, the end of the text ruled off with a neat horizontal line and signed “long tablet of Gan-Gal”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A private house at Ur gave up over two thousand texts, administrative, lexical, mathematical and literary, those that can be dated coming from the first half of the 18th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A round school tablet at Yale carries nothing but a diagram, a square with both its diagonals drawn in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Along one diagonal is an accurate approximation of the square root of 2 in sexagesimal notation, with one side labelled with its length and the product of the two written there as well, so the scribe is using what would be called Pythagoras’ theorem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tablet known as Plimpton 322 goes further, showing that a method existed for generating Pythagorean triples, sets of three whole numbers in which the square of one is the sum of the squares of the other two, a millennium and a half before Euclid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What that tablet was for is still argued over, with no agreement on how its numbers were worked out or on whether it belonged to scribal education or to something more like research.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the advanced work was harder than anything a scribe would meet in a career, and reads as knowledge pursued for its own sake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Before Pythagoras: The Culture of Old Babylonian Mathematics,” exhibition, New York University, 2010, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/index.html. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Tablet Illustrating Pythagoras’ Theorem and the Square Root of 2,” YBC 7289, Yale Babylonian Collection, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/ybc-7289/. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Table of Whole-Number Sides of Pythagorean Triangles,” Plimpton 322, Columbia University, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/plimpton-322/. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “School Tablet with Multiplication Table,” B6063, University of Pennsylvania Museum, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/b-6063/. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “Texts, Tablets, and Teaching: Scribal Education in Nippur and Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 40, no. 2 (1998), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/texts-tablets-and-teaching/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg/1280px-YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg","title":"Tablet YBC 7289","desc":"The obverse of a round school tablet in the Yale Babylonian Collection, a square with both its diagonals drawn in, photographed with the numbers written along them transcribed alongside. A. Urcia, Yale Peabody Museum of Natural History, and Theodor Langhorne Franklin, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg","alt":"A round reddish clay tablet, chipped at the edges, incised with a square and both of its diagonals, cuneiform numbers cut along one side and one diagonal, with modern numerals printed beside each group"}},
{"id":"wh-195","num":195,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because a diary was kept night after night for seven centuries, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a record in which a comet of 164 BCE can still be identified as Halley’s.","questions":["Watching the sky was never separable from reading it, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recorded that when Jupiter appears in Elul the land will eat good food.","Setting the twelve signs against the twelve months of the year, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave every month its own stones, its own plants and its own trees."],"answer":"Babylonian astronomy","answerText":"Babylonian astronomy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Diaries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 BCE – 75 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zodiac tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Seleucid, 305 – 64 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Babylonian astronomy</b> was the systematic watching of the sky, written down in cuneiform over more than seven centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The astronomical diaries run from about 750 BCE to perhaps as late as 75 CE, and what survives of them shows the sky mapped carefully and the heavenly bodies observed day by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Assyrian kings were sent reports by named observers, and one of them admits that two or three times during these days we have looked for Mars, but could not see it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seven moving bodies, the sun and moon among them, had names of their own, Sin for the moon and Shamash for the sun, Dilbat for Venus and Kaimanu for Saturn, and their places were noted against the constellations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eclipses were watched with the greatest possible care and their duration and extent recorded, the moon’s disc being read as four parts standing for four countries, so that a total eclipse was called a darkness of the countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Observing was inseparable from divining: when Jupiter appears in Elul the land will eat good food, and when the moon occults Jupiter a great king will die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tablet from Uruk sets the twelve signs of the zodiac against the twelve months of the year, giving each month its own stones, plants and trees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another from the same city records the phases of the moon, then divides the sign of the Ram into twelve parts of its own, then casts horoscopes on the agreement between events and the movement of the planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 164 BCE the diaries noted a comet that had appeared in the east in the path of Anu, near the Pleiades and Taurus, and passed west along the path of Ea, which has since been identified as Halley’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greeks and Romans later divided the day into hours by adapting what they took from Babylonia and from Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["R. Campbell Thompson, <i>The Reports of the Magicians and Astrologers of Nineveh and Babylon</i>, vol. 2, English Translation and Transliteration (London: Luzac, 1900), https://archive.org/details/reportsofmagicia02thom. [Open access]","Christina Griffith, “Halley’s Comet: A Frequent Guest in Earth’s Cosmic Backyard,” <i>Expedition</i> 61, no. 1 (2019), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/halleys-comet/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Calendrier zodiacal du signe de la vierge,” inv. AO 6448, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010167087. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Tablette : observations astronomiques suivies d’horoscopes,” inv. AO 6483, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010167126. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Time and Cosmos in Greco-Roman Antiquity,” exhibition, New York University, 2016, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/time-cosmos/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/British_Museum%2C_Babylon_Astronomical_Diary%2C_331-330_BC.jpg/1280px-British_Museum%2C_Babylon_Astronomical_Diary%2C_331-330_BC.jpg","title":"An astronomical diary from Babylon","desc":"Part of a diary of 331–330 BCE recording a lunar eclipse, and the earliest contemporary record of the battle of Gaugamela, in the British Museum. Brigade Piron, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum,_Babylon_Astronomical_Diary,_331-330_BC.jpg","alt":"A broken clay tablet mounted on a metal stand against an amber background, its face ruled into close lines of small, sharply cut cuneiform"}},
{"id":"wh-196","num":196,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because a pupil's practice tablet went back into the water bucket to be softened and used again, most of what survives from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is there by accident.","questions":["Teaching in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began not with words but with wedges: repeated verticals, then corners, pressed into clay with a reed stylus until the hand made them without thinking.","Since few schoolrooms have been identified, much teaching in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is thought to have gone on in private houses, one of which at Ur gave up over two thousand texts."],"answer":"edubba","answerText":"edubba","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Babylonian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">School at Ur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nippur exercise</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1730 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>edubba</b>, or tablet house, was the Sumerian school where a scribe learned to write, and the exercise tablets left in it are how that teaching is known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Few schoolrooms are known, and much of the work went on in private houses: the house at 7 Quiet Street in Ur gave up over two thousand texts, the datable ones from the first half of the 18th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A pupil began with the stylus itself, a trimmed reed pressed into clay to leave repeated vertical wedges, then corner wedges, and only afterwards combinations that made signs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Syllables came next, about 80 enough to spell anything a scribe needed, then lists of personal names and of words grouped by subject, the trees and wooden objects running past 800 entries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Literature came last, and its range was wide: myths, praises of kings, city laments, hymns to gods and temples, disputations between animals, seasons and tools, proverbs, and comic pieces about school life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The commonest exercise tablet at Nippur carries two columns, the teacher's example on the left and on the right a scratch pad written and erased until it wore thin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small round tablets called lentils took a single line from the teacher for copying underneath; one, from a hymn to Lipit-Eshtar, reads “She guided your finger on the clay”, the 'she' being Nisaba, goddess of writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most tablets went back into a bucket of water to be used again, and a brick-lined box of exercise fragments was found sunk into the floor of one house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In one Sumerian dialogue a supervisor passes on his teacher's rules, that zeal is proper for a task and time-wasting is taboo, and the young scribe answers that he has run the household and worked the fields and resents being lectured as a shirker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing stayed the property of that class, and a hymn copied long afterwards called the scribal art the mother of orators and father of masters, delightful and never satiating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Steve Tinney, “Texts, Tablets, and Teaching: Scribal Education in Nippur and Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 40, no. 2 (1998), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/texts-tablets-and-teaching/. [Open access]","“The advice of a supervisor to a younger scribe (E-dub-ba-a C),” <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i> t.5.1.3, Faculty of Oriental Studies, University of Oxford, https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.5.1.3. [Open access]","Stephen J. Tinney, “From Clay to Computers,” <i>Expedition</i> 50, no. 3 (2008), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/from-clay-to-computers/. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “The Uses of Writing: Inscribed Objects and Texts in the Middle East Galleries,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-uses-of-writing/. [Open access]","Ann Guinan, Gary Oller, and Dorothy Ormsby, “Nippur Rebaked: The Conservation of Cuneiform Tablets,” <i>Expedition</i> 18, no. 3 (1976), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/nippur-rebaked/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Cuneiform_tablet_with_schoolwork_BM_104096.jpg/1280px-Cuneiform_tablet_with_schoolwork_BM_104096.jpg","title":"School tablet with a proverb","desc":"A small round school tablet of the Old Babylonian period carrying a proverb: the teacher's version is on this face and the pupil's copy on the other. British Museum 104096. Zunkir, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuneiform_tablet_with_schoolwork_BM_104096.jpg","alt":"A palm-sized disc of pale clay held upright on a clear museum mount, its face ruled into four bands filled with wedge-shaped signs and the lowest part of the disc left blank"}},
{"id":"wh-197","num":197,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Since the southern alluvium had mud, reeds and dates but no metal, no timber and no hard coloured stone, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was how the cities got everything else.","questions":["Cuneiform texts name three lands beyond the Lower Sea, Dilmun, Magan and Meluhha, always in that order, which is the clearest map anyone has of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by sea.","Wool worked into cloth was the surplus southern Mesopotamia had to spend, which made textiles the main thing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to offer for the metals and stone the south lacked."],"answer":"Mesopotamian trade","answerText":"Mesopotamian trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Textile trade</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sargon of Akkad</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2334 – 2279 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assur caravans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 2nd millennium BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mesopotamian trade</b> was the exchange that kept the southern cities supplied, since almost everything an urban life needed had to be brought in from somewhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The alluvium gave mud and marsh reeds to build with, bitumen oozing from the ground to waterproof boats and containers, poor stone in accessible outcrops and the date palm; beyond those it gave nothing, no metals, no tall hardwood timbers and no hard, colourful stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the south did have was water and land, and pasture on the uplands either side of the rivers for great herds of sheep and goats, whose wool the cities spun and wove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of the 4th millennium BCE textiles had become its chief manufactured commodity, and cloth, with some specially prepared foods and ointments, was what it paid the highlands with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lapis lazuli came from Afghanistan, more than 1,900 kilometres (1,200 miles) off across mountain and desert, and since Sumer controlled neither the source nor the plateau between, the supply ran through independent middlemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The texts name three lands beyond the Lower Sea, and always in that order or its reverse, which reads as a sequence in space: Dilmun, Magan and Meluhha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dilmun was Bahrain and the Arabian shore beside it, Magan a land of copper in what is now Oman, and Meluhha the Indus civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sargon of Akkad boasted that ships from all three docked at his capital, and a hymn has Enki order the Dilmun boats loaded with timber and the boats of Meluhha to carry gold and silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What came back was copper and other metals, wood, pearls and animals, and the traffic left its mark: Indus seals at Ur, carnelian beads, probably Indian, on the cloak in Queen Puabi's tomb, and Shu-ilishu, whose cylinder seal names him a translator of the Meluhhan language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Trade also went overland, and merchants from Assur carried tin north into central Anatolia early in the 2nd millennium BCE and left their tablets at Kanesh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Holly Pittman, “Highlands and Lowlands,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/highlands-and-lowlands/. [Open access]","Gregory L. Possehl, “The Middle Asian Interaction Sphere,” <i>Expedition</i> 49, no. 1 (2007), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-middle-asian-interaction-sphere/. [Open access]","Maurizio Tosi and Marcello Piperno, “Lithic Technology Behind the Ancient Lapis Lazuli Trade,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 1 (1973), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/lithic-technology-behind-the-ancient-lapis-lazuli-trade/. [Open access]","“Enki and the world order,” <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i> t.1.1.3, Faculty of Oriental Studies, University of Oxford, https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.1.1.3. [Open access]","Robert Maddin, Tamara Stech Wheeler, and James D. Muhly, “Tin in the Ancient Near East,” <i>Expedition</i> 19, no. 2 (1977), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/tin-in-the-ancient-near-east/. [Open access]","Gregory L. Possehl, “Shu-ilishu’s Cylinder Seal,” <i>Expedition</i> 48, no. 1 (2006), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/shu-ilishus-cylinder-seal/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Akkadian_cylinder_seal_with_inscription_Shu-ilishu%2C_interpreter_of_the_Meluhhan_language%2C_Louvre_Museum_AO_22310.jpg/1280px-Akkadian_cylinder_seal_with_inscription_Shu-ilishu%2C_interpreter_of_the_Meluhhan_language%2C_Louvre_Museum_AO_22310.jpg","title":"The cylinder seal of Shu-ilishu","desc":"A rolled impression of the cylinder seal of Shu-ilishu, whose inscription names him a translator of the Meluhhan language. Louvre AO 22310, photographed for the 19th-century catalogue of the Collection de Clercq. Louis de Clercq, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akkadian_cylinder_seal_with_inscription_Shu-ilishu,_interpreter_of_the_Meluhhan_language,_Louvre_Museum_AO_22310.jpg","alt":"A wide black-and-white photograph of a clay seal impression showing a row of standing figures in fringed robes, one carrying a vessel and another an animal, with columns of cuneiform signs at each end"}},
{"id":"wh-198","num":198,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because their kings replaced the old habit of naming each year after some great event, dating by a ruler's regnal years is one lasting thing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Babylon.","questions":["The carved boundary stone called a kudurru, which put a royal grant of land under the protection of the gods, first appears under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and is what they are best known for.","The conquest of Babylonia was gradual rather than sudden, since the Sea-Country kings in the far south went on ruling independently after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had taken the north."],"answer":"Kassites","answerText":"Kassites","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kassite Nippur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Period ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Kassites</b> were the people whose kings ruled Babylonia through much of the later 2nd millennium BCE, in a period that ends about 1150.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their conquest was not one blow: it succeeded quickly over much of the country and slowly in the south, where the Sea-Country kings kept their independence, and only after further fighting did the administration centre on Babylon again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They ruled as a caste rather than as a migration, keeping their own names while gradually taking on the civilisation of Babylon, which their coming left largely as it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much about them is still not known, and the administrative tablets dug out of their levels at Nippur are among the few things that speak directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their most durable gift was a way of counting time: in place of the old system, under which each year was known by an elaborate title taken from some great event, they dated by the years of the king's reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their capital was Dur-Kurigalzu near Babylon, where copper oxhide ingots of the kind that circulated all over the Bronze Age Mediterranean have been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Nippur they put up a building resembling their temple of Ningal at Ur, and a Kassite ration document survives dated by the reign of King Kurigalzu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A seal impression from those levels carries stars and lozenges, elements taken as characteristically Kassite, along with foxes, which are not common on seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What they are best remembered for is the <i>kudurru</i>, a conical block of stone engraved with a royal grant of land and guarded by divine emblems and a string of curses; the real title-deed was a sealed clay tablet, and cutting the stone was optional, adding the gods' protection to the owner's rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dynasty's fall is tied to Elamite aggression, and the monuments carried off as spoil lay in the mounds of Susa until a French expedition brought them up again: the stele of Naram-Sin, Hammurabi's laws and a series of Kassite kudurrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["L. W. King, <i>A History of Babylon: From the Foundation of the Monarchy to the Persian Conquest</i> (London: Chatto and Windus, 1915), 214–58, https://archive.org/details/historyofbabylon00kinguoft. [Open access]","McGuire Gibson, “Nippur 1972–1973,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 1 (1973), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/nippur-1972-1973/. [Open access]","McGuire Gibson, “The Twelfth Season at Nippur,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 4 (1974), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-twelfth-season-at-nippur/. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, “Archaeometry and Shipwrecks,” <i>Expedition</i> 53, no. 1 (2011), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/archaeometry-and-shipwrecks/. [Open access]","A. John Gwinnett and Leonard Gorelick, “Ancient Lapidary,” <i>Expedition</i> 22, no. 1 (1979), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/ancient-lapidary/. [Open access]","Ann Guinan, Gary Oller, and Dorothy Ormsby, “Nippur Rebaked: The Conservation of Cuneiform Tablets,” <i>Expedition</i> 18, no. 3 (1976), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/nippur-rebaked/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Kudurru_Melishipak_Louvre_Sb23.jpg","title":"The kudurru of Melishipak","desc":"The upper part of a Kassite boundary stone recording a royal land grant, carrying the star, crescent and sun-disc of Ishtar, Sin and Shamash above a seated deity receiving the king and his daughter. Excavated at Susa by Jacques de Morgan; Louvre Sb 23. Jastrow, released into the. public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kudurru_Melishipak_Louvre_Sb23.jpg","alt":"The rounded top of a dark stone stele carved in relief: an eight-pointed star, a crescent and a rayed sun-disc in a row, and below them a deity seated on a throne facing a standing bearded king who raises one hand and leads a smaller figure by the wrist"}},
{"id":"wh-199","num":199,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Although the name comes from a word for highland, and the Babylonians used it for the country east of them, the chief city of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood out in the plain.","questions":["The accounting tablets from Susa are the earliest writing of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and they stay undeciphered because number signs and logograms give no idea at all of the language behind them.","Rulers titled kings of Anshan and Susa built the empire that let <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> compete with Assyria and Babylonia, reaching its greatest political and military successes between about 1300 and 1100 BCE."],"answer":"Elam","answerText":"Elam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proto-Elamite</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2900 – 2600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Middle Elamite</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Susa destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Elam</b> was the civilisation of what is now south-western Iran; its name comes from a word for highland: the Sumerians wrote of the country NIM and the Babylonians of <i>elamtu</i>, the land east and north-east of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet Susa, its chief city, stood out in the plain, miles from the mountains behind it, on rich fields that helped keep it settled without a break from the 4th millennium BCE to the 12th or 13th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Proto-Elamite, an accounting script, is known from over 1,400 tablets and fragments at Susa and from Tepe Sialk and Tall-i-Ghazir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seal impressions date them to about 2900 to 2600 BCE, they are read from right to left and top to bottom, and their figures are decimal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are still undeciphered, because they seem to be written with number signs and logograms alone, which stand for words rather than sounds, so nothing can be recovered of the language behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Elamite language, written later in Mesopotamian cuneiform, remains largely enigmatic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elamite rule runs from the end of the 3rd millennium BCE to about 640 BCE, when Ashurbanipal's army destroyed Susa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a long stretch rank was a ladder of offices, not a single crown, and the business documents of Susa appear to record the climb: the <i>sukkal</i> of the city rose to <i>sukkal</i> of Elam and Simash, then to <i>sukkalmah</i> at the top, while the bare title of king counted for little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between about 1300 and 1100 BCE a line titled kings of Anshan and Susa built the Middle Elamite empire, when Elam competed for power with Assyria and Babylonia and achieved the greatest political and military successes of its long history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Anshan is Tall-i Malyan in Fars, nearly 200 hectares (500 acres) of mounds 46 kilometres (29 miles) north of Shiraz, where inscribed bricks bore out the identification; and one of them, Untash-Napirisha, replaced his first ziggurat at Chogha Zanbil, his new lowland city, with the largest ever built in the ancient Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Edith Porada, “Aspects of Elamite Art and Archaeology,” <i>Expedition</i> 13, no. 3-4 (1971), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/aspects-of-elamite-art-and-archaeology/. [Open access]","Christopher L. Hamlin, “A Proto-Elamite Account Tablet from Susa,” <i>Expedition</i> 10, no. 4 (1968), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-proto-elamite-account-tablet-from-susa/. [Open access]","George G. Cameron, <i>History of Early Iran</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1936), 71–72, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.183377. [Open access]","Elizabeth Carter and Matthew W. Stolper, “Middle Elamite Malayan,” <i>Expedition</i> 18, no. 2 (1976), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/middle-elamite-malayan/. [Open access]","Philip Jones, “An Elamite Inscribed Brick,” <i>Expedition</i> 56, no. 3 (2014), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/an-elamite-inscribed-brick/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Zigorat_Chogha_Zanbil_Temple.jpg/1280px-Zigorat_Chogha_Zanbil_Temple.jpg","title":"The ziggurat at Chogha Zanbil","desc":"The face and central staircase of the ziggurat Untash-Napirisha raised at his new Elamite city, in what is now Khuzestan in south-western Iran; the outer skin is of baked brick over a mud-brick core. Atsed, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zigorat_Chogha_Zanbil_Temple.jpg","alt":"A broad wall of pale mud brick rising in stepped terraces under a clear sky, with a wide flight of brick steps climbing to a narrow doorway at its centre and a paved court in the foreground"}},
{"id":"wh-200","num":200,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Letters 17 to 30 of the Amarna archive are the surviving half of a correspondence between Egypt and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose kings addressed the pharaoh as their brother.","questions":["A vassal treaty found at Hattusa put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Hittite lordship, and the gods called to witness it include four whose names are Indo-European.","The ruling house of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was of Indo-European descent, which shows in royal names like Artatama and Tushratta, in a horse-breeding vocabulary, and in a word for its military aristocracy."],"answer":"Mitanni","answerText":"Mitanni","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Power grows</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amarna Age</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suppiluliuma I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1355 – 1320 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mitanni</b> was the kingdom of northern Mesopotamia and Syria that was amassing power by about 1500 BCE and stood with Assyria, Babylon, Egypt and Hatti at the centre of the civilised world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the centuries before it rose, Hurrian names had already spread across the whole northern arc from the Syrian coast to the borders of Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tell al-Rimah in the Sinjar may have lain in a political no-man's-land as the fortunes of Assyria and Mitanni to either side waxed and waned, and its cylinder seals of that date are mostly Mitannian and Syrian in style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A hoard of frit from the same site includes a green-glazed cosmetic capsule imitating a Mitannian seal design, two stags either side of a palmette tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ruling class was of Indo-European descent, which shows in royal names such as Artatama and Tushratta, in the horse-breeding terminology of its texts, and in the word <i>mairya-</i> used of its military aristocracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> By the Amarna Age, roughly 1400 to 1300 BCE, it had consolidated itself and was taking northern Syria, while Assyria broke free of its rule and made itself a great power in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its letters are numbers 17 to 30 of the archive found at Tell el-Amarna, which survives almost entirely as what Egypt received, not what it sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In it the great kings call one another brother where a vassal writes to my lord, my sun, and Akkadian is the language of negotiation, though letter 24 is Hurrian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tushratta addresses the pharaoh as a son-in-law he loves and signs himself the father-in-law who loves him; the commonest complaint in the archive is Egyptian gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A vassal treaty brought the kingdom under Hittite lordship, Suppiluliuma I recognising Shattiwaza as its future king and Shattiwaza recognising him as his lord; of the gods called to witness it four names are Indo-European, the oldest such list known, and one long read as the god Mitra may instead be a class of treaty gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Theresa Howard Carter, “Early Assyrians in the Sinjar,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/early-assyrians-in-the-sinjar/. [Open access]","Priscila Scoville, “The ancient Near East in contact: an introduction to the Egypt-Mitanni affairs in the Amarna Letters,” <i>Estudos Internacionais</i> 6, no. 2 (2018): 65–78, https://doi.org/10.5752/P.2317-773X.2018v6n2p65. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, “The Hittites and the Aegean World,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 2 (1974), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-hittites-and-the-aegean-world/. [Open access]","David Crownover, “Some Frit from Northern Mesopotamia,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/some-frit-from-northern-mesopotamia/. [Open access]","Jaan Lahe and Vladimir Sazonov, “Mitra esmamainimine Hetiidi kuninga Šuppiluliuma I ja Mitanni kuninga Šattiwaza lepingus?,” <i>Mäetagused</i> 73 (2019): 5–14, https://doi.org/10.7592/MT2019.73.lahe_sazonov. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amarna_letter._Letter_from_Tushratta_king_of_Mitanni_to_Amenhotep_III._From_Tell_el-Amarna%2C_Egypt._1st_half_of_the_14th_century_BCE._British_Museum.jpg/1280px-Amarna_letter._Letter_from_Tushratta_king_of_Mitanni_to_Amenhotep_III._From_Tell_el-Amarna%2C_Egypt._1st_half_of_the_14th_century_BCE._British_Museum.jpg","title":"A letter from Tushratta","desc":"Tushratta of Mitanni writes to Amenhotep III of Egypt, negotiating a diplomatic marriage and asking for much gold as a bride-price; from Tell el-Amarna, first half of the 14th century BCE, in the British Museum. Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amarna_letter._Letter_from_Tushratta_king_of_Mitanni_to_Amenhotep_III._From_Tell_el-Amarna,_Egypt._1st_half_of_the_14th_century_BCE._British_Museum.jpg","alt":"A thick rectangular clay tablet standing upright in a museum mount, its face covered edge to edge with close-packed lines of cuneiform divided by ruled horizontal bands"}},
{"id":"rm-041","num":"41","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy wrote that the two seas around Italy carry Etruscan names, the Tuscan and the Adriatic, a memory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Roman readers took for granted.","questions":["A wreck off Giens went down about 500 BCE with a thousand Etruscan wine amphorae in six lashed layers, showing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as cargo rather than as the reputation Greek writers gave it.","Hieron of Syracuse sent triremes to Cumae in 474 BCE and humbled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a great sea fight, after which the Tyrrhenian passed steadily into other hands."],"answer":"Etruscan sea power","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Alalia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 – 535 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gravisca texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 520 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grand Ribaud F</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 475 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cumae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">474 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan sea power</b> is the shipping, trade and naval force the cities of Etruria exercised over the Tyrrhenian and the Adriatic in the archaic centuries, and the reputation that came with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy took it for granted that before Rome's supremacy Etruscan power reached widely by sea and land, and that Italy's two seas bore Etruscan names, the Tuscan and the Adriatic after the colony of Atria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo supplied the darker half: the Tyrrheni had been strong under one ruler and took to the sea as pirates only once that union dissolved, though acting together they could make long expeditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One fleet action is securely attested, the ships of Caere fighting beside the Carthaginians against Phocaean Greeks off Alalia in Corsica between 540 and 535 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Diodorus still calls the Tyrrhenians masters of the sea in 474 BCE, in the sentence describing the triremes Hieron of Syracuse sent to Cumae, which humbled them in a great sea fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> How much of that tradition holds is open, since the piracy was a reputation spread and orchestrated by writers in Greek, and whether the ships amounted to a thalassocracy is asked rather than settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ports are not in doubt: Pyrgi and Gravisca served Caere and Tarquinia on the Tyrrhenian coast, Spina and Adria stood on the Adriatic, and Genoa held a resident Etruscan-speaking community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The sanctuary at Gravisca has yielded 119 Greek inscriptions, most from about 550 to 520 BCE, one of them a dedication to Apollo cut into a stone anchor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A century before Marseille was founded, Etruscan merchants were already working the coasts of Provence and Languedoc, and it is to them that the arrival of wine in Gaul is credited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One cargo shows the scale: the wreck at Grand Ribaud off Giens went down about 500 to 475 BCE with about a thousand Etruscan wine amphorae lashed in five or six layers, some 40 tonnes (44 tons), though whether Etruscans or Greeks from Marseille sailed it is argued still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan sea power","tags":["concept","history","archaeology","italy","etruscan","iron age","warfare"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.2.2, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5B*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.51, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 4 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/11C*.html. [Open access]","Jean-René Jannot, \"Les navires étrusques, instruments d'une thalassocratie ?,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 139, no. 3 (1995): 743–78, https://www.persee.fr/doc/crai_0065-0536_1995_num_139_3_15514. [Open access]","Stéphane Bourdin, \"Fréquentation ou intégration : les présences allogènes dans les emporia étrusques et ligures (VIe–IVe siècles av. J.-C.),\" in <i>Espaces d'échanges en Méditerranée : Antiquité et Moyen Âge</i>, ed. François Clément, John Tolan and Jérôme Wilgaux (Rennes: Presses universitaires de Rennes, 2006), 19–39, https://books.openedition.org/pur/7832. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC8546786. [Open access]","Luc Long, Lucien-François Gantés and Michel Rival, \"L'épave Grand Ribaud F : un chargement de produits étrusques du début du Ve siècle avant J.-C.,\" in <i>Gli Etruschi da Genova ad Ampurias: Atti del XXIV Convegno di Studi Etruschi ed Italici, Marseille–Lattes, 26 settembre – 1 ottobre 2002</i>, vol. 2 (Pisa: Istituti editoriali e poligrafici internazionali, 2006), 455–95, https://www.studietruschi.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/ATTISE24_28_LONG-ET-ALII.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Etruscan_Ship.JPG/1920px-Etruscan_Ship.JPG","title":"Etruscan sea power","desc":"Tomb of the Ship, Tarquinia (detail). Chiar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_Ship.JPG","alt":"Painted ships with masts, rigging and dark hulls on a wall of the Tomb of the Ship at Tarquinia, about 450 BCE"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-042","num":"42","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Herodotus says the Phocaeans won the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yet lost forty of their sixty ships, a Cadmean victory that cost them Corsica and sent them to found Velia instead.","questions":["The men of Agylla stoned their share of the prisoners taken at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and were told by the Pythia to honour the dead with rites and horse races.","An archaic temple of Athena at Velia has given up two helmets that its excavators read as spoils dedicated after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of them of Italic Negau type."],"answer":"Battle of Alalia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 541 – 535 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Velia founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athena temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">540 – 530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Etruscan Aléria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 540 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Alalia</b> is the sea fight of about 540 BCE in which a Phocaean fleet based on Corsica met an allied Etruscan and Carthaginian one, and Caere’s part in it is among the best attested facts of archaic Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells it: Phocaeans fleeing the Persian conquest of their city sailed for Cyrnus, where at an oracle’s command they had founded Alalia twenty years earlier, and stayed five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They plundered all their neighbours, so the Tyrrhenians and Carthaginians made common cause and came at them with sixty ships each, against the Phocaeans’ sixty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the sea he calls Sardonian the Phocaeans won, yet it was a Cadmean victory: forty ships lost, and the twenty left useless with their rams twisted awry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So they took their families off Alalia and sailed to Rhegium, and later founded Hyele, now Velia, the Cyrnus the oracle meant being a hero and not the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The captured crews were divided by lot and the men of Agylla — Caere — stoned theirs; when everything passing the spot was crippled in turn, the Pythia told them to honour the dead with rites and horse races, kept up in Herodotus’s day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He never calls the battle Alalia, which is the colony’s name rather than the fight’s, and the place is unknown; the tradition rests on seven passages, not on his account alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle later cites the Etruscans and Carthaginians as peoples bound to one another by commercial agreements, the footing such an alliance stood on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Corsica passed to Etruria: the burial ground at Aléria, in use from the late 6th century BCE, is the only necropolis of Etruscan character recorded in France, its Etruscan occupation put at about 540 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Velia a temple of Athena of 540–530 BCE has given up painted pottery, vases inscribed ‘sacred’ and arms and armour, among them a Chalcidian helmet and one of Italic Negau type, which the excavators take for offerings after the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of Alalia","tags":["event","history","italy","etruscan","iron age","warfare","greece"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.166–67, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D166. [Open access]","Michel Gras, \"Marseille, la bataille d'Alalia et Delphes,\" <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 13 (1987): 161–81, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_1987_num_13_1_1755. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC8546786. [Open access]","Federica Sacchetti et al., \"Aléria – Les nécropoles : analyse spatiale d'une occupation diachronique,\" <i>ADLFI. Archéologie de la France – Informations</i>, Corse (2024), https://journals.openedition.org/adlfi/155947. [Open access]","Ministero della Cultura, \"MiC, archeologia: rinvenuti i resti del tempio arcaico di Atena sull'acropoli di Velia,\" comunicato stampa, 1 February 2022, https://cultura.gov.it/comunicato/22137. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 3.1280a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1944), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D3%3Asection%3D1280a. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/Battle_of_Alalia_%28Hutchinson%27s_story_of_the_nations%29.jpg","title":"Battle of Alalia","desc":"The battle as imagined by Ambrose Dudley for <i>Hutchinson's Story of the Nations</i> in 1915; no ancient depiction of it survives. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Alalia_(Hutchinson%27s_story_of_the_nations).jpg","alt":"A 1915 painting of the sea fight off Alalia: a beaked galley grappled against a larger ship, its decks crowded with armed men"},"difficulty":4},
{"id":"rm-043","num":"43","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Strabo says the Greeks grew so strong that they gave the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to this part of Italy together with Sicily, though by his own day nearly all of it had been barbarised.","questions":["Polybius calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Greater Hellas, and says that when the club-houses of the Pythagoreans were burnt the leading men of every city there perished at once.","Settlement in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened at Pithekoussai in the mid-8th century BCE and closed with the Panhellenic foundation of Thurii in 444 to 443 BCE, some three centuries later."],"answer":"Magna Graecia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 443 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taras leads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 385 – 355 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Magna Graecia</b>, in Greek <i>Megale Hellas</i>, is the name for the Greek cities planted along the coasts of southern Italy from the middle of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Settlement opened at Pithekoussai on Ischia, then Cumae, and a flood followed: Sybaris and Croton from Achaia at the end of the century, Metapontion and Poseidonia from Sybaris, Elea from Phocaea in 540 BCE, and last the Panhellenic foundation of Thurii in 444 to 443 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once they prospered the settlers raised some of the largest temples in the Greek world, in a country whose own peoples were often unfriendly and pressing outwards themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo says the Greeks grew so strong that they gave this part of Italy, together with Sicily, the name Magna Graecia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius calls it Greater Hellas and shows a working political world: when the club-houses of the Pythagoreans were burnt the leading men of every city perished at a stroke and sedition ran through all the Greek towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It was the Achaeans who were trusted to settle it, and Croton, Sybaris and Caulonia then made a league with a common temple of Zeus Amarius and took the laws of Achaia, until Dionysius of Syracuse and their neighbours forced them to give it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A later league was led by Taras under Archytas, who is said to have been elected general seven times between the mid-380s and the mid-350s BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name itself was elastic: it once took in the whole Greek world, and shrank until in the Roman period it meant the Greek-inhabited parts of Italy from Cumae to Tarentum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How far its cities felt themselves one region is doubtful, and local quarrels and Italian neighbours weighed more with them than Rome until Roman consolidation of the south after 320 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strabo’s verdict is the end of it: by his day everything but Taras, Rhegium and Neapolis had been barbarised, held by Lucanians, Bruttians and Campanians who were Campanian in name and Roman in fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Magna Graecia","tags":["place","region","history","greece","italy","geography","migration"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 6.1.2, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 3 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/6A*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.39, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","William Owens, review of <i>Rome and the Western Greeks, 350 BC–AD 200: Conquest and Acculturation in Southern Italy</i>, by Kathryn Lomas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.03.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.03.12/. [Open access]","John Dillon, review of <i>Archytas of Tarentum: Pythagorean, Philosopher and Mathematician King</i>, by Carl A. Huffman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.03.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.34/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG/1920px-Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG","title":"Magna Graecia","desc":"The Temple of Concordia at Agrigento, the Greek Akragas, which the ancient name took in along with the rest of Sicily. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG","alt":"A Doric temple of honey-coloured stone standing complete on a ridge, its columns and pediment intact"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"rm-044","num":"44","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy says the Aricians broke at the first charge until cohorts from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wheeled on the Etruscan rear, and its tyrant later sheltered Tarquinius Superbus until his death.","questions":["Strabo calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the oldest of all the Sicilian and Italiote cities, founded by men who agreed it should count as a colony of Chalcis while bearing another town's name.","Tyrrhenians, Umbrians and Daunians marched on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 524 BCE and were beaten, a defeat that helped erode the Etruscan hold on southern Italy."],"answer":"Cumae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 720 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attack repulsed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">524 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aricia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 504 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tarquin dies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">495 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cumae</b> is the oldest Greek city on the Italian mainland, in the ground no later than 730 to 720 BCE and settled from Pithekoussai, the island off the Bay of Naples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Strabo calls it the oldest of all the Sicilian and Italiote cities, led out by Hippocles of Cumae and Megasthenes of Chalcis, who agreed it should be a colony of Chalcis under the other's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius gives a different pair of founders, Eretrians and Chalcidians, and a city celebrated throughout Italy for its wealth, holding the best of the Campanian plain and the havens around Misenum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Sibyl prophesied there, and a long gallery cut through the tuff below the acropolis has long been shown as her grotto, though as it stands it began as a covered way for troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Rome's share in that oracle is the Sibylline Books, which Dionysius says a foreign woman sold to Tarquinius, three of nine after she had burnt the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 524 BCE Tyrrhenians, Umbrians and Daunians marched on the city envying its prosperity and were beaten, a defeat which with the sea fight off the same coast in 474 BCE eroded the Etruscan presence in southern Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Twenty years later the Aricians begged help against Aruns, son of Porsena, and Livy says the Cuman cohorts let the Etruscans press past them in pursuit, wheeled on their rear and cut them to pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius names the commander Aristodemus, sent out with the poorest citizens in ten unseaworthy ships by aristocrats who hoped he would die, and who killed the Tyrrhenian general himself and came home to seize the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tarquinius Superbus died there in 495 BCE, having gone to that tyrant once the Latins were broken, and Roman envoys buying corn in a famine lost their money and pack animals to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Campanians afterwards became masters of the place and used its people badly, yet Strabo still found many traces of Greek decorum and usage preserved there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cumae","tags":["city","history","italy","greece","iron age","religion","warfare"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.4.4, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5D*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 7.2–12, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 4 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1943), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/7A*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.62, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.21, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC8546786. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Cuma_-_Antro_della_Sibilla.JPG/1920px-Cuma_-_Antro_della_Sibilla.JPG","title":"Cumae","desc":"The approach to the Antro della Sibilla, Parco archeologico di Cuma. DanieleDaniele1, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuma_-_Antro_della_Sibilla.JPG","alt":"A deep passage cut straight through pale tuff rock at Cumae, ending at the dark trapezoidal mouth of the Sibyl's gallery"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Cumae","at":[14.0536,40.8486]}},
{"id":"rm-045","num":"45","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy's twelve lictors, the curule chair and the toga praetexta are the plainest signs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the number twelve coming from the custom of twelve sovereign cities.","questions":["Workmen summoned from all Etruria to finish the Capitoline temple, and soothsayers called in to read public prodigies, are cases of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Livy states outright.","The tradition that Roman boys once learnt Etruscan literature as they later learnt Greek is the boldest claim made for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Livy half doubts it himself."],"answer":"Etruscan influence on Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Veii stormed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">396 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caere absorbed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Etruria and Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 – 89 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan influence on Rome</b> is the debt the Romans acknowledged to their northern neighbours in religion, insignia, building and letters, together with the question of how deep it really went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Etruria began just across the Tiber, its heartland roughly between Rome and Florence, so the two were neighbours long before either was a power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome's own tradition brought its Tarquin kings from Tarquinii, the first of them a rich man who left because, as the son of a Corinthian refugee, he could rise no higher among Etruscans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Livy derives the twelve lictors, the curule chair and the toga praetexta from the Etruscans, and the number twelve from the custom by which their twelve sovereign cities each gave one lictor to a jointly elected king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tarquinius Superbus sent for workmen from every part of Etruria to finish the temple of Jupiter on the Capitol, and it was Etruscan soothsayers whom Rome employed to read prodigies affecting the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Latin alphabet itself was taken over from the Greek through Etruscan, which is why its 23 letters run in much the same order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy also reports, and half doubts, a tradition that Roman boys were once taught Etruscan literature as they were taught Greek in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How far all this made Rome an Etruscan city is disputed,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the Roman picture of these neighbours is itself suspect, since Greek and Roman writers dwell on their wealth and their decadence, distortions aimed less at Etruscan life than at their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The traffic then reversed: Rome stormed Veii in 396 BCE, and Caere was absorbed to become the first people remembered as Roman citizens without the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Etruria was taken piece by piece thereafter, by roads, colonies and alliances with its ruling families,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and its sanctuaries went on being used through the expansion that ended its independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan influence on Rome","tags":["concept","history","italy","etruscan","iron age","religion","language"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Winfred P. Lehmann and Jonathan Slocum, \"Latin Online: Series Introduction,\" Linguistics Research Center, University of Texas at Austin, https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/eieol/latol. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Valeria Riedemann Lorca, review of <i>A Companion to the Etruscans</i>, ed. Sinclair Bell and Alexandra A. Carpino, Blackwell Companions to the Ancient World, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.04.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.04.41/. [Open access]","Michel Humbert, \"L'incorporation de Caere dans la civitas Romana,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome</i> 84, no. 1 (1972): 231–68, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.1972.923. [Open access]","Francesca Boitani and Iefke van Kampen, \"Veio,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 21 (2012): 553–88, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2012_num_21_1_4754. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg/1920px-Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg","title":"Etruscan influence on Rome","desc":"Relief from Iulia Concordia showing three lictors with their fasces, Museo Nazionale Concordiese, Portogruaro. Following Hadrian, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors,_National_Museum_of_Concordia,_Portogruaro,_Italy.jpg","alt":"A limestone relief of three men in togas walking abreast, each carrying a bundle of rods over one shoulder"},"difficulty":3},
{"id":"rm-046","num":"46","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy counted 244 years from the founding of the city to its liberation, the whole span of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended when two consuls replaced its last king.","questions":["After the last king went, a priest called the king for sacrifices took over the religious duties of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and was placed under the Pontifex Maximus.","Romans did not begin writing their own history until about 200 BCE, so the whole account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a reconstruction made centuries after it ended."],"answer":"Roman Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditionally</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 – 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regal years</span><span class=\"dt-v\">244, by Livy's count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Forum levelled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Roman Kingdom</b> is the period Rome put at the start of its own history, running from the founding of the city to the expulsion of its last king, which Livy reckoned at 244 years in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The throne was remembered as elective rather than hereditary: when Romulus died the hundred senators held power five days each in rotation, a year they called the interregnum, after which the people chose a king and the senate ratified the choice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Romulus is credited with those first hundred senators, the Patres whose descendants were the patricians,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and Servius Tullius with grading the citizens by property into classes and centuries, the first class alone counting eighty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy has the last king reign 25 years and be driven out, after which two consuls were elected in the very assembly of centuries Servius was said to have made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the kings had done in religion passed to a king for sacrifices, placed under the Pontifex Maximus so that he should be king in nothing but name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> How much of that is history is a real question, since Romans did not begin writing their own until about 200 BCE and the regal narrative mixes fact and fiction to an uncertain degree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On that reading the tradition grows gradually more reliable only from the middle of the 4th century BCE, long after the last king is supposed to have gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Excavation gives the same centuries another shape: the marshy Forum valley was filled and levelled in the 7th century BCE, the first move to bind the hills around a common space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> From about that time come the first Regia, the huts of the burial ground beside it, and the monumental cult building by the river at Sant’Omobono.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The temple of Jupiter on the Capitol went up at the end of the period, on a scale that invites comparison with the great sanctuaries of Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Roman Kingdom","tags":["era","history","italy","politics","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.17, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Filippo De Rossi, review of <i>A Critical History of Early Rome: From Prehistory to the First Punic War</i>, by Gary Forsythe, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.08.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.08.40/. [Open access]","Dominik Maschek, review of <i>The Genesis of Roman Architecture</i>, by John North Hopkins, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.10.30/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg/1920px-C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg","title":"Roman Kingdom","desc":"Silver denarius of C. Marcius Censorinus, its obverse showing the jugate heads of Numa Pompilius and Ancus Marcius. Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg","alt":"Both faces of a worn silver coin: two overlapping bearded heads facing right, and a rider standing across two galloping horses"},"difficulty":2},
{"id":"rm-048","num":"48","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Strabo has the first founders wall three of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Ancus take in two more, and Servius add the last two behind a trench, a mound and a wall.","questions":["Livy puts the earliest Romans on one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Sabines with their citadel on another, the Albans on a third, and newcomers on a fourth.","Rome kept a festival called the Septimontium whose own seven places were not the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a sign that the familiar list came later than the ground."],"answer":"Seven Hills of Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Forum levelled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Seven and wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 525 BCE, on one reading</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Seven Hills of Rome</b> are the Palatine, Capitoline, Aventine, Caelian, Esquiline, Viminal and Quirinal, the high ground the city grew across on the left bank of the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has them taken up by different people: the earliest Romans on the Palatine, the Sabines on the Capitoline with its citadel, the Albans on the Caelian, and the Aventine handed to newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancus is said to have joined the Aventine to the Palatine by settling the ground round the altar of Venus Murcia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also brought the Janiculum across the river inside the city's bounds, not because the room was wanted but to keep an enemy off so strong a position, and reached it by the first bridge thrown over the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Servius added the Quirinal and the Viminal, then the Esquiline, where he lived himself to give the place importance, and ringed the whole with mound, moat and wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo describes that work: a deep trench, its earth heaped on the inner side, a mound some six stadia long, and a wall with towers running from the Colline Gate to the Esquiline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What Servius is said to have extended was the pomerium, the strip the Etruscans consecrated by augury and marked with boundary stones when they founded a city, rather than the wall alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The census that prompted the enlargement counted 80,000 men who could bear arms, on the authority of the oldest Roman historian Livy knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The seven as a fixed list came later than the ground itself, and Rome kept a festival called the Septimontium whose own seven were places rather than these hills, on a list handed down under the name of Antistius Labeo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One reconstruction puts the traditional seven and their wall in the third quarter of the 6th century BCE and reads that festival as the memory of it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while the marshy Forum valley between the hills had been filled and levelled a century earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Seven Hills of Rome","tags":["place","geography","history","italy","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.3.7, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5C*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Linda Zollschan, review of <i>From “Roma quadrata” to “la grande Roma dei Tarquini”: A Study of the Literary Tradition on Rome's Territorial Growth under the Kings</i>, by Adam Ziółkowski, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.04.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.04.36/. [Open access]","Dominik Maschek, review of <i>The Genesis of Roman Architecture</i>, by John North Hopkins, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.10.30/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"The seven hills of Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"kind":"region","within":"Rome","area":[[12.474,41.884],[12.478,41.897],[12.487,41.906],[12.503,41.904],[12.508,41.893],[12.504,41.88],[12.491,41.876],[12.479,41.878]]}},
{"id":"rm-049","num":"49","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy has Romulus take the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as his station for the augury while Remus took the Aventine, and fortify it first of all once he was sole ruler.","questions":["The Lupercalia was run on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose name Livy traces to an Arcadian city called Pallantium and whose cliffs and marshy valleys made it a stronghold.","Because the emperors lived on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its Latin name passed first to their own residence and afterwards to any palace at all."],"answer":"Palatine Hill","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wall remains</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">perhaps 378 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Palatine Hill</b> is the centremost of Rome's seven, an irregular quadrilateral about 2 kilometres (1.2 miles) round whose highest point stands 43 metres (141 feet) above the level of the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is flat-topped, with two summits called the Palatium and the Cermalus, ringed by cliffs far more formidable than they now look and almost surrounded by marshy valleys traversed by winding streams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only the narrow ridge of the Velia joined it to the Esquiline, so it was a position of great natural strength commanding the one permanent crossing of the lower Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient tradition is unanimous in putting the earliest nucleus of Rome here, and modern scholars have generally agreed with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy has Romulus take the hill as his station for the augury while Remus took the Aventine, and says his first work as sole ruler was to fortify the Palatine, where he had been brought up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy also reports that the hill was first called Pallantium, after a city of that name in Arcadia, and that the name afterwards became Palatium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Lupercalia was celebrated there, a festival he traces to the Arcadian Evander, in which young men ran about naked in honour of the Lycaean Pan, whom the Romans called Inuus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo counts the Palatine among the three hills the first founders walled, though the surviving defences are later: a 6th-century BCE stretch at the north-west corner, and one on the west and south known as the wall of Romulus, perhaps a fort of 378 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the hill came into favour it filled with the houses of the aristocracy, the oldest on record built in 330 BCE, and every sanctuary on it that can be placed belongs to the later republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Augustus lived on it and his successors built palaces across it, and because they did the hill's own name passed first to the imperial residence and then to any palace at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Palatine Hill","tags":["place","geography","history","italy","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.5, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Palatinus,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 374–80, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Palatinus.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.3.7, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5C*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Circus_Maximus_et_Domus_Augustana_-_Rome_111001.JPG/1280px-Circus_Maximus_et_Domus_Augustana_-_Rome_111001.JPG","title":"Palatine Hill","desc":"Central part of the Circus Maximus and ruins of the Domus Augustana on Palatine Hill. Jean-Pol GRANDMONT, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Circus_Maximus_et_Domus_Augustana_-_Rome_111001.JPG","alt":"A long grassed hollow in the foreground and a hill rising behind it, its crest carrying tiers of ruined brick arches and vaults"},"locator":{"name":"Palatine Hill","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-050","num":"50","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy says a human head came to light in the foundations of Jupiter's temple on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the soothsayers read as portending the head of all the world.","questions":["Geese sacred to Juno are said to have saved the citadel on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when the Gauls climbed its cliff by night without waking the sentries or the dogs.","Smallest of Rome's hills, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried the citadel on one summit and the temple of Jupiter, Juno and Minerva on the other, with the Asylum in the dip between."],"answer":"Capitoline Hill","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traditionally 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gauls on the Arx</span><span class=\"dt-v\">390 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Capitoline Hill</b> is the smallest of Rome's hills, some 460 metres (500 yards) long and 180 metres (200 yards) wide on average, lying between the Forum and the Campus Martius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Steep cliffs ring it on every side but the south-east, where it was reached from the forum valley, and it falls into three parts: a summit at either end and a dip between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern summit held the Arx, the citadel; on the southern, Tarquin established the worship of Jupiter, Juno and Minerva, which marked that point as the religious centre of the community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That dip was called the Asylum, on the story that Romulus received there the refugees of other towns, and the hill's official name was <i>Arx et Capitolium</i>, for its double nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only later did Capitolium spread to the whole hill, and the word could also stand for the temple of Jupiter alone, as its most significant part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Livy reports that while the foundations were dug a human head came to light with the face perfect, which soothsayers from the city and from Etruria read as portending that the spot would be the head of all the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roman antiquarians derived the name Capitolium from <i>caput</i>, 'head', on the strength of that story, though the hill was also called Tarpeius and Saturnius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The precipice at its south-west corner, from which those convicted of capital crimes were thrown, was the <i>saxum Tarpeium</i>, where Livy has a commander's daughter open the citadel to the Sabines and die crushed beneath their shields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same rock carries his account of the Gallic attack of 390 BCE, when the enemy climbed it at night without waking the sentries or the dogs and were betrayed by the geese sacred to Juno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The temple was dedicated in the first year after the kings were driven out, by a consul who finished the prayer with his hand on the door-post after being told his son was dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Capitoline Hill","tags":["place","geography","history","italy","religion","archaeology"],"sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Capitolinus Mons,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 95–98, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Capitolinus.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.47, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Parte_della_Rupe_Tarpea_-_Li_Monumenti_Pi%C3%BA_Celebri_Di_Roma_Antica_E_Moderna_Disegnati_Da_Vero_Ed_Incisi_Dai_Migliori_Artisti_Rome_Presso_Po_-_1850.jpg","title":"Capitoline Hill","desc":"Parte della Rupe Tarpea, from Li monumenti piú celebri di Roma antica e moderna, 1850. Pietro Datri, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parte_della_Rupe_Tarpea_-_Li_Monumenti_Pi%C3%BA_Celebri_Di_Roma_Antica_E_Moderna_Disegnati_Da_Vero_Ed_Incisi_Dai_Migliori_Artisti_Rome_Presso_Po_-_1850.jpg","alt":"An engraving of a high rock face with houses standing along its crest and two figures walking on the road below"},"locator":{"name":"Capitoline Hill","at":[12.4831,41.8933],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"gr-306","num":306,"category":"Athens","question":"Seven hundred Athenian households were banished as accursed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on a list of names handed to Cleomenes by Isagoras, who had been on the losing side of the faction-strife.","questions":["One expedition of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke up at Eleusis when the Corinthians concluded they were acting wrongly and went home, and the other king Demaratus followed them.","So little came of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Sparta afterwards summoned Hippias from Sigeum and proposed restoring him, only for Socles of Corinth to talk the allies out of it."],"answer":"Spartan intervention at Athens","answerText":"Spartan intervention at Athens","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Spartan intervention at Athens</b> was the run of expeditions Sparta sent into Attica after helping to expel the tyrants, part of the four Dorian comings Herodotus counts, twice as invaders and twice as helpers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Isagoras, on the losing side of the faction-strife with Cleisthenes, invited the aid of Cleomenes, who had been his friend since the besieging of the Peisistratidae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Cleomenes first sent a herald demanding the banishment of Cleisthenes and many others as the Accursed, at Isagoras's instruction, since the guilt was held to lie with the Alcmaeonids and not with his friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleisthenes secretly departed, and Cleomenes appeared with no great force and banished seven hundred Athenian households named for him by Isagoras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then tried to dissolve the Council and hand the offices of government to Isagoras's faction, which the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> numbers at three hundred friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> The Council resisted, Cleomenes and his partisans seized the acropolis, and the rest of the Athenians united and besieged them for two days, the Lacedaemonians leaving under truce on the third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The prisoners left behind were put to death, the Athenians brought back Cleisthenes and the seven hundred households, and envoys were sent to Sardis who gave Darius earth and water and were blamed for it at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cleomenes mustered an army from the whole Peloponnesus without declaring its purpose, which was to set Isagoras up as tyrant, and broke in as far as Eleusis while the Boeotians took Oenoe and Hysiae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It broke up before any battle when the Corinthians decided they were acting wrongly and left and Demaratus, the other king, did the same, after which Sparta forbade both kings to go out with one army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later the Lacedaemonians sent for Hippias from Sigeum and asked their allies to help restore him, but Socles of Corinth spoke against it and the allies entreated them not to harm a Greek city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.70, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D70. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.72, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.73, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D73. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.74, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D74. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.75, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D75. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.76, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D76. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.91, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D91. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.93, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D93. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 20, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-307","num":307,"category":"Athens","question":"Herodotus cannot give the lineage of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, saying only that the man's house was notable and that his kinsfolk sacrifice to Zeus of Caria.","questions":["It was on the instruction of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Cleomenes demanded the banishment of the Accursed, a charge that fell on the Alcmaeonids and spared his own friends.","Winning in the Comradeships only to see his rival take the commons into his party, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was archon in the very year the ten tribes were made."],"answer":"Isagoras","answerText":"Isagoras","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Isagoras</b> son of Tisandrus was, with Cleisthenes the Alcmaeonid, one of the two principal holders of power at Athens once the tyrants had been removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls him a man of a notable house but says he cannot give his lineage, remarking only that his kinsfolk sacrifice to Zeus of Caria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> is blunter about where he stood, calling him a friend of the tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the faction-strife that followed he had the better of it among the Comradeships, and Cleisthenes answered by enlisting the people and offering to hand the government over to the multitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once his rival had drawn in the Athenian people, which was then debarred from all rights, Herodotus says he was stronger by far than the opposing faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Isagoras was nonetheless eponymous archon in the year the reforms were carried, which the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts in the fourth year after the deposition of the tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Losing power, he devised a counter-plot and invited the aid of Cleomenes, a friend since the besieging of the Peisistratidae, of whom it was said that he regularly went to see Isagoras's wife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At his instruction the Spartan demanded the banishment of Cleisthenes and many other Athenians as the Accursed, a charge that fell on the Alcmaeonids and left his own friends untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seven hundred households were then banished on a list he supplied, and the Council was to be dissolved and the offices of government handed to him and three hundred of his partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Council resisted, the citizens besieged the acropolis for two days, and he came out under truce with Cleomenes while those left behind were put to death, the army mustered afterwards having as its undeclared purpose to set him up as tyrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.66, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D66. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.70, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D70. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.72, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.73, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D73. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.74, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D74. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 20, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-308","num":308,"category":"Athens","question":"Herodotus says that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named none of the ten new tribes after the sons of Ion, and added Aias only because the hero was a neighbour and an ally.","questions":["Demanded as one of the Accursed and secretly departing, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was sent for again by the Athenians once Cleomenes had left the acropolis under truce.","Herodotus traces the descent of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a marriage into the house of a Sicyonian tyrant, and on to a brother's daughter who bore Pericles."],"answer":"Cleisthenes of Athens","answerText":"Cleisthenes of Athens","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Cleisthenes of Athens</b> was an Alcmaeonid, son of Megacles and of Agariste, whose father was the tyrant Cleisthenes of Sicyon, and Herodotus says he was named after his mother's father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same passage calls him the man who gave the Athenians their tribes and their democracy, and traces the line on to his brother's daughter Agariste, the mother of Pericles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the tyrants were removed he and Isagoras were the two principal holders of power, and Herodotus adds that he was reputed to have bribed the Pythian priestess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Getting the worst of it in the political clubs, he took the commons into his party, offering, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says, to hand the government over to the multitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Cleomenes came at Isagoras's asking and demanded the banishment of the Accursed he secretly departed, and the Athenians sent to bring him back once the siege of the acropolis was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He divided the Athenians into ten tribes instead of four, calling none after the sons of Ion but taking names from other heroes, all native except Aias, added as a neighbour and an ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus supposes he followed the lead of his grandfather and namesake out of contempt for the Ionians, and says that once he had won the people over he was stronger by far than the rival faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> credits him with a Council of five hundred instead of four hundred, and with making men known officially by their demes rather than by their fathers' names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It says the tribal heroes were picked by the Pythian priestess from a list of a hundred, and that he enacted the law about ostracism, making the constitution much more democratic than Solon's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> With the people in control of affairs he was their leader and head of the people, and he carried the changes in the archonship of Isagoras, the fourth year after the tyrants fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.66, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D66. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.72, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.73, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D73. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.131, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D131. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 20, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-309","num":309,"category":"Athens","question":"Geleon, Aegicores, Argades and Hoples were the four the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced, and the Pythia picked the new heroes from a list of a hundred.","questions":["Pausanias saw statues of the heroes who named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and could not tell which Cecrops or which Pandion of the several kings so called was meant.","Making the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ten rather than twelve kept the old Thirds out of the reckoning, since using them would have left the multitude unmixed."],"answer":"Cleisthenic tribes","answerText":"Cleisthenic tribes","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Cleisthenic tribes</b> were the ten phylai into which Cleisthenes divided the Athenians in place of the four they had had before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus names the old four after the sons of Ion, Geleon, Aegicores, Argades and Hoples, and says the new names were taken from other heroes, all native to the country except Aias, added because he was a neighbour and an ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives the purpose as mixing the citizens up so that more might take part in the government, and traces to it the saying that one should not draw distinctions between tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It explains why he did not make twelve: that would have used the existing Thirds of the four old tribes, and the multitude would not have been mixed up at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each tribe instead drew on all three regions, the land being portioned into thirty parts, ten in the suburbs, ten on the coast and ten inland, assigned by lot three to a tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The heroes the tribes were named after were picked by the Pythian priestess from a previously chosen list of a hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias saw their statues standing near the Council Chamber and lists them as Hippothoon, Antiochus, Ajax, Leos, Erechtheus, Aegeus, Oeneus and Acamas, with Cecrops and Pandion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Of the last two he says plainly that he does not know which of the men bearing each name was the one honoured, there having been an earlier and a later Cecrops and two kings called Pandion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Herodotus counts the change in officers as well as in names, ten tribe-wardens in place of four, with ten districts assigned to each tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribe then became the unit the state was organised by, fifty men from each sitting on the Council of Five Hundred and the generals coming to be elected one from every tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.66, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D66. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.5.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D5%3Asection%3D1. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.5.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D5%3Asection%3D2. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.5.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D5%3Asection%3D3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Monument_of_the_Eponymous_Heroes.jpg/1920px-Monument_of_the_Eponymous_Heroes.jpg","title":"Cleisthenic tribes","alt":"A long low stone base with a row of upright posts along its edge, standing in an archaeological park among olive trees","desc":"Monument of the Eponymous Heroes on the Agora in Athens. Викидим, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_of_the_Eponymous_Heroes.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-310","num":310,"category":"Athens","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made the official designation of an Athenian so that nobody should call attention to the citizens newly enfranchised by Cleisthenes.","questions":["Registered on the roll of his <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at eighteen, an Athenian was voted on by its members on oath, and could be sold by the state if he lost his appeal.","Some took their names from their localities and others from their founders, since a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> no longer always corresponded to the place it was named for."],"answer":"deme","answerText":"deme","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"A <b>deme</b> was a local unit of the Athenian countryside, and Herodotus says Cleisthenes assigned ten districts to each of his ten tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he named some of them from their localities and others from their founders, the demes no longer all corresponding to the places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He made all the inhabitants of each one fellow-demesmen, so that men should be designated officially by their demes rather than by their fathers' names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The purpose given is that nobody should call attention to the newly enfranchised citizens, and the same work adds that Athenians came to use their demes as surnames in private life as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Demarchs were appointed with the duties the ship-commissioners had had, the demes taking the place of the ship-commissions, while the clans, brotherhoods and priesthoods of each were left on the ancestral plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Thucydides describes the Athenians as having long lived scattered over Attica in independent townships, and says most of them still kept house in the country when the Peloponnesian War began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their trouble at abandoning those houses and the hereditary temples was deep, each man feeling that he was bidding farewell to his own native city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the developed constitution the deme was where a man became a citizen, those of citizen parentage on both sides being registered on its roll at eighteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The demesmen voted on oath whether he had reached the lawful age and was a freeman of legitimate birth, and one who lost could appeal, the deme electing five of its own to plead against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> If he lost again the state sold him and if he won the demesmen were compelled to register him, and the examination of the Nine Archons opened by asking to what deme a man's father belonged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 2.16, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 42, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 55, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Remains_of_the_ancient_road_and_the_ancient_city_of_Rhamnus_on_July_22%2C_2020.jpg/1920px-Remains_of_the_ancient_road_and_the_ancient_city_of_Rhamnus_on_July_22%2C_2020.jpg","title":"Deme","alt":"A stony ancient road running down a scrub-covered hillside towards ruined walls on a headland, with the sea and mountains beyond","desc":"The ruins of the ancient deme of Rhamnous, of the Aiantis tribe, in northeastern Attica. George E. Koronaios, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_ancient_road_and_the_ancient_city_of_Rhamnus_on_July_22,_2020.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-311","num":311,"category":"Athens","question":"Ten belonged to the suburbs, ten to the coast and ten inland, and each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was assigned to a tribe by lot so no tribe lacked a share in all three.","questions":["Twelve ship-boards were assigned to each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the four old Athenian tribes, and over the ship-boards stood the commissioners appointed for the levies and the expenditure.","Because each of the four Ionian tribes already held three, twelve tribes would have kept the older <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> system in use and left the Athenian multitude unmixed."],"answer":"trittys","answerText":"trittys","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"abstract":"A <b>trittys</b> was a third of an Athenian tribe, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says Cleisthenes portioned out the land among the demes into thirty such parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ten of them belonged to the suburbs, ten to the coast and ten to the inland district, and he gave these parts the name of Thirds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They were assigned among the tribes by lot, three to each, in order that every tribe might have a share in all the districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same work explains why he did not arrange the citizens in twelve tribes: that would have meant using the division already in place, the four old tribes having contained twelve Thirds between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Had he done so the multitude would not have been mixed up at all, which was the purpose the whole rearrangement was for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The older Thirds are described under Solon, when there were four tribes and four tribal kings, three Thirds assigned from each tribe and twelve ship-boards to each Third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Over the ship-boards stood the office of ship-commissioners, appointed for the levies and the expenditure, and clauses ordering them to levy or to spend out of the ship-commission fund recurred often in Solon's laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regions the new Thirds were cut from had been the ground of Athenian faction, since the three parties of the years after Solon derived their names from the places where their farms were situated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were the Men of the Coast under Megacles son of Alcmaeon, the Men of the Plain who desired the oligarchy, and the Hillmen who had appointed Peisistratus over them as an extreme advocate of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus, the earlier of the two sources, does not use the word at all, reporting only that Cleisthenes made ten tribe-wardens in place of four and assigned ten districts to each tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.66, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D66. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 13, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/L%27Attique_apr%C3%A8s_Clisthene.jpg/1920px-L%27Attique_apr%C3%A8s_Clisthene.jpg","title":"Trittys","alt":"A coloured map of the Attic peninsula cut into thirty small districts, each numbered from one to ten, with three regions outlined in red and labelled Paralie, Asty and Mesogee, and an inset plan of the quarters of Athens","desc":"Claude Zygiel, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%27Attique_apr%C3%A8s_Clisthene.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-312","num":312,"category":"Athens","question":"Cleomenes tried to dissolve the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and hand the offices to Isagoras' faction, and it resisted him, after which he seized the acropolis and was besieged.","questions":["No measure could be put to the Athenian people that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had not prepared and published in advance, its proposer being liable to indictment if he carried one.","Eumelides rescued Lysimachus from the executioner, saying no citizen ought to die unsentenced, and the people stripped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the power to fine, imprison and kill."],"answer":"Council of Five Hundred","answerText":"Council of Five Hundred","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Council of Five Hundred</b> was the body Cleisthenes made in place of the council of four hundred, taking fifty men from each of his ten new tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its members were appointed by lot, and the presidency was filled by each tribe in turn in an order settled by lot, the first four selected holding it for 36 days and the latter six for 35.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those serving as presidents dined together in the Round-house at the state's expense and convened the meetings of both the Council and the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Council met on every day but holidays and the assembly four times in each presidency, with the business, each day's agenda and the place of meeting put up in writing beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The presidents gave the first audience to heralds and to ambassadors, and dispatches were delivered to them by their bearers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Council prepared the resolutions the people voted on, and no measure could be passed that it had not prepared and published in writing in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It had once held sovereign power to fine, imprison and put to death, and lost it when Eumelides of Alopece rescued Lysimachus from the executioner, saying that no citizen ought to die without the sentence of a jury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Afterwards every verdict and penalty it passed had to be brought before the jury-court, and its scrutiny of the incoming councillors and of the Nine Archons became subject to appeal in the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its members drew five obols a day and those acting as president an obol more for food, and a man might sit on it twice where the other offices could be held once only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus shows it acting on its own account within a few years of its founding, resisting Cleomenes when he tried to dissolve it and entrust the government to Isagoras' faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.72, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 45, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D45. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 62, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D62. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Remains_of_the_Old_Bouleuterion_and_Metroon_in_the_Ancient_Agora_of_Athens_on_March_23%2C_2021.jpg/1920px-Remains_of_the_Old_Bouleuterion_and_Metroon_in_the_Ancient_Agora_of_Athens_on_March_23%2C_2021.jpg","title":"Council of Five Hundred","alt":"Low courses of ancient stone foundations and wall blocks running across a grassy archaeological site, with trees and a scrubby hillside behind under an overcast sky","desc":"Remains of the Old Bouleuterion and Metroon in the Ancient Agora of Athens. George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_Old_Bouleuterion_and_Metroon_in_the_Ancient_Agora_of_Athens_on_March_23,_2021.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-313","num":313,"category":"Athens","question":"Otanes told the Persian conspirators that the rule of the multitude has the loveliest name of all, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and does none of the things a monarch does.","questions":["Determining offices by lot, holding power accountable and deliberating in public are the three things Herodotus has Otanes name as what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually does.","Maeandrius proclaimed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Samians while reserving six talents and a hereditary priesthood for himself, and was told he had better account for the money instead."],"answer":"isonomia","answerText":"isonomia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Isonomia</b> is the Greek word for equality under the law, and Herodotus puts it at the head of the case for government by the many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the debate he sets among the Persian conspirators, Otanes says that the rule of the multitude has in the first place the loveliest name of all, isonomia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then defines it by what it does: it determines offices by lot, it holds power accountable, and it conducts all deliberating publicly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each is the opposite of what a ruler answerable to nobody does, since such a man upsets the ancestral ways, kills indiscriminately and is the best confidant of slander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Otanes was outvoted and asked in return only that neither he nor his descendants be subject to any of the others, a house Herodotus says was still free in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Maeandrius of Samos set up an altar to Zeus the Liberator on learning of Polycrates' death, called an assembly and proclaimed isonomia to the citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He reserved six talents of Polycrates' wealth for himself and the priesthood of that cult for his descendants, and a Samian rose to say he was unfit to rule and had better account for the monies he had handled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristagoras made a pretence of giving up his tyranny and gave Miletus isonomia so that the Milesians might readily join his revolt from Darius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides has it among the fair professions of the Greek civil wars, the popular leaders crying political equality and the oligarchs a moderate aristocracy while each sought prizes for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In none of these passages is it applied to Athens, and where Herodotus explains why the Athenians grew great once rid of their tyrants he writes instead of isegoria, equal speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.80, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D80. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.83, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D83. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.142, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D142. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.37, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D37. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.78, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D78. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 3.82, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D82. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-314","num":314,"category":"Athens","question":"Fewer than six thousand sherds and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was void, and the man whose name appeared most often left Athens for ten years with the enjoyment of his estate.","questions":["The first man banished by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Hipparchus son of Charmus, a relative of the tyrants, whom Cleisthenes is said to have made the law chiefly to be rid of.","An illiterate countryman handed Aristides a sherd at an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and asked him to write Aristides on it, being tired of hearing the man called the Just."],"answer":"ostracism","answerText":"ostracism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Ostracism</b> was the Athenian procedure for banishing a citizen for ten years by vote, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> credits the law to Cleisthenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That work says it was enacted out of the suspicion felt against the men in the positions of power, because Peisistratus had set himself up as tyrant while leader of the people and general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not put in force until the archonship of Phaenippus, two years after Marathon, when the first man banished was a relative of the tyrants, the son of Charmus, of the deme Collytus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For three years the Athenians went on ostracizing the friends of the tyrants, at whom the legislation had been aimed, and then in the fourth used it to remove anyone else who seemed too great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xanthippus son of Ariphron was the first man unconnected with the tyranny to go, and Aristides followed, until the expedition of Xerxes brought all the exiles home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The assembly voted in the sixth presidency of each year on whether an ostracism should be held at all, the question being put with the other business of that meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch describes the vote itself: each man wrote on a sherd the name of the citizen he wanted removed and carried it to a part of the market-place fenced round with wooden rails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The magistrates counted the sherds in gross first, since with fewer than six thousand the ostracism was void, and then the man written by the most was banished for ten years with the enjoyment of his estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch tells of an illiterate countryman who handed Aristides his sherd and asked him to write Aristides on it, being tired of hearing the man everywhere called the Just.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides has Themistocles ostracized and living at Argos when the Spartans came for him, and Herodotus has Aristides cross from Aegina before Salamis, banished by the people and yet the most just man in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i>, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, rev. Arthur Hugh Clough, 1859, Wikisource, https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Plutarch%27s_Lives_%28Clough%29/Aristides. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.79, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D79. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.135, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D135. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Athen_Stoa_Ostrakismos_2.jpg/1920px-Athen_Stoa_Ostrakismos_2.jpg","title":"Ostracism","alt":"Twelve broken pot bases mounted in rows on a museum wall, each with a Greek name scratched around the ring through the black glaze","desc":"These ostraka from 482 BCE were recovered from a well near the Acropolis. The ostraca in the upper row read 'THEMISTHOKLES PHREARIOS', while the others read 'THEMISTHOKLES NEOKLEOS'. Xocolatl, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athen_Stoa_Ostrakismos_2.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-315","num":315,"category":"Athens","question":"The presidents of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled Athens at that time, says Herodotus, where Thucydides gives the same charge to the nine archons with full powers.","questions":["Twelve of them went to each of the four old tribes, and Cleisthenes replaced every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a deme and its officer with a demarch.","Clauses ordering an officer to levy or to spend out of the fund of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recurred in laws of Solon no longer in force when the Constitution of the Athenians was written."],"answer":"naukrary","answerText":"naukrary","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"abstract":"A <b>naukrary</b> was one of the units the Athenians were divided into before Cleisthenes, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> assigns twelve of them to each of the four old tribes, beside three Thirds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over them was established an office whose holders were appointed for the levies and the expenditures that were made, the same passage setting the arrangement under Solon, when there were still four tribes and four tribal kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That work says clauses reading 'the ship-commissioner to levy' and 'to spend out of the ship-commission fund' occurred frequently in the laws of Solon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It adds that those laws were no longer in force in its own day, so it knows the clauses as quotations rather than as anything still used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleisthenes put the demes in the place of the naukraries and appointed demarchs with the same duties their officers had had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The unit is best known from a disagreement between two historians about who was governing Athens when Cylon tried to make himself tyrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus says the men who raised Cylon's followers from the goddess's statue were the presidents of the naukraroi, the rulers of Athens at that time, and that the suppliants were subject to any penalty save death and were slain all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says the watch was left to the nine archons with full powers, adds that at that time most political functions were discharged by the nine archons, and gives the killing to those Athenians who were charged with the duty of keeping guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Godley renders the phrase as the presidents of the naval boards and Rackham has ship-commissioners for the same office, the Greek in Herodotus reading naukraroi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither passage explains what the unit was for, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> describes only its levies and its fund.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.71, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Historiae</i> 5.71, ed. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0125%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-316","num":316,"category":"Athens","question":"Aristotle lists the temple of Olympian Zeus among the works of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside the pyramids of Egypt, as a device for keeping subjects poor and busy.","questions":["The spring below the acropolis was Fairwater while it lay open and Nine Pipes after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> altered it, and Pausanias says Peisistratus embellished it.","Hadrian dedicated the temple and its gold and ivory statue centuries after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had begun the building and left it standing unfinished."],"answer":"Peisistratid building programme","answerText":"Peisistratid building programme","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Peisistratid building programme</b> is the work the tyrants of Athens put up in the city, and Aristotle names its largest piece outright, the building of the temple of Olympian Zeus by the Peisistratids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He lists it with the pyramids in Egypt, the votive offerings of the Cypselids and the temples at Samos raised by Polycrates, all as instances of one device of tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The device is to make the subjects poor so that no guard need be kept against them, and to leave a people busy with their daily affairs no leisure to plot against their ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The temple was left standing unfinished, and Pausanias saw it complete only because Hadrian dedicated the building and a gold and ivory statue exceeding every other in size but the colossi at Rhodes and Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Athenians told him the ancient sanctuary there had been built by Deucalion, and pointed to a grave near the temple as their evidence that Deucalion had lived at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Thucydides says the spring below the citadel went by the name of Callirhoe, or Fairwater, while it lay open, and was called Enneacrunos, or Nine Pipes, after the alteration the tyrants made to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that it stood near enough the old city to be used for the most important offices, and that the old fashion of taking its water before marriage was still kept up in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias saw it centuries later, says Peisistratus had embellished it, and remarks that although there were cisterns all over Athens this was the only fountain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides had also read the faded inscription on the altar in the Pythian precinct, which a grandson of the tyrant dedicated during his own archonship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The precinct of the temple was about four stades round and full of statues when Pausanias walked it, every city having dedicated a likeness of Hadrian and the Athenians a colossus behind the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 2.15, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 5.1313b, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1944), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D5%3Asection%3D1313b. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.14.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D14%3Asection%3D1. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.18, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Constantinou_Dimitrios_-_The_Temple_of_Olympian_Zeus_and_the_Acropolis_in_the_background_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Constantinou_Dimitrios_-_The_Temple_of_Olympian_Zeus_and_the_Acropolis_in_the_background_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"The temple of Olympian Zeus and the Acropolis","alt":"A sepia photograph of a group of tall fluted columns with leafy Corinthian capitals standing on open ground at the right, two more columns alone in the middle distance, a low town behind them and the rock of the Acropolis rising beyond it","desc":"Constantinou Dimitrios, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantinou_Dimitrios_-_The_Temple_of_Olympian_Zeus_and_the_Acropolis_in_the_background_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-317","num":317,"category":"Athens","question":"Xenophon dates a year by an eclipse of the moon one evening and by the burning of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Athens, when Callias was archon.","questions":["The foundations that carry the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stand between the Erechtheion and the Parthenon, on the rock the Athenians of Pausanias' day called the Acropolis and once called the Polis.","A shoot a cubit long sprang the next day from the olive burnt with the rest of the sanctuary, on the acropolis rock where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands."],"answer":"Old Temple of Athena","answerText":"Old Temple of Athena","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Old Temple of Athena</b> is the name given to a large set of foundations on the Athenian acropolis, standing between the Erechtheion and the Parthenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Xenophon dates a year by an eclipse of the moon one evening and by the burning of the old temple of Athena at Athens, when Pityas was ephor at Sparta and Callias archon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus, writing earlier, says the fetters of the Chalcidian and Boeotian prisoners could still be seen in his own time hanging from acropolis walls that the Persians' fire had charred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He places them opposite the temple which faces west, and says a tenth of the ransom bought a four-horse chariot standing to the left of the entrance into the outer porch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Persians reached the town in the archonship of Calliades it was deserted, and the few who held the sacred precinct fenced it with doors and logs, believing the oracle's wooden wall meant that and not the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Some of the barbarians climbed the sheer cliff near the precinct of Cecrops' daughter Aglaurus, opened the gates, murdered the suppliants, plundered the sacred precinct and set fire to the entire acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In that acropolis, Herodotus adds, is a shrine of Erechtheus, and in the shrine an olive tree and a pool of salt water set there by Poseidon and Athena as tokens when they contended for the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The olive was burnt with the rest of the sacred precinct, and on the day after the burning the Athenians ordered up to sacrifice saw a shoot about a cubit long sprung from the stump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias saw a double building there centuries later, with sea-water in a cistern that sent forth the noise of waves when a south wind blew, and the outline of a trident on the rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He calls the most holy symbol the image of Athena on what is now the Acropolis but in early days the Polis, or 'City', and reports a legend that it fell from heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.77, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D77. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.51, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D51. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.53, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D53. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.55, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 1.6.1, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0206%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.26, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D26. [Open access]","Jane E. Harrison and Margaret de G. Verrall, <i>Mythology and Monuments of Ancient Athens</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890), 414, https://archive.org/details/mythologymonumen00paus. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Foundations_of_the_Old_Athena_Temple_%28foreground%29.jpg/1280px-Foundations_of_the_Old_Athena_Temple_%28foreground%29.jpg","title":"Foundations of the Old Temple of Athena","alt":"A grainy photograph looking down on the Athenian acropolis, with long rectangular lines of squared stone blocks laid out on the ground in the foreground and the porch of the Erechtheion standing behind them","desc":"Foundations of the Old Athena Temple (foreground). Plate from Martin Luther D'Ooge, The Acropolis of Athens, 1909. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foundations_of_the_Old_Athena_Temple_(foreground).jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-318","num":318,"category":"Athens","question":"Xenophon says nobody even attempts to date the beginning of mining at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that the dumps are small beside the virgin silver-laden hills.","questions":["Alcibiades told the Spartans that fortifying Decelea would deprive the Athenians of their revenues from the silver mines at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and of their gains from land and law courts.","Nicias son of Niceratus owned a thousand men in the mines at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and let them out to a Thracian for an obol a day each, net."],"answer":"Laurion","answerText":"Laurion","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Laurion</b> is the mining district of Attica, and Xenophon calls the silver in its soil a gift of divine providence, since of the many states near Athens by land and sea not one has even a thin vein of the ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He puts beside it the land that yields no fruit if sown and yet, when quarried, feeds many times the number it could support if it grew corn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mines had been worked so many generations, he says, that nobody even attempts to date the beginning of the work, and the dumps are small beside the virgin silver-laden hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The silver-yielding ground was continually being found to extend further rather than to shrink, and where other trades slump when more men enter them, more diggers here only multiply the ore discovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor is silver like furniture, which a man stops buying once he has enough for his house, since nobody has ever owned so much of it as to want no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon had heard that Nicias son of Niceratus once owned a thousand men in the mines and let them to Sosias the Thracian for an obol a day each, net, with every vacancy filled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hipponicus had 600 slaves let out on the same terms for a mina a day and Philemonides 300 for half a mina, and others owned numbers in proportion to their capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts a great strike in the archonship of Nicomedes, when the working of the mines at Maronea had given the state a profit of 100 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Alcibiades advised the Spartans that fortifying Decelea would deprive the Athenians at once of their revenues from the silver mines at Laurium, and of their gains from the land and the law courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says that occupation cost them their whole country and more than 20,000 slaves, a great part of them craftsmen, who deserted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 1, trans. E. C. Marchant, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0210%3Atext%3DWays%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 4, trans. E. C. Marchant, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0210%3Atext%3DWays%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.91, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D91. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 7.27, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D27. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Greece_Lavrion_Mines_1903.jpg/1280px-Greece_Lavrion_Mines_1903.jpg","title":"Miners at Lavrion, 1903","alt":"A sepia photograph of a large crowd of miners in caps on a bare hillside, many of them holding up oil lamps, with pit buildings and a winding tower on the ridge behind","desc":"Greek miners—just going down—at the famous Mines of Ancient Laurion, Greece. From stereograph card. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece_Lavrion_Mines_1903.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-319","num":319,"category":"Athens","question":"Every judge desires them above all, says the chorus of the <i>Birds</i>, promising that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> will nest in their money-bags and lay coins for them.","questions":["The uniformity of style and type is remarkable, since the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keep an archaic head of Athena and an incuse square reverse through class after class.","The reverse of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a bird to the right with its head facing, wings closed, an olive-spray and a crescent behind it, and three letters."],"answer":"Athenian owls","answerText":"Athenian owls","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Athenian owls</b> are the silver coins of Athens, and their reverse gives them the name: an owl to the right with its head facing and its wings closed, an olive-spray behind it, and the letters ΑΘΕ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The obverse carries the head of Athena in a close-fitting crested helmet, rude and archaic on the earliest pieces, with a large prominent eye and each lock of hair ending in a twisted curl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the later coins the helmet is adorned in front with three olive-leaves and a floral scroll behind, and a crescent-moon joins the olive-spray behind the owl on the tetradrachm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The whole series is remarkable for its uniformity of style and type, and the scale ran from the decadrachm down through the tetradrachm at about 17.5 grams to fractions of a single obol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> credits Solon with raising the standard of the measures, the weights and the coinage, the mina going from 70 drachmas to the full hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon says that at most other ports a merchant is compelled to ship a return cargo, because the local currency has no circulation in other states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Athens he may export silver instead, and wherever he sells it he is sure to make a profit on the capital invested, which Xenophon counts among the advantages of the city as a commercial centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chorus of Aristophanes' <i>Birds</i> promises the judges that the owls of Laurium will never be wanting to them, homing with them, building their nests in their money-bags and laying coins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the <i>Frogs</i> the chorus calls the old coins not counterfeit at all but the finest of all coins, the only ones of the proper stamp, of resounding metal among Greeks and foreigners everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It sets them against the base bronze minted yesterday or the day before, which the city uses instead, as it uses worthless men in place of the good ones it despises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 3, trans. E. C. Marchant, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0210%3Atext%3DWays%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Birds</i> 1101–1117, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr. (New York: Random House, 1938), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0026%3Acard%3D1102. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 10, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Frogs</i> 718–737, trans. Matthew Dillon, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0032%3Acard%3D718. [Open access]","Barclay V. Head, <i>Historia Numorum: A Manual of Greek Numismatics</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1887), 310–312, https://archive.org/details/historianumorumm00headuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Tetradrachm_Athens_450_reverse_CdM_Paris.jpg","title":"Reverse of an Athenian tetradrachm, about the 450s BCE","alt":"A worn silver coin showing an owl standing to the right with its head turned to face the viewer, an olive spray and a crescent above its shoulder, and three Greek letters down the right edge","desc":"Silver tetradrachm issued by Athens, about the 450s BCE, reverse: owl, olive spray and crescent moon, with the inscription ΑΘΕ. Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tetradrachm_Athens_450_reverse_CdM_Paris.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-320","num":320,"category":"Athens","question":"A request from Epidaurus for Athenian olive wood is where Herodotus begins the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the trees given on condition of yearly dues to Athena and Erechtheus.","questions":["An oracle bade the Athenians wait thirty years and then mark out a precinct for Aeacus, one turn in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that they could not stomach.","During the fighting that came out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Corinth sold Athens twenty ships at five drachmas apiece, its law forbidding an outright gift."],"answer":"enmity between Athens and Aegina","answerText":"enmity between Athens and Aegina","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>enmity between Athens and Aegina</b> is traced by Herodotus to the barren land of Epidaurus, whose people were told by the priestess at Delphi to set up images of Damia and Auxesia in the wood of the cultivated olive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They asked the Athenians for the trees, it being said that at that time there were no olives anywhere save at Athens, and got them on condition of yearly dues to Athena and Erechtheus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Aeginetans, still subject to Epidaurus and crossing there even for their own lawsuits, began to build ships, revolted, and carried the images off to a place of their own called Oea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Athenians sent one trireme to fetch them back, and their own account has the crew driven mad by a thunderstorm and an earthquake until they killed one another and a single man returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Aeginetans and the Argives say instead that the Argives crossed secretly from Epidaurus, fell on the Athenians unawares and cut them off from their ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> All three accounts agree that one man came home, and the wives of the dead gathered round him and stabbed him with the brooch-pins of their garments, each asking him where her husband was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Athenians thought that more dreadful than the disaster itself and could punish the women only by changing their dress to a linen tunic that needed no pins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Argos and Aegina made brooches the chief thing offered to the two goddesses and forbade anything Attic in the temple, even pottery, and their women wore longer pins ever after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When it came to war an oracle bade Athens wait thirty years, mark out a precinct for Aeacus and begin in the thirty-first, which the Athenians could not stomach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Aeginetans then ambushed the sacred ship at Sunium with many leading Athenians aboard, and Corinth, a close friend at that time, sold Athens twenty ships at five drachmas apiece because its law forbade a free gift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.82–83, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D82. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.85–86, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D85. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.87–88, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D87. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.89, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D89. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.87, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D87. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.89, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D89. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-321","num":321,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Tyrannies established almost everywhere in place of hereditary monarchy, and Hellas beginning to fit out fleets, is how Thucydides describes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which closes with the Persian Wars.","questions":["Lacedaemon put down the tyrants at last, having obtained good laws very early, near the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Marathon and the armada close.","The 8th century is commonly taken as the breaking-point at which the polis emerged, near the start of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though that shift may be less drastic."],"answer":"Archaic period","answerText":"Archaic period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 479 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Archaic period</b> is the age of Greek history between the Dark Age and the Classical, and Thucydides opens his sketch of these centuries with a Hellas still removing and settling, unable to attain the quiet which must precede growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The late return of the Hellenes from Ilium caused many revolutions, he writes, and it was the citizens driven into exile by the factions that followed who founded the cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many years had to elapse before Hellas could send out colonies, as Athens did to Ionia and most of the islands and the Peloponnesians to most of Italy and Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As the power of Hellas grew and the acquisition of wealth became more an object, tyrannies were established almost everywhere in place of the old hereditary monarchy with its definite prerogatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hellas began to fit out fleets, the Corinthians being the first to approach the modern style of naval architecture, and the earliest sea-fight in history was fought between Corinth and Corcyra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Corinth, planted on an isthmus, had from time out of mind been a commercial emporium, and the epithet 'wealthy' given it by the old poets marks the money that bought her a navy and put down piracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tyrants were at last put down by Lacedaemon, which had obtained good laws at a very early period and kept the same form of government for more than four hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not many years after that deposition the battle of Marathon was fought, and ten years afterwards the barbarian returned with the armada, which is where the period is conventionally closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The 8th century is commonly taken as the breaking-point at which the polis emerged, though a longer perspective makes that shift less drastic and the 6th century more important than is often thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tyranny itself is now read as a response to economic and political crisis, and the tyrants and lawgivers of these centuries as a varied group that resists easy definition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.12, trans. Richard Crawley (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1910), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, trans. Richard Crawley (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1910), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.18, trans. Richard Crawley (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1910), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200–479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]","Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-322","num":322,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Hybrid monsters, lions, lotus-flowers and rosettes strewn as filling ornament mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when Greek workshops took up eastern motifs across pottery, ivory and bronze.","questions":["Griffin, siren and bull protomes fixed to cauldrons at Delphi are among the most characteristic bronzes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which runs through the 7th century.","A kit of Levantine motifs, ivory work, monumental sculpture and alphabetic writing is what Greeks took up in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, largely through Phoenician contact."],"answer":"Orientalising period","answerText":"Orientalising period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 8th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Orientalising period</b> is the phase of Greek art that runs through the 7th century BCE, the change setting in at the end of the 8th, when workshops across the Greek world took up eastern motifs and techniques.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the East came hybrid monsters such as the sphinx and the siren, animals such as the lion, isolated motifs like the lotus-flower and the rosette, and a habit of laying decoration in long bands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground between the figures was strewn with rosettes and other filling ornament, a manner that reads as an imitation of woven textiles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The change is put down to the commercial activity of the Phoenicians, scattered by Assyria in the 8th century, and was artistic and industrial rather than political.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The borrowings have been gathered into an orientalising kit: Levantine decorative motifs, new pottery and metalwork technologies, oriental-style monumental sculpture, ivory work, masonry techniques, burial and banqueting customs, and alphabetic writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus says the Phoenicians who came with Cadmus brought the alphabet to Hellas, unknown to the Greeks before, and that the Ionians learned the letters from them and named them after them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Delphi shows the period in bronze: rod stands and tripods of Cypro-Cretan type, and appliqués in the form of griffin, siren and bull protomes, among the most characteristic pieces of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The winged sphinx began in Egypt and reached Greece in the winged and female version of the Levant, where it had become a standard feature of Phoenician work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Imports and imitations are distinguished from orientalising art proper, which is Greek in essence and manufacture and oriental only in inspiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One reading makes the innovation inseparable from the crystallisation of the polis, the new art serving elites in the forming communities as a mode of social recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.58, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 333, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Hélène Sader, review of <i>Phoenicians and the Making of the Mediterranean</i>, by Carolina López-Ruiz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.06.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.06.11/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Bronzes du haut-archaïsme à Delphes: Trépieds, chaudrons et vaisselle de bronze (fin VIIIe-VIIe siècle)</i>, by Hélène Aurigny, Fouilles de Delphes 5, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.08/. [Open access]","Carolina López-Ruiz, review of <i>Un art citoyen: recherches sur l'orientalisation des artisanats en Grèce proto-archaïque</i>, by Thomas Brisart, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.11.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.11.45/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Protom_d%27un_grif%C3%B3%2C_bronze%2C_segona_meitat_del_segle_VII_aC._Museu_Arqueol%C3%B2gic%2C_Ol%C3%ADmpia-2.jpg","title":"Bronze griffin protome from Olympia, before the middle of the 7th century BCE","alt":"A hammered bronze head and long curving neck of a griffin, its beak open, a knobbed spike rising from the crown and the eye socket set with bone","desc":"Protome of a griffin. Hammered bronze. The eyes are encrusted with bone, and the now lost iris was made of a semi-precious stone. Before the middle of the 7th century BCE. National Archaeological Museum of Olympia. Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protom_d%27un_grif%C3%B3,_bronze,_segona_meitat_del_segle_VII_aC._Museu_Arqueol%C3%B2gic,_Ol%C3%ADmpia-2.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-323","num":323,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Pear-shaped lekythoi, aryballoi and skyphoi strewn with rosettes and cut with incised scale-patterns are the stock of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Athens took the market from by about 550 BCE.","questions":["A jug bearing the name of Pyrrhos, no later than 700 BCE and the earliest known signature on a pot, belongs to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found across Sicily and Italy.","Shapes, figures and above all the incised line passed from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Athenian workshops, whose black-figure then drove it off the market."],"answer":"Corinthian pottery","answerText":"Corinthian pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protocorinthian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 650 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Corinthian pottery</b> is the painted ware of Corinth, and it falls into five classes: the Protocorinthian of about 750 to 650 BCE, then vases with incised scale-patterns, vases with floral ornament and unincised figures, the same with the figures incised, and finally large animal friezes of about 600 to 550.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dominating form is the alabastron or lekythos, a pear-shaped vase with a flat round lip and a flat handle, beside the aryballos, the skyphos, the pyxis and a small krater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Protocorinthian vases turn up at Syracuse, a Corinthian foundation of 735 BCE, at Megara Hyblaea, in Italian trench-tombs of about 750 to 650, and at the Argive Heraion, on Aegina and more rarely at Tiryns, Athens and Eleusis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of them carries the name of Pyrrhos in an inscription that cannot be much later than 700 BCE, which makes it the earliest known signature on a pot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides puts commerce behind all this: Corinth was planted on an isthmus and had from time out of mind been an emporium, wealthy enough in the old poets' epithet to buy itself a navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The incised line arrives with a class of vases covered in scale-patterns, cut with a compass whose graving-tool left the centre points of the arcs visible, a technique long familiar in metalwork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Towards the end of the 8th century an influx of eastern motifs transformed the style, bringing conventionalised rosettes, leaves and flowers strewn all over the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ground-ornaments of that kind are almost unknown in eastern art itself and seem to be drawn from embroidered textiles, and purple accessories become common alongside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The finest work is miniature: some twenty pear-shaped lekythoi survive with minute figures in an advanced style, the Macmillan lekythos at their head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Corinthian shapes, figures and above all incision shaped Athenian black-figure, and by about 550 BCE Attic had taken the market, for reasons nobody has settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, trans. Richard Crawley (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1910), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 306, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 307–309, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 311–312, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The History of Greek Vases: Potters, Painters and Pictures</i>, by John Boardman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.23/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Aryballos_Macmillan.JPG","title":"The Macmillan aryballos, attributed to the Chigi Painter","alt":"A small Protocorinthian perfume flask whose mouth is modelled as a lion's head, its body banded with narrow friezes of tiny figures and animals","desc":"Perfume bottle, aryballos, with lion-head mouth, attributed to the Chigi Painter; British Museum GR 1889.4-18.1. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aryballos_Macmillan.JPG"}},
{"id":"gr-324","num":324,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Air let in, then shut off, then let in again is how the kiln makes the colour of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose figures are silhouettes with details cut through them.","questions":["Athens took the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Corinth along with its shapes, and held the market with it until red-figure reversed the scheme.","Magnetite crystals under 200 nanometres across colour the glassy slip of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the unslipped clay beside it cannot match because oxygen reaches it."],"answer":"black-figure technique","answerText":"black-figure technique","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Incision from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attic heyday</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 560 – 520 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>black-figure technique</b> paints its figures as silhouettes in a slip of fine clay and cuts their details through it with a graver, an adjunct to figure-decoration that was entirely new and was probably taken from metalwork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The colour comes from the kiln rather than the brush, since one clay serves for both pot and slip and three stages of firing are what separate them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first, air is let in and every surface turns brighter shades of red and orange, the iron compound formed being mostly hematite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the second the air is shut off, every surface goes black, and a thin glassy layer forms on the slipped areas alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the third, oxygen works back into the unslipped clay and returns it to red, while the slipped surfaces cannot take it through that vitreous layer and stay black.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The black is a highly resistant potash-alumino-silicate glass coloured by magnetite crystals under 200 nanometres across, made from an illitic Attic clay worked down to a fine particle size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beyond incision the painters added accessory red and white for expressive purposes, laid on before the pot went into the fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No Greek kiln has been found, so the ovens are known from the painted vases themselves: tall conical furnaces fed by fires from beneath, with one door for putting the pots in and another for watching them bake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Corinthian pinakes are the best of that evidence, and the potters' hymn ascribed to Homer names the demons who spoil a firing, the Unquenchable, the Crasher, the Smasher and the Savage Conqueror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Corinthian shapes and above all incision passed to Athens, where the technique held the market until red-figure reversed the scheme and the Andokides Painter began the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 311, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 215–216, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Sanchita Balachandran, \"Bringing Back the (Ancient) Bodies: The Potters' Sensory Experiences and the Firing of Red, Black and Purple Greek Vases,\" <i>Arts</i> 8, no. 2 (2019): 70, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts8020070. [Open access]","Lars Lühl, Bernhard Hesse, Ioanna Mantouvalou, Max Wilke, Sammia Mahlkow, Eleni Aloupi-Siotis, and Birgit Kanngiesser, \"Confocal XANES and the Attic Black Glaze: The Three-Stage Firing Process through Modern Reproduction,\" <i>Analytical Chemistry</i> 86, no. 14 (2014): 6924–30, https://doi.org/10.1021/ac500990k. [Paywalled]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The History of Greek Vases: Potters, Painters and Pictures</i>, by John Boardman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.23/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Amphora_with_equestrian_scenes%2C_Pointed_Nose_Painter%2C_Tyrrhenian_group%2C_Greek%2C_Attic%2C_c._550_BC%2C_black-figure_technique_-_Krannert_Art_Museum%2C_UIUC_-_DSC06540.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Attic black-figure amphora, Pointed Nose Painter, about 550 BCE","alt":"A tall two-handled jar whose body carries bands of black horsemen and animals on the orange clay, their manes, harness and muscles picked out by fine lines cut through the black","desc":"Amphora with equestrian scenes, Pointed Nose Painter, Tyrrhenian group, Greek, Attic, about 550 BCE, black-figure technique. Exhibit in the Krannert Art Museum, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign. Daderot, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphora_with_equestrian_scenes,_Pointed_Nose_Painter,_Tyrrhenian_group,_Greek,_Attic,_c._550_BC,_black-figure_technique_-_Krannert_Art_Museum,_UIUC_-_DSC06540.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-325","num":325,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"The finest work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the Vatican amphora on which Ajax and Achilles bend over a board, one calling four and the other three.","questions":["Ten vases carry the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and twice the signature says he both painted and made the pot rather than only made it.","The inside of a Munich cup by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is filled by Dionysos sailing in a ship shaped like a fish, its mast overgrown by a vine."],"answer":"Exekias","answerText":"Exekias","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vases dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 – 530 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Exekias</b> is among the most accomplished of the Athenian black-figure artists, and ten vases carry his name: four amphorae, four cups and two fragments, with a handful of unsigned pieces given to him besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most Attic artists sign with a word meaning that they made the pot, and he twice writes that he both painted and made it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His finest is an amphora in the Vatican with Ajax and Achilles bent over a board game, the one calling four and the other three, and the Dioskouroi with Tyndareus and Leda on the reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carries his signature in iambic form and the name Onetorides praised as beautiful, while the other three signed amphorae are in London, Paris and Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cup in Munich is a masterpiece of its kind: inside, Dionysos sails in a ship shaped like a fish with the mast and yard overgrown by a vine, and outside are large eyes and groups of warriors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His subjects come from the ordinary stock of the day, and what marks them out is the care in every detail, the delicacy and minuteness of the incising, the sparing use of accessory colour and the naming of almost every figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wide-bowled cup with a low foot, which the eye-cups of Ionia had already made popular, seems to have been brought to Athens by him and stayed in favour right through the red-figure period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where other painters set palmettes and lotus-buds under the handles he puts an elaborate system of spirals, a pattern descended from Mycenaean art by way of the Melian amphorae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A neck-amphora of about 540 BCE in New York, 47 centimetres (18 inches) high and still carrying its lid and knob, is painted with a chariot wedding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He stands midway between Kleitias of the François vase and the transitional work that came after, and narrative reached its full development with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 379–381, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 357, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 375, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Jenifer Neils, review of <i>How to Read Greek Vases</i>, by Joan R. Mertens, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.07.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.07.23/. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta neck-amphora with lid and knob, Greek, Attic, attributed to Exekias, ca. 540 BCE, accession 17.230.14a, b, https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/250551. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The History of Greek Vases: Potters, Painters and Pictures</i>, by John Boardman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.23/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP218568.jpg","title":"Neck-amphora with lid, attributed to Exekias, about 540 BCE","alt":"A tall lidded two-handled jar painted with a four-horse chariot carrying a bride and groom, a lyre-player beside the team and attendants at either end, with a large black spiral under each handle and bands of meander below","desc":"Terracotta neck-amphora with lid and knob, Greek, Attic, attributed to Exekias, about 540 BCE; 47 cm high. Metropolitan Museum of Art, Rogers Fund, 1917, accession 17.230.14a, b. Public domain, Open Access.","credit":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/250551"}},
{"id":"gr-326","num":326,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Ergotimos made me and Kleitias painted me are each written twice on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, once in the wedding procession and once beside Theseus on the rim.","questions":["Almost every figure on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named, and so are an altar, a seat, a hydria, a fountain-house and the stone in a centaur's hand.","Pygmies fight cranes round the foot of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with six figured registers above them and Ajax carrying the dead body of Achilles on each handle."],"answer":"François Vase","answerText":"François Vase","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>François Vase</b> is an Attic black-figure volute krater of about 570 BCE, signed by Ergotimos as potter and Kleitias as painter, and now in Florence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is 66 centimetres (26 inches) high with a rim 57 centimetres (22 inches) across, and carries six figured registers, with Ajax lifting the dead Achilles on the handles and pygmies fighting cranes round the foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The potting is as inventive as the drawing, the neck rising in two degrees with no offset lip and the handles owing something, perhaps, to the volutes of monumental architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two friezes run round the neck, the hunt for the Calydonian boar above the chariot race at the funeral games of Patroklos, and Theseus with the Athenian youths above the battle of Lapiths and centaurs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wedding of Peleus and Thetis runs unbroken round the shoulder, a subject Sophilos had signed on a dinos a decade earlier, and beneath it Achilles chases Troilos while Hephaistos rides back to Olympus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Almost every figure is named, and so are an altar, a seat, a hydria, a fountain-house and the stone in a centaur's hand, the alphabet of the lettering leaving no doubt that the work is Athenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The names include spellings found nowhere else, Poseipon for Poseidon and Aphrogite for Aphrodite among them, and both signatures are written twice, once in the wedding procession and once beside Theseus on the rim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysos steps out of the procession in full frontal view with an amphora on his shoulder, probably the golden jar that Homer says held the bones of Achilles and Patroklos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The glaze inside fired red and is worn by metal dippers, and the holes drilled round the handles proved to be an ancient repair in lead, so the krater was used and mended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fragments came from a plundered tomb near Chiusi, gathered in the 1840s by the painter Alessandro François, and whether the scenes make one programme is argued still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The François Vase: New Perspectives</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Mario Iozzo and Adrienne Lezzi-Hafter, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.04.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.32/. [Open access]","“Florence 4209 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Florence+4209&object=Vase. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 73, 370, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 23.83–92, trans. A. T. Murray, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D23%3Acard%3D62. [Open access]","“London 1971.11-1.1 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=London+1971.11-1.1&object=Vase. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Vaso_fran%C3%A7ois%2C_firenze%2C_03.JPG","title":"The François Vase in Florence","alt":"One side of a large two-handled clay pot, its breaks and joins plainly visible, painted in horizontal bands: winged sphinxes and fighting figures at the top, a chariot race below them with Greek names written beside the drivers, a procession of gods and horses round the widest part, and a low decorated foot","desc":"The François Vase, an Attic black-figure volute krater signed by the potter Ergotimos and the painter Kleitias, in the National Archaeological Museum of Florence. Sailko, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaso_fran%C3%A7ois,_firenze,_03.JPG"}},
{"id":"gr-327","num":327,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"A stand in New York barely 9 centimetres (3.5 inches) across and the great krater in Florence are both signed by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, working with the potter Ergotimos.","questions":["A chariot race at the funeral games puts Odysseus first, though he does not compete in Homer, which is one reason <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is thought to have worked from a recital.","Only Exekias, Nearchos and the Amasis Painter are reckoned to rival <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Attic black-figure, and his style has been called dry, precise and careful."],"answer":"Kleitias","answerText":"Kleitias","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 – 560 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Kleitias</b> was an Athenian vase-painter working about 570 BCE, and he is known by name because he wrote it on his own work, in the form that reads Kleitias painted me.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He worked with the potter Ergotimos, who signed beside him to say that he had made it, which was not the usual thing: most early Attic artists used one formula implying that the same man made and painted the pot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two signed pieces that survive could hardly differ more in size: the François Vase in Florence stands 66 centimetres (26 inches) high, the other is a stand in New York 5.7 centimetres (2 inches) high and 9 centimetres (3.5 inches) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That small disc carries a single Gorgon's face, staring out with its tongue between its teeth and its beard drawn in fine incised lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His achievement in black-figure is reckoned to be rivalled only by Exekias, Nearchos and the Amasis Painter, and his style has been called dry, precise and careful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He labelled copiously, and 130 names are painted on the krater, naming not only the gods and the heroes but the odd inanimate object as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The names do not always follow the poems, and his chariot race at the funeral games puts Odysseus first, though Odysseus does not compete in the <i>Iliad</i>, and Diomedes third, though Homer makes him the winner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He is unlikely to have owned a written copy of the poem and probably worked from a recital he had heard, giving the drivers plausible names and leaning, perhaps, on a better-read friend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Theseus appears on the krater in festal dress, among the earliest pictures of the hero in Athenian art, which begins to show him about 575 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Elsewhere he took a shape that painters were already using and made it a story by naming it, so that a warrior carrying a dead comrade off the field becomes Ajax and Achilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Florence 4209 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Florence+4209&object=Vase. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 370, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 379, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta stand, Greek, Attic, signed by Ergotimos as potter and by Kleitias, ca. 570 BCE, accession 31.11.4, https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/253342","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>Tradition and Originality: A Study of Exekias</i>, by E. Anne Mackay, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2010.08.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.34/. [Open access]","Barry B. Powell, review of <i>Image and Myth: A History of Pictorial Narration in Greek Art</i>, by Luca Giuliani, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.04.35, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.35/. [Open access]","Sarah Morris, review of <i>Homer and the Artists: Text and Picture in Early Greek Art</i>, by Anthony M. Snodgrass, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 1999.10.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.10.32/. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The François Vase: New Perspectives</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Mario Iozzo and Adrienne Lezzi-Hafter, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.04.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.32/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP-12501-003.jpg","title":"Terracotta stand signed by Ergotimos and Kleitias","alt":"A small round clay disc seen from above, painted with a single staring face: round eyes, curled hair, tusks at the corners of the mouth and a tongue hanging between the teeth, the beard drawn as a dense fringe of fine incised lines","desc":"Terracotta stand, Greek, Attic, about 570 BCE, signed by Ergotimos as potter and by Kleitias, 5.7 cm high and 9 cm across. Metropolitan Museum of Art, accession 31.11.4, Fletcher Fund, 1931. Public domain (Open Access), via the Metropolitan Museum of Art.","credit":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/253342"}},
{"id":"gr-328","num":328,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Where other black-figure painters gave women's flesh a coat of added white, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would sometimes draw them in outline against the bare clay instead.","questions":["A lekythos in New York by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows two women at an upright loom, the warp hanging in weighted threads and the woven band forming above.","Vases signed Amasis made me are given by museums to the potter of that name and their painting to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who left no name of his own."],"answer":"Amasis Painter","answerText":"Amasis Painter","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 560 – 515 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Amasis Painter</b> is the name given to an Athenian black-figure painter at work from about 560 to 515 BCE, and it is borrowed rather than his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The vases he decorated carry the signature Amasis made me, running along the edge of the picture, which is a potter's formula, so he is called after the man who threw the pots while his own name goes unrecorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Few vases carry that signature, but his manner is individual enough that a good many unsigned ones have been given to him as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His drawing is accurate and his detail careful and delicate, close to Exekias in that, though his subjects are more monotonous and his figures a good deal more rigid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much in his work suggests a connection with Ionia, and since his name is Egyptian more than one writer has thought him a foreigner, from Naukratis, from Daphnae, or from somewhere in Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He was fond of the olpe, a jug carrying its picture in a panel to the right of the handle only, and four of his signed vases take that shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also painted one of the earliest Attic alabastra, a small oil flask whose form came from Egyptian models and reached Athens about the middle of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where other painters gave women's flesh a coat of added white, he would sometimes leave them in outline against the bare clay, which has been read as an experiment and as an economy alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A lekythos of his in New York shows two women at an upright loom, the warp hanging in weighted threads and the finished band forming along the beam above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On a neck amphora in Boston he set Herakles and Apollo hauling at the Delphic tripod with Hermes stepping between them, hand raised, his name misspelt above his head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 382–383, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","“Boston 01.8027 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Boston+01.8027&object=Vase. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta lekythos, Greek, Attic, attributed to the Amasis Painter, ca. 550–530 BCE, accession 31.11.10, https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/253348","J. Michael Padgett, review of <i>The Colors of Clay: Special Techniques in Athenian Vases</i>, by Beth Cohen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.03.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.09/. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>Corpus vasorum antiquorum. Deutschland, Bd 99; Berlin, Antikensammlung, Bd. 16: Attische Salbgefässe</i>, by Nina Zimmermann-Elseify, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2016.04.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.04.50/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP-32672-005.jpg","title":"Women at an upright loom, on a lekythos by the Amasis Painter","alt":"Detail of a clay pot: two women in patterned dresses stand either side of a tall loom, its warp hanging in a curtain of straight threads ending in a row of small weights, with a woven band and a decorated beam across the top","desc":"Detail of a terracotta lekythos, Greek, Attic, about 550 to 530 BCE, attributed to the Amasis Painter: women weaving at a warp-weighted loom. Metropolitan Museum of Art, accession 31.11.10, Fletcher Fund, 1931. Public domain (Open Access), via the Metropolitan Museum of Art.","credit":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/253348"}},
{"id":"gr-329","num":329,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Nine-tenths of the painted vases recovered in Etruria came from Vulci alone, one measure of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that sent Athenian pots west across the Mediterranean.","questions":["Merchants in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> scratched notes under the feet of the pots they shipped, one recording 34 lekythoi for 37 obols and another a batch of six kraters.","Between 60 and 80 per cent of Etruscan tombs of the later 6th and 5th centuries held Athenian pottery, carried there by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that fed Vulci and Cerveteri."],"answer":"Attic pottery trade","answerText":"Attic pottery trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reaches Etruria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 620 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In most tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Attic pottery trade</b> carried Athenian painted vases across the Mediterranean, and it is why the pots found in Greece itself are few and small beside the crowds of them taken from the cemeteries of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vases by the Nettos Painter, working late in the 7th century BCE, were the first Attic pieces to reach Etruria, and the numbers rose sharply in the opening decades of the 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athenian pottery turns up in every Etruscan city of any size, from Hadria at the mouth of the Po down to the gates of Rome, and nine-tenths of all the painted vases recovered in Etruria came from Vulci alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between 60 and 80 per cent of Etruscan tombs of the later 6th and 5th centuries held Attic pottery, most often cups, kraters, amphorae and lekythoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not bought for the grave alone, since the same wares appear in houses and in some forty sanctuaries, in large centres and small, in richer and poorer houses alike, and many pots carry ancient repairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The merchants who moved them left their mark under the foot, scratching commercial signs into the clay and sometimes a note of the batch with its price.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One krater now in London lists six kraters, twelve cups, twenty vinegar-cups and twenty saucers with figures in drachmas and obols beside them, and a shorter note gives 34 lekythoi for 37 obols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristophanes has Dionysos tell Euripides that he can buy the offending oil-flask for an obol, the sort of sum those lists imply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At such prices Attic pottery in Etruria cannot be called a luxury ware, and it made up only a small part of the crockery in an Etruscan house, exactly as fine pottery did in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some have argued from this that the vases travelled as little more than ballast, though the numbers tell against it, and the potters plainly knew their customers, sending west the revels, warriors, athletes and Herakles the Etruscan buyer wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Adolfo Domínguez, review of Christoph Reusser, <i>Vasen für Etrurien: Verbreitung und Funktionen attischer Keramik im Etrurien des 6. und 5. Jahrhunderts vor Christus</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2003.06.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.06.11/. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 70, 76–77, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 2 (London: John Murray, 1905), 238–41, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient02walt. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The François Vase: New Perspectives</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Mario Iozzo and Adrienne Lezzi-Hafter, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.04.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.32/. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Frogs</i> 1236, ed. Matthew Dillon, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0032%3Acard%3D1225. [Open access]","Mariachiara Franceschini, review of Sheramy D. Bundrick, <i>Athens, Etruria, and the Many Lives of Greek Figured Pottery</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2019.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.30/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Eracle%2C_Atena_e_Dioniso%2C_550-530_aC%2C_anfora_attica_da_Vulci_-FG.jpg/1920px-Eracle%2C_Atena_e_Dioniso%2C_550-530_aC%2C_anfora_attica_da_Vulci_-FG.jpg","title":"An Athenian amphora found at Vulci","alt":"A two-handled clay jar standing in a museum case, its upper body painted with black figures on an orange ground: a draped figure at the left, a figure wearing a spotted lion skin in the middle and a draped figure at the right, under a band of palmettes round the neck, with a black lower body on a small foot","desc":"Herakles, Athena and Dionysos on an Attic black-figure amphora of about 550 to 530 BCE found at Vulci, now in the Museo Gregoriano Etrusco of the Vatican Museums, inv. 16590. Fabrizio Garrisi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eracle,_Atena_e_Dioniso,_550-530_aC,_anfora_attica_da_Vulci_-FG.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-330","num":330,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"The figures on a pot in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are not painted at all: they are left in the colour of the clay while the black slip is brushed over everything around them.","questions":["An amphora in Boston of about 530 BCE shows Achilles and Ajax twice over, once in black-figure and once in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that was then new at Athens.","Detail in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is drawn with a brush rather than cut with a graver, in relief lines for the firm contours and dilute gloss for the softer markings."],"answer":"red-figure technique","answerText":"red-figure technique","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Invented</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>red-figure technique</b> reverses the older scheme: the figures are left in the colour of the clay and the black slip is carried over everything around them, so the figures are never painted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was an invention rather than a development, no intermediate stage between the two schemes being possible, and it appears at Athens in the last quarter of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The contours were drawn round each figure with a brush, a fine one at first and then a broader that laid a band about 3 millimetres (0.1 inches) wide; filling in the rest of the surface was mechanical work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inner detail was put in with a brush rather than cut with a graver, in relief lines for the firm markings, gloss thinned to a warm brown for the softer ones, and masses of solid black for hair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A fragment at Sèvres catches the sequence half done, its figures finished and bordered in black while the ground was never filled in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What it bought was a subtlety of drawing no graver could reach, and the painters who took it up were draughtsmen before anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some workshops hedged for a while, and bilingual vases carry one technique on each side: an amphora in Boston of about 530 BCE has Achilles and Ajax at their game in red-figure on one face and in black-figure on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rivalry the new drawing set off is written on the pots themselves, and an amphora signed by Euthymides carries beside its revellers the boast that Euphronios never managed anything like it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Black-figure went on being made in quantity into the early 5th century, but the younger painters preferred the new method, and the old one was left to a few conservative uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Red-figure pots were still being made and exported in the 4th century, by which time Athens was no longer the centre it had been and the busiest workshops lay in the Greek cities of southern Italy and Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 221–23, 400–402, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The History of Greek Vases: Potters, Painters and Pictures</i>, by John Boardman, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.23/. [Open access]","J. Michael Padgett, review of <i>The Colors of Clay: Special Techniques in Athenian Vases</i>, by Beth Cohen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.03.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.09/. [Open access]","“Boston 01.8037 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Boston+01.8037&object=Vase. [Open access]","“Munich 2307 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Munich+2307&object=Vase. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Kylix_with_crowned_athlete%2C_Antiphon_painter%2C_Greek%2C_Attic%2C_c._485_BC%2C_red-figure_technique_on_earthenware_-_Krannert_Art_Museum%2C_UIUC_-_DSC06524.jpg/1920px-Kylix_with_crowned_athlete%2C_Antiphon_painter%2C_Greek%2C_Attic%2C_c._485_BC%2C_red-figure_technique_on_earthenware_-_Krannert_Art_Museum%2C_UIUC_-_DSC06524.jpg","title":"Attic red-figure kylix, Antiphon Painter, about 485 BCE","alt":"A shallow two-handled drinking cup seen from above on a museum stand, its inside black except for a round panel where a young man stands in the orange colour of the clay, a cloak over one arm and a staff in his hand, his body and drapery drawn in fine dark lines, the panel ringed by a meander border","desc":"Kylix with crowned athlete, Antiphon Painter, Greek, Attic, about 485 BCE, red-figure technique on earthenware. Exhibit in the Krannert Art Museum, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign. Daderot, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kylix_with_crowned_athlete,_Antiphon_painter,_Greek,_Attic,_c._485_BC,_red-figure_technique_on_earthenware_-_Krannert_Art_Museum,_UIUC_-_DSC06524.jpg"}},
{"id":"geo-001","num":1,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"California","answerText":"California","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 September 1850</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31st state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"California"},"facts":[["Capital","Sacramento"],["Population","39,431,263 (2024)"],["Largest city","Los Angeles"],["Total area","423,967 km² (163,695 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Flag_of_California.svg","credit":"Donald Graeme Kelley (design), vectorised by Devin Cook, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_California.svg)","alt":"The flag of California: a brown grizzly bear walking on a patch of green grass, with a red star above it at the hoist, the words CALIFORNIA REPUBLIC beneath, and a red band along the bottom."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>California</b> is a Pacific coast state of the United States and the most populous of the fifty, with a resident population the Census Bureau put at 39.4 million in July 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is more than eight million ahead of Texas, the next largest, and roughly one American in eight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its total area of 423,967 square kilometres (163,695 square miles) makes it the third largest state by extent, behind only Alaska and Texas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground runs from a long Pacific shoreline through the farmed floor of the Central Valley to the granite of the Sierra Nevada, and drops away in the south-east into the desert basin of Death Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That spread of landscapes shows in what the federal government protects there, from the Channel Islands off the southern coast to Joshua Tree in the deserts and Lassen Volcanic among the northern peaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Gold was found in the Sierra foothills in the late 1840s, and the rush that followed brought migrants from across the country and from overseas in numbers that reshaped the territory within a few years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> California was admitted to the Union as the thirty-first state on 9 September 1850, having skipped the long territorial apprenticeship most states served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the early 1960s it had overtaken New York to become the most populous state, a position it has held ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its economy grew unusually broad rather than resting on one staple, taking in diversified agriculture, fisheries, forestry and mining alongside aircraft plants, shipyards, tourism, the film industry and the technology sector now identified with Silicon Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sacramento, in the northern Central Valley, is the seat of state government, while Los Angeles is by a wide margin the largest city at about 3.9 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"California: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/california-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"California,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ca/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Tunnel_View%2C_Yosemite_Valley%2C_Yosemite_NP_-_Diliff.jpg/1920px-Tunnel_View%2C_Yosemite_Valley%2C_Yosemite_NP_-_Diliff.jpg","title":"Yosemite Valley, California","desc":"Yosemite Valley.","credit":"Diliff, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tunnel_View,_Yosemite_Valley,_Yosemite_NP_-_Diliff.jpg","alt":"A view of Yosemite Valley."}},
{"id":"geo-002","num":2,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Texas","answerText":"Texas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 December 1845</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Texas"},"facts":[["Capital","Austin"],["Population","31,290,831 (2024)"],["Largest city","Houston"],["Total area","695,662 km² (268,596 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Flag_of_Texas.svg","credit":"Paul B. Joiner, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Texas.svg)","alt":"The flag of Texas: a blue vertical band at the hoist bearing a single white star, beside a white bar above a red bar."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Texas</b> is the largest state of the United States outside Alaska, covering 695,662 square kilometres (268,596 square miles) in the south-centre of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is also the second most populous, with 31.3 million residents in July 2024 and a gap of some eight million to California above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its border with Mexico runs along the Rio Grande, whose great bend the National Park Service protects as Big Bend National Park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The land falls from the Guadalupe Mountains and the high plains of the west, through the hill country of the centre, to the barrier islands of the Gulf coast at Padre Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Houston, with about 2.4 million people, is the largest city, ahead of San Antonio and Dallas, while the seat of government is at Austin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Texas declared independence from Mexico in 1836 and then spent nearly a decade as a sovereign nation, the Republic of Texas, with its own president, its own currency and its own foreign legations, before it joined the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was admitted as the twenty-eighth state on 29 December 1845, the only one to enter the Union directly from independent statehood rather than by graduating from a federal territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ceremonial transfer of government followed on 19 February 1846, when Anson Jones, the last president of the republic, handed authority to James Pinckney Henderson, the first governor of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That short national history left marks the state has never given up, from the lone star of its flag to the standing of the Alamo in its public memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cattle, cotton, oil and gas built the modern economy in turn, and the Gulf coast around Houston remains one of the great concentrations of refining and petrochemical industry anywhere in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Clinton Drake, \"Texas: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 28 November 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/texas-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Texas,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/tx/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Canyon%2C_Rio_Grande%2C_Texas.jpeg/1920px-Canyon%2C_Rio_Grande%2C_Texas.jpeg","title":"Big Bend National Park, Texas","desc":"Big Bend National Park.","credit":"Michael from Minnesota (@Michael), CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canyon,_Rio_Grande,_Texas.jpeg","alt":"A view of Big Bend National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-003","num":3,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Florida","answerText":"Florida","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 March 1845</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Florida"},"facts":[["Capital","Tallahassee"],["Population","23,372,215 (2024)"],["Largest city","Jacksonville"],["Total area","170,312 km² (65,758 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Flag_of_Florida.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Florida.svg)","alt":"The flag of Florida: a red diagonal cross on a white field, with the state seal at the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Florida</b> is the long peninsula at the south-eastern corner of the United States, dividing the Atlantic Ocean from the Gulf of Mexico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the twenty-seventh state on 3 March 1845 and is now the most populous of the south-eastern states and the second most populous southern state after Texas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Census Bureau counted 23.4 million residents in July 2024, and the state has for years ranked among those with the fastest-growing immigrant populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its total area of 170,312 square kilometres (65,758 square miles) includes an unusually large share of water, some 12,000 square kilometres (4,700 square miles) more than its land area alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital is Tallahassee, in the north-western panhandle, a long way from the peninsula where most of the population lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Low, flat and wet, the southern half of the peninsula drains slowly southward through the sawgrass of the Everglades, which the National Park Service protects together with the neighbouring swamp of Big Cypress and its endangered panthers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Offshore the Dry Tortugas and Biscayne parks take in reefs, keys and shallows that are far more water than land, and the barrier beaches of Canaveral and Gulf Islands run along either coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Spanish settlers came early and left the oldest European masonry in the country: the fort of Castillo de San Marcos still stands at St. Augustine, with Fort Matanzas guarding the inlet to its south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The climate and the scenery of the Sunshine State have long drawn visitors in enormous numbers, and tourism has overtaken agriculture and manufacturing as the main component of the economy, with the growing service sector drawing migrants in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jacksonville, at just over a million people, is the largest city by population, while Miami anchors the far larger metropolitan area at the peninsula's south-eastern tip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Florida: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/florida-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Florida,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/fl/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sunset_over_the_River_of_Grass%2C_NPSphoto%2C_G.Gardner_%289255157507%29.jpg/1920px-Sunset_over_the_River_of_Grass%2C_NPSphoto%2C_G.Gardner_%289255157507%29.jpg","title":"the Everglades, Florida","desc":"Everglades National Park.","credit":"Everglades NPS from Homestead, Florida, United States, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunset_over_the_River_of_Grass,_NPSphoto,_G.Gardner_(9255157507).jpg","alt":"A view of Everglades National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-004","num":4,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Rhode Island","answerText":"Rhode Island","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 May 1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Rhode Island"},"facts":[["Capital","Providence"],["Population","1,112,308 (2024)"],["Largest city","Providence"],["Total area","4,001 km² (1,545 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Flag_of_Rhode_Island.svg","credit":"Xrmap flag collection 2.7, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rhode_Island.svg)","alt":"The flag of Rhode Island: a gold anchor on white above a blue ribbon reading HOPE, ringed by thirteen gold stars."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Rhode Island</b> is the smallest state of the United States, tucked into the New England coast between Connecticut and Massachusetts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its total area is 4,001 square kilometres (1,545 square miles), of which only 2,678 square kilometres (1,034 square miles) is land — the rest is Narragansett Bay and the coastal waters that give the Ocean State its nickname.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 1.11 million people live there, so that despite its size it is a long way from being the least populous state, and it is among the most densely settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bay cuts deep inland from the south, breaking the state into a mainland and a scatter of islands and peninsulas, and putting a great deal of shoreline into a very small compass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Providence stands at the head of that bay and is both the capital and the largest city, with roughly 195,000 residents of the state's 1.11 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The colony began as a refuge rather than as a venture: Roger Williams, banished from the Massachusetts Bay colony for his opinions and sheltered by the region's Native peoples, founded Providence in 1636.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He argued that to molest any person for professing a doctrine or practising a worship merely religious is to persecute them, and the town was built to put that principle into practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tolerance that followed drew others, and Touro Synagogue at Newport, which the National Park Service maintains as a historic site, is the oldest surviving synagogue building in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rhode Island was the last of the thirteen original states to ratify the federal constitution, holding out until 29 May 1790 over its suspicion of central power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the 19th century the fast water of the Blackstone valley turned the first American textile mills, and the National Park Service treats that corridor as the birthplace of the country's industrial revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Rhode Island: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/rhode-island-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Rhode Island,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ri/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Breakers_01.jpg","title":"The Breakers, Rhode Island","desc":"The Breakers.","credit":"User:Itub, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Breakers_01.jpg","alt":"A view of The Breakers."}},
{"id":"geo-504","num":504,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Providence","answerText":"Providence","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1636</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1900</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Rhode Island","dot":"Providence"},"facts":[["State","Rhode Island"],["Population","194,706 (2024)"],["Largest city","Providence"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Flag_of_Providence%2C_Rhode_Island.png","credit":"City of Providence, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Providence,_Rhode_Island.png)","alt":"The flag of Providence: the city seal in gold and blue on a dark blue field, showing a scene captioned WHAT CHEER? with figures greeting a canoe."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Providence</b> is the capital of Rhode Island and its largest city, standing at the head of Narragansett Bay where the Providence River meets the tidewater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roughly 195,000 people live within the city itself<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, about a sixth of the state's 1.11 million, in a state small enough that almost all of it lies within an hour's drive of here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town was founded in 1636 by Roger Williams, who had been banished from the Massachusetts Bay colony for his religious opinions and was sheltered by the region's Native peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He settled at a place the Narragansett called Moshassuck and renamed it Providence, in acknowledgement of what he took to be a merciful deliverance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The National Park Service keeps a memorial on the site of the freshwater spring around which the first houses of Providence Plantations gathered, a four-and-a-half acre greenspace at the foot of College Hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Williams held that to molest any person, Jew or Gentile, for professing a doctrine or practising a worship merely religious is to persecute them, and he made the place a shelter for those distressed in conscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That principle, radical in 17th-century New England, drew dissenters of many kinds and shaped the colony's later reputation for religious liberty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For most of its history the state had no single capital at all, and its legislature moved between several towns before Providence took the role permanently at the opening of the present State House in 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 19th-century city grew on textiles, jewellery and silverware, trades fed by the water power of the Blackstone valley just to the north, which the National Park Service treats as the birthplace of American industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its harbour and its universities have kept it the commercial and cultural centre of the state as those mills have closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Rhode Island: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/rhode-island-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"History &amp; Culture,\" Roger Williams National Memorial, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/rowi/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Rhode Island,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ri/index.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Aerial_view_of_downtown_Providence%2C_September_2015.JPG/1920px-Aerial_view_of_downtown_Providence%2C_September_2015.JPG","title":"Providence","desc":"Providence, Rhode Island, seen from a distance.","credit":"Pi.1415926535, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_downtown_Providence,_September_2015.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Providence, Rhode Island."}},
{"id":"cnh-001","num":"1","category":"China · History","question":"Because it never produced an epic, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives only in pieces — a few lines in a geography here, a passing allusion in a ritual compendium there.","answer":"Chinese mythology","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chief sources</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 BCE – 100 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pangu myth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd century CE</span></div>","traditional":"中國神話","hanzi":"中国神话","pinyin":"Zhōngguó shénhuà","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chinese mythology</b> was never handed down as a single work: it survives as material scattered through texts written for other purposes, among them geographies, ritual compendia and philosophical treatises. Chinese literature is unusual in having no epic or narrative genre at its origin, and mythology appears in it only sporadically and in an entirely fragmentary state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sources are infinitely divided and dispersed, so a figure may be known from a few lines in one book and an allusion in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The richest single repertory is the <i>Shanhaijing</i>, or <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i>, one of several compilations made as imperial China took shape, around the turn of the era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It alone is given over entirely to cosmography; the <i>Huainanzi</i>, the <i>Lüshi chunqiu</i> and the <i>Liji</i> carry their myth alongside ritual, statecraft and natural philosophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What they have least of is a creation story: a world made by a creator standing outside it never took hold in China, and the poverty of early accounts of the world’s origin has long puzzled scholars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pangu, the giant from whose body the world is formed, is the best known of the few and a late one, first written down in the 3rd century CE, after most of the figures he appears to precede.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That poverty is disputed, though: a silk manuscript from a Chu tomb at Changsha has Fuxi and Nüwa beget four gods who part heaven from earth and set the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The flood is the best-attested early story, and no deluge: at least six versions can be told apart in the <i>Book of Documents</i> alone, local traditions of a great inundation and of setting the world in order, none saying where the water came from or laying blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In China proper Nüwa fights the water and mends the world, while among peoples of the south a brother and sister survive to found a new humanity; of ancient Chinese belief little else reaches us but through tomb painting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chinese mythology","questions":["The giant Pangu, from whose body the world is formed, is a latecomer to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, his earliest written versions dating only from the 3rd century CE.","No angry god sends the flood in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: the water is simply there, and Nüwa is remembered for fighting it and repairing the world afterwards."],"tags":["concept","mythology","religion","literature","china","text"],"difficulty":1,"sources":["Léon Vandermeersch, “Écriture et littérature en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 8 (1986): 53–66, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1986.927. [Open access]","Jean-Pierre Diény, “Mythologie et sinologie,” <i>Études chinoises</i> 9, no. 1 (1990): 129–50, https://doi.org/10.3406/etchi.1990.1120. [Open access]","Vera V. Dorofeeva-Lichtmann, “Conception of Terrestrial Organization in the Shan hai jing,” <i>Bulletin de l’École française d’Extrême-Orient</i> 82 (1995): 57–110, https://doi.org/10.3406/befeo.1995.2297. [Open access]","Marc Kalinowski, “Mythe, cosmogénèse et théogonie dans la Chine ancienne,” <i>L’Homme</i> 36, no. 137 (1996): 41–60, https://doi.org/10.3406/hom.1996.370035. [Open access]","Max Kaltenmark, “La légende de la ville immergée en Chine,” <i>Cahiers d’Extrême-Asie</i> 1 (1985): 1–10, https://doi.org/10.3406/asie.1985.855. [Open access]","Rémi Mathieu, “Le corbeau dans la mythologie de l’ancienne Chine,” <i>Revue de l’histoire des religions</i> 201, no. 3 (1984): 281–309, https://doi.org/10.3406/rhr.1984.4312. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/Anonymous-Fuxi_and_N%C3%BCwa.jpg","title":"Chinese mythology","desc":"Fuxi and Nüwa. Nüwa is on the left. Fuxi is her brother. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous-Fuxi_and_N%C3%BCwa.jpg","alt":"Fuxi and Nüwa"}},
{"id":"cnh-002","num":"2","category":"China · History","question":"Chinese tradition sets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the first dynasty, and no two ancient accounts agree on which culture heroes belong on the list.","answer":"Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yao’s accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2357 BCE or 2145 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanhuang annals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 8th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"三皇五帝","hanzi":"三皇五帝","pinyin":"Sān Huáng Wǔ Dì","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors</b> are the rulers Chinese tradition sets before the first dynasty, and no two ancient accounts agree on who they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian’s <i>Records of the Grand Historian</i> opens not with the sovereigns but with five emperors — Huangdi, Zhuanxu, Ku, Yao and Shun — his rule being to accept only what a trusted book did not contradict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By that rule he kept two treatises on the five emperors, which the <i>Chunqiu</i> and the <i>Guoyu</i> leave standing, and refused the <i>Shanhaijing</i> outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That book, compiled between the 4th and 1st centuries BCE, holds more myth by itself than all the other classics together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Three Sovereigns were set in front of his work eight centuries later by Sima Zhen, whose annals credit Fuxi with marriage rites, with nets for hunting and fishing, and with officials named after dragons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The eight-figure arrangement came out of five-phases cosmology: once the elements were held to generate one another rather than conquer, Huangdi could no longer stand first and three sovereigns were placed before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Zhen had to move Nüwa into Huangdi’s old place to manage it, and admitted the triad fitted the scheme badly, she and Fuxi both answering to wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two belong together in any case, and appear on 2nd-century-CE reliefs in Shandong as a man and a woman with their serpent tails entwined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Traditional chronologies put Yao’s accession at 2357 BCE and at 2145, and every disagreement between them falls before 841 BCE, the earliest year Chinese dating fixes securely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the reigns preserve is the mythic content of ancestral legend embedded in the Confucian classics,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and a conception of legitimate rule rooted in myth ran on from them through the whole of Chinese history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors","questions":["Sima Qian opened his history with only five of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a Tang commentator prefixed the other three to the book eight centuries afterwards.","Five-phases cosmology reshaped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when the elements were held to generate one another, so Huangdi lost first place and three predecessors were found for him."],"tags":["people","mythology","history","china","text"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxiii–clxxxv, cxcii and ccxv–ccxvi, and Annales des Trois Souverains, 3–7, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Vérité historique et langage de l’histoire en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 13–28, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.931. [Open access]","Jean Levi, review of Rémi Mathieu, <i>Étude sur la mythologie et l’ethnologie de la Chine ancienne</i>, <i>L’Homme</i> 27, no. 101 (1987): 146–48, https://www.persee.fr/doc/hom_0439-4216_1987_num_27_101_368774. [Open access]","Françoise Lauwaert, “Le saint, le boiteux et l’héritier. À propos de la fonction impériale en Chine,” <i>L’Homme</i> 38, no. 148 (1998): 79–98, https://doi.org/10.3406/hom.1998.370576. [Open access]","Marc Kalinowski, “Mythe, cosmogénèse et théogonie dans la Chine ancienne,” <i>L’Homme</i> 36, no. 137 (1996): 41–60, https://doi.org/10.3406/hom.1996.370035. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Yellow_emperor_Wu_Family_Shrine.jpg","title":"Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors","desc":"Rubbing of the relief on stone walls in Wu Family Shrine (ACE2nd century). The Yellow Emperor, one of the mythical Five Sovereigns. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_emperor_Wu_Family_Shrine.jpg","alt":"Yellow Emperor, Wu Family Shrine"}},
{"id":"cnh-003","num":3,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Heaven and earth lay mingled like a hen's egg until <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew inside it for 18,000 years, pushing the sky up by one <i>zhang</i> every day.","questions":["Chinese tradition holds that the sacred peaks were the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the sun and moon his eyes, and the human race the creatures that had lived on his body.","No text of the Han period or earlier mentions <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose creation story first appears in the 3rd century CE and was doubted by the very source that records it."],"answer":"Pangu","answerText":"Pangu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chaos endured</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18,000 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest witness</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wei Zhao, c. 204 – 273 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fullest account</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Xu Zheng, 3rd century CE</span></div>","traditional":"盤古","hanzi":"盘古","pinyin":"Pángǔ","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Pangu</b> is the giant of Chinese myth whose growth forced heaven and earth apart and whose body, at his death, became the features of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No surviving text of the Warring States, Qin or Han periods names him, and the first written versions belong to the 3rd century CE, more than a thousand years after Chinese records become continuously datable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest witness is a fragment of Wei Zhao's lost <i>Dongji</i>, preserved in a Tang Buddhist commentary, which reports what people commonly said and dismisses it as resting on no historical record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fullest early account is Xu Zheng's <i>Sanwu liji</i>: heaven and earth lay mingled like a hen's egg, Pangu grew inside for 18,000 years, and sky, earth and giant each gained a <i>zhang</i> a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That egg is the image of the <i>huntian</i> cosmology of his own century, which held that heaven encloses the earth as a shell encloses the yolk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A second strand, known best from the <i>Shuyi ji</i>, tells what became of him: his head made the sacred peaks, his eyes the sun and moon, his blood the rivers, and the creatures on his body the human race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its age is unsettled, and one work long quoted for it looks instead like a Daoist compilation assembled centuries after Xu Zheng.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Daoists of the Six Dynasties made Pangu a god in his own right, identifying him with Yuanshi tianzun, one of the three highest Daoist deities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His origin is disputed: the name has been read as a form of Panhu, a dog ancestor honoured in the south, and as an Indian import, while oral versions among Miao and Yao communities point to a life outside the written record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Official history kept its distance, since Sima Qian began with the Five Emperors and the Tang supplement on the Three Sovereigns passes him over, but private histories from the Song made him the first ruler, and his temples stand from Lingnan to Hebei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]","Wu Xiaodong, “The Rhinoceros Totem and Pangu Myth: An Exploration of the Archetype of Pangu,” <i>Oral Tradition</i> 16, no. 2 (2001): 364–80, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/16ii/Wu.pdf. [Open access]","Marc Kalinowski, “Mythe, cosmogénèse et théogonie dans la Chine ancienne,” <i>L’Homme</i> 36, no. 137 (1996): 41–60, https://doi.org/10.3406/hom.1996.370035. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxiii–clxxxv, and Annales des Trois Souverains, 3–7, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Vérité historique et langage de l’histoire en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 13–28, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.931. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/Pangu.jpg","title":"Pangu","desc":"A portrait of Pangu from the Sancai Tuhui, an illustrated encyclopedia compiled by Wang Qi and printed in 1609. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pangu.jpg","alt":"Woodblock portrait of Pangu, headed with his name in Chinese"}},
{"id":"cnh-004","num":4,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Carved on shrine walls and coffin lids across Han China, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Fuxi appear with human faces and the bodies of snakes, their tails twined together.","questions":["When the sky broke it was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who patched it, and she who is said to have made the first people by moulding them out of yellow earth.","To complete the Three Sovereigns a Tang commentator slipped <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between Fuxi and Shennong, then admitted the triad fitted the five phases badly."],"answer":"Nüwa","answerText":"Nüwa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Han tomb murals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">86 – 49 BCE, at Luoyang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wu Liang Shrine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd century CE, in Shandong</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buddhist jewel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">435 CE, at Pingcheng</span></div>","traditional":"女媧","hanzi":"女娲","pinyin":"Nǚwā","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Nüwa</b> is the goddess of Chinese myth who made the first people by moulding them out of yellow earth, and who patched the sky when it broke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is almost always shown beside Fuxi, the two with human faces and the bodies of snakes, their tails sometimes twined together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pair are among the figures most often carved in Han funerary art, on the walls of ancestral shrines, on stone coffins and across the domed ceilings of tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the Wu Liang Shrine in Shandong, Fuxi holds a carpenter's square for the order he brings the human world, and a small boy between them stands for the children the two give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet the two began apart: the texts that first name them keep them independent, and the <i>Huainanzi</i> is where they are first named in one passage, without their relationship being described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their pairing is credited to a change in Han religion that gave female deities male companions, and the couple were also recorded as the gods of the sun and the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In China proper she alone fights the flood and repairs the world, while among peoples of the south a brother and sister survive it to found a new humanity, and that pair is often Fuxi and Nüwa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A silk manuscript from a Chu tomb at Changsha has the two of them beget four gods who part heaven from earth, set the seasons and lay out the mountains and rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Her standing rose as she was written into the dynastic past: Ban Gu made her Fuxi's subordinate, Zheng Xuan raised her to one of the Three Sovereigns, and Sima Zhen slipped her between Fuxi and Shennong to complete that triad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Her image outlasted the Han, travelling to the tombs of Gansu and Turfan, and by 435 CE it had reached Pingcheng, where she and Fuxi hold a Buddhist wish-granting jewel between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Jinchao Zhao, “Integration and Transformation: A Study of the Sun and the Moon Depicted in the Imagery of Fuxi and Nüwa,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 13–45, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2019.7.2.13-45. [Open access]","Fan Zhang, “Chinese-Buddhist Encounter: Synthesis of Fuxi-Nüwa and Cintamani in Early Medieval Chinese Art,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 87–111, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2019.7.2.87-111. [Open access]","Max Kaltenmark, “La légende de la ville immergée en Chine,” <i>Cahiers d’Extrême-Asie</i> 1 (1985): 1–10, https://doi.org/10.3406/asie.1985.855. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, ccxiv–ccxv, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Xiaoyuncong_nvwa.jpg","title":"Nüwa","desc":"Nüwa, from Xiao Yuncong's illustrations to the Heavenly Questions, a Qing woodblock imprint held by the National Palace Museum, Taipei. Xiao Yuncong, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xiaoyuncong_nvwa.jpg","alt":"Woodblock print of Nüwa, serpent-tailed, rising among flames and broken rock"}},
{"id":"cnh-005","num":5,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Looking up at the sky and down at the earth, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have drawn the eight trigrams, three stacked lines each unbroken for yang or broken for yin.","questions":["A Tang commentator gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a snake's body, writing to replace knotted cords, the rite of marriage, and officials named after the dragon whose omen he received.","Tradition hands the 64 hexagrams to King Wen of Zhou and hands the eight trigrams under them to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who made the figures but not the book."],"answer":"Fuxi","answerText":"Fuxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 3322 BCE, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Han carvings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st – 2nd century CE, in Henan and Shandong</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tang supplement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sima Zhen, early 8th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"伏羲","hanzi":"伏羲","pinyin":"Fúxī","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Fuxi</b> is the culture hero of Chinese myth who is credited with the eight trigrams, and the figure with whom the received account of the Chinese past effectively begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Great Appendix to the <i>Book of Changes</i> has him look up at the forms in the sky, look down at the patterns on the earth, and study the markings of birds and beasts before he draws the figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A trigram is three stacked lines, each of them either unbroken for yang or broken for yin, and the eight combinations were taken to hold the workings of the whole world in shorthand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same passage credits him with knotting cords into nets for hunting and fishing, which is one of the explanations offered for his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Tang commentator gathers much more: he replaced knotted cords with writing, set the rite by which a man and a woman married, and named his officials after the dragon whose omen he had received.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That commentator also makes him the son of a woman who conceived after walking in a giant's footprints, gives him a snake's body and a human head, and has him succeed the fire-bringer Suiren.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tradition that hands him the trigrams stops short of handing him the book: the 64 hexagrams and the words hung on them went to King Wen of Zhou and his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He could also stand where Pangu stands, and one Han apocryphon opens its account of the parting of heaven and earth with him and never mentions Pangu at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Han funerary carving he holds a carpenter's square for the order he brings the human world, and he is nearly always shown beside Nüwa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two were separate in the texts that name them earliest, so the couple is a creation of the Han, whose image later travelled west to the tombs of Gansu and Turfan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 11–12, 382–83, 423, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, 5–7, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Jinchao Zhao, “Integration and Transformation: A Study of the Sun and the Moon Depicted in the Imagery of Fuxi and Nüwa,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 13–45, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2019.7.2.13-45. [Open access]","Fan Zhang, “Chinese-Buddhist Encounter: Synthesis of Fuxi-Nüwa and Cintamani in Early Medieval Chinese Art,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 87–111, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2019.7.2.87-111. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/%E4%BC%8F%E7%BE%B2%E6%B0%8F%E7%95%AB%E5%85%AB%E5%8D%A6%EF%BC%88%E5%BB%BF%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%B2%E9%80%9A%E4%BF%97%E8%A1%8D%E7%BE%A9%EF%BC%89.jpg","title":"Fuxi draws the eight trigrams","desc":"Fuxi draws the eight trigrams, an illustration from the Nianyishi tongsu yanyi, a popular retelling of the dynastic histories printed in 1736. Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BC%8F%E7%BE%B2%E6%B0%8F%E7%95%AB%E5%85%AB%E5%8D%A6%EF%BC%88%E5%BB%BF%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%B2%E9%80%9A%E4%BF%97%E8%A1%8D%E7%BE%A9%EF%BC%89.jpg","alt":"Woodblock print of Fuxi at a table bearing the eight-trigram diagram, a dragon-horse rising from the river below"}},
{"id":"cnh-006","num":6,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Wood cut into a share and bent into a handle: <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> taught ploughing and weeding to all under heaven, and set the market at midday so that each got what he wanted.","questions":["The herbal that bears the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lists 365 drugs, one for each day of the year, though its contents show it was compiled under the Han.","With the body of a man and the head of an ox, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reigned by the power of fire and rules the three months of summer in the ritual calendar."],"answer":"Shennong","answerText":"Shennong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 BCE or earlier, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">120 years, then buried at Changsha</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Herbal ascribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">365 drugs, compiled under the Han</span></div>","traditional":"神農","hanzi":"神农","pinyin":"Shénnóng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Shennong</b>, the Divine Husbandman, is the culture hero of Chinese myth who taught farming, and he stands second among the Three Sovereigns in the commonest reckoning of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Great Appendix to the <i>Book of Changes</i> says that after Fuxi died he arose, cut wood into a ploughshare and bent wood into a plough-handle, and taught ploughing and weeding to all under heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same passage gives him the midday market, where people brought their goods together, made their exchanges and withdrew, each having got what he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It presents both as ideas taken from the hexagrams rather than as inventions of his own wit, which is how that text accounts for most of what the early sovereigns did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Tang commentator supplies the rest of the portrait: he had the body of a man and the head of an ox, reigned by the power of fire, and named his officials after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That account has him whip the plants and trees with a red lash, test the hundred kinds of plant himself, and so find the first drugs that cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that tasting comes the <i>Shennong bencao jing</i>, the herbal bearing his name, which set out 365 drugs, one for each day of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The book is not his: its own contents show it was put together under the Han, and what is known of its drugs survives only in quotation inside a much later materia medica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ritual calendar sets him in the shape of the year as well, making him the divine ruler of the three months of summer, the season whose element is fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He is the one figure every version of the Three Sovereigns keeps, whether the other two are Fuxi and Huangdi, or Suiren and Fuxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, 13–15, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 383, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","Emil Bretschneider, <i>Botanicon Sinicum</i>, part 3, <i>Botanical Investigations into the Materia Medica of the Ancient Chinese</i> (Shanghai: Kelly and Walsh, 1895), 1–3, https://archive.org/details/botaniconsinicu01bretgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Lî Kî</i>, Sacred Books of the East 27 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1885), 268, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0027unse. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Xu_Jetian_-_Shennong.jpg","title":"Shennong","desc":"Shennong chewing a sprig of herbs, an eighteenth-century ink painting on silk held by the Bibliothèque nationale de France; the inscription names him Yandi, king by the virtue of fire. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xu_Jetian_-_Shennong.jpg","alt":"Ink painting of a horned Shennong, dressed in leaves, chewing a sprig of herbs"}},
{"id":"cnh-007","num":7,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Bears, leopards, panthers, lynxes and tigers were trained by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who beat Yandi at Banquan in three engagements before the lords made him Son of Heaven.","questions":["Sima Qian opened his history on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than anyone older, saying that what the hundred schools told of him was not fit for a gentleman to repeat.","The ritual calendar puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the centre between the four seasons, ruling by the power of earth, whose colour is the one his name carries."],"answer":"Yellow Emperor","answerText":"Yellow Emperor","answerDate":"","traditional":"黃帝","hanzi":"黄帝","pinyin":"Huángdì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Yellow Emperor</b>, Huangdi, is the figure with whom Chinese history was made to begin: Sima Qian opened the <i>Shiji</i> on the Annals of the Five Emperors, and the first of the five is he.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He is given as a son of Shaodian, of the family Gongsun, and called Xuanyuan after the hill where his mother bore him, having conceived on seeing lightning circle the Great Bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his time the house of Shennong had grown weak and the lords fought one another, so they took refuge with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He trained bears, leopards, panthers, lynxes and tigers, beat Yandi at Banquan in three engagements and destroyed Chiyou at Zhuolu, and the lords then raised him to Son of Heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name is a colour: the omen of his reign was the power of earth, whose colour is yellow, and he named his officials after the clouds as Fuxi had named his after dragons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The ritual calendar sets him at the centre, between the four seasons, where the emperor wears yellow robes, rides yellow horses and eats from round vessels made to resemble the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Great Appendix passes over him quickly, granting him with Yao and Shun only the upper and lower garments, the canoe and the oar, the ox and the horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sima Qian left out the inventions the legends heap on his reign, and said why: the <i>Book of Documents</i> records only from Yao onward, and what the hundred schools told of Huangdi was not fit for a gentleman to repeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Every ruler after him in that chapter is his descendant, Zhuanxu his grandson and Ku his great-grandson, then Yao and Shun, so the whole line was made to begin in one man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of it is dated, and Sima Qian knew it: he let his exact chronology reach back only to 841 BCE and counted by generations before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxvii–cxc, and 25–39, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 383–85, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Lî Kî</i>, Sacred Books of the East 27 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1885), 280–81, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0027unse. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Vérité historique et langage de l’histoire en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 13–28, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.931. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Yellow_Emperor_%28Huangdi%29.jpg","title":"The Yellow Emperor","desc":"Portraits of Famous Men: Yellow Emperor (Huangdi), ink and colour on silk mounted as an album leaf, by an unknown Chinese artist, held by the Philadelphia Museum of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Yellow_Emperor_(Huangdi).jpg","alt":"Album-leaf portrait of the Yellow Emperor in the flat imperial crown with beaded tassels"}},
{"id":"cnh-008","num":8,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"The <i>Book of Documents</i> says that, according to the teachings of antiquity, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first to produce disorder, which spread until the quiet people became robbers and murderers.","questions":["Sima Qian allows only four lines to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who was the most terrible and remained invincible until the Yellow Emperor seized him and put him to death at Zhuolu.","A lost book quoted by a Tang scholar gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 81 brothers with beasts' bodies, heads of bronze and foreheads of iron, who ate sand and forged the first weapons."],"answer":"Chiyou","answerText":"Chiyou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2700 – 2600 BCE, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Sima Qian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 lines, then killed at Zhuolu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In legend</span><span class=\"dt-v\">81 brothers with heads of bronze</span></div>","traditional":"蚩尤","hanzi":"蚩尤","pinyin":"Chīyóu","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Chiyou</b> is the rebel the Yellow Emperor destroyed at Zhuolu, and the oldest name the <i>Book of Documents</i> records: it says that, according to the teachings of antiquity, he was the first to produce disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That disorder spread among the quiet, orderly people until all became robbers and murderers, and among the people of Miao the five punishments were turned into engines of oppression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> James Legge noted that no name older than Yao is mentioned anywhere else in that collection, and that so cursory a reference is itself evidence it is a genuine record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sima Qian allows him four lines: while Xuanyuan drilled the lords with shield and lance, Chiyou was the most terrible and remained invincible, until he made trouble, disobeyed the imperial orders, and was seized and killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he was is never said, and the <i>Rites of Dai the Elder</i> calls him an ambitious man sprung from the people, where Chavannes read Sima Qian's own wording to mean a lord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Everything monstrous about him is later, and reaches us in fragments, Chinese myth having survived sporadically and in pieces rather than as epic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Tang scholar Zhang Shoujie quotes a lost book for 81 brothers all called Chiyou, with beasts' bodies and human voices, heads of bronze and foreheads of iron, who ate sand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They made the five kinds of weapon, swords and lances and great crossbows, and killed without reason, where the Great Appendix to the <i>Book of Changes</i> gives the bow and arrow to the sages as a benefit producing awe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In that same account the Yellow Emperor could not put him down by goodness and justice, so he sighed at heaven, which sent down a dark woman with a sealed writing on war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He stayed useful after death: his image was drawn to frighten the empire into obedience, and Tang, founder of Shang, named his rebellion as the precedent for punishing lords who would not serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 27–29, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 27 and 255, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 384–85, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), 190, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Écriture et littérature en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 8 (1986): 53–66, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1986.927. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%9C%E7%9C%81%E6%B2%82%E5%8D%97%E5%8E%BF_%E6%B1%89%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F%E7%9F%B3_%E8%9A%A9%E5%B0%A4%E4%BA%94%E5%85%B5.jpg","title":"Chiyou and the five weapons","desc":"Rubbing of a Han pictorial stone from Yinan, Shandong, showing Chiyou with the five weapons: a bow and arrows above his head, blades in both hands and swords sprouting from his limbs. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%9C%E7%9C%81%E6%B2%82%E5%8D%97%E5%8E%BF_%E6%B1%89%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F%E7%9F%B3_%E8%9A%A9%E5%B0%A4%E4%BA%94%E5%85%B5.jpg","alt":"Rubbing of a Han stone relief: a squat beast-faced figure with a bow and arrows above his head and blades sprouting from his arms and legs"}},
{"id":"cnh-009","num":9,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the lords raised Xuanyuan to Son of Heaven in place of the house of Shennong, so the fight is what makes the Yellow Emperor emperor.","questions":["Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Yellow Emperor had already beaten Yandi in three engagements at Banquan, a field the old accounts put only a few li away.","Sima Qian gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two lines, saying only that Chiyou disobeyed, that the lords were called to arms, and that he was seized there and put to death."],"answer":"Battle of Zhuolu","answerText":"Battle of Zhuolu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in a reign of c. 2697 – 2599 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Two battles</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 rounds at Banquan, then Zhuolu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named as an omen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the Zuo Commentary, Duke Xi, year 25</span></div>","traditional":"涿鹿之戰","hanzi":"涿鹿之战","pinyin":"Zhuōlù zhī zhàn","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Zhuolu</b> is the fight in which the Yellow Emperor destroyed Chiyou, and in the <i>Shiji</i> it is the act that makes him emperor: the lords raised Xuanyuan to Son of Heaven after it, in place of the house of Shennong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian allows it two lines: Chiyou made trouble and disobeyed the imperial orders, so Huangdi called the lords to arms, gave battle in the country of Zhuolu, seized him and put him to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the second of a pair, easily run together, since he had already beaten Yandi in three engagements at Banquan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fields are neighbours, Banquan being the spring of Mount Ban, one li from Zhuolu by some accounts and five by others, and Zhuolu itself was Huangdi's capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commentators place it south of Baoan in Xuanhua, in what is now Hebei, on a headwater of the Yongding river, in a reign some chronologies put between 2697 and 2599 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Neither the <i>Book of Documents</i> nor the <i>Book of Songs</i> so much as names Huangdi, and what the Documents does notice is the loser, saying that according to the teachings of antiquity Chiyou was the first to produce disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting is old in the record all the same: a diviner in the <i>Zuo Commentary</i> consults the tortoise-shell for the marquis of Jin and calls the answer auspicious, being the omen of the Yellow Emperor's battle at Banquan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was therefore famous long before Sima Qian wrote, and Chavannes concluded that the legends of this sovereign are ancient whatever the classics omit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Legge observed that other writers tell the struggle quite differently, with dragons, mists and the invention of the compass playing conspicuous parts, which is the sort of material Chinese myth preserves only in fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Victory brought no rest: Huangdi hunted down whoever would not submit, cut passages through the mountains, opened roads, and went east to the sea, west to Kongtong and north against the Xunyu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 27–31, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 27 and 255, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ew, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), 195–96, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.60723. [Open access]","Térence Billeter, “Un ancêtre légendaire au service du nationalisme chinois,” <i>Perspectives chinoises</i> 47 (1998): 46–53, https://doi.org/10.3406/perch.1998.2298. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Écriture et littérature en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 8 (1986): 53–66, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1986.927. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/%E6%B2%B3%E5%8C%97%E6%B6%BF%E9%B9%BF%E2%80%94%E9%BB%84%E5%B8%9D%E5%9F%8E_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The plain at Zhuolu","desc":"The plain and hills at Zhuolu in Hebei, where the battle is traditionally placed, seen from the terrace of the modern memorial hall built there to the Yellow Emperor. 中国郑开亮, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B2%B3%E5%8C%97%E6%B6%BF%E9%B9%BF%E2%80%94%E9%BB%84%E5%B8%9D%E5%9F%8E_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A paved terrace with a bronze incense burner and rows of banners, looking out over a wooded plain to a long line of hills"}},
{"id":"cnh-010","num":10,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"The <i>Book of Documents</i> opens on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who is called reverential, intelligent, accomplished and thoughtful, naturally and without effort, his bright influence reaching all four quarters of the land.","questions":["Told by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a round year is 366 days, the Xi and He brothers were to square it with the four seasons by an intercalary month.","Seventy years on the throne, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put aside his own son as insincere and quarrelsome and took Shun, an unmarried man among the lower people, in his place."],"answer":"Yao","answerText":"Yao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Received date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">accession 2357 BCE, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70 years before seeking a successor</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Year length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">366 days, with an intercalary month</span></div>","traditional":"堯","hanzi":"尧","pinyin":"Yáo","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Yao</b> is the sage-king with whom the <i>Book of Documents</i> begins, and the oldest ruler it describes: its first chapter, the Canon of Yao, opens by examining into antiquity and finding him styled Fangxun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is reverential, intelligent, accomplished and thoughtful, naturally and without effort, the bright influence of those qualities felt through the four quarters and above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He harmonised the nine classes of his kindred, polished his own people and united the myriad states, which is rule by example: the Great Appendix to the <i>Book of Changes</i> says he and Shun simply wore their robes and good order followed everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sima Qian's portrait is more concrete: goodness like heaven's, knowledge a god's, near at hand like the sun and from afar like a cloud, in a yellow cap and a plain robe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The one office the Canon gives him is the calendar: he set the Xi and He brothers at the four quarters to watch the sun, moon and stars, and told them a round year is 366 days, squared by an intercalary month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Then the flood, whose waters in their vast extent embrace the hills and overtop the great heights, with Gun employed over Yao's own objection, laboured nine years without accomplishing the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seventy years on the throne, Yao sought a successor, put his own son aside as insincere and quarrelsome, and took Shun, an unmarried man among the lower people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That refusal is what the tradition kept: Confucius called him great as a sovereign, matched only by heaven, his virtue so vast the people could find no name for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Mencius</i> denies it was Yao's to give, answering that the sovereign cannot give the throne to another and that heaven gave it to Shun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The one testable thing in the Canon is the stars, since precession was unknown there until long after Yao's supposed date and the four culminating stars he names could not have been computed backwards; they fit the 24th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 24–28 and 32–39, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 42–74, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 383–84, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 78, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 354, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Ma_Lin_-_Emperor_Yao.jpg/1280px-Ma_Lin_-_Emperor_Yao.jpg","title":"Emperor Yao","desc":"Yao as imagined by the Song painter Ma Lin, a hanging scroll in colour on silk in the National Palace Museum, Taipei; the inscription calls his virtue lofty and says he governed by letting his robes hang down. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ma_Lin_-_Emperor_Yao.jpg","alt":"Painted hanging scroll of a bearded man in a red and dark robe and a small black cap, one hand raised, with an inscription in the silk above him"}},
{"id":"cnh-011","num":11,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"The <i>Book of Documents</i> gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a canon of his own, opening on a man styled Chonghua who was profound, wise, accomplished and intelligent, mild and courteous and truly sincere.","questions":["Sent to plaster the granary roof, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came down on two broad rush hats when his father set fire to it beneath him, and later escaped a filled-in well by a side passage.","Confucius asked whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> might not be instanced as having governed efficiently without exertion, doing nothing but gravely and reverently occupying his imperial seat."],"answer":"Shun","answerText":"Shun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Received date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">accession 2255 BCE, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Called to office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at 30, then 30 years with Yao</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Banished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 criminals, to the four frontiers</span></div>","traditional":"舜","hanzi":"舜","pinyin":"Shùn","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Shun</b> is the commoner Yao raised to the throne, and the <i>Book of Documents</i> gives him a canon of his own, opening on a man styled Chonghua, profound, wise, accomplished and intelligent, mild and courteous and truly sincere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian makes the household murderous: his father Gusou blind, his mother dead, his half-brother Xiang arrogant, and Gusou forever seeking to kill him, while Shun served all three scrupulously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twice they tried in earnest: they fired the granary roof he was plastering, and he came down on two broad rush hats; they filled in a well he was digging, and he left by a side passage he had cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is also the man things gathered around: wherever he settled, a village formed in a year, a town in two and a city in three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yao tested him with his own two daughters, and in the great plains at the foot of the mountains, where the tempests of wind, thunder and rain did not make him go astray.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The canon then reads like an inventory of government: he regulated the pitch-pipes and the measures of length, capacity and weight, divided the land into twelve provinces, and toured the four quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He banished four criminals to the frontiers, among them Gun, who had failed for nine years against the flood and was held prisoner till death, and all under heaven acknowledged the justice of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He appointed the ministers the tradition remembers by name, Yu over the works, Qi over the fields, Gao Yao over crime and Kui over the music, and had them examined every three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Confucius made him the model of effortless rule, asking whether Shun might not be instanced as governing efficiently without exertion, doing nothing but gravely occupying his seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Mencius</i> says plainly that he was born near the wild tribes of the east, and the Bamboo Books, recovered from a tomb in the 3rd century CE, date his succession to Yao's 73rd year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 38–44, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 12 and 72–83, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 159, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 316, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fb/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Emperor_Shun.jpg","title":"Emperor Shun","desc":"Portraits of Famous Men: Emperor Shun, an album leaf by an unknown Chinese artist, held by the Philadelphia Museum of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Emperor_Shun.jpg","alt":"Album-leaf portrait bust of Shun in the flat imperial crown with beaded tassels, in blue and green robes"}},
{"id":"cnh-012","num":12,"category":"Mythology","question":"The tradition credits <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with carriages on land, boats on water, sledges in icy places and spiked shoes for the hills, and with passing his own door three times without going in.","questions":["Shun is said to have handed the throne to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose survey divided the land into nine provinces and set each one's tribute by the quality of its soil.","When the princes went to the son of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than to the minister he had named, the sequence of abdications ended and the throne began passing from father to son."],"answer":"Yu the Great","answerText":"Yu the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Received date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Xia opens 2205 BCE, or 1989 BCE by another reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17, in both received systems</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Shû King</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 provinces surveyed and graded by soil</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Succeeded by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">his son Qi, not by Yi, who served 7 years</span></div>","traditional":"大禹","hanzi":"大禹","pinyin":"Dà Yǔ","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Yu the Great</b> is the figure on whom the Chinese mythical sequence closes and the dynastic record is said to open: the man who drained the flood, surveyed what was left and passed the throne to his son. The <i>Shû King</i> has him describing his own labours, in which he mounted four conveyances — carriages on land, boats on water, sledges in icy places and shoes spiked for the hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He opened passages for the streams throughout the nine provinces and led them to the sea, while one colleague taught the people to take fish and birds and another to sow grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is quoted saying that he stayed with his wife four days after marrying, and heard his infant son wailing without turning aside; the tradition adds that he passed his own door three times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Confucius could find no flaw in him: coarse food, poor clothing and a low house, with all his strength spent on the ditches and water channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The survey credited to him is the Tribute of Yü, which divides the land into nine provinces, grades the soil of each and fixes what it owes accordingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Zuo Commentary adds that at the height of Xia virtue the provinces sent in their metal, nine great cauldrons were cast, and the strange creatures of each region were figured on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then comes the break: Shun had presented Yu to Heaven and Yu presented his minister Yi, but when Yi withdrew after the mourning the princes went to Qi, saying he was their sovereign's son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Asked whether that meant Yu fell short of Yao and Shun, the <i>Mencius</i> answers that it did not — Yi had served seven years where the others served decades, Qi was able, and the change was Heaven's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The received chronologies open the Xia in 2205 or 1989 BCE and agree on seventeen kings, and Chavannes judged the whole stretch prehistory rather than history, rebuilt from scattered fragments with more art than science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 16–18 and 64–75, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 79, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Seuen, third year, 293, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 358–59, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), clxvi and cxc, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/King_Yu_of_Xia.jpg/1280px-King_Yu_of_Xia.jpg","title":"Yu the Great","desc":"Standing Portrait of King Yu of Xia, hanging scroll, colour on silk, by Ma Lin, 13th century. National Palace Museum, Taipei. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Yu_of_Xia.jpg","alt":"A standing figure in a long robe and a flat-topped ceremonial cap, holding a tablet, painted on silk."}},
{"id":"cnh-013","num":13,"category":"Mythology","question":"Snakes and dragons held the land in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and people nested in trees on the low ground and dug caves on the high until the channels were opened.","questions":["Gun laboured nine years without success in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and was banished to a mountain to die, after which his son took up the same task and finished it.","No ark appears in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: the waters are not a punishment to be survived but a disorder of the river channels, to be dug open and led away to the sea."],"answer":"Chinese flood myth","answerText":"Chinese flood myth","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Set in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the reigns of Yao and Shun, from 2357 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gun's attempt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 years, unaccomplished</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yu's labour</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 years by one account, 13 by another</span><span class=\"dt-k\">His own door</span><span class=\"dt-v\">passed 3 times, never entered</span></div>","traditional":"洪水神話","hanzi":"洪水神话","pinyin":"hóngshuǐ shénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Chinese flood myth</b> is the calamity that fills the reigns of Yao and Shun and closes the age of the sage rulers: a rising of the waters fought for a generation and at last drained away. The Canon of Yao has the sovereign asking for a man to correct it, describing waters that embrace the hills, overtop the great heights and threaten the heavens while the lower people groan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Mencius</i> adds what the land was like beneath them: snakes and dragons held it, and people nested in trees on the low ground and dug caves on the high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gun was put to the work over Yao's own objection that he rejected orders and was baleful to his colleagues, and after nine years the waters had not stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shun, inspecting, found his works unworthy of praise, banished him to Mount Yu to die there, and set his son to the same task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The remedy is not an ark but drainage: Yu dug open the obstructed channels and conducted the waters to the sea, driving the snakes and dragons into the marshes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Legge noted that the calamity is nowhere presented as a deluge or a sudden accumulation of water, but as the river channels being broken up and disordered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How long the work took is told differently: the <i>Mencius</i> has him eight years away from home, while the <i>Shiji</i> quotes the books of Xia for thirteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 19th-century missionaries pressed an identification with Noah's flood that Legge himself accepted only as an imperfect echo and that Bunsen dismissed outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the tradition builds on the draining is administrative rather than moral — the survey of the land, its division into nine provinces and the fixing of tribute — and Confucius remembered the man who did it as one who spent all his strength on ditches and water channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 32–35 and 64–75, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 250–51 and 279–80, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), cxxx and 99–100, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 2, 73–74, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 79, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/%E7%A6%B9%E7%8E%8B%E5%88%86%E4%BB%98%E8%A1%86%E5%B0%87%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4.jpg/1280px-%E7%A6%B9%E7%8E%8B%E5%88%86%E4%BB%98%E8%A1%86%E5%B0%87%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4.jpg","title":"Yu ordering the water works","desc":"Woodblock illustration from the Ming novel Youxia zhizhuan, 17th century, digitised in the Guben xiaoshuo jicheng. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%A6%B9%E7%8E%8B%E5%88%86%E4%BB%98%E8%A1%86%E5%B0%87%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4.jpg","alt":"Line woodcut in a circular frame: a seated ruler under a draped canopy gestures toward three kneeling officials, the panel title running down each side in characters."}},
{"id":"cnh-014","num":14,"category":"Mythology","question":"Relying on his archery, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> neglected the business of the people and gave himself to hunting, until the minister he had raised up turned the palace against him.","questions":["Two archers bear the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the record, one in the service of a legendary emperor and one who seized the throne of the Xia and then lost it.","Confucius's questioner named <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a man skilful at archery who did not die a natural death, setting him against Yu and Ji, who worked the fields and won the empire."],"answer":"Houyi","answerText":"Houyi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ku from 2436 BCE, then Tai Kang from 2188 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held the throne</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 years, by one account</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Put away</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 good officers, for 1 flatterer</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Confucius names</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 men who did not die naturally</span></div>","traditional":"后羿","hanzi":"后羿","pinyin":"Hòu Yì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Houyi</b> is the archer of Chinese legend, and the name is more a title than a person: it is borne by a bowman serving the legendary emperor Ku and again by a descendant under Yao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is that descendant who is remembered for shooting into the sky, freeing the moon from an eclipse and scattering the false suns that were spoiling the crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The classics carry another of the name, the prince of Qiong, who in the Songs of the Five Sons blocks the return of the Xia king Tai Kang from a hundred-day hunt beyond the Lo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Zuo Commentary tells it at length: he took advantage of the people's discontent to supersede the line of Xia, then relied on his archery, neglected their business and gave himself to the beasts of the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He put away four good officers and made his chief minister Zhuo of Han, a slanderous man whose own house had cast him out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Zhuo flattered within the palace and bribed without it, cajoled the people and encouraged the hunting, until inside and outside all were ready to have him instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Returning from the field one day he was killed by his own servants, who boiled him and gave his flesh to his sons; they could not eat it and died in the gate of Qiong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Zhuo took his wife and had by her the strong man Ao, and it ended only when Shao Kang was raised to the throne: the princes of Qiong perished, the commentary says, because they had lost the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Confucius made no reply when a questioner set that archery against the husbandry of Yu and Ji and observed that neither he nor Ao died a natural death, though he praised the man for esteeming virtue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Mencius</i> gives the killing instead to Feng Meng, a pupil who had learned the whole science and reckoned only his teacher above him — and holds the teacher partly to blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 268–69, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 78–79, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Seang, fourth year, 424, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 141, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 328, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4.Recueil_Historique_des_Principaux_Traits_de_la_Vie_des_Empereurs_Chinois.95%E5%B8%A7%E5%9B%BE.%E5%BD%A9%E7%BB%98%E5%86%8C%E9%A1%B5.%E7%BA%A618%E4%B8%96%E7%BA%AA_%28%E5%90%8E%E7%BE%BFcrop%29.jpg","title":"An archer in the hunt","desc":"Detail from an 18th-century painted album of exemplary imperial episodes, Recueil Historique des Principaux Traits de la Vie des Empereurs Chinois, Bibliotheque nationale de France. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4.Recueil_Historique_des_Principaux_Traits_de_la_Vie_des_Empereurs_Chinois.95%E5%B8%A7%E5%9B%BE.%E5%BD%A9%E7%BB%98%E5%86%8C%E9%A1%B5.%E7%BA%A618%E4%B8%96%E7%BA%AA_%28%E5%90%8E%E7%BE%BFcrop%29.jpg","alt":"Painted in colour on silk: a rider in a red robe on a white horse draws a bow and aims upward, a quiver at his back, with trees and other riders behind him."}},
{"id":"cnh-015","num":15,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"The oldest surviving telling of the story, in Zhang Heng's <i>Ling Xian</i>, has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> steal the drug of immortality from her husband Houyi and flee to the moon.","questions":["A naming taboo under two Tang emperors turned Heng into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the name by which the woman in the moon has been known ever since.","Round cakes sold at the mid-autumn festival were painted with a white rabbit and a lunar beauty, whom buyers named for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, said to have fled to the moon."],"answer":"Chang'e","answerText":"Chang'e","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ku from 2436 BCE, as the wife of Houyi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Set down by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Zhang Heng, 78 – 139 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Name changed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under 2 Tang emperors who bore Heng</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Festival</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th month, from the 11th day to the 15th</span></div>","traditional":"嫦娥","hanzi":"嫦娥","pinyin":"Cháng'é","translations":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Chang'e</b> is the woman who lives in the moon, and the oldest surviving account of how she came to be there makes her a thief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The astronomer Zhang Heng, who lived from 78 to 139 CE, wrote in his <i>Ling Xian</i> that when Houyi begged the drug of immortality from the Queen Mother of the West, Heng E stole it and fled for refuge to the mansion of the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Houyi was her husband and the drug was his, so the story turns on a theft inside a marriage rather than on any quest of her own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arriving, she was not rewarded but transformed, becoming the chanchu, the toad whose outline Chinese observers traced on the face of the moon and which was thought to swallow it during an eclipse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same legend is carried in the <i>Huainanzi</i>, so more than one Han work preserved it. <br><br> Her name was not always Chang'e: it was Heng, taken from a line in the ancient odes praising a lady as being like the moon advancing to the full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was altered because two Tang emperors bore Heng as a personal name, and a naming taboo then put the character out of ordinary use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around her the moon filled with other tenants: a hare squatting at the foot of a cassia tree and pounding drugs for the immortals, and a man named Wu Kang condemned to hew that tree for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cassia was said to be most visible at mid-autumn, so taking a degree in the examinations held at that season was described as plucking a leaf from the cassia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Fuzhou in the 1860s the festival of the middle of autumn ran from the eleventh to the fifteenth of the eighth month, and the round cakes baked for it were painted with a white rabbit and a lunar beauty, whom people named after an ancient woman said to have visited the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 94, 30–31, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, 288–89, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), nos. 55 and 140, 22 and 53–54, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Religious Portions of the Shih King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 248, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 300, 94–95, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 64–66, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/%E5%8F%A4%E4%BB%8A%E7%99%BE%E7%BE%8E%E5%9C%96%E4%B9%8B%E5%AB%A6%E5%A8%A5%EF%BC%88One_hundred_beauties_Chang%27e%EF%BC%89.png/1280px-%E5%8F%A4%E4%BB%8A%E7%99%BE%E7%BE%8E%E5%9C%96%E4%B9%8B%E5%AB%A6%E5%A8%A5%EF%BC%88One_hundred_beauties_Chang%27e%EF%BC%89.png","title":"Chang'e in the moon","desc":"Drawing by Wu Youru, died about 1893, from the album Gujin baimei tu, published by Hunan Fine Arts Publishing House. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%A4%E4%BB%8A%E7%99%BE%E7%BE%8E%E5%9C%96%E4%B9%8B%E5%AB%A6%E5%A8%A5%EF%BC%88One_hundred_beauties_Chang%27e%EF%BC%89.png","alt":"Line drawing: a woman in flowing robes stands among banked clouds inside the disc of the full moon, beside a flowering tree and a railed terrace, with an inscription in characters at the upper left."}},
{"id":"cnh-016","num":16,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"A giant who raced the sun, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drank the Yellow River and the Wei dry, set out north for the Great Marsh, and died of thirst on the road before he reached it.","questions":["The staff that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> threw down as he died became Deng Forest, which another chapter calls the Peach Forest, three hundred li around and full of horses.","Three characters added in a later chapter, that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not measure his strength, turn a giant keeping pace with the sun into one racing it and losing."],"answer":"Kuafu","answerText":"Kuafu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Yellow Emperor's age, c. 2697 BCE by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drank dry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 rivers, the Yellow River and the Wei</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deng Forest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">also called the Peach Forest, 300 li around</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Descended from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Gonggong, 3 generations back</span></div>","traditional":"夸父","hanzi":"夸父","pinyin":"Kuāfù","translations":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Kuafu</b> is a giant of Chinese myth who chased the sun and died of thirst, and his name describes rather than names him: kua means large and fu is a word for a man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> tells it in a few dozen characters: he ran with the sun and entered it, drank the Yellow River and the Wei dry, made north for the Great Marsh, died on the road, and his cast-off staff became Deng Forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same book makes him neither solitary nor obscure: he descends from Houtu, three generations below Gonggong, wears a yellow snake in each ear, and belongs to a country of large men east of Nie'er.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deng Forest is the Peach Forest that another chapter puts on Mount Kuafu, three hundred li around and full of horses, in the far west of modern Henan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yugu, where he overtakes the sun, is also Yuyuan, and the <i>Huainanzi</i>, assembled under the Han prince Liu An, who died in 122 BCE, says the sun reaching Yuyuan is what dusk means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The chapters of the great wilderness open the story with three characters the first telling lacks — that he did not measure his strength — which turn a man keeping pace with the sun into one racing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the usual reading now, though the older wording says only that he ran alongside the sun as far as Yugu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those chapters end it differently again: the winged dragon Yinglong, having killed Chiyou, killed Kuafu too, so the giant falls in the Yellow Emperor's war rather than to thirst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Liezi</i> carries the story as well, adding that the staff was soaked in the fat and flesh of the corpse before it grew, and that Deng Forest spread thousands of li.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That book's own standing is disputed: Herbert Giles called it a forgery of the 1st or 2nd century CE, while his son Lionel held that a genuine core lay under later accretions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Zhao Kuifu 赵逵夫, “‘夸父逐日’神话的历史文化内涵” [The historical and cultural content of the ‘Kuafu chases the sun’ myth], <i>文学遗产</i> [Literary Heritage] 2020, no. 5, https://ir.nwnu.edu.cn/bitstream/39RV6HYL/61950/1/%E2%80%9C%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E9%80%90%E6%97%A5%E2%80%9D%E7%A5%9E%E8%AF%9D%E7%9A%84%E5%8E%86%E5%8F%B2%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%86%85%E6%B6%B5.pdf. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 412, 132–33, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Sun Shulin 孫樹林, “从古籍中的黄帝神话传说记载管窥古人之宇宙观” [The cosmology of the ancients seen through records of the Yellow Emperor myths in the old books], <i>島根大学外国語教育センタージャーナル</i> 10 (2015): 67–78, https://ir.lib.shimane-u.ac.jp/31500. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A History of Chinese Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1901), 68–69, https://archive.org/details/cu31924086054776. [Open access]","Lionel Giles, trans., <i>Taoist Teachings from the Book of Lieh Tzŭ</i> (London: John Murray, 1912), 13–15, https://archive.org/details/taoistteachingsf00unse. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Spirits_and_the_Supernatural_-_pic47_-_%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E7%A5%9E%E5%9C%96.png/1280px-Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Spirits_and_the_Supernatural_-_pic47_-_%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E7%A5%9E%E5%9C%96.png","title":"Kuafu striding after the sun","desc":"Vectorised from an illustration in the Gujin Tushu Jicheng, the imperial encyclopaedia compiled between 1700 and 1725, in its section on spirits and the supernatural. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Spirits_and_the_Supernatural_-_pic47_-_%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E7%A5%9E%E5%9C%96.png","alt":"Line drawing: a barefoot giant strides through banked clouds towards the disc of the sun at the upper left, a snake threaded through his ears and another held in his raised hand, the title in characters down the right edge."}},
{"id":"cnh-017","num":17,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"The daughter of Yandi drowned in the Eastern Sea and came back as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a small bird that carries wood and stones from the western hills to dam it up.","questions":["Tao Yuanming wrote that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries small twigs meaning to fill the vast sea, which turned a drowned girl's bird into a figure for holding on.","On Mount Fajiu lives a bird shaped like a crow, with a patterned head, a white beak and red feet, called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because that is what its cry sounds like."],"answer":"Jingwei","answerText":"Jingwei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Her father</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Yandi, placed from 2737 BCE in 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The mountain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fajiu, where 1 river also rises</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the western hills to 1 sea, in the east</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Praised by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tao Yuanming, 365 – 427 CE</span></div>","traditional":"精衛","hanzi":"精卫","pinyin":"Jīngwèi","translations":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Jingwei</b> is a small bird of Chinese myth that carries twigs and pebbles out over the water and drops them in, meaning to fill up the sea, and she was a girl before she was a bird.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> finds her on Mount Fajiu, where the zhe trees grow: a bird shaped like a crow, with a patterned head, a white beak and red feet, whose cry is its own name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The passage calls her the youngest daughter of Yandi: she went wandering on the Eastern Sea, drowned there and did not come back, and so became Jingwei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ever since, the passage says, she has carried wood and stones from the western hills to dam up the Eastern Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The entry then closes like the geography it belongs to rather than like a story, noting that the Zhang River rises on that mountain and runs east into the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her father Yandi is Shennong the Divine Husbandman, who first shaped timber into ploughs and taught the people farming, and whose reign one traditional reckoning places from 2737 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is called Yandi, the Blazing Emperor, because he took fire as the emblem of his government, and a second fire sign was added to his name to say so twice over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tao Yuanming, who lived from 365 to 427 CE, read the book and wrote that Jingwei carries small twigs meaning to fill the vast sea, which made her a figure for holding on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The older gloss is less kind: a 19th-century dictionary explains the saying the story left behind as being like the tomtit carrying pebbles, in the sense of wasting one's powers on a hopeless task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both readings are still in use, and Hong Kong sets the story for primary schools as an example of knowing a thing cannot be done and doing it anyway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), no. 401, 157, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Education Bureau, Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, “精衞填海,” in 《郁文華章》（小學篇）：「選篇分析」, no. 22, https://www.edb.gov.hk/attachment/tc/curriculum-development/kla/chi-edu/resources/primary/lang/culture/03_022.pdf. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 609, 187, and pt. III, 366, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 238–39, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A History of Chinese Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1901), 128–29, https://archive.org/details/cu31924086054776. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic130_-_%E7%B2%BE%E8%A1%9B%E5%9C%96.png/1280px-Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic130_-_%E7%B2%BE%E8%A1%9B%E5%9C%96.png","title":"The Jingwei bird","desc":"Vectorised from an illustration in the Gujin Tushu Jicheng, the imperial encyclopaedia compiled between 1700 and 1725, in its section on the animal kingdom. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic130_-_%E7%B2%BE%E8%A1%9B%E5%9C%96.png","alt":"Line drawing of a long-tailed bird in flight seen from above, wings spread and streaming tail feathers, the plumage worked in fine scale-like strokes."}},
{"id":"cnh-018","num":18,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"Chinese legend blames the tilt of heaven to the north-west on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who struck Mount Buzhou with his head after losing a fight and brought the sky-pillar down.","questions":["The name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is really the title of an office, something like Minister of Works, and the classics have Yao refuse to employ him and Shun banish him.","After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raised a flood that drove people up the hills and into the trees, Nüwa smelted five-coloured stones to mend the sky and propped its corners with a turtle's legs."],"answer":"Gonggong","answerText":"Gonggong","traditional":"共工","hanzi":"共工","pinyin":"Gònggōng","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fuxi, from 2852 BCE by 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shennong, or Zhuanxu, 2 generations after Huangdi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Broke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mount Buzhou, 1 of 8 sky-pillars, in the north-west</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Banished by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shun, as 1 of the 4 great criminals</span></div>","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Gonggong</b> is a figure of Chinese legend remembered above all as a rebel who raised a flood across the world, and whom later ages turned into a god of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name is not really a personal name but the title of an office, roughly Minister of Works or overseer of the waters, and one reading has that office name passing to the family that held it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The old books cannot agree where to place him, calling him a minister of Fuxi, a vassal of Shennong, or a lord in the days of Zhuanxu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Huainanzi</i> says he stirred the flood waters until they beat against Kongsang, the four seas swirled together, and the people all climbed the hills and took to the trees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> makes him a descendant of Yandi and the father of Houtu, setting him in the line that Kuafu also comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What every version keeps is his defeat: he contended for the throne, lost, and in his rage struck Mount Buzhou with his head until it came down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That mountain was the sky-pillar of the north-west, so heaven has tilted that way ever since, carrying the sun, moon and stars towards it, while the earth falls short in the south-east and every river runs there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Who beat him differs, for the <i>Liezi</i> and the <i>Huainanzi</i> name Zhuanxu and Sima Zhen names Zhurong, and Édouard Chavannes noted that tradition puts Gonggong generations earlier and so gives the fight to his descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nüwa then smelted five-coloured stones to mend the sky and cut the legs from a giant sea turtle to prop its corners, and by one account she raised an army and killed him herself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The classics historicise all of it, for Yao refused to employ him, calling him a fine talker whose work turned out badly, and Shun banished him as one of four great criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 284, 89, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), 23–24 and 39, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 49–50 and 67, and Annales des trois souverains, 11, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 393 and 601, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Sun Shulin 孫樹林, “从古籍中的黄帝神话传说记载管窥古人之宇宙观” [The cosmology of the ancients seen through records of the Yellow Emperor myths in the old books], <i>島根大学外国語教育センタージャーナル</i> 10 (2015): 69 and 72, https://ir.lib.shimane-u.ac.jp/31500. [Open access]","Lionel Giles, trans., <i>Taoist Teachings from the Book of Lieh Tzŭ</i> (London: John Murray, 1912), 85, https://archive.org/details/taoistteachingsf00unse. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/%E5%85%B1%E5%B7%A5%E6%B0%8F%E9%A0%AD%E8%A7%B8%E4%B8%8D%E5%91%A8%E5%B1%B1.jpg/1280px-%E5%85%B1%E5%B7%A5%E6%B0%8F%E9%A0%AD%E8%A7%B8%E4%B8%8D%E5%91%A8%E5%B1%B1.jpg","title":"Gonggong butts Mount Buzhou","desc":"A woodblock illustration from the lower volume of the Ming vernacular chronicle 按鑑演義帝王御世盤古至唐虞傳, whose artist is not named. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%85%B1%E5%B7%A5%E6%B0%8F%E9%A0%AD%E8%A7%B8%E4%B8%8D%E5%91%A8%E5%B1%B1.jpg","alt":"Woodblock print in a circular frame: a bearded figure in a robe leans forward and drives his head against a tall cracked peak at the left, with bare branches, a small sun disc and banked cloud around him, and the title in characters down both side panels."}},
{"id":"cnh-019","num":19,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"In the Tribute of Yu the western tribes bring hair-cloth and skins from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the cosmogonists later made the central mountain of the earth.","questions":["The <i>Huainanzi</i> piles the walls of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in nine gradations and grows a pearl tree, a jade tree and a tree of immortality on its terraces in the far west.","King Mu of Zhou is said to have driven his eight steeds to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and met the Queen Mother of the West there, a journey Sima Qian left out of his annals."],"answer":"Kunlun","answerText":"Kunlun","traditional":"崑崙","hanzi":"昆仑","pinyin":"Kūnlún","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Tribute of Yu, 1 of the Shu King's documents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Visited by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">King Mu of Zhou, who reigned from 1001 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Measured at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,090 li round and 11,000 li high, in 1 account</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walled in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 gradations, with 12 towers of five-coloured jade</span></div>","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Kunlun</b> is the mountain at the centre of the world in Chinese tradition, standing somewhere in the far west, with the great rivers of the earth rising at its foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name begins as a plain geographical one: in the Tribute of Yu the wild tribes of the west bring hair-cloth and skins from Kunlun, and the document says nothing else about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Bamboo Annals</i> then have King Mu of Zhou lead a punitive expedition there in his 17th year and see the Western Wangmu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sima Qian left that journey out of his annals of Zhou altogether, and Édouard Chavannes observed that if the oldest texts are to be believed the figure Mu met is a barbarian chief rather than a queen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the cosmogonists made of the name is another thing entirely, for they raised it into the central mountain of the earth and the source of the four great rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> gives it 10,090 li round and 11,000 li high, with a blue, a white, a red and a black river running at its base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Huainanzi</i> piles its walls in nine gradations and grows on it a pearl tree, a jade tree, a star-gem tree and a tree of immortality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its foot runs the Yellow Water, which winds three times and comes back to its source, and whoever drinks it escapes death, while the Yellow River rises there and the Weak Water issues from a hollow rock into the Moving Sands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Twelve towers of five-coloured jade stand on its terraces, where the immortals grow sesame and coriander and the Queen Mother of the West keeps her court beside the Lake of Gems, feasting her guests on peaches that ripen once in three thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The <i>Erya</i> calls the mountain Sumi in the west, which is the Chinese name for Sumeru, and 19th-century sinologists therefore read the whole paradise as Indian cosmology transplanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 380, 108–9, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), 127, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, King Mu, 17th year, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 272 and 594, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 265, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 16 and 137, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Kunlun_Mountains.jpg/1280px-Kunlun_Mountains.jpg","title":"A snow peak on the western frontier","desc":"Photographed near Tashkurgan in Xinjiang in 2010 by Preston Rhea, who titles the view the Kunlun Mountains. CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kunlun_Mountains.jpg","alt":"A snow-covered peak stands far off beyond a wall of bare dark ridges, with a wide river running through green pasture in the foreground and a few animals and low buildings along the far bank."}},
{"id":"cnh-020","num":20,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"In the <i>Bamboo Annals</i> King Mu of Zhou drives west to Kunlun and is entertained by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the Lake of Gems, where the peaches of immortality grow.","questions":["The image of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives above all in the relief carvings of Han grave chambers, and Taoist writers gave her a palace on Kunlun walled in gold.","A consort was invented for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Dongwanggong, the Eastern King Lord, whose name openly imitates her own and who is the male principle to her female."],"answer":"Queen Mother of the West","answerText":"Queen Mother of the West","traditional":"西王母","hanzi":"西王母","pinyin":"Xīwángmǔ","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Entertained</span><span class=\"dt-v\">King Mu of Zhou, about 985 BCE by 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Han grave chambers, 206 BCE to 220 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Her palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000 li round, ramparted in gold</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Her peaches</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ripen once in 3,000 years, by 1 account</span></div>","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Queen Mother of the West</b> is the goddess of the Chinese west, who keeps her court on Kunlun at the head of the immortals and admits a favoured mortal from time to time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole legend grew by accretion over centuries from the slender basis of a name that turns up in a few very early traditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those notices are thin: the apocryphal Books of Zhou and the <i>Bamboo Annals</i> both have King Mu entertained by her in the west, and the <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> carries an obscure mention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Édouard Chavannes read the oldest texts to mean a barbarian chief rather than a queen, and the sober suggestion of the 19th century was that Wangmu named either a region or a sovereign in the ancient west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Liezi</i> built an allegorical tale on those hints, and the credulity of Han Wu Di produced a whole literature of her visits to him with her fairy troop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Taoist writers then furnished her mountain: a palace a thousand li round, ramparted in massive gold with battlements of precious stones, above the Lake of Gems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beside that lake grows the peach tree of the immortals, whose fruit confers immortality on whoever she allows to taste it, and the accounts differ, one giving fruit once in three thousand years and another three thousand years of leaf before three thousand more of ripening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ripening fixed her birthday, kept as the Feast of Peaches and served with bears' paws, monkeys' lips, dragon liver and phoenix marrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She sends azure birds out as her messengers, and a consort was eventually found for her in Dongwanggong, the Eastern King Lord, whose name openly imitates her own and who was paired with her as the male principle to her female.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Long before any of that was written she was carved everywhere in Han funerary art, surviving above all in the relief work on the stones and bricks of grave chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 572, 178–79, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 272, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 136–37, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 265, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, King Mu, 17th year, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Nataša Vampelj Suhadolnik, “Image and Role of the Queen Mother of the West in Han Grave Art,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 3, no. 2 (2015): 197–212, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2015.3.2.197-212. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Peach_Festival_of_the_Queen_Mother_of_the_West%2C_Freer_Gallery_of_Art.jpg/1280px-Peach_Festival_of_the_Queen_Mother_of_the_West%2C_Freer_Gallery_of_Art.jpg","title":"Peach Festival of the Queen Mother of the West","desc":"A handscroll in ink and colour on silk, Ming or Qing dynasty, 17th or 18th century, by an anonymous painter once credited to Fang Chunnian. Freer Gallery of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peach_Festival_of_the_Queen_Mother_of_the_West,_Freer_Gallery_of_Art.jpg","alt":"A blue-and-green landscape painting: a terraced palace of tiled halls and pavilions stands on a stone platform among pines, small figures on its balconies and crossing a bridge below, with green and blue peaks and banked gold cloud behind and an orchard at the right."}},
{"id":"cnh-021","num":21,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"In 219 BCE a magician from Qi told the First Emperor of the isles in the Eastern Sea and sailed for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with several thousand young men and women.","questions":["The immortals on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are said to feed on the gems scattered along its shores and on a fountain of life that never stops running.","Sima Qian had no patience with the magicians who ran the sea after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, setting them down as charlatans who lived on a sovereign's credulity."],"answer":"Penglai","answerText":"Penglai","traditional":"蓬萊","hanzi":"蓬莱","pinyin":"Pénglái","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sought from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">219 BCE, under the First Emperor of Qin</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Its neighbours</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fangzhang and Yingzhou, 2 more isles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in a book of the 5th or 4th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Its fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">several 1,000 young men and women, driven back</span></div>","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Penglai</b> is the first of the three isles of the immortals, which the Chinese of the Qin placed in the Eastern Sea, out of sight but not far off, opposite their own coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its neighbours are Fangzhang and Yingzhou, and the same island goes under the further names Penghu and Pengqiu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All three are peopled by immortals whose shining bodies are fed on the gems lying scattered along their shores and on the fountain of life that runs there for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest writer to put islands of the immortals out in the ocean is the <i>Liezi</i>, of the 5th or 4th century BCE, who is also the first to describe the fruit of immortality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By that time the princes of the coastal states of Qi and Yan were already sending ships out to look for them, talked into it by a magician called Song Wuji.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Anqi Sheng, a drug-seller on the eastern shore who was said to have lived a thousand years, was summoned by the First Emperor and talked with him for three days and nights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On taking his leave he told the emperor to look for him again in the isle of the immortals, and it was on that word that the great expedition was launched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 219 BCE a professor of magic arts from Qi named Xu Shi, otherwise Xu Fu, announced the isles to the emperor as he toured his new empire and begged leave to sail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He went at the head of several thousand young men and women, and the story has the fleet sight the islands and then be driven back by contrary winds, which has been read since as a memory of some attempt to settle Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sima Qian, writing a century later, had no patience with any of it and set the men who ran the sea after enchanted isles down as charlatans living on a sovereign's credulity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 559, 174, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, 163–64, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 3, 309 and 703, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 72, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 647, 197, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, LIII and XCV, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/La_expedici%C3%B3n_de_Xu_Fu%2C_por_Utagawa_Kuniyoshi.jpg/1280px-La_expedici%C3%B3n_de_Xu_Fu%2C_por_Utagawa_Kuniyoshi.jpg","title":"Xu Fu's fleet sets out for Penglai","desc":"A woodblock triptych of about 1843 by Utagawa Kuniyoshi, whose title has the First Emperor build ten great ships and send the court magician Xu Fu after the herbs of long life. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_expedici%C3%B3n_de_Xu_Fu,_por_Utagawa_Kuniyoshi.jpg","alt":"A colour woodblock print across three sheets: three great junks under patterned sails, their prows carved as beasts and their decks crowded with small figures, ride a blue sea, with a robed man standing on the leading prow pointing ahead and a snow-capped peak on the far horizon."}},
{"id":"cnh-022","num":22,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"Chinese vases, fans and teapots are crowded with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Taoist group whose members stand between them for youth and age, wealth and poverty, and both sexes.","questions":["No source earlier than the Yuan dynasty knows the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a fixed set, though several of them had been celebrated separately in Taoist legend for centuries before.","Only three of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were real people, and the histories that mention those three say nothing whatever about the magical powers the later stories give them."],"answer":"Eight Immortals","answerText":"Eight Immortals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grouped in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Yuan dynasty, 1280 to 1368</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or as early as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1250 to 1279, in the last Southern Song years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Historical</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 of the 8, the other 5 being fable</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lists differ</span><span class=\"dt-v\">across 4 early works, 1 sharing almost no names</span></div>","traditional":"八仙","hanzi":"八仙","pinyin":"Bāxiān","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Eight Immortals</b> are a group of Taoist figures so constantly painted that few subjects in Chinese art rival them, turning up on vases, teapots, fans, scrolls and embroidery alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Between them they stand for the whole of humanity: an old man and a youth, a soldier and a scholar, a nobleman and a beggar, a cripple and a woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each is known by an emblem rather than a face, so that Zhang Guolao rides his donkey, Lan Caihe plays his flute, Li Tieguai leans on an iron crutch and Lü Dongbin carries a sword.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So much luck attaches to them that the number itself took some of it on, and China has an Eight Genii table, an Eight Genii bridge and an Eight Genii vermicelli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One series of pictures shows them crossing the sea, each riding a monster of his own, and another sets them in pairs with their symbols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> As a fixed set they are late, for the Qing scholar Zhao Yi could find no trace of them as a group before the Yuan dynasty, and the most generous reading pushes them back only to the closing decades of the Southern Song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Several had been celebrated singly as immortals long before that, so what those years supplied was the assembly rather than the figures gathered into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor was the membership settled: four early works give four lists, one of which shares almost no names with the rest, and the single woman is absent from two of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only three were real people — Zhongli Quan, Zhang Guolao and Lü Dongbin — and Lü took his degree about 770 before withdrawing to live as a recluse on Mount Hua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The sober records of those three credit them with no supernatural power whatever, and the tales do not keep their own chronology, handing He Xiangu a peach from a man born after her death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, no. 251, 338, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 207 and 562–63, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 288–89, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 35–36, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 36–38, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 63–64, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Octagonal_vase_depicting_the_Eight_Great_Immortals_of_Daoism%2C_China%2C_Longquan_ware%2C_Yuan_dynasty%2C_1271-1368%2C_mold-stamped_ceramic_under_glaze_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco%2C_CA_-_DSC07490.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"The Eight Immortals on a Yuan celadon vase","desc":"An octagonal Longquan celadon vase of the Yuan dynasty, whose eight faces are mould-stamped with the Eight Immortals in unglazed panels. Asian Art Museum, San Francisco. Photograph by Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octagonal_vase_depicting_the_Eight_Great_Immortals_of_Daoism,_China,_Longquan_ware,_Yuan_dynasty,_1271-1368,_mold-stamped_ceramic_under_glaze_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco,_CA_-_DSC07490.jpg","alt":"A pale green celadon vase with eight flat sides, its shoulder and foot moulded with flower panels and its waist carrying a row of unglazed orange panels, each holding a standing bearded figure in long robes among curling clouds."}},
{"id":"cnh-023","num":23,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"A monster to be killed in Europe, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a bringer of rain and the essence of the yang principle, and turns evil only in the legends Buddhism carried in from India.","questions":["Chinese writers give the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nine resemblances, among them the horns of a deer, the head of a camel and the claws of a hawk, and they do not agree on all nine.","The opening hexagram of the <i>Book of Changes</i> is a ladder of them, from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lying hid in the deep to the one on the wing in the sky."],"answer":"Chinese dragon","answerText":"Chinese dragon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First seen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Fuxi, from 2852 BCE by 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chief of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 360 scaly creatures, in a Han dictionary</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Graded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 5 colours by an emperor, 1101 to 1126</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Imperial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 claws to a foot, 4 for lesser dragons</span></div>","traditional":"龍","hanzi":"龙","pinyin":"lóng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Chinese dragon</b>, the long, is chief of the four divinely constituted creatures and a spirit of the waters, a bringer of rain rather than a monster to be killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is the essence of the yang principle, and can transform itself, shrinking to the size of a silkworm or swelling until it fills the space between heaven and earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the spring equinox it ascends into the sky, and at the autumn equinox it buries itself in rivers or in the depths of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chinese writers give it nine resemblances — a deer's horns, a camel's head, a snake's neck, a hawk's claws — but do not agree on all nine, and one has it hear through its horns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A pearl of magic power goes with it, on its forehead by one account and beneath its chin by another, and a Han dictionary makes it chief of the 360 scaly creatures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It is the emblem of the emperor, whose throne is the dragon seat, and only imperial robes carried a beast with five claws to a foot where lesser dragons have four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first hexagram of the <i>Book of Changes</i> runs from the dragon lying hid in the deep to the one on the wing in the sky, the whole a figure for the great man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four kinds are listed: the celestial dragon holding up the mansions of the gods, the spiritual one that makes wind and rain, the earth dragon that lays out the rivers, and one guarding hidden treasure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The dragon kings of the seas came the other way about, being the nagas of India brought in by Buddhism, and an emperor of the 12th century canonised five coloured dragon spirits as kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Belief held firm because the creature was constantly seen, any cloud with a serpentine tail being a dragon, and in drought a paper one was carried through the streets to ask for rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 57–59, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 451, 141–43, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, no. 94, 307, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 208–10, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 677–85, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 7 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1922), 409–12, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch07doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Nine_Dragons%2C_detail%2C_Song_Dynasty.jpg","title":"Two of Chen Rong's Nine Dragons","desc":"A detail of the handscroll <i>Nine Dragons</i>, painted in ink by Chen Rong in 1244, under the Southern Song. Reproduced from Michael Sullivan's <i>The Arts of China</i>. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nine_Dragons,_detail,_Song_Dynasty.jpg","alt":"An ink painting on a pale ground: two scaled, whiskered dragons coiling through banks of wash-laid cloud, one turning its horned head back over its shoulder and the other reaching forward with a splayed clawed foot, with a red seal impression at the top right."}},
{"id":"cnh-024","num":24,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"English calls it the Chinese phoenix, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is an omen bird that comes only when a kingdom is tranquil and its ruler fond of peace, and leaves when it is not.","questions":["The male of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the feng and the female the huang, and the pair's inseparable fellowship is Chinese poetry's covert way of speaking about lovers.","A regent of the Han told his boy emperor that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been seen on the coast, and an official was sent off at once to make it offerings."],"answer":"Fenghuang","answerText":"Fenghuang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First came</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Huangdi, from 2697 BCE by 1 annal</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Danced for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shun, at the 9 parts of his music</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Worshipped from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">84 BCE, under Zhaodi of the Han</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last seen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 1368, as the Ming took the throne</span></div>","traditional":"鳳凰","hanzi":"凤凰","pinyin":"fènghuáng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Fenghuang</b> is a bird of wondrous form and mystic nature, one of the four supernatural creatures, and English calls it the Chinese phoenix on many writers' authority rather than on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The male is the feng and the female the huang, and the compound of the two names is what Chinese itself calls the bird.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Accounts of its shape differ and none settles on one animal, giving it a hen's head, a man's eye, a serpent's neck, a swallow's brow, a tortoise's back and the outward semblance of a dragon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its fish-like tail carries twelve feathers, or thirteen in a year with an intercalary month, its song has five harmonious notes, and its plumage the five colours, each standing for a cardinal virtue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is king of the 360 kinds of feathered creature, comes out of the East, alights on no tree but the wutong, eats the fruit of the bamboo and drinks only from clear streams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> What made it matter is that it appears only where a kingdom is tranquil and its ruler fond of peace, and leaves a state that is disordered and warlike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The <i>Bamboo Annals</i> have it come to Huangdi by the Luo and nest in the courts at Shun's accession, and the <i>Shu King</i> has it dance into the hall when the nine parts of Shun's music had been played.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That made a sighting worth manufacturing, and the first official worship of the bird was got up in 84 BCE by the regent Huo Guang, who told a boy emperor one had appeared on the eastern coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An officer was sent to make it offerings; at the next accession a flock obliged in Shandong, and the emperor marked the event with a general amnesty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Ming historiographer wrote that a scholar would never have committed such a blunder, and the last recorded coming was in 1368, on the tomb of the father of the man then taking the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), 88, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, Hwang-ti, 50th year, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 184, 40–41, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 76, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 666–69, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 669–71, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic001_-_%E9%B3%B3%E5%87%B0%E5%9C%96.png","title":"The fenghuang in the Imperial Encyclopaedia","desc":"A plate from the Gujin Tushu Jicheng, the imperial encyclopaedia compiled under Chen Menglei and printed in the 1720s, in its section on the animal kingdom. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic001_-_%E9%B3%B3%E5%87%B0%E5%9C%96.png","alt":"A woodblock plate in black line on a pale ground: a long-tailed bird with a crested head and streaming tail plumes standing among rocks and foliage, with two columns of Chinese characters at the right edge."}},
{"id":"cnh-025","num":25,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"Confucius wept when hunters caught a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his own state, saying the creature comes only when there is an intelligent king, and the <i>Spring and Autumn Annals</i> stop at that entry.","questions":["A picture of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bringing a child hangs in bedrooms and rides before the bridal chair, and a boy of unusual promise is called the gift of the unicorn.","An emperor of the Han thought he had caught a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and renamed the period of his reign after it, the beast being an omen that a wise ruler is on the throne."],"answer":"Qilin","answerText":"Qilin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the parks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">of Huangdi, from 2697 BCE by 1 annal</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Again under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Yao, in the 7th year of his reign</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caught</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 14th year of Duke Ai of Lu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed a reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 121 BCE, after a Han emperor's supposed catch</span></div>","traditional":"麒麟","hanzi":"麒麟","pinyin":"qílín","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Qilin</b> is one of the four supernatural creatures of Chinese tradition, beside the dragon, the phoenix and the tortoise, and English calls it the Chinese unicorn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The male is the qi and the female the lin, and the compound of the two is what Chinese calls the creature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The authorities do not agree: an early glossary gives it a deer’s body, an ox’s tail and a single horn, though later pictures give it a pair, while the dictionary of about 100 CE makes it a benevolent beast with a horse’s body and a horn of flesh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The fleshy tip marks a creature able for war that wills to have peace, and later writers add that it eats nothing living, and never treads on growing grass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What mattered was never its shape but its timing: it comes only where a wise ruler reigns or a sage is about to be born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> That made a sighting worth arranging: the <i>Bamboo Annals</i> put them in Huangdi’s parks and one in the seventh year of Yao, and a Han emperor who thought he had caught one renamed his reign period after the catch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The famous appearance went wrong: in Duke Ai of Lu’s fourteenth year hunters took a lin west of the capital and broke its foreleg, and the man given it threw it out as unlucky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Confucius went to look, knew it at once, and wept, because the creature comes only when there is an intelligent king and this one had come at the wrong time and been hurt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Spring and Autumn Annals</i> stop at that entry, which is why the capture is famous, though the translator who put them into English doubted the line was Confucius’s and thought the beast an antelope uncommon in Lu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What outlived the omen is a domestic hope: the qilin bringing a child is painted in bedrooms and carried before the bridal chair, and a bright boy is called the gift of the unicorn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Gae, fourteenth year, 834, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), Prolegomena, 85–86, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 389, 127, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 109, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 43, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 672–76, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 322, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Tuhui_zongyi_%28Principles_of_Painting%29_Qilin.jpeg","title":"The qilin in a Ming painting manual","desc":"A woodblock plate from the <i>Tuhui zongyi</i>, the painting manual edited by Yang Erzeng and printed in 1607; the copy reproduced is in the Philadelphia Museum of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuhui_zongyi_(Principles_of_Painting)_Qilin.jpeg","alt":"A woodblock plate in black line on aged paper: a scaled, cloven-hoofed beast with a bearded dragon-like head, a pair of horns swept back over its shoulders and a bushy flame-shaped tail, bounding through grass beneath a pine, with its name in two Chinese characters at the upper right."}},
{"id":"cnh-026","num":26,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divide the Chinese sky into quarters, dragon east, bird south, tiger west and tortoise north, with seven of the 28 lunar mansions to each.","questions":["The <i>Book of Rites</i> has an army march under banners of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Red Bird in front, the Dark Warrior behind, the Azure Dragon left and the White Tiger right.","A tiger's coat was said to turn white at five hundred years, which is why the western quarter of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the White Tiger rather than an ordinary one."],"answer":"Four Symbols","answerText":"Four Symbols","answerDate":"","traditional":"四象","hanzi":"四象","pinyin":"Sìxiàng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Four Symbols</b> are the four quarters of the Chinese sky, each named for a creature: the Azure Dragon in the east, the Vermilion Bird in the south, the White Tiger in the west and the Dark Warrior in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are a way of dividing the stars rather than a bestiary, since the 28 lunar mansions are shared out seven to a quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are also not the four supernatural creatures — the qilin, phoenix, tortoise and dragon — which the same manual lists as a set of its own, and only the dragon and the tortoise belong to both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Each quarter carries a season as well as a direction, the dragon standing for spring and the east together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The tiger is white because a tiger's coat was said to turn white at five hundred years, and its name came to mean the western quarter and then the west in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest use is military: the <i>Book of Rites</i> directs that on the march the banner of the Red Bird go in front, the Dark Warrior behind, the Azure Dragon on the left and the White Tiger on the right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its translator's note explains that each of those names covers the seven constellations of that quarter, so an army moved under a diagram of the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern figure is really a tortoise, and Dark Warrior is the name of an emperor who in one legend beat a demon king that had turned into one, the victor's name sticking to the quarter the tortoise stands for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scheme was old enough to keep time by, and a commentator on the Canon of Yao read the middle of spring off it, from the seven southern constellations showing towards evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It outlived the astronomy, since officials and wealthy men were still carried to the grave under catafalque hangings worked with a dragon and a tiger, for the eastern and western quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Lî Kî</i>, Sacred Books of the East 27 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1885), the Khü Lî, sect. i, pt. iv, 8, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0027unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Les Cinq Empereurs, 44, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Les Cinq Empereurs, 47, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, no. 91, 307, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 187, 61, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, no. 94, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 681, 684, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Soochow_Planisphere_%28Detail_2%29.jpg/1920px-Soochow_Planisphere_%28Detail_2%29.jpg","title":"The Suzhou planisphere","desc":"A rubbing of the star chart engraved on stone at Suzhou in about 1247, from a map drawn by Huang Shang around 1190 and cut by Wang Zhiyuan; the rubbing is of the 16th century. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soochow_Planisphere_(Detail_2).jpg","alt":"A star chart in gold on a near-black ground: a great disc of the sky crossed by a band for the Milky Way, its stars joined into constellations by fine lines, cut into unequal wedges by radial lines that run from an inner circle of circumpolar stars out to a rim of small labelled panels."}},
{"id":"cnh-027","num":27,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the mask on Shang and Zhou bronzes, two great eyes over curved tusks, and its name means glutton after a man the Zuo Commentary says Shun banished.","questions":["The oldest text to use the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means a man rather than a mask, one of four wicked ones cast out to the four distant regions for greed beyond measure.","Cataloguers of Chinese bronzes proposed the tiger, the storm god and the Tibetan mastiff for the face of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and none of the three carried the day."],"answer":"Taotie","answerText":"Taotie","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Duke Wan's 18th year, in the Zuo Commentary</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Banished with</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 other wicked ones, by Shun</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On bronzes of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Shang, c. 2000 – 1001 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"饕餮","hanzi":"饕餮","pinyin":"tāotiè","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Taotie</b> is the monster face that stares out of early Chinese bronzes, and its name means glutton after a man rather than a beast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Zuo Commentary, under the eighteenth year of Duke Wan, sets out four wicked men whom Shun banished to the four distant regions: Chaos, Monster, Block and Glutton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Glutton was the worthless descendant of an officer of Huangdi, greedy of eating and drinking, craving money and property, insatiable in his exactions and sparing neither the orphan nor the widow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Grand Historian tells the same story, and later commentators matched those four one for one against the four criminals the Canon of Shun says he put away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The equations are not worth much in themselves, as their reporter admits, but the two lists are plainly two forms of one legend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The mask is the thing a reader is likelier to have seen: a face with enormous eyes and heavy jaws armed with curved tusks, moulded in relief on ritual wine jars and food vessels of the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sits on a ground of squared spirals that the cataloguers read as cloud and thunder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One explanation reached for the tradition that Yu the Great cast the first bronze vessels and moulded them with the shapes of the storm gods of the hills and waters, so that people might know their dreaded features and keep clear of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the face actually meant was not settled: the tiger, the all-devouring storm god and the Tibetan mastiff were each proposed within a few years of one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The scholar who gathered those readings declined to choose between them, regretting that no evidence had been given for any, and asked only that the thing be treated as a deity rather than as ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Wan, eighteenth year, 283, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Les Cinq Empereurs, 78, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), 40, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Berthold Laufer, <i>Jade: A Study in Chinese Archaeology and Religion</i>, Field Museum of Natural History Publication 154 (Chicago, 1912), 184–85, https://archive.org/details/jadestudyinchin00lauf. [Open access]","Stephen W. Bushell, <i>Chinese Art</i>, vol. 1, Victoria and Albert Museum Art Handbook (London: His Majesty's Stationery Office, 1904), 90, https://archive.org/details/chineseart1bush. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Late_Shang_bronze_lei_with_turquoise_taotie_design.jpg/1920px-Late_Shang_bronze_lei_with_turquoise_taotie_design.jpg","title":"A taotie face laid in turquoise","desc":"Photographed in the National Museum of China, where the photographer records it as a late Shang bronze lei with a turquoise taotie design. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Late_Shang_bronze_lei_with_turquoise_taotie_design.jpg","alt":"A small bronze vessel faced with a mosaic of turquoise tesserae in blue and brown: a symmetrical monster mask laid across its front, with a raised vertical flange down the middle for the nose, a great curled eye and arched brow to either side of it, and a domed lid above set with a small horned head."}},
{"id":"cnh-028","num":28,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"Traditionally said to describe the charts on Yu the Great's nine bronze vessels, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> surveys hills and waters and fills them with gods, omen beasts and impossible peoples.","questions":["Kuafu, Jingwei, the Queen Mother of the West and a winged dragon that killed Chiyou are all described in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, distrusted as geography and indispensable as myth.","A geographical compilation of the Zhou by one reckoning, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was already ancient when a 4th-century scholar edited it, along with the dictionary of ancient terms and the <i>Elegies of Chu</i>."],"answer":"Classic of Mountains and Seas","answerText":"Classic of Mountains and Seas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhou at latest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1122 – 250 BCE, by 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edited by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Guo Pu, 276 – 324 CE</span></div>","traditional":"山海經","hanzi":"山海经","pinyin":"Shānhǎi jīng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b><i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i></b> is the oldest Chinese geography to survive apart from the Tribute of Yu, and it is a geography of a strange kind: it walks from hill to hill, naming at each the trees, the ores, the water and the creature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese bibliographers file it with the geographies, though it has long been read with distrust, and its 19th-century defenders concluded only that it is at least as old as the Zhou and probably older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tradition made it a description of the charts engraved on the nine bronze vessels Yu the Great cast after draining the flood, which would set it at the beginning of the record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A French compiler in Shanghai a century ago called it a compilation of the Zhou, full of strange statements the scholars of his day disbelieved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Guo Pu, who lived from 276 to 324, edited it along with the old dictionary of terms and the <i>Elegies of Chu</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What has kept it in hand is its second half, where the survey passes beyond the seas and the countries stop being possible. There are headless people whose eyes sit in the breast, people with one arm or three faces, pygmies who walk hand in hand against birds that would carry them off, and giants whose eyebrows hang over their carts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its animals are omens as much as animals: a beast is described, then what its appearance brings, which is why a red pheasant of the Xiaohua hills was painted on doors against fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A winged dragon lives in the far south, and having killed Chiyou it could not rise again and hid beneath the earth, so drought follows wherever it lies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kuafu chasing the sun, Jingwei filling the sea and a Mount Kunlun 11,000 li high are all in it, and a modern study calls the book the wellspring of the country's ancient myths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 35, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 408–9, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 386–90, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 683 and 707, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 380, 108–9, and pt. II no. 56, 301, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Zhao Kuifu 赵逵夫, “‘夸父逐日’神话的历史文化内涵” [The historical and cultural content of the ‘Kuafu chases the sun’ myth], 文学遗产 [Literary Heritage] 2020, no. 5, https://ir.nwnu.edu.cn/bitstream/39RV6HYL/61950/1/%E2%80%9C%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E9%80%90%E6%97%A5%E2%80%9D%E7%A5%9E%E8%AF%9D%E7%9A%84%E5%8E%86%E5%8F%B2%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%86%85%E6%B6%B5.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Wu-Renchen-Shanhai-jing-guangzhu-Kui.jpg","title":"A page of the book","desc":"Kui, described in Commons as a one-legged bull-shaped creature, from Wu Renchen's annotated <i>Shanhaijing guangzhu</i> printed in 1786; reproduced from <i>Ritual and Scripture in Chinese Popular Religion</i>, edited by David Johnson. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wu-Renchen-Shanhai-jing-guangzhu-Kui.jpg","alt":"A woodblock page in black line within a heavy ruled border: a hornless ox-like beast with long ears and a bushy tail crouches on the empty page, its name and four short columns of Chinese text ranged down the right-hand margin."}},
{"id":"cnh-029","num":29,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"Chiefly the work of a minister of Chu who drowned himself in the Miluo, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds the <i>Li Sao</i> and the <i>Nine Songs</i> and stands as a class of its own.","questions":["Wild, irregular and so allegorical that much of it would be unintelligible without the commentary, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first gathered in the 1st century BCE and survives in an edition of 17 books.","The poet is said to have clasped a stone and gone into the river on the fifth of the fifth moon, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands behind the Dragon Boat Festival."],"answer":"Chuci","answerText":"Chuci","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Its poet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Qu Yuan, 332 – 295 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collected by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Liu Xiang, in the 1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"楚辭","hanzi":"楚辞","pinyin":"Chǔcí","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b><i>Chuci</i></b>, the Elegies of Chu, is a collection of southern poetry given a class of its own at the head of Chinese belles-lettres, a place it has held since the Sui history's bibliography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is chiefly the work of Qu Yuan, a minister of the state of Chu who lived from 332 to 295 BCE and stood in high favour with Prince Huai until a jealous rival denounced him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Degraded and disregarded, he went to the bank of the Miluo, told a fisherman that the world was foul and he alone clean, then clasped a stone to his chest and went under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His chief piece is the <i>Li Sao</i>, Falling into Trouble, nearly 400 lines in which he leaves the world in a phoenix car drawn by dragons and is refused at the palace of God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Poems by his nephew Song Yu and by Jing Cha, both nearly contemporary, make up the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The pieces were first gathered by Liu Xiang in the 1st century BCE, and the earliest form now surviving is Wang Yi's edition in 17 books, which appends work by eight later hands and carries his commentary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhu Xi of the Song cut a selection to 8 books and annotated it again, and though later editors took liberties with the poems the old commentary came down uncorrupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The verse itself is unlike anything before it, wild and irregular in metre, and so allusive that without the commentary much of it would now be unintelligible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among the Nine Songs inside the collection is the Genius of the Mountain, clad in wistaria and girdled with ivy, riding a red pard through a grove no daylight reaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Qu Yuan went into the river on the fifth of the fifth moon, and the people of Chu cast rice in after him, which is the origin of the Dragon Boat Festival and of the rice cake in water-flag leaves eaten on the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 181, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A History of Chinese Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1901), 50–53, https://archive.org/details/cu31924086054776. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 200–201, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 466, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 326, 107, and pt. I no. 642, 196, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Li_sao_illustr%C3%A9_3_10.png","title":"The Mountain Spirit, with her poem","desc":"The ninth of the Nine Songs in an annotated edition of the <i>Chuci</i> published under the title <i>Li sao</i>, with illustrations by Xiao Yuncong, 1645. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_sao_illustr%C3%A9_3_10.png","alt":"An open woodblock book: on the left the poem printed in columns of Chinese characters, on the right a framed cut of a woman in leaf robes with a flowering staff, riding a spotted leopard through cloud, a small attendant beside her and a second beast below."}},
{"id":"cnh-030","num":30,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"Jade stands for purity, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bears that name, and a Song emperor who had signed a humiliating peace put him on the list of gods in 1012.","questions":["Born to a queen who dreamed of Lord Lao on a dragon, and later a hermit who healed the sick, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is in legend a king's son who gave his throne away.","Called the high god of the Taoists and, a century ago, the most popular divinity in China, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> issues writs of pardon that are bought at paper shops and burnt."],"answer":"Jade Emperor","answerText":"Jade Emperor","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Birthday</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 9th of the 1st moon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First listed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 10th month of 1012</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statue cast</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 1013, by Zhenzong</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to 1116 by 1 manual</span></div>","traditional":"玉皇大帝","hanzi":"玉皇大帝","pinyin":"Yùhuáng Dàdì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Jade Emperor</b> is the high god of the Taoists and was, a century ago, the most popular divinity in China, jade standing for purity in a name also given as Yuhuang Shangdi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Legend makes him the son of a queen of Guangyan Miaole who, past her prime and childless, dreamed that Lord Lao came riding a dragon with a boy in his arms and gave her the child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was born at noon on the ninth day of the first moon, gave the palace wealth to the poor, took the throne at his father's death and resigned it days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He lived as a hermit at Puming in Shaanxi and on Mount Xinyan in Yunnan, attained perfection, and spent the rest of his life healing the sick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Buddhists and Taoists both claim him, the former identifying him with Indra, and a sinner could buy a printed writ of his pardon at a paper shop, to be filled in and burnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His place in the record is later and stranger: in 1005 the Song emperor Zhenzong signed a humiliating peace with the Khitan, and to prop up his throne began announcing messages from heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A pennant of yellow silk twenty feet long flew from a gate of the capital with a letter tied to it, praising his virtue and promising his house seven hundred generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the tenth month of 1012 he told his ministers that an Immortal had brought him a letter from the Jade Emperor, and the official history notes the name entering the list of divinities then for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had a statue cast in 1013, and bought the silence of the minister likeliest to object with a cask said to hold wine and found to hold pearls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A 19th-century manual dates the worship instead to 1116 under Huizong and the Taoist Lin Lingsu, while a biographical dictionary makes the god a magician who raced another to heaven on dragons and won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 130–32, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 510–11 and 524, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 9–12, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 391, 127–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 957, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Jade_Emperor._Ming_Dynasty.jpg","title":"The Jade Emperor enthroned","desc":"A 16th-century hanging scroll in the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, catalogued there as the Daoist deity the Jade Emperor. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jade_Emperor._Ming_Dynasty.jpg","alt":"A painting on silk, worn and torn at the upper right: a bearded god in wide white robes with dark embroidered borders sits cross-legged on a lotus throne, hands folded in his lap, a small jewelled crown on his head and a plain gold halo behind it, with scrolled clouds in pink, green and ochre filling the ground."}},
{"id":"cnh-031","num":31,"category":"Myth and legend","difficulty":3,"question":"Buddhism brought the Sanskrit Naraka to China, where it became <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an earth-prison whose sentences end, since a soul that has served its term is reborn among the six classes of living beings.","questions":["A Taoist handbook put the entrance to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a mountain near Fengdu in Sichuan, the Indian hell being reckoned far too deep underground to lie anywhere near China.","Under the Song, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reorganised into ten courts under the Jade Emperor, so the underworld came to be run like a Chinese magistracy, with a register of lives kept by a clerk."],"answer":"Diyu","answerText":"Diyu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reshaped under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Huizong, 1101 – 1126</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Descent told of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Wanli reign, 1573 – 1620</span></div>","traditional":"地獄","hanzi":"地狱","pinyin":"dìyù","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Diyu</b>, written with characters meaning \"earth-prison\", is the Chinese underworld, the term chosen to render the Sanskrit Naraka when Buddhism brought a place of punishment for the wicked into China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a final destination but a phase of transmigration, closer to a purgatory: the shortest term in any one hell is five hundred years, each of its days worth fifty above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A soul that leaves it is reborn among the six classes of living beings, which are gods, men, asuras, animals, hungry ghosts and those still under torment below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ruler is Yanluo, the Sanskrit Yama, whom Buddhism took from Brahmanism, and who by one legend was a king of Vaisali sworn to be reborn in hell for victory in battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He won, and now rules in a palace where devils pour molten copper down his throat three times a day, his generals serving as jailers and his sister Yami taking the women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Sanskrit hell lay some 450,000 kilometres (280,000 miles) underground, too far from China, and a Taoist handbook to the underworld, the <i>Yuli chaozhuan</i>, put its entrance in a mountain near Fengdu in Sichuan instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A story set in the Ming reign of Wanli has a governor lowered down the shaft in a box, walking to an iron gate and taking tea with Yanluo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How many hells there are was never settled: Buddhism counted eight, the northern school added eight cold ones, another reckoning gives 136 with two more for women and for suicides, and one sutra 180.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under the Song the underworld was reorganised as ten courts and placed under the Jade Emperor, giving it the shape of a Chinese magistracy rather than an Indian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fittings followed: a life-register kept by a clerk who once lengthened an emperor's by altering a numeral, a bridge only the good cross, and a pond of blood for women dead in childbirth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 7 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1922), 250–59, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch07doruoft. [Open access]","Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 179, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 247, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 207, 327, and pt. II no. 18, 355, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 245 and 462, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 197, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin01dool. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 105, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Jin_Chushi._Yama_the_Fifth_King_of_Hell%2C_late_12_century._Metmuseum.jpg/1920px-Jin_Chushi._Yama_the_Fifth_King_of_Hell%2C_late_12_century._Metmuseum.jpg","title":"A king of hell holds court","desc":"One of a set of hanging scrolls of the Ten Kings of Hell, painted in ink and colour on silk by Jin Chushi before 1195 and now in the Metropolitan Museum of Art; the file's uploader identifies this king as Yama. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jin_Chushi._Yama_the_Fifth_King_of_Hell,_late_12_century._Metmuseum.jpg","alt":"A tall silk scroll in a gold brocade mount, read from top to bottom. A red-robed king in a winged official's cap sits scowling behind a plain wooden desk while a clerk in green, in a black scholar's cap, leans in holding out an open document. Below them blue-skinned demons drag a barefoot man in white towards a large round mirror on a stand, and at the foot of the scroll an armoured demon swings two swords among snakes and thorn trees where small figures are being tormented."}},
{"id":"cnh-032","num":32,"category":"Myth and legend","difficulty":4,"question":"A soul whose merits outweigh its faults passes from the first of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> straight to the tenth, where it is reborn, and never sees the mirror of guilt at all.","questions":["Most of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hold sixteen small hells apiece, each with its own speciality, its own list of offences, and its own list of the good deeds that excuse a soul.","The whole scheme of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from a Taoist monk's handbook rather than from canonical Buddhism, and it is arranged throughout like a Chinese magistrate's court."],"answer":"ten courts of hell","answerText":"ten courts of hell","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in a Southern Song album, 1200 – 1279</span><span class=\"dt-k\">A judge of it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Bao Zheng, died 1062</span></div>","traditional":"十殿閻羅","hanzi":"十殿阎罗","pinyin":"Shí Diàn Yánluó","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>ten courts of hell</b> are the received popular account of Diyu, the Chinese underworld: ten halls, each with a presiding king, through which a dead soul is passed in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The whole scheme descends from one Taoist book, a handbook to the underworld by a monk named Tan Chi who claimed to have gone down and come back, rather than from canonical Buddhism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is arranged throughout like a Chinese magistrate's court, with registers, warrants, lictors and the rest of a yamen's paraphernalia, and its officials are promoted or demoted on their record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The First Court keeps the register of the living and the dead, and a soul whose merits outweigh its faults goes straight from it to the Tenth to be reborn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A guilty soul is dragged instead before a mirror inscribed \"no good man comes before this mirror of guilt\", and then to the Second Court, where the punishments begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Each court between them holds sixteen small hells with their own specialities, among them knives, ice, copper cauldrons and wild beasts, and its own list of the offences it exists to punish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those lists read like a legal code rather than a theology: maiming, denying a deposit left in trust, kidnapping children to sell to monasteries, prescribing for patients without knowing medicine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each court also lists what exempts a soul from it, and the living could petition the ten kings on a dead parent's behalf in hope of a lighter penalty or none at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its judges are dead Chinese officials: Bao Zheng, the Song judge said to be no more bribable than the king of the dead himself, was given the Fifth Court by popular opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Tenth Court settles where a soul is reborn, and before it goes Granny Meng gives it a broth of oblivion, a measure taken after escaped souls began telling the living what happens below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 7 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1922), 262–301, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch07doruoft. [Open access]","Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 179, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 618, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 197, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin01dool. [Open access]","Cleveland Museum of Art, “Album of Daoist and Buddhist Themes: Kings of Hells: Leaf 29,” accession 2004.1.29, https://clevelandart.org/art/2004.1.29. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/China%2C_Southern_Song_dynasty_-_Album_of_Daoist_and_Buddhist_Themes-_Kings_of_Hells-_Leaf_29_-_2004.1.29_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif/lossy-page1-1920px-China%2C_Southern_Song_dynasty_-_Album_of_Daoist_and_Buddhist_Themes-_Kings_of_Hells-_Leaf_29_-_2004.1.29_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif.jpg","title":"A king of hell in his hall","desc":"Leaf 29 of a Chinese album of Daoist and Buddhist themes, painted in the 1200s and belonging to the album's series on the Ten Kings of Hells; the Cleveland Museum of Art, which holds it, says the king's identity is unclear. Made available under CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China,_Southern_Song_dynasty_-_Album_of_Daoist_and_Buddhist_Themes-_Kings_of_Hells-_Leaf_29_-_2004.1.29_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif","alt":"An ink outline drawing on tan paper, with no colour but a red seal at the foot. A bearded official in a winged cap sits on a throne at the head of a flight of steps, holding a narrow plaque across his chest; a hanging scroll beside him shows a plan of walled buildings. Attendants stand below the dais with trays and staves, an armoured demon waits at the left and a beaked demon at the right, and two old men in long robes bow with their hands clasped at the foot of the steps."}},
{"id":"cnh-033","num":33,"category":"Myth and legend","difficulty":3,"question":"The god of the kitchen, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, keeps account of the good and evil done by the family he lodges with, and goes up at the year's end to report on them.","questions":["Honey or sticky rice is fed to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before he leaves for heaven, so that his lips will stick together and he can say little about the household's year.","With the ancestral tablet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was one of the two things worshipped in every Chinese house, his printed image pasted up on the wall beside the cooking range."],"answer":"Zao Jun","answerText":"Zao Jun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Goes up</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on the 24th of the 12th moon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Birthday</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 3rd of the 8th moon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First sacrifice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Han Wudi, 140 – 86 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"灶君","hanzi":"灶君","pinyin":"Zàojūn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Zao Jun</b>, the god of the kitchen, is the household deity of Chinese popular religion, his image pasted up over the cooking range in what was reckoned to be every family in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With the ancestral tablet he was one of the two objects of worship a Chinese house was never without, honoured with incense and candles on the first and fifteenth of every month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is a Taoist figure and goes by several names, among them the god of the stove and Zhang Dan, which means the one who makes out the bill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His office is not cooking but watching: he keeps account of the good and evil done by the family he lodges with, and Taoist books make him the chief of heaven's secret service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the end of every year he goes up and reports on the household's conduct to the Jade Emperor, and is away over the turn of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> His picture is torn down and burnt with mock-money, which is how he travels, and beans and straw are thrown on the roof so the family may hear his footsteps or his horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before he leaves he is feasted, and the prudent give him honey or balls of glutinous rice so that his lips stick together and he can say little up above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A printed address bought at a shop is read out before the image by the head of the house and then burnt to carry it up, like a memorial sent to a magistrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A new picture is pasted up for his return, showing an old man and, in most modern versions, his wife the goddess of the hearth, among almanac tables and painted animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The legend of his worship has it beginning with a Han magician who claimed the god had freed him from age and hunger, and who persuaded Emperor Wu to offer the first imperial sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 44 and 166–67, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 597 and 622, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 81–85, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 6, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 458–59, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/%E5%8F%B8%E5%91%BD%E7%AB%88%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E4%B8%89%E6%95%99%E6%BA%90%E6%B5%81%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E5%A4%A7%E5%85%A8%EF%BC%89.jpg","title":"The god of the stove and his clerk","desc":"A woodblock illustration which the file's own record identifies as the Stove God of the Sanjiao yuanliu soushen daquan, a Chinese compendium of the gods of the three religions, in a 1909 edition. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%B8%E5%91%BD%E7%AB%88%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E4%B8%89%E6%95%99%E6%BA%90%E6%B5%81%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E5%A4%A7%E5%85%A8%EF%BC%89.jpg","alt":"A black and white woodblock line drawing. A bearded man in a tall official's cap and long belted robes stands on a terrace beneath a banana plant, his hands folded inside his sleeves; a smaller attendant in a cap stands at his shoulder holding a bound volume against his chest, with a table and a railing behind them."}},
{"id":"cnh-034","num":34,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"Legend has the Yellow Emperor hang two spirits' portraits on peach-wood above his doors, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are still painted or pasted on doors across China.","questions":["Two generals who sat up armed outside a sick Tang emperor's bedroom were later painted on his palace gates, and so joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> guarding ordinary houses.","Renewed every New Year and set one to each leaf of a double door, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were once to be seen on almost every house front in China."],"answer":"Menshen","answerText":"Menshen","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Quoted by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wang Chong, 27 – 97 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Told again in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Fengsu tongyi, late 2nd century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New pair under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tang Taizong, 627 – 650 CE</span></div>","traditional":"門神","hanzi":"门神","pinyin":"ménshén","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Menshen</b> are the guardian gods of the door, a pair of armed figures set one to each leaf of a Chinese double door and renewed at the New Year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name is plural and behaves more like an office than a person: several different pairs have held it, and one household's pair need not be its neighbour's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are counted among a standard set of six household spirits invoked in charms, alongside the ancestral tablets and the god of the stove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The oldest pair are two spirits of a legend which the <i>Lunheng</i>, written by Wang Chong in the 1st century, quotes from the <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On a mountain in the eastern sea stood a peach tree three thousand li round, and the door by which the ghosts came and went lay between its north-eastern boughs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Shen Shu and Yu Lu had charge of that gate, binding with cords of reed any ghost that had done harm and feeding it to the tigers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yellow Emperor, hearing of it, is said to have raised a peach-wood figure and had the two painted with tigers and cords on house doors, and the custom is traced from that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second-century study of custom, the <i>Fengsu tongyi</i>, of which only a third survives, has officials cut peach-wood figures on the year's last night and paint a tiger on the doorway; by the 19th century many households pasted up the two names instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That pair were displaced in popular favour by two generals of the Tang emperor Taizong, Qin Shubao and Yuchi Gong, who watched armed outside his door through a sickness and were then painted on his palace gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The minister Wei Zheng took the back gates, and it has been argued that the pictures came first and the gods after them, an image put up to keep harm out ending as a god in its own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Wang Ch'ung, <i>Lun-hêng, Part I: Philosophical Essays of Wang Ch'ung</i>, trans. Alfred Forke (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz; London: Luzac; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1907), 243, https://archive.org/details/lunheng01wang. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 728, 220–21, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 131, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 172–74, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 151–52, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 710–11, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 3 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 261, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch03doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/%E9%96%80%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E6%96%B0%E5%88%BB%E5%87%BA%E5%83%8F%E5%A2%9E%E8%A3%9C%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E8%A8%98%EF%BC%89.jpg/1280px-%E9%96%80%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E6%96%B0%E5%88%BB%E5%87%BA%E5%83%8F%E5%A2%9E%E8%A3%9C%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E8%A8%98%EF%BC%89.jpg","title":"The door gods on a pair of doors","desc":"A woodblock illustration which the file's own record identifies as the door gods of the Xinke chuxiang zengbu Soushen ji, an illustrated Chinese compendium of the gods, and dates to 1600. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%96%80%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E6%96%B0%E5%88%BB%E5%87%BA%E5%83%8F%E5%A2%9E%E8%A3%9C%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E8%A8%98%EF%BC%89.jpg","alt":"A black and white woodblock line drawing of a pair of closed double doors studded with rows of round bosses, a bearded warrior in armour painted on each leaf, each holding a long-hafted weapon upright and turning his head towards the other; the two characters for door god are cut into the top right corner."}},
{"id":"cnh-035","num":35,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":1,"question":"Because the twelve earthly branches also number the double hours, an animal of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> governs a time of day and a compass point as well as a year of birth.","questions":["A fortune-teller may rule that guests born under one animal of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must stay away from a wedding, lest some influence fall on them at the wrong hour.","Asking why the rat does not drive off the horse if water beats fire, a 1st-century sceptic attacked the elemental logic behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its twelve animals."],"answer":"Chinese zodiac","answerText":"Chinese zodiac","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest witness</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wang Chong, born 27 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In use by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 1st or 2nd century BCE, perhaps</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Animal years by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 2nd century CE, on one view</span></div>","traditional":"生肖","hanzi":"生肖","pinyin":"shēngxiào","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Chinese zodiac</b> is a cycle of twelve animals — rat, ox, tiger, hare, dragon, serpent, horse, goat, monkey, cock, dog and pig — one to each of the twelve earthly branches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The branches are a counting series rather than a band of constellations, and pairing them with ten heavenly stems gives the sixty combinations of the sexagenary cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those sixty pairs named days from great antiquity and were applied to years only under the Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same branches divide the day into twelve double hours and mark twelve points of the compass, so an animal governs an hour and a direction as well as a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is not a zodiac in the astronomical sense: the twelve divisions of the ecliptic, where the sun and moon meet, are a separate set and the nearer analogue of the western signs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A birth is written as eight characters, a stem and a branch each for the year, month, day and hour, and the year's branch is what makes a person belong to an animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A fortune-teller comparing those characters with the five elements may find that a certain animal must be kept away from a wedding, a coffining or a house-raising, so those born in its year stay at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Charms were printed for each of the twelve to cure a person born in that branch's year, and every one of the sixty years had a god of its own with an image in the temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The scheme invited that reasoning, since each animal takes an element from its branch and the five elements conquer one another in a fixed order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wang Chong took it apart in the 1st century, asking why the rat does not drive off the horse if water beats fire; he is also the oldest Chinese witness to the twelve, whose origin, Turkic or Chinese, was long disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Wang Ch'ung, <i>Lun-hêng, Part I: Philosophical Essays of Wang Ch'ung</i>, trans. Alfred Forke (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz; London: Luzac; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1907), 6 and 105–06, https://archive.org/details/lunheng01wang. [Open access]","Wang Ch'ung, <i>Lun-hêng, Part II: Miscellaneous Essays of Wang Ch'ung</i>, trans. Alfred Forke, 2nd ed. (1911; repr. New York: Paragon Book Gallery, 1962), appendix II, 479–80, https://archive.org/details/lunheng02wang. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 296, 348–49, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II nos. 302–03, 351–52, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 340–42, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 176–77, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 3 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 262, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch03doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Tang_Pottery_Zodiac_Figures_%289948253544%29.jpg/1920px-Tang_Pottery_Zodiac_Figures_%289948253544%29.jpg","title":"Pottery figures of the twelve animals","desc":"A row of Tang pottery figures with human bodies and animal heads, which the file's own record identifies as zodiac figures in the Shaanxi Provincial Museum, Xi'an. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_Pottery_Zodiac_Figures_(9948253544).jpg","alt":"Four standing pottery figures in a museum case, each with a human body in a long belted robe and its hands folded inside its sleeves, and each with the head of an animal: from the left a rodent, a horned ox, a broad-faced cat and a long-eared hare, all of them looking upward and standing on ringed plinths."}},
{"id":"cnh-036","num":36,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"Because the river of heaven runs full to the brim, one drop of rain sweeps away the bridge of birds and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lose their one night together that year.","questions":["Girls once tried to thread a needle in the dark before a stick of incense on the night the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> meet, counting a success as an omen of skill to come.","A single line in a Han treatise, saying magpies fill the Milky Way on the seventh night of the seventh month, is the oldest notice of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> anywhere."],"answer":"Cowherd and Weaver Girl","answerText":"Cowherd and Weaver Girl","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest notice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a Han treatise, by 122 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">They meet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on the 7th night of the 7th month</span></div>","traditional":"牛郎織女","hanzi":"牛郎织女","pinyin":"Niúláng Zhīnǚ","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Cowherd and Weaver Girl</b> are two stars on opposite banks of the Milky Way, whom Chinese tradition makes a husband and wife kept apart and allowed to meet one night a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Weaver Girl is Vega, in Lyra; the Cowherd was less firmly placed, some putting him in Aquila and others in a group drawn from Capricorn and Sagittarius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oldest notice is one line in the <i>Huainanzi</i>, put together in 21 books under Liu An, a prince who died in 122 BCE: the two are parted all year except on the seventh night of the seventh month, when magpies fill the Milky Way and let her cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From that obscure sentence a literature grew, its most famous stanza by Cao Pi, first emperor of Wei, on the two gazing at each other across the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the fuller telling she is a god's daughter, kept at her loom until her father married her to the herdsman on the far bank, whereupon she gave up weaving altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He separated them in anger, sending the husband across the river of stars and allowing one meeting a year, for which myriads of magpies make a bridge she walks over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything therefore turns on the weather, since the river of heaven runs full to the brim and one extra drop of rain floods it and sweeps the bird-bridge away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It attached itself to a real journey, a Han envoy's search for the source of the Yellow River, held to be the Milky Way continued on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He sailed upstream to a city where a woman sat spinning and a young man watered an ox; she gave him her shuttle, and an astronomer at home found a wandering star had crossed between the two at that hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The festival is women's: melons, fruit, cakes, flowers and incense are set out for the two stars, and girls thread a needle in the dark, a success being an omen of skill to come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 311, 97–98, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 189–91, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 60–61, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 593–94, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 126, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 412, 132, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Niulang_and_Zhinv_%28Long_Corridor%29.JPG/1920px-Niulang_and_Zhinv_%28Long_Corridor%29.JPG","title":"The meeting on the bridge of magpies","desc":"A painted panel in the Long Corridor of the Summer Palace in Beijing, which the file's own record identifies as the reunion of the Weaver Girl and the Cowherd on the bridge of magpies and dates to about the end of the 19th century. The painter is not named. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niulang_and_Zhinv_(Long_Corridor).JPG","alt":"A wide painted panel on a wooden beam, framed by scrolling clouds. On the left a woman in green and blue robes strides forward with long yellow and blue ribbons streaming behind her; on the right a man in a broad conical hat turns towards her, and between them two small children reach out with their arms. Three magpies fly overhead."}},
{"id":"cnh-037","num":37,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"Realgar wine drunk at the summer festival turns the wife back into a giant serpent in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the shock of the sight frightens her husband to death.","questions":["The oldest clear ancestor of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a Southern Song tale in which three women met at West Lake prove to be an otter, a white snake and a black chicken.","A late Ming collection of stories gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its settled form, in which the snake is a menace and her husband ends the story as a monk."],"answer":"Legend of the White Snake","answerText":"Legend of the White Snake","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled form</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a woodblock print of 1624</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Compiler</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Feng Menglong, 1574 – 1645</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reshaped</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the mid-18th century</span></div>","traditional":"白蛇傳","hanzi":"白蛇传","pinyin":"Báishé zhuàn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Legend of the White Snake</b> is one of the best known stories in China: a snake spirit takes a woman's form, marries a young man of Hangzhou, and is shut under a lakeside pagoda by a monk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its oldest clear ancestor is a Southern Song tale of three women met at West Lake, who prove under exorcism to be a black chicken, a white snake and an otter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Behind that lies a plainer skeleton: a young man passes a night at a beautiful stranger's mansion, sickens as his vital essence drains, and finds on returning that the house is a graveyard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The settled form is late Ming, a tale known from a woodblock print of 1624 in Feng Menglong's <i>Stories to Caution the World</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There the snake is a menace rather than a wife: she possesses her husband, who is twice arrested over goods she gave him, and the monk Fahai buries her and her maid Little Green under the pagoda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From the middle of the 18th century the retellings turn on the marriage instead, and she takes on the virtues of a female immortal as a good wife and mother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The episodes now known everywhere were added then: the meeting in the rain on West Lake, the borrowed umbrella, and the realgar wine of the summer festival, which turns her back into a giant serpent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sight frightens her husband to death, so she goes to Mount Kunlun for the herb that revives him, and when Fahai keeps him at the Golden Mountain monastery she leads an army of sea creatures against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is imprisoned under the pagoda once her son is born, and the son frees her by his filial devotion and by placing first in the civil examinations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A recent comparative study links these tales, and the European stories of marriage with a demon woman, to the taming of a shape-shifting empousa in a 3rd-century life of Apollonius of Tyana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aaron Balivet, “The Many Transformations of White Snake: Gender, Ritual, and Performance in Late Imperial China” (PhD diss., University of Wisconsin–Madison, 2020), 5–8, https://asset.library.wisc.edu/1711.dl/MKNK5FP5V6YM79E/R/file-f7383.pdf. [Open access]","Whalen Lai, “From Folklore to Literate Theater: Unpacking Madame White Snake,” <i>Asian Folklore Studies</i> 51, no. 1 (1992): 51–66, https://web.archive.org/web/20250512083239/https://asianethnology.org/article/1066/download. [Open access]","A. B. Starostina, “On the Question of the Origin of Legends about Melusine and Madame White Snake,” <i>Shagi/Steps</i> 9, no. 3 (2023): 87–107, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-3-87-107. [Open access]","Feng Menglong, comp., <i>Jingshi tongyan</i> [Stories to caution the world], juan 28, “Bai niangzi yongzhen Leifengta,” Chinese Text Project, https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?if=gb&chapter=730335. [Open access]","<i>Qingping shantang huaben</i> [Plain tales from Peaceful Mountain Hall], “Xihu san ta ji,” Chinese Text Project, https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?if=gb&chapter=185607. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Peking_Opera_The_Legend_of_the_White_Snake_%286986544727%29.jpg/1920px-Peking_Opera_The_Legend_of_the_White_Snake_%286986544727%29.jpg","title":"The tale on the Peking opera stage","desc":"Three of the tale's characters in a performance the photographer records as given at Zhenjiang in December 1985 by a touring Peking opera troupe. Just some dust, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peking_Opera_The_Legend_of_the_White_Snake_(6986544727).jpg","alt":"Three costumed opera performers before a pale curtain. On the left a figure in a green embroidered robe, with a sword at the hip and a jewelled headdress, gestures with one hand; in the centre a figure in white with a pearled headdress holds a tasselled whisk; on the right a figure in a blue scholar's robe and a soft blue cap looks down."}},
{"id":"cnh-038","num":38,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"A ball of fire seen climbing the mast meant that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was leaving the ship, and sailors who saw it come down again believed she would see them home.","questions":["Weaving at her loom, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dreamt of her father and brothers in a storm, and lost her father's junk when she opened her mouth to answer her own mother.","Two figures called Favourable-wind-ear and Thousand-mile-eye stand one on each side of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in her temples, listening for a fair breeze and watching a thousand li off."],"answer":"Mazu","answerText":"Mazu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the Song, 960 – 1280 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ningbo temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded in the 12th century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UNESCO listing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 2009</span></div>","traditional":"媽祖","hanzi":"妈祖","pinyin":"Māzǔ","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mazu</b> is the Chinese goddess of the sea, worshipped by sailors, fishermen and traders alike, whose cult began in Fujian under the Song, the dynasty of 960 to 1280.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> She was born in a fishing village on Meizhou Island in Putian, and was known in her life for filial piety, for healing, for divining as a village medium, and for saving people from disaster at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the telling written down at Fuzhou in the 1860s she fell asleep over her weaving, head on the loom, and dreamt of her father and two brothers caught in a storm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She took her father's junk in her mouth and her brothers' in her hands, and when her mother called she opened her mouth to answer, being an obedient girl, and let him go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Days later the news came that the brothers had been saved and the father drowned; emperors afterwards honoured her 36 times, with 16 official titles and more than a hundred popular ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In her temples an assistant stands on each side of her own image, Favourable-wind-ear, who catches the least breath of a fair breeze, and Thousand-mile-eye, who sees a thousand li away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sailors carried embers from the censer before her image in a small red bag or hung them aboard, and in a storm knelt at the bow with incense calling on her by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A ball of fire on the mast was taken for the goddess herself: going up it meant she was leaving and disaster was near, and coming down it promised she would see the junk in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Merchants carried the cult with them: the grandest temple in Fuzhou's southern suburbs was built by traders from Ningbo, and the finest building in Ningbo was a Mazu temple founded by Fujian men in the 12th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Emigration took her to Taiwan and to the Chinese towns of Southeast Asia, and in 2009 UNESCO put Mazu belief and customs on its list of intangible cultural heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 262–64, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin01dool. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>The Middle Kingdom</i>, vol. 1 (London: W. H. Allen, 1883), 123, https://archive.org/details/b29352897_0001. [Open access]","Dingying Lin, Zhiming Zhou, Xiaobin Zhang and Zhanhong Wu, “Analysis of the Factors Influencing the Dissemination of Mazu Culture in Southeast Asia during the Qing Dynasty,” <i>PLOS One</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0325164, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0325164. [Open access]","Suastiwi Triatmodjo, Ted Tsung-te Tsai, M. Agus Burhan, Hanggar Budi Prasetya and Endah Budiarti, “Mythologization of the Goddess Mazu: Readings through Spacial Setting and Activity in the Interior of Cu An Kiong Temple,” <i>Harmonia</i> 22, no. 2 (2022): 381–91, https://doi.org/10.15294/harmonia.v22i2.40627. [Open access]","Hsiao-Ming Chang and Ching-Hui Lin, “Mazu Belief and Happiness: A Sequential Mediation Model Involving Mental Health and Positive Emotions among Taiwanese Followers,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 16 (2025): 1571796, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1571796. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 288, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Wood_Statue_of_Mazu_Late_19th_century_CE_Qing_Dynasty_%281644-1911_CE%29_China.jpg/1920px-Wood_Statue_of_Mazu_Late_19th_century_CE_Qing_Dynasty_%281644-1911_CE%29_China.jpg","title":"A Qing image of the goddess","desc":"A gilded and painted wooden figure of the goddess enthroned, which the photographer's own record dates to the late 19th century, under the Qing. No holding museum is named. Mary Harrsch, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wood_Statue_of_Mazu_Late_19th_century_CE_Qing_Dynasty_(1644-1911_CE)_China.jpg","alt":"A carved wooden figure seated square-on behind museum glass. She wears a flat-topped crown with a fringed headdress falling over each ear, her face painted a reddish brown; a heavy gilded robe carved with dragons and clouds covers her shoulders and knees, and both hands hold a broad curved tablet across her front. The pedestal below is carved with a lotus."}},
{"id":"cnh-039","num":39,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"A general beheaded in 219 was made a duke, then a prince, and in 1594 a god, after which the empire worshipped him as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with about 1,600 official temples.","questions":["Though his shrines are called temples of the military god, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is honoured by scholars too, on a legend that he could recite the <i>Zuo Commentary</i> from beginning to end.","After crediting him with their victories over the Taiping rebels, the Qing in 1856 named <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a sage and made him the equal of Confucius in the eyes of the state."],"answer":"Guandi","answerText":"Guandi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">219 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made a god</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1594, by the Wanli emperor</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named a sage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1856, under the Qing</span></div>","traditional":"關帝","hanzi":"关帝","pinyin":"Guāndì","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guandi</b> is the Chinese god of war, the deified form of Guan Yu, a general beheaded in 219 CE in the wars that broke the Han empire into three kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was born at Jieliang in Hedong, the later Jiezhou in Shanxi, and changed his given name twice in early life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shut in a back room by his parents, he broke out through the window, heard a girl and her father weeping, and killed the magistrate and the uncle who were forcing her into concubinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fleeing west with his description in every officer's hands, he stooped to wash at a brook and found his face turned a dark ruddy colour; asked his name at the barrier he said Guan, which had never been his family name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Zhuozhou he lifted the stone a butcher named Zhang Fei had rolled over his well, a straw-shoe seller called Liu Bei parted their fight, and in a peach orchard the three swore to die on one day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He kept that oath: taken prisoner with two of his lord's wives and shut up with them for a night, he stood at the door until morning holding a lantern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sun Quan took and beheaded him, and the deification then ran through four centuries of imperial decrees: duke early in the 12th century, prince in 1128, and a Mongol title around 1330.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1594 the Ming emperor Wanli raised him to the rank of <i>di</i>, meaning 'god', and from that year the state worshipped him officially.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Qing credited him with their victories over the Taiping and in 1856 named him a sage, the equal of Confucius; he had by then about 1,600 official temples, in the capital and in every prefectural and district city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is a god of literature as well, on the strength of a legend that he could recite the <i>Zuo Commentary</i> from end to end, and Buddhists made him a guardian of their monasteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 297, 92–93, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 113–17, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 6 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1920), 71–72, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch06doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 6 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1920), 80–81, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch06doruoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), no. 1009, 383–84, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 7 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1922), 315–16, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch07doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Anonymous_-_Guan_Yu_-_2001.442_-_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg/1920px-Anonymous_-_Guan_Yu_-_2001.442_-_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg","title":"The god on a Qing scroll","desc":"A hanging scroll of the god in ink, colour and gold on silk, which the Metropolitan Museum of Art catalogues as the work of an unidentified artist of about 1700. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Guan_Yu_-_2001.442_-_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg","alt":"A tall painted silk scroll mounted in gold brocade. A bearded figure in a green embroidered robe stands on banks of green and pink cloud with a golden disc behind his head, one hand gripping a long halberd hung with a red pennant. A dark-faced attendant in armour stands close behind him and a younger figure in red, hands joined, at his left. A gold cartouche of Chinese characters sits in the top right corner."}},
{"id":"cnh-040","num":40,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":4,"question":"Standard tables open the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Fuxi in 2852 BCE and carry it down through Shennong, the Yellow Emperor and Yao, giving each an exact year of accession.","questions":["Behind the genealogies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies the theory of the five elements, its emperors being symbols of five natural forces that succeed one another by destroying one another.","Compiling in the 1st century BCE, the Grand Historian drew up a table by generations running back through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Yellow Emperor, but set down exact years only from 841 BCE."],"answer":"Chinese legendary period","answerText":"Chinese legendary period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Opens with</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fuxi, 2852 BCE by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Table ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shun, 2255 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Exact years from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">841 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"傳說時代","hanzi":"传说时代","pinyin":"chuánshuō shídài","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Chinese legendary period</b> is the division of early history that tradition furnishes with named rulers and exact years of accession, marked off from the purely mythical epochs placed before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A standard table opens with Fuxi, whose reign is placed in 2852 BCE, and runs through Shennong at 2737, the Yellow Emperor at 2697, Yao at 2356 and Shun at 2255.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A note under it observes that numerous dissimilar schemes were propounded by historical compilers, and that their mutually antagonistic views are themselves the evidence against every date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian said of the three dynasties that they are ancient and their chronology cannot be made precise, so his own exact years begin only with the Gonghe regency, in 841 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two rival systems of dates were still in roughly equal credit long afterwards, and every difference between them falls before that year, which is read as proof that his limit holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The organising idea behind the earliest genealogies seems to have been the theory of the five elements, its five emperors being symbols of natural forces that succeed one another by destroying one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That doctrine was held to have been taught by Confucius himself, and the short text recording it is among the works Sima Qian says he drew his own table from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had critics inside the tradition: in the 1st century CE Wang Chong tested it against the animals of the cycle, asking why the rat does not drive off the horse if water beats fire, or the cock eat the hare if metal beats wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Myth also grew forward into the record, since a man is not made a god without some cause being assigned, and that cause tends to take the form of a marvellous narrative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against all of it stands the annalist, whose very title means an impartial recorder, and whose office was taken seriously enough that the historians of Qi died in 547 BCE rather than falsify their chronicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. III, 365–66, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxliii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxviii–cxc, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 68–69, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Wang Ch’ung, <i>Lun-hêng, Part I: Philosophical Essays of Wang Ch’ung</i>, trans. Alfred Forke (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz; London: Luzac; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1907), 105–07, https://archive.org/details/lunheng01wang. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 63, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/%E6%AD%A6%E6%A2%81%E7%A5%A0%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E7%9F%B3.jpg","title":"A carved stone of the Wu Liang shrine","desc":"An ink rubbing of one of the carved stones of the Wu Liang shrine in Shandong, a Han family shrine of the 2nd century CE, reproduced in Édouard Chavannes's archaeological survey of northern China. The record names the stone and not the figures carved on it. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%AD%A6%E6%A2%81%E7%A5%A0%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E7%9F%B3.jpg","alt":"An ink rubbing printed white on dark grey, of a stone slab with a gabled top. The gable carries a frieze of animals and small figures above a band of scrollwork. Below it runs a row of standing robed figures, each framed in its own narrow upright compartment under a scalloped canopy. Two further bands beneath hold seated figures, horses and carriages."}},
{"id":"gr-331","num":331,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is anonymous and takes his name from the potter whose signature is cut into the foot of the pots, saying he made rather than painted them.","questions":["Fewer than twenty vases survive from the hand of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, generally thought one of the first to practise the red-figure technique and possibly the artist who invented it.","A bilingual amphora in Munich shows Herakles feasting twice over, its red-figure side given to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its black-figure side to the Lysippides Painter."],"answer":"Andokides Painter","answerText":"Andokides Painter","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 515 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bilinguals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Andokides Painter</b> is an anonymous Athenian vase-painter of the later 6th century BCE, named after the potter Andokides, who employed him and whose signature his pots carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reads <i>epoiesen</i>, 'made it', and is cut into the foot: it names the man who shaped and fired the pot, not the one who drew the figures on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fewer than twenty vases survive from his hand, mostly amphorae with a few cups, their scenes drawn from the stories of Herakles and Dionysos and the tale of Ajax and Achilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is generally thought the first painter to work regularly in the red-figure technique after its invention at Athens around 530 to 520 BCE, and possibly its inventor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is best known for bilingual vases, which carry the same design twice, once in red-figure and once in the older black-figure, so that the two techniques stand side by side on one pot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> An amphora in Berlin, signed on the foot, shows Herakles making off with the Delphic tripod while Apollo hauls at one of its legs and Athena watches; wrestlers fill the other side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A bilingual amphora in Munich has Herakles reclining at a feast with Athena beside him on both faces, the red-figure side given to this painter and the black-figure side to the Lysippides Painter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether those black-figure faces are a second man's work or the same painter in his older manner has never been settled, and the two have been proposed as one artist working in both techniques.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A third amphora, in the Louvre, is decorated in a special technique like red-figure but on a white ground, with Amazons arming on one side and women bathing on the other, one swimming and another diving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His date is fixed from outside the pottery, by the securely dated reliefs of the Siphnian Treasury at Delphi, and it has been argued that Psiax, a finer and bolder draughtsman, realised what red-figure could do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["“Andokides Painter,” Union List of Artist Names, ID 500030596, Getty Research Institute, http://vocab.getty.edu/page/ulan/500030596. [Open access]","“Andokides Painter (red-figure),” Kerameikos.org, https://kerameikos.org/id/andokides_painter_red_figure. [Open access]","“Berlin F 2159 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Berlin+F+2159&object=Vase. [Open access]","“Munich 2301 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Munich+2301&object=Vase. [Open access]","“Louvre F 203 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Louvre+F+203&object=Vase. [Open access]","Amanda Herring Brendle, review of <i>Bilingual Vases: The Andokides Painter and the Lysippides Painter</i>, by Rui Morais, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2018.05.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.05.17/. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 386–387, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Jenifer Neils, review of <i>The Art of Vase-Painting in Classical Athens</i>, by Martin Robertson, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 1993.02.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.02.11/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Andokides_Painter_ARV_3_1_Herakles_Apollon_tripod_-_wrestlers_%2801%29.jpg/1920px-Andokides_Painter_ARV_3_1_Herakles_Apollon_tripod_-_wrestlers_%2801%29.jpg","title":"Amphora signed by the potter Andokides, painted by the Andokides Painter, about 525 BCE","alt":"A tall two-handled jar with a lid, standing on a museum plinth and black all over except for a band of figures in the orange colour of the clay: a bearded man in a lion skin strides away carrying a three-legged stand while a long-haired figure catches at it, a helmeted woman with spear and shield stands behind him and another woman watches from the right, and a signature is cut into the black foot","desc":"Attic red-figure amphora type A with lid. Side A: Athena, Herakles and Apollon struggling for the tripod, Artemis. Side B: young coach, two wrestlers. Athens, late archaic, about 525 BCE. Potter: Andokides, signed on the foot. Painter: Andokides Painter. Findspot Vulci; Berlin, Altes Museum (Antikensammlung) F 2159. ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andokides_Painter_ARV_3_1_Herakles_Apollon_tripod_-_wrestlers_(01).jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-332","num":332,"category":"Archaic Greece","difficulty":3,"question":"Archaic Greek sculptors carved the standing nude male <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the left foot advanced and the arms held at the sides, setting it over graves and in sanctuaries.","questions":["Older museum catalogues called the standing archaic youth an Apollo, but the modern name for the type is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since very few of the statues can be shown to be gods.","Cut from Naxian or Parian marble, painted, and sometimes over three metres tall, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood on Attic graves and as a dedication in Greek sanctuaries."],"answer":"kouros","answerText":"kouros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last example</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>kouros</b> is a free-standing statue of a young man, carved in the round and nude, standing frontally with the left foot advanced, the arms held down at the sides and the hands clenched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The example in New York is over life-size at 1.93 metres (6 feet 4 inches), cut from island marble, and keeps that scheme exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word itself is a modern label: nineteenth-century catalogues generally called such a figure an Apollo, though very few of them can be shown to represent a god at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus records that Egyptian sculptors worked to a measured canon, dividing the body into 21 parts and a quarter, and that an early Samian statue stood 'with the arms stretched stiffly down the sides and the legs separated in a stride', much as Egyptian work did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly every archaic male statue in Greece follows that same scheme, so the type is a convention held in common rather than the invention of a single workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The statues had two settled uses, standing over graves and serving as dedications in sanctuaries, and free sculpture in Greece grew out of those two habits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Marble came from Naxos and Paros, and the finished surface was painted; red survives on the hair, the eyebrows, the nipples and inside the nostrils of the best-preserved pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scale ranged from figures well under life-size to the colossal kouros dedicated to Poseidon at Sounion, which stands 3.05 metres (10 feet) tall, and the Dipylon head belongs to a statue of about 3 metres (10 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An unfinished kouros from Naxos survives still worked with the point, caught at the stage before any surface was smoothed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The series closes with the funerary Aristodikos of about 500 to 490 BCE, whose arms come a little away from the thighs and whose hair is cropped short, and who is reckoned the last of the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“New York 32.11.1 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=New+York+32.11.1&object=sculpture. [Open access]","“Athens, NM 3938 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+NM+3938&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>The Library of History</i> 1.98.5–9, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1933), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 36, 93–94, 100, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","“Archaic Period,” National Archaeological Museum, Athens, https://www.namuseum.gr/en/collection/archaiki-periodos/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Marble_statue_of_a_kouros_%28youth%29_MET_DT263.jpg/1920px-Marble_statue_of_a_kouros_%28youth%29_MET_DT263.jpg","title":"Marble kouros in the Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York","alt":"A nude marble statue of a standing young man, facing forward with the left foot advanced, arms straight down at the sides and hands closed, long hair falling behind the shoulders.","desc":"Marble statue of a kouros (youth), Metropolitan Museum of Art, accession 32.11.1, Fletcher Fund, 1932. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_statue_of_a_kouros_(youth)_MET_DT263.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-333","num":333,"category":"Archaic Greece","difficulty":3,"question":"Standing frontally in a richly patterned chiton, with one hand drawing the skirt tight against the legs, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the draped female counterpart of the kouros.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the statue type found in numbers on the Athenian Acropolis, thrown down when the Persians sacked the city and buried where the returning Athenians found it.","Analysis of one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from an Attic grave recovered gold and lead foil and eleven pigments, so the type as first made was painted rather than white."],"answer":"kore","answerText":"kore","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 – 630 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last examples</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>kore</b> is a free-standing archaic Greek statue of a standing young woman, carved in the round and always clothed, the draped counterpart of the nude kouros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The type varies little: all stand erect, looking straight to the front, the left foot slightly advanced, the right arm bent forward at the elbow to hold an offering while the left hand pulls the skirt tight across the legs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most wear a chiton pinned along the upper arm to make sleeves and, over it, a heavier garment folded into a diplois, whose deep folds are where the Attic sculptors spent their skill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whom they represent has never been settled: they can hardly be Athena, whose attributes could not have vanished without a trace, so the likeliest reading is that they are worshippers, though some call them priestesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One inscription records the offering of 'a maiden' to Poseidon by a fisherman, so such a figure could be a conventional gift rather than a portrait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest is the Nikandre, dedicated to Apollo on Delos about 640 to 630 BCE, life-size at 1.90 metres (6 feet 3 inches) but only 0.17 metres (7 inches) deep and so nearly flat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The largest group came out of the Athenian Acropolis, many together in a pit north-west of the Erechtheum, thrown down when the Persians sacked the city and buried where the returning Athenians found them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They stood over graves as well, and the Phrasikleia from Myrrhinous in Attica survives entire, cut from Parian marble by Aristion of Paros, 1.79 metres (5 feet 10 inches) tall and wearing a wreath, a necklace, earrings and bracelets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Re-examination of her surface recovered gold and lead foil and eleven pigments, and paint on an Acropolis figure 0.55 metres (1 foot 10 inches) high made the chiton blue and edged the outer garment red and blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The series runs out about 500 to 490 BCE with statues whose folds fall with real weight and whose faces are solemn and no longer smiling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 164–165, 167, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","“Archaic Period,” National Archaeological Museum, Athens, https://www.namuseum.gr/en/collection/archaiki-periodos/. [Open access]","“Athens, NM 1 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+NM+1&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Amanda Claridge, “Looking for Colour on Greek and Roman Sculpture,” <i>Journal of Art Historiography</i> 5 (December 2011): 4, https://arthistoriography.files.wordpress.com/2011/12/claridge.pdf. [Open access]","“Athens, Acropolis 675 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+Acropolis+675&object=sculpture. [Open access]","“Athens, Acropolis 674 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+Acropolis+674&object=sculpture. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Phrasikleia_Kore%2C_Atenas%2C_Grecia%2C_2019_01.jpg/1280px-Phrasikleia_Kore%2C_Atenas%2C_Grecia%2C_2019_01.jpg","title":"The Phrasikleia Kore, National Archaeological Museum, Athens","alt":"A marble statue of a standing young woman in a long close-fitting chiton, facing forward, wearing a wreath and a necklace, her hair in beaded braids over the shoulders, holding a bud against her chest with the right hand and drawing the skirt with the left.","desc":"Statue of a kore in Parian marble that stood on the grave of Phrasikleia at Merenda, ancient Myrrhinous, in Attica, made by the sculptor Aristion of Paros; accession 4889, National Archaeological Museum of Athens. Benjamín Núñez González, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phrasikleia_Kore,_Atenas,_Grecia,_2019_01.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-334","num":334,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Greek sculptors of the 6th century BCE turned almost every mouth up at the corners, so a warrior fighting or dying on a pediment wears the same <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a goddess.","questions":["The ends of the lips were the hardest thing an early sculptor had to cut, and Attic workshops spent a century refining the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into something like an expression.","Statues carved just before the Persian sack have mouths that no longer turn up, and one droops at the corners, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> helps date a sculpture."],"answer":"archaic smile","answerText":"archaic smile","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aristion stele</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 510 – 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>archaic smile</b> is the upturned mouth that Greek sculptors gave to almost every figure they carved between about 600 and 480 BCE, whatever that figure was doing. Its simplest form is a single curve, as on the colossal head of Hera from Olympia, whose mouth is cut as one arc and so produces the smile in its most primitive form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not there from the start: on an early statue from Eleutherna in Crete the mouth is merely a straight line, with no attempt at expression of any kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ends of the lips were the hardest part of the face for an early carver, who was content to stop them with a vertical cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Attic workshops complicated the shape until the mouth was divided into three curves and the lips were worked off into the cheek by an imperceptible transition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the smile is not is a mood, and the west pediment of the temple at Aegina proves it: those warriors wear the same conventional smile whether they are fighting or dying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their limbs and muscles are drawn up to ease the pain of their wounds while their faces keep the archaic smile hardly modified at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the east pediment, carved a little later, the fallen warrior does not smile without reason: the same form is transformed by clenched teeth and drawn lips into an expression of anguish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same shape carries a different feeling on the grave stele of Aristion, signed by Aristokles about 510 to 500 BCE, where the form of the smile is preserved and its effect entirely changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On the latest Acropolis statues, made shortly before 480 BCE, one mouth has lost the convention and another actually droops at the corners, and the severe sculpture of the next generation leaves the smile behind altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 138, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 133, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 172–174, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Frank Bigelow Tarbell, <i>A History of Greek Art</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1922), 156–157, https://archive.org/details/historyofgreekar00tarbuoft. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 204–205, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 179, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","“Athens, NM 29 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, http://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+NM+29&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Frank Bigelow Tarbell, <i>A History of Greek Art</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1922), 151–152, https://archive.org/details/historyofgreekar00tarbuoft. [Open access]","Ali Abd AL Mohsen Ali, “Characteristics of The Transitional Phase In Greek Sculpture Between Archaic And Classical,” <i>Al-Academy</i>, no. 114 (December 2024): 293–308, https://jcofarts.uobaghdad.edu.iq/index.php/jcofarts/article/view/1518. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Aphaia_pediment_head_W-III_Glyptothek_Munich_75.jpg/1280px-Aphaia_pediment_head_W-III_Glyptothek_Munich_75.jpg","title":"Head of a warrior from the temple at Aegina","alt":"A marble head of a young man in a pushed-back crested helmet, tilted a little backwards, with almond-shaped eyes, a band of small snail curls across the brow, and a mouth clearly turned up at both corners.","desc":"Marble head of a warrior from the west pediment of the temple at Aegina, identified by the file's record as warrior III and dated to about 505–500 BCE; Glyptothek, Munich. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aphaia_pediment_head_W-III_Glyptothek_Munich_75.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-335","num":335,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Under life size and cut from Parian marble about 530 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named for a heavy garment that recent readings of her paint call a god's robe.","questions":["The overfall that gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> her modern name is divided along one arm and unbroken along the other, so the garment may never have been a peplos at all.","A heavy fall of cloth turns the lower half of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a pillar, and that stiffness once made her look like a late imitation of much older work."],"answer":"Peplos Kore","answerText":"Peplos Kore","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 525 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.18 m (3 ft 10 in)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">February 1886, on the Acropolis</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Peplos Kore</b> is a marble statue of a young woman set up on the Athenian Acropolis about 530 to 525 BCE, named in modern times for the heavy garment she seems to wear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was found west of the Erechtheum in February 1886, is cut from Parian marble, and stands 1.18 metres (3 feet 10 inches) without her plinth, well under life size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She is nearly whole, missing only the separately carved left forearm and the lower right corner of the skirt with the front of the feet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The left arm reached forward to hold an offering; the right hung at her side, closed on something thin enough to need a hole bored through the fist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The garment will not behave: the overfall is divided along the left arm but unbroken on the right, sewn to nothing, though pin holes show it was fastened at both shoulders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her paint was still vivid when she came out of the ground, and a watercolour made for the first report records more of it than survives now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That scheme ran a border of palmettes and lotus on a red ground beneath a green meander, set three green stripes on the girdle, and made her hair dark red and her lips red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A bronze wreath was fixed into holes cut round her head, some nails still in place, and the shaft of a <i>meniskos</i> stood broken off level with the crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her pillar-like lower half brought a charge of archaism, a later carver imitating an older manner, which her early cataloguers refused, matching her instead to the Moschophoros and placing her among the earliest Acropolis figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A reading of the colour now turns that round, taking the garment for an <i>ependytes</i>, the robe of the gods, and the stiff form for a cult image copied in stone, though much of any such reconstruction is guesswork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Perseus Digital Library, “Athens, Acropolis 679 (Sculpture),” Perseus Art and Archaeology Artifact Browser, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens,+Acropolis+679&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Guy Dickins, <i>Catalogue of the Acropolis Museum</i>, vol. 1, <i>Archaic Sculpture</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1912), 223–25, https://archive.org/details/catalogueacropo00cassgoog. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 174, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Guy Dickins, <i>Catalogue of the Acropolis Museum</i>, vol. 1, <i>Archaic Sculpture</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1912), 16, https://archive.org/details/catalogueacropo00cassgoog. [Open access]","Amanda Claridge, “Looking for Colour on Greek and Roman Sculpture,” <i>Journal of Art Historiography</i> 5 (December 2011): 4, http://arthistoriography.files.wordpress.com/2011/12/claridge.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Watercolor_of_Acropolis_kore_679_by_%C3%89mile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_%28ArchEph_1887%2C_pl_9%29.jpg/1280px-Watercolor_of_Acropolis_kore_679_by_%C3%89mile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_%28ArchEph_1887%2C_pl_9%29.jpg","title":"Peplos Kore","alt":"A colour plate showing the same statue twice, front and back: a standing young woman in a long skirt and a belted overfall, her hair in red braids, with painted borders of palmettes and meander on the garment.","desc":"The Peplos Kore as her colour survived at the moment of discovery, drawn for the excavation report of 1887. Watercolour by Emile Gillieron, lithograph by K. Grundman. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Watercolor_of_Acropolis_kore_679_by_%C3%89mile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_(ArchEph_1887,_pl_9).jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-336","num":336,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Broken into ten pieces to be smuggled abroad and recovered from a Paris dealer in 1937, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a life-size Attic kouros whose base epigram names a dead warrior.","questions":["Heavier and rounder in the flesh than any kouros before it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keeps the old frontal stance while its thighs, calves and buttocks swell out of the type.","An epigram on the base usually read with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asks a passer-by to stop and pity Kroisos, whom fierce Ares destroyed among the front ranks."],"answer":"Anavysos Kouros","answerText":"Anavysos Kouros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 520 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.94 m (6 ft 4 in)</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Anavysos Kouros</b> is a life-size marble statue of a naked young man, carved in Attica about 530 to 520 BCE and now in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands 1.94 metres (6 feet 4 inches) high, is cut from Parian marble, and holds the kouros scheme exactly: frontal, the weight even on both feet with the left one slightly advanced, arms down the flanks, fists closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What separates it from the type is the flesh: the chest is broad, the waist narrow, and the abdomen, thighs, calves and buttocks swell into contours its first publisher called almost feminine and in marked contrast to other kouroi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hair is set in ten spiral curls across the forehead, bound by a narrow fillet, and falls behind in twenty parallel tresses whose divisions curve rather than forming the continuous rectangular grid of earlier statues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Red paint survives on that fillet, on the hair of the head and pubes and on the irises, and the almond eyes with their prominent balls probably carried painted pupils too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The statue marked a grave, and it is usually read with an inscribed base carrying two lines of verse: 'Stand and pity by the <i>sema</i> of Kroisos, dead, whom fierce Ares once destroyed among the front ranks.'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That name is why the figure is also called the Kroisos Kouros, and a <i>sema</i> is the grave marker itself, the thing the passer-by is asked to stop beside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether base and statue belong together has been doubted, and most accounts keep the pity the verse asks for well away from the smiling figure above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was smuggled out of Greece by sea, broken into ten pieces for packing, and recovered by Greek police in Paris from a dealer, reaching the museum in three cases early in August 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Andreas Panayotakis reassembled it with plaster in the left arm and leg, and it went on show in the archaic gallery, the missing arm pieces arriving later from Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Perseus Digital Library, “Athens, NM 3851 (Sculpture),” Perseus Art and Archaeology Artifact Browser, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens,+NM+3851&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Alex. Philadelpheus, “The Anavysos Kouros,” <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 36 (1935–1936): 1–4, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1935-1936_36. [Open access]","Seth Nathaniel Estrin, “Objects of Pity: Art and Emotion in Archaic and Classical Greece” (PhD diss., University of California, Berkeley, 2016), 1, https://escholarship.org/uc/item/54d8k6q1. [Open access]","Paul Lemerle, “Musée National,” <i>Bulletin de correspondance hellénique</i> 61 (1937): 441–42, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bch_0007-4217_1937_num_61_1_2741. [Open access]","Paul Lemerle, “Musée National,” <i>Bulletin de correspondance hellénique</i> 62 (1938): 443–46, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bch_0007-4217_1938_num_62_1_2717. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","archaic greece","sculpture","attica"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Kouros_from_Anabyssos_Kroisos_NAMA_3851_102565.jpg/1280px-Kouros_from_Anabyssos_Kroisos_NAMA_3851_102565.jpg","title":"Anavysos Kouros","alt":"A life-size marble statue of a naked young man seen from slightly to his left, standing with the left foot advanced, arms held down along the flanks and both fists closed, long braided hair falling to the shoulders, and a paler plaster join across the lower left leg.","desc":"Kouros in Parian marble, 1.94 metres (6 feet 4 inches) tall, from about 530 BCE; found at Anavysos in Attica, where it stood at the grave of Kroisos. National Archaeological Museum of Athens 3851. Zde, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kouros_from_Anabyssos_Kroisos_NAMA_3851_102565.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-337","num":337,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Cut from Hymettian marble about 570 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a bearded Athenian carrying a calf across his shoulders to Athena, the animal's legs gathered in his crossed hands.","questions":["An inscription running right to left on the poros base of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> records that Rhombos, son of Palos, dedicated the statue, and the figure is generally read as Rhombos himself.","The beard was left as a smooth raised surface for paint and the eyes hollowed for inlays now lost, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keeps the kouros stance while carrying its offering."],"answer":"Moschophoros","answerText":"Moschophoros","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Moschophoros</b>, or calf-bearer, is a marble statue of a bearded man carrying a calf across his shoulders, dedicated on the Athenian Acropolis about 570 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cut from Hymettian marble and 1.65 metres (5 feet 5 inches) high without its base, it keeps the kouros pose, frontal with the left leg advanced and both feet flat, while the beard, the cloak and the animal mark the man out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A thin himation hangs from both shoulders nearly to the knees, leaving the chest bare, and is told from the flesh only by its flatter surface and the two incised lines of its border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The eyes were hollowed for inlays now lost, the beard left as a smooth raised surface for paint rather than carved, and green colour survives behind the calf, whose forelegs are twisted impossibly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Crossed arms and crossed legs bind man and animal into one group, a motif common in small bronzes but used here, as far as is known, on a large marble statue for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The greater part of the figure was found in 1864 in digging the foundations of the old museum on the Acropolis, and the base with the feet in 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two were joined, and the inscription on the poros block below runs from right to left to say that Rhombos, son of Palos, dedicated it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first letter of the name is broken away, so it is a restoration: a K was proposed when the base was first published, the reading Rh in 1890, and later opinion took the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The statue was at first called a Hermes Kriophoros, an Apollo Nomios or Theseus with the Marathonian bull, and is now read as the dedicator himself, not a portrait but a type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Conservation in 2014 replaced the plaster in-fillings standing for the missing lower legs with six steel supports, uncovered the original attachment to the base, and redefined the height from 1.61 to 1.65 metres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Perseus Digital Library, “Athens, Acropolis 624 (Sculpture),” Perseus Art and Archaeology Artifact Browser, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens,+Acropolis+624&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Guy Dickins, <i>Catalogue of the Acropolis Museum</i>, vol. 1, <i>Archaic Sculpture</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1912), 156–59, https://archive.org/details/catalogueoftheac005891mbp. [Open access]","Acropolis Museum, “Male statue with its base. The ‘Calf-bearer’,” Explore Collections, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/male-statue-its-base-calf-bearer. [Open access]","Acropolis Museum, “‘Calf-Bearer’,” Conservation of Antiquities, 2014, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/conservation-antiquities_calf-bearer. [Open access]","Henri Lechat, <i>La sculpture attique avant Phidias</i> (Paris: Alphonse Picard et fils, 1904), 106, https://archive.org/details/lasculptureattiq92lech. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","archaic greece","sculpture","attica","religion"],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.65 m (5 ft 5 in)</span></div>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/ACMA_Moschophoros.jpg","title":"Moschophoros","alt":"A marble statue of a bearded man standing frontally with a calf laid across his shoulders, its four legs gathered in his crossed hands at his chest, a thin cloak hanging from his shoulders and leaving the torso bare, the eye sockets empty, and the legs ending in restored blocks below the knees.","desc":"The Moschophoros photographed in the old Acropolis Museum in 2005, before the statue was remounted in 2014 and the in-fillings standing for its missing lower legs were removed. Acropolis Museum 624. Marsyas, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACMA_Moschophoros.jpg"}},
{"id":"gr-338","num":338,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Excavation at Dreros, Isthmia and Thermos shows how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> developed over two centuries from a one-room hall into a colonnaded building roofed in painted terracotta.","questions":["Since the burnt offering was made at an altar standing in front of it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> served chiefly as a house for the god's image and for the gifts left beside it.","Built of mudbrick on stone footings with wooden columns, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried its ornament in painted terracotta, and at Olympia its wooden posts were replaced one by one in stone."],"answer":"archaic temple","answerText":"archaic temple","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dreros temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 775 – 750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Isthmia temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thermos temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 630 – 610 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Olympia Heraion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["building","religion","art","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"abstract":"The <b>archaic temple</b> was a roofed house built for a god's image, and the sacrifice that gave it its purpose was burnt outside, at an altar before its door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest one known stands at Dreros on Crete, a single walled room about eleven metres by seven of small dressed stones, dated to the second quarter of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its plan is a hall rather than a shrine, with a stone-lined hearth in the middle of the floor, one or two axial columns carrying the roof, and a bench in a corner for offerings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beside that bench a later hand built a box of upright slabs packed with goats' horns, and on this altar of horns probably stood three figures of hammered bronze over wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing about it was monumental, and what the next two centuries added was scale, a colonnade and a roof of baked clay. <br><br> By about 700 BCE the temple of Poseidon at Isthmia had stone walls painted with panels outside and a surround of seven by nineteen wooden columns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> East of it lay the sacrificial ground, still strewn with ash, burnt bones and the smooth pebbles worshippers brought to share symbolically in the killing, and later a stone altar forty metres long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Thermos in Aetolia a temple of mudbrick and timber on stone foundations went up about 630 to 610 BCE, dressed in terracotta roof tiles, gutters, moulded antefixes, a sphinx for the gable and ten painted metope plaques.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under it lay a long Geometric hall ringed by an oval colonnade whose thick ash layers, holding burnt bone and bronze statuettes, show it too had served a god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Heraion at Olympia, of about 600 BCE and the earliest monumental temple in Greece, stood on wooden columns exchanged for stone as the timbers rotted, so its capitals differ by date<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>; at Corinth a stone temple of about 540 replaced one of about 625.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 8, 297-301, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","D. J. Blackman, \"Dreros,\" in <i>The Princeton Encyclopedia of Classical Sites</i>, ed. Richard Stillwell (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1976), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0006:entry=dreros. [Open access]","Oscar Broneer, \"Isthmia,\" in <i>The Princeton Encyclopedia of Classical Sites</i>, ed. Richard Stillwell (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1976), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0006:entry=isthmia. [Open access]","Nancy Bookidis, \"Thermos,\" in <i>The Princeton Encyclopedia of Classical Sites</i>, ed. Richard Stillwell (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1976), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0006:entry=thermos. [Open access]","\"Olympia, Temple of Hera,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Olympia%2C+Temple+of+Hera&object=Building. [Open access]","\"Corinth, Temple of Apollo,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Corinth%2C+Temple+of+Apollo&object=Building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-55-28.jpg/1920px-Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-55-28.jpg","title":"Temple of Apollo, Corinth","desc":"The archaic Doric temple of Apollo at Corinth, built about 540 BCE on the site of an earlier temple of about 625. Berthold Werner, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-55-28.jpg","alt":"Seven weathered limestone Doric columns standing on a stepped platform, five of them still carrying a stretch of architrave, with a mountain ridge behind."}},
{"id":"gr-339","num":339,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"A baseless column of twenty shallow flutes, capped by a convex echinus under a square abacus, is the mark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the older of the two systems Greek temples were built in.","questions":["Triglyphs were explained in antiquity as boards nailed over sawn beam ends, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was read as carpentry turned to stone, with mutules under the cornice standing for rafter ends.","A triglyph must sit over each column and another must close the frieze, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met at every corner a conflict solved only by a wide metope or a narrowed bay."],"answer":"Doric order","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Aphaia, Aegina</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parthenon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">447 – 432 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vitruvius</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Doric order</b> is one of the two systems of column and entablature that Greek architects built their temples in, and Vitruvius, writing in the 1st century BCE, held it the first to arise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Doric column stands straight on the platform with no base of its own, and its shaft carries twenty shallow flutes meeting in sharp edges rather than the flat fillets of the Ionic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital is a spreading convex moulding, the echinus, under a square slab called the abacus, and abacus, echinus, necking and the shaft top were cut from one block.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The shaft was usually built of drums pegged together with wood, though at the temple of Aphaia on Aegina, of about 500 to 480 BCE, all but three columns were cut in one piece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Archaic shafts taper strongly from foot to summit, which lent an air of stability, and the flutes were cut after the column stood so the edges came out sharp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Above the columns lies the architrave, a plain band closed by a fillet with short strips and rows of peg-like guttae under it; the fillet was painted red, the strips and guttae blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The frieze above alternates triglyphs, blocks cut with vertical channels, with metopes, the roughly square panels between them, which were often filled with sculpture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vitruvius derived it from carpentry: triglyphs stood for boards fixed over the sawn ends of the ceiling beams and painted with blue wax, metopes for the gaps between, and the sloping mutules under the cornice for the rafter ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also records a rule the order cannot keep, since a triglyph should stand over the centre of every column and another should close the frieze at the corner, and at the corner the two collide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Either the corner metope runs half a triglyph too wide or the end bay is narrowed instead, and the narrowing was built: on the Parthenon the corner bays measure 3.68 metres (12 feet) against 4.29 (14 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Doric order","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","art","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.1, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 47-48, 79-80, 87-88, 104, 184-85, 218, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","\"Aegina, Temple of Aphaia,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Aegina%2C+Temple+of+Aphaia&object=Building. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.2, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.3, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","\"Athens, Parthenon,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+Parthenon&object=Building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Doric-order-labeled.jpg","title":"The parts of the Doric order","desc":"A rendering of the corner of a Doric temple with the parts named: the architrave with its taenia, regulae and guttae, the frieze of triglyphs and metopes, and the mutules, geison and sima above. J. Matthew Harrington (Nefasdicere), CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doric-order-labeled.jpg","alt":"A grey rendering of the corner of a Greek temple, its fluted columns carrying a plain band, a row of channelled blocks alternating with blank panels, and a projecting cornice, with the parts labelled in English."}},
{"id":"gr-340","num":340,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Named for the Greek cities of the Asia Minor coast, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives its columns a moulded base of their own and a capital wound into a pair of spiral volutes.","questions":["A column of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands about nine diameters high on 24 flutes, and the architrave above it is stepped back into three overlapping bands rather than left as one plain face.","Builders in Asia Minor set dentils straight on the architrave while Athens kept a continuous sculpted frieze, two regional forms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the Doric entablature kept one fixed arrangement."],"answer":"Ionic order","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ephesos temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">columns c. 560 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Erechtheion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">421 – 406 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Proportions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 diameters, 24 flutes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ionic order</b> is one of the two systems of column and entablature Greek architects built their temples in, and it takes its name from the Ionian cities of the Asia Minor coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vitruvius held it younger than the Doric and read it as a woman's figure, the base standing in place of a shoe, the volutes hanging like curled ringlets, and the flutes falling like the folds of a matron's robe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An Ionic column rises from a moulded base of its own, in two families: the Asiatic, a torus set over a hollow trochilos, and the Attic, two tori separated by a scotia, which reached its settled form at the Erechtheion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its shaft carries 24 flutes, deeper than the Doric and parted by flat fillets rather than sharp edges, though archaic builders used other numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital's mark is a pair of volutes, spirals wound out from a small circle called the eye, set out by Vitruvius as quarter-circles each half the eye's width smaller than the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It is the slenderer of the two: Vitruvius allows the Ionic column nine diameters against the Doric's seven, and the north porch of the Erechtheion stands 7.64 metres (25 feet) high on a lower diameter of 0.817 metres (2 feet 8 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The architrave is broken into three overlapping bands, the fasciae, where the Doric presents one plain face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The frieze runs unbroken, taking the name zophoros when it carries figure sculpture, where the Doric frieze is cut into triglyphs and metopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The entablature is not one thing, though: in Asia Minor a jutting course of dentils could sit straight on the architrave with no frieze, as at Priene, while Athenian builders kept the frieze and left the dentils out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor was any of it fixed at the start: in the archaic temple of Artemis at Ephesos the flutes do not space evenly under one capital, and the variation of parts there has been called as marked as in a Gothic building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ionic order","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","art","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.1, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 83-84, 101, 103, 108, 139, 184-85, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 3.5, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","\"Athens, Erechtheion,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+Erechtheion&object=Building. [Open access]","W. R. Lethaby, <i>Greek Buildings Represented by Fragments in the British Museum</i> (London: B. T. Batsford, 1908), 22-23, 33, https://archive.org/details/greekbuildingsre00leth. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Vitruvius_the_Ten_Books_on_Architecture_-_The_Ionic_Order_according_to_Vitruvius.png","title":"The Ionic order, drawn to Vitruvius's proportions","desc":"A plate from Morris Hicky Morgan's 1914 translation of Vitruvius, captioned \"The Ionic order according to Vitruvius compared with the order of the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus\" and noting the difference between the Roman and the Greek relation of the baluster-side of the capital to the echinus. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vitruvius_the_Ten_Books_on_Architecture_-_The_Ionic_Order_according_to_Vitruvius.png","alt":"A line drawing of two Ionic orders side by side, each a fluted column on a moulded base under a capital of spiral volutes carrying an architrave of three overlapping bands; the left is closed by a plain sculptured frieze, the right by a row of small blocks, with a compass diagram of the volute's spiral between them."}},
{"id":"gr-341","num":341,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"A Greek temple built on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries a single ring of columns right round its walls, leaving a covered walk between that colonnade and the enclosed building inside it.","questions":["Doubling the surrounding colonnade of a temple laid out on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> makes it dipteral, while engaging those columns against the cella wall instead makes the building pseudoperipteral.","The same <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was set round cellas of quite different shapes, which is one reason for thinking the ring was applied to the temple rather than grown out of it."],"answer":"peripteral plan","answerText":"peripteral plan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple C</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zeus, Olympia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 470 – 456 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vitruvius</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 by 11 columns</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stylobate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3:1 archaic to 2:1 later</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>peripteral plan</b> sets a single ring of columns right round the walls of a Greek temple, so that what a worshipper met was a colonnade with a house inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vitruvius lists it among seven elementary forms, the temple in antis, the prostyle, the amphiprostyle, the peripteral, the pseudodipteral, the dipteral and the hypaethral, each named for what its columns do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His peripteral temple carries six columns in front and six behind with eleven along each flank counting the corners, standing one intercolumniation clear of the walls to leave a walk right round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He calls that walk the pteroma, and the house within keeps porches of its own, a pronaos in front and usually an opisthodomos behind, the cella between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Doubling the ring makes a temple dipteral, as at Ephesos; engaged columns suggesting a ring that is not there make it pseudoperipteral, as at Akragas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It was not the natural end of a series: had the temple grown from in antis through double in antis to peripteral, cella and surround would have agreed from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They do not, and the colonnade is better read as deliberately applied as a sign of religious distinction, a considerable time passing before it fitted the cella within.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same ring was laid round quite different buildings: a cella in antis at Temple D at Selinus, one doubly in antis at the temple of Zeus at Olympia, a prostyle at Temple C, an amphiprostyle at the Parthenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its proportions moved: archaic stylobates often approach three to one, classical ones about two and a half, Hellenistic ones about two, though exceptions are frequent enough to make the plan weak evidence of date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Temple C at Selinus, of the first half of the 6th century BCE, is six by seventeen, its early date attested by that elongated plan, where the temple of Zeus at Olympia, of about 456 BCE, is six by thirteen on a stylobate of 64.12 by 27.68 metres (210 by 91 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","art","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 3.2, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.4, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 127, 308-13, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","\"Selinus, Temple C,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Selinus%2C+Temple+C&object=Building. [Open access]","\"Olympia, Temple of Zeus,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Olympia%2C+Temple+of+Zeus&object=Building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Peripteros_1.1_-_Anthony_Rich%2C_Illustrated_Companion_to_the_Latin_Dictionary%2C_and_Greek_Lexicon%2C_p._491.jpg","title":"Peripteros","desc":"Peripteros. Woodcut from Anthony Rich, The Illustrated Companion to the Latin Dictionary and Greek Lexicon (1849), p. 491. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Anthony Rich, Illustrated Companion to the Latin Dictionary and Greek Lexicon (1849), p. 491, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peripteros_1.1_-_Anthony_Rich,_Illustrated_Companion_to_the_Latin_Dictionary,_and_Greek_Lexicon,_p._491.jpg","alt":"Ground plan of a temple: a single ring of columns runs right round a rectangular walled building with a porch at each end."}},
{"id":"gr-342","num":342,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Oak columns at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were exchanged for stone one at a time as the timbers rotted, so its Doric capitals differ in profile according to when each was replaced.","questions":["Every fourth year the Sixteen Women wove a robe for Hera and held foot races for girls at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose track was the Olympic stadium shortened by about a sixth.","Pausanias found the quoit of Iphitus and a table by Colotes inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and was told a wounded soldier’s body had turned up in its decayed roof."],"answer":"Heraion of Olympia","answerText":"Heraion of Olympia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Doric peripteral, 6 by 16 columns</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stylobate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18.75 by 50.01 m (62 by 164 feet)</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Heraion of Olympia</b>, Hera's temple in the sanctuary of Zeus, dates from about 600 BCE and is the earliest monumental temple in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is Doric and peripteral, six columns by sixteen on a two-stepped platform, its stylobate 18.75 by 50.01 metres (62 by 164 feet), with an east-opening cella between a pronaos and a deeper opisthodomos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its walls were sun-dried brick and its columns and entablature wood, the materials of a Mycenaean palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oaken columns were exchanged for stone one at a time as the timbers rotted, so the capitals run from archaic through developed to decadent by the date of each replacement, and one oak still stood in the rear chamber in Pausanias's day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Buttresses ran along the cella walls, four a side, with a later colonnade of fourteen Doric columns in front of them making two narrow aisles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias gives it 169 feet long, 63 wide and not short of 50 high, credits it to the people of Scillus about eight years after Oxylus took the throne of Elis, and names no architect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every fourth year the Sixteen Women wove Hera a robe and held the Heraea, foot races for unmarried girls in three age classes, hair down and right shoulder bared, over the Olympic track shortened by a sixth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Winners took olive crowns, a portion of the cow sacrificed to Hera, and the right to set up inscribed statues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside stood a table of ivory and gold by Colotes holding the victors' crowns, the quoit of Iphitus with the Olympic truce lettered round it in a circle, and a small ivory couch called Hippodameia's toy; Polemo of Ilium, in the earlier 2nd century BCE, listed 33 silver cups and gold vessels kept there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its age was the point: Polemo called it the old temple of Hera, and the guide Aristarchus told Pausanias that repairing the decayed roof turned up a wounded soldier's body between the tiles and the ceiling, from the battle in the Altis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["building","archaeology","religion","greece","archaic","place"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.16, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.20, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","\"Olympia, Temple of Hera,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Olympia%2C+Temple+of+Hera&object=Building. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 17, 21, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","J. G. Frazer, <i>Studies in Greek Scenery, Legend and History</i> (London: Macmillan, 1917), 134, 138, 144, https://archive.org/details/studiesingreeksc00frazuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Curtius_Olympia_3_t_02.jpg","title":"The Heraion of Olympia under excavation","desc":"The Heraion seen from the south-east during the German excavations, with workmen standing at points of the plan. Photograph by Gebr. Romaidis of Patras, published in Ernst Curtius, ed., Die Ausgrabungen zu Olympia, vol. 3 (Berlin, 1877-78). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Gebr. Romaidis, Patras, in Ernst Curtius, ed., Die Ausgrabungen zu Olympia, vol. 3 (Berlin, 1877-78), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curtius_Olympia_3_t_02.jpg","alt":"A temple reduced to its lowest courses and stumps of columns, with drums and capitals lying on the ground and men standing among them on a hillside."}},
{"id":"gr-343","num":343,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Built about 580 BCE on the island of Korkyra, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries the earliest sculptured pediment surviving from a Greek temple, a limestone gable filled by a colossal winged Gorgon.","questions":["Doric and pseudo-dipteral, eight columns by seventeen on a platform of 47.9 by 22.4 metres (157 by 73 feet), the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divided its cella into three aisles with two interior rows of ten columns.","Filling a shallow gable by size rather than depth, the sculptor of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the centre to a Gorgon and her beasts and squeezed Zeus and a giant into one corner."],"answer":"Temple of Artemis at Corfu","answerText":"Temple of Artemis at Corfu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 580 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 by 17 columns</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pediment</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22.16 m wide</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Temple of Artemis at Corfu</b>, on the island the Greeks called Korkyra, went up about 580 BCE and carries the earliest sculptured pediment that survives from a Greek temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is Doric and pseudo-dipteral, eight columns by seventeen on a platform of 47.9 by 22.4 metres (157 by 73 feet), with an east-opening cella between a pronaos and an opisthodomos, each of them carrying two columns between the wall ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two interior rows of ten columns apiece divided the cella into three aisles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its west pediment is limestone, 22.16 metres (73 feet) wide and 3.15 metres (10 feet 4 inches) high at the apex, and at the centre stands a colossal winged Gorgon, snakes in her hair and eyes staring, in the kneeling posture archaic art used for running flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pegasus and Chrysaor stand at her shoulders, and a great cat fills the breadth on either side, head turned to the front and body in profile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Hesiod has the two of them spring from Medusa at the moment Perseus cut off her head, so the pediment does not show that moment but sets all three together and whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The corners work on a much smaller scale: Zeus swings his thunderbolt at a giant at one end, and at the other a seated woman sits before a bearded man lying dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A shallow triangle is awkward to fill, and this sculptor filled it by size rather than by depth, giving the Gorgon and the cats the height and squeezing the gods into the ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The figures are square in build and conceived from front and side at once, chest full-face above legs in profile, with much of the detail incised rather than modelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Corinth held Corcyra and the staring Gorgon was a Corinthian motif; the same group stood at both ends of the roof, which later temples did not repeat, and in the Archaeological Museum of Corfu, which holds the pediment, the monster is taken for Artemis herself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["building","art","archaeology","religion","greece","archaic"],"sources":["\"Corcyra, Temple of Artemis,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Corcyra%2C+Temple+of+Artemis&object=Building. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 159, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","\"Corcyra, Temple of Artemis, west pediment,\" cast 12, Museum of Classical Archaeology, University of Cambridge, https://museum.classics.cam.ac.uk/collections/casts/corcyra-temple-artemis-west-pediment. [Open access]","\"Archaeological Museum of Corfu,\" Ministry of Culture and Sports, Odysseus, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/1/eh151.jsp?obj_id=3420. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Theogony</i> 270-283, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0130%3Acard%3D270. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/West_pediment_from_the_Temple_of_Artemis_in_Corfu.jpg/1920px-West_pediment_from_the_Temple_of_Artemis_in_Corfu.jpg","title":"West pediment of the Temple of Artemis at Corfu","desc":"The reconstructed west pediment, with the winged Gorgon at the centre, a great cat on either side and small figures in the corners. Dr.K., CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Dr.K., CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_pediment_from_the_Temple_of_Artemis_in_Corfu.jpg","alt":"A wide shallow stone gable. A crouching winged figure with a grimacing face fills the centre, a long cat lies on each side of her, and tiny figures stand in the narrow corners."}},
{"id":"gr-344","num":344,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Herodotus says the islanders of Siphnos were the richest of their day and built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Delphi from a tenth of the yield of their gold and silver mines.","questions":["In place of the two columns that would ordinarily stand between its wall ends, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Delphi carried draped women wearing a basket-shaped head-dress, among the earliest caryatids in Greek building.","A carved frieze runs round all four sides of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its gods and heroes labelled in letters once painted red, and its north face shows the giants fighting the Olympians."],"answer":"Siphnian Treasury","answerText":"Siphnian Treasury","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 - 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ionic, 2 columns in antis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Footprint</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8.90 by 6.35 m (29 by 21 feet)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Frieze</span><span class=\"dt-v\">0.64 m (2 feet 1 inch) high</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Siphnian Treasury</b> is a small marble building put up at Delphi by the islanders of Siphnos towards the end of the 6th century BCE, and it is the most richly decorated structure the sanctuary has yielded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It stands on the south side of the Sacred Way a few paces west of the Sicyonian treasury, raised on a tower-like foundation of tufa that lifts it clear of the road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On that platform rose an Ionic building in antis with its front to the west, 8.90 by 6.35 metres (29 by 21 feet), its entablature of Parian marble and its mouldings cut with a depth and clearness matched almost nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two supports between the projecting wall ends were not columns but draped women wearing the basket-shaped head-dress called a polos, among the earliest free-standing caryatids in Greek architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus says the Siphnians were then the richest of the islanders, from gold and silver mines worked on the island, and that they made the treasury out of a tenth of their income.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias adds that the god had ordered the tithe, and that when greed made them withhold it the sea flooded the mines and hid them from sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A sculptured frieze 0.64 metres (2 feet 1 inch) high ran round all four sides and has been recovered almost entire, its figures named by inscriptions once picked out in red paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The north side shows the gods fighting the giants and is the finest of the four, with the Mother of the Gods driving into the battle in a car drawn by lions that savage a giant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The east side sets a Homeric combat before an assembly of seated deities, Menelaus, Hector and Aeneas among the warriors whose names are cut beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its pediment carries the struggle of Heracles and Apollo for the Delphic tripod with Athena standing between them, and the building is called Siphnian chiefly because Pausanias mentions that treasury immediately after the Sicyonian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["building","archaeology","history","art","greece","archaic period","delphi"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.57, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.11.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","J. G. Frazer, <i>Pausanias's Description of Greece, Translated with a Commentary</i>, vol. 5, <i>Commentary on Books IX., X.</i> (London: Macmillan, 1898), 272-76, https://archive.org/details/pausaniassdescri05pausuoft. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 135-40, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","Harold North Fowler and James Rignall Wheeler, <i>A Handbook of Greek Archaeology</i> (New York: American Book Company, 1909), 133, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeka00fowluoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Siphniens_-_2_-_%28Gl.III.A.21%29.jpg/1920px-Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Siphniens_-_2_-_%28Gl.III.A.21%29.jpg","title":"The reconstructed facade of the Siphnian Treasury","desc":"Adolphe Giraudon's photograph of about 1910, showing the pediment, the sculptured frieze and the carved capitals of the treasury as reassembled at Delphi. The printed caption below calls it the treasury of the Cnidians, one of the identifications proposed after the excavation. Adolphe Giraudon, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Adolphe Giraudon, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Siphniens_-_2_-_(Gl.III.A.21).jpg","alt":"A triangular pediment carved with standing figures and a horse, set over a long sculptured band of seated figures, chariots and galloping horses, the whole resting on an entablature carried by two carved capitals."}},
{"id":"gr-345","num":345,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Pausanias found no treasure left in any of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the small stone houses that single cities built along the Sacred Way to hold what they had dedicated to Apollo.","questions":["The Thebans built one from the spoils of Leuctra and the Athenians one from Marathon, two of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Pausanias passed in order as he climbed towards the temple.","Herodotus says the six golden bowls of Gyges stood in one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that went by a city's name although a tyrant, not the city, had paid for it."],"answer":"treasuries at Delphi","answerText":"treasuries at Delphi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sicyonian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 - 601 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Syracusan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 413 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Theban</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 371 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>treasuries at Delphi</b> were small stone houses put up inside the sanctuary of Apollo by single cities, and by single men, to hold the offerings they had dedicated to the god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Votive offerings, weapons and other valuables were stored in them, and they probably served too as gathering places for the city that had built them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They lined the Sacred Way, the paved road climbing from the south-eastern entrance to the temple, whose course has been likened to a great S reversed and whose stones are grooved against slipping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias described the monuments of the sanctuary in strict topographical order, and the French excavations laid them bare in that same succession, which is how most of the treasuries are identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He gives their reasons: the Thebans built from the spoils of Leuctra, the Athenians from Datis's army at Marathon, the Syracusans from the great Athenian disaster, the Potidaeans out of piety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Others paid for a building instead: the god ordered the Siphnians to give a tenth of the revenue of their mines, while of the Cnidians Pausanias cannot tell whether they built for a victory or to show their wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus records six golden bowls of Gyges weighing thirty talents in the treasury of the Corinthians, which he says was in truth the treasury of Cypselus, and a gold lion of Croesus stood there too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sicyonians built twice, an earlier round house of the 7th century BCE whose fluted columns and curved architrave were buried in the foundations of the 6th-century building that replaced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Athenian treasury, about 10 by 6 metres (33 by 20 feet) and Doric with two columns between its wall ends, carried thirty marble metopes of Heracles and Theseus worked with the precision of fine Attic vases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A step of it still reads 'Athenians' and 'Marathon', and one of the hymns to Apollo is cut on its walls with the musical notation;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> yet Pausanias found no treasure left in any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","archaeology","history","art","religion","greece","archaic period","delphi"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.11, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.14, 1.50-51, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","J. G. Frazer, <i>Pausanias's Description of Greece, Translated with a Commentary</i>, vol. 5, <i>Commentary on Books IX., X.</i> (London: Macmillan, 1898), 259, 270-82, https://archive.org/details/pausaniassdescri05pausuoft. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 203-4, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","Harold North Fowler and James Rignall Wheeler, <i>A Handbook of Greek Archaeology</i> (New York: American Book Company, 1909), 154, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeka00fowluoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Vue_de_Delphes_avec_le_Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Ath%C3%A9niens_et_le_Mus%C3%A9e._-_%28Gl.III.A.3%29.jpg/1920px-Vue_de_Delphes_avec_le_Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Ath%C3%A9niens_et_le_Mus%C3%A9e._-_%28Gl.III.A.3%29.jpg","title":"The Athenian treasury on its terrace at Delphi","desc":"A photograph of about 1910 by the Alinari brothers looking across the sanctuary of Apollo, with the Athenian treasury on its terrace at the right, the foundations and scattered blocks of other buildings running down the slope below it, and the museum in the valley beyond. The printed caption reads 'DELPHES - Grece. Tresor des Atheniens et vue du Musee'. Fratelli Alinari, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Fratelli Alinari, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_de_Delphes_avec_le_Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Ath%C3%A9niens_et_le_Mus%C3%A9e._-_(Gl.III.A.3).jpg","alt":"A small stone building with two columns in its front standing on a terrace on a bare mountainside, with courses of ruined walls and scattered blocks below it and a white-roofed building in the valley beyond."}},
{"id":"gr-346","num":346,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Whoever carved <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to fit one group into a long triangle whose centre seems to want a colossal figure and whose angles have barely room for a man lying down.","questions":["Sculptors solved the corners of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with fallen warriors, reclining river gods, or the sun rising from the sea at one end and the moon sinking at the other.","The quiet scene went on the front gable and the violent one at the back, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Olympia sets a still chariot team against a fight of Lapiths and Centaurs."],"answer":"pediment sculpture","answerText":"pediment sculpture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Poros gables</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aegina</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Pediment sculpture</b> filled the triangular field between the horizontal cornice of a Greek temple and the raking cornices of its gable, a field whose back wall is the tympanum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the frieze it is one of only two places the orders prescribe for sculpture, and acroteria were set on the three angles of every pediment besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The field is the difficulty: its height runs from a centre wanting a colossal figure to angles with barely room for a reclining one, and a single group may not vary much in the size of its figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among the earliest that survive are the poros gables of the Athenian Acropolis, almost all combats, which in every case give the antagonist a snake’s or a fish’s tail to fill the angles with its coils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One, around 570 BCE, is 14.1 metres (46 feet) long and 1.44 metres (4 feet 9 inches) high at the centre: lions devour a bull in the middle, Heracles wrestles Triton on the left, and on the right stands a winged three-bodied creature whose identity is unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Relief gave way to figures worked almost in the round and merely set against the background, which the architectural frame’s strong projection all but forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A chariot was the commonest device: the length of car and horses takes up the narrowing field and separates the middle group from the smaller figures at the sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The corners were filled with the dying, or with reclining river gods: at Olympia, where Pausanias notes that the pediment narrows again, they are the Alpheius and the Cladeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Symmetry became the rule: at Aegina, where an earlier set was discarded and replaced, twelve figures besides Athena stood in the west gable and at least ten in the east, each answering one across the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> And the front took the quieter scene and the back the violent one, so Olympia set the still preparation for a chariot race against Lapiths and Centaurs, and the Parthenon the birth of Athena against her contest with Poseidon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","art","sculpture","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.24.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D24. [Open access]","Harold North Fowler and James Rignall Wheeler, <i>A Handbook of Greek Archaeology</i> (New York: American Book Company, 1909), 121, 202–04, 215–16, 223–26, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeka00fowluoft. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 39–42, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","“Hekatompedon. West pediment,” Ακρ. 3, Acropolis Museum, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/hekatompedon-west-pediment. [Open access]","“Aegina, Temple of Aphaia,” building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Aegina,+Temple+of+Aphaia&object=Building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Olimpyamuseum002.jpg/1920px-Olimpyamuseum002.jpg","title":"The west pediment of the Temple of Zeus at Olympia","desc":"Sculptures of the west pediment of the Temple of Zeus at Olympia, arranged as they stood in the gable, in the Archaeological Museum of Olympia. Made by Numbers, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olimpyamuseum002.jpg","alt":"Marble figures from a temple gable set out in a long triangle, tapering from a tall standing figure at the centre to figures lying down at each end."}},
{"id":"gr-347","num":347,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Because the square field of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> practically limits a group to two or three figures, Doric temples favoured subjects that divided neatly into separate scenes.","questions":["Where the Ionic frieze ran as one unbroken band above the architrave, the Doric frieze was interrupted, and its sculpture went into the square field of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between projecting triglyphs.","At Selinus, Perseus beheading the Gorgon fills one square <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Heracles carrying the Cercopes slung from a pole fills another, each figure turning full front above legs in profile."],"answer":"metope","answerText":"metope","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Selinus Temple C</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest metopes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","art","sculpture","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"abstract":"A <b>metope</b> is the roughly square panel between two triglyphs in the frieze of a Doric building, and its character follows from that: the Ionic frieze runs unbroken, while the Doric frieze wants decoration interrupted into separate fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many were left undecorated, a plain white surface of stucco or marble, but the square face was an inviting field for a decorator, and the projecting triglyphs either side framed whatever went into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some were given individual frames besides, broad bands above and below and narrow ones at the sides, as on the box-like Perseus, Heracles and Apollo panels from Selinus now at Palermo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Depth of recess governed the carving: a deep triangular gable took figures nearly in the round where the shallower panel took half relief, and blue triglyphs projected clearly from surfaces of red or white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The square field practically limits a composition to groups of two figures, or sometimes three, and a third is in most cases cramped close against the margin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>  <br><br> The favourite subjects are naturally those that divide easily into separate scenes: the labours of Heracles and of Theseus, or combats of gods with giants and of Greeks with centaurs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A single scene is occasionally spread over two panels or more, but such a treatment could only be made tolerable by accepted convention, and to that it never attained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Temple C at Selinus, put up on the highest point of the acropolis in the first half of the 6th century BCE, carried carved panels on its triglyph frieze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its earliest set, little later than the beginning of that century, shows Perseus beheading the Gorgon before Athena and Heracles carrying the Cercopes slung head downwards from a pole, chests full to the front above legs in profile, the heads disproportionately large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What such a field carried is not always recoverable: the tufa slabs found at the Sicyonian treasury at Delphi were read as metopes by one scholar and as parts of a frieze by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 148-49, 221-22, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 42-44, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 162-63, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","\"Selinus, Temple C (Building),\" Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Selinus,+Temple+C&object=Building. [Open access]","J. G. Frazer, <i>Pausanias's Description of Greece, Translated with a Commentary</i>, vol. 5, <i>Commentary on Books IX., X.</i> (London: Macmillan, 1898), 270, https://archive.org/details/pausaniassdescri05pausuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Metopa_decapita_Meduca_-_Flickr_-_Rino_Porrovecchio.jpg/1920px-Metopa_decapita_Meduca_-_Flickr_-_Rino_Porrovecchio.jpg","title":"metope","desc":"Metope with Perseus beheading Medusa, watched by Athena, from Temple C at Selinunte, in the Museo archeologico regionale Antonio Salinas, Palermo. Rino Porrovecchio, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metopa_decapita_Meduca_-_Flickr_-_Rino_Porrovecchio.jpg","alt":"Carved stone panel showing Perseus driving a sword into the neck of Medusa, with Athena standing at the left edge"}},
{"id":"gr-348","num":348,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Archaic builders in Aeolis and on Lesbos raised the volutes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> vertically out of the shaft and set a palmette in the triangle between them.","questions":["Strictly two-faced and unornamented on its narrow sides, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was hard to place at a building's corner and was probably used indoors or on votive columns standing alone.","Made only in the second half of the 6th century BCE and largely confined to the north-east Aegean, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a short career that its awkward two-sided form helps explain."],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","answer":"Aeolic capital","answerText":"Aeolic capital","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 500 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Aeolic capital</b> is an Archaic column head on which two spiral volutes rise vertically out of the shaft and then spring outward, leaving a triangle between them that is filled by a great palmette.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath the volutes a leaf-carved torus, or a group of smaller mouldings, binds the two spirals together, and below that hangs a girdle of overhanging leaves whose serrated lower edge is cut off from the shaft by a deep cavetto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Examples come from temples at Neandria in the Troad and at Nape on Lesbos, and from finds at Mitylene, at Larissa near Smyrna and at Aegae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were made in the second half of the 6th century BCE, across an area covering roughly the north-eastern quarter of the Aegean basin, from Lesbos and Aeolis as far as eastern Thrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How the form developed is not clear: some examples are very plain while others carry subsidiary volutes and decorative detail, and nobody has managed to order those traits chronologically or fit them into a coherent stemma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Ionic capital differs in each of these respects, since its volutes lie horizontally and are joined by a cushion, while the girdle of hanging leaves has hardened into the egg-and-tongue echinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the Aeolic form is strictly two-faced and carries no ornament on its narrow sides, it is harder to fit into a building than an Ionic one, and the usual Ionic solution at a corner was impracticable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That has led scholars to suppose it was used by preference in interior colonnades, or on isolated votive columns standing on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The difficulties it posed probably explain both its slight success in the Greek world and the brevity of its career.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its origin is generally traced to the Levant, and the very word 'proto-Aeolic', coined for the Levantine and Cypriot pieces treated as prototypes, already encodes a view about which way the influence ran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","art","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["William J. Anderson and R. Phené Spiers, <i>The Architecture of Ancient Greece</i>, rev. and rewritten by William Bell Dinsmoor (London: B. T. Batsford, 1927), 71-72, 101, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.209411. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 94, 202, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","Jacques des Courtils, \"Alâzeytin Kalesi et l'architecture éolique,\" <i>Anatolia Antiqua</i> 19 (2011): 385-92, https://www.persee.fr/doc/anata_1018-1946_2011_num_19_1_1108. [Open access]","Antoine Hermary, \"Chypre et la Carie à la fin de l'époque archaïque,\" <i>Anatolia Antiqua</i> 19 (2011): 371-75, https://www.persee.fr/doc/anata_1018-1946_2011_num_19_1_1106. [Open access]","Thierry Petit, \"La forêt qui cache l'arbre. De l'‘Arbre-de-la-Vie’ levantin aux diptères ioniques et à l'ordre corinthien,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 33 (2008): 309-27, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ktema_0221-5896_2008_num_33_1_1110. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Aeolic_Capital.jpg/1920px-Aeolic_Capital.jpg","title":"Aeolic capital from Old Smyrna","desc":"An Aeolic capital from the temple of Athena at Old Smyrna, in the Izmir Museum of History and Art. Isabeau, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aeolic_Capital.jpg","alt":"A limestone capital whose two large spiral volutes spring outward from a central block, with a small carved palmette in the triangle between them."}},
{"id":"gr-349","num":349,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Greek foundries built a clay core, coated it in wax, modelled the surface on the wax and then melted it out, a hollow method called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that made life-size statues possible.","questions":["Solid bronze statues wasted costly metal and were unmanageably heavy, so sixth-century Greek workshops turned to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which leaves only a thin skin of metal around a fireproof core.","Greek smiths once beat bronze into plates and riveted them together limb by limb, a laborious method that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced for anything larger than a statuette."],"answer":"lost-wax casting","answerText":"lost-wax casting","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hollow casting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in Greece, c. 600 – 501 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Samian founders</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Rhoikos and Theodoros, fl. c. 540 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","technology","art","sculpture","greece","archaic"],"abstract":"<b>Lost-wax casting</b> makes a hollow bronze object by modelling it in wax, encasing the wax in clay, and melting the wax away so that molten metal can fill the space it leaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything turns on the core, a mass of fireproof material shaped like the finished piece but standing back from its surface by the thickness of the metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wax may be laid straight onto that core and modelled there, or a clay original moulded first and a wax skin brushed inside the empty mould before the core is packed behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bronze rods driven through the wax hold the core steady once it has run out, tubes let the wax escape and the metal in, and vents release the trapped air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mould and crucible were always clay, and melting a bronze of nine parts copper to one of tin sometimes needed more than 1,000 °C (1,830 °F) while clay turns unstable beyond 1,100 °C (2,010 °F), leaving the founder a narrow band and no instrument to measure it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Greeks worked bronze long before they cast statues in it, and the earliest large figures were cast solid or built up from hammered plates, a technique they called <i>sphyrelaton</i>, 'beaten with the hammer'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias saw one at Sparta, a Zeus he took for the oldest bronze anywhere, made not in a single piece but of limbs hammered separately and fastened with nails rather than solder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both methods seem to have lasted into the 6th century BCE, when hollow casting arrived and spared the waste of costly metal and the dead weight of a solid statue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias credited the discovery to two Samians, Rhoikos and Theodoros, a tradition read since as the Greek adoption of a far older craft rather than its invention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Little survives, since bronze was always worth melting down, and the workshops are known mainly from casting pits such as one found on the Athenian Agora in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>Catalogue of the Bronzes, Greek, Roman, and Etruscan, in the Department of Greek and Roman Antiquities, British Museum</i> (London: Printed by Order of the Trustees, 1899), xxix–xxxiii, xl, https://archive.org/details/catalogueofbronz00brit. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1911), 23–26, https://archive.org/details/rshandbookofgree00garduoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.17.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.14.8, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Gerhard Zimmer, “Les ateliers de fabrication des grands bronzes,” in <i>L’artisanat métallurgique dans les sociétés anciennes en Méditerranée occidentale: techniques, lieux et formes de production</i>, Publications de l’École française de Rome 332 (Rome: École française de Rome, 2004), 341–51, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_2004_act_332_1_7755. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Terracotta_casting_mould_for_a_statue_of_Apollo._AGMA.jpg/1920px-Terracotta_casting_mould_for_a_statue_of_Apollo._AGMA.jpg","title":"Clay casting mould for a bronze Apollo","desc":"Terracotta casting mould for the lower half of a bronze statue of Apollo, found in a casting pit near the temple of Apollo; middle of the 6th century BCE, Ancient Agora Museum, Athens. Dorieo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terracotta_casting_mould_for_a_statue_of_Apollo._AGMA.jpg","alt":"Fragments of a thick clay mould, reassembled around a modern support into the shape of a standing figure, in a museum case beside a panel diagramming a casting pit and furnace."}},
{"id":"gr-350","num":350,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Ancient critics preferred to call it <i>melic</i>, verse sung to a musical accompaniment, where the modern catch-all name for it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, points instead at the lyre.","questions":["Alexandrian scholars drew up a canon of nine poets working in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from Alkman to Bacchylides, whose songs were made either for a single voice or for a trained chorus.","Because so little survives except where later grammarians and scholiasts quote it, most <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read in scraps, and before 1897 only Pindar could be judged from substantial remains."],"answer":"lyric poetry","answerText":"lyric poetry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First fixed date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">676 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Terpander's victory at Sparta</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 melic poets, Alkman to Bacchylides</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Lyric poetry</b> is the modern name for the Greek verse that was neither epic nor drama, and the Greeks of the classical age preferred <i>melic</i>, a term covering all poetry that was sung to a musical accompaniment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their word <i>melos</i> held three things together at once, the words, the melody and the rhythm, and Plato has Socrates say exactly that of song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The catch-all use of 'lyric' for everything outside epic and drama is modern and inaccurate, since it implies a lyre, while epic itself was once sung and elegy and iambus soon dropped their music.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle, setting out the kinds of poetry, names epic, tragedy, comedy and dithyramb, and has no such general category at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Alexandrian scholars instead fixed a canon of nine melic poets, running from Alkman and Alkaios through Sappho, Stesichoros, Ibykos, Anakreon and Simonides to Pindar and Bacchylides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The songs divide into monody, sung by one voice to the lyre or the pipe and given over to the deepest feelings of the individual, and choral melic, performed by a body of singers who danced as they sang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Choral song was public and ceremonial, a solemn expression of the united voice of the state, and it was built in the triad of strophe, antistrophe and epode.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Such a performance required a professional who wrote the words, composed the music and drilled the dancers, usually for one occasion only, whether a victory, a harvest, a holy day, a birth, a death or a marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Almost none of this immense body of song survives entire, and what is left is preserved in quotation, scattered through the manuscripts of more than eighty later authors, grammarians, scholiasts and geographers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The great exception arrived in 1896, when a papyrus reached the British Museum carrying enough of Bacchylides to be edited the following year, for until then Pindar alone could be judged from considerable remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xviii–xix, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 3.398d, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0168:book=3:section=398d. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 76, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1447a, trans. W. H. Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1447a. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xx, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xxi–xxiii, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 95–96, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), vii–viii, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Richard C. Jebb, ed., <i>Bacchylides: The Poems and Fragments</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1905), v, https://archive.org/details/bacchylidespoems00bacciala. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Terracotta_amphora_%28jar%29_MET_DT272.jpg/1920px-Terracotta_amphora_%28jar%29_MET_DT272.jpg","title":"A singer with the kithara","desc":"Attic red-figure amphora attributed to the Berlin Painter, about 490 BCE. The front shows a young man singing and playing the kithara. Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terracotta_amphora_%28jar%29_MET_DT272.jpg","alt":"A tall black Greek vase painted in orange with a young man in a long robe, head tilted back in song, playing a large stringed kithara."}},
{"id":"wh-201","num":"201","category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"The farming year of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned on a summer flood that rose at Aswan in June, peaked from August to September, and receded by the end of October, when sowing began.","questions":["Historians divide <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into an Old, a Middle and a New Kingdom, each a stretch of unified rule, separated by Intermediate Periods in which central authority came apart.","The tombs and temples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were built of stone at the desert edge and stand, while its towns were mudbrick on the floodplain and lie buried under Nile silt."],"answer":"Ancient Egypt","answerText":"Ancient Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Dynastic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2686 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2686 – 2181 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ptolemaic rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">305 – 30 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ancient Egypt</b> was the civilisation of the lower Nile, and for some 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) from the Mediterranean south into Nubia the valley is still dense with its great buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Nile runs about 6,825 kilometres (4,240 miles), fed from Africa's equatorial plateau by the White Nile and from the Ethiopian highlands by the Blue Nile and the Atbara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before twentieth-century damming its summer flood was driven by monsoon rain over those highlands, rising at Aswan as early as June, peaking from August to September, and receding by the end of October, when sowing began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prosperity was tied overwhelmingly to that cycle, and a weak flood is associated with serious human consequences right through the recorded history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surviving record is skewed by the same river: tombs and temples were stone at the desert edge and still stand, while settlements from capitals to villages were mudbrick on the floodplain and lie buried under silt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Out of the Naqada farming culture, which spread through Upper Egypt and from its middle phase into the north, grew a state organised around a divine king whose office was held to keep chaos out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kings of the 1st and 2nd Dynasties, roughly 3050 to 2750 BCE, were buried at Abydos in the desert cemetery now called Umm el-Qa'ab.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What followed is divided into an Old, a Middle and a New Kingdom, each a long stretch of unified rule separated by Intermediate Periods in which central authority came apart, then a Late Period, Persian rule and the Ptolemaic dynasty, ending in 30 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those dates are a construct rather than a record, resting on astronomical observations, king-lists and documents open to more than one reading, so that rival High and Low chronologies a century apart have long been argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A set of 48 new radiocarbon dates published in 2025 ruled the Low Chronology out for the Old and Middle Kingdoms, though a standard teaching chronology still warns that Egyptian dates are only certain after 664 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["culture","history","archaeology","egypt","africa","bronze age"],"difficulty":1,"sources":["Joseph G. Manning, Francis Ludlow, Alexander R. Stine, William R. Boos, Michael Sigl, and Jennifer R. Marlon, “Volcanic Suppression of Nile Summer Flooding Triggers Revolt and Constrains Interstate Conflict in Ancient Egypt,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 900, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-017-00957-y. [Open access]","Pınar Erdil et al., “Resolution of the High versus Low Debate for Old and Middle Kingdom Egypt,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 5 (2025): e0314612, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314612. [Open access]","Josef Wegner, “Excavating the Residence of an Ancient Egyptian Mayor,” <i>Expedition</i> 41, no. 3 (1999): 8–24, https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/excavating-the-residence-of-an-ancient-egyptian-mayor/. [Open access]","Matthew Adams, “The Search for Egypt’s First Kings,” <i>Expedition</i> 65, no. 3 (2024), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-search-for-egypts-first-kings/. [Open access]","Branislav Anđelković, “The Molding Power of Ideology: Political Transformations of Predynastic Egypt,” <i>Etnoantropološki problemi</i> 9, no. 3 (2014): 713–22, https://doi.org/10.21301/EAP.v9i3.9. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Egyptian Chronology,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Ancient_Egypt_map-en.svg","title":"Ancient Egypt","desc":"Map of ancient Egypt, showing the Nile up to the fifth cataract and the major cities and sites of the Dynastic period, c. 3150 to 30 BCE; Cairo and Jerusalem are shown as reference cities. Jeff Dahl, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Egypt_map-en.svg","alt":"A map of the Nile running from the Mediterranean south to the fifth cataract, with the major cities and sites of dynastic Egypt marked along the valley"}},
{"id":"wh-202","num":"202","category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Sown in autumn on ground the flood had just drained, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reaped between February and May and then pounded spikelet by spikelet to free the grain.","questions":["Egypt struck no coinage, so wages, pensions and taxes were reckoned in food, and the bread and beer that stood for money were milled from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> above all.","Grown in the Levant from about 9700 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arrived in the Nile valley already domesticated, and the traits that made it a crop were not acquired in Egypt."],"answer":"emmer wheat","answerText":"emmer wheat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 9700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated in Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1130 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Emmer wheat</b> is a hulled wheat, its grains locked in husks that threshing alone will not break, and it was the staple bread grain of ancient Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was among the earliest crops taken into cultivation, grown in the Levant from about 9700 BCE, and it reached the Nile valley already domesticated rather than being tamed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egypt grew it almost without rain: the summer flood spread water and fresh silt across the valley floor, and what the fields yielded followed from how high that water rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A low flood left ground dry and unworked, a high one swept the banks away, and either meant a short harvest, so the height was watched from gauges such as the nilometer on Elephantine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The water was caught in natural depressions and led on by canals, later by dykes and reservoirs, the oldest known being the Sadd el-Kafara near Helwan, built in the 27th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Because the flood came in late summer, the crop had to go in during autumn and grow through the cool months, which ruled out anything needing heat and wet together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground was broken with a hoe or an ard once the water had drained, in October or November, and the seed was scattered and trodden in by animals to keep it from birds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Six months on, between February at the earliest and May at the latest, the ears were cut with sickles, then threshed, winnowed and sieved to break the spikelets apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What came out was issued rather than sold: Egypt struck no coinage, and wages, pensions and taxes were reckoned in kind, most often as bread and beer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The scale was institutional: the mortuary temple of Ramesses III is recorded turning out over 2,000 loaves and 144 jars of beer a day, and when the flood failed that same accounting drove farmers to sell land to meet their taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["plant","agriculture","archaeology","history","egypt","bronze age"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Michael F. Scott, Laura R. Botigué, Selina Brace, Chris J. Stevens, Victoria E. Mullin and Alice Stevenson, “A 3,000-Year-Old Egyptian Emmer Wheat Genome Reveals Dispersal and Domestication History,” <i>Nature Plants</i> 5, no. 11 (2019): 1120–28, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41477-019-0534-5. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Food Production in Egypt,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foodproduction/index.html. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Cereal Production,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foodproduction/cereal.html. [Open access]","Mohamed Megahed and Hana Vymazalová, “Vysoká přehrada: projekt a jeho důsledky,” <i>Pražské egyptologické studie</i> 14 (2015): 10–16, https://sites.ff.cuni.cz/pes/wp-content/uploads/sites/13/2015/11/Mohamed_Megahed-Hana_Vymazalova_10-16.pdf. [Open access]","Vanessa Smith, “Food Fit for the Soul of a Pharaoh: The Mortuary Temple’s Bakeries and Breweries,” <i>Expedition</i> 48, no. 2 (2006), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/food-fit-for-the-the-soul-of-a-pharaoh/. [Open access]","Joseph G. Manning, Francis Ludlow, Alexander R. Stine, William R. Boos, Michael Sigl, and Jennifer R. Marlon, “Volcanic Suppression of Nile Summer Flooding Triggers Revolt and Constrains Interstate Conflict in Ancient Egypt,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 900, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-017-00957-y. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Agricultural_Scenes%2C_Tomb_of_Nakht_MET_DT306954.jpg/1920px-Agricultural_Scenes%2C_Tomb_of_Nakht_MET_DT306954.jpg","title":"Agricultural scenes in the tomb of Nakht","desc":"Facsimile by Norman de Garis Davies of the agricultural scenes on the east wall of the offering chapel in the Theban tomb of Nakht (TT 52), 18th Dynasty, c. 1400–1390 BCE. Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agricultural_Scenes,_Tomb_of_Nakht_MET_DT306954.jpg","alt":"A painted facsimile of a tomb wall; in the lowest band men plough with a pair of oxen, hoe, break clods with mallets and scatter seed among trees, while above them others reap with sickles and measure a heap of grain"}},
{"id":"wh-203","num":203,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Sequence-dated by its pottery into three phases running from about 4000 to 3000 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the Upper Egyptian culture out of which the first Egyptian state grew.","questions":["Black-topped pots in shallow pits, then rich tombs with copper and cylinder jars, mark the successive phases of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the 4th-millennium culture of Upper Egypt.","Long argued to be either a single state or a set of rival chiefdoms, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread from Upper Egypt into the Delta, where its pottery displaced the local tradition."],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","answer":"Naqada culture","answerText":"Naqada culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Naqada I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 – 3500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naqada II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3500 – 3200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naqada III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3200 – 3000 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Naqada culture</b> is the archaeological culture of Upper Egypt through the 4th millennium BCE, the period in which scattered farming villages along the Nile became one of the world's earliest states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is named after a cemetery near the town of Naqada, where Flinders Petrie plotted the objects in nearly three thousand graves during a single season of digging in 1895.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No calendar dates were available, so he ordered the graves by their pottery into a relative sequence, and Werner Kaiser rebuilt that scheme in 1957 into the numbered phases still in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They run from about 4000 to 3500 BCE, from about 3500 to 3200, and from about 3200 to 3000, and each is defined chiefly by its pots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first phase the dead lay in shallow oval pits much as in the Badarian period before it, and black-topped red ware is the characteristic vessel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The second brought marl and rough wares, copper, stone vessels and superbly worked flint knives, along with obsidian and lapis lazuli carried in from outside Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some tombs are now large and well furnished while others stay plain, and the goods in them are mostly everyday objects, as though the afterlife were a copy of life on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the third, cemeteries in Upper Egypt hold extremely wealthy burials, cylinder jars are a standard offering and the first writing appears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Delta had a Lower Egyptian culture of its own, and only in that last phase did Naqadan pottery, and then Naqadan potters, displace it, while a settlement at Tell el-Farkha burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Whether all this was one state from the start or a set of rival chiefdoms around Abydos, Hierakonpolis and Naqada is still argued, and the evidence is largely pots, graves and imports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"sources":["Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “The Naqada Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2001), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/background.html. [Open access]","W. M. Flinders Petrie and J. E. Quibell, <i>Naqada and Ballas. 1895</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch, 1896), 1–2, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028748261. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Chronology of the Naqada Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2001), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/chronology.html. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Burial customs of Predynastic Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2001), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/burialcustoms/predynastic.html. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Naqada Period: foreign relations,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2001), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/foreign.html. [Open access]","Jade Bajeot, “Technical Traditions and Social Boundaries. The Case of Predynastic Egypt,” <i>Études et Travaux</i> 38 (2025): 35–59, https://doi.org/10.12775/EtudTrav.38.002. [Open access]","Krzysztof M. Ciałowicz, “New Discoveries at Tell el-Farkha and the Beginnings of the Egyptian State,” <i>Études et Travaux</i> 30 (2017): 231–50, https://doi.org/10.12775/EtudTrav.30.011. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “The Political Unification of Egypt in the Naqada Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/inegypt.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","history","egypt","pottery"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Jar_decorated_with_painted_figures_of_boats_%28D-ware%29%2C_baked_clay_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_S_413_p02.jpg/1920px-Jar_decorated_with_painted_figures_of_boats_%28D-ware%29%2C_baked_clay_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_S_413_p02.jpg","title":"Painted Naqada pottery","desc":"Painted clay vessels of the Naqada culture in the Museo Egizio, Turin, which identifies the boat-decorated jar as S 413 and dates the group to about 3700–3300 BCE. Museo Egizio, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jar_decorated_with_painted_figures_of_boats_%28D-ware%29,_baked_clay_-_Museo_Egizio,_Turin_S_413_p02.jpg","alt":"Four clay vessels against a dark ground: a tall plain red jar, a pale jar painted with rows of many-oared boats carrying cabins, a jar painted with nested arcs and hatched panels, and a shallow bowl painted with spirals"}},
{"id":"wh-204","num":204,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Carved on a slab of greywacke with a paint-grinding hollow still cut into one face, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a king wearing both crowns of Egypt and striking down his enemies.","questions":["Hundreds of plain slate slabs ground eye-paint in Naqada graves, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that same object made enormous, given to a temple at Hierakonpolis and carved on both faces.","Two long-necked leopards twine their necks around the grinding hollow of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose top register writes a catfish and a chisel as the name of an early Egyptian king."],"answer":"Narmer Palette","answerText":"Narmer Palette","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign of Narmer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Dynastic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2686 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Narmer Palette</b> is a shield-shaped slab of dark greywacke, 63 cm (25 inches) long and carved in low relief on both faces, and now in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Palettes of this kind were ordinary in prehistoric Egypt: hundreds of plain slate slabs have come from Naqada cemeteries, found in front of the face among beads, combs and hairpins and stained green where malachite was ground into eye-paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This one keeps that working hollow, a shallow circle framed by the entwined necks of two long-necked leopards, so it is a real palette made monumental rather than a plaque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its excavators, who lifted it from a heap of votive objects buried beneath the temple at Hierakonpolis, supposed it was given there to paint the god's image or the king before a ceremony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both faces are cut into horizontal registers, and the figures already follow conventions Egyptian art would keep for three thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At the top of each face a rectangular frame flanked by two cow-eared Hathor heads holds a catfish and a chisel, read since the excavation as the king's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The catfish is secure; the chisel has never had a convincing explanation, so the reading is conventional rather than certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the face with the hollow the king walks in the red crown of Lower Egypt behind four standard-bearers towards ten enemies whose severed heads lie between their legs, and below, drawn as a bull, he breaks down a walled town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the other face he wears the white crown of Upper Egypt and swings a mace at a kneeling captive, while a falcon draws a rope through the lip of a human head sprouting papyrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egypt's antiquities ministry calls the palette a record of unification while stating that unification was in reality gradual, and the king's own place is still argued: first of the First Dynasty, or of a 'Dynasty 0' before it, and perhaps the Menes of later lists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["J. E. Quibell and F. W. Green, <i>Hierakonpolis, Part II</i>, Egyptian Research Account Fifth Memoir (London: Bernard Quaritch, 1902), 41-43. https://archive.org/details/hierakonpolis00petrgoog [Open access]","\"Narmer,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/narmer.html [Open access]","\"Art in Archaic Egypt,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/art/archaicegyptart-index.htm [Open access]","Josep Cervelló-Autuori, \"Was King Narmer Menes?,\" <i>Archeo-Nil</i> 15 (2005): 31-46. https://doi.org/10.3406/arnil.2005.896 [Open access]","Gabor Takacs, \"Note on the name of king Narmer,\" <i>Linguistica</i> 37, no. 1 (1997): 53-58. https://doi.org/10.4312/linguistica.37.1.53-58 [Open access]","\"Early Dynastic Period,\" Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt. https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/early-dynastic-period [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/Narmer_Palette_serpopard_side.jpg","title":"Narmer Palette","desc":"The side of the Narmer Palette with the two serpopards. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narmer_Palette_serpopard_side.jpg","alt":"A shield-shaped stone palette carved in bands: at the top a king in the red crown walks with attendants and standard-bearers towards rows of bound bodies whose heads lie between their legs; in the middle two long-necked leopards whose necks loop around a shallow round hollow; at the foot a bull breaking down a walled town."}},
{"id":"wh-205","num":205,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Later Egyptians credited the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a first king called Menes, while radiocarbon modelling now reads it as a period of several centuries rather than a single event.","questions":["Three inscriptions naming a king known chiefly from Abydos have been found in the eastern Delta, evidence that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had begun before the 1st Dynasty.","Radiocarbon modelling places the accession of Aha between 3111 and 3045 BCE, narrowing historical estimates for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had ranged from 3400 to 2900 BCE."],"answer":"unification of Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Accession of Aha</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3111 – 3045 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Dynastic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2686 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>unification of Egypt</b> is the process by which the Nile valley and the Delta came under one king, conventionally placed at the opening of the 1st Dynasty about 3100 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later Egyptians credited it to Menes, legendary first king of the Greek sources, whose name derives from the Meni heading New Kingdom lists, though his identification with any contemporary king is highly uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their own annals reached further back: the Palermo Stone, a fragment of a 5th-Dynasty slab, begins with predynastic kings of the period before the union of North and South.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its top row preserves nine names of predynastic kings of Lower Egypt, and had the line been complete it might once have held some 120.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Below it each reign opens with one formula, an appearance as King of Upper Egypt, an appearance as King of Lower Egypt, then the Union of the Two Lands, a union performed afresh at every accession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Archaeology gives no such moment, and the Naqada culture may have been a single political unit throughout or a set of areas each under its own ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The eastern Delta, long thought marginal, now looks far more important, and at Tell el-Farkha the phase from Naqada IIIA to the middle of IIIB reads as one of competition between the most influential Naqadan proto-kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three inscriptions there name Iry-Hor, a king known chiefly from Abydos, taken as proof that he had some connection with the Delta and more probably ruled the whole of Egypt before the 1st Dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Radiocarbon modelling places the accession of Aha, first king of that dynasty, between 3111 and 3045 BCE, where historical estimates had ranged from 3400 to 2900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It reads the stretch from Naqada IID/IIIA to that accession as the period in which the country moved from cultural to political unification, a Predynastic period of six or seven centuries against four to five millennia for the same change in southwest Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"unification of Egypt","difficulty":2,"sources":["\"Menes,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/menes.html [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Records of Egypt</i>, vol. 1 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1906), 51-52, 57-58, sections 78, 93-94, 140. https://archive.org/details/ancientrecordsof01brea [Open access]","\"The Political Unification of Egypt in the Naqada Period,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/inegypt.html [Open access]","Krzysztof Marek Ciałowicz, \"New Discoveries at Tell el-Farkha and the Beginnings of the Egyptian State,\" <i>Etudes et Travaux</i> 30 (2017): 231-50. https://doi.org/10.12775/EtudTrav.30.011 [Open access]","Michael Dee et al., \"An absolute chronology for early Egypt using radiocarbon dating and Bayesian statistical modelling,\" <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society A</i> 469, no. 2159 (2013): 20130395. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3780825/ [Open access]","\"Early Dynastic Period,\" Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt. https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/early-dynastic-period [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/240911_CvZ-Palermo-stone.jpg/1920px-240911_CvZ-Palermo-stone.jpg","title":"The Palermo Stone","desc":"The Palermo Stone, the largest surviving fragment of the Royal Annals of the Old Kingdom, in the Museo archeologico regionale Antonino Salinas, Palermo. CvZ, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:240911_CvZ-Palermo-stone.jpg","alt":"A dark stone fragment carved with horizontal registers of hieroglyphs, the topmost row divided into small rectangular compartments each holding a royal name."}},
{"id":"wh-206","num":206,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"In the pictorial script called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most signs stand for consonants alone, and a determinative was added after a word to show which class of thing was meant.","questions":["Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were kept for monuments and temples while everyday writing used cursive hands, the surviving record leans heavily towards eternity rather than the business of ordinary life.","Standardised already in the first dynasties, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remained the anchor of Egyptian writing from the late 4th millennium BCE until the Roman Period, some three thousand years."],"answer":"Egyptian hieroglyphs","answerText":"Egyptian hieroglyphs","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First writing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Standardised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the 3rd Dynasty</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Egyptian hieroglyphs</b> are the pictorial script of ancient Egypt, in which every sign is a recognisable image — a reed, an owl, a jackal — and in which two basic kinds of sign share the work, some denoting sounds and some denoting ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sound signs stand for consonants alone, singly or in groups of two or three, so the vowels a speaker supplied were never written down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That leaves a great deal of ambiguity, since about, but and bite would share one spelling, and the remedy was a determinative placed after most words to show the class of thing meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second habit, the phonetic complement, repeats a consonant already carried by a two- or three-letter sign as a reminder of how to read it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those reminders served the eye as well, because inscriptions were composed as sequences of square blocks and an extra sign often completed one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Writing appears in the Nile valley in the late 4th millennium BCE, emerging among other systems of signs as a way of sharpening the ideological claims of the powers that preceded the pharaonic state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Standardisation came quickly, and already in the first dynasties there were settled norms for the direction of writing, the shapes of individual signs and the spelling of single words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The script then held for some three thousand years, the anchor of writing in Egypt from the earliest inscriptions until the Roman Period, yet it was never the everyday hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Simplified forms were in use within the first few hundred years of writing, and Greek writers later named the two standard cursives hieratic, meaning priestly, and demotic, meaning popular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hieroglyphs stayed with monument and temple, set beside formal art in what one account calls a horizon of eternity, so the surviving record leans towards the eternal rather than the everyday, and the signs themselves run from the purely pictorial to the strictly phonetic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","egypt","writing","language"],"sources":["“The System of Hieroglyphic Writing, a Short Introduction,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/system1.html [Open access]","“The Sound (Phonetic) Signs in Egyptian Writing,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/phoneticsigns.html [Open access]","“The Development of Writing,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/development.html [Open access]","“The Hieratic Script,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/hieratic.html [Open access]","“Scope of Writing in Ancient Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/scope.html [Open access]","Pascal Vernus, “La naissance de l’écriture dans l’Égypte pharaonique : une problématique revisitée,” <i>Archéo-Nil</i> 26 (2016): 105–34. https://doi.org/10.3406/arnil.2016.1105 [Open access]","Stéphane Polis, “The Functions and Toposyntax of Ancient Egyptian Hieroglyphs. Exploring the Iconicity and Spatiality of Pictorial Graphemes,” <i>Signata</i> 9 (2018): 291–363. https://doi.org/10.4000/signata.1920 [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Wall_inscriptions_at_Karnak_Temple_Complex_in_Luxor%2C_Egypt_-_IMG_7842_%2833825908220%29.jpg/1920px-Wall_inscriptions_at_Karnak_Temple_Complex_in_Luxor%2C_Egypt_-_IMG_7842_%2833825908220%29.jpg","title":"Hieroglyphs on a temple wall at Karnak","desc":"Columns of carved hieroglyphs, divided by ruled lines, on a wall of the Karnak temple complex at Luxor. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Photograph by je_blanco, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wall_inscriptions_at_Karnak_Temple_Complex_in_Luxor,_Egypt_-_IMG_7842_(33825908220).jpg","alt":"A sandstone wall carved with vertical columns of hieroglyphic signs, among them owls, vipers, reeds, hands and seated figures, separated by ruled lines."}},
{"id":"wh-207","num":207,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Because Greek was still read while the Egyptian scripts were not, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered a way back into hieroglyphs by carrying one priestly decree written out three times over.","questions":["The decree cut on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> orders that copies be set up in every temple of the first, second and third rank, so the broken slab is one survivor of many.","Praise for a young king’s remitted debts and his capture of a rebel city fills much of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose priests met at Memphis in 196 BCE to vote him honours."],"answer":"Rosetta Stone","answerText":"Rosetta Stone","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decree</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 196 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regnal year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th of Ptolemy V</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Rosetta Stone</b> is a broken slab of dark stone carrying one priestly decree written out three times, in hieroglyphs, in the everyday Egyptian script called demotic, and in Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Priests assembled at Memphis passed it on 27 March 196 BCE, the ninth year of Ptolemy V, to mark the first commemoration of his coronation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fourteen lines of the hieroglyphic text survive against 32 of the demotic and 54 of the Greek, the two upper corners and the lower right having broken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of it lists the young king's benefactions, from debts of grain and money remitted to disaffected soldiers allowed back onto their land, set beside the storming of a rebel city the Greek calls Lycopolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In return the priests voted him statues, shrines and festivals, and the closing clause orders the decree cut in all three writings and set up in every temple of the first, second and third rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That instruction is why the slab is one copy among many, and broken duplicates have since been recovered elsewhere in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the time anyone tried to read it the scripts had long been silent, hieroglyphs still being carved in 394 CE and demotic still written at Philae into the 5th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> French soldiers turned the slab up near Rashid in the middle of July 1799, and the 19 July often given is arbitrary, since news of the find reached the Institut d’Égypte only on 29 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It passed to Britain under the capitulation of 1801 and reached London the next year, though ink impressions and engraved plates had already gone out across Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The way in was the cartouche, since the Greek named Ptolemy and the ring of signs repeated five times had to spell him, while an obelisk from Philae naming Cleopatra gave shared signs their sound values, enough for an alphabet of names though Coptic was needed to read connected Egyptian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["E. A. Wallis Budge, <i>The Rosetta Stone</i> (London: British Museum, 1913), https://archive.org/details/rosettastone00budguoft. [Open access]","E. A. Wallis Budge, <i>The Decrees of Memphis and Canopus</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1904), https://archive.org/details/decreesofmemphis01budg. [Open access]","E. A. Wallis Budge, <i>The Decrees of Memphis and Canopus</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1904), 168, https://archive.org/details/decreesofmemphis01budg. [Open access]","Jean Leclant, “Champollion, la pierre de Rosette et le déchiffrement des hiéroglyphes,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 116, no. 3 (1972): 557–65, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1972.12797. [Open access]","Jean Leclant, “Le Lieutenant Bouchard, l’Institut d’Égypte et la pierre de Rosette,” <i>Bulletin de la Société française d’égyptologie</i> 146 (1999): 6–24, https://doi.org/10.3406/bsfe.1999.2411. [Open access]","Michel Dewachter, “Du texte au signe, la pierre de Rosette et les premières collections d’antiquités égyptiennes,” <i>Bulletin de la Société française d’égyptologie</i> 146 (1999): 25–58, https://doi.org/10.3406/bsfe.1999.2412. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["object","archaeology","history","egypt","writing","language"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/The_Rosetta_Stone.jpg/1920px-The_Rosetta_Stone.jpg","title":"The Rosetta Stone","desc":"The Rosetta Stone photographed for the British Museum's own sixpenny guide to it of 1922, showing the hieroglyphic, demotic and Greek texts one above another. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Photograph by Donald Macbeth, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Rosetta_Stone.jpg","alt":"A broken slab of dark stone densely covered in three bands of writing, hieroglyphs at the top, a cursive script across the middle and Greek capitals below, with both upper corners and the lower right corner broken away."}},
{"id":"wh-208","num":208,"category":"World History","question":"Egypt wrote on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, made by slicing the pith of a tall marsh sedge into strips, laying them in two crossed layers, and pressing them into a sheet.","questions":["Pliny lists nine grades of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from the thirteen-finger sheets once reserved for sacred books down to the coarse wrapping stuff sold to merchants, never more than twenty sheets to a roll.","One of Egypt's main exports was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, still being written on in the early Islamic period, though its production fell away as parchment and cloth-based paper took over."],"answer":"papyrus","answerText":"papyrus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest roll</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st Dynasty, c. 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Latest dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1087 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","archaeology","history","egypt","writing","technology"],"abstract":"<b>Papyrus</b> is a writing material made from the pith of <i>Cyperus papyrus</i>, a giant sedge reaching about 4 m (13 feet) along the banks of the Nile and in its marshes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Theophrastus, writing in the 4th century BCE, says it stands in water some two cubits deep, that its stalk is three-cornered, and that Egyptians built boats from it and wove sails, mats and ropes from the rind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To make a sheet the green rind was peeled away and the white pith cut lengthwise into thin strips, which were laid side by side with their edges overlapping and a second layer set across them at right angles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pressing or beating fused the two layers, the plant's own sap doing the work of an adhesive, and the dried sheet was burnished smooth with a stone or shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pliny describes the same crossed layers but credits the muddy Nile water the table was moistened with, and he adds that a roll never held more than twenty sheets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He sorts the product into nine grades, the best thirteen fingers across and once kept for sacred books, the coarsest fit only for wrapping goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sheet heights varied with the period, commonly 38 to 42 cm (15 to 17 inches) in the Middle Kingdom and 16 to 20 cm (6 to 8 inches) in the New Kingdom, the tallest known 53.5 cm (21 inches) and from Gurob.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest surviving roll comes from the First Dynasty tomb of Hemaka at Saqqara, so the material long predates the story Pliny passes on, dating its discovery to Alexander's founding of Alexandria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Under Roman rule the codex arrived, leaves bound as a book rather than pasted into a roll, and in the late empire such books largely replaced rolls, parchment and later paper displacing papyrus itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It was one of Egypt's main exports and still written on in the early Islamic period, the latest datable example an Arabic document of 1087 CE, though production had fallen away in the 7th and 8th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Ayman S. Taha, Mohamed Z. M. Salem, Wael A. A. Abo Elgat, Hayssam M. Ali, Ashraf A. Hatamleh, and Eslam M. Abdel-Salam, “Assessment of the Impact of Different Treatments on the Technological and Antifungal Properties of Papyrus (<i>Cyperus Papyrus</i> L.) Sheets,” <i>Materials</i> 12, no. 4 (2019): 620. https://doi.org/10.3390/ma12040620 [Open access]","Theophrastus, <i>Enquiry into Plants</i> IV.8.3–4, trans. Arthur Hort, Loeb Classical Library (London: Heinemann, 1916), 1:347–49. https://archive.org/details/enquiryintoplan01theo [Open access]","“Papyrus Making,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/craft/papyrusmaking.html [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> XIII.23–24, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Bohn, 1855), 3:187–89. https://archive.org/details/naturalhistoryof31855plin [Open access]","“Papyrus: Size and Format,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/craft/papyrussize.html [Open access]","“Papyrus as Writing Material,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/craft/papyrus.html [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> XIII.21, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Bohn, 1855), 3:185–86. https://archive.org/details/naturalhistoryof31855plin [Open access]","“Writing Material: Codex,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/craft/codex.html [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Making_papyrus.jpg/1920px-Making_papyrus.jpg","title":"Strips laid in crossed layers","desc":"A modern demonstration of the method: wet strips of papyrus pith laid side by side on a board, with a second layer set across them, to be pressed and dried into a sheet. FrogUnderALilyPad, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Making_papyrus.jpg","alt":"Hands laying pale strips of papyrus pith side by side on a board, a second layer crossing them at right angles, with a rolling pin and more strips beside it."}},
{"id":"wh-209","num":209,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"The Egyptians called their king nswt or ity, and the word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that we use instead comes from per-aa, 'great house', which first named the palace rather than the man.","questions":["By the Middle Kingdom every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried five names, among them a Horus name and a birth name preceded by 'son of Ra' and written inside a cartouche.","A religious text sets out what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was for: to judge between men, to content the gods, to make what is Right happen and to annihilate what is Wrong."],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","answer":"pharaoh","answerText":"pharaoh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Five names by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2025 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sobekneferu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 BCE</span></div>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["title","history","religion","egypt","monarchy","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Pharaoh</b> is the English name for the king of ancient Egypt, taken from the Egyptian <i>per-aa</i>, 'great house', a phrase that named the palace and its government before it came to mean the monarch in person, and which reached English through Hebrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Egyptians used other words for their ruler: <i>nswt</i> and <i>ity</i> were the commonest, and their term for kingship itself was <i>nsyt</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kingship comes into view with the unification of Egypt around 3000 BCE and keeps its distinctive character until the Hellenistic and Roman periods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the First Dynasty the king was identified with Horus, the god of order, and from the Fourth Dynasty he bore the title 'son of Ra' as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was held to be divine in nature yet housed in a mortal body, and that double character is the heart of Egyptian kingship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By the Middle Kingdom he carried five names: a Horus name, a name of the Two Ladies, a Gold Horus name, a throne name taken at accession, and the birth name introduced as 'son of Ra'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The throne name and the birth name were each enclosed in an oval the Egyptians called <i>shen</i>, 'ring', which early Egyptologists renamed the cartouche.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A granite sphinx of Ramesses II carries the full five-fold titulary along its base, and his successor Merenptah added cartouches of his own to the shoulders after his father's death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One religious text states the office plainly: Ra placed the king on earth to judge between men, to content the gods, to make what is Right happen and to annihilate what is Wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was worshipped during his lifetime as well as after it, and although the office was normally male a few women held it, among them Sobekneferu, whose birth name is introduced as 'daughter of Ra'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1905), 73, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","“What is a king in Ancient Egypt?,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/king/what.html. [Open access]","“The five names of a king,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/kingname/index.html. [Open access]","Jennifer Houser Wegner, “The Granite Sphinx of Ramesses II,” <i>Expedition</i> 54, no. 3 (2012), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-granite-sphinx-of-ramesses-ii/. [Open access]","“What does a king have to do in ancient Egypt?,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/king/kingrepertory.html. [Open access]","“The cult of the reigning King in ancient Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/king/cult.html. [Open access]","“Nomen,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/kingname/nomen.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Head_of_a_King_with_a_Nemes_Headdress_MET_LC-2021_41_96_EGDP031852.jpg/1920px-Head_of_a_King_with_a_Nemes_Headdress_MET_LC-2021_41_96_EGDP031852.jpg","title":"A king in the nemes headcloth","desc":"Head of a king wearing the nemes headdress, limestone, Egypt, ca. 332-30 BCE. Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_a_King_with_a_Nemes_Headdress_MET_LC-2021_41_96_EGDP031852.jpg","alt":"A broken limestone head on a black museum mount, wearing a striped headcloth that falls in lappets beside the face, with a small rearing cobra carved at the brow."}},
{"id":"wh-210","num":210,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"The Step Pyramid at Saqqara, Sneferu’s first true pyramid at Meydum and Khufu’s great pyramid at Giza were all raised during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the age of Dynasties 3 to 6.","questions":["An official whose title is conventionally translated vizier organised the pyramid work forces in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when the whole administration existed chiefly to supply the palace and its cult.","Cedar came from Byblos and an expedition reached Punt under Sahure, but by the long reign of Pepy II the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had lost its hold on parts of Upper Egypt."],"answer":"Old Kingdom","answerText":"Old Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2686 – 2181 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty 4</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 2450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty 6</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2300 – 2181 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Old Kingdom</b> is the modern name for the Third to Sixth Dynasties of Egypt, about 2686 to 2181 BCE, one of the three main classical periods of Egyptian culture<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. Nearly all its royal building stands in one strip of desert between Meydum and Abu Rowash, the cemetery country of Memphis; everything else counts as provincial<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>. Netjerikhet, later called Djoser, was buried at Saqqara in the Step Pyramid, the earliest pyramidal structure surviving in Egypt and the first architectural complex made entirely of dressed stone<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>. Sneferu opened the Fourth Dynasty with the first true pyramid, at Meydum, then built two more at Dahshur for unknown reasons; his successor Khufu raised the great pyramid at Giza<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. No pyramid stood alone: each was the centre of a complex with a temple at its foot, a valley temple joined by a causeway and a satellite pyramid, where priests maintained the king’s cult<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. <br><br> The administration centred on the palace, and its principal aim was to supply the king’s household and keep the royal cult going in pyramids, sun temples and local temples<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. From the Fourth Dynasty the highest office carried the title conventionally translated vizier, who organised the pyramid work forces and to whom other palace officials answered; he also served as chief justice and often as chief of all the king’s works<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>. How many men a pyramid took can only be guessed at: Herodotus reports a hundred thousand at Khufu’s, where recent research calculates about 20,000 to 30,000<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>. Abroad the kings imported cedar from Byblos, left inscribed objects as far east as Ebla in Syria, and recorded an expedition to Punt under Sahure on the Palermo Stone<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>. From the end of the Fifth Dynasty an overseer of Upper Egypt shows the centre tightening its grip, but in the long reign of Pepy II it lost control of parts of Upper Egypt, and with that the Old Kingdom ends<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>.","difficulty":2,"tags":["era","history","egypt","architecture","monarchy","bronze age"],"sources":["“Egypt in the Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ok/general.html. [Open access]","“Egyptian Kings of the Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/oldkingdomkings.html. [Open access]","“Saqqara,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/saqqara. [Open access]","“Giza Plateau,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/giza-plateau. [Open access]","“Administration in Ancient Egypt: Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/administration/ok.html. [Open access]","“Administration in the Old Kingdom: the Provinces,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/administration/okprovince.html. [Open access]","“Pyramids in Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/index.html. [Open access]","“The workmen at a pyramid,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/workmen.html. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1905), 82–83, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","“Foreign relations of the Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foreignrelations/oldkingdom.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Bent_Pyramid_of_Snofru.jpg/1920px-Bent_Pyramid_of_Snofru.jpg","title":"The Bent Pyramid at Dahshur","desc":"The Bent Pyramid, built for Sneferu at Dahshur in the Fourth Dynasty, photographed in March 2023. Darer101, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Darer101, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bent_Pyramid_of_Snofru.jpg","alt":"A large limestone pyramid in desert sand whose sides change angle part way up, rising more steeply below and more shallowly above, with small figures of visitors at its foot."}},
{"id":"wh-211","num":211,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Netjerikhet, first king of the Third Dynasty, was buried at Saqqara under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the earliest pyramidal structure surviving in Egypt and the first complex built entirely of dressed stone.","questions":["A stone mastaba enlarged on the ground and then raised by ever smaller rectangular blocks became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the transitional form between the flat tomb and the smooth-sided pyramid.","Late Period sources make Imhotep, who had been a principal minister, the architect of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and turned him into a god of medicine the Greeks equated with Asklepios."],"answer":"Step Pyramid of Djoser","answerText":"Step Pyramid of Djoser","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2686 – 2667 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Third Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2686 – 2600 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Step Pyramid of Djoser</b> at Saqqara was raised for Netjerikhet, first king of the Third Dynasty; it is the earliest pyramidal structure surviving in Egypt and the first complex built entirely of dressed stone<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. Contemporary sources give only his Horus name, Netjerikhet; the name Djoser by which he is now known is attested nowhere until much later texts<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. It began as a stone mastaba, the flat-topped rectangular tomb earlier kings were buried under, then was widened on the ground and raised by further blocks, each smaller than the one below<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. The result was a terraced building, the transitional form between the mastaba and the smooth-sided pyramids that followed, and the first large structure of stone known in history<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. It stands at the centre of a funerary complex enclosed by a niched wall 277 by 544 metres (909 by 1,785 feet), the ancestor of the walled complexes of later reigns, which added a valley temple and a causeway<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>. <br><br> Egyptian memory gave the work to Imhotep, Netjerikhet's principal minister; his standing as architect of the complex, and as a deified son of the Memphite god Ptah, appears only in Late Period sources<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. Manetho preserved a tradition that building in stone was introduced under this king, whom he calls Tosorthros; earlier stone structures are now known, but the reign does mark the beginning of extensive building in stone<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. Imhotep's reputation outlasted the dynasty by millennia: scribes poured him a libation before work, and he became a god of medicine whom the Greeks called Imouthes and took for Asklepios<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. Every king of the Third Dynasty laid out a complex on the same large scale, and none of them was finished, the reigns being short<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. The first true pyramid came a dynasty later at Meydum, where Sneferu's builders began a step pyramid and changed the plan as it went up; Saqqara served Memphis as a cemetery for two thousand years more<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>.","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["building","history","egypt","architecture","religion","bronze age"],"sources":["“Egypt in the Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ok/general.html. [Open access]","“King Djoser,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/kingdjoser.html. [Open access]","“Evolution of the Pyramids,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/shape.html. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1905), 112–14, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","“Saqqara,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/saqqara. [Open access]","“Pyramids in Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Saqqara%2C_Pyramid_of_Djoser%2C_Ancient_Egypt.jpg/1920px-Saqqara%2C_Pyramid_of_Djoser%2C_Ancient_Egypt.jpg","title":"The Step Pyramid of Djoser at Saqqara","desc":"The Step Pyramid at Saqqara, seen across the desert with visitors on the path at the right for scale. Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saqqara,_Pyramid_of_Djoser,_Ancient_Egypt.jpg","alt":"A terraced stone pyramid of weathered pale blocks rising in receding stages from flat desert sand under a clear blue sky, with a line of visitors on a walkway at the right."}},
{"id":"wh-212","num":212,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Raised for Khufu on the plateau opposite modern Cairo, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood about 147 metres (481 feet) high, and no building anywhere exceeded that height for another 3,800 years.","questions":["Herodotus was told that a hundred thousand men had laboured for twenty years on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where modern estimates of the workforce run nearer twenty to thirty thousand.","Cosmic-ray imaging in 2017 revealed a void at least thirty metres long above the great inclined gallery of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first large internal feature found there since the 19th century."],"answer":"Great Pyramid of Giza","answerText":"Great Pyramid of Giza","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2589 – 2566 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fourth Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2613 – 2494 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Great Pyramid of Giza</b> is the tomb raised for Khufu, founder of Egypt’s Fourth Dynasty, on the desert plateau opposite modern Cairo<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. It is the oldest and largest of the three pyramids there, the others belonging to his son Khafre and grandson Menkaure, and nothing built anywhere exceeded its height for 3,800 years<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. It stood about 147 metres (481 feet) high on a square base some 230 metres (755 feet) a side, laid out so accurately that the average error in their equality, squareness and level is under a ten-thousandth<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. It is limestone almost throughout, by one estimate some 2,300,000 blocks averaging two and a half tons, and the finely dressed casing that once sheathed it was quarried away in later centuries<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. The pyramid stood at the centre of a complex rather than alone: a funerary temple at its foot, a causeway to a valley temple, boat burials, and three smaller pyramids for queens<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. <br><br> How the Egyptians raised it is still argued over: almost nothing survives to describe the work, and the ramps, the setting of the angle and the lifting of the blocks are reconstructed rather than recorded<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>. Herodotus was told that a hundred thousand men had laboured on it for twenty years, a tradition some early Egyptologists accepted, where recent estimates of the workforce run nearer twenty to thirty thousand<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. Its interior is unusually elaborate, entered in the eighteenth course of the north face and running through a great inclined gallery to a granite burial chamber with hollow chambers stacked above it<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. The passages were sealed behind granite plugs and portcullises, the masonry above is visibly hastier than that below, and at least two changes of plan can be read in the building itself<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. In 2017 a team imaging it with cosmic-ray muons reported a void at least thirty metres long above that gallery, found by three kinds of detector and the first large internal feature since the 19th century<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>.","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","history","archaeology","architecture","religion","egypt"],"sources":["Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, “Giza Plateau,” Egypt’s Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, accessed 21 August 2026, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/giza-plateau/. [Open access]","Digital Egypt for Universities, “The workmen at a pyramid,” University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/workmen.html. [Open access]","Digital Egypt for Universities, “How a pyramid was built,” University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/how.html. [Open access]","Digital Egypt for Universities, “Table of all known royal pyramids in Egypt,” University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/owners.html. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1905), 117–19, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","Kunihiro Morishima et al., “Discovery of a Big Void in Khufu’s Pyramid by Observation of Cosmic-Ray Muons,” <i>Nature</i> 552 (2017): 386–90, https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.01576. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Giza_Great_Pyramid_of_Khufu_%289793898043%29.jpg/1920px-Giza_Great_Pyramid_of_Khufu_%289793898043%29.jpg","title":"Great Pyramid of Giza","desc":"A corner of Khufu's pyramid at Giza, its stripped core stepping up from the desert floor, photographed in 2002. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giza_Great_Pyramid_of_Khufu_(9793898043).jpg","alt":"A corner of the Great Pyramid rising steeply against a blue sky, its rough limestone blocks stepping upwards, with people standing at its base for scale."}},
{"id":"wh-213","num":213,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Carved directly out of the limestone bedrock beside Khafre's valley temple, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives a Fourth Dynasty king the body of a lion, the animal standing for his power.","questions":["A red granite slab set between the forepaws of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> records a dream in which the sun god, complaining that the desert sand had reached him, promised Thutmose IV the kingdom.","Worshipped in the Eighteenth Dynasty as Horemakhet, or Horus in the Horizon, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now threatened by groundwater rising beneath the Giza plateau from the canals and suburbs of Cairo."],"answer":"Great Sphinx of Giza","answerText":"Great Sphinx of Giza","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Khafre's reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2558 – 2532 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fourth Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2613 – 2494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dream Stela</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1390 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","egypt","religion","monarchy"],"abstract":"The <b>Great Sphinx of Giza</b> is a colossal figure with a lion's body and a king's head, carved directly out of the limestone bedrock during the Fourth Dynasty, about 2613 to 2494 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Egypt a sphinx was a recurring portrait of the reigning king, the lion's body standing for the Pharaoh's power, and this one is the oldest known and by far the largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The evidence points to Khafre, builder of the second Giza pyramid, whose valley temple analysis has shown was finished before work began on the Sphinx and the temple in front of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The face has also been found to resemble Khafre's statues closely, though a standard history of 1905 could still say only that the attribution was not yet determined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figure sits low on the plateau beside that temple, its rock base about 22 metres (72 feet) above sea level against roughly 68 metres (223 feet) at the pyramid above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> By the Eighteenth Dynasty it was worshipped as Horemakhet, 'Horus in the Horizon', a form of the sun god, and Amenhotep II built a temple beside it for that god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His son Thutmose IV set a red granite slab between the forepaws, cut from an architrave of a neighbouring building, on which the god appears to the sleeping prince, complains that the desert sand has reached him, and promises the kingdom in return for clearing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Egyptologists read that surviving slab as a much later copy rather than the original record, and its lower third has flaked away, so more than half the text is lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The body was buried again through most of the centuries that followed, and when the front was dug clear in 1818 the paws proved to stretch some 15 metres (50 feet) forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The figure now faces a hazard its builders never met, in groundwater rising beneath the plateau from the irrigation canals and spreading suburbs of Cairo, which stands within a few metres of the surface behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, “The Great Sphinx,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/monuments/the-great-sphinx/. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1905), 120, 327, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Records of Egypt</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Eighteenth Dynasty</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1906), 320–23, https://archive.org/details/ancientrecordsof02brea. [Open access]","Howard Vyse, <i>Appendix to Operations Carried on at the Pyramids of Gizeh in 1837</i>, vol. 3 (London: James Fraser, 1842), 108–11, https://archive.org/details/operationscarri03vysegoog. [Open access]","Sayed Hemeda and Alghreeb Sonbol, “Sustainability Problems of the Giza Pyramids,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 8, no. 8 (2020), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-020-0356-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Sphinx_partially_excavated.jpg/1920px-Sphinx_partially_excavated.jpg","title":"The Great Sphinx after clearance","alt":"Sepia photograph of the Great Sphinx with its head and chest clear of the sand and its flanks still buried, two pyramids on the skyline behind.","desc":"The Sphinx of Giza, partially excavated, with two pyramids in the background. Photograph by Maison Bonfils, Beirut, between 1867 and 1899. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sphinx_partially_excavated.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-214","num":214,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"A body prepared by Egyptian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent about forty days drying in natron, a salt fetched from Wadi el-Natrun, within a procedure that ran to some seventy days in all.","questions":["Herodotus reports that Egyptian embalmers showed a bereaved family painted wooden models and offered three grades of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at three prices, the costliest drawing the brain out through the nostrils.","Labelled vessels from a Twenty-sixth Dynasty workshop at Saqqara have matched the substances used in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to their Egyptian names, showing conifer mixtures where myrrh had long been assumed."],"answer":"mummification","answerText":"mummification","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proto-embalming</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4300 – 3100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Whole process</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 70 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Saqqara workshop</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 664 – 525 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","egypt","religion","science"],"abstract":"<b>Mummification</b> is the deliberate preservation of a dead body, and in Egypt a requirement rather than a courtesy: the corpse had to go on serving as the dwelling of the ka and the ba, the aspects of a person that outlived it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The practice reaches far back, an early treatment of the dead beginning around 4300 BCE, before Egypt had kings to bury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In its developed form the embalmers drew the organs out through an incision about ten centimetres (four inches) long in the left side of the body and placed them in canopic jars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four jars were used, their stoppers carved as the heads of the four sons of Horus, each set of viscera under the care of a goddess named on the jar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The body was then dried in natron, a salt fetched from Wadi el-Natrun, for about forty days, and the whole procedure with its rituals ran to some seventy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Then came the wrapping, in bandages of linen, with protective amulets laid among them at particular points on the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, who saw the trade at work, reports that the embalmers showed a family painted wooden models and offered three grades at three prices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his account of the costliest the brain is drawn out through the nostrils with an iron hook and the flank opened with a stone knife, the cheapest being no more than a purge of the belly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ceramic vessels from a Twenty-sixth Dynasty embalming workshop at Saqqara are labelled with their contents and their use, and the residues in them include resins carried from tropical forests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two substances long translated as myrrh and sacred oil proved to be conifer mixtures, while scans of child mummies show the treatment varied with age at death, with status and with the place of burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, “Mummification Museum,” Discover Egypt's Monuments, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/museums/mummification-museum/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i>, trans. A. D. Godley, 2.86–88, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=86. [Open access]","E. A. Wallis Budge, <i>The Mummy: Chapters on Egyptian Funereal Archaeology</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1893), 160–62, https://archive.org/details/mummyhandbookofe00budguoft. [Open access]","Maxime Rageot et al., “Biomolecular analyses enable new insights into ancient Egyptian embalming,” <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7947 (2023): 287–93, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05663-4. [Open access]","Stephanie Zesch et al., “The multifaceted nature of Egyptian mummification: Paleoradiological insights into child mummies,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 12 (2024): e0316018, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0316018. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/Egyptian_-_A_Complete_Set_of_Canopic_Jars_-_Walters_41171%2C_41172%2C_41173%2C_41174_-_Group.jpg","title":"A complete set of canopic jars","desc":"Four Egyptian canopic jars, their stoppers carved as the heads of the four sons of Horus, who guarded the organs drawn from the body during embalming. Walters Art Museum, Baltimore, acquired by Henry Walters. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Walters Art Museum (41171, 41172, 41173, 41174), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_-_A_Complete_Set_of_Canopic_Jars_-_Walters_41171,_41172,_41173,_41174_-_Group.jpg","alt":"Four squat stone jars in a row, each with a carved stopper: a human head, a baboon, a jackal and a falcon."}},
{"id":"wh-215","num":215,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Each ancient Egyptian took from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whatever selection of its more than 190 spells could be afforded, and had them copied onto papyrus, linen, coffins and the walls of the tomb.","questions":["A Ramesside ostracon prices a decorated papyrus of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at one deben of silver, about half a year's wage for a workman or the price of three donkeys.","The best known scene in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to spell 125, where the heart of a dead person is weighed in a balance against the feather of Maat before Osiris."],"answer":"Book of the Dead","answerText":"Book of the Dead","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest spells</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1773 – 1650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1580 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st – 2nd century CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Book of the Dead</b> is not a book at all but a loose corpus of more than 190 separate spells<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, from which each ancient Egyptian took whatever selection was wanted for the journey after death. The title is a modern one, borrowed from the German <i>Totenbuch</i> that nineteenth-century scholars used, perhaps shaped by an Arabic phrase villagers used for papyri found in tombs<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. The Egyptians themselves called it the Spells for Going Forth by Day, and going forth by day meant the <i>ba</i>, the soul, leaving the tomb to travel with the sun god across the sky<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. Its spells did not appear from nowhere: many were revised out of the Pyramid Texts carved inside late Old Kingdom pyramids and the Coffin Texts written inside Middle Kingdom coffins<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. The earliest recognisable spells are not on papyrus but on heart scarabs and coffins of the Thirteenth Dynasty, and the corpus only came into its own at the close of the Seventeenth Dynasty and through the Eighteenth<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. <br><br> No two manuscripts are alike, because the choice, length and quality of a copy followed what its owner wanted and could pay for<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. A Ramesside ostracon prices a decorated papyrus at one deben of silver, which was about half a year's wage for a workman, or three donkeys<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>. Spells were accordingly written on every surface a burial could offer: leather, linen bandages, cartonnage cases, coffins, funerary figures, amulets and tomb walls<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. The most reproduced picture in the whole corpus belongs to spell 125, in which the heart of the dead person is weighed against the feather of Maat in the hall of Osiris<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. Even Tutankhamun was buried without a papyrus of it, his spells being on the tomb walls, his golden mask and his funerary figures, and the last attested spells of all were written in Demotic in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>.","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["text","history","religion","egypt","literature","writing"],"sources":["Foy Scalf, “What Is the Book of the Dead?” in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 21–28, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Peter F. Dorman, “The Origins and Early Development of the Book of the Dead,” in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 29–40, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Holger Kockelmann, “How a Book of the Dead Manuscript Was Produced,” in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 67–74, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Foy Scalf, “The Death of the Book of the Dead,” in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 139–48, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Marcus Müller, “Das Totenbuch,” Totenbuch-Projekt, Universität Bonn, February 2012, https://totenbuch.awk.nrw.de/projekt/das-totenbuch. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/eg/original/DT11633.jpg","title":"The weighing of the heart, from the Book of the Dead of Nauny","desc":"A detail of the papyrus made for Nauny, a chantress of Amun. In the lower register jackal-headed Anubis kneels at a great balance, steadying the pan that holds her heart while a baboon sits on the beam beneath a sun disc; Nauny and a goddess stand at the left and Osiris presides enthroned at the right, wearing the atef crown and holding the crook and flail. Third Intermediate Period, Dynasty 21, about 1050 BCE; papyrus and paint. Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, Rogers Fund, 1930 (30.3.31).","credit":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, accession 30.3.31, public domain (CC0), https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/548344","alt":"A painted papyrus in two registers. Below, two women in long white robes face a tall balance; a kneeling figure with a jackal's head holds its plumb line, a small baboon perches on the crossbeam under a sun disc, and a seated god in a tall crown faces them from the right. Columns of hieroglyphs fill the spaces between the figures."}},
{"id":"wh-216","num":216,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had no founder and no scripture, its gods could merge freely, so the sun god joined to Amun became Amun-Re, the effective state god of the New Kingdom.","questions":["Ordinary worshippers in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rarely passed the outer courts, so devotion gathered at gateways and outer walls, where gods bore epithets promising a hearing to anyone who called.","The governing idea of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was maat, the right order of cosmos and society at once, personified as a goddess and set against isfet, its exact opposite."],"answer":"ancient Egyptian religion","answerText":"ancient Egyptian religion","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["practice","religion","history","egypt","mythology","bronze age"],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Amun's bark</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2025 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amun-Re</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Djême</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th – 8th century CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Ancient Egyptian religion</b> was polytheistic, a large and shifting company of gods worshipped along the Nile for more than three thousand years, and it had no founder, no creed and no scripture. Its governing idea was <i>maat</i>, the right order of the cosmos and of society at once, personified as a goddess and called the daughter of Re; its exact opposite was <i>isfet</i>, disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Maat present among people was held to guarantee the balance of the heavens as much as harmony on earth, and keeping her there was the king's charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gods were not exclusive and names merged freely: the sun god joined to Amun became Amun-Re, the effective state god of the New Kingdom,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> while Ptah, Sokar and Osiris fused into one mummiform god of resurrection. Re was god of the living and Osiris god of the dead, and each night the sun descended into the netherworld to unite with Osiris and be reborn at dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside a temple the daily ritual centred on a cult statue permanently installed in the rear of the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At festivals the god came out instead as a portable image carried in a bark, a rite attested at Thebes as early as the Eleventh Dynasty, when Amun began crossing the river to the west bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most Egyptians never went in, since admittance was strictly controlled and ordinary worshippers reached the interior only on festival days and only as far as the outer courts and hypostyle halls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Devotion therefore collected outside, at gateways, at shrines built against the outer walls, and around gods given epithets that promised a hearing, among them Amun-Who-Hears-the-Cry-of-Woe and Amun of Opet-Who-Answers-the-Poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Households kept their own dead close, with busts of ancestors set near the door and stelae naming a relative an 'excellent spirit of Re',<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and when Christianity spread the old sanctuaries were resettled rather than abandoned, a town growing inside the enclosure of the temple of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Bernadette Menu, “Maât, ordre social et inégalités dans l'Égypte ancienne,” <i>Droit et cultures</i> 69, no. 1 (2015): 51–73, https://doi.org/10.4000/droitcultures.3510. [Open access]","Foy Scalf, catalog entries, in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 198, 348, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Egyptian Chronology,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/index.html. [Open access]","Martina Ullmann, “Thebes: Origins of a Ritual Landscape,” in <i>Sacred Space and Sacred Function in Ancient Thebes</i>, ed. Peter F. Dorman and Betsy M. Bryan, Studies in Ancient Oriental Civilization 61 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2007), 3–25, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/saoc61.pdf. [Open access]","Peter J. Brand, “Veils, Votives, and Marginalia: The Use of Sacred Space at Karnak and Luxor,” in <i>Sacred Space and Sacred Function in Ancient Thebes</i>, ed. Peter F. Dorman and Betsy M. Bryan, Studies in Ancient Oriental Civilization 61 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2007), 51–83, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/saoc61.pdf. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Ancestor Busts,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2002), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/religion/ancestor.html. [Open access]","Heike Behlmer, “Christian Use of Pharaonic Sacred Space in Western Thebes: The Case of TT 85 and 87,” in <i>Sacred Space and Sacred Function in Ancient Thebes</i>, ed. Peter F. Dorman and Betsy M. Bryan, Studies in Ancient Oriental Civilization 61 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2007), 163–75, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/saoc61.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Ear_Stela_of_Ramessumerysutekh_MET_LC-59_99_1_EGDP030127.jpg/1920px-Ear_Stela_of_Ramessumerysutekh_MET_LC-59_99_1_EGDP030127.jpg","title":"Ear stela of Ramessumerysutekh","desc":"A limestone votive stela carved with rows of human ears, dedicated by Ramessumerysutekh, ca. 1295–1186 BCE. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, accession 59.99.1. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ear_Stela_of_Ramessumerysutekh_MET_LC-59_99_1_EGDP030127.jpg","alt":"A rectangular limestone slab carved in low relief with several rows of human ears above a line of hieroglyphs."}},
{"id":"wh-217","num":217,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Egypt spent roughly a century and a half after the Old Kingdom divided between rival houses at Herakleopolis and Thebes, a stretch called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ending with Mentuhotep II.","questions":["Middle Kingdom texts remember the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as famine and chaos, yet its pottery, provincial art and well-stocked cemeteries suggest that local life carried on much as before.","The dynasties later numbered seven to ten belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the seventy kings ruling seventy days that Manetho assigned them have no contemporary support."],"answer":"First Intermediate Period","answerText":"First Intermediate Period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2181 – 2055 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 to 11</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reunification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2055 BCE, under Mentuhotep II</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","egypt","archaeology","monarchy","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>First Intermediate Period</b> is the name Egyptologists give to the century and a half separating the Old Kingdom from the Middle Kingdom, dated on one common scheme to about 2181 to 2055 BCE and on another ending about 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its opening was a real break, since the weakness of the last Sixth Dynasty rulers left provincial governors free of Memphis, and those governors raised forces of their own and fought one another for territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rulers of the age are numbered in later kinglists as the seventh to eleventh dynasties, and much of that neatness is a fact about the lists rather than about Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Manetho's seventh dynasty is seventy kings ruling seventy days, for which no evidence exists at all and which may be a later literary metaphor for chaos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is likewise no contemporary support for dividing the ninth dynasty from the tenth, a split that may record nothing more than a list written across two columns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Two rival houses did emerge: a Herakleopolitan line in the north, whose cemetery there stayed in use into the Middle Kingdom, and a Theban eleventh dynasty ruling only southern Egypt from about 2160 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Middle Kingdom writers remember the age as famine, poverty and collapsed order, and the Lament of Ipuwer, long read as a report of it, is now dated by its language to that later period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The material record disagrees: regional styles flourished in pottery, coffins and relief carving, provincial economies appear to have improved once free of central demands, and local rulers boasted of feeding the hungry and giving water to the thirsty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Coffin Texts appear now, funerary spells written inside private coffins that opened to ordinary Egyptians an afterlife the Pyramid Texts had reserved for kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether a drier climate and weaker Nile floods helped bring the Old Kingdom down is argued three ways, from no crisis to catastrophe, and the period ends when Mentuhotep II of Thebes reunited the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["“First Intermediate Period,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/first-intermediate-period. [Open access]","“Egypt in the First Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/1inter/index.html. [Open access]","“First Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/1stintermediateperkings.html. [Open access]","“Mentuhotep,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/mentuhotepII.html. [Open access]","Jean-Daniel Stanley and Sarah E. Wedl, “Significant Depositional Changes Offshore the Nile Delta in Late Third Millennium BCE: Relevance for Egyptology,” <i>E&amp;G Quaternary Science Journal</i> 70, no. 1 (2021): 83–92, https://doi.org/10.5194/egqsj-70-83-2021. [Open access]","“Egyptian Literary Compositions of the Middle Kingdom and Second Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/literature/midegsummaries.html. [Open access]","Carmen Pérez Die, “La nécropole de la Première Période Intermédiaire - début du Moyen Empire à Héracléopolis Magna,” in <i>Des Néferkarê aux Montouhotep</i>, TMO 40 (Lyon: Maison de l’Orient, 2005), 239–54, https://www.persee.fr/doc/mom_1955-4982_2005_act_40_1_2403. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Stela_of_the_mayor_Ituerneheh%2C_Egypt%2C_possibly_from_Naga-ed-Deir%2C_First_Intermediate_Period%2C_c._210-2040_BC%2C_painted_limestone_-_Harvard_Semitic_Museum_-_Cambridge%2C_MA_-_DSC06206.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Funerary stela of the mayor Ituerneheh","desc":"A painted limestone stela of the First Intermediate Period, probably from Naga ed-Deir, carved with the mayor Ituerneheh holding a staff, a smaller female figure beside him, and columns of hieroglyphs. Harvard Semitic Museum. Photograph by Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stela_of_the_mayor_Ituerneheh,_Egypt,_possibly_from_Naga-ed-Deir,_First_Intermediate_Period,_c._210-2040_BC,_painted_limestone_-_Harvard_Semitic_Museum_-_Cambridge,_MA_-_DSC06206.jpg","alt":"A painted limestone slab carved in low relief with a standing man in a kilt holding a staff, a smaller woman at his side, and columns of hieroglyphs above and around them."}},
{"id":"wh-218","num":218,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Mentuhotep II of Thebes reunited Egypt to open the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose twelfth-dynasty kings founded Itjtawy, fortified Lower Nubia and left the literature that fixed classical Egyptian.","questions":["Fifteen fortresses rose along the Nile in Lower Nubia during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, built in about 32 years by kings from Senusret I to Senusret III to hold a new frontier.","Thebes and its god Amun rose from obscurity in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when a Theban house reunified Egypt and the oldest evidence for the temple at Karnak was laid down."],"answer":"Middle Kingdom","answerText":"Middle Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reunified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2055 BCE, under Mentuhotep II</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty 12</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1985 – 1795 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Senusret III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1874 – 1855 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11th, 12th and 13th</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["era","history","egypt","archaeology","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>Middle Kingdom</b> is the phase of ancient Egyptian history that opens with the reunification of the country under Mentuhotep II of Thebes and runs from the later eleventh dynasty through the twelfth into the thirteenth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Egyptian ministry dates the reunification to about 2055 BCE and the twelfth dynasty to about 1985 to 1795 BCE, where University College London puts Mentuhotep II at 2046 to 1995 BCE and closes the dynasty with Sobekneferu about 1794 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The king's Theban origins carried his city and its god Amun out of obscurity, and the oldest evidence for Karnak, later the wealthiest religious establishment in Egypt, belongs to this period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was buried across the river at Deir al-Bahari under a terraced monument cut partly into the cliff, which served as a partial model for the temple of Hatshepsut beside it five centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The age is a cultural high point above all for its writing, and the grammar of these compositions, called Middle Egyptian, remained the language of carved official inscriptions into the Roman period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Amenemhat I, who may have served Mentuhotep IV as vizier, founded a new royal residence called Itjtawy near modern Lisht, and its exact site has still not been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is named in three of the compositions the period is remembered for, the Tale of Sinuhe, the Teaching of Amenemhat and the Lament of Neferty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The twelfth dynasty drove the southern frontier into Lower Nubia, a region long worked for ebony, ivory, incense, gold and copper and read as a periphery held for its resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Fifteen fortifications went up along the Nile there in about 32 years, from Senusret I to Senusret III, guarding the border, regulating the traders who passed and housing mining and military expeditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Senusret III fixed that frontier at the Second Cataract around Semna, and under him the provinces were brought down, the great rock-cut tomb chapels ceasing while the Fayum and Lisht cemeteries grew in scale and wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Middle Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/middle-kingdom. [Open access]","\"Kings of the Middle Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/middlekingdom.html. [Open access]","\"Amenemhat (I) Sehetepibre,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/amenemhatI.html. [Open access]","Eduardo Ferreira, \"The Lower Nubian Egyptian Fortresses in the Middle Kingdom: A Strategic Point of View,\" <i>Athens Journal of History</i> 5, no. 1 (2019): 31–52, https://doi.org/10.30958/ajhis.5-1-2. [Open access]","\"Senusret (III) Khakaure,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/senusretIII.html. [Open access]","Roxana Flammini, \"Ancient Core-Periphery Interactions: Lower Nubia During Middle Kingdom Egypt (ca. 2050–1640 B.C.),\" <i>Journal of World-Systems Research</i> 14, no. 1 (2008): 50–74, https://doi.org/10.5195/jwsr.2008.348. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Mentuhotep_II_Deir_el-Bahari.JPG/1920px-Mentuhotep_II_Deir_el-Bahari.JPG","title":"The mortuary temple of Mentuhotep II at Deir el-Bahari","desc":"The terraced mortuary temple of Mentuhotep II at Deir el-Bahari, in the Theban necropolis, built partly against the cliff behind it. Rémih, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mentuhotep_II_Deir_el-Bahari.JPG","alt":"Ruins of a stone temple terrace on the desert floor beneath a high limestone cliff, with a line of column stumps standing on the platform and a colonnade running along its left side."}},
{"id":"wh-219","num":219,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Levantine settlers in the eastern Delta gave Egypt its fifteenth dynasty, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Greek name renders an Egyptian title meaning ruler of foreign lands.","questions":["Teeth from the Avaris cemeteries carry more incomers before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took power than during their rule, which fits a Delta-born elite rather than an invading army.","Ahmose sacked Avaris and drove the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out to open the eighteenth dynasty, and the chariot and the composite bow entered Egypt during their century in the north."],"answer":"Hyksos","answerText":"Hyksos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1638 – 1530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15th, ruling from Avaris</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Expelled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Ahmose, c. 1550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Second Intermediate, c. 1700 – 1550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","history","egypt","archaeology","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Hyksos</b> is the Greek form of the Egyptian title <i>heqa khasut</i>, ruler of foreign lands, and it names the fifteenth dynasty, which held northern Egypt from about 1638 to 1530 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Levantine migrants had been settling the north-eastern Delta since the twelfth dynasty, and a contemporary Egyptian text gives one of their leaders that title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How they came to power is not known; the weakening thirteenth dynasty could not prevent it and moved its capital from Itjtawy to Thebes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From Avaris, the modern Tell al-Dab'a, they held the country as far south as al-Qusiya in Middle Egypt, Memphis included, while Nubian Kerma rose in the south and Egyptian kings at Thebes held the stretch between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their material culture is a variant of the Levantine Middle Bronze Age, seen at Avaris in pottery, weaponry, house plans and tombs placed inside living areas, and the composite bow and the horse and chariot entered Egypt in these years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The invasion story comes from Manetho, a priest writing twelve centuries afterwards whose account survives only in quotations by later historians such as Josephus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Reference works still carry it: University College London's Digital Egypt says they invaded at some unknown point, while noting that Egyptology agrees neither on the length of the Second Intermediate Period nor on the number and sequence of its kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strontium isotope ratios in 75 tooth samples from the Avaris cemeteries found more incomers before their rule than during it, which fits a ruling class of Near Eastern descent born and raised in the Delta rather than an army from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same study found far more non-local women than men, suggesting men stayed where they were born and wives came from elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Theban kings of the seventeenth dynasty fought back and Ahmose sacked Avaris to open the eighteenth dynasty, though not even the year of reunification is known; Seqenenre Taa seems to have died campaigning, but the Levantine axes read from his wounds were types Egyptian forces also carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Second Intermediate Period,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/second-intermediate-period. [Open access]","“Egypt in the Second Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/2inter/index.html. [Open access]","“Kings of the Second Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/2interkings/index.html. [Open access]","“Ahmose,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/ahmose.html. [Open access]","Chris Stantis et al., “Who Were the Hyksos? Challenging Traditional Narratives Using Strontium Isotope (87Sr/86Sr) Analysis of Human Remains from Ancient Egypt,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 7 (2020): e0235414, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0235414. [Open access]","Uroš Matić, “Ethnicity of the Hyksos Forces and the Death of Pharaoh Seqenenre Tao,” <i>Etnoantropološki problemi</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): 821–49, https://doi.org/10.21301/eap.v19i3.5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6e/Scarab_with_the_Name_of_the_Hyksos_King_Khayan_MET_D_457_bottom.jpg","title":"Seal ring of the Hyksos king Khyan","desc":"The engraved underside of a glazed scarab naming the Hyksos king Khyan, mounted as a swivel ring in a gold bezel, in the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scarab_with_the_Name_of_the_Hyksos_King_Khayan_MET_D_457_bottom.jpg","alt":"The oval underside of a pale green scarab set in a gold ring bezel, cut with a royal cartouche between scroll patterns, with an ankh sign below"}},
{"id":"wh-220","num":220,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Egypt reached the Fourth Cataract and Syria during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the period of the eighteenth to twentieth dynasties whose kings abandoned the pyramid for rock-cut tombs at Thebes.","questions":["Two viziers served at once, at Thebes and Memphis, during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when a viceroy of Kush governed Nubia and was among the most important officials in the kingdom.","Akhenaten's turn to the Aten and the long reign of Ramesses II both fall within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which began about 1550 BCE with the expulsion of the Hyksos and ended about 1069 BCE."],"answer":"New Kingdom","answerText":"New Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th, 19th and 20th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ahmose</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1525 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ramesses II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1279 – 1213 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["era","history","egypt","archaeology","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>New Kingdom</b> is the phase of ancient Egyptian history running from about 1550 to 1069 BCE, covering the eighteenth, nineteenth and twentieth dynasties, when Egypt was the leading power of the ancient Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It opens with Ahmose, whose reign University College London dates to about 1550 to 1525 BCE, and ends with a country that had weakened steadily until it split in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its kings carried the frontier south to the Fourth Cataract in Nubia and north into what is now Syria, and the wealth that came back paid for building across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thebes was never the administrative capital, but from this period it ranked first in religious terms, and the temple of its god Amun at Karnak became one of the most important in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its kings were buried there as well, giving up the pyramid for rock-cut tombs in the Valley of the Kings on the west bank of the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Government kept broadly the shape it had held in the Middle Kingdom, headed by the vizier, though from the early eighteenth dynasty two are attested, one residing at Thebes and one at Memphis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nubia was administered separately by the viceroy of Kush, an office created at the start of that dynasty and among the most important in the kingdom because the gold mines lay in his charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Akhenaten broke the pattern by proclaiming a single god in the sun-disk Aten and remaking royal ideology, art, architecture and language with it, and the Levantine territories were lost in his reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sety I recovered much of that ground, Ramesses II fought the Hittites and then made peace with them, and Merenptah beat off a large attack by the raiders Egyptian sources call the Sea Peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The record that survives is overwhelmingly monumental, so a 2024 lead-isotope study of kohl from Nubian graves, some of New Kingdom date, adds evidence of another kind by tracing part of it to the Egyptian mining site of Gebel el-Zeit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["\"New Kingdom (about 1550-1069 BC),\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nk/index.html. [Open access]","\"Kings of the New Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/newkingdomkings.html. [Open access]","\"Thebes,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/thebes/index.html. [Open access]","\"Central Administration in the New Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/administration/nkcentral.html. [Open access]","\"The viceroy of Kush,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/viceroy.html. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]","Rennan Lemos et al., \"Bronze Age Supply Chains between Ancient Egypt and Nubia Revealed by Lead Isotope Analysis of Kohl Samples,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 27633, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-79162-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Templo_de_Karnak%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_142.jpg/1920px-Templo_de_Karnak%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_142.jpg","title":"The temple of Amun at Karnak","desc":"Standing columns and a ruined pylon in the temple of Amun at Karnak, Luxor, the Theban sanctuary the kings of the New Kingdom built up above all others. Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Templo_de_Karnak,_Luxor,_Egipto,_2022-04-03,_DD_142.jpg","alt":"A wide paved court with two tall standing stone columns, a ruined pylon of carved blocks behind them, palm trees at the sides and fallen masonry on the ground"}},
{"id":"wh-221","num":221,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Taking the throne name Maatkare and a full royal titulary, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled Egypt as king beside her young stepson Thutmose III for 21 years in the 15th century BCE.","questions":["The terraced mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahari carries reliefs in which the god Amun fathers <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an argument in stone that made her kingship a matter of descent.","After her death the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut from monument after monument and left out of the king lists, surviving in Manetho's later summary only as a queen called Amenses."],"answer":"Hatshepsut","answerText":"Hatshepsut","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1479 – 1458 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Co-rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 years with Thutmose III</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thutmose III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to c. 1425 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","archaeology","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Hatshepsut</b> was a queen of the eighteenth dynasty who took the full titles of a king and ruled Egypt in her own right for about twenty years in the 15th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was a daughter of Thutmose I and a wife of Thutmose II, his son by another marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Thutmose II died his heir, Thutmose III, was still a young boy by another wife, and Hatshepsut seems to have taken over the business of government on his behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within a few years she had assumed a royal titulary of her own, taking the throne name Maatkare, and the two ruled as kings together for 21 years until her death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her reign shaped how the office itself was understood, working out an ideology of kingship bound to the god Amun that defined the role for the rest of the New Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Her claim to the throne was argued in stone rather than simply asserted, and the argument survives on the walls of her mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahari, the terraced building called Djeser-djeseru, the Holy of Holies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One set of reliefs has her father Thutmose I naming her his heir; another makes the god Amun her true father, a divine-birth sequence turning her kingship into a matter of descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other walls record a seaborne expedition to Punt, somewhere towards the southern end of the Red Sea, whose people, houses, trees and animals are drawn in unusual detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the rock-cut chapel of Speos Artemidos in Middle Egypt she went further, presenting herself as the ruler who had set Egypt in order after the disorder of the Hyksos, who had in fact been expelled a century before her reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After her death her name and her image were cut from monument after monument and left out of the later king lists, so that she reaches Manetho's summary twelve centuries on only as a queen called Amenses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["\"Hatshepsut,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/hatshepsut.html. [Open access]","\"Thutmose III,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/thutmosisiii.html. [Open access]","\"Speos Artemidos,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/temple/speos.html. [Open access]","\"Hatshepsut Temple,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/hatshepsut-temple. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Templo_funerario_de_Hatshepsut%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_13.jpg/1920px-Templo_funerario_de_Hatshepsut%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_13.jpg","title":"The mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahari","desc":"Aerial view at sunrise of the mortuary temple of Hatshepsut at Deir el-Bahari, on the west bank at Luxor. Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Templo_funerario_de_Hatshepsut,_Luxor,_Egipto,_2022-04-03,_DD_13.jpg","alt":"An aerial view at sunrise of a three-terraced stone temple with long rows of square columns, set against a high desert cliff."}},
{"id":"wh-222","num":222,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Taking the throne name Menkheperre and leading at least 17 campaigns into Syria and Palestine, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pushed Egypt's border to the Euphrates and had the record cut into Karnak.","questions":["The Annals cut into the Amun temple at Karnak count the prisoners and tribute brought back from the campaigns of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who ruled beside Hatshepsut for 21 years before ruling alone.","Buried in tomb 34 in the Valley of the Kings, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built the festival hall at Karnak and a temple of millions of years called Henket-ankh on the Theban west bank."],"answer":"Thutmose III","answerText":"Thutmose III","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1479 – 1425 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sole rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1458 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaigns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 17, in years 23 to 42</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","warfare","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Thutmose III</b> was a king of the eighteenth dynasty who reigned from about 1479 to 1425 BCE, one of the longest reigns in Egyptian history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to the throne as a young boy, so his aunt Hatshepsut took a royal titulary of her own and ruled beside him for 21 years; his sole rule began at her death and ran for more than thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In those years he led at least 17 campaigns into Syria and Palestine, reached the Euphrates, and carried Egypt's border to its furthest extent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The kings of his dynasty were pressing into a region of small city-states, none with the resources to resist them, though farther north the Mitanni and Hittite empires blocked any advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He built or rebuilt temples the length of the Nile valley and into Nubia, left as deep a mark on the Amun temple at Karnak as Hatshepsut had, and was buried in the Valley of the Kings in tomb 34.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The record of the campaigns was cut into the walls at Karnak as the Annals, and it is a ledger rather than a narrative, an enumeration built around the capture and quantity of prisoners and tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Counted up, it records at least 7,942 foreigners brought into Egypt across the twelve campaign years it covers, from year 23 to year 42.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About two thirds are servants received as tribute or as the contribution owed by Nubia rather than prisoners taken in battle, and the famous first campaign, the siege of Megiddo, yields 340 captives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the fighting won was not a province either: the city-states remained loose vassals who paid occasional tribute and were given no Egyptian administration of the kind installed in Nubia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the west bank at Thebes he raised a temple of millions of years of his own, the Henket-ankh, 'the temple which endows Menkheperre with life', built on three levels where the cultivated land met the desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Thutmose III,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/thutmosisiii.html. [Open access]","\"Asia and Egypt,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foreignrelations/asiank.html. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]","H&eacute;ctor Horacio Gerv&aacute;n, \"Introducci&oacute;n e integraci&oacute;n de extranjeros en el Egipto del Reino Nuevo: una aproximaci&oacute;n cuantitativa a los Anales de Tuthmosis III,\" <i>Anuario de la Escuela de Historia Virtual</i> 5, no. 5 (2014): 1&ndash;22, https://doi.org/10.31049/1853.7049.v0.n5.9364. [Open access]","Myriam Seco &Aacute;lvarez and Javier Mart&iacute;nez Bab&oacute;n, \"The Temple of Millions of Years of the Pharaoh Thutmose III (Luxor): An Update on the Research,\" <i>&Eacute;tudes et Travaux</i> 33 (2021): 179&ndash;220, https://doi.org/10.12775/EtudTrav.33.011. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Egypt-3B-023_-_Festival_Hall_%282217355364%29.jpg/1920px-Egypt-3B-023_-_Festival_Hall_%282217355364%29.jpg","title":"The festival hall of Thutmose III at Karnak","desc":"Akh-Menu of Tuthmosis III, the festival hall, Karnak Temple, Luxor, Egypt. Dennis G. Jarvis, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Dennis G. Jarvis, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt-3B-023_-_Festival_Hall_(2217355364).jpg","alt":"A long pillared hall of pale sandstone open to the sky, a row of square clerestory windows above its columns and fallen blocks on the gravel in front of it."}},
{"id":"rm-047","num":"47","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The roads from the north and east came down to the Tiber crossing through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where they met the road running under the Capitol out to the Porta Trigemina.","questions":["Livy says the one foreign rite Romulus took over was the worship of Hercules at an altar in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, kept by two families until it passed to public slaves.","In the panic after Cannae a Gaulish and a Greek couple were buried alive beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in a stone vault Livy says had held human victims once before."],"answer":"Forum Boarium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First temples</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">213 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">212 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Forum Boarium</b> was the cattle market of ancient Rome, the low ground between the Palatine, the Capitoline and the Aventine, reaching from the Velabrum down to the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a crossing before it was a market: the routes from the north and east came down the Vicus Iugarius and the Vicus Tuscus to the Tiber at the Pons Sublicius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There they met the road running from the Campus Martius between the Capitol and the river to the Porta Trigemina, and the road down the Circus Maximus valley from the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A bronze bull said to have been brought from Aegina stood in it, and some ancient authorities derived the name from that rather than from the trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The space was public property and hard to keep clear: buildings crowded in until the name covered a whole district, and boundary stones under Tiberius and Claudius fixed its eastern edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It held the Ara Maxima, the earliest centre of the cult of Hercules at Rome, which tradition placed where he killed the robber Cacus for stealing part of Geryon's herd and dragging the beasts backwards by the tail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy says this was the only foreign rite Romulus adopted, performed by Greek custom as Evander had instituted it and kept by the Potitii and Pinarii until it passed to public slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dionysius writes that the Romans call it the Greatest Altar, hold none in greater veneration, and swear oaths and pay tithes on it to make a bargain unalterable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The twin temples of Fortuna and Mater Matuta stood at the northern edge, credited by tradition to Servius Tullius, burnt in 213 BCE and rebuilt the year after, on ground where monumental temple building had begun between about 650 and 550 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The market was also a place of dread: in the panic after Cannae a Gaulish and a Greek couple were buried alive beneath it, in a stone vault Livy says had held human victims once before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Forum Boarium","tags":["place","geography","history","italy","religion","iron age"],"sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Forum Boarium,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 224–25, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Forum_Boarium.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Herculis Invicti Ara Maxima,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 253–54, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Ara_Maxima_Herculis.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.39–40, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1B*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 22.56–57, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0144%3Abook%3D22%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","\"Fortuna et Mater Matuta, Aedes,\" in <i>Digital Augustan Rome</i>, ed. David Gilman Romano and Nicholas L. Stapp (Tucson: University of Arizona, 2016), https://www.digitalaugustanrome.org/records/fortuna-et-mater-matuta-aedes/. [Open access]","Dominik Maschek, review of <i>The Genesis of Roman Architecture</i>, by John North Hopkins, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.10.30/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Franz_Alt_Rom_Forum_Boarium_1859.jpg/1920px-Franz_Alt_Rom_Forum_Boarium_1859.jpg","title":"Forum Boarium","desc":"Franz Alt, Rom, Forum Boarium, 1859, with cattle and an ox-cart in the square before the round temple. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Alt_Rom_Forum_Boarium_1859.jpg","alt":"A watercolour of a Roman square with a round colonnaded temple on the right, a church and tall bell tower behind, and an ox-cart, cattle and townspeople in the foreground"},"locator":{"name":"Forum Boarium","at":[12.4811,41.8889],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-051","num":"51","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Dionysius says the Romans repaired the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whenever storm or age damaged it, adding nothing to make it grander, so that it stayed the hut it had been.","questions":["Vitruvius points to the thatched roof of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as plain evidence of how simply the ancients had lived, its straw kept up beside the marble of the empire.","Crows dropped burning meat from an altar onto the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 12 BCE, and the fire was counted among the portents attending the death of Agrippa."],"answer":"casa Romuli","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hilltop huts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>casa Romuli</b> was a hut of straw with a thatched roof standing at the south-west corner of the Palatine, near the top of the Scalae Caci, which the Romans kept as the house of Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius says the twins were reared as herdsmen and lived on the hills in huts they built themselves, roofs and all, out of sticks and reeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of these, he writes, still stood in his own day on the flank of the Palatine that faces the Circus, and was held sacred by those who had charge of such things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they did with it is the point: nothing was added to make it grander, and whenever storm or age damaged it they repaired it back to what it had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vitruvius makes the same use of it, citing a thatched roof on a house of Romulus as plain evidence of how simply the ancients had lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A second casa Romuli, probably a copy, stood on the Capitoline, and it is that one Vitruvius names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The hut burned at least twice and was rebuilt each time, once when a rite the pontifices were performing inside it got out of hand,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and again in 12 BCE when crows dropped burning meat on it from an altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Neither fire was treated as an accident: the first was counted among the portents that sent the Romans to the Sibylline books, the second among those that attended the death of Agrippa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The hut was still on record in the 4th century CE, more than a thousand years after the settlement it stood for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind the memory lies something real, since the Palatine did carry huts whose traces survive, and the hilltop settlements of Rome grew together into one place across the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"casa Romuli","tags":["building","history","religion","italy","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Casa Romuli,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 101–2, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Casa_Romuli.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.79, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>The Architecture of Marcus Vitruvius Pollio</i> 2.1.5, trans. Joseph Gwilt (London: Priestley and Weale, 1826), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Vitruvius/2*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 48.43, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 5 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/48*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 54.29, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/54*.html. [Open access]","Dominik Maschek, review of <i>The Genesis of Roman Architecture</i>, by John North Hopkins, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.10.30/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Germalus_Huts_Model_%2849351033787%29.jpg/1920px-Germalus_Huts_Model_%2849351033787%29.jpg","title":"casa Romuli","desc":"A reconstruction model of the huts of the Germalus, one of the Palatine's two summits, made by Alberto Davico in 1950 and labelled as a model in the display. Jamie Heath, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germalus_Huts_Model_%2849351033787%29.jpg","alt":"A museum model of a hillside village of small oval thatched huts, with the outlines of their floors cut into the rock around them"},"locator":{"name":"casa Romuli","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-052","num":"52","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Plutarch says men were fetched from Tuscany to prescribe every detail of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which began with a trench of first-fruits and a furrow ploughed round the boundary.","questions":["Timaeus put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 813 BCE, Cincius at 728, Fabius Pictor at 747 and Cato at 751, so the Romans never agreed on the year.","The day chosen for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was 21 April, kept as the country's birthday, though a shepherds' festival called the Parilia had been held on it long before."],"answer":"founding of Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traditionally 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parilia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City forms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>founding of Rome</b> is the act the Romans put at the head of their own history, fixed to a single day and carried out, they said, by a rite learned from Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch has Romulus send to Tuscany for men who prescribed every detail of it in accordance with sacred writings, and taught it to him as a religious rite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A circular trench was dug and the first-fruits of everything good and necessary were laid in it, and every man threw in a handful of soil from the country he had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They called the trench <i>mundus</i>, the word they also used for the heavens, and took it as the centre from which the city was marked out in a circle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The founder shod a plough with a bronze share, yoked a bull and a cow, and drove a deep furrow round the boundary with every clod turned inwards; where a gate was to stand the share was lifted out and a gap left, so the wall was sacred and the gates were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The day was 21 April, kept as the birthday of the country, though a pastoral festival called the Parilia had been held on it before there was a city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Velleius puts the founding on the Palatine on that festival in the sixth Olympiad, which is 753 BCE by the reckoning of Varro that became standard and 751 by Cato's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The year was never settled among the Romans themselves: Timaeus gave 813 BCE, Cincius 728, Fabius Pictor 747, Cato 751 and Polybius 750.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy's account of the day is a quarrel rather than a ceremony, with the brothers taking auguries on separate hills, disputing which sign counted, and Remus killed for leaping the new wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Modern work on early Rome reaches the opposite conclusion from the tradition, finding people living on the site before the 8th century BCE and no single state there until the 7th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"founding of Rome","tags":["event","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 11–12, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.8, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.74, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Gary Forsythe, review of <i>The Beginnings of Rome: Italy and Rome from the Bronze Age to the Punic Wars (c. 1000–264 BC)</i>, by Tim Cornell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.03.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.03.26/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Fregio_architettonico_col_solco_primario_della_colonia_aquileiese%2C_I_sec._dc.jpg/1920px-Fregio_architettonico_col_solco_primario_della_colonia_aquileiese%2C_I_sec._dc.jpg","title":"founding of Rome","desc":"A Roman relief of the first furrow being ploughed at the founding of the colony of Aquileia, 1st century CE, the veiled official guiding a plough drawn by a bull and a cow. Sailko, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fregio_architettonico_col_solco_primario_della_colonia_aquileiese,_I_sec._dc.jpg","alt":"A marble relief in which a yoked bull and cow draw a plough guided by a man with his toga drawn over his head, with a group of togate men following behind"},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-053","num":"53","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy reports that some writers thought <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were suckled not by a wolf but by Larentia, whom the shepherds nicknamed 'she-wolf' for her unchaste life.","questions":["Being twins with no claim to precedence from seniority, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put the naming of their city to augury, one watching from the Palatine and the other from the Aventine.","The curule aediles Cnaeus and Quintus Ogulnius set a group of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as infants suckled by the she-wolf beside the Ficus Ruminalis, long before Livy wrote."],"answer":"Romulus and Remus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traditionally 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wolf group</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the 290s BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Romulus and Remus</b> are the twin brothers Roman tradition made the founders of the city, born to a Vestal at Alba Longa who named Mars as their father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has the king order them thrown into the Tiber, but the river was over its banks, so the men left the cradle in the standing water, and the flood as it fell set it down on dry ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A thirsty she-wolf came down from the hills to the crying and gave the babies her teats, and the king's flock-master Faustulus found her licking them and carried them home to his wife Larentia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy passes on the deflating version in the same breath: some writers held that Larentia was nicknamed 'she-wolf' among the shepherds for her unchaste life, and that this was where the marvel came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tradition was never one story: Plutarch opens his life of Romulus with rival accounts of who founded Rome and whose son Romulus was,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and Dionysius reports a version in which Remus was seized during the Lupercalia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Grown, the brothers put their grandfather Numitor back on the throne at Alba and then set out to build a city where they had been exposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Being twins, with no claim to precedence from seniority, they put it to the gods of the place by augury, Romulus watching from the Palatine and Remus from the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The signs were disputed, the quarrel turned violent, and Remus was killed for leaping the new wall, which left Rome with a fratricide at the head of its own foundation story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story was public and official long before Livy wrote it down: the curule aediles Cnaeus and Quintus Ogulnius set a group of the founders as infants suckled by the wolf beside the Ficus Ruminalis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dionysius could still see a bronze wolf and two infants of ancient workmanship beside the Lupercal,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and modern historians weigh the cycle element by element as a tradition rather than a record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Romulus and Remus","tags":["person","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.4, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.6–7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.79–80, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 1–2, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]","Gary Forsythe, review of <i>The Beginnings of Rome: Italy and Rome from the Bronze Age to the Punic Wars (c. 1000–264 BC)</i>, by Tim Cornell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.03.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.03.26/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Campana_relief_with_a_scene_depicting_the_moment_when_the_shepherd_Faustulus_finds_the_she-wolf_with_the_twins_Romulus_and_Remus%2C_2nd_century_AD%2C_Pergamon_Museum%2C_Berlin_%2849808961057%29.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Romulus and Remus","desc":"A Roman terracotta relief of the shepherd Faustulus finding the she-wolf with the twins, 2nd century CE, Pergamon Museum, Berlin. Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campana_relief_with_a_scene_depicting_the_moment_when_the_shepherd_Faustulus_finds_the_she-wolf_with_the_twins_Romulus_and_Remus,_2nd_century_AD,_Pergamon_Museum,_Berlin_%2849808961057%29.jpg","alt":"A terracotta relief in which a she-wolf stands beneath a rocky overhang suckling two infants, while a shepherd in a broad hat raises his hand in surprise at the right"},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-054","num":"54","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The twins beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are not part of the same work, having been added in the Renaissance and traditionally given to Antonio del Pollaiolo.","questions":["Restoration showed that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been cast in a single piece, a technique typical of the Middle Ages, and radiocarbon analysis in 2007 gave a medieval date.","A medieval date for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> severs it from the ancient she-wolves that Cicero, Sallust and Livy describe, though its traditional attribution was Etruscan."],"answer":"Capitoline Wolf","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Capitoline Wolf</b> is the bronze she-wolf in the Palazzo dei Conservatori in Rome, about 75 centimetres (30 inches) high, her head turned aside and her dugs heavy with milk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The twins beneath her are not part of the same work: they were added in the Renaissance and are traditionally given to the sculptor Antonio del Pollaiolo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient Rome certainly had statues of the subject, since the curule aediles Cnaeus and Quintus Ogulnius set a group of the founders as infants suckled by the wolf beside the Ficus Ruminalis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius describes another beside the Lupercal on the Palatine, a she-wolf suckling two infants, the figures in bronze and of ancient workmanship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The traditional attribution put the Conservatori bronze in an Etruscan workshop of the 5th century BCE, which made it one of those ancient statues rather than a copy of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That attribution was overturned from the workshop rather than the library: the restoration of the statue showed it had been cast in a single piece, a technique typical of the Middle Ages,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and radiocarbon analysis in 2007 gave a medieval date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the redating costs is the link to the ancient texts, since a medieval bronze cannot be any of the she-wolves that Cicero, Sallust and Livy describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is not a settled question: the statue was never unanimously accepted as an early or middle Republican masterpiece, and dating proposals are still being made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The uncertainty is about the object rather than the image, whose earliest forms are on coins showing the twins at the wolf, and which the imperial age set beside the meeting of Mars and Rhea Silvia as an emblem of the foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The motif is not only Roman: an infant abandoned to nature and saved by an animal recurs in other ancient traditions and is tied to the idea of kingship, and the wolf and twins are still a symbol of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Capitoline Wolf","tags":["object","art","history","italy","archaeology"],"sources":["“Capitoline She-wolf,” Museo Tattile Statale Omero, Ancona, https://museoomero.it/en/opere/capitoline-she-wolf/. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.79, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Genevieve Gessert, review of <i>She-Wolf: The Story of a Roman Icon</i>, by Cristina Mazzoni, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.09. [Open access]","Eric M. Moormann, review of <i>Pseudoantike Skulptur II: Klassizistische Statuen aus antiker und nachantiker Zeit</i>, by Sascha Kansteiner, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.08.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.08.19/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Capitoline_she-wolf_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome%2C_Italy_-_DSC05797.jpg/1920px-Capitoline_she-wolf_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome%2C_Italy_-_DSC05797.jpg","title":"Capitoline Wolf","desc":"The Capitoline Wolf in the Palazzo dei Conservatori, Rome, with the Renaissance twins beneath her. Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capitoline_she-wolf_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome%2C_Italy_-_DSC05797.jpg","alt":"A bronze she-wolf standing with her head turned to the viewer's left, mouth open, with two bronze infant boys kneeling beneath her to suckle"}},
{"id":"rm-055","num":"55","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Caesar told the crowd at his aunt's funeral that his family descended from Venus through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, giving his stock the sanctity of kings and the reverence owed to gods.","questions":["Livy gives two versions of the meeting between Latinus and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one a battle followed by peace, the other a parley in which the king simply gave his hand on it.","Alba Longa was built to bridge the four centuries between the fall of Troy, which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have escaped, and the founding of Rome in 753 BCE."],"answer":"Aeneas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1183 BCE, on one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alba founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 years after Lavinium</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Aeneas</b> is the Trojan whom Roman tradition made the ancestor of the Romans, spared at the sack of Troy along with Antenor because both had argued for peace and the surrender of Helen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy takes him through Macedonia and Sicily to the Laurentian coast, where the Trojans landed with nothing left but their arms and their ships and began to plunder the neighbourhood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tradition forks there: in one version Latinus was beaten in battle and then made peace, in the other he stepped out in front of his lines, asked the strangers who they were, and gave his hand on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Either way he gave Aeneas his daughter Lavinia, and the town Aeneas built took her name; war with Turnus of the Rutulians and with Mezentius of Caere followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aeneas called Trojans and Aborigines alike by the single name Latins, won his last battle, and was buried by the river Numicius, where he was addressed as Jupiter Indiges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legend's real work was to bridge four centuries, Troy having fallen in 1183 BCE on the reckoning that became standard, and Rome having been founded in 753.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Alba Longa filled the gap: Livy has Ascanius leave Lavinium to his mother and build Alba thirty years later, and the Silvian kings run from him down to Numitor and Amulius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It anchored a cult as well, since Dionysius reports that the Penates brought from Troy kept returning to Lavinium when they were moved to Alba, so six hundred men were sent back to keep their rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It anchored a family too: Livy notes that the Julian house claimed Iulus as the founder of their name, and Dionysius says his descendants held a priestly prerogative down to his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Julius Caesar used the claim in public, telling the crowd at his aunt Julia's funeral that the Julii went back to Venus, so that his stock had at once the sanctity of kings and the reverence owed to gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aeneas","tags":["person","history","religion","italy","bronze age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.3, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.45–70, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1C*.html. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 6, in <i>The Lives of the Caesars</i>, trans. J. C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.8, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Ara_Pacis_Aeneas-Numa_%2814727962756%29.jpg/1920px-Ara_Pacis_Aeneas-Numa_%2814727962756%29.jpg","title":"Aeneas","desc":"A relief panel of the Ara Pacis in Rome, a veiled figure sacrificing beside a small shrine of the Penates; scholars are divided over whether the sacrificer is Aeneas or Numa. Amphipolis, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ara_Pacis_Aeneas-Numa_%2814727962756%29.jpg","alt":"A weathered marble relief of a bearded man with his head veiled standing at a rustic altar, attendants and a sow at the left and a small temple above them"},"locator":{"name":"Lavinium","at":[12.4784,41.6616],"zoom":7}},
{"id":"rm-056","num":"56","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy says the Tullii, Servilii, Quinctii, Geganii, Curiatii and Cloelii entered the Roman senate from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose people were moved to Rome when their city fell.","questions":["Rome's war with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was for Livy a civil war between parent and child, since Lavinium was an offshoot of Troy and that city an offshoot of Lavinium.","One hour undid the four centuries <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had stood, and every building but the temples was levelled while its people walked out towards Rome."],"answer":"Alba Longa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 years after Lavinium</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 753 BCE, under Rome's 3rd king</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Alba Longa</b> is the city Roman tradition placed on the Alban Hills as the mother of Rome, built by Ascanius thirty years after Lavinium and named for the long ridge it was laid out along.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its kings, all bearing the name Silvius, fill the centuries between the fall of Troy and the founding of Rome, running down to Numitor and his brother Amulius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius makes it the mother of the towns of Latium as well, the Trojans moving up from Lavinium to build a larger walled city and sending colonists out from it to found the cities of the Prisci Latini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy calls the war that finished it a civil war between parent and child, both peoples being of Trojan descent, Lavinium an offshoot of Troy and Alba of Lavinium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was settled without a regular engagement, and the outcome was that one of the two cities was destroyed while the two nations were blended into one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy describes the demolition as a thing done in silence, the Albans wandering through houses they were seeing for the last time until the cavalry shouted them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They left their hearths and household gods behind, the Romans levelled every public and private building, and one hour undid the work of the four centuries Alba had stood; only the temples were spared, by the king's order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What followed is the point the story exists to make: the Roman citizen body was doubled, the Caelian hill was taken into the city, and Alban nobles were put into the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy names them — the Tullii, Servilii, Quinctii, Geganii, Curiatii and Cloelii — and says the enlarged senate needed a house of its own, the Curia Hostilia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the legend accounts for patrician families at Rome who were not Roman, and the yearly festival of the Latins went on being held on the mountain above Alban ground, with a common sacrifice to Jupiter Latiaris and Rome in charge of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Alba Longa","tags":["place","city","history","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.3, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.45, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1C*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.49, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg/1920px-Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg","title":"Alba Longa","desc":"The Reduction of Alba Longa by Tullus Hostilius, a Florentine chest panel by Lo Scheggia of about 1430–35. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg","alt":"A long painted panel showing a walled pink city on a ridge with armies of mounted and armoured men crossing the open ground before it"},"locator":{"name":"Alba Longa","at":[12.6503,41.7469],"zoom":7}},
{"id":"rm-057","num":"57","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Claudius held Secular Games in 47 CE as Rome's eight hundredth year, counting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the era whose epoch Varro fixed at 753 BCE.","questions":["Romans dated a document by naming the year's two consuls rather than by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which belonged to scholars and historians rather than to clerks.","Philip and his son were consuls when Rome kept its thousandth year in April 248, an anniversary counted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a foundation placed in 753 BCE."],"answer":"ab urbe condita","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Epoch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 BCE, Varro's reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome's 800th</span><span class=\"dt-v\">47 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome's 1000th</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 248 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ab urbe condita</b> is Latin for 'from the founding of the city', and names the practice of counting years from the beginning of Rome itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The epoch is 753 BCE on the reckoning that Varro settled and that became standard, where Cato had given 751 and earlier writers ranged from 813 down to 728.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was never how a Roman dated a document, though: the ordinary practice was to name the year's two consuls, and Dionysius quotes a censor's record that gives the consuls and then counts the years since the kings were expelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An era running from the founding therefore sat beside other ways of counting and belonged to scholars and historians rather than to clerks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy opens his history by measuring his subject that way, saying it goes back beyond 700 years, from small and humble beginnings to a greatness that begins to be overburdened by itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy also treats the founding as the edge of what a historian can vouch for, since what happened before the city or while it was being built suits the poets better than authentic records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The count did surface in public, but only when a round number came up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tacitus reports Secular Games held under the consulship of Claudius and Vitellius, eight hundred years from the foundation of Rome, which is 47 CE and which Claudius treated as the city's eighth centenary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Historia Augusta</i> describes the same thing at the millennium, gladiatorial shows and races in the Circus for the thousandth anniversary of the founding, held in April 248 while Philip and his son were consuls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even there the year is identified by its consuls, which is the whole point of the era: the count from the founding was how Rome thought about its own age rather than how it wrote a date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"ab urbe condita","tags":["concept","history","italy","chronology"],"sources":["Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.8, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.74, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i>, preface, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3Dpr. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 11.11, trans. John Jackson, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Tacitus/Annals/11A*.html. [Open access]","<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>The Three Gordians</i> 33, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Gordiani_Tres*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Fasti_Consularii_%28detail%29_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg/1920px-Fasti_Consularii_%28detail%29_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg","title":"ab urbe condita","desc":"A detail of the Roman consular and triumphal lists in the Capitoline Museums, each entry naming a magistrate, his office and the people he triumphed over, with a year number beside it. Jose Luiz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fasti_Consularii_%28detail%29_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg","alt":"Close-up of a marble slab cut with lines of Latin capitals in red-filled letters, each line ending in a Roman numeral"}},
{"id":"rm-058","num":"58","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy has envoys asking Rome's neighbours for intermarriage, and their refusal is what the tradition makes the cause of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at games held in honour of Consus.","questions":["Dionysius counts the women taken in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 683 and says Romulus married them to as many Roman men by the rites of each woman's own country.","The fighting that followed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended when the women ran between the armies, appealing to fathers on one side and husbands on the other."],"answer":"rape of the Sabine women","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 BCE or a few years after</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Women seized</span><span class=\"dt-v\">683, on one count</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>rape of the Sabine women</b> is the mass seizure of women from neighbouring peoples that Roman tradition placed at the very beginning of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy gives the motive: Rome was a match for its neighbours in war but had no women and no right of intermarriage, so its greatness threatened to last one generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Envoys who asked the surrounding nations for alliance and intermarriage were rebuffed everywhere, and asked in return whether Rome had opened an asylum for women as well as men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Romulus then announced games in honour of Consus and had them cried in the neighbouring towns, so that Caenina, Antemnae and Crustumerium came, and the whole Sabine people with their families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At a signal the young men seized the women who had come to watch; Dionysius puts their number at 683 and says Romulus married them to as many Roman men by each woman's own rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Both the date and the motive were argued over: Dionysius reports some placing it in Romulus's first year and Gellius in his fourth, with the motive given as a shortage of women, a pretext for war, or a design for alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch gives the Roman defence — neither wantonness nor a wish to do mischief but a purpose of binding two peoples together — beside the rival claim that only thirty were taken because Romulus wanted war rather than marriages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The episode left its mark on Roman weddings, since Talasius, the name shouted as one woman was carried past, became the cry at a Roman marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> War followed, Caenina attacking first and the Sabines under Titus Tatius last, and ended when the women ran between the armies, appealing to fathers on one side and husbands on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What was made was not a peace but a union: one state, the kingship shared, Rome the seat of government, the Romans taking the name Quirites and the thirty curiae named after Sabine women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"rape of the Sabine women","tags":["event","history","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.9, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D9. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.30–31, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2B*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 14–15, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Fregio_della_basilica_emilia%2C_ratto_delle_sabine.JPG/1920px-Fregio_della_basilica_emilia%2C_ratto_delle_sabine.JPG","title":"The seizure of the Sabine women on the Basilica Aemilia frieze","desc":"Fragments of the marble frieze from the Basilica Aemilia in the Roman Forum, 1st century BCE, carved with scenes of Rome's earliest legends.","credit":"Photo by Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fregio_della_basilica_emilia%2C_ratto_delle_sabine.JPG","alt":"A long fragmentary marble relief in a lit case, its broken slabs set in dark plaster, showing striding draped men carrying off women who twist away."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-059","num":"59","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy says the injured Sabine parents flocked to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because his was the most influential name in those parts, and the war that followed ended in a shared kingship.","questions":["One of Rome's three early centuries of knights, the Titienses, was named after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Sabine king who ruled at Rome beside Romulus until he was killed at a sacrifice.","Bribing the commander's daughter to let his troops into the Roman citadel is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seized it, and after the peace he lived on that hill while Romulus stayed on the Palatine."],"answer":"Titus Tatius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Joint reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 6th year, at Lavinium</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Titus Tatius</b> is the king of the Sabines whom Roman tradition makes Romulus's colleague on the throne, the one man said to have ruled Rome beside its founder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He enters the story after the seizure of the Sabine women, when the injured parents flocked to him from all sides because his was the most influential name in those parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Caenina, Antemnae and Crustumerium thought him too slow and attacked on their own account, so his war came last of the four and was much the most serious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took the Roman citadel by bribing the daughter of its commander to let his men in, and they crushed her under their shields once they were inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting that followed ran through the valley that later held the Forum, and stopped only when the Sabine women threw themselves between the lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the treaty made was not a peace between two states but one state out of both: the kingship was shared, Rome was the seat of government, and the Romans took the name Quirites from Cures, his own town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His name stayed in the institutions, since one of the three early centuries of knights was the Titienses, and a hundred Sabines were added to the patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dionysius credits him with altars to Sun and Moon, Saturn, Vesta, Vulcan and Diana, and with tables to Juno Quiritis in every curia; Plutarch puts his house on the citadel hill and Romulus's on the Palatine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two ruled five years in harmony, their one joint war against Cameria, until kinsmen of Tatius set upon envoys who had come to seek redress and killed them, and he shielded the offenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The dead men's friends killed him at the altar as he sacrificed at Lavinium, and Romulus, who buried him on the Aventine, pursued nobody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Titus Tatius","tags":["ruler","history","mythology","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.50–52, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2B*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 19–23, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/L._Titurius_L._f._Sabinus_Cr344-1c.jpg/1920px-L._Titurius_L._f._Sabinus_Cr344-1c.jpg","title":"Titus Tatius on a Roman denarius of 89 BCE","desc":"Silver denarius of the moneyer L. Titurius Sabinus, Rome, 89 BCE: the bearded head of King Tatius labelled SABIN, and two soldiers carrying off Sabine women.","credit":"Photo by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L._Titurius_L._f._Sabinus_Cr344-1c.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin side by side: a bearded head in profile with letters around it, and two striding soldiers each lifting a woman off the ground."},"locator":{"name":"Cures","at":[12.6866,42.1825],"zoom":7}},
{"id":"rm-060","num":"60","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The asylum between two groves below the Capitol, the hundred senators called Patres and the bodyguard of three hundred Celeres all belong in Livy to the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Rome's first king.","questions":["Dionysius says the patricians killed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the senate-house and carried the pieces of his body out under their robes, one of several ancient accounts of how Rome's first king ended.","A storm at the Goat's Marsh is where Livy has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disappear from the sight of his army, after which the Romans hailed him as a god and the son of a god."],"answer":"Romulus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">37 years, from 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vanished or died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">aged 54 or 55</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Romulus</b> is the first king of Rome in the Roman account of themselves, credited not only with founding the city but with giving it its institutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has him take twelve lictors, and prefers the explanation that the number came from the Etruscans to the one that made it the twelve birds of his augury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To fill a city built larger than its people, he opened a refuge between two groves below the Capitol, and free men and slaves came in from the states round about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He made a hundred senators, called Patres for their rank, whose descendants were the patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He kept three hundred men about him in peace as well as war, the Celeres, and was dearer to the people than to the patricians and dearest of all to his soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His wars were with the towns nearest Rome, and from the first he carried the arms of a king he had killed himself up to the Capitol and marked out there the temple of Jupiter Feretrius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The last was against Veii, which gave up land by the Tiber and the salt works at its mouth, sent fifty hostages and took a truce of a hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His end is told two ways: a storm at the <i>Caprae Palus</i> in the Campus Martius hid him from his army, after which the Romans hailed him as a god and the son of a god, and Proculus Julius swore he had met him coming down from heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Livy passes on a dim tradition that the senators tore him limb from limb, and Dionysius the charge that they killed him in the senate-house and carried the pieces out under their robes, because he had grown harsh and arbitrary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was said to have reigned 37 years and to have been in his mid-fifties, and Rome kept his name in the god Quirinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Romulus","tags":["ruler","history","mythology","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.15, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.16, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.55–56, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2B*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 27–29, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Jean-auguste-dominique_ingres%2C_romolo%2C_vincitore_di_acron%2C_porta_il_bottino_nel_tempio_di_giano%2C_1812%2C_00.jpg/1920px-Jean-auguste-dominique_ingres%2C_romolo%2C_vincitore_di_acron%2C_porta_il_bottino_nel_tempio_di_giano%2C_1812%2C_00.jpg","title":"Ingres, Romulus, Victor over Acron","desc":"Painted by Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres in 1812: Romulus leads away the arms stripped from Acron, king of Caenina, whose body lies on the right.","credit":"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-auguste-dominique_ingres%2C_romolo%2C_vincitore_di_acron%2C_porta_il_bottino_nel_tempio_di_giano%2C_1812%2C_00.jpg","alt":"A wide painting: a laurel-crowned man in a red cloak strides forward with one arm raised, a trophy of gilded armour carried behind him and a dead man lying at the right."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-061","num":"61","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy rejects the story that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> studied under Pythagoras, pointing out that the philosopher taught in southern Italy more than a century after this Sabine king was called to Rome.","questions":["The flamens of Jupiter, Mars and Quirinus, the Vestals, the twelve Salii with their shields and the first Pontifex Maximus are all credited by Livy to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Rome's second king.","The temple of Janus, open in war and shut in peace, is said to have stayed shut through the whole reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and to have been shut only twice after him."],"answer":"Numa Pompilius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">43 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">aged over 80</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Numa Pompilius</b> is the second king of Rome in the tradition, a Sabine from Cures whom the senators chose for his justice and piety though it tipped the balance of power towards the Sabines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy will not have the old story that he learned from Pythagoras, since the philosopher taught in southern Italy more than a century later, and puts his character down to the austere Sabine discipline instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was made king by augury, seated on a stone on the citadel facing south while an augur laid a hand on his head and asked Jupiter for signs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He set out to refound by law and custom the city that Romulus had founded by arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He built the temple of Janus as an index of war and peace and shut it, and Plutarch says Rome kept it shut for the whole of his reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> To hold a people with no war left to fear he taught them to fear the gods instead, and gave out that he was advised at night by the nymph Egeria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He appointed a flamen for Jupiter so that the king's rites would go on when kings took the field, and flamens for Mars and Quirinus beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He chose the Vestal virgins, gave them a public stipend and made their persons inviolable, and set twelve Salii to carry the shields called <i>ancilia</i> through the city in a dancing procession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first Pontifex Maximus was handed the whole of it written out and sealed, with which victims on which days at which temples and how the cost was met, and funerals, the dead and prodigies added to his charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy gives him 43 years of peace, and Plutarch has him die past eighty and be buried under the Janiculum in two stone coffins, one for his body and one for the books he had written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Numa Pompilius","tags":["ruler","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.18, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.19, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.21, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Numa</i> 20–22, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Numa*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/R%C3%B6m._Republik-_L._Pomponius_Molo_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5485585.jpg/1920px-R%C3%B6m._Republik-_L._Pomponius_Molo_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5485585.jpg","title":"Numa sacrificing, on a Roman denarius of 97 BCE","desc":"Denarius of L. Pomponius Molo: the head of Apollo, and on the reverse Numa Pompilius with the lituus at a lighted altar as a youth brings up a goat, labelled NVMA POMPIL.","credit":"Münzkabinett, Berlin (2358342), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%B6m._Republik-_L._Pomponius_Molo_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5485585.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a silver coin with notched edges: a laurelled head in profile, and two standing figures at a small altar with flames, a goat between them."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-062","num":"62","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Censorinus gives 355 days and twelve months for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one more day than the moon's twelve months fill, and says an odd number was thought fuller and luckier.","questions":["January and February were added and the year's start moved away from March under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why December still carries the name of a tenth month.","An intercalary month of 22 days, slipped into February every other year, is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was meant to be held to the sun, a task later left to the pontiffs."],"answer":"calendar of Numa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attributed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Numa, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Year of Numa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 months, 355 days</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>calendar of Numa</b> is the twelve-month year that Roman writers credited to their second king, put in place of an older year of ten months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What that older year had been they did not agree: Plutarch gives 360 days in months running from under 20 to 35, and Censorinus 304 days in ten named months of 30 or 31.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Censorinus is candid that the change itself was disputed, some historians holding that Rome had kept twelve months from the beginning, and others crediting the reform to Tarquinius rather than to Numa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The new year ran to 355 days, one more than twelve lunar months fill, which he puts down to a belief that an odd number was fuller and the luckier for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> January and February were made out of 51 added days together with one taken from each of the six hollow months, which gave January 29 days and February 28.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The order moved with them, since March had opened the year and became the third month while January, named from Janus, came first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The old order is still legible in the names, December being the tenth month and Quintilis and Sextilis the fifth and sixth counted from March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> February, left alone with an even number of days and thought the unluckier for it, was the month of purification and of offerings to the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because a lunar year drifts from the sun, an intercalary month of 22 or 23 days was put into February every other year, between the Terminalia and the Regifugium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy has that adjustment arranged so the days came round to the same point of the sun every twentieth year, and the pontiffs who later held the power to intercalate used it so freely that the civil year ran far from the natural one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"calendar of Numa","tags":["concept","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.19, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Numa</i> 18, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Numa*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Numa</i> 19, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Numa*.html. [Open access]","Censorinus, <i>De die natali</i> 20, ed. Friedrich Hultsch (Leipzig: Teubner, 1867), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Censorinus/text*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Calendarium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Calendarium.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Fasti_Antiates_maiores_reconstruction.png/1920px-Fasti_Antiates_maiores_reconstruction.png","title":"The Fasti Antiates Maiores, reconstructed","desc":"A painted calendar from Antium of the 1st century BCE, its surviving fragments shown in blue: twelve month columns with their day totals, and a thirteenth headed INTER for the intercalary month.","credit":"Reconstruction drawing by Levaring, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fasti_Antiates_maiores_reconstruction.png","alt":"A wide grid of thirteen columns of Latin abbreviations in red and black, with irregular blue patches marking the fragments that survive and Roman numerals along the foot."}},
{"id":"rm-063","num":"63","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> entertain the Alban envoys at dinner while his own demanded redress at Alba, so that the refusal and the guilt of the war would be Alba's.","questions":["The Caelian hill was taken into the city and the senate-house called the Curia Hostilia was built for the enlarged senate under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the third king of Rome.","Mettius Fufetius was torn apart between two four-horse chariots on the order of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a punishment Livy says the Romans never used again."],"answer":"Tullus Hostilius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">32 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">died in a fire with his household</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tullus Hostilius</b> is the third king of Rome in the tradition, chosen after the death of Numa and remembered as his opposite, a man Livy calls more warlike even than Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Believing the state was growing soft for want of a war, he went looking for a pretext and found one in the cattle-raiding that went both ways across the Alban border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He kept the Alban envoys at a sumptuous dinner while his own were demanding redress at Alba, so that the refusal, and with it the guilt of the war, should be Alba's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first Alban war was settled by a combat of triplets, and in the next campaign, against Fidenae and Veii, the Alban commander Mettius Fufetius drew his men off to the hills mid-battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tullus had him tied between two four-horse chariots driven in opposite directions, which Livy calls the first and last punishment of its kind among the Romans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Alba Longa itself was pulled down and its people ordered to Rome, and what Livy describes is not a sack but a silence, families wandering their own houses for the last time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The number of citizens was doubled, the Caelian hill was taken into the city and made the king's own residence, and Alban nobles were enrolled among the senators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the enlarged senate he built a senate-house that was still called the Curia Hostilia in the time of Livy's fathers, and he raised ten troops of knights from the Albans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Sabine war followed, and then prodigies: a shower of stones on the Alban Mount, a voice from the grove above it, and a plague that the king would not allow to interrupt the campaigning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He died in a fire that took his whole household with him, which Livy puts down to Jupiter's anger at rites from Numa's books performed wrongly, while Dionysius reports that most held it arson by his successor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tullus Hostilius","tags":["ruler","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.22, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.28, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.31, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D31. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.35, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3B*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg/1920px-Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg","title":"The Reduction of Alba Longa by Tullus Hostilius","desc":"Tempera, gold and silver on panel by Lo Scheggia, about 1430-35, from the front of a Florentine marriage chest: the fall of Alba imagined as a Renaissance siege.","credit":"Lo Scheggia, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg","alt":"A long narrow painted panel: a pink walled city on a hill with armies of armoured horsemen and foot soldiers spread across the plain before it."},"locator":{"name":"Alba Longa","at":[12.6503,41.7469],"zoom":7}},
{"id":"rm-064","num":"64","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Which side each set of triplets fought for is disputed in the sources for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Livy follows the majority in making the Horatii the Romans.","questions":["The one man left standing among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> feigned flight so that his three wounded opponents would follow at intervals, and killed them one after another.","Dionysius says the six who fought as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were cousins, the sons of twin sisters married on the same day to a Roman and an Alban."],"answer":"Horatii and Curiatii","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Tullus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left standing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of the 6</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Horatii and Curiatii</b> are the two sets of triplets who, in Roman tradition, settled the war between Rome and Alba Longa by fighting three against three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls the story one of the most widely celebrated in antiquity and admits that the accounts still disagree over which set was Roman, following the majority in giving the Horatii to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius makes the six of them cousins: an Alban named Sicinius married his twin daughters on one day to a Roman and an Alban, and each bore three sons at a birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In that version Tullus objects that it would be impious to arm cousins against one another, and the Alban commander answers that he had already sounded his own three in private.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before the fight the two peoples were bound by treaty, which Livy uses to set down the fetial forms: the pure grass fetched from the citadel, the <i>pater patratus</i>, and the flint that killed the swine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two of the Romans fell at once and all three Albans were wounded, which left one Horatius untouched and facing three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He ran, judging that wounded men would follow at unequal speeds, and killed them one at a time as they came up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His sister met him at the Capene gate and knew on his shoulder the cloak she had made for her betrothed among the Curiatii, and he killed her for weeping over an enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was condemned for treason by two appointed judges, appealed to the people at the king's own prompting, and was acquitted for his courage rather than for the justice of his cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An expiation was owed all the same, so his father made him pass under a beam laid across the street with his head covered, and Livy says it still stood in his day, kept in repair at public cost and called the Sister's Beam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Horatii and Curiatii","tags":["event","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.24, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D24. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.25, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.26, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D26. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.13, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3A*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.14, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3A*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Cavalier_d%27Arpino_-_Combat_of_the_Horatii_and_the_Curiatii_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Cavalier_d%27Arpino_-_Combat_of_the_Horatii_and_the_Curiatii_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Combat of the Horatii and the Curiatii","desc":"A fresco by Giuseppe Cesari on the wall of the room named after the story in the Palazzo dei Conservatori, Rome: four brothers already down and the last two closing.","credit":"Giuseppe Cesari, Capitoline Museums (MC 9006), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cavalier_d%27Arpino_-_Combat_of_the_Horatii_and_the_Curiatii_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"A wall painting in a decorated frame: two armies drawn up in ranks behind a clearing where four armoured men lie dead and two others fight."}},
{"id":"rm-065","num":"65","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The first bridge over the Tiber, the Pons Sublicius, and the port city of Ostia at its mouth are both credited to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the fourth king of Rome.","questions":["Livy has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> write down the fetial law for declaring war, under which a herald recited his demand at the frontier and waited 33 days before war was proclaimed.","Captured Latins from Politorium, Tellenae and Ficana were settled on the Aventine by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a grandson of Numa who had the old king's religious commentaries set up in public."],"answer":"Ancus Marcius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">founded Ostia and bridged the Tiber</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ancus Marcius</b> is the fourth king of Rome in the tradition, a grandson of Numa through his daughter, and Livy makes him a mixture of that grandfather and of Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He began by having the pontiff copy Numa's religious commentaries out of the old king's own papers and set them up in a public place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Latins took that for weakness and raided Roman land, reckoning that this king would spend his reign among chapels and altars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His answer was to write down the fetial law for declaring war, taken, Livy says, from the Aequicoli: a herald with his head bound in wool recites the demand at the frontier, at the gate and in the forum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Satisfaction was then allowed 33 days before war was proclaimed, a form Livy says the fetials still followed in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The war with the Latins took Politorium, Tellenae and Ficana, and the captured populations were carried off to Rome and settled on the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Politorium was razed once the Latins had reoccupied it, and the fighting ended in a pitched battle at Medullia that brought many thousands more into citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Janiculum across the river was walled and garrisoned, not for want of room but to keep a strong position out of enemy hands, and joined to the city by the first bridge over the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That bridge was the Pons Sublicius, named from the piles it stood on and built of wood without metal, which the pontiffs kept in repair with sacrifices as a sacred thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also dug the Fossa Quiritium, built a prison overlooking the Forum, and carried Roman power to the sea, walling Ostia at the Tiber mouth and setting salt pits on both banks, in a reign Livy gives as 24 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ancus Marcius","tags":["ruler","history","italy","iron age","religion"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.44–45, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3C*.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Pons Sublicius,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 401–2, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP%2A/Pons_Sublicius.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg/1920px-C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg","title":"Numa and Ancus Marcius on a Roman denarius","desc":"Denarius of the moneyer C. Marcius Censorinus, whose family claimed descent from both kings: their bearded heads are shown one behind the other on the obverse.","credit":"Photo by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin: two overlapping bearded heads in profile facing right, and a rider standing across the backs of two galloping horses."},"locator":{"name":"Ostia","at":[12.2917,41.7558],"zoom":8}},
{"id":"rm-066","num":"66","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"An eagle lifted the cap from the head of the Etruscan newcomer who became <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and his wife Tanaquil read it as a sign that the crown was coming to him.","questions":["Told to cut a whetstone with a razor to test his art, the augur Attus Navius did it, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up changing the centuries of knights.","The hundred new senators called the Lesser Houses, the first marking out of the Circus Maximus and the shops around the Forum are all credited by Livy to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Rome's fifth king."],"answer":"Tarquinius Priscus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">38 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">murdered by hired shepherds</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tarquinius Priscus</b> is the fifth king of Rome in the tradition and the first to come from outside Latium, a rich man of Tarquinii whose father was a Corinthian exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He moved to Rome at his wife Tanaquil's urging, since at Tarquinii he was still the son of a foreigner, while at Rome nobility was recent and won by merit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the Janiculum an eagle took the cap off his head, circled the carriage and put it back, which Tanaquil, expert like most Etruscans in such signs, read as the crown coming to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He entered the city as Lucius Tarquinius Priscus, made himself known by hospitality and kindness, and was trusted by Ancus with the guardianship of the king's sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says he was the first man to canvass for the crown and make a set speech for it, sending those boys out hunting on the day of the election.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A hundred new senators were added, called the Lesser Houses, and the plunder of Apiolae paid for games on a new scale, with a space first marked out for what became the Circus Maximus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wooden stands were allotted to patricians and knights to watch from, and the ground round the Forum was divided into building sites for arcades and shops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When he tried to change the centuries of knights, the augur Attus Navius told him nothing could be altered without the birds, and cut a whetstone through with a razor to prove his art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The king kept the names and numbers and only doubled the men in each, to 1,800, and went on to beat the Sabines and take town after town from the Latins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The works of peace were a stone wall, conduits draining the valleys to the Tiber and a platform levelled on the Capitol for a temple of Jupiter, until in his 38th year two shepherds sent by the sons of Ancus drove an axe into his head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tarquinius Priscus","tags":["ruler","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.38, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.40, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D40. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Sebastiano_Ricci_-_Tarquin_the_Elder_Consulting_Attius_Navius_-_72.PA.15_-_J._Paul_Getty_Museum.jpg/1920px-Sebastiano_Ricci_-_Tarquin_the_Elder_Consulting_Attius_Navius_-_72.PA.15_-_J._Paul_Getty_Museum.jpg","title":"Tarquin the Elder consulting Attus Navius","desc":"Oil by Sebastiano Ricci, J. Paul Getty Museum: the king in the red cloak, and the augur kneeling with his razor at the whetstone, one finger raised to the birds.","credit":"Sebastiano Ricci, J. Paul Getty Museum (72.PA.15), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sebastiano_Ricci_-_Tarquin_the_Elder_Consulting_Attius_Navius_-_72.PA.15_-_J._Paul_Getty_Museum.jpg","alt":"A Baroque painting: a laurel-crowned man in a red cloak stands at the right, hand to his mouth, watching an old bearded man kneel over a block with a blade."},"locator":{"name":"Tarquinii","at":[11.7561,42.2492],"zoom":7,"within":"Tarquinia"}},
{"id":"rm-067","num":"67","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy has Tarquinius Superbus set the plebs to dig <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a tunnel to take all the city's sewage, while Dionysius says the sewers were begun by the earlier Tarquin.","questions":["Strabo says wagons loaded with hay could pass through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Rome's other sewers in places, so closely were they vaulted with fitted stone.","The winding course of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the Argiletum through the Forum to the Tiber shows that it began as a stream in a marshy valley rather than as a dug tunnel."],"answer":"Cloaca Maxima","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Tarquins, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">from the Argiletum through the Forum to the Tiber</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cloaca Maxima</b> is the great drain of Rome, which carried the water off the Forum and the valleys between the hills and emptied it into the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy gives the work to Tarquinius Superbus, who set the plebs to it once the temple was finished, and calls it a tunnel underground to receive all the sewage of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius gives the sewers to the earlier Tarquin, who began the digging of them, and Livy himself has that king drain the low ground round the Forum into the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To Dionysius the aqueducts, the paved roads and the sewers were the three works in which the greatness of Rome could best be seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He adds, on the authority of Gaius Acilius, that the censors once let out the cleaning and repair of the neglected sewers for a thousand talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo describes them vaulted with close-fitting stone and wide enough in places for a wagon loaded with hay to pass through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The channel measured in one surviving stretch is 4.2 m (13.8 feet) high and 3.2 m (10.5 feet) wide, built of peperino, vaulted and paved with blocks of lava.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its winding course, from the Argiletum where it gathers the water of the Esquiline, Viminal and Quirinal, through the Forum and the Velabrum to the river, is the course of a stream in a marshy valley rather than a line laid out by engineers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Writers under the empire describe it as underground from the first, but Plautus calls it a <i>canalis</i>, an open channel, which has suggested that it was not roofed until later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Little if anything of the first drain survives: some branch channels near the temple of Saturn may be of the 5th century BCE at latest, and the rest is of the 3rd century and after, much of it from Agrippa's restorations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cloaca Maxima","tags":["building","history","technology","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.38, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.67, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3D*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.3.8, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5C*.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Cloaca Maxima,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 126–27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP%2A/Cloaca_Maxima.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Cloaca_Maxima_-_%283070_%3B_A1%29.jpg/1920px-Cloaca_Maxima_-_%283070_%3B_A1%29.jpg","title":"The Cloaca Maxima on the Tiber","desc":"An albumen photograph taken between 1860 and 1888, catalogued as a view of the Cloaca Maxima, with the round temple of the Forum Boarium and the campanile of Santa Maria in Cosmedin above.","credit":"Photo by Edmond Behles, University of Strasbourg, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cloaca_Maxima_-_%283070_%3B_A1%29.jpg","alt":"A sepia photograph of a river running past a long stone embankment wall, with a small arch at the waterline on the left and a round colonnaded temple and a bell tower on the bank above."},"locator":{"name":"Cloaca Maxima","at":[12.4803,41.8889],"zoom":9,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-068","num":"68","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Dionysius says the seats of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were divided among the thirty curiae so that every spectator sat in his own section, and that it held 150,000 in his day.","questions":["Livy dates the first starting gates in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to 329 BCE, which shows that nothing permanent stood in the valley before then, whatever the kings were credited with.","The valley between the Palatine and the Aventine where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was laid out had been used for horse races in honour of Consus long before any building stood there."],"answer":"Circus Maximus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Tarquins, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Starting gates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first attested 329 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Circus Maximus</b> is the oldest and largest of Rome's racecourses, laid out in the valley between the Palatine and the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The valley suited it, running about 600 m (660 yards) long and 150 m (160 yards) wide, with the slopes on either side for a crowd to stand on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Races in honour of Consus were held there in the tradition long before any building stood in it, and it was at those games that the Sabine women were seized.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy has Tarquinius Priscus first mark out a space there after the plunder of Apiolae, with places allotted to patricians and knights to build wooden stands on props.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius gives that king covered seats raised on scaffolding, where the spectators had stood before, and the places divided among the thirty curiae so that each had its own section.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy also has the last Tarquin set the plebs to the stands, so that the tradition divides between the two kings and modern topography treats both as tradition rather than record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first firm date is Livy's notice that the starting gates were put up in the circus in 329 BCE, which makes it plain nothing permanent had stood there before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those gates were probably of wood, since a century later a poet quoted by Cicero has the chariots sent out from painted jaws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By Dionysius's own day the course ran three and a half stades, ringed on three sides by a canal ten feet deep and wide and by porticoes of three storeys, the lowest of stone and the two above of wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He gives the circuit as eight stades and the crowd as 150,000, with vaulted starting-places all opened by a single rope and an outer portico of shops through which the crowd came and went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Circus Maximus","tags":["building","history","italy","iron age","sport"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.68, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3D*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Circus Maximus,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 114–20, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP%2A/Circus_Maximus.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Topographical_diagram_of_the_Circus_Maximus_by_Samuel_Ball_Platner.jpg/1920px-Topographical_diagram_of_the_Circus_Maximus_by_Samuel_Ball_Platner.jpg","title":"Plan of the Circus Maximus","desc":"A printed plan by Samuel Ball Platner: the starting gates at the left, the long spina down the middle with the two obelisks, the curved end at the right, and the Palatine above.","credit":"Samuel Ball Platner, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Topographical_diagram_of_the_Circus_Maximus_by_Samuel_Ball_Platner.jpg","alt":"An engraved ground plan: a long narrow arena with tiers of seating hatched along both sides, a straight barrier down the centre and a rounded end."},"locator":{"name":"Circus Maximus","at":[12.4857,41.8859],"zoom":9,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-069","num":"69","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Flames were seen to play about the head of the sleeping boy who became <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Tanaquil read the omen and had him brought up as a son of the house.","questions":["Tanaquil told the crowd from a palace window that the wounded king would recover and had ordered them to obey <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who judged cases for days as though the power were lent.","The temple of Diana on the Aventine was built jointly by Rome and the Latin peoples under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Livy reads as an admission of Roman predominance."],"answer":"Servius Tullius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">44 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">killed at his daughter's urging</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Servius Tullius</b> is the sixth king of Rome in the tradition, and the one the Romans credited with their oldest institutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy tells of flames playing about his head as he slept, seen by many, which Tanaquil read as a promise; the boy was then brought up as a child of the house and married to the king's daughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether he was born a slave Livy doubts, preferring the account in which his father was the leading man of Corniculum, killed when the town fell, and his mother spared as a captive of rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Tarquin was struck down, Tanaquil closed the palace, dressed the wound and told the crowd from an upper window that the king lived and had ordered them to obey Servius meanwhile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He sat in the royal chair, judged some cases and adjourned others as though consulting the king, and by the time the mourning was heard the throne was his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He preferred statecraft to arms, and his chief work abroad was the temple of Diana on the Aventine, built jointly by Rome and the Latins, which Livy reads as their admission of Roman predominance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dionysius adds that the cities paid for it together and that a bronze pillar stood in the temple, engraved with the council's decrees and the names of the cities that had joined, still there in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Sabine heifer belongs to that temple: a prodigy whose sacrifice was to give empire to the sacrificer's city, until a Roman priest sent the Sabine to wash in the Tiber and offered it himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His end came out of his own household, and the street kept the memory of it, for his daughter Tullia drove her carriage over his body in what was afterwards called the Vicus Sceleratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy gives him 44 years and says that with him all lawful kingship at Rome perished, adding that some held he had meant to lay the power down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Servius Tullius","tags":["ruler","history","italy","iron age","religion"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.39, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D39. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.41, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.45, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D45. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.48, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D48. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.26, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4B*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Servius_Tullius_When_a_Child%2C_Asleep_in_the_Apartment_of_King_Tarquinius_Priscus%2C_Angelica_Kauffmann%2C_1785.webp/1920px-Servius_Tullius_When_a_Child%2C_Asleep_in_the_Apartment_of_King_Tarquinius_Priscus%2C_Angelica_Kauffmann%2C_1785.webp","title":"Servius Tullius as a child, asleep in the palace of Tarquinius Priscus","desc":"Angelica Kauffmann, 1785, painted in grisaille. The scene is the prodigy Livy reports at 1.39: flames playing about the sleeping boy's head, a servant fetching water to douse them, and the queen Tanaquil holding him back so that the sign should not be disturbed.","credit":"Angelica Kauffmann, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Servius_Tullius_When_a_Child%2C_Asleep_in_the_Apartment_of_King_Tarquinius_Priscus%2C_Angelica_Kauffmann%2C_1785.webp","alt":"Painting in shades of grey: a boy sleeps on a couch with flames about his head while a woman restrains a servant carrying a vessel of water."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-070","num":70,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a citizen's place in the army and on the tax lists followed the rating of his property rather than his birth, and each class was divided into centuries.","questions":["Livy ranks Servius beside Numa as a founder of institutions for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which sorted the citizens into classes by wealth and turned four regions of the city into tribes.","The richest men carried the heaviest levy and the heaviest tax under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while everyone below the lowest rating made up one century that owed neither service nor money."],"answer":"Servian constitution","answerText":"Servian constitution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 44-year reign of Servius Tullius, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Centuries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">193, of which 98 in the first class</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City tribes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, replacing the earlier 3</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Servian constitution</b> is the arrangement that Roman tradition credits to Servius Tullius, under which the duties of peace and war were laid on each man in proportion to what he owned rather than on all alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls it by far the greatest of his works in peacetime and ranks Servius with Numa as a founder, the one of religious law, the other of the grades of dignity and fortune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rating sorted the citizens into classes, and each class into centuries of juniors, who took the field, and seniors, who stayed to hold the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy's first class is those whose property reached 100,000 in the old copper reckoning, formed into 80 centuries and armed in bronze with helmet, round shield, greaves and coat of mail; each class beneath lost a piece of that equipment as the rating fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Below the last rating came a single century, excused the levy, and Dionysius adds that it paid no tax of any kind either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius counts 193 centuries altogether, 98 of them in the first class once the 18 centuries of cavalry are reckoned with its 80.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cavalry followed the same principle: Livy has 10,000 from the treasury for each horse and certain widows assessed 2,000 a year towards its keep, so the burden was shifted from the poor on to the rich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Servius also cut the city into four regions named after the hills and made tribes of them, so that levy and tax rested on where a man lived, not on the three tribes of descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The countryside was divided in the same way, though the number was already disputed when Dionysius wrote: he reports 26 parts from Fabius and 31 from Vennonius, and says Cato gave no figure at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the people were afterwards summoned to vote by these same centuries, the reform settled who decided as well as who paid, the wealthiest centuries being called first and the poorest hardly ever reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.14, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#14. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.15, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#15. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.18, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#18. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.20, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#20. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg/1920px-Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg","title":"The census frieze: a citizen gives his declaration","desc":"Left panel of the so-called census frieze from the Altar of Domitius Ahenobarbus, marble, late 2nd century BCE, from the Campus Martius, now in the Louvre (Ma 975). A clerk writes on a tablet while a citizen declares his property and a soldier is armed at the right. The relief is several centuries later than the reform Roman writers ascribed to Servius Tullius, and shows the procedure as the Republic knew it.","credit":"Photograph by Jastrow, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg","alt":"Marble relief of a seated clerk writing on a tablet, a standing man in a toga declaring, a second seated official, and a soldier putting on a plumed helmet."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-071","num":71,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Roman citizens voting in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called up by centuries in order of wealth, so that the richest were asked first and the poorest were seldom reached at all.","questions":["Because an army could not lawfully be commanded inside the pomerium, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met out on the Campus Martius with a guard posted while the people gave their votes.","Livy has the first two consuls elected by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the prefect of the city, on the regulations that Roman writers ascribed to Servius Tullius."],"answer":"comitia centuriata","answerText":"comitia centuriata","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Instituted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the reign of Servius Tullius, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Centuries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">193, called in order of wealth</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">elected by it, after 244 years of kings</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>comitia centuriata</b> is the Roman assembly in which citizens voted not as individuals but in the centuries their property rating had placed them in, each century casting a single vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman writers derive it from the rating ascribed to Servius Tullius, and Livy says the older order had given every man the same weight, which Servius replaced with a graduation that left nobody openly disfranchised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The knights were summoned first, then the 80 centuries of the first class, and if these agreed the question was settled before the classes beneath them were reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius sets out the same order and says the calling seldom went past the fourth class, so that the fifth and the century of the poor were as good as superfluous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He reads the arrangement as a deception rather than a franchise, since every man was asked his opinion, yet a century of few counted for as much as a century of many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Aulus Gellius preserves the jurist Laelius Felix on how the three assemblies differ: the curiate votes by birth, the centuriate by rating and age, the tribal by district and place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same passage gives the reason they met on the Campus Martius: holding them within the pomerium was against religion, since an army may be commanded outside the city and not within it, and a guard stood watch while the people voted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What it decided was the heaviest business of the state: Livy has the first two consuls elected by the centuries under the prefect of the city, on the regulations of Servius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It went on to choose consuls, praetors, censors and decemvirs, to make law of what the senate laid before it, to decide on war, and to hear a citizen's appeal against a capital sentence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That the arrangement was altered later is plain from both Livy and Dionysius, but no ancient writer says when the change came or what it consisted of, and it is reconstructed from passing allusions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.20, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#20. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.21, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#21. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Noctes Atticae</i> 15.27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Gellius/15*.html#27. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Comitia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Comitia.html. [Open access]"],"locator":{"name":"Campus Martius","at":[12.4772,41.8978],"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/P._Licinius_Nerva_Cr292-1.jpg/1920px-P._Licinius_Nerva_Cr292-1.jpg","title":"A Roman voting scene on a denarius of P. Licinius Nerva","desc":"Silver denarius, Crawford 292/1. Roma helmeted on the obverse; on the reverse two citizens on the raised gangway, one taking a tablet from an attendant below and one dropping his into the basket. The type shows Roman voting without naming which assembly it is.","credit":"Photograph by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P._Licinius_Nerva_Cr292-1.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin: a helmeted head on the left; on the right, small figures on a railed gangway, one bending to take a tablet, another placing one in a basket."}},
{"id":"rm-072","num":72,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took in the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline, and Dionysius says the king it is named after was the last to enlarge the circuit of the city.","questions":["Most of what still stands of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is of Grotta Oscura tufa, quarried in the territory of Veii and laid after the Gauls had shown that Rome lay open.","Where no cliff protected the city, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran as an earth rampart with a broad ditch before it, across the tablelands of the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline."],"answer":"Servian Wall","answerText":"Servian Wall","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the reign of Servius Tullius, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in squared stone, from 377 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Agger</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 stadia long, 50 Roman feet thick</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","history","italy","iron age","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>Servian Wall</b> is the circuit of wall, rampart and ditch that Roman tradition ascribes to Servius Tullius, and whose remains still stand at points across modern Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy says the king surrounded the city with a mound, moats and a wall, and that in doing so he extended the pomerium, the consecrated strip that no one might build on or cultivate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The work followed the census: with 80,000 counted, Livy writes, the city plainly had to be enlarged, so the Quirinal and Viminal were taken in, and then the Esquiline, where the king went to live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius gives the same two hills and calls Servius the last king to enlarge the circuit, which he did only after consulting the auspices as the law directed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that by his own day the wall was hard to make out among the buildings crowded against it, though it kept traces of its old structure in several places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest stretches that survive are of grey cappellaccio tufa, and are usually assigned to a wall of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of what stands is later: after the Gauls had shown that Rome lay open, the circuit was reinforced on largely the old line in blocks of Grotta Oscura tufa, quarried in the territory of Veii soon after that town fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy has the censors let a contract for a wall of squared stone, paid for by a tax that drove debtors into fresh borrowing, and a later year given over to repairing walls and towers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the ground offered no cliff, across the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline, the defence was an agger, an earth rampart of seven stadia with a ditch before it thirty Roman feet deep and a hundred wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its line ran from the Tiber to the Capitol, north-east along the cliffs of that hill and the Quirinal, south over the Esquiline and round the Caelian and Palatine, and back to the river past the Forum Boarium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.13, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#13. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Murus Servii Tullii,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 350-55, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Murus_Servii_Tullii.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Esquilino_-_mura_serviane_all%27auditorium_1120875.JPG/1920px-Esquilino_-_mura_serviane_all%27auditorium_1120875.JPG","title":"Blocks of the wall built into a later building at Largo Leopardi","desc":"A stretch on the Esquiline, at Largo Leopardi in Rome, where the great tufa blocks stand incorporated in the brickwork of the building known as the Auditorium of Maecenas. Dionysius wrote in his own day that the wall was hard to make out among the buildings crowded against it.","credit":"Photograph by Lalupa, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Esquilino_-_mura_serviane_all%27auditorium_1120875.JPG","alt":"Large squared blocks of pale tufa standing at the corner of a brick building on a modern street, with an iron railing and an earth bank to the left."},"locator":{"name":"Servian Wall","at":[12.5017,41.9017],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-073","num":73,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A man gave his name, his age, his wife and children and the worth of his goods on oath at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and whoever stayed away from it risked imprisonment or death.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closed with the whole people drawn up in arms while a boar, a ram and a bull were led three times round them and sacrificed to Mars.","Livy makes the first count 80,000 and Dionysius 84,700 at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Republic afterwards entrusted to two magistrates elected for that business alone."],"answer":"Roman census","answerText":"Roman census","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Instituted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Servius Tullius, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">every fifth year, by censors from 443 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First count</span><span class=\"dt-v\">80,000 in Livy, 84,700 in Dionysius</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","practice"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman census</b> is the sworn registration of the citizens and their property, instituted in Roman tradition by Servius Tullius so that the duties of peace and war might fall on each man in proportion to what he owned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says the work was hurried along by a law that threatened imprisonment and even death against anyone who evaded assessment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A man declared on oath his own name and his father's, or his patron's if he was a freedman, and stated his age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was then asked whether he had a wife, and gave her name with the number, names and ages of his children, while widows and orphans were entered by their guardians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius adds that Servius made freedmen declare with everyone else and put them in the four city tribes, where their descendants still stood in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The register closed with a muster, the whole people under arms in the field before the city and drawn up by centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A boar, a ram and a bull were led three times round the army and sacrificed to Mars, whose field it was, and that purification gave the ceremony its name, the lustrum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy puts the count at 80,000 and says Fabius Pictor, the oldest of the Roman historians, took it for the number who could bear arms, while Dionysius gives 84,700 from the censors' own records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the Republic the consuls took the register until 443 BCE, when it passed to two censors of its own, an office that carried with it the <i>regimen morum</i>, the oversight of how citizens lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lustrum was meant to fall in every fifth year and gave its name to that span, though between 443 and 294 BCE only 21 were performed where 30 would have been regular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.22, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#22. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Censor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 260-66, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Censor.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Lustrum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 719-20, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Lustrum.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n3.jpg/1920px-Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n3.jpg","title":"The victims led round the army: ram, boar and bull","desc":"Right panel of the census frieze from the Altar of Domitius Ahenobarbus, marble, late 2nd century BCE, from the Campus Martius, now in the Louvre. Attendants lead a ram and a boar past soldiers with oval shields, the bull's hindquarters at the left edge. This is the triple offering Livy and Dionysius put at the close of the count.","credit":"Photograph by Jastrow, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n3.jpg","alt":"Marble relief of two half-dressed attendants leading a fleecy ram and a bristled boar, with helmeted soldiers holding tall oval shields behind them."},"locator":{"name":"Campus Martius","at":[12.4772,41.8978],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-074","num":74,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Roman writers make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the seventh and last king, who took the throne by violence, kept a bodyguard about him, and let the senate dwindle by filling no vacancy in it.","questions":["Failing to storm Gabii, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent his son Sextus there as a pretended refugee, and answered his request for orders by striking the heads off the tallest poppies in his garden.","The spoils of Suessa Pometia gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the forty talents of silver he set aside for the temple of Jupiter, and the Volscian war he began ran on for two centuries."],"answer":"Tarquinius Superbus","answerText":"Tarquinius Superbus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 years, the last of the seven, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 244 years of kings, by Livy's count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Volscian war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened by him, over 200 years long</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","history","italy","iron age","warfare"],"abstract":"<b>Tarquinius Superbus</b> is the seventh and last king of Rome in Roman tradition, who took the throne by violence after the murder of Servius Tullius and reigned without being elected by the people or confirmed by the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says he earned the name by refusing his father-in-law burial, on the plea that Romulus had none, and by killing the leading men he suspected of favouring Servius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius draws the same surname from his bearing: he gave audience only to those he had sent for, did his business at home before a few intimates, and decided disputes by his mood rather than by law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both make the bodyguard the mark of the reign, a company of armed men, Roman and foreign, camped about the palace at night and attending him by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He tried capital cases without assessors, which let him kill, banish or fine anyone he suspected or disliked, or merely hoped to squeeze money out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He let the senate dwindle by filling no vacancy, so that its numbers and its standing fell together, and was the first king to make war, peace, treaties and alliances without asking it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius adds that he had the tables of Servius's laws taken out of the Forum and destroyed, abolished the tax based on the rating, and made the poorest citizen pay as much as the richest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was no mean soldier for all that: he opened the long war with the Volsci, took Suessa Pometia, and set aside the forty talents of silver from its spoils for a temple to Jupiter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gabii he took by fraud instead, sending his son Sextus there as a pretended refugee from his cruelty until the town made him its commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked for orders, the king walked in his garden and struck the heads off the tallest poppies with his stick, and after 25 years he was shut out of the city and the kingship was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.49, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D49. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.53, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D53. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.54, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.41, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#41. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.43, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#43. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Lawrence_Alma-Tadema_11.jpeg","title":"Tarquinius Superbus striking the poppy heads","desc":"Lawrence Alma-Tadema, 1867, oil on panel, private collection. The king walks in his garden with his stick while his son's messenger waits for an answer, the scene Livy gives at 1.54, in which the silent gesture told Sextus what to do with the leading men of Gabii.","credit":"Lawrence Alma-Tadema, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lawrence_Alma-Tadema_11.jpeg","alt":"Painting of a man in dark red robes holding a staff among tall red poppies, two figures in white watching him from the left, one carrying a tray."},"locator":{"name":"Gabii","at":[12.7158,41.8869]}},
{"id":"rm-075","num":75,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Digging the foundations of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned up a human head with the face perfect, which the soothsayers read as a sign that the place would be the head of all the world.","questions":["Vowed by one Tarquin and built by another, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held three shrines side by side under one pediment, Juno on one hand, Minerva on the other and Jupiter in the middle.","Terminus alone refused to be moved from the ground cleared for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Romans read the omen to mean that the bounds of their empire would stand firm for ever."],"answer":"Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus","answerText":"Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vowed by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tarquinius Priscus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 3rd consulship, by the Republic</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 shrines under one pediment, sides near 200 feet</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus</b> was the great Capitoline temple of Rome, vowed by Tarquinius Priscus and built by Tarquinius Superbus on the height Livy calls the Tarpeian Mount.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dionysius says the elder Tarquin vowed temples to Jupiter, Juno and Minerva in a Sabine war and squared off the peak with retaining walls and banks of earth, but did not live to raise them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To free the ground for one deity the younger king deconsecrated the shrines already standing on it, some of them vowed by Titus Tatius in his battle with Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The omens allowed every removal but one: Terminus would not be called out from his borders, which was read to mean that the bounds of the empire would stand firm for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Digging the foundations then turned up a human head with the face still perfect, and the soothsayers of the city and of Etruria alike gave out that the place would be the head of all the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius has the Capitol take its name from that head, the Romans calling heads <i>capita</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What it cost is already in dispute in Livy, who prefers Fabius's forty talents from the spoils of Pometia to Piso's forty thousand pounds of silver, and says the money barely paid for the foundations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Workmen were fetched from all Etruria, the treasury met the bills, and the plebs were compelled to labour on it besides serving in the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The king was driven out before the work was done, and Dionysius says the Roman people finished it in the third consulship of the Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He makes it a high base some 800 feet in circuit with its sides close to 200, holding three shrines side by side under one pediment, Juno on one hand, Minerva on the other and Jupiter between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.59, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#59. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.61, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#61. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.53, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D53. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_showing_sacrifice.jpg/1920px-Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_showing_sacrifice.jpg","title":"Sacrifice before the Capitoline temple","desc":"Marble panel from a lost arch of Marcus Aurelius, later 2nd century CE, now in the Capitoline Museums. The emperor pours the offering while the bull is brought up, and behind them stands the temple front, with figures in the pediment. The building shown is a later rebuilding on the same foundations, not the temple the Tarquins raised.","credit":"Photograph by MatthiasKabel, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_showing_sacrifice.jpg","alt":"Marble relief of a veiled man pouring an offering, attendants and a bull beside him, with a columned temple front and a sculpted pediment behind."},"locator":{"name":"Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus","at":[12.4831,41.8933],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-076","num":76,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A stranger offered nine of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Tarquin, burned three when he refused her price, burned three more when he laughed, and got that price for the last three.","questions":["Kept in a stone chest under the Capitoline temple, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were opened only at the senate's order, when a prodigy or a disaster called for the gods to be appeased.","It was on the instruction of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Rome held its first lectisternium during a plague and later sent to Epidaurus to fetch Aesculapius."],"answer":"Sibylline Books","answerText":"Sibylline Books","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Acquired</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under the Tarquins, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Books</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 offered, 6 burned, 3 bought</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Keepers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 men, later 10, then 15</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Sibylline Books</b> were a collection of oracles kept by the Roman state and opened, at the senate's order, when a prodigy or a disaster called for the gods to be appeased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aulus Gellius tells from the old annals how a stranger, an old woman unknown at Rome, brought nine books to Tarquin and asked a price he thought absurd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She burned three and asked the same sum for the six, and when he laughed the harder she burned three more and asked it again for the last three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius has the king call in the augurs, who told him he had thrown away a gift of the gods, so he paid in full for what was left; the woman handed the books over, bade him guard them, and was never seen again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Which Tarquin bought them is not agreed, some accounts naming Priscus and others Superbus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> They were kept underground in the temple of Jupiter on the Capitol, in a stone chest, and nobody but their keepers might look at them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those keepers grew with the state, from two men under the kings to ten and then fifteen, with public slaves to help them and no inspection allowed in the slaves' absence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy shows what consulting them meant: a pestilence brought the first lectisternium at Rome, six gods propitiated for eight days on three couches spread with the finest coverlets to be had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another plague, and the books sent an embassy to Epidaurus to fetch Aesculapius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether they foretold anything or only prescribed what should be done is hard to say, since the originals burned with the temple and their replacements were scraped together from Italy, Greece and Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.62, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#62. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Noctes Atticae</i> 1.19, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Gellius/1*.html#19. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.47, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Sibyllini Libri,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1043-44, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Sibyllini_Libri.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Nicolai_Abildgaard%2C_Tarquinius_og_sibyllen%2C_1809%2C_KMS3724%2C_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst.jpg","title":"Tarquin and the Sibyl","desc":"Nicolai Abildgaard, 1809, Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen. The woman stands with the surviving rolls under her arm while the wreathed king leans forward from his couch to take them, the moment at which he pays for three what he would not pay for nine.","credit":"Nicolai Abildgaard, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolai_Abildgaard,_Tarquinius_og_sibyllen,_1809,_KMS3724,_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst.jpg","alt":"Painting in muted tones of an old woman in a dark cloak holding rolled scrolls under one arm, a wreathed man leaning from a couch to grasp her arm."},"locator":{"name":"Capitoline Hill","at":[12.4831,41.8933],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-077","num":77,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy sets the story of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the siege of Ardea, where princes praising their wives over wine rode to Rome to see how each was spending the evening.","questions":["Told that the mind sins and not the body, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that she acquitted herself of the fault but not of the penalty, and then took her own life.","Dionysius has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drive to Rome in black with a dagger hidden under her robes, where Livy instead has her send for her father and her husband."],"answer":"Lucretia","answerText":"Lucretia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Story set</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at the siege of Ardea, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consequence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the end of 244 years of kings</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accounts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, differing on where she died</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","italy","iron age","event"],"abstract":"<b>Lucretia</b> is the Roman noblewoman whose rape by the king's son and death by her own hand are, in Roman tradition, what brought the monarchy down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy sets the beginning of it at the siege of Ardea, where the princes fell to praising their own wives over wine and rode to Rome to see how each was passing the evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They found the king's daughters-in-law at their feasting and Lucretia at her wool with her maids about her, late at night in the hall at Collatia, and gave her the prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius tells it without any wager, having Sextus lodge at Collatia in the house of his kinsman while on his father's military business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both accounts agree on what followed: he was received as a guest, and went armed to her room while the household slept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In Livy, when the threat of death would not move her, he threatened to kill her and lay a slave's body beside hers so that it might be called adultery, and by that threat he had his way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She sent for her father and her husband, each with one friend he could trust, and told them plainly what had been done and by whom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They urged that it is the mind that sins and not the body, and that where there has been no consent there is no guilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She answered that she acquitted herself of the fault but not of the penalty, so that no unchaste woman should live by her example, and stabbed herself with a knife she had hidden in her dress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius has her drive to Rome instead, in black with a dagger under her robes, tell her story to the leading men in her father's house and die there in his arms, at which those present cried that they would sooner die a thousand deaths than bear such things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.57, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.58, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.64, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#64. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.66, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#66. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.67, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#67. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Sandro_Botticelli_078.jpg/1920px-Sandro_Botticelli_078.jpg","title":"Lucretia's body in the square","desc":"Sandro Botticelli, from his panel telling the story of Lucretia. Her body has been carried out and laid on a bier under a triumphal arch; a man stands over it on the plinth with a drawn sword while the armed crowd gathers, which is where the Roman accounts turn from her death to the overthrow of the kings.","credit":"Sandro Botticelli, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sandro_Botticelli_078.jpg","alt":"Painting of a woman's body laid out on a low bier in a public square, a man with a raised sword standing above her on a column base, armed men crowding in on both sides."},"locator":{"name":"Collatia","at":[12.6666,41.9251]}},
{"id":"rm-078","num":78,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy dates the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from an oath sworn over the knife drawn from Lucretia's wound, that those swearing would suffer nobody to reign at Rome again.","questions":["Dionysius has the king ride for the city during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only to find the gates shut and the battlements full of armed men, and turn back to a camp already lost.","The two magistrates first chosen at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were named to exercise the power of the kings, under a title Dionysius glosses for his Greek readers as counsellors."],"answer":"expulsion of the kings","answerText":"expulsion of the kings","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">244 years of kings, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tarquinius Superbus, 25 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, chosen by the centuries</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>expulsion of the kings</b> is the rising that Roman tradition places at the end of Tarquinius Superbus's reign and treats as the beginning of the Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy has the men standing over Lucretia's body swear, on the knife drawn from her wound, that they would drive out the king and his house and suffer nobody else to reign at Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They carried her body down into the forum at Collatia, set a guard on the gates so that no word should reach the king, and marched under arms to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There Brutus, who happened to hold the office of tribune of the celeres, summoned the people and spoke of Sextus, of citizens set to clearing out ditches and sewers, and of the murder of Servius Tullius and the chariot driven over his body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crowd voted to strip the king of his sovereignty and to banish him with his wife and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius gives the same programme in constitutional form: a vote of banishment against Tarquin and his posterity for ever from the city and from Roman territory, then an interrex to nominate magistrates and the centuriate assembly to accept or refuse them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Spurius Lucretius, the dead woman's father, was made interrex, and Brutus and Collatinus were the men he named to exercise the power the kings had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their office was to be called <i>consules</i>, a word Dionysius explains to his Greek readers as meaning counsellors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tarquin, hearing what was happening, rode for the city and found the gates shut and the walls full of armed men, so turned back to a camp the consuls' letters had reached before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy closes the reign at 25 years and the whole age of kings at 244, and has the first two consuls elected in the assembly of centuries by the prefect of the city, on the regulations of Servius Tullius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.59, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.75, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#75. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.76, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#76. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.85, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#85. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Gavin_Hamilton_-_The_Death_of_Lucretia_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Gavin_Hamilton_-_The_Death_of_Lucretia_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"The oath over Lucretia's body","desc":"Gavin Hamilton, oil on canvas. Brutus holds up the knife drawn from the wound while the other three lay hands on it and swear, and Lucretia is supported at the left. Livy makes this oath the beginning of the rising.","credit":"Gavin Hamilton, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gavin_Hamilton_-_The_Death_of_Lucretia_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"Painting of four men in Roman dress reaching towards a raised dagger held by a bearded man in armour, a dying woman supported at the left of the group."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-079","num":79,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keep up the appearance of an idiot under his uncle's rule, and not even object to the nickname that the pretence earned him.","questions":["Sent to Delphi with the king's sons, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Apollo a rod of gold hidden inside a hollow staff of cornel wood, as an emblem of what he was.","Knowing him at a distance by his escort of lictors, Arruns Tarquinius rode at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and each drove his spear through the other's shield so that both fell dying."],"answer":"Lucius Junius Brutus","answerText":"Lucius Junius Brutus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">one of the first 2, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">annual, where the 7 kings ruled for life</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 1st war against the exiled king</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"<b>Lucius Junius Brutus</b> is the man Roman tradition credits with leading the rising against Tarquinius Superbus and with holding, alongside Collatinus, the first of the annual magistracies that replaced the kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy makes him the son of the king's sister, says his brother was killed on his uncle's orders, and has him take refuge in the appearance of an idiot rather than trust laws that gave him no protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius has the king put both his father and his elder brother to death for the family's inherited wealth, and derives the surname itself from the folly he put on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sent to Delphi in the company of the king's sons, more as their butt than their companion, he carried a rod of gold inside a hollow staff of cornel wood and gave it to Apollo as an emblem of himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oracle promised supreme power at Rome to whichever of them first kissed his mother, and Brutus, taking the words another way, pretended to stumble on landing and kissed the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He held the office of tribune of the celeres when Lucretia died, and it was he who drew the knife from the wound and put the oath to the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy dates the beginning of liberty from this time less because the consuls' power was smaller than a king's than because it was limited to a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As consul he had to preside over the punishment of his own sons for conspiring to bring the Tarquins back, and Livy notes that the office laid on a father the duty of seeing the sentence carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Arruns Tarquinius knew him at a distance by his escort of lictors and rode at him crying that here went the man who had driven them out, proud in their own insignia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each drove his spear through the other's shield, and both fell dying from their horses, so that Brutus did not outlive the first war of the Republic he had made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.59, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.5, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.68, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#68. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Capitoline_Brutus_Musei_Capitolini_MC1183.jpg/1920px-Capitoline_Brutus_Musei_Capitolini_MC1183.jpg","title":"The Capitoline Brutus","desc":"Bronze head with inlaid eyes, in the Capitoline Museums in Rome, set on a bust added long afterwards. It has been called Brutus since the 16th century, but nothing on it or in any ancient writer names the man, and the identification is a modern convention rather than evidence.","credit":"Photograph by Jastrow, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capitoline_Brutus_Musei_Capitolini_MC1183.jpg","alt":"Dark bronze portrait head of a bearded man with inlaid eyes, mounted on a draped bust, against a patterned yellow wall."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-080","num":80,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Because certain sacrifices had been the king's to perform in person, the Republic made a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to carry them out, under the chief pontiff so that he should be king in name only.","questions":["Dionysius puts in Brutus's mouth the proposal for a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that the royal name might be kept for form's sake by a man holding it for life and doing nothing else.","Exempt from every military and civil duty and barred from any other office, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was inaugurated like a flamen rather than elected like a magistrate."],"answer":"rex sacrorum","answerText":"rex sacrorum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at the end of the kings, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">to keep the royal name for form's sake</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 only, the public sacrifices</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["title","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>rex sacrorum</b> was the Roman priest who took over the sacrifices the kings had made in person, created when the monarchy ended so that the gods should lose nothing by the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says a king for sacrifices was appointed to supply the place of the kings in those duties, and that his office was made subordinate to the pontifex maximus lest he should be king in anything more than name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy adds his own view that the Romans went to unreasonable lengths in devising safeguards for their liberty, down to the smallest points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius puts the proposal in Brutus's mouth, arguing that the name of the kingly power, which had entered the state under favourable auguries, should be preserved for form's sake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The man was to hold the honour for life, exempt from all military and civil duties, and, like the king at Athens, to exercise this single function and no other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He was not elected as a magistrate was but inaugurated, as the flamens were, in the assembly of the curiae that the pontiffs called for the purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aulus Gellius preserves the rule in the old jurists' words, that the calate assembly was summoned for the inaugurating of a king or of flamens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was called to the meetings of the college of pontiffs on occasion, and his residence stood on the Sacred Way beside the chief pontiff's own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So the one thing of the monarchy that Rome kept was the title, fenced about on every side: no command, no other office, no part in affairs, and a superior in the college.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy's word for him is <i>rex sacrificulus</i> and Dionysius calls him the king of sacred rites, and <i>rex sacrorum</i> is the title that became usual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.74, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#74. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Pontifex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 939-42, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Pontifex.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Rex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 991-94, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Rex.html. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Noctes Atticae</i> 15.27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Gellius/15*.html#27. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Bucchero_rex_sacrorum_2.jpg/1920px-Bucchero_rex_sacrorum_2.jpg","title":"A bucchero cup scratched REX","desc":"Fragment of a bucchero vessel found at the Regia in the Roman Forum, end of the 6th century BCE, Museo Nazionale Romano at the Baths of Diocletian. The graffito names the owner as the rex, which the museum reads as either one of the last kings or the priest who took the king's rites over.","credit":"Photograph by Juliana Bastos Marques, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucchero_rex_sacrorum_2.jpg","alt":"Dark grey pottery fragment on a clear stand, with faint scratched letters running across its surface."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4864,41.8919],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-081","num":81,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Each of the thirty divisions of the Roman people carried one vote in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, settled by a majority of its own members, so that sixteen of them carried the whole.","questions":["Gellius keeps the old jurists' rule that where the vote is taken by kinds of men the assembly is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, summoned by a lictor where the centuries were summoned by a horn.","By the Punic Wars the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had become a form, with thirty lictors performing the ceremony in place of the thirty divisions that no longer came."],"answer":"comitia curiata","answerText":"comitia curiata","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Divisions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 curiae, 16 of them a majority</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Romulus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Superseded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the centuries, under the 6th king</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","concept"],"abstract":"The <b>comitia curiata</b> was the oldest of the Roman assemblies, in which citizens voted not as individuals but in the thirty curiae into which the people were divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy has Romulus make that division after the peace with the Sabines and fix the women's names to the curiae, though tradition did not remember whether the thirty were picked for age, for distinction, or by lot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gellius keeps the old jurists' definition, that where the vote is taken by kinds of men the assembly is curiate, and the detail that a lictor called it where a horn-blower called the centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each curia carried one collective vote, settled by a majority of its own members, so that sixteen curiae carried the assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was done when fifteen stood against fifteen is not recorded anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius says that in this assembly the citizen of smallest means had an equal vote with the greatest, and that the poor, being far more numerous, carried what they pleased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy makes the same point about the old order, which Servius replaced with a graduation that left nobody openly disfranchised while putting the decision with the wealthiest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The curiae kept business of their own: admission to a curia, the wills made before the assembly when it was called for sacred business, and the adrogation of a man into another family under the pontiffs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They also passed the <i>lex curiata de imperio</i>, a second meeting at which a magistrate already chosen was formally invested and given the right to hold assemblies himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the Punic Wars the thing was a form: the curiae no longer came, and thirty lictors performed the ceremony in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Noctes Atticae</i> 15.27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Gellius/15*.html#27. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Comitia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 329-42, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Comitia.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.20, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#20. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg/1920px-Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg","title":"Three lictors with their fasces","desc":"Stone relief from Iulia Concordia, National Museum of Concordia at Portogruaro. Lictors attended the magistrates, and it was a lictor who called the curiate assembly together, where a horn-blower called the centuries; by the late Republic thirty lictors were performing the assembly's business in place of the thirty curiae.","credit":"Photograph by Following Hadrian, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors,_National_Museum_of_Concordia,_Portogruaro,_Italy.jpg","alt":"Carved stone panel showing three men in togas walking in step, each carrying a bundled rod over his shoulder."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.485,41.8928],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-082","num":82,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Romulus created a hundred of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Livy says, either because the number was adequate or because there were only a hundred heads of houses to be found.","questions":["When Romulus died the hundred <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled alone, dividing into ten decuries and passing the insignia between them five days at a time for a year.","Brutus filled the thinned order with leading equestrians called conscripti, where the older members kept the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the house was afterwards addressed by both words together."],"answer":"patres","answerText":"patres","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 by Romulus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grew to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200, then 300 under the 5th king</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Refilled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to 300 by Brutus, with conscripti</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","title"],"abstract":"The <b>patres</b> were the hundred senators Roman tradition has Romulus create, the fathers whose descendants were called patricians and whose council grew into the Senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy is not sure why a hundred: either the number was adequate, he says, or there were only a hundred heads of houses who could be made senators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the kings the body was the king's own council, its members appointed by him and offering advice that he could follow or reject as he pleased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew with the city, to two hundred when the Sabine tribe was joined to the Ramnes and to three hundred when the third tribe came in, which Livy and Dionysius both place under Tarquinius Priscus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the death of Romulus it governed alone: the hundred divided into ten decuries, one man from each holding power five days at a time, with the insignia and the lictors passing between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That year without a king is where the interregnum got its name, and the name long outlasted the arrangement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tarquinius Superbus thinned the order by killing senators and filling no vacancy, so one of Brutus's first acts as consul was to bring it back to three hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He took the new men from the leading citizens of equestrian rank, and they were known as <i>conscripti</i> where the older members kept the name <i>patres</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From that came the way of addressing the whole house, <i>patres conscripti</i>, which is <i>patres et conscripti</i>, the fathers and the enrolled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy says the measure did wonders for harmony in the state and brought patricians and plebeians together, and the figure of three hundred then stood for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.17, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Senatus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1013-22, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Senatus.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Rex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 991-94, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Rex.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Togato_Barberini.jpg/1280px-Togato_Barberini.jpg","title":"A Roman carrying his ancestors","desc":"The Togatus Barberini, a marble statue of a man in a toga holding a portrait head of an ancestor in each hand. Carrying the likenesses of one's forebears was how a Roman family displayed its standing, and it is the same word for father that gave the senators their name.","credit":"Photograph by Carlo Dell'Orto, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Togato_Barberini.jpg","alt":"Marble statue of a standing man in a heavy toga, holding a carved portrait head at waist height in each hand."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4854,41.8929],"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-083","num":83,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Livy says Romulus made a hundred senators called <i>patres</i>, and that their descendants were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the hereditary order that held Rome's priesthoods and auspices.","questions":["When tribunes demanded plebeian consuls, the consuls answered that only <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could take the auspices, since Rome's dealings with the gods belonged to that one order.","So rarely was an outsider admitted by a curiate law that fewer than fifty families of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survived by the end of the Republic, and Caesar had to make more."],"answer":"patricians","answerText":"patricians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traced to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Romulus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Houses added</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Tullus Hostilius, c. 673 – 642 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Families left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">fewer than 50 by the end of the Republic</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>patricians</b> were Rome's hereditary aristocracy, the families descended from the <i>patres</i>, and they alone could take the auspices by which the state asked the gods whether to act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dionysius says Romulus separated the men eminent for birth, approved for virtue and wealthy for those times from the obscure, the lowly and the poor, calling the first fathers and the second plebeians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers disagreed about the name: some took <i>patres</i> from their being fathers of families, some from their age, some from their sharing out land with the poorer citizens, and most from the protection they gave the whole state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A hostile version held that only these men could name their fathers, and pointed to the heralds who summoned patricians by their own names and their fathers' while ox horns called the plebeians together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius rejects it, calling the herald a mark of honour and the horn one of speed, since a crowd cannot be named man by man in a short time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The order was built from the clans of three tribes, the Ramnes, the Tities and the Luceres, the last of them long unequal to the others and long called the fathers of the lesser clans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A king could enrol new houses: when Alba Longa fell, Tullus Hostilius brought the Tullii, the Servilii, the Quinctii, the Geganii, the Curiatii and the Cloelii into the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius has Romulus give this order the priesthoods, the magistracies and the courts while the plebeians farmed, herded and traded, and let every plebeian choose a patrician to protect him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Birth alone made a patrician and poverty could not unmake one; the only way out was adoption into a plebeian house, which meant leaving one's clan and its rites behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A curiate law could raise an outsider but seldom did, so fewer than fifty such families were left by the end of the Republic, and Caesar and then Augustus made new ones to fill the old priesthoods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.8, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2A*.html#8. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.9, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2A*.html#9. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Patricii,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 875-77, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Patricii.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Fragment_from_the_Ara_Pacis_%28Altar_of_Peace%29%2C_between_13_and_9_BC%2C_discovered_on_the_Campus_Martius_in_Rome_in_1568%2C_RomeMoi%2C_Auguste%2C_Empereur_de_Rome_exhibition%2C_Grand_Palais%2C_Paris_-_14648840744.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Leading families in procession on the Ara Pacis","desc":"A fragment of the processional frieze of the Ara Pacis, carved between 13 and 9 BCE and found on the Campus Martius in 1568. The line is made up of members of the ruling house with priests, magistrates and senators, the children among them in the toga.","credit":"Photo by Following Hadrian, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A weathered marble relief of several adults in heavy draped togas walking in profile, with two children at knee height among them."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":9,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-084","num":84,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Shut out of the curiae, the old clans and every office, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were Rome's commons, citizens who fought in the army but could not marry a patrician.","questions":["When Alba fell its leading houses were made patrician and the rest were settled on the Caelian, which is where Rome's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first appear as an order of their own.","Ancus Marcius moved thousands of conquered Latins to Rome and gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Aventine to live on, after taking Politorium, Tellenae, Ficana and Medullia."],"answer":"plebeians","answerText":"plebeians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Distinct order</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from Tullus Hostilius, c. 673 – 642 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aventine given</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Ancus Marcius, c. 642 – 617 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Organised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 tribes under Servius Tullius, c. 578 – 535 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>plebeians</b> were the commons of Rome, citizens but not citizens in full right, and they stood outside the curiae, the old clans, the Senate and every civil and priestly office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their name carries the root of <i>impleo</i> and <i>compleo</i>, the filling of a space, and their citizenship was so thin that they are sometimes called <i>peregrini</i>, foreigners tolerated on condition of service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy and Dionysius put them at Rome from the beginning, among the crowd of free men and slaves who came to the asylum Romulus opened between the two groves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They appear as an order of their own under Tullus Hostilius: when Alba Longa was destroyed its leading houses were enrolled among the patricians and the rest were settled on the Caelian in submission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancus Marcius then took Politorium, Tellenae, Ficana and Medullia and moved many thousands of Latins to Rome, assigning them the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> They were Latins, of the same stock as the Ramnes, and they far outnumbered the patricians, yet they belonged to none of the three tribes, none of the thirty curiae and none of the patrician clans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They fought in the army all the same, and had no right of marriage with a patrician, no appeal against a sentence, and no share of the public land beyond pasturing cattle on it for a fee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they did keep was their own rites, regulated by the patrician pontiffs, their own clans, and a father's power over his children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tarquinius Priscus meant to make new tribes of their leading families and name them after himself, but the augur Attus Navius held that nothing could be changed without the birds' consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Servius Tullius gave them an order of their own instead, four city districts and 26 in the country, each under a tribune with its own rites and assemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Plebes or Plebs, Plebeii,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 923-27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebs.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.9, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2A*.html#9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/View_of_the_Caelian_Hill%2C_Rome%2C_with_the_church_of_Santi_Giovanni_e_Paolo%2C_seen_from_the_Aventine%2C_Rome_MET_DP873705.jpg/1920px-View_of_the_Caelian_Hill%2C_Rome%2C_with_the_church_of_Santi_Giovanni_e_Paolo%2C_seen_from_the_Aventine%2C_Rome_MET_DP873705.jpg","title":"The Caelian seen from the Aventine","desc":"A wash drawing of about 1750 by Joseph Vernet, looking across to the Caelian hill from the Aventine. Tradition settled the Albans on the first of those hills and the Latins Ancus Marcius brought to Rome on the second.","credit":"Joseph Vernet, c. 1750, Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"An ink and grey-wash drawing of low tiled roofs in the foreground and a further hill beyond, crowned by a bell tower and a long arcaded church, with open country in the distance."},"locator":{"name":"Aventine Hill","at":[12.4833,41.8833],"zoom":9,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-085","num":85,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Every Roman <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had its own rites, a shrine to keep them in, and a name its members shared, ending in <i>-ius</i> for a man and <i>-ia</i> for a woman.","questions":["By the Twelve Tables the property of a man who died intestate with no agnates passed to his <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the clan of families that bore his name and kept its rites together.","Adrogation took a man out of his <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and freed him of its rites, a renunciation made before the curiate assembly and known as the forswearing of the rites."],"answer":"gens","answerText":"gens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traced to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Romulus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Divisions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 tribes, 30 curiae, 300 clans</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alban houses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 taken in under Tullus Hostilius, c. 673 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","concept"],"abstract":"A <b>gens</b> was a Roman clan, a body of households that shared one name, one set of rites and a claim on one another's property, and the word holds the same element as the verb <i>gigno</i>, 'to beget', and means kin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scaevola the pontiff defined its members as those who bore the same name, were born free, had no slave among their forebears and had suffered no loss of civil standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Festus says a gens is made up of many households and Cincius that his own are the men called by his name; the shared name generally ended in <i>-ius</i> for a man and <i>-ia</i> for a woman, as in Julia and Cornelia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each had rites of its own, performed at set times at its own small shrine, and the pontiffs saw to it that they were not allowed to lapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They bound every member, so a man adopted, adrogated or set free from his father's power lost his clan and its rites together, the adrogated renouncing his before the curiate assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By the Twelve Tables the household of a man who died intestate and without agnates passed to his clansmen, as did the care of a madman left with no guardian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius has Romulus divide the people into three tribes, each tribe into ten curiae, and each curia into ten parts again, with the land split into thirty shares to match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> If those smallest parts are the clans, the arrangement gives a hundred to a tribe and three hundred in all, which is the figure the sources use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> To have one was long to be a patrician, and the kings took whole clans in: Tullus Hostilius enrolled six Alban houses, and the Claudii came from Sabine country under Attius Clausus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the law allowing the two orders to marry was put forward, the patricians complained that it would throw the rights of the houses into confusion, and some clans did come to hold plebeian families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Gens,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 567-70, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Gens.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.7, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2A*.html#7. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Patricii,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 875-77, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Patricii.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Sarcophagus_of_Lucius_Cornelius_Scipio_Barbatus%2C_280_BC%2C_General_of_Third_Samnite_War_%28Consul_in_298_BC%29_%2848465463042%29.jpg/1920px-Sarcophagus_of_Lucius_Cornelius_Scipio_Barbatus%2C_280_BC%2C_General_of_Third_Samnite_War_%28Consul_in_298_BC%29_%2848465463042%29.jpg","title":"The sarcophagus of Scipio Barbatus","desc":"The stone coffin of Lucius Cornelius Scipio Barbatus, consul in 298 BCE, now in the Vatican Museums, with two further inscribed slabs from the same tomb hung above it. The painted inscription opens with his name, and it is the clan name that comes first.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A large grey stone chest with a scrolled lid and a band of carved rosettes, its lower face cut with lines of Latin lettering picked out in red, standing against a dark red museum wall below two inscribed stone panels."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-086","num":86,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"The third of a Roman's names, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marked his household within the clan and came from a peculiarity of body or mind, or from something an ancestor had done.","questions":["Naso, Caecus, Cato, Capito, Brutus and Cicero are each a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the hereditary third name that told one household of a clan from another.","A Roman was known by a given name, a clan name and a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a fourth might be added for a victory, as Africanus was to Scipio."],"answer":"cognomen","answerText":"cognomen","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Order</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 names, with a 4th for an honour</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named on</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th day for a boy, 8th for a girl</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lucumo renamed under Ancus, c. 642 – 617 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","history","italy","iron age","concept"],"abstract":"A <b>cognomen</b> was the third of a Roman's names, marking the household he belonged to inside his clan, where the first name was his own and the second his clan's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That first name was one of only about thirty Varro counted in use, so it did little to tell one man from another and the two names after it did the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In early times a boy was given his when he came of age, an age Gellius reports the Romans counted as running to seventeen, and later custom moved the naming to the ninth day after birth, the eighth for a girl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The clan name generally ended in <i>-ius</i>, and a woman bore only its feminine form, so every daughter of the Cornelii was Cornelia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two names were enough to designate a man and were all that many plebeians had, among them Gaius Marius, Quintus Sertorius and Gnaeus Pompeius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The third name came from a peculiarity of body or mind, or from some deed of the man reckoned the household's founder, and Naso, Caecus, Capito, Cato, Brutus, Scipio and Cicero are all of that kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most were hereditary and ran on to the last members of a household, though a few ended with the man they had been given to for a particular reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fourth name was an honour for something done — Africanus, Asiaticus, Macedonicus — voted sometimes by the people in assembly and sometimes simply taken by the man himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The order was fixed at given name, clan name, household name and honour, and to take a clan or household name that was not yours was a forgery at law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An outsider took the whole apparatus on: the Etruscan Lucumo entered Rome and gave his name as Lucius Tarquinius Priscus, and the Sabine Attius Clausus was known there as Appius Claudius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Nomen,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 800-803, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Nomen.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Gens,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 567-70, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Gens.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.16, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 10.28, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/10*.html#28. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Funerary_inscription%2C_Epitaph_of_Quintus_Iulius_Quinti_%28CIL_08%2C_03151%29%2C_soldier_of_the_Third_Legion_Augusta%2C_lived_28_years._Inscription_from_Lambaesis_%28Numidia%29%2C_Lambaesis_Museum%2C_Algeria.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"A Roman name cut in full on a gravestone","desc":"The tombstone of a soldier of the Third Legion Augusta, from Lambaesis in Numidia. The second line reads Q · IVLIVS · Q · F · POL and the third begins FORTVNATVS: the given name, the clan name, his father's name, his voting tribe and last the household name.","credit":"Photo by Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A tall sandstone slab standing in a garden, carved at the top with the bust of a bearded man in profile and below it seven lines of Latin capitals."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-087","num":87,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"The augurs defined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a strip running the whole circuit of the city, marked by boundary stones, inside which alone the auspices of the city could be taken.","questions":["Ploughed with a bull and a cow and lifted where the gates were to stand, the furrow of a new town left the line called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, within which the auspices were taken.","Only a man who had taken land from an enemy might enlarge the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and even so the Aventine was left outside it until Claudius brought it in."],"answer":"pomerium","answerText":"pomerium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First line</span><span class=\"dt-v\">round the Palatine, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Extended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Servius Tullius, c. 578 – 535 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aventine in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Claudius, 49 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>pomerium</b> was Rome's sacred boundary, a line marked by stones set at intervals that fixed the limit within which the auspices of the city could be taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The augurs' own books on the auspices define it as the space designated along the whole circuit of the city, outside the walls and marked off by fixed bounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name joins <i>post</i> and <i>murus</i>, behind the wall, though Livy would rather call it a going-round of the wall, since the strip lay on both sides and neither could be said to be behind the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A town was founded by yoking a bull and a cow to a plough and drawing a furrow around the site with the clods all falling inward, the little mound they made standing for the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At three points the plough was carefully lifted and carried a few feet, and those breaks marked where the gates would stand, three being what Etruscan ritual required of any new town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The strip itself might be neither built on nor ploughed, and as the city grew the boundary stones were carried forward to keep pace with the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gellius says the oldest line ran round the foot of the Palatine, while Tacitus describes a wider circuit from the Forum Boarium round the hill and up to the shrine of the Lares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Servius Tullius took in the Quirinal, the Viminal and the Esquiline, having first consulted the auspices as the law required, and Dionysius calls him the last king to widen the city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The right to move the line belonged only to a man who had enlarged Roman territory at an enemy's expense, and that was the ground Sulla claimed when he asked for the honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Aventine stayed outside it for centuries, Messala holding that Remus had taken his auspices there and found the birds against him, until Claudius at last brought the hill within.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 13.14, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/13*.html#14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Pomoerium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 936-37, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Pomoerium.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Pomerium,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 392-96, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Pomerium.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.13, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#13. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Cippus_of_the_pomerium_Claudius_Musei_Vaticani_inv.9268.jpg/1920px-Cippus_of_the_pomerium_Claudius_Musei_Vaticani_inv.9268.jpg","title":"A boundary stone of the sacred line","desc":"A marker of the pomerium set up under Claudius, now in the Vatican Museums. The last lines record that, the boundaries of the Roman people having been enlarged, he widened the line and marked it out afresh.","credit":"Photo by Marie-Lan Nguyen, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A thick, pitted limestone slab with a shouldered top, cut with nine lines of worn Latin capitals, standing against a grey wall."},"locator":{"name":"pomerium","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-088","num":88,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Rome's civic centre, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as a marshy valley between the Palatine and the Capitol, with a burial ground on its edge, and had to be drained before it could be built on.","questions":["Tarquinius Priscus drained the low valleys into the Tiber and divided the ground round the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into building plots, where arcades and rows of shops went up.","Games, funerals, courts and assemblies all took place in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a levelled oblong of about seven <i>iugera</i> ringed by shops, temples and porticoes."],"answer":"Forum Romanum","answerText":"Forum Romanum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Valley</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a marsh below the Palatine, before c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drained</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Tarquinius Priscus, c. 616 – 579 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Extent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 iugera, on Varro's figure</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","history","italy","iron age","building"],"abstract":"The <b>Forum Romanum</b> was Rome's market place and afterwards its civic centre, the low ground between the Palatine and the Capitol where the city came to do its business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The valley began as a marsh crossed by a stream, which served as one of the defences of the Palatine settlement and kept it apart from the Capitol and the Quirinal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Geology bears the swamp out, and Ovid remembered it, writing that wet marshes held the ground where the fora now stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the edge of the valley lay a burial ground whose latest graves come down to the end of the 7th century or the middle of the 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has the Sabines drive the Romans in flight across the whole of the ground the Forum would later occupy, which is the oldest thing his account says of the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Tarquinius Priscus drained the low parts of the city round it by conduits emptying into the Tiber, and the graves went out of use at about the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same king divided the ground about the Forum into building plots, and arcades and rows of shops were put up along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One row, the old shops, stood on the south side facing away from the sun, and a newer row was later set opposite them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The finished thing was a levelled oblong of about seven <i>iugera</i>, ringed by houses, temples, basilicas and porticoes, and it served both as a market and as a place of judgement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Games were held there at festivals and funerals from the earliest times and justice was administered there, so that it became the place people came for business, then for politics, and at last for idleness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Forum Romanum,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 230-36, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Forum_Romanum.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.38, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Forum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 546-48, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Forum.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Foro_Romano_visto_dai_Musei_Capitolini_Roma.jpg/1920px-Foro_Romano_visto_dai_Musei_Capitolini_Roma.jpg","title":"The Forum valley from the Capitoline","desc":"The low ground between the Capitol and the Palatine, seen from above. Almost everything standing here is Republican or later; what the card describes is the valley itself, which was a marsh before it was drained.","credit":"Photo by Wolfgang Moroder, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A wide sunlit view down into a long open archaeological site: a triple arch at the left, rows of broken columns and paving beyond, and a tall group of columns at the right, with churches and modern buildings around the edges."},"locator":{"name":"Forum Romanum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-089","num":89,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Under the black paving called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands a broken pillar whose inscription, the oldest Latin from Rome, seems to lay down rites for the king in the Comitium.","questions":["Festus says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marks a place of ill omen, meant for the death of Romulus though he was never buried there, while others name Faustulus or Hostilius.","Two pedestals beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would suit a pair of recumbent lions, and between them sits a block on which the black stone itself may once have stood."],"answer":"Lapis Niger","answerText":"Lapis Niger","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscription</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE, on the pillar beneath</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tufa column</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shrine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd half of the 4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","history","italy","iron age","building"],"abstract":"The <b>Lapis Niger</b> is a paving of black marble in the Comitium, the open inaugurated ground before the senate house where Rome came together for its curiate assemblies and its lawsuits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Festus explains the black stone in the Comitium as marking a place of ill omen, set apart for the death of Romulus, though he says it never came to be used for his burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other accounts put in the grave instead Faustulus, who reared him, or Hostilius, the grandfather of King Tullus Hostilius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius knew of a stone lion in the chief part of the Forum beside the platform of the orators, said to have been set over the body of Faustulus, who was killed trying to part the quarrelling brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elsewhere he says Hostilius was buried by the kings in that same chief part of the Forum and honoured with a monument and an inscription to his courage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The pavement is Taenarian marble, about 4 by 3 metres (13 by 10 feet), laid level with the Comitium's own floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath it stands a group of far older monuments: a four-sided inscribed pillar with its top broken away, a conical column of tufa, and a small shrine resting on two long bases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inscription is the oldest Latin writing known from Rome, and what survives of it appears to be a law about rites that the king, or those attending him, were to perform in the Comitium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How much of the pillar is missing is not known, so nothing fuller can be argued from it, and the lettering is placed at about 500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two pedestals of the shrine would suit a pair of recumbent lions, and the block set between them may be where the black stone itself once stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Sepulchrum Romuli,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 482-84, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Sepulcrum_Romuli.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.87, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html#87. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.1, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3A*.html#1. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Comitium,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 134-38, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Comitium.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Rostra,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 450-52, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Rostra.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Lapis_Niger_in_the_Roman_Forum_%28149201570%29.jpg","title":"The monuments under the black paving","desc":"An engraved view of the group uncovered below the Lapis Niger: the two long bases with their pedestals, the block set between them, the broken inscribed pillar, and the conical column of tufa at the right.","credit":"Christian Hülsen, public domain, via the National Library of Poland and Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A fine line engraving of a low platform of stone blocks seen from one corner, carrying two shaped pedestals with a block between them, a broken squared pillar, and a round tapering column standing on a separate base."},"locator":{"name":"Lapis Niger","at":[12.4847,41.8925],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-090","num":90,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Year by year the pontifex maximus set down what had happened, and that record, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ran from the beginning of the state until it was closed in 133 BCE.","questions":["Livy blames the obscurity of the earliest times on records few and scanty, and on the burning of the city, which destroyed the pontifical commentaries where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were kept.","Livy will not judge the truth of what is told of the city's beginnings, so for the kings we fall back on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and on writers five centuries later."],"answer":"annales maximi","answerText":"annales maximi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kept</span><span class=\"dt-v\">yearly by the pontifex maximus, to 133 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 4th century BCE, with the archives</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First historians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fabius and Cincius, 3rd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","italy","iron age","concept"],"abstract":"The <b>annales maximi</b> were the record of each year's events kept by the pontifex maximus, and they are the nearest thing Rome had to a contemporary account of its own early history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They ran from the beginning of the state down to the pontificate of Publius Mucius Scaevola in 133 BCE, and then the keeping of them stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost nothing else was set down at the time, and Livy says that written records are the only trustworthy memorials of events and that in those days they were few and scanty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What did exist, in the pontifical commentaries and in the public and private archives, nearly all perished when the city was burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy therefore treats what is told of the age before the city was built as fitter to adorn a poet's work than a historian's, and refuses to argue that it is either true or false.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The first Romans to write their own history were Quintus Fabius and Lucius Cincius, who lived through the Punic Wars, five centuries after the last king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both wrote in Greek, and Dionysius says each set out with great exactness the events he had been present at while touching only summarily on the early ones that followed the founding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius names the Roman authorities he himself built on — Cato, Fabius Maximus, Valerius Antias, Licinius Macer, the Aelii, the Gellii and the Calpurnii — and calls their works like the Greek annals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He came to Italy in the year Augustus ended the civil war, and spent 22 years learning the language and reading those writers before he began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the whole account of the kings reaches a reader through men writing long afterwards, out of a record that was thin to begin with and burnt besides.","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Pontifex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 939-42, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Pontifex.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i>, preface, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3Dpr. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.6, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1A*.html#6. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.7, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1A*.html#7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Livius_-_Ab_urbe_condita%2C_nel_anno_MCCCCLXXXXIII_adi_XI_del_mese_di_febraio_-_2399185_S.jpg/1920px-Livius_-_Ab_urbe_condita%2C_nel_anno_MCCCCLXXXXIII_adi_XI_del_mese_di_febraio_-_2399185_S.jpg","title":"Livy's first decade, printed at Venice in 1493","desc":"The end of Livy's preface at the left and the opening of his first book at the right, in an Italian translation printed at Venice in February 1493. The woodcut shows Aeneas received by King Latinus.","credit":"BEIC digital library, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A printed folio page in two columns of dense early Italian type, with decorated initial letters and a woodcut at the top right showing a group of robed figures meeting under arches."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4864,41.8919],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-091","num":91,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Rome drove out its last king and put in his place two magistrates elected each year, and so began the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lasted until the rule of the emperors.","questions":["Livy dates the liberty of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the moment the consul's power was limited to a single year, rather than from any weakening of what the kings had held.","Polybius could not say whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a monarchy, an aristocracy or a democracy, since the consuls, the senate and the people each made it look like one of the three."],"answer":"Roman Republic","answerText":"Roman Republic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traditionally 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 BCE, when Octavian became Augustus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 244 years, on Livy's count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 a year, each for one year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["era","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman Republic</b> was the government Rome set up when it drove out its last king and put annually appointed magistrates where he had been, the king having until then been the only magistrate and held all the power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy counts the whole of the regal government, from the founding of the city to its liberation, as 244 years, of which the last king reigned 25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two consuls were then chosen in the assembly of centuries, on the arrangement Servius Tullius had made, and the first of them were Lucius Junius Brutus and Lucius Tarquinius Collatinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy refers the origin of liberty to this moment less because the power of the kings was weakened than because the consul held it for one year only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first consuls kept all the old jurisdiction and the old insignia, but one of them alone carried the rods, so that the sight of two sets of them should not terrify the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Brutus had the people swear that they would suffer no man to reign at Rome, and he called an assembly to rehearse that oath when his colleague's name began to be spoken of with suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the expulsion onwards two consuls were appointed every year, and other magistracies were added as time went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They came to be divided into the greater and the lesser: the consuls, the praetors and the censors were elected in the assembly of centuries and held the greater auspices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius, writing when the state was at its height, said it was impossible even for a Roman to pronounce with certainty whether the whole system was monarchical, aristocratic or democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fix the eye on the power of the consuls and it looked like a monarchy, on the senate and it looked aristocratic, on the power of the mass of citizens and it was plainly a democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.11, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#11. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Magistratus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 723-25, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Magistratus.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Anonymous_Rome_Cr198-1.jpg/1920px-Anonymous_Rome_Cr198-1.jpg","title":"A Republican denarius with the city's name on it","desc":"An anonymous silver denarius struck at Rome: the helmeted head of Roma with the mark of value at the left, and on the other side the Dioscuri riding with ROMA cut into a tablet below them. It carries no ruler's name, only the state's.","credit":"Photo by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin against black: at the left a head in a winged helmet facing right, at the right two horsemen with spears galloping above a panel lettered ROMA."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Republic","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"kind":"region","label":[12.48,41.89],"area":[[-9.6,43.9],[-8,43.85],[-5,43.75],[-3.5,43.55],[-1.8,43.45],[-1.5,44.5],[-1.3,46.2],[-2.5,47.3],[-4.9,48.4],[-3,48.9],[-1.5,49.7],[0,49.65],[1.5,50.9],[2.5,51.25],[4.3,51.95],[5.9,51.8],[6.1,51],[6.6,50.4],[7.3,49.8],[7.9,49],[8.2,48.4],[7.6,47.6],[8.2,47.4],[9.4,47],[9.6,46.6],[10.4,46.4],[11.5,46.2],[12.4,46.4],[13.5,46.5],[14.2,46.1],[14.6,45.6],[15.8,45],[16.6,44.2],[17.7,43.4],[18.8,42.6],[19.5,41.9],[20.2,41.5],[20.7,41.8],[21.7,42],[22.6,42.1],[23.5,41.6],[24.6,41.5],[25.5,41.1],[26.2,40.6],[26.5,40.1],[27.5,40.8],[29,41.3],[31,41.4],[32.5,41.9],[34,42],[35.5,41.9],[36.5,41.3],[35.8,40.6],[34.5,40.6],[33,40.4],[32,40.3],[31.2,39.6],[30.6,38.8],[30.3,38],[30.8,37.2],[31.5,36.9],[33,36.3],[34.5,36.5],[35.6,36.7],[36.3,37.2],[37,37.2],[38,36.6],[38.6,35.8],[38,34.5],[36.5,33],[35.6,32.4],[35.2,32.5],[34.9,32],[34.3,31.2],[33.5,30],[33,28],[33.5,25],[33,23.9],[31.5,24],[29,26],[26.5,29],[25,31],[23.5,31.5],[22,31.2],[20.5,31.5],[19.5,30.8],[17.5,30.7],[15.5,31.5],[13.5,32.3],[11.8,32.6],[10.5,33.4],[9.5,34.3],[8.5,35.2],[7.5,35.6],[6.8,36.2],[6,36.9],[5.8,37.1],[3,37.6],[0,37.2],[-3,36.6],[-5.4,36.05],[-6.5,36.7],[-9.5,37]]}},
{"id":"rm-092","num":92,"category":"The early Republic","question":"All the king's powers passed to the two <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the fall of the monarchy, except the priestly ones, which were kept apart and given to a rex sacrorum.","questions":["Twelve lictors carrying the fasces walked in single file before a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whenever he appeared in public, though inside the city the axes were taken out of the bundles.","If one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died in office the other had to call the assembly of centuries to elect a replacement, who held powers in no way inferior to his own."],"answer":"consul","answerText":"consul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Instituted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on the fall of the kings, 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 men, for one year each</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 305 BCE; before that, praetores</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Year began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January, from 153 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["title","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"A <b>consul</b> was the highest magistrate of the Roman Republic, one of two who held the office together and laid it down again after a single year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is probably <i>con</i> with the root of <i>salio</i>, so that consuls are those who go together; in the earliest times the title was <i>praetores</i> instead, and <i>consules</i> came in only in 305 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the fall of the monarchy every power the king had held passed to them, except the one that had made him the state's high priest, which was kept apart and given to the rex sacrorum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They were elected in the assembly of centuries, presided over by a consul or a dictator, or by an interrex where neither was to be had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The day on which they entered office moved again and again before it settled on the first of January in 153 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Within the city the whole internal machinery of the state was under their charge: they brought embassies before the senate, consulted it on urgent matters and carried out its decrees in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They summoned the assemblies of the people and presided over them, introduced measures and saw that what the people had decreed was done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twelve lictors with the fasces went before a consul in a line one behind another, and he never appeared in public without them, the insignia of the office being those the kings had used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside the city and a thousand paces around it the axes were taken out of the bundles, a change credited to Valerius Publicola, who carried the law giving a citizen the right of appeal from a magistrate to the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> If a consul died or laid down office, his colleague had to call the assembly and have a successor chosen, and such a man held powers in no way inferior to those of the consul elected at the year's beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Consul,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 352-57, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Consul.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#12. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/M._Iunius_Brutus_Cr433-1.jpg/1920px-M._Iunius_Brutus_Cr433-1.jpg","title":"A consul walking between his lictors","desc":"A silver denarius struck at Rome in 54 BCE by the moneyer Marcus Junius Brutus. The head is Libertas; on the other face his ancestor, the first consul, walks between two lictors with an attendant going before them, and BRVTVS is cut below.","credit":"Photo by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Two faces of a silver coin against black: a woman's head in profile at the left, and at the right four small figures in procession above the letters BRVTVS."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-093","num":93,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Rome dated a year by naming its two consuls, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the official list of them, running from the expulsion of the kings to the death of Augustus.","questions":["Cut in four double panels on the west and south walls of the Regia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named the dictators and censors as well, with the triumphs on the pilasters beside them.","Livy warned that funeral speeches and lying inscriptions on family busts had falsified the record, so even a list like the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may credit a house with offices it never held."],"answer":"Fasti Consulares","answerText":"Fasti Consulares","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Covers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 BCE to the death of Augustus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on the Regia, in 4 double panels</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Each year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 consuls, and any suffect</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>Fasti Consulares</b> were the official register of the consuls of each year, kept among the public records, and they are the frame on which the chronology of the Republic hangs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <i>Fas</i> is divine law, and the days on which legal business could be done without impiety were the lawful days, so the books that marked them came to be called fasti themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word then widened, and in prose writers it is the technical term for the registers of consuls, dictators, censors and the other magistrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For nearly four and a half centuries that calendar was priestly knowledge, and a man who wanted to go to law had to ask the few who held it on what day he might bring his suit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then Gnaeus Flavius, a freedman's son and clerk to Appius Claudius, put up a calendar on whitened boards in the Forum with the lawful days marked, and made public the legal forms that had been shut in the pontiffs' closets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The great surviving list was cut into the Regia, in four double panels on its west and south sides, with the register of triumphs on the pilasters of the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It ran from the expulsion of the kings to the death of Augustus and gave the consuls, the dictators with their cavalry commanders, the censors with the purifications they closed, and the triumphs and ovations in order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fragments of the marble came to light in the Forum in 1547 and were set up on the Capitol, from which they are usually called the Fasti Capitolini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A list of this kind fixes a year by naming its two consuls, and where one of them died in office the man chosen in his place was entered with powers no less than his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy warns that the record was falsified all the same by funeral speeches and lying inscriptions on family busts, each house claiming for itself commands and offices it never held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Fasti,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 521-23, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Fasti.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Regia,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 440-43, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Regia.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.46, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D46. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Consul,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 352-57, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Consul.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.40, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D40. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Fasti_Consularii_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg/1920px-Fasti_Consularii_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg","title":"What is left of the consular list","desc":"The surviving fragments set into a wall of the Palazzo dei Conservatori in Rome. The small plaque at the foot says that it is not clear in what order the pieces should be put back.","credit":"Photo by José Luiz Bernardes Ribeiro, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A tall marble wall into which many irregular inscribed fragments have been set at intervals, most of the surface blank between them, with a dedicatory inscription above and a small lettered block on a pedestal below."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4864,41.8919],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-094","num":94,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Cicero defines <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the power without which no war can be waged and no army held together, and a tribune of the plebs never received it.","questions":["Consuls were elected in the assembly of centuries, but only the curiate assembly could confer <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the command without which they could not touch military affairs.","A man holding <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was called imperator, and under the Republic the title followed the name, so a proconsul styled himself Marcus Tullius Cicero Imperator."],"answer":"imperium","answerText":"imperium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conferred by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a curiate law of the 30 curiae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 consuls a year, and by praetors</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 509 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"<b>Imperium</b> was the power the Roman state gave a man to command its armies, and Cicero defines it as that without which military affairs cannot be administered, an army held together, or a war waged at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands against <i>potestas</i>: one may properly speak of a consul's potestas or of a consul's imperium, but a tribune of the plebs had potestas alone and never received the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Consuls were elected in the assembly of centuries, yet only the assembly of curies could confer the command, which Livy has Camillus call the assembly that confers the supreme command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The practice reached back to the kings, for Numa, Tullus Hostilius and Ancus Marcius are each said to have carried a curiate law about their own imperium once they had been chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without such a law a consul could not touch military affairs, and the senate once refused to recognise a Roman as a commander because he had not received it in due form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Republic the pure imperium could not be held or exercised inside the city, though it was sometimes granted for the single day of a triumph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belonged to no office of itself, so that a man might hold military command with no title but that of imperator, which is what receiving it made of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The title then came after the name, and a proconsul in Cilicia could properly style himself Marcus Tullius Cicero Imperator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the field the powers it carried were almost unchecked: a commander could make what demands he chose on the allies, levy and pick his soldiers, punish any man under him and spend whatever sum he decided from public funds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why the plebs pressed for one of the consulships, arguing that no just administration was possible while the executive power stayed in the other order's hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Imperium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 628-30, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Imperium.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.52, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D52. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#12. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Consul,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 352-57, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Consul.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.37, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D37. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/L._Cornelius_Sulla%2C_AR_denarius%2C_84-83_BC%2C_RRC_359-2.jpg/1920px-L._Cornelius_Sulla%2C_AR_denarius%2C_84-83_BC%2C_RRC_359-2.jpg","title":"A commander's own coin, naming the title","desc":"A silver denarius struck at a military mint travelling with Sulla's army in 84 or 83 BCE. The head is Venus with Cupid; the other face sets a jug and a lituus between two trophies, with IMPER above and ITERVM below — imperator for the second time.","credit":"Photo by Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Two faces of a silver coin: a woman's head in profile with a small winged figure beside her, and on the other side a jug and a curved staff between two armour trophies, with Latin lettering above and below."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-095","num":95,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Rods bound into a bundle with an axe in the middle, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were carried before the higher magistrates by lictors walking one behind another.","questions":["Publicola had the axes taken out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> inside Rome and lowered them before the assembly, so that the people saw their dignity was greater than a consul's.","When the second board of decemvirs each took twelve lictors, the 120 of them filled the Forum carrying <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the axes tied in, and Livy says they looked like ten kings."],"answer":"fasces","answerText":"fasces","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 lictors before a consul</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dictator</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 lictors; his second in command, 6</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Praetor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 lictors in the city, 6 outside</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Axes out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">inside Rome, from 509 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>fasces</b> were rods bound into a bundle with an axe set in the middle, the iron of it standing out from them, and they were carried by lictors before the higher magistrates of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome took them, and the office of lictor with them, from the Etruscans, and Livy traces the number twelve to the twelve cities of Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The lictors went before the magistrate one behind another, and the one nearest him, the <i>proximus lictor</i>, was the man he gave his orders to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was they who carried out the punishment of condemned citizens, and they who made passers-by give way, dismount and uncover the head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twelve went before each of the kings, and at the fall of the monarchy each consul had the twelve axes just as a king had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> To stop the people supposing that two kings had replaced one, the consuls settled that only one of them should be preceded by the axes and the other by rods alone, taking them in turn a month at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Valerius Publicola, who gave the people the right of appeal, then had the axes taken out altogether in Rome and left only one consul preceded by lictors, the other attended by a single servant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coming into the assembly he had the bundles lowered before it, and the crowd took the point at once: the dignity of the people stood higher than the consul's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Outside the city and at the head of an army each consul kept his axes and his own lictors; a dictator had them in Rome as well, with 24 lictors, and a praetor had two in the city and six beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second board of decemvirs appeared with twelve lictors apiece, the 120 of them filling the Forum with the axes tied in, which Livy says gave them the look of ten kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Fasces,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 520-21, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Fasces.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Lictor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 703-4, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Lictor.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 5.2, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/5A*.html#2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Osimo%2C_municipio%2C_rilievo_romano_con_corteo_di_fasci_littori%2C_I_secolo_ac-I_dc%2C_dal_foro_di_auximum.jpg/1920px-Osimo%2C_municipio%2C_rilievo_romano_con_corteo_di_fasci_littori%2C_I_secolo_ac-I_dc%2C_dal_foro_di_auximum.jpg","title":"A file of lictors with their bundles","desc":"A relief from the forum of Auximum, now in the town hall at Osimo, carved between the 1st century BCE and the 1st century CE. The togate figures walk one behind another with the bundles slanted across their shoulders.","credit":"Photo by Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A broken marble relief set on a round base: several men in heavy togas walk in single file, two of them carrying long bound bundles slanting up across the shoulder."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-096","num":96,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Two or more men of equal power held every ordinary Roman office under the rule of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each able to stop whatever his fellow was doing, and the dictator alone was exempt.","questions":["The consuls took the running of the office in alternate months because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave them identical rights, and neither could carry anything through against the other's refusal.","Latin called each consul the other's <i>collega</i> and their shared tenure a <i>collegium</i>, and from those words comes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the rule that boards of tribunes and quaestors followed too."],"answer":"collegiality","answerText":"collegiality","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 BCE, with the first consuls</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 a year, equal in power</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Term</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 year, then answerable to the people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dictator</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 man, 6 months, no colleague</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"<b>Collegiality</b> was the republican rule that a magistracy belonged to two or more men at once, each of them invested with the same powers as his fellows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its partner was annuality: a magistrate held office for a single year and then went back to being a private citizen, answerable for what he had done in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy dates the beginning of Roman liberty to the limiting of consular authority to one year rather than to any weakening of the powers the kings had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each of two consuls was the other's <i>collega</i> and their joint tenure a <i>collegium</i>, though the word properly described an association of at least three men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because their rights were equal neither could act over the other's objection, so the two could do nothing unless they agreed, and one consul might hear an appeal against his colleague's sentence or veto outright what he was doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> To keep the pair out of each other's way the business of the office was in practice carried by one of them at a time, the two changing over month by month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same shape ran down the ladder of office, and Roman writers speak of a college of tribunes of the plebs and a college of quaestors, so that a magistrate almost never stood alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the arrangement cost was unity of command, and Polybius, describing a consul who has taken the field, found his power over war and money so nearly unchecked that on that evidence alone the constitution might be called a monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The dictator was the deliberate exception, a single magistrate whom no colleague could obstruct, and the price of that freedom was a term of six months instead of twelve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Annuality proved the easier principle to bend, for Polybius knew a senate that could supersede a commander whose year had run out or keep him in the field, while a consul laying down office still answered to the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Consul,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 354, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Consul.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Collegium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 310-11, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Collegium.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Dictator,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 406, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Dictator.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#12. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.15, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Relief_curule_chair_Massimo.jpg/1920px-Relief_curule_chair_Massimo.jpg","title":"Funerary relief with a curule chair","desc":"Marble funerary relief from the Torre Gaia on the Via Casilina at Rome, 50 BCE to 50 CE, now in the Palazzo Massimo alle Terme. The folding curule chair in the centre was the seat of the higher magistrates, and a figure in the toga stands on either side of it.","credit":"Museo Nazionale Romano, Palazzo Massimo alle Terme; photograph via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_curule_chair_Massimo.jpg","alt":"A worn marble relief showing a folding X-framed chair in the centre with a draped figure standing on each side of it."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-097","num":97,"category":"The early Republic","question":"While his countrymen hacked down the Pons Sublicius behind him, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held its far end alone against the Etruscans and then went into the Tiber in full armour.","questions":["Two ancient accounts of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disagree about the ending, Livy having him swim the Tiber to safety and Polybius having him give up his life in it deliberately.","Rome answered by setting up a statue and giving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as much public land as he could drive a plough around in one day, though lameness ended his career."],"answer":"Horatius Cocles","answerText":"Horatius Cocles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508 BCE, in Porsena's war</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 bridge, with 2 companions</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rewarded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a statue, and land ploughed in 1 day</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Endings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2: Livy has him swim, Polybius drown</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"<b>Horatius Cocles</b> was the Roman who held the wooden bridge over the Tiber against an Etruscan army while his countrymen cut it down behind him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The story belongs to the war that followed the expulsion of the kings, when Lars Porsena of Clusium marched on the city and the Janiculum was taken by a sudden assault.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His surname was explained by a lost eye, struck out in an earlier battle, though Plutarch also passes on a story that his flattened nose made the crowd think of a Cyclops and get the word wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two men stood with him, Spurius Larcius and Titus Herminius, and he sent them back when only a little of the bridge was left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bridge was the Pons Sublicius, made of wood and fastened without metal of any kind, and it has been suggested that the iron was left out so that it could be pulled down when danger came from the Etruscan bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Livy has him pray to Father Tiberinus, leap into the river fully armed and swim across through the missiles, and calls the deed more famous than believable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius adds a spear driven through the buttock above the hip and a current split into eddies by the piles, and brings him ashore without losing his arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius tells it the other way, and has him plunge in once the bridge was cut and deliberately give up his life, counting his country's safety and his future fame worth more than the years he had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rewards are much the same in every version, a statue set up in the Comitium or the middle of the Forum and as much public land as he could plough round in a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius says that every man and woman gave him a day's food in a famine, and that his lameness afterwards kept him from the consulship and from any command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 5.23, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/5B*.html#23. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 5.24, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/5B*.html#24. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 5.25, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/5B*.html#25. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.55, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#55. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Publicola</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Publicola*.html#16. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Pons Sublicius,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 401-2, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Pons_Sublicius.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Le_Brun%2C_Charles_-_Horatius_Cocles_defending_the_Bridge_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Le_Brun%2C_Charles_-_Horatius_Cocles_defending_the_Bridge_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Horatius Cocles Defending the Bridge","desc":"Charles Le Brun painted this about 1642. It is an imagining of the story rather than evidence for it: the fighting is at the bridge head on the left, workmen break the timbers away on the right, a winged Victory carries a wreath, and the river god of the Tiber pours from an urn in the foreground.","credit":"Painting by Charles Le Brun, about 1642; via the Google Art Project and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Brun,_Charles_-_Horatius_Cocles_defending_the_Bridge_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"An oil painting of a battle at the head of a wooden bridge, with soldiers fighting on the left, workmen levering the bridge apart on the right, a winged figure above holding a laurel wreath, and a reclining river god pouring water from a jar below."},"locator":{"name":"Pons Sublicius","at":[12.4767,41.8844],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"}},
{"id":"rm-098","num":98,"category":"The early Republic","question":"The exiled Tarquins fled to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Clusium, whose Etruscan army shut Rome in until famine and a run of Roman heroics are said to have moved him to terms.","questions":["Tacitus writes of a temple that survived when the city was surrendered, which is one of two traces suggesting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually took Rome rather than withdrawing from it impressed.","Gaius Mucius held his right hand in an altar fire in front of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the hostage Cloelia swam the Tiber out of his camp, in the stories Rome told about the siege."],"answer":"Lars Porsena","answerText":"Lars Porsena","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War on Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 508 BCE, for the exiled Tarquins</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">hostages given, Veientine land returned</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tacitus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3.72, on a city that was surrendered</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pliny</span><span class=\"dt-v\">34.39, on a treaty banning iron arms</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"<b>Lars Porsena</b> was the Etruscan king of Clusium who made war on Rome soon after the kings were driven out, and whose war closes that story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exiled Tarquins came as suppliants, warning him not to let a new fashion for expelling kings go unpunished, and Livy says he judged an Etruscan on the Roman throne an honour to his nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The blockade brought corn to famine prices, and the senate bought the loyalty of the plebs by taking the salt trade into public hands and lifting the harbour dues and the war tax from the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome's answer in the tradition is a run of single deeds: Gaius Mucius crossed the river to kill him, struck down the secretary sitting beside him by mistake, and was seized.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Threatened with burning, he put his right hand into the altar fire and left it there, told the king that three hundred more had sworn the same oath, and took from the ruined hand the name Scaevola.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The terms that followed are not a victory: Rome gave hostages and handed territory back to Veii, and only then did the king take his men off the Janiculum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The hostage Cloelia swam the Tiber at the head of the other girls, was sent back to keep faith with the treaty, and was returned by the king with half the hostages of her choosing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy ends it handsomely, with the Tarquins abandoned, the hostages sent home and the Veientine land restored, and Plutarch leaves the camp stocked with provisions and an equestrian statue for Cloelia on the Sacred Way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Two survivals cut across that, the first being Tacitus, who calls the temple of Jupiter one that neither Porsenna when the city was surrendered nor the Gauls when they captured it could violate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second is Pliny, who quotes the treaty the king granted the Roman people after the expulsion of the kings, in which iron was to be used only for tilling the fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.9, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D9. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.15, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.72, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb (London: Macmillan, 1873), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0080%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 34.39, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D34%3Achapter%3D39. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Publicola</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Publicola*.html#19. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Mucius_Scaevola_vor_Porsenna_Rubens_van_Dyck.jpg/960px-Mucius_Scaevola_vor_Porsenna_Rubens_van_Dyck.jpg","title":"Mucius Scaevola before Porsenna","desc":"Painted in the workshop of Peter Paul Rubens about 1626, and attributed in part to Anthony van Dyck. The king sits enthroned at the left, Mucius holds his hand over the altar fire, and the secretary he killed by mistake lies in the foreground with the sword still in him. A 17th-century imagining of the scene, not a Roman one.","credit":"After Peter Paul Rubens, possibly with Anthony van Dyck, about 1626 to 1628; via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mucius_Scaevola_vor_Porsenna_Rubens_van_Dyck.jpg","alt":"An oil painting of a bearded king seated on a throne at the left, gesturing towards a young soldier who holds his right hand in the flames on a small altar, with a dead man in the foreground and armed onlookers behind."},"locator":{"name":"Clusium","at":[11.95,43.0167],"zoom":8}},
{"id":"rm-099","num":99,"category":"The early Republic","question":"The Latin army trying to put the Tarquins back was broken at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in Tusculan country, by a dictator who vowed a temple to Castor while the fighting was still going on.","questions":["Two horsemen of more than human size were said to have watered their horses in the Forum with news of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why the temple of Castor stands where it does.","Livy gives one consulship for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and reports that other authors give another, before admitting that the confusion of the lists makes any year impossible to fix."],"answer":"Battle of Lake Regillus","answerText":"Battle of Lake Regillus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 BCE, or 496 in some sources</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Lake Regillus, in Tusculan territory</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple vowed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to Castor, dedicated 484 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Anniversary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 July, with a ride of the knights</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>battle of Lake Regillus</b> was the fight in which Rome broke the army of the Latins trying to put the Tarquins back, somewhere in Tusculan territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has the dictator Aulus Postumius take the field with Titus Aebutius as his second in command, and says the Romans forced a battle on hearing the Tarquins were with the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was fought as a run of duels between commanders, and Livy says hardly a leader on either side left unwounded except the dictator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aged Tarquinius Superbus rode at Postumius and was carried off with a wound in the side, while Octavius Mamilius of Tusculum and Aebutius crippled each other in one shock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Titus Herminius, who had stood with Horatius on the bridge, ran Mamilius through and was killed by a javelin while stripping the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Postumius turned the day by ordering his bodyguard to treat any fleeing Roman as an enemy and making the cavalry dismount to fight in front of the standards, and vowed a temple to Castor as it went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius adds the part Rome remembered: two horsemen of more than human size fought at the head of the Roman line, appeared that afternoon in the Forum, watered their horses at the spring by Vesta’s temple, told the city it had won, and were gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The temple went up where they were seen, at the south-east corner of the Forum beside that spring, dedicated in 484 BCE by the dictator’s son and called Castor's temple although raised for both brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The victory was kept on the Ides of Quintilis with sacrifices and a ride of the men who held the public horse, crowned with olive, and the Postumii still put the spring and the twins on their coins four centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When it happened is not recoverable: Livy reports that some authors give the battle to a different consulship, and says outright that the confusion of the lists makes any year impossible to fix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.19, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.21, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.13, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6A*.html#13. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Aedes Castoris,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 102-5, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Aedes_Castoris.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Lacus Iuturnae,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 311-13, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Lacus_Juturnae.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Tommaso_Laureti_-_Battle_of_Lake_Regillus_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Tommaso_Laureti_-_Battle_of_Lake_Regillus_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"The Battle of Lake Regillus","desc":"Tommaso Laureti frescoed this between 1587 and 1594, and the photograph takes in the inscriptions and statues of the room below it. A late 16th-century imagining of the battle rather than any Roman record of it: the fight is all cavalry, with a winged Victory overhead.","credit":"Fresco by Tommaso Laureti, 1587 to 1594; via the Google Art Project and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tommaso_Laureti_-_Battle_of_Lake_Regillus_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"A wall fresco of a cavalry battle, horses rearing and falling across the whole width of it, with a winged figure carrying a shield in the sky above and carved plaques and statues set into the wall beneath."},"locator":{"name":"Lake Regillus","at":[12.6931,41.8403],"kind":"battle","zoom":9}},
{"id":"rm-100","num":100,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Mutual help in war and an equal share of the spoils were the terms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sworn between Rome and the Latin cities in the consulship of Spurius Cassius.","questions":["Cicero recalled a bronze column behind the Rostra, standing until shortly before his own day, on which the text of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been cut four centuries earlier.","The consul who gave his name to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was condemned for aiming at a throne a few years later, and Livy weighs two accounts of how he died."],"answer":"Foedus Cassianum","answerText":"Foedus Cassianum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sworn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">493 BCE, by Spurius Cassius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">with all the Latin cities</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Terms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">equal spoils; private suits in 10 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still readable</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on a bronze column, 4 centuries later</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["event","history","politics","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Foedus Cassianum</b> was the treaty that bound Rome and the cities of Latium into a defensive alliance, and it carries the name of Spurius Cassius, consul in the year it was sworn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cicero asks who does not know that a treaty was made with all the Latins in the consulship of Spurius Cassius and Postumus Cominius, the year conventionally counted as 493 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy sets it in the middle of the first secession of the plebs, and says one consul stayed in Rome to make it while the other went against the Volscians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius gives the reason for it: the Latins had not stirred during the sedition, had welcomed the return of the people, and had helped Rome against those who did revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also preserves the terms, which open by declaring peace between the Romans and all the cities of the Latins for as long as the heavens and the earth stay where they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Neither side was to make war on the other, bring in a foreign enemy, or grant a safe passage to anyone marching against the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each was to help the other with all its forces when attacked, and each was to take an equal share of the spoils of their common wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A private suit was to be settled within ten days and in the country where the contract had been made, and nothing could be added or taken away without the consent of Rome and of all the Latins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cicero remembered the text still cut on a bronze column behind the Rostra until shortly before his own day, four centuries after the oath was sworn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cassius was condemned for aiming at a throne after proposing to share conquered land with Latins and Hernici, and Livy weighs two accounts of his death, a private trial by his own father or a public one that ended with his house pulled down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.95, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6D*.html#95. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Pro Balbo</i> 53, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1891), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0020%3Atext%3DBalb.%3Asection%3D53. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Pro Balbo</i> 54, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1891), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0020%3Atext%3DBalb.%3Asection%3D54. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.41, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Ligue-latine-carte.png","title":"Latium and the cities of the Latin League","desc":"A modern map of Latium drawn by a Wikimedia contributor, with the Latin cities marked in orange and the neighbouring peoples named around them. Lake Regillus is shown east of the Alban Lake; the treaty itself, as Dionysius quotes it, names no cities at all.","credit":"Map by Cassius Ahenobarbus, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ligue-latine-carte.png","alt":"A relief map of the coast and hills south-east of the Tiber, with Roma and the other Latin towns marked as orange dots and the Etrusci, Sabini, Aequi, Hernici, Volsci and Aurunci lettered across the surrounding country."},"locator":{"name":"Latium","at":[12.7,41.73],"kind":"region","area":[[12.23,41.78],[12.45,41.92],[12.66,42.06],[12.82,41.98],[12.98,41.86],[13.12,41.68],[13.28,41.36],[13.1,41.23],[12.85,41.35],[12.63,41.44],[12.42,41.6],[12.28,41.72]]}},
{"id":"wh-223","num":223,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Founding a new capital at Akhetaten in his sixth year and worshipping the sun as a rayed disc, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke with the older gods and had Amun's name erased.","questions":["The Great Hymn to the Aten, cut into a courtier's tomb at Amarna, is the principal source for the beliefs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the king who came to the throne as Amenhotep IV.","Boundary stelae cut into the cliffs of Middle Egypt, written in Late Egyptian rather than the older formal language, record the new city that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dedicated to the sun-disc."],"answer":"Akhenaten","answerText":"Akhenaten","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1351 – 1334 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Highest year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Akhetaten</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1347 – 1332 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","religion","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Akhenaten</b> was a king of ancient Egypt's eighteenth dynasty, a son of Amenhotep III who came to the throne as Amenhotep IV and whose highest attested regnal year is 17.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In his sixth year he took the name Akhenaten, 'one who is akh for the Aten', and founded a new royal residence in Middle Egypt, midway between Memphis and Thebes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He called it Akhetaten, 'the horizon of the Aten', after the god he worshipped: the sun in its most visible form, a disc whose rays end in hands offering life to the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The names and images of Amun and his consort Mut were erased on accessible monuments throughout Egypt and Nubia, and royal ideology, art, architecture and the written language changed with the religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The city held perhaps 30,000 people and lasted about 15 years: Tutankhamun had left it for Memphis some two years after Akhenaten's death, and its temples and palaces were then demolished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The foundation is recorded on boundary stelae cut into the surrounding cliffs and dated to year 6, among the oldest statements surviving from the founding of any city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are also the first royal monuments written mainly in Late Egyptian, far closer to New Kingdom speech than the formal Middle Egyptian of earlier inscriptions, so their wording itself read as new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their text says the king is never to pass the city's limits, which earlier studies took for a vow to stay inside for life, though he is known to have travelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chief source for the new belief is the Great Hymn to the Aten, cut in the tomb of the courtier Ay, which praises the sun as creator but leaves every other god out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly 400 cuneiform tablets from the site carry the diplomatic correspondence of the period, passing between the great kings of Egypt, Mitanni, Hatti, Alashiya and Babylonia and the mayors of the Syro-Palestinian towns, and they are a key source for the 14th-century Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["\"Akhenaten (Amenhotep IV),\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/akhnaton.html. [Open access]","\"The Boundary Stelae of Akhenaten,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/boundary.html. [Open access]","\"Belief in One God in Ancient Egypt,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/belief.html. [Open access]","\"Amarna: The Cuneiform Tablets,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/cuneiform.html. [Open access]","\"About Amarna,\" The Amarna Project, https://www.amarnaproject.com/about-amarna/. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]","Cl&eacute;ment Dutrey, \"Distribution de l'information et strat&eacute;gies relationnelles dans le corpus de correspondances amarniennes : approche par l'analyse de r&eacute;seaux,\" <i>Journal of Historical Network Research</i> 6, no. 1 (2021), https://doi.org/10.25517/jhnr.v6i1.85. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Akhenaten%2C_Nefertiti_and_their_children.jpg/1920px-Akhenaten%2C_Nefertiti_and_their_children.jpg","title":"Akhenaten and Nefertiti beneath the Aten","desc":"House altar showing Akhenaten and Nefertiti with three of their daughters beneath the rayed disc of the Aten, 18th dynasty, about 1340 BCE. Gerbil, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Gerbil, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akhenaten,_Nefertiti_and_their_children.jpg","alt":"A limestone relief of a royal couple seated facing each other with small daughters on their laps and knees, the sun-disc above sending down long rays that end in tiny hands, some of them holding ankh signs."}},
{"id":"wh-224","num":224,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Coming to the throne as a boy and reigning roughly a decade, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put the gods of Egypt back after the Amarna period and set up restoration stelae at Karnak.","questions":["Tomb 62 in the Valley of the Kings is modest in size and decoration, but the roughly 5,000 objects packed into it belonged to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who died still in his teens.","Two years into the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the court left Akhetaten for Memphis, the cult of the Aten was abandoned, and the gods of Egypt were reinstalled."],"answer":"Tutankhamun","answerText":"Tutankhamun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1336 – 1327 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 9 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">KV62, Valley of the Kings</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","religion","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Tutankhamun</b> was a king of ancient Egypt's eighteenth dynasty who came to the throne as a boy under the throne name Nebkheperure and reigned roughly a decade, from about 1336 to 1327 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For Egyptian history his importance is the reinstallation of the gods after the Amarna period, recorded on restoration stelae set up at Karnak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court left Akhetaten for Memphis perhaps two years into the reign, and the cult of the Aten was abandoned utterly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under Tutankhamun the city Akhenaten had founded ceased to be the royal power-base, its monuments were dismantled by later rulers, and he was in time struck from the record himself for the connection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He died still in his teens of a cause that is not known, and was buried across the river from Thebes in the Valley of the Kings, where more than sixty New Kingdom tombs are cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> His tomb, number 62 in that valley, is modest beside the others in both size and decoration, most likely because he came to the throne so young and ruled so briefly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only the burial chamber's walls carry any decoration, and where other royal tombs are covered in funerary texts such as the Amduat, his shows a single scene from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rest of the painting gives the funeral or the king in the company of gods, and the small chamber meant the roughly 5,000 objects put in were stacked very tightly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those objects came from the life of the palace: clothes, jewellery, cosmetics, incense, furniture, toys, vessels of many materials, chariots and weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tomb's size has been argued over, since the larger one in which his successor Ay was buried may have been begun for him, and it has been suggested that KV62 already existed as a private tomb or a store and was enlarged to take the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"Tutankhamun,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/tutankhamun.html. [Open access]","\"The Boundary Stelae of Akhenaten,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/boundary.html. [Open access]","\"Tomb of Tutankhamun,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/tomb-of-tutankhamun. [Open access]","\"Valley of the Kings,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/valley-of-the-kings. [Open access]","\"About Amarna,\" The Amarna Project, https://www.amarnaproject.com/about-amarna/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Gold_Death_Mask_of_Tutankhamun.jpg/1920px-Gold_Death_Mask_of_Tutankhamun.jpg","title":"The gold mask of Tutankhamun","desc":"The gold death mask of Tutankhamun, from his burial in the Valley of the Kings. Thomas Clouet, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Thomas Clouet, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_Death_Mask_of_Tutankhamun.jpg","alt":"A gold funerary mask of a young king in a striped headcloth of gold and blue glass, a vulture and a cobra above the brow, a plaited false beard below the chin and a broad inlaid collar across the chest."}},
{"id":"wh-225","num":225,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Fighting the Hittites at Qadesh and then making peace with them in his twenty-first year, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a bigger mark on Egypt's monuments than any other king.","questions":["The Great Temple at Abu Simbel was cut into the rock of Nubia for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose four seated colossi dominate its façade and whose own statue sits among the gods inside.","Moving the capital to Piramesse in the eastern Delta and adding a courtyard and a pylon at Luxor, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left his name at almost every site in Egypt."],"answer":"Ramesses II","answerText":"Ramesses II","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1279 – 1213 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hittite treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">regnal year 21</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">KV7, Valley of the Kings</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","warfare","building","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Ramesses II</b> was a king of ancient Egypt's nineteenth dynasty, the son of Sety I, who reigned from about 1279 to 1213 BCE under the throne name Usermaatre-setepenre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the number and scale of his monuments and the length of his reign he is one of the most important rulers Egypt had, and he left a larger mark on its monumental landscape than any other king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His name has been found at almost every Egyptian site, he moved the capital to Piramesse, modern Qantir in the eastern Delta, and he was buried in tomb 7 in the Valley of the Kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His war against the Hittites produced the battle of Qadesh, which was recorded in a pictorial and written composition unparalleled in extent among Egyptian sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his twenty-first year he made a peace treaty with the Hittite king Hattusili III, and both the Egyptian and the Hittite versions of it survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Karnak the bulk of the Great Hypostyle Hall and all of its decoration belong to him and his father, a forest of 134 columns 15 metres (49 feet) high around a central twelve that stand 21 metres (69 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Luxor he added a peristyle courtyard and a massive pylon fronted by colossal statues and a pair of obelisks 25 metres (82 feet) high, one of which has stood in the Place de la Concorde in Paris since 1836.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around 1264 BCE he had the Great Temple of Abu Simbel cut into the living rock in Nubia, its façade dominated by four seated colossi, one of which an ancient earthquake brought down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four gods are seated in its sanctuary, Amun-Ra, Ra-Horakhty, Ptah and a deified Ramesses II, and on two days a year, 22 February and 22 October, the sun reaches them down the length of the hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second rock-cut temple beside it belongs to Hathor and to his great royal wife Nefertari, whose colossi on the façade are the same size as his own, which is very rare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"Ramesses II Usermaatre-setepenre,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/ramsesii.html. [Open access]","\"Abu Simbel,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/abu-simbel. [Open access]","\"Luxor Temple,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/luxor-temple. [Open access]","\"Karnak,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/karnak. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Templo_de_Rams%C3%A9s_II%2C_Abu_Simbel%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-02%2C_DD_03.jpg/1920px-Templo_de_Rams%C3%A9s_II%2C_Abu_Simbel%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-02%2C_DD_03.jpg","title":"The Great Temple of Abu Simbel","desc":"The rock-cut façade of the Great Temple at Abu Simbel, with the four seated colossi of Ramesses II. Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Templo_de_Rams%C3%A9s_II,_Abu_Simbel,_Egipto,_2022-04-02,_DD_03.jpg","alt":"A temple front cut straight into a sandstone cliff, four huge seated figures of a king flanking a low doorway, the second from the left broken off at the waist with its head and shoulders lying on the ground in front."}},
{"id":"wh-226","num":226,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Ramesses II's four divisions were strung out over 55 kilometres when the Hittite chariotry struck at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a fight both sides afterwards claimed to have won.","questions":["The Poem and the Bulletin, cut on temple walls across Egypt, record <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where an Egyptian army met the Hittites of Muwatalli II on the Orontes in 1274 BCE.","Egypt and Hatti met in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the fifth year of Ramesses II, and their war ran on for another sixteen years before the two kings made peace."],"answer":"Battle of Kadesh","answerText":"Battle of Kadesh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1274 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regnal year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, of Ramesses II</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Divisions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, named for gods</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">regnal year 21</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","history","egypt","warfare","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Kadesh</b> was fought in 1274 BCE between Ramesses II of Egypt and the Hittite king Muwatalli II at the city of Kadesh on the Orontes, in the fifth year of the Egyptian reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Egypt had been pressing into Syria and Palestine since the beginning of the eighteenth dynasty, a region of small city-states none of which could resist it, while farther north the Hittite empire blocked any advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Egyptian army marched in four divisions named for the gods Amun, Ra, Ptah and Seth, with the king at the head of the first of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the Hittite chariotry struck, those divisions were strung out over 55 kilometres (34 miles), elements of the Amun and Ra divisions were scattered, and at Kadesh itself the numerical advantage lay with the Hittites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ramesses came close to defeat there, though he was not beaten, and the war with the Hittites ran on until his twenty-first year, when he made a peace treaty with their king Hattusili III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> With Megiddo it is one of only two battles of the late Bronze Age that are relatively well known, and it is the best documented of them from the Egyptian side, Hittite sources for it being scarce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ramesses had it recorded in a pictorial and written composition unparalleled in extent among Egyptian sources, cut on the walls of his temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The written account survives in two forms usually called the Poem and the Bulletin, which date the army's setting out to year 5, second month of the Shemu season, day 9.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hieratic Papyrus of Pentaur carries an account of the battle in a manuscript written under Merenptah, a generation after it was fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both sides claimed the victory, and there is no impartial source against which either claim can be tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"Ramesses II Usermaatre-setepenre,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/ramsesii.html. [Open access]","\"Asia: Foreign Relations with Egypt in the New Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foreignrelations/asiank.html. [Open access]","Sheikha Obaid Al-Harbi, \"Military Arrangements for the Battle of Kadesh in 1274 BC During the Era of the New Kingdom,\" <i>Journal of the College of Education for Women</i> 33, no. 4 (2022): 12&ndash;28, https://doi.org/10.36231/coedw.v33i4.1627. [Open access]","Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric Servajean, <i>Quatre &eacute;tudes sur la bataille de Qadech</i>, CENiM 6 (Montpellier, 2012), http://www.enim-egyptologie.fr/cahiers/6/Servajean_CENIM6.pdf. [Open access]","Maxim V. Panov, \"The Battle of Kadesh: Paleographic Notes on the Papyrus of Pentauret,\" <i>Sovremennye Issledovaniya Sotsialnykh Problem</i> 11, no. 3 (2019): 65&ndash;84, http://journal-s.org/index.php/mrsp/article/view/12264. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Battle_scene_from_the_Great_Kadesh_reliefs_of_Ramses_II_on_the_Walls_of_the_Ramesseum_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Battle_scene_from_the_Great_Kadesh_reliefs_of_Ramses_II_on_the_Walls_of_the_Ramesseum_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"The Kadesh reliefs of the Ramesseum","desc":"A line drawing of the battle scene from the Great Kadesh reliefs on the walls of the Ramesseum, published in James Henry Breasted's History of Egypt, 1927. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"James Henry Breasted, History of Egypt (1927), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_scene_from_the_Great_Kadesh_reliefs_of_Ramses_II_on_the_Walls_of_the_Ramesseum_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A line drawing of a carved battle scene: a king towering in a chariot at the left drawing a bow, ranks of chariots along the top and bottom, a mass of overturned chariots and fallen men in the middle, and at the right a walled city ringed by water with rows of figures inside it."}},
{"id":"wh-227","num":227,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Cut into the rock of a desert valley under a pyramid-shaped peak, the tombs of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the kings of the New Kingdom and were nearly all robbed in antiquity.","questions":["Egyptians buried their dead on the west bank because the sun died there, and it is why the kings chose the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opposite Thebes for their own tombs.","Thutmose I is the first king confirmed to lie in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where royal tomb walls carry funerary texts such as the Amduat and the Book of Gates."],"answer":"Valley of the Kings","answerText":"Valley of the Kings","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th, 19th and 20th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 60, plus 20 unfinished</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Thutmose I, from c. 1504 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","history","egypt","religion","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>Valley of the Kings</b> is a desert valley on the west bank of the Nile opposite Thebes, modern Luxor, where the rulers of Egypt's eighteenth, nineteenth and twentieth dynasties were buried, along with some relatives and a few high-ranking commoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thebes was never the administrative capital, but from the New Kingdom onward it ranked first in religious terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The west bank was chosen because the sun god set, and so died, in the west in order to be reborn in the east, which is why Egyptian cemeteries generally lay on that side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The kings were laid under a pyramid-shaped peak rising from the cliffs, the pyramid being a symbol of rebirth and a natural one a sign of the divine, and the peak was sacred to Hathor as Mistress of the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Isolation was the other reason: having seen what became of the Old and Middle Kingdom pyramids, and knowing robbery happened even in their own day, they chose hidden underground tombs in a secluded valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first New Kingdom ruler confirmed to lie there is Thutmose I, the third king of the eighteenth dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ineni, the official in charge of cutting that tomb, recorded that he oversaw the excavation of the cliff-tomb of his king 'in privacy; none seeing, none hearing'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The burial chambers and the corridors approaching them were carved out of the rock, and the walls of most royal tombs carry funerary texts such as the Amduat and the Book of Gates, which helped the dead king reach the afterlife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The valley divides into an eastern and a western branch, the western holding only a handful of tombs, and there are over sixty in all with about twenty unfinished ones little more than pits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The workmen of the necropolis lived in a village at Deir el-Medina on the same west bank, where their own tombs include an architect's and a sculptor's, and most of the royal tombs were emptied in remote antiquity, Tutankhamun's excepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"Valley of the Kings,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/valley-of-the-kings. [Open access]","\"Thebes, Finds from the Valley of the Kings,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/thebes/tombs/kings.html. [Open access]","\"Tomb of Tutankhamun,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/tomb-of-tutankhamun. [Open access]","\"Thebes,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/thebes/index.html. [Open access]","C&eacute;dric Larcher et al., \"Deir el-M&eacute;dina (2022),\" <i>Bulletin arch&eacute;ologique des &Eacute;coles fran&ccedil;aises &agrave; l'&eacute;tranger</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.4000/baefe.8324. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Kir%C3%A1lyok_v%C3%B6lgye_%28keleti_v%C3%B6lgy_k%C3%B6z%C3%A9ps%C5%91_r%C3%A9sz%C3%A9nek_s%C3%ADrjai%29.jpg/1920px-Kir%C3%A1lyok_v%C3%B6lgye_%28keleti_v%C3%B6lgy_k%C3%B6z%C3%A9ps%C5%91_r%C3%A9sz%C3%A9nek_s%C3%ADrjai%29.jpg","title":"Tomb entrances in the East Valley","desc":"The central part of the East Valley, its tomb entrances labelled with their KV numbers by the photographer. Kingtut, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Kingtut at Hungarian Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kir%C3%A1lyok_v%C3%B6lgye_(keleti_v%C3%B6lgy_k%C3%B6z%C3%A9ps%C5%91_r%C3%A9sz%C3%A9nek_s%C3%ADrjai).jpg","alt":"A bare pale-limestone valley seen from above, with a dozen walled ramps and doorways cut into its slopes and floor, small groups of visitors on the paths between them, and yellow arrows and KV numbers added to the photograph to label each tomb."}},
{"id":"wh-228","num":228,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Called Ipet-sut, 'The Most Select of Places', the temples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> housed the cult of Amun and a priesthood whose wealth gave it great political power.","questions":["A forest of 134 columns stands in the Great Hypostyle Hall at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where nearly every king of the New Kingdom left some mark on the temple of Amun.","A processional way lined with sphinxes joined Luxor Temple to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and in the Opet Festival the gods travelled along it to visit Amenemopet."],"answer":"Karnak","answerText":"Karnak","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2112 BCE – 306 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Precincts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3: Amun, Mut and Montu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hypostyle Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">134 columns, up to 21 metres (69 feet)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Obelisk</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hatshepsut's, almost 30 metres (98 feet)</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","history","egypt","religion","building","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Karnak</b> is a complex of temples on the east bank at Thebes, modern Luxor, in Upper Egypt, called Ipet-sut, 'The Most Select of Places', by the Egyptians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cult of Amun of Thebes was conducted in its Great Temple of Amun, which was extremely wealthy and whose priesthood held great political power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into several precincts, the largest Amun's, with his wife Mut's to the south, Montu's to the north, and Akhenaten's temple to the Aten on the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line of kings who reunified Egypt after the First Intermediate Period came from Thebes, the city became one of the most important in the country, and Amun's importance rose with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the earliest evidence for his temple, in the reign of Intef II, to the Ptolemaic period it was lavished with royal attention, additions were still made under Rome, and nearly every New Kingdom king left his mark here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Like most Egyptian temples the Great Temple of Amun is set on an east-west axis, temples being models of the cosmos and that layout mirroring the sun god's path across the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Exceptionally it has a north-south axis as well, turning it towards Luxor Temple, home of a version of Amun particular to south Luxor, and a sphinx-lined processional way joined the two for the Opet Festival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Great Hypostyle Hall is a forest of 134 columns 15 metres (49 feet) high around a central twelve of 21 metres (69 feet), begun perhaps by Amenhotep III and carried through by Sety I and his son Ramesses II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That hall was conceived as a temple of millions of years in its own right, and in it Sety I became a divine being by associating himself with Amun, Ra and Atum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hatshepsut raised an obelisk of almost 30 metres (98 feet) nearby, Thutmose III added a hall at the eastern end for various gods and his own cult, and the sacred lake where priests purified themselves is still there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["\"Karnak,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/karnak. [Open access]","\"Luxor Temple,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/luxor-temple. [Open access]","Hanane Gaber, \"Formes et m&eacute;canisme de divinisation de S&eacute;thi Ier dans la grande salle hypostyle du temple d'Amon &agrave; Karnak,\" <i>&Eacute;gypte Nilotique et M&eacute;diterran&eacute;enne</i> 19 (2026): 131&ndash;175, http://www.enim-egyptologie.fr/revue/2026/7/Gaber_ENiM19_p131-175.pdf. [Open access]","\"Thutmose III,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/thutmosisiii.html. [Open access]","\"Ramesses II Usermaatre-setepenre,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/ramsesii.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Luxor_Karnak-Tempel_2016-03-21_Gro%C3%9Fe_S%C3%A4ulenhalle_04.jpg/1920px-Luxor_Karnak-Tempel_2016-03-21_Gro%C3%9Fe_S%C3%A4ulenhalle_04.jpg","title":"The Great Hypostyle Hall at Karnak","desc":"Columns of the Great Hypostyle Hall in the Precinct of Amun-Re at Karnak. Djehouty, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Djehouty, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxor_Karnak-Tempel_2016-03-21_Gro%C3%9Fe_S%C3%A4ulenhalle_04.jpg","alt":"A view looking up between huge sandstone columns with spreading capitals, carved all over with hieroglyphs and still holding traces of paint, carrying stone beams against a pale sky."}},
{"id":"wh-229","num":229,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Egypt went upstream past the First Cataract into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for its gold and copper, and in the New Kingdom governed it through a viceroy of Kush.","questions":["Senusret III built forts at Semna to hold a border in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Kerma culture later ruled the lower stretch of it after Egypt lost control.","Thutmose I pushed the conquest of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as far as the Fourth Cataract, destroying the Kerman state and making the region an Egyptian province."],"answer":"Nubia","answerText":"Nubia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Contact</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2600 BCE, under Snefru</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lower Nubia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st to 2nd Cataract</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Conquered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to the 4th Cataract, under Thutmose I</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Viceroy of Kush</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 18th dynasty</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","history","egypt","geography","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Nubia</b> is the stretch of the Nile valley upstream of Egypt, its lower part running from the First to the Second Cataract and its upper part beyond, in modern southern Egypt and Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What drew Egypt to it was raw materials, gold and copper above all, with hard wood and ivory traded through the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Egyptians are attested there as early as the Old Kingdom at Buhen, officials' biographies of the sixth dynasty describe expeditions south, and a campaign is recorded under Snefru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the early Middle Kingdom fighting led to the conquest of Lower Nubia, Senusret I fortified Buhen, and Senusret III built more forts to hold a slightly more southerly border at Semna, leading several campaigns himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Upstream, south of the Second Cataract, lay the Kerma culture, which by its classical phase had formed a state centred on Kerma with large tombs and a massive building called the Deffufa, and which developed no writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Egypt lost control during the Second Intermediate Period, when Lower Nubia was ruled by the kings of Kerma and Nubians are well attested living in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Thutmose I the conquest reached the Fourth Cataract and the Kerman state was destroyed, and the whole province was placed under a 'viceroy of Kush' with his seat at Miam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That office was created at the start of the eighteenth dynasty, and its holder was among the most important officials of the New Kingdom because the gold mines were in his charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Local government was left to local leaders, who were brought to the Egyptian court while young to be given an Egyptian education, and Middle Kingdom forts such as Buhen were rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ramesses II raised many temples in Lower Nubia and the material culture of the province is fully Egyptian, but traffic ran both ways, and lead isotopes show some of the galena in kohl from Nubian graves was mined at Gebel el-Zeit on Egypt's Red Sea coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["\"Nubia in the Old Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/ok.html. [Open access]","\"Foreign Relations: Nubia in the Middle Kingdom and Second Intermediate Period,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/mk.html. [Open access]","\"Nubia in the New Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/nk.html. [Open access]","\"Nubia: Kerma,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/kerma.html. [Open access]","\"The Viceroy of Kush,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/viceroy.html. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]","Rennan Lemos et al., \"Bronze Age Supply Chains between Ancient Egypt and Nubia Revealed by Lead Isotope Analysis of Kohl Samples,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 27633, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-79162-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Western_Deffufa_-_Kerma.jpg/1920px-Western_Deffufa_-_Kerma.jpg","title":"The Western Deffufa at Kerma","desc":"The Western Deffufa, a large mud-brick temple in the ancient city of Kerma in Sudan. Walter Callens, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Walter Callens, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Deffufa_-_Kerma.jpg","alt":"A huge weathered block of mud brick standing several storeys high in flat desert, its sides eroded into rounded towers, with the low walls of a ruined town spread around its foot."}},
{"id":"wh-230","num":230,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Egypt spent the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divided, with kings at Tanis in the Delta and Upper Egypt governed in practice by the high priest of Amun at Thebes.","questions":["Libyan dynasties from Bubastis and Sais and then Kushite kings from Napata all ruled during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended with an Assyrian conquest.","Between the New Kingdom and the Late Period lies the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when for much of the time Egypt was divided into two or more separate parts."],"answer":"Third Intermediate Period","answerText":"Third Intermediate Period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1069 – 664 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21st to 25th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kushite rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from about 725 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Assyrian conquest, 664 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","egypt","monarchy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Third Intermediate Period</b> runs from about 1069 to 664 BCE, between the New Kingdom and the Late Period, and for much though not all of that time Egypt was divided into two or more parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ramesses XI, the last king of the New Kingdom, no longer ruled the whole country in effect by the end of his reign, and after his death a line of kings ruled from Tanis in the north-eastern Delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their authority was acknowledged everywhere, but Upper Egypt in practice governed itself under the high priest of Amun at Thebes, who held the highest military office as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians call the arrangement a theocracy, since the highest authority of all was the god Amun himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Around 945 BCE a military man of Libyan origin from Bubastis took direct control of the country as Sheshonq I, founding the twenty-second dynasty, campaigning in the Levant and building on a large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From the middle of that dynasty the country fell apart again in civil strife: after Osorkon II it was no longer united, and by the twenty-third many local rulers were independent, wearing royal regalia and taking royal titles of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Egyptologists use the name 'twenty-third dynasty' for different groups of kings, and it expresses the disintegration of the state rather than a single line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Meanwhile the Kushite kingdom centred on Napata, near the Fourth Cataract in what is now Sudan, was rising, and from about 725 BCE its kings invaded Egypt and ruled it as the twenty-fifth dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Piye is known from a stela at Napata reporting a campaign into Egypt, though whether that campaign annexed the country at once is uncertain, and the exact duration and nature of Kushite rule is still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Neo-Assyrian empire had by then reached Palestine, and though the Kushite kings held Egypt for a time the Assyrians prevailed, sacking Thebes under Assurbanipal and, when Tanutamani withdrew, installing the kings of Sais to rule Egypt for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Third Intermediate Period,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/third-intermediate-period. [Open access]","\"Egypt in the Third Intermediate Period (1069-664 BC),\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/3inter/index.html. [Open access]","\"Nubia: The Napatan Period,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/napatan.html. [Open access]","\"Kings of the Third Intermediate Period,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/3rdintermediateperkings.html. [Open access]","\"Late Period,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/late-period. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Psusennes.jpg/1280px-Psusennes.jpg","title":"The silver coffin of Psusennes I","desc":"The silver anthropoid coffin of Psusennes I, a king of the twenty-first dynasty who ruled from Tanis, in the Egyptian Museum, Cairo. Jerzy Strzelecki, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Jerzy Strzelecki, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Psusennes.jpg","alt":"The head and shoulders of a silver coffin lid shaped as a king, a gold band across the brow with a cobra at its edge, inlaid eyes, and a crook and flail laid across the chest."}},
{"id":"wh-231","num":231,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Spread from the Himalaya to the Arabian Sea, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built cities of wide gridded streets and covered drains, yet left no palaces, royal tombs or monuments to any ruler.","questions":["Standard weights, bricks in fixed proportions and chert blades carried 500 kilometres (300 miles) into Gujarat show how far the conventions of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached during its urban phase.","Divided into Early, Mature and Late phases, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held its cities for some seven hundred years before aridity and a weakening monsoon coincided with their contraction after 1900 BCE."],"answer":"Indus Valley Civilisation","answerText":"Indus Valley Civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Urban phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3: Early, Mature, Late</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, cores over 50 hectares (120 acres)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Contraction</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Indus Valley Civilisation</b>, also called the Harappan civilisation, was the Bronze Age urban society of the north-western Indian subcontinent, and its mature phase ran from about 2600 to 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its remains lie across what is now Pakistan and north-western India, over a range that eclipsed those of contemporary Mesopotamia and Egypt, reaching from the Himalaya to the Arabian Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeologists divide it into three phases, Early or Pre-Harappan, Mature or Urban, and Late or Post-Harappan, of which only the middle one is a civilisation of cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Five settlements are usually counted as cities, among them Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, Rakhigarhi and Dholavira, with urban cores often far larger than 50 hectares (120 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wide streets running north to south, crossed by narrower lanes running east to west, and drains laid beneath them are the signature of their planning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the excavations have not produced is as telling as what they have, for there are no palaces, no temples that shut anyone out, no rich tombs and no monuments raised to glorify an individual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shared standards of measurement and of recording appear instead, and long-distance exchange worked on the same scale: blades of Rohri chert were carried some 500 kilometres (300 miles) from Sindh into Gujarat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its craftspeople commanded a highly specialised pyrotechnology, and stretched scarce wood with dung and the leftovers of crop processing to keep their kilns and hearths burning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Households in Gujarat ate widely, and the fish bones from five sites there run to 24 species across fourteen families, cut, charred and chewed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Growing aridity and a weakening monsoon from about 2100 BCE, and a sharp hydroclimatic shift in the Himalaya around 4,200 years ago, coincide with the contraction of the cities after seven centuries of urban life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Charusmita Surendra Gadekar, Rajesh Sasidharan Vasantha and Abhayan Girija Sasidharan, “Early Harappan interaction between Sindh and Gujarat, as evidenced by lithic tools,” <i>Journal of Lithic Studies</i> 8, no. 1 (2021), https://doi.org/10.2218/jls.5161. [Open access]","Carla Lancelotti, Juan José García-Granero, Charusmita S. Gadekar, Marco Madella and P. Ajithprasad, “‘Not all that burns is wood’. A social perspective on fuel exploitation and use during the Indus urban period (2600-1900 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 3 (2018): e0192364, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192364. [Open access]","G. S. Abhayan, Pramod P. Joglekar, P. Ajithprasad and S. V. Rajesh, “Ichthyoarchaeological perspectives on roles of fish and red meat during Indus Civilization in Gujarat, India,” <i>Frontiers in Environmental Archaeology</i> 4 (2025): 1517351, https://doi.org/10.3389/fearc.2025.1517351. [Open access]","E. A. Niederman, D. F. Porinchu and B. S. Kotlia, “Hydroclimate change in the Garhwal Himalaya, India at 4200 yr BP coincident with the contraction of the Indus civilization,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 23082, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-02496-5. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","bronze age","india","pakistan"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Mohenjo-daro-2010.jpg/1280px-Mohenjo-daro-2010.jpg","title":"Excavated streets and house walls at Mohenjo-daro","desc":"Baked-brick walls, lanes and doorways of one of the largest Indus cities, in the Sindh province of Pakistan.","credit":"Photograph by Comrogues, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0","alt":"Ruins of a city of low baked-brick walls forming rooms and narrow straight lanes, under a pale sky, with green fields on the horizon."}},
{"id":"wh-232","num":232,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Standing on an old channel of the Ravi in Pakistani Punjab, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first Indus site to be found, and the civilisation is named after it.","questions":["Gated walls around every neighbourhood, weights beside one of the gateways and a great open clearing in the north-east are the marks of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an Indus city of many mounds rather than one.","Teeth from the urban-phase cemetery at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry isotopes suggesting that almost everyone buried there had come to the city as a child from somewhere else."],"answer":"Harappa","answerText":"Harappa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3700 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ravi phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3700 – 2800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kot Dijian phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2800 – 2600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Urban phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Harappa</b> is a city of the Indus Valley Civilisation, standing on the left bank of a former channel of the Ravi in the Sahiwal district of Punjab, in Pakistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The civilisation is named after it, this having been the first of its sites to be found, beside a modern village of the same name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of five major urban centres so far identified, with Mohenjo-daro, Ganweriwala, Rakhigarhi and Dholavira, each of them set in a wide landscape of villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first settlers chose one of the oldest soils in the region, close to fertile alluvium and raised enough to offer some protection from flooding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Different parts of the settlement were lived in at different times, so what survives is not one tell but a group of mounds, from which colonial brick-mining later took much of the architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Occupation runs from about 3700 to 1300 BCE, through a Ravi phase, a Kot Dijian phase from about 2800, and the urban Harappan phase of about 2600 to 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Massive gated walls enclosed each of the city's neighbourhoods, weights have been found beside one of those gateways, and a large clearing fills the north-eastern quadrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its households burnt dung as well as wood, and lived on barley and wheat grown in two seasons, the summer crops on the monsoon rains and the winter crops on what those rains left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isotopes in teeth from its urban-phase cemetery suggest that those buried there were almost all first-generation immigrants, so the graves record who came to the city rather than who was born in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After 1900 BCE the plan grew disordered and sanitation declined, and the skeletons of the post-urban burial ground carry more infection than those laid down before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Carla Lancelotti, Juan José García-Granero, Charusmita S. Gadekar, Marco Madella and P. Ajithprasad, “‘Not all that burns is wood’. A social perspective on fuel exploitation and use during the Indus urban period (2600-1900 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 3 (2018): e0192364, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192364. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Yong Jun Kim, Eun Jin Woo, Nilesh Jadhav, Pranjali Waghmare, Yogesh Yadav, Avradeep Munshi et al., “Archaeological and anthropological studies on the Harappan cemetery of Rakhigarhi, India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 2 (2018): e0192299, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192299. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Nathaniel James, Alexia Decaix, Isabell Villasana, J. Mark Kenoyer, Richard H. Meadow and Jade d’Alpoim Guedes, “Taphonomy and labour at the Indus Valley site of Harappa (3700–1300 BC),” <i>Antiquity</i> 99, no. 403 (2025): 83–100, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.196. [Open access]","Benjamin Valentine, George D. Kamenov, Jonathan Mark Kenoyer, Vasant Shinde, Veena Mushrif-Tripathy, Erik Otarola-Castillo and John Krigbaum, “Evidence for Patterns of Selective Urban Migration in the Greater Indus Valley (2600-1900 BC): A Lead and Strontium Isotope Mortuary Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 4 (2015): e0123103, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0123103. [Open access]","Gwen Robbins Schug, K. Elaine Blevins, Brett Cox and Kelsey Gray, “Infection, Disease, and Biosocial Processes at the End of the Indus Civilization,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e84814, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0084814. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","pakistan"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Harappa_Ruins_-_VI_-_Mounds.jpg/1280px-Harappa_Ruins_-_VI_-_Mounds.jpg","title":"Excavated brickwork on one of the mounds at Harappa","desc":"House foundations cut into a mound of the site, in the Sahiwal district of Punjab, Pakistan.","credit":"Photograph by Hassan Nasir, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0","alt":"A shallow excavation of straight brick foundation walls laid out in blocks, cut into a sandy mound, with flat green plain and trees on the horizon."}},
{"id":"wh-233","num":233,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Raised on brick platforms beside the Indus on Pakistan's Larkana Plain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drained its houses through pipes and soak pits and washed in a Great Bath on its western mound.","questions":["No palace, no exclusionary temple and no lavish burial has been found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Pillared Hall could hold hundreds of people and had no benches to seat any of them.","Measured as a spread of house sizes, the gap between rich and poor at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> narrowed over time, even as the Indus city grew more prosperous and its street plan took shape."],"answer":"Mohenjo-daro","answerText":"Mohenjo-daro","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Urban phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest houses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Mohenjo-daro</b> is one of the largest cities of the Indus Valley Civilisation, standing near the Indus River on Pakistan's Larkana Plain and built up through the urban phase of about 2600 to 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of five major urban centres of that civilisation so far identified, and what survives of it is a cluster of mounds rather than a single one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its neighbourhoods were raised on massive platforms, and the drainage and public buildings that served them took the labour of thousands of people to put up and to keep working.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water moved through wells, pipes, gutters and soak pits that carried waste away from bathing platforms inside individual houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the western mound stand its largest structures, among them the Great Bath and the building early excavators misnamed the Stupa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Hundreds of houses have been dug, many of them with bathing platforms, private wells and second storeys, and the brickwork was fine enough that much of the city still stands to be measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Pillared Hall ran more than 30 metres (100 feet) to a side on at least 20 brick pillars, and, having no benches at all, offered a bright enclosed space that could hold hundreds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nothing found in the city marks out a ruler, there being no palace, no temple that shuts anyone out, no lavish burial and no art that glorifies an individual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ritual imagery speaks of cosmic order rather than of any king, and the city is counted among the more collectively governed of early urban centres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether its largest houses belonged to an elite is still argued, and measured as a spread of house sizes the gap between rich and poor narrowed over the city's life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Adam S. Green, Iqtedar Alam and Cameron Petrie, “Inequality declined in the Bronze Age city of Mohenjo-daro,” <i>Antiquity</i> (2026): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2026.10359. [Open access]","Amy E. Thompson, Jessica Munson, Scott G. Ortman, Andrés G. Mejía Ramón, Gary M. Feinman, Gabriela Cervantes Quequezana, Pablo Cruz, Adam S. Green, Dan Lawrence and Paul Roscoe, “Assessing neighborhoods, wealth differentials, and perceived inequality in preindustrial societies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400699121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12036974/. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Yong Jun Kim, Eun Jin Woo, Nilesh Jadhav, Pranjali Waghmare, Yogesh Yadav, Avradeep Munshi et al., “Archaeological and anthropological studies on the Harappan cemetery of Rakhigarhi, India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 2 (2018): e0192299, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192299. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Gary M. Feinman, David Stasavage, David M. Carballo, Sarah B. Barber, Adam Green, Jacob Holland-Lulewicz, Dan Lawrence et al., “The distribution of power and inclusiveness across deep time,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 12 (2026): eaec1426, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12998527/. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","pakistan"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Great_Bath_of_Mohenjo-daro.jpg/1280px-Great_Bath_of_Mohenjo-daro.jpg","title":"The Great Bath on the western mound at Mohenjo-daro","desc":"The brick-lined tank, with steps down into it, among the standing architecture of the western mound.","credit":"Photograph by Soban, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0","alt":"A large rectangular brick-paved tank sunk below ground level, with wide brick steps leading down into it, surrounded by standing brick walls and a mound of further ruins beyond."}},
{"id":"wh-234","num":234,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Surviving mostly on small stone stamp seals, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs to no more than fourteen signs in a line and has resisted eighty years of claimed decipherments.","questions":["Because no bilingual text has been found and the language behind it is unknown, readings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still range from Indo-Aryan through Dravidian to a purely numerical system.","A seal carrying the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read from left to right, and the sealing stamped from it is read the other way, from right to left."],"answer":"Indus script","answerText":"Indus script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Indus script</b> is the undeciphered sign system of the Indus Valley Civilisation, and one of the shared marks, along with the weights, the pottery styles and the seals themselves, that make a site recognisably Indus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It survives mainly on stamp seals, and on pottery and other objects of stone, terracotta and copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Counts of the surviving texts run from about 3,000 to about 3,800, and a single line carries no more than 14 signs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Around 400 distinct signs have been identified, though one tally reaches 676.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A seal is read from left to right, and the sealing stamped from it the other way about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The seals are small stone stamps, mostly about 6 to 13 square centimetres (1 to 2 square inches), cut in intaglio with a line of signs above an animal, and the same standard forms recur seal after seal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The animals engraved on them include the unicorn, the buffalo, the rhinoceros, the elephant and the short-horned bull.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eighty years of claimed decipherments have failed, the texts being short, no bilingual having been found and the underlying language unknown, and readings still range from an Indo-Aryan language through a Dravidian one to a purely numerical system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Statistical models place the script's conditional entropy nearer to that of natural languages than to non-linguistic sign systems, and the signs that open a text differ from those that close it, which is internal evidence of syntax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has since been argued that no such method can settle whether the script is writing at all, and the same models fit texts found in Mesopotamia and West Asia far worse than ones from the Indus valley itself, which may mean the signs were put to a different use abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Nisha Yadav, Hrishikesh Joglekar, Rajesh P. N. Rao, Mayank N. Vahia, Ronojoy Adhikari and Iravatham Mahadevan, “Statistical Analysis of the Indus Script Using n-Grams,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 5, no. 3 (2010): e9506, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0009506. [Open access]","Rajesh P. N. Rao, Nisha Yadav, Mayank N. Vahia, Hrishikesh Joglekar, R. Adhikari and Iravatham Mahadevan, “A Markov model of the Indus script,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 33 (2009): 13685–90, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2721819/. [Open access]","Fabio Tamburini, “On automatic decipherment of lost ancient scripts relying on combinatorial optimisation and coupled simulated annealing,” <i>Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence</i> 8 (2025): 1581129, https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2025.1581129. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Yong Jun Kim, Eun Jin Woo, Nilesh Jadhav, Pranjali Waghmare, Yogesh Yadav, Avradeep Munshi et al., “Archaeological and anthropological studies on the Harappan cemetery of Rakhigarhi, India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 2 (2018): e0192299, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192299. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","writing","language","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Clevelandart_1973.160.jpg/1280px-Clevelandart_1973.160.jpg","title":"Steatite seal with a two-horned bull and an inscription","desc":"A square Indus stamp seal, 3.2 centimetres (1.3 inches) to a side, with a line of signs cut above the animal.","credit":"Cleveland Museum of Art, 1973.160, purchase from the J. H. Wade Fund; public domain","alt":"A small square grey stone seal photographed against a plain background, carved with a horned bull standing below a horizontal line of angular signs."}},
{"id":"wh-235","num":235,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Wide north-south streets, narrower east-west lanes and household drains running beneath them are the signature of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, repeated from city to city across a very large area.","questions":["Bricks cut two widths to the length and stone weights running from a gram to more than ten kilograms show how far <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried a single set of standards.","For all the drains, platforms and standard bricks of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most people in the civilisation are thought never to have lived in one of its cities at all."],"answer":"Indus urban planning","answerText":"Indus urban planning","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Indus urban planning</b> is the set of shared building conventions behind the cities of the Indus Valley Civilisation: well-organised streets, sanitation and water management, and a standardised system of measurement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wide streets ran north to south, crossed by narrower lanes running east to west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neighbourhoods stood on massive platforms, and the drainage and public buildings that served them took the labour of thousands of people to raise and to keep working.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Households ran a pipe from a bathing platform indoors to the drains laid under the lanes, and drew water from brick wells built to be watertight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bricks themselves were made to a fixed proportion, two widths to the length, which allowed header-and-stretcher bonding and durable joints, and the quality of that masonry is why so much of it is still standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That proportion holds across the cities, and production on such a scale needed coordination among many brickmakers; comparable brickmaking is not found in Mesopotamia until nearly a thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stone weights run from about 1 gram to well over 10 kilograms (0.04 to 22 pounds), cut from hard stones carried down from the highlands and standardised closely enough to work as one system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They carry no mark of whose system they belong to, unlike the competing marked weights of contemporary Mesopotamia, so a user never had to say which was meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They turn up at rural sites as well as in the cities, and weights reported beside the gateway into a Harappa neighbourhood may point to taxation, though what was taxed and by whom is unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over the life of a city its houses grew more uniform and sat more squarely on its street plan, though most people in the civilisation are thought never to have lived in a city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Gwen Robbins Schug, K. Elaine Blevins, Brett Cox and Kelsey Gray, “Infection, Disease, and Biosocial Processes at the End of the Indus Civilization,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e84814, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0084814. [Open access]","Amy E. Thompson, Jessica Munson, Scott G. Ortman, Andrés G. Mejía Ramón, Gary M. Feinman, Gabriela Cervantes Quequezana, Pablo Cruz, Adam S. Green, Dan Lawrence and Paul Roscoe, “Assessing neighborhoods, wealth differentials, and perceived inequality in preindustrial societies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400699121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12036974/. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, Iqtedar Alam and Cameron Petrie, “Inequality declined in the Bronze Age city of Mohenjo-daro,” <i>Antiquity</i> (2026): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2026.10359. [Open access]","Danika Parikh and Cameron A. Petrie, “‘We are inheritors of a rural civilisation’: rural complexity and the ceramic economy in the Indus Civilisation in northwest India,” <i>World Archaeology</i> 51, no. 2 (2019): 252–72, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643715/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","bronze age","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Water_canal_for_the_great_bath_-_Mohenjo-daro.jpg/1280px-Water_canal_for_the_great_bath_-_Mohenjo-daro.jpg","title":"A brick-lined water channel beside the Great Bath at Mohenjo-daro","desc":"The channel runs deep between two brick walls and passes under a corbelled brick roof.","credit":"Photograph by Quratulain, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0","alt":"A narrow, deep channel between two high walls of small flat bricks, roofed over at the far end by bricks stepped inward to meet."}},
{"id":"wh-236","num":236,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Set among the salt flats and rocky islands of the Rann of Kachchh, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut a linked series of reservoirs deep into the bedrock around its walls and gateways.","questions":["One of only four Indus cities dug at length, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood for some fifteen centuries before an earthquake damaged it about four thousand years ago.","Cubic stone weights of the standard Indus kind, an open clearing inside the walls and reservoirs in the bedrock are what excavation has found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in Gujarat."],"answer":"Dholavira","answerText":"Dholavira","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4950 – 3450 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earthquake</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4450 – 4150 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Dholavira</b> is one of the five major urban centres so far identified in the Indus Valley Civilisation, lying in the island belt of the Rann of Kachchh, in Gujarat in western India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is also one of only four Indus cities to have been excavated at length, alongside Harappa, Mohenjo-daro and Rakhigarhi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The settlement stood for about 1,500 years, from roughly 4,950 to 3,450 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its excavated plans show city walls and gateways, and a series of reservoirs cut deep into the bedrock around the town and joined to one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water storage on that scale is why the site is reached for whenever the water supply of Indus settlements is discussed, though how those settlements were watered is still poorly understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> An extensive open clearing lies enclosed within the walls, of the kind found at other Indus cities and often read as room for people to gather, though open ground can also be an accident of how a site forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cubic stone weights of the standard Indus kind have been recovered there, part of a family of Bronze Age weight systems across Western Eurasia that one study finds consistent with descent from a single original unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country around it is one of saline waste, rocky islands and active faults, and an earthquake damaged the town between about 4,450 and 4,150 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Khirsara, another Indus site in the same part of Gujarat, farmers turned from wheat and barley to drought-resistant millets at about 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That shift marks an abrupt arid event now recognised across many dated records, and it falls inside the same few centuries as the earthquake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Javed N. Malik, Eshaan Srivastava, Mahendrasinh S. Gadhavi, Franz Livio, Nayan Sharma, Shreya Arora, Nicolò Parrino, Pierfrancesco Burrato and Attilio Sulli, “Holocene surface-rupturing paleo-earthquakes along the Kachchh Mainland Fault: shaping the seismic landscape of Kachchh, western India,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 11612, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-62086-z. [Open access]","Adam S. Green and Cameron A. Petrie, “Landscapes of Urbanization and De-Urbanization: A Large-Scale Approach to Investigating the Indus Civilization’s Settlement Distributions in Northwest India,” <i>Journal of Field Archaeology</i> 43, no. 4 (2018): 284–99, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7665814/. [Open access]","Nicola Ialongo, Raphael Hermann and Lorenz Rahmstorf, “Bronze Age weight systems as a measure of market integration in Western Eurasia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 27 (2021): e2105873118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8271817/. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Yong Jun Kim, Eun Jin Woo, Nilesh Jadhav, Pranjali Waghmare, Yogesh Yadav, Avradeep Munshi et al., “Archaeological and anthropological studies on the Harappan cemetery of Rakhigarhi, India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 2 (2018): e0192299, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192299. [Open access]","Anil K. Pokharia, Rajesh Agnihotri, Shalini Sharma, Sunil Bajpai, Jitendra Nath, R. N. Kumaran and Bipin Chandra Negi, “Altered cropping pattern and cultural continuation with declined prosperity following abrupt and extreme arid event at ~4,200 yrs BP: Evidence from an Indus archaeological site Khirsara, Gujarat, western India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 10 (2017): e0185684, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0185684. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","india"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Water_storage_tanks_at_Dholavira_Citadel.jpg/1280px-Water_storage_tanks_at_Dholavira_Citadel.jpg","title":"A stone-lined water tank at Dholavira","desc":"The tank is cut below ground and faced with courses of flat stone, with a stepped ledge running down one side.","credit":"Photograph by Prof Ranga Sai, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0","alt":"A rectangular pit faced with courses of flat stone slabs, a stepped ledge running down one side, open to the sky and edged with dry grass."}},
{"id":"wh-237","num":237,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Built over a tidal salt marsh at the head of the Gulf of Cambay, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was later left high and dry as the sea level dropped away from it.","questions":["A circular steatite seal found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is neither wholly Indian nor Sumerian in workmanship, and closely resembles the seals of the Persian Gulf islands.","Kilns with vitrified inner surfaces and high-grade copper ingots have been dug at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a smaller Indus site on the Gujarat coast rather than one of the great cities."],"answer":"Lothal","answerText":"Lothal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Harappan phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Lothal</b> is a settlement of the Indus Valley Civilisation on the Gujarat coast of western India, of the urban phase that ran from about 2600 to 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its excavator described it as an important harbour town at the head of the Gulf of Cambay, and the name has stuck: later work calls it the major port of the civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the excavations turned up to support that reading was a circular steatite seal, neither wholly Indian nor Sumerian in workmanship, closely resembling the seals found on the islands of the Persian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seals of that kind are thought to have been made at the entrepôts of a cosmopolitan Gulf trade, Bahrain among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ground the town stood on has since been reconstructed from satellite imagery and from the microscopic fossils in its sediments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A broad tidal channel once ran along the western side of the town, and an estuary lay to the east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lothal grew over a tidal salt marsh, and was left high and dry as the sea level dropped away from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of the smaller Indus sites rather than one of the five cities, and among those, with Rupar and Kalibangan, that have produced human remains and burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kilns with vitrified inner surfaces and high-grade copper ingots have been dug there, which points to skilled copper smelting on the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farming at Lothal has been argued to have differed from that of the great cities because the rainfall and the country differ, but systematically collected plant remains have not yet borne the idea out, so how the smaller settlements fed themselves is still an open question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["S. R. Rao, “A ‘Persian Gulf’ Seal from Lothal,” <i>Antiquity</i> 37, no. 146 (1963): 96–99, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00037194. [Paywalled]","A. S. Khadkikar, C. Rajshekhar and K. P. N. Kumaran, “Palaeogeography around the Harappan port of Lothal, Gujarat, western India,” <i>Antiquity</i> 78, no. 302 (2004): 896–903, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00113535. [Paywalled]","Aditya Prakash Kanth, Manager Rajdeo Singh and Buddha Rashmi Mani, “Archaeometallurgical characterisation of ancient copper slags from pre-Harappan site, Kunal, India,” <i>Analytical Science Advances</i> 3, no. 7–8 (2022), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10989616/. [Open access]","C. A. Petrie, E. Lightfoot, P. J. Jones, J. R. Walker et al., “Animal movement on the hoof and on the cart and its implications for understanding exchange within the Indus Civilisation,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 158, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-50249-3. [Open access]","C. A. Petrie and J. Bates, “‘Multi-cropping’, Intercropping and Adaptation to Variable Environments in Indus South Asia,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 30, no. 2 (2017), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6991972/. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","india"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Lothal_Harappan_Civilization.jpg/1280px-Lothal_Harappan_Civilization.jpg","title":"The Lothal archaeological site, Gujarat","desc":"Brick embankment walls and steps enclosing a wide flat expanse, on the coastal plain of Gujarat.","credit":"Photograph by Ranjith Kumar Inbasekaran, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0","alt":"A wide flat expanse of bare earth enclosed by low brick walls, with brick steps at one side and a line of dark structures on a bank beyond, under a blue sky."}},
{"id":"wh-238","num":238,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"After about 1900 BCE the interaction sphere retracted and village life took over, a change called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, for which no single cause has ever been agreed.","questions":["Drought episodes rising from about 4,200 years ago are the leading explanation for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though disease, warfare, shifting rivers and broken trade routes have all been proposed as well.","The valley was already as arid as it is today by 2800 BCE, which is the awkward fact for any account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that rests on drying alone."],"answer":"decline of the Indus civilisation","answerText":"decline of the Indus civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Harappan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drought episodes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 4200 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>decline of the Indus civilisation</b> is the contraction of its cities and its wider exchange network after about 1900 BCE, a change variously called a collapse and a metamorphosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through the Late Harappan phase, from about 1900 to 1300 BCE, the interaction sphere retracted, the urban centres lost population, and agrarian village life took over for most of the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlement shifted from many small sites around a few large centres to a de-nucleated pattern, and in parts of north-western India the density of sites may actually have risen after 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Since most people in the civilisation are thought never to have lived in a city, a turn towards villages is a change in where complexity lay rather than a simple emptying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> No single cause is agreed: hydroclimate anomalies, disease, warfare, rivers moved by tectonics, political instability, changes of crop and the breaking of trade routes have all been proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The climate case is the one most often made, and it rests on an increase in drought episodes from about 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lake core from the Garhwal Himalaya records a drawdown beginning at that date and a dry anomaly through the following century and a half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Khirsara in Gujarat the farmers answered by turning from wheat and barley to drought-resistant millets, and the site carried on with its prosperity reduced rather than ending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Charcoal and crop remains elsewhere show rising aridity and a weakening monsoon from about 2100 BCE, worked around for generations before the cities gave way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The awkward fact for any simple drying story is that the valley was already about as arid as it is now by 2800 BCE, and what changed after 2000 BCE was the steadiness of the rainfall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["E. A. Niederman, D. F. Porinchu and B. S. Kotlia, “Hydroclimate change in the Garhwal Himalaya, India at 4200 yr BP coincident with the contraction of the Indus civilization,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 23082, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-02496-5. [Open access]","Gwen Robbins Schug, K. Elaine Blevins, Brett Cox and Kelsey Gray, “Infection, Disease, and Biosocial Processes at the End of the Indus Civilization,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e84814, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0084814. [Open access]","Adam S. Green and Cameron A. Petrie, “Landscapes of Urbanization and De-Urbanization: A Large-Scale Approach to Investigating the Indus Civilization’s Settlement Distributions in Northwest India,” <i>Journal of Field Archaeology</i> 43, no. 4 (2018): 284–99, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7665814/. [Open access]","Anil K. Pokharia, Rajesh Agnihotri, Shalini Sharma, Sunil Bajpai, Jitendra Nath, R. N. Kumaran and Bipin Chandra Negi, “Altered cropping pattern and cultural continuation with declined prosperity following abrupt and extreme arid event at ~4,200 yrs BP: Evidence from an Indus archaeological site Khirsara, Gujarat, western India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 10 (2017): e0185684, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0185684. [Open access]","Carla Lancelotti, Juan José García-Granero, Charusmita S. Gadekar, Marco Madella and P. Ajithprasad, “‘Not all that burns is wood’. A social perspective on fuel exploitation and use during the Indus urban period (2600-1900 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 3 (2018): e0192364, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192364. [Open access]","Danika Parikh and Cameron A. Petrie, “‘We are inheritors of a rural civilisation’: rural complexity and the ceramic economy in the Indus Civilisation in northwest India,” <i>World Archaeology</i> 51, no. 2 (2019): 252–72, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643715/. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","archaeology","bronze age","climate"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Archaeological_artifacts_recovered_from_Khirsara%2C_Indus_Valley_Civilization.jpg/1280px-Archaeological_artifacts_recovered_from_Khirsara%2C_Indus_Valley_Civilization.jpg","title":"Objects excavated at Khirsara, an Indus site in Gujarat","desc":"A plate from the study that dated the site's turn to drought-resistant millets to about 4,200 years ago.","credit":"Pokharia et al., PLOS ONE 12, no. 10 (2017): e0185684, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0","alt":"A labelled plate of excavated finds: painted potsherds, a small jar, a shell bangle, copper implements, agate and carnelian beads, and two inscribed seals."}},
{"id":"wh-239","num":239,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Ancient DNA puts steppe pastoralist ancestry in the Swat valley in the first half of the 2nd millennium BCE, the strongest evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the subcontinent.","questions":["A genome from the Indus civilisation lacks the steppe ancestry now universal in the northwest, which places the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after that civilisation rather than during it.","Paternal lineages arriving from Central Asia in the Bronze Age, beside far older maternal ones, show that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were strongly male-driven."],"answer":"Indo-Aryan migrations","answerText":"Indo-Aryan migrations","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Central Asia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2100 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reaching South Asia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Indo-Aryan migrations</b> are the movements that carried Indo-European speech into the Indian subcontinent, a late event of the middle to late Bronze Age, and the case for them now rests largely on ancient DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Upstream lies the Yamnaya complex of the steppes north of the Black and Caspian Seas, which appeared about 3300 BCE and whose descendants reached the Indo-Iranians through the Corded Ware, Fatyanovo and Sintashta cultures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes from 523 ancient people show no steppe pastoralist ancestry at the Bactria-Margiana sites of Central Asia before 2100 BCE, and communities there mixing with people who carried it by about 2100 to 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Samples from the Swat valley put that ancestry further south in the first half of the 2nd millennium BCE, where it now makes up as much as 30 per cent of the ancestry of some groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its profile matches that of Bronze Age eastern Europe, which ties the two dispersals to one movement of people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> An individual of the Indus civilisation, sequenced separately, carries little if any of that ancestry, so what is near-universal in the northwest today was not there then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across the subcontinent it is commonest in northern populations and thins towards the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The influx was strongly male-driven: the paternal lineages arrive from Central Asia in the Bronze Age, while the maternal lineages of the subcontinent are far older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Groups of traditionally priestly status, among them the custodians of the liturgy in Sanskrit, carry more steppe ancestry than a simple mixture model predicts, which is a second and independent line of argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The weak point is material culture, different enough that some archaeologists see no connection at all, and how much immigration took place remains extremely controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nick Patterson, Priya Moorjani, Nadin Rohland, Rebecca Bernardos, Swapan Mallick, Iosif Lazaridis et al., “The formation of human populations in South and Central Asia,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6457 (2019): eaat7487, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6822619/. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nadin Rohland, Swapan Mallick, Matthew Mah, Mark Lipson, Nathan Nakatsuka et al., “An Ancient Harappan Genome Lacks Ancestry from Steppe Pastoralists or Iranian Farmers,” <i>Cell</i> 179, no. 3 (2019): 729–35, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6800651/. [Open access]","Marina Silva, Marisa Oliveira, Daniel Vieira, Andreia Brandão, Teresa Rito, Joana B. Pereira, Ross M. Fraser et al., “A genetic chronology for the Indian Subcontinent points to heavily sex-biased dispersals,” <i>BMC Evolutionary Biology</i> 17 (2017): 88, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12862-017-0936-9. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, David Anthony, Leonid Vyazov, Romain Fournier et al., “The genetic origin of the Indo-Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 639 (2025): 132–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11922553/. [Open access]","Rishabh Jain, Sachin M. Rathod, Pankaj Jha et al., “Layers in the sand: The genetic imprint of migration, culture, and Indus craft in the Thar Desert,” <i>Human Genetics and Genomics Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 100623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13213775/. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","genetics","migration","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Yamnaya-related_migrations.jpg/1280px-Yamnaya-related_migrations.jpg","title":"Yamnaya-related migrations, drawn after Anthony and after Narasimhan et al.","desc":"A map made for Wikipedia from those studies: the homeland north of the Black Sea at 3300 BCE, arrows west into Europe and east across the steppe, and one reaching the subcontinent about 1500 BCE.","credit":"Map by Joshua Jonathan, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0","alt":"A physical map of Eurasia with the Yamnaya homeland marked north of the Black Sea and black arrows running west into Europe, east across the steppe and south-east into the Indian subcontinent, each labelled with a date BCE."}},
{"id":"wh-240","num":240,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Named for a body of Sanskrit texts rather than for any state, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follows the Indus civilisation in northern South Asia and is known chiefly from those texts.","questions":["Literature made first in the Punjab and later in the valley of the Ganges lets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> be traced by where its texts were composed.","Ten kings in coalition met Sudas of the Tritsus on the banks of the Parushni during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in a battle one of the hymns describes."],"answer":"Vedic period","answerText":"Vedic period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rigveda composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Language arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Vedic period</b> is the age of the Sanskrit texts called the Vedas, following the Indus civilisation in northern South Asia and named for a body of literature rather than for any state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its language came in with the spread of Indo-European speech into the subcontinent, an event of the middle to late Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vedic Sanskrit is the oldest recorded stage of Indo-Aryan, from which the modern languages of northern India and Pakistan descend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three literary strata are distinguished inside the period: the four Vedas, then the Brahmanas, then the Sutras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The four are collections of hymns and prayers, made chiefly to accompany the pressing of soma and the offering of melted butter to the gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three of them were together called the threefold knowledge, and the Atharvaveda joined them only after a long struggle, being later in its language and much of its matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hymns ask the gods for wealth in cattle, for offspring, for long life and for victory, and they are not folk poetry but the skilled work of a priestly class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also record the quarrels of their own world: ten kings in coalition met Sudas of the Tritsus on the banks of the Parushni and were driven back as they tried to cross it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the earlier half of the period the centre lay in the Punjab; in the later half the writing turned speculative and prosaic and the centre had moved to the valley of the Ganges, until settlement reached from the mouths of the Indus to those of the Ganges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Groups of traditionally priestly status, the custodians of that liturgy, still carry more steppe ancestry than a simple mixture model predicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","quote":{"book":"rigveda","n":"534","cite":"Hymn 7.18","text":"<p>Together came the Pakthas, the Bhalanas, the Alinas, the Sivas, the Visanins. Yet to the Trtsus came the Arya's Comrade, through love of spoil and heroes' war, to lead them.</p><p>Fools, in their folly fain to waste her waters, they parted inexhaustible Parusni. Lord of the Earth, he with his might repressed them: still lay the herd and the affrighted herdsman.</p>"},"sources":["Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nick Patterson, Priya Moorjani, Nadin Rohland, Rebecca Bernardos, Swapan Mallick, Iosif Lazaridis et al., “The formation of human populations in South and Central Asia,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6457 (2019): eaat7487, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6822619/. [Open access]","Rishabh Jain, Sachin M. Rathod, Pankaj Jha et al., “Layers in the sand: The genetic imprint of migration, culture, and Indus craft in the Thar Desert,” <i>Human Genetics and Genomics Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 100623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13213775/. [Open access]","Niels Schoubben, “Animacy, postpositions, and the spatial cases in Niya Prakrit,” <i>Journal of South Asian Languages and Linguistics</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 87, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12458993/. [Open access]","Marina Silva, Marisa Oliveira, Daniel Vieira, Andreia Brandão, Teresa Rito, Joana B. Pereira, Ross M. Fraser et al., “A genetic chronology for the Indian Subcontinent points to heavily sex-biased dispersals,” <i>BMC Evolutionary Biology</i> 17 (2017): 88, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12862-017-0936-9. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","history","literature","language","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/1672_CE_manuscript_copy%2C_10th_century_BCE_Samaveda_Kauthuma_Samhita_Veyagana%2C_Schoyen_Collection_Norway.jpg/1280px-1672_CE_manuscript_copy%2C_10th_century_BCE_Samaveda_Kauthuma_Samhita_Veyagana%2C_Schoyen_Collection_Norway.jpg","title":"A folio of the Samaveda, copied in 1672","desc":"One of the four Vedas, in a manuscript made long after the hymns were composed; the small figures above the line belong to its notation for chanting.","credit":"Photograph by Ms Sarah Welch of a folio in the Schoyen Collection, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0","alt":"A wide palm-leaf-shaped manuscript folio written in Devanagari, eight close-set lines of ink on aged paper, with small numerals written above the letters."}},
{"id":"wh-241","num":241,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Falling into ten cycles, six of them the work of single families of seers, the 1,028 hymns of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> make it the oldest surviving book of the subcontinent.","questions":["About 250 hymns celebrate Indra and at least 200 Agni across the ten cycles of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose ninth book is given over entirely to Soma.","So close is its language to Avestan that whole stanzas of one can be turned word for word into the other, which places the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> near the root of Indo-Iranian."],"answer":"Rigveda","answerText":"Rigveda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Rigveda</b> is the oldest of the four Vedas and the oldest surviving book of the Indian subcontinent, a collection of hymns in Vedic Sanskrit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That language is the oldest recorded stage of Indo-Aryan, and it reached the subcontinent with the spread of Indo-European speech in the middle to late Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It stands so close to Avestan that whole stanzas of the one can be turned word for word into the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surviving Shakala recension holds 1,017 hymns, or 1,028 counting the eleven Valakhilya hymns inserted in the middle of the eighth book, where another school counted 1,025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are grouped in ten books called mandalas, or cycles, and the tradition counts 10,580 verses, 153,826 words and 432,000 syllables in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Books 2 to 7 are the family books, each handed down within the family of seers that made it and each arranged on the same plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Books 1, 8 and 10 are not one family's work but sets of groups gathered by authorship, and the ninth is unified instead by its subject, every hymn in it addressed to Soma, a collection put together only after the first eight books had been combined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 250 hymns celebrate Indra, nearly a quarter of the whole, and at least 200 celebrate Agni, the fire of the sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hymns ask for wealth in cattle, for offspring, for long life and for victory, and in the later ones purely abstract powers such as Wrath and Faith are deified for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of the latest turns on the origin of things and closes by doubting whether even the highest god knows how the world began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","quote":{"book":"rigveda","n":"966","cite":"Hymn 10.129","text":"<p>Who verily knows and who can here declare it, whence it was born and whence comes this creation? The Gods are later than this world's production. Who knows then whence it first came into being?</p><p>He, the first origin of this creation, whether he formed it all or did not form it, Whose eye controls this world in highest heaven, he verily knows it, or perhaps he knows not.</p>"},"sources":["Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology</i>, Grundriss der Indo-Arischen Philologie und Altertumskunde III.1.A (Strassburg: Karl J. Trübner, 1897), https://archive.org/stream/vedicmythology00macd_0/vedicmythology00macd_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Niels Schoubben, “Animacy, postpositions, and the spatial cases in Niya Prakrit,” <i>Journal of South Asian Languages and Linguistics</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 87, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12458993/. [Open access]","Rishabh Jain, Sachin M. Rathod, Pankaj Jha et al., “Layers in the sand: The genetic imprint of migration, culture, and Indus craft in the Thar Desert,” <i>Human Genetics and Genomics Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 100623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13213775/. [Open access]","Ralph T. H. Griffith, trans., <i>The Hymns of the Rigveda</i>, vol. 1 (Benares: E. J. Lazarus and Co., 1889), https://archive.org/stream/hymnsrigveda02grifgoog/hymnsrigveda02grifgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"tags":["text","history","literature","language","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Rigveda_MS2097.jpg","title":"Two folios of a Rigveda manuscript, India, early 19th century","desc":"The text opens with the invocation and the first hymn to Agni; the red marks above and below the line are the pitch accents.","credit":"Manuscript MS 2097, Schoyen Collection, via Wikimedia Commons; public domain","alt":"Two paper folios written in Devanagari, ruled in red at the margins, with small red strokes marked above and below the letters."}},
{"id":"wh-242","num":242,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Meaning put together, and so refined, the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is set against the Prakrits, the popular dialects it was distinguished from.","questions":["About four thousand rules written like algebraic formulae, in which brevity is sought at the cost of clarity, fixed the grammar of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 4th century BCE.","Official inscriptions ran in Middle Indian for centuries after Ashoka, yet verses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> inside those same inscriptions show that it was still a living literary language."],"answer":"Sanskrit","answerText":"Sanskrit","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vedic Sanskrit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Panini's grammar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Sanskrit</b> is the classical language of the Indian subcontinent, and its name, samskrita, means literally 'put together', so 'refined' or 'elaborate'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is set against the Prakrits, the popular dialects, and the word is absent from the older grammarians but turns up in the earliest epic, the Ramayana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vedic Sanskrit is the oldest recorded stage of Indo-Aryan, from which Middle Indo-Aryan and then the modern languages of the north descend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The family reached the subcontinent with the spread of Indo-European speech in the middle to late Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its alphabet, worked out on phonetic principles, was already settled before the 4th century BCE and has not been altered since, the simple vowels first, then the diphthongs, then the consonants in order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Panini's grammar, of about the 4th century BCE, casts the language into some four thousand rules written like algebraic formulae, in which brevity alone is sought, at the cost of being clear or unambiguous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It superseded every predecessor, came to be treated as an infallible authority, and the whole later literature of India was pressed into the mould it made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was not a language of books alone: a later grammarian speaks of it as used in the world and calls its words current in the world, and Panini gives rules for the accent of calling from a distance, of salutation, and of question and answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the time of Ashoka, official decrees were composed in Middle Indian dialects for centuries, yet Sanskrit verses set inside those inscriptions show it living on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its liturgy is still in the keeping of groups of traditionally priestly status, who carry more steppe ancestry than a simple mixture model predicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","William Dwight Whitney, <i>Sanskrit Grammar, Including Both the Classical Language and the Older Dialects of Veda and Brahmana</i> (Leipzig: Breitkopf and Härtel, 1879), https://archive.org/stream/sanskritgrammari00whit_0/sanskritgrammari00whit_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Niels Schoubben, “Animacy, postpositions, and the spatial cases in Niya Prakrit,” <i>Journal of South Asian Languages and Linguistics</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 87, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12458993/. [Open access]","Rishabh Jain, Sachin M. Rathod, Pankaj Jha et al., “Layers in the sand: The genetic imprint of migration, culture, and Indus craft in the Thar Desert,” <i>Human Genetics and Genomics Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 100623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13213775/. [Open access]","Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nick Patterson, Priya Moorjani, Nadin Rohland, Rebecca Bernardos, Swapan Mallick, Iosif Lazaridis et al., “The formation of human populations in South and Central Asia,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6457 (2019): eaat7487, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6822619/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","language","literature","india"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Junagadh_inscription_of_Rudradaman_%28portion%29.jpg","title":"Part of the Junagadh rock inscription of Rudradaman","desc":"A rubbing of a Sanskrit prose inscription at Girnar in Gujarat, of the 2nd century CE, with the plate's line numbers down the margin.","credit":"Plate by J. F. Fleet, via Wikimedia Commons; public domain","alt":"A black-and-white rubbing of an inscription cut into rough rock, lines of angular letters showing white against the dark stone, numbered down the right margin."}},
{"id":"wh-243","num":243,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Dug since 1959 at Yanshi in Henan, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> centred a four-level settlement hierarchy on one great site in the Yiluo river basin.","questions":["Palaces of unprecedented scale and craft under elite supervision belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose towns were nonetheless shrinking, unfortified, and without segregated elite burials.","Archaeologists went looking for the Xia in the ground where the records place them, and what they found instead was named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is disputed to this day."],"answer":"Erlitou culture","answerText":"Erlitou culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Yiluo river basin, Henan</span></div>","traditional":"二里頭文化","hanzi":"二里头文化","pinyin":"Èrlǐtóu wénhuà","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Èrlǐtóu wénhuà</span></div>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Erlitou culture</b> is the Bronze Age culture of the central plains of northern China, of about 1900 to 1500 BCE, named after its type site at Yanshi in Henan province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the earliest bronze-casting culture of central China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Archaeologists found the site in 1959 while looking for remains of the Xia people in the country where the ancient records place them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it belongs to the Xia or to the Shang has been argued ever since, several hundred articles deep by the middle of the 1990s and still unsettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some scholars connect its rise to the climatic deterioration around 2000 BCE that is thought to have brought down the Late Neolithic settlements of the Longshan period before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its setting is the Yiluo river basin, a rich alluvial pocket between the modern provinces of Shanxi and Henan, which through the 3rd millennium BCE grew from nine roughly equal villages of no more than 150 people into a chiefdom of two and then three tiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What follows is a stratified and bureaucratically governed state on four settlement levels, 148 large villages, 43 supravillages, 10 small centres and Erlitou itself above them, holding a population estimated at between about 28,000 and 82,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its palaces are of unprecedented scale and its crafts were made under elite supervision, and yet the towns were shrinking, no fortifications have been found, and the cemeteries hold no separated elite burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its drinking and pouring vessels travelled far: 24 of them were buried in thirteen elaborate tombs at Dadianzi, away to the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> And its bronzes carry no highly radiogenic lead, the metal that arrives only with the Erligang phase that follows it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Xuelian Zhang, Shihua Qiu, Lianzhen Cai, Hong Xu, Haitao Zhao and Guoliang Chen, “14C Dating of the Erlitou Site,” <i>Radiocarbon</i> 64, no. 4 (2022): 819–32, https://doi.org/10.1017/RDC.2021.72. [Paywalled]","David S. Sandeford, “Organizational complexity and demographic scale in primary states,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 5, no. 5 (2018): 171137, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.171137. [Open access]","Thomas Cucchi, Lingling Dai, Marie Balasse, Chunqing Zhao, Jiangtao Gao et al., “Social Complexification and Pig (<i>Sus scrofa</i>) Husbandry in Ancient China: A Combined Geometric Morphometric and Isotopic Approach,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 7 (2016): e0158523, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0158523. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Xiaojia Tang, Shui Shen, Xin Su et al., “From rammed earth to stone wall: Chronological insight into the settlement change of the Lower Xiajiadian culture,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0273161, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0273161. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/20260520_Bronze_Jue_from_Erlitou.jpg/1280px-20260520_Bronze_Jue_from_Erlitou.jpg","title":"Bronze jue from the Erlitou site, Yanshi","desc":"A three-legged bronze pouring vessel of the shape called a jue, excavated at Erlitou in Yanshi, Henan, and now in the Henan Museum. Vessels like this one are among the earliest cast bronzes known from the central plains; the metal has weathered to a green and brown crust.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A small dark bronze vessel on three thin splayed legs, with a long narrow trough spout rising at one side and a pointed tail at the other, its surface pitted and streaked with green corrosion, lit against a dark museum background."}},
{"id":"wh-244","num":244,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"The transmitted Chinese records make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first of the dynasties, founded by Yu the Great, who earned the throne by mastering a catastrophic flood.","questions":["Only three chapters about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survived into the <i>Book of Documents</i>, which is nearly all the Chinese canon preserves of the first of its dynasties.","Tang the Successful dethroned and banished the tyrant Jie to end the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, then feared that later ages would quote his rebellion as an excuse for their own."],"answer":"Xia dynasty","answerText":"Xia dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2205 – 1767 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also given as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21st – 16th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"夏朝","hanzi":"夏朝","pinyin":"Xià cháo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Xià cháo</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Xia dynasty</b> is the first of the dynasties in the transmitted Chinese records, placed before the Shang and dated in Chinese scholarship to the 21st to 16th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It takes its name from Xia, a small principality in Henan of which Yu the Great was made earl before the throne came to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yu and his descendants are said to have held that throne for 439 years, and the traditional reckoning runs from about 2205 to 1767 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Very little of them reaches the Chinese canon: only three documents of Xia went into the <i>Book of Documents</i>, either because the recorder's duty was hardly exercised so early or because what he wrote was almost wholly lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The last of the line was Jie, a tyrant in the records, whom Tang the Successful dethroned and kept in banishment; Tang was then ashamed, afraid that later ages would quote the deed as an apology for rebellion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Chinese scholarship has generally identified the dynasty with the Bronze Age remains at Erlitou in Yanshi, and it was that identification which carried the beginning of Chinese civilisation back beyond the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Whether those remains are Xia or early Shang is not settled, and the search that turned them up was itself a search for the Xia in the country the ancient documents give them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others place a Xia capital at Xinzhai in Henan instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The flood story has been pressed into service as well: an outburst flood dated to about 1920 BCE has been offered as the historical Great Flood, fixing the dynasty's start near 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> But the settlement that case rests on has been read as destroyed by an earthquake rather than by water, and the Great Flood's part in the making of the early dynasties is not confirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Xuelian Zhang, Shihua Qiu, Lianzhen Cai, Hong Xu, Haitao Zhao and Guoliang Chen, “14C Dating of the Erlitou Site,” <i>Radiocarbon</i> 64, no. 4 (2022): 819–32, https://doi.org/10.1017/RDC.2021.72. [Paywalled]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Confucianism, Part I</i>, 2nd ed., Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1899), 63–64, 84–86, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofeas0003jame. [Open access]","Thomas Cucchi, Lingling Dai, Marie Balasse, Chunqing Zhao, Jiangtao Gao et al., “Social Complexification and Pig (<i>Sus scrofa</i>) Husbandry in Ancient China: A Combined Geometric Morphometric and Isotopic Approach,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 7 (2016): e0158523, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0158523. [Open access]","Peijun Shi, Fenggui Liu, Xingmin Meng, Qiang Zhou, Deyong Yu et al., “Recent Jishishan earthquake ripple hazard provides a new explanation for the destruction of the prehistoric Lajia Settlement 4000a B.P.,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 11630, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-60433-8. [Open access]","Qinglong Wu, Zhijun Zhao, Li Liu, Darryl E. Granger, Hui Wang et al., “Outburst flood at 1920 BCE supports historicity of China’s Great Flood and the Xia dynasty,” <i>Science</i> 353, no. 6299 (2016): 579–82, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaf0842. [Paywalled]","Zhongpei Zhang, “Processus d’évolution de la civilisation chinoise antique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 148, no. 2 (2004): 533–42, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2004.22728. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Yu_ji_tu._LOC_2002626770.jpg/1280px-Yu_ji_tu._LOC_2002626770.jpg","title":"Yu ji tu, the Map of the Tracks of Yu","desc":"A rubbing of the Yu ji tu, engraved on stone in 1136 CE and now in the Forest of Stone Steles Museum in Xi'an. It is named for the flood-taming journeys the records attribute to the founder of the Xia, and lays the Yellow River and the Yangtze in heavy line over a grid whose squares each stand for about a hundred li. The sheet reproduced here was published in 1903 and carries a later cataloguer's pencilled notes and a library stamp.","credit":"Library of Congress, Geography and Map Division (https://www.loc.gov/item/2002626770/), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A map of China drawn in black ink over a fine grid of squares, the Yellow River and the Yangtze picked out as heavy winding lines and hundreds of small Chinese place names filling the land, with a title cartouche in the upper left and pencilled notes and a library stamp along the bottom edge."}},
{"id":"wh-245","num":245,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Ruling along the Yellow River from about 1600 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the Bronze Age dynasty of the central plains and the earliest dynasty archaeology can vouch for.","questions":["A ditch in the palace area at Zhengzhou held nearly a hundred human skulls, most likely prisoners taken while the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was conquering the country around Anyang.","King Wu of Zhou overthrew the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in about 1046 BCE, and the people of one cemetery in Henan are thought to be its survivors, moved off by the victors."],"answer":"Shang dynasty","answerText":"Shang dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1046 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商朝","hanzi":"商朝","pinyin":"Shāng cháo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Shāng cháo</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Shang dynasty</b> ruled along the Yellow River from about 1600 to 1046 BCE, and it is the earliest dynasty of Chinese history that archaeological evidence supports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the Bronze Age dynasty of the central plains, and the copper objects it left carry highly radiogenic lead whose source is one of the enduring puzzles of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its writing is the oracle bone inscriptions, tens of thousands of them, the earliest known Chinese characters and the source of most of what is known of Shang life, politics and religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seat of the dynasty moved more than once: Zhengzhou and Anyang were both capitals of it, and frequent relocations are among the changes visible in the central plains over these centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chinese excavations at Yinxu in Henan in 1928 put the beginning of the ancient civilisation of China at the end of the dynasty, a starting point later work has pushed a great deal further back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Zhengzhou Shang City, dated to about 1509 to 1315 BCE, was the capital of the early dynasty, and what has been found there is chiefly of the Erligang period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1974 a ditch 15 metres (50 feet) long, holding nearly a hundred human skulls, was excavated in its palace area, and strontium, carbon and nitrogen isotopes point to prisoners of war taken as Shang forces conquered the country around Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The late dynasty left a record of its own weather: about 55,000 pieces of inscribed bone carry written observations spanning roughly two centuries, enough to index rainfall and flooding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its burials divide the living sharply, yet eleven genomes from a late Shang cemetery at Xingyang show no genetic difference between the high-grade tombs and the low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the transmitted account King Wu of Zhou conquered the dynasty in about 1046 BCE, and the people of that cemetery are taken for Shang who were moved away from the capital by the victors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Jiaxin Tang, Rui Wang, Qingli Wei, Haifeng He, Chen Deng et al., “Social Stratification Without Genetic Differentiation at the Xisima Site in the Late Shang Dynasty,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 43, no. 1 (2026): msaf316, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12770819/. [Open access]","Fang Fang, Jingwen Liao, Xiaomin Zeng and Juzhong Zhang, “The Truth of Unusual Deaths under Military Expansion: Evidence from the Stable Isotopes of a Human Skull Ditch in the Capital City of the Early Shang Dynasty, China,” <i>Genes</i> 13, no. 11 (2022): 2077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9690788/. [Open access]","Ke Ding, Siyang Li, Aijun Ding, Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang et al., “Archeological data with AI- and physics-based modeling explain typhoon-induced disasters in inland China around 3000 yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 10 (2026): eaeb1598, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12959396/. [Open access]","Zhongpei Zhang, “Processus d’évolution de la civilisation chinoise antique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 148, no. 2 (2004): 533–42, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2004.22728. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Shang_Dynasty_City_Wall%2C_Zhengzhou_09.jpg/1280px-Shang_Dynasty_City_Wall%2C_Zhengzhou_09.jpg","title":"The Shang city wall at Zhengzhou","desc":"A stretch of the rammed-earth wall of Zhengzhou Shang City, the capital of the early dynasty, still standing as a low bank through the modern city in Henan. The site is dated to about 1509 to 1315 BCE; a footpath now runs along the top of it.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A broad bank of bare packed earth planted with young trees, running away from the viewer as a low ridge with a worn footpath along its crest, apartment blocks and a building under construction standing in the haze beyond."}},
{"id":"wh-246","num":246,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Carved on turtle plastrons and ox shoulder blades that had been drilled and cracked with heat, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest form of systematic Chinese writing.","questions":["More than 160,000 inscribed pieces carry the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and of the four thousand-odd distinct signs it uses only about a thousand have been read with confidence.","In the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a sign first drawn as a kind of wheat came to write the verb ‘to come’, the two words having sounded the same."],"answer":"oracle bone script","answerText":"oracle bone script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1045 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the late Shang, at Anyang</span></div>","traditional":"甲骨文","hanzi":"甲骨文","pinyin":"jiǎgǔwén","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">jiǎgǔwén</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Oracle bone script</b>, jiaguwen in Chinese, is the earliest form of systematic Chinese writing, carved on turtle plastrons and the shoulder blades of cattle in the late Shang, from about 1250 to 1045 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bones were prepared by drilling pits into them and cracking them with heat, and what is written on them are divination records made for the royalty and nobility, most of them found at Anyang in the central plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 160,000 inscribed pieces are known, carrying some four thousand distinct signs, and by another count more than 4,600.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only about a thousand of those have been deciphered, their meanings and their corresponding modern Chinese characters confirmed; the rest are known as shapes rather than as words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was already a fully developed writing system when it first appears, and the bones excavated at Huayuanzhuang East in Yinxu belong to the reign of King Wu Ding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Many of the signs began as pictures of what they name, and one of the main characters for disaster is a pattern resembling water waves, which is taken to mean flooding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The script also borrowed sound: a sign first drawn as a kind of wheat came to write the verb ‘to come’, the two words having been pronounced alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That device is called jiajie in Chinese scholarship and the rebus principle elsewhere, and it is what lets a picture stand for a word no picture could show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Semantic association dominated the early formation of the signs, and a shift towards writing sound is visible afterwards, in the bronze inscriptions of the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reading a bone is not a matter of running down the lines, since the order moves about the surface, and one sign may be written in strikingly different ways from one piece to the next, so a rubbing, a hand-drawn facsimile and a transcription are consulted together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","writing","language","china"],"sources":["Ludovica Ottaviano and Silvia Ferrara, “Mechanisms at the core of the Chinese script invention,” <i>Cognitive Linguistics</i> 37, no. 1 (2026): 39–62, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12949599/. [Open access]","Pengjie Wang, Kaile Zhang, Xinyu Wang, Shengwei Han, Yongge Liu et al., “An open dataset for oracle bone character recognition and decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03807-x. [Open access]","Bang Li, Jing Yang, Yujie Liang, Xiaobin Hu, Zengmao Ding et al., “OBIMD: A Multi-modal Dataset for Contextual Interpretation of Oracle Bone Inscriptions,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 13 (2026): 681, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-026-06967-0. [Open access]","Ke Ding, Siyang Li, Aijun Ding, Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang et al., “Archeological data with AI- and physics-based modeling explain typhoon-induced disasters in inland China around 3000 yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 10 (2026): eaeb1598, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12959396/. [Open access]","Mei Wang and Weihong Deng, “A dataset of oracle characters for benchmarking machine learning algorithms,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 87, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-02933-w. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_%28for_divination%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_%28for_divination%29.jpg","title":"Inscribed ox scapula from the reign of Wu Ding","desc":"An oracle bone of about 1200 BCE, carrying the work of the Bin group of diviners, who belong to the first period of the late Shang and so to the reign of King Wu Ding. The incised characters have been filled with red pigment to make them legible; the bone has broken along its lower edge.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A broad pale plate of bone, roughly triangular and cracked across the bottom, its whole surface covered in columns of small angular incised characters picked out in dark red."}},
{"id":"wh-247","num":247,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Last capital of the Shang and one of the largest metal consumers in Eurasia in the 2nd millennium BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew its metal from more than 500 km (300 miles) away.","questions":["All the kingly tombs of the Shang lie in one cemetery at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where thousands of lesser graves were dug over four phases and most of the rich ones were robbed in antiquity.","An extinct water buffalo was first named from bones found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Shang capital whose oracle bones carry most of what is known of the dynasty's divinations."],"answer":"Yinxu","answerText":"Yinxu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1045 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb M1004</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1243 – 1157 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"殷墟","hanzi":"殷墟","pinyin":"Yīnxū","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Yīnxū</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Yinxu</b>, the ruins of Yin, is the site of the last capital of dynastic Shang China, at Anyang in northern Henan, and it was occupied from about 1250 to 1045 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists divide it into four chronological phases, Yinxu I to IV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of the largest metal consumers in Eurasia during the 2nd millennium BCE, and the metal was brought in from regions more than 500 km (300 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what is known of Shang divination comes from this ground: the oracle bones were mostly found at Anyang, and are the divination records of the royalty and nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bones excavated at Huayuanzhuang East belong to the reign of King Wu Ding and are kept at the Anyang workstation of the Institute of Archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The dead are the site's other archive: all the kingly tombs lie at Xibeigang, and thousands more graves fill the Western Area, taken for cluster burials of lineage members across all four phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rank is legible in them, in the quality and quantity of the metal, in the inscriptions on the bronzes, in the numbers of human and animal sacrifices, and in the size, position and orientation of the burial chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the rich tombs were looted in antiquity, though M1004, a kingly tomb dated to the site's second phase between 1243 and 1157 BCE, still gave up 941 weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An extinct water buffalo, <i>Bubalus mephistopheles</i>, was first discovered and named from bones found here, and is thought to have died out before the Eastern Zhou, around three thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chinese excavation began at the site in 1928, and what came out of it was what first carried the ancient civilisation of China back to the end of the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury et al., “Social hierarchy and the choice of metal recycling at Anyang, the last capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-75920-x. [Open access]","Zhongpei Zhang, “Processus d’évolution de la civilisation chinoise antique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 148, no. 2 (2004): 533–42, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2004.22728. [Open access]","Pengjie Wang, Kaile Zhang, Xinyu Wang, Shengwei Han, Yongge Liu et al., “An open dataset for oracle bone character recognition and decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03807-x. [Open access]","Ke Ding, Siyang Li, Aijun Ding, Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang et al., “Archeological data with AI- and physics-based modeling explain typhoon-induced disasters in inland China around 3000 yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 10 (2026): eaeb1598, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12959396/. [Open access]","Xue-Yuan Liang, Xin-Meng Liu, Xiao-Le Lei, Qingbo Hu, Xiaoning Guo et al., “Ancient mitogenomes reveal the genetic relationship among extinct and extant water buffaloes,” <i>iScience</i> 29 (2026): 116354, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13276306/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Oracle_bones_pit.JPG/1280px-Oracle_bones_pit.JPG","title":"Pit YH127 of oracle bones at Yinxu","desc":"A round shaft sunk into the earth at Yinxu and packed with inscribed bone and turtle plastron, shown here as the pit is presented on the site. A human skeleton lies against the far wall. Pits like this one held the divination records of the late Shang court in their thousands.","credit":"Photograph by Xuan Che, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Looking down into a round pit cut in pale earth, its floor heaped with hundreds of overlapping flat pieces of bone and turtle shell, a human skeleton lying along one side, the whole lit from above against dark surrounding ground."}},
{"id":"wh-248","num":248,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Cast for banqueting and for offering food and drink to the ancestors, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have no real parallel elsewhere in Bronze Age Eurasia, where the metal went mostly into weapons and tools.","questions":["Ceramic moulds and cores fired at surprisingly low temperatures made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possible, and casting them was so closely controlled that the workshops cluster around the Shang capital.","About 21,000 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry inscriptions, from clan emblems of a few characters under the early Shang to Western Zhou texts running to 500 characters."],"answer":"Chinese ritual bronzes","answerText":"Chinese ritual bronzes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cast</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under the Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1045 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"青銅禮器","hanzi":"青铜礼器","pinyin":"qīngtóng lǐqì","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">qīngtóng lǐqì</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Chinese ritual bronzes</b> are the vessels cast in enormous numbers under the Shang and Zhou dynasties, between about 1600 and 221 BCE, and used in banqueting, in offering food and drink for ancestor worship, and in signalling rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making them was the most prominent handicraft industry of Bronze Age China, and it was bound into the ritual and political systems of both dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The preference for putting bronze into vessels is peculiar to China, since elsewhere in Bronze Age Eurasia the typical products were weapons, tools and personal ornaments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Great quantities of copper, tin and lead went into them, and they were then buried in the tombs of the elite, taken out of circulation for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Casting carried symbolic weight and was tightly controlled: the workshops cluster around the Shang capital at Yinxu, and a vessel mould found far from it is a rarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They were cast in ceramic moulds and cores, and it is those moulds that made the complex casting methods used in early China possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The moulds of the early Shang were fired remarkably cool, in many cases at only 200 to 300 °C (390 to 570 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 21,000 of the bronzes carry inscriptions, from the clan emblems of a few signs that appear in the early Shang to Western Zhou texts running to 500 characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The metal itself divides the ranks: the highest elite had vessels alloyed to a controlled recipe from very pure copper, while the lower elites got metal that had been mixed and recycled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One question about them is still open, since where the highly radiogenic lead in the copper objects of the Shang came from is among the standing enigmas of Bronze Age China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury et al., “Social hierarchy and the choice of metal recycling at Anyang, the last capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-75920-x. [Open access]","Yunbing Zong, Shengkun Yao, Jianfeng Lang, Xuexiang Chen, Jiadong Fan et al., “Structural and compositional analysis of a casting mold sherd from ancient China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 3 (2017): e0174057, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0174057. [Open access]","Bichen Yan, Siran Liu, Matthew L. Chastain, Shugang Yang and Jianli Chen, “A new FTIR method for estimating the firing temperature of ceramic bronze-casting moulds from early China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 3316, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-82806-z. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Ludovica Ottaviano and Silvia Ferrara, “Mechanisms at the core of the Chinese script invention,” <i>Cognitive Linguistics</i> 37, no. 1 (2026): 39–62, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12949599/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Shang_Bronze_Niu_%28Ox%29_Zun%2C_M54_Tomb.jpg/1280px-Shang_Bronze_Niu_%28Ox%29_Zun%2C_M54_Tomb.jpg","title":"Bronze ox zun from tomb M54 at Yinxu","desc":"A wine vessel of the type called a zun, cast in the form of a standing ox with a lidded opening in its back, from tomb M54 at Yinxu in Anyang. Every surface, the horns and hooves included, carries fine relief ornament of spirals and animal masks; the metal has weathered green and reddish brown.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A small bronze vessel shaped as a stocky standing ox with curved horns and a raised knob on the hinged lid set into its back, its whole body covered in dense low-relief scrollwork, lit against a dark museum background."}},
{"id":"wh-249","num":249,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Only one top royal burial at Anyang has been found unrobbed, and it is the tomb of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a queen and one of the consorts of King Wu Ding.","questions":["About 1.6 tonnes (1.8 tons) of metal came out of the tomb of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and her vessels were alloyed from very pure copper where lesser people got recycled stock.","A jade parrot from the tomb of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the first scientific evidence that the Shang burnt jade in the offering the records call the Liao sacrifice."],"answer":"Fu Hao","answerText":"Fu Hao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1045 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Yinxu, Anyang</span></div>","traditional":"婦好","hanzi":"妇好","pinyin":"Fù Hǎo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Fù Hǎo</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Fu Hao</b> was a queen of the Shang and one of the royal consorts of King Wu Ding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is known from inscriptions cast into high-quality bronze ritual vessels, whose names have been matched against the same names in the records on the oracle bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those records are the divinations of the Shang royalty and nobility, and they were found for the most part at Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bones excavated at Huayuanzhuang East belong to Wu Ding's own reign, and of the more than 4,600 signs the corpus uses only about a thousand have been deciphered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her tomb is the only intact top royal burial so far excavated at Anyang, and something like 1.6 tonnes (1.8 tons) of metal came out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is one of only a small handful of tombs at the site with more than a hundred bronzes that were found unrobbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The metal grades its owner: her ritual vessels were alloyed to a controlled recipe from very pure copper, where people of lower rank in the western cemetery had bronze that had been mixed and recycled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vessels were cast in ceramic moulds and cores, the mould-making that made the complex casting methods of early China possible and was the most prominent handicraft industry of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A jade parrot from her tomb has given the first scientific evidence that the Shang burnt jade in the offering called the Liao sacrifice, confirming what the oracle bone inscriptions and later records say of the rite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For all that, she was not the greatest of the Shang dead: the kingly tombs at Xibeigang run to as much as seven times the size of hers, though every one of them was looted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["person","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury et al., “Social hierarchy and the choice of metal recycling at Anyang, the last capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-75920-x. [Open access]","Rong Wang, Chang-sui Wang and Ji-gen Tang, “A jade parrot from the tomb of Fu Hao at Yinxu and Liao sacrifices of the Shang Dynasty,” <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 362 (2018): 368–82, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.220. [Paywalled]","Pengjie Wang, Kaile Zhang, Xinyu Wang, Shengwei Han, Yongge Liu et al., “An open dataset for oracle bone character recognition and decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03807-x. [Open access]","Ke Ding, Siyang Li, Aijun Ding, Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang et al., “Archeological data with AI- and physics-based modeling explain typhoon-induced disasters in inland China around 3000 yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 10 (2026): eaeb1598, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12959396/. [Open access]","Bichen Yan, Siran Liu, Matthew L. Chastain, Shugang Yang and Jianli Chen, “A new FTIR method for estimating the firing temperature of ceramic bronze-casting moulds from early China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 3316, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-82806-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/20260408_Bronze_Rectangular_Ding_with_Kui-Dragon_ShapedFeet_and_Inscription_of_Fu_Hao.jpg/1280px-20260408_Bronze_Rectangular_Ding_with_Kui-Dragon_ShapedFeet_and_Inscription_of_Fu_Hao.jpg","title":"Rectangular ding from the tomb of Fu Hao","desc":"A four-legged bronze cauldron from Fu Hao's tomb at Yinxu, now in the Henan Museum, its feet cast in the form of kui dragons and its sides carrying bands of animal ornament. The museum records it as bearing her name, which is the kind of inscription by which the tomb's occupant was identified.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A deep rectangular bronze vessel, thickly encrusted with green corrosion, standing on four legs shaped as curling dragons, with two upright loop handles rising from its rim and relief ornament across each side, lit in a dark museum case."}},
{"id":"wh-250","num":250,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"The sacrificial pits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Sichuan were packed with ivory laid over bronzes, gold and jade, the leading site of the ancient Shu culture of the Chengdu Plain.","questions":["Carnelian beads from the pits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were cut from stone quarried more than 1,000 km (600 miles) to the north, the earliest such evidence in Bronze Age China.","Residues found in the ash layers at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show that silk was used there as a material carrier for communication between Heaven and Earth."],"answer":"Sanxingdui","answerText":"Sanxingdui","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pit K4</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3,148 – 2,966 BP</span></div>","traditional":"三星堆","hanzi":"三星堆","pinyin":"Sānxīngduī","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Sānxīngduī</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Sanxingdui</b> is a Bronze Age site in Sichuan, in the western suburbs of Guanghan on the south bank of the Yazi river, and it is the leading representative of the ancient Shu culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That culture runs from the late Neolithic to the early Western Zhou, and the pits for which the site is known belong to about 1200 to 1000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The founding population of the Shu came chiefly from the millet and rice farming communities of East Asia, with only limited gene flow from the pastoralists of the western steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The sacrificial pits lie in the core of the site and hold ivory, seashells, bronze, pottery, jade, stone, gold, bone and horn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of them were found in 1986 and six more by 2019, excavated the year after; radiocarbon puts the fourth at 3,148 to 2,966 years before the present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They were filled in a deliberate order, the ivory laid over the bronzes, the gold and the jade beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Layers of ash lie above the artefacts, and silk residues found in them are evidence that silk was used here as a material carrier for communication between Heaven and Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The bronzes tie the place to the rest of Bronze Age China: over half of those found at Sanxingdui carry the same highly radiogenic lead as the vessels of the Central Plains, and it appears in both at virtually the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eleven carnelian beads from the pits were cut from stone that does not come from southern China at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their raw material lies more than 1,000 km (600 miles) to the north, which is the earliest direct geochemical evidence for long-distance carnelian exchange in Bronze Age China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Ruru Chen, Zhenbin Xie, Qing Xiao, Chong Wang and Rui Wen, “Insight into the microbial diversity and community in the sacrificial pits of Sanxingdui site (Sichuan, China),” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 15 (2024): 1489025, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2024.1489025. [Open access]","Hailing Zheng, Ruling Yang, Jianbo Guo, Zhenbin Xie, Jingxuan Liu et al., “Evidence of the use of silk by bronze age civilization for sacrificial purposes in the Yangtze River basin of China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 29175, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-78687-7. [Open access]","Meiting Yan, Jiancheng Liu, Chunlei Qin, Xuemei He, Xiaoguang Li et al., “Sourcing the origins of carnelian in early Chinese civilizations,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 7 (2026): e2524563123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12913004/. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Lisiteng Luo, Yanling Liu, Yalan Liu, Yunhui Liu, Jie Zhong et al., “Paternal genomic history and Y-chromosome phylogeny from the Chengdu Plain reveal multiple Indigenous East Asian farmer contributions to ancient Shu,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 24, no. 1 (2026): 54, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-026-02527-5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Ancient_Bronze_Mask_from_Sanxingdui_2.jpg/1280px-Ancient_Bronze_Mask_from_Sanxingdui_2.jpg","title":"Bronze mask from Sanxingdui","desc":"One of the cast bronze faces from the sacrificial pits at Sanxingdui, now in the museum on the site. The eyes are enormous almonds with the pupils standing proud of them, the nose is a blade, and the ears are broad wings pierced for fixing; the metal has weathered to grey and green.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A large bronze face seen from one side on a museum turntable, with a wide flat brow, a huge almond eye whose pupil projects from the socket, a sharply ridged nose, a long straight mouth and an outsized pointed ear."}},
{"id":"ps-001","num":1,"category":"Psychology","question":"Both a natural and a social science, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> studies mind and behaviour by experiment and measurement, reaching mental processes only through what can be observed or reported.","questions":["Questions about mind and behaviour belonged to philosophy for centuries before Wilhelm Wundt opened the world's first institute for experimental <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Leipzig in 1879.","Much of what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knows rests on Western and largely American samples, and concern about reproducibility has pushed the field towards preregistration, larger samples and open data."],"answer":"psychology","answerText":"psychology","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">As a science</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the late 1800s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First institute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1879, Leipzig</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Psychology</b> is the scientific study of mind and behaviour, and it puts the methods of science to work on questions people have always asked about why they think, feel and act as they do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Behaviour can be watched directly, while mental processes cannot, so they are reached at one remove, through observable behaviour, biological indicators and what people report about themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That double subject matter puts the discipline in two places at once: behaviour is at root biological, which makes it a natural science, and it is shaped by dealings with other people, which makes it a social one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such questions belonged to philosophy until the late 1800s, when psychology came to be accepted as an academic discipline of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conventional marker is Leipzig, where Wilhelm Wundt founded the world's first institute for experimental psychology in 1879, having begun lecturing there four years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The field is many enterprises rather than one: the British Psychological Society recognises nine applied specialisms, among them clinical, forensic, educational, health, occupational and sport psychology, with work running from the living brain to the behaviour of large organisations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What holds them together is method rather than subject matter, since a psychological claim is expected to be put in terms that measurement can bear on and that evidence can count against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Turned on the field's own literature, that standard has proved uncomfortable: nine in ten researchers answering a recent survey in <i>Nature</i> agreed there is a reproducibility crisis, and studies of statistical power persistently find it below, and sometimes well below, 50%.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The remedies now spreading are preregistration of study protocols, registered reports reviewed before the results are known, open data and materials, and funding for replication studies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A second limit is who gets studied, since the literature remains overwhelmingly Western and its data largely American, so for many findings the reach beyond the people they were found in is simply unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","history"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","Wilhelm Wundt Institute for Psychology, “History of Experimental Psychology in Leipzig,” Leipzig University, https://www.lw.uni-leipzig.de/en/wilhelm-wundt-institute-for-psychology/working-groups-1/cognitive-and-biological-psychology/history-of-experimental-psychology-in-leipzig. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]","Michael Muthukrishna et al., “Beyond Western, Educated, Industrial, Rich, and Democratic (WEIRD) Psychology: Measuring and Mapping Scales of Cultural and Psychological Distance,” <i>Psychological Science</i> 31, no. 6 (2020): 678–701, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7357184/. [Open access]","Marcus R. Munafò et al., “A Manifesto for Reproducible Science,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 1 (2017): 0021, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7610724/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Psychologisch_Laboratorium_Heymans.jpg/1920px-Psychologisch_Laboratorium_Heymans.jpg","title":"psychology","desc":"A room in Gerard Heymans's psychological laboratory at Groningen, photographed in 1923. Collection of the University Museum of Groningen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Psychologisch_Laboratorium_Heymans.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a laboratory room: wooden benches carrying a large black acoustic horn and other instruments, a wall panel of switches, dials and gauges, and wires strung across the ceiling."}},
{"id":"ps-002","num":2,"category":"Psychology","question":"Psychology states its own scope as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, naming both what an organism does and the perceiving, remembering and deciding that can only be inferred from it.","questions":["Watson's manifesto of 1913 would have cut the phrase in half, and the cognitive revolution put the missing part back, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a settlement rather than an obvious pairing.","Only the first of the two things named by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be watched, so the evidence for the second is always somebody doing or saying something observable."],"answer":"behaviour and mental processes","answerText":"behaviour and mental processes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Behaviour alone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1913, on Watson's programme</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Both halves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">again from the 1950s</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Behaviour and mental processes</b> is the standard statement of what psychology studies, and the working definitions the discipline gives of itself name both halves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Behaviour is what an organism does that somebody else could watch or record — a key pressed, a sentence spoken, a rat turning left, a pulse quickening — and it is observable in the same way by anyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mental processes are the perceiving, remembering, reasoning, deciding and feeling that behaviour is taken to express, and none of them can be watched at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are reached at one remove, through observable behaviour, biological indicators and what people report about themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That asymmetry is the discipline's standing practical problem: both halves are claimed, and the evidence for the second is always of the first kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The pairing is a settlement rather than an obvious truth, and psychology has redrawn it more than once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Watson's manifesto of 1913 made behaviour the whole of it, opening with the claim that psychology as the behaviourist views it is “a purely objective experimental branch of natural science” whose theoretical goal is the prediction and control of behaviour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Introspection, he wrote, formed no essential part of its methods, and on the same view there was no dividing line between man and other animals, so a law of behaviour could be sought in whatever species showed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behaviourism then dominated experimental psychology for several decades, until by the 1950s work in linguistics, neuroscience and computer science had revived the mind as a subject of scientific inquiry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The break is drawn more sharply in textbooks than it was in the laboratory, since behaviourists had themselves worked on memory, perception, problem solving and learning, and what moved was the emphasis rather than the whole subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","behaviour"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","John B. Watson, “Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It,” <i>Psychological Review</i> 20 (1913): 158–77, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Watson/views.htm. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.2 History of Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-2-history-of-psychology. [Open access]","Charles I. Abramson, “Problems of Teaching the Behaviorist Perspective in the Cognitive Revolution,” <i>Behavioral Sciences</i> 3, no. 1 (2013): 55–71, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4217616/. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-003","num":3,"category":"Psychology","question":"A survey of 174 behavioural biologists found them contradicting one another about what counts as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a plant bending its leaves towards the light among the cases that divided them.","questions":["Defined as the internally coordinated responses of a whole organism to stimuli, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> excludes what is better understood as growth, so a rabbit's thicker winter coat does not count.","Psychology treats a key pressed, a sentence spoken and a quickened pulse alike as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the observable side of its subject and the evidence its other claims finally rest on."],"answer":"behaviour","answerText":"behaviour","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Behaviour</b> is what a whole living organism does: its internally coordinated responses, actions and inactions alike, to stimuli inside or outside itself, excluding responses better understood as developmental change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That definition had to be proposed because none was agreed on, and the survey behind it found 52% of 174 members of three behaviour-focused societies contradicting themselves within one questionnaire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The disagreements are about the edges: whether a plant bending its leaves towards the light behaves, whether unicellular algae swimming up a nutrient gradient do, whether sweating or a heart rate quickened by a nightmare counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Growth is generally excluded, a rabbit's thicker winter coat being development rather than conduct, while a person deciding to do nothing tomorrow if it rains divides opinion because nothing has yet been done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Definitions of this kind are not housekeeping: without one, teaching, research design and questions such as whether plants behave have nothing to settle them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Psychology uses the word for a narrower job, as the publicly observable side of what it studies and the evidence on which its claims about mental processes finally rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anything a person or an animal does that can be watched or recorded will serve, from a key pressed and a sentence spoken to a biological indicator such as a quickened heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before any of it can be counted it has to be pinned down, which is why researchers are told to fix clear criteria for what will be recorded and how it will be classified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Observers are then checked against one another, since anyone closely involved in a project may unconsciously skew what they see towards what they expect to find.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word is not confined to our own species either, and comparative psychology takes the same problems into others, each of which brings its own natural history and behavioural tendencies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","biology"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["Daniel A. Levitis, William Z. Lidicker Jr., and Glenn Freund, “Behavioural Biologists Don't Agree on What Constitutes Behaviour,” <i>Animal Behaviour</i> 78, no. 1 (2009): 103–10, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2760923/. [Open access]","John B. Watson, “Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It,” <i>Psychological Review</i> 20 (1913): 158–77, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Watson/views.htm. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.2 Approaches to Research,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-2-approaches-to-research. [Open access]","Charles I. Abramson, “Why the Study of Comparative Psychology Is Important to Neuroscientists,” <i>Frontiers in Behavioral Neuroscience</i> 16 (2022): 1095033, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9872648/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Phototropism.jpg/1920px-Phototropism.jpg","title":"one of the cases that divides the definition","desc":"A potted orchid leaning towards a window: whether a plant turning to the light is behaving is one of the borderline cases on which behavioural biologists disagree. Tangopaso, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phototropism.jpg","alt":"A potted orchid on a windowsill, its leaves and flower spike leaning out towards the window while its pale aerial roots grow away from it into the room."}},
{"id":"ps-004","num":4,"category":"Psychology","question":"Perceiving, remembering and deciding are each a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and none of them can be watched, which is why psychology gets at them through behaviour, brain measures and what people say.","questions":["Donders subtracted one reaction time from another in 1868 to put a duration on a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is the oldest way of measuring something nobody can see.","Reading a fast answer as one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a slow one as another is a reverse inference, and controlling for strength of preference made the famous timing difference disappear."],"answer":"mental process","answerText":"mental process","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>mental process</b> is something a mind does — perceiving, attending, remembering, reasoning, judging, deciding, feeling — and it is the half of psychology's subject that nobody can watch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cognition is the usual umbrella for the thinking ones, taking in perception, knowledge, problem solving, judgment, language and memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because none of them is open to view, they are reached at one remove, through observable behaviour, biological indicators and what people report about themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Report does not reach all of them either, since not all aspects of cognition are consciously experienced and much of the organising work the brain does goes on without any awareness of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A mental process is therefore an inferred thing rather than an observed one, proposed to account for a pattern in what can actually be measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest instrument for the job is a clock: as far back as 1868 Franciscus Donders subtracted the time taken to respond to one unvarying stimulus from the time taken to respond to one of two events, and read the difference as the time needed to tell them apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mental chronometry has measured the time course of mental operations that way ever since, latterly alongside recordings of brain activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reasoning runs backwards, though, from a measurement to the process presumed to have produced it, and that is the step where it goes wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A much-cited line of work read fast choices as intuitive and slow ones as deliberative: controlling for how strongly people preferred one option removed the timing difference altogether, and altering a game's parameters could replicate, remove or reverse it at will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same caution applies to processes said to run unconsciously, where the absence of awareness is often assumed rather than tested, and where what counts as a proper test differs from one field to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","cognition"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “7.1 What Is Cognition?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/7-1-what-is-cognition. [Open access]","Michael I. Posner, “Timing the Brain: Mental Chronometry as a Tool in Neuroscience,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 3, no. 2 (2005): e51, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC548951/. [Open access]","Ian Krajbich et al., “Rethinking Fast and Slow Based on a Critique of Reaction-Time Reverse Inference,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 6 (2015): 7455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4500827/. [Open access]","Guido Hesselmann and Pieter Moors, “Definitely Maybe: Can Unconscious Processes Perform the Same Functions as Conscious Processes?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 6 (2015): 584, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4421938/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Hipp_Chronoscope%2C_c._1890%2C_view_1_-_Museum_of_Science_and_Industry_%28Chicago%29_-_DSC06506.JPG/1920px-Hipp_Chronoscope%2C_c._1890%2C_view_1_-_Museum_of_Science_and_Industry_%28Chicago%29_-_DSC06506.JPG","title":"a Hipp chronoscope, about 1890","desc":"A Hipp chronoscope of about 1890, the kind of clock with which 19th-century laboratories timed a response. Museum of Science and Industry, Chicago. Daderot, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hipp_Chronoscope,_c._1890,_view_1_-_Museum_of_Science_and_Industry_(Chicago)_-_DSC06506.JPG","alt":"A brass clock movement with two dials under a glass dome, standing on four black and gilt columns above a wooden base, with a driving weight hanging on a chain between them."}},
{"id":"ps-005","num":5,"category":"Psychology","question":"Psychology's claim to be a science rests on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which turns an idea into a falsifiable prediction and then lets evidence rather than authority settle it.","questions":["Studies of statistical power keep finding it below 50%, which is one reason a single result produced by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is weaker evidence than a headline suggests.","Preregistration, registered reports and open data were added to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so that a prediction can be told apart from an explanation invented once the results were in."],"answer":"scientific method","answerText":"scientific method","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>scientific method</b> is the procedure by which a claim about the world is made answerable to evidence, and it is what psychology applies to behaviour and mental processes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Knowledge reached that way is empirical, grounded in objective and tangible evidence that can be observed time and again regardless of who is doing the observing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A theory is a well-developed set of ideas proposing an explanation for observed phenomena, and a hypothesis is a testable prediction about how the world will behave if that idea is right, usually put as an if-then statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has to be falsifiable, which means capable of being shown to be incorrect: a claim that no possible observation could count against is not being tested by any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ideas are then carried into the world and tested, and observations in the world suggest new ideas, the two directions running as deduction and induction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Applied to people the procedure is harder than the diagram suggests, and one study is weaker evidence than it looks: surveys of statistical power persistently find it below, and sometimes well below, 50%.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Low power raises the chance of a false positive as well as a false negative, and it does so most where the effect looked for is small and the field is crowded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sharpest form of that argument holds that most published research findings are false, and it turns on how likely a hypothesis was to be true before the study rather than on the figure reported after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The response has been to make transparency part of the method rather than an optional extra, through preregistered protocols, reports reviewed before the results are known, open data and materials, and funded replication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What that buys is being measured rather than assumed: preregistered psychology studies have larger samples and far more often justify them by a power analysis, but no robust evidence has yet been found that preregistering prevents selective analysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","John P. A. Ioannidis, “Why Most Published Research Findings Are False,” <i>PLoS Medicine</i> 2, no. 8 (2005): e124, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1182327/. [Open access]","Marcus R. Munafò et al., “A Manifesto for Reproducible Science,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 1 (2017): 0021, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7610724/. [Open access]","Olmo R. van den Akker et al., “Preregistration in Practice: A Comparison of Preregistered and Non-preregistered Studies in Psychology,” <i>Behavior Research Methods</i> 56, no. 6 (2024): 5424–33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11335781/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-006","num":6,"category":"Psychology","question":"The computational, algorithmic and implementational tiers, and the four questions about mechanism, development, history and fitness, are both schemes of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and neither ranks one answer above another.","questions":["Because the same function can be carried by more than one neural substrate, a description at one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not a placeholder for one further down.","Asking what a trait is for and asking how its machinery works are questions at different <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so an answer to one leaves the other untouched."],"answer":"levels of analysis","answerText":"levels of analysis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Levels of analysis</b> are the different grains at which one and the same behaviour can be described and explained, and psychology works at several of them at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That span is why the discipline is counted a natural science and a social one together, its subject being biological at root and shaped throughout by dealings with other people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One much-used scheme divides a single explanation into three: a computational level asking what problem is being solved, an algorithmic level asking how it is solved, and an implementational level asking how that is realised in neural hardware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second scheme cuts differently, putting four questions to any trait — its mechanism, its development in the individual, its evolutionary history and its effect on fitness — of which two are proximate and two evolutionary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither scheme ranks its levels, and seeking answers to all four questions expands an explanation rather than replacing any part of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Confusing one level with another is a standing source of bad argument, because a question about what a trait is for is not answered by describing the machinery that carries it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pull is usually downwards, towards the smallest parts available, and the objection to that is practical rather than sentimental: describing a system's parts in isolation is not enough to understand behaviour in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Psychology's own span runs from genetic interaction networks through neural networks to socio-cultural ones, and a mechanism has to be found at each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The levels divide within psychology too, separating broad cognitive domains such as language from task-specific functions such as naming, and both from the elementary processing units beneath them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There is a technical reason they will not collapse together: the same function can be mediated by more than one neural substrate, so surface similarity in what people do may hide different mechanisms underneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Randolph M. Nesse, “Tinbergen's Four Questions: Two Proximate, Two Evolutionary,” <i>Evolution, Medicine, and Public Health</i> 2019, no. 1 (2019): 2, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6343817/. [Open access]","Tobias U. Hauser et al., “Computational Psychiatry of ADHD: Neural Gain Impairments across Marrian Levels of Analysis,” <i>Trends in Neurosciences</i> 39, no. 2 (2016): 63–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4746317/. [Open access]","Francisco J. Parada and Alejandra Rossi, “If Neuroscience Needs Behavior, What Does Psychology Need?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 433, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5883085/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","Morten Overgaard and Jesper Mogensen, “A Framework for the Study of Multiple Realizations: The Importance of Levels of Analysis,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 2 (2011): 79, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3131792/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-007","num":7,"category":"Psychology","question":"Clinical, forensic, educational, health and occupational work are all <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is defined by aiming at a practical problem rather than at knowledge for its own sake.","questions":["A standard set for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asks that the best available research be integrated with clinical expertise, in the context of a patient's characteristics, culture and preferences.","Passport control and criminal investigation pushed face identification into the laboratory, which shows how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and basic work feed each other rather than dividing the discipline in two."],"answer":"applied psychology","answerText":"applied psychology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Applied psychology</b> is the part of the discipline aimed at a practical problem rather than at knowledge for its own sake, and the distinction it draws is about the purpose of a piece of work rather than its subject or its methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standard definitions put it plainly: basic research aims at the advancement of knowledge regardless of any particular use, applied research has a problem-solving rationale oriented towards practical objectives, and experimental development turns research knowledge into new or improved products and services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line between them is not a wall, since many academics pursue both at once and more of them say they prefer applied work than basic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between the two sits use-inspired basic research, where a problem in the world shapes the hypotheses taken into the laboratory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Britain the applied side is organised into nine recognised specialisms, among them clinical, forensic, educational, health, occupational, and sport and exercise psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Where psychologists actually work reflects that balance: about a quarter in universities and a quarter in hospitals and health services, the rest spread across government, business, schools, medical schools and independent practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The traffic runs in both directions, and work on individual differences in face identification was pushed into the laboratory by the demands of passport control and criminal investigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where research meets practice there is a formal standard: evidence-based practice in psychology is defined as the integration of the best available research with clinical expertise, in the context of the patient's characteristics, culture and preferences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether it works that way is argued over, one critique holding the model to be scientocentric rather than genuinely tripartite, since the other two parts are legitimated by appeal to research evidence rather than on ground of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The categories mark what a piece of work is for rather than how good it is, which is why so many researchers sit across them rather than at one end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["J. M. Santos, H. Horta, and H. Luna, “The Relationship between Academics' Strategic Research Agendas and Their Preferences for Basic Research, Applied Research, or Experimental Development,” <i>Scientometrics</i> 127, no. 7 (2022): 4191–4225, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9285191/. [Open access]","Karen Lander, Vicki Bruce, and Markus Bindemann, “Use-Inspired Basic Research on Individual Differences in Face Identification: Implications for Criminal Investigation and Security,” <i>Cognitive Research: Principles and Implications</i> 3 (2018): 26, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6021459/. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.4 Careers in Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-4-careers-in-psychology. [Open access]","Henrik Berg, “Evidence-Based Practice in Psychology Fails to Be Tripartite: A Conceptual Critique of the Scientocentrism in Evidence-Based Practice in Psychology,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 10 (2019): 2253, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6794362/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-008","num":8,"category":"Psychology","question":"With 54 divisions in the American Psychological Association alone, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are named partly for what they study and partly for the settings where their problems arise.","questions":["Cognitive, developmental, social and biological work sit beside clinical, forensic and occupational practice among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which share a method more than they share a subject.","Critics describe the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a colourful picture of different subject matters whose interconnection is tenuous, with no agreement on what the core concepts even mean."],"answer":"subfields of psychology","answerText":"subfields of psychology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>subfields of psychology</b> are the areas the discipline divides into, some named for what they study and some for the settings their problems come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the study side sit biopsychology and evolutionary psychology, sensation and perception, cognitive psychology, developmental psychology, personality psychology and social psychology, each marked out by the question it asks rather than by any method of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the applied side sit clinical and counselling psychology, forensic psychology, health psychology, industrial-organisational psychology, and sport and exercise psychology, each marked out by where its problems arise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lists are national as well as intellectual, and the specialisms recognised by the British professional body number nine, a shorter and more institutional set than the American one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For scale, the American Psychological Association has 54 divisions representing a wide variety of specialties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Where psychologists actually work follows the applied side more than the academic one, with about a quarter in universities, a quarter in hospitals and health services, and the rest spread across government, business, schools, medical schools and independent practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the divisions do not mark is a set of separate methods, since the common factor across the branches is a shared method and methodology rather than a shared subject matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One account puts that sharply, calling psychology a very colourful picture of different subject matters whose interconnectedness is often rather tenuous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The complaint is an old one and has not gone away: fragmentation and isolation within the discipline persist, and there is no consensus about the content and definition of its core concepts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not everyone holds that a single foundation is the remedy, and one argument is that psychology needs foundations held reflectively and continually renewed rather than fixed ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.3 Contemporary Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-3-contemporary-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.4 Careers in Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-4-careers-in-psychology. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]","Mariann Märtsin, “Psychology: A Discipline in Need of Reflective Foundations,” <i>Integrative Psychological and Behavioral Science</i> 54, no. 3 (2020): 694–700, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7263803/. [Open access]","Roland Mayrhofer and Fabian Hutmacher, “The Principle of Inversion: Why the Quantitative-Empirical Paradigm Cannot Serve as a Unifying Basis for Psychology as an Academic Discipline,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 596425, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7750523/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-009","num":9,"category":"Psychology","question":"Comte ranked disciplines by the complexity of their subject in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with physics at the top, the social sciences at the bottom and biology in between.","questions":["A test of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counted positive results in 2,434 papers and found the odds about 2.3 times higher in the social sciences than in the physical.","Bibliometric work puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> down to how far a field can reach consensus as its subject grows complex, rather than to how carefully its people work."],"answer":"hierarchy of the sciences","answerText":"hierarchy of the sciences","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>hierarchy of the sciences</b> is an old proposal, due to Auguste Comte, that disciplines can be ordered by the complexity of their subject and the precision of the knowledge they reach, with the physical sciences at the top, the social sciences at the bottom and the biological sciences in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology is awkward to place on it, being counted a natural science and a social science at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ordering has been tested rather than merely asserted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One study read 2,434 papers that explicitly tested a hypothesis and counted how often the result came out supporting it, finding the odds of a positive result about 2.3 times higher in the social sciences than in the physical ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology and psychiatry did not sit where the ranking predicts but at its extreme, with roughly five times the odds of a positive result of space science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A second study measured nearly 29,000 papers on properties such as the number of authors, the age of the references, the proportion of monographs cited and the use of first-person pronouns, and found the biological sciences taking intermediate values between the physical and the social.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the pattern reflects, on that account, is how far a field can reach consensus and accumulate knowledge, which falls as its subject grows more complex and less controllable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is worth holding on to, because the popular form of the ranking sorts fields into hard and soft ones and is usually meant as a verdict on how carefully their people work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Psychology's best-known trouble is not its own either, since studies of statistical power persistently find it below, and sometimes well below, 50%, across time and across the different disciplines examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its neighbours pull it both ways at once, its span running from genetic interaction networks through neural networks to socio-cultural ones, with the parts described in isolation never enough to account for behaviour in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"sources":["Daniele Fanelli, “‘Positive' Results Increase Down the Hierarchy of the Sciences,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 5, no. 4 (2010): e10068, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2850928/. [Open access]","Daniele Fanelli and Wolfgang Glänzel, “Bibliometric Evidence for a Hierarchy of the Sciences,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 8, no. 6 (2013): e66938, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3694152/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","Francisco J. Parada and Alejandra Rossi, “If Neuroscience Needs Behavior, What Does Psychology Need?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 433, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5883085/. [Open access]","Marcus R. Munafò et al., “A Manifesto for Reproducible Science,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 1 (2017): 0021, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7610724/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-010","num":10,"category":"Psychology","question":"Psychology's results look obvious to a reader who has just met them, an effect of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lifts the judged likelihood of an outcome as soon as it is known.","questions":["After the 2014 Taiwan mayoral elections, 88% of participants recalled forecasts closer to the result than the ones they had actually made, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> measured in the field.","Radiologists who had seen a clear mammogram spotted the same abnormality in a degraded copy better than colleagues who had not, showing that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaches perception too."],"answer":"hindsight bias","answerText":"hindsight bias","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hindsight bias</b> is the tendency to treat an outcome as having been likely once it is known, so that judgements of probability made in hindsight exceed the ones made in foresight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is measured in two ways: in a hypothetical design one group answers in ignorance while another answers knowing the outcome and instructed to set that knowledge aside, and in a memory design people give a judgement, learn what happened, and then try to recall what they first said.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The recalled judgement moves towards the outcome, and the standard models agree that knowing it alters the memory itself rather than merely the report of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The size of the shift is not fixed, and one experiment found it larger the more information had been encoded beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The alternative to testing such judgements is to trust them, which leaves intuition, other people's authority and blind luck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Outside the laboratory the effect is easy to find: asked after the 2014 mayoral elections in Taiwan to recall their own forecasts, 88% of participants remembered predicting something closer to the result than they actually had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It reaches seeing as well as remembering, since radiologists shown a clear mammogram first were afterwards more accurate at finding the same abnormality in a heavily degraded copy than colleagues who had not seen it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That bears directly on any judgement made after the event, because something hard to make out at the time looks plain to anyone who already knows it is there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For a discipline whose findings are about people the consequence is sharp, since the same reader who could not have predicted a result will judge it obvious once told.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This is an argument for the method rather than against common sense: a claim earns its place by surviving a prediction made in advance, not by sounding right afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","cognition"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Karolin Salmen, Florian K. G. Ermark, and Klaus Fiedler, “Pragmatic, Constructive, and Reconstructive Memory Influences on the Hindsight Bias,” <i>Psychonomic Bulletin and Review</i> 30, no. 1 (2023): 331–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9971109/. [Open access]","K. Kaida and N. Kaida, “Memory Load of Information Encoded Amplifies the Magnitude of Hindsight Bias,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 18, no. 4 (2023): e0283969, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10085031/. [Open access]","Hayden M. Schill, Samantha M. Gray, and Timothy F. Brady, “Visual Hindsight Bias for Abnormal Mammograms in Radiologists,” <i>Journal of Medical Imaging</i> 10, suppl. 1 (2023): S11910, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10190961/. [Open access]","Y. H. Chen et al., “Can Knowledge of Election Results Change Recall of Our Predictions? Neural Correlates of Political Hindsight Bias,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 14, no. 10 (2019): e0220690, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6786518/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-011","num":11,"category":"Psychology","question":"The question of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now treated as badly put, since inheritance sets boundaries and experience decides where inside them a person falls.","questions":["Parents supply both the DNA and the rearing home, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are so hard to tell apart, and why 22% of the variance in household chaos proved heritable.","Body mass is strongly heritable within a population while the rise in obesity is put down to a changed environment, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refusing to separate."],"answer":"nature and nurture","answerText":"nature and nurture","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nature and nurture</b> names the question of how far differences between people come from what they inherit and how far from what happens to them, and the modern answer is that the question was badly put.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genes do not exist in a vacuum, and each person is a unique interaction between a genetic makeup and an environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two ideas do the work the dichotomy could not: a range of reaction, in which inheritance sets boundaries and experience decides where inside them somebody falls, and genetic-environmental correlation, in which genes influence the environment a person meets while that environment influences which genes are expressed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Epigenetics adds that one genotype can be expressed in different ways and lead to very different outcomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Separating the two is hard for a plain reason: parents are the source of both the DNA and the rearing environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That makes an apparently environmental finding hard to trust, since the influence of a parent's behaviour on a child cannot be known without controlling for the DNA the two share.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measures of the environment turn out to be partly heritable themselves, and in one study 22% of the variance in household chaos was attributable to genetic factors, with 37% of its association with school achievement down to shared genes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The traffic does not all run that way, and an adoption design that removes the shared-genes explanation still found hostile parenting carrying the effect of conflict between parents onto children's behaviour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Family-based quasi-experimental methods combined with genomic data now allow genetic and environmental confounds to be controlled without being measured directly, though no one design removes them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Obesity shows why the old question misleads, body mass being strongly heritable within a population while the rise in obesity over recent decades is put down to a changed environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","genetics"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “3.1 Human Genetics,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/3-1-human-genetics. [Open access]","Sara A. Hart, Callie Little, and Elsje van Bergen, “Nurture Might Be Nature: Cautionary Tales and Proposed Solutions,” <i>npj Science of Learning</i> 6 (2021): 2, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7794571/. [Open access]","Gordon T. Harold et al., “The Nature of Nurture: Disentangling Passive Genotype-Environment Correlation from Family Relationship Influences on Children's Externalizing Problems,” <i>Journal of Family Psychology</i> 27, no. 1 (2013): 12–21, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3576129/. [Open access]","Tom A. McAdams, Rosa Cheesman, and Yasmin I. Ahmadzadeh, “Annual Research Review: Towards a Deeper Understanding of Nature and Nurture: Combining Family-Based Quasi-experimental Methods with Genomic Data,” <i>Journal of Child Psychology and Psychiatry</i> 64, no. 4 (2023): 693–707, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10952916/. [Open access]","Sarah E. Jackson, Clare H. Llewellyn, and Lee Smith, “The Obesity Epidemic — Nature via Nurture: A Narrative Review of High-Income Countries,” <i>SAGE Open Medicine</i> 8 (2020): 2050312120918265, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7222649/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-012","num":12,"category":"Psychology","question":"Psychology looks for the causes of behaviour, which commits it to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the claim that a decision is produced by what went before it.","questions":["Reading the readiness potential as the brain deciding before its owner knows made it the standard laboratory evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, until the potential was reinterpreted.","Across 5,268 people in 20 countries, blame was assigned whatever the scenario said about causation, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not by itself remove responsibility in most participants' judgement."],"answer":"determinism","answerText":"determinism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Determinism</b> is the claim that decisions are causally determined by what went before them, or determined for all practical purposes, and it is the position a science that looks for the causes of behaviour starts from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The free will set against it is not the feeling of choosing but a stronger claim: that some decisions are both undetermined and genuinely the causes of what follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sharper worry is not determinism at all but epiphenomenalism, the possibility that decisions are not the causes of actions in the first place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Psychology's contribution has been a measurement rather than an argument, and it is the readiness potential, a slow negative electrical potential recorded from the scalp that builds in the run-up to a voluntary movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Timed against the moment people report having decided, that report falls only about 200 milliseconds before the movement and hundreds of milliseconds after the potential has begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The result was read as showing the brain committing to a movement before its owner knows of it, which is how a laboratory measurement entered a philosophical argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reinterpretation is that the potential reflects ongoing stochastic fluctuations in neural activity rather than a decision being taken, and that its apparent onset does not correspond to a physiologically real event at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What people themselves believe has also been measured, and belief in free will turns out to be predicted better by belief in a non-physical mind than by any view about causation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether determinism is felt to excuse anything varies by place: across 5,268 people in 20 countries responsibility was ascribed regardless, though Western participants separated the deterministic case from a comparison one where most Asian participants did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The practical fear that telling people their choices are caused will make them behave worse has not held up well either, one study finding fewer shocks administered after such a message rather than more, on a sample of only 40.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","philosophy"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Aaron Schurger et al., “What Is the Readiness Potential?,” <i>Trends in Cognitive Sciences</i> 25, no. 7 (2021): 558–70, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8192467/. [Open access]","Mark Balaguer, “Free Will, Determinism, and Epiphenomenalism,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 2623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6333639/. [Open access]","Emilie A. Caspar et al., “The Influence of (Dis)belief in Free Will on Immoral Behavior,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 8 (2017): 20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5239816/. [Open access]","David Wisniewski, Robert Deutschländer, and John-Dylan Haynes, “Free Will Beliefs Are Better Predicted by Dualism than Determinism Beliefs across Different Cultures,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 14, no. 9 (2019): e0221617, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6738589/. [Open access]","Ivar R. Hannikainen et al., “For Whom Does Determinism Undermine Moral Responsibility? Surveying the Conditions for Free Will across Cultures,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 10 (2019): 2428, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848273/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-013","num":13,"category":"Psychology","question":"Explaining a whole by taking it apart and accounting for it through its components is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the strategy behind much of modern medicine.","questions":["A simulated 6502 chip running Donkey Kong was analysed by the standard methods of neuroscience, and the failure to recover how it works is the sharpest case against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yet made.","Emergence is the objection to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since novel properties arise in self-organising systems and the whole becomes not merely more but very different from its parts."],"answer":"reductionism","answerText":"reductionism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Reductionism</b> is the strategy of explaining something by taking it apart and accounting for it through its components, and that divide-and-conquer approach has been responsible for tremendous successes in modern medicine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it promises is that the properties of a whole follow from the properties of its parts, so that knowing the parts amounts to knowing the thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Emergence is the standing objection: novel and coherent structures, patterns and properties arise during self-organisation in complex systems, and the whole becomes not merely more but very different from the sum of its parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second obstacle is formal rather than philosophical, since the same function can be realised by more than one underlying substrate, so an account at one level does not translate cleanly into one below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of this cancels the record: reduction identified specific disease agents and produced treatments aimed at them, so the argument is about limits rather than about worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its sharpest demonstration came from treating a microprocessor as though it were a brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standard methods of neuroscience were applied to a fully simulated MOS 6502 running Donkey Kong, Space Invaders and Pitfall, and the structure they recovered looked impressive while getting nowhere near an understanding of how the processor really works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lesioning single transistors picked out game-specific effects, although transistors implement simple functions such as adders rather than anything game-specific, and some transistors showed strong tuning to screen brightness despite having no direct relation to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The general moral is that where parts interact in a nonlinear way, information about each of them separately is insufficient to explain the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The response has not been to abandon the parts but to study how they combine, since which social behaviour an animal produces depends jointly on the identity of the other animal and on states such as hunger, which no circuit examined alone would reveal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","philosophy"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Eric Jonas and Konrad Paul Kording, “Could a Neuroscientist Understand a Microprocessor?,” <i>PLoS Computational Biology</i> 13, no. 1 (2017): e1005268, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5230747/. [Open access]","Michael P. W. Grocott, “Integrative Physiology and Systems Biology: Reductionism, Emergence and Causality,” <i>Extreme Physiology and Medicine</i> 2, no. 1 (2013): 9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3710203/. [Open access]","Andrew C. Ahn et al., “The Limits of Reductionism in Medicine: Could Systems Biology Offer an Alternative?,” <i>PLoS Medicine</i> 3, no. 6 (2006): e208, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1459480/. [Open access]","Morten Overgaard and Jesper Mogensen, “A Framework for the Study of Multiple Realizations: The Importance of Levels of Analysis,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 2 (2011): 79, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3131792/. [Open access]","Sarah Y. Dickinson et al., “From Reductionism toward Integration: Understanding How Social Behavior Emerges from Integrated Circuits,” <i>Frontiers in Integrative Neuroscience</i> 16 (2022): 862437, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9010670/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-014","num":14,"category":"Psychology","question":"How mental states relate to physical ones is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and psychology meets it as a working difficulty rather than as an abstraction.","questions":["Dualism, monism and emergentism are the three answers usually given to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and surveys find most people taking the middle one.","Psychiatry meets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a dilemma, since claiming an independent mental sphere leads to substance dualism while reducing it to the physical loses the word mental."],"answer":"mind-body problem","answerText":"mind-body problem","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>mind-body problem</b> is the question of how mental states stand to physical ones, and psychology meets it as a working difficulty rather than as an abstraction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three answers are usually distinguished: dualism, on which minds are in principle independent of the bodies they are only temporarily attached to; monism, on which the mind is the same thing as the brain; and emergentism, on which the mind is based on the brain and is more than just its activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked which they hold, people mostly take the middle position, and in a survey of 850 volunteers emergentism scored highest and dualism lowest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A smaller study using a different instrument found the same shape, with 53 of 94 participants classed as non-reductive materialists against 32 reductive materialists and 9 dualists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both samples were European, so that distribution is a fact about those participants rather than about people at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The scientific form of the problem is the explanatory gap, and explaining how neuronal activity gives rise to cognition is arguably the outstanding challenge of cognitive neuroscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One recent proposal holds a physicalist line by treating cognitive states as causally consistent abstractions of neuronal states rather than as a second set of things acting on them, on the model of temperature as an abstraction of molecular motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The question bites hardest in psychiatry, which faces a dilemma: save the notion of mental disorder by claiming an independent sphere of the mental and end in substance dualism, or reduce it to the physical and lose the word mental altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One way out is to treat the mental and the somatic as phenomenologically different but complementary aspects of one substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The stakes are practical rather than academic, since dualistic thinking can limit the therapeutic interventions offered to patients with major psychiatric disorders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","philosophy"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Tapani Riekki, Marjaana Lindeman, and Jari Lipsanen, “Conceptions about the Mind-Body Problem and Their Relations to Afterlife Beliefs, Paranormal Beliefs, Religiosity, and Ontological Confusions,” <i>Advances in Cognitive Psychology</i> 9, no. 3 (2013): 112–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4158462/. [Open access]","Marek Havlík et al., “Do Personality Features Influence Our Intuitions of the Mind-Body Problem? A Pilot Study,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 1219, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6062648/. [Open access]","Moritz Grosse-Wentrup et al., “Neuro-cognitive Multilevel Causal Modeling: A Framework That Bridges the Explanatory Gap between Neuronal Activity and Cognition,” <i>PLoS Computational Biology</i> 20, no. 12 (2024): e1012674, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11717354/. [Open access]","Michael Jungert, “Mental Realities: The Concept of Mental Disorder and the Mind-Body Problem,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 4 (2013): 809, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3817358/. [Open access]","Walter Glannon, “Mind-Brain Dualism in Psychiatry: Ethical Implications,” <i>Frontiers in Psychiatry</i> 11 (2020): 85, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7063027/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-015","num":15,"category":"Psychology","question":"Examining one's own conscious experience as objectively as possible is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the founding method of experimental psychology and the first one it discarded.","questions":["Training did not save <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which stayed highly subjective with very little agreement between individuals, and psychology turned to what could be watched instead.","A beeper worn through an ordinary day, cueing a report of inner experience at that exact moment, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rebuilt to avoid the errors of remembering."],"answer":"introspection","answerText":"introspection","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Introspection</b> is the examination of one's own conscious experience as objectively as possible, and it was the founding method of experimental psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wundt used it experimentally, with instruments timing responses to stimuli, and Titchener built structuralism on the contents it reported, attending to what mental processes contain rather than what they are for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It failed on a practical point rather than a philosophical one: despite efforts to train people in the process, it remained highly subjective and there was very little agreement between individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It never left entirely, since what people report about themselves is still one of the routes by which psychology reaches a mental process, beside observable behaviour and biological indicators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The case for taking it seriously again is that first-person and third-person enquiry are complementary rather than competing, and that phenomena of a primarily first-person nature cannot be explored while bypassing what first-person enquiry uncovers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Descriptive experience sampling is the most developed of the disciplined versions: a randomised beeper worn through an ordinary day cues the wearer to attend to their inner experience at the precise moment it sounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About six samples are collected over some three hours and gone through in an interview within a day, and the cycle is repeated several times over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What that design is trying to avoid is retrospection, which mixes experience together with beliefs about the self, beliefs about what experience ought to be like, and plain misremembering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Accuracy can also be measured directly, and participants asked to estimate their own reaction times reproduced both a task-switching cost and a memory-search cost in their estimates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those estimates were compressed rather than mistaken, running at 45 milliseconds against 76 measured for the switch and 40 against 81 for the larger memory set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Ulrich W. Weger, Johannes Wagemann, and Christian Tewes, “Editorial: The Challenges and Opportunities of Introspection in Psychology: Theory and Method,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 10 (2019): 2196, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6794359/. [Open access]","Leiszle R. Lapping-Carr and Christopher L. Heavey, “Pristine Inner Experience and Descriptive Experience Sampling: Implications for Psychology,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 8 (2017): 2170, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5733077/. [Open access]","Daniel Bratzke and Donna Bryce, “Timing of Internal Processes: Investigating Introspection about the Costs of Task Switching and Memory Search,” <i>Attention, Perception, and Psychophysics</i> 84, no. 5 (2022): 1501–8, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9232406/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.2 History of Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-2-history-of-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-016","num":16,"category":"Psychology","question":"The demand that a finding not depend on who produced it is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and in psychology it is enforced by procedures rather than by good intentions.","questions":["One brain-imaging dataset run through 6,912 pipelines gave maps in which no voxel was significant throughout, which is a hard limit on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in analysis.","Fixing criteria before recording and comparing what different observers saw are the everyday guards on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the second of them measured as inter-rater reliability."],"answer":"objectivity","answerText":"objectivity","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Objectivity</b> in science is the demand that a finding should not depend on who produced it, and scientific knowledge is called empirical because it is grounded in evidence that can be observed time and again regardless of who is observing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology's difficulty is that most of its evidence passes through a person before it becomes data, whether a participant reporting or a researcher deciding what to write down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The named risk is observer bias, since people closely involved in a research project may unconsciously skew their observations to fit their goals or expectations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The everyday guards against it are procedural: fix clear criteria in advance for the behaviours to be recorded and how they will be classified, then compare observations of the same event made by different people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That comparison has a name and a number, inter-rater reliability being the measure of consistency between observers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Those guards discipline the person watching, and a larger discretion sits further downstream, in the analysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One brain-imaging dataset was put through 6,912 analysis pipelines and five thresholding methods, giving 34,560 significance maps, and no voxel came out significant across all of them while 90.3% were significant in at least some.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The conclusion drawn was that with enough flexibility a researcher determined to find activation in practically any brain region will very likely succeed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blinding is the obvious remedy, though its value is harder to demonstrate than expected, since across 142 meta-analyses and 1,153 trials no evidence was found of an average difference in estimated treatment effect between blinded and unblinded ones, on intervals wide enough to admit a considerable difference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Objectivity is better understood as a set of procedures than as a state of mind, and it does not require excluding first-person evidence, since first-person and third-person enquiry are complementary rather than competing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.2 Approaches to Research,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-2-approaches-to-research. [Open access]","Joshua Carp, “On the Plurality of (Methodological) Worlds: Estimating the Analytic Flexibility of fMRI Experiments,” <i>Frontiers in Neuroscience</i> 6 (2012): 149, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3468892/. [Open access]","Helene Moustgaard et al., “Impact of Blinding on Estimated Treatment Effects in Randomised Clinical Trials: Meta-epidemiological Study,” <i>BMJ</i> 368 (2020): l6802, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7190062/. [Open access]","Ulrich W. Weger, Johannes Wagemann, and Christian Tewes, “Editorial: The Challenges and Opportunities of Introspection in Psychology: Theory and Method,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 10 (2019): 2196, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6794359/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-017","num":17,"category":"Psychology","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a well-developed set of ideas explaining observed phenomena, and the hypothesis drawn from it must be capable of being shown incorrect.","questions":["Psychology's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crisis is that they come and go with little cumulative progress, being neither decisively refuted nor accepted as established knowledge.","Ego depletion looked well supported and then measured near zero across many laboratories, which is the shortage of robust phenomena that starves <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of anything to explain."],"answer":"theory","answerText":"theory","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>theory</b> in science is a well-developed set of ideas proposing an explanation for observed phenomena, and a hypothesis is drawn from it as a testable prediction of how the world will behave if the idea is right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prediction has to be falsifiable, capable of being shown to be incorrect, or there is nothing for the evidence to bear on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology has no shortage of theories, being influenced by all of its historical perspectives at once, and on one account its branches are held together by a shared method and methodology rather than by anything they jointly say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What it is short of is theories that accumulate, and the complaint now has a name: theories come and go with little cumulative progress, neither decisively refuted nor accepted as established knowledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is the theory crisis, and it is diagnosed as three problems rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The first is a shortage of robust phenomena, the stable and reproducible effects that constrain theorising in more settled sciences, with ego depletion as the example: well supported at first, then found near zero in multi-laboratory work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second is construct validity, since most psychological constructs have never been properly validated and reliability tends to be measured in place of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third is causation, because interventions on psychological variables are fat-handed and change several things at once, which leaves what caused what hard to establish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The remedy recommended is phenomenon-driven work and patient construct validation rather than mathematical formalisation on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stakes reach past psychology, and biologists have begun citing its crisis as a warning, since testing a precise hypothesis drawn from a misapplied or misunderstood theory is a form of research waste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Laura F. Bringmann, “The Theory Crisis in Psychology: How to Move Forward,” <i>Perspectives on Psychological Science</i> 16, no. 4 (2021): 779–88, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8273366/. [Open access]","Shinichi Nakagawa et al., “Poor Hypotheses and Research Waste in Biology: Learning from a Theory Crisis in Psychology,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11792729/. [Open access]","Roland Mayrhofer and Fabian Hutmacher, “The Principle of Inversion: Why the Quantitative-Empirical Paradigm Cannot Serve as a Unifying Basis for Psychology as an Academic Discipline,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 596425, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7750523/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.3 Contemporary Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-3-contemporary-psychology. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-018","num":18,"category":"Psychology","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a testable prediction of how the world will behave if an idea is right, often put as an if-then statement.","questions":["Exploratory work generates a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and confirmatory work tests one, the first using small flexible experiments and the second rigid pre-specified designs.","When experts scored ten dimensions of quality in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, none of the ten reached even moderate agreement at first, and only half did after refinement."],"answer":"hypothesis","answerText":"hypothesis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>hypothesis</b> is a testable prediction about how the world will behave if an idea is right, and it is often worded as an if-then statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is drawn from a theory, a well-developed set of ideas proposing an explanation for observed phenomena, and it has to be falsifiable, which means capable of being shown to be incorrect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ideas are carried into the world and tested while observations in the world suggest new ideas, so a hypothesis is both what deduction tests and what induction produces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not every study is testing one, and the working distinction is between exploratory and confirmatory research.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Exploratory studies are small and flexible, with hypotheses loosely articulated or evolving across experiments, where confirmatory studies use rigid pre-specified designs and hypotheses stated in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Treating the first as though it were the second is the common error, since exploratory work buys efficiency with smaller samples and less fastidious designs and so favours catching possibilities over ruling out false ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What makes one hypothesis better than another has been broken into ten dimensions, among them validity, significance, novelty, feasibility, testability and clarity, with a short form of validity, significance and feasibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Judging them turns out to be harder than stating them, and in a validation of those metrics none of the ten reached even moderate agreement between expert raters at first, with half doing so after the raters were calibrated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A prediction is also only as good as what it came from, and testing a precise hypothesis drawn from a misapplied or misunderstood theory is a form of research waste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In psychology that bites harder than elsewhere, because most of its constructs have never been properly validated, so the terms a hypothesis is stated in may not pick out anything stable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","Jonathan Kimmelman, Jeffrey S. Mogil, and Ulrich Dirnagl, “Distinguishing between Exploratory and Confirmatory Preclinical Research Will Improve Translation,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 12, no. 5 (2014): e1001863, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028181/. [Open access]","Xia Jing et al., “Development, Validation, and Usage of Metrics to Evaluate the Quality of Clinical Research Hypotheses,” <i>BMC Medical Research Methodology</i> 25 (2025): 11, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11737058/. [Open access]","Shinichi Nakagawa et al., “Poor Hypotheses and Research Waste in Biology: Learning from a Theory Crisis in Psychology,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11792729/. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Laura F. Bringmann, “The Theory Crisis in Psychology: How to Move Forward,” <i>Perspectives on Psychological Science</i> 16, no. 4 (2021): 779–88, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8273366/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-019","num":19,"category":"Psychology","question":"A precise description of a variable, saying exactly how and what is measured, is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and without one nobody else can repeat the experiment.","questions":["Calling two different phenomena by one name, or one phenomenon by two, was blamed a century ago on a vague link between theory and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a study actually uses.","Running 84 specifications of one question about smartphones and parenting turned the association positive in some of them, which shows how much rests on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chosen."],"answer":"operational definition","answerText":"operational definition","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"An <b>operational definition</b> is a precise description of a variable, stating exactly how and what a researcher measures in a particular experiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is what allows other people to interpret the data and to repeat the experiment if they choose to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In observational work it takes the form of criteria fixed before anything is recorded, saying which behaviours count and how they are to be classified, so that what different observers write down can be compared at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The choice is rarely forced, and one question can usually be operationalised in a good many defensible ways, none of them obviously the right one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which one is taken is not a detail of wording, because two studies using the same word may not be measuring the same thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That failure has two names, both over a century old: the jingle fallacy, in which two or more distinct phenomena are labelled with the same term, and the jangle fallacy, in which different terms are used for one phenomenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their originators attributed both to a vague connection between psychological theory and its operationalisation in empirical studies, and they are still treated as a significant threat to the validity of research.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The modern way of showing what such choices are worth is to make all of them, and one study of smartphone use and parenting ran 84 analytic specifications of the same question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The associations came out weak and mixed, and in some specifications more smartphone use went with better parenting rather than worse, which is the point of running the whole set, since a result that depends on one very specific combination of choices is fragile rather than established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Underneath both difficulties lies the same gap, since most psychological constructs have never been properly validated and most articles reviewed reported no validity evidence whatsoever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.3 Analyzing Findings,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-3-analyzing-findings. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.2 Approaches to Research,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-2-approaches-to-research. [Open access]","Barbara Hanfstingl et al., “Detecting Jingle and Jangle Fallacies by Identifying Consistencies and Variabilities in Study Specifications: A Call for Research,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 15 (2024): 1404060, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11393684/. [Open access]","Kathryn L. Modecki et al., “Tuning into the Real Effect of Smartphone Use on Parenting: A Multiverse Analysis,” <i>Journal of Child Psychology and Psychiatry</i> 61, no. 8 (2020): 855–65, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7496662/. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Laura F. Bringmann, “The Theory Crisis in Psychology: How to Move Forward,” <i>Perspectives on Psychological Science</i> 16, no. 4 (2021): 779–88, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8273366/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-020","num":20,"category":"Psychology","question":"Nine job titles are protected in British law while the bare word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not, so the label can be used by someone the regulator has never heard of.","questions":["A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a psychiatrist may both conduct therapy, but only the second holds a medical degree and may prescribe medication.","Most European countries regulate the practice of clinical psychology but not neuropsychology, so what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is entitled to do depends on which country is asking."],"answer":"psychologist","answerText":"psychologist","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"Who counts as a <b>psychologist</b> is a legal question as much as an academic one, and the answer depends on the country and on what the person actually does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the United Kingdom the health regulator protects nine titles under the heading of practitioner psychologists: practitioner psychologist and registered psychologist, together with the clinical, forensic, counselling, health, educational, occupational, and sport and exercise varieties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bare word is not among them, so somebody may lawfully call themselves a psychologist while being unable to call themselves a clinical one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of the nine are approved as part of the regulatory order but are not titles a registrant can register under on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beside the regulated professions the professional body recognises a wider psychological workforce in practitioner and applied roles that are not regulated by law, and calls the growth of that workforce integral to the future of the health service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the United States the gate is licensure, and only those who pass the examination may call themselves licensed clinical or counselling psychologists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is also what separates them from psychiatrists, who hold a doctor of medicine: both may conduct therapy and counselling, psychiatrists may prescribe medication, and licensed clinical psychologists may administer and interpret psychological tests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Regulation is uneven across Europe too, since most countries regulate the practice of clinical psychology but not of neuropsychology, and the definition of a clinical neuropsychologist varies from country to country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The European certificate intended to standardise such specialisms exists so far only for psychotherapy and for work and organisational psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this reaches most of the discipline, since about a quarter of psychologists work in universities and much of the field — biopsychology, cognition, development, personality, social psychology — is research rather than practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","institution"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["Health and Care Professions Council, “The Professions We Regulate: Practitioner Psychologists,” https://www.hcpc-uk.org/about-us/who-we-regulate/the-professions/. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.4 Careers in Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-4-careers-in-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.3 Contemporary Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-3-contemporary-psychology. [Open access]","Laura Hokkanen et al., “Clinical Neuropsychology as a Specialist Profession in European Health Care: Developing a Benchmark for Training Standards and Competencies Using the Europsy Model?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 559134, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7573555/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-021","num":21,"category":"Psychology","question":"Hume held that the mind's whole creative power is compounding and transposing what the senses afford, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its boldest form, the thesis that experience is our only source of ideas.","questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knowledge in a given domain can be gained, if at all, only by experience, which is divided into the outward senses and the inward awareness of our own mental operations.","Asked where basic perceptual abilities come from, 1,188 people including professional scientists appealed to learning rather than innateness, which suggests an intuitive <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about the origins of human knowledge."],"answer":"empiricism","answerText":"empiricism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Empiricism</b> is the thesis that, for a given subject, we have no source of knowledge or of the concepts we use other than experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The experience meant is of two kinds: sense experience, through the outward senses, which yields knowledge of external objects, and reflective experience, the conscious awareness of our own mental operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The thesis is always relative to a subject area, so one may be an empiricist about the physical sciences and a rationalist about mathematics, and the two conflict only when formulated to cover the same ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It promises no knowledge, saying only that knowledge can be gained, if at all, by experience; Hume concluded on those terms that causation is never actually known, but presupposed by a habit of the mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hume put the positive claim starkly: all the materials of thinking are derived from our outward or inward sentiment, and the mind's whole creative power amounts to compounding, transposing, augmenting or diminishing what the senses afford.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In psychology the word now travels chiefly in a methodological sense: scientific knowledge is empirical in that it rests on objective, tangible evidence that can be observed repeatedly whoever is doing the observing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On that understanding facts are established only by evidence collected through research, and theories and hypotheses earn their standing by being tested against observation, which suggests further ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Quantitative-empirical methods dominate the sciences and psychology alike, and the founders of the academic discipline in the 19th century were already predominantly experimental in orientation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That dominance is argued about, one critique holding that the quantitative-empirical method cannot serve as a unifying basis for the discipline because it misses important dimensions of human nature, while granting that it has revealed a great deal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The older doctrine has an experimental afterlife too, since 1,188 people asked where basic perceptual and cognitive abilities come from, professional psychologists and neuroscientists among them, appealed to learning over innateness and dated those abilities far later than they in fact emerge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","philosophy","science"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Peter Markie and M. Folescu, “Rationalism vs. Empiricism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2023/entries/rationalism-empiricism/. [Open access]","David Hume, <i>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</i>, ed. L. A. Selby-Bigge, sec. 2, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9662/pg9662.txt. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","Roland Mayrhofer and Fabian Hutmacher, “The Principle of Inversion: Why the Quantitative-Empirical Paradigm Cannot Serve as a Unifying Basis for Psychology as an Academic Discipline,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 596425, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7750523/. [Open access]","Jinjing Jenny Wang and Lisa Feigenson, “Is Empiricism Innate? Preference for Nurture Over Nature in People's Beliefs About the Origins of Human Knowledge,” <i>Open Mind</i> 3 (2019): 89–100, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8412204/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-022","num":22,"category":"Psychology","question":"The three theses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are that some propositions are knowable by intuition alone, that we have innate knowledge of certain truths, and that some concepts belong to our rational nature.","questions":["Holding that experience may trigger our awareness of a truth without ever supplying it is the mark of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which treats reason as a source that the senses cannot match.","Inquiry looks impossible if we either know the answer already or would not recognise it, a paradox answered by the doctrine of recollection, an early form of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Greek thought."],"answer":"rationalism","answerText":"rationalism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Rationalism</b> is the view that, in some subject area, reason supplies knowledge or concepts that sense experience could not have provided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three theses are essential to it: that some propositions are knowable by intuition alone and others by deduction from them, intuition being a direct insight in which we simply see a proposition to be true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second, the innate knowledge thesis, holds that we know some truths as part of our own nature; experience may trigger awareness of that knowledge but does not provide it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The third, the innate concept thesis, makes some of the concepts we use part of that same nature, so experience neither supplies them nor settles what they contain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To count as a rationalist is to adopt at least one innate thesis; most add that the senses could not have supplied what reason gives and that what it gives is superior, though neither addition is required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> An early version is the doctrine of recollection in Plato's <i>Meno</i>, answering the paradox that a man can inquire neither into what he knows, having no need, nor into what he does not, not knowing what to seek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Descartes reduced the method to four precepts: accept nothing not clearly and distinctly known, divide each difficulty into as many parts as needed, ascend from the simplest to the most complex, and enumerate so completely that nothing is omitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Superiority was argued two ways: Descartes held that what is intuited is certain beyond doubt while sense experience is always somewhat uncertain, and Plato located the advantage in the objects known, which are unchanging and eternal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology defined itself against that inheritance, becoming its own discipline in the late 1800s by adopting a method of observation and experiment rather than one resting on logical argument or previous authorities alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What it left behind is a question rather than an answer — how much structure the mind brings to experience — which still drives much research in psychology and neuroscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","philosophy","science","psychology"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Peter Markie and M. Folescu, “Rationalism vs. Empiricism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2023/entries/rationalism-empiricism/. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Meno</i>, trans. Benjamin Jowett, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1643/pg1643.txt. [Open access]","René Descartes, <i>Discourse on the Method of Rightly Conducting One's Reason and of Seeking Truth in the Sciences</i>, trans. John Veitch, pt. 2, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/59/pg59.txt. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","Jinjing Jenny Wang and Lisa Feigenson, “Is Empiricism Innate? Preference for Nurture Over Nature in People's Beliefs About the Origins of Human Knowledge,” <i>Open Mind</i> 3 (2019): 89–100, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8412204/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-023","num":23,"category":"Psychology","question":"The claim that pairs of thoughts become linked by an organism's past experience, so that calling one to mind brings the other with it, is the core of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in all its forms.","questions":["Resemblance, contiguity in time or place, and cause or effect were counted as the three principles of connection among ideas by an early statement of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 18th century.","The gradually rising learning curve that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> predicts turns out to be an artefact of averaging, since individual animals show an abrupt, step-like rise from untrained to trained responding."],"answer":"associationism","answerText":"associationism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Associationism</b> connects learning to thought through an organism's causal history: pairs of thoughts become associated by past experience, so that bringing one to mind brings the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a single theory but a constellation of separable theses, serving as accounts of learning, of thinking, of mental structure, and of how thought is implemented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What ties them together is a commitment to a certain arationality of thought: states are associated because of the history that caused them, not because of any rational relation between what they mean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Locke is usually credited with the doctrine taking hold, though what he wrote was an account of unreasonableness — a connection of ideas owing to chance or custom rather than nature, which he called a degree of madness found in most men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hume gave the first serious treatment, counting three principles of connection among ideas — resemblance, contiguity in time or place, and cause or effect — while admitting the list could not easily be proved complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> James recast the business as physiology, insisting that association holds between objects thought of rather than between ideas, and that there is no elementary causal law of association but the law of neural habit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On his statement of it, when two elementary brain processes have been active together or in succession, one on recurring tends to propagate its excitement into the other, so contiguity, similarity and contrast are derivatives of one deeper fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The doctrine has been the engine behind empiricism for centuries, running from the British empiricists through the behaviourists to today's connectionists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its account of learning is challenged where it is most exposed: the gradually rising learning curve is an artefact of averaging across subjects, individual animals showing an abrupt, often step-like rise from untrained to trained responding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has proved durable all the same, since reinforcement learning formalises the same problem: how an agent comes to select beneficial actions by associating stimuli and responses with their consequences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","philosophy","science"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Eric Mandelbaum and Raphaël Millière, “Associationist Theories of Thought,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Fall 2025 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2025/entries/associationist-thought/. [Open access]","John Locke, <i>An Essay Concerning Human Understanding</i>, bk. 2, chap. 33, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/10615/10615-h/10615-h.htm. [Open access]","David Hume, <i>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</i>, ed. L. A. Selby-Bigge, sec. 3, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9662/pg9662.txt. [Open access]","William James, <i>The Principles of Psychology</i>, vol. 1, chap. 14, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57628/pg57628.txt. [Open access]","C. R. Gallistel, Stephen Fairhurst, and Peter Balsam, “The Learning Curve: Implications of a Quantitative Analysis,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 101, no. 36 (2004): 13124–31, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC516535/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-024","num":24,"category":"Psychology","question":"Locke asked his readers to suppose the mind to be white paper, void of all characters, which is the figure now remembered as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in psychology and philosophy alike.","questions":["Leibniz granted that nothing is in the intellect that was not first in the senses, then excepted the intellect itself, which is the standard reply to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a theory of mind.","The doctrine that the mind brings no ideas into the world and is furnished entirely by experience is known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though its author still allowed the mind faculties of its own."],"answer":"blank slate","answerText":"blank slate","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>blank slate</b> is the doctrine that the mind brings no ideas or principles into the world with it and is furnished entirely by experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Locke's own figure is not a slate but white paper: he asks us to suppose the mind void of all characters and without any ideas, then answers in one word how it comes to be furnished, from experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Experience has two fountains for him, sensation and the mind's reflection on its own operations, and the Latin tag now attached to his name appears nowhere in the Essay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His first book attacks innate principles directly: universal consent would prove nothing even if it existed, since agreement can be reached another way, and children cannot make sense of the maxims they are supposed to have been born knowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dilemma he presses is that innateness is either a real alternative to his account and plainly false, or trivially true and no threat to it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The polemic is nearly a sideshow, since the prize is won only by a positive account of knowledge built from experience, and that account gives the mind faculties to work on its ideas, so the slate is blank of ideas rather than of powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Leibniz replied with an exception to the axiom: nothing is in the intellect that was not first in the senses, except the intellect itself, which supplies being, substance, unity, identity and cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He added that the exception partly agreed with the Essay itself, which already made the mind's reflection on its own nature a source of ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Locke put the doctrine to work on children, holding that nine parts in ten of the men we meet are what they are by their education, and that the almost insensible impressions of infancy have lasting consequences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The intuition reaches well beyond philosophy, since American adults, adults from a culture believing in reincarnation, children and professional scientists alike explain basic perceptual and cognitive abilities by learning rather than innateness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","philosophy","science"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["John Locke, <i>An Essay Concerning Human Understanding</i>, bks. 1–2, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/10615/10615-h/10615-h.htm. [Open access]","Jerry Samet, “The Historical Controversies Surrounding Innateness,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Summer 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2024/entries/innateness-history/. [Open access]","Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, <i>New Essays Concerning Human Understanding</i>, trans. Alfred Gideon Langley, bk. 2, chap. 1, https://archive.org/stream/newessaysconcern00leibuoft/newessaysconcern00leibuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","John Locke, <i>Some Thoughts Concerning Education</i>, sec. 1, https://archive.org/stream/somethoughtsconc00lockuoft/somethoughtsconc00lockuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Jinjing Jenny Wang and Lisa Feigenson, “Is Empiricism Innate? Preference for Nurture Over Nature in People's Beliefs About the Origins of Human Knowledge,” <i>Open Mind</i> 3 (2019): 89–100, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8412204/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-025","num":25,"category":"Psychology","question":"Minds and bodies being fundamentally distinct kinds of substance, one characterised by thought and the other by extension, is the position known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the philosophy of mind.","questions":["Elisabeth of Bohemia objected that existing accounts tied causal power to extension, which left <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unable to say how an unextended mind could move a body at all.","A doctrine claiming that the mind would continue to be all that it is even if the body were not is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it made interaction the hardest question of all."],"answer":"substance dualism","answerText":"substance dualism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Substance dualism</b> is the view that minds and bodies are fundamentally distinct kinds of substance, the mind characterised by thought and the body by extension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A substance in that sense stands in need of nothing beyond itself in order to exist, and each is known through a single principal attribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Descartes reached it by doubt: he could suppose he had no body and no world, but not that he did not exist, and so was a substance whose whole essence consists only in thinking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that reasoning the mind is wholly distinct from the body, more easily known than it, and would continue to be all that it is even if the body were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two shifts came with it: dualism was joined to a new mechanistic conception of nature, and the argument for the mind's immateriality moved from the intellect to consciousness, taken to include sensory awareness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is in that mechanistic setting that interaction became the leading objection, since two radically different kinds of thing seem to lack what they would need in common to act on one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elisabeth of Bohemia pressed the point first, in a correspondence she began in 1643, and she framed it exactly: existing accounts tied causal power to extension, so how could an unextended mind move a body?<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She asked about the mind acting on the body rather than the body on the mind, and rejected the reply appealing to the Scholastic notion of heaviness as unintelligible and inconsistent with the mechanist account of nature he had himself argued for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The doctrine held the field regardless: in 1949 it could still be called the official doctrine to which most philosophers, psychologists and religious teachers subscribed with minor reservation, and only then did the consensus evaporate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It survives as an entry point to the subject in undergraduate teaching, where the thinking thing and the extended thing are introduced and then set against simple bodily illusions demonstrating a contrary account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","philosophy","psychology","science"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Howard Robinson and Ralph Weir, “Dualism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/. [Open access]","René Descartes, <i>Discourse on the Method of Rightly Conducting One's Reason and of Seeking Truth in the Sciences</i>, trans. John Veitch, pt. 4, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/59/pg59.txt. [Open access]","René Descartes, <i>Selections from the Principles of Philosophy</i>, trans. John Veitch, pt. 1, secs. 51–53, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4391/pg4391.txt. [Open access]","Lisa Shapiro, “Elisabeth, Princess of Bohemia,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Fall 2021 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2021/entries/elisabeth-bohemia/. [Open access]","Scott Hamilton and Trevor J. Hamilton, “Pedagogical Tools to Explore Cartesian Mind-Body Dualism in the Classroom: Philosophical Arguments and Neuroscience Illusions,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 6 (2015): 1155, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4530257/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-026","num":26,"category":"Psychology","question":"The thesis that everything is physical, including whatever at first glance looks biological or moral or mathematical, goes under the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the philosophy of mind.","questions":["Sensible qualities being nothing in the object but so many motions of matter pressing on the sense organs is a 17th-century statement of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> applied to perception.","Every event with a cause having a physical cause, together with mental events causing physical ones, is the argument from causal closure for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a thesis about mind."],"answer":"materialism","answerText":"materialism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Materialism</b> is the thesis that everything is physical, a claim about the nature of the world rather than about how it should be described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does not deny that the world holds items seeming otherwise — biological, psychological, moral, mathematical — but insists these are in the end physical, or bear an important relation to the physical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word is old, entering English late in the 17th century; physicalism was coined in the 1930s by the Vienna Circle and meant at first a thesis about language, every statement being synonymous with a physical one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two are now often used interchangeably, though the second is the safer word, since one may be a materialist about mind and still hold that physics refers to entities not happily called material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ancestry is traced to Democritus and the claim that all is atoms and the void; the standard 19th-century history opens by saying materialism is as old as philosophy but not older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Hobbes gave it a psychology: the qualities we call sensible are, in the object causing them, so many motions of matter pressing on the organs, and sense is nothing but the appearance motion produces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A century later a French physician concluded boldly that man is a machine, that the universe holds a single substance differently modified, and that the soul is an empty word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The 20th-century version is the identity theory, on which experiences just are brain processes rather than merely correlated with them; it dates from the 1950s and was named, apparently first, by a psychologist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The argument most often given runs from causal closure: every event with a cause has a physical cause, mental events do cause physical ones, and one effect may not have two independent causes, so mental events must be physical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The honest caveat comes from within: an overwhelmingly materialist culture sets a lower standard of argument for the thesis than against it, and dualism is still the second most popular answer among professional philosophers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","philosophy","psychology","science"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Daniel Stoljar, “Physicalism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2024/entries/physicalism/. [Open access]","J. J. C. Smart, “The Mind/Brain Identity Theory,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2022 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2022/entries/mind-identity/. [Open access]","Thomas Hobbes, <i>Leviathan</i>, pt. 1, chap. 1, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3207/pg3207.txt. [Open access]","Julien Offray de La Mettrie, <i>Man a Machine</i>, trans. Gertrude Carman Bussey, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/52090/pg52090.txt. [Open access]","Howard Robinson and Ralph Weir, “Dualism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-027","num":27,"category":"Psychology","question":"Holding that a body of concepts or knowledge is part of an individual's initial state, needing no input from the environment, is the commitment that defines <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a position.","questions":["One catalogue found at least six meanings bundled into the key word of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them present at birth, adapted over evolution, and shared by all members of a species.","The claim that instincts arise by the slow accumulation of slight profitable variations gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an evolutionary footing while leaving its central term as vague as it had been."],"answer":"nativism","answerText":"nativism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nativism</b> holds that some body of concepts or knowledge is part of an individual's initial state, or develops from that state without needing input from the environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In scientific psychology innate and instinctive have named the same cluster of properties, instinct being a now antiquated word for an innate psychological trait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Darwin set the modern terms: no complex instinct could be produced except by the slow accumulation of numerous slight profitable variations, and instincts are not always perfect and are liable to mistakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The trouble is that the key word carries six meanings at once: present at birth, a behavioural difference caused by a genetic one, adapted over evolution, unchanging through development, shared by all of a species, and not learned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The objection is not the ambiguity but the equivocation it invites, since a trait called innate for being present at birth is then treated as innate in the sense of insensitive to the environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Psychologists have long warned of it, Morgan observing in 1896 that terms in his field are used in different senses by different authors; one count puts the properties bundled into innateness at 26.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A second complaint is that innateness claims draw attention from the many subtle ways the environment contributes to development, an objection raised by biologists, psychologists and philosophers alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A psychological explanation has been offered for its staying power: innateness is a cornerstone of folk biology, and people across cultures assume each species has an essence determining its appearance and behaviour, holding an essence placeholder even when they can say nothing about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tendency is measurable rather than merely deliberative, since participants shown genetic concepts categorise faster when genes and fate are paired than when the two are kept apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Attempts to rescue a usable notion define it as genetic specification, invariance across developmental environments, or canalisation against disturbance, while eliminativists would drop the distinction; what is undisputed is that the everyday concept is out of step with recent biology and psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","philosophy","science"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Mark Cain, “Innateness and Contemporary Theories of Cognition,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/innateness-cognition/. [Open access]","Paul Griffiths and Stefan Linquist, “The Distinction Between Innate and Acquired Characteristics,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2025 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2025/entries/innate-acquired/. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i>, 6th ed., chap. 8, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2009/pg2009.txt. [Open access]","C. Lloyd Morgan, <i>Habit and Instinct</i>, https://archive.org/stream/habitandinstinc00morggoog/habitandinstinc00morggoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Wren A. Gould and Steven J. Heine, “Implicit Essentialism: Genetic Concepts Are Implicitly Associated with Fate Concepts,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 7, no. 6 (2012): e38176, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3369887/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-028","num":28,"category":"Psychology","question":"Insisting that inner observation alters what it observes and that time has only one dimension, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> denied that empirical psychology could ever be a natural science proper.","questions":["Only cognition whose certainty is apodictic counted as science for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which left chemistry a systematic art and psychology a natural description of the soul.","The doctrine that space and time are forms of sensibility, taken from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and given a naturalistic reading, sent 19th-century physiologists to study how spatial representation arises."],"answer":"Immanuel Kant","answerText":"Immanuel Kant","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 April 1724</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Königsberg, East Prussia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 February 1804</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Immanuel Kant</b> is the central figure in modern philosophy, a synthesiser of early modern rationalism and empiricism who set the terms for much of the two centuries that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fundamental idea of his critical philosophy is human autonomy, and his central claim about nature is that the human understanding is itself the source of the general laws that structure all our experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That claim bears directly on psychology, because he also held a demanding view of what a science is: only cognition whose certainty is apodictic deserves the name, and everything resting on merely empirical grounds is called science by courtesy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that measure chemistry was a systematic art rather than a science, since no law of the approach and retreat of the parts of matter could be presented in advance of experience and made mathematical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Empirical psychology he placed further still from that rank, and gave three reasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The first is that mathematics cannot reach the phenomena of inner sense, whose pure intuition is time, and time has only one dimension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second is that the manifold of internal observation can be separated only in thought and cannot be kept apart and recombined at will, so the discipline cannot even manage chemistry’s experimental trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third is the sharpest: another thinking subject is not amenable to investigation of this kind, and the observation itself alters and distorts the state of the object observed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What was left, on this verdict, was a natural description of the soul rather than a science of it, and later readers have split over whether the obstacle was the impossibility of mathematising inner sense or the absence of genuine laws that prediction and experiment could confirm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The irony is that the doctrine which did shape the new discipline was the other one, since treating space and time as forms of sensibility that need not be innate sent physiologists and psychologists to investigate empirically how spatial representation arises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["person","philosophy","psychology","science"],"difficulty":1,"sources":["Michael Rohlf, “Immanuel Kant,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Fall 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2024/entries/kant/. [Open access]","Immanuel Kant, <i>Metaphysical Foundations of Natural Science</i>, preface, trans. Ernest Belfort Bax, https://archive.org/stream/prolegomenametap00kantuoft/prolegomenametap00kantuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Eric Watkins and Marius Stan, “Kant’s Philosophy of Science,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Fall 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/kant-science/. [Open access]","Andrew Brook and Julian Wuerth, “Kant’s View of the Mind and Consciousness of Self,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2023/entries/kant-mind/. [Open access]","Immanuel Kant, <i>The Critique of Pure Reason</i>, trans. J. M. D. Meiklejohn, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Immanuel_Kant_by_Johann_Christoph_Frisch.jpg","title":"Immanuel Kant","desc":"Painting by Johann Christoph Frisch, a copy after the portrait by Johann Gottlieb Becker. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Immanuel_Kant_by_Johann_Christoph_Frisch.jpg","alt":"A half-length painted portrait of a slight, elderly man in a powdered wig and dark coat, turned slightly to one side against a plain dark ground"}},
{"id":"ps-029","num":29,"category":"Psychology","question":"Reading character from the shape of the head, on the theory that the brain is a set of organs whose size shows in the skull, was the business of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 19th century.","questions":["An MRI study of 5,724 people found that scalp curvature predicted no lifestyle measure at all, which is the most rigorous test <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has ever been given.","The founder counted 27 organs of the brain and his collaborator 35, a disagreement that did little to slow <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on its way across Europe."],"answer":"phrenology","answerText":"phrenology","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First presented</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1796, on the Continent</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Britain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1814</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Phrenology</b> professed to be a philosophy of the human mind, taking the brain as the organ of mind, the brain as a set of distinct organs, and the size of each organ as something legible in the shape of the head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was first presented to public consideration on the Continent in 1796 and in Britain in 1814.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gall counted 27 functional organs and Spurzheim later declared 35, and the two lectured across Europe to theatres filled to the brim, one explaining the findings while the other dissected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They differed about what the bumps meant, Gall holding that a certain bulge in the cranium made a man a murderer or a thief, and his collaborator insisting that God could never create anything innately bad and that people made themselves into murderers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The claims were specific enough to be embarrassing: those of a mirthful disposition were to show two prominent bumps on the forehead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The system carried the prejudices of its century in its own apparatus, the standard British textbook indexing skulls by nation and race alongside those of named murderers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Derivatives outlived it, a Belgian priest founding psychognomy from phrenology together with craniofacial measurement, physiognomy and graphology, and a Brazilian institute devoted to it staying active until the early 1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The central claim was eventually tested rather than dismissed, using structural MRI from 5,724 people to measure local scalp curvature and matching 23 lifestyle measures to phrenological faculties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The result was no evidence for it: brain folding explained very little of the variance in scalp curvature, and the head-shape regressions produced no statistically significant or meaningful effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives of the enterprise is not the reading of bumps but the anatomy done alongside it, and the bare idea underneath the system that mental functions belong to particular parts of the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["theory","psychology","science","history"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["O. Parker Jones, F. Alfaro-Almagro, and S. Jbabdi, “An Empirical, 21st Century Evaluation of Phrenology,” <i>Cortex</i> 106 (2018): 26–35, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6143440/. [Open access]","Mohammad Bilal, Bryan Edwards, Marios Loukas, Rod J. Oskouian, and R. Shane Tubbs, “Johann Gaspar Spurzheim: A Life Dedicated to Phrenology,” <i>Cureus</i> 9, no. 5 (2017): e1295, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5493471/. [Open access]","J. G. Spurzheim, <i>The Physiognomical System of Drs. Gall and Spurzheim</i>, https://archive.org/stream/b24925020/b24925020_djvu.txt. [Open access]","George Combe, <i>A System of Phrenology</i>, https://archive.org/stream/systemofphrenolo00comb/systemofphrenolo00comb_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles André, “Paul Bouts and the Short Life of Psychognomy in Brazil,” <i>Arquivos de Neuro-Psiquiatria</i> 80, no. 8 (2022): 862–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703887/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/Numbered_phrenological_head%2C_1825_Wellcome_L0008290.jpg","title":"Numbered phrenological head, 1825","desc":"Front, back and side views of a head divided into numbered organs, from a work associated with Johann Gaspar Spurzheim. Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Numbered_phrenological_head,_1825_Wellcome_L0008290.jpg","alt":"Three engraved views of a bald human head, front, back and in profile, its surface divided into small numbered compartments covering the whole skull"}},
{"id":"ps-030","num":30,"category":"Psychology","question":"The claim that a particular mental function depends on a particular part of the brain is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the argument over it is older than agreement that the mind is in the head.","questions":["Removing a portion of the cerebral hemispheres without abolishing intelligence, and finding that losing one sensation lost them all, was 19th-century evidence against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its strict form.","Because one behavioural outcome can be reached by different neurocognitive systems, degeneracy leaves <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without any area that is both necessary and sufficient for a function."],"answer":"localisation of function","answerText":"localisation of function","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Localisation of function</b> is the claim that a particular mental function depends on a particular part of the brain, and the argument is older than agreement that the mind is in the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Egyptians placed the higher functions in the heart and Greek scholars stayed undecided for centuries; once the head won, a second argument opened over whether they lay in the ventricles or in the nervous tissue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tissue prevailed, and the 19th century made what remained an experimental question by removing parts of animal brains and watching what was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Flourens reported that a fairly extensive portion of the cerebral hemispheres could be cut away, front, back, above or side, without intelligence being lost, so a restricted portion sufficed for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As removal went further intelligence weakened and died out, and once one sensation was lost all were, from which he concluded that the faculties have no separate seats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ninety years later Lashley reached a comparable verdict from mazes and lesions, naming equipotentiality: the capacity of any intact part of an area to do what destruction of the whole removes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He hedged it carefully: it is not absolute, probably applies only to the association areas and to functions more complex than simple sensitivity, and is bounded by a law of mass action under which performance falls with the extent of injury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The lesion method was long the gold standard for showing a region necessary to a function and activation studies for showing one sufficient, so together they were expected to settle it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That hope was given up in the face of degeneracy, since one behavioural outcome can be reached by different neurocognitive systems, which leaves it doubtful that any area is both necessary and sufficient for a function.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What replaced the search for seats is the mapping of large-scale distributed networks: a language network answers to meaningful sentences and not to non-words, yet spreads across frontal, temporal and cingulate cortex beside other networks of the same shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Eliasz Engelhardt, “Cerebral Localization of the Mind and Higher Functions: The Beginnings,” <i>Dementia &amp; Neuropsychologia</i> 12, no. 3 (2018): 321–25, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6200162/. [Open access]","Pierre Flourens, <i>Examen de la phrénologie</i>, https://archive.org/stream/examendelaphrno00flougoog/examendelaphrno00flougoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Karl S. Lashley, <i>Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence</i>, https://archive.org/stream/brainmechanismsi00lash/brainmechanismsi00lash_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Sarah Genon, Andrew Reid, Robert Langner, Katrin Amunts, and Simon B. Eickhoff, “How to Characterize the Function of a Brain Region,” <i>Trends in Cognitive Sciences</i> 22, no. 4 (2018): 350–64, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7978486/. [Open access]","Rodrigo M. Braga, Lauren M. DiNicola, Hannah C. Becker, and Randy L. Buckner, “Situating the Left-Lateralized Language Network in the Broader Organization of Multiple Specialized Large-Scale Distributed Networks,” <i>Journal of Neurophysiology</i> 124, no. 5 (2020): 1415–48, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8356783/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Campbell_AW.Histological_studies_on_the_localisation_of_cerebral_function_Plate_1.jpg","title":"A cortical map of 1905","desc":"Plate 1 of Alfred Walter Campbell, Histological Studies on the Localisation of Cerebral Function, Cambridge University Press, 1905. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campbell_AW.Histological_studies_on_the_localisation_of_cerebral_function_Plate_1.jpg","alt":"Two line drawings of a human brain, one from the side and one cut down the middle, with the cortex divided into areas marked by different hatchings and stipplings and labelled prefrontal, frontal, precentral, postcentral, parietal, temporal, limbic, olfactory, visuo-sensory, visuo-psychic, audito-sensory and audito-psychic"}},
{"id":"ps-031","num":31,"category":"Psychology","question":"The posterior part of the third frontal convolution of the left hemisphere, argued in the 1860s to be the seat of articulate language, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it gave localisation its first solid case.","questions":["A patient with no paralysis of the articulatory muscles, who understood speech and could write but could not coordinate speech, was the evidence that made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most famous region in the brain.","Imaging of the preserved brain of that first patient showed damage reaching well beyond <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, into connections projecting to areas far from the lesion itself."],"answer":"Broca's area","answerText":"Broca's area","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Broca's area</b> is the posterior part of the third frontal convolution of the left hemisphere, named for the surgeon who argued in the 1860s that it is the seat of articulate language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The symptom he defined was aphémie, the loss or alteration of the faculty of articulate language in a person with no paralysis of the muscles of articulation, who understands what is said and can make himself understood by gesture or in writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such a patient has ideas and knows how to express them, having lost only the coordinating faculty of speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At autopsy of such cases a very evident lesion was found almost constantly in the posterior half of the third frontal convolution, sometimes confined to it, and about nineteen times in twenty on the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He drew the general moral himself: it is enough for one intellectual faculty to stand in special relation to a circumscribed part of the brain for the localisation of the others to become very probable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The priority was not his alone, since Dax had connected speech to the left hemisphere decades earlier, and the long period between Willis and 1861 was full of contributory work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He expected exceptions too, writing that the rule would probably suffer a few rare ones in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The brain of his first patient was preserved rather than sectioned, and imaging much later showed the damage extending well beyond the convolution that carries his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tractography applied to that lesion found disruption reaching connections which project to areas distant from it, so the classic case was never the clean local injury the textbooks made of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The view of language circuitry has since shifted from a few cortical centres to a distributed and dynamically interconnected system, and the classical modular model, still useful as a first approach to the aphasias, is no longer thought to describe the networks language actually uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["place","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Paul Broca, <i>Exposé des titres et travaux scientifiques</i>, https://archive.org/stream/BIUSante_110133x002x20/BIUSante_110133x002x20_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Eliasz Engelhardt, “Cerebral Localization of Higher Functions: The Period between Thomas Willis and Paul Broca,” <i>Dementia &amp; Neuropsychologia</i> 13, no. 2 (2019): 238–43, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6601299/. [Open access]","Michel Thiebaut de Schotten et al., “From Phineas Gage and Monsieur Leborgne to H.M.: Revisiting Disconnection Syndromes,” <i>Cerebral Cortex</i> 25, no. 12 (2015): 4812–27, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4635921/. [Open access]","Grigorios Nasios, Efthymios Dardiotis, and Lambros Messinis, “From Broca and Wernicke to the Neuromodulation Era: Insights of Brain Language Networks for Neurorehabilitation,” <i>Behavioural Neurology</i> 2019 (2019): 9894571, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6679886/. [Open access]","Carmen Barba et al., “Beyond Broca and Wernicke: Epilepsy Surgery in the Language Areas,” <i>Epileptic Disorders</i> 28, no. 3 (2026): 581–601, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13276694/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cerveau_de_Louis_Victor_Leborgne_dit_Tantan_1.jpg/1280px-Cerveau_de_Louis_Victor_Leborgne_dit_Tantan_1.jpg","title":"The brain of Louis Victor Leborgne","desc":"The preserved brain of Broca's first aphasic patient, deposited by Broca at the Musee Dupuytren in 1861. Photograph by Selbymay, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cerveau_de_Louis_Victor_Leborgne_dit_Tantan_1.jpg","alt":"A human brain preserved in fluid in a stoppered glass jar, its convolutions pale and slumped, standing behind a handwritten museum label and a black and white ten centimetre scale bar"}},
{"id":"ps-032","num":32,"category":"Psychology","question":"The posterior superior temporal gyrus of the left hemisphere, associated with understanding language rather than producing it, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the classical account of the aphasias.","questions":["Fluent speech without sense, together with a failure to follow what is said, is the syndrome that follows damage to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than to the frontal speech region.","Interrupting the arcuate fasciculus was predicted in 1874 to give a third aphasia, distinct from damage to the frontal speech region and from damage to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> itself."],"answer":"Wernicke's area","answerText":"Wernicke's area","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wernicke's area</b> lies in the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the left hemisphere and is associated with understanding language rather than producing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was identified in 1874, when a second kind of aphasia demanded an account: patients who spoke fluently but without sense and did not follow what was said to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What he offered was not a second centre bolted onto the first but a model, in which the sensory and the motor images of speech sit in different places and are joined by fibres running through the white matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third form of aphasia followed from it as a prediction, Leitungsaphasie or conduction aphasia, produced not by destroying either centre but by interrupting the tracts that join the frontal lobe to the occipito-temporal lobe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He named the tract that would have to be cut, the arcuate bundle of Burdach, and worked out what a deaf-mute with each of the three forms would look like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The programme was connectionist from the beginning, and he went on to sponsor the first white-matter atlas of the occipital lobe and to write its prologue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tract has held up: in a cohort of people with chronic aphasia after left-hemisphere stroke, the integrity of the arcuate fasciculus bore on spontaneous speech, word retrieval, repetition and comprehension alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The map has not held up as well, since four decades of neuroimaging have complemented and often challenged the classical views, and the notion of a motor speech centre in particular has been entirely transformed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Comprehension is now described as the integration of auditory and visual input with semantic and syntactic information, drawing on connectivity that reaches well past a single gyrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What lasted is the shape of the argument rather than the chart: that a symptom can be predicted from the anatomy of connections, and that damage between centres matters as much as damage to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["place","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Carl Wernicke, <i>Der aphasische Symptomencomplex</i>, https://archive.org/stream/b24763445/b24763445_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Pascale Tremblay and Simona M. Brambati, “A Historical Perspective on the Neurobiology of Speech and Language: From the 19th Century to the Present,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 15 (2024): 1420133, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11445075/. [Open access]","Pinaki D. Wani, “From Sound to Meaning: Navigating Wernicke’s Area in Language Processing,” <i>Cureus</i> 16, no. 9 (2024): e69833, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11491986/. [Open access]","Maria V. Ivanova, Allison Zhong, A. Turken, Juliana V. Baldo, and Nina F. Dronkers, “Functional Contributions of the Arcuate Fasciculus to Language Processing,” <i>Frontiers in Human Neuroscience</i> 15 (2021): 672665, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8267805/. [Open access]","Stephanie J. Forkel, Sajedha Mahmood, Francesco Vergani, and Marco Catani, “The White Matter of the Human Cerebrum: Part I. The Occipital Lobe by Heinrich Sachs,” <i>Cortex</i> 62 (2015): 182–202, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4298656/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-033","num":33,"category":"Psychology","question":"A nerve carrying not the quality of what stimulates it but a quality of its own is the doctrine of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, argued from electricity producing a different sensation in each sense.","questions":["Pressure on the eye raising a spot of light, and on the ear a humming, was the evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the older view that a nerve is a passive conductor.","Labelled-line coding in sensory neuroscience is the surviving descendant of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the 19th-century claim that we feel the state of our nerves rather than the world."],"answer":"specific nerve energies","answerText":"specific nerve energies","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The doctrine of <b>specific nerve energies</b> holds that a sensory nerve carries not the quality of what stimulates it but a quality of its own, so that we sense the state of the nerve rather than the state of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument was empirical: electricity applied to any organ of sense produces a sensation, but a different one in each — light in one nerve, sound in another, taste in a third, pain in a fourth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mechanical pressure does the same, raising a luminous spectrum in one nerve, a humming in another and pain in a third, which told against the older view of a nerve as a passive conductor tuned to certain qualities of bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conclusion was that each nerve of sense has powers of its own which the exciting cause merely renders manifest: we do not feel the knife that hurts us but the painful state of our nerves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A consequence was drawn at once: blind people cannot recognise colours with their fingers, and the doctrine explains why they cannot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its author was the leading physiologist of his generation, teaching physiology as a comprehensive theory of life and opposing the speculative natural philosophy of his day with an empirical idealism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The doctrine survives in sensory neuroscience as labelled-line coding, the idea that a distinct population of fibres carries a distinct quality, still argued modality by modality rather than settled in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where it has given ground is on the tissue itself, since several visual cortical areas develop without visual experience and handle their content whatever sense delivers it, a property called supramodality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His consequence about blindness has given ground too: congenitally blind adults using a device that turns images into sound have passed the World Health Organization blindness threshold on a standard acuity chart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What remains is the half that never depended on the nerve: what a sensation is like is fixed by the machinery it runs through, not by the stimulus that started it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["theory","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"sources":["Johannes Müller, <i>Elements of Physiology</i>, trans. William Baly, bk. 3, sec. 4, https://archive.org/stream/Muller1838na12J-a/Muller1838na12J-a_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Ulrich Zeller and Ingmar Werneburg, “A Life Dedicated to Research and Ideal: Johannes Müller between Empirical Universality and Idealistic Vitalism Mirrored in Lecture Notes from 1851,” <i>Theory in Biosciences</i> 143, no. 3 (2024): 161–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11347478/. [Open access]","Omar Páez, Pedro Segura-Chama, Angélica Almanza, Francisco Pellicer, and Francisco Mercado, “Properties and Differential Expression of H+ Receptors in Dorsal Root Ganglia: Is a Labeled-Line Coding for Acid Nociception Possible?,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 12 (2021): 733267, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8576393/. [Open access]","Luca Cecchetti, Ron Kupers, Maurice Ptito, Pietro Pietrini, and Emiliano Ricciardi, “Are Supramodality and Cross-Modal Plasticity the Yin and Yang of Brain Development? From Blindness to Rehabilitation,” <i>Frontiers in Systems Neuroscience</i> 10 (2016): 89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5099160/. [Open access]","Ella Striem-Amit, Miriam Guendelman, and Amir Amedi, “‘Visual’ Acuity of the Congenitally Blind Using Visual-to-Auditory Sensory Substitution,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 7, no. 3 (2012): e33136, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3306374/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-034","num":34,"category":"Psychology","question":"Trained as an army surgeon and later professor of physics at Berlin, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> invented the ophthalmoscope and argued that seeing is an unconscious judgement about what would ordinarily have made such an image.","questions":["Having stated the conservation of force in an 1847 pamphlet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned to the senses, measured the curvature of the focusing lens, and made perception a judgement rather than a picture on the retina.","Treating the cochlea as a bank of resonators and the retina as three kinds of colour fibre, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reduced differences of pitch and of hue to differences between the nerves that carry them."],"answer":"Hermann von Helmholtz","answerText":"Hermann von Helmholtz","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1894</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hermann von Helmholtz</b> was a Prussian army physician who became professor of physiology at Königsberg and Heidelberg and professor of physics at Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name was made in 1847 by a pamphlet read to the Physical Society of Berlin, <i>Die Erhaltung der Kraft</i>, which stated the conservation of force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He invented the ophthalmoscope in 1851, bringing the retina of a living eye into view, and the ophthalmometer in 1855, with which he confirmed that the lens changes curvature as the eye focuses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His <i>Handbuch der physiologischen Optik</i> of 1867 came in three parts, on the optics of the eye, the sensations of vision and the theory of visual perception: the physics, the physiology and then the psychology of seeing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <i>On the Sensations of Tone</i> did as much for hearing, treating the arches of the cochlea as resonators, so that a difference of pitch became a difference between nerve fibres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Both rest on the doctrine that a sensation follows the nerve rather than the stimulus: nerves, he wrote, are telegraph wires, carrying one current whatever apparatus is fixed to their ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same reasoning let him adopt Thomas Young's proposal that three kinds of retinal fibre suffice for every colour, which looked confirmed when only three cone pigments were found in the primate retina in 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A sign is not a picture, so perception must be interpretation: we believe we see whatever object would, under ordinary conditions, have produced the image we are conscious of, which made him an empiricist against Ewald Hering, for whom spatial vision was given rather than learned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He named these acts unconscious judgements and expected resistance, a judgement being taken for the summit of conscious thought; single-cell recording has since found support for both men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is still usually named the chief exponent of unconscious inference, though the term was Wilhelm Wundt's in 1862, and one recent account of colour vision calls the phrase a grander word for ignorance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","biology","germany"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["Hermann von Helmholtz, <i>Popular Lectures on Scientific Subjects</i>, trans. E. Atkinson, with an introduction by John Tyndall (London: Longmans, Green, 1873), https://archive.org/stream/popularlectures00helmgoog/popularlectures00helmgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Hermann L. F. Helmholtz, <i>On the Sensations of Tone as a Physiological Basis for the Theory of Music</i>, trans. Alexander J. Ellis, 3rd ed., chap. 6 (London: Longmans, Green, 1895), https://archive.org/stream/onsensationsofto00helmrich/onsensationsofto00helmrich_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Wade, “Helmholtz at 200,” <i>i-Perception</i> 12, no. 4 (2021): 20416695211022374, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8255614/. [Open access]","Claudia Cristalli, “Unconscious Inferences in Perception in Early Experimental Psychology: From Wundt to Peirce,” <i>Journal of the History of the Behavioral Sciences</i> 58, no. 4 (2022): 432–48, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9796652/. [Open access]","Semir Zeki, “The Paton Prize Lecture 2021: A Colourful Experience Leading to a Reassessment of Colour Vision and Its Theories,” <i>Experimental Physiology</i> 107, no. 11 (2022): 1189–1208, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11514330/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/29/Hermann_von_Helmholtz_Litho.jpg","title":"Hermann von Helmholtz in 1857","desc":"Lithograph by Rudolf Hoffmann after a photograph by Schallenberg of Bonn, printed by J. Haller and published by George Andre Lenoir. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_von_Helmholtz_Litho.jpg","alt":"A half-length lithograph portrait of a man in his thirties with dark hair combed back and a moustache, wearing a dark coat, waistcoat and bow tie, above a facsimile of his signature"}},
{"id":"ps-035","num":35,"category":"Psychology","question":"Stimulating a frog's nerve at two measured distances from its muscle and timing the twitch gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as roughly 27 metres a second, far slower than electricity in a wire.","questions":["Myelin insulates an axon and lets the impulse leap between the nodes of Ranvier, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is very much higher in a sheathed fibre than in a bare one.","Axon diameter, the thickness of the myelin sheath and the spacing of the nodes of Ranvier together set <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which in a bird's retina does not always favour the sheathed fibres."],"answer":"nerve conduction velocity","answerText":"nerve conduction velocity","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nerve conduction velocity</b> is the speed at which an impulse travels along a nerve fibre, and for most of the history of physiology it was held to be beyond measuring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Johannes Müller's textbook lists the guesses then in circulation, Haller at 9,000 feet a minute, Sauvages at 32,400 and another physiologist at 57,600 million feet a second, and observes that none of them rests on sound experimental principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His own verdict was that we shall probably never attain the power of measuring the velocity of nervous action, there being no immense space across which to compare its propagation as there is with light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That pessimism was answered in the winter of 1849 to 1850, by stimulating a frog's nerve at measured distances from its muscle and reading the delay before contraction off a galvanometer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The impulse turned out to travel at roughly 27 metres a second, which settled a live argument, since a nerve carrying a purely electrical signal ought to have conducted like a metal wire, orders of magnitude faster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He was convinced by the result, and a nervous system that works at a readable speed is one whose workings can be timed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The modern figure depends entirely on the fibre, the fastest human ones, which carry the sense of where the limbs are, running at something like 80 to 120 metres a second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What makes the difference is myelin: the sheath insulates the axon and is broken at the nodes of Ranvier, so that the impulse leaps from node to node instead of creeping along the membrane, gaining speed while spending less energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Axon diameter, the thickness of the sheath and the spacing of the nodes all enter, which makes the speed a property of the whole arrangement rather than of the nerve as such.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nor does a sheath guarantee haste, since recordings in the bird retina find some myelinated axons slower than unmyelinated ones, and velocities across species that track their way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Johannes Müller, <i>Elements of Physiology</i>, trans. William Baly, bk. 3, sec. 3, https://archive.org/stream/Muller1838na12J-a/Muller1838na12J-a_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Maria Şerban, “Measurement under Uncertainty: Theory-Measurement Relations in Early Electrophysiological Research,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 47, no. 4 (2025): 55, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12669311/. [Open access]","Ning Zhang, Rulan Yi, Fuwang Zhong, Yali Lu, Wenjia Chen, Zhidan Ke, Yi Zhang, Liang Zhou, Pengyu Wang, and Wei Li, “Oligodendrocytes and Myelination: Pioneering New Frontiers in Cognitive Neuroscience,” <i>Frontiers in Neuroscience</i> 19 (2025): 1618468, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12319009/. [Open access]","Gerrit E. Gmel, Paul F. Vollebregt, Marjolein E. G. Thijssen, Rosana Santos Escapa, Eleanor McAlees, Dave Mugan, John L. Parker, and Charles H. Knowles, “Electrophysiological Responses in the Human S3 Nerve during Sacral Neuromodulation for Fecal Incontinence,” <i>Frontiers in Neuroscience</i> 15 (2021): 712168, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8545143/. [Open access]","Christoph T. Block, Malte T. Ahlers, Christian Puller, Max Manackin, Dipti R. Pradhan, and Martin Greschner, “Saltatory Axonal Conduction in the Avian Retina,” <i>Journal of Physiology</i> 603, no. 21 (2025): 6533–47, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12576306/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Helmholtz_pendulum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany%2C_1895-1910_Wellcome_L0057770.jpg","title":"Helmholtz pendulum","desc":"A pendulum myograph made by the Berliner Physikalische Werkstatten in Berlin between 1895 and 1910. The swing stimulates a nerve at a chosen distance from its muscle and records both the stimulus and the contraction on smoked glass. Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helmholtz_pendulum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany%2C_1895-1910_Wellcome_L0057770.jpg","alt":"A dark cast-iron laboratory instrument on a heavy base, a rectangular frame carrying a curved metal arc through which a pivoted arm swings past small brass contact blocks, with cloth-covered wires at the top and a knurled brass weight at one corner"}},
{"id":"ps-036","num":36,"category":"Psychology","question":"Grinding the legs of a compass flat so that they would press rather than prick, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> measured how far apart two touches must lie before the skin feels them as two.","questions":["Comparing lifted weights and drawn lines, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found that the smallest difference a person notices stands in a ratio to the standard rather than being a fixed amount.","The first clear case of a nerve whose action is to inhibit came from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and his brother Eduard, who found in 1846 that stimulating the vagus slows the heart."],"answer":"Ernst Heinrich Weber","answerText":"Ernst Heinrich Weber","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Touch measured</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1829</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1846</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ernst Heinrich Weber</b> was an anatomist and physiologist at Leipzig, one of three scientific brothers credited by a younger contemporary with founding the mechanics of the circulation, the muscles, the joints and the ear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His own subject was the skin, the first of the senses to be put to methodical and measuring investigation, and his instrument was a compass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He ground its legs flat to a third of a Paris line across so that the ends would press rather than prick, pain being never as local as a firm touch, and closed it by degrees until two impressions began to be felt as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figure was written down, confirmed on another day and the survey carried over the body part by part, the observer rested before fatigue could spoil it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even past that limit the patch still felt elongated, so someone reporting a single point could often still say which way the line joining the ends had run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He measured lifted weights and drawn lines the same way, hunting the smallest difference still noticed, and found that what gets noticed is a ratio rather than an amount.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> We take in the ratios of tones, he wrote, without knowing their vibration numbers, and the proportions of a building without measuring it in inches, and we compare weights the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fechner later built a logarithmic law on those measurements, and Ewald Hering, editing the work in 1905, held that Weber had been wrongly made co-responsible for it, his own view implying sensation directly proportional to magnitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With his brother Eduard he showed in 1846 that stimulating the vagus nerve slows the heart, the first clear case of a nerve whose office is to inhibit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The compass survives in the clinic and is a poor measure of tactile acuity, performance staying above chance even at zero separation, so an orientation judgement is recommended instead; the real limit is set by afferent density and the skin's own elasticity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","biology","germany"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Ernst Heinrich Weber, <i>Tastsinn und Gemeingefühl</i>, ed. Ewald Hering, Ostwalds Klassiker der exakten Wissenschaften 149 (Leipzig: Wilhelm Engelmann, 1905), https://archive.org/stream/tastsinnundgemei00webe/tastsinnundgemei00webe_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Hermann von Helmholtz, “On Thought in Medicine,” in <i>Popular Lectures on Scientific Subjects</i>, 2nd ser., trans. E. Atkinson (London: Longmans, Green, 1881), 199–236, https://archive.org/stream/lecturespopularo00helmrich/lecturespopularo00helmrich_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Beatrice Paradiso, Dainius H. Pauza, Clara Limback, Giulia Ottaviani, and Gaetano Thiene, “From Psychostasis to the Discovery of Cardiac Nerves: The Origins of the Modern Cardiac Neuromodulation Concept,” <i>Biology</i> 13, no. 4 (2024): 266, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11047897/. [Open access]","Jonathan Tong, Oliver Mao, and Daniel Goldreich, “Two-Point Orientation Discrimination versus the Traditional Two-Point Test for Tactile Spatial Acuity Assessment,” <i>Frontiers in Human Neuroscience</i> 7 (2013): 579, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3772339/. [Open access]","Davide Deflorio, Massimiliano Di Luca, and Alan M. Wing, “Skin Properties and Afferent Density in the Deterioration of Tactile Spatial Acuity with Age,” <i>Journal of Physiology</i> 601, no. 3 (2023): 517–33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10107475/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Ernst_Heinrich_Weber_Litho.jpg","title":"Ernst Heinrich Weber in 1856","desc":"Lithograph by Rudolf Hoffmann after a photograph by Schenk of Jena, from the Gallerie ausgezeichneter Naturforscher published in Vienna. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernst_Heinrich_Weber_Litho.jpg","alt":"A half-length lithograph portrait of an elderly man with long side-whiskers and swept-back hair, in a dark double-breasted coat over a high white stock, above a facsimile of his signature"}},
{"id":"ps-037","num":37,"category":"Psychology","question":"Setting out to find a functional relation between body and soul while writing a book on the hereafter, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded psychophysics and made sensation grow with the logarithm of the stimulus.","questions":["Writing satire under the pen name Dr Mises and arguing in 1848 that plants have souls, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nonetheless gave psychology its first workable way of putting a number to a sensation.","Asking people which shapes they preferred rather than deducing beauty from principles, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded an aesthetics from below and held that liking follows association more than form."],"answer":"Gustav Theodor Fechner","answerText":"Gustav Theodor Fechner","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1887</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Gustav Theodor Fechner</b>, born on 19 April 1801, was a professor at Leipzig who wrote satires and verse under the pen name Dr Mises before the work that made his name in psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He held throughout that body and soul are two modes of appearance of one and the same underlying being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1848 he gave that view its boldest form in <i>Nanna</i>, arguing that plants have a soul-life of their own, a book long set aside as mysticism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The three volumes of <i>Zend-Avesta</i>, in 1851, carried it to the heavens and the hereafter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Psychophysics grew out of writing that book: what he wanted was a functional relation between the two modes of appearance, as physics had for the colour and intensity of light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He defined the new subject as an exact doctrine of the relations of dependence between body and soul, or more generally between the physical and the psychical world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It expressly stops at appearances and declines to reach behind them for the essence of body or soul in the metaphysician's sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The route was long — he compared himself to a man on a circle's rim walking the whole way round to reach something one step behind him — and ended in a formula: the magnitude of a sensation goes not with the stimulus but with its logarithm, the threshold taken as the unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He sent the idea to the physicist Wilhelm Weber in 1850 hoping it might be 'a happy one', and was told it was correct and acute but not yet happy, a happy idea being one that meets new facts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also founded an aesthetics from below, settling questions of taste by asking people rather than by deducing from principles, and held that liking is governed less by an object's form than by what a life has associated with it — the formal half of which has fared worse, since people rating triangles do not peak at the golden ratio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","philosophy","germany"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Gustav Theodor Fechner, <i>Elemente der Psychophysik</i>, vol. 1 (Leipzig: Breitkopf und Härtel, 1860), https://archive.org/stream/elementederpsych001fech/elementederpsych001fech_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Gustav Theodor Fechner, <i>Elemente der Psychophysik</i>, vol. 2 (Leipzig: Breitkopf und Härtel, 1860), https://archive.org/stream/elementederpsych02fech/elementederpsych02fech_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Gustav Theodor Fechner, <i>On Life after Death</i>, trans. Hugo Wernekke (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle and Rivington, 1882), https://archive.org/stream/onlifeafterdeath00fechuoft/onlifeafterdeath00fechuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Giulia Parovel, “G. T. Fechner (1848): Plants as Sentient Living Beings,” <i>Plant Signaling and Behavior</i> 21, no. 1 (2026): 2632571, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12928611/. [Open access]","Stefan A. Ortlieb, Werner A. Kügel, and Claus-Christian Carbon, “Fechner (1866): The Aesthetic Association Principle — A Commented Translation,” <i>i-Perception</i> 11, no. 3 (2020): 2041669520920309, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7264472/. [Open access]","Jay Friedenberg, “Aesthetic Judgment of Triangular Shape: Compactness and Not the Golden Ratio Determines Perceived Attractiveness,” <i>i-Perception</i> 3, no. 3 (2012): 163–75, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3485848/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Title_page_%22Vergleichende_Anatomie_der_Engel%22%2C_Fechner_1825_Wellcome_L0015342.jpg","title":"Comparative Anatomy of Angels, 1825","desc":"The title page of Vergleichende Anatomie der Engel, a sketch published at Leipzig under Fechner's pen name Dr Mises. Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Title_page_%22Vergleichende_Anatomie_der_Engel%22%2C_Fechner_1825_Wellcome_L0015342.jpg","alt":"A foxed letterpress title page in blackletter reading Vergleichende Anatomie der Engel, eine Skizze von Dr. Mises, with a former owner's inked signature down the right margin and the imprint Leipzig 1825 at the foot"}},
{"id":"ps-038","num":38,"category":"Psychology","question":"Setting out to show that no fundamental difference divides the mental faculties of man and the higher mammals, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the difference in mind one of degree and not of kind.","questions":["Circulating printed queries to observers abroad in 1867 and asking for observation rather than memory, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tried to settle whether the same face means the same feeling everywhere.","Predicting in 1859 that psychology would rest on the gradual acquirement of every mental power, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent his last two decades on books about mind, emotion and expression."],"answer":"Charles Darwin","answerText":"Charles Darwin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Origin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1859</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Descent of Man</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Expression</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1872</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Charles Darwin</b> ended the <i>Origin of Species</i> in 1859 by predicting that psychology would rest on a new foundation, the acquirement of each mental power by gradation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He made the case twelve years later in the <i>Descent of Man</i>, one chapter of which had, he said, no object but to show that no fundamental difference divides the mental faculties of man and the higher mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conclusion is the sentence the book is remembered by: the difference in mind between man and the higher animals, great as it is, is one of degree and not of kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The evidence was gathered instance by instance — a chimpanzee cracking a fruit with a stone, a monkey taught to open palm-nuts that later used stones unprompted — with curiosity, memory, imitation and reason traced through animals in incipient form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next year he gave expression a book of its own, written in four months between the proofs of the <i>Descent</i> and the sixth edition of the <i>Origin</i>, and illustrated with photographs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> To find whether expressions are inherited he circulated printed queries in 1867 among observers abroad, asking that observation and not memory be trusted, and had 36 replies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His reasoning was that a movement expressing one emotion across distinct human groups is likely innate, since a gesture acquired in early life would differ from group to group as languages do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same continuity argument was also used to arrange human groups on a scale, the book speaking of gradations between what it calls 'the highest men of the highest races' and 'the lowest savages'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The method did not survive: a case built from anecdotes drew the answering rule that no action may be read as the work of a higher mental faculty if a lower one will explain it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The question he set did survive, and a recent review finds emotional expressions largely universal in their production, with cultural nuances differing between spontaneous and posed ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","biology","evolution"],"difficulty":1,"sources":["Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i> (London: John Murray, 1859), 488, https://archive.org/stream/onoriginofspecie1859darw/onoriginofspecie1859darw_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>The Descent of Man, and Selection in Relation to Sex</i>, vol. 1, chaps. 2–3 (New York: D. Appleton, 1871), https://archive.org/stream/descentofmansel01darw/descentofmansel01darw_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals</i>, chap. 1 (London: John Murray, 1872), https://archive.org/stream/bub_gb_NWAJAAAAQAAJ/bub_gb_NWAJAAAAQAAJ_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Louise Barrett, “Introduction to the Special Collection: The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 4 (2022): e57, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10426026/. [Open access]","Domenica Bruni, Pietro Perconti, and Alessio Plebe, “Anti-Anthropomorphism and Its Limits,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 2205, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6249301/. [Open access]","Adèle Gallant, Marie-Pier Mazerolle, Annalie Pelot, and Annie Roy-Charland, “Cultural Differences in the Production of Emotional Facial Expressions: A Review,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 17 (2026): 1699374, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12907163/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Expression_of_the_Emotions_Plate_I.png","title":"Plate I from The Expression of the Emotions","desc":"Photographs of crying and screaming children, reproduced in the chapter on suffering and weeping. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expression_of_the_Emotions_Plate_I.png","alt":"Six numbered oval photographs arranged in three rows, each showing a crying or screaming infant or small child, one of them held in the arms of an older child"}},
{"id":"ps-039","num":39,"category":"Psychology","question":"Defining life as the continuous adjustment of internal relations to external relations four years before the <i>Origin</i> appeared, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the mind a product of evolution.","questions":["Because experiences were thought to accumulate across a race rather than a lifetime, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could treat an innate structure as inherited experience and hand each side of the nativist quarrel half the victory.","Darwin borrowed the phrase 'survival of the fittest' from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and later editions of the <i>Origin</i> say psychology will rest on a foundation he had already laid."],"answer":"Herbert Spencer","answerText":"Herbert Spencer","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Principles</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1855</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Herbert Spencer</b> brought out a <i>Principles of Psychology</i> in 1855, four years before the <i>Origin of Species</i>, and it set out an account of mind as a product of evolution before Darwin had published one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its governing formula is a definition of life as the continuous adjustment of internal relations to external relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mind is the highest reach of that adjustment, so a mental process is to be understood by asking what it fits its owner for rather than what it is made of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instinct is handled as compound reflex action, one rung on a continuous ladder running from the simplest nervous response up to reasoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mechanism was inheritance: experiences accumulate not within a single life but across a race, since individuals transmit the constitutions they receive along with whatever their own habits have altered in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Innate structure therefore became inherited experience, which settled the quarrel between empiricism and nativism by awarding each side half of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darwin took the phrase 'survival of the fittest' from him and called it more accurate than his own natural selection, and where the <i>Origin</i> had first said psychology would rest on a new foundation, its later editions said the foundation was already well laid by him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The programme reached further than psychology proper, since recapitulation and a psychological Lamarckism carried it into psychoanalysis, where inherited unconscious memories rested on the same principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> All of it depended on the inheritance of acquired characteristics, which biological work between the wars disproved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What outlived the mechanism was the question it taught psychology to ask, which is what a mental process is for, while the habit-and-heredity thread is now cautiously compared with epigenetic inheritance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","philosophy","science","evolution"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Herbert Spencer, <i>The Principles of Psychology</i> (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1855), https://archive.org/stream/b21965377/b21965377_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i>, 6th ed. (London: John Murray, 1876), https://archive.org/stream/onoriginspecies00dallgoog/onoriginspecies00dallgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i> (London: John Murray, 1859), 488, https://archive.org/stream/onoriginofspecie1859darw/onoriginofspecie1859darw_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Geoffrey Marcaggi and Fabian Guénolé, “Freudarwin: Evolutionary Thinking as a Root of Psychoanalysis,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 892, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6018481/. [Open access]","Mariagrazia Portera and Mauro Mandrioli, “Who’s Afraid of Epigenetics? Habits, Instincts, and Charles Darwin’s Evolutionary Theory,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 43, no. 1 (2021): 20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7875938/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Herbert_Spencer_at_46.jpg","title":"Herbert Spencer at 46","desc":"A vignetted photographic portrait of 1866, reproduced in the Life and Letters of Herbert Spencer. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herbert_Spencer_at_46.jpg","alt":"A vignetted profile portrait of a balding man with long side-whiskers and curling hair at the back, in a dark coat and bow tie, facing right, captioned in script beneath"}},
{"id":"ps-040","num":40,"category":"Psychology","question":"Counting eminent relatives and taking reputation for a fair test of ability, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argued that a highly gifted race of men could be bred by judicious marriages over several generations.","questions":["Looking for a way to weigh nature and nurture in just scales, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned to the life-history of twins and gave psychology a method it has used ever since.","Setting parents against children to see how ability descends, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arrived at regression and at the correlation that grew out of it, both now standard everywhere."],"answer":"Francis Galton","answerText":"Francis Galton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Eugenics coined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1883</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regression</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1889</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Francis Galton</b> was a London polymath and one of the first researchers into human heredity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <i>Hereditary Genius</i>, in 1869, opens by proposing to show that a man's natural abilities are derived by inheritance under exactly the same limitations as physical features, so that a highly gifted race of men could be produced by judicious marriages over several generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence was a count of eminent relatives, reputation being taken as a fair test of high ability, beginning with the judges of England from 1660.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He saw the objection that social advantage might explain the pattern, and tried to meet it by setting the sons of eminent men against the adopted nephews of popes, who had the advantages without the descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wanting to weigh nature and nurture in just scales, he turned to the life-history of twins, the first use of twins as a method for that question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He coined the word eugenics in 1883 for the science of improving stock, defining it to take in every influence tending to give the more suitable races or strains of blood a better chance of prevailing speedily over the less suitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The statistics came out of the same work, since setting parents against children gave him regression, the finding that the stature of adult offspring is on the whole more mediocre than that of their parents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Correlation grew from that, and both are now everywhere, regression in particular as a routine trap in medicine, where an extreme measurement moves back towards the middle on being repeated whether or not anything was done to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The programme he named outlived him as an organised movement, whose statistical wing was known as the biometricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It reached the statute book: an Act of 1913 in Britain allowed those deemed feeble-minded to be institutionalised indefinitely, and a Supreme Court ruling of 1927 in the United States upheld enforced eugenic sterilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","research methods","evolution"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["Francis Galton, <i>Hereditary Genius: An Inquiry into Its Laws and Consequences</i> (London: Macmillan, 1869), https://archive.org/stream/hereditarygenius1869galt/hereditarygenius1869galt_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Francis Galton, <i>Inquiries into Human Faculty and Its Development</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1908), https://archive.org/stream/inquiriesintohum00galtuoft/inquiriesintohum00galtuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Francis Galton, <i>Natural Inheritance</i> (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://archive.org/stream/naturalinherita00galt/naturalinherita00galt_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Alex Aylward, Daniel J. Fairbanks, Maria Kiladi, and Gregory Radick, “Eugenics and the Genetics Society,” <i>Heredity</i> 135, no. 5 (2026): 301–12, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13219441/. [Open access]","Kaj S. Emanuel, Jari Dahmen, Inger N. Sierevelt, Mats Brittberg, and Gino M. M. J. Kerkhoffs, “Regression to the Mean: Statistical Bias Can Mislead Interpretation in Cartilage and Osteoarthritis Clinics and Research,” <i>Cartilage</i> 16, no. 4 (2025): 405–8, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11558649/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Francis_Galton_1850s.jpg","title":"Francis Galton in middle life","desc":"A photographic portrait scanned from Karl Pearson's Life, Letters and Labours of Francis Galton, where it is dated to his middle life. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francis_Galton_1850s.jpg","alt":"A seated three-quarter-length photographic portrait of a balding man with side-whiskers in a dark frock coat over a patterned waistcoat, hands folded over a paper in his lap, in a carved chair"}},
{"id":"ps-041","num":41,"category":"Psychology","question":"Squeezing a dynamometer, naming colours against the clock and bisecting a 50 cm line were among the ten tasks proposed in 1890 as the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of experimental psychology.","questions":["Galton had asked that any such measure be checked against an independent estimate of a person's powers, and eleven years later class marks at Columbia showed the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> predicting nothing.","Because acuity and speed seemed easier to measure than judgement, the earliest form of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recorded reaction times and skin sensitivity rather than what a person could reason out."],"answer":"mental test","answerText":"mental test","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1901</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>mental test</b> is a short standardised task whose result is recorded and set beside other people's, and the phrase was coined by James McKeen Cattell in a paper of 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology, he opened, cannot attain the certainty and exactness of the physical sciences unless it rests on a foundation of experiment and measurement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten were proposed, among them pressure on a dynamometer, the two-point threshold, the least noticeable difference in weight, reaction time for sound, time for naming colours, bisecting a 50 cm line, judging ten seconds, and letters remembered on one hearing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind it lay the anthropometric laboratory Francis Galton had called for, where a man might get himself and his children weighed, measured and their bodily faculties tested by the best methods known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He appended remarks to the paper, and the first of them named the step the programme never took.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The measures, he wrote, should be compared with an independent estimate of the man's powers, so that we might learn which of them are the most instructive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eleven years passed before anyone did it, and then Clark Wissler, a doctoral student at Columbia, set the laboratory tests against the students' own class marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He found that the tests showed little intercorrelation, that the physical measures correlated among themselves but hardly with them, and that class marks correlated well with each other and not with the tests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His verdict was carefully scoped: the tests seemed to have some value with children in the lower schools but told us nothing about the general worth of college students or adults, and he refused to conclude that testing was therefore a waste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ten tests have not come back, but a positive association between mental speed and reasoning is consistently reported, and in one recent study it came out of medium size, larger for the more complex speed tasks than the simplest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","research methods"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["J. McKeen Cattell, “Mental Tests and Measurements,” <i>Mind</i> 15, no. 59 (1890): 373–81, https://archive.org/stream/sim_mind_1890-07_15_59/sim_mind_1890-07_15_59_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Francis Galton, “Remarks,” appended to J. McKeen Cattell, “Mental Tests and Measurements,” <i>Mind</i> 15, no. 59 (1890): 380–81, https://archive.org/stream/sim_mind_1890-07_15_59/sim_mind_1890-07_15_59_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Clark Wissler, <i>The Correlation of Mental and Physical Tests</i> (New York: Columbia University, 1901), https://archive.org/stream/correlationment00wissgoog/correlationment00wissgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Natalie Borter, Katja Schlegel, and Stefan J. Troche, “How Speededness of a Reasoning Test and the Complexity of Mental Speed Tasks Influence the Relation between Mental Speed and Reasoning Ability,” <i>Journal of Intelligence</i> 11, no. 5 (2023): 89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10219451/. [Open access]","Francis Galton, <i>Inquiries into Human Faculty and Its Development</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1908), https://archive.org/stream/inquiriesintohum00galtuoft/inquiriesintohum00galtuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/K._Pearson%2C_The_Life%2C_Letters_and_Labours_Wellcome_L0032610.jpg","title":"Handbill for Galton's Anthropometric Laboratory","desc":"The laboratory's own printed notice, reproduced in Karl Pearson's Life, Letters and Labours of Francis Galton. Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K._Pearson%2C_The_Life%2C_Letters_and_Labours_Wellcome_L0032610.jpg","alt":"A printed handbill on orange paper headed ANTHROPOMETRIC LABORATORY, for the measurement in various ways of Human Form and Faculty, listing four numbered purposes and charging threepence or fourpence for the principal measurements"}},
{"id":"ps-042","num":42,"category":"Psychology","question":"Family studies of inherited feeble-mindedness and army testing on a scale of nearly two million men gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the numbers it wanted from the new science of psychology.","questions":["Indiana in 1907 and California in 1909 passed compulsory sterilisation laws, and the psychological test soon gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a way of deciding who belonged on the list.","When the author of the most influential immigrant testing study withdrew it in 1930 as without foundation, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost the evidence that psychology had appeared to give it."],"answer":"eugenics","answerText":"eugenics","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First US law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1907, Indiana</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Army testing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1917–19</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrawn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1930</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Eugenics</b> is the attempt to improve a population by controlling who has children, and in the decades after 1900 psychology supplied it with the instrument it had lacked, a test returning a number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Henry Goddard's <i>Kallikak Family</i> of 1912 traced two lines of descent from one man and called the result a natural experiment in which nobody could doubt that true heredity was at work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His own text concedes that we do not know feeble-mindedness to be a unit character and that there are many reasons for thinking it cannot be, and assumes it anyway to simplify the illustration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The book sold widely and was taken seriously, and the legislation had begun before it: Indiana passed the first compulsory sterilisation law in 1907 and California followed in 1909.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over the 20th century the health authorities of California alone recommended sterilisation for more than 20,000 people held in state institutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The instrument arrived at scale during the First World War, when 1,726,966 men were given a psychological examination before the end of January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reading those results, Carl Brigham concluded in 1923 that the average intelligence of immigrants was declining, and that the newer arrivals were intellectually inferior to the older ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own figures showed the score rising with years of residence in the United States, and he redrew that curve as a fall in the quality of successive arrivals rather than as a sign that the test measured familiarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seven years later he published a review holding that comparative studies of national and racial groups may not be made with existing tests, and that one of the most pretentious of them, his own, was without foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His reason was the one the data had shown all along, that a test in a language may be given only to people with equal opportunity to have acquired that language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","history","science","research methods"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["Henry Herbert Goddard, <i>The Kallikak Family: A Study in the Heredity of Feeble-Mindedness</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1912), https://archive.org/stream/kallikakfamilya00goddgoog/kallikakfamilya00goddgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Robert M. Yerkes, ed., <i>Psychological Examining in the United States Army</i>, Memoirs of the National Academy of Sciences 15 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1921), https://archive.org/stream/psychologicalexa00yerkuoft/psychologicalexa00yerkuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Carl C. Brigham, <i>A Study of American Intelligence</i> (Princeton, NJ: Princeton University Press, 1923), https://archive.org/stream/studyofamericani00briguoft/studyofamericani00briguoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Carl C. Brigham, “Intelligence Tests of Immigrant Groups,” <i>Psychological Review</i> 37, no. 2 (1930): 158–65, https://archive.org/stream/sim_psychological-review_1930-03_37_2/sim_psychological-review_1930-03_37_2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Ingrid Grenon and Joav Merrick, “Intellectual and Developmental Disabilities: Eugenics,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 2 (2014): 201, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4202623/. [Open access]","Marie Kaniecki, Nicole L. Novak, Sarah Gao, Natalie Lira, Toni Ann Treviño, Kate O’Connor, and Alexandra Minna Stern, “Racialization and Reproduction: Asian Immigrants and California’s Twentieth-Century Eugenic Sterilization Program,” <i>Social Forces</i> 102, no. 2 (2023): 706–29, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10569381/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-043","num":43,"category":"Psychology","question":"The German physician <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened his 1845 textbook by asking which organ must be diseased wherever there is madness, and answered that it could only be the brain.","questions":["Against the psychicists, who read insanity as godless disobedience, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led the German somaticists in treating mental disorder as a bodily condition open to ordinary medical study.","Though remembered for the doctrine that mental illness is brain disease, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also named anger, fright and sorrow among the commonest immediate causes of insanity."],"answer":"Wilhelm Griesinger","answerText":"Wilhelm Griesinger","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1817</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Textbook</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1845, 2nd edition 1861</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wilhelm Griesinger</b> was a German physician whose 1845 textbook <i>Die Pathologie und Therapie der psychischen Krankheiten</i> made the somatic view of madness, the doctrine that mental illness is bodily illness, the working assumption of German psychiatry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its opening section asks which organ must necessarily and invariably be diseased where there is madness, and answers that physiological and pathological facts show this organ can only be the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The claim was polemical, since German psychiatry had for decades been led by the psychicists, who read insanity in moral terms as the fruit of disobedient and godless behaviour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against them the somaticists held that mental disorder arises from bodily conditions, and is therefore open to the ordinary anatomical and clinical study medicine gives any other disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was no pure materialist, naming anger, fright and sorrow among the commonest immediate causes of insanity, and the doctrine had a practical edge in his advocacy of humane asylum care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> General paralysis of the insane, a fatal dementia with slurred speech and grandiose delusions, seemed to settle the argument, accounting for up to 20% of male asylum admissions around 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1913 Hideyo Noguchi and J. W. Moore demonstrated the syphilis spirochaete <i>Treponema pallidum</i> in the brains of paretic patients at a New York state hospital, giving a psychiatric syndrome a named cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A treatment followed, and the malaria fever therapy introduced in 1917 took the 1927 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for the therapeutic value of inoculating such patients with malaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Both halves have since been qualified, penicillin having displaced fever therapy from the 1940s, while a recent study of the asylum records found outcomes far poorer than the published claims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The larger promise remains outstanding, since psychiatric diagnosis still rests on clinical symptoms rather than on any bodily sign, and biological markers are as yet little integrated into care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","biology","history","germany"],"difficulty":4,"sources":["Lukas Bergner, Hubertus Himmerich, Kenneth C. Kirkby, and Holger Steinberg, “Descriptions of Disordered Eating in German Psychiatric Textbooks, 1803–2017,” <i>Frontiers in Psychiatry</i> 11 (2020): 504157, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.504157. [Open access]","Robert C. Bransfield, Charlotte Mao, and Rosalie Greenberg, “Microbes and Mental Illness: Past, Present, and Future,” <i>Healthcare</i> 12, no. 1 (2023): 83, https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare12010083. [Open access]","Wilhelm Griesinger, <i>Mental Pathology and Therapeutics</i>, trans. C. Lockhart Robertson and James Rutherford (London: New Sydenham Society, 1867), bk. 1, ch. 1, §1, https://archive.org/details/mentalpathologyt00grieuoft. [Open access]","Alison Clayton, “Malaria Therapy for Neurosyphilis at Mont Park Hospital for the Insane in Australia, 1927–1928,” <i>History of Psychiatry</i> 37, no. 2 (2026): 135–58, https://doi.org/10.1177/0957154x251403921. [Open access]","Hideyo Noguchi and J. W. Moore, “A Demonstration of Treponema Pallidum in the Brain in Cases of General Paralysis,” <i>Journal of Experimental Medicine</i> 17, no. 2 (1913): 232–38, https://doi.org/10.1084/jem.17.2.232. [Open access]","“Julius Wagner-Jauregg – Facts,” NobelPrize.org, Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1927/wagner-jauregg/facts/. [Open access]","Itziar María Béjar-Botello, Sara Jiménez-Fernández, Gloria Pérez-Guerrero, Blanca Iglesias-Rosado, Luis Gutiérrez-Rojas, Jesús Herrera-Imbroda, and Inmaculada Romera, “Prognostic Biomarkers and Precision Psychiatry: A Review of the Available Evidence,” <i>Biomedicines</i> 14, no. 3 (2026): 558, https://doi.org/10.3390/biomedicines14030558. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Wilhelm_Griesinger%2C_001.jpg","title":"Wilhelm Griesinger","desc":"A lithograph portrait by M. Wiese, reproduced in Beschreibung des Bürger-Hospitals zu Stuttgart, published at Stuttgart in 1859. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilhelm_Griesinger%2C_001.jpg","alt":"A three-quarter-length lithograph portrait of a bearded man in his thirties, his hair combed back, wearing a dark coat and light waistcoat with a watch chain and a knotted black cravat, one hand resting at his hip"}},
{"id":"ps-044","num":44,"category":"Psychology","question":"The universal fluid of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was supposed to flow freely again once a patient, seated in a ring round an oak tub and holding an iron rod, had been brought to a crisis.","questions":["A blindfolded boy in a Paris orchard fainted while embracing an ordinary tree, which the royal commissioners of 1784 took as proof that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the work of imagination.","Convulsions came from imagination without the fluid and nothing came from the fluid without imagination, said the 1784 report that killed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as physics without killing its effects."],"answer":"animal magnetism","answerText":"animal magnetism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mesmer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1734–1815</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Paris practice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1778</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Royal commission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1784</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Animal magnetism</b> was the doctrine, advanced from the 1770s by the Viennese physician Franz Anton Mesmer, that a subtle invisible fluid fills the universe and that illness follows when its flow through the body is blocked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His 1766 Vienna dissertation had argued that the planets act on the human frame as the moon acts on the tides, and the fluid was that idea brought indoors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He began with magnets, then found them dispensable and treated by passes of the hands, bringing on a crisis of convulsions, laughter or sleep that was taken for the cure at work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Driven from Vienna in 1778 after his treatment of the blind pianist Maria Theresia von Paradis collapsed, he set up in Paris, where patients sat in a ring round an oak tub called the baquet, each holding an iron rod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vogue was enormous, and in March 1784 the king named a commission of four Paris physicians and five academicians, Benjamin Franklin and Lavoisier among them, to test whether the fluid existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The commissioners saw that the question was not whether patients had crises but whether the fluid produced them, and so arranged for people not to know when they were being magnetised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A woman known to be sensitive was blindfolded and magnetised, and her sensations no longer answered to the parts at which the operator worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In an orchard at Passy a boy of twelve, chosen by Mesmer's pupil Deslon, was blindfolded and led to embrace four ordinary trees while a magnetised fifth stood at a distance, and at the fourth he fainted into a crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The report concluded that imagination without magnetism produces convulsions and magnetism without imagination produces nothing, and that the existence of the fluid is absolutely destitute of proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fluid is gone and the effects were not, so that explanation survives in the study of suggestion and expectancy, while the practice ran on through artificial somnambulism into hypnosis, a name dating from Braid's <i>Neurypnology</i> of 1843.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","history","research methods"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Benjamin Franklin et al., <i>Report of Dr. Benjamin Franklin, and Other Commissioners, Charged by the King of France, with the Examination of the Animal Magnetism, as Now Practised at Paris</i> (London: J. Johnson, 1785), 19, 23–24, 56–57, 67–68, 105–6, https://archive.org/details/b22393857. [Open access]","Gavriel Knafo and Joel Weinberger, “Exploring the Role of Conscious and Unconscious Processes in Hypnosis: A Theoretical Review,” <i>Brain Sciences</i> 14, no. 4 (2024): 374, https://doi.org/10.3390/brainsci14040374. [Open access]","Curtis E. Margo and Lynn E. Harman, “The Intriguing Blindness of Maria Theresia von Paradis and the Instructive Potential of Medical Biography,” <i>Cureus</i> 17, no. 2 (2025): e78948, https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.78948. [Open access]","James Kennaway, “Musical Hypnosis: Sound and Selfhood from Mesmerism to Brainwashing,” <i>Social History of Medicine</i> 25, no. 2 (2011): 271–89, https://doi.org/10.1093/shm/hkr143. [Open access]","Burkhard Peter, “Hypnosis in Psychotherapy, Psychosomatics and Medicine: A Brief Overview,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 15 (2024): 1377900, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1377900. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Animal_magnetism%2C_1814.jpg","title":"The operator putting his patient into a crisis","desc":"An engraving from E. Sibly, A Key to Physic and the Occult Sciences, published in 1814, with the fluid drawn as dotted arcs streaming from the operator's hands. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Animal_magnetism,_1814.jpg","alt":"A stipple engraving of a man in an 18th-century coat and breeches standing over a seated woman in a long gown, one hand raised and one held out towards her, with three dotted arcs curving through the air from his hands to her face and body"}},
{"id":"ps-045","num":45,"category":"Psychology","question":"Chains and purges gave way at the York Retreat to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a regime of routine, occupation and kindly authority that worked on whatever self-command a patient had left.","questions":["Because the desire of esteem was thought to govern the insane more powerfully than fear, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> required attendants to address patients as far as possible as rational beings.","The state asylums built across America from the 1840s promised cure through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and filled instead with poor patients whom admission law kept inside long after treatment had given way to containment."],"answer":"moral treatment","answerText":"moral treatment","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">York Retreat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 1796</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tuke's account</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1813</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Asylum era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 1800s – 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Moral treatment</b> was the regime, adopted at the end of the 18th century, that set out to cure madness by kindness, routine, occupation and whatever self-control the patient had left, in place of chains, purges and terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Retreat, a Quaker house near York that took its first patients in 1796, is its clearest statement, and Samuel Tuke's account of 1813 says that neither chains nor corporal punishment were tolerated there on any pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its psychology is the interesting part: most of the insane were held to keep a considerable degree of self-command, strengthened by treating them as far as possible as rational beings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fear was allowed a place and a small one, since the desire of esteem was thought to work more powerfully, and patients were seen to restrain themselves before strangers merely passing through the house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philippe Pinel developed the <i>traitement moral</i> in Paris from 1793, and the regime crossed to America with Benjamin Rush, Dorothea Dix and Thomas Story Kirkbride, who built recreation, schooling and work into the asylum day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its promise was curability, and the state asylums raised on it from the 1840s, among them the New York State Lunatic Asylum at Utica in 1843, were meant to be places of rest, refuge and cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Demand outran them at once, since Utica admitted 276 patients in its first year and had to be enlarged, while admission and discharge law kept poor patients inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Small therapeutic establishments became large overcrowded ones that all but abandoned treatment for containment, the wealthy going to quieter private asylums and the poor to custodial care at the lowest possible cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The founding story has itself been qualified, since the striking of the chains at Bicêtre is a myth attached to Pinel, who acted hesitantly where his colleague Jean-Baptiste Pussin drove it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Historians of policy count it the first of four American cycles of reform, each promising that early treatment would prevent lasting disability and none of the first three delivering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["practice","psychology","science","history","institution"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Samuel Tuke, <i>Description of the Retreat, an Institution near York, for Insane Persons of the Society of Friends</i> (York: W. Alexander, 1813), ch. 5, esp. 141, 157–58, https://archive.org/details/b33520240. [Open access]","Yiran Vanessa Zhang, “Utica State Hospital: Psychiatric Reform, Institutionalization, and Patient Justice in 19th Century America and Today,” <i>Yale Journal of Biology and Medicine</i> 98, no. 3 (2025): 349–55, https://doi.org/10.59249/bias7665. [Open access]","Preethy George, Nev Jones, Howard Goldman, and Abram Rosenblatt, “Cycles of Reform in the History of Psychosis Treatment in the United States,” <i>SSM – Mental Health</i> 3 (2023): 100205, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ssmmh.2023.100205. [Open access]","Heinz-Peter Schmiedebach, “Historische Anmerkungen zur Ethik in der Psychiatrie: Herausforderungen im 19. Jahrhundert,” <i>Der Nervenarzt</i> 95, no. 7 (2024): 641–45, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00115-024-01657-x. [Open access]","Christopher Holligan, “Educative Psychological Treatment at Edinburgh's Royal Asylum: Unfolding <i>The Morningside Mirror</i>, 1845–1882,” <i>Social History of Medicine</i> 39, no. 1 (2026): 1–23, https://doi.org/10.1093/shm/hkae022. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Engraving_of_the_original_Retreat_near_York_instituted_1792.jpg","title":"The original building of the Retreat, York","desc":"A wood engraving after a painting by Cave, from Daniel Hack Tuke's Reform in the Treatment of the Insane, the Retreat's centenary volume. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Engraving_of_the_original_Retreat_near_York_instituted_1792.jpg","alt":"A wood engraving of a plain three-storey house with lower wings, standing among trees above a stream, with a woman in a bonnet, a laden donkey and two other figures on the path in front of it, captioned Original Building of the Retreat, York, Instituted 1792"}},
{"id":"ps-046","num":46,"category":"Psychology","question":"A handbook of 1873 and 1874 announced a borderline science between physiology and psychology, and its author <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened rooms for a laboratory at Leipzig five years later.","questions":["A long study of language, myth and custom stands beside the experimental handbook, because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held that the higher mental processes lay beyond the reach of the laboratory altogether.","Because only one volume of his handbook was ever translated, English-speaking psychology inherited the experimentalism of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and almost none of the philosophy that went with it."],"answer":"Wilhelm Wundt","answerText":"Wilhelm Wundt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Handbook</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1873–74, 5th edition 1902–3</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leipzig rooms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1879</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wilhelm Wundt</b> was the German physiologist and philosopher whose handbook of 1873 and 1874, the <i>Grundzüge der physiologischen Psychologie</i>, is usually taken to mark the birth of experimental psychology as a discipline of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The book announced a borderline science standing between physiology and psychology, one taking the points of contact between inner and outer life as its subject, which neither older discipline could sidestep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its method was experiment, since a fleeting conscious process cannot be held fast by attention while the observer is also its object, and pure self-observation can under no circumstances claim accuracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His readers took him for the man who gathered what Weber, Fechner, Helmholtz and Lotze had done separately into one centre, and persuaded both scientists and philosophers to accept the new science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rooms for a laboratory were set apart at Leipzig in 1879 and his institute drew students from around the world, which turned a programme on paper into a discipline with a workforce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The programme was narrower than its reputation, since he held the experimental method to reach the sensory side of mental life and to fail beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The higher processes went to <i>Völkerpsychologie</i>, the study of language, myth and custom as products of the common mental life, which he called the chief source of knowledge about them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only one volume of the handbook was ever translated into English, so the part carrying his philosophy never reached the readers who took his name for their founder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What crossed the Atlantic was the experimentalism without the rest, passed on through his student Titchener and fixed by Edwin Boring's history, which opens on the line that before him there had been psychology enough but no psychologists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians treat that portrait as a construction rather than a record, so the textbook founder and the man who put the higher processes outside the laboratory are not the same person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","history","germany"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["Wilhelm Wundt, <i>Principles of Physiological Psychology</i>, trans. Edward Bradford Titchener (London: Swan Sonnenschein, 1904), introduction, §§1–2, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Wundt/Physio/intro.htm. [Open access]","Robert H. Wozniak, “Introduction to <i>Grundzüge der physiologischen Psychologie</i>, Wilhelm Wundt (1874),” in <i>Classics in Psychology, 1855–1914: Historical Essays</i> (Bristol: Thoemmes Press, 1999), https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Wundt/Physio/wozniak.htm. [Open access]","James McKeen Cattell, “The Psychological Laboratory at Leipsic,” <i>Mind</i> 13 (1888): 37–51, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Cattell/leipsic.htm. [Open access]","Jeremy Trevelyan Burman, “Meaning-Change Through the Mistaken Mirror: On the Indeterminacy of ‘Wundt’ and ‘Piaget’ in Translation,” <i>Review of General Psychology</i> 26, no. 1 (2022): 22–48, https://doi.org/10.1177/10892680211017521. [Open access]","Claudia Cristalli, “Unconscious Inferences in Perception in Early Experimental Psychology: From Wundt to Peirce,” <i>Journal of the History of the Behavioral Sciences</i> 58, no. 4 (2022): 432–48, https://doi.org/10.1002/jhbs.22211. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Erstausgaben_f%C3%BCr_Wikipedia_IV_035.jpg","title":"Völkerpsychologie, volume 1, 1900","desc":"The title page of the first volume of Wundt's Völkerpsychologie, subtitled an investigation of the developmental laws of language, myth and custom, published at Leipzig by Wilhelm Engelmann in 1900. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erstausgaben_f%C3%BCr_Wikipedia_IV_035.jpg","alt":"A letterpress title page reading Völkerpsychologie, Eine Untersuchung der Entwicklungsgesetze von Sprache, Mythus und Sitte, von Wilhelm Wundt, Erster Band, Die Sprache, Erster Theil, with the imprint Leipzig, Verlag von Wilhelm Engelmann, 1900 at the foot"}},
{"id":"ps-047","num":47,"category":"Psychology","question":"Nearly half the research done at the Leipzig laboratory was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the measurement of how long a mental operation takes, and its students carried the method home to laboratories of their own.","questions":["Subtracting the time to answer one stimulus from the time to choose between two was the founding trick of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its founding difficulty, since inserting a stage may change the others.","Roughly 3,800 papers a year still turn on simple and choice reaction times, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remains a cornerstone binding psychology to the techniques of neuroscience."],"answer":"mental chronometry","answerText":"mental chronometry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Donders</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leipzig rooms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1879</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Journal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Philosophische Studien, from 1882</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mental chronometry</b> is the measurement of how long mental operations take, and it was the characteristic work of the psychological laboratory Wilhelm Wundt opened at Leipzig in 1879.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rooms were set apart in the university's Convict building with a yearly grant for apparatus, and by 1888 the laboratory filled four of them with two more about to be added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nineteen students were carrying on original research in a single semester, from everywhere but England, and the example was already being copied at Berlin, Bonn, Göttingen, Johns Hopkins, Harvard, Pennsylvania, Princeton, Cambridge and Copenhagen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wundt founded a journal to carry the results, the <i>Philosophische Studien</i>, which had issued fifteen parts since 1882, and the work in it fell under four heads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those were the measurement of sensation, the duration of mental processes, the time-sense, and attention, memory and the association of ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nearly half of everything done there belonged to the second head, since only experiment can hold a fleeting process still enough to be observed, and a process that takes time can be timed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The method came from Donders, who in 1868 subtracted the time to answer a single unvarying stimulus from the time to answer one of two, and called the difference the time to discriminate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He saw the trouble himself, writing that the course taken was not irreproachable, since a man who enters a room by two different doors is unlikely to leave by a third in the same interval both times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The assumption that inserting a stage leaves the others untouched has been argued over ever since, and cognitive neuroscience has had to ask whether brain activity is separable in the way the subtraction requires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The method outlived the doubts, since roughly 3,800 papers a year still turn on simple and choice reaction times, and the technique remains a cornerstone binding psychology to neuroscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","research methods","history"],"difficulty":4,"sources":["James McKeen Cattell, “The Psychological Laboratory at Leipsic,” <i>Mind</i> 13 (1888): 37–51, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Cattell/leipsic.htm. [Open access]","Michael I. Posner, “Timing the Brain: Mental Chronometry as a Tool in Neuroscience,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 3, no. 2 (2005): e51, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0030051. [Open access]","David M. Alexander, Chris Trengove, and Cees van Leeuwen, “Donders Is Dead: Cortical Traveling Waves and the Limits of Mental Chronometry in Cognitive Neuroscience,” <i>Cognitive Processing</i> 16, no. 4 (2015): 365–75, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10339-015-0662-4. [Open access]","José M. Medina, Willy Wong, José A. Díaz, and Hans Colonius, “Advances in Modern Mental Chronometry,” <i>Frontiers in Human Neuroscience</i> 9 (2015): 256, https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2015.00256. [Open access]","Wilhelm Wundt, <i>Principles of Physiological Psychology</i>, trans. Edward Bradford Titchener (London: Swan Sonnenschein, 1904), introduction, §2, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Wundt/Physio/intro.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/Binet_-_Introduction_%C3%A0_la_psychologie_exp%C3%A9rimentale%2C_Fig_9.png","title":"The Hipp chronoscope","desc":"A diagram of the Hipp chronoscope, the clock used to time reactions, from Alfred Binet's Introduction à la psychologie expérimentale of 1894. Public domain, via the Bibliothèque nationale de France and Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Binet_-_Introduction_%C3%A0_la_psychologie_exp%C3%A9rimentale%2C_Fig_9.png","alt":"A line diagram of a clockwork instrument with two dials on a rack of gearing at the right, wired through a commutator, a rheostat, two batteries and a pair of contact keys at the left, each part lettered"}},
{"id":"ps-048","num":48,"category":"Psychology","question":"Biology has a morphology, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the school that claimed the same office for psychology, asking what is there in consciousness and in what quantity rather than what it is there for.","questions":["Sensation and affection were the only elements <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would admit, each nothing at all apart from attributes like quality, intensity and duration, any one of which at zero abolishes it.","Because trained observers never came to agree about what they found in their own consciousness, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not outlive the man who named it, though the laboratory it worked in did."],"answer":"structuralism","answerText":"structuralism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Titchener</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867–1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manifesto</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reply to Watson</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1914</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Structuralism</b> was the school that set experimental psychology the task of analysing consciousness into its elements, and it took its programme from a comparison with biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As biology divides into morphology, physiology and ontogeny, so Edward Titchener held that experimental psychology was the morphology of mind, ravelling the elemental processes out of the tangle of consciousness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its worker, he wrote, tries to discover first of all what is there and in what quantity, not what it is there for, his task being a vivisection that yields structural rather than functional results.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The elements came out at two, sensation and affection, with the idea a sub-species of the first, and after twenty years of minute analysis he could report no trace of a mental krypton or argon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An element is nothing apart from its constitutive attributes, quality and intensity and duration among them, and if any one of those takes the value zero the sensation ceases to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The method was trained introspection, and the distinction he drew is exact: from the structural standpoint introspection observes an Is, from the functional standpoint an Is-for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Unschooled introspection slides irresistibly into the second, reporting what a thing is for thought or for conduct rather than the mental material itself, which is why observers had to be schooled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rival camp was functionalism, and which of the two mattered, the parts or the working whole, was the dominant question as psychology was separating itself from philosophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then behaviourism denied there was anything there to observe, and Titchener answered in 1914 that the methods of science are in the last resort observational and its problems in the last resort analytical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The school did not outlive him, because the training never produced agreement between observers, and psychology kept the laboratory while giving up the mental anatomy it had been built to do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["theory","psychology","science","history"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["E. B. Titchener, “The Postulates of a Structural Psychology,” <i>Philosophical Review</i> 7 (1898): 449–65, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/structuralism.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “Structural and Functional Psychology,” <i>Philosophical Review</i> 8 (1899): 290–99, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/strucfunc.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “On ‘Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It,’” <i>Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society</i> 53 (1914): 1–17, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/watson.htm. [Open access]","Lisa Kampen, “Pragmatism as Idealism? The Case of Mary Whiton Calkins,” <i>Journal of the History of the Behavioral Sciences</i> 61, no. 3 (2025): e70028, https://doi.org/10.1002/jhbs.70028. [Open access]","OpenStax, “1.2 History of Psychology,” in <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: Rice University, 2020), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-2-history-of-psychology. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ps-049","num":49,"category":"Psychology","question":"The Cornell laboratory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had rooms named optics, acoustics, haptics and reaction, one for each avenue by which a sensation might enter and be taken apart.","questions":["Wundt reached America chiefly through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who carried over the experimental half of the programme and left the philosophy that had gone with it behind.","Told by the behaviourists that there was nothing there to observe, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered in 1914 that the methods of science are in the last resort observational."],"answer":"Edward Titchener","answerText":"Edward Titchener","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cornell laboratory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 1891</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Edward Titchener</b> was an Englishman who studied under Wundt at Leipzig and then spent his career at Cornell, where he led the American school of structuralism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He held that the method of every science can be summed up in a single word as observation, and that the sciences differ not in their subject matter, which is human experience, but in the point of view they take on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His laboratory at Cornell opened in 1891 with a suite of six rooms and by 1898 had ten, named for the work done in them: optics, acoustics, haptics, reaction, taste and smell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The floor plan is the doctrine in architecture, a room for each avenue by which a sensation might enter and be taken apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American laboratory, he wrote, must serve instruction as well as research, so every student from the third undergraduate year was handled in the laboratory rather than in a lecture room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That is why so much of his working life went into manuals of laboratory practice and into training observers, since the method could produce nothing until there were people schooled to use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He worked at the method itself as well, holding in 1912 that the word introspection had grown equivocal and reserving it for procedures that were scientifically available and had actually been employed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the behaviourists said there was nothing there to observe, he answered in 1914 that the methods of science are in the last resort observational and its problems in the last resort analytical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was the point through which Wundt reached America, and what he carried over was the experimental half of the programme rather than the philosophy that had gone with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He died in 1927 and the school did not outlast him, leaving behind the laboratory teaching he had built and very little of the mental anatomy it had been built to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","history"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["E. Bradford Titchener, “A Psychological Laboratory,” <i>Mind</i> 7 (1898): 311–31, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/lab.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “The Postulates of a Structural Psychology,” <i>Philosophical Review</i> 7 (1898): 449–65, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/structuralism.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “The Schema of Introspection,” <i>American Journal of Psychology</i> 23 (1912): 485–508, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/introspection.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “On ‘Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It,’” <i>Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society</i> 53 (1914): 1–17, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/watson.htm. [Open access]","Lisa Kampen, “Pragmatism as Idealism? The Case of Mary Whiton Calkins,” <i>Journal of the History of the Behavioral Sciences</i> 61, no. 3 (2025): e70028, https://doi.org/10.1002/jhbs.70028. [Open access]","OpenStax, “1.2 History of Psychology,” in <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: Rice University, 2020), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-2-history-of-psychology. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Edward_Bradford_Titchener.jpg","title":"Edward Bradford Titchener","desc":"A studio portrait photograph dated to about 1890, photographer unknown. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Bradford_Titchener.jpg","alt":"A head-and-shoulders studio photograph of a young man in pince-nez spectacles, with a full moustache and pointed beard and his hair combed back, wearing a checked jacket over a waistcoat and a broad pale cravat"}},
{"id":"ps-050","num":50,"category":"Psychology","question":"Würzburg's observers reported states of mind with no picture, sound or feeling in them, and whether such <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exists was the question two laboratories could not settle between them.","questions":["Titchener answered the Würzburg claim by reporting his own mind as a picture gallery, since on his account <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a thing that could not exist at all.","Two laboratories with trained observers and controlled tasks disagreed about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and neither had any procedure by which it could convict the other of error."],"answer":"imageless thought","answerText":"imageless thought","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Würzburg work</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1901</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Titchener's reply</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1909</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Washburn's address</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1921</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Imageless thought</b> is thinking that carries no sensory content at all, and whether any such thing exists was the question that broke the first psychology of consciousness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The claim came from Oswald Külpe's laboratory at Würzburg, where observers set simple tasks of judgment reported states they could not describe as any image, sound or feeling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marbe, Watt, Ach and Messer named these states <i>Bewusstseinslagen</i>, conscious attitudes, and Binet's observer said of one that it was something vague, too difficult to describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cornell answered that the reports were incomplete, and that a properly trained observer would find the imagery the Würzburg observers had overlooked or failed to catch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Titchener would not hedge: he did not at all believe the thought-element existed, and reported his own mind as a fairly complete picture gallery, not of finished paintings but of impressionist notes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both laboratories used trained observers, controlled tasks and the same method, and reached opposite answers, with no procedure that either could use to convict the other of error.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He set about repairing the method instead, holding by 1912 that the word introspection had grown equivocal and reserving it for procedures that were scientifically available and had actually been used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where a report describes a purely inner process with no external stimulus behind it, Margaret Floy Washburn told the American Psychological Association in 1921, there is no guarantee of any common factor at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is the realm, she said, where introspection is most vulnerable, and the failure to agree left the method open to attack from behaviourists and psychoanalysts alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The episode is still cited as the standard case against first-person method, though the argument now turns less on whether it failed than on what would count as doing it properly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","history","research methods"],"difficulty":4,"sources":["Edward Bradford Titchener, <i>Lectures on the Experimental Psychology of the Thought-Processes</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), lectures 3–5, https://archive.org/details/lecturesonexperi00titc. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “The Schema of Introspection,” <i>American Journal of Psychology</i> 23 (1912): 485–508, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/introspection.htm. [Open access]","Margaret Floy Washburn, “Introspection as an Objective Method,” <i>Psychological Review</i> 29 (1922): 89–112, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Washburn/. [Open access]","Russell T. Hurlburt, Ben Alderson-Day, Charles Fernyhough, and Simone Kühn, “Can Inner Experience Be Apprehended in High Fidelity? Examining Brain Activation and Experience from Multiple Perspectives,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 8 (2017): 43, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2017.00043. [Open access]","Jaakko Belt, “Phenomenological Skepticism Reconsidered: A Husserlian Answer to Dennett's Challenge,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 2058, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.02058. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://archive.org/download/lecturesonexperi00titc/page/n6_large.jpg","title":"Lectures on the Experimental Psychology of the Thought-Processes, 1909","desc":"The title page of the book in which Titchener answered the Würzburg claim, published by Macmillan in 1909. Public domain, scanned by the Internet Archive.","credit":"https://archive.org/details/lecturesonexperi00titc","alt":"A letterpress title page on browned paper reading Lectures on the Experimental Psychology of the Thought-Processes, by Edward Bradford Titchener, with the imprint New York, The Macmillan Company, 1909 at the foot"}},
{"id":"gw-001","num":1,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"India","answerText":"India","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1947</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"India"},"facts":[["Capital","New Delhi"],["Population","1.46 billion (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Area","3,287,263 km² (1,269,219 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/Flag_of_India.svg","credit":"Government of India, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_India.svg)","alt":"The flag of India: three horizontal bands of saffron, white and green, with a navy-blue wheel of 24 spokes at the centre of the white band."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","india"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>India</b>'s modern history turns on the ending of British rule, a thing argued over for a generation and then settled in a matter of months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> To secure Indian cooperation in the Second World War the Churchill government sent the Cripps Mission in 1942, which failed to satisfy nationalist leaders but put the idea of independence after the war into circulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Congress Party under M. K. Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru demanded a free and united country, while the Muslim League under Muhammad Ali Jinnah mobilised Muslim voters behind the demand for a separate one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gandhi's Quit India movement, the cost of the war and the naval mutiny of 1946 convinced Britain that the subcontinent could no longer be governed from London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The formal process began with a British government report of 16 May 1946 which recommended an interim government charged with devising a constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> India joined the United Nations on 30 October 1945, nearly two years before it was independent, because that interim government was allowed to conduct relations with other states before sovereignty was transferred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British Parliament's India Independence Act of 18 July 1947 then created the Union of India and Pakistan out of the former British India, and the two came into being together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The line drawn between them was religious — Pakistan for Muslims and India for Hindus — although India chose to be a secular republic, and some 35 million Muslims remained in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nehru led the new India as prime minister and Jinnah led Pakistan, and independence came at the head of a much wider unravelling of European empires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 1945 and 1960 some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence, by no single pattern — some transfers peaceful and orderly, others following a protracted revolution — and so many new members changed the composition of the United Nations itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “India,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/in.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: India,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/india. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Indian Independence: What Led to Partition in 1947?,” Classroom Resources, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/indian-independence/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Pakistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/pakistan. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Taj_Mahal_%28Edited%29.jpeg/1920px-Taj_Mahal_%28Edited%29.jpeg","title":"Taj Mahal, India","desc":"Taj Mahal.","credit":"Yann ; edited by Jim Carter, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taj_Mahal_(Edited).jpeg","alt":"A view of Taj Mahal."}},
{"id":"gw-002","num":2,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"China","answerText":"China","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1911</span><span class=\"dt-k\">PRC founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"China"},"facts":[["Capital","Beijing"],["Population","1.42 billion (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Asia"],["Area","9,600,000 km² (3,706,600 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg","credit":"Design by Zeng Liansong, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg)","alt":"The flag of China: a red field with one large gold star in the upper hoist corner and four smaller gold stars curving beside it."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>China</b>'s dealings with the West began as trade rather than diplomacy: for sixty years from 1784, when the Philadelphia ship <i>Empress of China</i> made the first direct American voyage, the contact was private and largely commercial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That trade grew under a Chinese system which confined foreign merchants to the single port of Guangzhou, so the consuls sent there were never formally received as representatives of a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Formal recognition between the Empire of China and the United States came on or about 16 June 1844, when the envoy Caleb Cushing presented his credentials to the official Qiying and opened treaty negotiations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through the 19th century the Qing met one foreign incursion after another: two Opium Wars cost them Hong Kong, forced open the treaty ports and gave foreign powers large concessions with extraterritorial rule in the major cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Defeat by Japan in 1894 and 1895 cost more territory still, including Taiwan and parts of Manchuria, and ended Chinese suzerainty over Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Imperialist demands, frustration with the foreign Manchu court and a wish for a less parochial and more unified country fed a nationalism that turned revolutionary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Revolutionaries in the south then revolted successfully against the Qing, ending an imperial system more than two thousand years old and putting the Republic of China in its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Chinese Communist Party, founded in Shanghai in 1921, first worked inside a united front with the Nationalists and marched with them in the Northern Expedition against the warlords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That collaboration ended in the White Terror of 1927, and after the Japanese invasion of Manchuria in 1931 and the full-scale civil war that followed the Second World War, Mao Zedong declared the People's Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which government held China's founding seat at the United Nations was settled only in 1971, when the General Assembly voted to restore the lawful rights of the People's Republic there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “China,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: China,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/china. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Chinese Revolution of 1911,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/chinese-rev. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Chinese Revolution of 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/chinese-rev. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Restoration of the Lawful Rights of the People's Republic of China in the United Nations,” resolution 2758, 26th session, 25 October 1971, United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192054. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg/1920px-The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg","title":"Great Wall of China, China","desc":"Great Wall of China.","credit":"Severin.stalder, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg","alt":"A view of Great Wall of China."}},
{"id":"gw-003","num":3,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"United States","answerText":"United States","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declared 4 July 1776</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1783, Treaty of Paris</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United States of America"},"facts":[["Capital","Washington, D.C."],["Population","347 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern America"],["Area","9,833,517 km² (3,796,742 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a4/Flag_of_the_United_States.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States.svg)","alt":"The flag of the United States: thirteen alternating red and white stripes, with fifty small white stars on a blue rectangle in the upper hoist corner."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","north america","united states"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>United States</b> began as thirteen British colonies whose Continental Congress appointed a committee of five on 10 June 1776 to draft a statement of independence for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It included John Adams, Benjamin Franklin, Robert R. Livingston and Roger Sherman, but the writing was delegated to Thomas Jefferson, who drafted the text between 11 and 28 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Congress spent the whole of 3 July and most of the following day revising it before adopting it, and delegates began signing the engrossed parchment on 2 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Declaring independence and securing it were different things: in 1782 Franklin rejected British overtures for a settlement that would have left the thirteen states with some autonomy inside the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Formal negotiations opened on 27 September 1782 and two months of hard bargaining produced preliminary articles in which Britain accepted American independence and boundaries and agreed to evacuate its forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Those articles also settled fishing rights on the Newfoundland banks and prewar debts owed to British creditors, and promised restitution of property lost by Americans loyal to the British cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Franklin had refused throughout to make a separate peace apart from France, which had been the American side's staunch ally through the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The new country then grew westward by purchase and by war: in 1803 it bought 530 million acres of North America from France for 15 million dollars, territory Spain had held since 1762 which made up all or part of fifteen modern states between the Mississippi and the Rocky Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under President James K. Polk the annexation of Texas in 1845, the Oregon Treaty with Britain in 1846 and the Mexican-American War, closed by the Treaty of Guadalupe-Hidalgo in 1848, delivered the future states of Texas, California, Nevada, New Mexico, Arizona, Utah, Washington and Oregon, with parts of five more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations dates the country's membership from 24 October 1945, the day the organisation itself came into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United States of America,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/us.html. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Declaration of Independence (1776),” Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/declaration-of-independence. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Treaty of Paris, 1783,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1776-1783/treaty. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Louisiana Purchase, 1803,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/louisiana-purchase. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Annexation of Texas, the Mexican-American War, and the Treaty of Guadalupe-Hidalgo, 1845–1848,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/texas-annexation. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Statue_of_Liberty%2C_New_York_Harbor%2C_USA-13Nov2011.jpg/1920px-Statue_of_Liberty%2C_New_York_Harbor%2C_USA-13Nov2011.jpg","title":"the Statue of Liberty, the United States","desc":"The Statue of Liberty in New York Harbor.","credit":"Mike Durkin from Forest Hills, NY, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Liberty,_New_York_Harbor,_USA-13Nov2011.jpg","alt":"A green copper statue holding a torch on an island in a harbour."}},
{"id":"gw-004","num":4,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Indonesia","answerText":"Indonesia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">recognised 1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Indonesia"},"facts":[["Capital","Jakarta"],["Population","286 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","1,910,931 km² (737,815 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Flag_of_Indonesia.svg","credit":"Drawn by Gabbe, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Indonesia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Indonesia: two equal horizontal bands, red above white."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","indonesia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Indonesia</b> was ruled from the Netherlands long before it was a country of its own, and the first American contact with the archipelago was commercial rather than diplomatic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States appointed its first consul there on 24 November 1801, to the city then called Batavia, and for a century and a half afterwards the relationship stayed at that informal level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Formal recognition waited on independence, and came when Ambassador H. Merle Cochran presented his credentials and a message of congratulation from President Truman to President Sukarno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cochran had until then been the American representative to the United Nations Commission for Indonesia, the body set up to settle the dispute with the Dutch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new state joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade within months of that recognition, and the World Trade Organization on the day it came into being in 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its independence belonged to a wider unravelling, in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence from their European rulers between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There was no single pattern to it: some transfers were peaceful and orderly, others followed a protracted revolution, and the arrival of so many new members changed the composition of the United Nations itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In April 1955 Indonesia hosted the meeting that gave that movement a voice, when representatives of 29 Asian and African governments gathered at Bandung to discuss decolonisation, economic development and the place of the Third World in the Cold War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It co-sponsored the conference with Burma, India, Pakistan and Sri Lanka, and most of the 24 further states they brought together had themselves only recently emerged from colonial rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its declared principles were self-determination, mutual respect for sovereignty, non-aggression, non-interference and equality; Indonesia had taken its own seat at the United Nations on 28 September 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Indonesia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/id.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Indonesia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/indonesia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Bandung Conference (Asian-African Conference), 1955,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/bandung-conf. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Indonesia and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/indonesia_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Pradaksina.jpg/1920px-Pradaksina.jpg","title":"Borobudur, Indonesia","desc":"Borobudur.","credit":"Heri nugroho, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pradaksina.jpg","alt":"A view of Borobudur."}},
{"id":"gw-005","num":5,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Pakistan","answerText":"Pakistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1947</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Pakistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Islamabad"],["Population","255 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Area","796,095 km² (307,374 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/32/Flag_of_Pakistan.svg","credit":"Drawn by User:Zscout370, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pakistan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Pakistan: a dark green field bearing a white crescent and five-pointed star, with a vertical white band at the hoist."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","pakistan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Pakistan</b> was made out of British India, and the argument that made it ran through the last decade of British rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Muslim League under Muhammad Ali Jinnah pressed the claim that India's Muslims were a nation entitled to self-determination, while the Congress Party under M. K. Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru demanded a free and united country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The demand for a separate territorial state was fluid and limited in the early 1940s, but the League mobilised Muslim voters of the lower and middle classes behind it and won a large mandate in the Muslim provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Britain helped consolidate the League by installing its ministries in 1942 and 1943, and through the Cripps Mission had already floated the idea that regions might secede from a future union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Partition divided the subcontinent on religious lines, Pakistan for Muslims and India for Hindus, although some 35 million Muslims remained in a secular Indian republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> President Truman sent a congratulatory message to Jinnah, by then Governor General of the Dominion of Pakistan, on its emergence among the family of nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American Embassy opened the same day at Karachi, which was the new country's first capital, with Charles W. Lewis Jr. as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pakistan joined the Commonwealth on partition from India, left it in 1972 and rejoined in 1989.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was among the five governments — with Burma, India, Indonesia and Sri Lanka — that co-sponsored the Bandung conference of April 1955, where 29 Asian and African states met to argue for self-determination and non-alignment in the Cold War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its seat at the United Nations dates from 30 September 1947, a few weeks after the country itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Pakistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Pakistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/pakistan. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Indian Independence: What Led to Partition in 1947?,” Classroom Resources, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/indian-independence/. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Pakistan,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/pakistan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Bandung Conference (Asian-African Conference), 1955,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/bandung-conf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Badshahi_Mosque_front_picture.jpg/1920px-Badshahi_Mosque_front_picture.jpg","title":"Badshahi Mosque, Pakistan","desc":"Badshahi Mosque.","credit":"Romero Maia, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badshahi_Mosque_front_picture.jpg","alt":"A view of Badshahi Mosque."}},
{"id":"gw-006","num":6,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Nigeria","answerText":"Nigeria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nigeria"},"facts":[["Capital","Abuja"],["Population","238 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","923,768 km² (356,669 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Flag_of_Nigeria.svg","credit":"Design by Michael Taiwo Akinkunmi, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nigeria.svg)","alt":"The flag of Nigeria: three equal vertical bands, green, white and green."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","nigeria"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nigeria</b> came to independence as a federation, and it did so under British sovereignty until the day it did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Federation of Nigeria in a congratulatory message from Secretary of State Christian Herter, broadcast to the Nigerian people over Voice of America radio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A letter from President Eisenhower followed the next day, delivered to Prime Minister Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, and diplomatic relations opened at once with a Nigerian embassy in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American Embassy was established at Lagos, which was then the seat of government and remained so until the capital moved inland a generation later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nigeria joined the Commonwealth in the same year, following its independence from Britain, and joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade six weeks after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its membership of the Commonwealth was suspended between 1995 and 1999, the only interruption in more than sixty years and a measure of how far military rule had taken the country from the association's stated standards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That independence came in the middle of the fastest phase of European withdrawal, when some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There was no single pattern to it: in some places the transfer was peaceful and orderly, in others independence followed a protracted revolution, and a few of the new countries were ruled by dictators or military juntas for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The creation of so many states at once — many of them poor, several of them holding strategic ground or natural resources — altered the composition of the United Nations and the political complexity of every region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nigeria took its own seat there on 7 October 1960, a week after becoming a country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Nigeria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ng.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nigeria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/nigeria. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Nigeria,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/nigeria. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Nigeria and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/nigeria_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Zuma_Rock.jpg","title":"Zuma Rock, Nigeria","desc":"Zuma Rock.","credit":"Jeff Attaway, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zuma_Rock.jpg","alt":"A view of Zuma Rock."}},
{"id":"gw-503","num":503,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Washington, D.C.","answerText":"Washington, D.C.","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Residence Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Government moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1800</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United States of America","dot":"Washington, D.C."},"facts":[["Country","United States"],["Population","5.3 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern America"],["Planned by","Pierre Charles L'Enfant"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","united states"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Washington, D.C.</b> was planned as a capital before it existed as a city: Congress passed a Residence Act, and President George Washington chose the ground for it from land ceded by Maryland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He appointed three commissioners to survey the site and oversee the design of the capital and its buildings, and they hired the French engineer Pierre Charles L'Enfant to plan the new city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> L'Enfant put the Capitol at the elevated east end of the Mall, on a rise then called Jenkins' Hill, which he described as a pedestal waiting for a monument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was dismissed the following year, having refused to produce any drawings for the building itself — he claimed to carry the design in his head — and having declined the commissioners' authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A competition suggested by Thomas Jefferson drew seventeen unsatisfactory plans before a late entry from William Thornton was accepted, and Washington selected the design in 1793.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The government arrived before its buildings were ready: Congress, the Supreme Court and the Library of Congress moved into a Capitol whose north wing alone had been made habitable, some rooms still unfinished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Department of State came the same year from Philadelphia, first to the Treasury building and then to one of the Six Buildings, moving four times in the city's first sixteen months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The President's House was begun a year after the site was chosen, to a design by the Irish-born architect James Hoban, and John Adams and his wife Abigail moved into it still unfinished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> British troops set fire to the Capitol on 24 August 1814 and only a sudden rainstorm prevented its complete destruction; Hoban was appointed to rebuild the President's House, and James Monroe moved back in three years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Capitol still stands on its plateau 27 metres (88 feet) above the Potomac at the eastern end of the National Mall, grown with the country to more than 140,000 square metres (1.5 million square feet) and over 600 rooms, and the city has been the country's capital throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United States of America,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/us.html. [Open access]","Architect of the Capitol, “History of the U.S. Capitol Building,” https://www.aoc.gov/explore-capitol-campus/buildings-grounds/capitol-building/history. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Buildings of the Department of State,” Department History, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/buildings. [Open access]","The White House, “The White House,” About the White House, https://www.whitehouse.gov/about-the-white-house/the-white-house/. [Open access]","Architect of the Capitol, “U.S. Capitol Building,” Explore the Capitol Campus, https://www.aoc.gov/explore-capitol-campus/buildings-grounds/capitol-building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Washington%2C_D.C._-_2007_aerial_view.jpg/1920px-Washington%2C_D.C._-_2007_aerial_view.jpg","title":"Washington, D.C.","desc":"Washington, D.C., seen from a distance.","credit":"Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington,_D.C._-_2007_aerial_view.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Washington, D.C.."}},
{"id":"gw-007","num":7,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Brazil","answerText":"Brazil","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 May 1824</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Brazil"},"facts":[["Capital","Brasília"],["Population","213 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","8,515,767 km² (3,287,956 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Flag_of_Brazil.svg","credit":"Design by Raimundo Teixeira Mendes and others, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Brazil.svg)","alt":"The flag of Brazil: a green field with a large yellow diamond, inside it a blue globe carrying white stars and a curved band reading ORDEM E PROGRESSO."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","brazil"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Brazil</b> was a Portuguese colony whose ports drew American merchant ships long before it had a government of its own, and consulates appeared at Pernambuco and Rio de Janeiro in the 1810s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What made Rio more than a port was the Napoleonic wars: the King of Portugal fled across the Atlantic, and the American legation to Portugal followed him there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It sat in Rio de Janeiro from 1810 until it closed in July 1821, and returned to Lisbon in 1822 along with the King himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The colony he left behind did not remain one, and the United States recognised the Kingdom of Brazil when President Monroe received José Silvestre Rebello as its chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Monroe appointed Condy Raguet as the first American representative to the new state the following March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were unusually easy for the region, and at the beginning of the 20th century they were sometimes called an unwritten alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Brazil sent an expeditionary force to fight alongside the Americans in the Italian campaign of the Second World War, and has been an increasingly weighty power in the hemisphere since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That closeness was helped by a deliberate change of American policy: Franklin Roosevelt took office promising to deal with Central and South America by cooperation and trade rather than by force, dedicating the country in his inaugural address to the policy of the good neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His Secretary of State Cordell Hull backed a declaration at the Montevideo conference of December 1933 that no state has the right to intervene in the internal or external affairs of another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Brazil has been a member of the United Nations since 24 October 1945, the day the organisation came into being, and joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade three years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Brazil,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/br.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Brazil,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/brazil. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Portugal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/portugal. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Good Neighbor Policy, 1933,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/good-neighbor. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Brazil and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/brazil_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Christ_the_Redeemer_-_Cristo_Redentor.jpg/1920px-Christ_the_Redeemer_-_Cristo_Redentor.jpg","title":"Christ the Redeemer (statue), Brazil","desc":"Christ the Redeemer (statue).","credit":"Arne Müseler, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christ_the_Redeemer_-_Cristo_Redentor.jpg","alt":"A view of Christ the Redeemer (statue)."}},
{"id":"gw-008","num":8,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Bangladesh","answerText":"Bangladesh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1971</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bangladesh"},"facts":[["Capital","Dhaka"],["Population","176 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Area","148,460 km² (57,320 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/Flag_of_Bangladesh.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bangladesh.svg)","alt":"The flag of Bangladesh: a bottle-green field with a red disc set slightly towards the hoist."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","bangladesh"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Bangladesh</b> was made by a war, and the shape of the state that fought it was set by an earlier partition: the division of India created West and East Pakistan, two territories with no common border which shared a religion but differed in language, ethnicity and culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the parliamentary elections of 1970 an overwhelming number of East Pakistanis voted for a party that argued for autonomy for the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Army and the existing government blocked it from taking office and jailed its leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mass protests that followed were brutally suppressed by the Pakistani Army, and the suppression drove an enormous movement of refugees into neighbouring India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> East Pakistani guerrilla forces, supported by India, fought the Pakistani Army through the late autumn, and open war between the two states began on 3 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United States had reasons to hesitate over it: Pakistan had helped it reach a rapprochement with the People's Republic of China, and India had signed a treaty of mutual assistance with the Soviet Union that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Washington wished to avoid another war between the two, but also feared that Pakistan would be greatly weakened if its eastern province seceded, and so supported it at first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Recognition of the new country came on 4 April 1972 in a press statement from Secretary of State William Rogers, with a message from President Nixon to Prime Minister Sheikh Mujibur Rahman offering relations at ambassadorial level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate had stood in Dhaka since 29 August 1949, when the city was the seat of Pakistan's eastern wing rather than of a country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bangladesh joined the Commonwealth in 1972 after independence from Pakistan, and took its own seat at the United Nations on 17 September 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bangladesh,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bd.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bangladesh,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bangladesh. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The South Asia Crisis and the Founding of Bangladesh, 1971,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/south-asia. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Bangladesh,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/bangladesh. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Indian Independence: What Led to Partition in 1947?,” Classroom Resources, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/indian-independence/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Kaikhali_Sundarbans_15-09-2011_%285%29.JPG/1920px-Kaikhali_Sundarbans_15-09-2011_%285%29.JPG","title":"the Sundarbans, Bangladesh","desc":"The Sundarbans.","credit":"Joydeep, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaikhali_Sundarbans_15-09-2011_(5).JPG","alt":"A boat moored beside mangroves in tidal water."}},
{"id":"gw-009","num":9,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Russia","answerText":"Russia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Russia"},"facts":[["Capital","Moscow"],["Population","144 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","17,098,246 km² (6,601,668 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Flag_of_Russia.svg","credit":"Design attributed to Peter the Great, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Russia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Russia: three equal horizontal bands, white above blue above red."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","russia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Russia</b>'s dealings with the United States began badly: Francis Dana was appointed minister in 1780 and the Russian government refused his credentials when he reached St Petersburg the following August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He stayed two years as a private citizen to argue the American cause, and in 1795 Russia again declined an appointed American consul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came from the Russian side in 1803, when Czar Alexander I issued a ukase accepting Levett Harris as American consul at St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Formal relations followed on 14 July 1809, when Andrei Dashkov presented his credentials to President Madison, after two years of soundings between the two countries' ministers in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That arrangement held for a century and then broke: the United States cut off relations on 6 December 1917, shortly after the Bolsheviks seized power in the October Revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> President Wilson withheld recognition because the new government refused to honour the Tsarist state's debts, ignored existing treaties and seized American property, and because it had made a separate peace with Germany at Brest-Litovsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The refusal lasted almost sixteen years, ending on 16 November 1933 when Franklin Roosevelt closed negotiations in Washington with the Soviet foreign commissar Maxim Litvinov, and William Bullitt presented his credentials in Moscow that December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An alliance had seemed improbable as late as 1939, when Stalin's pact with Nazi Germany, the occupation of eastern Poland and the Winter War against Finland soured relations further, yet the cooperation that followed was essential to Germany's defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mikhail Gorbachev's decision to loosen the Soviet hold on eastern Europe produced a momentum of its own, bringing down the Berlin Wall in November 1989 and communist rule across the region, and the union did not survive it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus met on 8 December 1991 and the United States recognised the Russian Federation as the Soviet Union's successor before the year was out; the United Nations still dates the country's membership from 24 October 1945, the seat having been the union's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Russian Federation,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ru.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Russia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/russia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Recognition of the Soviet Union, 1933,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/ussr. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Collapse of the Soviet Union,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/collapse-soviet-union. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “U.S.-Soviet Alliance, 1941–1945,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/us-soviet. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_in_Moscow.jpg/1920px-Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_in_Moscow.jpg","title":"Saint Basil's Cathedral, Russia","desc":"Saint Basil's Cathedral.","credit":"Tsy1980, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_in_Moscow.jpg","alt":"A view of Saint Basil's Cathedral."}},
{"id":"gw-505","num":505,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Islamabad","answerText":"Islamabad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital since</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1963</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Pakistan","dot":"Islamabad"},"facts":[["Country","Pakistan"],["Population","1.1 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Replaced","Karachi"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","pakistan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Islamabad</b> is a capital that was decided on rather than grown into: the administrative centre of Pakistan was deliberately shifted from Karachi to a new city on the Potohar Plateau in the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The move set the government apart from the country's commercial life while keeping it accessible, and the new city was laid out beside the existing town of Rawalpindi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Capital Development Authority was established in 1960 to develop and maintain the new capital and the land around it, and it still administers the city and the master plan drawn for its wider territory in 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Karachi, the seat it replaced, had held the government from the beginning: the American Embassy opened there on the day Pakistan became independent, with Charles W. Lewis Jr. as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was in Karachi that the first Constituent Assembly of Pakistan met, in the Sindh Assembly Building on 10 August 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Muhammad Ali Jinnah was elected its president unanimously the next day, and the assembly formally approved the national flag at the same sitting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The territories that made up the new country were meanwhile to be governed under the Government of India Act of 1935, the constitutional order inherited from British rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The decision to build a capital of its own came under General Muhammad Ayub Khan, who took power in 1958 and set about remaking the country's institutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of his first steps was to appoint a Constitution Commission in February 1960, charged with proposing how democracy might be strengthened and shaped to the country's own circumstances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city that came out of that programme is the capital the United Nations records for Pakistan, and the authority that built it still describes it as a place set apart from commerce and marked by its quiet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Pakistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pk.html. [Open access]","Capital Development Authority, “About Islamabad,” Government of Pakistan, https://www.cda.gov.pk. [Open access]","National Assembly of Pakistan, “History,” https://na.gov.pk/en/content.php?id=75. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Pakistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/pakistan. [Open access]","Capital Development Authority, “CDA History,” Government of Pakistan, https://www.cda.gov.pk/cdaHistory. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Skyline_of_Islamabad_from_Pakistan_Monument_%28Landscape%29.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Islamabad_from_Pakistan_Monument_%28Landscape%29.jpg","title":"Islamabad","desc":"Islamabad, seen from a distance.","credit":"Bilalnaqeeb, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Islamabad_from_Pakistan_Monument_(Landscape).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Islamabad."}},
{"id":"gw-010","num":10,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Ethiopia","answerText":"Ethiopia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Italy invades</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1895 and 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eritrea leaves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ethiopia"},"facts":[["Capital","Addis Ababa"],["Population","135 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","1,104,300 km² (426,373 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Flag_of_Ethiopia.svg","credit":"Drawn by User:SKopp, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ethiopia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Ethiopia: three equal horizontal bands of green, yellow and red, with a blue disc at the centre bearing a yellow five-pointed star and rays."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","ethiopia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ethiopia</b> was long isolated, and its emperors began to consolidate the country in the middle of the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two Italian invasions forced it into much closer contact with world politics, the second of them ending in occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first official dealing with the United States came on 27 December 1903, when Menelik II and the American representative Robert P. Skinner signed a treaty of commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both were by then long established as states, and the treaty spoke of perpetuating and strengthening the friendly relations that already existed between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were good until the occupation and strengthened after the Second World War, and the country has taken an active part in African and world affairs since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Addis Ababa became the meeting place of a continent: in May 1963 the heads of 32 independent African states gathered there to sign the charter creating the Organisation of African Unity, Africa's first post-independence continental institution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That body expressed a pan-African vision of an Africa united, free and in control of its own destiny; it lasted until 1999, and the African Union was launched as its successor in 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was founded in the middle of the European withdrawal from the continent, in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Relations with the United States cooled after the Ethiopian revolution, as the Derg aligned itself with international communism, and improved sharply with the fall of the Mengistu regime in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eritrea, which had been under Ethiopian sovereignty, declared its independence on 27 April 1993; Ethiopia itself has been a member of the United Nations since 13 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ethiopia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/et.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ethiopia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ethiopia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Eritrea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/eritrea. [Open access]","African Union, “About the African Union,” https://au.int/en/overview. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Lalibela%2C_san_giorgio%2C_esterno_24.jpg/1920px-Lalibela%2C_san_giorgio%2C_esterno_24.jpg","title":"Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela, Ethiopia","desc":"Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela.","credit":"Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lalibela,_san_giorgio,_esterno_24.jpg","alt":"A view of Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela."}},
{"id":"gw-011","num":11,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Mexico","answerText":"Mexico","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War with US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1846–48</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mexico"},"facts":[["Capital","Mexico City"],["Population","132 million (2025)"],["Region","Central America"],["Area","1,964,375 km² (758,449 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fc/Flag_of_Mexico.svg","credit":"Drawn by Alex Covarrubias, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mexico.svg)","alt":"The flag of Mexico: three equal vertical bands of green, white and red, with the national coat of arms — an eagle on a cactus holding a snake — at the centre of the white band."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","north america","mexico"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mexico</b> was under Spanish sovereignty until Napoleon Bonaparte's invasion of Spain in 1808 gave Latin Americans an opening to fight for independence from colonial rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Earlier Mexican attempts to win recognition from the United States had failed, largely because successive American presidents wished to remain formally neutral while Spanish forces and independence fighters were still at war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came when President Monroe received José Manuel Zozaya as Mexican minister, and the question of granting it had divided American leaders — Henry Clay and John Quincy Adams took opposite views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American Legation in Mexico City was established on 1 June 1825, when Joel Roberts Poinsett presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The relationship has been intimate and often contentious ever since, and diplomatic relations have not been severed since 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The American push for territorial expansion then brought the two countries to war, and the settlement that ended it moved the border a very long way south and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under President James K. Polk the annexation of Texas in 1845 and the Oregon Treaty with Britain in 1846 preceded a conflict closed by the Treaty of Guadalupe-Hidalgo in 1848, together the greatest American territorial expansion to that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those years brought within American control the future states of Texas, California, Nevada, New Mexico, Arizona and Utah, along with parts of several more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Political instability followed in Mexico and strained the relationship further, until Franklin Roosevelt set American dealings with the hemisphere on a different footing, promising cooperation and trade rather than military force and declaring his country opposed to armed intervention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mexico has been a member of the United Nations since 7 November 1945 and joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in August 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mexico,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mx.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mexico,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mexico. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Annexation of Texas, the Mexican-American War, and the Treaty of Guadalupe-Hidalgo, 1845–1848,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/texas-annexation. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Good Neighbor Policy, 1933,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/good-neighbor. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Mexico and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/mexico_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Chichen_Itza_3.jpg/1920px-Chichen_Itza_3.jpg","title":"Chichen Itza, Mexico","desc":"Chichen Itza.","credit":"Daniel Schwen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chichen_Itza_3.jpg","alt":"A view of Chichen Itza."}},
{"id":"gw-012","num":12,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Japan","answerText":"Japan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Perry arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 July 1853</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kanagawa treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1854</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Japan"},"facts":[["Capital","Tokyo"],["Population","123 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Asia"],["Area","377,930 km² (145,920 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Flag_of_Japan.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Japan: a plain white field with a large crimson disc at its centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","japan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Japan</b> was governed by shoguns who had expelled most foreigners in 1639 and afterwards limited trade to Dutch and Chinese ships holding special charters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That closure held for two centuries, which made it very difficult for American merchants — interested since the early 19th century in extending their China trade — to reach the islands at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two things changed by the 1850s: Chinese ports had opened to regular trade, and the annexation of California had given the United States a harbour on the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Commodore Matthew Perry led four ships into the bay below Edo, the capital that is now Tokyo, and delivered a letter from President Fillmore announcing that his country sought relations and that he would return for an answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The letter carried no force of recognition, which waited for the Treaty of Peace and Amity signed at Kanagawa the following spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Consular relations began when Townsend Harris accepted appointment as consul at Shimoda on 4 August 1855, and further posts followed at Kanagawa and Nagasaki in 1862 and Hakodate in 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ninety years later that relationship had become a war, and its end brought a foreign administration to the country for the first time in its history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Allies had planned during the war how to disarm Japan, deal with its colonies, stabilise its economy and prevent it from rearming, and at Potsdam they called for unconditional surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> General Douglas MacArthur took charge of the Supreme Command of Allied Powers in September 1945 and directed seven years of military, political, economic and social reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in 1955 and the United Nations on 18 December 1956, eleven years after the surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Japan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jp.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Japan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/japan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The United States and the Opening to Japan, 1853,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/opening-to-japan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Occupation and Reconstruction of Japan, 1945–52,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/japan-reconstruction. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Japan and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/japan_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/View_of_Mount_Fuji_from_%C5%8Cwakudani_20211202.jpg/1920px-View_of_Mount_Fuji_from_%C5%8Cwakudani_20211202.jpg","title":"Mount Fuji, Japan","desc":"Mount Fuji.","credit":"Suicasmo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Mount_Fuji_from_%C5%8Cwakudani_20211202.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Fuji."}},
{"id":"gw-507","num":507,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Brasília","answerText":"Brasília","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Congress moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Brazil","dot":"Brasília"},"facts":[["Country","Brazil"],["Population","4.6 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Replaced","Rio de Janeiro"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","brazil"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Brasília</b> is the last of a series of seats, and the series is the clearest way to see how Brazil's government moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was a Portuguese colony until 1815 and then part of a united kingdom with Portugal until 1822, when the empire of Brazil began under Dom Pedro I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rio de Janeiro had been the centre of that world since the Napoleonic wars, when the King of Portugal fled across the Atlantic and the American legation followed him there, staying from 1810 until it closed in July 1821.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American consulates had appeared at Rio and at Pernambuco in the 1810s, and Rio remained the seat of government through the empire and the republic that followed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Chamber of Deputies was created by the first Brazilian constitution, imposed by the emperor in 1824, and opened its first legislature on 6 May 1826.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It sat first in the Cadeia Velha, the old gaol where Tiradentes had been held for three years before his execution, and it was there that the law abolishing slavery was passed in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The building was demolished in 1922 as unsafe and the Palácio Tiradentes went up in its place — the first put up specifically to house the Chamber — and was inaugurated on 6 May 1926.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Chamber sat there for 34 years, and its own list of seats then ends with a single entry that has run ever since: the Palácio do Congresso Nacional, in the new capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The move took the government a long way inland from the coastal cities where it had been carried on since the colony, and the whole Congress went with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the capital the United Nations records for Brazil, and the government the city was built to house has sat there ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Brazil,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/br.html. [Open access]","Câmara dos Deputados, “Conheça a Câmara,” Congresso Nacional, Brasil, https://www2.camara.leg.br/a-camara/conheca. [Open access]","Câmara dos Deputados, “Cronologia histórica e legislativa do Brasil,” Congresso Nacional, Brasil, https://www2.camara.leg.br/a-camara/conheca/historia/cronoindice.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Portugal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/portugal. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Brazil,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/brazil. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Bras%C3%ADlia_Panorama.jpg/1920px-Bras%C3%ADlia_Panorama.jpg","title":"Brasília","desc":"Brasília, seen from a distance.","credit":"seier+seier, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bras%C3%ADlia_Panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Brasília."}},
{"id":"gw-013","num":13,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Egypt","answerText":"Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">recognised 26 April 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suez Canal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nationalised 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Egypt"},"facts":[["Capital","Cairo"],["Population","118 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Area","1,002,000 km² (386,874 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Flag_of_Egypt.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Egypt.svg)","alt":"The flag of Egypt: three equal horizontal bands of red, white and black, with a gold eagle of Saladin at the centre of the white band."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","egypt"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Egypt</b> was part of the Ottoman Empire when the United States became independent, and its foreign relations were conducted through the Sultan's government at Istanbul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain established a protectorate over it in 1882, which gave London effective control of Egyptian foreign affairs while the country remained nominally Ottoman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 17 March 1849 until independence the United States kept a consular and quasi-diplomatic presence in Cairo through an Agent and Consul General, beginning with Daniel Smith McCauley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence was recognised in a letter from President Warren G. Harding to King Ahmed Fuad, delivered by the American diplomatic agent at Cairo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Egypt and Syria then united to form the United Arab Republic on 22 February 1958, with its capital at Cairo; Syria seceded in 1961 and Egypt kept the name officially for years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The event that made the country a centre of world attention came in between, when President Gamal Abdel Nasser announced the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company, the joint British-French enterprise that had owned and run the canal since its construction in 1869.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nasser offered full economic compensation, but the British and French governments were outraged, having long been suspicious of his opposition to their influence in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He in turn resented what he saw as European efforts to perpetuate a colonial domination that was supposed to have ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The crisis brought Britain, France and Israel to military action and left the Eisenhower administration alarmed at a war between its own NATO allies and an emergent power; it also increased Soviet influence in Egypt and Syria, which is what the Eisenhower Doctrine of 1957 was framed against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Egypt has been a member of the United Nations since 24 October 1945 and joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in May 1970.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Egypt,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/eg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Egypt,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/egypt. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Suez Crisis, 1956,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/suez. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Egypt and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/egypt_e.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Eisenhower Doctrine, 1957,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/eisenhower-doctrine. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Pyramids_of_the_Giza_Necropolis.jpg/1920px-Pyramids_of_the_Giza_Necropolis.jpg","title":"Giza pyramid complex, Egypt","desc":"Giza pyramid complex.","credit":"KennyOMG, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyramids_of_the_Giza_Necropolis.jpg","alt":"A view of Giza pyramid complex."}},
{"id":"gw-014","num":14,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Philippines","answerText":"Philippines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded by Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1946</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Philippines"},"facts":[["Capital","Manila"],["Population","117 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","300,000 km² (115,831 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/Flag_of_the_Philippines.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Philippines.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Philippines: a white triangle at the hoist bearing a golden sun and three stars, beside horizontal bands of blue above red."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","philippines"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Philippines</b> passed from one empire to another before it became a country: Spain ceded its longstanding colony to the United States in the Treaty of Paris that closed the Spanish-American War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That war ended Spain's colonial empire in the western hemisphere and secured the United States as a Pacific power, and the same treaty transferred Guam and Puerto Rico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 4 February 1899, two days before the American Senate ratified it, fighting broke out between American forces and Filipino nationalists under Emilio Aguinaldo, who wanted independence rather than a change of colonial ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The war that followed lasted three years and killed more than 4,200 American and over 20,000 Filipino combatants, with as many as 200,000 Filipino civilians dead of violence, famine and disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Annexation had been controversial in the United States itself, argued for out of a wish for commercial opportunity in Asia, a belief that Filipinos could not govern themselves, and a fear that another power would take the islands instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Independence came half a century later, on the American national day, when President Truman recognised the Republic of the Philippines as an independent state by proclamation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A treaty signed the same day renounced all American claims to the islands, which had until then been under American sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established that day too, when Ambassador Paul V. McNutt presented his credentials at Manila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country had already been a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, nine months before it became independent — the same order of events as India's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in December 1979 and the World Trade Organization on the day it came into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Philippines,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ph.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Philippines,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/philippines. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Spanish-American War, 1898,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Philippine-American War, 1899–1902,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/war. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Philippines and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/philippines_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Banaue-terrace.JPG/1920px-Banaue-terrace.JPG","title":"Banaue Rice Terraces, the Philippines","desc":"Banaue Rice Terraces.","credit":"User: (WT-shared) Roundtheworld at wts wikivoyage, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banaue-terrace.JPG","alt":"A view of Banaue Rice Terraces."}},
{"id":"gw-015","num":15,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Democratic Republic of the Congo","answerText":"Democratic Republic of the Congo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dem. Rep. Congo"},"facts":[["Capital","Kinshasa"],["Population","113 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Area","2,345,410 km² (905,568 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Flag_of_the_Democratic_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg","credit":"Drawn by User:Nightstallion, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Democratic_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Democratic Republic of the Congo: a sky-blue field crossed diagonally by a red stripe edged in yellow, with a yellow star in the upper hoist corner."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","congo"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Democratic Republic of the Congo</b> was the Belgian Congo until the day it was proclaimed independent of Belgian sovereignty, and it has been confused with its neighbour ever since — the smaller Republic of the Congo across the river became independent the same year, out of French Equatorial Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> President Eisenhower sent a congratulatory message to President Joseph Kasavubu, and the American Embassy opened the same day at Léopoldville, the city now called Kinshasa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the months before independence the Congolese had elected Kasavubu president and Patrice Lumumba prime minister, along with a senate, an assembly and similar bodies in each of the country's many provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Eisenhower administration had high hopes of a stable, pro-Western central government, and those hopes vanished within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 5 July soldiers of the Force Publique mutinied against their white Belgian commanders at the Thysville garrison, and the new state descended into chaos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What followed was the first of several proxy confrontations between the United States and the Soviet Union over newly independent, non-aligned African states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The decolonisation of sub-Saharan Africa ran from the late 1950s to the mid-1970s and produced dozens of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It belonged to a wider unravelling in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960, by no single pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some of the new countries had stable governments almost at once; others were ruled by dictators or military juntas for decades, or endured long civil wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> This one took its seat at the United Nations on 20 September 1960, less than three months after the Belgian flag came down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic Republic of the Congo,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cd.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Democratic Republic of the Congo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/congo-democratic-republic. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Congo, Decolonization, and the Cold War, 1960–1965,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/congo-decolonization. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Republic of the Congo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/congo-republic. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Virunga_National_Park-107997.jpg/1920px-Virunga_National_Park-107997.jpg","title":"Virunga National Park, the Democratic Republic of the Congo","desc":"Virunga National Park.","credit":"Yvette Kaboza, CC BY-SA 3.0 igo, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virunga_National_Park-107997.jpg","alt":"A view of Virunga National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-510","num":510,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Addis Ababa","answerText":"Addis Ababa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 May 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">OAU founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May 1963</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ethiopia","dot":"Addis Ababa"},"facts":[["Country","Ethiopia"],["Population","4.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Seat of","the African Union"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","ethiopia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Addis Ababa</b> is the capital of a country that was never colonised, and its 20th century can be read from the comings and goings of the foreign missions in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American Legation there was established on 6 July 1909, when Hoffman Philip presented his credentials as minister resident and consul general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That followed the first official dealing between the two states, a treaty of commerce signed by Menelik II and Robert P. Skinner in December 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Italian forces invaded and occupied the city, and although the United States never publicly recognised Italian authority in Ethiopia it withdrew its representation: the minister left on 4 March 1937 and the consulate closed at the end of that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation reopened on 31 August 1943, once the occupation was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Two decades later the city became the meeting place of a continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The heads of 32 independent African states gathered there to sign the charter creating the Organisation of African Unity, Africa's first post-independence continental institution and the expression of a pan-African vision of an Africa united, free and in control of its own destiny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There were 32 of them to gather because European rule had been unravelling for a generation: some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The organisation lasted until 1999 and the African Union was launched as its successor in 2002, taking in all 55 states of the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its Commission, the secretariat that carries out the union's daily work, is based in the city, which is why an Ethiopian capital is where African business is done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ethiopia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/et.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ethiopia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ethiopia. [Open access]","African Union, “About the African Union,” https://au.int/en/overview. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","African Union, “The AU Commission,” https://au.int/en/commission. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Addis_Ababa_skyline.jpg/1920px-Addis_Ababa_skyline.jpg","title":"Addis Ababa","desc":"Addis Ababa, seen from a distance.","credit":"Simfan34, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Addis_Ababa_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Addis Ababa."}},
{"id":"gw-016","num":16,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Vietnam","answerText":"Vietnam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1954, at Geneva</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 April 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1977</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Vietnam"},"facts":[["Capital","Hanoi"],["Population","102 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","331,317 km² (127,922 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Flag_of_Vietnam.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vietnam.svg)","alt":"The flag of Vietnam: a plain red field with a single large five-pointed yellow star in the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","vietnam"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Vietnam</b> was one of the three French colonies of Indochina, and by the late 1940s nationalist uprisings against French rule were taking their toll despite American financial help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> France promised autonomy but offered only a limited independence in which it kept charge of defence and foreign policy, and tried to counter the popular nationalist Ho Chi Minh by reinstating the former emperor Bao Dai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ho had not begun as an opponent of the United States: disappointed by the Versailles Conference’s indifference to colonised peoples, he made repeated requests for American aid before war broke out between his Viet Minh and French troops in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Washington recognised the State of Viet Nam on 7 February 1950 and ten days later raised its consulate general at Saigon to a legation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four years later the French garrison at Dien Bien Phu fell after a four-month siege, France pulled out of the region, and the powers meeting at Geneva split the territory along the 17th parallel with elections promised to reunite it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Those elections were never held: the United States backed the southern leader Ngo Dinh Diem when he refused a vote the communists seemed likely to win, and never recognised the northern government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1959 southern communist guerrillas had begun a large-scale insurgency, and after two American destroyers reported being fired on in the Gulf of Tonkin, Congress authorised President Johnson to take whatever measures he thought necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That resolution became the legal basis on which he and Nixon prosecuted the war, which Nixon took office expecting to end within a year and instead widened with secret bombing of North Vietnamese base camps in Cambodia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The troop withdrawals he began alongside it never afterwards stopped, and embassy staff were evacuated from Saigon just before the southern government surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No American mission stood in the country for the next twenty years, until relations were resumed on 5 August 1995, eighteen years after it had taken its seat at the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Viet Nam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Vietnam,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/vietnam. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Dien Bien Phu and the Fall of French Indochina, 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/dien-bien-phu. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “U.S. Involvement in the Vietnam War: the Gulf of Tonkin and Escalation, 1964,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/gulf-of-tonkin. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Ending the Vietnam War, 1969–1973,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/ending-vietnam. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Ha_Long_Bay_in_2019.jpg/1920px-Ha_Long_Bay_in_2019.jpg","title":"Ha Long Bay, Vietnam","desc":"Ha Long Bay.","credit":"Taewangkorea, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ha_Long_Bay_in_2019.jpg","alt":"A view of Ha Long Bay."}},
{"id":"gw-017","num":17,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Iran","answerText":"Iran","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 1850</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1883</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1980</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iran"},"facts":[["Capital","Tehran"],["Population","92 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Area","1,630,848 km² (629,674 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/Flag_of_Iran.svg","credit":"Design by Hamid Nadimi, construction by ISIRI, SVG by SiBr4; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iran.svg)","alt":"The flag of Iran: three horizontal bands of green, white and red, with a red emblem in the centre and a band of repeated white script along the inner edges of the green and red stripes."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","iran"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Iran</b> and the United States were both long-established states when they first dealt with each other formally, and the approach came from the Persian side, whose chargé d’affaires at Constantinople offered in April 1850 to seek authority for a treaty of commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The treaty was finalised the following year and then failed, because the Persian government took no action on a most-favoured-nation clause the U.S. Senate had added in its ratification resolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A resident mission followed a generation later, when the chargé d’affaires S. G. W. Benjamin presented his credentials to the Shah of Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During the Second World War the country was the setting for the first meeting of the three Allied leaders, and Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin spent four days there at the end of November 1943 agreeing to invade northern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oil dominated the decade that followed: the government nationalised the assets of the Anglo-Iranian Oil Company in March 1951, and the dispute drew in the United States as a mediator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> American documents released only decades afterwards record what the official account left out: the part played by the Central Intelligence Agency, and the covert action that led to the overthrow of Prime Minister Mohammad Mosadeq on 19 August 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The volume published in 1989 had carried none of it, and the criticism that followed changed United States law to oblige the government to publish a thorough and reliable record of its foreign policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A quarter of a century after the coup the monarchy fell in what the State Department’s own historians describe as one of the 20th century’s most significant revolutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Student militants seized the American embassy and its staff on 4 November 1979, and the failure to secure their release led Washington to break off relations the following spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hostages were freed on 20 January 1981, but relations were never restored and Switzerland has served ever since as the protecting power for American interests in a founding member of the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Iran (Islamic Republic of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ir.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iran,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iran. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Tehran Conference, 1943,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/tehran-conf. [Open access]","James C. Van Hook, ed., “Preface,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1952–1954, Iran, 1951–1954</i> (Washington: U.S. Government Publishing Office, 2017), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1951-54Iran/preface. [Open access]","Linda Qaimmaqami, ed., “Preface,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1977–1980, Volume XI, Part 1, Iran: Hostage Crisis, November 1979–September 1980</i> (Washington: U.S. Government Publishing Office, 2020), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1977-80v11p1/preface. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Hall_of_Hundred_Columns_%28Throne_Hall%29_in_Persepolis.jpg/1920px-Hall_of_Hundred_Columns_%28Throne_Hall%29_in_Persepolis.jpg","title":"Persepolis, Iran","desc":"Persepolis.","credit":"Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hall_of_Hundred_Columns_(Throne_Hall)_in_Persepolis.jpg","alt":"A view of Persepolis."}},
{"id":"gw-018","num":18,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Turkey","answerText":"Turkey","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 February 1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 April 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 February 1927</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turkey"},"facts":[["Capital","Ankara"],["Population","88 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","783,562 km² (302,535 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Flag_of_Turkey.svg","credit":"Drawn by David Benbennick, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Turkey.svg)","alt":"The flag of Turkey: a red field bearing a white crescent moon with a small white five-pointed star to its right."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","turkey"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Turkey</b> arose in the place of the Ottoman Empire, and it was with the Ottomans — both states long established already — that the United States first dealt, when an American team of Captain James Biddle, David Offley and Charles Rhind presented credentials to the Turkish foreign minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They negotiated a treaty of navigation and commerce, and diplomatic relations followed the next year with an American legation at Constantinople, opened when David Porter presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation was raised to embassy rank in 1906, and the connection then held until the Ottoman government cut it off after the United States declared war on Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the war was over the Allies excluded the defeated Central Powers from the Paris Peace Conference — Germany, Austria-Hungary, Bulgaria and the Ottoman Empire alike — and settled the peace among themselves at Versailles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The empire did not survive the peace, and Washington re-established relations with the republic that replaced it by an exchange of notes at Angora.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country mattered to the United States in a new way after the Second World War, when Britain announced that from 31 March 1947 it would stop paying for the Greek government’s civil war against the Greek communists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> President Truman went before a joint session of Congress that March to ask for aid to Greece and for aid to its neighbour as well, which had depended on the same British money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The principle he set out — that the United States would help any democratic nation threatened from outside or within — turned American policy away from staying out of distant quarrels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Greek civil war and the tensions beside it were among the reasons Western Europe and the United States built the North Atlantic alliance, and in 1952 its members agreed to admit both countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The republic has belonged to the United Nations since the organisation was founded, and the UN now lists it under the Turkish spelling of its name, Türkiye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Türkiye,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Turkey,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/turkey. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Truman Doctrine, 1947,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/truman-doctrine. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nato. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Hagia_Sophia_%28228968325%29.jpeg/1920px-Hagia_Sophia_%28228968325%29.jpeg","title":"Hagia Sophia, Turkey","desc":"Hagia Sophia.","credit":"Adli Wahid, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagia_Sophia_(228968325).jpeg","alt":"A view of Hagia Sophia."}},
{"id":"gw-513","num":513,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Cairo","answerText":"Cairo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 February 1974</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Egypt","dot":"Cairo"},"facts":[["Country","Egypt"],["Population","20.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Also capital of","the United Arab Republic"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","egypt"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Cairo</b> was the working seat of Egyptian government at a time when Egypt’s foreign relations were not conducted from it at all, but through the Ottoman Sultan’s government at Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain established a protectorate in 1882, taking effective control of those relations while the country remained nominally Ottoman, so the American presence in the city stayed a quasi-diplomatic one under an Agent and Consul General.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was that officer, J. Morton Howell, who delivered President Warren G. Harding’s letter recognising Egyptian independence to King Ahmed Fuad, and the American post in the city changed character with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Howell became the diplomatic representative, and on 24 June 1922 his position was raised to envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary, which is how the American legation in Egypt came to be established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomacy there was broken by war: between 5 and 10 June 1967 Israel defeated Egypt, Jordan and Syria and occupied the Sinai, Gaza, the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights, and Egypt accused Washington of helping it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The day after relations were cut an American interests section opened inside the Spanish embassy here, an arrangement that kept a channel open for nearly seven years without any formal tie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What ended it was Henry Kissinger’s shuttle diplomacy after the next war, when he travelled between Jerusalem and this city and put together an Egyptian-Israeli disengagement in eight days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two governments agreed to resume relations, and the American embassy in the city reopened the same day with Ambassador-designate Hermann F. Eilts in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four years later President Anwar Sadat signed the Camp David framework with President Carter and Menachem Begin, and a peace treaty between Israel and Egypt followed in March 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American post here has been in turn a consulate under Ottoman suzerainty, a legation, an interests section inside somebody else’s embassy and an embassy of its own, and through all of it the city remained the capital of a founding member of the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Egypt,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/eg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Egypt,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/egypt. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The 1967 Arab-Israeli War,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/arab-israeli-war-1967. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Kissinger's Shuttle Diplomacy, 1974,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/shuttle-diplomacy. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Camp David Accords and the Arab-Israeli Peace Process,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1977-1980/camp-david. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Cairo_skyline%2C_Nile_River%2C_Egypt.jpg/1920px-Cairo_skyline%2C_Nile_River%2C_Egypt.jpg","title":"Cairo","desc":"Cairo, seen from a distance.","credit":"Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cairo_skyline,_Nile_River,_Egypt.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Cairo."}},
{"id":"gw-019","num":19,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Germany","answerText":"Germany","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">German Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 January 1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reunified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Germany"},"facts":[["Capital","Berlin"],["Population","84 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Area","357,581 km² (138,063 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Flag_of_Germany.svg","credit":"Drawn by User:SKopp, User:Madden and others, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany.svg)","alt":"The flag of Germany: three equal horizontal bands of black, red and gold."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","germany"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Germany</b> was a conglomeration of more than 300 kingdoms, duchies, principalities and free cities in the 18th century, and took form across the 19th under the helmsmanship of the largest of those monarchies, Prussia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prussia was the first of them to recognise the United States, signing a treaty of amity and commerce with it in 1785, and John Quincy Adams was sent as minister twelve years later to renew it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His letters of credence were addressed to a king who died before he could be received, so the new king recognised him at a private audience as the minister accredited to his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Washington had said in 1849 that it would recognise any unified German government that appeared capable of maintaining its power, so when the empire was proclaimed it re-accredited its minister to Prussia as minister to the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were rocky in the first half of the 20th century, the two states fighting on opposite sides in both world wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> After the second of them American, British and Soviet forces divided and occupied the country, and its capital, lying far inside the Soviet-controlled east, was divided with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first crisis of the Cold War in Europe began on 24 June 1948, when Soviet forces blockaded the rail, road and water access to the Allied sectors and the western powers answered by flying in food and fuel until it was lifted eleven months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second opened with Khrushchev’s demand of November 1958 that the western powers pull their forces out of West Berlin within six months, and ended three years later with the building of the Berlin Wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thousands of Germans brought that wall down on 9 November 1989, two years after Ronald Reagan had stood beside it and called on Mikhail Gorbachev to tear it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The communist governments of eastern Europe collapsed one after another, and within a year the country was whole again, holding the United Nations seat it had entered in the 1970s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Germany,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/de.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Germany,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/germany. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Airlift, 1948–1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Crisis, 1958–1961,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/berlin-crises. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Fall of Communism in Eastern Europe, 1989,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/fall-of-communism. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Schloss_Neuschwanstein_2013.jpg/1920px-Schloss_Neuschwanstein_2013.jpg","title":"Neuschwanstein Castle, Germany","desc":"Neuschwanstein Castle.","credit":"Thomas Wolf , www.foto-tw.de, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_Neuschwanstein_2013.jpg","alt":"A view of Neuschwanstein Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-020","num":20,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Thailand","answerText":"Thailand","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 March 1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 October 1882</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1946</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Thailand"},"facts":[["Capital","Bangkok"],["Population","72 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","513,140 km² (198,124 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Flag_of_Thailand.svg","credit":"Drawn by Zscout370, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Thailand.svg)","alt":"The flag of Thailand: five horizontal bands of red, white, blue, white and red, the blue band in the middle twice the width of the others."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","thailand"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Thailand</b> was one of the only countries in south and southeast Asia to avoid colonisation in the 19th century, and its first official dealing with the United States was a treaty of amity and commerce — the first treaty the Americans made with any Asian country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was negotiated by Edmund Roberts, a special agent sent to the Far East under instructions to make treaties with Cochinchina, Siam and the powers of Arabia on the Red Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ratifications were exchanged three years later, and a consular presence followed in 1859, when John Hassett Chandler became acting vice consul in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American legation in the country, then called Siam, opened when John A. Halderman presented his credentials as minister resident and consul general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was raised to an embassy only after the Second World War, Edwin F. Stanton being promoted to become the first American ambassador in April 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The kingdom’s place in the Cold War was fixed in September 1954, when it joined the United States, France, Britain, New Zealand, Australia, the Philippines and Pakistan in forming the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined after learning of a newly established “Thai Autonomous Region” in Yunnan province in southern China, and what it feared was Chinese communist subversion on its own soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only one other southeast Asian state joined, since Burma and Indonesia preferred to stay neutral, Malaya found formal support politically difficult, and the Geneva agreements of 1954 barred Vietnam, Cambodia and Laos from any military alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The organisation was headquartered in the kingdom’s own capital and kept no forces of its own, though it ran joint exercises each year and sponsored fellowships for southeast Asian scholars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state that housed it had entered the United Nations eight years earlier under the name Siam, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in November 1982, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Thailand,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/th.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Thailand,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/thailand. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO), 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/seato. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Thailand and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/thailand_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Siam to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 101 (I), 15 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209878. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%932.jpg/1920px-%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%932.jpg","title":"Wat Arun, Thailand","desc":"Wat Arun.","credit":"Mastertongapollo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%932.jpg","alt":"A view of Wat Arun."}},
{"id":"gw-021","num":21,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"United Kingdom","answerText":"United Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty of Paris</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1783</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ireland leaves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United Kingdom"},"facts":[["Capital","London"],["Population","70 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","243,610 km² (94,058 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_%283-5%29.svg","credit":"Vector drawing by Yaddah after the Acts of Union 1800, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg)","alt":"The flag of the United Kingdom: a blue field with a broad white-edged red cross upright and a red saltire edged in white running corner to corner."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","united kingdom"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>United Kingdom</b> was the state the thirteen American colonies declared their independence from, and the war that followed ran on for five years after the declaration, until General Cornwallis surrendered to George Washington on 19 October 1781.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came two years later at Paris, where David Hartley, a Member of Parliament representing King George III, signed the treaty alongside John Adams, Benjamin Franklin and John Jay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two states have been on generally cordial terms ever since, the exceptions being a break in relations over the War of 1812 and the strain caused by the possibility that Britain might recognise the Confederacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American consulates spread across the islands in the decades that followed: the first in Scotland at Leith in 1798, one at Belfast two years earlier still, and others at Liverpool, Bristol, Falmouth, Cardiff and a dozen smaller ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most were closed again as trade moved on: Aberdeen in 1922, Dundee in 1940, Glasgow in 1965 and Liverpool not until 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The union itself changed shape after the First World War, when the Anglo-Irish Treaty gave independence to 26 Irish counties, constituted as the Irish Free State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Six counties of northeast Ulster chose to remain part of the kingdom with limited self-government, and it is that territory whose area the World Bank measures today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised the Irish Free State as a state with autonomous control over its own foreign relations in June 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Britain signed the Brussels Treaty of March 1948 with France, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg, each bound to help defend any of the others attacked, and that idea became the North Atlantic alliance the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same months British and American aircraft supplied western Berlin through the Soviet blockade, flying in food and fuel from airbases in western Germany, and both governments did so as founding members of the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gb.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The United Kingdom,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/united-kingdom. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Airlift, 1948–1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nato. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): United Kingdom,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GBR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2022. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ireland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ireland. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Stonehenge2007_07_30.jpg/1920px-Stonehenge2007_07_30.jpg","title":"Stonehenge, the United Kingdom","desc":"Stonehenge.","credit":"garethwiscombe, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stonehenge2007_07_30.jpg","alt":"A view of Stonehenge."}},
{"id":"gw-514","num":514,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Manila","answerText":"Manila","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded by Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 December 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">SEATO formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1954</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Philippines","dot":"Manila"},"facts":[["Country","Philippines"],["Population","13.7 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","philippines"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Manila</b> was the capital of a longstanding Spanish colony until an American squadron sailed into its bay in the spring of 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first battle of the war with Spain was fought there on 1 May, when Commodore George Dewey’s Asiatic Squadron defeated the Spanish naval force defending the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The war ended with a treaty signed at Paris under which Spain agreed to sell the archipelago to the United States for twenty million dollars, and the Senate ratified it by a margin of one vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Filipinos who had been fighting for independence from Spain found the transfer no more than a change of colonial ruler, and Emilio Aguinaldo’s forces took control of most of Luzon and proclaimed an independent Philippine Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The war that followed lasted more than three years, and a civil government was established under the future President William Howard Taft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The islands convened their first elected assembly in 1907, and American opposition at home to colonial rule never went away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Independence came on the American national holiday, when President Truman proclaimed the Republic of the Philippines an independent state and the two governments signed a treaty by which the United States renounced all its claims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American ambassador, Paul V. McNutt, presented his credentials on the same day, which is the date diplomatic relations between the two states are counted from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eight years later the republic joined the United States, France, Britain, New Zealand, Australia, Thailand and Pakistan in forming the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization, partly out of its close ties with Washington and partly from concern at a communist insurgency threatening its own government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The new organisation put its headquarters in Bangkok rather than here, and this city has been throughout the seat of a state that has belonged to the United Nations since the organisation was founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Philippines,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ph.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Philippines,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/philippines. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Spanish-American War, 1898,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Philippine-American War, 1899–1902,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/war. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO), 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/seato. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Manila_skyline_day.jpg/1920px-Manila_skyline_day.jpg","title":"Manila","desc":"Manila, seen from a distance.","credit":"Mike Gonzalez ( TheCoffee ), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manila_skyline_day.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Manila."}},
{"id":"gw-022","num":22,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tanzania","answerText":"Tanzania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">joined 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tanzania"},"facts":[["Capital","Dodoma"],["Population","71 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","947,303 km² (365,756 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Flag_of_Tanzania.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tanzania.svg)","alt":"The flag of Tanzania: a green upper triangle and a blue lower one divided by a black diagonal band edged in yellow."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","tanzania"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tanzania</b> was called Tanganyika when it became independent, and Britain had administered it not as a colony but as a United Nations Trust Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Kennedy sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Julius Nyerere on the day it happened, and the American consulate general at Dar es Salaam was raised to embassy status the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Security Council recommended admission to the United Nations at its 986th meeting, and the General Assembly voted the country in at the 1078th plenary meeting of its sixteenth session, still under the name Tanganyika.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in the same year, following its independence from Britain, and entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade that December too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The united republic that carries the present name takes in the island of Zanzibar, and the Commonwealth’s own record still lists Dar es Salaam as an acting capital beside the official one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Independence came in the middle of a wave: some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960, by no single pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Some of the new countries had stable governments almost at once; others were ruled by dictators or military juntas for decades, or endured long civil wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Relations with the United States were cordial from independence onward, and they improved further after the attacks on the American embassies at Dar es Salaam and Nairobi on 7 August 1998.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat has since worked with the country on trade policy, on debt management and on regulating its petroleum sector, and a Tanzanian, Dr William Shija, became Secretary-General of the Commonwealth Parliamentary Association in 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened, and to the United Nations since five days after the British flag came down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Republic of Tanzania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tanzania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tanzania. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “United Republic of Tanzania,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/united-republic-tanzania. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tanzania and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tanzania_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Tanganyika to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1667 (XVI), 14 December 1961, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205649. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Kilimanjaro_from_Amboseli.jpg/1920px-Kilimanjaro_from_Amboseli.jpg","title":"Mount Kilimanjaro, Tanzania","desc":"Mount Kilimanjaro.","credit":"Sergey Pesterev, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kilimanjaro_from_Amboseli.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Kilimanjaro."}},
{"id":"gw-023","num":23,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"France","answerText":"France","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 February 1778</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 July 1798</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"France"},"facts":[["Capital","Paris"],["Population","67 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Area","551,500 km² (212,935 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Flag_of_France.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg)","alt":"The flag of France: three equal vertical bands of blue, white and red."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","france"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>France</b> is among the oldest allies of the United States: its monarchy recognised American independence when Conrad Alexandre Gérard, secretary of the king’s council of state, signed two treaties with Benjamin Franklin, Silas Deane and Arthur Lee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> French military and economic help during the American War of Independence was crucial to the victory, and the first American consular post in the country opened at Bordeaux the following month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began that August when Gérard presented his credentials to Congress, and Franklin was appointed the first American minister a month later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The revolution of 1789 overthrew the government of Louis XVI, and Thomas Jefferson’s rule that the United States should acknowledge as rightful any government formed by the will of a nation has been policy ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So relations survived every new French government constituted after 1789, most of them in the 19th century; each time the resident American representative simply submitted fresh credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The one real rupture came over the XYZ affair, when Congress abrogated the treaties of 1778 and the consular convention with them; a treaty signed at Mortefontaine in 1800 ended the Quasi-War that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A century later it was one of the Big Four, with Britain, the United States and Italy, that dominated the Paris Peace Conference and produced the Treaty of Versailles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany invaded on 15 May 1940 and the government left Paris for Bordeaux; parliament met at Vichy that July and granted Marshal Pétain full and extraordinary powers, and the American embassy moved near the new regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the war it signed the Brussels Treaty of March 1948 with Britain, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg, each bound to defend any of the others attacked, and the North Atlantic alliance was built on that idea the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1956 it landed troops beside Britain’s over the Suez Canal and drew a public censure from Washington that soured relations for a time; it has held a United Nations seat since the organisation was founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “France,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: France,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/france. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nato. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Suez Crisis, 1956,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/suez. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Tour_Eiffel_Wikimedia_Commons_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Tour_Eiffel_Wikimedia_Commons_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Eiffel Tower, France","desc":"Eiffel Tower.","credit":"Benh LIEU SONG, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_Eiffel_Wikimedia_Commons_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A view of Eiffel Tower."}},
{"id":"gw-024","num":24,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"South Africa","answerText":"South Africa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1910</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 November 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Africa"},"facts":[["Capital","Pretoria (administrative)"],["Population","65 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Africa"],["Area","1,221,037 km² (471,445 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Flag_of_South_Africa.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Africa.svg)","alt":"The flag of South Africa: a green horizontal Y shape edged in white and gold, separating red above from blue below, with a black triangle at the hoist."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","south africa"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>South Africa</b> began as a union of four colonies joined together as a self-governing Dominion of the British Empire, sovereign in its domestic affairs while London kept control of its relations with the wider world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It gained control of its external policy over the next two decades, and the Balfour Declaration issued at the end of the Imperial Conference of 1926 recognised the United Kingdom and the Dominions as autonomous communities equal in status and subordinate to one another in nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Statute of Westminster codified that relationship in December 1931, which is the year the Commonwealth counts its membership from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States had recognised the Union two years earlier, when President Hoover accepted the credentials of Eric Hendrik Louw as its envoy in Washington and the South African legation there opened on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation followed at Cape Town, moved to Pretoria the next year, and both missions were raised to embassies in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The government declared the country a republic on the fifty-first anniversary of the union, the American ambassador was reaccredited to it that same day, and it left the Commonwealth in the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By then the election of the Nationalist Party in 1948 had begun apartheid, the Afrikaans name that party gave to a harsh, institutionalised system of racial segregation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many segregationist policies dated from the early decades of the century, but that election marked the beginning of legalised racism’s harshest features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Truman administration chose not to protest, its foremost aim being to limit Soviet expansion, and successive administrations quietly supported the regime as an ally against communism in southern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Violent internal protest, weakening white commitment, international sanctions, economic struggle and the end of the Cold War brought minority rule down; a democratic government was formed in 1994, the country rejoined the Commonwealth, and it has sat at the United Nations since 1945 and traded under the GATT since 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “South Africa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/za.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Africa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The End of Apartheid,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/apartheid. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “South Africa and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/south_africa_e.htm. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “South Africa,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/south-africa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Table_Mountain_DanieVDM.jpg/1920px-Table_Mountain_DanieVDM.jpg","title":"Table Mountain, South Africa","desc":"Table Mountain.","credit":"Danie van der Merwe from Cape Town, South Africa, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Table_Mountain_DanieVDM.jpg","alt":"A view of Table Mountain."}},
{"id":"gw-515","num":515,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Kinshasa","answerText":"Kinshasa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 July 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dem. Rep. Congo","dot":"Kinshasa"},"facts":[["Country","Democratic Republic of the Congo"],["Population","13.7 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Formerly","Léopoldville"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","congo"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Kinshasa</b> was called Léopoldville when the country around it became independent of Belgium, and the American embassy opened there the same day with John D. Tomlinson as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the months before that the Congolese had elected a president, Joseph Kasavubu, a prime minister, Patrice Lumumba, a senate and an assembly, and similar bodies in each of the country’s many provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Eisenhower administration had high hopes of a stable, pro-Western central government, and those hopes vanished in a matter of days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soldiers of the Force Publique mutinied against their white Belgian commanders at the Thysville base on 5 July, seeking better pay and greater authority; the mutiny spread and violence broke out across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Belgium sent troops to restore order without asking either Kasavubu or Lumumba, and the government appealed to the United Nations for troops of its own and demanded that the Belgians withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Security Council answered on 13 July, authorising an intervention force and calling on Belgium to withdraw — and the UN’s own record of that resolution identifies the country by this city, under its old name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Katanga province had declared its independence two days earlier and South Kasai followed in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lawrence Devlin, the CIA chief of station here, described the situation as a classic communist takeover, and his reports helped convince Washington that Lumumba had to be removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kasavubu dismissed Lumumba in September, Lumumba dismissed Kasavubu, and the first Cold War proxy confrontation over the newly independent, non-aligned states of Africa was under way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There were dozens of those states — some three dozen countries in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960 — and this city has been the capital of one of them at the United Nations ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic Republic of the Congo,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cd.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Democratic Republic of the Congo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/congo-democratic-republic. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Congo, Decolonization, and the Cold War, 1960–1965,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/congo-decolonization. [Open access]","UN Security Council, resolution 143 (1960), adopted at the 873rd meeting, 13 July 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/619961. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Boulevard_du_30_juin%2C_Kinshasa.jpg/1920px-Boulevard_du_30_juin%2C_Kinshasa.jpg","title":"Kinshasa","desc":"Kinshasa, seen from a distance.","credit":"MONUSCO/Myriam Asmani, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boulevard_du_30_juin,_Kinshasa.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kinshasa."}},
{"id":"gw-025","num":25,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Italy","answerText":"Italy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Unification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 April 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Italy"},"facts":[["Capital","Rome"],["Population","59 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","302,068 km² (116,629 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/03/Flag_of_Italy.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy.svg)","alt":"The flag of Italy: three equal vertical bands of green, white and red."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","italy"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Italy</b> did not exist as a state when the United States first dealt with the peninsula: Washington had relations with the Kingdom of Sardinia, the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies and the Papal States, and legations at Naples from 1831, Turin from 1840 and Rome from 1848.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It kept consulates at virtually every seaport as well, because trade, shipping and the protection of seamen were the main American business there, and 41 of them are listed for the ground the country now covers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The modern state began in 1861 with the unification of most of the peninsula under the House of Savoy, and it took in Venetia and the former Papal States, Rome among them, by 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation to Sardinia simply became the legation to the new kingdom, its minister presenting fresh credentials on the day the United States recognised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations stayed warm for eighty years and broke only once, when Benito Mussolini’s government declared war on the United States and stayed at war with it for two years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The kingdom gave way to a republic in 1946, and relations resumed with the successor state as they had been with its predecessor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had been one of the Big Four — with Britain, France and the United States — that dominated the Paris Peace Conference and produced the Treaty of Versailles a generation earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The republic joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in May 1950 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is a founding member of what became the European Union, having been in it from the first day of 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations admitted it only in 1955, a decade after the organisation was founded and nine years after the republic replaced the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Italy,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/it.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Italy,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/italy. [Open access]","European Union, “Italy – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/italy_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Italy and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/italy_e.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Colosseo_2020.jpg/1920px-Colosseo_2020.jpg","title":"Colosseum, Italy","desc":"Colosseum.","credit":"FeaturedPics, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colosseo_2020.jpg","alt":"A view of Colosseum."}},
{"id":"gw-026","num":26,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Kenya","answerText":"Kenya","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1895</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1963</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1963</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kenya"},"facts":[["Capital","Nairobi"],["Population","58 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","591,958 km² (228,556 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Flag_of_Kenya.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kenya.svg)","alt":"The flag of Kenya: horizontal bands of black, red and green separated by thin white stripes, with a red and white Maasai shield and two crossed spears over the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","kenya"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The ground that became <b>Kenya</b> was under British colonial rule as part of British East Africa from 1895 until independence 68 years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two days before the flag changed, President Johnson’s personal representative, Secretary of the Interior Stewart Udall, delivered a message of congratulation to Prime Minister Jomo Kenyatta with the rank of special ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the new state on the day it became independent, and the American embassy at Nairobi was established the following March when Ambassador William Attwood presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth in the same year, following its independence from Britain, and the General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations at the 1281st plenary meeting of its eighteenth session.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade less than two months after independence and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Independence came at the end of a wave: some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960, by no single pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Some of those countries had stable governments almost at once; others were ruled by dictators or military juntas for decades, or endured long civil wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1998 the American embassy in the capital was the target of a terrorist bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It killed 12 American citizens, 32 foreign service nationals and 247 Kenyans, and injured about 5,000 Kenyans along with 6 Americans and 13 foreign service nationals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has held its seat at the United Nations ever since the December it was admitted, and the Commonwealth counts its membership from the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Kenya,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ke.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kenya,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kenya. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Kenya,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/kenya. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Kenya and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/kenya_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Kenya to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1976 (XVIII), 16 December 1963, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/203764. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Masai_Mara_at_Sunset.jpg/1920px-Masai_Mara_at_Sunset.jpg","title":"Maasai Mara, Kenya","desc":"Maasai Mara.","credit":"Danijel Mihajlovic, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masai_Mara_at_Sunset.jpg","alt":"A view of Maasai Mara."}},
{"id":"gw-027","num":27,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Myanmar","answerText":"Myanmar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 January 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1948</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Myanmar"},"facts":[["Capital","Naypyidaw"],["Population","55 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","676,577 km² (261,228 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Flag_of_Myanmar.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Myanmar.svg)","alt":"The flag of Myanmar: three horizontal bands of yellow, green and red with a large white five-pointed star across the centre."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","myanmar"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Myanmar</b> was called Burma when it left the British Empire, and the United States established relations with it before it was formally independent — an unconventional sequence the Office of the Historian remarks on in its own summary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The independence agreed between Burmese leaders and the British government in January 1947 ran in stages, and it allowed a provisional government to conduct relations with other states before full sovereignty arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During that year the Burmese chose a constituent assembly, wrote a constitution and set up that provisional government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 18 September 1947 the two states announced that they had agreed to exchange representatives with the rank of ambassador, and the American consulate general at Rangoon was raised to an embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Formal recognition followed on 4 January 1948, in a congratulatory message from President Truman to President Sao Shwe Thaike, the Saopha of Yawnghwe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted the Union of Burma at the 131st plenary meeting of its second special session, three months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade that July, one of the earliest members of the post-war trading system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization was formed in 1954, only two southeast Asian states joined it: this country and Indonesia both preferred to keep their neutrality rather than take a side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rest of the region was far less troubled by communism as a threat to internal stability than the two states that did join, the Philippines and Thailand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened, and to the United Nations — which spells the capital Nay Pyi Taw — since the spring of 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Myanmar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burma,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burma. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Myanmar and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/myanmar_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Union of Burma to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 188 (S-2), 19 April 1948, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/210015. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO), 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/seato. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Bagan%2C_Burma.jpg","title":"Bagan, Myanmar","desc":"Bagan.","credit":"Corto Maltese 1999, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagan,_Burma.jpg","alt":"A view of Bagan."}},
{"id":"gw-516","num":516,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Hanoi","answerText":"Hanoi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liaison office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 January 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Vietnam","dot":"Hanoi"},"facts":[["Country","Vietnam"],["Population","4.5 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Formerly","French Indochina"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","vietnam"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hanoi</b> got its first American consulate while the country around it was still a French colony and general war was already under way in the north between Ho Chi Minh’s Viet Minh and French troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Washington recognised the State of Viet Nam three years later it dealt with Saigon instead, raising the consulate general there to a legation and then, in 1952, to an embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fall of the French garrison at Dien Bien Phu in May 1954 ended the First Indochina War, France pulled out of the region, and the powers at Geneva divided the country along the 17th parallel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States was not a signatory to the Geneva accords and did not recognise the government in this city, conducting relations solely with the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It backed the southern leader Ngo Dinh Diem when he refused the elections meant to reunify the country, which the communists seemed likely to win.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> By 1959 southern communist guerrillas had begun a large-scale insurgency, and after two American destroyers reported being fired on in the Gulf of Tonkin, Congress gave President Johnson authority to take whatever measures he thought necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nixon inherited the war in 1969 believing he could end it honourably within a year, and instead ordered secret bombing of North Vietnamese base camps in Cambodia while beginning troop withdrawals that never stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Henry Kissinger held his first private session with the northern leadership in August of that year and met the politburo member Le Duc Tho intermittently for months with no apparent progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American embassy in Saigon was evacuated in April 1975, and for twenty years the United States had no mission anywhere in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A liaison office opened here on 28 January 1995, relations were resumed that August, and the embassy here opened the day after the documents were signed, eighteen years after the reunified country had taken its seat at the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Viet Nam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Vietnam,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/vietnam. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Dien Bien Phu and the Fall of French Indochina, 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/dien-bien-phu. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “U.S. Involvement in the Vietnam War: the Gulf of Tonkin and Escalation, 1964,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/gulf-of-tonkin. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Ending the Vietnam War, 1969–1973,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/ending-vietnam. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Purple_haze_of_Hanoi_Skyline_%2822507027147%29.jpg/1920px-Purple_haze_of_Hanoi_Skyline_%2822507027147%29.jpg","title":"Hanoi","desc":"Hanoi, seen from a distance.","credit":"cloud.shepherd, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Purple_haze_of_Hanoi_Skyline_(22507027147).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hanoi."}},
{"id":"gw-028","num":28,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Colombia","answerText":"Colombia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 1819</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 June 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Panama leaves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1903</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Colombia"},"facts":[["Capital","Bogotá"],["Population","53 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","1,141,748 km² (440,831 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Flag_of_Colombia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colombia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Colombia: a broad yellow band across the top half, with blue and red bands of equal width below it."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","colombia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Colombia</b> effectively won its independence from Spain by 1819, and when the United States recognised it three years later the federation of that name took in the territory of four present-day republics: itself, Ecuador, Venezuela and Panama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Monroe recognised it by receiving the Colombian chargé d’affaires Manuel Torres in Washington, and an American minister presented his credentials in Bogotá the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American consulates on the ground the country now covers were opened in 1823 in the Caribbean ports of Cartagena and Santa Marta, and others followed at Barranquilla, Bucaramanga, Buenaventura, Cali and Medellín; none of them remain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were not interrupted when Ecuador and Venezuela left the federation in 1830, and the state went on changing its own name — New Granada until 1856, the Grenadine Confederation until 1863, then the United States of Colombia, and the Republic of Colombia from 1886.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Panama separated in 1903, after the Colombian congress rejected as financially unacceptable a canal treaty its own foreign minister had signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A treaty of friendship and commerce concluded with New Granada in 1846 is still in force, and the American legation in the capital was raised to an embassy in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A separate boundary settled in 1928 gave Nicaragua the Mosquito Coast and the Corn Islands and this country the islands of San Andrés, Providencia and Santa Catalina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nicaragua reopened the question at the International Court of Justice in 2001, and the Court held in 2012 that the disputed cays — Alburquerque, Bajo Nuevo, the East-Southeast Cays, Quitasueño, Roncador, Serrana and Serranilla — belonged to this country and not to Nicaragua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It moved the maritime boundary eastwards all the same, since Nicaragua’s relevant coast was more than eight times the length of the other side’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country was among the states that founded the United Nations, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in 1981 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since April 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Colombia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/co.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Colombia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/colombia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Building the Panama Canal, 1903–1914,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/panama-canal. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Colombia and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/colombia_e.htm. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Territorial and Maritime Dispute (Nicaragua v. Colombia),” Case 124, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/124. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Ca%C3%B1o_Cristales_01.jpg/1920px-Ca%C3%B1o_Cristales_01.jpg","title":"Caño Cristales, Colombia","desc":"Caño Cristales.","credit":"Astromario, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ca%C3%B1o_Cristales_01.jpg","alt":"A view of Caño Cristales."}},
{"id":"gw-029","num":29,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"South Korea","answerText":"South Korea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Korean War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1950–53</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Korea"},"facts":[["Capital","Seoul"],["Population","52 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Asia"],["Area","100,401 km² (38,765 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/Flag_of_South_Korea.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Korea.svg)","alt":"The flag of South Korea: a white field with a red and blue circular taegeuk in the centre and four groups of black bars at the corners."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","korea"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"During the Second World War the United States and the Soviet Union agreed to divide Korea temporarily at the 38th parallel in order to oversee the removal of Japanese forces, and neither would afterwards let its own ally be threatened by unification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviets supported Kim Il Sung in the north; the Americans backed Syngman Rhee in the south, and <b>South Korea</b> is the state that grew out of that half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Washington recognised the Republic of Korea, with its capital at Seoul, as an independent state in a White House statement, and diplomatic relations followed in March 1949 when Ambassador John Myun Chang presented his credentials to President Truman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States did not then make the peninsula a key part of its defensive strategy for east Asia, and American forces withdrew in the late 1940s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Assuming Washington did not regard the southern state as a vital interest, Kim’s army attacked in June 1950 and almost conquered the whole peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The American military returned, leading a force authorised by the United Nations that pushed the northern army back above the 38th parallel and beyond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> China entered the war in late 1950, the two sides reached an uneasy truce in 1953, and the division that exists today was crystallised by it; the same year the two states signed a mutual security treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The end of the Cold War changed the peninsula’s diplomatic landscape: this state established relations with the Soviet Union in 1990 and with China in 1992, both longstanding allies of its northern neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both Koreas were admitted to the United Nations simultaneously in 1991, 42 years after the southern one was first recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in April 1967 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Republic of Korea (South Korea),” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/korea-south. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Korean War and Japan's Recovery,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Denuclearization and the Two Koreas, 1993–2001,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1993-2000/two-koreas. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Korea, Republic of and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/korea_republic_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Front_view_of_the_Imperial_Throne_Hall_Geunjeongjeon_at_Gyeongbokgung_Palace_with_blue_sky_in_Seoul.jpg/1920px-Front_view_of_the_Imperial_Throne_Hall_Geunjeongjeon_at_Gyeongbokgung_Palace_with_blue_sky_in_Seoul.jpg","title":"Gyeongbokgung, South Korea","desc":"Gyeongbokgung.","credit":"Basile Morin, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_view_of_the_Imperial_Throne_Hall_Geunjeongjeon_at_Gyeongbokgung_Palace_with_blue_sky_in_Seoul.jpg","alt":"A view of Gyeongbokgung."}},
{"id":"gw-030","num":30,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Sudan","answerText":"Sudan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1967</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sudan"},"facts":[["Capital","Khartoum"],["Population","52 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Area","1,878,000 km² (725,100 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Flag_of_Sudan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sudan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sudan: horizontal bands of red, white and black with a green triangle at the hoist."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","sudan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Sudan</b> had been under foreign control as an Anglo-Egyptian condominium, and the United States recognised the new republic on the first day of 1956 in a letter delivered by its liaison officer in the capital to Abdel Fattah Mohammed del Maghrabi, president of the Supreme Commission of Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Six weeks later that liaison office was raised to embassy status, with the same officer, Arthur Beach, as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence came in the middle of a wave: some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960, by no single pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some had stable governments almost at once; others were ruled by dictators or military juntas for decades, or endured long civil wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country at the 574th plenary meeting of its eleventh session, ten months after the flag changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The American embassy closed on 6 June 1967 after demonstrations and rock-throwing outside it, and the government severed relations the following day in the wake of that month’s Arab-Israeli war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American interests section opened inside the Netherlands embassy in the capital that August, and full relations were not restored until the embassy reopened in July 1972.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ambassador Cleo A. Noel, Jr. was assassinated at his post there in March 1973, eight months after the embassy had reopened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy suspended operations again in February 1996 and did not resume them until May 2002, its ambassador having sat out part of the interval in Nairobi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country lost its southern third when South Sudan seceded, and the arithmetic of that is visible in the record: the World Bank’s measurement of its surface area falls from 2,505,810 square kilometres to the present figure between 2011 and 2012, while the United Nations profile for the country carries no surface-area row at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sudan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sd.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sudan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sudan. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Sudan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SDN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2008:2022. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Sudan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1110 (XI), 12 November 1956, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207992. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Mer%C3%B6e_Pyramids_with_camles_%2835119048185%29.jpg/1920px-Mer%C3%B6e_Pyramids_with_camles_%2835119048185%29.jpg","title":"Meroë, Sudan","desc":"The pyramids of Meroë.","credit":"Hans Birger Nilsen, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mer%C3%B6e_Pyramids_with_camles_(35119048185).jpg","alt":"Steep-sided pyramids standing in desert sand."}},
{"id":"gw-517","num":517,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Tehran","answerText":"Tehran","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1883</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Allied conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1943</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy seized</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 November 1979</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iran","dot":"Tehran"},"facts":[["Country","Iran"],["Population","9.0 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Protecting power","Switzerland"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","iran"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tehran</b> got its first resident American mission in June 1883, when the chargé d’affaires S. G. W. Benjamin presented his credentials to the Shah of Persia and the American legation in the city was established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two states had first dealt with each other formally a generation earlier, and the treaty of commerce they negotiated then never came into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city was the setting for the first meeting of the three Allied leaders of the Second World War, Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin spending four days there at the end of November 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They used it to settle the timing of the invasion of northern France, to agree that the Soviet Union would declare war on Japan once Germany was beaten, and to discuss the fate of Poland and Germany after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eight years later the government nationalised the assets of the Anglo-Iranian Oil Company, and the dispute that followed drew the United States in as a mediator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> American documents released only decades afterwards record what the official account left out — the part played by the Central Intelligence Agency, and the covert action that led to the overthrow of Prime Minister Mohammad Mosadeq on 19 August 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The volume published in 1989 had carried none of it, and the criticism that followed changed United States law to oblige the government to publish a thorough and reliable record of its foreign policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A quarter of a century after the coup the monarchy fell in what the State Department’s own historians describe as one of the 20th century’s most significant revolutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Student militants seized the American embassy here and its staff on 4 November 1979, and the failure to secure their release led Washington to break off relations the following spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hostages were freed on 20 January 1981, relations were never restored, and Switzerland has acted ever since as the protecting power for American interests in the country whose capital this is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Iran (Islamic Republic of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ir.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iran,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iran. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Tehran Conference, 1943,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/tehran-conf. [Open access]","James C. Van Hook, ed., “Preface,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1952–1954, Iran, 1951–1954</i> (Washington: U.S. Government Publishing Office, 2017), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1951-54Iran/preface. [Open access]","Linda Qaimmaqami, ed., “Preface,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1977–1980, Volume XI, Part 1, Iran: Hostage Crisis, November 1979–September 1980</i> (Washington: U.S. Government Publishing Office, 2020), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1977-80v11p1/preface. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Skyline_of_Tehran_on_a_hot_summer_day_in_2015_-_04.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Tehran_on_a_hot_summer_day_in_2015_-_04.jpg","title":"Tehran","desc":"Tehran, seen from a distance.","credit":"GolhaMedia, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Tehran_on_a_hot_summer_day_in_2015_-_04.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Tehran."}},
{"id":"gw-031","num":31,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Uganda","answerText":"Uganda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 October 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1973–79</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uganda"},"facts":[["Capital","Kampala"],["Population","51 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","241,550 km² (93,263 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Flag_of_Uganda.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uganda.svg)","alt":"The flag of Uganda: six horizontal bands of black, yellow and red repeated twice, with a white disc in the centre carrying a grey crested crane."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","uganda"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Uganda</b> had been under British sovereignty until President Kennedy sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Milton Obote on the day it became independent, which is the day the United States recognised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American consulate general at Kampala was raised to embassy status the same day, with Olcott H. Deming as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth in the same year, following its independence from Britain, and the General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations at the 1158th plenary meeting of its seventeenth session, a fortnight after the flag changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade a fortnight later still and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Independence came at the end of a wave: some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960, by no single pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Some of those countries had stable governments almost at once; others were ruled by dictators or military juntas for decades, or endured long civil wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> This one closed its American embassy on 10 November 1973, when every United States diplomatic officer was withdrawn from the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Department of State later gave its reasons: persistent internal security problems, operating difficulties for American programmes and personnel, repeated public threats against embassy officials and other Americans by senior Ugandan officials, and finally the abrupt expulsion of the marine guard that protected the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations themselves were never interrupted — the Ugandan embassy in Washington stayed open, and West Germany represented American interests in the meantime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American embassy was re-established on 18 June 1979, and the Commonwealth Secretariat still counts the country’s membership from the year it became independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Uganda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ug.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uganda,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uganda. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Uganda,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/uganda. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Uganda and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/uganda_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Uganda to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1758 (XVII), 25 October 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/204481. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Murchison_Twin_Falls%2C_Uganda_%2816093084916%29.jpg","title":"Murchison Falls, Uganda","desc":"Murchison Falls.","credit":"Rod Waddington, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murchison_Twin_Falls,_Uganda_(16093084916).jpg","alt":"A view of Murchison Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-032","num":32,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Spain","answerText":"Spain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 February 1783</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1986</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Spain"},"facts":[["Capital","Madrid"],["Population","48 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","506,008 km² (195,371 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/Flag_of_Spain.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain.svg)","alt":"The flag of Spain: a red band, a broader yellow band and another red band, with the national coat of arms toward the hoist side of the yellow."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","spain"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The Continental Congress sent John Jay to <b>Spain</b> in 1779 to persuade the court to recognise the new American republic, and he spent two years there without success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Madrid was unwilling to risk its relations with the Congress in Philadelphia until it became clear that Britain and the United States were actually going to sign a treaty ending the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came when the American chargé d’affaires William Carmichael was officially received at court, though he had already been in the country for nine months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A consulate opened at Barcelona at the end of 1797, and a treaty of friendship, limits and navigation had been signed two years before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the two and a half centuries since, the two states have broken relations exactly once — in 1898, when the minister closed the legation in the capital on the day relations were severed and Congress declared war four days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The war that followed cost the country the last of its overseas empire: the treaty guaranteed the independence of Cuba, forced Spain to cede Guam and Puerto Rico to the United States, and had it sell the Philippines for twenty million dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Relations resumed the next year, and the American legation was raised to embassy rank in 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During the civil war of 1936 to 1939 the American embassy moved first to the consulate at Barcelona and then out of the country altogether, to St Jean de Luz in France, before being re-established at Burgos in April 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in August 1963 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a member of what is now the European Union at the start of 1986, and has held a seat at the United Nations since 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Spain,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/es.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Spain,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/spain. [Open access]","European Union, “Spain – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/spain_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Spain and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/spain_e.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Spanish-American War, 1898,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/SF_maig_2_cropped.jpg","title":"Sagrada Família, Spain","desc":"Sagrada Família.","credit":"Canaan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SF_maig_2_cropped.jpg","alt":"A view of Sagrada Família."}},
{"id":"gw-033","num":33,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Algeria","answerText":"Algeria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">French conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 June 1967</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Algeria"},"facts":[["Capital","Algiers"],["Population","47 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Area","2,381,741 km² (919,595 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Flag_of_Algeria.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Algeria.svg)","alt":"The flag of Algeria: a green half and a white half, with a red crescent and five-pointed star across the join."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","algeria"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"When the United States became independent, <b>Algeria</b> was a nominal vassal of the Ottoman Empire with the power to conduct its own foreign relations, and it recognised the young republic before most of Europe did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition took the form of a peace treaty signed in September 1795, ending a state of war the Dey of Algiers had declared on the United States a decade earlier, when Congress could raise neither a navy nor the tribute the Barbary states demanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Formal American consular representation at Algiers began the following March, when Joel Barlow presented his credentials to Dey Hassan; an earlier appointee, John Paul Jones, had died before reaching the post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consulate was interrupted from 1812 to 1815 by the Algerian War, one of the Barbary wars, and the United States sent a squadron against Algiers in 1815 rather than go on paying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Self-rule ended with the French conquest of 1830, and in 1848 the French government formally incorporated the coast into France itself as the departments of Algiers, Oran and Constantine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country voted for independence in a referendum on 1 July 1962, and France recognised it as an independent state two days later, on the same day as the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American consulate general at Algiers was raised to embassy status that September, with William J. Porter as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the Democratic and Popular Republic of Algeria at the 1146th plenary meeting of its seventeenth session, five days after the flag came down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Relations were severed in June 1967 in the wake of that month’s Arab-Israeli war, an American interests section moved into the Swiss embassy, and the two embassies did not reopen until November 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence came at the end of a wave in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960, and this one has held a United Nations seat since October 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Algeria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Algeria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/algeria. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Democratic and Popular Republic of Algeria to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1754 (XVII), 8 October 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/204478. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Barbary Wars, 1801–1805 and 1815–1816,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/barbary-wars. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Tassili_n%27Ajjer-Tamghit_02.jpg/1920px-Tassili_n%27Ajjer-Tamghit_02.jpg","title":"Tassili n'Ajjer, Algeria","desc":"Tassili n'Ajjer.","credit":"IssamBarhoumi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tassili_n%27Ajjer-Tamghit_02.jpg","alt":"Weathered sandstone towers on a desert plateau."}},
{"id":"gw-518","num":518,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Ankara","answerText":"Ankara","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 April 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Notes exchanged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 February 1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO</span><span class=\"dt-v\">joined 1952</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turkey","dot":"Ankara"},"facts":[["Country","Turkey"],["Population","5.0 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Formerly","Angora"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","turkey"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ankara</b> was called Angora in English when the United States and the republic that had replaced the Ottoman Empire exchanged the notes there that restored relations between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For almost a century before that the American mission had sat at Constantinople, where David Porter presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires in September 1831 and where the legation was raised to embassy rank in 1906.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That mission ended when the Ottoman government severed relations in April 1917, 16 days after the United States declared war on Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The list of American chiefs of mission shows the gap exactly: Abram Elkus left in April 1917 and nobody succeeded him until Joseph Clark Grew presented credentials in October 1927, a decade later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Grew served into 1932, and every ambassador since has been accredited to the republic rather than to an empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The city mattered to Washington in a new way after the Second World War, when Britain announced that from 31 March 1947 it would stop paying for the Greek government’s war against the Greek communists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> President Truman went before a joint session of Congress that March to ask for aid to Greece and for aid to its neighbour as well, which had depended on the same British money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The principle he set out — that the United States would help any democratic nation threatened from outside or within — turned American policy away from staying out of distant quarrels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Greek civil war and the tensions beside it were among the reasons western Europe and the United States built the North Atlantic alliance, and in 1952 its members agreed to admit both countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city has been the seat of that member ever since, and the United Nations lists it as the capital under the modern spelling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Türkiye,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Turkey,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/turkey. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Chiefs of Mission for Turkey,” Department History, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/people/chiefsofmission/turkey. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Truman Doctrine, 1947,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/truman-doctrine. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nato. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Ankara_Ankara_Panorama_1-2_%2842487385781%29.jpg/1920px-Ankara_Ankara_Panorama_1-2_%2842487385781%29.jpg","title":"Ankara","desc":"Ankara, seen from a distance.","credit":"Murray Foubister, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ankara_Ankara_Panorama_1-2_(42487385781).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Ankara."}},
{"id":"gw-034","num":34,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Iraq","answerText":"Iraq","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 January 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mandate ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1932</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kuwait invaded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 August 1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iraq"},"facts":[["Capital","Baghdad"],["Population","47 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","435,052 km² (167,975 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Flag_of_Iraq.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iraq.svg)","alt":"The flag of Iraq: three horizontal bands of red, white and black, with green Arabic script across the centre of the white band."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","iraq"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"American commercial dealings with the Ottoman Empire, which included the ground that became <b>Iraq</b>, began in the late 1700s, and the first American consul for Baghdad was appointed in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Henry Haynes did the job from Constantinople at first, and presented his credentials to the Ottoman authorities in Baghdad only in January 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the empire dissolved after the First World War the United States supported British administration of the territory as a mandate, but insisted it be groomed for independence rather than kept as a colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mandate rested on the Treaty of Versailles, the settlement produced by a conference the defeated Central Powers were excluded from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recognition came when the American ambassador in London signed the Anglo-American-Iraqi Convention, whose preamble states that the United States recognises Iraq as an independent state; the mandate itself was terminated in 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The American legation at Baghdad was established in March 1931 and raised to an embassy at the end of 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1958 the country formed an Arab Union with the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan, which the United States recognised by an exchange of notes with the new state’s foreign ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thirty years later it emerged from the Iran-Iraq war with its state intact and some $37 billion owed to Gulf creditors, and Saddam Hussein asked Kuwait and the United Arab Emirates to cancel the debt on the argument that it had been payment for protecting the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kuwait refused, and Saddam revived a long-standing quarrel over the Warbah and Bubiyan islands, which gave secure access to Iraq’s ports on the waterway to the Persian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Security Council met on the day of the invasion that followed and demanded an immediate and unconditional withdrawal, and the country has held a United Nations seat since December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Iraq,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/iq.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iraq,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iraq. [Open access]","UN Security Council, resolution 660 (1990), adopted at the 2932nd meeting, 2 August 1990, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/94220. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Gulf War, 1991,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/gulf-war. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Ziggarat_of_Ur_001.jpg/1920px-Ziggarat_of_Ur_001.jpg","title":"Ziggurat of Ur, Iraq","desc":"Ziggurat of Ur.","credit":"Tla2006 at English Wikipedia, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziggarat_of_Ur_001.jpg","alt":"A view of Ziggurat of Ur."}},
{"id":"gw-035","num":35,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Argentina","answerText":"Argentina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 1816</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Falklands war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 1982</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Argentina"},"facts":[["Capital","Buenos Aires"],["Population","46 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","2,780,400 km² (1,073,500 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Flag_of_Argentina.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Argentina.svg)","alt":"The flag of Argentina: three horizontal bands of light blue, white and light blue, with a golden sun face in the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","argentina"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"Buenos Ayres, the government the United States recognised as the predecessor of <b>Argentina</b>, had declared its independence from Spain seven years earlier, and recognition took the form of appointing Caesar Rodney minister plenipotentiary to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rodney presented his credentials that December, and the country was then paralysed by the quarrel between Centralist and Federalist forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Federalist provinces ratified a constitution in 1854 establishing the Argentine Confederation, which the Centralists in Buenos Aires repudiated while declaring themselves independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American minister moved with the argument: he closed the legation in Buenos Aires about 1 May 1857 and took it to Paraná, returning it only in February 1862, after the Confederation had collapsed and the country had been reunified as the Argentine Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation at Buenos Aires was raised to embassy rank on 1 October 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A military coup in 1942 brought General Pedro Ramírez to power, and his insistence on neutrality in the Second World War led Washington to refuse the country’s requests for Lend-Lease aid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four decades later its forces landed on the Falkland Islands, which Spanish speakers call the Islas Malvinas, and overwhelmed the small British garrison within hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The claim went back to independence from Spain; Britain had established a presence on the islands from 1833 and developed them as a colony, and the question of their future sovereignty had been the subject of inconclusive talks since the 1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The junta formalised its control over the islands and over South Georgia and the South Sandwich Islands as well, expelled the British administration, and Margaret Thatcher ordered a naval task force to the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state has belonged to the United Nations since the organisation was founded and to the World Trade Organization since the day it opened, and covers the area the World Bank has measured at the same figure every year for two decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Argentina,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ar.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Argentina,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/argentina. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Crisis in the South Atlantic: The Reagan Administration and the Anglo-Argentine War of 1982,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1981-1988/south-atlantic. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Argentina and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/argentina_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Argentina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2002:2022. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Aerial_Foz_de_Igua%C3%A7u_26_Nov_2005.jpg","title":"Iguazu Falls, Argentina","desc":"Iguazu Falls.","credit":"Mariordo (Mario Roberto Durán Ortiz), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_Foz_de_Igua%C3%A7u_26_Nov_2005.jpg","alt":"A view of Iguazu Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-036","num":36,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Afghanistan","answerText":"Afghanistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 July 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Soviet invasion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 November 1946</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Afghanistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Kabul"],["Population","44 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Area","652,864 km² (252,072 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","afghanistan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"British authorities notified the American State Department in August 1919 that <b>Afghanistan</b> was officially free and independent in its affairs, and President Harding recognised it two years later by receiving an Afghan mission at the White House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed only in 1935, when William H. Hornibrook presented his credentials — he was accredited to Persia at the same time and lived at Tehran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A legation of its own opened at Kabul in June 1942 and was raised to embassy rank six years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country, together with Iceland and Sweden, at the 47th plenary meeting of its first session.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In February 1979 the American ambassador at Kabul, Adolph Dubs, was assassinated at his post, and every head of the mission after him was a chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the end of that year the Soviet Union sent thousands of troops in and took complete military and political control of the capital and much of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the only time the Soviet Union invaded a country outside the Eastern Bloc, and the historians who wrote that milestone add that ‘intervention’ describes it better than ‘invasion’, since Soviet domination had been growing since 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The embassy closed in January 1989 over doubts that the government left behind could protect diplomats once Soviet forces had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reopened as a liaison office in December 2001, seven weeks after a coalition campaign began against the Taliban and the sanctuary they had given Osama bin Laden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the World Trade Organization in July 2016, seven decades after taking the United Nations seat it has held since 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Afghanistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/af.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Afghanistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/afghanistan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Soviet Invasion of Afghanistan and the U.S. Response, 1978–1980,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1977-1980/soviet-invasion-afghanistan. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Afghanistan, Iceland and Sweden to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 34 (I), 9 November 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209856. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Afghanistan and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/afghanistan_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Afghanistan%27s_Grand_Canyon.jpg","title":"Band-e Amir National Park, Afghanistan","desc":"Band-e Amir National Park.","credit":"USAID, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afghanistan%27s_Grand_Canyon.jpg","alt":"A view of Band-e Amir National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-519","num":519,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Berlin","answerText":"Berlin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Airlift</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1948–49</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reunification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Germany","dot":"Berlin"},"facts":[["Country","Germany"],["Population","3.9 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Boroughs","12"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","germany"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Berlin</b> was where the United States signed a consular convention with the new German Empire in December 1871, and where its legation was raised to embassy rank in 1893.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the Second World War American, British and Soviet forces divided and occupied the country, and the city — itself split into occupation zones — lay far inside the Soviet-controlled east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States, the United Kingdom and France held its western portions while Soviet troops held the eastern sector, and the question of who would keep the western zones produced the first crisis of the Cold War in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began on 24 June 1948, when Soviet forces blockaded the rail, road and water access to the Allied-controlled areas, and the western powers answered by flying in food and fuel from airbases in the west of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviets lifted the land blockade on 12 May 1949, and the division of the city among the four victors was then frozen in place by the Cold War, despite the postwar agreements to unify the zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 10 November 1958 Khrushchev demanded that the three western powers pull their forces out of the western half within six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That ultimatum opened a three-year crisis over the city’s future, and the western half was by then too populous and too well established for another airlift to save it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thousands of Germans brought the wall down on 9 November 1989, two years after Ronald Reagan had stood beside it and called on Mikhail Gorbachev to tear it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The communist governments of eastern Europe collapsed one after another, and within a year the country was whole again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Of the people the city counted at the end of 2025, 976,419 were foreign nationals, and it is the seat of a state that has held its United Nations seat since the 1970s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Germany,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/de.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Germany,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/germany. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Airlift, 1948–1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Crisis, 1958–1961,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/berlin-crises. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Fall of Communism in Eastern Europe, 1989,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/fall-of-communism. [Open access]","Amt für Statistik Berlin-Brandenburg, “Einwohnerbestand Berlin – Grunddaten,” https://www.statistik-berlin-brandenburg.de/a-i-5-hj. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Berlin_Downtown_Skyline.jpg/1920px-Berlin_Downtown_Skyline.jpg","title":"Berlin","desc":"Berlin, seen from a distance.","credit":"Nicolas Völcker, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlin_Downtown_Skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Berlin."}},
{"id":"gw-037","num":37,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Canada","answerText":"Canada","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Balfour Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1927</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Canada"},"facts":[["Capital","Ottawa"],["Population","41 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern America"],["Area","9,984,670 km² (3,855,103 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Flag_of_Canada.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Canada.svg)","alt":"The flag of Canada: red bands at each end and a white square in the middle carrying a red maple leaf."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","north america","canada"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The Dominion of <b>Canada</b> was formed in 1867, but as an integral part of the British Empire its foreign relations stayed under the control of London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over the next six decades it gradually won greater control of its external affairs, spurred in part by the demands of managing its relationship with the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officials of the two governments dealt with each other increasingly through joint commissions and military cooperation, and the two even negotiated a bilateral fisheries treaty in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only in 1926 did the United Kingdom acknowledge that the dominion was fully autonomous in its foreign affairs, in the Balfour Declaration issued by that year’s Imperial Conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it as an independent state the following February, when Vincent Massey presented his credentials in Washington; an American legation opened in the capital that June and became an embassy in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Some of the boundary between the two states had been settled long before any of that: the Rush-Bagot pact of 1817 had the two sides eliminate their fleets from the Great Lakes apart from small patrol vessels, and the Webster-Ashburton treaty settled the disputed boundary with Maine in 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its size is worth stating carefully, because the two sources this deck usually leans on disagree about it and one of them is plainly wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s series reports more than 15 million square kilometres, half as much again as the country actually holds, so the area here is taken from the United Nations and the population from the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two disagree the other way about people: the United Nations counts about 40 million and the World Bank, following the national statistical service, more than 41.6 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has held a United Nations seat since November 1945 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Canada,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ca.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Canada,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/canada. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Canada and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/canada_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Canada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Rush-Bagot Pact, 1817 and Convention of 1818,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/rush-bagot. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Webster-Ashburton Treaty, 1842,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/webster-treaty. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Moraine_Lake_17092005.jpg/1920px-Moraine_Lake_17092005.jpg","title":"Moraine Lake, Canada","desc":"Moraine Lake.","credit":"Gorgo, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moraine_Lake_17092005.jpg","alt":"A view of Moraine Lake."}},
{"id":"gw-038","num":38,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Yemen","answerText":"Yemen","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Aden taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Yemen"},"facts":[["Capital","Sana'a"],["Population","42 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","527,968 km² (203,850 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Flag_of_Yemen.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Yemen.svg)","alt":"The flag of Yemen: three equal horizontal bands of red, white and black."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","yemen"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The ground that is now <b>Yemen</b> has been divided north from south since the 19th century: the north belonged to the Ottoman Empire, while the south fell under British influence from 1839, when Britain captured the port of Aden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the Ottoman collapse in 1918 the north became an independent republic while the south stayed British until 1967, when British authorities left in the wake of an intense campaign against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Kingdom of the Yemen in March 1946, in a letter from President Truman to the Imam Yahya, and the first American minister to it lived at Jidda and was accredited to Saudi Arabia as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country, together with Pakistan, at the 92nd plenary meeting of its second session.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American legation opened at Taiz only in 1959 and became an embassy in 1963, though from 1962 to 1967 nobody was accredited to the state at all and a chargé d’affaires ran the mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The embassy moved from Taiz to Sana’a in 1966, following the seat of government of the Yemen Arab Republic, and the republic severed relations the following June in the wake of that month’s Arab-Israeli war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the south, the United States recognised the People’s Republic of Southern Yemen on 7 December 1967 and opened an embassy at Aden the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy lasted less than two years: the southern state severed relations in October 1969, and it changed its own name to the People’s Democratic Republic of Yemen the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two states were formally unified as the Republic of Yemen in 1990, and the Security Council was still legislating about the country a quarter of a century later, adopting resolution 2216 at its 7426th meeting in April 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The united republic joined the World Trade Organization in June 2014, 67 years after the seat it has held at the United Nations since 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Yemen,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ye.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Yemen,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/yemen. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Yemen and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/yemen_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Yemen and Pakistan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 108 (II), 30 September 1947, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209881. [Open access]","UN Security Council, resolution 2216 (2015), adopted at the 7426th meeting, 14 April 2015, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/791250. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%B4%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%85_%D8%AD%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%85%D9%88%D8%AA.jpg/1920px-%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%B4%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%85_%D8%AD%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%85%D9%88%D8%AA.jpg","title":"Shibam, Yemen","desc":"Shibam.","credit":"Bkar6190, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%B4%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%85_%D8%AD%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%85%D9%88%D8%AA.jpg","alt":"A view of Shibam."}},
{"id":"gw-039","num":39,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Morocco","answerText":"Morocco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 June 1786</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1912–56</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Morocco"},"facts":[["Capital","Rabat"],["Population","38 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Area","446,550 km² (172,414 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Flag_of_Morocco.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Morocco.svg)","alt":"The flag of Morocco: a red field with a green five-pointed star outlined in the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","morocco"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Morocco</b> was an independent kingdom when the Barbary states around it owed at least nominal allegiance to the Ottoman sultan, and it recognised the United States by signing a treaty of peace and friendship at Marrakech in June 1786.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first American consul, James Simpson, arrived at Tangier in December 1797 and served for more than twenty years, until his death in 1820.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sultan gave the United States a building for the consulate’s use the year after that, which was the first property abroad the American government owned and is now an institute for Moroccan studies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Consulates followed at Essaouira, Larache, Tétouan, Casablanca, Rabat, Safi and El Jadida, most of them opened in the second half of the 19th century and most closed again before 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Permanent diplomatic relations began only in March 1905, when the consulate at Tangier was raised to a legation under Samuel Gummere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country became a French protectorate in 1912, with parts of it under Spanish control, and the United States did not acknowledge either protectorate until Secretary of State Robert Lansing wrote to the French ambassador in October 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> France recognised Moroccan independence in a declaration of 2 March 1956, and the United States congratulated the kingdom five days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consulate at Rabat became an embassy that June, and Tangier — an international city that was still technically Moroccan — was fully integrated into the country in October of the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the kingdom at the 574th plenary meeting of its eleventh session, the same sitting at which it admitted Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in 1987 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened, having held its United Nations seat since 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Morocco,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ma.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Morocco,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/morocco. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Morocco and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/morocco_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Morocco to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1111 (XI), 12 November 1956, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207993. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Barbary Wars, 1801–1805 and 1815–1816,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/barbary-wars. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Ksar_A%C3%AFt_Benhaddou%2C_Marocco_%28%D8%A3%D9%8A%D8%AA_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%AD%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%8C_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%BA%D8%B1%D8%A8%2C_%E2%B4%B0%E2%B5%A2%E2%B5%9C_%E2%B5%83%E2%B4%B0%E2%B4%B7%E2%B4%B7%E2%B5%93%29.jpg/1920px-Ksar_A%C3%AFt_Benhaddou%2C_Marocco_%28%D8%A3%D9%8A%D8%AA_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%AD%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%8C_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%BA%D8%B1%D8%A8%2C_%E2%B4%B0%E2%B5%A2%E2%B5%9C_%E2%B5%83%E2%B4%B0%E2%B4%B7%E2%B4%B7%E2%B5%93%29.jpg","title":"Aït Benhaddou, Morocco","desc":"Aït Benhaddou.","credit":"Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ksar_A%C3%AFt_Benhaddou,_Marocco_(%D8%A3%D9%8A%D8%AA_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%AD%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%8C_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%BA%D8%B1%D8%A8,_%E2%B4%B0%E2%B5%A2%E2%B5%9C_%E2%B5%83%E2%B4%B0%E2%B4%B7%E2%B4%B7%E2%B5%93).jpg","alt":"A view of Aït Benhaddou."}},
{"id":"gw-520","num":520,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Bangkok","answerText":"Bangkok","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratifications</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 April 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 October 1882</span><span class=\"dt-k\">SEATO seat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1954</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Thailand","dot":"Bangkok"},"facts":[["Country","Thailand"],["Population","10.4 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Seat of","SEATO"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","thailand"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Bangkok</b> is where the United States and Siam exchanged ratifications of a treaty of amity and commerce in April 1836, three years after signing it — the first treaty the Americans made with any Asian country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had been negotiated by Edmund Roberts, a special agent sent to the Far East under instructions to make treaties with Cochinchina, Siam and the powers of Arabia on the Red Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consular presence followed in 1859, when John Hassett Chandler became acting vice consul in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American legation in the country opened in October 1882, when John A. Halderman presented his credentials as minister resident and consul general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was raised to an embassy only after the Second World War, Edwin F. Stanton becoming the first American ambassador in April 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The city became the headquarters of the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization, formed in September 1954 by the United States, France, Britain, New Zealand, Australia, the Philippines, Pakistan and the kingdom itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The organisation kept no military forces of its own but hosted joint exercises for its members every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As the threat it was built against shifted from invasion to internal subversion, it worked instead at strengthening the economies and living standards of the southeast Asian states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It sponsored meetings and exhibitions on cultural, religious and historical subjects, and its non-Asian members paid for fellowships for southeast Asian scholars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state whose capital this is entered the United Nations under the name Siam in the organisation’s first full year, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in November 1982, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Thailand,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/th.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Thailand,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/thailand. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO), 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/seato. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Thailand and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/thailand_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Siam to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 101 (I), 15 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209878. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Bangkok_skyline_view_from_Mahanakhon_Tower.jpg/1920px-Bangkok_skyline_view_from_Mahanakhon_Tower.jpg","title":"Bangkok","desc":"Bangkok, seen from a distance.","credit":"Bigcitydata, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangkok_skyline_view_from_Mahanakhon_Tower.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Bangkok."}},
{"id":"gw-040","num":40,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","answer":"Angola","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Portuguese landfall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1482</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 May 1993</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Angola</b> was reached by Portuguese ships in 1482 at a point in what is now its north, where they met the Kingdom of the Congo holding land from modern Gabon south to the Kwanza River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Portugal took the coastal strip piece by piece across the 16th century through a mixture of treaty and war, and its interest there turned quickly to the trade in enslaved people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many scholars hold that by the 19th century this coast was the largest single source of enslaved people shipped to the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Administrative control of the interior was secured only at the beginning of the 20th century, four hundred years after the first landings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As decolonisation moved through the rest of Africa after the Second World War, Portugal went on treating its African possessions as overseas provinces, and three separate independence movements grew up against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A military coup in Portugal in 1974 installed a government willing to let go, and the Alvor Accords promised power to a coalition of all three movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The coalition broke apart into fighting, and the Eastern Bloc-backed Popular Movement for the Liberation of Angola took the capital as the Portuguese abandoned it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States acknowledged the former colony's independence but withheld recognition from the government that movement declared, citing the Cuban combat troops that remained and their part in conflicts elsewhere in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country all the same, at the end of 1976 and under the name the People's Republic of Angola, and it has since grown to more than five times the population it had at independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American recognition came in May 1993, after multiparty elections and a peace agreement, with an embassy following under Edmund De Jarnette in July 1994; the country had entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade a few weeks earlier and joined the World Trade Organization in 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Angola","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Angola"},"facts":[["Capital","Luanda"],["Population","39 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Area","1,246,700 km² (481,350 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","angola"],"sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Angola,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ao.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Angola,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/angola. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Angola and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/angola_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Angola,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AGO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=1975:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the People's Republic of Angola to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 31/44, 1 December 1976, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/199650. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Flag_of_Angola.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Angola.svg)","alt":"The flag of Angola: red over black, with a yellow machete, cogwheel and star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Kalandula_waterfalls_of_the_Lucala-River_in_Malange%2C_Angola.JPG/1920px-Kalandula_waterfalls_of_the_Lucala-River_in_Malange%2C_Angola.JPG","title":"Kalandula Falls, Angola","desc":"Kalandula Falls.","credit":"Paulo César Santos, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalandula_waterfalls_of_the_Lucala-River_in_Malange,_Angola.JPG","alt":"A view of Kalandula Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-041","num":41,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ukraine","answerText":"Ukraine","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declared 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ukraine"},"facts":[["Capital","Kyiv"],["Population","39 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","603,550 km² (233,030 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","ukraine"],"abstract":"<b>Ukraine</b> sat at the United Nations from the day the organisation was founded, four and a half decades before it was an independent state, because the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic held a seat of its own beside the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> United Nations papers from the 1980s list the two delegations separately, one after the other on the same communications: a note handed to the president of a conference in Paris in 1981 carries both names in a single row of signatories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In every other respect it was a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and the seat was a diplomatic arrangement rather than a foreign policy of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A few days after the failed hard-line coup against Mikhail Gorbachev in August 1991, it declared independence from the Soviet Union, as Belarus did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Bush administration, watching the union come apart, put the prevention of nuclear catastrophe and a stable transition to new political orders ahead of any quick recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Recognition came on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced it in an address to the nation on the dissolution of the Soviet Union, and diplomatic relations began the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy opened in Kyiv on 23 January 1992, with Jon Gundersen as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the World Trade Organization in May 2008, sixteen years after that embassy opened and rather later than most of its western neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 2 March 2022 an emergency special session of the General Assembly adopted a resolution entitled ‘Aggression against Ukraine’, demanding that Russian forces withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The invasion has cost it a large share of its people: the World Bank's series records 44.3 million in 2021, 41.0 million in 2022 and 37.7 million in 2023, against 45.8 million in 2015, so roughly one recorded person in seven has gone in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ukraine,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ua.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ukraine,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ukraine. [Open access]","UN Conference on the Least Developed Countries, “Credentials of Representatives to the Conference: Communication Dated 9 September 1981 Addressed to the President of the Conference,” A/CONF.104/18, Paris, 10 September 1981, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/25148. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Aggression against Ukraine,” resolution ES-11/1 of 2 March 2022, A/RES/ES-11/1, https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/n22/293/36/pdf/n2229336.pdf. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Ukraine and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/ukraine_e.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Collapse of the Soviet Union, 1989–1991,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/collapse-soviet-union. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Ukraine,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/UKR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Flag_of_Ukraine.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ukraine.svg)","alt":"The flag of Ukraine: two equal horizontal bands, blue above yellow."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/80-391-0151_Kyiv_St.Sophia%27s_Cathedral_RB_18_2_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-80-391-0151_Kyiv_St.Sophia%27s_Cathedral_RB_18_2_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Saint Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv, Ukraine","desc":"Saint Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv.","credit":"Rbrechko, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:80-391-0151_Kyiv_St.Sophia%27s_Cathedral_RB_18_2_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A view of Saint Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv."}},
{"id":"gw-042","num":42,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Poland","answerText":"Poland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Partitions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1772, 1793, 1795</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic proclaimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 January 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Poland"},"facts":[["Capital","Warsaw"],["Population","37 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","312,696 km² (120,733 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","poland"],"abstract":"<b>Poland</b> at the height of its power was a commonwealth that included Lithuania, Belarus and much of Ukraine, whose nobility elected the king and in which a single dissenting vote — the liberum veto — stopped any legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That arrangement invited foreign intervention and civil war, and left the country open to stronger neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Austria, Russia and Prussia took a first partition of it in 1772; the reforms behind the constitution of 1791 brought a second in 1793, and the suppression of a revolt a third in 1795.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It then vanished from the map of Europe until 1918, and the Grand Duchy of Warsaw that Napoleon made out of Prussian Poland in 1807 did not outlive his defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A republic was proclaimed on 3 November 1918, Józef Piłsudski became head of state eleven days later, and the pianist Ignacy Jan Paderewski formed a cabinet as prime minister that January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The thirteenth of Woodrow Wilson's Fourteen Points had called for an independent Poland with access to the sea, and the United States recognised the provisional government on 22 January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Versailles fixed the western border and cut a corridor to the Baltic along the Vistula, leaving Danzig, which had a German majority, a free city supervised by the League of Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The League proposed an eastern ethnic frontier known as the Curzon Line, but the country went to war with the Soviet Union in 1920 over its boundaries of 1772, and the Treaty of Riga left the border well east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Germany invaded on 1 September 1939 the American ambassador followed the government to France and then to London, and an embassy reopened in Warsaw only in July 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has held a United Nations seat since 1945, entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in 1967, the World Trade Organization in 1995 and the European Union in 2004, and its recorded population has fallen from 38.0 million in 2015 to 36.4 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Poland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pl.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Poland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/poland. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Poland and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/poland_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Poland – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/poland_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Poland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/POL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Flag_of_Poland.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Poland.svg)","alt":"The flag of Poland: two equal horizontal bands, white above red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Wawel_%284%29.jpg/1920px-Wawel_%284%29.jpg","title":"Wawel Castle, Poland","desc":"Wawel Castle.","credit":"Monika Towiańska, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wawel_(4).jpg","alt":"A view of Wawel Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-521","num":521,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"London","answerText":"London","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1 June 1785</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised to embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 June 1893</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First UN Assembly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1946</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United Kingdom","dot":"London"},"facts":[["Country","United Kingdom"],["Population","9.2 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Host to","the first UN Assembly"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","united kingdom"],"abstract":"<b>London</b> is where the United States first kept a mission in Europe: diplomatic relations and the American Legation were established on 1 June 1785, when John Adams presented his credentials as minister plenipotentiary to George III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Adams found the reception so cool that he closed the legation in 1788, and the post then stood vacant for four years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A separate American consulate opened in the city in 1790 and stayed open, through everything that followed, until 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations broke when the United States declared war on 18 June 1812, though the chargé d'affaires Jonathan Russell did not close the legation until the end of July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Quincy Adams, son of the first minister, presented his own credentials in the city on 8 August 1815, and the mission has run without a break since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It became an embassy on 22 June 1893, when Thomas Bayard presented his credentials to the Court of St James.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1930 the city hosted the third of five conferences held to limit the fleets of the largest naval powers — a series that began at Washington in 1921 and closed with a second London meeting in 1935 — at which Britain, the United States, Japan, France and Italy tried to revise and extend the Five Power Treaty of 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1940 it held the exiled governments of Poland, Belgium, Czechoslovakia, Greece, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Norway and Yugoslavia, and one American ambassador was accredited to all of them at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first session of the United Nations General Assembly opened here on 10 January 1946 and adopted its first resolution, establishing a commission on the problems raised by the discovery of atomic energy, at a plenary meeting a fortnight later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That session resumed at Flushing Meadow in New York the same year; the country whose capital this is has held a United Nations seat since 1945 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gb.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: United Kingdom,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/united-kingdom. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The London Naval Conference, 1930,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/london-naval-conf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Poland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/poland. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Establishment of a Commission to Deal with the Problems Raised by the Discovery of Atomic Energy,” resolution 1 (I), 24 January 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209570. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The United Kingdom and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/united_kingdom_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/The_Thames_and_London_Skyline_from_Waterloo_Bridge_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4537341.jpg/1920px-The_Thames_and_London_Skyline_from_Waterloo_Bridge_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4537341.jpg","title":"London","desc":"London, seen from a distance.","credit":"Anthony Parkes, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Thames_and_London_Skyline_from_Waterloo_Bridge_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4537341.jpg","alt":"A wide view of London."}},
{"id":"gw-043","num":43,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Uzbekistan","answerText":"Uzbekistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uzbekistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Tashkent"],["Population","37 million (2025)"],["Region","Central Asia"],["Area","448,969 km² (173,348 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","uzbekistan"],"abstract":"<b>Uzbekistan</b> was a constituent republic of the Soviet Union until that state came apart, and it has been governed from its own capital only since the last days of 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its independence was declared in the weeks after the failed hard-line coup against Mikhail Gorbachev in August 1991, as the union's other republics went the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation on the dissolution of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 19 February 1992 and an American embassy opened in the capital a month after that, with Michael Mozur as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the republic without a vote at its 82nd meeting on 2 March 1992, one of a run of admissions that year as the union's successor states took seats of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The country inherited from Soviet planning the diversion of the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya for cotton, and with it the drying of the sea those rivers fed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The states of the basin set up an International Fund for Saving the Aral Sea, and the General Assembly has passed resolutions on cooperation with that fund, the most recent adopted without a vote in March 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by about a fifth in a decade, from 30.7 million in 2015 to 37.1 million, which is quick growth for a country with so little water to spare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its government joined the United States as an ally in the war on terror, though the State Department’s own account of the relationship describes that alignment as brief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under Central Asia, and it took its seat there a little over two months after the union it had belonged to ceased to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Uzbekistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/uz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uzbekistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uzbekistan. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Uzbekistan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/226, 2 March 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/147690. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Cooperation between the United Nations and the International Fund for Saving the Aral Sea,” resolution 75/266, 3 March 2021, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3904094. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Uzbekistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/UZB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Collapse of the Soviet Union, 1989–1991,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/collapse-soviet-union. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Flag_of_Uzbekistan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uzbekistan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Uzbekistan: blue, white and green bands separated by thin red fimbriations, with a white crescent and twelve stars in the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg","title":"Registan, Uzbekistan","desc":"Registan.","credit":"Registan_-_Gusjer.jpg : Gustavo Jeronimo from Aranjuez, Spain derivative work: MrPanyGoff, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg","alt":"A view of Registan."}},
{"id":"gw-044","num":44,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Malaysia","answerText":"Malaysia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Penang, 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1963</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malaysia"},"facts":[["Capital","Kuala Lumpur"],["Population","36 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","330,621 km² (127,653 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","malaysia"],"abstract":"<b>Malaysia</b> did not exist under that name until 1963, and American dealings with the territory that became it go back to the 19th century, when United States merchants traded at several of its ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That commerce stayed small, and it was only in the 20th century, and mostly after independence, that the relationship grew into anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American consul was appointed to Penang in 1918, a further post opened at Kuala Lumpur in 1948, and a third at Kuching on Borneo in 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Federation of Malaya, until then part of the British Empire, became independent on 31 August 1957, and the United States recognised it the same day in a letter from Secretary of State John Foster Dulles to Prime Minister Tunku Abdul Rahman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on that date too, the consulate general at Kuala Lumpur being raised to an embassy with its consul general, Thomas K. Wright, left in charge as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In 1963 the federation joined with Singapore, Sarawak and Borneo to make the Federation of Malaysia, and American ambassadors were accredited to the new state from then on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 24 October 1957, under eight weeks after independence, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it as the Federation of Malaya at its 678th plenary meeting on 17 September 1957, within a fortnight of independence and six years before the name it bears now existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government is split between two cities, the United Nations recording one as the capital and the other as the administrative capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 31.2 million in 2015 to 36.0 million, a rise of about a seventh in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Malaysia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/my.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malaysia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malaysia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Malaysia and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/malaysia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Malaysia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MYS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Federation of Malaya to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1134 (XII), 17 September 1957, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207454. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Flag_of_Malaysia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malaysia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Malaysia: fourteen red and white stripes with a blue canton bearing a yellow crescent and fourteen-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Petronas_Tower_spire.JPG/1920px-Petronas_Tower_spire.JPG","title":"Petronas Towers, Malaysia","desc":"Petronas Towers.","credit":"Descon7, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petronas_Tower_spire.JPG","alt":"A view of Petronas Towers."}},
{"id":"gw-045","num":45,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saudi Arabia","answerText":"Saudi Arabia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1932</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 December 2005</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Saudi Arabia"},"facts":[["Capital","Riyadh"],["Population","35 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","about 2,150,000 km² (830,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","saudi arabia"],"abstract":"<b>Saudi Arabia</b> was recognised by the United States under an earlier name: on 1 May 1931 the American minister at London told the Hejazi minister there that Washington recognised the Kingdom of Hejaz and Nejd and its Dependencies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A royal decree of 18 September 1932 changed the state's name to the one it carries now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A provisional agreement on diplomatic and consular representation followed in 1933, but a permanent mission took another seven years to arrange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Full relations began on 4 February 1940, when Bert Fish presented his credentials as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary; he was accredited to Egypt as well and lived at Cairo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation opened at Jidda on 1 May 1942 under James S. Moose Jr. as chargé d'affaires, and a consulate general opened at Dhahran two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legation was raised to an embassy on 18 March 1949, when J. Rives Childs presented his credentials as ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy stayed on the Red Sea coast for another 35 years, moving inland only in September 1984, when the liaison office in the capital was raised to embassy rank and the Jidda mission became a consulate general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During the war of 1973 the Arab members of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries embargoed oil exports to the United States, and the State Department's own account says the strain on relations with the kingdom showed how hard it was to reconcile support for Israel with close ties to the Arab oil monarchies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The kingdom was among the states that founded the United Nations in 1945, and it entered the World Trade Organization only 60 years after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its two main international counts disagree about both of its headline figures: the United Nations gives 34.6 million people and 2,206,714 km², the World Bank 37.0 million and 2,149,690 km², so Folio's own figures sit inside a real spread rather than on an agreed number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saudi Arabia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sa.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saudi Arabia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saudi-arabia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Saudi Arabia and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/saudi_arabia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Saudi Arabia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SAU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Oil Embargo, 1973–1974,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/oil-embargo. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Saudi Arabia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SAU/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/Flag_of_Saudi_Arabia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saudi_Arabia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Saudi Arabia: a green field bearing the shahada in white Thuluth script above a white sword."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Mada%27in_Saleh_2017.jpg/1920px-Mada%27in_Saleh_2017.jpg","title":"Hegra (Mada'in Salih), Saudi Arabia","desc":"Hegra (Mada'in Salih).","credit":"Ahmad AlHasanat, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mada%27in_Saleh_2017.jpg","alt":"A view of Hegra (Mada'in Salih)."}},
{"id":"gw-522","num":522,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the official capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dodoma","answerText":"Dodoma","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Union formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1964</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tanzania","dot":"Dodoma"},"facts":[["Country","Tanzania"],["Population","262,000 (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Status","the official capital"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","tanzania"],"abstract":"<b>Dodoma</b> is the official capital of a country whose government has never entirely moved there, and the two international bodies that keep country profiles record the split in different ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat lists the capital as two cities at once, one marked acting and this one marked official; the United Nations Statistics Division simply gives this one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits inland on the central plateau, several hundred kilometres from the Indian Ocean port where the state's foreign missions and much of its business remain, which is what the word acting in the Commonwealth's entry is doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the United Nations count it holds about a quarter of a million people, which makes it one of the smallest capitals of any country of comparable size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The designation has not pulled the embassies after it: the United States established diplomatic relations at the coastal city in December 1961, by raising its consulate general there to embassy rank, and has kept its mission on the coast ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The country it serves was Tanganyika when it became independent from British administration as a United Nations trust territory, and President John F. Kennedy sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Julius Nyerere on 9 December 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted Tanganyika at its 1078th plenary meeting five days after that message, so the state took its United Nations seat within a week of ceasing to be a trust territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mainland state joined with the newly independent Zanzibar in 1964 to make the united republic that exists now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 9 December 1961, the day of independence itself, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in the same year, the Secretariat's own entry dating the accession to 1961 and attaching the reason, independence from Britain, to the date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Republic of Tanzania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tz.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “United Republic of Tanzania,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/united-republic-tanzania. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tanzania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tanzania. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Tanganyika to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1667 (XVI), 14 December 1961, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205649. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tanzania and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tanzania_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Central_Dodoma.JPG/1920px-Central_Dodoma.JPG","title":"Dodoma","desc":"Dodoma, seen from a distance.","credit":"Victor Mikheev, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Dodoma.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Dodoma."}},
{"id":"gw-751","num":751,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the acting capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dar es Salaam","answerText":"Dar es Salaam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy bombed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1998</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council response</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 August 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tanzania","dot":"Dar es Salaam"},"facts":[["Country","Tanzania"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Status","the acting capital"],["US embassy","since 1961"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","tanzania"],"abstract":"<b>Dar es Salaam</b> is the seat from which its country is actually governed and where its foreign missions sit, although another city inland holds the official designation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat records the arrangement plainly, listing the capital as two cities, this one marked acting and the other marked official.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate general stood here before independence, and on 9 December 1961 it was raised to embassy rank, with William R. Duggan left in charge as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That was the same day President John F. Kennedy sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Julius Nyerere recognising Tanganyika, until then under British administration as a United Nations trust territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The embassy has stayed in the city through the union with Zanzibar in 1964 and through the designation of the inland capital, so the working seat and the official one have been apart for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 7 August 1998 that embassy and the American embassy at Nairobi were bombed within minutes of each other, in the attacks that put both cities into the Security Council's record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Security Council met six days later and adopted a resolution strongly condemning the attacks in Kenya and Tanzania, calling on all states to help bring those responsible to justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The State Department's own account of the relationship says that relations between the two countries, cordial since independence, improved further after the bombings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on the day of its independence and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened in 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country's people are counted at just over 70 million, a figure that has risen by more than a third in a decade, and the United Nations files it under Eastern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Republic of Tanzania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tz.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “United Republic of Tanzania,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/united-republic-tanzania. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tanzania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tanzania. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1189 (1998),” adopted at its 3915th meeting, 13 August 1998, S/RES/1189(1998), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/258404. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tanzania and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tanzania_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Dar_Es_Salaam_Skyline_%2834011488084%29.jpg","title":"Dar es Salaam","desc":"Dar es Salaam, seen from a distance.","credit":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dar_Es_Salaam_Skyline_(34011488084).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Dar es Salaam."}},
{"id":"gw-046","num":46,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mozambique","answerText":"Mozambique","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 June 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 November 1975</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mozambique"},"facts":[["Capital","Maputo"],["Population","36 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","799,380 km² (308,642 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","mozambique"],"abstract":"<b>Mozambique</b> was under Portuguese sovereignty until 1975, and everything in its diplomatic record happens within a few months of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the People's Republic of Mozambique on 25 June 1975, in a letter from President Gerald Ford to President Samora Moisés Machel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it at the 2351st plenary meeting of its thirtieth session, on 16 September, under that same name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established a week later, when Secretary of State Henry Kissinger and Foreign Minister Joaquim Chissano initialled a joint communiqué.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy opened on 8 November at Lourenço Marques, the city that has since been renamed Maputo, with Johnnie Carson as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Within four months of taking its seat the new state closed its border with Southern Rhodesia and imposed sanctions on it, at real cost to its own economy, since the landlocked territory next door depended on Mozambican railways and ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Security Council took that decision up at its 1892nd meeting on 17 March 1976 and adopted a resolution on it unanimously, one of whose subjects is economic assistance to the country that had acted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in July 1992, seventeen years after independence and the year a long civil war ended, and joined the World Trade Organization in August 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown faster than almost any country in this collection, from 26.5 million in 2015 to 35.6 million, a third more in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations and the World Bank give the same figure for that count and the same figure for the country’s area, which is not true of every country here, and both file it under Eastern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mozambique,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mozambique,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mozambique. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Mozambique and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/mozambique_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Mozambique,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MOZ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the People's Republic of Mozambique to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 3365 (XXX), 16 September 1975, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/189347. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 386 (1976),” adopted at its 1892nd meeting, 17 March 1976, S/RES/386(1976), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/93715. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Flag_of_Mozambique.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mozambique.svg)","alt":"The flag of Mozambique: green, black and yellow bands with a red hoist triangle bearing a star, a book, a hoe and a rifle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/ISS059-E-92404_-_View_of_Mozambique.jpg/1920px-ISS059-E-92404_-_View_of_Mozambique.jpg","title":"Bazaruto Archipelago, Mozambique","desc":"Bazaruto Archipelago.","credit":"Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, NASA Johnson Space Center, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS059-E-92404_-_View_of_Mozambique.jpg","alt":"A view of Bazaruto Archipelago."}},
{"id":"gw-047","num":47,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ghana","answerText":"Ghana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 March 1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 March 1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1957</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ghana"},"facts":[["Capital","Accra"],["Population","35 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","238,537 km² (92,100 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","ghana"],"abstract":"<b>Ghana</b> was proclaimed independent of the British Empire on 6 March 1957, under a name it had not carried as a colony: until that day it was the Gold Coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it the same day, in a press statement issued by the Department of State rather than a letter from a president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on that date as well, the American consulate general at the capital being raised to embassy status with its consul general, Donald W. Lamm, left in charge as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The whole sequence of proclamation, recognition and embassy therefore happened inside 24 hours, which is quick even by the standards of a decade in which a great many colonies became states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country two days later, at the 668th plenary meeting of its eleventh session, the last sitting of a session that had opened the previous November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 17 October 1957, seven months after independence and before most of the continent had any independence to trade on, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened at the start of 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 28.7 million in 2015 to 35.1 million, about a fifth more in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two international counts agree on that figure to the thousand, and their measurements of the country's area differ by six square kilometres, which is as close as this collection's sources ever come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under Western Africa, the grouping that runs along the Gulf of Guinea from Senegal round to Nigeria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital holds about 2.5 million people by that same count, roughly one citizen in fourteen, which is a smaller share than most West African capitals take of their own countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ghana,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gh.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ghana,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ghana. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Ghana and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/ghana_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Ghana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GHA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Ghana to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1118 (XI), 8 March 1957, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/208000. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Flag_of_Ghana.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ghana.svg)","alt":"The flag of Ghana: red, gold and green horizontal bands with a black five-pointed star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Cape_Coast_Castle%2C_Cape_Coast%2C_Ghana.JPG","title":"Cape Coast Castle, Ghana","desc":"Cape Coast Castle.","credit":"Rjruiziii, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Coast_Castle,_Cape_Coast,_Ghana.JPG","alt":"A view of Cape Coast Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-048","num":48,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Peru","answerText":"Peru","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 May 1826</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 May 1827</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Peru"},"facts":[["Capital","Lima"],["Population","35 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","1,285,216 km² (496,225 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","peru"],"abstract":"<b>Peru</b> proclaimed its independence from Spain in July 1821 under General José de San Martín, and the United States recognised it five years later by appointing James Cooley chargé d'affaires on 2 May 1826.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cooley presented his credentials at the capital on 21 May the following year, opening the American legation there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1836 the republic joined the Peru-Bolivian Confederation, and the United States recognised that state too, appointing James B. Thornton chargé d'affaires in March 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thornton died in the country in January 1838; his successor J. C. Pickett was appointed that June but did not present his credentials until January 1840, a gap the State Department's own account attributes to the confederation dissolving in 1839.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Separate relations resumed after that, and the legation at the capital was raised to an embassy on 24 April 1920, when Ambassador William E. Gonzales presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations have been interrupted by territorial disputes with neighbours, by periods of military rule and by coups against constitutional government, but the two countries have never severed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has held a United Nations seat since 31 October 1945, which makes it an original member; the recognition guide's own summary says 1949 and is simply wrong about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in October 1951 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 30.5 million in 2015 to 34.6 million, and the two international counts agree on that figure exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They agree on its area to within four hundredths of a square kilometre, which no other country in this collection can claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Peru,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pe.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Peru,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/peru. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Peru and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/peru_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Peru,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PER/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Peru,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PER/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Flag_of_Peru.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Peru.svg)","alt":"The flag of Peru: three vertical bands, red, white and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Machu_Picchu%2C_2023_%28012%29.jpg/1920px-Machu_Picchu%2C_2023_%28012%29.jpg","title":"Machu Picchu, Peru","desc":"Machu Picchu.","credit":"Draceane, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Machu_Picchu,_2023_(012).jpg","alt":"A view of Machu Picchu."}},
{"id":"gw-523","num":523,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Paris","answerText":"Paris","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pact signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 August 1928</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Government left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Summit collapsed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"France","dot":"Paris"},"facts":[["Country","France"],["Population","11.0 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Also known for","the Pact of Paris"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","france"],"abstract":"<b>Paris</b> has given its name to more than one agreement, and the plainest case is the Kellogg-Briand Pact, an attempt to outlaw war altogether that fifteen nations signed there on 27 August 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The State Department's own account calls it the Pact of Paris for the city in which it was signed, and says it did little to stop the militarism of the 1930s or to prevent the war that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its origin was a proposal by the French foreign minister Aristide Briand for a bilateral agreement with the United States, published as an open letter in April 1927.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decade earlier the city had hosted the conference that produced the Treaty of Versailles, at which four powers dominated the settlement of the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both episodes made the city a byword for a certain kind of diplomacy, conducted among great powers and named after the place rather than the parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It has also been abandoned: Germany invaded on 15 May 1940 and the French government withdrew from the city to Bordeaux.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Parliament met at Vichy that July and granted Marshal Pétain full and extraordinary powers, and the American embassy moved to be near the new regime rather than stay in the occupied capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A summit was to open here in May 1960 to discuss divided Germany, arms control and a general relaxation of tension between the Soviet Union and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American U-2 was shot down over Soviet territory on 1 May, and when President Eisenhower refused to promise an end to the overflights the Soviet delegation walked out just as the meeting was about to begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 11 million people in the city and its surroundings, a sixth of the country's whole population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “France,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: France,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/france. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Kellogg-Briand Pact, 1928,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/kellogg. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “U-2 Overflights and the Capture of Francis Gary Powers, 1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/u2-incident. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Paris_skyline_from_the_observation_deck_of_the_Montparnasse_tower%2C_July_2015.jpg/1920px-Paris_skyline_from_the_observation_deck_of_the_Montparnasse_tower%2C_July_2015.jpg","title":"Paris","desc":"Paris, seen from a distance.","credit":"Joe deSousa, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris_skyline_from_the_observation_deck_of_the_Montparnasse_tower,_July_2015.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Paris."}},
{"id":"gw-049","num":49,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Madagascar","answerText":"Madagascar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First agent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 February 1866</span><span class=\"dt-k\">French rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 June 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Madagascar"},"facts":[["Capital","Antananarivo"],["Population","33 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","587,041 km² (226,658 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","madagascar"],"abstract":"<b>Madagascar</b> dealt with the United States as an independent kingdom long before it was a colony: John P. Finkelmeier was appointed commercial agent at Tamatave in February 1866, and the two countries concluded a commercial convention the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A treaty of peace, friendship and commerce followed in 1881, and Finkelmeier had by then been made consul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> France assumed sovereignty over the island as a colony in 1890, which ended that separate relationship without closing the American post on the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American consulate moved inland from Tamatave to the highland capital, then spelled Tananarive, on or about 8 July 1916, and the mission has stayed in that city ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence came on 25 June 1960 under the name the Malagasy Republic, and President Dwight Eisenhower wrote to President Philibert Tsiranana the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The consulate was raised to embassy rank on that date, with the former consul J. Roland Jacobs as chargé d'affaires, and Frederic P. Bartlett was appointed the first ambassador that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it as the Malagasy Republic at the 864th plenary meeting of its fifteenth session, on 20 September 1960, at the same sitting that admitted Côte d’Ivoire and several other newly independent African states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 30 September 1963, three years after independence, and joined the World Trade Organization in November 1995, some months after that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 25.4 million in 2015 to 32.7 million, nearly a third more in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two international counts agree on that figure and put the island's area within 254 km² of one another, a difference of four hundredths of one per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Madagascar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Madagascar,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/madagascar. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Madagascar and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/madagascar_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Madagascar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Madagascar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Malagasy Republic to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1478 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205972. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Flag_of_Madagascar.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Madagascar.svg)","alt":"The flag of Madagascar: a white vertical band at the hoist beside horizontal red and green bands."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Adansonia_grandidieri_Pat_Hooper.jpg","title":"Avenue of the Baobabs, Madagascar","desc":"Avenue of the Baobabs.","credit":"Pat Hooper from Chicago, IL, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adansonia_grandidieri_Pat_Hooper.jpg","alt":"A view of Avenue of the Baobabs."}},
{"id":"gw-050","num":50,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Côte d'Ivoire","answerText":"Côte d'Ivoire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1986</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Côte d'Ivoire"},"facts":[["Capital","Yamoussoukro"],["Population","33 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","322,462 km² (124,503 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","cote divoire"],"abstract":"<b>Côte d'Ivoire</b> has been known to the outside world under two names, and the change was made by the state itself rather than by translation: it was recognised in 1960 as the Republic of the Ivory Coast and renamed in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The territory had been under French sovereignty until the day it became independent, 7 August 1960, and the American record treats the recognition and the independence as one event on that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Dwight Eisenhower wrote to Prime Minister Félix Houphouët-Boigny that same day to say the United States recognised the new republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on that date as well, the American consulate at Abidjan being raised to embassy status with its former consul, Donald R. Norland, as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the state six weeks later, at the 864th plenary meeting of its fifteenth session on 20 September 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That sitting was one of the busiest in the organisation's history: the resolution admitting this country is numbered 1484 and the one admitting Madagascar 1478, six apart in the same volume and adopted at the same meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The country keeps its official capital and its working seat in different cities, and the United Nations footnotes its own profile to say so, naming Yamoussoukro the capital and Abidjan the administrative capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That inland capital is a small place by the standards of the country around it, holding about 231,000 people against a national count of nearly 33 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The recorded population has risen from 25.2 million in 2015 to 32.7 million, nearly a third more in a decade, which is close to the growth Madagascar shows over the same years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two international counts agree on that figure and differ over the country's area by two square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Côte d'Ivoire,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ci.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cote d'Ivoire (Ivory Coast),” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cote-divoire. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cote d'Ivoire,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CIV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Cote d'Ivoire,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CIV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of the Ivory Coast to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1484 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206134. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Malagasy Republic to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1478 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205972. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Flag_of_C%C3%B4te_d%27Ivoire.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_C%C3%B4te_d%27Ivoire.svg)","alt":"The flag of Côte d'Ivoire: three vertical bands, orange, white and green."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Basilique_notre_Dame_de_la_Paix_de_Yamoussoukro_5.jpg/1920px-Basilique_notre_Dame_de_la_Paix_de_Yamoussoukro_5.jpg","title":"the Basilica of Our Lady of Peace, Côte d'Ivoire","desc":"The Basilica of Our Lady of Peace at Yamoussoukro.","credit":"Didierwiki, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilique_notre_Dame_de_la_Paix_de_Yamoussoukro_5.jpg","alt":"A domed basilica with a colonnade above open lawns."}},
{"id":"gw-524","num":524,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the administrative capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Pretoria","answerText":"Pretoria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Credentials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 September 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised to embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 March 1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Africa","dot":"Pretoria"},"facts":[["Country","South Africa"],["Population","2.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Africa"],["Seat of","the President and Cabinet"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","south africa"],"abstract":"<b>Pretoria</b> is where the President and the Cabinet of South Africa meet, which is what makes it the administrative capital of a country with three of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations footnotes its own country profile to record the division, naming this city the administrative capital, Cape Town the legislative one and Bloemfontein the judiciary one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not the first seat of the American mission: the United States opened a legation at Cape Town on 19 December 1929, appointing the consul general Ralph J. Totten as minister resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Totten presented those credentials in February 1930, and the legation was moved here six months later, on 20 June, with Totten promoted to envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He presented his new credentials on 8 September 1930, and the American mission has been seated in this city ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Department of State announced in December 1948 that the two countries' legations would be raised to embassies, and North Winship presented his credentials as the first American ambassador on 23 March 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country around it was created on 31 May 1910 by joining four colonies into a self-governing dominion whose foreign relations London still controlled, and it declared itself a republic on the same date in 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat records that the country joined in 1931, left in 1961 and rejoined in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> While the government seated here enforced apartheid, the General Assembly adopted at its 2185th plenary meeting, on 30 November 1973, a convention declaring that system a crime against humanity, annexed to the resolution itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city holds about 2.5 million people by the United Nations count, and the country has held a seat there since November 1945 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “South Africa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/za.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “South Africa,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/south-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Africa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-africa. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “South Africa and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/south_africa_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “International Convention on the Suppression and Punishment of the Crime of Apartheid,” resolution 3068 (XXVIII), 30 November 1973, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/191217. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/View_of_Pretoria%2C_Tshwane%2C_Gauteng%2C_South_Africa_-_20308763589.jpg/1920px-View_of_Pretoria%2C_Tshwane%2C_Gauteng%2C_South_Africa_-_20308763589.jpg","title":"Pretoria","desc":"Pretoria, seen from a distance.","credit":"South African Tourism, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Pretoria,_Tshwane,_Gauteng,_South_Africa_-_20308763589.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Pretoria."}},
{"id":"gw-752","num":752,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the legislative capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cape Town","answerText":"Cape Town","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 December 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Credentials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1930</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Africa","dot":"Cape Town"},"facts":[["Country","South Africa"],["Region","Southern Africa"],["Seat of","Parliament"],["First US legation","1929"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","south africa"],"abstract":"<b>Cape Town</b> is where the South African Parliament sits, which is what makes it the legislative capital of a country whose executive and judicial seats are elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat states the division in that form, giving each city the institution that earns it its title rather than a bare label.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations records the same three-way split in a footnote to its country profile, though it names only the administrative capital in the field itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This was the first seat of American diplomacy in the country: a legation was established here on 19 December 1929, when the consul general Ralph J. Totten was appointed minister resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Totten presented his credentials to the Union government on 18 February 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Four months later the legation was moved inland to Pretoria and Totten was promoted, so the American mission's stay in this city lasted half a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country had been made on 31 May 1910 by joining four colonies into a self-governing dominion of the British Empire, sovereign at home while London kept control of its foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Balfour Declaration of 1926 called the United Kingdom and the dominions autonomous communities equal in status and in no way subordinate to one another, and the Statute of Westminster gave that formula the force of law in December 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States had already recognised the union on 5 November 1929, when President Herbert Hoover accepted the credentials of Eric Hendrik Louw as its first minister in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in June 1948 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since it opened; in between, the General Assembly adopted at its 2185th plenary meeting on 30 November 1973 a convention declaring apartheid a crime against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “South Africa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/za.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “South Africa,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/south-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Africa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-africa. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “South Africa and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/south_africa_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “International Convention on the Suppression and Punishment of the Crime of Apartheid,” resolution 3068 (XXVIII), 30 November 1973, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/191217. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Cape_Town_Skyline.jpg/1920px-Cape_Town_Skyline.jpg","title":"Cape Town","desc":"Cape Town, seen from a distance.","credit":"Bernard303, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Town_Skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Cape Town."}},
{"id":"gw-753","num":753,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the judicial capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bloemfontein","answerText":"Bloemfontein","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1910</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rejoined Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Africa","dot":"Bloemfontein"},"facts":[["Country","South Africa"],["Region","Southern Africa"],["Seat of","the Supreme Court of Appeal"],["Status","the judicial capital"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","south africa"],"abstract":"<b>Bloemfontein</b> is the seat of the Supreme Court of Appeal of South Africa, and that court is the whole reason it counts as one of the country's three capitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat sets the division out institution by institution: parliament at Cape Town makes that the legislative capital, the President and Cabinet at Pretoria make that the administrative one, and this court makes this the judicial one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations records the same three-way arrangement in a footnote to its country profile, calling this city the judiciary capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the least visible of the three from outside the country, since the American record of its own missions names the other two cities and never this one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation went to Cape Town in 1929 and to Pretoria in 1930, and the embassy that grew out of it has stayed in the second of those cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The country the court serves was assembled on 31 May 1910 out of four colonies, as a dominion sovereign at home whose external relations London kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Exactly 51 years later, on 31 May 1961, it declared itself a republic, and the American ambassador was reaccredited to the new state the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Secretariat records that the country joined the Commonwealth in 1931 on independence from Britain, left it in that same republican year of 1961, and rejoined in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between those dates the General Assembly adopted, at its 2185th plenary meeting on 30 November 1973, a convention declaring apartheid a crime against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country has held a United Nations seat since 7 November 1945, belonged to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from June 1948, and has been in the World Trade Organization since the day it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “South Africa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/za.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “South Africa,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/south-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Africa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-africa. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “South Africa and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/south_africa_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “International Convention on the Suppression and Punishment of the Crime of Apartheid,” resolution 3068 (XXVIII), 30 November 1973, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/191217. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Bloemfontein_skyline.jpg/1920px-Bloemfontein_skyline.jpg","title":"Bloemfontein","desc":"Bloemfontein, seen from a distance.","credit":"ShupingM, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloemfontein_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Bloemfontein."}},
{"id":"gw-051","num":51,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nepal","answerText":"Nepal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations begin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 February 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1959</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nepal"},"facts":[["Capital","Kathmandu"],["Population","30 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Area","147,181 km² (56,827 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","nepal"],"abstract":"<b>Nepal</b> was recognised by the United States as a kingdom in its own right on 21 April 1947, when Joseph C. Satterthwaite delivered a letter from President Harry Truman to the reigning king as the president's personal representative on a special mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country had never been a colony, so there was no transfer of sovereignty to date the recognition from, and the mission was sent to a court rather than to a new government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began the following February, when the kingdom set up a legation to the United States under Commanding General Kaiser Shum Shere Jung Bahadur Rana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That minister was simultaneously ambassador to Great Britain, and the whole legation staff lived at the Nepalese embassy in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American embassy in the country was not established until 6 August 1959, eleven years after relations began, with L. Douglas Heck as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The United States has given the country economic aid since 1951, and the State Department's own account says that aid is now directed at economic development and at keeping democratic institutions viable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It took a United Nations seat on 14 December 1955, admitted alongside a group of other applicants in a single resolution at the Assembly’s 555th plenary meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Security Council draft four days earlier, naming eighteen applicants including this one, had failed to win the votes it needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 23 April 2004, having never been a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown only slowly, from 27.8 million in 2015 to 29.6 million, and the two international counts agree on that figure and on the country's area to within one square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Nepal,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/np.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nepal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/nepal. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Nepal and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/nepal_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Nepal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NPL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Nepal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NPL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Admission of New Members: Draft Resolution,” S/3502, 10 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/536578. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9b/Flag_of_Nepal.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nepal.svg)","alt":"The flag of Nepal: two stacked crimson pennants with a blue border, bearing a white moon and sun."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/The_Annapurna_range_from_Pokhara.jpg/1920px-The_Annapurna_range_from_Pokhara.jpg","title":"the Annapurna range, Nepal","desc":"The Annapurna range from Pokhara.","credit":"Jmhullot, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Annapurna_range_from_Pokhara.jpg","alt":"Snow peaks rising beyond a lake and green hills."}},
{"id":"gw-052","num":52,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cameroon","answerText":"Cameroon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Trusteeship ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cameroon"},"facts":[["Capital","Yaoundé"],["Population","30 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Area","475,442 km² (183,569 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","cameroon"],"abstract":"<b>Cameroon</b> became independent by the ending of a United Nations trusteeship rather than by a transfer from a colonial power, which is why the American record dates recognition and independence to the same New Year's Day of 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> France had administered the territory under that trusteeship, and the United Nations brought it to an end on 1 January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition took the form of a notification rather than a letter: the American consul general at the capital, Bolard More, told the authorities that his post was being raised to an embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the embassy therefore began on the same date, with More as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The republic that emerged that January was only part of the territory carrying the name now, since a second portion had been administered by Britain under its own trusteeship and joined later, which is why the country has two official languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted it as the Republic of Cameroun, under the French spelling, at the 864th plenary meeting of its fifteenth session on 20 September 1960 — the same sitting that admitted Madagascar and Côte d'Ivoire, and the resolution numbered two before Madagascar's in the same volume.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 3 May 1963 and joined the World Trade Organization in December 1995, some months after that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen faster than most countries in this collection, from 22.8 million in 2015 to 29.9 million, almost a third more in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two international counts agree on that figure exactly and put its area within 210 km² of each other, a difference of four hundredths of one per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under Middle Africa, the grouping that runs from Chad and the Central African Republic south to Angola.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cameroon,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cameroon,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cameroon. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cameroon and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cameroon_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cameroon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CMR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Cameroun to Membership of the United Nations,” resolution 1476 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205969. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Cameroon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CMR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Flag_of_Cameroon.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cameroon.svg)","alt":"The flag of Cameroon: three vertical bands, green, red and yellow, with a yellow star on the red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Mount_Cameroon_2.jpg","title":"Mount Cameroon, Cameroon","desc":"Mount Cameroon.","credit":"FORCHA101, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Cameroon_2.jpg","alt":"A long forested volcano behind palms and houses."}},
{"id":"gw-053","num":53,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Venezuela","answerText":"Venezuela","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Separated from Colombia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 February 1835</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty in force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 November 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Venezuela"},"facts":[["Capital","Caracas"],["Population","29 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","916,445 km² (353,841 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","venezuela"],"abstract":"<b>Venezuela</b> reached independence from Spain by 1819 not on its own but as part of the Republic of Colombia, the federation the United States recognised in 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It separated from that federation in 1830, and only then did it need recognising in its own right, thirteen years after the wars of independence had settled the wider question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That came on 28 February 1835, in an unusual form: the United States issued an exequatur to Nicholas D. C. Moller as Venezuelan consul in New York, which is a recognition of a state expressed through the acceptance of its consul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed four months later, when the American chargé d'affaires John G. A. Williamson presented his credentials on 30 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate had already been open at Maracaibo since 1824, more than a decade before either event, and it stayed open until 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The first commercial treaty between the two republics was signed on 20 January 1836, ratifications were exchanged that May, and it came into force in June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation at the capital was raised to an embassy in 1939, with Frank P. Corrigan as the first ambassador, a century and four years after the exequatur that began the relationship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has held a United Nations seat since 15 November 1945, which makes it an original member, and it entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade only in August 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has fallen rather than risen over the last decade, from 30.6 million in 2015 to 28.4 million by 2020, recovering only slightly since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two international counts differ over its area by 17,640 km², a spread of nearly two per cent, which is wide for a country neither source treats as disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Venezuela (Bolivarian Republic of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ve.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Venezuela,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/venezuela. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Venezuela and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/venezuela_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Venezuela, RB,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VEN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Venezuela, RB,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VEN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Flag_of_Venezuela.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Venezuela.svg)","alt":"The flag of Venezuela: yellow, blue and red horizontal bands with an arc of eight white stars on the blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Salto_Chur%C3%BAn_Mer%C3%BA.JPG/1920px-Salto_Chur%C3%BAn_Mer%C3%BA.JPG","title":"Angel Falls, Venezuela","desc":"Angel Falls.","credit":"Heribert Dezeo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salto_Chur%C3%BAn_Mer%C3%BA.JPG","alt":"A view of Angel Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-525","num":525,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Rome","answerText":"Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1797–1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 December 1941</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 January 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Italy","dot":"Rome"},"facts":[["Country","Italy"],["Population","4.2 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Also known for","the Rome Statute"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","italy"],"abstract":"<b>Rome</b> became the capital of a united Italy only in 1871, after the kingdom formed in 1861 absorbed Venetia and the former Papal States in the wake of the Franco-Prussian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American mission followed the capital rather than leading it: George P. Marsh oversaw its move from Turin to Florence in 1865 and from Florence to this city in 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A separate American legation to the Papal States had been established here in 1848, and an American consulate had been open in the city since 1797, closing only in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation was raised to an embassy in 1893, though the first appointee declined, and Wayne MacVeagh presented his credentials here as the first American ambassador in March 1894.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy was closed on 11 December 1941, when Italy declared war on the United States, and relations were not re-established until October 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ambassador Alexander C. Kirk reopened it on 8 January 1945, presenting his credentials to a country still at war within its own borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has since given its name to the treaty that founded the International Criminal Court, adopted by a United Nations conference of plenipotentiaries that sat here from 15 June to 17 July 1998.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country it governs entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in May 1950 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It took its United Nations seat on 14 December 1955, admitted in the same resolution as Nepal after a decade in which the Cold War had kept new members out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 4.2 million people in the city, roughly one Italian in fourteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Italy,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/it.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Italy,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/italy. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Italy and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/italy_e.htm. [Open access]","United Nations Diplomatic Conference of Plenipotentiaries on the Establishment of an International Criminal Court, “Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court,” A/CONF.183/9, 17 July 1998, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/260261. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Rome_skyline_panorama.jpg/1920px-Rome_skyline_panorama.jpg","title":"Rome","desc":"Rome, seen from a distance.","credit":"Nicholas Hartmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rome_skyline_panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Rome."}},
{"id":"gw-054","num":54,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Australia","answerText":"Australia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1901</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 January 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statute adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1942</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Australia"},"facts":[["Capital","Canberra"],["Population","27 million (2025)"],["Region","Oceania"],["Area","about 7.7 million km² (3.0 million sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","australia"],"abstract":"<b>Australia</b> was made on the first day of the 20th century, when six colonies were joined into a self-governing dominion of the British Empire, sovereign at home while London kept control of its dealings with everyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Control of that external policy came gradually over the next four decades rather than at any single moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Balfour Declaration of 1926 called the United Kingdom and the dominions autonomous communities equal in status and in no way subordinate to one another, and the Statute of Westminster gave that formula legal force in December 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The statute did not apply here until the country adopted it, which its parliament did only on 9 October 1942, backdating the effect to the day war began with Germany in September 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the country on 8 January 1940, when the two governments jointly announced that they were establishing diplomatic relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Richard Gardiner Casey presented credentials as the first Australian minister in Washington that March, and Clarence E. Gauss reached the reverse post in July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two legations were raised to embassies in 1946, the White House announcing it in July and the ambassadors presenting credentials that September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took a United Nations seat on 1 November 1945, which makes it an original member, and it has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown from 23.8 million in 2015 to between 27.0 and 27.6 million, the United Nations giving the lower figure and the World Bank the higher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two also differ over the country's area by about 49,000 km², a spread larger than several of the countries in this collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Australia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/au.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Australia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/australia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Australia and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/australia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Australia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Australia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Flag_of_Australia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Australia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Australia: a blue field with the Union Jack in the canton, the Commonwealth Star below it and the Southern Cross at the fly."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/ULURU.jpg/1920px-ULURU.jpg","title":"Uluru, Australia","desc":"Uluru.","credit":"Ek2030372672, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ULURU.jpg","alt":"A view of Uluru."}},
{"id":"gw-055","num":55,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Niger","answerText":"Niger","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 February 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace Corps</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Niger"},"facts":[["Capital","Niamey"],["Population","28 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","1,267,000 km² (489,190 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","niger"],"abstract":"<b>Niger</b> had been under French sovereignty until 3 August 1960, when President Dwight Eisenhower sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Hamani Diori recognising the new republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on the same date by an arrangement worth noticing: Donald R. Norland presented credentials as chargé d'affaires on 2 August, to take effect the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American embassy in the capital was not established until 3 February 1961, six months later, with Joseph W. Schutz in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Peace Corps programme began in 1962 and has run ever since, and the State Department's own account notes that American aid there is administered through non-governmental organisations rather than through a resident development mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That account also lists the country's memberships, among them the African Union, the West African Monetary Union, the Economic Community of West African States, and the commissions that govern the Niger river and Lake Chad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted it at the 864th plenary meeting of its fifteenth session, on 20 September 1960, one of a run of admissions at that single sitting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on the last day of 1963 and joined the World Trade Organization in December 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by two fifths in a decade, from 19.9 million in 2015 to 27.9 million, which is the fastest growth of any country carded so far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both international counts give that figure and both give the country's area as exactly 1,267,000 km², a rare case of complete agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations files it under Western Africa, though most of its territory lies in the Sahara well north of the coast that grouping is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Niger,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ne.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Niger,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/niger. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Niger and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/niger_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Niger,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NER/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of the Niger to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1482 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205975. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Niger,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NER/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Flag_of_Niger.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Niger.svg)","alt":"The flag of Niger: orange, white and green horizontal bands with an orange disc in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/1997_277-9A_Agadez_mosque.jpg","title":"the Great Mosque of Agadez, Niger","desc":"The Great Mosque of Agadez.","credit":"Dan Lundberg, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1997_277-9A_Agadez_mosque.jpg","alt":"A tall mud-brick minaret studded with projecting timbers."}},
{"id":"gw-056","num":56,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"North Korea","answerText":"North Korea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Truce</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanctions imposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 October 2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"North Korea"},"facts":[["Capital","Pyongyang"],["Population","27 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Asia"],["Area","120,538 km² (46,540 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","north korea"],"abstract":"<b>North Korea</b> exists because the United States and the Soviet Union agreed during the Second World War to divide the peninsula temporarily at the 38th parallel, so as to oversee the removal of Japanese forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither power would then allow its own Korean ally to be threatened by unification: the Soviets supported Kim Il Sung in the north and the Americans Syngman Rhee in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States did not treat Korea as a key part of its defensive strategy for East Asia, and American forces withdrew from the south in the late 1940s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Assuming from that withdrawal that the south was not a vital American interest, Kim’s army attacked in June 1950 and came close to conquering the entire peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American military returned at the head of a United Nations-authorised force, pushed the northern army back above the parallel and beyond, and China entered the war late that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Only in 1953 did the two sides reach an uneasy truce, which crystallised the division between north and south that exists today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither Korean state joined the United Nations for another 38 years after that truce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That changed on 8 August 1991, when the Security Council unanimously recommended both for membership in a single resolution at its 3001st meeting, and the General Assembly admitted them together on 17 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the country’s nuclear weapon tests the Council acted against it, adopting resolution 1718 unanimously on 14 October 2006, which demanded that it conduct no further test or ballistic missile launch and retract its announced withdrawal from the treaty on non-proliferation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown only slowly, from 25.6 million in 2015 to 26.6 million, and the two international counts agree on that figure and put its area within two square kilometres of each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic People's Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kp.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Korean War, 1950–1953,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 702 (1991),” adopted at its 3001st meeting, 8 August 1991, S/RES/702(1991), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/125255. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1718 (2006),” adopted at its 5551st meeting, 14 October 2006, S/RES/1718(2006), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/584729. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRK/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRK/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Flag_of_North_Korea.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Korea.svg)","alt":"The flag of North Korea: a wide red band between blue bands, with a red star on a white disc near the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Laika_ac_Mt._Paekdu_%287998657081%29.jpg/1920px-Laika_ac_Mt._Paekdu_%287998657081%29.jpg","title":"Paektu Mountain, North Korea","desc":"Paektu Mountain.","credit":"Laika ac from USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laika_ac_Mt._Paekdu_(7998657081).jpg","alt":"A view of Paektu Mountain."}},
{"id":"gw-526","num":526,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nairobi","answerText":"Nairobi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1963</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy bombed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1998</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council response</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 August 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kenya","dot":"Nairobi"},"facts":[["Country","Kenya"],["Population","4.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["US embassy","since 1964"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","kenya"],"abstract":"<b>Nairobi</b> became the capital of an independent country on 12 December 1963, after nearly seventy years in which the territory around it was governed as part of British East Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Kenya on that day, two days after the Secretary of the Interior Stewart Udall had delivered a message of congratulation from President Lyndon Johnson to Prime Minister Jomo Kenyatta as special ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and an American embassy in the city both began on 2 March 1964, when Ambassador William Attwood presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took a United Nations seat on 16 December 1963, four days after independence and before that embassy existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 4.6 million people in the city, roughly one Kenyan in twelve, and it is the only city in the country that the organisation's profile names at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 7 August 1998 the American embassy here was bombed, and the embassy at Dar es Salaam was bombed within minutes of it, the two attacks putting both capitals into the Security Council's record on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The State Department's own account records the toll in this city: 12 American citizens killed, 32 locally engaged staff and 247 Kenyan citizens, with about 5,000 Kenyans injured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Security Council met six days later and adopted a resolution strongly condemning the attacks in Kenya and Tanzania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 5 February 1964, less than two months after independence and before the American ambassador had presented his credentials, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its people are counted at about 57.5 million, a figure that has risen by more than a fifth in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Kenya,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ke.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kenya,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kenya. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1189 (1998),” adopted at its 3915th meeting, 13 August 1998, S/RES/1189(1998), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/258404. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Kenya and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/kenya_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kenya,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KEN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Nairobi_skyline_from_Gem_Hotel.jpg/1920px-Nairobi_skyline_from_Gem_Hotel.jpg","title":"Nairobi","desc":"Nairobi, seen from a distance.","credit":"Daniel Case, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nairobi_skyline_from_Gem_Hotel.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Nairobi."}},
{"id":"gw-057","num":57,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Syria","answerText":"Syria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">French Mandate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 September 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Union with Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1958–61</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Syria"},"facts":[["Capital","Damascus"],["Population","26 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","185,180 km² (71,498 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","syria"],"abstract":"<b>Syria</b> was part of the Ottoman Empire when the United States became independent, so its dealings with the world were conducted through the sultan's government at Istanbul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the empire collapsed the League of Nations approved a French mandate over it and Lebanon in 1922, giving France exclusive control of both countries' foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States appointed George Wadsworth agent and consul general in October 1942, a quasi-diplomatic presence in the capital while Washington waited to decide whether the country was effectively independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came on 8 September 1944 by an exchange of letters rather than a proclamation: Wadsworth offered full and unconditional recognition on receipt of written assurances about American rights, and the foreign minister Jamil Mardam Bey gave them the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wadsworth then presented credentials as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country ceased to exist as a separate state on 22 February 1958, when it joined Egypt to form the United Arab Republic, whose capital was Cairo; the American embassy in Damascus was reduced to a consulate general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It seceded on 28 September 1961 and was recognised again twelve days later, the consulate general being raised back to embassy rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Syria severed relations on 6 June 1967, the day after the Arab-Israeli war of that month began, and an American interests section worked out of the Italian embassy from February 1974 until relations were restored in June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country has held a United Nations seat since the organisation was founded in 1945, through all of that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen steeply over the last decade, from 19.4 million in 2015 to 25.6 million, and the two international counts agree on that figure and on the country’s area exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Syrian Arab Republic,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sy.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Syria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/syria. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Syrian Arab Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Syrian Arab Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The 1967 Arab-Israeli War,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/arab-israeli-war-1967. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Flag_of_Syria_%282025-%29.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Syria_%282025-%29.svg)","alt":"The flag of Syria: green, white and black horizontal bands with three red stars on the white."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Palmyra_03.jpg/1920px-Palmyra_03.jpg","title":"Palmyra, Syria","desc":"Palmyra.","credit":"Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palmyra_03.jpg","alt":"A view of Palmyra."}},
{"id":"gw-058","num":58,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mali","answerText":"Mali","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Senegal withdraws</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mali"},"facts":[["Capital","Bamako"],["Population","25 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","1,240,192 km² (478,841 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","mali"],"abstract":"<b>Mali</b> was recognised by the United States twice in one year, under two different states, and the second recognition is the one that matters now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 20 June 1960 President Dwight Eisenhower congratulated President Modibo Keïta on the independence of the Federation of Mali, a union of the Republic of Senegal and the Soudanese Republic that had just left French sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations with the federation began the same day, the American consulate general at Dakar being raised to embassy rank under Donald A. Dumont.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Senegal withdrew from the federation on 20 August, two months later, and on 22 September the National Assembly of the Soudanese Republic legislated that it would become the Republic of Mali.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised that republic on 24 September, when the American consul at Bamako, John Gunther Dean, told the government so on instructions from Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On the same date the consulate general at Bamako became an embassy under Dean, and the embassy at Dakar was reaccredited to Senegal, which formally ended relations with the federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the republic four days after that, at the 876th plenary meeting of its fifteenth session on 28 September 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade only in January 1993, more than thirty years after independence, and joined the World Trade Organization in May 1995, a few months after that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen by more than a third in a decade, from 18.6 million in 2015 to 25.2 million, growth of the kind the Sahel countries around it also show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two international counts agree on that figure exactly, and differ over the country's area by two square kilometres out of more than a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mali,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ml.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mali,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mali. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Mali and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/mali_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Mali,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MLI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Mali to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1491 (XV), 28 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206141. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Mali,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MLI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Flag_of_Mali.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mali.svg)","alt":"The flag of Mali: three vertical bands, green, yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Djenne_great_mud_mosque.jpg/1920px-Djenne_great_mud_mosque.jpg","title":"Great Mosque of Djenné, Mali","desc":"Great Mosque of Djenné.","credit":"Ruud Zwart, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djenne_great_mud_mosque.jpg","alt":"A view of Great Mosque of Djenné."}},
{"id":"gw-059","num":59,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Burkina Faso","answerText":"Burkina Faso","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 December 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Burkina Faso"},"facts":[["Capital","Ouagadougou"],["Population","24 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","274,200 km² (105,869 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","burkina faso"],"abstract":"<b>Burkina Faso</b> was recognised under a different name: on 5 August 1960 President Dwight Eisenhower sent a message of congratulation to Prime Minister Maurice Yaméogo on the independence of Upper Volta, until then under French sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state changed its own name in 1984, and the American guide to its recognitions still files the country under both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on the same August day, by the device of a chargé d'affaires presenting credentials the day before to take effect on the day of independence itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The man who did that, Donald R. Norland, had performed the identical manoeuvre for Niger two days earlier, so one American official opened relations with two new countries inside a week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An embassy of its own followed in the capital on the last day of that year, with Robert McKinnon in charge, five months after the relationship had begun without one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted it as the Republic of Upper Volta at the 864th plenary meeting of its fifteenth session on 20 September 1960, in a resolution numbered 1483; the one admitting Niger at the same sitting is numbered 1482, so the two states entered the organisation minutes apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 3 May 1963, the same day Cameroon did, and joined the World Trade Organization in June 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen by more than a quarter in a decade, from 18.8 million in 2015 to 24.1 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both international counts give that figure and both give the country's area as exactly 274,200 km², complete agreement of the kind Niger also shows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations files it under Western Africa, though it has no coast at all, its territory sitting inland behind the states that do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Burkina Faso,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bf.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burkina Faso (Upper Volta),” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burkina-faso. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Burkina Faso and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/burkina_faso_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Burkina Faso,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BFA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of the Upper Volta to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1483 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206133. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Niger,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/niger. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of the Niger to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1482 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205975. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Flag_of_Burkina_Faso.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burkina_Faso.svg)","alt":"The flag of Burkina Faso: red over green with a yellow five-pointed star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/2016.05-441-131ap_wall_Lorop%C3%A9ni_Ruins_nr.Lorop%C3%A9ni%28Poni_Prv.%29%2CBF_sun15may2016-1106h.jpg/1920px-2016.05-441-131ap_wall_Lorop%C3%A9ni_Ruins_nr.Lorop%C3%A9ni%28Poni_Prv.%29%2CBF_sun15may2016-1106h.jpg","title":"Ruins of Loropéni, Burkina Faso","desc":"Ruins of Loropéni.","credit":"Rik Schuiling / TropCrop-TCS, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2016.05-441-131ap_wall_Lorop%C3%A9ni_Ruins_nr.Lorop%C3%A9ni(Poni_Prv.),BF_sun15may2016-1106h.jpg","alt":"A view of Ruins of Loropéni."}},
{"id":"gw-527","num":527,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Naypyidaw","answerText":"Naypyidaw","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations begin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 January 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1948</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Myanmar","dot":"Naypyidaw"},"facts":[["Country","Myanmar"],["Population","547,000 (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["US embassy","at Rangoon since 1947"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","myanmar"],"abstract":"<b>Naypyidaw</b> is the capital of Myanmar and the smallest capital of any large country in this collection: the United Nations counts about 547,000 people in it, against nearly 55 million in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same profile spells the name Nay Pyi Taw, in three words, which is the form the Burmese government itself uses and the reason the city appears under several spellings in English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not where the American mission sits, and it is not where that mission has ever sat: relations began at Rangoon, the former capital on the delta, and the record of them names no other city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They began there in an unusual order, before independence rather than after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Burmese leaders had agreed with the British in January 1947 on a staged process, and the provisional government created under it was allowed to conduct relations with other states before full sovereignty arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> So on 18 September 1947 the two governments agreed to exchange representatives with the rank of ambassador, and the American consulate general at Rangoon was raised to an embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition of the Union of Burma as an independent sovereign state followed on 4 January 1948, in a congratulatory message from President Harry Truman to President Sao Shwe Thaike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country at the 131st plenary meeting of its second special session, on 19 April 1948, fifteen weeks after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in July 1948, three months after that, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 51.1 million in 2015 to 54.9 million, and the two international counts agree on that figure and on the country's area to within thirteen square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Myanmar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burma,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burma. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Myanmar and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/myanmar_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Myanmar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MMR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Union of Burma to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 188 (S-2), 19 April 1948, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/210015. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Hluttaw_Complex%2C_Naypyidaw.jpg/1920px-Hluttaw_Complex%2C_Naypyidaw.jpg","title":"Naypyidaw","desc":"Naypyidaw, seen from a distance.","credit":"Peerapat Wimolrungkarat , Thai Government photographer, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hluttaw_Complex,_Naypyidaw.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Naypyidaw."}},
{"id":"gw-060","num":60,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Taiwan","answerText":"Taiwan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US embassy arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 December 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN seat changes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 October 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US embassy closes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 February 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2002</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Taiwan"},"facts":[["Seat of government","Taipei"],["Population","about 23 million"],["Region","Eastern Asia"],["Area","36,197 km² (13,976 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","taiwan"],"abstract":"<b>Taiwan</b> is invisible to most of the institutions that describe countries, because they are organised around membership of the United Nations and it holds no seat there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American consular posts existed on the island while it was a colony of the Empire of Japan, at Danshui from 1898 and at Taipei, then called Taihoku, from 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Chinese Communists established the People's Republic on the mainland in October 1949, the government of the Republic of China withdrew across the strait, and the American embassy followed it to Taipei on 19 December that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Washington went on recognising that government as the government of all China for another three decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Twice in that period the strait itself became a crisis, in 1954 and 1955 and again in 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly settled the question of the seat on 25 October 1971, adopting at its 1976th plenary meeting a resolution restoring what it called the lawful rights of the People's Republic in the organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 1 January 1979 the United States recognised that republic as the sole legitimate government of China and withdrew recognition from the one in Taipei on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The embassy there closed on 28 February and a liaison office in Beijing became an embassy the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One international body does list the island in its own right: the World Trade Organization admitted it on 1 January 2002 as the Separate Customs Territory of Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen and Matsu, shortened to Chinese Taipei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That body admits customs territories rather than states, which is why a place with no United Nations seat can hold a seat in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: China,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/china. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Chinese Revolution of 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/chinese-rev. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Taiwan Strait Crises: 1954–55 and 1958,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/taiwan-strait-crises. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Restoration of the Lawful Rights of the People's Republic of China in the United Nations,” resolution 2758 (XXVI), 25 October 1971, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192054. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Separate Customs Territory of Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen and Matsu (Chinese Taipei) and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/chinese_taipei_e.htm. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/Flag_of_the_Republic_of_China.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_China.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Republic of China: a red field with a blue canton bearing a white twelve-pointed sun."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Taipei_Taiwan_Taipei-101-Tower-01.jpg/1920px-Taipei_Taiwan_Taipei-101-Tower-01.jpg","title":"Taipei 101, Taiwan","desc":"Taipei 101.","credit":"CEphoto, Uwe Aranas, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taipei_Taiwan_Taipei-101-Tower-01.jpg","alt":"A tall tiered tower rising above a city."}},
{"id":"gw-061","num":61,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sri Lanka","answerText":"Sri Lanka","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 February 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations begin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 October 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sri Lanka"},"facts":[["Capital","Colombo"],["Population","22 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Area","65,610 km² (25,332 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","sri lanka"],"abstract":"<b>Sri Lanka</b> was recognised under the name Ceylon on 4 February 1948, and the date was not chosen by Washington: it was the one already set in an agreement between the British and Ceylonese governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Harry Truman's letter conveying recognition was dated the day before and addressed to the governor general, Sir Henry Moore, since the new state entered the world as a dominion within the British Commonwealth rather than as a republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began later that year, on 29 October, when the Ceylonese embassy opened in Washington under Sir Claude Corea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American embassy in the reverse direction opened only in August 1949, when Felix Cole presented his credentials at Colombo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in July 1948, less than six months after independence and before the American embassy at Colombo existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A United Nations seat took far longer: it was admitted only on 14 December 1955, under resolution 995, in the single batch that ended a decade of deadlock over new members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government is divided between two cities, and the United Nations footnotes its own profile to say so, naming Colombo the capital and Sri Jayewardenepura Kotte the legislative capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two international counts of the population differ by nearly 7%, the United Nations giving 23.2 million and the World Bank 21.8 million, which is one of the widest gaps in this collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The World Bank's series also shows a fall since 2020, from 21.9 million to 21.8 million, rather than the growth almost every other country carded so far records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the island's area, by contrast, the two agree to the square kilometre, which makes the population gap look less like measurement and more like a question about who is counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sri Lanka,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sri Lanka,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sri-lanka. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Sri Lanka and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/sri_lanka_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sri Lanka,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LKA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Sri Lanka,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LKA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Flag_of_Sri_Lanka.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sri_Lanka.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sri Lanka: a golden lion holding a sword on maroon, beside green and orange stripes, within a gold border."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Sigiriya_%28141688197%29.jpeg/1920px-Sigiriya_%28141688197%29.jpeg","title":"Sigiriya, Sri Lanka","desc":"Sigiriya.","credit":"Wrobell, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigiriya_(141688197).jpeg","alt":"A view of Sigiriya."}},
{"id":"gw-062","num":62,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Malawi","answerText":"Malawi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 July 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 April 1976</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malawi"},"facts":[["Capital","Lilongwe"],["Population","22 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","118,480 km² (45,745 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","malawi"],"abstract":"<b>Malawi</b> had been under British sovereignty as Nyasaland, and both its independence and its recognition by the United States fall on the same day, 6 July 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition took the form of an administrative act rather than a message: the American consulate at Blantyre was raised to embassy rank as the country became independent, with Edward W. Holmes as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy carried a curious label for six years, being designated as at Zomba while physically standing at Blantyre, and it moved to the present capital only on 1 April 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth in the same year it became independent, the Secretariat's own entry attaching the reason to the date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It took a United Nations seat on 1 December 1964, five months after independence and while its American embassy was still labelled for a city it did not stand in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 28 August 1964, less than two months after becoming a state, and joined the World Trade Organization in May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen by about a third in a decade, from 17.1 million in 2015 to 22.2 million, which is fast for a country of its size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The three bodies that publish its area do not agree: the United Nations gives 117,726 km², the World Bank 118,480 and the Commonwealth Secretariat a round 118,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The spread is only about 0.7%, but on a country this narrow it is the difference between two published figures a reader might meet on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations files it under Eastern Africa, and the lake that fills much of its length lies along its eastern side, which is why the country is so much longer than it is wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Malawi,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mw.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malawi,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malawi. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Malawi and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/malawi_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Malawi,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MWI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Malawi,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/malawi. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Malawi,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MWI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/Flag_of_Malawi.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malawi.svg)","alt":"The flag of Malawi: black, red and green horizontal bands with a rising red sun on the black."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Lake_Malawi%2C_shore_island.jpg/1920px-Lake_Malawi%2C_shore_island.jpg","title":"Lake Malawi, Malawi","desc":"Lake Malawi.","credit":"i_pinz, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Malawi,_shore_island.jpg","alt":"A sandy shore and open water with an island beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-528","num":528,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bogotá","answerText":"Bogotá","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 June 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised to embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1939</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Colombia","dot":"Bogotá"},"facts":[["Country","Colombia"],["Population","10.8 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Treaty still in force","1846"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","colombia"],"abstract":"<b>Bogotá</b> has been the seat of a government the United States recognised since 1822, but of four differently named states in that time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came on 19 June that year, when President James Monroe received Manuel Torres as Colombian chargé d'affaires, and the federation Torres represented then took in the territory of today's Colombia, Ecuador, Venezuela and Panama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Richard Anderson of Kentucky presented his credentials here on 16 December 1823, opening the American mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations with the government in this city were not interrupted when Ecuador and Venezuela left the federation in 1830, though the state's name changed four times: New Granada from 1830, the Grenadine Confederation from 1856, the United States of Colombia from 1863 and the Republic of Colombia from 1886.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Panama separated in 1903, and the mission stayed where it was through that too, so one address in one city has served every one of those states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A treaty of friendship and commerce concluded with New Granada in 1846 and proclaimed in 1848 is still in force, which makes it one of the oldest American treaties anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation was raised to an embassy in 1939, with Spruille Braden as the first ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 10.8 million people in the city, footnoting that the figure covers the built-up area running out into Soacha, Chía and Funza rather than the city alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country around it entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade only in October 1981, more than thirty years after that agreement began, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since April 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 47.0 million in 2015 to 53.4 million, and the two international counts agree on that figure and put the country's area within 1,129 km² of each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Colombia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/co.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Colombia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/colombia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Colombia and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/colombia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Colombia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Colombia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Bogot%C3%A1_Colombia_panorama.jpg/1920px-Bogot%C3%A1_Colombia_panorama.jpg","title":"Bogotá","desc":"Bogotá, seen from a distance.","credit":"Dori, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bogot%C3%A1_Colombia_panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Bogotá."}},
{"id":"gw-063","num":63,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Zambia","answerText":"Zambia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 February 1982</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Zambia"},"facts":[["Capital","Lusaka"],["Population","22 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","752,612 km² (290,585 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","zambia"],"abstract":"<b>Zambia</b> had been under British sovereignty as Northern Rhodesia, and its independence, its recognition by the United States and the opening of an American embassy all fall on one day, 24 October 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition was an administrative act rather than a message from a president: the American consulate at Lusaka was raised to embassy rank as the country became independent, with Robert C. Foulon as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That was one of two British territories in the region to become independent that year, Malawi having done so in July by the same device of raising an American consulate to an embassy on the day itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The State Department's own account of the relationship notes the country's transition from one-party rule to democracy in 1991 and its role as a haven for refugees from across Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It took a United Nations seat on 1 December 1964, five weeks after independence, and the American mission in the country has never moved from the city it opened in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade only in February 1982, eighteen years after becoming a state, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen by about a third in a decade, from 16.4 million in 2015 to 21.9 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two international counts agree on that figure and differ over the country's area by two square kilometres out of three quarters of a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations files it under Eastern Africa, though it lies well inland, with no coast on either ocean and its trade routes running out through its neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It borders eight states, more than any other country carded so far, which is the arithmetic of sitting inland in the middle of a continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Zambia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/zm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Zambia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/zambia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Zambia and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/zambia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Zambia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ZMB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malawi,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malawi. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Zambia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ZMB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Flag_of_Zambia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Zambia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Zambia: a green field with orange, black and red stripes at the fly beneath an orange eagle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Cataratas_Victoria%2C_Zambia-Zimbabue%2C_2018-07-27%2C_DD_04.jpg/1920px-Cataratas_Victoria%2C_Zambia-Zimbabue%2C_2018-07-27%2C_DD_04.jpg","title":"Victoria Falls, Zambia","desc":"Victoria Falls.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cataratas_Victoria,_Zambia-Zimbabue,_2018-07-27,_DD_04.jpg","alt":"A view of Victoria Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-064","num":64,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kazakhstan","answerText":"Kazakhstan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 February 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 2015</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kazakhstan"},"facts":[["Capital","Astana"],["Population","21 million (2025)"],["Region","Central Asia"],["Area","2,724,900 km² (1,052,100 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","kazakhstan"],"abstract":"<b>Kazakhstan</b> was a constituent republic of the Soviet Union until that state dissolved, and the United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation on the dissolution itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations date from the same announcement, so recognition and relations share a sentence in the American record as well as a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy that followed opened not in the present capital but at Alma-Ata, the city now called Almaty, on 3 February 1992 with William Harrison Courtney as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That city was the capital at the time; the seat of government moved north later, and the mission's own first address therefore records a capital the country no longer has.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Bush administration, watching the union come apart, had put the prevention of nuclear catastrophe and a stable transition to new political orders ahead of speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted the republic at its 82nd plenary meeting on 2 March 1992, the same sitting that admitted Uzbekistan, in resolutions numbered two apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It waited far longer for the trade organisation, joining only on 30 November 2015, more than twenty years after that body opened, and it was never party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade that preceded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 18.1 million in 2015 to 20.8 million, growth of about a seventh in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two international counts agree on that figure and give the country's area as identical, at 2,724,902 km².<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That makes it the largest landlocked state in the world by a wide margin: it is bigger than all of western Europe and reaches no ocean at any point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Kazakhstan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kazakhstan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kazakhstan. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Kazakhstan and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/kazakhstan_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kazakhstan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KAZ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Collapse of the Soviet Union, 1989–1991,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/collapse-soviet-union. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Kazakhstan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/224, 2 March 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/147692. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Kazakhstan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KAZ/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Flag_of_Kazakhstan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kazakhstan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Kazakhstan: a sky-blue field with a golden sun and steppe eagle, and a national ornament down the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Charyn_Canyon%2C_Kazakhstan_03.jpg/1920px-Charyn_Canyon%2C_Kazakhstan_03.jpg","title":"Charyn Canyon, Kazakhstan","desc":"Charyn Canyon.","credit":"Bgag, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charyn_Canyon,_Kazakhstan_03.jpg","alt":"A view of Charyn Canyon."}},
{"id":"gw-065","num":65,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Chad","answerText":"Chad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 February 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1980–82</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Chad"},"facts":[["Capital","N'Djamena"],["Population","21 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Area","1,284,000 km² (495,755 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","chad"],"abstract":"<b>Chad</b> declared its independence from French Equatorial Africa on 11 August 1960, and President Dwight Eisenhower sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister François Tombalbaye recognising the new republic the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on that date by an arrangement common in that season of independences: the American consul at Brazzaville, in another country entirely, presented credentials as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An embassy inside the country followed on 1 February 1961, at the capital then called Fort Lamy, with Frederic L. Chapin in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That city has since been renamed N'Djamena, so the American record names the same place twice under two names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took a United Nations seat on 20 September 1960, the day the General Assembly held the 864th plenary meeting of its fifteenth session and admitted a run of newly independent African states, Cameroon and Niger among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The embassy was closed on 24 March 1980, when American personnel were evacuated after heavy fighting broke out in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reopened on 15 January 1982, with John Blane as principal officer and chargé d'affaires, after nearly two years in which the United States had no mission in the country at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 12 July 1963, three years after independence, and joined the World Trade Organization in October 1996, some months after that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen by more than two fifths in a decade, from 14.6 million in 2015 to 21.0 million, among the fastest growth of any country carded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both international counts give that figure and both give the country's area as exactly 1,284,000 km², complete agreement of the kind Niger and Burkina Faso also show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Chad,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/td.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Chad,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/chad. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Chad and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/chad_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Chad,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TCD/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Chad,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TCD/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Cameroun to Membership of the United Nations,” resolution 1476 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205969. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of the Niger to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1482 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205975. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Flag_of_Chad.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Chad.svg)","alt":"The flag of Chad: three vertical bands, blue, yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Ounianga_Serir.jpg","title":"Lakes of Ounianga, Chad","desc":"Lakes of Ounianga.","credit":"Jacques Taberlet, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ounianga_Serir.jpg","alt":"A view of Lakes of Ounianga."}},
{"id":"gw-529","num":529,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Seoul","answerText":"Seoul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations begin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 March 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Korea","dot":"Seoul"},"facts":[["Country","South Korea"],["Population","10.0 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Asia"],["Named in","the 1949 recognition"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","south korea"],"abstract":"<b>Seoul</b> is named in the document that recognised the country around it: a White House statement of 1 January 1949 recognised the Republic of Korea, with its capital at this city, as an independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 25 March that year, when Ambassador John Myun Chang presented his credentials to President Truman in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state had been created out of the southern half of a peninsula the United States and the Soviet Union agreed during the Second World War to divide temporarily at the 38th parallel, so as to oversee the removal of Japanese forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neither power would then let its own Korean ally be threatened by unification, and American forces withdrew from the south in the late 1940s without treating it as central to their defence of East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The northern army attacked in June 1950 and came close to conquering the entire peninsula before an American-led United Nations force pushed it back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Only in 1953 did the two sides reach an uneasy truce, and the United States and this country signed a mutual security treaty that year designed to protect it from its neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neither Korean state held a United Nations seat for another 38 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Security Council recommended both for membership in a single resolution on 8 August 1991, and the General Assembly admitted them together at the very first plenary meeting of its forty-sixth session, on 17 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations counts just under 10 million people in the city itself, counted for the city proper rather than for the wider built-up area that spreads well beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country around it holds some 51.7 million, so roughly one citizen in five lives inside the capital's own boundary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Republic of Korea (South Korea),” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/korea-south. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Korean War, 1950–1953,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 702 (1991),” adopted at its 3001st meeting, 8 August 1991, S/RES/702(1991), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/125255. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea and the Republic of Korea to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/1, 17 September 1991, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/133631. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Seoul_from_Namsan_3.jpg/1920px-Seoul_from_Namsan_3.jpg","title":"Seoul","desc":"Seoul, seen from a distance.","credit":"kallerna, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seoul_from_Namsan_3.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Seoul."}},
{"id":"gw-066","num":66,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Chile","answerText":"Chile","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1810</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 March 1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Chile"},"facts":[["Capital","Santiago"],["Population","20 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","756,102 km² (291,932 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","chile"],"abstract":"<b>Chile</b> was an audiencia of the Viceroyalty of Peru under Spanish rule, and its distance from the eastern United States shaped the relationship from the start, since ships had to round Cape Horn to reach its main port at Valparaíso.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> French armies invaded Spain in 1808 and captured Ferdinand VII, which set revolutionary movements going across Spain's American colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Revolutionaries here declared independence on 18 September 1810, and several years of fighting followed before the outcome was settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1822 President James Monroe had concluded that Spain could not recover its American colonies, and in March he asked Congress for the money to send ministers to five of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition therefore came through the Senate rather than by proclamation: on 27 January 1823 it confirmed Heman Allen of Vermont as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country has held a United Nations seat since the organisation was founded in 1945, which makes it an original member, thirteen decades after that first American minister was confirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in March 1949, among the earliest Latin American states to do so, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A free trade agreement with the United States signed in 2003 is described in the American record as the landmark of the modern relationship, the two governments having consulted frequently since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 18.0 million in 2015 to 19.9 million, and both international counts give that figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They differ over its area by about 1,140 km², a spread of 0.15% on a country that runs more than 4,000 km from end to end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Chile,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cl.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Chile,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/chile. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Chile and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/chile_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Chile,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Chile,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/Flag_of_Chile.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Chile.svg)","alt":"The flag of Chile: white over red, with a blue square bearing a white star in the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Cuernos_del_Paine_in_Torres_del_Paine_National_Park.jpg/1920px-Cuernos_del_Paine_in_Torres_del_Paine_National_Park.jpg","title":"Torres del Paine National Park, Chile","desc":"Torres del Paine.","credit":"Pedro Szekely from Los Angeles, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuernos_del_Paine_in_Torres_del_Paine_National_Park.jpg","alt":"Jagged peaks lit red at sunrise above a lake."}},
{"id":"gw-067","num":67,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Romania","answerText":"Romania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union of principalities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1878</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1881</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Romania"},"facts":[["Capital","Bucharest"],["Population","19 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","238,398 km² (92,046 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","romania"],"abstract":"<b>Romania</b> came into being as a single state in 1862, when the Ottoman sultan allowed the principalities of Moldavia and Wallachia to unite under that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charles of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen became prince in 1866, the country sided with Russia in the war against the Ottomans of 1877 and 1878, and the treaties of San Stefano and Berlin recognised its independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charles became king in 1881, and the United States recognised the new kingdom on 7 April that year, when the American chargé d'affaires Eugene Schuyler had an audience with him and said so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American relations with the country then swung between warm and cold across the 20th century: it fought on the Allied side in the First World War and declared war on the United States in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations began improving in the 1960s as the government distanced its foreign policy from the Soviet Union's, particularly after the invasion of Czechoslovakia in 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The 1970s brought cultural, scientific and educational exchanges, and the 1980s brought criticism of the country's human rights record that strained the relationship again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since the revolution of December 1989 unseated the communist government the relationship has been a strengthened one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took a United Nations seat on 14 December 1955, in the single batch of admissions that ended a decade of Cold War deadlock, and joined the European Union on 1 January 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in November 1971, unusually early for a state in the Soviet bloc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population is falling rather than rising, from 19.8 million in 2015 to 19.0 million, one of the few countries carded so far to show that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Romania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ro.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Romania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/romania. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Romania and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/romania_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Romania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ROU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/Flag_of_Romania.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Romania.svg)","alt":"The flag of Romania: three vertical bands, blue, yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Castelul_Bran2.jpg/1920px-Castelul_Bran2.jpg","title":"Bran Castle, Romania","desc":"Bran Castle.","credit":"Dobre Cezar, CC BY-SA 3.0 ro, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castelul_Bran2.jpg","alt":"A view of Bran Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-068","num":68,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Somalia","answerText":"Somalia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union and independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 January 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 December 2018</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Somalia"},"facts":[["Capital","Mogadishu"],["Population","20 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","637,657 km² (246,201 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","somalia"],"abstract":"<b>Somalia</b> was made on 1 July 1960 out of two territories with different colonial rulers: a United Nations trust territory administered by Italy and a British protectorate that had become independent five days earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Somali Republic that day, in a congratulatory message from President Dwight Eisenhower to President Aden Abdulla Osman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on the same date, the American consulate general at Mogadiscio, now spelled Mogadishu, being raised to embassy rank with Andrew G. Lynch as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took a United Nations seat on 20 September 1960, the day the General Assembly admitted a run of newly independent African states at a single sitting, Cameroon and Niger among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thirty years later the central government collapsed, and the American embassy closed on 5 January 1991 with all its personnel withdrawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were not severed, however, and that distinction is the point: the United States kept a dialogue going through its embassy in Nairobi, with transitional authorities and then, from 17 January 2013, with a newly recognised central government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy in the capital reopened only on 2 December 2018, under Ambassador Donald Yamamoto, after almost 28 years in which the mission to the country had been run from another one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen by more than two fifths in a decade, from 13.8 million in 2015 to 19.7 million, among the fastest growth of any country carded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country holds the longest mainland coastline in Africa, running from the Gulf of Aden round the Horn and south along the Indian Ocean, and its capital stands on that southern stretch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two international counts now agree on the current figure and differ over the country's area by three square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Somalia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/so.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Somalia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/somalia. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Somalia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SOM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Somalia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SOM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Cameroun to Membership of the United Nations,” resolution 1476 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205969. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of the Niger to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1482 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205975. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Flag_of_Somalia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Somalia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Somalia: a light blue field with a single white five-pointed star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Laas_Geel_rock_paintings.JPG/1920px-Laas_Geel_rock_paintings.JPG","title":"Laas Geel, Somalia","desc":"The rock paintings at Laas Geel.","credit":"Theodor Hoffsten, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laas_Geel_rock_paintings.JPG","alt":"Red and white painted cattle on a rock shelter wall."}},
{"id":"gw-530","num":530,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Khartoum","answerText":"Khartoum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition delivered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 February 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Operations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 May 2002</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sudan","dot":"Khartoum"},"facts":[["Country","Sudan"],["Population","5.7 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Where","the Blue and White Niles meet"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","sudan"],"abstract":"<b>Khartoum</b> is where the United States recognised Sudan, and the recognition was carried across the city by hand: on 1 January 1956 the American liaison officer there, Arthur Beach, delivered a letter to the president of the Supreme Commission of Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country had been under an Anglo-Egyptian condominium until that day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 15 February, when the American liaison office in the city was raised to embassy rank with Beach himself as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy closed on 6 June 1967 after demonstrations and stone-throwing outside it when the Arab-Israeli war of that month broke out, and Sudan severed relations the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American interests section opened inside the Netherlands embassy here on 14 August that year, so the United States kept a presence in the city without having a mission of its own in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were restored on 25 July 1972 when the embassy reopened under George C. Moore, but the following March Ambassador Cleo A. Noel Jr. was assassinated at post in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy suspended operations again on 7 February 1996, and full operations resumed only on 23 May 2002, with the ambassador working from Nairobi in the interval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city stands where the Blue Nile and the White Nile meet, the United Nations counts about 5.7 million people in it, and the country around it took a seat there on 12 November 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That profile records no surface area for the country at all, an omission rare enough to be worth noticing, so its figure of 1,878,000 km² rests on the World Bank alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 40.0 million in 2015 to 51.7 million, and that is the count for what remains after the southern third of the country seceded in 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sudan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sd.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sudan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sudan. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Sudan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SDN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sudan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SDN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The 1967 Arab-Israeli War,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/arab-israeli-war-1967. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Khartoum_panorama-1_-_by_ScubaBeer.jpg","title":"Khartoum","desc":"Khartoum, seen from a distance.","credit":"Chris from Falmouth, UK, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khartoum_panorama-1_-_by_ScubaBeer.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Khartoum."}},
{"id":"gw-069","num":69,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Senegal","answerText":"Senegal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrawal from it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised separately</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Senegal"},"facts":[["Capital","Dakar"],["Population","19 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","196,712 km² (75,951 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","senegal"],"abstract":"<b>Senegal</b> became independent inside a federation rather than on its own: the Federation of Mali, made up of the Republic of Senegal and the Soudanese Republic, took its independence from France on 20 June 1960, and the United States recognised it that day in a message from President Dwight Eisenhower to President Modibo Keita.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The arrangement lasted two months, and on 20 August 1960 Senegal withdrew from the federation and declared its own independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 22 September the National Assembly of the Republic of Soudan legislated that the other half would become the Republic of Mali, so one recognised state had become two unrecognised ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both were recognised on the same day, 24 September 1960, and the record shows how a single diplomatic mission is divided in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American embassy at Dakar, accredited until then to the federation, informed the Senegalese foreign minister of the recognition and was reaccredited to Senegal under Roswell D. McClelland, while the consulate general at Bamako was raised to embassy rank for Mali.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Four days later the General Assembly admitted the republic by resolution 1490 (XV), adopted at its 876th plenary meeting on 28 September 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a contracting party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 27 September 1963 and a founding member of the World Trade Organization when that body replaced the agreement on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by close to a third in the decade to 2025, from 14.6 million to 18.9 million, and roughly one person in six now lives in the capital, whose urban area is put at 3.06 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The two international counts of its territory differ by two square kilometres, 196,712 against 196,710, a gap of one part in a hundred thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The two halves of the vanished federation were admitted in consecutive resolutions at that one meeting, Senegal as 1490 and Mali as 1491, so a union of two ended as two seats side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Senegal,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Senegal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/senegal. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mali,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mali. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Senegal to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1490 (XV), adopted at the 876th plenary meeting, 28 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206140. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Senegal and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/senegal_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Senegal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SEN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Senegal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SEN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Mali to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1491 (XV), adopted at the 876th plenary meeting, 28 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206141. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Flag_of_Senegal.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Senegal.svg)","alt":"The flag of Senegal: three vertical bands of green, yellow and red with a green five-pointed star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/RetbaLakeShore.jpg/1920px-RetbaLakeShore.jpg","title":"Lake Retba, Senegal","desc":"Lake Retba.","credit":"Jjm2311, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RetbaLakeShore.jpg","alt":"A view of Lake Retba."}},
{"id":"gw-070","num":70,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guatemala","answerText":"Guatemala","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence from Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 August 1824</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised separately</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 April 1844</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 August 1882</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guatemala"},"facts":[["Capital","Guatemala City"],["Population","19 million (2025)"],["Region","Central America"],["Area","108,889 km² (42,042 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","guatemala"],"abstract":"<b>Guatemala</b> was recognised by the United States twice, twenty years apart, having first appeared in the world as part of something larger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After independence from Spain in 1821 it joined the Federation of Central American States in 1823 with Honduras, Nicaragua, Costa Rica and El Salvador, and President James Monroe received the federation's envoy Antonio José Cañaz on 4 August 1824.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That reception both recognised the federation and opened relations, and chargé d'affaires John Williams presented credentials to it on 3 May 1826.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independent Guatemala was recognised on 5 April 1844 by an exequatur issued to its consul-general Antonio de Aycinena, and relations began when chargé d'affaires Elijah Hise presented credentials in January 1849.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy on 10 August 1882, when Henry C. Hall presented credentials as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The sharpest episode is set out in the American government's own guide: in June 1954 the Central Intelligence Agency, concerned about the threat of communism, assisted in the overthrow of President Jacobo Arbenz Guzmán, who had been elected in November 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A five-member junta under Colonel Elfego Monzón took power on 2 July under a pact concluded at San Salvador, which the State Department judged non-communist, popular and in control of the whole territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Secretary of State John Foster Dulles instructed the embassy to resume relations on 12 July, and Ambassador John Peurifoy informed the foreign minister the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That it can be described so openly is recent: the official documentary volume of 1983 left the covert operation out after references to it were denied in declassification, and a supplement of 287 documents appeared only in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was a founding member of the United Nations on 21 November 1945, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 10 October 1991 and to the World Trade Organization on 21 July 1995, and the two international counts of its people and its land agree to within 119 people and one square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Guatemala,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gt.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guatemala,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guatemala. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Guatemala and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/guatemala_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guatemala,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GTM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Guatemala,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GTM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","Susan Holly, ed., “Preface,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1952–1954, Guatemala</i> (Washington, DC: U.S. Government Printing Office, 2003), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54Guat/preface. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Flag_of_Guatemala.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Guatemala.svg)","alt":"The flag of Guatemala: three vertical bands of sky blue, white and sky blue, with the national coat of arms on the white band."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Tikal_3.jpg/1920px-Tikal_3.jpg","title":"Tikal, Guatemala","desc":"Tikal.","credit":"Mundo Maya, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tikal_3.jpg","alt":"A view of Tikal."}},
{"id":"gw-071","num":71,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ecuador","answerText":"Ecuador","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised within Colombia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 June 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left that federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised separately</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 June 1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 December 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ecuador"},"facts":[["Capital","Quito"],["Population","18 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","257,217 km² (99,312 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","ecuador"],"abstract":"<b>Ecuador</b> was recognised by the United States in a letter that never used the word, and that letter is the nearest thing the relationship has to a founding charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its territory was first recognised as part of Colombia on 19 June 1822, when President James Monroe received Manuel Torres as Colombian chargé d'affaires, and it left that federation in 1830.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 13 January 1832 President Juan José Flores wrote to announce that he was 'at the head of the Government of the State of the Equator', and Secretary of State Edward Livingston replied on 4 June at the direction of President Andrew Jackson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livingston acknowledged the letter, thanked Flores for assuring that American citizens would keep the rights guaranteed them under an existing treaty with Colombia, and passed on Jackson's hope that Ecuador, New Granada and Venezuela might reunite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The State Department treats that reply as an effective recognition of the separation, which is why the date given is 1832 rather than that of any declaration made in Quito.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Two further acts confirmed it: instructions of 12 May 1834 sending Seth Sweetser's commission as consul at Guayaquil called that city one 'in Ecuador' and told him to seek an exequatur from that state's government, granted on 24 February 1835.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In April 1836 Secretary of State John Forsyth authorised the chargé in New Granada to negotiate with the Ecuadorian minister expected at Bogotá, and a treaty of peace, friendship, navigation and commerce followed in 1839.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first resident American diplomatic agent reached Quito in 1848, sixteen years after that letter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its United Nations membership is dated 21 December 1945, and it never joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade at all, acceding straight to the World Trade Organization on 21 January 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population rose by an eighth in the decade to 2025, from 16.3 million to 18.3 million, and the two international counts of its area differ by 847 square kilometres, a third of one per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ecuador,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ec.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ecuador,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ecuador. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Ecuador and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/ecuador_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Ecuador,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ECU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Ecuador,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ECU/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/Flag_of_Ecuador.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ecuador.svg)","alt":"The flag of Ecuador: horizontal bands of yellow, blue and red, the yellow band double width, with the national coat of arms in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Lobo_marino_%28Zalophus_californianus_wollebaeki%29%2C_Punta_Pitt%2C_isla_de_San_Crist%C3%B3bal%2C_islas_Gal%C3%A1pagos%2C_Ecuador%2C_2015-07-24%2C_DD_11.JPG/1920px-Lobo_marino_%28Zalophus_californianus_wollebaeki%29%2C_Punta_Pitt%2C_isla_de_San_Crist%C3%B3bal%2C_islas_Gal%C3%A1pagos%2C_Ecuador%2C_2015-07-24%2C_DD_11.JPG","title":"Galápagos Islands, Ecuador","desc":"Galápagos Islands.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobo_marino_(Zalophus_californianus_wollebaeki),_Punta_Pitt,_isla_de_San_Crist%C3%B3bal,_islas_Gal%C3%A1pagos,_Ecuador,_2015-07-24,_DD_11.JPG","alt":"A view of Galápagos Islands."}},
{"id":"gw-531","num":531,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kampala","answerText":"Kampala","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 October 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 November 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy re-established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 June 1979</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uganda","dot":"Kampala"},"facts":[["Country","Uganda"],["Population","3.1 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Where","on hills just north of Lake Victoria"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","uganda"],"abstract":"<b>Kampala</b> is where relations between Uganda and the United States were opened, broken off in practice and opened again, all within seventeen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the country on 9 October 1962, in a congratulatory message from President John F. Kennedy to Prime Minister A. Milton Obote, Uganda having previously been under British sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began the same day, when the American consulate general in the city was raised to embassy status with Olcott H. Deming as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted Uganda sixteen days later, by resolution 1758 (XVII), adopted at its 1158th plenary meeting on 25 October 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country had joined the Commonwealth that year on independence from Britain, and had become a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 23 October, two days before it took its United Nations seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The embassy here closed on 10 November 1973, when all American diplomatic personnel were withdrawn from the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The State Department later named four reasons: persistent internal security problems, operating difficulties for American programmes and personnel, repeated public threats against embassy officials and other Americans by high Ugandan officials, and finally the abrupt expulsion of the Marine security guard that protected the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations themselves were not interrupted, Uganda kept its own embassy in Washington, and American interests here were represented in the meantime by the Federal Republic of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy was re-established on 18 June 1979 with David Halstead as chargé d'affaires ad interim, more than five years after the withdrawal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has grown with the country, whose recorded population rose by more than a third in the decade to 2025, from 37.5 million to 51.4 million, while the capital's own urban area is now put at 3.14 million, about one Ugandan in sixteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Uganda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ug.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uganda,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uganda. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Uganda to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1758 (XVII), adopted at the 1158th plenary meeting, 25 October 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/204481. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Uganda and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/uganda_e.htm. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Uganda,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/uganda. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Uganda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/UGA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Skyline_of_Kampala.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Kampala.jpg","title":"Kampala","desc":"Kampala, seen from a distance.","credit":"Flöschen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Kampala.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kampala."}},
{"id":"gw-072","num":72,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Netherlands","answerText":"Netherlands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1782</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1814</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 May 1942</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Netherlands"},"facts":[["Capital","Amsterdam"],["Population","18 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Area","41,543 km² (16,040 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","netherlands"],"abstract":"The <b>Netherlands</b> was the second country in the world to recognise the United States, and it did so while both were at war with the same enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1779 the Continental Congress commissioned Henry Laurens as minister to negotiate a treaty and a loan, and the Royal Navy captured him on the way to Europe carrying a draft of it, after which Britain declared war on the Dutch republic in 1780.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came on 19 April 1782, when The Hague accepted the credentials of Minister John Adams, and diplomatic relations and the American legation there began the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The relationship then broke for thirteen years: the legation closed in May 1801, ostensibly to reduce expenditure but in truth because Dutch foreign policy was by then run from Paris, and the country was annexed outright to the French Empire in 1810.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Full sovereignty returned with Napoleon's defeat, and the two exchanged ministers again in 1814 and 1815.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The second interruption was the German invasion, after which the legation closed on 15 July 1940 and reopened in London a month later, beside the Dutch government in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was there, on 8 May 1942, that the mission was raised to embassy rank, Anthony J. Drexel Biddle Jr. presenting his new credentials as ambassador to a government sitting in another country's capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has been a member of the United Nations since 10 December 1945 and was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 January 1948, becoming a founding member of the World Trade Organization when that replaced the agreement on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two international counts of its total area agree to within three square kilometres, at 41,543 and 41,540.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The European Union publishes a third and smaller figure, 37,391 square kilometres, which is the land alone: a tenth of the country as the other two count it is water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Netherlands (Kingdom of the),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nl.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Netherlands,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/netherlands. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The Netherlands and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/netherlands_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Netherlands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NLD/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Netherlands – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/netherlands_en?etrans=en. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Netherlands: three horizontal bands of red, white and blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/KinderdijkMolens02.jpg","title":"Kinderdijk, the Netherlands","desc":"Kinderdijk.","credit":"Lucas Hirschegger, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KinderdijkMolens02.jpg","alt":"A view of Kinderdijk."}},
{"id":"gw-073","num":73,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cambodia","answerText":"Cambodia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 May 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 April 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cambodia"},"facts":[["Capital","Phnom Penh"],["Population","18 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","181,035 km² (69,898 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","cambodia"],"abstract":"<b>Cambodia</b>'s relations with the United States were opened, severed, reopened, evacuated and opened again, five turns in 44 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came on 7 February 1950, by a Department of State announcement, the kingdom having been a French protectorate within Indochina and an independent state inside the French Union since 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were established on 11 July 1950 when Donald R. Heath presented credentials, though he was resident at Saigon, and a legation opened at Phnom Penh on 14 November under Don V. Catlett.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation was raised to embassy rank on 24 June 1952, and the first ambassador actually resident in the capital, Robert M. McClintock, presented credentials only on 2 October 1954.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cambodia severed relations on 3 May 1965, holding the United States responsible for cross-border air attacks by South Vietnam that had killed Cambodian citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were re-established on 2 July 1969 and the embassy reopened on 16 August, only for it to close on 12 April 1975 as all American personnel were evacuated amid the fighting between government forces and communist insurgents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Five days later President Gerald Ford acknowledged the fall of the Khmer Republic, and Secretary of State Henry Kissinger explained that the new government had refused to establish relations with any country at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations then ran the country for a time: the Security Council established a Transitional Authority in Cambodia by resolution 745 (1992), adopted at its 3057th meeting on 28 February 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American mission returned on 11 November 1991 under Charles Twining, and the embassy reopened with normal relations resumed when he presented credentials as ambassador on 17 May 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955 and reached the trading system very late, acceding to the World Trade Organization only on 13 October 2004 without ever having been a party to the agreement before it, and the two international counts of its area differ by five square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cambodia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kh.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cambodia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cambodia. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 745 (1992),” adopted at its 3057th meeting, 28 February 1992, S/RES/745(1992), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/138379. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cambodia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cambodia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Cambodia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KHM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Flag_of_Cambodia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cambodia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Cambodia: horizontal bands of blue, red and blue with a white outline of Angkor Wat in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Angkor_Wat.jpg/1920px-Angkor_Wat.jpg","title":"Angkor Wat, Cambodia","desc":"Angkor Wat.","credit":"Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Wat.jpg","alt":"A view of Angkor Wat."}},
{"id":"gw-074","num":74,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Zimbabwe","answerText":"Zimbabwe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Unilateral declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council response</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 April 1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 August 1980</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Zimbabwe"},"facts":[["Capital","Harare"],["Population","17 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","390,757 km² (150,872 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","zimbabwe"],"abstract":"<b>Zimbabwe</b> is a country the United States recognised on the day it came into being, having spent the previous fifteen years refusing to recognise the state that occupied the same ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The territory had been under British sovereignty as Southern Rhodesia, and in 1965 its minority white government issued a unilateral declaration of independence under the name Rhodesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Security Council responded at its 1258th meeting on 12 November 1965, calling on all states not to recognise the minority régime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American guide states the position flatly: the United States never recognised the independence of Rhodesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition of the Republic of Zimbabwe came instead on 18 April 1980, the day independence was attained, when the American embassy at Salisbury, now Harare, was established with Jeffrey Davidow as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomatic relations date from that same act, and the State Department records that the United States was the first country to establish an embassy in the new state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Admission to the United Nations followed on 25 August 1980, by a resolution adopted at the first plenary meeting of the eleventh special session of the General Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its place in the trading system is older than the country: the territory was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 11 July 1948, and Zimbabwe joined the World Trade Organization on 5 March 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen by nearly a fifth in the decade to 2025, from 14.4 million to 17.0 million, while the capital's own urban area is put at just over 1.5 million, fewer than one Zimbabwean in eleven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The two international counts of its area agree to within three square kilometres, at 390,757 and 390,760.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Zimbabwe,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/zw.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Zimbabwe,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/zimbabwe. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 216 (1965),” adopted at its 1258th meeting, 12 November 1965, S/RES/216(1965), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/90483. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Zimbabwe to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution S-11/1, adopted at the 1st plenary meeting, 25 August 1980, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/13180. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Zimbabwe and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/zimbabwe_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Zimbabwe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ZWE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Zimbabwe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ZWE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Flag_of_Zimbabwe.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Zimbabwe.svg)","alt":"The flag of Zimbabwe: seven horizontal stripes of green, gold, red and black, with a white triangle at the hoist bearing a red star and the Zimbabwe Bird."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_%2833736918448%29.jpg/1920px-Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_%2833736918448%29.jpg","title":"Great Zimbabwe, Zimbabwe","desc":"Great Zimbabwe.","credit":"Andrew Moore from Johannesburg, South Africa, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_(33736918448).jpg","alt":"A view of Great Zimbabwe."}},
{"id":"gw-532","num":532,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Madrid","answerText":"Madrid","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 February 1783</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 June 1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1913</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Spain","dot":"Madrid"},"facts":[["Country","Spain"],["Population","6.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Where","on the Meseta, near the centre of Iberia"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","spain"],"abstract":"<b>Madrid</b> spent four years declining to receive an American envoy before it recognised the country he came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Continental Congress appointed John Jay minister plenipotentiary on 29 September 1779 and sent him to the Spanish court, where he spent two years and was never formally received, Spain being unwilling to risk relations with Britain while the war's outcome was open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came only once it was clear Britain and the United States would sign a treaty ending that war, and it took the form of a reception: on 20 February 1783 the city received William Carmichael as chargé d'affaires, though he had been in Spain since May 1782.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American record dates the recognition, the establishment of diplomatic relations and the founding of the legation here all to that one act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spain sent its own chargé d'affaires, Diego Gardoqui, to the United States in June 1785, and the two signed a Treaty of Friendship, Limits, and Navigation on 27 October 1795.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations have been broken exactly once since, and for a year: Spain severed them on 21 April 1898 and the American minister Stewart Woodford closed the legation that day, four days before Congress approved a declaration of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bellamy Storer was appointed minister on 12 April 1899 and presented credentials that June, and the legation was raised to embassy rank when Joseph Willard presented his in October 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The civil war moved the mission out of the capital altogether, first to the American consulate at Barcelona, open since 1797, then across the border to Saint-Jean-de-Luz in France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It returned to Spain on 13 April 1939, not here but at Burgos, where H. Freeman Matthews was received as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spain joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955 under one resolution headed <i>Admission of New Members</i> that took in several states at once, has been a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade since 1963, and has belonged to the European Union since 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Spain,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/es.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Spain,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/spain. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Spain and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/spain_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Spain – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/spain_en?etrans=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Madrid-skyline-251210-1.jpg/1920px-Madrid-skyline-251210-1.jpg","title":"Madrid","desc":"Madrid, seen from a distance.","credit":"FDV, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madrid-skyline-251210-1.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Madrid."}},
{"id":"gw-075","num":75,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guinea","answerText":"Guinea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1958</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1958</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 February 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 October 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guinea"},"facts":[["Capital","Conakry"],["Population","15 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","245,857 km² (94,926 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","guinea"],"abstract":"<b>Guinea</b> took its United Nations seat six weeks after the United States recognised it, and two months before the American embassy in its capital had opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It had been under French sovereignty as part of French West Africa, and the United States recognised the Republic of Guinea on 1 November 1958, in a letter to that effect from President Dwight Eisenhower to Prime Minister Sékou Touré.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations came rather later than the recognition, on 13 February 1959, when the American embassy at Conakry was established with Robert W. Rinden as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Security Council recommended admission to the United Nations at its 842nd meeting on 9 December 1958, and the General Assembly acted on that recommendation three days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The admitting resolution, 1325 (XIII), was adopted at the Assembly's 789th plenary meeting on 12 December 1958, while relations with the United States were still to be established, which happened on 13 February 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its place in the trading system runs the other way about: it came to the old General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade very late indeed, acceding on 8 December 1994, three weeks before the World Trade Organization was founded to replace it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It then joined that new body on 25 October 1995, in its first year of operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by more than a quarter in the decade to 2025, from 11.8 million to 15.1 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About one person in eight lives in the capital, whose urban area is put at 1.89 million, and the country is filed by the United Nations under Western Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two international counts of its area differ by 24 square kilometres, one part in ten thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Guinea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guinea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guinea. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 131 (1958),” adopted at its 842nd meeting, 9 December 1958, S/RES/131(1958), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/112103. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Guinea to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1325 (XIII), adopted at the 789th plenary meeting, 12 December 1958, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207051. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Guinea and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/guinea_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GIN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GIN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Flag_of_Guinea.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Guinea.svg)","alt":"The flag of Guinea: three vertical bands of red, yellow and green."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Mount_Nimba_Strict_Nature_Reserve-108445.jpg/1920px-Mount_Nimba_Strict_Nature_Reserve-108445.jpg","title":"Mount Nimba, Guinea","desc":"Mount Nimba.","credit":"Guy Debonnet, CC BY-SA 3.0 igo, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Nimba_Strict_Nature_Reserve-108445.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Nimba."}},
{"id":"gw-076","num":76,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Benin","answerText":"Benin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 February 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Benin"},"facts":[["Capital","Porto-Novo"],["Population","15 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","114,763 km² (44,310 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","benin"],"abstract":"<b>Benin</b> was recognised under a different name, and the American record still carries that name: the United States recognised the Republic of Dahomey on 1 August 1960, in a congratulatory message from President Dwight Eisenhower to Prime Minister Maga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence from French sovereignty was proclaimed that same day, and the country was renamed the Republic of Benin in 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on 1 August as well, when Donald R. Norland took up his post as chargé d'affaires, having presented his credentials the day before to take effect on the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy itself was established six months later, on 15 February 1961, under Converse Hettinger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was placed not in the official capital but at Cotonou, where the government actually sits, and the guide records no American mission at Porto-Novo at any point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted the country on 20 September 1960 by resolution 1481 (XV), adopted at its 864th plenary meeting, one of a run of consecutive resolutions seating newly independent African states that day, Niger's being the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It came to the trading system early and to its successor late, becoming a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 12 September 1963 and a member of the World Trade Organization only on 22 February 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by 30 per cent in the decade to 2025, from 11.4 million to 14.8 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The official capital is small by comparison, its urban area put at 285,000 people, about one person in 52, so the seat of government and the largest city are the same place and the capital is neither.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two international counts of its area differ by three square kilometres, at 114,763 and 114,760, which is one part in forty thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Benin,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bj.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Benin,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/benin. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Dahomey to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1481 (XV), adopted at the 864th plenary meeting, 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205974. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Benin and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/benin_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Benin,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BEN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Benin,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BEN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of the Niger to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1482 (XV), 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205975. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Flag_of_Benin.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Benin.svg)","alt":"The flag of Benin: a green vertical band at the hoist beside horizontal bands of yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Ganvi%C3%A9_fishing_village_on_stilts_in_Benin_%2810282059623%29_%282%29.jpg/1920px-Ganvi%C3%A9_fishing_village_on_stilts_in_Benin_%2810282059623%29_%282%29.jpg","title":"Ganvie, Benin","desc":"Ganvie.","credit":"jbdodane, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganvi%C3%A9_fishing_village_on_stilts_in_Benin_(10282059623)_(2).jpg","alt":"A view of Ganvie."}},
{"id":"gw-077","num":77,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Rwanda","answerText":"Rwanda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Message dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Rwanda"},"facts":[["Capital","Kigali"],["Population","15 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","26,338 km² (10,169 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","rwanda"],"abstract":"<b>Rwanda</b> is one of the countries the United States recognised by a letter written before the event it recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President John F. Kennedy's congratulatory message to President Grégoire Kayibanda is dated 28 June 1962, and the recognition it carried took effect on 1 July, the day independence was attained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The territory had been administered by Belgium as part of the trust territory of Ruanda-Urundi, a single United Nations trusteeship that became two countries on the same date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 1 July 1962, when the American embassy at Kigali was set up with David J. S. Manbey as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its entry in the American guide then records nothing further at all: no closure, no severance, no evacuation and no interruption in the 63 years since, which is a short entry for a good reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted the country by resolution 1748 (XVII), adopted at its 1122nd plenary meeting on 18 September 1962, eleven weeks after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It became a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 1 January 1966 and a member of the World Trade Organization on 22 May 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen by a quarter in the decade to 2025, from 11.6 million to 14.6 million, in a country of 26,338 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two international counts of that area agree exactly, to the square kilometre, which they seldom do: the World Bank rounds larger countries to the nearest ten, so a figure ending in eight is a country small enough to be given whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About one Rwandan in thirteen lives in the capital, whose urban area is put at 1.09 million, and the country is filed by the United Nations under Eastern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Rwanda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/rw.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Rwanda,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/rwanda. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Rwanda to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1748 (XVII), adopted at the 1122nd plenary meeting, 18 September 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/204474. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Rwanda and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/rwanda_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Rwanda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/RWA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Rwanda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/RWA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Flag_of_Rwanda.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rwanda.svg)","alt":"The flag of Rwanda: horizontal bands of sky blue, yellow and green, with a golden sun in the upper right."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Volcanoes_National_Park_Banner_Image.gif","title":"Volcanoes National Park, Rwanda","desc":"Volcanoes National Park.","credit":"Volcanoes National Park, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volcanoes_National_Park_Banner_Image.gif","alt":"A view of Volcanoes National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-533","num":533,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Algiers","answerText":"Algiers","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty of peace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1795</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 March 1796</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 June 1967</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Algeria","dot":"Algiers"},"facts":[["Country","Algeria"],["Population","2.7 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Where","on the Mediterranean, below the Atlas"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","algeria"],"abstract":"<b>Algiers</b> recognised the United States 167 years before the United States recognised the country it is now the capital of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At American independence it was a nominal vassal of the Ottoman Empire with the power to conduct its own foreign relations, and in 1785 its dey declared war on the new republic and captured several of its ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Britain had been quick to tell the Barbary states that American vessels no longer had its navy's protection, and the Confederation government could raise neither a fleet nor the tribute that would buy peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Recognition came by treaty: the Treaty of Peace and Amity was signed here on 5 September 1795, its original in Turkish, its first article promising peace between the citizens of the United States and Hassan Bashaw, dey of Algiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It freed 83 American captives and committed the United States to paying tribute, and the Senate consented to it the following March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> American consular representation began on 4 March 1796 when Joel Barlow presented credentials to the dey, John Paul Jones having been appointed in 1792 and died before reaching the post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That presence was interrupted from July 1812 to July 1815 by the second Barbary war, and ended when France conquered the city in 1830, an appointee choosing not to take up his post at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> France incorporated the coast into itself in 1848 as the departments of Algiers, Oran and Constantine, and self-rule returned only when Algerians voted for independence on 1 July 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the country two days later, and the consulate general here was raised to embassy rank on 29 September 1962 with William J. Porter as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were severed again on 6 June 1967 after that month's Arab-Israeli war, American interests passing to an interests section in the Swiss embassy, and both embassies reopened on 12 November 1974, twelve years after the country took the United Nations seat granted it by resolution 1754 (XVII).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Algeria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Algeria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/algeria. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Barbary Wars, 1801–1805 and 1815–1816,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/barbary-wars. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace and Amity, Signed at Algiers September 5, 1795,” The Barbary Treaties 1786–1816, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/bar1795t.asp. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Democratic and Popular Republic of Algeria to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1754 (XVII), adopted at the 1146th plenary meeting, 8 October 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/204478. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/The_Bay_of_Algiers.jpg/1920px-The_Bay_of_Algiers.jpg","title":"Algiers","desc":"Algiers, seen from a distance.","credit":"Manini row, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bay_of_Algiers.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Algiers."}},
{"id":"gw-078","num":78,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Burundi","answerText":"Burundi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Message dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy restored</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1963</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Burundi"},"facts":[["Capital","Gitega"],["Population","14 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","27,834 km² (10,747 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","burundi"],"abstract":"<b>Burundi</b> and its northern neighbour were one United Nations trusteeship until the day they became two countries, and the record of the separation is unusually exact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Kingdom of Burundi on 1 July 1962, the day the trusteeship over Ruanda-Urundi, administered by Belgium, was ended, with President John F. Kennedy's letter to Mwami Mwambutsa IV sent three days beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Embassy Usumbura, in the city now called Bujumbura, was established the same day under Herbert V. Olds as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its rank then went down before it went up: the mission became a legation on 15 December 1962 and an embassy again only on 16 September 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guide offers no reason for the demotion and records nothing at all after the restoration, so those fifteen months are the whole of the movement in 63 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly seated both halves of the old trusteeship at one sitting, its 1122nd plenary meeting on 18 September 1962, in consecutive resolutions: Rwanda as 1748 and this country as 1749.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 13 March 1965 and a member of the World Trade Organization on 23 July 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by 30 per cent in the decade to 2025, from 11.0 million to 14.4 million, in a country of 27,834 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That works out at 554 people to the square kilometre, and the political capital is small against it, its urban area put at about 899,000 people, roughly one Burundian in sixteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two international counts of the area differ by four square kilometres, at 27,834 and 27,830, one part in seven thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Burundi,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bi.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burundi,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burundi. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Kingdom of Burundi to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1749 (XVII), adopted at the 1122nd plenary meeting, 18 September 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/204475. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Rwanda to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1748 (XVII), adopted at the 1122nd plenary meeting, 18 September 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/204474. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Burundi and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/burundi_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Burundi,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BDI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Burundi,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BDI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Flag_of_Burundi.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burundi.svg)","alt":"The flag of Burundi: a white saltire dividing red and green fields, with a white disc at the centre bearing three red stars."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Lake_Tanganyika.jpg/1920px-Lake_Tanganyika.jpg","title":"Lake Tanganyika, Burundi","desc":"Lake Tanganyika.","credit":"Orrling, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Tanganyika.jpg","alt":"A long lake with hills along the far shore."}},
{"id":"gw-079","num":79,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bolivia","answerText":"Bolivia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised in confederation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 March 1837</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised separately</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 May 1848</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1942</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bolivia"},"facts":[["Capital","Sucre"],["Population","12.6 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","1,098,581 km² (424,164 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","bolivia"],"abstract":"<b>Bolivia</b> was recognised twice over, and the first recognition was of a country that no longer exists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It declared independence from Spain on 6 August 1825, but the United States first recognised it only indirectly, as part of the Peru-Bolivian Confederation, when James B. Thornton was appointed chargé d'affaires on 16 March 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thornton had been commissioned to Peru and was received by the confederation instead, which dissolved two years later, in 1839.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition of the republic in its own right waited another nine years, until 30 May 1848, when the Department of State appointed John Appleton chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 23 years therefore separate the declaration of 1825 from the recognition of 1848, and the guide dates the relationship from the later of the two, not the earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Appleton presented his credentials on 3 January 1849, and the American legation opened at the seat of government, La Paz, on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy by an arrangement announced jointly on 4 January 1942 and effective on 31 March, when the Bolivian ambassador presented his letters in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was a founding member of the United Nations from 14 November 1945 but came to the trading system very late, acceding to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade only on 8 September 1990 and to the World Trade Organization on 12 September 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by about a seventh in the decade to 2025, from 11.0 million to 12.6 million, spread across 1,098,581 square kilometres, on which the two international counts differ by a single square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The constitutional capital named by the United Nations is not the seat of government and is much the smaller of the two, its urban area put at about 278,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bolivia (Plurinational State of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bo.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bolivia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bolivia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Bolivia, Plurinational State of and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/bolivia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bolivia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BOL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Bolivia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BOL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Flag_of_Bolivia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bolivia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Bolivia: three horizontal bands of red, yellow and green."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Vista_del_Salar_de_Uyuni.JPG/1920px-Vista_del_Salar_de_Uyuni.JPG","title":"Salar de Uyuni, Bolivia","desc":"The Salar de Uyuni.","credit":"Ximena Medina Sancho, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_del_Salar_de_Uyuni.JPG","alt":"A white salt flat running to the horizon under a deep blue sky."}},
{"id":"gw-080","num":80,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tunisia","answerText":"Tunisia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-1795</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty of Peace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 28 August 1797</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 March 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tunisia"},"facts":[["Capital","Tunis"],["Population","12 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Area","163,610 km² (63,170 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","tunisia"],"abstract":"<b>Tunisia</b> recognised the United States in 1795 and was itself recognised by the United States 161 years later, and the American consulate in its capital outlasted the whole interval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At American independence Tunis was nominally dependent on the Ottoman Empire but able to conduct its own foreign policy and conclude treaties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its recognition cannot be dated more closely than mid-1795, when the Tunisian authorities accepted the appointment of an American consular representative, and the guide says so rather than inventing a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The man holding American consular authority over all the Barbary states, Joseph Donaldson, was named consul for Tunis on 28 March 1795 but stayed at his post in Algiers, so a French merchant in Tunis, Joseph Étienne Famin, was deputised to act instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Famin arranged a truce that took effect on 15 June 1796 and then negotiated the Treaty of Peace and Friendship with the bey, signed on or about 28 August 1797.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> French control arrived by two treaties, the Treaty of Bardo of 12 May 1881 and the La Marsa Convention of 8 June 1883, the second of which stated explicitly that the country was a French protectorate and ended its power to conduct diplomacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence was recognised on 22 March 1956, when the American consul general in Tunis, Morris N. Hughes, sent a congratulatory message to the bey and the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consulate general there had been in place, with interruptions, since the 1790s, and was raised to embassy rank on 5 June 1956, a status recognised the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country on 12 November 1956, by resolution 1112 (XI) adopted at its 574th plenary meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It came to the trading system late, joining the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 19 August 1990 and the World Trade Organization on 29 March 1995, and its recorded population has grown by only 8 per cent in the decade to 2025, from 11.4 million to 12.3 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Tunisia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tunisia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tunisia. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Tunisia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1112 (XI), adopted at the 574th plenary meeting, 12 November 1956, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207994. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tunisia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tunisia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Tunisia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TUN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Flag_of_Tunisia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tunisia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Tunisia: a red field with a white disc at the centre bearing a red crescent and five-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/The_Amphitheatre_of_El_Jem%2C_built_around_AD_238_in_Thysdrus_in_Africa_Proconsularis%2C_the_estimated_capacity_is_35%2C000%2C_Tunisia_-_52717762494.jpg/1920px-The_Amphitheatre_of_El_Jem%2C_built_around_AD_238_in_Thysdrus_in_Africa_Proconsularis%2C_the_estimated_capacity_is_35%2C000%2C_Tunisia_-_52717762494.jpg","title":"Amphitheatre of El Jem, Tunisia","desc":"Amphitheatre of El Jem.","credit":"Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Amphitheatre_of_El_Jem,_built_around_AD_238_in_Thysdrus_in_Africa_Proconsularis,_the_estimated_capacity_is_35,000,_Tunisia_-_52717762494.jpg","alt":"A view of Amphitheatre of El Jem."}},
{"id":"gw-534","num":534,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Baghdad","answerText":"Baghdad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 January 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 March 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iraq","dot":"Baghdad"},"facts":[["Country","Iraq"],["Population","7.0 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Where","on the Tigris, above its junction with the Euphrates"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","iraq"],"abstract":"<b>Baghdad</b> had an American consul before the country around it existed: John Henry Haynes was appointed on 10 August 1888, when the city was Ottoman, and presented his credentials here on 8 January 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Iraq on 9 January 1930, when its ambassador in London signed the Anglo-American-Iraqi Convention, whose preamble states that the United States recognises Iraq as an independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same treaty acknowledged the special relations between Britain and Iraq, then a mandate, and Iraq ended that status in 1932, taking a founding United Nations seat in December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation here opened on 30 March 1931 under Alexander K. Sloan, who was already the consul, and was raised to embassy rank on 28 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One curiosity of the record: when Iraq and Jordan formed an Arab Union in May 1958 the United States recognised it but established no relations with it, relations with each kingdom continuing unbroken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The union was dissolved after a coup here on 14 July 1958, and Iraq severed relations with the United States on 7 June 1967 in the wake of that month's Arab-Israeli war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American interests section opened in the Belgian embassy here on 1 October 1972 and was raised to an embassy again on 26 November 1984, by agreement between President Ronald Reagan and Foreign Minister Tariq Aziz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It closed on 12 January 1991 after the invasion of Kuwait that the Security Council had condemned at its 2932nd meeting on 2 August 1990, and Iraq severed relations again that February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Coalition forces invaded on 20 March 2003 and seized the city on 9 April, the Coalition Provisional Authority followed on 12 May, and it transferred sovereignty to an interim government on 28 June 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy reopened inside the Green Zone that same day, twenty days after the Security Council endorsed the incoming government by resolution 1546 (2004), in a country now counted at 47 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Iraq,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/iq.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iraq,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iraq. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1546 (2004),” adopted at its 4987th meeting, 8 June 2004, S/RES/1546(2004), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/523025. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 660 (1990),” adopted at its 2932nd meeting, 2 August 1990, S/RES/660(1990), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/94220. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Iraq,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IRQ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Tigris_River_in_Baghdad_%282016%29.jpg/1920px-Tigris_River_in_Baghdad_%282016%29.jpg","title":"Baghdad","desc":"Baghdad, seen from a distance.","credit":"Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff from Washington D.C, United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tigris_River_in_Baghdad_(2016).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Baghdad."}},
{"id":"gw-081","num":81,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"South Sudan","answerText":"South Sudan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">January 2011</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 2011</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council recommendation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 July 2011</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 2011</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"S. Sudan"},"facts":[["Capital","Juba"],["Population","12 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","646,883 km² (249,762 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","south sudan"],"abstract":"<b>South Sudan</b> went from a declaration of independence to a United Nations seat in five days: it declared on 9 July 2011 and was admitted on the 14th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country declared independence on 9 July 2011, and President Barack Obama declared the same day that the United States formally recognised it as a sovereign and independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That followed the referendum on self-determination held in January 2011, which the State Department describes as the full implementation of the Comprehensive Peace Agreement signed between north and south in 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American mission was already in place: a consulate had been opened at Juba on 11 November 2005, after that agreement, and it was upgraded to an embassy on the day of independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Barrie Walkley, the consul general, served as chargé d'affaires until Susan D. Page was confirmed as ambassador designate on 18 October 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Security Council recommended admission at its 6582nd meeting on 13 July 2011, and the General Assembly admitted the country the next day, adopting resolution 65/308 without a vote at its 108th plenary meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its United Nations profile carries no surface area field at all, and neither does the profile of Sudan, the country it separated from, so the two halves of the old state are alike in that omission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The area given here therefore rests on a single institutional source, the World Bank, which puts it at 646,883 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by about a tenth in the decade to 2025, from 11.1 million to 12.2 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only about one South Sudanese in 32 lives in the capital, whose urban area is put at 386,000 people, so this is a country whose largest city holds barely three per cent of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “South Sudan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ss.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Sudan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-sudan. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1999 (2011),” adopted at its 6582nd meeting, 13 July 2011, S/RES/1999(2011), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/706967. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of South Sudan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 65/308, adopted without a vote at the 108th plenary meeting, 14 July 2011, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/710041. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): South Sudan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SSD/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: South Sudan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SSD/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/Flag_of_South_Sudan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Sudan.svg)","alt":"The flag of South Sudan: horizontal bands of black, red and green separated by white stripes, with a blue triangle at the hoist bearing a gold star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Fishing_in_Sudd_wetland_-_by_CPWF_Basin_Focal_Project.jpg/1920px-Fishing_in_Sudd_wetland_-_by_CPWF_Basin_Focal_Project.jpg","title":"the Sudd, South Sudan","desc":"Fishing in the Sudd.","credit":"Water, food and livelihoods in River Basins, Photographer: Karen Conniff, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fishing_in_Sudd_wetland_-_by_CPWF_Basin_Focal_Project.jpg","alt":"A fisherman poling a dugout canoe through a wetland."}},
{"id":"gw-082","num":82,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Belgium","answerText":"Belgium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 January 1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the Dutch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 October 1919</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Belgium"},"facts":[["Capital","Brussels"],["Population","11.8 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Area","30,689 km² (11,849 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","belgium"],"abstract":"<b>Belgium</b> was recognised by three different sets of people on three different dates, and the last of them was the country it had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It declared independence from the Kingdom of the Netherlands on 4 October 1830, and most of the European powers recognised that independence in fact on 20 December of the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 6 January 1832, when the Department of State issued an exequatur to the Belgian consul at New York, which is a recognition delivered through a commercial appointment rather than a diplomatic one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations followed on 18 June 1832, when Baron D. Behr sent his full powers to Secretary of State Edward Livingston, and the American legation at Brussels opened on 25 September when Hugh Legaré presented credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Netherlands itself held out until the Treaty of London of 19 April 1839, nine years after the declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legation became an embassy on 3 October 1919, and the embassy closed on 15 July 1940 after the German invasion, a chargé d'affaires being accredited that November to the Belgian government established in England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reopened at Brussels on 14 September 1944 under Ernest de W. Mayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was a founding member of the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 January 1948 and of the World Trade Organization from 1 January 1995, and joined the United Nations on 27 December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has been a member of the European Union since 1 January 1958, one of the six that founded it, and a party to Schengen since 26 March 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three institutions give its area three different figures, 30,528, 30,667 and 30,689 square kilometres, a spread of half a per cent between the smallest and the largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Belgium,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/be.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Belgium,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/belgium. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Belgium and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/belgium_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Belgium – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/belgium_en?etrans=en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Belgium,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BEL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Flag_of_Belgium_%28civil%29.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belgium_(civil).svg)","alt":"The flag of Belgium: three vertical bands of black, yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Grand-Place%2C_Brussels_-_panorama%2C_June_2018.jpg/1920px-Grand-Place%2C_Brussels_-_panorama%2C_June_2018.jpg","title":"Grand-Place, Belgium","desc":"Grand-Place.","credit":"Celuici, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand-Place,_Brussels_-_panorama,_June_2018.jpg","alt":"A view of Grand-Place."}},
{"id":"gw-083","num":83,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Haiti","answerText":"Haiti","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1804</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 July 1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1915 – 1934</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 March 1943</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Haiti"},"facts":[["Capital","Port-au-Prince"],["Population","12 million (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","27,750 km² (10,710 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","haiti"],"abstract":"<b>Haiti</b> won its independence from France in 1804 and waited 58 years for the United States to recognise it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The State Department's own guide states both facts in a single sentence — independence in 1804, recognition in 1862 — and gives no reason at all for the 58 years between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came on 12 July 1862, when President Abraham Lincoln commissioned Benjamin F. Whidden to act as an American diplomatic representative under the title commissioner and consul-general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations and the American legation at Port-au-Prince began on 1 October that year, when Whidden presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guide describes the country as the second-oldest republic in the Western Hemisphere, after the United States itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> American forces occupied the country from 1915, after what the guide calls numerous periods of intense political and economic disorder, and were withdrawn 19 years later, in 1934, when the guide records that the country regained sovereign rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy through a joint announcement on 23 March 1943 covering the United States and seven American republics at once, John Campbell White presenting credentials as ambassador on 14 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was among the states that joined the United Nations at its founding, on 24 October 1945, the day the Charter came into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came to the trading system early, as a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 January 1950, and to the World Trade Organization on 30 January 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen by about an eighth in the decade to 2025, from 10.5 million to 11.9 million, on an area of 27,750 square kilometres on which the two international counts agree exactly, and the capital's urban area holds about 2.7 million of those people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Haiti,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ht.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Haiti,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/haiti. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Haiti and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/haiti_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Haiti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HTI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Haiti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HTI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Flag_of_Haiti.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Haiti.svg)","alt":"The flag of Haiti: horizontal bands of blue and red with the national coat of arms on a white panel at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Citadelle_Laferri%C3%A8re.jpg/1920px-Citadelle_Laferri%C3%A8re.jpg","title":"Citadelle Laferrière, Haiti","desc":"Citadelle Laferrière.","credit":"Rémi Kaupp, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citadelle_Laferri%C3%A8re.jpg","alt":"A view of Citadelle Laferrière."}},
{"id":"gw-535","num":535,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Buenos Aires","answerText":"Buenos Aires","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 1816</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation moved away</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation returned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 February 1862</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Argentina","dot":"Buenos Aires"},"facts":[["Country","Argentina"],["Population","15.1 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Where","on the Río de la Plata"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","argentina"],"abstract":"<b>Buenos Aires</b> was itself the thing the United States recognised: the entry in the American guide is headed with the city's name, not the country's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Government of Buenos Ayres, which the guide calls the predecessor of Argentina, had declared independence from Spain on 9 July 1816 and was recognised on 27 January 1823, when Caesar Rodney was appointed minister plenipotentiary to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rodney presented his credentials on 27 December that year, which is the date relations began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mission then left the city for five years, because the country it served had split in two: Centralists here repudiated the constitution the Federalist provinces ratified in 1854 and declared themselves independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> James A. Peden held letters of credence to both sides, resided here until about 1 May 1857, then closed the legation and moved it to Paraná.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He brought it back on 25 February 1862, after the Argentine Confederation collapsed and the country was reunified as the Argentine Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation was raised to embassy rank on 1 October 1914, and Frederic Jessup Stimson presented his credentials as the first ambassador on 8 January 1915.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were then interrupted without being severed: after a coup, the United States judged the Farrell government of 1944 not in sympathy with the declared Argentine policy of joining the defence of the hemisphere, and told its ambassador not to enter into official relations with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ambassador Norman Armour was recalled to Washington on 27 June 1944 and a chargé d'affaires ran the embassy here until relations resumed on 19 April 1945, a decision taken at an inter-American conference in Mexico City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city now holds about 15.1 million people in its urban area against a national 45.9 million, a third of the whole country, which itself came to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade only in 1967 and whose land the two international counts measure 16,027 square kilometres apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Argentina,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ar.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Argentina,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/argentina. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Argentina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Argentina and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/argentina_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Argentina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Buenos_Aires_skyline_day.jpg/1920px-Buenos_Aires_skyline_day.jpg","title":"Buenos Aires","desc":"Buenos Aires, seen from a distance.","credit":"Luis Argerich from Buenos Aires, Argentina, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buenos_Aires_skyline_day.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Buenos Aires."}},
{"id":"gw-084","num":84,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Jordan","answerText":"Jordan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 May 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 January 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 April 2000</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Jordan"},"facts":[["Capital","Amman"],["Population","11.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","89,318 km² (34,486 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","jordan"],"abstract":"<b>Jordan</b> was recognised by the United States under a name it no longer uses, and nearly three years after it became independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Transjordan had been a mandated territory placed under British protection after the First World War, and declared its independence with British agreement on 25 May 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American recognition came only on 31 January 1949, by a White House announcement, and the entry is headed with the older name: the Kingdom of Transjordan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations and the American legation followed on 18 February 1949, when Wells Stabler presented his credentials as chargé d'affaires at Amman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The whole of the guide's entry is those two acts and one sentence of background, with nothing recorded since — no closure, no severance and no interruption in 76 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955, under the single resolution headed <i>Admission of New Members</i> that took in several states at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It came to the trading system very late and by a single step, acceding directly to the World Trade Organization on 11 April 2000 without ever having been a party to the agreement that preceded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by a fifth in the decade to 2025, from 9.5 million to 11.5 million, and the two international counts of it agree to within about 300 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same United Nations profile counts about 699,000 refugees and others of concern to UNHCR in the country, and puts nearly one Jordanian in five in the capital, whose urban area it gives as 2.11 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its area is a figure on which the two international counts do not differ at all — both give 89,318 square kilometres — where the World Bank usually rounds a country of this size to the nearest ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Jordan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jo.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Jordan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/jordan. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Jordan and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/jordan_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Jordan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/JOR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Jordan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/JOR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Flag_of_Jordan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jordan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Jordan: horizontal bands of black, white and green with a red triangle at the hoist bearing a white seven-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Al_khazneh.jpg/1920px-Al_khazneh.jpg","title":"Petra, Jordan","desc":"Petra.","credit":"Susanahajer, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_khazneh.jpg","alt":"A view of Petra."}},
{"id":"gw-085","num":85,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dominican Republic","answerText":"Dominican Republic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence from Haiti</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1844</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence regained</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1865</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1866</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 July 1904</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dominican Rep."},"facts":[["Capital","Santo Domingo"],["Population","11.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","48,671 km² (18,792 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","dominican republic"],"abstract":"The <b>Dominican Republic</b> declared its independence twice, from two different rulers, and was recognised after the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It declared itself independent of neighbouring Haiti in 1844, reverted to Spanish rule in 1861, and won its independence again in 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American recognition followed on 17 September 1866, in the form an exequatur issued to J. W. Currier as Dominican consul general at New York, which is a recognition delivered through a commercial appointment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations waited another 18 years, until 26 March 1884, when John M. Langston presented credentials as chargé d'affaires — while accredited to Haiti as well and resident at Port-au-Prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A legation of its own opened at Santo Domingo only on 23 July 1904, under Thomas C. Dawson, 38 years after the recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legation became an embassy through the same joint announcement of 23 March 1943 that raised the missions of the United States and seven American republics together, Avra M. Warren presenting credentials on 17 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was among those that joined the United Nations at its founding, on 24 October 1945, the day the Charter came into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 19 May 1950 and a member of the World Trade Organization from 9 March 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by about a tenth in the decade to 2025, from 10.4 million to 11.5 million, and about 28 in every hundred of those people live in the capital's urban area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The World Bank's area series is unusable here: it gives 146,839 square kilometres from 2019 onward against the 48,670 it gave every year until 2018, a threefold jump with nothing behind it, so the figure here rests on the United Nations alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Dominican Republic,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/do.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Dominican Republic,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/dominican-republic. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Dominican Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/dominican_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Dominican Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DOM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Dominican Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DOM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2018:2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Flag_of_the_Dominican_Republic.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dominican_Republic.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Dominican Republic: a white cross dividing blue and red quarters, with the national coat of arms at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Dominikanische_Republik_Santo_Domingo_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Col%C3%B3n_01.jpg/1920px-Dominikanische_Republik_Santo_Domingo_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Col%C3%B3n_01.jpg","title":"the Alcázar de Colón, the Dominican Republic","desc":"The Alcázar de Colón in Santo Domingo.","credit":"Georg Karl Ell, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dominikanische_Republik_Santo_Domingo_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Col%C3%B3n_01.jpg","alt":"A stone Renaissance palace with two tiers of arches."}},
{"id":"gw-086","num":86,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"United Arab Emirates","answerText":"United Arab Emirates","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 December 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 December 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 June 1974</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United Arab Emirates"},"facts":[["Capital","Abu Dhabi"],["Population","11.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","83,600 km² (32,300 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","united arab emirates"],"abstract":"The <b>United Arab Emirates</b> is a federation of seven states that the United States recognised the day after it was formed and that the United Nations seated within a week of that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seven Trucial sheikhdoms — Abu Dhabi, Dubai, Sharjah, Ajman, Umm al-Qaiwain, Fujairah and Ras al-Khaimah — had been under British protection, and the United States had very limited dealings with any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The federation declared its independence on 2 December 1971, on the completion of treaties with Great Britain, and the United States recognised it on 3 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it six days later, by resolution 2794 (XXVI) adopted at its 2007th plenary meeting on 9 December 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on 20 March 1972, when William A. Stoltzfus Jr. presented credentials — though he was accredited to several Persian Gulf states at once and resided in Kuwait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An embassy in the capital came only on 24 June 1974, when Michael Edmund Sterner presented credentials as the first American ambassador actually resident in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reached the World Trade Organization on 10 April 1996, in that body's second year, without ever having been a party to the agreement it replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two international counts of its area stand 27,624 square kilometres apart: the United Nations gives 71,024 and the World Bank 98,648, the larger figure exceeding the smaller by 39 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The figure given here, 83,600, sits between the two, which is why it stands rather than being moved toward either of them, and neither source explains the gap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by a third in the decade to 2025, from 8.5 million to 11.5 million, and only about one resident in eight lives in the capital's urban area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Arab Emirates,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ae.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: United Arab Emirates,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/united-arab-emirates. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the United Arab Emirates to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 2794 (XXVI), adopted at the 2007th plenary meeting, 9 December 1971, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192057. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “United Arab Emirates and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/united_arab_emirates_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): United Arab Emirates,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: United Arab Emirates,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Flag_of_the_United_Arab_Emirates.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Arab_Emirates.svg)","alt":"The flag of the United Arab Emirates: horizontal bands of green, white and black with a red vertical band at the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Burj_Khalifa_%28worlds_tallest_building%29_and_the_Dubai_skyline_%2825781049892%29.jpg/1920px-Burj_Khalifa_%28worlds_tallest_building%29_and_the_Dubai_skyline_%2825781049892%29.jpg","title":"Burj Khalifa, the United Arab Emirates","desc":"Burj Khalifa.","credit":"imran shahabuddin, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burj_Khalifa_(worlds_tallest_building)_and_the_Dubai_skyline_(25781049892).jpg","alt":"A view of Burj Khalifa."}},
{"id":"gw-536","num":536,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kabul","answerText":"Kabul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 June 1942</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised to embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 June 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 January 1989</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Operations suspended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Afghanistan","dot":"Kabul"},"facts":[["Country","Afghanistan"],["Population","4.1 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Where","in a valley of the Hindu Kush"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","afghanistan"],"abstract":"<b>Kabul</b> has held an American mission that arrived 21 years after the recognition it belonged to, was raised, was left without an ambassador, closed, reopened and finally moved to another country altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Afghanistan on 26 July 1921, when President Warren Harding received an Afghan mission at the White House, British authorities having notified the State Department in August 1919 that the country was officially free and independent in its affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations followed 14 years later, on 4 May 1935, and even then the minister who presented credentials was accredited to Persia as well and resided at Tehran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A legation in the city itself opened only on 6 June 1942 under Charles W. Thayer, 21 years after the recognition, and became an embassy on 5 June 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 14 February 1979 the American ambassador here, Adolph Dubs, was assassinated at post, and from his death the mission was headed by a chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The embassy closed on 30 January 1989, over concerns that the incoming government would not be able to protect diplomats once Soviet forces had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It returned as a liaison office on 17 December 2001, five weeks after the city fell to a coalition campaign that had begun on 7 October, and the United States recognised the Interim Authority on 22 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The office was re-designated an embassy on 17 January 2002, and on 31 August 2021 that embassy suspended operations and transferred them to Doha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took its United Nations seat on 19 November 1946, the Security Council having recommended it at the 57th meeting on 29 August that year, in one resolution covering Afghanistan, Iceland and Sweden together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city holds about 4.11 million people, roughly one Afghan in eleven of the 43.8 million the two international counts agree on, and the country reached the World Trade Organization only on 29 July 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Afghanistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/af.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Afghanistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/afghanistan. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 8 (1946),” adopted at its 57th meeting, 29 August 1946, S/RES/8(1946), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/111971. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Afghanistan and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/afghanistan_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Afghanistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AFG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/Kabul%2C_Afghanistan_-_panoramio_%281%29.jpg","title":"Kabul","desc":"Kabul, seen from a distance.","credit":"seair21, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kabul,_Afghanistan_-_panoramio_(1).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kabul."}},
{"id":"gw-087","num":87,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cuba","answerText":"Cuba","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic installed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 May 1902</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 February 1923</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 January 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 July 2015</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cuba"},"facts":[["Capital","Havana"],["Population","10.9 million (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","109,884 km² (42,426 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","cuba"],"abstract":"<b>Cuba</b> was occupied by the country that recognised it, for four years, before there was anything to recognise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spain relinquished sovereignty after its defeat in the war of 1898, and American forces remained as an occupying power until the Republic of Cuba was formally installed on 19 May 1902.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States gave up its occupation authority the next day but claimed a continuing right to intervene on the island, a condition written into the Platt Amendment and repealed only in 1934, by a Treaty of Relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American legation at Havana began on 27 May 1902, when Herbert Goldsmith Squiers presented his credentials, and the legation became an embassy on 10 February 1923 following an act of Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were severed on 3 January 1961, the United States citing action by the Cuban government that placed crippling limitations on its mission's ability to work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An American interests section opened in Havana on 1 September 1977 under the protection of the Swiss government, and stood there for almost 38 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations resumed on 20 July 2015, when both countries raised their interests sections to embassy rank on a date agreed by Presidents Barack Obama and Raúl Castro in an exchange of letters dated 30 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was among those that joined the United Nations at its founding, on 24 October 1945, and was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 January 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population is falling: the United Nations counts 10.94 million for 2025 against 11.28 million in 2015, a loss of about a thirtieth in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only about one Cuban in five lives in the capital, whose urban area is put at 2.14 million, on an island of 109,884 square kilometres that the two international counts measure four apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cuba,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cu.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cuba,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cuba. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cuba and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cuba_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cuba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Cuba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Flag_of_Cuba.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cuba.svg)","alt":"The flag of Cuba: five blue and white stripes with a red triangle at the hoist bearing a white five-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Vi%C3%B1ales_Valley.jpg/1920px-Vi%C3%B1ales_Valley.jpg","title":"Viñales Valley, Cuba","desc":"Viñales Valley.","credit":"Severin.stalder, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vi%C3%B1ales_Valley.jpg","alt":"A view of Viñales Valley."}},
{"id":"gw-088","num":88,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Czechia","answerText":"Czechia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federal republic ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 December 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council recommendation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 January 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Czechia"},"facts":[["Capital","Prague"],["Population","10.9 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","78,871 km² (30,452 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","czechia"],"abstract":"<b>Czechia</b> came into being at midnight and was recognised the same day: the Czech and Slovak Federal Republic ceased to exist on 31 December 1992, and the United States formally recognised the new state on 1 January 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on that date as well, so recognition, relations and independence all share a single day, and the White House press secretary marked it by saying the United States looked forward to full and mutually productive relations with the new Czech state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The break is known as the Velvet Divorce, and the American guide uses that name for it in its own text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No new embassy had to be opened: Adrian A. Basora, ambassador to the federal republic since June 1992, simply served as ambassador to the new country from 1 January 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Security Council recommended admission to the United Nations at its 3158th meeting on 8 January, and the country took its seat on 19 January 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 15 April 1993 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has belonged to the European Union since 1 May 2004 and to the Schengen area since 21 December 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population is a case where the United Nations count is the outlier: that source gives 10.61 million for 2025 where Eurostat gives 10.91 million and the World Bank 10.89 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The figure used here follows the two that agree, which is the practice wherever a country runs its own statistical service and the World Bank reports what that service publishes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On area there is no such argument: all three sources give 78,871 square kilometres, and about one Czech in eight lives in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Czechia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Czechia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/czech-republic. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 801 (1993),” adopted at its 3158th meeting, 8 January 1993, S/RES/801(1993), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/156781. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Czech Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/czech_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Czechia – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/czechia_en?etrans=en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Czechia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CZE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Flag_of_the_Czech_Republic.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Czech_Republic.svg)","alt":"The flag of Czechia: horizontal white and red bands with a blue triangle at the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Charles_Bridge_%28Karl%C5%AFv_most%29%2C_Vltava_River%2C_Prague%2C_2015.jpg/1920px-Charles_Bridge_%28Karl%C5%AFv_most%29%2C_Vltava_River%2C_Prague%2C_2015.jpg","title":"Charles Bridge, Czechia","desc":"Charles Bridge.","credit":"Peter K Burian, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Bridge_(Karl%C5%AFv_most),_Vltava_River,_Prague,_2015.jpg","alt":"A view of Charles Bridge."}},
{"id":"gw-089","num":89,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Honduras","answerText":"Honduras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 August 1824</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised separately</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1853</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 February 1856</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Honduras"},"facts":[["Capital","Tegucigalpa"],["Population","11 million (2025)"],["Region","Central America"],["Area","112,492 km² (43,433 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","honduras"],"abstract":"<b>Honduras</b> was recognised twice, 29 years apart, and the first recognition was shared with four neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became independent of Spain in 1821 and joined the Federation of Central American States two years later, alongside Guatemala, Nicaragua, Costa Rica and El Salvador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised that federation on 4 August 1824, when President James Monroe received its envoy, so this country's first recognition is the same act that recognised Guatemala.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the federation broke up between 1838 and 1840, it was recognised as a separate independent state on 19 April 1853, by the appointment of Solon Borland as envoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Borland was accredited to four countries at once — Honduras, Costa Rica, Nicaragua and El Salvador — and never presented his credentials in the capital at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The American legation, at Comayagua and Tegucigalpa, opened on 22 February 1856, and the first minister actually resident in the country, James R. Partridge, presented credentials on 25 April 1862.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy through the joint announcement of 23 March 1943 that raised the missions of the United States and seven American republics together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 17 December 1945, in its founding year, and came to the trading system very late, acceding to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade only on 10 April 1994 and becoming a founding member of the World Trade Organization nine months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by a fifth in the decade to 2025, from 9.2 million to 11.0 million, and the two international counts of it agree to within about 150 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About one Honduran in eight lives in the capital, and the two international counts of the country's area differ by two square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Honduras,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Honduras,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/honduras. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Honduras and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/honduras_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Honduras,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HND/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Honduras,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HND/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Flag_of_Honduras_%281949%E2%80%932022%2C_2026%E2%80%93present%29.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Honduras.svg)","alt":"The flag of Honduras: three horizontal bands of blue, white and blue, with five blue five-pointed stars on the white band."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Copan_sculpture.jpg","title":"Copán, Honduras","desc":"Copán.","credit":"Adalberto Hernandez Vega from Copan Ruinas, Honduras, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copan_sculpture.jpg","alt":"A view of Copán."}},
{"id":"gw-537","num":537,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ottawa","answerText":"Ottawa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominion formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomy acknowledged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 November 1943</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Canada","dot":"Ottawa"},"facts":[["Country","Canada"],["Population","1.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern America"],["Where","on the Ottawa River, facing Quebec"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","canada"],"abstract":"<b>Ottawa</b> received an American legation 60 years after the country it governs was formed, because for most of that time the country did not conduct its own foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Dominion of Canada was formed in 1867 but remained an integral part of the British Empire, its external affairs controlled from London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officials of the two governments dealt with each other through joint commissions and military cooperation all the same, and the two even negotiated a bilateral fisheries treaty in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was only in 1926 that the United Kingdom acknowledged full Canadian autonomy in foreign affairs, at the Imperial Conference that produced the Balfour Declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That declaration held the United Kingdom and the Dominions to be autonomous communities within the British Empire, equal in status and in no way subordinate one to another in any aspect of their domestic or external affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Recognition followed on 18 February 1927, when Vincent Massey presented credentials in Washington as Canadian envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary, and relations date from the same act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation here opened on 1 June 1927 under William Phillips, and was raised to embassy rank on 19 November 1943 when Ray Atherton presented credentials as ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 9 November 1945, in its founding year, and was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 January 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city itself is modest against the country: its urban area holds about 1.38 million people, roughly one Canadian in 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two international counts of the country's population differ by more than a million — 40.13 against 41.65 — and its area cannot be taken from the second at all, whose series gives 15.63 million square kilometres against the 9,984,670 the United Nations records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Canada,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ca.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Canada,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/canada. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Canada and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/canada_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Canada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Canada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ottawa_skyline_2018.jpg/1920px-Ottawa_skyline_2018.jpg","title":"Ottawa","desc":"Ottawa, seen from a distance.","credit":"Dennis G. Jarvis, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ottawa_skyline_2018.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Ottawa."}},
{"id":"gw-090","num":90,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Portugal","answerText":"Portugal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 May 1791</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 June 1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation at Rio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1810 – 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1986</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Portugal"},"facts":[["Capital","Lisbon"],["Population","10.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","92,230 km² (35,610 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","portugal"],"abstract":"<b>Portugal</b> was the first neutral country to open diplomatic relations with the United States, and the guide says so in its opening line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lisbon suggested an exchange of permanent missions in early 1791, which amounted to a recognition in fact, and made it formal on 13 May 1791 by accepting the credentials of the American minister David Humphreys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations and the American legation date from that same act, so this is one of the older continuous relationships in the whole record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consular presence is older still: George Washington appointed a consul for Madeira on 17 June 1790, the second consular appointment made under the American constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consulate opened in the Azores in 1795, at Ponta Delgada, is described by the State Department as the oldest continuously operating American consulate anywhere in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legation left the country for eleven years without relations breaking: the king fled to Brazil during the Napoleonic wars, the mission followed him to Rio de Janeiro from 1810, and it returned to Lisbon in 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955, under the single resolution that admitted several states at once, and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 6 May 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has belonged to the European Union since 1 January 1986 and to the Schengen area since 26 March 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population is counted three ways for 2025: the United Nations gives 10.41 million, Eurostat 10.75 million and the World Bank 10.80 million, so the figure here sits inside a spread of nearly 400,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On area the three are almost identical — 92,226, 92,226 and 92,230 square kilometres — and about 28 in every hundred Portuguese live in the capital's urban area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Portugal,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pt.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Portugal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/portugal. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Portugal and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/portugal_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Portugal – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/portugal_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Portugal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Portugal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Flag_of_Portugal.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal.svg)","alt":"The flag of Portugal: green and red vertical bands with the national coat of arms over an armillary sphere on the division."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Bel%C3%A9m_Tower_in_Lisbon%2C_Portugal.jpg/1920px-Bel%C3%A9m_Tower_in_Lisbon%2C_Portugal.jpg","title":"Belém Tower, Portugal","desc":"Belém Tower.","credit":"Lisbon Photoshoots, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bel%C3%A9m_Tower_in_Lisbon,_Portugal.jpg","alt":"A view of Belém Tower."}},
{"id":"gw-091","num":91,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tajikistan","answerText":"Tajikistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 February 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tajikistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Dushanbe"],["Population","10.8 million (2025)"],["Region","Central Asia"],["Area","141,400 km² (54,590 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","tajikistan"],"abstract":"<b>Tajikistan</b> was recognised neither by a note nor at a ceremony but in a presidential address to the American nation about the end of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and the United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation on the dissolution of that union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations came separately, on 19 February 1992, again by a presidential announcement rather than by any ceremony in either capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American embassy at Dushanbe opened on 13 March 1992 with Edmund McWilliams as chargé d'affaires, three weeks after the relations it served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country on 2 March 1992, by resolution 46/228, adopted without a vote at its 82nd meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its entry into the world trading system fell 21 years later to the day: it became a member of the World Trade Organization on 2 March 2013, having never been a party to the agreement that preceded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by a quarter in the decade to 2025, from 8.6 million to 10.8 million, and the United Nations and the World Bank agree on that figure to within about 300 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The capital is small against that: its urban area is put at about 894,000 people, roughly one Tajik in twelve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two international counts of its area differ by 21 square kilometres, the United Nations giving 141,400 and the World Bank 141,379.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Those figures put 75.4 people on each square kilometre, and the currency in which its economy is reported is the somoni, at about 10.9 to the American dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Tajikistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tj.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tajikistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tajikistan. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Tajikistan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/228, adopted without a vote at the 82nd meeting, 2 March 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/149007. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tajikistan and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tajikistan_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Tajikistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TJK/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Tajikistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TJK/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Flag_of_Tajikistan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tajikistan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Tajikistan: horizontal bands of red, white and green, with a gold crown and seven stars on the white band."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/Iskander-kul%2C_Tajikistan.JPG","title":"Iskanderkul, Tajikistan","desc":"Iskanderkul.","credit":"Rjruiziii, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iskander-kul,_Tajikistan.JPG","alt":"A view of Iskanderkul."}},
{"id":"gw-092","num":92,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Papua New Guinea","answerText":"Papua New Guinea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 1975</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Papua New Guinea"},"facts":[["Capital","Port Moresby"],["Population","10.8 million (2025)"],["Region","Melanesia"],["Area","462,840 km² (178,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","papua new guinea"],"abstract":"<b>Papua New Guinea</b> was recognised by a letter carried to its independence ceremonies by the governor of Iowa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Robert D. Ray, serving as the personal representative of President Gerald Ford, presented that letter to the new government on 16 September 1975, the day of independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state formed that day was made of New Guinea, Papua — which had been a trust territory administered by Australia — and the islands nearby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mission was already there: an American consulate had opened at Port Moresby on or about 1 July 1974, in what was then the self-governing Territory of Papua and New Guinea, and it opened to the public that September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On independence day the consulate general was simply raised to embassy rank, with Mary S. Olmsted, who had opened it, in charge as ambassador-designate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The ambassador here has since been accredited to the Solomon Islands and Vanuatu as well, while remaining resident in this country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country on 10 October 1975, by resolution 3368 (XXX) adopted at its 2383rd plenary meeting, 24 days after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It became a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 16 December 1994, two weeks before that agreement was replaced, and joined the World Trade Organization on 9 June 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by nearly a quarter in the decade to 2025, from 8.7 million to 10.8 million, and the two international counts of its area agree exactly at 462,840 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only about one person in 29 lives in the capital, whose urban area is put at 375,000 people, and the United Nations files the country under Melanesia rather than under any continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Papua New Guinea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Papua New Guinea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/papua-new-guinea. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Papua New Guinea to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 3368 (XXX), adopted at the 2383rd plenary meeting, 10 October 1975, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/189350. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Papua New Guinea and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/papua_new_guinea_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Papua New Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PNG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Papua New Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PNG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Flag_of_Papua_New_Guinea.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Papua_New_Guinea.svg)","alt":"The flag of Papua New Guinea: a diagonal division, red above with a golden bird of paradise and black below with the Southern Cross in white."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Mount_Wilhelm.jpg","title":"Mount Wilhelm, Papua New Guinea","desc":"Mount Wilhelm.","credit":"Nomadtales ( talk · contribs ), CC BY-SA 2.1 au, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Wilhelm.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Wilhelm."}},
{"id":"gw-538","num":538,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sana'a","answerText":"Sana'a","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 March 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy moved here</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1972</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Yemen","dot":"Sana'a"},"facts":[["Country","Yemen"],["Population","2.9 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Where","in the highlands above the Red Sea coast"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","yemen"],"abstract":"<b>Sana'a</b> became an American embassy city because the capital moved to it, not because the mission chose it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Kingdom of the Yemen on 4 March 1946, in a letter from President Harry Truman to the Imam Yahya, and relations were agreed the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American minister presented credentials on 30 September 1946 while accredited to Saudi Arabia as well and resident at Jidda, and the legation that finally opened in 1959 was at Taiz, the old capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That legation was raised to embassy rank on 28 January 1963, though from 1962 to 1967 no minister or ambassador was accredited at all and a chargé d'affaires ran it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1966 the embassy moved from Taiz to this city, which the guide describes as the new capital of the Yemen Arab Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were severed on 7 June 1967 in the wake of the Arab-Israeli conflict, and resumed only after a visit by Secretary of State William Rogers in July 1972, when the embassy here reopened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Meanwhile the United States kept a separate embassy at Aden for the south, opened on 7 December 1967 and closed on 26 October 1969 when that state severed relations; it never reopened, even after the two Yemens announced their union on 22 May 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country's United Nations seat dates from 30 September 1947, which is the northern state's admission carried through the merger, and it joined the World Trade Organization only on 26 June 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Security Council has since acted on the war here, adopting resolution 2216 at its 7426th meeting on 14 April 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city holds about 2.87 million people, roughly one Yemeni in fifteen of the 41.8 million the two international counts agree on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Yemen,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ye.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Yemen,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/yemen. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Yemen and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/yemen_e.htm. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 2216 (2015),” adopted at its 7426th meeting, 14 April 2015, S/RES/2216(2015), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/791250. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Yemen,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/YEM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Sana%27a_skyline.jpg/1920px-Sana%27a_skyline.jpg","title":"Sana'a","desc":"Sana'a, seen from a distance.","credit":"Rod Waddington, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sana%27a_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Sana'a."}},
{"id":"gw-093","num":93,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sweden","answerText":"Sweden","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 April 1783</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 April 1818</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 December 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sweden"},"facts":[["Capital","Stockholm"],["Population","10.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","447,424 km² (172,750 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","sweden"],"abstract":"<b>Sweden</b> offered its friendship to the United States before it was asked, and the guide quotes the man who said so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Swedish minister in Paris approached Benjamin Franklin in 1782 suggesting a treaty, remarking that he hoped it would be remembered that Sweden was the first power in Europe which had voluntarily and without solicitation offered its friendship to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Franklin had been appointed minister plenipotentiary to Sweden on 28 September 1782 but never went there, staying in Paris where he was already minister to France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition therefore took the form of a signature in a third country: the Treaty of Amity and Commerce was signed at Paris on 3 April 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sweden appointed its first consul to the United States that September, and the United States named its first consul to Stockholm only 35 years later, in May 1818.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomatic relations began on 29 April 1818, when Jonathan Russell presented credentials as minister to the court of Sweden and Norway, which were not then separate countries, and they have been unbroken since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy in 1947, when H. Freeman Matthews was appointed the first American ambassador and presented credentials on 5 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 19 November 1946, the Security Council having recommended it at the 57th meeting on 29 August in one resolution covering Afghanistan, Iceland and Sweden together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 30 April 1950, a founding member of the World Trade Organization, and has belonged to the European Union since 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its area is given three ways and one of the three has broken: the United Nations says 438,574 square kilometres, the European Union 447,424, and the World Bank's series jumped from 447,420 to about 528,500 in 2013 and has stayed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sweden,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/se.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sweden,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sweden. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 8 (1946),” adopted at its 57th meeting, 29 August 1946, S/RES/8(1946), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/111971. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Sweden and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/sweden_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Sweden – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/sweden_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Sweden,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SWE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2010:2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/Flag_of_Sweden.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sweden.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sweden: a yellow Nordic cross on a blue field."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/20130202_Stockholm_Sk%C3%A4rg%C3%A5rd_%2816%29.jpg/1920px-20130202_Stockholm_Sk%C3%A4rg%C3%A5rd_%2816%29.jpg","title":"the Stockholm archipelago, Sweden","desc":"The Stockholm archipelago.","credit":"Belola80, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20130202_Stockholm_Sk%C3%A4rg%C3%A5rd_(16).jpg","alt":"Wooded islands and frozen channels seen from the air."}},
{"id":"gw-094","num":94,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Greece","answerText":"Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Otho acknowledged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 April 1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 November 1837</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consul at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 January 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1981</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Greece"},"facts":[["Capital","Athens"],["Population","10.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","131,957 km² (50,949 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","greece"],"abstract":"<b>Greece</b> was recognised by a decision to negotiate rather than by any declaration, and the guide dates it to 7 November 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that day the American government empowered its minister at London, Andrew Stevenson, to enter upon the negotiation of a treaty of commerce and navigation with the King of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stevenson had been approached in April of that year by the Greek minister at London, who wanted relations of navigation between the two countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An earlier chance had been declined in a very careful sentence: invited by Britain, France and Russia in April 1833 to acknowledge Prince Otho of Bavaria as king, Secretary of State Edward Livingston replied that the invariable practice of the United States was to recognise whichever government actually exercised political power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then said the assurances of the three mediating powers would enable him to acknowledge Otho, which is a willingness to recognise expressed without recognising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A consulate came first in practice: Gregory A. Perdicaris was appointed in June 1837 and took up his post at Athens on 7 January 1838.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was among those that joined the United Nations at its founding, on 25 October 1945, the day the Charter came into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 March 1950 and has belonged to the European Union since 1 January 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its population is a case where the United Nations is the outlier: that source gives 9.94 million for 2025 where Eurostat gives 10.41 million and the World Bank 10.41 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its area is agreed to within 300 square kilometres by all three, and about 32 in every hundred Greeks live in the capital's urban area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Greece,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Greece,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/greece. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Greece and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/greece_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Greece – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/greece_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Greece,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRC/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Greece,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Flag_of_Greece.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Greece.svg)","alt":"The flag of Greece: nine blue and white stripes with a white cross on a blue square at the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Oia_-_Santorini_2019.jpg/1920px-Oia_-_Santorini_2019.jpg","title":"Santorini, Greece","desc":"Santorini.","credit":"User:Anna.Tsolidou, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oia_-_Santorini_2019.jpg","alt":"Whitewashed terraces above a flooded volcanic caldera."}},
{"id":"gw-095","num":95,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Azerbaijan","answerText":"Azerbaijan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1918 – 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Azerbaijan"},"facts":[["Capital","Baku"],["Population","10.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","86,600 km² (33,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","azerbaijan"],"abstract":"<b>Azerbaijan</b> was an independent state once before, briefly, and the guide records that it was recognised then too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the collapse of the Russian Empire in 1917 it became independent and was recognised in fact by the Allies in January 1920, only for that independence to end when the Red Army arrived in April of the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its second recognition came on 25 December 1991, in the same presidential address to the American nation on the dissolution of the Soviet Union that recognised the other former republics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 19 February 1992, announced in a press statement, and the American embassy at Baku opened on 16 March that year under Robert Finn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country on 2 March 1992, by resolution 46/230, adopted without a vote at its 82nd meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That was the same meeting and the same day at which Tajikistan was admitted by the resolution two numbers before it, one of several sittings at which the successor states arrived together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is not a member of the World Trade Organization: its accession working party was established on 16 July 1997 and the process is still open, so it has spent 28 years in the queue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown by about a sixteenth in the decade to 2025, from 9.6 million to 10.2 million by the World Bank's count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations puts it slightly higher, at 10.40 million, so the two counts differ by about 1.5 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On area they do not differ at all — both give 86,600 square kilometres — and rather more than a fifth of the country lives in the capital's urban area, put at 2.31 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Azerbaijan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/az.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Azerbaijan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/azerbaijan. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Azerbaijan Republic to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/230, adopted without a vote at the 82nd meeting, 2 March 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/147688. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Tajikistan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/228, adopted without a vote at the 82nd meeting, 2 March 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/149007. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accession status: Azerbaijan,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_azerbaidjan_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Azerbaijan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AZE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Azerbaijan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AZE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Flag_of_Azerbaijan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Azerbaijan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Azerbaijan: horizontal bands of blue, red and green, with a white crescent and eight-pointed star on the red band."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Qobustan_giri%C5%9F_qayas%C4%B1_2026_%281%29.jpg/1920px-Qobustan_giri%C5%9F_qayas%C4%B1_2026_%281%29.jpg","title":"Gobustan National Park, Azerbaijan","desc":"Gobustan National Park.","credit":"Rəcəb Yaxşı, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qobustan_giri%C5%9F_qayas%C4%B1_2026_(1).jpg","alt":"A view of Gobustan National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-539","num":539,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Rabat","answerText":"Rabat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 June 1881</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 March 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Morocco","dot":"Rabat"},"facts":[["Country","Morocco"],["Population","1.9 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Where","on the Atlantic, at the mouth of the Bou Regreg"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","morocco"],"abstract":"<b>Rabat</b> held an American consulate for 75 years before it held an embassy, and the embassy arrived the week the country became independent again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The relationship it belongs to is the oldest the United States has: Morocco recognised the new republic on 23 June 1786, when a treaty of peace and friendship was signed at Marrakech by the American minister Thomas Barclay and Sidi Muhammad, sultan of Morocco.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate followed at Tangier in 1797, and in 1821 Sultan Mawlay Suleiman gave the United States a building there — the first property abroad the country owned, and now an institute for Moroccan studies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consulate in this city opened on 25 June 1881, one of eight the guide lists across the country, most of which had closed by the end of the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Permanent diplomatic relations began elsewhere and late: the consulate at Tangier was raised to a legation on 8 March 1905, 119 years after the treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The United States refused at first to recognise the French and Spanish protectorate established in 1912, and acknowledged it only on 20 October 1917, on entering the First World War, whereupon its minister at Tangier was downgraded to a diplomatic agent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence was recognised on 7 March 1956, in a statement congratulating Morocco on the Franco-Moroccan declaration of 2 March by which France had recognised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy was established here on 11 June 1956, and the legation at Tangier became a consulate general on the same day, the mission following the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 12 November 1956 by resolution 1111 (XI), adopted at the 574th plenary meeting, the sitting that admitted Tunisia in the resolution immediately after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city holds about 1.86 million people, roughly one Moroccan in 21 of the 38.4 million the two international counts agree on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Morocco,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ma.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Morocco,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/morocco. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Morocco to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1111 (XI), adopted at the 574th plenary meeting, 12 November 1956, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207993. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Tunisia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1112 (XI), adopted at the 574th plenary meeting, 12 November 1956, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207994. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Morocco,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Kasbah_of_the_Oudayas%2C_Rabat%2C_Morocco.jpg/1920px-Kasbah_of_the_Oudayas%2C_Rabat%2C_Morocco.jpg","title":"Rabat","desc":"Rabat, seen from a distance.","credit":"Bikerhiker75, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kasbah_of_the_Oudayas,_Rabat,_Morocco.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Rabat."}},
{"id":"gw-096","num":96,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Israel","answerText":"Israel","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 May 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 March 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 May 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 July 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Israel"},"facts":[["Seat of government","Jerusalem"],["Population","10.1 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","22,072 km² (8,522 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","israel"],"abstract":"<b>Israel</b> was recognised by the United States on the day it declared itself a state, and the entry for it in the American recognition guide is two sentences long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Harry Truman issued a statement of recognition on 14 May 1948, following the proclamation of independence made the same date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations waited another ten months, until Ambassador James Grover McDonald presented his credentials on 28 March 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> United Nations membership came six weeks after that, by resolution 273 (III), adopted at the 207th plenary meeting on 11 May 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Assembly had voted eighteen months earlier on the future government of Palestine, in resolution 181 (II) of 29 November 1947, and that resolution is still cited in the tables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>  <br><br>  It is cited in them because the seat of government is disputed, and the United Nations statistical profile says so in a footnote rather than leaving the question unstated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The footnote reads that the designation and the data are provided by Israel, and that the position of the UN on Jerusalem is stated in resolution 181 (II) and in subsequent General Assembly and Security Council resolutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A second footnote, attached to the population of the city, records that the count includes East Jerusalem, so the dispute is carried in the numbers as well as in the name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trade membership is much older than the World Trade Organization: the country acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 5 July 1962 and joined the WTO on 21 April 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two international counts of its area agree to within two square kilometres, and its population rose from about 9.85 million in 2023 to about 10.12 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Israel,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/il.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Israel,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/israel. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Israel to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 273 (III), adopted at the 207th plenary meeting, 11 May 1949, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/210373. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Future Government of Palestine,” resolution 181 (II), 29 November 1947, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/210008. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Israel and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/israel_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Israel,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ISR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Israel,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ISR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Flag_of_Israel.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Israel.svg)","alt":"The flag of Israel: a white field with two horizontal blue stripes and a blue Star of David at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Israel-2013-Aerial_21-Masada.jpg/1920px-Israel-2013-Aerial_21-Masada.jpg","title":"Masada, Israel","desc":"Masada.","credit":"Godot13, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Israel-2013-Aerial_21-Masada.jpg","alt":"A view of Masada."}},
{"id":"gw-097","num":97,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Hungary","answerText":"Hungary","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 December 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Hungary"},"facts":[["Capital","Budapest"],["Population","9.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","93,030 km² (35,920 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","hungary"],"abstract":"<b>Hungary</b> had American consuls for half a century before it had an American minister, because until 1918 it was not a state the United States could send one to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A consular agent was appointed at Pesth in 1869 and a consul in 1874; the post became a consulate general in 1904 and closed in 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As the Central Powers faced defeat the Hungarian parliament declared independence from Austria on 17 October 1918, an independent government was formed on 1 November, and a republic was proclaimed on 16 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Treaty of Saint-Germain of 10 September 1919 recognised that independence and the Treaty of Trianon of 4 June 1920 fixed the new borders, which cost the country three-quarters of its prewar territory and two-thirds of its prewar population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States ratified neither treaty, so recognition had to wait for a Joint Resolution of Congress ending the state of war, passed on 2 July 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>  <br><br>  The National Assembly accepted the terms of that resolution on 12 August and authorised its government to negotiate, and a treaty establishing friendly relations was signed on 29 August 1921 and entered into force on 17 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation opened at the capital on 26 December 1921 under Ulysses Grant-Smith, who had been sent two years earlier as commissioner to report on political developments and promote commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were severed on 11 December 1941 and resumed after the war, and on 28 November 1966 the legation was raised to an embassy, one of the last two American missions anywhere to be upgraded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955 under resolution 995 (X), which admitted sixteen states at once, and acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 9 September 1973, while still a communist state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its population is falling slowly, from about 9.59 million in 2023 to about 9.51 million in 2025, and the two international measures of its area differ by seven square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Hungary,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hu.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Hungary,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/hungary. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Hungary and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/hungary_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Hungary,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HUN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Hungary,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HUN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Flag_of_Hungary.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hungary.svg)","alt":"The flag of Hungary: horizontal bands of red, white and green."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Hungarian_Parliament_Building_from_across_the_Danube%2C_2025-01-11.jpg/1920px-Hungarian_Parliament_Building_from_across_the_Danube%2C_2025-01-11.jpg","title":"Hungarian Parliament Building, Hungary","desc":"Hungarian Parliament Building.","credit":"Kilyann Le Hen, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hungarian_Parliament_Building_from_across_the_Danube,_2025-01-11.jpg","alt":"A view of Hungarian Parliament Building."}},
{"id":"gw-098","num":98,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Austria","answerText":"Austria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic proclaimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 November 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 August 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 May 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Austria"},"facts":[["Capital","Vienna"],["Population","9.2 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Area","83,879 km² (32,386 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","austria"],"abstract":"<b>Austria</b> was recognised twice in the 20th century, and the second time it had to be declared to have existed all along.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Emperor Charles I abdicated on 12 November 1918 and a republic was proclaimed the next day, out of the empire that had concluded an armistice with the Allies nine days earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States did not ratify the Treaty of Saint-Germain, which formally dissolved the Dual Monarchy, so a Joint Resolution of Congress ended the state of war on 2 July 1921 instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition followed on 24 August 1921 with the signing in the capital of a treaty establishing friendly relations, proclaimed in force on 8 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Arthur Hugh Frazier presented credentials as chargé d'affaires on 26 November 1921, and Albert Henry Washburn arrived as the first American minister the following June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>  <br><br>  After Germany annexed the country in March 1938 the legation closed on 30 April and became a consulate general, which itself closed on 9 July 1941 with every other American consulate in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the end of the Moscow Conference on 1 November 1943 the American, British and Soviet foreign ministers agreed that the annexation was null and void and that they favoured re-establishing a free and independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A provisional government was formed on 25 April 1945, a republic was proclaimed on 14 May, and on 8 August the country and its capital were divided into four occupation zones under an Allied Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Four Powers recognised the republic within its 1937 boundaries on 7 January 1946; accession to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade came on 19 October 1951, and the occupation ended with the Austrian State Treaty of 15 May 1955, which declared the state free, independent and neutral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> United Nations membership followed seven months after that treaty, under resolution 995 (X), and the population has risen from about 9.13 million in 2023 to about 9.21 million in 2025, the two international measures of the area differing by one square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Austria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/at.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Austria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/austria. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Austria and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/austria_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Austria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Austria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/Flag_of_Austria.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Austria.svg)","alt":"The flag of Austria: horizontal bands of red, white and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Hallstatt_-_Zentrum_.JPG/1920px-Hallstatt_-_Zentrum_.JPG","title":"Hallstatt, Austria","desc":"Hallstatt.","credit":"C.Stadler/Bwag, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hallstatt_-_Zentrum_.JPG","alt":"A view of Hallstatt."}},
{"id":"gw-540","num":540,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Luanda","answerText":"Luanda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liaison office opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Angola","dot":"Luanda"},"facts":[["Country","Angola"],["Population","8.0 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Where","on the Atlantic, in the north-west of the country"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","angola"],"abstract":"<b>Luanda</b> was the seat of a government the United Nations admitted more than sixteen years before the United States would recognise it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Portugal granted independence on 11 November 1975 while rival nationalist movements were fighting for control, and the American guide records that the Portuguese abandoned the city that year and the Popular Movement for the Liberation of Angola took power there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new state applied to join the United Nations at once: the Secretary-General circulated the president's letter of 22 April 1976 at the end of that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Security Council referred the application to its Committee on the Admission of New Members at its 1931st meeting on 22 June, and the committee met the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There the American representative urged that a decision be deferred, and the United Kingdom, France, Italy and Japan supported the deferral while stating that they fully supported the application and wished no delay of even a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>  <br><br>  Consideration was renewed on 19 November at the request of three members of the Council, a draft recommending admission was introduced with nine sponsors, and the Assembly admitted the country at its 84th plenary meeting on 1 December 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American position did not move until 1993, recognition being withheld over the presence of Cuban combat troops and their part in regional conflicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A liaison office opened in the city on 10 January 1992, the year of the first multiparty elections, with Jeffrey Millington as its director.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Bill Clinton formally recognised the government on 19 May 1993, using the announcement to urge the opposition movement UNITA to accept a negotiated settlement, and relations were established on 14 July 1994 when Ambassador Edmund T. De Jarnette presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city holds about 8.04 million of the country's 39.04 million people by the United Nations count, roughly one Angolan in five, although the World Bank measure of the largest city, drawn on a wider boundary, is higher still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Angola,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ao.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Angola,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/angola. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the People’s Republic of Angola to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 31/44, adopted at the 84th plenary meeting, 1 December 1976, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/199650. [Open access]","UN Secretary-General, “Application of the People’s Republic of Angola to Membership in the United Nations: Note by the Secretary-General,” A/31/85–S/12064, 30 April 1976, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/671486. [Open access]","UN Security Council Committee on the Admission of New Members, “Report Concerning the Application of the People’s Republic of Angola for Membership in the United Nations,” S/12109, 23 June 1976, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/225044. [Open access]","UN Security Council Committee on the Admission of New Members, “Report Concerning the Application of the People’s Republic of Angola for Membership in the United Nations,” S/12234, 22 November 1976, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/225171. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Angola,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AGO/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2024. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Luanda_Skyline_-_Angola_2015_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Luanda","desc":"Luanda, seen from a distance.","credit":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luanda_Skyline_-_Angola_2015_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Luanda."}},
{"id":"gw-099","num":99,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Belarus","answerText":"Belarus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">National Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 March 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 August 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 January 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Belarus"},"facts":[["Capital","Minsk"],["Population","9 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","207,600 km² (80,150 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","belarus"],"abstract":"<b>Belarus</b> held a seat at the United Nations for 46 years before the United States recognised it as a state, because it took that seat as a Soviet republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had been occupied by the Russian empire since the end of the 18th century, declared a short-lived National Republic on 25 March 1918, and was then forcibly absorbed by the Bolsheviks into what became the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its United Nations membership dates from 24 October 1945, the day the Charter entered into force, so it is among the original members while its own government answered to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Occupied by Nazi Germany, it was retaken in 1944 and remained under Soviet control for another 46 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sovereignty was declared on 27 July 1990 and independence from the Soviet Union on 25 August 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>  <br><br>  American recognition and diplomatic relations both came on 25 December 1991, announced in the address President George H. W. Bush made to his nation on the dissolution of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An embassy was established at the capital on 31 January 1992, with John Ford as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The relationship has been a poor one since: Ambassador Karen B. Stewart was recalled on 12 March 2008 after a threatened expulsion, and each side went on to cut the other's mission to five diplomats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its accession to the World Trade Organization has been open longer than most states have been members, its working party having been established on 27 October 1993 and the process still unfinished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two international counts of its population differ by about 88,000 and both sit close to nine million, and the two measures of its area agree to within 29 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Belarus,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/by.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Belarus,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/belarus. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accession status: Belarus,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_belarus_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Belarus,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BLR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Belarus,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BLR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/85/Flag_of_Belarus.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belarus.svg)","alt":"The flag of Belarus: a broad red band above a green band, with a red-on-white ornamental pattern down the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Bialowieza_National_Park_in_Poland0029.JPG/1920px-Bialowieza_National_Park_in_Poland0029.JPG","title":"Belovezhskaya Pushcha, Belarus","desc":"Belovezhskaya Pushcha.","credit":"Jacek Karczmarz, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bialowieza_National_Park_in_Poland0029.JPG","alt":"A view of Belovezhskaya Pushcha."}},
{"id":"gw-100","num":100,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Switzerland","answerText":"Switzerland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 1829</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1853</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 September 2002</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Switzerland"},"facts":[["Capital","Bern"],["Population","9 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Area","41,291 km² (15,943 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","switzerland"],"abstract":"<b>Switzerland</b> was recognised by the United States 173 years before it joined the United Nations, and it applied for that membership only in 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Congress of Vienna re-established its independence and neutrality in 1815, and its government was sending honorary consuls to Washington and New York as early as 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first official record of American recognition is the appointment of John Godfrey Boeker as consul general at Basel on 30 November 1829; he took up the post in October 1830.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A convention on the disposal of property was concluded on 18 May 1847, and diplomatic relations and an American legation both began on 29 June 1853, when Theodore S. Fay presented his credentials as minister resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy exactly a century later, Frances E. Willis being appointed the first American ambassador on 20 July 1953 and presenting her credentials on 9 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>  <br><br>  The application to the United Nations was made by letter of 20 June 2002 from the president and the chancellor of the Swiss Confederation, written on behalf of the Federal Council and circulated by the Secretary-General.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country on 10 September 2002 by resolution 57/1, more than half a century after the Charter entered into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Membership of the trading system had come far earlier, with accession to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 1 August 1966 and to the World Trade Organization on 1 July 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two international counts of its population sit between about 8.97 and 9.09 million, close enough that neither reading is a correction of the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its largest city is not its capital, and the gap is wide: the World Bank's measure of the largest city gives about 1.46 million against the roughly 426,000 the United Nations records for the seat of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Switzerland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ch.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Switzerland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/switzerland. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Swiss Confederation to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 57/1, 10 September 2002, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/473420. [Open access]","UN Secretary-General, “Application of the Swiss Confederation for Admission to Membership in the United Nations: Note by the Secretary-General,” A/56/1009–S/2002/801, 24 July 2002, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/469401. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Switzerland and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/switzerland_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Switzerland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Switzerland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Flag_of_Switzerland.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Switzerland.svg)","alt":"The flag of Switzerland: a square red field bearing a bold white cross."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Matterhorn_from_Domh%C3%BCtte_-_2.jpg","title":"Matterhorn, Switzerland","desc":"Matterhorn.","credit":"Photo: chil, on Camptocamp.org Derivative work: Zacharie Grossen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matterhorn_from_Domh%C3%BCtte_-_2.jpg","alt":"A view of Matterhorn."}},
{"id":"gw-541","num":541,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kyiv","answerText":"Kyiv","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 January 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Memorandum signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 December 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 2022</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ukraine","dot":"Kyiv"},"facts":[["Country","Ukraine"],["Population","3.0 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Where","on the Dnieper, in the north of the country"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","ukraine"],"abstract":"<b>Kyiv</b> is spelled two ways in the American recognition guide, whose heading for the mission there still reads \"Establishment of the American Embassy in Kiev (Kyiv), 1992\", while the United Nations statistical tables now write only the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy was established in the city on 23 January 1992, with Jon Gundersen as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition and diplomatic relations had come a month earlier, on 25 December 1991, announced in the address President George H. W. Bush made on the dissolution of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state governed from here had sat in the United Nations since 24 October 1945, taking its seat as a Soviet republic, so its membership preceded its recognition as an independent country by 46 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three years after the embassy opened, the government in this city gave up the nuclear weapons left on its territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>  <br><br>  On 5 December 1994 the presidents of Ukraine, the Russian Federation and the United States and the prime minister of the United Kingdom signed a memorandum on security assurances in connection with Ukraine's accession to the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons, circulated to the United Nations two days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In its first paragraph the other three reaffirmed their commitment to respect the independence and sovereignty and the existing borders of Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In its second they reaffirmed their obligation to refrain from the threat or use of force against its territorial integrity or political independence, and that none of their weapons would ever be used against it except in self-defence or otherwise in accordance with the Charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly returned to the question at an emergency special session in resolution ES-11/1 of 2 March 2022, headed \"Aggression against Ukraine\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city holds about 2.97 million people by the United Nations count and about 3.02 million by the World Bank's measure of the largest city, the two agreeing to within two per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ukraine,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ua.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ukraine,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ukraine. [Open access]","Permanent Representatives of the Russian Federation, Ukraine, the United Kingdom and the United States, “Memorandum on Security Assurances in Connection with Ukraine’s Accession to the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons,” letter dated 7 December 1994, A/49/765–S/1994/1399, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/169471. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Aggression against Ukraine,” resolution ES-11/1 of 2 March 2022, A/RES/ES-11/1, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3959039. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Ukraine,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/UKR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Aerial_view_kyiv.jpg/1920px-Aerial_view_kyiv.jpg","title":"Kyiv","desc":"Kyiv, seen from a distance.","credit":"Arne Müseler, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_kyiv.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kyiv."}},
{"id":"cnh-041","num":41,"category":"China · History","difficulty":2,"question":"Potters were at work in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> thousands of years before the first farmers were, at about sixteen sites whose vessels are the oldest known anywhere in the world.","questions":["A line along the Qinling mountains and the Huai River divides <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a northern half that farmed the millets and a southern half that grew rice.","Towards the end of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a city of some 300 hectares (740 acres) stood on the Yangtze delta behind dams raised about 5,100 years ago."],"answer":"Neolithic China","answerText":"Neolithic China","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9000 – 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Neolithic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7000 – 5000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Millet cultures</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liangzhu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 2300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"中國新石器時代","hanzi":"中国新石器时代","pinyin":"Zhōngguó Xīnshíqì Shídài","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Neolithic China</b> is the long stretch of the East Asian past in which people came to live in settled villages on cultivated crops and domestic animals, and finished their stone tools by grinding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pottery there is far older than farming: about sixteen sites have yielded vessels made between roughly 20,000 and 10,000 years ago, which is the earliest record of the craft anywhere in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settled communities had formed by about 11,000 years ago in the Yellow River valley of the north and the Yangtze valley of the south, and those two regions divide the period still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The line between them runs east and west along the Qinling mountains and the Huai River; north of it the staple crops were the millets, and south of it rice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Early Neolithic proper, about 7000 to 5000 BCE, is dated by the first clear evidence of those domesticated grains and by pots varied enough to have been made for particular jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Storage pits at Cishan in Hebei held broomcorn millet between about 10,300 and 8,700 years ago, and the site is reported to have yielded more than 50,000 kilograms (110,000 pounds) of grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the south the lower Yangtze is an independent centre of rice domestication, where rice was being harvested by about 10,000 years ago in a process that ran on for millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pigs were being managed by roughly 9,000 years ago, perhaps in several places at once, and in the north they came to be fed on millet, as isotopes show the dogs were too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Liangzhu, on the Yangtze delta, held a city of some 300 hectares (740 acres) behind dams raised 5,100 years ago, and buried finely carved jades with its richest dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Social complexity and urban growth spread through the late Neolithic Longshan centuries in north and south alike, and it was then that wheat, cattle, sheep and goats reached northern China from western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","china"],"sources":["Zhang Chi, “The Neolithic Stone Implements of China: A Review,” <i>Internet Archaeology</i> 26 (2009), sec. 1, https://doi.org/10.11141/ia.26.32. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “The Origins of Specialized Pottery and Diverse Alcohol Fermentation Techniques in Early Neolithic China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 26 (2019): 12767–74, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6600912/. [Open access]","Hitomi Hongo, Hiroki Kikuchi, and Hiroo Nasu, “Beginning of Pig Management in Neolithic China: Comparison of Domestication Processes between Northern and Southern Regions,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 30–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214443/. [Open access]","Houyuan Lu et al., “Earliest Domestication of Common Millet (<i>Panicum miliaceum</i>) in East Asia Extended to 10,000 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 18 (2009): 7367–72, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2678631/. [Open access]","Jiajing Wang et al., “New Evidence for Rice Harvesting in the Early Neolithic Lower Yangtze River, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 12 (2022): e0278200, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0278200. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens et al., “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Bin Liu et al., “Earliest Hydraulic Enterprise in China, 5,100 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13637–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748178/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/20241110_Painted_Pottery_Basin%2C_Dahecun_Site%2C_Yangshao_Culture.jpg/1280px-20241110_Painted_Pottery_Basin%2C_Dahecun_Site%2C_Yangshao_Culture.jpg","title":"Painted pottery basin from the Dahecun site, Yangshao culture","desc":"A painted earthenware basin excavated at the Dahecun site in Zhengzhou, Henan, and held in the site museum there. The record names the site and the Yangshao culture and gives no date for the vessel itself. Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20241110_Painted_Pottery_Basin,_Dahecun_Site,_Yangshao_Culture.jpg","alt":"A wide shallow earthenware basin lit against a dark background, mended from sherds with the cracks and fills showing. Its flaring rim and outer wall carry a broad painted band of black and red: rows of dark ovals with pale centres, hatched leaf shapes and fine parallel lines. The inside is plain buff clay."}},
{"id":"cnh-043","num":43,"category":"China · History","difficulty":2,"question":"In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the northern crop was sown on loess and needed no water works, while the southern one stood in fields that had to be built and drained first.","questions":["At Tanghu on the Middle Yellow River both crops of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were being grown together by 7,800 years ago, the rice a newcomer carried up from the Yangtze.","The bones of Yangtze delta farmers carry more marks of stress than northern ones, which is one measure of what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost the people who lived by it."],"answer":"millet and rice farming","answerText":"millet and rice farming","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Millet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 5800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First rice fields</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bunded paddies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 2000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"粟作與稻作","hanzi":"粟作与稻作","pinyin":"sùzuò yǔ dàozuò","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Millet and rice farming</b> are the two systems early China ran on: dry-sown grain on the loess of the north, and a crop grown standing in water in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because millet yields less from the same ground than wet rice, the northern millet cultures of 5000 to 3000 BCE covered far more country than their rice-growing neighbours on the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest rice fields, from about 6,000 years ago, were little more than pits dug in freshwater wetland with simple water controls, none of them larger than 10 square metres (110 square feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over the next two thousand years they became bunded rectangular paddies engineered for irrigation and drainage, which is to say fields that had to be built before they could be sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Millet needed none of that, and spread from a crescent around the Bohai Sea, where it was first cultivated about 5800 BCE, across eastern Asia in fits and starts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That spread quickened in the 4th millennium BCE, and in northern China millet farming carried a population that grew almost exponentially from 6000 to 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice went the other way, following the coast and the rivers south, crossing the Taiwan Strait about 5,000 years ago and reaching mainland Southeast Asia in the millennium after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two met in the middle: at Tanghu on the Middle Yellow River, broomcorn millet and rice were being grown together by 7,800 years ago, the rice a newcomer from the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the two did to the people who grew them differed: Neolithic rice farmers of the Yangtze delta carry more marks of physical stress on their bones than the millet farmers of the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The marks are heaviest in the late Neolithic, when the scale of paddy cultivation was growing and the diet came to turn on rice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["practice","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china"],"sources":["Christian Leipe et al., “Discontinuous Spread of Millet Agriculture in Eastern Asia and Prehistoric Population Dynamics,” <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 9 (2019): eaax6225, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6760930/. [Open access]","Ting Ma et al., “Holocene Coastal Evolution Preceded the Expansion of Paddy Field Rice Farming,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 117, no. 39 (2020): 24138–43, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7533829/. [Open access]","Jianping Zhang et al., “Early Mixed Farming of Millet and Rice 7800 Years Ago in the Middle Yellow River Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 12 (2012): e52146, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0052146. [Open access]","Kenji Okazaki et al., “Paleo-Health of Neolithic Wet-Rice Farmers in the Yangtze River Delta: A Comparison with Early Millet Farmers in Northern China,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 132, no. 2 (2024): 65–77, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13134063/. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens et al., “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Panicum_miliaceum_subsp._miliaceum_sl18.jpg/1280px-Panicum_miliaceum_subsp._miliaceum_sl18.jpg","title":"A panicle of broomcorn millet","desc":"A ripening head of broomcorn millet, Panicum miliaceum, one of the two millets of early northern China. The photograph is a modern one of the species growing beside a cemetery in Vienna rather than of any Chinese crop. Stefan.lefnaer, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panicum_miliaceum_subsp._miliaceum_sl18.jpg","alt":"A single arching head of broomcorn millet against a clear blue sky, its fine branching stalks hung with hundreds of pale green grains, rising from a broad green leaf and stem at the left of the frame."}},
{"id":"cnh-044","num":44,"category":"China · History","difficulty":4,"question":"About thirty flutes cut from the wing bones of red-crowned cranes come from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them drilled with seven holes and still playable.","questions":["Residues in sixteen vessels from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are of a drink of rice, honey and fruit that was being fermented as early as the 7th millennium BCE.","Silk protein has been found in three tombs at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that are 8,500 years old, alongside bone needles and rough tools for weaving."],"answer":"Jiahu","answerText":"Jiahu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7000 – 5500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fermented drink</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 7000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silk in tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"賈湖","hanzi":"贾湖","pinyin":"Jiǎhú","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jiahu</b> was a village in central Henan, on the upper reaches of the Huai, lived in from about 9,000 to 7,500 years ago in three long spells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its fields grew the earliest domesticated rice known in northern China, a crop of the Yangtze whose presence so far north is usually read as an introduction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sixteen of its pots have been tested for what they held, and the answer was a fermented drink of rice, honey and fruit — hawthorn, grape, or both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was being made as early as the 7th millennium BCE, and the vessels are shaped for the work: perforated basins and narrow-mouthed jars with high flaring necks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three of its graves have yielded the protein of silk, 8,500 years old and the earliest direct evidence of the material, and with it bone needles and rough tools for weaving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some thirty flutes have come out of the site, each cut from the ulna of a red-crowned crane with the joints sawn off and finger holes drilled down the shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The holes number two, five, six, seven or eight, and most of the flutes have seven; marks left on the bone show that the spacing was set out before any hole was cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The best preserved of them was laid in a grave and still plays, giving something close to a seven-note scale — the oldest playable instrument known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site has also been credited with possibly the earliest Chinese pictographic writing, a claim that the works reporting it state as a question rather than a finding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The climate around it was warm and damp, which is why mulberry grew there for the silkworm, and why the village could settle and farm at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","music"],"sources":["Yuxuan Gong et al., “Biomolecular Evidence of Silk from 8,500 Years Ago,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 12 (2016): e0168042, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0168042. [Open access]","Patrick E. McGovern et al., “Fermented Beverages of Pre- and Proto-historic China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 101, no. 51 (2004): 17593–98, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC539767/. [Open access]","Henan Museum 河南博物院, “贾湖骨笛 [The Jiahu Bone Flute],” accessed 27 August 2026, https://www.chnmus.net/ch/collection/treasure/details.html?id=508164979145447651. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “The Origins of Specialized Pottery and Diverse Alcohol Fermentation Techniques in Early Neolithic China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 26 (2019): 12767–74, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6600912/. [Open access]","Jianping Zhang et al., “Early Mixed Farming of Millet and Rice 7800 Years Ago in the Middle Yellow River Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 12 (2012): e52146, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0052146. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/20260520_Bone_flute_from_Jiahu.jpg","title":"A bone flute from Jiahu","desc":"A bone flute from the Jiahu site, in the collection of the Henan Museum. Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20260520_Bone_flute_from_Jiahu.jpg","alt":"A slender tube of pale bone lying horizontally on two small clear supports against a dark background, gently curved along its length and pierced by a row of seven round finger holes, with a smaller eighth hole beside the last of them."},"locator":{"name":"Jiahu","at":[113.667,33.613]}},
{"id":"cnh-045","num":45,"category":"China · History","difficulty":3,"question":"More than three thousand sites of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are known in Henan alone, and its influence reached seven regions beyond the middle Yellow River.","questions":["Pig bones from the Xipo cemetery carry the signature of a millet diet, which is one measure of how closely the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> managed the animals that fed it.","Migrants from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the highlands of northwest Sichuan, where they brewed with a mould-and-rice starter as they had done on the Yellow River."],"answer":"Yangshao culture","answerText":"Yangshao culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foxtail leads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 3500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Westward move</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 – 3000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"仰韶文化","hanzi":"仰韶文化","pinyin":"Yǎngsháo wénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Yangshao culture</b> is the Neolithic culture of the middle Yellow River, dating from about 7,000 to 5,000 years ago and centred on what are now Henan, Shaanxi and Shanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than three thousand of its sites are known in Henan alone, and its influence reached beyond the middle Yellow River into seven regions around it, from Hebei and Ningxia to Qinghai, Hubei and Sichuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its villages were permanent, its pots painted, its pigs and dogs domestic, and its fields dry-farmed on the two millets with a little rice besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Broomcorn millet led at first and foxtail overtook it, a change that had happened on the western Loess Plateau by about 5,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes from the culture’s core and from its edges are alike enough that its spread is read as a movement of people rather than as neighbours copying a fashion in pots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pigs were kept in earnest: at Xipo their bones carry the signature of a diet of millet, and the foddering grew heavier through the period as pork became the main meat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Set against the bones of the people buried in the same cemetery, the pig data show animals fed by hand rather than left to forage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Middle Neolithic genomes from the Yellow and the Yangtze basins are markedly different from each other, and yet genes moved both ways between them, so the millet north and the rice south were in touch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some of these people went west and south between about 6,000 and 5,000 years ago, as far as the highlands of northwest Sichuan, where they seem to have joined local foragers and taken up wild foods beside their millet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They carried their rituals with them, brewing with a starter of mould and rice for what look like communal drinking festivals danced as well as drunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","agriculture"],"sources":["Lei Sun et al., “The Demic Expansion of Yangshao Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 186, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12218936/. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “Archaeological Evidence for Initial Migration of Neolithic Proto Sino-Tibetan Speakers from Yellow River Valley to Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 51 (2022): e2212006119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9907151/. [Open access]","Quan Zhang et al., “Stable Isotopes Reveal Intensive Pig Husbandry Practices in the Middle Yellow River Region by the Yangshao Period (7000–5000 BP),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257524, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257524. [Open access]","Yishi Yang et al., “Shift in Subsistence Crop Dominance from Broomcorn Millet to Foxtail Millet around 5500 BP in the Western Loess Plateau,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 939340, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2022.939340. [Open access]","Jianxue Xiong et al., “The Genomic History of East Asian Middle Neolithic Millet- and Rice-Agricultural Populations,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 5, no. 10 (2025): 100976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12791001/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Jiangzhai_settlement_model%2C_Yangshao_culture%2C_Lintong%2C_Shaanxi.jpg","title":"Model of the Yangshao village at Jiangzhai","desc":"A museum model of the Yangshao village at Jiangzhai in Lintong, Shaanxi, shown in the National Museum of China. It is a modern reconstruction rather than the excavated remains, and the small fenced enclosures in it are labelled as animal pens. Prof. Gary Lee Todd, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiangzhai_settlement_model,_Yangshao_culture,_Lintong,_Shaanxi.jpg","alt":"A large model village seen from above: scores of small round huts with domed thatched roofs, and a few square ones, set around an open central space of bare earth. Paths run between them, a watercourse curves round the outside, and two small fenced enclosures carry printed labels."}},
{"id":"cnh-046","num":46,"category":"China · History","question":"Earthen structures read as animal pens stood in the middle of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Yangshao village at Xi'an whose house floors were fired to a hard red crust.","questions":["Archaeologists have given the opening stage of the Yangshao culture the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Shaanxi village lived in between roughly 4800 and 4300 BCE.","Bone chemistry from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives a mixed carbon signature rather than a pure millet one, so wild plants still fed its farmers on the Guanzhong Plain."],"answer":"Banpo","answerText":"Banpo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4800 – 4300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Xi'an, Shaanxi</span></div>","traditional":"半坡","hanzi":"半坡","pinyin":"Bànpō","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","agriculture"],"abstract":"<b>Banpo</b> is a Neolithic village on the Guanzhong Plain at Xi'an in Shaanxi, and among the most fully uncovered settlements of the Yangshao culture that farmed the middle Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Carbon and nitrogen measured on human bone from the site put its occupation between roughly 4800 and 4300 BCE, early in the long Yangshao sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name has since been borrowed twice over, for the Banpo phase that opens the Yangshao culture<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and for the Banpo culture whose cemeteries and pottery turn up across the Guanzhong region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Villages of that phase were laid out to a pattern, the houses standing inside a ring ditch with the burial ground and the pottery kilns set apart from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Their pots are recognisable at a glance: bottles with pointed bottoms, round-bottomed basins and bowls, narrow-mouthed jars with swelling bellies, and covers made for burial urns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In the middle of the settlement, as in neighbouring Jiangzhai, excavators found earthen structures they read as pens, which would mean the village kept its pigs together rather than household by household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Broad tracts of red burnt earth survive in the floors of its houses, showing that a floor could already be fired deliberately to harden and warm it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> What the villagers actually ate is less tidy than a field of millet suggests, since the carbon in their bones averages about −14.8 per mil, a mixed signal that leaves wild plants a considerable share of the diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The single Banpo individual whose protein and energy sources have been plotted separately falls between the C3 and C4 lines on both counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The phase did not last, and pottery of the Dongzhuang type marks the passage from Banpo to the Miaodigou culture that took its place;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> a Y-chromosome lineage recovered from a Banpo skeleton is now read as a marker of Yangshao millet farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Xinyi Liu and Rachel E. B. Reid, “The Prehistoric Roots of Chinese Cuisines: Mapping Staple Food Systems of China, 6000 BC–220 AD,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0240930, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0240930. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens, Yijie Zhuang, and Dorian Q. Fuller, “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Quan Zhang et al., “Stable Isotopes Reveal Intensive Pig Husbandry Practices in the Middle Yellow River Region by the Yangshao Period (7000–5000 BP),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257524, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257524. [Open access]","Xin Wang et al., “Millet Manuring as a Driving Force for the Late Neolithic Agricultural Expansion of North China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 5552, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5882897/. [Open access]","Minglei Lv et al., “Ancient Genomes from the Tang Dynasty Capital Reveal the Genetic Legacy of Trans-Eurasian Communication at the Eastern End of Silk Road,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 22 (2024): 267, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11577736/. [Open access]","Yishi Yang et al., “Shift in Subsistence Crop Dominance from Broomcorn Millet to Foxtail Millet around 5500 BP in the Western Loess Plateau,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 939340, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2022.939340. [Open access]","Wenqing Liu, “The Evolution of Cold Adaptation Technology within Ancient Buildings in Amur River Basin Viewed from Archaeology,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 21 (2022): 14470, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9656652/. [Open access]","Mengge Wang et al., “Multiple Human Population Movements and Cultural Dispersal Events Shaped the Landscape of Chinese Paternal Heritage,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 41, no. 7 (2024): msae122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11232699/. [Open access]","Jianxue Xiong et al., “The Genomic History of East Asian Middle Neolithic Millet- and Rice-Agricultural Populations,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 5, no. 10 (2025): 100976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12791001/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Banpo_utgr%C3%A4vning.jpg/1920px-Banpo_utgr%C3%A4vning.jpg","title":"The excavated village at Banpo","desc":"House floors, storage pits and pottery vessels left in place under the museum hall built over part of the settlement. Photograph by Bairuilong, 2014, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Bairuilong, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banpo_utgr%C3%A4vning.jpg","alt":"A wide excavation floor of pale earth under artificial light, showing the sunken outlines of round and rectangular house floors, several pits, and a group of large pottery jars standing where they were found."},"locator":{"name":"Banpo","at":[109.0512,34.2732]}},
{"id":"cnh-047","num":47,"category":"China · History","question":"Low tiered platforms holding jade burials, and hardly any walls or moats, mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which flourished in the Liao valleys of northeastern China from about 4500 BCE.","questions":["A wattle-and-daub building at Niuheliang held a life-size female statue, the Goddess Temple of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose burial offerings are jade and almost never anything useful.","Ritual at the great centres of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stopped around 3350 BCE, yet its villages carried on for centuries, so its end was no sudden collapse."],"answer":"Hongshan culture","answerText":"Hongshan culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4500 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ritual peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3850 – 3350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"紅山文化","hanzi":"红山文化","pinyin":"Hóngshān wénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","religion"],"abstract":"The <b>Hongshan culture</b> is a Neolithic culture of the Liao and Daling river valleys in northeastern China, dated between about 4500 and 3000 BCE and counted among the earliest complex societies in East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its remains cover more than 200,000 square kilometres (77,000 square miles) of the region,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and it draws attention because it rose well before dynastic civilisation appeared on the Central Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes tie its westernmost site in Hebei closely to the Liaoning core, so the culture is read as having spread with people rather than as a fashion copied by neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What sets it apart from its contemporaries on the Yellow River is what it lacks: no massive walls, no moats, no grand houses, and public building instead as low platforms raised in circular or square tiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hundreds of painted pottery cylinders were sometimes built into these platforms, which held burial crypts and slab graves whose offerings are almost all jade and hardly ever anything useful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That absence of everyday goods is read as marking ritual specialists rather than the merely rich, and a wattle-and-daub building at Niuheliang with a life-size female statue has been named the Goddess Temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ordinary life was plainer than the monuments suggest, the houses round or square, half sunk into the ground and about 20 square metres (215 square feet), timber-framed with daubed walls and a hearth in the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Villages farmed millet, gathered nuts, kept pigs and sheep, hunted deer and worked leather, stone and clay; a survey near Chifeng found some twenty clusters of them, each perhaps 500 people, none ruling the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Niuheliang scatters its ceremonial architecture and jade burials across more than sixteen localities in poor hill country, and looks less like a town than a pilgrimage centre for a whole region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its end is not the total collapse it is often called: ritual at the great centres peaked between roughly 3850 and 3350 BCE and then stopped, while people went on living in small settlements for centuries after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Christian E. Peterson et al., “Hongshan Chiefly Communities in Neolithic Northeastern China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 13 (2010): 5756–61, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2851873/. [Open access]","Rui Wang et al., “Genetic Formation of Neolithic Hongshan People and Demic Expansion of Hongshan Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 42, no. 6 (2025): msaf139, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12204185/. [Open access]","Chao Ning et al., “Ancient Genomes from Northern China Suggest Links between Subsistence Changes and Human Migration,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 2700, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7264253/. [Open access]","Deke Xu et al., “Synchronous 500-Year Oscillations of Monsoon Climate and Human Activity in Northeast Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 10 (2019): 4105, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6739325/. [Open access]","C. Adam Berrey, Robert D. Drennan, and Christian E. Peterson, “Local Economies and Household Spacing in Early Chiefdom Communities,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 5 (2021): e0252532, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0252532. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Hongshan_Culture_Jade_Pig_Dragon_01.jpg/1920px-Hongshan_Culture_Jade_Pig_Dragon_01.jpg","title":"Hongshan jade pig-dragon","desc":"A coiled jade carving with a snouted head, a large central perforation and a small hole for suspension — the form by which Hongshan jades are recognised. Shanghai Museum. Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, CC0.","credit":"Gary Lee Todd, Wikimedia Commons, CC0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hongshan_Culture_Jade_Pig_Dragon_01.jpg","alt":"A pale green jade ornament shaped like a curled-up animal, its snouted head with pricked ears meeting its tail, pierced through the middle by a wide round hole and near the head by a small one, standing on a clear mount against a dark blue ground."},"locator":{"name":"The Hongshan culture","at":[118.8825,42.255],"kind":"region","area":[[117.2,41.6],[117.35,42.65],[118.85,43.6],[120.55,43.35],[122.35,42.55],[122.6,41.55],[121.45,40.75],[119.75,40.6],[118.3,40.9]]}},
{"id":"cnh-048","num":48,"category":"China · History","question":"Earth walls enclosing 300 hectares, dams holding the water off the low ground and 30 kilometres of canals make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the builder of China's earliest waterworks.","questions":["Human bones worked into tools went into the canals of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for over two centuries, a practice found in no earlier Neolithic culture of China.","An unusually wet spell around 2400 BCE ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose capital on the Yangtze delta was abandoned and then buried under a metre of clay."],"answer":"Liangzhu culture","answerText":"Liangzhu culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 2300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dams built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3250 – 2850 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City abandoned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2400 – 2370 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"良渚文化","hanzi":"良渚文化","pinyin":"Liángzhǔ wénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","technology"],"abstract":"The <b>Liangzhu culture</b> is the late Neolithic culture of the Yangtze delta, dated between about 3300 and 2300 BCE, and it left one of the earliest cities in East Asia and the earliest formal waterworks known in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its capital, near what is now Hangzhou, was enclosed by earth walls around some 300 hectares (740 acres), roughly 1.9 by 1.7 kilometres (1.2 by 1.1 miles), with the palace platform of Mojiaoshan raised in the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beyond the walls stood dams and levees built to hold water off the low ground, the Tangshan levees alone running at least 5 kilometres (3 miles) and standing 2 to 7 metres (7 to 23 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The builders bundled clay into grass-wrapped packages like sandbags, and the knots in that grass survive well enough to be radiocarbon dated, which puts the high dams at about 3250 to 2850 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Building them has been estimated at 3,000 people working nearly eight years to shift 2.88 million cubic metres (3.8 million cubic yards) of earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some 30 kilometres (19 miles) of moats, ditches and canals ran inside and around the city, controlled at eight water gates, and the delta's rice fields were drained and manured behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That scale has made Liangzhu a test case for the old argument that water control is what builds despotic states, though its own record puts ritual at least as far forward as engineering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Human bones worked into tools were dropped into the canals for more than two centuries, a practice unknown in earlier Neolithic China and perhaps a mark of what city life did to the standing of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Outside the core the pull was weaker: at Jiangzhuang the villagers hunted deer, kept a mixed herd of pigs and wild boar and stayed out of intensive farming altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city was abandoned in an unusually wet spell dated to roughly 2400 to 2370 BCE and buried under a metre (3 feet) of clay, and the delta's cultures then failed together in the aridity that set in around 2050 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Bin Liu et al., “Earliest Hydraulic Enterprise in China, 5,100 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13637–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748178/. [Open access]","Haiwei Zhang et al., “Collapse of the Liangzhu and Other Neolithic Cultures in the Lower Yangtze Region in Response to Climate Change,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 48 (2021): eabi9275, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8626068/. [Open access]","Junmei Sawada et al., “Worked Human Bones and the Rise of Urban Society in the Neolithic Liangzhu Culture, East Asia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 31441, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15673-7. [Open access]","Yifei Liu et al., “Herding or Hunting? Animal Exploitation at Jiangzhuang (Jiangsu, China) During the Liangzhu Period (3300–2300 BC),” <i>Animals</i> 14, no. 23 (2024): 3461, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11640202/. [Open access]","Vernon L. Scarborough, “The Hydraulic Lift of Early States Societies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13600–601, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748232/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Liangzhu_Archaeological_Site%2C_2019-07-20_05.jpg/1920px-Liangzhu_Archaeological_Site%2C_2019-07-20_05.jpg","title":"A section cut through the Liangzhu city wall","desc":"The south wall of the city, opened and roofed for display, showing the layer of loose rock the earth rampart was built on. Photograph by Siyuwj, 2019, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Siyuwj, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liangzhu_Archaeological_Site,_2019-07-20_05.jpg","alt":"A wide trench cut down through banked earth under a timber shelter, its floor paved with a long spread of rough pale stones, with a flight of steps running up the near side and visitors looking down from a walkway above."},"locator":{"name":"The Liangzhu culture","at":[119.6667,30.15],"kind":"region","area":[[119,30.15],[119.35,30.95],[119.9,31.6],[120.75,32],[121.6,31.8],[122,31.15],[121.85,30.45],[121.35,29.95],[120.55,29.8],[119.65,29.85]]}},
{"id":"cnh-049","num":49,"category":"China · History","question":"A tube square outside and round within, called a cong, and a pierced disc called a bi are the two commonest shapes taken by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the ritual stone of a delta culture.","questions":["Bamboo drills turned with wet quartz sand, and cords dragged under a paste of emery, are how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was worn into shape, since nephrite is far too tough to carve.","Grave M3 at Sidun held 24 discs, 33 tubes and 14 tools of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around a man of about twenty, several of them burned or deliberately broken."],"answer":"Liangzhu jade","answerText":"Liangzhu jade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 2300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sidun M3</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2320 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"良渚玉器","hanzi":"良渚玉器","pinyin":"Liángzhǔ yùqì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","prehistory","archaeology","art","china","religion"],"abstract":"<b>Liangzhu jade</b> is the worked nephrite buried with the dead of the Liangzhu culture of the Yangtze delta between about 3300 and 2300 BCE, and it is what that culture is chiefly known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two shapes carry most of it: the <i>cong</i>, a tube square on the outside and round and hollow within, and the <i>bi</i>, a wide flat disc pierced by a small central hole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The cong originates with Liangzhu, and a cong and a bi together are the essential ritual furniture of a high-ranking grave, the largest discs laid on the chest of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many cong carry a face in relief around the corners of the tube, a figure set over a beast mask, and tools of flint and shark teeth found beside the jades may be what cut those lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither shape has a use, and the pair has been called the earliest Chinese objects given forms wholly divorced from any function, ornamental or utilitarian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Nephrite is too tough to carve, so it was worn away instead: bamboo drills turned with water and quartz sand, and cords dragged through the stone under a paste of quartz and emery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A long hole was bored from both ends at once, and where the two bores met they left a ridge part way down and elliptical grooves along the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Workshops lie among the hills and rivers west of Lake Tai, within reach of the stone, the water, the sand and the grinding slabs, and unfinished pieces and waste turn up at several of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Tomb M12 at Fanshan the masks on several jades are cut so nearly alike that one pair of hands may have made them all, perhaps those of the man buried there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grave M3 at Sidun, dated to about 2320 BCE, held 24 bi, 33 cong, 14 jade tools and 9 ornaments around a man of about twenty, five cong broken in half and many of the jades burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Bin Liu et al., “Earliest Hydraulic Enterprise in China, 5,100 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13637–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748178/. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Jean M. Green, “Unraveling the Enigma of the Bi: The Spindle Whorl as the Model of the Ritual Disk,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 32, no. 1 (1993): 105–24, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17022. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Cleveland Museum of Art, “Ceremonial Disc (Bi) 玉璧, 3300–2200 BCE,” accession 1917.974, accessed 28 August 2026, https://clevelandart.org/art/1917.974. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Jade_Cong_King%2C_2018-06-09_01.jpg/1920px-Jade_Cong_King%2C_2018-06-09_01.jpg","title":"The King of Cong, from Fanshan tomb M12","desc":"The largest and most finely worked Liangzhu cong known, square outside and bored round through the middle, with stacked faces carved at each corner. Zhejiang Provincial Museum. Photograph by Siyuwj, 2018, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Siyuwj, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jade_Cong_King,_2018-06-09_01.jpg","alt":"A squat block of pale cream jade, square in plan with rounded corners and a wide round hole bored down through it, each corner cut into stacked bands of a face with barred eyes and a nose, the flat surfaces covered in fine incised spirals."}},
{"id":"cnh-050","num":50,"category":"China · History","question":"Ten or so walled towns across Henan and Shandong, standing in clusters and ringed by moats, are the mark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the late Neolithic Yellow River.","questions":["A ditch cut into the wall base at Taosi held 31 people of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some of their skulls broken by blows, and a woman with an ox horn in her pelvis.","Rather than one people, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a set of regional groups — Hougang II, Wangwan III, Taosi, Kexingzhuang II — named after a town in Shandong."],"answer":"Longshan culture","answerText":"Longshan culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early–Middle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 – 2500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2500 – 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Erlitou follows</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"龍山文化","hanzi":"龙山文化","pinyin":"Lóngshān wénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>Longshan culture</b> is the late Neolithic of the Yellow River plains, about 3000 to 1900 BCE, and is less one culture than a set of regional ones sharing a way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are told apart by region: Hougang II in northern Henan, Wangwan III near Luoyang, Taosi in Shanxi, Kexingzhuang II in Shaanxi, and the Shandong Longshan that named the whole horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That name comes from Chengziyai near Longshan town in Shandong, 17.6 hectares (43 acres) of houses and kilns inside a wall that survives 2 to 3 metres (7 to 10 feet) high and once stood about 6.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten or so walled sites are known across the Henan and Shandong heartland and others as far off as Hubei and Sichuan, standing in regional clusters; unwalled settlements of the same date are smaller and plainer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The walled ones appear to grow larger through the period, read as a hierarchy forming, with major centres coming to hold power over minor ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Walls were of rammed earth in the south and east and of stone further north, mostly rectangular, moated and pierced by one to three gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Raising one took most of the people who lived there, and was done with ceremony: at Bianxianwang pots stood upright in the wall base beside a pig of thirteen to eighteen months and a dog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later the offerings are human — two children in ceramic coffins beneath Guchengzhai's southern wall, a child at Wangchenggang whose skull had been parted from the neck — and those killed are thought to have been war captives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A ditch cut into the wall base at Taosi held 31 people, some of the skulls broken by blows, along with a woman of about 35 with an ox horn driven into her pelvis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Metal was only just beginning: one slice of it from Zhoujiazhuang is the sole Longshan metallurgical find near the Zhongtiao mountains, and when Taosi fell its basin emptied, while the centres of the Central Plain went on moving for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Paola Dematté, “Longshan-Era Urbanism: The Role of Cities in Predynastic China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 38, no. 2 (1999): 119–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17124. [Open access]","Qian Yang, “Conflict and Identity: The Ritual of Wall Construction in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76786. [Open access]","Anne P. Underhill, “Variation in Settlements during the Longshan Period of Northern China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 197–228, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17047. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1d/Taosi_plan.png","title":"Plan of the walled town at Taosi","desc":"The early-period enclosure, the far larger middle-period city that replaced it, a small walled section and the cemetery, set between the Nan River and Song Village Creek. Drawn after Huang Kejia et al.; CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"पाटलिपुत्र, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taosi_plan.png","alt":"A line plan on white, with pale blue watercourses running through it and two red outlines: a smaller rounded enclosure labelled early period city site, and a much larger one around it labelled middle period city site, with a cemetery marked at its south-east corner and a one-kilometre scale bar below."}},
{"id":"cnh-051","num":51,"category":"China · History","question":"Walls up to 3.6 centimetres thick, pinched by hand and fired below 700 °C, are how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins, in the hands of foragers rather than farmers.","questions":["Kilns of wood and mud worked at about 620 °C, and clay dug from the soils nearest each village, lie behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> through its long sequence of wares.","The smear of a hand smoothing wet clay, and no pattern at all, is the only mark on the earliest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which foragers were making twelve thousand years ago."],"answer":"Chinese Neolithic pottery","answerText":"Chinese Neolithic pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early wares</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 – 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Specialised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 7000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wheel-made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"新石器時代陶器","hanzi":"新石器时代陶器","pinyin":"Xīnshíqì shídài táoqì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","prehistory","archaeology","technology","china","art"],"abstract":"<b>Chinese Neolithic pottery</b> is the fired clay of China's long Neolithic, a craft older there than farming and the chief means by which archaeologists still tell one culture of the period from another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest pieces are thick and crumbly, their walls 2.9 to 3.6 centimetres (1.1 to 1.4 inches), tempered with unsieved crushed calcite or quartz and cracked across the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were pinched into shape by hand and fired at roughly 600 to 700 °C (1,100 to 1,300 °F), and the earliest of all, from Zengpiyan, probably below 250 °C (480 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their makers were foragers rather than farmers, comfortable but without ranks, and the freshwater shellfish they had begun to eat is part of why the pots appear when they do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Slab building and then coiling came after the pinching, and the marks on the earliest surfaces are the smear of a hand smoothing the clay rather than any decoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest pots of all come from north of the Nanling mountains, and the Yangtze cultures that follow appear to descend from that northern tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the early Neolithic the shapes have become specialised, made for particular jobs rather than one vessel doing everything, and some of them were built for fermenting drink.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later the wheel arrives, and Longshan jars from Henan carry its marks inside the belly where the potter's hand could not reach, beside grey wares and black ones polished plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The clay was dug close by: three Longshan sites in one district of Henan differ enough in composition to show each choosing its own soils, and one of them, surveyed over 16,800 square metres (4.2 acres), has yielded no kiln at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kilns were built of wood and mud, and one in Fujian was worked at about 620 °C (1,150 °F), which is what Neolithic kilns reach across Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Tracey L-D Lu, “Early Pottery in South China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 49, no. 1 (2010), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29097. [Open access]","Guishan Chen et al., “The Firing Temperatures of Burnt Clay from the Chinese Neolithic Cultural Relics and Its Paleoenvironmental Implications,” <i>Heliyon</i> 9, no. 10 (2023): e20628, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10569999/. [Open access]","Linyu Xia et al., “Compositional Analysis of Longshan Period Pottery and Ceramic Raw Materials in the Yongcheng Region, Henan Province,” <i>Materials</i> 18, no. 12 (2025): 2681, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12194330/. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “The Origins of Specialized Pottery and Diverse Alcohol Fermentation Techniques in Early Neolithic China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 26 (2019): 12767–74, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6600912/. [Open access]","Chi Zhang and Hsiao-chun Hung, “The Neolithic of Southern China—Origin, Development, and Dispersal,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 47, no. 2 (2008): 299–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17291. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/The_Pot_of_Yuchanyan._Hunan_Museum.jpg/1920px-The_Pot_of_Yuchanyan._Hunan_Museum.jpg","title":"The pot from Yuchanyan cave","desc":"Among the oldest pottery vessels known anywhere, rebuilt from its sherds: the dark original fragments are set into a white restoration. Hunan Museum. Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Gary Lee Todd, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Pot_of_Yuchanyan._Hunan_Museum.jpg","alt":"A wide conical vessel standing on a clear ring, most of it filled in with smooth white plaster, with a broad band of the original dark grey-brown potsherds running down one side, their surfaces roughly textured and cracked."}},
{"id":"cnh-052","num":52,"category":"China · History","question":"Chinese tradition puts the first dynasty on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Zhongyuan of the middle Yellow River, and has complexity spreading outward from there by conquest and imitation.","questions":["Erlitou, a site of some 400 hectares near Yanshi, is the earliest unbroken link on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between its Neolithic villages and the Shang layers above them.","Walled towns in Shandong, Hubei and Sichuan, and a city behind dams on the Yangtze delta, show that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not the only place where complexity rose."],"answer":"Central Plain","answerText":"Central Plain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2100 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Erlitou</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"中原","hanzi":"中原","pinyin":"Zhōngyuán","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","geography"],"abstract":"The <b>Central Plain</b>, the Zhongyuan, is the country of the middle and lower Yellow River around Henan, and it is where Chinese tradition puts the first dynasty and so the beginning of Chinese civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In that account the Xia ruled here from the 21st to the 17th century BCE, and social complexity spread outward from the plain over the next two thousand years by conquest and by imitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology went looking for it, and a survey in 1959, prompted by texts placing the Xia homeland in western Henan and southern Shanxi, found Erlitou near Yanshi, a site of some 400 hectares (990 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its own deposit lies sandwiched between a Neolithic layer below and an early Shang one above, four building phases thick, and the assemblage in it was named the Erlitou culture after the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plain's sequence runs on unbroken from Neolithic villages through Erlitou into the Shang, which is not true everywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It was not alone: walled towns of the same centuries stand in Shandong, Hubei, Hunan and Sichuan, gathered in regional clusters rather than spreading out from any one place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the northeast, tiered earthen platforms held burials furnished almost wholly with carved jade, in districts of perhaps five hundred people each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the Yangtze delta a walled city of some 300 hectares (740 acres) stood behind dams and levees a thousand years before Erlitou was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Set beside the contemporary chiefdoms of the Chifeng country to its north, the Erlitou polity looks larger and perhaps more centralised, but its social and political system was not different in kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The single-line story has accordingly given way to one in which several polities made Chinese civilisation between them, and the plain's distinction is not that it began first but that its line never broke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Paola Dematté, “Longshan-Era Urbanism: The Role of Cities in Predynastic China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 38, no. 2 (1999): 119–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17124. [Open access]","Christian E. Peterson et al., “Hongshan Chiefly Communities in Neolithic Northeastern China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 13 (2010): 5756–61, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2851873/. [Open access]","Bin Liu et al., “Earliest Hydraulic Enterprise in China, 5,100 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13637–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748178/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Late_Neolithic_cultures_in_China.jpg/1920px-Late_Neolithic_cultures_in_China.jpg","title":"Late Neolithic cultures of China","desc":"Fifteen culture areas of the late Neolithic ringed on a relief map, with Longshan on the Central Plain as one among them. Drawn after Li Liu and Xingcan Chen, The Archaeology of China (Cambridge, 2012), p. 170; the base map’s country and sea names are in German. Map by Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Ismoon, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Late_Neolithic_cultures_in_China.jpg","alt":"A coloured relief map of China and its neighbours with fifteen thin black ellipses drawn across it, each numbered and keyed at the right to a late Neolithic culture, spread from the Liao river in the north-east to Yunnan and Taiwan in the south."}},
{"id":"cnh-053","num":53,"category":"Ancient","question":"The walled Bronze Age city of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood on the Chengdu Plain in the Sichuan Basin, and is generally taken for a capital of the ancient state of Shu.","questions":["Eight pits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held bronze, gold, jade and ivory deliberately broken or burnt before burial, among them a bronze figure over two and a half metres (eight feet) tall.","Bronze casting at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew on the same alloys and moulds as the Shang, but the masks, figures and trees it made resemble nothing found in central China."],"answer":"Sanxingdui","answerText":"Sanxingdui","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pits filled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"三星堆","hanzi":"三星堆","pinyin":"Sānxīngduī","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sanxingdui</b> is a Bronze Age walled city on the Chengdu Plain, in the Sichuan Basin of south-west China, occupied from roughly 1700 to 1150 BCE and generally taken for a capital of the ancient state of Shu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its walls enclose about 500 hectares (1,200 acres), and the settlement reaches some 1,200 hectares (3,000 acres) if the neighbourhoods around it are counted; inner earth walls divide the interior into what look like palace and workshop quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nine walled towns of the earlier Baodun culture had stood across the plain, and Sanxingdui was the one centre left when they declined, with every contemporary site near it far smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was contemporary with the Erligang and Late Shang traditions of eastern China, and one of its bronze trees was cast by the alloy recipes and piece-mould work of that same technical tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it made with them resembles nothing found in central China, and the place of Shu in early Chinese civilisation is argued over still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Eight pits have been found in the southern part of the site, two of them in 1986 and six more from 2019, each holding high-value objects deliberately broken or burnt before they were buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between about 1200 and 1000 BCE they received bronze, gold, jade, ivory, cowrie shells and carnelian beads, laid down in ordered layers rather than thrown in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among them are a bronze figure 2.62 metres (8 feet 7 inches) tall, a mask 1.38 metres (4 feet 6 inches) wide, a bronze tree 3.95 metres (13 feet) high and a gold-sheathed staff 1.42 metres (4 feet 8 inches) long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trace-element work on the carnelian points to raw material carried from far to the north, and silk has been detected in the ash lying over the objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Why the pits were filled is unsettled: a sacrifice, or a hurried destruction as power passed to Jinsha, in what is now Chengdu, where the same images go on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","bronze age","china","art"],"sources":["Rowan Flad, Timothy J. Horsley, Jade D’Alpoim Guedes, He Kunyu, Gwen Bennett, Pochan Chen, Li Shuicheng, and Jiang Zhanghua, “Survey, Excavation, and Geophysics at Songjiaheba — A Small Bronze Age Site in the Chengdu Plain,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 52, no. 1 (2013): 119–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/38721. [Open access]","Kuei-chen Lin, “On Craft Production and the Settlement Pattern of the Jinsha Site Cluster on the Chengdu Plain,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 367–400, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76789. [Open access]","Weijie Zhao, “New Archeological Marvels of Ancient Shu Civilization,” <i>National Science Review</i> 8, no. 7 (2021): nwab071, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8310767/. [Open access]","Hailing Zheng et al., “Evidence of the Use of Silk by Bronze Age Civilization for Sacrificial Purposes in the Yangtze River Basin of China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 29175, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11589763/. [Open access]","Meiting Yan et al., “Sourcing the Origins of Carnelian in Early Chinese Civilizations,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 7 (2026): e2524563123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12913004/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Ancient_Bronze_Mask_from_Sanxingdui_1.jpg/1920px-Ancient_Bronze_Mask_from_Sanxingdui_1.jpg","title":"Bronze mask from Sanxingdui","desc":"One of the large bronze masks recovered from the sacrificial pits, with wide almond eyes, a hooked nose and outspread ears. Sanxingdui Museum, Guanghan, Sichuan.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A green-patinated bronze mask of a face with wide almond-shaped eyes under heavy brows, a broad flat nose, a straight closed mouth and large ears spread out at each side, standing on a white plinth in a museum case."}},
{"id":"cnh-054","num":54,"category":"Ancient","question":"Chinese tradition makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first of the Three Dynasties, seventeen kings in the Central Plain before the Shang, though the received chronologies cannot agree on when they reigned.","questions":["The dynastic cycle begins with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose last king Jie is said to have forfeited Heaven’s favour to Tang of the Shang through his own misrule.","No writing survives from the centuries assigned to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Taosi and Erlitou are the Central Plain sites most often matched to the capitals its texts describe."],"answer":"Xia dynasty","answerText":"Xia dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17, in the received lists</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rival reckonings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opens 2205 BCE, or 1989 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"夏朝","hanzi":"夏朝","pinyin":"Xià cháo","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Xia dynasty</b> is the name traditional Chinese historiography gives to the earliest state in the country, placed in the Central Plain of the middle Yellow River and standing first in the sequence of Three Dynasties that runs Xia, Shang, Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sima Qian counted seventeen kings, but the received chronologies cannot agree when they reigned: one opens the dynasty in 2205 BCE and runs it 439 years, another opens it in 1989 and runs it 432.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A state-sponsored chronology published in 2000 gave a third answer, about 2070 to 1600 BCE, and that is the span most archaeological writing now works with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its founder is Yu, the oldest of the culture heroes the Western Zhou knew, remembered for draining the flood that had covered the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its last king, Jie, is said to have ruled so badly that the lords went over to Tang of the Shang, who drove him into exile and took the title of Son of Heaven in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That story is the dynastic cycle in its first turn: a house holds the favour of Heaven, forfeits it through the misconduct of a final ruler, and gives way to another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The scheme may itself be a Zhou invention, a linear succession of three hereditary houses composed to support a theory of dynastic cycles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing written survives from the centuries the Xia is assigned to, and what is told of it comes from texts set down long afterwards, some of it plainly legend, as when ten suns are said to have risen together in one reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tradition also grew backwards: the Western Zhou knew Yu alone, Confucius knew two sage rulers before him, and the Warring States knew older kings still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeologically, Taosi and Erlitou, the two great Central Plain centres between about 2300 and 1500 BCE, are the sites most often matched to the capitals the texts describe, and neither match is settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxc–cxciii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 167–71, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Xia_Dynasty_Jade_Yue_Axe.jpg/1920px-Xia_Dynasty_Jade_Yue_Axe.jpg","title":"A jade yue axe, labelled Xia","desc":"A ceremonial jade blade of the kind Chinese museums assign to the Xia period. National Museum of China, Beijing.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A flat wedge-shaped blade of pale grey-green jade with a single round drilled hole near its centre, mounted against a cream museum wall."}},
{"id":"cnh-055","num":55,"category":"Ancient","question":"Born to a queen who escaped through a hole in the wall, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have won back the Xia from ten li square and five hundred men.","questions":["The <i>Shiji</i> passes straight from Xiang to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with nothing between, and only the Zuo Commentary tells how the Xia was lost to usurpers and taken back.","After hiding as a shepherd and then as a cook, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gathered the remnant of the Xia people, destroyed the usurper’s two sons and sacrificed again to Yu."],"answer":"Shaokang","answerText":"Shaokang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Interregnum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 to 40 years, say the commentators</span><span class=\"dt-k\">He began with</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 li square and 500 men</span></div>","traditional":"少康","hanzi":"少康","pinyin":"Shàokāng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shaokang</b> is the king who, in the traditional account of the Xia, won the dynasty back a generation after it had been taken by usurpers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The received king list runs from Yu to his son Qi, then to Taikang, who went hunting beyond the Lo for a hundred days and lost the kingdom, then to Zhongkang and to Xiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sima Qian passes over what came next in silence: in his annals Xiang dies and his son Shaokang takes power, with nothing between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The story of those years survives instead in the Zuo Commentary, where two speakers set it out centuries later to press arguments about wars of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the traditional account of the Xia in miniature, kept not as a record but as a lesson, and reaching us through the uses later writers found for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Ao, son of the usurper Zhuo, killed the lords of Zhenguan and Zhenxun and destroyed Xiang; Xiang’s queen was pregnant, escaped through a hole in the wall and reached her own state of Reng, where Shaokang was born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He grew up as master of the herdsmen of Reng, and when Ao sent a man to look for him he fled again and became cook to the ruler of Yu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That ruler gave him his two daughters in marriage and the town of Lun, ten <i>li</i> square, with five hundred men, and this was the whole of what he began with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gathered what was left of the people of Xia and revived its offices, set one man to watch Ao and another to mislead Ao’s brother, and destroyed them both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Restored, he sacrificed to the Xia kings with Yu as the correlate of Heaven; the commentary draws the moral that the usurpers fell because they had lost the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["ruler","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Ai, year 1, 794, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Hsiang, year 4, 424–25, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 165–68, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 78–79, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/%E5%B0%91%E5%BA%B7%EF%BC%88%E5%A4%8F%E5%95%86%E5%90%88%E5%82%B3%EF%BC%89.jpg/1920px-%E5%B0%91%E5%BA%B7%EF%BC%88%E5%A4%8F%E5%95%86%E5%90%88%E5%82%B3%EF%BC%89.jpg","title":"Shaokang in a Qing woodblock","desc":"A line drawing from an illustrated Qing edition of the Combined Biographies of Xia and Shang — an artist's idea, some three thousand years later, of a king known only from texts.","credit":"Unknown artist, 1814. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A woodblock line drawing of a standing figure in a tall crown and long trailing robes with hands folded at the waist, inside a heavy printed border."}},
{"id":"cnh-056","num":56,"category":"Ancient","question":"Nine provinces are set out in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each with its limits, its soil, the works done in it and the tribute it owes to the capital.","questions":["Sima Qian copied the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whole into his annals of the Xia, where the survey of the land stands as the founder’s first act of government.","Yu is named only twice in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, at its opening and its close, and without those two sentences it reads as the record of a spreading settlement."],"answer":"Tribute of Yu","answerText":"Tribute of Yu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Provinces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9, each graded for soil and tribute</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Domains</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, each 500 li wide</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yu is named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 times, at the opening and the close</span></div>","traditional":"禹貢","hanzi":"禹贡","pinyin":"Yǔ gòng","translations":"","abstract":"The <b><i>Tribute of Yu</i></b> is a chapter of the <i>Book of Documents</i> describing how the land was set in order after the flood, and it is the oldest geographical treatise in the Chinese tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It divides the country into nine provinces and works through them one at a time in a fixed order: the province’s limits, the works carried out in it, the quality of its soil, the tribute it owes and the roads it travels by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The soils are described as found — whitish and mellow in one province, red and clayey in another, miry in a third — and the fields and revenue of each are ranked by class and grade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second part runs through the nine mountain ranges and the nine great watercourses, and closes with a scheme of concentric squares five hundred <i>li</i> wide, from the sovereign’s own domain out through the nobles to the wild lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian copied the whole chapter into his annals of the Xia, where it stands at the head of the founder’s reign as the act that made the realm governable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The chapter is placed under Yu, but it names him only twice, in its opening sentence and its closing one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Take those two sentences away and what is left reads as the record of a settlement spreading slowly over drained ground, driving the older inhabitants before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The works it describes are far beyond one man in any case, so tradition credited Yu alone with the continuous labour of several generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No state of that age governed a country of the size it maps: the Three Dynasties era held dozens of polities at once, and the nine provinces are a scheme rather than a survey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whoever composed it was setting out a theory of how the country might have been brought to order by the founder of the Xia, and glorifying him by giving him the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","geography","china","literature"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 64–76, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Introduction, 16–19, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 102–3, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 100–102, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Yuji_tu_-_enhanced_contrast.png/1280px-Yuji_tu_-_enhanced_contrast.png","title":"The Map of the Tracks of Yu","desc":"A rubbing of the stone map carved in 1136 and now in the Stele Forest at Xi'an, drawn on a ruled grid and naming the mountains and rivers of the Tribute of Yu.","credit":"Rubbing in the Library of Congress, contrast enhanced. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A square map in black line on white, covered edge to edge by a fine ruled grid, with river courses drawn as heavy winding lines and hundreds of small Chinese place names between them."}},
{"id":"cnh-057","num":57,"category":"Ancient","question":"Before the battle at Kan, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> charged the lord of Hu with abandoning the three commencements of the year and called it a punishment appointed by Heaven.","questions":["The lords passed over Yu’s chosen minister and went to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because he was their sovereign’s son, and with that inheritance the Xia became a dynasty rather than a succession.","Archers on the left, spearmen on the right and drivers between them were told by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that failing in their work would count as disregarding his own orders."],"answer":"Qi of Xia","answerText":"Qi of Xia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hosts at Kan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6, under six nobles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In a chariot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 men: driver, archer, spearman</span></div>","traditional":"夏啟","hanzi":"夏启","pinyin":"Xià Qǐ","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Qi of Xia</b> is the second king of the traditional list and the one with whom the dynasty properly begins: he took his father Yu’s place by inheritance, which is what turns a house into a dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yu had named his minister Yi to follow him, as the rulers before him had done, but when Yi withdrew at the end of the mourning the lords went to Qi instead, saying he was their sovereign’s son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether Yu had thereby fallen short of the sages before him was argued in the Warring States, and the answer given was that Yi had served too briefly to be known, that Qi was able, and that the change was Heaven’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reign is remembered for one event, a battle at Kan against the lord of Hu, whose lands lay west across the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Book of Documents</i> preserves the speech he is said to have made before it, and it is the earliest piece in that collection to have been claimed as contemporary with what it describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He called together the six nobles who led his hosts and charged the lord of Hu with wasting the five elements and abandoning the three commencements of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The charge is put obscurely, but the calendar is the point: each house fixed the month its year began in, and to begin it elsewhere was to refuse the new dynasty’s authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is executing, he says, a punishment appointed by Heaven — the language the tradition would later use for the fall of dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then he sets out what each man must do, the archer on the chariot’s left and the spearman on its right with the driver between, and warns that to fail in it is to disregard him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Obedience will be rewarded before his ancestors and disobedience punished before the altar of the land, children included; a king on campaign carried both spirits with him, the ancestral tablet in its own chariot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["ruler","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 76–78, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 163–66, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Introduction, 18 and 26, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 358–59, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/%E5%90%AF_%E6%B8%85%E4%BA%BA%E7%BB%98.jpg/1280px-%E5%90%AF_%E6%B8%85%E4%BA%BA%E7%BB%98.jpg","title":"Qi of Xia, painted under the Qing","desc":"A portrait on silk of the Xia's second king by an unknown Qing artist, made some three thousand years after the reign it shows.","credit":"Unknown artist. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A painted portrait on silk of a bearded man in a green scholar's cap and pale green robe, shown from the chest up against a plain ochre ground."}},
{"id":"cnh-058","num":58,"category":"Ancient","question":"Told that his cruelties were dangerous, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pointed to the sun and said he sat as firm on his throne as it sat in the sky.","questions":["Tang imprisoned by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the Terrace of Xia and then released, went on to raise an army, drive him to Mingtiao and take the throne in his place.","The people of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are quoted asking when the sun would expire and saying they would all perish with it, in the speech Tang made before attacking him."],"answer":"Jie of Xia","answerText":"Jie of Xia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 Xia kings</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Defections from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Kong Jia, 4 reigns earlier</span></div>","traditional":"夏桀","hanzi":"夏桀","pinyin":"Xià Jié","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Jie of Xia</b> is the last king of the Xia in the traditional account, and the first of the bad last rulers on whom the Chinese dynastic cycle turns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lords had been falling away since the reign of Kong Jia, four kings before him, and under Jie they revolted in growing numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is said to have cared nothing for virtue and to have terrorised the hundred families until they would not bear him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He summoned Tang, the lord who was to supplant him, imprisoned him at the Terrace of Xia and then let him go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tang practised virtue, the lords took refuge with him and he led an army against Jie, who fled to Mingtiao and died in exile, saying he regretted not killing Tang when he had him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>Book of Documents</i> keeps the speech Tang made before that campaign, and its most telling feature is that he had to argue his own people into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They asked what the crimes of the Xia were to them and why they were being called away from their fields; he answered that Heaven had laid the charge on him, and ended by threatening to put their children to death with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their own curse on Jie is quoted in it: he had once pointed to the sun and said he sat as firm on his throne as it sat in the sky, and they asked in reply when that sun would expire, saying they would all perish with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later writers made him and the last Shang king a pair who lost the throne by losing the people, driving them to their rivals as an otter drives fish into deep water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pairing runs deeper than the moral: the tyrant gaoling the founder who will replace him is told of the Shang’s last king too, and Jie’s personal name ends in a cyclical character, which is a Shang habit and no other Xia king’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 169–71, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 169–70, notes, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 84–86, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 300, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/%E6%AC%BD%E5%AE%9A%E6%9B%B8%E7%B6%93%E5%9C%96%E8%AA%AA_%E6%94%BE%E6%A1%80%E5%8D%97%E5%B7%A2%E5%9C%96.jpg/1280px-%E6%AC%BD%E5%AE%9A%E6%9B%B8%E7%B6%93%E5%9C%96%E8%AA%AA_%E6%94%BE%E6%A1%80%E5%8D%97%E5%B7%A2%E5%9C%96.jpg","title":"The banishment of Jie","desc":"A plate from the imperially commissioned illustrated Shujing of 1905. Jie rides away from the walled place named Nanchao, to which the tradition has him banished.","credit":"Sun Jianai, 1905. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A Chinese line drawing of a bearded rider on a horse among bare trees, with a walled gateway on a bluff above him and a labelled hill in the foreground."}},
{"id":"cnh-059","num":59,"category":"Ancient","question":"In the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, five brothers waiting north of the Lo sing that the people are the root of a country and that when the root is firm the country is tranquil.","questions":["Taikang hunted beyond the Lo for a hundred days and was barred from returning, and his five brothers turned Yu’s Cautions into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the <i>Book of Documents</i>.","Belonging to the chapters made out from tablets found in a wall and presented in 97 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been called a later fabrication since the Song."],"answer":"Songs of the Five Sons","answerText":"Songs of the Five Sons","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taikang's hunt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 days beyond the Lo</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Songs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, one from each brother</span></div>","traditional":"五子之歌","hanzi":"五子之歌","pinyin":"Wǔ zǐ zhī gē","translations":"","abstract":"<b><i>Songs of the Five Sons</i></b> is a chapter of the <i>Book of Documents</i> in which five brothers lament the throne their eldest has lost, and it is the most quoted text assigned to the Xia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taikang, Yu’s grandson, is said to have held the throne like a personator of the dead — the stand-in set in a dead man’s place at a sacrifice — and to have idled his virtue away until the people wavered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He hunted beyond the Lo for a hundred days without returning, and the lord of Qiong barred him from recrossing the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His five brothers, who had followed their mother after him, were waiting on the north bank; hearing of it, they put the Cautions of Yu into songs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first opens on the line most often taken from the collection: the people are the root of a country, and when the root is firm the country is tranquil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The same song has him say that in dealing with millions he should feel as anxious as a man driving six horses with rotten reins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second lists what has never been anything but a prelude to ruin: a palace that is a wild of lust, a country a wild for hunting, a liking for spirits and music, lofty roofs, carved walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last three mourn the throne lost, the ancestor’s canons thrown into confusion, the temple overturned and the sacrifices put out, and close with the brothers unable to overtake the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its route to us is the point of it: it is not among the 29 documents a scholar hid in a wall before the burning of the books, but among the further ones deciphered from tablets found in another wall and presented to the throne in 97 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Critics from the Song onwards judged those additional chapters by their style and called them a later fabrication; the argument has run both ways since, and they are printed with the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["text","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 78–81, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Introduction, 7–9, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Introduction, 10–11, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 165–66, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/%E6%AC%BD%E5%AE%9A%E6%9B%B8%E7%B6%93%E5%9C%96%E8%AA%AA_%E4%BB%B2%E5%BA%B7%E8%82%87%E4%BD%8D%E5%9C%96.jpg/1280px-%E6%AC%BD%E5%AE%9A%E6%9B%B8%E7%B6%93%E5%9C%96%E8%AA%AA_%E4%BB%B2%E5%BA%B7%E8%82%87%E4%BD%8D%E5%9C%96.jpg","title":"Zhongkang begins his reign","desc":"The plate for the chapter that follows the Songs in the same imperially commissioned illustrated Shujing of 1905: Taikang's younger brother takes the throne he had lost.","credit":"Sun Jianai, 1905. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A Chinese line drawing of a robed ruler enthroned in a tiled hall under a curved roof, with two attendants beside him and a dozen officials standing on the paving below."}},
{"id":"cnh-060","num":60,"category":"Ancient","question":"Robbers opening a Wei king’s grave in 279 CE found the tablets of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a chronicle running from the Yellow Emperor down to 298 BCE.","questions":["Where the received chronology opens the Xia in 2205 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opens it in 1989 and gives the dynasty 432 years, a disagreement its compiler’s date makes worth hearing.","Fifteen works came out of the tomb, and only a <i>Book of Changes</i> and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were thought worth keeping once the tablets had been transcribed."],"answer":"Bamboo Annals","answerText":"Bamboo Annals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 295 BCE, with King Xiang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">279 CE, in a tomb at Ji</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Covers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Huangdi to 298 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia opens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1989 BCE, not 2205</span></div>","traditional":"竹書紀年","hanzi":"竹书纪年","pinyin":"Zhúshū jìnián","translations":"","abstract":"<b><i>Bamboo Annals</i></b> is a chronicle of early China found in a tomb in 279 CE, running from the Yellow Emperor down to 298 BCE, and it reaches a reader by a different route from every other account of that age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Robbers opened the grave of King Xiang of Wei, dead since 295 BCE, and found bamboo tablets written in small seal script carrying more than 100,000 words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were damaged in the handling before reaching the imperial library, and the emperor set his best scholars to transcribe them into current characters and put them in order, which took two years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifteen works could be made out; most were legend and were soon neglected, but two were not — a copy of the <i>Book of Changes</i> agreeing with the received text, and the annals themselves, in twelve or thirteen chapters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scholar who saw them in the library the next year records both the damage and those two, which is what makes the find hard to dismiss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their chronology is not the received one: they open the Xia in 1989 BCE rather than 2205, give it 432 years, and have Yu publish the seasons of Xia in his first year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where they disagree they have a claim to be heard, since their compiler belonged to Wei and lived under the last princes he describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> But tablets were lost and their order confused, passages seem to have drifted in from the eleven books of fragmentary sayings the same tomb held, and the notes printed with the entries have Yu born of a mother who swallowed a pearl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The annals proper carry showers of gold, monstrous animals and changes of sex, which have been likened to the marvels in Livy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chinese scholars have called the whole thing a forgery imposed on the world by one of the men who transcribed it, while modern work has gone the other way, screening the Xia entries and arguing that many are historical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 105–7, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 176–77, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxc–cxciii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 117–18, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Eastern_Zhou_Bamboo_Slips_from_Chu_Grave.jpg/1920px-Eastern_Zhou_Bamboo_Slips_from_Chu_Grave.jpg","title":"Eastern Zhou bamboo slips","desc":"Not the annals, which survive only as a text: these are inscribed slips from a Chu grave, sealed in preservative, of the kind and period found in King Xiang's tomb. Henan Provincial Museum, Zhengzhou.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Ten narrow strips of bamboo written over in black brush characters, each sealed upright in a glass tube of clear liquid and laid in a row beside a long bronze blade on a white museum tray."}},
{"id":"cnh-061","num":61,"category":"Ancient","question":"Noticing that the later a Chinese source is the longer the legendary age it describes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked in the 1920s whether the Xia had existed.","questions":["Seven volumes of <i>Debating Ancient History</i> came out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which held that early Chinese history was a tale retold with new material added at the front.","The 400-hectare site of Erlitou, found in 1959 where the texts put the Xia, is the archaeological answer to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its case against the early dynasties."],"answer":"Doubting Antiquity School","answerText":"Doubting Antiquity School","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1920s, in China</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 volumes of Debating Ancient History</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Erlitou found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1959, in western Henan</span></div>","traditional":"疑古派","hanzi":"疑古派","pinyin":"Yígǔ pài","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Doubting Antiquity School</b> is the name given to a movement in Chinese historical scholarship of the 1920s that put the early dynasties, and the Xia above all, on trial as evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founder noticed a pattern in the records: the later a source, the longer the legendary age it describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Western Zhou knew only Yu; Confucius, in the Spring and Autumn period, knew Yao and Shun before him; people of the Warring States knew older kings still, Huangdi and Shennong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that he argued that early Chinese history is a tale told and retold, with new material added at the front rather than the back, and he questioned openly whether the Xia had existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The school edited seven volumes of <i>Debating Ancient History</i>, weeding out entries it judged unauthentic, and it noted how much plain legend the classical Xia records carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The doubt was not new: a Chinese scholar of the 18th century had called a chief source for the Xia a fabrication imposed on the world, and a French translator of the Chinese annals wrote in 1895 that the Xia belonged to prehistory rather than to history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What answered the school was not argument but digging: in 1959 a survey went looking for the Xia where the texts put it, in western Henan, and found the 400-hectare site of Erlitou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese scholarship has grown steadily more confident since, screening the old records’ Xia entries against other classical writing and bronze inscriptions and finding many defensible; the identification of Erlitou itself still rests on place and date rather than on writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At a seminar on Xia culture held in 1990 the Western participants were about evenly split on whether the dynasty existed, while the Chinese participants were unanimous that it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sceptics’ methodological worry has not gone away either: a programme that sets archaeology to find the capitals named in texts lets written history decide what archaeology looks for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["school of thought","history","archaeology","china","literature"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxv–clxvi, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 176–77, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/Gu_Jiegang_1920s.png","title":"The school's founder in the 1920s","desc":"Gu Jiegang, who set out the argument that the legendary age of Chinese history grew longer the later the source describing it.","credit":"Photographer unknown, 1920s. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"An oval black-and-white studio portrait of a young man in round wire spectacles and a plain high-collared gown, looking straight at the camera."}},
{"id":"cnh-062","num":62,"category":"Ancient","question":"Between the Longshan cultures and the Shang, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the central Yellow River for some four hundred years, in four phases of roughly a century each.","questions":["The white and grey three-legged ritual vessels of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were made in the Yiluo basin and grow scarcer with every kilometre away from it.","Rammed-earth foundations and bronze vessels appear only in the last two phases of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so most of what makes it look like a state is late."],"answer":"Erlitou culture","answerText":"Erlitou culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, of about 100 years each</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Centre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 400 hectares</span></div>","traditional":"二里頭文化","hanzi":"二里头文化","pinyin":"Èrlǐtóu wénhuà","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Erlitou culture</b> is the archaeological culture of the central Yellow River between about the 20th and the 17th centuries BCE, named for a site in the Luoyang basin and standing between the Longshan cultures and the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon dates spread it over some four hundred years, and it is divided into four phases of roughly a century each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its strata overlie the local Longshan culture and are probably continuous with it, and it was replaced without a gap by the Erligang phase of the Shang, though how far that succession was political as well as cultural is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what makes it look like a state belongs to its last two phases: the great rammed-earth foundations and the bronze vessels appear only in the third and fourth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ritual pottery is the culture’s signature — white and fine grey vessels standing on three legs, a form taken over from Shandong — made in the Yiluo basin and growing scarcer with distance from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the third phase the culture reached furthest, and the same vessels turn up at sites in western Henan and southern Shanxi, so alike in shape that they were more likely carried from the centre than copied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That pattern reads as prestige goods passed down to lesser elites in the periphery, and as a shared set of rituals rather than a shared population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The expansion went towards resources: Dongxiafeng in the Jinnan basin, at 25 hectares one of the largest sites of the period there, sits five kilometres (three miles) from the copper and salt of the Zhongtiao mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The centre itself, at about 400 hectares, was the only settlement of that size in the Central Plain, and people appear to have moved into it rather than a town having grown where it stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> White pottery goes out of use under the Erligang, its place in ceremony taken by bronze vessels doing the same work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/Pottery_he_%28wine_vessel%29%2C_Erlitou_Culture%2C_Erlitou_site%2C_Yanshi%2C_Henan.jpg","title":"A pottery he from Erlitou","desc":"A pale grey three-legged wine vessel from the type site at Yanshi in Henan, of the form the culture's ritual pottery takes.","credit":"Uncredited photograph, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A pale grey pottery jug standing on three hollow pointed legs, with a domed top, a short spout, a strap handle and a band of small impressed dots round its shoulder."}},
{"id":"cnh-063","num":63,"category":"Ancient","question":"A palace and temple district of 10.8 hectares, boxed in by four intersecting roads, stood on the highest ground of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in western Henan.","questions":["No wall ever ran round the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the whole volume of its public building was seven times smaller than at the older centre it followed.","Rulers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> walled a bronze and turquoise workshop before they walled their own palace, controlling the making of power’s symbols first."],"answer":"Erlitou site","answerText":"Erlitou site","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phase I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 100 hectares</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phase II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 300 hectares</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At its height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 400 hectares</span></div>","traditional":"二里頭遺址","hanzi":"二里头遗址","pinyin":"Èrlǐtóu yízhǐ","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Erlitou site</b> is a Bronze Age settlement in the Yiluo basin of western Henan, the largest of its time in the Central Plain and the place its whole archaeological culture is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was found in 1959 by a survey looking for the Xia where the texts put it, and is divided into four phases from its layers and the sequence of its pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first, from about 1750 BCE, it covered more than 100 hectares and was the most densely peopled place in the region, though no house or public building of that phase has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the second it tripled to more than 300 hectares and began to be planned, with a palace and temple district of 10.8 hectares on the highest ground, boxed in by four intersecting roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A workshop walled in rammed earth went up south of it, given over to bronze and turquoise and drawing metal from southern Shanxi, so the rulers controlled the making of power’s symbols before they walled the palace itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Burials from late in that phase fall into four or five grades by the labour spent on the pit and the goods put in it, and the richest, the ones with bronze, are found at no other site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inside the palace district only the top two grades were buried, along with a small number of corpse dumps that include people buried alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elsewhere the dead lie close to the living, artisans near where they had worked, and rich and poor to some extent mixed across the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the place has not got is a wall around it, where the older centre at Taosi had one, and the whole volume of its public building was seven times smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reached about 400 hectares at its height, the only settlement of that size in the Central Plain, and its political weight fell away in the last phase, as the Erligang centres rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Pottery_drain_pipe%2C_Erlitou_culture%2C_Erlitou_site%2C_Yanshi%2C_Henan.jpg/1920px-Pottery_drain_pipe%2C_Erlitou_culture%2C_Erlitou_site%2C_Yanshi%2C_Henan.jpg","title":"A drain pipe from Erlitou","desc":"A cord-marked ceramic drain pipe from the site at Yanshi in Henan.","credit":"Uncredited photograph, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A thick cylindrical pipe of pale grey fired clay, its outer surface covered in fine cord impressions, lying on its side in a museum case."}},
{"id":"cnh-064","num":64,"category":"Ancient","question":"Cast in moulds built of several pieces, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave China its first ritual vessels in metal and the emblem its rulers would use for the rest of the Bronze Age.","questions":["One walled workshop of about a hectare produced all the known <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and what survives in it is slag, crucible fragments and clay moulds rather than vessels.","Moulds for rims 36 centimetres across survive among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with no matching vessel found, because only medium and small tombs at the site have been dug."],"answer":"Erlitou bronze","answerText":"Erlitou bronze","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Foundry from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vessels from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foundry area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1 hectare</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest moulds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">36 cm rim diameter</span></div>","traditional":"二里頭青銅器","hanzi":"二里头青铜器","pinyin":"Èrlǐtóu qīngtóngqì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Erlitou bronze</b> is the metalwork of the Erlitou site in Henan, and it includes the earliest cast bronze ritual vessels known in China, which appear from about 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Metal had been worked in the country for centuries before that — ornaments, small tools and weapons, hammered or cast in simple stone moulds — and it carried no particular rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Taosi, where burials are sharply graded, the jade and the fine pottery went into the great tombs while a copper bell turned up in a small one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What changed at Erlitou was the technique: a vessel was cast in a mould built of several pieces, allowing shapes and surfaces no single stone mould can give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method appears to have been devised for ritual vessels in particular, and those vessels became the chief emblem of political and religious power for the rest of the Chinese Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The foundry took up about a hectare in the south of the site, walled in rammed earth together with the turquoise workshop, and it is the only place such vessels are known to have been cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives there is the waste of the work: thick deposits of slag, the remains of crucibles, and clay moulds for tools, weapons and ritual vessels alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of those moulds were cut for vessels up to 36 centimetres (14 inches) across the rim, and no matching vessel has turned up, since only medium and small tombs have been excavated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The copper behind it came from no state monopoly, for mining and smelting in the mountains that supplied Erlitou were small, local and sporadic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nor was it all vessels: the same enclosure turned out bronze plaques whose faces were set with neatly cut turquoise, fitted together like a jigsaw into animal masks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Qin Xiaoli, “Turquoise Ornaments and Inlay Technology in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 55, no. 2 (2016): 208–39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/59199. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/20260408_Bronze_Jue_from_Erlitou.jpg/1280px-20260408_Bronze_Jue_from_Erlitou.jpg","title":"A bronze jue from Erlitou","desc":"A thin-walled three-legged wine cup unearthed at the Erlitou site in Yanshi, Henan Museum.","credit":"Photo by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A small green-corroded bronze cup on three splayed legs, with a long trough-shaped pouring spout at one end and a pointed lip at the other, lit against a dark museum case."}},
{"id":"cnh-065","num":65,"category":"Xia","question":"Commissioned by the Chinese government in 1996, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the Xia, Shang and Western Zhou absolute dates where firm chronology had begun only in 841 BCE.","questions":["Some 200 specialists in history, astronomy, archaeology and radiocarbon dating spent four and a half years on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose table was announced in Beijing in November 2000.","Wiggle-matched radiocarbon dates, retro-calculated astronomical events and tested textual entries were combined by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which put the Xia at 2070 to 1600 BCE."],"answer":"Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commissioned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1996, by the Chinese government</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 November 2000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Specialists</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 200, in four fields</span></div>","traditional":"夏商周斷代工程","hanzi":"夏商周断代工程","pinyin":"Xià Shāng Zhōu Duàndài Gōngchéng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project</b> is a Chinese state research programme that ran from 1996 to 2000 and set out to fix absolute dates for the three dynasties before the imperial age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Firm Chinese chronology began only in 841 BCE, and the Grand Historian said plainly why he stopped there: the Three Dynasties are ancient, and their chronology cannot be made precise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two millennia of argument since had produced at least 44 dates for the Zhou conquest of the Shang, the earliest 1130 BCE and the latest 1018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The programme came out of a senior official’s embarrassment on a 1995 visit to Egypt, whose precise chronology reached back to 2750 BCE, and its government backing drew criticism worldwide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 200 specialists in history, astronomy, archaeology and radiocarbon dating worked for four and a half years, in teams that ran independently and compared their results only at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For the conquest, a series of samples from a stratified pit at Fengxi was wiggle-matched against the calibration curve, cutting the range of possible dates from 112 years to 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A day-by-day account of the campaign preserved in the <i>Hanshu</i>, and a record in the <i>Guoyu</i> putting Jupiter in Quail Fire, then left one date in that span: 20 January 1046 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The report printed two rivals beside it, 1044 and 1027 BCE, the first resting on seven obscure graphs of a bronze inscription that has eight published readings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The table published on 9 November 2000 gave reign years for the late Shang and Western Zhou kings, and for the Xia only a framework of 2070 to 1600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nothing in it pushes the dates back — the received Xia span was already the 21st to the 17th century — and what it certainly left behind is a Chinese archaeology built on radiocarbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project","difficulty":5,"tags":["institution","history","archaeology","science","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Menghan Qiu, Linyao Du, Richard Staff, Ziyun Yan, Tingting Yan, Huihui Cao, Chris Gosden, Ruiliang Liu, and Guanghui Dong, “Chronology of Early China: A Radiocarbon Databank for Chinese Archaeology,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 12 (2025): 1665, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12540757/. [Open access]","Xianglong Chen, “Radiocarbon Dating and Its Applications in Chinese Archeology: An Overview,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 11 (2023): 1064717, https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/feart.2023.1064717/full. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxvii–clxxxviii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Li_gui_inscription.jpg","title":"A rubbing of the Li gui inscription","desc":"The inscription cast inside an early Western Zhou bronze vessel, whose reading is one of the disputed points in dating the Zhou conquest.","credit":"Rubbing uploaded by 陇右甘南, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A rubbing of an ancient Chinese bronze inscription, the angular seal-script characters showing white on a black ground in five vertical columns."}},
{"id":"cnh-066","num":66,"category":"Shang","question":"Ruling the Central Plain from about 1600 to 1046 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest Chinese dynasty attested in records of its own time, its diviners’ oracle bones.","questions":["Horse-drawn chariots, the royal hunt and writing all appear under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Anyang, its last seat, and at none of the centres that came before it.","Shared pottery and shared elite goods mark the reach of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though they show a culture rather than proof that one state ruled it all."],"answer":"Shang dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Middle Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商朝","hanzi":"商朝","pinyin":"Shāng cháo","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang dynasty</b> is the second of the three dynasties that ruled China before the imperial age, holding the Central Plain from about 1600 to 1046 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology now divides its five and a half centuries in three: an early phase to about 1400 BCE, a middle phase to about 1250, and a late phase down to the Zhou conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early phase is known from Zhengzhou and its neighbours as the Erligang horizon, which is defined mostly by its pottery rather than by anything written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The centre moved more than once, and at Anyang two of its seats lie side by side: the walled town of Huanbei, which lasted less than a century from the 14th, and Yinxu after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Anyang has three things no earlier centre can show — horse-drawn chariots, the royal hunt and writing — together with a bronze industry unmatched in scale and organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That writing makes the Shang the earliest Chinese dynasty attested in records of its own time, the inscriptions its court diviners left on oracle bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How far its rule reached is far from clear, since the sites sharing its pottery and its elite goods share a culture, which is not the same as belonging to one state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither Erlitou before it nor Anyang itself was walled, and the inscriptions name settlements rather than capitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At least 10,000 late Shang burials have been dug outside the royal cemetery, and more than four in five hold a single coffin, a few pots and perhaps a cowry shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor was the countryside simple: Guandimiao, a village 200 kilometres (125 miles) from Anyang and the only one of its date nearly fully excavated, made pottery for a whole region and used bone tools from the capital’s own workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang dynasty","difficulty":2,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Yanfeng Hou, Roderick Campbell, Zhipeng Li, Yan Zhang, Suting Li, and Yuling He, “The Guandimiao Bone Assemblage (and What It Says about the Shang Economy),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 281–310, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72094. [Open access]","James B. Stoltman, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, and George Rapp, “Ceramic Production in Shang Societies of Anyang,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 48, no. 1 (2009): 183–203, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29082. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ceremonial_Axe%2C_China%2C_Shang_dynasty%2C_14th-11th_century_BC%2C_cast_bronze_-_Arthur_M._Sackler_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC00776.jpg/1280px-Ceremonial_Axe%2C_China%2C_Shang_dynasty%2C_14th-11th_century_BC%2C_cast_bronze_-_Arthur_M._Sackler_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC00776.jpg","title":"A Shang ceremonial axe","desc":"A cast bronze axe of the 14th to 11th century BCE, its upper band worked with the animal masks characteristic of Shang bronzework.","credit":"Photo by Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A broad bronze axe head, green and rust with corrosion, its flaring crescent blade below a band of stylised animal masks and a row of downward-pointing triangles."}},
{"id":"cnh-067","num":67,"category":"Shang","question":"The fullest connected account of the Shang in any transmitted text is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which names every king from the ancestor Xie down to the dynasty’s last.","questions":["Closing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its historian names his own sources: the sacrificial odes for the ancestor Xie, and the Documents and the Odes from Tang the Victorious onward.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives the Yin thirty generations and the Xia seventeen, against only fifteen for the Zhou ancestors over the same stretch, which is arithmetic that will not reconcile."],"answer":"Basic Annals of Yin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE, under the Han</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chapter</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, of 130</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Covers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 kings, to Di Xin</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fixed dates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">none before 841 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"殷本紀","hanzi":"殷本纪","pinyin":"Yīn běnjì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Basic Annals of Yin</b> is the chapter of Sima Qian’s <i>Shiji</i> that carries the Shang, and it is the fullest connected account of the dynasty the transmitted texts give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It opens with the ancestor Xie, born after his mother swallowed an egg let fall by a dark bird, and runs through every king to the last, closing with a Shang prince set over Song to continue the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dynasty carries two names in it: Shang, from the land granted to Xie, and Yin, taken when Pan Geng moved his people to a place of that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The historian names his own sources at the close of the chapter — the sacrificial odes for Xie, and from Tang the Victorious onward the Documents and the Odes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is a short shelf: of the 29 chapters of the received Documents only nine treat anything before the Zhou, and five poems of the Odes concern the Yin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The story’s shape is a moral one, in which Tang takes the mandate of Heaven from the last Xia king and the Shang keep it until Di Xin, the 31st, throws it away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A cycle of that kind may be a Zhou idea laid over the past rather than a record of it, and the chapter was written more than a thousand years after the events it opens with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its own arithmetic strains: seventeen generations are given to the Xia and thirty to the Yin, against fifteen for the Zhou ancestors across the same stretch of time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor does it carry dates, since no year in the transmitted record is fixed before 841 BCE, so the annals hand down an order of kings with no calendar behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is no longer the only witness, because the Shang left writing of their own at Anyang, against which the chapter can now be read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Basic Annals of Yin","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxxxix–cxl, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 173–75, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 207–8, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/%E3%80%8E%E5%8F%B2%E8%A8%98%E3%80%8F%E5%94%90%E5%86%99%E6%9C%AC.jpg/1280px-%E3%80%8E%E5%8F%B2%E8%A8%98%E3%80%8F%E5%94%90%E5%86%99%E6%9C%AC.jpg","title":"A Tang manuscript copy","desc":"The close of chapter 29 of Sima Qian's history in a Tang-dynasty copy, the small characters between the columns being a commentator's notes.","credit":"ColBase, National Institutes for Cultural Heritage, Japan (Tokyo National Museum TB-1573), CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A pale manuscript scroll written in vertical columns of Chinese brush characters, with smaller commentary characters interleaved between them and a red seal near the left edge."}},
{"id":"cnh-068","num":68,"category":"Shang","question":"Remembered as the lord of Bo who took the mandate from the last Xia king, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bears a posthumous title glossed as the one who puts away cruelty and suppresses the tyrant.","questions":["Finding a hunter’s nets drawn on all four sides of a plain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took three of them away, and the lords said his virtue reached the birds and the beasts.","Yi Yin reached <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> either by talking his way from savoury flavours to government or after five summonses, and Mencius rejected the first of those accounts outright."],"answer":"Tang of Shang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE, in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 Shang kings</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Descent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14th from Xie</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 before Bo</span></div>","traditional":"商湯","hanzi":"商汤","pinyin":"Shāng Tāng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tang of Shang</b> is the founder of the Shang in the traditional account, the lord who overthrew the last Xia king and took the mandate of Heaven from him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tang is a posthumous title, glossed as the one who puts away cruelty and suppresses the tyrant; the name the annals give him is Tian Yi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the ancestor Xie down to him the line had shifted its seat eight times, and he settled at Bo, taking over the residence of an older king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name repays a second look, since from an ancestor six generations before him every Shang ruler carries one of the ten cyclical characters, said to mark the day he was born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He began, the annals say, with the count of Ge, who had left off making the sacrifices, and it was by punishing him that Tang started.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One saying of his is quoted plain: men look into water to see their faces, and one looks at the people to know whether a government is good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Another is the net, which he came upon drawn across all four sides of a plain by a hunter praying that everything under heaven should walk into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tang took away three of the sides and prayed instead that only what had tired of living should enter, and the lords said his virtue reached the birds and the beasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His minister Yi Yin comes down in two versions: a man who could reach him only by entering a bride’s household and talking his way from savoury flavours to the art of government, or a private man sent for five times before he would come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mencius rejected the first, saying he had heard Yi Yin approached Tang by the doctrines of Yao and Shun and not by his knowledge of cookery, and the historian who gives both seems not to have pressed it either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tang of Shang","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 175–76, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 177, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 178–79, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 180, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 364, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Emperor_Tang_of_the_Shang_Dynasty.jpg/1280px-Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Emperor_Tang_of_the_Shang_Dynasty.jpg","title":"Tang of Shang imagined","desc":"An album leaf of the 19th or early 20th century, from a set of portraits of famous men, painted some three and a half thousand years after the reign it shows.","credit":"Philadelphia Museum of Art, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A painted album leaf showing a bearded ruler in a flat-topped crown hung with strings of white beads, wearing a dark robe patterned with pale discs."}},
{"id":"cnh-069","num":69,"category":"Shang","question":"North-west of modern Anyang, on the Yellow River’s alluvial plain, lie the ruins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last Shang capital, which the oracle bones call Great Settlement Shang.","questions":["No inscription from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> names Pan Geng or the two kings after him, so the site’s own written record opens with Wu Ding, ninth from the dynasty’s end.","One foundry at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has given up more than 10,000 mould fragments, and the settlement round it was among the largest consumers of metal anywhere in Bronze Age Eurasia."],"answer":"Yinxu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, Yinxu I – IV</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wu Ding, 9th king from the end</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2006</span></div>","traditional":"殷墟","hanzi":"殷墟","pinyin":"Yīnxū","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Yinxu</b> is the last capital of the Shang, a spread of ruins on both banks of the Huan river north-west of modern Anyang in Henan, listed as a World Heritage site in 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oracle-bone inscriptions found there call the place Great Settlement Shang, which is literally a big urban centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Occupation ran for rather more than two centuries, from about 1250 to 1046 BCE, and archaeologists divide it into four phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tradition puts the move here earlier, under Pan Geng about 1300, but no inscription names him or the two kings after him, so the site’s own written record opens with Wu Ding, ninth from the end of the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It sat on the alluvial plain of the Yellow River with mountains to the west, on fertile floodplain soil among forests and abundant wildlife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Holocene climatic optimum was drawing to a close as the settlement grew, and the region turned cooler and drier around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its farmers widened what they sowed, to millet, rice, sorghum, barley and wheat together, which may have been insurance against a failed harvest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Society there was ordered by lineage, the king’s own line above the princely and other royal ones, and the artisans grouped by the trade they owed the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its appetite for metal was extraordinary — the settlement became one of the largest consumers of it anywhere in Eurasia in the 2nd millennium BCE — and one foundry alone has given up more than 10,000 mould fragments, most for ritual vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The clay was chosen as carefully as the metal: those moulds were made of untempered loess, worked to concentrate its silt and so raise the porosity that keeps flaws out of a casting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Yinxu","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Hua Zhang, Deborah C. Merrett, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, Yuling He, Hongbin Yue, Zhanwei Yue, and Dongya Y. Yang, “Osteoarthritis, Labour Division, and Occupational Specialization of the Late Shang China — Insights from Yinxu (ca. 1250–1046 B.C.),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0176329, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5413014/. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 11, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","James B. Stoltman, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, and George Rapp, “Ceramic Production in Shang Societies of Anyang,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 48, no. 1 (2009): 183–203, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29082. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Yinxu_Palace_Foundation_%2845144971564%29.jpg/1280px-Yinxu_Palace_Foundation_%2845144971564%29.jpg","title":"A palace platform at Yinxu","desc":"The rammed-earth foundation of a hall at the site, its column holes marked out by modern red posts.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A low rectangular platform of packed earth with two rows of short red-painted posts standing in it, fenced off, with a modern museum building to the right."}},
{"id":"cnh-070","num":70,"category":"Shang","question":"In 1899 a noted antiquarian dosing himself with ground bone saw characters on the pieces, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the Shang a record written in their own time.","questions":["Some 160,000 inscribed pieces have come out of the ground since the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, carrying more than 4,600 characters of which about a thousand have been read.","Excavation at Anyang began in 1928, following up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and turned that site into the most thoroughly studied piece of ground in China."],"answer":"discovery of the oracle bones","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1899, in a prescription</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1928, at Anyang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pieces found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 160,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Characters read</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,000 of 4,600</span></div>","traditional":"甲骨文的發現","hanzi":"甲骨文的发现","pinyin":"jiǎgǔwén de fāxiàn","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>discovery of the oracle bones</b> is the recognition, in 1899, that bones being sold and ground up as medicine carried writing, and that the writing was the Shang dynasty’s own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A noted antiquarian, ill and dosing himself with ground bone under a prescription, saw characters on the pieces and understood what he was swallowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before them the Shang was a literary tradition, known from texts written a thousand years and more after it, with no Chinese writing surviving from any earlier time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bones proved to be the record of royal divination: cattle shoulder blades and turtle plastrons were heated until they cracked, and the question, sometimes with its answer, was cut beside the crack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They were traced back to Anyang, where excavation began in 1928 and where the last Shang capital came to light, and the work there shaped Chinese archaeology as a discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What the find opened is very large and still mostly unread, since about 160,000 inscribed pieces have since come out of the ground, most of them fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They carry more than 4,600 distinct characters, of which only about a thousand have been deciphered, with their meanings and modern equivalents agreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The inscriptions date themselves by the day alone, in the repeating cycle of sixty names, which makes them an awkward foundation on which to build an absolute chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They do record eleven possible eclipses, and five lunar ones from a single group of diviners have been calculated to a thirty-year window between 1201 and 1181 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they have not settled is where Chinese writing came from: nothing earlier survives, which some read as an invention made at the Anyang court for divination and others as an accident of what perishes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"discovery of the oracle bones","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Pengjie Wang, Kaile Zhang, Xinyu Wang, Shengwei Han, Yongge Liu, Jinpeng Wan, Haisu Guan, Zhebin Kuang, Lianwen Jin, Xiang Bai, and Yuliang Liu, “An Open Dataset for Oracle Bone Character Recognition and Decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11379903/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 11, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Hua Zhang, Deborah C. Merrett, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, Yuling He, Hongbin Yue, Zhanwei Yue, and Dongya Y. Yang, “Osteoarthritis, Labour Division, and Occupational Specialization of the Late Shang China — Insights from Yinxu (ca. 1250–1046 B.C.),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0176329, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5413014/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_%28for_divination%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_%28for_divination%29.jpg","title":"An inscribed ox scapula","desc":"An oracle bone of the Bin group of diviners, from the reign of Wu Ding, its cut strokes picked out in red.","credit":"Photo by Gary Lee Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A cracked, roughly rectangular slab of pale bone densely covered in columns of small incised Chinese characters, the strokes filled with red."}},
{"id":"cnh-071","num":71,"category":"Shang","question":"Cut into turtle plastrons and cattle shoulder blades, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest Chinese writing that survives, and about half of its signs are still unread.","questions":["Only about 5% of the signs in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are basic shapes, against 48% in early cuneiform and 90% in Egyptian hieroglyphs, the rest being built by combination.","One character may be written in markedly different ways in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is the chief obstacle to reading it and to teaching a machine to recognise it."],"answer":"oracle bone script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Distinct signs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 4,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Basic shapes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 170</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Read so far</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,000 signs</span></div>","traditional":"甲骨文","hanzi":"甲骨文","pinyin":"jiǎgǔwén","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Oracle bone script</b> is the writing of the late Shang, cut into turtle plastrons and cattle shoulder blades between about 1250 and 1046 BCE, and the earliest Chinese writing that survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is a script fully able to represent the language, and it leans heavily on pictures: about 60% of its shapes depict something, against 70% in the earliest cuneiform and 95% in Egyptian hieroglyphs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roughly 170 basic shapes can be made out, and each stands for what it draws, or for something beside it, or for a word that merely sounds the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost everything else is built from those by modifying or combining them, so that only about 5% of its signs are basic, where the figure is 48% for early cuneiform and 90% for Egyptian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The corpus runs to some 4,000 distinct signs, though nobody can give an exact figure, and at least half of them are still unread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One character can be written in markedly different ways, the parts arranged differently or the strokes made differently, and that fluidity is the chief obstacle to reading it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It defeats machines as well as people: recognition rates fall away sharply on the variant forms as against the standard ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A single fragment may carry several separate inscriptions, and the order in which its characters are to be read is not simply down a column.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bones were drilled and heat-cracked before use and come out of the ground fragile and broken, so the script is studied through rubbings, hand facsimiles and transcriptions rather than the originals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it records is not only divination: administrative notes and even a scribe’s writing practice are among the things cut into the bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"oracle bone script","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","language","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Bang Li, Jing Yang, Yujie Liang, Xiaobin Hu, Zengmao Ding, Xu Peng, Shengwei Han, Peichao Qin, Donghao Luo, Taisong Jin, Feng Gao, Yongge Liu, and Rongrong Ji, “OBIMD: A Multi-modal Dataset for Contextual Interpretation of Oracle Bone Inscriptions,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 13 (2026): 681, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13128845/. [Open access]","Pengjie Wang, Kaile Zhang, Xinyu Wang, Shengwei Han, Yongge Liu, Jinpeng Wan, Haisu Guan, Zhebin Kuang, Lianwen Jin, Xiang Bai, and Yuliang Liu, “An Open Dataset for Oracle Bone Character Recognition and Decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11379903/. [Open access]","Ludovica Ottaviano, Kathryn Kelley, Mattia Cartolano, and Silvia Ferrara, “Metaphors and the Invention of Writing,” <i>Topics in Cognitive Science</i> 18, no. 1 (2026): 245–63, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12831613/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Hua Zhang, Deborah C. Merrett, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, Yuling He, Hongbin Yue, Zhanwei Yue, and Dongya Y. Yang, “Osteoarthritis, Labour Division, and Occupational Specialization of the Late Shang China — Insights from Yinxu (ca. 1250–1046 B.C.),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0176329, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5413014/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Shang_dynasty_inscribed_scapula_Ink_rubbing.jpg/1280px-Shang_dynasty_inscribed_scapula_Ink_rubbing.jpg","title":"An ink rubbing of an inscribed bone","desc":"A rubbing of a large inscribed ox bone in the National Museum of China; the script is normally studied from rubbings like this rather than from the fragile originals.","credit":"Rubbing of unknown authorship, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"An ink rubbing in which a broken slab of bone appears as a black shape, with columns of small angular Chinese characters standing out in white and the breaks running white across it."}},
{"id":"cnh-072","num":72,"category":"Shang","question":"In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a cattle shoulder blade or turtle plastron was hollowed behind and burned until the face split, and the record was cut beside the crack.","questions":["One record of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reads that on the gui-mao day it was cracked, Ke tested, and ten bovines should be offered to Shang Jia.","Nearly 22,000 divination sentences survive from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against barely 400 records of an outcome, and the inscribed bones were a minority of those used."],"answer":"Shang divination","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Practised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sentences read</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nearly 22,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Outcomes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">barely 400 recorded</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Diviner group</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 25 men</span></div>","traditional":"商代占卜","hanzi":"商代占卜","pinyin":"Shāngdài zhānbǔ","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang divination</b> is the practice by which the late Shang court put questions to its ancestors and to the powers, by burning bone until it cracked and reading the crack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The material was cattle shoulder blades and turtle plastrons, prepared beforehand by drilling hollows into the back so that heat applied there would split the face in a controlled way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was cut beside the crack follows a form that repeats: the day, the diviner who tested it, and the proposition put.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of them reads that on the gui-mao day it was cracked and Ke tested, and that on the next jia-chen day ten bovines should be offered to Shang Jia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The day named is not arbitrary, since an ancestor received his offering on the day whose name he carried, and Shang Jia takes his on a jia day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The kings divined continually to settle that offerings were right in kind, number, colour and combination, and killed by the correct ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They acted as though divination could find the particular contract that would satisfy a given ancestor and lift a curse, since the dead were held to keep power over the living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diviners worked in groups, known by their names appearing on the same bones, by what they asked about and by the pits their bones came from, one such group counting about 25 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over the two centuries at Anyang the practice grew systematic, ending in a fixed cycle of five rites to the dead kings and queens run through the ten-day weeks, and diviners other than the king became markedly fewer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What survives is a sample and not a record: a large annotated corpus holds nearly 22,000 divination sentences and barely 400 notes of an outcome, and inscribed bones were only a minority of those used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang divination","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","religion","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Bang Li, Jing Yang, Yujie Liang, Xiaobin Hu, Zengmao Ding, Xu Peng, Shengwei Han, Peichao Qin, Donghao Luo, Taisong Jin, Feng Gao, Yongge Liu, and Rongrong Ji, “OBIMD: A Multi-modal Dataset for Contextual Interpretation of Oracle Bone Inscriptions,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 13 (2026): 681, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13128845/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Shang_Inscribed_Tortoise_Plastron_%28for_divination%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Inscribed_Tortoise_Plastron_%28for_divination%29.jpg","title":"An inscribed tortoise plastron","desc":"A plastron used for divination in the reign of Wu Ding, its surface crossed by cracks with characters cut in groups beside them.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A pale oval tortoise shell plate mounted on a fabric board, crossed by branching dark cracks, with small angular Chinese characters incised in groups between them."}},
{"id":"cnh-073","num":73,"category":"Shang","question":"Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the 22nd Shang king, the dynasty’s own written record begins, no inscription at Anyang naming any of the three kings before him.","questions":["For three years <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not speak, leaving government to his chief minister, before dreaming of the sage he then had searched for across the country.","Five lunar eclipses in the records of the diviners working for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have been calculated to a window between 1201 and 1181 BCE."],"answer":"Wu Ding","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1192 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22nd Shang king</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eclipses dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1201 – 1181 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silent for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 years, in tradition</span></div>","traditional":"武丁","hanzi":"武丁","pinyin":"Wǔ Dīng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Wu Ding</b> is the Shang king with whom the dynasty’s own written record begins, the 22nd of its line and the first at Anyang whose reign the inscriptions reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronology project puts him at 1250 to 1192 BCE, and the late Shang is usually reckoned from him rather than from the move to Anyang three reigns earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No inscription found there names any of those three predecessors, so the site’s writing and his reign begin together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A group of about 25 diviners was at work in his later years, and five lunar eclipses recorded among them have been calculated to a window between 1201 and 1181 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those eclipses are among the few fixed points from which his regnal years can be worked out at all, and the later Shang kings divined far more themselves and used far fewer diviners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The transmitted account gives him a different kind of greatness: he came to the throne meaning to make the Shang flourish again, and had found nobody able to help him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For three years he did not speak, leaving every decision to his chief minister while he watched how the country behaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Then he dreamed of a sage, compared the face against all his ministers and officers without finding it, had it drawn, and had the country searched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The man was found among convicts raising earthworks at the difficult passes of Fu; Wu Ding said that was he, talked with him, made him counsellor, and gave him the name of the place, so that he is called Fu Yue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When a pheasant settled on a sacrificial tripod and cried it was read as a warning that he favoured his own father’s rites above the other ancestors’, and the record says that once he reformed his government the dynasty prospered again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Wu Ding","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","history","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 195–96, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 196–97, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 11, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/%E9%99%88%E4%B9%A6%E7%94%BB%E5%8E%86%E4%BB%A3%E5%B8%9D%E7%8E%8B%E9%81%93%E7%BB%9F%E5%9B%BE%E9%92%B1%E9%99%88%E7%BE%A4%E4%B9%A6%E8%B5%9E%E5%90%88%E5%86%8C8.png/1280px-%E9%99%88%E4%B9%A6%E7%94%BB%E5%8E%86%E4%BB%A3%E5%B8%9D%E7%8E%8B%E9%81%93%E7%BB%9F%E5%9B%BE%E9%92%B1%E9%99%88%E7%BE%A4%E4%B9%A6%E8%B5%9E%E5%90%88%E5%86%8C8.png","title":"Wu Ding dreams of a helper","desc":"A painted album leaf whose title reads that Gaozong of Yin dreamed he was granted a good helper; Gaozong is Wu Ding's temple name.","credit":"Palace Museum, Beijing, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A painted album leaf: at the right a palace roof with two standing figures on the terrace above and guards asleep below it, at the left a page of Chinese calligraphy."}},
{"id":"cnh-074","num":74,"category":"Shang","question":"Known first from the oracle bones and then from her own grave at Anyang, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a consort of Wu Ding who oversaw a domain of her own and led troops.","questions":["The only intact top royal burial yet dug at Anyang is that of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why more is known of her than of people who outranked her at court.","Ritual bronzes made for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were cast from very pure copper by controlled alloying, where those of lesser elites were remelted and carry more impurities."],"answer":"Fu Hao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consort of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wu Ding, r. c. 1250 – 1192 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Metal in tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1.6 tonnes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Royal tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">up to 7 times larger</span></div>","traditional":"婦好","hanzi":"妇好","pinyin":"Fù Hǎo","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Fu Hao</b> is a consort of the Shang king Wu Ding, known first from the oracle bones and afterwards from her own grave at Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That order is what makes her remarkable: an individual named in a record of the 2nd millennium BCE and then met in the ground is a rare thing in Chinese archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fu is a term for a high-status woman or a royal spouse, and under a strictly patrimonial system such consorts held privileges and kinds of authority that were theirs alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The inscriptions show women of that rank overseeing territorial domains of their own, presenting tribute and gifts, leading troops into battle and going hunting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She was one of several of Wu Ding’s consorts, Fu Jing being another, and by no means the most important person buried at the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her grave is the only intact top royal burial yet excavated at Anyang, which is why more is known of her than of people who outranked her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It held something like 1.6 tonnes of metal, and the kingly tombs at Xibeigang, looted over and over, were up to seven times its size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her ritual bronzes were cast from very pure copper by carefully controlled alloying, where the bronzes of lesser elites were made by remelting and carry more impurities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A woman who had borne male heirs was revered after death alongside the principal male ancestors, so the dead of her rank kept a place in the ritual round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the Shang sources give is description rather than praise: they record what she did, and the moral archetypes of what a woman ought to be are a Zhou habit rather than a Shang one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Fu Hao","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Sheri A. Lullo, review of <i>Women in Ancient China</i>, by Bret Hinsch, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 413–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76794. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 11, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Hua Zhang, Deborah C. Merrett, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, Yuling He, Hongbin Yue, Zhanwei Yue, and Dongya Y. Yang, “Osteoarthritis, Labour Division, and Occupational Specialization of the Late Shang China — Insights from Yinxu (ca. 1250–1046 B.C.),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0176329, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5413014/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Fuhao_Owl_Zun_front.jpg/1280px-Fuhao_Owl_Zun_front.jpg","title":"An owl-shaped zun made for Fu Hao","desc":"A bronze wine vessel in the form of a standing owl, cast with an inscription naming her.","credit":"Photo by Cangminzho, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A green and grey bronze vessel shaped as a standing owl on two thick legs and a tail, its surface covered in raised spiral and animal-mask ornament, with a crested lid."}},
{"id":"cnh-075","num":75,"category":"Shang","question":"Burial M5 at Xiaotun in Anyang, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is one of the very few Shang graves found entirely intact and one of only two whose occupant can be named.","questions":["Jades already centuries old when they were buried, made by Neolithic cultures, were among the roughly 1,500 objects in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Anyang.","Inscriptions on ritual vessels from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, matched against names in the oracle bones, are what let its occupant be identified as an individual."],"answer":"tomb of Fu Hao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wu Ding, c. 1250 – 1192 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Objects</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,500</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronzes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">468 or 210, by count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Two ding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">128 and 117.5 kilograms (282 and 259 pounds)</span></div>","traditional":"婦好墓","hanzi":"妇好墓","pinyin":"Fù Hǎo mù","translations":"","abstract":"<b>tomb of Fu Hao</b> is burial M5 at Xiaotun in Anyang, the grave of a consort of the Shang king Wu Ding and one of the very few Shang tombs found entirely intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It lies away from the royal cemetery at Xibeigang, and beside the graves in that cemetery it is a small burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What was in it made its reputation: about 1,500 objects, among them weapons, bells, blades, mirrors and bronze sculptures of tigers, together with a great quantity of jade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of its bronze ding stand 80 centimetres (31 inches) high and weigh 128 and 117.5 kilograms (282 and 259 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of the jades were already ancient when they were buried, pieces made by Neolithic cultures, which suggests they were valued for their age as much as for their look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Jade figures in formal poses and ceremonial dress went into the grave with her, probably meant as servants who would go on presenting offerings to the ancestors for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of only about five rich-bronze burials at Anyang found unlooted, and one of only two anywhere on the site whose occupant can be named at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The naming is done by matching inscriptions on the ritual vessels against names in the oracle bones, which is why an individual of the 2nd millennium BCE can be met here at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the name is unsettled, since several scholars read the graph as Zi rather than Hao, so the woman everyone calls Fu Hao may in fact be Fu Zi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So is the count of what it held: one study gives 468 bronzes in the tomb and another 210, and the two are almost certainly counting different things rather than contradicting each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"tomb of Fu Hao","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Sheri A. Lullo, review of <i>Women in Ancient China</i>, by Bret Hinsch, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 413–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76794. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Fu_Hao_Tomb%2C_c._1200_BC%2C_Reign_of_King_Wu_Ding%2C_Shang_Dynasty_1.jpg/1280px-Fu_Hao_Tomb%2C_c._1200_BC%2C_Reign_of_King_Wu_Ding%2C_Shang_Dynasty_1.jpg","title":"The tomb of Fu Hao, reconstructed at Yinxu","desc":"A full-size reconstruction of the burial chamber at the site museum in Anyang, laid out as the excavators recorded it: bronze vessels, bells and stone objects across the floor, with the skeletons of sacrificed attendants in the ledges along the walls. The objects shown are replicas, the originals being held in the National Museum of China in Beijing and the Henan Museum in Zhengzhou.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A rectangular pit with earth walls, its floor crowded with bronze vessels, two large bells and stone blades, and human skeletons laid out in narrow ledges along both side walls."}},
{"id":"cnh-076","num":76,"category":"Shang","question":"Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the ruler was the head of the apex lineage rather than an officer over subjects, and power passed to a king's brothers or to his sons.","questions":["A monopoly of approach to the royal ancestors, who were held to carry requests to the high god, is what made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> more than the leadership of one family among many.","Emblems on bronzes once read as lineage names may instead be the names of some 70 government offices, which would make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a good deal more bureaucratic than it looks."],"answer":"Shang kingship","answerText":"Shang kingship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In place</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31, in the traditional list</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Offices named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 70</span></div>","traditional":"商代王權","hanzi":"商代王权","pinyin":"Shāngdài wángquán","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang kingship</b> is the authority the Shang king held over the ruling house, the armies and the rites owed to the royal dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The traditional list of those kings runs to 31, Wu Ding standing 22nd in it and Di Xin last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Power passed dynastically inside the house, in hereditary succession from a king to his brothers or to his sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That house sat at the head of a society ordered by kinship, and on the fullest recent account the king was the leader of its apex lineage rather than an officer set over subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A much-quoted line in the <i>Zuozhuan</i> calls sacrifice and war the great affairs of the state, which for the Shang may be better put as the great affairs of the lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both grew more regular over the two centuries at Anyang, turning from occasional undertakings into systematic ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What lifted the king above every other head of a family was his monopoly of approach to the royal ancestors, who were held to carry human business up to the high god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rank was physical as well as ritual, since the casting and circulation of metal followed the hierarchy closely: the highest elite were given vessels of carefully controlled alloy made from very pure copper, while those below them had secondary alloys with more impurities in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Beneath the king his delegates and messengers were mostly <i>zi</i> princes, ranked by whether they held high military rank, marked <i>ya</i>, or high official rank, marked <i>bo</i>, and the heir was styled <i>Xiao Wang</i>, 'the Small King'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The emblems on bronzes, long read as the names of lineages, have lately been re-read as the names of some 70 government offices covering the army, finance, construction, communication and the running of the palace, each passing to the senior man of a lineage and then to one of his descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","china","bronze age","politics"],"sources":["Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Vessel_for_wine_%28Fangyi%29._2_pictographs._Shang_Dynasty%2C_1300-1050_BCE._Bronze._From_China._Victoria_and_Albert_Museum%2C_London.jpg/1280px-Vessel_for_wine_%28Fangyi%29._2_pictographs._Shang_Dynasty%2C_1300-1050_BCE._Bronze._From_China._Victoria_and_Albert_Museum%2C_London.jpg","title":"A Shang bronze vessel with its owner's mark","desc":"A fangyi, a lidded wine container of the Shang period, in the Victoria and Albert Museum in London. The vessel and its lid carry an inscription of two pictographs, the kind of sign long read as the emblem of the owner's lineage and lately re-read as the name of an office.","credit":"Photograph by Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin, CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A tall rectangular bronze vessel with a hipped, roof-like lid topped by a small knob, its sides covered in dense angular animal-mask ornament."}},
{"id":"cnh-077","num":77,"category":"Shang","question":"Rain for the crops and floods held short of devastation were both in the gift of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who was sovereign over the natural world and over the ancestral spirits themselves.","questions":["No one could approach <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> directly, so a request was laid before the ancestors instead, for them to carry up to a power that stood far from the living.","The king alone could receive the spirits of the royal dead and of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ruling the four quarters as the surrogate of a god whose grant his throne was."],"answer":"Shangdi","answerText":"Shangdi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"上帝","hanzi":"上帝","pinyin":"Shàngdì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shangdi</b> is the high god of the Shang, a name meaning 'High Sovereign', and the earliest references to him are the oracle-bone inscriptions themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was sovereign over the whole natural world and over the ancestral spirits, who settled the destiny of humans in his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The forces of nature were his: the rain a crop needed came from him, and so did a river flood held short of devastation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Command was his defining power, and the inscriptions carry the phrase <i>Di ming</i>, 'the decree of Di', a deity above all others being the one who could issue one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Yet he stood so far from the realm of the living that there was no direct way to approach him, so a request had to be laid before the ancestors to be carried up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Those ancestors were themselves graded, the older the more powerful, and all of them ranked below the powers of the pantheon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The one man who could cross that distance was the king, who alone could <i>bin</i>, 'receive the spirits', of Di and of the royal dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He ruled the four quarters as Di’s surrogate, and the right to rule was itself held to be Di’s grant, so that no line separated his religious authority from his political one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether he was one god at all is unsettled, since he has been identified with the first ancestor of the Shang and read instead as a general word for divinity, the graph being used of heads of lineages in the plural and as an honorific for an individual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Zhou afterwards spoke of <i>Tian</i>, 'Heaven', and Shangdi has been thought the same power under another name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["deity","religion","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 16, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Lisa Raphals, “Languages of Fate: Semantic Fields in Chinese and Greek,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 70–106, at 74, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Oracle_Bone_Script_Di_Variants.jpg/960px-Oracle_Bone_Script_Di_Variants.jpg","title":"The graph for Di in oracle-bone script","desc":"Sixteen variant forms of the graph di as it is written in the oracle-bone inscriptions, cut from rubbings and gathered into one plate. The name Shangdi is known first from inscriptions of this kind, and how the graph should be read is part of what is still argued over.","credit":"Compilation by the Wikimedia Commons user Quinlan718, CC BY-SA 4.0.","alt":"A grid of sixteen small black-and-white cut-outs, each showing a different angular form of the same incised character: a horizontal bar or triangle above a forked, crossed stem."}},
{"id":"cnh-078","num":78,"category":"Shang","question":"Making the dead into proper ancestors, who would then guide the nature powers and the high god, was the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a religion that belonged to every rank.","questions":["Far fewer cattle lie in the sacrificial pits than the inscriptions record being offered under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the likeliest answer being that the living ate them at the feast.","Burials, sacrificial pits, architecture, writing and feasting are all forms taken by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its traces sit all over a Shang site rather than in one building."],"answer":"Shang ancestor worship","answerText":"Shang ancestor worship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One ox</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 200 kilograms (440 pounds) of meat</span></div>","traditional":"商代祖先崇拜","hanzi":"商代祖先崇拜","pinyin":"Shāngdài zǔxiān chóngbài","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang ancestor worship</b> is the cult the Shang paid to their own dead, a religion held to have belonged to all Shang people and one of the basic components of their society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its work was not simply to remember the dead but to make them into proper ancestors, who would then guide the nature powers and the high god on the living’s behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It took several forms at once — burials, sacrificial pits, architecture, writing and feasting — so its traces lie all over a Shang site rather than in one building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rites ran on a calendar, repeated through the year and addressed to the high ancestors, the dead rulers and the consort queens alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Meat was cooked and presented in the great tripods, fermented drink was offered in three-legged beakers, and under the Shang such vessels and the jades beside them were tied to communicating with the ancestral spirits and to little else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Spirits were held to be beyond control unless the living acted, which made the whole relationship a dangerous one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet far fewer cattle lie in the sacrificial pits than the inscriptions record being offered, and the likeliest answer is that the living ate them, at feasts that went with the sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One head of cattle yields over 200 kilograms (440 pounds) of meat, and by the late phases at Anyang cattle had passed pigs in dietary importance even in ordinary quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So the same beasts fed the living and the dead, and by the end of the period cattle had overtaken human captives in royal sacrificial importance, while wild animals were almost never offered at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spending on burial fell across the centuries, read not as impoverishment but as a deliberate move from showing rank in material to showing it in symbol, and the cult itself is credited with driving the bronze industry that served it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","religion","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 15, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Clevelandart_1960.42.jpg/1280px-Clevelandart_1960.42.jpg","title":"A jue, the Shang cup for a libation","desc":"A bronze jue of the Shang period in the Cleveland Museum of Art. Vessels of this shape poured the fermented drink offered to the ancestral spirits, and a clan sign is cast inside the bowl.","credit":"Cleveland Museum of Art, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A slender bronze vessel on three long blade-like legs, with a deep bowl drawn out into a pouring lip at one end and a pointed tail at the other, two capped posts rising from its rim, and dense spiral ornament round its body."}},
{"id":"cnh-079","num":79,"category":"Shang","question":"The excavated pits at Anyang run about ten people to every animal, where the inscriptions record <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and animal offerings in something like parity.","questions":["Marking a royal funeral, answering a disaster, consecrating a building or securing fertility are among the occasions for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a practice known across many societies.","More than 80 skulls under the East Gate at Shimao show <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in China is older than the Shang by half a millennium, and its victims can now be sexed by their DNA."],"answer":"human sacrifice","answerText":"human sacrifice","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang record</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Shimao</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 80 skulls at 1 gate</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pit ratio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 10 people to 1 animal</span></div>","traditional":"人祭","hanzi":"人祭","pinyin":"rénjì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>human sacrifice</b> is the killing of a person as an offering, known across many societies and undertaken to mark a royal funeral, to answer a disaster, to atone for a wrong, to consecrate a building or a place, or to secure fertility and health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Victims were also killed to serve a high-ranking person as retainers in the next world, so a single offering may be commemorative, expiatory, propitiatory and dedicatory at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In China the practice is older than the Shang: at Shimao, a walled city on the Loess Plateau, more than 80 human skulls lie buried beneath the East Gate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two forms were used there, mass pits at the gates and at the palace, and victims entombed with the owner of a high-status grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient DNA has since corrected the record: 9 of the 10 East Gate victims were men, three of them read as women from the bones alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Shang the inscriptions show animals and people offered in something like parity, while the excavated pits run about ten people to every animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One divination proposes an exorcism to three high ancestors with a hundred jars of millet ale, a hundred Qiang captives and three hundred specially reared sheep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Qiang were a neighbouring people whose prisoners of war were offered like other flesh, and another inscription asks simply whether Qiang should be used in the offering to an ancestress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What royal practice divided was not the human from the animal but the wild from the domesticated, and a person could be brought into the second class through enslavement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor did any of it begin at Anyang: at Xiaoshuangqiao near Zhengzhou, a middle Shang site, the final report counts 33 pits holding human remains, one of them with at least 32 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","religion","archaeology","history","china"],"sources":["María Constanza Ceruti, “Frozen Mummies from Andean Mountaintop Shrines: Bioarchaeology and Ethnohistory of Inca Human Sacrifice,” <i>BioMed Research International</i> 2015 (2015): 439428, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4543117/. [Open access]","Zehui Chen et al., “Ancient DNA from Shimao City Records Kinship Practices in Neolithic China,” <i>Nature</i> 648, no. 8094 (2025): 659–67, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12711557/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Shang_Sacrificial_Pit.jpg/1280px-Shang_Sacrificial_Pit.jpg","title":"A sacrificial pit at the Yin Ruins","desc":"An excavated pit kept and displayed where it was dug, at the on-site museum of the Yin Ruins in Anyang. The human remains lie as the excavators recorded them, under glass.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A shallow rectangular pit of pale earth seen from above through glass, with several human skeletons lying in it at different angles, some incomplete."}},
{"id":"cnh-080","num":80,"category":"Shang","question":"Shaft tombs cut with four ramps running out to the cardinal points are what makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recognisable, and their number identifies nine kings who ruled at Yinxu.","questions":["The cruciform tomb M1004 and the ramped M260 both lie in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which held the tallest and heaviest bronze the Shang ever cast before the robbers reached it.","Elsewhere at Anyang the dead lie among the houses, so the separateness of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, north-west of the settlement, is itself a statement about who was buried there."],"answer":"Xibeigang","answerText":"Xibeigang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings identified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ramps</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, to the cardinal points</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tallest bronze</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 centimetres (52 inches)</span></div>","traditional":"西北岡","hanzi":"西北冈","pinyin":"Xīběigāng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Xibeigang</b> is the royal cemetery of the late Shang, the group of largest burials at Anyang, lying north-west of the settlement and set apart from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their number identifies nine kings as having ruled at Yinxu during the late Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are shaft tombs, but cut with four ramps running out to the cardinal directions, a layout that belonged to the ritual rather than to the convenience of digging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Below the body many Shang graves have a small waist pit, and it often holds a sacrificed animal, most often a dog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Elsewhere at Anyang the dead lie among and beside the houses, so the separateness of this ground is itself a statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> M1004 is the cruciform tomb long known to be royal, and at the very bottom of it lay two monumental four-legged ding, one carrying wild buffalo heads in relief and the other the heads of Sika deer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That pair is thought to have belonged to Fu Ji, the first heir of Wu Ding, who died before taking the throne, since an heir apparent had the right to a royal tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> M260, east of the cemetery near Wuguancun, is a large tomb with one long narrow southern ramp, and the tallest and heaviest bronze the Shang ever cast, 133 centimetres (52 inches) high, came out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of the thousands of tombs at Anyang were looted in antiquity and the royal ones emptied over and over, so what remains in them is what the robbers left; nor did tombs on this scale begin here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Size alone does not measure the effort a Shang burial took: outside this cemetery graves of very different sizes carry comparable goods, and what the ritual turned on was the interment rather than the hole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Yinxu_-_%E6%AE%B7%E5%A2%9F_%285067245178%29.jpg/1280px-Yinxu_-_%E6%AE%B7%E5%A2%9F_%285067245178%29.jpg","title":"The plan of royal grave M1400 at Xibeigang","desc":"The outline of one of the royal tombs in the East Zone at Xibeigang, marked out in clipped hedges above the filled-in shaft at the Yinxu site park. What is traced on the ground is the rectangular shaft with a ramp running away from it.","credit":"Photograph by tak.wing, CC BY-SA 2.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A wide lawn on a hazy day, with the plan of a large rectangular pit and a long approach ramp picked out in low clipped hedges, sprinklers playing beyond it."}},
{"id":"cnh-081","num":81,"category":"Shang","question":"A king might use nine ding under the Zhou rules, the second rank seven and the fourth one, so a grave's set of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be read as a statement of standing.","questions":["The names and uses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> come from the received textual tradition rather than from residue analysis, which is why a steamer known for grain can turn up holding skulls.","Wine vessels grew fewer in graves and food vessels more common after the Zhou conquest, one sign that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had stopped being only a way of speaking to the ancestors."],"answer":"Chinese ritual bronzes","answerText":"Chinese ritual bronzes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang and Zhou</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">King's ding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9, then 7, 5, 3 or 1 below</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Basic set</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 pieces, a gu and a jue</span></div>","traditional":"青銅禮器","hanzi":"青铜礼器","pinyin":"qīngtóng lǐqì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chinese ritual bronzes</b> are cast vessels made for the offerings owed to the dead, used in banqueting, in presenting food and drink to the ancestors, and in signalling the standing of whoever owned them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each shape carries a name of its own: the ding a cooking tripod, the gui a grain server, the hu a liquor container, the jian a water container, the yan a steamer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those functions come from the received textual tradition rather than from residue or wear analysis of the vessels themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mostly the two agree, but not always, since grain is the likely thing steamed in a yan and human skulls have been found in yan in three burials at Yinxu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The smallest set of all is two pieces, a gu beaker and a jue pitcher, and a grave holding even that was not a commoner’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Shang society can be ranked from its bronze assemblages as readily as from the size and shape of its graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the Zhou the rule was written down: a king was entitled to nine ding, the second rank seven, the third five and the fourth three or one, with grain vessels, liquor and water containers and bells specified by number to match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Burial finds bear it out, one tomb in Gansu of the 11th or 10th century BCE holding 7 ding, 1 yan, 2 zun, 1 zhi, 1 jiao, 1 jia, 1 he, 2 you and 3 gui.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The conquest changed what they were for as well as who might have them, wine vessels growing fewer in graves and food vessels of the xu, gou and gui types more common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They stopped being uniquely a means of speaking to the ancestors and became a way of recording an event or an office, many carrying an inscription that ties the vessel to one person or lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","art","archaeology","history","china"],"sources":["Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/20260520_Ding_Set_and_Gui_Set_from_Guo_State_Tombs.jpg/1280px-20260520_Ding_Set_and_Gui_Set_from_Guo_State_Tombs.jpg","title":"Five ding and four gui from the Guo state tombs","desc":"A graded set of five tripod cauldrons above four lidded grain vessels, Western Zhou, from the Guo state tombs and held by the Pingdingshan Museum. A set of this size is one grade of the ranked allowance, and the diagram on the wall behind sets out the ranks it belongs to.","credit":"Photograph by the Wikimedia Commons user Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0.","alt":"A museum case with five bronze tripod cauldrons on an upper shelf, each a little smaller than the one before, and four lidded round bronze bowls with loop handles on the shelf below."}},
{"id":"cnh-082","num":82,"category":"Shang","question":"One large four-legged ding from Erligang took 19 mould pieces and 5 cores and six separate pours, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the scale the Shang worked it.","questions":["A clay model within, clay moulds without, and the molten alloy poured into the space between the two is the whole of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the method China's ritual bronzes were made by.","Hollow-legged pots shaped over inner moulds may be the ancestor of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the potter's technique standing behind the one that made the bronzes."],"answer":"piece-mould casting","answerText":"piece-mould casting","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Erligang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One ding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 mould pieces, 5 cores</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pours</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6, in 3 stages</span></div>","traditional":"塊範法","hanzi":"块范法","pinyin":"kuàifàn fǎ","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Piece-mould casting</b> is how China’s ritual bronzes were made, casting being the shaping of metal while it is molten by pouring it into a mould, where forging beats it into shape cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Here a clay model sits within and clay moulds without, and the molten alloy of copper with tin and lead is poured into the space between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ancestor is a potter’s trick: hollow-legged vessels were shaped over inner moulds, the three legs made singly and joined, or the clay coiled around three leg moulds at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bronze form of it, inner model parts inside outer piece moulds, was first established at Erligang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Metal spacers held model and mould apart while the bronze went in, and their common use in the south suggests that particular trick was worked out there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A single large four-legged ding from Erligang took 19 mould pieces and 5 cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those were a two-piece bottom mould round a cross-shaped core, five moulds and one core for the four walls, and twelve moulds and four cores for the legs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The metal went in six times over, in three stages: the bottom first, then the walls and handles joined to it, then the feet cast onto the base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far the moulds were reused is not agreed, one account having decorated mould sections used again and again across many vessel types, another that each vessel had its own mould and never the same one twice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the second reading no two are exactly alike in size or weight, and either way the shapes followed what the method allowed, with lids, handles and ornament cast on to a vessel already made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["practice","technology","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Shang_Copper_Ingot%2C_Bronze_Casting_Implements%2C_%26_Clay_Mould_Fragments_%2845148962364%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Copper_Ingot%2C_Bronze_Casting_Implements%2C_%26_Clay_Mould_Fragments_%2845148962364%29.jpg","title":"Clay moulds, an ingot and the tools for cutting them","desc":"Fragments of clay mould, one with its ornament cut into the inner face and another shaped as an animal head, beside a copper ingot and the bronze knife and fine needles used to carve the moulds. On-site museum of the Yin Ruins at Anyang; the ingot came from Xiaotun South.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A museum case holding several grey clay mould fragments, one carved with a scrolled design on its inner face and another shaped as an animal head, beside a lumpy green-crusted copper ingot, a long bronze knife and four fine bronze needles."}},
{"id":"cnh-083","num":83,"category":"Shang","question":"In the oracle bones the graph for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> works as a verb of sacrifice, the king its subject, a dead king or queen its indirect object and the offering its direct one.","questions":["Among Shang bronzes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the one vessel used specifically to prepare and offer meat to the dead, and burnt animal bone has been found inside examples taken from burials.","The <i>Mozi</i> has a Xia king cast the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Kunwu, and its oracle promises that the nine will pass from Xia to Yin and from Yin to Zhou."],"answer":"ding","answerText":"ding","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Small fangding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22.9 centimetres (9 inches)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nine ding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">of the myth, passing 3 kingdoms</span></div>","traditional":"鼎","hanzi":"鼎","pinyin":"dǐng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>ding</b> is the Chinese ritual cauldron, standing on three legs or four, and among Shang bronzes the one vessel used specifically to prepare and offer meat to the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What most vessel names mean is taken from later texts rather than from residue analysis, but the ding is one place the two meet, burnt animal bone having been found inside examples taken from burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, bones in a ding are not proof of a feast, and one study of two Anyang cemeteries will not read the food and drink vessels that way without more work, since they may be provisions for the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the oracle-bone inscriptions the graph works as a verb of sacrifice, the king usually its subject, a dead king or queen its indirect object and the offering itself its direct one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One divination asks whether meat cut by the yue axe should be offered in the ding to Tang, the dynasty’s founder, and whether danger will follow; another whether thirty oxen prepared in a set way should go to Father Ding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They range enormously in size, from a four-legged example under 23 centimetres (9 inches) high to the largest bronze the Shang ever cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four-legged ding were far rarer than three-legged ones and belonged strictly to the ruling class and its heirs, though one might be bequeathed to a queen mother after her death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That scarcity is the stratification of Shang bronze users cast in metal, and the metal followed rank as well, the finest work using very pure copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Out of all this grew the myth that made the ding the token of a dynasty’s right to rule, the <i>Mozi</i> having a Xia king send a man to dig ore in the mountains and rivers and cast the vessels at Kunwu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its oracle promised ding that would boil without kindling and move without being carried, and said the nine would pass through three kingdoms, Xia losing them to the Yin and the Yin to the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","art","archaeology","history","china"],"sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 54, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/HouMuWuDingFullView.jpg/1280px-HouMuWuDingFullView.jpg","title":"The Hou Mu Wu ding","desc":"The largest surviving Shang bronze, a four-legged ding 133 centimetres high, cast in the late Shang and recovered from the royal cemetery at Anyang. A mobile phone left on the plinth gives the scale.","credit":"Photograph by the Wikimedia Commons user Mlogic, CC BY-SA 3.0.","alt":"A massive rectangular bronze cauldron on four thick cylindrical legs, with two upright loop handles on its rim, its sides covered in bands of dense scroll ornament and animal masks, standing on a low museum plinth."}},
{"id":"cnh-084","num":84,"category":"Shang","question":"A face seen from the front resolving into an animal in profile on either side is the split image characteristic of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose commonest motif is the taotie.","questions":["The old reading of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as pictures of the animals given in sacrifice fails on the list, since buffalo, tigers, owls and elephants were almost never offered.","Cut into the mould rather than worked on the finished metal, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has an ancestry in Neolithic jades already shaped like birds, turtles, cicadas and silkworms."],"answer":"Shang bronze decoration","answerText":"Shang bronze decoration","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cast from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Split image</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 face, 2 profiles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named beasts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6, besides dragons</span></div>","traditional":"商代青銅器紋飾","hanzi":"商代青铜器纹饰","pinyin":"Shāngdài qīngtóngqì wénshì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang bronze decoration</b> is the dense animal ornament cast into the surface of ritual vessels, wild and uncanny creatures covering the feasting bronzes of the Anyang elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its most prominent motif is the taotie, a face of beasts with open fanged mouths whose horns nevertheless mark them as plant-eaters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of it is set as a split image, so that a face seen from the front resolves into an animal in profile on either side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That doubling has been explained as a way of showing a solid creature on a flat surface, as a reflection of dualism in Shang thought, and as decoration whose illusion says how important its owner was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ancestry is older than bronze, since Neolithic jades were already shaped like or decorated with birds, turtles, cicadas and silkworms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The ornament was cut into the mould rather than worked on the finished metal, and on the pair of monumental four-legged cauldrons from one royal tomb the heads of wild deer and wild buffalo stand out in relief with whole animals engraved inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not everything cast on a vessel is ornament: the emblems long read as the names of lineages, and lately as the names of offices, are writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor was the work generic, since the founders knew whom each vessel was being made for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the creatures mean is disputed, and the old answer that they show the animals given in sacrifice does not survive the list, because almost none of them was ever offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What buffalo, tigers, horned owls, elephants, deer and big-horned sheep have in common is wildness, and one vessel makes the point exactly, a split buffalo named by its backward horns and by the graph for buffalo cast inside, whose fanged mouth belongs to no buffalo at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["art","object","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/%E5%95%86%E4%BB%A3%E6%99%9A%E6%9C%9F%E9%A5%95%E9%A4%AE%E7%BA%B9%E6%96%9D2.jpg/1280px-%E5%95%86%E4%BB%A3%E6%99%9A%E6%9C%9F%E9%A5%95%E9%A4%AE%E7%BA%B9%E6%96%9D2.jpg","title":"A late Shang jia and its taotie bands","desc":"A bronze jia of the late Shang in the National Museum of China. Two registers of ornament run round the body, each mask divided down the middle by a vertical flange, which is the split composition the taotie is built on.","credit":"Photograph by the Wikimedia Commons user 三猎, CC BY-SA 4.0.","alt":"A bronze vessel on three long blade-like legs with a wide mouth, a loop handle and two capped posts rising from the rim, its body wrapped in two bands of dense scrolled ornament divided by raised vertical ribs."}},
{"id":"cnh-085","num":85,"category":"Shang","question":"Blades called zhang, taken to be sceptres, and ge and yue axes are the commonest forms of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and nothing shows any of the weapons was ever used.","questions":["Xiaotun lies near no source of the stone, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut from material carried in from Xinjiang, southern Henan or Liaoning, and some arrived as tribute.","Very little <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the graves of ordinary people at Anyang, though more than 2,000 pieces are known from the capital, most of them weapons and sceptres."],"answer":"Shang jade","answerText":"Shang jade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Worked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yinxu jades</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 2,000 known</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One workshop</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600+ unfinished, 260 grinders</span></div>","traditional":"商代玉器","hanzi":"商代玉器","pinyin":"Shāngdài yùqì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang jade</b> is the worked stone of the Shang, made into weapons and sceptres far more than into vessels, and some of it inscribed with clan insignia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Blades called zhang, taken to be sceptres, along with ge and yue axes, are the commonest forms, and the same shapes turn up later at Sanxingdui far to the south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing shows the weapons were ever used, and what marks them as ceremonial is the decoration: birds, figures of men with animals, and most often the mask with its pair of bulging eyes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ring-shaped jades and ring dragons carried on from the Neolithic, now cut flat with fine incisions rather than modelled in the round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than 2,000 jade objects had been dug at Yinxu by the mid-1980s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The work was done on the spot, two houses inside the palace and temple complex holding over 600 unfinished stone objects, many of them zhang blades, with 260 grinding stones beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xiaotun lies near no source of the stone, so it came in from a distance, Hetian in Xinjiang, Nanyang in southern Henan and Xiuyan in Liaoning all being proposed; five jade ge daggers reached the court as tribute from one polity alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who did the cutting is unsettled, the older view making the Shang jade workers slaves, while the workshop’s place inside the palace precinct suggests elite hands at the finishing and artisans elsewhere in the ancient world often held rank of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Very little of it reached ordinary graves, since in the Anyang cemeteries away from the royal ground the proportion of jade in a burial is extremely low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Workshops of this kind are commoner than the literature suggests, being dug and published far less often than elite burials, and by the late Shang jade had in any case lost its place as the foremost prestige material to bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","art","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 76, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Shang_Jade_Ge_Dagger-Axe%2C_Yinxu.jpg/1280px-Shang_Jade_Ge_Dagger-Axe%2C_Yinxu.jpg","title":"A jade ge from the Yin Ruins","desc":"A dagger-axe blade of pale jade from Anyang, one of the commonest Shang jade forms. The drilled hole is for lashing the blade to a shaft, and an excavation number has been inked on beside it.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A long flat blade of creamy, faintly veined jade lying on a red mount, tapering to a rounded point at one end and squared off at the other, with a small drilled hole near the squared end."}},
{"id":"cnh-086","num":86,"category":"Shang","question":"Dogs, nets, fire and beaters made the Shang hunt a large-scale event closely paralleling war, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what the elite rode in it.","questions":["Large tombs at Anyang are frequently accompanied by pits holding the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where more than four in five graves at the site are small pits with a coffin and a few pots.","That the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is new at Anyang rests on present evidence, and Zhengzhou can hardly be dug at all with nearly ten million people living on top of it."],"answer":"Shang chariot","answerText":"Shang chariot","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Anyang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One Zhou pit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 chariots, 56+ horses</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At the conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 war chariots</span></div>","traditional":"商代戰車","hanzi":"商代战车","pinyin":"Shāngdài zhànchē","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang chariot</b> is the light horse-drawn vehicle that appears at Anyang and at no earlier Chinese centre on present evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it came from is argued, a western origin having long been proposed and defended by Chinese scholars as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of three things the last capital can show that Erlitou and Zhengzhou cannot, the others being the royal hunt and writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> But that rests on present evidence, and the earlier centres are barely dug, since systematic excavation at Zhengzhou is out of the question with nearly ten million people living there now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the elite are found riding is the hunt, a large-scale event closely paralleling warfare and run with dogs, nets, fire and beaters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The bones from the palace and temple area bear that out, being full of wild and exotic animals matching the game the oracle bones record the king killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The vehicle went into the ground with its owner, and the large tombs at Anyang are frequently accompanied by chariot pits with ramps and rich furnishings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing of the kind attends the ordinary dead, more than four in five burials at the site being small pits with a coffin, a few pots and perhaps a cowry shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The custom outlasted the dynasty and grew: two pits beside one Western Zhou marquis held over 56 horses and 19 chariots, and another in the north-west at least 29 horses and three wooden vehicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The animals chosen there were young adults of four to twelve years, of which 21 of the 22 that could be sexed were male, and by the conquest the chariot counted in the field too, the Zhou king being remembered as leading three hundred of them against the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","Zexian Huang, Qiang Ma, Chengrui Zhang, Ruoxin Cheng, Furen Hou, Yi Wu, Feng Luo, and Yue Li, “Early Evidence of Post-Mortem Fetal Extrusion in Equids: A Case from the Western Zhou Period (1045–771 BCE) Site of Yaoheyuan in Northwestern China,” <i>Animals</i> 14, no. 14 (2024): 2106, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11273478/. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Shang_Chariot_Sacrificial_Pit_05.jpg/1280px-Shang_Chariot_Sacrificial_Pit_05.jpg","title":"A chariot pit at the Yin Ruins","desc":"One of the chariot burials at Anyang, the two spoked wheels standing where they settled in the earth with the axle, hub and draught pole between them. The vehicle is the excavated one; the skeletons laid in the pit are replacements.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"An excavated rectangular pit with earth walls, a large spoked wheel standing at either side with its spokes preserved as ridges in the soil, and the pole and axle of a chariot lying between them among scattered bones."}},
{"id":"cnh-087","num":87,"category":"Shang","question":"The oracle bones call an enemy polity fang and drop the word when it turns ally, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is fought against a label that shifts rather than a fixed set of peoples.","questions":["Most campaigns of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put 3,000 to 5,000 men in the field, but one against the Qiang, whose country lay close by, deployed 13,000.","Noblemen sent out to levy troops from friendly cities, and waystations built along the routes they travelled, are how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was supplied with men."],"answer":"Shang warfare","answerText":"Shang warfare","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaigns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vs the Qiang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,000 men in one campaign</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Other fang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3,000 – 5,000 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One royal tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">941 weapons</span></div>","traditional":"商代戰爭","hanzi":"商代战争","pinyin":"Shāngdài zhànzhēng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang warfare</b> is the campaigning of the Shang king and his allies against the polities the inscriptions call fang, the word for a people beyond Shang control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a relational label rather than an ethnic one, since a group counted as fang while it was an enemy and lost the word once it became an ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What those polities were like is not recoverable from the inscriptions, which say nothing of their social or political organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wars were fought for hegemony and for control of natural resources, and the ability to beat Qiang armies and offer Qiang captives was itself a demonstration of the king’s power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Force was raised rather than kept, noblemen being sent out to inspect and levy troops from friendly yi cities across the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A king and his officials were constantly on the road, to hunt, to raise troops, to drill them, to inspect the crops, to collect tribute, to attack a neighbour, to trade or to settle new ground, camping at waystations built along the routes they used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The scale varied with the enemy: against most fang the Shang put 3,000 to 5,000 men in the field, but against the Qiang, whose country lay close by, one campaign deployed 13,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On one reading of the court’s office names the military branch was divided into minute duties, which is as near as the record comes to an order of battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the fighting was equipped with shows in one royal tomb alone, which held 941 weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It ended with the Zhou, who came against the last Shang king with three hundred war chariots and three thousand picked warriors besides their allies’ troops, and whose alliance included the Qiang on whom the Shang had spent so much force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","warfare","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Gideon Shelach, “The Qiang and the Question of Human Sacrifice in the Late Shang Period,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 35, no. 1 (1996), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17070. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Shang_Bronze_Ge_Dagger-Axes%2C_Yinxu.jpg/1280px-Shang_Bronze_Ge_Dagger-Axes%2C_Yinxu.jpg","title":"Bronze dagger-axes from the Yin Ruins","desc":"Ge dagger-axe blades from Anyang, mounted for display at the on-site museum of the Yin Ruins, with three more weapons laid on the shelf beneath them. Several keep the squared tang and the slots by which the blade was lashed to its shaft.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A lit museum case with six green-patinated bronze blades mounted on a pale panel, each a long leaf shape with a squared tang at one end, and three more weapons lying on the shelf below."}},
{"id":"cnh-088","num":88,"category":"Shang","question":"Which excavated site answers to which name in the transmitted record is the standing problem of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with Zhengzhou argued to be both the first seat and the second.","questions":["One account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has the Three Dynasties chasing new sources of metal, another has population expansion and fortified outposts planted in the periphery.","The layers at Yinxu for Pan Geng and the two kings after him are meagre and low in status, which is why one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is doubted as his."],"answer":"Shang capitals","answerText":"Shang capitals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Seats</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Yanshi and Zhengzhou, 2 walled cities</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last seat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Yinxu, c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商代都城","hanzi":"商代都城","pinyin":"Shāngdài dūchéng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang capitals</b> are the successive seats of the Shang court, semipermanent cities that served as centres of political, military and religious power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court moved more than once and the sites are known: Yanshi and Zhengzhou in the early period, a walled town at Huanbei in the middle, and Yinxu at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is not known is which of them answers to which name in the transmitted record, since the later texts give the seats names the excavators cannot attach with confidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhengzhou has been argued to be both the first Shang capital and the second, called Bo and Ao in later literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor is it clear that Yinxu is the Yin to which Pan Geng is said to have moved, since the layers there for his reign and the two after it are meagre, small in scale and low in status, no parallel to a royal capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Why the court moved at all is argued, and one popular answer is that the Three Dynasties were chasing new sources of metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The copper was certainly far off, in the Zhongtiao Mountains and along the middle and lower reaches of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That reading has been set aside for another, in which the strategy was population expansion and the planting of fortified outposts in the periphery, practised from Erlitou to the end of Erligang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third account makes moving the capital an instrument of policy in itself, first plainly visible under Pan Geng, with the earlier moves the experiments that taught it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What all of it rests on is the divination records, so the Shang political map has to be reconstructed from oracle-bone inscriptions rather than read off the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Shang_Dynastic_Capitals_as_Recorded_in_Historical_Documents_%2810197655116%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Dynastic_Capitals_as_Recorded_in_Historical_Documents_%2810197655116%29.jpg","title":"The capital moves, as three texts give them","desc":"A panel at the Yin Ruins museum setting three early Chinese records side by side against six Shang kings. The three do not agree: where one puts Zu Yi's seat at pi, the others have geng and xing, and Pan Geng's is yin in two of them and bo in the third.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A museum wall panel headed “The Relocation of Shang Dynastic Capitals as Recorded in Historical Documents”, carrying a table of three rows, one for each text, and six columns, one for each king, filled with short romanised place names."}},
{"id":"cnh-089","num":89,"category":"Shang","question":"Two rings of rammed earth enclose the 24 square kilometres of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose inner city held a few dozen foundations taken for palaces and temples.","questions":["The only hard evidence for casting ritual vessels by the piece-mould method in its period comes from a foundry in the outer city of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the periphery cast only tools.","Monumental four-legged ding were found in three caches at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, laid side by side in adapted wells and storage pits with their mouths up and their feet down."],"answer":"Zhengzhou Shang City","answerText":"Zhengzhou Shang City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walled area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 24 square kilometres</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foundations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 – 2,000 square metres</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caches</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, of monumental ding</span></div>","traditional":"鄭州商城","hanzi":"郑州商城","pinyin":"Zhèngzhōu Shāngchéng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zhengzhou Shang City</b> is the early Shang capital in central Henan, identified with one of the two seats the later texts call Bo and Ao, and one of the largest places built anywhere in Bronze Age China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It covered about 24 square kilometres (9 square miles), ringed by two circuits of rammed earth that made an inner city and an outer one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The royal elite lived inside the inner ring, where a few dozen rammed-earth foundations taken for palaces and temples run from 100 to 2,000 square metres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two large bronze foundries lie in the outer city, one of them at Nanguanwai about 700 metres south of the inner wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across some 0.8 hectares it has given up crucibles, slag, clay moulds, casting waste, copper ore and the remains of smelting furnaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its moulds were cut for tools, weapons and ritual vessels alike, and the shapes and ornament of early Shang bronzes found across the region match them, which makes this the place the vessels were made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only site anywhere with hard evidence for casting ritual vessels by the piece-mould method in its period, while the metallurgy sites out in the periphery cast only weapons and tools from stone moulds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Casting was held at the centre, then, but mining was not, since there is no evidence of direct control over the copper regions and the strategy looks instead like securing the networks along which metal moved, with outposts planted near the ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three caches at the site held monumental four-legged ding, buried in adapted wells, storage pits and refuse pits, the vessels set side by side with their mouths up and their feet down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They come in pairs or graduated sets rather than singly, every one larger than anything else found there, and how much more lies below is not knowable, since nearly ten million people live on top of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/20240817_Zhengzhou_Shang_City_Site_02.jpg/1280px-20240817_Zhengzhou_Shang_City_Site_02.jpg","title":"The inner wall, still standing in the middle of a city","desc":"A surviving stretch of the rammed-earth circuit that enclosed the inner city, its horizontal building courses still visible in the eroded face. Tower blocks stand a few hundred metres behind it: the modern city sits directly on top of the Shang one, which is why so much of the site can only be dug in the gaps between buildings.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A long earthen bank perhaps ten metres high, grassed along its top and bare tan soil on its steep face, running behind a wide paved plaza; a black stone marker stands in the foreground and white apartment towers rise beyond the bank."}},
{"id":"cnh-090","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"A wall of only 7.5 hectares at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Hubei encloses the clearest case of the early Shang reaching far beyond the Central Plain.","questions":["Bronze vessels resembling those of Zhengzhou were buried in the Lijiazui tombs at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Hubei outpost that cast weapons and tools but no ritual vessels of its own.","The Tonglüshan copper mines lie close to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose bronzes carry the same highly radiogenic lead found at Zhengzhou, Xin’gan and Sanxingdui."],"answer":"Panlongcheng","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Erligang period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walled area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7.5 hectares (19 acres)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Whole site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">64 – 110 hectares, disputed</span></div>","traditional":"盤龍城","hanzi":"盘龙城","pinyin":"Pánlóngchéng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Pánlóngchéng</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Panlongcheng</b> is a walled Bronze Age settlement in Hubei, in the middle Yangtze valley, and the clearest case of the early Shang reaching far beyond the Central Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands close to abundant copper deposits, and has produced evidence of bronze making from the Erlitou period, before that expansion reached it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wall encloses only 7.5 hectares (19 acres) and the settlement around it is many times that, though by how much is argued: the excavation report gives a near-square of 1,000 by 1,100 metres and so 110 hectares, but that takes in ravine and lake, and 64 hectares is the better figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Erlitou material had already spread here, and in Hubei it sat alongside indigenous cultural components rather than coming to dominate the assemblage as it did in southern Shanxi and southern Shaanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mine remains of Erlitou and Erligang date are known from several sites in the Yangxin region nearby, and the Tonglüshan mines at Daye lie close by as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Elite burials of Erligang date at Lijiazui have yielded bronze vessels that resemble those found at Zhengzhou, which suggests the vessels were made at the primary centre and sent out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Panlongcheng itself shows no sign of casting ritual vessels: like the other metallurgy sites of the periphery it worked bronze, but its products were weapons and tools cast from stone moulds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two Lijiazui tombs are ranked with the aristocrats and lesser military leaders, and with retainers and related elite, rather than at the royal grades known from Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mining and metallurgy look instead to have been organised locally, and outposts like this one are read as a strategy of resource absorption: securing the networks along which metal moved rather than controlling the digging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its bronzes carry highly radiogenic lead, a rare signature that turns up at Zhengzhou, Xin’gan and Sanxingdui at virtually the same time, and after thirty years of argument its source may be several sources rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Panlongcheng","sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson, and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond Ritual Bronzes: Identifying Multiple Sources of Highly Radiogenic Lead across Chinese History,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/20231126_Palace_in_Panlongcheng_%28114140%29.jpg/1280px-20231126_Palace_in_Panlongcheng_%28114140%29.jpg","title":"Inside the wall, a palace foundation","desc":"One of the excavated foundations within the walled area, laid out for visitors in the archaeological park. The low banks follow the lines of the wall trenches and the timber stumps stand where the posts did; the earth platform beneath them is the ancient part.","credit":"Photograph by Yumeto, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A rectangular earth platform in mown parkland, its rooms marked out by low walls of packed earth about knee height, with rows of short timber posts set at intervals; a rope fence and a boardwalk run across the foreground."}},
{"id":"cnh-091","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Sixty grains of wheat or more turn up at most Shang sites, but the millet of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went on feeding almost everyone until the Zhou.","questions":["Isotopes from Anyang put dogs, pigs, rats and sacrificial victims on the same mostly millet diet that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced, poor in meat beside the people who were buried rather than offered.","Cooler, drier weather late in the dynasty drove the deforestation and multicropping of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, marked by a sharp rise in the number of stone knives and sickles."],"answer":"Shang agriculture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Practised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 millets, dry-farmed on loess</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wheat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60+ grains at most Shang sites</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enamel defects</span><span class=\"dt-v\">80.9% of Yinxu commoners</span></div>","traditional":"商代農業","hanzi":"商代农业","pinyin":"Shāngdài nóngyè","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang agriculture</b> was the dry farming of millet on the loess plains of northern China, the base on which everything else about the dynasty rested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two millets had been the region’s crops since the Neolithic, and the divide between the millet-eating north and the rice-and-broad-spectrum south shows in the carbon isotopes of human bone from 6000 BCE and grew sharper after 5000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wheat and barley reached China from south-west Asia after 2000 BCE, and Shang sites are the first in central China to yield them in quantity, sixty grains or more from most of those examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They still fed almost nobody: wheat’s part in subsistence stayed minimal until the Zhou, after about 1000 BCE, when it began to climb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One explanation for that slowness is the kitchen, since wheat and barley fold into a tradition of grinding and baking more readily than into the steaming and boiling of the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Millet was drunk as well as eaten, and the ale brewed from it went to the ancestors by the hundred jars in a single royal offering, so the crop fed the dead as well as the living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isotopes from Anyang put dogs, pigs, rats and human sacrificial victims on much the same mostly millet diet, poor in meat beside the people who were buried rather than offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cattle had arrived from the west in the 3rd millennium BCE and by the late Shang were being supplied to the capital in enormous numbers, foddered in part on millet by-products, having displaced pigs as the commonest sacrificial animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By then the climate had turned cooler and drier, and the answer to it was deforestation and multicropping, marked archaeologically by a sharp rise in the number of stone knives and sickles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people who did that work paid for it: among the commoners of Yinxu 80.9 per cent carry the enamel defects of childhood stress, 30.3 per cent cribra orbitalia, and 40.0 per cent of adults the bone marks of infection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang agriculture","sources":["Hua Zhang et al., “Osteoarchaeological Studies of Human Systemic Stress of Early Urbanization in Late Shang at Anyang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0151854, “Archaeological Context” and “Results,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0151854. [Open access]","Xinyi Liu and Rachel E. B. Reid, “The Prehistoric Roots of Chinese Cuisines: Mapping Staple Food Systems of China, 6000 BC–220 AD,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0240930, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0240930. [Open access]","Zhenhua Deng et al., “Assessing the Occurrence and Status of Wheat in Late Neolithic Central China: The Importance of Direct AMS Radiocarbon Dates from Xiazhai,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 29, no. 1 (2020): 61–73, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00334-019-00732-7. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Lu Peng, Katherine Brunson, Yuan Jing, and Li Zhipeng, “Zooarchaeological and Genetic Evidence for the Origins of Domestic Cattle in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 56, no. 1 (2017): 92–120, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/64744. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","agriculture","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/20240817_Stone_Sickles%2C_Zhengzhou_Shang_City_Site%2C_Shang.jpg/1280px-20240817_Stone_Sickles%2C_Zhengzhou_Shang_City_Site%2C_Shang.jpg","title":"Sickle blades from Zhengzhou Shang City","desc":"Eight stone sickle blades, the commonest farming tool the period leaves behind and the one whose numbers rise late in the dynasty. The blade at the right is set on a clear modern rod to show how it was hafted; the handle is the museum's, not the Shang's.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Eight curved blades of dark grey and grey-green stone, each roughly the length of a hand and worked to a long cutting edge, mounted in two columns on a pale museum wall; a ninth is fixed at an angle to a clear rod that stands in for a wooden handle."}},
{"id":"cnh-092","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Guandimiao’s 23 kilns and 22 houses are almost all that is known of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the villages and lesser centres that lay outside the Shang capitals.","questions":["A village of fewer than a hundred people made pottery for a whole region and oracle bones for itself, which is most of what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has left to study.","The hairpins and arrowheads of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> match the mass-produced work of a bone workshop 200 kilometres away, though its pottery went nowhere near the capital."],"answer":"Shang countryside","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Guandimiao</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Houses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22, semi-subterranean</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kilns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23, updraft</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">269, under 100 people</span></div>","traditional":"商代農村","hanzi":"商代农村","pinyin":"Shāngdài nóngcūn","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Shang countryside</b> is everything outside the capitals — the villages and lesser centres that fed and supplied the court — and next to none of it has been dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Anyang itself has been excavated on a great scale, with over 10,000 burials besides the royal cemetery, and residential quarters, refuse and storage pits, drains, roads and workshops with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the early Shang the pattern outside the capital was of medium-sized regional centres placed at the main water-transport nodes near metal sources, whose remains resemble Zhengzhou’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For the late Shang the picture rests on one site: Guandimiao in Xingyang, Henan, found in 2006 during salvage work ahead of the south-to-north water diversion, and the only village of its time in China to be nearly completely excavated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was small, with 22 semi-subterranean houses and 269 tombs over perhaps 150 years, and a population that probably never passed a hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It also held 23 updraft kilns, near enough one to a household, and made pottery on a scale far beyond anything a hundred people could use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those pots did not go to Anyang 200 kilometres (125 miles) away, being unlike Anyang’s own, so the likely market was the country around, which makes the rural economy far more specialised than anyone had supposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Anyang the grey ware everybody used was made locally, and thin-section work picks out only a handful of vessels as probably foreign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What did move was bone: the village’s finest hairpins and arrowheads are identical in every respect to the mass-produced work of the Tiesanlu workshop at Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Guandimiao made oracle bones of its own as well, chiselled by a local specialist, and the polity such places composed reads less as a territory than as a network of lineages with the king at the head of the senior one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang countryside","sources":["Hou Yanfeng et al., “The Guandimiao Bone Assemblage (and What it Says about the Shang Economy),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 281–310, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72094. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","James B. Stoltman, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, and George (Rip) Rapp, “Ceramic Production in Shang Societies of Anyang,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 48, no. 1 (2009): 182–203, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29082. [Open access]","Hua Zhang et al., “Osteoarchaeological Studies of Human Systemic Stress of Early Urbanization in Late Shang at Anyang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0151854, “Archaeological Context,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0151854. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Guandimiao_Site_in_1973_by_HEXAGON_satellite.jpg/1280px-Guandimiao_Site_in_1973_by_HEXAGON_satellite.jpg","title":"The country around Guandimiao, from orbit in 1973","desc":"Field strips, gullies and clustered village houses in Xingyang, photographed by an American reconnaissance satellite in November 1973 and declassified since. Nothing Shang is visible: the village was found in 2006 only because a canal was being cut through it, which is the whole difficulty of the subject.","credit":"KH-9 HEXAGON satellite photograph by the CIA, NRO and USGS; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A grey monochrome aerial photograph of flat farmland divided into hundreds of narrow field strips, cut by branching pale gullies, with three or four dense clusters of small village buildings and a straight railway line across the top."}},
{"id":"cnh-093","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Ten heavenly stems paired in turn with twelve earthly branches give the sixty day-names of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had no way of numbering a year at all.","questions":["Five lunar eclipses recorded by one group of diviners are what fixes part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to between 1201 and 1181 BCE, there being no era count to read.","A ten-day week called the xun is the unit the Shang court planned in, and it is ten days long because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had ten heavenly stems."],"answer":"Shang calendar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Day cycle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60 days, 10 stems, 12 branches</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Week</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 days, the xun</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lunar eclipses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, dated 1201 – 1181 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商代曆法","hanzi":"商代历法","pinyin":"Shāngdài lìfǎ","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Shang calendar</b> is the reckoning of time behind the oracle-bone inscriptions, and it is a calendar of days above all, since those inscriptions carry day notations and nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A day was named by pairing ten signs called the heavenly stems with twelve called the earthly branches, each taken in turn, so that the pairs run out at sixty and start again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sixty are called jiazi after the first of them, and there is no year anywhere in the count: it was not applied to numbering years until the Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That the shells use the signs for days rather than years was worked out early, from the plain fact that the combinations on them exceed the sixty a year cycle allows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ten stems make a ten-day week, the xun, and that is the unit the court planned in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The round of five sacrificial rites the late kings ran through those weeks took about a Julian year to complete, which is the nearest thing the Shang had to a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A calendar built this way puts its meaning in the cycle rather than in the sequence, so what a day is fit or unfit for comes round again every ten, twelve or at most sixty days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because there is no era count, a Shang date cannot simply be read off the bone and has to be recovered from astronomy instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eleven possible solar and lunar eclipses survive in the corpus, and five lunar ones from a single group of diviners are generally taken to be real observations, which calculation places between 1201 and 1181 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The day-count outlived everything around it: Chinese records of the 17th and 18th centuries CE are still dated by stems and branches, and a modern database of them has to convert every date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang calendar","sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Frank H. Chalfant, “Early Chinese Writing,” <i>Memoirs of the Carnegie Museum</i> 4, no. 1 (1906): 21, 35, https://archive.org/details/earlychinesewrit01chal. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual: A Handbook of Biographical, Historical, Mythological, and General Literary Reference</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), part 2, s.v. “The Ten Stems (Cyclical Signs),” https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Mu-chou Poo, “How to Steer through Life: Negotiating Fate in the Daybook,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 107–28, at 113–14, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","Pao K. Wang et al., “Construction of the REACHES Climate Database Based on Historical Documents of China,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 5 (2018): 180288, https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2018.288. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","science","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Heji_37986_Ganzhi_table.jpg/1280px-Heji_37986_Ganzhi_table.jpg","title":"The sixty days, cut into an ox shoulder blade","desc":"A complete table of the cycle on a scapula catalogued as Heji 37986, in the hand of the Huang diviner group and so from the reigns of the last two Shang kings. Each entry is a stem above a branch, and the columns run down through all sixty before the count starts again.","credit":"Photograph in the Yinxu Museum, rotated and cropped by Kanguole; released CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A pale curved slab of ox shoulder blade lit against a grey wall, its face covered with columns of small incised two-character groups in an early angular script, each character made of straight scored strokes."}},
{"id":"cnh-094","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Chinese is the world’s only primary script still in continuous use, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the ancestor of every character written today.","questions":["Bronze inscriptions written into the soft clay of a casting mould run beside the incised oracle bones, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives in cast metal as well as in shell and bone.","Half a million images spanning 3,000 years show that modern characters are more complex than their ancestors in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which upends the popular story of steady simplification."],"answer":"Shang writing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE, on bone</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, oracle bone to regular</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Modern shapes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">clerical script, c. 200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Korea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mokkan, 538 – 660 CE</span></div>","traditional":"商代文字","hanzi":"商代文字","pinyin":"Shāngdài wénzì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang writing</b> is the earliest Chinese writing there is, and what makes it matter beyond its own dynasty is that the characters written today descend from it, Chinese being the world’s only primary script still in continuous use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two Shang scripts run together: the oracle-bone script incised into shell and bone, and the bronze inscriptions, which were often written into the soft clay of the mould a vessel was cast in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bronze inscriptions begin in the Shang and are coeval with the bones, then become characteristic of the Western Zhou, so the line out of the Shang runs through cast metal as much as through divination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That the bone forms belong to that line was shown almost as soon as the bones surfaced, by setting their date-marks beside the cyclical characters already known from later writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Reading the ones still undeciphered works the same way, by comparison across the stages the script passed through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Those stages are conventionally counted as five — oracle bone, bronze, seal, clerical and regular — and everything before the small seal script is grouped together as ancient characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shapes a reader would recognise today appear only with the clerical script of the early Han, a thousand years after the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The script left China as well: wooden tablets from the Korean kingdom of Paekche, of the 6th and 7th centuries CE, carry both practice at individual characters and attempts at composition in Sinitic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The popular account of what happened over those three millennia is one of steady simplification, and it does not survive being measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Half a million images spanning more than 3,000 years show complexity climbing to the seal script and falling after it, with modern characters still more complex than their oracle-bone ancestors, and 400 people who could not read any of them judged the pairs the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang writing","sources":["Simon J. Han, Piers Kelly, James Winters, and Charles Kemp, “Simplification Is Not Dominant in the Evolution of Chinese Characters,” <i>Open Mind</i> 6 (2022): 264–79, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9987343/. [Open access]","Mengru Wang et al., “Study on the Evolution of Chinese Characters Based on Few-Shot Learning: From Oracle Bone Inscriptions to Regular Script,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272974, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272974. [Open access]","Marjorie Burge, “Wooden Inscriptions and the Culture of Writing in Sabi Paekche,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 47–73, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76771. [Open access]","Ming Yan, Jinger Pan, and Reinhold Kliegl, “The Beijing Sentence Corpus II: A Cross-Script Comparison between Traditional and Simplified Chinese Sentence Reading,” <i>Behavior Research Methods</i> 57 (2025): 60, https://doi.org/10.3758/s13428-024-02523-z. [Open access]","Frank H. Chalfant, “Early Chinese Writing,” <i>Memoirs of the Carnegie Museum</i> 4, no. 1 (1906): 35 and plate L, https://archive.org/details/earlychinesewrit01chal. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","writing","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/%E9%A6%AC-Chinese_characters_evolution.png/1280px-%E9%A6%AC-Chinese_characters_evolution.png","title":"One character, from the Shang to now","desc":"A family tree of the character for horse, beginning with Shang bronze and oracle-bone forms in which the mane and legs are still drawn. Six Warring States branches are crossed out as dead ends, so the descent is a surviving line rather than the only one; the diagram marks the split between ancient and modern script at the Han.","credit":"Diagram by the Wikimedia contributor Panates, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A branching chart running top to bottom. At the top are angular pictorial forms of a horse with a mane and four legs; lines lead down through progressively squarer forms, six branches ending in red crosses, to the modern printed characters at the foot."}},
{"id":"cnh-095","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Most of what is known of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from a rare radiogenic lead that reached Sanxingdui, Panlongcheng and Xin’gan at the same moment as the Central Plain.","questions":["Carnelian carried more than a thousand kilometres south to Sanxingdui puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one exchange sphere reaching from Sichuan to the Mongolian Plateau.","That Anyang’s copper came from the Yangzi is unreferenced and its jade from Xinjiang unsupported, so claims about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outrun the sourcing studies."],"answer":"Shang neighbours","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Exchange sphere</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1500 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiogenic lead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 50% of the lead used</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carnelian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1,000 km to the north</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ore fields</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, Tongling and Tonglüshan</span></div>","traditional":"商代方國","hanzi":"商代方国","pinyin":"Shāngdài fāngguó","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Shang neighbours</b> are the peoples and polities around the Shang state, and what can be said about them comes mostly from things that moved rather than from anything the inscriptions say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clearest of those things is a rare, highly radiogenic lead that supplied over half the lead used across an area running from the Yellow River to the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It appears at almost the same moment in the bronzes of the Central Plain and in those of Sanxingdui in Sichuan, Panlongcheng in Hubei and Xin’gan in Jiangxi, and then vanishes under the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where it came from has been argued over for thirty years — south-west China, the middle Yangtze, the Qinling and Zhongtiao mountains, even Africa — and the likeliest answer now is that there was no single source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mining itself spread across the south at the end of the Erlitou period, with the ore fields at Tongling in Jiangxi and Tonglüshan in Hubei both worked by the early Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Carnelian beads buried at Sanxingdui between about 1200 and 1000 BCE were cut from stone that came from more than 1,000 kilometres (600 miles) to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beads of the same period from Gansu, Shaanxi and Beijing carry the same northern signature, which puts the Sichuan Basin, the Central Plain, the Loess Plateau and the southern Mongolian Plateau in one exchange sphere between 1500 and 1000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that, the long-distance claims made most often about the Shang are the ones with least behind them: that Anyang’s copper ore came from the Yangtze is unsubstantiated and unreferenced, and no sourcing study supports the jade of Anyang and Xin’gan coming from Xinjiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The one such claim with provenance work behind it is that the north’s proto-porcelain was southern, and even there the work is contested and the studies disagree about which part of the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the record bears is that the Shang sat inside a very wide network whose direction of travel is mostly unproven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang neighbours","sources":["Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson, and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond Ritual Bronzes: Identifying Multiple Sources of Highly Radiogenic Lead across Chinese History,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Meiting Yan et al., “Sourcing the Origins of Carnelian in Early Chinese Civilizations,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 7 (2026): e2524563123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12913004/. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Haichao Li, “Hierarchical, Ethnic, and Provenance Features of Western Zhou Period Proto-Porcelain in Northern China: New Evidence of a Redistribution System during the Western Zhou Dynasty,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 417–43, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108233. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Tongl%C3%BCshan_Shang%26Zhou_No.2_Mining_Site.jpg/1280px-Tongl%C3%BCshan_Shang%26Zhou_No.2_Mining_Site.jpg","title":"Where some of the copper came out","desc":"The No. 2 mining site of the Shang and Zhou periods at Tonglüshan in Hubei, an irregular oval mound about 40 metres across with a low adit at its foot and the ore body breaking through the grass at the right. The metal moved a long way; the workings did not, and there is nothing at the surface to say who the miners answered to.","credit":"Photograph by Huanokinhejo, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A low grassed hillock under a pale sky, thick with summer weeds and small trees, with a dark rectangular opening at its base beside a small blue sign, and weathered reddish rock outcropping on its right flank."}},
{"id":"cnh-096","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Fu Hao’s tomb alone held 1.6 tonnes of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and she was one royal consort among several rather than the greatest person buried at Anyang.","questions":["Very pure copper with tin in controlled amounts went into the high elite’s vessels, while everyone below them got remelted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carrying the impurities of mixed metal.","Lead was added to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 2 to 5 per cent to lower its melting point, but never as carefully measured out as the tin was."],"answer":"Shang bronze","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiogenic lead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alloy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">copper, tin and 2 – 5% lead</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fu Hao’s tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.6 tonnes of metal</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Metal drawn from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 500 km away</span></div>","traditional":"商代青銅器","hanzi":"商代青铜器","pinyin":"Shāngdài qīngtóngqì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang bronze</b> is an alloy of copper with tin and lead, and the difficulty of it lies as much in getting the three metals as in casting them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The appetite was enormous: Anyang became one of the largest consumers of metal anywhere in Eurasia in the 2nd millennium BCE, and one tomb alone, Fu Hao’s, held 1.6 tonnes of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was one of a king’s consorts and by no means the most important person buried there, so her grave is the tip of the supply rather than a measure of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Feeding that took a system able to draw metal from more than 500 kilometres (300 miles) away and to turn out a wide range of objects for people of very different rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of the copper came from the southern ore fields, Tongling in Jiangxi and Tonglüshan in Hubei both being worked by the early Shang, and the mining there appears to have been organised locally rather than run from the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What the chemistry shows is that the metal was not shared out evenly, and the difference lies in how it was alloyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ritual vessels of the high elite were made by primary alloying, from very pure copper with tin added in controlled amounts, and that practice is found nowhere else in Bronze Age central China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything lower down the order was secondary metal, mixed and remelted, carrying the impurities that come with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lead was put in deliberately at about 2 to 5 per cent, which lowers the melting point and makes the alloy run better into a mould, though never as carefully measured as the tin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That lead is the part still unexplained: a strangely radiogenic kind appears on the Yellow River plain quite suddenly around 1400 BCE, the obvious candidate deposit in central China has been ruled out as its only supplier, and the least awkward reading now is that several sources fed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang bronze","sources":["Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Wei-dong Sun et al., “Origin of the Mysterious Yin-Shang Bronzes in China Indicated by Lead Isotopes,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6 (2016): 23304, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep23304. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson, and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond Ritual Bronzes: Identifying Multiple Sources of Highly Radiogenic Lead across Chinese History,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Fuhao_Owl_Zun_front.jpg/1280px-Fuhao_Owl_Zun_front.jpg","title":"The metal the high elite got","desc":"One of a pair of owl-shaped zun from Fu Hao's tomb, inscribed with her name. A vessel of this class is what the chemistry calls primary alloying: very pure copper with tin added in measured quantity, a mixture nobody below the highest rank received. A fine vertical line runs down the front, of the kind a piece-mould join leaves.","credit":"Photograph by Cangminzho, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A dark green-grey bronze vessel in the shape of a standing owl, about knee height, with a crested cap, swept-back plumes above the head, wings folded in raised spirals against its sides, and a body covered in dense low-relief pattern."}},
{"id":"cnh-097","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"The family that produces a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lives across the tropical Indo-Pacific, so every one found on the north China plain had been carried a very long way.","questions":["A study costing Anyang grave goods by the labour behind them rates a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at two and a half times a freshwater one, which is a judgement about distance.","The <i>Book of Documents</i> has the Shang king Pan Geng rebuking his ministers for hoarding gems and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the oldest picture of them as treasure."],"answer":"cowrie shell","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Marine shell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">rated 250, freshwater 100</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemeteries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 studied closely at Anyang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chu supply noted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">852 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"海貝","hanzi":"海贝","pinyin":"hǎibèi","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>cowrie shell</b> is the shell of a marine snail of the family Cypraeidae, smooth and oval, with a mantle the animal can draw over the outside of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The family belongs to the tropical Indo-Pacific, ranging from the east coast of Africa to Polynesia, so a cowrie on the north China plain had already been carried a very long way before anyone buried it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Shell of one kind or another is among the commonest classes of object in Shang graves, ranking with bronze and pottery at the two Anyang cemeteries that have been studied closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At one of the two, though, nearly all of it lies in two exceptionally rich burials, so how common shell looks depends on which ground is counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where grave goods are costed by the labour they stand for, a marine shell is rated at two and a half times a freshwater one, which is a judgement about distance rather than about beauty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The traffic ran both ways: the late Shang court drew turtle shell from the south, where it was plentiful, along with metal, in a long-distance exchange of prestige goods with the polities of the periphery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Far inland to the north-west, seashells and other non-local materials in the graves of bronze-using cultures are read as evidence of trade with the Central Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The received text of the <i>Book of Documents</i> has the Shang king Pan Geng rebuking his ministers for hoarding cowries and gems, which is the oldest picture of them as treasure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The entry that has the southern state of Chu supplying tortoise shells and cowries for currency, and notes that both had grown scarce, belongs to the 9th century BCE, nearly two centuries after the dynasty ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bronze imitations came later still, small cast pieces nicknamed metallic cowries and made when the shells would no longer stretch to what was wanted, so calling the Shang cowrie money is reading a later system backwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"cowrie shell","sources":["Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 29–30 and 54, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “The Qiang and the Question of Human Sacrifice in the Late Shang Period,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 35, no. 1 (1996), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17070. [Open access]","Terrien de Lacouperie, <i>Catalogue of Chinese Coins from the VIIth Cent. B.C. to A.D. 621</i> (London: British Museum, 1892), introduction, vii–xii, https://archive.org/details/catalogueofchine00terr. [Open access]","Teresa Stephanie Tay, Hsien Rong Samuel Lee, and Mei Lin Neo, “Spawning and Larval Development of Two Tropical Cowries (Gastropoda: Cypraeidae), <i>Cypraea tigris</i> and <i>Mauritia arabica</i>, under Laboratory Conditions,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 10, no. 4 (2023): 221332, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10090884/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["object","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Shang_Shell_Money%2C_Yinxu.jpg/1280px-Shang_Shell_Money%2C_Yinxu.jpg","title":"Shell from the Yin Ruins","desc":"Two groups of cowries, each shell opened at the back as one must be to go on a string, shown beside the other shell the site yields: two bivalves, a long slender shell and a cluster of small spirals. It is a museum case, so the grouping is the curator's rather than the Shang's.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"About twenty small pale oval shells laid in two loose clusters on a white museum plinth, most with a ragged hole broken through the rounded upper surface, with three larger shells and a group of tiny spiral shells arranged in a row above them."}},
{"id":"cnh-098","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"The bronze bell called nao is the chief surviving instrument of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and two Anyang burials each held a graded set of three of them.","questions":["Instruments are absent from the earliest bronzes at Dasikong and then run at 5 to 7 per cent of them, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is something a grave might hold rather than usually did.","Bells were counted among the ritual vessels rather than apart from them, which is the clearest thing the inventories say about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and what it was for."],"answer":"Shang music","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nao bells</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in sets of 3</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Share of bronzes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 – 7% at Dasikong</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhou rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 ding for a king</span></div>","traditional":"商代音樂","hanzi":"商代音乐","pinyin":"Shāngdài yīnyuè","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang music</b> is known from the instruments buried with the dead, chief among them the bronze bell called nao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those bells came in graded sets of three: one late Shang tomb at Anyang lists two such sets in its inventory and another lists one, counted among the ritual vessels rather than set apart from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fu Hao’s tomb, the only intact top royal burial dug at Anyang, held bells among its bronzes, beside weapons, blades, mirrors and sculptures of tigers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Instruments are all the same a small part of what a Shang grave holds: at the Dasikong cemetery they are absent from the bronzes of the earliest period and then run between about 5 and 7 per cent of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That share stays low through all four periods, which puts music among the things a burial might contain rather than among the things it usually did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The bronzes at Dasikong multiply sharply in the last period, when the ground seems to have become a burial place for groups reaching materials once kept for the greatest Shang elite, and the share that is instruments does not move with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the first of those Anyang tombs the six bells stand inside a total of 85 vessels, which is about the room music took in a great burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Zhou the numbers a person might own were fixed by rank, nine ding for a king, seven for the second rank and five for the third, and chime bells were specified in the same way as the vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ritual and music together, liyue, became the means by which the Zhou court carried its ethical order, and the essence of that system was the division of classes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The classical Chinese account of instruments, later still, sorts them by the eight materials of nature, metal, stone, silk, bamboo, gourd, clay, skin and wood, a scheme taken from the eight trigrams rather than from how anything is played.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang music","sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 56–58, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","J. A. van Aalst, <i>Chinese Music</i> (Shanghai: Statistical Department of the Inspectorate General of Customs, 1884), 47–49, https://archive.org/details/chinesemusic00vana. [Open access]","Xin Yu et al., “Sacrificial Animals as Evidence for the Disintegration of the Ritual and Music System: A Case Study of the Zhuolu Site (790–410 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 2 (2025): e0318926, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0318926. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","music","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Bronze_Nao_Bells%2C_Late_Shang%2C_13th-11th_C._BC.jpg/1280px-Bronze_Nao_Bells%2C_Late_Shang%2C_13th-11th_C._BC.jpg","title":"Two nao of a set","desc":"Late Shang nao in the Palace Museum, Taipei. A nao stands mouth upward on a hollow shank rather than hanging, and there is no clapper inside it; the ornament is the same vocabulary of bosses and beast faces the vessels carry.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Two bronze bells heavily patinated bright green, each an open-mouthed cup widening upward on a short hollow stem, mounted on clear perspex rods; the nearer one is decorated with raised scrolls and round bosses in panels."}},
{"id":"cnh-099","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Casting tools in the graves at Xiaomintun tie the people buried there to the foundry beside them, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are identified at all.","questions":["Osteoarthritis in 167 Yinxu skeletons is worse in the upper body and worse at one site than the other, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did to the people who worked in them.","Worked bone at one excavated village runs from tools anybody could make to pieces identical to the output of Anyang’s largest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a continuum rather than a divide."],"answer":"Shang craft workshops","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Anyang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Skeletons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">167, from 2 sites</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bone working</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 groups, by skill</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thin sections</span><span class=\"dt-v\">61, of Anyang pottery</span></div>","traditional":"商代手工業作坊","hanzi":"商代手工业作坊","pinyin":"Shāngdài shǒugōngyè zuōfang","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang craft workshops</b> are the production quarters of the late Shang capital, which grew as social stratification intensified and the division of labour increased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The commoners of Yinxu appear to have been sorted into occupational groups doing specialised craft production, living in neighbourhoods segregated by trade that archaeologists identify from the workshops found beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Xiaomintun the ground holds bronze foundries, houses and graves together, and casting tools in many of the burials tie the people there to the foundry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Xin’anzhuang, next to pottery and bone workshops and a foundry of its own, nothing in the graves links the dead to a particular trade, so they are taken for non-specialist commoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A study of 167 skeletons from the two examined osteoarthritis at eight joints and three indicators in the spine, and found high frequencies at both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Men carry significantly more of it in the upper body than women, which is read as repetitive lifting and carrying, and Xiaomintun carries more than Xin’anzhuang in both sexes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The women of Xiaomintun are affected too, raising the possibility that casting was a family business and that this is how an occupational neighbourhood came about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those workshops were fed on a large scale: cattle bone was the main material for the mass-produced hairpins and other objects turned out at the capitals, with meat and bone moving through complex systems of supply and redistribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Skill ran as a continuum rather than a divide, and at one excavated village the worked bone falls into four groups, from expedient tools anybody could make, through a local specialist’s oracle bones, to pieces identical in every respect to the mass-produced work of Anyang’s largest bone workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery ran the other way: thin sections of 61 vessels and raw materials from Anyang found that the grey ware everybody used was made locally, with only a handful probably foreign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang craft workshops","sources":["Hua Zhang, Deborah C. Merrett, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, Yuling He, Hongbin Yue, Zhanwei Yue, and Dongya Y. Yang, “Osteoarthritis, Labour Division, and Occupational Specialization of the Late Shang China — Insights from Yinxu (ca. 1250–1046 B.C.),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0176329, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5413014/. [Open access]","Hou Yanfeng et al., “The Guandimiao Bone Assemblage (and What it Says about the Shang Economy),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 281–310, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72094. [Open access]","Hua Zhang et al., “Osteoarchaeological Studies of Human Systemic Stress of Early Urbanization in Late Shang at Anyang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0151854, “Archaeological Context,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0151854. [Open access]","James B. Stoltman, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, and George (Rip) Rapp, “Ceramic Production in Shang Societies of Anyang,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 48, no. 1 (2009): 182–203, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29082. [Open access]","Lu Peng, Katherine Brunson, Yuan Jing, and Li Zhipeng, “Zooarchaeological and Genetic Evidence for the Origins of Domestic Cattle in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 56, no. 1 (2017): 92–120, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/64744. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Shang_Bone_Hairpins_%2844955835175%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Bone_Hairpins_%2844955835175%29.jpg","title":"What the bone workshops made","desc":"Bone hairpins in the Yin Ruins museum, five of the commonest thing the capital's workshops turned out. They are plain, made to a pattern and mostly from cattle bone, and it is their sheer number rather than any one of them that shows the scale of the industry.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Five slender pins of pale bone laid in a row on a frosted glass shelf beneath a small red museum label reading “bone hairpin”; each tapers to a point, four are near-straight and the fifth is a flattened leaf shape."}},
{"id":"cnh-100","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Jade lay nearest the body, ritual vessels between the two coffins and the human victims outside the outer chamber, which is the order <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> imposed on a rich grave.","questions":["Only about 200 of the more than 20,000 graves dug at Anyang are unlooted and still hold bronze vessels, which is the remainder from which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to be reconstructed.","A hollow averaging 0.08 cubic metres (3 cubic feet) cost almost nothing to dig, and yet in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it lies under the larger graves alone and marks most of a cemetery’s goods."],"answer":"Shang burial practice","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Graves dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000, at Anyang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unlooted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 200, holding bronzes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Waist pits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">135 of 351, at Dasikong</span></div>","traditional":"商代葬俗","hanzi":"商代葬俗","pinyin":"Shāngdài zàngsú","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang burial practice</b> is the way the Shang buried their dead, and it worked to one formula, the richest graves following a standard ideal and the poorer ones simplifying it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dead were laid on their backs, extended, in a rectangular shaft oriented north–south, usually inside a timber chamber holding one or two coffins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Building a great one went in stages: the shaft, a small pit cut in the middle of its floor, the chamber raised over that, and the gap outside it rammed and sometimes packed with attendants, animals and pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The inner coffin went down last, vessels, weapons and instruments were set between the two, planks and mats covered them, more attendants went on top, and the shaft was refilled layer by layer with offerings as it rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That left a set of zones with their own contents, jade and personal things nearest the body, vessels and weapons above the coffin and on the ledges, and the human victims outside the chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Rank showed in the shaft: royal tombs carried up to four ramps, rich graves outside the cemetery none at all, and the lineage cemeteries below them far less of everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Non-Shang peoples used the same range, the graves of the Siwa culture running from large ones with a secondary ledge and a wooden coffin down to small pits with neither.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than 20,000 graves have now been dug across the site at Anyang and about 4,600 published, but only some 200 are unlooted and hold bronze vessels, so the formula is read from a small remainder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pit under the coffin is the cheapest mark of all, averaging about 0.08 cubic metres (3 cubic feet), and yet at Dasikong the 135 graves that have one hold 76 per cent of the goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of the dead were laid face down instead, often east–west rather than north–south, at up to 30 per cent of some Anyang cemeteries and rarely before or after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang burial practice","sources":["Jessica Rawson, Konstantin Chugunov, Yegor Grebnev, and Limin Huan, “Chariotry and Prone Burials: Reassessing Late Shang China’s Relationship with Its Northern Neighbours,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 33, no. 2 (2020): 135–68, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:51b8bf31-edcf-408d-a149-4bff736dcd89. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 40–43, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Zhengyuan Wang, “Ritual Re-representation and the Making of Sociality: Rethinking Elite Burials in the Late Shang Yinxu,” <i>Archaeology International</i> 28, no. 1 (2025): 63–67, https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10220948/1/ai-3683-wang.pdf. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “The Qiang and the Question of Human Sacrifice in the Late Shang Period,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 35, no. 1 (1996), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17070. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","archaeology","religion","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Fu_Hao_Tomb%2C_c._1200_BC%2C_Reign_of_King_Wu_Ding%2C_Shang_Dynasty_%2810198751423%29.jpg/1280px-Fu_Hao_Tomb%2C_c._1200_BC%2C_Reign_of_King_Wu_Ding%2C_Shang_Dynasty_%2810198751423%29.jpg","title":"Attendants on the ledge","desc":"A step cut into the wall of the shaft carries the dead who went down with the tomb’s owner, and the rammed layers of the refill run below it; from the reconstructed tomb at the Yin Ruins, Anyang.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Human bones lying on a shelf of pale earth cut into the side of a pit, lit from above, with banded layers of packed reddish soil below."}},
{"id":"geo-005","num":5,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Alaska","answerText":"Alaska","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 January 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">49th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Alaska"},"facts":[["Capital","Juneau"],["Population","740,133 (2024)"],["Largest city","Anchorage"],["Total area","1,723,337 km² (665,384 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/Flag_of_Alaska.svg","credit":"Benny Benson, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Alaska.svg)","alt":"The flag of Alaska: eight gold stars on a dark blue field, seven of them forming the Big Dipper and a larger one standing alone in the upper corner."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Alaska</b> is by a wide margin the largest state of the United States, filling the north-western corner of the continent and cut off from the rest of the country by the width of western Canada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its total area is 1,723,337 square kilometres (665,384 square miles), nearly two and a half times that of Texas, though a seventh of that is water rather than land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fewer than 750,000 people live in all that space, so the largest state is also one of the least populous and much the most thinly settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land runs from the rainforest and fjords of the southern panhandle through interior taiga to alpine tundra, and the highest mountain in North America, 6,190 metres (20,310 feet) high, rises in Denali National Park and Preserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Anchorage is much the largest city, with about 290,000 residents, while the seat of government sits at Juneau on the panhandle, a town of some 32,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> People have lived here for more than ten thousand years, Alaska being the American end of the Pleistocene crossroad that joined the continent to Siberia whenever the sea fell far enough to bare the floor of the Bering Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russia claimed the coast after Vitus Bering was sent to explore it in 1725, but never supported more than about four hundred settlers, and its interest faded after defeat in the Crimean War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1867 Secretary of State William Seward agreed to buy the territory for $7.2 million, roughly two cents an acre, and it was formally transferred on 18 October that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Critics called the purchase Seward's Folly, and he was vindicated only when gold was found in the Yukon in 1896 and Alaska became the gateway to the Klondike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was governed as a territory for nearly a century, at times by the military and at times hardly at all, before entering the Union as the 49th state on 3 January 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Jason G. Gauthier, \"History and the Census: Alaska and Its People,\" U.S. Census Bureau History, 1 January 2024, https://www.census.gov/about/history/stories/monthly/2024/january-2024.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Alaska,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ak/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Purchase of Alaska, 1867,\" Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/alaska-purchase. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Wonder_Lake_and_Denali.jpg/1920px-Wonder_Lake_and_Denali.jpg","title":"Denali, Alaska","desc":"Denali.","credit":"Denali National Park and Preserve, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wonder_Lake_and_Denali.jpg","alt":"A view of Denali."}},
{"id":"geo-501","num":501,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Sacramento","answerText":"Sacramento","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1848</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1854</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"California","dot":"Sacramento"},"facts":[["State","California"],["Population","535,798 (2024)"],["Largest city","Los Angeles"],["Rank in state","6th largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Flag_of_Sacramento%2C_California.svg","credit":"Henry W. Schmitt and DevinCook, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sacramento,_California.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sacramento: broad curving bands of blue, white and dark navy suggesting two rivers meeting, with a green shape at the upper hoist and a gold one at the fly, and CITY OF SACRAMENTO in white capitals along the foot."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Sacramento</b> is the capital of California<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, standing in the Central Valley<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> where the American River joins the Sacramento.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 536,000 people live in the city, which makes it the sixth largest in the state, far behind Los Angeles and San Diego but the seat of government for both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground it occupies has been the homeland of the Nisenan people since time immemorial, and Europeans first settled at Sutter's Fort, built in 1839 by a Swiss immigrant on a Mexican land grant he called New Helvetia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the ten years that followed, the fort was the economic centre of the first permanent colonial foothold in the valley, and its industries rested on the exploited labour of the Native people who had helped build it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The discovery of gold at Sutter's Mill in Coloma in 1848 began the rush that undermined John Sutter's own businesses and brought a far greater exploitation of Native people across California.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The city itself was founded in December 1848 by his son, John A. Sutter Jr., as an outgrowth of the fort, and it grew as migrants poured into a territory that became the thirty-first state in 1850.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That new government had nowhere settled to sit: between 1849 and 1854 the capital moved between four towns, most of them less than five years old, which earned it the nickname of the floating capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sacramento held the legislature for about four months in 1852 before a severe flood drove it away, and after a year at Benicia the seat of government returned here for good in 1854.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its position on the river made it the natural entrepot of the gold country, a distribution point and a commercial centre for the whole northern interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the terminus of the wagon trains, the stagecoach and riverboat lines, the telegraph, the pony express and the first transcontinental railroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["California Office of Historic Preservation, \"Old Sacramento,\" California Historical Landmark no. 812, California State Parks, https://ohp.parks.ca.gov/ListedResources/Detail/812. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","California State Parks, \"Sutter's Fort State Historic Park,\" https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=485. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, \"California: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/california-state-guide. [Open access]","California State Capitol Museum, \"Planning,\" Capitol History, California State Parks, https://capitolmuseum.ca.gov/about-the-capitol/history-of-the-capitol/planning/. [Open access]","California State Parks, \"California Indian Heritage Center Park Property,\" https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=22628. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Sacramento%2C_California_skyline.jpg/1920px-Sacramento%2C_California_skyline.jpg","title":"Sacramento","desc":"Sacramento, California, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacramento,_California_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Sacramento, California."}},
{"id":"geo-006","num":6,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hawaii","answerText":"Hawaii","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 August 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Hawaii","zoom":14},"facts":[["Capital","Honolulu"],["Population","1,446,146 (2024)"],["Largest city","Honolulu"],["Total area","28,313 km² (10,932 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Flag_of_Hawaii.svg","credit":"Dbenbenn, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hawaii.svg)","alt":"The flag of Hawaii: the British Union Flag in the upper hoist corner, over eight horizontal stripes of white, red and blue."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hawaii</b> is the only state of the United States outside North America and the only one made up entirely of islands, a volcanic chain in the central Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its 28,313 square kilometres (10,932 square miles) hold more sea than land: only 16,635 square kilometres (6,423 square miles) of that is dry ground, the rest being the water between and around the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 1.45 million people live there<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, and Honolulu is both the capital and much the largest place, with some 345,000 residents in the urban core the census counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Two of the world's most active volcanoes, Kīlauea and Mauna Loa, stand within Hawaiʻi Volcanoes National Park, which climbs from the sea to 4,170 metres (13,680 feet) and is a World Heritage Site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The islands were settled long before any written record, and their people divided the land into ahupuaʻa running from the mountains to the shore and lived under a body of sacred law, the kapu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> James Cook reached the archipelago in 1778 and named it the Sandwich Islands, and by 1810 Kamehameha I had brought the whole chain under a single crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised Hawaiian independence by treaty in 1826, Britain and France jointly guaranteed it in 1843, and the two governments exchanged diplomatic representatives from 1853.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In January 1893 a revolutionary party gathered at ʻIolani Palace forced Queen Liliʻuokalani, the last sovereign of the kingdom, to give up control of her government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Congress then passed a joint resolution of annexation, which President McKinley signed in July 1898, and the islands were annexed that August and organised as a United States territory two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pearl Harbor was attacked on 7 December 1941, bringing the United States into the Second World War<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>, and Hawaii entered the Union as the 50th state on 21 August 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Lavonda Kay Broadnax, \"Hawaii: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/hawaii-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Hawaii,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/hi/index.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Hawaii,\" A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/hawaii. [Open access]","\"Annexation of Hawaii: Topics in Chronicling America,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 12 June 2025, https://guides.loc.gov/chronicling-america-annexation-hawaii. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Episode_4_of_Kilauea%27s_December_2024_eruption_2025-01-16.jpg/1920px-Episode_4_of_Kilauea%27s_December_2024_eruption_2025-01-16.jpg","title":"Kīlauea, Hawaii","desc":"Kīlauea.","credit":"USGS/K. Lynn, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Episode_4_of_Kilauea%27s_December_2024_eruption_2025-01-16.jpg","alt":"A view of Kīlauea."}},
{"id":"geo-502","num":502,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Austin","answerText":"Austin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1846</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Texas","dot":"Austin"},"facts":[["State","Texas"],["Population","993,588 (2024)"],["Largest city","Houston"],["Rank in state","5th largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Flag_of_Austin%2C_Texas.svg","credit":"Alex Microbe, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Austin,_Texas.svg)","alt":"The flag of Austin: on white, a gold-edged shield in red, white and blue bearing a lamp of learning, under a winged crest with a cross and sword, above CITY OF AUSTIN in blue capitals."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Austin</b> is the capital of Texas and the seat of Travis County, standing on the Colorado River at the eastern edge of the Hill Country, where the Edwards Plateau breaks towards the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Just under a million people live in the city, which makes it the fifth largest in Texas, after Houston, San Antonio, Dallas and Fort Worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was built to order: in 1839 the three-year-old Republic of Texas chose the site for a permanent capital and named the town for Stephen F. Austin, who first brought Anglo-American settlers there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A commission sent out by the Texas Congress bought 7,735 acres along the river, taking in a hamlet called Waterloo, at the urging of President Mirabeau B. Lamar, who wanted the government pushed west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Edwin Waller laid out a grid fourteen blocks square either side of Congress Avenue, running north from the water to Capitol Square, and Congress first met that November in a log cabin behind a stockade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The choice was fought over at once: in 1842, after Mexican troops took San Antonio, Sam Houston ordered the national archives moved away for safety, and the townspeople formed a vigilance committee and refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When rangers came for the papers that December, Angelina Eberly fired a cannon at them, and the committee overtook the wagons and brought the archives back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Austin's population fell below 200 before a convention meeting there in 1845 approved annexation to the United States, and authority passed from the republic's last president to the first state governor on 19 February 1846.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A statewide vote settled the question for good in 1872, and the granite Capitol, paid for with three million acres of Panhandle land and crowned with a zinc Goddess of Liberty, was dedicated in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city won another statewide election in 1881 to become the site of the University of Texas, whose research from the 1960s drew the electronics industry to a town that had lived on government and learning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["David C. Humphrey, \"Austin, TX (Travis County),\" Handbook of Texas Online, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 27 August 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/austin-tx-travis-county. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Clinton Drake, \"Texas: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 28 November 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/texas-state-guide. [Open access]","Texas State Preservation Board, \"Capitol History,\" https://tspb.texas.gov/prop/tc/tc-history/history/index.html. [Open access]","Claudia Hazlewood, \"Archives War,\" Handbook of Texas Online, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 27 August 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/archives-war. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Downtown_Austin_Skyline_-_Lady_Bird_Lake_%2854987239041%29.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Austin_Skyline_-_Lady_Bird_Lake_%2854987239041%29.jpg","title":"Austin","desc":"Austin, Texas, seen from a distance.","credit":"ajay_suresh, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Austin_Skyline_-_Lady_Bird_Lake_(54987239041).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Austin, Texas."}},
{"id":"geo-007","num":7,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Michigan","answerText":"Michigan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 January 1837</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">as a free state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Michigan"},"facts":[["Capital","Lansing"],["Population","10,140,459 (2024)"],["Largest city","Detroit"],["Total area","250,487 km² (96,714 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Flag_of_Michigan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Michigan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Michigan: on dark blue, an eagle above a shield showing a man on a lakeshore at sunrise, flanked by an elk and a moose over Latin mottoes."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Michigan</b> is bordered by four of the five Great Lakes<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, and that is what shapes it: of its 250,487 square kilometres (96,714 square miles) almost 99,000 are Great Lakes water rather than land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 10.1 million people live there<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, more than 645,000 of them in Detroit, the largest city, while Lansing, the capital, has about 114,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The north is older ground and emptier: Isle Royale lies far out in Lake Superior, and copper has been worked on the Keweenaw Peninsula for more than 7,000 years, first by Indigenous people who made it into tools and trade goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pictured Rocks raises sandstone cliffs straight out of Lake Superior, and at Sleeping Bear Dunes the bluffs stand 137 metres (450 feet) above Lake Michigan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lakes are also why the country was fought over: at the River Raisin in January 1813 the War of 1812 produced the greatest victory for Tecumseh's Confederation and the British, and the heaviest American defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The aftermath of that battle led to the federal laws that forced the removal, relocation and assimilation of native nations, and its consequences continue today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Michigan was admitted to the Union as a free state on 26 January 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of the 19th century the south-east was on its way to putting the world on wheels: maritime trade on the Detroit River had left a workforce skilled in metal and machine trades, and coachbuilders were already there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> R. E. Olds, Henry Ford, David Buick, Walter Chrysler and Henry Leland worked out between them how to make cars in quantity, and the modern assembly line of the early 1900s made them affordable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That built a middle class and a labour movement with it, and in the Second World War the region turned from cars to tanks, jeeps and aircraft as what Roosevelt called the Arsenal of Democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Michigan: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/michigan-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Michigan,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/mi/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"MotorCities National Heritage Area,\" U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/places/motorcities-national-heritage-area.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Pictured_Rocks_Bridalveil_Falls.jpg/1920px-Pictured_Rocks_Bridalveil_Falls.jpg","title":"Pictured Rocks, Michigan","desc":"Pictured Rocks National Lakeshore.","credit":"National Park Service, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pictured_Rocks_Bridalveil_Falls.jpg","alt":"A view of Pictured Rocks National Lakeshore."}},
{"id":"geo-503","num":503,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tallahassee","answerText":"Tallahassee","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1824</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spanish mission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1656 – 1704</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Florida","dot":"Tallahassee"},"facts":[["State","Florida"],["Population","205,089 (2024)"],["Largest city","Jacksonville"],["Rank in state","9th largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/Flag_of_Tallahassee%2C_Florida_%282020%E2%80%93present%29.svg","credit":"City of Tallahassee, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tallahassee,_Florida_%282020%E2%80%93present%29.svg)","alt":"The flag of Tallahassee: on white, a blue disc bearing a white star swept about by a curving line, above CITY OF TALLAHASSEE in dark capitals."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tallahassee</b> is the capital of Florida, in the north-western panhandle<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, and with about 205,000 residents it is only the ninth largest city in a state of 23 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its site has been a seat of power for a very long time: Anhaica Apalache, the chief town of the Apalachee, stood here, and Hernando de Soto's expedition wintered in it in 1539 and 1540.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Spanish missionaries reached the Apalachee province in 1633, and in 1656 the crown chose the village of San Luis as its western capital and moved it to the ridge where the mission stands today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 1,400 people lived at San Luis, among them a Spanish deputy governor and one of the most powerful Apalachee chiefs, around a plaza with a council house on one side and a Franciscan church on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1704 raids out of Carolina destroyed most of the Apalachee missions and San Luis was burned and abandoned, its people killed, enslaved or driven into exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States took Florida by treaty in 1821 and inherited two colonial capitals at opposite ends of it, Pensacola in the west and St Augustine on the Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Territorial citizens asked Congress for a road between them, and the two years of construction that began in 1824 opened an interior nobody could previously cross without a guide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That road brought the establishment of Tallahassee, and it brought homesteaders onto land held by American Indians, whose displacement ran into the Second Seminole War of 1835 to 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Florida became the twenty-seventh state on 3 March 1845 with its government already fixed here, and here it has stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peninsula that now holds most of the state's people was barely settled then, which is why the most populous state of the south-east is governed from one of its smaller cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, \"Florida: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/florida-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Andrew Jackson Trail,\" U.S. Department of the Interior, https://home.nps.gov/articles/andrew-jackson-trail.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"San Luis de Talimali,\" National Historic Landmark Nomination, updated documentation, 2004, https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/67d92cf2-58d6-4530-b79d-4b182a00beae. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/TallahasseeCityscape.png","title":"Tallahassee","desc":"Tallahassee, Florida, seen from a distance.","credit":"Urbantallahassee, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TallahasseeCityscape.png","alt":"A wide view of Tallahassee, Florida."}},
{"id":"geo-008","num":8,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Louisiana","answerText":"Louisiana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1812, as the 18th state</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Purchased</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1803, from France</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Louisiana"},"facts":[["Capital","Baton Rouge"],["Population","4,597,740 (2024)"],["Largest city","New Orleans"],["Total area","135,659 km² (52,378 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e0/Flag_of_Louisiana.svg","credit":"Curtis Vann, Jr., public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Louisiana.svg)","alt":"The flag of Louisiana: on dark blue, a white pelican feeding three chicks in its nest, above a ribbon reading UNION JUSTICE CONFIDENCE."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Louisiana</b> sits at the mouth of the Mississippi, and of its 135,659 square kilometres (52,378 square miles) nearly 24,000 are water rather than land<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — the delta, the bayous and the marsh behind the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 4.6 million people live there<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, New Orleans being the largest city with some 363,000 residents and Baton Rouge, the capital, about 221,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its deep past stands at Poverty Point in the north-east, where earthworks raised 3,000 years ago took five million hours of labour and sat at the centre of a trading network reaching hundreds of miles across the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name is French: Robert de La Salle, an early explorer of the river, called the country Louisiana after Louis XIV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spain had held it since 1762, France took it back in 1800 and took possession in 1802, and Americans who floated their produce down the river to the sea were alarmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Jefferson wrote that there was on the globe one single spot whose possessor was the natural and habitual enemy of the United States, and that it was New Orleans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He sent James Monroe to join Robert Livingston in Paris to buy that city; Napoleon, his army in St Domingue destroyed by yellow fever and war with England looming, offered the whole territory instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They took it, 530 million acres for $15 million, land that is now all or part of fifteen states, and Louisiana itself joined the Union in 1812 as the eighteenth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What grew there resembles nowhere else in the country: a Cajun culture, and a Creole culture of mixed French, African American and Native American heritage, ethnically and linguistically distinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Cane River plantations preserve the second of those, and in the French Quarter the National Park Service keeps the roots of jazz, the music New Orleans gave the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Louisiana: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/louisiana-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Louisiana,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/la/index.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Louisiana Purchase, 1803,\" Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/louisiana-purchase. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Atchafalaya_Basin.jpg","title":"the Atchafalaya Basin, Louisiana","desc":"Atchafalaya Basin.","credit":"U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, photographer not specified or unknown, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atchafalaya_Basin.jpg","alt":"A view of Atchafalaya Basin."}},
{"id":"geo-505","num":505,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Juneau","answerText":"Juneau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1880, as a gold camp</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1906</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Alaska","dot":"Juneau"},"facts":[["State","Alaska"],["Population","31,572 (2024)"],["Largest city","Anchorage"],["Rank in state","3rd largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Flag_of_Juneau%2C_Alaska.svg","credit":"Svgalbertian, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Juneau,_Alaska.svg)","alt":"The flag of Juneau: on pale blue, a green mountain and a band of water inside a rounded outline, beside JUNEAU and a gold star over ALASKA'S CAPITAL CITY."},"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Juneau</b> is the capital of Alaska, bordered by the ocean and nestled at the foot of Mount Roberts, on the shore of the Gastineau Channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 31,600 people live in the city and borough, which makes the seat of government only the third largest place in the state, some 160 behind Fairbanks and a tenth the size of Anchorage<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, in a state of about 740,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is one of just two state capitals in the country that cannot be reached by car, so a visitor arrives by aeroplane, by ferry or by cruise ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town began as a gold camp: in 1880 Joe Juneau and Richard Harris found gold on the creek they named Gold Creek, which still runs through the city into the channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The placers were soon worked out, but under them lay vast deposits of quartz lode, and those grew into two of the most famous hard-rock mines in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The town they paid for became a regional shipping and distribution point, with docks and warehouses, fisheries, hydro-electric power, banking and lumber behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1900 the territorial capital was moved from Sitka, Juneau became one of three district court headquarters — one of four by 1909 — and the city incorporated at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That move was slow and took almost the whole decade, which is why the city dates its capital status from 1906 rather than from the year Congress decided it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mining shaped the place for another half-century, and the Perseverance Trail still climbs from the middle of town to the ruins of the old workings, past waterfalls and mountain goats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Mendenhall Glacier reaches down almost to the edge of the city, and the trails beside it show how life returns to ground the ice has just left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Juneau, Alaska,\" Alaska Public Lands, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 2 February 2024, https://home.nps.gov/anch/planyourvisit/juneau-alaska.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"The Valentine Building, Juneau, Alaska,\" National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/355a4365-0cd2-4aac-9e30-89f7038ff78d. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Alaskan Hotel, Juneau, Alaska,\" National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78000526_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Downtown_Juneau%2C_Alaska_from_the_Goldbelt_Tram.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Juneau%2C_Alaska_from_the_Goldbelt_Tram.jpg","title":"Juneau","desc":"Juneau, Alaska, seen from a distance.","credit":"Cullen328, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Juneau,_Alaska_from_the_Goldbelt_Tram.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Juneau, Alaska."}},
{"id":"geo-009","num":9,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Maine","answerText":"Maine","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 March 1820</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">under the Missouri Compromise</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Maine"},"facts":[["Capital","Augusta"],["Population","1,405,012 (2024)"],["Largest city","Portland"],["Total area","91,633 km² (35,380 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Flag_of_Maine.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Maine.svg)","alt":"The flag of Maine: on dark blue, a shield showing a pine tree and a resting moose, flanked by a farmer and a sailor, under a star and the motto DIRIGO."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Maine</b> is the largest of the New England states and the north-eastern corner of the country<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, with 91,633 square kilometres (35,380 square miles) of territory<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and only about 1.4 million people on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Acadia National Park protects the highest rocky headlands on the Atlantic coastline of the United States, and takes four million visits a year doing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Portland is the largest city with fewer than 70,000 residents, and Augusta, the capital, is only the tenth largest with about 19,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Inland the country runs to forest and mountain: Katahdin Woods and Waters looks out at Katahdin, and the Appalachian Trail, 2,190 miles of footpath conceived in 1921 and finished in 1937, runs from here to Georgia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the far north the St John valley is Acadian country, where families share a religion, languages and a history that hold them together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Europeans wintered here before they settled anywhere else in the north: in 1604 Pierre Dugua's French expedition stopped on Saint Croix Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The winter was cruel — iced in and cut off from fresh water and game, 35 of the 79 men died before spring, when native people traded game for bread and the rest began to recover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The expedition moved on that summer, but the French presence in North America had begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Maine became a state on 15 March 1820 under the Missouri Compromise<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, a bargain to hold the balance in Congress that admitted Missouri with slavery and Maine free, and barred slavery elsewhere in the Louisiana Territory north of latitude 36° 30′.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Frances Perkins National Monument here honours the first woman to hold a presidential cabinet post, the Secretary of Labor behind Social Security and the shorter working week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Jennifer Dickinson, \"Maine: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/maine-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Maine,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/me/index.htm. [Open access]","Ken Drexler, \"Missouri Compromise: Primary Documents in American History,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, 7 March 2019, https://guides.loc.gov/missouri-compromise. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Acadia_National_Park_02.JPG/1920px-Acadia_National_Park_02.JPG","title":"Acadia National Park, Maine","desc":"Acadia National Park.","credit":"Plh1234us, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acadia_National_Park_02.JPG","alt":"A view of Acadia National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-506","num":506,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Honolulu","answerText":"Honolulu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1845</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ʻIolani Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun 1879</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Hawaii","dot":"Honolulu","zoom":14},"facts":[["State","Hawaii"],["Urban core","344,967 (2024)"],["City and county","998,747 (2024)"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Flag_of_Honolulu%2C_Hawaii.svg","credit":"Dyfsunctional, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Honolulu,_Hawaii.svg)","alt":"The flag of Honolulu: on yellow, a round seal reading CITY AND COUNTY OF HONOLULU and STATE OF HAWAII around a shield with a mountain, a ship and a sunburst."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Honolulu</b> is the capital of Hawaii and its largest city, on the south coast of Oʻahu, and a city in an unusual sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The census counts about 345,000 people in the urban core<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, while the city and county, which is the whole island, holds nearly a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been the seat of government since 1845, when Kamehameha III moved the royal residence from Lahaina on Maui and took over a coral-block palace on this ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American consuls had been at the port since 1820, and Kamehameha I had brought the whole chain under one rule some ten years before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kalākaua came to the throne in 1874, found the coral building falling down, pulled it down and commissioned a palace modelled on the ones he had seen in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> ʻIolani Palace, begun in 1879, is the only royal palace in the United States, built in a style called American Florentine that exists in no other building, and fitted with indoor plumbing, electric light and an early telephone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was both residence and capitol for the last two monarchs, Kalākaua and his sister Queen Liliʻuokalani, and after the kingdom was overthrown in 1893 it served as the capitol of every government that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> West of the city lies Pearl Harbor, attacked on 7 December 1941 in the act that brought the United States into the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The USS Arizona, sunk at her mooring there, is the resting place of many of the 1,177 of her crew who died that morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The memorial built over her draws about 1.4 million visitors a year to a state that has been in the Union since 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Counties: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" CO-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/counties/totals/co-est2024-alldata.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"ʻIolani Palace, Honolulu, Hawaiʻi,\" Places, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/places/iolanipalace.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Pearl Harbor National Memorial,\" U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/perl/index.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Hawaii,\" A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/hawaii. [Open access]","Lavonda Kay Broadnax, \"Hawaii: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/hawaii-state-guide. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Downtown_Honolulu_Panorama_%284670974859%29.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Honolulu_Panorama_%284670974859%29.jpg","title":"Honolulu","desc":"Honolulu, seen from a distance.","credit":"Daniel Ramirez from Honolulu, USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Honolulu_Panorama_(4670974859).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Honolulu."}},
{"id":"geo-010","num":10,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Oklahoma","answerText":"Oklahoma","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1907, as the 46th state</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reserved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1828, for Native Americans</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Oklahoma"},"facts":[["Capital","Oklahoma City"],["Population","4,095,393 (2024)"],["Largest city","Oklahoma City"],["Total area","181,037 km² (69,899 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6e/Flag_of_Oklahoma.svg","credit":"Xrmap flag collection, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Oklahoma.svg)","alt":"The flag of Oklahoma: on blue, a buff rawhide shield hung with seven feathers and crossed by a pipe and an olive branch, above OKLAHOMA in white."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Oklahoma</b> is a Choctaw word meaning red people<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, and the state it names covers 181,037 square kilometres (69,899 square miles) of the southern plains<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 4.1 million residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oklahoma City is both the capital and much the largest city, with some 713,000 people, and Tulsa in the north-east is the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The land came to the United States in 1803 with the Louisiana Purchase, and in 1828 Congress reserved it for Native Americans and required everyone else to withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reservation is why the Cherokee walked here: forced from Georgia, Alabama and Tennessee in 1838 and 1839, they travelled by foot, horse, wagon and steamboat into Indian Territory, which is now Oklahoma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were not the only nation moved, and the territory was policed from outside it, Judge Isaac Parker's court at Fort Smith holding jurisdiction over Indian Territory until 1896.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The plains beyond were fought over as the army pushed in: on 27 November 1868 Custer's 7th Cavalry made a surprise dawn attack on the Cheyenne village of the peace chief Black Kettle, on the Washita.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Older routes crossed the same ground — the Santa Fe Trail's freight wagons between western Missouri and New Mexico, and from 1857 John Butterfield's overland mail on its arcing sweep to the Far West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Act by act, tribal land was taken into United States territory, and Oklahoma did not become a state until 1907, as the 46th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The drought of the 1930s made a Dust Bowl of its rural counties and drove thousands of farmers into long migrations west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 19 April 1995 the Alfred P. Murrah Federal Building in Oklahoma City was destroyed, and the ground where it stood is now a national memorial to those killed, those who survived and those who came to help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Karen B. Walfall, \"Oklahoma: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/oklahoma-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Oklahoma,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ok/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Wichita_Mountains%2C_Oklahoma%2C_USA.jpg/1920px-Wichita_Mountains%2C_Oklahoma%2C_USA.jpg","title":"the Wichita Mountains, Oklahoma","desc":"The Wichita Mountains.","credit":"OKJaguar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wichita_Mountains,_Oklahoma,_USA.jpg","alt":"Low granite mountains under a clear sky."}},
{"id":"geo-507","num":507,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Lansing","answerText":"Lansing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1847</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1872 – 1879</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Michigan","dot":"Lansing"},"facts":[["State","Michigan"],["Population","114,336 (2024)"],["Largest city","Detroit"],["Rank in state","6th largest"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Lansing</b> is the capital of Michigan, a city of about 114,000 in the middle of the state and only the sixth largest of its cities<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, in a state of 10.1 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is the capital because of an argument: Michigan became a state in 1837 and pressed a territorial courthouse in Detroit into service as its first capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Detroit was the largest city and expected to keep the seat of government<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, but the state constitution of 1835 required the legislature to fix a permanent one by 1847.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The lobbying between rival towns was intense and acrimonious, no candidate could carry a majority, and the legislature settled instead on an almost completely undeveloped area in the middle of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That surprising choice became the city of Lansing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The capitol built there between 1872 and 1879, to a design by Elijah E. Myers, was one of the first statehouses modelled on the national capitol in Washington: Renaissance Revival, cruciform, under a tall dome of cast and sheet iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the third capitol in the state's history and the only one to survive, and its cornerstone was laid on 2 October 1873 before a crowd of tens of thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its main front looks east down Michigan Avenue towards East Lansing, where Michigan Agricultural College had been founded in 1855 and is now Michigan State University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The town also built cars: Ransom Eli Olds went into his father's machine shop here in 1885, built his first petrol car in 1896, and moved the works to Detroit in 1899 for want of capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He came back, founding the Reo Motor Car Company plant in Lansing in 1904 on the success of the curved-dash Oldsmobile of 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, \"Michigan: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/michigan-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Michigan State Capitol,\" National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000396_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Reo Motor Car Company Plant,\" National Historic Landmarks, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/subjects/nationalhistoriclandmarks/reo-motor-car-company-plant.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Lansing_Skyline_2022.jpg/1920px-Lansing_Skyline_2022.jpg","title":"Lansing","desc":"Lansing, Michigan, seen from a distance.","credit":"WeaponizingArchitecture, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lansing_Skyline_2022.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Lansing, Michigan."}},
{"id":"geo-011","num":11,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"New York","answerText":"New York","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declared 9 July 1776</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">adopted 20 April 1777</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>New York</b> is a state of the north-eastern United States, covering 141,297 square kilometres (54,555 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and holding about 19.9 million people, with Albany as its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It runs across a region of contrast, from the Atlantic shores of Long Island and the skyscrapers of Manhattan through the rivers, mountains and lakes of the interior to the plains of the Great Lakes region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dutch traders were there first: the West India Company had established the town of New Amsterdam at the southern end of Manhattan by 1626, when it sent Peter Minuit to oversee its business on the Hudson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> New York declared its independence on 9 July 1776, becoming one of the original thirteen states of the federal Union, and adopted its first constitution on 20 April 1777.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the autumn of that year American forces met a British army at Saratoga and forced it to surrender, a victory that renewed hopes of independence and won the war the foreign recognition it needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What made the state rich, though, was water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its canal system has run without a break since 1825, longer than any other constructed transport system in North America, and the corridor it threads crosses 843 kilometres (524 miles) of the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The canals built a network that made New York the Empire State and the nation’s foremost commercial and financial centre, and they opened the interior of the continent to settlers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Populations along the route at Syracuse, Rochester and Buffalo quadrupled between 1830 and 1850, and from 1892 to 1924 Ellis Island in the harbour processed over 12 million arriving immigrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> New York City, with about 8.5 million residents, remains by a wide margin the largest city in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New York"},"facts":[["Capital","Albany"],["Population","19,867,248 (2024)"],["Largest city","New York City"],["Total area","141,297 km² (54,555 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Flag_of_New_York.svg","credit":"State of New York, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_York.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state arms: two robed women flanking a shield of a sunrise over a river with ships, an eagle on a globe above, and a scroll below reading Excelsior."},"sources":["Angela McMillian, “New York: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/new-york-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, “Today in History,” New York: State Resource Guide, Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-york-state-guide/today-in-history. [Open access]","National Park Service, “A Crucial American Victory,” Saratoga National Historical Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 20 April 2024, https://www.nps.gov/sara/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Erie Canalway National Heritage Corridor,” U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/erie/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Historic Immigration Station,” Ellis Island, Statue of Liberty National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 3 February 2025, https://www.nps.gov/elis/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/3Falls_Niagara.jpg/1920px-3Falls_Niagara.jpg","title":"Niagara Falls, New York","desc":"Niagara Falls.","credit":"Saffron Blaze, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3Falls_Niagara.jpg","alt":"A view of Niagara Falls."}},
{"id":"geo-508","num":508,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Baton Rouge","answerText":"Baton Rouge","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1849, and again from 1882</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dedicated 1932</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Baton Rouge</b> stands on the east bank of the Mississippi as the capital of Louisiana, a city of about 221,000 people and, after New Orleans, the second largest in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Louisiana, whose 4.6 million residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> make it a middling state by population, had entered the Union in 1812 as the eighteenth, named by the French explorer Robert de La Salle for Louis XIV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before the 1840s its government sat at New Orleans, and the move upriver was made good by a capitol built at Baton Rouge between 1847 and 1849.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its architect, James Dakin, called the design ‘castellated Gothic’, and as a Gothic Revival public building in America it is a unique example of a rare type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mark Twain, passing 35 years later, blamed Sir Walter Scott for it, doubting that this ‘little sham castle’ would ever have been built without his medieval romances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The arrangement did not hold: the interiors were destroyed in December 1862 while the building served as a garrison, and the state was governed once more from New Orleans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was rebuilt between 1880 and 1882 and housed the government again until 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 1930 Huey Long, elected governor two years earlier, wanted the old building turned over to some collector of antiques, having already commissioned Weiss, Dreyfous and Seiferth of New Orleans to design a replacement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What they produced was a sculptured skyscraper of 34 storeys rising 137 metres (450 feet), the tallest building in the South when it was built, and it was dedicated in May 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 8 September 1935 a Baton Rouge physician shot Long in a corridor on its first floor, ending the career of one of the most controversial politicians of the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Louisiana","dot":"Baton Rouge"},"facts":[["State","Louisiana"],["Population","220,907 (2024)"],["Largest city","New Orleans"],["Rank in state","2nd largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Flag_of_Baton_Rouge%2C_Louisiana.svg","credit":"Niagara, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Baton_Rouge,_Louisiana.svg)","alt":"A red flag with the words Baton Rouge in white script, and at the hoist a navy shield bearing a fleur-de-lis, a castle and a Union Jack."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, “Louisiana: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/louisiana-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Louisiana State Capitol, Baton Rouge, Louisiana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73000862_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Louisiana State Capitol, Baton Rouge, Louisiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/78001421_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Downtown_Baton_Rouge.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Baton_Rouge.jpg","title":"Baton Rouge","desc":"Baton Rouge, Louisiana, seen from a distance.","credit":"getmahesh, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Baton_Rouge.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Baton Rouge, Louisiana."}},
{"id":"geo-012","num":12,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Idaho","answerText":"Idaho","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 July 1890, as the 43rd state</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Women voting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th state to allow it</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Idaho</b> is a state of the American north-west covering 216,443 square kilometres (83,569 square miles), fourteenth largest of the fifty,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about two million residents and Boise as both its capital and its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name is thought to come from a Shoshone phrase meaning ‘gem of the mountains’, though the derivation has never been settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shoshonean peoples have camped along the Snake River, which they call Bia Ogwaide, since time immemorial, fishing it for salmon, steelhead and sturgeon, and were probably the first to see fossils weathering out of its bluffs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nez Perce say the same of their own country to the north, in the words of their tribal executive committee: ‘We did not travel here; we are of this land.’<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the middle of the 19th century the Oregon Trail was carrying thousands of newcomers west from the eastern states, and life for the Shoshoneans was turned upside down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The territory was admitted as the forty-third state on 3 July 1890, and Idaho became the fourth state in the country to give women the vote, decades ahead of national suffrage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its landscape carries an unusual amount of the continent’s deep past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Craters of the Moon, a national monument since 1924, preserves the lava fields of the Snake River Plain,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> while the Hagerman valley, arid sagebrush scrub today, was Pliocene wetland, forest and grassland teeming with animals now long extinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A harder chapter is kept at Minidoka: after the attack on Pearl Harbor, an executive order of February 1942 moved nearly 120,000 people of Japanese ancestry into ten isolated relocation centres across seven states, one of them here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Most were American citizens, and they were held behind barbed wire and guard towers in barracks covered with tar paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Idaho"},"facts":[["Capital","Boise"],["Population","2,001,619 (2024)"],["Largest city","Boise"],["Total area","216,443 km² (83,569 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a4/Flag_of_Idaho.svg","credit":"Paul B. Evans, after the Adjutant General of Idaho and Emma Edwards Green, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Idaho.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state seal — a woman and a miner flanking a shield of a river and mountains, an elk's head above — over a red and gold scroll reading State of Idaho."},"sources":["Lavonda Kay Broadnax, “Idaho: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 21 February 2024, https://guides.loc.gov/idaho-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Before the Fossils: Shoshoneans, the Oregon Trail, and Settlement,” Hagerman Fossil Beds National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/hafo/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Nez Perce National Historical Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 29 December 2022, https://www.nps.gov/nepe/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Craters of the Moon National Monument and Preserve, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 22 January 2024, https://www.nps.gov/crmo/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Minidoka National Historic Site, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/miin/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Shoshone_Falls%2C_Idaho.jpg/1920px-Shoshone_Falls%2C_Idaho.jpg","title":"Shoshone Falls, Idaho","desc":"Shoshone Falls.","credit":"Frank Schulenburg, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shoshone_Falls,_Idaho.jpg","alt":"A view of Shoshone Falls."}},
{"id":"geo-509","num":509,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Augusta","answerText":"Augusta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Western</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1754</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">chosen 1827</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Augusta</b> is the capital of Maine, a city of about 19,000 people on the Kennebec River — only the tenth largest in a state of 1.4 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its beginnings were commercial rather than political: Fort Western was raised here in 1754 under Gershom Flagg, a master carpenter from Boston, for the Proprietors of the Kennebec Purchase, as a fortified fur trading post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its main building is 30 metres (100 feet) long and largely original, the two log blockhouses and the stockade around it reconstructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In September 1775 the fort was the starting point and main supply base of Benedict Arnold’s expedition against Quebec, which gives it a place in the War of Independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Maine became a state in 1820 its border with New Brunswick was undefined, and thousands of acres of valuable timber were at stake as agents from both sides claimed the harvest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That quarrel is what settled the town’s future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Congress passed a bill on 16 January 1827 to put an arsenal at Augusta, whose central position on the Kennebec made it the logical site, and a month later the town was chosen for the state capital for the same reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kennebec Arsenal that followed, eight granite buildings on forty acres of the east bank raised between 1828 and 1838, is the most intact early 19th-century munitions depot in the United States, and its size answered a boundary dispute that ran until 1842 and nearly brought a third war with Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A house completed here in 1833 became for more than thirty years the home of James G. Blaine — congressman, senator, Secretary of State and presidential candidate — whose work to improve relations with Latin America began the Pan-American movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His family gave it to the state in 1919, and it has been the governor’s residence since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Maine","dot":"Augusta"},"facts":[["State","Maine"],["Population","19,168 (2024)"],["Largest city","Portland"],["Rank in state","10th largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fort Western, Augusta, Maine,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000009_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Kennebec Arsenal, Augusta, Maine,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000046_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “James G. Blaine House, Augusta, Maine,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000024_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Augusta%2C_Maine_downtown.jpg/1920px-Augusta%2C_Maine_downtown.jpg","title":"Augusta","desc":"Augusta, Maine, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augusta,_Maine_downtown.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Augusta, Maine."}},
{"id":"geo-013","num":13,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"West Virginia","answerText":"West Virginia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1863</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Loyal Virginia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">capitol at Wheeling, 1861 – 1863</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>West Virginia</b> is an Appalachian state covering 62,756 square kilometres (24,230 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.77 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital and largest city alike at Charleston, a place of some 46,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is bordered by Pennsylvania to the north, Maryland and Virginia to the east, Kentucky to the south-west and Ohio to the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Adena raised Grave Creek Mound above the Ohio here long before any of that: 19 metres (62 feet) high and 73 metres (240 feet) across, it is the largest conical earth mound in the New World and one of the largest mortuary mounds anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state itself exists because Virginia seceded in 1861 and its western counties would not go with it, so delegates from 27 of them met at Wheeling from 13 to 15 May that year in what became the First Wheeling Convention, 436 members strong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The custom house at Wheeling then served as the capitol of loyal Virginia from June 1861 until June 1863, and housed the convention that framed the new state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Abraham Lincoln signed the proclamation admitting West Virginia on 20 June 1863, and the capital then refused to settle: Wheeling first, then Charleston, then Wheeling again, and Charleston ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was at Charles Town, in the eastern panhandle, that John Brown had been convicted of treason, conspiracy and murder after his raid on Harpers Ferry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coal made the state and set it fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Matewan on 19 May 1920 the police chief Sid Hatfield stood in the way of coal-company detectives evicting striking miners from company housing, and the shooting that followed left seven detectives, two miners and the town’s mayor dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Hatfield was assassinated for it the next year, which touched off the Battle of Blair Mountain of 1921 — the largest and most violent labour uprising in American history, and the beginning of the end of company rule in the coalfields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"West Virginia"},"facts":[["Capital","Charleston"],["Population","1,769,979 (2024)"],["Largest city","Charleston"],["Total area","62,756 km² (24,230 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Flag_of_West_Virginia.svg","credit":"Militaryace and Denelson83, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_West_Virginia.svg)","alt":"A white flag with a blue border, bearing the state arms — a farmer and a miner flanking a rock dated 20 June 1863, with two crossed rifles and a liberty cap below — wreathed in rhododendron."},"sources":["Angela McMillian, “West Virginia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/west-virginia-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Grave Creek Mound, Moundsville, West Virginia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000751_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “West Virginia Independence Hall, Wheeling, West Virginia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000660_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Matewan Historic District, Matewan, West Virginia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/93000303_text. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/New_River_Gorge_Bridge.jpg/1920px-New_River_Gorge_Bridge.jpg","title":"the New River Gorge, West Virginia","desc":"New River Gorge Bridge.","credit":"JaGa, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_River_Gorge_Bridge.jpg","alt":"A view of New River Gorge Bridge."}},
{"id":"geo-510","num":510,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Oklahoma City","answerText":"Oklahoma City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 April 1889, in the land run</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by vote, 11 June 1910</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Oklahoma City</b> is the capital of Oklahoma and much the largest city in it, with about 713,000 residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> in a state of 4.1 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was made in a single afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> President Benjamin Harrison set noon on 22 April 1889 for the opening of nearly two million acres to settlers, and somewhere between 50,000 and 100,000 people took part in what became known as ‘Harrison’s Horse Race’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They raced for their claims on foot, on horseback, and by train, wagon and bicycle, and many were after town lots rather than farmland — for merchants and professionals the future town was the real prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Guthrie, 40 kilometres (25 miles) to the north, was the town the government favoured, and it became the capital of Oklahoma Territory in 1890 and of the state when Oklahoma entered the Union in 1907.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Numbers settled the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Guthrie held steady at roughly 10,000 people between 1900 and 1910 while Oklahoma City rose from about the same figure to nearly 60,000, and Governor Charles Haskell called a statewide vote between Guthrie, Oklahoma City and Shawnee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was held on Saturday 11 June 1910 and Oklahoma City won overwhelmingly; that night Haskell had the state seal brought to the Lee-Huckins Hotel, wrote the proclamation on hotel stationery and set up temporary offices there, and the Supreme Court upheld the transfer in 1911.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oil arrived next: the Oklahoma City Number One discovery well was completed on 4 December 1928, and by 1935 the field had produced 409 million barrels of crude and 95 companies were employing twelve thousand people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Capitol itself stands above an oil pool, and in 1941 a well was brought in by directional drilling on the south plaza of its main entrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Oklahoma","dot":"Oklahoma City"},"facts":[["State","Oklahoma"],["Population","712,919 (2024)"],["Largest city","Oklahoma City"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Flag_of_Oklahoma_City%2C_Oklahoma.png","credit":"City Council of Oklahoma City, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Oklahoma_City,_Oklahoma.png)","alt":"A white flag with a thin red border, bearing the round blue and red city seal in the middle, quartered with a covered wagon, an oil derrick and an atom, and lettered Seal of the City of Oklahoma City."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Guthrie Historic District, Guthrie, Oklahoma,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001664_text. [Open access]","Karen B. Walfall, “Oklahoma: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/oklahoma-state-guide. [Open access]","Linda D. Wilson, “Oklahoma City,” The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture, Oklahoma Historical Society, last updated 8 June 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=OK025. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Downtown_Oklahoma_City_skyline_%282%29.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Oklahoma_City_skyline_%282%29.jpg","title":"Oklahoma City","desc":"Oklahoma City, seen from a distance.","credit":"Greater Oklahoma City Chamber and Oklahoma City Convention and Visitors Bureau (CVB) (uploaded by Chamber employee Lillie-Beth Brinkman lbrinkman@okcchamber.com), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Oklahoma_City_skyline_(2).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Oklahoma City."}},
{"id":"geo-014","num":14,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Maryland","answerText":"Maryland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 April 1788, as the 7th state</span><span class=\"dt-k\">National capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Annapolis, 1783 – 1784</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Maryland</b> covers 32,131 square kilometres (12,406 square miles) on the mid-Atlantic coast<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.26 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Annapolis and its largest city at Baltimore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nearly a fifth of that area is water rather than land, the Chesapeake Bay reaching so far up into the state that it very nearly cuts it in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the Union as the seventh state on 28 April 1788, and in 1791 gave up land of its own for the new national capital at Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For nine months before that, Annapolis had been the national capital itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Maryland State House, begun in 1772 and finished about 1779, is the oldest building in the country still in active use as a seat of state government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Congress sat in it from 26 November 1783 until 13 August 1784, and two things happened there that shaped the republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> George Washington appeared before Congress to resign his post as commander in chief, establishing the precedent of civilian leadership, and on 14 January 1784 the Treaty of Paris was ratified in the same room, giving formal end to the Revolutionary War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thirty years later the British came for Baltimore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They gathered in September 1814, bombarded Fort McHenry for 25 hours, failed to break the city’s defences and withdrew — and Francis Scott Key, watching and seeing a huge flag of 9 by 13 metres (30 by 42 feet) still over the garrison, wrote four stanzas to a popular tune that became the national anthem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A national monument in the state now commemorates Harriet Tubman, the Underground Railroad’s most famous conductor, who led people out of slavery to freedom in the North.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Maryland"},"facts":[["Capital","Annapolis"],["Population","6,263,220 (2024)"],["Largest city","Baltimore"],["Total area","32,131 km² (12,406 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Flag_of_Maryland.svg","credit":"Michael Wheeler, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Maryland.svg)","alt":"A flag quartered with the black and gold check of the Calvert arms and the red and white botonny cross of the Crossland arms, the quarters alternating diagonally."},"sources":["Ken Drexler, “Maryland: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/maryland-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Maryland State House, Annapolis, Maryland,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000385_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Star-Spangled Banner,” Fort McHenry National Monument and Historic Shrine, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 8 September 2020, https://www.nps.gov/fomc/learn/historyculture/the-star-spangled-banner.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Harriet Tubman Underground Railroad National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/hatu/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Chesapeake_Bay_Bridge_July_2017-6.jpg/1920px-Chesapeake_Bay_Bridge_July_2017-6.jpg","title":"Chesapeake Bay, Maryland","desc":"The Chesapeake Bay Bridge crossing the bay.","credit":"JefferyGoldman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chesapeake_Bay_Bridge_July_2017-6.jpg","alt":"A long steel bridge striding across open water."}},
{"id":"geo-511","num":511,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Albany","answerText":"Albany","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867 – 1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Schuyler Mansion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hamilton married there, 1780</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Albany</b> is the capital of New York, a city of about 101,000 on the Hudson<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and only the sixth largest in a state of 19.9 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Dutch were on this stretch of the river first: the upper Hudson lay within the patroonship of Rensselaerswyck, and the museum of the colonial Dutch across the water shows finds from the Fort Orange excavations in Albany itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city’s grandest house belonged to Philip Schuyler, one of the wealthiest men of the Revolution, who directed the invasion of Canada in 1776 and planned the defences against the three-pronged British invasion of New York the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was also an architect of the destructive campaigns of 1779 against the Haudenosaunee and their allies, supplying George Washington with intelligence and invasion routes and afterwards working to claim Haudenosaunee land for the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His Albany home and his war work alike depended on enslaved as well as paid labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That house saw the war at close quarters: General John Burgoyne was held there as a prisoner after his surrender at Saratoga in 1777, Elizabeth Schuyler married Alexander Hamilton in it in 1780, and Loyalist rangers tried to carry the general off from it in July 1781.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city’s other monument took a generation to build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thomas Fuller began the New York State Capitol in 1867, and by 1875 the walls had reached the top of the second storey and the money had run out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A board of Frederick Law Olmsted, Leopold Eidlitz and Henry Hobson Richardson was appointed to review the design, the style was altered in 1876 from Second Empire to French Renaissance, and Isaac Perry saw it finished in 1899.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eidlitz’s Assembly Chamber carries the largest stone vault in the world, and Richardson’s western stair, modelled on the one at the Paris Opera, was carved so lavishly that it is still called the million dollar staircase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New York","dot":"Albany"},"facts":[["State","New York"],["Population","101,317 (2024)"],["Largest city","New York City"],["Rank in state","6th largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Flag_of_Albany%2C_New_York.svg","credit":"Albany Common Council, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Albany,_New_York.svg)","alt":"A flag of three horizontal bands, orange, white and blue, with the city arms on the white band: two figures flanking a shield of wheat sheaves and a beaver, under a sailing ship, over a scroll reading Assiduity."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","New York State Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation, “Schuyler Mansion State Historic Site,” https://parks.ny.gov/historic-sites/33/details.aspx. [Open access]","National Park Service, “New York State Capitol, Albany, New York,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000519_text. [Open access]","New York State Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation, “Crailo State Historic Site,” https://parks.ny.gov/historic-sites/30/details.aspx. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Albany_Downtown_Skyline_Keybank_and_SUNY_Building.jpg/1920px-Albany_Downtown_Skyline_Keybank_and_SUNY_Building.jpg","title":"Albany","desc":"Albany, New York, seen from a distance.","credit":"This image or media was taken or created by Matt H. Wade . To see his entire portfolio, click here . @ thatmattwade This image is protected by copyright ! If you would like to use it, please read this first., CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albany_Downtown_Skyline_Keybank_and_SUNY_Building.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Albany, New York."}},
{"id":"geo-015","num":15,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Nevada","answerText":"Nevada","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Comstock Lode</span><span class=\"dt-v\">silver rush from 1859</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nevada</b> is the seventh largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 286,380 square kilometres (110,572 square miles) of mostly grassland and barren country<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> holding about 3.27 million people, with Carson City as its capital and Las Vegas much its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Silver made it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Virginia City, on the Comstock Lode, was the first silver rush town in the country, and the first place in the West where the methods of large-scale industrial and corporate enterprise were intensely applied and developed — the mines forced deep shafts down after the veins and the problems that came with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By August 1860 the town had 42 stores, 42 saloons, two stamp mills, five lumber yards and 868 dwellings for a population of 2,345, while the neighbouring Gold Hill held 638 — and the whole of Nevada then counted 6,857 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Statehood came four years later, on 31 October 1864, with Carson City chosen as the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Las Vegas, now much the biggest place in the state, was not founded until 15 May 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What changed the scale of Nevada was water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hoover Dam was raised in the Black Canyon of the Colorado on the Arizona line, where summer temperatures in the gorge reach 52 °C (125 °F) and cloudbursts and sudden floods were among the difficulties; the first concrete was placed on 6 June 1933 and the last on 29 May 1935, and the dam was dedicated that September, two years ahead of schedule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At 221 metres (726 feet) from the lowest foundation rock to the crest it was the highest dam in the world when it was built, and it remains the highest concrete dam in the western hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its reservoir was made deep enough to hold the normal flow of the river, average floods included, for two years, and the work has been compared as engineering to the Panama Canal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Nevada"},"facts":[["Capital","Carson City"],["Population","3,267,467 (2024)"],["Largest city","Las Vegas"],["Total area","286,380 km² (110,572 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Flag_of_Nevada.svg","credit":"Caleb Moore, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nevada.svg)","alt":"A cobalt blue flag with a device in the upper hoist quarter: a silver star above the words Battle Born on a gold scroll, between two sprays of sagebrush, with the state name below."},"sources":["Angela McMillian, “Nevada: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/nevada-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Virginia City Historic District, Storey and Lyon Counties, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000458_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Hoover Dam, Clark County, Nevada, and Mohave County, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/81000382_text. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/2017_Aerial_view_Hoover_Dam_4774.jpg/1920px-2017_Aerial_view_Hoover_Dam_4774.jpg","title":"Hoover Dam, Nevada","desc":"Hoover Dam.","credit":"Mariordo (Mario Roberto Durán Ortiz), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2017_Aerial_view_Hoover_Dam_4774.jpg","alt":"A view of Hoover Dam."}},
{"id":"geo-512","num":512,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Boise","answerText":"Boise","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Boise Basin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">gold rush, 1863</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assay Office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first assay 2 March 1872</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Boise</b> is the capital of Idaho and much the largest city in it, with about 238,000 residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> in a state of two million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gold brought it into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Elias Davidson Pierce found gold on the Clearwater River in 1860, which drew a rush of miners into northern Idaho the next year, and further placer strikes followed on the Salmon River in 1862 and in the Boise Basin in 1863.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With some 20,656 miners on the ground, Congress made Idaho a territory on 4 March 1863, and between 1861 and 1866 its gold output came to somewhere between 42 and 52 million dollars — second only to Nevada in those years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Getting the metal to market was another matter: the mines lay so far from anywhere, behind so many mountains, that from 1864 there was a strong demand for a federal mint or assay office in Idaho.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Congress appropriated 75,000 dollars for one at Boise by an act of 19 February 1869.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Drawn up under Alfred B. Mullett, Supervising Architect of the Treasury, and built of local Boise sandstone, the Assay Office was substantially finished by June 1871, though machinery arrived so late that no assay was made in it until 2 March 1872.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By then the rich surface placers were largely worked out, and its dignity and stone stood in sharp contrast to the log and wooden buildings of the young territorial capital around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Idaho became a state in 1890,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and the statehouse in Boise was completed in 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Outside it stands a bronze Lincoln on a base of sandstone quarried at nearby Table Rock, given to the state in 1915 and declaring on its east face that Idaho is related to Abraham Lincoln more than any other state — he having signed the act that created the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Idaho","dot":"Boise"},"facts":[["State","Idaho"],["Population","237,963 (2024)"],["Largest city","Boise"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Boise%2C_Idaho%27s_official_flag.png","credit":"“Man got cards”, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boise,_Idaho%27s_official_flag.png)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the city seal in the middle: two men flanking a quartered shield under a scroll reading Peril, Energy, Success, ringed by the words Seal of Boise City."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Assay Office, Boise, Idaho,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000305_text. [Open access]","Lavonda Kay Broadnax, “Idaho: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 21 February 2024, https://guides.loc.gov/idaho-state-guide. [Open access]","Idaho State Historical Society, “Idaho State Capitol,” https://history.idaho.gov/capitol/. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Boise_Downtown_Panoramic.jpg/1920px-Boise_Downtown_Panoramic.jpg","title":"Boise","desc":"Boise, Idaho, seen from a distance.","credit":"Patrick Rodriguez, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boise_Downtown_Panoramic.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Boise, Idaho."}},
{"id":"geo-016","num":16,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Utah","answerText":"Utah","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 January 1896</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mormon trek</span><span class=\"dt-v\">into the valley, 1847</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Utah</b> is the eleventh largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 219,882 square kilometres (84,897 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 3.5 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Salt Lake City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is nicknamed the Beehive State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brigham Young led the Mormon trek west from the Missouri Valley through the rugged Wasatch Mountains in 1847, and tradition holds that, threading his way down Emigration Canyon, he first saw the valley unobstructed from the knoll where a stone monument now stands and is said to have declared ‘this is the place’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The route he pioneered became known as the Mormon Trail, and carried the migrations into the Great Salt Lake Valley until 1850, when Parley Pratt opened the Golden Pass Trail to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the city they laid out, the temple on Temple Square took forty years to build, going up between 1853 and 1893 under Truman O. Angell and Joseph Young.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The Tabernacle organ beside it was first used in 1867 with 700 pipes working; it has run on electricity since 1915 and now has nearly 11,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Meanwhile the railroads were closing on one another across the desert, and the two lines met at Promontory on 10 May 1869 to complete the first transcontinental railroad, an occasion marked with four spikes of precious metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Utah itself did not become a state until 4 January 1896.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bingham Canyon had flourished on gold and silver from 1863 to 1893, and in 1887 Colonel Enos A. Wall filed claim to the vast body of low-grade copper ore lying under it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The open pit that followed became the most productive of all the low-grade copper mines that soon appeared, and it still yields about 14 percent of American copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Utah"},"facts":[["Capital","Salt Lake City"],["Population","3,503,613 (2024)"],["Largest city","Salt Lake City"],["Total area","219,882 km² (84,897 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Flag_of_Utah.svg","credit":"The State of Utah, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Utah.svg)","alt":"A flag of navy, white and red bands cut as snowy peaks, with a gold beehive inside a navy and gold hexagon over a white star at the centre."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Utah: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/utah-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Emigration Canyon, Salt Lake County, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000737_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Temple Square, Salt Lake City, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000738_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Golden Spike National Historical Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 20 June 2025, https://www.nps.gov/gosp/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Bingham Canyon Open Pit Copper Mine, Salt Lake County, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000736_text. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Delicate_arch_sunset.jpg/1920px-Delicate_arch_sunset.jpg","title":"Delicate Arch, Utah","desc":"Delicate Arch.","credit":"Palacemusic, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delicate_arch_sunset.jpg","alt":"A view of Delicate Arch."}},
{"id":"geo-513","num":513,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Charleston","answerText":"Charleston","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Clendenin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1788</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by vote in 1877, settled 1885</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Charleston</b> is the capital of West Virginia and its largest city, a place of about 46,000 people<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> in a state of 1.77 million,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> standing on the flat floodplain where the Elk River joins the Great Kanawha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Settlers had reached the neighbourhood before the Revolution and abandoned it in 1779, though most were back by 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1788 George Clendenin and his brothers became the first settlers on the site of the present city, building Fort Clendenin near the river; it was renamed Fort Lee in 1792, and Clendenin was instrumental in the forming of Kanawha County.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Salt made the valley’s early money, and the fortunes of the Kanawha rested in their turn on saltmaking, agriculture, land speculation, coal, timber and processing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The floodplain is narrow, which is why the town built upward rather than outward: a disproportionate number of its early buildings ran to several storeys, and the Kanawha Valley Bank went up twenty of them in 1928 on the ground the old state capitol had stood on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Where the capital should sit was argued over for fifteen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It came here from Wheeling in 1870 and went back to Wheeling again, until the citizens of West Virginia voted in 1877 to make Charleston the permanent seat of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first state capitol building in the city opened in 1885, and after that the town’s growth was assured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The railways arrived with it — the Kanawha and Michigan reached Charleston in 1884, the Charleston, Clendenin and Sutton opened north into the interior in 1890, and branch lines were soon snaking out into most of the outlying country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A town of 3,162 people in 1870 held 6,742 by 1890; the Victorian capitol burned down in 1921, and the building raised to replace it was designed by Cass Gilbert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"West Virginia","dot":"Charleston"},"facts":[["State","West Virginia"],["Population","46,482 (2024)"],["Largest city","Charleston"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Flag_of_Charleston%2C_West_Virginia.svg","credit":"City of Charleston, West Virginia; vectorised by TheVexillologistofKingwood, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Charleston,_West_Virginia.svg)","alt":"A gold flag with a blue border, bearing the city arms in the middle — a quartered shield with a pick, a riverboat and crossed tools, under a red scroll reading Charleston and the date MDCCXCIV."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","West Virginia State Historic Preservation Office, “Downtown Charleston Historic District,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://wvculture.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/Downtown-charleston-historic-district.pdf. [Open access]","West Virginia State Historic Preservation Office, “Downtown Charleston Historic District: Additional Documentation,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, 2024, https://wvculture.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/WV_KanawhaCo_DowntownCharlestonHistoricDistrictAD_form.pdf. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, “West Virginia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/west-virginia-state-guide. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Charleston%2C_West_Virginia_-_Aerial_%2838749393954%29.jpg","title":"Charleston","desc":"Charleston, West Virginia, seen from a distance.","credit":"formulanone from Huntsville, United States, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charleston,_West_Virginia_-_Aerial_(38749393954).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Charleston, West Virginia."}},
{"id":"geo-017","num":17,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Minnesota","answerText":"Minnesota","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 May 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mesabi opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mountain Iron Mine, 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Minnesota</b> is the twelfth largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 225,163 square kilometres (86,936 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.8 million residents, its capital at St. Paul and its largest city at Minneapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the North Star State, it entered the Union on 11 May 1858.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long before that a portage trail of 13.7 kilometres (8.5 miles) — Gichi Onigaming, the Great Carrying Place — let travellers past the falls and gorges of the Pigeon River, part of an ancient trade route between the Great Lakes and the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between 1731 and 1804 thousands of men shuttled furs and supplies over that Grand Portage to the North West Company depot, its headquarters and its rendezvous with the canoes out of Montreal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elsewhere the pipestone quarries have been worked for over 3,000 years, and 23 tribal nations are affiliated with ground where Indigenous quarriers still work them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The pine forests came next: the St. Croix Boom, opened in 1856, was where the great log drives ended and the timber was sorted, scaled and rafted for the sawmills downstream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At the peak the stored logs could cover 14 kilometres (nine miles) of river; the last went through on 12 June 1914, by which time the forests had been almost entirely cut over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Minneapolis meanwhile learned to make fine flour from hard spring wheat: a French purifier installed for Cadwallader Washburn in 1871 took out the bran and left the middlings, and after an explosion levelled his A Mill in 1878 he rebuilt it with automatic all-roller gradual-reduction machinery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In 1890 a prospector employed by the Merritt brothers uncovered the Mesabi Range, the largest iron ore deposit in the world, whose beds lay flat and near the surface and so could be taken by open pit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> From the first shipment out of the Mountain Iron Mine in 1892 until 1961 the range supplied over two billion gross tons, more than half the ore mined in the United States in those years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Minnesota"},"facts":[["Capital","St. Paul"],["Population","5,793,151 (2024)"],["Largest city","Minneapolis"],["Total area","225,163 km² (86,936 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Minnesota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/minnesota-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Grand Portage National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/grpo/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Pipestone National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 4 November 2020, https://www.nps.gov/pipe/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “St. Croix Boom Site, Washington County, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000407_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Washburn A Mill Complex, Minneapolis, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/83004388_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Mountain Iron Mine, St. Louis County, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/68000052_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Flag_of_Minnesota.svg","credit":"Minnesota State Emblems Redesign Commission, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Minnesota.svg)","alt":"A flag whose hoist is a dark blue kite shape bearing a white eight-pointed star, with a plain light blue field on the fly."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Spit_Rock_Lighthouse_2012.jpg/1920px-Spit_Rock_Lighthouse_2012.jpg","title":"Split Rock Lighthouse, Minnesota","desc":"Split Rock Lighthouse.","credit":"JMLAX101, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spit_Rock_Lighthouse_2012.jpg","alt":"A view of Split Rock Lighthouse."}},
{"id":"geo-514","num":514,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Annapolis","answerText":"Annapolis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from St. Mary's, 1695</span><span class=\"dt-k\">National capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 Nov 1783 – 13 Aug 1784</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naval Academy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1845</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Annapolis</b> is the capital of Maryland, a city of about 41,000 on a peninsula between Spa and College Creeks at the mouth of the Severn River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Maryland's capital had been at St. Mary's until 1695, when the royal governor Sir Francis Nicholson moved it to this more central spot, then a sparsely settled place called Anne Arundel Town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The new town of about 40 hectares (100 acres), soon enlarged to over 57 hectares (140 acres), was renamed for Princess Anne, the future queen, and incorporated in 1696.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its plan is a modified baroque one, of the kind used in the gardens at Versailles and proposed for London after the fire of 1666, reserving the highest ground for the state house and the church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A circle 161 metres (528 feet) across carries the State House and a smaller one of 104 metres (340 feet) the Anglican church, with radial streets running out from both — though those meeting the State House are set in a pinwheel, so none is on axis with its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Maryland State House, begun in 1772, is the oldest building in the country still in use as a seat of state government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between 26 November 1783 and 13 August 1784 it served as the nation's capital, the Continental Congress having come from Princeton because the building offered a national legislature the space and prestige it wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There Washington resigned his post as commander in chief before Congress, setting the precedent of civilian leadership of the military, and there the Congress ratified the Treaty of Paris on 14 January 1784, formally ending the Revolutionary War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In September 1786 the Annapolis Convention met here with representatives from six states and entertained the resolution out of which the Constitutional Convention grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States Naval Academy was established in 1845 on the site of Fort Severn, a four-hectare (10-acre) army reservation of fourteen buildings, and has trained many of the officers who have commanded the navy since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Maryland","dot":"Annapolis"},"facts":[["State","Maryland"],["Population","40,689 (2024)"],["Largest city","Baltimore"],["Rank in state","7th largest"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Flag_of_Annapolis%2C_Maryland.svg","credit":"RCraig09, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Annapolis,_Maryland.svg)","alt":"A white flag bearing a royal crown above a thistle and a Tudor rose on one stem, over a gold scroll reading VIXI LIBER ET MORIAR."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Maryland: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/maryland-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Colonial Annapolis Historic District, Anne Arundel County, Maryland,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000383_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Maryland State House, Annapolis, Maryland,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000385_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “United States Naval Academy, Annapolis, Maryland,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000386_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Aerial_view_of_Annapolis%2C_Maryland_LCCN2011631342.tif/lossy-page1-1920px-Aerial_view_of_Annapolis%2C_Maryland_LCCN2011631342.tif.jpg","title":"Annapolis","desc":"Annapolis, Maryland, seen from a distance.","credit":"Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Annapolis,_Maryland_LCCN2011631342.tif","alt":"A wide view of Annapolis, Maryland."}},
{"id":"geo-018","num":18,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"New Jersey","answerText":"New Jersey","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1787</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Trenton, from 1790</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>New Jersey</b> is the 47th largest American state, covering only 22,591 square kilometres (8,723 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> but holding about 9.5 million people, its capital at Trenton and its largest city Newark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Garden State, it was admitted to the Union on 18 December 1787, and Trenton became the official capital in 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Princeton's Nassau Hall, built in 1754–56 and named for King William III of Nassau, was the first important collegiate building of the middle colonies and for nearly fifty years held the whole college.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It served as barracks and hospital during the Revolution and saw the British last stand at the Battle of Princeton; from June to November 1783 the Continental Congress sat in it, thanked Washington for the war and received news that the peace treaty was signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1791 Alexander Hamilton and his associates chartered the Society for Establishing Useful Manufactures, capital $500,000, to prove American manufacturing profitable and so free the country of British goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The 20-metre (65-foot) head of the Great Falls of the Passaic at Paterson was to drive it; a first scheme for a canal to tidewater was abandoned as too costly in 1792, and Hamilton had Pierre Charles L'Enfant examine the raceway plan that replaced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was the first attempt in the United States to harness the whole power of a major river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In October 1858 William Parker Foulke dug bones from a marl pit at Haddonfield that proved to be the first relatively complete dinosaur skeleton found anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Joseph Leidy, who described it as <i>Hadrosaurus foulkii</i>, found the thigh bone 40 percent longer than the upper arm and concluded that it reared onto its hind legs, against Richard Owen, who coined 'Dinosauria' in 1841 and thought such creatures lumbering quadrupeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A century later, at Holmdel in 1965, Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson stumbled with the Horn Antenna on the microwave background radiation filling the universe, confirming the Big Bang and winning the 1978 Nobel Prize for Physics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Jersey"},"facts":[["Capital","Trenton"],["Population","9,500,851 (2024)"],["Largest city","Newark"],["Total area","22,591 km² (8,723 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “New Jersey: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-jersey-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nassau Hall, Princeton, New Jersey,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000465_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Great Falls of the Passaic/Society for Establishing Useful Manufactures Historic District, Paterson, New Jersey,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000391_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Hadrosaurus foulkii Leidy Site, Haddonfield, New Jersey,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/94001648_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Horn Antenna, Holmdel, New Jersey,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/89002457_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Flag_of_New_Jersey.svg","credit":"Author of xrmap, with modifications by Denelson83 and ChrisRuvolo, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Jersey.svg)","alt":"A buff flag bearing the state arms — a blue shield with three ploughs, a horse's head above it, and two robed women holding a liberty staff and a cornucopia, over a scroll reading LIBERTY AND PROSPERITY and the date 1776."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Delaware_Water_Gap_aerial_view.jpg/1920px-Delaware_Water_Gap_aerial_view.jpg","title":"the Delaware Water Gap, New Jersey","desc":"The Delaware Water Gap from the air.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delaware_Water_Gap_aerial_view.jpg","alt":"A river cutting a notch through a forested ridge."}},
{"id":"geo-515","num":515,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Carson City","answerText":"Carson City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Eagle Valley townsite, 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">act of 1869, built 1871</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Carson City</b> is the capital of Nevada, a city of about 58,000 in Eagle Valley and the state's sixth largest place, far behind Las Vegas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four men bought the Eagle Ranch from John Mankin in 1858, and Abraham Curry, credited with making it a town, had a townsite surveyed that September, the lots sold at first for $50 or given to anyone who would build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nevada obtained statehood on 31 October 1864, and Carson City was chosen as its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An act for a state capitol was signed on 23 February 1869, and Joseph Gosling's design was built by Peter Cavanaugh and Son in stone quarried at the state prison: a two-storey Grecian cross under a cupola 9 metres (30 feet) across and 37 metres (120 feet) above the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What paid for it was the Comstock Lode, where Virginia City became the first silver-rush town and where large-scale industrial mining was worked out between 1860 and 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> As the lode poured out silver, the cost and risk of hauling bullion to San Francisco became a problem, and the answer was a branch of the United States Mint in the small town itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was designed by Alfred B. Mullett, supervising architect of the Treasury from 1866 to 1874, whose other western work was the Portland custom house and the old San Francisco Mint, Curry superintended the building and became its first superintendent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1887 the Nevada legislature set up a commission to found a school for Native Americans in Ormsby County, and the Stewart Indian School opened in December 1890, named for Senator William M. Stewart, who pressed the idea in Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It was one of about 25 off-reservation Bureau of Indian Affairs boarding schools then operating, the only one in Nevada's history and the state's only secondary school for Native people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Comstock mines paid $125,335,925 in dividends by 1882 and dominated western mining through the 1870s, the decade in which Carson City got its capitol and its mint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Nevada","dot":"Carson City"},"facts":[["State","Nevada"],["Population","58,148 (2024)"],["Largest city","Las Vegas"],["Rank in state","6th largest"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Flag_of_Carson_City%2C_Nevada.gif","credit":"City of Carson City, Nevada, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Carson_City,_Nevada.gif)","alt":"A white flag crossed by a blue band, with the city seal at the hoist — an eagle over the capitol dome and a horse-drawn wagon, ringed by the words Carson City Nevada and the date 1858."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Nevada: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/nevada-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Abraham Curry House, Carson City, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/87000501_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Virginia City Historic District, Storey County, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000458_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nevada State Capitol, Carson City, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75002126_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “U.S. Mint, Carson City, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75002127_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Stewart Indian School, Carson City, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/85002432_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Skyline_of_Carson_City%2C_NV.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Carson_City%2C_NV.jpg","title":"Carson City","desc":"Carson City, Nevada, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Carson_City,_NV.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Carson City, Nevada."}},
{"id":"geo-019","num":19,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Massachusetts","answerText":"Massachusetts","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 February 1788</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Harvard</span><span class=\"dt-v\">established 1636</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lowell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">canals from 1822</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Massachusetts</b> is the seventh smallest of the American states, covering 27,336 square kilometres (10,554 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 7.1 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Boston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is officially a Commonwealth rather than a state, and it was admitted to the Union on 6 February 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Harvard College was established in 1636, the first colonial institution of higher learning, and its liberal arts course was patterned on those of Oxford and Cambridge; Massachusetts Hall, put up between 1718 and 1720, is its oldest surviving building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The college was meant chiefly to supply clergy for the colonies, though its graduates went into every walk of colonial life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Springfield an ordnance works that had supplied the whole north-eastern theatre in the Revolution was made a federal arsenal by act of Congress in 1794, seven years after Daniel Shays and his farmers tried and failed to raid it on 25 January 1787.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> That rising did as much as anything to build support for a stronger national government, and so bore indirectly on the making of the Constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The works then helped settle the principle of interchangeable parts: in 1822 a workman there, Thomas Blanchard, built a machine for turning gun stocks that proved the forerunner of the machinery for making standardised parts of every other kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nathan Appleton and Patrick Tracy Jackson began harnessing the Pawtucket Falls of the Merrimack in the same year, and by 1826 the first of their canals were finished and the city of Lowell established, named for the textile manufacturer Francis Cabot Lowell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Within two decades Lowell was the cotton-textile centre of the country, and it is reckoned the first American city developed primarily to be industrial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A quieter experiment ran alongside: Henry David Thoreau lived in a one-room cabin at Walden Pond from 4 July 1845 to 6 September 1847, finishing one book there and gathering the material for <i>Walden, or Life in the Woods</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Massachusetts"},"facts":[["Capital","Boston"],["Population","7,136,171 (2024)"],["Largest city","Boston"],["Total area","27,336 km² (10,554 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Massachusetts: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/massachusetts-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Massachusetts Hall, Harvard University, Cambridge, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000769_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Springfield Armory, Springfield, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000898_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Lowell Locks and Canals Historic District, Lowell, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76001972_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Walden Pond, Concord, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000790_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Flag_of_Massachusetts.svg","credit":"State of Massachusetts, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Massachusetts.svg)","alt":"A white flag bearing a blue shield on which a Native man stands holding a bow and arrow beside a white star, ringed by a blue scroll with the Latin motto and topped by an arm holding a sword."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/White_Crest_Beach_Wellfleet.jpg/1920px-White_Crest_Beach_Wellfleet.jpg","title":"Cape Cod, Massachusetts","desc":"Cape Cod.","credit":"This image or media was taken or created by Matt H. Wade . To see his entire portfolio, click here . @ thatmattwade This image is protected by copyright ! If you would like to use it, please read this first., CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Crest_Beach_Wellfleet.jpg","alt":"A view of Cape Cod."}},
{"id":"geo-516","num":516,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Salt Lake City","answerText":"Salt Lake City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mormon emigration of 1847</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Douglas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1864 – 1866</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Salt Lake City</b> is the capital of Utah and much its largest city, with about 218,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Brigham Young led the Mormon emigration of 1847 west from the Missouri Valley through the Wasatch Mountains, and tradition holds that he first saw the valley unobstructed from a knoll at the foot of Emigration Canyon, where a stone monument now stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Beehive House was built for Young in his two capacities, as second president of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and as first governor of Deseret, later Utah Territory, and was an official residence as much as a family home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ulysses S. Grant, William Tecumseh Sherman, Ralph Waldo Emerson, Mark Twain, Horace Greeley and Dom Pedro of Brazil were all entertained there in his lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1893 the church bought the house as the official residence of its presidents, and Lorenzo Snow and Joseph F. Smith were the only two who lived in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The territory's dealings with Washington were the most turbulent in the history of the American territorial system: it rejected public schools, federal land policy, the two-party system, parts of the common law and the primacy of the civil courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> President James Buchanan sent a military expedition in 1857 to compel the Mormon population to cooperate with federal officials, and between 1862 and 1887 Congress banned polygamy, reformed the courts, dissolved the Nauvoo Legion and put a commission over the territory's elections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only in the defeated South after the Civil War had the government interfered so far in a region's own affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Colonel Patrick Connor and the California Volunteers founded Fort Douglas in the foothills above the city in September 1862, nominally to guard the transcontinental telegraph and the mail routes, and in practice giving the government a permanent base close to the Mormon capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Council Hall, put up between 1864 and 1866 for the city's own offices, doubled as the territorial capitol until 1894.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Utah","dot":"Salt Lake City"},"facts":[["State","Utah"],["Population","217,783 (2024)"],["Largest city","Salt Lake City"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Flag_of_Salt_Lake_City_%282020%29.svg","credit":"Arianna Meinking and Elio Kennedy-Yoon for Salt Lake City Corporation, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Salt_Lake_City_(2020).svg)","alt":"A flag with a blue upper half and a white lower half, a white sego lily with a gold centre set in the blue near the hoist."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Utah: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/utah-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Emigration Canyon, Salt Lake County, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000737_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Brigham Young Complex, Salt Lake City, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000626_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old City Hall (Council Hall), Salt Lake City, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000846_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fort Douglas, Salt Lake City, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000628_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Salt_Lake_City_panorama.jpg","title":"Salt Lake City","desc":"Salt Lake City, seen from a distance.","credit":"Original uploader was user:Skyguy414 at en.wikipedia, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salt_Lake_City_panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Salt Lake City."}},
{"id":"geo-020","num":20,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Ohio","answerText":"Ohio","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 March 1803</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canal opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ohio and Erie, 1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Flyer III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1905</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ohio</b> is the 34th largest of the American states, covering 116,098 square kilometres (44,826 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 11.9 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Columbus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Buckeye State, it became the 17th state on 1 March 1803.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long before that the Hopewell, whose presence in southern Ohio runs from about 300 BCE to 250 CE, raised geometric earthworks across what was the focal area of that culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Newark group was among the largest and most complicated of them, covering something like 5 square kilometres (2 square miles) of circles, rectangles and octagons joined by parallel walls, with mounds and smaller rings inside and outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of it went under the growing town, and three portions survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Ohio and Erie Canal, joining Cleveland to Portsmouth, was finished in 1832, and with the Miami and Erie it formed the trunk of a network of some 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) that tied Lake Erie to the Ohio River and reached the canals of New York, Pennsylvania and Indiana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Cut between 1820 and 1845, it was among the most extensive and successful antebellum canal systems in the country, and it brought population, towns and trade in a rush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Ripley, on a hill above the Ohio River and northern Kentucky, the Presbyterian minister John Rankin built a house in 1828 that became one of the first stations on the Underground Railroad, and from it he is thought to have helped as many as 2,000 escaping slaves northward into Canada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His <i>Letters on American Slavery</i>, published as a book in 1826, were among the first clearly argued antislavery writings printed west of the Appalachians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 1905 Wilbur and Orville Wright built the Flyer III, the first aeroplane capable of sustained and controlled flight and fit for practical use, their object being a machine of practical utility rather than “a useless and extravagant toy.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Ohio"},"facts":[["Capital","Columbus"],["Population","11,883,304 (2024)"],["Largest city","Columbus"],["Total area","116,098 km² (44,826 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Ohio: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/ohio-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Newark Earthworks, Licking County, Ohio,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000614_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ohio and Erie Canal, Cuyahoga County, Ohio,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000607_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “John Rankin House, Ripley, Ohio,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000485_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wright Flyer III, Dayton, Ohio,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/90001747_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/Flag_of_Ohio.svg","credit":"John Eisenmann, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ohio.svg)","alt":"A swallow-tailed flag of red and white stripes with a blue triangle at the hoist, in which seventeen white stars surround a red disc within a white ring."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Cuyahoga_Valley_National_Park_20.jpg/1920px-Cuyahoga_Valley_National_Park_20.jpg","title":"Cuyahoga Valley, Ohio","desc":"Cuyahoga Valley National Park.","credit":"Niagara66, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuyahoga_Valley_National_Park_20.jpg","alt":"A view of Cuyahoga Valley National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-517","num":517,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"St. Paul","answerText":"St. Paul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Snelling</span><span class=\"dt-v\">site bought 1805</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Northern</span><span class=\"dt-v\">merged 1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kellogg-Briand</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Pact of 1928</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>St. Paul</b> is the capital of Minnesota and its second largest city, with about 307,000 people, standing on the Mississippi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lieutenant Zebulon Pike bought the site of the future Fort Snelling from the Sioux in 1805 while looking for the river's source, paying Chief Little Crow and his people a few baubles, some rum and the promise of $2,000 or its worth in trade goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fort, on the bluff commanding the meeting of the Minnesota and the Mississippi, was the first American fort in what is now Minnesota, and it mattered to the security of the north-west and to the army's own change from a frontier force into a modern one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> James J. Hill, born in Ontario in 1838 and in St. Paul by 1856, drove his lines to the Pacific: he bought the St. Paul and Pacific Railroad in 1878 and built from it a network across the north-west, merged in 1890 into the Great Northern Railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He took the Northern Pacific in 1893 and the Chicago, Burlington and Quincy soon after, but his attempt to gather them into the Northern Securities Company was defeated in 1901 under the new Sherman Anti-Trust Law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Hill's mansion was the showplace of the city until his death in 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Frank Billings Kellogg, a lawyer of the same city, was Calvin Coolidge's secretary of state from 1925 to 1929, and under him the department moved away from the interventionism of the administrations before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He negotiated the Kellogg-Briand Pact of 1928, in which 62 nations eventually agreed to renounce war as an instrument of national policy, and it brought him the Nobel Peace Prize for 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Fitzgeralds moved to 593 Summit Avenue about 1914 and later to number 599, their permanent house in the city, and Scott returned there in July 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the 1920s, with <i>The Great Gatsby</i>, he had come to embody the disillusioned literary outlook of the post-war years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Minnesota","dot":"St. Paul"},"facts":[["State","Minnesota"],["Population","307,465 (2024)"],["Largest city","Minneapolis"],["Rank in state","2nd largest"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Minnesota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/minnesota-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fort Snelling, Hennepin County, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000401_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “James J. Hill House, Saint Paul, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000405_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Frank B. Kellogg House, Saint Paul, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001035_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “F. Scott Fitzgerald House, Saint Paul, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000440_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Flag_of_Saint_Paul%2C_Minnesota.svg","credit":"Dyfsunctional, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saint_Paul,_Minnesota.svg)","alt":"A yellow flag crossed by a broad blue band, bearing a red shield charged with a log cabin, a capitol dome and a winged wheel, under a blue star and over a red scroll reading SAINT PAUL."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Saint_Paul_skyline%2C_West_Side_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Saint Paul","desc":"Saint Paul, Minnesota, seen from a distance.","credit":"Cliff from I now live in Arlington, VA (Outside Washington DC), USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Paul_skyline,_West_Side_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Saint Paul, Minnesota."}},
{"id":"geo-021","num":21,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Illinois","answerText":"Illinois","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 December 1818</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Springfield, from 1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pullman built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1880 – 1884</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Illinois</b> is the 25th largest of the American states, covering 149,995 square kilometres (57,914 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 12.7 million residents, its capital at Springfield and its largest city Chicago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Prairie State and the Land of Lincoln, it became a state on 3 December 1818, and Springfield took the capital in 1839.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cahokia, east of St. Louis, was the largest Mississippian community in eastern North America, covering more than 1,600 hectares (4,000 acres) and lived in from about 800 to 1500 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than a hundred mounds stand there: flat-topped platforms carrying temples and officials' quarters, and burial mounds holding the graves of important people, often with retainers beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Monks Mound, the largest, covers about 6 hectares (15 acres) and climbs in four terraces to roughly 30 metres (100 feet) above the valley floor, and the town around it was laid out on north-south and east-west axes, with a palisade about its inner precinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> George Pullman had a model industrial town built south of Chicago between 1880 and 1884, planned throughout by an architect and a landscape engineer working to one scheme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was the first serious American attempt to plan a workers' town whole, industry, housing, churches and recreation coordinated under one scheme against the crowded and unsanitary districts of other industrial cities, and the Pullman strike of 1894 made it a landmark of the labour movement as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1889 Jane Addams and Ellen Gates Starr moved into a decayed mansion on Halsted Street, by then in an immigrant slum, and made of it Hull-House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It was not the first of its kind in the country, but it answered the needs of those who came to it as nothing else then did, and its closeness to them earned a reputation well beyond America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Addams had come to the work after Rockford Seminary and a broken start in medicine, and her father, a miller, a bank president and a state senator, had been a friend of Lincoln.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Illinois"},"facts":[["Capital","Springfield"],["Population","12,710,158 (2024)"],["Largest city","Chicago"],["Total area","149,995 km² (57,914 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Illinois: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/illinois-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Cahokia Mounds, Madison and St. Clair Counties, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000899_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pullman Historic District, Chicago, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000054_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Jane Addams' Hull-House, Chicago, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000315_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Flag_of_Illinois.svg","credit":"Denelson83, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Illinois.svg)","alt":"A white flag bearing the state seal in outline — an eagle on a boulder holding a shield and a banner reading State Sovereignty National Union, with a rising sun behind — over the state's name in blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Starved_Rock%2C_Starved_Rock%2C_Illinois%2C_April_2009.jpg/1920px-Starved_Rock%2C_Starved_Rock%2C_Illinois%2C_April_2009.jpg","title":"Starved Rock, Illinois","desc":"Starved Rock above the Illinois River.","credit":"HeatherMarieKosur, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Starved_Rock,_Starved_Rock,_Illinois,_April_2009.jpg","alt":"A wooded sandstone butte standing over a wide river."}},
{"id":"geo-518","num":518,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Trenton","answerText":"Trenton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Trent's Town</span><span class=\"dt-v\">laid out from 1719</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 December 1776</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1790</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Trenton</b> is the capital of New Jersey, a city of about 91,000 people on the Delaware and the fifth largest place in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> William Trent, a Scottish merchant who had settled in Philadelphia about 1682 and traded in partnership with William Penn and James Logan, bought some 320 hectares (800 acres) across the Assunpink Creek at the Falls of the Delaware in 1714, and enlarged the tract over the years that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He built a summer house on the river bank in 1719, moved into it for good in 1721, and had a township laid out that he called Trent's Town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The barracks that still stand were begun on 31 May 1758 and finished in March 1759, put up by the New Jersey General Assembly to winter 300 British troops during the French and Indian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were one of five raised at Trenton, Burlington, Elizabethtown, Perth Amboy and New Brunswick so that soldiers need not be billeted by force on private householders, and the other four are gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On Christmas night 1776 Washington crossed the Delaware, and the next morning surprised and took the thousand-man German garrison of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The barracks, of undressed native stone and built in a U with a balcony the length of its inner side, is the only major building still standing that belongs to that battle, the rest of the field lying under the modern town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The crossing has taken on an almost legendary character in American memory, but it was a realistic and carefully planned stroke meant to rescue a failing cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By it Washington carried the war to the enemy and gave the new nation and his often-beaten army a taste of victory at the lowest ebb of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Trent's own house, where a Loyalist doctor tended American and Hessian wounded alike during the battle, later held three governors of New Jersey among its tenants,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the town became the state's official capital in 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Jersey","dot":"Trenton"},"facts":[["State","New Jersey"],["Population","91,193 (2024)"],["Largest city","Newark"],["Rank in state","5th largest"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Flag_of_Trenton%2C_New_Jersey.svg","credit":"Dyfsunctional, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Trenton,_New_Jersey.svg)","alt":"A flag divided blue at the hoist and yellow at the fly, with the round city seal in the middle — a horse's head above a blue shield bearing three gold wheatsheaves, over the date 1792."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “New Jersey: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-jersey-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “William Trent House, Trenton, New Jersey,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000388_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Barracks, Trenton, New Jersey,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000506_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Washington's Crossing, Mercer County, New Jersey and Bucks County, Pennsylvania,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000650_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Aerial_view_of_Trenton_bridges%2C_December_2022.jpg/1920px-Aerial_view_of_Trenton_bridges%2C_December_2022.jpg","title":"Trenton","desc":"Trenton, New Jersey, seen from a distance.","credit":"James Loesch from Trenton, New Jersey, USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Trenton_bridges,_December_2022.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Trenton, New Jersey."}},
{"id":"geo-022","num":22,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Virginia","answerText":"Virginia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Jamestown</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1607</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Africans landed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1619</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 June 1788</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Virginia</b> is the 35th largest of the American states, covering 110,787 square kilometres (42,775 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 8.8 million residents, its capital at Richmond and its largest city Virginia Beach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Old Dominion, it entered the Union as the 10th state on 25 June 1788, and it is the birthplace of more American presidents than any other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> England's first permanent colony was planted at Jamestown in 1607 by a joint venture called the Virginia Company, twenty years after the lost colony at Roanoke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1619 an English privateer put ashore ‘20 and odd’ Africans, traded to the colonists for victuals, and Native American, English and African met at one small outpost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Williamsburg began in 1633 as a palisaded barrier called Middle Plantation, became the colony's capital in 1699 and was renamed for William III, and for eighty years it was a political and cultural centre of American life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Washington, Patrick Henry, George Wythe, Thomas Jefferson and George Mason all studied, taught or held office there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> George Washington inherited Mount Vernon in 1752 on the death of his half-brother Lawrence, and for the twenty years before the Revolution farming it was his main occupation; he retired there in 1797 and was buried on the grounds two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His first military errand came in October 1753, carrying a British ultimatum to the French in the Ohio valley, whose refusal began the French and Indian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Thomas Jefferson designed the whole of Monticello, lived there through the most productive years of his life and died there in 1826.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> His plan of government for the Northwest Territory produced the ordinance of 1787, the first ban Congress passed on the spread of slavery;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> as a young politician he had argued for prohibiting slavery in new American territories, yet he never freed his own slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Virginia"},"facts":[["Capital","Richmond"],["Population","8,811,195 (2024)"],["Largest city","Virginia Beach"],["Total area","110,787 km² (42,775 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Virginia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/virginia-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Historic Jamestowne, Colonial National Historical Park, last updated 1 November 2021, https://www.nps.gov/jame/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Williamsburg Historic District, Williamsburg, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000925_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Mount Vernon, Fairfax County, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000833_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Monticello, Albemarle County, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000826_text. [Open access]","Peter Onuf, “Thomas Jefferson: Life in Brief,” Miller Center of Public Affairs, University of Virginia, https://millercenter.org/president/jefferson/life-in-brief. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Flag_of_Virginia.svg","credit":"Commonwealth of Virginia; vectorised by LadyofHats and others, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Virginia.svg)","alt":"A deep blue flag bearing the state seal in a white circle — a woman in armour standing over a fallen figure, with the motto Sic Semper Tyrannis beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Skyline_Drive_in_the_Fall_%2821852619608%29.jpg/1920px-Skyline_Drive_in_the_Fall_%2821852619608%29.jpg","title":"Shenandoah National Park, Virginia","desc":"Shenandoah National Park.","credit":"Shenandoah National Park from Virginia, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_Drive_in_the_Fall_(21852619608).jpg","alt":"A view of Shenandoah National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-519","num":519,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Boston","answerText":"Boston","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1712 – 1713</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tea Party</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1773</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Subway opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 September 1897</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Boston</b> is the capital of Massachusetts, its largest city and its oldest, with about 673,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Old State House, put up in 1712 and 1713 as the town's second town house, is the oldest Georgian public building left in the country, and it held the offices of the Province of Massachusetts Bay and of Suffolk County.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1776 until 1798 it was the seat of the Commonwealth's government, taking its present name when the new house on Beacon Hill was finished; fire had gutted it in 1747 and it was rebuilt the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Old South Meeting House, built in 1729 and 1730, seated so many that town meetings moved there when Faneuil Hall proved too small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The most consequential was the heated session of 16 December 1773 that ended in the Boston Tea Party; under the occupation the building was stripped of its fittings and used as a riding school for the King's cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Before 1805 the black inhabitants of the city worshipped in the churches of whites, and on 8 August that year the Reverend Thomas Paul and twenty others founded the First African Baptist Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Black labourers and craftsmen built its plain Federal meeting house on Beacon Hill, the oldest surviving African American church building in the country, and as the only sizeable hall in the city owned by black people it became the centre of their community as well as their worship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cut off from political life and with little schooling to be had, such congregations became a training ground for leadership, which is why the church came to hold so large a place in black American life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Tremont Street Subway, opened on 1 September 1897, was the first underground public transport system in the country and only the fifth in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 50 million rides were taken on it in its first full year, and its success led at once to a similar system in New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Massachusetts","dot":"Boston"},"facts":[["State","Massachusetts"],["Population","673,458 (2024)"],["Largest city","Boston"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Massachusetts: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/massachusetts-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old State House, Boston, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000779_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old South Meeting House, Boston, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000778_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “African Meeting House, Boston, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000087_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tremont Street Subway, Boston, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000788_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Flag_of_Boston.svg","credit":"City of Boston; vectorised by Svgalbertian, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Boston.svg)","alt":"A light blue flag bearing the round city seal — a view of the harbour town ringed by a gold band with a Latin motto, over the city’s Latin foundation inscription and the date 1630."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Boston_skyline_from_East_Boston_November_2016_panorama_5.jpg/1920px-Boston_skyline_from_East_Boston_November_2016_panorama_5.jpg","title":"Boston","desc":"Boston, seen from a distance.","credit":"King of Hearts, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boston_skyline_from_East_Boston_November_2016_panorama_5.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Boston."}},
{"id":"geo-023","num":23,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Washington","answerText":"Washington","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Columbia River</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reached in 1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">San Juan crisis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1859</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1889</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Washington</b> is the 18th largest of the American states, covering 184,661 square kilometres (71,298 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 8 million residents and its largest city at Seattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its archaeological record documents Interior and Coast Salish habitation stretching back thousands of years, and it was granted statehood on 11 November 1889 after several decades as a territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1775 the Spanish navigator Bruno Heceta sailed into a great bay he named Assumption and noted in his log that its currents made him think it the mouth of some great river, but he was swept back out into the Pacific before he could look closer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American captain and fur trader Robert Gray found the Columbia in 1792, the climax of a long search for the legendary Great River of the West, and it gave the United States a claim to the Pacific Northwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That led on to the Lewis and Clark expedition of 1804 to 1806 and to Fort Astoria in 1811, and provoked British exploration and fur posts in answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The quarrel over who owned the San Juan archipelago came to a head in 1859, by which time 25 Americans had settled there and the Hudson's Bay Company had some 5,000 sheep and 20 employees on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> General Winfield Scott, then 74, proposed a joint military and naval occupation until the title could be settled, each side keeping a hundred men on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The two nations held armed camps there for thirteen years, and the sovereignty was resolved only by the Treaty of Washington of 1871 and the German Emperor's arbitration that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pioneer Square is the heart of old Seattle and holds some of the handsomest Richardsonian Romanesque fronts in the north-west, the best preserved being the Pioneer Building by Elmer H. Fisher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Good stone being scarcer there than brick, the style was worked locally in brick with terracotta detail, what sandstone there was coming from Chuckanut, Wilkeson, Tenino and the San Juans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Washington"},"facts":[["Capital","Olympia"],["Population","7,958,180 (2024)"],["Largest city","Seattle"],["Total area","184,661 km² (71,298 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Washington: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/washington-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Chinook Point, Pacific County, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000747_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “American and English Camps, San Juan Island, Washington,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000369_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pioneer Building, Pergola, and Totem Pole, Seattle, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/77001340_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Flag_of_Washington.svg","credit":"Washington Secretary of State, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Washington.svg)","alt":"A dark green flag bearing the state seal in the middle — a portrait bust of George Washington in a gold ring lettered with the state's name and the date 1889."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Rainier20200906.jpg/1920px-Rainier20200906.jpg","title":"Mount Rainier, Washington","desc":"Mount Rainier.","credit":"Caleb Riston, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rainier20200906.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Rainier."}},
{"id":"geo-520","num":520,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Columbus","answerText":"Columbus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehouse bill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 January 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ace of Aces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 aircraft, 1918</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Columbus</b> is the capital of Ohio and its largest city, with about 933,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town's fear of losing the capital ended on 26 January 1838, when the new statehouse bill passed and the shops shut for a day of drinking and dancing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The competition the commissioners then staged drew more than sixty entries, one of them Gothic, though the panic of 1837 had not subsided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Work stopped for years, the foundations were covered over with earth and neighbours grazed their cows on the Capitol mound, and in 1843 a resolution to move the capital away passed the Senate and was stopped only with great effort in the House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city secured it at last in the winter of 1846 with the Act to Provide for the Erection of a New State House, in which the legislature appropriated no money but turned the whole workforce of the state penitentiary over to the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Capitol was not occupied until 1857, and it is reckoned one of the outstanding statements of Greek Revival architecture in America, its scale and daring simplicity among the most distinguished in native American design.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Ohio Theatre was the work of Thomas White Lamb, who began in 1909 designing for William Fox in New York and built more than 300 theatres in styles from Adam and French Baroque to Hindu and Persian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is one of the few picture palaces still refurbished and in use, a gilded extravaganza offering escape from the realities of the Depression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eddie Rickenbacker grew up in a plain frame house in the city, formed his interest in motor cars and aeroplanes there, and came home to it from the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One of the country's leading racing drivers before the war, he flew in the first combat mission ordered by an American commander of an American squadron, shot down 26 German aircraft in under six months to become the American Ace of Aces, and afterwards bought Eastern Air Lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Ohio","dot":"Columbus"},"facts":[["State","Ohio"],["Population","933,263 (2024)"],["Largest city","Columbus"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Flag_of_Columbus%2C_Ohio.svg","credit":"City of Columbus, Ohio; vectorised by Alex43223, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Columbus,_Ohio.svg)","alt":"A vertical tricolour of gold, white and crimson, the white band bearing the round city seal — an eagle over a shield with a sailing ship, ringed by stars and a laurel wreath."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Ohio: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/ohio-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ohio Statehouse, Columbus, Ohio,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72001011_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ohio Theatre, Columbus, Ohio,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73001437_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Captain Edward V. Rickenbacker House, Columbus, Ohio,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76001426_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Columbus_Downtown_skyline_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Columbus_Downtown_skyline_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Columbus","desc":"Columbus, Ohio, seen from a distance.","credit":"Trëxo, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columbus_Downtown_skyline_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Columbus, Ohio."}},
{"id":"geo-024","num":24,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Oregon","answerText":"Oregon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Migration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1843</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded by Britain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1846</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Admitted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1859</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Timberline Lodge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1935 – 1938</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Oregon</b> is the 9th largest of the American states, covering 254,799 square kilometres (98,379 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 4.3 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Salem and its largest city Portland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first wagons went west from Missouri in 1836, when a missionary party under Marcus and Narcissa Whitman set out for the Willamette valley and had to abandon them 320 kilometres (200 miles) short of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the spring of 1843 a wagon train of nearly a thousand people gathered at Independence, Missouri, and the Great Migration reached Oregon Country safely against a chorus of doubters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its success turned on South Pass, a valley 19 kilometres (12 miles) wide that was virtually the only place between the plains and Oregon where wagons could cross the Rocky Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1846 so many Americans had settled the region that Britain ceded Oregon Country to the United States,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and Congress passed the act admitting Oregon to the Union in 1859.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Placer gold was found at Rich Gulch on Jackson Creek in the Rogue River valley late in December 1851 or early in January 1852, by two prospectors from California.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Jacksonville sprang up that summer as a tent city of 500, and by 8 January 1853, when it became the seat of Jackson County, it held 900 people and up to 200 frame buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The mines drew Chinese labourers east of the Cascades, and at John Day the Kam Wah Chung Company building, a trading post of about 1866 on The Dalles Military Road, became the commercial, social and spiritual centre of the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Ing Hay, a trained herbal doctor and pulsologist who reached the region in 1885, went into partnership there in early 1887 with Lung On, and the rooms and papers left behind are perhaps the best collection of their kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On the south slope of Mount Hood at about 1,800 metres (6,000 feet), Timberline Lodge was built between 1935 and 1938 by artists and craftsmen of the Works Progress Administration, and Franklin D. Roosevelt dedicated it in 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Oregon"},"facts":[["Capital","Salem"],["Population","4,272,371 (2024)"],["Largest city","Portland"],["Total area","254,799 km² (98,379 sq mi)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture,” Oregon National Historic Trail, https://www.nps.gov/oreg/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “An Act for the Admission of Oregon into the Union,” 35th Cong., 2nd sess., 11 Stat. 383, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-11/pdf/STATUTE-11-Pg383.pdf. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Jacksonville Historic District, Jackson County, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000950_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Kam Wah Chung Company Building, John Day, Oregon,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73001575_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Timberline Lodge, Government Camp, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73001572_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Flag_of_Oregon.svg","credit":"User:Denelson83, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Oregon.svg)","alt":"A navy blue flag lettered STATE OF OREGON in gold above a gold shield ringed with stars, showing a covered wagon, oxen, a sunset over the sea and ships, with the year 1859 below it."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Crater_Lake_winter_pano2.jpg/1920px-Crater_Lake_winter_pano2.jpg","title":"Crater Lake, Oregon","desc":"Crater Lake.","credit":"WolfmanSF, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crater_Lake_winter_pano2.jpg","alt":"A view of Crater Lake."}},
{"id":"geo-521","num":521,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Springfield","answerText":"Springfield","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839 – 1876</span><span class=\"dt-k\">House Divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 June 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1874</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dana House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1902</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Springfield</b> is the capital of Illinois and its 7th largest city, with about 113,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Old State Capitol, designed by John F. Rague, served as the state's fifth capitol from 1839 until 1876, and Abraham Lincoln sat in the legislature there from December 1840.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He pleaded more than 200 cases in the building's Supreme Court room, and on 16 June 1858, accepting the Republican nomination for the Senate, he made the House Divided speech from the floor of that hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Through the presidential campaign of 1860 he worked from the governor's office in the same building, which stayed his headquarters until he left for Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stephen A. Douglas, three times sent to the Senate by the legislature meeting there and first at 34, spoke in the hall on 25 April 1861 at his old rival's request, rallying southern Illinois for the Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The plain frame house at Eighth and Jackson streets is the only one Lincoln ever owned, bought in January 1844 from the Reverend Charles Dresser, who had married him to Mary Todd two years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was home for sixteen years apart from his term in Congress, grew from a one-storey cottage into a full two storeys of twelve rooms, and none of the family lived in it again after the grand levee of 6 February 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lincoln lay in state in the capitol the night before his burial on 3 May 1865,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the citizens of Springfield organised the National Lincoln Monument Association at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Work on the tomb began in 1869 and it was dedicated in 1874, an attempt to kidnap the body for ransom was foiled in 1876, and in 1901 the casket was moved into a cement vault 3 metres (10 feet) below the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Frank Lloyd Wright designed the Dana House in 1902 and built it around the family's old home, pulling that down as he went but keeping one room intact, as a condition of the owner's inheritance required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Illinois","dot":"Springfield"},"facts":[["State","Illinois"],["Population","112,949 (2024)"],["Largest city","Chicago"],["Rank in state","7th largest"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Illinois: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/illinois-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old State Capitol, Springfield, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000331_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Lincoln Home, Springfield, Illinois,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000076_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Lincoln Tomb, Springfield, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000330_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Susan Lawrence Dana House, Springfield, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74000774_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Flag_of_Springfield%2C_Illinois.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Springfield,_Illinois.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a large red star outlined in white within a ring of small white stars, the city and state named in gold letters along the bottom."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Downtown_Springfield_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Springfield","desc":"Springfield, Illinois, seen from a distance.","credit":"spfldnet, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Springfield_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Springfield, Illinois."}},
{"id":"geo-025","num":25,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Arizona","answerText":"Arizona","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pueblo Grande</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 – 1450 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mission founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1700</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silver strike</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1877</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 February 1912</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Arizona</b> is the 6th largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 295,234 square kilometres (113,990 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 7.6 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital and largest city alike at Phoenix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It reached statehood on 14 February 1912, the last of the 48 conterminous states to be admitted, and women won the vote there that same year, eight years before the country as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hohokam culture of the southern desert began about 100 BCE, kept to the desert valleys and lived by intensive irrigation farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Pueblo Grande, now inside Phoenix, a village of those farmers raised a platform building 6 metres (20 feet) high covering more than a hectare (3 acres), with a ball court and large canals heading water out of the Salt River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The site was lived in from about 1 to 1450 CE, which is what makes it a record of the whole development of the culture rather than of one moment in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Mission San Xavier del Bac was founded in 1700 by the Jesuit Eusebio Francisco Kino, at a place then described as beyond the rim of Christendom and the furthest northern reach of Spain's empire in America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The present church, the third or perhaps the fourth on the site, was begun in 1783 and finished in 1797, and has stood under the flags of Spain, Mexico and the United States in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Silver was found in the San Pedro valley in 1877 by Ed Schieffelin, who had been told by troopers at Fort Huachuca that the only thing he would find out there was his tombstone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The boom town that grew on the strike took that name and became, briefly, the biggest and most notorious town in Arizona Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It is remembered less for its mines than for sixty seconds on 26 October 1881, when four men walked down Fremont Street to meet five others in a horse yard and fought the gun battle known as the OK Corral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Arizona"},"facts":[["Capital","Phoenix"],["Population","7,582,384 (2024)"],["Largest city","Phoenix"],["Total area","295,234 km² (113,990 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Arizona: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/arizona-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pueblo Grande Ruin and Irrigation Sites, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000185_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “San Xavier del Bac Mission, Tucson, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000191_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tombstone Historic District, Tombstone, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000171_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Flag_of_Arizona.svg","credit":"Madden, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Arizona.svg)","alt":"A flag whose upper half is a fan of thirteen red and gold rays and whose lower half is dark blue, with a large copper five-pointed star across the middle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Canyon_River_Tree_%28165872763%29.jpeg/1920px-Canyon_River_Tree_%28165872763%29.jpeg","title":"the Grand Canyon, Arizona","desc":"Grand Canyon.","credit":"Lennart Sikkema, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canyon_River_Tree_(165872763).jpeg","alt":"A view of Grand Canyon."}},
{"id":"geo-522","num":522,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Richmond","answerText":"Richmond","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Liberty or Death</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 March 1775</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1785</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tredegar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd in the US, 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Penny Savings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1903</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Richmond</b> is the capital of Virginia and its 3rd largest city, with about 234,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In St John's Church on 23 March 1775 Patrick Henry made the Liberty or Death speech, which swept the Virginia Convention meeting there from a tone of conciliation to resolutions putting the colony into a state of defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thomas Jefferson designed the state Capitol while he was in France, helped by the architect Charles-Louis Clérisseau and taking as his model the Maison Carrée, a Roman temple at Nîmes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Begun in 1785 and complete in its temple form by 1798, it introduced neo-classicism to American public building and was the first use of the temple form for a public building since Roman times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Houdon's statue of George Washington in Continental uniform was set in the rotunda in 1796, and the building houses the oldest legislature in the country, grown out of the colonial House of Burgesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The same building was the meeting place of the Confederate Congress through the years the city was the Confederate capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What made it worth holding was as much iron as politics: under Joseph R. Anderson, who had run it since 1841, the Tredegar Iron Works ranked third among American ironworks in 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Short of iron and coal, Anderson bought mines, furnaces and ore boats of his own with Confederate money, building one of the earliest far-reaching vertical combinations in the American iron industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Maggie Lena Walker, born in the city in 1867 to a mother who had been enslaved, became executive secretary-treasurer of the Independent Order of Saint Luke in 1899 and in 25 years took it from 57 chapters to 1,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1903 she led the order into banking with the Saint Luke Penny Savings Bank, the first American woman known to found and head a bank, and she ran it as president and then as chairman until her death in 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Virginia","dot":"Richmond"},"facts":[["State","Virginia"],["Population","233,655 (2024)"],["Largest city","Virginia Beach"],["Rank in state","3rd largest"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Virginia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/virginia-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “St. John’s Episcopal Church, Richmond, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000920_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Virginia State Capitol, Richmond, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000911_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tredegar Iron Works, Richmond, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71001048_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Maggie Lena Walker House, Richmond, Virginia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/75002100_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Flag_of_Richmond%2C_Virginia.svg","credit":"HoosierMan1816, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Richmond,_Virginia.svg)","alt":"A flag with a dark blue field above white and red bars, carrying the white silhouette of a boatman poling a flat boat through waves, ringed by ten white stars."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Richmond%2C_Virginia_skyline_01.jpg/1920px-Richmond%2C_Virginia_skyline_01.jpg","title":"Richmond","desc":"Richmond, Virginia, seen from a distance.","credit":"Ɱ, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richmond,_Virginia_skyline_01.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Richmond, Virginia."}},
{"id":"geo-026","num":26,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"New Mexico","answerText":"New Mexico","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Taos Pueblo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 900 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">San Estevan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1629 – 1642</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 January 1912</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trinity test</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 July 1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>New Mexico</b> is the 5th largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 314,917 square kilometres (121,590 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 2.1 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Santa Fe and its largest city Albuquerque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Santa Fe has been the capital since 1610, and the territory was admitted to the Union on 6 January 1912.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taos Pueblo is the last site of a Puebloan occupation of the Taos valley reaching back to at least 900 CE, and stands as evidence of how far that people adapted to centuries of change around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest phase identified in the valley, the Valdez, is dated to about 900 to 1200 CE by cross-dating a mineral-painted ware known as Taos Black-on-white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its sites are pithouses and pithouse villages with work areas and rooms built of jacal and adobe beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The great church of San Estevan del Rey at Acoma was built between 1629 and 1642 and repaired in 1799 and 1800, and is the finest of the Spanish colonial mission churches in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Mission architecture here is neither as baroque as the churches of Texas nor as polished as those of California: the plan and general form are Spanish, while the construction and much of the ornament are Puebloan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Los Alamos was set up in 1943 to design and build a nuclear weapon, and by the end of that year the implosion method, in which high explosive squeezes a subcritical mass of plutonium to supercriticality, had reached a point where a test was unavoidable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Testing it meant spending the country's entire supply of plutonium, so the first task was to devise a way of recovering the metal should the device fail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The world's first nuclear device was fired on 16 July 1945 at what is now the White Sands Missile Range, on the ground called the Trinity Site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Mexico"},"facts":[["Capital","Santa Fe"],["Population","2,130,256 (2024)"],["Largest city","Albuquerque"],["Total area","314,917 km² (121,590 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “New Mexico: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-mexico-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Taos Pueblo, Taos County, New Mexico,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000496_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “San Estevan del Rey Mission Church, Acoma, New Mexico,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000417_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Trinity Site, Socorro County, New Mexico,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000493_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Flag_of_New_Mexico.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Mexico.svg)","alt":"A plain yellow flag bearing a red sun symbol in the centre, a circle with four groups of four rays reaching out from it."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/2026_White_Sands_National_Park_Dunes_Clouds_and_Sun.jpg/1920px-2026_White_Sands_National_Park_Dunes_Clouds_and_Sun.jpg","title":"White Sands, New Mexico","desc":"The gypsum dunes of White Sands.","credit":"Pablorgalindo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2026_White_Sands_National_Park_Dunes_Clouds_and_Sun.jpg","alt":"White dunes stretching to the horizon under a broken sky."}},
{"id":"geo-523","num":523,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Olympia","answerText":"Olympia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First legislature</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 February 1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bigelow House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pioneer Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in use from 1856</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">bought 1901</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Olympia</b> is the capital of Washington and the 24th largest city in the state, with about 56,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Territorial Governor Isaac I. Stevens divided the territory into legislative and judicial districts, called an election for 20 January 1854 and fixed the first legislature to meet a month later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That session met in a hired room above a restaurant, ratified the choice of Olympia as the seat of government and drew up a code of laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The second met in the Masonic Hall in December 1854, and Edmond Sylvester, who had founded the town, gave the territory 4.9 hectares (12 acres) on Capitol Hill to build on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pioneer Capitol that followed measured 12 by 24 metres (40 by 80 feet), a two-storey wooden building like a New England colonial church with a small tower and no steeple, the House meeting above and the Council below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It was still in use at statehood and at the constitutional convention of 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Foundations for a new capitol were laid in 1893 but the depression stopped the work, and in 1901 the legislature bought the Thurston County Courthouse for $350,000 instead, making it the state's second permanent Capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ernest Flagg's design for the campus had got no further than its foundation, and when he was called back he proposed a group of buildings rather than one, so that work could go on as space was needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The commission took the idea and not the architect: a national competition drew 37 designs, and Wilder and White of New York won it with a plan built round a large domed building meant to read from a distance as one broad base under the dome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Among the oldest buildings left in the town is the Bigelow House of 1854, Gothic Revival, home of Daniel R. Bigelow, a pioneer lawyer who sat in that first territorial legislature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Washington","dot":"Olympia"},"facts":[["State","Washington"],["Population","56,271 (2024)"],["Largest city","Seattle"],["Rank in state","24th largest"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Washington: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/washington-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Capitol Building, Olympia, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75001877_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Washington State Capitol Historic District, Olympia, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79002564_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Daniel R. Bigelow House, Olympia, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79002562_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Olympia%2C_WA_Aerial_Scene.jpg/1920px-Olympia%2C_WA_Aerial_Scene.jpg","title":"Olympia","desc":"Olympia, Washington, seen from a distance.","credit":"Libbyflyguy, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olympia,_WA_Aerial_Scene.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Olympia, Washington."}},
{"id":"geo-027","num":27,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Georgia","answerText":"Georgia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Colony founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1732</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Savannah settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1733</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Echota</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1825 – 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nobel Prize</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1964</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Georgia</b> is the 24th largest of the American states, covering 153,910 square kilometres (59,425 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 11.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital and largest city alike at Atlanta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The colony was established in 1732 and settled at Savannah the year after, the last of the thirteen to be founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was also the only one founded and ruled by a board of trustees sitting in London, so that for its first two decades there was no governor or governing body in the colony itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long before that, Etowah stood above its river in the north of the state, a Mississippian centre of three large platform mounds and a village inside an encircling ditch that a palisade once ran beside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It served the smaller villages around it, and is one of three sites in the country to give extensive evidence of the Southeastern Ceremonial Complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> New Echota, called New Town until 1825, was the capital of the Cherokee Nation for the thirteen years to 1838, its government patterned on that of the United States and its territory reaching across the north of the state into three neighbouring ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Cherokee Phoenix, edited by Elias Boudinot, was the first newspaper printed in a Native American language, set in English and in the syllabary Sequoyah had devised in 1821, and it appeared from 21 February 1828 until 1834.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Those years ended in the signing of a treaty that led to the removal of the Cherokee to the west and to the near-total disappearance of the town itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Martin Luther King Jr. was born in his grandfather's house at 501 Auburn Avenue in Atlanta and was raised in that community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He was pastor of the Dexter Avenue and Ebenezer Baptist churches, stood at the front of the civil rights movement of the 1950s and 1960s, organised the largest marches the country had seen and was given the Nobel Peace Prize in 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Georgia"},"facts":[["Capital","Atlanta"],["Population","11,180,878 (2024)"],["Largest city","Atlanta"],["Total area","153,910 km² (59,425 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Georgia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/georgia-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Etowah Mounds, Bartow County, Georgia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000272_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “New Echota, Gordon County, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000869_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Martin Luther King Jr. Historic District, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74000677_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Flag_of_the_State_of_Georgia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_State_of_Georgia.svg)","alt":"A flag of three broad bars, red above white above red, with a blue canton carrying the state arms in gold within a ring of thirteen white stars."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Aerial_view_of_wetlands_in_Okefenokee.jpg/1920px-Aerial_view_of_wetlands_in_Okefenokee.jpg","title":"the Okefenokee Swamp, Georgia","desc":"Okefenokee Swamp.","credit":"Ryan Hagerty, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_wetlands_in_Okefenokee.jpg","alt":"A view of Okefenokee Swamp."}},
{"id":"geo-524","num":524,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Salem","answerText":"Salem","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Methodist mission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1834</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Willamette</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First mill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kay mill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1896</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Salem</b> is the capital of Oregon and its 2nd largest city, with about 180,000 people, some 80 kilometres (50 miles) south of Portland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Oregon was the domain of the fur trade until 1834, when the Reverend Jason Lee and a small band of Methodist missionaries set up a station on the Willamette about 16 kilometres (10 miles) north of the present city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the first mission established anywhere in the American north-west, and it ran satellite stations at The Dalles, Clatsop Plains, Oregon City and south of Puget Sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mission expanded to Salem in 1841, where a house for Lee and a parsonage for the preacher in charge were put up, and those white-painted frame buildings are the only structures left from the mission period anywhere in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The preacher in charge also directed the Indian Manual Labor Training School, the forerunner of Willamette University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Willamette was founded by those missionaries and settlers in 1842, which makes it the oldest institution of higher learning west of the Missouri River, and the territorial legislature gave it that name in 1853.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Waller Hall, its oldest surviving building, was a considerable undertaking for the 1860s and was begun only after four years of collecting the money for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mechanised woollen industry of the Pacific coast dates from 1857, when the first full textile factory on that coast opened here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thomas Kay, Squire Farrar and C. P. Bishop founded their own woollen mill in 1889, and when fire destroyed its frame building in 1895 the present mill was finished the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For a time it was the largest plant of its kind in the state, and it stayed under the family's ownership and management until 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Oregon","dot":"Salem"},"facts":[["State","Oregon"],["Population","180,406 (2024)"],["Largest city","Portland"],["Rank in state","2nd largest"]],"sources":["National Park Service, “Salem Downtown State Street–Commercial Street Historic District, Salem, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/01001067_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Jason Lee House, Salem, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73001580_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Waller Hall, Willamette University, Salem, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75001593_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Thomas Kay Woolen Mill, Salem, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73001579_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Flag_of_Salem%2C_Oregon_2022.svg","credit":"JKeaglePhD, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Salem,_Oregon_2022.svg)","alt":"The flag of Salem: a blue vertical band at the hoist beside a white field bearing a large pink cherry blossom, with a white seven-pointed star cut out of its centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Salem_Oregon_aerial.jpg/1920px-Salem_Oregon_aerial.jpg","title":"Salem","desc":"Salem, Oregon, seen from a distance.","credit":"Oregon Department of Transportation, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salem_Oregon_aerial.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Salem, Oregon."}},
{"id":"geo-028","num":28,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"South Carolina","answerText":"South Carolina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Charlesfort</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1562 – 1563</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Santa Elena</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1566 – 1587</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stono rising</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 September 1739</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 May 1788</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>South Carolina</b> is the 40th largest of the American states, covering 82,933 square kilometres (32,020 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.5 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Columbia and its largest city Charleston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the thirteen original colonies, it was named Carolina after King Charles I, divided from North Carolina in 1710, and ratified the Constitution on 23 May 1788 as the eighth state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two centuries before that its coast was contested ground: the French under Jean Ribault built Charlesfort in 1562 and gave it up three years before the Spanish arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pedro Menéndez de Avilés founded Santa Elena on the same ground in 1566 as an outpost against French intrusion into Spanish Florida, and the town was that province's capital until 1587.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At least one French fort and five Spanish ones were built in the vicinity of the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> By the Revolution it was reckoned one of the richest colonies in America, its merchants and planters forming a strong governing class, and more battles and skirmishes of that war were fought here than in any other state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wealth rested on slavery, and on 9 and 10 September 1739, some 32 kilometres (20 miles) south-west of Charleston, the Stono rising became one of the most serious insurrections of the colonial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Witnesses said the rebels marched like a disciplined company, and the conditions behind it were plain enough: many of the enslaved had been brought from Africa recently and kept much of their own culture, they far outnumbered the white population, and the Spanish in Florida were a standing military threat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Union forces took St Helena Island in 1861 the planters fled and left the Gullah people, who had kept their own language and customs, and northern missionaries arrived the next year to teach them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Robert Smalls, born into slavery at Beaufort in 1839, made himself nationally famous by carrying off the steamer <i>Planter</i>, then sat in the state assembly from 1868 and in the senate from 1870 before going to Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"South Carolina"},"facts":[["Capital","Columbia"],["Population","5,478,831 (2024)"],["Largest city","Charleston"],["Total area","82,933 km² (32,020 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “South Carolina: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/south-carolina-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Charlesfort–Santa Elena Site, Beaufort County, South Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001822_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Stono River Slave Rebellion Site, Charleston County, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001840_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Penn School Historic District, St. Helena Island, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001824_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Robert Smalls House, Beaufort, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001823_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Flag_of_South_Carolina.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Carolina.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a white crescent in the upper hoist corner and a white palmetto tree in the middle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Baldcypress_%28Taxodium_distichum%29_on_Congaree_National_Park_Low_Boardwalk_trail.jpg/1920px-Baldcypress_%28Taxodium_distichum%29_on_Congaree_National_Park_Low_Boardwalk_trail.jpg","title":"Congaree National Park, South Carolina","desc":"Congaree National Park.","credit":"Miguel.v, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baldcypress_(Taxodium_distichum)_on_Congaree_National_Park_Low_Boardwalk_trail.jpg","alt":"A view of Congaree National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-525","num":525,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Phoenix","answerText":"Phoenix","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Canal dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Townsite</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1870</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indian School</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1891</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Phoenix</b> is the capital of Arizona and its largest city, with about 1.7 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under the modern city lies Pueblo Grande, where Hohokam farmers raised a platform building 6 metres (20 feet) high with a ball court beside it and dug large canals to lead water out of the Salt River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No settlement other than theirs was made in the area through the Spanish and Mexican periods, and the land passed to the United States after the war with Mexico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anglo settlers arrived only in the 1860s: farmers moved into the valley in 1867 and had dug a canal and harvested a crop by the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1870 the settlement held 240 people and was moved 3 kilometres (two miles) west, where the first official townsite was laid out, and in 1871 it became the seat of Maricopa County.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The territorial capital wandered for 25 years before it settled here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It began at Fort Whipple in the Chino valley, moved to Prescott in April 1864 because of Confederate sympathies around Tucson, went to Tucson itself in 1867 when the southern counties had the larger population, and returned to Prescott in 1877.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Phoenix took it for good in 1889, the legislature and territorial offices sitting at first in the municipal buildings until the business of the territory outgrew them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Phoenix Indian School, founded in 1891, was the only federal boarding school in Arizona away from a reservation and by 1900 the second largest in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It belonged to the policy of assimilation that governed federal Indian education from the late 1880s to the 1930s, and its outing system placed pupils as day labourers in the growing city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Arizona","dot":"Phoenix"},"facts":[["State","Arizona"],["Population","1,673,164 (2024)"],["Largest city","Phoenix"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pueblo Grande Ruin and Irrigation Sites, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000185_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Phoenix Townsite, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78000550_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Arizona State Capitol Building, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74000455_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Phoenix Indian School Historic District, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/01000521_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Flag_of_Phoenix%2C_Arizona.svg/1280px-Flag_of_Phoenix%2C_Arizona.svg.png","credit":"City of Phoenix, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Phoenix,_Arizona.svg)","alt":"A deep maroon flag bearing a large white disc in which a stylised bird rises with its wings curving round to close the circle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Downtown_Phoenix_Skyline_%286974043971%29.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Phoenix_Skyline_%286974043971%29.jpg","title":"Phoenix","desc":"Phoenix, Arizona, seen from a distance.","credit":"Alan Stark from Goodyear, AZ, United States, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Phoenix_Skyline_(6974043971).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Phoenix, Arizona."}},
{"id":"geo-029","num":29,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"North Carolina","answerText":"North Carolina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1799</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mint gold</span><span class=\"dt-v\">through 1828</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NC Mutual</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 October 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First flight</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1903</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>North Carolina</b> is the 28th largest of the American states, covering 139,391 square kilometres (53,819 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 11 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Raleigh and its largest city Charlotte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became the twelfth state after the Revolution and lies on the Atlantic coast midway between New York and Florida, bounded by Virginia, South Carolina, Georgia and Tennessee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gold was found on John Reed's farm along Little Meadow Creek in 1799, which opened the earliest documented gold mining within the present United States and led to the country's first gold rush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Through 1828 all the native gold coined by the United States Mint came from North Carolina, and largely from Cabarrus and the counties around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A strike near Dahlonega in 1829 spread the fever across the southern Appalachians, whose fields produced somewhere between $50 million and $65 million before 1860.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Eclipsed by California and largely worked out by 1860, those fields had still made an industry and trained the miners who went on to open the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Seven black men met at Durham on 20 October 1898 to organise the North Carolina Mutual Life Insurance Company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It grew out of the mutual benefit societies and fraternal orders that had offered life insurance since the 18th century and had become, after the church, the most important social institution in African American life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only two of the seven lasted beyond the first hard year, and most of the credit for organising the firm belongs to John Merrick, a former slave and a prosperous barber in the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1903 the Wright brothers flew the first aeroplane at Kitty Hawk, a flight of 37 metres (120 feet) in 12 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"North Carolina"},"facts":[["Capital","Raleigh"],["Population","11,046,024 (2024)"],["Largest city","Charlotte"],["Total area","139,391 km² (53,819 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “North Carolina: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/north-carolina-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Reed Gold Mine, Cabarrus County, North Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000587_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “North Carolina Mutual Life Insurance Company Building, Durham, North Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/75001258_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bb/Flag_of_North_Carolina.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Carolina.svg)","alt":"A flag with a blue vertical band at the hoist bearing a white star between the letters N and C above two dated scrolls, beside a red bar above a white one."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Cape_Hatteras_Lighthouse_December_2020.jpg/1920px-Cape_Hatteras_Lighthouse_December_2020.jpg","title":"Cape Hatteras, North Carolina","desc":"Cape Hatteras Lighthouse.","credit":"Jschildk, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Hatteras_Lighthouse_December_2020.jpg","alt":"A tall lighthouse painted in a black and white spiral."}},
{"id":"geo-526","num":526,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Santa Fe","answerText":"Santa Fe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">winter 1609 – 10</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Barrio de Analco</span><span class=\"dt-v\">settled 1620</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pueblo Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1680 – 1693</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Santa Fe</b> is the capital of New Mexico and its 4th largest city, with about 91,000 people,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and has been the capital since 1610.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Don Pedro de Peralta, third governor of the province of Nuevo México, established the settlement in the winter of 1609 and 1610 with a palacio for a fortress, a plaza laid out before it and a wall around the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The wall did not survive; the palace did, and houses for the Spanish officers and officials went up on the other three sides of the plaza.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That building, the Palace of the Governors, is one of the oldest public buildings of European origin in America and has been in constant use since the early 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For nearly three hundred years it was the seat of government in what its nomination calls the oldest continuously occupied capital in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the Pueblo Revolt the Spanish were driven out, and for the thirteen years from 1680 to 1693 Pueblo people from Galisteo, Cienega and San Marcos lived in the casa real itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A fragment of adobe wall dug out beneath one room in 1962 is thought to date from that occupation rather than from any earlier pueblo, and storage pits found in the 1970s are believed to be theirs as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Barrio de Analco, settled in 1620 and rebuilt in the 18th century, is one of the oldest neighbourhoods of European inception in what is now the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its name means 'the other side of the water', the far bank of the Santa Fe River from the quarter where officials and prominent citizens lived and had their own chapel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> To the traders who came up the Santa Fe Trail the plaza was the goal of the whole trek, the place where they parked their wagons, unloaded them and laid out their goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Mexico","dot":"Santa Fe"},"facts":[["State","New Mexico"],["Population","90,551 (2024)"],["Largest city","Albuquerque"],["Rank in state","4th largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Palace of the Governors, Santa Fe, New Mexico,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000489_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Santa Fe Plaza, Santa Fe, New Mexico,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000491_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Barrio De Analco Historic District, Santa Fe, New Mexico,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/68000032_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “New Mexico: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-mexico-state-guide. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Flag_of_Santa_Fe%2C_New_Mexico.png","credit":"City of Santa Fe, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico.png)","alt":"The flag of Santa Fe: on gold, a cream disc lettered LA VILLA REAL DE LA SANTA FE DE SAN FRANCISCO DE ASIS in blue around a shield of castle, lion and eagle above stars and stripes, with ribbons reading 1610, 1821 and 1846."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Panoramic_view_of_downtown_Santa_Fe%2C_New_Mexico.jpg/1920px-Panoramic_view_of_downtown_Santa_Fe%2C_New_Mexico.jpg","title":"Santa Fe","desc":"Santa Fe, New Mexico, seen from a distance.","credit":"Road Travel America, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoramic_view_of_downtown_Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Santa Fe, New Mexico."}},
{"id":"geo-030","num":30,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Pennsylvania","answerText":"Pennsylvania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Meadowcroft</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 16,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Colony founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1681</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Valley Forge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1777 – 1778</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drake's well</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 August 1859</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Pennsylvania</b> is the 33rd largest of the American states, covering 119,280 square kilometres (46,054 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 13.1 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Harrisburg and its largest city Philadelphia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the original thirteen colonies, it was established in 1681 by William Penn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philadelphia was both the colony's capital and the first capital of the new nation during the Revolution, but it held neither honour for long: the state moved its business to Lancaster in 1799, and Harrisburg became its enduring capital in 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meadowcroft Rockshelter, on the north bank of Cross Creek about 48 kilometres (30 miles) south-west of Pittsburgh, was used and reused as a short-term hunting and gathering camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Material from its lower levels has been radiocarbon dated to 16,000 BP and pre-Clovis times, and it is those remains and the well-stratified sequence above them that make the shelter exceptional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The bitter winter of 1777 and 1778 at Valley Forge was endured by the ragged and hungry troops who staggered into camp on 19 December 1777.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The training and discipline imposed there made a force that could meet a professional enemy on equal terms, and news of the French alliance was announced on the Grand Parade on 6 May 1778.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Pennsylvania Rock Oil Company sent Edwin L. Drake to look at its property near Titusville in December 1857, and he concluded that only drilling would produce oil in quantity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He hired a driller who had worked on salt wells and raised a derrick housing a steam engine that drove a white oak battering ram into the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Drake struck oil on 27 August 1859, the world's first oil well, and the petroleum boom that followed has only grown since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Pennsylvania"},"facts":[["Capital","Harrisburg"],["Population","13,078,751 (2024)"],["Largest city","Philadelphia"],["Total area","119,280 km² (46,054 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Pennsylvania: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/pennsylvania-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Meadowcroft Rockshelter, Washington County, Pennsylvania,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/78002480_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Valley Forge, Chester and Montgomery Counties, Pennsylvania,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000657_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Drake Oil Well, Titusville, Pennsylvania,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000695_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Flag_of_Pennsylvania.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pennsylvania.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state arms in the middle: a shield with a ship, a plough and three sheaves, held between two rearing black horses under an eagle, above a red scroll."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Independence_Hall.jpg/1920px-Independence_Hall.jpg","title":"Independence Hall, Pennsylvania","desc":"Independence Hall.","credit":"Rdsmith4, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Independence_Hall.jpg","alt":"A view of Independence Hall."}},
{"id":"geo-527","num":527,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Atlanta","answerText":"Atlanta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Appropriation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">$1 million, 1883</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">finished 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Herndon Home</span><span class=\"dt-v\">planned 1906</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fox Theatre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1929</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Atlanta</b> is the capital of Georgia and its largest city, with about 520,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state has had five authorised capitals and four capitol buildings, three of them built by the state itself: one at Louisville in the 18th century, one at Milledgeville in 1807 and the present one here, finished in 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city gave the state the five-acre City Hall Lot in the middle of town to build it on, and in 1883 the legislature appropriated a million dollars for the work under a board of five commissioners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Wesley Dobbs coined the name Sweet Auburn for Auburn Avenue, which was called 'the richest Negro street in the world'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its success was bound up with the residential pattern forced on black Atlantans by the rise of Jim Crow, so that the district stands both as a result of segregation and as a case for the merits of black enterprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Alonzo F. Herndon was the wealthiest black man in the city: he ran its largest barber shop, was president of an insurance company, and owned and let some fifty houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From about 1906 his first wife, Adrienne Herndon, planned the family a house beside Atlanta University, overlooking the city from the west, with a terraced roof garden and rooms meant for entertaining.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> W. E. B. Du Bois eulogised him in <i>The Crisis</i> in 1927 as a man whose money had bought him power and whose friendships had kept it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Fox Theatre, a movie palace the size of a city block, opened on Christmas Day 1929 at the height of that building type's golden age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It had been planned as a mosque for some 5,000 members of a shriners' order, and was enlarged at the suggestion of the film magnate William Fox into an auditorium ringed with shops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Georgia","dot":"Atlanta"},"facts":[["State","Georgia"],["Population","520,070 (2024)"],["Largest city","Atlanta"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Georgia State Capitol, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71001099_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sweet Auburn Historic District, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76000631_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Herndon Home, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/00000261_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fox Theatre, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74002230_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/90/Flag_of_Atlanta%2C_Georgia.svg","credit":"Devinkelly, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Atlanta,_Georgia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Atlanta: on dark blue, a gold circular seal showing a phoenix rising from flames with wings spread, RESURGENS above and ATLANTA, GA. below, and the dates 1847 and 1865 at either side."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Aerial_of_Downtown_Atlanta%2C_GA.jpg/1920px-Aerial_of_Downtown_Atlanta%2C_GA.jpg","title":"Atlanta","desc":"Atlanta, seen from a distance.","credit":"Ron Reiring, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_of_Downtown_Atlanta,_GA.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Atlanta."}},
{"id":"geo-031","num":31,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Wisconsin","answerText":"Wisconsin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Effigy mounds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 1000 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cornish miners</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 May 1848</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wisconsin</b> is the 23rd largest of the American states, covering 169,635 square kilometres (65,496 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.0 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Madison and its largest city Milwaukee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nearly a fifth of that area is water, and most of it is not the state's own: 24,195 square kilometres (9,342 square miles) is its share of the Great Lakes, against 5,172 square kilometres (1,997 square miles) of inland lakes and rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the Union on 29 May 1848 and is nicknamed the Badger State and America's Dairyland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between about 700 and 1000 CE people of the Effigy Mound Culture raised earth mounds across the region in the shapes of animals and in linear and conical forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Man Mound near Baraboo is a rare human figure among them, and the only monumental representation of the human form surviving from prehistoric times in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When Congress created Wisconsin Territory in 1836 the old lead region of the south-west was its most populous corner, and Governor Henry Dodge chose Belmont as the territorial capital on that ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The legislature sat there for one session of 46 days, from 25 October to 9 December 1836, and used it to pick Madison instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Unemployed tin miners had begun coming to Mineral Point from Cornwall in 1830, and they built stone cottages along Shake Rag Street below the ridge where they dug their lead pits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Dairying was already becoming the leading agricultural pursuit in the state when William Henry took the university's chair of agriculture in 1880 and began testing the butterfat content of Jersey and Holstein milk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Stephen Babcock's butterfat test of 1890 revolutionised the dairy industry worldwide, and the cold-curing process worked out beside it made Wisconsin a leading producer of high-quality cheddar cheese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Wisconsin"},"facts":[["Capital","Madison"],["Population","5,960,975 (2024)"],["Largest city","Milwaukee"],["Total area","169,635 km² (65,496 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Wisconsin: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/wisconsin-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Man Mound, Sauk County, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78000138_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “First Capitol, Belmont, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000036_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pendarvis, Mineral Point, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/71000038_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “University of Wisconsin Dairy Barn, Madison, Wisconsin,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/02000600_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Flag_of_Wisconsin.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wisconsin.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag with the state name above and the year 1848 below, and between them the state arms: a sailor and a miner flanking a quartered gold shield, beneath a badger and a scroll reading Forward."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Apostle_Island_Sea_Cave_in_Winter.jpg/1920px-Apostle_Island_Sea_Cave_in_Winter.jpg","title":"the Apostle Islands, Wisconsin","desc":"Apostle Islands.","credit":"Jeff the quiet, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apostle_Island_Sea_Cave_in_Winter.jpg","alt":"A view of Apostle Islands."}},
{"id":"geo-528","num":528,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Columbia","answerText":"Columbia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at Columbia, 1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">College</span><span class=\"dt-v\">chartered 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Secession vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 December 1860</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">February 1865</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Columbia</b> is the capital of South Carolina and its second largest city, with 144,788 residents against Charleston's 157,665.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The colony had built its first State House at Charleston in 1753; a second went up here in 1790, and the legislature held its first meeting in the new town that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> South Carolina College was chartered in 1801, an antebellum college of the classics on a campus where enslaved people worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Robert Mills, a native South Carolinian who studied under Hoban and Latrobe and became the first American-trained federal architect, designed a classical brick mansion here for the merchant Ainsley Hall, built in 1823 at the centre of a four-acre block.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hall died before it was finished and nobody ever lived in it, so the house is remembered for its architect, the designer of the Washington Monument, who served under seven presidents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On Monday 17 December 1860 the South Carolina Convention of the People assembled in the First Baptist Church and declared unanimously that the state should leave the Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Smallpox was raging in the city, so the delegates voted to reassemble at Charleston, where the Ordinance of Secession was adopted and signed three days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In February 1865 Sherman's army occupied Columbia, seized the contents of the plant that had been printing Confederate currency, and burned the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sherman ordered a number of public buildings put to the torch, the old State House among them, but for reasons nobody has explained he spared the unfinished new capitol that John Niernsee had been raising since 1851 out of granite from the Congaree River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The college closed during the war and reopened under Reconstruction as the University of South Carolina, a model for multiracial education, before being segregated again until 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"South Carolina","dot":"Columbia"},"facts":[["State","South Carolina"],["Population","144,788 (2024)"],["Largest city","Charleston"],["Rank in state","2nd largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","South Carolina General Assembly, “State House History,” South Carolina Student Connection, https://www.studentconnection.scstatehouse.gov/history. [Open access]","University of South Carolina, “Our History,” https://www.sc.edu/about/our_history/index.php. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ainsley Hall House, Columbia, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000595_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “First Baptist Church, Columbia, South Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000800_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Confederate Printing Plant, Columbia, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79002393_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “South Carolina Statehouse, Columbia, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000598_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Flag_of_Columbia%2C_South_Carolina.svg","credit":"Nate Puza, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Columbia,_South_Carolina.svg)","alt":"The flag of Columbia: on dark navy, a sweeping band of royal blue, pale blue and white curving from the lower hoist to the upper fly, with a white seven-pointed star in the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Fall_skyline_of_Columbia_SC_from_Arsenal_Hill.jpg","title":"Columbia","desc":"Columbia, South Carolina, seen from a distance.","credit":"Akhenaton06, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fall_skyline_of_Columbia_SC_from_Arsenal_Hill.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Columbia, South Carolina."}},
{"id":"geo-032","num":32,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Indiana","answerText":"Indiana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grouseland</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1804 – 1812</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tippecanoe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1811</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 December 1816</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Railway Union</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1893 – 1897</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Indiana</b> is the 38th largest of the American states, covering 94,326 square kilometres (36,420 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.9 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Indianapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Hoosier State, it became the 19th state on 11 December 1816, with its first capital at Corydon and the seat of government moved to Indianapolis in 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> William Henry Harrison lived at Grouseland, his mansion at Vincennes, from about 1804 until 1812, the years in which he was territorial governor of Indiana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tecumseh, a Shawnee who had lived among white settlers and saw what further land sales would cost, came there to press a confederation of nations bound to sell no more, and warned that he would meet settlers with force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From Grouseland in 1811 Harrison mounted the offensive that ended at Tippecanoe, where Tecumseh's brother the Prophet was defeated and the hope of a confederation with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Eugene Debs founded the American Railway Union at Terre Haute in June 1893, wanting one organisation for every railway worker rather than one for the firemen and another for the engineers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It struck the Great Northern over a wage cut in April 1894, James J. Hill gave in after eighteen days, and by that June the union counted 150,000 members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Pullman strike followed and collapsed against the employers, a federal injunction and the arrival of soldiers, and Debs was jailed for six months for defying the injunctions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> More than 100,000 people met him at Chicago in November 1895, but the union never recovered and disbanded in June 1897, half a year after he declared for socialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sarah Breedlove, a laundress in St Louis, went into business in 1905 with $1.50, married Charles J. Walker the next year, and by 1910 had moved her whole operation to Indianapolis, where the Walker Building housed a company employing some 3,000 women along with a theatre and a ballroom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Indiana"},"facts":[["Capital","Indianapolis"],["Population","6,924,275 (2024)"],["Largest city","Indianapolis"],["Total area","94,326 km² (36,420 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Indiana: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/indiana-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “William Henry Harrison Home (Grouseland), Vincennes, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000018_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Eugene V. Debs House, Terre Haute, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000008_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Madame C. J. Walker Building, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/80000062_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Flag_of_Indiana.svg","credit":"Paul Hadley, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Indiana.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a gold torch with rays streaming from it, ringed by thirteen gold stars with five more inside them and one larger star above the flame under the word INDIANA."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Indiana_Dunes_National_Lakeshore%2C_Michigan_City%2C_Indiana%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2012-10-20%2C_DD_03.jpg/1920px-Indiana_Dunes_National_Lakeshore%2C_Michigan_City%2C_Indiana%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2012-10-20%2C_DD_03.jpg","title":"the Indiana Dunes","desc":"Indiana Dunes National Park.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indiana_Dunes_National_Lakeshore,_Michigan_City,_Indiana,_Estados_Unidos,_2012-10-20,_DD_03.jpg","alt":"A view of Indiana Dunes National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-529","num":529,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Raleigh","answerText":"Raleigh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Site chosen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State house</span><span class=\"dt-v\">burned June 1831</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1833 – 1840</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Daniels</span><span class=\"dt-v\">editor, 1894 – 1948</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Raleigh</b> is the capital of North Carolina and its second largest city, with 499,825 residents against Charlotte's 943,476.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constitutional convention of 1787 resolved that the capital must lie within 16 kilometres (10 miles) of Isaac Hunter's tavern in Wake County, and nothing came of it for four years while opponents called the county a wilderness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In early 1792 the commissioners met at Hunter's, adjourned to the house of Senator Joel Lane, and ten days later voted to buy 400 hectares (1,000 acres) of his plantation for £1,378.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The surveyor William Christmas centred his plan on Union Square, kept for the State House, with four public parks at its corners, and the rest sold off lot by lot at auction to pay for the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Governor Alexander Martin proposed the name, for Sir Walter Raleigh, who had sent the first English colonists to North Carolina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The state house burned in June 1831, and the next year the General Assembly appropriated $50,000 for a new Capitol of stone from a quarry in the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ithiel Town and Alexander Jackson Davis were engaged as architects, and Governor David Lowry Swain laid the cornerstone on 4 July 1833.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> David Paton, a young architect from Edinburgh, took charge of the work, writing to William Strickland of Philadelphia about the design as it rose, and the building was finished in the spring of 1840.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Christ Church, begun in 1846 to Richard Upjohn's design, was among the first Gothic Revival churches in the southern states and set the style there for a long time after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Josephus Daniels edited the Raleigh <i>News and Observer</i> from 1894 until his death in 1948, a leading voice of reform by his biographer's account and one of the chief spokesmen for Black disfranchisement in the white supremacy campaign of 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"North Carolina","dot":"Raleigh"},"facts":[["State","North Carolina"],["Population","499,825 (2024)"],["Largest city","Charlotte"],["Rank in state","2nd largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Kelly Agan, Christy E. Allen and Rebecca Hyman, “Raleigh: The History of North Carolina's Capital, Past and Present,” NCpedia, Government &amp; Heritage Library, State Library of North Carolina, revised January 2025, https://www.ncpedia.org/geography/raleigh. [Open access]","National Park Service, “North Carolina State Capitol, Raleigh, North Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000476_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Christ Episcopal Church, Raleigh, North Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/87002597_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Josephus Daniels House (Wakestone), Raleigh, North Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76001342_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/25/Flag_of_Raleigh%2C_North_Carolina.svg","credit":"Svgalbertian Original version by Miss Kate Denson, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Raleigh,_North_Carolina.svg)","alt":"The flag of Raleigh: three vertical stripes of red, white and red, the white bearing the city seal, a green oak tree ringed by CITY OF RALEIGH and NORTH CAROLINA inside a green oak wreath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Raleigh_downtown_skyline_-_panoramio_crop.png/1920px-Raleigh_downtown_skyline_-_panoramio_crop.png","title":"Raleigh","desc":"Raleigh, North Carolina, seen from a distance.","credit":"TheTexasNationalist99, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raleigh_downtown_skyline_-_panoramio_crop.png","alt":"A wide view of Raleigh, North Carolina."}},
{"id":"geo-033","num":33,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Kentucky","answerText":"Kentucky","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 June 1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Big Bone Lick</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dug 1803 – 1807</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ovariotomy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1809</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nursing Service</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1925</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Kentucky</b> is the 37th largest of the American states, covering 104,656 square kilometres (40,408 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 4.6 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Frankfort and its largest city Louisville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was admitted on 1 June 1792 as the 15th state and the first west of the Appalachian Mountains, and its government was organised three days later in a Lexington tavern under Isaac Shelby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A committee chose Frankfort as the permanent seat, and the General Assembly met there for the first time on 1 November 1793.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Big Bone Lick, a salt source among sulfur springs south-west of Cincinnati, drew mammals including mammoths and mastodons, which became trapped in its soft marshy bog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thomas Jefferson, who had read of the fossils, sent Meriwether Lewis there in October 1803, and when those specimens went down with a boat near Natchez he sent William Clark back in 1807 for a three-week dig that yielded nearly 300 bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Enslaved African Americans worked from the 18th century in the depths of Mammoth Cave making saltpetre, the main ingredient of the black gunpowder that mattered so much in the War of 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After that war the cave became a tourist destination, and the guides who found and opened its routes from 1838 were enslaved young men: Stephen Bishop, Mat and Nick Bransford and Alfred Croghan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Danville on Christmas Day 1809, well before practical anaesthesia, Ephraim McDowell performed the first successful ovariotomy on Jane Todd Crawford, who had ridden some 97 kilometres (60 miles) to reach him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Mary Breckinridge, having watched nurse-midwives at work in France, Britain and a remote corner of Scotland, founded the Frontier Nursing Service at Wendover in 1925, the first attempt to professionalise midwifery in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its outposts each covered about 180 square kilometres (70 square miles) under a registered nurse-midwife, in a county where 43 midwives attended 265 births against one physician's 40.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Kentucky"},"facts":[["Capital","Frankfort"],["Population","4,588,372 (2024)"],["Largest city","Louisville"],["Total area","104,656 km² (40,408 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Kentucky: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/kentucky-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Big Bone Lick,” Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/big-bone-lick.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “African American History,” Mammoth Cave National Park, https://www.nps.gov/maca/learn/historyculture/african-american-history.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Dr. Ephraim McDowell House, Danville, Kentucky,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000355_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wendover, Leslie County, Kentucky,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/75000792_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Flag_of_Kentucky.svg","credit":"Commonwealth of Kentucky, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kentucky.svg)","alt":"A navy flag bearing the state seal: two men, one in buckskin and one in a frock coat, clasping hands within a ring reading UNITED WE STAND, DIVIDED WE FALL, below the words COMMONWEALTH OF KENTUCKY and above a spray of goldenrod."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Historic_Entrance_%28Mammoth_Cave%2C_Kentucky%2C_USA%29_2_%2837773583192%29.jpg/1920px-Historic_Entrance_%28Mammoth_Cave%2C_Kentucky%2C_USA%29_2_%2837773583192%29.jpg","title":"Mammoth Cave, Kentucky","desc":"The historic entrance to Mammoth Cave.","credit":"James St. John, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historic_Entrance_(Mammoth_Cave,_Kentucky,_USA)_2_(37773583192).jpg","alt":"A dark cave mouth in a mossy limestone bluff."}},
{"id":"geo-530","num":530,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Harrisburg","answerText":"Harrisburg","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1812</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1816 – 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 February 1897</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Present capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dedicated 1906</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Harrisburg</b> is the capital of Pennsylvania and only its ninth largest city, with 50,649 residents against Philadelphia's 1,573,916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philadelphia was the colony's capital and the first capital of the new nation, the state moved its business to Lancaster in 1799, and Harrisburg became its enduring capital in 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An Act approved in February 1810 accepted four hectares (ten acres) from William Maclay beside a tract John Harris had already given the state, and ordered two fireproof office buildings for the Land Office, the Treasury, the Surveyor-General and the Auditor-General.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The commissioners invited architects to submit plans and elevations, offering $400 for the design adopted and $200 for the next best, and retained Stephen Hills as architect and builder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hills, born at Ashford in Kent in 1771 and apprenticed seven years to a house-wright there, had reached Boston by 1797 and Harrisburg before 1810, and his two office buildings were ready for the government's arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The main building, begun about 1816 and finished in 1822 at a cost of $148,000 without furnishings, had its cornerstone laid in 1819 by Governor Findlay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It burned on Tuesday 2 February 1897, after 75 years in which its halls had heard Thaddeus Stevens, Simon and Donald Cameron, Matthew Quay and Andrew Curtin, and had received Lafayette in 1825, Charles Dickens in 1843 and the Prince of Wales in 1860.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The present Capitol was begun in 1898 and dedicated on 4 October 1906.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Middletown, 16 kilometres (10 miles) to the south-east,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> the Three Mile Island Unit 2 reactor partially melted down on 28 March 1979, the most serious accident in the history of American commercial nuclear power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A relief valve stuck open while the control room's instruments showed it shut, so the staff did not realise coolant was pouring out; the releases were small and the commission records no detectable health effects on workers or the public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Pennsylvania","dot":"Harrisburg"},"facts":[["State","Pennsylvania"],["Population","50,649 (2024)"],["Largest city","Philadelphia"],["Rank in state","9th largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Pennsylvania: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/pennsylvania-state-guide. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “The First Capitol Buildings, Harrisburg, Pennsylvania,” HABS No. PA-394, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/pa/pa0300/pa0394/data/pa0394data.pdf. [Open access]","U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission, “Christopher M. Crane Clean Energy Center,” Operating Nuclear Power Reactors, https://www.nrc.gov/info-finder/reactors/tmi1.html. [Open access]","U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission, “Backgrounder on the Three Mile Island Accident,” NRC Library Fact Sheets, https://www.nrc.gov/reading-rm/doc-collections/fact-sheets/3mile-isle.html. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Flag_of_Harrisburg%2C_Pennsylvania.svg","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Harrisburg,_Pennsylvania.svg)","alt":"The flag of Harrisburg: a gold border round a blue field bearing a red-edged white keystone with the Capitol dome in blue, a sun and a fleur-de-lis, and HARRISBURG, PA curved in gold beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Harrisburg%2C_PA_Skyline_2021.jpg/1920px-Harrisburg%2C_PA_Skyline_2021.jpg","title":"Harrisburg","desc":"Harrisburg, Pennsylvania, seen from a distance.","credit":"Jeffrey Hayes, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harrisburg,_PA_Skyline_2021.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Harrisburg, Pennsylvania."}},
{"id":"geo-034","num":34,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tennessee","answerText":"Tennessee","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1796</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jubilee Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1873 – 1876</span><span class=\"dt-k\">X-10 reactor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 November 1943</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sun studio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened January 1950</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tennessee</b> is the 36th largest of the American states, covering 109,153 square kilometres (42,144 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 7.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Nashville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined as the 16th state in 1796, is home to the most visited national park in the country, the Great Smoky Mountains, and has in Nashville what is called the country music capital of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> William Blount governed the Southwest Territory from the mansion he built at Knoxville in 1792.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He presided over the convention that met in January 1796 and proclaimed the territory a state, and the first legislature sent him to the United States Senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fisk University was one of many schools the American Missionary Association and the Western Freedmen's Aid Commission founded for the freedmen, and took its name from the general then running the Freedmen's Bureau in Tennessee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> To raise money its officials formed a choir to tour the North singing spirituals; the first trip was no financial success, but it gave the group a name, the Jubilee Singers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They went north again in 1872, sang at the World Peace Jubilee in Boston and toured for years at home and abroad, and the money saved the university and raised Jubilee Hall between 1873 and 1876.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Land near Clinton was bought late in 1942 under the pretence of a demolition range, and the X-10 pilot reactor went into operation at Oak Ridge on 4 November 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It was the world's first full-scale nuclear reactor, the first to produce significant heat and measurable plutonium, and in 1946 the first to make radioactive isotopes for medical therapy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Memphis, in a studio opened in January 1950, Sam Phillips recorded B.B. King, Howlin' Wolf, Ike Turner, Elvis Presley, Johnny Cash, Jerry Lee Lewis, Carl Perkins and Roy Orbison inside eight years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Tennessee"},"facts":[["Capital","Nashville"],["Population","7,227,750 (2024)"],["Largest city","Nashville"],["Total area","109,153 km² (42,144 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Tennessee: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/tennessee-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Blount Mansion, Knoxville, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000726_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Jubilee Hall, Fisk University, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000817_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “X-10 Reactor, Oak Ridge National Laboratory, Oak Ridge, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000720_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sun Record Company, Memphis Recording Service, Memphis, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/03001031_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Flag_of_Tennessee.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tennessee.svg)","alt":"A red flag with a narrow blue stripe down the fly edge and, in the middle, three white stars set in a ring inside a blue disc bordered in white."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/View_atop_Cliff_Tops_on_Mount_LeConte%2C_GSMNP%2C_TN.jpg/1920px-View_atop_Cliff_Tops_on_Mount_LeConte%2C_GSMNP%2C_TN.jpg","title":"the Great Smoky Mountains, Tennessee","desc":"Great Smoky Mountains National Park.","credit":"AppalachianCentrist, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_atop_Cliff_Tops_on_Mount_LeConte,_GSMNP,_TN.jpg","alt":"A view of Great Smoky Mountains National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-531","num":531,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Madison","answerText":"Madison","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chosen capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">North Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1851</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Armory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened May 1894</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jacobs House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Madison</b> is the capital of Wisconsin and its second largest city, with 285,300 residents against Milwaukee's 563,531.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territorial legislature sat at Belmont for a single session of 46 days in the autumn of 1836, and used it to pick Madison as the capital over some twenty other places that wanted the honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Building began the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The university opened its first building, North Hall, on 17 September 1851, and began extension work in 1885 with farmers' institutes, short courses in farming and summer schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first decade of the 20th century it established the ideal of a state university's service to its community so effectively that the arrangement was imitated nationally and abroad under the name of the Wisconsin Idea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The drill hall of the Armory, opened in May 1894, was for years the largest room in the city and the place its political meetings were held; William McKinley spoke at a rally there that October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Republican state conventions of 1902 and 1904 renominated Robert La Follette for governor in it, and the second of them is of national importance in the history of the Progressive movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> La Follette opened that campaign with a new tactic, the roll call: he went into each Stalwart legislator's district, spoke on the questions the legislature had lately voted on, and then read out how the local member had voted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 17 May the state central committee met in the Armory, awarded a majority of the disputed seats to the Progressives by unanimous vote, and the Stalwarts walked out to the Fuller Opera House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herbert Jacobs and his wife came to Taliesin in the autumn of 1936 with what they called a challenge, and the house Frank Lloyd Wright built them here was the first Usonian house in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Wisconsin","dot":"Madison"},"facts":[["State","Wisconsin"],["Population","285,300 (2024)"],["Largest city","Milwaukee"],["Rank in state","2nd largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “First Capitol, Belmont, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000036_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “North Hall, University of Wisconsin, Madison, Wisconsin,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000021_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “University of Wisconsin Armory and Gymnasium, Madison, Wisconsin,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/93001618_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Herbert A. Jacobs House, Madison, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74000073_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Flag_of_Madison%2C_Wisconsin_%282018%E2%80%93present%29.svg","credit":"Original: Alan De Smet at English Wikipedia Updated edit: User:HarJIT, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Madison,_Wisconsin_%282018–present%29.svg)","alt":"The flag of Madison: a broad white diagonal band across a light blue field, carrying a black cross-shaped device around a gold disc."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Downtown_Madison_Skyline_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Madison_Skyline_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Madison","desc":"Madison, Wisconsin, seen from a distance.","credit":"Corey Coyle, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Madison_Skyline_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Madison, Wisconsin."}},
{"id":"geo-035","num":35,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Missouri","answerText":"Missouri","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1812</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 August 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pony Express</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 3 April 1860</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shelley case</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 May 1948</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Missouri</b> is the 21st largest of the American states, covering 180,540 square kilometres (69,707 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Jefferson City and its largest city Kansas City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was organised as a territory in 1812 and, after Louisiana, was the second state of the Louisiana Purchase to be admitted, on 10 August 1821.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ste. Genevieve, the old French town on the Mississippi, keeps the house of Louis Bolduc, a lead miner, merchant and planter of its early years, and an academy of 1807 that served as a school until the Civil War closed it for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At St. Joseph the Patee House was the headquarters of Russell, Majors and Waddell, who owned the Pony Express, and the cannon that opened the service was fired in front of it on 3 April 1860.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Eads Bridge, three ribbed steel arches on granite-faced limestone piers, carried a lower and an upper deck across the Mississippi at St. Louis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> It was the first bridge James Eads designed and the only one he built, and steel was used on it for the first time as the main metal of a major structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A two-family flat of 1906 in the same city gave its name to Shelley v. Kraemer, a case about whether private agreements restricting property to white owners could be enforced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The state supreme court had ordered the covenant enforced in December 1946, the state arguing that because it stood ready to enforce such covenants against white owners it could enforce them against Black ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 3 May 1948 the Supreme Court held, six to nothing with three justices not sitting, that no racial covenant of any kind could be enforced by a state court, since to do so would be state action forbidden by the Fourteenth Amendment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The decision reinstated the force of that amendment and left the doctrine of separate but equal open to challenge on every front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Missouri"},"facts":[["Capital","Jefferson City"],["Population","6,245,466 (2024)"],["Largest city","Kansas City"],["Total area","180,540 km² (69,707 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Missouri: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/missouri-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ste. Genevieve Historic District, Ste. Genevieve, Missouri,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000892_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Patee House, St. Joseph, Missouri,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000414_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Eads Bridge, St. Louis, Missouri,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000946_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Shelley House, St. Louis, Missouri,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/88000437_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Flag_of_Missouri.svg","credit":"Marie Watkins Oliver, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Missouri.svg)","alt":"A flag of three horizontal bands, red above white above blue, with the state seal in the middle inside a blue ring of white stars, two bears holding a shield under the motto SALUS POPULI SUPREMA LEX ESTO."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/St_Louis_night_expblend_cropped.jpg","title":"the Gateway Arch, Missouri","desc":"Gateway Arch.","credit":"St_Louis_night_expblend.jpg : Daniel Schwen derivative work: ← fetch comms, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Louis_night_expblend_cropped.jpg","alt":"A view of Gateway Arch."}},
{"id":"geo-532","num":532,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Indianapolis","answerText":"Indianapolis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nomination</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Harrison, 1888</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenaeum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1893 – 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fieldhouse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927 – 1928</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Indianapolis</b> is the capital of Indiana and much the largest city in it, with 891,484 residents against Fort Wayne's 273,203.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state's first capital was Corydon, and the seat of government was moved here in 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Athenaeum, built as Das Deutsche Haus for the Socialer Turnverein in two campaigns, a German Romanesque wing in 1893 and 1894 and a Renaissance block in 1897 and 1898, is a fine example of Germanic architectural influence in the Midwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the house at 1204 North Delaware Street, Benjamin Harrison accepted the Republican nomination for the presidency in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The front porch was not there at the time: he added it himself after coming home from Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Joseph Jarrett Cole began at 19 as a salesman for a carriage company here in 1888, opened a carriage works of his own in 1904, and turned it over to making luxury motor cars in 1907.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first Cole car reached the market in 1909; he pioneered eight-cylinder engines and aluminium gears and wheels, and assembled his cars from parts bought in from other makers rather than building the whole thing himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Butler Fieldhouse, put up in 1927 and 1928, is the earliest of the great college fieldhouses, which with rule changes that quickened the game transformed college basketball over the following decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was the largest indoor sports place in the state until the late 1960s, and the finals of a high school tournament famous enough to be called Hoosier Hysteria are played in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> James Whitcomb Riley, the Hoosier Poet, spent the last 23 years of his life as a paying guest at 528 Lockerbie Street and died in the house on 22 July 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Indiana","dot":"Indianapolis"},"facts":[["State","Indiana"],["Population","891,484 (2024)"],["Largest city","Indianapolis"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Indiana: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/indiana-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Athenaeum (Das Deutsche Haus), Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000032_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Benjamin Harrison House, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000010_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Cole Motor Car Company, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/83000128_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Butler Fieldhouse, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/83003573_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “James Whitcomb Riley House, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000799_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Flag_of_Indianapolis.svg","credit":"Roger E. Gohl, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Indianapolis.svg)","alt":"The flag of Indianapolis: a navy field quartered by a white cross, with a red disc at the crossing bearing a white five-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Panorama_of_downtown_Indianapolis_skyline%2C_looking_northeast.jpg/1920px-Panorama_of_downtown_Indianapolis_skyline%2C_looking_northeast.jpg","title":"Indianapolis","desc":"Indianapolis, seen from a distance.","credit":"Momoneymoproblemz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_of_downtown_Indianapolis_skyline,_looking_northeast.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Indianapolis."}},
{"id":"geo-036","num":36,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Arkansas","answerText":"Arkansas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Arkansas Post</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1686</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Survey base</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 November 1815</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 June 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Central High</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1957</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Arkansas</b> is the 29th largest of the American states, covering 137,732 square kilometres (53,179 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 3.1 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Little Rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became the 25th state on 15 June 1836, long after the peoples who had built a thriving culture along the Mississippi by about 500 CE and the Spanish and French who came exploring in the 16th and 17th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The migration routes to the West Coast went round it, so for the first 150 years of its life as territory and state it stayed rural, thinly settled and without a large city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arkansas Post, the trading post Henri de Tonti founded in 1686, was the first European foothold in the lower Mississippi valley, and Montgomery's Tavern there held the territory's first General Assembly in February 1820.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In a swamp in the east, where three counties meet, a stone marks the base established on 10 November 1815 from which United States engineers surveyed the lands of the Louisiana Purchase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The first survey run from that point was made to satisfy the land bounties promised to the soldiers of the War of 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Rohwer the people held in a Japanese American relocation camp designed and built the funerary monuments of its cemetery, the largest and most elaborate of the three such cemeteries that survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The house of Daisy and L. C. Bates was the command post of the Little Rock school crisis, a haven for the nine Black students and the place they planned from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 24 September 1957 President Eisenhower signed an executive order, and nine hours and 49 minutes later a thousand soldiers of the 101st Airborne deployed in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> They collected the nine teenagers at the Bates house, and Central High School desegregated while the nation and much of the world watched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Arkansas"},"facts":[["Capital","Little Rock"],["Population","3,088,354 (2024)"],["Largest city","Little Rock"],["Total area","137,732 km² (53,179 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Arkansas: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/arkansas-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Arkansas Post National Memorial, Arkansas County, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000198_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Louisiana Purchase Survey Marker, Blackton, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72000206_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Rohwer Relocation Center Memorial Cemetery, Desha County, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/92001882_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Daisy Bates House, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/01000072_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Little Rock Central High School, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/77000268_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Flag_of_Arkansas.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Arkansas.svg)","alt":"A red flag bearing a large white diamond edged in blue, the state name across it in blue with one star above and three below, and 25 white stars set in the blue border."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Fordyce_Bathhouse_Hot_Springs_NP_2023.jpg/1920px-Fordyce_Bathhouse_Hot_Springs_NP_2023.jpg","title":"Bathhouse Row, Hot Springs, Arkansas","desc":"The Fordyce Bathhouse on Bathhouse Row.","credit":"Larry D. Moore, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fordyce_Bathhouse_Hot_Springs_NP_2023.jpg","alt":"The front of a large early twentieth-century bathhouse."}},
{"id":"geo-533","num":533,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Frankfort","answerText":"Frankfort","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly met</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1793</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liberty Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1796 – 1800</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Old State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1827 – 1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dedicated 1910</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Frankfort</b> is the capital of Kentucky and only its fourteenth largest city, with 28,610 residents against Louisville's 640,796.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A committee chose it as the permanent seat of government, and the General Assembly met there for the first time on 1 November 1793.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Brown built Liberty Hall in the new capital between 1796 and 1800, on the pattern of the finest Federal architecture he had seen in the East, and above all in Philadelphia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its achievement is not that it was built on the frontier but that, remote as that frontier was, it is one of the most perfect examples of Federal domestic building ever put up in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More than two years after the second state house burned, the legislature approved a rebuilding in 1827, and the commissioners took the plans of Gideon Shryock, who was 25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What he raised between 1827 and 1830 caught the essence of the American contribution to the Greek Revival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A special session of the General Assembly in February 1905 chose a 14-hectare (34-acre) site in the south of the town and voted $40,000 to buy it, and ground was broken that May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The new Capitol was dedicated on 1 June 1910, having cost $1,820,000 with the land, the building and the furnishings together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Greenhill Cemetery a limestone obelisk was raised in July 1924 by the Women's Relief Corps of the Grand Army of the Republic, inscribed to the Black soldiers of Franklin County who fought in the Civil War, with the 140 names of the men on its other three faces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is the only monument in the state to any of the nearly 30,000 Kentucky African Americans who served in the Union army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Kentucky","dot":"Frankfort"},"facts":[["State","Kentucky"],["Population","28,610 (2024)"],["Largest city","Louisville"],["Rank in state","14th largest"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Kentucky: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/kentucky-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Liberty Hall, Frankfort, Kentucky,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000344_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Statehouse, Frankfort, Kentucky,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000346_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Kentucky State Capitol, Frankfort, Kentucky,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000804_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Colored Soldiers Monument in Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/97000701_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Flag_of_Frankfort%2C_Kentucky.svg","credit":"City of Frankfort, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Frankfort,_Kentucky.svg)","alt":"The flag of Frankfort: on dark blue, a pale blue river-like band edged in white curving across the lower hoist, and a large gold five-pointed star at the fly with a blue star at its heart."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Downtown_-_Frankfort%2C_Kentucky_-_DSC09295.JPG/1920px-Downtown_-_Frankfort%2C_Kentucky_-_DSC09295.JPG","title":"Frankfort","desc":"Downtown Frankfort, Kentucky.","credit":"Daderot, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_-_Frankfort,_Kentucky_-_DSC09295.JPG","alt":"A street of nineteenth-century brick storefronts."}},
{"id":"geo-037","num":37,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Alabama","answerText":"Alabama","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1819</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sloss furnaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">blown 12 April 1882</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Redstone stand</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Church bombing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 September 1963</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Alabama"},"facts":[["Capital","Montgomery"],["Population","5,157,699 (2024)"],["Largest city","Huntsville"],["Total area","135,767 km² (52,420 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Alabama</b> is the 30th largest of the American states, covering 135,767 square kilometres (52,420 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Montgomery and its largest city Huntsville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Set off as a territory in 1817, it entered the Union on 14 December 1819 as the 22nd state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its government sat at St. Stephens, then Huntsville, Cahaba and Tuscaloosa, before the legislature settled on Montgomery in 1846.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Delegates from six seceded states met in the new capitol in February 1861, adopted a provisional constitution for the Confederate States of America, and sat there until the capital moved to Richmond in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Booker T. Washington was chosen to run a normal school the legislature had chartered at Tuskegee, and at his death in 1915 it taught 38 trades and professions to 1,537 students.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> James Withers Sloss left his partners in 1881 to build his own blast furnaces at Birmingham, and the first was blown on 12 April 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> They made nothing but pig iron until they were last tapped in 1970, and by 1898 Birmingham was said to be the third largest point of its export in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It never caught Pittsburgh at steel, its ores carrying too much phosphorus for the ordinary Bessemer furnace and too little for the basic one, and the loading, tapping and casting were done largely by Black workers, almost 40 per cent of all southern iron and steel workers by 1907.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Huntsville a stand built from salvaged steel in 1953 was the first in the country able to hold a whole launch vehicle rather than an engine, and rockets proved on it put the first American satellite in orbit on 31 January 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Some 50 unsolved dynamite bombings between 1947 and 1965 earned the city the name Bombingham, and the blast that killed four schoolgirls at the Sixteenth Street Baptist Church on 15 September 1963 did much to carry the Civil Rights Act of 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Alabama: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/alabama-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Alabama State Capitol, Montgomery, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000152_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tuskegee Institute, Tuskegee, Alabama,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000151_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sloss Blast Furnaces, Birmingham, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72000162_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Redstone Test Stand, Huntsville, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76000341_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sixteenth Street Baptist Church, Birmingham, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/80000696_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Flag_of_Alabama.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Alabama.svg)","alt":"A white flag bearing a broad crimson saltire, a diagonal cross running from corner to corner."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Edmund_Pettus_Bridge_03.jpg/1920px-Edmund_Pettus_Bridge_03.jpg","title":"the Edmund Pettus Bridge, Alabama","desc":"Edmund Pettus Bridge.","credit":"Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edmund_Pettus_Bridge_03.jpg","alt":"A view of Edmund Pettus Bridge."}},
{"id":"geo-534","num":534,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Nashville","answerText":"Nashville","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Nashborough</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1780</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1845 – 1859</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parthenon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1897</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grand Ole Opry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1943 – 1974</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Tennessee","dot":"Nashville"},"facts":[["State","Tennessee"],["Population","704,963 (2024)"],["Largest city","Nashville"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Nashville</b> is the capital of Tennessee and its largest city, the balance of its consolidated metropolitan government holding 704,963 residents against Memphis's 610,919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> James Robertson and his band of settlers built a fort above the Cumberland River in about 1780; the log Fort Nashborough that later stood in Riverfront Park was a replica of 1930, and it was pulled down in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town has been known as the Athens of the South since the 1820s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> William Strickland's capitol, its cornerstone laid on 4 July 1845 and its last terrace stone not set until 1859, is a prime example of the Greek Revival, with 28 Ionic columns 10 metres (33 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strickland died in 1854 and was laid, at his own request, in a vault in the north-east wall; his son Francis finished the building, whose fossil-bearing limestone was cut in large part by convicts from the state penitentiary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The slim tower over its roof copies the Choragic Monument of Lysicrates, raised in Athens in 335 BCE to hold a tripod won by a stage chorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the state's centennial exposition the city put up a Parthenon of wood and plaster in 1897 at a cost of $30,000, and liked it well enough to keep it and lay a park around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 1920 it was past repair, and the concrete replica that took its place consumed eleven years of research and building, its concrete mixed to look like weathered marble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Union Station was dedicated in 1900 to the design of Richard Montfort, an engineer of the Louisville and Nashville Railroad who had no architectural training and took the Allegheny County courthouse for his model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> WSM was the first station to broadcast country music nationally, in 1925, and from 1943 to 1974 its Grand Ole Opry played at the Ryman Auditorium, a Gothic Revival tabernacle finished in 1892, carrying a rural southern style to the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fort Nashborough, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/11000454_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Parthenon, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72001236_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tennessee State Capitol, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000894_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nashville Union Station and Trainshed, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/69000178_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ryman Auditorium, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000819_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Flag_of_Nashville%2C_Tennessee.png","credit":"City of Nashville, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nashville,_Tennessee.png)","alt":"The flag of Nashville: on navy with a yellow stripe at the fly, a white disc in a ring of yellow points bearing a mounted figure, a fleur-de-lis above, and METROPOLITAN GOVERNMENT OF NASHVILLE AND DAVIDSON COUNTY around it in blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Nashville%2C_TN_skyline.jpg/1920px-Nashville%2C_TN_skyline.jpg","title":"Nashville","desc":"Nashville, Tennessee, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nashville,_TN_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Nashville, Tennessee."}},
{"id":"geo-038","num":38,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Mississippi","answerText":"Mississippi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grand Village</span><span class=\"dt-v\">abandoned 1730</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 December 1817</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836 – 1840</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vicksburg falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1863</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Mississippi"},"facts":[["Capital","Jackson"],["Population","2,943,045 (2024)"],["Largest city","Jackson"],["Total area","125,438 km² (48,432 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Mississippi</b> is the 32nd largest of the American states, covering 125,438 square kilometres (48,432 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 2.9 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital and largest city alike at Jackson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was admitted to the Union on 10 December 1817 as the 20th state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Natchez the Grand Village of the Natchez, three flat-topped mounds along St Catherine Creek, was the governmental and religious seat of that people in the late 17th and early 18th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> French explorers first reached it in 1682, and it was abandoned in 1730 after the Natchez were beaten by a combined force of French, Choctaw and Tunica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> William Nichols built the Old Capitol at Jackson between 1836 and 1840, the largest and most impressive Greek Revival building raised in the state before the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The Married Women's Property Act passed there in 1839 was the first law by any state to protect the property of married women, and it set a precedent others followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> So was the Black Code of 1865, whose restrictions on the freedmen helped bring on the first federal civil rights law, in 1866, and the Fourteenth Amendment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Vicksburg commanded the Mississippi River from a bluff, and after a siege of 47 days John C. Pemberton called his chief officers together on 3 July 1863 and surrendered the city to Ulysses S. Grant the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Isaiah Thornton Montgomery and his cousin Benjamin Green, who had been enslaved on the Davis plantation at Davis Bend, founded the Black town of Mound Bayou on railroad land in 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 30 September 1962 segregationist rioters fought federal troops at the Lyceum in Oxford over the court-ordered admission of James Meredith to the University of Mississippi, a turning point in the enforcement of Brown v. Board of Education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Mississippi: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/mississippi-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Grand Village of the Natchez Site, Natchez, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000408_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Capitol, Jackson, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000087_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pemberton's Headquarters, Vicksburg, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000319_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Montgomery, I. T., House, Mound Bayou, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76001092_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Lyceum–The Circle Historic District, University, Mississippi,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/08001092_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/Flag_of_Mississippi.svg","credit":"Rocky Vaughn, Sue Anna Joe and Dominique Pugh, copyrighted free use, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mississippi.svg)","alt":"A dark blue panel between gold and red vertical bands, carrying a white magnolia blossom ringed by twenty white stars and one gold star, above the words IN GOD WE TRUST."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Illinois_Monument.jpg/1920px-Illinois_Monument.jpg","title":"Vicksburg, Mississippi","desc":"Vicksburg National Military Park.","credit":"KaitlinRatliff, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illinois_Monument.jpg","alt":"A view of Vicksburg National Military Park."}},
{"id":"geo-535","num":535,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jefferson City","answerText":"Jefferson City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cole County jail</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lohman's Landing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1834</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1914 – 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 October 1924</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Missouri","dot":"Jefferson City"},"facts":[["State","Missouri"],["Population","42,564 (2024)"],["Largest city","Kansas City"],["Rank in state","16th largest"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Jefferson City</b> is the capital of Missouri and only its sixteenth largest city, with 42,564 residents against Kansas City's 516,032.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Cole County jail of about 1825 had the sheriff's quarters on its ground floor and iron-sheathed cells above, and its thick limestone walls and tiny barred windows let no prisoner out in 111 years of use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lohman's Landing on the riverfront, whose first stone section went up in 1834, served in turn as warehouse, inn, store and wharf-master's office before Charles F. Lohman bought it in 1852 and gave it his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state's second capitol, designed by Stephen Hills and in use by 1840, burned in February 1911, and the legislature voted for a new one soon afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A State Capitol Commission toured other capitols for ideas and ran a national competition, won in 1912 by Egerton Swartwout and Evarts Tracy, both former associates of McKim, Mead and White.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Their building went up between 1914 and 1917 and was dedicated on 6 October 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is faced in pale grey Missouri limestone, carries 134 columns of the Corinthian order and stands on a high bluff above the Missouri River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It copies no other capitol and keeps the standard form all the same, a domed centre between two wings for the legislative houses, and its murals and statues make much of the state's frontier past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three streets away the governor's mansion of 1871 is the work of George Ingham Barnett, born at Nottingham and trained in London, who came to St. Louis in 1839 and practised there for 50 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The legislature voted $50,000 for it and it cost $74,960 in the end, and the red granite columns of its portico were quarried on the land of B. Gratz Brown, the first governor to live in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Cole County Jail, Jefferson City, Cole County, Missouri,” HABS No. MO-1334, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mo/mo0500/mo0565/data/mo0565data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Jefferson Landing Building, Jefferson City, Cole County, Missouri,” HABS No. MO-1194, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mo/mo0100/mo0163/data/mo0163data.pdf. [Open access]","Holly K. Chamberlain, “Missouri State Capitol, Jefferson City, Cole County, Missouri,” HABS No. MO-1840, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mo/mo1000/mo1092/data/mo1092data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Governor's Mansion, 100 Madison Street, Jefferson City, Cole County, Missouri,” HABS No. MO-1841, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mo/mo1000/mo1093/data/mo1093data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Flag_of_Jefferson_City%2C_Missouri.svg","credit":"Isaiah Jimmerson (vectorized by Deltaspace42), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jefferson_City,_Missouri.svg)","alt":"The flag of Jefferson City: on dark red, the white outline of the Capitol dome at the hoist, a white five-pointed star at the fly, and a white and slate-blue bridge over waves along the foot."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Jefferson_City_Bridge_2025_Cropped.jpg/1920px-Jefferson_City_Bridge_2025_Cropped.jpg","title":"Jefferson City","desc":"Jefferson City, Missouri, across the Missouri River.","credit":"Antony-22, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jefferson_City_Bridge_2025_Cropped.jpg","alt":"A river bridge with a town on the far bank."}},
{"id":"geo-039","num":39,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Delaware","answerText":"Delaware","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Holy Trinity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1698 – 1699</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 December 1787</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1787 – 1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Powder mills</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1802</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Delaware"},"facts":[["Capital","Dover"],["Population","1,051,917 (2024)"],["Largest city","Wilmington"],["Total area","6,446 km² (2,489 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Delaware</b> is the 49th largest of the American states, covering 6,446 square kilometres (2,489 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.05 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Dover and its largest city Wilmington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is nicknamed the First State because it was the first to ratify the Constitution, on 7 December 1787, and it takes its name from Thomas West, Lord De La Warr, for whom the explorer Samuel Argall named the bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Swedish Lutherans laid the cornerstone of Holy Trinity at Wilmington on 28 May 1698 and dedicated the church on Trinity Sunday the year after, on ground that had been used for burial for sixty years already.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was built of Brandywine granite with brick around the openings, and when its side walls began to spread, small porticos were raised against them in 1749 and a larger one in 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state house at Dover went up between 1787 and 1792 on foundations of brick taken from the building of 1722 it replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> That house served as the seat of the state until 1932 and as the courthouse of Kent County until 1873.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Éleuthère Irénée du Pont de Nemours founded his powder company on the Brandywine in 1802, and the mills he put up were patterned on the French ones in which he had learned the trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> About twenty of them survive, generally in pairs, the oldest of them built in 1822 under his own supervision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Their stone walls stand about a metre (three feet) thick and are heavily buttressed, while the roof and the wall facing the creek were framed as lightly as they could be made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> An explosion would then blow out across the water, the effect being almost that of firing a colossal mortar where there was the least chance of doing harm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Delaware: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/delaware-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Holy Trinity Church (Old Swedes), Seventh & Church Street, Wilmington, New Castle County, Delaware,” HABS No. DE-7, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/de/de0100/de0161/data/de0161data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Delaware State House, East side of The Green, Dover, Kent County, Delaware,” HABS No. DE-199, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/de/de0200/de0223/data/de0223data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “DuPont Powder Mill, Hagley, New Castle County, Delaware,” HABS No. DE-2, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/de/de0100/de0154/data/de0154data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Flag_of_Delaware.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Delaware.svg)","alt":"A pale blue-green flag with a buff diamond at its centre bearing the state arms — a farmer and a rifleman flanking a shield of wheat, maize and an ox — above the date December 7, 1787."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Cape_Henlopen_State_Park_beach_looking_north_July_2022.jpeg/1920px-Cape_Henlopen_State_Park_beach_looking_north_July_2022.jpeg","title":"Cape Henlopen, Delaware","desc":"Cape Henlopen State Park.","credit":"Dough4872, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Henlopen_State_Park_beach_looking_north_July_2022.jpeg","alt":"A view of Cape Henlopen State Park."}},
{"id":"geo-536","num":536,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Little Rock","answerText":"Little Rock","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">French Rock</span><span class=\"dt-v\">named 1722</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Old State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun 1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arsenal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">surrendered 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun 1899</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Arkansas","dot":"Little Rock"},"facts":[["State","Arkansas"],["Population","204,774 (2024)"],["Largest city","Little Rock"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Little Rock</b> is the capital of Arkansas and its largest city, with 204,774 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bénard de la Harpe came up the river in April 1722 and noted a steep outcrop of clean limestone on the right bank, which his party called the French Rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A letter to the Secretary of War in 1805 reported that the stony shore had caused the place to be named the Little Rocks, and from 1818 the rock served as a landmark and the starting point for most land surveys south of the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gideon Shryock of Kentucky drew the state house begun there in 1833, but his plans cost more than the state would pay, and his assistant George Weigart cut them down under Governor John Pope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was built is a stuccoed brick block in the Greek Revival manner, its central portico carried on four Doric columns, and it may be the first professionally designed public building in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The secession conventions of 1861 met in it, and it saw the Reconstruction violence of 1874, but its most notable years were 1912 to 1916, when the state Board of Health worked from it with the University of Arkansas Medical School against hookworm, malaria and the fevers that came almost every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The War Department voted $14,000 for an arsenal in the town after statehood, and building began in 1838 on the ground that is now MacArthur Park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 8 February 1861 Captain Totten gave it up intact, with 10,000 stand of arms, to Governor Henry M. Rector.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Douglas MacArthur was born in the building on 26 January 1880, and in 1892 the government traded it to the city on condition that the grounds be kept as a park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The present capitol, begun in 1899 on a hill west of the centre, resembles the Capitol at Washington and is a fine piece of classical design in the Ionic order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Little Rock, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000124_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old State House, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000037_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “U.S. Arsenal Building, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000127_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Arkansas State Capitol, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74000494_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/Flag_of_Little_Rock%2C_Arkansas.svg","credit":"Dyfsunctional, copyrighted free use, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Little_Rock,_Arkansas.svg)","alt":"The flag of Little Rock: a white field crossed by a blue and gold horizontal band and a green and gold vertical one, with a gold-rimmed seal at the centre showing a rocky outcrop beside water under the state outline, lettered CITY OF LITTLE ROCK and ARKANSAS."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Skyline_of_Little_Rock%2C_Arkansas_-_20050319.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Little_Rock%2C_Arkansas_-_20050319.jpg","title":"Little Rock","desc":"Little Rock, Arkansas, seen from a distance.","credit":"Broooooooce (Bruce W. Stracener) ., Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Little_Rock,_Arkansas_-_20050319.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Little Rock, Arkansas."}},
{"id":"geo-040","num":40,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Connecticut","answerText":"Connecticut","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 January 1788</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Morgan built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1841</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Twain house</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1874 – 1891</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nautilus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 January 1955</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Connecticut"},"facts":[["Capital","Hartford"],["Population","3,675,069 (2024)"],["Largest city","Bridgeport"],["Total area","14,357 km² (5,543 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Connecticut</b> is the 48th largest of the American states, covering 14,357 square kilometres (5,543 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 3.7 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Hartford and its largest city Bridgeport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the six New England states and one of the original thirteen colonies, it became a state on 9 January 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Charles W. Morgan</i>, built in 1841 at New Bedford in Massachusetts for $48,849.85, is the only wooden whaleship left from the American fleet of the 19th century, and she lies at Mystic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Her frame and planking are live oak and her upper decks yellow pine, and she was the usual round-bowed, square-rigged whaler of her day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Hartford, Edward Tuckerman Potter designed for Samuel Clemens a house of a kind the town had not seen, asymmetrical and patterned in black and vermilion brick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Clemens oversaw the building himself, adding a patch of tin to the roof so that he could hear the rain on it and a window above the dining-room fireplace so that he could watch the flames meet the snowflakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He is remembered for Tom Sawyer and Huckleberry Finn and so for the Midwest, but he did not write in Missouri: the great books came in the 17 years he lived in this house, from its completion in 1874 until money troubles took the family to Europe in 1891.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The state house that went up at Hartford between 1872 and 1880 is white marble from the quarries at East Canaan with granite from Westerly, in what its architect called modern secular Gothic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 17 January 1955 Commander Eugene P. Wilkinson cast off from the submarine base on the Thames and signalled to the crowds ashore, “Underway on Nuclear Power”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It was the first time the power of the atom had moved a ship; the idea went back to 1939 and a physicist at the Naval Research Laboratory, and the boat had taken from 1949 to 1955 to build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Connecticut: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/connecticut-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Charles W. Morgan, Mystic, Connecticut,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000804_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Mark Twain House, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000884_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Connecticut State Capitol, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000834_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “USS Nautilus, Groton, Connecticut,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/79002653_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Flag_of_Connecticut.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Connecticut.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a white shield with three grapevines, each carrying bunches of purple grapes, above a white ribbon reading QUI TRANSTULIT SUSTINET."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/GilletteCastleCT%284%29_AM_1504_Vibrant.jpg/1920px-GilletteCastleCT%284%29_AM_1504_Vibrant.jpg","title":"Gillette Castle, Connecticut","desc":"Gillette Castle State Park.","credit":"Philip McQuade, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GilletteCastleCT(4)_AM_1504_Vibrant.jpg","alt":"A view of Gillette Castle State Park."}},
{"id":"geo-537","num":537,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Montgomery","answerText":"Montgomery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1847, rebuilt 1851</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inauguration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bus boycott</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1955 – 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Selma march</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1965</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Alabama","dot":"Montgomery"},"facts":[["State","Alabama"],["Population","195,818 (2024)"],["Largest city","Huntsville"],["Rank in state","4th largest"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Montgomery</b> is the capital of Alabama and its fourth largest city, with 195,818 residents against Huntsville's 230,402.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capitol was finished on 26 October 1847 and first occupied by the legislature that December, standing on a rise at the head of Dexter Avenue, the town's main business street.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two years later, on 14 December 1849 and with the legislature in session, fire took almost the whole building and left only the masonry foundations and part of the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was rebuilt on Stephen Decatur Button's plans, dome and portico and all, and the legislature met in it again on 1 October 1851, the clock going into the tower four months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jefferson Davis was sworn in on its west portico on 18 February 1861, and the permanent Confederate constitution was adopted in the building on 11 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Martin Luther King was called to the pastorate of Dexter Avenue Baptist Church, at the foot of that same street, in 1954, to a congregation of about 600 that held most of the city's Black intellectuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In December 1955 Rosa Parks refused to give up her seat on a city bus and was arrested, and the anger that followed produced the Montgomery Improvement Association to fight bus segregation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decision handed down in 1956 by Judge Frank M. Johnson at the federal courthouse struck down segregated seating on those buses and carried the principle of <i>Brown v. Board of Education</i> out of the schools and into the rest of public life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The boycott ran for 381 days and ended on 21 December 1956, when the Supreme Court held that bus segregation was illegal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Freedom Rides came through in 1961 and the march from Selma ended here in 1965, and between them these actions desegregated schools and public places across the South.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Alabama State Capitol, Montgomery, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000152_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Dexter Avenue Baptist Church, Montgomery, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74000431_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “United States Post Office and Courthouse (Frank M. Johnson, Jr. Federal Building), Montgomery, Alabama,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/98000611_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Alabama: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/alabama-state-guide. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Skyline_views_of_downtown_area%2C_Montgomery%2C_Alabama_LCCN2010637559.tif/lossy-page1-1920px-Skyline_views_of_downtown_area%2C_Montgomery%2C_Alabama_LCCN2010637559.tif.jpg","title":"Montgomery","desc":"Montgomery, Alabama, seen from a distance.","credit":"Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_views_of_downtown_area,_Montgomery,_Alabama_LCCN2010637559.tif","alt":"A wide view of Montgomery, Alabama."}},
{"id":"geo-041","num":41,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"New Hampshire","answerText":"New Hampshire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 June 1788</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The Elms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1800</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chase born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 January 1808</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amoskeag</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17,000 workers at its peak</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Hampshire"},"facts":[["Capital","Concord"],["Population","1,409,032 (2024)"],["Largest city","Manchester"],["Total area","24,214 km² (9,349 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>New Hampshire</b> is the 46th largest of the American states, covering 24,214 square kilometres (9,349 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.4 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Concord and its largest city Manchester.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Granite State and lying in the north-eastern corner of the country, it became a state on 21 June 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Manchester was planned, built and dominated for more than a hundred years by the group of Boston men who created and ran the Amoskeag Manufacturing Company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At its height early in the 20th century it was the largest textile plant in the world, employing as many as 17,000 people in some thirty mills over 740,000 square metres (8 million square feet) of floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The mills were directed from an office in Boston, so it fell to the agent on the spot to plan production and to handle everything to do with labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Ebenezer Webster moved his family into a farmhouse called The Elms in 1800, near the tavern at Salisbury he had kept since 1784 and lived in for the fifteen years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His son Daniel was away at Dartmouth then, and bought the place in 1829 when his brother Ezekiel died, keeping it as an experimental farm and as the burial ground of his parents and four of his brothers and sisters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Once the railway was finished in 1847 he could come up from Boston in three hours, and he spent his visits walking the farm, hunting and fishing and seeing old friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Salmon Portland Chase was born on 13 January 1808 at Cornish, in a frame house of about 1790 that is thought to be the only building still standing that had anything to do with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He went on to serve in all three branches of the federal government, as an antislavery senator, as Secretary of the Treasury through most of the Civil War, and, on Abraham Lincoln's nomination of 6 December 1864, as Chief Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “New Hampshire: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-hampshire-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Straw, William Parker, House, Manchester, New Hampshire,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/87002068_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Daniel Webster Family Home, West Franklin, New Hampshire,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74000196_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Salmon P. Chase Birthplace, Cornish Flat, New Hampshire,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/75000133_text. [Open access]","Federal Judicial Center, “Chase, Salmon Portland,” Biographical Directory of Article III Federal Judges, https://www.fjc.gov/history/judges/chase-salmon-portland. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Flag_of_New_Hampshire.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Hampshire.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state seal: a frigate on the stocks before a rising sun, ringed by laurel and nine gold stars, with the date 1776 beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/White_Mountains_12_30_09_81.jpg/1920px-White_Mountains_12_30_09_81.jpg","title":"Mount Washington, New Hampshire","desc":"Mount Washington.","credit":"Harvey Barrison, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Mountains_12_30_09_81.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Washington."}},
{"id":"geo-538","num":538,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jackson","answerText":"Jackson","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mansion built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839 – 1841</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Evers house</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Evers killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1963</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Mississippi","dot":"Jackson"},"facts":[["State","Mississippi"],["Population","141,449 (2024)"],["Largest city","Jackson"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Jackson</b> is the capital of Mississippi and its largest city,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with 141,449 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The English-born William Nichols was state architect when the governor's mansion went up between 1839 and 1841, and he was supervising the building of the capitol at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tilghman M. Tucker was the first governor to occupy it, in 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only seven governors' mansions raised in the 19th century are still lived in by governors, four of them built before the Civil War, and of those four the Mississippi house is the most distinguished piece of architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eudora Welty grew up in a Tudor Revival house on Pinehurst Street beside the campus of Belhaven College, in a garden her mother Chestina planted and which has been restored to how it stood between 1925 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Medgar Evers applied to the law school of the University of Mississippi in 1954, the year the Supreme Court held school segregation unconstitutional, and was refused on the ground that his letters of recommendation would not serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He became the first field secretary the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People had in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Selling insurance in the Delta before that, he had found he could not press policies on people who had not enough to eat, and used the visits instead to sign them into the local chapter or to help them start one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A house was built for his family in 1956 on what is now Margaret Walker Alexander Drive, a low ranch house of the sort that went up everywhere after the war, with a carport at one end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was shot dead in its driveway in 1963, and the house is listed for the seven years he lived in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Mississippi: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/mississippi-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Mississippi Governor's Mansion, Jackson, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000085_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Welty, Eudora, House, Jackson, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/02001388_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Evers, Medgar, House, Jackson, Mississippi,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/00001459_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/Flag_of_Jackson%2C_Mississippi.svg","credit":"Nameneko at English Wikipedia, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jackson,_Mississippi.svg)","alt":"The flag of Jackson: a green field quartered by a white cross, with a blue disc ringed in gold at the centre bearing a large gold five-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/JacksonMS_Downtown_Panorama.jpg/1920px-JacksonMS_Downtown_Panorama.jpg","title":"Jackson","desc":"Jackson, Mississippi, seen from a distance.","credit":"chmeredith from Jackson, MS, USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JacksonMS_Downtown_Panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Jackson, Mississippi."}},
{"id":"geo-042","num":42,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Vermont","answerText":"Vermont","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Convention</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 4 June 1777</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">adopted 8 July 1777</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 March 1791</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rockingham</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1787–1800</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Vermont"},"facts":[["Capital","Montpelier"],["Population","648,493 (2024)"],["Largest city","Burlington"],["Total area","24,906 km² (9,616 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Vermont</b> is the 45th largest of the American states, covering 24,906 square kilometres (9,616 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 648,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Montpelier and its largest city Burlington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It entered the Union as the 14th state on 4 March 1791, and for the fourteen years before that it had governed itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement began in a tavern at Windsor, where on 4 June 1777 72 delegates from a territory then calling itself New Connecticut met to frame a constitution for a proposed independent republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> While they sat, a letter was read out from Thomas Young of Philadelphia, a close friend of Ethan Allen, advising them on the steps to statehood and recommending the name Vermont instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The delegates gathered again on 2 July in the tavern kept by Elijah West, and on the 8th they adopted the constitution the building is now named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That document was the first in the country to prohibit slavery and to establish universal manhood suffrage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A legislature chosen in March 1778 met at Windsor on the 12th of that month, and by setting a government going under the 1777 constitution Vermont became a republic independent of Great Britain and of every other state in North America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rockingham began a meeting house on 9 June 1787 and finished it in 1800, a plain two-storey building put up by the town under a master builder known as General Fuller and used both as a house of worship and as a town house for public business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Services stopped there in 1839 and voters met in it until about 1870, after which relic hunters badly damaged the empty building; subscriptions and a town appropriation restored it in 1906, and it was rededicated on 15 August 1907.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Barre grew around what one survey calls the vital granite industry in Vermont, where firms such as Grearson and Lane, founded in 1897, cut and polished stone in timber-framed sheds fitted with lathes, grindstones, saws and boring machines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Vermont: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/vermont-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Old Constitution House, Windsor, Vermont,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/71000075_text. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Rockingham Meeting House, Rockingham, Windham County, Vermont,” HABS No. VT-22, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/vt/vt0000/vt0004/data/vt0004data.pdf. [Open access]","Holly K. Chamberlain, “Grearson & Lane Company, Granite Turning Shed, Barre, Washington County, Vermont,” HAER No. VT-9, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/vt/vt0000/vt0099/data/vt0099data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Flag_of_Vermont.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vermont.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state arms — a lone pine before green hills, with a cow and three sheaves of wheat — under a red deer's head, over a scroll reading FREEDOM AND UNITY."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/South_from_Mount_Mansfield.jpg/1920px-South_from_Mount_Mansfield.jpg","title":"the Green Mountains, Vermont","desc":"Green Mountains.","credit":"Mike9827, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_from_Mount_Mansfield.jpg","alt":"A view of Green Mountains."}},
{"id":"geo-539","num":539,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Dover","answerText":"Dover","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Penn warrant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">signed at Dover, 1683</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Seat of state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1777</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Woodburn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built c. 1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State College</span><span class=\"dt-v\">established 1891</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Delaware","dot":"Dover"},"facts":[["State","Delaware"],["Population","40,191 (2024)"],["Largest city","Wilmington"],["Rank in state","2nd largest"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Dover</b> is the capital of Delaware and, with about 40,000 residents, the second largest place in the state after Wilmington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre is The Green, a small 18th-century square whose east side is taken up by the Old State House and the Supreme Court building, with the Kent County court house and private homes round the rest, many of them 18th-century and some wearing Victorian fronts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The square has been the seat of Delaware's government since 1777, and almost every decision the state has taken since was made on it or near it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Declaration of Independence was read out in front of the state house in July 1776 and King George's portrait ceremonially burned there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On or very near the same site Delaware became the first state to ratify the federal constitution, on 7 December 1787.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside the state house the General Assembly of what the nomination calls the northernmost slave state recorded unqualified disapproval of secession on 3 January 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town plan of 1717 set aside two public squares for houses of worship, and Christ Church, begun in 1704 as a mission of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel, moved onto one of them in 1734.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dover is older still: the founder of the Hillyard family took out a warrant for 1,200 hectares (3,000 acres) in 1683, signed there, the only surviving evidence that the Proprietor ever visited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His descendant Charles Hillyard built Woodburn around 1790 as a country seat north of the town, which had grown around it by the middle of the next century; local tradition, resting on its position and its owner's abolitionist leanings, makes it a station on the underground railroad, and it houses the governor now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the head of the St Jones River stands Loockerman Hall, a brick Georgian house of 1730 or earlier, one of the finest pre-Revolutionary houses left in the state, which served seventy years as classroom and dormitory for the Delaware State College founded in 1891.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old State House, The Green, Dover, Delaware,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/71000219_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Governor's House (Woodburn), Kings Highway, Dover, Delaware,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72000279_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Loockerman Hall, Delaware State College Campus, Dover, Delaware,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/71000218_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Christ Church, Water and South State Streets, Dover, Delaware,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72001500_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Dover_Delaware.jpg/1920px-Dover_Delaware.jpg","title":"Dover","desc":"Downtown Dover, Delaware.","credit":"Tim Kiser ( w:User:Malepheasant ), CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dover_Delaware.jpg","alt":"A main street of low brick shopfronts."}},
{"id":"geo-043","num":43,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Montana","answerText":"Montana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">created 26 May 1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 November 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silver at Butte</span><span class=\"dt-v\">found 1874</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Copper at Butte</span><span class=\"dt-v\">found 1883</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Montana"},"facts":[["Capital","Helena"],["Population","1,137,233 (2024)"],["Largest city","Billings"],["Total area","380,831 km² (147,040 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Montana</b> is the 4th largest of the American states, covering 380,831 square kilometres (147,040 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.14 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Helena and its largest city Billings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Abraham Lincoln signed the act creating the Territory of Montana on 26 May 1864, and 25 years later, on 8 November 1889, it became the 41st state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fort Benton on the Missouri had been a fur trading post before the territory existed, and in the gold years of the 1860s it was the head of steamboat navigation and so the commercial centre of the whole region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Supplies came up the river to the town and went overland from there to Helena, Virginia City and the smaller camps, until placer mining fell away in the 1870s and the railways reached the territory in the 1880s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Butte prospered from gold as early as 1864 as well, but its first residents put up as few buildings as they could and nothing from that era survives in the centre of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Its fortunes turned in 1874 when silver was found, and again in 1883 with copper, which quickly became the chief product of the mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A run of lawsuits, take-overs and mergers between 1884 and 1906 gathered the workings under the Anaconda Company, and the methods changed with the ore body from overhead stoping to block caving to open-pit mining.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> For more than 80 years Butte Hill was one of the largest and most productive underground mining districts in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Cornish miners left the failing tin workings at home for Centerville and Walkerville, and Chinese and Irish neighbourhoods in the town were joined by Italians at Meaderville and Finns on East Broadway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The city's first skyscraper, an eight-storey steel-framed block, went up in 1901; by 1931 copper had fallen to five cents a pound, and a general strike against Anaconda in 1935 lengthened the bread lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Montana: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/montana-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Engineering Record, “Fort Benton Bridge, Spanning the Missouri River, Fort Benton, Chouteau County, Montana,” HAER No. MT-9, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mt/mt0000/mt0075/data/mt0075data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Engineering Record, “Butte Historic District, Butte, Silver Bow County, Montana,” HAER No. MT-35, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mt/mt0000/mt0040/data/mt0040data.pdf. [Open access]","Monica E. Hawley, “Butte Mineyards, Butte, Silver Bow County, Montana,” HAER No. MT-36, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mt/mt0000/mt0041/data/mt0041data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Flag_of_Montana.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Montana.svg)","alt":"A blue flag with MONTANA in gold across the top, above the state seal: mountains and a waterfall behind a plough, a pick and a shovel, on a scroll reading ORO Y PLATA."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Lake_McDonald%2C_Glacier_National_Park%2C_Montana_%28crop%29.jpg/1920px-Lake_McDonald%2C_Glacier_National_Park%2C_Montana_%28crop%29.jpg","title":"Glacier National Park, Montana","desc":"Lake McDonald in Glacier National Park.","credit":"TLJones007, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_McDonald,_Glacier_National_Park,_Montana_(crop).jpg","alt":"A mountain reflected in a still lake."}},
{"id":"geo-540","num":540,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hartford","answerText":"Hartford","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hooker arrived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1633</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named Hartford</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1637</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burying ground</span><span class=\"dt-v\">set apart 1640</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Atheneum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened July 1844</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Connecticut","dot":"Hartford"},"facts":[["State","Connecticut"],["Population","122,129 (2024)"],["Largest city","Bridgeport"],["Rank in state","4th largest"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Hartford</b> is the capital of Connecticut and its fourth largest city, with 122,129 residents, after Bridgeport, Stamford and New Haven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The congregation that founded it gathered at Newtown, now Cambridge in Massachusetts, in 1632, and Thomas Hooker arrived from England the next year and was recognised as its minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1636 the church moved as a body to the west bank of the Connecticut River, and in 1637 the settlers named the place after Hertford in England, the birthplace of Samuel Stone, the teacher who had come with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They raised four meeting houses on or near the same ground: a plain hipped-roof box in 1636, a framed one with glass windows and a small steeple in 1640, a third with a tall spire in 1739 and the present church in 1807.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Ancient Burying Ground beside it was set apart by the town in 1640 and took every burial in Hartford until 1803, and Samuel Stone's marker of 1663 is the oldest still standing there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Wadsworth Atheneum on Main Street was begun in April 1842 and finished in July 1844 to a castellated Gothic design by Ithiel Town, in cream granite from Glastonbury, at a cost of nearly $35,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its nomination calls it the first free art museum in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Henry Hobson Richardson was commissioned in September 1875 to design the commercial block on Main Street that still carries the name of the Cheney silk family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city calls itself the insurance capital of the world, and one of the firms behind the claim began in 1851 as the American Temperance Life Insurance Company, which would write policies only for people who pledged to abstain from alcohol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Renamed Phoenix Mutual Life in 1861, it stayed downtown when others left for the suburbs, and its elliptical glass tower of 1963 by Max Abramovitz anchored Constitution Plaza, the first urban renewal project in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “First Church of Christ and the Ancient Burying Ground, 60 Gold Street, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72001324_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wadsworth Atheneum, 25 Atheneum Square North, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000709_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Cheney Building, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78002852_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Phoenix Life Insurance Company Building, One American Row, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/04001462_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Flag_of_Hartford%2C_Connecticut.svg","credit":"City of Hartford, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hartford,_Connecticut.svg)","alt":"The flag of Hartford: on navy, the city seal in white and gold, an eagle above a shield showing a stag in a landscape, the motto POST NUBILA PHOEBUS on a scroll, with CITY OF HARTFORD above and CONNECTICUT below."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Hartford%2C_CT_skyline_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Hartford%2C_CT_skyline_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Hartford","desc":"Hartford, Connecticut, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hartford,_CT_skyline_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hartford, Connecticut."}},
{"id":"geo-044","num":44,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Wyoming","answerText":"Wyoming","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Bedlam</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suffrage bill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">passed 1869</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1886</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 July 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Wyoming"},"facts":[["Capital","Cheyenne"],["Population","587,618 (2024)"],["Largest city","Cheyenne"],["Total area","253,335 km² (97,813 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Wyoming</b> is the 10th largest of the American states, covering 253,335 square kilometres (97,813 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the least populous of the fifty, with about 588,000 residents;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cheyenne is both its capital and its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became the 44th state on 10 July 1890 and is nicknamed the Equality State, having been the first to enfranchise women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Fort Laramie the army's Engineer Department raised the officers' quarters known as Old Bedlam in 1849,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and the sutler built his store in three goes: adobe about 1850, stone in 1865 and lime concrete about 1885.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The capitol at Cheyenne was begun in 1886 and carries a gold-leafed dome and pinnacle visible for miles in any direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first victory for women's suffrage in the United States had come in the Territory of Wyoming, where a bill passed the council by 6 votes to 2 and the lower house by 6 to 4 in 1869.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Esther Morris, who had heard Susan B. Anthony lecture in Illinois before moving west, was a force behind it and lobbied members of the territorial council herself, with the help of legislators' wives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Warnings of disaster were answered by the elections of 1870, which passed off smoothly, and when Wyoming applied for admission in 1889 Democrats opposed it on the ground that a full-suffrage state would encourage others to extend the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Joseph M. Carey, the territorial delegate in Washington, telegraphed home that suffrage might have to be given up to gain statehood, and the legislature answered, “We will remain out of the Union a hundred years rather than come in without the women.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Congress voted for statehood in 1890, the House by 139 to 127, and Utah and Colorado soon followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between 1870 and 1910 there were 480 separate initiatives in 33 states to amend their constitutions and give women the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Wyoming: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/wyoming-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wyoming State Capitol and Grounds, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73001935_text. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Old Bedlam, Fort Laramie, Goshen County, Wyoming,” HABS No. WYO-8, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/wy/wy0000/wy0002/data/wy0002data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Sutler’s Store, Fort Laramie, Goshen County, Wyoming,” Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/wy/wy0000/wy0003/data/wy0003data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Flag_of_Wyoming.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wyoming.svg)","alt":"A red flag with a white and blue border, bearing the white silhouette of a bison with the state seal set on its flank."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Yellowstone_National_Park_%28WY%2C_USA%29%2C_Old_Faithful_Geyser_--_2022_--_2599.jpg/1920px-Yellowstone_National_Park_%28WY%2C_USA%29%2C_Old_Faithful_Geyser_--_2022_--_2599.jpg","title":"Old Faithful, Wyoming","desc":"Old Faithful.","credit":"Dietmar Rabich, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellowstone_National_Park_(WY,_USA),_Old_Faithful_Geyser_--_2022_--_2599.jpg","alt":"A view of Old Faithful."}},
{"id":"geo-541","num":541,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Concord","answerText":"Concord","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legislature</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first sat 13 March 1782</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Concord wagon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first built 1813</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Concord coach</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first built 1826</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pierce died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 October 1869</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Hampshire","dot":"Concord"},"facts":[["State","New Hampshire"],["Population","44,674 (2024)"],["Largest city","Manchester"],["Rank in state","3rd largest"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Concord</b> is the capital of New Hampshire and its third largest city, with 44,674 residents, after Manchester and Nashua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legislature first sat here on 13 March 1782, in the North Meeting House, and found it so cold that it adjourned to Judge Timothy Walker's store on North Main Street, the council meeting in the minister's south parlour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lewis Downing reached the town in 1812 and built the first Concord wagon the year after, enlarging his wagon-building trade for the next thirteen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1826, with J. Stephens Abbot, he made New Hampshire's first stage coach, the Concord coach, which grew famous quickly, each one built by hand and ornamented individually.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The partnership was dissolved in 1847 and both men went on making coaches; Downing's son had joined at seventeen and was still president of Abbot, Downing & Company when he died in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Franklin Pierce came back to a house at 52 South Main Street after leaving the White House in 1857, boarding with Willard Williams, a mechanic in one of the town's coach and wagon works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nathaniel Hawthorne, his college roommate and lifelong friend, came to stay for ten days in the spring of 1864 in failing health, and the two then travelled north together to Plymouth, where Hawthorne died in a hotel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the front porch of the same house in 1865 Pierce spoke to a crowd gathered to protest at the assassination of Lincoln, and reassured them of his own patriotism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His wife had died there in 1863 and he died there himself on 8 October 1869, in the south-east front bedroom, a funeral service was held in the house, and the body then lay in state at the capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The gasholder house raised on South Main Street in 1888 held 3,400 cubic metres (120,000 cubic feet) until a natural gas pipeline reached the town in 1952, and is the last of its kind in the country still holding its original gasholder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Concord Historic District, Concord, New Hampshire,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75000234_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Downing, Lewis, Jr., House, 33 Pleasant Street, Concord, New Hampshire,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/87001425_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Franklin Pierce House, 52 South Main Street, Concord, New Hampshire,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79000318_text. [Open access]","William L. Taylor, “Concord Gasholder House, South Main Street, Concord, Merrimack County, New Hampshire,” HAER No. NH-7, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/nh/nh0100/nh0131/data/nh0131data.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Downtown_Concord_HD.JPG/1920px-Downtown_Concord_HD.JPG","title":"Concord","desc":"Downtown Concord, New Hampshire.","credit":"Teemu08 at en.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Concord_HD.JPG","alt":"A main street of tall brick blocks."}},
{"id":"geo-045","num":45,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Colorado","answerText":"Colorado","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cliff dwellings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun about 1200 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Balcony House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">enlarged in the 1240s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 August 1876</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cripple Creek</span><span class=\"dt-v\">gold struck 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Colorado"},"facts":[["Capital","Denver"],["Population","5,957,493 (2024)"],["Largest city","Denver"],["Total area","269,601 km² (104,094 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Colorado</b> is the 8th largest of the American states, covering 269,601 square kilometres (104,094 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.96 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Denver is both its capital and its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Centennial State and the Highest State, it became the 38th state on 1 August 1876.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the south-western corner of the state, from about 1200 CE, the people the survey calls the Anasazi began raising dwellings in the alcoves high above Soda Canyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Balcony House, named for the balconies still standing across two pairs of its rooms, holds 38 rooms and two ceremonial chambers called kivas, set around two plazas inside a single alcove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Building began at the north side of the north plaza, a second campaign in the 1240s put up the central core, and the generation after that doubled the number of rooms and finished the place as it now stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That last generation sealed the cross wall between the two plazas and dug a tunnel near the entrance, both of which suggest a growing concern with who could get in and where they could go; the people left around 1300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They had reached the dwelling from above, by ropes and by holds in the rock face, and the ladder that climbs about 9 metres (30 feet) to it from below is modern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Six centuries later the prospector Robert Womack, who had searched these mountains for about fifteen years, sank a shaft 15 metres (48 feet) deep and struck a rich gold vein in 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Others made discoveries near his claim and companies bought them up, and by 1893 Cripple Creek was the focus of a rush that made it one of the most productive gold districts in American history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Womack's own mine, soon eclipsed by its neighbours, worked almost without a break from 1890 to 1928 and again from 1936 until 1954, spending its last four years as a tourist attraction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Colorado: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/colorado-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Balcony House, Mesa Verde National Park, Cortez vicinity, Montezuma County, Colorado,” HABS No. CO-178, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/co/co0300/co0380/data/co0380data.pdf. [Open access]","Kurt Schweigert, “El Paso Lode (Gold King Mine), Cripple Creek vicinity, Teller County, Colorado,” HAER No. CO-92, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/co/co0900/co0925/data/co0925data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/46/Flag_of_Colorado.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colorado.svg)","alt":"Three horizontal bands, blue, white and blue, with a large red letter C at the hoist enclosing a gold disc."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Maroon_Bells_%2811553%29a.jpg/1920px-Maroon_Bells_%2811553%29a.jpg","title":"the Maroon Bells, Colorado","desc":"Maroon Bells.","credit":"Rhododendrites, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maroon_Bells_(11553)a.jpg","alt":"A view of Maroon Bells."}},
{"id":"geo-542","num":542,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Montpelier","answerText":"Montpelier","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chartered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 August 1781</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1833 – 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1857 – 1859, after fire</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Clothespins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">factory built 1918</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Vermont","dot":"Montpelier"},"facts":[["State","Vermont"],["Population","7,915 (2024)"],["Largest city","Burlington"],["Rank in state","7th largest"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Montpelier</b> is the capital of Vermont and the smallest state capital in the United States, with 7,915 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The township was granted in 1780 and chartered on 14 August 1781 by Timothy Bigelow and 59 associates, to hold 9,300 hectares (23,040 acres), north-east of the geographic centre of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grist, saw and wool-carding mills grew up along the Winooski and its North Branch, and later in the 19th century steam replaced water power and the railway came in beside the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The National Clothespin Company raised its building on Granite Street in 1918, in that same strip between water and rails, and as demand fell and cheap imports arrived it became the last works of its kind in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> College Hall, put up between 1868 and 1872, is an outstanding French Second Empire building and one of the town's architectural landmarks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The legislature resolved in 1831 to build a new state house, and a committee of four went to look at the capitols of Connecticut, Massachusetts and New Hampshire before accepting a design from Ammi B. Young, a 32-year-old architect of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was his first large commission; he took the exterior from the Grecian Doric of the Theseion at Athens and the interior from the Ionic; the building was begun in 1833, occupied in 1836 and finished two years later at a cost of $132,007.23.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The timber interior burned in January 1857 and Thomas William Silloway rebuilt to the old design, adding a bay at either end and two at the back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He replaced Young's low saucer dome with a taller timber-framed one about 17 metres (57 feet) high, sheathed in copper and covered with gold leaf, above walls of Barre granite from the Wheaton Quarry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thomas Waterman Wood, a native of the town who became president of the National Academy, built himself a Rural Gothic summer cottage above the gorge in 1850 and painted the townspeople, from every walk of life, as his subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Elizabeth Andre et al., “National Clothespin Factory, One Granite Street, Montpelier, Washington County, Vermont,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/05001422_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Vermont State House, Montpelier, Vermont,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000739_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Athenwood and Thomas W. Wood Studio, 41–39 Northfield Street, Montpelier, Vermont,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74000259_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “College Hall, Montpelier, Vermont,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75000146_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Flag_of_Montpelier%2C_Vermont.svg","credit":"Original: Chet Larrow Vector: Jack Ryan Morris, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Montpelier,_Vermont.svg)","alt":"The flag of Montpelier: green hills across the foot of a dark navy field, with a ring of gold stars in the sky above them."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Montpelier%2C_Vermont.jpg/1920px-Montpelier%2C_Vermont.jpg","title":"Montpelier","desc":"Downtown Montpelier, Vermont.","credit":"Doug Kerr, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montpelier,_Vermont.jpg","alt":"A street of painted brick shopfronts."}},
{"id":"geo-046","num":46,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Kansas","answerText":"Kansas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the act of 1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lecompton</span><span class=\"dt-v\">convention met 1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 January 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Brown v. Board</span><span class=\"dt-v\">decided 17 May 1954</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Kansas"},"facts":[["Capital","Topeka"],["Population","2,970,606 (2024)"],["Largest city","Wichita"],["Total area","213,100 km² (82,278 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Kansas</b> is the 15th largest of the American states, covering 213,100 square kilometres (82,278 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 2.97 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Topeka and its largest city Wichita.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was admitted to the Union as a free state on 29 January 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Kansas-Nebraska Act of 1854 let the territory decide for itself whether it would be free or slave, and outsiders of opposing sympathies poured in to take it by weight of numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rival constitutions were approved in mass assemblies at Topeka, Lecompton, Lawrence and Wyandotte, and the plain white frame hall at Lecompton is where the proslavery draft was written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lecompton was the territorial capital from August 1855 until January 1861 and the headquarters of the proslavery side, and the second territorial legislature sat in the hall from 12 January to 20 February 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A special election late that year chose delegates for a constitutional convention; free-state men refused to take part, holding that it had been rigged, and all 60 delegates returned were proslavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The constitution they framed was supported by President Buchanan and rejected by Congress, and it inflamed the quarrel between the sections that broke out shortly afterwards in civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Almost a century later the Monroe Elementary School at Topeka, built in 1926 by Thomas W. Williamson, was one of four schools in the city serving the Black community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sumner Elementary, the neighbourhood school, refused to enrol Linda Brown because she was black, and Monroe was the segregated school she attended instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The case that followed, Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka, ended on 17 May 1954 with the Supreme Court holding that “separate educational facilities are inherently unequal”, which took away the legal basis for segregation in the 21 states that had segregated schoolrooms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Kansas: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/kansas-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Lecompton Constitution Hall, Lecompton, Kansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000312_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sumner Elementary School and Monroe Elementary School, Topeka, Kansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/87001283_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Flag_of_Kansas.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kansas.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag with a sunflower on a twisted bar above the state seal — a river steamer, ox wagons, a farmer ploughing and a herd of bison — and KANSAS in gold beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Monument_Rocks.jpg/1920px-Monument_Rocks.jpg","title":"Monument Rocks, Kansas","desc":"Monument Rocks (Kansas).","credit":"Wistar92, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_Rocks.jpg","alt":"A view of Monument Rocks (Kansas)."}},
{"id":"geo-543","num":543,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Helena","answerText":"Helena","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">summer 1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Masonic charter</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1865</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mansions built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1885</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cathedral</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1908 – 1924</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Montana","dot":"Helena"},"facts":[["State","Montana"],["Population","34,729 (2024)"],["Largest city","Billings"],["Rank in state","6th largest"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Helena</b> is the capital of Montana and its sixth largest city, with 34,729 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gold was found in Last Chance Gulch in the summer of 1864 and the news brought a stampede of hopeful miners, and within months a citizens' committee had laid out streets, elected commissioners and given the place its present name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Masons first gathered in March 1865 for the funeral of a man who had asked for their rites, took out a charter that November as Helena City Lodge No. 10, and had outgrown two halls by 1884.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> T. F. Mathias and Fred Heinlein designed the second Masonic Temple that followed, three storeys of brick with stone trim in the Renaissance manner, hailed as a mark of local craftsmanship and materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> William A. Chessman, born in Massachusetts in 1830, came in 1865 from the placer diggings of California and Virginia City and rose to head the water company that became the city's own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The house he built at 6th and Ewing in 1885 was bought by the State of Montana in 1913 as the first official governor's mansion, and it served nine governors over 46 years before a new one replaced it in 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Samuel T. Hauser raised a mansion of his own the same year, in a city that already had a good many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hauser had been one of the party whose 1870 expedition produced the idea of setting Yellowstone aside, and in July 1885 President Cleveland made him governor of the Territory of Montana, the first resident of the territory to hold the office rather than a man sent from the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Cathedral of Saint Helena went up between 1908 and 1924 in tawny Bedford limestone under a steeply pitched red-tile roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is Gothic Revival, loosely patterned after the cathedral at Cologne and a near replica of the Votivkirche in Vienna, cruciform in plan, with a long nave and single-storey aisles running to twin transepts and a semicircular apse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Second Masonic Temple, Broadway and Jackson Streets, Helena, Lewis and Clark County, Montana,” HABS No. MT-34, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mt/mt0000/mt0018/data/mt0018data.pdf. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Former Montana Executive Mansion, Helena, Montana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000357_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Hauser Mansion, Helena, Montana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79001404_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Cathedral of Saint Helena, Helena, Montana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/80004270_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Helena_Montana_2006.jpg/1920px-Helena_Montana_2006.jpg","title":"Helena","desc":"Helena, Montana, seen from a distance.","credit":"RTC at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helena_Montana_2006.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Helena, Montana."}},
{"id":"geo-047","num":47,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Nebraska","answerText":"Nebraska","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 March 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital chosen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 July 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kennard House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1869</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fairview</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Bryan's home 1902 – 1921</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Nebraska"},"facts":[["Capital","Lincoln"],["Population","2,005,465 (2024)"],["Largest city","Omaha"],["Total area","200,330 km² (77,348 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Nebraska</b> is the 16th largest of the American states, covering 200,330 square kilometres (77,348 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Lincoln and its largest city Omaha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Cornhusker State, it became a state on 1 March 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of the first questions the new state faced was where to put its capital, and the legislature, sitting at Omaha, which had been the territorial capital since 1854, named three of its own officers to decide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Governor David Butler, Secretary of State Thomas Perkins Kennard and Auditor John Gillespie chose the site of Lincoln on 29 July 1867, largely at Kennard's urging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether the place would survive at all, let alone remain the capital, was uncertain until the depression years of the 1870s had passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1869 the Chicago architect John Keys Winchell designed three similar masonry houses in the town, one for each commissioner, and building them did much to persuade Nebraskans that Lincoln had a future; only Kennard's still stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> William Jennings Bryan practised law in Illinois before moving to Lincoln, and in 1891 he became the first Democrat Nebraska sent to Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He took the Democratic convention at Chicago in 1896 with a speech for bimetallism that ended “you shall not crucify mankind upon a cross of gold”, was nominated by the Democrats and the Populists together, travelled 29,000 kilometres (18,000 miles) and made 600 speeches in 27 states, and lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Willa Cather lived at Red Cloud from the age of eleven until she left for the state university at seventeen, and that house is minutely described in <i>The Song of the Lark</i> and in two later stories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> She held that a writer gathers his thematic material before he is fifteen, and the people and the prairies she remembered from those years are the raw material of <i>O Pioneers!</i> and <i>My Ántonia</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Nebraska: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/nebraska-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Thomas P. Kennard House, Lincoln, Nebraska,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/69000134_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fairview, Lincoln, Nebraska,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000947_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Willa Cather House, Red Cloud, Nebraska,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000139_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Flag_of_Nebraska.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nebraska.svg)","alt":"A blue flag bearing the state seal in gold and grey: a blacksmith at his anvil, a steamboat, a train and mountains, ringed by GREAT SEAL OF THE STATE OF NEBRASKA and the date MARCH 1ST 1867."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Chimney_Rock_NE.jpg/1920px-Chimney_Rock_NE.jpg","title":"Chimney Rock, Nebraska","desc":"Chimney Rock National Historic Site.","credit":"Mike Tigas from Columbia, MO, United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chimney_Rock_NE.jpg","alt":"A view of Chimney Rock National Historic Site."}},
{"id":"geo-544","num":544,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Cheyenne","answerText":"Cheyenne","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Site chosen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Town surveyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Depot built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1886 – 1887</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Wyoming","dot":"Cheyenne"},"facts":[["State","Wyoming"],["Population","65,704 (2024)"],["Largest city","Cheyenne"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Cheyenne</b> is the capital of Wyoming and its largest city, with 65,704 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 4 July 1867, with the Union Pacific construction crew still at Julesburg in Colorado, its chief engineer Grenville Dodge met General C. C. Augur where the line would cross Crow Creek, to fix a place for an army post and supply depot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town was surveyed within a week and the lots sold by the railway's own land company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first houses and businesses were tents, shanties, adobes and prefabricated buildings put up, taken down and put up again as the tracks moved west, and lots that first sold for $150 reached $2,000 in the speculation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the time the construction crew arrived they found a town of some 4,000 people with two daily newspapers, more than 200 businesses and a working town government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It called itself the Magic City of the Plains, and its own newspaper wrote that Cheyenne was a creation of the Union Pacific and by that corporation's acts would stand or fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The depot that stands there now was designed by the Boston firm of Van Brunt & Howe and built in 1886 and 1887, Richardsonian Romanesque on massive stone walls, on the site of the wooden station of 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It faces the capitol ten blocks north along Capitol Avenue, begun in 1886 under David W. Gibbs and William Dubois, which became the state capitol on Wyoming's admission in 1890 and was finished in stages down to 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Erasmus Nagle's mansion, finished in 1888 at a cost of $50,000, was built of rough grey sandstone blocks rejected for the capitol, and the contractor who rejected them was proved right when they began to flake in the 1950s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among those the cattle boom drew was Moreton Frewen, a younger son of English country gentry, whose house survives as a token of the tie between the decline of British agriculture and the first stage of ranching on the high plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Union Pacific Railroad Depot, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73001934_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wyoming State Capitol and Grounds, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73001935_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Moreton Frewen House, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75001904_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nagle-Warren Mansion, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/76001955_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Flag_of_Cheyenne%2C_Wyoming.svg","credit":"Jack Ryan Morris, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cheyenne,_Wyoming.svg)","alt":"The flag of Cheyenne: a brown border round a white field bearing the city seal in brown and gold, a bison ringed by CITY OF CHEYENNE and THE EQUALITY STATE, with 1867 and WYOMING beneath it."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Lincolnway_-_Cheyenne_-_Wyoming_%2851837641009%29.jpg/1920px-Lincolnway_-_Cheyenne_-_Wyoming_%2851837641009%29.jpg","title":"Cheyenne","desc":"Downtown Cheyenne, Wyoming.","credit":"Tony Webster, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lincolnway_-_Cheyenne_-_Wyoming_(51837641009).jpg","alt":"A block of old commercial buildings on a wide street."}},
{"id":"geo-048","num":48,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"South Dakota","answerText":"South Dakota","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ingalls arrive</span><span class=\"dt-v\">De Smet, 1879</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pa's house</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1887</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 November 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wounded Knee</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 December 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"South Dakota"},"facts":[["Capital","Pierre"],["Population","924,669 (2024)"],["Largest city","Sioux Falls"],["Total area","199,729 km² (77,116 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>South Dakota</b> is the 17th largest of the American states, covering 199,729 square kilometres (77,116 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 925,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Pierre and its largest city Sioux Falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Mount Rushmore State, it became the 40th state on 2 November 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ingalls family reached De Smet in 1879, after homesteading had failed for them in Wisconsin and Kansas, and Charles Ingalls kept time for the railway then being built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their first year there is the subject of <i>By the Shores of Silver Lake</i>, and <i>The Long Winter</i> and <i>Little Town on the Prairie</i> follow the family to 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He gave up farming for carpentry and put up a storey-and-a-half frame house in the town in 1887, the last home of his wife and of his daughter Mary, whose blindness gave her a part in the books, since much of what Laura set down she had first described aloud to her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On 29 December 1890, on the Pine Ridge reservation in the south-west of the state, the United States Army's Seventh Cavalry and the followers of the Miniconjou chief Big Foot met at Wounded Knee Creek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The site's nomination records that the event is called the Wounded Knee Massacre or the Battle of Wounded Knee according to the perspective of the writer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> New laws and a run of commissions had lately taken great tracts of land from the Lakota, and they and other tribes confined to reservations had turned to the Ghost Dance to hold on to their own way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The army's guns stood on the hill above the camp; many of the Miniconjou died in the dry ravine below it and others got away along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A party from the Pine Ridge agency found eleven wounded survivors on 1 January 1891, a burial party laid 146 men, women and children in a mass grave on the hill, and the survivors dedicated a granite monument over it on 28 May 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “South Dakota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/south-dakota-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ingalls House, De Smet, South Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75001717_text. [Open access]","Richard E. Jensen and Eli Paul, “Wounded Knee Battlefield, Pine Ridge Indian Reservation, South Dakota,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, National Park Service, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000719_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Flag_of_South_Dakota.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Dakota.svg)","alt":"A mid-blue flag with the state seal at its centre inside a gold sunburst, ringed by the words SOUTH DAKOTA and THE MOUNT RUSHMORE STATE."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Mount_Rushmore_detail_view_%28100MP%29.jpg/1920px-Mount_Rushmore_detail_view_%28100MP%29.jpg","title":"Mount Rushmore, South Dakota","desc":"Mount Rushmore.","credit":"Thomas Wolf , www.foto-tw.de, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Rushmore_detail_view_(100MP).jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Rushmore."}},
{"id":"geo-545","num":545,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Denver","answerText":"Denver","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Denver City</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded spring 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Auraria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">laid out October 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One city</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the act of 1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mint building</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1897 – 1904</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Colorado","dot":"Denver"},"facts":[["State","Colorado"],["Population","729,019 (2024)"],["Largest city","Denver"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Denver</b> is the capital of Colorado and its largest city, with 729,019 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the spring of 1858 General William E. Larimer Jr. founded Denver City on a jumped claim, with four cabins, one at each corner of Larimer and Fifteenth Streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fifteenth Street, called F Street until late in the century, had been the military trail from Santa Fe and Fort Union up to the trading posts north of the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across Cherry Creek, an Indian trader named John T. Smith gave up his claim at the mouth of the creek for a nominal fee on 29 October 1858, and the town of Auraria was laid out with eight cabins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A territorial act of 1864 deeded the ground to the town fathers and made one city of Auraria, Denver and Highland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Congress had created the Territory of Colorado in February 1861, and on 21 April 1862 it provided for a branch mint at Denver for the coinage of gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Treasury bought a private mint at Sixteenth and Market Streets from Clark, Gruber & Company and opened it in September 1863, but it struck no coin at all: bullion was melted, refined, assayed, stamped and handed back to whoever had brought it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From 1869 it did less still, only melting, assaying and returning, and not until 1895 did an act provide for a United States Mint at Denver for the coinage of gold and silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A site at the corner of Colfax Avenue and Delaware Street was bought on 22 April 1896, and the new building went up intermittently under James Knox Taylor from 1897 to 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The House of Lions on Pennsylvania Street, usually called the Molly Brown House, is what its nomination calls an emotional landmark of Coloradans as much as a historical one, standing for the rags-to-riches turn in the state's early years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Larimer Square, Denver, Colorado,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000468_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Auraria 9th Street Historic District, Denver, Colorado,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000466_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Denver Mint, Denver, Colorado,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72000270_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Molly Brown House, Denver, Colorado,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72000269_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Flag_of_Denver%2C_Colorado.svg","credit":"Margaret Overbeck, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Denver,_Colorado.svg)","alt":"The flag of Denver: on navy, a white and red zigzag of mountain peaks across the lower half with a gold disc for the sun above."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Denver%2C_Colorado_skyline_%28cropped_3x5%29.jpg/1920px-Denver%2C_Colorado_skyline_%28cropped_3x5%29.jpg","title":"Denver","desc":"Denver, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denver,_Colorado_skyline_(cropped_3x5).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Denver."}},
{"id":"geo-049","num":49,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"North Dakota","answerText":"North Dakota","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dakota Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">organised in 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 November 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">League in power</span><span class=\"dt-v\">elections of 1916</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mill opens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 October 1922</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"North Dakota"},"facts":[["Capital","Bismarck"],["Population","796,568 (2024)"],["Largest city","Fargo"],["Total area","183,108 km² (70,698 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>North Dakota</b> is the 19th largest of the American states, covering 183,108 square kilometres (70,698 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 797,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Bismarck and its largest city Fargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It and South Dakota were both part of Dakota Territory, organised in 1861, and each became a state on 2 November 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Long before that the Hidatsa lived in earthlodge villages along the Knife River, and the one called Big Hidatsa was occupied until about 1850.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It covers about 6 hectares (15 acres), is crossed by ditches thrown up as parts of it were abandoned or enlarged, and is thought to hold the best defined lodge depressions of any large village on the Great Plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It stood among the villages near Fort Mandan, where Lewis and Clark passed the winter of 1804 to 1805, and Charbonneau and Sacagawea came from another Hidatsa village about 2.4 kilometres (a mile and a half) to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Farmers here had complained since the Farmers Alliance of the 1890s that they were at the mercy of the milling and railway companies of <b>Minnesota</b>, which docked them for wet or impure grain and charged heavily to carry it away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Bills to buy the state a grain elevator passed in 1893, 1909 and 1911 and came to nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Nonpartisan League, founded in 1915, made a state-owned mill one of the main planks of its platform, won the elections of 1916, and carried its programme through the legislature of 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That programme also produced the Bank of North Dakota, a state hail insurance scheme and a homebuilding plan, and its first mill, at Drake, ground flour from the middle of August 1919 and closed in 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Building at Grand Forks began in 1920 and stopped that autumn, and Governor R. A. Nestos pressed a button on 30 October 1922 to start the mill that is still the only one owned by an American state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “North Dakota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/north-dakota. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Big Hidatsa Village Site, Mercer County, North Dakota,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000600_text. [Open access]","Steven R. Hoffbeck, “North Dakota Mill and Elevator, Grand Forks, North Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, National Park Service, 1991, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/92000433_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Flag_of_North_Dakota.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Dakota.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a bald eagle with spread wings holding an olive branch and arrows, a fan of gold rays and thirteen stars above it, and NORTH DAKOTA on a red scroll below."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/View_of_Theodore_Roosevelt_National_Park.jpg/1920px-View_of_Theodore_Roosevelt_National_Park.jpg","title":"Theodore Roosevelt National Park, North Dakota","desc":"Theodore Roosevelt National Park.","credit":"NPS/Mark Hoffman, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Theodore_Roosevelt_National_Park.jpg","alt":"A view of Theodore Roosevelt National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-546","num":546,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Topeka","answerText":"Topeka","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Town settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Convention</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 October – 11 November 1855</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1855</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Curtis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">vice president, 1929 – 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Kansas","dot":"Topeka"},"facts":[["State","Kansas"],["Population","125,467 (2024)"],["Largest city","Wichita"],["Rank in state","5th largest"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Topeka</b> is the capital of Kansas and the seat of Shawnee County, the fifth largest city in the state with 125,467 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was settled in December 1854,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> three months after Anthony Ward brought his family to a farm about 1.6 kilometres (a mile) to the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Constitution Hall, begun as a shop in 1855, was the town's first stone building, and the Free State convention was called to order in its unplastered upper room on 23 October and sat until 11 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The constitution framed there drew on those of New York and Ohio, prohibited slavery and adopted homestead protection, and it passed by 1,731 votes to 46 in a popular election on 15 December 1855.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Congress rejected it and President <b>Franklin Pierce</b> condemned it, and on 4 July 1856, with John Brown and his partisans looking on, federal troops dispersed the Free State legislature as it sat in the hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Kansas entered the Union as a free state on 29 January 1861<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> under the Wyandotte Constitution, for which the Topeka document set the course.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charles Curtis was born in North Topeka on 25 January 1860, and after his mother died he was brought up partly by his father's parents in the town and partly on the Kaw reservation in Morris County.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His grandmother there, Julie Conville Pappan, was a granddaughter of the Kaw chief White Plume; Curtis reported for the North Topeka Times from 1877, read law in a Topeka office, and was elected county attorney for Shawnee County in 1884.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He sat in the House of Representatives from 1893 to 1907 and in the Senate from 1907 to 1929, apart from two years from 1913, and was influential in Indian and farm legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1929 he became the first vice president from west of the Mississippi, the first from Kansas and the first of Native American ancestry, and he retired from office after the ticket of <b>Herbert Hoover</b> and Curtis was beaten in 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Constitution Hall—Topeka, Topeka, Kansas,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/08000669_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ward-Meade House, Topeka, Kansas,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75000727_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Kansas: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/kansas-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Charles Curtis House, Topeka, Kansas,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000778_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/Flag_of_Topeka%2C_Kansas.svg","credit":"The Greater Topeka Partnership, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Topeka,_Kansas.svg)","alt":"The flag of Topeka: a white band at the hoist against a blue field, overlaid by a large gold sunflower with a blue centre and one green petal, and a white five-pointed star towards the fly."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/View_from_Kansas_State_Capitol_dome%2C_looking_east.jpg/1920px-View_from_Kansas_State_Capitol_dome%2C_looking_east.jpg","title":"Topeka","desc":"Topeka, Kansas, seen from a distance.","credit":"Mike Linksvayer, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Kansas_State_Capitol_dome,_looking_east.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Topeka, Kansas."}},
{"id":"geo-050","num":50,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Iowa","answerText":"Iowa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cornerstone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Old Capitol, 4 July 1840</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 December 1846</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amana founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1855</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to Des Moines, 1857</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Iowa"},"facts":[["Capital","Des Moines"],["Population","3,241,488 (2024)"],["Largest city","Des Moines"],["Total area","145,746 km² (56,273 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Iowa</b> is the 26th largest of the American states, covering 145,746 square kilometres (56,273 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 3.24 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with Des Moines its capital and its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It takes its name from the Iowa people who lived there, and it became a state on 28 December 1846.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The building on the campus at Iowa City called the Old Capitol was raised in the <b>Greek Revival</b> manner as the third territorial capitol, and was the first permanent state capitol from 1846 to 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its architect, John Francis Rague, had won the $200 prize for the first <b>Illinois</b> capitol at Springfield in 1837 and sent in plans for this one in 1839; the cornerstone was laid on 4 July 1840 and he resigned nine days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Iowa's first governor was inaugurated there, its first six General Assemblies met there and the state constitution was drafted there, and when the legislature left for Des Moines in 1857 the University of Iowa took it over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1714 a group in <b>Germany</b> withdrew from the Lutheran Church and called itself the Community of True Inspiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Persecuted at home, 800 of them followed Christian Metz to the United States between 1843 and 1846, founded six villages near Buffalo in New York and two in <b>Canada</b>, and agreed to hold all property in common except their clothes and household goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By 1854 the spread of Buffalo was pressing on them, and they began moving to Iowa, where Amana, a name meaning 'remaining true', was founded in 1855.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Middle, High, West, South and East Amana followed over the next seven years, and they bought the town of Homestead outright to give themselves a railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Incorporated under Iowa law in 1859 as the Amana Society and governed by thirteen elected elders, it was the most successful and longest lived of the utopian communities founded in 19th-century America, and it exists still in altered form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Iowa: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/iowa-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Capitol, Iowa City, Iowa,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72000475_text. [Open access]","Blanche H. Schroer and Ray H. Mattison, “Amana Colonies, Iowa,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, National Park Service, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000336_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Flag_of_Iowa.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iowa.svg)","alt":"Three vertical bands, blue, white and red, the white bearing an eagle carrying a blue streamer that reads OUR LIBERTIES WE PRIZE AND OUR RIGHTS WE WILL MAINTAIN, with IOWA in red beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Loess_Hills_0633.jpg/1920px-Loess_Hills_0633.jpg","title":"the Loess Hills, Iowa","desc":"The Loess Hills.","credit":"Chris Light, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loess_Hills_0633.jpg","alt":"Steep grass-covered hills rolling away under a wide sky."}},
{"id":"geo-547","num":547,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Lincoln","answerText":"Lincoln","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Goodhue dies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tower finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unicameral</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first sat in 1937</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Nebraska","dot":"Lincoln"},"facts":[["State","Nebraska"],["Population","300,619 (2024)"],["Largest city","Omaha"],["Rank in state","2nd largest"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Lincoln</b> is the capital of Nebraska<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and, with 300,619 residents, the second largest city in the state after Omaha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capitol, begun in 1922 and finished in 1932, was designed by Bertram Grosvenor Goodhue, who had spent twenty years building in the Gothic manner with Ralph Adams Cram before breaking away in 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His first study was wholly classical, and by 1919, having seen Eliel Saarinen's work, he had arrived at a tall tower over low wings, a classical skyscraper meant as a new American style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wings were built round the old statehouse of 1888, which came down in 1925; the tower, 122 metres (400 feet) high, was finished in 1930, and Lee Lawrie's Sower, 5.8 metres (19 feet) tall, was hoisted on to the top.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hartley Burr Alexander, professor of philosophy at the state university, wrote the sculptural programme and chose its inscriptions, which run from Moses on Sinai to Nebraska's admission to the Union, with every county's name carved round the base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The body that meets in it is unlike any other state legislature in the country, and George Norris made it so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Norris began practising law in Nebraska in 1885, sat in the House from 1902 and in the Senate from 1912 to 1943, a Republican who called himself an independent and said he would rather be right than regular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He wore out two sets of tyres driving about the state to campaign for a single chamber, arguing that two houses merely copied a Parliament of Commons and Lords in a country that recognises one class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The amendment carried in 1934 by 286,086 votes to 193,152 and made the legislature nonpartisan as well, so that a candidate's party is not printed on the ballot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the new body first sat in 1937 its membership had fallen from 133 to 43 and its committees from 61 to 18, and its session ran 98 days against the last two-chamber one's 110.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nebraska State Capitol, Lincoln, Nebraska,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000372_text. [Open access]","Nebraska Legislature, “History of the Nebraska Unicameral,” Nebraska Legislature, https://nebraskalegislature.gov/about/history_unicameral.php. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Senator George W. Norris House, McCook, Nebraska,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/67000006_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Nebraska: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/nebraska-state-guide. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/Flag_of_Lincoln%2C_Nebraska_%282022%29.svg","credit":"Ed Mejia, American Marketing Association Lincoln, Lincoln Young Professionals Group, City of Lincoln, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lincoln,_Nebraska_%282022%29.svg)","alt":"The flag of Lincoln: a teal upper half over a dark green lower half, with tan rays fanning out from a central round emblem of quartered arcs and upright lines around a small white star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Nighttime_skyline_of_downtown_Lincoln%2C_Nebraska%2C_USA_%282015%2C_from_Arnold_Heights_Park%29.jpg/1920px-Nighttime_skyline_of_downtown_Lincoln%2C_Nebraska%2C_USA_%282015%2C_from_Arnold_Heights_Park%29.jpg","title":"Lincoln","desc":"Lincoln, Nebraska, seen from a distance.","credit":"Hanyou23, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nighttime_skyline_of_downtown_Lincoln,_Nebraska,_USA_(2015,_from_Arnold_Heights_Park).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Lincoln, Nebraska."}},
{"id":"geo-548","num":548,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Pierre","answerText":"Pierre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plate buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 March 1743</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oahe Mission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1873</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plate found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1913</span><span class=\"dt-k\">St. Charles</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 1 January 1911</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"South Dakota","dot":"Pierre"},"facts":[["State","South Dakota"],["Population","13,788 (2024)"],["Largest city","Sioux Falls"],["Rank in state","11th largest"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Pierre</b> is the capital of South Dakota<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and, with 13,788 residents, the second smallest state capital in the country and the eleventh largest city in its own state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On a hill at Fort Pierre, across the Missouri, François and Louis-Joseph de La Vérendrye buried a lead plate on 30 March 1743.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It bore the names of Louis XV of <b>France</b>, the Marquis de Beauharnois, viceroy of French <b>Canada</b>, their father Pierre and themselves, and it was meant to found a French claim to the upper Missouri and draw <b>Louisiana</b> as the whole Mississippi drainage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were among the first Europeans to reach the middle of the continent, 61 years before Lewis and Clark, and their journeys were written off at the time for finding no passage to the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plate lay there until 1913, when children playing on the hill turned it up, and it has been with the South Dakota State Historical Society ever since; a granite monument marks the hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> North of the town the Oahe Mission was founded in 1873 as the headquarters of missionary work among the Sioux, and in 1877 the Reverend S. R. Riggs built its chapel from lumber brought up the river from Yankton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It opened a boarding school for Indian children in 1883 and another for girls in the 1890s, and though the mission closed in 1914 services were held there in the Sioux language until 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pierre's own prospects were pressed hardest by Charles L. Hyde, who arrived in 1887, traded land and put up nine brick business blocks expecting the town to become a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He opened the St. Charles Hotel on 1 January 1911 at a reception given by the governor, and until the governor's mansion was finished in the 1940s it housed several of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That same year Hyde was indicted for fraudulent advertising through the mails, and the case went as far as the White House before President <b>William Howard Taft</b> pardoned him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Verendrye Site, Fort Pierre, South Dakota,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001899_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Oahe Chapel, Pierre, South Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/80003725_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “St. Charles Hotel, Pierre, South Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/80003724_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “South Dakota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/south-dakota-state-guide. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Flag_of_Pierre%2C_South_Dakota.png","credit":"Unknown author , colorized by John G. Moisan, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pierre,_South_Dakota.png)","alt":"The flag of Pierre: on white, a round seal in green and gold showing a sunrise over hills and a steamboat on a river, lettered CITY OF PIERRE above and INCORPORATED 1883 below."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Downtown_Pierre%2C_South_Dakota.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Pierre%2C_South_Dakota.jpg","title":"Pierre","desc":"Downtown Pierre, South Dakota.","credit":"WeaponizingArchitecture, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Pierre,_South_Dakota.jpg","alt":"A wide main street of low storefronts."}},
{"id":"geo-549","num":549,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Bismarck","answerText":"Bismarck","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Camp Hancock</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 April 1872</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tribune</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first issue, 11 July 1873</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Custer news</span><span class=\"dt-v\">telegraphed 5 July 1876</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1889, with Bismarck the capital</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"North Dakota","dot":"Bismarck"},"facts":[["State","North Dakota"],["Population","77,772 (2024)"],["Largest city","Fargo"],["Rank in state","2nd largest"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Bismarck</b> is the capital of North Dakota<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and, with 77,772 residents, the second largest city in the state after Fargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army set up Camp Hancock on the line of the Northern Pacific Railway on 16 April 1872, though no troops arrived until 8 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The post guarded the building of the railway and was held by companies D and H of the 17th Infantry until 1877, after which it served as a supply depot for Fort Abraham Lincoln and the posts further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its military life ended on 16 April 1894, when the War Department handed the ground to the Department of Agriculture and the weather station moved in; the two-storey supply building of 1872 still stands and appears to be the oldest in the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The railway reached the Missouri a month after Colonel Clement A. Lounsberry arrived in May 1873 to open a three-man newspaper office, having settled long before on publishing where the Northern Pacific would cross the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The first Bismarck Tribune came off a hand press on 11 July 1873, promising its best efforts towards building up the town, and it is still the oldest newspaper in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lounsberry's correspondent Mark Kellogg rode with George Custer to cover the campaign of 1876 and was one of the few civilians killed at the Little Bighorn on 25 June, where Custer and 261 men died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The steamer Far West brought the news down the Yellowstone, and on 5 July Lounsberry telegraphed it to the New York Herald and brought out a special edition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Marshall H. Jewell, who bought the paper in 1884, campaigned for statehood for nearly twelve years and got it in 1889, with Bismarck as the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Northern Pacific's own depot, put up after the fire of 8 August 1898 had levelled much of the centre, was designed by Charles Reed and Allen Stem of St. Paul in a Spanish mission manner rare on the northern plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Camp Hancock Site, Bismarck, North Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72001004_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Bismarck Tribune Building, Bismarck, North Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/82001309_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Northern Pacific Railway Depot, Bismarck, North Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/77001022_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “North Dakota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/north-dakota. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Flag_of_Bismarck%2C_North_Dakota.svg","credit":"original by Mark Kenneweg; vectorization by Flagvisioner, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bismarck,_North_Dakota.svg)","alt":"The flag of Bismarck: a gold border round a field of white over red, with BISMARCK in blue capitals whose A is a gold star, NORTH DAKOTA in blue on a gold ribbon, and a blue disc at the hoist bearing a white star in a gold wreath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/94/Aerial_Photo_of_the_city_of_Bismarck._Looking_Northeast.jpg","title":"Bismarck","desc":"Bismarck, North Dakota, seen from a distance.","credit":"North Dakota Department of Transportation, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_Photo_of_the_city_of_Bismarck._Looking_Northeast.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Bismarck, North Dakota."}},
{"id":"geo-550","num":550,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Des Moines","answerText":"Des Moines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to Des Moines, 1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Uncle Henry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836 – 1916</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wallaces' Farmer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1895</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Historical Building</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898 – 1910</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Iowa","dot":"Des Moines"},"facts":[["State","Iowa"],["Population","213,096 (2024)"],["Largest city","Des Moines"],["Rank in state","largest"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Des Moines</b> is the capital of <b>Iowa</b><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and its largest city, with 213,096 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legislature moved there from the Old Capitol at Iowa City in 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The capitol complex stands above the meeting of the Des Moines and Raccoon rivers, and across Grand Avenue from the capitol is the State Historical Building, put up in two campaigns between 1898 and 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Oliver O. Smith designed it in a Beaux-Arts classicism, low and spread out where the capitol is compact and vertical, so that it answers the older building without competing with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The firm of Proudfoot, Bird and Rawson dominated architecture in the state for thirty years from 1900, putting up thirty buildings at Ames, eighteen at the University of Iowa and five at Northern Iowa, as well as the Hotel Fort Des Moines, once the largest in Iowa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Henry Wallace, born on a farm near West Newton in <b>Pennsylvania</b> on 19 March 1836, edited the Iowa Homestead from 1885 and in 1895 helped found Wallaces' Farmer, which he edited until his death in 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He argued for scientific farming, did much to make Iowa State a serious agricultural research institution, and pressed to get its findings out to farmers through extension work, institutes and trains fitted up for teaching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1908 <b>Theodore Roosevelt</b> put him on the Country Life Commission, beside Gifford Pinchot and Liberty Hyde Bailey, to examine the whole farm problem from soil waste to spiritual waste; it held thirty hearings and reported in 1909.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1911 he presided over the third National Conservation Congress at Kansas City and gave its keynote address.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Consulted by President <b>William Howard Taft</b> about whether to keep his Secretary of Agriculture, he wired his own and other Midwestern papers to raise a campaign for the man, and Taft named an otherwise new cabinet and kept him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Iowa: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/iowa-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Capitol, Iowa City, Iowa,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72000475_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Iowa State Historical Building, Des Moines, Iowa,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78001251_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Hotel Fort Des Moines, Des Moines, Iowa,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/82002636_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wallace, Henry, House, Des Moines, Iowa,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/93000412_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/03/Flag_of_Des_Moines%2C_Iowa.svg","credit":"Mysid, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Des_Moines,_Iowa.svg)","alt":"The flag of Des Moines: a red wedge at the hoist, and across the navy field three white bands each carrying a row of navy semicircular arches."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Morning_Skyline_-_Des_Moines%2C_Iowa_-_Winter_on_the_Des_Moines_River_%2824805016620%29.jpg/1920px-Morning_Skyline_-_Des_Moines%2C_Iowa_-_Winter_on_the_Des_Moines_River_%2824805016620%29.jpg","title":"Des Moines","desc":"Des Moines, Iowa, seen from a distance.","credit":"Tony Webster from Minneapolis, Minnesota, United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morning_Skyline_-_Des_Moines,_Iowa_-_Winter_on_the_Des_Moines_River_(24805016620).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Des Moines, Iowa."}},
{"id":"gw-101","num":101,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sierra Leone","answerText":"Sierra Leone","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1787</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1896</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 September 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sierra Leone"},"facts":[["Capital","Freetown"],["Population","8.8 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","71,740 km² (27,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","sierra leone"],"abstract":"<b>Sierra Leone</b> began as a settlement for freed slaves, on a peninsula the British bought in 1787 from the Temne ruler King Nimbana and ran through the Sierra Leone Company, founded by the abolitionists who had campaigned against the slave trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The black poor from England, labourers from Nova Scotia, Maroons and recaptives were landed there, and by 1800 their descendants formed a distinct group with a language of its own, the Creoles or Krios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Crown Colony established in 1808 covered only that settlement and its surroundings; the far larger interior was not brought under a protectorate until 1896.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Chiefs objected, and their protests culminated in the Hut Tax War of 1898, a revolt led by Bai Bureh of Kasseh against a levy on the size of a homestead; Britain crushed it and kept the tax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A constitution of 1947 amalgamated the two territories in preparation for independence, after which party allegiance replaced the colonial divide as the sharpest line in politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Independence came on 27 April 1961, and the United States recognised the new state in a message from President John F. Kennedy dated the day before, raising its consulate general to an embassy the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade three weeks later, joined the Commonwealth that year, and was admitted to the United Nations in September at the Assembly's 1018th plenary meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The One Party Act of 1978 closed off independent political alternatives, and armed conflict broke out on 23 March 1991 when forces crossed from Liberia into the town of Bomaru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Security Council answered with a peacekeeping mission in October 1999, a ban on imports of the country's rough diamonds in July 2000, and a request a month later that the Secretary-General negotiate an independent special court with the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Its two international measures of area differ by about 560 square kilometres, the United Nations profile giving the larger of the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sierra Leone,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sl.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sierra Leone,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sierra-leone. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Sierra Leone,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/sierra-leone. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Sierra Leone and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/sierra_leone_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Sierra Leone to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1623 (XVI), adopted at the 1018th plenary meeting, 27 September 1961, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205641. [Open access]","Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Sierra Leone, “Historical Antecedents to the Conflict,” in <i>Witness to Truth: Report of the Sierra Leone Truth and Reconciliation Commission</i>, vol. 3A, chap. 1 (Freetown, 2004), https://www.sierraleonetrc.org/index.php/view-the-final-report/download-table-of-contents/volume-three-a/item/witness-to-the-truth-volume-three-a-chapter-1?category_id=13. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1270 (1999) on Establishment of the UN Mission in Sierra Leone,” adopted at the 4054th meeting, 22 October 1999, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/287753. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1306 (2000) on Sanctions against Import of Diamonds from Sierra Leone,” adopted at the 4168th meeting, 5 July 2000, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/417948. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1315 (2000) on Establishment of a Special Court for Sierra Leone,” adopted at the 4186th meeting, 14 August 2000, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/420605. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Flag_of_Sierra_Leone.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sierra_Leone.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sierra Leone: three horizontal bands of green, white and blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Tiwa_Island_-_flickr.jpg/1920px-Tiwa_Island_-_flickr.jpg","title":"Tiwai Island, Sierra Leone","desc":"Tiwai Island.","credit":"Dorothy Voorhees, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiwa_Island_-_flickr.jpg","alt":"A broad river running past dense rainforest."}},
{"id":"gw-102","num":102,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Togo","answerText":"Togo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">German rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1884</span><span class=\"dt-k\">French mandate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Togo"},"facts":[["Capital","Lomé"],["Population","8.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","56,785 km² (21,925 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","togo"],"abstract":"<b>Togo</b> is the eastern part of a German colony that was later cut in two: Togo and Cameroon were placed under German rule in July 1884, in a short-lived turn to a more active colonial policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Germany lost the territory in the First World War, and the line dividing it between British and French administration was fixed by a declaration signed on 10 July 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> France governed the eastern portion under a League of Nations mandate defined by an instrument of 20 July 1922, which allowed it to be run as an integral part of French territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The General Assembly then placed that portion under the international trusteeship system at the 62nd plenary meeting of its first session, on 13 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The western portion took a different road: a plebiscite was held there on 9 May 1956 under United Nations supervision, and the Assembly recorded that a majority had voted for union with an independent Gold Coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> It approved that union in December 1956 and ended the trusteeship on the day the Gold Coast became independent, so the British-administered half left with Ghana in March 1957.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> For the French portion the Assembly noted in December 1959 that France and Togoland had agreed on 27 April 1960 as the date of independence, and recommended admission to the United Nations upon it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Attorney General William Rogers carried President Eisenhower's message of recognition to Prime Minister Sylvanus Olympio that day, and the American consulate at the capital was raised to an embassy under Jesse M. MacKnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Admission came at the Assembly's 864th plenary meeting that September, and the trading system followed, with the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 20 March 1964 and the World Trade Organization from 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in June 2022, its application approved by heads of government meeting at Kigali, more than sixty years after the half Britain administered had left to become part of Ghana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Togo,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Togo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/togo. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Togo,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/togo. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Togo and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/togo_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Togolese Republic to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1477 (XV), adopted at the 864th plenary meeting, 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205971. [Open access]","United Nations, <i>Trusteeship Agreement for the Territory of Togoland under French Administration as Approved by the General Assembly on 13 December 1946</i>, T/Agreement/7 (Lake Success, NY: United Nations, 1947), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3996001. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “The Future of Togoland under British Administration,” resolution 1044 (XI), adopted at the 619th plenary meeting, 13 December 1956, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207747. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Date of the Independence of the Trust Territory of Togoland under French Administration,” resolution 1416 (XIV), adopted at the 846th plenary meeting, 5 December 1959, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206571. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Kolonialpolitik,” LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/kaiserreich/aussenpolitik/kolonien.html. [Open access]","John Dring, <i>Report by the Plebiscite Administrator on the Plebiscite Held in Togoland under United Kingdom Administration on 9 May 1956</i>, T/1269 (New York: United Nations, 6 July 1956), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3932223. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Flag_of_Togo.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Togo.svg)","alt":"The flag of Togo: five green and yellow stripes with a white star on a red canton."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Togo_Taberma_house_02.jpg/1920px-Togo_Taberma_house_02.jpg","title":"Koutammakou, Togo","desc":"Koutammakou.","credit":"Erik Kristensen, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Togo_Taberma_house_02.jpg","alt":"A view of Koutammakou."}},
{"id":"gw-103","num":103,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Laos","answerText":"Laos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1949, within the French Union</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1975</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Laos"},"facts":[["Capital","Vientiane"],["Population","7.9 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","236,800 km² (91,430 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","laos"],"abstract":"<b>Laos</b> had been a protectorate of France and became an independent state within the French Union in 1949, which is where its modern statehood begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the kingdom on 7 February 1950 in an announcement by its State Department, and opened consular relations that April with a vice consul at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those first officers were posted to several places at once, being assigned to Saigon and Phnom Penh as well, so the mission was for years part of one Indochinese establishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on 29 July 1950, when Donald R. Heath presented his credentials to the king while resident at Saigon and accredited to Cambodia and Vietnam too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A legation of its own opened on 22 August 1950 and was raised to an embassy by a joint announcement of the two governments on 10 August 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Four months later the General Assembly admitted the country at its 555th plenary meeting, by a resolution headed simply Admission of New Members and carried with 56 votes in favour and one abstention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The proclamation of the Lao People's Democratic Republic in December 1975 altered the relationship rather than ending it, the American chief of mission being downgraded from ambassador to chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That post was redesignated chargé d'affaires on 9 January 1987, and an ambassador returned only when Charles B. Salmon Jr. presented his credentials on 6 August 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Accession to the World Trade Organization came later still, on 2 February 2013, nearly forty years after the kingdom the Americans had recognised ceased to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its two international counts of population agree almost exactly and both put it just under 7.9 million, and the two published measures of its area are identical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Lao People’s Democratic Republic,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/la.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Laos,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/laos. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Lao People’s Democratic Republic and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/lao_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Lao PDR,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LAO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Flag_of_Laos.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Laos.svg)","alt":"The flag of Laos: red bands above and below a broad blue band bearing a white disc."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/The_river_of_Kuang_si_waterfalls.jpg","title":"Kuang Si Falls, Laos","desc":"Kuang Si Falls.","credit":"NruasPaoYPP, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_river_of_Kuang_si_waterfalls.jpg","alt":"A view of Kuang Si Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-104","num":104,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Hong Kong","answerText":"Hong Kong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Joint Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 December 1984</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Transfer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1997</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Hong Kong"},"facts":[["Status","Special Administrative Region of China"],["Population","7.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Asia"],["Area","1,107 km² (427 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","territory","asia","hong kong"],"abstract":"<b>Hong Kong</b> was a British colony, and the United States had a consular post in it by 1843, more than a century and a half before it was handed to China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its future was settled by treaty rather than by war: the joint declaration of the two governments on the question of Hong Kong was signed at Peking on 19 December 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Parliament gave effect to it in the Hong Kong Act 1985, whose first section provides that from 1 July 1997 the Crown should no longer have sovereignty or jurisdiction over any part of the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That section was to come into force on the exchange of instruments of ratification of the declaration, so an Act of one parliament was made to wait upon a treaty with another state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Basic Law that now governs it declares the region an inalienable part of China and authorises a high degree of autonomy, with executive, legislative and independent judicial power including final adjudication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It also provides that the socialist system shall not be practised there and that the previous capitalist system and way of life shall remain unchanged for fifty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The territory had already entered the world trading system in its own right, acceding to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 23 April 1986 while still a British dependency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It became a founding member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995, two and a half years before the transfer, and sits there as Hong Kong, China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its own census counted 7,413,070 people in 2021 against 7,336,585 five years earlier, a rise of about one per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The international counts differ by roughly a hundred thousand, and the World Bank's series has fallen in each of the last two years rather than risen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “China, Hong Kong SAR,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: China,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/china. [Open access]","Hong Kong Act 1985, c. 15, s. 1, The National Archives, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1985/15/section/1/enacted. [Open access]","Constitutional and Mainland Affairs Bureau, “The Basic Law of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the People’s Republic of China, Chapter I: General Principles,” Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, https://www.basiclaw.gov.hk/en/basiclaw/chapter1.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Hong Kong, China and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/hong_kong_china_e.htm. [Open access]","Census and Statistics Department, “2021 Population Census,” Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, https://www.censtatd.gov.hk/en/scode600.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Hong Kong SAR, China,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HKG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Flag_of_Hong_Kong.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hong_Kong.svg)","alt":"The flag of Hong Kong: a white five-petalled bauhinia flower on a red field."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Hong_Kong_island_and_Victoria_Harbour.jpg/1920px-Hong_Kong_island_and_Victoria_Harbour.jpg","title":"Victoria Harbour, Hong Kong","desc":"Victoria Harbour.","credit":"PrzemekJaczewski, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hong_Kong_island_and_Victoria_Harbour.jpg","alt":"A dense harbour city of towers seen from above."}},
{"id":"gw-105","num":105,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Turkmenistan","answerText":"Turkmenistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Neutrality</span><span class=\"dt-v\">recognised 12 December 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turkmenistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Ashgabat"],["Population","7.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Central Asia"],["Area","491,210 km² (189,660 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","turkmenistan"],"abstract":"<b>Turkmenistan</b> was a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and the United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, in the address President George H. W. Bush made to his nation on the dissolution of that union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 19 February 1992, announced in a press statement, and an American embassy opened at the capital on 17 March with Jeffrey White as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That record gives the city as Ashkabad, the spelling then in use, where the United Nations profile now carries the form the country itself uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it without a vote at its 82nd meeting on 2 March 1992, three months after the union it had belonged to ceased to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four years later that body recognised and supported the permanent neutrality the country had declared, at its 90th plenary meeting on 12 December 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The same resolution called on member states to respect and support that status, and to respect the country's independence, sovereignty and territorial integrity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its published size has a step in it: the World Bank's surface-area series carries one figure for every year to 2018 and a figure about 3,100 square kilometres larger from 2019 onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations profile still gives the earlier of the two, which is why the two official sources differ over how large the country is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In February 2007 the two governments agreed to look for ways of cooperating on political and human-rights reform, on economic and agricultural reform, and on education, health care, energy and security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its population has grown by about a fifth in a decade, from 6.2 million in 2015, and the two international counts of it now agree to within a thousand people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Turkmenistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Turkmenistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/turkmenistan. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Turkmenistan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/229, adopted without vote at the 82nd meeting, 2 March 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/283190. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Maintenance of International Security,” resolution 50/80 A, adopted at the 90th plenary meeting, 12 December 1995, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/239930. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Turkmenistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TKM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Turkmenistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TKM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Flag_of_Turkmenistan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Turkmenistan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Turkmenistan: a green field with a white crescent and five stars beside a vertical carpet-pattern stripe."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Darvasa_gas_crater_panorama.jpg/1920px-Darvasa_gas_crater_panorama.jpg","title":"Darvaza gas crater, Turkmenistan","desc":"Darvaza gas crater.","credit":"Tormod Sandtorv, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darvasa_gas_crater_panorama.jpg","alt":"A view of Darvaza gas crater."}},
{"id":"gw-106","num":106,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Libya","answerText":"Libya","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ottoman province</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1711 – 1835</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Italian colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1912 – 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 December 1951</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Libya"},"facts":[["Capital","Tripoli"],["Population","7.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Area","1,759,540 km² (679,362 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","libya"],"abstract":"<b>Libya</b> was a semi-independent province of the Ottoman Empire from 1711 to 1835, and Tripoli conducted its own foreign relations until the Ottomans reasserted their formerly nominal suzerainty in that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had formally recognised the independence of the United States by a peace treaty signed at Tripoli on 4 November 1796, which the Dey of Algiers also signed on a claim to authority that the pasha denied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second treaty of 10 June 1805 was concluded without that supervision; an American consul had arrived in 1799 and the consulate closed at the outbreak of the Tripolitan War two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Ottoman Empire ceded the territory to Italy by the Treaty of Ouchy on 18 October 1912, and it stayed an Italian colony until 1947, under joint British and French occupation from 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It declared independence on 24 December 1951 as the United Kingdom of Libya, and President Truman sent a message of recognition to King Idris I on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Admission to the United Nations took four years more and came only as part of a package: on 14 December 1955 the Assembly took in sixteen states at once, this one among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Cuba's request each was voted on separately, and the president of that sitting recorded that the Assembly had accepted every one of the sixteen the Security Council had recommended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Security Council returned to the country in 2011, imposing an arms embargo at its 6491st meeting on 26 February and banning flights over Libyan airspace on 17 March by ten votes with five abstentions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United States had suspended its embassy at the capital the day before the first of those votes, as it had done from 1980 to 2006 without ever formally severing relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two international measures of its area differ by more than 83,000 square kilometres, a gap of about five per cent, and the larger of them is the World Bank's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Libya,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ly.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Libya,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/libya. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Libya,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBY/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “555th Plenary Meeting, Wednesday, 14 December 1955,” Official Records of the Tenth Session, A/PV.555, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/646501. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1970 (2011) on the Situation in the Libyan Arab Jamahiriya,” adopted at the 6491st meeting, 26 February 2011, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/698927. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1973 (2011) on a Ban on Flights in the Libyan Arab Jamahiriya Airspace,” adopted at the 6498th meeting, 17 March 2011, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/699777. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Flag_of_Libya.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Libya.svg)","alt":"The flag of Libya: red, black and green bands with a white crescent and star at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Leptis_Magna_%2829%29_%288288918733%29.jpg/1920px-Leptis_Magna_%2829%29_%288288918733%29.jpg","title":"Leptis Magna, Libya","desc":"Leptis Magna.","credit":"joepyrek from Richmond, Va, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leptis_Magna_(29)_(8288918733).jpg","alt":"A view of Leptis Magna."}},
{"id":"gw-107","num":107,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kyrgyzstan","answerText":"Kyrgyzstan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 February 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 December 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kyrgyzstan"},"facts":[["Capital","Bishkek"],["Population","7.3 million (2025)"],["Region","Central Asia"],["Area","199,949 km² (77,201 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","kyrgyzstan"],"abstract":"<b>Kyrgyzstan</b> had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and the United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, in the address President George H. W. Bush made to his nation on the dissolution of that union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition and diplomatic relations were announced together in that one speech, so the relationship begins with a broadcast rather than with credentials or an exchange of notes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy opened at the capital on 1 February 1992 under Edmund McWilliams as chargé d'affaires ad interim, and Edward Hurwitz presented his credentials as the first ambassador on 17 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country without a vote at its 82nd meeting on 2 March 1992, under a resolution headed with the name it then used, the Republic of Kyrgyzstan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The succession in that post has been unbroken since, the department's own database showing chargés d'affaires filling every gap between one ambassador and the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Accession to the World Trade Organization followed on 20 December 1998, less than seven years after the union it had belonged to dissolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sits in that body under a different name from the one it took its United Nations seat with, appearing there as the Kyrgyz Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its population has grown by more than a fifth in a decade, from about 6.04 million in 2015, and is rising by well over a hundred thousand a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The two international counts of it differ by about 48,000 people, well under one per cent, and their two measures of its area differ by a single square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Roughly one person in seven lives in the capital, which the United Nations puts at a little over a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Kyrgyzstan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kyrgyzstan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kyrgyzstan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Chiefs of Mission for Kyrgyzstan,” Principal Officers and Chiefs of Mission, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/people/chiefsofmission/kyrgyzstan. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Kyrgyzstan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/225, adopted without vote at the 82nd meeting, 2 March 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/283186. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Kyrgyz Republic and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/kyrgyz_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kyrgyz Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KGZ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Flag_of_Kyrgyzstan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kyrgyzstan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Kyrgyzstan: a red field bearing a yellow sun crossed by the roof-poles of a yurt."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Issyk-Kul%2C_Kyrgyzstan_%2843713946455%29.jpg/1920px-Issyk-Kul%2C_Kyrgyzstan_%2843713946455%29.jpg","title":"Issyk-Kul, Kyrgyzstan","desc":"Issyk-Kul.","credit":"Ninara from Helsinki, Finland, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Issyk-Kul,_Kyrgyzstan_(43713946455).jpg","alt":"A wide mountain lake with a sandy beach in front of bare hills."}},
{"id":"gw-108","num":108,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Paraguay","answerText":"Paraguay","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 May 1811</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1852</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 November 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1942</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Paraguay"},"facts":[["Capital","Asunción"],["Population","7 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","406,752 km² (157,048 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","paraguay"],"abstract":"<b>Paraguay</b> declared its independence from Spain on 15 May 1811, and the United States waited 41 years before recognising it as a separate and independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That recognition took the form of a full power issued on 27 April 1852 to John M. Pendleton, the American chargé d'affaires at Buenos Aires, to negotiate a treaty of commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and an American legation at the capital began nine years later, on 26 November 1861, when Commissioner Charles A. Washburn presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation was raised to an embassy by a joint announcement of the two governments on 4 January 1942, the change taking effect on 31 March when the Paraguayan ambassador presented his letters of credence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wesley Frost, promoted from minister, presented his own credentials a fortnight after that, on 15 April 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its United Nations membership dates from 24 October 1945, which makes it an original member of the organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 6 January 1994, less than a year before that agreement's successor came into being, and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly half its people live in one place: the United Nations counts 3.28 million in Greater Asunción, which it defines as the capital's own district together with nineteen districts of Central Department.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against a national figure the two international sources put within a hundred people of each other, that is about 47 in every hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They agree on its area to the square kilometre as well, and its population has risen steadily by a little under ninety thousand a year since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Paraguay,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/py.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Paraguay,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/paraguay. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Paraguay and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/paraguay_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Paraguay,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRY/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Paraguay,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRY/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/Flag_of_Paraguay.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Paraguay.svg)","alt":"The flag of Paraguay: red, white and blue bands with an emblem at the centre of the white."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/La_Sant%C3%ADsima_Trinidad_de_Paran%C3%A1_-_Church_ruins.jpg/1920px-La_Sant%C3%ADsima_Trinidad_de_Paran%C3%A1_-_Church_ruins.jpg","title":"the Jesuit missions of the Guaranis, Paraguay","desc":"The church ruins at La Santísima Trinidad de Paraná.","credit":"P. Hughes, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Sant%C3%ADsima_Trinidad_de_Paran%C3%A1_-_Church_ruins.jpg","alt":"Red stone church ruins standing on cropped grass."}},
{"id":"gw-109","num":109,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nicaragua","answerText":"Nicaragua","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">joined 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 November 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 December 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1909</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nicaragua"},"facts":[["Capital","Managua"],["Population","7 million (2025)"],["Region","Central America"],["Area","130,374 km² (50,338 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","central america","nicaragua"],"abstract":"<b>Nicaragua</b> joined the Federation of Central American States in 1823, after independence from Spain and the Mexican Empire, alongside Guatemala, Honduras, Costa Rica and El Salvador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised that federation rather than its members, President Monroe receiving Antonio José Cañaz as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary on 4 August 1824.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It withdrew from the federation on 5 November 1838, and eleven years passed before it was recognised on its own account, when President Polk received the chargé d'affaires Eduardo Carcache on 24 December 1849.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American mission in the country opened on 18 February 1851, and relations were severed on 1 December 1909 after President Zelaya ordered the execution of two American citizens who had aided a rebellion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Marines landed at Bluefields soon afterwards, Zelaya resigned within a fortnight, and the Marines then stayed in the country until 1932 apart from nine months in 1925 and 1926.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were restored on 21 February 1911 and interrupted again in 1926, when the American minister told General Chamorro that no government taking power by force would be recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chamorro claimed the presidency in January of that year and never won it; recognition went instead to Adolfo Díaz, whom a reconstituted Congress elected in November and whom the United States accepted within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was withheld once more in 1947, when Anastasio Somoza García seized control and the Nicaraguan ambassador was received in Washington only in a private capacity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has belonged to the United Nations since 24 October 1945, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 28 May 1950, and joined the World Trade Organization on 3 September 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two international counts of its people agree to within five hundred, and their measures of its area differ by four square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Nicaragua,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ni.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nicaragua,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/nicaragua. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Nicaragua and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/nicaragua_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Nicaragua,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NIC/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Nicaragua,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NIC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Flag_of_Nicaragua.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nicaragua.svg)","alt":"The flag of Nicaragua: blue, white and blue bands with the national coat of arms at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Ometepe_Island_with_Concepcion_Volcano_-_From_Lake_Nicaragua_-_Nicaragua_-_01_%2831377352020%29.jpg/1920px-Ometepe_Island_with_Concepcion_Volcano_-_From_Lake_Nicaragua_-_Nicaragua_-_01_%2831377352020%29.jpg","title":"Ometepe, Nicaragua","desc":"Ometepe and the Concepción volcano.","credit":"Adam Jones from Kelowna, BC, Canada, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ometepe_Island_with_Concepcion_Volcano_-_From_Lake_Nicaragua_-_Nicaragua_-_01_(31377352020).jpg","alt":"A cloud-capped volcano rising from a lake."}},
{"id":"gw-110","num":110,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Serbia","answerText":"Serbia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">FRY declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 March 1999</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 2000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Union ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 June 2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Serbia"},"facts":[["Capital","Belgrade"],["Population","6.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","88,444 km² (34,148 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","serbia"],"abstract":"<b>Serbia</b> was one of the two republics that declared a new Federal Republic of Yugoslavia in April 1992, after Slovenia, Croatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina and Macedonia had seceded from the old federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 21 May 1992 the United States announced that it would not recognise that new state as the successor to the Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, which is what the two republics claimed it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American ambassador was recalled from the capital while the mission stayed open under a chargé d'affaires, and relations were severed on 23 March 1999, one day before the NATO bombing began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly then admitted the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia as a new member on 1 November 2000, without a vote at its 48th meeting, so the state took a seat rather than inheriting one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eleven days later an exchange of letters between Presidents Clinton and Kostunica established full diplomatic relations, and the embassy at the capital reopened in May 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The federation became Serbia and Montenegro in 2003, Montenegro invoked its right to secede and declared independence on 3 June 2006, and two days later Serbia declared itself the successor to the union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kosovo's own declaration of independence, adopted on 17 February 2008, was referred by the General Assembly to the International Court of Justice in October of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Serbia filed a written statement, written comments and replies to the judges' questions in those proceedings, and in July 2010 the Court held that the declaration had not violated international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Court described Security Council resolution 1244 of 10 June 1999 as having established a temporary and exceptional legal régime that superseded the Serbian legal order on an interim basis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations counts its people expressly without Kosovo but marks its area with no such note, and its population is falling: the World Bank's series is down more than half a million since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Serbia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/rs.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Serbia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/serbia. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 55/12, adopted without vote at the 48th meeting, 1 November 2000, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/426129. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Accordance with International Law of the Unilateral Declaration of Independence in Respect of Kosovo,” Overview of the Case, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/141. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Serbia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SRB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/Flag_of_Serbia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Serbia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Serbia: red, blue and white bands with the national coat of arms toward the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/P037152-661920_-_Belgrade_fortress_stand_at_the_downtown_of_Belgrade.jpg/1920px-P037152-661920_-_Belgrade_fortress_stand_at_the_downtown_of_Belgrade.jpg","title":"Belgrade Fortress, Serbia","desc":"Belgrade Fortress.","credit":"Oliver Bunic, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P037152-661920_-_Belgrade_fortress_stand_at_the_downtown_of_Belgrade.jpg","alt":"A view of Belgrade Fortress."}},
{"id":"gw-111","num":111,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bulgaria","answerText":"Bulgaria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 October 1908</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 May 1909</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bulgaria"},"facts":[["Capital","Sofia"],["Population","6.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","110,996 km² (42,856 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","bulgaria"],"abstract":"<b>Bulgaria</b> had diplomatic relations with the United States for six years before it was recognised by it, which is the reverse of the usual order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John B. Jackson, the American minister to Greece, Romania and Serbia, presented his credentials at the capital on 19 September 1903, and that date marks the establishment of relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition waited on independence, which was declared on 5 October 1908; on 3 May 1909 Secretary of State Knox instructed a chargé to convey President Taft's congratulations on the country's admission to the community of sovereign and independent states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An earlier revolt against Ottoman rule in 1876 had produced two treaties in quick succession, San Stefano establishing a large Bulgarian state and Berlin in 1878 replacing it with a smaller principality north of the Balkan Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A consular agency opened at the capital on 12 January 1912 reporting to Bucharest, became a consulate general in 1915, and an American legation followed on 18 March 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were severed when the country declared war on the United States on 13 December 1941, though the United States did not declare war in return until 5 June 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Roosevelt believed it would not have declared war without pressure from Nazi Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955, one of the sixteen states the General Assembly took in together at its 555th plenary meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Accession to the World Trade Organization came on 1 December 1996 and to the European Union on 1 January 2007, and the euro area followed on 1 January 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its population is falling fast, the World Bank's series down about 550,000 since 2015, and the United Nations count sits some 280,000 above the two European ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bulgaria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bulgaria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bulgaria. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Bulgaria and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/bulgaria_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Bulgaria,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/bulgaria_en. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “555th Plenary Meeting, Wednesday, 14 December 1955,” Official Records of the Tenth Session, A/PV.555, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/646501. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bulgaria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BGR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/Flag_of_Bulgaria.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bulgaria.svg)","alt":"The flag of Bulgaria: horizontal bands of white, green and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Rila_Monastery%2C_August_2013.jpg/1920px-Rila_Monastery%2C_August_2013.jpg","title":"Rila Monastery, Bulgaria","desc":"Rila Monastery.","credit":"Raggatt2000, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rila_Monastery,_August_2013.jpg","alt":"A view of Rila Monastery."}},
{"id":"gw-112","num":112,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"El Salvador","answerText":"El Salvador","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declared 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 June 1863</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"El Salvador"},"facts":[["Capital","San Salvador"],["Population","6.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Central America"],["Area","21,041 km² (8,124 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","central america","el salvador"],"abstract":"<b>El Salvador</b> declared independence from Spain in 1821 along with the other Central American provinces, and joined the United Provinces of Central America formed two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised that federation rather than its members, President Monroe receiving its envoy on 4 August 1824.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The union broke up between 1838 and 1840, and the country was recognised separately on 1 May 1849, when a full power and a letter of credence were issued to E. George Squier, the American chargé to Guatemala.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had declared itself an independent republic in 1839, and diplomatic relations and an American legation both began at the capital on 15 June 1863 under James R. Partridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations then passed briefly to a second union, when in September 1896 Honduras, Nicaragua and Salvador agreed to form the Greater Republic of Central America to exercise their external sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The United States received a minister from that body on 24 December 1896, President Cleveland noting that the responsibilities of the individual republics towards the United States remained wholly unaffected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The union was dissolved on 29 November 1898 and the states promptly resumed conducting their own relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were interrupted again on 4 December 1931, when the United States declined to recognise a new revolutionary government under a treaty of 1923 about governments coming to power by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has belonged to the United Nations since 24 October 1945, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 22 May 1991, and joined the World Trade Organization on 7 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two international counts of its people agree to within five hundred, and their measures of its area differ by a single square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “El Salvador,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sv.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: El Salvador,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/el-salvador. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “El Salvador and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/el_salvador_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: El Salvador,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): El Salvador,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Flag_of_El_Salvador.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_El_Salvador.svg)","alt":"The flag of El Salvador: blue, white and blue bands with the national coat of arms at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Santa_Ana_Volcano.USAF.C-130.1.jpg","title":"Santa Ana Volcano, El Salvador","desc":"Santa Ana Volcano.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Ana_Volcano.USAF.C-130.1.jpg","alt":"A view of Santa Ana Volcano."}},
{"id":"gw-113","num":113,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Republic of the Congo","answerText":"Republic of the Congo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Free France</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Brazzaville, 1940 – 43</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1965</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Congo"},"facts":[["Capital","Brazzaville"],["Population","6.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Area","342,000 km² (132,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","republic of the congo"],"abstract":"The <b>Republic of the Congo</b> was part of French Equatorial Africa, and during the Second World War its capital became the symbolic capital of Free France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence from that federation was proclaimed on 15 August 1960, and President Eisenhower sent a message of recognition to President Fulbert Youlou on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and an American embassy at the capital began on that date as well, with Alan W. Lukens as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country without a vote a month later, under a resolution headed with the name that tells it apart from its neighbour, the Republic of the Congo at Brazzaville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Relations were then disrupted for twelve years: the United States withdrew its diplomatic and consular representatives in August 1965, citing arrest, detention, incommunicado interrogation and forced departure of its officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Congolese embassy in Washington closed on 8 August 1965 and moved to New York, and the American embassy at the capital closed once its own staff had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two governments agreed to resume relations on 7 June 1977, and the embassy reopened on 30 October that year with Jay Katzen as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations stayed distant until the end of the socialist era and improved after the country adopted multi-party politics in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 3 May 1963, three years after independence, and joined the World Trade Organization on 27 March 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is growing fast, up by more than a quarter in a decade, and more than a third of it now lives in the capital's urban area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Congo,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Republic of the Congo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/congo-republic. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Congo and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/congo_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of the Congo (Brazzaville) to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1486 (XV), adopted without vote at the 864th plenary meeting, 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/663483. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Congo, Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Republic of the Congo: a yellow diagonal band between green and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Loxodonta_cyclotis_29048923.jpg/1920px-Loxodonta_cyclotis_29048923.jpg","title":"Nouabalé-Ndoki National Park, the Republic of the Congo","desc":"Nouabalé-Ndoki National Park.","credit":"Matt Muir, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loxodonta_cyclotis_29048923.jpg","alt":"A view of Nouabalé-Ndoki National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-114","num":114,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Singapore","answerText":"Singapore","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 August 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 April 1966</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Singapore"},"facts":[["Capital","Singapore"],["Population","6 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","728 km² (281 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","singapore"],"abstract":"<b>Singapore</b> is a city-state whose capital and country are the same place, and the American consulate there is older than the state by well over a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joseph Balestier was appointed consul on 4 July 1836, at a port American merchants in the China trade had been calling at on their way to and from Canton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island had been under British sovereignty since the early 19th century, became independent of the British Empire in 1963, joined the Federation of Malaysia, and broke away in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the new state on 11 August 1965 in a letter from Secretary of State Dean Rusk to the Singaporean foreign minister, S. Rajaratnam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth the same year on leaving that federation, and the United Nations admitted it on 21 September 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> An American embassy followed on 4 April 1966 under Richard H. Donald as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It belonged to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 20 August 1973 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country is getting physically larger: the World Bank's surface-area series records 719 square kilometres for 2015 and 728 for 2023, so the reclamation of land from the sea shows up as arithmetic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its three published areas differ, the Commonwealth giving 720 where the United Nations and the World Bank agree with each other, so any figure for it carries a date as well as a number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two international counts of its people differ by about 240,000, and the United Nations puts all but a few thousand of them inside the capital city itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Singapore,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Singapore,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/singapore. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Singapore,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/singapore. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Singapore and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/singapore_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Singapore,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SGP/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Singapore,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SGP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Flag_of_Singapore.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Singapore.svg)","alt":"The flag of Singapore: a red band above a white one, with a crescent and five stars in the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Supertree_Grove%2C_Gardens_by_the_Bay%2C_Singapore_-_20120712-02.jpg/1920px-Supertree_Grove%2C_Gardens_by_the_Bay%2C_Singapore_-_20120712-02.jpg","title":"Gardens by the Bay, Singapore","desc":"Gardens by the Bay.","credit":"Shiny Things ., CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Supertree_Grove,_Gardens_by_the_Bay,_Singapore_-_20120712-02.jpg","alt":"A view of Gardens by the Bay."}},
{"id":"gw-115","num":115,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Denmark","answerText":"Denmark","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 June 1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 October 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1827</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1973</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Denmark"},"facts":[["Capital","Copenhagen"],["Population","6 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","42,938 km² (16,579 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","denmark"],"abstract":"<b>Denmark</b> recognised the United States rather than the other way about, the American consul at the capital receiving his exequatur from the Danish government on or about 9 June 1792.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That same act established consular relations, and diplomatic relations followed on 12 October 1801, when the Danish minister resident presented his credentials in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation opened at the capital on 20 September 1827 under Henry Wheaton as chargé d'affaires, and the two governments have not severed relations since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation did close for the war: German forces occupied the city on 9 April 1940, the American minister left on 5 June, and the legation itself shut on 20 December 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took up residence at Ottawa as minister to a Danish government that remained in Danish hands until German authorities seized complete control in August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legation reopened on 16 June 1945 after the country's liberation, and the relationship since has been close enough for the United States to keep an early-warning radar facility at Thule in Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1917 Denmark had sold the Danish West Indies to the United States, and those islands are now the United States Virgin Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was among the original members of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 28 May 1950, and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the European Union on 1 January 1973 and negotiated an opt-out from the euro, so it keeps the krone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its published area and population expressly exclude the Faroe Islands and Greenland, which the United Nations shows separately, and the three sources for its size differ by only eighteen square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Denmark,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Denmark,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/denmark. [Open access]","European Union, “Denmark,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/denmark_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Denmark and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/denmark_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Denmark,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DNK/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Flag_of_Denmark.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Denmark.svg)","alt":"The flag of Denmark: a white cross offset toward the hoist on a red field."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/The_Nyhavn_Canal_3.jpg/1920px-The_Nyhavn_Canal_3.jpg","title":"Nyhavn, Denmark","desc":"Nyhavn.","credit":"European Commission, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Nyhavn_Canal_3.jpg","alt":"A view of Nyhavn."}},
{"id":"gw-116","num":116,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lebanon","answerText":"Lebanon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">French mandate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">approved 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 September 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 November 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 October 1952</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Lebanon"},"facts":[["Capital","Beirut"],["Population","5.8 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","10,452 km² (4,036 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","lebanon"],"abstract":"<b>Lebanon</b> was part of the Ottoman Empire when the United States became independent, and its foreign relations were conducted through the Sultan's government at Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After that empire collapsed the League of Nations approved a French mandate for Lebanon and Syria in 1922, which gave France exclusive control of their foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States appointed George Wadsworth Agent and Consul General on 9 October 1942, a quasi-diplomatic presence at the capital while it decided whether the country was effectively independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition then came by an exchange of letters: on 7 September 1944 that agent offered full and unconditional recognition upon written assurances about American rights, and the Lebanese foreign minister gave them the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wadsworth presented his credentials as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary on 16 November 1944, and the legation was raised to an embassy on 3 October 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The United States withdrew every member of its embassy staff on 6 September 1989, after supporters of a faction in the civil war surrounded the building and stopped it functioning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy reopened on 29 November 1990, when a new ambassador presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has belonged to the United Nations since 24 October 1945, and in August 2006 the Security Council called unanimously for a full cessation of hostilities, by Hizbollah of all attacks and by Israel of all offensive military operations, and strengthened its interim force there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population fell and then rose again: the World Bank's series runs from 6.47 million in 2015 down to 5.70 in 2020 and back up in every year since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two international sources agree on the present figure to within five hundred people, and their measures of its area differ by two square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Lebanon,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lb.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Lebanon,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/lebanon. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Lebanon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Lebanon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1701 (2006) on the Full Cessation of Hostilities in Lebanon,” adopted at the 5511th meeting, 11 August 2006, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/581053. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Flag_of_Lebanon.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lebanon.svg)","alt":"The flag of Lebanon: red bands above and below a white one bearing a green cedar."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Temple_of_Bacchus%2C_Baalbek%2C_Lebanon_%2849890013476%29.jpg/1920px-Temple_of_Bacchus%2C_Baalbek%2C_Lebanon_%2849890013476%29.jpg","title":"Baalbek, Lebanon","desc":"Baalbek.","credit":"Lodo from Moscow, Russia, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Bacchus,_Baalbek,_Lebanon_(49890013476).jpg","alt":"A view of Baalbek."}},
{"id":"gw-117","num":117,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Finland","answerText":"Finland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 May 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 April 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Finland"},"facts":[["Capital","Helsinki"],["Population","5.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","338,363 km² (130,643 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","finland"],"abstract":"<b>Finland</b> declared its independence from the Russian Empire on 6 December 1917, shortly after the Bolshevik revolution, and a civil war followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 7 May 1919 in a letter from Secretary of State Robert Lansing to the Finnish foreign minister Rudolf Holsti, having held back over concerns about the country's political instability and its ties with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 27 May 1919, when the acting secretary of state accepted Armas Herman Saastamoinen as provisional envoy of the de facto government, President Wilson being away at the Paris peace conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation opened at Helsingfors on 19 March 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were severed on 30 June 1944 and the legation shut the same day, the State Department pointing to the Finnish government's admission of a military partnership with Germany against the Allies. <br><br> A special mission opened at Helsinki on 16 January 1945 after the armistice, full relations resumed on 1 September 1945, and the legation became an embassy on 15 November 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations only on 14 December 1955, ten years after that organisation was founded, and the European Union on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has been in the euro area since 1999 and the Schengen area since 2001, and it had joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 25 May 1950 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Military non-alignment ended after Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine: it applied on 18 May 2022 and deposited its instrument of accession on 4 April 2023, becoming the alliance's 31st member on the anniversary of the day the treaty was signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its own figures have to be read twice, because the United Nations profile counts the autonomous Åland Islands in the population it prints and excludes them from the surface area printed beneath it, where Eurostat gives 338,363 square kilometres and 5,635,971 people for the whole country and the World Bank 5,646,436 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Finland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fi.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Finland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/finland. [Open access]","European Union, “Finland – EU country profile,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/finland_en. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “NATO member countries,” https://www.nato.int/en/about-us/organization/nato-member-countries. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Finland and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/finland_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Finland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FIN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Suomenlinna_aerial.JPG","title":"Suomenlinna, Finland","desc":"Suomenlinna.","credit":"Migro, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suomenlinna_aerial.JPG","alt":"A view of Suomenlinna."}},
{"id":"gw-118","num":118,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Liberia","answerText":"Liberia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1847</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 September 1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 February 1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 2016</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Liberia"},"facts":[["Capital","Monrovia"],["Population","5.7 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","111,369 km² (43,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","liberia"],"abstract":"<b>Liberia</b> began in 1822, when the American Colonization Society founded a settlement there for freedmen and recaptured slaves, and in 1847 that settlement constituted itself as a republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several European states recognised it and the United States did not, and in his annual message of December 1861 Abraham Lincoln told Congress that if any good reason existed for persevering longer in withholding recognition of the independence and sovereignty of Haiti and Liberia, he was unable to discern it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Unwilling to inaugurate what he called a novel policy without the approval of Congress, he asked it to consider paying for a chargé d’affaires near each of the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Recognition followed on 23 September 1862, when the American minister to England, Charles F. Adams, was empowered to conclude a treaty of commerce and navigation with the republic, which he and the Liberian president signed on 25 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American legation at Monrovia were established on 23 February 1864, when commissioner and consul general Abraham Hanson presented his credentials. <br><br> The legation was raised to embassy status on 6 May 1949 under Ambassador Edward R. Dudley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has belonged to the United Nations since 2 November 1945 and joined the World Trade Organization on 14 July 2016, long after the members that founded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its civil wars brought the Security Council in: on 19 September 2003 it established a mission of up to 15,000 military personnel and demanded that the Liberian parties meet their obligations under the Comprehensive Peace Agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That agreement was adopted at Accra on 18 August 2003, and the Act establishing the country’s Truth and Reconciliation Commission calls it the formal end of the civil strife and wars, giving the commission the period from January 1979 to 14 October 2003 to investigate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its population is rising quickly, the World Bank’s series running from 4,659,431 in 2015 to 5,731,206 in 2025, while the two published figures for its area differ by a single square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Liberia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Liberia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/liberia. [Open access]","Abraham Lincoln, “First Annual Message,” 3 December 1861, The American Presidency Project, University of California, Santa Barbara, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/first-annual-message-9. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Liberia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Liberia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Liberia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/liberia_e.htm. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1509 (2003) on the Establishment of the United Nations Mission in Liberia,” adopted at the 4830th meeting, 19 September 2003, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/502506. [Open access]","Republic of Liberia, “An Act to Establish the Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Liberia,” enacted 12 May 2005, Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Liberia, https://trcofliberia.org/about/trc-mandate.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Loxodontacyclotis.jpg/1920px-Loxodontacyclotis.jpg","title":"Sapo National Park, Liberia","desc":"Sapo National Park.","credit":"Thomas Breuer, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loxodontacyclotis.jpg","alt":"A view of Sapo National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-119","num":119,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Norway","answerText":"Norway","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 October 1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO founder</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 April 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy to Oslo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Norway"},"facts":[["Capital","Oslo"],["Population","5.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","323,772 km² (125,009 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","norway"],"abstract":"<b>Norway</b> was transferred from Danish to Swedish rule by the Treaty of Kiel in 1814, and accepted a union with Sweden under a common monarch while keeping its own constitution and national assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American consulates had opened at Christiania, which took the name Oslo in 1925, and at Christiansand in 1809, nearly a century before independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It demanded a flag and a consular service of its own to promote its maritime commerce, and when Sweden would not concede, the assembly declared the union at an end on 7 June 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sweden accepted, a treaty of separation was signed on 26 October, and Secretary of State Elihu Root told the Norwegian foreign minister on 30 October that the United States recognised the separation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same message established relations by stating that Christian Hauge would be received as chargé d'affaires, and the first treaty between the two countries had been signed in 1893, while the union still stood. <br><br> Germany invaded on 9 April 1940, the government left Oslo for England, and the American legation closed on 15 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A legation near the Norwegian government opened in London on 2 August 1940 and became an embassy in May 1942 under Anthony J. Drexel Biddle Jr., who was accredited also to seven other exiled governments, and it moved back to Oslo on 31 May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 27 November 1945, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 10 July 1948 and the World Trade Organization at its founding on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was one of the twelve countries whose foreign ministers signed the North Atlantic Treaty in Washington on 4 April 1949, and its own, Halvard M. Lange, later helped draft the alliance's report on non-military cooperation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How large it is depends on what is counted: the United Nations gives 323,772 square kilometres and footnotes its population as including Svalbard and Jan Mayen, where the World Bank gives 624,500, nearly twice as much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Norway,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/no.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Norway,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/norway. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Norway,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NOR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “NATO member countries,” https://www.nato.int/en/about-us/organization/nato-member-countries. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Norway and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/norway_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Geirangerfjord_.jpg/1920px-Geirangerfjord_.jpg","title":"Geirangerfjord, Norway","desc":"Geirangerfjord.","credit":"Andreas Trepte, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geirangerfjord_.jpg","alt":"A view of Geirangerfjord."}},
{"id":"gw-120","num":120,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Slovakia","answerText":"Slovakia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Czechoslovakia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 October 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 December 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Slovakia"},"facts":[["Capital","Bratislava"],["Population","5.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","49,035 km² (18,933 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","slovakia"],"abstract":"<b>Slovakia</b> was part of the Kingdom of Hungary under the Dual Monarchy of 1867 to 1918, while Bohemia and Moravia belonged to its Austrian half; the two were joined in one state only in 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Czecho-Slovak National Council in Paris as a de facto government at war with the German and Austro-Hungarian empires on 3 September 1918, and it declared independence on 18 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What that war made was not recognised in the next: when German forces occupied Prague in March 1939 the United States refused to recognise either the protectorate over Bohemia and Moravia or the establishment of a Slovak state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate general opened at Bratislava in 1947 and shut in 1950, after the communist government alleged that American diplomatic personnel were engaged in espionage and demanded their numbers be cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The federation ended by negotiation: Slovakia declared itself sovereign in July 1992, talks with the Czech Republic followed, and on 31 December 1992 the Czech and Slovak Federal Republic ceased to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 1 January 1993 the two became separate states and the United States recognised both that day, saying an ambassador would be appointed shortly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American embassy at Bratislava followed three days later under Paul Hacker as chargé d’affaires, and the first ambassador, Theodore E. Russell, presented his credentials that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 19 January 1993, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade that 15 April, and the World Trade Organization at its founding on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined NATO on 29 March 2004 with six other countries, the largest wave of enlargement in the alliance’s history, and the European Union that 1 May, entering the Schengen area in 2007 and the euro area in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations and Eurostat give exactly the same area, 49,035 square kilometres, and different populations: 5,475 thousand, which the United Nations footnotes as a projection, against Eurostat’s 5,419,451 and the World Bank’s 5,413,813.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Slovakia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Czechoslovakia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/czechoslovakia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Slovakia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/slovakia. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “NATO member countries,” https://www.nato.int/en/about-us/organization/nato-member-countries. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The Slovak Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/slovak_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Slovakia – EU country profile,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/slovakia_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Slovak Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SVK/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Spissky_Hrad-LV-2.jpg/1920px-Spissky_Hrad-LV-2.jpg","title":"Spiš Castle, Slovakia","desc":"Spiš Castle.","credit":"Pierre Bona, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spissky_Hrad-LV-2.jpg","alt":"A view of Spiš Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-121","num":121,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ireland","answerText":"Ireland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 July 1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1973</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ireland"},"facts":[["Capital","Dublin"],["Population","5.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","69,825 km² (26,960 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","ireland"],"abstract":"<b>Ireland</b> gained independence from the United Kingdom on 6 December 1921, when representatives of the two states signed the Anglo-Irish Treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That agreement gave independence to 26 counties, constituted as the Irish Free State, while six counties of northeast Ulster chose to remain part of the United Kingdom with limited self-government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American recognition took two and a half years more and passed through the former sovereign: on 24 June 1924 the British ambassador in Washington asked the United States to approve his government’s plan for an Irish minister to handle matters relating exclusively to the Free State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Secretary of State Charles E. Hughes replied on 28 June that the President would be pleased to receive a duly accredited minister plenipotentiary of the Free State, which recognised it as a state with autonomous control over its foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 7 October 1924, when Timothy A. Smiddy presented his credentials in Washington, and the American legation at Dublin opened on 27 July 1927 under Frederick A. Sterling. <br><br> The connections between the two countries date to the mid-19th century, when a large number of Irish immigrants came to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations at the 555th plenary meeting of the General Assembly on 14 December 1955, in the same admission as Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 22 December 1967 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined what is now the European Union on 1 January 1973 and the euro area on 1 January 1999, and it has negotiated an opt-out from the Schengen area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its published sizes disagree by 455 square kilometres, at 69,825 for the United Nations, 69,947 for Eurostat and 70,280 for the World Bank, and so do its populations, which run from a projected 5,308 thousand through 5,439,898 to 5,484,367.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ireland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ie.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ireland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ireland. [Open access]","European Union, “Ireland – EU country profile,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/ireland_en. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “555th Plenary Meeting, Wednesday, 14 December 1955,” Official Records of the Tenth Session, A/PV.555, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/646501. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Ireland and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/ireland_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Ireland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IRL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Ireland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IRL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Cliffs-Of-Moher-OBriens-From-South.JPG/1920px-Cliffs-Of-Moher-OBriens-From-South.JPG","title":"Cliffs of Moher, Ireland","desc":"Cliffs of Moher.","credit":"Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cliffs-Of-Moher-OBriens-From-South.JPG","alt":"A view of Cliffs of Moher."}},
{"id":"gw-122","num":122,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Central African Republic","answerText":"Central African Republic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ubangi-Shari</span><span class=\"dt-v\">French colony to 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 February 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Central African Rep."},"facts":[["Capital","Bangui"],["Population","5.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Area","622,984 km² (240,535 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","central african republic"],"abstract":"The <b>Central African Republic</b> was the French colony of Ubangi-Shari, where an administration resting on a handful of officials and soldiers granted possession of the Ubangi to more than forty concession companies, which divided it between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Porterage was what made that colony work: the carrying of loads and of people took on a scale it had never had before in the first years of European rule, the object being to link the Ubangi basin with the basin of Lake Chad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Independence from French Equatorial Africa was proclaimed on 13 August 1960, and the United States recognised the country the same day in a congratulatory message from President Eisenhower to Prime Minister David Dacko.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on that date as well, when the American consul at Brazzaville, Alan W. Lukens, presented his letter of credence as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy opened at Bangui on 10 February 1961, with Lukens as resident chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations without a vote at its 864th plenary meeting on 20 September 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 3 May 1963 and the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Armed conflict brought the Security Council in: at its 7153rd meeting on 10 April 2014 it unanimously established a multidimensional integrated stabilisation mission in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its people are thinly spread, at about 8.8 to the square kilometre across 622,984 square kilometres, and the United Nations gives its income per head as 366 US dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population is growing fast all the same: the World Bank’s series runs from 4,629,320 in 2015 to 5,513,282 in 2025, up nearly a fifth in ten years, and the two published figures for 2025 agree to the person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Central African Republic,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cf.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Central African Republic,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/central-african-republic. [Open access]","Maurice Guimendego, review of <i>Des affaires et des hommes: Noirs et Blancs, commerçants et fonctionnaires dans l’Oubangui du début du siècle</i>, by Jean Cantournet, <i>Cahiers d’études africaines</i> 32, no. 128 (1992): 715–17, https://www.persee.fr/doc/cea_0008-0055_1992_num_32_128_1538_t1_0715_0000_1. [Open access]","Pierre Mollion, “Le portage en Oubangui-Chari, 1890-1930,” <i>Revue d’histoire moderne et contemporaine</i> 33, no. 4 (1986): 542–68, https://doi.org/10.3406/rhmc.1986.1377. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Central African Republic to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1488 (XV), adopted at the 864th plenary meeting, 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206138. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The Central African Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/central_african_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 2149 (2014) on the Establishment of the United Nations Multidimensional Integrated Stabilization Mission in the Central African Republic,” adopted unanimously at the 7153rd meeting, 10 April 2014, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/768393. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Central African Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAF/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Dzanga_Bai_forest_clearing_with_African_forest_elephants%2C_Central_African_Republic.jpg/1920px-Dzanga_Bai_forest_clearing_with_African_forest_elephants%2C_Central_African_Republic.jpg","title":"Dzanga-Sangha, the Central African Republic","desc":"Dzanga Bai in the Dzanga-Sangha reserve.","credit":"JosepMGracia, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dzanga_Bai_forest_clearing_with_African_forest_elephants,_Central_African_Republic.jpg","alt":"Forest elephants gathered in a clearing at the edge of rainforest."}},
{"id":"gw-123","num":123,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"New Zealand","answerText":"New Zealand","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 September 1907</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">joined 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 February 1942</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statute adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 November 1947</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"New Zealand"},"facts":[["Capital","Wellington"],["Population","5.3 million (2025)"],["Region","Oceania"],["Area","268,107 km² (103,517 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","new zealand"],"abstract":"<b>New Zealand</b> was raised from a colony to a self-governing Dominion of the British Empire on 26 September 1907, sovereign in its domestic affairs while the United Kingdom kept control of its relations with the wider world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Balfour Declaration of 1926 recognised the United Kingdom and the Dominions as autonomous communities within the empire, equal in status and in no way subordinate one to another in any aspect of their domestic or external affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Kingdom put that into law in the Statute of Westminster of December 1931, whose terms would not take effect in a Dominion until that Dominion’s own government adopted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the country as independent, with autonomous control of its foreign relations, on 16 February 1942, when the New Zealand envoy Walter Nash presented his credentials to President Roosevelt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The statute was not adopted at home until the Statute of Westminster Adoption Act became law on 25 November 1947, five years after the recognition it would have justified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An American legation opened at Wellington on 1 April 1942 and was raised to an embassy in December 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat dates its membership to 1931 and the Statute of Westminster, so three bodies give three dates for one transition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polynesian settlers reached both main islands about 1280, dated by radiocarbon on the bones and gnawed seeds of the Pacific rat they brought with them, a millennium later than earlier chronologies proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Treaty of Waitangi, signed in 1840, is the nation’s founding agreement, celebrated and argued over ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was an original member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 30 July 1948 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization; its United Nations population expressly excludes the Cook Islands, Niue and Tokelau, and stands at 5,252,000 where the World Bank counts 5,324,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “New Zealand,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: New Zealand,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/new-zealand. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “New Zealand,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/new-zealand. [Open access]","Museum of New Zealand Te Papa Tongarewa, “The Treaty of Waitangi,” https://www.tepapa.govt.nz/discover-collections/read-watch-play/maori/treaty-waitangi. [Open access]","Janet M. Wilmshurst, Atholl J. Anderson, Thomas F. G. Higham, and Trevor H. Worthy, “Dating the Late Prehistoric Dispersal of Polynesians to New Zealand Using the Commensal Pacific Rat,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 22 (2008): 7676–80, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0801507105. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “New Zealand and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/new_zealand_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: New Zealand,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NZL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Milford_Sound_%28New_Zealand%29.JPG/1920px-Milford_Sound_%28New_Zealand%29.JPG","title":"Milford Sound, New Zealand","desc":"Milford Sound.","credit":"Maros M r a z ( Maros ), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milford_Sound_(New_Zealand).JPG","alt":"A view of Milford Sound."}},
{"id":"gw-124","num":124,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Palestine","answerText":"Palestine","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Partition vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 November 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Observer State</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 November 2012</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wall opinion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 2004</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Policies opinion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 July 2024</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Palestine"},"facts":[["Status","UN non-member observer State"],["Population","5.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","6,025 km² (2,326 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","palestine"],"abstract":"<b>Palestine</b> is the name the United Nations gives to the West Bank and Gaza, two areas separated by Israeli territory and together 6,025 square kilometres with a population it puts at about 5.6 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly voted in 1947 to partition the British mandate into an Arab state and a Jewish state, with Jerusalem under an international régime, by resolution 181 (II) of 29 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 29 November 2012, the same date 65 years later, the Assembly accorded Palestine non-member observer State status by resolution 67/19, adopted 138 to 9 with 41 abstentions, recalling its 1988 acknowledgement of the proclamation of the State of Palestine by the Palestine National Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its United Nations profile accordingly carries no membership date, where every other country in this collection has one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That profile names East Jerusalem as the capital city and footnotes the entry: the designation and the data are provided by the State of Palestine, and the position of the United Nations on Jerusalem is stated in resolution 181 (II) and in later resolutions of the Assembly and the Security Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The 2012 resolution bore in mind that the annexation of East Jerusalem is not recognised by the international community, and emphasised the need to find a way through negotiations to resolve the status of Jerusalem as the capital of two States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly has twice asked the International Court of Justice for an advisory opinion on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Court held on 9 July 2004 that the wall Israel was building there, including in and around East Jerusalem, was contrary to international law and must be dismantled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 19 July 2024 it gave a further opinion, on the legal consequences arising from the policies and practices of Israel in the occupied Palestinian territory, including East Jerusalem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its people are densely settled, at about 929 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations population figure is footnoted as including East Jerusalem, standing at 5,590,000 where the World Bank counts 5,413,596.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “State of Palestine,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ps.html. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Future Government of Palestine,” resolution 181 (II), 29 November 1947, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/210008. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Status of Palestine in the United Nations,” resolution 67/19, adopted at the 44th plenary meeting, 29 November 2012, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/739031. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Legal Consequences of the Construction of a Wall in the Occupied Palestinian Territory,” Overview of the Case, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/131. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Legal Consequences arising from the Policies and Practices of Israel in the Occupied Palestinian Territory, including East Jerusalem,” Case 186, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/186. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: West Bank and Gaza,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PSE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Church_of_the_Nativity_%287703592746%29.jpg/1920px-Church_of_the_Nativity_%287703592746%29.jpg","title":"Church of the Nativity, Palestine","desc":"Church of the Nativity.","credit":"Neil Ward, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_the_Nativity_(7703592746).jpg","alt":"A view of Church of the Nativity."}},
{"id":"gw-125","num":125,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Oman","answerText":"Oman","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1880 to 1915</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 October 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 April 1972</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Oman"},"facts":[["Capital","Muscat"],["Population","5.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","309,980 km² (119,684 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","oman"],"abstract":"<b>Oman</b> and the United States recognised each other in 1833, when a treaty of amity and commerce was signed at Muscat on 21 September by the special agent Edmund Roberts and Seyyid Said bin Sultan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those dealings of the 1830s were among the earliest official American contacts with the Middle East, and they were not built on: relations stayed sporadic for the next century and a half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate opened at Muscat in 1880 and lasted until 1915, after which American interests in the country were handled by diplomats posted elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established only on 17 April 1972, 139 years after the treaty, when William A. Stoltzfus Jr. presented his credentials — accredited to Bahrain, Kuwait, Qatar and the United Arab Emirates as well, and resident in Kuwait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy opened at Muscat on 4 July 1972, and the first resident ambassador, William D. Wolle, presented his credentials on 17 July 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Omani rulers had held an empire on the East African coast, where their forts protected the economic interests of the sultanate of Zanzibar, on a coast whose Swahili culture ran from Mogadishu in Somalia to Mozambique and Madagascar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 7 October 1971 and the World Trade Organization on 9 November 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population has risen by about 31 per cent in ten years, from 4,184,895 in 2015 to 5,494,691 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations and the World Bank differ about its size by 480 square kilometres, at 309,980 against 309,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations records a sex ratio of 166 men for every 100 women, thins its people to 17.8 to the square kilometre, and puts 1.5 million of them — more than a quarter — in Muscat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Oman,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/om.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Oman,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/oman. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Oman and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/oman_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Oman,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/OMN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Oman,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/OMN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Stéphane Pradines, “Des forts omanais en Afrique orientale : une protection des intérêts économiques du sultanat de Zanzibar,” in <i>Défense des côtes et cartographie historique</i>, Actes du 124e Congrès national des sociétés historiques et scientifiques (Paris: Éditions du CTHS, 2002), 163–80, https://www.persee.fr/doc/acths_0000-0001_2002_act_124_4_6070. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Sultan_Qaboos_Grand_Mosque_%281%29.jpg/1920px-Sultan_Qaboos_Grand_Mosque_%281%29.jpg","title":"the Sultan Qaboos Grand Mosque, Oman","desc":"The Sultan Qaboos Grand Mosque at Muscat.","credit":"Mostafameraji, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Qaboos_Grand_Mosque_(1).jpg","alt":"A domed mosque with minarets behind flowering gardens."}},
{"id":"gw-126","num":126,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mauritania","answerText":"Mauritania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 November 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 December 1969</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mauritania"},"facts":[["Capital","Nouakchott"],["Population","5.3 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","1,030,700 km² (397,956 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","mauritania"],"abstract":"<b>Mauritania</b> had been under French sovereignty, and the United States recognised the Islamic Republic on 28 November 1960 in a congratulatory message from President Eisenhower to Prime Minister Daddah.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began the same day, when Ambassador Henry S. Villard presented his credentials, and American ambassadors to the country were for a time accredited to Senegal as well and resident at Dakar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy at Nouakchott was established on 14 July 1962 under a chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mauritania severed relations with the United States on 7 June 1967, in the wake of the June 1967 Arab-Israeli war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two governments resumed relations on 22 December 1969, announcing the decision in a joint communiqué, and the embassy at Nouakchott reopened on 4 March 1970.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It joined the United Nations on 27 October 1961, more than a year after independence, and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 30 September 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has belonged to the World Trade Organization since 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Slavery was first explicitly outlawed there in 1981, by Order No. 081-234, a law the United Nations Special Rapporteur on contemporary forms of slavery describes as vague in its definition, without measures for its implementation, and concerned to compensate enslavers for their losses rather than to make reparation to victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Slavery was not criminalised until an Act of 2007, and in 2012 the constitution was amended to outlaw slavery and servitude and to classify such acts as crimes against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population has risen by about a third in ten years, from 3,965,959 in 2015 to 5,315,065 in 2025, and its area of 1,030,700 square kilometres is the same figure at the United Nations and the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mauritania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mauritania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mauritania. [Open access]","Tomoya Obokata, “Visit to Mauritania: Report of the Special Rapporteur on Contemporary Forms of Slavery, Including Its Causes and Consequences,” A/HRC/54/30/Add.2, UN Human Rights Council, 2023, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4018290. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Mauritania and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/mauritania_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Mauritania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MRT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Richat_Structure_ISS030-E-12516.jpg/1920px-Richat_Structure_ISS030-E-12516.jpg","title":"Richat Structure, Mauritania","desc":"Richat Structure.","credit":"NASA, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richat_Structure_ISS030-E-12516.jpg","alt":"A view of Richat Structure."}},
{"id":"gw-127","num":127,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Costa Rica","answerText":"Costa Rica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 August 1848</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 April 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 March 1851</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Costa Rica"},"facts":[["Capital","San José"],["Population","5.2 million (2025)"],["Region","Central America"],["Area","51,100 km² (19,730 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","costa rica"],"abstract":"<b>Costa Rica</b> was part of the United Provinces of Central America, which had separated from Mexico in 1823, and although the union had broken into five states by 1839 it did not formally declare its independence until 30 August 1848.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence from Spain had come with the rest of Central America in 1821, so two dates are defensible and the guide records both, the earlier one on the page for El Salvador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It notified the United States of its independence on 5 September 1848, and recognition followed on 24 April 1849, when Ephraim G. Squier, the American chargé d’affaires in Guatemala, was given full powers to negotiate with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That act was the whole of it: Squier never transmitted his letter of credence or visited the country, and reached no agreement by correspondence with its foreign minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on 24 March 1851, when the Costa Rican minister Felipe Molina presented his credentials in Washington, where he served until his death in 1855.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An American consulate opened at San José in 1852, with further posts at Port Limón and Punta Arenas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American minister accredited to the country, Solon Borland in 1853, was appointed to Honduras, Nicaragua and El Salvador as well, and presented his credentials only in Nicaragua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its constitution of 1949 proscribes the army as a permanent institution, providing instead for the police forces needed to keep public order and allowing military forces only by continental agreement or for national defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was an original member of the United Nations from 2 November 1945, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 24 November 1990 and the World Trade Organization at its founding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations and the World Bank agree on its size to the square kilometre, at 51,100, and on its people to the thousand, at about 5,153,000, of whom 1.4 million live in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Costa Rica,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Costa Rica,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/costa-rica. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: El Salvador,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/el-salvador. [Open access]","Constitute Project, “Costa Rica 1949 (rev. 2020) Constitution,” https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Costa_Rica_2020. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Costa Rica and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/costa_rica_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Costa Rica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CRI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Arenal_volcano_%2870785p%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Arenal_volcano_%2870785p%29_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Arenal Volcano, Costa Rica","desc":"Arenal Volcano.","credit":"Rhododendrites, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arenal_volcano_(70785p)_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A view of Arenal Volcano."}},
{"id":"gw-128","num":128,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kuwait","answerText":"Kuwait","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 June 1951</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 June 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 September 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kuwait"},"facts":[["Capital","Kuwait City"],["Population","5 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","17,818 km² (6,880 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","kuwait"],"abstract":"<b>Kuwait</b> was a British protectorate under the Anglo-Kuwaiti Treaty of 1899, and became fully independent on 19 June 1961, when an exchange of notes with the United Kingdom terminated that treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American recognition did not wait for it: the Department of State noted in July 1961 that the United States had for some time recognised Kuwait as a sovereign state, as shown by its consular officers receiving exequaturs from the Ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The other evidence it gave was a bilateral agreement on non-immigrant passport visas, concluded directly with the government of Kuwait by an exchange of notes in December 1960, while the protectorate still stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American consulate there had opened on 27 June 1951 and to the public on 15 October, under Consul Enoch Duncan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 22 September 1961 by agreement between the two governments, and the embassy opened the same day, when the American consul, Dayton Mak, was designated chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Cooperation deepened in 1987, when a maritime protection regime was put in place to keep the Gulf open to 11 Kuwaiti tankers reflagged with American markings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iraq invaded on 2 August 1990; the American ambassador and his staff left on 13 December, and the embassy reopened on 1 March 1991 after Iraqi forces were expelled by the United States and a multinational coalition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 3 May 1963 and the United Nations 11 days later, on 14 May, and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its two published populations disagree by about 161,000: the United Nations gives 5,026,000 and the World Bank 4,865,298, where their figures for its area differ by two square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recorded sex ratio is 157 men to every 100 women, and the United Nations puts 3 million of its people — some three-fifths — in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Kuwait,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kw.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kuwait,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kuwait. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The State of Kuwait and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/kuwait_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kuwait,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KWT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Kuwait,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KWT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Kuwait_City_Kuwait_Towers_Panoramic_View_1.jpg/1920px-Kuwait_City_Kuwait_Towers_Panoramic_View_1.jpg","title":"the Kuwait Towers, Kuwait","desc":"The Kuwait Towers.","credit":"Zairon, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuwait_City_Kuwait_Towers_Panoramic_View_1.jpg","alt":"Three slender towers carrying blue-tiled spheres above a bay."}},
{"id":"gw-129","num":129,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Panama","answerText":"Panama","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1903</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 November 1903</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canal opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1914</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canal to Panama</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1999</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Panama"},"facts":[["Capital","Panama City"],["Population","4.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Central America"],["Area","75,320 km² (29,081 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","panama"],"abstract":"The area that became <b>Panama</b> was part of Colombia, and American consular relations there date from 1823, when David Craig was appointed consular commercial agent at Panama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Panamanians revolted against the Colombian government on 3 November 1903, declared an independent republic and set up a provisional junta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it three days later, when Secretary of State John Hay instructed the vice-consul-general at Panama City to enter into relations with the new government once it appeared to be a de facto government having the support of its own people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 13 November, when President Roosevelt accepted the credentials of Philippe Bunau-Varilla as Panama’s minister, and an American mission was established at Panama on 25 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A treaty of 1904 let the United States build and operate a canal across the country and govern a Canal Zone ten miles wide around it; the canal was completed in 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were then interrupted four times in 37 years — in 1931, 1949, 1964 and 1968 — each time by a change of government or a quarrel over the Zone, and each time resumed within weeks or months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 1964 break was Panama’s own: President Roberto Chiari severed relations on 10 January, accusing the United States of unprovoked aggression after clashes in the Canal Zone that followed a flag-raising incident between Panamanian and American students.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two states restored relations by a joint declaration of 3 April 1964, agreeing to appoint special ambassadors to seek the prompt elimination of the causes of conflict without preconditions of any kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States transferred the Canal Zone in 1979 and the canal itself in 1999, and Panama joined the World Trade Organization on 6 September 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has belonged to the United Nations since 13 November 1945, and the United Nations and the World Bank agree on its area at 75,320 square kilometres and on its people at about 4,571,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Panama,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pa.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Panama,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/panama. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Panama and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/panama_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Panama,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PAN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Panama,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PAN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Panama_Canal_Miraflores_Locks.jpg","title":"the Panama Canal, Panama","desc":"The Miraflores Locks on the Panama Canal.","credit":"User:Stan Shebs, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panama_Canal_Miraflores_Locks.jpg","alt":"A cargo ship in a canal lock chamber."}},
{"id":"gw-130","num":130,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Croatia","answerText":"Croatia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 2013</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Croatia"},"facts":[["Capital","Zagreb"],["Population","3.9 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","56,594 km² (21,851 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","croatia"],"abstract":"<b>Croatia</b> had been a constituent part of Yugoslavia, and the United States recognised it as an independent state on 7 April 1992, announcing the decision in a White House statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 6 August 1992, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in remarks to the press, and the American embassy at Zagreb opened on 25 August under a chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations without a vote at its 86th plenary meeting on 22 May 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It later brought Serbia before the International Court of Justice under the Genocide Convention, and on 3 February 2015 the Court dismissed its claim in its entirety, holding it unnecessary to rule on other questions such as succession to responsibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 30 November 2000 and the European Union on 1 July 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It adopted the euro and entered the Schengen area on the same day, 1 January 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations and Eurostat give exactly the same area, 56,594 square kilometres, where the World Bank’s surface-area series gives 88,070, half as much again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is falling steeply: the World Bank’s series runs from 4,151,725 in 2015 to 3,876,200 in 2025, a loss of about 7 per cent in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Court had ruled on the preliminary objections in that case on 18 November 2008, and heard witnesses and witness-experts in hearings running from 3 March to 1 April 2014 before giving its judgment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its capital holds 685,000 people, about a sixth of the country, and the United Nations counts 3,848,000 people in all against Eurostat’s 3,874,350, a difference of some 26,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Croatia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Croatia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/croatia. [Open access]","European Union, “Croatia – EU country profile,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/croatia_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Croatia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/croatia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Croatia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HRV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Croatia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HRV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Croatia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/238, adopted at the 86th plenary meeting, 22 May 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/148233. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Application of the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide (Croatia v. Serbia),” Overview of the Case, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/118. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/View_in_Plitvice_Lakes_National_Park.jpg/1920px-View_in_Plitvice_Lakes_National_Park.jpg","title":"Plitvice Lakes National Park, Croatia","desc":"Plitvice Lakes National Park.","credit":"Zysko serhii, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_in_Plitvice_Lakes_National_Park.jpg","alt":"A view of Plitvice Lakes National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-131","num":131,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Georgia","answerText":"Georgia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 July 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Georgia"},"facts":[["Capital","Tbilisi"],["Population","3.8 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","69,700 km² (26,911 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","georgia"],"abstract":"<b>Georgia</b> had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and the United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation on the dissolution of the USSR.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 24 March 1992, announced in a statement by the press secretary Marlin Fitzwater and printed in the Public Papers of the Presidents, and the American embassy at Tbilisi was established on 23 April under a chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations unanimously at its 88th plenary meeting on 31 July 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 2008 it brought proceedings against the Russian Federation at the International Court of Justice over actions on and around Georgian territory, alleging breaches of the 1965 convention against racial discrimination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Georgia relied on Article 22 of that convention for the Court’s jurisdiction, and the Court, acting under its own rules, urgently called on both parties to act so that any order on provisional measures could have its proper effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> It joined the World Trade Organization on 14 June 2000, eight years after the United Nations, and its people have grown more numerous since 2015, from 3,725,276 to 3,935,766 by the World Bank's count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its United Nations population figure is footnoted as including Abkhazia and South Ossetia, and stands at 3,807,000 where the World Bank counts 3,935,766 — the count that says it takes in the two territories being the smaller of the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two agree exactly about its size, at 69,700 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations records 87 men there for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital holds 1.08 million people, more than a quarter of the country, in a corridor of valleys between the Greater and Lesser Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Georgia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ge.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Georgia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/georgia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Georgia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/georgia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Georgia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GEO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Georgia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GEO/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Georgia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/241, adopted at the 88th plenary meeting, 31 July 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/150203. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Application of the International Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial Discrimination (Georgia v. Russian Federation),” Overview of the Case, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/140. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Gergeti_Trinity_Church_09.23.jpg/1920px-Gergeti_Trinity_Church_09.23.jpg","title":"Gergeti Trinity Church, Georgia","desc":"Gergeti Trinity Church.","credit":"This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote . Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author and may send me a message. (สามารถใช้ภาพได้อิสระ แต่กรุณาใส่เครดิตผู้ถ่ายและอาจส่ง ข้อความบอกกล่าวด้วย ) Please do not upload an updated image here without consultation with the Author. The author would like to make corrections only at his own source. This ensures that the changes are preserved. Please if you think that any changes should be required, please inform the author. Otherwise you can upload a new image with a new name. Please use one of the templates derivative or extract ., CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gergeti_Trinity_Church_09.23.jpg","alt":"A view of Gergeti Trinity Church."}},
{"id":"gw-132","num":132,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Eritrea","answerText":"Eritrea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 May 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Eritrea"},"facts":[["Capital","Asmara"],["Population","3.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","121,144 km² (46,774 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","eritrea"],"abstract":"<b>Eritrea</b> had been under Ethiopian sovereignty, and it declared its independence on 27 April 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it the same day, when the American consulate at Asmara informed Eritrean authorities of the decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The independence had been settled by a referendum which the United Nations sent an observer mission to verify — a mission named for that one task — and on which the Secretary-General reported that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 11 June 1993, when that consulate at Asmara was raised to embassy status under a chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Security Council recommended its admission to the United Nations on 26 May 1993, at its 3218th meeting, and the General Assembly admitted it without a vote two days later, a month after the declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> War with Ethiopia over their border brought the Security Council back: on 31 July 2000 it unanimously established a United Nations mission in the two countries, of up to 100 military observers with civilian support, in anticipation of a peacekeeping operation it had yet to authorise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its published sizes differ by about 600 square kilometres, the United Nations giving 121,144 and the World Bank 121,766.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its two published populations, by contrast, agree to the person, at 3,607,003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That figure has grown by about a sixth in ten years, from 3,105,546 in 2015, and the United Nations puts 929,000 of them — almost a quarter — in the capital, on a highland spine that rises steeply from a hot Red Sea coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The same resolution asked the parties to help deploy mine-action experts, to assess the mines and unexploded ordnance left by the fighting and to advise on clearing them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Eritrea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/er.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Eritrea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/eritrea. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Eritrea to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 47/230, adopted at the 104th plenary meeting, 28 May 1993, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/197307. [Open access]","UN Secretary-General, “United Nations Observer Mission to Verify the Referendum in Eritrea: Report of the Secretary-General,” 11 August 1993, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/197315. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1312 (2000) on the Establishment of the United Nations Mission in Ethiopia and Eritrea,” adopted unanimously at the 4181st meeting, 31 July 2000, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/419692. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Eritrea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ERI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Eritrea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ERI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 828 (1993) on the Admission of Eritrea to Membership in the United Nations,” adopted at the 3218th meeting, 26 May 1993, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/197208. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Asmara_Eritrea_Fiat_Tagliero_Building_%2830476150040%29.jpg/1920px-Asmara_Eritrea_Fiat_Tagliero_Building_%2830476150040%29.jpg","title":"Asmara, Eritrea","desc":"The Fiat Tagliero building in Asmara.","credit":"Clay Gilliland from Chandler, U.S.A., CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asmara_Eritrea_Fiat_Tagliero_Building_(30476150040).jpg","alt":"A modernist service station with long cantilevered wings."}},
{"id":"gw-133","num":133,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mongolia","answerText":"Mongolia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plebiscite</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 1987</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mongolia"},"facts":[["Capital","Ulaanbaatar"],["Population","3.5 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Asia"],["Area","1,564,116 km² (603,909 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","mongolia"],"abstract":"For roughly 200 years before 1911 the territory that is now <b>Mongolia</b> lay within the borders of the Empire of China, and after that empire collapsed a group of Mongolian nationalists established a new republic in 1924 with the assistance of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mongolians affirmed their independence in a plebiscite in October 1945, but the country remained close to the Soviet Union and its relations with the United States were slow to develop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations at its 1043rd plenary meeting on 27 October 1961, by resolution 1630 (XVI) — the resolution printed immediately before the one admitting Mauritania, which the same meeting took in on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The United States had not blocked that admission — it abstained — and had signed several multilateral treaties to which Mongolia was also a party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of that, the guide records, had constituted recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Recognition came 26 years later, on 27 January 1987, when the two states established diplomatic relations in a joint communiqué.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Kennedy, Johnson, Nixon and Carter administrations had each entertained the possibility before that, in internal discussions and in informal ones with Mongolian representatives, without success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy opened at Ulaanbaatar on 17 April 1988 under a chargé d’affaires, and the country joined the World Trade Organization on 29 January 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations and the World Bank agree about its size to a fraction of a square kilometre, at 1,564,116, and it holds 2.3 people to each of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population has grown by about 18 per cent in ten years, from 3,026,864 in 2015 to 3,568,978 in 2025, and the United Nations puts 1.55 million of them in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mongolia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mongolia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mongolia. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Mongolian People’s Republic to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1630 (XVI), adopted at the 1043rd plenary meeting, 27 October 1961, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205643. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Mongolia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/mongolia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Mongolia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Mongolia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Islamic Republic of Mauritania to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1631 (XVI), adopted at the 1043rd plenary meeting, 27 October 1961, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205644. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Gobi_Desert.jpg","title":"Gobi Desert, Mongolia","desc":"Gobi Desert.","credit":"CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gobi_Desert.jpg","alt":"A view of Gobi Desert."}},
{"id":"gw-134","num":134,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Uruguay","answerText":"Uruguay","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1828</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 January 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 July 1870</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uruguay"},"facts":[["Capital","Montevideo"],["Population","3.4 million (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","173,626 km² (67,037 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","uruguay"],"abstract":"<b>Uruguay</b> became independent of Spain in 1811 and was then annexed by Brazil until 1825, and after a three-year federation with Argentina it became an independent nation in 1828.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Oriental Republic on 25 January 1836 by an unusual act: it issued an exequatur to John Darby as Uruguayan consul general at New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations waited another 31 years, until 2 October 1867, when the American minister resident Alexander Asboth presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Asboth was accredited to Argentina as well and lived at Buenos Aires, so for three more years the United States was represented there by a minister resident in another country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation opened at Montevideo on 6 July 1870 under the minister resident John L. Stevens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Secretary of State announced on 11 January 1941 that the President had approved raising that legation to an embassy; William Dawson was appointed on 11 February and presented his credentials as the first ambassador on 12 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has belonged to the United Nations since 18 December 1945, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 6 December 1953 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is almost static: the World Bank’s series runs from 3,368,017 in 2015 to 3,384,688 in 2025, a rise of half a per cent in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nearly half its people live in the capital, which the United Nations puts at 1,744,700 out of 3,385,000 in all, spread at 19.5 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two published areas differ by about 2,600 square kilometres, the United Nations giving 173,626 and the World Bank 176,220.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Uruguay,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/uy.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uruguay,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uruguay. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Uruguay and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/uruguay_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Uruguay,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/URY/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Uruguay,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/URY/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Basilica_del_Sanct%C3%ADsimo_Sacramento.jpg","title":"Colonia del Sacramento, Uruguay","desc":"Colonia del Sacramento.","credit":"JGHowes , photographer ( Canon AE-1 camera)., Attribution, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_del_Sanct%C3%ADsimo_Sacramento.jpg","alt":"A view of Colonia del Sacramento."}},
{"id":"gw-135","num":135,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Puerto Rico","answerText":"Puerto Rico","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded by Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 November 1953</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Puerto Rico"},"facts":[["Status","Territory of the United States"],["Population","3.2 million (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","8,868 km² (3,424 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","puerto rico"],"abstract":"<b>Puerto Rico</b> is a Caribbean island of 8,868 square kilometres, and the United Nations gives it a country profile of its own without a membership date, because it is not a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spain ceded sovereignty over it, with Guam and the Philippines, in the peace treaty that ended the war of 1898 and closed the Spanish colonial empire in the western hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has belonged to the United States ever since, and the United Nations profile for the United States expressly excludes its data.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 27 November 1953, at its 459th plenary meeting, the General Assembly resolved that information about it need no longer be transmitted under Article 73e of the Charter, the article that binds a state administering a territory whose people have not yet attained full self-government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That decision took it off the list of places the United States reported on to the United Nations, and no later resolution has put it back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its population is falling steeply: the World Bank’s series runs from 3,535,167 in 2015 to 3,184,835 in 2025, a loss of about a tenth in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations puts the figure slightly higher, at 3,235,000, and the two published areas differ by two square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Three-quarters of its people live in and around the capital, whose metropolitan area the United Nations counts at 2,451,400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people are densely settled, at 372 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations records 89 men there for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It uses the United States dollar, and the profile footnotes that its national accounts are compiled in accordance with the System of National Accounts of 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Puerto Rico,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Spanish-American War, 1898,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Cessation of the Transmission of Information under Article 73e of the Charter in Respect of Puerto Rico,” resolution 748 (VIII), adopted at the 459th plenary meeting, 27 November 1953, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/211641. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Puerto Rico,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Puerto Rico,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Castillo_San_Felipe_Del_Morro_%2816869927311%29.jpg/1920px-Castillo_San_Felipe_Del_Morro_%2816869927311%29.jpg","title":"Castillo San Felipe del Morro, Puerto Rico","desc":"Castillo San Felipe del Morro.","credit":"Chad Sparkes from Kissimmee,Florida, United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo_San_Felipe_Del_Morro_(16869927311).jpg","alt":"A view of Castillo San Felipe del Morro."}},
{"id":"gw-136","num":136,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","answerText":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dayton signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ICJ judgment</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 February 2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bosnia and Herz."},"facts":[["Capital","Sarajevo"],["Population","3.1 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","51,209 km² (19,772 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","bosnia and herzegovina"],"abstract":"<b>Bosnia and Herzegovina</b> had been a constituent part of Yugoslavia, and the United States recognised it as an independent state on 7 April 1992, announcing the decision in a White House statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations without a vote at its 86th plenary meeting on 22 May 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 6 August 1992, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in remarks to the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Victor Jackovich presented his credentials as American ambassador on 23 June 1993, but there was no embassy for him to work in: one was established on 10 November 1993 on the premises of the American embassy in Vienna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An embassy in Sarajevo itself followed only on 4 July 1994, with Jackovich as ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The war was ended by the General Framework Agreement for Peace, reached at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base near Dayton, Ohio, in November 1995 and formally signed at Paris on 14 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country’s constitution is Annex 4 of that agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It brought Serbia and Montenegro before the International Court of Justice under the Genocide Convention, and on 26 February 2007 the Court found that Serbia had violated its obligation to prevent the genocide at Srebrenica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Court also found that other acts before it had not been accompanied by the specific intent that defines genocide — the intent to destroy a protected group in whole or in part — and that financial compensation was not the appropriate reparation, since it had not been shown that the genocide would have been averted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population is falling steeply: the World Bank’s series runs from 3,518,541 in 2015 to 3,140,095 in 2025, and the two published areas differ by a single square kilometre, at 51,209 and 51,210.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bosnia and Herzegovina,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ba.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bosnia and Herzegovina,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bosnia-herzegovina. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/237, adopted at the 86th plenary meeting, 22 May 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/148234. [Open access]","Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe, “Dayton Peace Agreement,” OSCE Mission to Bosnia and Herzegovina, https://www.osce.org/bih/126173. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Application of the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide (Bosnia and Herzegovina v. Serbia and Montenegro),” Overview of the Case, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/91. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bosnia and Herzegovina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BIH/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Bosnia and Herzegovina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BIH/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Mostar_Old_Town_Panorama_2007.jpg/1920px-Mostar_Old_Town_Panorama_2007.jpg","title":"Stari Most, Bosnia and Herzegovina","desc":"Stari Most.","credit":"Ramirez, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mostar_Old_Town_Panorama_2007.jpg","alt":"A view of Stari Most."}},
{"id":"gw-137","num":137,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Armenia","answerText":"Armenia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1918 to 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Armenia"},"facts":[["Capital","Yerevan"],["Population","3 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","29,743 km² (11,484 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","armenia"],"abstract":"<b>Armenia</b> was an independent republic for a brief period from 1918 to 1920, and the United States recognised it on 23 April 1920, when Secretary of State Bainbridge Colby delivered a note to its representative in Washington informing him of President Wilson’s decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That note specified that the recognition “in no way predetermines the territorial frontiers, which … are matters for later delimitation”, the territory expected to compose the republic having been under Ottoman and Russian sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the request of the Paris Peace Conference’s Supreme Council, Wilson then arbitrated the boundary to be set between Armenia and Turkey, and submitted his determinations on 22 November 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By then the republic was going: local communists took power late in 1920 after an invasion by the Soviet Red Army, and in 1922 Armenia became part of the Trans-Caucasian Soviet Socialist Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became the Armenian Soviet Socialist Republic in 1936, and the dissolution of the Soviet Union in 1991 created the opportunity for bilateral relations again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomatic relations were established on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in his address to the nation on that dissolution, and an American embassy opened at Yerevan on 3 February 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country to the United Nations without a vote at its 82nd plenary meeting on 2 March 1992, and it joined the World Trade Organization on 5 February 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American consulate had been opened at Erzurum, in Ottoman Armenia, as long ago as 1896, and was transferred to Trebizond in 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two published populations differ by about 135,000: the United Nations gives 2,952,000 and the World Bank 3,086,700, where their figures for its area differ by three square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations records 87 men there for every 100 women, and puts a third of the country’s people in Yerevan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Armenia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/am.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Armenia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/armenia. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Armenia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/227, adopted at the 82nd plenary meeting, 2 March 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/147689. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The Republic of Armenia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/armenia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Armenia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Armenia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Monasterio_Khor_Virap%2C_Armenia%2C_2016-10-01%2C_DD_25.jpg/1920px-Monasterio_Khor_Virap%2C_Armenia%2C_2016-10-01%2C_DD_25.jpg","title":"Khor Virap, Armenia","desc":"Khor Virap.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monasterio_Khor_Virap,_Armenia,_2016-10-01,_DD_25.jpg","alt":"A view of Khor Virap."}},
{"id":"gw-138","num":138,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Namibia","answerText":"Namibia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 September 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 March 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 March 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Namibia"},"facts":[["Capital","Windhoek"],["Population","3.1 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Africa"],["Area","825,229 km² (318,623 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","namibia"],"abstract":"<b>Namibia</b> had been under South African sovereignty, and the Security Council set out the terms of its independence twelve years before it came: resolution 435, adopted 12 votes to none with two abstentions at the 2087th meeting on 29 September 1978, established a United Nations Transition Assistance Group for the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Independence came on 21 March 1990, and on that date the United States recognised the country, established diplomatic relations with it and opened an embassy, all by a single act on the day the territory attained independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its liaison office at Windhoek was simply elevated to embassy status, with Roger A. McGuire as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations without a vote a month later, on 23 April 1990, at the first meeting of its eighteenth special session.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth the same year, the Secretariat recording that it did so after independence from South Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 15 September 1992 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population has grown by about 30 per cent in ten years, from 2,374,300 in 2015 to 3,092,816 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its people are thinly spread even so, at 3.8 to the square kilometre across 825,229 of them, most of the country being desert or dry savanna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three published figures for its size fall within about 1,200 square kilometres of one another, at 825,229 for the United Nations, 824,290 for the World Bank and 824,000 for the Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The United Nations puts 417,400 people in the capital and counts the country’s population as 3,093,000, within 200 of the World Bank’s figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Namibia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/na.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Namibia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/namibia. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 435 (1978) on the Establishment of the United Nations Transition Assistance Group for Namibia,” adopted at the 2087th meeting, 29 September 1978, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/71636. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Namibia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution S-18/1, adopted at the 1st meeting of the eighteenth special session, 23 April 1990, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/89542. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Namibia,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/namibia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Namibia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/namibia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Namibia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NAM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Namibia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NAM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Sossusvlei_south_view.jpg/1920px-Sossusvlei_south_view.jpg","title":"Sossusvlei, Namibia","desc":"Sossusvlei.","credit":"Hans Hillewaert, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sossusvlei_south_view.jpg","alt":"A view of Sossusvlei."}},
{"id":"gw-139","num":139,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lithuania","answerText":"Lithuania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Restoration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 September 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Lithuania"},"facts":[["Capital","Vilnius"],["Population","2.9 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","65,286 km² (25,207 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","lithuania"],"abstract":"The United States recognised <b>Lithuania</b> on 28 July 1922, when its commissioner at Riga, Evan Young, informed the Lithuanian foreign office of the decision, Secretary of State Charles Evans Hughes having instructed him three days earlier to tell Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania on that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began the same day, Young continuing as the American representative to all three Baltic states with the new rank of minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation at Kovno, later Kaunas, was established on 31 May 1930; before that the representatives accredited to Lithuania had lived at Riga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It closed on 5 September 1940, after the Soviet Union occupied and annexed the three states that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States never recognised the forcible incorporation: it let Lithuanian representatives accredited by the last independent government stay on with diplomatic status, and its position remained that diplomatic relations continued uninterrupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It recognised the restoration of Lithuania’s independence on 2 September 1991 and resumed normal relations four days later, by a memorandum of understanding signed by a deputy assistant secretary of state and the Lithuanian first deputy foreign minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy opened at Vilnius on 2 October 1991, and the guide records that the United States views the present government as a legal continuation of the interwar republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country to the United Nations on 17 September 1991, by resolution 46/6.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 31 May 2001, the European Union on 1 May 2004, the Schengen area in 2007 and the euro area on 1 January 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its three published populations differ: the United Nations gives 2,830,000 against Eurostat’s 2,890,664 and the World Bank’s 2,888,774, while their figures for its area lie within six square kilometres of one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Lithuania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lt.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Lithuania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/lithuania. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Lithuania to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/6, 17 September 1991, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/133633. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Lithuania and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/lithuania_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Lithuania – EU country profile,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/lithuania_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Lithuania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LTU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Kry%C5%BEi%C5%B3_kalnas_%28G%C3%B3ra_Krzy%C5%BCy%29.JPG/1920px-Kry%C5%BEi%C5%B3_kalnas_%28G%C3%B3ra_Krzy%C5%BCy%29.JPG","title":"Hill of Crosses, Lithuania","desc":"Hill of Crosses.","credit":"Pudelek (Marcin Szala), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kry%C5%BEi%C5%B3_kalnas_(G%C3%B3ra_Krzy%C5%BCy).JPG","alt":"A view of Hill of Crosses."}},
{"id":"gw-140","num":140,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Qatar","answerText":"Qatar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 February 1973</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Qatar"},"facts":[["Capital","Doha"],["Population","3.1 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","11,627 km² (4,489 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","qatar"],"abstract":"<b>Qatar</b> had been a protectorate under Great Britain, with which the United States had a very limited relationship before 1971, and it declared independence on 3 September that year, following the termination date of its treaties with that country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the State of Qatar two days later, on 5 September, when the Department of State made an announcement to that effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 19 March 1972, when William A. Stoltzfus Jr. presented his credentials — the same envoy who presented them in Oman a month later, accredited to a number of Persian Gulf states at once and resident in Kuwait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An American embassy at Doha followed on 24 February 1973 under a chargé d’affaires ad interim, and Robert P. Paganelli became its first resident ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 21 September 1971, eighteen days after independence and sixteen after the American announcement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 7 April 1994 and joined the World Trade Organization on 13 January 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its two published populations differ by about 144,000: the United Nations gives 3,116,000 and the World Bank 2,972,215.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Either way the figure has grown by more than a quarter in ten years, the World Bank's series running from 2,344,557 in 2015 to just under three million now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations records 246 men there for every 100 women, puts 98.9 per cent of the population in towns and cities, and gives its income per head as 66,633 US dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their two figures for its area differ as well, at 11,627 square kilometres against 11,490, and the capital holds 637,300 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Qatar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/qa.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Qatar,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/qatar. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Qatar and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/qatar_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Qatar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/QAT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Qatar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/QAT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Oman,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/oman. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Doha_skyline_%26_museum.jpg/1920px-Doha_skyline_%26_museum.jpg","title":"Museum of Islamic Art, Doha, Qatar","desc":"Museum of Islamic Art, Doha.","credit":"Haakon S. Krohn, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doha_skyline_%26_museum.jpg","alt":"A view of Museum of Islamic Art, Doha."}},
{"id":"gw-141","num":141,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Jamaica","answerText":"Jamaica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Jamaica"},"facts":[["Capital","Kingston"],["Population","2.8 million (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","10,990 km² (4,243 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","jamaica"],"abstract":"Through the 19th century the United States kept several consular posts in colonial British <b>Jamaica</b>, the earliest at Montego Bay and Falmouth dating from 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were placed to watch American trade interests in the Caribbean, and they were all closed by September 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island became independent on 6 August 1962, remaining a member of the British Commonwealth, and the Commonwealth Secretariat records that it joined in that year following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised that independence on 16 August 1962, with the establishment of the American embassy at Kingston: recognition, diplomatic relations and an embassy were all one act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Irving G. Cheslaw served as the first chargé d’affaires ad interim there, on an island the Commonwealth describes as lying 630 kilometres north-east of mainland Central America, with mountains and plateaus inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The country joined the United Nations on 18 September 1962, a month after that recognition, and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 31 December 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 9 March 1995, in that organisation's first months, having been a party to the trade agreement it replaced for 31 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its population is nearly static: the World Bank’s series runs from 2,802,534 in 2015 to 2,837,077 in 2025, a rise of about one per cent in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations gives the same figure to the thousand, at 2,837,000, and the two agree on its area to the square kilometre, at 10,990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About a fifth of its people live in the capital, which the United Nations puts at 589,800, and it gives the country's income per head as 5,063 US dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Jamaica,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Jamaica,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/jamaica. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Jamaica,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/jamaica. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Jamaica and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/jamaica_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Jamaica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/JAM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Jamaica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/JAM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Dunns_River_Falls_climb.JPG/1920px-Dunns_River_Falls_climb.JPG","title":"Dunn's River Falls, Jamaica","desc":"Dunn's River Falls.","credit":"Breakyunit at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunns_River_Falls_climb.JPG","alt":"A view of Dunn's River Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-142","num":142,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Gambia","answerText":"Gambia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 August 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1965</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Gambia"},"facts":[["Capital","Banjul"],["Population","2.8 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","11,295 km² (4,361 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","gambia"],"abstract":"The <b>Gambia</b> had been under British sovereignty, and the United States recognised it on 18 February 1965 in an unusual way: by attending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Assistant Secretary for African Affairs G. Mennen Williams represented President Lyndon B. Johnson as his personal representative, with the rank of special ambassador, at the ceremonies marking independence held at the capital, Bathurst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 9 August 1965, when Ambassador Mercer Cook presented his credentials; Cook was accredited to Senegal as well and lived at Dakar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy at Bathurst, now Banjul, was established on 9 September 1965 under a chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 21 September 1965 and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 22 February that year — four days after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It joined the World Trade Organization on 23 October 1996, more than thirty years after the trade agreement that preceded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat records that it joined in 1965 following independence from Britain, left in 2013 and rejoined in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth also calls it the smallest country in West Africa: a mainly flat, low-lying strip bisected by the River Gambia, with rocky hills to the east, surrounded by Senegal but for a short Atlantic coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population has grown by more than a quarter in ten years, from 2,224,529 in 2015 to 2,822,093 in 2025, a figure the United Nations gives to the thousand as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The three published areas run from 11,295 square kilometres at the United Nations through 11,300 at the World Bank to 11,600 at the Commonwealth, and 443,400 people live in the capital, in a country whose income per head the United Nations puts at 620 US dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Gambia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Gambia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/gambia. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Gambia, The,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/gambia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The Gambia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/the_gambia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: The Gambia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GMB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): The Gambia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GMB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/JamesIsland.jpg/1920px-JamesIsland.jpg","title":"Kunta Kinteh Island, the Gambia","desc":"Kunta Kinteh Island.","credit":"Niels Elgaard Larsen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JamesIsland.jpg","alt":"A view of Kunta Kinteh Island."}},
{"id":"gw-143","num":143,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Gabon","answerText":"Gabon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 March 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Gabon"},"facts":[["Capital","Libreville"],["Population","2.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Area","267,668 km² (103,347 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","gabon"],"abstract":"<b>Gabon</b> sits on the Atlantic coast of Middle Africa, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 267,668 square kilometres and its population as 2,593,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is 10.1 people to the square kilometre, and by 2019 close to nine in ten of them lived in towns rather than in the countryside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers 91.2 per cent of the land area, and 53.7 per cent of the sites that matter most for land-based biodiversity are protected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Libreville, held 823,900 people in 2019, counted together with the neighbouring port of Owendo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independence from French Equatorial Africa was proclaimed on 17 August 1960, and the United States recognised the new state the same day in a congratulatory message from President Dwight D. Eisenhower to Prime Minister Léon M’ba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomatic relations were established on that same date, when Alan W. Lukens, resident at Brazzaville, presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy at Libreville followed on 20 March 1961, under a chargé d’affaires ad interim of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Security Council recommended the country for membership of the United Nations on 23 August 1960, six days after independence, and it was admitted on 20 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 3 May 1963, and became a member of the World Trade Organization when that body opened on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The World Bank measures the surface area at 267,670 square kilometres, within two of the United Nations figure, and income per head rose from 7,047 US dollars in 2015 to 8,071 in 2023, with industry making up 53.4 per cent of gross value added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Gabon,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ga.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Gabon,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/gabon. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Gabon and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/gabon_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Gabon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GAB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 153 (1960) of 23 August 1960,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/112125. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Gabon_Loango_National_Park_View_of_Lodge_from_water.jpeg/1920px-Gabon_Loango_National_Park_View_of_Lodge_from_water.jpeg","title":"Loango National Park, Gabon","desc":"Loango National Park.","credit":"Kurt Dundy at English Wikipedia, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gabon_Loango_National_Park_View_of_Lodge_from_water.jpeg","alt":"A view of Loango National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-144","num":144,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Botswana","answerText":"Botswana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Botswana"},"facts":[["Capital","Gaborone"],["Population","2.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Africa"],["Area","581,730 km² (224,610 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","botswana"],"abstract":"<b>Botswana</b> is landlocked in the centre of southern Africa, bordered by Namibia, Zambia, Zimbabwe and South Africa, and most of it is covered by deep Kalahari sand, with rocky hills in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A large inland river delta in the north-west, where the Okavango spreads out and dies in the desert, holds a rich variety of wildlife and is a World Heritage site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives the surface area as 582,000 square kilometres and the population as 2,562,000 in 2025, at 4.5 people to the square kilometre, with that population growing by 1.6 per cent a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat and the World Bank both put the area more exactly at 581,730 square kilometres, and the Commonwealth gave the population as 2.352 million in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The capital, Gaborone, held 269,300 people in 2018 and is also the headquarters of the Southern African Development Community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The country had been under British sovereignty as Bechuanaland, and became independent on 30 September 1966.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised it the same day, when an American embassy was established at Gaberones — the older spelling of the capital — with Charles H. Pletcher as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in 1966 following independence from Britain, and the United Nations on 17 October that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was also a founding member of the Southern African Development Co-ordination Conference, formed with eight other countries at Lusaka in Zambia in 1980, which grew into the present community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diamonds, copper, nickel, cattle and wildlife are its listed natural resources; income per head reached 7,820 US dollars in 2023; and it joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 28 August 1987 and the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Botswana,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bw.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Botswana,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/botswana. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Botswana,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/botswana. [Open access]","Southern African Development Community, “Botswana,” Member States, https://www.sadc.int/member-states/botswana. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Botswana and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/botswana_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Botswana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BWA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Okavango_Delta%2C_Botswana1.jpg/1920px-Okavango_Delta%2C_Botswana1.jpg","title":"Okavango Delta, Botswana","desc":"Okavango Delta.","credit":"Joachim Huber, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Okavango_Delta,_Botswana1.jpg","alt":"A view of Okavango Delta."}},
{"id":"gw-145","num":145,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Moldova","answerText":"Moldova","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 August 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Moldova"},"facts":[["Capital","Chișinău"],["Population","2.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Europe"],["Area","33,846 km² (13,068 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","moldova"],"abstract":"<b>Moldova</b> is a landlocked country of eastern Europe whose surface area the United Nations gives as 33,847 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two published populations do not agree, and the reason is printed in a footnote: the United Nations gives 2,996,000 for 2025 including the Transnistria region, while the World Bank series, which follows the national statistical service, gives 2,360,527.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That second series has fallen every year since 2015, when it stood at 2,835,978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital, Chișinău, held 504,300 people in 2019, and the country counts 85.1 men for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers 11.8 per cent of the land, agriculture still employs 51.1 per cent of those in work, and recorded unemployment is 1.3 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The country had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and the United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation on the dissolution of that state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were agreed at a White House meeting between Bush and President Mircea Snegur on 18 February 1992, and an American embassy at Chișinău followed on 13 March that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992 and the World Trade Organization on 26 July 2001, and its present constitution came into force on 27 August 1994, repealing the one of 15 April 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe established a mission there on 4 February 1993 to help settle the Transdniestrian conflict, and its 57 participating states support a settlement based on the country’s independence, sovereignty and territorial integrity within its internationally recognised borders, with a special status for Transdniestria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Moldova applied to join the European Union on 3 March 2022, was given a European perspective on 23 June that year by unanimous agreement of the leaders of all 27 member states, and the European Council decided on 14 December 2023 to open accession negotiations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Moldova,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/md.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Moldova,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Moldova,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/moldova. [Open access]","Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe, “OSCE Mission to Moldova,” https://moldova.osce.org/. [Open access]","Constitute Project, “Moldova’s Constitution of 1994 with Amendments through 2016,” https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Moldova_2016. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Republic of Moldova and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/moldova_e.htm. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “Moldova,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/moldova_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Stanca_deasupra_Rautului_Butuceni.jpg","title":"Orheiul Vechi, Moldova","desc":"Orheiul Vechi.","credit":"Alex Prodan md, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanca_deasupra_Rautului_Butuceni.jpg","alt":"A view of Orheiul Vechi."}},
{"id":"gw-146","num":146,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Albania","answerText":"Albania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 December 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 June 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 March 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Albania"},"facts":[["Capital","Tirana"],["Population","2.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","28,748 km² (11,100 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","albania"],"abstract":"<b>Albania</b> lies on the western side of the Balkan peninsula in southern Europe, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 28,748 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two published populations are far apart: the United Nations projects 2,772,000 for 2025, while the World Bank series gives 2,349,580 and has fallen in every one of the past ten years, from 2,731,293 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital, Tirana, held 484,600 people in 2019, forest covers 28.8 per cent of the land, and agriculture still employs 34.9 per cent of everyone in work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mutual recognition with the United States came on 28 July 1922, when the American Commissioner Maxwell Blake was authorised by Secretary of State Charles Evans Hughes to notify the Albanian foreign minister of de jure recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 4 December 1922, when Ulysses Grant-Smith presented his credentials as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary at Tirana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Italian forces invaded in 1939 and occupied Tirana on 8 April; relations ended on 5 June that year, when the Albanian foreign minister told the American minister that Italy had taken control of Albania’s foreign affairs, and the legation closed on 16 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An informal American mission sent in 1945 to study reopening them was withdrawn in November 1946, and the two states resumed relations only on 15 March 1991, a gap of nearly 52 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Albania had joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955, in the middle of that gap, and joined the World Trade Organization on 8 September 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It had taken part in NATO’s Membership Action Plan since the plan began in 1999, signed an accession protocol in July 2008 and became a member of the alliance on 1 April 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The European Union identified it as a potential candidate at Thessaloniki in June 2003, granted it candidate status in June 2014, opened accession negotiations at a first intergovernmental conference in July 2022, and in December 2025 it opened the last of all 33 chapters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Albania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/al.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Albania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ALB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Albania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/albania. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Albania and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/albania_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “Albania,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/albania_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Amphitheatre_of_Butrint_2009.jpg/1920px-Amphitheatre_of_Butrint_2009.jpg","title":"Butrint, Albania","desc":"Butrint.","credit":"Geoff Wong, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphitheatre_of_Butrint_2009.jpg","alt":"A view of Butrint."}},
{"id":"gw-147","num":147,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lesotho","answerText":"Lesotho","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Lesotho"},"facts":[["Capital","Maseru"],["Population","2.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Africa"],["Area","30,355 km² (11,720 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","lesotho"],"abstract":"<b>Lesotho</b> is a landlocked kingdom in southern Africa, entirely surrounded by South Africa, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 30,355 square kilometres and its population as 2,363,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Southern African Development Community calls it the only country in the world with all of its territory more than 1,000 metres above sea level, and says more than 75 per cent of it is mountainous, with only a quarter counted as lowland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its natural resources are listed as diamonds, wildlife, mohair, wool and water, and forest covers just 1.1 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only about a quarter of its people live in towns, most of them on the lower land to the west, and the capital, Maseru, held 201,900 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head has fallen rather than risen, from 1,121 US dollars in 2015 to 916 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The country had been under British sovereignty as Basutoland, and became independent on 4 October 1966.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised the Kingdom of Lesotho that same day, when an American embassy was established at Maseru with Richard St. F. Post as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 17 October 1966 and the Commonwealth the same year, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lesotho was a founding member of the Southern African Development Co-ordination Conference, formed at Lusaka in Zambia in April 1980, which became the Southern African Development Community in 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under a treaty signed in 1986 the Lesotho Highlands Water Project harnesses the Senqu, or Orange, river to send water to the Gauteng region of South Africa and to generate hydro-electricity for Lesotho; the Commonwealth gives the area as 30,350 square kilometres and the population as 2.14 million in 2022; and the country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 8 January 1988 and the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Lesotho,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ls.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Lesotho,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/lesotho. [Open access]","Southern African Development Community, “Kingdom of Lesotho,” Member States, https://www.sadc.int/member-states/lesotho. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Lesotho,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/lesotho. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Lesotho and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/lesotho_e.htm. [Open access]","Lesotho Highlands Development Authority, “Project Description,” https://www.lhda.org.ls/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Maletsunyanefalls.JPG/1920px-Maletsunyanefalls.JPG","title":"Maletsunyane Falls, Lesotho","desc":"Maletsunyane Falls.","credit":"BagelBelt, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maletsunyanefalls.JPG","alt":"A view of Maletsunyane Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-148","num":148,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guinea-Bissau","answerText":"Guinea-Bissau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 September 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 June 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guinea-Bissau"},"facts":[["Capital","Bissau"],["Population","2.2 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","36,125 km² (13,948 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","guinea-bissau"],"abstract":"<b>Guinea-Bissau</b> lies on the Atlantic coast of western Africa, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 36,125 square kilometres and its population as 2,250,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by more than a quarter in ten years, from 1,786,457 in 2015 to 2,249,515 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 69.8 per cent of the land, 59.5 per cent of the sites that matter most for land-based biodiversity are protected, and agriculture employs half of everyone in work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Bissau, held 578,800 people in 2019, and income per head was 855 US dollars in 2023 against 587 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country had been under Portuguese sovereignty, and the Security Council recommended it unanimously for membership of the United Nations at its 1791st meeting on 12 August 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the Republic of Guinea-Bissau nearly a month later, on 10 September 1974, when President Gerald R. Ford sent a letter to that effect to the President of the Council of State, Luís de Almeida Cabral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the republic at its 2233rd plenary meeting on 17 September 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 3 September 1975, when the Guinea-Bissau ambassador Gil Vicente Vaz Fernandes presented his credentials to Ford, and an American embassy at Bissau followed on 30 June 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That embassy suspended operations on 14 June 1998, at the start of a civil war, and has not reopened: since 13 February 2003 the American ambassador to Senegal has been accredited to Guinea-Bissau as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 17 March 1994 and the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995, and the World Bank measures the surface area at 36,130 square kilometres, within five of the United Nations figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Guinea-Bissau,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guinea-Bissau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guinea-Bissau,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guinea-bissau. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 356 (1974), adopted at its 1791st meeting, of 12 August 1974,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/93484. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Guinea-Bissau to membership in the United Nations,” A/RES/3205 (XXIX), United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/189827. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Guinea-Bissau and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/guinea_bissau_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Guinea-Bissau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Bubaque_port.jpg/1920px-Bubaque_port.jpg","title":"Bubaque, Guinea-Bissau","desc":"Bubaque.","credit":"Nammarci, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bubaque_port.jpg","alt":"A view of Bubaque."}},
{"id":"gw-149","num":149,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Slovenia","answerText":"Slovenia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Slovenia"},"facts":[["Capital","Ljubljana"],["Population","2.1 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","20,273 km² (7,827 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","slovenia"],"abstract":"<b>Slovenia</b> lies in southern Europe where the Alps, the Pannonian plain and the Dinaric uplands meet above a short Adriatic coast, and both the United Nations and the European Union give its area as 20,273 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank series puts it at 20,480 square kilometres, about one per cent higher, and is the outlier of the three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 2,117,000 by the United Nations count and 2,130,850 by Eurostat, at about 105 people to the square kilometre, and the capital, Ljubljana, held 286,500 in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Slovenia had been a constituent part of Yugoslavia and won its independence from it in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised it as an independent state on 7 April 1992, when the administration of George H. W. Bush announced the decision in a White House statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It joined the United Nations on 22 May 1992, diplomatic relations with the United States were established on 6 August that year, and an American embassy opened at Ljubljana on 25 August under a chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It had joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 30 October 1994, and became a member of the World Trade Organization on 30 July 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a member of NATO on 29 March 2004, alongside six other states, in the largest wave of enlargement in the alliance’s history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the European Union on 1 May 2004, adopted the euro on 1 January 2007 and entered the Schengen area on 21 December that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a parliamentary democratic republic whose head of state is a directly elected president, and it has no regions but is divided into 212 municipalities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Slovenia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/si.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Slovenia – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/slovenia_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Slovenia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/slovenia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Slovenia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/slovenia_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Slovenia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SVN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Lake_Bled_from_the_Mountain.jpg/1920px-Lake_Bled_from_the_Mountain.jpg","title":"Lake Bled, Slovenia","desc":"Lake Bled.","credit":"Canadianhockey91, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Bled_from_the_Mountain.jpg","alt":"A view of Lake Bled."}},
{"id":"gw-150","num":150,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Equatorial Guinea","answerText":"Equatorial Guinea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 October 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 October 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ties suspended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 March 1976</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Eq. Guinea"},"facts":[["Capital","Malabo"],["Population","1.9 million (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Area","28,051 km² (10,831 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","equatorial guinea"],"abstract":"<b>Equatorial Guinea</b> is made up of a mainland region in Middle Africa and islands in the Gulf of Guinea, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 28,052 square kilometres and its population as 1,938,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by a third in ten years, from 1,453,923 in 2015, and 111.4 men are counted for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Malabo, held 296,800 people in 2019, and income per head was 6,558 US dollars in 2023, down from 9,069 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country had been under Spanish colonial rule for most of the modern era and gained independence from Spain on 12 October 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the time of independence, the American guide to recognition records, it had one of the highest incomes per head and the highest literacy rates in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised that independence on 28 October 1968, when President Lyndon Johnson appointed Albert W. Sherer, the ambassador to Togo, to act as ambassador to Equatorial Guinea as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 21 November 1968, with Sherer resident at Lomé, and an American embassy at Santa Isabel, now Malabo, followed on 1 August 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Security Council had recommended admission unanimously at its 1458th meeting on 6 November 1968, and the General Assembly admitted the republic at its 1714th plenary meeting on 12 November, a month after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States suspended relations on 14 March 1976, after its ambassador and consul were declared personae non gratae in what the State Department called an unwarranted affront, and resumed them on 19 December 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The embassy at Malabo was closed on 31 October 1995 and its work moved to Yaoundé, and it reopened in 2006, when Donald J. Johnson presented his credentials on 23 November as the first resident ambassador in more than ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Equatorial Guinea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gq.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Equatorial Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNQ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Equatorial Guinea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/equatorial-guinea. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 260 (1968), adopted at its 1458th meeting, of 6 November 1968,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/90762. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Equatorial Guinea to membership in the United Nations,” A/RES/2384 (XXIII), United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/202552. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Pico_Basil%C3%A9.jpg","title":"Pico Basile, Equatorial Guinea","desc":"Pico Basile.","credit":"Serge Moons, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pico_Basil%C3%A9.jpg","alt":"A view of Pico Basile."}},
{"id":"gw-151","num":151,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Latvia","answerText":"Latvia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Restoration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 September 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Latvia"},"facts":[["Capital","Riga"],["Population","1.9 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","64,594 km² (24,940 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","latvia"],"abstract":"<b>Latvia</b> lies on the eastern shore of the Baltic Sea in northern Europe, and both the United Nations and the European Union give its area as 64,594 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its population is 1,854,000 by the United Nations count and 1,856,932 by Eurostat, and it has fallen in almost every year since 2015, when the World Bank series stood at 1,977,527.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The country counts 86.6 men for every 100 women, and its capital, Riga, held 633,800 people in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised Latvia on 28 July 1922, when the American Commissioner at Riga, Evan Young, informed the Latvian foreign office; Secretary of State Charles Evans Hughes had instructed him by telegram on 25 July to advise Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania of the decision on the 28th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established the same day, and a legation at Riga opened on 13 November 1922 under Frederick W. B. Coleman, who was accredited to all three Baltic states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The legation closed on 5 September 1940, after the Soviet Union occupied and annexed the three states that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States never recognised that incorporation, allowed Latvian representatives accredited by the last independent government to remain with diplomatic status, and held that relations had continued uninterrupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It recognised the restoration of Latvian independence on 2 September 1991, resumed normal relations on 5 September and opened an embassy at Riga on 2 October, and it regards the present government as a legal continuation of the interwar republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Latvia joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991 and the World Trade Organization on 10 February 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a member of NATO on 29 March 2004 and of the European Union on 1 May that year, entered the Schengen area on 21 December 2007 and adopted the euro on 1 January 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Latvia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lv.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Latvia – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/latvia_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Latvia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/latvia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Latvia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/latvia_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Latvia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LVA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/House_of_Blackheads_at_Dusk_3%2C_Riga%2C_Latvia_-_Diliff.jpg/1920px-House_of_Blackheads_at_Dusk_3%2C_Riga%2C_Latvia_-_Diliff.jpg","title":"House of the Blackheads (Riga), Latvia","desc":"House of the Blackheads (Riga).","credit":"Diliff, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_of_Blackheads_at_Dusk_3,_Riga,_Latvia_-_Diliff.jpg","alt":"A view of House of the Blackheads (Riga)."}},
{"id":"gw-152","num":152,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"North Macedonia","answerText":"North Macedonia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 April 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 February 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 September 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"North Macedonia"},"facts":[["Capital","Skopje"],["Population","1.8 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","25,713 km² (9,928 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","north macedonia"],"abstract":"<b>North Macedonia</b> is landlocked in the southern Balkans, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 25,713 square kilometres and its population as 1,814,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has fallen in every year since 2015, when the World Bank series stood at 1,912,430, and 94.8 men are counted for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Skopje, held 589,800 people in 2019, forest covers 39.4 per cent of the land, and income per head was 8,606 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country achieved independence in 1991 with the peaceful breakup of Yugoslavia, but recognition was delayed by Greece’s objection to its use of the name Macedonia, and it was first recognised under the provisional designation ‘the former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations under that designation on 8 April 1993, and the World Trade Organization on 4 April 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States opened a liaison office at Skopje on 3 December 1993, recognised the former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia on 9 February 1994, and confirmed on 13 September 1995 that relations existed, President Clinton announcing that he had received a positive response from President Kiro Gligorov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Christopher R. Hill presented his credentials on 29 July 1996 as the first American ambassador, and Washington began using the constitutional name Republic of Macedonia in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Prespa Accord signed with Greece in June 2018 settled the decades-long dispute over the name, and in February 2019 the country became the Republic of North Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became NATO’s 30th member on 27 March 2020, having joined the Partnership for Peace in 1995 and the Membership Action Plan in 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The European Union identified it as a potential candidate at Thessaloniki in 2003, its stabilisation and association agreement was the first in the region and has been in force since 2004, it applied in March 2004 and was granted candidate status in December 2005, and accession negotiations opened at an intergovernmental conference in July 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “North Macedonia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Macedonia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/macedonia. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “North Macedonia,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/north-macedonia_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “North Macedonia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/macedonia_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: North Macedonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MKD/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Ohrid_from_Samuel%27s_Fortress_2.jpg/1920px-Ohrid_from_Samuel%27s_Fortress_2.jpg","title":"Lake Ohrid, North Macedonia","desc":"Lake Ohrid.","credit":"kallerna, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ohrid_from_Samuel%27s_Fortress_2.jpg","alt":"A view of Lake Ohrid."}},
{"id":"gw-153","num":153,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kosovo","answerText":"Kosovo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomy ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1989</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resolution 1244</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 June 1999</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 February 2008</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ICJ opinion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 July 2010</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kosovo"},"facts":[["Population","1.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Balkans"],["EU status","Potential candidate"],["US recognition","18 February 2008"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","kosovo"],"abstract":"<b>Kosovo</b> is a landlocked territory of the western Balkans whose population the World Bank puts at 1,576,876 in 2025, down from 1,788,274 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ottomans ruled it for more than four centuries, until Serbia acquired the territory during the First Balkan War of 1912 and 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was partitioned in 1913 between Serbia and Montenegro, incorporated after the First World War into the Kingdom of the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, and made an autonomous province of Serbia within socialist Yugoslavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yugoslav constitution of 1974 gave it the standing of a socialist autonomous province with nearly the same rights as the six constituent republics, and in 1989 Slobodan Milošević ended that autonomy and imposed direct rule from Belgrade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peaceful resistance led by Ibrahim Rugova gave way to an armed one in 1997, and a NATO air campaign ran for 78 days from March to June 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Security Council adopted resolution 1244 at its 4011th meeting on 10 June 1999, by 14 votes to none with one abstention, suspending Belgrade’s governance and placing the territory under a United Nations interim administration with a NATO peacekeeping force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That force, KFOR, deployed on 12 June 1999 and is still there, its headquarters at Camp Film City in Pristina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kosovo declared independence on 17 February 2008, and the United States recognised it the next day, when Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice announced President George W. Bush’s decisions in a press statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly asked the International Court of Justice whether the declaration accorded with international law, and on 22 July 2010 the Court held that it did not violate international law, resolution 1244 having been silent on the territory’s final status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The European Union acknowledged the declaration in February 2008 while calling Kosovo a case sui generis, its stabilisation and association agreement came into force on 1 April 2016, and the Union lists its membership status as potential candidate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["World Bank, “Population, total: Kosovo,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/XKX/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kosovo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kosovo. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 1244 (1999), adopted at its 4011th meeting, on 10 June 1999,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/274488. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “NATO’s role in Kosovo,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natolive/topics_48818.htm. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Accordance with International Law of the Unilateral Declaration of Independence in Respect of Kosovo,” Advisory Opinion of 22 July 2010, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/141. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “Kosovo,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/kosovo_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Gra%C4%8Danica_Monastery%2C_2010.jpg/1920px-Gra%C4%8Danica_Monastery%2C_2010.jpg","title":"Gračanica Monastery, Kosovo","desc":"Gračanica Monastery.","credit":"Quinn Dombrowski, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gra%C4%8Danica_Monastery,_2010.jpg","alt":"A stone Byzantine church with clustered domes."}},
{"id":"gw-154","num":154,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bahrain","answerText":"Bahrain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resident envoy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 June 1974</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bahrain"},"facts":[["Capital","Manama"],["Population","1.6 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","778 km² (300 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","bahrain"],"abstract":"<b>Bahrain</b> is an island state in the Persian Gulf, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 778 square kilometres and its population as 1,643,000 in 2025, or about 2,099 people to the square kilometre; the World Bank series gives 1,600,366 for the same year, up from 1,370,322 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The World Bank’s area series both disagrees and moves, from 785 square kilometres in 2020 to 790 and then 800 by 2023, where the United Nations figure stands still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nearly nine in ten of its people live in towns, forest covers 0.8 per cent of the land, and income per head was 28,453 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Manama, held 600,400 people in 2019, and 163.4 men are counted for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1968 the British government announced its decision, reaffirmed in March 1971, to end its treaty relationships with the Persian Gulf sheikdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Bahrain first joined the other eight states then under British protection — Qatar and the seven Trucial Sheikhdoms, now the United Arab Emirates — in an effort to form a union of Arab emirates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The nine had still not agreed terms of union by 1971, which prompted Bahrain to declare itself fully independent on 15 August that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the State of Bahrain the same day, in a State Department press release, and opened an embassy at Manama on 21 September 1971 under a chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bahrain joined the United Nations on that same date, and the ambassador first accredited to it, who presented credentials on 17 February 1972, lived at Kuwait for the whole of his posting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Joseph W. Twinam presented his credentials on 10 June 1974 and became the first American ambassador resident at Manama, and the country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 13 December 1993 and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bahrain,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bh.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bahrain,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bahrain. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Bahrain, Kingdom of and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/bahrain_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bahrain,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Bahrain,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Tree_of_Life%2C_Bahrain_-_%E0%B4%9C%E0%B5%80%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%86_%E0%B4%AE%E0%B4%B0%E0%B4%82%2C_%E0%B4%AC%E0%B4%B9%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%88%E0%B5%BB_01.JPG/1920px-Tree_of_Life%2C_Bahrain_-_%E0%B4%9C%E0%B5%80%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%86_%E0%B4%AE%E0%B4%B0%E0%B4%82%2C_%E0%B4%AC%E0%B4%B9%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%88%E0%B5%BB_01.JPG","title":"Tree of Life (Bahrain), Bahrain","desc":"Tree of Life (Bahrain).","credit":"കാക്കര, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tree_of_Life,_Bahrain_-_%E0%B4%9C%E0%B5%80%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%86_%E0%B4%AE%E0%B4%B0%E0%B4%82,_%E0%B4%AC%E0%B4%B9%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%88%E0%B5%BB_01.JPG","alt":"A view of Tree of Life (Bahrain)."}},
{"id":"gw-155","num":155,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Timor-Leste","answerText":"Timor-Leste","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 May 2002</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 May 2002</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 September 2002</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 August 2024</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Timor-Leste"},"facts":[["Capital","Dili"],["Population","1.4 million (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","14,919 km² (5,760 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","timor-leste"],"abstract":"<b>Timor-Leste</b> occupies the eastern half of the island of Timor in south-eastern Asia, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 14,919 square kilometres and its population as 1,418,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by nearly a fifth in ten years, from 1,205,131 in 2015, and only about three in ten of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Dili, held 281,100 people in 2019, income per head was 1,503 US dollars in 2023, the country uses the United States dollar as its own currency, and there are 95.4 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory had been a Portuguese colony until 1975 and was under Indonesian sovereignty from 1976 to 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After a popular referendum held under United Nations auspices in 1999, the International Force for East Timor kept the peace until formal independence; before that the United States had had limited relations of any sort with the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Security Council had placed the territory under a United Nations transitional administration by resolution 1272, adopted unanimously at its 4057th meeting on 25 October 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Independence came on 20 May 2002, and the United States recognised the country the same day, opening an embassy at Dili under Chargé d’Affaires Shari Villarossa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Security Council recommended admission without a vote at its 4542nd meeting on 23 May 2002, three days after independence, and the General Assembly admitted the Democratic Republic of Timor-Leste on 27 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grover Rees 3rd became the first American ambassador when he presented his credentials on 10 December 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the World Trade Organization on 30 August 2024, and the World Bank measures its surface area at 14,870 square kilometres against the United Nations figure of 14,919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Timor-Leste,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tl.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Timor-Leste,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TLS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Timor-Leste,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/timor-leste. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 1272 (1999), adopted at its 4057th meeting, on 25 October 1999,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/291410. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 1414 (2002), adopted at its 4542nd meeting, on 23 May 2002,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/465317. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Timor-Leste and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/timor_leste_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Timor-Leste,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TLS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Christ_Dili.jpg/1920px-Christ_Dili.jpg","title":"Cristo Rei of Dili, Timor-Leste","desc":"Cristo Rei of Dili.","credit":"Kok Leng, Yeo from Singapore, Singapore, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christ_Dili.jpg","alt":"A view of Cristo Rei of Dili."}},
{"id":"gw-156","num":156,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Estonia","answerText":"Estonia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation Tallinn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Restoration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Estonia"},"facts":[["Capital","Tallinn"],["Population","1.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","45,339 km² (17,505 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","estonia"],"abstract":"<b>Estonia</b> lies on the eastern shore of the Baltic Sea in northern Europe, and its three published areas do not agree: 45,261 square kilometres at the United Nations, 45,336 at Eurostat and 45,340 at the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 1,344,000 by the United Nations count and 1,369,995 by Eurostat, and the capital, Tallinn, held 441,300 people in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Income per head reached 30,201 US dollars in 2023, against 17,733 in 2015, and the country is divided into 15 counties and 79 municipalities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Estonia on 28 July 1922, when the American Commissioner at Riga, Evan Young, informed the Estonian foreign office; Secretary of State Charles Evans Hughes had instructed him by telegram on 25 July to advise Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania of the decision on the 28th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Young continued as the American representative to all three Baltic states, with the new rank of minister, and no separate legation was opened at Tallinn until 30 June 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That legation closed on 5 September 1940, after the Soviet Union occupied and annexed the three states in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States never recognised the incorporation, permitted Estonian representatives accredited by the last independent government to remain with diplomatic status, and regards the present government as a legal continuation of the interwar republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It recognised the restoration of Estonian independence on 2 September 1991, and normal relations resumed two days later, when a memorandum of understanding was signed by Deputy Assistant Secretary Curtis Kamman and the Estonian foreign minister, Lennart Meri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Estonia joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991 and the World Trade Organization on 13 November 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a member of NATO on 29 March 2004 and of the European Union on 1 May that year, entered the Schengen area on 21 December 2007 and adopted the euro on 1 January 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Estonia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ee.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Estonia – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/estonia_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Estonia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/estonia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Estonia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/estonia_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Estonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/EST/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Old_Town_of_Tallinn%2C_Tallinn%2C_Estonia_-_panoramio_%2858%29.jpg/1920px-Old_Town_of_Tallinn%2C_Tallinn%2C_Estonia_-_panoramio_%2858%29.jpg","title":"Tallinn Old Town, Estonia","desc":"Tallinn Old Town.","credit":"Ben Bender, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Town_of_Tallinn,_Tallinn,_Estonia_-_panoramio_(58).jpg","alt":"A view of Tallinn Old Town."}},
{"id":"gw-157","num":157,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Trinidad and Tobago","answerText":"Trinidad and Tobago","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 October 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Trinidad and Tobago"},"facts":[["Capital","Port of Spain"],["Population","1.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","5,130 km² (1,980 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","caribbean","trinidad and tobago"],"abstract":"<b>Trinidad and Tobago</b> is a two-island state at the southern end of the Caribbean, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 5,127 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its published populations differ by a tenth: the United Nations gives 1,511,000 for 2025 where the World Bank gives 1,367,764 and the Commonwealth Secretariat 1.4 million for 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth counts the islands separately, at about 4,800 square kilometres for Trinidad and 300 for Tobago, and describes three mountain ranges and high waterfalls on the larger island and a single range, beaches and coral reefs on the smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Just over half the population lives in towns, the urban agglomeration of the capital, Port of Spain, held 544,300 people in 2019, and income per head was 16,960 US dollars in 2023, down from 18,781 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Spanish and then British colonial rule, the two islands achieved independence on 31 August 1962 and joined the Commonwealth that year, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the new state the same day by establishing an embassy at Port-of-Spain, with William H. Christensen as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> William Henry Hastie, a judge of the United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit, led the American delegation at the independence ceremonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Robert G. Miner presented his credentials as the first American ambassador on 1 December 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 18 September 1962 and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 23 October that year, less than two months after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It became a member of the World Trade Organization on 1 March 1995, and the American guide to recognition calls it one of the most industrialised countries in the English-speaking Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Trinidad and Tobago,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tt.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Trinidad and Tobago,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TTO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Trinidad and Tobago,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/trinidad-and-tobago. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Trinidad and Tobago,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/trinidad-and-tobago. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Trinidad and Tobago and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/trinidad_tobago_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Digging_asphalt%2C_Pitch_Lake%2C_Trinidad%2C_W.I.%2C_by_Detroit_Publishing_Company%2C_1890s%2C_from_the_Library_of_Congress_-_det.4a05483.jpg/1920px-Digging_asphalt%2C_Pitch_Lake%2C_Trinidad%2C_W.I.%2C_by_Detroit_Publishing_Company%2C_1890s%2C_from_the_Library_of_Congress_-_det.4a05483.jpg","title":"Pitch Lake, Trinidad and Tobago","desc":"Pitch Lake.","credit":"Detroit Publishing Company, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Digging_asphalt,_Pitch_Lake,_Trinidad,_W.I.,_by_Detroit_Publishing_Company,_1890s,_from_the_Library_of_Congress_-_det.4a05483.jpg","alt":"A view of Pitch Lake."}},
{"id":"gw-158","num":158,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cyprus","answerText":"Cyprus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":["Cyprus","N. Cyprus","Cyprus U.N. Buffer Zone"]},"facts":[["Capital","Nicosia"],["Population","1.4 million (2025)"],["Region","Western Asia"],["Area","9,251 km² (3,572 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","cyprus"],"abstract":"<b>Cyprus</b> is an island in the eastern Mediterranean, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 9,251 square kilometres against Eurostat’s 9,253.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its published populations count different territories: the United Nations gives 1,371,000 for 2025 and marks the figure as referring to the whole country, the World Bank agrees at 1,370,754, and Eurostat gives 979,865, which is the area the government controls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital, Nicosia, held 269,500 people in 2018, forest covers 18.7 per cent of the land, and income per head was 37,077 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island had been a British Crown Colony since 1925 and became independent in 1960, and the United States recognised it on 16 August that year in a State Department press release.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and an American embassy at Nicosia began the same day, when the consulate general was elevated, and Ambassador Fraser Wilkins presented his credentials on 19 September 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Cyprus joined the United Nations on 20 September 1960 and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 15 July 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1983 a Turkish-controlled area in the north of the island declared its independence, calling itself the Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Turkey has been the only country in the world to recognise it, and the United States, which never has, has maintained diplomatic relations with Cyprus without interruption since 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It became a member of the World Trade Organization on 30 July 1995 and of the European Union on 1 May 2004, and adopted the euro on 1 January 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a presidential republic whose president is both head of state and head of government, and the European Union records it as still in the process of joining the Schengen area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cyprus,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cy.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Cyprus – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/cyprus_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cyprus,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cyprus. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cyprus and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cyprus_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cyprus,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Roca_de_Afrodita%2C_Chipre%2C_2021-12-10%2C_DD_65.jpg/1920px-Roca_de_Afrodita%2C_Chipre%2C_2021-12-10%2C_DD_65.jpg","title":"Petra tou Romiou, Cyprus","desc":"Petra tou Romiou.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roca_de_Afrodita,_Chipre,_2021-12-10,_DD_65.jpg","alt":"A view of Petra tou Romiou."}},
{"id":"gw-159","num":159,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mauritius","answerText":"Mauritius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 March 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 March 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 April 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">SADC member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 August 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mauritius"},"facts":[["Capital","Port Louis"],["Population","1.3 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","2,040 km² (788 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","mauritius"],"abstract":"<b>Mauritius</b> is an island state in the south-west Indian Ocean, about 2,400 kilometres from the south-east coast of Africa, whose land area the Southern African Development Community gives as 2,040 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main island is 67 kilometres long and 46 at its widest, and owes its origin to submarine volcanic activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The published areas differ because they count different islands: the United Nations gives 1,979 square kilometres and says in a footnote that this excludes Saint Brandon and Agalega, while the Commonwealth gives 1,864 for Mauritius itself, 104 for Rodrigues and 2,050 including the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is 1,268,000 by the United Nations count, which does include those islands, and 1,243,741 by the World Bank; forest covers 19.5 per cent of the land and income per head was 11,304 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> English is the official language, French and Creole are widely spoken, and the country is a parliamentary democracy in which coalition government is the norm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Mauritius had been under British sovereignty and became independent on 12 March 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it that same day, when an American embassy was established at Port Louis with William B. Hussey as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 24 April 1968 and the Commonwealth in 1968, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the Southern African Development Community in 1995, attending the bloc’s 15th summit at Midrand in South Africa on 28 August as its 12th member state and its first island nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 2 September 1970, and became a member of the World Trade Organization when that body opened on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mauritius,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mu.html. [Open access]","Southern African Development Community, “Mauritius,” Member States, https://www.sadc.int/member-states/mauritius. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Mauritius,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/mauritius. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Mauritius,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MUS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mauritius,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mauritius. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Mauritius and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/mauritius_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Le_Morne_Peninsula_in_Mauritius_%2853697779236%29.jpg/1920px-Le_Morne_Peninsula_in_Mauritius_%2853697779236%29.jpg","title":"Le Morne Brabant, Mauritius","desc":"Le Morne Brabant.","credit":"\"dronepicr\", CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Morne_Peninsula_in_Mauritius_(53697779236).jpg","alt":"A view of Le Morne Brabant."}},
{"id":"gw-160","num":160,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Eswatini","answerText":"Eswatini","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"eSwatini"},"facts":[["Capitals","Mbabane and Lobamba"],["Population","1.3 million (2025)"],["Region","Southern Africa"],["Area","17,364 km² (6,704 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","eswatini"],"abstract":"<b>Eswatini</b> is a landlocked kingdom in southern Africa, bordered by Mozambique to the east and by South Africa on every other side, whose area the Southern African Development Community gives as 17,364 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations puts the population at 1,256,000 in 2025 and the Commonwealth Secretariat at 1.16 million in 2022, and only about a quarter of it lives in towns, at 72.3 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has two capitals, and the United Nations records the division in a footnote of its own: Mbabane is the administrative capital and Lobamba the legislative one, which is also the seat of the royal court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mbabane held only 68,000 people in 2018, income per head was 3,717 US dollars in 2023, and the Commonwealth gives the area a little higher, at 17,400 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its listed natural resources are sugar, food products, wood pulp and wildlife, and the community calls it the only absolute monarchy in southern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The territory had been under British sovereignty and became independent on 6 September 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Swaziland that same day, when an American embassy was established at Mbabane with Chris C. Pappas, Jr., as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 24 September 1968, and the Commonwealth in 1968 following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was a founding member of the Southern African Development Co-ordination Conference, formed at Lusaka in Zambia in April 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 2018 the country was renamed Eswatini, having joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 8 February 1993 and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995 under its former name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Eswatini,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sz.html. [Open access]","Southern African Development Community, “Kingdom of Eswatini,” Member States, https://www.sadc.int/member-states/kingdom-eswatini. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Eswatini,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/kingdom-eswatini. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Swaziland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/swaziland. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Eswatini and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/swaziland_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Granite_monolith.jpg","title":"Sibebe, Eswatini","desc":"Sibebe.","credit":"theswazigirl, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Granite_monolith.jpg","alt":"A view of Sibebe."}},
{"id":"gw-161","num":161,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Djibouti","answerText":"Djibouti","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 June 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 June 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Djibouti"},"facts":[["Capital","Djibouti"],["Population","1.2 million (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","23,200 km² (8,960 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","djibouti"],"abstract":"<b>Djibouti</b> is a small country of the Horn of Africa, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 23,200 square kilometres and its population as 1,184,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by about a sixth in ten years, from 1,020,313 in 2015, and the World Bank measures the same area at 23,200 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly four in five of its people live in towns, forest covers 0.2 per cent of the land, and income per head was 3,469 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, which shares the country’s name, held 568,800 people in 2019 — close to half the country — though the United Nations notes that the figure is for the surrounding cercle rather than the city alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory that became Djibouti had been under French sovereignty, and the American post there was a consulate general to the French Territory of the Afars and the Issas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the Republic of Djibouti on 27 June 1977, in a congratulatory message from President Jimmy Carter to President Hassan Gouled Aptidon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established the same day, and rather than an embassy being opened that consulate general became the American embassy at Djibouti, under Walter S. Clarke as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 20 September 1977, less than three months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 16 December 1994, five months before the World Trade Organization admitted it on 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Services employ 92.9 per cent of everyone in work, and recorded exports rose from 251 million US dollars in 2015 to 4,466 million in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Djibouti,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dj.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Djibouti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DJI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Djibouti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DJI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Djibouti,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/djibouti. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Djibouti and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/djibouti_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Abbe-8.jpg/1920px-Abbe-8.jpg","title":"Lake Abbe, Djibouti","desc":"Lake Abbe.","credit":"Jbcombes, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbe-8.jpg","alt":"A view of Lake Abbe."}},
{"id":"gw-162","num":162,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Fiji","answerText":"Fiji","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 August 1844</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crown colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1874</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 July 1971</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Fiji"},"facts":[["Capital","Suva"],["Population","930,000 (2025)"],["Region","Melanesia"],["Area","18,272 km² (7,055 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","fiji"],"abstract":"<b>Fiji</b> is an island country of Melanesia, and the United Nations and the Commonwealth Secretariat both give its surface area as 18,272 square kilometres, with a population of 933,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That population has barely moved in a decade, from 918,578 in 2015 to 933,154 in 2025, at 51.1 people to the square kilometre; forest covers 60.6 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Suva, held 178,300 people in 2018, and income per head was 5,889 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States kept a consular presence here long before Britain took the islands: Commercial Agent John B. Williams was appointed the first consul to the Fiji Islands on 19 August 1844, resident at Auckland in New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island chain became a Crown colony within the British Empire thirty years later, in 1874.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Fiji gained independence from British rule on 10 October 1970, and the United States recognised it that day in a statement by Charles W. Yost, its representative in the United Nations Security Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations three days later, on 13 October 1970.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 22 July 1971, when Fiji’s ambassador Semesa K. Sikivou presented his credentials to President Richard M. Nixon, and an American embassy at Suva followed on 1 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No American ambassador lived there until John P. Condon presented his credentials on 11 April 1978, his predecessors having been resident at Wellington; a military coup in 1987 began what the guide to American recognition calls the coup cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Commonwealth suspended Fiji’s membership three times — from October 1987 to October 1997, from June 2000 to December 2001 and from December 2006 to September 2014 — and the country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 16 November 1993 and the World Trade Organization on 14 January 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Fiji,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fj.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Fiji,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/fiji. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Fiji,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FJI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Fiji,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/fiji. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Fiji and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/fiji_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Nanuya.jpg/1920px-Nanuya.jpg","title":"Yasawa Islands, Fiji","desc":"Yasawa Islands.","credit":"Schnop79, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanuya.jpg","alt":"A view of Yasawa Islands."}},
{"id":"gw-163","num":163,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Comoros","answerText":"Comoros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy Moroni</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 August 1985</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Comoros"},"facts":[["Capital","Moroni"],["Population","880,000 (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","1,861 km² (719 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","comoros"],"abstract":"<b>Comoros</b> is an archipelago in the Mozambique Channel, and its two published areas count different islands: the United Nations gives 2,235 square kilometres and the World Bank 1,861, a difference of nearly 400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difference is Mayotte, which France governs and which the General Assembly has treated as Comorian since resolution 31/4 of 21 October 1976; the question was still on its agenda in September 2025, 49 years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 883,000 by the United Nations count and 882,847 by the World Bank, at 474 people to the square kilometre, and it joined the United Nations on 12 November 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 18.9 per cent of the land, the capital, Moroni, held 62,400 people in 2018, and income per head was 1,703 US dollars in 2023, against 1,329 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised the Republic of the Comoros on 15 August 1977, and recognition and diplomatic relations came in one act: a joint communiqué issued by the two governments announcing them together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The first American chargé d’affaires ad interim, Robert S. Barrett IV, presented his credentials the next day and was resident at Antananarivo in Madagascar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American embassy at Moroni was established on 26 August 1985.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It closed on 30 September 1993, after eight years, and later ambassadors were accredited from Port Louis in Mauritius instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 6 March 2006 the responsibility moved again, from the embassy at Port Louis to the one at Antananarivo — the third capital to hold it, and the one it started from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Comoros became a member of the World Trade Organization on 21 August 2024, 49 years after joining the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Comoros,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/km.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Comoros,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Comoros,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Comoros,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/comoros. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Question of the Comorian Island of Mayotte,” A/RES/31/4, adopted at the 39th plenary meeting, 21 October 1976, United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/188920. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Question of the Comorian island of Mayotte,” A/DEC/79/576, 5 September 2025, United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4087867. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Comoros and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/comoros_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Mount_Karthala_%2811000398163%29.jpg","title":"Mount Karthala, the Comoros","desc":"Mount Karthala.","credit":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Karthala_(11000398163).jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Karthala."}},
{"id":"gw-164","num":164,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guyana","answerText":"Guyana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1831</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 May 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 July 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1966</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guyana"},"facts":[["Capital","Georgetown"],["Population","840,000 (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","214,969 km² (83,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","guyana"],"abstract":"<b>Guyana</b> lies on the northern coast of South America, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 214,969 square kilometres and its population as 836,000 in 2025 — 4.2 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers 93.8 per cent of the land, the capital, Georgetown, held 109,900 people in 2018, and 94.8 men are counted for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head has more than tripled in three years, from 6,779 US dollars in 2020 to 20,189 in 2023, having been 5,640 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dutch colonies of Essequibo, Demerara and Berbice, which make up the modern country, were ceded to Great Britain at the Congress of Vienna and established as a British colony in 1831.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Guyanese independence on 26 May 1966 by establishing an American embassy at Georgetown, with Delmar R. Carlson as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 5 July 1966, six weeks after independence, and became a member of the World Trade Organization when that body opened on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September 1966 and the Commonwealth that year, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Commonwealth gives the population as 786,560 in 2022 and rounds the area to 215,000 square kilometres, while the World Bank’s series gives 835,986 for 2025, a figure that has risen by a tenth since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of that territory is claimed by Venezuela, and Guyana has taken the dispute to the International Court of Justice as <i>Arbitral Award of 3 October 1899</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Court held public hearings on the merits at the Peace Palace from 4 to 11 May 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Guyana,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gy.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guyana,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guyana. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Guyana and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/guyana_e.htm. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Guyana,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/guyana. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guyana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUY/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Arbitral Award of 3 October 1899, Guyana v. Venezuela,” https://www.icj-cij.org/case/171. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/GuyanaKaieteurFalls2004.jpg","title":"Kaieteur Falls, Guyana","desc":"Kaieteur Falls.","credit":"Sorenriise at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GuyanaKaieteurFalls2004.jpg","alt":"A view of Kaieteur Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-165","num":165,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Solomon Islands","answerText":"Solomon Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 July 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 September 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 July 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Solomon Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Honiara"],["Population","840,000 (2025)"],["Region","Melanesia"],["Area","28,896 km² (11,157 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","solomon islands"],"abstract":"<b>Solomon Islands</b> is an archipelago of Melanesia, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 28,896 square kilometres and its population as 839,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by nearly a third in ten years, from 639,415 in 2015, and fewer than a quarter of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three published areas do not agree: 28,896 at the United Nations, 28,900 at the World Bank and 30,407 at the Commonwealth Secretariat, which also gives the population as 686,900 for 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The capital, Honiara, held 81,800 people in 2018, and income per head has barely moved in a decade, from 2,045 US dollars in 2015 to 2,042 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American relations with the islands began in the late 19th century, when United States vessels started stopping at them, and in the Second World War they became the site of intense fighting, particularly on Guadalcanal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The islands had been under British sovereignty, and the United States recognised them on 7 July 1978, when Senator John H. Glenn, as the President’s personal representative, delivered a letter to that effect from President Jimmy Carter to Prime Minister Peter Kenilorea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They joined the United Nations on 19 September 1978, and diplomatic relations were established on 9 October, when Mary S. Olmsted presented her credentials as ambassador — accredited to Papua New Guinea as well, and resident at Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American consulate at Honiara was upgraded to an embassy on 4 July 1988.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That embassy closed on 30 July 1993 and reopened on 27 January 2023, after nearly thirty years, during which the ambassador again lived at Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth in 1978 following independence from Britain, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 28 December 1994 and the World Trade Organization on 26 July 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Solomon Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sb.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Solomon Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Solomon Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2018:2023. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Solomon Islands,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/solomon-islands. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Solomon Islands,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/solomon-islands. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Solomon Islands and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/solomon_islands_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Marovo_Lagoon_%2816_August_2008%29.jpg/1920px-Marovo_Lagoon_%2816_August_2008%29.jpg","title":"Marovo Lagoon, the Solomon Islands","desc":"Marovo Lagoon.","credit":"Xplore Dive from Mooloolaba, Australia, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marovo_Lagoon_(16_August_2008).jpg","alt":"A shallow turquoise lagoon fringed with palms."}},
{"id":"gw-166","num":166,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bhutan","answerText":"Bhutan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recommended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 February 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 October 1999</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bhutan"},"facts":[["Capital","Thimphu"],["Population","800,000 (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Area","38,394 km² (14,824 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","bhutan"],"abstract":"<b>Bhutan</b> is a landlocked country of the eastern Himalaya, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 38,394 square kilometres and its population as 797,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank agrees closely, at 796,682 people and 38,390 square kilometres, and that population has grown by about a twelfth in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only two in five of its people live in towns, at 20.8 to the square kilometre across the country as a whole, and the capital, Thimphu, held 203,300 of them in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 3,839 US dollars in 2023 against 2,954 in 2015, recorded exports were 812 million dollars against 782 million, and forest covers 71.2 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Security Council recommended the country for membership of the United Nations unanimously, at its 1566th meeting, on 10 February 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted it at its 1934th plenary meeting on 21 September 1971, more than seven months after that recommendation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bhutan is not a member of the World Trade Organization: its working party on accession was established on 6 October 1999 and the accession has not been completed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Agriculture employed 58 per cent of everyone in work in 2015 and 43.8 per cent by 2023, while services rose from 32.4 to 40.9 per cent and industry from 9.7 to 15.3.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Use of the internet rose from 39.8 people in every hundred in 2015 to 88.4 by 2023, and the fertility rate fell from 1.8 births per woman to 1.4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 75.6 years for women and 71.8 for men, under-five mortality has fallen from 30.6 deaths per thousand live births to 20.5, and the country counts 114.6 men for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bhutan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bt.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bhutan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BTN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Bhutan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BTN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2018:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 292 (1971), adopted at its 1566th meeting, of 10 February 1971,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/90800. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Admission of Bhutan to membership in the United Nations,” A/RES/2751 (XXVI), United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192050. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accession status: Bhutan,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_bhoutan_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Paro_Taktsang%2C_Bhutan_%28edited%29.jpg/1920px-Paro_Taktsang%2C_Bhutan_%28edited%29.jpg","title":"Paro Taktsang, Bhutan","desc":"Paro Taktsang.","credit":"Original: Nina R from Africa Derivative work: UnpetitproleX, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paro_Taktsang,_Bhutan_(edited).jpg","alt":"A view of Paro Taktsang."}},
{"id":"gw-167","num":167,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Macau","answerText":"Macau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 January 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sea area since</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2015</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Macao"},"facts":[["Population","720,000 (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Asia"],["Area","33 km² (13 sq mi)"],["Density","22,563 per km²"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","asia","macau"],"abstract":"<b>Macau</b> lies on the southeastern coast of China, in the Pearl River Delta, and consists of a peninsula and islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land has grown by reclamation, from around 29.5 square kilometres in 2010 to 32.9 at the end of 2018, and since 2015 it has also exercised jurisdiction over a maritime area of 85 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s own area series records the same growth, holding at 32.9 square kilometres through 2020 and 33 from 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives the area as 33 square kilometres and the population as 722,000 in 2025, which is 22,563 people to the square kilometre; the World Bank counts 685,900, having recorded 642,900 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Agriculture employs 0.5 per cent of those in work, and income per head fell from 72,404 US dollars in 2015 to 37,103 in 2020 before recovering to 64,158 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At the by-census of 2016, 88.7 per cent of the population was of Chinese ethnicity, down 3.7 percentage points on 2011, and 1.8 per cent of Portuguese ethnicity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among residents aged three and over, 80.1 per cent spoke mostly Cantonese at home, 5.5 per cent Mandarin, 3.0 per cent Tagalog, 2.8 per cent English and 0.6 per cent Portuguese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the population was born in mainland China, 43.6 per cent against the 40.7 per cent born in the territory itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Chief Executive is selected by election or through consultation held locally, serves five years and may not serve more than two consecutive terms; an amendment in 2012 raised the election committee from 300 members to 400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 11 January 1991, four years before the World Trade Organization opened, and was a founding member of that body on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations, “Common Core Document Forming Part of the Reports of States Parties: Macao, China,” HRI/CORE/CHN-MAC/2019, 5 August 2020, United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3884318. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Macao SAR, China,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2018:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “China, Macao SAR,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mo.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Macao, China and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/macao_china_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Macao SAR, China,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAC/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/%E5%A4%A7%E4%B8%89%E5%B7%B4%E7%89%8C%E5%9D%8A.jpg/1920px-%E5%A4%A7%E4%B8%89%E5%B7%B4%E7%89%8C%E5%9D%8A.jpg","title":"Ruins of St. Paul's, Macau","desc":"Ruins of St. Paul's.","credit":"GG001213, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A4%A7%E4%B8%89%E5%B7%B4%E7%89%8C%E5%9D%8A.jpg","alt":"A view of Ruins of St. Paul's."}},
{"id":"gw-168","num":168,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Luxembourg","answerText":"Luxembourg","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1878</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 July 1903</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 July 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Luxembourg"},"facts":[["Capital","Luxembourg"],["Population","680,000 (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Area","2,586 km² (998 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","luxembourg"],"abstract":"<b>Luxembourg</b> is landlocked in western Europe, and its three published areas differ a little: 2,586 square kilometres at the United Nations, 2,590 at the World Bank and 2,595 at Eurostat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is 680,000 by the United Nations count, 681,973 by Eurostat and 686,970 by the World Bank, having grown by a fifth since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Income per head was 128,936 US dollars in 2023 against 105,475 in 2015, and more than nine in ten of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a grand duchy and a parliamentary constitutional monarchy, whose Chamber of Deputies has 60 members elected every five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country achieved full independence in 1867, and the United States recognised it on 31 May 1878, when President Rutherford B. Hayes issued an exequatur to François Berger, the consul general ad interim of Luxembourg at New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomatic relations began on 17 July 1903, when the American ambassador to the Netherlands presented his credentials to Luxembourg and stayed resident at The Hague; in 1923 the accreditation passed to the ambassador at Brussels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany occupied the country on 10 May 1940 and the American legation closed on 15 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States then accredited its ambassador to Canada to the Luxembourg government in exile at Ottawa, and when that government moved to the United Kingdom on 14 October 1943 credentials were presented to it in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The legation at Luxembourg reopened on 23 September 1944, and the representation was raised to ambassador on 21 September 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Luxembourg was a founding member of the United Nations on 24 October 1945 and of the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 1 January 1948, joined the European Communities at their start on 1 January 1958, entered the Schengen area on 26 March 1995 and adopted the euro on 1 January 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Luxembourg,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lu.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Luxembourg,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LUX/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Luxembourg – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/luxembourg_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Luxembourg,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LUX/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Luxembourg,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/luxembourg. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Luxembourg and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/luxembourg_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Luxembourg_City_pano_Wikimedia_Commons.jpg/1920px-Luxembourg_City_pano_Wikimedia_Commons.jpg","title":"Luxembourg City, Luxembourg","desc":"Luxembourg City above the Alzette valley.","credit":"Benh LIEU SONG, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxembourg_City_pano_Wikimedia_Commons.jpg","alt":"An old town on a rock spur above a green river valley."}},
{"id":"gw-169","num":169,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Suriname","answerText":"Suriname","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 December 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 March 1978</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Suriname"},"facts":[["Capital","Paramaribo"],["Population","640,000 (2025)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","163,820 km² (63,251 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","suriname"],"abstract":"<b>Suriname</b> is on the northeastern coast of South America, and the United Nations and the World Bank agree exactly on its surface area, 163,820 square kilometres, with a population of 640,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At 4.1 people to the square kilometre it is thinly settled, two in three of them in towns, and the capital, Paramaribo, held 239,500 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by a tenth since 2015, when the World Bank counted 581,627, forest covers 95 per cent of the land, and agriculture employs only 7.4 per cent of those in work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head has fallen rather than risen, from 8,814 US dollars in 2015 to 6,792 in 2020 and 5,976 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory had been a Dutch colony dating from 1667, was accorded increasing degrees of autonomy from 1951, and was granted independence by the Netherlands in 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A ceremony to proclaim that independence was held at Paramaribo on 25 November 1975, and the United States was represented at it by the Secretary of the Navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That same day President Gerald Ford wrote to President Johan Henri Eliza Ferrier extending American recognition and saying that he hoped for the prompt establishment of diplomatic relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Relations and an American embassy at Paramaribo were established on that date too, with Robert L. Flanagin as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Suriname joined the United Nations on 4 December 1975, nine days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1980 the elected parliamentary government was overthrown and a military regime installed in its place; a democratic government was re-established in 1991, and the country acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 22 March 1978 and to the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Suriname,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sr.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Suriname,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SUR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Suriname,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/suriname. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Suriname and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/suriname_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Suriname,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SUR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Houses_at_Waterkant%2C_Paramaribo.JPG/1920px-Houses_at_Waterkant%2C_Paramaribo.JPG","title":"Paramaribo, Suriname","desc":"The Waterkant at Paramaribo.","credit":"Mark Ahsmann, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houses_at_Waterkant,_Paramaribo.JPG","alt":"Tall white-painted wooden colonial houses along a street."}},
{"id":"gw-170","num":170,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Montenegro","answerText":"Montenegro","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 March 1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrawn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 December 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 June 2006</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 June 2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Montenegro"},"facts":[["Capital","Podgorica"],["Population","620,000 (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","13,812 km² (5,333 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","montenegro"],"abstract":"<b>Montenegro</b> is in the western Balkans, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 13,812 square kilometres and its population as 633,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 623,129.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Income per head was 11,885 US dollars in 2023 against 6,455 in 2015, two in three of its people live in towns, and the capital, Podgorica, held 177,200 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Great Powers of Europe recognised an independent and sovereign Principality of Montenegro at the Congress of Berlin in 1878.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 3 March 1905, when President Theodore Roosevelt approved the Diplomatic and Consular Act, which assigned the American mission in Greece responsibility for representation in Montenegro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the First World War the kingdom was subsumed into the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, and President Woodrow Wilson decided on 30 December 1920 to withdraw recognition of an independent Montenegro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 21 January 1921 the acting Secretary of State told Montenegro’s honorary consul-general that, in view of the present status of Montenegro, the government no longer considered it necessary to accord recognition to her diplomatic and consular officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Republic of Montenegro declared independence from the State Union of Serbia and Montenegro on 3 June 2006, and the United States recognised it again on 13 June, 85 years after withdrawing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 15 August 2006, and the country had joined the United Nations on 28 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It applied for European Union membership in 2008, was granted candidate status in 2010, and accession negotiations opened on 29 June 2012; all 33 screened chapters have been opened and 16 provisionally closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 29 April 2012 and became a member of NATO on 5 June 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Montenegro,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/me.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Montenegro,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Montenegro,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/montenegro. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “Montenegro,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/montenegro_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Montenegro and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/montenegro_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Kotor_aerial_1.jpg/1920px-Kotor_aerial_1.jpg","title":"Bay of Kotor, Montenegro","desc":"Bay of Kotor.","credit":"kallerna, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kotor_aerial_1.jpg","alt":"A view of Bay of Kotor."}},
{"id":"gw-171","num":171,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Western Sahara","answerText":"Western Sahara","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">ICJ opinion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 October 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">MINURSO set up</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 April 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"W. Sahara"},"facts":[["Capital","El Aaiún"],["Population","601,000 (2025)"],["Region","Northern Africa"],["Area","266,000 km² (103,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","africa","western sahara"],"abstract":"<b>Western Sahara</b> is a territory in northwestern Africa of 266,000 square kilometres, with 601,000 people in 2025 and its largest settlement, El Aaiún, holding 232,400 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations notes that this area comprises the Northern Region, the former Saguia el Hamra, and the Southern Region, the former Rio de Oro, the two parts of the territory Spain colonised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is desert of 2.3 people to the square kilometre, 2.5 per cent of it forested, and the Moroccan dirham is the currency in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In December 1974 the General Assembly asked the International Court of Justice whether the territory had belonged to no one when Spain colonised it, and what legal ties it had with Morocco and the Mauritanian entity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Court answered on 16 October 1975 that it had not been unowned, and that ties of allegiance existed between the Sultan of Morocco and some of its tribes, and rights over land between the Mauritanian entity and the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It found no tie of territorial sovereignty with either, and so nothing to affect self-determination or the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples of 14 December 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 29 April 1991 the Security Council unanimously established the United Nations Mission for the Referendum in Western Sahara, to organise and supervise a vote on self-determination with the Organization of African Unity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That vote has not been held, and the Secretariat’s 2025 working paper for the Special Committee on decolonization sets out both parties’ positions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Morocco holds that a solution should rest on its autonomy initiative and on its territorial integrity, while the Frente Popular para la Liberación de Saguía el-Hamra y Rio de Oro holds that this is the last colony in Africa, whose people have an inalienable right to self-determination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Secretary-General reported in 2024 that the situation was marked by low-intensity hostilities, with intermittent firing across the berm, and that the lack of a full ceasefire remained a major setback.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Western Sahara,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/eh.html. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Western Sahara: Overview of the Case,” Advisory Opinion of 16 October 1975, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/61. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples,” A/RES/1514, 14 December 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206145. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 690, adopted at its 2984th meeting, on 29 April 1991,” S/RES/690, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/112199. [Open access]","UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Western Sahara: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/17, 8 January 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4073767. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Dakhla%2C_Western_Sahara_%2811%29.jpg/1920px-Dakhla%2C_Western_Sahara_%2811%29.jpg","title":"Dakhla, Western Sahara, Western Sahara","desc":"Dakhla, Western Sahara.","credit":"YoTuT from United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dakhla,_Western_Sahara_(11).jpg","alt":"A view of Dakhla, Western Sahara."}},
{"id":"gw-172","num":172,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Malta","answerText":"Malta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Euro area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2008</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malta"},"facts":[["Capital","Valletta"],["Population","570,000 (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","316 km² (122 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","malta"],"abstract":"<b>Malta</b> is an archipelago in the central Mediterranean, and the European Union gives its area as 316 square kilometres and its population as 574,250, where the United Nations gives 545,000 for 2025 and the World Bank 568,847 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is among the most crowded countries on earth, at more than 1,700 people to the square kilometre, with 94.7 per cent of them living in towns and 1.4 per cent of the land forested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes islands that are mainly low, rocky and flat, with many coastal cliffs, the three largest inhabited, and Sicily to the north with Libya and Tunisia to the south and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Malta was a Crown Colony of the United Kingdom from 1814 until 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 18 September 1964, when President Lyndon B. Johnson sent a letter to Prime Minister Giorgio Borg Olivier, and the embassy at Valletta was established three days later under Harrison Lewis as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The first American ambassador, George J. Feldman, presented his credentials on 5 October 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Malta joined the Commonwealth in 1964 following independence from Britain, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 17 November and joined the United Nations on 1 December, both within eleven weeks of that recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995 and of the European Union on 1 May 2004, joined the Schengen area on 21 December 2007 and adopted the euro on 1 January 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a single-chamber parliamentary republic with six seats in the European Parliament, and its income per head of 40,900 euros in 2023 ranked ninth in the Union against an average of 38,100.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tourism is central, with 2,287,000 arrivals in 2022 against 659,000 in 2020, and the United Nations counts the population of the capital as that of the Northern Harbour and Southern Harbour districts rather than of Valletta itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Malta,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mt.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Malta – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/malta_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Malta,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MLT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Malta,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/malta. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malta,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malta. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Malta and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/malta_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Facade_Hagar_Qim.jpg","title":"Ħaġar Qim, Malta","desc":"Ħaġar Qim.","credit":"Hamelin de Guettelet, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facade_Hagar_Qim.jpg","alt":"A view of Ħaġar Qim."}},
{"id":"gw-173","num":173,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Maldives","answerText":"Maldives","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 August 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1982, left 2016, rejoined 2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Maldives"},"facts":[["Capital","Malé"],["Population","530,000 (2025)"],["Region","Southern Asia"],["Area","300 km² (116 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","maldives"],"abstract":"The <b>Maldives</b> is a chain of coral atolls in the Indian Ocean, and the United Nations gives its land area as 300 square kilometres and its population as 530,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 527,799 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes around 1,200 small coral islands and sandbanks sheltered by barrier reefs, about 200 of them inhabited, with India and Sri Lanka the nearest neighbours some 600 and 645 kilometres (370 and 400 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> UNESCO records that Maldivian coral reefs hold the richest diversity in the region and are the seventh largest in the world, accounting for about 5 per cent of the world’s reef area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only two people in five live in a town, 2.7 per cent of the land is forested, and 1,675,000 visitors arrived by air in 2022 against 555,000 in 2020, the year the economy contracted by 32.9 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 12,530 US dollars in 2023, against 9,645 in 2015 and 7,394 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Maldives became independent from Britain in 1965, and the United States recognised it on 10 August that year, when President Lyndon B. Johnson sent a message to Prime Minister Ibrahim Nasir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 21 September 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 9 April 1966, when the American ambassador to Ceylon, Cecil B. Lyon, presented his credentials while remaining resident at Colombo, and no American embassy has ever opened at Malé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country became a republic in 1968, and the ambassador at Colombo was reaccredited to it on 13 January 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 19 April 1983 and joined the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995; it joined the Commonwealth in 1982, withdrew on 13 October 2016 and rejoined on 1 February 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Maldives,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mv.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Maldives,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Maldives,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/maldives. [Open access]","UNESCO, “Baa Atoll Biosphere Reserve,” Man and the Biosphere Programme, https://www.unesco.org/en/mab/baa-atoll. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Maldives,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/maldives. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Maldives and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/maldives_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Maldives_soft_coral.jpg/1920px-Maldives_soft_coral.jpg","title":"Maldives, the Maldives","desc":"Maldives.","credit":"Tchami, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maldives_soft_coral.jpg","alt":"A view of Maldives."}},
{"id":"gw-174","num":174,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cabo Verde","answerText":"Cabo Verde","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 July 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 July 2008</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2013</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cabo Verde"},"facts":[["Capital","Praia"],["Population","527,000 (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","4,033 km² (1,557 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","cape verde"],"abstract":"<b>Cabo Verde</b> is an archipelago in the Atlantic off West Africa, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 4,033 square kilometres and its population as 527,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 524,877 for 2024 and an area of 4,030.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its capital, Praia, held 167,500 in 2018, two people in three live in towns, and 11 per cent of the land is forested, while life expectancy at birth is 79.5 years for women and 73.3 for men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 4,961 US dollars in 2023 against 3,415 in 2015, and services carry the economy, with agriculture at 5.2 per cent of value added and industry at 12.5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tourism is larger than the country: 785,000 non-resident tourists stayed in its hotels in 2022, against 180,000 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islands were under Portuguese sovereignty until 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the Republic of Cape Verde on 5 July 1975, when President Gerald R. Ford sent a letter to that effect to its president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 19 July, when the Cape Verdean ambassador presented his credentials to Ford, and the country joined the United Nations on 16 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first American ambassador presented her credentials on 18 November 1976 while accredited to Guinea-Bissau as well and resident at Bissau, and the embassy at Praia opened only on 28 January 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the World Trade Organization on 23 July 2008, and its money is the Cabo Verde escudo, exchanged at 105.7 to the US dollar in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2013 it was renamed Cabo Verde, the form the United Nations and the World Trade Organization both use, while the American guide still files it under Cape Verde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cabo Verde,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cv.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cabo Verde,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CPV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cape Verde,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cape-verde. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cabo Verde and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cape_verde_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Cabo Verde,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CPV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2019:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Cape_Verde_Pico_do_Fogo_b.jpg/1920px-Cape_Verde_Pico_do_Fogo_b.jpg","title":"Pico do Fogo, Cabo Verde","desc":"Pico do Fogo.","credit":"Cayambe, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Verde_Pico_do_Fogo_b.jpg","alt":"A view of Pico do Fogo."}},
{"id":"gw-175","num":175,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Brunei","answerText":"Brunei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 June 1850</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1888</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1984</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Brunei"},"facts":[["Capital","Bandar Seri Begawan"],["Population","466,000 (2025)"],["Region","South-eastern Asia"],["Area","5,765 km² (2,226 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","brunei"],"abstract":"<b>Brunei</b> is a small state on the northern coast of Borneo, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 5,765 square kilometres and its population as 466,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 462,721 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a coastal plain rising to mountains in the east, with the South China Sea to the north and the Malaysian state of Sarawak on every other side, dividing the country in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 72.1 per cent of the land, 77.9 per cent of the people live in towns, and the capital, Bandar Seri Begawan, held only 40,800 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Industry is 60.7 per cent of value added against agriculture’s 1.2, and income per head was 32,963 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tourism is slight and was all but extinguished: 218,000 visitors arrived in 2015, 62,000 in 2020, and 4,000 by air in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The United States signed a Treaty of Peace, Friendship, Commerce and Navigation with Brunei on 23 June 1850, which entered into force on 11 July 1853 and is still in effect today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A consulate followed from 1865 to 1868, the post called Brunei, Borneo, and Consul Captain C. Lees Moses presented his credentials there on 18 July 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Brunei became a British protectorate in 1888, at which point the United Kingdom took over its foreign affairs, and a constitution of 1959 declared it self-governing while leaving foreign affairs, security and defence to Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised its full independence on 1 January 1984, and Ambassador Barrington King presented his credentials at Bandar Seri Begawan on 28 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Brunei joined the United Nations on 21 September 1984 and the Commonwealth the same year following independence, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 9 December 1993 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Brunei Darussalam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bn.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Brunei Darussalam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Brunei Darussalam,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/brunei-darussalam. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Brunei Darussalam,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/brunei. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Brunei Darussalam and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/brunei_darussalam_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/The_golden_domed_Sultan_Omar_Ali_Saifuddin_Mosque_%2818404149198%29.jpg/1920px-The_golden_domed_Sultan_Omar_Ali_Saifuddin_Mosque_%2818404149198%29.jpg","title":"Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien Mosque, Brunei","desc":"Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien Mosque.","credit":"Jorge Láscar from Melbourne, Australia, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_golden_domed_Sultan_Omar_Ali_Saifuddin_Mosque_(18404149198).jpg","alt":"A view of Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien Mosque."}},
{"id":"gw-176","num":176,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Belize","answerText":"Belize","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 October 1981</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Belize"},"facts":[["Capital","Belmopan"],["Population","423,000 (2025)"],["Region","Central America"],["Area","22,965 km² (8,867 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","belize"],"abstract":"<b>Belize</b> is on the Caribbean coast of Central America, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 22,965 square kilometres and its population as 423,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 417,072 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a long east coast, mainly flat and swampy, with low mountains in the south, Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula to the north and Guatemala to the west and south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tropical forest covers 58.4 per cent of the land, fewer than half its people live in towns, and 373,000 visitors arrived in 2022 against 144,000 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Income per head was 7,489 US dollars in 2023, agriculture is 10.5 per cent of value added, and the capital, Belmopan, held only 23,000 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States opened a consulate in British Honduras on 3 March 1847, fifteen years before the territory became a British colony in 1862.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The colony took the name Belize in June 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the 1960s the United States sought and failed to mediate the dispute between Belize and Guatemala over contested Belizean territory, which dates back to a treaty of 1859.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Belize became independent from the United Kingdom on 21 September 1981, and the United States recognised it five weeks later, on 29 October, by raising its consulate general to an embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 25 September 1981 and the Commonwealth that year following independence, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 7 October 1983 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Guatemala and Belize put the territorial claim to the International Court of Justice by a special agreement of 8 December 2008 with a protocol of 25 May 2015, and the Court was seised of the dispute on 12 June 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Belize,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bz.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Belize,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BLZ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Belize,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/belize. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Belize,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/belize. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Belize and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/belize_e.htm. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Guatemala’s Territorial, Insular and Maritime Claim: Guatemala/Belize,” https://www.icj-cij.org/case/177. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Belize_Blue_Hole_%28TMP%29_%2816912331906%29.jpg/1920px-Belize_Blue_Hole_%28TMP%29_%2816912331906%29.jpg","title":"the Great Blue Hole, Belize","desc":"The Great Blue Hole.","credit":"The TerraMar Project, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belize_Blue_Hole_(TMP)_(16912331906).jpg","alt":"A deep circular sinkhole in a pale turquoise reef."}},
{"id":"gw-177","num":177,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bahamas","answerText":"Bahamas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 July 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accession talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 18 July 2001</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bahamas"},"facts":[["Capital","Nassau"],["Population","403,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","13,940 km² (5,382 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","bahamas"],"abstract":"The <b>Bahamas</b> is an archipelago in the western Atlantic, and the three institutions that measure it disagree: the United Nations gives its surface area as 13,940 square kilometres, the Commonwealth 14,000 and the World Bank 13,880.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 403,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count and 401,283 in 2024 by the World Bank’s, with 279,700 of them in the capital, Nassau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes nearly 700 coral islands, around 30 of them inhabited, generally flat and low-lying, 100 kilometres (60 miles) southeast of Florida and 80 kilometres (50 miles) northeast of Cuba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 50.9 per cent of the land, 83.1 per cent of the people live in towns, and the country holds 75.2 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 35,896 US dollars in 2023, and 1,452,000 visitors arrived in 2022 against 441,000 in 2020, more than three times the country’s own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Bahamas became fully independent from the United Kingdom in 1973 and joined the Commonwealth that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 10 July 1973, and the act of recognition was the opening of an embassy: relations and the embassy at Nassau began that same day, with Moncrieff J. Spear as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 18 September 1973, ten weeks after that recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not yet a member of the World Trade Organization: its accession working party was established on 18 July 2001, and more than two decades later the accession is still in progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Bahamian dollar was exchanged at one to the US dollar in 2024, and services carry the economy, agriculture supplying 0.5 per cent of value added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bahamas,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bs.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “The Bahamas,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/bahamas. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bahamas,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Bahamas,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bahamas. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accession status: Bahamas,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_bahamas_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Bahamas,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2019:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Bahamas_1989_%28535%29_Little_Exuma_Williamstown_%2824569757813%29.jpg/1920px-Bahamas_1989_%28535%29_Little_Exuma_Williamstown_%2824569757813%29.jpg","title":"Exuma, the Bahamas","desc":"Exuma.","credit":"Rüdiger Stehn from Kiel, Deutschland, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bahamas_1989_(535)_Little_Exuma_Williamstown_(24569757813).jpg","alt":"A view of Exuma."}},
{"id":"gw-178","num":178,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Iceland","answerText":"Iceland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Foreign affairs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 April 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1941</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 June 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO founder</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 April 1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iceland"},"facts":[["Capital","Reykjavík"],["Population","398,000 (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","103,000 km² (39,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","iceland"],"abstract":"<b>Iceland</b> is an island in the North Atlantic, and the United Nations and the World Bank both give its surface area as 103,000 square kilometres, with a population of 398,000 in 2025 by the first count and 386,506 in 2024 by the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It holds four people to the square kilometre, 93.9 per cent of them in towns and 216,400 in the capital, Reykjavík.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only 0.5 per cent of the land is forested, and income per head was 80,827 US dollars in 2023 against 52,930 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has grown by about a sixth in a decade, from 330,815 in 2015 to 386,506 in 2024, while visitors numbered 1,289,000 in 2015, 486,000 in 2020 and 698,000 in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany occupied Denmark on 9 April 1940, and Iceland took control of its own foreign relations the next day, without yet declaring itself independent of the Danish crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In July and December 1940 Icelandic officials asked the United States to place the island under its protection under the Monroe Doctrine, to forestall a feared German invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States was unenthusiastic at first, but concluded that occupying Iceland was a matter of its own national defence, and did so on 7 July 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and an American legation at Reykjavík followed on 30 September 1941, when Lincoln MacVeagh presented his credentials, three years before there was an independent Iceland to have relations with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After a plebiscite the country formally became an independent republic on 17 June 1944, and the United States recognised it that same day in public statements of congratulation by President Franklin D. Roosevelt and Secretary of State Cordell Hull; the legation was raised to an embassy on 3 November 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Iceland was one of the twelve countries that created NATO on 4 April 1949, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 21 April 1968 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Iceland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/is.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Iceland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ISL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iceland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iceland. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Iceland and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/iceland_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Gullfoss_from_the_Air_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Gullfoss, Iceland","desc":"Gullfoss.","credit":"Nickspix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gullfoss_from_the_Air_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A view of Gullfoss."}},
{"id":"gw-179","num":179,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Vanuatu","answerText":"Vanuatu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 July 1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 September 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 July 2024</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Vanuatu"},"facts":[["Capital","Port Vila"],["Population","335,000 (2025)"],["Region","Melanesia"],["Area","12,189 km² (4,706 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","vanuatu"],"abstract":"<b>Vanuatu</b> is an island country in the southwestern Pacific, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 12,189 square kilometres and its population as 335,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 327,777 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a double chain of 13 principal islands and many smaller ones, volcanic and coral in origin and around 65 of them inhabited, about 800 kilometres (500 miles) west of Fiji and nearly 1,800 kilometres (1,100 miles) east of Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 36.3 per cent of the land, only a quarter of the people live in towns, and the capital, Port Vila, held 52,700 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agriculture is 24.4 per cent of value added, income per head was 3,492 US dollars in 2023, and 30,000 visitors arrived in 2022 against 90,000 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain and France held the islands together, under the joint sovereignty of a condominium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Vanuatu gained its independence on 30 July 1980, and the United States recognised the new republic soon afterwards, when President Jimmy Carter sent a message to that effect to its government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in 1980, whose own record gives the reason as the ending of the Anglo-French condominium rather than independence from Britain, and the United Nations on 15 September 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were not established until 30 September 1986, when representatives of both states signed an agreement to that effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ambassadorial relations began on 10 April 1987, when the American ambassador to Papua New Guinea presented his credentials, no mission having been opened at Port Vila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States opened its embassy at Port Vila only on 19 July 2024, 44 years after recognising the country, which had joined the World Trade Organization on 24 August 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Vanuatu,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vu.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Vanuatu,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VUT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Vanuatu,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/vanuatu. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Vanuatu,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/vanuatu. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Vanuatu and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/vanuatu_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Mount_Yasur_eruption_2006%2C_Tanna_Island%2C_Vanuatu%2C_VAN_0516.jpg/1920px-Mount_Yasur_eruption_2006%2C_Tanna_Island%2C_Vanuatu%2C_VAN_0516.jpg","title":"Mount Yasur, Vanuatu","desc":"Mount Yasur.","credit":"Rolf Cosar, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Yasur_eruption_2006,_Tanna_Island,_Vanuatu,_VAN_0516.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Yasur."}},
{"id":"gw-180","num":180,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"New Caledonia","answerText":"New Caledonia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1853</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Matignon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 June 1988</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nouméa Accord</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 May 1998</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendums</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018, 2020 and 2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"New Caledonia"},"facts":[["Capital","Nouméa"],["Population","295,000 (2025)"],["Region","Melanesia"],["Area","19,100 km² (7,375 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","new caledonia"],"abstract":"<b>New Caledonia</b> is a group of Pacific islands about 1,500 kilometres (930 miles) east of Australia, administered by France as a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 19,100 square kilometres and its population as 295,000 in 2025, where the Secretariat’s own working paper gives a land area of 18,575 square kilometres and 268,500 people at the start of 2023, and the World Bank 292,639 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its exclusive economic zone covers 1,422,543 square kilometres, and the main island, Grande Terre, accounts for 16,750 of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is Nouméa, 71.1 per cent of the people live in towns, forest covers 45.8 per cent of the land, and income per head was 34,981 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 41 per cent of the population is Melanesian, mainly Kanak, and 24 per cent of European origin; French is official and about 27 Kanak languages are spoken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> James Cook named Grande Terre New Caledonia in 1774, and France annexed the territory on 24 September 1853, making it an overseas territory with limited autonomy in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Kanak independence movement launched in the 1970s led to the violence of the 1980s known as the <i>événements</i>, in which about 80 people died between 1984 and 1988.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Matignon Agreements of 26 June 1988, between the Front de libération nationale kanak et socialiste, the anti-independence Rassemblement pour la Calédonie dans la République and France, established three provinces to restore the balance of power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nouméa Accord, signed on 5 May 1998, transferred non-sovereign powers and institutions to New Caledonia between 1998 and 2018 and provided for up to three referendums on accession to full sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All three were held, on 4 November 2018, 4 October 2020 and 12 December 2021, and the General Assembly had declared in 1986 that France must transmit information on the territory under Chapter XI of the Charter, affirming its people’s inalienable right to self-determination under resolution 1514.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “New Caledonia: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/11, 18 March 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4080540. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “New Caledonia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nc.html. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Implementation of the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples,” A/RES/41/41 A, 2 December 1986, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192415. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples,” A/RES/1514, 14 December 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206145. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: New Caledonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NCL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Isle_of_Pines.jpg/1920px-Isle_of_Pines.jpg","title":"Isle of Pines (New Caledonia), New Caledonia","desc":"Isle of Pines (New Caledonia).","credit":"Speddie23, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isle_of_Pines.jpg","alt":"A view of Isle of Pines (New Caledonia)."}},
{"id":"gw-181","num":181,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Barbados","answerText":"Barbados","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Barbados"},"facts":[["Capital","Bridgetown"],["Population","283,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","431 km² (166 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","barbados"],"abstract":"<b>Barbados</b> is an island in the eastern Caribbean, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 431 square kilometres and its population as 283,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 430 square kilometres and 287,370 for 2022 and the World Bank 282,467 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is 657 people to the square kilometre, and yet only 31.2 per cent of them live in what the United Nations counts as towns, the capital, Bridgetown, holding 89,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a fairly flat island rising through terraced tablelands to Mount Hillaby at 336 metres (1,102 feet) and surrounded by coral reefs, with Saint Lucia to the north, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines to the west and Trinidad and Tobago to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 14.7 per cent of the land, and income per head was 23,804 US dollars in 2023 against 18,954 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States kept a consular presence on the island from 11 June 1823, when Barbados was still a British colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> After a constitutional conference with Great Britain in June 1966 at which Barbados negotiated its independence, it became an independent state within the British Commonwealth on 30 November 1966.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised it that same day by opening its embassy at Bridgetown, with George Dolgin as chargé d’affaires ad interim, so that the recognition and the mission were one act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Barbados joined the Commonwealth in 1966 following independence, and the United Nations on 9 December, nine days after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 15 February 1967 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bridgetown has since served the United States as a base for its diplomatic relations with the wider Caribbean region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Barbados,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bb.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Barbados,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/barbados. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Barbados,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Barbados,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/barbados. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Barbados and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/barbados_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Bathsheba%2C_Barbados_08.jpg/1920px-Bathsheba%2C_Barbados_08.jpg","title":"Bathsheba, Barbados, Barbados","desc":"Bathsheba, Barbados.","credit":"Postdlf, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bathsheba,_Barbados_08.jpg","alt":"A view of Bathsheba, Barbados."}},
{"id":"gw-182","num":182,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"French Polynesia","answerText":"French Polynesia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sovereignty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1880</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Re-inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 2013</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Fr. Polynesia"},"facts":[["Capital","Papeete"],["Population","282,000 (2025)"],["Region","Polynesia"],["Area","3,687 km² (1,424 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","french polynesia"],"abstract":"<b>French Polynesia</b> is a group of 118 islands in five archipelagos scattered across 2.5 million square kilometres of the South Pacific, administered by France as a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 3,687 square kilometres and its population as 282,000 in 2025, where the Secretariat’s working paper gives a land area of about 3,500 square kilometres and 279,000 for 2022 and the World Bank 281,807 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its exclusive economic zone covers 5.5 million square kilometres, the capital is Papeete with 136,000 people, and 61.9 per cent of the population lives in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 43.1 per cent of the land, income per head was 22,759 US dollars in 2023, and the population is about 65 per cent Polynesian, 16 per cent of mixed descent, 12 per cent white and 5 per cent of Chinese origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people arrived in successive waves of migration between the 4th century and the end of the 14th; Magellan reached the islands in 1521 and European settlement began after Captain Wallis arrived in 1767.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From the early 19th century the Pomare dynasty extended its influence over Tahiti and the Tuamotu and Leeward Islands, and signed a protectorate treaty with France in 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1880 King Pomare V granted France sovereignty over the islands that were dependencies of the Tahitian crown, creating the French Establishments in Oceania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These became an overseas territory when the French Union was established in 1946, have been called French Polynesia since 1957, and were reclassified as an overseas collectivity by a constitutional amendment in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people reaffirmed their wish to remain a territory of France in a referendum held in 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly put the territory back on the list of non-self-governing territories by resolution 67/265, adopted without a vote at its 82nd plenary meeting on 17 May 2013, on a draft moved by Nauru, Solomon Islands and Tuvalu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “French Polynesia: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/7, 8 January 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4073636. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “French Polynesia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pf.html. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Self-Determination of French Polynesia,” A/RES/67/265, 17 May 2013, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/755481. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Self-Determination of French Polynesia: Revised Draft Resolution,” A/67/L.56/Rev.1, 1 March 2013, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/744942. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: French Polynesia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PYF/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Bora_Bora_-_Mt_Otemanu.jpg/1920px-Bora_Bora_-_Mt_Otemanu.jpg","title":"Bora Bora, French Polynesia","desc":"Bora Bora and Mount Otemanu.","credit":"rachel_thecat, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bora_Bora_-_Mt_Otemanu.jpg","alt":"A green volcanic peak rising from a turquoise lagoon."}},
{"id":"gw-183","num":183,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"São Tomé and Príncipe","answerText":"São Tomé and Príncipe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 July 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accession talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 26 May 2005</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"São Tomé and Principe"},"facts":[["Capital","São Tomé"],["Population","240,000 (2025)"],["Region","Middle Africa"],["Area","964 km² (372 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","sao tome and principe"],"abstract":"<b>São Tomé and Príncipe</b> is a two-island state in the Gulf of Guinea, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 964 square kilometres and its population as 240,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 960 square kilometres and 235,536 people for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is 250 people to the square kilometre, 73.6 per cent of them living in towns and 80,100 in the capital, São Tomé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its forest is shrinking, from 57.3 per cent of the land in 2015 to 54.1 in 2020 and 52.8 by 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agriculture is 13.4 per cent of value added and industry 13.7, leaving services at 72.9, income per head was 2,963 US dollars in 2023 against 1,589 in 2015, and the currency is the dobra, exchanged at 22 to the US dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tourism is very small, with 15,000 visitors in 2021 against 26,000 in 2015, and the country exported goods worth an estimated 3 million US dollars in 2024, while its population grows at 2 per cent a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were under Portuguese sovereignty until 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised the Democratic Republic of São Tomé and Príncipe on 12 July 1975, when President Gerald R. Ford sent a letter to that effect to its president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 16 September 1975, two months after that recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 10 October 1975 by an exchange of diplomatic notes, and the American guide records no embassy, no consulate and no resident ambassador at any point since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is not a member of the World Trade Organization: its accession working party was established on 26 May 2005 and the accession is still in progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sao Tome and Principe,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/st.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sao Tome and Principe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/STP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sao Tome and Principe,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sao-tome-and-principe. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accession status: Sao Tome and Principe,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_sao_tome_principe_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Sao Tome and Principe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/STP/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Pico_C%C3%A3o_Grande.jpg/1920px-Pico_C%C3%A3o_Grande.jpg","title":"Pico Cão Grande, São Tomé and Príncipe","desc":"Pico Cão Grande.","credit":"Philippe Bourachot, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pico_C%C3%A3o_Grande.jpg","alt":"A view of Pico Cão Grande."}},
{"id":"gw-184","num":184,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Samoa","answerText":"Samoa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 1856</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Samoa"},"facts":[["Capital","Apia"],["Population","219,000 (2025)"],["Region","Polynesia"],["Area","2,842 km² (1,097 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","samoa"],"abstract":"<b>Samoa</b> is an archipelago in the central South Pacific about halfway between New Zealand and Hawaii, of nine islands, four of them inhabited, the largest being Savai’i and Upolu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 2,842 square kilometres and its population as 219,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 2,800 and 198,400 for 2022 and the World Bank 218,019 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only 18.1 per cent of its people live in towns, 36,100 of them in the capital, Apia, and forest covers 57.8 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Agriculture is 12.5 per cent of value added and income per head was 4,765 US dollars in 2023, while visitors fell from 128,000 in 2015 to 23,000 in 2020 and had recovered only to 49,000 by 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American merchants called at the islands in the first half of the 19th century, and a commercial agent was posted at Apia as early as 1844.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The first American consul, Jonathan S. Jenkins, took up the post at Apia on 17 May 1856, and that office closed on 23 September 1927.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Western Samoa had been a trust territory administered by New Zealand, and the United States recognised its independence on 1 January 1962, when Senator Oren Long delivered a congratulatory message from President John F. Kennedy to its two heads of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 14 July 1971, when Ambassador Kenneth Franzheim II presented his credentials at Apia; every American ambassador since has been accredited to New Zealand as well and resident at Wellington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An embassy was established at Apia on 15 November 1988 and has been led by a chargé d’affaires ever since, the ambassador having never moved there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country changed its official name to the Independent State of Samoa in 1997, joined the Commonwealth in 1970 after the New Zealand administration ended, hosted its heads of government meeting in 2024, and joined the World Trade Organization on 10 May 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Samoa,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/samoa. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Samoa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ws.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/WSM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Samoa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/samoa. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Samoa and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/samoa_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Lalomanu_Beach_-_Samoa.jpg/1920px-Lalomanu_Beach_-_Samoa.jpg","title":"Lalomanu, Samoa","desc":"Lalomanu.","credit":"Neil, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lalomanu_Beach_-_Samoa.jpg","alt":"A view of Lalomanu."}},
{"id":"gw-185","num":185,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Lucia","answerText":"Saint Lucia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1815</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Associated state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 February 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 1979</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Saint Lucia"},"facts":[["Capital","Castries"],["Population","180,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","616 km² (238 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","saint lucia"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Lucia</b> is a volcanic island in the eastern Caribbean between Martinique to the north and Saint Vincent to the south-west, and the United Nations and the Commonwealth both give its surface area as 616 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its population is 180,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count, 183,630 in 2022 by the Commonwealth’s and 179,744 in 2024 by the World Bank’s, at 292 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a central ridge of mountains, many rivers, boiling sulphur springs and sandy beaches; forest covers 34 per cent of the land and only 18.8 per cent of the people live in towns, 22,300 of them in the capital, Castries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 13,555 US dollars in 2023, agriculture is 1.3 per cent of value added, and 356,000 visitors arrived in 2022, twice the island’s own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British colonial rule was established in 1815, and the island took part in the West Indies Federation from 1958 until its collapse in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> With the other Windward Islands it became a state associated with the United Kingdom in 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A United States special representative was appointed to it and four neighbouring territories on 1 September 1977, concurrently with his posts as ambassador to Barbados and to Grenada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saint Lucia achieved full independence on 22 February 1979, and the United States, which had informally recognised it as an associated state, did not recognise its independence formally until it appointed Sally Angela Shelton as ambassador on 17 May 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She presented her credentials on 11 June 1979, and every American ambassador since has been resident in Barbados, the United States keeping no official presence on the island itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saint Lucia joined the Commonwealth in 1979 following independence and the United Nations on 18 September that year, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 13 April 1993 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Lucia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lc.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Saint Lucia,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/saint-lucia. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Lucia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LCA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saint Lucia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saint-lucia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Saint Lucia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/saint_lucia_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Pitons-aerial.jpg","title":"Gros Piton, Saint Lucia","desc":"Gros Piton.","credit":"Caribbeandy, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pitons-aerial.jpg","alt":"A view of Gros Piton."}},
{"id":"gw-186","num":186,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guam","answerText":"Guam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Naval rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1899 – 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Organic Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Census</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guam"},"facts":[["Capital","Hagåtña"],["Population","169,000 (2025)"],["Region","Micronesia"],["Area","541 km² (209 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","guam"],"abstract":"<b>Guam</b> is the southernmost and largest of the Mariana Islands, about 2,200 kilometres (1,370 miles) south of Tokyo, and a non-self-governing territory administered by the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 541 square kilometres and its population as 169,000 in 2025, where the working paper gives a land area of 544 and the 2020 census figure of 153,836, and the World Bank 540 and 167,777 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island is two geological halves of about equal size, a coralline limestone plateau in the north and mountains in the south, with Apra Harbor among the largest deep-water ports in the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its exclusive economic zone covers 214,059 square kilometres, the capital is Hagåtña, English and CHamoru are its languages, and income per head was 35,904 US dollars in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 32.8 per cent CHamoru, 29.1 per cent Filipino and 13.2 per cent other Pacific islanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The island was inhabited by the CHamoru, a people of Malayo-Polynesian descent, when missionaries from Spain arrived in the late 17th century, and the United States Navy administered it from 1899 to 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1950 Congress passed the Organic Act of Guam, which established institutions of local government and contains a bill of rights, and since then the territory has been under the general supervision of the Secretary of the Interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Guam is an unincorporated territory, so not all provisions of the Constitution apply to it, and because only states choose presidential electors its residents cannot vote for the President while living there, although people born on Guam are United States citizens and may vote once resident in a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has a 15-member legislature, a governor and a delegate to Congress, and its principal sources of revenue are tourism and the United States military.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly first addressed the transmission of information on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946, and the United Nations still counts Guam separately from the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Guam: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/9, 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4080736. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Guam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gu.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Guam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Two_Lovers_Point_from_Tumon_Bay.JPG/1920px-Two_Lovers_Point_from_Tumon_Bay.JPG","title":"Two Lovers Point, Guam","desc":"Two Lovers Point.","credit":"Abasaa, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Two_Lovers_Point_from_Tumon_Bay.JPG","alt":"A view of Two Lovers Point."}},
{"id":"gw-187","num":187,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Curaçao","answerText":"Curaçao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomous</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 2010</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Curaçao"},"facts":[["Capital","Willemstad"],["Population","186,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","444 km² (171 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","curacao"],"abstract":"<b>Curaçao</b> is an island in the southern Caribbean and one of the four countries of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, and the United Nations and the World Bank both give its surface area as 444 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their population figures diverge sharply, the United Nations giving 186,000 for 2025 and the World Bank 155,967 for 2024, a difference of about a fifth that neither source explains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The capital is Willemstad, the currency is the Netherlands Antillean guilder, exchanged at 1.8 to the US dollar in 2024, and the island holds 437 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations counts Curaçao separately from the Netherlands for statistical purposes, and notes that its figure for the capital’s population is really the island’s total excluding some neighbourhoods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly considered a communication from the Government of the Netherlands concerning the Netherlands Antilles and Surinam at its 557th plenary meeting on 15 December 1955, adopting resolution 945 by 21 votes to 10 with 33 abstentions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 10 October 2010 the Netherlands Antilles were dissolved: Curaçao and Sint Maarten became autonomous countries, while Bonaire, Sint Eustatius and Saba became special municipalities of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Curaçao is responsible for matters such as good governance, education and its own judicial system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kingdom as a whole has overarching responsibility for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law, and agreements made on those matters apply to the entire Kingdom and bind all four countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In other areas, among them healthcare, tourism and employment, each of the four countries determines its own policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Dutch ministries of foreign affairs and defence, and the Dutch embassies and consulates around the world, serve all four countries, and the Netherlands must take account of the Caribbean part’s interests when it makes foreign policy for the Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Curaçao,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Curacao,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUW/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Curacao,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUW/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Communication from the Government of the Netherlands concerning the Netherlands Antilles and Surinam,” A/RES/945, 15 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/208173. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Caribbean Parts of the Kingdom,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Handelskade_in_Willemstad.jpg/1920px-Handelskade_in_Willemstad.jpg","title":"Willemstad, Curaçao","desc":"Willemstad.","credit":"Coolcaesar, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Handelskade_in_Willemstad.jpg","alt":"A view of Willemstad."}},
{"id":"gw-188","num":188,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kiribati","answerText":"Kiribati","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US agent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 May 1888</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 July 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 September 1999</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kiribati"},"facts":[["Capital","Tarawa"],["Population","136,000 (2025)"],["Region","Micronesia"],["Area","811 km² (313 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","kiribati"],"abstract":"<b>Kiribati</b> is a country of 33 islands in the central Pacific, only 20 of them inhabited, most of them very low-lying atolls, and it holds the South Pacific’s largest marine reserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth and the World Bank give its land area as 811 and 810 square kilometres, where the United Nations gives 726 and says in a footnote that this is land area only, excluding 84 square kilometres of uninhabited islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That footnote is the whole of the difference, since 726 and 84 make 810.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its population is 136,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count, 134,518 in 2024 by the World Bank’s and 119,400 in 2022 by the Commonwealth’s, and it is governed from Tarawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States appointed a commercial agent, Adolph Rick, to Butaritari in the Gilbert Islands on 25 May 1888, to protect American trade in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> With the Ellice Islands the Gilberts became a British protectorate in 1892 and the whole group a British colony in 1916; Japan occupied several of the islands during the Second World War, after which they reverted to their former colonial status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain expanded self-government during the 1960s, the Gilbert and Ellice Islands separated in 1975, and Kiribati became independent on 12 July 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised the republic on 20 September 1979, when the two countries signed a Treaty of Friendship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They agreed to establish diplomatic relations on 12 August 1980, and William Bodde Jr. was appointed Envoy Extraordinary and Minister Plenipotentiary on 26 September 1980, stationed at Suva in Fiji because there were no diplomatic facilities on Kiribati.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth in 1979 following independence but did not join the United Nations until 14 September 1999, twenty years after becoming independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Kiribati,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/kiribati. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Kiribati,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KIR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Kiribati,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ki.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kiribati,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kiribati. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kiribati,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KIR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Line5304_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg/1920px-Line5304_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg","title":"Tarawa, Kiribati","desc":"Tarawa.","credit":"LCDR Eric Johnson, NOAA Corps., Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Line5304_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg","alt":"A view of Tarawa."}},
{"id":"gw-189","num":189,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Seychelles","answerText":"Seychelles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 August 1996</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Seychelles"},"facts":[["Capital","Victoria"],["Population","133,000 (2025)"],["Region","Eastern Africa"],["Area","457 km² (176 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","seychelles"],"abstract":"<b>Seychelles</b> is an island country in the western Indian Ocean, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 457 square kilometres, where the Commonwealth gives 452 and the World Bank 460.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 133,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count and 121,354 in 2024 by the World Bank’s, whose series jumps from 99,258 in 2021 to 119,878 in 2022, a break rather than a year’s growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes over 100 islands in two groups, an inner group of about 40 that are rocky and mountainous and an outer group that is low-lying and coralline, with Kenya about 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) west and Madagascar about 1,100 kilometres (700 miles) south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 73.3 per cent of the land, 57.1 per cent of the people live in towns, and 28,100 of them in the capital, Victoria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 16,735 US dollars in 2023, and 332,000 visitors arrived in 2022, two and a half times the country’s own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Seychelles attained independence from British sovereignty on 29 June 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised it that same day, when representatives of the two states signed an agreement at Victoria relating to the establishment, operation and maintenance of a tracking and telemetry facility on the island of Mahé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 1 July 1976, when the American consulate at Victoria was raised to embassy status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That embassy closed on 30 August 1996 and relations were conducted through the American embassy in Mauritius for 27 years, until it re-opened on 1 July 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 21 September 1976 and the Commonwealth in 1976 following independence, and became a member of the World Trade Organization on 26 April 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Seychelles,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sc.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Seychelles,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/seychelles. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Seychelles,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYC/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Seychelles,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/seychelles. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Seychelles and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/seychelles_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Seychelles,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/Valle%C3%A9_de_mai_at_Praslin.jpg","title":"the Vallée de Mai, the Seychelles","desc":"The Vallée de Mai on Praslin.","credit":"Brocken Inaglory, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valle%C3%A9_de_mai_at_Praslin.jpg","alt":"A dense forest of coco de mer palms."}},
{"id":"gw-190","num":190,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Grenada","answerText":"Grenada","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 November 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 February 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Grenada"},"facts":[["Capital","Saint George’s"],["Population","117,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","345 km² (133 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","grenada"],"abstract":"<b>Grenada</b> is a volcanic island country in the eastern Caribbean about 160 kilometres (100 miles) north of Venezuela, with a ridge of mountains running north to south, steep valleys and large areas of tropical rainforest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 345 square kilometres and its population as 117,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 344 and 112,519 for 2022 and the World Bank 117,207 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 52.1 per cent of the land, only 36.4 per cent of the people live in towns, and income per head was 11,246 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of the southern Grenadines are a dependency of Grenada, and 149,000 visitors arrived in 2022 against 54,000 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives the capital’s population as 39,300, but its own footnote says the figure refers to Saint George Parish rather than to the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Grenada gained independence from the United Kingdom on 7 February 1974, and the United States recognised it on the occasion of that independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 29 November 1974, when the Grenadian ambassador, Marie J. McIntyre, presented her credentials to President Gerald R. Ford.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American diplomatic mission followed on 25 February 1975, when Ambassador Theodore R. Britton presented his credentials at Saint George’s; he was also ambassador to Barbados and resident at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States maintained relations through the joint American and Organization of Eastern Caribbean States military invasion of the island in 1983, which followed the assassination of Prime Minister Maurice Bishop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American embassy at Saint George’s was established on 2 February 1984 and is staffed by a chargé d’affaires who reports to the ambassador resident in Bridgetown, and Grenada joined the United Nations on 17 September 1974 and the World Trade Organization on 22 February 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Grenada,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/grenada. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Grenada,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gd.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Grenada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRD/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Grenada,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/grenada. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Grenada and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/grenada_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/St_Georges_Grenada_Fort_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"St. George's, Grenada, Grenada","desc":"St. George's, Grenada.","credit":"cwi.aida, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Georges_Grenada_Fort_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A view of St. George's, Grenada."}},
{"id":"gw-191","num":191,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Micronesia","answerText":"Micronesia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1989</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trusteeship end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 December 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Micronesia"},"facts":[["Capital","Palikir"],["Population","114,000 (2025)"],["Region","Micronesia"],["Area","702 km² (271 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","micronesia"],"abstract":"<b>Micronesia</b>, formally the Federated States of Micronesia, is a country in the western Pacific, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 702 square kilometres and its population as 114,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 700 and 113,160 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 92.1 per cent of its land, only 22.8 per cent of its people live in towns, and the capital, Palikir, holds about 7,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 4,084 US dollars in 2023 against 2,911 in 2015, and the United States dollar is its currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Visitors numbered 31,000 in 2015 and 18,000 in 2019, counted as arrivals in the states of Kosrae, Chuuk, Pohnpei and Yap and excluding the country’s own citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Second World War the islands formed part of the United Nations Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, administered by the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised Micronesian independence on 3 November 1986, when President Ronald Reagan noted in Proclamation 5564 that it had fulfilled its obligations under the terms of the trusteeship agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From that day the Federated States of Micronesia were no longer under American trusteeship, and a Compact of Free Association agreed between the two countries entered into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An Office of the United States Representative opened the same day, and Michael G. Wygant presented his credentials on 2 October 1987.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That office was upgraded to embassy status on 20 September 1989, and the first American ambassador, Aurelia Erskine Brazeal, presented her credentials on 18 September 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Security Council did not determine that the trusteeship had terminated until 22 December 1990, when it found by 14 votes to 1 that the objectives of the agreement had been fully attained for Micronesia, the Marshall Islands and the Northern Mariana Islands, whose peoples had approved their new status in plebiscites; the country joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Micronesia (Federated States of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fm.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Micronesia, Fed. Sts.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FSM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Micronesia, Fed. Sts.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FSM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Micronesia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/micronesia. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 683, adopted at its 2972nd meeting, on 22 December 1990,” S/RES/683, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/103696. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Nan_madol.jpg","title":"Nan Madol, Micronesia","desc":"Nan Madol.","credit":"NOAA, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nan_madol.jpg","alt":"A view of Nan Madol."}},
{"id":"gw-192","num":192,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Aruba","answerText":"Aruba","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Aruba"},"facts":[["Capital","Oranjestad"],["Population","108,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","180 km² (69 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","aruba"],"abstract":"<b>Aruba</b> is an island in the southern Caribbean and one of the four countries of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, and the United Nations and the World Bank agree on its size: 180 square kilometres and about 108,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is 601 people to the square kilometre, its capital is Oranjestad with 29,900, and its currency is the Aruban florin, exchanged at 1.8 to the US dollar in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations counts Aruba separately from the Netherlands for statistical purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten each have their own government, headed by a Governor who represents the King, where in the Netherlands the head of government is the King himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Governor is appointed by the King for six years and may be reappointed once, so may serve at most 12 years; he has no ministerial responsibility and takes no part in day-to-day government, and he appoints an Advisory Council of five members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Each of the three also has a Minister Plenipotentiary in The Hague, representing its government in the Netherlands and in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Together those ministers and the ministers of the Dutch government make up the Council of Ministers for the Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Netherlands maintains a single Representation across the three countries, with its headquarters at Willemstad in Curaçao and an office at Oranjestad, which advises the Dutch Minister of the Interior and Kingdom Relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kingdom as a whole answers for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law, while each country decides its own policy on matters such as healthcare, tourism and employment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The General Assembly considered a communication from the Government of the Netherlands concerning the Netherlands Antilles and Surinam at its 557th plenary meeting on 15 December 1955, adopting resolution 945 by 21 votes to 10 with 33 abstentions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Aruba,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/aw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Aruba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ABW/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Aruba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ABW/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Governance of Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/governance-of-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Representation of the Netherlands in Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/representation-of-the-netherlands-in-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Caribbean Parts of the Kingdom,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Communication from the Government of the Netherlands concerning the Netherlands Antilles and Surinam,” A/RES/945, 15 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/208173. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Arikok_National_Park.jpg/1920px-Arikok_National_Park.jpg","title":"Arikok National Park, Aruba","desc":"Arikok National Park.","credit":"Misty Johnson, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arikok_National_Park.jpg","alt":"A view of Arikok National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-193","num":193,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"United States Virgin Islands","answerText":"United States Virgin Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Danish rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1754 – 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Transferred</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1917</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"U.S. Virgin Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Charlotte Amalie"],["Population","104,900 (2023)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","352 km² (136 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","virgin islands"],"abstract":"The <b>United States Virgin Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter and an unincorporated, organized territory of the United States, administered by the Office of Insular Affairs of the Department of the Interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They lie in the eastern Caribbean, 1,730 kilometres (1,075 miles) southeast of the southeastern tip of the contiguous United States and 97 kilometres (60 miles) east of Puerto Rico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There are four main islands, Saint Croix, Saint John, Saint Thomas and Water Island, and the capital, Charlotte Amalie, is on Saint Thomas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population figures diverge widely: the Secretariat’s working paper gives 104,917 for 2023 and the World Bank 104,377 for 2024, where the United Nations statistics division gives 84,000 for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land area is 352 square kilometres by the working paper, 347 by the statistics division and 350 by the World Bank, and the exclusive economic zone covers 33,744 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> English or English creole is spoken by 74.7 per cent of the people, Spanish or Spanish creole by 16.78 per cent and French or French creole by 6.57 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tourism is the primary economic activity, accounting for 80 per cent of output and of employment, and the manufacturing sector is petroleum refining, rum distilling, textiles, electronics, pharmaceuticals and watch assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 36,983 US dollars in 2022 and unemployment 3.7 per cent in October 2024, and the territory has a unicameral legislature of 15 senators, a governor and a delegate to the United States Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islands belonged to Denmark from 1754 to 1917, as the Danish West Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 31 March 1917 the Government of Denmark transferred ownership to the United States, and they have been an organized, unincorporated territory ever since; the General Assembly first addressed the transmission of information on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “United States Virgin Islands: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/16, 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4080346. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Virgin Islands, U.S.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VIR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “United States Virgin Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vi.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Virgin Islands, U.S.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VIR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/StJohnTrunkBay.jpg","title":"Trunk Bay, the United States Virgin Islands","desc":"Trunk Bay.","credit":"Ben Whitney, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StJohnTrunkBay.jpg","alt":"A view of Trunk Bay."}},
{"id":"gw-194","num":194,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tonga","answerText":"Tonga","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">United</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1845</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1886</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 June 1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1972</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tonga"},"facts":[["Capital","Nuku’alofa"],["Population","104,000 (2025)"],["Region","Polynesia"],["Area","747 km² (288 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","tonga"],"abstract":"<b>Tonga</b> is two roughly parallel chains of around 170 islands in the southwestern Pacific, only 36 of them inhabited, the western ones volcanic and well above sea level and the eastern ones low coral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 747 square kilometres and its population as 104,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 748 and 105,690 for 2022 and the World Bank 104,175 for 2024, down from 106,095 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 12.4 per cent of the land, only 23.1 per cent of the people live in towns, and 22,900 of them are in the capital, Nuku’alofa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Income per head was 4,859 US dollars in 2023 against 3,805 in 2015, and the currency is the pa’anga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands were united in 1845 under King George Tupou I and became a constitutional monarchy in 1875.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States and the King of Tonga signed a Treaty of Amity, Commerce and Navigation at Nuku’alofa on 2 October 1886, which is the act that recognised the kingdom, and it entered into force in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A consul-general was appointed to Nuku’alofa in 1897 but was resident at Apia in Samoa, and the post closed in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Tonga became a protectorate of Great Britain in 1900 the British Foreign Office took control of all its external affairs, the United States ceased to appoint consuls-general, and on 28 July 1920 the Foreign Office terminated the 1886 treaty on the King’s behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tonga became an independent and sovereign nation within the British Commonwealth on 4 June 1970, joining the Commonwealth that year on withdrawing from the protectorate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised it a second time on 4 October 1972 by appointing Kenneth Franzheim II as ambassador; since 1979 its ambassador to Fiji has also been accredited to Tonga and resident at Suva, an embassy at Nuku’alofa opened in 2023, and the country joined the World Trade Organization on 27 July 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Tonga,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/tonga. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Tonga,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/to.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Tonga,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TON/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tonga,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tonga. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tonga and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tonga_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Royal_Palace%2C_Nuku%27alofa%2C_Nov_18.jpg/1920px-Royal_Palace%2C_Nuku%27alofa%2C_Nov_18.jpg","title":"Nukuʻalofa, Tonga","desc":"Nukuʻalofa.","credit":"Uhooep, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Palace,_Nuku%27alofa,_Nov_18.jpg","alt":"A view of Nukuʻalofa."}},
{"id":"gw-196","num":196,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Vincent and the Grenadines","answerText":"Saint Vincent and the Grenadines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded to Britain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1763</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 December 1981</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"St. Vin. and Gren."},"facts":[["Capital","Kingstown"],["Population","100,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","389 km² (150 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","saint vincent"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</b> lies in the eastern Caribbean and consists of the island of Saint Vincent and the northern Grenadines, a group of 32 smaller islands and cays to the south; the southern Grenadines belong to Grenada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 389 square kilometres and its population as 100,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 390 and 110,900 for 2022 and the World Bank 100,616 for 2024, down from 106,960 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country is volcanic and mountainous with one volcano still active, and 73.2 per cent of its land is forested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Just over half its people live in towns, 26,600 of them in the capital, Kingstown, and income per head was 10,767 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Saint Vincent was ceded to Great Britain in 1763, was briefly under French rule from 1779 until 1796, and then returned to British rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It passed through a variety of stages as a colony and Commonwealth state and, after a referendum in 1979, became the last of the Windward Islands to reach independence, on 27 October that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A United States special representative had been appointed to it and four neighbouring territories on 1 September 1977, concurrently with his posts as ambassador to Barbados and to Grenada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States, which had informally recognised Saint Vincent as a federated state in the British Commonwealth, did not recognise its independence formally until it appointed Milan D. Bish as ambassador on 23 November 1981, more than two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Relations were established on 8 December 1981, when the Vincentian ambassador presented his credentials and Bish presented his at Bridgetown the same day, being concurrently ambassador to Antigua and Barbuda, Barbados, Dominica and Saint Lucia and special representative to Saint Christopher and Nevis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every American ambassador to the country has been resident at Bridgetown, and it joined the United Nations on 16 September 1980, the Commonwealth in 1979 following independence, and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “St Vincent and the Grenadines,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/st-vincent-and-grenadines. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Vincent and the Grenadines,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vc.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Vincent and the Grenadines,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VCT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saint Vincent and the Grenadines,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saint-vincent-grenadines. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Saint Vincent and the Grenadines and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/saint_vincent_grenadines_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Soufriere.jpg","title":"La Soufrière (volcano), Saint Vincent and the Grenadines","desc":"La Soufrière (volcano).","credit":"CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soufriere.jpg","alt":"A view of La Soufrière (volcano)."}},
{"id":"gw-197","num":197,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Antigua and Barbuda","answerText":"Antigua and Barbuda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Antigua and Barb."},"facts":[["Capital","Saint John’s"],["Population","94,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","442 km² (171 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","antigua and barbuda"],"abstract":"<b>Antigua and Barbuda</b> is a dry tropical country of three islands in the eastern Caribbean: Antigua, with secluded bays, sandy beaches, coral reefs and volcanic rocks rising to about 400 metres (1,300 feet) at Mount Obama; flat, coral Barbuda with its large lagoon; and tiny, rocky, uninhabited Redonda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth gives their areas as 280, 161 and 1.6 square kilometres, where the United Nations gives 442 for the whole and the World Bank 440.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population is 94,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count, 97,928 in 2022 by the Commonwealth’s and 93,772 in 2024 by the World Bank’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only a quarter of its people live in towns, 20,800 of them in the capital, Saint John’s, forest covers 19.2 per cent of the land, and income per head was 21,787 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Visitors numbered 265,000 in 2022 against 125,000 in 2020, nearly three times the country’s own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Antigua and Barbuda became an independent state within the Commonwealth of Nations on 1 November 1981, with Queen Elizabeth as its first Queen and Vere Cornwall Bird as its first prime minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised it as an independent state the same day, by raising its consulate general at Saint John’s to embassy status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on that date, with Paul Byrnes as principal officer, and Ambassador Milan D. Bish presented his credentials on 8 January 1982 while resident at Bridgetown in Barbados.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American embassy at Saint John’s closed on 30 June 1994, and every ambassador since has remained resident at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 11 November 1981 and the Commonwealth in 1981 following independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Antigua and Barbuda,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/antigua-and-barbuda. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Antigua and Barbuda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ag.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Antigua and Barbuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ATG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Antigua and Barbuda,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/antigua-barbuda. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Antigua and Barbuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ATG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Antigua_Shirley%27s_Heights_English_Harbour.jpg/1920px-Antigua_Shirley%27s_Heights_English_Harbour.jpg","title":"English Harbour, Antigua and Barbuda","desc":"English Harbour.","credit":"Dr. Thomas Liptak, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antigua_Shirley%27s_Heights_English_Harbour.jpg","alt":"A view of English Harbour."}},
{"id":"gw-198","num":198,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Isle of Man","answerText":"Isle of Man","answerDate":"","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Isle of Man"},"facts":[["Capital","Douglas"],["Population","84,000 (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","572 km² (221 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","isle of man"],"abstract":"The <b>Isle of Man</b> is one of the three Crown Dependencies of the United Kingdom, with Jersey and Guernsey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Crown Dependencies are not part of the United Kingdom but are self-governing dependencies of the Crown, which means they have their own directly elected legislative assemblies, their own administrative, financial and legal systems and their own courts of law, though most rules and regulations are the same as the United Kingdom’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Kingdom’s constitutional relationship with them is managed by its Ministry of Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives the island’s surface area as 572 square kilometres and its population as 84,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 570 square kilometres and 84,160 people for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The capital is Douglas, holding 27,200; 52.7 per cent of the people live in towns, forest covers 6.1 per cent of the land, and the currency is the pound sterling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That population has barely moved in a decade, the World Bank giving 83,593 for 2015 against 84,160 for 2024, a rise of about half a per cent in nine years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations counts the island separately from the United Kingdom for statistical purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The islands are collectively referred to as “the islands” in the Immigration Act 1971 and the British Nationality Act 1981, and the Channel Islands among them comprise the Bailiwick of Jersey and the Bailiwick of Guernsey, which includes Herm, Sark and Alderney.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Common Travel Area is an administrative arrangement between the United Kingdom, Ireland and the three Crown Dependencies, implemented in United Kingdom domestic law by statute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Someone living on the island who applies there holds a Crown Dependency variant British passport, while one who applies directly to His Majesty’s Passport Office may hold a non-variant one, and dual nationality is recognised on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["His Majesty’s Passport Office, “Isle of Man: Knowledge Base Profile,” Home Office, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/isle-of-man-knowledge-base-profile/isle-of-man-knowledge-base-profile. [Open access]","Ministry of Justice, “Crown Dependencies: Jersey, Guernsey and the Isle of Man,” https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/crown-dependencies-jersey-guernsey-and-the-isle-of-man. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Isle of Man,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/im.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Isle of Man,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IMN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Home Office, “Common Travel Area,” https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/common-travel-area/common-travel-area-accessible. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Isle of Man,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IMN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Laxey_Wheel_-_panoramio_%282%29.jpg/1920px-Laxey_Wheel_-_panoramio_%282%29.jpg","title":"Laxey Wheel, the Isle of Man","desc":"Laxey Wheel.","credit":"Lobster1, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laxey_Wheel_-_panoramio_(2).jpg","alt":"A view of Laxey Wheel."}},
{"id":"gw-199","num":199,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Andorra","answerText":"Andorra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 March 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 February 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Andorra"},"facts":[["Capital","Andorra la Vella"],["Population","83,000 (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","468 km² (181 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","andorra"],"abstract":"<b>Andorra</b> is a landlocked principality in the eastern Pyrenees, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 468 square kilometres and its population as 83,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 470 and 81,938 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That population has grown by about a seventh in a decade, from 72,174 in 2015; 88 per cent of it lives in towns, 22,600 of them in the capital, Andorra la Vella, and 34 per cent of the land is forested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 46,812 US dollars in 2023 against 38,655 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a co-principality, with the Bishop of Urgell in Spain as one co-prince and the President of France as the other, an arrangement that dates back centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That, together with its position, is why Andorra was traditionally isolated from European and international affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The American guide records no act of recognition at all: Andorra has considered itself independent since medieval times and so was already independent when the United States declared its own independence in 1776.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Unlike many other independent states of the late 18th century, Andorra never signed a bilateral treaty with the United States, which would have legally constituted recognition of the new republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It ratified a modern constitution on 14 March 1993, providing for its independence and sovereignty as a representative democracy, and the General Assembly admitted it to membership without a vote at its 108th meeting on 28 July that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 21 February 1995 by an exchange of letters, the Andorran government asking for full relations and Secretary of State Warren Christopher reciprocating by appointing the American consul general in Barcelona to represent the United States to Andorra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every American ambassador to Andorra has also been accredited to Spain and resident in Madrid, while officials of the consulate general in Barcelona conduct day-to-day relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Andorra,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ad.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Andorra,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AND/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Andorra,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AND/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Andorra,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/andorra. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Principality of Andorra to Membership in the United Nations,” A/RES/47/232, 28 July 1993, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/174476. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Andorra_la_Vella_-_view2.jpg/1920px-Andorra_la_Vella_-_view2.jpg","title":"Andorra la Vella, Andorra","desc":"Andorra la Vella.","credit":"Tiia Monto, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andorra_la_Vella_-_view2.jpg","alt":"A view of Andorra la Vella."}},
{"id":"gw-200","num":200,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cayman Islands","answerText":"Cayman Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1661 – 1671</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1831</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Jamaica</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1863</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 November 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cayman Is."},"facts":[["Capital","George Town"],["Population","76,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","264 km² (102 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","cayman islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Cayman Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and comprise three islands: Grand Cayman, Cayman Brac and the adjacent Little Cayman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They lie some 290 kilometres (180 miles) west of Jamaica and about the same distance south of Cuba, with a land area of 264 square kilometres and an exclusive economic zone of 119,137 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The population figures differ: the Secretariat’s working paper gives 84,738 for 2023, of whom 46 per cent are Caymanian and 54 per cent are not, where the United Nations statistics division gives 76,000 for 2025 and the World Bank 74,457 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The statistics division counts the whole population as urban, forest covers 53 per cent of the land, income per head was 97,583 US dollars in 2023 against 76,379 in 2015, and life expectancy at birth is 83.1 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islands were reportedly sighted by Christopher Columbus in the late 15th century and were first settled by the British between 1661 and 1671.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A tradition of self-government began to grow from 1734, and a legislative assembly known as the Assembly of Justices and Vestry was established in 1831.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Slavery was abolished in 1834.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1863 the Cayman Islands became a dependency of Jamaica, and when Jamaica became independent 99 years later the islands remained under the British Crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The current constitution entered into force on 6 November 2009 and was amended in November 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under it the Governor, appointed by the British Crown, is responsible for external affairs, defence and internal security, and the General Assembly first addressed the transmission of information on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Cayman Islands: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/5, 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4079488. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Cayman Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ky.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cayman Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Cayman Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Seven_Mile_Beach_cayman_sun.jpg","title":"Seven Mile Beach, Grand Cayman, the Cayman Islands","desc":"Seven Mile Beach, Grand Cayman.","credit":"User2369, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seven_Mile_Beach_cayman_sun.jpg","alt":"A view of Seven Mile Beach, Grand Cayman."}},
{"id":"gw-202","num":202,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dominica","answerText":"Dominica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1978</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dominica"},"facts":[["Capital","Roseau"],["Population","66,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","750 km² (290 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","dominica"],"abstract":"<b>Dominica</b> is a small, fertile, volcanic island in the eastern Caribbean between the French islands of Guadeloupe and Marie-Galante to the north and Martinique to the south, with a range of high forested mountains and several active volcanoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations, the Commonwealth and the World Bank all give its surface area as 750 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is 66,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count, 72,000 in 2022 by the Commonwealth’s and 66,205 in 2024 by the World Bank’s, down from 70,410 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Forest covers 63.8 per cent of the land, 70.8 per cent of the people live in towns, and 14,900 of them are in the capital, Roseau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Income per head was 9,786 US dollars in 2023 against 7,680 in 2015, agriculture supplies 18.4 per cent of value added against 16.5 in 2015, and the currency is the East Caribbean dollar, exchanged at 2.7 to the US dollar in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Great Britain granted independence to the Commonwealth of Dominica on 3 November 1978, the island having been a British colony before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised it the same day, with Frank V. Ortiz Jr., its ambassador to Barbados, attending the independence celebrations as the personal representative of President Jimmy Carter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first American ambassador to Dominica, Sally Angela Shelton, presented her credentials at Bridgetown in Barbados, where she remained resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States maintains no official presence on the island, its ambassador and embassy staff being resident at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dominica joined the Commonwealth in 1978 following independence and the United Nations on 18 December that year, and the United States maintained relations through the political discontent, arising from long-term economic problems, that led to an interim government in 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Dominica,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/dominica. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Dominica,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dm.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Dominica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DMA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Dominica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DMA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Dominica,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/dominica. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Dominica_Boiling_Lake.jpg/1920px-Dominica_Boiling_Lake.jpg","title":"Boiling Lake, Dominica","desc":"Boiling Lake.","credit":"Bayukjdr, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dominica_Boiling_Lake.jpg","alt":"A view of Boiling Lake."}},
{"id":"gw-203","num":203,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bermuda","answerText":"Bermuda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sighted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1505</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1609</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crown rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1684</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1968</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bermuda"},"facts":[["Capital","Hamilton"],["Population","65,000 (2025)"],["Region","Northern America"],["Area","53 km² (21 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","bermuda"],"abstract":"<b>Bermuda</b> is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and consists of 8 major and 130 smaller islands in the western Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies about 917 kilometres (570 miles) east of the North Carolina coast, and its land area of 53.35 square kilometres sits inside an exclusive economic zone of 450,370 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population figures differ: the Secretariat’s working paper gives 63,905 for 2024, the World Bank 64,636 for the same year, and the United Nations statistics division 65,000 for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That division counts the whole population as urban and files the territory under the code 060 in the region it calls Northern America, and life expectancy at birth was 82.4 years in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2016 census recorded a population about 52 per cent black, 31 per cent white, 9 per cent of mixed race and 8 per cent other races, and the language is English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were sighted in 1505 by the Spanish explorer Juan de Bermúdez and were called ‘La Bermuda’ by 1510, but stayed uninhabited until 1609, when British settlers bound for Virginia were wrecked on a reef.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> King James I extended the charter of the Virginia Company to cover Bermuda in 1612, and government passed to the British Crown after that charter was annulled in 1684.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bermuda was among the territories on which the United Kingdom transmitted information under Article 73 e of the Charter, as the General Assembly noted on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The constitution of 1968, last revised in 2003, leaves the Governor responsible for defence, external affairs, internal security and the police, with a premier heading a cabinet drawn from a 36-member House of Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At a referendum in 1995, 73.6 per cent of those voting rejected independence on a turnout of 58.8 per cent, and income per head reached 125,842 US dollars in 2023 against 104,855 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Bermuda: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/3, 5 March 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4079355. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Bermuda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bm.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bermuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BMU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Horseshoebay.Bermuda.JPG/1920px-Horseshoebay.Bermuda.JPG","title":"Horseshoe Bay, Bermuda, Bermuda","desc":"Horseshoe Bay, Bermuda.","credit":"Ekem, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horseshoebay.Bermuda.JPG","alt":"A view of Horseshoe Bay, Bermuda."}},
{"id":"gw-204","num":204,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Greenland","answerText":"Greenland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Danish colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1721</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Danish county</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Home rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Self-government</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 June 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Greenland"},"facts":[["Capital","Nuuk"],["Population","57,000 (2025)"],["Region","Northern America"],["Area","2,166,086 km² (836,330 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","greenland"],"abstract":"<b>Greenland</b> is the world’s largest island and a self-governing region within the Kingdom of Denmark, with a surface area of 2,166,086 square kilometres (836,330 square miles), about 81 per cent of it under ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division puts its population density at 0.1 people per square kilometre; counting only the ice-free ground, Statistics Greenland makes it 0.3, the lowest in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population is about 57,000, the World Bank giving 56,831 for 2025 where the statistics division rounds to 56,000, and 87 per cent of it is counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every town and settlement stands on the coast, most of them on the south-west, and no roads run between them, so travel is by sea or by air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Nuuk, held 19,880 people on 1 January 2024; the island runs 2,670 kilometres (1,660 miles) north to south and has 44,087 kilometres (27,400 miles) of coastline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Greenland became a Danish colony in 1721 and a Danish county in 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly ended the transmission of information on the island under Article 73 e of the Charter at its 499th plenary meeting, on 22 November 1954.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Home rule was granted in 1979 and self-government established on 21 June 2009, and the island has its own flag and issues its own stamps but has no currency of its own, the Danish krone being legal tender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It entered the European Economic Community with Denmark in 1973 and left in 1985, after a referendum held in 1982.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agriculture, with forestry and fishing, made up 18.3 per cent of gross value added in 2023, income per head was 54,374 US dollars that year, and the statistics division files the island under the code 304 in the region it calls Northern America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Statistics Greenland, <i>Greenland in Figures 2024</i>, 21st ed. (Nuuk: Statistics Greenland, 2024), 4, https://stat.gl/publ/en/GF/2024/pdf/Greenland%20in%20Figures%202024.pdf. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Greenland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gl.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Greenland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Cessation of the Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter in Respect of Greenland,” A/RES/849(IX), 22 November 1954, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/211980. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Greenland_Ilulissat-25.jpg","title":"Ilulissat Icefjord, Greenland","desc":"Ilulissat Icefjord.","credit":"Michael Haferkamp, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greenland_Ilulissat-25.jpg","alt":"A view of Ilulissat Icefjord."}},
{"id":"gw-205","num":205,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Faroe Islands","answerText":"Faroe Islands","answerDate":"","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Faeroe Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Tórshavn"],["Population","55,000 (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","1,393 km² (538 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","faroe islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Faroe Islands</b> are an archipelago in the North Atlantic which the United Nations files under Northern Europe with the code 234 and a surface area of 1,393 square kilometres (538 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Statistics Faroe Islands lists 18 islands and measures each of them separately, from Streymoy at 374.89 square kilometres down to Lítla Dímun at 0.83, the last of them the only one with no residents at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The highest point is Slættaratindur on Eysturoy at 880 metres (2,890 feet), and the largest lake is Leitisvatn on Vágar at 3.57 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two closest counts of the population agree: Statistics Faroe Islands recorded about 55,000 through 2025 and the World Bank gives 54,900 for that year, where the statistics division rounds to 56,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown in every year the World Bank records, from 48,898 in 2015, and 14.6 per cent of it is counted as foreign-born, a figure that includes people born in Denmark and Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The capital is Tórshavn, with 20,800 people in 2018, and only 42.2 per cent of the population was counted as urban in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Danish krone, and for statistical purposes the United Nations excludes the islands from its figures for Denmark, treating them as a separate area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> European Union law does not reach them: the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union provides at Article 355 that the Treaties ‘shall not apply to the Faeroe Islands’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth was 82.8 years for women and 78.6 for men in 2025, the fertility rate 2.2 children per woman, and forest covers 0.1 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Exports were worth 2,364 million US dollars in 2024 against imports of 1,963 million, a surplus of 401 million, and the agricultural production index, set at 100 for 2014 to 2016, stood at 108 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Faroe Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fo.html. [Open access]","Statistics Faroe Islands, “Islands, Mountains, Islets and Lakes,” https://hagstova.fo/en/environment/geography/islands-mountains-islets-og-lakes. [Open access]","Statistics Faroe Islands, “Population,” https://hagstova.fo/en/population/population/population. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Faroe Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FRO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","European Union, <i>Consolidated Version of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union</i>, art. 355, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:12016E355. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/G%C3%A1sadalur_%2851256491640%29.jpg/1920px-G%C3%A1sadalur_%2851256491640%29.jpg","title":"Gásadalur, the Faroe Islands","desc":"Gásadalur.","credit":"Mike Maguire, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%A1sadalur_(51256491640).jpg","alt":"A view of Gásadalur."}},
{"id":"gw-206","num":206,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Kitts and Nevis","answerText":"Saint Kitts and Nevis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1623</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Associated state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 September 1983</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 September 1983</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"St. Kitts and Nevis"},"facts":[["Capital","Basseterre"],["Population","47,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","261 km² (101 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","saint kitts and nevis"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Kitts and Nevis</b> is a two-island federation in the Caribbean with its capital at Basseterre, its area given as 261 square kilometres (101 square miles) by the United Nations statistics division and 260 by the World Bank, and the Commonwealth measuring Saint Kitts at 176 and Nevis at 93.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The population is about 47,000, the statistics division and the World Bank agreeing to within a hundred people, though the Commonwealth Secretariat gives 53,000 for 2022, a figure the World Bank’s series never passes through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only 30.8 per cent of the population is counted as urban, income per head was 22,625 US dollars in 2023, and the currency is the East Caribbean dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Industry made up 23 per cent of gross value added in 2023 against 1.6 per cent for agriculture and 75.4 for services, and women held 31.2 per cent of the seats in parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 76.3 years for women and 68.8 for men, and the fertility rate is 1.5 children per woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were the site of Great Britain’s first Caribbean settlement, in 1623, and passed through centuries of British and French colonial rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Together with Anguilla they became a self-governing state in association with Great Britain in 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saint Christopher and Nevis declared independence on 19 September 1983 and the United States recognised it the same day, Ambassador Milan D. Bish heading the American delegation at the ceremonies, and the country joined the United Nations four days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been called Saint Kitts and Nevis since 3 August 1988, and it joined the Commonwealth in 1983 on independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States keeps no official presence in the federation, a consular agent resident in nearby Antigua assisting its citizens there, and every American ambassador to it has been resident at Bridgetown in Barbados.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saint Kitts and Nevis,” https://history.state.gov/countries/saint-kitts-nevis. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Kitts and Nevis,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kn.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “St Kitts and Nevis,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/st-kitts-and-nevis. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Kitts and Nevis,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KNA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: St. Kitts and Nevis,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KNA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/BrimstoneHill01.jpg/1920px-BrimstoneHill01.jpg","title":"Brimstone Hill Fortress National Park, Saint Kitts and Nevis","desc":"Brimstone Hill Fortress National Park.","credit":"Ukexpat, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BrimstoneHill01.jpg","alt":"A view of Brimstone Hill Fortress National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-207","num":207,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"American Samoa","answerText":"American Samoa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty of Berlin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratification Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 February 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">To the Interior</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1951</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elected governor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1977</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"American Samoa"},"facts":[["Capital","Pago Pago"],["Population","46,000 (2025)"],["Region","Polynesia"],["Area","199 km² (77 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","american samoa"],"abstract":"<b>American Samoa</b> is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter and an unincorporated, unorganized territory of the United States, administered by the Interior Department’s Office of Insular Affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies in the South Pacific about 3,700 kilometres (2,300 miles) south-west of Hawaii, and its seven islands are Tutuila, Aunuu, the Manu’a Islands of Ofu, Olosega and Ta’u, and the atolls of Rose Island and Swains Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land area is 200 square kilometres by the working paper and the World Bank and 199 by the statistics division, and its exclusive economic zone 404,391.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population is falling: the World Bank’s series runs from 52,878 in 2015 to 46,029 in 2025, and the statistics division gives −1.6 per cent a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 85.4 per cent of it, the languages are English and Samoan, and the United Nations files the territory under Polynesia with code 016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Not all provisions of the Constitution or the laws of the United States apply there, and a person born in the territory neither of whose parents is a citizen is a United States national rather than a citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such nationals may live and work anywhere in the United States but may not vote in its federal elections, while everyone in the territory over 18 votes locally and for its delegate to the House of Representatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The archipelago is thought to have been settled some 3,000 years ago from South-East Asia, the Dutch reached it in 1722, and the Treaty of Berlin of 1899 apportioned its eastern islands to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deeds ceding those islands were accepted under the Ratification Act of 20 February 1929, which made American Samoans United States nationals, and Executive Order 10264 of 29 June 1951 moved the territory from the Navy to the Interior Department.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The governor has been elected by popular vote since 1977, the legislature is the bicameral Fono, and tuna canning is the largest private employer, at a minimum wage of 6.36 dollars an hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “American Samoa: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/1, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4109505. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “American Samoa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/as.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: American Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ASM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: American Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ASM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Pola_Islands_Tutuila_NPS.jpg/1920px-Pola_Islands_Tutuila_NPS.jpg","title":"National Park of American Samoa, American Samoa","desc":"National Park of American Samoa.","credit":"Tavita Togia, National Park Service, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pola_Islands_Tutuila_NPS.jpg","alt":"A view of National Park of American Samoa."}},
{"id":"gw-208","num":208,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Turks and Caicos Islands","answerText":"Turks and Caicos Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1799, by Great Britain</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Separate colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Own governor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in force 15 October 2012</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turks and Caicos Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Cockburn Town"],["Population","47,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","948 km² (366 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","turks and caicos islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Turks and Caicos Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and comprise 40 islands and cays 145 kilometres (90 miles) north of Haiti and the Dominican Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Six are permanently inhabited: Grand Turk, where the capital Cockburn Town stands, Providenciales, which holds the great majority of the population, North Caicos, Middle Caicos, South Caicos and Salt Cay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land area is 948.2 square kilometres, a figure the statistics division measures to the shoreline at low water, and the exclusive economic zone runs to 154,068 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population figures differ: the working paper gives 50,828 for 2024 where the statistics division gives 47,000 for 2025 and the World Bank 46,855, a quarter more than the 36,605 the World Bank recorded in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Income per head reached 30,349 US dollars in 2023, tourism and financial services carry the economy, and wetlands cover nearly half the surface of the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first inhabitants were the Arawak-speaking Taino, and Great Britain annexed the islands in 1799 as part of the Bahamas, attaching them later to Jamaica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They became a separate colony in 1962, when Jamaica became independent, but kept close constitutional ties with the Bahamas until a governor replaced their administrator after Bahamian independence in 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Turks and Caicos Islands Constitution Order 2011, in force on 15 October 2012, is the fifth constitution since 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under it the legislature consists of the Crown and a House of Assembly of a Speaker, fifteen elected members, four appointed members and the Attorney General, who may not vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Governor keeps responsibility for defence, external affairs, internal security including the police, public appointments and the regulation of international financial services, and the United Nations files the territory under the Caribbean with code 796.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Turks and Caicos Islands: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/15, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111022. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Turks and Caicos Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tc.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Turks and Caicos Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TCA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","The Turks and Caicos Islands Constitution Order 2011, SI 2011/1681, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2011/1681/made. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Turtle_Cove_Providenciales_Beach.jpg/1920px-Turtle_Cove_Providenciales_Beach.jpg","title":"Providenciales, the Turks and Caicos Islands","desc":"Providenciales.","credit":"Tim Sackton, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turtle_Cove_Providenciales_Beach.jpg","alt":"A view of Providenciales."}},
{"id":"gw-209","num":209,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Northern Mariana Islands","answerText":"Northern Mariana Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Covenant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN trusteeship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ended 22 December 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US immigration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 2008</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"N. Mariana Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Garapan"],["Population","44,000 (2025)"],["Region","Micronesia"],["Area","457 km² (176 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","northern mariana islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Northern Mariana Islands</b> are a commonwealth in political union with the United States, lying in Micronesia, with a land area of 457 square kilometres (176 square miles) by the statistics division and 460 by the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their government sits at Capital Hill on Saipan, though the statistics division names Garapan as the capital, and 91.7 per cent of the population is counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has fallen in every year the World Bank records, from 51,514 in 2015 to 43,541 in 2025, and the statistics division puts the annual rate at −1.6 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Half the people living there were born abroad, imports ran to 7,225 million US dollars in 2022 against exports of 2,072 million, and forest covers 53 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 81.0 years for women and 77.4 for men, and the currency is the United States dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were part of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, which the United States administered on behalf of the United Nations from 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Covenant making them a United States territory passed into federal law in 1975, a constitution was adopted in 1977, and the first constitutional government took office in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 22 December 1990, at its 2972nd meeting and by fourteen votes to one, the Security Council determined that the Trusteeship Agreement had ceased to apply to them, to the Federated States of Micronesia and to the Marshall Islands, whose peoples had approved new status agreements in plebiscites the Trusteeship Council observed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The statistics division gave the islands a code of their own, 580, in 1991, having counted them until then within the Pacific Islands Trust Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Federal minimum wage rules reached the commonwealth in 2007 and federal immigration law in 2008, and the Department of Homeland Security took over its immigration and border controls in June 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Northern Mariana Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mp.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Northern Mariana Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Northern Mariana Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNP/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","Office of Insular Affairs, US Department of the Interior, “Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands,” https://www.doi.gov/oia/islands/cnmi. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 683 (1990), Adopted by the Security Council at Its 2972nd Meeting, on 22 December 1990,” S/RES/683(1990), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/103696. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Managaha_SW_2014.JPG/1920px-Managaha_SW_2014.JPG","title":"Managaha, the Northern Mariana Islands","desc":"Managaha.","credit":"Marcus Hsu, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Managaha_SW_2014.JPG","alt":"A view of Managaha."}},
{"id":"gw-210","num":210,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sint Maarten","answerText":"Sint Maarten","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Own country</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 2010</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hurricane Irma</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 2017</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sint Maarten"},"facts":[["Capital","Philipsburg"],["Population","44,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","34 km² (13 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","sint maarten"],"abstract":"<b>Sint Maarten</b> is a country of the Kingdom of the Netherlands in the Caribbean of 34 square kilometres (13 square miles), which the United Nations files as Sint Maarten (Dutch part) under the code 534, with about 44,000 people and its capital at Philipsburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population density is 1,291.9 people to the square kilometre, the whole of it is counted as urban, and 69.5 per cent of the people living there were born abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has grown in every year the World Bank records, from 37,471 in 2015 to 43,923 in 2025, and income per head was 39,237 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Services make up 93.3 per cent of gross value added against 6.7 for industry, and 373,000 tourists arrived in 2022 against 505,000 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Netherlands Antillean guilder, and the statistics division excludes the island from its figures for the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 10 October 2010 Curaçao and Sint Maarten became autonomous countries, while Bonaire, Sint Eustatius and Saba became special municipalities of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The statistics division gave Sint Maarten a code of its own that year, having counted it until then within the Netherlands Antilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kingdom as a whole answers for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law, while each of its four countries decides its own policy on matters such as healthcare, tourism and employment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Governor appointed by the King for six years, renewable once, heads the government without ministerial responsibility, and a Minister Plenipotentiary in The Hague sits with the Dutch ministers in the Council of Ministers for the Kingdom, though Sint Maarten, unlike Aruba and Curaçao, has none in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hurricane Irma struck on 6 September 2017 and destroyed many homes and much infrastructure, and the Netherlands set aside 550 million euros for reconstruction on two conditions, an Integrity Chamber and improved border controls, with up to 470 million of it administered by the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sint Maarten (Dutch part),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sx.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sint Maarten (Dutch part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SXM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Caribbean Parts of the Kingdom,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Responsibilities of the Netherlands, Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/responsibilities-of-the-netherlands-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Governance of Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/governance-of-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Reconstruction of St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/reconstruction-of-st-maarten. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Maho_Beach.jpg/1920px-Maho_Beach.jpg","title":"Maho Beach, Sint Maarten","desc":"Maho Beach.","credit":"Patrick Hawks, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maho_Beach.jpg","alt":"An airliner passing low over sunbathers on a beach."}},
{"id":"gw-211","num":211,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Liechtenstein","answerText":"Liechtenstein","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sovereignty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1806</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Confederation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1815</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">again in 1866</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Liechtenstein"},"facts":[["Capital","Vaduz"],["Population","40,000 (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Area","160 km² (62 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","liechtenstein"],"abstract":"<b>Liechtenstein</b> is a country of 160 square kilometres (62 square miles) — the figure the statistics division and the World Bank both give — in the region the United Nations calls Western Europe, with about 40,000 people and its capital at Vaduz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only 14.4 per cent of its population is counted as urban, and 69.4 per cent of the people living there were born abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 201,150 US dollars in 2023, industry making up 42.1 per cent of gross value added against 57.7 for services and 0.2 for agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 85.5 years for women and 82.2 for men, forest covers 41.9 per cent of the land, and 98.5 per cent of its important sites for terrestrial biodiversity are protected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Swiss franc, and the World Bank’s series has the population rising in every year from 37,494 in 2015 to 41,024 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Formerly part of the Holy Roman Empire, Liechtenstein achieved sovereignty in 1806, joined the German Confederation in 1815 and became independent again in 1866.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It yielded the conduct of its foreign affairs to Switzerland for much of the twentieth century, so its direct diplomatic connections with other states are of relatively recent origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States executed its first treaty with Liechtenstein in 1926, when Switzerland still represented it abroad, an exchange of notes between the Swiss and American governments extending the rights of a United States–Swiss agreement to Liechtenstein’s citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began pursuing membership of international organisations in its own right at the end of the twentieth century, and joined the United Nations on 18 September 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations with the United States were established on 10 February 1997, when Madeleine M. Kunin, the American ambassador to Switzerland, became the first accredited to Liechtenstein, resident at Bern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Liechtenstein,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/li.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Liechtenstein,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LIE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Liechtenstein,” https://history.state.gov/countries/liechtenstein. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Liechtenstein,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LIE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Castillo%2C_Vaduz%2C_Liechtenstein%2C_2022-10-23%2C_DD_65.jpg/1920px-Castillo%2C_Vaduz%2C_Liechtenstein%2C_2022-10-23%2C_DD_65.jpg","title":"Vaduz Castle, Liechtenstein","desc":"Vaduz Castle.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo,_Vaduz,_Liechtenstein,_2022-10-23,_DD_65.jpg","alt":"A view of Vaduz Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-212","num":212,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"British Virgin Islands","answerText":"British Virgin Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dutch settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1648</span><span class=\"dt-k\">British control</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1666</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ministerial rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"British Virgin Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Road Town"],["Population","40,000 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","151 km² (58 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","british virgin islands"],"abstract":"The <b>British Virgin Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and comprise some 60 islands, islets and cays, twenty of them inhabited and filed by the United Nations under the Caribbean with code 092.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They lie about 100 kilometres (60 miles) east of Puerto Rico and 25 kilometres (16 miles) from the United States Virgin Islands, with which they form one archipelago, the major islands being Tortola, Virgin Gorda, Anegada and Jost Van Dyke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land area is 153 square kilometres by the working paper, 151 by the statistics division and 150 by the World Bank, against an exclusive economic zone of 80,117 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The population is about 40,000 — 39,471 for 2024 in the working paper and 39,732 for 2025 at the World Bank — and income per head was 40,478 US dollars in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Road Town, the capital, stands on Tortola, the largest island; the language is English, the currency the United States dollar, and financial services and tourism carry the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest known inhabitants were the Arawaks and the Caribs, and the Dutch made the first permanent European settlement in 1648.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British planters took control in 1666 and the islands became a British colony, and ministerial government arrived with the constitution of 1967 under a new office of Chief Minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Virgin Islands Constitution Order 2007 the British Crown appoints a governor answering for defence, internal security, external affairs, the terms of service of public servants and the administration of the courts, with some external-affairs matters delegated to the territorial government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Commission of Inquiry into the territory’s governance was established in January 2021, and its report was made public on 29 April 2022 with 49 recommendations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It found unlawful a 20-year residence requirement the Cabinet had applied to belonger status since 2004, contrary to the 1977 Immigration and Passport Act, and a review published in September 2023 made 15 further recommendations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “British Virgin Islands: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/4, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111018. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “British Virgin Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vg.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: British Virgin Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VGB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: British Virgin Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VGB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Virgin_Gorda_North_Sound_2010.jpg/1920px-Virgin_Gorda_North_Sound_2010.jpg","title":"Virgin Gorda, the British Virgin Islands","desc":"Virgin Gorda.","credit":"Gruepig, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgin_Gorda_North_Sound_2010.jpg","alt":"A view of Virgin Gorda."}},
{"id":"gw-213","num":213,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Gibraltar","answerText":"Gibraltar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded to Britain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1713, Treaty of Utrecht</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN appeal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">resolution 2070, 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Brussels talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 November 1984</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Gibraltar"},"facts":[["Capital","Gibraltar"],["Population","40,000 (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","6 km² (2.3 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","gibraltar"],"abstract":"<b>Gibraltar</b> is a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom: a narrow peninsula running south from the south-western coast of Spain, joined to it by an isthmus about 1.6 kilometres (1 mile) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Algeciras lies 8 kilometres (5 miles) across the bay and Africa 32 kilometres (20 miles) across the strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its area is itself contested: the administering Power gives 5.8 square kilometres and Spain, which claims sovereignty, 4.8, while the statistics division gives 6 and the World Bank rounds to 10.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The population was 37,936 on 2022 data and 40,126 for 2025 at the World Bank, at 4,012.6 to the square kilometre; the currency is the Gibraltar pound, at par with sterling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There is no known natural resource and no farmland, so the economy rests on tourism and on financial services — banking, insurance, shipping and online gaming — with output of £3.1 billion in 2024/25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Spain ceded sovereignty to the United Kingdom under the Treaty of Utrecht in 1713; the United Kingdom holds that its territorial waters follow from sovereignty over the land, and Spain that article 10 ceded only the city, castle, port and fortifications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly first appealed to both governments to hold talks in resolution 2070 (XX) of 1965, and in 2025 urged them to reach a definitive solution in the spirit of the Brussels Declaration of 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Gibraltar Constitution Order 2006 the Governor answers for external affairs, defence, internal security and certain public appointments, and the elected government for everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parliament may make laws for the peace, order and good government of Gibraltar and the British Crown retains the same power, unused since 2006; appeals run from the supreme court through a court of appeal to the Privy Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An agreement on taxation between Spain and the United Kingdom regarding Gibraltar took effect on 4 March 2021, after Britain left the European Union, and the United Nations files the territory under Southern Europe with code 292.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Gibraltar: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/8, 23 February 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4105789. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Gibraltar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gi.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Gibraltar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GIB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Gibraltar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GIB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/View_of_the_Rock_of_Gibraltar_from_the_Strait.jpg/1920px-View_of_the_Rock_of_Gibraltar_from_the_Strait.jpg","title":"the Rock of Gibraltar, Gibraltar","desc":"The Rock of Gibraltar.","credit":"rob Stoeltje, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Rock_of_Gibraltar_from_the_Strait.jpg","alt":"A steep limestone headland rising from the sea."}},
{"id":"gw-214","num":214,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Monaco","answerText":"Monaco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1793, by France</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">regained in 1814</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty renewed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2005</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 May 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Monaco"},"facts":[["Capital","Monaco"],["Population","38,000 (2025)"],["Region","Western Europe"],["Area","2 km² (0.77 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","monaco"],"abstract":"<b>Monaco</b> is a sovereign principality of 2 square kilometres (0.77 square miles) on the Mediterranean coast, with about 38,000 people at 25,732.2 to the square kilometre, all of them counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole state is one city, also called Monaco, the currency is the euro, and the United Nations files the principality under Western Europe with code 492.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its area is a case for checking a source before quoting it: the World Bank’s surface-area series gives 74.9 square kilometres, some 37 times the statistics division’s figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 256,581 US dollars in 2023, services making up 88.5 per cent of gross value added, and 70.2 per cent of the people living there were born abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 88.7 years for women and 84.7 for men, and the World Bank’s series has the population peaking at 38,956 in 2023 and easing to 38,341 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When the United States declared independence in 1776 Monaco was already a sovereign country, under the military protection of Louis XVI of France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was annexed to the First French Republic in 1793, regained its independence and sovereignty in 1814, and had both reaffirmed by the Treaty of Vienna the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1815 to 1861 it stood under the protectorship of the Kingdom of Piedmont-Sardinia, and it has been under the military safeguard of France since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Monaco and the United States exchanged their first consular agents after the American Civil War, and the marriage of Grace Kelly to Prince Rainier III in 1956 strengthened the tie further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The principality joined the United Nations on 28 May 1993 and the Council of Europe in October 2004, and when France and Monaco renegotiated their 1918 treaty in 2005 the new provisions reaffirmed Monaco’s independence and sovereignty, after which it began to receive foreign ambassadors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Monaco,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mc.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Monaco,” https://history.state.gov/countries/monaco. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Monaco,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MCO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Monaco,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MCO/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/The_Casino%2C_Monte_Carlo_-_panoramio_cropped.jpg/1920px-The_Casino%2C_Monte_Carlo_-_panoramio_cropped.jpg","title":"Monte Carlo Casino, Monaco","desc":"Monte Carlo Casino.","credit":"Steve Hedin, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Casino,_Monte_Carlo_-_panoramio_cropped.jpg","alt":"A view of Monte Carlo Casino."}},
{"id":"gw-215","num":215,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Marshall Islands","answerText":"Marshall Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Self-governing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Compact</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 October 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trusteeship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ended 22 December 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Marshall Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Majuro"],["Population","36,000 (2025)"],["Region","Micronesia"],["Area","181 km² (70 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","marshall islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Marshall Islands</b> are a republic in Micronesia of 181 square kilometres (70 square miles), with about 36,000 people at 201.6 to the square kilometre and their capital at Majuro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has fallen in every year the World Bank records, from 48,800 in 2015 to 36,282 in 2025, a quarter of it gone in a decade, and the statistics division puts the annual rate at −3.5 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the United States dollar, income per head was 6,952 dollars in 2023, and about 77 per cent of the population is counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agriculture makes up 19.7 per cent of gross value added against 12.4 for industry and 67.9 for services, and imports ran to 18,375 million US dollars in 2022 against exports of 1,277 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gave the islands a code of their own, 584, in 1991, having counted them until then within the Pacific Islands Trust Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The republic became self-governing in 1979, and on 25 June 1983 it began negotiating a Compact of Free Association with the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Compact was agreed and entered into force on 21 October 1986, ending the trusteeship agreement and giving the islands the right to exercise self-determination, while the United States kept responsibility for their defence and the continued use of Kwajalein Atoll for military purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the independence of the Marshall Islands that day, and an Office of the United States Representative opened at Majuro under Samuel B. Thomsen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 22 December 1990 the Security Council determined, by fourteen votes to one at its 2972nd meeting, that the Trusteeship Agreement had ceased to apply to the islands, to the Federated States of Micronesia and to the Northern Mariana Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 26 July 1989, the representative’s office was raised to an embassy that September, William Bodde Jr. was appointed ambassador on 27 June 1990, and the republic joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Marshall Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mh.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Marshall Islands,” https://history.state.gov/countries/marshall-islands. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Marshall Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MHL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 683 (1990), Adopted by the Security Council at Its 2972nd Meeting, on 22 December 1990,” S/RES/683(1990), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/103696. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/IJN_Nagato.jpg/1920px-IJN_Nagato.jpg","title":"Bikini Atoll, the Marshall Islands","desc":"Bikini Atoll.","credit":"AshS1987, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IJN_Nagato.jpg","alt":"A view of Bikini Atoll."}},
{"id":"gw-216","num":216,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"San Marino","answerText":"San Marino","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 May 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Extradition treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1906</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consular relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 April 1925</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"San Marino"},"facts":[["Capital","San Marino"],["Population","34,000 (2025)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","61 km² (24 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","san marino"],"abstract":"<b>San Marino</b> is a republic of 61 square kilometres (24 square miles) enclosed by Italy, with about 34,000 people at 550.4 to the square kilometre and its capital, of the same name, holding 4,500 of them in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank rounds the area to 60 square kilometres and gives 34,109 people for 2025, a figure the statistics division rounds to 34,000, and the United Nations files the republic under Southern Europe with code 674.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The currency is the euro, income per head was 58,917 US dollars in 2023, and 97.4 per cent of the population is counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Industry makes up 40.1 per cent of gross value added against 59.9 for services and almost nothing for agriculture, which employs 0.3 per cent of those in work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 87.3 years for women and 84.4 for men, and the fertility rate is 1.2 children per woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Regent Captains of San Marino wrote to President Abraham Lincoln on 29 March 1861 offering him honorary citizenship, and his reply of 7 May is taken as the moment the United States recognised the republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lincoln wrote that “although your dominion is small, your State is nevertheless one of the most honored, in all history”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two states signed a treaty of extradition at Rome on 10 January 1906 and established consular relations on 9 April 1925, the consul at Florence generally serving as the American representative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No ambassador was accredited to the republic until 2006, and Ronald Spogli, who was also ambassador to Italy, presented his credentials on 8 March 2007 from Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> San Marino joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992, and the World Bank’s series has its population rising from 32,897 in 2015 to 34,109 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “San Marino,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: San Marino,” https://history.state.gov/countries/san-marino. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: San Marino,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SMR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: San Marino,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SMR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Guaita_Fortress_-_San_Marino_-_2024_02_13_-_GT_02_%28details%29.jpg/1920px-Guaita_Fortress_-_San_Marino_-_2024_02_13_-_GT_02_%28details%29.jpg","title":"Guaita, San Marino","desc":"Guaita.","credit":"Terragio67, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guaita_Fortress_-_San_Marino_-_2024_02_13_-_GT_02_(details).jpg","alt":"A view of Guaita."}},
{"id":"gw-217","num":217,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Åland","answerText":"Åland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">To Russia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1809</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Demilitarised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1856</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Convention</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1921, by ten states</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomy Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Åland"},"facts":[["Capital","Mariehamn"],["Population","30,836 (2025)"],["Region","Northern Europe"],["Area","1,586 km² (612 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","åland"],"abstract":"<b>Åland</b> is an autonomous, demilitarised, Swedish-speaking region of Finland, a group of islands of 1,586 square kilometres (612 square miles) in the Baltic between Finland and Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There are 6,757 islands of at least a quarter of a hectare and some 20,000 smaller ones and skerries, and 30,836 residents at the end of 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main island holds 70 per cent of the land and 90 per cent of the people; Mariehamn, the only town, was founded in 1861 and holds 11,957, with 1,990 in the outer archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 40 per cent of the inhabitants were born outside Åland, most in Finland or Sweden but some from about 110 countries in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has 16 municipalities, the smallest of them, Sottunga, being the smallest in all Finland, and the United Nations gave the islands a code of their own in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Åland passed to the Russian Empire in 1809, and after British and French troops took the fortress at Bomarsund in the Crimean War it was demilitarised by a one-sided Russian commitment at Paris in 1856.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the League of Nations settled the islands’ constitutional affiliation in 1921, an international convention signed by ten states confirmed that demilitarisation and added neutralisation, so no military may be present, the islands may not be fortified, and they must be kept outside any theatre of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia is not a party to the 1921 convention, but the Moscow Treaty of 1940 and the Paris Peace Treaty of 1947 both carry demilitarisation provisions, though neither mentions neutralisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Autonomy rests on an Act on the Autonomy of Åland passed by the Parliament of Finland, the third of which took effect on 1 January 1993; amending it needs a constitutional majority in Finland and the consent of the 30-member Lagting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Åland acceded to the European Union with Finland in 1995 after two referendums and on a protocol of its own, which treats it as a third territory for indirect taxation; the treaties apply only as that protocol provides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Government of Åland, “Facts about Åland,” https://www.aland.ax/en/facts-about-aland. [Open access]","Statistics and Research Åland, “Facts about Åland,” https://www.asub.ax/en/facts-about-aland. [Open access]","Nordic Statistics Database, “AREA02: Land Use, Square Kilometres by Reporting Country, Unit and Time,” https://pxweb.nordicstatistics.org/pxweb/en/Nordic%20Statistics/Nordic%20Statistics__Geography%20and%20climate__Land%20use/AREA02.px/. [Open access]","European Union, <i>Consolidated Version of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union</i>, art. 355, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:12016E355. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/KasterholmsCastel.jpg/1920px-KasterholmsCastel.jpg","title":"Kastelholm Castle, Åland","desc":"Kastelholm Castle.","credit":"OmJagKundeDrömma, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KasterholmsCastel.jpg","alt":"A view of Kastelholm Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-219","num":219,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Anguilla","answerText":"Anguilla","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Colonised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1650</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UK dependency</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in force 1982</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last amended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Anguilla"},"facts":[["Capital","The Valley"],["Population","16,010 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","96 km² (37 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","anguilla"],"abstract":"<b>Anguilla</b> is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, lying 240 kilometres (150 miles) east of Puerto Rico, 113 kilometres (70 miles) north-west of Saint Kitts and Nevis and 8 kilometres (5 miles) north of Sint Maarten and Saint Martin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is relatively flat, its few rolling hills rising to 65 metres (213 feet), and its main island runs 26 kilometres (16 miles) at its longest and 5 kilometres (3 miles) at its widest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land area is 96 square kilometres by the working paper and 91 by the United Nations statistics division, against an exclusive economic zone of 92,178 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population is 16,010 on the working paper’s 2025 estimate where the statistics division gives 15,000 for the same year, all of it counted as urban, and the capital is The Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> English is spoken by 97 per cent of the people, with Spanish and Chinese also spoken, and the currency is the East Caribbean dollar, pegged to the United States dollar at 2.6882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Anguilla was originally inhabited by the Arawaks and was colonised by British and Irish settlers in 1650, after which it was periodically attached to Saint Kitts and Nevis and to various regional structures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a dependency of the United Kingdom in 1980, and the Anguilla Constitution Order came into force in 1982 and has been amended in 1990, 2019 and 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under it the government consists of the Governor, the Executive Council and the House of Assembly, the Governor answering for defence, external affairs, internal security including the police, international financial services and their regulation, and public service appointments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tourism and financial services carry the economy, income per head was 28,850 US dollars on the 2025 estimate, and services made up 80.4 per cent of gross value added in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations files the territory under the Caribbean with code 660, and the General Assembly first took note of the information transmitted on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Anguilla: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/2, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4104532. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Anguilla,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ai.html. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","The Anguilla Constitution Order 1982, SI 1982/334, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1982/334/made. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Anguilla_Shoal_Bay_is_the_BEST_beach_in_the_Caribbean._-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Anguilla_Shoal_Bay_is_the_BEST_beach_in_the_Caribbean._-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Shoal Bay, Anguilla","desc":"Shoal Bay in Anguilla.","credit":"onj, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anguilla_Shoal_Bay_is_the_BEST_beach_in_the_Caribbean._-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A white sand beach curving along clear turquoise water."}},
{"id":"gw-220","num":220,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Palau","answerText":"Palau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Compact</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trusteeship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ended 10 November 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Palau"},"facts":[["Capital","Melekeok"],["Population","18,000 (2025)"],["Region","Micronesia"],["Area","459 km² (177 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","palau"],"abstract":"<b>Palau</b> is a republic in the western Pacific of 459 square kilometres (177 square miles), with about 18,000 people at 38.4 to the square kilometre and the United States dollar as its currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations names Melekeok as its capital, though the figure it gives for the capital’s population, 11,400 in 2018, refers by its own footnote to Koror, and the American embassy stands at Koror too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population is almost flat: the World Bank’s series runs from 17,770 in 2015 to 17,663 in 2025, moving by less than one per cent across the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Income per head was 15,899 US dollars in 2023, gross domestic product 282 million, and agriculture made up 3.3 per cent of gross value added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations files the republic under Micronesia with code 585, having counted it until 1994 within the Pacific Islands Trust Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Palau formed part of the United Nations Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, administered by the United States after the Second World War, and began the process towards independence in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people approved a new status agreement in a plebiscite observed by a visiting mission of the Trusteeship Council, and its legislature adopted a resolution approving the same agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> President Bill Clinton issued proclamation 6726 on 27 September 1994, recording that the United States had fulfilled its obligations under the Trusteeship Agreement, and the Compact of Free Association took effect on 1 October, which is the date of American recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 10 November 1994 the Security Council, meeting for the 3455th time, determined unanimously that the objectives of the Trusteeship Agreement had been fully attained and that it had ceased to apply to Palau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Palau joined the United Nations on 15 December 1994, an American ambassador resident at Manila was accredited to it on 6 December 1996, and an embassy of its own opened at Koror on 10 October 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Palau,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pw.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Palau,” https://history.state.gov/countries/palau. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Palau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PLW/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 956 (1994), Adopted by the Security Council at Its 3455th Meeting, on 10 November 1994,” S/RES/956(1994), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/163867. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Ngerukewid-2016-aerial-view-Luka-Peternel.jpg/1920px-Ngerukewid-2016-aerial-view-Luka-Peternel.jpg","title":"Rock Islands, Palau","desc":"Rock Islands.","credit":"Luka Peternel, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ngerukewid-2016-aerial-view-Luka-Peternel.jpg","alt":"A view of Rock Islands."}},
{"id":"gw-221","num":221,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cook Islands","answerText":"Cook Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Free association</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2001, reaffirmed 2015</span><span class=\"dt-k\">High income</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2019</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 September 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cook Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Avarua"],["Population","13,000 (2025)"],["Region","Polynesia"],["Area","236 km² (91 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","cook islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Cook Islands</b> are a self-governing state in free association with New Zealand, lying in Polynesia between French Polynesia and American Samoa, with about 13,000 people and their capital at Avarua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives the islands 236 square kilometres (91 square miles), a figure its own footnote says excludes Niue, which it calls part of the Cook Islands but administered separately because of remoteness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its figure for the capital’s population, 13,100 in 2018, refers by another footnote to the whole island of Rarotonga, so it is not a figure for Avarua at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the New Zealand dollar, the whole population is counted against a density of 55.3 people to the square kilometre, and the statistics office took its most recent census of population and dwellings in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations files the islands under Polynesia with code 184, and for statistical purposes its data for New Zealand exclude them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The free-association arrangement was established in 1965, and the islands are part of the Realm of New Zealand, so they share a head of state, King Charles III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They conduct their own affairs, hold an international legal personality of their own and conduct their own international relations, while New Zealand carries a constitutional responsibility to answer requests for help with foreign affairs, disasters and defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cook Islanders hold New Zealand citizenship, and a large Cook Islands community lives in New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The principles of the partnership were set out in a Joint Centenary Declaration signed in 2001 and reaffirmed in a joint statement in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands graduated in 2019 to the category of high-income country, ending their eligibility for official development assistance and making them the first Pacific island country or territory to do so since 2000, and the United States recognised them as a sovereign and independent state on 25 September 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cook Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ck.html. [Open access]","New Zealand Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade, “Cook Islands,” https://www.mfat.govt.nz/en/countries-and-regions/pacific/cook-islands. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cook Islands,” https://history.state.gov/countries/cook-islands. [Open access]","Cook Islands Statistics Office, https://www.stats.gov.ck/. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Aitutaki_from_top_2006.jpg","title":"Aitutaki, the Cook Islands","desc":"Aitutaki.","credit":"Mr Bullitt, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aitutaki_from_top_2006.jpg","alt":"A palm-fringed shore above a pale blue lagoon."}},
{"id":"gw-222","num":222,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nauru","answerText":"Nauru","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">full member 1999</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 September 1999</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nauru"},"facts":[["Capital","None; offices at Yaren"],["Population","12,000 (2025)"],["Region","Micronesia"],["Area","21 km² (8 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","nauru"],"abstract":"<b>Nauru</b> is a republic in Micronesia of 21 square kilometres (8 square miles) — a single raised coral island — with about 12,000 people at 601.3 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has no official capital: government offices stand in the district of Yaren, and the figure the United Nations gives for that capital, 11,300 in 2018, refers by its own footnote to the whole of Nauru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank, which files the republic under its Nauruan name Naoero, has the population rising in every year from 10,954 in 2015 to 12,025 in 2025, and gives the land area as 20 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The currency is the Australian dollar, income per head was 14,812 US dollars in 2023, and industry made up 36.5 per cent of gross value added against 61 for services and 2.4 for agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unemployment stood at 23 per cent at the last measurement the statistics division carries, and gross domestic product was 176 million US dollars in 2023 against 104 million in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nauru passed through several colonial and military occupations before becoming a United Nations trust territory after the Second World War, and the Commonwealth records that it joined on attaining full independence as a former trust territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It achieved independence in 1968 and joined the Commonwealth the same year as an associate member, becoming a full member in 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Nauru on 24 October 1976, when its ambassador to Australia, Marshall Green, presented credentials while remaining resident at Canberra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That accreditation moved from Canberra to Suva on 24 April 1996, when the American ambassador to Fiji, Don L. Gevirtz, presented his credentials, and there has never been an American embassy in Yaren.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The republic joined the United Nations on 14 September 1999, in the same year it took up full Commonwealth membership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Nauru,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nr.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Nauru,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/nauru. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nauru,” https://history.state.gov/countries/nauru. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Naoero,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NRU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Naoero,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NRU/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Buada_Lagoon%2C_Nauru_2007.jpg/1920px-Buada_Lagoon%2C_Nauru_2007.jpg","title":"Buada Lagoon, Nauru","desc":"Buada Lagoon.","credit":"Lorrie Graham/AusAID, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buada_Lagoon,_Nauru_2007.jpg","alt":"A view of Buada Lagoon."}},
{"id":"gw-225","num":225,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tuvalu","answerText":"Tuvalu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 May 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 2000</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tuvalu"},"facts":[["Capital","Funafuti"],["Population","10,000 (2025)"],["Region","Polynesia"],["Area","26 km² (10 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","tuvalu"],"abstract":"<b>Tuvalu</b> is a Polynesian country of 26 square kilometres (10 square miles) by the United Nations statistics division and 25.6 by the Commonwealth Secretariat, with about 10,000 people at 316.4 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth gives its capital as Vaiaku, where the government offices stand, on the islet of Fongafale in Funafuti atoll, while the statistics division names Funafuti and counts 7,000 people there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population is falling: the World Bank’s series runs from 10,963 in 2015 to 9,492 in 2025, where the Commonwealth recorded 11,790 in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The currency is the Australian dollar, income per head was 6,880 US dollars in 2023, and gross domestic product was 68 million against 36 million in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under Polynesia with code 798 and marks it both a least developed country and a small island developing State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were called the Ellice Islands under British sovereignty, as part of the Gilbert and Ellice Islands Colony, and took the name Tuvalu on becoming an independent country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised Tuvalu on 1 October 1978, when Elizabeth G. Stevens, acting as the President’s personal representative, delivered a letter to that effect from President Jimmy Carter to Governor General Penitala Teo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 10 May 1979, when Ambassador Inoke Faletau presented his credentials to President Carter, and Tuvalu joined the Commonwealth in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John P. Condon became the first American ambassador to Tuvalu on 24 January 1980, accredited also to Fiji and Tonga and resident at Suva, and every ambassador since has stayed at Suva with no mission established at Funafuti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tuvalu joined the United Nations on 5 September 2000, 22 years after the United States recognised it, and its people had had only very occasional contact with Americans before the late 1970s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Tuvalu,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tv.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Tuvalu,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/tuvalu. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tuvalu,” https://history.state.gov/countries/tuvalu. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Tuvalu,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TUV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Funafuti_airport_-_Fiji_Airways.jpg","title":"Funafuti, Tuvalu","desc":"Funafuti.","credit":"Michael Coghlan, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funafuti_airport_-_Fiji_Airways.jpg","alt":"A view of Funafuti."}},
{"id":"gw-226","num":226,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Pierre and Miquelon","answerText":"Saint Pierre and Miquelon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Organic law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 February 2007</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Territorial Council</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 members, 5 years</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"St. Pierre and Miquelon"},"facts":[["Capital","Saint-Pierre"],["Population","6,000 (2025)"],["Region","Northern America"],["Area","242 km² (93 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","saint pierre and miquelon"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Pierre and Miquelon</b> is a French overseas collectivity of 242 square kilometres (93 square miles) in the North Atlantic, east of the Canadian coast, made up of several islands and islets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 6,000 people live there at 24.2 to the square kilometre, the capital is Saint-Pierre, the official language is French and the currency is the euro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The highest point is the Morne de la Grande Montagne on Miquelon, at 240 metres (790 feet), and the archipelago runs north-east to south-west along the line of the Appalachians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its climate is cold and humid and dominated by the sea, averaging 5.4 degrees Celsius over the year, 15.5 in August and −3.3 in February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 600 species of vascular plants grow there, some 500 of them native, alongside 177 species of moss, and more than 300 species of bird are recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It became an overseas collectivity under the organic law of 21 February 2007, the same law that reorganised Saint-Barthélemy and Saint-Martin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Local power is exercised by a Territorial Council of 19 members elected for five years by universal suffrage, and by its president, while the French State is represented by a prefect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Taxation and customs fall to the territory, along with the powers conferred elsewhere on French regions and departments, and the law of the French Republic applies directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archipelago is one of the European Union’s overseas countries and territories, listed in Annex II to the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, to which the special arrangements for association in Part Four of that treaty apply rather than the treaties in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fishing, processing and aquaculture remain the principal route of economic diversification, construction depends heavily on public procurement, and the United Nations files the archipelago under Northern America with code 666.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Pierre and Miquelon,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pm.html. [Open access]","Overseas Countries and Territories Association, “Saint-Pierre-and-Miquelon,” https://www.overseas-association.eu/saint-pierre-and-miquelon. [Open access]","Overseas Countries and Territories Association, “OCTs,” https://www.overseas-association.eu/octs. [Open access]","European Union, <i>Consolidated Version of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union</i>, art. 355, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:12016E355. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Sainte-Pierre_aerial.jpg/1920px-Sainte-Pierre_aerial.jpg","title":"Saint-Pierre, Saint Pierre and Miquelon","desc":"The island of Saint-Pierre from the air.","credit":"Doc Searls from Santa Barbara, USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sainte-Pierre_aerial.jpg","alt":"A low bare island with a town on its northern shore."}},
{"id":"gw-227","num":227,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Helena","answerText":"Saint Helena","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1502</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1659</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crown colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Saint Helena"},"facts":[["Capital","Jamestown"],["Population","5,000 (2025)"],["Region","Western Africa"],["Area","309 km² (119 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","africa","saint helena"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Helena</b> is a volcanic island in the South Atlantic, 1,900 kilometres (1,200 miles) from Angola, and the seat of a non-self-governing territory called Saint Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ascension lies 1,200 kilometres (750 miles) to the north-west and Tristan da Cunha 2,400 kilometres (1,500 miles) to the south, the three holding 309 square kilometres (119 square miles) of land in an exclusive economic zone of 1,641,294.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Secretariat counts 4,122 people on Saint Helena in June 2025, about 800 on Ascension and 221 on Tristan da Cunha, each island having its own seat: Jamestown, Georgetown and Edinburgh of the Seven Seas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Saint Helena pound and income per head was £9,210 in 2023/24; Ascension has no permanent population, so no such figure exists for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Legislative Council has 15 seats of which 12 are elected, six are held by women, including the Chief Minister and the Speaker, and there are no political parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The island was reportedly found in 1502 by a Spanish navigator in Portuguese service and was a port of call for ships bound to the East Indies by the end of that century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Netherlands claimed it in 1633 without settling it, and the British East India Company took possession and settled it in 1659, two years after Oliver Cromwell granted a charter to govern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a Crown colony in 1833, a British dependent territory in 1981 and a British overseas territory in 2002, and the General Assembly first noted the information transmitted on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The constitution that came into force in September 2009 changed the territory’s name from Saint Helena and its Dependencies, giving the three island groups equal standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The administering Power calls Tristan da Cunha the most remote inhabited island in the world: no airport, nine scheduled sailings a year, and a voyage from Cape Town of at least seven days; the United Nations files the territory under Western Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Saint Helena: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/13, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111021. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Helena,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sh.html. [Open access]","The St Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha Constitution Order 2009, SI 2009/1751, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/1751/contents/made. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Overlooking_Jamestown_from_the_south.jpg/1920px-Overlooking_Jamestown_from_the_south.jpg","title":"Jamestown, Saint Helena, Saint Helena","desc":"Jamestown, Saint Helena.","credit":"Kevstan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Overlooking_Jamestown_from_the_south.jpg","alt":"A view of Jamestown, Saint Helena."}},
{"id":"gw-228","num":228,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Montserrat","answerText":"Montserrat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1632</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crown colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plymouth abandoned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in force 2011</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Montserrat"},"facts":[["Capital","Brades; Plymouth abandoned 1997"],["Population","4,400 (2025)"],["Region","Caribbean"],["Area","103 km² (40 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","montserrat"],"abstract":"<b>Montserrat</b> is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, in the Leeward Islands of the eastern Caribbean, 43 kilometres (27 miles) south-west of Antigua and 64 kilometres (40 miles) north-west of Guadeloupe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole island is volcanic, and of its 103 square kilometres (40 square miles) only 40.1 are habitable, inside an exclusive economic zone of 7,582 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its capital, Plymouth, was abandoned in 1997 after the volcanic eruption, and most government offices now stand at Brades, which the statistics division records as Brades Estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 4,400 people live there, income per head was 19,261 US dollars in 2024, the public sector is the largest employer, and the currency is the East Caribbean dollar at 2.70 to the United States dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Volcanic activity keeps the most fertile land, pasture and fishing grounds restricted or unreachable, so the island depends largely on imports and its food security is low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Christopher Columbus named the island after a monastery near Barcelona; its Indigenous name is Alliouagana, the Land of the Prickly Bush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first European settlers, mostly Irish, arrived in 1632, and the island later took in indentured Irish servants transported to the British West Indies, before a major slave uprising on Saint Patrick’s Day in 1768.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> France occupied it intermittently during the eighteenth century until it was confirmed as British in 1783, and it became a Crown colony in 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Federation of the West Indies broke up in 1962 Montserrat chose Crown colony status over associated statehood, and the General Assembly had first noted the information transmitted on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under the Montserrat Constitution Order 2010, in force from September 2011, the Governor answers for internal security, external affairs, defence, the public service and the regulation of international financial services, and the United Nations files the island under the Caribbean with code 500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Montserrat: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/10, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111020. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Montserrat,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ms.html. [Open access]","The Montserrat Constitution Order 2010, SI 2010/2474, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2010/2474/contents/made. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Soufriere_Hills.jpg/1920px-Soufriere_Hills.jpg","title":"Soufrière Hills, Montserrat","desc":"Soufrière Hills.","credit":"CommonismNow, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soufriere_Hills.jpg","alt":"A view of Soufrière Hills."}},
{"id":"gw-229","num":229,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Falkland Islands","answerText":"Falkland Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">On the UN list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reservations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GA resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2065 (XX)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Falkland Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Stanley"],["Population","3,662 (2021 census)"],["Region","South America"],["Area","12,173 km² (4,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","falkland islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Falkland Islands</b>, which the United Nations calls the Falkland Islands (Malvinas), are a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom: East Falkland, West Falkland and hundreds of smaller islands, about 12,173 square kilometres (4,700 square miles) in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They lie in the South Atlantic 770 kilometres (480 miles) north-east of Cape Horn and 480 kilometres (300 miles) east of the mainland, at 0.3 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 2021 census, which every household was required to answer except British Forces personnel and their families, counted 3,662 residents: 2,974 in Stanley, 354 in Camp and 334 civilians at the Mount Pleasant Complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Falkland Islands pound, and a British defence force based at Mount Pleasant numbered about 830 personnel on 28 November 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> South Georgia lies 1,300 kilometres (800 miles) to the south-east and the South Sandwich Islands 750 kilometres (470 miles) beyond it, and the Governor is Commissioner for both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands have been on the United Nations list of non-self-governing territories since 1946, after the United Kingdom transmitted information under Article 73 e of the Charter in accordance with General Assembly resolution 66 (I).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the Fourth Committee’s twenty-fifth meeting, on 6 December 1946, Argentina entered a reservation that it did not recognise British sovereignty over the islands, and the United Kingdom a parallel reservation that it did not recognise Argentine sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dispute between the two governments over sovereignty is recognised by the General Assembly in its resolution 2065 (XX) and in the later resolutions on the question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constitution approved in 2008 came into force on 1 January 2009 and has been protested by Argentina; under it the legislature elects its own speaker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Policy is considered by an Executive Council of three members of the Legislative Assembly elected annually from among themselves, and the United Nations files the territory under South America, its Spanish register naming it Islas Malvinas (Falkland).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Falkland Islands (Malvinas): Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/6, 31 March 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111019. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Falkland Islands (Malvinas),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fk.html. [Open access]","The Falkland Islands Constitution Order 2008, SI 2008/2846, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2008/2846/contents/made. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Aerial_photo_Port_Stanley.jpg","title":"Stanley, Falkland Islands, the Falkland Islands","desc":"Stanley, Falkland Islands.","credit":"CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_photo_Port_Stanley.jpg","alt":"A view of Stanley, Falkland Islands."}},
{"id":"gw-231","num":231,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Niue","answerText":"Niue","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Free association</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Partnership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 July 2022</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 September 2023</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 November 2025</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Niue"},"facts":[["Capital","Alofi"],["Population","2,000 (2025)"],["Region","Polynesia"],["Area","260 km² (100 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","niue"],"abstract":"<b>Niue</b> is a self-governing state in free association with New Zealand, a single raised coral island of 260 square kilometres (100 square miles) in the South Pacific with its capital at Alofi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 2,000 people live there, at seven to the square kilometre, and the currency is the New Zealand dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is part of the Realm of New Zealand, so the two share a head of state, the King in Right of New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Niue Constitution Act 1974 New Zealand provides the economic and administrative assistance Niue needs, and help with foreign affairs, defence and security if the Niue government asks for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In return the two consult regularly on matters of mutual concern, and Niue may not enter defence arrangements that would engage New Zealand’s sovereign competencies or act in ways that might undermine the Realm’s vital security interests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The free-association arrangement dates from 1974, and all Niueans are New Zealand citizens under New Zealand law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division files Niue under Polynesia and excludes it from its data for New Zealand, though its Cook Islands profile still describes the island as part of the Cook Islands, administered separately because of remoteness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> New Zealand and Niue signed a Statement of Partnership on 21 July 2022, and at their first Leaders’ Meeting on 13 November 2025 the two prime ministers signed a Political Declaration affirming the free-association relationship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Niue as a sovereign and independent state on 25 September 2023, when Secretary of State Antony Blinken and Premier Dalton Tagelagi signed a joint statement establishing diplomatic relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small-scale agriculture and the processing of organic produce for export — honey, vanilla and noni juice — make up the primary sector, tourism is a large contributor, almost everything else is imported, and the United Nations gives the island the code 570.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Niue,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nu.html. [Open access]","New Zealand Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade, “Niue,” https://www.mfat.govt.nz/en/countries-and-regions/pacific/niue. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Niue,” https://history.state.gov/countries/niue. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Cook Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ck.html. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Avatele_Beach%2C_looking_southwest.jpg/1920px-Avatele_Beach%2C_looking_southwest.jpg","title":"Niue, Niue","desc":"Niue.","credit":"Pseudopanax at English Wikipedia, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Avatele_Beach,_looking_southwest.jpg","alt":"A view of Niue."}},
{"id":"gw-232","num":232,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Vatican City","answerText":"Vatican City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lateran Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">signed 11 February 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Vatican"},"facts":[["Capital","Vatican City"],["Population","882 residents (2024)"],["Region","Southern Europe"],["Area","0.44 km² (0.17 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","vatican city"],"abstract":"<b>Vatican City</b> is a sovereign state of 0.44 square kilometres (0.17 square miles), or 44 hectares, on a hill by the right bank of the Tiber inside Rome, and the United Nations files it as the Holy See under Southern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its territory is partly walled and takes in Saint Peter’s Square as far as the marble strip that joins the ground to the outer parts of the Colonnade, a boundary anyone may cross freely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five entrances are guarded by the Pontifical Swiss Guard and the Gendarmerie Corps, and the currency is the euro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 31 December 2024 the state had 673 citizens, 458 of them living inside the walls including 120 Swiss Guards, and 882 residents in all, citizens and non-citizens together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 30 per cent of its citizens live outside the state, most of them because their work is diplomatic, and 66 cardinals hold its citizenship, ten of whom live inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The hill held a private circus built by the emperor Caligula, and north of it, in a necropolis along a secondary road, lies the burial place of Saint Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Constantine raised a great basilica over that grave between 324 and 326, and the present Saint Peter’s replaced it in the 16th and 17th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state itself was created by the Lateran Treaty between the Holy See and Italy, signed on 11 February 1929 and ratified on 7 June that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its personality as a sovereign entity under international law, separate from the Holy See, is universally recognised, and its structures are deliberately reduced to the minimum its functions require.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the territory is so small, many of the Holy See’s offices stand in buildings elsewhere in Rome which the Lateran Treaty protects with the immunity foreign embassies enjoy, and the United States recognised the Holy See on 10 January 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Governorate of Vatican City State, “Geography,” https://www.vaticanstate.va/en/state-and-government/general-informations/geography.html. [Open access]","Governorate of Vatican City State, “Population,” https://www.vaticanstate.va/en/state-and-government/general-informations/population.html. [Open access]","Governorate of Vatican City State, “Origins and Characteristic,” https://www.vaticanstate.va/en/state-and-government/general-informations/origins-and-characteristic.html. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Holy See,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/va.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Holy See,” https://history.state.gov/countries/holy-see. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Basilica_di_San_Pietro_in_Vaticano_September_2015-1a.jpg/1920px-Basilica_di_San_Pietro_in_Vaticano_September_2015-1a.jpg","title":"St. Peter's Basilica, Vatican City","desc":"St. Peter's Basilica.","credit":"Alvesgaspar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_di_San_Pietro_in_Vaticano_September_2015-1a.jpg","alt":"A view of St. Peter's Basilica."}},
{"id":"gw-233","num":233,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Pitcairn Islands","answerText":"Pitcairn Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sighted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1767, by Robert Pitcairn</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1790, from HMS Bounty</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Order</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 2010</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Pitcairn Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Adamstown"],["Population","35 (2023)"],["Region","Polynesia"],["Area","35.5 km² (13.7 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","pitcairn islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Pitcairn Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and lie midway between New Zealand and South America at 25 degrees south and 130 degrees west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They comprise four islands — Pitcairn, Henderson, Ducie and Oeno — of which only Pitcairn is inhabited, with 35.5 square kilometres (13.7 square miles) of land in all and 4.35 on Pitcairn itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their exclusive economic zone of 842,000 square kilometres is now a marine protected area, and the United Nations files the territory under Polynesia with code 612, having previously listed it as Pitcairn Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Secretariat counted 35 people in 2023 where the territory’s own government says around fifty; the capital and only settlement is Adamstown, above Bounty Bay, the currency is the New Zealand dollar and there are no political parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Incomes rest largely on tourism, the sale of stamps and coins, handicrafts and honey, and the territory receives budgetary aid from the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands are named after Robert Pitcairn, a British midshipman who sighted them in 1767, and Pitcairn was uninhabited when castaways from HMS <i>Bounty</i> — nine mutineers and eighteen Polynesians — arrived in 1790; Pitcairn Island itself runs about 3.2 kilometres (2 miles) by 1.6 kilometres (1 mile).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most islanders today trace their descent from those mutineers and their Tahitian companions, and the official languages are English and Pitkern, a mixture of eighteenth-century English and Tahitian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A new constitution took effect in March 2010, replacing the Pitcairn Order 1970 and the Royal Instructions of the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Governor is appointed by the British Crown, and in practice the United Kingdom’s High Commissioner to New Zealand holds that office concurrently, so responsibility for administering the island rests with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Island Council and the Deputy Mayor are elected every two years in November and the Mayor every three, and the General Assembly first noted the information transmitted on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Pitcairn: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/12, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4102971. [Open access]","The Pitcairn Constitution Order 2010, SI 2010/244, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2010/244/contents/made. [Open access]","The Pitcairn Order 1970, SI 1970/1434, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1970/1434/contents/made. [Open access]","Government of the Pitcairn Islands, https://www.government.pn/. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Adamstown1.jpg","title":"Adamstown, Pitcairn Islands, the Pitcairn Islands","desc":"Adamstown, Pitcairn Islands.","credit":"Makemake at de.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adamstown1.jpg","alt":"A view of Adamstown, Pitcairn Islands."}},
{"id":"gr-351","num":351,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Greek verse written in couplets of a hexameter followed by a shorter pentameter, and carried by the reed pipe rather than the lyre, is called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whatever its subject.","questions":["Callinus of Ephesus called his countrymen to arms and Mimnermus wrote of love, yet both worked in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a kind of verse defined by its metre and not by its subject.","The couplet Simonides used to commemorate the dead became the verse of the Greek epigram in general, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> long outlived the reed pipe that had once accompanied it."],"answer":"elegy","answerText":"elegy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Callinus and Archilochus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Metre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2-line couplet: hexameter, then pentameter</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Elegy</b> in Greek is a matter of metre rather than of mood, since any poem built from couplets that pair a dactylic hexameter with a shorter pentameter counts as one, whatever its subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two words lie behind the name and the Greeks kept them apart, <i>elegos</i> for a song of mourning barely distinguishable from the dirge, and <i>elegeion</i> for the couplet as a form of verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Where the word itself came from is not known and it may not be Greek at all, one suggestion making it a Carian borrowing and another a repeated cry of grief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The couplet enters literature early in the 7th century BCE with Callinus of Ephesus and Archilochus, and it is a variation on the heroic hexameter rather than a form invented apart from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its second line falls into two equal halves that each break off a syllable short, and it was this twice-repeated dying fall that ancient critics heard as fitted to the expression of grief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Roman poets inherited the doctrine that the unequal couplet had carried lament first and fulfilled desire afterwards, along with the admission that nobody could say who had published the first of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archaic poems that survive are in fact of every variety, and the melancholy the modern word carries is not what marked the ancient genre out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Callinus and Tyrtaeus used the metre to call men to arms, Solon to argue politics and defend his own legislation, and Mimnermus to write of love and the shortness of youth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was carried by the <i>aulos</i>, the reed pipe, rather than by the lyre, so the Greeks never counted it as melic verse, and it soon became a measure recited as readily as sung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its ordinary home was the symposium, though a papyrus of Simonides on the dead of Plataea shows it at public occasions too, and from commemorating the dead it became the verse of the epitaph and then of the epigram at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Ross Hardie, <i>Res Metrica: An Introduction to the Study of Greek and Roman Versification</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1920), 49–51, https://archive.org/details/resmetrica0000unse. [Open access]","Horace, <i>Ars Poetica</i> 75–78, trans. C. Smart, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0065:card=73. [Open access]","Krystyna Bartol, “Elegijne nastroje: wczesna elegia grecka i nie tylko,” <i>Poznańskie Studia Polonistyczne. Seria Literacka</i> 18 (2011): 11–22, https://doi.org/10.14746/pspsl:2011.18.2. [Open access]","Herbert Kynaston, <i>Extracts from the Greek Elegiac Poets, from Callinus to Callimachus</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1880), v–viii, https://archive.org/details/extractsfromgre01kynagoog. [Open access]","Cecilia Nobili, “Threnodic Elegy in Sparta,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51, no. 1 (2011): 26–48, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/1631. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 76–81, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Livio Sbardella, “Aulodes and Rhapsodes: Performance and Forms of Greek Elegy from Mimnermus to Hermesianax,” <i>Aitia</i> 8, no. 1 (2018), https://doi.org/10.4000/aitia.2247. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), cxxiv–cxxv, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Aulos_player_Met_24.97.28.jpg/1280px-Aulos_player_Met_24.97.28.jpg","title":"A player of the aulos","desc":"Attic red-figure lekythos attributed to the Brygos Painter, about 480 BCE, showing a girl playing the double reed pipe that accompanied elegy. Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, 24.97.28. Photograph by Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aulos_player_Met_24.97.28.jpg","alt":"An orange figure on a black Greek oil flask: a woman in a long pleated robe stands playing a double reed pipe with both hands, a spotted pipe-case hanging behind her and a couch at her side."}},
{"id":"gr-352","num":352,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Blame was the business of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the archaic Greek genre that took its name from the metre in which, Aristotle says, its poets lampooned one another.","questions":["Archilochus rounded on Lycambes and Hipponax on the artist Bupalus, each of them naming his enemy outright, which is what the archaic Greeks expected of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a form.","A servant called Iambe made the grieving Demeter laugh with jokes at once obscene and aggressive, and Greek tradition made her the inventor of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which kept that laughter."],"answer":"iambus","answerText":"iambus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Archilochus of Paros</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 poets, Archilochus to Hipponax</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Staple line</span><span class=\"dt-v\">iambic trimeter, 6 feet in 3 pairs</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Iambus</b> is the third of the great forms of archaic Greek verse beside epic and melic song, and what defines it is not its metre but its business, which is blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its poets proclaim <i>psogos</i> against people who break the rules a community lives by, so the genre acts as a censor inside a society that thinks in terms of praise and blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word itself is not Greek and its origin is unknown, though it belongs with <i>dithyrambos</i> and <i>thriambos</i>, names of songs tied to the worship of Dionysus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek tradition instead derived it from Iambe, the servant at Eleusis who in the <i>Homeric Hymn to Demeter</i> alone broke the grieving goddess out of her silence with jokes both obscene and aggressive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle gave a different account, holding that the metre kept the name because it was the one in which the early poets had lampooned each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Iambic verse has almost no early history and appears suddenly in the middle of the 7th century BCE, already finished and exact, in the hands of Archilochus of Paros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is Ionian, it had a place in the popular festivals of Demeter and Iakchos, and the same ritual roots produced the performances that became tragedy and comedy at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexandrian scholars fixed a canon of three, Archilochus, Semonides of Amorgos and Hipponax of Ephesus, and each is remembered with a named enemy, Lycambes for the first and the artist Bupalus for the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The staple line is the trimeter, six iambic feet counted in three pairs, which Aristotle called the most conversational of all metres because ordinary talk keeps falling into it, and Hipponax slowed it by lengthening the last foot but one into the limping <i>scazon</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Horace has rage arm Archilochus with the iambus, and comedy and tragedy then took the measure over for dialogue, so one poet's weapon of abuse became the working line of the Athenian stage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Antonio Aloni, “La performance giambica nella Grecia arcaica,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 1, no. 1 (2006): 83–107, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/79. [Open access]","William Ross Hardie, <i>Res Metrica: An Introduction to the Study of Greek and Roman Versification</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1920), 68–70, 98–99, https://archive.org/details/resmetrica0000unse. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1448b, trans. W. H. Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1448b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1449a, trans. W. H. Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1449a. [Open access]","Horace, <i>Ars Poetica</i> 79–82, trans. C. Smart, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0065:card=73. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 85–88, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Revellers_BM_VaseE508.jpg/1280px-Revellers_BM_VaseE508.jpg","title":"Revellers with a barbitos","desc":"Detail from an Attic red-figure bell-krater of 450 to 440 BCE, said to be from Aegina, showing three revellers on a komos, one of them carrying a barbitos. The drinking party and the revel are the setting most iambic verse was performed in. British Museum, London, 1893,0712.13. Photograph by Marie-Lan Nguyen, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Revellers_BM_VaseE508.jpg","alt":"Three orange figures on a black Greek mixing bowl stride along in headbands and loose cloaks, the middle one carrying a tall lyre with two long curving arms."}},
{"id":"gr-353","num":353,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"One famous fragment shrugs off a shield abandoned in a bush to a Thracian Saian, a piece of soldierly candour that made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the byword for the anti-heroic.","questions":["Paros kept an open-air shrine with altars where a poet was honoured beside the gods, founded with Delphi's blessing, and the poet worshipped there was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, dead some centuries before.","Lycambes broke off his daughter's betrothal and was pursued in verse for the rest of the poet's life, which is the best-known story told about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Paros."],"answer":"Archilochus","answerText":"Archilochus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Paros, then Thasos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of the 3 classical iambic poets</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Archilochus</b> of Paros is the first Greek poet who speaks in his own voice about his own life, and antiquity ranked him beside Homer and Hesiod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He belongs to the middle of the 7th century BCE, and iambic verse has almost no history before him, appearing suddenly in his hands already finished and exact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The life the ancients told is a soldier's: a son of Telesicles who left Paros for the colony on Thasos, served as a mercenary and was killed in a battle against Naxos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His most quoted lines shrug at a shield he left in a bush for a Thracian Saian to boast over, since he had saved himself and could get another no worse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That refusal of the heroic pose is why he was read as Homer's opposite, though a fragment on Telephus, where flight is forced by a god, suggests the theme is more ambivalent than plain defiance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The quarrel that made his name was with Lycambes, who promised him his daughter Neobule and then broke off the match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A papyrus at Cologne, published in 1974, gave back his first nearly complete poem, an epode of 35 lines in which he and a younger sister of Neobule talk their way to a seduction, though whether that is love poetry or revenge on the family is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later tradition said his verses drove Lycambes and his daughters to hang themselves, but much of that life is drawn from the poems, where the speaking voice may be a mask rather than the man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Paros went on to make him a hero: a citizen named Mnesiepes, with Delphi's approval, laid out an open-air precinct called the Archilocheion, with altars at which the gods and Archilochus were sacrificed to together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its inscription tells how the boy, driving a cow to market by moonlight, traded jokes with a band of women, then found the cow gone and a lyre at his feet, and understood they were the Muses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Rafael de Carvalho Matiello Brunhara and Thiago Koslowsky da Rosa, “O fragmento de Télefo e a fuga em Arquíloco,” <i>Cadernos de Letras da UFF</i> 28, no. 56 (2018): 49–63, https://doi.org/10.22409/cadletrasuff.2018n56a544. [Open access]","William Ross Hardie, <i>Res Metrica: An Introduction to the Study of Greek and Roman Versification</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1920), 69, https://archive.org/details/resmetrica0000unse. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 86–88, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Miroslav Marcovich, “A New Poem of Archilochus: <i>P.Colon.</i> inv. 7511,” <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 16, no. 1 (1975): 5–14, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/8631. [Open access]","Matteo Rivoli, “L'iscrizione di Mnesiepes dall'Archilocheion di Paro,” <i>Axon</i> 4, no. 1 (2020): 141–64, https://doi.org/10.30687/Axon/2532-6848/2020/01/008. [Open access]","Antonio Aloni, “La performance giambica nella Grecia arcaica,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 1, no. 1 (2006): 83–107, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/79. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Archilochos_fragment_PK7511r.jpg","title":"The Cologne papyrus of Archilochus","desc":"Papyrus Cologne inv. 7511, recto, of the 1st or 2nd century CE, carrying the epode that gave back the first nearly complete poem of Archilochus. Papyrussammlung Köln. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archilochos_fragment_PK7511r.jpg","alt":"A tall strip of golden-brown papyrus, ragged at top and bottom, closely written with about forty lines of Greek in unspaced capital letters."}},
{"id":"gr-354","num":354,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Antiquity called her simply the Poetess, as Homer was the Poet, and an epigram ascribed to Plato made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a tenth Muse beside the nine.","questions":["Of the nine books Alexandria edited, almost nothing survives whole, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read in quotations and torn papyri, with a single complete hymn to Aphrodite among them.","Mytilene struck her face on its coins, and Herodotus knew poems in which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> attacked her brother Charaxus over his affair with a courtesan in Egypt."],"answer":"Sappho","answerText":"Sappho","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Eresos and Mytilene, Lesbos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 books at Alexandria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Sappho</b> of Lesbos is the most famous woman of the ancient Greek world and was reckoned by universal consent in antiquity its greatest poet of love.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was born at Eresos to a noble family and lived at Mytilene, on an island the Greeks reckoned the most musical they knew, whose lyric flowered at the very moment its politics tore it apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Her dialect is the Aeolic actually spoken on Lesbos rather than a literary one, and her verse technique is of extraordinary variety, using perhaps more metres than any other Greek poet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of them, the four-line stanza built on the hendecasyllable that Alcaeus used as well, has carried her name in European verse ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexandria edited her into nine books arranged probably by metre, and she was called simply the Poetess, as Homer was the Poet, while an epigram ascribed to Plato made her a tenth Muse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Almost none of it survives whole, and she is read in quotations by later critics and in torn papyri, with a single complete poem, a hymn asking Aphrodite for help in love.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The songs are addressed to a circle of women, and what that circle was, a school, a cult group or a company of friends, is not settled by anything the fragments themselves say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus already knew poems in which she attacked her brother Charaxus over his affair with a courtesan in Egypt, and a papyrus published this century has given back a poem that names him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her own city struck her face on its coins, Horace set her in the underworld beside Alcaeus with the shades listening in silence, and a line of women poets followed her across Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later antiquity could not place a woman who was neither a courtesan nor silent, so comedy made her one, and the tale of her leap from the Leucadian cliff for love of the ferryman Phaon belongs to that invention rather than to her life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 226–31, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Anni Arukask, “The Metrical Structure of the Sapphic Hendecasyllable and Sappho's Aiolikon in Lesbian Poetry,” <i>Studia Metrica et Poetica</i> 7, no. 1 (2020): 97–113, https://doi.org/10.12697/smp.2020.7.1.04. [Open access]","Giulia Donelli, “Herodotus, the Old Sappho and the Newest Sappho,” <i>Lexis</i> 39 (2021): 13–34, https://doi.org/10.30687/Lexis/2724-1564/2021/01/001. [Open access]","Horace, <i>Odes</i> 2.13, trans. John Conington, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0025:book=2:poem=13. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 90–93, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Athina Dimopoulou, “La violence politique à Lesbos à l'époque d'Alcée et Pittacos,” <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 45, no. 1 (2019): 23–36, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_2019_num_45_1_4695. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Sappho_coin_British_Museum.jpg/960px-Sappho_coin_British_Museum.jpg","title":"Sappho on a coin of Mytilene","desc":"Copper-alloy coin of Mytilene on Lesbos, 2nd century CE, carrying a head of Sappho and the local form of her name, PSAPPHO. British Museum, London, BNK,G.510. Photograph by Caeciliusinhorto, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sappho_coin_British_Museum.jpg","alt":"A small, heavily worn bronze coin held in a museum mount, showing a woman’s head in profile facing right with her hair bound up, and Greek letters around the edge."}},
{"id":"gr-355","num":355,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"The aristocratic songs of faction that Mytilene's symposia sang, against Melanchros and Myrsilos and finally against Pittacus himself, are the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, our one witness from inside.","questions":["Like Archilochus before him, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost his shield to the enemy at Sigeum and sang about it, telling the herald that the Athenians had hung it up in Athena's temple.","Horace heard him in the underworld chanting of shipwreck, exile and war, and the shades pressed closer when <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sang of battle and tyrants driven out."],"answer":"Alcaeus","answerText":"Alcaeus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Mytilene, Lesbos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Exile</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 years, under Pittacus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 books at Alexandria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Alcaeus</b> of Mytilene is the poet of a city at war with itself, and the one direct witness to the faction fighting that convulsed Lesbos around 600 BCE, since he was among the men doing the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He belongs with Sappho to the Aeolic tradition of the island, and the two are named together constantly, working in the same dialect and sharing the hendecasyllabic line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His verse was sung at the symposia of the <i>hetairiai</i>, the political clubs of aristocrats bound by blood and by common interest, which is where his songs of faction found their audience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For eight years he and the other nobles fought the tyrant Melanchros, and when Melanchros was killed they got Myrsilos in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Lesbians at last handed power to Pittacus, later counted among the Seven Sages, to end the fighting; Alcaeus called him a tyrant and spent 15 years in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He wandered in Thrace and sailed as far as Egypt, while his brother Antimenidas took service with the Babylonians, and in the end Pittacus pardoned him and he came home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Like Archilochus he lost his shield, at Sigeum against the Athenians, and sang about it himself, telling the herald that the enemy had hung it up in the temple of Athena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alexandria edited him into ten books, all of it occasional verse: hymns, drinking songs, love songs, and above all the <i>stasiotika</i>, the songs of faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Horace, who made the stanza named after him his own, heard him in the underworld chanting of shipwreck, exile and war, with the silent dead pressing closer for the songs of battle and tyrants driven out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The paradox of the island is that its violence peaked exactly when its lyric did, on what the Greeks reckoned the most musical ground they knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Athina Dimopoulou, “La violence politique à Lesbos à l'époque d'Alcée et Pittacos,” <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 45, no. 1 (2019): 23–36, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_2019_num_45_1_4695. [Open access]","Anni Arukask, “The Metrical Structure of the Sapphic Hendecasyllable and Sappho's Aiolikon in Lesbian Poetry,” <i>Studia Metrica et Poetica</i> 7, no. 1 (2020): 97–113, https://doi.org/10.12697/smp.2020.7.1.04. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 210–11, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 91–92, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Horace, <i>Odes</i> 2.13, trans. John Conington, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0025:book=2:poem=13. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Alkaios_Sappho_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2416_full.jpg/1280px-Alkaios_Sappho_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2416_full.jpg","title":"Alcaeus and Sappho","desc":"Side A of an Attic red-figure kalathos attributed to the Brygos Painter, about 470 BCE, found at Akragas in Sicily: Alcaeus with a barbitos on the left and Sappho on the right, both named in the painted inscriptions. Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich, 2416. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alkaios_Sappho_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2416_full.jpg","alt":"A tall black Greek vase in a museum case, painted in orange with a bearded man in a long robe holding a lyre with two long curving arms and, facing him, a woman in a pleated robe carrying another."}},
{"id":"gr-356","num":356,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Herodotus counted five Aeolian cities dividing one island, a sixth having been enslaved by Methymna, and that island, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was reckoned the most musical ground in Greece.","questions":["Terpander of Antissa, Arion of Methymna, Sappho of Eresos and Alcaeus of Mytilene were all born on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, within a century or so of one another.","The stanza forms named after Sappho and Alcaeus are called Aeolic after the dialect spoken on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where both of them were made."],"answer":"Lesbos","answerText":"Lesbos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Aeolic song</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Terpander, Arion, Sappho, Alcaeus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, once 6 before Arisba fell</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"abstract":"<b>Lesbos</b> is the large island off the coast of Asia Minor that produced more of the earliest Greek song than anywhere else, and the Greeks reckoned it the most musical ground they knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus counted five Aeolian cities dividing it between them, a sixth, Arisba, having been enslaved by its own kinsmen at Methymna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sappho and Alcaeus stand for the Aeolic tradition of Greek poetry, which is the island's own, and the metres they used are still classified as Aeolic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first name in that tradition is Terpander of Antissa, credited with giving the lyre seven strings instead of four and with the first musical victory at the Spartan Carneia, a distinction early Greek tradition hands out freely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He shaped the kitharodic nome, the solo sung to the lyre, into a piece of seven marked parts, and his supremacy in that form was said to have lasted two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Arion of Methymna followed him, a lyre-player second to none in his age, whom Herodotus names as the first man known to compose a dithyramb, give it that name and teach it at Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story that a dolphin carried him ashore at Taenarus after pirates put him overboard was told by Corinthians and Lesbians alike, and it is the scene in which he puts on his singing robes to die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then came Sappho of Eresos and Alcaeus of Mytilene, whose work is monody rather than choral song, sung by one voice and built in short stanzas that follow one another unbroken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their shared hendecasyllabic line and the four-line stanzas named after each of them carried Aeolic verse into Latin and, through Horace, into modern European poetry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The paradox of the place is that all this came at the moment its politics tore it apart, and the generation that made the songs also fought the faction wars Alcaeus sang about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.151, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=151. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.23, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=23. [Open access]","Athina Dimopoulou, “La violence politique à Lesbos à l'époque d'Alcée et Pittacos,” <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 45, no. 1 (2019): 23–36, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_2019_num_45_1_4695. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 77–78, 90–92, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), lx–lxii, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Anni Arukask, “The Metrical Structure of the Sapphic Hendecasyllable and Sappho's Aiolikon in Lesbian Poetry,” <i>Studia Metrica et Poetica</i> 7, no. 1 (2020): 97–113, https://doi.org/10.12697/smp.2020.7.1.04. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","geography","greece","archaic greece","literature","poetry"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Lesbos_1211x897.jpg/1280px-Lesbos_1211x897.jpg","title":"Lesbos from orbit","desc":"Landsat image of Lesbos, 2000, in false colour: the Gulf of Kalloni cuts deep into the middle of the island from the south and the Gulf of Gera into its south-eastern corner, with the coast of Asia Minor at the top right. NASA, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lesbos_1211x897.jpg","alt":"A satellite view of a green and pink island in a near-black sea, with one long inlet biting deep into its centre from the south and a smaller one into its south-eastern corner, and a mainland coast at the top right."}},
{"id":"gr-357","num":357,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Ionian elegy turned from war to the shortness of youth in the hands of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose long poem was named after Nanno, a player of the reed pipe.","questions":["Propertius rated the love poetry of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> above Homer’s, and the Alexandrians took from him the plan of a book of elegies named for a beloved.","Men are like the leaves that the season brings out, says a fragment of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> borrowed straight from the Iliad, and youth is enjoyed for a cubit's length of time."],"answer":"Mimnermus","answerText":"Mimnermus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Colophon or Smyrna, in Ionia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nanno</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 long elegy, or a book of them</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Mimnermus</b> is the elegist who turned the metre away from the battlefield towards pleasure and its shortness, and he gave elegy the pensive erotic tone it kept afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He flourished in the second half of the 7th century BCE, and the ancient notices make him a man of Colophon or of Smyrna, the city Colophon had settled and Lydia by then ruled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His best-known work is the <i>Nanno</i>, a long poem or a gathering of poems named after a woman who played the reed pipe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was remembered as a singer rather than a reciter, nicknamed the clear-toned one, and his elegy was performed to that pipe rather than spoken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The note struck again and again is that youth is short and old age hateful, and Horace summed the creed up as the view that nothing is pleasant without love and jesting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One fragment takes Homer's image of men as the leaves a spring brings out, and adds that we enjoy the flower of youth for a cubit's length of time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another follows the Sun through his endless labour, sleeping in a hollow winged bed of gold that carries him back over the sea from west to east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war poems are there as well, richer than those of Tyrtaeus or Callinus, one of them telling how Smyrna was taken, and he brought the settlers of both his cities straight from Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The plan of the <i>Nanno</i>, a book of elegies gathered under a beloved's name, became a formative idea for the elegists of Alexandria and for their Roman imitators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Propertius wrote that his verse was worth more in love than Homer's, which is close to the highest thing a Roman elegist could say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Kynaston, <i>Extracts from the Greek Elegiac Poets, from Callinus to Callimachus</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1880), viii, 8–9, 61–62, https://archive.org/details/extractsfromgre01kynagoog. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 81, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Livio Sbardella, “Aulodes and Rhapsodes: Performance and Forms of Greek Elegy from Mimnermus to Hermesianax,” <i>Aitia</i> 8, no. 1 (2018), https://doi.org/10.4000/aitia.2247. [Open access]","G. L. Huxley, “Mimnermus and Pylos,” <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 2, no. 2 (1959): 109–10, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12441. [Open access]","William Ross Hardie, <i>Res Metrica: An Introduction to the Study of Greek and Roman Versification</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1920), 50, https://archive.org/details/resmetrica0000unse. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"]},
{"id":"gr-358","num":358,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Nearly 1,400 lines survive under one name because the collection was used as a schoolbook, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by far the best preserved of the Greek elegists.","questions":["Cyrnus is named again and again through the poems as a seal set on them, so that a later reader could tell what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote from what anyone else did.","Megara’s old families lost to men the poet called base, and one of the best-known couplets of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> says the city is still a city but its people are different."],"answer":"Theognis","answerText":"Theognis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 520 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Megara</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Corpus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1,400 elegiac lines</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Theognis</b> of Megara is by far the best preserved of the Greek elegists, and the roughly 1,400 lines that come down under his name are the largest body of archaic elegy there is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They survive because the collection was put together as a schoolbook in the 3rd century BCE, and they show the interpolation a schoolbook attracts, with passages of Solon, Mimnermus, Tyrtaeus and Euenus grown into the text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Editors therefore use the name for the poems the manuscripts attribute to him rather than as a claim that one Megarian poet wrote them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost all of it is addressed to Cyrnus, son of Polypaus, a younger man whom the speaker instructs in how a noble ought to live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The repetition of that name is deliberate: it is called a seal set on the verses, so that a theft will be noticed and nobody can pass them off as his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The politics are those of a beaten aristocrat in a city famous for its factions, who calls the men now in charge base and himself and his friends noble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cyrnus, this city is still a city, but the citizens are different, runs one elegy, which has those citizens wearing goatskins and grazing outside the walls a generation before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Good and bad in these poems are political words before they are moral ones, marking the old families off from the new men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was driven into exile and poverty, and some of the poems are the exile waiting, telling himself to drink when his enemies drink and let nobody count his wounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon and Plato both quote his couplet on learning good from the good, and it is as a store of such maxims that the collection was assembled at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 83–84, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Sara De Martin, “Criticising Change: From Theognis to Plato,” <i>Lexis</i> 40 (2022): 387–414, https://doi.org/10.30687/Lexis/2724-1564/2022/02/006. [Open access]","Herbert Kynaston, <i>Extracts from the Greek Elegiac Poets, from Callinus to Callimachus</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1880), 68–69, https://archive.org/details/extractsfromgre01kynagoog. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Meno</i> 95d, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0178:text=Meno:section=95d. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Memorabilia</i> 1.2.20, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0208:book=1:chapter=2:section=20. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/POxy.v0023.n2380.a.01_Theognis.jpg","title":"A papyrus of Theognis","desc":"Oxyrhynchus Papyrus 2380, one of only two papyrus fragments of Theognis, carrying lines 254 to 278 and copied in the 2nd or 3rd century CE. Sackler Library, Oxford. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POxy.v0023.n2380.a.01_Theognis.jpg","alt":"Two ragged brown fragments of papyrus, photographed beside a centimetre scale, carrying lines of Greek written in capital letters with holes and losses across them."}},
{"id":"gr-359","num":359,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Driven out of Teos when the Persians came, and later the ornament of Polycrates' court and then of Athens, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sang of wine and desire at one court after another.","questions":["Herodotus has Polycrates lying in the men's quarters with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Teos beside him, and refusing to turn or answer a herald from the Persian governor.","A book of later imitations printed in 1554 gave Europe the image of a genial old toper, which has all but replaced the real <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a court poet with a sharp tongue."],"answer":"Anacreon","answerText":"Anacreon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 545 – 510 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Teos, Abdera, Samos, Athens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 books at Alexandria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Anacreon</b> of Teos is the Greek poet of wine, desire and the drinking party, and he spent his working life at the courts of tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He left Teos with his fellow citizens in 545 BCE, when the Persian general Harpagus threatened Ionia, and helped to found the colony of Abdera in Thrace, where he seems to have lost his shield in battle as Archilochus and Alcaeus had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He appears next on Samos, at the court Polycrates had made famous for its magnificence, and Herodotus puts him beside the tyrant in the men's quarters when a Persian herald came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After Polycrates was murdered he took an invitation from Hipparchus to Athens, where he knew Xanthippus the father of Pericles, and when Hipparchus in turn was assassinated he seems to have gone on to the princes of Thessaly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexandria divided him into five books of songs, iambics, elegies and epigrams, and Strabo said his poetry was full of Polycrates, though the tyrant's name is in none of the surviving fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Asked why his songs were not addressed to the gods, he is said to have answered that the boys of the court were his gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The popular picture of a tipsy and amorous old man leaves out his talent for satire, and his attack on Artemon is as effective a weapon as anything Archilochus threw at Lycambes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Teos put his face on its coins, and Pausanias saw his statue on the Athenian Acropolis standing beside that of Xanthippus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Anacreontea</i>, a body of imitations written across many centuries and first printed in 1554, took such hold of European taste that the graceful old toper has largely displaced the poet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His dates come from ancient chronographers rather than from records, and the synchronisms that fix him, Ibycus and Polycrates together were built long afterwards out of unequal grids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 281–82, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 283–85, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 280–81, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 94, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Massimiliano Ornaghi, “I Policrati ibicei. Ibico, Anacreonte, Policrate e la cronografia dei poeti della ‘corte' di Samo,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 3, no. 1 (2008): 1–72, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/143. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.121, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=121. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Anacreon_monte_calvo.jpg/1280px-Anacreon_monte_calvo.jpg","title":"The Monte Calvo Anacreon","desc":"Marble statue of a bearded singer from a Roman villa at Monte Calvo near Rieti, carved in the 2nd century CE after a Greek original of the 5th century BCE and identified by modern scholarship as Anacreon. Ny Carlsberg Glyptotek, Copenhagen, IN 491. Photograph by ChrisO, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anacreon_monte_calvo.jpg","alt":"A life-size white marble statue of a bearded man standing naked in a museum gallery, a cloak slung over both shoulders and both arms broken away, with a tree-trunk support at his side."}},
{"id":"gr-360","num":360,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Born at Rhegion where Ionians and Dorians had settled together, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went east to Samos and became, with Anacreon, one of the first poets to work for a tyrant.","questions":["A flock of cranes was said to have witnessed the murder and betrayed the killers, which is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is remembered by people who have never read a line of him.","The early poems follow Stesichorus into heroic legend and the later ones turn to roses, spring and desire, which is the two-part career of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Rhegion."],"answer":"Ibycus","answerText":"Ibycus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 560 – 525 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Rhegion, then Samos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of the 9 lyric poets</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Ibycus</b> was a Greek lyric poet of the 6th century BCE, born at Rhegion in southern Italy and counted among the nine whom Alexandria fixed as the canon of melic verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rhegion had been founded by Chalcidians from Ionian Euboea together with Dorian fugitives from Messene, so both dialects were spoken there and it is not known which stock he came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He led a wandering life like the rhapsodes until he settled on Samos, at the court of Polycrates, where Anacreon was with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two of them are the first poets known to have put their muse at a tyrant's disposal, and how much independence either kept cannot now be recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His work falls into two periods: before Samos he followed Stesichorus into heroic legend, telling of the Argonauts, of Herakles and of the men who fought at Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Samos the manner changes, and what survives is full of roses, spring, strange birds and the bright sleepless dawn that wakes the nightingales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plato has Parmenides quote him on old age: an old race-horse trembling as he is led out to run again, which is how the poet says desire takes him against his will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His songs for boys' choruses were especially praised, and he was said to have brought an Aeolo-Ionic spirit into verse whose language and music were Dorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is best known, though, for a story: that robbers killed him on a lonely shore and he called on a passing flock of cranes to avenge him, which they did when one of the murderers pointed them out in a crowd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That tale is first told more than four centuries after his death and no earlier poet mentions it, and even his dates are chronographers' work, built long afterwards by synchronising him with Polycrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 268–71, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 105, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Massimiliano Ornaghi, “I Policrati ibicei. Ibico, Anacreonte, Policrate e la cronografia dei poeti della ‘corte' di Samo,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 3, no. 1 (2008): 1–72, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/143. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xx, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Parmenides</i> 137a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Parm.:section=137a. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Heinrich_Schwemminger_-_Die_Kraniche_des_Ibykus_-_3739_-_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg/1280px-Heinrich_Schwemminger_-_Die_Kraniche_des_Ibykus_-_3739_-_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg","title":"The cranes of Ibycus","desc":"Heinrich Schwemminger, The Cranes of Ibycus, a 19th-century painting of Schiller’s ballad on the legend: the poet dying on the shore with his lyre, calling to the birds overhead while his killers stand over him. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_Schwemminger_-_Die_Kraniche_des_Ibykus_-_3739_-_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg","alt":"A dark 19th-century painting: a fair-haired man in a red robe lies on the ground clutching a lyre and raising one arm towards birds in the sky, while two half-naked men with knives stand over him."}},
{"id":"gr-361","num":361,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Struck blind for slandering Helen, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote a recantation saying she never sailed and never saw Troy, and the story goes that his sight came back.","questions":["Ancient critics called him the lyric Homer and complained that he was long, since the Oresteia of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone ran to two books and his whole work to 26.","Not to know three lines of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a Greek way of calling someone illiterate, which says how thoroughly the choral poet of Himera had been read."],"answer":"Stesichorus","answerText":"Stesichorus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Matauros, Himera, Catana</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 books at Alexandria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Stesichorus</b> is the great figure of early Greek choral poetry, and the name is a title rather than a name, since it means chorus-setter and the man himself was called Tisias.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came from Matauros in the west of Italy and became a citizen of Himera in Sicily during the long struggle against the tyrant Phalaris, retiring to Catana when Phalaris won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he did for Greek literature was threefold: he brought the epic saga west, he invented the stately narrative manner of lyric, and he made the heroes rather than the gods the chief theme of choral song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Antiquity called him the lyric Homer and said he bore the weight of the epic on his lyre, and the metres named after him are built out of halves of the epic hexameter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scale was epic as well: the <i>Oresteia</i> alone filled two books and his collected work at Alexandria filled 26, which is why the critics who praised him also complained that he was long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The one personal story told of him is the Palinode, which Plato reports: struck blind for what he had said of Helen, he wrote that the tale was untrue, that she never boarded the ships nor came to the towers of Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The recantation is an argument as much as an act of piety: it was hard to believe that Troy had let itself be destroyed rather than give Helen up, and easier to conclude that the Trojans never had her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His reach can be measured by a proverb: not to know three lines of him was a Greek way of saying that a man could not read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost nothing survives whole, partly because he was too plain to need a grammarian's notes, but a papyrus found at Lille gave back a long stretch of his Theban story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even his dates are unsteady, the ancient chronographers having fixed them by tying him to other poets rather than to any record of his own life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 101–3, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedrus</i> 243a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Phaedrus:section=243a. [Open access]","Massimiliano Ornaghi, “Un poeta senza età. Note di cronografia stesicorea e alcmanico-saffico-simonidea,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 9, no. 2 (2014): 1–46, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/1071. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xxx–xxxi, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Antonietta Gostoli, “Some Aspects of the Theban Myth in the Lille Stesichorus,” <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 19, no. 1 (1978): 23–27, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/7551. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/POxy_XXXII_2617.jpg/1280px-POxy_XXXII_2617.jpg","title":"P.Oxy. XXXII 2617, a papyrus of the Geryoneis","desc":"Fragments of a papyrus roll from Oxyrhynchus in Egypt, mounted together, carrying part of the <i>Geryoneis</i> — the poem in which Heracles kills the three-bodied herdsman Geryon and takes his cattle.","credit":"Oxyrhynchus Papyri database, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POxy_XXXII_2617.jpg","alt":"Dozens of small brown papyrus fragments mounted in rows on a pale board, each carrying a few lines of Greek capital letters, with a measuring scale along the bottom edge."}},
{"id":"gr-362","num":362,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Herodotus says the epitaph of the seer Megistias at Thermopylae was paid for by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Ceos, and tradition gives him all three of the inscriptions there.","questions":["Charged with avarice because he wrote for a fixed price rather than for vague patronage, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came nearer than any earlier Greek poet to selling his work on the open market.","Greece turned to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than to Pindar to write the record of its Persian War dead, and at 80 he could still record a 56th victory with a chorus."],"answer":"Simonides","answerText":"Simonides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 556 – 468 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Iulis on Ceos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at Syracuse, aged 89</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Simonides</b> of Ceos was the first Greek to make a profession of poetry, and the first whom Greece asked to write the record of a war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was born at Iulis on Ceos about 556 BCE, was called to Athens by Hipparchus, and after the Peisistratids fell he lived with the Scopadae at Crannon and the Aleuadae at Larissa in Thessaly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When those princes went over to Persia he returned to Athens, and there beat Aeschylus in a competition for an elegy on Marathon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For years afterwards he commemorated the men and the cities that had fought at Artemisium, Salamis and Plataea, and Herodotus names him as the author of an epitaph at Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The best known of those lines is the one for the Spartan dead: stranger, go tell the Lacedaemonians that we lie here obedient to their commands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He was charged with avarice, but what he had actually done was to write for a fixed price rather than for unspecified patronage, which was the nearest thing then possible to selling work on the open market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is credited with introducing the epinikion, the artistic ode for a victory at the games, and he worked in nearly every other kind as well, from dithyrambs and dirges to epigrams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A tradition also made him the inventor of a system of memory and the man who brought four Ionic letters into the Athenian alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At 80 he could record a 56th victory won with a chorus, and he spent his last years at the court of Hiero in Syracuse, dying there at 89.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A papyrus published in the 20th century added a long elegy on the dead of Plataea to what survived of him, which had been little more than quoted epigrams and the fragment on Danae adrift in her chest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 105–8, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 302–3, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Cecilia Nobili, “Threnodic Elegy in Sparta,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51, no. 1 (2011): 26–48, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/1631. [Open access]","Massimiliano Ornaghi, “Un poeta senza età. Note di cronografia stesicorea e alcmanico-saffico-simonidea,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 9, no. 2 (2014): 1–46, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/1071. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.228, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=228. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/InscriptieTherm.jpg","title":"The epitaph for the Spartan dead at Thermopylae","desc":"A modern slab at Thermopylae cut with the couplet ancient tradition gives to Simonides: stranger, announce to the Lacedaemonians that here we lie, obedient to their commands.","credit":"Photograph by Napoleon Vier and Sofie Debognies, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:InscriptieTherm.jpg","alt":"A reddish stone slab set flat in a cobbled pavement, cut with four lines of widely spaced Greek capital letters."}},
{"id":"gr-363","num":363,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"After the tables were cleared and a libation poured, the men of the house reclined for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, at which wine was mixed with water and the songs went round the couches.","questions":["Athenian mixing bowls, jugs, drinking cups and wine-coolers all take their shapes from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the scenes painted on them are largely of the parties they were used at.","Both Plato and Xenophon wrote a book named after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Greeks took from the East in the 7th century BCE when the couch replaced the chair at dinner."],"answer":"symposium","answerText":"symposium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">from the East, in place of the chair</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Libations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, then the paean</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>symposium</b> was the drinking party that followed dinner in a Greek house, a formal occasion with its own order of business rather than a night of casual drinking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not the banquet Homer describes, which is eaten sitting on chairs: the couched version was innovated in the East and reached the Greeks in the later 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Guests were as many as there were couches, so the party stayed small, and one city could hold several at once, each admitting one group under strict rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon gives the opening: the tables were carried out, the company poured a libation and sang a hymn, and only then did the drinking begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three libations were poured, to Zeus and Hera, to the Heroes and to Zeus the Saviour, and the paean sung after the third introduced everything that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Wine was drunk mixed with water and the proportion was argued over: one of water to two of wine was a toper's mixture, Anacreon urged ten to five, and half and half was thought to bring on madness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How hard a company drank was its own decision, and in Plato's <i>Symposium</i> the guests agree that each will drink as he pleases, then send the flute-girl away so they can talk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later that evening Alcibiades appoints himself president of the drinking, demands the largest cup in the house and calls for the wine-cooler, which is the other way an evening was governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of what followed went round the couches in order, a branch of myrtle passing from singer to singer in place of the lyre, and the games included kottabos, in which players flicked their wine lees at a target.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Athenian fine pottery takes its shapes from this room, and the comic poet Epicharmus set out where an evening could lead: a feast, a drinking-party, a revel, a swinery, a summons, a condemnation and a fine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Federica Cordano, “La percezione antica dell'Orientalizzante,” <i>Aristonothos. Rivista di Studi sul Mediterraneo Antico</i> 3 (2008): 1–22, https://doi.org/10.6092/2037-4488/368. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Symposium</i> 2.1, trans. O. J. Todd, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0212:text=Sym.:chapter=2:section=1. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Symposium</i> 176a–e, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Sym.:section=176a. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Symposium</i> 213e, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Sym.:section=213e. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xcix–ci, 222, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Jorge Bravo, “Boys, Herms, and the Objectification of Desire on Athenian Sympotic Vases,” <i>Eugesta</i> 7 (2017), https://doi.org/10.54563/eugesta.499. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 275–76, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","history","greece","archaic greece","food","art"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Fresco_painting_from_lateral_walls_of_the_Tomb_of_the_Diver_depicting_a_symposium_scene%2C_5th_century_BC%2C_Paestum_Archaeological_Museum.jpg/1280px-Fresco_painting_from_lateral_walls_of_the_Tomb_of_the_Diver_depicting_a_symposium_scene%2C_5th_century_BC%2C_Paestum_Archaeological_Museum.jpg","title":"A symposium painted in the Tomb of the Diver","desc":"The long wall of a 5th-century BCE tomb at Paestum in southern Italy: three couches with low tables beneath them, one man playing the lyre, another the double pipes, and cups in several hands.","credit":"Photograph by Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_painting_from_lateral_walls_of_the_Tomb_of_the_Diver_depicting_a_symposium_scene,_5th_century_BC,_Paestum_Archaeological_Museum.jpg","alt":"A wide painted wall showing three couches side by side, each holding one or two reclining men, with small tables underneath and musical instruments and drinking cups in their hands."}},
{"id":"gr-364","num":364,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"A guest handed the myrtle branch had to take up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> his neighbour had begun, and the name of these drinking songs means 'crooked' for reasons antiquity could never settle.","questions":["Athenaeus preserved a whole Athenian songbook of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which the most famous promises to carry a sword in a branch of myrtle as Harmodius and Aristogeiton did.","Plato quotes a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the <i>Gorgias</i> as a catch everyone had heard over their cups, ranking health first among the good things of life, then beauty, then honest wealth."],"answer":"skolion","answerText":"skolion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Harmodius song</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">attributed to Callistratus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attic collection</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before 450 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"abstract":"A <b>skolion</b> was a song sung over the wine at a Greek drinking party, and its name means 'crooked', which the ancients themselves could not agree how to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some said the songs went round the room in a zigzag order, others that the melody was winding, others that the drinkers were too tipsy to manage what was easy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dicaearchus distinguished three kinds of banquet song: the paean the company sang after the libations, the songs each guest took up in turn as a myrtle branch went round in place of the lyre, and the harder ones left to the skilled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristophanes shows the drill taught in the <i>Wasps</i>, where the tables are spread, the libations poured and the piper finishes his prelude before the pupil must show how he takes up a song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The metre is nearly always logaoedic, and Terpander was called the inventor, though what he did was give an old rhythm a full accompaniment rather than make it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Attic skolia Athenaeus preserved were a songbook put together in Athens by aristocrats shortly before 450 BCE, improvised in manner, twisting a famous poem's thought or sketching a scene from a story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plato quotes one in the <i>Gorgias</i> as a catch everyone had heard over their cups: health is best, then beauty, and third is wealth got without guile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The most celebrated is the Harmodius song, attributed by Hesychius to Callistratus and datable to about 500 BCE, which begins by promising to carry a sword in a branch of myrtle as Harmodius and Aristogeiton did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It repeats the popular Athenian belief that the Hipparchus those two killed in 514 BCE was the tyrant, which is the story Thucydides broke off his narrative to correct, insisting that the elder son Hippias held the tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Another mourns the exiles who held the rock of Leipsydrion against Peisistratus, and beside these popular songs skolia were written as art poetry by Alcaeus, Pindar, Timocreon and Aristotle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xcv–ciii, 478–79, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 90, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 1216–22, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr., Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0044:card=1219. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=6:chapter=54. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Gorgias</i> 451e, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0178:text=Gorg.:section=451e. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Naples_-_Tyrannicide_group_-_Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton.jpg/1280px-National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Naples_-_Tyrannicide_group_-_Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton.jpg","title":"Harmodius and Aristogeiton","desc":"A Roman marble copy of the Greek bronze group of the two Athenians whose killing of Hipparchus in 514 BCE is celebrated in the best known of all the skolia. Naples National Archaeological Museum.","credit":"Photograph by Elliott Brown, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Naples_-_Tyrannicide_group_-_Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton.jpg","alt":"Two life-size marble statues of nude men striding forward side by side, the younger with one arm raised above his head, the older bearded and holding a cloak over his outstretched arm."}},
{"id":"gr-365","num":365,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Herodotus calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a story-writer and a fellow-slave of the courtesan Rhodopis under Iadmon the Samian, and records that the Delphians who killed him later had to pay for it.","questions":["Socrates spent his last days in prison turning the fables of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into verse, which Plato reports as very nearly the only poetry the philosopher ever wrote.","The beast fable is older than <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since Hesiod already has a hawk tell a captive nightingale that the strong take whom they please and only a fool resists."],"answer":"Aesop","answerText":"Aesop","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Floruit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 572 – 569 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the 52nd Olympiad</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">564 BCE, at Delphi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Life of Aesop</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st c. BCE – 2nd c. CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Aesop</b> is the name attached to the Greek beast fable, and what the ancient evidence suggests is a story-figure invented to provide the fables with an author rather than a writer whose works survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus, the earliest witness, calls him a story-writer and a slave of Iadmon the Samian, a fellow-slave of the courtesan Rhodopis, and says the Delphians killed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The killing left a trace that looks administrative: obeying an oracle, Delphi offered compensation to anyone who would take it, and only the grandson of Iadmon came forward to claim it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient chronographers gave him a floruit in the 52nd Olympiad and put his death at Delphi in 564 BCE, but everything else in the life comes from stories rather than from record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He is in any case not a poet, since the fable was a kind of story that anyone might put into verse or collect in prose, and tradition simply made him its author.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The form is older than the name, and Hesiod already tells one, in which a hawk seizes a nightingale and informs her that the strong take whom they please and only a fool resists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 422 BCE Aristophanes could raise a laugh from the Delphi story on stage, where an old man begins it and is cut off before he reaches the point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plato has Socrates spend his last days in prison turning Aesop's fables into verse, which is the clearest measure of how thoroughly the stories were known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A fictional biography, the <i>Life of Aesop</i>, was put together between the 1st century BCE and the 2nd century CE, and its fullest version makes him a grotesquely ugly slave who is given the power of speech by a goddess and then outwits his philosopher master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The fables are anonymous popular matter rather than one man's work, and about a hundred of them touch on religion, with Zeus overshadowing the other gods and Hermes made the butt of the jokes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.134, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=134. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 89, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 1446–48, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr., Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0044:card=1446. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedo</i> 60c–d, trans. Harold North Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0170:text=Phaedo:section=60d. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Works and Days</i> 202–12, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0132:card=202. [Open access]","Ioannis M. Konstantakos, “Life of Aesop and Adventures of Criticism: A Review-Article on Manolis Papathomopoulos' Recent Edition of the Vita Aesopi, Version G,” <i>Myrtia</i> 28 (2013): 355–92, https://revistas.um.es/myrtia/article/view/188071. [Open access]","Michał Wojciechowski, “Gods, Religion and Its Criticism in the Greek Aesopic Fables,” <i>Studia Warmińskie</i> 60 (2023): 357–81, https://doi.org/10.31648/sw.8722. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","storytelling"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Aesop_and_fox--red-figure_cup--Greece--c_450_BC.jpg","title":"A man and a fox on a red-figure cup","desc":"The tondo of a Greek cup of about 450 BCE in the Vatican Museums: a bald, big-headed man wrapped in his mantle listens to a fox that seems to be talking to him. The man has been taken for Aesop since 1843, but nothing on the cup names him.","credit":"Unknown Greek artist, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aesop_and_fox--red-figure_cup--Greece--c_450_BC.jpg","alt":"The circular interior of a pottery cup, bordered with a meander pattern, showing a seated bearded man in a mantle facing a small fox that raises one paw towards him."}},
{"id":"gr-366","num":366,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Where Hesiod had generated the world by births and unions among gods, the thinkers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set out instead to explain it from principles inherent in the world itself.","questions":["Not one complete work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives, so the whole subject is built from quoted fragments and from later writers' reports of what each thinker is supposed to have held.","The questions of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are what Socrates says he burned to answer as a young man: why each thing comes to be, and whether we think with blood or air or fire."],"answer":"Presocratic philosophy","answerText":"Presocratic philosophy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inquiry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in the Greek cities of Ionia and Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cited as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">DK, as in DK22B35</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Presocratic philosophy</b> is the modern name for the inquiry begun in the Greek cities during the 6th and 5th centuries BCE by thinkers whom antiquity recognised as the first philosophers and scientists of its tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What marks the work off is a commitment to explain the world naturalistically, out of principles inherent in the world itself, rather than by the doings of persons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hesiod is the contrast: his <i>Theogony</i> begins with Chaos, then wide-bosomed Earth, then Eros, and generates everything else by births and unions until the gods have divided their honours among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle, looking back, said that most of the earliest philosophers recognised only material principles, meaning that of which all things consist, from which they come and into which they are ultimately resolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plato lets Socrates describe the inquiry from inside, as a youthful eagerness to know why each thing comes to be and perishes, and whether we think with blood or air or fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> These men belonged to a wider company of sages, and what set their wisdom apart was that its first literary form moved away from the poetry and music that had carried Greek thought until then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Not one complete work survives, so the subject is built from fragments, meaning passages that later writers quote, and from testimonia, meaning their reports of what a thinker held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standard collection numbers each thinker and sorts the evidence into testimonies and fragments, which is why a passage is cited in a form like DK22B35 rather than by any page.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Material does still turn up, and the Derveni Papyrus, found in 1962 and probably written late in the 4th century BCE, proved to be a commentary by someone who knew the philosophical theories of his day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The label is itself an 18th-century coinage made current in the 19th, and read strictly as chronology it fails, since the last of these thinkers were contemporaries of Socrates and even of Plato.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 983b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=983b. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Theogony</i> 116–38, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0130:card=116. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedo</i> 96a–b, trans. Harold North Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0170:text=Phaedo:section=96a. [Open access]","Livio Rossetti, “Introduction. La circulation du savoir à l'époque de Thalès (et peu après),” <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 48, no. 2 (2022): 15–23, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_2022_num_48_2_5249. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Derveni_Papyrus_-_Fragments_on_display_in_the_Archaelogical_Museum_of_Thessaloniki.jpg/1280px-Derveni_Papyrus_-_Fragments_on_display_in_the_Archaelogical_Museum_of_Thessaloniki.jpg","title":"The Derveni Papyrus on display","desc":"The charred fragments of the Derveni Papyrus, laid out in eight lit windows of a case in the Archaeological Museum of Thessaloniki. Found in 1962, it is the oldest surviving Greek manuscript and its author knew the philosophical theories of his own day.","credit":"Photograph by Gts-tg, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Derveni_Papyrus_-_Fragments_on_display_in_the_Archaelogical_Museum_of_Thessaloniki.jpg","alt":"A dark museum display table with eight lit rectangular windows, each holding rows of small blackened papyrus scraps against a white backing."}},
{"id":"gr-367","num":367,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Herodotus says a battle between Lydians and Medes broke off when day turned to night, a loss of daylight <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had foretold to the Ionians within the year.","questions":["Aristotle reports that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the permanent entity to be water and the earth to float on it, though the reasons he gives for that view are his own guesses.","Plato tells how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell into a pit while watching the stars and was laughed at by a servant girl for missing what lay at his own feet."],"answer":"Thales","answerText":"Thales","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 580 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Miletus, in Ionia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eclipse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dated 585 BCE by tradition</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Thales</b> of Miletus is the man Aristotle named as the first to inquire into nature in this way, and he seems to have lived around the beginning of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle gives the doctrine plainly: the permanent entity is water, which is why Thales also held that the earth floats on water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reasons are Aristotle's own guesses rather than a report, and he offers that Thales had noticed that the nourishment of everything is moist and that seeds have a moist nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guesswork matters because the doxographers say Thales wrote no book, so there is nothing of his own against which Aristotle can be checked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Read closely, the water doctrine turns out to be two arguments rather than one: a biological claim that water is the source of life, and a physical claim that water holds the earth up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Greek <i>arche</i>, meaning 'first principle', is Aristotle's word and not Thales', who never said that things which perish turn back into water, a question the later Ionians were the first to raise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The famous stories are about his standing rather than his doctrine, and the oldest is in Herodotus: Thales foretold a loss of daylight to the Ionians, and when day turned to night in the middle of a battle the Lydians and Medes stopped fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tradition dates that eclipse to 585 BCE, but a prediction of the kind described was almost certainly beyond anything he could have done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus also credits him with a political proposal, that the Ionians set up a single council at Teos and count their own cities as no more than demes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plato has him fall into a pit while watching the stars, to a servant girl's laughter, and Aristotle has him corner the olive presses to show that a philosopher could be rich if he cared to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 983b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=983b. [Open access]","Ryszard Legutko, “Thales on Water,” <i>Peitho. Examina Antiqua</i> 8, no. 1 (2017): 81–90, https://doi.org/10.14746/pea.2017.1.4. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.74, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=74. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.170, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=170. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Theaetetus</i> 174a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Theaet.:section=174a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 1259a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=1:section=1259a. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science","astronomy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/NMBSevenSagesMosaicBaalbekRectified.jpg/1280px-NMBSevenSagesMosaicBaalbekRectified.jpg","title":"The Seven Sages, in a mosaic from Baalbek","desc":"Seven portrait roundels of the Sages of Greece, each labelled in Greek with a name and a maxim, set around the Muse Calliope. The mosaic dates to the 3rd century CE, and Thales was counted among the Seven in the ancient lists.","credit":"Photograph by Clemens Schmillen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NMBSevenSagesMosaicBaalbekRectified.jpg","alt":"A square mosaic floor panel with seven circular portraits of bearded men ranged around a larger central roundel of a young woman in a yellow robe, Greek lettering beside each face."}},
{"id":"gr-368","num":368,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"The one surviving sentence of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> calls the first principle neither water nor any other familiar element but the indefinite, from which come all the heavens and the worlds in them.","questions":["Asked what holds the earth up, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that it stays put for want of any reason to move, sitting at the centre and related alike to every point of the edge.","Aristotle judged the earth-at-rest argument of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ingenious but not true, while antiquity credited him with a sundial gnomon, a globe and the first map of land and sea."],"answer":"Anaximander","answerText":"Anaximander","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 – 545 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Miletus, after Thales</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aged 64</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 58th Olympiad</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Anaximander</b> of Miletus, son of Praxiades, was remembered as the successor of Thales, and he is the first Greek thinker of whom a sentence survives in something close to his own words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Simplicius, commenting on Aristotle more than a thousand years later, quotes him saying that the first principle is neither water nor any other of the familiar elements but the indefinite, <i>to apeiron</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From it come all the heavens and the worlds in them, and the things out of which things come to be are also those into which they pass away, as must be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then the clause everyone remembers: they give penalty and recompense to one another for their injustice, in accordance with the ordering of time, which even Simplicius called rather poetical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The picture is of a system that keeps itself in balance, in sharp contrast with the quarrelsome personified gods who interfere in the heavens and in human affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The balance shows in the measurements as well as the language, since the intervals between earth, fixed stars, moon and sun are equal and the sun is reckoned the same size as the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He asked what holds the earth up, and answered that it stays where it is for want of any reason to move, sitting at the centre and related alike to every point of the edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle, who preserves that argument, calls it ingenious but not true, and elsewhere counts Anaximander among those who must have had some conception of matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He is credited with the gnomon of a sundial, set up at Sparta to mark the solstices, with a globe, and with the first map to draw the outline of land and sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herodotus is the earliest witness to what such maps looked like, and he laughs at them: drawn round as if turned by compasses, ringed by Ocean, with Asia and Europe made the same size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>De Caelo</i> 295b, trans. J. L. Stocks (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1922), https://archive.org/details/decaelo00aris. [Open access]","Maria Antonietta Salamone, “Equality and Justice in Early Greek Cosmologies: The Paradigm of the 'Line of the Horizon',” <i>Philosophy and Cosmology</i> 18 (2017): 22–31, http://ispcjournal.org/journals/2017/Salamone_Philosophy_and_Cosmology_vol_18.pdf. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.36, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=4:chapter=36. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 1069b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=12:section=1069b. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 2.1–2, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science","astronomy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/Anaximander_Mosaic.jpg","title":"A Roman mosaic identified as Anaximander","desc":"A floor mosaic of the early 3rd century CE from Johannisstrasse in Trier, showing a seated bearded philosopher with a sundial in his hands. It is identified as Anaximander, to whom antiquity credited the sundial gnomon.","credit":"Roman mosaic, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anaximander_Mosaic.jpg","alt":"A square mosaic panel with braided and chevron borders framing a round central picture of an elderly bearded man seated on a stool, holding a fan-shaped sundial."}},
{"id":"gr-369","num":369,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Where his predecessors had named a stuff and stopped, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> supplied a mechanism: air condensing into wind, cloud, water, earth and stone, and rarefying into fire.","questions":["Plutarch illustrates the theory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with breath, which comes out cool through pursed lips and warm through an open mouth, tying condensation to cooling.","Aristotle set <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside Diogenes as holding air to be prior to water and, of all the bodily elements, most truly the first principle of things."],"answer":"Anaximenes","answerText":"Anaximenes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 546 – 526 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Miletus, after Anaximander</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 63rd Olympiad</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Anaximenes</b> of Miletus, son of Eurystratus and remembered as the pupil of Anaximander, was the third and last of the Milesian thinkers, and Apollodorus made him a contemporary of the fall of Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He put air where Anaximander had put the indefinite, which looks like a step back towards the water of Thales until you see what it bought him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Air is neutral enough to serve as a principle and yet has properties of its own, among them colour, temperature, humidity, motion, taste and smell, where the indefinite had almost none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what made possible the thing his predecessors never supplied, a stated mechanism by which one thing becomes another, and the mechanism is condensation and rarefaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rarefied, air becomes fire; condensed further and further it becomes wind, then cloud, then water, then earth, and at last stones, and the rest come to be from these.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch ties the two processes to cooling and heating with an example anyone can try, since breath through pursed lips comes out cool and through an open mouth comes out warm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle set him beside Diogenes of Apollonia as holding air to be prior to water and, of all the bodily elements, most truly the first principle of things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That framing is Aristotle's own, and it carries his assumption that the stuff persists underneath as a substratum, which the Milesians may never have held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Read the other way about, the originating stuff is not inert matter but an active source of life, which is what the older Greek word for nature carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Of the man himself little survives: he held that the stars go round the earth and never under it, and he is said to have written simply and unaffectedly in the Ionic dialect, which is prose rather than the verse the poets still used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 984a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=984a. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 2.3, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]","Nestor-Luis Cordero, “The Dynamic Conception of Being in the First Philosophers and the Notion of φύσις,” <i>Journal of Ancient Philosophy</i> 16, no. 2 (2022): 1–23, https://www.revistas.usp.br/filosofiaantiga/article/view/203154. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 983b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=983b. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"]},
{"id":"gr-370","num":370,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Thales, Anaximander and Anaximenes together make up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the doxographers who arranged them as a master-and-pupil succession may have been tidying rather than reporting.","questions":["The thinkers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew no line between the questions we would call scientific and those we would call philosophical, and pursued both with the same methods.","The city that housed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell to the Persians in 494 BCE, and Athens later fined Phrynichus a thousand drachmas for putting that loss on the stage."],"answer":"Milesian school","answerText":"Milesian school","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 526 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Thales, Anaximander, Anaximenes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Miletus fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Milesian school</b> is the name given to three thinkers of a single Ionian city, Thales, Anaximander and Anaximenes, with whom Greek philosophy is conventionally begun, all three of them active during the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The doxographers made them a succession, Anaximander the pupil of Thales and Anaximenes the pupil of Anaximander, and ran the same line on through Anaxagoras as far as Socrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That master-and-pupil chain may not be true, but their views resemble one another closely enough to justify treating the three of them together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What each did was name an originating stuff, water and then the indefinite and then air, and account for the world out of it, which is the pattern Aristotle later described as recognising only material principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The framing is his, and reading them as committed to one stuff persisting underneath as a substratum presumes a theory of change that had not yet been formulated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What they plainly did share was a refusal to call in help from outside the natural world, so that the account of the cosmos is complete in its own terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They drew no line between the questions we would call scientific and those we would call philosophical, and saw no need for distinct methods to answer them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their appetite for measuring and explaining what happens in the sky and on the ground was as strong as their interest in causes and principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miletus itself was both the setting and part of the reason, a city at the height of its wealth that Herodotus calls the glory of Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persians took it in 494 BCE, mining the walls and enslaving the people, and when Phrynichus put the fall on the Athenian stage the whole theatre wept and the city fined him a thousand drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.28, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=28. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.18, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=18. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.21, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=21. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 1.13, 2.1–3, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 983b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=983b. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Milet_2013-03-25q.jpg/1280px-Milet_2013-03-25q.jpg","title":"The site of Miletus","desc":"The city Herodotus calls the glory of Ionia now lies inland among standing water and scattered ruins. Everything left above ground is later than the three thinkers, the archaic city having been destroyed in 494 BCE.","credit":"Photograph by Rabe!, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milet_2013-03-25q.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a flat green site scattered with low stone ruins and shallow pools of standing water, with a cultivated plain and hazy blue hills beyond."}},
{"id":"gr-371","num":371,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Xenophanes, a contemporary, has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stop the beating of a puppy because he recognised in its yelping the soul of a friend of his.","questions":["The early evidence makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> famous as an expert on the soul's fate, an authority on ritual and a wonder-worker, and not at all as the mathematician of the modern picture.","Plato reports that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was honoured above all for a way of living, and that his followers were still recognisable in Plato's own day for keeping to it."],"answer":"Pythagoras","answerText":"Pythagoras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 – 490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Samos, then Croton</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Emigrated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at about 40</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Pythagoras</b> is among the most famous of Greek philosophers and among the least securely known, since he wrote nothing and no contemporary left a detailed account of his thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He grew up on Samos and moved at about forty to Croton in southern Italy, where most of his activity took place, and he lived from about 570 to about 490 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later antiquity turned him into a semi-divine figure who had originated everything true in Greek philosophy, and treatises were forged in his name to prove it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Getting behind that glorification is what scholars call the Pythagorean question, and the way through it is to prefer the earliest evidence to the later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that evidence his fame rested on none of what the modern picture gives him: he was an expert on the fate of the soul, an authority on ritual, a wonder-worker with a golden thigh, and the founder of a strict way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest surviving word about him is his contemporary Xenophanes', who has him stop the beating of a puppy because he recognised in its yelping the soul of a friend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells how the Thracian Salmoxis was said to have been his slave on Samos and to have taught his countrymen that they would never die, and calls Pythagoras one of the greatest Greek teachers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus also reports a rule about burial, that nothing woollen may go into the grave, which he says is called Orphic and Bacchic but is in fact Egyptian and Pythagorean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plato says he was honoured above all for a way of living, and that his successors were still known in Plato's own day for keeping to the Pythagorean life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As for the theorem, there is evidence that he valued relationships between numbers of that kind, but it is not likely that he proved it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Carl Huffman, “Pythagoras,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2024 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2024/entries/pythagoras/. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 8.6, 8.36, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.95, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=4:chapter=95. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.81, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=81. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 600b, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0168:book=10:section=600b. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Pythagoras_in_the_Roman_Forum%2C_Colosseum.jpg/1280px-Pythagoras_in_the_Roman_Forum%2C_Colosseum.jpg","title":"A marble head identified as Pythagoras","desc":"A Roman copy of a Greek original of the 2nd or 1st century BCE, conventionally called Pythagoras. No likeness made in his lifetime survives, and a portrait of that date belongs to the tradition that had already remade him.","credit":"Photograph by Szilas, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pythagoras_in_the_Roman_Forum,_Colosseum.jpg","alt":"A white marble bust of a bearded man wearing a thick cloth turban wound round his head, seen against a plain ochre wall."}},
{"id":"gr-372","num":372,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Since ten was the complete number, the astronomers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needed ten revolving bodies, and with only nine visible they supplied a counter-earth for the tenth.","questions":["Aristotle's phrase 'the so-called Pythagoreans' both records what the adherents of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called and quietly doubts their connection with the man himself.","The followers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> split into those who kept the rules handed down as things heard and those who pursued mathematics, each denying the other was the genuine article."],"answer":"Pythagoreanism","answerText":"Pythagoreanism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 350 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in the Greek cities of Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Opposites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 pairs, per Aristotle</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Pythagoreanism</b> covers several different things, and keeping them apart is most of the difficulty: the way of life Pythagoras taught, the philosophy of a group active in the 5th and 4th centuries BCE, and a later habit of calling almost any earlier thinker a Pythagorean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle writes of 'the so-called Pythagoreans', which records what that group was called and at the same time questions their connection with the man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He names none of them, but the philosophy he assigns them is close to what survives of Philolaus of Croton, who made limiters and unlimiteds his first principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their claim, as Aristotle reports it, is that the principles of mathematics are the principles of everything and that numbers are the ultimate things in the physical universe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They found analogues in number for justice, for soul or mind and for opportunity, and they saw that the ratios of the musical scales are numbers as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The method shows plainest in their astronomy: ten being the complete number, the revolving bodies had to be ten, and with only nine visible they supplied a counter-earth for the tenth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One group among them set out ten pairs of principles, among them limit and unlimited, odd and even, right and left, male and female, light and darkness, and good and evil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plato names them just once, for holding that harmony and astronomy are kindred sciences, the ears being framed for the movements of harmony as the eyes are for the heavens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The communities themselves split in two, the acusmatici keeping rules handed down as things heard, among them not to stir a fire with a knife and to abstain from beans, and the mathematici pursuing mathematics and the study of nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each side denied the other was truly Pythagorean, and the communities died out around the middle of the 4th century BCE, having given women an unusually large part for an ancient school.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Carl Huffman, “Pythagoreanism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Summer 2024 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2024/entries/pythagoreanism/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 985b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=985b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986a. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 530d, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0168:book=7:section=530d. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 8.17–19, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]"],"tags":["school of thought","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Gaffurio_Pythagoras.png/1280px-Gaffurio_Pythagoras.png","title":"The harmonic ratios, in a woodcut of 1492","desc":"Four panels from Franchino Gaffurio’s <i>Theorica musicae</i>, with numbered bells, glasses, monochord strings and pipes, and Philolaus named in the last. It is a late witness to the doctrine rather than an ancient one, printed nearly two thousand years after the school it depicts.","credit":"Woodcut from Gaffurio, Theorica musicae, 1492, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaffurio_Pythagoras.png","alt":"A black and white woodcut in four panels showing robed men striking bells, glasses, strings and pipes, each object marked with a number such as 16, 12, 9, 8, 6 or 4."}},
{"id":"gr-373","num":373,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"It was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who noticed that Ethiopians make their gods snub-nosed and black while Thracians make theirs red-haired, and that horses with hands would draw horse-shaped gods.","questions":["Aristotle preserves the argument of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that calling the gods born is as impious as calling them mortal, since either way there was a time when they did not exist.","By his own reckoning <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent 67 years tossing his cares up and down the land of Greece, having set out at 25, which makes him about 92 when he wrote it."],"answer":"Xenophanes","answerText":"Xenophanes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Floruit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">540 – 537 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the 60th Olympiad</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wandered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">67 years, from age 25</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Xenophanes</b> of Colophon was a travelling poet of the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE who turned his verse on the gods, and who says he spent 67 years tossing his cares up and down the land of Greece after setting out at 25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is remembered above all for a criticism of anthropomorphism in religion: mortals suppose that gods are born, wear their own clothes, and have a voice and a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ethiopians make their gods snub-nosed and black and Thracians make theirs blue-eyed and red-haired, and if horses and oxen had hands to draw with, their gods would come out looking like horses and oxen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The complaint against the poets is moral as well: Homer and Hesiod had given the gods everything that counts as a reproach among men, theft and adultery and mutual deceit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle preserves a sharper argument, that calling the gods born is as impious as calling them mortal, since either way there was a time when they did not exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In place of all that he set one god greatest among gods and men, not at all like mortals in body or in thought, which reads as monotheism until you notice the plural in the same line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius reports him holding that the substance of god is spherical and in no way resembles a man, is all eye and all ear but does not breathe, and is the whole of mind and thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was an observer too, and fossil sea creatures found inland at Syracuse, Paros and Malta gave him the ground for a cycle in which the earth is drowned and dried out again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About knowledge he was as careful as about the gods, holding that no mortal will ever know anything about them with certainty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later readers made him the founder of Eleatic philosophy on the strength of brief remarks by Plato and Aristotle, though Aristotle also says he gave no definite teaching at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Lesher, “Xenophanes,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Summer 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2023/entries/xenophanes/. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.18–20, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Sophist</i> 242d, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Soph.:section=242d. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Rhetoric</i> 2.23, trans. J. H. Freese, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0060:book=2:chapter=23. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","poetry","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Xenophanes_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg","title":"An imagined Xenophanes, engraved in 1655","desc":"A plate from Thomas Stanley’s <i>History of Philosophy</i>, showing a bearded man in a turban leaning on a staff. No ancient portrait of Xenophanes is known, and this belongs to the long habit of illustrating philosophers nobody had seen.","credit":"Engraving from Thomas Stanley, The History of Philosophy, 1655, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xenophanes_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg","alt":"A finely hatched engraving of an elderly bearded man in a turban and heavy draped robes, leaning on a long staff in an open landscape, with his name lettered beneath."}},
{"id":"gr-374","num":374,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"The line usually given to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about never stepping twice into the same river is Plato's paraphrase, and his own words put the emphasis elsewhere.","questions":["Sea is the purest water and the most polluted, said <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, being drinkable and healthy for fish but undrinkable and harmful for men.","The one book of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was deposited in the temple of Artemis and made deliberately obscure, so that none but adepts should approach it."],"answer":"Heraclitus","answerText":"Heraclitus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Ephesus, in Ionia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Book</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1, in 3 discourses</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Heraclitus</b> of Ephesus was active around 500 BCE and wrote a single book, which he is said to have deposited in the temple of Artemis and to have made deliberately obscure so that none but adepts should approach it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius says it was divided into three discourses, on the universe, on politics and on theology, and Theophrastus, who had read it, thought parts of it half-finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The most famous thing he is supposed to have said is that you cannot step twice into the same river, but that is Plato's paraphrase rather than his own words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The one river fragment carrying the density of his style puts the emphasis differently: on those stepping into the same rivers, other and other waters flow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a claim about how a thing stays the same, since a body of water is a river precisely because its water changes, and stop the flow and you have a lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Flux is a special case of a wider idea, the unity of opposites, which he illustrates plainly: sea is the purest water and the most polluted, healthy for fish and harmful for men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His cosmology fits in a sentence, and it is the first surviving Greek text to use <i>kosmos</i> for the world: this world-order, the same for all, no god or man made, but ever was and is and will be, everliving fire kindled in measures and quenched in measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle set him beside Hippasus as making fire the first principle, and elsewhere beside Empedocles among those who held that destruction alternates without end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That second report sits badly with the fragment itself, which has the world always existing while only portions of it are transformed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He belonged to no school and implies he taught himself, and he has been called a material monist and a process philosopher, an empiricist and a mystic, the first real philosopher and an obscurantist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Daniel W. Graham, “Heraclitus,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Winter 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2023/entries/heraclitus/. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Cratylus</i> 402a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Crat.:section=402a. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.5–6, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>De Caelo</i> 279b, trans. J. L. Stocks (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1922), https://archive.org/details/decaelo00aris. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 984a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=984a. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Filosofo_detto_eraclito%2C_da_villa_dei_papiri%2C_peristilio_quadrato.JPG/1280px-Filosofo_detto_eraclito%2C_da_villa_dei_papiri%2C_peristilio_quadrato.JPG","title":"A bronze bust long called Heraclitus","desc":"Found in the Villa of the Papyri at Herculaneum and now in Naples, inv. 5623. The museum says the identification remains problematic: the piece was once taken for Heraclitus and is now more often thought to be Empedocles.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Filosofo_detto_eraclito,_da_villa_dei_papiri,_peristilio_quadrato.JPG","alt":"A dark bronze bust of an elderly bearded man with a deeply lined brow and hollow eye sockets, wearing a wreath and a draped cloak, on a pale stone plinth."}},
{"id":"gr-375","num":375,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"Almost everything we have of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives because Simplicius, writing a thousand years later, copied 52 verses into a commentary and explained that the original had become scarce.","questions":["A goddess promises <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two things in his poem: the unshaken heart of well-rounded reality, and the notions of mortals, in which there is no genuine trustworthiness.","Plato says that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> protested all his life, in prose and in verse, that one must never let the thought prevail that not-being is."],"answer":"Parmenides","answerText":"Parmenides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 515 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Elea, in southern Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Poem</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 verses, 160 surviving</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Parmenides</b> was a philosopher of Elea, a Greek colony on the Tyrrhenian coast of southern Italy, born about 515 BCE by an inference from Plato, who has him at about 65 meeting a Socrates of about twenty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius calls him the son of Pyres and a pupil of Xenophanes who followed him in nothing, and says it was a Pythagorean named Ameinias who drew him to the life of study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Speusippus, Plato’s successor at the Academy, reported that he drew up the laws for his own city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A portrait head found at Elea in the 1960s carries an inscription naming him Parmeneides, son of Pyres, Ouliadēs, Natural Philosopher, and tying him to a cult of Apollo the Healer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote one work, a poem in the hexameters of epic and perhaps eight hundred verses long, of which about 160 survive, from a single word to the 62 lines of fragment 8.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It opens on a chariot ride to a goddess who promises two things: the unshaken heart of well-rounded reality, and the notions of mortals, in which there is no genuine trustworthiness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first is an argument that What Is is ungenerated and deathless, whole and uniform, still and perfect, complete from every side like the bulk of a well-rounded globe, a unity Aristotle read as one in definition and therefore finite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The other road is refused outright, and Plato quotes the refusal: never let this thought prevail, that not-being is, but keep your mind from this way of investigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The longer second half was an ordinary cosmology of heavens, sun, moon, stars, earth and living creatures, and it is the part we know least about, since later readers quoted the metaphysics and let the rest go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That the metaphysics survives whole is owed to Simplicius, who transcribed 52 of its verses in the 6th century CE and gave his reason: the scarcity of Parmenides’ treatise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["John Palmer, “Parmenides,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2025 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2025/entries/parmenides/. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Parmenides</i> 127b, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Parm.:section=127b. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.21–23, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Sophist</i> 237a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Soph.:section=237a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986b. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Busto_di_Parmenide.jpg/1280px-Busto_di_Parmenide.jpg","title":"The inscribed herm from Elea","desc":"A marble head on a pillar cut with his name and titles, found at Elea in the 1960s and shown at the archaeological park of Velia. The carving is Roman work of the 1st century CE, five centuries after him, so it is an idea of Parmenides rather than a likeness.","credit":"Photograph by Sergio Spolti, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busto_di_Parmenide.jpg","alt":"A weathered marble portrait head of a bearded man mounted on a tall rectangular pillar, against a rough stone wall, with lines of Greek capitals carved on the pillar."}},
{"id":"gr-376","num":376,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"The book of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is usually called a defence of Parmenidean monism, but Plato has him deny that and call it a young man’s polemic, stolen before he could publish it.","questions":["Aristotle preserves four arguments by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against motion, among them the flying arrow that is motionless and the quickest runner who never overtakes the slowest.","Plato calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Eleatic Palamedes, whose art can make the same things appear to his hearers alike and unlike, one and many, at rest and in motion."],"answer":"Zeno of Elea","answerText":"Zeno of Elea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Elea, in southern Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Floruit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">464 – 460 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Zeno of Elea</b> was born about 490 BCE, a date got from Plato’s dialogue, which brings him to Athens at nearly 40 in the company of Parmenides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius calls him the son of Teleutagoras and the adopted son of Parmenides, says he preferred his own small city to Athens, and reports that Aristotle called him the inventor of dialectic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His life story is mostly a plot against a local tyrant, told several ways, in which he bit off the man’s ear, or his nose, and held on until he was stabbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote one book of arguments, and the account of it as a defence of Parmenidean monism is what the young Socrates guesses in Plato’s dialogue and what Plato’s Zeno immediately denies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a support for Parmenides against people jeering at him, Zeno says, paying them back with interest, written in a spirit of controversy when he was young and stolen before he could decide whether to publish it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Every argument in it aims to show that things are not many, and the pattern is an antinomy: if existences are many, they must be both like and unlike, which is impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plato elsewhere calls him the Eleatic Palamedes, whose art can make the same things appear to his hearers alike and unlike, one and many, at rest and in motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The four arguments against motion survive only in Aristotle’s summary: the half-way point must be reached first, the quickest runner never overtakes the slowest, the flying arrow is motionless, and two rows pass each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle names no tortoise, only the quickest runner and the slowest, though a cup painted in Italy about a century earlier shows a runner outstripping a large one and is taken for the earliest response to the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He calls the reasoning fallacious, the arrow assuming that time is made of indivisible moments and the half-way argument assuming that an unlimited number of points cannot be passed in a limited time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["John Palmer, “Zeno of Elea,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Summer 2025 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2025/entries/zeno-elea/. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Parmenides</i> 127e–128e, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Parm.:section=127e. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.25–29, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Physics</i> 6.9, 239b, trans. R. P. Hardie and R. K. Gaye, in <i>The Works of Aristotle Translated into English</i>, vol. 2, ed. W. D. Ross (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1930), https://archive.org/details/works0002aris. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedrus</i> 261d, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Phaedrus:section=261d. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Zeno_of_Elea_Tibaldi_or_Carducci_Escorial.jpg/1280px-Zeno_of_Elea_Tibaldi_or_Carducci_Escorial.jpg","title":"Zeno at the doors of Truth and Falsity","desc":"A fresco of the 1590s in the library of the Escorial, lettered ZENON HELEATES, in which he points a crowd of young men at two doorways marked <i>Falsitas</i> and <i>Veritas</i>. No ancient portrait of him is known, and the scene is an emblem of dialectic rather than anything he is recorded as doing.","credit":"Fresco by Pellegrino Tibaldi, El Escorial, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zeno_of_Elea_Tibaldi_or_Carducci_Escorial.jpg","alt":"A wide painted frieze in which an elderly bald man in an orange robe stretches out both arms toward two dark doorways lettered Falsitas and Veritas, while a press of half-draped young men surges after him."}},
{"id":"gr-377","num":377,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"Parmenides, Zeno and Melissus are counted together as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, although the last of the three was a Samian, and not an Eleate at all.","questions":["Plato is the first to speak of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a group, calling it a sect in his own region and starting it from Xenophanes and even earlier.","Aristotle dismisses the two chief members of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as contentious reasoners whose premisses are false and whose conclusions do not follow."],"answer":"Eleatic school","answerText":"Eleatic school","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 – 440 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Elea, in southern Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Members</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, of whom 2 were Eleates</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Eleatic school</b> is the name given to a group of thinkers linked to Elea, a Greek colony on the coast of southern Italy, whose common thesis is that what there is, is one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plato is the earliest to treat them as a group, calling them the Eleatic sect in our region and starting them from Xenophanes and even earlier, and elsewhere bringing on a stranger from Elea as one of the followers of Parmenides and Zeno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The membership antiquity settled on is Parmenides, Zeno and Melissus, with Xenophanes attached at the head of the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two of those four were not Eleates at all: Xenophanes was a wandering poet from Colophon in Ionia, and Melissus was a Samian whom his countrymen elected admiral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The succession is thinner than it looks, since Aristotle reports only that Parmenides is said to have been Xenophanes’ disciple, and Diogenes Laertius says Parmenides was instructed by him but was no follower of his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The one doctrine they are credited with covers real disagreements, and Aristotle separates the versions: for Parmenides what is one is one in definition and therefore finite, for Melissus it is materially one and therefore unlimited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diogenes gives Melissus his own summary, a universe unlimited, unchangeable, immovable, one and uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle handles the pair roughly, calling both of them contentious reasoners whose premisses are false and whose conclusions do not follow, and Melissus’ argument gross and palpable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The label travelled further than the doctrine, and Diogenes has even the atomist Leucippus born at Elea, though some say Abdera and others Miletus, and made a pupil of Zeno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the group really did was set the problem the next generation had to solve, since after it any account of a changing world of many things had to say first how change and plurality were possible at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plato, <i>Sophist</i> 216a, 242d, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Soph.:section=216a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Physics</i> 1.2–3, 185a–186a, trans. R. P. Hardie and R. K. Gaye, in <i>The Works of Aristotle Translated into English</i>, vol. 2, ed. W. D. Ross (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1930), https://archive.org/details/works0002aris. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.21–30, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Velia_Porta_Rosa_Esterno_P1170089.JPG/1280px-Velia_Porta_Rosa_Esterno_P1170089.JPG","title":"The Porta Rosa at Elea","desc":"A stone arch over the road that crossed the city now called Velia, in southern Italy, which gives the school its name. Two of the four thinkers grouped under that name were not Eleates at all: Xenophanes came from Colophon in Ionia and Melissus from Samos.","credit":"Photograph by Crompippo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Velia_Porta_Rosa_Esterno_P1170089.JPG","alt":"A round-headed stone arch standing in a gap between two overgrown earth banks, with a grassy track leading up to it and bright sky beyond."}},
{"id":"gr-378","num":378,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"The story that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leapt into the craters of Etna to prove his own divinity is the one part of his life we can fairly confidently rule out.","questions":["Everything is made of four roots moved by Love and Strife, said <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who named the roots after gods rather than after fire, earth, air and water.","Homer and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have nothing in common but the metre, Aristotle wrote, so that one should be called a poet and the other a scientist."],"answer":"Empedocles","answerText":"Empedocles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 484 – 424 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Acragas, in Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Poems</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, or perhaps 1</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Empedocles</b> was a Sicilian of Acragas, born early in the 5th century BCE to an aristocratic family whose grandfather had kept horses and won at Olympia, and Aristotle says he died at 60.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote in the hexameters of epic, and antiquity knew two poems under his name, <i>On Nature</i> and the <i>Purifications</i>, though whether that is two works or one is now argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A papyrus at Strasbourg published in 1999 carries lines of <i>On Nature</i> that editors had long assigned to the <i>Purifications</i>, making the physics and the religion harder to pull apart than they looked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle could not settle what to call him, and in the <i>Poetics</i> says that Homer and Empedocles have nothing in common but the metre, so that one should be called a poet and the other a scientist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was remembered as a doctor and an orator besides, and Plato has Socrates explain colour by the effluences and passages that Empedocles held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The physics makes everything out of four roots, named as gods rather than as fire, earth, air and water, moved by two forces, Love drawing them into one and Strife pulling them apart, in a cycle without end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle credits him with being the first to make the material elements four, and the first to make the source of motion two contrary forces instead of one, while complaining that he uses neither consistently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own verses greet his fellow citizens as an immortal god, no longer mortal, crowned with ribbons and garlands and followed by thousands who want prophecies or a cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Out of that grew the story everybody knows, that he threw himself into the craters of Etna to make good the claim, and was found out when the mountain threw up one of his bronze sandals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius gives it as one report among several, on the authority of Hippobotus, and the leap is the one part of his life that can be ruled out with any confidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["K. Scarlett Kingsley and Richard Parry, “Empedocles,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Winter 2024 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2024/entries/empedocles/. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 8.62, 8.69, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 985a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=985a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1447b, trans. W. H. Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1447b. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Meno</i> 76c, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0178:text=Meno:section=76c. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","science","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Agrigento%2C_Valley_of_the_Temples_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Agrigento%2C_Valley_of_the_Temples_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The temple ridge at Acragas","desc":"The Greek city of Acragas in Sicily, now Agrigento, with the Temple of Concordia standing on the ridge. Empedocles opens by greeting the people who dwell in the great city beside the yellow Acragas, on the lofty citadel.","credit":"Photograph by Maarten Sepp, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrigento,_Valley_of_the_Temples_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A long dry ridge covered in olive trees and scattered blocks of fallen stone, with a small honey-coloured Doric temple standing at its far end and a modern town on the hills beyond."}},
{"id":"gr-379","num":379,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"I write these things as they seem to me to be true, wrote <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, for the tales told by the Greeks are, as it appears to me, many and absurd.","questions":["At Thebes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> traced his descent to a god in the sixteenth generation, and the priests walked him past 345 wooden statues, father to son, with no god at the end.","Alone in Aristagoras’ council <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opposed the Ionian revolt, listing all the nations subject to Darius, and then advised seizing the temple treasure to buy command of the sea."],"answer":"Hecataeus of Miletus","answerText":"Hecataeus of Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Miletus, in Ionia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, both lost</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Hecataeus of Miletus</b> was a Greek writer of the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE, the son of Hegesander, working in prose at a time when nearly everything worth saying was said in verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Demetrius quotes his opening, and it is a remarkable way to begin a book: Hecataeus of Miletus thus relates — I write these things as they seem to me to be true, for the tales told by the Greeks are, as it appears to me, many and absurd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Antiquity knew a prose history under his name and a description of the earth, and nothing of either survives except in later quotation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo, following Eratosthenes, puts him with Anaximander as the two who came after Homer in geography, saying that Anaximander first published a chart and that Hecataeus left a work identifiable from his other writings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Heraclitus, a contemporary, put him in a short list of men whose reading had taught them nothing: much learning does not teach understanding, else it would have taught Hesiod and Pythagoras, and again Xenophanes and Hecataeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus names him four times and never twice in the same relation, which is a fair measure of how one writer used another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Twice he is the adviser at Miletus, alone in opposing the Ionian revolt after listing the nations Darius ruled, and afterwards telling Aristagoras to fortify Leros and wait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once he is corrected: at Thebes he traced his own descent to a god in the sixteenth generation, and the priests walked him past 345 wooden statues, each the son of the one before, with no god at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Once he is an authority, cited for the claim that Athens drove out the Pelasgians unjustly, after which Herodotus gives the Athenians’ own account instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> And in the middle of all this Herodotus laughs at the mapmakers who draw the world as round as if made with compasses, with the Ocean river running about it, and names nobody at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.36, 5.125, 6.137, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=36. [Open access]","Demetrius, <i>On Style</i> 12, ed. and trans. W. Rhys Roberts (Cambridge: at the University Press, 1902), https://archive.org/details/demetriusonstyle01deme. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 1.1.11, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=1:chapter=1:section=11. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.1, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.143, 4.36, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=143. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","geography","greece","archaic greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Bunbury_Vol_1_Map_02_Hecataeus_p_148.jpg/1280px-Bunbury_Vol_1_Map_02_Hecataeus_p_148.jpg","title":"The world according to Hecataeus","desc":"A reconstruction engraved for Edward Bunbury’s <i>A History of Ancient Geography</i> in 1879, drawn from the fragments. No map of his survives, and the shape here — a disc with the Ocean running round it — is the one Herodotus laughed at.","credit":"Engraving from Edward Bunbury, A History of Ancient Geography, London: John Murray, 1879, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bunbury_Vol_1_Map_02_Hecataeus_p_148.jpg","alt":"An engraved circular world map lettered THE WORLD ACCORDING TO HECATAEUS, with a hatched band of Oceanus round the rim and the land divided into Europa, Asia and Libya."}},
{"id":"gr-380","num":380,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"The reputation of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the father of anatomy rests on the excision of one eyeball, and on discoveries made by a writer two centuries after him.","questions":["Health is the equality of the powers in the body and disease the sole rule of one of them, said <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, borrowing the language of politics for medicine.","The earliest Greek on record to put understanding in the brain, and to separate understanding from perceiving, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who wrote chiefly on medicine."],"answer":"Alcmaeon of Croton","answerText":"Alcmaeon of Croton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Croton, in southern Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Book</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1, in fragments</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Alcmaeon of Croton</b> was a Greek writer of the early 5th century BCE from a city in southern Italy whose doctors Herodotus calls the best in Greece, though whether he practised medicine is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius says he wrote chiefly on medicine and preserves his opening, addressed to three men who may have been Pythagoreans: of things invisible, as of mortal things, only the gods have certain knowledge; to us, as men, only inference is possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He probably wrote his book between 500 and 450 BCE, and nearly every other question about him is argued over, his exact dates included, and what he owed to Pythagoras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle wrote a book in reply to him, and in the <i>Metaphysics</i> compares him with a group of Pythagoreans for making opposites the principles of things, leaving open who took the idea from whom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is left of him is a handful of quotations and reports, most of them about the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The claim that made his name is that the brain is the seat of understanding, and that understanding is a different thing from perceiving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Theophrastus reports him saying that man alone understands while other creatures perceive without understanding, and that all the senses are connected in some way with the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plato has Socrates recall the question unsettling him in his youth: whether it is the blood, or air, or fire by which we think, or whether the brain furnishes the sensations of hearing and sight and smell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His account of health is borrowed from politics, since the equality of the powers in the body — wet and dry, hot and cold, bitter and sweet — keeps a person well, and the sole rule of any one of them makes them ill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is often called the father of anatomy, and the evidence will not carry it: it goes no further than the excision of one eyeball, and the detailed work on the eye credited to him is a later writer’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Carl Huffman, “Alcmaeon,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2025 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2025/entries/alcmaeon/. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 8.83, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986a. [Open access]","Theophrastus, <i>De sensibus</i> 25–26, trans. George M. Stratton, in <i>Theophrastus and the Greek Physiological Psychology before Aristotle</i> (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1917), https://archive.org/details/theophrastusgree00stra. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedo</i> 96b, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0170:text=Phaedo:section=96b. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","science","philosophy","greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Crotone%2C_parco_archeologico_di_capo_colonna%2C_tempio_di_hera_lacinia%2C_01.jpg/1280px-Crotone%2C_parco_archeologico_di_capo_colonna%2C_tempio_di_hera_lacinia%2C_01.jpg","title":"The last column at Croton","desc":"One Doric column left standing of the temple of Hera Lacinia at Capo Colonna, the great sanctuary of Croton, the city he came from. Of his own book only a few sentences survive, quoted by later writers.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crotone,_parco_archeologico_di_capo_colonna,_tempio_di_hera_lacinia,_01.jpg","alt":"A single fluted Doric column standing on a low stepped platform on a grassy headland above a flat blue sea, with scattered foundation blocks around it."}},
{"id":"gr-381","num":381,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The fullest account <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives of itself is a rock face in western Iran, and what it says there is a king's version of his own seizure of power.","questions":["Herodotus says that Darius divided <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into twenty provinces, which they call satrapies, and fixed for each of them a yearly tribute in silver or in gold.","Over 2,100 Elamite tablets from Persepolis record the food moved through one region of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with everyone in the state's employ drawing a fixed ration."],"answer":"Achaemenid Empire","answerText":"Achaemenid Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 330 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">from Ionia to Gandara</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lands claimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23, at Behistun</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> was the Persian state that ran from Ionia in the west to Gandara in the east, and the fullest account it gives of itself is cut into a rock face in western Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius put his own words there in three languages: I am Darius, the great king, the king of kings, the king of Persia, the king of the provinces, the son of Hystaspes, the grandson of Arsames, the Achaemenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lists 23 lands that came to him — Persia, Susiana, Babylonia, Assyria, Arabia, Egypt, the islands of the sea, Sparda, Ionia, Media and the rest — and says that by the grace of Auramazda they became subject and brought him tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crisis that made him king is described there in moral rather than political terms: when Cambyses had gone into Egypt the people became hostile, and the Lie multiplied in the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a king’s account of his own seizure of power, and the empire’s working papers read nothing like it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Over 2,100 clay tablets from Persepolis, written in Elamite, record the movement of food through one region in the 15 years down to 493 BCE, with everyone in the state’s employ drawing a fixed ration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus turns the same empire into a tax table, saying that Darius divided it into 20 provinces, which they call satrapies, and fixed what each should pay yearly by the talent in silver or gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that the Persians called Darius the merchant, Cambyses the master and Cyrus the father, because Darius made petty profit out of everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon, writing later still, has Cyrus send out satraps while keeping the garrison commanders answerable to nobody but himself, so that a satrap who grew insolent would meet opposition inside his own province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> And Aeschylus put the empire on an Athenian stage as a council of elders left behind at the royal abode, waiting for news of a king who does not come back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.89, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=89. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/persepolis-fortification-tablets. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i>, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=1. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 8.6.1, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=8:chapter=6:section=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["state","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Behistun_inscription_reliefs.jpg/1280px-Behistun_inscription_reliefs.jpg","title":"Darius on the rock at Behistun","desc":"The relief in the cliff face in western Iran: Darius with one foot on a prostrate rival, a line of roped captives before him, and above them the winged figure the excavators name as the god Auramazda. The panels around them carry the same text in Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian.","credit":"Photograph by Hara1603, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Behistun_inscription_reliefs.jpg","alt":"A weathered rock relief of a robed king standing with a raised hand and one foot on a fallen man, faced by a row of bound figures roped at the neck, with a winged disc above and dense panels of cuneiform around them."}},
{"id":"gr-382","num":382,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Even the words on the tomb of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reach us in two versions, one reported by Aristobulus and a quite different one by Onesicritus.","questions":["Herodotus says he could give three other accounts of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and follows the Persians who wish not to magnify the story but to tell the truth.","The Massagetae queen Tomyris filled a skin with human blood and pushed into it the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, telling the dead man she was giving him his fill of blood."],"answer":"Cyrus the Great","answerText":"Cyrus the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 559 – 530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">30 years less one, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Median rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">128 years, less the Scythians</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Cyrus the Great</b> founded the Persian empire in the middle of the 6th century BCE, and Herodotus opens by saying he could give three other versions of him, and follows the Persians who wish not to magnify the story but to tell the truth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The version he chose is a folk tale: Astyages, king of the Medes, dreams that a vine grows from his daughter and covers the whole of Asia, and orders her newborn son killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The child is not killed, is brought up among herdsmen, and in the end leads the Persians against his own grandfather, ending Median rule over Asia beyond the Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Astyages, deposed after 35 years, was kept in Cyrus’ own house until he died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says the same of the end: many stories are told of Cyrus’ death, and the one he gives is the one he finds most credible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In it he is killed fighting the Massagetae, one year short of thirty on the throne, and their queen Tomyris fills a skin with human blood and pushes his head into it, giving him, as she had promised, his fill of blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek accounts admire him even so, which is not how they usually treat a Persian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aeschylus has the ghost of Darius call him blessed in good fortune, a king who established peace for all his people and won the favour of the gods by his right-mindedness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plato says that under Cyrus the Persians kept the due measure between slavery and freedom, and so became free and then masters of many others; Xenophon made him the subject of a book on how a ruler comes to be obeyed willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even the words on his tomb reach us twice over: Aristobulus read there, O man, I am Cyrus, I established the Persian empire and was king of Asia, grudge me not therefore this monument, while Onesicritus read, I, Cyrus, king of kings, lie here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.95, 1.107–130, 1.214, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=95. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 1.1.1, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=1:chapter=1:section=1. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 15.3.7, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=15:chapter=3:section=7. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 765–772, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=759. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 694a, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=694a. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Pasargadae%2C_Ir%C3%A1n%2C_2016-09-24%2C_DD_01.jpg/1280px-Pasargadae%2C_Ir%C3%A1n%2C_2016-09-24%2C_DD_01.jpg","title":"The tomb at Pasargadae","desc":"A gabled stone chamber on six receding steps, on the plain of Pasargadae in southern Iran. Strabo reports that the chamber had been robbed by Alexander’s day, and gives two different readings of the inscription on it; the stone now carries none.","credit":"Photograph by Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pasargadae,_Ir%C3%A1n,_2016-09-24,_DD_01.jpg","alt":"A plain limestone burial chamber with a pitched roof standing on a broad plinth of six receding steps, alone on a dry plain under a deep blue sky."}},
{"id":"gr-383","num":383,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"What Cyrus did with Croesus after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives in three incompatible written versions and in one painted vase older than any of them.","questions":["Told that if he marched he would destroy a great empire, Croesus began <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and destroyed his own, having ruled 14 years and stood a siege of 14 days.","Sardis fell during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a Mardian who climbed the one stretch of the acropolis so sheer that nobody had thought to set a guard on it."],"answer":"Persian conquest of Lydia","answerText":"Persian conquest of Lydia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sardis fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 546 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">after a siege of 14 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Croesus reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Persian conquest of Lydia</b> ended the kingdom of Croesus and brought Persia to the Aegean, and Aeschylus sums it up in a line: the Lydians and Phrygians Cyrus won to his rule, and the whole of Ionia he subdued by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Croesus had sent to Delphi to ask whether he should march against the Persians, and was told that if he did he would destroy a great empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sardis fell after a siege of 14 days, taken by a Mardian who climbed the one stretch of the acropolis where no guard had been set, the stretch round which an earlier king had not carried the lion his concubine bore him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Croesus had ruled 14 years and been besieged 14 days, and Herodotus observes that in fulfilling the oracle he had destroyed his own great empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Afterwards, on Croesus’ own advice, Cyrus is said to have forbidden the Lydians weapons of war and set their sons to lyre-playing and shop-keeping, so that they should never revolt again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What Cyrus then did with Croesus survives in three incompatible versions and one picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Herodotus he is set on a pyre with twice seven Lydian boys, and when it is lit he calls on Apollo, and a storm breaks out of a clear and windless sky and puts the fire out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Bacchylides, writing a generation earlier, Croesus builds the pyre himself rather than be enslaved, Zeus quenches it, and Apollo carries him and his family off to live among the Hyperboreans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An Athenian vase painted about the same time shows him enthroned on the burning pyre in royal robes and a laurel crown, a sceptre in one hand and a libation pouring from the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> And in Xenophon there is no pyre at all: Croesus is brought before Cyrus and greets him as his sovereign lord, since fortune grants that he should bear the title henceforth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.53, 1.84, 1.86–87, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=86. [Open access]","Bacchylides, <i>Ode 3</i>, ed. and trans. Richard C. Jebb, <i>Bacchylides: The Poems and Fragments</i> (Cambridge: at the University Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/cu31924052998659. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 7.2.9, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=7:chapter=2:section=9. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 765–772, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=759. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.155, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=155. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Kroisos_stake_Louvre_G197.jpg/1280px-Kroisos_stake_Louvre_G197.jpg","title":"Croesus on the pyre, painted about 500 BCE","desc":"An Attic red-figure amphora by Myson, now in the Louvre, showing him crowned with laurel and enthroned on the burning logs, pouring a libation while an attendant sets a torch to them. It is the oldest of the surviving versions and it agrees with none of the written ones.","credit":"Amphora by Myson, photograph by Bibi Saint-Pol, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kroisos_stake_Louvre_G197.jpg","alt":"A red-figure vase painting of a bearded king in a laurel wreath seated on a throne raised on a stack of logs, holding a long sceptre and tipping a shallow bowl, while a naked youth stoops at the right to touch a torch to the wood."}},
{"id":"gr-384","num":384,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Cyrus answered the offer that opened <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a fable of a flute-player who netted the fish that would not dance to his piping, and told them to stop dancing now.","questions":["Only two cities answered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by leaving, the Phocaeans loading families, goods and even their temple statues aboard and sailing, and the Teians doing the same.","After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Bias of Priene told the Ionians to sail together to Sardinia and found one city for all of them, and they did not."],"answer":"Persian conquest of Ionia","answerText":"Persian conquest of Ionia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionia fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 545 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">to Harpagus, city by city</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cities that left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, Phocaea and Teos</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Persian conquest of Ionia</b> followed straight on the fall of Lydia, and it opened with a refusal that Cyrus answered with a fable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ionians and Aeolians offered to become his subjects on the terms they had held under Croesus, having refused him before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He told them of a flute-player who piped to the fish, and when they would not come out netted them and told them, as they leapt, to stop dancing now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The work was given to Harpagus, a Mede, who took the cities one at a time by driving the people inside their walls and raising earthworks against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aeschylus gives the result in half a line, the whole of Ionia subdued by force, and Herodotus’ summary is drier: thus Ionia was enslaved for the second time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Phocaea was the first city he attacked, and its people were the earliest Greeks to make long voyages, finding the Adriatic, Tyrrhenia, Iberia and Tartessus in fifty-oared ships and founding Marseilles, where Strabo describes temples of Ephesian Artemis and Delphian Apollo in the citadel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides has them beating a Carthaginian fleet while that foundation was going on, and the king of Tartessus, hearing that Median power was growing, gave them the money to wall their city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wall did not save it: they asked Harpagus for a day to deliberate, and while his army stood off they launched their ships and put families, goods and the statues from their temples aboard; the Teians did the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These were the only Ionians who left; the rest fought well, lost their cities and did as they were told, and Miletus had made its own treaty with Cyrus and struck no blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two ways out had been offered and neither taken: Bias of Priene, put by one account at the head of the Seven Sages, told them to sail together to Sardinia and found one city for all Ionians, and Thales had earlier urged a single council at Teos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.141, 1.162–170, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=141. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=13. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 765–772, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=759. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 4.1.4, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=4:chapter=1:section=4. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 1.82–88, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Panionium_theatre.jpg/1280px-Panionium_theatre.jpg","title":"The meeting place on Mount Mycale","desc":"Rows of rough seating on the north slope of Mount Mycale, at the site identified as the Panionion, the common sanctuary of the Ionian cities. It is where Herodotus has the Ionians assemble after the conquest and hear the advice they did not take.","credit":"Photograph by Isabeau, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panionium_theatre.jpg","alt":"Curving tiers of rough limestone seating cut into a wooded hillside, weathered and overgrown with dry grass and scrub, with pines behind."}},
{"id":"gr-385","num":385,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Aeschylus gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one flat line in the roll of Persian kings, fourth in succession, the son of Cyrus, and passes no verdict on him at all.","questions":["Herodotus has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stab the Apis calf in the thigh, and kills him at the end with his own sword falling into his thigh in the same place.","Darius cut into the rock at Behistun that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a brother Smerdis and afterwards slew him, and that the people did not know he was slain."],"answer":"Cambyses II","answerText":"Cambyses II","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 522 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">7 years and 5 months, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">conquered 525 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Cambyses II</b> was Cyrus’ son and successor and the king who took Egypt in 525 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and almost all that is told of him comes from Herodotus, who thought him mad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came back from a failed march to find Memphis keeping festival for a new Apis, took the rejoicing for mockery of his losses, and had the magistrates killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then drew his dagger on the calf, aimed at the belly and struck the thigh, and asked the priests whether their god was flesh and blood, able to feel iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that act, the Egyptians said, the madness dated: he killed his brother Smerdis on the strength of a dream, and Herodotus concludes he was insane, since only a madman derides religion and custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The end is shaped to match: springing onto his horse, he lost the cap from his scabbard and the naked blade went into his thigh, in the place where he had wounded Apis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus is no neutral hearer of Egyptian stories about him: when they claimed him as one of their own, the son of a daughter of Apries, he calls it false, saying they falsify the story to claim kinship with Cyrus’ house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persian royal record confirms one thing only: Darius cut into the rock at Behistûn that Cambyses had a brother Smerdis, of the same mother and father, that he slew him, and that the people did not know it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aeschylus gives him a single flat line in the roll of kings — fourth in succession, the son of Cyrus ruled the host — and no verdict at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plato explains him by his upbringing: Cyrus’ sons were reared over-pampered and undisciplined, so that the one killed the other and then, mad with drink, lost his throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> And Xenophon ends his own Cyrus with a deathbed speech dividing the empire between the brothers, the throne to Cambyses and a satrapy to the younger, and stops before any of it happens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.2, 3.27–38, 3.64–66, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=29. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 773, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=759. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 695b, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=695b. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 8.7.11, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=8:chapter=7:section=11. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i>, 2nd ed. (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1912), 21, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00breauoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Cambyses_apis.JPG","title":"Apis stela of Cambyses II","desc":"A round-topped stela from the Serapeum at Saqqara, dated to the sixth year of Cambyses, showing the king kneeling at the left as an Egyptian pharaoh before the Apis bull at the right, above a block of hieroglyphic text.","credit":"Photograph by Jona Lendering, livius.org, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain)","alt":"A weathered round-topped stone stela carved in low relief: at the upper left a kneeling man with arms raised faces a standing bull at the upper right, with cartouches above them and rows of hieroglyphs filling the lower half."}},
{"id":"gr-386","num":386,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The rock at Behistun carries <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>'s own account of his accession, and it ends by asking whoever reads it to believe what is written and not to hold it for lies.","questions":["Getting six fellow conspirators past the palace guards in Herodotus, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tells them that when it is necessary to lie one should lie, since liars and truth-tellers are equally after advantage.","Told that Sardis had been burnt, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shot an arrow into the sky and set a servant to say to him at every dinner, master, remember the Athenians."],"answer":"Darius I","answerText":"Darius I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 522 – 486 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">36 years, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Behistun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">carved by 510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Darius I</b> took the Persian throne on the death of Cambyses, and is the one Achaemenid king who left his own account of how he did it, cut into a cliff at Behistun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It opens: he is Darius, the great king, the king of kings, the king of Persia, the king of the provinces, the son of Hystaspes, the Achaemenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cambyses, it says, killed his brother Smerdis in secret, after which a Magian called Gaumata rebelled and lied to the people, calling himself Smerdis, the son of Cyrus, and no Persian or Mede dared take the kingdom back until he did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine more rebel kings follow, and of every one it says the same thing, that he lied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The end argues with the reader: let what he has done be believed and not held for lies, let a later king beware of lies and destroy the liar, for he was not a liar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The carving is too high to be read from the road, so copies were sent into all lands, and fragments of an Aramaic one survive among the papyri of the Jewish garrison at Elephantine in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus gives the same skeleton other flesh: seven conspirators kill the Magian and then debate how to be governed, Darius arguing that nothing is better than the rule of the one best man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He knows the debate is doubted and insists on it: sentiments were uttered there which some Greeks find incredible, but there is no doubt they were spoken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His Darius is untroubled by the Lie: to get the seven past the guards he says that when it is necessary to lie one should lie, liars and truth-tellers being equally after advantage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The throne comes to him by a groom's trick, a mare tethered overnight so that his horse would neigh first at sunrise, while Plato says he was no king's son and an Athenian chorus in Aeschylus remembers a god-like king who did no wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.72, 3.80–87, 5.105, 7.4, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=72. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 852–856, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=852. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 695c–d, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=695c. [Open access]","A. Cowley, <i>Aramaic Papyri of the Fifth Century B.C.</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1923), 248–49, https://archive.org/details/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Behistun_relief.jpg/1280px-Behistun_relief.jpg","title":"The relief at Behistun","desc":"The rock-cut relief above the inscription: Darius stands at the left with one foot on the prostrate Gaumata and a hand raised to the winged figure of Ahuramazda, while the defeated rebel kings are led before him roped together at the neck, with panels of cuneiform around and below.","credit":"Photograph by Patrick C (dynamomosquito on Flickr), via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 2.0)","alt":"A weathered rock-cut relief: at the left a bearded king in a long robe raises one hand towards a winged disc carrying a human figure and rests a foot on a man lying beneath him, while a line of bound men in varied headgear stands before him roped together at the neck, with blocks of cuneiform writing filling the spaces around and beneath."}},
{"id":"gr-387","num":387,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Xenophon says the governor of a Persian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to keep up horsemen and archers, feed the garrisons, and answer at a yearly review for the state of the land itself.","questions":["Oroetes governed the Phrygian, Lydian and Ionian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Sardis with a thousand spearmen of his own, and Darius thought it unsafe to send an army openly against him.","Cyrus the Younger held Lydia as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> along with command of everyone who mustered in the plain of Castolus, so that the office joined a whole province to an army."],"answer":"Satrapy","answerText":"Satrapy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 330 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Provinces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"A <b>satrapy</b> was a province of the Achaemenid Empire and its governor a satrap, a word Darius uses in Old Persian at Behistun of Dadarshish, whom he calls his servant and satrap in Bactria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon sets out what it required: a governor kept up a fixed number of horsemen, archers, slingers and light infantry, and fed the garrisons in the citadels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Once a year the troops came to the place of muster, the king reviewing those near his residence and sending trusted agents to the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The land was judged the same way, so a governor whose province was populous and well cultivated gained more territory, presents and a seat of honour, while one whose lay untilled was punished and replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In principle the two duties went to separate officers, one over the residents and the tribute and one over the troops, but Xenophon adds that a satrap attends to both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That made a satrapy a province with an army inside it, and Cyrus the Younger held his with command of everyone who mustered in the plain of Castolus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What held such a thing together was paper: travellers drew rations at stations a day apart against a sealed document, and the Persepolis tablets record Artaphernes, satrap of Sardis, authorising two journeys at the empire's far end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It could also fail, as Herodotus' Oroetes shows: governing the Phrygian, Lydian and Ionian province from Sardis with a guard of a thousand spearmen, he killed a fellow governor and ambushed a messenger Darius had sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Darius judged it unsafe to send an army openly against a satrap while still new to the throne, and asked which Persian would deal with him by cunning rather than force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thirty volunteered; the lot fell to Bagaeus, who came to Sardis with sealed letters that a royal scribe read out one by one, until the guards lowered their spears at one forbidding them to guard Oroetes, and killed him at the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Oeconomicus</i> 4.5–11, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0212:text=Ec.:chapter=4:section=5. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.126–128, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=127. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 6, https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/persepolis-fortification-tablets. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Anabasis</i> 1.1.2, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0202:book=1:chapter=1:section=2. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","geography","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Istanbul_Archaeological_Museum_Satrap_Sarcophagus_Long_side_Satrap_about_to_kill_a_panther_022.jpg/1280px-Istanbul_Archaeological_Museum_Satrap_Sarcophagus_Long_side_Satrap_about_to_kill_a_panther_022.jpg","title":"The Satrap Sarcophagus","desc":"A long side of the marble sarcophagus from the royal necropolis at Sidon, now in Istanbul, which is named from the figure carved on it rather than from any inscription: he rides down a panther among other horsemen, between borders of egg-and-dart and palmette.","credit":"Photograph by Dosseman, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A carved marble frieze in low relief: a rider with his arm raised behind him drives a rearing horse to the left, a panther runs between the horses at the centre, and a second rider approaches from the right, with bands of egg-and-dart and palmette ornament above and below."}},
{"id":"gr-388","num":388,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus counted 111 posting stations along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between Sardis and Susa, and made it 450 parasangs, or 90 days of walking for a man on foot.","questions":["Asked by Cleomenes how far it was to the king along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Aristagoras of Miletus answered truthfully that it was three months inland, and was told to leave Sparta before sunset.","A man and a horse waited at every day's interval along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that a letter passed from hand to hand, stopped by neither snow nor rain nor heat nor darkness."],"answer":"Royal Road","answerText":"Royal Road","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">111, Sardis to Susa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On foot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">90 days, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Royal Road</b> ran from Sardis to Susa, and Herodotus counted it out: 111 posting stations, 450 parasangs, 13,500 stades, and 90 days of walking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It kept to inhabited and guarded country, with a defile and a fortress at the Halys, two more on the Cilician frontier, and ferries over four navigable rivers, three of them called the Tigris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Adding the stretch from Ephesus to Sardis made 14,040 stades and three days more, and Herodotus works that sum in order to check a story he has already told.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In it Aristagoras of Miletus, asking Sparta to march inland against the king, was asked by Cleomenes how far it was and made the mistake of telling the truth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three months, he said, and Cleomenes told him to be out of Sparta before sunset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Along the same stations ran the courier post, the angareion, with a man and a horse standing at each day's interval, and Herodotus says nothing mortal travels faster, stopped by neither snow nor rain nor heat nor darkness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon credits Cyrus with the invention, saying he measured how far a horse could be ridden hard in one day, set his stations at that distance, and put an officer at each to take in spent riders and send on fresh ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The system's own paperwork survives, since the Persepolis tablets record travellers drawing a day's rations at each station, and express horses kept permanently at the posts for the fast messengers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Read against those, Herodotus' single rider for a whole day's journey looks slow, and the horses were more likely changed at every station, some 30 km (20 miles) apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Armies used the road as well as letters, since Xenophon's Cyrus set out from Sardis and made the Maeander in three stages and 22 parasangs, and Darius' inscription has him send a messenger down it to his satrap in Bactria with orders to march.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.50, 5.52–54, 8.98, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=52. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 8.6.17–18, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=8:chapter=6:section=17. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 6, https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/persepolis-fortification-tablets. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Anabasis</i> 1.2.5, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0202:book=1:chapter=2:section=5. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","geography","technology","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/PTF_1208-101.jpg/1280px-PTF_1208-101.jpg","title":"An Achaemenid administrative tablet","desc":"A clay tablet of the Achaemenid administration, its face covered with lines of cuneiform. Thousands of such tablets survive from Persepolis, and among them are the day-by-day records of the rations issued to travellers along the empire's roads.","credit":"Photograph by اردشیر خسروی, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A palm-sized slab of reddish baked clay, rounded on one side and broken on the other, its whole face ruled into about a dozen lines of wedge-shaped cuneiform signs."}},
{"id":"gr-389","num":389,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus counted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by penning 10,000 men behind a low wall and filling it again until all had passed through, then doubted his own figure of 1,700,000.","questions":["Asking after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the chorus of Aeschylus wonders whether the drawing of the bow has triumphed or the might of the sharp spear-head has prevailed.","At Plataea the men of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> threw away their bows, fought at a fence of shields and broke the Spartan spears short, and Herodotus says they lacked armour rather than courage."],"answer":"Persian army","answerText":"Persian army","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Land force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,700,000, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Counted by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">pens of 10,000 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">beaten in 479 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"abstract":"The <b>Persian army</b> that invaded Greece was a coalition, and Herodotus dresses its Persian contingent in soft caps called tiaras and tunics scaled with iron like a fish, carrying wicker shields, short spears, long bows and reed arrows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gives the land force as 1,700,000, counted by packing 10,000 men together, drawing a line round them, building a low wall on it, and filling the pen again until all had passed through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then doubts it, reckoning that a choenix of wheat a day for each man would take more than a million bushels, and wondering how they were fed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon, who met such an army at Cunaxa, has it marching in national divisions, each a solid square, with white-cuirassed horsemen, wicker shields, Egyptians behind wooden ones reaching to their feet, and scythed chariots in front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo says its men were made from the age of five, taught to ride, to shoot and to speak the truth, run in companies of fifty, and hardened to heat, cold and rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Greeks thought of that army as archers, and Aeschylus asked an Athenian audience outright: has the drawing of the bow triumphed, or the might of the sharp spear-head?<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the same play Atossa asks whether the bow suits Greek hands, and is told far from it: they have lances for close fight and shields that serve for armour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Plataea the test came: the Persians threw away their bows to meet the charge, fought at a fence of shields and then hand to hand, laying hold of the Spartan spears and breaking them short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus' verdict is not that they lacked courage: they were neither less valorous nor weaker, he says, but had no armour and fought as though naked against men fully armed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius counts differently, his inscription saying only that after he became king he fought nineteen battles and took nine kings, so that the empire's own war record numbers battles rather than soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.60–61, 7.187, 9.62–63, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=60. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 147–149, 239–240, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=140. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Anabasis</i> 1.8.9–10, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0202:book=1:chapter=8:section=9. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 15.3.18, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=15:chapter=3:section=18. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Persepolis_The_Persian_Soldiers.jpg/1280px-Persepolis_The_Persian_Soldiers.jpg","title":"Guardsmen on the Apadana stairs","desc":"A relief from the Apadana at Persepolis showing files of guardsmen in Persian and Median dress, the Medes in rounded caps and boots, each with a long spear and some with a round shield, beneath a winged disc flanked by sphinxes.","credit":"Photograph by Arad, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)","alt":"A carved stone relief of six bearded men standing in file, each holding an upright spear taller than himself; three wear tall fluted headdresses and long pleated robes and the others rounded caps and trousers, two of them carrying large round shields, with a winged disc and sphinxes on the band above."}},
{"id":"gr-390","num":390,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says the corps called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was held at exactly ten thousand men, another being chosen whenever one of them fell out through death or sickness.","questions":["Sent in at Thermopylae once the Medes had been roughly handled, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fared no better, their spears being shorter than the Greek ones in a pass too narrow for numbers.","Mardonius kept the whole corps of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for his army in Greece after Salamis, all but their general Hydarnes, who would not quit the king's person."],"answer":"Immortals","answerText":"Immortals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Strength</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thermopylae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">sent in, 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">After Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">kept in Greece, 479 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Immortals</b> are known from Herodotus, who says ten thousand picked Persians served under Hydarnes son of Hydarnes and explains the name himself: whenever one fell out through death or sickness another was chosen, so they were never more nor fewer than ten thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were the best men in the army and the most richly adorned, standing out by the gold they wore, and their food came on their own camels, apart from the rest of the host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the march out of Sardis they framed the king, a thousand of them carrying golden pomegranates on their spear-shafts instead of a spike and surrounding the nine thousand who carried silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon knows a body of the same size without the name, saying Cyrus took ten thousand spearmen who guarded the palace day and night and went drawn up on either side of him when he travelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo describes what such a man carried: a rhomb-shaped shield, a quiver, a battle-axe and a short sword, a cap rising like a tower, and a breastplate of iron scales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> At Thermopylae Xerxes sent them in when the Medes had been roughly handled, and it was thought they would easily accomplish the task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They fared neither better nor worse than the Medes, since their spears were shorter than the Greek ones and their numbers no use in a narrow space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What opened the pass was not the corps but a path, and Hydarnes led them out of camp at lamp-lighting time to take it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Salamis Mardonius chose the whole corps for the army he kept in Greece, all but Hydarnes himself, who said that he would not quit the king's person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two other records stand beside that Greek name: Darius' inscription has a Hydarnes leading an army for him a generation earlier, and Aeschylus, never using the name, has the king's choicest troops, foremost in loyalty, killed to the last man on an islet off Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.41, 7.83, 7.211, 7.215, 8.113, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=83. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 7.5.68, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=7:chapter=5:section=68. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 441–464, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=441. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 15.3.19, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=15:chapter=3:section=19. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Archers_frieze_Louvre_AOD488.jpg/1280px-Archers_frieze_Louvre_AOD488.jpg","title":"The archers frieze from Susa","desc":"Glazed brick spearmen from the palace of Darius at Susa, now in the Louvre, each with a bow and quiver slung at his back. Friezes of this kind are often labelled as the Immortals, though nothing on them carries that name, which is Greek and comes from Herodotus.","credit":"Photograph by Jastrow, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain)","alt":"A wall of turquoise glazed bricks beneath a palmette border, moulded in low relief with four bearded men in file wearing long patterned robes, each holding an upright spear in both hands and carrying a bow and a quiver slung over the shoulder."}},
{"id":"gr-391","num":391,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Darius' <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened with a bridge over the Bosphorus by Mandrocles of Samos and two marble pillars lettered in Assyrian and in Greek with the nations of his army.","questions":["During Darius' <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Scythian kings sent him a bird, a mouse, a frog and five arrows, which he read as a surrender and Gobryas read as a threat.","Left to guard the Danube bridge during Darius' <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Ionian tyrants were told by Histiaeus that each of them held his own city by the king's power alone."],"answer":"Scythian campaign","answerText":"Scythian campaign","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 513 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700,000, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left in Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Megabazus, 80,000 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"Darius' <b>Scythian campaign</b> took a Persian army across the Bosphorus on a bridge by Mandrocles of Samos, and Herodotus says the king set two marble pillars beside it, lettered in Assyrian and in Greek with the names of every nation in his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His census gave 700,000 men besides 600 ships, a figure Justin repeats, and the Byzantines later carried the pillars off to build an altar of Artemis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the Danube he tied sixty knots in a thong and left it with the Ionian tyrants guarding the bridge, to untie one a day and sail home if the knots ran out before he returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Scythians would not fight but withdrew before him, and Strabo says he found himself in danger of being cut off in a waterless plain, and turned back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their kings sent him a bird, a mouse, a frog and five arrows, which Darius read as a surrender of earth and water and Gobryas as a warning that unless the Persians turned into birds or mice or frogs they would not get home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The knots ran out, and the Scythians came to the bridge and told the Ionians to break it and be free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miltiades the Athenian was for doing it, but Histiaeus of Miletus answered that each of them held his city by Darius' power and would lose it if that power fell, and the rest came round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin gives the war a different cause altogether, a Scythian king's daughter refused in marriage, and says the losses out of so vast a number were scarcely accounted a disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Darius' own monument records a different Scythian war, one he won, crossing the water, killing many, taking their leader Skunkha prisoner and setting another over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What it left was Megabazus in Europe with 80,000 men, and Aeschylus later set on an Athenian stage the ghost of the man who bridged the Bosphorus, condemning his son for shackling a strait like a slave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.87–89, 4.98, 4.131–132, 4.137, 4.143, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=4:chapter=87. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.3.14, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=7:chapter=3:section=14. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.5, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 23, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 745–752, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=739. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Behistun_relief_Skunkha.jpg/500px-Behistun_relief_Skunkha.jpg","title":"Skunkha at the end of the line","desc":"The last of the bound captives on the Behistun relief, in the tall pointed cap of the Saka, with a cuneiform label cut above his head. His figure was added to the rock after the rest, for a Scythian war that Darius did commemorate.","credit":"Photograph by Leen van Dorp, livius.org, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0)","alt":"A tall narrow detail of a weathered rock relief: a bearded man in a very high pointed cap stands with his hands bound behind him and a rope at his neck, with lines of cuneiform cut in the rock above him."}},
{"id":"gr-392","num":392,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Carrying a bronze tablet engraved with a map of all the earth, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pointed along it as far as Susa and asked Sparta to free the Ionians from the Persian king.","questions":["Athens voted twenty ships after hearing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Herodotus remarks that it is easier to deceive many than one, since Cleomenes alone had sent him away.","Having raised Ionia against Darius, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed Miletus to another man, sailed for Thrace and was killed besieging a town whose people were ready to leave under treaty."],"answer":"Aristagoras","answerText":"Aristagoras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">raised in 499 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Sparta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">offered 50 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 497 BCE, in Thrace</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Aristagoras</b> was deputy tyrant of Miletus, a city Strabo describes with four harbours, one of them able to take a fleet, and with colonies peopling the whole Black Sea and the Propontis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He promised the Persians Naxos, quarrelled with the commander of the fleet sent to take it, failed, and then raised Ionia in revolt against the king who had made him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He went to Sparta carrying a bronze tablet engraved with a map of all the earth, the sea and the rivers, and pointed along it through Lydia, Phrygia, Cappadocia, Cilicia and Armenia to Susa, where the great king kept his storehouses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The map came out of his own city, Anaximander of Miletus being remembered as the first to draw the outline of land and sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His argument was that the Persians carried bows and short spears and went to battle in trousers, so that Sparta might have all Asia instead of fighting Argives who had no gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Cleomenes asked how far it was to the king, and on being told three months sent him away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came back in a suppliant's garb and offered ten talents, then more, then fifty, until Cleomenes' daughter Gorgo, eight or nine years old, said the stranger would corrupt her father unless he left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens heard the same speech and voted twenty ships, which Herodotus calls the beginning of troubles for Greeks and foreigners alike, remarking that it is easier to deceive many than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides notes that the islands had been left for Darius and the Phoenician fleet, and Justin runs the sequence to its end: the Ionians beaten at sea, and Darius turning his whole fury on Athens for having helped them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristagoras did not see it out, for Herodotus says he showed little courage, handed Miletus to another man, sailed for Thrace and was killed there besieging a town whose people were ready to leave under treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.30–38, 5.49–51, 5.97, 5.124–126, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=49. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 2.1–2, trans. R. D. Hicks, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), 1:131, https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=6. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.5, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 23, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.16, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=16. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Miletus_-_Ancient_Greek_theatre_02.jpg/1280px-Miletus_-_Ancient_Greek_theatre_02.jpg","title":"The theatre at Miletus","desc":"The standing theatre on the site of Miletus in Turkey. Strabo describes the city with four harbours, one of which would take a fleet; the site now lies among fields, with no sea in sight.","credit":"Photograph by Bernard Gagnon, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)","alt":"A large ruined theatre of pale stone on a green plain: a high wall with an arched tunnel entrance at the left, tiers of broken seating curving away to the right, and fallen blocks scattered over the grass in front."}},
{"id":"gr-393","num":393,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"To reach Aristagoras while the roads were watched, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shaved the head of a trusted slave, marked it, and waited for the hair to grow before sending him to Miletus.","questions":["Darius wrote to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the most precious of possessions is a wise and loyal friend, and invited him to leave Miletus and share the royal table and counsels at Susa.","Artaphernes told <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Sardis that he had stitched the shoe which Aristagoras then put on, and later sent his embalmed head to the king at Susa."],"answer":"Histiaeus","answerText":"Histiaeus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Message sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 499 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At the bridge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Danube, c. 513 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 493 BCE, impaled</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Histiaeus</b> was tyrant of Miletus, a city Strabo gives four harbours and colonies throughout the Black Sea, and he had saved Darius' bridge over the Danube by arguing that every Ionian tyrant held his city by the king's power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius rewarded him with Myrcinus in Thrace, then, warned that a clever Greek in a country of timber and silver was a danger, wrote him a letter of great warmth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There was nothing he longed for so much as to see him, the king said, the most precious of possessions being a wise and loyal friend, and he invited him to leave Miletus and share his table and counsels at Susa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was an honour that could not be refused and could not be left, and he stayed disliking his detention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So he shaved the head of his most trusted slave, marked it, waited for the hair to grow, and sent the man to Aristagoras with no message except that he should shave the man and look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus says he did it because the roads were guarded, and Aeneas Tacticus, in a manual of siegecraft of the next century, gives the same reason, no letter-carrier being able to pass unnoticed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aulus Gellius adds what the Greek accounts do not, that the slave had long had bad eyes, so the shaving could pass for a cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the marks said was revolt, and he reckoned that if Ionia rose he would be sent down to the coast to settle it, while if Miletus stayed quiet he would never see it again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was sent, and Artaphernes met him at Sardis with the whole thing understood: you stitched this shoe, he said, and Aristagoras put it on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin ends the revolt at sea, and Histiaeus in flight, taken by Harpagus and impaled by Artaphernes, who sent his head embalmed to Susa, where Darius rebuked them for not bringing him alive and had it washed and buried with honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.137, 5.11, 5.23–24, 5.35, 6.1, 6.30, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=35. [Open access]","Aeneas Tacticus, <i>On the Defence of Fortified Positions</i> 31.28–29, trans. members of the Illinois Greek Club, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1923), 171, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.43525. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=6. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>The Attic Nights</i> 17.9, trans. William Beloe (London: J. Johnson, 1795), 3:287, https://archive.org/details/atticnights03gelluoft. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.5, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 23, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Temple_of_Artemis_Sardis_Turkey.jpg/1280px-Temple_of_Artemis_Sardis_Turkey.jpg","title":"Sardis","desc":"The ruins of the temple of Artemis on the site of Sardis in Turkey. Sardis was the seat of Artaphernes, who told Histiaeus that he had stitched the shoe Aristagoras put on, and who had him impaled there and his head sent to Susa.","credit":"Photograph by Simon Jenkins, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 2.0)","alt":"Two tall Ionic columns still standing among fallen column drums and block foundations on a grassy archaeological site, with eroded pale hillsides and green ridges rising behind under a clear sky."}},
{"id":"gr-394","num":394,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began when Aristagoras made a pretence of giving up his own tyranny at Miletus and then put down the tyrants the Persians had installed elsewhere.","questions":["To finish the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Persians netted the islands, their men linking hands in a line from one sea to the other and walking each island through to hunt the people down.","Nepos says Darius gave as his reason for invading Greece that during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Ionians had stormed Sardis with Athenian help and killed his garrison there."],"answer":"Ionian Revolt","answerText":"Ionian Revolt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 – 493 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athens sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 ships, Eretria 5</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">tribute fixed, 493 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Ionian Revolt</b> was the rising of the Greek cities of Asia against Darius, and it began with Aristagoras making a pretence of giving up his own tyranny at Miletus and granting the city equality of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He did the same through the rest of Ionia, banishing some of the tyrants the Persians had installed and handing others over to the cities they had governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens sent twenty ships and Eretria five, the allies marched inland and burnt Sardis, and the Athenians then went home and would not come back however he begged them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It spread far wider than Ionia, to Cyprus, to the Hellespont and to Caria, where an ambush laid on the road at Pedasus destroyed a Persian army by night with three of its generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides sets out what was at stake, the coastal cities having been reduced by Cyrus and the islands left for Darius and the Phoenician fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Persians took the cities back one at a time, and once Miletus had fallen they netted the islands, the men linking hands in a line from the northern sea to the southern and walking the whole island through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Artaphernes then measured the land by parasangs, fixed each city's tribute at about what it had paid before, and made the Ionians agree to settle their quarrels by law instead of by robbing one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mardonius went further the next spring, deposing every Ionian tyrant and setting up democracies in their cities, which Herodotus records for the wonder of Greeks who doubt that a Persian ever argued for democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Artaphernes is also one of the few men in this story to appear in a Persian document, authorising travellers' rations twice in the tablets from Persepolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Justin runs the war to its end at sea, and Nepos says Darius gave as his reason for invading Greece that the Ionians had stormed Sardis with Athenian help and killed his garrison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.37, 5.99–103, 5.121, 6.31, 6.42–43, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=37. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 4, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 311, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.5, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 23, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.16, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=16. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 6, https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/persepolis-fortification-tablets. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Reliefs_in_Persepolis_%D9%86%DA%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%87_%D9%87%D8%A7%DB%8C_%D8%AA%D8%AE%D8%AA_%D8%AC%D9%85%D8%B4%DB%8C%D8%AF_09.jpg/1280px-Reliefs_in_Persepolis_%D9%86%DA%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%87_%D9%87%D8%A7%DB%8C_%D8%AA%D8%AE%D8%AA_%D8%AC%D9%85%D8%B4%DB%8C%D8%AF_09.jpg","title":"A delegation on the Apadana stairs","desc":"One of the files of subject peoples carved on the great stairway of the Apadana at Persepolis: five men in long robes and soft caps carry bowls, folded cloth and stacked vessels towards the king, walking past a stylised tree between borders of rosettes.","credit":"Photograph by Mostafa Meraji, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A grey stone relief of five bearded men in long robes and soft round caps walking in file to the left, carrying shallow bowls, folded cloth and a stack of vessels, past a tall stylised tree, with bands of carved rosettes above and below."}},
{"id":"gr-395","num":395,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says one soldier's fire caught the reed roofs and spread house to house, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed a city the Ionians had entered unopposed.","questions":["The Persians later made the loss of Cybebe's temple in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> their pretext for burning the temples of Greece, and Athens' twenty ships were long remembered for it.","Charon of Lampsacus said only that the Athenians took everything but the king's wall, a far quieter account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than the one Herodotus gives."],"answer":"burning of Sardis","answerText":"burning of Sardis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">498 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionian Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 – 493 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athens sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 ships, Eretria 5</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>burning of Sardis</b> in 498 BCE was the most famous stroke of the Ionian Revolt, and in the long run it did the Greeks more harm than good. Sardis had been the capital of the Lydian kings and was now the seat of Persian power in western Anatolia, the far end of the Royal Road and the residence of Artaphernes, satrap of Lydia and brother of Darius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo describes a large city with a strong citadel below a mountain whose stream once carried down the gold dust behind the proverbial wealth of Croesus, and Aeschylus a generation later calls the place 'rich in gold'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Athens sent twenty ships and Eretria five, the Eretrians repaying an old debt to Miletus rather than doing Athens a favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristagoras stayed at Miletus and gave the command to his brother Charopinus and a citizen named Hermophantus, and the force left its ships near Ephesus and marched inland over Tmolus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They took the lower city without a fight, but Artaphernes held the citadel with a large garrison, though a Roman account has the Ionians storming Sardis and putting the garrison to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plunder was impossible because most houses were built of reeds and even the brick ones were roofed with them, so when a soldier set one alight the fire ran from roof to roof across the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lydians and Persians driven from the burning streets massed in the marketplace by the Pactolus and turned to fight, and the Ionians withdrew to Tmolus and reached their ships after dark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Persian troops caught the retreating army at Ephesus and routed it, Athens quit the war for good, and a temple of the goddess Cybebe that had burned became the stated Persian pretext for burning Greek temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charon of Lampsacus, an earlier writer, recorded only that the Athenians took everything but the king's wall and went back to Miletus, which is a smaller and quieter event than the one Herodotus describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.25, 5.52, 5.99–103, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=101. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 13.4.5, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=13:chapter=4:section=5. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Of Herodotus's Malice</i> 24, in <i>Plutarch's Morals</i>, ed. William W. Goodwin, vol. 4 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1878), 344–45, https://archive.org/details/plutarchsmorals04plut. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 4, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 311, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 45–52, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=41. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran","anatolia"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Sardis_citadel.jpg/1280px-Sardis_citadel.jpg","title":"The citadel of Sardis","desc":"A stretch of fortification wall still standing on the citadel ridge at Sardis, above the plain of the Hermus. Herodotus says the Ionians took the town below but never this height, which Artaphernes held with a large garrison while the houses burned.","credit":"Photograph by Ken Mayer, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0)","alt":"A broken stretch of stone wall standing on a scrub-covered ridge, its courses of masonry ending raggedly at both sides, with a wide hazy valley of fields and low hills falling away behind under a pale sky."}},
{"id":"gr-396","num":396,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus explains the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the ruin of a fleet that mutinied after seven days of drill under the sun and sat in the shade rather than row.","questions":["Of the 353 Ionian triremes drawn up for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Samians sailed away but for eleven, and the Chians broke the enemy line and lost most of their ships.","Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Persians offered the crews safety for deserting and enslavement for fighting, and Herodotus says afterwards that the Ionians all blamed each other."],"answer":"Battle of Lade","answerText":"Battle of Lade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionian fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">353 triremes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 ships</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Lade</b> in 494 BCE ended the Ionian Revolt at sea, and Herodotus tells it as a story about an alliance that came apart before the fighting began. Meeting at the Panionium, the Ionians raised no land army but left Miletus to defend its own walls, manning every ship in haste and gathering at Lade, a small island off the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus lists the contingents city by city, from the Milesians' 80 ships on the eastern wing to the Samians' 60 on the western, 353 triremes in all against a Persian fleet of 600.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides had already recorded that Ionia was a real sea power under Cyrus and Cambyses, and that the Phocaeans beat a Carthaginian fleet while they were founding Marseilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persian commanders, doubting they could win, sent the exiled Ionian tyrants among the crews with an offer and a threat: desert and nothing would be burnt, fight and lose and be enslaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius of Phocaea, who had brought three ships, was given command and drilled the fleet in rowing through the enemy line, keeping the crews at work all day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They obeyed for seven days, then, unused to the labour and worn by the sun, refused to go on, pitched tents on the island and sat in the shade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seeing the disorder, the Samians took an offer from their own exiled tyrant and sailed off when the Phoenicians came on, all but 11 ships whose captains stayed and were named on a pillar at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Lesbians followed and most of the rest with them, and the Chians, 100 ships with 40 picked marines apiece, broke the enemy line and took many ships before losing most of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus refuses to say who was brave and who was not, since the Ionians all blamed each other, and the sea they fought over is now a plain, the Maeander having filled the gulf between Priene and Miletus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Lade_1.jpg/1280px-Lade_1.jpg","title":"The island of Lade today","desc":"The hill that was once the island of Lade, standing above cotton fields near Miletus. The gulf the two fleets fought in has been filled by the Maeander, a process Pausanias already describes as under way in his own day.","credit":"Photograph by O. Mustafin, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0)","alt":"A long low wooded hill with scattered white houses and a slender minaret rising from a wide flat field of green crops under a clear blue sky."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.7–6.17, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=14. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=13. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 10.25, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=10:chapter=25. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.7, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=7. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.24.11, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=8:chapter=24:section=11. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","event","history","warfare","greece","iran"]},
{"id":"gr-397","num":397,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says the Persians mined the walls and took the city in the sixth year of the revolt, and after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the survivors were settled by Darius on the Tigris.","questions":["An oracle had promised that the city would become a banquet and glorious gifts for many, which Herodotus reckoned fulfilled by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the burning of Didyma.","Greek writers blamed luxury and faction rather than siegecraft for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a proverb ran that Milesians had once been brave."],"answer":"sack of Miletus","answerText":"sack of Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th year of the revolt</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 – 493 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>sack of Miletus</b> in 494 BCE ended the Ionian Revolt on land, and the Greeks remembered it less as a defeat than as a city being taken off the map. Miletus had been the greatest of the Ionian cities, and Ephorus credited its people with beating the Scythians and planting colonies all round the Hellespont and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greek writers explained its fall by luxury and faction rather than by siegecraft, and Heraclides of Pontus tells how the people trampled their enemies' children under oxen and the rich later burnt their enemies alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A proverb ran that Milesians had once been brave, and Aristotle is quoted for the judgement that pleasure had dissolved everything valiant in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Lade the Persians besieged the city by sea and by land, mined its walls and used every device against it, taking it in the sixth year from Aristagoras' revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the men were killed, the women and children were made slaves, and Herodotus says the calamity matched an oracle that had promised Miletus would become a banquet and glorious gifts for many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The captives were taken to Susa and settled by Darius at Ampe, near the mouth of the Tigris, while the Persians kept the plain and gave the hill country to Carians from Pedasa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus writes that Miletus was left empty of Milesians, and those who escaped sailed with the Samians to Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oracle of Apollo at Didyma was plundered and burnt, though Strabo puts that at the order of Xerxes and says the priestly Branchidae gave up the god's treasure and fled with the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias makes it Xerxes as well, who took the bronze Apollo that Seleucus later sent back, while a charcoal layer dug on the Taxiarchis hill at Didyma fits either date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Miletus_Delphinion_in_2015_3355.jpg/1280px-Miletus_Delphinion_in_2015_3355.jpg","title":"The Delphinion at Miletus","desc":"The sanctuary of Apollo Delphinios in the centre of Miletus, now an empty rectangle of scattered marble in the grass. Herodotus ends his account of the siege by writing that Miletus was left empty of Milesians.","credit":"Photograph by Dosseman, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A wide flat archaeological enclosure of grass and low scrub, edged and crossed by scattered blocks and slabs of white marble, with dry hills and a hazy mountain range beyond."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.18–6.22, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=19. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.5, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=5. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.16.3, 8.46.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=8:chapter=46:section=3. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>Deipnosophists</i> 12.26, trans. C. D. Yonge, attalus.org, https://www.attalus.org/old/athenaeus12a.html. [Open access]","Udo Schlotzhauer, review of <i>Der archaische Heiligtumsbefund vom Taxiarchis-Hügel in Didyma</i>, by Helga Bumke, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.04/. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran","anatolia"]},
{"id":"gr-398","num":398,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Athens fined <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a thousand drachmas and forbade his play for ever, because a tragedy on the fall of Miletus had set the whole theatre weeping.","questions":["Herodotus and Strabo give different titles to the lost tragedy by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which nothing else survives, and Callisthenes is Strabo's authority for the fine.","In Aristophanes' <i>Wasps</i> old jurymen hum the sweet songs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose tragic victory of 476 BCE Themistocles paid for as choregos."],"answer":"Phrynichus","answerText":"Phrynichus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Miletus fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000 drachmas</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Later victory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">476 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Phrynichus</b> was an Athenian tragic poet of the early 5th century BCE, and he is remembered chiefly for one lost play that cost him a fine and was forbidden for ever. Soon after Miletus fell to the Persians in 494 BCE he put a tragedy on that disaster before an Athenian audience, and Herodotus says the whole theatre fell to weeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They fined him a thousand drachmas for reminding them of a calamity that touched them so closely, and forbade anyone ever to stage the play again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing of it survives but the title, which Herodotus gives as <i>The Fall of Miletus</i> and Strabo, citing Callisthenes, as <i>The Taking of Miletus by Darius</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus sets that grief against the Sybarites, who made no such return although every Milesian, young and old, had shaved his head in mourning when Sybaris was taken by Croton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus is precise about the ground of the fine: not that the play was bad, but that it recalled a calamity the Athenians felt as their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither the fine nor the ban ended his career, and he won the tragic prize in 476 BCE, with Themistocles paying as his choregos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch quotes the tablet Themistocles set up to record it, which names the choregos, the poet and the archon of the year and nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aeschylus later staged the <i>Persians</i>, a tragedy on the same war set in Susa among Persian elders waiting for news, so that the grief on stage was the enemy's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens went on singing Phrynichus long afterwards: in Aristophanes' <i>Wasps</i> the old jurymen come through the streets before dawn with lanterns, humming the sweet old songs of his <i>Sidonian Women</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Athen_Theatre_of_Dionysus_BW_2017-10-09_14-29-49.jpg/1280px-Athen_Theatre_of_Dionysus_BW_2017-10-09_14-29-49.jpg","title":"The theatre of Dionysus","desc":"The theatre of Dionysus below the Acropolis at Athens, where the tragedies were staged. What stands is later work in stone; the theatre that wept at Phrynichus' play was largely wood.","credit":"Photograph by Berthold Werner, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)","alt":"A ruined open-air theatre seen from above: a paved semicircular orchestra at the centre, curved tiers of broken stone seating rising around it, and a scatter of fallen blocks and dark trees behind."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.21, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=21. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.7, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 5.4, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=5. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 1–25, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=1. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 219–220, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr., Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0044:card=220. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","people","history","literature","greece","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-399","num":399,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The fleet was wrecked rounding Athos and the Brygi wounded its general by night, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned back without reaching Athens or Eretria.","questions":["Herodotus records that at the start of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Ionian tyrants were deposed and democracies set up, which he tells for the wonder of Greeks who doubt a Persian argued for democracy.","About 300 ships and more than 20,000 men were lost on Athos during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some drowned, some frozen and some, Herodotus says, taken by wild beasts."],"answer":"Mardonius' campaign","answerText":"Mardonius' campaign","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">492 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 300</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Men lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Mardonius' campaign</b> of 492 BCE was the first Persian expedition sent against Athens and Eretria, and it turned back within the year without reaching either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius replaced his other generals and gave it to Mardonius son of Gobryas, a young man lately married to the king's daughter Artozostre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sailing along Ionia he did something Herodotus records for the wonder of Greeks who doubt a Persian ever argued for democracy: he deposed the Ionian tyrants and set up democracies in their cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army crossed the Hellespont and marched through Europe, the fleet took Thasos without a blow, and Macedonia was added to the subject peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin has Persia already bound to the Macedonian house a generation earlier, when Megabazus demanded hostages of Amyntas and Bubares married Amyntas' daughter instead of fighting him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> From Acanthus, which Strabo places on the isthmus of Athos and Pliny on the far side of it, the fleet put out to round the promontory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Acte runs a long way into the Aegean and ends in a high mountain looking towards it, and Thucydides describes the small towns along it, peopled by mixed races speaking two languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A north wind, great and irresistible, caught the ships as they passed and drove many of them onto Athos, and Herodotus reports about 300 ships lost and more than 20,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some were dashed on the rocks, some drowned, some died of cold and some, he says, were carried off by the wild beasts of that coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Brygi of Thrace then attacked the camp in Macedonia by night and wounded Mardonius, and though he subdued them he led the army home, an expedition Herodotus calls inglorious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Mount_Athos%2C_from_sea_level_to_top.jpg/1280px-Mount_Athos%2C_from_sea_level_to_top.jpg","title":"The flank of Athos","desc":"The wooded slopes and grey crags of Mount Athos falling to the sea. Herodotus says a north wind drove the Persian fleet onto this coast, and that some of the men who reached the shore were taken by wild beasts.","credit":"Photograph by Marooned, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A steep wooded mountainside of dark green trees broken by pale grey crags, descending from left to right under a deep blue sky, with a small cluster of white buildings low on the slope and the sea just visible at the far right."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.43–6.45, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=44. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7, fragment 31, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=7:chapter=fragments. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 4.109, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=4:chapter=109. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>Natural History</i> 4.17, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0137:book=4:chapter=17. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 7.3–4, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 70–72, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"]},
{"id":"gr-400","num":400,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The heralds who asked Athens and Sparta for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were thrown into a pit and a well, and told to fetch the king's tokens from down there.","questions":["Aegina gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Darius, and Athens gladly took that as a pretext to go to Sparta and accuse the islanders of betraying Greece.","Darius asked the Scythians and the Macedonians for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> long before he asked the Greeks, and his own inscription instead counts 23 lands that brought him tribute."],"answer":"earth and water","answerText":"earth and water","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Darius' heralds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 491 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xerxes' heralds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 481 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Earth and water</b> were what a Persian king's heralds asked for as a token of submission, and a city that gave them had answered the question of war or surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says Darius sent heralds through Greece to learn whether the Greeks meant to fight him or give themselves up, and set his coastal cities building warships and horse transports at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The demand was older than that: Darius had sent it to the Scythian king Idanthyrsus in the field, and Persian envoys had asked it of Amyntas of Macedonia, who gave it readily and feasted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many mainland Greeks gave earth and water, and so did every island the heralds reached, Aegina among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens took that as a move against itself, and went to Sparta to accuse the Aeginetans of betraying Greece, gladly using the gift as a pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Athens and Sparta answered differently: the heralds were thrown into the pit at Athens and into a well at Sparta, and told to fetch the king's earth and water from there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus will not say what befell Athens for it, and doubts the usual explanation, but he records that the anger of Agamemnon's herald Talthybius settled on Sparta until two men went to Xerxes to die for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias answers the question Herodotus leaves open, saying the wrath fell at Athens not on the city but on the house of Miltiades, who was responsible for the heralds' deaths there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch adds that Themistocles had the King's interpreter put to death by decree for prostituting the speech of Greece to barbarian terms, and Diodorus records a later vote to tithe the Greeks who went over willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Darius' own inscription at Behistun counts submission differently, listing 23 lands that became subject by the grace of Auramazda and brought him tribute, which is submission in the language of provinces and taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Persepolis_-_Royal_audience_low-relief.jpg","title":"A royal audience","desc":"An Achaemenid relief of a royal audience, the king enthroned with the crown prince behind him while an official bows before him with his hand raised to his mouth. It is the gesture of submission that the demand for earth and water asked for at a distance.","credit":"Photograph by Philippe Chavin, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)","alt":"A dark stone relief in a museum behind a rope barrier: a bearded king in a long robe sits on a high chair holding a staff, a second figure stands behind him, and to the right a man bows slightly with one hand raised to his lips, with two incense burners between them."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.126, 5.18, 6.48–6.49, 7.133–7.134, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=48. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 6.2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=6. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.12.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=3:chapter=12:section=7. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.3, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=3. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), 4–5, https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","concept","history","warfare","greece","iran"]},
{"id":"wh-251","num":251,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Land granted to the king's own kin after the fall of the Shang seeded the regional states of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and those states outlived it as independent powers.","questions":["Firm Chinese chronology begins only in 841 BCE, so the conquest that opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a date reconstructed from astronomy and inscriptions rather than one anybody recorded.","A capital of some 8.62 square kilometres (3.33 square miles) on the Feng river inside modern Xi'an served the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose westernmost vassal seat lay at Yaoheyuan in Ningxia."],"answer":"Western Zhou","answerText":"Western Zhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1045 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">877 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Firm dates from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">841 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"西周","hanzi":"西周","pinyin":"Xī Zhōu","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Xī Zhōu</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Western Zhou</b> is the first phase of Zhou rule in China, commonly dated from 1045 to 771 BCE and followed by the Eastern Zhou from 770 to 221 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its founding act was the conquest of the Shang, which the Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project dated to 20 January 1046 BCE by retrocalculating the celestial positions recorded for King Wu's attack, though its own report carries rival proposals of 1044 and 1027.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Firm Chinese chronology begins only in 841 BCE, the first year of the Gonghe reign, from which Sima Qian could compile an absolute year-by-year table of events in twelve states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The royal domain lay in the Guanzhong basin of what is now central Shaanxi, and a capital called Fengyi stood on the western bank of the Feng river inside modern Xi'an.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That site covers about 8.62 square kilometres (3.33 square miles) and has given up artificial river channels, rammed-earth palace foundations, housing, cemeteries, buried hoards of bronze and whole quarters of workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One workshop fired coarse sand-tempered cooking pots between 877 and 771 BCE in kilns stoked with grasses, millet, rice and wheat residues and the wood of oak, elm and sweetgum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To hold the conquered territory the court granted land to members of the royal Ji clan and to allied lineages, whose regional states served as political alliances and military outposts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bronze inscriptions distinguish the rulers of those satellite states who belonged to the royal lineage from the heads of merely affiliated ones, and many of the domains grew into the independent states of the Eastern Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yaoheyuan in Ningxia shows how far that reached, a walled town of about 96 hectares (240 acres) with a palace foundation, a bronze foundry and inscribed oracle bones, probably the westernmost vassal capital of the early Western Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The highly radiogenic lead that is the chemical signature of Shang bronze appears to disappear under the Zhou, though the data on either side of that short window are thin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Zhikun Ma, Shu Liu, Xuan Yi, Liya Tang, Zhongyang Fu et al., “Fuel types and use in late Western Zhou (877 – 771 BCE) industrial contexts in Northwest China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 20166, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11364631/. [Open access]","Zexian Huang, Qiang Ma, Chengrui Zhang, Ruoxin Cheng, Furen Hou et al., “Early Evidence of Post-Mortem Fetal Extrusion in Equids: A Case from the Western Zhou Period (1045–771 BC) Site of Yaoheyuan in Northwestern China,” <i>Animals</i> 14, no. 14 (2024): 2106, https://doi.org/10.3390/ani14142106. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Li_Gui%2C_2016-09-09_01.jpg/1280px-Li_Gui%2C_2016-09-09_01.jpg","title":"The Li gui, a Western Zhou food vessel","desc":"A bronze gui in the National Museum of China, cast in the earliest years of Zhou rule. Its inscription records the day of the conquest of the Shang, and the seven graphs at the heart of that passage have been read eight different ways, which is why the vessel sits at the centre of the argument over when the conquest happened.","credit":"Photograph by Siyuwj, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A round bronze bowl with two large loop handles, standing on a square pedestal base, its surface weathered grey-green and cast in bands of coiled relief ornament, lit inside a museum case."}},
{"id":"wh-252","num":252,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"The Zhou justified their conquest by arguing that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had already passed once before, from the Xia to the Shang, and so could pass again.","questions":["Mencius made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a theory of history, holding that benevolence won the Three Dynasties their thrones and the want of it lost them again.","Floods, earthquakes and civil war were read as signs that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was being withdrawn, a doctrine Xunzi rejected by insisting Heaven's course is constant."],"answer":"Mandate of Heaven","answerText":"Mandate of Heaven","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhou conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duke of Zhou</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1042 – 1035 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Western Han</span><span class=\"dt-v\">206 BCE – 8 CE</span></div>","traditional":"天命","hanzi":"天命","pinyin":"Tiānmìng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Tiānmìng</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Mandate of Heaven</b>, <i>tianming</i>, is the doctrine that Heaven grants the right to rule and takes it back from a house that governs badly, so power is held on terms, not by right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Zhou proclaimed it to justify their conquest of the Shang, and the Duke of Zhou argued from an earlier transfer, Xia to Shang, conceding that the defeated dynasty had genuinely held it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Book of Documents</i> states the condition in its own words, that the Supreme Ruler is not invariable in his decree, which its commentators gloss as the decree leaving one family for another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Addressing the conquered, the Zhou tell the people of the Shang that the kings of Zhou now hold the celestial decree, and reproach them for triflingly rejecting it themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Shang had their own version of the idea in Shangdi and the divine command, and the graph for Heaven appears in their oracle bones only in the form meaning 'great'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Early Zhou documents show not the swagger of a victorious people but what has been called a concerned consciousness, and beside it the words for virtue and reverence take on moral weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mencius made the idea a theory of history: only a moral man receives the Mandate, and benevolence was how the Three Dynasties gained the throne and the want of it how they lost it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He set a limit on it too: a sovereign may present a man to Heaven, but cannot make Heaven give that man the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xunzi rejected the doctrine outright, insisting that Heaven's course is constant, unchanged by the sage Yao and by the tyrant Jie alike, so order and disorder are not Heaven's doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its afterlife is long: under the Western Han the evolving concept shaped how disasters were answered, and the same two characters, read as the nature Heaven confers, were taken by Song scholars for human nature and by Ming Jesuits for the soul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","religion","philosophy","china"],"sources":["W. H. Medhurst, trans., <i>Ancient China. The Shoo King, or the Historical Classic</i> (Shanghae: Mission Press, 1846), “The Instructions of E” and “The Numerous Regions,” https://archive.org/details/ancientchinashoo00confuoft. [Open access]","Maja Maria Kosec, “Humanization of Chinese Religion: From Heaven (tian 天) to Ritual (li 礼) in Xu Fuguan and Li Zehou,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 203–27, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2023.11.3.203-227. [Open access]","Dawid Rogacz, “In the Shadow of the Decay. The Philosophy of History of Mencius and Xunzi,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 5, no. 1 (2017): 147–71, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2017.5.1.147-171. [Open access]","Zhen Yang and Francis Ludlow, “Climatic and societal impacts of volcanic eruptions in the Western Han Dynasty (206 BCE–8 CE): a comparative study,” <i>Climate of the Past</i> 21, no. 11 (2025): 2061–81, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-21-2061-2025. [Open access]","Qinghe Xiao, “The Christian interpretations on the nature of heaven's mandate in late Ming and early Qing dynasties,” <i>HTS Teologiese Studies/Theological Studies</i> 80, no. 3 (2024): a9994, https://doi.org/10.4102/hts.v80i3.9994. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Da_Yu_ding_inscription.jpg/1280px-Da_Yu_ding_inscription.jpg","title":"The inscription of the Da Yu ding","desc":"A rubbing of the long inscription cast inside the Da Yu ding, a bronze cauldron of the early Western Zhou. It opens by recalling that King Wen received Heaven's great charge, and it is one of the earliest surviving statements of the claim in the Zhou's own words rather than in a text handed down.","credit":"Public domain rubbing uploaded by User:Mountain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A rubbing in two panels, white archaic Chinese characters on black, set in tight vertical columns; the graphs are angular and pictorial, quite unlike modern script."}},
{"id":"wh-253","num":253,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Half a million images spanning three millennia show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not steadily simplify, and that the modern forms are in fact more complex than the earliest.","questions":["Because the earliest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were built from visual metaphor rather than sound, specialists count the system among the world's few independent inventions of writing.","The five recognised stages of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run from the oracle bones through bronze, seal and official forms to the regular script still written today."],"answer":"Chinese characters","answerText":"Chinese characters","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze script</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1045 – 771 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"漢字","hanzi":"汉字","pinyin":"Hànzì","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Hànzì</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Chinese characters</b> are the units of the oldest writing system still in daily use, and one of only three scripts known to have been invented from nothing rather than adapted from a neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest large body of them is cut into oracle bones and cast into bronzes, from the late Shang between 1250 and 1045 BCE and the Western Zhou between 1045 and 771 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their shapes were built by visual metaphor and metonymy, a part standing for the whole or a picture extended to a related idea, rather than by writing down the sounds of speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sound entered the system only as a secondary refinement, so a written form maps straight onto a meaning instead of reaching it through a pronunciation, which is what makes the older sound-first accounts of how writing evolves look too narrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same metaphorical machinery is at work in the two other pristine inventions, the Mesopotamian and the Egyptian scripts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Five stages of the script are conventionally distinguished: oracle bone, bronze, seal, official and the regular script written today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything older than the small seal script, which appeared once the first Qin emperor had unified the country, is grouped together as the ancient characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The familiar belief that the characters have grown steadily simpler turns out to be a claim that can be measured, and half a million images spanning more than 3,000 years do not bear it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is no consistent simplification through time, and modern characters are, contrary to the popular belief, visually more complex than their earliest known counterparts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Simplicity appears to trade against distinctiveness, and across writing systems generally the complexity of a character depends chiefly on how large a linguistic unit it stands for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","history","language","writing","china"],"sources":["Ludovica Ottaviano and Silvia Ferrara, “Mechanisms at the core of the Chinese script invention,” <i>Cognitive Linguistics</i> 37, no. 1 (2026): 39–62, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12949599/. [Open access]","Mengru Wang, Yu Cai, Li Gao, Ruichen Feng, Qingju Jiao et al., “Study on the evolution of Chinese characters based on few-shot learning: From oracle bone inscriptions to regular script,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272974, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272974. [Open access]","Simon J. Han, Piers Kelly, James Winters and Charles Kemp, “Simplification Is Not Dominant in the Evolution of Chinese Characters,” <i>Open Mind</i> 6 (2022): 264–79, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9987343/. [Open access]","Ludovica Ottaviano, Kathryn Kelley, Mattia Cartolano and Silvia Ferrara, “Metaphors and the Invention of Writing,” <i>Topics in Cognitive Science</i> 18, no. 1 (2026): 245–63, https://doi.org/10.1111/tops.12768. [Open access]","Helena Miton and Olivier Morin, “Graphic complexity in writing systems,” <i>Cognition</i> 214 (2021): 104771, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8346946/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/%E9%A6%AC-Chinese_characters_evolution.png/1280px-%E9%A6%AC-Chinese_characters_evolution.png.jpg","title":"The character for horse, traced through the scripts","desc":"A modern map of the descent of one character, 馬 for horse, from the Shang oracle bones and bronzes down through Western and Eastern Zhou, the Qin small seal and the Han clerical hands to the regular script and its simplified form. The red crosses mark the regional Warring States hands that died out, and the chart is careful to say that nothing above its top line is clear evidence.","credit":"Diagram by Panates, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A branching chart of brush and incised forms of one Chinese character, spiky and pictorial at the top under labels for the Shang and Zhou, growing squarer and more regular downwards through Qin and Han, with several side branches struck through by red crosses and two settled modern forms at the foot."}},
{"id":"wh-254","num":254,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Proteins recovered from soil in three tombs at Jiahu put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in China 8,500 years ago, long before any surviving cloth of it.","questions":["Residues in the ash above the Sanxingdui pits show <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> being used as a material carrier for communication between Heaven and Earth.","Before the mulberry moth was domesticated, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was drawn from several wild species, and the Palmyra finds turn out to be Indian tasar rather than Chinese."],"answer":"silk","answerText":"silk","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest silk</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanxingdui pits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"絲","hanzi":"丝","pinyin":"Sī","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Sī</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Silk</b> is a protein fibre spun by moth caterpillars into a cocoon, and the protein that makes up most of the thread is fibroin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has been a luxury throughout recorded history, and the raising of silkworms mattered to ancient trade as much as it did to technology and to culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oldest direct evidence comes from three tombs at the Neolithic site of Jiahu, where mass spectrometry found the key peptides of silk fibroin in soil 8,500 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rough weaving tools and bone needles were excavated with them, so the people buried there could probably weave and sew, which no cloth of that age survives to show directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where and when sericulture began is nevertheless still called an unsolved question in archaeology, because textiles rarely survive and the species that made a given fibre is hard to establish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Two methods now separate them: antibodies raised against the two silks show no cross-reaction with each other, and proteomics supplies biomarkers for the domestic mulberry moth and the Chinese oak moth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before the mulberry silkworm was domesticated, silks were taken from a range of moth species with regional specificity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Applied to archaeological material from Palmyra, the same kind of analysis identified wild silk from the Indian tasar moth rather than anything Chinese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Bronze Age China the fibre also carried meaning, and residues in the ash layers over the sacrificial pits at Sanxingdui show silk used as a material carrier for communication between Heaven and Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tree that feeds the domestic moth travelled as well: white mulberry comes from East Asia and particularly China, and reached Central Asia through exchanges that ran along the Silk Road in both directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","technology","china"],"sources":["Yuxuan Gong, Li Li, Decai Gong, Hao Yin and Juzhong Zhang, “Biomolecular Evidence of Silk from 8,500 Years Ago,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 12 (2016): e0168042, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0168042. [Open access]","Hailing Zheng, Ruling Yang, Jianbo Guo, Zhenbin Xie, Jingxuan Liu et al., “Evidence of the use of silk by bronze age civilization for sacrificial purposes in the Yangtze River basin of China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 29175, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-78687-7. [Open access]","Boyoung Lee, Elisabete Pires, A. Mark Pollard and James S. O. McCullagh, “Species identification of silks by protein mass spectrometry reveals evidence of wild silk use in antiquity,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 4579, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-08167-3. [Open access]","Jincui Gu, Qingqing Li, Boyi Chen, Chengfeng Xu, Hailing Zheng et al., “Species identification of Bombyx mori and Antheraea pernyi silk via immunology and proteomics,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9 (2019): 9381, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-45698-8. [Open access]","Caihui Wang, Yutian Yang, Lu Yang, Xue Ling, Zhikun Ma et al., “The chloroplast genome elucidates the origin of mulberry in Central Asia,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 16 (2025): 1592308, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2025.1592308. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Life_cycle_of_a_silkworm_from_egg_to_moth_-_DPLA_-_2f77d2d5eff44d5b14a55e47dec2687d.jpg/1280px-Life_cycle_of_a_silkworm_from_egg_to_moth_-_DPLA_-_2f77d2d5eff44d5b14a55e47dec2687d.jpg","title":"The silkworm from egg to moth","desc":"An engraved plate of 1882 from an American instruction book on silk culture, laying the whole life cycle out on a mulberry branch and keying it below: the moth laying eggs, the worm at three days and at ten, its moults, the full-grown worm about to spin, the cocoon in its floss, the cocoon opened to show the chrysalis, and the moth emerging.","credit":"Plate from the Women's Silk Culture Association, 1882, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A pale engraved plate showing a mulberry branch with caterpillars of several sizes feeding on the leaves, moths with folded wings, two loops of raw silk thread at the lower left, and a cocoon cut open to show the chrysalis inside, with a numbered key printed underneath."}},
{"id":"wh-255","num":255,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"The earliest working of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in China belongs to the Xinglongwa culture of the Liao River, and the craft reached its height at Niuheliang two millennia later.","questions":["From the middle of the 4th millennium BCE <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shifted from personal ornament to ritual object, appearing in rich burials just as elite groups were emerging.","No text before the Eastern Zhou explains the cong tube or the bi disc, so the meaning of the abstract <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shapes is genuinely lost."],"answer":"jade","answerText":"jade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6200 – 5400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hongshan peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3500 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liangzhu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3200 – 2000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"玉","hanzi":"玉","pinyin":"Yù","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Yù</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Jade</b> in China is chiefly nephrite, a stone made of two amphibole minerals, tremolite and actinolite, which are told apart by the ratio of magnesium to iron in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Prehistoric jades are found in large numbers across the mainland and throughout the Neolithic, and their distribution is divided into seven regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest is the Liao River, where jade use begins with the Xinglongwa culture of about 6200 to 5400 BCE and craftsmanship reaches its height in the late Hongshan, about 3500 to 3000 BCE, at Niuheliang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the lower Yangzi around Lake Tai jade first appears in the Majiabang and Songze cultures and becomes prevalent in the Liangzhu, about 3200 to 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Early jades are mostly personal ornaments with little relation to the wealth of a grave, but from the middle of the 4th millennium BCE ritual items come to dominate, alongside the first elite groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three forms recur across the regions: the cong, a tube square outside and cylindrical within, often carved with face masks; the bi, a flat disc with a small central hole; and the yazhang tablet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Liangzhu bi in Cleveland, dated between 3300 and 2200 BCE, is 32 centimetres (12.5 inches) across with an opening of only 5.4 centimetres (2.1 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No text earlier than the Eastern Zhou says what those abstract shapes meant, and the familiar reading of a round heaven and a square earth comes from much later writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One proposal derives the bi from the discoidal spindle whorl, a flat perforated disc that was an ordinary Neolithic tool, linking the two on formal, historical and contextual grounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other jades are shaped like or decorated with birds, turtles, cicadas and silkworms, and Shang diviners later used the bones of animals the Neolithic had carved in jade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","art","china"],"sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Jean M. Green, “Unraveling the Enigma of the Bi: The Spindle Whorl as the Model of the Ritual Disk,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 32, no. 1 (1993): 105–24, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17022. [Open access]","Cleveland Museum of Art, “Ceremonial Disc (Bi) 玉璧, 3300–2200 BCE,” accession 1917.974, accessed 29 August 2026, https://clevelandart.org/art/1917.974. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient Chinese Jades and Bronzes,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Yi Bao, Chaohong Zhao, Yuesheng Li and Xuemei Yun, “A method of determining heated ancient nephrite jades in China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 13523, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30564-w. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Ritual_object_(cong)%2C_China%2C_probably_Jiangsu_or_Zhejiang_province%2C_Neolithic_period%2C_Liangzhu_culture%2C_3300-2000_BC%2C_nephrite_-_Arthur_M._Sackler_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC00793.jpg/1280px-Ritual_object_(cong)%2C_China%2C_probably_Jiangsu_or_Zhejiang_province%2C_Neolithic_period%2C_Liangzhu_culture%2C_3300-2000_BC%2C_nephrite_-_Arthur_M._Sackler_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC00793.jpg","title":"A Liangzhu cong","desc":"A nephrite cong of the Liangzhu culture, probably from Jiangsu or Zhejiang, in the Arthur M. Sackler Museum at Harvard. The tube is square on the outside and cylindrical within, and each corner is cut into tiers carrying the reduced eyes and bars of the face motif.","credit":"Photograph by Daderot, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A tall stone tube of dark grey-brown nephrite standing on a white plinth, square in plan with a round bore through it, its four corners stepped into horizontal tiers incised with pairs of small circles and straight bars."}},
{"id":"wh-256","num":256,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Because it is named for a technology rather than fixed to a date, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins at different times in different places and is barely used of the Americas at all.","questions":["Lead in tin ingots recovered off Israel gives their parent ores an age of about 291 million years, putting the reach of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tin trade as far as Europe.","Copper and tin seldom occur together, so almost every culture of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to join exchange networks running over very long distances to get both."],"answer":"Bronze Age","answerText":"Bronze Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Near East</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eastern collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"青銅時代","hanzi":"青铜时代","pinyin":"Qīngtóng Shídài","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Qīngtóng Shídài</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Bronze Age</b> is the middle period of the three-age system, the age in which, on the definition that named it, weapons and cutting tools were made of copper or bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is named for a technology rather than pinned to a date, so it opens at different times in different places, and the phrase has meant different things in different disciplines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egyptologists barely need it, since the Old Kingdom is the Levantine Early Bronze Age and the Middle Kingdom the Levantine Middle Bronze Age, and Egypt periodises its own history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The term is largely absent from the archaeology of Oceania, the Americas and Africa south of the Sahara, and remarkably resilient everywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making the metal took a long chain of specialists, from mining and smelting to casting, annealing and recasting, which presumes a community able to feed people who grow no food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Bronze therefore travels with cities, complex states and writing, which appears in Mesopotamia and Egypt late in the 4th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Copper and tin seldom lie together, so almost every Bronze Age culture joined exchange networks over vast distances, and the period looks less like a stage than a web.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far that web reached can now be measured: lead in tin ingots recovered off the coast of Israel gives their parent ores a model age of about 291 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That excludes Anatolian, central Asian and Egyptian deposits, which formed either much earlier or much later, and points instead to European tin of the Variscan orogeny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the eastern Mediterranean the linked Aegean, Egyptian, Syro-Palestinian and Hittite world collapsed about 3,200 years ago, which palaeoclimate data from Cyprus tie to abrupt climate change, famine and the invasions recorded as the Sea Peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["era","history","archaeology","technology","bronze age"],"sources":["Julien Cooper and Yegor Grebnev, “Introduction: Bronze Age Civilizations,” <i>Old World: Journal of Ancient Africa and Eurasia</i> 3, no. 1 (2023): 2–4, https://doi.org/10.1163/26670755-20230009. [Open access]","Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, “Of the Different Periods to Which the Heathen Antiquities May Be Referred,” in <i>Guide to Northern Archaeology</i>, ed. the Earl of Ellesmere (London: James Bain, 1848), 65, https://archive.org/details/guidetonorthern00ellegoog. [Open access]","Christopher Woods, “Visible Language: The Earliest Writing Systems,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 15–17, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","Daniel Berger, Jeffrey S. Soles, Alessandra R. Giumlia-Mair, Gerhard Brügmann, Ehud Galili et al., “Isotope systematics and chemical composition of tin ingots from Mochlos (Crete) and other Late Bronze Age sites in the eastern Mediterranean Sea: An ultimate key to tin provenance?” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0218326, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218326. [Open access]","David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Uluburun_copper_ingot_oxhide_shaped.jpg/1280px-Uluburun_copper_ingot_oxhide_shaped.jpg","title":"A copper ingot from the Uluburun wreck","desc":"An oxhide-shaped copper ingot of about 1300 BCE, raised from the Uluburun shipwreck off the Turkish coast and now in the Bodrum Museum of Underwater Archaeology. The four projecting handles are a standard shipping form, and the wreck also carried the tin ingots whose lead has since been used to argue for a European source.","credit":"Photograph by Zunkir, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A thick rectangular slab of corroded metal, brown and streaked with green, drawn out into a pointed handle at each of its four corners, standing upright on a museum mount in a dark case."}},
{"id":"wh-257","num":257,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"A tin content above one per cent is usually taken to show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made on purpose rather than smelted by accident from a tin-bearing copper ore.","questions":["At Anyang the purest metal went to the ritual vessels of the highest elite, while lesser burials got <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mixed and recycled from older objects.","A peat bog in Serbia records lead pollution from about 3600 BCE, the earliest such trace in Europe and the cost of making <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at scale."],"answer":"bronze","answerText":"bronze","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tin bronze known</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 4th millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Balkan lead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"青銅","hanzi":"青铜","pinyin":"Qīngtóng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Qīngtóng</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Bronze</b> is copper alloyed with tin, and in archaeometallurgy it counts as the real copper alloy of antiquity, made deliberately to improve on what copper alone can do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tin makes the melt more fluid and easier to cast, lowers copper's melting point, and at about a tenth of the mixture stiffens the metal more than arsenic or zinc will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tin content above one per cent is usually read as deliberate alloying rather than an accident of the ore, though some workers set the bar at five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There were four routes to it: adding metallic tin to molten copper, reducing cassiterite in the melt under charcoal at about 1200 °C (2200 °F), smelting a natural copper-tin ore, or co-smelting a copper ore with cassiterite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first bronze was probably an accident of the third kind, recognised afterwards by its golden colour, its hardness and how much better it cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Tin was not the only choice, and Anatolian smiths of the Early Bronze Age also alloyed with antimony, nickel and lead before that variety narrowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The metal was reused as well as made, and at Anyang what went back into the crucible followed rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ritual vessels for the highest elite were cast from very pure copper by carefully controlled alloying, while lower elites had bronzes made by secondary alloying from metal carrying more impurities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Working out where any of it came from is harder than it looks, because the lead isotope signatures of different ore regions overlap, as a statistical treatment of the candidate sources makes plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The industry left a mark of its own: a peat bog in western Serbia carries anthropogenic lead from about 3600 BCE, the earliest such pollution documented in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","science","bronze age"],"sources":["Omid Oudbashi, S. Mohammadamin Emami and Parviz Davami, “Bronze in Archaeology: A Review of the Archaeometallurgy of Bronze in Ancient Iran,” in <i>Copper Alloys: Early Applications and Current Performance — Enhancing Processes</i>, ed. Luca Collini (Rijeka: InTech, 2012), under “3.3 Tin bronze,” https://cdn.intechopen.com/pdfs/30478/InTech-Bronze_in_archaeology_a_review_of_the_archaeometallurgy_of_bronze_in_ancient_iran.pdf. [Open access]","Gonca Dardeniz, “Why did the use of antimony-bearing alloys in Bronze Age Anatolia fall dormant after the Early Bronze Age? A case study from Resuloğlu (Çorum, Turkey),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 6 (2020): e0234563, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0234563. [Open access]","Jack Longman, Daniel Veres, Walter Finsinger and Vasile Ersek, “Exceptionally high levels of lead pollution in the Balkans from the Early Bronze Age to the Industrial Revolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 25 (2018): E5661–68, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1721546115. [Open access]","Sarah De Ceuster, Jurian Hoogewerff and Patrick Degryse, “Provenancing ancient materials with lead isotopes: overlap uncovered,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 4628, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-88909-1. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury et al., “Social hierarchy and the choice of metal recycling at Anyang, the last capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-75920-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Bronze_Age_hoard_(FindID_177968).jpg/1280px-Bronze_Age_hoard_(FindID_177968).jpg","title":"A founder's hoard from Suffolk","desc":"Five pieces of a Late Bronze Age hoard found in Suffolk, each shown front and back against a ruler: two fragments of socketed axes, the end of an unidentified implement, and two lumps of run copper alloy. Broken tools and casting waste collected like this are what a founder's hoard is, though why such deposits were buried is still argued over.","credit":"Photograph from the Portable Antiquities Scheme and the Trustees of the British Museum, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Ten corroded green-brown lumps of metal laid out in a row on a white ground above two rulers and two handwritten finds labels; some are curved fragments of thin cast walls, others are shapeless blobs of solidified metal."}},
{"id":"wh-258","num":258,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Ancient DNA from 435 people has traced the ancestry of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back to three genetic clines rather than to any single earlier population.","questions":["Burial mounds dominate the landscape the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left behind, and isotopes from the bones in them show a herding economy of sheep, goat and cattle.","Spreading from Hungary to Kazakhstan by 3000 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rode horses of the line from which all modern domestic stock descends."],"answer":"Yamnaya culture","answerText":"Yamnaya culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Complex</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 2600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ancestors formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Maximal extent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Yamnaya culture</b> is the archaeological complex that appeared across the steppes north of the Black and Caspian seas about 3300 BCE and, by 3000 BCE, reached from Hungary in the west to Kazakhstan in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known above all from burial mounds, which dominate the archaeological landscape of the region and hold most of what survives of its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient DNA from 435 individuals has now placed its origins among the Eneolithic peoples who came before it, and finds three genetic clines rather than one ancestral group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Caucasus-lower Volga cline ran between a Neolithic southern end and a northern end on the lower Volga, and people of that cline contributed about four-fifths of Yamnaya ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moving west along the Dnipro and Don they mixed with local hunter-gatherers to form the Serednii Stih groups, from whom Yamnaya ancestors took shape around 4000 BCE and then grew quickly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Stable carbon and nitrogen isotopes from 105 people and 50 animals across these mounds show a herding economy built on sheep, goat and cattle on the grasslands north of the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The genetic boundary between the Caucasus groups and the steppe groups beside them is sharp, and it follows the eco-geographic line between mountain and open grassland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yamnaya groups of about 3200 to 2600 BCE also rode horses of the DOM2 line, the direct ancestors of modern domestic stock, and took them into their diet and their rituals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That mobility is what makes the culture matter beyond its own steppe, since its descendants carried both people and languages far into Europe and Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The region stayed a crossroads long afterwards: genomes spanning 7000 BCE to 1800 CE from what is now Ukraine show ancestry there fluctuating from period to period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","genetics","bronze age"],"sources":["Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, David Anthony, Leonid Vyazov, Romain Fournier et al., “The genetic origin of the Indo-Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 639 (2025): 132–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11922553/. [Open access]","Corina Knipper, Sabine Reinhold, Julia Gresky, Nataliya Berezina, Claudia Gerling et al., “Diet and subsistence in Bronze Age pastoral communities from the southern Russian steppes and the North Caucasus,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 10 (2020): e0239861, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0239861. [Open access]","David Anthony, Martin Trautmann and Volker Heyd, “Horse genetics, archaeology, and the beginning of riding,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 20 (2026): eady7336, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ady7336. [Open access]","Chuan-Chao Wang, Sabine Reinhold, Alexey Kalmykov, Antje Wissgott, Guido Brandt et al., “Ancient human genome-wide data from a 3000-year interval in the Caucasus corresponds with eco-geographic regions,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 10 (2019): 590, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-08220-8. [Open access]","Lehti Saag, Olga Utevska, Stanislav Zadnikov, Iryna Shramko, Kyrylo Gorbenko et al., “North Pontic crossroads: Mobility in Ukraine from the Bronze Age to the early modern period,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11 (2025): eadr0695, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adr0695. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Kurgans_in_southern_Ukraine.png/1280px-Kurgans_in_southern_Ukraine.png.jpg","title":"Kurgans in southern Ukraine","desc":"Three burial mounds of the steppe standing in worked fields, from a study of the plants that survive on them. Mounds like these were raised from the local loess, most of them between about 5,000 and 700 years ago, and they run from 10 to 110 metres (33 to 360 feet) across and 1 to 15 metres (3 to 49 feet) high.","credit":"Photographs by Iwona Dembicz and colleagues, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Three photographs of low grassy mounds rising out of flat farmland: one seen across a ripe wheat field, one with a van parked at its foot for scale, and a larger one with three people standing on its crest above a green crop."}},
{"id":"wh-259","num":259,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"An open dataset traces inherited words across 160 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sorting 25,731 lexemes into 4,981 cognate sets and marking every word known to have been borrowed sideways.","questions":["Anatolian, Tocharian, Greek, Armenian, Baltic, Slavic, Germanic, Italic and Celtic are all branches of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose common ancestor survives only as a reconstruction.","Ancient DNA ties the spread of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to herders of the steppes north of the Black and Caspian seas between about 3300 and 1500 BCE."],"answer":"Indo-European languages","answerText":"Indo-European languages","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dispersal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Languages coded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">160, in 13 clades</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Indo-European languages</b> are a family whose branches include Anatolian, Tocharian, Greek, Armenian, Albanian, Indic, Iranian, Baltic, Slavic, Germanic, Italic and Celtic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are held to descend from one reconstructed ancestor, Proto-Indo-European, recovered from the inherited words the daughter languages still share.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An open dataset now tracks those shared words across 160 languages, sorting 25,731 lexemes into 4,981 cognate sets against 170 reference meanings in the core vocabulary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was built by 89 linguists from 355 cited sources and marks every known case of a word borrowed sideways rather than inherited, since borrowing is what most often misleads a family tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reconstruction can be read for content as well as for shape: only one or perhaps two cereal terms can be recovered for the basal stage of the family, while the core stage acquired a fuller set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That documents a shift from a largely non-agricultural economy to a mixed one of herding and farming between the two stages of the protolanguage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also means the eastern Yamnaya of the Don-Volga steppe, where evidence of farming is lacking, is not a perfect match for the core Indo-European speech community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient DNA nonetheless ties the family’s spread to the Yamnaya complex north of the Black and Caspian seas, and puts its dispersal roughly between 3300 and 1500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genomes from South and Central Asia show steppe ancestry reaching the subcontinent in the 2nd millennium BCE, which is where the Indic branch enters the record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Folktales told in these societies track the language tree rather than mere distance, and one of them, <i>The Smith and the Devil</i>, has been traced back to the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","language","genetics","migration"],"sources":["Cormac Anderson, Matthew Scarborough, Lechosław Jocz, Martin Joachim Kümmel, Thomas Jügel et al., “The Indo-European Cognate Relationships dataset,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 12 (2025): 1541, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12405575/. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, David Anthony, Leonid Vyazov, Romain Fournier et al., “The genetic origin of the Indo-Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 639 (2025): 132–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11922553/. [Open access]","Guus Kroonen, Anthony Jakob, Axel I. Palmér, Paulus van Sluis and Andrew Wigman, “Indo-European cereal terminology suggests a Northwest Pontic homeland for the core Indo-European languages,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 10 (2022): e0275744, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0275744. [Open access]","Sara Graça da Silva and Jamshid J. Tehrani, “Comparative phylogenetic analyses uncover the ancient roots of Indo-European folktales,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 3, no. 1 (2016): 150645, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.150645. [Open access]","Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nick Patterson, Priya Moorjani, Nadin Rohland, Rebecca Bernardos et al., “The formation of human populations in South and Central Asia,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6457 (2019): eaat7487, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6822619/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Indo-European_languages_distribution.png/1280px-Indo-European_languages_distribution.png.jpg","title":"Where Indo-European languages are spoken today","desc":"The countries and regions in which an Indo-European language is the majority tongue. The band running from Ireland and Iberia across Europe to Iran and northern India is the old extent; the reach into the Americas, Australia and southern Africa dates from European settlement after 1500 CE rather than from the ancient dispersal.","credit":"Map by Industrius and Radosław Botev, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A world map with Europe, Iran, northern India, most of the Americas, Australia, New Zealand and small parts of southern Africa shaded red, and the rest of Asia and Africa left grey."}},
{"id":"wh-260","num":260,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Every horse alive descends from a steppe population of the lower Volga-Don, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left the Botai herders of Kazakhstan with no surviving line at all.","questions":["Setting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 2200 BCE, where the modern clade’s favourable mutations first appear, is disputed by those who point to animals milked and ridden centuries earlier.","Sampling from 8000 to 1000 BCE shows that domestic horses reach the southern Caucasus and Anatolia only in the Bronze Age, well after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the steppes."],"answer":"domestication of the horse","answerText":"domestication of the horse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Botai keeping</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">DOM2 mutations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2200 – 2100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mobility spreads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>domestication of the horse</b> turned a hunted grassland animal, among the most heavily taken large game of Ice Age Europe, into the one that carried people and goods across Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mapped from 273 ancient genomes, the horses alive today descend from a population of the western Eurasian steppes, above all the lower Volga-Don region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people of Botai in Central Asia bridled, milked and corralled horses about 3500 BCE, but that lineage left no descendants among modern stock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The modern line replaced almost every local population as it spread rapidly across Eurasia, moving in step with equestrian material culture including the spoke-wheeled chariots of Sintashta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A further 475 genomes put reproductive control of that lineage at about 2200 BCE, achieved by close-kin mating and shortened generation times after a bottleneck beginning no earlier than about 2700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Riding left marks in the genome too, with strong selection at two genes, GSDMC and ZFPM1, tied to locomotion and temperament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that reading the 2200 BCE date refutes the familiar picture of great horse herds accompanying the steppe migrations into Europe around 3000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against it, a genetic shift alone is argued not to define domestication at all: horses of several backgrounds were managed, milked and ridden long before, and Yamnaya groups of about 3200 to 2600 BCE already rode animals of the modern line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Selection for endurance and temperament began centuries earlier still, which makes the process protracted and regionally varied rather than a single breakthrough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From outside the steppe it looks like an arrival: in the southern Caucasus and Anatolia, sampled from about 8000 to 1000 BCE, domestic horses appear only in the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","genetics","bronze age"],"sources":["Pablo Librado, Naveed Khan, Antoine Fages, Mariya A. Kusliy, Tomasz Suchan et al., “The origins and spread of domestic horses from the Western Eurasian steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8550961/. [Open access]","Pablo Librado, Gaetan Tressières, Lorelei Chauvey, Antoine Fages, Naveed Khan et al., “Widespread horse-based mobility arose around 2200 BCE in Eurasia,” <i>Nature</i> 631, no. 8022 (2024): 819–25, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11269178/. [Open access]","David Anthony, Martin Trautmann and Volker Heyd, “Horse genetics, archaeology, and the beginning of riding,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 20 (2026): eady7336, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ady7336. [Open access]","Silvia Guimaraes, Benjamin S. Arbuckle, Joris Peters, Sarah E. Adcock, Hijlke Buitenhuis et al., “Ancient DNA shows domestic horses were introduced in the southern Caucasus and Anatolia during the Bronze Age,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 38 (2020): eabb0030, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abb0030. [Open access]","Elaine Turner, “Horse, Reindeer and Bison Hunters at Solutré: An Archaeozoological Analysis of a Magdalenian Bone Assemblage,” <i>Bonner zoologische Beiträge</i> 50 (1999): 143–82, https://archive.org/details/biostor-136046. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Horse_geographic_and_genetic_affinities.webp","title":"How the modern horse line took over Eurasia","desc":"Ancestry in ancient horse genomes, mapped at three moments. Before 3000 BCE and through the 3rd millennium the sampled sites carry a patchwork of lineages; after about 2000 BCE almost every site across Europe and the steppe carries the same one, the ancestor of the domestic horse of today. Figure 1 from the study of 273 ancient genomes on which the card rests.","credit":"Figure by Pablo Librado and colleagues, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Three stacked maps of Europe and northern Asia, each dotted with pie charts showing the ancestry of horses sampled at that site. In the first two the pies are many colours; in the third, for after 2000 BCE, almost all of them are a single orange."}},
{"id":"wh-261","num":261,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"The spoke-wheeled vehicle built by the Sintashta culture of the steppes is the earliest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it spread across Eurasia with the horses that drew it.","questions":["Scenes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are carved on the slabs above the Shaft Graves at Mycenae, though whether they show war, hunting or funeral games has never been settled.","Because no <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> existed in northern or central Europe at the time, a group at Mycenae using one could only have come from the Near East."],"answer":"chariot","answerText":"chariot","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sintashta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mycenae stelae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1450 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>chariot</b> is a light two-wheeled vehicle drawn by horses, and the spoke-wheeled version built by the Sintashta culture of the steppes is the earliest of the kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The modern domestic horse line spread rapidly across Eurasia in step with equestrian material culture, the Sintashta chariots among it, replacing almost every local horse population as it went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chariots were not the beginning of the relationship, since horses of several backgrounds had been managed, milked and ridden for centuries before any of this.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How far back riding itself goes is contested, and the skeletal changes to hip, femur and lower back often read as a rider’s marks are a weaker guide than they look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those features have been used to argue for mounted riding among the Yamnaya in the 4th and 3rd millennia BCE, a claim the method itself cannot carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the Aegean the earliest images of the vehicle are carved on the stone slabs that stood over the Shaft Graves at Mycenae, the first large-scale relief sculpture of the mainland Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether those scenes show warfare, hunting or races at funeral games has never been settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their presence has been used to argue about where the buried came from, since chariots did not exist in northern or central Europe at the time and a chariot-using group could only have come from the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Keeping such teams took knowledge as well as animals, and from the 4th millennium before the present written sources describe training systems and husbandry in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many of the practices they set down are still in use, which makes the chariot age the beginning of a continuous tradition rather than a closed episode.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","technology","bronze age"],"sources":["Pablo Librado, Naveed Khan, Antoine Fages, Mariya A. Kusliy, Tomasz Suchan et al., “The origins and spread of domestic horses from the Western Eurasian steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8550961/. [Open access]","David Anthony, Martin Trautmann and Volker Heyd, “Horse genetics, archaeology, and the beginning of riding,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 20 (2026): eady7336, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ady7336. [Open access]","Lauren Hosek, Robin J. James and William T. T. Taylor, “Tracing horseback riding and transport in the human skeleton,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10 (2024): eado9774, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ado9774. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Weronika Klecel and Elżbieta Martyniuk, “From the Eurasian Steppes to the Roman Circuses: A Review of Early Development of Horse Breeding and Management,” <i>Animals</i> 11, no. 7 (2021): 1859, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8300240/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Dispersion_of_double-horse_burials_%28complete_skeletons_only%29_2000-1300_BC.jpg/1280px-Dispersion_of_double-horse_burials_%28complete_skeletons_only%29_2000-1300_BC.jpg","title":"Bronze Age double-horse burials","desc":"The 42 sites across Poland, Ukraine, the middle Volga, the Urals, northern Kazakhstan and Greece where a matched pair of horses was buried whole between about 2000 and 1300 BCE. A pair is the signature of a chariot team, so the spread of these graves tracks the spread of the vehicle; the red marker is Husiatyn in western Ukraine.","credit":"Map by Przemysław Makarowicz and colleagues, drawn by J. Niebieszczański, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A relief map reaching from the Baltic to Kazakhstan, with numbered horse symbols clustered in southern Poland and western Ukraine, strung along the middle Volga and the Urals, spread across northern Kazakhstan, and marked at three points in Greece."}},
{"id":"wh-262","num":262,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Vases from Egypt, the Levant, Cyprus and Italy at its harbours, and colonies on Kythera and Rhodes, place the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the centre of a Bronze Age sea network.","questions":["Crete had no tin of its own, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> imported the scarcer half of bronze by sea, along with the copper that Knossos seems to have controlled.","Genomes from Bronze Age Crete are mostly those of local farming stock, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew out of the island's own Neolithic population rather than from an incoming people."],"answer":"Minoan civilisation","answerText":"Minoan civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 1050 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New palaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Knossos burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1375 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan civilisation</b> of Bronze Age Crete sat at the centre of a sea network that reached Egypt, the Levant, Cyprus and Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vases from all four turn up at the southern harbour of Kommos, whose shipshed and purpose-built transport amphorae show produce moving out as steadily as goods came in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Northwards the influence ran up a chain of islands that specialists call the Western String, the route by which Crete drew silver and lead from the mines of Attica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two places look like actual colonies rather than influenced neighbours, Kastri on Kythera and probably Trianda on Rhodes, while Cretan pottery reaches Miletus, Iasos and Knidos on the coast of Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Knossos seems to have held the copper trade with Cyprus or south-eastern Turkey, since bronzework across the southern Aegean fell into permanent decline once the palace burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether any of this amounted to an empire has never been settled, and Thucydides' report that Minos ruled the sea has been argued over ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trade and the hiring of Cretan craftsmen abroad account for everything actually found, without needing to credit the island with a fleet or a warrior caste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tin was not local either, and the ingots recovered at Mochlos on the north coast show Crete importing the scarcer half of bronze from a source still in dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor were the Cretans themselves newcomers, since Early Bronze Age Aegean genomes are homogeneous and descend mainly from the region's Neolithic farmers, with only small-scale gene flow from east of the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Isotopes say the islanders largely stayed put, while mainland ancestry entered gradually between the 17th and the 12th centuries BCE and marriage between close kin was practised at a rate unmatched in the ancient DNA record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","geography","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger, Jeffrey S. Soles, Alessandra R. Giumlia-Mair, Gerhard Brügmann, Ehud Galili et al., “Isotope systematics and chemical composition of tin ingots from Mochlos (Crete) and other Late Bronze Age sites in the eastern Mediterranean Sea: An ultimate key to tin provenance?” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0218326, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218326. [Open access]","Florian Clemente, Martina Unterländer, Olga Dolgova, Carlos Eduardo G. Amorim, Francisco Coroado-Santos et al., “The genomic history of the Aegean palatial civilizations,” <i>Cell</i> 184, no. 10 (2021): 2565–86, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2021.03.039. [Open access]","Michael Richards, Colin Smith, Olaf Nehlich, Vaughan Grimes, Darlene Weston et al., “Finding Mycenaeans in Minoan Crete? Isotope and DNA analysis of human mobility in Bronze Age Crete,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272144, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272144. [Open access]","Eirini Skourtanioti, Harald Ringbauer, Guido Alberto Gnecchi Ruscone, Raffaela Angelina Bianco, Marta Burri et al., “Ancient DNA reveals admixture history and endogamy in the prehistoric Aegean,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 7, no. 2 (2023): 290–303, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-022-01952-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Ship_procession_fresco%2C_part_2%2C_Akrotiri%2C_Greece.jpg/1280px-Ship_procession_fresco%2C_part_2%2C_Akrotiri%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Ships of the Bronze Age Aegean","desc":"Part of a painted frieze from a house at Akrotiri on Thera, a town deep in the Minoan orbit and buried by the eruption of its own volcano. Oared ships with awnings and deck cabins pass between leaping dolphins, and it is shipping of this kind on which Crete's reach across the eastern Mediterranean rested.","credit":"Wall painting from Akrotiri, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A cracked plaster wall painting of long oared ships seen from the side, each with a canopy over the deck and small figures aboard, with leaping dolphins in the pale space between them."},"locator":{"name":"Minoan civilisation","at":[25.1631,35.2981],"kind":"region","area":[[23.35,35.75],[24.5,35.95],[25.3,36.6],[25.6,36.6],[25.75,36.15],[26.55,35.45],[26.45,34.8],[25.5,34.75],[24.3,34.75],[23.35,35.05]]}},
{"id":"wh-263","num":263,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Oxhide ingots, Baltic amber and a shipwreck full of a merchant's tackle place the palaces of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the western end of a trading world reaching to Egypt.","questions":["The burning of the palaces around 1200 BCE ended <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Boeotia, the Argolid, Messenia and Laconia lost most of their people in the century that followed.","Mainland ancestry appears on Crete between the 17th and the 12th centuries BCE, marking the centuries in which the weight of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over the island steadily grew."],"answer":"Mycenaean Greece","answerText":"Mycenaean Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1050 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Greece</b> is the Late Bronze Age culture of the Greek mainland, and its palaces stood at the western end of a trading world reaching from the Baltic to Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper moved along it as four-handled ingots of about 20 kilograms (45 pounds), the so-called oxhide type, found from Syria and Cyprus to Crete, Sicily and Sardinia and painted in Egyptian tombs from the reign of Thutmose III onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ship wrecked off Cape Gelidonya carried a ton of such metal with scrap bronze, weights, scarabs and a cylinder seal, the working kit of a merchant captain rather than a royal cargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all the amber in Mycenaean graves is Baltic, and one proposal has it arriving along a chain of royal gift-exchanges across Europe of which these kings were the southernmost link.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crete came within that reach as well, mainland ancestry appearing on the island gradually between the 17th and the 12th centuries BCE as the influence of the mainland over it intensified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the Cretan cemetery of Armenoi, one of the few places off the palaces to yield the newly adopted Linear B script, four of the buried grew up somewhere other than Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around 1200 BCE the mainland palaces burned, and Boeotia, western Attica, the Corinthia, the Argolid, Messenia and Laconia lost most of their people over the century that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Egyptian sources name raiders who had wrecked the Levant, Cyprus and Anatolia, yet there is no sound evidence that they reached the Aegean at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every single-cause explanation offered has oversimplified, whether a change in the nature of warfare, the severing of trade routes or drought, for a collapse spread across a century and half a continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cores from Cyprus do record an abrupt dry episode and famine running through those decades, so climate belongs in the account even though it cannot carry the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","warfare","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Eirini Skourtanioti, Harald Ringbauer, Guido Alberto Gnecchi Ruscone, Raffaela Angelina Bianco, Marta Burri et al., “Ancient DNA reveals admixture history and endogamy in the prehistoric Aegean,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 7, no. 2 (2023): 290–303, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-022-01952-3. [Open access]","Michael Richards, Colin Smith, Olaf Nehlich, Vaughan Grimes, Darlene Weston et al., “Finding Mycenaeans in Minoan Crete? Isotope and DNA analysis of human mobility in Bronze Age Crete,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272144, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272144. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Mycenaean_%27stirrup_jar%27%2C_clay_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_S_7137_p01.jpg/1280px-Mycenaean_%27stirrup_jar%27%2C_clay_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_S_7137_p01.jpg","title":"A Mycenaean jar in the Egyptian Museum","desc":"A small clay stirrup jar, the closed shape Mycenaean potters made for oil and poured through the narrow spout at the shoulder. The Museo Egizio in Turin dates it to the Egyptian New Kingdom, between about 1539 and 1292 BCE, which puts it at the far end of the trade described here.","credit":"Photograph by the Museo Egizio, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A squat wheel-made pot of buff clay banded in reddish brown, with a short narrow spout rising from the shoulder and the top broken away, standing on a clear museum mount."}},
{"id":"wh-264","num":264,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Dated exactly, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would tie the calendars of the Aegean, Egypt and the Near East to the ice cores and the lake beds, which is why it is fought over.","questions":["Radiocarbon puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the late 17th or early 16th century BCE and the pottery sequences the better part of a century later, a gap nobody has closed.","A carbonised olive shrub on Therasia and a tree-ring series spanning 1700 to 1500 BCE both shift the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> towards the 16th century, narrowing an old argument without ending it."],"answer":"Minoan eruption","answerText":"Minoan eruption","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiocarbon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1627 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Archaeology</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Therasia shrub</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1560 – 1540 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan eruption</b> blew out the middle of the Aegean island of Thera and was one of the largest volcanic events of the past 4,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ash fell across the eastern Mediterranean and left a single geological marker that, dated precisely, could anchor the ice cores, the cave deposits and the lake sediments alongside the archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It would then tie the separate calendars of the Aegean, Egypt and the Near East to one another and to that environmental record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This is why the date has been fought over for decades, and why the fight matters a long way beyond one island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two bodies of evidence disagree: radiocarbon has pointed to the late 17th or early 16th century BCE, while pottery sequences and Egyptian links point to the mid-16th or early 15th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Part of the gap turns out to lie in the measuring, since the international calibration curve treats a whole hemisphere as one and Mediterranean and Anatolian wood is offset from it by up to 22 radiocarbon years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Growing seasons of different length and timing are one cause and the move from older counting methods to accelerator mass spectrometry another, and correcting for both also settles a long quarrel over Mesopotamian chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An annual-resolution radiocarbon series built from calendar-dated tree rings between 1700 and 1500 BCE shifts the answer for Thera towards the 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A carbonised olive shrub buried by the eruption on neighbouring Therasia dates slightly younger than an earlier branch, raising the odds on the middle of that century and overlapping sulfate markers already found in the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon on Egyptian objects of the 17th and early 18th Dynasties puts the eruption before the reign of Ahmose, and a chemical shift in dated tree rings around 1560 BCE may be the event itself, on a reading its authors say needs further substantiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","geology","science","archaeology","bronze age"],"sources":["Charlotte L. Pearson, Peter W. Brewer, David Brown, Timothy J. Heaton, Gregory W. L. Hodgins et al., “Annual radiocarbon record indicates 16th century BCE date for the Thera eruption,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 8 (2018): eaar8241, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aar8241. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Lukas Wacker, Ulf Büntgen, Christopher Bronk Ramsey, Michael W. Dee et al., “Radiocarbon offsets and old world chronology as relevant to Mesopotamia, Egypt, Anatolia and Thera (Santorini),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 13785, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-69287-2. [Open access]","Charlotte Pearson, Kostas Sbonias, Iris Tzachili and Timothy J. Heaton, “Olive shrub buried on Therasia supports a mid-16th century BCE date for the Thera eruption,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 6994, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-33696-w. [Open access]","Hendrik J. Bruins and Johannes van der Plicht, “The Minoan Thera eruption predates Pharaoh Ahmose: Radiocarbon dating of Egyptian 17th to early 18th Dynasty museum objects,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0330702, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0330702. [Open access]","Charlotte Pearson, Matthew Salzer, Lukas Wacker, Peter Brewer, Adam Sookdeo and Peter Kuniholm, “Securing timelines in the ancient Mediterranean using multiproxy annual tree-ring data,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 117, no. 15 (2020): 8410–15, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1917445117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Santorini%E2%80%99s_Hidden_Worlds_%28154523%29.jpg","title":"What the eruption left of Thera","desc":"Thera from Landsat 9. The crescent is what remains of a round island: Therasia lies across the water to the west, Aspronisi to the south, and the dark lava islands at the centre have grown inside the flooded caldera since. The olive shrub that gives the eruption one of its dates was buried on Therasia.","credit":"NASA Earth Observatory image by Wanmei Liang, using Landsat data from the U.S. Geological Survey, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A satellite view of pale islands around a dark blue bay: a long crescent on the east and south, a smaller island on the west, a sliver to the south-west, and two small dark islands at the centre of the water."}},
{"id":"wh-265","num":265,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Built from the signs of an older Cretan script for a language it fits badly, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recorded palace accounts in Greek and vanished when those palaces burned.","questions":["A syllabary with no way at all to write a closed syllable, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was pressed into service for Greek, a language thick with consonant clusters and final consonants.","Greece could write in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 1200 BCE and then could not write at all for four centuries, until an alphabet of Semitic origin reached it."],"answer":"Linear B","answerText":"Linear B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Archives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Writing returns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Linear B</b> is the writing of the Mycenaean palaces, a mainly syllabic script of some 89 signs standing for bare vowels and for open consonant-and-vowel syllables, with more than a hundred ideograms beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not built for Greek: 45 of its signs have close equivalents in Linear A, the older and still unread writing of Crete, from which it was derived in order to write a different language altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where, when, why, by whom and in what circumstances that adaptation was made are five questions with proposals attached and no answers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On one reading the two are a single repertoire of signs governed by two different sets of language-specific rules, and the first hands to write Linear B were scribes trained in the older one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fit is poor all the same, since a syllabary with no sign for a closed syllable was set to work on a language thick with consonant clusters and final consonants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It survives in only two forms, clay tablets legible because the fires that destroyed the palaces baked them, and one to three words painted on the shoulder of a coarse stirrup jar before firing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tablets come from palaces alone, while some 140 of the painted jars are known, clustered in Boeotia, the Argolid and Crete and largely made in western Crete, which is how Greek is known to have been spoken there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writing here was an instrument of palace administration and had no life outside it, though these tablets are also the earliest Greek anyone can read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So when the palaces burned around 1200 BCE, writing disappeared with them, along with the economies they ran and the great building programmes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greek was written again only some four centuries later, in an alphabet of Semitic origin whose date and circumstances of adoption are both still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","writing","language","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Theodore Nash, review of <i>Aegean Linear Script(s): Rethinking the Relationship between Linear A and Linear B</i>, by Ester Salgarella, Cambridge Classical Studies, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.04.30/. [Open access]","“Palace of Nestor: Description,” Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Clay_tablets_inscribed_with_Linear_B.jpg/1280px-Clay_tablets_inscribed_with_Linear_B.jpg","title":"Linear B tablets from Pylos","desc":"A case in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens holding tablets from the palace of Pylos, which the museum dates to the 13th century BCE. Both of the shapes the script was written on are here: the long thin palm-leaf tablets carrying a line or two, and the rectangular page tablets that hold more. None of it was meant to last, and it survives only because the fire that destroyed the palace baked the clay.","credit":"Photograph by HerrGutmannsWiki, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A tall lit museum case holding about thirty small clay tablets mounted on a pale board, some long and narrow, some rectangular, each carrying faint incised lines of writing, above a lower shelf of seal stones and small fragments."}},
{"id":"wh-266","num":266,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Eight languages are attested in the archives of their capital, and their own was the earliest Indo-European ever written down, which makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a state that dealt with everybody.","questions":["Named after a people of the Hebrew Bible who were not them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran a Bronze Age state in central Anatolia and were forgotten so completely that later Greek writers never mention them.","Ancient genomes barely find in Anatolia the steppe ancestry that marks every other Indo-European region, which makes the language of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the hardest of the family to place."],"answer":"Hittites","answerText":"Hittites","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hattusa, from c. 1650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Hittites</b> were the people of a Bronze Age state in central Anatolia, one of the powers whose entanglement with Egypt, the Aegean and the Levant made up the Late Bronze Age world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is not their own: it was fixed on them from the Hatti-land of Assyrian records and the Hittites of the Hebrew Bible, who were in fact the Iron Age states of northern Syria and southeastern Turkey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Land and capital alike came from the Hattians who were there before them, the site already appearing as Hattush in Old Assyrian merchants' records when the Hittites took it over around 1650 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their own language belongs to the Anatolian branch of Indo-European and is the earliest of that family committed to writing, set down in cuneiform borrowed from Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eight separate languages have been identified in the archives of their capital, which is what the paperwork of a state dealing with everybody looks like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Where that language came from is a live question, because ancient genomes show Anatolia reshaped by movement within West Asia and barely touched by the steppe migrations that carried Indo-European everywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The answer now proposed is a population of the Caucasus and the lower Volga which supplied about four-fifths of the ancestry of the Yamnaya and at least a tenth of that of the Bronze Age central Anatolians who spoke Hittite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On that reading the parent tongue of both branches was last spoken as one between about 4400 and 4000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the horses that drew their chariots arrived rather than being bred at home, appearing suddenly and in quantity at the end of the 3rd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state itself was gone by about 1200 BCE, its capital emptied and burnt and then left alone for centuries and its memory so thoroughly lost that Herodotus and Pausanias, both of them from Asia Minor, say nothing of it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["people","state","history","archaeology","genetics","bronze age"],"sources":["David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, “The Hittites and the Aegean World,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 2 (1974): 2–10, https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-hittites-and-the-aegean-world/. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis, Songül Alpaslan-Roodenberg, Ayşe Acar, Ayşen Açıkkol, Anagnostis Agelarakis et al., “The genetic history of the Southern Arc: A bridge between West Asia and Europe,” <i>Science</i> 377, no. 6609 (2022): eabm4247, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abm4247. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, David Anthony, Leonid Vyazov, Romain Fournier et al., “The genetic origin of the Indo-Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 639, no. 8053 (2025): 132–42, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08531-5. [Open access]","Silvia Guimaraes, Benjamin S. Arbuckle, Joris Peters, Sarah E. Adcock, Hijlke Buitenhuis et al., “Ancient DNA shows domestic horses were introduced in the southern Caucasus and Anatolia during the Bronze Age,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 38 (2020): eabb0030, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abb0030. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Kadesh_Treaty%2C_written_in_Akkadian._1269_BCE._From_Bo%C4%9Fazk%C3%B6y%2C_Turkey._Clay_tablet._Museum_of_the_Ancient_Orient%2C_Istanbul%2C_Turkey.jpg/1280px-Kadesh_Treaty%2C_written_in_Akkadian._1269_BCE._From_Bo%C4%9Fazk%C3%B6y%2C_Turkey._Clay_tablet._Museum_of_the_Ancient_Orient%2C_Istanbul%2C_Turkey.jpg","title":"The treaty with Egypt","desc":"Two fragments of the peace treaty between Hattusili and Ramesses II, found at Boğazköy, the Hittite capital, and now in the Museum of the Ancient Orient in Istanbul, which dates them to 1269 BCE. It is written in neither Hittite nor Egyptian but in Akkadian, and in the Mesopotamian script the Hittites had borrowed.","credit":"Photograph by Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Two fragments of a thick clay tablet mounted on a clear perspex stand, the larger covered edge to edge in fine wedge-shaped cuneiform and cracked into pieces that have been fitted back together."}},
{"id":"wh-267","num":267,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"The Lions' Gate, a rock sanctuary of carved gods and an archive on its citadel all belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Hittite capital high on the Anatolian plateau.","questions":["Long read as a city sacked at the end of the Bronze Age, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now looks to have been emptied by its own administration first and burnt only afterwards.","Juniper rings record a severe run of dry years around 1198 BCE, just as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the Hittite state that ruled from it were given up for good."],"answer":"Hattusa","answerText":"Hattusa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yazılıkaya</span><span class=\"dt-v\">cult site from c. 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1198 – 1196 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hattusa</b> in central Anatolia was the capital of the Hittite kingdom and empire, the political and religious centre of one of the major powers of the ancient Near East between about 1650 and 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From it the Hittites held central, southern and southeastern Anatolia together with the northern Levant and northern Syria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site is on the World Heritage list for its urban organisation and for what survives of its temples, royal residences and fortifications, above all the sculpture of the Lions', the Sphinxes' and the Royal Gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its defences had to be carried across a gorge, and that stretch survives as cuttings and traces worked into the rock rather than as standing wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The citadel of Büyükkale held the archives, and the first season of digging there brought out some 2,500 tablets and fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> About 3 kilometres (2 miles) to the north lies Yazılıkaya, a labyrinth of limestone slabs that had been a place of worship since the 16th century BCE and was made over by Hattusili III and his son Tudhaliya IV into a setting for the kingdom's pantheon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two processions of gods are carved along its main chamber, one female and one male, meeting on the back wall in the goddess Hebat and the storm god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Feeding the place took a vast storage silo in the early Hittite period and later a scatter of smaller ones, alongside heavy clearance of the landscape and a farming strategy that leaned on water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Juniper rings from central Anatolia record an unusually severe run of dry years from about 1198 to 1196 BCE, at the point the Hittite state gave way along with the rest of the Late Bronze Age world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city was long read as the victim of a sack, but excavation now indicates that it was emptied by the royal administration first and burnt only afterwards, and why it was given up is still not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","bronze age"],"sources":["Sturt W. Manning, Cindy Kocik, Brita Lorentzen and Jed P. Sparks, “Severe multi-year drought coincident with Hittite collapse around 1198–1196 BC,” <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7949 (2023): 719–24, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05693-y. [Open access]","L. Repola, M. Marazzi and S. Tilia, “Constructing and Representing: A New Project for 3D Surveying of Yazılıkaya – Hattuša,” <i>International Archives of the Photogrammetry, Remote Sensing and Spatial Information Sciences</i> XLII-5/W1 (2017): 395–402, https://doi.org/10.5194/isprs-archives-XLII-5-W1-395-2017. [Open access]","Leopoldo Repola, “Extraction of Constructive Geometries from Numerical Models: The City Walls in the Hattusa Gorge,” <i>Disegnare con</i> 16, no. 30 (2023): 10.1–10.13, https://disegnarecon.univaq.it/ojs/index.php/disegnarecon/article/view/1103. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, “The Hittites and the Aegean World,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 2 (1974): 2–10, https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-hittites-and-the-aegean-world/. [Open access]","David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Lion_Gate%2C_Hattusa_01.jpg/1280px-Lion_Gate%2C_Hattusa_01.jpg","title":"The Lion Gate","desc":"One of the three carved gateways the site is best known for, with the Sphinxes' and the Royal Gates. The lions face outward from the jamb stones, and past the gap the wall runs on over bare upland; the same circuit had to be carried across a gorge, where it survives only as cuttings worked into the rock.","credit":"Photograph by Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A gap in a wall of huge rough-cut blocks, flanked by two lions carved in relief out of the massive jamb stones, mouths open and facing outward; beyond the gate the wall runs on across dry open upland under a blue sky."}},
{"id":"wh-268","num":268,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"A port whose wealth came from opening the Mediterranean onto the Syrian interior, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> traded with the Aegean, Cyprus, Egypt and western Asia until it was destroyed.","questions":["Tablets recording frantic preparations against the Sea Peoples, and a burnt layer with arrowheads scattered through it, date the fall of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to about 1190 BCE.","The alphabetic cuneiform of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to the Northwest Semitic tradition from which the Greek alphabet later came, and its texts already mark where words end."],"answer":"Ugarit","answerText":"Ugarit","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zenith</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1192 – 1190 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ramesses III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1188 BCE or 1177 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ugarit</b> was a Bronze Age port kingdom on the north Syrian coast, and its wealth came from a position that opened the Mediterranean directly onto the Syrian heartland, Anatolia and Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its harbour towns sat inside a network of long-distance trade and exchange running between the Aegean, Cyprus, the Levant, Egypt and western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper reached that coast as the four-handled oxhide ingots found from Cyprus to Sardinia, and Ras Shamra, the modern name of the site, is among the places they turn up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The kingdom belonged to the linkage of Aegean, Egyptian, Syro-Palestinian and Hittite civilisations that made up the Late Bronze Age world of the eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Writing there was alphabetic cuneiform, a set of wedge-shaped signs used as letters rather than as syllables, and one of the Northwest Semitic systems from whose tradition the Greek alphabet later came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Ugaritic texts already mark where one word ends and the next begins, most of them along the lines of the spoken phrase and a minority, found chiefly in everyday documents, along the grammar instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tablets give a glimpse of the frantic preparations the city and its neighbours made, in vain, against the invasions of the Sea Peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Gibala on the kingdom's southern edge a destruction layer holds bronze arrowheads scattered through the town, walls thrown down, houses burnt and the ash of the fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon on that layer, anchored to Hittite, Levantine and Egyptian kings and to astronomical observation, dates the terminal destructions of the northern Levant to 1192 to 1190 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ramesses III afterwards claimed that no land had been able to stand before them, and listed Hatti, Carchemish, Arzawa and Alashiya as cut off at one time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","bronze age"],"sources":["David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Karel Van Lerberghe, Tom Boiy, Klaas Vansteenhuyse et al., “The Sea Peoples, from Cuneiform Tablets to Carbon Dating,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 6 (2011): e20232, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0020232. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","Joseph Lam, review of <i>The Semantics of Word Division in Northwest Semitic Writing Systems: Ugaritic, Phoenician, Hebrew, Moabite and Greek</i>, by Robert S. D. Crellin, Contexts of and Relations between Early Writing Systems, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.22/. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Cindy Kocik, Brita Lorentzen and Jed P. Sparks, “Severe multi-year drought coincident with Hittite collapse around 1198–1196 BC,” <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7949 (2023): 719–24, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05693-y. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Syrien%2C_historische_Stadt_Ugarit.1.JPG/1280px-Syrien%2C_historische_Stadt_Ugarit.1.JPG","title":"Ras Shamra, the site of Ugarit","desc":"The excavated city on the Syrian coast, its wall footings standing a course or two high among the spring flowers. The cuneiform tablets that made the place famous came out of this ground; the destruction that ended the kingdom is dated at its southern harbour town to about 1190 BCE.","credit":"Photograph by Feldstein, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Low stone wall footings of many small rooms laid out across a green field of grass and wildflowers, standing only a course or two high, with trees and a modern town along the horizon."}},
{"id":"wh-269","num":269,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Cut on rock faces at an Egyptian mining site in the Sinai, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest alphabetic writing anyone has found, and it is only partly readable.","questions":["Borrowing the shapes of hieroglyphs without the system behind them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was devised among Semitic speakers working inside Egypt as miners, builders and mercenaries.","Latin, Cyrillic, Arabic and Devanagari all descend from the tradition the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened, which needs signs in tens where older systems needed them in hundreds."],"answer":"Proto-Sinaitic script","answerText":"Proto-Sinaitic script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wadi el-Hol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1850 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or as late as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Proto-Sinaitic script</b> is the earliest known alphabetic writing, linear pictographic signs cut on statuettes, stone panels and rock faces at Serabit el-Khadem, an Egyptian mining site in the Sinai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A closely similar script turned up in the 1990s on two single-line rock inscriptions at Wadi el-Hol, in the desert near Thebes in Upper Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither group has a stratified archaeological context, so the dating is argued over, some placing them at the opening of the 2nd millennium BCE and others as late as the 14th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The signs plainly take their shapes from hieroglyphs and are just as plainly not used as Egyptian used them, so the alphabet was invented among Semitic speakers inside the Egyptian realm, borrowing the pictures without the system behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Asiatics worked in Egypt through the Middle Kingdom as builders, miners and mercenaries, which is the setting such an invention needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Each sign is held to stand for the first sound of the Semitic word its picture names, and it is on that assumption that the Sinai texts have been partially read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they give up are phrases like 'for the Lady' and 'chief of the miners'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The assumption is disputed, on the ground that most letters of the consonantal script carry no association with any particular object at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is not disputed is the economy of the thing: an alphabet needs signs in tens where a logographic or syllabic system needs them in hundreds, and this earliest West Semitic version wrote consonants alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Latin, Cyrillic, Arabic, Devanagari and Bengali all descend from that tradition, and Greek with them, though a script easier to learn does not by itself make a population literate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","technology","archaeology","writing","language","bronze age"],"sources":["Joseph Lam, “The Invention and Development of the Alphabet,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 189–92, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","Christopher Woods, “Visible Language: The Earliest Writing Systems,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 21, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","L. R. Siddall, review of <i>Writing: Theory and History of the Technology of Civilization</i>, by Barry B. Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.04.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.04.24/. [Open access]","Joseph Lam, review of <i>The Semantics of Word Division in Northwest Semitic Writing Systems: Ugaritic, Phoenician, Hebrew, Moabite and Greek</i>, by Robert S. D. Crellin, Contexts of and Relations between Early Writing Systems, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.22/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Proto-Sinaitic_inscription_number_345_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Sinai 345, drawn in 1916","desc":"Alan Gardiner's drawing of one of the inscriptions from Serabit el-Khadem. Its signs are recorded from four places on a small carved animal, the shoulder, the two sides and the space between the paws, and it was on drawings of this kind that the script was first partly read.","credit":"Drawing by Alan H. Gardiner, published 1916, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A line drawing of four short rows of simple pictographic signs copied from an inscribed object, among them a hand, an eye, a fish and a wavy line, each row labelled for where on the object it was cut, with the number 345 beneath."}},
{"id":"wh-270","num":270,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Kings addressing each other as brothers and mayors begging for troops share one box of 382 tablets, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the city Akhenaten built as his capital.","questions":["Written mostly in Akkadian whatever the sender spoke at home, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the diplomatic correspondence of an Egyptian court in the 14th century BCE.","Counted as a network, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> split in two, the great kings organising around trade and the Syrian mayors sitting in a web structured by war."],"answer":"Amarna letters","answerText":"Amarna letters","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1365 – 1330 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1352 – 1338 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Amarna letters</b> are the diplomatic correspondence of the Egyptian court in the 14th century BCE, 382 clay tablets from the city Akhenaten built as his capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A woman of Amarna came upon nearly 400 of them in 1887, and Petrie afterwards located the building they had come from and found more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most are addressed to Amenhotep III and then to his successor Akhenaten, and the whole archive covers a window of only fifteen to thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are written mostly in Akkadian, a dialect of Babylonian and the language diplomacy used across the whole region, whatever the sender spoke at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some forty are to or from the rulers Egypt treated as great kings, those of Babylonia, Mitanni and Hatti, whose own empire ran from central Anatolia into the northern Levant, with Assyria joining once Mitanni fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Between those kings the language is brotherhood, and the business is precious goods and the marriages of royal women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rest of the archive is different in kind, the mayors of the Syrian and Palestinian towns writing about raids and rivals and asking for troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Counted as a network the split is sharp: the great kings organise their exchanges around trade, while the mayors sit in a web structured by war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything at the site is closely dated, the city having been lived in for little more than a decade, so the quantities of Mycenaean pottery there against almost no Minoan are read as a sign of who by then dominated Aegean trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives is one palace's in-tray rather than a whole world, and it leans heavily on Rib-Hadda of Byblos, whose letters take up so much of the dossier that his persistence bends the picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["text","history","archaeology","writing","bronze age"],"sources":["Clément Dutrey, “Distribution de l’information et stratégies relationnelles dans le corpus de correspondances amarniennes : approche par l’analyse de réseaux,” <i>Journal of Historical Network Research</i> 6, no. 1 (2021): 1–40, https://doi.org/10.25517/jhnr.v6i1.85. [Open access]","“Amarna: The Cuneiform Tablets,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/cuneiform.html. [Open access]","Alex Aissaoui, “The Amarna Diplomacy in IR Perspective: A System of States in the Making,” <i>Estudos Internacionais</i> 6, no. 2 (2018): 9–29, https://periodicos.pucminas.br/index.php/estudosinternacionais/article/view/14069. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Cindy Kocik, Brita Lorentzen and Jed P. Sparks, “Severe multi-year drought coincident with Hittite collapse around 1198–1196 BC,” <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7949 (2023): 719–24, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05693-y. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/EA_256_amarna_letter.jpg","title":"One letter, EA 256","desc":"Knudtzon's 1915 copy of a single tablet from the archive, its cuneiform set out obverse, reverse and edges with the lines numbered down the sides and the names of sender and recipient at the head. His edition is still how the corpus is referred to, every letter in it carrying an EA number.","credit":"Hand copy by Jørgen Alexander Knudtzon, Die El-Amarna-Tafeln, 1915, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A printed line drawing of a clay tablet's inscription, rows of wedge-shaped cuneiform signs arranged in ruled blocks labelled obverse and reverse, with line numbers down both sides and the heading EA 256 above."}},
{"id":"wh-271","num":271,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Goods from at least seven cultures lay in the single hull of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from Baltic amber and African ebony to Cypriot copper and a Kassite cylinder seal.","questions":["Unlike a palace store filled over decades, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds one voyage's goods together, everything that was aboard on the day the ship went down.","More than a hundred cobalt blue glass ingots came up from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, raw Egyptian glass of the sort Mycenaean beadmakers imported and worked into jewellery."],"answer":"Uluburun shipwreck","answerText":"Uluburun shipwreck","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1320 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Uluburun shipwreck</b> is a merchant ship that went down off south-western Turkey about 1320 BCE, the densest single record of Late Bronze Age exchange yet found, with goods from at least seven cultures in one hull.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its bulk cargo was raw metal, roughly 360 copper ingots and 160 tin ingots weighing some 12 tons, the two in about the ten-to-one ratio that makes bronze, though where the tin was mined is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of the copper travelled as oxhide ingots, four-handled slabs of about 23 kilograms (51 pounds) cast from Cypriot ore, a shape found from Syria and Crete as far west as Sicily and Sardinia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around the metal lay materials from far beyond the sea the ship was crossing: African ebony logs, elephant tusks, hippopotamus teeth, ostrich eggshells, Baltic amber beads and a stone mace of northern Balkan type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There were also more than a hundred cobalt blue glass ingots, raw glass made in Egypt of the kind Mycenaean beadmakers imported, which the Amarna letters call mekku-stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What makes a wreck worth more than a storeroom is that it is a moment rather than an accumulation, a miniature of the whole eastern Mediterranean trade caught on one day of one voyage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A palace store holds goods that came in over decades and a tomb what mourners chose to bury, so neither shows what one ship was carrying at once. Read that way the cargo argues for a circuit rather than a line: the Levantine coast to Cyprus, on to the Aegean, sometimes west to Sardinia, then home past North Africa and Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also separates the traffic, raw materials moving in bulk while small finished luxuries of ivory, gold and carved stone travelled among them as the gifts kings sent one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whose ship it was is still argued: the crew's weapons, galley wares and lutes look Syro-Canaanite while other belongings point to passengers from the Greek mainland, and such vessels may have had no nationality at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","object","archaeology","history","bronze age","turkey","cyprus"],"sources":["\"Uluburun Late Bronze Age Shipwreck Excavation,\" Institute of Nautical Archaeology, https://nauticalarch.org/projects/uluburun-late-bronze-age-shipwreck-excavation/. [Open access]","N. Fawcett and J. C. Zietsman, \"Uluburun – The Discovery and Excavation of the World's Oldest Known Shipwreck,\" <i>Akroterion</i> 46 (2001): 5–20, https://doi.org/10.7445/46-0-116. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, \"Archaeometry and Shipwrecks: A Review Article,\" <i>Expedition</i> 53, no. 1 (2011), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/archaeometry-and-shipwrecks/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"Why Central Asia's Mushiston Is Not a Source for the Late Bronze Age Tin Ingots from the Uluburun Shipwreck,\" <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 11 (2023): 1211478, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2023.1211478. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Bodrum_Museum_Uluburun_shipwreck_reconstruction_in_2015_3707.jpg","title":"The Uluburun ship reconstructed, Bodrum Museum of Underwater Archaeology","desc":"A full-size reconstruction showing how the cargo was stowed in the hull, with the copper oxhide ingots stacked low along the keel and the jars and smaller goods packed around them.","credit":"Photo by Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodrum_Museum_Uluburun_shipwreck_reconstruction_in_2015_3707.jpg","alt":"A reconstructed wooden ship's hull seen from above in a museum, its floor filled with rows of dark four-handled metal ingots and rounded storage jars."}},
{"id":"wh-272","num":272,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Copper lies all round the Near East, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that hardens it into bronze lies in no verified deposit there, so every bronze workshop had to import it.","questions":["Donkey caravans from Assur crossed more than 1,000 kilometres of Anatolia carrying <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and textiles, and the merchants' letters at Kanesh count it out by the talent.","Panned from stream beds like alluvial gold, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leaves no trace once a deposit is worked out, which is why its ancient sources are still being argued over."],"answer":"tin","answerText":"tin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named in texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the mid-3rd millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Anatolian wars</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1895 – 1835 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tin</b> is the metal that turns copper into bronze, and hardly any of it occurs anywhere near the societies that used the most of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was won not from hard rock but from cassiterite, an oxide weathered out of granite and washed down into stream beds, where it was panned much as prospectors later panned for gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is part of why the ancient workings are so hard to find, since a tin stream panned out in antiquity leaves nothing behind, though the granite that fed it remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No verified deposits worth the name lie in Mesopotamia, Egypt, the Levant or the Aegean, which is why tin has been called the mystery metal of the ancient Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sumerian <i>AN.NA</i> and Akkadian <i>annaku</i> both mean 'tin', the earliest such texts date from the middle of the 3rd millennium BCE, and Gudea of Lagash said in the 22nd century BCE that his came from Meluhha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because the metal had to travel, the tin trade is the earliest long-distance commerce in a bulk commodity that survives in its own traders' writing. From the start of the 2nd millennium BCE merchants from Assur drove donkey caravans more than 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) into central Anatolia, where no single power ruled and each city hosted a merchant colony called a <i>karum</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their letters from Kanesh count tin by the talent beside the textiles it travelled with and the silver it came home as, and record about seven wars among the Anatolian kingdoms, between 1895 and 1835 BCE, that shut the roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Mari itinerary a century later carries tin on westward through Aleppo, Qatna, Ebla and Hazor to a man at Ugarit whose title may mean Cretan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where any of it was mined is still argued, with the Taurus, Central Asia and Cornwall all read out of the isotopes, so a trade documented in thousands of letters has no agreed source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","history","bronze age","geology"],"sources":["Robert Maddin, Tamara Stech Wheeler, and James D. Muhly, \"Tin in the Ancient Near East: Old Questions and New Finds,\" <i>Expedition</i> 19, no. 2 (1977), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/tin-in-the-ancient-near-east/. [Open access]","İrfan Albayrak and Melike Zoroğlu, \"Eski Asurca Belgeler Işığında Eski Anadolu'da Yol ve Ticaret Güvenliği,\" <i>Ankara Üniversitesi Dil ve Tarih-Coğrafya Fakültesi Dergisi</i> 63, no. 2 (2023): 1358–83, https://doi.org/10.33171/dtcfjournal.2023.63.2.19. [Open access]","Wayne Powell et al., \"Tin from Uluburun Shipwreck Shows Small-Scale Commodity Exchange Fueled Continental Tin Supply across Late Bronze Age Eurasia,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 48 (2022): eabq3766, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9710885/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"Isotope Systematics and Chemical Composition of Tin Ingots from Mochlos (Crete) and Other Late Bronze Age Sites in the Eastern Mediterranean Sea: An Ultimate Key to Tin Provenance?,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0218326, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218326. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"Why Central Asia's Mushiston Is Not a Source for the Late Bronze Age Tin Ingots from the Uluburun Shipwreck,\" <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 11 (2023): 1211478, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2023.1211478. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Letter_sent_by_two_Assyrian_merchants_to_their_partner_discussing_various_aspects_of_their_business_%2C1900-1800_BCE_Kultepe%2C_Turkey_Fitzwilliam_Museum%2C_Cambridge.jpg","title":"A letter between Assyrian merchants at Kanesh, 1900–1800 BCE","desc":"Two merchants write to their partner about their business. Thousands of such letters survive from the Assyrian colony at Kanesh, and they are the fullest record anywhere of how the tin trade was actually run. Fitzwilliam Museum, Cambridge.","credit":"Photo by SpeakingArch, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Letter_sent_by_two_Assyrian_merchants_to_their_partner_discussing_various_aspects_of_their_business_%2C1900-1800_BCE_Kultepe%2C_Turkey_Fitzwilliam_Museum%2C_Cambridge.jpg","alt":"A small rectangular clay tablet standing upright in a museum case, its surface covered in closely packed rows of wedge-shaped cuneiform signs."}},
{"id":"wh-273","num":273,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Copper daggers, flint arrowheads, stone wristguards and an inverted-bell drinking pot travel together as the package of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from Iberia to Britain after 2750 BCE.","questions":["Ancient DNA gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two different answers at once, since its pots reached Iberia without migrants while in Britain they came with a near-total change of population.","Named for a vessel shape rather than a place or a people, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had crossed western Europe and northwest Africa by 2500 BCE and vanished before 1800 BCE."],"answer":"Bell Beaker culture","answerText":"Bell Beaker culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Spread from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Disappeared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2200 – 1800 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Bell Beaker culture</b> is the assemblage of grave goods that spread across western and central Europe from about 2750 BCE and had gone again between 2200 and 1800 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is named for its pot, a drinking vessel with an inverted-bell profile, and it travels as a package with a copper dagger, barbed flint arrowheads, a stone wristguard and V-perforated buttons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest radiocarbon dates for Beaker pottery are around 2750 BCE in Atlantic Iberia, which is one reason that coast is proposed as its birthplace, though the Lower Rhine and several separate origins are argued as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 2500 BCE the complex reached across western Europe and into northwest Africa, France, Italy and central Europe, and within another century it was in Britain and Ireland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lipid residues in a small set of Beaker vessels from central Germany point to dairy products, one of the few direct signs of what the pots actually held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Whether that spread carried people or only objects was argued over for a century, and ancient DNA has now answered it differently in each region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genome-wide data from 400 prehistoric Europeans, 226 of them buried with Beakers, found only slight affinity between Iberia and central Europe, and a separate transect of Iberian genomes likewise finds little outside influence there between the Neolithic and the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Britain the same package arrived with steppe-related ancestry, and about 90 per cent of the island's gene pool was replaced within a few hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Bohemia 271 genomes show the Beaker groups losing their Y-chromosome diversity outright about 2400 BCE, while their overall ancestry shifted back towards the Neolithic farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The wristguards make a smaller version of the same point: they are so unworn that they read as prestige objects, yet the bones of the Bohemian men buried with them suggest they had drawn bows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","bronze age","europe","genetics"],"sources":["Iñigo Olalde et al., \"The Beaker Phenomenon and the Genomic Transformation of Northwest Europe,\" <i>Nature</i> 555, no. 7695 (2018): 190–96, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5973796/. [Open access]","Jessica Ryan, Jocelyn Desideri, and Marie Besse, \"Bell Beaker Archers: Warriors or an Ideology?,\" <i>Journal of Neolithic Archaeology</i> (2018): 97–122, https://doi.org/10.12766/jna.2018S.6. [Open access]","Luka Papac et al., \"Dynamic Changes in Genomic and Social Structures in Third Millennium BCE Central Europe,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 35 (2021): eabi6941, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8386934/. [Open access]","Cristina Valdiosera et al., \"Four Millennia of Iberian Biomolecular Prehistory Illustrate the Impact of Prehistoric Migrations at the Far End of Eurasia,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 13 (2018): 3428–33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5879675/. [Open access]","Adrià Breu et al., \"Pottery Spilled the Beans: Patterns in the Processing and Consumption of Dietary Lipids in Central Germany from the Early Neolithic to the Late Bronze Age,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301278, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301278. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/-2350_Hockergrab_%28Glockenbecherkultur%2C_2500-2200_v._Chr.%29_Nationalmuseum_Prag_anagoria.jpg","title":"A Bell Beaker burial from Tisice, Bohemia, 2500–2200 BCE","desc":"The body laid on its side with the knees drawn up, the single-grave rite of the eastern half of the Beaker world. It is the Bohemian cemeteries of burials like this one that carry the ancient DNA and the skeletal evidence discussed here. National Museum, Prague.","credit":"Photo by anagoria, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:-2350_Hockergrab_%28Glockenbecherkultur%2C_2500-2200_v._Chr.%29_Nationalmuseum_Prag_anagoria.jpg","alt":"A human skeleton in a museum display, lying on its side with the legs sharply bent up towards the chest, laid in a shallow bed of earth."}},
{"id":"wh-274","num":274,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Flat graves for almost everybody and a few great mounds heaped with gold mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, central Europe's first society to divide its dead by rank.","questions":["Ancient DNA from cemeteries of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows men staying where they were born and women marrying in, so sons inherited the farmstead and daughters left.","Named after a village near Prague, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran from about 2200 to 1550 BCE and left princely tombs at Leubingen, Helmsdorf and Łęki Małe."],"answer":"Únětice culture","answerText":"Únětice culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2200 – 1550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leubingen tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1942 BCE, give or take ten years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Łęki Małe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2130 – 1800 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Únětice culture</b> is the Early Bronze Age culture of central Europe, current from about 2200 to 1550 BCE across Bohemia, Moravia, central Germany and western Poland, and named after a village near Prague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the first society in temperate Europe whose graves divide people sharply by rank: most of its dead lie in flat inhumation graves, while a few were buried under great mounds with gold and bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The richest of those, at Leubingen in central Germany, was raised over a timber chamber whose oak has been tree-ring dated to 1942 BCE, give or take ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Łęki Małe in Greater Poland a chain of such barrows went up between about 2130 and 1800 BCE beside the fortified settlement of Bruszczewo, a differentiated society that held together for perhaps fourteen generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The man in the princely tomb at Helmsdorf did not die in bed: three of his bones carry injuries from a bladed weapon made around the moment of death, placed as though someone meant to kill him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In Bohemia, at the culture's core, 271 genomes spanning the 3rd millennium record a population turnover of at least 40 per cent as this new social order emerged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ancient DNA shows how such a society was held together: 46 people buried at Leubingen resolve into five family pedigrees, a community organised patrilineally in which men stayed and women came in from elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Far to the south, in Lower Austria, 129 genomes from Únětice and neighbouring cemeteries give the same picture, and carry stretches of shared DNA linking communities a long way apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sons inherited the farmstead and daughters married out, which is how a landholding elite reproduces itself, and here it can be seen centuries before any written law says so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The grave goods follow the same logic: at Leubingen how much a person was buried with varied with sex, age and where they had come from, while what kinds of object they were given did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","bronze age","europe","genetics"],"sources":["Sandra Penske et al., \"Kinship Practices at the Early Bronze Age Site of Leubingen in Central Germany,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 3871, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10873355/. [Open access]","Janusz Czebreszuk et al., \"Chronology and Social Significance of the 'Princely' Barrow Cemetery in Łęki Małe and the Central European Early Bronze Age,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0300591, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0300591. [Open access]","Nicole Nicklisch et al., \"Bioarchaeological Investigations of the Princely Grave at Helmsdorf Attesting to the Violent Death of an Early Bronze Age Ruler,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 16139, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9515160/. [Open access]","Anja Furtwängler et al., \"Tracing Social Mechanisms and Interregional Connections in Early Bronze Age Societies in Lower Austria,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2025): 131, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12775072/. [Open access]","Luka Papac et al., \"Dynamic Changes in Genomic and Social Structures in Third Millennium BCE Central Europe,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 35 (2021): eabi6941, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8386934/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/MUFT_-_Leubingen_F%C3%BCrstengrab_1_Rekonstruktion.jpg","title":"The Leubingen princely grave, reconstructed","desc":"A museum reconstruction of the timber chamber under the Leubingen mound and the way its occupant was laid out with his metalwork. Museum for Prehistory in Thuringia, Weimar.","credit":"Photo by Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MUFT_-_Leubingen_F%C3%BCrstengrab_1_Rekonstruktion.jpg","alt":"A museum display of a tent-shaped wooden chamber built of split logs, with a figure laid out inside it on the floor among small metal objects."}},
{"id":"wh-275","num":275,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Gold on bronze marks a sun, a crescent and a scatter of stars on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them a cluster of seven usually read as the Pleiades.","questions":["Copper from a mine in Salzburg and gold from a Cornish river went into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so a picture of the sky is also a map of Bronze Age trade.","Rather than being cast in one piece, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was hammered out of a flat blank over about ten heatings, spiralling outwards from its centre."],"answer":"Nebra sky disc","answerText":"Nebra sky disc","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hoard buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Disc made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 to 200 years earlier</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carnon pick</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1620 – 1497 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Nebra sky disc</b> is a bronze disc about 32 centimetres (13 inches) across, inlaid with gold showing a sun or full moon, a crescent and a scatter of stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A cluster of seven of those stars is widely read as the Pleiades, and on that reading the disc is a gauge for when to add a leap month and keep the lunar and solar years together, though not everyone accepts it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was buried on the Mittelberg hill in Saxony-Anhalt with two swords, two axes, two spiral arm-rings and a chisel, and those bronzes date the hoard to about 1600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The disc itself is probably a century or two older than the burial, and it belongs to the Únětice culture of Early Bronze Age central Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is not a casting: the metal was cast flat and then hot-forged, heated and hammered about ten times, working outwards in a spiral from the centre, in an alloy of copper with about 2.6 per cent tin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What lifts it above being a beautiful object is that its materials come from opposite ends of Europe, and that the picture on it was edited. The copper matches the ores of the Mitterberg mine in Salzburg so closely that other European deposits have been ruled out, while the gold matches natural gold from the Carnon River in Cornwall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An antler pick from the alluvial workings of the Carnon valley has been radiocarbon dated to 1620 – 1497 BCE, showing that those streams were being worked in the same era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The disc was altered over generations rather than made at one moment, and the arcs along its edges and the curved shape at its foot were added long after the first image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the reading of the team that published it, that first version encodes real astronomical knowledge, which later hands replaced with a plainer concern for where the sun rises and sets, and at last with myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","archaeology","history","bronze age","europe","astronomy"],"sources":["Sebastian Dieck et al., \"Archaeometallurgical Investigation of the Nebra Sky Disc,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 28868, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11582812/. [Open access]","Ernst Pernicka et al., \"Why the Nebra Sky Disc Dates to the Early Bronze Age: An Overview of the Interdisciplinary Results,\" <i>Archaeologia Austriaca</i> 104 (2020): 89–122, https://doi.org/10.1553/archaeologia104s89. [Open access]","State Museum of Prehistory, Halle, \"Nebra Sky Disc,\" https://www.landesmuseum-vorgeschichte.de/en/nebra-sky-disc. [Open access]","\"Nebra Sky Disc,\" Memory of the World Register, UNESCO, https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/nebra-sky-disc. [Open access]","Sandra Penske et al., \"Kinship Practices at the Early Bronze Age Site of Leubingen in Central Germany,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 3871, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10873355/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Himmelsscheibe_von_Nebra_-_Landesmuseum_f%C3%BCr_Vorgeschichte_in_Halle_-_HD.jpg","title":"The Nebra sky disc, State Museum of Prehistory, Halle","desc":"The original disc, where it is kept. The gold circle, crescent and scattered dots belong to the first version of the image; the arc along the right edge and the curved shape at the foot were added later.","credit":"Photo by Shonagon, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Himmelsscheibe_von_Nebra_-_Landesmuseum_f%C3%BCr_Vorgeschichte_in_Halle_-_HD.jpg","alt":"A round blue-green bronze disc inlaid with gold shapes: a full circle, a crescent, scattered dots including a tight cluster of seven, an arc along one edge and a curved boat-like shape at the bottom."}},
{"id":"wh-276","num":276,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"No copper and no tin lay in the ground of southern Scandinavia, so every bronze of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was carried in from the Alps, Slovakia or Britain.","questions":["Hollowed oak coffins under mounds have kept the wool, hair and skin of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so we know what its people wore and not only what they were buried with.","Thousands of ships cut into rock outcrops are the commonest image left by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose crossings to Norway ran out of sight of land for tens of kilometres."],"answer":"Nordic Bronze Age","answerText":"Nordic Bronze Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze Age</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oak coffins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1100 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Nordic Bronze Age</b> is the Bronze Age of southern Scandinavia, from about 1700 to 500 BCE, and it is the richest metal-using culture in Europe to have had no metal of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Analysis of 550 Scandinavian bronzes puts the start about 2100 BCE through the Únětice hubs of central Germany, carrying high-impurity metal from the Austrian Inn valley and Slovakia and high-tin metal from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Únětice world fell about 1600 BCE the supply faltered and old stock was melted down again, but the break opened a direct route to the Carpathian basin and the eastern Alps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That same eastern route carried Baltic amber south, and by 1600 BCE it was reaching Mediterranean cities in quantity, which is the likeliest thing the north was paying with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the region put at the centre of its imagery was not the land but the sea, and thousands of boat images are cut into outcrops across southern Scandinavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Simulating the crossing between Jutland and south-west Norway suggests the direct open-water route of about 110 kilometres (68 miles), out of sight of land for up to 50, was preferred to 700 kilometres of coast-hugging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The culture is also the best preserved in Bronze Age Europe, since its elite were laid in hollowed oak coffins under mounds between about 1500 and 1100 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The waterlogged and slightly acid conditions inside dissolved the bones but kept the wool, hair and skin, so a young woman buried at Egtved 3,400 years ago is known by her corded skirt and blouse rather than by her skeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strontium in her teeth, hair and wool was read in 2015 as showing that she was born abroad and had travelled far in her final months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A 2019 study found that agricultural lime has shifted the strontium of modern Danish water, and that lime-free values put her origin close to her grave instead, a question now reopened against a baseline built from 628 ancient people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","culture","archaeology","bronze age","europe","history"],"sources":["Heide W. Nørgaard, Ernst Pernicka, and Helle Vandkilde, \"Shifting Networks and Mixing Metals: Changing Metal Trade Routes to Scandinavia Correlate with Neolithic and Bronze Age Transformations,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 6 (2021): e0252376, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8208583/. [Open access]","Marta Díaz-Guardamino et al., \"Boats on the Rocks: Late Prehistoric Nautical Iconography and Landscape, from Northwest Iberia to Scandinavia,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 6 (2026): e0349417, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13249362/. [Open access]","Boel Bengtsson et al., \"Seafaring and Navigation in the Nordic Bronze Age: The Application of an Ocean Voyaging Tool,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 4 (2025): e0320791, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11964266/. [Open access]","Karin Margarita Frei et al., \"Tracing the Dynamic Life Story of a Bronze Age Female,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 5 (2015): 10431, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4440039/. [Open access]","Erik Thomsen and Rasmus Andreasen, \"Agricultural Lime Disturbs Natural Strontium Isotope Variations: Implications for Provenance and Migration Studies,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 3 (2019): eaav8083, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6415960/. [Open access]","Karin Margarita Frei et al., \"Bronze Age Non-Elite Mobility in Denmark Examined through a New Human-Based Strontium Isotope Baseline,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 2 (2026): e0341434, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12880727/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/DO-4368-Egtvedpigens_dragt.jpg","title":"The Egtved girl's clothing, as it lay in the coffin","desc":"The short corded skirt, the blouse and the bronze belt disc of a young woman buried in an oak coffin in Jutland about 3,400 years ago. The bones dissolved in the acid water inside; the wool did not. National Museum of Denmark.","credit":"Photo by Roberto Fortuna and Kira Ursem, Nationalmuseet, Denmark, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DO-4368-Egtvedpigens_dragt.jpg","alt":"Brown woollen clothing laid out as found in a grave: a short skirt of hanging cords below a round bronze plate, with a short-sleeved top above it."}},
{"id":"wh-277","num":277,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Walled mud-brick towns in the desert oases of Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which traded with the Indus, Mesopotamia and the Syro-Anatolian world.","questions":["Copper from Iran and tin from a Tajik mine went into the bronze of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose farmers were also growing grapes in four cultivated varieties.","Genetically the people of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> descend from the region's own Chalcolithic farmers, and today's Tajiks and Yagnobi descend in turn from them."],"answer":"Oxus civilisation","answerText":"Oxus civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Main phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2300 – 1950 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gonur Depe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2500 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-2nd millennium BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Oxus civilisation</b>, also called the Bactria-Margiana Archaeological Complex, is the Bronze Age culture of southern Central Asia, in the oases of what are now Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan and northern Afghanistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It built the first proto-urban centres of the region, walled compounds of mud brick holding thousands of people and watered by irrigation, one of which, Ulug-depe, covers 13 hectares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its farmers grew grapes as well as grain, and pips from Sapalli and Djarkutan in southern Uzbekistan fall into four shapes under cultivation, those from a temple larger and closer to domesticated forms than those from ordinary houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its bronze was made from copper out of polymetallic deposits in Iran and from the copper-tin mine at Mushiston in Tajikistan, the first tin deposit anywhere tied directly to finished bronzes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later it came to lean on metal from the territory of the Andronovo herders to the north, whose own copper came from the Tian Shan mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the civilisation matter is where it sat, in a network of exchange called the Middle Asian Interaction Sphere that ran to the Indus, to Mesopotamia and to the Syro-Anatolian world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Gonur Depe, the administrative and religious centre of ancient Margiana, sacred rooms held miniature stone columns shaped like a chess rook, cut from onyx, marble, gypsum and limestone, and nobody knows what they were for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its people were not incomers: genetically they descend from the local Chalcolithic population of the region, with only occasional individuals of steppe or South Asian ancestry among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Indo-Iranian speakers of Central Asia today, the Tajiks and the Yagnobi, draw their ancestry from those same people, with steppe gene flow added later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The civilisation ended in the middle of the 2nd millennium BCE, its great sites giving way to small villages spread over a wider area, at a pace that differed from valley to valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","bronze age","asia"],"sources":["Perle Guarino-Vignon et al., \"Genetic Analysis of a Bronze Age Individual from Ulug-Depe (Turkmenistan),\" <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 13 (2022): 884612, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9441711/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"The Rise of Bronze in Central Asia: New Evidence for the Origin of Bronze Age Tin and Copper from Multi-Analytical Research,\" <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 11 (2023): 1224873, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2023.1224873. [Open access]","Guanhan Chen et al., \"Morphotype Broadening of the Grapevine (<i>Vitis vinifera</i> L.) from Oxus Civilization 4000 Years Ago,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 16331, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9522827/. [Open access]","Élise Luneau, \"Effondrement ou évolution de la civilisation de l'Oxus ? Une révision de la transition de l'âge du Bronze à l'âge du Fer en Asie centrale méridionale,\" <i>Les Nouvelles de l'Archéologie</i> 163 (2021): 36–43, https://doi.org/10.4000/nda.11987. [Open access]","Natalia Nikolaevna Skakun and Vera Vladimirovna Terekhina, \"Manufacturing Technology of Stone Miniature Columns from the Bronze Age Site Gonur Depe, Southern Turkmenistan,\" <i>Journal of Lithic Studies</i> 8, no. 2 (2021), https://doi.org/10.2218/jls.4490. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/BMAC_bronze_tools_%28cropped%29_Gonur_Depe.jpg","title":"Bronze tools from Gonur Depe","desc":"Excavated bronzes of the Oxus civilisation, from the study that traced its copper to Iranian deposits and its tin to the mine at Mushiston in Tajikistan.","credit":"Figure by Berger, Kaniuth, Boroffka, Brugmann, Kraus, Lutz, Teufer, Wittke and Pernicka, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BMAC_bronze_tools_%28cropped%29_Gonur_Depe.jpg","alt":"Several corroded green-brown bronze objects photographed against a plain ground: flat blades, a shaft-hole implement and long pins."}},
{"id":"wh-278","num":278,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Ten genomes from Ashkelon show European-related ancestry arriving with the Iron Age, the first hard test of whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were migrants or only a fashion.","questions":["Almost everything written about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from the victory inscriptions of the pharaohs who say they beat them, which is a poor kind of witness.","Terracotta coffins in the shape of a body, long attributed to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, turn out to copy an Egyptian burial custom rather than an Aegean one."],"answer":"Sea Peoples","answerText":"Sea Peoples","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the records</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destructions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1192 – 1190 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ashkelon burials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1379 – 1126 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Sea Peoples</b> is the modern label for groups named in Egyptian, Hittite and Ugaritic records of the 14th to 11th centuries BCE and blamed by the pharaohs for wrecking the eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nearly everything said about them comes from royal inscriptions, above all the temple of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu, which is a victory monument before it is a chronicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Egypt's account of its own wars cannot be checked against itself, so whether people really moved has to be asked where they are said to have settled rather than where they are said to have been beaten. In the 12th century BCE, Ashkelon, Ashdod and Ekron, three of the five cities the Hebrew Bible calls Philistine, changed sharply in their building and their pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether that was migration or the borrowing of Aegean fashions was argued over for decades with no way of deciding between them, since pots can travel without people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Genomes from ten people buried at Ashkelon settle part of it, since the early Iron Age population carries European-related ancestry that the Bronze Age population before it does not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four of those ten are infants buried beneath houses of the late 12th century BCE, and their bones are carbon-dated between 1379 and 1126 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In individuals of the later Iron Age that ancestry has gone, diluted within no more than two centuries, so a migration did happen and left no lasting mark on the gene pool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The objects say something similar about labels, since the terracotta anthropoid coffins long called Philistine copy an Egyptian burial tradition rather than an Aegean one, taken up by people wealthy enough to want it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The firmest date for the whole episode comes from radiocarbon rather than a pharaoh's regnal year, since destruction layers in the northern Levant fall between 1192 and 1190 BCE, and the label still does not say who these people were or whether they ever acted as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","event","archaeology","history","migration","genetics","bronze age"],"sources":["Michal Feldman et al., \"Ancient DNA Sheds Light on the Genetic Origins of Early Iron Age Philistines,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 7 (2019): eaax0061, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6609216/. [Open access]","Marco Rossi, \"Anthropoid 'Philistine' Sarcophagi: Concluding Remarks?,\" <i>Asia Anteriore Antica</i> 6 (2024): 63–80, https://doi.org/10.36253/asiana-2467. [Open access]","The Epigraphic Survey, <i>Medinet Habu</i>, vol. 1, <i>Earlier Historical Records of Ramses III</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 8 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1930), https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip8.pdf. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"The Sea Peoples, from Cuneiform Tablets to Carbon Dating,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 6 (2011): e20232, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0020232. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Records of Egypt</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Twentieth to the Twenty-sixth Dynasties</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1906), §§59–64, https://archive.org/details/ancient-records-of-egypt-vol-4_202012. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Prisoners_from_the_Battle_of_the_Nile_Delta.jpg","title":"Bound prisoners on the walls of Medinet Habu","desc":"A 19th-century plate of the reliefs of Ramesses III, showing captives taken in the sea battle. The monument is the fullest source for the Sea Peoples and it was built to say that Egypt won.","credit":"Drawing by Carl Richard Lepsius, from Denkmaler aus Aegypten und Aethiopien (1859), sheet 211, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prisoners_from_the_Battle_of_the_Nile_Delta.jpg","alt":"A line engraving of rows of bound captives in profile, hands tied behind them, each row of figures wearing a different headdress and dress."}},
{"id":"wh-279","num":279,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Three consecutive dry years around 1198 BCE in Anatolian tree rings mark the sharpest moment yet dated within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a shock is not the same as a cause.","questions":["Writing, palaces and monumental building all stop in the Aegean around 1200 BCE in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which a potter painted marks that only look like Linear B.","Palace states from Greece to the edge of Mesopotamia failed between the mid-13th and the late 12th century BCE in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, unevenly and for no one reason."],"answer":"Late Bronze Age collapse","answerText":"Late Bronze Age collapse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Destructions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dry years</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1198 – 1196 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Levant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1192 – 1190 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Late Bronze Age collapse</b> is the run of destructions, abandonments and political failures that ended the palace states of the eastern Mediterranean between the middle of the 13th century BCE and the end of the 12th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A slow shift to drier, cooler conditions around 1200 BCE has long been part of the explanation, but societies adapt to slow change, and adapting is what the Hittites had done for five centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What juniper rings and stable isotopes from central Anatolia record instead is a short shock: three consecutive and unusually severe dry years, about 1198 to 1196 BCE, at the moment the Hittite state failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the northern Levant, stratified radiocarbon anchored in king lists and astronomical observations puts the terminal destructions between 1192 and 1190 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pollen and isotopes from Cyprus and the Syrian coast read a sequence out of the same years: famine first, then seaborne raiding, then political and economic failure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Precision of that order is rare for any ancient catastrophe, and it still does not prove cause, since the chronology itself is contested and radiocarbon from northern Greece runs decades earlier than the conventional dates of the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the collapse took away most completely was administration, and with it the writing that had existed to serve it and almost nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around 1200 BCE writing, the palace buildings and their economies, and monumental building of every kind ceased in the Aegean, and the emblems of the palatial world stopped being drawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On a jar from the Argolid a potter painted three or four marks along the belly that look vaguely like Linear B signs without being any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether that is a joke at the expense of a vanished bureaucracy or the memory of a script nobody could read any more, it is what the end of a writing system looks like from underneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","era","history","archaeology","climate","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Sturt W. Manning et al., \"Severe Multi-Year Drought Coincident with Hittite Collapse around 1198–1196 BC,\" <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7949 (2023): 719–24, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946833/. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"The Sea Peoples, from Cuneiform Tablets to Carbon Dating,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 6 (2011): e20232, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0020232. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","Kenneth Wardle, Thomas Higham, and Bernd Kromer, \"Dating the End of the Greek Bronze Age: A Robust Radiocarbon-Based Chronology from Assiros Toumba,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 9 (2014): e106672, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0106672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg","title":"The hearth of the throne room at Pylos","desc":"The great round hearth of the megaron in the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, which burned either late in Late Helladic IIIB or early in IIIC — one of the palace destructions that gave the collapse its name.","credit":"Photo by YukioSanjo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg","alt":"A large low circular hearth of plastered clay set in the floor of a ruined hall, ringed by the stone bases of four columns, under a modern shelter."}},
{"id":"wh-280","num":280,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Ore lies almost everywhere while tin lies almost nowhere, which is the strongest reason <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced bronze rather than any advantage in the metal itself.","questions":["A furnace too cool to melt the metal left <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a spongy bloom to be hammered clean of slag, so nothing could be cast the way bronze was.","Much of sub-Saharan Africa took up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without any age of bronze before it, and at Bassar in Togo smelting begins in the 5th century BCE."],"answer":"ironworking","answerText":"ironworking","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Africa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 3,000 years of it</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bassar smelting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ironworking</b> is the winning of iron from its ore and the shaping of what comes out, and unlike bronze it could not be poured, since early furnaces gave a spongy bloom that had to be hammered clean of slag.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What iron offered at first was not a better metal but a commoner one, since its ores lie almost everywhere while copper is uncommon and tin is rare. Iron objects are older than iron smelting, and some of the earliest were worked from meteorites, which supply metal that needs no furnace to reduce it from ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That story has been overstated, though, because a high nickel content is not a firm test of meteoritic origin, metallography is needed to decide, and many objects long called meteoritic turn out to be ordinary bloomery iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Working meteorites appears to have been irregular and sporadic rather than a stage every society passed through on its way to smelting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Bronze Age had run on metal carried thousands of kilometres along routes that shifted repeatedly, failed outright at least once, and left whole regions melting down old stock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Iron needed no such network, which is the strongest reason it became the ordinary metal for tools rather than any advantage in the metal itself. Nothing in that is a ladder of improvement: the neat sequence of stone, bronze and iron is a scheme proposed in the 1830s for arranging a museum collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of sub-Saharan Africa makes the point by having no age of bronze at all, and iron has been produced there for more than 3,000 years, with technical choices multiplied to a degree unequalled on other continents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Bassar in northern Togo smelting begins in the 5th century BCE, and about 50,000 tonnes of iron were made there between the 13th and the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["technology","concept","archaeology","history","africa","iron age","bronze age"],"sources":["Vladimir Zavyalov and Nataliya Terekhova, \"Meteoritic Iron Artefacts Redux,\" <i>Archeologické Rozhledy</i> 71, no. 2 (2019): 155–67, https://doi.org/10.35686/AR.2019.8. [Open access]","Caroline Robion-Brunner et al., \"Origines et étapes de la diversité des techniques sidérurgiques en Afrique de l'Ouest : le cas de la production du fer dans le Bassar (Togo),\" <i>Afriques</i> 13 (2022), https://doi.org/10.4000/afriques.3408. [Open access]","Nikos Nerantzis et al., \"Iron Working in Aegean Thrace during Antiquity: New Evidence for Smelting Activity of the 4th Century BC at the City on the Molyvoti Peninsula,\" <i>Scientific Culture</i> 8, no. 3 (2022): 105–23, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6640260. [Open access]","Heide W. Nørgaard, Ernst Pernicka, and Helle Vandkilde, \"Shifting Networks and Mixing Metals: Changing Metal Trade Routes to Scandinavia Correlate with Neolithic and Bronze Age Transformations,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 6 (2021): e0252376, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8208583/. [Open access]","Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, \"Of the Different Periods to Which the Heathen Antiquities May Be Referred,\" in <i>Guide to Northern Archaeology</i>, ed. the Earl of Ellesmere (London: James Bain, 1848), 67, https://archive.org/details/guidetonorthern00ellegoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Bloomery_slag_%28FindID_511447%29.jpg","title":"A fragment of bloomery slag","desc":"The waste of the process, and the commonest thing it leaves behind: a heavy, bubbled lump still rich enough in iron to pull a magnet. Recorded by the Portable Antiquities Scheme.","credit":"Photo by Tom Redmayne for the Portable Antiquities Scheme, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloomery_slag_%28FindID_511447%29.jpg","alt":"A dense grey-brown lump of slag on a plain background, its broken surface pitted with round air bubbles and streaked with rust."}},
{"id":"wh-281","num":281,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Defined by a technology rather than a date, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opens near the Bronze Age collapse in the Near East and centuries later elsewhere.","questions":["Radiocarbon from Sindos in northern Greece puts the local <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> earlier than the pottery-based scheme allows, which its authors say requires the Greek periodisation to be revised.","In much of sub-Saharan Africa the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the first metal age there was, because no age of bronze came before it."],"answer":"Iron Age","answerText":"Iron Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE in the Near East</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aegean steel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th – 5th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Iron Age</b> is the last stage of the three-age system, and it is defined by use rather than by a date: in Thomsen's words, the period in which iron was used for the articles to which it is eminently suited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because that definition is technological, the period begins at a different time in every region, close to the Bronze Age collapse in the Near East and centuries later further north and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In much of sub-Saharan Africa it is the first metal age there was, since no age of bronze preceded it, and iron has been produced there for over 3,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Steel takes longer to master than iron, and the earliest systematic use of ultra-high carbon steel in the Aegean belongs to the 7th and early 5th centuries BCE, generations after iron had become the ordinary metal there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The label therefore marks a threshold in technique rather than a moment in time. <br><br> Its dates are a scheme rather than a measurement, and where the scheme has been tested against physics it has moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Greek Early Iron Age chronology was built from pottery styles tied to historical dates in the eastern Mediterranean, and had never been checked by a long series of radiocarbon dates from the Aegean itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon on 21 stratified animal bones from Sindos in northern Greece puts the period earlier than the conventional scheme allows, enough that its authors call for the Greek periodisation to be revised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The far north shows the other half of the problem, since pottery of the Finnish Iron Age is dated to between about 2,000 and 1,450 years before the present, long after Greece had passed into written history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the term names, then, is a technology and a state of the evidence, and the two do not arrive together anywhere.","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["era","concept","archaeology","history","iron age","technology"],"sources":["Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, \"Of the Different Periods to Which the Heathen Antiquities May Be Referred,\" in <i>Guide to Northern Archaeology</i>, ed. the Earl of Ellesmere (London: James Bain, 1848), 67, https://archive.org/details/guidetonorthern00ellegoog. [Open access]","Ümit Güder et al., \"Earliest Evidence for Systematic Use of Ultrahigh Carbon Steel in the Ancient Aegean in the Archaic Milesia,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 3 (2025): e0312244, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0312244. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Petro Pesonen et al., \"Pottery, Language and Iron: Reassessing Luukonsaari and Sirnihta Wares and Their Cultural Context,\" <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 52 (2025): 186–211, https://journals.uni-lj.si/DocumentaPraehistorica/article/view/20680. [Open access]","Caroline Robion-Brunner et al., \"Origines et étapes de la diversité des techniques sidérurgiques en Afrique de l'Ouest : le cas de la production du fer dans le Bassar (Togo),\" <i>Afriques</i> 13 (2022), https://doi.org/10.4000/afriques.3408. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Early_Iron_Age_hoard_%28FindID_512333%29.jpg","title":"A lump the record cannot assign to either age","desc":"Casting waste from a hoard of socketed axes in Lancashire, recorded by the Portable Antiquities Scheme as probably of the Late Bronze Age or Early Iron Age. Where the boundary falls is a question about the evidence rather than about the object.","credit":"Photo by Dot Boughton for the Portable Antiquities Scheme, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Iron_Age_hoard_%28FindID_512333%29.jpg","alt":"A rough lump of green-brown corroded copper alloy on a plain background beside a scale, its surface irregular where the metal overflowed a mould."}},
{"id":"wh-282","num":282,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Conquered kingdoms paid tribute as clients and were then annexed as provinces under royal governors by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which by the 7th century BCE reached from Anatolia to Egypt.","questions":["Nearly everything the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> says about itself survives in palace inscriptions whose later editions inflate their own figures, one booty of 1,235 sheep swelling to 100,225.","Nineveh fell to the armies of Babylon and the Medes in 612 BCE, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose last court held out at Harran with troops sent by Egypt."],"answer":"Neo-Assyrian Empire","answerText":"Neo-Assyrian Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">912 – 609 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 7th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nineveh falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">612 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b> was the state based on the Tigris in northern Mesopotamia that grew out of the earlier Middle Assyrian kingdom and dominated the Near East from about 912 to 609 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its height early in the 7th century BCE its authority ran from central Anatolia to the Mediterranean coast and Egypt, and eastward to the Persian Gulf and western Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What set it apart was that conquest usually ended in annexation: a beaten kingdom paid tribute as a client for a time, then became a province run by a governor answerable to the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roads and irrigation works tied those provinces to the centre, while people deported from conquered lands were resettled in the capitals, which grew larger than any city of the previous two millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Assyrian dating is unusually firm for so early a period, because a running list of year-names is pinned to a solar eclipse of 15 June 763 BCE, fixing many events to the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Tušḫan on the upper Tigris shows a province in practice: opened in 879 BCE, it had a governor's palace, a garrison, a city wall and archives of grain, cloth and timber floated downriver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Annexation did not always sweep local rulers away, since the first known governor of neighbouring Naʾiri, in office in 849 BCE, was probably an Aramean dynast handed an Assyrian title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Control of production was patchy too, as scribes tracked metal and cloth closely but barely followed pottery, and the elite Palace Ware used at Tušḫan was most likely made by potters on the spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost everything the empire says about itself comes from inscriptions written for the palace, and successive editions of the same annals inflate their own figures, one booty of 1,235 sheep swelling to 100,225.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The end came fast, with a Babylonian chronicle putting Nineveh's fall to the armies of Babylon and the Medes in 612 BCE and the last court holding out at Harran, with Egyptian help, until 610.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","archaeology","politics","iron age","asia","iraq"],"sources":["Ashish Sinha, Gayatri Kathayat, Harvey Weiss, Hanying Li, Hai Cheng, Justin Reuter, Adam W. Schneider, Max Berkelhammer, Selim F. Adalı, Lowell D. Stott, and R. Lawrence Edwards, “Role of Climate in the Rise and Fall of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 11 (2019): eaax6656, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aax6656. [Open access]","Alexander Johannes Edmonds, “Just a Series of Misunderstandings? Assyria and Bīt-Zamāni, Ḫadi-/Iḫtadi-libbušu, and Aramaic in the Early Neo-Assyrian State,” <i>Asia Anteriore Antica: Journal of Ancient Near Eastern Cultures</i> 3 (2021): 73–91, https://doi.org/10.36253/asiana-1188. [Open access]","Britt E. Hartenberger, James A. Davenport, and Timothy Matney, “Tracing Fineware Production in the Neo-Assyrian Empire: Neutron Activation Analysis of Common and Palace Ware in the Upper Tigris River Valley, Turkey,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0315378, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0315378. [Open access]","A. T. Olmstead, <i>Assyrian Historiography: A Source Study</i> (Columbia: University of Missouri, 1916), 6–8, https://archive.org/stream/assyrianhistorio00olmsrich/assyrianhistorio00olmsrich_djvu.txt. [Open access]","C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 1–23, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Taylor_Prism-2.jpg","title":"The empire in its own words","desc":"The Taylor Prism, carrying Sennacherib's account of his campaign in Judah. It claims 200,150 prisoners taken in a war whose one great objective, Jerusalem, was never captured.","credit":"Photo by David Castor, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taylor_Prism-2.jpg","alt":"A tall six-sided baked clay prism standing on end in a museum case, each narrow face ruled into columns of small cuneiform signs."}},
{"id":"wh-283","num":283,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Sennacherib enlarged <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a circuit of 21,815 cubits behind double walls and 15 gates, and fed it by canal from a mountain on the Urartian border.","questions":["Eighteen canals dug into the Khosr and a two-million-stone aqueduct at Jerwan were built to water <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last Assyrian capital, on the Tigris.","Armies of Babylon and the Medes destroyed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 612 BCE, the Tigris city whose builder had named its new palace the one without a rival."],"answer":"Nineveh","answerText":"Nineveh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 704 – 612 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Circuit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21,815 great cubits</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">612 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nineveh</b> was the last capital of the Neo-Assyrian Empire, an old city on the Tigris at what is now Mosul in northern Iraq, rebuilt on a new scale by Sennacherib after 704 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His inscriptions say the earlier circuit measured 9,300 cubits, that he added 12,515 cubits of the surrounding plain, and that the whole came to 21,815 great cubits behind double walls with 15 gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The old palace, which he calls too small, came down for one he named the Palace without a Rival, with a park beside it planted with mountain and Chaldean trees and with trees that bore wool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The streets were widened and straightened onto a royal boulevard, and a building rule had any citizen whose rebuilt house encroached on the royal road impaled over his own doorway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The labour was imported, with Chaldeans, Aramaeans, Mannaeans and the people of Que and Hilakku set to carry the basket and mould the bricks for a capital larger than any city of the previous two millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A city that size could not live on the Khosr, the small Tigris tributary running through it, which had carried its name in cuneiform since Middle Assyrian times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So most of the king's civic work was water: an inscription of 690 BCE has him digging eighteen canals into the Khosr and cutting a channel from Kisiri into the middle of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The bulk of the supply came from much further off, from Mount Tas on the Urartian border, and the canal carrying it had to be taken across a valley at Jerwan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The aqueduct built there runs more than 280 metres (920 feet) long and 22 metres (72 feet) wide, of well over two million dressed stones, and its excavators knew no structure of the kind surviving from before Roman times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Babylonian chronicle puts the city's fall to the armies of Babylon and the Medes in 612 BCE, after which what was left of the Assyrian kingdom held out at Harran further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["city","place","history","archaeology","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>The Annals of Sennacherib</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 2 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1924), 94–103, 152–54, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip2.pdf. [Open access]","Thorkild Jacobsen and Seton Lloyd, <i>Sennacherib's Aqueduct at Jerwan</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 24 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1935), v, 8–10, 36–38, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip24.pdf. [Open access]","Abdul Rahman Younis Abdul Rahman, “Al-Khawser River in Cuneiform Sources,” <i>Athar Alrafedain</i> 1, no. 1 (2012): 158–76, https://doi.org/10.33899/athar.2012.69780. [Open access]","Ashish Sinha, Gayatri Kathayat, Harvey Weiss, Hanying Li, Hai Cheng, Justin Reuter, Adam W. Schneider, Max Berkelhammer, Selim F. Adalı, Lowell D. Stott, and R. Lawrence Edwards, “Role of Climate in the Rise and Fall of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 11 (2019): eaax6656, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aax6656. [Open access]","C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 1–23, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Jerwan_archaeological_site%2C_part_of_Neo-Assyrian_king_Sennacherib%27s_canal_system_06.jpg","title":"The canal crossing a valley","desc":"The aqueduct at Jerwan, which carried a canal to Nineveh across a valley on more than 280 metres (920 feet) of dressed stone. Its excavators knew no other structure of the kind surviving from before Roman times.","credit":"Photo by Levi Clancy, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerwan_archaeological_site%2C_part_of_Neo-Assyrian_king_Sennacherib%27s_canal_system_06.jpg","alt":"A broad band of dressed limestone blocks in several courses running across a dry grassy valley, a modern dirt track cutting through it, with bare hills beyond."}},
{"id":"wh-284","num":284,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Nineveh yielded about 30,000 tablets and fragments collected under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a king whose own inscriptions claim he could read the script of Sumer and obscure Akkadian.","questions":["Esarhaddon left Assyria to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Babylonia to his brother Shamash-shum-ukin, an arrangement that broke into civil war and ended with the brother overthrown in 648 BCE.","The annals of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were re-edited so thoroughly that a governor’s small raid became the fourth royal campaign in one version and disappeared from the next."],"answer":"Ashurbanipal","answerText":"Ashurbanipal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">668 BCE – c. 631 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Brother deposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">648 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ashurbanipal</b> ruled Assyria from Nineveh from 668 to about 631 BCE, at the height of an empire that had become the largest the world had yet seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His father divided the succession, leaving him Assyria and making his brother Shamash-shum-ukin king of Babylonia, an arrangement set down on the same tablet that describes his schooling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unusually for a king, his inscriptions claim learning: he says he took the scribal art of Adapa, worked out hard problems of division and multiplication, and read the script of Sumer and the obscure Akkadian that is difficult to master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement broke into civil war, and after Shamash-shum-ukin was overthrown in 648 BCE his household, his concubines, officers, chariots and palace furniture, was paraded before his brother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His annals were rewritten to suit: a small raid led by a governor in his accession year becomes the king's fourth campaign in one edition, and has vanished altogether from the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What is not in dispute is the collection of tablets at Nineveh, most of it gathered in the second half of the reign, once that war had been won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 30,000 tablets and fragments were recovered from his own palace, from Sennacherib's palace and from the temple of Nabu, and are now in the British Museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are royal correspondence, medicine, astronomy and astrology, omens, incantations and literature, so the collection is the working library of a court as much as a library of letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Named scholars built it, among them the chief scribe Ištar-šumu-ēreš, who appears in some 80 letters and reports, most of them on astrology, and signed tablets from two stages of his career.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tablets are written in a hand so controlled it has been called a 'typewriter' script, and the collection, shattered in antiquity, is still being reassembled from its fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","person","history","archaeology","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 290, 378–79, 394, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Eva Miller, “Drawing Distinctions: Assyrians and Others in the Art of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Studia Orientalia Electronica</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 82–107, https://doi.org/10.23993/store.87846. [Open access]","Ali Yassin Aljbori, “Ashurbanipal Library: Past and Present,” <i>Athar Alrafedain</i> 3, no. 1 (2018): 2–19, https://doi.org/10.33899/athar.2020.164532. [Open access]","Babette Schnitzlein and Jon Taylor, “Typological Aspects of Scholarly Tablets in the Library of Ashurbanipal,” <i>KASKAL</i>, n.s., 1 (2024): 205–40, https://doi.org/10.30687/KASKAL/5235-1939/2024/01/015. [Open access]","Sophie Cohen, “The Scribe's Imprint: Ištar-šumu-ēreš and the Formation of Ashurbanipal's Library,” <i>KASKAL</i>, n.s., 1 (2024): 265–82, https://doi.org/10.30687/KASKAL/5235-1939/2024/01/017. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/British_Museum_Cuneiform_planisphere_K8538.jpg","title":"A round tablet of the constellations","desc":"A circular tablet of constellations from the library at Nineveh, restored from fragments. Part of it was accidentally turned to glass by the fire that destroyed the place where it lay.","credit":"Photo by BabelStone, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum_Cuneiform_planisphere_K8538.jpg","alt":"A round clay disc the colour of dark olive, chipped along one edge, its face divided into eight wedge-shaped sectors by ruled lines and each sector filled with small cuneiform signs."}},
{"id":"wh-285","num":285,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Assyria broke resistance by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, uprooting whole communities and settling them elsewhere in the empire, a method used routinely from the middle of the 9th century BCE.","questions":["Sargon II is said to have moved 27,280 people out of the kingdom of Israel, one instance of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by which Assyria governed the lands its army had taken.","Sennacherib’s claim of 200,150 people carried off from Judah with their livestock is a record of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the policy that filled the Assyrian capitals."],"answer":"mass deportation","answerText":"mass deportation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Regular policy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the mid-9th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Samaria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 721 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mass deportation</b> was one of the two instruments by which the Neo-Assyrian Empire held the Near East, the other being an army that took the field almost every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moving conquered people was an old idea, but it became a regular political method only from the middle of the 9th century BCE, used to break resistance and to make revolt harder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figures given are large: Sargon II is said to have taken 27,280 people out of the kingdom of Israel about 721 BCE, and Tiglath-pileser III 55,000 from a single district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sieges that produced them are described in the same voice, Sennacherib claiming 46 walled cities of Judah taken by battering-rams, siege engines, mines, tunnels and assault on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people are then counted as property, 200,150 of them, great and small, male and female, carried off with horses, mules, asses, camels, cattle and sheep as spoil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Those numbers are the state's own and they grow between editions, so the round 200,000 has been read as a scribe's flourish over a real 150.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The violence is real but graded rather than indiscriminate: at Ekron the officials who had led the revolt were killed and their bodies hung on stakes round the city while the rest were pardoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Burning captives, the only violence towards children the inscriptions describe, is rare, and falls on peoples the Assyrians placed furthest from themselves, poor towns in the mountains without treasure to take.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The palace reliefs put the same material on the walls, so that foreign bodies and foreign landscapes being reshaped by Assyrian hands became the decoration of the rooms where the empire was run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deportation was in the end a labour policy: those moved were resettled and absorbed, and the capitals they filled grew larger than any city of the previous two millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","concept","history","warfare","politics","iron age","migration"],"sources":["Vladimir Sazonov, “Küüditamispoliitika kui muistne tava Uus-Assüüria impeeriumis 9.–7. sajandini eKr,” <i>Mäetagused</i> 44 (2010): 153–74, http://www.folklore.ee/tagused/nr44/sazonov.pdf. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>The Annals of Sennacherib</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 2 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1924), 12, 32–33, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip2.pdf. [Open access]","A. T. Olmstead, <i>Assyrian Historiography: A Source Study</i> (Columbia: University of Missouri, 1916), 7–8, https://archive.org/stream/assyrianhistorio00olmsrich/assyrianhistorio00olmsrich_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Ben Dewar, “The Burning of Captives in the Assyrian Royal Inscriptions, and Early Neo-Assyrian Conceptions of the Other,” <i>Studia Orientalia Electronica</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 67–81, https://doi.org/10.23993/store.88852. [Open access]","Eva Miller, “Drawing Distinctions: Assyrians and Others in the Art of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Studia Orientalia Electronica</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 82–107, https://doi.org/10.23993/store.87846. [Open access]","Ashish Sinha, Gayatri Kathayat, Harvey Weiss, Hanying Li, Hai Cheng, Justin Reuter, Adam W. Schneider, Max Berkelhammer, Selim F. Adalı, Lowell D. Stott, and R. Lawrence Edwards, “Role of Climate in the Rise and Fall of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 11 (2019): eaax6656, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aax6656. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Detail_of_a_gypsum_wall_relief_depicting_the_deportation_of_the_inhabitants_of_the_city_of_Lachish_by_the_Assyrian_army._Reign_of_Sennacherib%2C_700-692_BCE%2C_from_Nineveh%2C_Iraq%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum.jpg/1920px-Detail_of_a_gypsum_wall_relief_depicting_the_deportation_of_the_inhabitants_of_the_city_of_Lachish_by_the_Assyrian_army._Reign_of_Sennacherib%2C_700-692_BCE%2C_from_Nineveh%2C_Iraq%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum.jpg","title":"Leaving with what they could carry","desc":"A detail of the gypsum wall reliefs from Nineveh showing the people of Lachish being deported by the Assyrian army, with a laden pack animal, under escort. Reign of Sennacherib, 700 to 692 BCE.","credit":"Photo by Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_a_gypsum_wall_relief_depicting_the_deportation_of_the_inhabitants_of_the_city_of_Lachish_by_the_Assyrian_army._Reign_of_Sennacherib%2C_700-692_BCE%2C_from_Nineveh%2C_Iraq%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum.jpg","alt":"A carved stone relief in shallow relief showing a heavily laden camel walking to the right, led by a man in a fringed tunic, over a background of overlapping scale-like shapes standing for hilly ground."}},
{"id":"wh-286","num":286,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Nabopolassar founded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from inside Assyria, and his allies the Medes and Scythians helped him take Nineveh in 612 BCE and Harran two years later.","questions":["The glazed lions, dragons and bulls of the Ishtar Gate belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose kings called themselves providers of Esagila and Ezida rather than conquerors.","Cyrus II took Babylonia in 539 BCE, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though tablets from Nippur suggest the change of officials there was sharper than usually assumed."],"answer":"Neo-Babylonian Empire","answerText":"Neo-Babylonian Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War with Assyria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">616 – 609 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nebuchadnezzar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">604 – 562 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">539 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neo-Babylonian Empire</b>, also called the Chaldaean monarchy, was the state ruled from Babylon between the end of the Assyrian empire and the Persian conquest, and its founder was Nabopolassar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of a revolt inside Assyria rather than an invasion from outside, since Nabopolassar was already in his tenth year as king of Babylon in 616 BCE, when the chronicle of the war opens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That war set Babylon, the Medes under Kyaxares and the Scythians against Assyria and its ally Egypt, and it took Nineveh in 612 BCE and the last Assyrian court at Harran two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His son Nebuchadnezzar II ruled from 604 to 562 BCE, and on his foundation bricks calls himself king of Babylon, provider of Esagila and Ezida, first-born son of Nabopolassar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The title counts temples supplied and a father named rather than campaigns won, which is a different way for a Mesopotamian king to say who he is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the empire left most visibly is glazed brick, and a lion from Nebuchadnezzar's throne room stands about 1.2 metres (4 feet) high and 1.8 metres (6 feet) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is white on a blue-green ground with a yellow mane, and lions like it ran as a dado round the foot of a wall of glazed columns, lotus buds and palmettes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Outside, along the processional street and on the Ishtar Gate, they were joined by dragons and bulls, the animals of the gods Marduk and Adad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The empire ended in 539 BCE, when the Persian king Cyrus II took Babylonia in a transfer of power generally described as having gone smoothly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tablets from a mound at Nippur called Tablet Hill suggest otherwise, showing changes among the higher officials at that moment, and they are the kind of family archive the city has produced rather than a royal annal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","archaeology","politics","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 1–23, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Brique de fondation de Nabuchodonosor II,” inv. AO 32450, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010156663. [Open access]","Robert H. Dyson, Jr., “A Babylonian Lion in Toronto,” <i>Expedition</i> 5, no. 2 (1963), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-babylonian-lion-in-toronto/. [Open access]","Bernhard Schneider, “Tracing Regime Change during the Transition from the Neo-Babylonian to the Achaemenid Empire at Nippur: Reconstruction of Archives Excavated in 1889,” <i>Asia Anteriore Antica: Journal of Ancient Near Eastern Cultures</i> 4 (2022): 115–34, https://doi.org/10.36253/asiana-1576. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “The Uses of Writing: Inscribed Objects and Texts in the Middle East Galleries,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-uses-of-writing/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Lion_Ishtar_gate_IAM_inv88.144.jpg/1920px-Lion_Ishtar_gate_IAM_inv88.144.jpg","title":"A lion in glazed brick","desc":"A striding lion from the Ishtar Gate at Babylon, of the reign of Nebuchadnezzar II. Istanbul Archaeology Museums, inv. 88.144.","credit":"Photo by Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lion_Ishtar_gate_IAM_inv88.144.jpg","alt":"A panel of glazed bricks showing a lion striding to the left in white relief, its mane picked out in ochre, against a ground of blue and green tiles."}},
{"id":"wh-287","num":287,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Necho II of Egypt lost to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Carchemish in 605 BCE, the year he took the Babylonian throne and began 43 years of building.","questions":["The magnetism in the bricks of the Ishtar Gate dates it to about 569 BCE, inside the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose name is stamped on its earliest phase.","Foundation bricks from Babylon call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> king of Babylon, provider of Esagila and Ezida, and first-born son of Nabopolassar, a title of temples rather than of conquests."],"answer":"Nebuchadnezzar II","answerText":"Nebuchadnezzar II","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">605 – 562 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carchemish</span><span class=\"dt-v\">605 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ishtar Gate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 569 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b> ruled Babylon from 605 to 562 BCE, the longest reign of the Chaldaean dynasty and the years in which the Neo-Babylonian Empire stood at its height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to the throne in the year of Carchemish, the battle on the Euphrates at which he beat Necho II of Egypt and took the Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That fight was not a scramble over Assyria's corpse but the last blow of a long war in which Egypt had been trying to keep Assyria alive against Babylon and its northern allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What survives of him is mostly building rather than campaigning, and his foundation bricks call him king of Babylon, provider of Esagila and Ezida, first-born son of Nabopolassar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bricks themselves were fired below 400 °C (750 °F), a low heat, and one a laboratory can still recover today from the state of the iron in the clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Ishtar Gate, the entrance complex to the city and the most famous thing Babylon has left, went up in three phases, the earliest of them in bricks inscribed with his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The magnetism locked into that clay as it was fired shows all three phases were built inside his reign, most probably one immediately after another, rather than added later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Matched against the Levantine reference curve for the strength of the ancient magnetic field, the intensity recorded in the gate's bricks fits best at about 569 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That falls after the Babylonian campaign against Judah and Jerusalem in 586 BCE, though not immediately after, so whether the gate was raised to mark that victory is left open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the gate and along the processional street beside it his workmen set lions, dragons and bulls in glazed brick, the animals of the gods Marduk and Adad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","person","history","archaeology","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["Anita Di Chiara, Lisa Tauxe, Helen Gries, Barbara Helwing, Matthew D. Howland, and E. Ben-Yosef, “An Archaeomagnetic Study of the Ishtar Gate, Babylon,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 1 (2024): e0293014, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0293014. [Open access]","C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 15–21, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Brique de fondation de Nabuchodonosor II,” inv. AO 32450, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010156663. [Open access]","Robert H. Dyson, Jr., “A Babylonian Lion in Toronto,” <i>Expedition</i> 5, no. 2 (1963), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-babylonian-lion-in-toronto/. [Open access]","Rodrigo P. da Silva, Diego R. G. Tudela, Casimiro S. Munita, Roberto Hazenfratz, Sonia H. Tatumi, Marcio Yee, and Juan C. R. Mittani, “Firing Temperature Determination and Thermoluminescence Dating of a Brick with Cuneiform Characters Found in the Ruins of Ancient Babylon,” <i>Revista do Museu de Arqueologia e Etnologia</i> 28 (2017): 58–69, https://doi.org/10.11606/issn.2448-1750.revmae.2017.133911. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Barillet_de_temple_de_shamash_a_sippar.jpg/1920px-Barillet_de_temple_de_shamash_a_sippar.jpg","title":"A building inscription, not an annal","desc":"A barrel cylinder from the temple of Shamash at Sippar, of the reign of Nebuchadnezzar II. Royal Museums of Art and History, Brussels, MRAH O 4984.","credit":"Photo by Applejuice, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barillet_de_temple_de_shamash_a_sippar.jpg","alt":"A barrel-shaped cylinder of pale clay lying on its side in a museum case, its whole surface ruled into bands and filled with lines of cuneiform."}},
{"id":"wh-288","num":288,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Greek writers place the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on terraces four plethra square, watered by engines from the river, but no Babylonian text mentions them at all.","questions":["One argument moves the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Nineveh, where Sennacherib describes a park like Mount Amanus beside his palace, fed by a canal he cut from Kisiri.","Diodorus credits the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> not to Semiramis but to a later king wanting to please a woman who missed the mountain meadows of her own country."],"answer":"Hanging Gardens of Babylon","answerText":"Hanging Gardens of Babylon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reign of Nebuchadnezzar II, 605 – 562 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Hanging Gardens of Babylon</b> are known only from Greek and Roman writers, and the fullest surviving account does not name the king who built them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus describes a garden four plethra on each side, rising tier on tier so that the whole looked like a theatre, its topmost gallery level with the battlements of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath the terraces ran galleries carrying the weight, roofed with stone beams and layered with reeds and bitumen, then baked brick, then lead against the damp, then earth deep enough for the largest trees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He credits the work not to Semiramis but to a later king who wanted to please a woman longing for the mountain meadows of the country she came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo agrees on the square of four plethra and the vaults, and adds engines beside the stairs at which men were kept continually raising water from the Euphrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Nothing found on the ground at Babylon has settled the matter, and there have been no Babylonian texts to fix the gardens' location or describe them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What does survive is a garden of the right kind in another city, since Sennacherib describes a great park like Mount Amanus planted beside his palace at Nineveh, with mountain and Chaldean trees and trees that bore wool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had the water for it, having cut a canal from Kisiri to bring the Khosr to his orchards and run it through irrigation ditches inside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later writers confused Nineveh with Babylon often enough that one argument moves the gardens there altogether and makes Sennacherib, not Nebuchadnezzar, their builder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That reading is disputed, since a garden and lake beside the palace would block the way into it, and the relief said to show them may be a summary of a reign rather than a picture of a place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","place","history","archaeology","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 2.10, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (1933), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/2A*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 16.1.5, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D16%3Achapter%3D1%3Asection%3D5. [Open access]","Robert Koldewey, <i>The Excavations at Babylon</i>, trans. Agnes S. Johns (London: Macmillan, 1914), 91–100, https://archive.org/stream/excavationsatbab0000robe/excavationsatbab0000robe_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Antanas Stančius and Petras Grecevičius, “Influence of Ancient Mesopotamian Aesthetics of Gardens/Parks and Water Installations on the Development of Landscape Architecture,” <i>Athens Journal of Architecture</i> 8, no. 1 (2022): 9–34, https://doi.org/10.30958/aja.8-1-1. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>The Annals of Sennacherib</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 2 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1924), 96–99, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip2.pdf. [Open access]","Thorkild Jacobsen and Seton Lloyd, <i>Sennacherib's Aqueduct at Jerwan</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 24 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1935), 31–33, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip24.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Athanasius_Kircher_-_Turris_Babel_-_Hanging_gardens_of_Babylon.jpg/1280px-Athanasius_Kircher_-_Turris_Babel_-_Hanging_gardens_of_Babylon.jpg","title":"Somebody's guess, 1679","desc":"The gardens as engraved for Athanasius Kircher's Turris Babel. The colonnades, obelisks and galleons come from the engraver's own century rather than from the ancient descriptions.","credit":"Athanasius Kircher, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athanasius_Kircher_-_Turris_Babel_-_Hanging_gardens_of_Babylon.jpg","alt":"A dense engraving of an enormous rectangular palace of tiered classical colonnades with planted terraces on its roofs, obelisks at its corners, sailing ships on the river beside it and a hilly city behind."}},
{"id":"wh-289","num":289,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Judaeans deported to Babylonia in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> settled at Al-Yahudu between Nippur and Uruk, where tablets record them farming and naming children after their god.","questions":["Jerusalem fell in 586 BCE and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed, though the sources disagree about its scale, 2 Kings counting 10,000 deported and Jeremiah 3,023.","Persia took Babylonia in 539 BCE, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had removed the court, the priests and the craftsmen of Judah but left its peasants on the land."],"answer":"Babylonian captivity","answerText":"Babylonian captivity","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deportations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">597 – 582 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jerusalem falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">586 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">539 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Babylonian captivity</b> was the deportation of Judaeans to Babylonia in a series of removals between 597 and 582 BCE, and it lasted until Persia took Babylonia in 539.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Zedekiah's rebellion in 588 BCE brought the Babylonian army back to Judah, and Jerusalem fell two years later after a siege that ended the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The burnt mud brick of that destruction can be dated by the magnetic direction locked into it as it cooled, which fixes the campaign that ended the kingdom of Judah at 586 BCE without reference to any text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the same campaign against which the building of Babylon's own Ishtar Gate is dated, the gate going up a little after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The removals took the court, the officials, the priests and the craftsmen, an elite rather than a population, and the land was not left empty: peasants stayed, some fled to Egypt, and a governor was set over what remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How many went is not agreed even inside the sources, as 2 Kings gives 10,000 in one verse and 7,000 with 1,000 smiths and craftsmen in another, while Jeremiah gives 3,023 for the same deportation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two later removals are counted at about 832 men after the city fell and 745 more after the governor left in charge was murdered in 582 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Babylonia the deported can be read about directly, in cuneiform tablets from Al-Yahudu, a country town somewhere between Nippur and Uruk where Judaean families settled and farmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tablets show them dealing with Babylonian neighbours and taking on some of their ways, while going on giving their children Judaean names built round the name of their god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those names say what the exiles thought was happening to them: 'God exiled', 'God will redeem us', 'God will heal us', 'God will comfort us'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","archaeology","iron age","migration","iraq"],"sources":["Phaswane S. Makuwa, “The Emptiness of Exilic and Early Persian Judah: A Historical Study,” <i>In die Skriflig</i> 48, no. 1 (2014), https://doi.org/10.4102/ids.v48i1.724. [Open access]","Itzhak Amar, “The Genealogy of Jeconiah (1 Chronicles 3:17-24) in Light of the Tablets from Àl-Yâhudu,” <i>Old Testament Essays</i> 37, no. 3 (2024): 1–23, https://doi.org/10.17159/2312-3621/2024/v37n3a1. [Open access]","Yoav Vaknin, Ron Shaar, Oded Lipschits, et al., “Reconstructing Biblical Military Campaigns Using Geomagnetic Field Data,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 44 (2022): e2209117119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2209117119. [Open access]","Bernhard Schneider, “Tracing Regime Change during the Transition from the Neo-Babylonian to the Achaemenid Empire at Nippur: Reconstruction of Archives Excavated in 1889,” <i>Asia Anteriore Antica: Journal of Ancient Near Eastern Cultures</i> 4 (2022): 115–34, https://doi.org/10.36253/asiana-1576. [Open access]","Anita Di Chiara, Lisa Tauxe, Helen Gries, Barbara Helwing, Matthew D. Howland, and E. Ben-Yosef, “An Archaeomagnetic Study of the Ishtar Gate, Babylon,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 1 (2024): e0293014, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0293014. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Al-Yahudu_Tablets3.jpg/1280px-Al-Yahudu_Tablets3.jpg","title":"The exiles' own paperwork","desc":"Cuneiform tablets from the Judaean settlements in Babylonia, displayed under the names of the towns they record, Al-Yahudu among them. Bible Lands Museum, Jerusalem.","credit":"Photo by עמית אבידן, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Yahudu_Tablets3.jpg","alt":"Small pillow-shaped clay tablets covered in cuneiform, mounted on pins in a dark museum case beside white-on-black labels reading Bit-Nashar, Al-Yahudu and URU sha Tub-Yama."}},
{"id":"wh-290","num":290,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Assyrian scribes named the northern of the two kingdoms of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after a dynasty that had already ended, calling it 'the house of Omri' for well over a century afterwards.","questions":["Personal names recorded in the northern kingdom are more varied than those of the southern one, one measurable difference between <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the paired Hebrew states of the Iron Age.","Samaria fell to Assyria in the 720s BCE and became a province, ending the northern half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while the southern kingdom survived as a tributary for more than a century."],"answer":"Israel and Judah","answerText":"Israel and Judah","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Iron Age II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 950 – 586 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Samaria taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">720s BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jerusalem falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">586 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Israel and Judah</b> were two neighbouring kingdoms in the southern Levant during the Iron Age II, roughly 950 to 586 BCE, the northern of them ruled from Samaria in the hills and the southern from Jerusalem in the highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are usually spoken of together, but they were separate states, and the clearest evidence of that is the working records each kept. Assyrian scribes called the northern kingdom <i>Bit Humri</i>, 'the house of Omri', after a dynasty that had ended long before, and kept the name for well over a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Samaria's own paperwork survives as ink-written potsherds listing shipments of wine and oil to the palace, and handwriting analysis of 31 of them concluded that two scribes most likely wrote them all, at Samaria itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Judah's administration shows instead in storage-jar handles stamped 'belonging to the king', which are absent from the northern kingdom's destruction layers, a gap read as the rivalry between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Personal names are a second measure: of 920 recorded from the two kingdoms the northern sample is much the smaller and yet the more varied, read as a more open society astride the trade routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jerusalem itself grew earlier than used to be thought, as 103 radiocarbon dates from five excavation areas put its westward spread in the 9th century BCE, with heavy fortification following a mid-8th-century earthquake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Assyria took the northern kingdom's outlying districts in 733 and 732 BCE and Samaria itself in the 720s, after which it became a province whose governor appears in the Assyrian year-lists like any other official.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Judah went on as a tributary, and when Sennacherib campaigned in 701 BCE his annals claim 46 walled cities taken and Hezekiah shut up 'like a caged bird' in Jerusalem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> They never claim the city itself, which is as near as a royal inscription comes to recording a failure; Judah lasted until Babylon destroyed Jerusalem in 586 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","archaeology","iron age","israel"],"sources":["Ariel Vishne, Mitka R. Golub, Eli Piasetzky, Israel Finkelstein, and Barak Sober, “Diversity Statistics of Onomastic Data Reveal Social Patterns in Hebrew Kingdoms of the Iron Age,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 20 (2025): e2503850122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2503850122. [Open access]","A. T. Olmstead, <i>History of Assyria</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1923), 131, 139, https://archive.org/stream/historyofassyria00olms/historyofassyria00olms_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 2–4, 26, 40, 341, 438–39, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Shira Faigenbaum-Golovin, Arie Shaus, Barak Sober, Eli Turkel, Eli Piasetzky, and Israel Finkelstein, “Algorithmic Handwriting Analysis of the Samaria Inscriptions Illuminates Bureaucratic Apparatus in Biblical Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 1 (2020): e0227452, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0227452. [Open access]","Yoav Vaknin, Ron Shaar, Oded Lipschits, et al., “Reconstructing Biblical Military Campaigns Using Geomagnetic Field Data,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 44 (2022): e2209117119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2209117119. [Open access]","Johanna Regev, Yuval Gadot, Joe Uziel, et al., “Radiocarbon Chronology of Iron Age Jerusalem Reveals Calibration Offsets and Architectural Developments,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 19 (2024): e2321024121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2321024121. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>The Annals of Sennacherib</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 2 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1924), 11–12, 32–34, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip2.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/LMLK_Seals_on_Jar_Handles.jpg/1280px-LMLK_Seals_on_Jar_Handles.jpg","title":"Belonging to the king","desc":"Storage-jar handles from Judah, each stamped with a royal seal. Herodian Quarter Museum, Old City of Jerusalem.","credit":"Photo by Tom Bahar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LMLK_Seals_on_Jar_Handles.jpg","alt":"Four broken pottery jar handles mounted in a museum case, each carrying a small oval stamp impression showing a winged emblem above a line of Hebrew letters."}},
{"id":"wh-291","num":291,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"A charred lump from a synagogue ark at En-Gedi was unwrapped by computer into Leviticus, a book of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and proved identical in its consonants to the medieval text.","questions":["The prologue to Sirach shows that by 132 BCE the three parts of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were already available in Greek, so both its shape and its first translation are that old.","Radiocarbon dates on 24 scroll samples trained a handwriting model that redated 135 more, pushing the manuscript record of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> older than palaeography had put it."],"answer":"Hebrew Bible","answerText":"Hebrew Bible","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest layers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 630 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Greek by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">132 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">En-Gedi scroll</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 4th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Hebrew Bible</b> is the collection of Jewish scriptures written mostly in Hebrew and built up over centuries out of texts composed, copied and revised by many hands rather than by single authors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its oldest layers are dated by language and content: the core of Deuteronomy to the late 7th century BCE, the early Deuteronomistic material to that period or the exile, the Priestly writings later still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Word-frequency analysis across 50 chapters of the first nine books separates those three bodies of writing, supporting divisions drawn long ago on other grounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The collection was already described in three parts by the 2nd century BCE, since the prologue to Sirach, written in Egypt in 132 BCE, speaks of 'the Law, the Prophets, and the rest of the books' as already turned into Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A few passages are not in Hebrew at all but in Aramaic, a change one ancient Greek translator marked by switching dialect where the book of Daniel changes language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Until the last century the oldest complete copies were medieval, the version fixed by the Masoretes and still followed by most printed editions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scrolls from the caves of the Judaean Desert then took the manuscript evidence back a thousand years, and radiocarbon dates on 24 of them trained a handwriting model that dated 135 more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both run older than the estimates by eye they replace, and one sample of Daniel falls between 230 and 160 BCE, overlapping the years the last part of that book is thought to have been written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A study of the Great Isaiah Scroll found a break in its columns at about column 27, evidence of two scribes whose hands are too alike for the eye to separate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A charred lump from a synagogue ark at En-Gedi, too fragile to unroll, was unwrapped by computer into the opening of Leviticus, dated to the 3rd or 4th century CE and identical in its consonants to the medieval text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["text","history","religion","literature","iron age","israel"],"sources":["Shira Faigenbaum-Golovin, Alon Kipnis, Axel Bühler, Eli Piasetzky, Thomas Römer, and Israel Finkelstein, “Critical Biblical Studies via Word Frequency Analysis: Unveiling Text Authorship,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0322905, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0322905. [Open access]","Henry Barclay Swete, <i>An Introduction to the Old Testament in Greek</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1900), 24, 58, https://archive.org/stream/introductiontool00swet/introductiontool00swet_djvu.txt. [Open access]","William Brent Seales, Clifford Seth Parker, Michael Segal, Emanuel Tov, Pnina Shor, and Yosef Porath, “From Damage to Discovery via Virtual Unwrapping: Reading the Scroll from En-Gedi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 2, no. 9 (2016): e1601247, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601247. [Open access]","Mladen Popović, Maruf A. Dhali, Lambert Schomaker, et al., “Dating Ancient Manuscripts Using Radiocarbon and AI-Based Writing Style Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0323185, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0323185. [Open access]","Mladen Popović, Maruf A. Dhali, and Lambert Schomaker, “Artificial Intelligence Based Writer Identification Generates New Evidence for the Unknown Scribes of the Dead Sea Scrolls Exemplified by the Great Isaiah Scroll,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 4 (2021): e0249769, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0249769. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Great_Isaiah_Scroll.jpg/1920px-Great_Isaiah_Scroll.jpg","title":"The Great Isaiah Scroll","desc":"The best preserved of the biblical scrolls from Qumran, photographed unrolled; the change of scribe that handwriting analysis reports falls about half way along. Israel Museum, Jerusalem.","credit":"Photograph by Ardon Bar Hama, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Isaiah_Scroll.jpg","alt":"A long parchment scroll photographed unrolled and laid out in three horizontal strips, each ruled into many narrow columns of small Hebrew script, with worn and torn edges."}},
{"id":"wh-292","num":292,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Papyri from a Judaean garrison at Elephantine record a temple of their god in Egypt, evidence that the early practice of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not confined to the temple at Jerusalem.","questions":["A Judaean historian of the 1st century CE counts three schools within <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Pharisees, the Sadducees and the Essenes, which is a picture of a plural rather than a single practice.","After Rome destroyed the temple in 70 CE the centre of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay in reading and applying the law in local assemblies, one of which kept its Leviticus scroll at En-Gedi."],"answer":"Judaism","answerText":"Judaism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yeb petition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">408 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">En-Gedi burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Judaism</b> is the religion of the Jewish people, built around one God and around the Torah, and it takes its name from Judah, the southern of the two Iron Age Hebrew kingdoms. Its formative centuries are the ones after that kingdom ended, when the temple in Jerusalem was rebuilt under Persian rule and a written law became what the community organised itself around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That law was authoritative without being fixed: through the Second Temple period it was interpreted, updated and in places rewritten, and works such as the Temple Scroll give versions that never entered the canon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jerusalem was not the only place of sacrifice either: papyri from a Judaean garrison at Elephantine in Egypt describe a temple of the god Ya'u standing there before Persian rule and pulled down by local enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 408 BCE the priests there wrote to the governor of Judaea for leave to rebuild it, saying that since the loss they had worn sackcloth, drunk no wine and offered neither meal-offering nor sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Away from Judaea the scriptures were read in other languages, an interpreter turning the lesson into Aramaic where that was spoken, while in Egypt a written Greek translation was made instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the 1st century CE it was plural rather than single, and a Judaean historian counts three schools among his own people, the Pharisees, the Sadducees and the Essenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The scrolls hidden in the caves of the Judaean Desert belong to the same world, and radiocarbon dating has pushed the copying of literary manuscripts there back before the scribal revival once credited with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome destroyed the temple in 70 CE, and the fullest account of the burning is by that same historian, writing for a Roman readership and putting the fire down to the defenders themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What outlasted the temple was the reading of the law in local assemblies, and the synagogue at En-Gedi kept a scroll of Leviticus in its ark until the town burnt about 600 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["religion","practice","history","iron age","israel"],"sources":["Simon Paganini, “Deuteronomy in the Second Temple Period: Law and Its Developing Interpretation,” <i>Verbum et Ecclesia</i> 34, no. 2 (2013), https://doi.org/10.4102/ve.v34i2.800. [Open access]","A. Cowley, ed., <i>Aramaic Papyri of the Fifth Century B.C.</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1923), 108–22, https://archive.org/stream/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Henry Barclay Swete, <i>An Introduction to the Old Testament in Greek</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1900), 3–4, 24, https://archive.org/stream/introductiontool00swet/introductiontool00swet_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Josephus, <i>The Wars of the Jews</i> 2.119, trans. William Whiston, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0148%3Abook%3D2%3Awhiston+chapter%3D8%3Awhiston+section%3D2. [Open access]","Mladen Popović, Maruf A. Dhali, Lambert Schomaker, et al., “Dating Ancient Manuscripts Using Radiocarbon and AI-Based Writing Style Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0323185, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0323185. [Open access]","Josephus, <i>The Wars of the Jews</i> 6.249–53, trans. William Whiston, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0148%3Abook%3D6%3Awhiston+chapter%3D4%3Awhiston+section%3D5. [Open access]","William Brent Seales, Clifford Seth Parker, Michael Segal, Emanuel Tov, Pnina Shor, and Yosef Porath, “From Damage to Discovery via Virtual Unwrapping: Reading the Scroll from En-Gedi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 2, no. 9 (2016): e1601247, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601247. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Elephantine_Temple_reconstruction_request.gif/1280px-Elephantine_Temple_reconstruction_request.gif","title":"The petition from Yeb","desc":"The letter of 408 BCE from Yedoniah and the priests of the Judaean garrison at Elephantine to the governor of Judaea, asking leave to rebuild their temple.","credit":"Plate from Sachau 1907, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephantine_Temple_reconstruction_request.gif","alt":"A sheet of papyrus photographed flat, carrying a dozen long lines of cursive Aramaic script, cracked and darkened down the left-hand side."}},
{"id":"wh-293","num":293,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Genome-wide data from 14 western sites finds almost no Levantine ancestry, so what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exported across the Mediterranean was a language, a script and a way of trading rather than a population.","questions":["A Roman-period geographer says the dye-works of Tyre made the city unpleasant to live in, which is also where the wealth of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came from along that coast.","Byblos, Sidon, Tyre and Arwad were separate city-states rather than one kingdom, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is better described as a coast and a network than as a country."],"answer":"Phoenicia","answerText":"Phoenicia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Purple works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tell el-Burak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">725 – 350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Phoenicia</b> was not a state but a string of independent city-states along the Levantine coast, and a Roman-period geographer reports that its own people still argued over whether Tyre or Sidon was its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cities faced the sea because the plain behind them is narrow, and their living came from shipping, timber, metals and above all from a dye. The same geographer says the Tyrians surpassed all nations in navigation, that their purple stood in the highest estimation, and that the sheer number of dye-works made the city unpleasant to live in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dye is far harder to find in the ground than in the texts: it is assumed to have been made all round the Mediterranean, yet only one excavated site anywhere shows sustained large-scale production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That site is Tel Shiqmona on the Carmel coast, in the border country between the Israelite kingdom and Phoenician Tyre, where workshops ran in sequence for about five centuries from roughly 1100 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Behind the harbours lay a working countryside: the estate at Tell el-Burak in Lebanon, in use from 725 to 350 BCE, has given up the first Iron Age wine press found in that country and a lime plaster stiffened with crushed pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reach of the trade shows in the ground at Sidon, whose long stratigraphy mixes Greek, Cypriot and Egyptian pottery with local wares and now carries a series of radiocarbon dates on short-lived samples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Phoenician settlements appear across the central and western Mediterranean during the 1st millennium BCE, and that spread has long been read as people moving out of the homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Genome-wide data for 210 individuals, 196 of them from 14 Phoenician and Punic sites, finds almost no Levantine ancestry in the western settlements, whose people instead resemble those of Sicily and the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What travelled was a language, a script, a religion and a way of trading rather than a population, a different sort of expansion from the one the word colony suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","state","history","archaeology","genetics","iron age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 16.2.22, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D16%3Achapter%3D2%3Asection%3D22. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 16.2.23, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D16%3Achapter%3D2%3Asection%3D23. [Open access]","Golan Shalvi, Naama Sukenik, Paula Waiman-Barak, et al., “Tel Shiqmona during the Iron Age: A First Glimpse into an Ancient Mediterranean Purple Dye ‘Factory’,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 4 (2025): e0321082, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0321082. [Open access]","Silvia Amicone, Adriano Orsingher, Emma Cantisani, et al., “Innovation through Recycling in Iron Age Plaster Technology at Tell el-Burak, Lebanon,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 24284, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-05844-x. [Open access]","Claude Doumet-Serhal, Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bernhard Weninger, et al., “An Interdisciplinary Approach to Iron Age Mediterranean Chronology through Combined Archaeological and 14C-Radiometric Evidence from Sidon, Lebanon,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 3 (2023): e0274979, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0274979. [Open access]","Harald Ringbauer, Ayelet Salman-Minkov, Dalit Regev, et al., “Punic People Were Genetically Diverse with Almost No Levantine Ancestors,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8070 (2025): 139–47, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08913-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Byblos_ancient_ruins,_old_Phoenician_city_of_Byblos,_Lebanon.jpg/1280px-Byblos_ancient_ruins,_old_Phoenician_city_of_Byblos,_Lebanon.jpg","title":"A city on a narrow coast","desc":"The excavated foundations of Byblos, one of the Phoenician city-states, with the sea on one side and the hills close behind it.","credit":"Photo © Vyacheslav Argenberg / vascoplanet.com, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byblos_ancient_ruins,_old_Phoenician_city_of_Byblos,_Lebanon.jpg","alt":"Low stone wall foundations of an ancient town on a headland, with cypress trees among them, a bay beyond and a built-up hillside in the distance."}},
{"id":"wh-294","num":294,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Herodotus says the Ionians learned their letters from the Phoenicians and went on calling them Phoenician, which is one ancient account of how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Greece.","questions":["Hebrew and Aramaic speakers took over the 22 signs of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without adding letters for sounds their own languages had and Phoenician did not.","The epitaph of Ahiram, cut on a sarcophagus lid at Byblos around 1000 BCE, is the earliest long text in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a consonantal script of 22 signs read right to left."],"answer":"Phoenician alphabet","answerText":"Phoenician alphabet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ahiram epitaph</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek adoption</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th – 8th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Phoenician alphabet</b> was a consonantal script of 22 signs, written from right to left, used by the Phoenician cities through the 1st millennium BCE and ancestral to most of the alphabets in use today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It writes consonants only and leaves the vowels to the reader, and its letters are no longer pictures, the shapes having drifted away from the objects their ancestors were drawn from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fixed order is the form the older alphabetic tradition settled into, and that order is already recognisable in the longer sign list of Bronze Age Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest long example is the epitaph of Ahiram, cut about 1000 BCE on the lid of a stone sarcophagus at Byblos and judged, when it was published, some four centuries older than the Moabite stele.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phoenician texts often mark their word divisions, and the marks seem to follow how a passage was to be spoken rather than where its grammar divides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When Hebrew and Aramaic speakers took the script over for their own languages they added no signs for the sounds Phoenician lacked, but made the 22 available letters do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shapes drifted apart all the same, so that distinct Hebrew and Aramaic scripts grew out of the same 22 signs across the 1st millennium BCE while the direction of writing stayed put.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the Phoenicians who came with Cadmus brought letters to a Greece that had not known them, that the Ionians were taught and changed a few shapes, and that the Greeks went on calling the characters Phoenician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How the transfer really happened is argued over, from a chance meeting of traders in some Mediterranean harbour to a formal and deliberate act, and the earliest Greek specimens are themselves written right to left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dividing speech into consonants and vowels, which the Greek version made explicit, is an analysis rather than something a language offers up, speech being a continuous wave of sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","technology","archaeology","writing","language","iron age"],"sources":["Joseph Lam, “The Invention and Development of the Alphabet,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 191–92, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","René Dussaud, “Les inscriptions phéniciennes du tombeau d’Ahiram, roi de Byblos,” <i>Syria</i> 5, no. 2 (1924): 135–57, https://doi.org/10.3406/syria.1924.3038. [Open access]","Joseph Lam, review of <i>The Semantics of Word Division in Northwest Semitic Writing Systems: Ugaritic, Phoenician, Hebrew, Moabite and Greek</i>, by Robert S. D. Crellin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.22/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.58, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]","L. R. Siddall, review of <i>Writing: Theory and History of the Technology of Civilization</i>, by Barry B. Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.04.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.04.24/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/National_Museum_of_Beirut_%E2%80%93_Ahiram_sarcophagus_inscription_1.jpg/1280px-National_Museum_of_Beirut_%E2%80%93_Ahiram_sarcophagus_inscription_1.jpg","title":"The epitaph of Ahiram","desc":"The inscription running along the lid of Ahiram's sarcophagus at Byblos, cut in Phoenician letters about 1000 BCE. National Museum of Beirut.","credit":"Photo by Onceinawhile, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Beirut_%E2%80%93_Ahiram_sarcophagus_inscription_1.jpg","alt":"The edge of a heavy limestone sarcophagus lid carrying a single line of angular incised letters, above a chest carved with a procession of figures in low relief."}},
{"id":"wh-295","num":295,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"The oldest surviving reckoning of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a Tyrian king-list quoted by Josephus, in which the sister of Pygmalion flees him in his seventh year and builds a city in Libya.","questions":["Elissa is said to have bought as much ground as an ox-hide would cover and cut it into strips, which is the legend attached to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a Latin epitome.","Convention dates the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to about 814 BCE, though what can be dated on the ground there is later and rests on no Carthaginian record at all."],"answer":"founding of Carthage","answerText":"founding of Carthage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 814 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Expansion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 7th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>founding of Carthage</b> is dated by convention to about 814 BCE, a date owed to Greek and Roman writers rather than to anything the city itself left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest chain of reasoning survives in Josephus, who quotes a historian working from Tyrian records: the kings of Tyre are listed in turn, and in the seventh year of Pygmalion 'his sister fled away from him, and built the city Carthage in Libya'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That source gives the sister no name and no story, only an interval: 155 years and 8 months from the reign of Hiram to the building of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story arrives later, in an epitome of a Latin history: Elissa flees a brother who murdered her husband for his gold, bargains for as much African ground as an ox-hide will cover, has the hide cut into the thinnest strips, and takes the hill afterwards called Byrsa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same account has Tyrians founding Utica before Carthage and the settlers paying their neighbours an annual rent for the ground, a trading post's arrangement rather than a conqueror's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What can be dated on the ground at Carthage runs later than that, and radiocarbon results from the site were among the evidence that reopened the conventional Iron Age chronology of the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The traditional date rests on Timaeus and on the epitome of Trogus, neither a Carthaginian record, so when Phoenicians reached the site and when ancient writers thought they did are two questions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Utica, Gadir and Carthage are the foundations the westward expansion is surveyed through, and it runs on through the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city became something no foundation story predicts: genome-wide data finds the people of Carthage itself taking most of their ancestry from a profile resembling that of Sicily and the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> North African ancestry is a minority contributor at every site sampled, Carthage included, so a tale about one party out of Tyre is a poor guide to who actually lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","archaeology","iron age","migration"],"sources":["Russell Clark, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of the Phoenician and Punic Mediterranean</i>, ed. Carolina López-Ruiz and Brian R. Doak, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.08.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.08.13/. [Open access]","Josephus, <i>Against Apion</i> 1.18, trans. William Whiston, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0216%3Abook%3D1%3Awhiston+section%3D18. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 18.4–5, trans. John Selby Watson, Attalus, https://www.attalus.org/translate/justin2.html. [Open access]","Claude Doumet-Serhal, Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bernhard Weninger, et al., “An Interdisciplinary Approach to Iron Age Mediterranean Chronology through Combined Archaeological and 14C-Radiometric Evidence from Sidon, Lebanon,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 3 (2023): e0274979, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0274979. [Open access]","Harald Ringbauer, Ayelet Salman-Minkov, Dalit Regev, et al., “Punic People Were Genetically Diverse with Almost No Levantine Ancestors,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8070 (2025): 139–47, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08913-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Byrsa_panorama.jpg/1280px-Byrsa_panorama.jpg","title":"The hill called Byrsa","desc":"The excavated summit of Byrsa at Carthage, looking out over the Gulf of Tunis. The Latin story derives the hill's name from the ox-hide Elissa is said to have cut into strips.","credit":"Photo by Asma Ben Gharbi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byrsa_panorama.jpg","alt":"Low ruined walls and column stumps on a hilltop among pines and scrub, with a bay, a modern white city and blue hills in the distance."}},
{"id":"wh-296","num":296,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Sargon's account of his eighth campaign praises the canals and orchards of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the same breath as it describes burning the crops and opening the granaries.","questions":["Assyrian records use the name Uruadri from the 13th century BCE, long before <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> existed as the mountain kingdom around Lake Van that Assyria later fought.","The kings of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote their own language in Mesopotamian cuneiform, and their fortresses survive as mudbrick walls that can be told from later brick by the grain in them."],"answer":"Urartu","answerText":"Urartu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Name in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 13th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Urartu</b> was an Iron Age kingdom in the mountains around Lake Van, in what is now eastern Turkey and Armenia, at its height between about 800 and 600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is older than the kingdom: Assyrian scribes write Uruadri from the 13th century BCE, when it stood not for a state but for a country that rose in revolt at a king's accession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its rulers set down a language of their own in the cuneiform script Mesopotamia had already adapted to Akkadian, Hittite, Hurrian and half a dozen others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly everything Assyria says about it was written to record a victory over it, so the picture has to be read against the grain of the men who wrote it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is a picture even so: Sargon's account of his eighth campaign has the Urartian king showing his people where the waters gushed out, cutting a channel that 'brought plenty, like the Euphrates', leading countless smaller channels off it, and making fruit and grapes as plentiful as rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The same text then burns those crops, opens the granaries and lets the Assyrian army eat the grain, which is the only reason the orchards are described at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That campaign ended at Musasir, the shrine of the god Haldi, out of which Sargon claims 6,170 people, 34 talents and 18 minas of gold, and 160 talents of silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the ground the kingdom survives as fortresses, and at Artaxata in Armenia a massive mudbrick wall is taken for part of an Urartian fort of about 2.5 hectares (6 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The brick itself can be told apart: the size, sorting and shape of the grains in Urartian mudbrick separate it statistically from the Hellenistic brick later raised on the same hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Assyria outlasted it, though not by long, and Urartu goes out of the record during the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","place","history","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Marta Lorenzon, Benjamín Cutillas-Victoria, Elisabeth Holmqvist, et al., “Exploring Mudbrick Architecture and Its Re-use in Artaxata, Armenia, during the 1st Millennium BC: A Multidisciplinary Study of Earthen Architecture in the Armenian Highlands,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 10 (2023): e0292361, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0292361. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 1, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from the Earliest Times to Sargon</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1926), 37–38, 292, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria1/ancient_records_assyria1_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Christopher Woods, “The Earliest Mesopotamian Writing,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 33, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 8–11, 86–87, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Sofia Kouki, Marta Lorenzon, Benjamín Cutillas-Victoria, et al., “Against the Grain: Leveraging Machine Learning to Analyze Mudbrick Structures,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 5 (2026): e0349295, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0349295. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Van_Fortress_From_Northwest.JPG/1280px-Van_Fortress_From_Northwest.JPG","title":"The rock of Van","desc":"The citadel ridge above Van in eastern Turkey, the seat of the Urartian kings. Its rock face is cut into terraces and its crest carries fortification walls.","credit":"Photo by Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_Fortress_From_Northwest.JPG","alt":"A long rocky ridge rising from a green plain, its face cut into terraces and its crest lined with ruined stone walls and a tower, under a clear sky."}},
{"id":"wh-297","num":297,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"A wall some 20 metres (65 feet) thick, burnt mudbrick over smashed pottery and a gate faced in ashlar mark the ruined defences of Sardis, capital of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taken by Cyrus in 547 BCE.","questions":["Herodotus derives the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Lydus and says the land was once Maeonia, while Assyrian annals know its king Gyges as Guggu, ruler of a place across the sea.","Scores of burial mounds at Bin Tepe, a massive rubble-and-mudbrick wall at Sardis, and ashlar cut midway through Alyattes's reign are the physical remains of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in western Anatolia."],"answer":"Lydia","answerText":"Lydia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 680 – 547 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sardis taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">547 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lydia</b> was a kingdom of western Anatolia with its capital at Sardis, beneath Mount Tmolus on the Hermus plain, whose kings came to rule every people west of the river Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its people spoke Lydian, an Indo-European language of the Anatolian family, written in an alphabet whose letters look Greek and which is certainly not descended straight from the Phoenician script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The script was read with the help of a bilingual funerary stele from the Sardis necropolis, its Aramaic half dated by the regnal year of a Persian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus derives the country's name from Lydus and says it was once called Maeonia, and that the Mermnad house of Croesus took Sardis from an older line claiming descent from Heracles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest Mermnad attested outside Greek writing is Gyges, who enters Assyrian records as Guggu of Luddi, a place across the sea \"whose name, the kings, my fathers, had not heard\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Ashurbanipal's annals have him beat off the Cimmerians and send two of their chiefs in fetters, then desert to Egypt and be overrun in his turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sardis was defended on a matching scale, by a wall about 20 metres (65 feet) thick of coursed rubble and mudbrick laid down in the last quarter of the 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cut ashlar masonry becomes an important part of Lydian building only midway through the reign of Alyattes, perhaps after that king came home from his war with the Medes about 585 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tombs are the largest class of monument the kingdom left, and scores of mounds cluster at Bin Tepe, 10 kilometres (6 miles) north of the city, the greatest called the tomb of Alyattes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wall fell with the city about 547 BCE under burnt mudbrick and smashed pottery, but Lydian stonework outlasted it: Darius's charter at Susa says the stone was wrought by \"Ionians and Sardians\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","place","history","archaeology","iron age","anatolia"],"sources":["Christopher Ratté, <i>Lydian Architecture: Ashlar Masonry Structures at Sardis</i>, Archaeological Exploration of Sardis Report 5 (Cambridge, MA: Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, 2011), xv, 3, 11, 63, 108, https://www.sardisexpedition.org/en/publications/r5. [Open access]","Enno Littmann, <i>Lydian Inscriptions, Part I</i>, Publications of the American Society for the Excavation of Sardis 6 (Leyden: E. J. Brill, 1916), 19, 23, https://archive.org/stream/lydianinscriptio00littuoft/lydianinscriptio00littuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 297–98, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","John Griffiths Pedley, <i>Ancient Literary Sources on Sardis</i>, Archaeological Exploration of Sardis Monograph 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1972), 1–2, https://www.sardisexpedition.org/en/publications/m2. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.6–7, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Tomb_of_Alyattes%2C_August_2024.jpg/1280px-Tomb_of_Alyattes%2C_August_2024.jpg","title":"The tomb of Alyattes at Bin Tepe","desc":"The greatest of the Lydian burial mounds, standing over the Hermus plain north of Sardis, with a smaller tumulus beside it.","credit":"Photograph by Chillenbro, 2024. CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Alyattes%2C_August_2024.jpg","alt":"A broad, bare, dome-shaped earthen mound rising above olive groves and ploughed fields under a clear sky, a second smaller mound at its right."}},
{"id":"wh-298","num":298,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Clay moulds at Guanzhuang, punches on a karshapana and struck electrum in Anatolia are three separate ways of making <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose stamp lets metal be counted instead of weighed.","questions":["Cast in clay moulds in Henan and struck with five small punches on the Ganges plain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appears in traditions that share an idea but not a technique.","A Samarian hoard holding both cut silver sheets and stamped pieces shows how slowly <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> displaced bullion that had to be weighed and tested at every payment."],"answer":"coinage","answerText":"coinage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Anatolia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Guanzhuang mint</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Punch-marked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th – 1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Coinage</b> is metal issued in standardised pieces under an authority's stamp, so that a payment can be counted out rather than weighed and tested at every exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It appears in three separate traditions within roughly the same century, in western Anatolia, on the Ganges plain and in the Central Plains of China, and the three do not share a technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Around the Aegean and on the Ganges the piece is struck, driven by a hammer against dies or punches, while in China it was cast in clay moulds like any other bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavation of a bronze foundry at Guanzhuang in Henan has produced clay moulds for casting spade coins, and their technical character shows the site working as a mint for standardised pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon dates put well-organised minting there at about 640 to 550 BCE, which makes it the oldest securely dated minting site known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> India's earliest coinage is the punch-marked coin, of silver or a silver alloy, thought to have circulated from the 7th century BCE and struck not from one die but with several small punches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Local issues carry one to four symbols, while the imperial karshapana bears five on its face, often overlapping or half off the flan, at a standard of about 3.4 grams (0.12 ounces).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its silver falls away sharply after the death of Ashoka in 232 BCE, as weak successors and lost territory brought on a rapid debasement of the coinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stamped metal did not sweep weighed metal away, and a hoard from the region of Samaria holds 4th-century BCE coins together with cut silver sheets of the century before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lead and silver isotopes now trace where Mediterranean coin metal came from, and they set a Phoenician world treating silver as a plain commodity against a Greek one monetising its economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","technology","iron age","china","india"],"sources":["Hao Zhao, Xiangping Gao, Yuchao Jiang, Yi Lin, Jin Zhu, Sicong Ding, Lijun Deng, and Ji Zhang, “Radiocarbon-Dating an Early Minting Site: The Emergence of Standardised Coinage in China,” <i>Antiquity</i> 95, no. 383 (2021): 1161–78, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2021.94. [Open access]","Amit Kumar Upadhyay, Neeraj Kumar Giri, Rajiv Prakash, and Hirdyesh Mishra, “Surface Elemental and Structural Analysis of Ancient Indian Punch-Marked Coins (600 to 200 BCE): Insights into Metallurgy and Economic Practices,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 30190, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-76356-3. [Open access]","F. Albarède, J. Blichert-Toft, F. de Callataÿ, G. Davis, P. Debernardi, L. Gentelli, H. Gitler, F. Kemmers, S. Klein, C. Malod-Dognin, J. Milot, P. Télouk, M. Vaxevanopoulos, and K. Westner, “From Commodity to Money: The Rise of Silver Coinage around the Ancient Mediterranean (Sixth–First Centuries BCE),” <i>Archaeometry</i> 63, no. 1 (2021): 142–55, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7821003/. [Open access]","Dana Ashkenazi, Maayan Cohen, Haim Gitler, Mati Johananoff, and Oren Tal, “A Material Study of Persian-Period Silver Coins and Hacksilber from Samaria,” <i>Materials</i> 18, no. 7 (2025): 1678, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11990297/. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Greek and Roman Coinage</i>, ed. William E. Metcalf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013-05-11.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Spade_Currency%2C_Zhou_Royal_Court%2C_Spring_%26_Autumn_Era_05.jpg/1280px-Spade_Currency%2C_Zhou_Royal_Court%2C_Spring_%26_Autumn_Era_05.jpg","title":"Hollow-handled spade coins of the Zhou royal house","desc":"Cast bronze in the shape of the tool it replaced, each blade carrying cast characters. Spring and Autumn period, Shanghai Museum.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, Shanghai Museum. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spade_Currency%2C_Zhou_Royal_Court%2C_Spring_%26_Autumn_Era_05.jpg","alt":"Three corroded green-brown bronze objects shaped like small spades, each with a narrow hollow handle above a flared blade cast with characters, mounted on a museum board."}},
{"id":"wh-299","num":299,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Assyrian kings took tribute from 28 mayors and many chieftains rather than from any king of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, to whom Herodotus gives a capital of seven walls and a line of four kings.","questions":["After Ashur fell the Babylonian king met the leader of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over its ruins and made a formal alliance, and Nineveh went the same way two years later.","Astyages was the last king of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, deposed by Cyrus, though Darius still faced a rebel in that country claiming descent from the house of Cyaxares."],"answer":"Medes","answerText":"Medes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Assyrian records</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th – 7th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nineveh falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">612 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Astyages deposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Medes</b> were an Iranian-speaking people of the Zagros mountains and the western plateau, and Herodotus makes them the masters of everything in Asia beyond the river Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They enter the record in Assyrian annals of the 9th century BCE, when Shalmaneser III came down to \"the lands of Messi and of the Medes\" and Shamshi-Adad V marched against \"the land of the Matai\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those annals describe a country of many small rulers rather than a kingdom: Tiglath-pileser III takes tribute from \"the chieftains of the Medes\" as far as Mount Bikni, and Sargon II from 28 mayors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Assyria carried them off in tens of thousands, 65,000 in a single campaign, and Sennacherib could still call them the distant Medes \"whose name no one among the kings, my fathers, had ever heard\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells another story altogether, of a people who chose Deioces as king, built him Ecbatana inside seven rings of wall, and were ruled by four kings in succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The two accounts meet at the fall of Assyria, where the Babylonian chronicle brings the Medes in under Cyaxares, and after Ashur fell the Mede and the Babylonian met over its ruins and made a formal alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nineveh went the same way two years later, and with it the empire that had been taking Median tribute for the better part of two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Median rule was itself ended by Cyrus, who deposed Astyages after what Herodotus reckons a reign of 35 years, and the Persians took Asia from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name outlived the power, for Darius records at Behistun a rebel who rose in Media claiming the family of Cyaxares, and Herodotus knew of that revolt as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whatever the Medes built is described in Assyrian, Babylonian, Old Persian and Greek, and never once in Median.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","state","history","politics","iron age","iran"],"sources":["Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 1, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from the Earliest Times to Sargon</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1926), 206–7, 257, 281, 286, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria1/ancient_records_assyria1_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 6, 118, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 9–11, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>The Behistan Inscription of King Darius: Translation and Critical Notes to the Persian Text</i> (Nashville: Vanderbilt University, 1908), 16–17, https://archive.org/stream/behistaninscrip00tolmgoog/behistaninscrip00tolmgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.95–130, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Medes_and_Persians_at_eastern_stairs_of_the_Apadana%2C_Persepolis.JPG/1280px-Medes_and_Persians_at_eastern_stairs_of_the_Apadana%2C_Persepolis.JPG","title":"Medes and Persians on the Apadana stairs at Persepolis","desc":"Two kinds of dress alternate along the eastern stairway, rounded caps and short belted coats beside fluted tiaras and long robes, identified as Medes and Persians.","credit":"Photograph by Bontenbal, 2011. CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medes_and_Persians_at_eastern_stairs_of_the_Apadana%2C_Persepolis.JPG","alt":"A carved stone frieze of bearded men walking in profile, some in tall fluted headdresses and long robes and others in rounded caps and short belted coats, each holding the hand of the man ahead."}},
{"id":"wh-300","num":300,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"A clay cylinder in Akkadian has Marduk seek a righteous prince and call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by name, then bring him into Babylon without battle to restore gods to their cities.","questions":["Herodotus says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took Babylon by drawing the Euphrates into a marsh and walking his army up the emptied channel, and died years later fighting the Massagetae.","Styling himself king of Anshan through Cambyses and Teispes, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took Babylon's old titles for his own and founded the empire Darius later claimed kinship with."],"answer":"Cyrus the Great","answerText":"Cyrus the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 559 – 530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Babylon taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">539 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cyrus the Great</b> founded the Persian empire, and the fullest statement of how he wished his conquest of Babylon understood is a clay cylinder written in Akkadian in his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a Babylonian text of an entirely conventional kind, denouncing Nabonidus for stopping the daily offerings and grinding his people down until Marduk sought out \"a righteous prince, after his own heart\" and called Cyrus by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On it he takes the old titles, king of Babylon, king of Sumer and Akkad, king of the four quarters of the world, and traces himself through Cambyses and an earlier Cyrus to Teispes, all styled kings of Anshan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That city was no invention of his: Assyrian annals list Anzan and Parsuash among the allies the king of Elam brought against Sennacherib at Halule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cylinder has him enter Babylon \"without battle and conflict\", the people kissing his feet, and returning gods to their cities with their people gathered back to their dwellings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Those restorations are Mesopotamian, naming Ashur, Susa, Agade and the cities beyond the Tigris, and the surviving text says nothing of Judah, ending in a prayer that the gods ask Marduk for long life for Cyrus and his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek writers tell the same conquest quite differently, Herodotus having him take Babylon by drawing the Euphrates off into a marsh and walking his men up the emptied channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus gives him his end as well, killed among the Massagetae after a reign one year short of thirty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon made him something else again, the model ruler of a long didactic romance on how it is that men come to obey willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> And Darius, who was no son of his, tied his own line to the same royalty at Behistun, naming Achaemenes as its founder and calling Cambyses son of Cyrus a man of his family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","ruler","history","politics","iron age","iran"],"sources":["Robert William Rogers, <i>Cuneiform Parallels to the Old Testament</i> (New York: Eaton and Mains, 1912), 380–84, https://archive.org/stream/cuneiformparalle00rogeuoft/cuneiformparalle00rogeuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 155–56, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.188–214, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=191. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 1.1, trans. Walter Miller (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]","Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>The Behistan Inscription of King Darius: Translation and Critical Notes to the Persian Text</i> (Nashville: Vanderbilt University, 1908), col. I, sections 2–3 and 10, https://archive.org/stream/behistaninscrip00tolmgoog/behistaninscrip00tolmgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Cyrus_Cylinder_front.jpg/1280px-Cyrus_Cylinder_front.jpg","title":"The Cyrus Cylinder","desc":"A barrel-shaped clay cylinder written in Akkadian in the name of Cyrus, from Babylon and now in the British Museum.","credit":"Photograph by Prioryman, 2012. CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyrus_Cylinder_front.jpg","alt":"A barrel-shaped clay cylinder, pale ochre and broken across the middle, its curved surface covered in close horizontal lines of cuneiform, resting on a clear museum mount."}},
{"id":"gc-001","num":1,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Guangdong","answerText":"Guangdong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty ports</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zones opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1980</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guangdong"},"facts":[["Capital","Guangzhou"],["Population","126M (2020)"],["Largest city","Guangzhou"],["Area","179,800 km² (69,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"廣東","hanzi":"广东","pinyin":"Guǎngdōng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guangdong</b> is a province on the southern coast of mainland China, and for most of its recorded history the sea rather than the land behind it has set its direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its great city, Guangzhou, stands at the head of the Pearl River estuary, and long contact with foreign traders gave the province an influence over its neighbours out of all proportion to its size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Tang collapsed in the 10th century the region was held at Canton by one of the generals who divided the south into seven separate houses while the north was fought over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Guangdong and neighbouring Fujian resisted the Manchu conquest longer than anywhere else, and Canton, where the last adherents of the Ming had entrenched themselves, was stormed on 26 November 1650.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Piracy followed the trade: a fleet of some six hundred junks worked the estuary for years and was broken only in 1810, when the provincial government turned a quarrel between its leaders to account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Qing then narrowed foreign trade to a single door, and in 1757 an imperial edict made Canton the sole staple and forbade foreign trade at every other port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreigners dealt through a guild of Chinese merchants first organised in 1720 and known to them as the Co-hong, under a system of security merchants instituted in 1754 that made one Chinese house answerable for each foreign ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement supported a whole industry of goods made for foreign buyers, of which the most distinctive was export painting, at its height under the Qing and fading in the early 20th century as photography arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also produced a war, and the Treaty of Nanking of 29 August 1842 ended the monopoly, opening four further ports and ceding Hong Kong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second opening began in July 1979, when the central government decided that Guangdong and Fujian should lead it, and the first special economic zones followed at Shenzhen, Zhuhai and Shantou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1910), 65–67, 296, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.69484. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39, 44, 49, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 203, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Jinghui Ao, Ziying Ye, Weicong Li, and Shanshan Ji, “Impressions of Guangzhou City in Qing Dynasty Export Paintings in the Context of Trade Economy: A Color Analysis of Paintings Based on K-means Clustering Algorithm,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 12, no. 1 (2024): art. 77, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-024-01195-4. [Open access]","Douglas Zhihua Zeng, “How Do Special Economic Zones and Industrial Clusters Drive China's Rapid Development?,” Policy Research Working Paper 5583, World Bank, March 2011, https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/310891468018256346. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/39002-Danxiashan_%2848989060302%29.jpg/1920px-39002-Danxiashan_%2848989060302%29.jpg","title":"Mount Danxia, Guangdong","desc":"The red sandstone peaks of Mount Danxia.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:39002-Danxiashan_(48989060302).jpg","alt":"Steep red sandstone hills above forest."}},
{"id":"gc-501","num":501,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Guangzhou","answerText":"Guangzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty port</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second Opium War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1857–1858</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guangdong","dot":"Guangzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Guangdong"],["Population","18.7M (2020)"],["Rank in China","5th largest"],["Population growth","+47.0% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","china"],"traditional":"廣州","hanzi":"广州","pinyin":"Guǎngzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guangzhou</b> is the capital of Guangdong, at the head of the Pearl River estuary, and the city Europeans long knew as Canton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Tang dynasty collapsed in the 10th century it was held through two or three reigns by one of the generals who divided the south among themselves while the north was fought over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Adherents of the fallen Ming entrenched themselves in the city against the Manchu conquest, and it was stormed on 26 November 1650 in a siege an eyewitness thought worth recording.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Qing the regulation of foreign trade began in 1702, when an official broker known to the foreigners as the Emperor's Merchant was made the sole channel through which they could buy tea and silk or sell anything of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That monopoly satisfied nobody, and the Canton merchants formed a guild of their own in 1720, working from 1754 under a system that made one of them answerable for each foreign ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> An imperial edict of 1757 then made the city the sole staple for foreign trade and closed every other port to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreigners were confined to a row of factories outside the wall, came up for the trading season and withdrew to Macao when it ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain replaced the East India Company's monopoly with a crown officer, and Lord Napier reached Macao on 15 July 1834; his attempt to address the governor directly rather than through the merchants ended in his withdrawal and death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The quarrel over opium that followed became a war, and the Treaty of Nanking of 29 August 1842 ended the city's monopoly by opening four other ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its workshops had meanwhile built an export trade in goods made for foreign taste, above all painting and porcelain, whose surviving pieces are now studied as maritime heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1910), 63–67, 71–74, 296, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.69484. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39, 44, 112–22, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 203–7, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Jinghui Ao, Ziying Ye, Weicong Li, and Shanshan Ji, “Impressions of Guangzhou City in Qing Dynasty Export Paintings in the Context of Trade Economy: A Color Analysis of Paintings Based on K-means Clustering Algorithm,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 12, no. 1 (2024): art. 77, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-024-01195-4. [Open access]","Jinghui Ao, Weicong Li, Shanshan Ji, and Simin Chen, “Maritime Silk Road Heritage: Quantitative Typological Analysis of Qing Dynasty Export Porcelain Bowls from Guangzhou from the Perspective of Social Factors,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 11, no. 1 (2023): art. 263, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-023-01103-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Guangzhou_Skyline%2C_shot_in_Panyu_District%2C_Guangzhou.jpg/1920px-Guangzhou_Skyline%2C_shot_in_Panyu_District%2C_Guangzhou.jpg","title":"Guangzhou","desc":"Guangzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"YikyuenG, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guangzhou_Skyline,_shot_in_Panyu_District,_Guangzhou.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Guangzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-002","num":2,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Shandong","answerText":"Shandong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">German lease</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">To Japan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1919</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shandong"},"facts":[["Capital","Jinan"],["Population","102M (2020)"],["Largest city","Linyi"],["Area","157,900 km² (61,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"山東","hanzi":"山东","pinyin":"Shāndōng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shandong</b> is a coastal province of northern China, a peninsula reaching into the Yellow Sea with the plain of the lower Yellow River behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was once an island, and was joined to the mainland only as alluvium carried down by the rivers silted up the water between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its standing in Chinese culture rests on two things above all: the memory of Confucius, and Mount Tai, one of the country's five sacred mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Qufu, where he was born, is a place of pilgrimage still, and its historic quarters have lately been rebuilt under conservation schemes in which the residents' own voices are easily lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Grand Canal crossed the province and long gave its produce an easy outlet north and south, before the railways began to take that trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Yellow River has been the other constant: having silted its bed up above the surrounding country, it broke through the embankments, flooded the low-lying west for years together and sought a new outlet to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its delta has been settled and resettled ever since, villages following the channels, because the silt that ruins one district improves the soil of another and makes it worth farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Germany then claimed the province as its sphere of development, and by a convention signed on 6 March 1898 the enclave of Kiaochow was ceded to it on lease, provisionally for ninety-nine years, together with the right to build railways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Resentment of that pressure fed the Boxer society, organised here with the sympathy of officials, and after the murder of the missionary S. M. Brooks on the last day of 1899 the governor Yuxian was held responsible for its rise and was replaced by Yuan Shikai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany lost the enclave in the First World War, but China did not get it back: article 156 of the Treaty of Versailles renounced those rights in favour of Japan, the railway to the provincial capital included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 3 (London: Longmans, Green, 1918), 88–89, 174–92, https://archive.org/details/cu31924017552310. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 78–82, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 382–86, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Yuting Liu, Yu Ye, Chang Jiang, Jinfeng Jiang, Fahao Wang, Jun Li, and Xiangping Zhang, “Dynamics of Rural Settlement Patterns in the Yellow River Delta in Response to River Diversion and Avulsion Since 1855,” <i>Rural Landscapes: Society, Environment, History</i> 11, no. 1 (2024): art. 2, https://doi.org/10.16993/rl.100. [Open access]","Yingchun Zhang and Zongjie Wu, “Heritage Conservation and Urban Landscaping of Ancient Pan Pool Neighborhood, Qufu: A Historical and Indigenous Perspective,” <i>Conservation Science in Cultural Heritage</i> 14 (2014): 175–89, https://doi.org/10.6092/issn.1973-9494/5451. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace with Germany,” 28 June 1919, part IV, section VIII, article 156, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiv.asp. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/50304-Taishan_%2849055660366%29.jpg/1920px-50304-Taishan_%2849055660366%29.jpg","title":"Mount Tai, Shandong","desc":"Mount Tai.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:50304-Taishan_(49055660366).jpg","alt":"A stone stairway climbing a wooded mountain."}},
{"id":"gc-003","num":3,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Henan","answerText":"Henan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1509 – 1315 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Northern Song</span><span class=\"dt-v\">960 – 1127 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Henan"},"facts":[["Capital","Zhengzhou"],["Population","99.4M (2020)"],["Largest city","Zhengzhou"],["Area","167,000 km² (64,500 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"河南","pinyin":"Hénán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Henan</b> lies at the heart of China's Central Plains, spanning the middle and lower reaches of the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fertility earned it the name of the Central Flower, and the name Zhongguo, the Middle Kingdom, later given to the whole country, may go back to the same idea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The valley of the Luo, running parallel to the Yellow River, is famous in Chinese history, while the mountains closing the province on the west leave few passes towards the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Songshan region at its centre, which takes in both Zhengzhou and Luoyang, is the core area of early Chinese civilisation, and settlement there has been traced from the Neolithic through the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beneath the modern provincial capital lies Zhengzhou Shang City, the seat of the early Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Excavations at Yinxu, in this province, were read in 1928 as showing that Chinese civilisation began towards the end of the Shang, and the ruins uncovered at Erlitou in Yanshi in 1960 as carrying its beginnings back to the Xia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both readings have since been qualified: several regions, among them the Yellow River and Yangtze basins, the country north and south of the Yanshan mountains and the western Liao basin, are now argued to have crossed into civilisation by about 3300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Song dynasty was founded from Kaifeng, where the army found their chosen general drunk and threw the yellow robe over him before he could reply; by the end of his seventeen-year reign most provinces had submitted, though the Liao and Xia kingdoms held out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kaifeng was besieged again when a Taiping column reached the provincial capital in June, was repulsed after three days and crossed the Yellow River westwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recognised traditional villages cluster in the north around Anyang and Hebi, in the centre near Pingdingshan and in the south at Xinyang, while eastern Henan has few, partly because the Yellow River has flooded it so long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 57–59, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Lijie Yan, Xia Wang, Li Zhang, and Fei Teng, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Clustering Patterns of Settlements from the Neolithic to the Bronze Age, 9000–3000 BP, in the Songshan Mountain Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 7 (2026): e0351644, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0351644. [Open access]","Zhongpei Zhang, “Processus d'évolution de la civilisation chinoise antique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 148, no. 2 (2004): 533–42, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2004.22728. [Open access]","Fang Fang, Jingwen Liao, Xiaomin Zeng, and Juzhong Zhang, “The Truth of Unusual Deaths under Military Expansion: Evidence from the Stable Isotopes of a Human Skull Ditch in the Capital City of the Early Shang Dynasty, China,” <i>Genes</i> 13, no. 11 (2022): 2077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9690788/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39–40, 245–46, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Man Zhang, Jiabin Wu, and Yu Liu, “Temporal and Spatial Patterns and Determinants of Traditional Villages in Henan Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 5 (2026): e0350290, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0350290. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/27427-Luoyang_%2849067744628%29.jpg/1920px-27427-Luoyang_%2849067744628%29.jpg","title":"the Longmen Grottoes, Henan","desc":"The Longmen Grottoes.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:27427-Luoyang_(49067744628).jpg","alt":"Buddha figures carved into a river cliff."}},
{"id":"gc-004","num":4,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jiangsu","answerText":"Jiangsu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ming capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1368 – 1403</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jiangsu"},"facts":[["Capital","Nanjing"],["Population","84.7M (2020)"],["Largest city","Suzhou"],["Area","107,200 km² (41,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"江蘇","hanzi":"江苏","pinyin":"Jiāngsū","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jiangsu</b> lies on the Yellow Sea in the lower reaches of the Yangtze and the Huai, flat almost throughout and threaded with canals and lakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name is a compound of two of its own cities, Jiangning — which is Nanjing — and Suzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Grand Canal runs the length of it, and Suzhou, Changzhou, Zhenjiang, Yangzhou and Huai'an all stand on its line, while in the south a dense web of canals and lakes carries traffic that heavier shipping cannot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhu Yuanzhang made Nanjing his capital when he expelled the Mongols and founded the Ming in 1368, and after a reign of thirty years left the throne to a grandson of sixteen, whom his own uncle overthrew five years later and who took the capital back to Peking in 1403.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Qing the city was the residence of the viceroy of the Two Jiangs, and had been the capital of the empire before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Taiping took Nanjing, Zhenjiang and Yangzhou, garrisoned them and put them in a state of defence, and were confident enough afterwards to send a division north to try for Peking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Winning the province back took years: Suzhou surrendered to the foreign-officered Ever-Victorious Force under Charles Gordon, its rebel commanders were executed by the governor once it had, and Nanjing fell last of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jiangsu is one of the country's great grain regions, growing wheat, rice, maize, soybeans, rapeseed and peanuts, rice above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That farmland is under pressure from the towns, and satellite mapping shows single-season rice giving way to built-up ground, the same conversion that has driven the province's stock of stored carbon down since 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lake Taihu shows the pressure in the water: across two decades of satellite images its cyanobacterial blooms have spread, beginning earlier in the year and ending later as the climate has warmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 153–56, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 43, 240–41, 261–69, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Kashif Ali Solangi, Canhua Yang, Farheen Solangi, Weirong Zhang, Jinling Zhang et al., “Estimating Rice Cropping Area and Analyzing Land Use and Land Cover Changes in Jiangsu Province Using Multispectral Satellite Imagery,” <i>Plants</i> 15, no. 5 (2026): 715, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12986608/. [Open access]","Zhenxing Xiong, Yihao Zhang, Maohong Liu, Yuan Gao, and Tianci Gu, “Assessing Land Urbanization and Ecological Planning Impact on Carbon Stock and Its Economic Value from a Multi-Scenario Perspective,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 30494, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-13896-2. [Open access]","Xinyi Li, Xin Hang, Shihua Zhu, Liangxiao Sun, and Yachun Li, “Response of Cyanobacterial Blooms to Climate Warming: Evidence from Satellite Observations and Long-Term Meteorological Data in Lake Taihu,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 38820, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12589429/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Suzhou_Zhuozheng_Yuan_2015.04.23_08-13-49.jpg/1920px-Suzhou_Zhuozheng_Yuan_2015.04.23_08-13-49.jpg","title":"the classical gardens of Suzhou, Jiangsu","desc":"The Humble Administrator's Garden at Suzhou.","credit":"Zhangzhugang, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suzhou_Zhuozheng_Yuan_2015.04.23_08-13-49.jpg","alt":"A pavilion and pond in a classical Chinese garden."}},
{"id":"gc-005","num":5,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Sichuan","answerText":"Sichuan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanxingdui</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Minor Han</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 – 263 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Sichuan"},"facts":[["Capital","Chengdu"],["Population","83.7M (2020)"],["Largest city","Chengdu"],["Area","486,000 km² (188,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"四川","pinyin":"Sìchuān","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Sichuan</b> is a province of south-western China, and its name means \"four rivers\", for the Yalong, the Min, the Chong and the Jialing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tibet lies to its west and Shaanxi and Gansu to its north, and its capital stands at the centre of the basin where most of its people have always lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest power that basin is known to have held was the kingdom of Shu, whose centre at Sanxingdui gave up two pits of bronzes in 1986 — a standing figure over two and a half metres tall, a great mask, and trees of bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charcoal from a further pit opened in 2021 dates to between 1199 and 1017 BCE, which is the late Shang, so those bronzes were being cast while the dynasty of the Central Plains ruled far to the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shu afterwards moved its centre to Jinsha, which lies inside modern Chengdu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Han the region was divided into five principalities, of which the one called Shu was seized by the Minor Han dynasty of 221 to 263 CE, whose capital stood where Chengdu stands now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its founder, Liu Bei, was a poor kinsman of the imperial house who had dealt in straw sandals in his youth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The last years of the Ming brought a rebellion that carried destruction across the whole province, and the Manchus who followed had to spread peace over it again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eastern Sichuan was then peopled from elsewhere, and a century ago a traveller found it rare to meet anyone who called himself a native: a man named instead the province his immigrant ancestor had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That mixing is still legible in the people: work on the Y chromosome across the Chengdu plain finds Han, Tibetan, Yi, Qiang and Hui whose paternal lines descend chiefly from indigenous East Asian millet and rice farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 104–6, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Weijie Zhao, “New Archeological Marvels of Ancient Shu Civilization,” <i>National Science Review</i> 8, no. 7 (2021): nwab071, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8310767/. [Open access]","Kuei-chen Lin, “On Craft Production and the Settlement Pattern of the Jinsha Site Cluster on the Chengdu Plain,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 367–400, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76789. [Open access]","Alexander Hosie, <i>Szechwan: Its Products, Industries and Resources</i> (Shanghai: Kelly and Walsh, 1922), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/szechwanitsprodu00hosi. [Open access]","Lisiteng Luo, Yanling Liu, Yalan Liu, Yunhui Liu, Jie Zhong et al., “Paternal Genomic History and Y-Chromosome Phylogeny from the Chengdu Plain Reveal Multiple Indigenous East Asian Farmer Contributions to Ancient Shu,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 24, no. 1 (2026): 54, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-026-02527-5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/1_jiuzhaigou_valley_wu_hua_hai_2011b.jpg/1920px-1_jiuzhaigou_valley_wu_hua_hai_2011b.jpg","title":"Jiuzhaigou, Sichuan","desc":"The lakes of Jiuzhaigou.","credit":"Chensiyuan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_jiuzhaigou_valley_wu_hua_hai_2011b.jpg","alt":"Clear turquoise lakes below wooded mountains."}},
{"id":"gc-502","num":502,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Jinan","answerText":"Jinan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rail to Qingdao</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1904</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Springs again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 2003</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shandong","dot":"Jinan"},"facts":[["Province","Shandong"],["Population","9.20M (2020)"],["Rank in China","28th largest"],["Population growth","+13.4% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","china"],"traditional":"濟南","hanzi":"济南","pinyin":"Jǐnán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jinan</b> is the capital of Shandong, standing on the right bank of the Yellow River and to the north-west of Mount Tai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Qing the province was governed from here, by a governor rather than by a viceroy of the kind that ruled the province next door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city is built over karst, and groundwater rising through the limestone feeds the springs it is famous for, of which Baotu is the chief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pumping for a growing city drew the water table down until the springs failed, and only when the wells the city had sunk for itself were closed did Baotu begin flowing again, on 6 September 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has flowed since, helped by artificial recharge and by an emergency plan that fixes the level at which warnings are issued, while the city has spread outwards until the belt where it meets the countryside runs north-east and east in an almost unbroken band.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its modern history turns on a railway, and on who was to be allowed to build it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Germany leased the enclave of Kiaochow in 1898 it took with it the right to build in Shandong, and an imperial charter of 1 June 1899 set up a company to run a line from the coast at Tsingtau up to Jinan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Chinese scheme for a line from Tientsin through Jinan to Chinkiang had been authorised at the end of 1897, and the German envoy opposed it at once, claiming that his country alone might build railways in the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German line was finished in 1904, and the trade of Shandong moved along it from the old port of Chefoo to Tsingtau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Article 156 of the Treaty of Versailles then handed Germany's rights in the enclave and in that railway to Japan rather than back to China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Changsuo Li, Liting Xing, Yanan Dong, Yuming Peng, Xuerui Xing et al., “Numerical Simulation and Protection of the Dynamic Change of Jinan Karst Spring Based on Coupling of Seepage and Conduit Flow,” <i>Heliyon</i> 8, no. 9 (2022): e10428, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9437806/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 78–82, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 3 (London: Longmans, Green, 1918), 88–90, https://archive.org/details/cu31924017552310. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace with Germany,” 28 June 1919, part IV, section VIII, article 156, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiv.asp. [Open access]","Dawei Mei, Ningning Liu, and Haijiao Yu, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Driving Factors of Urban–Rural Integration in the Jinan Metropolitan Area,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 17223, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13234028/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Panorama_of_Jinan_from_Mt_Qianfo.jpg/1920px-Panorama_of_Jinan_from_Mt_Qianfo.jpg","title":"Jinan","desc":"Jinan, seen from a distance.","credit":"Vikarna, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_of_Jinan_from_Mt_Qianfo.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Jinan."}},
{"id":"gc-006","num":6,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hebei","answerText":"Hebei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Millet at Cishan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aquifer recovery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hebei"},"facts":[["Capital","Shijiazhuang"],["Population","74.6M (2020)"],["Largest city","Baoding"],["Area","188,800 km² (72,900 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"河北","pinyin":"Héběi","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hebei</b> wraps around Beijing and Tianjin on the North China Plain, running from the Bohai Sea in the east to the Taihang mountains in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Qing it was Zhili, the metropolitan province, and its viceroy resided not where the government sits today but at Baoding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the mountains to the north lies Chengde, known also as Jehol, the warm river, and it is through that country that a traveller coming down from the Mongolian plateaux reaches the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plain has been farmed for a very long time: storage pits at Cishan, in Wu'an on the province's southern edge, held common millet dating back about 10,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That makes broomcorn millet the earliest dry-farming crop yet identified in East Asia, which its resistance to drought would go some way to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Foxtail and broomcorn millet were being domesticated across the north between roughly 8300 and 4300 BCE, and the yields they eventually reached fed a population that grew steadily from 5000 to 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xinglong, in northern Hebei on the Inner Mongolian border, shows the other side of that: at 5700 to 5600 BCE its people were still gathering wild plants in quantities that often outweighed the millet they grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farming a dry plain on that scale has since been done with groundwater, and the aquifer beneath it became one of the most severely depleted in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Since 2020 the water levels have risen again, passing their 2005 depth by 2024, in a reversal driven by bringing surface water up from the humid south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The province's ecologically richest ground lies in the north, on the plateau around Chengde and Zhangjiakou, and its poorest is the plain where most of its people live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 66–68, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang, Kam-biu Liu, Naiqin Wu, Yumei Li et al., “Earliest Domestication of Common Millet (<i>Panicum miliaceum</i>) in East Asia Extended to 10,000 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 18 (2009): 7367–72, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2678631/. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens, Yijie Zhuang, and Dorian Q. Fuller, “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Di Long, Yuancheng Xu, Yingjie Cui, Yanhong Cui, James J. Butler Jr. et al., “Unprecedented Large-Scale Aquifer Recovery through Human Intervention,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 7296, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-62719-5. [Open access]","Min-Na Gao, Yong-Xiang Hu, Guo-Fei Shang, and Wei Li, “Research on the Impact of Spatial and Temporal Changes in Ecological Product Value in Hebei Province Based on Land Use Changes,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 9571, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11926369/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Chengde_Mountain_Resort_22944-Chengde_%2844830038471%29.jpg/1920px-Chengde_Mountain_Resort_22944-Chengde_%2844830038471%29.jpg","title":"the Chengde Mountain Resort, Hebei","desc":"The imperial summer resort at Chengde.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chengde_Mountain_Resort_22944-Chengde_(44830038471).jpg","alt":"Imperial garden buildings beside a lake."}},
{"id":"gc-503","num":503,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Zhengzhou","answerText":"Zhengzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1509 – 1315 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">7·20 rainstorm</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 July 2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Henan","dot":"Zhengzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Henan"],["Population","12.6M (2020)"],["Rank in China","9th largest"],["Population growth","+46.1% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","china"],"traditional":"鄭州","hanzi":"郑州","pinyin":"Zhèngzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Zhengzhou</b> is the capital of Henan, standing where the western hills of the province give way to the plain of the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Songshan region it sits in is the core area of early Chinese civilisation, and its settlement clusters have been traced there from the Neolithic through the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beneath the modern streets lies Zhengzhou Shang City, the capital of the early Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavation since the 1950s has turned up inner and outer city walls, palaces, water supply and drainage, handicraft workshops, cellars of bronzes, sacrificial remains and tombs, most of them of the Erligang period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1974 a ditch was found in the palace area holding nearly a hundred human skulls, most of them sawn across just above the ear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Who those people were has been disputed ever since, and strontium, carbon and nitrogen isotopes measured on eleven of the skulls have been used to ask where they grew up and what they had eaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The answer proposed is that they were not slaves but prisoners taken from the Xiaomintun district of Anyang, which would open a bloody chapter between the two Shang capitals that nothing else records; the authors are careful to say that more work is needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The railway driven through Henan at the end of the Qing, joining the Yellow River basin to that of the Yangtze, was already drawing trade and activity towards the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As the built-up area spread, the city's heat island spread with it from the centre outwards, worsening until about 2014 and easing since as tree cover was planted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its other modern difficulty is the ground itself: after the extreme rainstorm of 20 July 2021 radar from orbit found zones of subsidence and of uplift within the city, the sinking keeping step with falling groundwater and worst in the districts built up most densely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Fang Fang, Jingwen Liao, Xiaomin Zeng, and Juzhong Zhang, “The Truth of Unusual Deaths under Military Expansion: Evidence from the Stable Isotopes of a Human Skull Ditch in the Capital City of the Early Shang Dynasty, China,” <i>Genes</i> 13, no. 11 (2022): 2077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9690788/. [Open access]","Zheng Zhou, Jiyuan Hu, Jiayao Wang, Lijun Wang, Tianrong Qiao et al., “Identifying Spatiotemporal Pattern and Trend Prediction of Land Subsidence in Zhengzhou Combining MT-InSAR, XGBoost and Hydrogeological Analysis,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 3848, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-87789-9. [Open access]","Yarong Yang, Fan Song, Jie Ma, Zheng Wei, Lili Song, and Wei Cao, “Spatial and Temporal Variation of Heat Islands in the Main Urban Area of Zhengzhou under the Two-Way Influence of Urbanization and Urban Forestry,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272626, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272626. [Open access]","Lijie Yan, Xia Wang, Li Zhang, and Fei Teng, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Clustering Patterns of Settlements from the Neolithic to the Bronze Age, 9000–3000 BP, in the Songshan Mountain Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 7 (2026): e0351644, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0351644. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 57–59, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Zhengzhou_CBD.JPG/1920px-Zhengzhou_CBD.JPG","title":"Zhengzhou","desc":"Zhengzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"Planetfinder, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhengzhou_CBD.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Zhengzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-007","num":7,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hunan","answerText":"Hunan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Middle Neolithic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8000 – 5000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taiping</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1852</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hunan"},"facts":[["Capital","Changsha"],["Population","66.4M (2020)"],["Largest city","Changsha"],["Area","211,800 km² (81,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"湖南","pinyin":"Húnán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hunan</b> means \"south of the lake\", and nearly the whole province lies south of Dongting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital stands on the right bank of the Xiang, the river down which most of its traffic has always moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cultivation shows early in this basin: the middle Neolithic of the middle and lower Yangtze, from 8000 to 5000 BCE, is the first phase of farming before domestication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its key cultures are the Pengtoushan-Zaoshi of the Li basin around the lake and the Datang of the middle and lower Xiang, and most of their sites sit on river terraces rather than in caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farming did not simply spread outward from them: the Gaomiao culture of the Yuan valley in the north of the province derived from Pengtoushan and yet appears to have given agriculture up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On Mount Heng an inscription of seventy-seven characters was long held to record the emperor Yu's work on the flood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was copied about 1210 from a stone then standing in a Taoist temple, published in the form now generally accepted about 1510 by a Hunan antiquary, and found broken on the summit in 1666 by a man who had reached it with ladders and hooks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Taiping crossed into the province in 1852 and their boats went up the Xiang past the provincial capital, taking one town after another for supplies without setting up a government in any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The armies raised against them left a legacy of their own: the Gelaohui, a Hunan society, grew out of a loyal club founded by Zeng Guofan during the rebellion and turned into an organisation for overthrowing the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dongting, the second largest freshwater lake in the country, still swells and shrinks between the dry season and the flood, while the province's forests, its chief carbon sinks, have been taking up less as transport and industrial land spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 131–32, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from \"The Middle Kingdom\"</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 15–16, 245–48, 433–34, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Chi Zhang and Hsiao-chun Hung, “The Neolithic of Southern China—Origin, Development, and Dispersal,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 47, no. 2 (2008): 299–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17291. [Open access]","Hongyong Xiang, Xiang Li, Jie Chen, Yuping Dai, Ting Yi et al., “The Dynamic Changes of Eco-Environmental Quality in Dongting Lake Wetland during 1990–2020, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 34526, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12494795/. [Open access]","Qikang Zhong, Yuxin Liu, Zhe Li, and Jiawei Zhu, “Impact of Land Use Change on Carbon Metabolism in Hunan Province: An Ecological Network Analysis Approach,” <i>iScience</i> 28, no. 9 (2025): 113209, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12392657/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/1_tianzishan_wulingyuan_zhangjiajie_2012.jpg/1920px-1_tianzishan_wulingyuan_zhangjiajie_2012.jpg","title":"Wulingyuan, Hunan","desc":"The sandstone pillars of Wulingyuan.","credit":"chensiyuan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_tianzishan_wulingyuan_zhangjiajie_2012.jpg","alt":"Tall narrow sandstone pillars rising out of forest."}},
{"id":"gc-504","num":504,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Nanjing","answerText":"Nanjing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ming capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1368 – 1403</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nanking treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 August 1842</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jiangsu","dot":"Nanjing"},"facts":[["Province","Jiangsu"],["Population","9.31M (2020)"],["Rank in China","27th largest"],["Population growth","+16.4% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"南京","pinyin":"Nánjīng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nanjing</b> is the capital of Jiangsu and stands on the lower Yangtze, where the two banks give it a composite landscape of mountains, hills and plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the capital of the empire long before Beijing was, and under the Qing it was the residence of the viceroy of the Two Jiangs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Four princes of the Qi reigned here in the 6th century, the last of them besieged in his own capital by a minister who turned on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Zhu Yuanzhang made it his seat when he expelled the Mongols and founded the Ming in 1368, calling it the Southern Capital, which is what its name means; his son took the capital back to Peking in 1403.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ming city walls still stand among modern ones, and the two carry quite different plants: the historic walls hold many more species, over half of them woody, where the modern walls are dominated by herbs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In the older romanisation the city was written Nanking, and the treaty that ended the First Opium War was signed here and carries that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was concluded on board Her Britannic Majesty's ship Cornwallis on 29 August 1842, and the ratifications were exchanged at Hong Kong the following 26 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its second article opened Canton, Amoy, Fuchau-fu, Ningpo and Shanghai to British residence and trade, and its third ceded Hong Kong in perpetuity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decade later the Taiping made the city their capital, held it while their armies went north and west, and lost it last of all the places they had taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Building along its river frontage has been restricted since 2021 under the policy of conserving the Yangtze rather than developing it, and the modern city is now large enough to be modelled building by building, commercial premises turning out far more carbon-intensive than homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Changran Sun, Gongxian Fang, Yangyang Lu, Zihan Zhu, Mingjiang Deng, and Guohua Fang, “Multiscale Spatiotemporal Evolution and Patterns of Riverine Ecological Security in Nanjing Yangtze River Basin,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 3 (2026): 115149, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.115149. [Open access]","Yuxin Chen, Zhenyu Wang, Quan Wen, Jing Meng, Jingwen Huo et al., “A Building-Scale Modeling Framework for Urban Net-Zero Transitions in Nanjing,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 8954, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-64016-7. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, ed., <i>Treaties between the Empire of China and Foreign Powers</i>, 4th ed. (Shanghai: North-China Herald, 1902), 1–5, https://archive.org/details/treatiesbetween00mayegoog. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 32, 43, 240–41, 269, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Wenxin Yu, Kaidi Wang, Yunfeng Yang, Sha Li, and Yao Xiong, “Environmental Drivers and Seasonal Dynamics of Spontaneous Plant Communities on Urban Walls: A Case Study in Nanjing, China,” <i>Plants</i> 15, no. 4 (2026): 541, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12944048/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 153, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Skyline_of_Nanjing_%28View_from_Nanjing_Railway_Station%29%2C_Jul_4_2024_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Nanjing_%28View_from_Nanjing_Railway_Station%29%2C_Jul_4_2024_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Nanjing","desc":"Nanjing, seen from a distance.","credit":"A Chinese ID, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Nanjing_(View_from_Nanjing_Railway_Station),_Jul_4_2024_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Nanjing."}},
{"id":"gc-008","num":8,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Zhejiang","answerText":"Zhejiang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Liangzhu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5300 – 4300 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Southern Song</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1127 – 1280 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Zhejiang"},"facts":[["Capital","Hangzhou"],["Population","64.6M (2020)"],["Largest city","Hangzhou"],["Area","105,500 km² (40,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"浙江","pinyin":"Zhèjiāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Zhejiang</b> lies at the southern edge of the Yangtze delta, meeting Shanghai and Jiangsu along its northern border, and is a mountainous province rather than a deltaic one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bay and plains were settled very early, and the lower Yangtze sequence runs from the Shangshan through the Kuahuqiao and Hemudu cultures to Liangzhu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pigs at Jingtoushan and at Kuahuqiao were eating cooked starchy plants and human waste by 8,000 years ago, which makes them the earliest managed pigs known in the lower Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Geese at the rice village of Tianluoshan about 7,000 years ago were bred locally over several generations and may have been fed cultivated paddy rice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Liangzhu, in what is now Hangzhou, walled a capital with dams, canals and a jade industry, and abandoned it in an exceptionally wet spell between 4,345 and 4,324 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Thousands of years later an Arab account records the sack of Canfu, a port near Hangzhou Bay, in which a hundred and twenty thousand Muslims, Jews, Christians and Parsees engaged in trade were destroyed, which says something about what that trade was worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Jin overran the north, the last emperor of the Northern Song saw his capital in ruins, fled to Nanjing and then to Hangzhou at the mouth of the Qiantang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nanjing was pillaged, but Hangzhou lay too far for the invaders, and it grew in size and strength into a famous capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That emperor, Gaozong, resigned in 1162 after a reign of thirty-six years and survived his abdication by twenty-four more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The province he left behind is still one of the country's leading ones for industry, and the great majority of its larger enterprises are privately owned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 139–41, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 40–41, 63, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Haiwei Zhang, Hai Cheng, Ashish Sinha, Christoph Spötl, Yanjun Cai et al., “Collapse of the Liangzhu and Other Neolithic Cultures in the Lower Yangtze Region in Response to Climate Change,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 48 (2021): eabi9275, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abi9275. [Open access]","Jiajing Wang, Yiyi Tang, Yunfei Zheng, Leping Jiang, Xiaolin Ma et al., “Early Evidence for Pig Domestication (8,000 cal. BP) in the Lower Yangtze, South China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 24 (2025): e2507123122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2507123122. [Open access]","Masaki Eda, Yu Itahashi, Hiroki Kikuchi, Guoping Sun, Kai-hsuan Hsu et al., “Multiple Lines of Evidence of Early Goose Domestication in a 7,000-y-old Rice Cultivation Village in the Lower Yangtze River, China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 12 (2022): e2117064119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2117064119. [Open access]","Junmei Sawada, Kazuhiro Uzawa, Minoru Yoneda, Yasuo Hagihara, Yu Itahashi et al., “Worked Human Bones and the Rise of Urban Society in the Neolithic Liangzhu Culture, East Asia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 31441, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15673-7. [Open access]","Fan Tu, Ruijia Ji, Jinyu Zhou, and Gabriel Hoh Teck Ling, “Shaping Industrial Spatial Density: How Floor Area Ratio Varies across Regions and Sectors in Zhejiang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 3 (2026): e0343089, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0343089. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/West_Lake%2C_Hangzhou_2025.jpg/1920px-West_Lake%2C_Hangzhou_2025.jpg","title":"West Lake, Zhejiang","desc":"West Lake at Hangzhou.","credit":"Takashishin, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Lake,_Hangzhou_2025.jpg","alt":"A causeway and pagoda across a broad lake."}},
{"id":"gc-505","num":505,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Chengdu","answerText":"Chengdu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plain settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shu capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 – 263 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Sichuan","dot":"Chengdu"},"facts":[["Province","Sichuan"],["Population","20.9M (2020)"],["Rank in China","4th largest"],["Population growth","+38.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"成都","pinyin":"Chéngdū","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Chengdu</b> is the capital of Sichuan and stands at the centre of the basin, on the plain that is the province's garden and its granary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plain's water supply never fails, because the Min is divided where it leaves the mountains at Guanxian — Dujiangyan today — into a network of streams and cross-channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Out of that hydraulic engineering grew the linpan, the wooded farmstead clusters that are the plain's own landscape and have a history of more than 2,300 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> People were there long before the water was managed: the late Neolithic Baodun period on the plain runs from about 2400 to 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jinsha, the largest known settlement of the Shi'erqiao culture that followed Sanxingdui, is not one site but a cluster of more than twenty published components in and around the modern city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At its heart lay a ceremonial precinct on the south bank of the Modi river, and once the people of Sanxingdui had dispersed Jinsha became the region's new focus of exchange between regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Han the surrounding country was one of five principalities, and the Minor Han dynasty of 221 to 263 CE ruled it from where the city now stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing is left of that dynasty's palace in the north-west of the city, and the walled enclosure shown to visitors as its imperial residence was really the seat of a Ming prince, a son of the dynasty's founder sent to govern the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The provincial annals call it the Shu Wang Fu, the residence of the Sichuan prince, and in 1665 it became the hall in which candidates sat the provincial examinations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> West of the city the Chengdu section of the Giant Panda National Park spreads across Dujiangyan, Chongzhou, Dayi, Qionglai and Pengzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Hosie, <i>Szechwan: Its Products, Industries and Resources</i> (Shanghai: Kelly and Walsh, 1922), 18–19, 26, https://archive.org/details/szechwanitsprodu00hosi. [Open access]","Kuei-chen Lin, “On Craft Production and the Settlement Pattern of the Jinsha Site Cluster on the Chengdu Plain,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 367–400, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76789. [Open access]","Kabilijiang Umel, Pingpu Zhu, Shuyu Zhang, Mikiko Ishikawa, and Liang Yu, “Perception Study of the Conservation and Utilization of Linpan Cultural Landscape Heritage: The Case of Dujiangyan City,” <i>Journal of Environmental Engineering and Landscape Management</i> 32, no. 1 (2024): 45–56, https://doi.org/10.3846/jeelm.2024.20773. [Open access]","Qianqian Wang, Chi Xu, Yubo Gou, Mingchun Zhang, Ke He et al., “Species Identification and Spatial Diversity Patterns of the Giant Panda National Park (GPNP) in Chengdu, Sichuan, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 16, no. 3 (2026): e73180, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12949331/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 105, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Anshun_Bridge_Jin_River_Chengdu_night_2026_dllu.jpg/1920px-Anshun_Bridge_Jin_River_Chengdu_night_2026_dllu.jpg","title":"Chengdu","desc":"Chengdu, seen from a distance.","credit":"Daniel Lu ( User:dllu ), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anshun_Bridge_Jin_River_Chengdu_night_2026_dllu.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Chengdu."}},
{"id":"gc-009","num":9,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Anhui","answerText":"Anhui","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Five Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">907 – 960 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taiping</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1853</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Anhui"},"facts":[["Capital","Hefei"],["Population","61.0M (2020)"],["Largest city","Hefei"],["Area","141,000 km² (54,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"安徽","pinyin":"Ānhuī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Anhui</b> takes its name from two of its own towns, Anqing and Huizhou, and sits inland at the head of the Yangtze delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Qing it was governed not from its present capital but from Anqing, in the south-west of the province on the left bank of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between the Yangtze and the Huai the Huoshan range runs from south-west to north-east, affording beautiful sites and difficult passages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> South of the river stands Huangshan, a confused chain whose valleys seldom exceed a few hundred yards in width and which is at its most tangled in the Huizhou country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chao Lake lies in the middle of the province, one of the largest bodies of fresh water in the country, and it is from there that the modern capital draws its drinking water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> During the Five Dynasties, while rival houses fought over the valley of the Yellow River, the south and west were portioned among seven ruling families, and this region was held by generals who had served the Tang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Taiping came through it on their northern march, taking Liuhe and Fengyang without meeting serious opposition before crossing into Henan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Huai is a boundary as well as a river: the Qinling-Huai line, which it marks across the province, is the conventional divide between northern and southern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ratoon rice, harvested twice from one sowing, grows almost entirely south of that line, and autumn temperatures north of it are what keep it from spreading further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Water now crosses the line the other way: the Yangtze-to-Huai diversion runs from the river to Chao Lake, along the Jianghuai Canal and northward from there, to relieve drought on the Huai and clean both the lake and the river, and building it turned farmland and forest into water and built ground faster than the province had changed in the decade before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 146–48, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39, 245–46, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Wenqing Ding, Guangzhi Shi, Hui Zha, Haojie Miao, Mengmin Lu, and Jing Jin, “Ecological Impacts and Supply Demand Evolution of the Yangtze to Huaihe Water Transfer Project in Anhui Section,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 20311, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11366761/. [Open access]","Wei Luo, Lixin Yuan, Shengmin Yan, Linlin Wang, Yongfu Yu et al., “Study on the Suitable Area of Ratoon Rice in China under Climate Change,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 12 (2025): e72724, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12710086/. [Open access]","Juan Huang, Jinyan Zhan, Haiming Yan, Feng Wu, and Xiangzheng Deng, “Evaluation of the Impacts of Land Use on Water Quality: A Case Study in the Chaohu Lake Basin,” <i>The Scientific World Journal</i> 2013 (2013): 329187, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3736410/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Huangshan_pic_4.jpg/1920px-Huangshan_pic_4.jpg","title":"Huangshan, Anhui","desc":"The granite peaks of Huangshan.","credit":"Chi King, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huangshan_pic_4.jpg","alt":"Granite peaks and pines above a sea of cloud."}},
{"id":"gc-506","num":506,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Shijiazhuang","answerText":"Shijiazhuang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rail concession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 May 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Building began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1903</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hebei","dot":"Shijiazhuang"},"facts":[["Province","Hebei"],["Population","11.2M (2020)"],["Rank in China","14th largest"],["Population growth","+10.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"石家莊","hanzi":"石家庄","pinyin":"Shíjiāzhuāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shijiazhuang</b> is the capital of Hebei and stands on the plain in the west of the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is not the province's old seat of government: under the Qing the viceroy of Zhili lived at Baoding, and the ancient prefecture on this stretch of the plain was Zhengding, a few miles to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city owes its existence to two railways that meet here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first is the Peking–Hankow line, carried south from Baoding on its way to the Yellow River; the second runs west to Taiyuan, the capital of Shanxi, and leaves the first at a station a few miles south of Zhengding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The concession for that western line went to the Russo-Chinese Bank under an agreement of 21 May 1898, before the Boxer rising, and the cost was then put at 25 million francs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When the route was surveyed the country proved to offer so many obstacles that the line would cost a great deal more, and fresh terms had to be settled with the Chinese government afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Construction began under the Peking–Hankow line's own engineers in 1903, on the metre gauge, and went slowly against the engineering difficulties of the hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Growth in people and in industry has left the modern city with a standing air-pollution problem that bears directly on its residents' health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aircraft flown through a haze that settled over it in November 2020 found the pollution dominated by fine particulates, with black carbon layered through the air above the streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its road emissions peak twice a day, between seven and nine in the morning and five and seven in the evening, and gather on the trunk roads through Qiaoxi, the central district that is the municipality's political, economic and financial heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Bing-Chun Liu, Arihant Binaykia, Pei-Chann Chang, Manoj Kumar Tiwari, and Cheng-Chin Tsao, “Urban Air Quality Forecasting Based on Multi-Dimensional Collaborative Support Vector Regression (SVR): A Case Study of Beijing-Tianjin-Shijiazhuang,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 7 (2017): e0179763, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5510805/. [Open access]","Xuesi Zhou, Xiaoshen Sun, Yang Yang, Xiaorui Zhang, Zhaochu Huang et al., “Aircraft Observations on a Continuous Haze Pollution Event in Shijiazhuang Area,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 10 (2023): 1066610, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2022.1066610. [Open access]","Percy Horace Kent, <i>Railway Enterprise in China: An Account of Its Origin and Development</i> (London: Edward Arnold, 1907), 92, 102, 169–70, https://archive.org/details/railwayenterpri00kent. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 66–67, 76, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Min-Na Gao, Yong-Xiang Hu, Guo-Fei Shang, and Wei Li, “Research on the Impact of Spatial and Temporal Changes in Ecological Product Value in Hebei Province Based on Land Use Changes,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 9571, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11926369/. [Open access]","Lili Ren, Xuliang Guo, Jiangling Wu, and Amit Kumar Singh, “Data Mining and Spatio-Temporal Characteristics of Urban Road Traffic Emissions: A Case Study in Shijiazhuang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 12 (2023): e0295664, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10718443/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Skyline_of_Shijiazhuang_2012.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Shijiazhuang_2012.jpg","title":"Shijiazhuang","desc":"Shijiazhuang, seen from a distance.","credit":"ping lin, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Shijiazhuang_2012.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Shijiazhuang."}},
{"id":"gc-010","num":10,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hubei","answerText":"Hubei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Heritage listing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hanyang works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1890s</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hubei"},"facts":[["Capital","Wuhan"],["Population","57.8M (2020)"],["Largest city","Wuhan"],["Area","185,900 km² (71,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"湖北","pinyin":"Húběi","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hubei</b> lies at the core of the middle reaches of the Yangtze, in central China, ringed by mountains on the east, west and north and low and flat in the middle where the basin opens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It borders Anhui to the east, Chongqing to the west, Shaanxi to the north-west, Jiangxi and Hunan to the south and Henan to the north, a position that earned it the name of the Nine Provinces' Thoroughfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Jing and Chu cultures grew up on that river network, and the province holds more immovable cultural heritage than all but six others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of the sites with provincial standing the largest class is historic buildings, among them the Golden Temple on Wudang Mountain and ancient Longzhong at Xiangyang, while the ancient sites include the ruins of Ji'nan City, the Chu capital, and of Qujialing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where all of it stands has been measured: it clusters most densely on Wuhan, while its geometric centre, dynasty by dynasty, falls around Jingshan and Jingmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the 1890s Zhang Zhidong, viceroy at Wuchang, argued against extending the railways along the coast and for great trunk lines through the interior, safely remote from the sea, beginning with one from Peking to Hankou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was transferred to Wuchang with orders to build it of Chinese material and under Chinese direction, found himself unable to do so, and established the Hanyang steel works to provide for the future instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The province is also where northern China gets much of its water: the central line of the South-to-North Water Diversion draws from the Danjiangkou Reservoir on the Han, the Yangtze's largest tributary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That reservoir supplies more than twenty cities, Beijing and Tianjin among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ecologically the province stands high in the east and the west and low in the middle, and it is on the Jianghan Plain between them that its network of corridors breaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Xiaojie Chen, Longyang Huang, and Changcheng Zhang, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Trade-offs/Synergies of Ecosystem Services in Hubei Province,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 35697, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12518542/. [Open access]","Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 3 (London: Longmans, Green, 1918), 85–86, https://archive.org/details/cu31924017552310. [Open access]","Wenyuan Zhang, Lihong Ma, Jiayu Xu, and Yujie Peng, “Spatial-Temporal Distribution Characteristics and Influencing Factors of Immovable Cultural Heritage in Hubei, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 24570, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12238651/. [Open access]","Di Long, Wenting Yang, Bridget R. Scanlon, Jianshi Zhao, Dagen Liu et al., “South-to-North Water Diversion Stabilizing Beijing's Groundwater Levels,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11, no. 1 (2020): 3665, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7374106/. [Open access]","Junfeng Zeng and Shushen Li, “Evaluating and Optimizing Ecological Networks in the Hubei Yangtze River Economic Belt Using an Importance-Sensitivity and Circuit-Graph Approach,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 43220, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12680631/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Wudang_Mountain_%2854131425234%29.jpg/1920px-Wudang_Mountain_%2854131425234%29.jpg","title":"the Wudang Mountains, Hubei","desc":"The Wudang Mountains.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wudang_Mountain_(54131425234).jpg","alt":"Temple roofs on a forested mountain ridge."}},
{"id":"gc-507","num":507,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Changsha","answerText":"Changsha","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tang kilns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">620 – 907 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taiping</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1852</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hunan","dot":"Changsha"},"facts":[["Province","Hunan"],["Population","10.0M (2020)"],["Rank in China","18th largest"],["Population growth","+42.7% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"長沙","hanzi":"长沙","pinyin":"Chángshā","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Changsha</b> is the capital of Hunan and stands on the right bank of the Xiang, in the lower reaches of the Dongting Lake system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has long been called a city of mountain and water, for its abundant surface water and for the basin it sits in with hills all around the built-up ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Tang the kilns here were a major centre of celadon production, and their opaque glazes are still studied for how the colour was got into them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The answer is a high-calcium glaze in which copper and iron ions and the separation of the melt into phases together decide what the eye sees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Taiping crossed into the province in 1852 and their boats went up the Xiang past the city, taking one town after another for supplies without setting up a government in any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The modern city has spread across the plain, its built-up ground growing nearly threefold between 2000 and 2023 and fastest between 2010 and 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Changsha is one of the ten hottest cities in the country, and its heat island is worst in August, the month with the highest temperatures and the least frequent rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its parks and waters push back against that: green patches cool the ground around them, and open water cools it further still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Measured across the county and the six urban districts, the city's ecological quality fell between 2003 and 2014 and then recovered part of the way by 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The worst of that loss was east of the Xiang, in the Xingma, Airport and Huangli groups, where the city has been growing outward fastest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Xinyi Qiu, Sung-Ho Kil, Hyun-Kil Jo, Chan Park, Wonkyong Song, and Yun Eui Choi, “Cooling Effect of Urban Blue and Green Spaces: A Case Study of Changsha, China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 3 (2023): 2613, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9916382/. [Open access]","Chan Lu, Lei Shi, Lihua Fu, Simian Liu, Jianqiao Li, and Zhenchun Mo, “Urban Ecological Environment Quality Evaluation and Territorial Spatial Planning Response: Application to Changsha, Central China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 4 (2023): 3753, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9961913/. [Open access]","Zuopeng Zhang, Zhe Li, and Zhirong Li, “Evaluating the Change and Trend of Construction Land in Changsha City Based GeoSOS-FLUS Model and Machine Learning Methods,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 9602, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11926109/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 131, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Lala Jia, Maolin Zhang, Yongqiang Yu, Zihan Li, and Yongbin Yu, “The Coloring Mechanism of Opaque Glazes from the Changsha Kiln of the Tang Dynasty,” <i>Materials</i> 17, no. 23 (2024): 5803, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11642496/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 245–48, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Night_view_of_Xiang_River_in_Changsha_2025032403.jpg/1920px-Night_view_of_Xiang_River_in_Changsha_2025032403.jpg","title":"Changsha","desc":"Changsha, seen from a distance.","credit":"Huangdan2060, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Night_view_of_Xiang_River_in_Changsha_2025032403.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Changsha."}},
{"id":"gc-011","num":11,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Guangxi","answerText":"Guangxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lingqu Canal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Qin, over 2,200 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taiping begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1844</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guangxi"},"facts":[["Capital","Nanning"],["Population","50.1M (2020)"],["Largest city","Nanning"],["Area","237,600 km² (91,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"廣西","hanzi":"广西","pinyin":"Guǎngxī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guangxi</b> is an autonomous region rather than a province, named for the Zhuang, and its country is a basin of mountainous hills falling away from the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It holds the second largest concentration of ethnic minorities in the country, settled in the pattern of large mixed communities and small concentrated ones that long contact produces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its nationally listed traditional villages gather around one main core at Guilin, with lesser ones at Hezhou, Liuzhou and Yulin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Nanling mountains along the northern border separate the Pearl River basin from the Yangtze, and under the Qin the Lingqu Canal was cut across them to join the headwaters of the Xiang to the Gui.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Water has run from one basin into the other ever since, and studies of freshwater fish treat the canal as a route by which populations have passed between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In May 1844 Hong Xiuquan, Feng Yunshan and two companions set out to visit a relative living near the Miao in Guangxi, keeping themselves on the road by peddling ink-stones and writing brushes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They preached and baptised as they went, and a kinsman who took a school in one of the districts stayed several years and baptised more than fifty converts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Out of that mission grew the Taiping rebellion, which within a decade held Nanjing and the towns along much of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No recruits ever came from Guangxi once the movement had left it, and when the last of its walled cities fell the Guangxi men among the prisoners were the only ones executed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the region's rural settlements sit today, and why they sit there, has been mapped across the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jiayi Yan, Jianyong Lin, Aihua Wang, Yadong Qie, Cong Hu et al., “Conserving Large Old Trees in Guangxi, South China: Diversity, Distribution and Conservation Strategies,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 16, no. 2 (2026): e73043, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12900623/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 238–43, 262–63, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Jianmei Tan, Jiayan Liu, Yi Zou, and Xiaoshu Wei, “Spatial Influencing Factors and Protection Strategies of Traditional Villages in Ethnic Areas: A Case Study of Guangxi,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 4 (2026): e0336147, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13098987/. [Open access]","Yun Chen, Guangmin Deng, Ziyu Le, and Cuizhang Fu, “Mountains, Lakes, and Ancient Drainage Networks Sculpt the Phylogeographic Architecture of the Stream Headwater Fish <i>Acrossocheilus kreyenbergii</i> in China,” <i>Genes</i> 16, no. 12 (2025): 1393, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12732399/. [Open access]","Wenjun Zheng, Wentao Cao, Guifang Li, Sijia Zhu, and Xianyan Zhang, “Spatial Pattern Characteristics and Factors for the Present Status of Rural Settlements in the Lijiang River Basin,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 5 (2023): 4124, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001701/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/87318-Li-River.jpg/1920px-87318-Li-River.jpg","title":"the Li River, Guangxi","desc":"The karst hills of the Li River.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:87318-Li-River.jpg","alt":"Steep limestone hills along a winding river."}},
{"id":"gc-508","num":508,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Hangzhou","answerText":"Hangzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Liangzhu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5300 – 4500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Song capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1127 – 1280 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Zhejiang","dot":"Hangzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Zhejiang"],["Population","11.9M (2020)"],["Rank in China","12th largest"],["Population growth","+37.2% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"杭州","pinyin":"Hángzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hangzhou</b> is the capital of Zhejiang and stands at the mouth of the Qiantang, at the southern edge of the Yangtze delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Liangzhu culture built its walled city in what is now the municipality, with enclosures, moats, dams and canals, and jade that carried religious meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rice cultivation was its economic base, and it is the late Neolithic of the whole delta seen at its most elaborate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Jin overran the north and the capital fell, the last emperor of the Northern Song fled first to Nanjing and then here; Nanjing was pillaged, but this city lay too far for the invaders and grew in size and strength into a famous capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That emperor, Gaozong, resigned in 1162 after a reign of thirty-six years and survived his abdication by twenty-four more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The city is known above all for West Lake, which sits higher than the river plain around it, so that apart from four small streams almost nothing feeds it naturally and its water has to be brought in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Keeping it clear has taken two thousand years of management and, lately, five things at once: preventing the lake silting into marsh, cutting the nutrients coming in, controlling those already in the sediment, bringing back the submerged plants and then holding the whole system steady.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Successful recoveries of this kind are rare anywhere, and rarer still in shallow subtropical lakes, where large ones such as Taihu, Chao and Dianchi have not managed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lake went onto the World Heritage List in 2011, the first Chinese entry in the lake category.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Beijing-Hangzhou Grand Canal ends here, and the greenway now laid along its banks is studied for how restorative the people walking it find it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Guoliang Bai, Yadong Tang, Yunli Liu, Zisen Liu, Lingwei Kong et al., “Sustainable Innovation for the Clear Waters of Hangzhou West Lake, China: Achievements and Insights,” <i>The Innovation</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 101148, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12957564/. [Open access]","Yue Wang, Mengjie Yang, Yijun Lu, Qiaoyi He, Youli Zhang et al., “Bridging Landscape and Perceived Restorativeness: An Empirical Study of Greenways along the Grand Canal, Hangzhou,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 17 (2026): 1742799, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12916418/. [Open access]","Junmei Sawada, Kazuhiro Uzawa, Minoru Yoneda, Yasuo Hagihara, Yu Itahashi et al., “Worked Human Bones and the Rise of Urban Society in the Neolithic Liangzhu Culture, East Asia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 31441, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15673-7. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 40–41, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 139–41, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Hangzhou_Skyline_on_West_Lake.jpg/1920px-Hangzhou_Skyline_on_West_Lake.jpg","title":"Hangzhou","desc":"Hangzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"Yinweichen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hangzhou_Skyline_on_West_Lake.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hangzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-012","num":12,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Yunnan","answerText":"Yunnan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Baiyangcun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2650 – 1690 BCE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Yunnan"},"facts":[["Capital","Kunming"],["Population","47.2M (2020)"],["Largest city","Kunming"],["Area","394,100 km² (152,200 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"雲南","hanzi":"云南","pinyin":"Yúnnán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Yunnan</b> is a plateau province in the south-western corner of China, its ground declining in steps from the north-west and mountainous over the great majority of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nearly half of China's animal and plant species are found within it, many of them ancient in origin, which gives the province both a high rate of endemism and a fragile ecology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It stands at the crossroads of temperate China, north-eastern India and tropical mainland South-East Asia, which is why its farming history matters well beyond its own borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rice and millet spread into it from central China in the 3rd millennium BCE, wheat and barley followed in the 2nd, and by the late 1st millennium the land was being worked in two seasons rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Baiyangcun, on the middle Jinsha, has given up rice, foxtail and broomcorn millet and wild soybean together, though whether its farmers carried rice southwards is doubted, early rice here having been grown differently from the way it was in mainland South-East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Panthays, a Muslim people long settled in the west of the province, date their origin from the Tang and were generally left unmolested so long as they obeyed the laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Mongol rule their numbers grew until they began to take part in the government, and they had done so ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During the Taiping rebellion the emperor's authority in Yunnan was practically in abeyance, and over the western half of the province it was superseded altogether by a Panthay revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Putting that rising down is one of the three things the regency of the 1860s and 1870s is credited with, beside quelling the Taiping and opening diplomatic relations with the treaty powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plateau cluster of cities around Kunming, Qujing and Yuxi still holds most of the province's people and makes most of its economy, and what its energy costs is now tracked city by city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Wenqi Tang, Jianqiang Li, Huiying Yan, Lianling Cha, Yuan Yang, and Linling Wang, “Multi-Scenario Simulation of Land Use Change Based on the Objectives of Ecological Protection and Food Security in Yunnan Province,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 37506, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12559345/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 51, 367–68, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Rita Dal Martello, “The Origins of Multi-Cropping Agriculture in Southwestern China: Archaeobotanical Insights from Third to First Millennium B.C. Yunnan,” <i>Asian Archaeology</i> 6, no. 1 (2022): 65–85, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9373101/. [Open access]","Feiling Yang, Jinming Hu, and Ruidong Wu, “Combining Endangered Plants and Animals as Surrogates to Identify Priority Conservation Areas in Yunnan, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6 (2016): 30753, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4990906/. [Open access]","Ning You, Libo Han, Shaomei Li, Jingzhen Ma, and Qing Xu, “Spatio-Temporal Dynamics of Per Capita Energy Consumption Carbon Emissions in the Central Yunnan Urban Agglomeration,” <i>Carbon Balance and Management</i> 21, no. 1 (2026): 80, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13224723/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Terrace_field_yunnan_china_denoised.jpg/1920px-Terrace_field_yunnan_china_denoised.jpg","title":"the Hani rice terraces, Yunnan","desc":"The Hani rice terraces at Honghe.","credit":"Jialiang Gao , www.peace-on-earth.org, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terrace_field_yunnan_china_denoised.jpg","alt":"Flooded rice terraces stepping down a hillside."}},
{"id":"gc-509","num":509,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Hefei","answerText":"Hefei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Urban growth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2000 – 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Anhui","dot":"Hefei"},"facts":[["Province","Anhui"],["Population","9.37M (2020)"],["Rank in China","26th largest"],["Population growth","+25.7% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"合肥","pinyin":"Héféi","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hefei</b> is the capital of Anhui and stands in the middle of the province, on hills and low mounds under a humid subtropical climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jianghuai watershed runs east to west across the whole of its territory, so the city sits astride the divide between the basin of the Yangtze and that of the Huai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has not always been the seat of anything so large: under the Qing this was the prefectural town of Luzhou, a little north of Chao Lake, while the province itself was governed from Anqing on the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chao Lake now lies inside the municipality, one of the largest bodies of fresh water in the country, fed by more than thirty rivers, and the city drinks from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its administrative area takes in four urban districts, the county-level city of Chaohu and four counties, and the four districts together are its economic and administrative core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The modern city has grown at a pace few others have matched, the share of its people living in towns rising steeply since 2000 and its built-up ground spreading out across the low hills to meet the lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is now reckoned one of the four principal cities of science and education in the country, a sub-central city of the Yangtze delta agglomeration, and one of the ten largest hubs on the high-speed rail network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within its own metropolitan area of seven cities it has become the core of the industrial carbon-emission network, carrying far more influence over the rest than the average.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Industrial emissions across those seven rose unevenly between 2010 and 2022, and the correlations between them tightened as the cities grew into one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The largest emitter of them all is not Hefei but Huainan, on the Huai to the north, which stands far above the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Zhiqiang Yao, Shi Chen, and Yinlan Huang, “Exploring the Threshold of Human Activity Impact on Urban Ecosystem Service Value: A Case Study of Hefei,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 24118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12230127/. [Open access]","Ran Yu, Hongsheng Cheng, Yun Ye, Qin Wang, Shuping Fan et al., “Optimization of the Territorial Spatial Patterns Based on MOP and PLUS Models: A Case Study from Hefei,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 3 (2023): 1804, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9914123/. [Open access]","Dawei Dai, Yuewen Liu, Shuhang Zhao, and Yue Chen, “Study on Spatial Correlation and Driving Factors of Industrial Carbon Emission in the Hefei Metropolitan Area,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 3445, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11772829/. [Open access]","Juan Huang, Jinyan Zhan, Haiming Yan, Feng Wu, and Xiangzheng Deng, “Evaluation of the Impacts of Land Use on Water Quality: A Case Study in the Chaohu Lake Basin,” <i>The Scientific World Journal</i> 2013 (2013): 329187, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3736410/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 146, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Skylines_of_Hefei_at_Tianehu.jpg/1920px-Skylines_of_Hefei_at_Tianehu.jpg","title":"Hefei","desc":"Hefei, seen from a distance.","credit":"钉钉, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skylines_of_Hefei_at_Tianehu.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hefei."}},
{"id":"gc-013","num":13,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jiangxi","answerText":"Jiangxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xianrendong</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 19,780 – 8,575 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassies passed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1793 and 1816</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jiangxi"},"facts":[["Capital","Nanchang"],["Population","45.2M (2020)"],["Largest city","Ganzhou"],["Area","166,900 km² (64,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"江西","pinyin":"Jiāngxī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jiangxi</b> lies along the southern bank of the middle and lower Yangtze, mountainous and hilly over most of its ground, flanked by the Wuyi range to the east, the Nanling to the south and the Luoxiao and Jiuling to the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poyang Lake, the largest body of fresh water in the country, sits in the north of the province and is joined to the Yangtze through a single outlet at Hukou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Five river systems feed it, the Gan, the Xin, the Fu, the Xiu and the Rao, and with their tributaries they drain almost the whole of Jiangxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two caves in the north of the province, Xianrendong and Diaotonghuan, have yielded some of the earliest pottery known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The stone toolkits found in them combine pebble tools with small flaked pieces of quartz, flint and crystal, and lie among quantities of land animal, fish and bird remains together with rice phytoliths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From that evidence the earliest occupants have been read as collectors of wild rice and their successors as cultivators of it, which would place the caves close to the beginning of rice farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> West of Poyang lie the vale and grotto of the White Deer, where Zhu Xi, the great commentator of Confucius, lived and taught, and which is a celebrated place of pilgrimage for Chinese scholars still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across the south of the province runs the Meiling pass, named for the plum trees around it and long known as the Ambassadors' Route, the way by which embassies travelled between Canton and Peking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lord Macartney returned to Canton over that pass in 1793 and Lord Amherst in 1816.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The kilns of Jingdezhen in the north-east became one of the most important centres of porcelain in Chinese history, and the glazes of their Yuan blue-and-white are analysed still for how the cobalt took its colour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Xu Song, Xi Chen, Bofu Zheng, and Wei Wan, “Spatiotemporal Patterns and Driving Mechanisms of Terrestrial Carbon Storage in Jiangxi Province,” <i>Carbon Balance and Management</i> 21, no. 1 (2026): 60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13001212/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 140–45, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Tracey L-D Lu, “Early Pottery in South China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 49, no. 1 (2010), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29097. [Open access]","Chi Zhang and Hsiao-chun Hung, “The Neolithic of Southern China—Origin, Development, and Dispersal,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 47, no. 2 (2008): 299–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17291. [Open access]","Jun Sun, Qijiang Li, Xiaoyan Xia, Min Tang, Yan Liang, and Linxin Ouyang, “Effect of Luanbai Glaze on the Coloration of Cobalt Pigment in Yuan Dynasty Jingdezhen Porcelains: An Experimental Study,” <i>Materials</i> 19, no. 11 (2026): 2254, https://doi.org/10.3390/ma19112254. [Open access]","Chun Fu, Libin Xiang, Yezhong Liu, and Tianming Zhou, “Response of Nitrogen Emissions to Land Use Changes and Driving Forces Analysis in Jiangxi Province under Multiple Scenarios,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 29599, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-12965-w. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/16137-Lushan_%2831834519847%29.jpg/1920px-16137-Lushan_%2831834519847%29.jpg","title":"Mount Lu, Jiangxi","desc":"Mount Lu.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16137-Lushan_(31834519847).jpg","alt":"Wooded mountain slopes above a valley."}},
{"id":"gc-510","num":510,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Wuhan","answerText":"Wuhan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hankou a port</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Towns merged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hubei","dot":"Wuhan"},"facts":[["Province","Hubei"],["Population","12.4M (2020)"],["Rank in China","10th largest"],["Population growth","+27.2% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"武漢","hanzi":"武汉","pinyin":"Wǔhàn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wuhan</b> is the capital of Hubei and stands at the confluence of the Yangtze and the Han, the core city of the urban cluster of the middle Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began as three separate towns at that meeting of the rivers — Hankou, Wuchang and Hanyang — and takes its name and its shape from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hankou lived on Yangtze commerce, and after it was opened as a port in 1861 it grew into a trading centre whose main streets ran parallel to the water while the cross-streets ended at the wharves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wuchang, on the southern bank opposite the other two, is the seat of the provincial government, and the lakes ringing it to the north, east and south pushed its roads outward into a radial pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was from Wuchang in the 1890s that Zhang Zhidong pressed for trunk railways through the interior rather than along the coast, and, unable to build one of Chinese material as he had undertaken, set up the Hanyang steel works instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The three municipalities were merged in 1927 to form the modern city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first bridge ever thrown across the Yangtze opened here in 1957, and the city has gone on adding them since, across the Han as well as the greater river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is nicknamed the City of a Hundred Lakes, and its parks and waters work as cold islands against the summer heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The old residential quarters of Wuchang are now assessed street by street for the quality of the public space in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its stretch of the river was long counted a gap in the range of the critically endangered Yangtze finless porpoise, but acoustic monitoring between 2020 and 2022 picked the animal up on nearly half the days listened to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Jufang Song, Yongxuan Qiao, and Yihan Liu, “Evaluation of the Cold Island Effect of the Urban Parks in the Main Urban Area of Wuhan from the Perspective of Supply and Demand,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 13 (2025): 1523210, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2025.1523210. [Open access]","Ran Peng, Keyuan Ding, Haixu Guo, Xueliang Liu, Yehao Liu et al., “The Impact of Rivers and Lakes on Urban Transportation Expansion: A Case Study of the Century-Long Evolution of the Road Network in Wuhan, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0298678, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0298678. [Open access]","Zhi-Tao Wang, Peng-Xiang Duan, Tomonari Akamatsu, Ke-Xiong Wang, and Ding Wang, “Increased Yangtze Finless Porpoise Presence in Urban Wuhan Waters of the Yangtze River during Fishing Closures,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 4 (2024): e11247, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.11247. [Open access]","Zixuan Guo, Hong Xu, Qiushuang Lin, and Xuanhe Li, “Deep Learning Assessment of Street Spatial Quality in Old Residential Communities of Wuchang, Wuhan, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 45176, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-28936-0. [Open access]","Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 3 (London: Longmans, Green, 1918), 85–86, https://archive.org/details/cu31924017552310. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Skyline_of_Wuhan-2.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Wuhan-2.jpg","title":"Wuhan","desc":"Wuhan, seen from a distance.","credit":"Majorantarktis, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Wuhan-2.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Wuhan."}},
{"id":"gc-014","num":14,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Liaoning","answerText":"Liaoning","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hongshan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4500 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manchu manifesto</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1618</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Liaoning"},"facts":[["Capital","Shenyang"],["Population","42.6M (2020)"],["Largest city","Shenyang"],["Area","148,600 km² (57,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"遼寧","hanzi":"辽宁","pinyin":"Liáoníng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Liaoning</b> is a province of north-eastern China facing the Bohai and the Yellow Sea, its ground descending southward from uplands in the east and west to the lowlands between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the one province of the north-east with both a seaboard and a land frontier, and its jurisdiction runs out over the water as well as the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Long before any of that the valleys of the Liao and the Daling held the Hongshan culture, whose core zone at Niuheliang, Dongshanzui and Hutougou holds the largest ceremonial platforms and nearly every known burial furnished with elaborate jade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Manchu state rose here: in 1618 the chieftain Tianming published a manifesto against the house of Ming, announcing to Heaven the seven things he was bound to revenge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were petty oppressions on people crossing the frontiers, help given to his enemies, the breaking of an oath and treaty of peace, and the killing of his envoys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He had already taken the title of emperor, and he overran the north-eastern provinces and dealt hard with the people of Liaodong before dying in 1627, leaving his revenge and his army to his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mukden, which is Shenyang, was the capital of the Manchu country of Shengjing, and the dynasty kept its pride in the place long after it had taken Peking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the modern century the province became one of the anchors of the country's heavy industrial base, the state building the Anshan steelworks here as it built the First Automobile Works in Jilin and the Daqing oilfield in Heilongjiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its Bohai ports drew metallurgy, military manufacturing and trade infrastructure to it, and among the north-eastern provinces it is the one that came to specialise in metal mining and smelting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The restructuring of state enterprises after the reform era closed a great many works and left a distinct abandoned-factory heritage behind it, and the Tiexi district of Shenyang has since been remade as an industrial heritage corridor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Boqian Jiang, Dan Wang, Qiuju Ye, Jiayi Luo, Bingqian Jin, and Haibo Yin, “Construction of Eco-Quality Dual-Suitable Distribution Areas in Liaoning Province,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 16 (2025): 1591921, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12307492/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 44–45, 455, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Lina Ke, Shusheng Yin, Shuting Wang, and Quanming Wang, “Spatiotemporal Changes Caused by the Intensive Use of Sea Areas in the Liaoning Coastal Economic Zone of China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0242977, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0242977. [Open access]","Christian E. Peterson et al., “Hongshan Chiefly Communities in Neolithic Northeastern China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 13 (2010): 5756–61, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2851873/. [Open access]","Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Mukden_Palace_drone_view_5_%28cropped_%26_rotated%29.jpg/1920px-Mukden_Palace_drone_view_5_%28cropped_%26_rotated%29.jpg","title":"Mukden Palace, Liaoning","desc":"The Mukden Palace at Shenyang.","credit":"Techyan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mukden_Palace_drone_view_5_(cropped_%26_rotated).jpg","alt":"The tiled roofs of a Manchu imperial palace."}},
{"id":"gc-511","num":511,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Nanning","answerText":"Nanning","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sponge City</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 2013</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Built land grew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1990 – 2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guangxi","dot":"Nanning"},"facts":[["Region","Guangxi"],["Population","8.74M (2020)"],["Rank in China","36th largest"],["Population growth","+31.3% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"南寧","hanzi":"南宁","pinyin":"Nánníng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nanning</b> is the capital of the Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region and stands in the south-central part of it, in a basin of alluvial plain ringed by hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Yong, a major tributary of the Pearl River system, runs east to west across the city and spreads a branching drainage network that has shaped how the built-up area grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That river is why the place mattered before it was a capital: under the Qing, Nanning Fu stood at the head of practicable steam navigation up the Yu, with landing places above and below the rapid downstream that made the passage possible all year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Just above the town the Zuo comes in, formed by two rivers that rise in Tonkin and meet at Lungchow near the frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rain falls here in half the year rather than through it, which in a basin makes the city unusually liable to flooding, and it is one of the pilots of the national Sponge City programme begun in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The ground beneath it is a red clay that is half clay particles by weight, its minerals quartz, kaolinite, illite, calcite and hematite, the last of these the iron oxide that cements the soil and gives it its colour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That clay swells and shrinks as it wets and dries, which is a standing problem for anyone building a road across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 1990 and 2020 the built-up land increased more than fivefold, spreading east and south-east onto the flat ground, and almost nine tenths of what it took was farmland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city's territory is divided into fifteen districts and counties, and it remains one of the major rice-growing areas of southern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its urban forests are studied for what they do to the carbon held in the soil beneath them, which is one of the few things a city can add to rather than spend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Yun-chuan Yang, Xiao-han Huang, Jiao-yin Wei, Li-ping Liao, Guo-qiang Feng et al., “Urban Expansion Drivers and Land Use Scenario Simulation in Nanning, China, Using the PLUS Model,” <i>Science Progress</i> 109, no. 1 (2026): 00368504261417161, https://doi.org/10.1177/00368504261417161. [Open access]","Wei Zhou, Zhao Wei, Ning Feng, Mi Luo, Bingpeng Qu, Qiren Luo, and Jianbing Zhang, “Urban Forest Plant Diversity Affects Soil Organic Carbon by Regulating Functional Genes in Nanning,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 3 (2026): 114856, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.114856. [Open access]","Sen Deng, Hongri Zhang, Jian Wei, Kaike Huang, Yanxin Song et al., “Hydrostructural and Dynamic Characteristics of Compacted Nanning Red Clay Considering Wetting-Drying Impacts,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 11483, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41777-9. [Open access]","Peng Li, Siyan Li, Jing Zhang, Liting Cen, Qi Li, and Hui Li, “Dietary Risk Analysis of Heavy Metal and Metalloid Pollution in Rice: Case Study of Nanning City in China,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 14 (2026): 1781196, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1781196. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 171–72, 196–98, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Nanning_skyline_2008.JPG/1920px-Nanning_skyline_2008.JPG","title":"Nanning","desc":"Nanning, seen from a distance.","credit":"Saigon punkid ( talk ), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanning_skyline_2008.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Nanning."}},
{"id":"gc-015","num":15,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Fujian","answerText":"Fujian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Min kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">907 – 960 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wuyishan park</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Fujian"},"facts":[["Capital","Fuzhou"],["Population","41.5M (2020)"],["Largest city","Quanzhou"],["Area","124,000 km² (47,900 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"福建","pinyin":"Fújiàn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Fujian</b> is a province on the south-eastern coast of China, facing Taiwan across the strait, and mountainous and long-shored at once: eight parts mountain, one part water and one part field, as the old description of it has it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its forests cover a higher proportion of the land than those of any other province, and it carries several hundred nationally listed traditional villages, thickest in the hill country away from the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> During the Five Dynasties, when rival houses fought over the valley of the Yellow River, Fujian was held by the King of Min.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Song and the Yuan, Quanzhou was one of the greatest maritime trading ports of China, and it is a World Heritage city now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ibn Battuta landed there — at Zayton, as the port was then known — and his accounts of it and of Canton and Hangzhou are among the fullest that survive of the trade of the southern coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That trade brought religions in with the cargoes, and the local Minyue traditions took in Buddhism, Islam, Christianity and Taoism over the centuries that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inland the Hakka built the great earth houses that draw visitors today, and what those visitors take to be authentic about them has itself become a subject of study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Wuyi Mountains hold what is reckoned the largest intact stretch of primary mid-subtropical forest anywhere at that latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Wuyishan became one of the first five national parks in China when the system was set up in 2021, and it guards the headwaters of the Min, the province's principal river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nearly everything the province earns is still earned within sight of the sea, its six coastal cities making the great bulk of what it makes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Mengjie Song, Xiaoxi Huang, Xueqiong Wei, Xuwei Tang, Zhixiang Rao et al., “Spatial Patterns and the Associated Factors for Breast Cancer Incidence in Fujian Province,” <i>BMC Women's Health</i> 23, no. 1 (2023): 247, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10170828/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39, 70–71, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Congzhi Li, Ying Pan, Weilun Yin, and Siren Lan, “Spatial Distribution and Influencing Factors of Rural Cultural Ecosystem Services: A Case Study of Fujian, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 10510, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-95600-y. [Open access]","Wenjun Chen and Chaoxiang Wen, “Spatial Benefit Assessment and Marine Climate Response of Coastal Zone in Fujian Province under Cross-System Interaction,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 11 (2024): e0306988, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0306988. [Open access]","Yong Zhu, Guoqiang Han, Ji'an Zhuang, and Mo Wang, “Authenticity and Place Attachment as Dual Pathways for Sustainable Tourism in the Historic Quanzhou West Street,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 44980, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-28662-7. [Open access]","Mingxia Zhang, Naifeng Lin, Guangyong You, Yan Wang, Lei Wang et al., “Variations and Influencing Factors of Vegetation Net Primary Productivity over 31 Years in Wuyishan National Park,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 29002, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-71812-6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Three_round%2C_one_oval%2C_one_square_%2816831811084%29.jpg/1920px-Three_round%2C_one_oval%2C_one_square_%2816831811084%29.jpg","title":"the Fujian tulou","desc":"A Fujian tulou.","credit":"Scott Edmunds, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_round,_one_oval,_one_square_(16831811084).jpg","alt":"A large circular earthen communal dwelling."}},
{"id":"gc-512","num":512,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Kunming","answerText":"Kunming","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lake divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1996</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grade lifted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2016</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Yunnan","dot":"Kunming"},"facts":[["Province","Yunnan"],["Population","8.46M (2020)"],["Rank in China","37th largest"],["Population growth","+31.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"昆明","pinyin":"Kūnmíng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Kunming</b> is the capital of Yunnan and stands in the north-central part of it on the plateau, with mountains on three sides and Dianchi Lake to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Qing it was Yunnan Fu, often called Yunnan Sheng, set among the table-lands towards the north-east of the province, with thirteen other prefectures ranged around it by distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its altitude at a low latitude gives it a subtropical highland climate fed by warm moist air off the Indian Ocean, and the smallest swing between its hottest and coldest months of any city in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dianchi is the largest body of fresh water in south-western China, and remarkably shallow for its size, so that what enters it is not quickly diluted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A causeway built in 1996 divides it into the small Caohai arm and the much larger outer lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because the lake sits at the lowest point of the city and has no other outlet for what the city discharges into it, its water fell to the worst national grade in 1986 and was not lifted out of that grade until 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cyanobacteria still bloom there from May to November, and the basin that drains into it takes in a wide stretch of central Yunnan across several administrative units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The microbial communities of the water are now studied for how they assemble and what drives them from one habitat within the lake to the next, which is a way of asking what the recovery has actually changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has grown very fast, its built-up ground spreading across the basin between 2012 and 2021 and running up against the lake it drains into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Migratory gulls have wintered in its parks and along that lake shore since the 1980s, and their gut microbiomes have been sequenced for what the birds carry with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ting Chen, Zhibin Ren, Yao Fu, and Chang Liu, “Correlation Analysis of Landscape Patterns and Urban Thermal Environment in Kunming Based on a Panel Data Model,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 27375, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-78118-7. [Open access]","Jun Chen, Zhizhong Zhang, Bowen Wang, Jiaojiao Yang, Guangxiu Cao et al., “Diversity, Assembly, and Habitat-Driven Dynamics of Microbial Communities in Eutrophic Dianchi Lake, Southwest China,” <i>Microorganisms</i> 14, no. 3 (2026): 554, https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms14030554. [Open access]","Wenjuan Pei, Yilong Peng, Kai Fan, Jiansheng Zhang, Yunchun Chen et al., “The Impact of Land Use on Eco-Environment in the Dianchi Basin,” <i>Heliyon</i> 10, no. 11 (2024): e32085, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e32085. [Open access]","Jun Zhang, Xue Zhang, Xueping Tan, and Xiaodie Yuan, “A New Approach to Monitoring Urban Built-Up Areas in Kunming and Yuxi from 2012 to 2021,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 19 (2022): 12198, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph191912198. [Open access]","Feng Liao, Jing Qian, Ruian Yang, Wenpeng Gu, Rufang Li et al., “Metagenomics of Gut Microbiome for Migratory Seagulls in Kunming City Revealed the Potential Public Risk to Human Health,” <i>BMC Genomics</i> 24, no. 1 (2023): 269, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12864-023-09379-1. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 208–10, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Kunming.%E6%98%86%E6%98%8E.skyline.sunset.2013.jpg/1920px-Kunming.%E6%98%86%E6%98%8E.skyline.sunset.2013.jpg","title":"Kunming","desc":"Kunming, seen from a distance.","credit":"FRED, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kunming.%E6%98%86%E6%98%8E.skyline.sunset.2013.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kunming."}},
{"id":"gc-016","num":16,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Shaanxi","answerText":"Shaanxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhaoxiang wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 272 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tang Chang'an</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 907 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shaanxi"},"facts":[["Capital","Xi'an"],["Population","39.5M (2020)"],["Largest city","Xi'an"],["Area","205,600 km² (79,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"陝西","hanzi":"陕西","pinyin":"Shǎnxī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shaanxi</b> lies along the middle Yellow River, and the Guanzhong plain at its centre carried Chang’an, capital of the Tang and the eastern end of the Silk Road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long before that the state of Qin rose on the same ground and walled its northern frontier, and 347 watchtowers of the wall built under King Zhaoxiang still stand, rammed out of the soil beneath them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They run through the north of the province between desert and loess gully, close enough to the Qin capital at Xianyang to be its first line of defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Going the other way meant crossing the Qinling, which the Qin–Shu road did by a family of routes — Baoxie, Ziwu, Chencang, Tangluo and Wuguan — at their height under the Sui and the Tang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nearly six hundred heritage sites are strung along it, thickest at Xi’an, Hanzhong and Ankang and thinning in the high country to a scatter of military passes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Genomes from Tang burials at the capital are mostly indistinguishable from the Neolithic farmers of the same valley, though several carry Western Eurasian ancestry, so the road shows in the graves as well as in the records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the twentieth century the province’s weight moved north to Yan’an, whose revolution sites at Yangjialing, Zaoyuan and Wangjiaping, with the seat of the Shaanxi–Gansu–Ningxia border region government, lead a route of memorials running its length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The north is loess country, and its villages live in yaodong, cave dwellings cut into hillsides or sunk as courtyards below the fields, which the firm soil allows and which the surrounding earth keeps at an even temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Across the middle the Qinling, taken with the Huai River, marks the line conventionally drawn between northern and southern China, and it works as a real barrier, plant populations on either side of it being genetically distinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its forests hold the densest wild giant panda population in the country, nearly a fifth of all the wild pandas there are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Minglei Lv et al., “Ancient Genomes from the Tang Dynasty Capital Reveal the Genetic Legacy of Trans-Eurasian Communication at the Eastern End of Silk Road,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 22 (2024): 267, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11577736/. [Open access]","Kankan Li, Ziqiang Huang, Yinghui Pang, Sijie Chen, Yijing Fan et al., “Cultural Heritage of the Qin-Shu Ancient Road in Shaanxi: Spatial Distribution Characteristics and Influencing Factors,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0331676, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0331676. [Open access]","Xingyi Li, Yangyang Tong, Xiaoning Ma, Jiachen Chang, and Binyan He, “The Building Concept of Border Defence Facilities of Qin: Watchtowers along the King of Zhaoxiang Great Wall in Shaanxi Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329298, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0329298. [Open access]","Yuxin Feng, Yunxia Tian, and Xiaoyu Lv, “The Network Characteristics of Classic Red Tourist Attractions in Shaanxi Province, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0299286, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0299286. [Open access]","Runze Liu, Xiang Wang, Lin Yuan, Yunting Gao, Yan Zhang et al., “Analyzing the Ecologicality and Functionality of Kiln Architecture in China through KH Coder Data Mining Algorithm and Hierarchical Event Coding,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 16189, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-97659-z. [Open access]","Chunxue Jiang, Tian Shi, Zhongmei Mo, and Cai Zhao, “Across a Phylogeographic Break in the Qinling Mountains-Huaihe River Line,” <i>BMC Ecology and Evolution</i> 24, no. 1 (2024): 107, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12862-024-02297-0. [Open access]","Chun Yang, Wanyu Wen, Yuhang Wang, Zhijiao Yang, Changcai Li et al., “Do Giant Pandas Prefer Steeper Habitats? A Case Study on Panda Spatial Utilization in the Qinling Mountains, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 3 (2025): e71074, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.71074. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/51714-Terracota-Army.jpg/1920px-51714-Terracota-Army.jpg","title":"the Terracotta Army, Shaanxi","desc":"The Terracotta Army near Xi'an.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:51714-Terracota-Army.jpg","alt":"Ranks of life-size pottery soldiers in an excavation pit."}},
{"id":"gc-513","num":513,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Nanchang","answerText":"Nanchang","answerDate":"","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jiangxi","dot":"Nanchang"},"facts":[["Province","Jiangxi"],["Population","6.26M (2020)"],["Rank in China","60th largest"],["Population growth","+24.0% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"南昌","pinyin":"Nánchāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nanchang</b> is the capital of Jiangxi and stands in the north-centre of the province, where the Gan runs north towards Poyang Lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Gan is the largest of the five river systems feeding that lake, and its basin covers almost the whole province, so the city sits near the gathering point of nearly all of Jiangxi's water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the flood season small steamers could once come up the Gan as far as this city, junks of middling tonnage as far as Ganzhou, and small craft as far as Nan'an in the far south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Above Ji'an the river runs through a set of rapids called the eighteen, which is much of what kept the larger boats below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Poyang itself is deep as well as broad, and rises nearly thirty feet above its ordinary level when the season turns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The city is the core of the urban cluster of the middle Yangtze and the political, economic, cultural and transport hub of the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is made up of the Donghu, Xihu, Qingyunpu, Qingshan Lake and Honggutan districts together with Xinjian and the outlying counties of Nanchang, Anyi and Jinxian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its built-up area is read in rings, the First and Second Ring Roads marking off a core, an inner suburban belt and an outer one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How strong the monsoon is varies a great deal from one year to the next, so the city meets heat waves, drought and cold spells in turn rather than a steady season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its parks are now studied for how evenly their cooling reaches the people living around them, and how safe its streets feel has been mapped from several kinds of data at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Li Wang, Liang Lu, Zhibin Tu, Liping Qiu, Xiaoqin Tong et al., “Spatiotemporal Aggregation and Population Distribution Characteristics of HIV/AIDS in Nanchang City,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 2 (2026): e0342375, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0342375. [Open access]","Youqiang Zhao, Liu Pinyi, Gong Peng, and Zhang Jian Ping, “Optimizing Spatial Equity of Urban Park Cooling Services: Integrating Landscape Metrics with K-Means and PSO Algorithms,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 3 (2026): e0344026, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0344026. [Open access]","Chun Fu, Libin Xiang, Yezhong Liu, and Tianming Zhou, “Response of Nitrogen Emissions to Land Use Changes and Driving Forces Analysis in Jiangxi Province under Multiple Scenarios,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 29599, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-12965-w. [Open access]","Linduo Yuan, Yuze Dan, Yi Leng, and Jiaxin Zhang, “Influencing Factors of Urban Safety Perception Based on the Combination of Multi-Source Data and Machine Learning,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 3972, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-34110-3. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 142, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Nanchang_Skyline.jpg/1920px-Nanchang_Skyline.jpg","title":"Nanchang","desc":"Nanchang, seen from a distance.","credit":"Akira CA, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanchang_Skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Nanchang."}},
{"id":"gc-017","num":17,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Guizhou","answerText":"Guizhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Guizhou province</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1413</span><span class=\"dt-k\">FAST first light</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2016</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guizhou"},"facts":[["Capital","Guiyang"],["Population","38.6M (2020)"],["Largest city","Bijie"],["Area","176,200 km² (68,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"貴州","hanzi":"贵州","pinyin":"Guìzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guizhou</b> is an inland province of south-western China on the Yunnan–Guizhou Plateau, made up of plateau mountain, hill and basin, and the only province in the country with no plains at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the Ming its mountain peoples were only loosely joined to the Central Plains, held under Tusi chieftains whom the terrain and the poor roads left largely to themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yuan drove official post roads through the country, and around 1413 the Ming made Guizhou a province and began replacing those chieftains with officials the central government sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlers followed from the Central Plains with their farming and their cattle, until the wars at the end of the dynasty emptied much of the new farmland again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Qing the Kangxi emperor froze the corvée tax and maize, potato and sweet potato arrived, so people and cropland grew together — until poppy took the best fields and cultivation climbed the slopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Fifty ethnic groups live in the province, forty-nine of them minorities, and the Miao, Bouyei, Dong and Shui are each more numerous here than in any other province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its 757 villages on the national register of traditional Chinese villages are second only to one other province’s, and they cluster in the south-east, at the heart of what was once called the Miaojiang Corridor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Congjiang County, in the Qiandongnan Miao and Dong Autonomous Prefecture, Zhuang villages still brew WeiJiu from glutinous rice, mountain spring water and an ancestral koji, in five stages ending in sealing and aging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Karst runs through most of the province, and working thin soil on limestone strips the ground back to bare rock, which makes rocky desertification the standing threat to its ecosystems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same rock has been an asset as well: a natural karst basin here, quiet at radio wavelengths, holds the largest and most sensitive filled-aperture radio telescope in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Qiuju Mao, Liang Xie, and Lai Fan, “Spatial Characteristics and Determinants of Traditional Village Distribution in Guizhou Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 5 (2025): e0324275, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0324275. [Open access]","Yetong Li, Qihua Ke, and Zhuodong Zhang, “Millennial Evolution of a Karst Socio-Ecological System: A Case Study of Guizhou Province, Southwest China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 22 (2022): 15151, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192215151. [Open access]","Lei Shao, Shucai Li, Long Yang, Shenbing Wei, Gang Shen et al., “Analysis of Microbial Structure and Function in Fermented Grains during the Fermentation Process of Congjiang WeiJiu Based on High-Throughput Sequencing,” <i>PeerJ</i> 14 (2026): e21180, https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.21180. [Open access]","Chuncan Meng, Yingqing Cen, and Xu Xue, “Response of Vegetation Greenness to Extreme Droughts and Possible Mechanisms in Guizhou Province, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): e72945, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.72945. [Open access]","Beibei Zhang and Zhongfa Zhou, “The Spatiotemporal Evolution of Ecosystem Health and Its Driving Factors in Karst Rocky Desertification Areas: A Case Study of Guizhou Province, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 5 (2025): e71374, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.71374. [Open access]","Weijie Zhao, “China’s ‘Sky Eye’: Exploring the Universe by Measuring Faint Cosmic Radio Signals,” <i>National Science Review</i> 10, no. 7 (2023): nwad003, https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwad003. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/%E9%BB%84%E6%9E%9C%E6%A0%91%E7%80%91%E5%B8%83_2026-07-31_01.jpg/1920px-%E9%BB%84%E6%9E%9C%E6%A0%91%E7%80%91%E5%B8%83_2026-07-31_01.jpg","title":"Huangguoshu Waterfall, Guizhou","desc":"The Huangguoshu Waterfall.","credit":"Kcx36, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%BB%84%E6%9E%9C%E6%A0%91%E7%80%91%E5%B8%83_2026-07-31_01.jpg","alt":"A wide waterfall falling into a green gorge."}},
{"id":"gc-514","num":514,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Shenyang","answerText":"Shenyang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Manchu rise</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1618 – 1627</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Industrial base</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1948</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Liaoning","dot":"Shenyang"},"facts":[["Province","Liaoning"],["Population","9.07M (2020)"],["Rank in China","30th largest"],["Population growth","+11.9% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"瀋陽","hanzi":"沈阳","pinyin":"Shěnyáng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shenyang</b> is the capital of Liaoning and stands in the middle of the province, in the transition between a branch of the Changbai mountains and the flood plain of the Liao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land is alluvial plain in the west with low hills to the north-east and south-east, and it is the communication, commercial, scientific and cultural centre of north-eastern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Manchus it was Mukden, the capital of their country of Shengjing, and the dynasty kept its pride in the city long after it had taken Peking and moved there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its winters are severe and dry and its summers hot; measured against Beijing they run several degrees colder on average, with the better part of a month of extra severely cold days in the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government designated it a key investment and industrial base in 1948, and before the 1980s it was the centre of heavy industrial development in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That history left a city built for industry, and with it decades of pollution the planning of the time did not account for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its urban area grew many times over across the century to 2010, and the rate of that growth rose sharply after 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The built-up area lay almost wholly north of the Hun until then, and the south bank has been the front of expansion ever since, spreading outward in rings that are now a four-ring road network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city has abundant wetlands, more of them than anywhere else in the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The labile carbon in those wetland soils and the microbes that work on it are now studied for what the city's growth has done to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Miao Liu, Yanyan Xu, Yuanman Hu, Chunlin Li, Fengyun Sun, and Tan Chen, “A Century of the Evolution of the Urban Area in Shenyang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 6 (2014): e98847, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0098847. [Open access]","Hanlong Gu, Chongyang Huan, and Fengjiao Yang, “Spatiotemporal Dynamics of Ecological Vulnerability and Its Influencing Factors in Shenyang City of China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 2 (2023): 1525, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20021525. [Open access]","Lei Fan, Zixian Li, and Yan Zhou, “Seasonal Outdoor Thermal Comfort and Neutral PET Thresholds in a Severe Cold Dwa Climate City of Shenyang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 15265, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-46003-0. [Open access]","Shiyun Wang, Jiaoyue Wang, Fengming Xi, Yan Yin, Na Zhao et al., “Variations in Wetland Soil Labile Organic Carbon Fractions and Microbial Characteristics along Urban-Rural Gradients,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 26243, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-12080-w. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 44–45, 455, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Skyline_of_Hunnan_District%2C_Shenyang_drone_view_3.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Hunnan_District%2C_Shenyang_drone_view_3.jpg","title":"Shenyang","desc":"Shenyang, seen from a distance.","credit":"Techyan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Hunnan_District,_Shenyang_drone_view_3.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Shenyang."}},
{"id":"gc-018","num":18,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Shanxi","answerText":"Shanxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yungang caves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">471 – 494 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pingyao listed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shanxi"},"facts":[["Capital","Taiyuan"],["Population","34.9M (2020)"],["Largest city","Taiyuan"],["Area","156,700 km² (60,500 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"山西","pinyin":"Shānxī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shanxi</b> is a province of northern China on the eastern side of the Loess Plateau, shaped as two mountain ranges flanking a trough: the Taihang east, the Lüliang west, and a chain of basins between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land falls from the north-east towards the south-west and is cut into fragments by dense gullies, which is what makes soil erosion the province’s abiding problem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Buddhist inheritance is the greatest thing it holds: on the southern cliff of Wuzhou Mountain, west of Datong, stand the Yungang Grottoes, one of China’s four major grotto complexes and a World Heritage Site since 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cave 6 was cut under Emperor Xiaowen of the Northern Wei and finished before the court moved to Luoyang in 494, its central pillar, 37 scenes from the life of the Buddha and Sinicized statues marking the point at which Buddhist art in China became Chinese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pingyao, in the middle of the province, is one of the four best-preserved ancient cities in the country and went onto the World Heritage list in 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Inside its walls the lacquerware, the paper-cuts, the Liu He Tai pillows and the cloth shoes are still made, and tourism has become the town’s pillar industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Coal is the fact that shapes everything else here: more than two fifths of the province’s surface sits over coal mines, and it is one of the country’s critical energy supply bases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Twelve mining areas are planned across it, and long mining has left subsidence over a wide area, damaging more than a thousand villages, so the province is now a national pilot for putting that ground back in order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its vegetation divides north from south — deciduous broadleaf and mixed forest below, temperate scrub and semi-arid grassland above — and the northern part lies inside the national sandstorm source control project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> All of it lies within the core conservation zone of the Yellow River Basin, which the Fen reaches by running the length of the trough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Guofeng Dang, Guibin Li, and Jinzhou Hu, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Driving Mechanisms of Multiple Scales Ecological Security in Shanxi Province,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 11626, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-44386-8. [Open access]","Lulu Chen, Huabin Chai, Weibing Du, Zengzeng Lian, Yu Wang et al., “Spatiotemporal Dynamics of Ecological Quality and Its Drivers in Shanxi Province and Its Planned Mining Areas,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 31023, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15550-3. [Open access]","Yan Lu, Yu Yan, Mengyao Wang, and Long Zhou, “Study on the Influencing Factors of Photovoltaic Siting in Coal Mining Subsidence Areas: Taking Shanxi Province as an Example,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 43603, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-26672-z. [Open access]","Jinfeng Wang, Ya Li, Sheng Wang, Qing Li, Lingfeng Li, and Xiaoling Liu, “Assessment of Multiple Ecosystem Services and Ecological Security Pattern in Shanxi Province, China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 6 (2023): 4819, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20064819. [Open access]","Shangxiao Qiao, Zeao Wang, Runping Zhang, Yu Wang, Cen Wang et al., “Microbial Community Analysis and Environmental Association in Cave 6 of the Yungang Grottoes,” <i>Microorganisms</i> 13, no. 12 (2025): 2788, https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms13122788. [Open access]","Xing Zhang and Jasni Dolah, “Designing Strategies of Pingyao Lacquerware Tourist Souvenirs Based on Tourists’ Demand,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 7 (2024): e0305662, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0305662. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/61292-Yungang-Grottoes_%2828498548881%29.jpg/1920px-61292-Yungang-Grottoes_%2828498548881%29.jpg","title":"the Yungang Grottoes, Shanxi","desc":"The Yungang Grottoes.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:61292-Yungang-Grottoes_(28498548881).jpg","alt":"A colossal seated Buddha carved from a cliff."}},
{"id":"gc-515","num":515,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Fuzhou","answerText":"Fuzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty port</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1842</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Fujian","dot":"Fuzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Fujian"],["Population","8.29M (2020)"],["Rank in China","39th largest"],["Population growth","+16.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"福州","pinyin":"Fúzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Fuzhou</b> is the capital of Fujian and the political, economic and cultural centre of the province, standing on its eastern coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ground is high in the north-west and low in the south-east, a pattern local description calls mountains backing the sea: extensions of the Daiyun range inland, hilly terraces through the middle, and river valley and coastal plain along the seaboard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That sequence of mountain, hill, plain and coast within the bounds of one city is unusually complete, and its irregular shore runs past a great many islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Min, the largest river in Fujian, crosses the whole territory from north-west to south-east and enters the East China Sea at Mawei, building an estuarine delta as it goes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was one of the five ports opened to British residence and trade by the treaty signed at Nanking in 1842, alongside Canton, Amoy, Ningpo and Shanghai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Summers here are punishing, and Fuzhou is counted one of the four furnace cities of China, with a strong urban heat island standing over its built-up core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who bears that heat is not evenly distributed across the city, and it has been assessed for the justice of the arrangement as well as for the temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The metropolitan area built around the city was the second in the country to be approved at national level, and it reaches well beyond the municipal boundary into the towns along the Min.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Land-use simulation and carbon assessment are now run together over that area to work out what it should become, rather than deciding the two questions one after the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ecological and recreational functions are planned together rather than separately, on the reasoning that in a city hemmed between hills and water the same ground has to do both jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Junting Bai, Rui Sun, Yifan Liu, Jie Chen, and Xiaohe Li, “Integrating Ecological and Recreational Functions to Optimize Ecological Security Pattern in Fuzhou City,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 778, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-84660-1. [Open access]","Fengjuan Zhou, Juan Wang, Zhongxiang Li, Daoyuan Zhou, Tingting Hu, and Fuhe Wang, “Multi-Scenario Simulation of Land Use Change and Landscape Ecological Vulnerability Analysis in Fuzhou City,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 6331, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-35642-y. [Open access]","Fengxiao Cao, Yimeng Zhou, Yu Luo, Yuming Shang, Jinsu Yang, and Di Yang, “A VSD-Based Framework for Assessing Climate Justice in Urban Outdoor Cooling Spaces: A Case Study of Fuzhou,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 13 (2025): 1724719, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2025.1724719. [Open access]","Qiuyi Zhang, Ronghui Huang, Changhua Zhu, Liyun Huang, and Di Yang, “Integrating Land Use Simulation and Carbon Assessment for Sustainable Urban Planning in Fuzhou Metropolitan Area,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 30382, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-13961-w. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, ed., <i>Treaties between the Empire of China and Foreign Powers</i>, 4th ed. (Shanghai: North-China Herald, 1902), 1–5, https://archive.org/details/treatiesbetween00mayegoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Fuzhou_Gulou_District_Panorama.jpg/1920px-Fuzhou_Gulou_District_Panorama.jpg","title":"Fuzhou","desc":"Fuzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"DannyFangles, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuzhou_Gulou_District_Panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Fuzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-019","num":19,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Chongqing","answerText":"Chongqing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wartime industry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1938 – 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Core widened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Chongqing"},"facts":[["Kind","Municipality"],["Population","32.1M (2020)"],["Population growth","+11.1% (2010–2020)"],["Area","82,400 km² (31,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","city"],"traditional":"重慶","hanzi":"重庆","pinyin":"Chóngqìng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Chongqing</b> is one of the four municipalities of China, governed directly from Beijing rather than through a province, and divided into 38 districts and counties of which 26 are districts, eight counties and four autonomous counties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands at the confluence of the Jialing and the Yangtze, in the low hills at the eastern end of the Sichuan basin, and is the standing example in the country of a mountain city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1938 the National Resources Commission moved its defence industry into the five south-western provinces, with more of its projects here than anywhere else, and the city became the political and economic centre of the Chinese hinterland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What a factory needed from its ground changed with the war: before it, rail and road and water together; during it, water alone — and above all concealment, which is why some works were dug into the mountainsides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commission had one power plant before the war and seven by the end of it, and by December 1945 it held 128 industrial and mining enterprises across the south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The built-up core of the modern city is Yuzhong, a small district of steep ground carrying 21 parks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government set out a pattern in 2014 of one urban area and two town groups, in which the city proper leads the urban clusters of the Three Gorges Reservoir area and of the Wuling mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That city proper was widened from nine districts to 21 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jialing, which joins the Yangtze in the middle of the built-up area, supplies the city with industrial, farm and domestic water and is a conservation area for the upper Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Together with Chengdu it forms an economic circle described as the fourth pole of growth in the country, and the light it throws at night has spread outwards from the two cities into the country between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yudan Zhang, Yuanqing Li, Yanan Chen, Shirao Liu, and Qingyuan Yang, “Spatiotemporal Heterogeneity of Urban Land Expansion and Urban Population Growth under New Urbanization,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 13 (2022): 7792, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph19137792. [Open access]","Yangjie Wu, “Searching for the Heritage of the Second Sino-Japanese War: A Study on the Site Selection Strategy of the Defence Industrial Buildings of the National Resources Commission (1937–1945),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 11 (2024): e0311436, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0311436. [Open access]","Aiping Gou, Xuyuan Wang, Jiangbo Wang, Chenjie Wang, and Guanzheng Tan, “Spatial Pattern and Heterogeneity of Green View Index in Mountainous Cities: A Case Study of Yuzhong District,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 12576, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-97946-9. [Open access]","Maolan Zhang, Guoming Zeng, Dong Liang, Yiran Xu, Yan Li et al., “An Analysis of the Colony Structure of Prokaryotes in the Jialing River Waters in Chongqing,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 9 (2022): 5525, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph19095525. [Open access]","Wenli Liang, Rui Liu, and Pinglang Kou, “Nighttime Light Dynamics Reveal Peri-Urban Brightening and Population Decoupling in the Chengdu Chongqing Economic Circle,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 4601, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-34706-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Dazu_2007_685_full_view.jpg/1920px-Dazu_2007_685_full_view.jpg","title":"the Dazu Rock Carvings, Chongqing","desc":"The Dazu Rock Carvings.","credit":"G41rn8, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dazu_2007_685_full_view.jpg","alt":"Painted figures carved in relief along a rock face."}},
{"id":"gc-516","num":516,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Xi'an","answerText":"Xi'an","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Qin Shihuang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">259 – 210 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tang capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 907 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shaanxi","dot":"Xi'an"},"facts":[["Province","Shaanxi"],["Population","12.2M (2020)"],["Rank in China","11th largest"],["Population growth","+43.9% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"西安","pinyin":"Xī'ān","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Xi’an</b> is the capital of Shaanxi and stands where Chang’an stood, the capital of the Tang and the point from which the Silk Road ran west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tang Chang’an was laid out as a grid of walled wards around a Palace City and an Imperial City, with two markets in it, and its eastern and western halves were governed as separate counties, Wannian and Chang’an.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wards had characters of their own — Pingkang, where the courtesans lived, Xuanyang with its offices, Tongyi with its temple — and a Tang tale such as <i>The Tale of Li Wa</i> can be followed ward by ward across the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genomes recovered from burials of that period are mostly indistinguishable from the Neolithic farmers of the middle Yellow River, though several carry Western Eurasian ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Hui, the largest of Shaanxi’s ethnic minorities and Muslim in the main, trace their origins here to the same dynasty, Han families having joined with Arab, Persian, Mongol and Uyghur ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A thousand years before any of that the First Emperor was buried east of the modern city, under a mound guarded by pits of life-sized ceramic soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their bronze weapons were long held to owe their condition to a chromium anti-rust coating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chromium turned out to have leached from nearby lacquer after burial, and the credit belongs to the soil the army was laid in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two cemeteries beside the mausoleum say something about how it was built: at Liyi lie local Qin workers who ate millet, and at Shanren prisoners thrown into a mass grave, some in iron leg shackles, who had eaten a poorer and more mixed diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How the complex was raised, and by whom, is still only partly known, and what those bones record of diet is among the few direct answers there are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Zhaoyi Ma, Jie He, and Shuaishuai Liu, “Representation of the Spatio-Temporal Narrative of The Tale of Li Wa,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 4 (2020): e0231529, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0231529. [Open access]","Minglei Lv et al., “Ancient Genomes from the Tang Dynasty Capital Reveal the Genetic Legacy of Trans-Eurasian Communication at the Eastern End of Silk Road,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 22 (2024): 267, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11577736/. [Open access]","Ying Ma, Benjamin T. Fuller, Weigang Sun, Songmei Hu, Liang Chen et al., “Tracing the Locality of Prisoners and Workers at the Mausoleum of Qin Shi Huang: First Emperor of China (259-210 BC),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6, no. 1 (2016): 26731, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep26731. [Open access]","Marcos Martinón-Torres, Xiuzhen Li, Yin Xia, Agnese Benzonelli, Andrew Bevan et al., “Surface Chromium on Terracotta Army Bronze Weapons Is Neither an Ancient Anti-Rust Treatment nor the Reason for Their Good Preservation,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9, no. 1 (2019): 5289, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-40613-7. [Open access]","Li Zhang, Fei Zhan, Wenfang Zhang, Sanping Song, and Shisheng Zhu, “Forensic Characteristics of 38 Y-STR Loci in the Hui Population from Shaanxi Province, Northwest China,” <i>Heliyon</i> 10, no. 19 (2024): e38501, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e38501. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Skyline_of_Xi%27an_CBD.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Xi%27an_CBD.jpg","title":"Xi'an","desc":"Xi'an, seen from a distance.","credit":"Derxxxxx, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Xi%27an_CBD.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Xi'an."}},
{"id":"gc-020","num":20,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Heilongjiang","answerText":"Heilongjiang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Railway contract</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Blackland rules</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Heilongjiang"},"facts":[["Capital","Harbin"],["Population","31.9M (2020)"],["Largest city","Harbin"],["Area","473,000 km² (183,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"黑龍江","hanzi":"黑龙江","pinyin":"Hēilóngjiāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Heilongjiang</b> is the northernmost and easternmost province of China, at the top of a north-east that meets Russia and Mongolia along its northern and western edges and North Korea in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cold that deep is what makes its soil: plant matter piles up faster than it can rot, and the residue is the black humus layer the region is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That black soil is rich in organic matter and holds a great deal of carbon, and it lies under cultivated land, forest, grassland, wetland and lake alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is now at high risk of degradation — worn by erosion on slopes, by wind, by tillage, by snowmelt runoff and by repeated freezing and thawing — and the province passed regulations on protecting and using its blackland in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Bei’an, where the ground has been farmed a long time, the organic matter and the nitrogen in the soil have both been falling year on year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What drew the modern province was a railway: after the Chinese Eastern Railway contract of 1898, Russia built railway workshops and locomotive repair shops at Harbin, Manzhouli and other places along the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan supplanted Russian influence in the north-east after the Russo-Japanese War of 1905 and founded the South Manchuria Railway Company to run railways, mining and steel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The coal and iron ore under the region laid the groundwork for the heavy industry that followed, and the north-east now carries hundreds of listed industrial heritage sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The province holds more forest than any other in China, though its state forest areas have long leaned on felling and processing timber rather than on anything of higher value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its cities are built for a cold season that runs more than three months, and Harbin, its capital, is the standing example in the country of a cold-region city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]","Chen Qu, Wen Li, Jia Xu, and Song Shi, “Blackland Conservation and Utilization, Carbon Storage and Ecological Risk in Green Space,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 4 (2023): 3154, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20043154. [Open access]","Jia Sui and Yingjie Dai, “Analysis of Conservation Practices for Black Soil Based on Organic Matter and Nitrogen Contents in the Black Soil Region of Northeast China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 23989, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-09426-9. [Open access]","Shuo Diao and Yude Geng, “Efficiency Evaluation and Nonlinear Multi-Objective Optimization of Forestry Industry Transformation,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 21216, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-47953-5. [Open access]","Wente Pan, Shuqi Li, Yang Ye, Yuan Huang, Haocheng Liu et al., “Morphological Pattern of Building Clusters in Cold Regions: Evidence from Harbin,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 24 (2022): 17083, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192417083. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Jingpo_Lake%2C_rowing_boat.jpg","title":"Jingpo Lake, Heilongjiang","desc":"Jingpo Lake.","credit":"drnan tu, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jingpo_Lake,_rowing_boat.jpg","alt":"A lake held behind a lava dam among wooded hills."}},
{"id":"gc-517","num":517,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Guiyang","answerText":"Guiyang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dasongshan tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">206 BCE – 1644 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Qianling park</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1957</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guizhou","dot":"Guiyang"},"facts":[["Province","Guizhou"],["Population","5.99M (2020)"],["Rank in China","65th largest"],["Population growth","+38.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"貴陽","hanzi":"贵阳","pinyin":"Guìyáng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guiyang</b> is the capital of Guizhou and stands among the hills at the middle of the province, on the watershed between the Yangtze and the Pearl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Very little of its ground is flat — mountain and hill take nearly all of it — so the city has had to be built by cutting into slopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tombs at Dasongshan, at Machang in the Gui’an New Area, were named among China’s ten leading archaeological finds of 2022, and they testify to the changes central Guizhou went through between the Han and the Ming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genomes from eight of the people buried there under the Song and the Ming are closest to farmers of the Yellow River Basin, with clear southern influences from Guangxi, coastal Fujian and Southeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of them are close instead to the Hmong-Mien peoples of the region, so more than one ancestry is represented in the same burial ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Migrants from the Central Plains and the Jiangnan brought ox-ploughing with them, which changed the swidden farming the Hmong-Mien speakers had practised in these mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rock beneath the city is Triassic dolomite and limestone, and the karst formed in it runs through the whole catchment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aha Reservoir in the south-west drains that karst country into a lake fed by five rivers, and the wetland park around it was named a national demonstration site in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three of the city’s parks were made by turning remnant mountain forest into public ground — Qianlingshan in 1957, Huaguoyuan in 2010, Yuelianghu in 2020 — and the old forest in them still carries more native species than the planted ground beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the city burns shows in its air: over the Huaxi district sulphate rather than nitrate dominates the water-soluble ions, where in a developed city nitrate would predominate instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yongfei Luan, Guohe Huang, Guanghui Zheng, and Yuee Wang, “Correlation between Spatio-Temporal Evolution of Habitat Quality and Human Activity Intensity in Typical Karst Basins,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 21 (2022): 14294, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192114294. [Open access]","Wen Wan, Huangzhen Huang, Changguo Hu, Xinglong Zhang, Qingting Zhou et al., “Ancient Genomes in Southwest China Revealed Genetic Interactions among Diverse Populations in the Historical Period,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23, no. 1 (2025): 174, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-025-02299-4. [Open access]","Yin Su, Bingze Ling, and Jintong Ren, “Seasonal and Spatial Variations in Eutrophication and Water Quality of Lake Aha, Guiyang City, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 5 (2025): e0321562, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0321562. [Open access]","Weize Wang, Xiaoyan Gao, Chunhua Cen, Mengping Jian, Zijin Wang, and Jingyi Yang, “Impact of Transforming Karst Mountainous Forests into Urban Parks on Plant Diversity Patterns,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 8 (2024): e70194, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.70194. [Open access]","Yunwu Li, Xianqin Wang, Peng Xu, Jiaqun Gui, Xingqiang Guo et al., “Chemical Characterization and Source Identification of PM2.5 in the Huaxi Urban Area of Guiyang,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 30451, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-81048-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Guiyang_Skyline.jpg/1920px-Guiyang_Skyline.jpg","title":"Guiyang","desc":"Guiyang, seen from a distance.","credit":"Ryedamien, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guiyang_Skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Guiyang."}},
{"id":"gc-021","num":21,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Xinjiang","answerText":"Xinjiang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Water mapped</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1900 – 2020s</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Xinjiang"},"facts":[["Capital","Ürümqi"],["Population","25.9M (2020)"],["Largest city","Ürümqi"],["Area","1,664,900 km² (642,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"新疆","pinyin":"Xīnjiāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Xinjiang</b> is an autonomous region in the far north-west and by a long way the largest of China’s provincial-level divisions, about a sixth of the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its structure is three mountain ranges flanking two basins: the Altai along the north, the Tianshan across the middle and the Kunlun along the southern rim, with the Junggar and the Tarim lying between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what fills those basins is desert, gobi and thin scrub, so water decides where anyone can live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The corridors between those ranges have carried people for thousands of years, and the earliest inhabitants of the north-west were formed about 6,000 years ago out of Ancient North Eurasian and Altai hunter-gatherer ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the early Bronze Age ancestry arrived at once from southern Central Asia and from the steppe, and by the Iron Age eastern Eurasian ancestry out of the Baikal region had become prominent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The eastern Tianshan was a channel in both directions, East Asian millet and painted pottery moving along it one way and West Asian wheat, barley, bronze and livestock the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An indigenous Tarim ancestry ran through all of that and on into the historical period, while the Iron Age people of the eastern Tianshan were formed from millet farmers and steppe herders together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pattern of the region’s rivers and canals has been reconstructed over 120 years from old survey sheets: dispersed in 1900, it drew together after the middle of the 20th century and has held that shape since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Out on the oasis plains it is cropland, irrigation and water policy that have driven the change, and the region now grows the great bulk of China’s cotton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Along the northern slope of the Tianshan, where most of the towns are, built-up land and dry farmland are both growing, grassland is shrinking fastest, and the glaciers are under increasing human pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Nuo Li, Wenjie Yu, Kexin Liu, Yihang Zhou, Hanlu Zhang, and Haiwei Zhang, “Reveal the Deployable Solar Energy Potential and Emission Reduction Benefits in the Arid Areas of Xinjiang,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 10437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-40841-8. [Open access]","Xue Zhao, Daxuan Zhang, Bing Sun, Shasha Yang, Yimeng Qi et al., “Tracing Bronze to Iron Age Population Dynamics in Northwest Xinjiang Using Ancient Time-Series Genomic Data,” <i>Genome Biology</i> 27, no. 1 (2026): 33, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-026-03943-0. [Open access]","Xiaomin Yang, Xinyue Zou, Haijun Li, Xiaoyu Yang, Bo Wang et al., “Ancient Genomes Uncover Dynamic Cultural and Genetic Interplay in the Eastern Tianshan,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 43, no. 3 (2026): msag057, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msag057. [Open access]","Raorao Su and Zhen Zhao, “Long-Term Evolution of Hydrographic Network Density in Arid Xinjiang, China: Reconstruction from Historical Maps,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 18764, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-49759-7. [Open access]","Hongbo Wang, Fuchang Jiang, and Xingpeng Wang, “Evaluation of Cotton Production Sustainability and Water Footprints in the Oasis Area of Southern Xinjiang,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 17 (2026): 1789172, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2026.1789172. [Open access]","Lina Tang, Alimujiang Kasimu, Haitao Ma, and Mamattursun Eziz, “Monitoring Multi-Scale Ecological Change and Its Potential Drivers in the Economic Zone of the Tianshan Mountains' North Slope,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 4 (2023): 2844, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20042844. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/The_Heavenly_Lake_%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0_%288064381762%29.jpg","title":"the Heavenly Lake of Tianshan, Xinjiang","desc":"The Heavenly Lake in the Tian Shan.","credit":"Caitriana Nicholson from 北京 ~ Beijing, 中国 ~ China, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Heavenly_Lake_%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0_(8064381762).jpg","alt":"An alpine lake below snow-covered mountains."}},
{"id":"gc-518","num":518,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Taiyuan","answerText":"Taiyuan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Jinyang founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">497 BCE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shanxi","dot":"Taiyuan"},"facts":[["Province","Shanxi"],["Population","5.30M (2020)"],["Rank in China","80th largest"],["Population growth","+26.2% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"太原","pinyin":"Tàiyuán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Taiyuan</b> is the capital of Shanxi and stands at the northern end of the Jinzhong Basin, on ground that runs from mountain through hill and plain to valley, a shape often likened to a dustpan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 497 BCE Zhao Yang, a noble of the state of Jin, founded Jinyang along the Fen here, and the city that grew from it was a political and military centre of China for over a thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives of Jinyang’s western wall runs for some 800 metres (2,600 feet) in Jinyuan district, its rammed layers still visible, part of it above ground and part still buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plants have taken the wall, and they both hold the earth together and break it apart, which is why conservators now argue about whether that green cap is a disease or a defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Jin Temple south-west of the city is among the earliest royal gardens in China and the best-preserved Tang- and Song-style temple garden still standing, with its painted sculptures, inscriptions and plaques.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Ninety-three heritage trees of fourteen species grow in its grounds, one cypress counted among the Three Wonders of the Jin Temple and a stand of old ginkgos remembered for Wang Qiong, a Ming official.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across the city the commonest heritage trees are jujube and pagoda tree, the jujube being one of the oldest cultivated fruits in the world, domesticated on the middle and lower Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A basin holds on to whatever a city puts into the air, and ozone runs above the national standard here through the summer, peaking in the middle of the afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The farmland around the city was long watered with sewage, and its soils were tested for seven heavy metals: the averages fell below the national risk values, except arsenic and cadmium at three points in Xiaodian district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Industry, fertiliser, pesticide and the irrigation water itself were all named as sources, in a city whose population is ageing quickly and whose summers are getting hotter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Yang Pan, Shijie Wang, Chunping Xie, and C. Y. Jim, “Species Diversity and Spatial Pattern of Heritage Trees in Taiyuan,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 17584, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-99731-0. [Open access]","Ruihua Shang, Chenyang Li, Xiaoju Yang, Pengju Han, and Weiwei Liu, “Research on MICP Restoration Technology for Earthen City Walls Damaged by Primary Vegetation Capping in China,” <i>Microorganisms</i> 13, no. 12 (2025): 2802, https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms13122802. [Open access]","Yifei Chen, Lin Ma, Chaomin Ren, Xiaojuan Yang, Lifang Zhao et al., “Projected Cardiovascular Mortality Attributable to Extreme Temperatures under Climate Scenarios in Taiyuan,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 3 (2026): 114970, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.114970. [Open access]","Lili Guo, Tianyu Gao, Bingxi Wang, Yang Cui, Qiusheng He et al., “Spatial Characteristics, Sources of Volatile Organic Compounds and Effects on O3 Formation in Summer in Taiyuan,” <i>Toxics</i> 14, no. 3 (2026): 220, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics14030220. [Open access]","Ying Zhao, Han Yan, and Fei Wang, “Distribution, Source, and Ecological Risk of Heavy Metals in Sewage Irrigation of Taiyuan, Shanxi Province,” <i>Toxics</i> 12, no. 2 (2024): 120, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics12020120. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Skyline_of_Taiyuan%2C_Oct_1_2023.png/1920px-Skyline_of_Taiyuan%2C_Oct_1_2023.png","title":"Taiyuan","desc":"Taiyuan, seen from a distance.","credit":"A Chinese ID, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Taiyuan,_Oct_1_2023.png","alt":"A wide view of Taiyuan."}},
{"id":"gc-022","num":22,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Gansu","answerText":"Gansu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Millet farmed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,900 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wheat arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,200 BP</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Gansu"},"facts":[["Capital","Lanzhou"],["Population","25.0M (2020)"],["Largest city","Lanzhou"],["Area","425,800 km² (164,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"甘肅","hanzi":"甘肃","pinyin":"Gānsù","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Gansu</b> is a province deep inland in north-western China, most of it standing on the second of the country’s great terraces, with plateau, mountain and desert interleaved across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arid and semi-arid country covers three quarters of the province, and soil erosion and desertification are its constant problems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its north-western half is the Hexi Corridor, a trench running from the Wushaoling mountains to the province’s western edge, with three inland river systems rising in it and a string of oases along them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That corridor is the far end of the eastern Silk Road, whose route from Xi’an to Dunhuang crosses loess plateau, the north-eastern rim of the Tibetan Plateau and the corridor itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Millet farming came west along it early: charred grains directly dated at Dadiwan, in Qin’an, give about 7,900 years ago, the earliest on the whole route, while wheat and barley did not reach China until roughly 5,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Gansu is a contact zone in the fullest sense, its people carrying Yellow River, western Eurasian steppe and Tibetan Plateau ancestry, and places such as Linxia and Xiahe are where languages have long mixed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Buddhism that travelled the same road left the Maijishan and Mogao grottoes, both World Heritage Sites, whose wall paintings are now threatened by the microbes living on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oases have carried nomadic and farming ways of life together since early times, and their growth has been traced across two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rain falls sparingly at the corridor’s north-western end and much more freely at its south-eastern, against an evaporation that dwarfs either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What grows is sparse and patchy, drought-tolerant shrubs and herbs on gravel, sand and clay with almost no surface humus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Hui Yu, Bo Zhang, Qianqian He, and Hou Xiao, “Primary Determinants of Water Yield Services in Arid NW China: An Empirical Analysis of Gansu Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329580, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0329580. [Open access]","Haiming Li, Yingyu Qian, Zeli Wang, Nathaniel James, Yifu Cui et al., “Multiple Waves of Westward Dry-Land Agriculture Expansions along the East Silk Road during the Neolithic Age,” <i>Fundamental Research</i> 6, no. 3 (2026): 1321–31, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fmre.2025.12.013. [Open access]","Xia Tang, Linshan Yang, Hao Qu, and Qi Feng, “The Evolution of Artificial Oasis over Two Millennia in Hexi Corridor, China,” <i>Heliyon</i> 9, no. 12 (2023): e22690, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2023.e22690. [Open access]","Wenxia Ma, Qiqi Chen, Fasi Wu, Dongpeng He, Yulong Duan et al., “Difference and Environmental Drivers of Bacterial Communities on Wall Paintings of the Maijishan and Mogao Grottoes, China,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 16 (2025): 1657118, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2025.1657118. [Open access]","Haodong Chen, Huangzhen Huang, Boxuan Zhao, Yiling Jiang, Mengting Xu et al., “Genetic and Linguistic Landscapes of Gansu-Qinghai Populations,” <i>Fundamental Research</i> 6, no. 3 (2026): 1332–42, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fmre.2026.04.001. [Open access]","Xinyi Zhou, Zhaoxiang Zhang, James F. White, Yingxiang Miao, and Shanjia Li, “Geographical Patterns and Drivers of Species and Phylogenetic Diversity of Desert Plant Communities in the Hexi Corridor,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 9 (2025): e72114, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.72114. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Crescent_Lake_from_the_Singing_Sand_Dunes_%2820230918101214%29.jpg/1920px-Crescent_Lake_from_the_Singing_Sand_Dunes_%2820230918101214%29.jpg","title":"Crescent Lake, Gansu","desc":"Crescent Lake at Dunhuang.","credit":"N509FZ, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crescent_Lake_from_the_Singing_Sand_Dunes_(20230918101214).jpg","alt":"A crescent-shaped spring among high sand dunes."}},
{"id":"gc-520","num":520,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Harbin","answerText":"Harbin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Railway from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pneumonic plague</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1910 – 1911</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Heilongjiang","dot":"Harbin"},"facts":[["Province","Heilongjiang"],["Population","10.0M (2020)"],["Rank in China","19th largest"],["Population growth","-5.9% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"哈爾濱","hanzi":"哈尔滨","pinyin":"Hā'ěrbīn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Harbin</b> is the capital of Heilongjiang and stands on the north-eastern part of the Northeast China Plain, in the basin of the Songhua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a plain city rather than an old walled one, grown up since the beginning of the 20th century and largely by self-organisation, with little central control over how it spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made it was a railway: after the Chinese Eastern Railway contract of 1898 Russia built railway workshops and locomotive repair shops here, and Japan supplanted that influence after the Russo-Japanese War of 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the winter of 1910 and the spring of 1911 pneumonic plague spread across the north-east, killing on a great scale and throwing the region into turmoil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wu Lien-teh brought it to an end within four months by compulsory quarantine, by isolating patients, by masks, by segmented martial law and by burning the dead, and China reached for the same measures against SARS in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese army ran a bacteriological laboratory on the edge of the city, Unit 731, and the ground it stood on has been examined since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A live strain of <i>Bacillus anthracis</i> was isolated from soil taken there and confirmed by its phenotype and its whole genome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A wider resampling of the country around the site found none at all, which points away from the strain having come from anything local and natural.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city is the standing example in China of a cold-region city, its cold season running more than three months, and its buildings are clustered for that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is greener than that suggests: a survey of 44 of its parks counted 38 butterfly species, with more of them on the urban fringe than at the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Wente Pan, Shuqi Li, Yang Ye, Yuan Huang, Haocheng Liu et al., “Morphological Pattern of Building Clusters in Cold Regions: Evidence from Harbin,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 24 (2022): 17083, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192417083. [Open access]","Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]","He Liu, Mingli Jiao, Siqi Zhao, Kai Xing, Ye Li et al., “Controlling Ebola: What We Can Learn from China’s 1911 Battle against the Pneumonic Plague in Manchuria,” <i>International Journal of Infectious Diseases</i> 33 (2015): 222–26, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijid.2015.02.013. [Open access]","Yarong Wu, Yuan Yuan, Bing Yuan, Jiaxin Li, Jinglin Wang, and Yujun Cui, “Bacteriologic and Genomic Investigation of <i>Bacillus anthracis</i> Isolated from World War II Site, China,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 30, no. 12 (2024): 2687–89, https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3012.231520. [Open access]","Kai Wang, Yuxin Qi, Yuandong Hu, and Dan Han, “Distribution and Influences on Butterfly Diversity in Urban Park Green Spaces: A Case Study of Harbin, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 6 (2025): e71554, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.71554. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Jihong_Bridge_and_Skyline_of_Daoli%26Daowai_District%2CHarbin_20251021.jpg/1920px-Jihong_Bridge_and_Skyline_of_Daoli%26Daowai_District%2CHarbin_20251021.jpg","title":"Harbin","desc":"Harbin, seen from a distance.","credit":"JacobLyu, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jihong_Bridge_and_Skyline_of_Daoli%26Daowai_District,Harbin_20251021.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Harbin."}},
{"id":"gc-023","num":23,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Shanghai","answerText":"Shanghai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty port</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aurore medicine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1912 – 1952</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"上海","pinyin":"Shànghǎi","translations":"","difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shanghai"},"facts":[["Kind","Municipality"],["Population","24.9M (2020)"],["Population growth","+8.0% (2010–2020)"],["Area","6,341 km² (2,448 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Shanghai</b> is a municipality on the eastern coast of China, standing on estuarine deltaic alluvial plain, flat country that rises hardly at all above the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yangtze Estuary in front of it is one of the largest alluvial estuaries in the world, divided by Chongming Island into two branches, the southern carrying the great bulk of the runoff and sediment that reaches the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its tides are semidiurnal and its tidal flats run a kilometre or two wide, reaching a good deal further at their broadest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was one of five ports opened to British residence and trade by the treaty signed at Nanking in 1842, alongside Canton, Amoy, Foochow and Ningpo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The English and the Chinese texts of that treaty do not say quite the same thing, the translators on each side having shaped their version towards the power their own side was claiming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The foreign settlements that followed brought institutions of their own: <i>l’Aurore</i>, a Jesuit and French university, taught medicine here from 1912 to 1952 without belonging wholly to the mission or to French imperial policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of its hundreds of students were not Christians, though the Jesuit administration kept a record of the baptisms among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Games arrived the same way: badminton moved out of the expatriate enclaves into Shanghai society between 1912 and 1949, and the assumptions it carried about who should play were reworked as women took it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The estuary’s flats have shrunk by more than a third in three decades as land was claimed from them, though less on the Shanghai bank than the Jiangsu one, its own being fed by abundant sediment and by artificial siltation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cultivated land is still the largest single land use here by area, and turning it into construction land is what has cut the municipality’s carbon stocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Ying Chen, Jinwei Dong, Xiangming Xiao, Min Zhang, Bo Tian, Yunxuan Zhou, Bo Li, and Zhijun Ma, “Land Claim and Loss of Tidal Flats in the Yangtze Estuary,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6, no. 1 (2016): 24018, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep24018. [Open access]","Yangyang Wu, Jinli Yang, Siliang Li, Yang Zhao, Chunzi Guo, Xiaodong Yang, Yue Xu, Fujun Yue, Zhonghua Zhang, Songyu Yang, Guanghong Zhou, Haobiao Wu, Panli Yuan, and Guangjie Luo, “Decoding Carbon Pathways of Shanghai Megacity through Historical Land Use Patterns and Urban Ecosystem Transformations,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 6326, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-90755-0. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, ed., <i>Treaties between the Empire of China and Foreign Powers</i>, 4th ed. (Shanghai: North-China Herald, 1902), 1–5, https://archive.org/details/treatiesbetween00mayegoog. [Open access]","Manli Li and Xujun Tian, “Psychological Dynamics among Chinese and British Negotiators and Translators: A Text-Analytical Study of Translation Manipulation in the English–Chinese Translation of the Treaty of Nanking,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0326356, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0326356. [Open access]","Steven Pieragastini and Martin Robert, “Imperial Mission: Jesuits, French Diplomacy, and Medical Education at <i>l’Aurore</i> University in Shanghai, 1912–1952,” <i>Medical History</i> 68, no. 2 (2024): 200–216, https://doi.org/10.1017/mdh.2024.17. [Open access]","Zhuomiao Zhao, Chang Liu, and Kohei Kawashima, “From Feminine Leisure to Urban Craze: The Popularization of Badminton in Republican Shanghai,” <i>Frontiers in Sports and Active Living</i> 8 (2026): 1717411, https://doi.org/10.3389/fspor.2026.1717411. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/The_Bund_2.jpg/1920px-The_Bund_2.jpg","title":"the Bund, Shanghai","desc":"The Bund and the Pudong skyline.","credit":"钉钉, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bund_2.jpg","alt":"A riverfront of old banking houses facing modern towers."}},
{"id":"gc-521","num":521,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Ürümqi","answerText":"Ürümqi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Glacier watched</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Glacier split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1993</span></div>","traditional":"烏魯木齊","hanzi":"乌鲁木齐","pinyin":"Wūlǔmùqí","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Xinjiang","dot":"Ürümqi"},"facts":[["Region","Xinjiang"],["Population","4.05M (2020)"],["Rank in China","125th largest"],["Population growth","+30.3% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Ürümqi</b> is the capital of Xinjiang and stands on the southern margin of the Junggar Basin, with mountains on three sides including the northern slopes of the Tianshan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It lies in the heart of the Eurasian landmass and is the inland city furthest from any sea in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits in the core zone of the Silk Road Economic Belt and is a hub of the Belt and Road Initiative, its confinement in a mountain-basin system making it a small model of the problems of arid Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Being ringed by mountains leaves it prone to winter temperature inversions, when a lid of warm air settles over colder air at the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Valley winds drain cold air downslope into the lower city at night, deepening the inversion, and the wind beneath it is too slow to carry off what the city emits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the head of the Ürümqi River sits Glacier No. 1, a reference glacier of the World Glacier Monitoring Service since 1959 and among the most closely watched in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It split into an eastern and a western branch in 1993 and has been losing both area and thickness ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The built-up area has spread northwards since 2000 because the mountains leave it nowhere else to go, taking farmland with it as it went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How a block is built changes what the air above it does, and the city’s form and its pollutants have been measured against one another for their effect on its heat island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The dry air that makes life hard here has also preserved a great deal, and Xinjiang holds the richest collection of leather relics in China, kept in museums that fight a running battle with salt-tolerant, spore-forming bacteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Wenya Zhang, Aliya Baidourela, Fuxiang Ma, Julati Xiaokelati, Gongxin Yang, Qian Sun, and Shiwei Wang, “Policy-Driven Carbon Sink Enhancement in Dryland Cities: A Case Study of Urumqi, a Core City on China's New Silk Road,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 11083, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-40905-9. [Open access]","Ren Cai, Xian Yang, and Keming Zhao, “Simulation Study of Low-Level Disturbances Affecting Near Surface Inversion Events in Urumqi,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 21568, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-05089-8. [Open access]","Hu Yuhan, Wang Feiteng, Zhang Chunxiang, Pan Ruiqi, Du Zhencai, and Ming Jing, “Climatic Controls on Surface Albedo and Mass Balance of Urumqi Glacier No. 1 in the Last Decade, 2014–2024,” <i>iScience</i> 28, no. 12 (2025): 113942, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2025.113942. [Open access]","Jiayu Fan, Xuegang Chen, Weihong Zhang, Mei Zhao, and Xinlu Yang, “Comparison of Mediating Effects of Air Pollutants on Urban Morphology and Urban Heat Island Intensity at Block Scale,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 18433, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-02665-w. [Open access]","Yuanyuan Wang, Yu Wang, Lilong Hou, Liwen Zhong, Hailiang Yang et al., “Assessment of Airborne and Surface Microbes on Leather Cultural Relics in Museums of Arid Regions Represented by Xinjiang, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 35107, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-19013-7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Urumqi_Skyline_July_2019.jpg/1920px-Urumqi_Skyline_July_2019.jpg","title":"Ürümqi","desc":"Ürümqi, seen from a distance.","credit":"Albertaont, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urumqi_Skyline_July_2019.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Ürümqi."}},
{"id":"gc-024","num":24,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jilin","answerText":"Jilin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Baekdu eruption</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 946 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"吉林","pinyin":"Jílín","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jilin"},"facts":[["Capital","Changchun"],["Population","24.1M (2020)"],["Largest city","Changchun"],["Area","187,400 km² (72,400 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Jilin</b> is a province in the middle of north-eastern China, its land tilting down from south-east to north-west, mountain and hill in the east giving way to plain across the centre and the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of that plain is black soil, a dark earth rich in organic matter that formed on loess-like clay, on river and flood deposits, on moraine and on windblown material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is fertile enough to have made the province one of the country’s principal grain bases, and the black soil belt of north-eastern China holds about an eighth of the world’s black soils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That soil is being worn away, and long intensive cultivation has thinned the plough layer, hardened the pan beneath it and cut the organic matter above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jilin passed the country’s first local regulations for protecting black land, and has taken conservation tillage across a large and still growing share of its fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the east the Changbai range carries some of the best-preserved temperate forest in Asia, its slopes banded by height from mixed conifer and broadleaf through birch to alpine grassland at the top.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the head of that range stands a volcano the Chinese call Changbaishan and the Koreans Paektu, sitting astride the border between the two countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its Millennium Eruption began late in 946 and was among the largest of the Common Era, laying ash across Russia and Japan and leaving its signal in Greenland ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its effect on climate was smaller than that suggests: the sulphur fell out quickly and in winter, so the aerosol veil it raised was a short-lived one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the modern era the province’s industry was framed around Changchun’s motor works, with power and chemicals beside it, and its film studio is now counted among the north-east’s industrial heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Yue Zhu, Jiafu Liu, and Baihao Zhang, “Evaluation and Trade-Offs/Synergies of Ecosystem Services in Jilin Province,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 24873, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-10338-x. [Open access]","Xu Sun, Rui Yan, Ling Zhao, and Yunxian Yan, “Determinants of Farmers' Decision-Making Behavior for Black Soil Protection in Northeast China — A Case of Jilin Province,” <i>PLOS One</i> 20, no. 12 (2025): e0339285, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0339285. [Open access]","Guzailinuer Aihemaitijiang, Lujuan Zhang, Mingtang Li, Yanan Chen, Jiquan Zhang, Feng Zhang, and Chunli Zhao, “PAH Contamination, Sources and Health Risks in Black Soil Region of Jilin Province, China,” <i>Toxics</i> 12, no. 12 (2024): 937, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics12120937. [Open access]","Zhuoma Wan, Yunga Wu, Peng Zhang, Zhijing Xie, Donghui Wu, and Stefan Scheu, “Drivers of Centipede and Spider Diversity and Biomass Along an Elevation Gradient on Changbai Mountain, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 10 (2025): e72074, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.72074. [Open access]","Giyoon Lee, Andrea Burke, William Hutchison, Patrick Sugden, Celeste Smith et al., “Phasing and Climate Forcing Potential of the Millennium Eruption of Mt. Baekdu,” <i>Communications Earth &amp; Environment</i> 5, no. 1 (2024): 549, https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-024-01713-z. [Open access]","Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/%E4%BB%8E%E9%95%BF%E7%99%BD%E5%B1%B1%E8%A5%BF%E5%9D%A1%E7%9C%8B%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0-2017-08-24_1.jpg/1920px-%E4%BB%8E%E9%95%BF%E7%99%BD%E5%B1%B1%E8%A5%BF%E5%9D%A1%E7%9C%8B%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0-2017-08-24_1.jpg","title":"Heaven Lake, Jilin","desc":"Heaven Lake in the Changbai Mountains.","credit":"Antigng, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BB%8E%E9%95%BF%E7%99%BD%E5%B1%B1%E8%A5%BF%E5%9D%A1%E7%9C%8B%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0-2017-08-24_1.jpg","alt":"A crater lake ringed by bare volcanic peaks."}},
{"id":"gc-522","num":522,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Lanzhou","answerText":"Lanzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built-up growth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2000 – 2020</span></div>","traditional":"蘭州","hanzi":"兰州","pinyin":"Lánzhōu","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Gansu","dot":"Lanzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Gansu"],["Population","4.36M (2020)"],["Rank in China","115th largest"],["Population growth","+20.6% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Lanzhou</b> is the capital of Gansu and sits at the geometric centre of China’s continental land map, with mountain ranges to its north and south and the Yellow River running between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A valley leaves a city nowhere to spread but along itself, so Lanzhou has grown as a belt, and its road network has taken the same strip shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The river enters at the confluence with the Datong and leaves at Dabaogou, crossing six counties and districts on its way through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city has given its name to one of the three most famous fast foods in China: Lanzhou beef noodles, eaten here for more than two hundred years and now listed as part of the country’s intangible cultural heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A bowl of them turns on the bouillon rather than the meat, and the city sells about a million of them a day across more than 1,500 restaurants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It stands on the upper Yellow River in the transition between the monsoon and non-monsoon climates, its natural vegetation grassland of herbs, with the Tengger and Badain Jaran deserts along its edges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same valley that shapes the city traps its air: the atmosphere over it is stably stratified and calm days are frequent, which keeps industrial and heating emissions from dispersing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That once made this one of the most polluted places in the country, and it remains a political and industrial centre for the north-west with a high proportion of heavy industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its built-up area grew far faster in the first decade of this century than in the second, the valley having filled up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It anchors the Lanzhou–Xining agglomeration, nine prefecture-level units across two provinces, counted an ecological barrier for the upper Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Minan Yang, Yongsheng Qian, Xin Li, Yueqing Ou, and Junwei Zeng, “River Valley Urban Network and Morphology: A Study on the Urban Morphology Evolution of Lanzhou,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0302686, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0302686. [Open access]","Zheqi Zhang, Jiaolong Jiang, Mingwu Zang, Kaihua Zhang, Dan Li, and Xiaoman Li, “Flavor Profile Analysis of Instant and Traditional Lanzhou Beef Bouillons Using HS-SPME-GC/MS, Electronic Nose and Electronic Tongue,” <i>Bioengineering</i> 9, no. 10 (2022): 582, https://doi.org/10.3390/bioengineering9100582. [Open access]","Juanli Li, Chengyu Feng, Xuanlong Ma, and Duo Wu, “Impact of the Urban Atmospheric Environment on Otolaryngologic Disease Outpatient Visits in Lanzhou, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 21556, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-07352-4. [Open access]","Jin Ma, Xuan Yang, Xiaodan Li, Lide Zhang, Jiaojiao Lei, Lina Wang, and Xuelu Liu, “Research on Temporal-Spatial Distribution Differences and Formation Mechanisms of NPP in the Lanzhou Section of the Yellow River Mainstream,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 29132, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15226-y. [Open access]","Tonghui Yu, Mengru Wang, Xinyu Li, Xuan Zhu, Mengting Yang, and Jiqiang Niu, “Land Spatial Optimization in Ecologically Fragile Regions of the Lanzhou Xining Urban Agglomeration Based on a Multidimensional Ecosystem Framework,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 8533, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-38134-1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Lanzhou_Skyline_201906.jpg/1920px-Lanzhou_Skyline_201906.jpg","title":"Lanzhou","desc":"Lanzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"Pieceofmetalwork, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lanzhou_Skyline_201906.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Lanzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-025","num":25,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Inner Mongolia","answerText":"Inner Mongolia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Erdaojingzi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3700 – 3330 BP</span></div>","traditional":"內蒙古","hanzi":"内蒙古","pinyin":"Nèi Měnggǔ","translations":"","difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Inner Mongolia"},"facts":[["Capital","Hohhot"],["Population","24.0M (2020)"],["Largest city","Chifeng"],["Area","1,183,000 km² (456,800 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Inner Mongolia</b> is an autonomous region stretched along China’s northern edge, mostly plateau, far from any ocean and rimmed with mountains, so its climate is markedly continental.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rainfall falls away westward against a far higher evaporation, which leaves the greater part of it arid or semi-arid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grassland, forest and desert are all extensive here, and of the grassland just over half is typical steppe, three tenths meadow steppe and a sixth desert steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> People have lived off this ground a very long time: the Hongshan culture, one of the earliest complex societies in Neolithic East Asia, spread over a great stretch of country reaching from what is now Inner Mongolia into Liaoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Erdaojingzi, in Chifeng, stands a Bronze Age settlement so well preserved that it is called the Pompeii of the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Millet fed the people there and their pigs and dogs alike, while their cattle and sheep grazed more widely, and their ancestry was closest to the farming populations of the Yellow River rather than to the earlier Neolithic groups of the west Liao country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The grassland has been degrading since the 1960s, and satellite records from 1982 to 2020 find most of it worn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The division runs along the 115th meridian, the west far barer than the east, and restoration programmes begun in the early 2000s have slowed the decline without reversing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sand is the other pressure, and the Kubuqi, which lies along the southern edge of the middle Yellow River, is planted with shrub shelterbelts under the Three Norths forestation programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The region also holds the richest mineral reserves in the country, seventeen of them among the national leaders, and its open-pit coal mines bear hard on the dry ground they stand on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Qi Shi, Wenlan Wang, Xuerui Zhu, Chuan Ye, and Jintai Li, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Drivers of Grassland Ecosystem Service Value in Inner Mongolia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 15201, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-45150-8. [Open access]","Haojun Zheng, Yao Huang, Wen Zhang, Changqing Song, Qing Zhang, Wenjuan Sun, Yongqiang Yu, Lijun Yu, Haigang Li, Cunhou Zhang, Wenfang Jiang, Xinyue Yang, and Guocheng Wang, “The Implementation of Ecological Protection in Inner Mongolia Has Slowed Down Grassland Degradation,” <i>Fundamental Research</i> 5, no. 6 (2025): 2719–30, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fmre.2024.10.006. [Open access]","Xinyu Guo, Guang Yang, ChenGuang Wang, Yue Ren, Xueying Han, Ning Wang, Guangpu Jia, and Shi Qiao, “Improvement Effects of Different Shrub Sand Fixation Plantations on Vegetation and Soil in the Kubuqi Desert,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 16 (2025): 1688154, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2025.1688154. [Open access]","Lijia Zhang, Zhenqi Hu, Dazhi Yang, Huanhuan Li, Bo Liu, He Gao, Congjie Cao, Yan Zhou, Junfang Li, and Shuchang Li, “Land Use Dynamic Evolution and Driving Factors of Typical Open-Pit Coal Mines in Inner Mongolia,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 15 (2022): 9723, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph19159723. [Open access]","Rui Wang, Lihuan Zhu, Hao Ma, Meiling Song, Guanju Ma et al., “Genetic Formation of Neolithic Hongshan People and Demic Expansion of Hongshan Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 42, no. 6 (2025): msaf139, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msaf139. [Open access]","Xiaohong Lv, Yao Yu, Lin Ban, Panxin Du, Jianxue Xiong et al., “Population History and Subsistence of Farming Communities in an Agro-Pastoral Transition Zone of Northern China: Ancient DNA and Isotopic Evidence,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13870, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-42242-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Hulun_Buir.jpg/1920px-Hulun_Buir.jpg","title":"the Hulunbuir grassland, Inner Mongolia","desc":"The Hulunbuir grassland.","credit":"Emmazzye, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hulun_Buir.jpg","alt":"Open green grassland rolling away under a wide cloudy sky."}},
{"id":"gc-524","num":524,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Changchun","answerText":"Changchun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Industry rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1946 – 1957</span></div>","traditional":"長春","hanzi":"长春","pinyin":"Chángchūn","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jilin","dot":"Changchun"},"facts":[["Province","Jilin"],["Population","9.07M (2020)"],["Rank in China","31st largest"],["Population growth","+3.4% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Changchun</b> is the capital of Jilin and a sub-provincial city standing in the hinterland of the north-eastern plain, on open flat ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It borders Heilongjiang to the north, the Yanbian Korean Autonomous Prefecture to the east and Liaoning to the south-west, with the Changbai mountains beyond its southern edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its river is the Yitong, the second tributary of the Second Songhua, and through it the city belongs to a basin draining four provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wetlands are plentiful around it, of lake, swamp and river kinds alike, and a century of industry has left its mark in their mud, mercury accumulating in the artificial wetlands strung along the river through the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The built city has grown outwards in rings rather than along an axis, and roughly an eighth of its central area is road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It is one of the cities of the old industrial north-east, a region whose factories were first built along the railway corridors the Russians and then the Japanese drove through it, and what it was built to make still shapes it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under the First Five-Year Plan the industrial framework of the whole province was set around Changchun’s automobile industry, with power and chemicals beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Changchun Film Studio came out of the same decades and is counted now among the region’s best-known light-industrial heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The architectural marks of the Manchukuo years — sawtooth roofs, arched portals — largely disappeared from the region’s factories in the decades that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its built-up area has expanded a great deal since the 1980s, and the road verges that came with it have been colonised by plants nobody planted, many of them from outside the region altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Ming Ma, Yichen Zhang, Jiquan Zhang, Mingda Li, Jiale Zhu, and Yang Wang, “Assessment of Urban Seismic Social Vulnerability Based on Game Theory Combination and TOPSIS Model: A Case Study of Changchun City,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 8189, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-92372-3. [Open access]","Jing Zong, Hongjie Zhang, Xuemei Li, Xinyu Bai, Yufei Hu, Dan Cui, Zhaojun Wang, and Gang Zhang, “Process of Mercury Accumulation in Urban Strip River Artificial Wetland Ecosystems: A Case Study of Changchun, a Typical Industrial City in Northeast China,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 15 (2024): 1392904, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2024.1392904. [Open access]","Qingxi Shen, Xue Tan, and Xipeng Li, “Relationship Between Spatial Pattern and Function of Urban Land Use in Changchun, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 9 (2023): e0291121, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0291121. [Open access]","Diyang Liu, Congcong Zhao, Yongfang Wang, and Yuandong Hu, “Distribution Characteristics and Adaptation Mechanisms of Exotic Spontaneous Plant Diversity in Urban Road Green Spaces of Changchun, China,” <i>Plants</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 107, https://doi.org/10.3390/plants15010107. [Open access]","Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Changchun_skyline_with_Ji_Tower_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Changchun_skyline_with_Ji_Tower_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Changchun","desc":"Changchun, seen from a distance.","credit":"wanghongliu, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Changchun_skyline_with_Ji_Tower_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Changchun."}},
{"id":"gc-026","num":26,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Beijing","answerText":"Beijing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Peking Man</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Water diverted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 2014</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"北京","pinyin":"Běijīng","translations":"","difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Beijing"},"facts":[["Kind","Municipality"],["Population","21.9M (2020)"],["Population growth","+11.6% (2010–2020)"],["Area","16,400 km² (6,330 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Beijing</b> is a municipality at the northern edge of the North China Plain, its ground high in the north-west and low in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Continuous mountains stand across the west and the north — the Taihang and the Yanshan, the ecological barriers of the whole region — and a wide plain fills the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That plain was laid down by five rivers, the Yongding, the Chaobai, the Ju, the Wenyu and the Juma, whose alluvial fans overlap and interlock into a stacked system of sand and clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> People were here long before any of that mattered to anyone: at Zhoukoudian, in the limestone karst south-west of the city, an international party of Chinese, Swedish, Austrian, French and Canadian palaeontologists dug a rich Ice Age fauna out of the caves in the 1920s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among it were the specimens first named <i>Sinanthropus pekinensis</i>, Peking Man, and counted now as <i>Homo erectus pekinensis</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Enamel proteins have since been recovered from six of those Middle Pleistocene individuals and their kin at two other Chinese sites, from about four hundred thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They carry a variant of one enamel gene found in no other human lineage at all — not in the <i>Homo erectus</i> of Dmanisi, nor at Atapuerca, nor in Denisovans, Neanderthals or ourselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Water has been the modern city’s oldest difficulty, a drought setting in from 1999 as the population climbed by millions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two thirds of the demand was drawn from wells in the decade to 2013, and the ground sank as a result, parts of it dropping several centimetres a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The water table fell steadily until the middle route of the South-to-North Water Diversion opened in 2014, since when it has been rising again, and the municipality has planted new forest across its plains in two rounds since 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Guangyao Duan, Huili Gong, Beibei Chen, Xiaojuan Li, Xingyao Pan, Min Shi, and Hang Zhang, “Spatiotemporal Heterogeneity of Land Subsidence in Beijing,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12, no. 1 (2022): 15120, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-16674-6. [Open access]","Cuiyun Cheng, Shuping Zhang, Meichun Zhou, Yanchun Du, and Chazhong Ge, “Identifying Important Ecosystem Service Areas Based on Distributions of Ecosystem Services in the Beijing–Tianjin–Hebei Region, China,” <i>PeerJ</i> 10 (2022): e13881, https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.13881. [Open access]","Peter Dodson, “China Shares Fossil Treasures with the World,” <i>The Anatomical Record</i> 308, no. 10 (2025): 2806–12, https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.25696. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu, Zhongyou Wu, E. Andrew Bennett, Song Xing, Qiang Ji et al., “Enamel Proteins from Six <i>Homo erectus</i> Specimens across China,” <i>Nature</i> 655, no. 8121 (2026): 141–47, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10478-8. [Open access]","Yuan Li, Rui Wang, Hongbo Ma, and Jian-Min Zhang, “Rising Groundwater Table Due to Restoration Projects Amplifies Earthquake Induced Liquefaction Risk in Beijing,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 1466, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-56525-2. [Open access]","Hongyuan Wei, Adu Gong, Jinhong Wan, Jiaming Zhang, Zexin Fu, and Mengfei Jiang, “Differentiating the Thermal Effects of Land Use Change and Functional Intensity Dynamics in Beijing,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 10701, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-44866-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/The_Forbidden_City_-_View_from_Coal_Hill.jpg/1920px-The_Forbidden_City_-_View_from_Coal_Hill.jpg","title":"the Forbidden City, Beijing","desc":"The Forbidden City.","credit":"Pixelflake, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Forbidden_City_-_View_from_Coal_Hill.jpg","alt":"The great halls and courtyards of an imperial palace."}},
{"id":"gc-525","num":525,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Hohhot","answerText":"Hohhot","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"呼和浩特","pinyin":"Hūhéhàotè","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Inner Mongolia","dot":"Hohhot"},"facts":[["Region","Inner Mongolia"],["Population","3.45M (2020)"],["Rank in China","146th largest"],["Population growth","+20.2% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Hohhot</b> is the capital of the Inner Mongolia Autonomous Region and stands near China’s northern border, on ground that inclines gradually from north-east to south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Daqing and Manhan mountains close around the city, leaving a pocket of land whose one wide opening faces south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Summers there are short and hot and winters dry and cold, cold enough that the city burns coal for heating half the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the difficulty its setting creates, because a pocket ringed by hills disperses very little of what is put into the air above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Temperature inversions are frequent through the winter and pollutants are held under them rather than carried away, so heavily polluted weather forms whenever the air goes still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The city is a centre of Mongolian medicine, a tradition built on the theory its practitioners call the Three Roots and Seven Essentials and on the harmony of people with the natural world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its pulse and urine diagnosis and its principles of cold and heat resemble those of Han Chinese medicine in form while resting on a different footing altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It works too with processed mineral and animal preparations that Han medicine does not use, which is part of why it needs a standard-setting system of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hohhot sits at one corner of the Hohhot, Baotou and Ordos triangle, the most important economic circle and urban belt in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That belt carries a heavy share of the region’s industry, most of Inner Mongolia’s industrial sulphur dioxide and carbon dioxide coming from it, and the city’s own surface is watched by satellite radar for the subsidence that comes with rapid building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Kailin Ji, Bing Sun, Haijun Zhou, Wenjing Sun, Xiaotao Fu, Ying Sun, Huifang Ren, Yangchao Lv, Xi Chun, and Zhiqiang Wan, “Impacts of COVID-19 Lockdown on PM2.5-Bound Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons in Hohhot, Northern China: Characteristics, Sources, and Source-Specific Health Risks,” <i>Environmental Health</i> 24, no. 1 (2025): 5, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12940-025-01161-y. [Open access]","Xue Wang, Chenghua Gao, Yuan Xia, Xiaoqian Xu, Lehui Li, Yan Liu, Xing Yao, Ning Cao, Zichao Li, and Xin Fang, “Effect of Air Pollutants and Meteorological Factors on Daily Outpatient Visits of Allergic Rhinitis in Hohhot, China,” <i>Journal of Asthma and Allergy</i> 16 (2023): 1217–28, https://doi.org/10.2147/JAA.S430062. [Open access]","Yuejuan Chen, Siai Du, Pingping Huang, Huifang Ren, Bo Yin, Yaolong Qi, Cong Ding, and Wei Xu, “Analysis and Prediction of Urban Surface Transformation Based on Small Baseline Subset Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar and Sparrow Search Algorithm–Convolutional Neural Network–Long Short-Term Memory Model,” <i>Sensors</i> 24, no. 8 (2024): 2634, https://doi.org/10.3390/s24082634. [Open access]","Yazhou Zhao, Zhou Yin, Xin Zhang, Yue Kuang, Yishu Zhao, Jing Liu, Qingling Zhang, and Yanping Li, “Collaborative Control Path of Pollution and Carbon Reduction in Industrial Field in Hohhot–Baotou–Ordos Region of Inner Mongolia,” <i>Heliyon</i> 10, no. 23 (2024): e40695, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e40695. [Open access]","Shenzhen Liu, Zili Yang, Alun, and Min Li, “Policy Text Analysis on Inheritance and Innovation of Mongolian Medicine Culture in China,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 14 (2026): 1814485, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1814485. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Hohhot_Central_Square.jpg/1920px-Hohhot_Central_Square.jpg","title":"Hohhot","desc":"Hohhot, seen from a distance.","credit":"David Wilmot from Wimbledon, United Kingdom, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hohhot_Central_Square.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hohhot."}},
{"id":"gc-027","num":27,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tianjin","answerText":"Tianjin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reserve made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Water diverted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 2014</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"天津","pinyin":"Tiānjīn","translations":"","difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Tianjin"},"facts":[["Kind","Municipality"],["Population","13.9M (2020)"],["Population growth","+7.2% (2010–2020)"],["Area","11,966 km² (4,620 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Tianjin</b> is a municipality on the western shore of Bohai Bay at the eastern edge of the North China Plain, low and flat, most of its ground standing only a few metres above the sea on a plain built of river and marine sediment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath it lie hundreds of metres of Neogene and Quaternary beds in which sand aquifers alternate with silt and clay, a confined system that compacts when it is pumped dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole Haihe river system drains to the sea here, gathering a great part of northern China into one outlet on the Tianjin coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The coast keeps a record of where it used to run: the Ancient Coast and Wetland reserve, approved in October 1992, holds shell dikes, oyster reefs and the Qilihai marsh together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the first and only state-level reserve in China where an ancient coast and a wetland lie in one place, and its oyster beaches have no equal elsewhere in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The shore around them is salt country, its shallow water table brackish and alkaline and the soils above it saline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city carried foreign concessions well into the twentieth century, and the Japanese one kept a directory of its own residents: a dentist named Otojiro Shirai opened a dental hospital there in 1936, on retiring from the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pumping the aquifer from the 1950s onwards sank the plain in five separate centres, the city itself dropping by metres between 1959 and 1983.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inland districts were still falling quickly into the 2000s, but the rates have eased since 2017 and the sinking is now concentrated in the reclaimed ground of the coastal Binhai New Area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The South-to-North Water Diversion has supplied the municipality since 2014, and with limits on pumping and deliberate recharge the water table has begun to recover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Haolin Zhao, Hongyue Zhou, Dashan Zhou, and Chaoying Zhao, “Two Decades of Land Subsidence in Tianjin, China, Measured with Multi-Temporal InSAR Observations,” <i>Sensors</i> 26, no. 4 (2026): 1203, https://doi.org/10.3390/s26041203. [Open access]","Huashan Xu, Yufen Ren, Hua Zheng, Zhiyun Ouyang, and Bo Jiang, “Analysis of Runoff Trends and Drivers in the Haihe River Basin, China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 17, no. 5 (2020): 1577, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph17051577. [Open access]","Chen Chen, ChaoFeng Shao, and YanMin Shi, “Dynamic Evaluation of Ecological Service Function Value of Qilihai Wetland in Tianjin,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 17, no. 19 (2020): 7108, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph17197108. [Open access]","Feng-Chou Cheng, Ling-Hsia Wang, and Chun-Pin Chiang, “The Overseas Expansion of Japanese Dentists in the Early 20th Century: The Case of a Japanese Immigrant Dentist in North China,” <i>Journal of Dental Sciences</i> 20, no. 3 (2025): 1925–27, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jds.2025.03.028. [Open access]","Haixia Zhao, Binjie Gu, Dechao Chen, Jiaojiao Tang, Xinliang Xu, Zhi Qiao, and Junqi Wang, “Physicochemical Properties and Salinization Characteristics of Soils in Coastal Land Reclamation Areas: A Case Study of China-Singapore Tianjin Eco-City,” <i>Heliyon</i> 8, no. 12 (2022): e12629, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2022.e12629. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/%E5%A4%A9%E6%B4%A5%E4%B9%8B%E7%9C%BC%E5%8C%971.jpg/1920px-%E5%A4%A9%E6%B4%A5%E4%B9%8B%E7%9C%BC%E5%8C%971.jpg","title":"the Tianjin Eye","desc":"The Tianjin Eye over the Hai River.","credit":"KangTyngrwey, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A4%A9%E6%B4%A5%E4%B9%8B%E7%9C%BC%E5%8C%971.jpg","alt":"A large ferris wheel built over a river bridge."}},
{"id":"gc-528","num":528,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Haikou","answerText":"Haikou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">A town from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2,200 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"海口","pinyin":"Hǎikǒu","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hainan","dot":"Haikou"},"facts":[["Province","Hainan"],["Population","2.87M (2020)"],["Rank in China","174th largest"],["Population growth","+40.4% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Haikou</b> is the capital of Hainan and stands on the northern shore of the island, across the Qiongzhou Strait from the south coast of the Chinese mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the only tropical coastal provincial capital in the country, governed by the monsoon and by the sea around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is believed to have first become a town in the Han dynasty, some 2,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its air is among the cleanest of any Chinese city, sitting in the lowest pollution tier of the 168 that are closely monitored, and what pollution there is comes from traffic and building rather than from industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the rain that falls on it comes between May and October, and the year here is warm and wet enough that humidity rarely drops far, the sunshine long and the solar radiation fierce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The city drinks mainly from the Nandu, which is the largest river on the island and drains a good part of its northern half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three treatment works supply the main urban area, and that water has been tracked from the river to the household tap for the microplastics it carries along the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the city’s eastern side lies Dongzhaigang, the first mangrove reserve established in China, and Hainan holds the richest mangrove flora in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inland from the coast the Li build their boat-shaped houses from the plants of the rainforest, a tradition now proposed for World Heritage listing as rural depopulation empties the villages that keep it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Haikou’s own green space has been mapped for what grows in it, and in a tropical city of this kind most of what grows has been planted rather than left standing, so the mature native trees that remain are the ones worth counting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Jichao Peng, Xinxin Wu, Jun Chen, Jin Qian, Nan Li, Yang Yi, Yue Huang, Juncai Lu, Wenxing Zhang, Zhengyu Li, Zhao Li, Min Li, and Xiaoran Liu, “Short-Term Effects of Ambient Air Pollutants and Meteorological Factors on Emergency Department Visits in Tropical Haikou, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 20849, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-03517-3. [Open access]","Xiangxiang Li, Yihan Yu, Mei Yang, Shaobai Wen, and Jun Zhang, “Tracking Microplastics Contamination in Drinking Water Supply Chain in Haikou, China: From Source to Household Taps,” <i>Toxics</i> 12, no. 11 (2024): 793, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics12110793. [Open access]","Hai-Li Zhang, Lin-Yuan Guo, Mir Muhammad Nizamani, and Hua-Feng Wang, “Distribution Patterns and Drivers of Urban Green Space and Plant Diversity in Haikou, China,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 14 (2023): 1202115, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2023.1202115. [Open access]","Yufeng Lin, Zifeng Luo, Xuan Gu, Yijuan Deng, Pingping Guo, Guogui Chen, Wenqing Wang, and Mao Wang, “Optimizing Mangrove Afforestation: Mollusc Biodiversity Comparisons Reveal Optimal Mudflat–Mangrove Area Ratio,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 9 (2024): e70330, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.70330. [Open access]","Guang-Hui Ma, Ming-Xun Ren, Ding-Hai Yang, and Xiao-Dong Mu, “Boat-Shaped Houses of the Indigenous Li People on Hainan Island, China: Plant Resources and Ecological Adaptations,” <i>Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine</i> 21, no. 1 (2025): 67, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13002-025-00818-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Haikou_skyline_in_2013_-_01.jpg/1920px-Haikou_skyline_in_2013_-_01.jpg","title":"Haikou","desc":"Haikou, seen from a distance.","credit":"Anna Frodesiak, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haikou_skyline_in_2013_-_01.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Haikou."}},
{"id":"gc-028","num":28,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hainan","answerText":"Hainan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Li settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,000 – 3,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rainforest park</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2020</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"海南","pinyin":"Hǎinán","translations":"","difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hainan"},"facts":[["Capital","Haikou"],["Population","10.1M (2020)"],["Largest city","Haikou"],["Area","33,900 km² (13,100 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Hainan</b> is the southernmost province of China, and the island it is named for lies at the northern end of the South China Sea shelf, facing Guangdong across the Qiongzhou Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The province as administered also takes in island groups far out in the sea, which is why its land is reckoned larger than the island itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island is low around its rim and high in the middle, rising through terraces and hills to Wuzhi Mountain and Parrot Mountain, and its climate is tropical monsoon, long in summer and without a winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Li are its aboriginal people, and the ancestry that dominates them is the one carried by the Tai-Kadai speakers of southern China and northern Vietnam, the Bai-Yue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their divergence from those mainland populations began three to four thousand years ago, when the ancestors of the Li migrated and settled the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Isolation deepened the bottleneck that followed, and malaria has left its own mark on them, in variants of malaria-related genes carried nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They build boat-shaped houses out of the forest around them, four kinds of them from 26 plant species across 13 families, the frames bound with lianas and strips of bamboo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those houses stay cooler and drier inside than the brick ones now replacing them, which is what centuries of adaptation to a rainforest look like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than three fifths of the island stays forested the year round, and a seventh of it became a national park in 2020, where camera trapping at Jianfengling has found fifteen mammal species from six orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The coast carries the richest mangrove flora in the country, 26 true mangroves and 11 semi-mangroves of the 37 species China holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Zihan Jin, Changsheng Xiong, Qiaolin Luan, and Fang Wang, “Dynamic Evolutionary Analysis of Land Use/Cover and Ecosystem Service Values on Hainan Island,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 1 (2022): 776, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20010776. [Open access]","Jia Geng, Mingsheng Yuan, Shen Xu, Tingting Bai, Yang Xiao, Xiaopeng Li, and Dong Xu, “Urban Expansion Was the Main Driving Force for the Decline in Ecosystem Services in Hainan Island during 1980–2015,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 23 (2022): 15665, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192315665. [Open access]","Hao Chen, Rong Lin, Yan Lu, Rui Zhang, Yang Gao, Yungang He, and Shuhua Xu, “Tracing Bai-Yue Ancestry in Aboriginal Li People on Hainan Island,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 39, no. 10 (2022): msac210, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msac210. [Open access]","Guang-Hui Ma, Ming-Xun Ren, Ding-Hai Yang, and Xiao-Dong Mu, “Boat-Shaped Houses of the Indigenous Li People on Hainan Island, China: Plant Resources and Ecological Adaptations,” <i>Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine</i> 21, no. 1 (2025): 67, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13002-025-00818-9. [Open access]","Yufeng Lin, Zifeng Luo, Xuan Gu, Yijuan Deng, Pingping Guo, Guogui Chen, Wenqing Wang, and Mao Wang, “Optimizing Mangrove Afforestation: Mollusc Biodiversity Comparisons Reveal Optimal Mudflat–Mangrove Area Ratio,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 9 (2024): e70330, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.70330. [Open access]","Wenbo Yan, Xiangxiang Lu, Zhigao Zeng, Shaoliang Xue, Qi Wang, Shiqin Mo, and Chunshen Liang, “Unveiling the Mammalian Diversity and Conservation Significance of Jianfengling Region: A Camera-Trapping Survey of Mammals in Hainan Tropical Rainforest National Park,” <i>Animals</i> 16, no. 5 (2026): 721, https://doi.org/10.3390/ani16050721. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Yalong_Bay_%E4%BA%9E%E9%BE%8D%E7%81%A3_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Yalong_Bay_%E4%BA%9E%E9%BE%8D%E7%81%A3_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Yalong Bay, Hainan","desc":"Yalong Bay at Sanya.","credit":"lienyuan lee, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yalong_Bay_%E4%BA%9E%E9%BE%8D%E7%81%A3_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A curving sandy beach with waves and a headland beyond."}},
{"id":"gc-529","num":529,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Yinchuan","answerText":"Yinchuan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Resettling from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1983</span></div>","traditional":"銀川","hanzi":"银川","pinyin":"Yínchuān","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Ningxia","dot":"Yinchuan"},"facts":[["Region","Ningxia"],["Population","2.86M (2020)"],["Rank in China","177th largest"],["Population growth","+43.4% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Yinchuan</b> is the capital of the Ningxia Hui Autonomous Region and stands on the Ningxia Plain, with the Helan mountains along its western side and the Ordos Plateau away to the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yellow River runs through it from south-west to north-east, and the oasis around the city is the artesian irrigation district of that river’s alluvial plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground under it is a subsidence basin opened by faulting in the Cainozoic, bounded by the East Helan fault zone to the west and filled deep with loose Quaternary deposit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its climate is arid with long cold winters, and what little rain falls comes almost entirely between July and September, against an evaporation many times greater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With so little water of its own, the farms and factories of the oasis draw on the Yellow River instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the Helan mountains along the city’s western edge, at Helankou, ancient pastoralists carved petroglyphs into the sandstone cliffs, and those carvings are the best record there is of how they lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rock there is weathering under freeze-thaw and wet-dry cycles, and the images are at risk of going with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The land steps down from the mountain front to the river in a series of plains, piedmont alluvial fans giving way to alluvial and lake deposits as the ground flattens eastward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city proper is built of three central districts, each with a character of its own: Xingqing the old commercial quarter, Jinfeng a newer area of business and housing, and Xixia the district of colleges and research institutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is the core city of its region, drawing people from Gansu, Shaanxi, Anhui and Zhejiang, and it has received many of those resettled within Ningxia since 1983, whose fitting into the city has itself been studied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Lina Mi, Juncang Tian, Jianning Si, Yuchun Chen, Yinghai Li, and Xinhe Wang, “Evolution of Groundwater in Yinchuan Oasis at the Upper Reaches of the Yellow River after Water-Saving Transformation and Its Driving Factors,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 17, no. 4 (2020): 1304, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph17041304. [Open access]","Zhao Ru, Long Dongping, Li Jing, Yang Meiling, and Wen Xinyu, “Ecological Migrants' Socio-Spatial Integration in Yinchuan City, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 10 (2022): e0275853, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0275853. [Open access]","Wengang Qu, Hui Qian, Chao Yang, Panpan Xu, Yanyan Gao, Leiqiang Wei, and Qi Long, “A Comprehensive Multicriteria Decision Analysis Framework for Shallow Geothermal Energy Suitability Evaluation in Yinchuan Area, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 6420, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-90559-2. [Open access]","Xiangchao Kong, Faguo He, Ran Lv, Lizhi Zhang, Xin Yang, and Tengfei Lu, “Experimental Study on the Degradation of Sandstone-Petroglyph Carriers in Helankou: Effects of Freeze-Thaw and Wet-Dry Cycles,” <i>iScience</i> 27, no. 8 (2024): 110576, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2024.110576. [Open access]","Guifen Lyu, Niwat Angkawisittpan, Xiaoli Fu, and Somchat Sonasang, “Investigating the Relationship between Built Environment and Urban Vitality Using Big Data,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 579, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-84279-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Skyline_of_Jinfeng_District%2C_Yinchuan_%2853724463112%29.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Jinfeng_District%2C_Yinchuan_%2853724463112%29.jpg","title":"Yinchuan","desc":"Yinchuan, seen from a distance.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Jinfeng_District,_Yinchuan_(53724463112).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Yinchuan."}},
{"id":"gc-029","num":29,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Ningxia","answerText":"Ningxia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hui here from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tang, 618 CE</span></div>","traditional":"寧夏","hanzi":"宁夏","pinyin":"Níngxià","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Ningxia"},"facts":[["Capital","Yinchuan"],["Population","7.20M (2020)"],["Largest city","Yinchuan"],["Area","66,400 km² (25,600 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Ningxia</b> is an autonomous region in north-western China, and the only one of the mainland’s province-level divisions that lies entirely inside the Yellow River basin, so everything about it turns on that river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The river enters at Xiaheyan in Zhongwei, crosses north-eastward through Qingtongxia, Yinchuan and Shizuishan and leaves for Inner Mongolia, watering an irrigated basin as it goes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That basin is alluvial plain, flat and cut through with ditches, the Yinchuan oasis lying in a fault-subsidence trough beneath the Helan mountains; the climate over it is continental and arid, and most of the little rain that falls comes between July and September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the farming rests on is the sunshine and the long frost-free season rather than the rain, which plainly cannot carry it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Helan mountains above the plain, at Helankou, ancient pastoralists cut petroglyphs into the sandstone, and those carvings are the best record there is of how they lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The rock is weathering under freeze-thaw and wet-dry cycles, and many of the images are now at risk of vanishing altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ningxia is the Hui Autonomous Region and holds about a third of all China’s Hui, a people formed of Han families joined with Arab, Persian, Mongol and Uyghur ones, and whose presence here is traced back to the Tang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> East of the river the land runs into the Mu Us sands and the Ordos, desert grassland carrying drought-hardy shrubs and herbs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Arable land there is scarce enough that the desert grassland is widely broken up for maize, fertilised heavily and watered by pumping the groundwater several times a season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The region is also the recognised home ground of the goji berry, long held to be the place where that plant is properly grown, and its berries carry metabolic signatures particular to where they were raised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Qiangqiang Yang, Zeyu Wei, Xiaocong Qiu, and Zengfeng Zhao, “Beta Diversity Patterns and Drivers of Macroinvertebrate Communities in Major Rivers of Ningxia, China,” <i>Animals</i> 15, no. 14 (2025): 2034, https://doi.org/10.3390/ani15142034. [Open access]","Zhanping Wang, Juncang Tian, and Kepeng Feng, “Response of Runoff towards Land Use Changes in the Yellow River Basin in Ningxia, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0265931, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265931. [Open access]","Xiangchao Kong, Faguo He, Ran Lv, Lizhi Zhang, Xin Yang, and Tengfei Lu, “Experimental Study on the Degradation of Sandstone-Petroglyph Carriers in Helankou: Effects of Freeze-Thaw and Wet-Dry Cycles,” <i>iScience</i> 27, no. 8 (2024): 110576, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2024.110576. [Open access]","Li Zhang, Fei Zhan, Wenfang Zhang, Sanping Song, and Shisheng Zhu, “Forensic Characteristics of 38 Y-STR Loci in the Hui Population from Shaanxi Province, Northwest China,” <i>Heliyon</i> 10, no. 19 (2024): e38501, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e38501. [Open access]","Peng Kang, Jinpeng Hu, Yaqing Pan, Xuan Qu, Yichao Ran, Chenxi Yang, and Bingru Liu, “Response of Soil Fungal-Community Structure and Function to Land Conversion to Agriculture in Desert Grassland,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 15 (2024): 1413973, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2024.1413973. [Open access]","Yan Yan, Wei Ma, Yage Li, Chen Zhang, Fang Li, Tianqing Huang, Beibei Gao, Huihui Meng, Yunfei Hu, and Huan Wu, “Untargeted Metabolomics Reveals Region-Specific Metabolic Signatures and Discriminative Markers in Goji Berry (Lycium barbarum L.),” <i>Metabolites</i> 16, no. 5 (2026): 326, https://doi.org/10.3390/metabo16050326. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Xi_Xia_Imperial_Tombs_%2854154641924%29.jpg/1920px-Xi_Xia_Imperial_Tombs_%2854154641924%29.jpg","title":"the Western Xia tombs, Ningxia","desc":"The Western Xia tombs.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xi_Xia_Imperial_Tombs_(54154641924).jpg","alt":"Eroded earthen tomb mounds on a desert plain."}},
{"id":"gc-530","num":530,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Xining","answerText":"Xining","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kumbum built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1379 – 1942</span></div>","traditional":"西寧","hanzi":"西宁","pinyin":"Xīníng","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Qinghai","dot":"Xining"},"facts":[["Province","Qinghai"],["Population","2.47M (2020)"],["Rank in China","203rd largest"],["Population growth","+11.7% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Xining</b> is the capital of Qinghai and stands in the valley of the Huangshui River, on the north-eastern margin of the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, at an average elevation of about 2,261 metres (7,418 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ground is high in the north-west and low in the south-east, shallow hills and river terraces rather than open plain, and its districts are strung along the water because the mountains leave nowhere else for them to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That valley is the heart of the Hehuang, the low and relatively level corner of the province where farming is possible, and it has been under the administration of the central government since the Han dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Military-farming colonies planted there over many centuries made it the settled core of Qinghai, and the traditional villages around it are more thickly clustered than anywhere else on the Tibetan Plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of those villages were raised under the Ming and the Qing, though a few are older than the Yuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The city stood on the Qinghai route of the Silk Road, and the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road ran from it across the interior of the plateau to Lhasa, the only such route the records carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Tangfan and Tea Horse roads carried the same traffic along the plateau’s edge, and the Tuyuhun migration route through Amdo crossed here too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kumbum Monastery, in a narrow valley at Lushar in Huangzhong, was begun in 1379 to mark the birthplace of Tsongkhapa, who founded the Gelug school of Tibetan Buddhism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Building went on there for 563 years, until 1942, and it is one of the six great Gelug monasteries and the centre of Tibetan Buddhism in the province; under the Ming, when the court moved settlers into the Hehuang, its halls took on Han building practice beside Tibetan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Han communities are concentrated around the city while Tibetans hold the cold high country, Hui and Salar the Yellow River valley and Mongolians the dry grassland, six peoples that took their present shape under the Yuan, Ming and Qing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Zhenxu Ning, Shuzhen He, Xinghao Liao, Chunguang Ma, and Jing Wu, “Health Impacts of a Cold Wave and Its Economic Loss Assessment in China's High-Altitude City, Xining,” <i>Archives of Public Health</i> 82, no. 1 (2024): 52, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13690-024-01284-7. [Open access]","Shibin Ma, Haiquan Xu, Rongfang Xin, Li Huang, Yonghao Hou, Jia Wang, and Xuesong Yang, “Integrated Effects of Land Cover, Topography, Urban Morphology, and PM2.5 on Land Surface Temperature,” <i>PLOS One</i> 21, no. 5 (2026): e0344297, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0344297. [Open access]","Liu Jin, Zongqi Wang, and Xiaohong Chen, “Spatial Distribution Characteristics and Influencing Factors of Traditional Villages on the Tibetan Plateau in China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 20 (2022): 13170, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192013170. [Open access]","Zhuoma Lancuo, Guangliang Hou, Changjun Xu, Yuying Liu, Yan Zhu, Wen Wang, and Yongkun Zhang, “Simulating the Route of the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road for One Branch of the Silk Road across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau,” <i>PLOS One</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0226970, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0226970. [Open access]","Jing Zhang, Yunying Ren, and Xiaofan An, “Temporal and Spatial Features of the Palace Building Space of Qinghai’s Kumbum Monastery and Its Evolution,” <i>PLOS One</i> 17, no. 10 (2022): e0262155, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0262155. [Open access]","Xin Jia, Harry F. Lee, Mengchun Cui, Chao Liu, Lin Zeng, Ricci P. H. Yue, Yang Zhao, and Huayu Lu, “Habitat Variability and Ethnic Diversity in Northern Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7, no. 1 (2017): 918, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-01008-8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Xining_skyline.jpg/1920px-Xining_skyline.jpg","title":"Xining","desc":"Xining, seen from a distance.","credit":"NoGhost, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xining_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Xining."}},
{"id":"gc-030","num":30,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Qinghai","answerText":"Qinghai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8,500 – 7,300 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tibetan Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 842 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"青海","pinyin":"Qīnghǎi","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Qinghai"},"facts":[["Capital","Xining"],["Population","5.92M (2020)"],["Largest city","Xining"],["Area","722,300 km² (278,900 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Qinghai</b> is a province of the north-eastern Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, and it takes its name from Qinghai Lake, the largest inland saltwater lake in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Humans first reached the plateau some 21,000 years ago but settled its northern part permanently only after about 8,000 years ago, moving up along the Yellow River as barley and sheep made the heights liveable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Shalongka, in the upper Yellow River valley in the province’s east, microlithic hunter-gatherers kept relatively fixed seasonal camps between 8,500 and 7,300 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Millet farmers of the Yangshao culture then came up from the Loess Plateau and founded the earliest settled villages on the plateau, at that same site between about 5,900 and 5,100 years ago, and the Qijia culture followed some 4,100 to 3,900 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Central government reached the Huangshui valley under the Han, and the military-farming colonies planted then made the Hehuang, the corner where that river meets the Yellow River, the settled core of the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> From 313 to 663 CE the region was held by the Tuyuhun, a polity founded by nomadic-pastoral Xianbei people who had come from the north-east, until the armies of the Tibetan Empire annexed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That empire, the Tubo, ran from 618 to 842 and moved people into the region in numbers to hold it, and out of that came the Amdo Tibetan identity and dialect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Tang-Tibet Ancient Road ran roughly 2,240 kilometres (1,390 miles) from Chang’an to Lhasa across the province, and is the only branch of the Silk Road recorded through the interior of the plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cemeteries at Dulan show that the Qinghai route was a major hub of that traffic, and the Tangfan and Tea Horse roads later carried goods and people the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Under the Ming the court moved settlers into the Hehuang to repair the damage of war, and the villages of that valley are still the thickest cluster of traditional settlement on the plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Jiesheng Sun, Yuanyuan Ding, Yong Wang, and Chongyi E, “Vegetation Dynamics and Its Driving Force in the Qinghai Lake Basin, China,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 16 (2025): 1691672, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2025.1691672. [Open access]","Xin Jia, Harry F. Lee, Mengchun Cui, Chao Liu, Lin Zeng, Ricci P. H. Yue, Yang Zhao, and Huayu Lu, “Habitat Variability and Ethnic Diversity in Northern Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7, no. 1 (2017): 918, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-01008-8. [Open access]","Jingyi Gao, Guangliang Hou, Yongming Xiao, Chongyi E, Haicheng Wei, Yongjuan Sun, Manping Sun, Hongpan Xue, Zhuoma Wende, Sunmei Jin, and Xiaoliang Chen, “Vegetation History and Survival Patterns of the Earliest Village on the Qinghai–Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 903192, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2022.903192. [Open access]","Kongyang Zhu, Panxin Du, Jiyuan Li, Jianlin Zhang, Xiaojun Hu, Hailiang Meng, Liang Chen, Boyan Zhou, Xiaomin Yang, Jianxue Xiong, Edward Allen, Xiaoying Ren, Yi Ding, Yiran Xu, Xin Chang, Yao Yu, Sheng Han, Guanghui Dong, Chuan-Chao Wang, and Shaoqing Wen, “Cultural and Demic Co-Diffusion of Tubo Empire on Tibetan Plateau,” <i>iScience</i> 25, no. 12 (2022): 105636, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2022.105636. [Open access]","Zhuoma Lancuo, Guangliang Hou, Changjun Xu, Yuying Liu, Yan Zhu, Wen Wang, and Yongkun Zhang, “Simulating the Route of the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road for One Branch of the Silk Road across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau,” <i>PLOS One</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0226970, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0226970. [Open access]","Liu Jin, Zongqi Wang, and Xiaohong Chen, “Spatial Distribution Characteristics and Influencing Factors of Traditional Villages on the Tibetan Plateau in China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 20 (2022): 13170, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192013170. [Open access]","Jing Zhang, Yunying Ren, and Xiaofan An, “Temporal and Spatial Features of the Palace Building Space of Qinghai’s Kumbum Monastery and Its Evolution,” <i>PLOS One</i> 17, no. 10 (2022): e0262155, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0262155. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/QinghaiHu1.jpg/1920px-QinghaiHu1.jpg","title":"Qinghai Lake","desc":"Qinghai Lake.","credit":"Andreas Gruschke (Freiburg), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QinghaiHu1.jpg","alt":"A deep blue lake with hills along the far shore."}},
{"id":"gc-531","num":531,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Lhasa","answerText":"Lhasa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tubo empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 842 CE</span></div>","traditional":"拉薩","hanzi":"拉萨","pinyin":"Lāsà","translations":"","difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Tibet","dot":"Lhasa"},"facts":[["Region","Tibet"],["Population","868k (2020)"],["Rank in China","282nd largest"],["Population growth","+55.1% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Lhasa</b> is the capital of the Tibet Autonomous Region and stands in the south-central Qinghai-Tibet Plateau at about 3,650 metres (11,980 feet) above the sea, ringed by mountains with the Lhasa River running through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That river gives the city its name and its water, running 568 kilometres (353 miles), and its basin holds a great deal of water by the standards of the plateau, little of which is drawn on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The climate is cold semi-arid, with dry winters and springs, a short summer in which the rain arrives, and air thin enough to let solar radiation and ultraviolet through with little to stop them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The city is the political and economic centre of the region and the spiritual heartland of Tibetan Buddhism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Potala Palace, the Jokhang Temple and Sera Monastery all stand here, symbols of faith and places of rest at once, and the religion is worked so deeply into the streets and temple precincts that it can be studied in them directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The empire whose road ended here began in the middle of the 7th century, when more than a dozen Tibetan tribes were united under the Bod tribe of the Yarlung valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That empire, the Tubo, ran from 618 to 842, annexed the Tuyuhun in 663 and threw its weight across the whole of western China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Tang-Tibet Ancient Road ended here, running from Chang’an with post stations set about 22 kilometres (14 miles) apart, the distance the Tang and Tubo caravanserai system worked to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is the only branch of the Silk Road recorded through the interior of the plateau, and 512 archaeological sites of the Tubo period are known across Tibet and Qinghai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tea will not grow at this height, so from about the 8th century the Tea Horse Road carried it up from the Han-farmed lowlands in trade for horses, and brick tea, butter tea and tsampa, a food of cooked barley, are still much of what is eaten here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Zimeng Guo, Junyu Chen, Hanyue Yu, Qiangying Zhang, Bu Duo, and Xiaomei Cui, “Characteristics, Sources and Potential Ecological Risk of Atmospheric Microplastics in Lhasa City,” <i>Environmental Geochemistry and Health</i> 46, no. 9 (2024): 347, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10653-024-02125-w. [Open access]","Lingling Wu, Yufeng Ren, Peng Huang, Shiyu Yuan, Chun Zhou, Zhongshun Gu, Yinan Guo, and Li Zhou, “Evaluation of GSMaP and MSWEP Precipitation Products for Runoff Simulation in the Lhasa River Basin,” <i>PLOS One</i> 21, no. 2 (2026): e0342995, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0342995. [Open access]","Dongzhu Gadan and Zaisheng Zhang, “Spiritual Places: Spatial Recognition of Tibetan Buddhist Spiritual Perception,” <i>PLOS One</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301087, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301087. [Open access]","Zhuoma Lancuo, Guangliang Hou, Changjun Xu, Yuying Liu, Yan Zhu, Wen Wang, and Yongkun Zhang, “Simulating the Route of the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road for One Branch of the Silk Road across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau,” <i>PLOS One</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0226970, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0226970. [Open access]","Kongyang Zhu, Panxin Du, Jiyuan Li, Jianlin Zhang, Xiaojun Hu, Hailiang Meng, Liang Chen, Boyan Zhou, Xiaomin Yang, Jianxue Xiong, Edward Allen, Xiaoying Ren, Yi Ding, Yiran Xu, Xin Chang, Yao Yu, Sheng Han, Guanghui Dong, Chuan-Chao Wang, and Shaoqing Wen, “Cultural and Demic Co-Diffusion of Tubo Empire on Tibetan Plateau,” <i>iScience</i> 25, no. 12 (2022): 105636, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2022.105636. [Open access]","Cai Wen, Qing Zhang, Fei Xie, and Jixin Jiang, “Brick Tea Consumption and Its Relationship with Fluorosis in Tibetan Areas,” <i>Frontiers in Nutrition</i> 9 (2022): 1030344, https://doi.org/10.3389/fnut.2022.1030344. [Open access]","Zhefei Zeng, Ziyi Liang, Yan Chen, Qi Shu, Junru Li, Norzin Tso, Mengyan Chen, Shutong Zhang, Xin Tan, La Qiong, and Junwei Wang, “Adaptation Analysis of Two Asteraceae Invasive Plants in Lhasa, Tibet,” <i>PeerJ</i> 13 (2025): e19667, https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.19667. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Lhasa_from_the_Pabonka_Monastery.JPG/1920px-Lhasa_from_the_Pabonka_Monastery.JPG","title":"Lhasa","desc":"Lhasa, seen from a distance.","credit":"Qeqertaq, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lhasa_from_the_Pabonka_Monastery.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Lhasa."}},
{"id":"gc-031","num":31,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tibet","answerText":"Tibet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">High plateau by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40,000 – 30,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tubo empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 842 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"西藏","pinyin":"Xīzàng","translations":"","difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Tibet"},"facts":[["Capital","Lhasa"],["Population","3.65M (2020)"],["Largest city","Lhasa"],["Area","1,228,400 km² (474,300 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Tibet</b> is an autonomous region holding the core of the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau in south-western China, its south-eastern canyons wet and sharply zoned by height, its north-western hinterland alpine and arid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaic hominins were on the plateau about 160,000 years ago, and modern humans had reached its high country, above 4,500 metres (14,800 feet), between roughly 40,000 and 30,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Handprints and footprints at Quesang in the south put people there at least 20,000 years ago, but that presence was intermittent, and permanent occupation dates only from the early Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Millet farmers from the Yellow River basin took up cold-tolerant barley and carried it onto the plateau between about 3,600 and 3,300 years ago, and rather than replacing the foragers already there they lived alongside them for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ancestry of Tibetans today traces mainly back to those Neolithic farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the middle of the 7th century more than a dozen Tibetan tribes were united under the Bod tribe of the Yarlung valley, and the Tubo empire that grew out of that ran from 618 to 842 and threw its weight across the whole of western China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its armies annexed the Tuyuhun in 663 and moved people north-east in numbers to hold the ground, and out of that came the Amdo Tibetan identity and dialect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Tang-Tibet Ancient Road belongs to the same period, running from Chang’an to Lhasa with post stations set about 22 kilometres (14 miles) apart, and it is the only branch of the Silk Road recorded through the interior of the plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tea will not grow at these heights, so from about the 8th century the Tea Horse Road carried it up from the Han-farmed lowlands in trade for horses, and brick tea, butter tea and tsampa, a food of cooked barley, are still much of what is eaten here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tibetan Buddhism shapes the built landscape as much as it does belief, and its greatest houses stand at Lhasa: the Potala Palace, the Jokhang Temple and Sera Monastery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Guanglin He, Mengge Wang, Xing Zou, Pengyu Chen, Zheng Wang, Yan Liu, Hongbin Yao, Lan-Hai Wei, Renkuan Tang, Chuan-Chao Wang, and Hui-Yuan Yeh, “Peopling History of the Tibetan Plateau and Multiple Waves of Admixture of Tibetans Inferred from Both Ancient and Modern Genome-Wide Data,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 12 (2021): 725243, https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2021.725243. [Open access]","Luo Guo, Xiaoyan Zhang, Zhan Lin, Zhuang Cui, Changping Li, and Su Wu, “Spatiotemporal Analysis of Plague Risk in Tibet: Multi-Source Data-Driven Ensemble Model Development and Validation,” <i>One Health</i> 22 (2026): 101354, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.onehlt.2026.101354. [Open access]","Kongyang Zhu, Panxin Du, Jiyuan Li, Jianlin Zhang, Xiaojun Hu, Hailiang Meng, Liang Chen, Boyan Zhou, Xiaomin Yang, Jianxue Xiong, Edward Allen, Xiaoying Ren, Yi Ding, Yiran Xu, Xin Chang, Yao Yu, Sheng Han, Guanghui Dong, Chuan-Chao Wang, and Shaoqing Wen, “Cultural and Demic Co-Diffusion of Tubo Empire on Tibetan Plateau,” <i>iScience</i> 25, no. 12 (2022): 105636, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2022.105636. [Open access]","Zhuoma Lancuo, Guangliang Hou, Changjun Xu, Yuying Liu, Yan Zhu, Wen Wang, and Yongkun Zhang, “Simulating the Route of the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road for One Branch of the Silk Road across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau,” <i>PLOS One</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0226970, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0226970. [Open access]","Cai Wen, Qing Zhang, Fei Xie, and Jixin Jiang, “Brick Tea Consumption and Its Relationship with Fluorosis in Tibetan Areas,” <i>Frontiers in Nutrition</i> 9 (2022): 1030344, https://doi.org/10.3389/fnut.2022.1030344. [Open access]","Dongzhu Gadan and Zaisheng Zhang, “Spiritual Places: Spatial Recognition of Tibetan Buddhist Spiritual Perception,” <i>PLOS One</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301087, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301087. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Mt._Everest_from_Gokyo_Ri_November_5%2C_2012.jpg/1920px-Mt._Everest_from_Gokyo_Ri_November_5%2C_2012.jpg","title":"Mount Everest, Tibet","desc":"The north face of Mount Everest.","credit":"Rdevany, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mt._Everest_from_Gokyo_Ri_November_5,_2012.jpg","alt":"A snow-covered peak rising above a glacier."}},
{"id":"gr-401","num":401,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Darius dismissed Mardonius and gave the command to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Mede and the king's own nephew, with orders to enslave Athens and Eretria and bring them before him.","questions":["Rather than round Athos where Mardonius' fleet was wrecked, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sailed from Samos island to island with 600 triremes, burning Naxos and sparing Delos on the way.","Herodotus has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry the enslaved Eretrians home to Darius after Marathon, while Ctesias says the Mede of the pair died on the field and Athens kept his body."],"answer":"Datis and Artaphernes","answerText":"Datis and Artaphernes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Expedition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 triremes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege of Eretria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Delos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 talents of frankincense</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Datis and Artaphernes</b> were the commanders Darius appointed for the expedition of 490 BCE against Athens and Eretria, after dismissing Mardonius, whose fleet had been wrecked rounding Athos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls Datis a Mede by birth and Artaphernes the king's own nephew, and gives their orders as to enslave both cities and bring the captives before Darius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They mustered on the Aleian plain in Cilicia, loaded horses into transports the king's tributary subjects had been ordered to build the year before, and sailed to Ionia with six hundred triremes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From Samos they crossed the Icarian sea island by island instead of following the Thracian coast, which Herodotus attributes to their fear of rounding Athos a second time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How large that force was is the first thing the sources fall out over, since Nepos counts five hundred ships with two hundred thousand foot and ten thousand horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Naxos was attacked first, its city and temples burnt, but at Delos the fleet anchored off Rheneia and Datis promised no harm to the birthplace of the two gods, burning three hundred talents of frankincense on the altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carystus was besieged until it came over, and Eretria held its walls for six days before two of its own leading men betrayed it on the seventh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plato remembered the campaign as Datis' alone, sent against Athens and Eretria with orders to bring them back in chains and a warning that failure would cost him his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus preserves a message Datis is said to have sent to Athens, claiming descent from Medus and demanding back a kingdom he said the Athenians had taken from that ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beaten at Marathon, they shipped the Eretrians to Susa, and Herodotus has both men live on, Artaphernes leading Lydians in Xerxes' invasion and Datis' sons its cavalry, while Ctesias has Datis killed on the field and the Athenians refusing to give back his body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Sacred_Way%2C_Delos_03.jpg/1280px-Sacred_Way%2C_Delos_03.jpg","title":"The Sacred Way at Delos","desc":"The processional avenue through the sanctuary of Apollo on Delos, lined with the bases of dedications. Datis anchored his fleet across the water at Rheneia rather than land here, and burnt three hundred talents of frankincense on the altar of the island where the two gods were born.","credit":"Photograph by Bernard Gagnon, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacred_Way,_Delos_03.jpg","alt":"A wide paved avenue of pale stone running away from the viewer between rows of low marble blocks and broken column shafts, with tumbled masonry in the foreground and a bare brown hillside behind."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.94–6.102, 6.115, 6.118–6.119, 7.74, 7.88, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=94. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 10.27, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=10:chapter=27. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 4, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 311, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Photius, <i>Bibliotheca</i> 72, epitomising Ctesias, <i>Persica</i>, in <i>The Library of Photius</i>, vol. 1, trans. J. H. Freese (London: SPCK, 1920), 98–99, https://archive.org/details/libraryp01phot. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 3.698c, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=698c. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","iran"]},
{"id":"gr-402","num":402,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Two leading citizens opened the gates on the seventh day and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed, with the city's temples burnt in revenge for Sardis and its people shipped to Darius as slaves.","questions":["Plato says Datis' men swept the island with a drag-net after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a later account counts 780 captives and has most of the population escape to the mountains.","Athens sent 4,000 settlers to help and then withdrew them on Eretrian advice, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was six days of fighting on the walls and no relief at all."],"answer":"sack of Eretria","answerText":"sack of Eretria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walls held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Betrayed by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 leading citizens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Captives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">780, by one later count</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>sack of Eretria</b> in 490 BCE was the destruction of the Euboean city by the Persian expedition under Datis and Artaphernes, ordered by Darius in return for its part in the burning of Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eretria had sent five ships to the Ionian Revolt, and when the Persians sailed against it the city asked Athens for help and received the four thousand settlers holding the land of the Chalcidian horse-breeders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eretrian counsel was divided between abandoning the city for the heights of Euboea and betraying it, and when Aeschines told the Athenians how matters stood they withdrew across the strait to Oropus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persians landed at Temenos, Choereae and Aegilea, put their horses ashore, and attacked the walls, where for six days many fell on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the seventh two men of repute, Euphorbus son of Alcimachus and Philagrus son of Cineas, gave up the city, and Pausanias later set the pair in his catalogue of those who betrayed Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The temples were plundered and burnt in return for the temple burnt at Sardis, and the townspeople were enslaved as Darius had commanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plato has the news reach Athens as a boast that not one Eretrian had got away, Datis' soldiers having swept the territory with a drag-net, a story he says terrified the Greeks whether true or not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo knew the razed ground as Old Eretria, its foundations still visible, with the later city built on part of the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Philostratus preserves a very different count from the settlement's own descendants: 780 taken, of whom about four hundred men and perhaps ten women survived the march inland, most of Eretria having fled to Caphereus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus says Darius did the captives no harm but settled them at Ardericca in Cissia near Susa, where they kept their old language into his own day, and their graves carried Greek verses on how far from home they lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_ancient_Eretria_on_January_16%2C_2020.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_ancient_Eretria_on_January_16%2C_2020.jpg","title":"The fortification wall of ancient Eretria","desc":"Remains of the fortification wall of ancient Eretria, its blocks running away across the excavated ground toward the modern town. Herodotus says the walls were strongly attacked and that for six days many fell on both sides, before two leading citizens gave the city up on the seventh.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_ancient_Eretria_on_January_16,_2020.jpg","alt":"A course of large squared limestone blocks standing in a grassy field, with the line of a ruined wall and scattered masonry running away to the right, and a hillside town under a blue sky behind."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.77, 5.99, 6.31, 6.99–6.101, 6.119, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=100. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 3.698d, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=698d. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 7.10.1–7.10.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=7:chapter=10. [Open access]","Philostratus, <i>Life of Apollonius of Tyana</i> 1.23–1.24, trans. F. C. Conybeare, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1912), 67–73, https://archive.org/details/lifeofapollonius00philuoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.1.10, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0198:book=10:chapter=1:section=10. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"]},
{"id":"gr-403","num":403,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Ten generals were split until the polemarch's vote sent them out, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with 6,400 dead on one side and 192 on the other.","questions":["Herodotus says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first time Greeks charged an enemy at a run, eight stades of open ground crossed by men the Persians took for madmen.","The Spartans would not march before the full moon and arrived after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was over, so Athens and a thousand Plataeans had fought it alone."],"answer":"Battle of Marathon","answerText":"Battle of Marathon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 6,400</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">192</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Charge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 stades</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Marathon</b> was fought in 490 BCE on the coastal plain of eastern Attica, where an Athenian army met the Persian force Datis and Artaphernes had brought over after destroying Eretria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hippias, the exiled tyrant of Athens, led the Persians to that plain because it was the ground in Attica best suited to riding horses and the nearest to Eretria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens sent to Sparta for help, but the Spartans would not march before the full moon, and their two thousand men reached Attica after the fighting and went to Marathon to look at the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Plataeans came in full force to the Athenian camp at the precinct of Heracles, and Nepos gives them as a thousand men in an army of ten thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nepos also has the Athenians fell trees to break up the Persian cavalry, and puts a hundred thousand foot and ten thousand horse against them, a figure Herodotus never offers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The ten generals were split on whether to fight, and the polemarch Callimachus held the eleventh vote, which Miltiades asked him to cast between enslaving Athens and leaving her free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line was drawn as long as the Persian one, leaving the centre a few ranks deep and both wings strong, and the men crossed the eight stades at a run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persians and Sacae broke the centre and pursued inland, but both wings won, let the beaten go, closed on the breakthrough and drove it to the sea, where seven ships were taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus counts about 6,400 dead among the invaders and 192 Athenians, Callimachus among them; Pausanias saw their grave on the plain, with a second for the Plataeans and the slaves who fought that day beside their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides says burial on the field was an honour Athens gave to no other war dead, and a funeral oration a century later told it as a victory won without waiting for allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Plain_of_Marathon_4.jpg/1280px-Plain_of_Marathon_4.jpg","title":"The plain of Marathon","desc":"The plain of Marathon, looking south-east toward the bay from beside the Tomb of the Athenians. It is the flatness that matters: Herodotus says Hippias brought the Persians here because it was the ground in Attica best suited to riding horses.","credit":"Photograph by Bob K, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0 1.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plain_of_Marathon_4.jpg","alt":"A wide, flat field of dry yellow and green grass under a deep blue sky, with a line of olive trees along the far edge and low hills faint on the horizon."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.102, 6.106, 6.108–6.117, 6.120, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=112. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.32.3–1.32.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=32. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 5, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 312–13, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.34.5, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=34. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Funeral Oration</i> 23–25, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0154:speech=2:section=23. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","event","history","warfare","greece","iran"]},
{"id":"gr-404","num":404,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Acquitted of tyranny in the Chersonese and then elected general, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked the polemarch for the vote that sent Athens out to fight at Marathon.","questions":["Seventy ships were voted to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a promise of easy gold, and the failure at Paros brought a fine of fifty talents and a thigh wound that killed him.","Driven from the Chersonese by the Phoenician fleet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Athens to be tried for tyranny, was acquitted, and was elected one of the ten generals who fought at Marathon."],"answer":"Miltiades","answerText":"Miltiades","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Marathon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chersonese guard</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Paros</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70 ships</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 talents</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Miltiades</b> was the Athenian general who commanded at Marathon in 490 BCE, son of Cimon and heir to a family lordship in the Thracian Chersonese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sons of Pisistratus sent him out when his brother Stesagoras died there, and he took the peninsula by seizing its leading men, kept a guard of five hundred, and married Hegesipyle, daughter of the Thracian king Olorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He held it as a subject of Darius, and Herodotus has him urge the Ionian tyrants at the Danube bridge to break it and leave the king to the Scythians, until Histiaeus talked them round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scythians drove him out and the Dolonci brought him back; when the Phoenician fleet came he fled for good, losing one ship and the son aboard it, whom Darius then treated well and married to a Persian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Athens his enemies prosecuted him for tyranny in the Chersonese; he was acquitted and elected one of the ten generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Marathon the generals were split, and it was Miltiades who put the choice to the polemarch Callimachus, whose vote sent the army out to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin makes him the man who argued against waiting for Sparta, and Nepos the one whose authority carried the Athenians out of the city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His reward, Nepos says, was to stand first among the ten in the Marathon painting in the Painted Stoa, urging his men on, and Pausanias found him there and a monument of his own on the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He then got seventy ships on a promise of gold, besieged Paros without taking it, and came home with a ruined thigh to be prosecuted; the people spared his life and fined him fifty talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus has him die of the wound with his son Cimon paying the fine, where Diodorus and Nepos have him die in the state prison unable to pay, Cimon giving himself up to prison to get the body back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Helmet_of_Miltiades_050911.jpg/1280px-Helmet_of_Miltiades_050911.jpg","title":"The helmet with Miltiades' name","desc":"A bronze Corinthian helmet in the Archaeological Museum of Olympia, with the name ΜΙΛΤΙΑΔΕΣ cut in Greek letters along the lower edge of the cheekpiece.","credit":"Photograph by Oren Rozen, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helmet_of_Miltiades_050911.jpg","alt":"A battered green-patinated bronze helmet in a museum case, seen from the side, its crown broken away and a line of shallow incised Greek capitals running along the lower edge of the cheekpiece."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.137, 6.39–6.41, 6.103–6.104, 6.109–6.110, 6.132–6.136, 6.140, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=104. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 3–8, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 310–14, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.9, 2.15, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 27, 37, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 10.30, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=10:chapter=30. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.15.3, 1.32.4, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=15. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","ruler","history","warfare","greece","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-405","num":405,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The eleventh vote belonged to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Miltiades told him it lay with him to enslave Athens or to leave her free and first among the cities of Greece.","questions":["Holding the right wing as the polemarch's place, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed in the fighting at the ships, and a marble Nike on an inscribed column went up for him on the Acropolis.","Pausanias calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the commander-in-chief the Athenians had elected, and found him among the most conspicuous figures in the Marathon painting in the Painted Stoa."],"answer":"Callimachus of Aphidnae","answerText":"Callimachus of Aphidnae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Polemarch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Casting vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11th of 11</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at Marathon, 490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nike monument</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 fragments</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Callimachus of Aphidnae</b> was the Athenian polemarch of 490 BCE, whose vote sent the army out to fight at Marathon and who was killed in the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The polemarch was the second of the old Athenian offices, added, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says, because some of the kings had proved cowardly in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the reform that work dates to 504, the ten generals were elected one from each tribe while the whole army stayed under the polemarch's command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus explains the arrangement differently, as an eleventh vote made by ancient custom equal in weight to the ten, and names the man who held it at Marathon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The generals were split on whether to fight, and Miltiades told him the choice was his: to enslave Athens, or to leave her free and first among the cities of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He voted to attack, and took the right wing when the line was drawn up, which Herodotus says was then the polemarch's place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was killed in the fighting at the ships, and Pausanias, who calls him the commander-in-chief the Athenians had elected, found him among the most conspicuous figures in the Marathon painting in the Painted Stoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A marble Nike on an inscribed column was set up for him on the Acropolis, reassembled by the Acropolis Museum out of 28 fragments of Parian and Pentelic marble and dated after 490.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What survives of its text names him polemarch of the Athenians, who fought for the Greeks at Marathon, and calls the thing a monument for the sons of the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The office outlived the command: Aristotle's polemarch no longer led armies but sacrificed to Artemis the Huntress, to whom, Xenophon says, Athens had vowed a goat for every enemy killed at Marathon and still paid five hundred a year, and he arranged the funeral games for the city's war dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Ancient_Greek_sculptures_of_Attica_03.jpg/1280px-Ancient_Greek_sculptures_of_Attica_03.jpg","title":"The Nike of Kallimachos","desc":"The monument dedicated for Callimachus on the Athenian Acropolis, standing in the Acropolis Museum: a winged Nike and its inscribed column, put back together from 28 fragments and mounted on a modern armature.","credit":"Photograph by Deiadameian, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Greek_sculptures_of_Attica_03.jpg","alt":"Battered white marble fragments of a winged figure and a column, mounted one above another on a tall steel rod in a museum gallery, with visitors below and a concrete pillar behind."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.109–6.111, 6.114, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=109. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 3.2, 22.2, 58.1, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22:section=2. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.15.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=15. [Open access]","Acropolis Museum, “The ‘Nike of Kallimachos’,” accessed 3 September 2026, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/nike-kallimachos. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Anabasis</i> 3.2.12, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0202:book=3:chapter=2:section=12. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-406","num":406,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The generals sent <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Sparta, and he was there the day after leaving Athens, only to be told that the law forbade marching before the full moon.","questions":["Above Tegea the god Pan called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by name and asked why Athens paid him no attention, which is why a precinct of Pan stands below the Acropolis.","The modern race is named after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though Plutarch gives the run from the battlefield to Thersippus or to Eucles and the earliest source sends him to Sparta instead."],"answer":"Pheidippides","answerText":"Pheidippides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">To Sparta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sparta reached</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 day after leaving Athens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spartan delay</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th day of the month</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Pheidippides</b> was the Athenian long-distance runner sent to Sparta to ask for help when the Persians landed at Marathon in 490 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, the earliest account, calls him Philippides, a herald who made long-distance running his calling, and says he was in Sparta on the day after leaving Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Spartans agreed to send help but would not break their law: it was the ninth day of the rising month, and they could not march out before the moon was full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin instead has them held at home four days by a religious scruple and the Athenians deciding not to wait, which is the same delay told as an Athenian choice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nepos calls him Phidippides and puts him among the couriers the Greeks called hemerodromoi, the day-runners who could cover great distances between sunrise and sunset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On the way, above Tegea on Mount Parthenium, he said Pan had called him by name and asked why the Athenians paid him no attention when he had often served them and would again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They believed him, and when they were prosperous they made a precinct of Pan beneath the Acropolis and kept his goodwill with yearly sacrifices and a torch-race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias saw the sanctuary on Mount Parthenium where Athenians and Tegeans agreed the meeting had happened, and reports that Pan promised to come to Marathon and fight on their side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The run everyone remembers is a different one, and no ancient writer gives it to him: Plutarch says the news of the victory was carried back by Thersippus of Eroeadae, or, as most had it, by Eucles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eucles is said to have run in armour straight from the field, burst in on the leading men, managed only 'Hail, we are victorious' and died on the spot, and it is that course the modern race is run on under Pheidippides' name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Panas_cave_7233916.JPG/1280px-Panas_cave_7233916.JPG","title":"The caves on the north slope of the Acropolis","desc":"Openings in the rock face below the Acropolis at Athens, one of them the cave of Pan. Herodotus says the Athenians gave Pan a precinct here, with a yearly sacrifice and a torch-race, after the runner reported that the god had spoken to him on the road to Sparta.","credit":"Photograph by C messier, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0 1.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panas_cave_7233916.JPG","alt":"A pale limestone cliff face in bright sun with three dark cave mouths opening at its foot, dry scrub growing from the rock and a modern rope barrier at the lower right."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.105–6.106, 6.120, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=105. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.28.4, 8.54.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=8:chapter=54. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>On the Glory of Athens</i> 347C, trans. Frank Cole Babbitt, Loeb Classical Library, LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/De_gloria_Atheniensium*.html. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 4, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 311, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.9, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 27, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-407","num":407,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The 192 Athenian dead were buried under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> where they fell, an honour Thucydides says the city gave to no other war dead of its own.","questions":["Pausanias saw slabs on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> naming the dead by tribe, and a second grave beside it for the Plataeans and for the slaves who fought that day.","The men under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were worshipped as heroes by the people of Marathon, who also honoured a ploughman the oracle named Echetlaeus after he vanished from the field."],"answer":"Soros","answerText":"Soros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenians in it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">192</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 6,400, no grave found</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Soros</b> is the burial mound raised on the plain of Marathon over the Athenians who fell in the battle of 490 BCE, and Pausanias saw it standing there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus counts 192 Athenian dead, out of an army that had been drawn up tribe by tribe with the polemarch on the right wing and the Plataeans last on the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burying them where they fell was an exception: Thucydides says Athens laid its war dead in the public cemetery in the most beautiful suburb of the city, and Marathon was its one exception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ordinary rite was elaborate, with the bones laid out for three days, then carried in procession in cypress coffins, one for each tribe, and an empty bier borne for the missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribal arrangement went out to Marathon with them, for Pausanias says the mound carried slabs giving the names of the dead according to their tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Beside it stood a second grave, for the Boeotian Plataeans and for the slaves, who, Pausanias says, fought then for the first time alongside their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also saw a trophy of white marble on the plain, the kind of thing Lysias says the Athenians set up on their own soil on behalf of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Of the Persian dead he could find nothing: the Athenians said they had buried them, as divine law requires, but he saw neither mound nor trace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The men under the mound were worshipped as heroes by the people of Marathon, along with Marathon himself and Heracles, and an oracle told them to honour as a hero the ploughman Echetlaeus, who killed many of the enemy and vanished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city's care for its war dead outlasted them, since the polemarch was still arranging the funeral games for the fallen in Aristotle's day, and Pausanias adds that horses and fighting men are heard on the plain every night, though nobody who goes to hear them profits by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Marathon_Tomb_of_the_Athenians_2.jpg/1280px-Marathon_Tomb_of_the_Athenians_2.jpg","title":"The Soros on the plain of Marathon","desc":"The burial mound of the Athenian dead, standing on the flat ground of the plain where the battle was fought. Pausanias saw slabs on it giving the names of the fallen by tribe; none is on it now.","credit":"Photograph by Tomisti, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marathon_Tomb_of_the_Athenians_2.jpg","alt":"A broad, smooth, dome-shaped mound of dry yellow grass rising from flat open ground against a clear blue sky, with a few small shrubs in front of it and cypresses to the left."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.111, 6.117, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=117. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.34.1–2.34.5, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=34. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.32.3–1.32.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=32. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 58.1, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=58:section=1. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Funeral Oration</i> 25, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0154:speech=2:section=25. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","warfare","greece","attica"]},
{"id":"gr-408","num":408,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus reports the charge that the Alcmaeonids arranged the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to bring the beaten Persian fleet round Sunium to an undefended Athens, and refuses to believe it.","questions":["A family accursed since Cylon's suppliants were killed at the altars was blamed for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and ostracism began to be used two years after the battle.","One thing Herodotus will not deny is that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> happened, and one thing he will not say is who was responsible for it."],"answer":"shield signal","answerText":"shield signal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First ostracism</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 years later</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Megacles exiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 year after that</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>shield signal</b> at Marathon is an accusation Herodotus reports and then refuses to believe, that somebody at Athens held up a shield to bring the beaten Persian fleet to the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the battle the Persians picked up the Eretrian captives they had left on an island and rounded Sunium, hoping to reach Athens before the army that had just beaten them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The charge at Athens was that the Alcmaeonids had arranged it, holding the shield up once the Persians were aboard their ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls this a wonder he cannot accept, since the family were tyrant-haters who had worked to bring the sons of Pisistratus down and, in his judgment, freed Athens more than Harmodius and Aristogeiton did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He allows that they might have had some grudge against the people and then dismisses it, since nobody at Athens was more esteemed or more honoured than they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What he will not give up is the fact itself: a shield was held up, he says, and that cannot be denied, but who did it he does not know and can say no further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The family had been accursed since the Cylonian affair, when suppliants were led from the altars and killed while the archon Megacles held the guard on the Acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch says Solon later persuaded the polluted men to stand trial before three hundred jurors, that the house of Megacles was condemned, the living banished and the bodies of the dead dug up and thrown beyond the border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The years after Marathon were the right weather for such a charge, since ostracism was first used two years later, on a kinsman of the tyrants, then on the Alcmaeonid Megacles son of Hippocrates, and in the fourth year on Xanthippus son of Ariphron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The taint outlived them all, and Plutarch records that Sparta later demanded Athens drive out the Cylonian pollution in order to be rid of Xanthippus' son Pericles, whose mother was of the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Attica_06-13_Sounion_05_Cape_Sounion.jpg/1280px-Attica_06-13_Sounion_05_Cape_Sounion.jpg","title":"Cape Sounion","desc":"The headland at the southern tip of Attica, where the land falls to open sea. Herodotus has the Persian fleet round this corner after the battle, hoping to reach Athens before the army that had beaten them at Marathon.","credit":"Photograph by A.Savin, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attica_06-13_Sounion_05_Cape_Sounion.jpg","alt":"A rocky headland covered in low grey-green scrub falling away to deep blue sea on the right, with a faint coastline on the horizon under a clear sky."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.115, 6.121–6.124, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=124. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.3–22.6, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22:section=3. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=126. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0063:chapter=12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 3, 33, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0055:chapter=33:section=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-409","num":409,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Sent home from Sparta with an olive crown and three hundred knights for escort, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reckoned by Herodotus the wisest man in Greece after Salamis.","questions":["Thucydides says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first who dared tell the Athenians to stick to the sea, and began the walls of Piraeus in his year as archon.","Ostracised and then condemned for dealing with Persia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended as the king's man at Magnesia, on fifty talents a year for his bread."],"answer":"Themistocles","answerText":"Themistocles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sparta's escort</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 knights</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Magnesia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 talents a year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Themistocles</b> was the Athenian who built the fleet that fought at Salamis and turned his city toward the sea, whom Thucydides calls the ablest judge of a sudden crisis of his generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says his family was too obscure to help him, his father Neocles being no conspicuous man and his mother an alien, and Nepos has him disinherited for living too freely before he threw himself into public affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As archon he began the walls of Piraeus, drawn by a place with three natural harbours, and Thucydides says he was the first who dared tell the Athenians to stick to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He read the oracle of the wooden wall as the ships, and Herodotus says that after Salamis he was reckoned by far the wisest man in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek commanders would not award the prize for valour, so he went to Sparta for the honour instead, and was sent home with an olive crown, the best chariot in Sparta and three hundred knights to escort him to the Tegean border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After the war he kept the Spartans talking while Athens rebuilt its walls in haste, out of whatever came to hand, tombstones and sculptured blocks among the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says the city tired of him as he kept reciting his own achievements to the assembly, and that he offended it further by building a temple of Artemis Best Counsellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was ostracised, then condemned in his absence for dealing with Persia, and went to the Persian king, spending the journey learning the language and the customs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides says he reached a standing at court that no Greek ever had, and was given Magnesia for his bread at fifty talents a year, Lampsacus for his wine and Myus for the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He died there of illness, Thucydides says, though the story went that he took poison, and Diodorus has him drink a bull's blood rather than lead a Persian army against Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Busto_di_temistocle%2C_da_originale_greco_del_V_secolo_ac%2C_dal_decumano_presso_il_casamento_del_temistocle.JPG/1280px-Busto_di_temistocle%2C_da_originale_greco_del_V_secolo_ac%2C_dal_decumano_presso_il_casamento_del_temistocle.JPG","title":"The herm of Themistocles","desc":"A Roman marble copy of a Greek portrait of the early 5th century BCE, in the archaeological museum at Ostia. The name THEMISTOKLES is cut in Greek letters across the breast, which is what ties the face to the man.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busto_di_temistocle,_da_originale_greco_del_V_secolo_ac,_dal_decumano_presso_il_casamento_del_temistocle.JPG","alt":"A white marble bust of a bearded man with a broad face, heavy jaw and short curled hair, cut off at the shoulders, with faint Greek capitals incised across the chest."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.143–7.144, 8.124–8.125, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=124. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.90–1.93, 1.135–1.138, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=138. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 1, 22, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=1:section=1. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Themistocles</i> 1–2, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 315–16, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.58, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=58. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","greece","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-410","num":410,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Rather than take ten drachmas a man from the Laurium silver, Athens spent it on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the ships built for a war with Aegina fought at Salamis instead.","questions":["The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> has the hundred talents from Maroneia lent out a talent each under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that a hundred rich men each built a trireme.","Plutarch says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew the charge that Themistocles had robbed the Athenians of spear and shield and brought them down to the rowing-pad and the oar."],"answer":"naval bill","answerText":"naval bill","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Passed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 483 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mine windfall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Share refused</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 drachmas a man</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 or 200, by different accounts</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>naval bill</b> was the measure by which Themistocles turned a windfall from the Attic silver mines into a war fleet instead of a payment to the citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the revenues from Laurium had brought great wealth into the treasury and each man was to have ten drachmas, until Themistocles persuaded the Athenians to build ships with the money instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> tells it differently: a strike at Maroneia gave the state a hundred talents, which Themistocles had lent to the hundred richest Athenians, a talent each, so that every one of them built a trireme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In that version he does not say what the money is for until the ships exist, and the state stays a creditor able to call the loans back in if the borrowers spend badly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The accounts differ on the size of the fleet as well, Herodotus giving two hundred ships where the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i>, Plutarch and Nepos all give a hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The argument used was not Persia: Plutarch says Themistocles worked on the city's bitter jealousy of Aegina rather than trying to frighten it with Darius, who was far off and inspired no serious fear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus agrees that the ships were built for the war with Aegina, and says it was that war which saved Greece by compelling the Athenians to become seamen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides allows both motives, naming the war with Aegina and the prospect of the barbarian invasion, and adds that the ships had no complete decks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nepos has the mine money going yearly to waste in the magistrates' hands until Themistocles got the hundred ships built, and puts their first use against Corcyra and the pirates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch also records the charge that followed the policy, that Themistocles had robbed his fellow-citizens of spear and shield and brought the people of Athens down to the rowing-pad and the oar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Athens_tetradrachm.jpg/1280px-Athens_tetradrachm.jpg","title":"An Athenian silver coin","desc":"Both faces of a silver coin of Athens in the Numismatic Museum there: the helmeted head of Athena on one side, her owl with an olive sprig and the letters ΑΘΕ on the other. Herodotus says the mine revenue was about to be shared out at ten drachmas a man before Themistocles had it spent on ships.","credit":"Photograph by Odysses, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_tetradrachm.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin side by side: on the left a helmeted profile head facing right, on the right a front-facing owl standing beside an olive sprig and three Greek letters."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.144, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=144. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22:section=7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 4, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=4:section=1. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Themistocles</i> 2, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 316, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.14, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=14. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","greece","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-411","num":411,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The silver from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was about to be shared out at ten drachmas a man before Athens voted to build warships with it instead.","questions":["Herodotus puts the find at Laurium and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> at Maroneia, but both are describing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that paid for the fleet of 483.","Nicias let out a thousand slaves at an obol a day each in the same mines as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Thucydides says twenty thousand deserted once Decelea was fortified."],"answer":"Laurion strike","answerText":"Laurion strike","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Strike</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 483 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Profit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nicias' slaves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000, at an obol a day</span><span class=\"dt-k\">After Decelea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000 slaves gone</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Laurion strike</b> of about 483 BCE was a rich new find in the silver mines of southern Attica, and its money is the money Athens spent on the fleet that fought at Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus names the source as the revenues from the mines at Laurium; the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts the find at Maroneia and the profit at a hundred talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What both agree on is the custom the windfall interrupted, that such a surplus was shared out among the citizens: ten drachmas a man in Herodotus, a proposal to distribute it in the <i>Constitution</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mines themselves were old by then, and Xenophon writes that nobody even attempts to date the beginning of the digging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He points out how small the spoil heaps are beside the untouched silver-bearing hills, and says the mines stand as they stood in his ancestors' day however many men have worked them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The digging was done by slaves in numbers, and Xenophon reports that Nicias once owned a thousand men there and let them out to Sosias the Thracian at an obol a day each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How much hung on that labour shows when it stopped: after Sparta fortified Decelea, Thucydides says more than twenty thousand slaves deserted, a great part of them craftsmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Xenophon writes his own proposal for the mines against the memory of that, recalling what the charge for slave labour had brought in before the trouble at Decelea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo, later still, says the silver mines of Attica were important at first and were exhausted by his own day, and that the workmen were putting the old refuse and slag back through the furnace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They got very pure silver that way, he says, because the earlier workmen had smelted unskilfully; and Herodotus adds that the ships the strike paid for were never used for the purpose they were built for, but came to serve Greece in her need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Level_Washery_in_Soureza_Lavrion_Greece.jpg/1280px-Level_Washery_in_Soureza_Lavrion_Greece.jpg","title":"An ore washery at Soureza","desc":"A level washery in the Soureza valley at Laurion, where crushed silver-bearing lead ore was washed to concentrate it: the flat floor and the channel running round it are both visible, with two visitors at the left for scale.","credit":"Photograph by Petros Tzeferis, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Level_Washery_in_Soureza_Lavrion_Greece.jpg","alt":"A large flat rectangular stone floor cut into a dry hillside, bounded by a shallow channel and low rubble walls, with pines around it and two people in sun hats standing at the left edge."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.144, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=144. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22:section=7. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 4, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0210%3Atext%3DWays%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 7.27, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=7:chapter=27. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.1.23, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=9:chapter=1:section=23. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","technology","greece","attica"]},
{"id":"gr-412","num":412,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the best and most just man in Athens, and has him cross from Aegina to warn Themistocles at Salamis that the fleet was encircled.","questions":["One of the ten generals at Marathon, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed his own day of supreme command to Miltiades, and later landed armed men on the islet of Psyttaleia.","After the allies turned from Sparta to Athens, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was chosen to assess the tribute, which Thucydides puts at 460 talents with the common treasury on Delos."],"answer":"Aristides","answerText":"Aristides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Marathon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First tribute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">460 talents, assessed 478 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Aristides</b> son of Lysimachus, of the Athenian deme Alopece, was the great rival of Themistocles in the generation that fought the Persian Wars, and later Greeks knew him by the surname 'the Just'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the name fastened on him because justice was the virtue he practised most continually, and thought it remarkable that a poor man should carry a title no king had ever coveted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether he was poor at all was already argued over: Demetrius of Phalerum reasoned from his archonship, drawn by lot among the richest property class, that he had money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Marathon he was one of the ten generals, and Plutarch has him give up his own day of supreme command to Miltiades and bring the rest to do the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Athenian Constitution</i> makes him and Themistocles the two heads of the people, the one trained in war and the other in politics, so that Athens used one as general and the other as counsellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It also records his ostracism in the years of the naval programme, and the recall of every exile three years later as Xerxes came on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus brings him back at Salamis, crossing from Aegina to call Themistocles, his bitter enemy, out of the quarrelling council to tell him the fleet was encircled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus breaks off there to say that, having inquired into the man's character, he had come to believe him the best and most just man in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the battle he took armed men from the Salamis shore, landed them on the islet of Psyttaleia and killed the Persians posted there; the next summer he led the eight thousand Athenians on the left of the line at Plataea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the allies turned from Sparta to Athens he was chosen to fix what each city should pay, an assessment Thucydides gives as 460 talents with the treasury on Delos; Nepos says he died too poor to pay for his own funeral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/The_island_of_Psyttaleia_on_August_20%2C_2021.jpg/1280px-The_island_of_Psyttaleia_on_August_20%2C_2021.jpg","title":"The islet of Psyttaleia","desc":"Psyttaleia seen across the water from the Saronic Gulf: a low, bare rock in the strait outside Salamis. Herodotus says Aristides took armed men off the Salamis shore, landed them here and killed every Persian on the islet.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_island_of_Psyttaleia_on_August_20,_2021.jpg","alt":"A long, low, treeless island lying across a stretch of dark blue sea, with pale rocky shores, a few small modern buildings along its spine and hills faint in the distance."},"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 1, 5–6, 26–27, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, in <i>Plutarch: The Parallel Lives</i>, vol. 2, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.7–22.8, 23.3–23.5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.79, 8.95, 9.28, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=79. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Aristides</i> 1–3, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 323–25, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","politics","warfare","greece","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-413","num":413,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 482 BCE a countryman who could not write handed the man himself a sherd, being tired of hearing him everywhere called the Just.","questions":["The Athenian Constitution puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the archonship of Nicomedes and says the exiles were all recalled three years later because Xerxes was marching on Attica.","Plutarch traces the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a story Themistocles set going, that his rival had abolished the public courts and set himself up as a monarch in all but the bodyguard."],"answer":"ostracism of Aristides","answerText":"ostracism of Aristides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 482 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sentence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recalled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd year after the vote</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Void below</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 sherds</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>ostracism of Aristides</b> was the vote, in the archonship of Nicomedes, that sent the Athenian his fellow citizens called 'the Just' out of the city for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The law was Cleisthenes', aimed at first against friends of the tyrants; the <i>Athenian Constitution</i> says that by the fourth year it was being turned on anyone who seemed too great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> explains the device as the resort of states that pursue equality above all things, banishing for fixed terms the men outstanding in wealth or popularity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch says liability fell on anyone above the common run in reputation, birth or eloquence, and that Themistocles set a story going that his rival had done away with the public courts and made himself a monarch in all but the bodyguard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people, high after Marathon, were vexed by anyone towering over them, and Plutarch adds that they gave their envy of his reputation the name of fear of tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Every voter scratched a name on a potsherd and carried it to a railed enclosure in the market place, where the archons counted; under six thousand sherds the vote was void.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch's story of the day is that an unlettered countryman handed Aristides his own sherd and asked him to write the name on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked what wrong he had suffered, the man said none at all, that he did not even know the fellow, and that he was tired of hearing him everywhere called 'the Just'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristides wrote his own name and handed the sherd back, and as he left the city he prayed that no crisis might come to make the Athenians remember him; Nepos tells the anecdote in the same shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Athenian Constitution</i> puts the recall in the third year, under the archon Hypsechides, with a line from Geraestus to Scyllaeum the exiles were not to live inside, where Nepos makes it about six years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Ostraka_for_Ostracism_-_Museum_of_the_Ancient_Agora_-_Joy_of_Museum.jpg/1280px-Ostraka_for_Ostracism_-_Museum_of_the_Ancient_Agora_-_Joy_of_Museum.jpg","title":"Ostraka in the Agora Museum","desc":"A case of voting sherds in the Museum of the Ancient Agora at Athens. Numbers 16, 17 and 18 read ARISTEIDES LYSIMACHO; number 33, in the row below, names Themistocles son of Neocles.","credit":"Photograph by Joyofmuseums, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ostraka_for_Ostracism_-_Museum_of_the_Ancient_Agora_-_Joy_of_Museum.jpg","alt":"Rows of numbered potsherds pinned to a pale museum wall, each with a personal name scratched through the black glaze or into the bare clay in Greek capitals."},"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 7–8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, in <i>Plutarch: The Parallel Lives</i>, vol. 2, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.1, 22.3–22.8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 3.1284a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=3:section=1284a. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Aristides</i> 1, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 323–24, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.79, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=79. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","politics","greece","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-414","num":414,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says the succession passed to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than to his elder half-brother Artobazanes, because he alone had been born after their father Darius came to the throne.","questions":["Aeschylus put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Athenian stage, with his mother dreaming that a yoked woman broke the harness and threw him down and his father's ghost blaming his youthful recklessness.","Diodorus says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed in his bedchamber by Artabanus, the captain of the royal bodyguard, who then persuaded one of his sons to execute another for the murder."],"answer":"Xerxes I","answerText":"Xerxes I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 486 – 465 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Invaded Greece</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Artabanus, 465 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Xerxes I</b> was the Achaemenid king who succeeded Darius I and carried out the invasion of Greece his father had been preparing when he died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the succession was disputed between Artobazanes, eldest of the sons born before Darius took the throne, and Xerxes, eldest of those born afterwards to Atossa daughter of Cyrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes pleaded his descent from Cyrus, and the exiled Spartan king Demaratus, then at Susa, is said to have told him to add that he alone had been born to a reigning father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus doubts the advice decided anything, since Atossa held complete sway, and says Xerxes was at first not eager to march west: he put down the Egyptian revolt first, laying Egypt under a harder slavery than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Persepolis the text cut four times above the winged bulls of his gateway calls him king of kings and king of the countries of many peoples, and says he made this portal of all lands by the grace of Ahura Mazda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus, no friend of his, still allows that of all the tens of thousands he led there was not one worthier than himself, in looks and grandeur, to hold that command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says he watched Salamis from a gilded throne set on a height above the strait, with secretaries stationed beside him to record what was done in the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aeschylus put him on the Athenian stage in the <i>Persians</i>, where his mother dreams of two women harnessed to his chariot and one of them snapping the yoke and throwing him down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same play his father's ghost calls it a disease of the soul that a mortal should have thought to shackle the sacred Hellespont like a slave and to master even Poseidon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus dates his death to the archonship of Lysitheus and gives it to Artabanus, captain of the bodyguard, let into the bedchamber at night, and says he had been king for more than twenty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/20101229_Gates_of_the_nations_Persepolis_Iran.jpg/1280px-20101229_Gates_of_the_nations_Persepolis_Iran.jpg","title":"The Gate of All Nations at Persepolis","desc":"The gateway Xerxes built at Persepolis, its doorway flanked by human-headed winged bulls. The inscription cut above them, four times over, is the one in which he calls himself king of the countries holding many kinds of people.","credit":"Photograph by Ggia, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20101229_Gates_of_the_nations_Persepolis_Iran.jpg","alt":"A tall stone doorway standing alone under a deep blue sky, its jambs carved as colossal winged bulls with human heads, with slender fluted columns visible through the opening."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.2–7.8, 7.187, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=2. [Open access]","Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>Ancient Persian Lexicon and the Texts of the Achaemenidan Inscriptions</i> (New York: American Book Company, 1908), 39, https://archive.org/details/ancientpersianle00tolm_0. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=13:section=1. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 176–200, 739–752, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=176. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.69, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=69. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","politics","iran","greece"]},
{"id":"gr-415","num":415,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A storm broke the first pair, so Xerxes had the water lashed three hundred times and the overseers beheaded, and new engineers built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across 674 moored ships.","questions":["Aeschylus calls them a yoke on the neck of the sea, and Isocrates says the king who built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sailed his ships across the mainland and marched his troops across the main.","Herodotus says the army took seven days and seven nights to pass over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the lash, the ten thousand Persians leading the way wearing garlands."],"answer":"Hellespont bridges","answerText":"Hellespont bridges","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Strait</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 stadia across</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships moored</span><span class=\"dt-v\">360 and 314</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crossing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 days and 7 nights</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Hellespont bridges</b> were the two pontoon roads Xerxes laid across the strait between Abydos and the Chersonese so that his army could march out of Asia into Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the first pair ran from Abydos to a headland between Sestos and Madytus, one built of flaxen cables by the Phoenicians and one of papyrus by the Egyptians, over a crossing of seven stadia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A storm broke them apart as soon as they were finished, and Xerxes had the water given three hundred lashes and a pair of fetters thrown into it, with words telling the bitter sea that the king would pass whether it wished or not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus has heard that branders went down with the whips, and says the overseers were beheaded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New engineers moored 360 ships and triremes to carry the bridge nearer the Black Sea and 314 for the other, set obliquely to the current, anchored at both ends and left with a gap for small craft to pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Six cables held each bridge, two of flax and four of papyrus, all of one thickness, though a cubit of the flax weighed a talent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over these went sawn timbers, then brushwood, then earth stamped down, with a fence on either side so that the animals should not see the water below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At sunrise Xerxes poured a libation into the strait and threw in a golden bowl and a Persian sword, and Herodotus cannot decide whether these were offerings to the sun or atonement for the whipping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crossing took seven days and seven nights without pause, and Diodorus says Xerxes left Sardis for Abydos only when word came that both this and the Athos canal were finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aeschylus calls the work a roadway cast as a yoke on the neck of the sea, Isocrates says the king sailed his ships across the mainland and marched his troops across the main, and Strabo records that the point opposite Abydos was still called the Zeugma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.33–7.36, 7.54–7.56, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=36. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 65–72, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=65. [Open access]","Isocrates, <i>Panegyricus</i> 89, trans. George Norlin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0144:speech=4:section=89. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.3, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=3. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 13.1.22, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=13:chapter=1:section=22. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","object","technology","warfare","history","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Canakkalebogaz.jpg/1280px-Canakkalebogaz.jpg","title":"The Dardanelles in rough weather","desc":"The strait the Greeks called the Hellespont, seen from a ship. Herodotus says a storm broke up the first pair of bridges as soon as they were finished, and that Xerxes had the water whipped three hundred times for it.","credit":"Photograph by Nerval, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canakkalebogaz.jpg","alt":"A wide channel of dark choppy water under heavy grey cloud, with a wooded headland and a small town along the far shore and two ships in the distance."}},
{"id":"gr-416","num":416,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says the diggers were driven by whippings for three years, and that the Phoenicians alone widened the top of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so that its sides would not cave in.","questions":["Herodotus thinks Xerxes ordered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of pride, wanting a memorial, since the ships could have been dragged over the twelve-stadia isthmus with no trouble at all.","Strabo says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could still be seen at Acanthus in his day, though Demetrius of Scepsis held that a rock ridge had always made it unnavigable."],"answer":"Athos canal","answerText":"Athos canal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 3 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Isthmus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 stadia across</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Width</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 triremes abreast</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fleet passed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Athos canal</b> was the channel Xerxes had cut through the isthmus at the landward end of the Athos peninsula so that his fleet could pass without rounding the mountain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says ships that had earlier tried to sail round Athos were wrecked, and that the work went on about three years, with triremes anchored off Elaeus as a base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Men from every nation in the army were driven to dig by whippings, in relays, along with the people living about Athos, under two Persian overseers, Bubares and Artachaees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The isthmus runs about twelve stadia from the sea by Acanthus to the sea opposite Torone, and cutting it would have made islands of Sane and five neighbouring towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each nation dug its own stretch and passed the spoil up in stages, and all but the Phoenicians made the trench the same width top and bottom, so the sides caved in and doubled their labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus judges that Xerxes ordered it out of pride, to display his power and leave a memorial, since the ships could have been dragged across with no trouble; it was cut wide enough for two triremes to row abreast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus gives another reason, that Xerxes hoped the scale of the works would frighten the Greeks before he arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Artachaees died of illness at Acanthus, and Herodotus says he was the tallest man in Persia and the loudest-voiced on earth, that the whole army poured libations at his tomb, and that the Acanthians sacrifice to him as a hero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides, fifty years later, puts Sane close to the canal and calls the promontory one running out from the king's dike, and Isocrates makes the channelling of Athos a plan whose fame is on every tongue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strabo says the cut could still be seen at Acanthus in his own day, but records Demetrius of Scepsis holding it had never been navigable, a flat rock nearly a stadium long having stopped the digging short of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.22–7.24, 7.117, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=23. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 4.109, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=4:chapter=109. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.2, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=2. [Open access]","Isocrates, <i>Panegyricus</i> 89, trans. George Norlin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0144:speech=4:section=89. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7, fragments 33 and 35, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=7:chapter=fragments. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","technology","warfare","history","greece","iran"]},
{"id":"gr-417","num":417,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says Xerxes spent four full years preparing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and set out before the fifth was out with a host he thought greater than any expedition ever known.","questions":["At Abydos, before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed into Europe, Xerxes watched his fleet from a seat of white stone, called himself blessed and then wept at how short human life is.","Aeschylus and Herodotus both put the fleet of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 1,207 ships, and Herodotus reckons the land army at a million and seven hundred thousand men."],"answer":"invasion of 480","answerText":"invasion of 480","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Preparations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crossing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,700,000, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,207 ships</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>invasion of 480</b> was the Persian attempt on Greece that Xerxes led in person, four full years in the preparing after Egypt had been subdued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls it by far the greatest expedition known, beside which Darius' Scythian march and the sons of Atreus against Troy are nothing, and asks what nation Xerxes did not bring out of Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says whole waters failed when the army drank, all but the greatest rivers, and Diodorus repeats the story that unfailing rivers ran dry and the sea was hidden by sails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The army crossed at Abydos and the fleet came through the cutting at Athos, and at Doriscus the whole force was counted by packing ten thousand men at a time inside a wall built to the height of a man's navel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Abydos Xerxes watched from a seat of white stone set up on a hill, and when he saw the ships and the shore covered with men he declared himself blessed and then wept, saying that of all that multitude not one would be alive in a hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The count gave a land army of a million and seven hundred thousand, and with the crews and the service train Herodotus reaches a total of 5,283,220 men brought as far as Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He puts the fleet at 1,207 warships from Asia, the figure Aeschylus also gives on the Athenian stage, where the Greeks are made to meet it with 310.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus counts differently and reaches not less than a million soldiers once the European allies had joined, with as many again in the warships and the supply train.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lysias, speaking two generations later, says only that twelve hundred ships came and that merely listing the nations of the land army would be a long task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides, looking back from a longer war, calls the Median war the greatest achievement of past times and says it found a speedy decision in two actions by sea and two by land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.20–7.21, 7.44–7.46, 7.60, 7.184–7.186, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=60. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 337–343, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=337. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.5, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=5. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.23, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=23. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Funeral Oration</i> 27, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0154:speech=2:section=27. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Archers_frieze_Louvre_AOD488.jpg/1280px-Archers_frieze_Louvre_AOD488.jpg","title":"Guardsmen in glazed brick from Susa","desc":"A frieze of archers in glazed brick from the palace of Darius I at Susa, now in the Louvre. It was made a generation before the invasion, and shows the royal guard of the empire that mustered it.","credit":"Photograph via Wikimedia Commons (public domain). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archers_frieze_Louvre_AOD488.jpg","alt":"Four life-size figures in long patterned robes striding right along a turquoise brick wall, each holding a spear upright with a bow and quiver slung behind the shoulder."}},
{"id":"gr-418","num":418,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> swore that if they won they would give Delphi the goods of every Greek state that had gone over to the Persians of its own free will.","questions":["Athens gave up its claim to command at sea rather than let the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> break apart over the leadership, judging the safety of Hellas more important than its own claim.","Gelon of Syracuse refused to join the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unless he led it, and told the envoys to go home and say that Hellas had lost the spring from her year."],"answer":"Hellenic League","answerText":"Hellenic League","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 481 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War won</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Victory tithe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 gold tripod at Delphi</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Hellenic League</b> was the alliance of Greek states that agreed to resist Xerxes, made up of the cities that refused the Persian king and bound by a common oath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus names the Greeks who submitted instead, the Thebans and all the Boeotians but Thespiae and Plataea among them, and says the allies swore that in victory they would dedicate to Delphi the goods of every Greek who had surrendered of free will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those concerned for the common welfare met, exchanged guarantees and resolved to end every feud among themselves, of which the greatest then running was between Athens and Aegina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They sent men into Asia to spy out the king's doings, and envoys to Argos, to Gelon of Syracuse, to Corcyra and to Crete, hoping that all of Greek blood might work to one end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus adds that the congress voted to make any Greek who joined the Persians willingly pay a tithe to the gods, and to send envoys urging in the neutrals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Not all came: Argos asked for a thirty-year truce and a share of the command, the Cretans put the question to Delphi and were told to stay out, and Gelon offered a great force on condition that he lead it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Told he could not, he sent the Athenian envoy home with the answer that Hellas had lost the spring from her year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sparta led by land and by sea, and Athens, which had a claim to the naval command, waived it when the allies resisted, judging that a quarrel over it would destroy Hellas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the victory the allies set apart a tithe for Delphi and made from it a gold tripod on a three-headed bronze serpent, on which the Spartan commander <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Pausanias_the_regent\">Pausanias</span> had a couplet cut claiming the monument for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Spartans erased it and cut instead the names of the cities that had helped overthrow the barbarian, and centuries later the bronze still stood at Delphi, though the gold had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.132, 7.145, 8.3, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=145. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.148–7.169, 9.81, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=162. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.3, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=3. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.132, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=132. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.13.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=13:section=9. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","warfare","politics","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Bronze_Serpent_Column_in_Istanbul.jpg/1280px-Bronze_Serpent_Column_in_Istanbul.jpg","title":"The Serpent Column","desc":"The bronze column that carried the allies' gold tripod at Delphi, standing today in a sunken pit in the Hippodrome at Istanbul. Its coils carry the names the Spartans cut there in place of Pausanias' couplet.","credit":"Photograph by Ian Scott, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 2.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_Serpent_Column_in_Istanbul.jpg","alt":"A tall bronze column of three intertwined snake bodies, green with age and broken off at the top, rising from a stone-lined pit below street level with railings and passers-by above."}},
{"id":"gr-419","num":419,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Thessalian messengers came to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asking for a force to hold the pass of Olympus, warning that they would otherwise have to make terms with the Persian.","questions":["After the force sent to Tempe came home, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> deliberated again and resolved to hold Thermopylae instead, with the fleet at Artemisium so each could learn the other's doings.","When the admirals voted at Poseidon's altar after Salamis, at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> each put himself first and most put Themistocles second, so no one won outright."],"answer":"congress at the Isthmus","answerText":"congress at the Isthmus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First met</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 481 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent to Tempe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Prize of valour</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, after Salamis</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>congress at the Isthmus</b> was the standing council through which the Greek states allied against Xerxes made their decisions, meeting at the sanctuary of Poseidon on the neck of land joining the Peloponnese to the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus describes it as men chosen from the cities best disposed towards Hellas, assembled in council for the Greek cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first business was to end the feuds among its own members and to send envoys to the Greeks who had not joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus says it voted that any Greek who went over to the Persians willingly should pay a tithe to the gods, and sent to the neutral states urging them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also had to settle who commanded: Thucydides says the Spartans took the leadership in virtue of their superior power, and Diodorus records the Argives offering to join if they were given a share of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Thessalian messengers came asking for a force to hold the pass of Olympus, warning that they would otherwise make terms with the Persian, and the council resolved to send one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When that force came back from Tempe the Greeks deliberated again and chose to hold Thermopylae instead, as narrower and nearer home, with the fleet at Artemisium so that each could learn the other's doings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Salamis the admirals came to the Isthmus to award the prize of valour, and at the altar of Poseidon each voted himself first, so every man had one vote and Themistocles far outstripped the rest in second places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch tells the same story and adds that the Spartans afterwards gave Eurybiades the crown for valour and Themistocles one for wisdom, with the best chariot in the city and three hundred picked youths to escort him to the border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the council resolved survives on stone as well as in books: Pausanias saw at Olympia the Zeus the allies dedicated after Plataea, with the cities that fought inscribed on its pedestal, the Lacedaemonians first and the Athenians second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.145, 7.172–7.175, 8.123, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=175. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.18, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=18. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.3, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=3. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.23.1–5.23.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=5:chapter=23. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=17:section=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","event","history","politics","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Isthmia_altar_of_Poseidon_with_wheel_ruts.jpg/1280px-Isthmia_altar_of_Poseidon_with_wheel_ruts.jpg","title":"The altar of Poseidon at Isthmia","desc":"The foundations of the great altar in the sanctuary of Poseidon on the Isthmus, worn with cart ruts. Herodotus says the admirals cast their votes for the prize of valour at this altar after Salamis.","credit":"Photograph by Mark Landon, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isthmia_altar_of_Poseidon_with_wheel_ruts.jpg","alt":"Long low courses of weathered limestone blocks running across dry scrubby ground, with further tumbled stone and a field wall behind."}},
{"id":"gr-420","num":420,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The Pythia first told the Athenians to flee to the ends of the earth, and only when they came back as suppliants did she give them the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead.","questions":["Some Athenians read the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the thorn hedge that had once fenced the Acropolis, while Themistocles argued it meant the ships and won the assembly over.","Themistocles said the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called Salamis divine rather than cruel, so the deaths it promised must be the enemy's and the Athenians should fight at sea."],"answer":"wooden wall oracle","answerText":"wooden wall oracle","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Answers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, from the Pythia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manning money</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 drachmas a man</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>wooden wall oracle</b> was the second answer the Pythia at Delphi gave the Athenians as Xerxes advanced, and the argument over its meaning decided how Athens would fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first, from the priestess Aristonice, told them to flee to the ends of the earth, since fire and the headlong god of war would bring the city low and its roofs run with gore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The envoys despaired, and on the advice of Timon son of Androbulus went back with suppliant boughs, refusing to leave until they were given a better answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second said that all within the sacred border of Cecrops would be taken, but that Zeus would grant the Trito-born a wall of wood, a stronghold for them and their children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ended with the lines that would trouble them: await not the host from Asia but turn your back and withdraw, and divine Salamis, you will bring death to women's sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Athens the elder men said the wooden wall was the thorn hedge that once fenced the acropolis; others said it was the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The readers of oracles took the Salamis lines to mean that a sea fight there would end in Athenian defeat, which told against the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Themistocles answered that had the verse meant the Athenians the god would have called Salamis cruel rather than divine, so it spoke of the enemy, and the assembly judged him the better counsellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later writers keep the argument but not the ambiguity: Nepos and Justin each give a single answer telling the Athenians to defend themselves with wooden walls, and Justin's Themistocles argues that a city is its people, not its buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch says Themistocles also put the sign of the vanished sacred serpent into the priests' mouths, the <i>Athenian Constitution</i> credits the Areopagus with finding eight drachmas a man to man the ships, and Herodotus records that a few who stayed and fenced the acropolis with doors and logs died believing theirs was the wooden wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.140–7.143, 8.51, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=141. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=10:section=1. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Themistocles</i> 2, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 316–17, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.12, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 83, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 23.1, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","religion","politics","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Temple_of_Apollo_in_Delphoi.jpg/1280px-Temple_of_Apollo_in_Delphoi.jpg","title":"The temple of Apollo at Delphi","desc":"The standing columns of the temple of Apollo at Delphi, under the cliffs of Parnassus. It was here that the Athenian envoys sat in the inner hall and were told to flee to the ends of the earth, and here that they came back with suppliant boughs.","credit":"Photograph by Kyri Th., via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Apollo_in_Delphoi.jpg","alt":"Five weathered stone column drums standing on a ruined platform of large blocks, with a grey limestone cliff and dark cypresses rising behind them."}},
{"id":"gr-421","num":421,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Ten thousand hoplites went by sea to hold the pass between Olympus and Ossa, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came home after a few days without meeting the Persians at all.","questions":["Herodotus says fear ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, once the Greeks learned of a second way into Thessaly through Perrhaebia, and not the friendly warning from Alexander of Macedon.","Diodorus gives a different reason for abandoning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, that the Thessalians and their neighbours had already given earth and water to the king's envoys."],"answer":"Tempe expedition","answerText":"Tempe expedition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 hoplites</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, without a fight</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Tempe expedition</b> was the first Greek attempt to stop Xerxes, a force sent north in 480 BCE to hold the pass where the Peneus leaves Thessaly for the sea between Olympus and Ossa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thessalian messengers had come to the allies asking that the pass of Olympus be guarded, warning that they would otherwise have to make terms with the Persian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus says about ten thousand men-at-arms went with cavalry, carried by sea through the Euripus and landed at Alus in Achaea to march up into Thessaly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Lacedaemonian general was Euaenetus son of Carenus, chosen from among the polemarchs though not of the royal house, and the Athenian was Themistocles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says Themistocles had wanted to meet the Persians at sea and led this army north only because many opposed him, Thessaly not yet being thought to be medizing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> They stayed a few days and left: messengers from Alexander son of Amyntas of Macedon pointed out the size of the army and the number of the ships, and advised them not to stay and be trodden underfoot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus judges that what really moved them was fear, since they had learned of another way into Thessaly through the hill country of the Perrhaebi near Gonnus, which was the road Xerxes in fact took.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus gives a different account, naming the Spartan commander Synetus and saying the generals despaired because the Thessalians and their neighbours had already given earth and water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The place itself is a chasm: Strabo says an earthquake tore Ossa from Olympus and let the Peneus drain what had been a lake, and Herodotus reports the Thessalian tradition that Poseidon made the passage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Xerxes came to see it he asked whether the river had another outlet, and on being told it had none remarked that the Thessalians had been wise to yield, since a dam would have put their whole country under water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.128–7.130, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=130. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.172–7.174, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=173. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=7:section=1. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.2, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=2. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.5.2, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=9:chapter=5:section=2. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Tempi1.jpg","title":"The vale of Tempe","desc":"The Peneus running through the defile between Olympus and Ossa, the pass the Greeks were sent to hold in 480 BCE and left again after a few days.","credit":"Photograph by Mzmona, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tempi1.jpg","alt":"A green river winding between steep wooded slopes, with a pale limestone cliff face rising on the right and the gorge narrowing into the distance under a clear sky."}},
{"id":"gr-422","num":422,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus lists 300 Spartans, 700 Thespians, 400 Thebans and thousands of Arcadians among the Greeks who held the pass at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 480 BCE.","questions":["After a mountain path was betrayed to the Persians, the survivors of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell back behind the wall and were buried under missiles on a hillock.","The Amphictyons set up pillars after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of which told any passing foreigner to go and say that the Spartans lie here obedient to their commands."],"answer":"Battle of Thermopylae","answerText":"Battle of Thermopylae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spartans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Epitaph</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4,000 against 3 million</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Thermopylae</b> was the three-day action of 480 BCE in which a small Greek force held the coast road into central Greece against Xerxes until a mountain path was betrayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the pass is about 15 metres (50 feet) across at its narrowest, with a precipitous spur of Oeta above and nothing but marsh and sea below, and a wall the Phocians had built long before running across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lists three hundred Spartans, seven hundred Thespians and four hundred Thebans, with a thousand from Tegea and Mantinea and more than a thousand other Arcadians, Corinthians, Phliasians and Mycenaeans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Locrians and a thousand Phocians came after messengers told them this was only an advance guard, the rest expected daily, and the invader a mortal who would fall from pride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias, comparing that muster with the one raised against the Gauls, notes that Herodotus gives no number for the Locrians and works one out himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Greeks held two days, sallying from the narrows and falling back behind the wall; on the third a mountain path was shown to the Persians and Leonidas sent most of the allies away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Knowing they would die, those who stayed came out beyond the narrows, where officers whipped the Persians forward and men were pushed into the sea or trampled by their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says they routed the enemy four times over the body of Leonidas and, when the encircling force arrived, retired to a hillock and fought with swords, hands and teeth until the missiles buried them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus tells it differently, Leonidas keeping only five hundred and dismissing the rest to fight another day, while Justin gives him four thousand at the outset and twenty thousand Persians on the summit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Amphictyons set pillars over the dead, one saying four thousand from the Peloponnese fought three million here and one telling a passing foreigner to say the Spartans lie obedient to their commands; Strabo saw both mound and pillar still standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.176, 7.201–7.228, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=225. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.9–11.11, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=9. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.11, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 30–31, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.20.1–10.20.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=20. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.4.16, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=9:chapter=4:section=16. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Thermopylae_panorama_1.jpg","title":"The pass at Thermopylae today","desc":"The wall of Mount Kallidromon above the road at Thermopylae. The flat ground in front of it was sea in 480 BCE; the Malian Gulf has since silted up, so the pass Herodotus measured at about 15 metres across is now a plain.","credit":"Photograph by Davide Mauro, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thermopylae_panorama_1.jpg","alt":"A steep wooded mountainside with pale limestone crags, rising above a modern road and a broad flat expanse of white gravel under a clear blue sky."}},
{"id":"gr-423","num":423,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The kingship at Sparta came to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only because his elder brothers Cleomenes and Dorieus had both died, and he had married Cleomenes' daughter.","questions":["Told the Persian arrows would blot out the sun, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have answered that it would then be pleasant to fight in the shade.","Xerxes had the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut off and impaled, which Herodotus reads as anger, since the Persians usually honoured a brave enemy."],"answer":"Leonidas I","answerText":"Leonidas I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chose</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 Spartans, all with sons</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bones brought home</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 years later</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Leonidas I</b> was the Agiad king of Sparta who commanded the Greeks at Thermopylae in 480 BCE and died holding the pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus traces his descent back through twenty names to Heracles and says he came to the kingship unexpectedly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was a younger son of Anaxandrides who had renounced any thought of ruling, and succeeded only when Cleomenes died without a son and Dorieus was killed in Sicily; he had also married Cleomenes' daughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He took to Thermopylae three hundred men he chose himself, all of whom had living sons, and brought the Thebans deliberately because they were suspected of medizing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus says he asked the ephors for a thousand men only, and when pressed answered that they were few for holding the pass but many for the task they were really going to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus reports that the Pythia had told the Spartans either their city would fall or a king of Heracles' line must die, and thinks Leonidas sent the allies away so as to leave a great name behind him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sayings collected under Plutarch's name have him answer a warning that the arrows would hide the sun by remarking that they would fight in the shade, and Xerxes' demand for his arms with 'come and take them'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Justin has him tell his men before the last day that they must expect to perish, and take care not to show more resolution in staying than in fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus says the Greeks routed the enemy four times over his body, and that Xerxes had the head cut off and impaled, which he reads as unusual anger in a people known for honouring brave men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forty years afterwards the bones were brought home from Thermopylae, and Pausanias saw the tomb at Sparta with a slab naming every man who had endured the fight, and yearly speeches and a contest open to Spartans alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.204–7.206, 7.220, 7.225, 7.238, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=220. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.4, 11.11, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=4. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Sayings of Spartans</i> 225B–225D, trans. Frank Cole Babbitt, in <i>Plutarch: Moralia</i>, vol. 3, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Sayings_of_Spartans*/main.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.14.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=3:chapter=14. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.11, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 30–31, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","politics","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Statue_of_a_hoplite_known_as_Leonidas_480-470_BCE_Sparta_Acropolis_Sanctuary_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_01.jpg/1280px-Statue_of_a_hoplite_known_as_Leonidas_480-470_BCE_Sparta_Acropolis_Sanctuary_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_01.jpg","title":"The marble hoplite from Sparta","desc":"A marble warrior of about 480 BCE found on the Sparta acropolis, in the sanctuary of Athena of the Bronze House, and now in the museum there. It is traditionally called Leonidas, but nothing on it names him.","credit":"Photograph by Mary Harrsch, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_a_hoplite_known_as_Leonidas_480-470_BCE_Sparta_Acropolis_Sanctuary_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_01.jpg","alt":"The upper half of a marble statue of a helmeted warrior, the tall crest of the helmet rising behind, the cheek pieces framing a bearded face with an archaic smile."}},
{"id":"gr-424","num":424,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus names <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the man who told Xerxes of the mountain path, and says he did it expecting a great reward from the king.","questions":["A price was put on the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the Amphictyons at Pylae, and he was killed years later at Anticyra for some quite different reason.","Some said two other men had guided the Persians, but Herodotus rejects that and puts <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on record as the guilty one instead."],"answer":"Ephialtes of Trachis","answerText":"Ephialtes of Trachis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Betrayal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent by night</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rival guides</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, named by others</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Ephialtes of Trachis</b> was the man Herodotus blames for the fall of Thermopylae, having shown Xerxes the mountain path by which the Greeks holding the pass were surrounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls him the son of Eurydemus and a Malian in one chapter and 'Epialtes the Trachinian' in the next, and says he came to the king expecting a great reward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes was delighted and sent Hydarnes out with his men at about lamp-lighting time, and Strabo's whole summary of the battle is that the barbarians made a circuit of the mountains by narrow paths and cut the Greeks to pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The path was not his discovery: Herodotus says the Malians had found it long before and used it to bring the Thessalians into Phocis when the Phocians had walled the pass off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fled to Thessaly in fear of the Spartans, and while he was in exile the Pylagori set a price on his head at the Amphictyonic meeting at Pylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He came back to Anticyra and was killed there by Athenades, a Trachinian, for a reason Herodotus says was quite different, though the Spartans honoured him for it all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another story made Onetes son of Phanagoras of Carystus and Corydallus of Anticyra the guides, and Herodotus rejects it because the price was set on Ephialtes and not on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus tells the same act without the name, calling him only a Trachinian who knew the mountains, and has Xerxes heap presents on him and send twenty thousand men out by night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Diodorus the Greeks are warned all the same, by a Persian named Tyrrhastiadas of Cyme who deserted in the dark to tell Leonidas, and Justin likewise puts twenty thousand on the summit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias, describing the Gauls two centuries later, says the Heracleots and Aenianians led Brennus over the same mountain, not from ill will to Greece but because they wanted the Celts gone from their country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.213–7.215, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=213. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.8, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=8. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.22.8–10.22.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=22. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.11, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 30–31, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.4.16, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=9:chapter=4:section=16. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Thermopylae_panorama_3.jpg/1280px-Thermopylae_panorama_3.jpg","title":"The ridge above Thermopylae","desc":"Mount Kallidromon on the left and the plain running away east, seen from above the pass. The path Ephialtes showed the Persians crossed this ridge and came down behind the Greek position.","credit":"Photograph by Davide Mauro, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thermopylae_panorama_3.jpg","alt":"A wide view along a wooded mountain flank on the left, with a modern road and pylons crossing a flat pale plain to the right and blue hills in the haze beyond."}},
{"id":"gr-425","num":425,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"When Leonidas dismissed his allies on the last morning, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone chose to stay, saying they would not abandon him but would die beside the Spartans.","questions":["Herodotus counts 700 of these Boeotian hoplites at the pass, and their general Demophilus son of Diadromes led the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who refused to leave with the other allies.","A year after the pass, 1,800 of the surviving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mustered at Plataea without hoplite armour, their town having been burnt by the Persians."],"answer":"Thespians","answerText":"Thespians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At the pass</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700, in 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, by Xerxes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,800 survivors, 479 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Thespians</b> were the men of Thespiae, a small Boeotian city whose 700 hoplites stayed with Leonidas at Thermopylae in 480 BCE and were killed there beside the Spartans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thespiae lay a little south of Mount Helicon in southern Boeotia, above the Crisaean Gulf, with a port of its own at Creusa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Xerxes' heralds asked for earth and water, every city in Boeotia gave them except Thespiae and Plataea, and the Greeks who resisted swore to tithe to Delphi the goods of any Greek who had submitted willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus lists 700 Thespians in the force that waited at the pass, beside 300 Spartans, 400 Thebans, 400 Corinthians and the Arcadian and other Peloponnesian contingents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the last morning, once the mountain path had been betrayed and Leonidas had sent the rest of the allies away, only the Thespians and the Thebans stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus distinguishes the two carefully: the Thebans were held back as hostages against their will, while the Thespians remained gladly under their general Demophilus son of Diadromes, saying they would not abandon Leonidas but would stay and die with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus tells it differently, keeping back the Thespians alone and giving Leonidas no more than 500 men in all, where the figures in Herodotus come to about 1,400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch, attacking Herodotus for malice against Thebes, quotes that passage back at him and accepts without argument that the Thespians stood by Leonidas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sailors who crossed from the fleet to view the field took all 4,000 of the Greek dead for Lacedaemonians and Thespians, though helots lay among them; the collective epigram at the pass counted only 'four thousand from the Peloponnese', and no Thespian stone is recorded there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xerxes burnt Thespiae, whose people had already left for the Peloponnese, and the following year 1,800 Thespian survivors mustered at Plataea with no hoplite armour at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.132, 7.202, 7.222, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=222. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.228, 8.25, 8.50, 9.30, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=25. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.9, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=9. [Open access]","Plutarch, “Of Herodotus's Malice,” in <i>Plutarch's Morals</i>, ed. William W. Goodwin, vol. 4 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1878), 352–53, https://archive.org/details/plutarchsmorals04plut. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.2.25, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0198:book=9:chapter=2:section=25. [Open access]"],"tags":["people","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Thespians_monument.jpg/1280px-Thespians_monument.jpg","title":"The monument to the 700 Thespians","desc":"A modern memorial beside the pass at Thermopylae, unveiled in 1997. The marble plinth is inscribed in Greek 'in memory of the seven hundred Thespians', and carries a headless winged bronze figure. No ancient portrait of the Thespians survives.","credit":"Photograph by Dmpexr, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thespians_monument.jpg","alt":"A white marble plinth on a paved terrace between dark cypresses, carrying a headless bronze figure with one outstretched wing; an electricity pylon rises behind."}},
{"id":"gr-426","num":426,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"While Leonidas held the pass, the Greek fleet fought three days off the north coast of Euboea in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, keeping Xerxes' ships out of the Euripus.","questions":["The Euboeans paid Themistocles thirty talents to keep the fleet from sailing away, which is how the Greeks came to fight the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> off their island rather than retreat south.","Pindar said the sons of the Athenians laid the corner-stone of freedom in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the fleet withdrew towards Salamis as soon as it heard that Leonidas was dead."],"answer":"Battle of Artemisium","answerText":"Battle of Artemisium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, over three days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">271 triremes, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian loss</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 ships wrecked off Euboea</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Artemisium</b> was the naval action fought off the north coast of Euboea in 480 BCE, on the same days as Thermopylae, between the Greek fleet and Xerxes' navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Artemisium is a beach on the Euboean shore where the Thracian sea narrows between Sciathus and Magnesia; a temple of Artemis Proseoea stood there, enclosed by slabs of white marble, one carrying an elegy on the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Greeks mustered 271 triremes under the Spartan Eurybiades, the Athenians providing 127 and manning twenty more for the Chalcidians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes' fleet had already been mauled by a storm off Cape Sepias in Magnesia, where ships were driven ashore and broken along the coast of Pelion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two positions were held as one: the fleet was to keep the Persians out of the Euripus while the men at Thermopylae held the pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Greeks nearly withdrew at the sight of the Persian fleet at Aphetae, and stayed only because the Euboeans paid Themistocles thirty talents, of which he passed five to Eurybiades and three to the Corinthian Adimantus, keeping the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scyllias of Scione, reckoned the best diver of his day, came over from the Persians with word that 200 ships had been sent round Euboea to trap the Greeks; that squadron was wrecked in a night storm on the Hollows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the first engagement the Greeks drew their sterns together with prows outward, took thirty ships and broke off at nightfall; on the third day the Persians came out at midday and neither side gave way, the Athenians ending with half their ships damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus tells it as two battles rather than three days, places it after Thermopylae had fallen, and counts 280 Greek triremes against Herodotus' 271.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Word of Leonidas' death was brought by Abronichus and the fleet withdrew, Themistocles cutting appeals to the Ionians into the rocks at the watering places; Plutarch quotes Pindar on the beach where \"Athenians' valiant sons set in radiance eternal Liberty's corner-stone\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.176, 8.1–8.2, 8.4–8.5, 8.8, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=1. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.11, 8.13–8.18, 8.21–8.23, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=15. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.12–11.13, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.5.22, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0198:book=9:chapter=5:section=22. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Beach_at_Cape_Artemisium_on_Euboea.jpg/1280px-Beach_at_Cape_Artemisium_on_Euboea.jpg","title":"The strait at Cape Artemisium","desc":"The beach on the north coast of Euboea where the Greek fleet lay, looking across the water to the mountains of Magnesia. The Persian fleet was moored on that far shore, at Aphetae.","credit":"Photograph by Marco Prins for Livius.org, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beach_at_Cape_Artemisium_on_Euboea.jpg","alt":"A pebbled shore and calm blue-green sea framed by an olive branch, with a long range of hazy mountains along the far horizon."}},
{"id":"gr-427","num":427,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"With Xerxes' army through the pass and coming south, Themistocles carried a decree for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sending families to Troezen, Aegina and Salamis and every man of fighting age aboard the fleet.","questions":["The sacred snake on the acropolis left its honey-cake untouched, and the priestess read the sign as the goddess departing, which made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> easier for Themistocles to carry.","The Troezenians voted two obols a day to each refugee family after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, let the boys pick fruit wherever they liked, and hired teachers for them."],"answer":"evacuation of Athens","answerText":"evacuation of Athens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Evacuated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, after Thermopylae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Areopagus gave</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 drachmas a man</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Troezen paid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 obols a day per family</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>evacuation of Athens</b> was the removal of the city's population to Troezen, Aegina and Salamis in 480 BCE, carried out in the days between the loss of Thermopylae and the arrival of Xerxes' army, which came down through Boeotia and took a deserted city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Greek fleet had come back from Artemisium to Salamis at the Athenians' request, and they had learned that the Peloponnesians were fortifying the Isthmus rather than standing in Boeotia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Landing in Attica, they proclaimed that every man should get his children and his household away as best he could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most sent their families to Troezen, and some to Aegina and to Salamis, both to obey the oracle of the wooden wall and for another reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A great snake was kept in the sacred precinct on the acropolis and given a honey-cake every month; that month the cake was untouched, and when the priestess explained what it meant they were the readier to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch tells the same story as stage management, with Themistocles putting the interpretation into the priests' mouths once human argument had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also gives the measure that carried it, a bill entrusting the city to Athena and ordering every man of fighting age aboard the ships, proposed by one Nicagoras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Troezenians voted to keep the refugees at public cost at two obols a day for each family, let the boys pick fruit wherever they liked, and hired teachers for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There was no public money to man the fleet, and Aristotle says the council of the Areopagus found eight drachmas a head, which is why he credits it with the victory at Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus compresses the whole thing into one movement of wives, children and portable goods across to Salamis; Plutarch keeps what was remembered afterwards, the aged left behind and the dog of Xanthippus that swam beside its master's trireme and died on the island, at the place still called Dog's Mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.40–8.41, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=41. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>The Athenian Constitution</i> 23, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.13, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=13. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.50–8.51, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=51. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Troezen_-_Acropolis_09.JPG/1280px-Troezen_-_Acropolis_09.JPG","title":"Troezen and its coast","desc":"The plain below the acropolis of Troezen, looking north across the Saronic Gulf towards Attica. Most Athenian families were sent here, and the Troezenians voted to keep them at public cost.","credit":"Photograph by Schuppi, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troezen_-_Acropolis_09.JPG","alt":"A scrubby rocky hillside in the foreground falling to a green cultivated plain, with a low wooded ridge beyond it and deep blue open sea across the top of the frame."}},
{"id":"gr-428","num":428,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A stone found in the Argolid carries what claims to be the Athenian order of 480 BCE to abandon the city, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been argued over ever since it appeared.","questions":["Herodotus and Aristotle both describe an improvised proclamation rather than a plan, and it is against them that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to be weighed as evidence for what Athens actually did.","Demosthenes shows that Athenian orators were reading a text of Themistocles' own measures aloud in the 340s BCE, more than two thousand years before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came out of the ground."],"answer":"Troezen decree","answerText":"Troezen decree","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decree dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, if genuine</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unearthed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1932 or 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1959</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Troezen decree</b> is an inscribed stone from Troezen carrying what purports to be the Athenian decree of 480 BCE that ordered the city abandoned and its men put aboard the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was turned up by accident in 1932 or 1933 and then stood for a quarter of a century in a farmer's house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A newly founded local society of friends of antiquity brought it out again, a visiting epigraphist took a squeeze of it in the summer of 1959, and within months the text was on American radio and in the international press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing so orderly appears in the fifth-century account: Herodotus has the fleet come back from Artemisium and the Athenians only then proclaim that every man should save his household as best he could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle is blunter, saying the generals had given up hope and made that proclamation, and that the council of the Areopagus found eight drachmas a head to get the ships manned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Against them stands Plutarch, who reports a real bill entrusting the city to Athena and ordering every man of fighting age aboard the ships, moved by one Nicagoras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A written decree of Themistocles certainly existed in the 4th century, because Demosthenes complains that a rival read it out to the assembly beside the decree of Miltiades and the oath sworn by the young men of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stone therefore sits at the meeting point of two questions, whether Athens planned the evacuation in advance or improvised it, and whether a text read aloud in the 340s BCE went back to 480 or was made to look as though it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its find-story settles neither, since it was out of the ground and out of sight for 25 years before anyone competent saw it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the city acted on a decree or on a general's despairing proclamation is thus not a question the stone can answer by itself, and the two writers nearest to the event describe an improvisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Georges Daux, “Trézène,” in “Chronique des fouilles et découvertes archéologiques en Grèce en 1959,” <i>Bulletin de correspondance hellénique</i> 84 (1960): 685–88, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bch_0007-4217_1960_num_84_2_20447. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.40–8.41, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=41. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>The Athenian Constitution</i> 23, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>On the False Embassy</i> 19.303, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0072:speech=19:section=303. [Open access]"],"tags":["text","history","archaeology","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Themistocle_decree_EM_13330.JPG/1280px-Themistocle_decree_EM_13330.JPG","title":"The stone itself","desc":"The stele in the Epigraphical Museum, Athens, inventoried as EM 13330 and published as SEG 22.274. The left half of the face is worn almost smooth; the lines that survive on the right carry the words for Salamis and for the acropolis.","credit":"Photograph by Schuppi, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Themistocle_decree_EM_13330.JPG","alt":"A tall rectangular slab of pale marble on a low plinth against a deep blue wall, its left side eroded blank and rows of Greek capitals still legible down the right."}},
{"id":"gr-429","num":429,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A handful of treasurers and poor men fenced the citadel with doors and logs, and their stand ended in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when Persians climbed the cliff by Aglaurus' shrine.","questions":["Herodotus says the olive of Athena put out a shoot a cubit long the day after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when Xerxes had the Athenian exiles go up and sacrifice in their own manner.","Mardonius came back the next year to pull down whatever the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had left standing, so the Athenians returned to a circuit wall surviving only in stretches."],"answer":"sack of the Acropolis","answerText":"sack of the Acropolis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Stormed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burnt again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, by Mardonius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Olive shoot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 cubit, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>sack of the Acropolis</b> was the storming and burning of the Athenian citadel by Xerxes' army in 480 BCE, days after the city below it had been emptied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A few temple treasurers and poor men had stayed behind and fenced the height with doors and logs, having decided that the wooden wall of the oracle meant this and not the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persians took the Areopagus opposite and shot arrows wrapped in tow and set alight, burning the barricade away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They held out even so, refusing the terms the Peisistratids brought and rolling boulders down on anyone who came at the gates, so that for a long time Xerxes could not take the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Men then climbed the sheer face at the front, by the precinct of Cecrops' daughter Aglaurus, where nobody had been posted because nobody thought it could be climbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some threw themselves from the wall and others fled into the chamber; the first men up opened the gates, killed the suppliants, then plundered the sanctuary and set the whole citadel alight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes sent a rider to Susa with the news and next day had the Athenian exiles in his train go up and sacrifice in their own manner, which Herodotus puts down to a dream or to remorse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They came back saying the olive of Athena, burnt with the rest, had put out a shoot a cubit long; Pausanias, telling the story six centuries later, has it grow two cubits on the day of the fire itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The next year Mardonius came back and pulled down whatever wall, house or temple was still standing, so that when the Athenians came home only stretches of the circuit stood, with a few houses spared because Persian officers had quartered in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pausanias was still shown fire-blackened statues of Athena on the citadel in the 2nd century CE, scorched, he says, when the king took the city with its fighting men gone to the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.51–8.53, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=53. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.54–8.55, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=55. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.13, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=13. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.27.2, 1.27.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=27. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.89, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=89. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Acropolis_North_wall.jpg/1280px-Acropolis_North_wall.jpg","title":"Column drums in the north wall","desc":"The north side of the Acropolis from below. The row of column drums set into the wall near the top came from a temple wrecked in 480 BCE and was laid into the new wall whole, rather than re-cut.","credit":"Photograph by Seligmanwaite, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acropolis_North_wall.jpg","alt":"A steep pale limestone cliff rising to a masonry wall, with a horizontal row of stacked cylindrical column drums built into the wall just below its top."}},
{"id":"gr-430","num":430,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Themistocles sent Sicinnus to tell the Persians the Greeks were about to run, and the trap they closed that night became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at first light.","questions":["Aeschylus, who counts 310 Greek ships against a thousand, describes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a crush in the narrows where the rear rammed its own front and splintered the oars.","Xerxes had a gilded throne set where he could watch, and secretaries beside it to record the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended with his brother Ariabignes dead and his fleet broken."],"answer":"Battle of Salamis","answerText":"Battle of Salamis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">378 triremes, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000, per Aeschylus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Salamis</b> was the sea-fight in the strait between Salamis and the Attic mainland in 480 BCE, in which the Greek fleet destroyed the greater part of the navy of Xerxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus counts 378 Greek triremes, Aeschylus 310, and Thucydides speaks of four hundred of which the Athenians found little short of two-thirds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With Athens burnt and the Peloponnesians pressing to fall back on the Isthmus, Themistocles warned Eurybiades that if the fleet left he would take the Athenians away and settle at Siris in Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the vote went against him he sent Sicinnus, his servant and his sons' attendant, across to the Persian commanders with word that the Greeks were about to run and were at odds among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persians believed it, put men ashore on the islet of Psyttaleia, and spent the night closing both ends of the strait so that nothing could get out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At dawn the Greeks sang the paean and came on, and Aeschylus has them shout to free their land, their children, their wives and the tombs of their fathers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the narrows the Persian line had no room, and ships in the rear pressing forward to be seen doing something for the king ran foul of their own front and splintered the banks of oars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ariabignes, a brother of Xerxes, was killed, and many Persians drowned who could not swim, while Greeks whose ships went down swam across to Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristides landed hoplites on Psyttaleia and killed the Persians posted there; Xerxes watched from a gilded throne with secretaries beside him writing down what was done, though Plutarch's own authorities put that seat in two different places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides has the Athenians claim afterwards that they gave the most ships, the ablest commander and the steadiest nerve, and that it was Themistocles above all who made the fighting happen in the straits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.48, 8.56–8.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=62. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.75–8.96, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=84. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 337–432, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=399. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 13–14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.74, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=74. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Battle_of_Salamis_battle_order.jpg/1280px-Battle_of_Salamis_battle_order.jpg","title":"The strait, with the fleets marked","desc":"An aerial view east along the strait, with the Greek line drawn inside the narrows on the left and the Persian fleet in the open water beyond. The dispositions are a modern reconstruction laid over a photograph of the modern coast.","credit":"Map by the Department of History, United States Military Academy; photograph by Jona Lendering; via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Salamis_battle_order.jpg","alt":"An oblique aerial photograph of a narrow sea channel between hilly coasts, with dark blue blocks marking a fleet inside the narrows and a much larger spread of red blocks in the open water to the east."}},
{"id":"gr-431","num":431,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Sparta supplied the admiral of the allied fleet although Athens supplied most of the ships, so it was the Spartan <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who had to be talked into fighting in the strait.","questions":["Themistocles answered 'Smite, but hear me' when <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raised his staff, and the fleet stayed to fight instead of falling back on the Isthmus.","After Salamis it was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who argued against breaking the bridges on the Hellespont, on the ground that a cornered Xerxes would be more dangerous in Europe than at home."],"answer":"Eurybiades","answerText":"Eurybiades","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commanded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, the allied fleet</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Took</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 of Themistocles' 30 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships led</span><span class=\"dt-v\">378, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Eurybiades</b> son of Euryclides was the Spartan who commanded the allied Greek fleet in 480 BCE, at Artemisium and then at Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta supplied the admiral although Athens supplied most of the ships, because the allies said they would break the fleet up rather than be led by the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Artemisium he was ready to withdraw once he saw the size of the Persian fleet, and the Euboeans, wanting time to get their households away, paid Themistocles thirty talents to keep the ships where they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Themistocles passed five of that money to him as though it were a present of his own, and he stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Salamis he was again for falling back on the Isthmus, where the Peloponnesian army was gathering behind a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch calls him faint-hearted in a crisis and preserves the exchange the tradition remembered: when he said that at the games those who start too soon are caned, Themistocles answered that those who lag behind win no crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When he raised his staff as though to strike, Themistocles said 'Smite, but hear me', and he listened and was won over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus states the arrangement without embarrassment: Eurybiades was the admiral, while Themistocles managed the fleet's affairs, and it was to him that everyone looked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The judgement of weakness is not the whole man, for after Salamis he argued against sailing to break the bridges on the Hellespont, saying that a Xerxes shut into Europe would have to make his cause prosper there and that a beaten enemy is better let go; the other Peloponnesian commanders agreed with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Greeks who met at the Isthmus could not agree who deserved the prize and went home leaving it undecided, but at Sparta the Lacedaemonians gave him a crown of olive for valour and Themistocles another for wisdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.2–8.5, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=5. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.49, 8.56–8.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=62. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.108–8.110, 8.123–8.124, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=124. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.12, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=12. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece"]},
{"id":"gr-432","num":432,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Alone among Xerxes' captains, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> advised him not to fight at sea before Salamis, saying the Greeks were as much stronger there as men are stronger than women.","questions":["Chased by an Athenian trireme, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rammed an allied ship of Calynda, and Xerxes, told she had sunk an enemy, said his men had become women and his women men.","The Athenians offered ten thousand drachmas to anyone who took <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alive, so angry were they that a woman was making war on Athens."],"answer":"Artemisia I of Caria","answerText":"Artemisia I of Caria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 cities: Halicarnassus, Cos, Nisyrus, Calydnos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 ships, 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Price on her</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 drachmas, alive</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Artemisia I of Caria</b> was the ruler of Halicarnassus who fought for Xerxes at Salamis in 480 BCE with five ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, a Halicarnassian himself, marvels most at her of all the king's captains: a woman who took over her dead husband's rule though she had a grown son, and went on campaign under no compulsion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her father was Lygdamis, her mother Cretan, and she led Halicarnassus, Cos, Nisyrus and Calydnos, all Dorian cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her ships were reckoned the best in the fleet after the Sidonians', and she gave the king the best advice of any of his allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked before the battle, she alone told him to spare his ships and keep off the sea, since the Greeks were as much stronger there as men are stronger than women and would scatter home if he waited; he was pleased and did the opposite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Chased in the fighting by an Athenian ship and hemmed in by her own side, she rammed and sank an allied Calyndian vessel with its king Damasithymus aboard, and the Athenian broke off, taking her for a Greek or a deserter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus will not say whether she meant it or the ship fell in her path, and notes that nobody from the Calyndian crew survived to accuse her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xerxes, watching, was told she had sunk an enemy and said that his men had become women and his women men; the Athenians, indignant that a woman was making war on them, had offered ten thousand drachmas for her alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Afterwards he asked her again, and she told him to march home and leave Mardonius behind, which was what he wanted to hear; he thanked her and sent her to Ephesus with his sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polyaenus collected further stratagems under her name, among them that she carried both Greek and Persian colours and flew whichever suited; and Pausanias saw her among the marble Persians of the Persian Stoa at Sparta, raised from the spoils of the war she lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.99, 8.68A–8.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=99. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.87–8.88, 8.93, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=87. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.101–8.103, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=102. [Open access]","Polyaenus, <i>Stratagems of War</i> 8.53, trans. R. Shepherd, https://www.attalus.org/translate/polyaenus8B.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.11.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=3:chapter=11. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","greece","iran"]},
{"id":"gr-433","num":433,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A march of 45 days took Xerxes back to the Hellespont, and Herodotus measures the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by saying he brought home as good as none of the host he came with.","questions":["Aeschylus sends the army home across a Strymon frozen out of season, and the sun then opening the ice, which is his version of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Herodotus records as plague and starvation.","Justin has the king cross to Abydos in a fishing-boat at the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and cannot resist the contrast with the man the whole ocean could scarcely hold."],"answer":"Persian retreat","answerText":"Persian retreat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Retreat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, after Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">March home</span><span class=\"dt-v\">45 days, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left behind</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300,000 men, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Persian retreat</b> was the withdrawal of Xerxes and most of his army from Greece after Salamis in 480 BCE, with Mardonius left in Thessaly and a picked force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mardonius held the season wrong for campaigning and chose his own troops — the Immortals, the thousand horse, and the Medes, Sacae, Bactrians and Indians entire — some 300,000 men by Herodotus' count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes reached the Hellespont in 45 days and brought back, Herodotus says, as good as none of his host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They ate the produce of every place they passed, and where there was none they ate grass, bark and leaves; plague and dysentery went with them, and the sick were left in the cities of Thessaly and Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They found the bridges broken by a storm and were ferried to Abydos, where food was plentiful and so many died of gorging and of the change of water that fewer still reached Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus knew another version, in which the king took ship from Eion and the pilot told him the only escape was to lighten the load, at which the Persians on deck bowed and leapt into the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He rejects it, arguing that a king would have sent his nobles into the hold and thrown an equal number of Phoenician rowers overboard, and that the return was by road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aeschylus, writing for men who had been there, brings the army home a different way again, through Boeotia, Phocis, Thessaly and Macedonia to the Strymon, which a god froze out of season so that they crossed on the ice; then the sun opened the middle and men went through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus makes the flight a second trick of Themistocles, who sent the same servant to warn Xerxes that the Greeks meant to destroy the bridge of boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Justin has him reach Abydos with a few attendants and cross in a fishing-boat, and cannot resist the contrast: the man whom the ocean could scarcely hold, shrunk into a little boat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.113–8.117, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=115. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.118–8.119, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=118. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 480–514, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=480. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.19, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=19. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.13, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 33–34, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Mount_Pangaion%2C_Mt_Paggaio_or_Paggeo%2C_winter_landscape%2C_horizontal_image%2C_Nikisiani%2C_Greece.jpg/1280px-Mount_Pangaion%2C_Mt_Paggaio_or_Paggeo%2C_winter_landscape%2C_horizontal_image%2C_Nikisiani%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Pangaion in winter","desc":"The snowy summit of Mount Pangaion in Thrace, on the road the army took home. Aeschylus names this mountain immediately before the night on which, he says, a god brought winter early and froze the Strymon.","credit":"Photograph by Zsolyomi, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Pangaion,_Mt_Paggaio_or_Paggeo,_winter_landscape,_horizontal_image,_Nikisiani,_Greece.jpg","alt":"A snow-covered rocky peak rising out of a bank of cloud, with dark frost-whitened wooded ridges below it and blue sky above."}},
{"id":"gr-434","num":434,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Alexander of Macedon carried an offer of rebuilt temples and self-rule to Salamis, the opening move of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the campaign left behind when Xerxes went home.","questions":["The council stoned Lycidas for proposing that his city consider the terms, and the women stoned his family, which is how Athens answered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the spring of 479 BCE.","Attica was found empty a second time, ten months after Xerxes had taken it, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with the city burnt again and the army withdrawn into Boeotia."],"answer":"Mardonius in Greece","answerText":"Mardonius in Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Winter</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 – 479 BCE, in Thessaly</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athens retaken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 months after Xerxes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, into Boeotia</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Mardonius in Greece</b> is the campaign of the winter and summer after Salamis, in which the general Xerxes left behind tried first to win Athens over and then took the city again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wintered in Thessaly and sent a man of Europus named Mys round the oracles to test them, though Herodotus says nobody could tell him what the question was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The answers, he says, advised making the Athenians an ally, and the envoy chosen was Alexander of Macedon, tied by marriage to a Persian house and a friend of Athens besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The terms were generous: by Diodorus' account the Athenians might have any land in Greece they chose, their walls and temples rebuilt, and their own laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They refused in two directions at once, telling Alexander that so long as the sun holds his present course they will make no agreement with Xerxes, and telling the Spartans that no gold or land could buy them, because of the burnt temples of their gods and because the Greeks are one in blood and speech, with shrines, sacrifices and ways of living in common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He marched south, found Attica empty again, and took a city with no men in it, ten months after Xerxes had taken it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From Athens he sent a second envoy, Murychides, with the same terms, expecting a captured Attica to soften them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A councillor named Lycidas proposed putting the offer to the people; the council stoned him to death, and the Athenian women, hearing of it, went to his house and stoned his wife and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Demosthenes told the same story to an Athenian jury a century and a half later but called the man Cyrsilus: the deed remembered and the name not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the answer was plain he burnt what was left of Athens, threw down every wall and house and temple still standing, and withdrew into Boeotia, where the ground suited his horse and Thebes was friendly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.133, 8.136, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=136. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.143–8.144, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=144. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.3–9.5, 9.13, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=5. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>On the Crown</i> 18.204, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0072:speech=18:section=204. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.28, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=28. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/View_of_the_Thessalian_plain_from_the_acropolis_of_ancient_Pharsalos.jpg/1280px-View_of_the_Thessalian_plain_from_the_acropolis_of_ancient_Pharsalos.jpg","title":"The Thessalian plain","desc":"The plain of Thessaly from the acropolis of ancient Pharsalos, with the modern town of Farsala below. This is the flat country the army wintered in, and the kind of ground its cavalry needed, which is why it went back to Boeotia rather than stay in Attica.","credit":"Photograph by Panegyrics of Granovetter, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Thessalian_plain_from_the_acropolis_of_ancient_Pharsalos.jpg","alt":"A wide green and gold farming plain seen from a wooded hillside, with a red-roofed town in the middle distance and low blue hills along the horizon."}},
{"id":"gr-435","num":435,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"After days facing each other across the Asopus, the Greeks moved by night and were caught strung out at dawn, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began badly and ended the invasion.","questions":["Herodotus says only 91 Spartans, 17 Tegeans and 52 Athenians died in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a figure Plutarch calls astonishing and replaces with 1,360.","Mardonius fell on a white horse among his thousand picked Persians, and with him the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned, the survivors running for a wooden stockade the Athenians had to breach."],"answer":"Battle of Plataea","answerText":"Battle of Plataea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, in Boeotia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek hoplites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">38,700, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">160 to 10,000, by source</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Plataea</b> was the land battle in Boeotia in 479 BCE that ended the Persian invasion of Greece, fought between the allied army under <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Pausanias_the_regent\">Pausanias</span> and the force Mardonius had kept in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus counts 38,700 Greek hoplites and about 69,500 light-armed men, and puts Mardonius' army at 300,000 with perhaps 50,000 Greek allies; Diodorus makes it 100,000 against 500,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two sides faced each other across the Asopus for days, neither willing to attack on the other's ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persian cavalry, mounted archers hard to close with, shot into the Greek lines and then fouled and blocked the Gargaphian spring, which was the whole army's water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Greeks moved by night to better ground; the move went wrong in the dark, and first light found the army strung out in pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Where Mardonius fought, on a white horse among a thousand picked Persians, the line held, and Herodotus says they were brave men but fought as good as naked, having no armour over their clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When he fell the rest gave way: killed by a Spartan named Aeimnestus, says Herodotus; by an Arimnestus who crushed his head with a stone, says Plutarch, who adds that an oracle had foretold exactly that death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The survivors ran for their wooden stockade, which the Spartans had no skill to storm; the Athenians came up and breached it, and the Tegeans were first through and took Mardonius' bronze manger, which they dedicated to Athena Alea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus then gives figures that have troubled every reader since: of 260,000 left after Artabazus fled with 40,000, scarcely 3,000 survived, while the Greeks lost 91 Spartans, 17 Tegeans and 52 Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch calls that astonishing, puts the Greek dead at 1,360, and argues that the graves themselves show the victory was shared; Diodorus says more than ten thousand were buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.28–9.32, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=29. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.49–9.52, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=49. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.63–9.70, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=70. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides%2A.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.30, 11.33, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=30. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/The_battlefield_of_Plataea_and_the_remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_the_city_on_April_10%2C_2019.jpg/1280px-The_battlefield_of_Plataea_and_the_remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_the_city_on_April_10%2C_2019.jpg","title":"The plain of Plataea","desc":"The plain north of Plataea, seen across the line of the city wall. The Asopus runs through the flat ground beyond, and the two armies faced each other across it for days before the fighting began.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_battlefield_of_Plataea_and_the_remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_the_city_on_April_10,_2019.jpg","alt":"A meadow of yellow wildflowers in the foreground with a low line of tumbled squared blocks, opening onto a wide patchwork of green and ploughed fields and hazy hills under a cloudy sky."}},
{"id":"gr-436","num":436,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The victor of Plataea ended walled into the temple of Athena of the Brazen House and starved, which is how Sparta dealt with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once his intriguing with the helots was proved.","questions":["Told to impale Mardonius' head as the Persians had done to Leonidas, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that the deed was fitter for barbarians than for Greeks.","A couplet cut on the Delphic tripod claiming the monument as his own was erased by the Spartans, and it was remembered against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when he was later recalled."],"answer":"Pausanias the regent","answerText":"Pausanias the regent","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Regent for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Pleistarchus, from 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commanded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, at Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statues owed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 in bronze, to the goddess</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Pausanias the regent</b> was the Spartan who commanded the Greek army at Plataea in 479 BCE, son of Cleombrotus and guardian of the boy king Pleistarchus, son of Leonidas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The command was his because Pleistarchus was still a child, and he took a kinsman, Euryanax son of Dorieus, as his colleague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls the victory he won there the most glorious of all the victories we know of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When an Aeginetan named Lampon urged him to impale Mardonius' head as the Persians had done to Leonidas, he answered that the deed was fitter for barbarians than for Greeks, and blameworthy even in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He had Mardonius' cooks lay out a Persian dinner beside a Laconian one, called the Greek generals in to look at both, and asked them to consider the folly of a man who came with all that to take away their poverty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Two years later, commanding the allied fleet at Byzantium, he had become insufferable: Thucydides says there was more of the mimicry of a despot in him than of a general, and the Ionians went to Athens to be rid of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Recalled to Sparta, he was censured for private oppressions and acquitted on the heaviest charge, which was medism, and was never given the command again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was remembered against him afterwards was the couplet he had cut on the tripod at Delphi claiming the monument for himself, which the Spartans erased and replaced with the names of the cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was also intriguing with the helots, promising them freedom and citizenship, and a courier from Argilos opened his last letter, found an order for his own death in it, and turned informer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He took sanctuary in the temple of Athena of the Brazen House and was walled in and starved, his mother laying the first brick against the door if Diodorus is right, and Delphi afterwards made Sparta give the goddess two bronze statues in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.10, 9.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=10. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.78–9.79, 9.82, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=79. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.94–1.95, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=95. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.128–1.134, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=132. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.45, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=45. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Remains_of_the_Temple_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_Temple_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","title":"The Brazen House","desc":"The site of the temple of Athena Chalkioikos on the acropolis of Sparta, with modern Sparta below and the Taygetus range beyond. Very little of the sanctuary is left above ground; this is where he took refuge and was walled in.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_Temple_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","alt":"A fenced patch of low stone foundations among grass and pines, with a town spread across the valley below and a long snow-topped mountain range on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gr-437","num":437,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Samian envoys asked the Greek fleet at Delos to free the Greeks of Asia, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to be fought on a beach opposite Samos in 479 BCE.","questions":["A rumour of victory in Boeotia ran through the line as it advanced, and Herodotus says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Plataea really did fall on the same day.","The Milesians, posted to guard the passes, led the beaten Persians into their enemies after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Ionia revolted from the king a second time."],"answer":"Battle of Mycale","answerText":"Battle of Mycale","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, the day of Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60,000 or 100,000, by source</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">250 triremes, per Diodorus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Mycale</b> was fought on the Ionian coast opposite Samos in 479 BCE, when the Greek fleet landed and stormed the beached Persian ships and the camp round them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Samian envoys came to the Greeks at Delos asking them to free the Greeks of Asia, and the fleet sailed east under the Spartan Leotychides and the Athenian Xanthippus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Persians would not fight at sea; they sent the Phoenicians away, beached the rest under the army Xerxes had left to hold Ionia, and fenced them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus puts that army at 60,000 under Tigranes, the tallest and noblest man in Persia; Diodorus makes it about 100,000, gathered from Sardis and nearby towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Leotychides sailed close inshore and called to the Ionians in the enemy camp to remember their freedom and the watchword 'Hebe', so as either to win them over or to have them mistrusted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> As the line advanced a rumour ran through it that Mardonius had been beaten in Boeotia, and a herald's wand was found at the water's edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus says the rumour was true and the battles fell on the same day, Plataea early and this one in the afternoon, with a precinct of Eleusinian Demeter on each field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Athenians and those with them had the beach and the level ground and pressed hard so the Spartans, circling through a ravine, should not have the victory; the shields went down and the Persians ran inside the wall, the Greeks coming in with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tigranes and Mardontes were killed, and the Milesians, posted to guard the passes, led the fleeing Persians into their enemies and killed them, so that Ionia revolted a second time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Greeks burnt the ships and the wall, then argued at Samos whether to move the Ionians west and leave Asia to the Persians; the Athenians refused, the islanders were sworn into the alliance, and the fleet sailed for the Hellespont, where the Athenians stayed the winter to take Sestos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.90–9.96, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=96. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.98–9.101, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=101. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.102–9.106, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=102. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.34, 11.36, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=34. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.89, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=89. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg/1280px-Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg","title":"Mount Mycale across the strait from Samos","desc":"The mainland headland of Mycale seen from the north coast of Samos. The Persian ships were beached on the shore beneath it in 479 BCE, and the Greek fleet crossed this water to attack them.","credit":"Photograph by Athens2004, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg","alt":"A hazy blue strait of sea seen from a scrub-covered hillside, with a long low rocky spit in the foreground and a high mountain ridge rising beyond the water."}},
{"id":"gr-438","num":438,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A tenth of the spoil taken at Plataea paid for a gold tripod at Delphi, and the twisted bronze that carried it is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, standing today in Istanbul.","questions":["Thucydides says the Spartans erased a couplet claiming the victory for Pausanias alone and cut the names of the allied cities on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its place.","The Phocian leaders took its gold and Constantine took what was left to his new capital, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives as three headless bronze snakes on a pedestal."],"answer":"Serpent Column","answerText":"Serpent Column","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, at Delphi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE, by the Phocians</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century CE, to Constantinople</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Serpent Column</b> is the bronze monument dedicated at Delphi from a tithe of the spoil taken at Plataea in 479 BCE, three snakes twined into one shaft whose heads carried a gold tripod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the Greeks set that tripod nearest the altar and made two further offerings from the same tithe, a bronze Zeus of ten cubits at Olympia and a Poseidon of seven at the Isthmus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides reports that Pausanias had a couplet cut on it claiming the victory for himself, that the Spartans erased the lines at once, and that they cut in their place the names of the cities which had helped overthrow the Persian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus gives a different couplet instead, in which the saviours of far-flung Hellas raise the gift after delivering their states from slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The list is what makes the object matter, being a record made at the time of which states claimed a share in the victory; Grosvenor counted 31 names on the coils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias the traveller, writing centuries later, saw only the bronze: the Phocian leaders, he says, had not left the gold as they left the metal under it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eusebius records that Constantine stripped the temples of their bronzes and had the Delphic tripods set up in the hippodrome of his new city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The coils have stood in that space ever since, and Grosvenor measured them at 5.7 metres (18 feet 9 inches), broken and headless, filled with stones dropped in by passers-by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One head survives in the museum at Istanbul, its torn edges showing it was wrenched away; how and when the others went, Grosvenor says, nobody knows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He could find nothing above the tenth coil, the letters there having worn off, but read the lower ones where buried earth had protected them, naming the Tirynthians, Plataeans, Thespians, Mycenaeans, Ceans, Malians and Tenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.81, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=81. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.132, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=132. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.33, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=33. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.13.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=13. [Open access]","Eusebius, <i>Life of Constantine</i> 3.54, trans. Ernest Cushing Richardson, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd ser., vol. 1, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace, New Advent, https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/25023.htm. [Open access]","Edwin A. Grosvenor, <i>The Hippodrome of Constantinople and Its Still Existing Monuments</i>, London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle and Rivington, 1889, 32, 50-54, https://archive.org/details/hippodromeconst00grosgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","history","warfare","greece","art"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Y%C4%B1lanl%C4%B1_S%C3%BCtun.jpg/1280px-Y%C4%B1lanl%C4%B1_S%C3%BCtun.jpg","title":"The column today, in the old hippodrome","desc":"The three twined bronze snakes stand in a sunken enclosure in Istanbul, headless, with the gold tripod they carried long gone and one of the holes broken in the coils plainly visible.","credit":"Photograph by Cobija, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Y%C4%B1lanl%C4%B1_S%C3%BCtun.jpg","alt":"A tall shaft of green-patinated bronze, made of three snake bodies twisted together, rising from a paved circular pit below street level, with a ragged hole in one coil and nothing at the top."}},
{"id":"gr-439","num":439,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus opens by asking what cause Greeks and barbarians had for making war on each other, and the nine books of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are his long answer to that question.","questions":["Rival accounts are left standing side by side in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the Athenians say Adeimantus fled under sail from Salamis and the Corinthians deny it flatly.","Herodotus asks his reader to hold, for the whole of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, that setting down what he has been told is not the same thing as believing it."],"answer":"Histories","answerText":"Histories","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Narrative ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, at Sestos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Books</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9, in the standard division</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xerxes' army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,283,220, by his own count</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Histories</b> of Herodotus is the earliest surviving prose account of the Persian Wars, nine books tracing the rise of Persia and its collision with the Greek cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens by naming itself an inquiry, set down so that human deeds should not fade with time and the great works of Greeks and barbarians alike should not lose their glory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That same sentence gives the subject as a question, namely what cause the two had for making war on one another, rather than as a chronicle of events.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Having reported Persian and Phoenician stories of the mythical abductions, he declines to say which is true and turns instead to the man he says he knows began the wrongs against the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds there that he will speak of small and great cities alike, since states once great have become small and human prosperity never stays in one place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His rule for the rest is stated outright: it is his business to set down what he is told, and no business of his to believe it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So rival versions stand side by side, as when the Athenians said the Corinthian Adeimantus hoisted sail and fled from Salamis, the Corinthians denied it flatly, and the rest of Greece bore them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He names informants where he can, among them Thersander of Orchomenus, who described a Persian weeping at a Theban banquet over the ruin he knew was coming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where a figure is his own arithmetic he says so, doubling the fighting men to allow for the baggage train and reaching 5,283,220 for the force Xerxes led as far as Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thucydides, a generation later, set out a deliberately different practice, composing speeches to what each occasion demanded and testing his narrative against eyewitnesses rather than taking it from the first source to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.1, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.5, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=5. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.152, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=152. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.94, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=94. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.16, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=16. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.186, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=186. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.22, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=22. [Open access]"],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg/1280px-POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg","title":"A papyrus fragment of Book VIII","desc":"Papyrus Oxyrhynchus 2099, a scrap of the eighth book written out in Greek capitals with no spaces between the words, from the rubbish mounds of a town in Roman Egypt.","credit":"Papyrus Oxyrhynchus 2099, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg","alt":"A ragged brown papyrus fragment with about a dozen lines of large Greek capital letters written continuously, the edges torn and several holes through the sheet."}},
{"id":"gr-440","num":440,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Born a subject of the Great King at Halicarnassus in Caria, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended his life as a colonist at Thurii, where the Suda says he lies buried in the market-place.","questions":["The writer who went upriver as far as Elephantine to look for himself, and took everything beyond it by question and hearsay, was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose subject was the war with Persia.","Aristotle quotes the opening sentence of the great war narrative as the exposition of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Thurii, where the received text of the same sentence says Halicarnassus."],"answer":"Herodotus","answerText":"Herodotus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 484 BCE, per Aulus Gellius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Halicarnassus, in Caria</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still writing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 431 BCE, into the Peloponnesian War</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Herodotus</b> was a Greek prose writer of the 5th century BCE, born at Halicarnassus in Caria, a city on the Anatolian coast that was then subject to the Persian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Aulus Gellius put his birth in 484 BCE, and his own work shows him alive into the early years of the Peloponnesian War, so his life spanned the rise and fall of Athenian pre-eminence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tradition has him spend time on Samos, return home and help drive out the tyrant Lygdamis, then leave again when he found himself disliked by his countrymen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He went as a volunteer to the new colony at Thurii in southern Italy, and the Suda says he died there and lies buried in its market-place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The travels behind the work took in much of Asia Minor, Egypt as far south as Aswan, Cyrene, Syria, perhaps lands as far east as Mesopotamia, and the northern coast of the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He tells the reader where his own eyes stop, having gone upriver to Elephantine to look for himself and taken everything beyond it by question and hearsay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To settle how old the worship of Heracles was he took ship to Tyre, saw the temple there with its pillar of gold and its pillar of emerald, and asked the priests when it had been built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Part way through the Egyptian book he draws a line outright, saying that all he has written so far rests on his own observation, judgement and inquiry, and that what follows is Egyptian report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even his name is unsettled in antiquity: the work as received opens 'Herodotus of Halicarnassus', but Aristotle quotes that same sentence as the exposition of 'Herodotus of Thurii'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was argued with from early on, and Plutarch devoted a whole treatise to the charge that he wrote of the Greek cities with malice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["A. D. Godley, general introduction to <i>Herodotus</i>, vol. 1, Loeb Classical Library (London: W. Heinemann, 1921), vii-xiv, https://archive.org/details/herodotus01hero. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.29, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=29. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.44, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=44. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.99, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=99. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Rhetoric</i> 3.9, trans. J. H. Freese, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0060:book=3:chapter=9. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.1, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","history","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Erodoto%2C_replica_del_III_sec._dc_da_orig._greco_del_400-350_ac_ca.%2C_6146.JPG/1280px-Erodoto%2C_replica_del_III_sec._dc_da_orig._greco_del_400-350_ac_ca.%2C_6146.JPG","title":"A Roman herm with the name cut on it","desc":"A marble portrait from the Farnese collection, now in Naples, copying a Greek original. The name is cut in Greek capitals across the shaft below the shoulders, which is what makes the identification secure.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erodoto%2C_replica_del_III_sec._dc_da_orig._greco_del_400-350_ac_ca.%2C_6146.JPG","alt":"A weathered white marble bust of a balding, heavily bearded man on a squared pillar, with worn Greek capital letters incised along the front of the pillar beneath him."}},
{"id":"gr-441","num":441,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Aeschylus set his tragedy at Susa and gave every speaking part to the losing side, so that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands as the one surviving Greek tragedy on a historical rather than a mythical subject.","questions":["A messenger at Susa counts 310 Greek ships against a thousand of the king's in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, then reports the shout that went up to free fatherland, children and wives.","Darius rises from his tomb to blame burnt temples and to foretell a defeat on Plataean soil in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was first produced at Athens in 472 BCE."],"answer":"Persians","answerText":"Persians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Produced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">472 BCE, at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">eight years after Salamis</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Persians</b> is a tragedy of Aeschylus, first produced at Athens in 472 BCE, and the one surviving Greek tragedy that takes a recent historical event rather than a myth for its subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It is set at Susa rather than anywhere in Greece, and everyone on stage is on the losing side: a chorus of Persian elders, the queen mother Atossa, the ghost of Darius, and Xerxes himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Atossa has to ask her counsellors where Athens lies, which lets an Athenian audience hear its own city and its own strength described from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A messenger brings the news of Salamis and gives the numbers: 310 Greek ships against a thousand of the king's, of which 207 were the fast ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He describes the Greek line coming on at dawn behind a paean and a shout to free fatherland, children and wives, the temples of the fathers' gods and the tombs of the ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The retreat is told as suffering rather than as manoeuvre, the army starving through Thrace until a god froze the Strymon out of season and the ice gave way under the men crossing at sunrise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Darius, raised from his tomb, reads the disaster as payment for temples burnt and images thrown down, and foretells a further defeat still to come on Plataean soil at the Dorian spear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The play ends with Xerxes coming on in torn clothes with a thin retinue, and a long antiphonal lament between him and the elders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The risk in taking such a subject was known at Athens: Herodotus says Phrynichus was fined a thousand drachmas for a play on the fall of Miletus that reminded the audience of its own loss, and the piece was banned for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Aeschylus had fought at Marathon, as his own epitaph records, and his play was staged again later in Sicily at the wish of Hiero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 215-248, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=215. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 331-352, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=331. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 395-432, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=402. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 480-514, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=480. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 800-831, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=800. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 909-930, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=909. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.21, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=21. [Open access]","A. O. Prickard, introduction to <i>The Persae of Aeschylus</i> (London: Macmillan, 1879), ix-xxxvii, https://archive.org/details/gashlouprsaiper01aescgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/The_Theatre_of_Dionysus_in_Athens_on_October_7%2C_2018.jpg/1280px-The_Theatre_of_Dionysus_in_Athens_on_October_7%2C_2018.jpg","title":"The theatre of Dionysus, below the Acropolis","desc":"The site where the play was first staged, seen from the slope above with the city beyond. Most of the stone now standing is later than 472 BCE, the theatre having been rebuilt more than once.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Theatre_of_Dionysus_in_Athens_on_October_7%2C_2018.jpg","alt":"A hillside above a semicircular ruined theatre with a flat paved orchestra and broken rows of stone seating, with a modern city and low hills spreading out behind it."}},
{"id":"gr-442","num":442,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Giving the Persian king earth and water, or marching in his army, was the charge of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Herodotus lists the peoples who did it without being compelled to.","questions":["The Greeks who declared war swore that a tenth of the property of everyone guilty of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would go to the god at Delphi if they came through the invasion.","Thebes was besieged after Plataea until it gave up the leading men accused of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whom Pausanias then carried off to Corinth and killed without a trial."],"answer":"Medism","answerText":"Medism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oath sworn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before the invasion of 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thebes besieged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, after Plataea</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Medism</b> was the Greek charge of taking the Persian side, named from the Medes, and it covered everything from giving the king earth and water to marching in his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The demand for earth and water was the test, and Xerxes sent no heralds at all to Athens or Sparta, since heralds of Darius had been thrown into the Pit at one city and a well at the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus lists those who gave themselves up without being compelled: Thessalians, Dolopes, Enienes, Perrhaebians, Locrians, Magnesians, Melians, Achaeans of Phthia, Thebans, and all Boeotia except Thespiae and Plataea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against them the Greeks who declared war swore that if they won they would dedicate to the god at Delphi a tenth of the property of every Greek who had gone over of free will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The charge was already a weapon between Greek cities: when Aegina gave earth and water to Darius, the Athenians went to Sparta and accused the islanders of betraying Hellas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Refusing was not always a matter of principle, and Herodotus says so outright: the Phocians alone in their region held out, and would certainly have gone over had the Thessalians not been on the other side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Plataea the allied army marched on Thebes and refused to leave the ground before the city until the leading men were handed over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Attaginus escaped and Pausanias let his sons go, saying that sons were not party to the treason; the men who were surrendered expected to buy off the charge, so he sent the army home and had them killed at Corinth without a trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Theban defence, put by Thucydides two generations later, was that the city had then been held by a narrow cabal which brought the Persians in to secure its own power, so that the city was not its own mistress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That same speech turns the charge round, arguing that Plataea kept clear only because Athens did, and afterwards 'Atticized' just as readily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.49, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=49. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.132, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=132. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.133, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=133. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.30, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=30. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.86-9.88, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=86. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.62, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=62. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","politics","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Ruins_at_the_Sanctuary_of_Apollo_in_Delphi_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg/1280px-Ruins_at_the_Sanctuary_of_Apollo_in_Delphi_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg","title":"The sanctuary of Apollo at Delphi","desc":"Terrace walls and foundations below the Phaedriades cliffs. It was to the god here that the Greeks who fought swore to dedicate a tenth of the property of every city that had gone over to the king of its own accord.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_at_the_Sanctuary_of_Apollo_in_Delphi_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg","alt":"Grassy terraces of tumbled ancient stone walls and cut blocks climbing a hillside, with dark cypresses in front of a high pale limestone cliff."}},
{"id":"gr-443","num":443,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Cut on a stone or a bronze to say whom it stands for, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was written in elegiac couplets, and Herodotus quotes three of them from the graves at Thermopylae.","questions":["Athens set a four-horse chariot on the acropolis out of Chalcidian ransom money and had a four-line <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut on it naming whom the city had beaten in battle.","The Spartans erased the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Pausanias had cut on the Delphic tripod claiming the victory for himself, and put the names of the allied cities in its place."],"answer":"epigram","answerText":"epigram","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Thermopylae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, three stones</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On the tripod</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, recut by Sparta</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"An <b>epigram</b> in Greek is an inscription, the word meaning exactly that, and the earliest are verse cut on a stone or an object to say what it is or whom it stands for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before prose was in use, anything an inscription said beyond a bare name had to be in verse, and the form settled on the elegiac couplet, which stayed the metre of inscriptions ever after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persian Wars produced the most famous group of them, and Herodotus quotes three cut over the graves at Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One stands for the whole force, saying that four thousand from the Peloponnese once fought three million there; one is for the Spartans alone, telling the passer-by to report that they lie obedient to orders; the third names the seer Megistias.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus adds that the Amphictyons put up all of them except the seer's, which Simonides had cut out of guest-friendship, so the earliest source gives him the least famous of the three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They were not only for the dead: out of a tenth of Chalcidian ransom money Athens set a bronze four-horse chariot on the acropolis with four lines naming whom the city had beaten and what the defeat had cost them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus writes that the fetters of those same prisoners still hung on walls the Persian fire had charred, which is his own eye rather than a report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One could also be taken away: the Spartans erased the couplet Pausanias had cut on the Delphic tripod claiming the victory for himself, and put the names of the allied cities in its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those names went on the bronze coils that carried the tripod, and 31 of them have been traced there since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Simonides is the name the later tradition attached to the whole class: 59 stand under it in the Palatine manuscript and 18 more in Planudes, with nine others doubtfully his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["J. W. Mackail, introduction and biographical index to <i>Select Epigrams from the Greek Anthology</i> (London: Longmans, Green, and Co., 1890), 1-7, 287-88, https://archive.org/details/selectepigramsfr00mackuoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.228, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=228. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.77, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=77. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.132, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=132. [Open access]","Edwin A. Grosvenor, <i>The Hippodrome of Constantinople and Its Still Existing Monuments</i>, London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle and Rivington, 1889, 32, 50-54, https://archive.org/details/hippodromeconst00grosgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","poetry","greece","history"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Cod._Pal._graec._23%2C_Anthologia_Palatina%2C_5r.jpg/1280px-Cod._Pal._graec._23%2C_Anthologia_Palatina%2C_5r.jpg","title":"A page of the Palatine manuscript","desc":"Codex Palatinus graecus 23 at Heidelberg, the Byzantine copy through which most surviving Greek epigrams have come down, including the couplet Herodotus read at Thermopylae.","credit":"Universitatsbibliothek Heidelberg, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cod._Pal._graec._23%2C_Anthologia_Palatina%2C_5r.jpg","alt":"A stained parchment page covered in close brown Greek minuscule writing in ruled lines, with red library stamps at the foot."}},
{"id":"gr-444","num":444,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Sparta asked Athens not to rebuild after the invasion, so Themistocles went to argue the point while the city hurried up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sparing no building for its stone.","questions":["Mardonius had pulled down whatever was left standing, and the foundations of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still show column drums taken from tombs and unworked blocks laid in as they came.","Spartan inspectors were quietly detained at Athens until the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood high enough to defend, and only then did Themistocles tell his hosts the city was fortified."],"answer":"Themistoclean Wall","answerText":"Themistoclean Wall","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, after the invasion</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">on the ruins Mardonius left</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Themistoclean Wall</b> is the circuit Athens threw round itself in 479 BCE, as soon as the Persians had gone, and the one the city kept for the rest of the classical period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There was little to repair: Mardonius had burnt Athens before withdrawing and pulled down whatever wall, house or temple was still standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides says only isolated stretches of the old circuit survived, and that most houses were ruins apart from a few the Persian grandees had taken as quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta sent an embassy asking Athens not to build, and to help pull down the walls of other cities outside the Peloponnese, arguing that a third invasion would then find no strong base of the kind Thebes had given it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides says the real motive was another, that the allies were alarmed at the new Athenian navy and at what the city had shown in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Themistocles had himself sent to Sparta at once and told the city to hold his colleagues back until the wall stood high enough to defend, with the whole population at work, men, women and children, and no building spared for its stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Sparta he denied the reports, advised his hosts to send men of their own to look, and had those inspectors quietly detained at Athens until his colleagues reached him and he could say outright that the city was walled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus tells it differently in one respect, making Themistocles offer himself as surety and be put under guard, so that the release becomes an exchange of hostages rather than a bluff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sparta showed no open anger, and Thucydides adds that feeling there was still friendly towards Athens because of what she had done against the Mede.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The haste is visible in the masonry, the foundations laid of stones of every kind, some neither worked nor fitted but set in the order the carriers brought them, with column drums from tombs and carved blocks built in among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.89, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=89. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.90, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=90. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.91-1.92, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=91. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.93, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=93. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.13, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=13. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.39-11.40, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=39. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","history","warfare","greece","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Remains_of_the_Themistoclean_Wall_in_Kerameikos_Cemetery_on_April_12%2C_2021.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_Themistoclean_Wall_in_Kerameikos_Cemetery_on_April_12%2C_2021.jpg","title":"A surviving stretch at the Kerameikos","desc":"Courses of stones of visibly different sizes, colours and finishes, on the line of the circuit of 479 BCE. The wall was repaired and rebuilt more than once afterwards, so not every block standing here belongs to the first building.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_Themistoclean_Wall_in_Kerameikos_Cemetery_on_April_12%2C_2021.jpg","alt":"A low ancient wall of mismatched limestone blocks in uneven courses running across a grassy excavated area, with a modern street railing and trees above it."}},
{"id":"gr-445","num":445,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Themistocles thought three harbours better than the one at Phaleron, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went up two wagons thick, of squared blocks clamped on the outside with iron and lead.","questions":["Only about half the height Themistocles intended was reached on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose bulk was meant to let a garrison of men unfit to row hold it alone.","Diodorus says a second embassy went to Sparta about a first-rate harbour for the common Greek good, and under that cover the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was finished before anyone expected."],"answer":"Piraeus wall","answerText":"Piraeus wall","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 479 BCE, on his urging</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Harbours</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, against one at Phaleron</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Triremes added</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 a year, per Diodorus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Piraeus wall</b> is the fortification Themistocles pressed on Athens round the rocky peninsula that replaced Phaleron as the city's harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias says the Piraeus was an old deme and no port at all until Themistocles was archon, Athens having used Phaleron because the sea comes nearest to the city there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What recommended the change was three natural harbours against Phaleron's one, and Diodorus adds that the Phaleric bay was small where this could be made the best and largest harbour in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says the walls had been begun in that archonship and that Themistocles now persuaded the city to finish them, having been the first to tell the Athenians to stick to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus puts the whole scheme after the war instead and tells it as a secret: Themistocles would not state his plan in the assembly, so the people chose Aristides and Xanthippus to hear it privately and report whether it was sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On their word a second embassy went to Sparta, arguing that a first-rate harbour served the common Greek interest against a Persian return, and the work was finished before anyone expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The wall was carried up to a thickness two wagons could meet on, of great squared stones with neither rubble nor mortar between them, clamped on the outside with iron and lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About half the intended height was reached, and the point of the bulk was that a small garrison of men unfit for service could hold it while everyone else went to the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus adds the policies that went with the harbour, 20 triremes to be built every year and the tax on metics and artisans removed, to draw in the crafts a navy needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides says Themistocles rated the Piraeus above the upper city and always advised going down into it and defying the world with the fleet; two generations later a Peloponnesian squadron found it unguarded and open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.93, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=93. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.1.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.41, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=41. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.42-11.43, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=42. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.93, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=93. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","history","warfare","greece","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Plan_Piraeus.svg/1920px-Plan_Piraeus.svg.png","title":"A plan of the ancient town","desc":"The peninsula with its three harbours, the wall circuit marked as the heavy line running round the coast and across the neck, and the gridded street plan between them.","credit":"Plan by Tomisti, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plan_Piraeus.svg","alt":"A line plan of a peninsula with three pale blue inlets, a heavy black line following the whole shore and cutting across the landward neck, and a grid of streets filling the middle."}},
{"id":"gr-446","num":446,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Gelon's horsemen were let into the Carthaginian naval camp as allies, killed Hamilcar at his sacrifice and fired the ships, which settled the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 480 BCE.","questions":["Herodotus was told in Sicily that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell on the very day of Salamis, while Diodorus puts it instead on the day of Thermopylae.","Carthage paid 2,000 talents and built two temples to hold copies of the treaty after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its captives quarried the stone for the temples at Acragas."],"answer":"Battle of Himera","answerText":"Battle of Himera","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, in northern Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enemy dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">150,000, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2,000 talents of silver</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Himera</b> was fought on the north coast of Sicily in 480 BCE between the forces of Gelon of Syracuse and Theron of Acragas and a Carthaginian army under Hamilcar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus puts that army at 300,000 Phoenicians, Libyans, Iberians, Ligurians, Elisyci, Sardinians and Corsicans; Diodorus gives the same figure with over 200 warships and more than 3,000 transports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus says a storm on the crossing sank the ships carrying the horses and chariots, and that Hamilcar, putting in at Panormus, remarked that the war was already finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gelon came up with 50,000 foot and over 5,000 horse, dug in outside the city behind a ditch and palisade, and sent his cavalry against the enemy parties out foraging, bringing in more than 10,000 prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What decided it was a ruse: at sunrise horsemen rode up to the Carthaginian naval camp, were let through as allies, killed Hamilcar at his sacrifice and set fire to the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The two armies were already engaged when the flames went up and word ran along the lines that the general was dead, and the Carthaginian side broke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gelon had ordered that no prisoners be taken; Diodorus puts the dead at 150,000, with 20 ships escaping only to founder in a storm and a few men reaching Carthage to report that everyone who had crossed to Sicily was gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> How Hamilcar died is told three ways: Herodotus reports that he vanished and could not be found, that the Carthaginians said he threw himself on his own sacrificial pyre, and Diodorus that the disguised horsemen cut him down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The sources disagree about the day as well, the Sicilians telling Herodotus that it fell on the day of Salamis while Diodorus places it on the day of Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The peace cost Carthage 2,000 talents of silver and two temples to hold copies of the treaty, and the captives were set to quarrying stone, so many of them going to Acragas that private households held 500 apiece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.165, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=165. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.166-7.167, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=166. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.20, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=20. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.21, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=21. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.22, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=22. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.24, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=24. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.25-11.26, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=25. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Himera_Viktoriatempel.jpg/1280px-Himera_Viktoriatempel.jpg","title":"The great temple at Himera","desc":"The Doric temple whose foundations still stand on the plain below the town, known by tradition as the Temple of Victory. Diodorus says Gelon had spoil from the battle nailed up on the most notable temples in the city.","credit":"Photograph by Clemensfranz, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Himera_Viktoriatempel.jpg","alt":"Seen from a hillside above, the rectangular stone foundations and stepped platform of a Greek temple standing in a field between a country road and a railway line."}},
{"id":"gr-447","num":447,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Envoys from Sparta and Athens came out to Sicily for help against Xerxes, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told them that in refusing him the command their year had lost its spring.","questions":["When the Megarians in Sicily surrendered, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made citizens of the rich men who had fought him and sold into slavery abroad the commons who had not.","Cadmus of Cos was sent to Delphi with money and orders to hand it over with earth and water if Persia won, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did instead of joining the Greeks."],"answer":"Gelon","answerText":"Gelon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Won at Himera</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, over Carthage</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 years, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 stades from Syracuse</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Gelon</b> was the ruler of Gela and then of Syracuse who made his city the strongest power in Sicily and broke a Carthaginian army at Himera in 480 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His family had come from Telos at the founding of Gela and held the priesthood of the underworld goddesses; he began in the guard of the tyrant Hippocrates and rose to command his cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Having taken Syracuse he handed Gela to his brother Hiero and set about enlarging his new city by moving people into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus says he razed Camarina and brought its people in, moved half the men of Gela, and when the Megarians surrendered made citizens of the rich who had fought him while selling the commons into slavery out of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Envoys from Sparta and Athens came asking help against Xerxes, and he answered that when he had asked them against the Carthaginians, and to avenge Dorieus, not one of them had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He offered a great army and fleet on condition of commanding the Greeks, and when the Spartan refused that and the Athenian refused him even the ships, he told them to go home and say their year had lost its spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What he did instead is the awkward part of the record: he sent Cadmus of Cos to Delphi with three ships and money, to watch the outcome and hand it over with earth and water if the Persians won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cadmus brought all of it back when the Greeks had won, which Herodotus records as an act of justice on Cadmus' part, not on Gelon's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus reads him quite differently, as a beneficent ruler who kept the city's law limiting funeral expense in his own case and handed the kingship to Hiero as he lay dying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He reigned seven years and was buried on his wife's estate 200 stades from the city, the whole population walking out behind him; the tomb was later destroyed by the Carthaginians and its towers thrown down by Agathocles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.153-7.154, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=153. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.156, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=156. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.157-7.158, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=157. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.159-7.162, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=159. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.163-7.164, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=163. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.38, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=38. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","politics","greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Coin_of_Gelon_I%2C_struck_at_the_Syracuse_mint%2C_dated_480-478_BC.jpg","title":"A Syracusan tetradrachm of his reign","desc":"Silver, struck about 480 BCE: a charioteer driving a slow four-horse team with Nike flying above, and on the other face a nymph's head ringed by dolphins. The coin names the city, not the ruler.","credit":"Photograph by Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Gelon_I%2C_struck_at_the_Syracuse_mint%2C_dated_480-478_BC.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin side by side: on the left a four-horse chariot with a driver and a small winged figure above, on the right a woman's head in profile with four dolphins around it."}},
{"id":"gr-448","num":448,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus makes it a quarrel between Sicilian tyrants where Diodorus makes it half a bargain struck with Xerxes, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 480 BCE has two quite different explanations.","questions":["Three years of hiring in Italy, Liguria, Galatia and Iberia went into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Diodorus puts at over 300,000 foot soldiers and 200 warships.","Once the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had failed, the city kept watch every night expecting Gelon to sail against it, and paid 2,000 talents of silver for peace."],"answer":"Carthaginian invasion of Sicily","answerText":"Carthaginian invasion of Sicily","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Launched</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, from Carthage</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Prepared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 years, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foot soldiers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300,000, per Diodorus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Carthaginian invasion of Sicily</b> was the expedition of 480 BCE that put in at Panormus under Hamilcar and was destroyed at Himera by Gelon of Syracuse and Theron of Acragas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Why it came is told two ways, and the two are not easily reconciled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus gives a purely Sicilian cause: Terillus, thrown out of Himera by Theron, brought Hamilcar in, helped by Anaxilas of Rhegium, who had married Terillus' daughter and handed his own children over as hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus instead makes it half of a concerted plan, Xerxes sending to Carthage and agreeing that he would attack the Greeks of Greece while Carthage subdued those of Sicily and Italy at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that account Carthage spent three years preparing, hiring mercenaries from Italy, Liguria, Galatia and Iberia and enrolling its own people from across Libya; Herodotus, who reports both the Sicilian and the Carthaginian accounts of the campaign, mentions no such agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Fighting Greeks in Sicily was not new to Carthage, since a generation earlier the Spartan Dorieus and the companions who sailed with him had been killed there by Phoenicians and Egestans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gelon threw that at the Greek envoys who came asking his help, saying he had once asked them against the Carthaginians and to avenge Dorieus, and that not one of them had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus puts the armament at more than 300,000 foot and 200 warships with over 3,000 transports, and says a storm on the crossing took the vessels carrying the horses and chariots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the defeat the city kept watch every night in the belief that Gelon would sail against it, went into public mourning, and filled its houses with lamentation for the missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The peace cost 2,000 talents of silver and two temples to hold copies of the treaty, and no Carthaginian army came back to Sicily for seventy years, until Egesta asked for one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.1, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=1. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.20, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=20. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.165-7.167, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=165. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.46, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=46. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.158, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=158. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.24, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=24. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.26, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=26. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 13.43, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=13:chapter=43. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/SicilyBC.svg/1280px-SicilyBC.svg.png","title":"Sicily before the invasion","desc":"Greek foundations in red, Phoenician and Punic ones in purple, Sicel and Elymian towns in white. Hamilcar put in at Panormus in the Punic north-west and marched east along the coast to Himera. The place names are in the mapmaker's German forms.","credit":"Map by Uwe Dedering, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SicilyBC.svg","alt":"A relief map of Sicily with coloured dots and labels round the coast, red for Greek towns in the south and east, purple for Punic ones in the west, white for inland settlements."}},
{"id":"gr-449","num":449,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Raised where the enemy had turned and run, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a claim as much as a memorial, and after Sybota both sides put one up and each counted itself the winner.","questions":["Diodorus says the Greeks laid down that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> be made of whatever wood came to hand rather than of stone, so that a memorial of enmity would soon disappear.","What was meant to last was not the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the field but the tenth of the spoil sent to Delphi, Olympia and the Isthmus in bronze and gold."],"answer":"tropaion","answerText":"tropaion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plataea tithe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, to three sanctuaries</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Olympia figure</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Zeus of 10 cubits</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Isthmus figure</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Poseidon of 7 cubits</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"A <b>tropaion</b> was the marker a Greek army raised on the ground where the enemy had turned and run, and the word behind the English trophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides records the practice constantly, as when the Athenians at Naupactus set one up on the spot they had put out from and turned the day, giving the enemy dead back under truce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a claim as much as a memorial, and could be contested: after Sybota the Corinthians raised one on the mainland and the Corcyraeans another on the island, each side counting itself the victor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Naupactus the beaten side raised one too, for the ships it had disabled inshore, and moored a captured vessel beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus reports a rule behind the custom: the Greeks laid down that such markers be made of whatever wood came to hand and not of stone, so that memorials of enmity should last a short time and disappear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What was meant to last went elsewhere, to the panhellenic sanctuaries, and was paid for out of a tenth of the spoil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the tithe of Plataea the Greeks made a gold tripod on a bronze serpent for the god at Delphi, a bronze Zeus ten cubits high for Olympia, and a Poseidon of seven cubits for the Isthmus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens had done the same after Marathon, and Pausanias saw the group at Delphi that its tithe had paid for, with Athena, Apollo and the general Miltiades among the tribal heroes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He adds that the Athenians later put statues of Antigonus, Demetrius and Ptolemy in the same place, the Egyptian out of goodwill and the Macedonians out of the dread they inspired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two habits pull against each other: a wooden marker on the field that was left to rot, and bronze and gold in a sanctuary meant to be read for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=54. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.92, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=92. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 13.24, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=13:chapter=24. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.81, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=81. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.10, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=10. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","history","warfare","greece","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Trophee_bataille_marathon.JPG/1280px-Trophee_bataille_marathon.JPG","title":"The trophy on the plain of Marathon","desc":"A restored Ionic column standing where the Athenians raised a trophy after the battle of 490 BCE. Surviving ancient pieces of the monument are held in museum collections. A trophy in permanent marble is the exception rather than the rule.","credit":"Photograph by karta24, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trophee_bataille_marathon.JPG","alt":"A tall slender white marble column with a scrolled Ionic capital, standing alone on a stepped base against a clear blue sky, with a line of dark cypresses behind it."}},
{"id":"gr-450","num":450,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Thucydides noted that Homer never calls anyone a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and thought it was because the Greeks had not yet been marked off from the rest of the world under one name.","questions":["In Herodotus the Athenians refuse Persian terms by naming their kinship in blood and speech and their shared shrines, everything the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was taken not to have.","The force the word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gained is caught by Aeschylus, whose Persian queen asks who is master of the Athenians and is told that they are the slaves of no man."],"answer":"barbarian","answerText":"barbarian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Delian League</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, treasury on Delos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persians staged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">472 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian claim</span><span class=\"dt-v\">432 BCE, envoys at Sparta</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"abstract":"<b>Barbarian</b> renders the Greek <i>barbaros</i>, and in the earliest verse it is a word about speech rather than about character. Homer uses the related adjective once, in the catalogue of ships, of the Carians about Miletus and the Maeander, whom one translator calls uncouth of speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides noticed the absence and drew a conclusion: Homer nowhere calls the Greeks by one name and does not use the word at all, probably because they had not yet been marked off from the rest of the world under one appellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After 480 BCE it carried a great deal more, and Aeschylus shows the freight eight years later, when a Persian queen asks who is set over the Athenians as shepherd and master and the chorus answers that they are called the slaves or subjects of no man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The antithesis that answer draws, free Greeks against a subject other, was the war's most durable export, and it was drawn by the side that won. <br><br> Herodotus puts a statement of Greekness in the mouths of Athenians refusing Persian terms: kinship of all Greeks in blood and speech, shrines and sacrifices held in common, and a like way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is spoken as a promise not to change sides rather than offered as a definition, which is how most of the war's thinking about Greekness survives. The political legacy went to Athens: the allies' contributions were fixed against the King, the professed object was to ravage his country, and the common treasury was placed on Delos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 432 BCE Athenian envoys at Sparta were saying they were tired of bringing the subject forward, and then bringing it forward: at Marathon they had faced the enemy alone, and at Salamis had gone aboard with all their people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herodotus knew the claim was contested and made it anyway, prefacing his judgement that the Athenians had saved Greece with a warning that it would displease most of his readers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 2.867, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0134:book=2:card=867. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.3, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=3. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 241-242, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=242. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.144, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=144. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.73, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=73. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.139, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=139. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","greece","warfare","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Greek-Persian_duel_1.jpg/1280px-Greek-Persian_duel_1.jpg","title":"A Greek and a Persian on a cup","desc":"Attic red-figure kylix of the 5th century BCE. The Greek fights naked but for helmet and shield, at close quarters; the Persian wears a patterned sleeved suit and a soft cap and draws a bow. The pairing is the antithesis Aeschylus puts into words.","credit":"Reproduction of an Attic kylix, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek-Persian_duel_1.jpg","alt":"The round interior of a Greek cup with a meander border, painted in orange on black: a helmeted nude warrior with a shield bearing a winged horse strides over a bearded archer in patterned trousers and a floppy cap who is drawing a bow."}},
{"id":"gr-451","num":451,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The alliance now called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was formed when the Ionians asked the Athenians to lead in place of a Spartan commander they had found violent after Mycale.","questions":["Thucydides says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as independent allies acting on a common congress, and dates its change to the siege of Naxos, the first member forced back in.","Mytilenean envoys at Olympia said they had joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to free the Greeks from the Mede, not to help Athens subjugate them."],"answer":"Delian League","answerText":"Delian League","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, after the Persian retreat</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First assessment</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, archonship of Timosthenes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First tribute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">460 talents</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Delian League</b> is the modern name for the alliance the ancient writers describe without one, as the Athenians and their allies. It grew out of the war it was meant to continue: after Mycale the Greeks took the Samians, Chians, Lesbians and other islanders into alliance and bound them by oath not to desert it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Command then changed hands, because the Ionians and the newly freed found Pausanias violent and asked the Athenians, as kinsmen, to lead instead; Sparta recalled him and the successor it sent found the allies no longer willing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristides administered the oath, by which the members swore to have the same friends and enemies, and lumps of iron were let sink in the sea to ratify it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Athens then fixed which cities were to give money and which ships, the professed object being to ravage the King's country, and the first assessment came to 460 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Thucydides is careful about what it was at the start: independent allies acting on the resolutions of a common congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It did not stay that way, and he dates the change precisely, to the siege of Naxos, the first ally forced back into an alliance it had tried to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His explanation puts much of the blame on the members, most of whom preferred to pay their share in money rather than serve, so that Athens grew her navy on their contributions while a revolt found them with neither ships nor practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The allies told it differently, and the Mytileneans arguing at Olympia for a Spartan alliance said they had become allies of the Greeks for liberation from the Mede and not allies of Athens for the subjugation of the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By their account only Lesbos and Chios still sent their own contingents, independent and free in name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.95, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=95. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.97, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=97. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.98, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.99, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=99. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.10, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=10. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 23, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.106, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=106. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/DelianLeagueAthenianEmpire.jpg/1280px-DelianLeagueAthenianEmpire.jpg","title":"The two alliances","desc":"Members of the alliance in pink and the Peloponnesian League in green, as they stood on the eve of the Peloponnesian War, two generations after the founding. Delos, Naxos, Lesbos, Chios and Samos are all on it. From a school atlas of 1912.","credit":"Map from W. L. Westermann, The Story of the Ancient Nations, 1912, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DelianLeagueAthenianEmpire.jpg","alt":"A tinted historical map of the Aegean with the coasts and islands of the eastern rim washed pink, the Peloponnese green, and a key at the lower left naming the Delian League, the Peloponnesian League and neutral states."}},
{"id":"gr-452","num":452,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides says the office of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first instituted by the Athenians, and that the alliance kept its common chest at Delos with the congresses held in the temple there.","questions":["The board called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took in the allies' contributions, which were fixed against the King and were known as tribute, and it outlived the island the money was first kept on.","Athens created the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to receive the money of the alliance, and Plutarch says moving that money to Athens was called unjust but advantageous."],"answer":"Hellenotamiai","answerText":"Hellenotamiai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treasury at Delos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 478 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Island purified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">426 BCE, all graves removed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still an office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">411 BCE, in the oligarchs' list</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Hellenotamiai</b>, the treasurers of the Greeks, were the officials who received the money of the alliance formed after the Persian invasions. Thucydides sets three facts side by side without comment: the allies' contributions were fixed against the King, the money was called tribute, and the office was first instituted by the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is equally plain about where the fund sat, saying that the common treasury was at Delos and that the congresses were held in the temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island was the obvious choice, being the old gathering-ground of the Ionians and the neighbouring islanders, who came to the festival with their wives and children for games, dancing and song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its standing was such that a Persian admiral respected it: Datis anchored across the water at Rhenea, sent word that he would harm neither the birthplace of the two gods nor its people, and burnt three hundred talents of frankincense on the altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Athenian care for the sanctuary was of a different kind: Peisistratus had purified as much of the island as could be seen from the temple, and in 426 BCE the Athenians purified the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every grave was taken up, and it was forbidden thereafter to die or to give birth on Delos, both being done across the water on Rhenea; the Athenians then held the Delian games for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides took an argument as well as a purification from those graves, since above half held Carians, identified by the arms buried with them and by the manner of interment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The money did not stay, and Plutarch, citing Theophrastus, says that when moving it from Delos to Athens was debated, and the Samians themselves proposed it, Aristides said the thing was unjust but advantageous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The board outlived the island: the oligarchic constitution drawn up at Athens in 411 BCE still lists the treasurers of the Greeks among its magistrates, and bars those managing the funds from the council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.104, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=104. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.8, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=8. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.97, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=97. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 30, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=30. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","title"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Delos%2C_overview_of_a_site.jpg/1280px-Delos%2C_overview_of_a_site.jpg","title":"The sanctuary on Delos","desc":"Foundations and scattered blocks on the terrace of the sanctuary of Apollo, where Thucydides places the alliance's common chest and the congresses of its members. Nothing above ground survives of the buildings the money was kept in.","credit":"Photograph by ftrc, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delos,_overview_of_a_site.jpg","alt":"A wide field of pale stone foundations, fallen blocks and low walls under a bright blue sky, with a dry hillside behind and a modern museum building in the distance."}},
{"id":"gr-453","num":453,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The money an ally paid into the common fund was called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Thucydides says most members preferred to pay their share rather than serve with ships and leave home.","questions":["Pericles answered the charge that Athens gilded herself with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by saying the allies gave neither horse nor ship nor hoplite, but money, which belonged to those who took it.","Athens drew 600 talents of silver a year in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War, when only the Chians, Lesbians and Corcyraeans still served with ships."],"answer":"phoros","answerText":"phoros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First fixed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, 460 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yearly average</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 talents in 431 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reserve</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 talents on the Acropolis</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Phoros</b> was the money paid into the common fund of the alliance led by Athens, and Thucydides says the word was simply the name given to the contributions when they were first fixed at 460 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A member could serve with ships instead of paying, but most preferred to commute the obligation, arranging to pay their share of the expense in money rather than leave home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides thinks that was the allies' own undoing, since Athens increased her navy on the funds they contributed while a revolt found them without resources or experience for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War only the Chians, the Lesbians and the Corcyraeans still furnished ships, and the rest sent infantry and money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The paying districts were Caria upon the sea with her Dorian neighbours, Ionia, the Hellespont, the Thracian towns and all the Cyclades except Melos and Thera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pericles told the assembly that year that an average revenue of 600 talents of silver came in yearly from the allies, and that 6,000 talents of coined silver still lay on the Acropolis out of 9,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Collecting it was no formality: in 428 BCE, needing money for the siege of Mytilene, they raised 200 talents from their own citizens for the first time and sent twelve ships to levy subsidies from the allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their commander Lysicles went up the country from Myus in Caria, across the plain of the Maeander, and was attacked and killed with many of his soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the money meant was argued in Athens itself, where Pericles' enemies said Hellas was manifestly subjected to tyranny when the city gilded and bedizened itself with her enforced contributions for the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His answer was that the allies furnished not a horse, not a ship, not a hoplite, but money simply, and that this belonged not to those who gave it but to those who took it, so long as they furnished what they took it for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.99, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=99. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.9, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=9. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.19, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=19. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","greece","politics","institution"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-1.JPG/1280px-EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-1.JPG","title":"The first stele of the tribute lists","desc":"Fragments of the Athenian record of the allies' payments, set into plaster in the Epigraphical Museum. The stele stood 3.56 metres (11 feet 8 inches) high, survives in 149 pieces, and carried a year's entries at a time from 454 to 440 BCE, each city's name followed by its sum in acrophonic numerals.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-1.JPG","alt":"Broken slabs of inscribed white marble mounted in grey plaster, carrying close-set columns of Greek capitals with red inventory numbers painted on the plaster below."}},
{"id":"gr-454","num":454,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Made two years after Salamis, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told each ally which should give money and which ships, and the total first came to 460 talents.","questions":["Plutarch says the allies asked for the man who made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, that he inspected their territories and revenues, and that he went out poor and came back poorer.","The allies praised the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a golden age, which Plutarch says was easier once the tariff had been doubled and then trebled within a short time."],"answer":"assessment of Aristides","answerText":"assessment of Aristides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assessed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, archonship of Timosthenes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First total</span><span class=\"dt-v\">460 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">By 431 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 talents a year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Later total</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,300 talents</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>assessment of Aristides</b> was the first fixing of what each member of the alliance led by Athens should pay, and the Athenian Constitution dates it two years after Salamis, in the archonship of Timosthenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides gives the result in a clause: the cities were told which should give money and which ships, and the tribute was first fixed at 460 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch gives the method, saying the allies asked the Athenians for Aristides and commissioned him to inspect their territories and revenues and then to fix the sums according to each member's worth and ability to pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His reputation is what the whole arrangement rested on, and Herodotus, who had no reason to flatter Athens, calls him the best and most just man in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch adds the detail that fixed the reputation: master for a time of the property of Hellas, he went out poor on the commission and came back poorer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The allies remembered the figure as a golden age, praising it the way men of old praised the age of Cronus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch is careful to say why they did, since the tariff was doubled and then trebled within a short time, and a thing is easier to praise once it has been replaced by something worse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pericles added nearly a third, and by the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War the yearly revenue from the allies averaged 600 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After his death, Plutarch says, the sum was enlarged little by little until it reached 1,300 talents, which he blames less on the length of the war than on those who raised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same man is remembered for the opposite too, since Theophrastus reports that when moving the money from Delos to Athens against the terms was debated, Aristides said the thing was unjust but advantageous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 23, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 24, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.79, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=79. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","institution"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/BL_Papyrus_131-10v_Constitution_of_Athens.jpg/1280px-BL_Papyrus_131-10v_Constitution_of_Athens.jpg","title":"The papyrus that dates the assessment","desc":"A leaf of British Library Papyrus 131, which carries the Athenian Constitution, the work that puts the assessment two years after Salamis in the archonship of Timosthenes. The main hand runs across the sheet with a second, smaller hand down the left margin.","credit":"British Library Papyrus 131, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BL_Papyrus_131-10v_Constitution_of_Athens.jpg","alt":"A pale brown papyrus sheet with visible vertical fibres, carrying eight lines of large Greek letters written without word spacing, and a column of smaller writing down the left edge."}},
{"id":"gr-455","num":455,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Left with his father's unpaid fine and a name for drink, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the fences off his farms, kept a daily table for the poor and named one of his twin sons Lacedaemonius.","questions":["Sparta called in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for Athenian skill at sieges, then sent his men home alone out of suspicion, which ended the policy that Greece needed two leaders.","Pericles could not match the private wealth of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and made jury service a paid office instead, a counter-measure the Athenian Constitution reports plainly."],"answer":"Cimon","answerText":"Cimon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Father fined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 talents, died in prison</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent from Ithome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 462 BCE, alliance broken</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last command</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 ships to Cyprus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 450 BCE, at Citium</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Cimon</b> led Athens while the alliance against Persia was becoming an empire, and was the son of Miltiades by Hegesipyle, a daughter of the Thracian king Olorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He began with nothing but the name: his father, condemned to a fine of 50 talents, died in prison, and the son was left with an unmarried sister, a reputation for drink and, one contemporary says, no literary education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His politics are best read from what he did with his money, since he had an estate large enough for a tyrant and spent it in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He took the fences off his farms so anyone might take the fruit and kept a daily table for the poor, though the sources differ on how wide the invitation was, Plutarch making it general and Aristotle restricting it to his own deme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Young men in fine clothes went with him, ready to change coats with any shabby elder they met and to press small change into the hands of poor men of quality in the market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Abroad his line was that Greece had two leaders and needed both, and he named one of his twin sons Lacedaemonius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That policy died at Ithome, where Sparta called Athens in for her skill at sieges, then took fright at the enterprising and revolutionary character of her allies and sent them home alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They knew the reason was suspicion rather than the one given, went away deeply offended, and on reaching home broke the alliance made against the Mede and swore to Argos instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Athens the answer to his generosity was public money: Pericles, whose own means could not match it, made jury service a paid office as a counter-measure to his rival's wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His last command was 200 ships to Cyprus, and he died besieging Citium, telling those about him to sail away at once and conceal the death, so that neither enemy nor allies knew whom they followed home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 4, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 27, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=27. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.102, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=102. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.112, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=112. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Kimon_statue.JPG/1280px-Kimon_statue.JPG","title":"The monument at Larnaca","desc":"A modern bust on the seafront at Larnaca, the Kition he was besieging when he died, inscribed by the city to Cimon the Athenian. No ancient portrait of him has been identified, so what stands for him is a memorial put up by the town he was attacking.","credit":"Photograph by Valantis Antoniades, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kimon_statue.JPG","alt":"A helmeted bust silhouetted on a tall white plinth against an orange sunset, with palm trees, flagpoles and a beach behind it and a ship on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gr-456","num":456,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one line, saying the Athenians took the town on the Strymon from the Medes and enslaved its people under the command of Cimon.","questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Athens voted three stone herms, and Aeschines says the generals were forbidden to inscribe their names so the honour would read as the people's.","The Persian who held the town during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused terms, burnt his household and himself, and threw the city's silver into the river."],"answer":"siege of Eion","answerText":"siege of Eion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Besieged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 476 BCE, Cimon commanding</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Honour voted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 stone herms, naming no general</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>siege of Eion</b> was the first operation Thucydides records for the new alliance, and he gives it in one line: the Athenians besieged and took the town on the Strymon from the Medes and enslaved its people, under the command of Cimon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch supplies the method, which was to beat the garrison in the field, shut it in the town, and then drive out the Thracians upstream who had been supplying it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Persian commander refused terms he was offered, and the two accounts of what he then did agree on everything but his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus calls him Boges and says he heaped a pyre, killed his children, wife, concubines and servants and threw them on it, scattered the city's gold and silver from the walls into the river, and cast himself into the fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch calls him Butes and has him fire the town and destroy his family, his treasures and himself with it, which is why Cimon took the place and got little from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus adds the detail that makes the story worth telling, since Boges was the only man driven out by the Greeks whom Xerxes thought valiant, and the Persians praised him still in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What Athens gained was land rather than plunder, and Plutarch says the fertile country round the town was handed over for settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The honour voted was three stone herms in the Stoa of the Hermae, and Aeschines says the condition was that the generals might not inscribe their own names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The point of that condition, he tells a fourth-century jury, was that the honour should read as the people's rather than the commanders'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch says the omission fooled nobody and that his contemporaries counted the herms a surpassing honour to Cimon, greater than anything Themistocles or his own father had been given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.98, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.107, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=107. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Aeschines, <i>Against Ctesiphon</i> 183, trans. Charles Darwin Adams, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0002:speech=3:section=183. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","warfare","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Three_marble_heads_of_Herme%2C_AGMA%2C_225288.jpg/1280px-Three_marble_heads_of_Herme%2C_AGMA%2C_225288.jpg","title":"Three herm heads from the Agora","desc":"Marble herm heads in the Ancient Agora Museum at Athens, the museum dating them to 510 to 500 BCE, about 425 BCE, and the 2nd century CE. The three herms voted for this campaign do not survive; these show the kind of monument they were.","credit":"Photograph by Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_marble_heads_of_Herme%2C_AGMA%2C_225288.jpg","alt":"Three bearded marble heads on stone blocks in a museum case, one weathered almost featureless, one crisply carved with ringlets and a long beard, with a bilingual label below."}},
{"id":"gr-457","num":457,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Pirates on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> invited Cimon to take their own town after an Amphictyonic judgement went against it, and he drove out the Dolopians and settled the island.","questions":["An oracle sent Athens for the bones of Theseus, and on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Cimon dug where an eagle was tearing a mound and found a coffin holding a very large man.","Thucydides says Athens enslaved the people of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and colonised it, where Plutarch says only that the Dolopians were driven out."],"answer":"Scyros","answerText":"Scyros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 475 BCE, Cimon commanding</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bones brought home</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after about 400 years away</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Scyros</b> is an island in the Aegean that Athens took and settled early in the life of its alliance, and the sources do not agree about who was living on it or what became of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides says the population was Dolopian, that the Athenians enslaved it and colonised the island; Diodorus says Pelasgians and Dolopes, and has Cimon appoint a founder and portion out the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch gives the pretext, which was piracy: the Dolopians farmed badly and robbed at sea, and had lately taken some Thessalian merchants who put in at Ctesium and thrown them into prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The merchants escaped and won their case before the Amphictyonic assembly, whereupon the town refused to pay and told the men who held the plunder to give it back themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those men wrote to Cimon in terror, inviting him to bring his fleet and take the place, which he did, driving out the Dolopians and, in Plutarch's phrase, making the Aegean a free sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What made the conquest popular at home was a body, since an oracle had told the Athenians to bring back the bones of Theseus, who had fled there and been killed by its king Lycomedes, though nobody knew the grave and the islanders would allow no search.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch says Cimon saw an eagle tearing at a mound, dug there, and found a coffin holding a man of extraordinary size with a bronze spear and a sword beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The relics came home in Cimon's own trireme, after an absence of about 400 years, and were met with processions and sacrifices as though the hero himself were returning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tomb was built in the middle of the city and became a refuge for runaway slaves and poor men afraid of the powerful, which is what Theseus had been in life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias reads the whole episode backwards from that, describing the campaign as Cimon avenging the death of Theseus and fetching his bones to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.98, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Theseus</i> 36, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Theseus*.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.17.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=17:section=6. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.60, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=60. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","greece","warfare","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Skyros-chora.JPG/1280px-Skyros-chora.JPG","title":"The town under its rock","desc":"The main village of the island, its white houses running down from the fortified crag that has been the citadel here since antiquity. The Dolopians Cimon expelled held the island from a settlement on this side of it.","credit":"Photograph by Han borg, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyros-chora.JPG","alt":"A steep brown crag topped by fortification walls, with several hundred flat-roofed white houses spilling down its lower slopes, dry hills behind and scrub in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gr-458","num":458,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides reports the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one sentence, saying the Athenians beat the Medes by land and by sea on the same day and destroyed a Phoenician fleet of 200 ships.","questions":["Plutarch lays out his sources' disagreements about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than settling them, one giving the enemy 600 ships and another 350, under two different commanders.","In Diodorus' account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Cimon manned captured ships with his own men in Persian dress, was welcomed into the camp as a friend, and attacked it at night."],"answer":"Battle of the Eurymedon","answerText":"Battle of the Eurymedon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 466 BCE, land and sea in a day</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200, per Thucydides</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">340, per Diodorus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Eurymedon</b> was a double victory won in Pamphylia, and Thucydides reports it in one sentence: the Athenians beat the Medes by land and sea on the same day and destroyed a Phoenician fleet of 200 ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Cimon sailed with 200 triremes Themistocles had built for speed, widened and decked over to carry more hoplites into a boarding fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the way he brought the Greek city of Phaselis over, the Chians in his fleet shooting messages to their old friends inside on arrows, and it agreed to pay 10 talents and join him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beyond that the accounts stop agreeing, and Plutarch lays the disagreement out rather than settling it, one authority giving the fleet to Tithraustes and another to Ariomandes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That fleet was 600 ships to one of his sources and 350 to another, and he takes refuge in the 200 captured, however many there had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Persians put into the river to avoid a fight, came out when pressed, achieved nothing and ran their ships ashore; Cimon then landed his hoplites straight from the sea fight against a fresh and larger army and broke it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch's verdict is that he beat Salamis with an infantry battle and Plataea with a naval one, and that he then destroyed the 80 Phoenician ships that arrived too late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus tells the land action differently, with Cimon manning the captured ships with his own men in Persian dress and tiaras and being welcomed into the camp as a friend before falling on it at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In that version most of them never knew who was attacking and took it for a raid by the neighbouring Pisidians, and the general Pherendates was killed in his tent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus gives the largest figures, 340 ships and over 20,000 men taken, and quotes the epigram on the tithe dedicated at Athens, claiming no such deed by land and sea had been done since the sea divided Europe from Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.100, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=100. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.61, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=61. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.62, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=62. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","greece","warfare","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/K%C3%B6pr%C3%BC%C3%A7ay_at_Eurymedon_Bridge_2013_5045.jpg/1280px-K%C3%B6pr%C3%BC%C3%A7ay_at_Eurymedon_Bridge_2013_5045.jpg","title":"The river the battle is named for","desc":"The Koprucay, the ancient Eurymedon, in its gorge in the Taurus foothills of southern Turkey. The fighting was far downstream from here, where the river reaches the Pamphylian plain and the sea.","credit":"Photograph by Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6pr%C3%BC%C3%A7ay_at_Eurymedon_Bridge_2013_5045.jpg","alt":"A blue-green river running between rocky wooded banks in a narrow valley, with a low stone parapet on a ledge at the left and forested ridges rising behind."}},
{"id":"gr-459","num":459,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides marks the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one clause as the first time an allied city was brought back into an agreement it had tried to leave, and says the rest followed.","questions":["Plutarch explains what led to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by Cimon's leniency, since he took money from allies who would not sail and ships from those who sent them without crews.","An exiled Themistocles was blown onto the Athenian squadron blockading the island during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and had to tell the ship's master who he was to get past."],"answer":"revolt of Naxos","answerText":"revolt of Naxos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 469 BCE, the first ally reduced</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>revolt of Naxos</b> is the moment Thucydides picks out as the one that changed what the alliance was, and he marks it in one clause: the island left, a war followed, and it came back after a siege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made it worth marking was the precedent rather than the fighting, since this was the first time a member city was brought back into an agreement it had tried to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that the rest followed in the order that circumstances prescribed, which is the whole later history of the alliance stated in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island was worth holding: Aristagoras had described it to a Persian satrap a generation earlier as small but rich in money and slaves, with Paros, Andros and the other Cyclades depending on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides gives the general cause of defections plainly: arrears of tribute and ships and failure of service, with Athenians severe and exacting towards men unused to hard work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He also blames the members themselves, since most had arranged to pay their share in money rather than serve, so that Athens grew her fleet on their contributions while a revolt found them with neither ships nor practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch explains the same outcome by the opposite behaviour, saying Cimon used no compulsion at all and simply took the money from those who would not sail and the ships from those who sent them without crews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In that account the allies were caught with the bait of their own ease, became farmers and merchants instead of fighters, and were tributary subjects before they knew it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both explanations end in the same place, which is a fleet paid for by men who could no longer man one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The siege has an incidental witness, since Thucydides has the exiled Themistocles carried by a storm onto the Athenian squadron blockading the island, and forced to tell the ship's master who he was to get past it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.98, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.99, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=99. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.137, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=137. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.31, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=31. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Gate_of_Temple_of_Apollo%2C_Palatia_and_akropolis_of_Naxos_Town%2C_530_BC%2C_144162.jpg/1280px-Gate_of_Temple_of_Apollo%2C_Palatia_and_akropolis_of_Naxos_Town%2C_530_BC%2C_144162.jpg","title":"The island town through its temple gate","desc":"Naxos town seen between the jambs of the marble gateway on the islet of Palatia, begun for a temple of Apollo about 530 BCE and never finished. It was already an old ruin, and the island already rich, when the revolt was put down.","credit":"Photograph by Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gate_of_Temple_of_Apollo%2C_Palatia_and_akropolis_of_Naxos_Town%2C_530_BC%2C_144162.jpg","alt":"Two massive white marble uprights and lintel blocks framing a view across water to a hillside town of flat-roofed white houses under a walled citadel."}},
{"id":"gr-460","num":460,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a quarrel over trading places on the Thracian coast and a mine, and it ended with walls razed, ships handed over and the mainland given up.","questions":["Herodotus, who had seen the island's mines, puts its yearly revenue at 200 talents, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fought to keep from Athens.","During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the besieged appealed to Sparta to invade Attica, and an earthquake and a helot rising stopped the promised invasion from ever coming."],"answer":"revolt of Thasos","answerText":"revolt of Thasos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 465 BCE, over marts and a mine</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlers lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 at Drabescus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Surrendered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 3 years, walls razed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>revolt of Thasos</b> was a member leaving the alliance over its own property rather than over its dues, the quarrel being about the trading places on the Thracian coast opposite and about a mine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus had seen those mines himself, and says the workings the Phoenicians opened between Aenyra and Coenyra had eaten away a whole hill in the searching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He puts the island's revenue at about 80 talents a year from the gold workings on the mainland and 200 on average from mainland and mines together, 300 at the best, with no tax paid on crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That wealth had already cost it once, since Darius made the Thasians pull down their walls and bring their ships to Abdera on a report from their neighbours that they meant to rebel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens beat them at sea, Plutarch putting the captured ships at 33, and landed on the island to besiege the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In the same season Athens sent 10,000 settlers of her own citizens and of the allies to the place on the Strymon called Nine Ways, and they were cut off in the interior at Drabescus by the assembled Thracians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Thasians appealed to Sparta to invade Attica, and Sparta promised to do it without telling Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The invasion never came, because an earthquake and the secession of the helots to Ithome gave Sparta a war of her own to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the third year of the siege the island took terms: it razed its walls, handed over its ships, paid what was demanded at once and tribute thereafter, and gave up its mainland possessions along with the mine, which is the settlement Darius had imposed a generation before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch adds that Cimon was then accused of taking a bribe from the king of Macedonia not to invade it, and acquitted, with Pericles among the accusers taking the floor only once and as a formality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.100, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=100. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.101, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=101. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.46, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=46. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.47, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=47. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/%CE%9F_%CE%9B%CE%B9%CE%BC%CE%AD%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%BA%CE%B1%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF_%CF%81%CF%89%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%8A%CE%BA%CF%8C_%CE%98%CE%AD%CE%B1%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF_%CE%BD%CE%B7%CF%83%CE%AF_%CF%84%CE%B7%CF%82_%CE%98%CE%AC%CF%83%CE%BF%CF%85_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-%CE%9F_%CE%9B%CE%B9%CE%BC%CE%AD%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%BA%CE%B1%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF_%CF%81%CF%89%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%8A%CE%BA%CF%8C_%CE%98%CE%AD%CE%B1%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF_%CE%BD%CE%B7%CF%83%CE%AF_%CF%84%CE%B7%CF%82_%CE%98%CE%AC%CF%83%CE%BF%CF%85_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The harbour town from the sea","desc":"Limenas on Thasos, the ancient city, seen from the water as a besieging fleet would have seen it. The pale curve on the wooded slope above is the theatre, of the Roman period; the walls the islanders were made to raze ran along this shore.","credit":"Photograph by Ioannis Houvardas, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9F_%CE%9B%CE%B9%CE%BC%CE%AD%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%BA%CE%B1%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF_%CF%81%CF%89%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%8A%CE%BA%CF%8C_%CE%98%CE%AD%CE%B1%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF_%CE%BD%CE%B7%CF%83%CE%AF_%CF%84%CE%B7%CF%82_%CE%98%CE%AC%CF%83%CE%BF%CF%85_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A low waterfront town of white and red-roofed buildings along a shore, backed by a steep pine-covered hill with a pale semicircular scar of ruins part way up it, under a clear sky."}},
{"id":"gr-461","num":461,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The rising called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began when helots and perioikoi seceded to Ithome after an earthquake, and it ended ten years later with the rebels leaving the Peloponnese for good.","questions":["Pausanias explains the earthquake behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as Poseidon's punishment for the ephors dragging suppliants from the altar at Taenarum and killing them.","Sparta called Athens in during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for her skill at sieges and then sent her home alone, which Diodorus calls the first cause of the estrangement between them."],"answer":"Third Messenian War","answerText":"Third Messenian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rising began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 464 BCE, after the earthquake</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dead at Sparta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 years on Ithome</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Third Messenian War</b> is the modern name for the rising that followed the Spartan earthquake, which the ancient writers call the secession to Ithome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says the helots went out, with the Thuriats and Aethaeans of the perioikoi, and adds that most of them were descended from the old Messenians enslaved in the famous war, so that all of them came to be called Messenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus makes the rising a calculation: more than 20,000 Lacedaemonians had died in the earthquake, and men who had stayed quiet from fear of Spartan power saw how few the survivors were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias gives the Messenian version, in which the cause is a sacrilege, since the ephors had dragged Spartan suppliants from the altar at Taenarum and killed them, and Poseidon razed the city for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Diodorus the rebels first marched on Sparta expecting to find it undefended, then learned that Archidamus had the survivors drawn up in a body and went instead to fortify a stronghold in Messenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Sparta called in her allies, and the Athenians came under Cimon because they were thought good at sieges, which is the summons that ended in their being sent home alone under suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus says the Athenians took that dismissal as the first cause of the estrangement between the two states, from which they later filled all Greece with calamities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The men on Ithome held out for ten years, and gave up only on terms: they were to leave the Peloponnese under safe conduct and never set foot in it again, and anyone found there afterwards was to be the slave of whoever caught him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides adds that Sparta had an old Delphic oracle about letting go the suppliant of Zeus at Ithome, and Pausanias has the same oracle threatening punishment if they harmed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They went out with their wives and children and were taken in by Athens, which settled them at Naupactus, a place it had lately taken from the Ozolian Locrians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.101, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=101. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.102, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=102. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.103, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=103. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.63, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=63. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.64, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=64. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 4.24, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=4:chapter=24. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","warfare","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Ithome_ancient_walls_01.jpg/1280px-Ithome_ancient_walls_01.jpg","title":"Ancient walling on Ithome","desc":"Courses of ancient masonry along the eastern summit of the mountain the rebels held, with the old Voulkano monastery on the crest beyond. The fortifications at Messene below are of the 4th century BCE, so how much of what stands here belongs to the siege is not settled.","credit":"Photograph by Mark Landon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ithome_ancient_walls_01.jpg","alt":"A line of large squared limestone blocks in two or three courses running along a bare rocky ridge, with scrub in front and a small tiled stone monastery on the skyline to the left."}},
{"id":"gr-462","num":462,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Plutarch says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left five houses standing, tore peaks from Taygetus, and killed the young men in a colonnade who did not chase a hare outside.","questions":["Thucydides reports that the Spartans believed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was retribution for taking helot suppliants from the temple of Poseidon at Taenarum and killing them.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Archidamus had the alarm sounded so his people gathered under arms, which is what met the helots coming in to finish the survivors."],"answer":"Spartan earthquake","answerText":"Spartan earthquake","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Struck</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 464 BCE, Archidamus 4th year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Houses left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, per Plutarch</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Spartan earthquake</b> struck in the fourth year of the reign of Archidamus, and Plutarch says it was greater than any before reported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his account it tore the Lacedaemonian land into chasms, shook Taygetus until peaks broke away, and left five houses standing in the whole city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus puts the dead above 20,000 and describes houses coming down over a long period as the ground kept moving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch preserves one detail that fixes the moment: young men and boys were exercising in a colonnade when a hare ran through, the boys chased it out oiled as they were, the older ones stayed, and the building fell on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their common tomb was still called Seismatias, the quake tomb, in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The two accounts of what saved the survivors differ in method and agree in effect, Diodorus having Archidamus seize his armour and lead the citizens out of the city, Plutarch having him sound the trumpet as though an enemy were coming so that they would gather to him armed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Plutarch that is what preserved Sparta, since the helots came in from the country to finish the survivors and found them drawn up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greeks looked for a reason and found a sacrilege, though they disagreed about whose: Thucydides says the Spartans had taken helot suppliants from the temple of Poseidon at Taenarum and killed them, and believed the shock a retribution for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias reports a Messenian version in which the men dragged from the altar were Lacedaemonians condemned to death, and Poseidon razed the city for the offence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The charge kept its edge, and a generation later the Athenians answered a Spartan demand about an old curse of their own by telling Sparta to drive out the curse of Taenarum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.63, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=63. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.128, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=128. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 4.24, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=4:chapter=24. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.101, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=101. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","religion","nature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/View_of_Mount_Taygetus_from_the_ruins_of_the_ancient_theatre_of_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg/1280px-View_of_Mount_Taygetus_from_the_ruins_of_the_ancient_theatre_of_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","title":"The city and the mountain","desc":"A fallen column drum and seat blocks in the grass at the ancient theatre of Sparta, with snow still on Taygetus behind. The standing remains here are later than the shock, which Plutarch says tore peaks from that range.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Mount_Taygetus_from_the_ruins_of_the_ancient_theatre_of_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","alt":"Long grass and olive trees over toppled grey stone blocks and a fluted column drum, with a high snow-streaked mountain range in the distance under a pale sky."}},
{"id":"gr-463","num":463,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides says the first open quarrel between Athens and Sparta arose from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when the Spartans grew afraid of what their allies might attempt with the besieged.","questions":["Cimon carried the vote to help Sparta by saying Hellas should not be crippled, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the answer his city got for it.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Athens broke off the alliance made against the Mede and swore the same oaths to Sparta's enemy Argos instead."],"answer":"dismissal from Ithome","answerText":"dismissal from Ithome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Athens sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 462 BCE, for skill at sieges</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cimon ostracised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">for 10 years, per Plutarch</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>dismissal from Ithome</b> was the moment Athens and Sparta stopped being allies, and Thucydides says plainly that the first open quarrel between them arose out of this expedition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta had asked for the Athenians because a long siege had shown her that she could not take the place by assault and they were reputed skilled at such work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sending them was argued about at Athens, where Ephialtes urged the people not to restore a rival city but to let haughty Sparta lie and be trodden underfoot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says Cimon carried the vote with the phrase that Hellas should not be crippled nor Athens robbed of its yoke-fellow, and that the Spartan envoy Pericleidas was still remembered in comedy sitting at the altars in his purple cloak, pale, asking for an army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias notes what made Cimon the natural man to send, since he was Sparta's proxenos in Athens and her patron there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When the assault still failed, Thucydides says the Lacedaemonians grew afraid of the enterprising and revolutionary character of the Athenians, and of what men they thought of alien stock might attempt with the besieged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They dismissed them alone of all the allies without stating the suspicion, saying only that they had no further need of them, and Diodorus has them claim the other allies were sufficient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Athenians understood the reason well enough, went away deeply offended, and on reaching home broke off the alliance made against the Mede and swore the same oaths to Sparta's enemy Argos instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch has two expeditions rather than one, an earlier that gave real help and a later that was sent off as a gathering of dangerous conspirators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his account the returning army turned on the men who had argued for going, and Cimon was ostracised for ten years on a trifling pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.102, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=102. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.64, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=64. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 4.24, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=4:chapter=24. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Messene%2C_Stadion%2C_Blick_nach_N%2C_Ithome_2015-09.jpg/1280px-Messene%2C_Stadion%2C_Blick_nach_N%2C_Ithome_2015-09.jpg","title":"Mount Ithome above the stadium of ancient Messene","desc":"The mountain the helots held against Sparta, seen from the north end of the stadium at Messene. The city below it was founded in 369 BCE, nearly a century after the siege the Athenians were sent to help with and then dismissed from.","credit":"Photograph by HerrAdams, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Messene,_Stadion,_Blick_nach_N,_Ithome_2015-09.jpg","alt":"A long stone stadium with rows of seating, and beyond it a broad wooded mountain rising under a clear sky."}},
{"id":"gr-464","num":464,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Plutarch says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was still in force when the exile came in arms to Tanagra to fight with his own tribe, but the generals were forbidden to receive him.","questions":["Plutarch says the charge behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was that the man loved Sparta and hated the people, while Cornelius Nepos names no charge and calls it the odium his father had met.","After the defeat at Tanagra Athens wanted back the man the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had sent away, and Plutarch says Pericles wrote the decree of recall out with his own hand."],"answer":"ostracism of Cimon","answerText":"ostracism of Cimon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ostracised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 461 BCE, for ten years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recalled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 457 BCE, after Tanagra</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>ostracism of Cimon</b> is the vote, around 461 BCE, that sent the foremost man in Athens away for ten years and left the field to Ephialtes and Pericles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The campaign opened with a trial, not a vote: after taking Thasos he was accused of taking a bribe not to invade Macedonia, and Pericles was his most ardent accuser.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle remembers that prosecution as the moment Pericles first made his name, challenging a serving general's audits while still young, and Plutarch says he spoke only once, as a formality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cimon was acquitted, but in the archonship of Conon the Council of the Areopagus was stripped of all but a few of its jurisdictions, which Plutarch places during an absence of his on service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On his return, Plutarch says, he tried to recall its jurisdiction and revive the aristocracy of Cleisthenes' day, whereupon his enemies banded together, renewed the slanders about his sister and called him Sparta's friend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What finished him was Ithome, where Sparta sent the Athenians home alone: they came back in a rage, moved against the laconisers and, on a trifling pretext, ostracised him for the decreed ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch's Pericles gives the charge outright, that he loved Sparta and hated the people, where Cornelius Nepos names none and says only that he met the odium his father had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The ten years were not seen out: when Sparta invaded Tanagra he came in arms to his own tribe, and Plutarch's two Lives disagree over who drove him off, the Council of Five Hundred or the friends of Pericles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He asked the comrades charged with laconising to clear themselves by fighting well; they set his armour in their midst and fell, a hundred by one account and all of them by the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beaten at Tanagra and expecting a Peloponnesian army in spring, Athens recalled him on a decree Pericles wrote himself, though Plutarch also reports a compact brokered by Elpinice dividing command abroad from power at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 14–15, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 9, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 25, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=25. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 27, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=27. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Cimon</i> 3, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 331–32, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/1235_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Ostrakon_against_Kimon%2C_son_of_Milthiades_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Ort.jpg/1280px-1235_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Ostrakon_against_Kimon%2C_son_of_Milthiades_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Ort.jpg","title":"An ostrakon cast against Cimon","desc":"A sherd of black-glazed pottery scratched in two lines, KIMON MILTIADO, found in the bed of the Eridanos and now in the Kerameikos Archaeological Museum in Athens. Sherds like it were the ballots of an ostracism, and the one above it carries the end of a different name.","credit":"Photograph by Giovanni Dall'Orto, Attribution licence. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1235_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Ostrakon_against_Kimon%2C_son_of_Milthiades_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Ort.jpg","alt":"A curved fragment of black-glazed pottery lying on a white surface, with Greek letters scratched through the glaze in two lines to show the red clay beneath."}},
{"id":"gr-465","num":465,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first prosecuted many Areopagites over their conduct in office, then in the archonship of Conon stripped their council of the powers that made it the constitution's safeguard.","questions":["Plutarch names only Aristides and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as keeping their hands clean of public money, and says the second of them tried to dethrone the Council of the Areopagus.","Aristotle names Aristodicus of Tanagra as the man who craftily murdered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Diodorus says only that he was done to death by night and nobody ever knew how."],"answer":"Ephialtes","answerText":"Ephialtes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reform</span><span class=\"dt-v\">462 BCE, archonship of Conon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 461 BCE, by Aristodicus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Ephialtes</b> son of Sophonides was the Athenian who broke the political power of the Council of the Areopagus, and whose few recorded years carried the city into a radical democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle says the constitution had stayed under the leadership of the Areopagites for about seventeen years after the Persian War, being gradually modified, until Ephialtes became head of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had the name of being incorruptible and just, and Plutarch lists him with Aristides alone among the politicians of the day as keeping his hands off the gains of public office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He first made away with many Areopagites by prosecuting them over their acts of administration, then in the archonship of Conon stripped the council of the added powers that made it the constitution's safeguard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he took he distributed, some to the Council of Five Hundred and the rest to the people and the jury-courts, and elsewhere Aristotle says that Ephialtes and Pericles together docked the Areopagus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He was no mere speaker: Callisthenes, quoted by Plutarch, has him take thirty ships past the Chelidonian isles, where Pericles took fifty, to show how far the Persians now kept away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At home he opposed Cimon, and when Sparta asked for help at Ithome he urged the Athenians not to restore a rival city but to let haughty Sparta lie and be trodden underfoot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Plutarch's Pericles makes him one of the friends Pericles commissioned to carry out his policy, and quotes Plato against the result, that it poured out for the citizens too much undiluted freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He was killed not long after: Aristotle says he was craftily murdered by Aristodicus of Tanagra, where Diodorus, calling the reform lawless, says only that he was done to death by night and nobody ever knew how.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Plutarch reports and rejects Idomeneus' charge that Pericles had his own friend assassinated out of envy, and Aristotle counts forward from the death, dating the archonship's opening to the zeugitai five years after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 25, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=25. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 26, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=26. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=2:section=1274a. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.77, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=77. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/View_of_the_Areopagus_from_the_Acropolis_in_Athens%2C_20240531_1329_9684.jpg/1280px-View_of_the_Areopagus_from_the_Acropolis_in_Athens%2C_20240531_1329_9684.jpg","title":"The Areopagus seen from the Acropolis","desc":"The bare limestone outcrop below the Acropolis that gave the council its name, with modern Athens beyond. What Ephialtes took from it went to the Council of Five Hundred, the assembly and the jury-courts.","credit":"Photograph by Jakub Halun, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Areopagus_from_the_Acropolis_in_Athens%2C_20240531_1329_9684.jpg","alt":"A bare pale rock outcrop rising out of trees in the middle distance, with people standing on its top and a city spreading to hills beyond."}},
{"id":"gr-466","num":466,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle credits Themistocles with part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, telling the Areopagites that men were conspiring against the constitution and telling Ephialtes that the council meant to arrest him.","questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the powers that had made a council of ex-archons the safeguard of the constitution sat instead with the Five Hundred, the assembly and the jury-courts.","Aristotle's Politics blames the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the pay Pericles later gave jurors for the stages by which Athens reached the democracy of his own day."],"answer":"reform of the Areopagus","answerText":"reform of the Areopagus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">462 BCE, archonship of Conon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ephialtes killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 461 BCE, soon afterwards</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>reform of the Areopagus</b> was the vote at Athens, in the archonship of Conon, that took from the council of former archons the powers which had made it the safeguard of the constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle says the constitution had run under the leadership of the Areopagites for about seventeen years after the Persian War, being gradually modified as the population grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attack came in two stages: Ephialtes first made away with many Areopagites by prosecuting them over their acts of administration, and only then moved on the council itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was taken was distributed rather than abolished, some of it to the Council of Five Hundred, some to the people and some to the jury-courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle credits Themistocles with part of it, having him warn the Areopagites that men were conspiring against the constitution while telling Ephialtes the council meant to arrest him, until the two denounced the Areopagites in Council and assembly alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch sets it during an absence of Cimon's on service, when the populace confounded the established order and plunged the city into unmitigated democracy, Pericles being now a man of power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The date is not agreed: Aristotle names the archon Conon, where Diodorus files the vote under Phrasicleides and calls it the destruction of renowned customs the fathers had followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The council that survived is the one Aeschylus put on stage, where Athena founds a tribunal on the hill of Ares to judge the first trial for bloodshed and tells Attica to keep neither anarchy nor tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ephialtes was killed not long afterwards, and five years after his death the roll from which the nine archons were drawn by lot was opened to the zeugitai, the first of them to hold office being Mnesitheides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle's Politics puts the vote, with the jury pay Pericles later introduced, among the stages by which the leaders of the people brought Athens to the democracy of his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 25, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=25. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 26, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=26. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 27, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=27. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=2:section=1274a. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Eumenides</i> 681–710, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0006:card=681. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.77, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=77. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 15, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Kleroterion%2C_AGMA%2C_225287.jpg/1280px-Kleroterion%2C_AGMA%2C_225287.jpg","title":"A kleroterion, the allotment machine of the Athenian courts","desc":"A marble slab cut with rows of slots, in the Ancient Agora Museum at Athens, into which citizens' bronze tickets were set so that jurors could be drawn by lot. Part of what the vote took from the Areopagus went to the courts these men sat in.","credit":"Photograph by Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kleroterion%2C_AGMA%2C_225287.jpg","alt":"A rough-edged white marble slab standing in a museum case, its face cut with about twenty vertical columns of narrow horizontal slots."}},
{"id":"gr-467","num":467,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Plutarch says the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was long and out of proportion, which is why almost every statue of him wears a helmet, and why the comic poets called him Squill-head.","questions":["Thucydides writes that under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> what was nominally a democracy became government by the first citizen, since his rank, ability and known integrity let him lead the multitude rather than be led.","Looking and sounding like the tyrant Peisistratus kept <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of politics as a young man, Plutarch says, and he came forward only once Aristides was dead and Themistocles banished."],"answer":"Pericles","answerText":"Pericles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 495 – 429 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foremost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 years, 15 in unbroken command</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Pericles</b> led Athens through the middle of the 5th century BCE, and Thucydides' judgement is that what was nominally a democracy became in his hands government by the first citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was of the foremost family on both sides: his father Xanthippus beat the King's generals at Mycale, and his mother Agariste was granddaughter of the Cleisthenes who expelled the tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says she dreamt she had borne a lion days before bearing him, and that his one fault of appearance was a long head, which is why nearly all his statues wear a helmet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The comic poets called him Squill-head for it, and the surname Olympian came chiefly from his speaking, since they said he thundered and lightened and wielded a thunderbolt in his tongue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Damon taught him music and used the lyre, Plutarch says, as a screen for politics; he heard Zeno of Elea, but the man who did most for him was Anaxagoras, whom that generation called Nous, Mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> As a young man he kept out of politics, thought to look and sound like the tyrant Peisistratus and afraid of being ostracised, and came forward only when Aristides was dead, Themistocles banished and Cimon abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The choice went against a nature Plutarch calls anything but popular, and he changed his way of living with it: one street only, to the market-place and the council-chamber, and no dining out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What Anaxagoras taught him against superstition shows in the one-horned ram, where Lampon the seer read the horn as a sign of sole rule and Anaxagoras cut the skull open to show the brain drawn to a point at its root.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch adds the sting: Anaxagoras had the applause that day and Lampon a little later, when Thucydides son of Melesias was overthrown and Pericles stood alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He stood first for forty years, fifteen in unbroken command through his annual generalship, and Thucydides says his rank, ability and known integrity let him lead the multitude rather than be led.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.65, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=65. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 3, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 4, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 6, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Pericles_Townley_BM_549.jpg/1280px-Pericles_Townley_BM_549.jpg","title":"The Townley bust of Pericles","desc":"A Roman marble herm in the British Museum, copied after a Greek original, with the name cut across the base. Every such portrait wears the helmet that hides the long head the comic poets made fun of.","credit":"Photograph by Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pericles_Townley_BM_549.jpg","alt":"A white marble bust of a bearded man wearing a helmet pushed back on his head, on a squared base carved with Greek capitals."}},
{"id":"gr-468","num":468,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle's list of what makes a constitution democratic reads like a description of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, running to allotment, short terms, no property qualification and an assembly sovereign over everything.","questions":["The pamphlet ascribed to Xenophon attacks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for letting the worst be better off than the good, then grants that it is the poor who man the ships.","Aristotle says that under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the people made itself master of everything and administered it all by decrees and by jury-courts, and defends that as safer than trusting a few."],"answer":"radical democracy","answerText":"radical democracy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 462 BCE at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Counted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 7th of 11 constitutions</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Radical democracy</b> is the modern name for the Athenian system after 462 BCE, when the old council's powers passed to bodies open to every citizen; Plutarch's own phrase for it is unmitigated democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's list of what makes a constitution democratic reads like a description of Athens: officials elected by all from all, and the government of each by all and of all by each in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds allotment to every office that does not need skill, no property qualification or a very low one, short terms, no repeated tenure, judicial power exercised by all citizens and an assembly sovereign over everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind the list he sets two ideas of liberty: equality counted by number rather than by worth, so that the majority decides, and living as one likes, since to live otherwise is a slave's life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Athenian Constitution</i> locates the people's power in the courts, saying that with the power of the vote the people becomes sovereign in the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It counts eleven changes of constitution and makes this the seventh, outlined by Aristides and completed by Ephialtes when he put down the Areopagite council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Of the constitution as it finally stood it says the people made itself master of everything, administering all by decrees and by jury-courts, and defends that, since a few are more easily corrupted by gain and influence than the many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hostile contemporary account is the pamphlet ascribed to Xenophon, whose author opens by saying he does not think well of a constitution that lets the worst be better off than the good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He grants the case all the same, since it is the poor who man the ships and give the city its strength, steersmen and boatswains and look-outs and shipwrights more than the hoplites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> And he notices what the people do not claim: no allotted share in the generalships or the cavalry commands, which they leave to the most influential men, while keeping the offices that are salaried and profitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 15, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 6.1317b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1317b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 9, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=9. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=41. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/AGMA_St%C3%A8le_de_la_d%C3%A9mocratie.jpg","title":"The stele of the Athenian law against tyranny","desc":"A bearded seated Demos crowned by a standing Demokratia, above the text of a law of 337 BCE, in the Ancient Agora Museum at Athens. The stone is more than a century later than the vote this card is about, and is the clearest picture the Athenians left of what they thought they had.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY-SA 2.5. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AGMA_St%C3%A8le_de_la_d%C3%A9mocratie.jpg","alt":"A tall marble stele with a pediment, carved at the top with a seated bearded man being crowned by a standing draped woman, and closely inscribed with Greek below."}},
{"id":"gr-469","num":469,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aeschines says a purifying sacrifice was carried round before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened, and that the herald then asked who above fifty years of age wished to speak first.","questions":["Aristophanes opens a comedy with one farmer alone on the Pnyx at daybreak, waiting for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while everyone else gossips in the marketplace and dodges the vermilion rope.","Aristotle says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met four times in each presidency, and that the chief of those four confirmed the magistrates and dealt with the food supply and the defence of the country."],"answer":"ecclesia","answerText":"ecclesia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>ecclesia</b> was the assembly of the Athenian citizens, and Aristotle counts an assembly sovereign over all matters among the marks that make a constitution democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It met four times in each presidency of the council's year, on days and at a place the presidents posted in advance along with the business to be taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of the four was the sovereign meeting, which confirmed the magistrates in office if they were thought to govern well and dealt with the food supply and the defence of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At that meeting informations were laid by anyone who wished, and the inventories of estates being confiscated were read out with the lists of suits over inheritances and heiresses, so that all knew of any estate falling vacant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the sixth presidency it also voted on whether an ostracism should be held that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A second meeting was given to petitions, at which anyone who wished might lay a suppliant-branch and speak to the people on any matter he liked, public or private.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other two took the rest, three items of sacred business, three audiences for heralds and embassies and three of secular business, though sometimes matters were taken without a preliminary vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aeschines describes the opening: a purifying sacrifice carried round, the herald's traditional prayers, and then the call asking who above fifty years of age wished to address the assembly, after which any other entitled Athenian might.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pamphlet ascribed to Xenophon puts the same openness sourly, saying that as things are any wretch who wants to can stand up and obtain what is good for him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristophanes opens the <i>Acharnians</i> with a farmer alone on the Pnyx at daybreak while the rest gossip in the marketplace and dodge the vermilion rope, and the presidents arrive at midday shoving for the front seats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=43. [Open access]","Aeschines, <i>Against Timarchus</i> 23, trans. Charles Darwin Adams (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0002:speech=1:section=23. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Acharnians</i> 1–42, in <i>The Eleven Comedies</i>, anonymous translation (New York: Liveright), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0240:card=1. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 6.1317b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1317b. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/The_Pnyx_plateau_on_March_13%2C_2021.jpg/1280px-The_Pnyx_plateau_on_March_13%2C_2021.jpg","title":"The Pnyx, where the assembly met","desc":"The open terrace on the hill west of the Acropolis, with the cut scarp and the steps of the speaker's platform on the right. The citizens stood on this ground; the Parthenon is on the height across the trees.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Pnyx_plateau_on_March_13%2C_2021.jpg","alt":"A wide bare terrace of pale ground on a hilltop, bounded on the right by a low cut rock face, with the Acropolis and Parthenon rising beyond trees on the left."}},
{"id":"gr-470","num":470,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle says one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was drawn by lot to preside for a day and a night, keeping the state seal and the keys of the temples where the public money lay.","questions":["The fifty men of one tribe served as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a tenth of the year, dining in the Round-house at public expense and calling the meetings of the council and the people.","Xenophon says some of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused to put an illegal question after Arginusae, and that all gave way in fear except Socrates son of Sophroniscus."],"answer":"prytaneis","answerText":"prytaneis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>prytaneis</b> were the fifty councillors of one Athenian tribe who presided over the council of five hundred and the assembly for a tenth of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each tribe took the presidency in turn, in an order settled by lot, the first four drawn holding it for 36 days and the last six for 35.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They dined together in the Round-house at public expense and called the meetings of both the council and the people, posting the business, the day's agenda and the place in writing beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They gave heralds and ambassadors their first hearing, and dispatches were delivered into their hands by the men who carried them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aeschines says it was the presiding officers who were commanded to declare the order of business, the matters of religion first, then the heralds and embassies, then the secular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One of them was drawn by lot as their head for a day and a night, could not hold it twice, kept the state seal and the keys of the temples holding the public money and documents, and slept in the Round-house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When a meeting was called he drew nine chairmen by lot, one from each of the other tribes, and handed them the agenda; they brought business forward, counted the votes and could close the sitting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A man could be head only once in a year though he might be one of the nine chairmen once in every presidency, and the board also conducted the elections of generals and cavalry commanders when the omens allowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon shows what presiding could cost when the assembly wanted to try the ten generals together after Arginusae: some of them refused to put an illegal question, and the crowd shouted for them to be summoned to court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They gave way in fear, all but Socrates son of Sophroniscus, who said he would act in no case except in accordance with the law, and Plato has Socrates tell the same story of himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=43. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 44, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=44. [Open access]","Aeschines, <i>Against Timarchus</i> 23, trans. Charles Darwin Adams (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0002:speech=1:section=23. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 1.7.14–15, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Xen.%20Hell.%201.7.14. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Apology</i> 32b, trans. Harold North Fowler, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1966), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.%20Apol.%2032b. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/%CE%98%CF%8C%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%91%CE%B3%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%AC%CF%82_1149.jpg/1280px-%CE%98%CF%8C%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%91%CE%B3%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%AC%CF%82_1149.jpg","title":"The Tholos, the Round-house of the Agora","desc":"The circular foundation of the building in which the presiding fifty dined at public expense, and in which their head and a third of the board stayed through the night.","credit":"Photograph by C messier, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%98%CF%8C%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%91%CE%B3%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%AC%CF%82_1149.jpg","alt":"A low circular wall of rough masonry enclosing a floor of scattered paving slabs, on bare ground with trees behind."}},
{"id":"gr-471","num":471,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle says a juror in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> voted with two bronze pebbles, one pierced and one whole, and pressed them against the lamp-stand so nobody could see which he held.","questions":["Any Athenian over thirty who was not in debt to the Treasury or disfranchised might sit in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, carrying a boxwood ticket cut with his name, his father's name and his deme.","Aristophanes gives an old juror boasting that no king's power exceeds his, since men on trial before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> flatter him, tell him fables and drag their children forward to whine."],"answer":"dikasteria","answerText":"dikasteria","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>dikasteria</b> were the jury-courts of Athens, and Aristotle counts judicial power exercised by all citizens on the greatest matters among the marks of a democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Any citizen over thirty might sit who was not in debt to the Treasury or disfranchised, and a man who sat without the right was tried by a jury which fixed his punishment itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each juror carried a boxwood ticket cut with his name, his father's name and his deme, and one letter of the alphabet as far as kappa, each tribe's jurors being divided into ten sections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The courts took their own letters from lambda onwards, drawn by lot, and an attendant went straight out and fixed each court's letter up at its entrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At each of the ten entrances stood staves as many as the jurymen, and lettered acorns for the courts to be filled were thrown into an urn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A juror voted with two bronze pebbles, one pierced through and one whole, and the herald proclaimed that the pierced one counted for the first speaker and the whole one for the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Taking them from the lamp-stand he pressed them against it so neither party could see which he held, then dropped the one he meant to count into a copper jar and the other into a wooden one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The copper jar's lid had a hole big enough for one pebble only, so nobody could put in two, and no man could claim his token, and with it his fee, unless he had voted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were emptied on to a board pierced with as many holes as there were votes, counted in two sets where the litigants could see, and the herald called the number; an equal vote acquitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristophanes gives the other side, an old juror boasting that no king's power is greater than his, since the accused flatter him, tell him fables out of Aesop and drag their children forward to whine at his feet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 63, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=63. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 68, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=68. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 69, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=69. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 548–575, in <i>The Complete Greek Drama</i>, vol. 2, ed. Eugene O'Neill, Jr. (New York: Random House, 1938), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristoph.%20Wasps%20548. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 6.1317b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1317b. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","athens"]},
{"id":"gr-472","num":472,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle says Pericles answered the wealth Cimon spent on the poor by making jury service the first office to carry <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on the advice of Damonides of Oea.","questions":["A drachma for an ordinary meeting of the assembly, half a drachma for the courts and five obols for the council were the rates of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Athenian Constitution sets out.","Aristophanes has a chorus of old jurors complain that their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must buy bread, wood and stew, and Philocleon's daughter fish the three obols from his mouth."],"answer":"misthos","answerText":"misthos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Misthos</b> was the pay an Athenian drew for serving the city, and Aristotle counts payment for public duties among the marks that make a constitution democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Athenian Constitution</i> says Pericles made service in the jury-courts a paid office as a popular counter-measure against Cimon's wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cimon had an estate large enough for a tyrant and spent it in public, and Pericles, whose own means could not match that, took the advice of Damonides of Oea and gave the multitude what was already their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reports the criticism too, that the courts deteriorated because it was always ordinary men rather than respectable ones who took care to cast lots for the duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> makes a related point about the council: where pay is plentiful the people draw all the trials to itself and strip even that office of its power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rates are set out plainly: a drachma for an ordinary meeting of the assembly and a drachma and a half for a sovereign one, half a drachma for the jury-courts, and five obols for the council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The presidents got an extra obol for food, the nine archons four obols each out of which they had to keep a herald and a flute-player, and the archon on Salamis a drachma a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pay for attending the assembly came last and was rejected the first time it was proposed, since men were not turning up and the presidents kept contriving devices to get a show of hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Agyrrhius then carried an obol, Heracleides of Clazomenae raised it to two, and Agyrrhius came back and made it three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristophanes gives the scale of it from below, a chorus of old jurors complaining that their small pay must buy bread, wood and stew, and Philocleon coming home to a daughter who kisses him and fishes the three obols out of his mouth with her tongue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 6.1317b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1317b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 27, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=27. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 62, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=62. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=41. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 291–300, 605–610, in <i>The Complete Greek Drama</i>, vol. 2, ed. Eugene O'Neill, Jr. (New York: Random House, 1938), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristoph.%20Wasps%20291. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Athens_-_450-425_BC_-_silver_obol_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg","title":"An Athenian silver obol","desc":"The owl, olive spray and first three letters of the city's name on the reverse of a coin about the size of a fingernail, in the Berlin cabinet. Six of these made a drachma, and three of them were a juror's day.","credit":"Photograph by ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_-_450-425_BC_-_silver_obol_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg","alt":"A small irregular silver coin held on two pins, stamped with a standing owl, an olive spray and a crescent, with three Greek letters at the right."}},
{"id":"gr-473","num":473,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Every military officer at Athens was elected by a show of hands rather than drawn by lot, which made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the great exception among the city's offices.","questions":["In every presidency the assembly voted on whether each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was administering his command satisfactorily, and one who lost that vote went before a jury-court.","Aristotle says one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the heavy infantry abroad, one guarded the country, two had the Piraeus between them and one dealt with the trierarchs."],"answer":"strategos","answerText":"strategos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"A <b>strategos</b> was one of the ten generals of Athens, and the office was the great exception to the lot, since every military officer there was elected by a show of hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were once chosen one from each tribe and later from the whole body of citizens together, the same vote deciding what each man was to command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One took the heavy infantry on foreign expeditions, one guarded the country and commanded any war fought in it, two had the Piraeus between them at Munychia and at the Point, and one dealt with the trierarchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest were sent out on expeditions as occasion arose, and beneath them stood ten regimental commanders, one from each tribe, who appointed the company commanders in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The elections were held by the first board of presidents after the sixth presidency in whose term the weather-omens were favourable, and needed a preliminary resolution of the council first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A general was not left alone once elected, since in every presidency a confirmatory vote was taken on whether he was administering his command satisfactorily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One who lost that vote went before a jury-court, which assessed his penalty if it convicted him and returned him to his command if it did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the field he could punish a breach of discipline with imprisonment, exile or a fine, though the <i>Athenian Constitution</i> says a fine was not usual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was also the one office a man might hold again and again where the rest could be held once, and the pamphlet ascribed to Xenophon says the people knew their business in leaving the generalships to the most influential men rather than drawing them by lot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What that made possible is Pericles, who Plutarch says held an imperial sway continuous and unbroken for fifteen years by his annual tenure of the office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 61, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=61. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=43. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 44, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=44. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 62, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=62. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["title","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Casualty_list_of_the_Erechtheid_tribe_Louvre_MR1060.jpg/1280px-Casualty_list_of_the_Erechtheid_tribe_Louvre_MR1060.jpg","title":"An Athenian tribe's list of its dead","desc":"The Erechtheid tribe's casualty stele in the Louvre, the rank and file set out in columns. The separate block at the foot opens with the word STRATEGOS, the general being listed apart from them along with the seer and the archers.","credit":"Photograph by Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casualty_list_of_the_Erechtheid_tribe_Louvre_MR1060.jpg","alt":"A tall weathered marble slab covered in columns of shallow-cut Greek capitals, with a short separate block of names at the bottom."}},
{"id":"gr-474","num":474,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The Athenian Constitution dates <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the archonship of Antidotus and gives its reason as nothing more than the large number of the citizens.","questions":["When the king of Egypt sent forty thousand measures of grain to be shared among the citizens, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put nearly five thousand men of illegal birth into slavery.","With the last of his legitimate sons dead, Pericles asked the assembly to suspend <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and they let him enrol his illegitimate son and give him his own name."],"answer":"Pericles' citizenship law","answerText":"Pericles' citizenship law","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">451 BCE, archonship of Antidotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suspended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 429 BCE, for its own author</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Pericles' citizenship law</b> confined the standing of an Athenian to men of citizen birth on both sides, and the <i>Athenian Constitution</i> dates it to the archonship of Antidotus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason it gives is the large number of the citizens, and nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rule was not peculiar to Athens: Aristotle says citizenship is in practice limited to the child of citizens on both sides rather than one, and that some states carry the requirement back two or three generations or further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own definition runs differently, a citizen being whoever has the right to share in deliberative or judicial office, and a state a body of such men numerous enough to live independently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He adds that not everyone a state cannot do without is counted among them, since artisans and hired labourers are indispensable and are not citizens in every constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch shows the law at work when the king of Egypt sent the people forty thousand measures of grain to be shared out among the citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A crop of prosecutions followed against men of illegal birth who had gone unnoticed until then, and many of them suffered at the hands of informers as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A little less than five thousand were convicted and sold into slavery, and the scrutiny left fourteen thousand and forty men adjudged to be Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Years afterwards the plague took most of his relatives and friends, and Plutarch says he was not seen to weep until the last of his legitimate sons was dead and he broke down laying the wreath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He then asked the assembly to suspend the law he had carried himself; they allowed it, his illegitimate son was enrolled and given his own name, and that son won a sea battle at Arginusae and was put to death by the people with his fellow generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 26, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=26. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 37, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 3.1275b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=3:section=1275b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 3.1278a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=3:section=1278a. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 36, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/%D0%A4%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B5_%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8_%D0%9B%D0%9F%28%D0%B8%D0%B7_%D0%B8%D0%B7%D0%B4_%D0%9A%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%29.jpg","title":"The papyrus of the Athenian Constitution","desc":"A facsimile of part of London Papyrus 131 in the British Museum, published in Kenyon's edition. This is the manuscript that carries the archonship the law is dated by, and it lay unread until the end of the 19th century.","credit":"Facsimile from F. G. Kenyon's edition, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A4%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B5_%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8_%D0%9B%D0%9F%28%D0%B8%D0%B7_%D0%B8%D0%B7%D0%B4_%D0%9A%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%29.jpg","alt":"A rectangular sheet of papyrus covered in nine lines of small brown cursive Greek writing, the fibres of the sheet visible across it."}},
{"id":"gr-475","num":475,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides gives the defection of Megara from Sparta as the opening of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and as the principal cause of the deadly hatred the Corinthians felt for Athens afterwards.","questions":["Athens met a Corinthian invasion of the Megarid during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the old men and the boys left in the city, since her armies were away at Aegina and in Egypt.","Euboea revolted, Megara cut down its Athenian garrison and King Pleistoanax came as far as Eleusis, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended in a truce meant to last thirty years."],"answer":"First Peloponnesian War","answerText":"First Peloponnesian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 460 – 446 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Thirty Years&#39; Peace, 446 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>First Peloponnesian War</b> is a modern name for the fighting between Athens and the Peloponnesians between the Persian invasions and the great war of 431, which Thucydides sets out in a digression he says explains the growth of Athenian power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened with a defection rather than a battle: Megara left the Spartan alliance for Athens, annoyed by a boundary war Corinth had forced on her, and the Athenians took Megara and Pegae and built her long walls down to Nisaea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides calls that the principal cause of the deadly hatred the Corinthians felt for Athens afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting that followed was scattered rather than pitched, a defeat at Halieis, a victory off Cecryphaleia, and then a great sea battle off Aegina where seventy ships were taken and a siege begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Corinthians came down into the Megarid reckoning that with armies away at Aegina and in Egypt Athens could not answer, and Athens answered with the old men and the boys left in the city, under Myronides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That battle was drawn, and in the second a division of the retreating Corinthians blundered into a walled field with a deep trench round it and no way out, where the Athenians ringed them and stoned every man inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens then beat the Boeotians at Oenophyta and took Boeotia and Phocis, pulled down the walls of Tanagra, held a hundred of the richest Locrians as hostages, finished her own long walls and reduced Aegina to tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A five years' truce followed, and Cimon died besieging Kitium on Cyprus while the fleet he had brought was running short of food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The end came when Euboea revolted, Megara revolted and cut down its Athenian garrison, and a Peloponnesian army under King Pleistoanax came into Attica as far as Eleusis and then went home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Pericles brought Euboea back and the Athenians made a truce for thirty years, giving up Nisaea, Pegae, Troezen and Achaea, the mainland footholds the whole war had been about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.97, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=97. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.103, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=103. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.105, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=105. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.106, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=106. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.108, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=108. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.112, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=112. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.114, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=114. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.115, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=115. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","warfare","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%B9%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%BF%CE%B3%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A7%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%95%CE%BB%CE%B5%CF%85%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_4.jpg/1280px-%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%B9%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%BF%CE%B3%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A7%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%95%CE%BB%CE%B5%CF%85%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_4.jpg","title":"The sanctuary at Eleusis","desc":"Rock-cut steps and fallen blocks on the archaeological site, with the modern town behind. A Peloponnesian army under King Pleistoanax came this far into Attica in the last year of the war, ravaged the country and turned back without going on to Athens.","credit":"Photograph by Meltedrainbow, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%B9%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%BF%CE%B3%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A7%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%95%CE%BB%CE%B5%CF%85%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_4.jpg","alt":"Wet rock-cut steps and worn paving running up a low slope on an archaeological site under a grey sky, with a bell tower and modern houses beyond."}},
{"id":"gr-476","num":476,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides says the victory at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went to the Lacedaemonians after heavy loss on both sides, where Diodorus says the fight ended in a tie and both armies claimed it.","questions":["A Spartan army returning from Doris found the pass over Geraneia held and the gulf unsafe, so it waited in Boeotia and fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> there.","Thessalian cavalry riding with the Athenians went over to the Spartans during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Diodorus says they then ambushed an Athenian supply train by night."],"answer":"Battle of Tanagra","answerText":"Battle of Tanagra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 457 BCE, in Boeotia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian levy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14,000, with 1,000 Argives</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Tanagra</b> was fought in Boeotia during the First Peloponnesian War, and the two surviving accounts of it do not agree on who won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Lacedaemonians had marched north to help the Dorians of Boeum, Kitinium and Erineum, the old home of their own people, against a Phocian attack, with fifteen hundred hoplites and ten thousand allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Having made the Phocians give the captured town back, they found the way home shut: the Athenians held Megara and Pegae and guarded the pass over Geraneia, and the Athenian fleet made the gulf unsafe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So they waited in Boeotia, and Thucydides adds a second reason, that a party in Athens had secretly encouraged them in the hope of ending the democracy and stopping the long walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Athenians came out with their whole levy, a thousand Argives and their other allies, fourteen thousand men in all, with Thessalian cavalry that went over to the Lacedaemonians during the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Thucydides gives the fight in one sentence, that after heavy loss on both sides the victory declared for the Lacedaemonians, who cut down the fruit trees of the Megarid on their way home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The suspicion ran both ways: Plutarch says the exiled Cimon came in arms to stand with his tribe, was refused, and asked the comrades charged with laconising to clear themselves by fighting, which they did and fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus tells it longer and differently, with the Athenians and Argives fighting the battle through until night ended it undecided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his account the Thessalians then ambushed a supply train from Attica by night, taken for friends by guards who did not know they had changed sides, and the second fight that followed ended in a tie, both armies claiming it and agreeing a truce of four months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias saw a slab in the Athenian public cemetery carved with horsemen fighting, set up for Melanopus and Macartatus, who died against Lacedaemonians and Boeotians on the borders of Eleon and Tanagra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.107, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=107. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.108, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=108. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.80, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=80. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.29, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=29. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","warfare","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/Asopos_River_at_AgiosThomasTanagras.jpg","title":"The Asopos at the pass of ancient Tanagra","desc":"The Asopos in winter, at the pass below the site of ancient Tanagra in eastern Boeotia. The ancient accounts place the battle at Tanagra and no closer, so the exact ground is not known.","credit":"Photograph by Agiosthomas, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asopos_River_at_AgiosThomasTanagras.jpg","alt":"A narrow brown river winding between banks of bare winter scrub and leafless trees, with a low bridge crossing it in the distance and pale hills beyond."}},
{"id":"gr-477","num":477,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides dates the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 62 days after the defeat at Tanagra, and says that after it the Athenians became masters of Boeotia and Phocis.","questions":["Diodorus rates the Athenian victory at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Marathon and Plataea, and complains that not one historian has described how it was fought or how the troops stood.","The funeral oration in Plato's <i>Menexenus</i> calls the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a two days' fight in which Athens defended the liberties of the Boeotians and restored men wrongly driven out."],"answer":"Battle of Oenophyta","answerText":"Battle of Oenophyta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 457 BCE, in Boeotia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">After Tanagra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">62 days, per Thucydides</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Oenophyta</b> was fought in Boeotia in about 457 BCE, when an Athenian army under Myronides beat the Boeotians and brought central Greece under Athenian control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides dates it 62 days after the defeat at Tanagra: the Athenians became masters of Boeotia and Phocis, pulled down the walls of Tanagra, took a hundred of the richest Opuntian Locrians as hostages and finished their own long walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus gives the quarrel behind it: the Thebans, humbled for having sided with Xerxes, asked Sparta to help them win the leadership of all Boeotia, promising in return to make war on Athens alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sparta agreed, judging a stronger Thebes a counterweight to Athens, and with an army already at Tanagra enlarged the Theban circuit wall and compelled the Boeotian cities to submit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Athenians elected Myronides son of Callias, who marched on the day he had named with whoever had turned up, saying that men late by choice would play an ignoble part in battle too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In Diodorus the Boeotians take the field as a nation once their land is wasted, hold the struggle all day, and break only after a hard fight, leaving Myronides master of every Boeotian city except Thebes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That exception is not in Thucydides, and the accounts differ in shape too: Thucydides has one battle after Tanagra where Diodorus puts an earlier victory, the taking of Tanagra and the wasting of Boeotia first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The funeral oration in Plato's <i>Menexenus</i> calls it a two days' battle fought for the liberties of the Boeotians, which restored men wrongly driven out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus ranks it with Marathon and Plataea, won single-handed against the people he thought the staunchest fighters in Greece, and complains that although it became famous no historian described how it was fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What it won lasted about ten years: in 447 the Boeotian exiles holding Orchomenus and Chaeronea beat Tolmides at Coronea, and Athens gave up all Boeotia to get its prisoners back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","boeotia"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.108, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=108. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.81, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=81. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.83, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=83. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Menexenus</i> 242b, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.%20Menex.%20242b. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.82, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=82. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.113, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=113. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Remains_of_the_Long_Walls_at_Moschato_on_25_February_2019.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_Long_Walls_at_Moschato_on_25_February_2019.jpg","title":"What the battle paid for: a fragment of the Long Walls","desc":"The footings of a tower of the Long Walls, in a park at Moschato between Athens and Piraeus. Thucydides puts the finishing of those walls among the things the Athenians did in the season that followed Oenophyta.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC0 1.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_Long_Walls_at_Moschato_on_25_February_2019.jpg","alt":"A low rectangle of weathered stone blocks, the foundation course of a square tower, set in a patch of grass in a small urban park with pine trees and a graffitied wall behind."}},
{"id":"gr-478","num":478,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"200 Athenian ships were diverted from Cyprus into the Nile at the start of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and took two-thirds of Memphis before the White Castle held them.","questions":["Megabyzus ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by diverting a canal, which left the Athenian ships high and dry on Prosopitis and let his army march over on foot and take it.","Thucydides and Diodorus disagree about how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended, one saying most of the army perished and the other that the Persians granted a truce letting them go."],"answer":"Egyptian expedition","answerText":"Egyptian expedition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 460 – 454 BCE, in Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 years, per Thucydides</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On Prosopitis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">besieged 18 months</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Egyptian expedition</b> was the Athenian intervention in a revolt of Egypt against Persia between about 460 and 454 BCE, and it ended in the heaviest defeat the Delian League ever suffered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says Inaros son of Psammetichus, a Libyan king based at Marea, brought almost the whole of Egypt out in revolt from Artaxerxes and invited the Athenians in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They broke off a campaign in Cyprus, took 200 ships into the Nile, mastered the river and two-thirds of Memphis, and attacked the last third, the White Castle, where the Persians had taken refuge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus, who has the Egyptians rise on the news of Xerxes' death and make Inaros king, says Athens voted 300 triremes and that Artaxerxes sent more than 300,000 men against them under Achaemenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus, writing nearest the events, calls that Achaemenes a son of Darius set over Egypt by Xerxes, and says Inaros killed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The King's next move was money rather than men: he sent an agent to Sparta to buy an invasion of Attica that would draw the Athenians home, and recalled him when it failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Megabyzus son of Zopyrus then arrived with a large army, beat the Egyptians and their allies, drove the Greeks out of Memphis and shut them on the island of Prosopitis for a year and six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He ended it by diverting the canal, which left the Athenian ships high and dry and joined most of the island to the mainland, so that his men marched over on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides says the enterprise came to ruin after six years, that most of the host perished with only a few reaching Cyrene, that Inaros was betrayed and crucified, and that a relief squadron of fifty ships was destroyed at the Mendesian mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus ends it quite differently: the Egyptians deserted, the Athenians burned their own ships, and the Persian generals, unwilling to lose many thousands against such men, granted a truce that let them leave Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","egypt"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.104, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=104. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.109, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=109. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.110, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=110. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.71, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=71. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.74, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=74. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.7, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=7. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.77, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=77. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Ruins_at_Mit_Rahina.jpg/1280px-Ruins_at_Mit_Rahina.jpg","title":"What is left of Memphis","desc":"Fallen blocks and column drums among the palms at Mit Rahina, the site of Memphis. Thucydides says the Athenians mastered the Nile and two-thirds of the city, and were still attacking the last third when the Persian army arrived.","credit":"Photograph by Darer101, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_at_Mit_Rahina.jpg","alt":"Scattered limestone blocks and round column drums lying on bare sandy ground among tall palms and a dark evergreen tree, with low modern buildings behind."}},
{"id":"gr-479","num":479,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The first of the Athenian quota lists, cut in the archonship of Ariston, is the only dating evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which no surviving historian narrates.","questions":["Plutarch says Pericles' enemies attacked the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the assembly, complaining that Athens was gilding herself like a wanton woman with money the allies were forced to pay.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the goddess's sixtieth of the tribute was declared at Athens and published on the Acropolis, city by city, at one mina per talent."],"answer":"transfer of the treasury","answerText":"transfer of the treasury","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 454 BCE, Delos to Athens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First quota list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">454/3, under Ariston</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rate to Athena</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 mina per talent</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>transfer of the treasury</b> was the removal of the Delian League's common fund from Delos, where Thucydides says the congresses were held in the temple, to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No surviving historian narrates the move, and its date rests on a stone: the first Athenian quota list, recording the sixtieth of the allied tribute paid to the goddess in the archonship of Ariston, 454/3 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were cut on the <i>lapis primus</i>, a stele 3.8 metres (12 feet) high and the most massive of Athenian inscriptions, which carried fifteen years of them and is put together from 184 fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rate was one mina per talent and the entries run city by city: 3,000 drachmas from the Aeginetans, 1,285 from the Abderites, 300 from the Thasians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What changed was the accounting rather than the tax: the goddess's share was declared at Athens and published on the Acropolis, where those sixtieths are usually thought to have paid for the building programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch says Pericles' enemies cried out in the assembly that the people had lost its good name by removing the public money of the Greeks from Delos into its own keeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pericles had robbed it of the one seemly excuse it had, that fear of the barbarian made it take the fund from a sacred island and guard it in a stronghold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hellas was insulted, they said, when she saw her own forced contributions gilding a city that, like a wanton woman, added precious stones and costly temples to her wardrobe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pericles answered that Athens owed no account so long as she kept the barbarian off: the allies furnished not a horse, not a ship, not a hoplite but money, which belongs to those who take it rather than those who give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sums are not agreed: Diodorus, telling the story to explain a war begun to escape an audit, has 8,000 talents moved and given to Pericles to guard, then puts the fund at 10,000, where Thucydides makes its peak 9,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Athenian Tribute List, 454/3 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 259), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/259. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2012. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.38, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=38. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.40, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=40. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-2.JPG/1280px-EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-2.JPG","title":"The stone the date comes from","desc":"Part of the first stele of the Athenian tribute lists in the Epigraphic Museum, Athens, its surviving inscribed fragments set into a plaster ground. Each year from 454/3 the allied cities were entered on it with the sixtieth of their tribute paid to Athena.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-2.JPG","alt":"A tall panel of pale plaster into which dozens of irregular marble fragments have been set, most of them carrying close-ruled lines of small incised Greek capitals."}},
{"id":"gr-480","num":480,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Diodorus says the Greek cities of Asia were to be free and no Persian warship to sail inside Phaselis under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, negotiated about 449 BCE.","questions":["Plutarch reports that Callisthenes denied the King had ever made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and said he kept his distance only out of fear of the defeat at the Eurymedon.","Thucydides narrates the Cyprus campaign and Cimon's death and then passes straight to the Sacred War, never mentioning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all."],"answer":"Peace of Callias","answerText":"Peace of Callias","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Agreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 449 BCE, if at all</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Satraps kept</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 days' journey from the sea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Doubted since</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Peace of Callias</b> is the treaty said to have ended the wars between Athens and Persia, negotiated about 449 BCE by Callias son of Hipponicus, and it may never have existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus gives the terms: the Greek cities of Asia to live under laws of their own making, Persian satraps to come no nearer the sea than a three days' journey, and no Persian warship inside Phaselis or the Cyanean Rocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In return the Athenians were not to send troops into the King's territory, and Diodorus places the agreement after the Cyprus campaign in which Cimon died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch has it differently, as the fruit of the Eurymedon a generation earlier, with the King to keep from the Greek coast as far as a horse could travel in a day and not to sail west of the Cyanean and Chelidonian isles in armoured warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that Athens built an altar of Peace for it and paid distinguished honours to Callias, and that a copy of the treaty stood in its place among the decrees Craterus collected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The doubts are ancient: Plutarch reports that Callisthenes denied the King ever made such terms, and said he simply kept his distance out of fear of that defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The proof offered was that Pericles with fifty ships and Ephialtes with thirty sailed beyond the Chelidonian isles and met no Persian fleet at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The heaviest difficulty is Thucydides, who narrates the Cyprus campaign, Cimon's death and the double victory off Salamis, then passes to the Sacred War without a word of any peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus supplies the one contemporary trace, that Callias son of Hipponicus and other Athenian envoys were at Susa on some other business, which he does not describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isocrates, writing in the 4th century, boasts that the Persians launched no warship this side of Phaselis, which is a term of the treaty stated as something Athens had achieved rather than something the King had signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.4, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=4. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Cim.%2013. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.112, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=112. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.151, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=151. [Open access]","Isocrates, <i>Panegyricus</i> 118, trans. George Norlin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Isoc.%204%20118. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Phaselis_City_Harbour_4761.jpg/1280px-Phaselis_City_Harbour_4761.jpg","title":"The line the King's ships were not to cross","desc":"The city harbour of Phaselis on the Lycian coast, its ancient quay blocks awash in the shallows. Both Diodorus and Isocrates use this town as the western limit of Persian naval movement, one as a term of the treaty and the other as a fact about Athenian power.","credit":"Photograph by Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phaselis_City_Harbour_4761.jpg","alt":"A quiet green bay enclosed by a wooded headland, with lines of squared ancient stone blocks lying just above and below the waterline in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gr-481","num":481,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Pericles told the assembly that the Spartans had never sought the trials of judgment the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> provided for, nor accepted them when Athens offered.","questions":["The Corcyraeans argued that an alliance with Athens would not break the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since a city in league with neither side could lawfully join either.","Athens gave back Nisaea, Achaea, Pegae and Troezen under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which held for fourteen of its stated term before the Thebans entered Plataea."],"answer":"Thirty Years' Peace","answerText":"Thirty Years' Peace","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Agreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 446 BCE, for 30 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 years, per Thucydides</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Broken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">431 BCE, at Plataea</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Thirty Years' Peace</b> was the treaty of about 446 BCE that closed the first war between Athens and the Peloponnesians, and Thucydides says it lasted fourteen of its thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was made once Athens had recovered Euboea, and by it the Athenians gave back Nisaea, Achaea, Pegae and Troezen, the places on the mainland they held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus adds that it followed the defeat at Coronea and a general revolt, that Pericles stormed Hestiaea and removed its people, and that Callias and Chares negotiated the peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its central article, which Pericles quotes in the assembly, bound each side to give and receive trials of judgment in their disputes and meanwhile to hold what it possessed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A second article let a city in league with neither side join whichever it pleased, and that is the clause the Corcyraeans put to the Athenians when they came asking for an alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Corinthians answered that it covered only a state bringing protection rather than a war, and reminded Athens that they had not voted against her over Samos, though the Peloponnese was divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That restraint had been real: the Samian revolt fell in the sixth year of the peace, Athens put it down and set up a democracy there, and no Peloponnesian army moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What came in 432 was demands rather than arbitration — raise the siege of Potidaea, restore Aegina to her own laws, repeal the decree against Megara, and from the last embassy, give all the Greeks their liberty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pericles told the Athenians that the Lacedaemonians had never sought the trial the articles provided nor accepted it when offered, and meant to clear themselves by war rather than by words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The peace ended in its fifteenth year when Thebans entered Plataea by night while it still held, and long afterwards the Spartans judged the fault their own, for that and for refusing the trial Athens had offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.115, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=115. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.2, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=2. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.7, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=7. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.140, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=140. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.35, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=35. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.40, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=40. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 7.18, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=7:chapter=18. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Troezen_-_Acropolis_05.JPG/1280px-Troezen_-_Acropolis_05.JPG","title":"One of the four places handed back","desc":"The acropolis of Troezen above the Saronic Gulf. Thucydides lists Troezen with Nisaea, Achaea and Pegae as the mainland holdings Athens gave up when the treaty was made.","credit":"Photograph by Schuppi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troezen_-_Acropolis_05.JPG","alt":"A steep scrub-covered hill rising to a bare rocky summit, with a blue gulf and a line of mountains beyond it under a clear sky."}},
{"id":"gr-482","num":482,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Herodotus says the Athenians left four thousand settlers on the lands of the Hippobotai in 506 BCE, the earliest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the sources record.","questions":["Plutarch says Pericles used a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to lighten the city of an idle crowd, relieve the poor, and set a garrison among the allies against revolt.","Thucydides says the Histiaeans were driven from their homes and their land taken over, which is the harshest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the record of the Athenian empire."],"answer":"cleruchy","answerText":"cleruchy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First</span><span class=\"dt-v\">506 BCE, on Euboea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Periclean waves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 450 – 440 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Histiaea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">446 BCE, expelled</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"A <b>cleruchy</b> was a settlement of Athenians planted on land taken from another state, each man holding a kleros, or lot, where he stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus records the earliest: after beating the Boeotians and the Chalcidians on a single day in 506 BCE, the Athenians left four thousand kleruchs on the lands of the Hippobotai, the men of substance among the Chalcidians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those Hippobotai they took alive were kept in fetters and later ransomed at two minas a head, their chains hung in the acropolis where Herodotus still saw them, and a tenth of the ransom bought a bronze four-horse chariot with a boasting epigram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Pericles sent a thousand settlers to the Chersonese, five hundred to Naxos, half that number to Andros and a thousand into Thrace to live among the Bisaltae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The purposes he gives are three at once: to lighten the city of an idle crowd, to relieve the embarrassments of the poor, and to give the allies a neighbouring garrison that would prevent rebellion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The harshest case is Histiaea on Euboea in 446 BCE, where Thucydides says the rest of the island was settled by agreement while the Histiaeans were driven from their homes and their territory taken over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch adds why they alone were used so inexorably, that they had captured an Attic ship and killed its crew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same settlement Athens swore an oath to Chalcis, preserved on stone, not to expel the Chalcidians from their city nor lay it waste, and to disfranchise, exile, imprison, execute or fine no man without an Athenian vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Chalcidians swore in return that they would not revolt by any means or device, would denounce anyone who did, and would pay whatever tribute they could persuade the Athenians to accept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two accounts of Chalcis do not sit together, since Plutarch has Pericles banish the Hippobotai in 446, which is most probably the confiscation Herodotus dates to 506 put in the wrong place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["institution","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.77, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=77. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2011. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.114, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=114. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 23, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2023. [Open access]","Athenian Regulations for Chalkis (IG I<sup>3</sup> 40), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/40. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/The_oldest_known_decree_of_the_Athenian_assembly_508-500_BC_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-The_oldest_known_decree_of_the_Athenian_assembly_508-500_BC_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"The oldest decree of the Athenian assembly","desc":"IG I3 1 in the Epigraphic Museum, Athens, cut about 500 BCE in early Attic letters. It concerns Athenians settled on Salamis, and is read either as establishing cleruchs there or as regulating their residence.","credit":"Photograph by TimeTravelRome, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_oldest_known_decree_of_the_Athenian_assembly_508-500_BC_%28cropped%29.jpg","alt":"A tall narrow marble stele standing against a dark blue wall, its upper half made of inscribed ancient fragments set into a plain restoration, the letters cut in an early angular Greek script."}},
{"id":"gr-483","num":483,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> record only the sixtieth given to Athena, a city's actual payment has to be reached by multiplying what the stone says by sixty.","questions":["One stele of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried the first fifteen years and is put together from 184 broken pieces, parts of whose arrangement are still disputed.","Samothrace appealed against its assessment by pleading that it was small and rough, one of the workings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Thucydides never describes."],"answer":"Athenian tribute lists","answerText":"Athenian tribute lists","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First stone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">454 – 440 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second stone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">439 – 432 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rate recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 mina per talent</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Athenian tribute lists</b> are the inscriptions on which Athens published, year by year, the sixtieth part of the allies' tribute given to Athena, and they are the fullest record the empire left of itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They give that sixtieth and not the tribute, at one mina per talent, so a city's payment has to be reached by multiplying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first fifteen years stood on one stele, the <i>lapis primus</i>, and the next eight on a second, after which each year had a stone of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both are reconstructions from broken pieces, the first from 184 fragments and the second from 73, and parts of the arrangement have been questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reassessments fell in the Great Panathenaic years 450 and 446 with a special one in 443/2, and a city could appeal: Samothrace pleaded that it was small, its land high and rough with little fit for farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> No list seems to have been cut for 449/8, which may belong with an episode Antiphon recalls, when the whole board of hellenotamiai was accused of embezzlement and put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of them, Sosias, was condemned but not yet executed when it came out how the money had really gone, and the people took him from the hands of the Eleven, the rest having died innocent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The stones carry headings as well as sums, and those of 434/3 divide the entries into cities that assessed themselves for tribute, cities that individuals signed up to pay for, and one unassessed city paying five drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those headings show that a city, or a faction inside one, could take the initiative and offer to pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They also record machinery no historian describes: in 425/4 Thoudippos carried a reassessment with heralds sent to four districts, ten assessors sworn in and fined for each day of delay, and a new court of a thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["text","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Athenian Tribute List, 454/3 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 259), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/259. [Open access]","Assessment of Tribute, 425/4 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 71), trans. Stephen Lambert and P. J. Rhodes, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/71. [Open access]","Athenian Tribute List 13, 442/1 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 270), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/270. [Open access]","Antiphon, <i>On the Murder of Herodes</i> 69-70, trans. K. J. Maidment, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Antiph.%205%2069. [Open access]","Athenian Tribute List 21, 434/3 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 278), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/278. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-3.JPG/1280px-EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-3.JPG","title":"A year of the list, read off the stone","desc":"Fragments of the Athenian tribute lists in the Epigraphic Museum, Athens. A district heading is legible down the left, with the cities of that panel and their sums entered under it in early Attic letters.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-3.JPG","alt":"Irregular white marble fragments set into pale plaster, closely covered with short lines of incised Greek capitals: a heading, then city names each followed by a figure."}},
{"id":"gr-484","num":484,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Copies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have been dug up at Smyrna, Olbia, Cos, Siphnos, Aphytis and Hamaxitos, and they do not all say the same thing.","questions":["Every allied city had to cut the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a stele in its own agora at its own expense, which is asked of no other Athenian decree.","Aristophanes' joke about weights and measures for the Olophyxians is the best clue to when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was passed, since it carries no date itself."],"answer":"Coinage Decree","answerText":"Coinage Decree","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">uncertain, 450 – 410 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Copies found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 7 allied cities</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Herald's fine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 drachmas</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Coinage Decree</b> required the allies of Athens to use Athenian coins, weights and measures, and survives only in the copies the allied cities were made to cut, found at Smyrna, Olbia, Cos, Siphnos, Aphytis and Hamaxitos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only decree of the Athenian empire known to have been required in every allied city, on a stele in that city's own agora and at its own expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heralds were to carry it to the Islands, Ionia, the Hellespont and the Thraceward region on pain of a ten-thousand-drachma fine, and anyone proposing to spend the reserved silver on anything else was to be taken to the Eleven and put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same districts and the same kind of penalty run through the tribute assessment of 425/4, so this is the empire's ordinary machinery rather than a special measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The copies do not agree, each cut locally from a text copied more than once on its way from the assembly: Aphytis leaves out two clauses about reminting that stand in the Smyrna copy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Aphytis has the fullest form of the oath added to the Council's, that no one shall strike silver coinage in the cities or use coinage, weights or measures other than the Athenians'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decree carries no date and no historian mentions it, so it has been placed anywhere between the 450s and the 410s BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best clue is a joke: in the <i>Birds</i> of 414 a dealer in decrees offers Cloudcuckooland one saying its people shall use the weights, measures and decrees of the Olophyxians, and is beaten off the stage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> If that was fresh, the decree stands near the year Thucydides describes, when Athens with its treasury low replaced the tribute with a twentieth on all goods carried by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens was still policing its coin two generations later, when Nikophon's law of 375/4 set a public approver among the bankers' tables to cut through any coin with a core of bronze or lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["text","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Athenian Decree on Coinage, Weights and Measures (IG I<sup>3</sup> 1453), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/1453. [Open access]","Assessment of Tribute, 425/4 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 71), trans. Stephen Lambert and P. J. Rhodes, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/71. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Birds</i> 1035-45, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr., Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristoph.%20Birds%201040. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 7.28, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=7:chapter=28. [Open access]","Law on approvers (dokimastai) of silver coinage, 375/4 BC (AIO 819), trans. Stephen Lambert, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/AIO/819. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Athens_-_454-404_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg/1280px-Athens_-_454-404_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","title":"The coin the allies were told to use","desc":"An Athenian silver tetradrachm of the empire years, its obverse showing Athena in a crested helmet, in the State Coin Collection at Munich. It is the currency the decree required across the alliance in place of the cities' own.","credit":"Photograph by ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_-_454-404_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","alt":"A worn oval silver coin on a blue cloth mount, struck with the head of a helmeted woman in profile facing right, the helmet crest and a spray of leaves visible above her brow."}},
{"id":"gr-485","num":485,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"A philosopher serving as general, Melissus son of Ithagenes, led a sortie during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that gave his city command of its own sea for a fortnight.","questions":["A quarrel with Miletus over Priene grew into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Athens put down only after a siege that ran into its ninth month.","Elpinice told Pericles that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had cost brave citizens not against Persians but in the subversion of an allied and kindred city."],"answer":"revolt of Samos","answerText":"revolt of Samos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">440 BCE, with Byzantium</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hostages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 boys and 50 men, to Lemnos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege</span><span class=\"dt-v\">into its 9th month</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>revolt of Samos</b> in 440 BCE was the most dangerous rebellion the Athenian empire faced before the Peloponnesian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew out of a war between Samos and Miletus over Priene: the Milesians got the worst of it, complained at Athens, and were joined by Samians who wanted their own constitution changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens sent forty ships, set up a democracy on the island and carried fifty boys and fifty men off to Lemnos as hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Samian exiles then hired seven hundred soldiers, made terms with Pissuthnes the satrap at Sardis, crossed at evening to seize the popular party, stole the hostages back out of Lemnos and handed the Athenian garrison to the Persian, and Byzantium revolted with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pericles beat seventy Samian ships off Tragia with 44 of his own, and once the reinforcements came he walled the city about with a triple wall and shut it in by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> While he was away with sixty ships to meet a reported Phoenician fleet, Melissus son of Ithagenes, a philosopher then serving as general, led a sortie that sank the guard ships and left Samos master of its own sea for fourteen days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both sides branded their prisoners, the Samians marking Athenians with an owl because the Athenians had marked Samians with the samaina, the boar-prowed ship Polycrates had built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city held out into the ninth month and then gave up its walls, its warships and hostages, and agreed to repay the cost of the war by instalments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Duris of Samos wrote that Pericles had the Samian captains crucified in the market-place of Miletus and clubbed to death after ten days, which Plutarch rejects because no other writer records it and Duris was writing about his own city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elpinice told Pericles after his victory speech that it was admirable to have lost so many brave citizens, not against Phoenicians or Medes as her brother Cimon had, but in the subversion of an allied and kindred city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","greece","athens","warfare"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.115, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=115. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.116, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=116. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.117, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=117. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 26, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2026. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 28, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2028. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/2019-08-28_Pythagorion_%28KPFC%29_01.jpg/1280px-2019-08-28_Pythagorion_%28KPFC%29_01.jpg","title":"The city Pericles besieged","desc":"Pythagoreio on Samos, which stands on the site of the ancient city, seen from the hills above with the Anatolian coast beyond. Athens shut this place in with a triple wall on the land side and with ships by sea.","credit":"Photograph by KPFC, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2019-08-28_Pythagorion_%28KPFC%29_01.jpg","alt":"A small red-roofed town on a low headland beside a wide calm sea, seen from a hillside of dry grass and olive trees, with a far coastline on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gr-486","num":486,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Making allies sail to Athens for their lawsuits kept jurors in pay all year and let the people manage the cities of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without leaving home.","questions":["Pericles told the assembly that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was by now in the nature of a tyranny, unjust to have taken up and unsafe to lay down.","A decree let Chalcis try its own officials but reserved exile, death and loss of rights to a court at Athens, which is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one city."],"answer":"Athenian empire","answerText":"Athenian empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chalcis decree</span><span class=\"dt-v\">446 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Piraeus duty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 per cent on goods</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Athenian empire</b> is the rule Athens came to exercise over the cities of its alliance, and its daily working was as much legal as military.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Allies with a case against Athens or each other came there for it, and the Athenians told the Spartans that trials ran under laws equal to both sides, that judgment often went against Athens, and that they were called contentious for allowing law at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their argument was that a power able to compel need not go to law at all, and that men resent injustice from an equal more than violence from a stronger, so moderation is read as robbery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Old Oligarch puts the same practice the other way up: making the allies sail to Athens keeps the jurors in pay through the year and lets the people run the allied cities from home, protecting democrats there and ruining their opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds the smaller profits, the one per cent at Piraeus, lodgings let and animals hired, and concludes that every ally must flatter the populace and take by the hand whoever comes into court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A settlement with Chalcis shows it inside one city, where the Chalcidians were to hold their own officials to account at home just as the Athenians did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The exception was any case carrying exile, execution or loss of civic rights, which went on appeal to Athens; and the decree was to stand on the Athenian acropolis at Chalcidian expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens also settled who paid tax to whom, leaving foreigners in Chalcis to pay the Chalcidians unless they already paid Athens or held an Athenian exemption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What to call it was argued in Athens itself: Pericles told the assembly their rule was by now in the nature of a tyranny, unjust to have taken up and unsafe to lay down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cleon said the same without the regret, that it was a tyranny over men held against their will, who plotted continually and obeyed only because Athens was stronger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["institution","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.77, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=77. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.16, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=16. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.17-18, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=17. [Open access]","Athenian Regulations for Chalkis (IG I<sup>3</sup> 40), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/40. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.63, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=63. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.37, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=37. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Kleroterion-_Museum_of_the_Ancient_Agora_-_Joy_of_Museum.jpg/1280px-Kleroterion-_Museum_of_the_Ancient_Agora_-_Joy_of_Museum.jpg","title":"The machine that filled the courts","desc":"A kleroterion in the Museum of the Ancient Agora, Athens, its slots cut in columns to allot jurors by lot. These were the courts an ally with a case had to sail to Athens to reach.","credit":"Photograph by Joyofmuseums, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kleroterion-_Museum_of_the_Ancient_Agora_-_Joy_of_Museum.jpg","alt":"A broken slab of grey stone standing on a plinth, its face cut with about a dozen vertical columns of narrow horizontal slots."}},
{"id":"gr-487","num":487,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Cargoes were shown by sample at the Deigma in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a Spartan raiding party once leapt ashore there and carried merchants off to its ships.","questions":["Xenophon would have given shipowners front seats at the theatre to draw more trade to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose moorings he called the finest and safest anywhere.","Socrates says he went down to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to pray to a goddess whose festival was then being held for the first time, in the opening line of the Republic."],"answer":"Piraeus","answerText":"Piraeus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Eetioneia wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">411 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deigma raided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">388 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Piraeus</b> was the harbour town of Athens and the busiest port in the Greek world, whose moorings Xenophon calls the finest and safest anywhere, where ships could ride out bad weather at anchor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens courted the merchants who used it, and Xenophon would have offered prizes to the market officials who settled disputes quickest, so that sailings were not held up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He would also have given shipowners front seats at the theatre and entertained them publicly, so that they came for the honour as well as the profit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Business was done at the Deigma, the exchange where cargoes were shown by sample, and when a Spartan squadron raided the port in 388 BCE its men leapt ashore there and carried merchants and shipowners off to the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias found the place with a market of its own for those living by the sea, a long portico, a precinct of Athena and Zeus in bronze, and a sanctuary of Aphrodite that Conon had built after Cnidus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The entrance was commanded from Eetioneia, the pier beside the harbour mouth, where in 411 BCE the Four Hundred built a wall so placed that a few men inside it could shut the port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Theramenes said aloud what it was for, not to keep the fleet at Samos out but to let the enemy's ships and army in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beside the wall they raised a great open gallery and made all the grain already landed be brought there, and everything that came in afterwards be unloaded and sold from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The port also carried what the city did not, and Plato opens the <i>Republic</i> there, with Socrates gone down to pray to a goddess whose festival was then being held for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He thought the citizens' procession fine and the marching of the Thracian contingent no worse, a foreign rite honoured in the harbour town at its own first festival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["place","history","greece","athens","geography"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 3.1-4, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Xen.%20Ways%203.1. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 5.1.21, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Xen.%20Hell.%205.1.21. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.1.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.1.3. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 8.90, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=8:chapter=90. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 327a, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.%20Rep.%20327a. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Remains_of_the_ancient_harbour_of_Zea_in_Piraeus_on_24_February_2019.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_ancient_harbour_of_Zea_in_Piraeus_on_24_February_2019.jpg","title":"Ancient stonework at Zea","desc":"Cut blocks at the water's edge of Zea, one of the harbours of Piraeus, with the modern town standing over them. What the stones belonged to is not settled; the uploader records them as fortifications with a question mark.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_ancient_harbour_of_Zea_in_Piraeus_on_24_February_2019.jpg","alt":"Weathered squared blocks of pale stone lying in shallow green water and on the bank above it, with pines and a palm behind and modern apartment blocks rising over them."}},
{"id":"gr-488","num":488,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Cratinus joked that Pericles had for ever so long been pushing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on in words while in fact never budging the thing.","questions":["When Attica was abandoned the country people camped in temples and towers, and afterwards the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were divided among them to live along.","Some Athenians privately invited a Spartan army into the city, hoping to end the democracy and demolish the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that were then being built."],"answer":"Long Walls","answerText":"Long Walls","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 458 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">To Piraeus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 stades</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pulled down</span><span class=\"dt-v\">404 BCE, to the flutes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Long Walls</b> were the fortifications the Athenians began about the middle of the 5th century BCE to join their city to the sea, one running down to the haven of Phaleron and the other to Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were a party question from the start, for while a Spartan army sat in Boeotia some Athenians privately invited it in, hoping to put down the democracy and pull down the walls then building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third wall was added later beside the one to Piraeus, and Plutarch names Callicrates as its contractor and reports that Socrates had heard Pericles propose the measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The comic poet Cratinus mocked how slowly it went, saying that Pericles had for ever so long been pushing the thing on in words while in fact he did not budge it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides gives the measurements Pericles laid before the assembly: the Phaleric wall 35 stades, the walls down to Piraeus 40, and the whole circuit of Piraeus with Munychia 60.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He records which stretches were manned as well, a watch on the outermost of the long walls only, and on half the circuit of Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the war came and Attica was given up to the enemy, the country people crowded into a city that had no room for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They camped in the empty places and the temples and the shrines of the heroes, in the turrets of the walls, and even in the Pelasgicum, which an oracle had said was best left empty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Afterwards the Long Walls themselves were divided among them, and they lived along them and through most of Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The surrender of 404 BCE took the walls down, and Xenophon says the Peloponnesians fell to the work with enthusiasm to the music of flute-girls, thinking that day the beginning of freedom for Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["building","history","greece","athens","warfare"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.107, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=107. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13.5, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013.5. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.17, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=17. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 2.2.23, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Xen.%20Hell.%202.2.23. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Remains_of_two_towers_from_the_Walls_of_Conon_%28Akti_Themistokleous%2C_Piraeus%29.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_two_towers_from_the_Walls_of_Conon_%28Akti_Themistokleous%2C_Piraeus%29.jpg","title":"What was built back afterwards","desc":"Towers of the Walls of Conon on the shore at Piraeus. These belong to the rebuilding of the early 4th century BCE, after the walls the card describes had been pulled down.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC0 1.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_two_towers_from_the_Walls_of_Conon_%28Akti_Themistokleous%2C_Piraeus%29.jpg","alt":"Two low squared-stone tower stubs standing on a shelf of bare rock above the sea, with a modern seafront wall and apartment block behind and hazy hills across the water."}},
{"id":"gr-489","num":489,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle credits <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with inventing the division of cities into blocks and says he cut up Piraeus, while noting his long hair and fussy reputation.","questions":["The city planned by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held ten thousand people in three classes and divided its land into sacred, common and private portions.","Straight streets were the modern fashion introduced by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Aristotle thought pleasanter but less safe than the tangled plan of older towns."],"answer":"Hippodamus of Miletus","answerText":"Hippodamus of Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">His city</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 people in 3 classes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lawsuit kinds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3: outrage, damage, homicide</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Hippodamus of Miletus</b>, son of Euryphon, is the man Aristotle credits with inventing the division of cities into blocks, and with cutting up Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle thought him an oddity too, wearing his hair long and expensive ornaments with it, the same cheap but warm clothes in summer as in winter, and wanting to be thought learned about nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was, on the same account, the first man outside politics to try to say what the best constitution would be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His city held ten thousand people in three classes, artisans, farmers and the armed men who fought for it, on land divided three ways: sacred to pay for offerings, common to feed the soldiers, private for the farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He held that lawsuits were of only three kinds, outrage, damage and homicide, and would have set one supreme court of chosen elders to hear the cases thought to have been decided wrongly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He would also have had each juror write a verdict on a tablet rather than drop a ballot, so that one who thought the accused guilty on some counts and not others need not perjure himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He proposed honours for anyone who found out something useful to the city, and public maintenance for the children of men killed in war, which Aristotle notes was already law at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On streets Aristotle is divided: houses laid out straight, in the modern fashion Hippodamus introduced, are pleasanter and handier, while the tangled plan of old towns is safer, being hard for a foreign army to find its way about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own answer was to lay out only parts of a city straight and the rest on the slant, as some farmers plant vines, and so have both beauty and security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later writers gave him more than Aristotle does: Strabo says Rhodes was founded by the same architect as Piraeus, adding ‘as they say', and Diodorus describes Thurii cut lengthwise by four named streets and across by three without naming him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","history","greece","architecture","politics"],"sources":["Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1267b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%202.1267b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1268a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%202.1268a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 7.1330b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%207.1330b. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.2.9, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0198:book=14:chapter=2:section=9. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.10, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=10. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-490","num":490,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The state paid each man of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crew a drachma a day, and the trierarchs added more of their own for the thranites of the uppermost bank.","questions":["Herodotus reckons two hundred men to a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them rowers, an art the Old Oligarch says Athenians picked up almost without noticing.","The crew of one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ate barley cakes kneaded with oil and wine at the oar and slept by turns, and reached Mytilene as the sentence was being read."],"answer":"trireme","answerText":"trireme","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE, at Corinth</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 200 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pay</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 drachma a day</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>trireme</b> was the oared warship on which Athenian power rested, a galley rowed at three levels whose uppermost bank of rowers were called thranites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides says the Corinthians were the first in Greece to build ships in the form then in use, and that Ameinocles the Corinthian shipwright made four for the Samians about three hundred years before the end of his war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He puts the oldest sea battle he knows of, between the Corinthians and the Corcyraeans, 260 years before that same point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A ship was reckoned at two hundred men, the figure Herodotus uses for the vessels of the Persian fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of them pulled an oar, and the Old Oligarch says the Athenians came by the skill almost without noticing, since a man often at sea takes up an oar, and so does his slave, and both learn the language of ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The state paid each man of a crew a drachma a day, and the trierarchs added something of their own for the thranites and for their servants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They spent on the ships as well, on badges and rigging, each trying to have the handsomest and the swiftest galley in the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What a rower carried was little enough to walk to another sea with: when the Peloponnesians planned a raid on Piraeus, every man was to take his oar, his cushion and the thong his oar turned in, and cross from Corinth on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The best measure of what the ship could do is the second galley sent to Mytilene, whose crew were promised a reward and rowed on barley cakes kneaded with oil and wine, eating at the oar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They slept and rowed by turns, no wind blew against them, and they came in just as the sentence was being read out, so that the city was saved by the margin of that one crossing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["object","history","greece","warfare","technology"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=13. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.184, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=184. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.19, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=19. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 6.31, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=6:chapter=31. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.93, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=93. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.49, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=49. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/ACMA_Relief_Lenormant.jpg/1280px-ACMA_Relief_Lenormant.jpg","title":"The Lenormant relief","desc":"A fragment of a marble relief in the Acropolis Museum, Athens, showing nine rowers of an Athenian trireme. The heads and arms of the upper bank stand above the outrigger, with the oars of the lower banks running out beneath them.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACMA_Relief_Lenormant.jpg","alt":"A broken block of pale marble carved in low relief with a row of seated rowers seen from the side, their oars sloping down in close ranks below them."}},
{"id":"gr-491","num":491,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Apollodorus says he mortgaged his property to man his ship first, hiring the best rowers with bonuses, which is what one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could cost the man who bore it.","questions":["The state gave the hull and a drachma a day to each man, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> laid the rest of the cost on one rich citizen for a year.","Demosthenes accuses two men of hiring out their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to whoever would take it cheapest and then asking for the crown."],"answer":"trierarchy","answerText":"trierarchy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sicilian fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">415 BCE, trierarchs vying</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Term</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 year per ship</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One man's bill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 talents over 7 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>trierarchy</b> was the duty laid on a rich Athenian to command a warship for a year and keep her in service largely at his own charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Old Oligarch puts the arithmetic of it plainly: the wealthy fit out the triremes, and the people are carried in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state provided the hull and paid each man of the crew a drachma a day, and the trierarch found the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the fleet that sailed for Sicily the trierarchs added something of their own for the thranites and their servants, and spent besides on badges and rigging, each trying to have the handsomest and swiftest ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An Athenian law of the last years of the war shows the duty as machinery: a trierarch who did not receive the rigging or the wooden gear from his predecessor could summon him before the superintendents on two witnesses, and the case went to court the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Apollodorus describes what bearing it took: the sailors his demesmen had listed did not appear except a very few, and those incompetent, so he dismissed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He mortgaged his property and borrowed money to be the first to man his ship, hiring the best sailors he could get with large bonuses and advance payments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He furnished her wholly from his own stores, taking nothing from the public ones, and made everything as magnificent as possible, outdoing all the other trierarchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What that came to is stated by a speaker in Lysias, who says that for seven years he equipped warships at a cost of six talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not everyone served in person, and Demosthenes says of two men that they sought out whoever would take their trierarchy on the lowest terms and let the service to him, and then asked for the crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["institution","history","greece","athens","warfare"],"sources":["[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.13, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 6.31, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=6:chapter=31. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Polycles</i> 7, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Dem.%2050%207. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Defence Against a Charge of Taking Bribes</i> 2, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Lys.%2021%202. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>On the Trierarchic Crown</i> 7, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Dem.%2051%207. [Open access]","Law about the Trierarchy, 410-404 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 236a), trans. Stephen Lambert, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/236a. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-492","num":492,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A speaker in Lysias lists thirty minae for a tragic chorus and five thousand drachmae for a chorus at the Dionysia, each of them one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> he had borne.","questions":["Aristotle says the archon appointed the three wealthiest men in Athens to lead the tragic choruses and heard claims that a second <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had fallen too soon after the first.","A man who thought a richer citizen should bear a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his place could offer to exchange estates with him, a procedure a speaker in Demosthenes credits to Solon."],"answer":"liturgy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tragic chorus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 minae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dionysia chorus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,000 drachmae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Promethea games</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 minae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Choregic stone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 440 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>liturgy</b> was a duty laid on a rich Athenian to pay for something the city wanted out of his own purse, and to be seen paying. Aristotle's <i>Athenian Constitution</i> shows the burden being placed: the archon appointed three chorus-leaders for the tragedies, the wealthiest men in Athens, and received those the tribes returned for the other competitions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He heard their pleas of exemption too, from having done the service before, from one not yet expired, or from age, a chorus-leader for the boys having to be over forty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A man who thought a richer citizen should carry it instead could offer to exchange estates with him, and a speaker in Demosthenes blesses Solon for laying down the order of that procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The load fell unevenly: Demosthenes says some services fell on resident aliens and others on citizens, but that from war levies and warship duty nobody was exempt, not even the descendants of Harmodius and Aristogiton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> What one cost is itemised by a speaker in Lysias: thirty minae on a tragic chorus, two thousand drachmae at the Thargelia, eight hundred on pyrrhic dancers at the Great Panathenaea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then five thousand drachmae on a men's chorus at the Dionysia, the tripod included, three hundred on a cyclic chorus at the Little Panathenaea,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and twelve minae on games at the Promethea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds fifteen minae for winning the warship race at Sunium and more than thirty on sacred missions and processions, and says that by the letter of the law he need not have spent a quarter of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The work was as real as the money: Antiphon's choregos fitted a training-room in the best part of his house and recruited his chorus without indicting a fine or extorting a pledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the outlay bought stands on a stone from Anagyrous, where Sokrates records his dedication, names Euripides as director, and lists fourteen members of the tragic chorus beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"liturgy","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","greece","athens","politics","religion"],"sources":["Lysias, <i>Defence Against a Charge of Taking Bribes</i> 1–2, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Lys.%2021%201. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Defence Against a Charge of Taking Bribes</i> 3–5, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Lys.%2021%203. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 56, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=56. [Open access]","Antiphon, <i>On the Chorus Boy</i> 11–13, trans. K. J. Maidment, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Antiph.%206%2011. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Phaenippus</i> 1, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Dem.%2042%201. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Leptines</i> 18, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Dem.%2020%2018. [Open access]","Choregic dedication from Anagyrous, after ca. 440 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 969), trans. Irene Vagionakis and Claudio Huayna Avila, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/969. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/20100410_athina153.JPG/1920px-20100410_athina153.JPG","title":"The Choragic Monument of Lysicrates","desc":"The monument raised in Athens in 335/4 BCE to carry the bronze tripod Lysicrates won as sponsor of the victorious boys' chorus at the Dionysia; the prize tripod stood on the acanthus finial at the top.","credit":"Photograph by Jean Housen, 2010, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20100410_athina153.JPG","alt":"A small round marble monument with engaged columns and a carved finial, standing on a square stone base in a sunlit square with neoclassical houses behind."}},
{"id":"gr-493","num":493,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Aristotle says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the naval multitude that won at Salamis and gave Athens her power at sea, made the democracy stronger than Solon had ever designed.","questions":["The Old Oligarch grants the poor their say because they man the ships, which is the argument behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Aristotle would rather have kept out of the citizen body.","At Samos the fleet swore itself to democracy against the Four Hundred, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could do once Athens had put her power on the water."],"answer":"nautikos ochlos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fed by tribute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dock guardians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Guard ships</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20, and 200 more</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>nautikos ochlos</b>, the naval crowd, is Aristotle's name for the poor Athenians who rowed the fleet and for the political weight rowing gave them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His <i>Politics</i> puts it in a clause: the naval multitude, cause of the victory off Salamis and thereby of the leadership of Athens by sea, made the democracy stronger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He treats that as accident rather than design: the people, having caused the naval victories of the Persian invasion, grew proud and took bad men as leaders, where Solon had given the fourth class no office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Old Oligarch says the same from the other side and without apology, that the poor are right to have more than the highborn because it is they who man the ships and give the city its strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He names the steersmen, boatswains, sub-boatswains, look-outs and shipwrights, and says these strengthen the city far more than the hoplites do, so that everyone should share the offices and speak his mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The empire paid for it: the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the tributes, the taxes and the allies together fed more than twenty thousand men, including six thousand jurymen and five hundred guardians of the docks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same list counts twenty guard-ships and two hundred more ships whose crews were chosen by lot, all maintained from public funds beside the archers, cavalry and council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aristotle drew the opposite conclusion from the same facts, writing that a state need not include the teeming population growing up around common sailors, since there is no need for these to be citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What that could come to was shown at Samos, where Thrasybulus and Thrasyllus bound the whole force by the most tremendous oaths to accept democracy and be enemies of the Four Hundred at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The soldiers deposed the generals they suspected and chose others, telling one another that they had the whole fleet and a city in Samos, and had lost nothing in losing men who could send them no money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"nautikos ochlos","difficulty":5,"tags":["concept","history","greece","athens","politics","warfare"],"sources":["[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.2, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=2. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 5.1304a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%205.1304a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Aristotelis Politica</i> 5.1304a, ed. W. D. Ross (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1957), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0057:book=5:section=1304a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%202.1274a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 24, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Ath.%20Pol.%2024. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 7.1327b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%207.1327b. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 8.75, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=8:chapter=75. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 8.76, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=8:chapter=76. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gr-494","num":494,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch says the works of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were each thought to need many generations and were all finished inside a single administration, which is what made them a wonder.","questions":["Pericles offered to pay for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> himself and put his own name on the dedications, at which the assembly told him instead to spare no public expense.","Accounts cut on stone in 434 BCE record the receipts and wages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, set against payments to the workers at Pentele who also placed the stones."],"answer":"Periclean building programme","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Accounts cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">434 BCE, at 25,000 dr.</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Propylaea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built in 5 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Acropolis grant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 talents a year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Periclean building programme</b> is the work Athens put up on the Acropolis and elsewhere in the middle of the 5th century BCE. Plutarch gives his argument: once the city was equipped for the war she should apply her abundance to works that would bring everlasting glory when finished and, in progress, bring the whole city under pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The point was to reach the men who did not sail or serve, so that the unwarlike throng of labourers should be neither shut out of the public receipts nor paid for idleness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lists the materials, stone and bronze and ivory and gold and ebony and cypress, and the trades: carpenter, moulder, bronze-smith, stone-cutter, dyer, painter, embroiderer, embosser.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides son of Melesias said he was playing fast and loose with the public money, so Pericles offered to pay for the works himself and inscribe his own name on them, at which the assembly bade him spare no expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What Plutarch found most wonderful was the speed, since each work was thought to need many generations and all were finished within a single administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Phidias was general overseer and the works had architects of their own: Callicrates and Ictinus for the Parthenon, Coroebus then Metagenes then Xenocles at Eleusis, Mnesicles for the Propylaea, finished in five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Odeon, roofed in a circular slope from one peak, was said to reproduce the Great King's pavilion,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Pausanias found the gateway's marble roof still unrivalled for the beauty and size of its stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The money was decreed rather than merely spent: the decrees Callias proposed order ten talents a year on the Acropolis, leave the plan to the architect of the Propylaea, and forbid Athena's other funds to be drawn on beyond ten thousand drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The accounts were published yearly: the Parthenon's for 434 BCE set receipts of 25,000 drachmas and gold staters of Lampsacus and Cyzicus against purchases, wages, and payments to the workers at Pentele who placed the stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Periclean building programme","difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","history","greece","athens","architecture","politics"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2012. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2014%201. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.22.4, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.22.4. [Open access]","Decrees relating to the treasury of the Other Gods (Kallias' decrees), 434/3 BC (OR 144), trans. Stephen Lambert and P. J. Rhodes, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/OR/144. [Open access]","Building accounts of the Parthenon, 434/3 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 449), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/449. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/2016-08-03_Greece%2C_Athens%2C_Acropolis%2C_Propylaea_DSC_9901_DxO_1.jpg/1920px-2016-08-03_Greece%2C_Athens%2C_Acropolis%2C_Propylaea_DSC_9901_DxO_1.jpg","title":"The Propylaea, and the temple beside it","desc":"Mnesicles' gateway to the Acropolis on the left, brought to completion in five years, with the small temple of Athena Nike on its bastion to the right.","credit":"Photograph by Laurens R. Krol, 2016, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2016-08-03_Greece,_Athens,_Acropolis,_Propylaea_DSC_9901_DxO_1.jpg","alt":"Marble columns and steps of a ruined gateway in warm evening light, with a small colonnaded temple standing on a stone bastion beyond it."}},
{"id":"gr-495","num":495,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Athenians' own inventories call the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the hundred-foot temple and hand over its gold dishes and silver bowls from one board of treasurers to the next each Panathenaia.","questions":["Vitruvius says Ictinus and Carpion published a book on the Doric temple of Minerva at Athens, which is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its own architects set out its proportions.","Venetian bombardment in 1687 fired the gunpowder stored inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had already served as a temple, a church and a mosque in turn."],"answer":"Parthenon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">447 – 438 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pediments done</span><span class=\"dt-v\">432 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Columns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 by 17</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wrecked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1687 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Parthenon</b> is the Doric temple of Athena on the Athenian acropolis, and the first building the city dedicated to her after the decision of 448 BCE to rebuild the sanctuaries the Persians had destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is peripteral, with eight columns at the narrow ends and seventeen along the flanks, and it stands on the site of an earlier temple begun between 490 and 480 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias, who called it the temple they name the Parthenon, found the birth of Athena worked in one gable and her contest with Poseidon for the land in the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The architects are given as Ictinus and Callicrates with Phidias in general charge,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and Pausanias says elsewhere that Ictinus was a contemporary of Pericles who also built the temple at Phigalia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vitruvius reports that Ictinus and Carpion published a book on the Doric temple of Minerva on the acropolis of Athens, so the men who raised it set out its proportions themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch calls its cella a hundred feet long,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the city's own inventories use that name, handing the contents of the <i>hekatompedon</i> temple from one board of treasurers to the next at each Panathenaia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What they list is treasure rather than furniture: three gold dishes weighing 2,544 drachmas, a gold maiden on a stele that nobody weighed, and a silver incense-burner of a thousand drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The front porch was inventoried separately, with 121 silver libation bowls at two talents and 432 drachmas, three silver drinking horns, and a golden bowl for lustrations left unweighed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The building carries a set of refinements meant to lighten the Doric order: the curve of the crepis and the stylobate, the entasis of the columns, tighter corner intercolumniations, and metopes narrowing towards the corners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stood whole for centuries, took serious fire damage in late Roman times, served as a church and then as a mosque, and was wrecked in 1687 CE when a Venetian bombardment fired the gunpowder stored inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Parthenon","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","history","greece","athens","architecture","religion"],"sources":["“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.24.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.24.5. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.41.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%208.41.9. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 7, preface 12, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013. [Open access]","Inventory of the Hekatompedon, 418/7 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 329), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/329. [Open access]","Inventory of the Pronaos, 426/5-423/2 BC and 414/3-411/0 BC (AIUK 4.4 no. 3), trans. Peter Liddel and Polly Low, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/AIUK44/3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/1010_Parthenon_of_the_Acropolis_of_Athens_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-1010_Parthenon_of_the_Acropolis_of_Athens_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg","title":"The Parthenon from one corner","desc":"The temple seen at an angle, so that the columns of the short end and the ring running down the long flank are both in view; the frame at the left is restoration work.","credit":"Photograph by Giles Laurent, 2025, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1010_Parthenon_of_the_Acropolis_of_Athens_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg","alt":"A roofless marble temple on a rock, its fluted columns standing in two rows meeting at a corner under a grey sky, with fallen blocks in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gr-496","num":496,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"An inscription under the feet of the Zeus at Olympia read that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, son of Charmides, an Athenian, made me, which is the only signature Pausanias records there.","questions":["Plutarch says an assistant named Menon was put up to accuse <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of embezzlement, which failed because the statue's gold could all be taken off and weighed.","The bronze Athena that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made from the Marathon tithe carried a spear-point and helmet-crest that sailors could see once they had passed Sunium."],"answer":"Phidias","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Acropolis art</span><span class=\"dt-v\">447 – 432 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Phidias</b> was the Athenian sculptor who had charge of the art of the Periclean acropolis<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> and made the gold and ivory Zeus at Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch says he was contractor for the great statue at Athens, is inscribed on the tablet as the workman who made it, and had almost everything under his superintendence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias saw a bronze Athena of his there, a tithe from the Persians who landed at Marathon, its spear-point and helmet-crest visible to men sailing to Athens once they had passed Sunium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same passage calls the Lemnian Athena the work of his best worth seeing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Olympia temple and image alike were made from spoils taken when Pisa was crushed by the Eleans, and an inscription under the feet of Zeus read: Phidias, son of Charmides, an Athenian, made me.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The god sat on a throne in gold and ivory, a garland copying olive shoots on his head, a Victory of ivory and gold in his right hand and a sceptre in his left with an eagle on it, his sandals and robe of gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias would not praise the men who had measured it, their records falling far short of the impression it made, and reports that the sculptor prayed for a sign and a thunderbolt fell where a bronze jar still covered the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Outside the sacred grove he was shown the building called the workshop, where the image had been wrought piece by piece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Athens it ended badly: an assistant named Menon was put up to accuse him, and embezzlement could not be proved, the gold having been cast about the statue on Pericles' advice so that it could all be taken off and weighed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> What told against him was the shield, where he had carved a bald old man lifting a stone who suggested himself, and a likeness of Pericles fighting an Amazon; he was led to prison and died there of sickness, or of poison as some say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phidias","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","greece","athens","art","religion"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.28.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.28.2. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.10.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.10.2. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.11.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.11.1. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.11.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.11.9. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.15.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.15.1. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 31, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2031%202. [Open access]","“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/View_of_the_Workshop_of_Pheidias_in_Ancient_Olympia_on_October_14%2C_2020.jpg/1920px-View_of_the_Workshop_of_Pheidias_in_Ancient_Olympia_on_October_14%2C_2020.jpg","title":"The workshop at Olympia","desc":"The site identified as the building Pausanias was shown as Phidias' workshop; the standing brick walls are those of an Early Christian basilica later raised within it, and the stone bases in front are ancient.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, 2020, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Workshop_of_Pheidias_in_Ancient_Olympia_on_October_14,_2020.jpg","alt":"Two tall walls of ancient brick standing in a sunlit grove, with rows of squared stone blocks and column bases laid out on the ground before them."}},
{"id":"gr-497","num":497,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Pericles told the assembly that the forty talents of pure gold on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were all removable, a reserve the city might spend on itself and afterwards restore.","questions":["Pausanias saw the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> standing in a tunic to the feet, a Victory four cubits high in one hand and the birth of Pandora carved on its pedestal.","The final account for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is cut in letters nearly three times the ordinary height, so that gods and men alike should read what it had cost."],"answer":"Athena Parthenos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Installed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">438 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold on it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 – 50 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Total cost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700 – 1,000 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nike held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 cubits high</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Athena Parthenos</b> was the colossal gold and ivory statue of Athena that stood inside the Parthenon, made by Phidias and apparently installed in 438 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Athenians accounted for it apart from the temple, under a board of overseers of its own, and the surviving fragments record gold bought by weight: six talents, 1,618 drachmas and one obol in a single entry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The price paid shows silver exchanged for that gold at fourteen to one, the same ratio the accounts give whenever surplus gold was sold off again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The final summary account of 438/7 BCE is cut in extraordinary letters, the totals nearly three times the height of the ordinary script on a carefully polished surface, and was plainly meant to state to gods and men what the thing had cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Enough of that total survives to show it lay between 700 and 1,000 talents, against a weight of gold given as 44 talents by Philochorus, rounded to 40 by Thucydides and put at 50 by Diodorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias saw the statue standing upright in a tunic reaching to the feet, with the head of Medusa worked in ivory on the breast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She held a Victory about four cubits high in one hand and a spear in the other, a shield lay at her feet and a serpent near the spear, which he took for Erichthonius, and the birth of Pandora was in relief on the pedestal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the middle of her helmet was set a likeness of the Sphinx, and on either side of it griffins in relief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city's treasurers weighed what she carried, an inventory of the hundred-foot temple listing a gold crown which Nike has at sixty drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pericles told the assembly that the gold on the goddess was all removable, a reserve the city might spend on its own preservation provided every penny of it was afterwards restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Athena Parthenos","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","history","greece","athens","art","religion"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.24.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.24.5. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.24.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.24.7. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]","Accounts of Pheidias' statue of Athena, 445-438 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 458), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/458. [Open access]","Accounts of Pheidias' statue of Athena, final summary account, 438/7 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 460), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/460. [Open access]","Inventory of the Hekatompedon, 418/7 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 329), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/329. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Varvakeion_Athena.jpg/1280px-Varvakeion_Athena.jpg","title":"The Varvakeion Athena","desc":"A small Roman marble statuette in Athens, the closest surviving reflection of the lost statue: the helmet with its sphinx and griffins, the aegis and Gorgon head, the Victory on a column and the shield with the serpent coiled inside it.","credit":"Photograph by Nikos Kitsakis, 2024, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varvakeion_Athena.jpg","alt":"A marble statuette of a helmeted goddess in a long tunic, a small winged figure on a column in her right hand and a round shield with a coiled snake at her left side."}},
{"id":"gr-498","num":498,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A band of 115 blocks running 160 metres (525 feet) round the top of the cella wall, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries some 378 figures and at least 220 animals.","questions":["The ancient sources that mention the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are all later than the building and disagree with it at points, so what moment it shows is still argued.","Above the east door the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows the peplos being handed over between the seated Olympian gods, which is where both halves of the procession arrive."],"answer":"Parthenon frieze","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Blocks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">115, running 160 m (525 feet)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.02 m (3 feet 4 inches)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sculpture done</span><span class=\"dt-v\">432 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elgin took</span><span class=\"dt-v\">56 blocks</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Parthenon frieze</b> is the continuous band of relief that ran round the top of the cella wall inside the outer colonnade, an Ionic frieze on a Doric building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was 115 blocks long, about 160 metres (525 feet) in all and 1.02 metres (3 feet 4 inches) high,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the temple's sculpture was finished six years after the building itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Some 378 human and divine figures move across it with at least 220 animals, most of them horses, whose weapons and bridle attachments were once applied in bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The procession begins at the south-west corner and divides into two files, one along the south side and one across the west end and up the north, both ending above the door at the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is shown is taken to be the procession of the Great Panathenaia, held every fourth year in the month of Hekatombaion, whose ceremonies reached their height on the day held to be Athena's birthday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The east end is nine blocks, longer than those of the other sides, and has a symmetry the rest lacks, being where both files arrive and where the peplos is handed over between the seated Olympian gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That robe was a public commission: ten Stewards of the Games, chosen by lot and holding office for four years, administered the procession and the contests and had the Robe made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An Athenian decree of 108 BCE honours the maidens who worked the wool for it, who had walked in the procession as prescribed and dedicated a silver bowl worth a hundred drachmas out of their own means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The reading is not secure: the ancient sources that mention the frieze are all later than the building, and the carving leaves out the Panathenaic ship on whose mast the peplos was raised like a sail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is scattered, some 50 metres (164 feet) of it in Athens and 80 metres (262 feet) in London, one block in Paris and fragments in five other museums,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> after Lord Elgin took 56 blocks away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Parthenon frieze","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","art","history","greece","athens","religion"],"sources":["“About the frieze,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/about-the-frieze/. [Open access]","“East frieze,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/explore-the-frieze/east-frieze/. [Open access]","“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 60, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Ath.%20Pol.%2060. [Open access]","Honours for the girls who worked on the robe for Athena, 108/7 BC (AIUK 1 no. 1), trans. Stephen Lambert, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/AIUK1/1. [Open access]","“West frieze,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/explore-the-frieze/west-frieze/. [Open access]","“The Parthenon,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/the-parthenon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Peplos_scene_BM_EastV_cropped.JPG/1920px-Peplos_scene_BM_EastV_cropped.JPG","title":"The peplos scene","desc":"Block V of the east frieze, the central scene above the door: two maidens carrying stools on their heads, a woman turned towards them, and a man folding a cloth with a child.","credit":"Photograph by Twospoonfuls, 2008, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peplos_scene_BM_EastV_cropped.JPG","alt":"A weathered marble relief panel of five draped figures in low relief, two of them balancing flat objects on their heads and two handling a large folded cloth."}},
{"id":"gr-499","num":499,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"All 92 panels of this temple's Doric frieze carry sculpture, which is unique in Greek architecture, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the first of its carving to be cut.","questions":["Gigantomachy east, Amazonomachy west, the sack of Troy north and Centauromachy south: the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> give four versions of one subject, which is a contest between two adversaries.","In Athens the surviving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hang in pairs between steel columns as many as the temple's own, the original marble mixed with casts of the 15 in London."],"answer":"Parthenon metopes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Panels</span><span class=\"dt-v\">92, every one carved</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 447 BCE, first of the sculpture</span><span class=\"dt-k\">South side</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 Centauromachy, 9 others</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elgin took</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 metopes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Parthenon metopes</b> are the 92 square panels of the Doric frieze that ran round the whole building between the architrave and the cornice, every one of them carved, an arrangement unique in Greek architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were the first of the temple's sculpture to be made, cut on the ground by separate teams of sculptors and stone-cutters, among them resident aliens who had come to Athens from the Aegean islands, which is why the styles differ a little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relief stands so high that seen level with them the triglyphs between would hardly show and the carving would read as one continuous band.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The subjects are set by side: the Gigantomachy on the east front, the Amazonomachy at the west, the sack of Troy along the north and the Centauromachy on the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> All four are the same subject in different dress, a contest between two adversaries, and all four are known from the sculpture of many other Greek temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The stories were old: Homer has a suitor warn a beggar off the wine with the tale of the centaur Eurytion, whom it made foolish in the hall of Peirithous when he went to the Lapiths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The heroes leapt up and dragged him out through the gateway with his ears and nostrils shorn off, and from that, Homer says, the feud arose between the centaurs and mankind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the giants, Apollodorus has Earth bring them forth in her anger over the Titans, matchless in bulk and terrible to look at, with the scales of dragons for feet, darting rocks and burning oaks at the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The south side does not keep to one story: 23 of its metopes are given to the Centauromachy and the nine in the middle to some other subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifteen were carried off to London by Lord Elgin,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and in Athens the surviving marble hangs in pairs between steel columns as many as the temple's own, mixed with casts of what is in foreign museums.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Parthenon metopes","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","art","history","greece","athens","religion"],"sources":["“The Parthenon,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/the-parthenon/. [Open access]","“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Odyssey</i> 21.293-304, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0136:book=21:card=288. [Open access]","Apollodorus, <i>Library</i> 1.6.1, trans. James George Frazer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Apollod.%201.6.1. [Open access]","“The Parthenon Gallery,” Acropolis Museum, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/exhibit-halls/parthenon-gallery. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/South_metope_1_from_the_Parthenon_at_the_Acropolis_Museum_in_Athens.jpg/1920px-South_metope_1_from_the_Parthenon_at_the_Acropolis_Museum_in_Athens.jpg","title":"South metope 1, in Athens","desc":"A Lapith and a centaur locked together, carved so deep that the figures stand almost clear of the panel behind them; the heads are lost, as on most of the metopes.","credit":"Photograph by Nikos Kitsakis, 2022, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_metope_1_from_the_Parthenon_at_the_Acropolis_Museum_in_Athens.jpg","alt":"A square marble panel in a museum, carved in very deep relief with a naked man and a rearing horse-bodied centaur struggling against each other, both headless."}},
{"id":"gr-500","num":500,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch names <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the architects of the Parthenon, working under Phidias, who is said to have had almost everything on the Acropolis under his superintendence.","questions":["An Athenian decree orders a temple and stone altar for the goddess of Victory built however Callicrates may specify, which is how one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is documented rather than described.","Vitruvius has one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> writing a book on the Doric temple of Minerva at Athens and roofing the vast cella of Ceres at Eleusis."],"answer":"Ictinus and Callicrates","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Parthenon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">447 – 438 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phigalia temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st after Tegea, per Pausanias</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nike temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 men to specify it with him</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ictinus and Callicrates</b> are the two architects Plutarch names for the Parthenon,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> working under Phidias, who had general charge of the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Almost everything else known of Ictinus is a book and two more temples, and of Callicrates a decree and a wall. Vitruvius lists him with Carpion among architects who published on their own buildings, theirs being the Doric temple of Minerva on the acropolis of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same passage says the cella of Ceres and Proserpine at Eleusis, of vast size, was carried up to the roof by Ictinus in the Doric style, without exterior columns and with plenty of room for the customary sacrifices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias found a third building of his in Arcadia: on Mount Cotilius above Phigalia, at a place called Bassae, the temple of Apollo the Helper, which including its roof is of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Of the temples in the Peloponnese, he says, this might be placed first after the one at Tegea for the beauty of its stone and for its symmetry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He names its architect as Ictinus, a contemporary of Pericles who built for the Athenians what is called the Parthenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Callicrates is documented rather than described: an Athenian decree, which also installs a priestess for Athena Nike to be allotted from all Athenian women, orders that the sanctuary be given gates in whatever way Callicrates may specify.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It orders further that a temple be built in whatever way he may specify, with a stone altar, and that three men chosen from the council draw up the specifications with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Plutarch adds that he was the contractor for one of the walls running down to the sea, so what survives of either man is a name attached to commissions rather than any account of a life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ictinus and Callicrates","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","greece","athens","architecture","art"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 7, preface 12 and 16, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D7. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.41.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%208.41.7. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.41.8, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%208.41.8. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.41.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%208.41.9. [Open access]","Decree about priestess and temple of Athena Nike, c. 450 or c. 438 BC (AIO 314), trans. Stephen Lambert, Josine Blok and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/AIO/314. [Open access]","“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/1972_bassai_temple.jpg/1920px-1972_bassai_temple.jpg","title":"Bassae, photographed in 1972","desc":"The temple of Apollo the Helper on Mount Cotilius, which Pausanias attributes to Ictinus, seen before the conservation work of the 1980s; a tent has covered it since.","credit":"Photograph by Tuh de, 1972, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1972_bassai_temple.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a Doric temple standing almost complete on a bare hillside, its colonnades roofless, with mountains behind."}},
{"id":"bio-001","num":1,"category":"What biology is","question":"The word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covers work at scales from a single protein to a whole ocean, and what holds all of it together as one subject is common descent.","questions":["Order, response, reproduction, growth, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution are the eight properties textbooks list to mark out what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes as its subject matter.","About 86 per cent of species were still undescribed on one 2011 estimate, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a science whose own subject matter has mostly not yet been catalogued."],"answer":"biology","answerText":"biology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","evolution"],"abstract":"<b>Biology</b> is the study of life, and its scope is unusually wide even among the sciences, reaching from a single molecule inside one cell to whole ecosystems and the living planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No one worker covers that range, so it is divided into fields defined by the scale or the group they work on, among them cell biology, physiology, botany, zoology, microbiology, ecology and palaeontology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they all study is picked out less by a definition than by a list of shared properties: order, response to the environment, reproduction, growth and development, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those properties appear along a hierarchy running from atoms and molecules through organelles, cells, tissues, organs and organisms to populations, communities, ecosystems and the biosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each level is assembled from the one below, so a question asked at one level rarely has its answer there and a physiologist and an ecologist studying the same animal may be asking unrelated questions. <br><br> What holds so scattered a subject together is heredity, which separates a living process from the growth of a crystal or the burning of a candle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every cell yet examined stores its hereditary information as double-stranded DNA built from the same four units, transcribes it into RNA and translates that into protein by the same machinery, and reads very nearly the same genetic code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That uniformity is inherited rather than coincidental, and the pattern of resemblances lets biologists reconstruct a family tree of living species with three great branches, the bacteria, the archaea and the eukaryotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Common descent is therefore the frame the rest of the subject is written inside, and why a result obtained in yeast or a fruit fly bears on human medicine at all. It is also why the work is nowhere near done: one widely cited 2011 estimate put the number of eukaryote species at about 8.7 million against some 1.2 million then catalogued, leaving roughly 86 per cent undescribed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Diversity of Genomes and the Tree of Life,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26866/. [Open access]","Camilo Mora, Derek P. Tittensor, Sina Adl, Alastair G. B. Simpson, and Boris Worm, “How Many Species Are There on Earth and in the Ocean?” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 9, no. 8 (2011): e1001127, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001127. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/PhylogeneticTree.png","title":"A phylogenetic tree of living things, from ribosomal RNA","desc":"The three domains of life drawn from ribosomal RNA sequence comparisons: bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes, all descending from a single trunk. Public domain, redrawn from a NASA Astrobiology Institute figure.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PhylogeneticTree.png","alt":"A branching diagram on a white ground with three coloured groups of branches rising from one black trunk, labelled Bacteria in purple on the left, Archaea in red in the middle and Eucarya in dark red on the right, each branch tipped with the name of a group such as Cyanobacteria, Halophiles, Animalia, Fungi and Plantae"}},
{"id":"bio-002","num":2,"category":"What biology is","question":"Fire consumes fuel, gives off waste, moves and grows, which is why no checklist of properties on its own has ever settled what counts as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and what does not.","questions":["Textbooks introduce <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a list of eight properties rather than a definition, among them order, response to the environment, reproduction, growth, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution.","A committee working for NASA settled on a self-sustaining chemical system capable of Darwinian evolution as a working definition of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, chosen because it is the requirement fire and growing crystals fail."],"answer":"life","answerText":"life","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","evolution"],"abstract":"<b>Life</b> has no definition biologists agree on, so textbooks introduce it with a list of properties instead: order, response to the environment, reproduction, growth and development, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each item does real work: a living thing is highly organised, holds its internal conditions steady, takes in energy and passes on what it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The trouble is that no item settles the matter by itself, since fire consumes fuel, gives off waste, moves and grows, and a crystal in solution grows and hands its handedness on to those that form from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Definitions built as checklists inherit that trouble, since a list requiring every property at once will not admit a single rabbit, which neither reproduces on its own nor evolves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind all of it lies a harder problem: with only one example to generalise from, which features of life on Earth are necessary and which are accidents of its history cannot be told apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The most used working definition calls life a self-sustaining chemical system capable of Darwinian evolution, which fire fails because nothing it produces inherits from it and the crystal because its defects are not passed on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a working definition rather than a settled one, framed so it can be applied without knowing which of the familiar properties are universal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In practice most definitions rest on the cell, the minimal self-reproducing unit and the vehicle by which genetic information is handed on in every living species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why viruses are the standing hard case: they reproduce, evolve and carry genes, but a virion has no metabolism of its own, and whether they are alive is a decision about the definition rather than a discovery about viruses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The list is therefore not a failure of rigour but an honest report of where the subject stands, and biology proceeds without settling it, because what makes one organism work is answerable where the general question is not.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Steven A. Benner, “Defining Life,” <i>Astrobiology</i> 10, no. 10 (2010): 1021–30, https://doi.org/10.1089/ast.2010.0524. [Open access]","Chris P. McKay, “What Is Life—and How Do We Search for It in Other Worlds?” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 2, no. 9 (2004): e302, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0020302. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Patrick Forterre, “Defining Life: The Virus Viewpoint,” <i>Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres</i> 40, no. 2 (2010): 151–60, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11084-010-9194-1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Onion_Root_Tip_Cells_Mitosis.jpg/1920px-Onion_Root_Tip_Cells_Mitosis.jpg","title":"Onion root tip cells under a phase-contrast microscope","desc":"Cells from the growing tip of an onion root, with blue arrows marking two of them caught dividing. Photograph by Catfaster, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Photograph by Catfaster, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Onion_Root_Tip_Cells_Mitosis.jpg","alt":"A pale blue-grey micrograph of about a dozen box-shaped plant cells packed together, their walls picked out as bright lines, with two blue arrows labelled A and B pointing at cells whose interiors are divided or filled with fibrous strands"}},
{"id":"bio-003","num":3,"category":"What biology is","question":"Atoms make molecules, molecules make organelles and cells make tissues, a nesting biologists call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which each level is composed of the one beneath it.","questions":["Because each works at a different level of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a biochemist, a cytologist, a physiologist and an ecologist can study the same animal and never once overlap.","Philosophers of biology point out that no single ladder of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fits every organism, so the textbook hierarchy is best read as a teaching scaffold rather than a law."],"answer":"biological organisation","answerText":"biological organisation","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"<b>Biological organisation</b> is the nesting of living matter into levels, each composed of the one below, from atoms and molecules through organelles, cells, tissues, organs and organ systems to organisms, populations, communities, ecosystems and the biosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lower rungs are chemistry: atoms bond into molecules, molecules into the macromolecules and organelles a cell is built from, and a human red blood cell is about eight micrometres across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Above the cell the definitions turn functional: a tissue is a group of similar cells doing one job, an organ a structure of two or more tissues, and an organ system a set of organs meeting one need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The organism is the level at which the assembly can independently perform every function life requires, and where the ladder stops describing a body and starts describing a world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Above it the units are ecological: a population is one species interbreeding in one place, a community the species sharing it and their interactions, and an ecosystem the non-living surroundings as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Convention on Biological Diversity defines the last of those as a dynamic complex of plant, animal and micro-organism communities and their non-living environment interacting as a functional unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ladder's use is organising the subject, since each level has its own methods and questions, and a biochemist and an ecologist can work on one animal without ever meeting. It organises explanation too, though it usually works in the background as an implicit framework rather than an explicit theory, and general definitions of it have not met wide acceptance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The uniformity of composition a single ladder assumes does not hold across all of biology, which is the standing objection, and recent work treats levels contextually instead, case by case rather than as one layer cake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> None of that makes the hierarchy useless: it is how the subject is taught and how a question is placed, but it maps the science rather than a structure discovered in the world.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “1.2 Structural Organization of the Human Body,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-2-structural-organization-of-the-human-body. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “44.1 The Scope of Ecology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/44-1-the-scope-of-ecology. [Open access]","United Nations, <i>Convention on Biological Diversity</i>, Rio de Janeiro, 5 June 1992, article 2, https://www.cbd.int/convention/articles/?a=cbd-02. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Daniel Stephen Brooks, “Levels of Organization in Biology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Summer 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2024/entries/levels-org-biology/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-004","num":4,"category":"What biology is","question":"A bacterium with the smallest known genome still needs 477 genes, because the thing it has to build is a whole <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the smallest object that is alive.","questions":["Everything alive is either one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or an assembly of them, and every one of them comes from a pre-existing one rather than forming out of nothing.","Enclosed by a membrane that separates its chemistry from the outside, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the smallest thing that can grow, respond and reproduce on its own."],"answer":"cell","answerText":"cell","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"The <b>cell</b> is the smallest unit that is alive, and the three claims making that precise are that every living thing is made of cells, that the cell is biology’s basic unit, and that new cells arise only from existing ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What makes one a unit rather than a patch of chemistry is its plasma membrane, a bilayer of phospholipids and proteins that provides the functional separation between inside and outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything needed to keep going and to copy itself sits within that boundary, which is why it is the minimal self-reproducing unit and the vehicle by which genetic information passes on in every living species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The requirement is not a light one: the bacterium with the smallest known genome is a parasite handed many of its small molecules ready-made, and it still carries 477 genes, with the floor for a viable cell probably 200 to 300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cells are correspondingly small, an <i>Escherichia coli</i> measuring about one micrometre across and two long, so a body is not one thing but an enormous number of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One recent estimate puts a 70 kg reference adult at about 30 trillion human cells, alongside roughly 38 trillion bacterial ones living in and on the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That second figure retires a much-repeated claim that bacteria outnumber human cells ten to one, since the ratio turns out to be close to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every organism yet examined is cellular, across the bacteria, the archaea and the eukaryotes, and the resemblance of their machinery is why all three are taken to descend from one ancestral cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A multicellular body is an assembly of that same unit rather than a different kind of thing, its cells specialised for different jobs but each of them still a cell. The unit is therefore where biology’s explanations bottom out: below it there is chemistry and above it there are assemblies, but the cell is the smallest thing that does what living things do.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Origin and Evolution of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9841/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Ron Sender, Shai Fuchs, and Ron Milo, “Revised Estimates for the Number of Human and Bacteria Cells in the Body,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 14, no. 8 (2016): e1002533, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002533. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Diversity of Genomes and the Tree of Life,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26866/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Ecoli_dividing.jpg/1920px-Ecoli_dividing.jpg","title":"A dividing Escherichia coli, magnified 21,674 times","desc":"A scanning electron micrograph of one Escherichia coli cell part-way through dividing in two, with a scale bar of one micrometre. Public domain, from the CDC Public Health Image Library, image 7137, by Evangeline Sowers and Janice Haney Carr.","credit":"CDC Public Health Image Library, image 7137, by Evangeline Sowers and Janice Haney Carr. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecoli_dividing.jpg","alt":"A grey scanning electron micrograph in which a single pale rod-shaped bacterium lies against a pitted background, pinched at its middle into two rounded halves still joined, with a one-micrometre scale bar and the microscope settings printed along the bottom"}},
{"id":"bio-005","num":5,"category":"What biology is","question":"Sweating when hot and shivering when cold are one loop run in opposite directions, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name for that reversal of any departure from a set point.","questions":["A sensor, a control centre and an effector are the parts every loop of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needs, and the loop works by reversing the deviation rather than switching on and off.","Insulin released after a meal brings blood glucose back into its normal range, one of the loops of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a body runs continuously and without any conscious control."],"answer":"homeostasis","answerText":"homeostasis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"<b>Homeostasis</b> is the maintenance of a relatively stable internal environment in the face of a changing outside world and varying internal activity, worked by negative feedback that reduces an error signal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A loop needs a sensor measuring the regulated variable, a mechanism setting its normal range, a detector comparing the two, a controller deciding what to do and effectors that do it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temperature is the familiar case and it divides the animal kingdom: an ectotherm takes its body temperature from its surroundings, an endotherm holds one from internal sources, and heat moves by radiation, evaporation, convection and conduction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a human that comes to sensors reporting a departure, a control centre comparing it with the normal range, and effectors sweating, shivering or altering blood flow until the departure is reversed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blood glucose runs on the same shape, cells in the pancreas detecting a rise after a meal and releasing insulin that takes glucose out of circulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Positive feedback does the opposite and is much rarer: in childbirth the stretching of the cervix releases oxytocin, contractions strengthen, the stretching increases, and the loop stops only when the baby is born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two things commonly taught are wrong: a set point is a range rather than a single value and can be shifted, and the loops are not switches, since control signals are present the whole time and continuously set the output.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fever is the clearest case of the first, an actively driven rise of 1 to 4 °C (2 to 7 °F) rather than the temperature loop breaking down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the most famous set point has moved, since the 37 °C (98.6 °F) standard rests on measurements taken in Leipzig in 1851, and one analysis of American records finds men born in the 1990s about 0.59 °C (1.1 °F) cooler than men born in the early 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word therefore names regulation rather than constancy, and what looks like a body holding still is a body correcting continuously.","sources":["Harold Modell, William Cliff, Joel Michael, Jenny McFarland, Mary Pat Wenderoth, and Ann Wright, “A Physiologist’s View of Homeostasis,” <i>Advances in Physiology Education</i> 39, no. 4 (2015): 259–66, https://doi.org/10.1152/advan.00107.2015. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “33.3 Homeostasis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/33-3-homeostasis. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “1.5 Homeostasis,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-5-homeostasis. [Open access]","Sharon S. Evans, Elizabeth A. Repasky, and Daniel T. Fisher, “Fever and the Thermal Regulation of Immunity: The Immune System Feels the Heat,” <i>Nature Reviews Immunology</i> 15, no. 6 (2015): 335–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/nri3843. [Open access]","Myroslava Protsiv, Catherine Ley, Joanna Lankester, Trevor Hastie, and Julie Parsonnet, “Decreasing Human Body Temperature in the United States since the Industrial Revolution,” <i>eLife</i> 9 (2020): e49555, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.49555. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Chuckwalla_%2854609766149%29.jpg/1920px-Chuckwalla_%2854609766149%29.jpg","title":"A chuckwalla basking on a rock in Joshua Tree National Park","desc":"An ectotherm at work: the lizard takes its body temperature from its surroundings, and flattening itself against sunlit rock is how it raises one. Photo by NPS/Robert Zuniga, public domain.","credit":"Photo by NPS/Robert Zuniga, Joshua Tree National Park. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chuckwalla_%2854609766149%29.jpg","alt":"A dark grey lizard with a pale speckled back lying flattened along the top of a sunlit granite boulder, its head turned towards the camera and a shadowed rock face behind it"}},
{"id":"bio-006","num":6,"category":"What biology is","question":"Breaking food down and building a body up are the two halves of one linked network of reactions, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name for the whole of it.","questions":["Enzymes let reactions run at body temperature that would otherwise need far hotter conditions, which is what makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possible inside a cell at all.","A single gut bacterium’s <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been reconstructed as 2,251 reactions among 1,136 different molecules, which is what a cell runs simply to stay alive."],"answer":"metabolism","answerText":"metabolism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"<b>Metabolism</b> is the whole set of chemical reactions going on inside a living thing, both those that release energy and those that use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into two halves that depend on each other: catabolic pathways break foodstuffs down into smaller molecules, yielding a usable form of energy and some of the building blocks a cell needs, while anabolic pathways spend that energy synthesising the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost none of it would happen unaided, since these reactions would normally proceed only at temperatures far above the one inside a cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Enzymes are what make it possible at body temperature, and a pathway is a series of them, the product of one reaction becoming the starting material for the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those series are linked to one another into a maze of interconnected reactions, which is why metabolism is better pictured as a network than as a line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The size of that network can be counted: a genome-scale reconstruction of <i>Escherichia coli</i> accounts for 1,366 genes, 2,251 reactions and 1,136 different metabolites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In an animal the breakdown runs in three stages, digestion cutting large molecules into their subunits, glycolysis converting each glucose into two pyruvate, and a third stage in the mitochondria yielding most of the cell’s energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of that energy is used where it is released: ATP carries it, serving as a convenient and versatile currency, made by adding a phosphate group to ADP and spent by taking it off again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Metabolism never stops, and the rate it runs at is what a body’s food supplies, but the familiar rule that this rate scales with body mass to the three-quarter power is not a law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Across 358 studies the scaling exponent varies, mostly between 0.5 and 1.0, and the variation is real biological difference rather than noise, which is worth knowing early about any relationship that fits a graph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.1 Energy and Metabolism,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-1-energy-and-metabolism. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Jeffrey D. Orth, Tom M. Conrad, Jessica Na, Joshua A. Lerman, Hojung Nam, Adam M. Feist, and Bernhard Ø. Palsson, “A Comprehensive Genome-Scale Reconstruction of <i>Escherichia coli</i> Metabolism—2011,” <i>Molecular Systems Biology</i> 7 (2011): 535, https://doi.org/10.1038/msb.2011.65. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “How Cells Obtain Energy from Food,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26882/. [Open access]","Douglas S. Glazier, “Variable Metabolic Scaling Breaks the Law: From ‘Newtonian’ to ‘Darwinian’ Approaches,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 289, no. 1985 (2022): 20221605, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2022.1605. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Carbon_Catabolism.png/1920px-Carbon_Catabolism.png","title":"A map of carbon catabolism","desc":"Glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, the beta-oxidation of fats and the electron transport chain drawn as one connected diagram, with every enzyme named. This is only the energy-releasing half of metabolism, and only for carbon compounds. Diagram by Mplanine, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Diagram by Mplanine, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carbon_Catabolism.png","alt":"A dense white chart covered in chemical structures joined by hundreds of curved arrows, grouped into labelled blocks headed Carbohydrate Metabolism, Citric Acid Cycle and Lipid Metabolism, with small coloured tags naming the enzyme at each step and a rainbow bar at lower right labelled Electron Transport Chain"}},
{"id":"bio-007","num":7,"category":"What biology is","question":"Comparing a treated group with one identical to it except for the treatment is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns a biological guess into a claim that evidence can settle.","questions":["Because no two organisms are alike, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in biology rests on independent replicates rather than on one animal, however striking that animal’s result looks.","Where the subject is a lineage that died out, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proceeds by predicting what will be found in rocks of a given age and then going to look."],"answer":"scientific method","answerText":"scientific method","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"The <b>scientific method</b> is the procedure by which a claim is made answerable to evidence, running from an observation to a question, a hypothesis, a prediction, a test and a report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Biology uses it in two modes: descriptive science, usually inductive, which observes in order to find what is there, and hypothesis-based science, usually deductive, which starts from a proposed answer and tests it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A test holds everything constant but one thing, comparing an experimental group with a control identical to it except for the manipulation, so that any difference can be laid at the manipulation’s door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What makes this harder than in chemistry is that no two organisms are alike, so a result rests on replicates, and the replicates have to be independent of one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They often are not: in one journal issue surveyed, 12 per cent of papers had treated repeated measures from the same animal as independent samples, and another 36 per cent were suspected of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Much of biology cannot be manipulated at all, since a lineage’s history is over, and there the test is a prediction about what will be found in rocks of a given age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tetrapod-like fish <i>Tiktaalik roseae</i> came from one Late Devonian locality on Ellesmere Island worked over six field seasons, which is what a test looks like when the subject died out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Work in a model organism rests on shared machinery, since every cell stores its information as DNA and expresses it by the same mechanism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It does not always carry across: genomic responses to burns, trauma and infection in mice correlate almost not at all with the human responses they were taken to model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The method is the one every science uses, and what changes is how much of it can be done to the subject; where manipulation is impossible, prediction and independent evidence carry the weight.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Stanley E. Lazic, “The Problem of Pseudoreplication in Neuroscientific Studies: Is It Affecting Your Analysis?” <i>BMC Neuroscience</i> 11 (2010): 5, https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2202-11-5. [Open access]","Thomas A. Stewart, Justin B. Lemberg, Emily J. Hillan, Isaac Magallanes, Edward B. Daeschler, and Neil H. Shubin, “The Axial Skeleton of <i>Tiktaalik roseae</i>,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 15 (2024): e2316106121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2316106121. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Junhee Seok, H. Shaw Warren, Alex G. Cuenca, Michael N. Mindrinos, Henry V. Baker, Weihong Xu, Daniel R. Richards, et al., “Genomic Responses in Mouse Models Poorly Mimic Human Inflammatory Diseases,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 9 (2013): 3507–12, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1222878110. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Tiktaalik_roseae_%28fish_to_amphibian_transitional_fossil%29_%28Fram_Formation%2C_Upper_Devonian%3B_Ellesmere_Island%2C_Arctic_Canada%29_1_%2849755118288%29.jpg","title":"A cast of the Tiktaalik roseae specimens, Field Museum","desc":"A cast of two of the Tiktaalik specimens from the Fram Formation of Ellesmere Island, on display at the Field Museum in Chicago; the flat skull is at the left and the scaled body at the right. Photograph by James St. John, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Photograph by James St. John, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiktaalik_roseae_%28fish_to_amphibian_transitional_fossil%29_%28Fram_Formation%2C_Upper_Devonian%3B_Ellesmere_Island%2C_Arctic_Canada%29_1_%2849755118288%29.jpg","alt":"A wide fossil slab photographed against a pale grey mount, with a flattened crocodile-like skull at the left and a long body of overlapping diamond scales running to the right, the bone a mottled brown and grey"}},
{"id":"bio-008","num":8,"category":"What biology is","question":"An experiment cannot show that a treatment works; what it can do is give evidence against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the claim that the treatment makes no difference at all.","questions":["A biologist who finds nothing has not shown there is nothing to find, because failing to reject the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may only mean the data could not tell the possibilities apart.","Stated before the data are collected, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what stops a surprising result being written up afterwards as though it had been predicted from the start."],"answer":"null hypothesis","answerText":"null hypothesis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"A hypothesis in biology is a testable proposal about how something works, and it earns its place by yielding a prediction that an experiment could contradict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <b>null hypothesis</b> is the version a statistical test is applied to: the claim that there is no difference between the groups being compared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The biologist’s own idea is the alternative, and a result is reported either as rejecting the null, where a difference is too large to be likely by chance, or as failing to reject it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A p-value is the probability of seeing an effect at least this large if there were no true effect, and it is not the probability that the null is true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That misreading is the commonest one, and its partner is treating a large p-value as evidence for the null, when it usually means only that the data cannot discriminate among competing hypotheses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In biology the design matters as much as the arithmetic, since a comparison is fair only if animals are assigned at random, the measurer does not know which group is which, and the sample size is set in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reporting guidelines for animal experiments exist because those items were routinely missing from published papers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The other failure is to the hypothesis rather than the experiment: of 807 ecologists and evolutionary biologists surveyed, 51 per cent had reported an unexpected finding as though it had been hypothesised from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the same survey 64 per cent had at least once not reported a result because it was not statistically significant, and 42 per cent had collected more data after checking whether it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of that is fraud, and all of it is why a hypothesis stated before the data are seen, and an experiment built so that it could refute one, are worth more than a striking result.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Jacob Shreffler and Martin R. Huecker, “Hypothesis Testing, P Values, Confidence Intervals, and Significance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557421/. [Open access]","Sander Greenland, Stephen J. Senn, Kenneth J. Rothman, John B. Carlin, Charles Poole, Steven N. Goodman, and Douglas G. Altman, “Statistical Tests, P Values, Confidence Intervals, and Power: A Guide to Misinterpretations,” <i>European Journal of Epidemiology</i> 31, no. 4 (2016): 337–50, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10654-016-0149-3. [Open access]","Nathalie Percie du Sert, Amrita Ahluwalia, Sabina Alam, Marc T. Avey, Monya Baker, et al., “Reporting Animal Research: Explanation and Elaboration for the ARRIVE Guidelines 2.0,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 18, no. 7 (2020): e3000411, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000411. [Open access]","Hannah Fraser, Tim Parker, Shinichi Nakagawa, Ashley Barnett, and Fiona Fidler, “Questionable Research Practices in Ecology and Evolution,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 7 (2018): e0200303, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0200303. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-009","num":9,"category":"What biology is","question":"Every feature of the treated group except the treatment itself is what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries, which is the whole reason a difference between them can be blamed on the treatment.","questions":["Without a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a biologist has a measurement and no comparison, so any change could as easily be the season, the cage or the batch of reagent.","When cases and controls are processed on different days, the processing day tracks the disease, and a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot rescue an experiment whose groups differ in more than one way."],"answer":"control group","answerText":"control group","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"A variable is anything in an experiment that can change, and a <b>control group</b> is a group carrying every feature of the experimental one except the manipulation being tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what licenses the inference: if the two groups differ afterwards, the difference can be laid at the manipulation rather than at some outside factor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The licence is only as good as the sameness, so a controlled biological experiment has to say how individuals were allocated to groups, who knew which group was which, and how the sample size was arrived at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also has to say what the experimental unit was, since the thing that was allocated is the thing that counts, however many measurements were taken from each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where some difference other than the manipulation tracks the groups themselves, that difference is a confound, and no amount of later analysis will separate it from the result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> High-throughput biology gives the clearest cases, since measurements made in different batches behave differently for technical reasons unrelated to the biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In one published proteomic test for ovarian cancer the disease turned out to be almost perfectly correlated with the day each sample was processed, and the assay was blocked pending further validation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blinding is the control on the observer rather than on the material, and its absence is measurable: across paired studies, not recording data blind inflates the mean reported effect size by about 27 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also rare, reported in 6.3 per cent of 960 animal behaviour studies,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while a survey of 2,671 in vivo publications found randomisation in 24.8 per cent and a sample size calculation in 0.7 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this is bureaucracy: the control is the part of an experiment that decides whether its result means anything, and the figures say it is the part most often left out.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Nathalie Percie du Sert, Amrita Ahluwalia, Sabina Alam, Marc T. Avey, Monya Baker, et al., “Reporting Animal Research: Explanation and Elaboration for the ARRIVE Guidelines 2.0,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 18, no. 7 (2020): e3000411, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000411. [Open access]","Jeffrey T. Leek, Robert B. Scharpf, Héctor Corrada Bravo, David Simcha, Benjamin Langmead, W. Evan Johnson, Donald Geman, Keith Baggerly, and Rafael A. Irizarry, “Tackling the Widespread and Critical Impact of Batch Effects in High-Throughput Data,” <i>Nature Reviews Genetics</i> 11, no. 10 (2010): 733–39, https://doi.org/10.1038/nrg2825. [Open access]","Luke Holman, Megan L. Head, Robert Lanfear, and Michael D. Jennions, “Evidence of Experimental Bias in the Life Sciences: Why We Need Blind Data Recording,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 13, no. 7 (2015): e1002190, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002190. [Open access]","Malcolm R. Macleod, Aaron Lawson McLean, Aikaterini Kyriakopoulou, Stylianos Serghiou, Arno de Wilde, et al., “Risk of Bias in Reports of In Vivo Research: A Focus for Improvement,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 13, no. 10 (2015): e1002273, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002273. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-010","num":10,"category":"What biology is","question":"Weighing the same mouse twice gives two answers, and the gap between them is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which biology reports alongside a figure rather than hiding.","questions":["Bias and variability are the two kinds of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and only the second of them gets smaller as a biologist takes more measurements of the same thing.","Where 47 per cent of herbarium specimens of one plant genus were identified as the wrong species, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies in naming the organism rather than in any instrument."],"answer":"measurement error","answerText":"measurement error","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"Biology begins in observation, and every observation carried into a result is a measurement with a <b>measurement error</b> attached to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Error comes in two kinds that behave differently: bias, the difference between the average of repeated measurements and the true value, and variability, the tendency of a process to give slightly different answers each time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bias does not average out with more data, which is why an instrument is calibrated against a reference rather than trusted, while variability is quantified as a standard deviation and shrinks as measurements are repeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The variability has more than one time scale, since short-term scatter is the precision of the instrument, and day-to-day variability from small changes in conditions and handling often exceeds it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is a fact about biology as much as about instruments, since a body, a cage and a field all change between one morning and the next. <br><br> The measurement a biologist makes first is usually the identity of the thing being measured, and identification is itself error-prone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A review of European herbarium specimens of ivy found an average error rate of 47 per cent in the original species identification, rising to 55 per cent for one species and falling to 23 per cent for the commonest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the field the problem is not misreading but missing, since observed counts usually represent only a subset of the community actually present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Failing to allow for that biases everything derived from the counts, species richness included, and it does so worst where the chance of detecting an animal is lowest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this makes measurement futile; it makes the error part of the result, which is why a figure in biology is reported with a range around it rather than on its own.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","“2.1.1.3. Bias and Accuracy,” in <i>NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods</i> (Gaithersburg, MD: National Institute of Standards and Technology), https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/section1/mpc113.htm. [Open access]","“2.1.1.4. Variability,” in <i>NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods</i> (Gaithersburg, MD: National Institute of Standards and Technology), https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/section1/mpc114.htm. [Open access]","Marina Coca-de-la-Iglesia, Angélica Gallego-Narbón, Alejandro Alonso, and Virginia Valcárcel, “High Rate of Species Misidentification Reduces the Taxonomic Certainty of European Biodiversity Databases of Ivies (<i>Hedera</i> L.),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 4876, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-54735-0. [Open access]","Ciar D. Noble, Carlos A. Peres, and James J. Gilroy, “Accounting for Imperfect Detection When Estimating Species-Area Relationships and Beta-Diversity,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 7 (2024): e70017, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.70017. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Disperis_nitida_herbarium_sheet.jpg/1920px-Disperis_nitida_herbarium_sheet.jpg","title":"A herbarium sheet, collected in Cameroon in 1975","desc":"A pressed orchid mounted with its collector’s label and a colour reference strip, held at Meise Botanic Garden. The sheet carries the plant and the name somebody gave it, and the name is a measurement like any other. Photograph by Meise Botanic Garden, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Meise Botanic Garden, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Disperis_nitida_herbarium_sheet.jpg","alt":"A tall cream sheet of card with a slender dried orchid taped down the middle, a single flower at its top and two leaves half way, a typed collector’s label at the lower left and a printed colour and greyscale calibration strip down the right-hand edge"}},
{"id":"bio-011","num":11,"category":"What biology is","question":"Because a typical animal cell is about a fifth the size of the smallest thing an unaided eye can resolve, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what made cell biology possible at all.","questions":["No radiation can resolve detail much smaller than its own wavelength, which is why a light <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stops at just under 0.2 micrometres however good its lenses are.","Fixing, sectioning and staining are what a specimen undergoes before a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is pointed at it, and each of those steps can alter the structure being looked at."],"answer":"microscope","answerText":"microscope","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","technology"],"abstract":"A typical animal cell is 10 to 20 micrometres across, about a fifth of the smallest particle the naked eye can make out, so a <b>microscope</b> is what puts cells within reach at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A compound light microscope magnifies up to about a thousand times, but contrast is too poor to see much that way, so cells are fixed in alcohol, acetic acid or formaldehyde, sectioned thinly and stained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What that bought biology was the cell itself: good light microscopes in the early nineteenth century are what showed plant and animal tissues to be aggregates of individual cells,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and cell theory followed from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It carries a standing caution, since any treatment used for fixing and embedding may alter the structure of the cell or its molecules in undesirable ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The instrument also has a hard floor, because no radiation resolves detail much smaller than its own wavelength, which for visible light gives a limit of just under 0.2 micrometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Electrons have a far shorter wavelength, and an electron microscope reaches about 2 nanometres on biological material, roughly a hundred times better than light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The price is that electrons are scattered by air, so the specimen sits in a vacuum, in which liquid water evaporates and unprotected biomolecules collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Freezing water fast enough that it solidifies without crystallising lets a molecule keep its shape inside that vacuum, which is what cryo-electron microscopy does and what the 2017 chemistry Nobel Prize was given for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Light was thought finished as a probe below 0.2 micrometres, a limit stated in 1873, until fluorescent labels switched on and off one at a time were used to get underneath it, work recognised by the 2014 chemistry Nobel Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A microscope therefore does not simply show what is there: what it shows is set by the radiation used, by what the specimen had to survive, and by what was labelled.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2017: Press Release,” 4 October 2017, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2017/press-release/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2014: Press Release,” 8 October 2014, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2014/press-release/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Indian_Muntjac_fibroblast_cells_%2824271618921%29.jpg","title":"Cultured muntjac fibroblasts under a fluorescence microscope","desc":"Three dyes and three targets: the nuclei stained blue, the actin filaments green and the mitochondria red, with everything unlabelled left black. The scale bar is 20 micrometres. Photograph by ZEISS Microscopy, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"ZEISS Microscopy, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Muntjac_fibroblast_cells_%2824271618921%29.jpg","alt":"Against a black ground, four flat sprawling cells glow in three colours: bright green fibres run the length of each cell, an oval blue nucleus sits at the centre of each, and small red specks are scattered through the green, with a red scale bar marked 20 micrometres at the lower right"}},
{"id":"bio-012","num":12,"category":"What biology is","question":"Projecting a micrograph onto a wall enlarges it without limit, but two points closer than 0.2 micrometres still merge, because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and magnification are not the same thing.","questions":["The wavelength of the light and the numerical aperture of the lens together fix the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and no refinement of the glass can get past what those two allow.","A teaching light microscope magnifies about 400 times at a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 200 nanometres, while an electron microscope magnifies 100,000 times and reaches 50 picometres."],"answer":"resolution","answerText":"resolution","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","technology"],"abstract":"Magnification is the enlargement of an object’s appearance, while <b>resolution</b> is an instrument’s ability to show two adjacent things as two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two come apart because an image can be enlarged as much as anyone likes, by projecting it on a screen, without ever separating two points the optics has already merged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The merging has a physical cause, since light behaves as a wave: a single point seen through a microscope appears as a blurred disc, and two close points give overlapping discs that read as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The limiting separation at which two objects stay distinct depends on the wavelength of the light and on the numerical aperture of the lens, which measures how wide the instrument opens its eye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With violet light and a numerical aperture of 1.4 the floor is just under 0.2 micrometres, a figure reached by microscope makers at the end of the 19th century and rarely matched by factory optics since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In a teaching laboratory a light microscope magnifies about 400 times at a resolution near 200 nanometres,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and oil immersion takes the magnification to about a thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An electron microscope magnifies 100,000 times and resolves about 50 picometres in principle,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> though lens aberrations and specimen damage hold it to a few nanometres on biological material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wall itself was stated in 1873, as half the wavelength of the light being used, and it stood for more than a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fluorescent labels switched on and off one at a time were used to get underneath it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while vitrified water let electron microscopy hold molecules in something near their real shape,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> so the ceiling has moved twice in living memory. The distinction is what survives all of it: magnification is cheap and resolution is not, which is why a micrograph is judged by the scale bar on it rather than by the number printed on the objective.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2014: Press Release,” 8 October 2014, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2014/press-release/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2017: Press Release,” 4 October 2017, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2017/press-release/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-013","num":13,"category":"What biology is","question":"Yeast, a weed, a fly, a worm and a mouse are the species biology knows best, each chosen as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for being cheap and quick to breed rather than for being typical.","questions":["Because a nematode grows into an adult of exactly 959 cells, it serves as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in which a whole course of development can be written down cell by cell.","Findings in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travel only as far as the shared machinery does, which is why mouse responses to burns and trauma turned out to match the human ones hardly at all."],"answer":"model organism","answerText":"model organism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"A <b>model organism</b> is a species studied in depth on the understanding that what is found in it will hold, with adjustments, for others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The set is small and each member was picked for convenience: yeast grows almost as fast as a bacterium, <i>Arabidopsis</i> produces thousands of seeds in eight to ten weeks, and a fruit fly goes from egg to adult in nine days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nematode <i>Caenorhabditis elegans</i> was established as a model on those grounds, and every cell division from the fertilised egg to the 959 cells of the adult was then followed under a microscope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That lineage proved invariant, every worm running the same programme, and the genes controlling its cell deaths were found to have counterparts in higher animals including humans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of it would work if organisms did not share their machinery: every cell stores its information as DNA and expresses it the same way,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and some cell-cycle genes work interchangeably in yeast and human cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The mouse is the foremost vertebrate model for the same reason, since about 85 per cent of the amino acids in a human protein are typically identical to those in an elephant’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inference is not guaranteed and can fail badly: genomic responses to burns, trauma and infection in mice correlate almost not at all with the human responses they were taken to model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The second cost is what the choice does to the map, since effort concentrates where the tools already are. In one enzyme database the top eight eukaryotic species account for 37 per cent of all entries, 88 per cent of the species present carry fewer than ten entries each, and of 111 bacterial phyla only 24 appear at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A model organism is therefore a bargain rather than a representative sample: it buys depth in a few species, and the rest of the living world is known in proportion to how easily it keeps on a bench.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Genetic Information in Eucaryotes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26909/. [Open access]","Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2002: Press Release,” 7 October 2002, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2002/press-release/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Junhee Seok, H. Shaw Warren, Alex G. Cuenca, Michael N. Mindrinos, Henry V. Baker, Weihong Xu, Daniel R. Richards, et al., “Genomic Responses in Mouse Models Poorly Mimic Human Inflammatory Diseases,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 9 (2013): 3507–12, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1222878110. [Open access]","Chelsea J. Vickers, Dean Fraga, and Wayne M. Patrick, “Quantifying the Taxonomic Bias in Enzymology,” <i>Protein Science</i> 30, no. 4 (2021): 914–21, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.4041. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Genetic_map_of_drosophila%2C_as_of_1926.png","title":"A linkage map of the fruit fly, published in 1926","desc":"The four linkage groups of Drosophila melanogaster set out as four lines, each mutant named and placed at its map distance from the top. This is what made the fly worth keeping: a species whose genetics could be written down. Public domain, from Thomas Hunt Morgan, The Theory of the Gene, 1926.","credit":"Thomas Hunt Morgan, The Theory of the Gene, 1926. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genetic_map_of_drosophila,_as_of_1926.png","alt":"A printed chart of four vertical lines headed with the Roman numerals one to four, each crossed by short tick marks with a number and a mutant name beside it, among them yellow, white, Notch, vermilion, black, purple, vestigial, ebony and eyeless"}},
{"id":"bio-014","num":14,"category":"What biology is","question":"Botany, zoology and microbiology divide life by group and physiology by process, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cuts across every one of them by working at the scale where all of them are the same.","questions":["A sequence from a yeast, a fly, a worm or a bacterium can be lined up against the human one, which is what gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a claim on every other part of the subject.","Biochemistry, biophysics and bioinformatics are hybrid names of recent date, but <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the one that reorganised the older divisions by group and by process."],"answer":"molecular biology","answerText":"molecular biology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"<b>Molecular biology</b> is the study of living things at the scale of the molecules that carry life out, and it is the field that cuts across every other way of dividing the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Biology divides first by group, so that botany, zoology and microbiology take a slice of the living world each,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with virology, mycology and entomology taking narrower slices still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It divides again by process, so that physiology asks how an organism works, genetics how it passes on what it is, developmental biology how it is built from one cell, and ecology how it lives among others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It divides a third time by level, ecology alone running from the single organism through populations and communities to whole ecosystems, and the same ladder holding below the organism as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fourth division is by what the work is for, which is where medicine, agriculture, conservation, biotechnology and forensic science sit, each drawing on all of the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One field can cut across the rest because the machinery is shared, so a result in yeast, a fly, a worm, a plant or a bacterium can be lined up against the human version by sequence similarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is why the boundaries are administrative rather than natural, and why so many of the newer names are hybrids: biochemistry, biophysics, neurobiology, bioinformatics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The divisions are how a person gets a purchase on the subject rather than how the subject is built, and no working biologist commands more than a corner of it. The reason is arithmetic as much as intellectual, since by 2023 the PubMed database held more than 36 million citations, was adding over 1.5 million a year, and indexed some 5,294 journals for MEDLINE alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A field is therefore also a way of choosing what to read, and the choice is forced: biology now produces more literature than any one reader can hold.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Genetic Information in Eucaryotes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26909/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “44.1 The Scope of Ecology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/44-1-the-scope-of-ecology. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, “MEDLINE PubMed Production Statistics,” accessed 5 September 2026, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/bsd/medline_pubmed_production_stats.html. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-015","num":15,"category":"What biology is","question":"Because a cell obeys the same chemical rules as a test tube, the science of what happens inside one is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a field with a foot in each subject.","questions":["Oxygen, carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen make up most of a body, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the study of what those elements do once they are assembled into a living thing.","An organism can neither create energy nor escape the loss of some as heat, which is the sort of constraint <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exists to trace through a living system."],"answer":"biochemistry","answerText":"biochemistry","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Biochemistry</b> is the chemistry of living things, and it exists because the boundary between biology and chemistry is a boundary of interest rather than of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A body is made of the same elements as everything else, four of them accounting for most of it — oxygen at about 65 per cent, carbon 18, hydrogen 10 and nitrogen 3 — and the reactions between them obey the same rules inside a cell as outside one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Physics binds just as tightly: an organism cannot create energy, only take it in and transform it, and every transformation loses some of it as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is why a living thing is a costly arrangement, since it is highly ordered and holds that order only by spending energy continuously and pushing disorder into its surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What biology adds to the chemistry is not an exemption but a catalyst, and reactions that would otherwise need far higher temperatures are made to run at the temperature of a cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The traffic runs the other way too, since physics supplies the instruments that make molecules visible, from the microscope to the imaging of frozen biomolecules that won the 2017 chemistry Nobel Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mathematics supplies the models, and population genetics is the clearest case, an account of how allele frequencies behave that is arithmetic before it is anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Geology supplies the timescale and the record, and the earth sciences are where the world a species evolved in has to be reconstructed from. None of this makes biology a branch of chemistry, because the questions differ: a chemist can say what a protein does, while a biologist asks why this organism has that protein and not another. The subject is therefore constrained by the sciences beneath it without being reducible to a list of their results, which is why its borders are the busiest part of it.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “2.1 Atoms, Isotopes, Ions, and Molecules: The Building Blocks,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/2-1-atoms-isotopes-ions-and-molecules-the-building-blocks. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.3 The Laws of Thermodynamics,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-3-the-laws-of-thermodynamics. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2017: Press Release,” 4 October 2017, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2017/press-release/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “19.2 Population Genetics,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-2-population-genetics. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-016","num":16,"category":"What biology is","question":"A molecule is not alive and a cell is, which is the standard case of an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: something a whole has that none of its parts has on its own.","questions":["Each starling in a flock attends to six or seven neighbours and to nothing else, so the shape of the whole flock is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that no bird in it represents.","Philosophers hold that an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must both depend on its base and be somewhat autonomous of it, so an account dropping either half is describing something else."],"answer":"emergent property","answerText":"emergent property","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"An <b>emergent property</b> is one a whole has and its parts do not, which in biology is the ordinary case rather than a curiosity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell gathers raw materials from its surroundings and builds a new cell in its own image, and nothing less than a cell can do that, so being alive appears at that level and not below it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Philosophers pair two claims deliberately here, since emergence is meant to join dependence on a base with some autonomy from it, and an account that drops either half stops being about emergence at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also divide the notion, weak emergence treating the higher-level facts as nothing over and above the base, and strong emergence claiming a greater distinctness between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What emergence is not, in either version, is the merely surprising, since being hard to predict is a fact about the predictor rather than about the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Biology supplies the clearest examples because its hierarchy is built by composition, running from atoms and molecules through cells and tissues to populations, communities and ecosystems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A flock is a case that has been measured rather than asserted, since each starling attends to a fixed number of neighbours, six or seven, rather than to every bird within a set distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No bird holds the shape of the flock, and the rule each one follows contains no description of it, yet the flock keeps its cohesion as its density changes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same shape of explanation runs downwards, since regulation is a property of a loop rather than of any part of it, and a sensor, a set point and an effector on their own regulate nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Emergence is therefore a reason to work at more than one level rather than a licence to stop explaining, and the useful question is always which properties of a whole its parts already fix.","sources":["Timothy O’Connor, “Emergent Properties,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2021 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2021/entries/properties-emergent/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","M. Ballerini, N. Cabibbo, R. Candelier, A. Cavagna, E. Cisbani, I. Giardina, V. Lecomte, et al., “Interaction Ruling Animal Collective Behavior Depends on Topological Rather than Metric Distance: Evidence from a Field Study,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 4 (2008): 1232–37, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0711437105. [Open access]","Harold Modell, William Cliff, Joel Michael, Jenny McFarland, Mary Pat Wenderoth, and Ann Wright, “A Physiologist’s View of Homeostasis,” <i>Advances in Physiology Education</i> 39, no. 4 (2015): 259–66, https://doi.org/10.1152/advan.00107.2015. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-017","num":17,"category":"What biology is","question":"Because a shape that looks purpose-built is only a hypothesis until performance is measured, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tests how far a structure's form is explained by what it does.","questions":["Wrasses and cichlids keep a similar jaw linkage across very different diets, a many-to-one result that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached by measuring mechanics rather than by comparing shapes.","Bone responds to the loads placed on it and also to hormones and to age, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> treats internal architecture as evidence rather than as proof."],"answer":"functional morphology","answerText":"functional morphology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"<b>Functional morphology</b> studies the relationship between the form of a biological structure and what that structure does, and its working question is how far functional demand explains a shape as against inheritance and development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tightest case is molecular, since the precise shape of a protein molecule determines its function in the cell, and that shape is specified by the order of amino acids along the chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The evidence there is an experiment rather than an inference, because a protein unfolded by a denaturing solvent will often refold into its original conformation once the solvent is taken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the level of a tissue the relationship is looser but still measurable, since bone responds to the mechanical loads placed on it, which is why the internal architecture of a bone is read as a record of how an animal moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even there the reading is not clean, since bone is also affected by hormones, metabolism and age, so a difference in architecture need not be a difference in loading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Whole organs are studied by measuring performance rather than by inspecting a shape, and the answer is often not the one the anatomy suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In fishes that feed by suction, skeletal levers and linkages transmit muscle force into a three-dimensional expansion of the mouth cavity, but the power for that expansion comes mostly from large regions of the body muscles rather than from the head alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The relationship also runs many to one, since different morphologies can produce similar mechanical properties, so several shapes may do a job equally well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wrasses and cichlids show that, their four-bar jaw linkages keeping much the same geometry across diets that differ in hardness and in how far the prey can flee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A structure's use therefore has to be established rather than assumed, and a shape that looks purpose-built is a hypothesis about performance until somebody measures it.","sources":["Alexander Blanke, Helmut Schmitz, Alessandra Patera, Hugo Dutel, and Michael J. Fagan, “Form–Function Relationships in Dragonfly Mandibles under an Evolutionary Perspective,” <i>Journal of the Royal Society Interface</i> 14, no. 128 (2017): 20161038, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2016.1038. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Christine M. Harper and Biren A. Patel, “Functional Morphology of Trabecular Bone in the Calcaneus of African Apes,” <i>Journal of Anatomy</i> 249, no. 4 (2026): 883–98, https://doi.org/10.1111/joa.70141. [Open access]","Ariel L. Camp and Sam Van Wassenbergh, “A Mechanical Perspective on Suction Feeding in Fishes,” <i>Journal of Experimental Biology</i> 228, no. 18 (2025): jeb250567, https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.250567. [Open access]","H. Camarillo, E. D. Burress, and M. M. Muñoz, “Four-Bar Geometry Is Shared among Ecologically Divergent Fish Species,” <i>Integrative Organismal Biology</i> 6, no. 1 (2024): obae019, https://doi.org/10.1093/iob/obae019. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-018","num":18,"category":"What biology is","question":"Because the rules of translation are partly arbitrary and yet the same in nearly every cell, biologists read that shared machinery as evidence of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than of convergence.","questions":["The genes of the translation system are conserved across bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes, which is the usual evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a formal proof of it is thought unlikely.","A single origin for every cellular organism alive is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asserts, and the ciliates that read stop codons as amino acids are the qualification it has to carry."],"answer":"common descent","answerText":"common descent","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","evolution"],"abstract":"<b>Common descent</b> is the claim that all cellular life on Earth has a single origin, and it rests on the universality of the genetic code and on the conservation of the genes that carry out translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shared machinery is specific rather than vague, since every cell stores its hereditary information in DNA, transcribes portions of it into RNA, and translates that RNA into protein in a way that is identical, with minor exceptions, in all living things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What makes the sharing evidence is that the rules are in part arbitrary, so a code with no chemical necessity behind it is better explained as an accident frozen into the earliest organisms and inherited since than as a solution each lineage found for itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The argument does not rest on the code alone, the genes of the translation system being conserved across the whole of cellular life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its own advocates state the limit plainly, holding that the comparative-genomic evidence is overwhelming while a formal demonstration is unlikely to be feasible in principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The word universal has to be qualified, because ciliates reassign stop codons to amino acids, and of 24 species whose codes were inferred from marine transcriptomes, nine matched no code table then on record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of them, <i>Condylostoma magnum</i>, reads all three standard stop codons as amino acids inside a message and as a stop only close to its far end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that unity the diversity is enormous and badly counted, the most cited estimate putting eukaryotic species at about 8.7 million, give or take 1.3 million, with some 1.2 million catalogued so far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Published estimates run from 2 million to 3 trillion, about 80% of the 8.7 million are hypothetical, and the disagreement cannot be settled without a better count of bacteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Unity and diversity are therefore claims of very different quality, one inferred from machinery every cell carries and the other extrapolated from the small fraction of the planet anyone has yet examined.","sources":["Eugene V. Koonin and Yuri I. Wolf, “The Common Ancestry of Life,” <i>Biology Direct</i> 5 (2010): 64, https://doi.org/10.1186/1745-6150-5-64. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Stephen M. Heaphy, Marco Mariotti, Vadim N. Gladyshev, John F. Atkins, and Pavel V. Baranov, “Novel Ciliate Genetic Code Variants Including the Reassignment of All Three Stop Codons to Sense Codons in <i>Condylostoma magnum</i>,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 33, no. 11 (2016): 2885–89, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msw166. [Open access]","Camilo Mora, Derek P. Tittensor, Sina Adl, Alastair G. B. Simpson, and Boris Worm, “How Many Species Are There on Earth and in the Ocean?,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 9, no. 8 (2011): e1001127, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001127. [Open access]","John J. Wiens, “How Many Species Are There on Earth? Progress and Problems,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 21, no. 11 (2023): e3002388, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3002388. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-019","num":19,"category":"What biology is","question":"Twelve identical bacterial populations went 30,000 generations before one of them began using citrate, which is the clearest measurement anyone has made of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in evolution.","questions":["Because the exponent linking metabolic rate to body mass turns out to vary systematically, biology's nearest thing to a law is better read as a product of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than of physical necessity.","Philosophers hold that a species is an individual rather than a kind, one reason the subject states so few laws and leans instead on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to explain what it finds."],"answer":"contingency","answerText":"contingency","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","evolution"],"abstract":"<b>Contingency</b> is the dependence of an outcome on the particular sequence of events that produced it, so that the same starting point run again need not arrive anywhere near the same place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philosophers of science once treated the absence of physics-style laws in biology as a shortcoming of the subject and now treat it as a legitimate practice of a different kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There are hardly any universal laws of the Newtonian sort in biology, and its best-known generalisations, Mendel's among them, are described as having exceptions and as holding only other things being equal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One argument for that is that biology adds nothing to the laws of nature, and shows instead how the laws of physics and chemistry play out under particular initial and boundary conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another is that a species is an individual rather than a kind, its organisms being parts of it rather than instances of it, so there can be no law about a species in the traditional sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The nearest thing biology has to a law is the scaling of metabolic rate with body mass, long assumed to follow an exponent of two-thirds or three-quarters because physical constraint required it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hundreds of studies have since documented systematic variation in that exponent, which has turned the subject from a search for a constant into an inquiry into why the value moves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Contingency itself has been measured rather than argued: twelve identical populations of <i>Escherichia coli</i> founded in 1988 went more than 30,000 generations without any of them using the citrate in their medium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One population then did, and replaying its history from frozen samples produced no citrate-users among 8.4 trillion ancestral cells while later clones evolved it readily, so a potentiating mutation had arisen by 20,000 generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even the near-universal genetic code is a case of the same thing, its arbitrary features being read as an accident frozen into the earliest cells rather than as a necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Zachary D. Blount, Christina Z. Borland, and Richard E. Lenski, “Historical Contingency and the Evolution of a Key Innovation in an Experimental Population of <i>Escherichia coli</i>,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 23 (2008): 7899–906, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0803151105. [Open access]","Alexander Reutlinger, Gerhard Schurz, Andreas Hüttemann, and Siegfried Jaag, “Ceteris Paribus Laws,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2024/entries/ceteris-paribus/. [Open access]","Jay Odenbaugh and Paul Griffiths, “Philosophy of Biology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2024/entries/biology-philosophy/. [Open access]","Douglas S. Glazier, “Variable Metabolic Scaling Breaks the Law: From ‘Newtonian’ to ‘Darwinian’ Approaches,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 289, no. 1985 (2022): 20221605, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2022.1605. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-020","num":20,"category":"What biology is","question":"A correlation between two measurements may be the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than of any influence between them, which is why biologists reach for an intervention.","questions":["Germ-free mice colonised with another animal's microbiota gained fat while eating less, an intervention worth running because an association can always be produced by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead.","Genetic variants serve as natural experiments because they are fixed at conception, though shared variation can still produce <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of its own between a risk factor and an outcome."],"answer":"confounding","answerText":"confounding","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"<b>Confounding</b> is what most often makes a correlation fail as evidence of cause, some third factor moving both of the things compared so that they rise and fall together with neither driving the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other standard failure is reverse causation, where the outcome alters the supposed cause: people with symptoms of heart disease may drink less alcohol, which makes drinking look protective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One way round both is to use genetic variants as natural experiments, a variant being fixed at conception and so beyond the reach of the disease it is being tested against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is not a clean escape either, since shared genetic variation can itself influence a supposed risk factor and an outcome at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The older biological answer is a sequence rather than a statistic: establish the association, isolate the suspected agent, then show that the agent alone reproduces the effect in something that did not have it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The gut microbiome is where that sequence is being worked through now, its composition having been associated with obesity and with insulin resistance in large cohorts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One such association did not survive sensitivity analyses using twin and sibling-matched data, which is what residual confounding looks like when somebody catches it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The causal step is an intervention, and germ-free mice colonised with the microbiota of normal animals gained adipose tissue and insulin resistance while eating less chow than the animals they were compared with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where the experiment can be run on people it sometimes reverses the expectation, as when beta-carotene given to prevent lung cancer raised the risk instead, at a pooled relative risk of 1.16 across eight trials and 167,141 participants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A correlation is the beginning of a biological question rather than an answer to one, and what settles it is an intervention somebody can actually perform.","sources":["Neil M. Davies, Michael V. Holmes, and George Davey Smith, “Reading Mendelian Randomisation Studies: A Guide, Glossary, and Checklist for Clinicians,” <i>BMJ</i> 362 (2018): k601, https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.k601. [Open access]","Roshni A. Patel, Joshua G. Schraiber, Matt Pennell, and Michael D. Edge, “Observational Epidemiological Studies Can Mitigate Genetic Confounding with Genetic Relatedness Matrices,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 19 (2026): e2533909123, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2533909123. [Open access]","David W. Evans, “How to Gain Evidence for Causation in Disease and Therapeutic Intervention: From Koch's Postulates to Counter-Counterfactuals,” <i>Medicine, Health Care and Philosophy</i> 25, no. 3 (2022): 509–21, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11019-022-10096-x. [Open access]","Connor Prosty, Khaled Katergi, Jesse Papenburg, Alexander Lawandi, Todd C. Lee, et al., “Causal Role of the Gut Microbiome in Certain Human Diseases: A Narrative Review,” <i>eGastroenterology</i> 2, no. 3 (2024): e100086, https://doi.org/10.1136/egastro-2024-100086. [Open access]","Jacek Kordiak, Filip Bielec, Sławomir Jabłoński, and Dorota Pastuszak-Lewandoska, “Role of Beta-Carotene in Lung Cancer Primary Chemoprevention: A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis and Meta-Regression,” <i>Nutrients</i> 14, no. 7 (2022): 1361, https://doi.org/10.3390/nu14071361. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-021","num":21,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur are common to all known life, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what an instrument looks for when testing whether somewhere could be habitable.","questions":["Four of the six elements of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> account for 96.5% of an organism's weight, a composition quite unlike that of the rock the organism is standing on.","Nitrogen limits plankton growth in the subtropical gyres and iron does so in upwelling regions, so even the elements of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are not equally available everywhere."],"answer":"CHNOPS","answerText":"CHNOPS","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>CHNOPS</b> is the set of six elements common to all known life — carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur — and their presence is one of the conditions of habitability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 92 naturally occurring elements, organisms are built from only a small selection, four of which, carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen, make up 96.5% of an organism's weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That composition differs markedly from the abundances in the Earth's crust, which is itself evidence of a distinctive chemistry rather than of life simply using whatever lay to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Carbon supplies the frame, having four electrons and four vacancies in its outer shell, so it forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbon atoms in chains and rings with no obvious upper limit on size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phosphorus shows how specific a job an element can hold: about 0.9 kg (2 pounds) sits in a human body, up to 90% of it as apatite in bones and teeth, the rest largely in the phosphate links of DNA and RNA and in the esters that carry energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The proportions are regular enough at sea to be assumed, and models of the carbon cycle give ocean particles a mean carbon to nitrogen to phosphorus ratio of 106 to 16 to 1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured instead, the ratio shifts from one ocean region to another with how much protein, carbohydrate and lipid the plankton are carrying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which element runs short first shifts as well, nitrogen limiting growth in the stratified subtropical gyres, iron in upwelling regions, and the two together in the water between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Manganese can be limiting alongside iron in parts of the Southern Ocean, so an element present in traces can still be the one that stops everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deciding which elements are genuinely essential is harder than a list of six suggests, since genes do not code for elements but for particular chemical species, an element in a given oxidation state with particular groups around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Karen Donaldson, Jonah Mack, Yuchen Shang, Ian Underwood, and Charles Cockell, “The Design of a Bioinspired Integrated Total Habitability Instrument for Planetary Exploration,” <i>Biomimetics</i> 10, no. 11 (2025): 742, https://doi.org/10.3390/biomimetics10110742. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Prinessa Chellan and Peter J. Sadler, “The Elements of Life and Medicines,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 373, no. 2037 (2015): 20140182, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2014.0182. [Open access]","Justin D. Liefer, Angelicque E. White, Zoe V. Finkel, Andrew J. Irwin, et al., “Latitudinal Patterns in Ocean C:N:P Reflect Phytoplankton Acclimation and Macromolecular Composition,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 46 (2024): e2404460121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2404460121. [Open access]","Thomas J. Browning and C. Mark Moore, “Global Analysis of Ocean Phytoplankton Nutrient Limitation Reveals High Prevalence of Co-limitation,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 14 (2023): 5014, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-40774-0. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-022","num":22,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Because the character of a cell's materials depends on how its atoms are linked rather than on which elements are present, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the working unit of biological chemistry.","questions":["A cubic micrometre of bacterium or yeast is estimated to hold two to four million proteins, which is the order of how many of one kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a cell carries.","One carbon atom is about 0.2 nm across, and a cluster of such atoms joined by covalent bonds is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose mass is quoted in daltons."],"answer":"molecule","answerText":"molecule","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>molecule</b> is a cluster of atoms held together by covalent bonds, and it is where the materials a cell is built from take on their character, which depends on how the atoms are linked rather than on which elements are present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An atom is a nucleus of protons and neutrons surrounded by electrons; the proton count is the atomic number and fixes the chemistry, while the neutrons may vary, giving isotopes that are physically distinguishable and chemically identical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A carbon atom is roughly 0.2 nm across, so about 5 million of them in a line would span a millimetre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Masses are quoted in daltons, and quantities are counted in moles, one mole being Avogadro's number of units, a figure that is now exact by definition at 6.02214076 × 10<sup>23</sup> per mole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The small organic molecules of a cell have molecular weights between 100 and 1000 and carry up to about 30 carbon atoms, sitting free in solution as subunits for larger molecules, as fuel, or as both at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A cell is water, inorganic ions and carbon-containing molecules, with water the most abundant of the three at 70% or more of the total mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counting the rest is harder than it sounds, and an estimate built from the protein fraction of cell mass and the average length of a protein gives 2 to 4 million protein molecules in a cubic micrometre of bacterium, yeast or mammalian cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured proteome censuses of yeast and mammalian cells often come out 3 to 10 times lower than that, a discrepancy the estimate was published to expose rather than to settle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Numbers of this kind are scattered so widely that finding even a simple one takes a long time, which is why they are gathered into a curated database with each figure's source attached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Counting is the point: a cell is a definite number of definite objects, and the chemistry becomes biology when somebody says how many.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","National Institute of Standards and Technology, “Avogadro Constant,” CODATA Internationally Recommended 2022 Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants, https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?na. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Ron Milo, “What Is the Total Number of Protein Molecules per Cell Volume? A Call to Rethink Some Published Values,” <i>BioEssays</i> 35, no. 12 (2013): 1050–55, https://doi.org/10.1002/bies.201300066. [Open access]","Ron Milo, Paul Jorgensen, Uri Moran, Griffin Weber, and Michael Springer, “BioNumbers—the Database of Key Numbers in Molecular and Cell Biology,” <i>Nucleic Acids Research</i> 38, suppl. 1 (2010): D750–53, https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkp889. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-023","num":23,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"The energy needed to pull two atoms apart is what defines the strength of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that number decides whether heat alone can undo it inside a cell.","questions":["Streptavidin holds biotin at a dissociation constant near 10 femtomolar without a single covalent link, which shows how far a crowd of weak examples of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can go.","A cell keeps its structures because one kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is about a hundred times stronger than the knocks molecules take from their neighbours."],"answer":"chemical bond","answerText":"chemical bond","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>chemical bond</b> is an attraction holding atoms together, and its strength is measured by the energy that must be supplied to break it, conventionally quoted in kilocalories per mole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That figure is what decides how the bond behaves inside a cell, since a typical covalent bond is about a hundred times stronger than the thermal knocks molecules take from their neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Covalent bonds are therefore not undone by heat but by particular chemical reactions, which in cells are controlled by highly specific catalysts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Noncovalent bonds are as a rule much weaker, and that weakness is the point, since a great deal of biology consists of molecules that must associate and come apart again readily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their strength also depends on where they are: water forms hydrogen bonds with polar molecules and surrounds ions, which is why ionic compounds and polar molecules dissolve in it while covalent bond strengths are unaffected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The weak ones matter because they add up, and large numbers of hydrogen bonds between adjacent regions of a folded protein chain help to hold its three-dimensional shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A crowd of them can be formidable: streptavidin binds the small molecule biotin at a dissociation constant of about 10<sup>−14</sup> M, one of the strongest noncovalent interactions known, with no covalent link involved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In DNA the same arithmetic is predictive rather than descriptive, the stability of a double helix being calculated from its sequence by adding up the contributions of neighbouring base pairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One such set of parameters, derived from 108 short duplexes, reconciled seven laboratories whose published values had appeared to disagree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bond strength is not a chemical footnote but the reason a cell can be stable and quick at once: covalent where nothing should change, weak and numerous where things must.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Ian D. Wadsworth, Komal Sethi, Sara Abu Jalboush, LeAnn Rogers, Thomas Hollis, and Adam R. Hall, “Scalable Production of Monovalent Streptavidin with High Yield and Purity,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 20814, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-51631-7. [Open access]","John SantaLucia, “A Unified View of Polymer, Dumbbell, and Oligonucleotide DNA Nearest-Neighbor Thermodynamics,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 95, no. 4 (1998): 1460–65, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.95.4.1460. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-024","num":24,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Two atoms that complete their outer shells by sharing a pair of electrons rather than transferring them are joined by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the strongest link a cell builds with.","questions":["A single bond lets one part of a molecule rotate about it while a double one does not, so the rigidity of a molecule is set by which kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it carries.","Penicillin works by leaving a permanent chemical adduct on its target, which is to say that the drug forms a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the enzyme it disables."],"answer":"covalent bond","answerText":"covalent bond","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>covalent bond</b> is formed when two atoms complete their outer electron shells by sharing a pair of electrons rather than by transferring them from one to the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sharing is often unequal, one atom drawing the pair towards itself, and the result is a polar covalent bond that carries a partial charge at each end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two atoms may share more than one pair, and a double bond is shorter and stronger than a single one and, unlike a single bond, prevents the two parts of the molecule from rotating about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carbon's four bonds point towards the corners of a regular tetrahedron, which is where the three-dimensional geometry of organic molecules comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a protein each amino acid is joined to the next by a covalent peptide bond, formed as a molecule of water is lost between them, and the repeating chain of atoms that results is the backbone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Some proteins carry covalent cross-links as well, and the commonest are sulfur-sulfur bonds made as a protein is prepared for export, which act as staples reinforcing a shape the chain has already found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peptide bond is planar, and quantum-chemical work attributes the tight backbone hydrogen-bond angle of about 155 degrees to the distribution of electrons around that plane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Planarity is an approximation rather than a law, and a neutron structure at 1.2 Å resolution shows the amide protons sitting off the plane, shifted towards the atoms they hydrogen-bond to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Covalent bonding is also how a large family of drugs works, since penicillin and the other beta-lactams disable their target by forming a covalent adduct with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same property is the risk: a reactive drug can form adducts with proteins it was never aimed at, which is why covalent medicines are watched for side effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Kuan Pern Tan, Khushboo Singh, Anirban Hazra, and M. S. Madhusudhan, “Peptide Bond Planarity Constrains Hydrogen Bond Geometry and Influences Secondary Structure Conformations,” <i>Current Research in Structural Biology</i> 3 (2021): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crstbi.2020.11.002. [Open access]","Yuya Hanazono, Yu Hirano, Kazuki Takeda, Katsuhiro Kusaka, Taro Tamada, and Kunio Miki, “Revisiting the Concept of Peptide Bond Planarity in an Iron-Sulfur Protein by Neutron Structure Analysis,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 20 (2022): eabn2276, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abn2276. [Open access]","Ghazaleh Jafari and Dustin Duncan, “Covalent Inhibitors in Antimicrobial Drug Development—Beyond β-Lactams,” <i>Molecules</i> 31, no. 12 (2026): 2186, https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules31122186. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-025","num":25,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Because one atom hands electrons to another instead of sharing them, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leaves two ions of opposite charge attracting each other across the gap.","questions":["Sodium chloride is called a salt rather than a molecule because its crystal is a balanced lattice of ions held by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a cluster of shared electrons.","An ion in water travels with a shell of water molecules around it, which is why an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is far weaker in a cell than in a dry crystal."],"answer":"ionic bond","answerText":"ionic bond","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"An <b>ionic bond</b> forms when one atom transfers electrons to another rather than sharing them, leaving two ions of opposite charge that attract one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A solid held together this way is a lattice rather than a molecule, which is why sodium chloride is called a salt: a crystal a millimetre across holds about 2 × 10<sup>19</sup> ions of each kind in a regular array with the charges exactly balanced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water takes such a solid apart, since ionic bonds are greatly weakened in it and each ion leaves surrounded by its own group of water molecules, while covalent bond strengths are untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a fact about water rather than about the salt, water molecules being polar and able to interact with charged ions as well as with other polar molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside a protein the same attraction turns up as one of the three kinds of weak bond that fold the chain, each 30 to 300 times weaker than a covalent bond and useful only in numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because an ion in water carries a shell of water with it, moving one across a membrane means stripping that shell away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ion channels do exactly that, a permeating ion shedding most or all of its water to pass the narrowest part of the channel, which is what makes the channel selective and puts a ceiling on its rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The gradients that result are a cell's electrical state, and in animal cells the membrane potential rests mainly on potassium leak channels and the potassium gradient across the plasma membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ions also hold larger molecules in shape, magnesium being the clearest case, since it defines much of the structural and catalytic chemistry of RNA and binds either directly or through its own shell of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An ionic bond is therefore weak, wet and everywhere, which is the opposite of the covalent bond's role in a cell and the reason both are needed.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Ion Channels and the Electrical Properties of Membranes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/. [Open access]","J. A. Cowan, “Understanding the Thermodynamics of Magnesium Binding to RNA Structural Motifs,” <i>Life</i> 14, no. 6 (2024): 765, https://doi.org/10.3390/life14060765. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-026","num":26,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Water is a liquid at room temperature only because each molecule is drawn to its neighbours by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a link so weak that thermal motion breaks it constantly.","questions":["The alpha helix and the beta sheet form from links between the N-H and C=O groups of a protein's backbone alone, so this kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be made by almost any sequence.","A always pairs with T and G with C because a purine and a pyrimidine of matching width can be joined across the helix by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at each contact."],"answer":"hydrogen bond","answerText":"hydrogen bond","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>hydrogen bond</b> is the weak attraction between a hydrogen atom already bound to an electron-hungry atom and another such atom nearby, as when one of water's hydrogens is drawn towards a neighbouring molecule's oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taken singly they are feeble and are broken by ordinary thermal motion, so each lasts an exceedingly short time and liquid water is a network of them continually breaking and re-forming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aggregate effect is not small: it is only because of these bonds between its molecules that water is a liquid at room temperature, with a high boiling point and high surface tension, rather than a gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are at their strongest when the three atoms involved lie on a straight line, and water weakens hydrogen bonds between dissolved molecules by offering competing partners of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In proteins the two commonest folding patterns, the alpha helix and the beta sheet, arise from hydrogen bonds between the N-H and C=O groups of the backbone alone, which is why many different amino acid sequences can form them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The geometry is measured rather than assumed: across a large body of solved structures the backbone-to-backbone bonds sit at a tightly restricted angle near 155 degrees, and the donor-acceptor distance varies with how deeply the pair is buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In DNA the two chains are held to each other by hydrogen bonds between the bases, a bulky two-ring purine always facing a single-ring pyrimidine, with A opposite T and G opposite C.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is what lets the double helix be regular, since every pair is the same width and the sugar-phosphate backbones can run outside the stack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The arrangement is quantitative as well as neat, a duplex's stability being computed from its sequence by summing neighbouring pairs, which is why one stretch of DNA comes apart before another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A bond that cannot hold anything on its own turns out to hold water together, fold proteins and carry the genetic code, entirely by being made in numbers.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Kuan Pern Tan, Khushboo Singh, Anirban Hazra, and M. S. Madhusudhan, “Peptide Bond Planarity Constrains Hydrogen Bond Geometry and Influences Secondary Structure Conformations,” <i>Current Research in Structural Biology</i> 3 (2021): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crstbi.2020.11.002. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Structure and Function of DNA,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26821/. [Open access]","John SantaLucia, “A Unified View of Polymer, Dumbbell, and Oligonucleotide DNA Nearest-Neighbor Thermodynamics,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 95, no. 4 (1998): 1460–65, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.95.4.1460. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-027","num":27,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"About 70% of a cell's weight is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and nearly every reaction inside one takes place dissolved in it rather than dry.","questions":["Nonpolar side chains pack into a protein's interior because they disturb the hydrogen-bonded network of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around them, which is what folds much of the chain.","A tardigrade in anhydrobiosis survives losing almost all its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the longer it stays in that state the longer it takes to revive."],"answer":"water","answerText":"water","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Water</b> is the most abundant substance in a cell, accounting for about 70% of its weight, and most reactions inside a cell take place in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell is water, inorganic ions and carbon-containing molecules, and a great deal of biological chemistry is simply the dealings between the first of those and the other two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The molecule is polar, its hydrogens carrying a slight positive charge and its oxygen a slight negative one, so it hydrogen-bonds to itself and interacts with ions and other polar molecules, which is why they dissolve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Life began in the ocean, and those conditions left a permanent mark on the chemistry of living things; water is also what pushes nonpolar molecules together, since they disturb its hydrogen-bonded network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A folded protein shows the result directly, its nonpolar side chains packed away in the interior while the polar ones face outward where they can meet water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That exclusion does mechanical work as well as structural, since elastin, the protein that lets an artery or a lung spring back, recoils because of the hydrophobic effect, with the ordering of water at its surface changing as the protein is stretched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The scale of that job is the point: arterial elastin goes through more than two billion stretch-and-release cycles in a human lifetime without ever being replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because so much rests on water, losing it is normally fatal, yet some small animals survive almost complete dehydration without damage, a state called anhydrobiosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among tardigrades that capacity differs between species and appears to follow the habitat rather than the diet, and the longer an animal has spent dried out, the longer it needs to come back to activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Water is therefore not the setting biology happens in but a participant in it, which is why so much of the chemistry ahead is really chemistry in water.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Nour M. Jamhawi, Ronald L. Koder, and Richard J. Wittebort, “Elastin Recoil Is Driven by the Hydrophobic Effect,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 12 (2024): e2304009121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2304009121. [Open access]","Milena Roszkowska, Bartłomiej Gołdyn, Daria Wojciechowska, Zofia Księżkiewicz, et al., “How Long Can Tardigrades Survive in the Anhydrobiotic State? A Search for Tardigrade Anhydrobiosis Patterns,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): e0270386, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0270386. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-028","num":28,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Oxygen pulls hard on the shared electrons and hydrogen pulls weakly, which makes water a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with negative charge at one end and positive at the other.","questions":["A membrane lipid has a water-loving head and water-fearing tails, so half of it behaves as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and half does not, which is why a bilayer assembles itself.","Proteins find their partners by matching patterns of partial charge across a surface, and that pattern exists only because each group in it either is or is not a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> itself."],"answer":"polar molecule","answerText":"polar molecule","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>polar molecule</b> is one whose charge is spread unevenly, gathered positive towards one end and negative towards the other, and water is the standard case, since its oxygen attracts the shared electrons strongly while its hydrogens attract them weakly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rule is worth carrying about: an O-H or an N-H bond is polar, while a C-H bond is not, carbon and hydrogen attracting the shared pair about equally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polar covalent bonds create permanent dipoles, so a large molecule carrying many polar groups has a pattern of partial positive and negative charges spread over its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two molecules whose patterns are complementary attract one another through those dipoles, by interactions that resemble ionic bonds and are weaker than them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The property sorts molecules into two kinds, since polar ones interact readily with water and dissolve in it while nonpolar ones do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Membrane lipids are deliberately both at once, each one amphipathic, with a polar head group and two hydrophobic hydrocarbon tails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Put in water they arrange themselves, burying the tails and turning the heads outward, which is why a bilayer forms spontaneously and why about 5 million lipid molecules sit in a single square micrometre of one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Patterns of surface charge are also how proteins find each other, and electrostatic complementarity across an interface has been shown to drive the brief encounters that much of cell signalling depends on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What such complementarity is worth can be measured rather than assumed, and in one enzyme's active site it accounts for at most a modest 300-fold of the catalysis, even though the hydrogen bonds shorten as charge builds up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polarity is a small asymmetry inside one molecule, and most of what water does to biology follows from it.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Lipid Bilayer,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/. [Open access]","Greta Grassmann, Lorenzo Di Rienzo, Giorgio Gosti, Marco Leonetti, Giancarlo Ruocco, Mattia Miotto, and Edoardo Milanetti, “Electrostatic Complementarity at the Interface Drives Transient Protein-Protein Interactions,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 10207, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-37130-z. [Open access]","Daniel A. Kraut, Paul A. Sigala, Brandon Pybus, Corey W. Liu, Dagmar Ringe, Gregory A. Petsko, and Daniel Herschlag, “Testing Electrostatic Complementarity in Enzyme Catalysis: Hydrogen Bonding in the Ketosteroid Isomerase Oxyanion Hole,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 4, no. 4 (2006): e99, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0040099. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-029","num":29,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Water sticking to itself is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and water sticking to a surface is adhesion, the pair of them holding a column of sap together in a tree.","questions":["A water strider stands on a film it cannot break, which exists because the hydrogen bonds between water molecules give the liquid the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that produces surface tension.","Sap in a tree is pulled rather than pushed, so the column depends on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and fails when drought raises the tension enough to admit a gas bubble."],"answer":"cohesion","answerText":"cohesion","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Cohesion</b> is the tendency of a liquid to hold to itself, and in water it comes from the hydrogen bonds between neighbouring molecules, which is why water is a liquid at room temperature with a high boiling point and a high surface tension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Adhesion is the same attraction pointed outwards, water forming hydrogen bonds with other polar molecules and interacting with ions, so it clings to surfaces built from either rather than beading away from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pair produce capillarity, the rise of a liquid into a narrow space, though the word covers more than that, standing for all the physical consequences of the thin skin that separates water from air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those consequences dominate at small scales, governing the transport of water inside plants, the dispersal of fungal spores, the water-repellency of arthropods and the fate of bacteria and viruses in respiratory drops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A water strider lives on that film, its legs and its turning gait built around a surface it can push against without breaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Cohesion is also what allows a tree to lift water, since sap in the xylem is pulled rather than pushed and travels under tension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A column held that way can fail, and under drought the rising tension lets gas emboli form and spread through the conduits, blocking them and in the worst case killing the tissue they feed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How much tension a species tolerates is measured as the water potential at which half its conduits have embolised, and across 22 eucalypts that resistance trades off against how fast the plant grows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The faster growers had wider vessels, better at conducting and worse at holding on, while the slower ones had narrower vessels with thicker walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two ordinary stickinesses therefore set both what small animals can walk on and how tall a tree can afford to be.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","David L. Hu, Ho-Young Kim, and Dominic Vella, “Editorial: Capillarity and Elastocapillarity in Biology,” <i>Interface Focus</i> 15, no. 2 (2025): 20250020, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsfs.2025.0020. [Open access]","Javad Meshkani, Hamed Rajabi, Alexander Kovalev, and Stanislav N. Gorb, “Kinematics and Directionality of Body Turning in Water Striders,” <i>Insect Science</i> 33, no. 1 (2026): 298–312, https://doi.org/10.1111/1744-7917.13486. [Open access]","Tiantian Pan, Vinod Jacob, Travis G. Britton, Julian Schrader, et al., “Evidence for a Trade-off between Growth Rate and Xylem Embolism Resistance in 22 <i>Eucalyptus</i> Species,” <i>New Phytologist</i> 251, no. 5 (2026): 2407–26, https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.71345. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-030","num":30,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Because much of the energy water absorbs goes into rearranging its hydrogen bonds, its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is high and a large heat input changes its temperature very little.","questions":["Flight raised the internal temperature of a dove by only six tenths of a degree, which is what a body mostly made of a liquid with a high <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> does with waste heat.","The ocean has absorbed most of the planet's extra heat, and the reason it can is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the seawater rather than anything about the water's depth."],"answer":"specific heat capacity","answerText":"specific heat capacity","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"The <b>specific heat capacity</b> of a substance is the energy needed to raise the temperature of a given mass of it, and water's is high, so large amounts of absorbed energy produce only small changes in how hot it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason is the hydrogen-bond network, since much of the energy goes into rearranging that before any of it appears as the motion of the molecules themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The magnitude is easy to hold onto, the kilocalorie having been defined as the energy needed to raise one litre of water by one degree centigrade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The effect operates at every scale at once, buffering a cell against the heat it makes, steadying the temperature of a pond or a lake, and moderating the seasons of a planet mostly covered in ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A working animal shows the small version of it, since in ringed turtle-doves a bout of flight raised internal body temperature by only 0.6°C (1.1°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Buffering is not the same as escaping the problem, because the heat still has to leave in the end, and a larger animal needs longer to shed what its muscles produce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The consequence is visible in how animals move, the travel speeds of the largest species falling short of what their energy budgets alone would allow, because the capacity to dissipate heat gives out first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the far end of the scale the ocean is where most of the planet's additional heat has gone, and its heat content is now tracked from year to year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 2022 the upper 2,000 metres (6,600 feet) held 10.9 ± 8.3 zettajoules more heat than in 2021, a record, with four basins at their highest since the 1950s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One property therefore keeps a cell from cooking itself and makes the ocean the planet's memory for heat.","sources":["Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Kristen E. Crandell, Donald R. Powers, and Bret W. Tobalske, “The Role of Plumage and Heat Dissipation Areas in Thermoregulation in Doves,” <i>Journal of Experimental Biology</i> 228, no. 4 (2025): jeb248200, https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.248200. [Open access]","Alexander Dyer, Ulrich Brose, Emilio Berti, Benjamin Rosenbaum, and Myriam R. Hirt, “The Travel Speeds of Large Animals Are Limited by Their Heat-Dissipation Capacity,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 21, no. 4 (2023): e3001820, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3001820. [Open access]","Lijing Cheng, John Abraham, Kevin E. Trenberth, John Fasullo, Tim Boyer, Michael E. Mann, et al., “Another Year of Record Heat for the Oceans,” <i>Advances in Atmospheric Sciences</i> 40, no. 6 (2023): 963–74, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00376-023-2385-2. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-031","num":31,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Water is biology's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taking up ions and polar molecules readily while pushing nonpolar ones together and leaving them out.","questions":["A row of negatively charged phosphates could not sit along a DNA backbone at all if the surrounding <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not weaken the repulsion between them.","Zooplankton in an oxygen minimum zone respond to changes of one per cent or less, because oxygen is a gas held in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that carries very little of it."],"answer":"solvent","answerText":"solvent","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>solvent</b> is a liquid that other substances dissolve in, and water is biology's, taking up ions and polar molecules readily while leaving nonpolar ones out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A salt goes into solution because the ionic bonds holding its lattice together are greatly weakened in water, each ion departing surrounded by its own group of water molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figure behind that is water's dielectric constant, about 80 at room temperature, which greatly attenuates the electrical attraction between charges and lets electrolytes dissociate and stay apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same screening lowers the cost of holding like charges close together, which is what allows the row of negatively charged phosphates along a nucleic acid backbone to exist at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What water will not dissolve matters as much, since nonpolar molecules disturb its hydrogen-bonded network and are pushed together rather than taken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Water is a participant as well as a medium, since it solvates nucleophiles, electrophiles and charged intermediates, lowering activation barriers and making reactions run that otherwise would not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The solution inside a cell is also nothing like dilute, an estimate built from cell composition giving 2 to 4 million protein molecules in a cubic micrometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Oxygen is the hard case, since large regions of the middle ocean hold very little of it, and those oxygen minimum zones are expected to spread as the climate warms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Animals there live near their physiological limits, and in the eastern tropical North Pacific many zooplankton groups shift their distribution in response to changes in oxygen of 1% or less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Being a good solvent is therefore a specification rather than a virtue, and life is built around the particular one water happens to be.","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Ron Milo, “What Is the Total Number of Protein Molecules per Cell Volume? A Call to Rethink Some Published Values,” <i>BioEssays</i> 35, no. 12 (2013): 1050–55, https://doi.org/10.1002/bies.201300066. [Open access]","K. F. Wishner, B. A. Seibel, C. Roman, C. Deutsch, et al., “Ocean Deoxygenation and Zooplankton: Very Small Oxygen Differences Matter,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaau5180, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aau5180. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-032","num":32,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Water is one of the few substances that expands as it freezes, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> floats on the liquid it came from instead of sinking through it.","questions":["A lake in winter stays liquid underneath because the layer of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on top is lighter than the water below and insulates it from the air.","Fishes in polar seas carry proteins that bind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and stop the crystals growing, which lowers the temperature at which their tissue freezes."],"answer":"ice","answerText":"ice","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Ice</b> is the solid form of water, and water is one of the few substances that expands as it freezes, the molar volume of the liquid being about 8% less than that of the solid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The liquid is densest at about 4°C (39°F) and swells again as it cools from there towards freezing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason is geometric, since each molecule can hydrogen-bond to its neighbours in a network,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and as the temperature falls from 4 to 0°C the ideal tetrahedral arrangements become more probable and longer-lived, holding molecules further apart than the less regular packing above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The solid therefore forms on top rather than at the bottom, and that layer, with water's high heat capacity, insulates what lies beneath and keeps it liquid while the air above is below freezing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life carries on underneath on that account, and the same substance is odd under pressure too, its melting point falling rather than rising, which is what lets a glacier slide over its bed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Freezing is still dangerous to a cell, and animals that meet it have evolved proteins that bind ice crystals, inhibit their growth and lower the temperature at which tissue freezes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four kinds of antifreeze protein have evolved separately in fishes, which is what has allowed teleosts to colonise polar seas again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The trick is not theirs alone, since winter-active spiders carry an ice-binding protein that halts crystal growth at −4°C (25°F) and is folded unlike any of the fish types, so it arose on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A plant cannot move away from it, so what matters there is where the ice forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Outside the cells it can even protect them, drawing water out and concentrating what remains until the inside freezes at a lower temperature, though prolonged drying of that kind ruptures the membrane instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","S. N. Bogan, N. Surendran, S. Hotaling, T. Desvignes, et al., “Temperature and Pressure Shaped the Evolution of Antifreeze Proteins in Polar and Deep Sea Zoarcoid Fishes,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 42, no. 10 (2025): msaf219, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msaf219. [Open access]","Laurie A. Graham, Stano Pekár, Ina M. Hainer, and Peter L. Davies, “Winter-Active Spiders (<i>Clubiona</i>) Have a Hyperactive Antifreeze Protein,” <i>The FEBS Journal</i> 293, no. 8 (2026): 2270–90, https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.70323. [Open access]","Lia Lamacque, Nicolas Dusart, Pierre Amato, Katline Charra-Vaskou, et al., “Ice Nucleation and Freezing Consequences in Perennial Plants,” <i>Physiologia Plantarum</i> 178, no. 3 (2026): e70961, https://doi.org/10.1111/ppl.70961. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-033","num":33,"category":"Biology","question":"A substance that gives up protons when it dissolves in water, each one joining a water molecule to make a hydronium ion, is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a base is what accepts them instead.","questions":["The vertebrate stomach shows how much <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a body will make on purpose, obligate scavengers holding theirs near pH 1.3 while foregut herbivores sit closer to 6.1.","Aspartate and glutamate carry carboxyl groups that hand a proton away inside the cell, which makes each of them an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and leaves its side chain negatively charged."],"answer":"acid","answerText":"acid","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"An <b>acid</b> is a substance that releases protons when it dissolves in water, each bare hydrogen nucleus attaching itself to a water molecule to make a hydronium ion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A base does the reverse, raising the concentration of hydroxyl ions, which are formed by removing a proton from a water molecule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water is faintly both, since a proton can leave one water molecule for another, so pure water carries equal traces of hydronium and hydroxyl and sits at the middle of the logarithmic pH scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inside of a cell is held close to that neutral point, which is one of the conditions its chemistry is built around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Acids and bases are ordinary furniture there: the carboxyl groups of aspartate and glutamate give a proton away and carry a negative charge, while the side chains of lysine and arginine take one on and carry a positive one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Away from that guarded interior, animals make acid deliberately, and the vertebrate stomach is the clearest case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across mammals and birds the acidity of the stomach tracks diet rather than size: obligate scavengers average about pH 1.3, generalist carnivores 2.2, omnivores 2.9 and foregut herbivores 6.1, with the human stomach near 1.5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The suggestion is that the stomach works as an ecological filter, killing most of what arrives before it can settle further down the gut, so the animals eating the dirtiest food keep the fiercest acid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A few organisms live in acid rather than making it, among them <i>Oxyplasma meridianum</i>, an archaeon taken from a rock on the Italian island of Vulcano, which grows between pH 0.5 and 4.0 and does best at 1.0; relatives in its order endure below zero on the scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Small shifts tell as well as large ones: <i>Pocillopora</i> corals in the southern Mexican Pacific lost 28.6 per cent of their skeletal density over 23 years, a decline reported as running alongside falling seawater pH and carbonate saturation rather than proved to be caused by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","DeAnna E. Beasley, Amanda M. Koltz, Joanna E. Lambert, Noah Fierer, and Rob R. Dunn, “The Evolution of Stomach Acidity and Its Relevance to the Human Microbiome,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 7 (2015): e0134116, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0134116. [Open access]","Olga V. Golyshina, Evgenii A. Lunev, Marco A. Distaso, Rafael Bargiela, Matthew C. Gaines, Bertram Daum, Manuel Ferrer, et al., “Oxyplasma meridianum gen. nov., sp. nov., an Extremely Acidophilic Organotrophic Member of the Order Thermoplasmatales,” <i>International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology</i> 74, no. 8 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1099/ijsem.0.006499. [Open access]","Andrés López-Pérez, Omar Jiménez-Gutiérrez, Héctor Reyes-Bonilla, Rafael A. Cabral-Tena, Gerardo Leyte-Morales, Francisco Medellín-Maldonado, et al., “Two Decades of Skeletal Density Decline in Pocillopora spp. Corals in the Mexican Pacific Ocean: Insight into a Tropical Eastern Pacific Acidification Scenario?” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 2 (2026): e0342741, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0342741. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-034","num":34,"category":"Biology","question":"On the logarithmic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> scale a solution at 5 carries a hundred times more protons than one at 7, so a shift of a single unit is never a small change.","questions":["A lysosome digests what a cell takes in at about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 5, two units below the cytosol around it, and its enzymes fall idle if they ever leak into that neutral surrounding.","<i>Sulfolobus acidocaldarius</i> grows best at a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 2 and 3 while a soda-lake <i>Bacillus</i> prefers 8.5 to 11.5, each carrying enzymes whose optima match its own water."],"answer":"pH","answerText":"pH","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>pH</b> measures how many protons a solution carries, on a logarithmic scale that fits an enormous range of concentrations into a handful of numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pure water sits at 7, where hydronium and hydroxyl ions are equally common; below that a solution is acidic and above it basic, and the inside of a cell is held close to that neutral point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The logarithm is the substance of the scale rather than a convenience, since each unit stands for a tenfold change in the concentration of protons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lysosome, the compartment in which a cell digests what it has taken in, runs near pH 5 against a cytosol of about 7.2, and a pump in its membrane keeps roughly a hundredfold difference across a single membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its enzymes are built to work at that acidity and go idle at the cytosol's, so a leaking lysosome does not digest the cell around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Newer work puts the lysosome lower still, near 4.5, and finds that it is not filled from the cytosol alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In yeast and in human cells the compartment also takes up protons pumped out by mitochondria across direct membrane contacts, and ageing breaks those contacts and lets the lysosome slip towards neutral, a loss an engineered linker between the two organelles can reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whole organisms are built around their own values: <i>Sulfolobus acidocaldarius</i>, from a Yellowstone spring measured at 2.4, grows best between 2 and 3, while a <i>Bacillus</i> from a soda lake in Inner Mongolia grows between 8.5 and 11.5, each carrying enzymes whose optima match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Human tissue can run the gradient backwards: in many tumours the fluid outside the cells turns acidic while the cytosol stays in a narrow alkaline band near 7.2 to 7.4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That reversal is now read as a feature of malignant progression rather than a by-product of it, one that contributes to invasion, immune evasion and resistance to treatment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Lysosomes,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9953/. [Open access]","Qingqing Liu, Seungmin Yoo, Zhixin A. Zhang, Liying Li, Hetian Su, Lingraj Vannur, Alexandra C. Wooldredge, et al., “Mitochondria-Lysosome Coupling Contributes to Lysosome Acidification and Aging,” <i>Molecular Cell</i> 86, no. 12 (2026): 2425–42, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.molcel.2026.05.004. [Open access]","Peng Zhou, Zu-Xin Zhang, Xiaoyong Pan, Xinfeng Dai, and Qiaoli Chen, “Leveraging AI-Driven Predictors of Enzyme pH Optima to Unravel Microbial Adaptation to Environmental pH,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 17 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2026.1788098. [Open access]","Fangquan Chen, Xiutao Cai, Qile Zhou, Zhen Huang, Jie Wang, Rui Kang, Daolin Tang, and Jiao Liu, “Compartmental pH Regulation in Cancer and Antitumor Immunity: Therapeutic Opportunities and Challenges,” <i>Apoptosis</i> 31, no. 8 (2026): 197, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10495-026-02408-w. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-035","num":35,"category":"Biology","question":"Because a mixture of a weak acid and the base it becomes soaks up added protons and replaces lost ones, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds the pH of a solution nearly steady while its capacity lasts.","questions":["Human blood is kept between 7.35 and 7.45 by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built from carbon dioxide, carbonic acid and bicarbonate, with phosphate, proteins and haemoglobin doing the same work beside it.","The Revelle factor measures how strong a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the ocean's carbonate system makes, a high value meaning a weak one, so that the same added carbon dioxide moves the pH further."],"answer":"buffer","answerText":"buffer","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>buffer</b> is a mixture that resists a change in pH, holding a weak acid together with the base it turns into, so that added protons are taken up and lost ones replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Blood is the clearest case: carbon dioxide from respiration joins water to make carbonic acid, which dissociates into bicarbonate and a proton, and the reaction stands in equilibrium, shifting whichever way its surroundings demand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Human blood is held between 7.35 and 7.45 and averages 7.40, a value below that band being an acidemia and one above it an alkalemia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bicarbonate is not the only system at work, since phosphate, the body's proteins and haemoglobin all buffer as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Something of the kind underlies a cell's near-neutral interior, because metabolism makes acid continuously and neutrality is not a resting state that comes for free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside muscle much of the work is done by particular molecules rather than dissolved salts, carnosine being the most abundant histidine dipeptide there and known chiefly for its pH buffering alongside antioxidant and metal-binding roles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sea is buffered by its carbonate system, and how strongly is measured by the Revelle factor, a high value meaning weak buffering, so that a given amount of added carbon dioxide moves the pH further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Weak buffering does not settle the outcome on its own: in the least ventilated and most poorly buffered oxygen-minimum waters acidification turns out to be negligible, little industrial carbon dioxide having reached them, while it is plain in the moderately ventilated water above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A buffer can also be thought of as a reserve that is spent, and one recent model treats the ageing body's declining capacity to buffer acid, excrete it through the kidney and breathe it off as a hidden erosion behind frailty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether that model holds is not settled, but its shape is the general point: a buffer does not abolish a problem, it holds a value steady until the reserve behind it is spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Erin Hopkins, Terrence Sanvictores, and Sandeep Sharma, “Physiology, Acid Base Balance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2022), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507807/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Belisa Parmeggiani, Bruna Klippel Ferreira, Patricia Fernanda Schuck, and Gustavo Costa Ferreira, “Considerations for Carnosine Actions in Biology,” <i>Neurochemical Research</i> 51, no. 2 (2026): 126, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11064-026-04735-5. [Open access]","Liang Xue, Christopher Sabine, Jianfang Chen, Siv K. Lauvset, Qinsheng Wei, Kuiping Li, and Wei-Jun Cai, “Ventilation and Buffering Capacity Effects on Ocean Acidification in Low Oxygen Environments,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2025): 228, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-67807-0. [Open access]","Wan-Hui Liao, Wolfgang Langhans, and Maciej Henneberg, “Acid–Base Dysregulation Links Aging Metabolism to Frailty,” <i>Aging Cell</i> 25, no. 4 (2026): e70466, https://doi.org/10.1111/acel.70466. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-036","num":36,"category":"Biology","question":"The chemistry of carbon compounds is called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its whole subject rests on an atom that makes four covalent bonds and links to its own kind in chains and rings.","questions":["Because carbon links to itself with no obvious limit, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counts its possible molecules in the billions — 166.4 billion for structures of no more than seventeen heavy atoms.","All five canonical nucleobases turned up in samples of the asteroid Bennu, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no longer taken to mean the chemistry of things that are alive."],"answer":"organic chemistry","answerText":"organic chemistry","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Organic chemistry</b> is the chemistry of carbon compounds, and its whole subject rests on one atom's habits: carbon forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbon atoms through stable carbon-carbon bonds to make chains and rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no obvious upper limit to the size of what follows, so the same element frames a sugar of six carbons and a protein of many thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Perhaps a thousand kinds of small carbon compound are present in a typical cell, and these together with the large ones are what a biologist means by organic molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside a cell they fall into four families — carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids — each assembled from a small set of repeating parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That the frame is carbon rather than something else has been tested rather than assumed: a systematic assessment of silicon, the obvious alternative, finds no planetary environment in which a life built primarily on silicon chemistry is plausible, since in water its capacity is sharply limited by the ubiquitous formation of silica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The reach of the chemistry is hard to overstate: a database enumerating every possible molecule of no more than seventeen atoms of carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, sulfur and the halogens holds 166.4 billion of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That counts what the rules of bonding allow rather than what exists, and the largest catalogue of compounds thought readily synthesisable holds 13.6 billion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word organic no longer carries its old sense of something made by life, and returned asteroid samples are where that is plainest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> All five canonical nucleobases have been found in material brought back from the asteroid Bennu, evidence that some of life's ingredients were put together without life in that asteroid's parent body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The detail matters as much as the headline: pyrimidines outnumber purines in the Bennu sample, as they do in the Orgueil meteorite but unlike Murchison, so this chemistry differs from body to body rather than arriving everywhere in one mixture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Janusz Jurand Petkowski, William Bains, and Sara Seager, “On the Potential of Silicon as a Building Block for Life,” <i>Life</i> 10, no. 6 (2020): 84, https://doi.org/10.3390/life10060084. [Open access]","Aylin Del Moral-Morales, Francisco L. Feitosa, Carolina Horta Andrade, and José L. Medina-Franco, “The (r)evolution of Chemical Space and Molecular Modeling: A Time-Resolved Perspective,” <i>Journal of Computer-Aided Molecular Design</i> 40, no. 1 (2026): 136, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10822-026-00850-1. [Open access]","Yasuhiro Oba, Toshiki Koga, Yoshinori Takano, Hiroshi Naraoka, Yuta Hirakawa, Sako Sunami, Yoshihiro Furukawa, et al., “Distribution of Extraterrestrial Nucleobases, Other N-Heterocycles, and Their Precursors in a Sample from Asteroid Bennu,” <i>Communications Chemistry</i> 9, no. 1 (2026): 132, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42004-026-01966-z. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-037","num":37,"category":"Biology","question":"Small recurring clusters of atoms such as hydroxyl, carboxyl and amino are each called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it is these rather than the carbon skeleton that largely decide how a molecule behaves.","questions":["Collagen's proline is hydroxylated in a step that depends on vitamin C, so scurvy is what follows when a single <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fails to be attached where it belongs.","One survey counted 64,397 phosphorylation sites and 14,245 acetylation sites across proteins, each of them a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> added after the protein was built and altering what it does."],"answer":"functional group","answerText":"functional group","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>functional group</b> is one of the small clusters of atoms that occur over and over again attached to a carbon skeleton — the methyl, hydroxyl, carboxyl, carbonyl, phosphate and amino groups among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each has chemical and physical properties of its own, and it is these rather than the skeleton beneath that largely settle how the molecule containing them behaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same frame carrying a different group is therefore a different substance, which is how a short list of groups generates an enormous variety of molecules from a limited stock of shapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A carboxyl group gives a proton away inside a cell and leaves what carries it negatively charged, while an amino group takes one on and leaves it positive, which is what makes aspartate and glutamate acidic residues and lysine and arginine basic ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The groups are not fixed at the moment a molecule is built, and a cell attaches and removes them afterwards, changing what a protein does without altering the sequence it was made from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That editing has been counted: one recent survey identified 64,397 phosphorylation sites across proteins — 43,721 on serine, 8,414 on threonine and 12,262 on tyrosine — along with 14,245 acetylation and 1,461 mono-methylation sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most such sites sit where an enzyme can reach them, but a 2025 study describes cryptic phosphosites buried inside a protein's core, exposed only while the chain is folding and more conserved than the ones on the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Modifying one of those can destabilise the folded structure and send the protein to be broken up, which makes the position of a group as consequential as its identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A single hydroxyl can decide whether a tissue holds together at all: the proline and lysine of collagen are hydroxylated inside the endoplasmic reticulum in a step that depends on vitamin C.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the vitamin is short that hydroxylation fails, and the result is scurvy, with weak connective tissue, bleeding gums and poor wound healing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Mukesh Kumar, Anthony P. Possemato, Barry M. Zee, Srikanth Subramanian, Jian Min Ren, Alissa J. Nelson, Bin Zhang, et al., “Expanding the Global Map of Protein Post-Translational Modifications with Immunoaffinity Enrichment and nDIA Analysis on the Orbitrap Astral Mass Spectrometer,” <i>Molecular &amp; Cellular Proteomics</i> 25, no. 5 (2026): 101562, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpro.2026.101562. [Open access]","Dino Gasparotto, Annarita Zanon, Valerio Bonaldo, Elisa Marchiori, Massimo Casagranda, Erika Di Domenico, Laura Copat, et al., “Mapping Cryptic Phosphorylation Sites in the Human Proteome,” <i>The EMBO Journal</i> 44, no. 22 (2025): 6704–31, https://doi.org/10.1038/s44318-025-00567-1. [Open access]","Nader Rahimi and Marjorie V. Launico, “Biochemistry, Collagen Synthesis,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2026), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507709/. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-038","num":38,"category":"Biology","question":"Molecules that share a chemical formula but arrange their atoms differently are called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the pairs among them that are mirror images of one another are the optical kind.","questions":["Because 11-cis-retinal flips to an all-trans <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the instant it absorbs a photon, vision begins with a change of shape rather than a change of composition.","Only the l-form of each amino acid is found in proteins, so life uses one member of every mirror-image <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pair and leaves the other very largely alone."],"answer":"isomer","answerText":"isomer","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"Molecules that share a chemical formula but differ in structure are called <b>isomers</b>, and the ones that come as mirror-image pairs are called optical isomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are widespread among organic molecules and are a large part of what makes the sugars so various, since each simple sugar exists in a d-form and an l-form that are mirror images of each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every amino acid but glycine does the same, and life uses one hand of each: only l-forms are ever found in proteins, though d-amino acids occur in bacterial cell walls and in some antibiotics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once that choice was established it could not be changed, because too much had come to depend on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difference need not be handedness at all: the glucose units of cellulose are joined in the β configuration where those of the storage polysaccharides are α, the same sugar differently arranged, and the reason one is a structural material and the other food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An isomerisation can be the working part of a mechanism rather than a curiosity of nomenclature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vision begins when 11-cis-retinal absorbs a photon and is reconfigured to all-trans-retinal, and the spent form has to be re-isomerised before the photoreceptor can respond again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fatty acids come in geometrical isomers, which differ in whether a double bond is cis or trans, and positional isomers, which differ in where along the chain that bond sits; industrial partial hydrogenation produces trans forms now studied as a cause of dyslipidaemia and cardiovascular disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mirror images can even carry different histories, and both forms of glutamic acid are present in the material returned from the asteroid Bennu with nitrogen isotope values differing by 87 parts per thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Since the two are chemically near-identical, a difference that wide implies they were formed or altered under unlike conditions, which unsettles the assumption that a molecule's two hands share one isotopic record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Ala Moshiri and Akrit Sodhi, “Illuminating Retinoid Flux in the Neurosensory Retina,” <i>Journal of Clinical Investigation</i> 136, no. 3 (2026): e201863, https://doi.org/10.1172/jci201863. [Open access]","Mona A. Hegazy, Bojana Vidovic, Shimaa Abobakr, Aleksandra Zeljkovic, Aleksandra Stefanovic, and Jelena Vekic, “Hidden Industrial Trans-Fatty Acids: Mechanistic Insights into Dyslipidemia, Cardiovascular Disease, and Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 26, no. 23 (2025): 11715, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms262311715. [Open access]","Allison A. Baczynski, Ophélie M. McIntosh, Danielle N. Simkus, Hannah L. McLain, Jason P. Dworkin, Daniel P. Glavin, Jamie E. Elsila, et al., “Multiple Formation Pathways for Amino Acids in the Early Solar System Based on Carbon and Nitrogen Isotopes in Asteroid Bennu Samples,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 8 (2026): e2517723123, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2517723123. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"bio-039","num":39,"category":"Biology","question":"Because water is consumed rather than expelled when a bond between two subunits is broken, digesting a polymer is a matter of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the reverse of the reaction that built it.","questions":["Lactose is split into glucose and galactose by lactase in the small intestine, and where that enzyme has declined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fails and the sugar reaches the colon undigested.","Every biological polymer is assembled by expelling water, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name for the reverse reaction, the one that consumes a molecule of water to break each bond."],"answer":"hydrolysis","answerText":"hydrolysis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Hydrolysis</b> is the breaking of a bond by a molecule of water, and it is the reverse of the condensation reaction that made the bond in the first place, in which a molecule of water was expelled as the two parts were joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same pair of reactions builds and dismantles all three great families of biological polymer, so sugars, nucleic acids and proteins are each assembled by losing one water molecule per subunit added and taken apart by consuming one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The condensation half also goes by the name of a dehydration reaction, and it is what joins two monosaccharides through a glycosidic bond between two of their carbons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hydrolysis is not only destructive: the terminal phosphate of ATP is split off by it, and the energy released is what drives reactions that would not otherwise run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nowhere in biology is a peptide, phosphodiester or glycosidic bond made by any other means than this expulsion of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That universality sits oddly with the medium, since water is also what spontaneously takes those polymers apart, and one recent review calls the arrangement a paradox at the heart of biochemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prebiotic chemistry answers it by drying the mixture out and wetting it again, and an experimental study of wet-dry cycling under gamma irradiation reports infrared evidence of peptide bond formation, though its authors describe the results as preliminary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In a living body the reaction is put to work rather than waited for, and lactose is hydrolysed into glucose and galactose by lactase at the brush border of the small intestine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That enzyme peaks shortly after birth and then declines whether or not milk keeps being drunk, a pattern shared with most other mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> People of northern European and northwestern Indian ancestry more often keep it into adult life, so whether one particular bond is hydrolysed at all differs from person to person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","E. Fonseca-González, C. A. Fuentes-Carreon, and A. Heredia-Barbero, “Experimental and Computational Simulation of the Prebiotic Peptide Bond Formation Driven by Wet-Dry Cycles and Gamma Ionizing Radiation: An Insight into Molecular Evolution,” <i>Journal of Molecular Evolution</i> 94, no. 2 (2026): 369–82, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00239-026-10309-4. [Open access]","Eric Goosenberg and Muriam Afzal, “Lactose Intolerance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532285/. [Open access]"]}
];

